if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-3409"]="# CHAPTER I. Who Will Be the New Bishop?\n\nIn the latter days of July in the year 185--, a most important question was for ten days hourly asked in the cathedral city of Barchester, and answered every hour in various ways--Who was to be the new bishop?\n\nThe death of old Dr. Grantly, who had for many years filled that chair with meek authority, took place exactly as the ministry of Lord ---- was going to give place to that of Lord ----. The illness of the good old man was long and lingering, and it became at last a matter of intense interest to those concerned whether the new appointment should be made by a conservative or liberal government.\n\nIt was pretty well understood that the outgoing premier had made his selection and that if the question rested with him, the mitre would descend on the head of Archdeacon Grantly, the old bishop's son. The archdeacon had long managed the affairs of the diocese, and for some months previous to the demise of his father rumour had confidently assigned to him the reversion of his father's honours.\n\nBishop Grantly died as he had lived, peaceably, slowly, without pain and without excitement. The breath ebbed from him almost imperceptibly, and for a month before his death it was a question whether he were alive or dead.\n\nA trying time was this for the archdeacon, for whom was designed the reversion of his father's see by those who then had the giving away of episcopal thrones. I would not be understood to say that the prime minister had in so many words promised the bishopric to Dr. Grantly. He was too discreet a man for that. There is a proverb with reference to the killing of cats, and those who know anything either of high or low government places will be well aware that a promise may be made without positive words and that an expectant may be put into the highest state of encouragement, though the great man on whose breath he hangs may have done no more than whisper that \"Mr. So-and-So is certainly a rising man.\"\n\nSuch a whisper had been made, and was known by those who heard it to signify that the cures of the diocese of Barchester should not be taken out of the hands of the archdeacon. The then prime minister was all in all at Oxford, and had lately passed a night at the house of the Master of Lazarus. Now the Master of Lazarus--which is, by the by, in many respects the most comfortable as well as the richest college at Oxford--was the archdeacon's most intimate friend and most trusted counsellor. On the occasion of the prime minister's visit, Dr. Grantly was of course present, and the meeting was very gracious. On the following morning Dr. Gwynne, the master, told the archdeacon that in his opinion the thing was settled.\n\nAt this time the bishop was quite on his last legs; but the ministry also were tottering. Dr. Grantly returned from Oxford, happy and elated, to resume his place in the palace and to continue to perform for the father the last duties of a son, which, to give him his due, he performed with more tender care than was to be expected from his usual somewhat worldly manners.\n\nA month since, the physicians had named four weeks as the outside period during which breath could be supported within the body of the dying man. At the end of the month the physicians wondered, and named another fortnight. The old man lived on wine alone, but at the end of the fortnight he still lived, and the tidings of the fall of the ministry became more frequent. Sir Lamda Mewnew and Sir Omicron Pie, the two great London doctors, now came down for the fifth time and declared, shaking their learned heads, that another week of life was impossible; and as they sat down to lunch in the episcopal dining-room, whispered to the archdeacon their own private knowledge that the ministry must fall within five days. The son returned to his father's room and, after administering with his own hands the sustaining modicum of madeira, sat down by the bedside to calculate his chances.\n\nThe ministry were to be out within five days: his father was to be dead within--no, he rejected that view of the subject. The ministry were to be out, and the diocese might probably be vacant at the same period. There was much doubt as to the names of the men who were to succeed to power, and a week must elapse before a cabinet was formed. Would not vacancies be filled by the outgoing men during this week? Dr. Grantly had a kind of idea that such would be the case but did not know, and then he wondered at his own ignorance on such a question.\n\nHe tried to keep his mind away from the subject, but he could not. The race was so very close, and the stakes were so very high. He then looked at the dying man's impassive, placid face. There was no sign there of death or disease; it was something thinner than of yore, somewhat grayer, and the deep lines of age more marked; but, as far as he could judge, life might yet hang there for weeks to come. Sir Lamda Mewnew and Sir Omicron Pie had thrice been wrong, and might yet be wrong thrice again. The old bishop slept during twenty of the twenty-four hours, but during the short periods of his waking moments, he knew both his son and his dear old friend, Mr. Harding, the archdeacon's father-in-law, and would thank them tenderly for their care and love. Now he lay sleeping like a baby, resting easily on his back, his mouth just open, and his few gray hairs straggling from beneath his cap; his breath was perfectly noiseless, and his thin, wan hand, which lay above the coverlid, never moved. Nothing could be easier than the old man's passage from this world to the next.\n\nBut by no means easy were the emotions of him who sat there watching. He knew it must be now or never. He was already over fifty, and there was little chance that his friends who were now leaving office would soon return to it. No probable British prime minister but he who was now in, he who was so soon to be out, would think of making a bishop of Dr. Grantly. Thus he thought long and sadly, in deep silence, and then gazed at that still living face, and then at last dared to ask himself whether he really longed for his father's death.\n\nThe effort was a salutary one, and the question was answered in a moment. The proud, wishful, worldly man sank on his knees by the bedside and, taking the bishop's hand within his own, prayed eagerly that his sins might be forgiven him.\n\nHis face was still buried in the clothes when the door of the bedroom opened noiselessly and Mr. Harding entered with a velvet step. Mr. Harding's attendance at that bedside had been nearly as constant as that of the archdeacon, and his ingress and egress was as much a matter of course as that of his son-in-law. He was standing close beside the archdeacon before he was perceived, and would also have knelt in prayer had he not feared that his doing so might have caused some sudden start and have disturbed the dying man. Dr. Grantly, however, instantly perceived him and rose from his knees. As he did so Mr. Harding took both his hands and pressed them warmly. There was more fellowship between them at that moment than there had ever been before, and it so happened that after circumstances greatly preserved the feeling. As they stood there pressing each other's hands, the tears rolled freely down their cheeks.\n\n\"God bless you, my dears,\" said the bishop with feeble voice as he woke. \"God bless you--may God bless you both, my dear children.\" And so he died.\n\nThere was no loud rattle in the throat, no dreadful struggle, no palpable sign of death, but the lower jaw fell a little from its place, and the eyes which had been so constantly closed in sleep now remained fixed and open. Neither Mr. Harding nor Dr. Grantly knew that life was gone, though both suspected it.\n\n\"I believe it's all over,\" said Mr. Harding, still pressing the other's hands. \"I think--nay, I hope it is.\"\n\n\"I will ring the bell,\" said the other, speaking all but in a whisper. \"Mrs. Phillips should be here.\"\n\nMrs. Phillips, the nurse, was soon in the room, and immediately, with practised hand, closed those staring eyes.\n\n\"It's all over, Mrs. Phillips?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"My lord's no more,\" said Mrs. Phillips, turning round and curtseying low with solemn face; \"his lordship's gone more like a sleeping babby than any that I ever saw.\"\n\n\"It's a great relief, Archdeacon,\" said Mr. Harding, \"a great relief--dear, good, excellent old man. Oh that our last moments may be as innocent and as peaceful as his!\"\n\n\"Surely,\" said Mrs. Phillips. \"The Lord be praised for all his mercies; but, for a meek, mild, gentle-spoken Christian, his lordship was--\" and Mrs. Phillips, with unaffected but easy grief, put up her white apron to her flowing eyes.\n\n\"You cannot but rejoice that it is over,\" said Mr. Harding, still consoling his friend. The archdeacon's mind, however, had already travelled from the death chamber to the closet of the prime minister. He had brought himself to pray for his father's life, but now that that life was done, minutes were too precious to be lost. It was now useless to dally with the fact of the bishop's death--useless to lose perhaps everything for the pretence of a foolish sentiment.\n\nBut how was he to act while his father-in-law stood there holding his hand? How, without appearing unfeeling, was he to forget his father in the bishop--to overlook what he had lost, and think only of what he might possibly gain?\n\n\"No, I suppose not,\" said he, at last, in answer to Mr. Harding. \"We have all expected it so long.\"\n\nMr. Harding took him by the arm and led him from the room. \"We will see him again to-morrow morning,\" said he; \"we had better leave the room now to the women.\" And so they went downstairs.\n\nIt was already evening and nearly dark. It was most important that the prime minister should know that night that the diocese was vacant. Everything might depend on it; and so, in answer to Mr. Harding's further consolation, the archdeacon suggested that a telegraph message should be immediately sent off to London. Mr. Harding, who had really been somewhat surprised to find Dr. Grantly, as he thought, so much affected, was rather taken aback, but he made no objection. He knew that the archdeacon had some hope of succeeding to his father's place, though he by no means knew how highly raised that hope had been.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dr. Grantly, collecting himself and shaking off his weakness, \"we must send a message at once; we don't know what might be the consequence of delay. Will you do it?'\n\n\"I! Oh, yes; certainly. I'll do anything, only I don't know exactly what it is you want.\"\n\nDr. Grantly sat down before a writing-table and, taking pen and ink, wrote on a slip of paper as follows:--\n\nBy Electric Telegraph. For the Earl of ----, Downing Street, or elsewhere. The Bishop of Barchester is dead. Message sent by the Rev. Septimus Harding.\n\n\"There,\" said he. \"Just take that to the telegraph office at the railway station and give it in as it is; they'll probably make you copy it on to one of their own slips; that's all you'll have to do; then you'll have to pay them half a crown.\" And the archdeacon put his hand in his pocket and pulled out the necessary sum.\n\nMr. Harding felt very much like an errand-boy, and also felt that he was called on to perform his duties as such at rather an unseemly time, but he said nothing, and took the slip of paper and the proffered coin.\n\n\"But you've put my name into it, Archdeacon.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said the other, \"there should be the name of some clergyman, you know, and what name so proper as that of so old a friend as yourself? The earl won't look at the name, you may be sure of that; but my dear Mr. Harding, pray don't lose any time.\"\n\nMr. Harding got as far as the library door on his way to the station, when he suddenly remembered the news with which he was fraught when he entered the poor bishop's bedroom. He had found the moment so inopportune for any mundane tidings, that he had repressed the words which were on his tongue, and immediately afterwards all recollection of the circumstance was for the time banished by the scene which had occurred.\n\n\"But, Archdeacon,\" said he, turning back, \"I forgot to tell you--the ministry are out.\"\n\n\"Out!\" ejaculated the archdeacon, in a tone which too plainly showed his anxiety and dismay, although under the circumstances of the moment he endeavoured to control himself. \"Out! Who told you so?\"\n\nMr. Harding explained that news to this effect had come down by electric telegraph, and that the tidings had been left at the palace door by Mr. Chadwick.\n\nThe archdeacon sat silent for awhile meditating, and Mr. Harding stood looking at him. \"Never mind,\" said the archdeacon at last; \"send the message all the same. The news must be sent to someone, and there is at present no one else in a position to receive it. Do it at once, my dear friend; you know I would not trouble you, were I in a state to do it myself. A few minutes' time is of the greatest importance.\"\n\nMr. Harding went out and sent the message, and it may be as well that we should follow it to its destination. Within thirty minutes of its leaving Barchester it reached the Earl of ---- in his inner library. What elaborate letters, what eloquent appeals, what indignant remonstrances he might there have to frame, at such a moment, may be conceived but not described! How he was preparing his thunder for successful rivals, standing like a British peer with his back to the sea-coal fire, and his hands in his breeches pockets--how his fine eye was lit up with anger, and his forehead gleamed with patriotism--how he stamped his foot as he thought of his heavy associates--how he all but swore as he remembered how much too clever one of them had been--my creative readers may imagine. But was he so engaged? No: history and truth compel me to deny it. He was sitting easily in a lounging chair, conning over a Newmarket list, and by his elbow on the table was lying open an uncut French novel on which he was engaged.\n\nHe opened the cover in which the message was enclosed and, having read it, he took his pen and wrote on the back of it--\n\nFor the Earl of ----, With the Earl of ----'s compliments\n\nand sent it off again on its journey.\n\nThus terminated our unfortunate friend's chances of possessing the glories of a bishopric.\n\nThe names of many divines were given in the papers as that of the bishop-elect. \"The British Grandmother\" declared that Dr. Gwynne was to be the man, in compliment to the late ministry. This was a heavy blow to Dr. Grantly, but he was not doomed to see himself superseded by his friend. \"The Anglican Devotee\" put forward confidently the claims of a great London preacher of austere doctrines; and \"The Eastern Hemisphere,\" an evening paper supposed to possess much official knowledge, declared in favour of an eminent naturalist, a gentleman most completely versed in the knowledge of rocks and minerals, but supposed by many to hold on religious subjects no special doctrines whatever. \"The Jupiter,\" that daily paper which, as we all know, is the only true source of infallibly correct information on all subjects, for awhile was silent, but at last spoke out. The merits of all these candidates were discussed and somewhat irreverently disposed of, and then \"The Jupiter\" declared that Dr. Proudie was to be the man.\n\nDr. Proudie was the man. Just a month after the demise of the late bishop, Dr. Proudie kissed the Queen's hand as his successor-elect.\n\nWe must beg to be allowed to draw a curtain over the sorrows of the archdeacon as he sat, sombre and sad at heart, in the study of his parsonage at Plumstead Episcopi. On the day subsequent to the dispatch of the message he heard that the Earl of ---- had consented to undertake the formation of a ministry, and from that moment he knew that his chance was over. Many will think that he was wicked to grieve for the loss of episcopal power, wicked to have coveted it, nay, wicked even to have thought about it, in the way and at the moments he had done so.\n\nWith such censures I cannot profess that I completely agree. The nolo episcopari, though still in use, is so directly at variance with the tendency of all human wishes, that it cannot be thought to express the true aspirations of rising priests in the Church of England. A lawyer does not sin in seeking to be a judge, or in compassing his wishes by all honest means. A young diplomat entertains a fair ambition when he looks forward to be the lord of a first-rate embassy; and a poor novelist, when he attempts to rival Dickens or rise above Fitzjeames, commits no fault, though he may be foolish. Sydney Smith truly said that in these recreant days we cannot expect to find the majesty of St. Paul beneath the cassock of a curate. If we look to our clergymen to be more than men, we shall probably teach ourselves to think that they are less, and can hardly hope to raise the character of the pastor by denying to him the right to entertain the aspirations of a man.\n\nOur archdeacon was worldly--who among us is not so? He was ambitious--who among us is ashamed to own that \"last infirmity of noble minds!\" He was avaricious, my readers will say. No;--it was for no love of lucre that he wished to be Bishop of Barchester. He was his father's only child, and his father had left him great wealth. His preferment brought him in nearly three thousand a year. The bishopric, as cut down by the Ecclesiastical Commission, was only five. He would be a richer man as archdeacon than he could be as bishop. But he certainly did desire to play first fiddle; he did desire to sit in full lawn sleeves among the peers of the realm; and he did desire, if the truth must out, to be called \"My lord\" by his reverend brethren.\n\nHis hopes, however, were they innocent or sinful, were not fated to be realized, and Dr. Proudie was consecrated Bishop of Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER II. Hiram's Hospital According to Act of Parliament\n\nIt is hardly necessary that I should here give to the public any lengthened biography of Mr. Harding up to the period of the commencement of this tale. The public cannot have forgotten how ill that sensitive gentleman bore the attack that was made on him in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" with reference to the income which he received as warden of Hiram's Hospital, in the city of Barchester. Nor can it yet be forgotten that a lawsuit was instituted against him on the matter of that charity by Mr. John Bold, who afterwards married his, Mr. Harding's, younger and then only unmarried daughter. Under pressure of these attacks, Mr. Harding had resigned his wardenship, though strongly recommended to abstain from doing so both by his friends and by his lawyers. He did, however, resign it, and betook himself manfully to the duties of the small parish of St. Cuthbert's, in the city, of which he was vicar, continuing also to perform those of precentor of the cathedral, a situation of small emolument which had hitherto been supposed to be joined, as a matter of course, to the wardenship of the hospital above spoken of.\n\nWhen he left the hospital from which he had been so ruthlessly driven, and settled himself down in his own modest manner in the High Street of Barchester, he had not expected that others would make more fuss about it than he was inclined to do himself; and the extent of his hope was, that the movement might have been made in time to prevent any further paragraphs in \"The Jupiter.\" His affairs, however, were not allowed to subside thus quietly, and people were quite as much inclined to talk about the disinterested sacrifice he had made, as they had before been to upbraid him for his cupidity.\n\nThe most remarkable thing that occurred was the receipt of an autographed letter from the Archbishop of Canterbury, in which the primate very warmly praised his conduct, and begged to know what his intentions were for the future. Mr. Harding replied that he intended to be rector of St. Cuthbert's, in Barchester, and so that matter dropped. Then the newspapers took up his case, \"The Jupiter\" among the rest, and wafted his name in eulogistic strains through every reading-room in the nation. It was discovered also that he was the author of that great musical work, Harding's Church Music,--and a new edition was spoken of, though, I believe, never printed. It is, however, certain that the work was introduced into the Royal Chapel at St. James's, and that a long criticism appeared in the \"Musical Scrutator,\" declaring that in no previous work of the kind had so much research been joined with such exalted musical ability, and asserting that the name of Harding would henceforward be known wherever the arts were cultivated, or religion valued.\n\nThis was high praise, and I will not deny that Mr. Harding was gratified by such flattery; for if Mr. Harding was vain on any subject, it was on that of music. But here the matter rested. The second edition, if printed, was never purchased; the copies which had been introduced into the Royal Chapel disappeared again, and were laid by in peace, with a load of similar literature. Mr. Towers of \"The Jupiter\" and his brethren occupied themselves with other names, and the undying fame promised to our friend was clearly intended to be posthumous.\n\nMr. Harding had spent much of his time with his friend the bishop; much with his daughter Mrs. Bold, now, alas, a widow; and had almost daily visited the wretched remnant of his former subjects, the few surviving bedesmen now left at Hiram's Hospital. Six of them were still living. The number, according to old Hiram's will, should always have been twelve. But after the abdication of their warden, the bishop had appointed no successor to him, no new occupants of the charity had been nominated, and it appeared as though the hospital at Barchester would fall into abeyance, unless the powers that be should take some steps towards putting it once more into working order.\n\nDuring the past five years, the powers that be had not overlooked Barchester Hospital, and sundry political doctors had taken the matter in hand. Shortly after Mr. Harding's resignation, \"The Jupiter\" had very clearly shown what ought to be done. In about half a column it had distributed the income, rebuilt the buildings, put an end to all bickerings, regenerated kindly feeling, provided for Mr. Harding, and placed the whole thing on a footing which could not but be satisfactory to the city and Bishop of Barchester, and to the nation at large. The wisdom of this scheme was testified by the number of letters which \"Common Sense,\" \"Veritas,\" and \"One that loves fair play\" sent to \"The Jupiter\", all expressing admiration and amplifying on the details given. It is singular enough that no adverse letter appeared at all, and, therefore, none of course was written.\n\nBut Cassandra was not believed, and even the wisdom of \"The Jupiter\" sometimes falls on deaf ears. Though other plans did not put themselves forward in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" reformers of church charities were not slack to make known in various places their different nostrums for setting Hiram's Hospital on its feet again. A learned bishop took occasion, in the Upper House, to allude to the matter, intimating that he had communicated on the subject with his right reverend brother of Barchester. The radical member for Staleybridge had suggested that the funds should be alienated for the education of the agricultural poor of the country, and he amused the house by some anecdotes touching the superstition and habits of the agriculturists in question. A political pamphleteer had produced a few dozen pages, which he called \"Who are John Hiram's heirs?\" intending to give an infallible rule for the governance of all such establishments; and, at last, a member of the government promised that in the next session a short bill should be introduced for regulating the affairs of Barchester and other kindred concerns.\n\nThe next session came, and, contrary to custom, the bill came also. Men's minds were then intent on other things. The first threatenings of a huge war hung heavily over the nation, and the question as to Hiram's heirs did not appear to interest very many people either in or out of the house. The bill, however, was read and re-read, and in some undistinguished manner passed through its eleven stages without appeal or dissent. What would John Hiram have said in the matter, could he have predicted that some forty-five gentlemen would take on themselves to make a law altering the whole purport of his will, without in the least knowing at the moment of their making it, what it was that they were doing? It is however to be hoped that the under-secretary for the Home Office knew, for to him had the matter been confided.\n\nThe bill, however, did pass, and at the time at which this history is supposed to commence, it had been ordained that there should be, as heretofore, twelve old men in Barchester Hospital, each with 1s. 4d. a day; that there should also be twelve old women to be located in a house to be built, each with 1s. 2d. a day; that there should be a matron, with a house and £70 a year; a steward with £150 a year; and latterly, a warden with £450 a year, who should have the spiritual guidance of both establishments, and the temporal guidance of that appertaining to the male sex. The bishop, dean, and warden were, as formerly, to appoint in turn the recipients of the charity, and the bishop was to appoint the officers. There was nothing said as to the wardenship being held by the precentor of the cathedral, nor a word as to Mr. Harding's right to the situation.\n\nIt was not, however, till some months after the death of the old bishop, and almost immediately consequent on the installation of his successor, that notice was given that the reform was about to be carried out. The new law and the new bishop were among the earliest works of a new ministry, or rather of a ministry who, having for awhile given place to their opponents, had then returned to power; and the death of Dr. Grantly occurred, as we have seen, exactly at the period of the change.\n\nPoor Eleanor Bold! How well does that widow's cap become her, and the solemn gravity with which she devotes herself to her new duties. Poor Eleanor!\n\nPoor Eleanor! I cannot say that with me John Bold was ever a favourite. I never thought him worthy of the wife he had won. But in her estimation he was most worthy. Hers was one of those feminine hearts which cling to a husband, not with idolatry, for worship can admit of no defect in its idol, but with the perfect tenacity of ivy. As the parasite plant will follow even the defects of the trunk which it embraces, so did Eleanor cling to and love the very faults of her husband. She had once declared that whatever her father did should in her eyes be right. She then transferred her allegiance, and became ever ready to defend the worst failings of her lord and master.\n\nAnd John Bold was a man to be loved by a woman; he was himself affectionate; he was confiding and manly; and that arrogance of thought, unsustained by first-rate abilities, that attempt at being better than his neighbours which jarred so painfully on the feelings of his acquaintance, did not injure him in the estimation of his wife.\n\nCould she even have admitted that he had a fault, his early death would have blotted out the memory of it. She wept as for the loss of the most perfect treasure with which mortal woman had ever been endowed; for weeks after he was gone the idea of future happiness in this world was hateful to her; consolation, as it is called, was insupportable, and tears and sleep were her only relief.\n\nBut God tempers the wind to the shorn lamb. She knew that she had within her the living source of other cares. She knew that there was to be created for her another subject of weal or woe, of unutterable joy or despairing sorrow, as God in his mercy might vouchsafe to her. At first this did but augment her grief! To be the mother of a poor infant, orphaned before it was born, brought forth to the sorrows of an ever desolate hearth, nurtured amidst tears and wailing, and then turned adrift into the world without the aid of a father's care! There was at first no joy in this.\n\nBy degrees, however, her heart became anxious for another object, and, before its birth, the stranger was expected with all the eagerness of a longing mother. Just eight months after the father's death a second John Bold was born, and if the worship of one creature can be innocent in another, let us hope that the adoration offered over the cradle of the fatherless infant may not be imputed as a sin.\n\nIt will not be worth our while to define the character of the child, or to point out in how far the faults of the father were redeemed within that little breast by the virtues of the mother. The baby, as a baby, was all that was delightful, and I cannot foresee that it will be necessary for us to inquire into the facts of his after-life. Our present business at Barchester will not occupy us above a year or two at the furthest, and I will leave it to some other pen to produce, if necessary, the biography of John Bold the Younger.\n\nBut, as a baby, this baby was all that could be desired. This fact no one attempted to deny. \"Is he not delightful?\" she would say to her father, looking up into his face from her knees, her lustrous eyes overflowing with soft tears, her young face encircled by her close widow's cap, and her hands on each side of the cradle in which her treasure was sleeping. The grandfather would gladly admit that the treasure was delightful, and the uncle archdeacon himself would agree, and Mrs. Grantly, Eleanor's sister, would re-echo the word with true sisterly energy; and Mary Bold--but Mary Bold was a second worshipper at the same shrine.\n\nThe baby was really delightful; he took his food with a will, struck out his toes merrily whenever his legs were uncovered, and did not have fits. These are supposed to be the strongest points of baby perfection, and in all these our baby excelled.\n\nAnd thus the widow's deep grief was softened, and a sweet balm was poured into the wound which she had thought nothing but death could heal. How much kinder is God to us than we are willing to be to ourselves! At the loss of every dear face, at the last going of every well-beloved one, we all doom ourselves to an eternity of sorrow, and look to waste ourselves away in an ever-running fountain of tears. How seldom does such grief endure! How blessed is the goodness which forbids it to do so! \"Let me ever remember my living friends, but forget them as soon as dead,\" was the prayer of a wise man who understood the mercy of God. Few perhaps would have the courage to express such a wish, and yet to do so would only be to ask for that release from sorrow which a kind Creator almost always extends to us.\n\nI would not, however, have it imagined that Mrs. Bold forgot her husband. She daily thought of him with all conjugal love, and enshrined his memory in the innermost centre of her heart. But yet she was happy in her baby. It was so sweet to press the living toy to her breast, and feel that a human being existed who did owe, and was to owe, everything to her; whose daily food was drawn from herself; whose little wants could all be satisfied by her; whose little heart would first love her and her only; whose infant tongue would make its first effort in calling her by the sweetest name a woman can hear. And so Eleanor's bosom became tranquil, and she set about her new duties eagerly and gratefully.\n\nAs regards the concerns of the world, John Bold had left his widow in prosperous circumstances. He had bequeathed to her all that he possessed, and that comprised an income much exceeding what she or her friends thought necessary for her. It amounted to nearly a thousand a year; when she reflected on its extent, her dearest hope was to hand it over, not only unimpaired but increased, to her husband's son, to her own darling, to the little man who now lay sleeping on her knee, happily ignorant of the cares which were to be accumulated in his behalf.\n\nWhen John Bold died, she earnestly implored her father to come and live with her, but this Mr. Harding declined, though for some weeks he remained with her as a visitor. He could not be prevailed upon to forego the possession of some small home of his own, and so remained in the lodgings he had first selected over a chemist's shop in the High Street of Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER III. Dr. and Mrs. Proudie\n\nThis narrative is supposed to commence immediately after the installation of Dr. Proudie. I will not describe the ceremony, as I do not precisely understand its nature. I am ignorant whether a bishop be chaired like a member of Parliament, or carried in a gilt coach like a lord mayor, or sworn like a justice of peace, or introduced like a peer to the upper house, or led between two brethren like a knight of the garter; but I do know that everything was properly done, and that nothing fit or becoming to a young bishop was omitted on the occasion.\n\nDr. Proudie was not the man to allow anything to be omitted that might be becoming to his new dignity. He understood well the value of forms, and knew that the due observance of rank could not be maintained unless the exterior trappings belonging to it were held in proper esteem. He was a man born to move in high circles; at least so he thought himself, and circumstances had certainly sustained him in this view. He was the nephew of an Irish baron by his mother's side, and his wife was the niece of a Scotch earl. He had for years held some clerical office appertaining to courtly matters, which had enabled him to live in London, and to entrust his parish to his curate. He had been preacher to the royal beefeaters, curator of theological manuscripts in the Ecclesiastical Courts, chaplain to the Queen's yeomanry guard, and almoner to his Royal Highness the Prince of Rappe-Blankenberg.\n\nHis residence in the metropolis, rendered necessary by duties thus entrusted to him, his high connexions, and the peculiar talents and nature of the man, recommended him to persons in power, and Dr. Proudie became known as a useful and rising clergyman.\n\nSome few years since, even within the memory of many who are not yet willing to call themselves old, a liberal clergyman was a person not frequently to be met. Sydney Smith was such and was looked on as little better than an infidel; a few others also might be named, but they were rarae aves and were regarded with doubt and distrust by their brethren. No man was so surely a Tory as a country rector--nowhere were the powers that be so cherished as at Oxford.\n\nWhen, however, Dr. Whately was made an archbishop, and Dr. Hampden some years afterwards regius professor, many wise divines saw that a change was taking place in men's minds, and that more liberal ideas would henceforward be suitable to the priests as well as to the laity. Clergymen began to be heard of who had ceased to anathematize papists on the one hand, or vilify dissenters on the other. It appeared clear that High Church principles, as they are called, were no longer to be surest claims to promotion with at any rate one section of statesmen, and Dr. Proudie was one among those who early in life adapted himself to the views held by the Whigs on most theological and religious subjects. He bore with the idolatry of Rome, tolerated even the infidelity of Socinianism, and was hand and glove with the Presbyterian Synods of Scotland and Ulster.\n\nSuch a man at such a time was found to be useful, and Dr. Proudie's name began to appear in the newspapers. He was made one of a commission who went over to Ireland to arrange matters preparative to the working of the national board; he became honorary secretary to another commission nominated to inquire into the revenues of cathedral chapters; he had had something to do with both the regium donum and the Maynooth grant.\n\nIt must not on this account be taken as proved that Dr. Proudie was a man of great mental powers, or even of much capacity for business, for such qualities had not been required in him. In the arrangement of those church reforms with which he was connected, the ideas and original conception of the work to be done were generally furnished by the liberal statesmen of the day, and the labour of the details was borne by officials of a lower rank. It was, however, thought expedient that the name of some clergyman should appear in such matters, and as Dr. Proudie had become known as a tolerating divine, great use of this sort was made of his name. If he did not do much active good, he never did any harm; he was amenable to those who were really in authority and, at the sittings of the various boards to which he belonged, maintained a kind of dignity which had its value.\n\nHe was certainly possessed of sufficient tact to answer the purpose for which he was required without making himself troublesome; but it must not therefore be surmised that he doubted his own power, or failed to believe that he could himself take a high part in high affairs when his own turn came. He was biding his time, and patiently looking forward to the days when he himself would sit authoritative at some board, and talk and direct, and rule the roost, while lesser stars sat round and obeyed, as he had so well accustomed himself to do.\n\nHis reward and his time had now come. He was selected for the vacant bishopric and, on the next vacancy which might occur in any diocese, would take his place in the House of Lords, prepared to give not a silent vote in all matters concerning the weal of the church establishment. Toleration was to be the basis on which he was to fight his battles, and in the honest courage of his heart he thought no evil would come to him in encountering even such foes as his brethren of Exeter and Oxford.\n\nDr. Proudie was an ambitious man, and before he was well consecrated Bishop of Barchester, he had begun to look up to archiepiscopal splendour, and the glories of Lambeth, or at any rate of Bishopsthorpe. He was comparatively young, and had, as he fondly flattered himself, been selected as possessing such gifts, natural and acquired, as must be sure to recommend him to a yet higher notice, now that a higher sphere was opened to him. Dr. Proudie was, therefore, quite prepared to take a conspicuous part in all theological affairs appertaining to these realms; and having such views, by no means intended to bury himself at Barchester as his predecessor had done. No! London should still be his ground: a comfortable mansion in a provincial city might be well enough for the dead months of the year. Indeed, Dr. Proudie had always felt it necessary to his position to retire from London when other great and fashionable people did so; but London should still be his fixed residence, and it was in London that he resolved to exercise that hospitality so peculiarly recommended to all bishops by St. Paul. How otherwise could he keep himself before the world? How else give to the government, in matters theological, the full benefit of his weight and talents?\n\nThis resolution was no doubt a salutary one as regarded the world at large, but was not likely to make him popular either with the clergy or people of Barchester. Dr. Grantly had always lived there--in truth, it was hard for a bishop to be popular after Dr. Grantly. His income had averaged £9,000 a year; his successor was to be rigidly limited to £5,000. He had but one child on whom to spend his money; Dr. Proudie had seven or eight. He had been a man of few personal expenses, and they had been confined to the tastes of a moderate gentleman; but Dr. Proudie had to maintain a position in fashionable society, and had that to do with comparatively small means. Dr. Grantly had certainly kept his carriage as became a bishop, but his carriage, horses, and coachman, though they did very well for Barchester, would have been almost ridiculous at Westminster. Mrs. Proudie determined that her husband's equipage should not shame her, and things on which Mrs. Proudie resolved were generally accomplished.\n\nFrom all this it was likely to result that Dr. Proudie would not spend much money at Barchester, whereas his predecessor had dealt with the tradesmen of the city in a manner very much to their satisfaction. The Grantlys, father and son, had spent their money like gentlemen, but it soon became whispered in Barchester that Dr. Proudie was not unacquainted with those prudent devices by which the utmost show of wealth is produced from limited means.\n\nIn person Dr. Proudie is a good-looking man; spruce and dapper, and very tidy. He is somewhat below middle height, being about five feet four; but he makes up for the inches which he wants by the dignity with which he carries those which he has. It is no fault of his own if he has not a commanding eye, for he studies hard to assume it. His features are well formed, though perhaps the sharpness of his nose may give to his face in the eyes of some people an air of insignificance. If so, it is greatly redeemed by his mouth and chin, of which he is justly proud.\n\nDr. Proudie may well be said to have been a fortunate man, for he was not born to wealth, and he is now Bishop of Barchester; nevertheless, he has his cares. He has a large family, of whom the three eldest are daughters, now all grown up and fit for fashionable life;--and he has a wife. It is not my intention to breathe a word against the character of Mrs. Proudie, but still I cannot think that with all her virtues she adds much to her husband's happiness. The truth is that in matters domestic she rules supreme over her titular lord, and rules with a rod of iron. Nor is this all. Things domestic Dr. Proudie might have abandoned to her, if not voluntarily, yet willingly. But Mrs. Proudie is not satisfied with such home dominion, and stretches her power over all his movements, and will not even abstain from things spiritual. In fact, the bishop is hen-pecked.\n\nThe archdeacon's wife, in her happy home at Plumstead, knows how to assume the full privileges of her rank and express her own mind in becoming tone and place. But Mrs. Grantly's sway, if sway she has, is easy and beneficent. She never shames her husband; before the world she is a pattern of obedience; her voice is never loud, nor her looks sharp: doubtless she values power, and has not unsuccessfully striven to acquire it; but she knows what should be the limits of a woman's rule.\n\nNot so Mrs. Proudie. This lady is habitually authoritative to all, but to her poor husband she is despotic. Successful as has been his career in the eyes of the world, it would seem that in the eyes of his wife he is never right. All hope of defending himself has long passed from him; indeed he rarely even attempts self-justification, and is aware that submission produces the nearest approach to peace which his own house can ever attain.\n\nMrs. Proudie has not been able to sit at the boards and committees to which her husband has been called by the State, nor, as he often reflects, can she make her voice heard in the House of Lords. It may be that she will refuse to him permission to attend to this branch of a bishop's duties; it may be that she will insist on his close attendance to his own closet. He has never whispered a word on the subject to living ears, but he has already made his fixed resolve. Should such attempt be made he will rebel. Dogs have turned against their masters, and even Neapolitans against their rulers, when oppression has been too severe. And Dr. Proudie feels within himself that if the cord be drawn too tight, he also can muster courage and resist.\n\nThe state of vassalage in which our bishop has been kept by his wife has not tended to exalt his character in the eyes of his daughters, who assume in addressing their father too much of that authority which is not properly belonging, at any rate, to them. They are, on the whole, fine engaging young ladies. They are tall and robust like their mother, whose high cheek-bones, and--we may say auburn hair they all inherit. They think somewhat too much of their grand-uncles, who have not hitherto returned the compliment by thinking much of them. But now that their father is a bishop, it is probable that family ties will be drawn closer. Considering their connexion with the church, they entertain but few prejudices against the pleasures of the world, and have certainly not distressed their parents, as too many English girls have lately done, by any enthusiastic wish to devote themselves to the seclusion of a Protestant nunnery. Dr. Proudie's sons are still at school.\n\nOne other marked peculiarity in the character of the bishop's wife must be mentioned. Though not averse to the society and manners of the world, she is in her own way a religious woman; and the form in which this tendency shows itself in her is by a strict observance of Sabbatarian rule. Dissipation and low dresses during the week are, under her control, atoned for by three services, an evening sermon read by herself, and a perfect abstinence from any cheering employment on the Sunday. Unfortunately for those under her roof to whom the dissipation and low dresses are not extended, her servants namely and her husband, the compensating strictness of the Sabbath includes all. Woe betide the recreant housemaid who is found to have been listening to the honey of a sweetheart in the Regent's park instead of the soul-stirring evening discourse of Mr. Slope. Not only is she sent adrift, but she is so sent with a character which leaves her little hope of a decent place. Woe betide the six-foot hero who escorts Mrs. Proudie to her pew in red plush breeches, if he slips away to the neighbouring beer-shop, instead of falling into the back seat appropriated to his use. Mrs. Proudie has the eyes of Argus for such offenders. Occasional drunkenness in the week may be overlooked, for six feet on low wages are hardly to be procured if the morals are always kept at a high pitch, but not even for grandeur or economy will Mrs. Proudie forgive a desecration of the Sabbath.\n\nIn such matters Mrs. Proudie allows herself to be often guided by that eloquent preacher, the Rev. Mr. Slope, and as Dr. Proudie is guided by his wife, it necessarily follows that the eminent man we have named has obtained a good deal of control over Dr. Proudie in matters concerning religion. Mr. Slope's only preferment has hitherto been that of reader and preacher in a London district church; and on the consecration of his friend the new bishop, he readily gave this up to undertake the onerous but congenial duties of domestic chaplain to his lordship.\n\nMr. Slope, however, on his first introduction must not be brought before the public at the tail of a chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. The Bishop's Chaplain\n\nOf the Rev. Mr. Slope's parentage I am not able to say much. I have heard it asserted that he is lineally descended from that eminent physician who assisted at the birth of Mr. T. Shandy, and that in early years he added an \"e\" to his name, for the sake of euphony, as other great men have done before him. If this be so, I presume he was christened Obadiah, for that is his name, in commemoration of the conflict in which his ancestor so distinguished himself. All my researches on the subject have, however, failed in enabling me to fix the date on which the family changed its religion.\n\nHe had been a sizar at Cambridge, and had there conducted himself at any rate successfully, for in due process of time he was an M.A., having university pupils under his care. From thence he was transferred to London, and became preacher at a new district church built on the confines of Baker Street. He was in this position when congenial ideas on religious subjects recommended him to Mrs. Proudie, and the intercourse had become close and confidential.\n\nHaving been thus familiarly thrown among the Misses Proudie, it was no more than natural that some softer feeling than friendship should be engendered. There have been some passages of love between him and the eldest hope, Olivia, but they have hitherto resulted in no favourable arrangement. In truth, Mr. Slope, having made a declaration of affection, afterwards withdrew it on finding that the doctor had no immediate worldly funds with which to endow his child, and it may easily be conceived that Miss Proudie, after such an announcement on his part, was not readily disposed to receive any further show of affection. On the appointment of Dr. Proudie to the bishopric of Barchester, Mr. Slope's views were in truth somewhat altered. Bishops, even though they be poor, can provide for clerical children, and Mr. Slope began to regret that he had not been more disinterested. He no sooner heard the tidings of the doctor's elevation than he recommenced his siege, not violently, indeed, but respectfully, and at a distance. Olivia Proudie, however, was a girl of spirit: she had the blood of two peers in her veins, and better still she had another lover on her books, so Mr. Slope sighed in vain, and the pair soon found it convenient to establish a mutual bond of inveterate hatred.\n\nIt may be thought singular that Mrs. Proudie's friendship for the young clergyman should remain firm after such an affair, but, to tell the truth, she had known nothing of it. Though very fond of Mr. Slope herself, she had never conceived the idea that either of her daughters would become so, and remembering their high birth and social advantages, expected for them matches of a different sort. Neither the gentleman nor the lady found it necessary to enlighten her. Olivia's two sisters had each known of the affair, as had all the servants, as had all the people living in the adjoining houses on either side, but Mrs. Proudie had been kept in the dark.\n\nMr. Slope soon comforted himself with the reflexion that, as he had been selected as chaplain to the bishop, it would probably be in his power to get the good things in the bishop's gift without troubling himself with the bishop's daughter, and he found himself able to endure the pangs of rejected love. As he sat himself down in the railway carriage, confronting the bishop and Mrs. Proudie as they started on their first journey to Barchester, he began to form in his own mind a plan of his future life. He knew well his patron's strong points, but he knew the weak ones as well. He understood correctly enough to what attempts the new bishop's high spirit would soar, and he rightly guessed that public life would better suit the great man's taste than the small details of diocesan duty.\n\nHe, therefore,--he, Mr. Slope,--would in effect be Bishop of Barchester. Such was his resolve, and to give Mr. Slope his due, he had both courage and spirit to bear him out in his resolution. He knew that he should have a hard battle to fight, for the power and patronage of the see would be equally coveted by another great mind--Mrs. Proudie would also choose to be Bishop of Barchester. Mr. Slope, however, flattered himself that he could outmanoeuvre the lady. She must live much in London, while he would always be on the spot. She would necessarily remain ignorant of much, while he would know everything belonging to the diocese. At first, doubtless, he must flatter and cajole, perhaps yield in some things, but he did not doubt of ultimate triumph. If all other means failed, he could join the bishop against his wife, inspire courage into the unhappy man, lay an axe to the root of the woman's power, and emancipate the husband.\n\nSuch were his thoughts as he sat looking at the sleeping pair in the railway carriage, and Mr. Slope is not the man to trouble himself with such thoughts for nothing. He is possessed of more than average abilities, and is of good courage. Though he can stoop to fawn, and stoop low indeed, if need be, he has still within him the power to assume the tyrant;--and with the power he has certainly the wish. His acquirements are not of the highest order, but such as they are, they are completely under control, and he knows the use of them. He is gifted with a certain kind of pulpit eloquence, not likely indeed to be persuasive with men, but powerful with the softer sex. In his sermons he deals greatly in denunciations, excites the minds of his weaker hearers with a not unpleasant terror, and leaves an impression on their minds that all mankind are in a perilous state, and all womankind, too, except those who attend regularly to the evening lectures in Baker Street. His looks and tones are extremely severe, so much so that one cannot but fancy that he regards the greater part of the world as being infinitely too bad for his care. As he walks through the streets his very face denotes his horror of the world's wickedness, and there is always an anathema lurking in the corner of his eye.\n\nIn doctrine he, like his patron, is tolerant of dissent, if so strict a mind can be called tolerant of anything. With Wesleyan-Methodists he has something in common, but his soul trembles in agony at the iniquities of the Puseyites. His aversion is carried to things outward as well as inward. His gall rises at a new church with a high-pitched roof; a full-breasted black silk waistcoat is with him a symbol of Satan; and a profane jest-book would not, in his view, more foully desecrate the church seat of a Christian than a book of prayer printed with red letters and ornamented with a cross on the back. Most active clergymen have their hobby, and Sunday observances are his. Sunday, however, is a word which never pollutes his mouth--it is always \"the Sabbath.\" The \"desecration of the Sabbath,\" as he delights to call it, is to him meat and drink: he thrives upon that as policemen do on the general evil habits of the community. It is the loved subject of all his evening discourses, the source of all his eloquence, the secret of all his power over the female heart. To him the revelation of God appears only in that one law given for Jewish observance. To him the mercies of our Saviour speak in vain, to him in vain has been preached that sermon which fell from divine lips on the mountain--\"Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth\"--\"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy.\" To him the New Testament is comparatively of little moment, for from it can he draw no fresh authority for that dominion which he loves to exercise over at least a seventh part of man's allotted time here below.\n\nMr. Slope is tall, and not ill-made. His feet and hands are large, as has ever been the case with all his family, but he has a broad chest and wide shoulders to carry off these excrescences, and on the whole his figure is good. His countenance, however, is not specially prepossessing. His hair is lank and of a dull pale reddish hue. It is always formed into three straight, lumpy masses, each brushed with admirable precision and cemented with much grease; two of them adhere closely to the sides of his face, and the other lies at right angles above them. He wears no whiskers, and is always punctiliously shaven. His face is nearly of the same colour as his hair, though perhaps a little redder: it is not unlike beef--beef, however, one would say, of a bad quality. His forehead is capacious and high, but square and heavy and unpleasantly shining. His mouth is large, though his lips are thin and bloodless; and his big, prominent, pale-brown eyes inspire anything but confidence. His nose, however, is his redeeming feature: it is pronounced, straight and well-formed; though I myself should have liked it better did it not possess a somewhat spongy, porous appearance, as though it had been cleverly formed out of a red-coloured cork.\n\nI never could endure to shake hands with Mr. Slope. A cold, clammy perspiration always exudes from him, the small drops are ever to be seen standing on his brow, and his friendly grasp is unpleasant.\n\nSuch is Mr. Slope--such is the man who has suddenly fallen into the midst of Barchester Close, and is destined there to assume the station which has heretofore been filled by the son of the late bishop. Think, oh, my meditative reader, what an associate we have here for those comfortable prebendaries, those gentlemanlike clerical doctors, those happy, well-used, well-fed minor canons who have grown into existence at Barchester under the kindly wings of Bishop Grantly!\n\nBut not as a mere associate for these does Mr. Slope travel down to Barchester with the bishop and his wife. He intends to be, if not their master, at least the chief among them. He intends to lead and to have followers; he intends to hold the purse-strings of the diocese and draw round him an obedient herd of his poor and hungry brethren.\n\nAnd here we can hardly fail to draw a comparison between the archdeacon and our new private chaplain, and despite the manifold faults of the former, one can hardly fail to make it much to his advantage.\n\nBoth men are eager, much too eager, to support and increase the power of their order. Both are anxious that the world should be priest-governed, though they have probably never confessed so much, even to themselves. Both begrudge any other kind of dominion held by man over man. Dr. Grantly, if he admits the Queen's supremacy in things spiritual, only admits it as being due to the quasi-priesthood conveyed in the consecrating qualities of her coronation, and he regards things temporal as being by their nature subject to those which are spiritual. Mr. Slope's ideas of sacerdotal rule are of quite a different class. He cares nothing, one way or the other, for the Queen's supremacy; these to his ears are empty words, meaning nothing. Forms he regards but little, and such titular expressions as supremacy, consecration, ordination, and the like convey of themselves no significance to him. Let him be supreme who can. The temporal king, judge, or gaoler can work but on the body. The spiritual master, if he have the necessary gifts and can duly use them, has a wider field of empire. He works upon the soul. If he can make himself be believed, he can be all powerful over those who listen. If he be careful to meddle with none who are too strong in intellect, or too weak in flesh, he may indeed be supreme. And such was the ambition of Mr. Slope.\n\nDr. Grantly interfered very little with the worldly doings of those who were in any way subject to him. I do not mean to say that he omitted to notice misconduct among his clergy, immorality in his parish, or omissions in his family, but he was not anxious to do so where the necessity could be avoided. He was not troubled with a propensity to be curious, and as long as those around him were tainted with no heretical leaning towards dissent, as long as they fully and freely admitted the efficacy of Mother Church, he was willing that that mother should be merciful and affectionate, prone to indulgence, and unwilling to chastise. He himself enjoyed the good things of this world and liked to let it be known that he did so. He cordially despised any brother rector who thought harm of dinner-parties, or dreaded the dangers of a moderate claret-jug; consequently, dinner-parties and claret-jugs were common in the diocese. He liked to give laws and to be obeyed in them implicitly, but he endeavoured that his ordinances should be within the compass of the man and not unpalatable to the gentleman. He had ruled among his clerical neighbours now for sundry years, and as he had maintained his power without becoming unpopular, it may be presumed that he had exercised some wisdom.\n\nOf Mr. Slope's conduct much cannot be said, as his grand career is yet to commence, but it may be premised that his tastes will be very different from those of the archdeacon. He conceives it to be his duty to know all the private doings and desires of the flock entrusted to his care. From the poorer classes he exacts an unconditional obedience to set rules of conduct, and if disobeyed he has recourse, like his great ancestor, to the fulminations of an Ernulfus: \"Thou shalt be damned in thy going in and in thy coming out--in thy eating and thy drinking,\" &c. &c. &c. With the rich, experience has already taught him that a different line of action is necessary. Men in the upper walks of life do not mind being cursed, and the women, presuming that it be done in delicate phrase, rather like it. But he has not, therefore, given up so important a portion of believing Christians. With the men, indeed, he is generally at variance; they are hardened sinners, on whom the voice of the priestly charmer too often falls in vain; but with the ladies, old and young, firm and frail, devout and dissipated, he is, as he conceives, all powerful. He can reprove faults with so much flattery and utter censure in so caressing a manner that the female heart, if it glow with a spark of Low Church susceptibility, cannot withstand him. In many houses he is thus an admired guest: the husbands, for their wives' sake, are fain to admit him; and when once admitted it is not easy to shake him off. He has, however, a pawing, greasy way with him, which does not endear him to those who do not value him for their souls' sake, and he is not a man to make himself at once popular in a large circle such as is now likely to surround him at Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER V. A Morning Visit\n\nIt was known that Dr. Proudie would immediately have to reappoint to the wardenship of the hospital under the act of Parliament to which allusion has been made; no one imagined that any choice was left to him--no one for a moment thought that he could appoint any other than Mr. Harding. Mr. Harding himself, when he heard how the matter had been settled, without troubling himself much on the subject, considered it as certain that he would go back to his pleasant house and garden. And though there would be much that was melancholy, nay, almost heartrending, in such a return, he still was glad that it was to be so. His daughter might probably be persuaded to return there with him. She had, indeed, all but promised to do so, though she still entertained an idea that that greatest of mortals, that important atom of humanity, that little god upon earth, Johnny Bold her baby, ought to have a house of his own over his head.\n\nSuch being the state of Mr. Harding's mind in the matter, he did not feel any peculiar personal interest in the appointment of Dr. Proudie to the bishopric. He, as well as others at Barchester, regretted that a man should be sent among them who, they were aware, was not of their way of thinking; but Mr. Harding himself was not a bigoted man on points of church doctrine, and he was quite prepared to welcome Dr. Proudie to Barchester in a graceful and becoming manner. He had nothing to seek and nothing to fear; he felt that it behoved him to be on good terms with his bishop, and he did not anticipate any obstacle that would prevent it.\n\nIn such a frame of mind he proceeded to pay his respects at the palace the second day after the arrival of the bishop and his chaplain. But he did not go alone. Dr. Grantly proposed to accompany him, and Mr. Harding was not sorry to have a companion, who would remove from his shoulders the burden of the conversation in such an interview. In the affair of the consecration Dr. Grantly had been introduced to the bishop, and Mr. Harding had also been there. He had, however, kept himself in the background, and he was now to be presented to the great man for the first time.\n\nThe archdeacon's feelings were of a much stronger nature. He was not exactly the man to overlook his own slighted claims, or to forgive the preference shown to another. Dr. Proudie was playing Venus to his Juno, and he was prepared to wage an internecine war against the owner of the wished-for apple, and all his satellites, private chaplains, and others.\n\nNevertheless, it behoved him also to conduct himself towards the intruder as an old archdeacon should conduct himself to an incoming bishop; and though he was well aware of all Dr. Proudie's abominable opinions as regarded dissenters, church reform, the hebdomadal council, and such like; though he disliked the man, and hated the doctrines, still he was prepared to show respect to the station of the bishop. So he and Mr. Harding called together at the palace.\n\nHis lordship was at home, and the two visitors were shown through the accustomed hall into the well-known room where the good old bishop used to sit. The furniture had been bought at a valuation, and every chair and table, every bookshelf against the wall, and every square in the carpet was as well known to each of them as their own bedrooms. Nevertheless they at once felt that they were strangers there. The furniture was for the most part the same, yet the place had been metamorphosed. A new sofa had been introduced, a horrid chintz affair, most unprelatical and almost irreligious; such a sofa as never yet stood in the study of any decent High Church clergyman of the Church of England. The old curtains had also given way. They had, to be sure, become dingy, and that which had been originally a rich and goodly ruby had degenerated into a reddish brown. Mr. Harding, however, thought the old reddish-brown much preferable to the gaudy buff-coloured trumpery moreen which Mrs. Proudie had deemed good enough for her husband's own room in the provincial city of Barchester.\n\nOur friends found Dr. Proudie sitting on the old bishop's chair, looking very nice in his new apron; they found, too, Mr. Slope standing on the hearth-rug, persuasive and eager, just as the archdeacon used to stand; but on the sofa they also found Mrs. Proudie, an innovation for which a precedent might in vain be sought in all the annals of the Barchester bishopric!\n\nThere she was, however, and they could only make the best of her. The introductions were gone through in much form. The archdeacon shook hands with the bishop, and named Mr. Harding, who received such an amount of greeting as was due from a bishop to a precentor. His lordship then presented them to his lady wife; the archdeacon first, with archidiaconal honours, and then the precentor with diminished parade. After this Mr. Slope presented himself. The bishop, it is true, did mention his name, and so did Mrs. Proudie too, in a louder tone, but Mr. Slope took upon himself the chief burden of his own introduction. He had great pleasure in making himself acquainted with Dr. Grantly; he had heard much of the archdeacon's good works in that part of the diocese in which his duties as archdeacon had been exercised (thus purposely ignoring the archdeacon's hitherto unlimited dominion over the diocese at large). He was aware that his lordship depended greatly on the assistance which Dr. Grantly would be able to give him in that portion of his diocese. He then thrust out his hand and, grasping that of his new foe, bedewed it unmercifully. Dr. Grantly in return bowed, looked stiff, contracted his eyebrows, and wiped his hand with his pocket-handkerchief. Nothing abashed, Mr. Slope then noticed the precentor and descended to the grade of the lower clergy. He gave him a squeeze of the hand, damp indeed, but affectionate, and was very glad to make the acquaintance of Mr.--oh yes, Mr. Harding; he had not exactly caught the name. \"Precentor in the cathedral,\" surmised Mr. Slope. Mr. Harding confessed that such was the humble sphere of his work. \"Some parish duty as well,\" suggested Mr. Slope. Mr. Harding acknowledged the diminutive incumbency of St. Cuthbert's. Mr. Slope then left him alone, having condescended sufficiently, and joined the conversation among the higher powers.\n\nThere were four persons there, each of whom considered himself the most important personage in the diocese--himself, indeed, or herself, as Mrs. Proudie was one of them--and with such a difference of opinion it was not probable that they would get on pleasantly together. The bishop himself actually wore the visible apron, and trusted mainly to that--to that and his title, both being facts which could not be overlooked. The archdeacon knew his subject and really understood the business of bishoping, which the others did not, and this was his strong ground. Mrs. Proudie had her sex to back her, and her habit of command, and was nothing daunted by the high tone of Dr. Grantly's face and figure. Mr. Slope had only himself and his own courage and tact to depend on, but he nevertheless was perfectly self-assured, and did not doubt but that he should soon get the better of weak men who trusted so much to externals, as both bishop and archdeacon appeared to do.\n\n\"Do you reside in Barchester, Dr. Grantly?\" asked the lady with her sweetest smile.\n\nDr. Grantly explained that he lived in his own parish of Plumstead Episcopi, a few miles out of the city. Whereupon the lady hoped that the distance was not too great for country visiting, as she would be so glad to make the acquaintance of Mrs. Grantly. She would take the earliest opportunity, after the arrival of her horses at Barchester; their horses were at present in London; their horses were not immediately coming down, as the bishop would be obliged, in a few days, to return to town. Dr. Grantly was no doubt aware that the bishop was at present much called upon by the \"University Improvement Committee:\" indeed, the committee could not well proceed without him, as their final report had now to be drawn up. The bishop had also to prepare a scheme for the \"Manufacturing Towns Morning and Evening Sunday School Society,\" of which he was a patron, or president, or director, and therefore the horses would not come down to Barchester at present; but whenever the horses did come down, she would take the earliest opportunity of calling at Plumstead Episcopi, providing the distance was not too great for country visiting.\n\nThe archdeacon made his fifth bow--he had made one at each mention of the horses--and promised that Mrs. Grantly would do herself the honour of calling at the palace on an early day. Mrs. Proudie declared that she would be delighted: she hadn't liked to ask, not being quite sure whether Mrs. Grantly had horses; besides, the distance might have been, &c. &c.\n\nDr. Grantly again bowed but said nothing. He could have bought every individual possession of the whole family of the Proudies and have restored them as a gift, without much feeling the loss; and had kept a separate pair of horses for the exclusive use of his wife since the day of his marriage, whereas Mrs. Proudie had been hitherto jobbed about the streets of London at so much a month, during the season, and at other times had managed to walk, or hire a smart fly from the livery stables.\n\n\"Are the arrangements with reference to the Sabbath-day schools generally pretty good in your archdeaconry?\" asked Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Sabbath-day schools!\" repeated the archdeacon with an affectation of surprise. \"Upon my word, I can't tell; it depends mainly on the parson's wife and daughters. There is none at Plumstead.\"\n\nThis was almost a fib on the part of the archdeacon, for Mrs. Grantly has a very nice school. To be sure it is not a Sunday-school exclusively, and is not so designated, but that exemplary lady always attends there for an hour before church, and hears the children say their catechism, and sees that they are clean and tidy for church, with their hands washed and their shoes tied; and Grisel and Florinda, her daughters, carry thither a basket of large buns, baked on the Saturday afternoon, and distribute them to all the children not especially under disgrace, which buns are carried home after church with considerable content, and eaten hot at tea, being then split and toasted. The children of Plumstead would indeed open their eyes if they heard their venerated pastor declare that there was no Sunday-school in his parish.\n\nMr. Slope merely opened his wide eyes wider and slightly shrugged his shoulders. He was not, however, prepared to give up his darling project.\n\n\"I fear there is a great deal of Sabbath travelling here,\" said he. \"On looking at the 'Bradshaw,' I see that there are three trains in and three out every Sabbath. Could nothing be done to induce the company to withdraw them? Don't you think, Dr. Grantly, that a little energy might diminish the evil?\"\n\n\"Not being a director, I really can't say. But if you can withdraw the passengers, the company I dare say will withdraw the trains,\" said the doctor. \"It's merely a question of dividends.\"\n\n\"But surely, Dr. Grantly,\" said the lady; \"surely we should look at it differently. You and I, for instance, in our position: surely we should do all that we can to control so grievous a sin. Don't you think so, Mr. Harding?\" and she turned to the precentor, who was sitting mute and unhappy.\n\nMr. Harding thought that all porters and stokers, guards, brakesmen, and pointsmen ought to have an opportunity of going to church, and he hoped that they all had.\n\n\"But surely, surely,\" continued Mrs. Proudie, \"surely that is not enough. Surely that will not secure such an observance of the Sabbath as we are taught to conceive is not only expedient but indispensable; surely--\"\n\nCome what come might, Dr. Grantly was not to be forced into a dissertation on a point of doctrine with Mrs. Proudie, nor yet with Mr. Slope, so without much ceremony he turned his back upon the sofa and began to hope that Dr. Proudie had found that the palace repairs had been such as to meet his wishes.\n\n\"Yes, yes,\" said his lordship; upon the whole he thought so--upon the whole, he didn't know that there was much ground for complaint; the architect, perhaps, might have--but his double, Mr. Slope, who had sidled over to the bishop's chair, would not allow his lordship to finish his ambiguous speech.\n\n\"There is one point I would like to mention, Mr. Archdeacon. His lordship asked me to step through the premises, and I see that the stalls in the second stable are not perfect.\"\n\n\"Why--there's standing there for a dozen horses,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Perhaps so,\" said the other; \"indeed, I've no doubt of it; but visitors, you know, often require so much accommodation. There are so many of the bishop's relatives who always bring their own horses.\"\n\nDr. Grantly promised that due provision for the relatives' horses should be made, as far at least as the extent of the original stable building would allow. He would himself communicate with the architect.\n\n\"And the coach-house, Dr. Grantly,\" continued Mr. Slope; \"there is really hardly room for a second carriage in the large coach-house, and the smaller one, of course, holds only one.\"\n\n\"And the gas,\" chimed in the lady; \"there is no gas through the house, none whatever, but in the kitchen and passages. Surely the palace should have been fitted through with pipes for gas, and hot water too. There is no hot water laid on anywhere above the ground-floor; surely there should be the means of getting hot water in the bedrooms without having it brought in jugs from the kitchen.\"\n\nThe bishop had a decided opinion that there should be pipes for hot water. Hot water was very essential for the comfort of the palace. It was, indeed, a requisite in any decent gentleman's house.\n\nMr. Slope had remarked that the coping on the garden wall was in many places imperfect.\n\nMrs. Proudie had discovered a large hole, evidently the work of rats, in the servants' hall.\n\nThe bishop expressed an utter detestation of rats. There was nothing, he believed, in this world that he so much hated as a rat.\n\nMr. Slope had, moreover, observed that the locks of the outhouses were very imperfect: he might specify the coal-cellar and the woodhouse.\n\nMrs. Proudie had also seen that those on the doors of the servants' bedrooms were in an-equally bad condition; indeed, the locks all through the house were old-fashioned and unserviceable.\n\nThe bishop thought that a great deal depended on a good lock and quite as much on the key. He had observed that the fault very often lay with the key, especially if the wards were in any way twisted.\n\nMr. Slope was going on with his catalogue of grievances, when he was somewhat loudly interrupted by the archdeacon, who succeeded in explaining that the diocesan architect, or rather his foreman, was the person to be addressed on such subjects, and that he, Dr. Grantly, had inquired as to the comfort of the palace merely as a point of compliment. He was sorry, however, that so many things had been found amiss: and then he rose from his chair to escape.\n\nMrs. Proudie, though she had contrived to lend her assistance in recapitulating the palatial dilapidations, had not on that account given up her hold of Mr. Harding, nor ceased from her cross-examinations as to the iniquity of Sabbatical amusements. Over and over again had she thrown out her \"Surely, surely,\" at Mr. Harding's devoted head, and ill had that gentleman been able to parry the attack.\n\nHe had never before found himself subjected to such a nuisance. Ladies hitherto, when they had consulted him on religious subjects, had listened to what he might choose to say with some deference, and had differed, if they differed, in silence. But Mrs. Proudie interrogated him and then lectured. \"Neither thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, thy manservant, nor thy maidservant,\" said she impressively, and more than once, as though Mr. Harding had forgotten the words. She shook her finger at him as she quoted the favourite law, as though menacing him with punishment, and then called upon him categorically to state whether he did not think that travelling on the Sabbath was an abomination and a desecration.\n\nMr. Harding had never been so hard pressed in his life. He felt that he ought to rebuke the lady for presuming so to talk to a gentleman and a clergyman many years her senior, but he recoiled from the idea of scolding the bishop's wife, in the bishop's presence, on his first visit to the palace; moreover, to tell the truth, he was somewhat afraid of her. She, seeing him sit silent and absorbed, by no means refrained from the attack.\n\n\"I hope, Mr. Harding,\" said she, shaking her head slowly and solemnly, \"I hope you will not leave me to think that you approve of Sabbath travelling,\" and she looked a look of unutterable meaning into his eyes.\n\nThere was no standing this, for Mr. Slope was now looking at him, and so was the bishop, and so was the archdeacon, who had completed his adieux on that side of the room. Mr. Harding therefore got up also and, putting out his hand to Mrs. Proudie, said: \"If you will come to St. Cuthbert's some Sunday, I will preach you a sermon on that subject.\"\n\nAnd so the archdeacon and the precentor took their departure, bowing low to the lady, shaking hands with the lord, and escaping from Mr. Slope in the best manner each could. Mr. Harding was again maltreated, but Dr. Grantly swore deeply in the bottom of his heart, that no earthly consideration should ever again induce him to touch the paw of that impure and filthy animal.\n\nAnd now, had I the pen of a mighty poet, would I sing in epic verse the noble wrath of the archdeacon. The palace steps descend to a broad gravel sweep, from whence a small gate opens out into the street, very near the covered gateway leading into the close. The road from the palace door turns to the left, through the spacious gardens, and terminates on the London road, half a mile from the cathedral.\n\nTill they had both passed this small gate and entered the close, neither of them spoke a word, but the precentor clearly saw from his companion's face that a tornado was to be expected, nor was he himself inclined to stop it. Though by nature far less irritable than the archdeacon, even he was angry: he even--that mild and courteous man--was inclined to express himself in anything but courteous terms.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. War\n\n\"Good heavens!\" exclaimed the archdeacon, as he placed his foot on the gravel walk of the close, and raising his hat with one hand, passed the other somewhat violently over his now grizzled locks; smoke issued forth from the uplifted beaver as it were a cloud of wrath, and the safety valve of his anger opened, and emitted a visible steam, preventing positive explosion and probable apoplexy. \"Good heavens!\"--and the archdeacon looked up to the gray pinnacles of the cathedral tower, making a mute appeal to that still living witness which had looked down on the doings of so many bishops of Barchester.\n\n\"I don't think I shall ever like that Mr. Slope,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Like him!\" roared the archdeacon, standing still for a moment to give more force to his voice; \"like him!\" All the ravens of the close cawed their assent. The old bells of the tower, in chiming the hour, echoed the words, and the swallows flying out from their nests mutely expressed a similar opinion. Like Mr. Slope! Why no, it was not very probable that any Barchester-bred living thing should like Mr. Slope!\n\n\"Nor Mrs. Proudie either,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\nThe archdeacon hereupon forgot himself. I will not follow his example, nor shock my readers by transcribing the term in which he expressed his feeling as to the lady who had been named. The ravens and the last lingering notes of the clock bells were less scrupulous and repeated in correspondent echoes the very improper exclamation. The archdeacon again raised his hat, and another salutary escape of steam was effected.\n\nThere was a pause, during which the precentor tried to realize the fact that the wife of a Bishop of Barchester had been thus designated, in the close of the cathedral, by the lips of its own archdeacon; but he could not do it.\n\n\"The bishop seems to be a quiet man enough,\" suggested Mr. Harding, having acknowledged to himself his own failure.\n\n\"Idiot!\" exclaimed the doctor, who for the nonce was not capable of more than such spasmodic attempts at utterance.\n\n\"Well, he did not seem very bright,\" said Mr. Harding, \"and yet he has always had the reputation of a clever man. I suppose he's cautious and not inclined to express himself very freely.\"\n\nThe new Bishop of Barchester was already so contemptible a creature in Dr. Grantly's eyes that he could not condescend to discuss his character. He was a puppet to be played by others; a mere wax doll, done up in an apron and a shovel hat, to be stuck on a throne or elsewhere, and pulled about by wires as others chose. Dr. Grantly did not choose to let himself down low enough to talk about Dr. Proudie, but he saw that he would have to talk about the other members of his household, the coadjutor bishops, who had brought his lordship down, as it were, in a box, and were about to handle the wires as they willed. This in itself was a terrible vexation to the archdeacon. Could he have ignored the chaplain and have fought the bishop, there would have been, at any rate, nothing degrading in such a contest. Let the Queen make whom she would Bishop of Barchester; a man, or even an ape, when once a bishop, would be a respectable adversary, if he would but fight, himself. But what was such a person as Dr. Grantly to do when such another person as Mr. Slope was put forward as his antagonist?\n\nIf he, our archdeacon, refused the combat, Mr. Slope would walk triumphant over the field, and have the diocese of Barchester under his heel.\n\nIf, on the other hand, the archdeacon accepted as his enemy the man whom the new puppet bishop put before him as such, he would have to talk about Mr. Slope, and write about Mr. Slope, and in all matters treat with Mr. Slope, as a being standing, in some degree, on ground similar to his own. He would have to meet Mr. Slope, to--Bah! the idea was sickening. He could not bring himself to have to do with Mr. Slope.\n\n\"He is the most thoroughly bestial creature that ever I set my eyes upon,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Who--the bishop?\" asked the other innocently.\n\n\"Bishop! no--I'm not talking about the bishop. How on earth such a creature got ordained!--they'll ordain anybody now, I know, but he's been in the church these ten years, and they used to be a little careful ten years ago.\"\n\n\"Oh! You mean Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Did you ever see any animal less like a gentleman?\" asked Dr. Grantly.\n\n\"I can't say I felt myself much disposed to like him.\"\n\n\"Like him!\" again shouted the doctor, and the assenting ravens again cawed an echo; \"of course, you don't like him: it's not a question of liking. But what are we to do with him?\"\n\n\"Do with him?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Yes--what are we to do with him? How are we to treat him? There he is, and there he'll stay. He has put his foot in that palace, and he'll never take it out again till he's driven. How are we to get rid of him?\"\n\n\"I don't suppose he can do us much harm.\"\n\n\"Not do harm!--Well, I think you'll find yourself of a different opinion before a month is gone. What would you say now, if he got himself put into the hospital? Would that be harm?\"\n\nMr. Harding mused awhile and then said he didn't think the new bishop would put Mr. Slope into the hospital.\n\n\"If he doesn't put him there, he'll put him somewhere else where he'll be as bad. I tell you that that man, to all intents and purposes, will be Bishop of Barchester!\" And again Dr. Grantly raised his hat and rubbed his hand thoughtfully and sadly over his head.\n\n\"Impudent scoundrel!\" he continued after a while. \"To dare to cross-examine me about the Sunday-schools in the diocese, and Sunday travelling too: I never in my life met his equal for sheer impudence. Why, he must have thought we were two candidates for ordination!\"\n\n\"I declare I thought Mrs. Proudie was the worst of the two,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"When a woman is impertinent, one must only put up with it, and keep out of her way in future, but I am not inclined to put up with Mr. Slope. 'Sabbath travelling!'\" and the doctor attempted to imitate the peculiar drawl of the man he so much disliked: \"'Sabbath travelling!' Those are the sort of men who will ruin the Church of England and make the profession of a clergyman disreputable. It is not the dissenters or the papists that we should fear, but the set of canting, low-bred hypocrites who are wriggling their way in among us; men who have no fixed principle, no standard ideas of religion or doctrine, but who take up some popular cry, as this fellow has done about 'Sabbath travelling.'\"\n\nDr. Grantly did not again repeat the question aloud, but he did so constantly to himself: What were they to do with Mr. Slope? How was he openly, before the world, to show that he utterly disapproved of and abhorred such a man?\n\nHitherto Barchester had escaped the taint of any extreme rigour of church doctrine. The clergymen of the city and neighbourhood, though very well inclined to promote High Church principles, privileges, and prerogatives, had never committed themselves to tendencies which are somewhat too loosely called Puseyite practices. They all preached in their black gowns, as their fathers had done before them; they wore ordinary black cloth waistcoats; they had no candles on their altars, either lighted or unlighted; they made no private genuflexions, and were contented to confine themselves to such ceremonial observances as had been in vogue for the last hundred years. The services were decently and demurely read in their parish churches, chanting was confined to the cathedral, and the science of intoning was unknown. One young man who had come direct from Oxford as a curate to Plumstead had, after the lapse of two or three Sundays, made a faint attempt, much to the bewilderment of the poorer part of the congregation. Dr. Grantly had not been present on the occasion, but Mrs. Grantly, who had her own opinion on the subject, immediately after the service expressed a hope that the young gentleman had not been taken ill, and offered to send him all kinds of condiments supposed to be good for a sore throat. After that there had been no more intoning at Plumstead Episcopi.\n\nBut now the archdeacon began to meditate on some strong measures of absolute opposition. Dr. Proudie and his crew were of the lowest possible order of Church of England clergymen, and therefore it behoved him, Dr. Grantly, to be of the very highest. Dr. Proudie would abolish all forms and ceremonies, and therefore Dr. Grantly felt the sudden necessity of multiplying them. Dr. Proudie would consent to deprive the church of all collective authority and rule, and therefore Dr. Grantly would stand up for the full power of convocation and the renewal of all its ancient privileges.\n\nIt was true that he could not himself intone the service, but he could procure the co-operation of any number of gentlemanlike curates well trained in the mystery of doing so. He would not willingly alter his own fashion of dress, but he could people Barchester with young clergymen dressed in the longest frocks and in the highest-breasted silk waistcoats. He certainly was not prepared to cross himself, or to advocate the real presence, but without going this length there were various observances, by adopting which he could plainly show his antipathy to such men as Dr. Proudie and Mr. Slope.\n\nAll these things passed through his mind as he paced up and down the close with Mr. Harding. War, war, internecine war was in his heart. He felt that, as regarded himself and Mr. Slope, one of the two must be annihilated as far as the city of Barchester was concerned, and he did not intend to give way until there was not left to him an inch of ground on which he could stand. He still flattered himself that he could make Barchester too hot to hold Mr. Slope, and he had no weakness of spirit to prevent his bringing about such a consummation if it were in his power.\n\n\"I suppose Susan must call at the palace,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Yes, she shall call there, but it shall be once and once only. I dare say 'the horses' won't find it convenient to come out to Plumstead very soon, and when that once is done the matter may drop.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose Eleanor need call. I don't think Eleanor would get on at all well with Mrs. Proudie.\"\n\n\"Not the least necessity in life,\" replied the archdeacon, not without the reflexion that a ceremony which was necessary for his wife might not be at all binding on the widow of John Bold. \"Not the slightest reason on earth why she should do so, if she doesn't like it. For myself, I don't think that any decent young woman should be subjected to the nuisance of being in the same room with that man.\"\n\nAnd so the two clergymen parted, Mr. Harding going to his daughter's house, and the archdeacon seeking the seclusion of his brougham.\n\nThe new inhabitants of the palace did not express any higher opinion of their visitors than their visitors had expressed of them. Though they did not use quite such strong language as Dr. Grantly had done, they felt as much personal aversion, and were quite as well aware as he was that there would be a battle to be fought, and that there was hardly room for Proudieism in Barchester as long as Grantlyism was predominant.\n\nIndeed, it may be doubted whether Mr. Slope had not already within his breast a better prepared system of strategy, a more accurately defined line of hostile conduct than the archdeacon. Dr. Grantly was going to fight because he found that he hated the man. Mr. Slope had predetermined to hate the man because he foresaw the necessity of fighting him. When he had first reviewed the carte du pays previous to his entry into Barchester, the idea had occurred to him of conciliating the archdeacon, of cajoling and flattering him into submission, and of obtaining the upper hand by cunning instead of courage. A little inquiry, however, sufficed to convince him that all his cunning would fail to win over such a man as Dr. Grantly to such a mode of action as that to be adopted by Mr. Slope, and then he determined to fall back upon his courage. He at once saw that open battle against Dr. Grantly and all Dr. Grantly's adherents was a necessity of his position, and he deliberately planned the most expedient methods of giving offence.\n\nSoon after his arrival the bishop had intimated to the dean that, with the permission of the canon then in residence, his chaplain would preach in the cathedral on the next Sunday. The canon in residence happened to be the Hon. and Rev. Dr. Vesey Stanhope, who at this time was very busy on the shores of the Lake of Como, adding to that unique collection of butterflies for which he is so famous. Or rather, he would have been in residence but for the butterflies and other such summer-day considerations; and the vicar-choral, who was to take his place in the pulpit, by no means objected to having his work done for him by Mr. Slope.\n\nMr. Slope accordingly preached, and if a preacher can have satisfaction in being listened to, Mr. Slope ought to have been gratified. I have reason to think that he was gratified, and that he left the pulpit with the conviction that he had done what he intended to do when he entered it.\n\nOn this occasion the new bishop took his seat for the first time in the throne alloted to him. New scarlet cushions and drapery had been prepared, with new gilt binding and new fringe. The old carved oak-wood of the throne, ascending with its numerous grotesque pinnacles half-way up to the roof of the choir, had been washed, and dusted, and rubbed, and it all looked very smart. Ah! how often sitting there, in happy early days, on those lowly benches in front of the altar, have I whiled away the tedium of a sermon in considering how best I might thread my way up amidst those wooden towers and climb safely to the topmost pinnacle!\n\nAll Barchester went to hear Mr. Slope; either for that or to gaze at the new bishop. All the best bonnets of the city were there, and moreover all the best glossy clerical hats. Not a stall but had its fitting occupant, for though some of the prebendaries might be away in Italy or elsewhere, their places were filled by brethren who flocked into Barchester on the occasion. The dean was there, a heavy old man, now too old, indeed, to attend frequently in his place, and so was the archdeacon. So also were the chancellor, the treasurer, the precentor, sundry canons and minor canons, and every lay member of the choir, prepared to sing the new bishop in with due melody and harmonious expression of sacred welcome.\n\nThe service was certainly very well performed. Such was always the case at Barchester, as the musical education of the choir had been good, and the voices had been carefully selected. The psalms were beautifully chanted; the Te Deum was magnificently sung; and the litany was given in a manner which is still to be found at Barchester, but, if my taste be correct, is to be found nowhere else. The litany in Barchester cathedral has long been the special task to which Mr. Harding's skill and voice have been devoted. Crowded audiences generally make good performers, and though Mr. Harding was not aware of any extraordinary exertion on his part, yet probably he rather exceeded his usual mark. Others were doing their best, and it was natural that he should emulate his brethren. So the service went on, and at last Mr. Slope got into the pulpit.\n\nHe chose for his text a verse from the precepts addressed by St. Paul to Timothy, as to the conduct necessary in a spiritual pastor and guide, and it was immediately evident that the good clergy of Barchester were to have a lesson.\n\n\"Study to show thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth.\" These were the words of his text, and with such a subject in such a place, it may be supposed that such a preacher would be listened to by such an audience. He was listened to with breathless attention and not without considerable surprise. Whatever opinion of Mr. Slope might have been held in Barchester before he commenced his discourse, none of his hearers, when it was over, could mistake him either for a fool or a coward.\n\nIt would not be becoming were I to travesty a sermon, or even to repeat the language of it in the pages of a novel. In endeavouring to depict the characters of the persons of whom I write, I am to a certain extent forced to speak of sacred things. I trust, however, that I shall not be thought to scoff at the pulpit, though some may imagine that I do not feel all the reverence that is due to the cloth. I may question the infallibility of the teachers, but I hope that I shall not therefore be accused of doubt as to the thing to be taught.\n\nMr. Slope, in commencing his sermon, showed no slight tact in his ambiguous manner of hinting that, humble as he was himself, he stood there as the mouth-piece of the illustrious divine who sat opposite to him; and having premised so much, he gave forth a very accurate definition of the conduct which that prelate would rejoice to see in the clergymen now brought under his jurisdiction. It is only necessary to say that the peculiar points insisted upon were exactly those which were most distasteful to the clergy of the diocese, and most averse to their practice and opinions, and that all those peculiar habits and privileges which have always been dear to High Church priests, to that party which is now scandalously called the \"high and dry church,\" were ridiculed, abused, and anathematized. Now, the clergymen of the diocese of Barchester are all of the high and dry church.\n\nHaving thus, according to his own opinion, explained how a clergyman should show himself approved unto God, as a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, he went on to explain how the word of truth should be divided; and here he took a rather narrow view of the question and fetched his arguments from afar. His object was to express his abomination of all ceremonious modes of utterance, to cry down any religious feeling which might be excited, not by the sense, but by the sound of words, and in fact to insult cathedral practices. Had St. Paul spoken of rightly pronouncing, instead of rightly dividing the word of truth, this part of his sermon would have been more to the purpose, but the preacher's immediate object was to preach Mr. Slope's doctrine, and not St. Paul's, and he contrived to give the necessary twist to the text with some skill.\n\nHe could not exactly say, preaching from a cathedral pulpit, that chanting should be abandoned in cathedral services. By such an assertion he would have overshot his mark and rendered himself absurd, to the delight of his hearers. He could, however, and did, allude with heavy denunciations to the practice of intoning in parish churches, although the practice was all but unknown in the diocese; and from thence he came round to the undue preponderance which, he asserted, music had over meaning in the beautiful service which they had just heard. He was aware, he said, that the practices of our ancestors could not be abandoned at a moment's notice; the feelings of the aged would be outraged, and the minds of respectable men would be shocked. There were many, he was aware, of not sufficient calibre of thought to perceive, of not sufficient education to know, that a mode of service which was effective when outward ceremonies were of more moment than inward feelings, had become all but barbarous at a time when inward conviction was everything, when each word of the minister's lips should fall intelligibly into the listener's heart. Formerly the religion of the multitude had been an affair of the imagination: now, in these latter days, it had become necessary that a Christian should have a reason for his faith--should not only believe, but digest--not only hear, but understand. The words of our morning service, how beautiful, how apposite, how intelligible they were, when read with simple and distinct decorum! But how much of the meaning of the words was lost when they were produced with all the meretricious charms of melody! &c. &c.\n\nHere was a sermon to be preached before Mr. Archdeacon Grantly, Mr. Precentor Harding, and the rest of them! Before a whole dean and chapter assembled in their own cathedral! Before men who had grown old in the exercise of their peculiar services, with a full conviction of their excellence for all intended purposes! This too from such a man, a clerical parvenu, a man without a cure, a mere chaplain, an intruder among them; a fellow raked up, so said Dr. Grantly, from the gutters of Marylebone! They had to sit through it! None of them, not even Dr. Grantly, could close his ears, nor leave the house of God during the hours of service. They were under an obligation of listening, and that too without any immediate power of reply.\n\nThere is, perhaps, no greater hardship at present inflicted on mankind in civilized and free countries than the necessity of listening to sermons. No one but a preaching clergyman has, in these realms, the power of compelling an audience to sit silent and be tormented. No one but a preaching clergyman can revel in platitudes, truisms, and untruisms, and yet receive, as his undisputed privilege, the same respectful demeanour as though words of impassioned eloquence, or persuasive logic, fell from his lips. Let a professor of law or physics find his place in a lecture-room, and there pour forth jejune words and useless empty phrases, and he will pour them forth to empty benches. Let a barrister attempt to talk without talking well, and he will talk but seldom. A judge's charge need be listened to perforce by none but the jury, prisoner, and gaoler. A member of Parliament can be coughed down or counted out. Town-councillors can be tabooed. But no one can rid himself of the preaching clergyman. He is the bore of the age, the old man whom we Sindbads cannot shake off, the nightmare that disturbs our Sunday's rest, the incubus that overloads our religion and makes God's service distasteful. We are not forced into church! No: but we desire more than that. We desire not to be forced to stay away. We desire, nay, we are resolute, to enjoy the comfort of public worship, but we desire also that we may do so without an amount of tedium which ordinary human nature cannot endure with patience; that we may be able to leave the house of God without that anxious longing for escape which is the common consequence of common sermons.\n\nWith what complacency will a young parson deduce false conclusions from misunderstood texts, and then threaten us with all the penalties of Hades if we neglect to comply with the injunctions he has given us! Yes, my too self-confident juvenile friend, I do believe in those mysteries which are so common in your mouth; I do believe in the unadulterated word which you hold there in your hand; but you must pardon me if, in some things, I doubt your interpretation. The Bible is good, the prayer-book is good, nay, you yourself would be acceptable, if you would read to me some portion of those time-honoured discourses which our great divines have elaborated in the full maturity of their powers. But you must excuse me, my insufficient young lecturer, if I yawn over your imperfect sentences, your repeated phrases, your false pathos, your drawlings and denouncings, your humming and hawing, your oh-ing and ah-ing, your black gloves and your white handkerchief. To me, it all means nothing; and hours are too precious to be so wasted--if one could only avoid it.\n\nAnd here I must make a protest against the pretence, so often put forward by the working clergy, that they are overburdened by the multitude of sermons to be preached. We are all too fond of our own voices, and a preacher is encouraged in the vanity of making his heard by the privilege of a compelled audience. His sermon is the pleasant morsel of his life, his delicious moment of self-exaltation. \"I have preached nine sermons this week,\" said a young friend to me the other day, with hand languidly raised to his brow, the picture of an overburdened martyr. \"Nine this week, seven last week, four the week before. I have preached twenty-three sermons this month. It is really too much.\"\n\n\"Too much, indeed,\" said I, shuddering; \"too much for the strength of any one.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he answered meekly, \"indeed it is; I am beginning to feel it painfully.\"\n\n\"Would,\" said I, \"you could feel it--would that you could be made to feel it.\" But he never guessed that my heart was wrung for the poor listeners.\n\nThere was, at any rate, no tedium felt in listening to Mr. Slope on the occasion in question. His subject came too home to his audience to be dull, and, to tell the truth, Mr. Slope had the gift of using words forcibly. He was heard through his thirty minutes of eloquence with mute attention and open ears, but with angry eyes, which glared round from one enraged parson to another, with wide-spread nostrils from which already burst forth fumes of indignation, and with many shufflings of the feet and uneasy motions of the body, which betokened minds disturbed, and hearts not at peace with all the world.\n\nAt last the bishop, who, of all the congregation, had been most surprised, and whose hair almost stood on end with terror, gave the blessing in a manner not at all equal to that in which he had long been practising it in his own study, and the congregation was free to go their way.\n\n# CHAPTER VII. The Dean and Chapter Take Counsel\n\nAll Barchester was in a tumult. Dr. Grantly could hardly get himself out of the cathedral porch before he exploded in his wrath. The old dean betook himself silently to his deanery, afraid to speak, and there sat, half-stupefied, pondering many things in vain. Mr. Harding crept forth solitary and unhappy; and, slowly passing beneath the elms of the close, could scarcely bring himself to believe that the words which he had heard had proceeded from the pulpit of Barchester cathedral. Was he again to be disturbed? Was his whole life to be shown up as a useless sham a second time? Would he have to abdicate his precentorship, as he had his wardenship, and to give up chanting, as he had given up his twelve old bedesmen? And what if he did! Some other Jupiter, some other Mr. Slope, would come and turn him out of St. Cuthbert's. Surely he could not have been wrong all his life in chanting the litany as he had done! He began, however, to have his doubts. Doubting himself was Mr. Harding's weakness. It is not, however, the usual fault of his order.\n\nYes! All Barchester was in a tumult. It was not only the clergy who were affected. The laity also had listened to Mr. Slope's new doctrine, all with surprise, some with indignation, and some with a mixed feeling, in which dislike of the preacher was not so strongly blended. The old bishop and his chaplains, the dean and his canons and minor canons, the old choir, and especially Mr. Harding who was at the head of it, had all been popular in Barchester. They had spent their money and done good; the poor had not been ground down; the clergy in society had neither been overbearing nor austere; and the whole repute of the city was due to its ecclesiastical importance. Yet there were those who had heard Mr. Slope with satisfaction.\n\nIt is so pleasant to receive a fillip of excitement when suffering from the dull routine of everyday life! The anthems and Te Deums were in themselves delightful, but they had been heard so often! Mr. Slope was certainly not delightful, but he was new, and, moreover, clever. They had long thought it slow, so said now many of the Barchesterians, to go on as they had done in their old humdrum way, giving ear to none of the religious changes which were moving the world without. People in advance of the age now had new ideas, and it was quite time that Barchester should go in advance. Mr. Slope might be right. Sunday had certainly not been strictly kept in Barchester, except as regarded the cathedral services. Indeed the two hours between services had long been appropriated to morning calls and hot luncheons. Then, Sunday-schools! Really more ought to have been done as to Sunday-schools--Sabbath-day schools Mr. Slope had called them. The late bishop had really not thought of Sunday-schools as he should have done. (These people probably did not reflect that catechisms and collects are quite as hard work to the young mind as bookkeeping is to the elderly, and that quite as little feeling of worship enters into the one task as the other.) And then, as regarded that great question of musical services, there might be much to be said on Mr. Slope's side of the question. It certainly was the fact that people went to the cathedral to hear the music, &c. &c\n\nAnd so a party absolutely formed itself in Barchester on Mr. Slope's side of the question! This consisted, among the upper classes, chiefly of ladies. No man--that is, no gentleman--could possibly be attracted by Mr. Slope, or consent to sit at the feet of so abhorrent a Gamaliel. Ladies are sometimes less nice in their appreciation of physical disqualification; provided that a man speak to them well, they will listen, though he speak from a mouth never so deformed and hideous. Wilkes was most fortunate as a lover, and the damp, sandy-haired, saucer-eyed, red-fisted Mr. Slope was powerful only over the female breast.\n\nThere were, however, one or two of the neighbouring clergy who thought it not quite safe to neglect the baskets in which for the nonce were stored the loaves and fishes of the diocese of Barchester. They, and they only, came to call on Mr. Slope after his performance in the cathedral pulpit. Among these Mr. Quiverful, the rector of Puddingdale, whose wife still continued to present him from year to year with fresh pledges of her love, and so to increase his cares and, it is to be hoped, his happiness equally. Who can wonder that a gentleman with fourteen living children and a bare income of £400 a year should look after the loaves and fishes, even when they are under the thumb of a Mr. Slope?\n\nVery soon after the Sunday on which the sermon was preached, the leading clergy of the neighbourhood held high debate together as to how Mr. Slope should be put down. In the first place, he should never again preach from the pulpit of Barchester cathedral. This was Dr. Grantly's earliest dictum, and they all agreed, providing only that they had the power to exclude him. Dr. Grantly declared that the power rested with the dean and chapter, observing that no clergyman out of the chapter had a claim to preach there, saving only the bishop himself. To this the dean assented, but alleged that contests on such a subject would be unseemly; to which rejoined a meagre little doctor, one of the cathedral prebendaries, that the contest must be all on the side of Mr. Slope if every prebendary were always there ready to take his own place in the pulpit. Cunning little meagre doctor, whom it suits well to live in his own cosy house within Barchester close, and who is well content to have his little fling at Dr. Vesey Stanhope and other absentees, whose Italian villas, or enticing London homes, are more tempting than cathedral stalls and residences!\n\nTo this answered the burly chancellor, a man rather silent indeed, but very sensible, that absent prebendaries had their vicars, and that in such case the vicar's right to the pulpit was the same as that of the higher order. To which the dean assented, groaning deeply at these truths. Thereupon, however, the meagre doctor remarked that they would be in the hands of their minor canons, one of whom might at any hour betray his trust. Whereon was heard from the burly chancellor an ejaculation sounding somewhat like \"Pooh, pooh, pooh!\" but it might be that the worthy man was but blowing out the heavy breath from his windpipe. Why silence him at all? suggested Mr. Harding. Let them not be ashamed to hear what any man might have to preach to them, unless he preached false doctrine; in which case, let the bishop silence him. So spoke our friend; vainly; for human ends must be attained by human means. But the dean saw a ray of hope out of those purblind old eyes of his. Yes, let them tell the bishop how distasteful to them was this Mr. Slope: a new bishop just come to his seat could not wish to insult his clergy while the gloss was yet fresh on his first apron.\n\nThen up rose Dr. Grantly and, having thus collected the scattered wisdom of his associates, spoke forth with words of deep authority. When I say up rose the archdeacon, I speak of the inner man, which then sprang up to more immediate action, for the doctor had bodily been standing all along with his back to the dean's empty fire-grate, and the tails of his frock coat supported over his two arms. His hands were in his breeches pockets.\n\n\"It is quite clear that this man must not be allowed to preach again in this cathedral. We all see that, except our dear friend here, the milk of whose nature runs so softly that he would not have the heart to refuse the Pope the loan of his pulpit, if the Pope would come and ask it. We must not, however, allow the man to preach again here. It is not because his opinion on church matters may be different from ours--with that one would not quarrel. It is because he has purposely insulted us. When he went up into that pulpit last Sunday, his studied object was to give offence to men who had grown old in reverence of those things of which he dared to speak so slightingly. What! To come here a stranger, a young, unknown, and unfriended stranger, and tell us, in the name of the bishop his master, that we are ignorant of our duties, old-fashioned, and useless! I don't know whether most to admire his courage or his impudence! And one thing I will tell you: that sermon originated solely with the man himself. The bishop was no more a party to it than was the dean here. You all know how grieved I am to see a bishop in this diocese holding the latitudinarian ideas by which Dr. Proudie has made himself conspicuous. You all know how greatly I should distrust the opinion of such a man. But in this matter I hold him to be blameless. I believe Dr. Proudie has lived too long among gentlemen to be guilty, or to instigate another to be guilty, of so gross an outrage. No! That man uttered what was untrue when he hinted that he was speaking as the mouthpiece of the bishop. It suited his ambitious views at once to throw down the gauntlet to us--at once to defy us here in the quiet of our own religious duties--here within the walls of our own loved cathedral--here where we have for so many years exercised our ministry without schism and with good repute. Such an attack upon us, coming from such a quarter, is abominable.\"\n\n\"Abominable,\" groaned the dean. \"Abominable,\" muttered the meagre doctor. \"Abominable,\" re-echoed the chancellor, uttering the sound from the bottom of his deep chest. \"I really think it was,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Most abominable and most unjustifiable,\" continued the archdeacon. \"But, Mr. Dean, thank God, that pulpit is still our own: your own, I should say. That pulpit belongs solely to the dean and chapter of Barchester Cathedral, and as yet Mr. Slope is no part of that chapter. You, Mr. Dean, have suggested that we should appeal to the bishop to abstain from forcing this man on us; but what if the bishop allow himself to be ruled by his chaplain? In my opinion the matter is in our own hands. Mr. Slope cannot preach there without permission asked and obtained, and let that permission be invariably refused. Let all participation in the ministry of the cathedral service be refused to him. Then, if the bishop choose to interfere, we shall know what answer to make to the bishop. My friend here has suggested that this man may again find his way into the pulpit by undertaking the duty of some of your minor canons, but I am sure that we may fully trust to these gentlemen to support us, when it is known that the dean objects to any such transfer.\"\n\n\"Of course you may,\" said the chancellor.\n\nThere was much more discussion among the learned conclave, all of which, of course, ended in obedience to the archdeacon's commands. They had too long been accustomed to his rule to shake it off so soon, and in this particular case they had none of them a wish to abet the man whom he was so anxious to put down.\n\nSuch a meeting as that we have just recorded is not held in such a city as Barchester unknown and untold of. Not only was the fact of the meeting talked of in every respectable house, including the palace, but the very speeches of the dean, the archdeacon, and chancellor were repeated; not without many additions and imaginary circumstances, according to the tastes and opinions of the relaters.\n\nAll, however, agreed in saying that Mr. Slope was to be debarred from opening his mouth in the cathedral of Barchester; many believed that the vergers were to be ordered to refuse him even the accommodation of a seat; and some of the most far-going advocates for strong measures declared that his sermon was looked upon as an indictable offence, and that proceedings were to be taken against him for brawling.\n\nThe party who were inclined to defend him--the enthusiastically religious young ladies and the middle-aged spinsters desirous of a move--of course took up his defence the more warmly on account of this attack. If they could not hear Mr. Slope in the cathedral, they would hear him elsewhere; they would leave the dull dean, the dull old prebendaries, and the scarcely less dull young minor canons to preach to each other; they would work slippers and cushions and hem bands for Mr. Slope, make him a happy martyr, and stick him up in some new Sion or Bethesda, and put the cathedral quite out of fashion.\n\nDr. and Mrs. Proudie at once returned to London. They thought it expedient not to have to encounter any personal application from the dean and chapter respecting the sermon till the violence of the storm had expended itself; but they left Mr. Slope behind them nothing daunted, and he went about his work zealously, flattering such as would listen to his flattery, whispering religious twaddle into the ears of foolish women, ingratiating himself with the few clergy who would receive him, visiting the houses of the poor, inquiring into all people, prying into everything, and searching with his minutest eye into all palatial dilapidations. He did not, however, make any immediate attempt to preach again in the cathedral.\n\nAnd so all Barchester was by the ears.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. The Ex-warden Rejoices in His Probable Return to the Hospital\n\nAmong the ladies in Barchester who have hitherto acknowledged Mr. Slope as their spiritual director must not be reckoned either the Widow Bold or her sister-in-law. On the first outbreak of the wrath of the denizens of the close, none had been more animated against the intruder than these two ladies. And this was natural. Who could be so proud of the musical distinction of their own cathedral as the favourite daughter of the precentor? Who would be so likely to resent an insult offered to the old choir? And in such matters Miss Bold and her sister-in-law had but one opinion.\n\nThis wrath, however, has in some degree been mitigated, and I regret to say that these ladies allowed Mr. Slope to be his own apologist. About a fortnight after the sermon had been preached, they were both of them not a little surprised by hearing Mr. Slope announced, as the page in buttons opened Mrs. Bold's drawing-room door. Indeed, what living man could, by a mere morning visit, have surprised them more? Here was the great enemy of all that was good in Barchester coming into their own drawing-room, and they had no strong arm, no ready tongue, near at hand for their protection. The widow snatched her baby out of its cradle into her lap, and Mary Bold stood up ready to die manfully in that baby's behalf, should, under any circumstances, such a sacrifice become necessary.\n\nIn this manner was Mr. Slope received. But when he left, he was allowed by each lady to take her hand and to make his adieux as gentlemen do who have been graciously entertained! Yes, he shook hands with them, and was curtseyed out courteously, the buttoned page opening the door as he would have done for the best canon of them all. He had touched the baby's little hand and blessed him with a fervid blessing; he had spoken to the widow of her early sorrows, and Eleanor's silent tears had not rebuked him; he had told Mary Bold that her devotion would be rewarded, and Mary Bold had heard the praise without disgust. And how had he done all this? How had he so quickly turned aversion into, at any rate, acquaintance? How had he over-come the enmity with which these ladies had been ready to receive him, and made his peace with them so easily?\n\nMy readers will guess from what I have written that I myself do not like Mr. Slope, but I am constrained to admit that he is a man of parts. He knows how to say a soft word in the proper place; he knows how to adapt his flattery to the ears of his hearers; he knows the wiles of the serpent, and he uses them. Could Mr. Slope have adapted his manners to men as well as to women, could he ever have learnt the ways of a gentleman, he might have risen to great things.\n\nHe commenced his acquaintance with Eleanor by praising her father. He had, he said, become aware that he had unfortunately offended the feelings of a man of whom he could not speak too highly; he would not now allude to a subject which was probably too serious for drawing-room conversation, but he would say that it had been very far from him to utter a word in disparagement of a man of whom all the world, at least the clerical world, spoke so highly as it did of Mr. Harding. And so he went on, unsaying a great deal of his sermon, expressing his highest admiration for the precentor's musical talents, eulogizing the father and the daughter and the sister-in-law, speaking in that low silky whisper which he always had specially prepared for feminine ears, and, ultimately, gaining his object. When he left, he expressed a hope that he might again be allowed to call; and though Eleanor gave no verbal assent to this, she did not express dissent: and so Mr. Slope's right to visit at the widow's house was established.\n\nThe day after this visit Eleanor told her father of it and expressed an opinion that Mr. Slope was not quite so black as he had been painted. Mr. Harding opened his eyes rather wider than usual when he heard what had occurred, but he said little; he could not agree in any praise of Mr. Slope, and it was not his practice to say much evil of anyone. He did not, however, like the visit, and simple-minded as he was, he felt sure that Mr. Slope had some deeper motive than the mere pleasure of making soft speeches to two ladies.\n\nMr. Harding, however, had come to see his daughter with other purpose than that of speaking either good or evil of Mr. Slope. He had come to tell her that the place of warden in Hiram's Hospital was again to be filled up, and that in all probability he would once more return to his old home and his twelve bedesmen.\n\n\"But,\" said he, laughing, \"I shall be greatly shorn of my ancient glory.\"\n\n\"Why so, Papa?\"\n\n\"This new act of Parliament that is to put us all on our feet again,\" continued he, \"settles my income at four hundred and fifty pounds per annum.\"\n\n\"Four hundred and fifty,\" said she, \"instead of eight hundred! Well, that is rather shabby. But still, Papa, you'll have the dear old house and the garden?\"\n\n\"My dear,\" said he, \"it's worth twice the money;\" and as he spoke he showed a jaunty kind of satisfaction in his tone and manner and in the quick, pleasant way in which he paced Eleanor's drawing-room. \"It's worth twice the money. I shall have the house and the garden and a larger income than I can possibly want.\"\n\n\"At any rate, you'll have no extravagant daughter to provide for;\" and as she spoke, the young widow put her arm within his, and made him sit on the sofa beside her; \"at any rate, you'll not have that expense.\"\n\n\"No, my dear, and I shall be rather lonely without her; but we won't think of that now. As regards income, I shall have plenty for all I want. I shall have my old house, and I don't mind owning now that I have felt sometimes the inconvenience of living in a lodging. Lodgings are very nice for young men, but at my time of life there is a want of--I hardly know what to call it, perhaps not respectability--\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa! I'm sure there's been nothing like that. Nobody has thought it; nobody in all Barchester has been more respected than you have been since you took those rooms in High Street. Nobody! Not the dean in his deanery, or the archdeacon out at Plumstead.\"\n\n\"The archdeacon would not be much obliged to you if he heard you,\" said he, smiling somewhat at the exclusive manner in which his daughter confined her illustration to the church dignitaries of the chapter of Barchester; \"but at any rate I shall be glad to get back to the old house. Since I heard that it was all settled, I have begun to fancy that I can't be comfortable without my two sitting-rooms.\"\n\n\"Come and stay with me, Papa, till it is settled--there's a dear Papa.\"\n\n\"Thank ye, Nelly. But no, I won't do that. It would make two movings. I shall be very glad to get back to my old men again. Alas! alas! There have six of them gone in these few last years. Six out of twelve! And the others I fear have had but a sorry life of it there. Poor Bunce, poor old Bunce!\"\n\nBunce was one of the surviving recipients of Hiram's charity, an old man, now over ninety, who had long been a favourite of Mr. Harding's.\n\n\"How happy old Bunce will be,\" said Mrs. Bold, clapping her soft hands softly. \"How happy they all will be to have you back again. You may be sure there will soon be friendship among them again when you are there.\"\n\n\"But,\" said he, half-laughing, \"I am to have new troubles, which will be terrible to me. There are to be twelve old women, and a matron. How shall I manage twelve women and a matron!\"\n\n\"The matron will manage the women, of course.\"\n\n\"And who'll manage the matron?\" said he.\n\n\"She won't want to be managed. She'll be a great lady herself, I suppose. But, Papa, where will the matron live? She is not to live in the warden's house with you, is she?\"\n\n\"Well, I hope not, my dear.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, I tell you fairly, I won't have a matron for a new stepmother.\"\n\n\"You shan't, my dear; that is, if I can help it. But they are going to build another house for the matron and the women, and I believe they haven't even fixed yet on the site of the building.\"\n\n\"And have they appointed the matron?\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"They haven't appointed the warden yet,\" replied he.\n\n\"But there's no doubt about that, I suppose,\" said his daughter.\n\nMr. Harding explained that he thought there was no doubt; that the archdeacon had declared as much, saying that the bishop and his chaplain between them had not the power to appoint anyone else, even if they had the will to do so, and sufficient impudence to carry out such a will. The archdeacon was of opinion that, though Mr. Harding had resigned his wardenship, and had done so unconditionally, he had done so under circumstances which left the bishop no choice as to his reappointment, now that the affair of the hospital had been settled on a new basis by act of Parliament. Such was the archdeacon's opinion, and his father-in-law received it without a shadow of doubt.\n\nDr. Grantly had always been strongly opposed to Mr. Harding's resignation of the place. He had done all in his power to dissuade him from it. He had considered that Mr. Harding was bound to withstand the popular clamour with which he was attacked for receiving so large an income as eight hundred a year from such a charity, and was not even yet satisfied that his father-in-law's conduct had not been pusillanimous and undignified. He looked also on this reduction of the warden's income as a shabby, paltry scheme on the part of government for escaping from a difficulty into which it had been brought by the public press. Dr. Grantly observed that the government had no more right to dispose of a sum of four hundred and fifty pounds a year out of the income of Hiram's legacy than of nine hundred; whereas, as he said, the bishop, dean, and chapter clearly had a right to settle what sum should be paid. He also declared that the government had no more right to saddle the charity with twelve old women than with twelve hundred; and he was, therefore, very indignant on the matter. He probably forgot when so talking that government had done nothing of the kind, and had never assumed any such might or any such right. He made the common mistake of attributing to the government, which in such matters is powerless, the doings of Parliament, which in such matters is omnipotent.\n\nBut though he felt that the glory and honour of the situation of warden of Barchester Hospital were indeed curtailed by the new arrangement; that the whole establishment had to a certain degree been made vile by the touch of Whig commissioners; that the place, with its lessened income, its old women, and other innovations, was very different from the hospital of former days; still the archdeacon was too practical a man of the world to wish that his father-in-law, who had at present little more than £200 per annum for all his wants, should refuse the situation, defiled, undignified, and commission-ridden as it was.\n\nMr. Harding had, accordingly, made up his mind that he would return to his old home at the hospital, and, to tell the truth, had experienced almost a childish pleasure in the idea of doing so. The diminished income was to him not even the source of momentary regret. The matron and the old women did rather go against the grain, but he was able to console himself with the reflection that, after all, such an arrangement might be of real service to the poor of the city. The thought that he must receive his reappointment as the gift of the new bishop, and probably through the hands of Mr. Slope, annoyed him a little, but his mind was set at rest by the assurance of the archdeacon that there would be no favour in such a presentation. The reappointment of the old warden would be regarded by all the world as a matter of course. Mr. Harding, therefore, felt no hesitation in telling his daughter that they might look upon his return to his old quarters as a settled matter.\n\n\"And you won't have to ask for it, Papa?\"\n\n\"Certainly not, my dear. There is no ground on which I could ask for any favour from the bishop, whom, indeed, I hardly know. Nor would I ask a favour, the granting of which might possibly be made a question to be settled by Mr. Slope. No,\" said he, moved for a moment by a spirit very unlike his own, \"I certainly shall be very glad to go back to the hospital; but I should never go there if it were necessary that my doing so should be the subject of a request to Mr. Slope.\"\n\nThis little outbreak of her father's anger jarred on the present tone of Eleanor's mind. She had not learnt to like Mr. Slope, but she had learnt to think that he had much respect for her father; and she would, therefore, willingly use her efforts to induce something like good feeling between them.\n\n\"Papa,\" said she, \"I think you somewhat mistake Mr. Slope's character.\"\n\n\"Do I?\" said he placidly.\n\n\"I think you do, Papa. I think he intended no personal disrespect to you when he preached the sermon which made the archdeacon and the dean so angry!\"\n\n\"I never supposed he did, my dear. I hope I never inquired within myself whether he did or no. Such a matter would be unworthy of any inquiry, and very unworthy of the consideration of the chapter. But I fear he intended disrespect to the ministration of God's services, as conducted in conformity with the rules of the Church of England.\"\n\n\"But might it not be that he thought it his duty to express his dissent from that which you, and the dean, and all of us here so much approve?\"\n\n\"It can hardly be the duty of a young man rudely to assail the religious convictions of his elders in the church. Courtesy should have kept him silent, even if neither charity nor modesty could do so.\"\n\n\"But Mr. Slope would say that on such a subject the commands of his heavenly Master do not admit of his being silent.\"\n\n\"Nor of his being courteous, Eleanor?\"\n\n\"He did not say that, Papa.\"\n\n\"Believe me, my child, that Christian ministers are never called on by God's word to insult the convictions, or even the prejudices of their brethren, and that religion is at any rate not less susceptible of urbane and courteous conduct among men than any other study which men may take up. I am sorry to say that I cannot defend Mr. Slope's sermon in the cathedral. But come, my dear, put on your bonnet and let us walk round the dear old gardens at the hospital. I have never yet had the heart to go beyond the courtyard since we left the place. Now I think I can venture to enter.\"\n\nEleanor rang the bell and gave a variety of imperative charges as to the welfare of the precious baby, whom, all but unwillingly, she was about to leave for an hour or so, and then sauntered forth with her father to revisit the old hospital. It had been forbidden ground to her as well as to him since the day on which they had walked forth together from its walls.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. The Stanhope Family\n\nIt is now three months since Dr. Proudie began his reign, and changes have already been effected in the diocese which show at least the energy of an active mind. Among other things absentee clergymen have been favoured with hints much too strong to be overlooked. Poor dear old Bishop Grantly had on this matter been too lenient, and the archdeacon had never been inclined to be severe with those who were absent on reputable pretences, and who provided for their duties in a liberal way.\n\nAmong the greatest of the diocesan sinners in this respect was Dr. Vesey Stanhope. Years had now passed since he had done a day's duty, and yet there was no reason against his doing duty except a want of inclination on his own part. He held a prebendal stall in the diocese, one of the best residences in the close, and the two large rectories of Crabtree Canonicorum and Stogpingum. Indeed, he had the cure of three parishes, for that of Eiderdown was joined to Stogpingum. He had resided in Italy for twelve years. His first going there had been attributed to a sore throat, and that sore throat, though never repeated in any violent manner, had stood him in such stead that it had enabled him to live in easy idleness ever since.\n\nHe had now been summoned home--not, indeed, with rough violence, or by any peremptory command, but by a mandate which he found himself unable to disregard. Mr. Slope had written to him by the bishop's desire. In the first place, the bishop much wanted the valuable co-operation of Dr. Vesey Stanhope in the diocese; in the next, the bishop thought it his imperative duty to become personally acquainted with the most conspicuous of his diocesan clergy; then the bishop thought it essentially necessary for Dr. Stanhope's own interests that Dr. Stanhope should, at any rate for a time, return to Barchester; and lastly, it was said that so strong a feeling was at the present moment evinced by the hierarchs of the church with reference to the absence of its clerical members, that it behoved Dr. Vesey Stanhope not to allow his name to stand among those which would probably in a few months be submitted to the councils of the nation.\n\nThere was something so ambiguously frightful in this last threat that Dr. Stanhope determined to spend two or three summer months at his residence in Barchester. His rectories were inhabited by his curates, and he felt himself from disuse to be unfit for parochial duty; but his prebendal home was kept empty for him, and he thought it probable that he might be able now and again to preach a prebendal sermon. He arrived, therefore, with all his family at Barchester, and he and they must be introduced to my readers.\n\nThe great family characteristic of the Stanhopes might probably be said to be heartlessness, but this want of feeling was, in most of them, accompanied by so great an amount of good nature as to make itself but little noticeable to the world. They were so prone to oblige their neighbours that their neighbours failed to perceive how indifferent to them was the happiness and well-being of those around them. The Stanhopes would visit you in your sickness (provided it were not contagious), would bring you oranges, French novels, and the last new bit of scandal, and then hear of your death or your recovery with an equally indifferent composure. Their conduct to each other was the same as to the world; they bore and forbore; and there was sometimes, as will be seen, much necessity for forbearing; but their love among themselves rarely reached above this. It is astonishing how much each of the family was able to do, and how much each did, to prevent the well-being of the other four.\n\nFor there were five in all; the doctor, namely, and Mrs. Stanhope, two daughters, and one son. The doctor, perhaps, was the least singular and most estimable of them all, and yet such good qualities as he possessed were all negative. He was a good-looking rather plethoric gentleman of about sixty years of age. His hair was snow-white, very plentiful, and somewhat like wool of the finest description. His whiskers were very large and very white, and gave to his face the appearance of a benevolent, sleepy old lion. His dress was always unexceptionable. Although he had lived so many years in Italy it was invariably of a decent clerical hue, but it never was hyperclerical. He was a man not given to much talking, but what little he did say was generally well said. His reading seldom went beyond romances and poetry of the lightest and not always most moral description. He was thoroughly a bon vivant; an accomplished judge of wine, though he never drank to excess; and a most inexorable critic in all affairs touching the kitchen. He had had much to forgive in his own family, since a family had grown up around him, and had forgiven everything--except inattention to his dinner. His weakness in that respect was now fully understood, and his temper but seldom tried. As Dr. Stanhope was a clergyman, it may be supposed that his religious convictions made up a considerable part of his character, but this was not so. That he had religious convictions must be believed, but he rarely obtruded them, even on his children. This abstinence on his part was not systematic, but very characteristic of the man. It was not that he had predetermined never to influence their thoughts, but he was so habitually idle that his time for doing so had never come till the opportunity for doing so was gone forever. Whatever conviction the father may have had, the children were at any rate but indifferent members of the church from which he drew his income.\n\nSuch was Dr. Stanhope. The features of Mrs. Stanhope's character were even less plainly marked than those of her lord. The far niente of her Italian life had entered into her very soul, and brought her to regard a state of inactivity as the only earthly good. In manner and appearance she was exceedingly prepossessing. She had been a beauty, and even now, at fifty-five, she was a handsome woman. Her dress was always perfect: she never dressed but once in the day, and never appeared till between three and four; but when she did appear, she appeared at her best. Whether the toil rested partly with her, or wholly with her handmaid, it is not for such a one as the author even to imagine. The structure of her attire was always elaborate and yet never over-laboured. She was rich in apparel but not bedizened with finery; her ornaments were costly, rare, and such as could not fail to attract notice, but they did not look as though worn with that purpose. She well knew the great architectural secret of decorating her constructions, and never descended to construct a decoration. But when we have said that Mrs. Stanhope knew how to dress and used her knowledge daily, we have said all. Other purpose in life she had none. It was something, indeed, that she did not interfere with the purposes of others. In early life she had undergone great trials with reference to the doctor's dinners, but for the last ten or twelve years her elder daughter Charlotte had taken that labour off her hands, and she had had little to trouble her--little, that is, till the edict for this terrible English journey had gone forth: since then, indeed, her life had been laborious enough. For such a one, the toil of being carried from the shores of Como to the city of Barchester is more than labour enough, let the care of the carriers be ever so vigilant. Mrs. Stanhope had been obliged to have every one of her dresses taken in from the effects of the journey.\n\nCharlotte Stanhope was at this time about thirty-five years old, and whatever may have been her faults, she had none of those which belong particularly to old young ladies. She neither dressed young, nor talked young, nor indeed looked young. She appeared to be perfectly content with her time of life, and in no way affected the graces of youth. She was a fine young woman, and had she been a man, would have been a very fine young man. All that was done in the house, and that was not done by servants, was done by her. She gave the orders, paid the bills, hired and dismissed the domestics, made the tea, carved the meat, and managed everything in the Stanhope household. She, and she alone, could ever induce her father to look into the state of his worldly concerns. She, and she alone, could in any degree control the absurdities of her sister. She, and she alone, prevented the whole family from falling into utter disrepute and beggary. It was by her advice that they now found themselves very unpleasantly situated in Barchester.\n\nSo far, the character of Charlotte Stanhope is not unprepossessing. But it remains to be said that the influence which she had in her family, though it had been used to a certain extent for their worldly well-being, had not been used to their real benefit, as it might have been. She had aided her father in his indifference to his professional duties, counselling him that his livings were as much his individual property as the estates of his elder brother were the property of that worthy peer. She had for years past stifled every little rising wish for a return to England which the doctor had from time to time expressed. She had encouraged her mother in her idleness, in order that she herself might be mistress and manager of the Stanhope household. She had encouraged and fostered the follies of her sister, though she was always willing, and often able, to protect her from their probable result. She had done her best, and had thoroughly succeeded in spoiling her brother, and turning him loose upon the world an idle man without a profession and without a shilling that he could call his own.\n\nMiss Stanhope was a clever woman, able to talk on most subjects, and quite indifferent as to what the subject was. She prided herself on her freedom from English prejudice, and, she might have added, from feminine delicacy. On religion she was a pure free-thinker, and with much want of true affection, delighted to throw out her own views before the troubled mind of her father. To have shaken what remained of his Church of England faith would have gratified her much, but the idea of his abandoning his preferment in the church had never once presented itself to her mind. How could he indeed, when he had no income from any other source?\n\nBut the two most prominent members of the family still remain to be described. The second child had been christened Madeline and had been a great beauty. We need not say had been, for she was never more beautiful than at the time of which we write, though her person for many years had been disfigured by an accident. It is unnecessary that we should give in detail the early history of Madeline Stanhope. She had gone to Italy when about seventeen years of age, and had been allowed to make the most of her surpassing beauty in the salons of Milan and among the crowded villas along the shores of the Lake of Como. She had become famous for adventures in which her character was just not lost, and had destroyed the hearts of a dozen cavaliers without once being touched in her own. Blood had flowed in quarrels about her charms, and she had heard of these encounters with pleasurable excitement. It had been told of her that on one occasion she had stood by in the disguise of a page and had seen her lover fall.\n\nAs is so often the case, she had married the very worst of those who sought her hand. Why she had chosen Paulo Neroni, a man of no birth and no property, a mere captain in the Pope's guard, one who had come up to Milan either simply as an adventurer or else as a spy, a man of harsh temper and oily manners, mean in figure, swarthy in face, and so false in words as to be hourly detected, need not now be told. When the moment for doing so came, she had probably no alternative. He, at any rate, had become her husband, and after a prolonged honeymoon among the lakes, they had gone together to Rome, the papal captain having vainly endeavoured to induce his wife to remain behind him.\n\nSix months afterwards she arrived at her father's house a cripple, and a mother. She had arrived without even notice, with hardly clothes to cover her, and without one of those many ornaments which had graced her bridal trousseau. Her baby was in the arms of a poor girl from Milan, whom she had taken in exchange for the Roman maid who had accompanied her thus far, and who had then, as her mistress said, become homesick and had returned. It was clear that the lady had determined that there should be no witness to tell stories of her life in Rome.\n\nShe had fallen, she said, in ascending a ruin, and had fatally injured the sinews of her knee; so fatally that when she stood, she lost eight inches of her accustomed height; so fatally that when she essayed to move, she could only drag herself painfully along, with protruded hip and extended foot, in a manner less graceful than that of a hunchback. She had consequently made up her mind, once and forever, that she would never stand and never attempt to move herself.\n\nStories were not slow to follow her, averring that she had been cruelly ill-used by Neroni, and that to his violence had she owed her accident. Be that as it may, little had been said about her husband, but that little had made it clearly intelligible to the family that Signor Neroni was to be seen and heard of no more. There was no question as to readmitting the poor, ill-used beauty to her old family rights, no question as to adopting her infant daughter beneath the Stanhope roof-tree. Though heartless, the Stanhopes were not selfish. The two were taken in, petted, made much of, for a time all but adored, and then felt by the two parents to be great nuisances in the house. But in the house the lady was, and there she remained, having her own way, though that way was not very conformable with the customary usages of an English clergyman.\n\nMadame Neroni, though forced to give up all motion in the world, had no intention whatever of giving up the world itself. The beauty of her face was uninjured, and that beauty was of a peculiar kind. Her copious rich brown hair was worn in Grecian bandeaux round her head, displaying as much as possible of her forehead and cheeks. Her forehead, though rather low, was very beautiful from its perfect contour and pearly whiteness. Her eyes were long and large, and marvellously bright; might I venture to say bright as Lucifer's, I should perhaps best express the depth of their brilliancy. They were dreadful eyes to look at, such as would absolutely deter any man of quiet mind and easy spirit from attempting a passage of arms with such foes. There was talent in them, and the fire of passion and the play of wit, but there was no love. Cruelty was there instead, and courage, a desire of masterhood, cunning, and a wish for mischief. And yet, as eyes, they were very beautiful. The eyelashes were long and perfect, and the long, steady, unabashed gaze with which she would look into the face of her admirer fascinated while it frightened him. She was a basilisk from whom an ardent lover of beauty could make no escape. Her nose and mouth and teeth and chin and neck and bust were perfect, much more so at twenty-eight than they had been at eighteen. What wonder that with such charms still glowing in her face, and with such deformity destroying her figure, she should resolve to be seen, but only to be seen reclining on a sofa.\n\nHer resolve had not been carried out without difficulty. She had still frequented the opera at Milan; she had still been seen occasionally in the salons of the noblesse; she had caused herself to be carried in and out from her carriage, and that in such a manner as in no wise to disturb her charms, disarrange her dress, or expose her deformities. Her sister always accompanied her and a maid, a manservant also, and on state occasions, two. It was impossible that her purpose could have been achieved with less; and yet, poor as she was, she had achieved her purpose. And then again the more dissolute Italian youths of Milan frequented the Stanhope villa and surrounded her couch, not greatly to her father's satisfaction. Sometimes his spirit would rise, a dark spot would show itself on his cheek, and he would rebel, but Charlotte would assuage him with some peculiar triumph of her culinary art and all again would be smooth for awhile.\n\nMadeline affected all manner of rich and quaint devices in the garniture of her room, her person, and her feminine belongings. In nothing was this more apparent than in the visiting card which she had prepared for her use. For such an article one would say that she, in her present state, could have but small need, seeing how improbable it was that she should make a morning call: but not such was her own opinion. Her card was surrounded by a deep border of gilding; on this she had imprinted, in three lines\n\nLa Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni. --Nata Stanhope.\n\nAnd over the name she had a bright gilt coronet, which certainly looked very magnificent. How she had come to concoct such a name for herself it would be difficult to explain. Her father had been christened Vesey as another man is christened Thomas, and she had no more right to assume it than would have the daughter of a Mr. Josiah Jones to call herself Mrs. Josiah Smith, on marrying a man of the latter name. The gold coronet was equally out of place, and perhaps inserted with even less excuse. Paulo Neroni had had not the faintest title to call himself a scion of even Italian nobility. Had the pair met in England Neroni would probably have been a count, but they had met in Italy, and any such pretence on his part would have been simply ridiculous. A coronet, however, was a pretty ornament, and if it could solace a poor cripple to have such on her card, who would begrudge it to her?\n\nOf her husband, or of his individual family, she never spoke, but with her admirers she would often allude in a mysterious way to her married life and isolated state, and, pointing to her daughter, would call her the last of the blood of the emperors, thus referring Neroni's extraction to the old Roman family from which the worst of the Caesars sprang.\n\nThe \"signora\" was not without talent and not without a certain sort of industry; she was an indomitable letter-writer, and her letters were worth the postage: they were full of wit, mischief, satire, love, latitudinarian philosophy, free religion, and, sometimes, alas, loose ribaldry. The subject, however, depended entirely on the recipient, and she was prepared to correspond with anyone but moral young ladies or stiff old women. She wrote also a kind of poetry, generally in Italian, and short romances, generally in French. She read much of a desultory sort of literature, and as a modern linguist had really made great proficiency. Such was the lady who had now come to wound the hearts of the men of Barchester.\n\nEthelbert Stanhope was in some respects like his younger sister, but he was less inestimable as a man than she as a woman. His great fault was an entire absence of that principle which should have induced him, as the son of a man without fortune, to earn his own bread. Many attempts had been made to get him to do so, but these had all been frustrated, not so much by idleness on his part as by a disinclination to exert himself in any way not to his taste. He had been educated at Eton and had been intended for the Church, but he had left Cambridge in disgust after a single term, and notified to his father his intention to study for the bar. Preparatory to that, he thought it well that he should attend a German university, and consequently went to Leipzig. There he remained two years and brought away a knowledge of German and a taste for the fine arts. He still, however, intended himself for the bar, took chambers, engaged himself to sit at the feet of a learned pundit, and spent a season in London. He there found that all his aptitudes inclined him to the life of an artist, and he determined to live by painting. With this object he returned to Milan, and had himself rigged out for Rome. As a painter he might have earned his bread, for he wanted only diligence to excel, but when at Rome his mind was carried away by other things: he soon wrote home for money, saying that he had been converted to the Mother Church, that he was already an acolyte of the Jesuits, and that he was about to start with others to Palestine on a mission for converting Jews. He did go to Judea, but being unable to convert the Jews, was converted by them. He again wrote home, to say that Moses was the only giver of perfect laws to the world, that the coming of the true Messiah was at hand, that great things were doing in Palestine, and that he had met one of the family of Sidonia, a most remarkable man, who was now on his way to western Europe, and whom he had induced to deviate from his route with the object of calling at the Stanhope villa. Ethelbert then expressed his hope that his mother and sisters would listen to this wonderful prophet. His father he knew could not do so from pecuniary considerations. This Sidonia, however, did not take so strong a fancy to him as another of that family once did to a young English nobleman. At least he provided him with no heaps of gold as large as lions, so that the Judaized Ethelbert was again obliged to draw on the revenues of the Christian Church.\n\nIt is needless to tell how the father swore that he would send no more money and receive no Jew, nor how Charlotte declared that Ethelbert could not be left penniless in Jerusalem, and how \"La Signora Neroni\" resolved to have Sidonia at her feet. The money was sent, and the Jew did come. The Jew did come, but he was not at all to the taste of \"La Signora.\" He was a dirty little old man, and though he had provided no golden lions, he had, it seems, relieved young Stanhope's necessities. He positively refused to leave the villa till he had got a bill from the doctor on his London bankers.\n\nEthelbert did not long remain a Jew. He soon reappeared at the villa without prejudices on the subject of his religion, and with a firm resolve to achieve fame and fortune as a sculptor. He brought with him some models which he had originated at Rome and which really gave such fair promise that his father was induced to go to further expense in furthering these views. Ethelbert opened an establishment, or rather took lodgings and a workshop, at Carrara, and there spoilt much marble and made some few pretty images. Since that period, now four years ago, he had alternated between Carrara and the villa, but his sojourns at the workshop became shorter and shorter and those at the villa longer and longer. 'Twas no wonder, for Carrara is not a spot in which an Englishman would like to dwell.\n\nWhen the family started for England, he had resolved not to be left behind, and, with the assistance of his elder sister, had carried his point against his father's wishes. It was necessary, he said, that he should come to England for orders. How otherwise was he to bring his profession to account?\n\nIn personal appearance Ethelbert Stanhope was the most singular of beings. He was certainly very handsome. He had his sister Madeline's eyes, without their stare and without their hard, cunning, cruel firmness. They were also very much lighter, and of so light and clear a blue as to make his face remarkable, if nothing else did so. On entering a room with him, Ethelbert's blue eyes would be the first thing you would see, and on leaving it almost the last you would forget. His light hair was very long and silky, coming down over his coat. His beard had been prepared in holy land, and was patriarchal. He never shaved and rarely trimmed it. It was glossy, soft, clean, and altogether not unprepossessing. It was such that ladies might desire to reel it off and work it into their patterns in lieu of floss silk. His complexion was fair and almost pink; he was small in height and slender in limb, but well-made; and his voice was of peculiar sweetness.\n\nIn manner and dress he was equally remarkable. He had none of the mauvaise honte of an Englishman. He required no introduction to make himself agreeable to any person. He habitually addressed strangers, ladies as well as men, without any such formality, and in doing so never seemed to meet with rebuke. His costume cannot be described because it was so various, but it was always totally opposed in every principle of colour and construction to the dress of those with whom he for the time consorted.\n\nHe was habitually addicted to making love to ladies, and did so without any scruples of conscience, or any idea that such a practice was amiss. He had no heart to touch himself, and was literally unaware that humanity was subject to such an infliction. He had not thought much about it, but, had he been asked, would have said that ill-treating a lady's heart meant injuring her promotion in the world. His principles therefore forbade him to pay attention to a girl if he thought any man was present whom it might suit her to marry. In this manner his good nature frequently interfered with his amusement, but he had no other motive in abstaining from the fullest declarations of love to every girl that pleased his eye.\n\nBertie Stanhope, as he was generally called, was, however, popular with both sexes--and with Italians as well as English. His circle of acquaintance was very large and embraced people of all sorts. He had no respect for rank, and no aversion to those below him. He had lived on familiar terms with English peers, German shopkeepers, and Roman priests. All people were nearly alike to him. He was above, or rather below, all prejudices. No virtue could charm him, no vice shock him. He had about him a natural good manner, which seemed to qualify him for the highest circles, and yet he was never out of place in the lowest. He had no principle, no regard for others, no self-respect, no desire to be other than a drone in the hive, if only he could, as a drone, get what honey was sufficient for him. Of honey, in his latter days, it may probably be presaged, that he will have but short allowance.\n\nSuch was the family of the Stanhopes, who, at this period, suddenly joined themselves to the ecclesiastical circle of Barchester close. Any stranger union it would be impossible perhaps to conceive. And it was not as though they all fell down into the cathedral precincts hitherto unknown and untalked of. In such case, no amalgamation would have been at all probable between the new-comers and either the Proudie set or the Grantly set. But such was far from being the case. The Stanhopes were all known by name in Barchester, and Barchester was prepared to receive them with open arms. The doctor was one of her prebendaries, one of her rectors, one of her pillars of strength; and was, moreover, counted on as a sure ally both by Proudies and Grantlys.\n\nHe himself was the brother of one peer, and his wife was the sister of another--and both these peers were lords of Whiggish tendency, with whom the new bishop had some sort of alliance. This was sufficient to give to Mr. Slope high hope that he might enlist Dr. Stanhope on his side, before his enemies could outmanoeuvre him. On the other hand, the old dean had many many years ago, in the days of the doctor's clerical energies, been instrumental in assisting him in his views as to preferment; and many many years ago also, the two doctors, Stanhope and Grantly, had, as young parsons, been joyous together in the common-rooms of Oxford. Dr. Grantly, consequently, did not doubt but that the newcomer would range himself under his banners.\n\nLittle did any of them dream of what ingredients the Stanhope family was now composed.\n\n# CHAPTER X. Mrs. Proudie's Reception--Commenced\n\nThe bishop and his wife had spent only three or four days in Barchester on the occasion of their first visit. His lordship had, as we have seen, taken his seat on his throne, but his demeanour there, into which it had been his intention to infuse much hierarchal dignity, had been a good deal disarranged by the audacity of his chaplain's sermon. He had hardly dared to look his clergy in the face, and to declare by the severity of his countenance that in truth he meant all that his factotum was saying on his behalf; nor yet did he dare to throw Mr. Slope over, and show to those around him that he was no party to the sermon, and would resent it.\n\nHe had accordingly blessed his people in a shambling manner, not at all to his own satisfaction, and had walked back to his palace with his mind very doubtful as to what he would say to his chaplain on the subject. He did not remain long in doubt. He had hardly doffed his lawn when the partner of all his toils entered his study and exclaimed even before she had seated herself:\n\n\"Bishop, did you ever hear a more sublime, more spirit-moving, more appropriate discourse than that?\"\n\n\"Well, my love; ha--hum--he!\" The bishop did not know what to say.\n\n\"I hope, my lord, you don't mean to say you disapprove?\"\n\nThere was a look about the lady's eye which did not admit of my lord's disapproving at that moment. He felt that if he intended to disapprove, it must be now or never; but he also felt that it could not be now. It was not in him to say to the wife of his bosom that Mr. Slope's sermon was ill-timed, impertinent, and vexatious.\n\n\"No, no,\" replied the bishop. \"No, I can't say I disapprove--a very clever sermon and very well intended, and I dare say will do a great deal of good.\" This last praise was added, seeing that what he had already said by no means satisfied Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"I hope it will,\" said she. \"And I am sure it was well deserved. Did you ever in your life, bishop, hear anything so like play-acting as the way in which Mr. Harding sings the litany? I shall beg Mr. Slope to continue a course of sermons on the subject till all that is altered. We will have at any rate, in our cathedral, a decent, godly, modest morning service. There must be no more play-acting here now;\" and so the lady rang for lunch.\n\nThe bishop knew more about cathedrals and deans and precentors and church services than his wife did, and also more of a bishop's powers. But he thought it better at present to let the subject drop.\n\n\"My dear,\" said he, \"I think we must go back to London on Tuesday. I find my staying here will be very inconvenient to the Government.\"\n\nThe bishop knew that to this proposal his wife would not object, and he also felt that by thus retreating from the ground of battle the heat of the fight might be got over in his absence.\n\n\"Mr. Slope will remain here, of course?\" said the lady.\n\n\"Oh, of course,\" said the bishop.\n\nThus, after less than a week's sojourn in his palace, did the bishop fly from Barchester; nor did he return to it for two months, the London season being then over. During that time Mr. Slope was not idle, but he did not again essay to preach in the cathedral. In answer to Mrs. Proudie's letters advising a course of sermons, he had pleaded that he would at any rate wish to put off such an undertaking till she was there to hear them.\n\nHe had employed his time in consolidating a Proudie and Slope party--or rather a Slope and Proudie party, and he had not employed his time in vain. He did not meddle with the dean and chapter, except by giving them little teasing intimations of the bishop's wishes about this and the bishop's feelings about that, in a manner which was to them sufficiently annoying, but which they could not resent. He preached once or twice in a distant church in the suburbs of the city, but made no allusion to the cathedral service. He commenced the establishment of two \"Bishop's Barchester Sabbath-day schools,\" gave notice of a proposed \"Bishop's Barchester Young Men's Sabbath Evening Lecture Room,\" and wrote three or four letters to the manager of the Barchester branch railway, informing him how anxious the bishop was that the Sunday trains should be discontinued.\n\nAt the end of two months, however, the bishop and the lady reappeared, and as a happy harbinger of their return, heralded their advent by the promise of an evening party on the largest scale. The tickets of invitation were sent out from London--they were dated from Bruton Street, and were dispatched by the odious Sabbath-breaking railway, in a huge brown paper parcel to Mr. Slope. Everybody calling himself a gentleman, or herself a lady, within the city of Barchester, and a circle of two miles round it, was included. Tickets were sent to all the diocesan clergy, and also to many other persons of priestly note, of whose absence the bishop, or at least the bishop's wife, felt tolerably confident. It was intended, however, to be a thronged and noticeable affair, and preparations were made for receiving some hundreds.\n\nAnd now there arose considerable agitation among the Grantlyites whether or no they would attend the episcopal bidding. The first feeling with them all was to send the briefest excuses both for themselves and their wives and daughters. But by degrees policy prevailed over passion. The archdeacon perceived that he would be making a false step if he allowed the cathedral clergy to give the bishop just ground of umbrage. They all met in conclave and agreed to go. They would show that they were willing to respect the office, much as they might dislike the man. They agreed to go. The old dean would crawl in, if it were but for half an hour. The chancellor, treasurer, archdeacon, prebendaries, and minor canons would all go, and would all take their wives. Mr. Harding was especially bidden to do so, resolving in his heart to keep himself far removed from Mrs. Proudie. And Mrs. Bold was determined to go, though assured by her father that there was no necessity for such a sacrifice on her part. When all Barchester was to be there, neither Eleanor nor Mary Bold understood why they should stay away. Had they not been invited separately? And had not a separate little note from the chaplain, couched in the most respectful language, been enclosed with the huge episcopal card?\n\nAnd the Stanhopes would be there, one and all. Even the lethargic mother would so far bestir herself on such an occasion. They had only just arrived. The card was at the residence waiting for them. No one in Barchester had seen them. What better opportunity could they have of showing themselves to the Barchester world? Some few old friends, such as the archdeacon and his wife, had called and had found the doctor and his eldest daughter, but the élite of the family were not yet known.\n\nThe doctor indeed wished in his heart to prevent the signora from accepting the bishop's invitation, but she herself had fully determined that she would accept it. If her father was ashamed of having his daughter carried into a bishop's palace, she had no such feeling.\n\n\"Indeed, I shall,\" she had said to her sister who had gently endeavoured to dissuade her, by saying that the company would consist wholly of parsons and parsons' wives. \"Parsons, I suppose, are much the same as other men, if you strip them of their black coats; and as to their wives, I dare say they won't trouble me. You may tell Papa I don't at all mean to be left at home.\"\n\nPapa was told, and felt that he could do nothing but yield. He also felt that it was useless for him now to be ashamed of his children. Such as they were, they had become such under his auspices; as he had made his bed, so he must lie upon it; as he had sown his seed, so must he reap his corn. He did not indeed utter such reflexions in such language, but such was the gist of his thought. It was not because Madeline was a cripple that he shrank from seeing her made one of the bishop's guests, but because he knew that she would practise her accustomed lures, and behave herself in a way that could not fail of being distasteful to the propriety of Englishwomen. These things had annoyed but not shocked him in Italy. There they had shocked no one; but here in Barchester, here among his fellow parsons, he was ashamed that they should be seen. Such had been his feelings, but he repressed them. What if his brother clergymen were shocked! They could not take from him his preferment because the manners of his married daughter were too free.\n\nLa Signora Neroni had, at any rate, no fear that she would shock anybody. Her ambition was to create a sensation, to have parsons at her feet, seeing that the manhood of Barchester consisted mainly of parsons, and to send, if possible, every parson's wife home with a green fit of jealousy. None could be too old for her, and hardly any too young. None too sanctified, and none too worldly. She was quite prepared to entrap the bishop himself, and then to turn up her nose at the bishop's wife. She did not doubt of success, for she had always succeeded; but one thing was absolutely necessary; she must secure the entire use of a sofa.\n\nThe card sent to Dr. and Mrs. Stanhope and family had been so sent in an envelope having on the cover Mr. Slope's name. The signora soon learnt that Mrs. Proudie was not yet at the palace and that the chaplain was managing everything. It was much more in her line to apply to him than to the lady, and she accordingly wrote him the prettiest little billet in the world. In five lines she explained everything, declared how impossible it was for her not to be desirous to make the acquaintance of such persons as the Bishop of Barchester and his wife, and she might add also of Mr. Slope, depicted her own grievous state, and concluded by being assured that Mrs. Proudie would forgive her extreme hardihood in petitioning to be allowed to be carried to a sofa. She then enclosed one of her beautiful cards. In return she received as polite an answer from Mr. Slope--a sofa should be kept in the large drawing-room, immediately at the top of the grand stairs, especially for her use.\n\nAnd now the day of the party had arrived. The bishop and his wife came down from town only on the morning of the eventful day, as behoved such great people to do, but Mr. Slope had toiled day and night to see that everything should be in right order. There had been much to do. No company had been seen in the palace since heaven knows when. New furniture had been required, new pots and pans, new cups and saucers, new dishes and plates. Mrs. Proudie had at first declared that she would condescend to nothing so vulgar as eating and drinking, but Mr. Slope had talked, or rather written her out of economy. Bishops should be given to hospitality, and hospitality meant eating and drinking. So the supper was conceded; the guests, however, were to stand as they consumed it.\n\nThere were four rooms opening into each other on the first floor of the house, which were denominated the drawing-rooms, the reception-room, and Mrs. Proudie's boudoir. In olden days one of these had been Bishop Grantly's bedroom, and another his common sitting-room and study. The present bishop, however, had been moved down into a back parlour and had been given to understand that he could very well receive his clergy in the dining-room, should they arrive in too large a flock to be admitted into his small sanctum. He had been unwilling to yield, but after a short debate had yielded.\n\nMrs. Proudie's heart beat high as she inspected her suite of rooms. They were really very magnificent, or at least would be so by candlelight, and they had nevertheless been got up with commendable economy. Large rooms when full of people and full of light look well, because they are large, and are full, and are light. Small rooms are those which require costly fittings and rich furniture. Mrs. Proudie knew this, and made the most of it; she had therefore a huge gas lamp with a dozen burners hanging from each of the ceilings.\n\nPeople were to arrive at ten, supper was to last from twelve till one, and at half-past one everybody was to be gone. Carriages were to come in at the gate in the town and depart at the gate outside. They were desired to take up at a quarter before one. It was managed excellently, and Mr. Slope was invaluable.\n\nAt half-past nine the bishop and his wife and their three daughters entered the great reception-room, and very grand and very solemn they were. Mr. Slope was downstairs giving the last orders about the wine. He well understood that curates and country vicars with their belongings did not require so generous an article as the dignitaries of the close. There is a useful gradation in such things, and Marsala at 20s. a dozen did very well for the exterior supplementary tables in the corner.\n\n\"Bishop,\" said the lady, as his lordship sat himself down, \"don't sit on that sofa, if you please; it is to be kept separate for a lady.\"\n\nThe bishop jumped up and seated himself on a cane-bottomed chair. \"A lady?\" he inquired meekly; \"do you mean one particular lady, my dear?\"\n\n\"Yes, Bishop, one particular lady,\" said his wife, disdaining to explain.\n\n\"She has got no legs, Papa,\" said the youngest daughter, tittering.\n\n\"No legs!\" said the bishop, opening his eyes.\n\n\"Nonsense, Netta, what stuff you talk,\" said Olivia. \"She has got legs, but she can't use them. She has always to be kept lying down, and three or four men carry her about everywhere.\"\n\n\"Laws, how odd!\" said Augusta. \"Always carried about by four men! I'm sure I shouldn't like it. Am I right behind, Mamma? I feel as if I was open;\" and she turned her back to her anxious parent.\n\n\"Open! To be sure you are,\" said she, \"and a yard of petticoat strings hanging out. I don't know why I pay such high wages to Mrs. Richards if she can't take the trouble to see whether or no you are fit to be looked at,\" and Mrs. Proudie poked the strings here, and twitched the dress there, and gave her daughter a shove and a shake, and then pronounced it all right.\n\n\"But,\" rejoined the bishop, who was dying with curiosity about the mysterious lady and her legs, \"who is it that is to have the sofa? What's her name, Netta?\"\n\nA thundering rap at the front door interrupted the conversation. Mrs. Proudie stood up and shook herself gently, and touched her cap on each side as she looked in the mirror. Each of the girls stood on tiptoe and rearranged the bows on their bosoms, and Mr. Slope rushed upstairs three steps at a time.\n\n\"But who is it, Netta?\" whispered the bishop to his youngest daughter.\n\n\"La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni,\" whispered back the daughter; \"and mind you don't let anyone sit upon the sofa.\"\n\n\"La Signora Madeline Vicinironi!\" muttered to himself the bewildered prelate. Had he been told that the Begum of Oude was to be there, or Queen Pomara of the Western Isles, he could not have been more astonished. La Signora Madeline Vicinironi, who, having no legs to stand on, had bespoken a sofa in his drawing-room! Who could she be? He however could now make no further inquiry, as Dr. and Mrs. Stanhope were announced. They had been sent on out of the way a little before the time, in order that the signora might have plenty of time to get herself conveniently packed into the carriage.\n\nThe bishop was all smiles for the prebendary's wife, and the bishop's wife was all smiles for the prebendary. Mr. Slope was presented and was delighted to make the acquaintance of one of whom he had heard so much. The doctor bowed very low, and then looked as though he could not return the compliment as regarded Mr. Slope, of whom, indeed, he had heard nothing. The doctor, in spite of his long absence, knew an English gentleman when he saw him.\n\nAnd then the guests came in shoals: Mr. and Mrs. Quiverful and their three grown daughters. Mr. and Mrs. Chadwick and their three daughters. The burly chancellor and his wife and clerical son from Oxford. The meagre little doctor without incumbrance. Mr. Harding with Eleanor and Miss Bold. The dean leaning on a gaunt spinster, his only child now living with him, a lady very learned in stones, ferns, plants, and vermin, and who had written a book about petals. A wonderful woman in her way was Miss Trefoil. Mr. Finnie, the attorney, with his wife, was to be seen, much to the dismay of many who had never met him in a drawing-room before. The five Barchester doctors were all there, and old Scalpen, the retired apothecary and tooth-drawer, who was first taught to consider himself as belonging to the higher orders by the receipt of the bishop's card. Then came the archdeacon and his wife with their elder daughter Griselda, a slim, pale, retiring girl of seventeen who kept close to her mother, and looked out on the world with quiet watchful eyes, one who gave promise of much beauty when time should have ripened it.\n\nAnd so the rooms became full, and knots were formed, and every newcomer paid his respects to my lord and passed on, not presuming to occupy too much of the great man's attention. The archdeacon shook hands very heartily with Dr. Stanhope, and Mrs. Grantly seated herself by the doctor's wife. And Mrs. Proudie moved about with well-regulated grace, measuring out the quantity of her favours to the quality of her guests, just as Mr. Slope had been doing with the wine. But the sofa was still empty, and five-and-twenty ladies and five gentlemen had been courteously warned off it by the mindful chaplain.\n\n\"Why doesn't she come?\" said the bishop to himself. His mind was so preoccupied with the signora that he hardly remembered how to behave himself en bishop.\n\nAt last a carriage dashed up to the hall steps with a very different manner of approach from that of any other vehicle that had been there that evening. A perfect commotion took place. The doctor, who heard it as he was standing in the drawing-room, knew that his daughter was coming, and retired into the furthest corner, where he might not see her entrance. Mrs. Proudie perked herself up, feeling that some important piece of business was in hand. The bishop was instinctively aware that La Signora Vicinironi was come at last, and Mr. Slope hurried into the hall to give his assistance.\n\nHe was, however, nearly knocked down and trampled on by the cortège that he encountered on the hall steps. He got himself picked up, as well as he could, and followed the cortège upstairs. The signora was carried head foremost, her head being the care of her brother and an Italian manservant who was accustomed to the work; her feet were in the care of the lady's maid and the lady's Italian page; and Charlotte Stanhope followed to see that all was done with due grace and decorum. In this manner they climbed easily into the drawing-room, and a broad way through the crowd having been opened, the signora rested safely on her couch. She had sent a servant beforehand to learn whether it was a right- or a left-hand sofa, for it required that she should dress accordingly, particularly as regarded her bracelets.\n\nAnd very becoming her dress was. It was white velvet, without any other garniture than rich white lace worked with pearls across her bosom, and the same round the armlets of her dress. Across her brow she wore a band of red velvet, on the centre of which shone a magnificent Cupid in mosaic, the tints of whose wings were of the most lovely azure, and the colour of his chubby cheeks the clearest pink. On the one arm which her position required her to expose she wore three magnificent bracelets, each of different stones. Beneath her on the sofa, and over the cushion and head of it, was spread a crimson silk mantle or shawl, which went under her whole body and concealed her feet. Dressed as she was and looking as she did, so beautiful and yet so motionless, with the pure brilliancy of her white dress brought out and strengthened by the colour beneath it, with that lovely head, and those large, bold, bright, staring eyes, it was impossible that either man or woman should do other than look at her.\n\nNeither man nor woman for some minutes did do other.\n\nHer bearers too were worthy of note. The three servants were Italian, and though perhaps not peculiar in their own country, were very much so in the palace at Barchester. The man especially attracted notice and created a doubt in the mind of some whether he were a friend or a domestic. The same doubt was felt as to Ethelbert. The man was attired in a loose-fitting, common, black-cloth morning-coat. He had a jaunty, fat, well-pleased, clean face on which no atom of beard appeared, and he wore round his neck a loose, black silk neck-handkerchief. The bishop essayed to make him a bow, but the man, who was well trained, took no notice of him and walked out of the room quite at his ease, followed by the woman and the boy.\n\nEthelbert Stanhope was dressed in light blue from head to foot. He had on the loosest possible blue coat, cut square like a shooting coat, and very short. It was lined with silk of azure blue. He had on a blue satin waistcoat, a blue neck-handkerchief which was fastened beneath his throat with a coral ring, and very loose blue trousers which almost concealed his feet. His soft, glossy beard was softer and more glossy than ever.\n\nThe bishop, who had made one mistake, thought that he also was a servant and therefore tried to make way for him to pass. But Ethelbert soon corrected the error.\n\n# CHAPTER XI. Mrs. Proudie's Reception--Concluded\n\n\"Bishop of Barchester, I presume?\" said Bertie Stanhope, putting out his hand frankly; \"I am delighted to make your acquaintance. We are in rather close quarters here, a'nt we?\"\n\nIn truth they were. They had been crowded up behind the head of the sofa--the bishop in waiting to receive his guest, and the other in carrying her--and they now had hardly room to move themselves.\n\nThe bishop gave his hand quickly, made his little studied bow, and was delighted to make--He couldn't go on, for he did not know whether his friend was a signor, or a count or a prince.\n\n\"My sister really puts you all to great trouble,\" said Bertie.\n\n\"Not at all!\" The bishop was delighted to have the opportunity of welcoming La Signora Vicinironi--so at least he said--and attempted to force his way round to the front of the sofa. He had, at any rate, learnt that his strange guests were brother and sister. The man, he presumed, must be Signor Vicinironi--or count, or prince, as it might be. It was wonderful what good English he spoke. There was just a twang of foreign accent, and no more.\n\n\"Do you like Barchester, on the whole?\" asked Bertie.\n\nThe bishop, looking dignified, said that he did like Barchester.\n\n\"You've not been here very long, I believe,\" said Bertie.\n\n\"No--not long,\" said the bishop and tried again to make his way between the back of the sofa and a heavy rector, who was staring over it at the grimaces of the signora.\n\n\"You weren't a bishop before, were you?\"\n\nDr. Proudie explained that this was the first diocese he had held.\n\n\"Ah--I thought so,\" said Bertie, \"but you are changed about sometimes, a'nt you?\"\n\n\"Translations are occasionally made,\" said Dr. Proudie, \"but not so frequently as in former days.\"\n\n\"They've cut them all down to pretty nearly the same figure, haven't they?\" said Bertie.\n\nTo this the bishop could not bring himself to make any answer, but again attempted to move the rector.\n\n\"But the work, I suppose, is different?\" continued Bertie. \"Is there much to do here, at Barchester?\" This was said exactly in the tone that a young Admiralty clerk might use in asking the same question of a brother acolyte at the Treasury.\n\n\"The work of a bishop of the Church of England,\" said Dr. Proudie with considerable dignity, \"is not easy. The responsibility which he has to bear is very great indeed.\"\n\n\"Is it?\" said Bertie, opening wide his wonderful blue eyes. \"Well, I never was afraid of responsibility. I once had thoughts of being a bishop, myself.\"\n\n\"Had thoughts of being a bishop!\" said Dr. Proudie, much amazed.\n\n\"That is, a parson--a parson first, you know, and a bishop afterwards. If I had once begun, I'd have stuck to it. But, on the whole, I like the Church of Rome the best.\"\n\nThe bishop could not discuss the point, so he remained silent.\n\n\"Now, there's my father,\" continued Bertie; \"he hasn't stuck to it. I fancy he didn't like saying the same thing over so often. By the by, Bishop, have you seen my father?\"\n\nThe bishop was more amazed than ever. Had he seen his father? \"No,\" he replied; he had not yet had the pleasure: he hoped he might; and, as he said so, he resolved to bear heavy on that fat, immovable rector, if ever he had the power of doing so.\n\n\"He's in the room somewhere,\" said Bertie, \"and he'll turn up soon. By the by, do you know much about the Jews?\"\n\nAt last the bishop saw a way out. \"I beg your pardon,\" said he, \"but I'm forced to go round the room.\"\n\n\"Well--I believe I'll follow in your wake,\" said Bertie. \"Terribly hot--isn't it?\" This he addressed to the fat rector with whom he had brought himself into the closest contact. \"They've got this sofa into the worst possible part of the room; suppose we move it. Take care, Madeline.\"\n\nThe sofa had certainly been so placed that those who were behind it found great difficulty in getting out; there was but a narrow gangway, which one person could stop. This was a bad arrangement, and one which Bertie thought it might be well to improve.\n\n\"Take care, Madeline,\" said he, and turning to the fat rector, added, \"Just help me with a slight push.\"\n\nThe rector's weight was resting on the sofa and unwittingly lent all its impetus to accelerate and increase the motion which Bertie intentionally originated. The sofa rushed from its moorings and ran half-way into the middle of the room. Mrs. Proudie was standing with Mr. Slope in front of the signora, and had been trying to be condescending and sociable; but she was not in the very best of tempers, for she found that, whenever she spoke to the lady, the lady replied by speaking to Mr. Slope. Mr. Slope was a favourite, no doubt, but Mrs. Proudie had no idea of being less thought of than the chaplain. She was beginning to be stately, stiff, and offended, when unfortunately the castor of the sofa caught itself in her lace train, and carried away there is no saying how much of her garniture. Gathers were heard to go, stitches to crack, plaits to fly open, flounces were seen to fall, and breadths to expose themselves; a long ruin of rent lace disfigured the carpet, and still clung to the vile wheel on which the sofa moved.\n\nSo, when a granite battery is raised, excellent to the eyes of warfaring men, is its strength and symmetry admired. It is the work of years. Its neat embrasures, its finished parapets, its casemated stories show all the skill of modern science. But, anon, a small spark is applied to the treacherous fusee--a cloud of dust arises to the heavens--and then nothing is to be seen but dirt and dust and ugly fragments.\n\nWe know what was the wrath of Juno when her beauty was despised. We know to what storms of passion even celestial minds can yield. As Juno may have looked at Paris on Mount Ida, so did Mrs. Proudie look on Ethelbert Stanhope when he pushed the leg of the sofa into her lace train.\n\n\"Oh, you idiot, Bertie!\" said the signora, seeing what had been done and what were to be the consequences.\n\n\"Idiot!\" re-echoed Mrs. Proudie, as though the word were not half strong enough to express the required meaning; \"I'll let him know--\" and then looking round to learn, at a glance, the worst, she saw that at present it behoved her to collect the scattered débris of her dress.\n\nBertie, when he saw what he had done, rushed over the sofa and threw himself on one knee before the offended lady. His object, doubtless, was to liberate the torn lace from the castor, but he looked as though he were imploring pardon from a goddess.\n\n\"Unhand it, sir!\" said Mrs. Proudie. From what scrap of dramatic poetry she had extracted the word cannot be said, but it must have rested on her memory, and now seemed opportunely dignified for the occasion.\n\n\"I'll fly to the looms of the fairies to repair the damage, if you'll only forgive me,\" said Ethelbert, still on his knees.\n\n\"Unhand it, sir!\" said Mrs. Proudie with redoubled emphasis, and all but furious wrath. This allusion to the fairies was a direct mockery and intended to turn her into ridicule. So at least it seemed to her. \"Unhand it, sir!\" she almost screamed.\n\n\"It's not me; it's the cursed sofa,\" said Bertie, looking imploringly in her face and holding up both his hands to show that he was not touching her belongings, but still remaining on his knees.\n\nHereupon the Signora laughed; not loud, indeed, but yet audibly. And as the tigress bereft of her young will turn with equal anger on any within reach, so did Mrs. Proudie turn upon her female guest.\n\n\"Madam!\" she said--and it is beyond the power of prose to tell of the fire which flashed from her eyes.\n\nThe signora stared her full in the face for a moment, and then turning to her brother said playfully, \"Bertie, you idiot, get up.\"\n\nBy this time the bishop, and Mr. Slope, and her three daughters were around her, and had collected together the wide ruins of her magnificence. The girls fell into circular rank behind their mother, and thus following her and carrying out the fragments, they left the reception-rooms in a manner not altogether devoid of dignity. Mrs. Proudie had to retire and re-array herself.\n\nAs soon as the constellation had swept by, Ethelbert rose from his knees and, turning with mock anger to the fat rector, said: \"After all it was your doing, sir--not mine. But perhaps you are waiting for preferment, and so I bore it.\"\n\nWhereupon there was a laugh against the fat rector, in which both the bishop and the chaplain joined, and thus things got themselves again into order.\n\n\"Oh! my lord, I am so sorry for this accident,\" said the signora, putting out her hand so as to force the bishop to take it. \"My brother is so thoughtless. Pray sit down, and let me have the pleasure of making your acquaintance. Though I am so poor a creature as to want a sofa, I am not so selfish as to require it all.\" Madeline could always dispose herself so as to make room for a gentleman, though, as she declared, the crinoline of her lady friends was much too bulky to be so accommodated.\n\n\"It was solely for the pleasure of meeting you that I have had myself dragged here,\" she continued. \"Of course, with your occupation, one cannot even hope that you should have time to come to us, that is, in the way of calling. And at your English dinner-parties all is so dull and so stately. Do you know, my lord, that in coming to England my only consolation has been the thought that I should know you;\" and she looked at him with the look of a she-devil.\n\nThe bishop, however, thought that she looked very like an angel and, accepting the proffered seat, sat down beside her. He uttered some platitude as to his deep obligation for the trouble she had taken, and wondered more and more who she was.\n\n\"Of course you know my sad story?\" she continued.\n\nThe bishop didn't know a word of it. He knew, however, or thought he knew, that she couldn't walk into a room like other people, and so made the most of that. He put on a look of ineffable distress and said that he was aware how God had afflicted her.\n\nThe signora just touched the corner of her eyes with the most lovely of pocket-handkerchiefs. Yes, she said--she had been sorely tried--tried, she thought, beyond the common endurance of humanity; but while her child was left to her, everything was left. \"Oh! my lord,\" she exclaimed, \"you must see that infant--the last bud of a wondrous tree: you must let a mother hope that you will lay your holy hands on her innocent head and consecrate her for female virtues. May I hope it?\" said she, looking into the bishop's eye and touching the bishop's arm with her hand.\n\nThe bishop was but a man and said she might. After all, what was it but a request that he would confirm her daughter?--a request, indeed, very unnecessary to make, as he should do so as a matter of course if the young lady came forward in the usual way.\n\n\"The blood of Tiberius,\" said the signora in all but a whisper; \"the blood of Tiberius flows in her veins. She is the last of the Neros!\"\n\nThe bishop had heard of the last of the Visigoths, and had floating in his brain some indistinct idea of the last of the Mohicans, but to have the last of the Neros thus brought before him for a blessing was very staggering. Still he liked the lady: she had a proper way of thinking and talked with more propriety than her brother. But who were they? It was now quite clear that that blue madman with the silky beard was not a Prince Vicinironi. The lady was married and was of course one of the Vicinironi's by right of the husband. So the bishop went on learning.\n\n\"When will you see her? said the signora with a start.\n\n\"See whom?\" said the bishop.\n\n\"My child,\" said the mother.\n\n\"What is the young lady's age?\" asked the bishop.\n\n\"She is just seven,\" said the signora.\n\n\"Oh,\" said the bishop, shaking his head; \"she is much too young--very much too young.\"\n\n\"But in sunny Italy, you know, we do not count by years,\" and the signora gave the bishop one of her very sweetest smiles.\n\n\"But indeed, she is a great deal too young,\" persisted the bishop; \"we never confirm before--\"\n\n\"But you might speak to her; you might let her hear from your consecrated lips that she is not a castaway because she is a Roman; that she may be a Nero and yet a Christian; that she may owe her black locks and dark cheeks to the blood of the pagan Caesars, and yet herself be a child of grace; you will tell her this, won't you, my friend?\"\n\nThe friend said he would, and asked if the child could say her catechism.\n\n\"No,\" said the signora, \"I would not allow her to learn lessons such as those in a land ridden over by priests and polluted by the idolatry of Rome. It is here, here in Barchester, that she must first be taught to lisp those holy words. Oh, that you could be her instructor!\"\n\nNow, Dr. Proudie certainly liked the lady, but, seeing that he was a bishop, it was not probable that he was going to instruct a little girl in the first rudiments of her catechism; so he said he'd send a teacher.\n\n\"But you'll see her yourself, my lord?\"\n\nThe bishop said he would, but where should he call.\n\n\"At Papa's house,\" said the Signora with an air of some little surprise at the question.\n\nThe bishop actually wanted the courage to ask her who was her papa, so he was forced at last to leave her without fathoming the mystery. Mrs. Proudie, in her second best, had now returned to the rooms, and her husband thought it as well that he should not remain in too close conversation with the lady whom his wife appeared to hold in such slight esteem. Presently he came across his youngest daughter.\n\n\"Netta,\" said he, \"do you know who is the father of that Signora Vicinironi?\"\n\n\"It isn't Vicinironi, Papa,\" said Netta; \"but Vesey Neroni, and she's Doctor Stanhope's daughter. But I must go and do the civil to Griselda Grantly; I declare nobody has spoken a word to the poor girl this evening.\"\n\nDr. Stanhope! Dr. Vesey Stanhope! Dr. Vesey Stanhope's daughter, of whose marriage with a dissolute Italian scamp he now remembered to have heard something! And that impertinent blue cub who had examined him as to his episcopal bearings was old Stanhope's son, and the lady who had entreated him to come and teach her child the catechism was old Stanhope's daughter! The daughter of one of his own prebendaries! As these things flashed across his mind, he was nearly as angry as his wife had been. Nevertheless, he could not but own that the mother of the last of the Neros was an agreeable woman.\n\nDr. Proudie tripped out into the adjoining room, in which were congregated a crowd of Grantlyite clergymen, among whom the archdeacon was standing pre-eminent, while the old dean was sitting nearly buried in a huge arm chair by the fire-place. The bishop was very anxious to be gracious, and, if possible, to diminish the bitterness which his chaplain had occasioned. Let Mr. Slope do the fortiter in re, he himself would pour in the suaviter in modo.\n\n\"Pray don't stir, Mr. Dean, pray don't stir,\" he said as the old man essayed to get up; \"I take it as a great kindness, your coming to such an omnium gatherum as this. But we have hardly got settled yet, and Mrs. Proudie has not been able to see her friends as she would wish to do. Well, Mr. Archdeacon, after all, we have not been so hard upon you at Oxford.\"\n\n\"No,\" said the archdeacon, \"you've only drawn our teeth and cut out our tongues; you've allowed us still to breathe and swallow.\"\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" laughed the bishop; \"it's not quite so easy to cut out the tongue of an Oxford magnate--and as for teeth--ha, ha, ha! Why, in the way we've left the matter, it's very odd if the heads of colleges don't have their own way quite as fully as when the hebdomadal board was in all its glory; what do you say, Mr. Dean?\"\n\n\"An old man, my lord, never likes changes,\" said the dean.\n\n\"You must have been sad bunglers if it is so,\" said the archdeacon; \"and indeed, to tell the truth, I think you have bungled it. At any rate, you must own this; you have not done the half what you boasted you would do.\"\n\n\"Now, as regards your system of professors--\" began the chancellor slowly. He was never destined to get beyond such beginning.\n\n\"Talking of professors,\" said a soft clear voice, close behind the chancellor's elbow; \"how much you Englishmen might learn from Germany; only you are all too proud.\"\n\nThe bishop, looking round, perceived that that abominable young Stanhope had pursued him. The dean stared at him as though he were some unearthly apparition; so also did two or three prebendaries and minor canons. The archdeacon laughed.\n\n\"The German professors are men of learning,\" said Mr. Harding, \"but--\"\n\n\"German professors!\" groaned out the chancellor, as though his nervous system had received a shock which nothing but a week of Oxford air could cure.\n\n\"Yes,\" continued Ethelbert, not at all understanding why a German professor should be contemptible in the eyes of an Oxford don. \"Not but what the name is best earned at Oxford. In Germany the professors do teach; at Oxford, I believe, they only profess to do so, and sometimes not even that. You'll have those universities of yours about your ears soon, if you don't consent to take a lesson from Germany.\"\n\nThere was no answering this. Dignified clergymen of sixty years of age could not condescend to discuss such a matter with a young man with such clothes and such a beard.\n\n\"Have you got good water out at Plumstead, Mr. Archdeacon?\" said the bishop by way of changing the conversation.\n\n\"Pretty good,\" said Dr. Grantly.\n\n\"But by no means so good as his wine, my lord,\" said a witty minor canon.\n\n\"Nor so generally used,\" said another; \"that is, for inward application.\"\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" laughed the bishop, \"a good cellar of wine is a very comfortable thing in a house.\"\n\n\"Your German professors, Sir, prefer beer, I believe,\" said the sarcastic little meagre prebendary.\n\n\"They don't think much of either,\" said Ethelbert, \"and that perhaps accounts for their superiority. Now the Jewish professor--\"\n\nThe insult was becoming too deep for the spirit of Oxford to endure, so the archdeacon walked off one way and the chancellor another, followed by their disciples, and the bishop and the young reformer were left together on the hearth-rug.\n\n\"I was a Jew once myself,\" began Bertie.\n\nThe bishop was determined not to stand another examination, or be led on any terms into Palestine, so he again remembered that he had to do something very particular, and left young Stanhope with the dean. The dean did not get the worst of it for Ethelbert gave him a true account of his remarkable doings in the Holy Land.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Harding,\" said the bishop, overtaking the ci-devant warden; \"I wanted to say one word about the hospital. You know, of course, that it is to be filled up.\"\n\nMr. Harding's heart beat a little, and he said that he had heard so.\n\n\"Of course,\" continued the bishop; \"there can be only one man whom I could wish to see in that situation. I don't know what your own views may be, Mr. Harding--\"\n\n\"They are very simply told, my lord,\" said the other; \"to take the place if it be offered me, and to put up with the want of it should another man get it.\"\n\nThe bishop professed himself delighted to hear it; Mr. Harding might be quite sure that no other man would get it. There were some few circumstances which would in a slight degree change the nature of the duties. Mr. Harding was probably aware of this, and would, perhaps, not object to discuss the matter with Mr. Slope. It was a subject to which Mr. Slope had given a good deal of attention.\n\nMr. Harding felt, he knew not why, oppressed and annoyed. What could Mr. Slope do to him? He knew that there were to be changes. The nature of them must be communicated to the warden through somebody, and through whom so naturally as the bishop's chaplain? 'Twas thus he tried to argue himself back to an easy mind, but in vain.\n\nMr. Slope in the meantime had taken the seat which the bishop had vacated on the signora's sofa, and remained with that lady till it was time to marshal the folk to supper. Not with contented eyes had Mrs. Proudie seen this. Had not this woman laughed at her distress, and had not Mr. Slope heard it? Was she not an intriguing Italian woman, half wife and half not, full of affectation, airs, and impudence? Was she not horribly bedizened with velvet and pearls, with velvet and pearls, too, which had not been torn off her back? Above all, did she not pretend to be more beautiful than her neighbours? To say that Mrs. Proudie was jealous would give a wrong idea of her feelings. She had not the slightest desire that Mr. Slope should be in love with herself. But she desired the incense of Mr. Slope's spiritual and temporal services, and did not choose that they should be turned out of their course to such an object as Signora Neroni. She considered also that Mr. Slope ought in duty to hate the signora, and it appeared from his manner that he was very far from hating her.\n\n\"Come, Mr. Slope,\" she said, sweeping by and looking all that she felt, \"can't you make yourself useful? Do pray take Mrs. Grantly down to supper.\"\n\nMrs. Grantly heard and escaped. The words were hardly out of Mrs. Proudie's mouth before the intended victim had stuck her hand through the arm of one of her husband's curates and saved herself. What would the archdeacon have said had he seen her walking downstairs with Mr. Slope?\n\nMr. Slope heard also, but was by no means so obedient as was expected. Indeed, the period of Mr. Slope's obedience to Mrs. Proudie was drawing to a close. He did not wish yet to break with her, nor to break with her at all, if it could be avoided. But he intended to be master in that palace, and as she had made the same resolution it was not improbable that they might come to blows.\n\nBefore leaving the signora he arranged a little table before her and begged to know what he should bring her. She was quite indifferent, she said--nothing--anything. It was now she felt the misery of her position, now that she must be left alone. Well, a little chicken, some ham, and a glass of champagne.\n\nMr. Slope had to explain, not without blushing for his patron, that there was no champagne.\n\nSherry would do just as well. And then Mr. Slope descended with the learned Miss Trefoil on his arm. Could she tell him, he asked, whether the ferns of Barsetshire were equal to those of Cumberland? His strongest worldly passion was for ferns--and before she could answer him he left her wedged between the door and the sideboard. It was fifty minutes before she escaped, and even then unfed.\n\n\"You are not leaving us, Mr. Slope,\" said the watchful lady of the house, seeing her slave escaping towards the door, with stores of provisions held high above the heads of the guests.\n\nMr. Slope explained that the Signora Neroni was in want of her supper.\n\n\"Pray, Mr. Slope, let her brother take it to her,\" said Mrs. Proudie, quite out loud. \"It is out of the question that you should be so employed. Pray, Mr. Slope, oblige me; I am sure Mr. Stanhope will wait upon his sister.\"\n\nEthelbert was most agreeably occupied in the furthest corner of the room, making himself both useful and agreeable to Mrs. Proudie's youngest daughter.\n\n\"I couldn't get out, madam, if Madeline were starving for her supper,\" said he; \"I'm physically fixed, unless I could fly.\"\n\nThe lady's anger was increased by seeing that her daughter also had gone over to the enemy, and when she saw, that in spite of her remonstrances, in the teeth of her positive orders, Mr. Slope went off to the drawing-room, the cup of her indignation ran over, and she could not restrain herself. \"Such manners I never saw,\" she said, muttering. \"I cannot and will not permit it;\" and then, after fussing and fuming for a few minutes, she pushed her way through the crowd and followed Mr. Slope.\n\nWhen she reached the room above, she found it absolutely deserted, except by the guilty pair. The signora was sitting very comfortably up to her supper, and Mr. Slope was leaning over her and administering to her wants. They had been discussing the merits of Sabbath-day schools, and the lady had suggested that as she could not possibly go to the children, she might be indulged in the wish of her heart by having the children brought to her.\n\n\"And when shall it be, Mr. Slope?\" said she.\n\nMr. Slope was saved the necessity of committing himself to a promise by the entry of Mrs. Proudie. She swept close up to the sofa so as to confront the guilty pair, stared full at them for a moment, and then said, as she passed on to the next room, \"Mr. Slope, his lordship is especially desirous of your attendance below; you will greatly oblige me if you will join him.\" And so she stalked on.\n\nMr. Slope muttered something in reply, and prepared to go downstairs. As for the bishop's wanting him, he knew his lady patroness well enough to take that assertion at what it was worth; but he did not wish to make himself the hero of a scene, or to become conspicuous for more gallantry than the occasion required.\n\n\"Is she always like this?\" said the signora.\n\n\"Yes--always--madam,\" said Mrs. Proudie, returning; \"always the same--always equally adverse to impropriety of conduct of every description;\" and she stalked back through the room again, following Mr. Slope out of the door.\n\nThe signora couldn't follow her, or she certainly would have done so. But she laughed loud, and sent the sound of it ringing through the lobby and down the stairs after Mrs. Proudie's feet. Had she been as active as Grimaldi, she could probably have taken no better revenge.\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" said Mrs. Proudie, catching the delinquent at the door, \"I am surprised you should leave my company to attend on such a painted Jezebel as that.\"\n\n\"But she's lame, Mrs. Proudie, and cannot move. Somebody must have waited upon her.\"\n\n\"Lame,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"I'd lame her if she belonged to me. What business had she here at all?--such impertinence--such affectation.\"\n\nIn the hall and adjacent rooms all manner of cloaking and shawling was going on, and the Barchester folk were getting themselves gone. Mrs. Proudie did her best to smirk at each and every one as they made their adieux, but she was hardly successful. Her temper had been tried fearfully. By slow degrees the guests went.\n\n\"Send back the carriage quick,\" said Ethelbert, as Dr. and Mrs. Stanhope took their departure.\n\nThe younger Stanhopes were left to the very last, and an uncomfortable party they made with the bishop's family. They all went into the dining-room, and then the bishop observing that \"the lady\" was alone in the drawing-room, they followed him up. Mrs. Proudie kept Mr. Slope and her daughters in close conversation, resolving that he should not be indulged, nor they polluted. The bishop, in mortal dread of Bertie and the Jews, tried to converse with Charlotte Stanhope about the climate of Italy. Bertie and the signora had no resource but in each other.\n\n\"Did you get your supper at last, Madeline?\" said the impudent or else mischievous young man.\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said Madeline; \"Mr. Slope was so very kind as to bring it me. I fear, however, he put himself to more inconvenience than I wished.\"\n\nMrs. Proudie looked at her but said nothing. The meaning of her look might have been thus translated; \"If ever you find yourself within these walls again, I'll give you leave to be as impudent and affected and as mischievous as you please.\"\n\nAt last the carriage returned with the three Italian servants, and La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni was carried out, as she had been carried in.\n\nThe lady of the palace retired to her chamber by no means contented with the result of her first grand party at Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. Slope versus Harding\n\nTwo or three days after the party, Mr. Harding received a note begging him to call on Mr. Slope, at the palace, at an early hour on the following morning. There was nothing uncivil in the communication, and yet the tone of it was thoroughly displeasing. It was as follows:\n\nWill you favour me by calling on me at the palace to-morrow morning at 9:30 A.M. The bishop wishes me to speak to you touching the hospital. I hope you will excuse my naming so early an hour. I do so as my time is greatly occupied. If, however, it is positively inconvenient to you, I will change it to 10. You will, perhaps, be kind enough to let me have a note in reply.\n\nBelieve me to be, My dear Mr. Harding, Your assured friend, OBH. SLOPE\n\nThe Palace, Monday morning, 20th August, 185--\n\nMr. Harding neither could nor would believe anything of the sort, and he thought, moreover, that Mr. Slope was rather impertinent to call himself by such a name. His assured friend, indeed! How many assured friends generally fall to the lot of a man in this world? And by what process are they made? And how much of such process had taken place as yet between Mr. Harding and Mr. Slope? Mr. Harding could not help asking himself these questions as he read and re-read the note before him. He answered it, however, as follows:\n\nI will call at the palace to-morrow at 9:30 A.M. as you desire.\n\nTruly yours,\n\nHigh Street, Barchester, Monday\n\nAnd on the following morning, punctually at half-past nine, he knocked at the palace door and asked for Mr. Slope.\n\nThe bishop had one small room allotted to him on the ground-floor, and Mr. Slope had another. Into this latter Mr. Harding was shown and asked to sit down. Mr. Slope was not yet there. The ex-warden stood up at the window looking into the garden, and could not help thinking how very short a time had passed since the whole of that house had been open to him, as though he had been a child of the family, born and bred in it. He remembered how the old servants used to smile as they opened the door to him; how the familiar butler would say, when he had been absent a few hours longer than usual, \"A sight of you, Mr. Harding, is good for sore eyes;\" how the fussy housekeeper would swear that he couldn't have dined, or couldn't have breakfasted, or couldn't have lunched. And then, above all, he remembered the pleasant gleam of inward satisfaction which always spread itself over the old bishop's face whenever his friend entered his room.\n\nA tear came into each eye as he reflected that all this was gone. What use would the hospital be to him now? He was alone in the world, and getting old; he would soon, very soon have to go and leave it all, as his dear old friend had gone; go, and leave the hospital, and his accustomed place in the cathedral, and his haunts and pleasures, to younger and perhaps wiser men. That chanting of his! Perhaps, in truth, the time for it was gone by. He felt as though the world were sinking from his feet; as though this, this was the time for him to turn with confidence to those hopes which he had preached with confidence to others. \"What,\" said he to himself, \"can a man's religion be worth if it does not support him against the natural melancholy of declining years?\" And as he looked out through his dimmed eyes into the bright parterres of the bishop's garden, he felt that he had the support which he wanted.\n\nNevertheless, he did not like to be thus kept waiting. If Mr. Slope did not really wish to see him at half-past nine o'clock, why force him to come away from his lodgings with his breakfast in his throat? To tell the truth, it was policy on the part of Mr. Slope. Mr. Slope had made up his mind that Mr. Harding should either accept the hospital with abject submission, or else refuse it altogether, and had calculated that he would probably be more quick to do the latter, if he could be got to enter upon the subject in an ill-humour. Perhaps Mr. Slope was not altogether wrong in his calculation.\n\nIt was nearly ten when Mr. Slope hurried into the room and, muttering something about the bishop and diocesan duties, shook Mr. Harding's hand ruthlessly and begged him to be seated.\n\nNow the air of superiority which this man assumed did go against the grain with Mr. Harding, and yet he did not know how to resent it. The whole tendency of his mind and disposition was opposed to any contra-assumption of grandeur on his own part, and he hadn't the worldly spirit or quickness necessary to put down insolent pretensions by downright and open rebuke, as the archdeacon would have done. There was nothing for Mr. Harding but to submit, and he accordingly did so.\n\n\"About the hospital, Mr. Harding?\" began Mr. Slope, speaking of it as the head of a college at Cambridge might speak of some sizarship which had to be disposed of.\n\nMr. Harding crossed one leg over another, and then one hand over the other on the top of them, and looked Mr. Slope in the face; but he said nothing.\n\n\"It's to be filled up again,\" said Mr. Slope. Mr. Harding said that he had understood so.\n\n\"Of course, you know, the income will be very much reduced,\" continued Mr. Slope. \"The bishop wished to be liberal, and he therefore told the government that he thought it ought to be put at not less than £450. I think on the whole the bishop was right, for though the services required will not be of a very onerous nature, they will be more so than they were before. And it is, perhaps, well that the clergy immediately attached to the cathedral town should be made as comfortable as the extent of the ecclesiastical means at our disposal will allow. Those are the bishop's ideas, and I must say mine also.\"\n\nMr. Harding sat rubbing one hand on the other, but said not a word.\n\n\"So much for the income, Mr. Harding. The house will, of course, remain to the warden, as before. It should, however, I think, be stipulated that he should paint inside every seven years, and outside every three years, and be subject to dilapidations, in the event of vacating, either by death or otherwise. But this is a matter on which the bishop must yet be consulted.\"\n\nMr. Harding still rubbed his hands and still sat silent, gazing up into Mr. Slope's unprepossessing face.\n\n\"Then, as to the duties,\" continued he, \"I believe, if I am rightly informed, there can hardly be said to have been any duties hitherto,\" and he gave a sort of half-laugh, as though to pass off the accusation in the guise of a pleasantry.\n\nMr. Harding thought of the happy, easy years he had passed in his old home; of the worn-out, aged men whom he had succoured; of his good intentions; and of his work, which had certainly been of the lightest. He thought of these things, doubting for a moment whether he did or did not deserve the sarcasm. He gave his enemy the benefit of the doubt, and did not rebuke him. He merely observed, very tranquilly, and perhaps with too much humility, that the duties of the situation, such as they were, had, he believed, been done to the satisfaction of the late bishop.\n\nMr. Slope again smiled, and this time the smile was intended to operate against the memory of the late bishop rather than against the energy of the ex-warden; so it was understood by Mr. Harding. The colour rose to his cheeks, and he began to feel very angry.\n\n\"You must be aware, Mr. Harding, that things are a good deal changed in Barchester,\" said Mr. Slope.\n\nMr. Harding said that he was aware of it. \"And not only in Barchester, Mr. Harding, but in the world at large. It is not only in Barchester that a new man is carrying out new measures and casting away the useless rubbish of past centuries. The same thing is going on throughout the country. Work is now required from every man who receives wages, and they who have to superintend the doing of work, and the paying of wages, are bound to see that this rule is carried out. New men, Mr. Harding, are now needed and are now forthcoming in the church, as well as in other professions.\"\n\nAll this was wormwood to our old friend. He had never rated very high his own abilities or activity, but all the feelings of his heart were with the old clergy, and any antipathies of which his heart was susceptible were directed against those new, busy, uncharitable, self-lauding men, of whom Mr. Slope was so good an example.\n\n\"Perhaps,\" said he, \"the bishop will prefer a new man at the hospital?\"\n\n\"By no means,\" said Mr. Slope. \"The bishop is very anxious that you should accept the appointment, but he wishes you should understand beforehand what will be the required duties. In the first place, a Sabbath-day school will be attached to the hospital.\"\n\n\"What! For the old men?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"No, Mr. Harding, not for the old men, but for the benefit of the children of such of the poor of Barchester as it may suit. The bishop will expect that you shall attend this school, and that the teachers shall be under your inspection and care.\"\n\nMr. Harding slipped his topmost hand off the other and began to rub the calf of the leg which was supported.\n\n\"As to the old men,\" continued Mr. Slope, \"and the old women who are to form a part of the hospital, the bishop is desirous that you shall have morning and evening service on the premises every Sabbath, and one weekday service; that you shall preach to them once at least on Sundays; and that the whole hospital be always collected for morning and evening prayer. The bishop thinks that this will render it unnecessary that any separate seats in the cathedral should be reserved for the hospital inmates.\"\n\nMr. Slope paused, but Mr. Harding still said nothing.\n\n\"Indeed, it would be difficult to find seats for the women; on the whole, Mr. Harding, I may as well say at once, that for people of that class the cathedral service does not appear to me the most useful--even if it be so for any class of people.\"\n\n\"We will not discuss that, if you please,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"I am not desirous of doing so; at least, not at the present moment. I hope, however, you fully understand the bishop's wishes about the new establishment of the hospital; and if, as I do not doubt, I shall receive from you an assurance that you accord with his lordship's views, it will give me very great pleasure to be the bearer from his lordship to you of the presentation to the appointment.\"\n\n\"But if I disagree with his lordship's views?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"But I hope you do not,\" said Mr. Slope.\n\n\"But if I do?\" again asked the other.\n\n\"If such unfortunately should be the case, which I can hardly conceive, I presume your own feelings will dictate to you the propriety of declining the appointment.\"\n\n\"But if I accept the appointment and yet disagree with the bishop, what then?\"\n\nThis question rather bothered Mr. Slope. It was true that he had talked the matter over with the bishop and had received a sort of authority for suggesting to Mr. Harding the propriety of a Sunday school and certain hospital services, but he had no authority for saying that these propositions were to be made peremptory conditions attached to the appointment. The bishop's idea had been that Mr. Harding would of course consent and that the school would become, like the rest of those new establishments in the city, under the control of his wife and his chaplain. Mr. Slope's idea had been more correct. He intended that Mr. Harding should refuse the situation, and that an ally of his own should get it, but he had not conceived the possibility of Mr. Harding openly accepting the appointment and as openly rejecting the conditions.\n\n\"It is not, I presume, probable,\" said he, \"that you will accept from the hands of the bishop a piece of preferment with a fixed predetermination to disacknowledge the duties attached to it.\"\n\n\"If I become warden,\" said Mr. Harding, \"and neglect my duty, the bishop has means by which he can remedy the grievance.\"\n\n\"I hardly expected such an argument from you, or I may say the suggestion of such a line of conduct,\" said Mr. Slope with a great look of injured virtue.\n\n\"Nor did I expect such a proposition.\"\n\n\"I shall be glad at any rate to know what answer I am to make to his lordship,\" said Mr. Slope.\n\n\"I will take an early opportunity of seeing his lordship myself,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Such an arrangement,\" said Mr. Slope, \"will hardly give his lordship satisfaction. Indeed, it is impossible that the bishop should himself see every clergyman in the diocese on every subject of patronage that may arise. The bishop, I believe, did see you on the matter, and I really cannot see why he should be troubled to do so again.\"\n\n\"Do you know, Mr. Slope, how long I have been officiating as a clergyman in this city?\" Mr. Slope's wish was now nearly fulfilled. Mr. Harding had become angry, and it was probable that he might commit himself.\n\n\"I really do not see what that has to do with the question. You cannot think the bishop would be justified in allowing you to regard as a sinecure a situation that requires an active, man merely because you have been employed for many years in the cathedral.\"\n\n\"But it might induce the bishop to see me, if I asked him to do so. I shall consult my friends in this matter, Mr. Slope; but I mean to be guilty of no subterfuge--you may tell the bishop that as I altogether disagree with his views about the hospital, I shall decline the situation if I find that any such conditions are attached to it as those you have suggested;\" and so saying, Mr. Harding took his hat and went his way.\n\nMr. Slope was contented. He considered himself at liberty to accept Mr. Harding's last speech as an absolute refusal of the appointment. At least, he so represented it to the bishop and to Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"That is very surprising,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"Not at all,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"you little know how determined the whole set of them are to withstand your authority.\"\n\n\"But Mr. Harding was so anxious for it,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Mr. Slope, \"if he can hold it without the slightest acknowledgement of your lordship's jurisdiction.\"\n\n\"That is out of the question,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"I should imagine it to be quite so,\" said the chaplain.\n\n\"Indeed, I should think so,\" said the lady.\n\n\"I really am sorry for it,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"I don't know that there is much cause for sorrow,\" said the lady. \"Mr. Quiverful is a much more deserving man, more in need of it, and one who will make himself much more useful in the close neighbourhood of the palace.\"\n\n\"I suppose I had better see Quiverful?\" said the chaplain.\n\n\"I suppose you had,\" said the bishop.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. The Rubbish Cart\n\nMr. Harding was not a happy man as he walked down the palace pathway and stepped out into the close. His preferment and pleasant house were a second time gone from him, but that he could endure. He had been schooled and insulted by a man young enough to be his son, but that he could put up with. He could even draw from the very injuries which had been inflicted on him some of that consolation which we may believe martyrs always receive from the injustice of their own sufferings, and which is generally proportioned in its strength to the extent of cruelty with which martyrs are treated. He had admitted to his daughter that he wanted the comfort of his old home, and yet he could have returned to his lodgings in the High Street, if not with exaltation, at least with satisfaction, had that been all. But the venom of the chaplain's harangue had worked into his blood, and sapped the life of his sweet contentment.\n\n\"New men are carrying out new measures and are carting away the useless rubbish of past centuries!\" What cruel words these had been; and how often are they now used with all the heartless cruelty of a Slope! A man is sufficiently condemned if it can only be shown that either in politics or religion he does not belong to some new school established within the last score of years. He may then regard himself as rubbish and expect to be carted away. A man is nothing now unless he has within him a full appreciation of the new era, an era in which it would seem that neither honesty nor truth is very desirable, but in which success is the only touchstone of merit. We must laugh at everything that is established. Let the joke be ever so bad, ever so untrue to the real principles of joking; nevertheless we must laugh--or else beware the cart. We must talk, think, and live up to the spirit of the times, and write up to it too, if that cacoethes be upon us, or else we are nought. New men and new measures, long credit and few scruples, great success or wonderful ruin, such are now the tastes of Englishmen who know how to live. Alas, alas! Under such circumstances Mr. Harding could not but feel that he was an Englishman who did not know how to live. This new doctrine of Mr. Slope and the rubbish cart, new at least at Barchester, sadly disturbed his equanimity.\n\n\"The same thing is going on throughout the whole country! Work is now required from every man who receives wages!\" And had he been living all his life receiving wages and doing no work? Had he in truth so lived as to be now in his old age justly reckoned as rubbish fit only to be hidden away in some huge dust-hole? The school of men to whom he professes to belong, the Grantlys, the Gwynnes, and the old high set of Oxford divines, are afflicted with no such self-accusations as these which troubled Mr. Harding. They, as a rule, are as satisfied with the wisdom and propriety of their own conduct as can be any Mr. Slope, or any Dr. Proudie, with his own. But unfortunately for himself Mr. Harding had little of this self-reliance. When he heard himself designated as rubbish by the Slopes of the world, he had no other resource than to make inquiry within his own bosom as to the truth of the designation. Alas, alas! The evidence seemed generally to go against him.\n\nHe had professed to himself in the bishop's parlour that in these coming sources of the sorrow of age, in these fits of sad regret from which the latter years of few reflecting men can be free, religion would suffice to comfort him. Yes, religion could console him for the loss of any worldly good, but was his religion of that active sort which would enable him so to repent of misspent years as to pass those that were left to him in a spirit of hope for the future? And such repentance itself, is it not a work of agony and of tears? It is very easy to talk of repentance, but a man has to walk over hot ploughshares before he can complete it; to be skinned alive as was St. Bartholomew; to be stuck full of arrows as was St. Sebastian; to lie broiling on a gridiron like St. Lorenzo! How if his past life required such repentance as this? Had he the energy to go through with it?\n\nMr. Harding, after leaving the palace, walked slowly for an hour or so beneath the shady elms of the close and then betook himself to his daughter's house. He had at any rate made up his mind that he would go out to Plumstead to consult Dr. Grantly, and that he would in the first instance tell Eleanor what had occurred.\n\nAnd now he was doomed to undergo another misery. Mr. Slope had forestalled him at the widow's house. He had called there on the preceding afternoon. He could not, he had said, deny himself the pleasure of telling Mrs. Bold that her father was about to return to the pretty house at Hiram's Hospital. He had been instructed by the bishop to inform Mr. Harding that the appointment would now be made at once. The bishop was of course only too happy to be able to be the means of restoring to Mr. Harding the preferment which he had so long adorned. And then by degrees Mr. Slope had introduced the subject of the pretty school which he hoped before long to see attached to the hospital. He had quite fascinated Mrs. Bold by his description of this picturesque, useful, and charitable appendage, and she had gone so far as to say that she had no doubt her father would approve, and that she herself would gladly undertake a class.\n\nAnyone who had heard the entirely different tone and seen the entirely different manner in which Mr. Slope had spoken of this projected institution to the daughter and to the father could not have failed to own that Mr. Slope was a man of genius. He said nothing to Mrs. Bold about the hospital sermons and services, nothing about the exclusion of the old men from the cathedral, nothing about dilapidation and painting, nothing about carting away the rubbish. Eleanor had said to herself that certainly she did not like Mr. Slope personally, but that he was a very active, zealous clergyman and would no doubt be useful in Barchester. All this paved the way for much additional misery to Mr. Harding.\n\nEleanor put on her happiest face as she heard her father on the stairs, for she thought she had only to congratulate him; but directly she saw his face she knew that there was but little matter for congratulation. She had seen him with the same weary look of sorrow on one or two occasions before, and remembered it well. She had seen him when he first read that attack upon himself in \"The Jupiter\" which had ultimately caused him to resign the hospital, and she had seen him also when the archdeacon had persuaded him to remain there against his own sense of propriety and honour. She knew at a glance that his spirit was in deep trouble.\n\n\"Oh, Papa, what is it?\" said she, putting down her boy to crawl upon the floor.\n\n\"I came to tell you, my dear,\" said he, \"that I am going out to Plumstead: you won't come with me, I suppose?\"\n\n\"To Plumstead, Papa? Shall you stay there?\"\n\n\"I suppose I shall, to-night: I must consult the archdeacon about this weary hospital. Ah me! I wish I had never thought of it again.\"\n\n\"Why, Papa, what is the matter?\"\n\n\"I've been with Mr. Slope, my dear, and he isn't the pleasantest companion in the world, at least not to me.\" Eleanor gave a sort of half-blush, but she was wrong if she imagined that her father in any way alluded to her acquaintance with Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Well, Papa.\"\n\n\"He wants to turn the hospital into a Sunday-school and a preaching-house, and I suppose he will have his way. I do not feel myself adapted for such an establishment, and therefore, I suppose, I must refuse the appointment.\"\n\n\"What would be the harm of the school, Papa?\"\n\n\"The want of a proper schoolmaster, my dear.\"\n\n\"But that would of course be supplied.\"\n\n\"Mr. Slope wishes to supply it by making me his schoolmaster. But as I am hardly fit for such work, I intend to decline.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa! Mr. Slope doesn't intend that. He was here yesterday, and what he intends--\"\n\n\"He was here yesterday, was he?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Yes, Papa.\"\n\n\"And talking about the hospital?\"\n\n\"He was saying how glad he would be, and the bishop too, to see you back there again. And then he spoke about the Sunday-school; and to tell the truth I agreed with him; and I thought you would have done so too. Mr. Slope spoke of a school, not inside the hospital, but just connected with it, of which you would be the patron and visitor; and I thought you would have liked such a school as that; and I promised to look after it and to take a class--and it all seemed so very--. But, oh, Papa! I shall be so miserable if I find I have done wrong.\"\n\n\"Nothing wrong at all, my dear,\" said he gently, very gently rejecting his daughter's caress. \"There can be nothing wrong in your wishing to make yourself useful; indeed, you ought to do so by all means. Everyone must now exert himself who would not choose to go to the wall.\" Poor Mr. Harding thus attempted in his misery to preach the new doctrine to his child. \"Himself or herself, it's all the same,\" he continued; \"you will be quite right, my dear, to do something of this sort; but--\"\n\n\"Well, Papa.\"\n\n\"I am not quite sure that if I were you I would select Mr. Slope for my guide.\"\n\n\"But I never have done so and never shall.\"\n\n\"It would be very wicked of me to speak evil of him, for to tell the truth I know no evil of him; but I am not quite sure that he is honest. That he is not gentlemanlike in his manners, of that I am quite sure.\"\n\n\"I never thought of taking him for my guide, Papa.\"\n\n\"As for myself, my dear,\" continued he, \"we know the old proverb--'It's bad teaching an old dog tricks.' I must decline the Sunday-school, and shall therefore probably decline the hospital also. But I will first see your brother-in-law.\" So he took up his hat, kissed the baby, and withdrew, leaving Eleanor in as low spirits as himself.\n\nAll this was a great aggravation to his misery. He had so few with whom to sympathize that he could not afford to be cut off from the one whose sympathy was of the most value to him. And yet it seemed probable that this would be the case. He did not own to himself that he wished his daughter to hate Mr. Slope, yet had she expressed such a feeling there would have been very little bitterness in the rebuke he would have given her for so uncharitable a state of mind. The fact, however, was that she was on friendly terms with Mr. Slope, that she coincided with his views, adhered at once to his plans, and listened with delight to his teaching. Mr. Harding hardly wished his daughter to hate the man, but he would have preferred that to her loving him.\n\nHe walked away to the inn to order a fly, went home to put up his carpet-bag, and then started for Plumstead. There was, at any rate, no danger that the archdeacon would fraternize with Mr. Slope; but then he would recommend internecine war, public appeals, loud reproaches, and all the paraphernalia of open battle. Now that alternative was hardly more to Mr. Harding's taste than the other.\n\nWhen Mr. Harding reached the parsonage, he found that the archdeacon was out, and would not be home till dinnertime, so he began his complaint to his elder daughter. Mrs. Grantly entertained quite as strong an antagonism to Mr. Slope as did her husband; she was also quite as alive to the necessity of combating the Proudie faction, of supporting the old church interest of the close, of keeping in her own set such of the loaves and fishes as duly belonged to it; and was quite as well prepared as her lord to carry on the battle without giving or taking quarter. Not that she was a woman prone to quarrelling, or ill-inclined to live at peace with her clerical neighbours; but she felt, as did the archdeacon, that the presence of Mr. Slope in Barchester was an insult to everyone connected with the late bishop, and that his assumed dominion in the diocese was a spiritual injury to her husband. Hitherto people had little guessed how bitter Mrs. Grantly could be. She lived on the best of terms with all the rectors' wives around her. She had been popular with all the ladies connected with the close. Though much the wealthiest of the ecclesiastical matrons of the county, she had so managed her affairs that her carriage and horses had given umbrage to none. She had never thrown herself among the county grandees so as to excite the envy of other clergymen's wives. She never talked too loudly of earls and countesses, or boasted that she gave her governess sixty pounds a year, or her cook seventy. Mrs. Grantly had lived the life of a wise, discreet, peace-making woman, and the people of Barchester were surprised at the amount of military vigour she displayed as general of the feminine Grantlyite forces.\n\nMrs. Grantly soon learned that her sister Eleanor had promised to assist Mr. Slope in the affairs of the hospital school, and it was on this point that her attention first fixed itself.\n\n\"How can Eleanor endure him?\" said she.\n\n\"He is a very crafty man,\" said her father, \"and his craft has been successful in making Eleanor think that he is a meek, charitable, good clergyman. God forgive me, if I wrong him, but such is not his true character in my opinion.\"\n\n\"His true character, indeed!\" said she, with something approaching scorn for her father's moderation. \"I only hope he won't have craft enough to make Eleanor forget herself and her position.\"\n\n\"Do you mean marry him?\" said he, startled out of his usual demeanour by the abruptness and horror of so dreadful a proposition.\n\n\"What is there so improbable in it? Of course that would be his own object if he thought he had any chance of success. Eleanor has a thousand a year entirely at her own disposal, and what better fortune could fall to Mr. Slope's lot than the transferring of the disposal of such a fortune to himself?\"\n\n\"But you can't think she likes him, Susan?\"\n\n\"Why not?\" said Susan. \"Why shouldn't she like him? He's just the sort of man to get on with a woman left, as she is, with no one to look after her.\"\n\n\"Look after her!\" said the unhappy father; \"don't we look after her?\"\n\n\"Ah, Papa, how innocent you are! Of course it was to be expected that Eleanor should marry again. I should be the last to advise her against it, if she would only wait the proper time, and then marry at least a gentleman.\"\n\n\"But you don't really mean to say that you suppose Eleanor has ever thought of marrying Mr. Slope? Why, Mr. Bold has only been dead a year.\"\n\n\"Eighteen months,\" said his daughter. \"But I don't suppose Eleanor has ever thought about it. It is very probable, though, that he has; and that he will try and make her do so; and that he will succeed too, if we don't take care what we are about.\"\n\nThis was quite a new phase of the affair to poor Mr. Harding. To have thrust upon him as his son-in-law, as the husband of his favourite child, the only man in the world whom he really positively disliked, would be a misfortune which he felt he would not know how to endure patiently. But then, could there be any ground for so dreadful a surmise? In all worldly matters he was apt to look upon the opinion of his eldest daughter as one generally sound and trustworthy. In her appreciation of character, of motives, and the probable conduct both of men and women, she was usually not far wrong. She had early foreseen the marriage of Eleanor and John Bold; she had at a glance deciphered the character of the new bishop and his chaplain; could it possibly be that her present surmise should ever come forth as true?\n\n\"But you don't think that she likes him?\" said Mr. Harding again.\n\n\"Well, Papa, I can't say that I think she dislikes him as she ought to do. Why is he visiting there as a confidential friend, when he never ought to have been admitted inside the house? Why is it that she speaks to him about your welfare and your position, as she clearly has done? At the bishop's party the other night I saw her talking to him for half an hour at the stretch.\"\n\n\"I thought Mr. Slope seemed to talk to nobody there but that daughter of Stanhope's,\" said Mr. Harding, wishing to defend his child.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Slope is a cleverer man than you think of, Papa, and keeps more than one iron in the fire.\"\n\nTo give Eleanor her due, any suspicion as to the slightest inclination on her part towards Mr. Slope was a wrong to her. She had no more idea of marrying Mr. Slope than she had of marrying the bishop, and the idea that Mr. Slope would present himself as a suitor had never occurred to her. Indeed, to give her her due again, she had never thought about suitors since her husband's death. But nevertheless it was true that she had overcome all that repugnance to the man which was so strongly felt for him by the rest of the Grantly faction. She had forgiven him his sermon. She had forgiven him his Low Church tendencies, his Sabbath-schools, and puritanical observances. She had forgiven his pharisaical arrogance, and even his greasy face and oily, vulgar manners. Having agreed to overlook such offences as these, why should she not in time be taught to regard Mr. Slope as a suitor?\n\nAnd as to him, it must also be affirmed that he was hitherto equally innocent of the crime imputed to him. How it had come to pass that a man whose eyes were generally so widely open to everything around him had not perceived that this young widow was rich as well as beautiful, cannot probably now be explained. But such was the fact. Mr. Slope had ingratiated himself with Mrs. Bold, merely as he had done with other ladies, in order to strengthen his party in the city. He subsequently amended his error, but it was not till after the interview between him and Mr. Harding.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. The New Champion\n\nThe archdeacon did not return to the parsonage till close upon the hour of dinner, and there was therefore no time to discuss matters before that important ceremony. He seemed to be in an especial good humour, and welcomed his father-in-law with a sort of jovial earnestness that was usual with him when things on which he was intent were going on as he would have them.\n\n\"It's all settled, my dear,\" said he to his wife as he washed his hands in his dressing-room, while she, according to her wont, sat listening in the bedroom; \"Arabin has agreed to accept the living. He'll be here next week.\" And the archdeacon scrubbed his hands and rubbed his face with a violent alacrity, which showed that Arabin's coming was a great point gained.\n\n\"Will he come here to Plumstead?\" said the wife.\n\n\"He has promised to stay a month with us,\" said the archdeacon, \"so that he may see what his parish is like. You'll like Arabin very much. He's a gentleman in every respect, and full of humour.\"\n\n\"He's very queer, isn't he?\" asked the lady.\n\n\"Well--he is a little odd in some of his fancies, but there's nothing about him you won't like. He is as staunch a churchman as there is at Oxford. I really don't know what we should do without Arabin. It's a great thing for me to have him so near me, and if anything can put Slope down, Arabin will do it.\"\n\nThe Reverend Francis Arabin was a fellow of Lazarus, the favoured disciple of the great Dr. Gwynne, a High Churchman at all points--so high, indeed, that at one period of his career he had all but toppled over into the cesspool of Rome--a poet and also a polemical writer, a great pet in the common-rooms at Oxford, an eloquent clergyman, a droll, odd, humorous, energetic, conscientious man, and, as the archdeacon had boasted of him, a thorough gentleman. As he will hereafter be brought more closely to our notice, it is now only necessary to add that he had just been presented to the vicarage of St. Ewold by Dr. Grantly, in whose gift as archdeacon the living lay. St. Ewold is a parish lying just without the city of Barchester. The suburbs of the new town, indeed, are partly within its precincts, and the pretty church and parsonage are not much above a mile distant from the city gate.\n\nSt. Ewold is not a rich piece of preferment--it is worth some three or four hundred a year at most, and has generally been held by a clergyman attached to the cathedral choir. The archdeacon, however, felt, when the living on this occasion became vacant, that it imperatively behoved him to aid the force of his party with some tower of strength, if any such tower could be got to occupy St. Ewold's. He had discussed the matter with his brethren in Barchester, not in any weak spirit as the holder of patronage to be used for his own or his family's benefit, but as one to whom was committed a trust on the due administration of which much of the church's welfare might depend. He had submitted to them the name of Mr. Arabin, as though the choice had rested with them all in conclave, and they had unanimously admitted that, if Mr. Arabin would accept St. Ewold's, no better choice could possibly be made.\n\nIf Mr. Arabin would accept St. Ewold's! There lay the difficulty. Mr. Arabin was a man standing somewhat prominently before the world, that is, before the Church of England world. He was not a rich man, it is true, for he held no preferment but his fellowship; but he was a man not over-anxious for riches, not married of course, and one whose time was greatly taken up in discussing, both in print and on platforms, the privileges and practices of the church to which he belonged. As the archdeacon had done battle for its temporalities, so did Mr. Arabin do battle for its spiritualities, and both had done so conscientiously; that is, not so much each for his own benefit as for that of others.\n\nHolding such a position as Mr. Arabin did, there was much reason to doubt whether he would consent to become the parson of St. Ewold's, and Dr. Grantly had taken the trouble to go himself to Oxford on the matter. Dr. Gwynne and Dr. Grantly together had succeeded in persuading this eminent divine that duty required him to go to Barchester. There were wheels within wheels in this affair. For some time past Mr. Arabin had been engaged in a tremendous controversy with no less a person than Mr. Slope, respecting the apostolic succession. These two gentlemen had never seen each other, but they had been extremely bitter in print. Mr. Slope had endeavoured to strengthen his cause by calling Mr. Arabin an owl, and Mr. Arabin had retaliated by hinting that Mr. Slope was an infidel. This battle had been commenced in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" a powerful newspaper, the manager of which was very friendly to Mr. Slope's view of the case. The matter, however, had become too tedious for the readers of \"The Jupiter,\" and a little note had therefore been appended to one of Mr. Slope's most telling rejoinders, in which it had been stated that no further letters from the reverend gentlemen could be inserted except as advertisements.\n\nOther methods of publication were, however, found, less expensive than advertisements in \"The Jupiter,\" and the war went on merrily. Mr. Slope declared that the main part of the consecration of a clergyman was the self-devotion of the inner man to the duties of the ministry. Mr. Arabin contended that a man was not consecrated at all, had, indeed, no single attribute of a clergyman, unless he became so through the imposition of some bishop's hands, who had become a bishop through the imposition of other hands, and so on in a direct line to one of the apostles. Each had repeatedly hung the other on the horns of a dilemma, but neither seemed to be a whit the worse for the hanging; and so the war went on merrily.\n\nWhether or no the near neighbourhood of the foe may have acted in any way as an inducement to Mr. Arabin to accept the living of St. Ewold, we will not pretend to say; but it had at any rate been settled in Dr. Gwynne's library, at Lazarus, that he would accept it, and that he would lend his assistance towards driving the enemy out of Barchester, or, at any rate, silencing him while he remained there. Mr. Arabin intended to keep his rooms at Oxford and to have the assistance of a curate at St. Ewold, but he promised to give as much time as possible to the neighbourhood of Barchester, and from so great a man Dr. Grantly was quite satisfied with such a promise. It was no small part of the satisfaction derivable from such an arrangement that Bishop Proudie would be forced to institute into a living immediately under his own nose the enemy of his favourite chaplain.\n\nAll through dinner the archdeacon's good humour shone brightly in his face. He ate of the good things heartily, he drank wine with his wife and daughter, he talked pleasantly of his doings at Oxford, told his father-in-law that he ought to visit Dr. Gwynne at Lazarus, and launched out again in praise of Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"Is Mr. Arabin married, Papa?\" asked Griselda.\n\n\"No, my dear, the fellow of a college is never married.\"\n\n\"Is he a young man, Papa?\"\n\n\"About forty, I believe,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Oh!\" said Griselda. Had her father said eighty, Mr. Arabin would not have appeared to her to be very much older.\n\nWhen the two gentlemen were left alone over their wine, Mr. Harding told his tale of woe. But even this, sad as it was, did not much diminish the archdeacon's good humour, though it greatly added to his pugnacity.\n\n\"He can't do it,\" said Dr. Grantly over and over again, as his father-in-law explained to him the terms on which the new warden of the hospital was to be appointed; \"he can't do it. What he says is not worth the trouble of listening to. He can't alter the duties of the place.\"\n\n\"Who can't?\" asked the ex-warden.\n\n\"Neither the bishop nor the chaplain, nor yet the bishop's wife, who, I take it, has really more to say to such matters than either of the other two. The whole body corporate of the palace together have no power to turn the warden of the hospital into a Sunday-schoolmaster.\"\n\n\"But the bishop has the power to appoint whom he pleases, and--\"\n\n\"I don't know that; I rather think he'll find he has no such power. Let him try it, and see what the press will say. For once we shall have the popular cry on our side. But Proudie, ass as he is, knows the world too well to get such a hornet's nest about his ears.\"\n\nMr. Harding winced at the idea of the press. He had had enough of that sort of publicity, and was unwilling to be shown up a second time either as a monster or as a martyr. He gently remarked that he hoped the newspapers would not get hold of his name again, and then suggested that perhaps it would be better that he should abandon his object. \"I am getting old,\" said he, \"and after all I doubt whether I am fit to undertake new duties.\"\n\n\"New duties?\" said the archdeacon; \"don't I tell you there shall be no new duties?\"\n\n\"Or perhaps old duties either,\" said Mr. Harding; \"I think I will remain content as I am.\" The picture of Mr. Slope carting away the rubbish was still present to his mind.\n\nThe archdeacon drank off his glass of claret and prepared himself to be energetic. \"I do hope,\" said he, \"that you are not going to be so weak as to allow such a man as Mr. Slope to deter you from doing what you know it is your duty to do. You know it is your duty to resume your place at the hospital now that parliament has so settled the stipend as to remove those difficulties which induced you to resign it. You cannot deny this, and should your timidity now prevent you from doing so, your conscience will hereafter never forgive you,\" and as he finished this clause of his speech, he pushed over the bottle to his companion.\n\n\"Your conscience will never forgive you,\" he continued. \"You resigned the place from conscientious scruples, scruples which I greatly respected, though I did not share them. All your friends respected them, and you left your old house as rich in reputation as you were ruined in fortune. It is now expected that you will return. Dr. Gwynne was saying only the other day--\"\n\n\"Dr. Gwynne does not reflect how much older a man I am now than when he last saw me.\"\n\n\"Old--nonsense,\" said the archdeacon; \"you never thought yourself old till you listened to the impudent trash of that coxcomb at the palace.\"\n\n\"I shall be sixty-five if I live till November,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"And seventy-five, if you live till November ten years,\" said the archdeacon. \"And you bid fair to be as efficient then as you were ten years ago. But for heaven's sake let us have no pretence in this matter. Your plea of old age is a pretence. But you're not drinking your wine. It is only a pretence. The fact is, you are half-afraid of this Slope, and would rather subject yourself to comparative poverty and discomfort than come to blows with a man who will trample on you, if you let him.\"\n\n\"I certainly don't like coming to blows, if I can help it.\"\n\n\"Nor I neither--but sometimes we can't help it. This man's object is to induce you to refuse the hospital, that he may put some creature of his own into it; that he may show his power and insult us all by insulting you, whose cause and character are so intimately bound up with that of the chapter. You owe it to us all to resist him in this, even if you have no solicitude for yourself. But surely, for your own sake, you will not be so lily-livered as to fall into this trap which he has baited for you and let him take the very bread out of your mouth without a struggle.\"\n\nMr. Harding did not like being called lily-livered, and was rather inclined to resent it. \"I doubt there is any true courage,\" said he, \"in squabbling for money.\"\n\n\"If honest men did not squabble for money, in this wicked world of ours, the dishonest men would get it all, and I do not see that the cause of virtue would be much improved. No--we must use the means which we have. If we were to carry your argument home, we might give away every shilling of revenue which the church has, and I presume you are not prepared to say that the church would be strengthened by such a sacrifice.\" The archdeacon filled his glass and then emptied it, drinking with much reverence a silent toast to the well-being and permanent security of those temporalities which were so dear to his soul.\n\n\"I think all quarrels between a clergyman and his bishop should be avoided,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"I think so too, but it is quite as much the duty of the bishop to look to that as of his inferior. I tell you what, my friend; I'll see the bishop in this matter--that is, if you will allow me--and you may be sure I will not compromise you. My opinion is that all this trash about the Sunday-schools and the sermons has originated wholly with Slope and Mrs. Proudie, and that the bishop knows nothing about it. The bishop can't very well refuse to see me, and I'll come upon him when he has neither his wife nor his chaplain by him. I think you'll find that it will end in his sending you the appointment without any condition whatever. And as to the seats in the cathedral, we may safely leave that to Mr. Dean. I believe the fool positively thinks that the bishop could walk away with the cathedral if he pleased.\"\n\nAnd so the matter was arranged between them. Mr. Harding had come expressly for advice, and therefore felt himself bound to take the advice given him. He had known, moreover, beforehand that the archdeacon would not hear of his giving the matter up, and accordingly, though he had in perfect good faith put forward his own views, he was prepared to yield.\n\nThey therefore went into the drawing-room in good humour with each other, and the evening passed pleasantly in prophetic discussions on the future wars of Arabin and Slope. The frogs and the mice would be nothing to them, nor the angers of Agamemnon and Achilles. How the archdeacon rubbed his hands and plumed himself on the success of his last move. He could not himself descend into the arena with Slope, but Arabin would have no such scruples. Arabin was exactly the man for such work, and the only man whom he knew that was fit for it.\n\nThe archdeacon's good humour and high buoyancy continued till, when reclining on his pillow, Mrs. Grantly commenced to give him her view of the state of affairs at Barchester. And then certainly he was startled. The last words he said that night were as follows:\n\n\"If she does, by heaven I'll never speak to her again. She dragged me into the mire once, but I'll not pollute myself with such filth as that--\" And the archdeacon gave a shudder which shook the whole room, so violently was he convulsed with the thought which then agitated his mind.\n\nNow in this matter the widow Bold was scandalously ill-treated by her relatives. She had spoken to the man three or four times, and had expressed her willingness to teach in a Sunday-school. Such was the full extent of her sins in the matter of Mr. Slope. Poor Eleanor! But time will show.\n\nThe next morning Mr. Harding returned to Barchester, no further word having been spoken in his hearing respecting Mr. Slope's acquaintance with his younger daughter. But he observed that the archdeacon at breakfast was less cordial than he had been on the preceding evening.\n\n# CHAPTER XV. The Widow's Suitors\n\nMr. Slope lost no time in availing himself of the bishop's permission to see Mr. Quiverful, and it was in his interview with this worthy pastor that he first learned that Mrs. Bold was worth the wooing. He rode out to Puddingdale to communicate to the embryo warden the goodwill of the bishop in his favour, and during the discussion on the matter it was not unnatural that the pecuniary resources of Mr. Harding and his family should become the subject of remark.\n\nMr. Quiverful, with his fourteen children and his four hundred a year, was a very poor man, and the prospect of this new preferment, which was to be held together with his living, was very grateful to him. To what clergyman so circumstanced would not such a prospect be very grateful? But Mr. Quiverful had long been acquainted with Mr. Harding, and had received kindness at his hands, so that his heart misgave him as he thought of supplanting a friend at the hospital. Nevertheless, he was extremely civil, cringingly civil, to Mr. Slope; treated him quite as the great man; entreated this great man to do him the honour to drink a glass of sherry, at which, as it was very poor Marsala, the now pampered Slope turned up his nose; and ended by declaring his extreme obligation to the bishop and Mr. Slope and his great desire to accept the hospital, if--if it were certainly the case that Mr. Harding had refused it.\n\nWhat man as needy as Mr. Quiverful would have been more disinterested?\n\n\"Mr. Harding did positively refuse it,\" said Mr. Slope with a certain air of offended dignity, \"when he heard of the conditions to which the appointment is now subjected. Of course you understand, Mr. Quiverful, that the same conditions will be imposed on yourself.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful cared nothing for the conditions. He would have undertaken to preach any number of sermons Mr. Slope might have chosen to dictate, and to pass every remaining hour of his Sundays within the walls of a Sunday-school. What sacrifices, or at any rate, what promises would have been too much to make for such an addition to his income, and for such a house! But his mind still recurred to Mr. Harding.\n\n\"To be sure,\" said he; \"Mr. Harding's daughter is very rich, and why should he trouble himself with the hospital?\"\n\n\"You mean Mrs. Grantly,\" said Slope.\n\n\"I meant his widowed daughter,\" said the other. \"Mrs. Bold has twelve hundred a year of her own, and I suppose Mr. Harding means to live with her.\"\n\n\"Twelve hundred a year of her own!\" said Slope, and very shortly afterwards took his leave, avoiding, as far as it was possible for him to do, any further allusion to the hospital. \"Twelve hundred a year!\" said he to himself as he rode slowly home. If it were the fact that Mrs. Bold had twelve hundred a year of her own, what a fool would he be to oppose her father's return to his old place. The train of Mr. Slope's ideas will probably be plain to all my readers. Why should he not make the twelve hundred a year his own? And if he did so, would it not be well for him to have a father-in-law comfortably provided with the good things of this world? Would it not, moreover, be much more easy for him to gain the daughter if he did all in his power to forward the father's views?\n\nThese questions presented themselves to him in a very forcible way, and yet there were many points of doubt. If he resolved to restore to Mr. Harding his former place, he must take the necessary steps for doing so at once; he must immediately talk over the bishop, quarrel on the matter with Mrs. Proudie, whom he knew he could not talk over, and let Mr. Quiverful know that he had been a little too precipitate as to Mr. Harding's positive refusal. That he could effect all this he did not doubt, but he did not wish to effect it for nothing. He did not wish to give way to Mr. Harding and then be rejected by the daughter. He did not wish to lose one influential friend before he had gained another.\n\nAnd thus he rode home, meditating many things in his mind. It occurred to him that Mrs. Bold was sister-in-law to the archdeacon, and that not even for twelve hundred a year would he submit to that imperious man. A rich wife was a great desideratum to him, but success in his profession was still greater; there were, moreover, other rich women who might be willing to become wives; and after all, this twelve hundred a year might, when inquired into, melt away into some small sum utterly beneath his notice. Then also he remembered that Mrs. Bold had a son.\n\nAnother circumstance also much influenced him, though it was one which may almost be said to have influenced him against his will. The vision of the Signora Neroni was perpetually before his eyes. It would be too much to say that Mr. Slope was lost in love, but yet he thought, and kept continually thinking, that he had never seen so beautiful a woman. He was a man whose nature was open to such impulses, and the wiles of the Italianized charmer had been thoroughly successful in imposing upon his thoughts. We will not talk about his heart: not that he had no heart, but because his heart had little to do with his present feelings. His taste had been pleased, his eyes charmed, and his vanity gratified. He had been dazzled by a sort of loveliness which he had never before seen, and had been caught by an easy, free, voluptuous manner which was perfectly new to him. He had never been so tempted before, and the temptation was now irresistible. He had not owned to himself that he cared for this woman more than for others around him, but yet he thought often of the time when he might see her next, and made, almost unconsciously, little cunning plans for seeing her frequently.\n\nHe had called at Dr. Stanhope's house the day after the bishop's party, and then the warmth of his admiration had been fed with fresh fuel. If the signora had been kind in her manner and flattering in her speech when lying upon the bishop's sofa, with the eyes of so many on her, she had been much more so in her mother's drawing-room, with no one present but her sister to repress either her nature or her art. Mr. Slope had thus left her quite bewildered, and could not willingly admit into his brain any scheme a part of which would be the necessity of his abandoning all further special friendship with this lady.\n\nAnd so he slowly rode along, very meditative.\n\nAnd here the author must beg it to be remembered that Mr. Slope was not in all things a bad man. His motives, like those of most men, were mixed, and though his conduct was generally very different from that which we would wish to praise, it was actuated perhaps as often as that of the majority of the world by a desire to do his duty. He believed in the religion which he taught, harsh, unpalatable, uncharitable as that religion was. He believed those whom he wished to get under his hoof, the Grantlys and Gwynnes of the church, to be the enemies of that religion. He believed himself to be a pillar of strength, destined to do great things, and with that subtle, selfish, ambiguous sophistry to which the minds of all men are so subject, he had taught himself to think that in doing much for the promotion of his own interests, he was doing much also for the promotion of religion. But Mr. Slope had never been an immoral man. Indeed, he had resisted temptations to immorality with a strength of purpose that was creditable to him. He had early in life devoted himself to works which were not compatible with the ordinary pleasures of youth, and he had abandoned such pleasures not without a struggle. It must therefore be conceived that he did not admit to himself that he warmly admired the beauty of a married woman without heart-felt stings of conscience; and to pacify that conscience he had to teach himself that the nature of his admiration was innocent.\n\nAnd thus he rode along meditative and ill at ease. His conscience had not a word to say against his choosing the widow and her fortune. That he looked upon as a godly work rather than otherwise; as a deed which, if carried through, would redound to his credit as a Christian. On that side lay no future remorse, no conduct which he might probably have to forget, no inward stings. If it should turn out to be really the fact that Mrs. Bold had twelve hundred a year at her own disposal, Mr. Slope would rather look upon it as a duty which he owed his religion to make himself the master of the wife and the money; as a duty too, in which some amount of self-sacrifice would be necessary. He would have to give up his friendship with the signora, his resistance to Mr. Harding, his antipathy--no, he found on mature self-examination that he could not bring himself to give up his antipathy to Dr. Grantly. He would marry the lady as the enemy of her brother-in-law if such an arrangement suited her; if not, she must look elsewhere for a husband.\n\nIt was with such resolve as this that he reached Barchester. He would at once ascertain what the truth might be as to the lady's wealth, and having done this he would be ruled by circumstances in his conduct respecting the hospital. If he found that he could turn round and secure the place for Mr. Harding without much self-sacrifice, he would do so; but if not, he would woo the daughter in opposition to the father. But in no case would he succumb to the archdeacon.\n\nHe saw his horse taken round to the stable, and immediately went forth to commence his inquiries. To give Mr. Slope his due, he was not a man who ever let much grass grow under his feet.\n\nPoor Eleanor! She was doomed to be the intended victim of more schemes than one.\n\nAbout the time that Mr. Slope was visiting the vicar of Puddingdale, a discussion took place respecting her charms and wealth at Dr. Stanhope's house in the close. There had been morning callers there, and people had told some truth and also some falsehood respecting the property which John Bold had left behind him. By degrees the visitors went, and as the doctor went with them, and as the doctor's wife had not made her appearance, Charlotte Stanhope and her brother were left together. He was sitting idly at the table, scrawling caricatures of Barchester notables, then yawning, then turning over a book or two, and evidently at a loss how to kill his time without much labour.\n\n\"You haven't done much, Bertie, about getting any orders,\" said his sister.\n\n\"Orders!\" said he; \"who on earth is there at Barchester to give one orders? Who among the people here could possibly think it worth his while to have his head done into marble?\"\n\n\"Then you mean to give up your profession,\" said she.\n\n\"No, I don't,\" said he, going on with some absurd portrait of the bishop. \"Look at that, Lotte; isn't it the little man all over, apron and all? I'd go on with my profession at once, as you call it, if the governor would set me up with a studio in London; but as to sculpture at Barchester--I suppose half the people here don't know what a torso means.\"\n\n\"The governor will not give you a shilling to start you in London,\" said Lotte. \"Indeed, he can't give you what would be sufficient, for he has not got it. But you might start yourself very well, if you pleased.\"\n\n\"How the deuce am I to do it?\" said he.\n\n\"To tell you the truth, Bertie, you'll never make a penny by any profession.\"\n\n\"That's what I often think myself,\" said he, not in the least offended. \"Some men have a great gift of making money, but they can't spend it. Others can't put two shillings together, but they have a great talent for all sorts of outlay. I begin to think that my genius is wholly in the latter line.\"\n\n\"How do you mean to live then?\" asked the sister.\n\n\"I suppose I must regard myself as a young raven and look for heavenly manna; besides, we have all got something when the governor goes.\"\n\n\"Yes--you'll have enough to supply yourself with gloves and boots; that is, if the Jews have not got the possession of it all. I believe they have the most of it already. I wonder, Bertie, at your indifference; that you, with your talents and personal advantages, should never try to settle yourself in life. I look forward with dread to the time when the governor must go. Mother, and Madeline, and I--we shall be poor enough, but you will have absolutely nothing.\"\n\n\"Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof,\" said Bertie.\n\n\"Will you take my advice?\" said his sister.\n\n\"Cela dépend,\" said the brother.\n\n\"Will you marry a wife with money?\"\n\n\"At any rate,\" said he, \"I won't marry one without; wives with money a'nt so easy to get now-a-days; the parsons pick them all up.\"\n\n\"And a parson will pick up the wife I mean for you, if you do not look quickly about it; the wife I mean is Mrs. Bold.\"\n\n\"Whew-w-w-w!\" whistled Bertie, \"a widow!\"\n\n\"She is very beautiful,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"With a son and heir all ready to my hand,\" said Bertie.\n\n\"A baby that will very likely die,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"I don't see that,\" said Bertie. \"But however, he may live for me--I don't wish to kill him; only, it must be owned that a ready-made family is a drawback.\"\n\n\"There is only one after all,\" pleaded Charlotte.\n\n\"And that a very little one, as the maidservant said,\" rejoined Bertie.\n\n\"Beggars mustn't be choosers, Bertie; you can't have everything.\"\n\n\"God knows I am not unreasonable,\" said he, \"nor yet opinionated, and if you'll arrange it all for me, Lotte, I'll marry the lady. Only mark this: the money must be sure, and the income at my own disposal, at any rate for the lady's life.\"\n\nCharlotte was explaining to her brother that he must make love for himself if he meant to carry on the matter, and was encouraging him to do so by warm eulogiums on Eleanor's beauty, when the signora was brought into the drawing-room. When at home, and subject to the gaze of none but her own family, she allowed herself to be dragged about by two persons, and her two bearers now deposited her on her sofa. She was not quite so grand in her apparel as she had been at the bishop's party, but yet she was dressed with much care, and though there was a look of care and pain about her eyes, she was, even by daylight, extremely beautiful.\n\n\"Well, Madeline, so I'm going to be married,\" Bertie began as soon as the servants had withdrawn.\n\n\"There's no other foolish thing left that you haven't done,\" said Madeline, \"and therefore you are quite right to try that.\"\n\n\"Oh, you think it's a foolish thing, do you?\" said he. \"There's Lotte advising me to marry by all means. But on such a subject your opinion ought to be the best; you have experience to guide you.\"\n\n\"Yes, I have,\" said Madeline with a sort of harsh sadness in her tone, which seemed to say--\"What is it to you if I am sad? I have never asked your sympathy.\"\n\nBertie was sorry when he saw that she was hurt by what he said, and he came and squatted on the floor close before her face to make his peace with her.\n\n\"Come, Mad, I was only joking; you know that. But in sober earnest, Lotte is advising me to marry. She wants me to marry this Mrs. Bold. She's a widow with lots of tin, a fine baby, a beautiful complexion, and the George and Dragon hotel up in the High Street. By Jove, Lotte, if I marry her, I'll keep the public-house myself--it's just the life to suit me.\"\n\n\"What,\" said Madeline, \"that vapid, swarthy creature in the widow's cap, who looked as though her clothes had been stuck on her back with a pitchfork!\" The signora never allowed any woman to be beautiful.\n\n\"Instead of being vapid,\" said Lotte, \"I call her a very lovely woman. She was by far the loveliest woman in the rooms the other night; that is, excepting you, Madeline.\"\n\nEven the compliment did not soften the asperity of the maimed beauty. \"Every woman is charming according to Lotte,\" she said; \"I never knew an eye with so little true appreciation. In the first place, what woman on earth could look well in such a thing as that she had on her head.\"\n\n\"Of course she wears a widow's cap, but she'll put that off when Bertie marries her.\"\n\n\"I don't see any of course in it,\" said Madeline. \"The death of twenty husbands should not make me undergo such a penance. It is as much a relic of paganism as the sacrifice of a Hindu woman at the burning of her husband's body. If not so bloody, it is quite as barbarous, and quite as useless.\"\n\n\"But you don't blame her for that,\" said Bertie. \"She does it because it's the custom of the country. People would think ill of her if she didn't do it.\"\n\n\"Exactly,\" said Madeline. \"She is just one of those English nonentities who would tie her head up in a bag for three months every summer, if her mother and her grandmother had tied up their heads before her. It would never occur to her to think whether there was any use in submitting to such a nuisance.\"\n\n\"It's very hard in a country like England, for a young woman to set herself in opposition to prejudices of that sort,\" said the prudent Charlotte.\n\n\"What you mean is that it's very hard for a fool not to be a fool,\" said Madeline.\n\nBertie Stanhope had been so much knocked about the world from his earliest years that he had not retained much respect for the gravity of English customs; but even to his mind an idea presented itself that, perhaps in a wife, true British prejudice would not in the long run be less agreeable than Anglo-Italian freedom from restraint. He did not exactly say so, but he expressed the idea in another way.\n\n\"I fancy,\" said he, \"that if I were to die, and then walk, I should think that my widow looked better in one of those caps than any other kind of head-dress.\"\n\n\"Yes--and you'd fancy also that she could do nothing better than shut herself up and cry for you, or else burn herself. But she would think differently. She'd probably wear one of those horrid she-helmets, because she'd want the courage not to do so; but she'd wear it with a heart longing for the time when she might be allowed to throw it off. I hate such shallow false pretences. For my part I would let the world say what it pleased, and show no grief if I felt none--and perhaps not, if I did.\"\n\n\"But wearing a widow's cap won't lessen her fortune,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"Or increase it,\" said Madeline. \"Then why on earth does she do it?\"\n\n\"But Lotte's object is to make her put it off,\" said Bertie.\n\n\"If it be true that she has got twelve hundred a year quite at her own disposal, and she be not utterly vulgar in her manners, I would advise you to marry her. I dare say she's to be had for the asking: and as you are not going to marry her for love, it doesn't much matter whether she is good-looking or not. As to your really marrying a woman for love, I don't believe you are fool enough for that.\"\n\n\"Oh, Madeline!\" exclaimed her sister.\n\n\"And oh, Charlotte!\" said the other.\n\n\"You don't mean to say that no man can love a woman unless he be a fool?\"\n\n\"I mean very much the same thing--that any man who is willing to sacrifice his interest to get possession of a pretty face is a fool. Pretty faces are to be had cheaper than that. I hate your mawkish sentimentality, Lotte. You know as well as I do in what way husbands and wives generally live together; you know how far the warmth of conjugal affection can withstand the trial of a bad dinner, of a rainy day, or of the least privation which poverty brings with it; you know what freedom a man claims for himself, what slavery he would exact from his wife if he could! And you know also how wives generally obey. Marriage means tyranny on one side and deceit on the other. I say that a man is a fool to sacrifice his interests for such a bargain. A woman, too generally, has no other way of living.\"\n\n\"But Bertie has no other way of living,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"Then, in God's name, let him marry Mrs. Bold,\" said Madeline. And so it was settled between them.\n\nBut let the gentle-hearted reader be under no apprehension whatsoever. It is not destined that Eleanor shall marry Mr. Slope or Bertie Stanhope. And here perhaps it may be allowed to the novelist to explain his views on a very important point in the art of telling tales. He ventures to reprobate that system which goes so far to violate all proper confidence between the author and his readers by maintaining nearly to the end of the third volume a mystery as to the fate of their favourite personage. Nay, more, and worse than this, is too frequently done. Have not often the profoundest efforts of genius been used to baffle the aspirations of the reader, to raise false hopes and false fears, and to give rise to expectations which are never to be realized? Are not promises all but made of delightful horrors, in lieu of which the writer produces nothing but most commonplace realities in his final chapter? And is there not a species of deceit in this to which the honesty of the present age should lend no countenance?\n\nAnd what can be the worth of that solicitude which a peep into the third volume can utterly dissipate? What the value of those literary charms which are absolutely destroyed by their enjoyment? When we have once learnt what was that picture before which was hung Mrs. Ratcliffe's solemn curtain, we feel no further interest about either the frame or the veil. They are to us merely a receptacle for old bones, an inappropriate coffin, which we would wish to have decently buried out of our sight.\n\nAnd then how grievous a thing it is to have the pleasure of your novel destroyed by the ill-considered triumph of a previous reader. \"Oh, you needn't be alarmed for Augusta; of course she accepts Gustavus in the end.\" \"How very ill-natured you are, Susan,\" says Kitty with tears in her eyes: \"I don't care a bit about it now.\" Dear Kitty, if you will read my book, you may defy the ill-nature of your sister. There shall be no secret that she can tell you. Nay, take the third volume if you please--learn from the last pages all the results of our troubled story, and the story shall have lost none of its interest, if indeed there be any interest in it to lose.\n\nOur doctrine is that the author and the reader should move along together in full confidence with each other. Let the personages of the drama undergo ever so complete a comedy of errors among themselves, but let the spectator never mistake the Syracusan for the Ephesian; otherwise he is one of the dupes, and the part of a dupe is never dignified.\n\nI would not for the value of this chapter have it believed by a single reader that my Eleanor could bring herself to marry Mr. Slope, or that she should be sacrificed to a Bertie Stanhope. But among the good folk of Barchester many believed both the one and the other.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. Baby Worship\n\n\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum,\" said or sung Eleanor Bold.\n\n\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum,\" continued Mary Bold, taking up the second part in this concerted piece.\n\nThe only audience at the concert was the baby, who however gave such vociferous applause that the performers, presuming it to amount to an encore, commenced again.\n\n\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum: hasn't he got lovely legs?\" said the rapturous mother.\n\n\"H'm 'm 'm 'm 'm,\" simmered Mary, burying her lips in the little fellow's fat neck, by way of kissing him.\n\n\"H'm 'm 'm 'm 'm,\" simmered the mamma, burying her lips also in his fat, round, short legs. \"He's a dawty little bold darling, so he is; and he has the nicest little pink legs in all the world, so he has;\" and the simmering and the kissing went on over again, as though the ladies were very hungry and determined to eat him.\n\n\"Well, then, he's his own mother's own darling: well, he shall--oh, oh--Mary, Mary--did you ever see? What am I to do? My naughty, naughty, naughty, naughty little Johnny.\" All these energetic exclamations were elicited by the delight of the mother in finding that her son was strong enough and mischievous enough to pull all her hair out from under her cap. \"He's been and pulled down all Mamma's hair, and he's the naughtiest, naughtiest, naughtiest little man that ever, ever, ever, ever, ever--\"\n\nA regular service of baby worship was going on. Mary Bold was sitting on a low easy chair, with the boy in her lap, and Eleanor was kneeling before the object of her idolatry. As she tried to cover up the little fellow's face with her long, glossy, dark brown locks, and permitted him to pull them hither and thither as he would, she looked very beautiful in spite of the widow's cap which she still wore. There was a quiet, enduring, grateful sweetness about her face which grew so strongly upon those who knew her, as to make the great praise of her beauty which came from her old friends appear marvellously exaggerated to those who were only slightly acquainted with her. Her loveliness was like that of many landscapes, which require to be often seen to be fully enjoyed. There was a depth of dark clear brightness in her eyes which was lost upon a quick observer, a character about her mouth which only showed itself to those with whom she familiarly conversed, a glorious form of head the perfect symmetry of which required the eye of an artist for its appreciation. She had none of that dazzling brilliancy, of that voluptuous Rubens beauty, of that pearly whiteness, and those vermilion tints which immediately entranced with the power of a basilisk men who came within reach of Madeline Neroni. It was all but impossible to resist the signora, but no one was called upon for any resistance towards Eleanor. You might begin to talk to her as though she were your sister, and it would not be till your head was on your pillow that the truth and intensity of her beauty would flash upon you, that the sweetness of her voice would come upon your ear. A sudden half-hour with the Neroni was like falling into a pit, an evening spent with Eleanor like an unexpected ramble in some quiet fields of asphodel.\n\n\"We'll cover him up till there shan't be a morsel of his little 'ittle 'ittle 'ittle nose to be seen,\" said the mother, stretching her streaming locks over the infant's face. The child screamed with delight, and kicked till Mary Bold was hardly able to hold him.\n\nAt this moment the door opened, and Mr. Slope was announced. Up jumped Eleanor and, with a sudden quick motion of her hands, pushed back her hair over her shoulders. It would have been perhaps better for her that she had not, for she thus showed more of her confusion than she would have done had she remained as she was. Mr. Slope, however, immediately recognized her loveliness and thought to himself that, irrespective of her fortune, she would be an inmate that a man might well desire for his house, a partner for his bosom's care very well qualified to make care lie easy. Eleanor hurried out of the room to readjust her cap, muttering some unnecessary apology about her baby. And while she is gone, we will briefly go back and state what had been hitherto the results of Mr. Slope's meditations on his scheme of matrimony.\n\nHis inquiries as to the widow's income had at any rate been so far successful as to induce him to determine to go on with the speculation. As regarded Mr. Harding, he had also resolved to do what he could without injury to himself. To Mrs. Proudie he determined not to speak on the matter, at least not at present. His object was to instigate a little rebellion on the part of the bishop. He thought that such a state of things would be advisable, not only in respect to Messrs. Harding and Quiverful, but also in the affairs of the diocese generally. Mr. Slope was by no means of opinion that Dr. Proudie was fit to rule, but he conscientiously thought it wrong that his brother clergy should be subjected to petticoat government. He therefore made up his mind to infuse a little of his spirit into the bishop, sufficient to induce him to oppose his wife, though not enough to make him altogether insubordinate.\n\nHe had therefore taken an opportunity of again speaking to his lordship about the hospital, and had endeavoured to make it appear that after all it would be unwise to exclude Mr. Harding from the appointment. Mr. Slope, however, had a harder task than he had imagined. Mrs. Proudie, anxious to assume to herself as much as possible of the merit of patronage, had written to Mrs. Quiverful, requesting her to call at the palace, and had then explained to that matron, with much mystery, condescension, and dignity, the good that was in store for her and her progeny. Indeed, Mrs. Proudie had been so engaged at the very time that Mr. Slope had been doing the same with the husband at Puddingdale Vicarage, and had thus in a measure committed herself. The thanks, the humility, the gratitude, the surprise of Mrs. Quiverful had been very overpowering; she had all but embraced the knees of her patroness, and had promised that the prayers of fourteen unprovided babes (so Mrs. Quiverful had described her own family, the eldest of which was a stout young woman of three-and-twenty) should be put up to heaven morning and evening for the munificent friend whom God had sent to them. Such incense as this was not unpleasing to Mrs. Proudie, and she made the most of it. She offered her general assistance to the fourteen unprovided babes, if, as she had no doubt, she should find them worthy; expressed a hope that the eldest of them would be fit to undertake tuition in her Sabbath-schools; and altogether made herself a very great lady in the estimation of Mrs. Quiverful.\n\nHaving done this, she thought it prudent to drop a few words before the bishop, letting him know that she had acquainted the Puddingdale family with their good fortune; so that he might perceive that he stood committed to the appointment. The husband well understood the ruse of his wife, but he did not resent it. He knew that she was taking the patronage out of his hands; he was resolved to put an end to her interference and reassume his powers. But then he thought this was not the best time to do it. He put off the evil hour, as many a man in similar circumstances has done before him.\n\nSuch having been the case, Mr. Slope naturally encountered a difficulty in talking over the bishop, a difficulty indeed which he found could not be overcome except at the cost of a general outbreak at the palace. A general outbreak at the present moment might be good policy, but it also might not. It was at any rate not a step to be lightly taken. He began by whispering to the bishop that he feared that public opinion would be against him if Mr. Harding did not reappear at the hospital. The bishop answered with some warmth that Mr. Quiverful had been promised the appointment on Mr. Slope's advice. \"Not promised?\" said Mr. Slope. \"Yes, promised,\" replied the bishop, \"and Mrs. Proudie has seen Mrs. Quiverful on the subject.\" This was quite unexpected on the part of Mr. Slope, but his presence of mind did not fail him, and he turned the statement to his own account.\n\n\"Ah, my lord,\" said he, \"we shall all be in scrapes if the ladies interfere.\"\n\nThis was too much in unison with my lord's feelings to be altogether unpalatable, and yet such an allusion to interference demanded a rebuke. My lord was somewhat astounded also, though not altogether made miserable, by finding that there was a point of difference between his wife and his chaplain.\n\n\"I don't know what you mean by interference,\" said the bishop mildly. \"When Mrs. Proudie heard that Mr. Quiverful was to be appointed, it was not unnatural that she should wish to see Mrs. Quiverful about the schools. I really cannot say that I see any interference.\"\n\n\"I only speak, my lord, for your own comfort,\" said Slope; \"for your own comfort and dignity in the diocese. I can have no other motive. As far as personal feelings go, Mrs. Proudie is the best friend I have. I must always remember that. But still, in my present position, my first duty is to your lordship.\"\n\n\"I'm sure of that, Mr. Slope; I am quite sure of that;\" said the bishop, mollified: \"and you really think that Mr. Harding should have the hospital?\"\n\n\"Upon my word, I'm inclined to think so. I am quite prepared to take upon myself the blame of first suggesting Mr. Quiverful's name. But since doing so, I have found that there is so strong a feeling in the diocese in favour of Mr. Harding that I think your lordship should give way. I hear also that Mr. Harding has modified the objections he first felt to your lordship's propositions. And as to what has passed between Mrs. Proudie and Mrs. Quiverful, the circumstance may be a little inconvenient, but I really do not think that that should weigh in a matter of so much moment.\"\n\nAnd thus the poor bishop was left in a dreadfully undecided step as to what he should do. His mind, however, slightly inclined itself to the appointment of Mr. Harding, seeing that by such a step he should have the assistance of Mr. Slope in opposing Mrs. Proudie.\n\nSuch was the state of affairs at the palace, when Mr. Slope called at Mrs. Bold's house and found her playing with her baby. When she ran out of the room, Mr. Slope began praising the weather to Mary Bold, then he praised the baby and kissed him, and then he praised the mother, and then he praised Miss Bold herself. Mrs. Bold, however, was not long before she came back.\n\n\"I have to apologize for calling at so very early an hour,\" began Mr. Slope, \"but I was really so anxious to speak to you that I hope you and Miss Bold will excuse me.\"\n\nEleanor muttered something in which the words \"certainly,\" and \"of course,\" and \"not early at all,\" were just audible, and then apologized for her own appearance, declaring, with a smile, that her baby was becoming such a big boy that he was quite unmanageable.\n\n\"He's a great big naughty boy,\" said she to the child, \"and we must send him away to a great big rough romping school, where they have great big rods and do terrible things to naughty boys who don't do what their own mammas tell them;\" and she then commenced another course of kissing, being actuated thereto by the terrible idea of sending her child away which her own imagination had depicted.\n\n\"And where the masters don't have such beautiful long hair to be dishevelled,\" said Mr. Slope, taking up the joke and paying a compliment at the same time.\n\nEleanor thought he might as well have left the compliment alone, but she said nothing and looked nothing, being occupied as she was with the baby.\n\n\"Let me take him,\" said Mary. \"His clothes are nearly off his back with his romping,\" and so saying she left the room with the child. Miss Bold had heard Mr. Slope say he had something pressing to say to Eleanor, and thinking that she might be de trop, took this opportunity of getting herself out of the room.\n\n\"Don't be long, Mary,\" said Eleanor as Miss Bold shut the door.\n\n\"I am glad, Mrs. Bold, to have the opportunity of having ten minutes' conversation with you alone,\" began Mr. Slope. \"Will you let me openly ask you a plain question?\"\n\n\"Certainly,\" said she.\n\n\"And I am sure you will give me a plain and open answer.\"\n\n\"Either that, or none at all,\" said she, laughing.\n\n\"My question is this, Mrs. Bold: is your father really anxious to go back to the hospital?\"\n\n\"Why do you ask me?\" said she. \"Why don't you ask himself?\"\n\n\"My dear Mrs. Bold, I'll tell you why. There are wheels within wheels, all of which I would explain to you, only I fear that there is not time. It is essentially necessary that I should have an answer to this question, otherwise I cannot know how to advance your father's wishes; and it is quite impossible that I should ask himself. No one can esteem your father more than I do, but I doubt if this feeling is reciprocal.\" It certainly was not. \"I must be candid with you as the only means of avoiding ultimate consequences, which may be most injurious to Mr. Harding. I fear there is a feeling--I will not even call it a prejudice--with regard to myself in Barchester, which is not in my favour. You remember that sermon--\"\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Slope, we need not go back to that,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"For one moment, Mrs. Bold. It is not that I may talk of myself, but because it is so essential that you should understand how matters stand. That sermon may have been ill-judged--it was certainly misunderstood; but I will say nothing about that now; only this, that it did give rise to a feeling against myself which your father shares with others. It may be that he has proper cause, but the result is that he is not inclined to meet me on friendly terms. I put it to yourself whether you do not know this to be the case.\"\n\nEleanor made no answer, and Mr. Slope, in the eagerness of his address, edged his chair a little nearer to the widow's seat, unperceived by her.\n\n\"Such being so,\" continued Mr. Slope, \"I cannot ask him this question as I can ask it of you. In spite of my delinquencies since I came to Barchester you have allowed me to regard you as a friend.\" Eleanor made a little motion with her head which was hardly confirmatory, but Mr. Slope if he noticed it, did not appear to do so. \"To you I can speak openly and explain the feelings of my heart. This your father would not allow. Unfortunately, the bishop has thought it right that this matter of the hospital should pass through my hands. There have been some details to get up with which he would not trouble himself, and thus it has come to pass that I was forced to have an interview with your father on the matter.\"\n\n\"I am aware of that,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"Of course,\" said he. \"In that interview Mr. Harding left the impression on my mind that he did not wish to return to the hospital.\"\n\n\"How could that be?\" said Eleanor, at last stirred up to forget the cold propriety of demeanour which she had determined to maintain.\n\n\"My dear Mrs. Bold, I give you my word that such was the case,\" said he, again getting a little nearer to her. \"And what is more than that, before my interview with Mr. Harding, certain persons at the palace--I do not mean the bishop--had told me that such was the fact. I own, I hardly believed it; I own, I thought that your father would wish on every account, for conscience' sake, for the sake of those old men, for old association and the memory of dear days long gone by, on every account I thought that he would wish to resume his duties. But I was told that such was not his wish, and he certainly left me with the impression that I had been told the truth.\"\n\n\"Well!\" said Eleanor, now sufficiently roused on the matter.\n\n\"I hear Miss Bold's step,\" said Mr. Slope; \"would it be asking too great a favour to beg you to--I know you can manage anything with Miss Bold.\"\n\nEleanor did not like the word manage, but still she went out and asked Mary to leave them alone for another quarter of an hour.\n\n\"Thank you, Mrs. Bold--I am so very grateful for this confidence. Well, I left your father with this impression. Indeed, I may say that he made me understand that he declined the appointment.\"\n\n\"Not the appointment,\" said Eleanor. \"I am sure he did not decline the appointment. But he said that he would not agree--that is, that he did not like the scheme about the schools and the services and all that. I am quite sure he never said that he wished to refuse the place.\"\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Bold!\" said Mr. Slope in a manner almost impassioned. \"I would not for the world say to so good a daughter a word against so good a father. But you must, for his sake, let me show you exactly how the matter stands at present. Mr. Harding was a little flurried when I told him of the bishop's wishes about the school. I did so perhaps with the less caution because you yourself had so perfectly agreed with me on the same subject. He was a little put out and spoke warmly. 'Tell the bishop,' said he, 'that I quite disagree with him--and shall not return to the hospital as such conditions are attached to it.' What he said was to that effect; indeed, his words were, if anything, stronger than those. I had no alternative but to repeat them to his lordship, who said that he could look on them in no other light than a refusal. He also had heard the report that your father did not wish for the appointment, and putting all these things together, he thought he had no choice but to look for someone else. He has consequently offered the place to Mr. Quiverful.\"\n\n\"Offered the place to Mr. Quiverful!\" repeated Eleanor, her eyes suffused with tears. \"Then, Mr. Slope, there is an end of it.\"\n\n\"No, my friend--not so,\" said he. \"It is to prevent such being the end of it that I am now here. I may at any rate presume that I have got an answer to my question, and that Mr. Harding is desirous of returning.\"\n\n\"Desirous of returning--of course he is,\" said Eleanor; \"of course he wishes to have back his house and his income and his place in the world; to have back what he gave up with such self-denying honesty, if he can have them without restraints on his conduct to which at his age it would be impossible that he should submit. How can the bishop ask a man of his age to turn schoolmaster to a pack of children?\"\n\n\"Out of the question,\" said Mr. Slope, laughing slightly; \"of course no such demand shall be made on your father. I can at any rate promise you that I will not be the medium of any so absurd a requisition. We wished your father to preach in the hospital, as the inmates may naturally be too old to leave it, but even that shall not be insisted on. We wished also to attach a Sabbath-day school to the hospital, thinking that such an establishment could not but be useful under the surveillance of so good a clergyman as Mr. Harding, and also under your own. But, dear Mrs. Bold, we won't talk of these things now. One thing is clear: we must do what we can to annul this rash offer the bishop has made to Mr. Quiverful. Your father wouldn't see Quiverful, would he? Quiverful is an honourable man, and would not for a moment stand in your father's way.\"\n\n\"What?\" said Eleanor. \"Ask a man with fourteen children to give up his preferment! I am quite sure he will do no such thing.\"\n\n\"I suppose not,\" said Slope, and he again drew near to Mrs. Bold, so that now they were very close to each other. Eleanor did not think much about it but instinctively moved away a little. How greatly would she have increased the distance could she have guessed what had been said about her at Plumstead! \"I suppose not. But it is out of the question that Quiverful should supersede your father--quite out of the question. The bishop has been too rash. An idea occurs to me which may perhaps, with God's blessing, put us right. My dear Mrs. Bold, would you object to seeing the bishop yourself?\"\n\n\"Why should not my father see him?\" said Eleanor. She had once before in her life interfered in her father's affairs, and then not to much advantage. She was older now and felt that she should take no step in a matter so vital to him without his consent.\n\n\"Why, to tell the truth,\" said Mr. Slope with a look of sorrow, as though he greatly bewailed the want of charity in his patron, \"the bishop fancies that he has cause of anger against your father. I fear an interview would lead to further ill-will.\"\n\n\"Why,\" said Eleanor, \"my father is the mildest, the gentlest man living.\"\n\n\"I only know,\" said Slope, \"that he has the best of daughters. So you would not see the bishop? As to getting an interview, I could manage that for you without the slightest annoyance to yourself.\"\n\n\"I could do nothing, Mr. Slope, without consulting my father.\"\n\n\"Ah!\" said he, \"that would be useless; you would then only be your father's messenger. Does anything occur to yourself? Something must be done. Your father shall not be ruined by so ridiculous a misunderstanding.\"\n\nEleanor said that nothing occurred to her, but that it was very hard; the tears came to her eyes and rolled down her cheeks. Mr. Slope would have given much to have had the privilege of drying them, but he had tact enough to know that he had still a great deal to do before he could even hope for any privilege with Mrs. Bold.\n\n\"It cuts me to the heart to see you so grieved,\" said he. \"But pray let me assure you that your father's interests shall not be sacrificed if it be possible for me to protect them. I will tell the bishop openly what are the facts. I will explain to him that he has hardly the right to appoint any other than your father, and will show him that if he does so he will be guilty of great injustice--and you, Mrs. Bold, you will have the charity at any rate to believe this of me, that I am truly anxious for your father's welfare--for his and for your own.\"\n\nThe widow hardly knew what answer to make. She was quite aware that her father would not be at all thankful to Mr. Slope; she had a strong wish to share her father's feelings; and yet she could not but acknowledge that Mr. Slope was very kind. Her father, who was generally so charitable to all men, who seldom spoke ill of anyone, had warned her against Mr. Slope, and yet she did not know how to abstain from thanking him. What interest could he have in the matter but that which he professed? Nevertheless there was that in his manner which even she distrusted. She felt, she did not know why, that there was something about him which ought to put her on her guard.\n\nMr. Slope read all this in her hesitating manner just as plainly as though she had opened her heart to him. It was the talent of the man that he could so read the inward feelings of women with whom he conversed. He knew that Eleanor was doubting him, and that, if she thanked him, she would only do so because she could not help it, but yet this did not make him angry or even annoy him. Rome was not built in a day.\n\n\"I did not come for thanks,\" continued he, seeing her hesitation, \"and do not want them--at any rate before they are merited. But this I do want, Mrs. Bold, that I may make to myself friends in this fold to which it has pleased God to call me as one of the humblest of his shepherds. If I cannot do so, my task here must indeed be a sad one. I will at any rate endeavour to deserve them.\"\n\n\"I'm sure,\" said she, \"you will soon make plenty of friends.\" She felt herself obliged to say something.\n\n\"That will be nothing unless they are such as will sympathize with my feelings; unless they are such as I can reverence and admire--and love. If the best and purest turn away from me, I cannot bring myself to be satisfied with the friendship of the less estimable. In such case I must live alone.\"\n\n\"Oh, I'm sure you will not do that, Mr. Slope.\" Eleanor meant nothing, but it suited him to appear to think some special allusion had been intended.\n\n\"Indeed, Mrs. Bold, I shall live alone, quite alone as far as the heart is concerned, if those with whom I yearn to ally myself turn away from me. But enough of this; I have called you my friend, and I hope you will not contradict me. I trust the time may come when I may also call your father so. May God bless you, Mrs. Bold, you and your darling boy. And tell your father from me that what can be done for his interest shall be done.\"\n\nAnd so he took his leave, pressing the widow's hand rather more closely than usual. Circumstances, however, seemed just then to make this intelligible, and the lady did not feel called on to resent it.\n\n\"I cannot understand him,\" said Eleanor to Mary Bold a few minutes afterwards. \"I do not know whether he is a good man or a bad man--whether he is true or false.\"\n\n\"Then give him the benefit of the doubt,\" said Mary, \"and believe the best.\"\n\n\"On the whole, I think I do,\" said Eleanor. \"I think I do believe that he means well--and if so, it is a shame that we should revile him and make him miserable while he is among us. But, oh, Mary, I fear Papa will be disappointed in the hospital.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. Who Shall Be Cock of the Walk?\n\nAll this time things were going somewhat uneasily at the palace. The hint or two which Mr. Slope had given was by no means thrown away upon the bishop. He had a feeling that if he ever meant to oppose the now almost unendurable despotism of his wife, he must lose no further time in doing so; that if he ever meant to be himself master in his own diocese, let alone his own house, he should begin at once. It would have been easier to have done so from the day of his consecration than now, but easier now than when Mrs. Proudie should have succeeded in thoroughly mastering the diocesan details. Then the proffered assistance of Mr. Slope was a great thing for him, a most unexpected and invaluable aid. Hitherto he had looked on the two as allied forces and had considered that, as allies, they were impregnable. He had begun to believe that his only chance of escape would be by the advancement of Mr. Slope to some distant and rich preferment. But now it seemed that one of his enemies, certainly the least potent of them, but nevertheless one very important, was willing to desert his own camp. Assisted by Mr. Slope what might he not do? He walked up and down his little study, almost thinking that the time might come when he would be able to appropriate to his own use the big room upstairs in which his predecessor had always sat.\n\nAs he revolved these things in his mind a note was brought to him from Archdeacon Grantly, in which that divine begged his lordship to do him the honour of seeing him on the morrow--would his lordship have the kindness to name an hour? Dr. Grantly's proposed visit would have reference to the reappointment of Mr. Harding to the wardenship of Barchester Hospital. The bishop having read his note was informed that the archdeacon's servant was waiting for an answer.\n\nHere at once a great opportunity offered itself to the bishop of acting on his own responsibility. He bethought himself however of his new ally and rang the bell for Mr. Slope. It turned out that Mr. Slope was not in the house, and then, greatly daring, the bishop with his own unassisted spirit wrote a note to the archdeacon saying that he would see him, and naming an hour for doing so. Having watched from his study-window that the messenger got safely off from the premises with this dispatch, he began to turn over in his mind what step he should next take.\n\nTo-morrow he would have to declare to the archdeacon either that Mr. Harding should have the appointment, or that he should not have it. The bishop felt that he could not honestly throw over the Quiverfuls without informing Mrs. Proudie, and he resolved at last to brave the lioness in her den and tell her that circumstances were such that it behoved him to reappoint Mr. Harding. He did not feel that he should at all derogate from his new courage by promising Mrs. Proudie that the very first piece of available preferment at his disposal should be given to Quiverful to atone for the injury done to him. If he could mollify the lioness with such a sop, how happy would he think his first efforts to have been!\n\nNot without many misgivings did he find himself in Mrs. Proudie's boudoir. He had at first thought of sending for her. But it was not at all impossible that she might choose to take such a message amiss, and then also it might be some protection to him to have his daughters present at the interview. He found her sitting with her account-books before her, nibbling the end of her pencil, evidently immersed in pecuniary difficulties, and harassed in mind by the multiplicity of palatial expenses and the heavy cost of episcopal grandeur. Her daughters were around her. Olivia was reading a novel, Augusta was crossing a note to her bosom friend in Baker Street, and Netta was working diminutive coach wheels for the bottom of a petticoat. If the bishop could get the better of his wife in her present mood, he would be a man indeed. He might then consider the victory his own forever. After all, in such cases the matter between husband and wife stands much the same as it does between two boys at the same school, two cocks in the same yard, or two armies on the same continent. The conqueror once is generally the conqueror forever after. The prestige of victory is everything.\n\n\"Ahem--my dear,\" began the bishop, \"if you are disengaged, I wished to speak to you.\" Mrs. Proudie put her pencil down carefully at the point to which she had totted her figures, marked down in her memory the sum she had arrived at, and then looked up, sourly enough, into her helpmate's face. \"If you are busy, another time will do as well,\" continued the bishop, whose courage, like Bob Acres', had oozed out now that he found himself on the ground of battle.\n\n\"What is it about, Bishop?\" asked the lady.\n\n\"Well--it was about those Quiverfuls--but I see you are engaged. Another time will do just as well for me.\"\n\n\"What about the Quiverfuls? It is quite understood, I believe, that they are to come to the hospital. There is to be no doubt about that, is there?\" and as she spoke she kept her pencil sternly and vigorously fixed on the column of figures before her.\n\n\"Why, my dear, there is a difficulty,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"A difficulty!\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"what difficulty? The place has been promised to Mr. Quiverful, and of course he must have it. He has made all his arrangements. He has written for a curate for Puddingdale, he has spoken to the auctioneer about selling his farm, horses, and cows, and in all respects considers the place as his own. Of course he must have it.\"\n\nNow, Bishop, look well to thyself and call up all the manhood that is in thee. Think how much is at stake. If now thou art not true to thy guns, no Slope can hereafter aid thee. How can he who deserts his own colours at the first smell of gunpowder expect faith in any ally? Thou thyself hast sought the battle-field: fight out the battle manfully now thou art there. Courage, Bishop, courage! Frowns cannot kill, nor can sharp words break any bones. After all, the apron is thine own. She can appoint no wardens, give away no benefices, nominate no chaplains, an' thou art but true to thyself. Up, man, and at her with a constant heart.\n\nSome little monitor within the bishop's breast so addressed him. But then there was another monitor there which advised him differently, and as follows. Remember, Bishop, she is a woman, and such a woman too as thou well knowest: a battle of words with such a woman is the very mischief. Were it not better for thee to carry on this war, if it must be waged, from behind thine own table in thine own study? Does not every cock fight best on his own dunghill? Thy daughters also are here, the pledges of thy love, the fruits of thy loins: is it well that they should see thee in the hour of thy victory over their mother? Nay, is it well that they should see thee in the possible hour of thy defeat? Besides, hast thou not chosen thy opportunity with wonderful little skill, indeed with no touch of that sagacity for which thou art famous? Will it not turn out that thou art wrong in this matter and thine enemy right; that thou hast actually pledged thyself in this matter of the hospital, and that now thou wouldest turn upon thy wife because she requires from thee but the fulfilment of thy promise? Art thou not a Christian bishop, and is not thy word to be held sacred whatever be the result? Return, Bishop, to thy sanctum on the lower floor and postpone thy combative propensities for some occasion in which at least thou mayest fight the battle against odds less tremendously against thee.\n\nAll this passed within the bishop's bosom while Mrs. Proudie still sat with her fixed pencil, and the figures of her sum still enduring on the tablets of her memory. \"£4 17s. 7d.\" she said to herself. \"Of course Mr. Quiverful must have the hospital,\" she said out loud to her lord.\n\n\"Well, my dear, I merely wanted to suggest to you that Mr. Slope seems to think that if Mr. Harding be not appointed, public feeling in the matter would be against us, and that the press might perhaps take it up.\"\n\n\"Mr. Slope seems to think!\" said Mrs. Proudie in a tone of voice which plainly showed the bishop that he was right in looking for a breach in that quarter. \"And what has Mr. Slope to do with it? I hope, my lord, you are not going to allow yourself to be governed by a chaplain.\" And now in her eagerness the lady lost her place in her account.\n\n\"Certainly not, my dear. Nothing I can assure you is less probable. But still, Mr. Slope may be useful in finding how the wind blows, and I really thought that if we could give something else as good to the Quiverfuls--\"\n\n\"Nonsense,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"it would be years before you could give them anything else that could suit them half as well, and as for the press and the public and all that, remember there are two ways of telling a story. If Mr. Harding is fool enough to tell his tale, we can also tell ours. The place was offered to him, and he refused it. It has now been given to someone else, and there's an end of it. At least I should think so.\"\n\n\"Well, my dear, I rather believe you are right,\" said the bishop, and sneaking out of the room, he went downstairs, troubled in his mind as to how he should receive the archdeacon on the morrow. He felt himself not very well just at present, and began to consider that he might, not improbably, be detained in his room the next morning by an attack of bile. He was, unfortunately, very subject to bilious annoyances.\n\n\"Mr. Slope, indeed! I'll Slope him,\" said the indignant matron to her listening progeny. \"I don't know what has come to Mr. Slope. I believe he thinks he is to be Bishop of Barchester himself, because I've taken him by the hand and got your father to make him his domestic chaplain.\"\n\n\"He was always full of impudence,\" said Olivia; \"I told you so once before, Mamma.\" Olivia, however, had not thought him too impudent when once before he had proposed to make her Mrs. Slope.\n\n\"Well, Olivia, I always thought you liked him,\" said Augusta, who at that moment had some grudge against her sister. \"I always disliked the man, because I think him thoroughly vulgar.\"\n\n\"There you're wrong,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"he's not vulgar at all; and what is more, he is a soul-stirring, eloquent preacher; but he must be taught to know his place if he is to remain in this house.\"\n\n\"He has the horridest eyes I ever saw in a man's head,\" said Netta; \"and I tell you what, he's terribly greedy; did you see all the currant pie he ate yesterday?\"\n\nWhen Mr. Slope got home he soon learnt from the bishop, as much from his manner as his words, that Mrs. Proudie's behests in the matter of the hospital were to be obeyed. Dr. Proudie let fall something as to \"this occasion only\" and \"keeping all affairs about patronage exclusively in his own hands.\" But he was quite decided about Mr. Harding; and as Mr. Slope did not wish to have both the prelate and the prelatess against him, he did not at present see that he could do anything but yield.\n\nHe merely remarked that he would of course carry out the bishop's views and that he was quite sure that if the bishop trusted to his own judgement things in the diocese would certainly be well ordered. Mr. Slope knew that if you hit a nail on the head often enough, it will penetrate at last.\n\nHe was sitting alone in his room on the same evening when a light knock was made on his door, and before he could answer it the door was opened, and his patroness appeared. He was all smiles in a moment, but so was not she also. She took, however, the chair that was offered to her, and thus began her expostulation:\n\n\"Mr. Slope, I did not at all approve your conduct the other night with that Italian woman. Anyone would have thought that you were her lover.\"\n\n\"Good gracious, my dear madam,\" said Mr. Slope with a look of horror. \"Why, she is a married woman.\"\n\n\"That's more than I know,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"however she chooses to pass for such. But married or not married, such attention as you paid to her was improper. I cannot believe that you would wish to give offence in my drawing-room, Mr. Slope, but I owe it to myself and my daughters to tell you that I disapprove of your conduct.\"\n\nMr. Slope opened wide his huge protruding eyes and stared out of them with a look of well-feigned surprise. \"Why, Mrs. Proudie,\" said he, \"I did but fetch her something to eat when she said she was hungry.\"\n\n\"And you have called on her since,\" continued she, looking at the culprit with the stern look of a detective policeman in the act of declaring himself.\n\nMr. Slope turned over in his mind whether it would be well for him to tell this termagant at once that he should call on whom he liked and do what he liked, but he remembered that his footing in Barchester was not yet sufficiently firm, and that it would be better for him to pacify her.\n\n\"I certainly called since at Dr. Stanhope's house, and certainly saw Madame Neroni.\"\n\n\"Yes, and you saw her alone,\" said the episcopal Argus.\n\n\"Undoubtedly, I did,\" said Mr. Slope, \"but that was because nobody else happened to be in the room. Surely it was no fault of mine if the rest of the family were out.\"\n\n\"Perhaps not, but I assure you, Mr. Slope, you will fall greatly in my estimation if I find that you allow yourself to be caught by the lures of that woman. I know women better than you do, Mr. Slope, and you may believe me that that signora, as she calls herself, is not a fitting companion for a strict evangelical unmarried young clergyman.\"\n\nHow Mr. Slope would have liked to laugh at her, had he dared! But he did not dare. So he merely said, \"I can assure you, Mrs. Proudie, the lady in question is nothing to me.\"\n\n\"Well, I hope not, Mr. Slope. But I have considered it my duty to give you this caution. And now there is another thing I feel myself called on to speak about: it is your conduct to the bishop, Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"My conduct to the bishop,\" said he, now truly surprised and ignorant what the lady alluded to.\n\n\"Yes, Mr. Slope, your conduct to the bishop. It is by no means what I would wish to see it.\"\n\n\"Has the bishop said anything, Mrs. Proudie?\"\n\n\"No, the bishop has said nothing. He probably thinks that any remarks on the matter will come better from me, who first introduced you to his lordship's notice. The fact is, Mr. Slope, you are a little inclined to take too much upon yourself.\"\n\nAn angry spot showed itself on Mr. Slope's cheeks, and it was with difficulty that he controlled himself. But he did do so, and sat quite silent while the lady went on.\n\n\"It is the fault of many young men in your position, and therefore the bishop is not inclined at present to resent it. You will, no doubt, soon learn what is required from you and what is not. If you will take my advice, however, you will be careful not to obtrude advice upon the bishop in any matter touching patronage. If his lordship wants advice, he knows where to look for it.\" And then having added to her counsel a string of platitudes as to what was desirable and what not desirable in the conduct of a strictly evangelical unmarried young clergyman, Mrs. Proudie retreated, leaving the chaplain to his thoughts.\n\nThe upshot of his thoughts was this, that there certainly was not room in the diocese for the energies of both himself and Mrs. Proudie, and that it behoved him quickly to ascertain whether his energies or hers were to prevail.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. The Widow's Persecution\n\nEarly on the following morning Mr. Slope was summoned to the bishop's dressing-room, and went there fully expecting that he should find his lordship very indignant and spirited up by his wife to repeat the rebuke which she had administered on the previous day. Mr. Slope had resolved that at any rate from him he would not stand it, and entered the dressing-room in rather a combative disposition; but he found the bishop in the most placid and gentlest of humours. His lordship complained of being rather unwell, had a slight headache, and was not quite the thing in his stomach; but there was nothing the matter with his temper.\n\n\"Oh, Slope,\" said he, taking the chaplain's proffered hand, \"Archdeacon Grantly is to call on me this morning, and I really am not fit to see him. I fear I must trouble you to see him for me;\" and then Dr. Proudie proceeded to explain what it was that must be said to Dr. Grantly. He was to be told in fact, in the civilest words in which the tidings could be conveyed, that Mr. Harding having refused the wardenship, the appointment had been offered to Mr. Quiverful and accepted by him.\n\nMr. Slope again pointed out to his patron that he thought he was perhaps not quite wise in his decision, and this he did sotto voce. But even with this precaution it was not safe to say much, and during the little that he did say, the bishop made a very slight, but still a very ominous gesture with his thumb towards the door which opened from his dressing-room to some inner sanctuary. Mr. Slope at once took the hint and said no more, but he perceived that there was to be confidence between him and his patron, that the league desired by him was to be made, and that this appointment of Mr. Quiverful was to be the last sacrifice offered on the altar of conjugal obedience. All this Mr. Slope read in the slight motion of the bishop's thumb, and he read it correctly. There was no need of parchments and seals, of attestations, explanations, and professions. The bargain was understood between them, and Mr. Slope gave the bishop his hand upon it. The bishop understood the little extra squeeze, and an intelligible gleam of assent twinkled in his eye.\n\n\"Pray be civil to the archdeacon, Mr. Slope,\" said he out loud, \"but make him quite understand that in this matter Mr. Harding has put it out of my power to oblige him.\"\n\nIt would be a calumny on Mrs. Proudie to suggest that she was sitting in her bedroom with her ear at the keyhole during this interview. She had within her a spirit of decorum which prevented her from descending to such baseness. To put her ear to a keyhole, or to listen at a chink, was a trick for a housemaid. Mrs. Proudie knew this, and therefore did not do it; but she stationed herself as near to the door as she well could, that she might, if possible, get the advantage which the housemaid would have had, without descending to the housemaid's artifice.\n\nIt was little, however, that she heard, and that little was only sufficient to deceive her. She saw nothing of that friendly pressure, perceived nothing of that concluded bargain; she did not even dream of the treacherous resolves which those two false men had made together to upset her in the pride of her station, to dash the cup from her lip before she had drunk of it, to sweep away all her power before she had tasted its sweets! Traitors that they were, the husband of her bosom and the outcast whom she had fostered and brought to the warmth of the world's brightest fireside! But neither of them had the magnanimity of this woman. Though two men have thus leagued themselves together against her, even yet the battle is not lost.\n\nMr. Slope felt pretty sure that Dr. Grantly would decline the honour of seeing him, and such turned out to be the case. The archdeacon, when the palace door was opened to him, was greeted by a note. Mr. Slope presented his compliments, &c. &c. The bishop was ill in his room and very greatly regretted, &c. &c. Mr. Slope had been charged with the bishop's views, and if agreeable to the archdeacon, would do himself the honour, &c. &c. The archdeacon, however, was not agreeable, and having read his note in the hall, crumpled it up in his hand, and muttering something about sorrow for his lordship's illness, took his leave, without sending as much as a verbal message in answer to Mr. Slope's note.\n\n\"Ill!\" said the archdeacon to himself as he flung himself into his brougham. \"The man is absolutely a coward. He is afraid to see me. Ill, indeed!\" The archdeacon was never ill himself, and did not therefore understand that anyone else could in truth be prevented by illness from keeping an appointment. He regarded all such excuses as subterfuges, and in the present instance he was not far wrong.\n\nDr. Grantly desired to be driven to his father-in-law's lodgings in the High Street, and hearing from the servant that Mr. Harding was at his daughter's, followed him to Mrs. Bold's house, and there found him. The archdeacon was fuming with rage when he got into the drawing-room, and had by this time nearly forgotten the pusillanimity of the bishop in the villainy of the chaplain.\n\n\"Look at that,\" said he, throwing Mr. Slope's crumpled note to Mr. Harding. \"I am to be told that if I choose I may have the honour of seeing Mr. Slope, and that too after a positive engagement with the bishop.\"\n\n\"But he says the bishop is ill,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Pshaw! You don't mean to say that you are deceived by such an excuse as that. He was well enough yesterday. Now I tell you what, I will see the bishop, and I will tell him also very plainly what I think of his conduct. I will see him, or else Barchester will soon be too hot to hold him.\"\n\nEleanor was sitting in the room, but Dr. Grantly had hardly noticed her in his anger. Eleanor now said to him with the greatest innocence, \"I wish you had seen Mr. Slope, Dr. Grantly, because I think perhaps it might have done good.\"\n\nThe archdeacon turned on her with almost brutal wrath. Had she at once owned that she had accepted Mr. Slope for her second husband, he could hardly have felt more convinced of her belonging body and soul to the Slope and Proudie party than he now did on hearing her express such a wish as this. Poor Eleanor!\n\n\"See him!\" said the archdeacon glaring at her. \"And why am I to be called on to lower myself in the world's esteem and my own by coming in contact with such a man as that? I have hitherto lived among gentlemen, and do not mean to be dragged into other company by anybody.\"\n\nPoor Mr. Harding well knew what the archdeacon meant, but Eleanor was as innocent as her own baby. She could not understand how the archdeacon could consider himself to be dragged into bad company by condescending to speak to Mr. Slope for a few minutes when the interests of her father might be served by his doing so.\n\n\"I was talking for a full hour yesterday to Mr. Slope,\" said she with some little assumption of dignity, \"and I did not find myself lowered by it.\"\n\n\"Perhaps not,\" said he. \"But if you'll be good enough to allow me, I shall judge for myself in such matters. And I tell you what, Eleanor; it will be much better for you if you will allow yourself to be guided also by the advice of those who are your friends. If you do not, you will be apt to find that you have no friends left who can advise you.\"\n\nEleanor blushed up to the roots of her hair. But even now she had not the slightest idea of what was passing in the archdeacon's mind. No thought of love-making or love-receiving had yet found its way to her heart since the death of poor John Bold, and if it were possible that such a thought should spring there, the man must be far different from Mr. Slope that could give it birth.\n\nNevertheless Eleanor blushed deeply, for she felt she was charged with improper conduct, and she did so with the more inward pain because her father did not instantly rally to her side--that father for whose sake and love she had submitted to be the receptacle of Mr. Slope's confidence. She had given a detailed account of all that had passed to her father, and though he had not absolutely agreed with her about Mr. Slope's views touching the hospital, yet he had said nothing to make her think that she had been wrong in talking to him.\n\nShe was far too angry to humble herself before her brother-in-law. Indeed, she had never accustomed herself to be very abject before him, and they had never been confidential allies. \"I do not the least understand what you mean, Dr. Grantly,\" said she. \"I do not know that I can accuse myself of doing anything that my friends should disapprove. Mr. Slope called here expressly to ask what Papa's wishes were about the hospital, and as I believe he called with friendly intentions, I told him.\"\n\n\"Friendly intentions!\" sneered the archdeacon.\n\n\"I believe you greatly wrong Mr. Slope,\" continued Eleanor, \"but I have explained this to Papa already; and as you do not seem to approve of what I say, Dr. Grantly, I will with your permission leave you and Papa together;\" so saying, she walked slowly out of the room.\n\nAll this made Mr. Harding very unhappy. It was quite clear that the archdeacon and his wife had made up their minds that Eleanor was going to marry Mr. Slope. Mr. Harding could not really bring himself to think that she would do so, but yet he could not deny that circumstances made it appear that the man's company was not disagreeable to her. She was now constantly seeing him, and yet she received visits from no other unmarried gentleman. She always took his part when his conduct was canvassed, although she was aware how personally objectionable he was to her friends. Then, again, Mr. Harding felt that if she should choose to become Mrs. Slope, he had nothing that he could justly urge against her doing so. She had full right to please herself, and he, as a father, could not say that she would disgrace herself by marrying a clergyman who stood so well before the world as Mr. Slope did. As for quarrelling with his daughter on account of such a marriage, and separating himself from her as the archdeacon had threatened to do, that, with Mr. Harding, would be out of the question. If she should determine to marry this man, he must get over his aversion as best he could. His Eleanor, his own old companion in their old happy home, must still be the friend of his bosom, the child of his heart. Let who would cast her off, he would not. If it were fated that he should have to sit in his old age at the same table with that man whom of all men he disliked the most, he would meet his fate as best he might. Anything to him would be preferable to the loss of his daughter.\n\nSuch being his feelings, he hardly knew how to take part with Eleanor against the archdeacon, or with the archdeacon against Eleanor. It will be said that he should never have suspected her.--Alas! he never should have done so. But Mr. Harding was by no means a perfect character. In his indecision, his weakness, his proneness to be led by others, his want of self-confidence, he was very far from being perfect. And then it must be remembered that such a marriage as that which the archdeacon contemplated with disgust, which we who know Mr. Slope so well would regard with equal disgust, did not appear so monstrous to Mr. Harding because in his charity he did not hate the chaplain as the archdeacon did, and as we do.\n\nHe was, however, very unhappy when his daughter left the room, and he had recourse to an old trick of his that was customary to him in his times of sadness. He began playing some slow tune upon an imaginary violoncello, drawing one hand slowly backwards and forwards as though he held a bow in it, and modulating the unreal chords with the other.\n\n\"She'll marry that man as sure as two and two make four,\" said the practical archdeacon.\n\n\"I hope not, I hope not,\" said the father. \"But if she does, what can I say to her? I have no right to object to him.\"\n\n\"No right!\" exclaimed Dr. Grantly.\n\n\"No right as her father. He is in my own profession and, for aught we know, a good man.\"\n\nTo this the archdeacon would by no means assent. It was not well, however, to argue the case against Eleanor in her own drawing-room, and so they both walked forth and discussed the matter in all its bearings under the elm-trees of the close. Mr. Harding also explained to his son-in-law what had been the purport, at any rate the alleged purport, of Mr. Slope's last visit to the widow. He, however, stated that he could not bring himself to believe that Mr. Slope had any real anxiety such as that he had pretended. \"I cannot forget his demeanour to myself,\" said Mr. Harding, \"and it is not possible that his ideas should have changed so soon.\"\n\n\"I see it all,\" said the archdeacon. \"The sly tartuffe! He thinks to buy the daughter by providing for the father. He means to show how powerful he is, how good he is, and how much he is willing to do for her beaux yeux; yes, I see it all now. But we'll be too many for him yet, Mr. Harding;\" he said, turning to his companion with some gravity and pressing his hand upon the other's arm. \"It would, perhaps, be better for you to lose the hospital than get it on such terms.\"\n\n\"Lose it!\" said Mr. Harding; \"why I've lost it already. I don't want it. I've made up my mind to do without it. I'll withdraw altogether. I'll just go and write a line to the bishop and tell him that I withdraw my claim altogether.\"\n\nNothing would have pleased him better than to be allowed to escape from the trouble and difficulty in such a manner. But he was now going too fast for the archdeacon.\n\n\"No--no--no! We'll do no such thing,\" said Dr. Grantly. \"We'll still have the hospital. I hardly doubt but that we'll have it. But not by Mr. Slope's assistance. If that be necessary, we'll lose it; but we'll have it, spite of his teeth, if we can. Arabin will be at Plumstead to-morrow; you must come over and talk to him.\"\n\nThe two now turned into the cathedral library, which was used by the clergymen of the close as a sort of ecclesiastical club-room, for writing sermons and sometimes letters; also for reading theological works and sometimes magazines and newspapers. The theological works were not disturbed, perhaps, quite as often as from the appearance of the building the outside public might have been led to expect. Here the two allies settled on their course of action. The archdeacon wrote a letter to the bishop, strongly worded, but still respectful, in which he put forward his father-in-law's claim to the appointment and expressed his own regret that he had not been able to see his lordship when he called. Of Mr. Slope he made no mention whatsoever. It was then settled that Mr. Harding should go out to Plumstead on the following day, and after considerable discussion on the matter the archdeacon proposed to ask Eleanor there also, so as to withdraw her, if possible, from Mr. Slope's attentions. \"A week or two,\" said he, \"may teach her what he is, and while she is there she will be out of harm's way. Mr. Slope won't come there after her.\"\n\nEleanor was not a little surprised when her brother-in-law came back and very civilly pressed her to go out to Plumstead with her father. She instantly perceived that her father had been fighting her battles for her behind her back. She felt thankful to him, and for his sake she would not show her resentment to the archdeacon by refusing his invitation. But she could not, she said, go on the morrow; she had an invitation to drink tea at the Stanhopes, which she had promised to accept. She would, she added, go with her father on the next day, if he would wait; or she would follow him.\n\n\"The Stanhopes!\" said Dr. Grantly. \"I did not know you were so intimate with them.\"\n\n\"I did not know it myself,\" said she, \"till Miss Stanhope called yesterday. However, I like her very much, and I have promised to go and play chess with some of them.\"\n\n\"Have they a party there?\" said the archdeacon, still fearful of Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Oh, no,\" said Eleanor; \"Miss Stanhope said there was to be nobody at all. But she had heard that Mary had left me for a few weeks, and she had learnt from someone that I play chess, and so she came over on purpose to ask me to go in.\"\n\n\"Well, that's very friendly,\" said the ex-warden. \"They certainly do look more like foreigners than English people, but I dare say they are none the worse for that.\"\n\nThe archdeacon was inclined to look upon the Stanhopes with favourable eyes, and had nothing to object on the matter. It was therefore arranged that Mr. Harding should postpone his visit to Plumstead for one day and then take with him Eleanor, the baby, and the nurse.\n\nMr. Slope is certainly becoming of some importance in Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. Barchester by Moonlight\n\nThere was much cause for grief and occasional perturbation of spirits in the Stanhope family, but yet they rarely seemed to be grieved or to be disturbed. It was the peculiar gift of each of them that each was able to bear his or her own burden without complaint, and perhaps without sympathy. They habitually looked on the sunny side of the wall, if there was a gleam on either side for them to look at; if there was none, they endured the shade with an indifference which, if not stoical, answered the end at which the Stoics aimed. Old Stanhope could not but feel that he had ill-performed his duties as a father and a clergyman, and could hardly look forward to his own death without grief at the position in which he would leave his family. His income for many years had been as high as £3,000 a year, and yet they had among them no other provision than their mother's fortune of £10,000. He had not only spent his income, but was in debt. Yet with all this he seldom showed much outward sign of trouble.\n\nIt was the same with the mother. If she added little to the pleasures of her children, she detracted still less: she neither grumbled at her lot, nor spoke much of her past or future sufferings; as long as she had a maid to adjust her dress, and had those dresses well made, nature with her was satisfied. It was the same with the children. Charlotte never rebuked her father with the prospect of their future poverty, nor did it seem to grieve her that she was becoming an old maid so quickly; her temper was rarely ruffled, and, if we might judge by her appearance, she was always happy. The signora was not so sweet-tempered, but she possessed much enduring courage; she seldom complained--never, indeed, to her family. Though she had a cause for affliction which would have utterly broken down the heart of most women as beautiful as she and as devoid of all religious support, yet she bore her suffering in silence, or alluded to it only to elicit the sympathy and stimulate the admiration of the men with whom she flirted. As to Bertie, one would have imagined from the sound of his voice and the gleam of his eye that he had not a sorrow nor a care in the world. Nor had he. He was incapable of anticipating to-morrow's griefs. The prospect of future want no more disturbed his appetite than does that of the butcher's knife disturb the appetite of the sheep.\n\nSuch was the usual tenor of their way; but there were rare exceptions. Occasionally the father would allow an angry glance to fall from his eye, and the lion would send forth a low dangerous roar as though he meditated some deed of blood. Occasionally also Madame Neroni would become bitter against mankind, more than usually antagonistic to the world's decencies, and would seem as though she was about to break from her moorings and allow herself to be carried forth by the tide of her feelings to utter ruin and shipwreck. She, however, like the rest of them, had no real feelings, could feel no true passion. In that was her security. Before she resolved on any contemplated escapade she would make a small calculation, and generally summed up that the Stanhope villa or even Barchester close was better than the world at large.\n\nThey were most irregular in their hours. The father was generally the earliest in the breakfast-parlour, and Charlotte would soon follow and give him his coffee, but the others breakfasted anywhere, anyhow, and at any time. On the morning after the archdeacon's futile visit to the palace, Dr. Stanhope came downstairs with an ominously dark look about his eyebrows; his white locks were rougher than usual, and he breathed thickly and loudly as he took his seat in his armchair. He had open letters in his hand, and when Charlotte came into the room, he was still reading them. She went up and kissed him as was her wont, but he hardly noticed her as she did so, and she knew at once that something was the matter.\n\n\"What's the meaning of that?\" said he, throwing over the table a letter with a Milan postmark. Charlotte was a little frightened as she took it up, but her mind was relieved when she saw that it was merely the bill of their Italian milliner. The sum total was certainly large, but not so large as to create an important row.\n\n\"It's for our clothes, Papa, for six months before we came here. The three of us can't dress for nothing, you know.\"\n\n\"Nothing, indeed!\" said he, looking at the figures which, in Milanese denominations, were certainly monstrous.\n\n\"The man should have sent it to me,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"I wish he had with all my heart--if you would have paid it. I see enough in it to know that three quarters of it are for Madeline.\"\n\n\"She has little else to amuse her, sir,\" said Charlotte with true good nature.\n\n\"And I suppose he has nothing else to amuse him,\" said the doctor, throwing over another letter to his daughter. It was from some member of the family of Sidonia, and politely requested the father to pay a small trifle of £700, being the amount of a bill discounted in favour of Mr. Ethelbert Stanhope and now overdue for a period of nine months.\n\nCharlotte read the letter, slowly folded it up, and put it under the edge of the tea-tray.\n\n\"I suppose he has nothing to amuse him but discounting bills with Jews. Does he think I'll pay that?\"\n\n\"I am sure he thinks no such thing,\" said she.\n\n\"And who does he think will pay it?\"\n\n\"As far as honesty goes I suppose it won't much matter if it is never paid,\" said she. \"I dare say he got very little of it.\"\n\n\"I suppose it won't much matter either,\" said the father, \"if he goes to prison and rots there. It seems to me that that's the other alternative.\"\n\nDr. Stanhope spoke of the custom of his youth. But his daughter, though she had lived so long abroad, was much more completely versed in the ways of the English world. \"If the man arrests him,\" said she, \"he must go through the court.\"\n\nIt is thus, thou great family of Sidonia--it is thus that we Gentiles treat thee, when, in our extremest need, thou and thine have aided us with mountains of gold as big as lions--and occasionally with wine-warrants and orders for dozens of dressing-cases.\n\n\"What, and become an insolvent?\" said the doctor.\n\n\"He's that already,\" said Charlotte, wishing always to get over a difficulty.\n\n\"What a condition,\" said the doctor, \"for the son of a clergyman of the Church of England.\"\n\n\"I don't see why clergymen's sons should pay their debts more than other young men,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"He's had as much from me since he left school as is held sufficient for the eldest son of many a nobleman,\" said the angry father.\n\n\"Well, sir,\" said Charlotte, \"give him another chance.\"\n\n\"What!\" said the doctor, \"do you mean that I am to pay that Jew?\"\n\n\"Oh, no! I wouldn't pay him, he must take his chance; and if the worst comes to the worst, Bertie must go abroad. But I want you to be civil to Bertie and let him remain here as long as we stop. He has a plan in his head that may put him on his feet after all.\"\n\n\"Has he any plan for following up his profession?\"\n\n\"Oh, he'll do that too; but that must follow. He's thinking of getting married.\"\n\nJust at that moment the door opened, and Bertie came in whistling. The doctor immediately devoted himself to his egg and allowed Bertie to whistle himself round to his sister's side without noticing him.\n\nCharlotte gave a sign to him with her eye, first glancing at her father, and then at the letter, the corner of which peeped out from under the tea-tray. Bertie saw and understood, and with the quiet motion of a cat he abstracted the letter and made himself acquainted with its contents. The doctor, however, had seen him, deep as he appeared to be mersed in his egg-shell, and said in his harshest voice, \"Well, sir, do you know that gentleman?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Bertie. \"I have a sort of acquaintance with him, but none that can justify him in troubling you. If you will allow me, sir, I will answer this.\"\n\n\"At any rate I shan't,\" said the father, and then he added, after a pause, \"Is it true, sir, that you owe the man £700?\"\n\n\"Well,\" said Bertie, \"I think I should be inclined to dispute the amount, if I were in a condition to pay him such of it as I really do owe him.\"\n\n\"Has he your bill for £700?\" said the father, speaking very loudly and very angrily.\n\n\"Well, I believe he has,\" said Bertie, \"but all the money I ever got from him was £150.\"\n\n\"And what became of the £550?\"\n\n\"Why, sir, the commission was £100 or so, and I took the remainder in paving-stones and rocking-horses.\"\n\n\"Paving-stones and rocking-horses!\" said the doctor. \"Where are they?\"\n\n\"Oh, sir, I suppose they are in London somewhere--but I'll inquire if you wish for them.\"\n\n\"He's an idiot,\" said the doctor, \"and it's sheer folly to waste more money on him. Nothing can save him from ruin,\" and so saying, the unhappy father walked out of the room.\n\n\"Would the governor like to have the paving-stones?\" said Bertie to his sister.\n\n\"I'll tell you what,\" said she. \"If you don't take care, you will find yourself loose upon the world without even a house over your head; you don't know him as well as I do. He's very angry.\"\n\nBertie stroked his big beard, sipped his tea, chatted over his misfortunes in a half-comic, half-serious tone, and ended by promising his sister that he would do his very best to make himself agreeable to the Widow Bold. Then Charlotte followed her father to his own room, softened down his wrath, and persuaded him to say nothing more about the Jew bill discounter, at any rate for a few weeks. He even went so far as to say he would pay the £700, or at any rate settle the bill, if he saw a certainty of his son's securing for himself anything like a decent provision in life. Nothing was said openly between them about poor Eleanor, but the father and the daughter understood each other.\n\nThey all met together in the drawing-room at nine o'clock, in perfect good humour with each other, and about that hour Mrs. Bold was announced. She had never been in the house before, though she had of course called, and now she felt it strange to find herself there in her usual evening dress, entering the drawing-room of these strangers in this friendly, unceremonious way, as though she had known them all her life. But in three minutes they made her at home. Charlotte tripped downstairs and took her bonnet from her, and Bertie came to relieve her from her shawl, and the signora smiled on her as she could smile when she chose to be gracious, and the old doctor shook hands with her in a kind benedictory manner that went to her heart at once and made her feel that he must be a good man.\n\nShe had not been seated for above five minutes when the door again opened and Mr. Slope was announced. She felt rather surprised, because she was told that nobody was to be there, and it was very evident from the manner of some of them that Mr. Slope was not unexpected. But still there was not much in it. In such invitations a bachelor or two more or less are always spoken of as nobodies, and there was no reason why Mr. Slope should not drink tea at Dr. Stanhope's as well as Eleanor herself. He, however, was very much surprised and not very much gratified at finding that his own embryo spouse made one of the party. He had come there to gratify himself by gazing on Madame Neroni's beauty and listening to and returning her flattery: and though he had not owned as much to himself, he still felt that if he spent the evening as he had intended to do, he might probably not thereby advance his suit with Mrs. Bold.\n\nThe signora, who had no idea of a rival, received Mr. Slope with her usual marks of distinction. As he took her hand, she made some confidential communication to him in a low voice, declaring that she had a plan to communicate to him after tea, and was evidently prepared to go on with her work of reducing the chaplain to a state of captivity. Poor Mr. Slope was rather beside himself. He thought that Eleanor could not but have learnt from his demeanour that he was an admirer of her own, and he had also flattered himself that the idea was not unacceptable to her. What would she think of him if he now devoted himself to a married woman!\n\nBut Eleanor was not inclined to be severe in her criticisms on him in this respect, and felt no annoyance of any kind, when she found herself seated between Bertie and Charlotte Stanhope. She had no suspicion of Mr. Slope's intentions; she had no suspicion even of the suspicion of other people; but still she felt well-pleased not to have Mr. Slope too near to her.\n\nAnd she was not ill-pleased to have Bertie Stanhope near her. It was rarely indeed that he failed to make an agreeable impression on strangers. With a bishop indeed who thought much of his own dignity it was possible that he might fail, but hardly with a young and pretty woman. He possessed the tact of becoming instantly intimate with women without giving rise to any fear of impertinence. He had about him somewhat of the propensities of a tame cat. It seemed quite natural that he should be petted, caressed, and treated with familiar good nature, and that in return he should purr, and be sleek and graceful, and above all never show his claws. Like other tame cats, however, he had his claws, and sometimes made them dangerous.\n\nWhen tea was over, Charlotte went to the open window and declared loudly that the full harvest moon was much too beautiful to be disregarded, and called them all to look at it. To tell the truth there was but one there who cared much about the moon's beauty, and that one was not Charlotte, but she knew how valuable an aid to her purpose the chaste goddess might become, and could easily create a little enthusiasm for the purpose of the moment. Eleanor and Bertie were soon with her. The doctor was now quiet in his armchair, and Mrs. Stanhope in hers, both prepared for slumber.\n\n\"Are you a Whewellite or a Brewsterite, or a t'othermanite, Mrs. Bold?\" said Charlotte, who knew a little about everything, and had read about a third of each of the books to which she alluded.\n\n\"Oh!\" said Eleanor; \"I have not read any of the books, but I feel sure that there is one man in the moon at least, if not more.\"\n\n\"You don't believe in the pulpy gelatinous matter?\" said Bertie.\n\n\"I heard about that,\" said Eleanor, \"and I really think it's almost wicked to talk in such a manner. How can we argue about God's power in the other stars from the laws which he has given for our rule in this one?\"\n\n\"How indeed!\" said Bertie. \"Why shouldn't there be a race of salamanders in Venus? And even if there be nothing but fish in Jupiter, why shouldn't the fish there be as wide awake as the men and women here?\"\n\n\"That would be saying very little for them,\" said Charlotte. \"I am for Dr. Whewell myself, for I do not think that men and women are worth being repeated in such countless worlds. There may be souls in other stars, but I doubt their having any bodies attached to them. But come, Mrs. Bold, let us put our bonnets on and walk round the close. If we are to discuss sidereal questions, we shall do so much better under the towers of the cathedral than stuck in this narrow window.\"\n\nMrs. Bold made no objection, and a party was made to walk out. Charlotte Stanhope well knew the rule as to three being no company, and she had therefore to induce her sister to allow Mr. Slope to accompany them.\n\n\"Come, Mr. Slope,\" she said, \"I'm sure you'll join us. We shall be in again in a quarter of an hour, Madeline.\"\n\nMadeline read in her eye all that she had to say, knew her object, and as she had to depend on her sister for so many of her amusements, she felt that she must yield. It was hard to be left alone while others of her own age walked out to feel the soft influence of the bright night, but it would be harder still to be without the sort of sanction which Charlotte gave to all her flirtations and intrigues. Charlotte's eye told her that she must give up just at present for the good of the family, and so Madeline obeyed.\n\nBut Charlotte's eyes said nothing of the sort to Mr. Slope. He had no objection at all to the tête-à-tête with the signora which the departure of the other three would allow him, and gently whispered to her, \"I shall not leave you alone.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said she; \"go--pray go, pray go, for my sake. Do not think that I am so selfish. It is understood that nobody is kept within for me. You will understand this too when you know me better. Pray join them, Mr. Slope, but when you come in speak to me for five minutes before you leave us.\"\n\nMr. Slope understood that he was to go, and he therefore joined the party in the hall. He would have had no objection at all to this arrangement, if he could have secured Mrs. Bold's arm; but this of course was out of the question. Indeed, his fate was very soon settled, for no sooner had he reached the hall-door than Miss Stanhope put her hand within his arm, and Bertie walked off with Eleanor just as naturally as though she were already his own property.\n\nAnd so they sauntered forth: first they walked round the close, according to their avowed intent; then they went under the old arched gateway below St. Cuthbert's little church, and then they turned behind the grounds of the bishop's palace, and so on till they came to the bridge just at the edge of the town, from which passers-by can look down into the gardens of Hiram's Hospital; and here Charlotte and Mr. Slope, who were in advance, stopped till the other two came up to them. Mr. Slope knew that the gable-ends and old brick chimneys which stood up so prettily in the moonlight were those of Mr. Harding's late abode, and would not have stopped on such a spot, in such company, if he could have avoided it; but Miss Stanhope would not take the hint which he tried to give.\n\n\"This is a very pretty place, Mrs. Bold,\" said Charlotte; \"by far the prettiest place near Barchester. I wonder your father gave it up.\"\n\nIt was a very pretty place, and now by the deceitful light of the moon looked twice larger, twice prettier, twice more antiquely picturesque than it would have done in truth-telling daylight. Who does not know the air of complex multiplicity and the mysterious interesting grace which the moon always lends to old gabled buildings half-surrounded, as was the hospital, by fine trees! As seen from the bridge on the night of which we are speaking, Mr. Harding's late abode did look very lovely, and though Eleanor did not grieve at her father's having left it, she felt at the moment an intense wish that he might be allowed to return.\n\n\"He is going to return to it almost immediately, is he not?\" asked Bertie.\n\nEleanor made no immediate reply. Many such a question passes unanswered without the notice of the questioner, but such was not now the case. They all remained silent as though expecting her to reply, and after a moment or two, Charlotte said, \"I believe it is settled that Mr. Harding returns to the hospital, is it not?\"\n\n\"I don't think anything about it is settled yet,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"But it must be a matter of course,\" said Bertie; \"that is, if your father wishes it. Who else on earth could hold it after what has occurred?\"\n\nEleanor quietly made her companion understand that the matter was one which she could not discuss in the present company, and then they passed on. Charlotte said she would go a short way up the hill out of the town so as to look back upon the towers of the cathedral, and as Eleanor leant upon Bertie's arm for assistance in the walk, she told him how the matter stood between her father and the bishop.\n\n\"And, he,\" said Bertie, pointing on to Mr. Slope, \"what part does he take in it?\"\n\nEleanor explained how Mr. Slope had at first endeavoured to tyrannize over her father, but how he had latterly come round and done all he could to talk the bishop over in Mr. Harding's favour. \"But my father,\" she said, \"is hardly inclined to trust him; they all say he is so arrogant to the old clergymen of the city.\"\n\n\"Take my word for it,\" said Bertie, \"your father is right. If I am not very much mistaken, that man is both arrogant and false.\"\n\nThey strolled up to the top of the hill and then returned through the fields by a foot-path which leads by a small wooden bridge, or rather a plank with a rustic rail to it, over the river to the other side of the cathedral from that at which they had started. They had thus walked round the bishop's grounds, through which the river runs, and round the cathedral and adjacent fields, and it was past eleven before they reached the doctor's door.\n\n\"It is very late,\" said Eleanor; \"it will be a shame to disturb your mother again at such an hour.\"\n\n\"Oh\"' said Charlotte, laughing, \"you won't disturb Mamma; I dare say she is in bed by this time, and Madeline would be furious if you did not come in and see her. Come, Bertie, take Mrs. Bold's bonnet from her.\"\n\nThey went upstairs and found the signora alone, reading. She looked somewhat sad and melancholy, but not more so perhaps than was sufficient to excite additional interest in the bosom of Mr. Slope; and she was soon deep in whispered intercourse with that happy gentleman, who was allowed to find a resting-place on her sofa. The signora had a way of whispering that was peculiarly her own, and was exactly the reverse of that which prevails among great tragedians. The great tragedian hisses out a positive whisper, made with bated breath, and produced by inarticulated tongue-formed sounds, but yet he is audible through the whole house. The signora, however, used no hisses and produced all her words in a clear, silver tone, but they could only be heard by the ear into which they were poured.\n\nCharlotte hurried and scurried about the room hither and thither, doing, or pretending to do many things; then, saying something about seeing her mother, ran upstairs. Eleanor was thus left alone with Bertie, and she hardly felt an hour fly by her. To give Bertie his due credit, he could not have played his cards better. He did not make love to her, nor sigh, nor look languishing, but he was amusing and familiar, yet respectful; and when he left Eleanor at her own door at one o'clock, which he did by the by with the assistance of the now jealous Slope, she thought that he was one of the most agreeable men and the Stanhopes decidedly the most agreeable family that she had ever met.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. Mr. Arabin\n\nThe Rev. Francis Arabin, fellow of Lazarus, late professor of poetry at Oxford, and present vicar of St. Ewold, in the diocese of Barchester, must now be introduced personally to the reader. He is worthy of a new volume, and as he will fill a conspicuous place in it, it is desirable that he should be made to stand before the reader's eye by the aid of such portraiture as the author is able to produce.\n\nIt is to be regretted that no mental method of daguerreotype or photography has yet been discovered by which the characters of men can be reduced to writing and put into grammatical language with an unerring precision of truthful description. How often does the novelist feel, ay, and the historian also and the biographer, that he has conceived within his mind and accurately depicted on the tablet of his brain the full character and personage of a man, and that nevertheless, when he flies to pen and ink to perpetuate the portrait, his words forsake, elude, disappoint, and play the deuce with him, till at the end of a dozen pages the man described has no more resemblance to the man conceived than the sign-board at the corner of the street has to the Duke of Cambridge.\n\nAnd yet such mechanical descriptive skill would hardly give more satisfaction to the reader than the skill of the photographer does to the anxious mother desirous to possess an absolute duplicate of her beloved child. The likeness is indeed true, but it is a dull, dead, unfeeling, inauspicious likeness. The face is indeed there, and those looking at it will know at once whose image it is, but the owner of the face will not be proud of the resemblance.\n\nThere is no royal road to learning, no short cut to the acquirement of any valuable art. Let photographers and daguerreotypers do what they will, and improve as they may with further skill on that which skill has already done, they will never achieve a portrait of the human face divine. Let biographers, novelists, and the rest of us groan as we may under the burdens which we so often feel too heavy for our shoulders; we must either bear them up like men, or own ourselves too weak for the work we have undertaken. There is no way of writing well and also of writing easily.\n\nLabor omnia vincit improbus. Such should be the chosen motto of every labourer, and it may be that labour, if adequately enduring, may suffice at last to produce even some not untrue resemblance of the Rev. Francis Arabin.\n\nOf his doings in the world, and of the sort of fame which he has achieved, enough has been already said. It has also been said that he is forty years of age, and still unmarried. He was the younger son of a country gentleman of small fortune in the north of England. At an early age he went to Winchester, and was intended by his father for New College; but though studious as a boy, he was not studious within the prescribed limits, and at the age of eighteen he left school with a character for talent, but without a scholarship. All that he had obtained, over and above the advantage of his character, was a gold medal for English verse, and hence was derived a strong presumption on the part of his friends that he was destined to add another name to the imperishable list of English poets.\n\nFrom Winchester he went to Oxford, and was entered as a commoner at Balliol. Here his special career very soon commenced. He utterly eschewed the society of fast men, gave no wine-parties, kept no horses, rowed no boats, joined no rows, and was the pride of his college tutor. Such at least was his career till he had taken his little go, and then he commenced a course of action which, though not less creditable to himself as a man, was hardly so much to the taste of the tutor. He became a member of a vigorous debating society, and rendered himself remarkable there for humorous energy. Though always in earnest, yet his earnestness was always droll. To be true in his ideas, unanswerable in his syllogisms, and just in his aspirations was not enough for him. He had failed, failed in his own opinion as well as that of others when others came to know him, if he could not reduce the arguments of his opponents to an absurdity and conquer both by wit and reason. To say that his object was ever to raise a laugh would be most untrue. He hated such common and unnecessary evidence of satisfaction on the part of his hearers. A joke that required to be laughed at was, with him, not worth uttering. He could appreciate by a keener sense than that of his ears the success of his wit, and would see in the eyes of his auditors whether or no he was understood and appreciated.\n\nHe had been a religious lad before he left school. That is, he had addicted himself to a party in religion, and having done so had received that benefit which most men do who become partisans in such a cause. We are much too apt to look at schism in our church as an unmitigated evil. Moderate schism, if there may be such a thing, at any rate calls attention to the subject, draws in supporters who would otherwise have been inattentive to the matter, and teaches men to think upon religion. How great an amount of good of this description has followed that movement in the Church of England which commenced with the publication of Froude's Remains!\n\nAs a boy young Arabin took up the cudgels on the side of the Tractarians, and at Oxford he sat for a while at the feet of the great Newman. To this cause he lent all his faculties. For it he concocted verses, for it he made speeches, for it he scintillated the brightest sparks of his quiet wit. For it he ate and drank and dressed and had his being. In due process of time he took his degree and wrote himself B.A., but he did not do so with any remarkable amount of academical éclat. He had occupied himself too much with High Church matters and the polemics, politics, and outward demonstrations usually concurrent with High Churchmanship to devote himself with sufficient vigour to the acquisition of a double first. He was not a double first, nor even a first class man, but he revenged himself on the university by putting firsts and double firsts out of fashion for the year and laughing down a species of pedantry which, at the age of twenty-three, leaves no room in a man's mind for graver subjects than conic sections or Greek accents.\n\nGreek accents, however, and conic sections were esteemed necessaries at Balliol, and there was no admittance there for Mr. Arabin within the list of its fellows. Lazarus, however, the richest and most comfortable abode of Oxford dons, opened its bosom to the young champion of a church militant. Mr. Arabin was ordained, and became a fellow soon after taking his degree, and shortly after that was chosen professor of poetry.\n\nAnd now came the moment of his great danger. After many mental struggles, and an agony of doubt which may be well surmised, the great prophet of the Tractarians confessed himself a Roman Catholic. Mr. Newman left the Church of England and with him carried many a waverer. He did not carry off Mr. Arabin, but the escape which that gentleman had was a very narrow one. He left Oxford for awhile that he might meditate in complete peace on the step which appeared to him to be all but unavoidable, and shut himself up in a little village on the sea-shore of one of our remotest counties, that he might learn by communing with his own soul whether or no he could with a safe conscience remain within the pale of his mother church.\n\nThings would have gone badly with him there had he been left entirely to himself. Everything was against him: all his worldly interests required him to remain a Protestant, and he looked on his worldly interests as a legion of foes, to get the better of whom was a point of extremest honour. In his then state of ecstatic agony such a conquest would have cost him little; he could easily have thrown away all his livelihood; but it cost him much to get over the idea that by choosing the Church of England he should be open in his own mind to the charge that he had been led to such a choice by unworthy motives. Then his heart was against him: he loved with a strong and eager love the man who had hitherto been his guide, and yearned to follow his footsteps. His tastes were against him: the ceremonies and pomps of the Church of Rome, their august feasts and solemn fasts, invited his imagination and pleased his eye. His flesh was against him: how great an aid would it be to a poor, weak, wavering man to be constrained to high moral duties, self-denial, obedience, and chastity by laws which were certain in their enactments, and not to be broken without loud, palpable, unmistakable sin! Then his faith was against him: he required to believe so much; panted so eagerly to give signs of his belief; deemed it so insufficient to wash himself simply in the waters of Jordan; that some great deed, such as that of forsaking everything for a true Church, had for him allurements almost past withstanding.\n\nMr. Arabin was at this time a very young man, and when he left Oxford for his far retreat was much too confident in his powers of fence, and too apt to look down on the ordinary sense of ordinary people, to expect aid in the battle that he had to fight from any chance inhabitants of the spot which he had selected. But Providence was good to him; there, in that all but desolate place, on the storm-beat shore of that distant sea, he met one who gradually calmed his mind, quieted his imagination, and taught him something of a Christian's duty. When Mr. Arabin left Oxford, he was inclined to look upon the rural clergymen of most English parishes almost with contempt. It was his ambition, should he remain within the fold of their church, to do somewhat towards redeeming and rectifying their inferiority and to assist in infusing energy and faith into the hearts of Christian ministers, who were, as he thought, too often satisfied to go through life without much show of either.\n\nAnd yet it was from such a one that Mr. Arabin in his extremest need received that aid which he so much required. It was from the poor curate of a small Cornish parish that he first learnt to know that the highest laws for the governance of a Christian's duty must act from within and not from without; that no man can become a serviceable servant solely by obedience to written edicts; and that the safety which he was about to seek within the gates of Rome was no other than the selfish freedom from personal danger which the bad soldier attempts to gain who counterfeits illness on the eve of battle.\n\nMr. Arabin returned to Oxford a humbler but a better and a happier man, and from that time forth he put his shoulder to the wheel as a clergyman of the Church for which he had been educated. The intercourse of those among whom he familiarly lived kept him staunch to the principles of that system of the Church to which he had always belonged. Since his severance from Mr. Newman, no one had had so strong an influence over him as the head of his college. During the time of his expected apostasy Dr. Gwynne had not felt much predisposition in favour of the young fellow. Though a High Churchman himself within moderate limits, Dr. Gwynne felt no sympathy with men who could not satisfy their faiths with the Thirty-nine Articles. He regarded the enthusiasm of such as Newman as a state of mind more nearly allied to madness than to religion, and when he saw it evinced by very young men, he was inclined to attribute a good deal of it to vanity. Dr. Gwynne himself, though a religious man, was also a thoroughly practical man of the world, and he regarded with no favourable eye the tenets of anyone who looked on the two things as incompatible. When he found that Mr. Arabin was a half Roman, he began to regret all he had done towards bestowing a fellowship on so unworthy a recipient; and when again he learnt that Mr. Arabin would probably complete his journey to Rome, he regarded with some satisfaction the fact that in such case the fellowship would be again vacant.\n\nWhen, however, Mr. Arabin returned and professed himself a confirmed Protestant, the Master of Lazarus again opened his arms to him, and gradually he became the pet of the college. For some little time he was saturnine, silent, and unwilling to take any prominent part in university broils, but gradually his mind recovered, or rather made its tone, and he became known as a man always ready at a moment's notice to take up the cudgels in opposition to anything that savoured of an evangelical bearing. He was great in sermons, great on platforms, great at after-dinner conversations, and always pleasant as well as great. He took delight in elections, served on committees, opposed tooth and nail all projects of university reform, and talked jovially over his glass of port of the ruin to be anticipated by the Church and of the sacrilege daily committed by the Whigs. The ordeal through which he had gone in resisting the blandishments of the lady of Rome had certainly done much towards the strengthening of his character. Although in small and outward matters he was self-confident enough, nevertheless in things affecting the inner man he aimed at a humility of spirit which would never have been attractive to him but for that visit to the coast of Cornwall. This visit he now repeated every year.\n\nSuch is an interior view of Mr. Arabin at the time when he accepted the living of St. Ewold. Exteriorly, he was not a remarkable person. He was above the middle height, well-made, and very active. His hair, which had been jet black, was now tinged with gray, but his face bore no sign of years. It would perhaps be wrong to say that he was handsome, but his face was nevertheless pleasant to look upon. The cheek-bones were rather too high for beauty, and the formation of the forehead too massive and heavy: but the eyes, nose, and mouth were perfect. There was a continual play of lambent fire about his eyes, which gave promise of either pathos or humour whenever he essayed to speak, and that promise was rarely broken. There was a gentle play about his mouth which declared that his wit never descended to sarcasm, and that there was no ill-nature in his repartee.\n\nMr. Arabin was a popular man among women, but more so as a general than a special favourite. Living as a fellow at Oxford, marriage with him had been out of the question, and it may be doubted whether he had ever allowed his heart to be touched. Though belonging to a church in which celibacy is not the required lot of its ministers, he had come to regard himself as one of those clergymen to whom to be a bachelor is almost a necessity. He had never looked for parochial duty, and his career at Oxford was utterly incompatible with such domestic joys as a wife and nursery. He looked on women, therefore, in the same light that one sees them regarded by many Romish priests. He liked to have near him that which was pretty and amusing, but women generally were little more to him than children. He talked to them without putting out all his powers, and listened to them without any idea that what he should hear from them could either actuate his conduct or influence his opinion.\n\nSuch was Mr. Arabin, the new vicar of St. Ewold, who is going to stay with the Grantlys at Plumstead Episcopi.\n\nMr. Arabin reached Plumstead the day before Mr. Harding and Eleanor, and the Grantly family were thus enabled to make his acquaintance and discuss his qualifications before the arrival of the other guests. Griselda was surprised to find that he looked so young, but she told Florinda her younger sister, when they had retired for the night, that he did not talk at all like a young man: and she decided with the authority that seventeen has over sixteen that he was not at all nice, although his eyes were lovely. As usual, sixteen implicitly acceded to the dictum of seventeen in such a matter, and said that he certainly was not nice. They then branched off on the relative merits of other clerical bachelors in the vicinity, and both determined without any feeling of jealousy between them that a certain Rev. Augustus Green was by many degrees the most estimable of the lot. The gentleman in question had certainly much in his favour, as, having a comfortable allowance from his father, he could devote the whole proceeds of his curacy to violet gloves and unexceptionable neck ties. Having thus fixedly resolved that the new-comer had nothing about him to shake the pre-eminence of the exalted Green, the two girls went to sleep in each other's arms, contented with themselves and the world.\n\nMrs. Grantly at first sight came to much the same conclusion about her husband's favourite as her daughters had done, though, in seeking to measure his relative value, she did not compare him to Mr. Green; indeed, she made no comparison by name between him and anyone else; but she remarked to her husband that one person's swans were very often another person's geese, thereby clearly showing that Mr. Arabin had not yet proved his qualifications in swanhood to her satisfaction.\n\n\"Well, Susan,\" said he, rather offended at hearing his friend spoken of so disrespectfully, \"if you take Mr. Arabin for a goose, I cannot say that I think very highly of your discrimination.\"\n\n\"A goose! No, of course, he's not a goose. I've no doubt he's a very clever man. But you're so matter-of-fact, Archdeacon, when it suits your purpose, that one can't trust oneself to any façon de parler. I've no doubt Mr. Arabin is a very valuable man--at Oxford--and that he'll be a good vicar at St. Ewold. All I mean is that, having passed one evening with him, I don't find him to be absolutely a paragon. In the first place, if I am not mistaken, he is a little inclined to be conceited.\"\n\n\"Of all the men that I know intimately,\" said the archdeacon, \"Arabin is, in my opinion, the most free from any taint of self-conceit. His fault is that he's too diffident.\"\n\n\"Perhaps so,\" said the lady; \"only I must own I did not find it out this evening.\"\n\nNothing further was said about him. Dr. Grantly thought that his wife was abusing Mr. Arabin merely because he had praised him, and Mrs. Grantly knew that it was useless arguing for or against any person in favour of or in opposition to whom the archdeacon had already pronounced a strong opinion.\n\nIn truth, they were both right. Mr. Arabin was a diffident man in social intercourse with those whom he did not intimately know; when placed in situations which it was his business to fill, and discussing matters with which it was his duty to be conversant, Mr. Arabin was from habit brazen-faced enough. When standing on a platform in Exeter Hall, no man would be less mazed than he by the eyes of the crowd before him, for such was the work which his profession had called on him to perform; but he shrank from a strong expression of opinion in general society, and his doing so not uncommonly made it appear that he considered the company not worth the trouble of his energy. He was averse to dictate when the place did not seem to him to justify dictation, and as those subjects on which people wished to hear him speak were such as he was accustomed to treat with decision, he generally shunned the traps there were laid to allure him into discussion, and, by doing so, not infrequently subjected himself to such charges as those brought against him by Mrs. Grantly.\n\nMr. Arabin, as he sat at his open window, enjoying the delicious moonlight and gazing at the gray towers of the church, which stood almost within the rectory grounds, little dreamed that he was the subject of so many friendly or unfriendly criticisms. Considering how much we are all given to discuss the characters of others, and discuss them often not in the strictest spirit of charity, it is singular how little we are inclined to think that others can speak ill-naturedly of us, and how angry and hurt we are when proof reaches us that they have done so. It is hardly too much to say that we all of us occasionally speak of our dearest friends in a manner in which those dearest friends would very little like to hear themselves mentioned, and that we nevertheless expect that our dearest friends shall invariably speak of us as though they were blind to all our faults, but keenly alive to every shade of our virtues.\n\nIt did not occur to Mr. Arabin that he was spoken of at all. It seemed to him, when he compared himself with his host, that he was a person of so little consequence to any, that he was worth no one's words or thoughts. He was utterly alone in the world as regarded domestic ties and those inner familiar relations which are hardly possible between others than husbands and wives, parents and children, or brothers and sisters. He had often discussed with himself the necessity of such bonds for a man's happiness in this world, and had generally satisfied himself with the answer that happiness in this world is not a necessity. Herein he deceived himself, or rather tried to do so. He, like others, yearned for the enjoyment of whatever he saw enjoyable, and though he attempted, with the modern stoicism of so many Christians, to make himself believe that joy and sorrow were matters which here should be held as perfectly indifferent, these things were not indifferent to him. He was tired of his Oxford rooms and his college life. He regarded the wife and children of his friend with something like envy; he all but coveted the pleasant drawing-room, with its pretty windows opening on to lawns and flower-beds, the apparel of the comfortable house, and--above all--the air of home which encompassed it all.\n\nIt will be said that no time can have been so fitted for such desires on his part as this, when he had just possessed himself of a country parish, of a living among fields and gardens, of a house which a wife would grace. It is true there was a difference between the opulence of Plumstead and the modest economy of St. Ewold, but surely Mr. Arabin was not a man to sigh after wealth! Of all men, his friends would have unanimously declared he was the last to do so. But how little our friends know us! In his period of stoical rejection of this world's happiness, he had cast from him as utter dross all anxiety as to fortune. He had, as it were, proclaimed himself to be indifferent to promotion, and those who chiefly admired his talents, and would mainly have exerted themselves to secure to them their deserved reward, had taken him at his word. And now, if the truth must out, he felt himself disappointed--disappointed not by them but by himself. The daydream of his youth was over, and at the age of forty he felt that he was not fit to work in the spirit of an apostle. He had mistaken himself, and learned his mistake when it was past remedy. He had professed himself indifferent to mitres and diaconal residences, to rich livings and pleasant glebes, and now he had to own to himself that he was sighing for the good things of other men on whom, in his pride, he had ventured to look down.\n\nNot for wealth, in its vulgar sense, had he ever sighed; not for the enjoyment of rich things had he ever longed; but for the allotted share of worldly bliss which a wife, and children, and happy home could give him, for that usual amount of comfort which he had ventured to reject as unnecessary for him, he did now feel that he would have been wiser to have searched.\n\nHe knew that his talents, his position, and his friends would have won for him promotion, had he put himself in the way of winning it. Instead of doing so, he had allowed himself to be persuaded to accept a living which would give him an income of some £300 a year should he, by marrying, throw up his fellowship. Such, at the age of forty, was the worldly result of labour which the world had chosen to regard as successful. The world also thought that Mr. Arabin was, in his own estimation, sufficiently paid. Alas! Alas! The world was mistaken, and Mr. Arabin was beginning to ascertain that such was the case.\n\nAnd here may I beg the reader not to be hard in his judgement upon this man. Is not the state at which he has arrived the natural result of efforts to reach that which is not the condition of humanity? Is not modern stoicism, built though it be on Christianity, as great an outrage on human nature as was the stoicism of the ancients? The philosophy of Zeno was built on true laws, but on true laws misunderstood and therefore misapplied. It is the same with our Stoics here, who would teach us that wealth and worldly comfort and happiness on earth are not worth the search. Alas, for a doctrine which can find no believing pupils and no true teachers!\n\nThe case of Mr. Arabin was the more singular, as he belonged to a branch of the Church of England well inclined to regard its temporalities with avowed favour, and had habitually lived with men who were accustomed to much worldly comfort. But such was his idiosyncrasy that these very facts had produced within him, in early life, a state of mind that was not natural to him. He was content to be a High Churchman, if he could be so on principles of his own and could strike out a course showing a marked difference from those with whom he consorted. He was ready to be a partisan as long as he was allowed to have a course of action and of thought unlike that of his party. His party had indulged him, and he began to feel that his party was right and himself wrong, just when such a conviction was too late to be of service to him. He discovered, when such discovery was no longer serviceable, that it would have been worth his while to have worked for the usual pay assigned to work in this world and have earned a wife and children, with a carriage for them to sit in; to have earned a pleasant dining-room, in which his friends could drink his wine, and the power of walking up the high street of his country town, with the knowledge that all its tradesmen would have gladly welcomed him within their doors. Other men arrived at those convictions in their start in life and so worked up to them. To him they had come when they were too late to be of use.\n\nIt has been said that Mr. Arabin was a man of pleasantry, and it may be thought that such a state of mind as that described would be antagonistic to humour. But surely such is not the case. Wit is the outward mental casing of the man, and has no more to do with the inner mind of thoughts and feelings than have the rich brocaded garments of the priest at the altar with the asceticism of the anchorite below them, whose skin is tormented with sackcloth and whose body is half-flayed with rods. Nay, will not such a one often rejoice more than any other in the rich show of his outer apparel? Will it not be food for his pride to feel that he groans inwardly while he shines outwardly? So it is with the mental efforts which men make. Those which they show forth daily to the world are often the opposites of the inner workings of the spirit.\n\nIn the archdeacon's drawing-room, Mr. Arabin had sparkled with his usual unaffected brilliancy, but when he retired to his bedroom, he sat there sad, at his open window, repining within himself that he also had no wife, no bairns, no soft sward of lawn duly mown for him to lie on, no herd of attendant curates, no bowings from the banker's clerks, no rich rectory. That apostleship that he had thought of had evaded his grasp, and he was now only vicar of St. Ewold's, with a taste for a mitre. Truly he had fallen between two stools.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. St. Ewold's Parsonage\n\nWhen Mr. Harding and Mrs. Bold reached the rectory on the following morning, the archdeacon and his friend were at St. Ewold's. They had gone over that the new vicar might inspect his church and be introduced to the squire, and were not expected back before dinner. Mr. Harding rambled out by himself and strolled, as was his wont at Plumstead, about the lawn and round the church; and as he did so, the two sisters naturally fell into conversation about Barchester.\n\nThere was not much sisterly confidence between them. Mrs. Grantly was ten years older than Eleanor, and had been married while Eleanor was yet a child. They had never, therefore, poured into each other's ears their hopes and loves; and now that one was a wife and the other a widow, it was not probable that they would begin to do so. They lived too much asunder to be able to fall into that kind of intercourse which makes confidence between sisters almost a necessity; moreover, that which is so easy at eighteen is often very difficult at twenty-eight. Mrs. Grantly knew this, and did not, therefore, expect confidence from her sister; yet she longed to ask her whether in real truth Mr. Slope was agreeable to her.\n\nIt was by no means difficult to turn the conversation to Mr. Slope. That gentleman had become so famous at Barchester, had so much to do with all clergymen connected with the city, and was so specially concerned in the affairs of Mr. Harding, that it would have been odd if Mr. Harding's daughters had not talked about him. Mrs. Grantly was soon abusing him, which she did with her whole heart, and Mrs. Bold was nearly as eager to defend him. She positively disliked the man, would have been delighted to learn that he had taken himself off so that she should never see him again, had indeed almost a fear of him, and yet she constantly found herself taking his part. The abuse of other people, and abuse of a nature that she felt to be unjust, imposed this necessity on her, and at last made Mr. Slope's defence an habitual course of argument with her.\n\nFrom Mr. Slope the conversation turned to the Stanhopes, and Mrs. Grantly was listening with some interest to Eleanor's account of the family, when it dropped out that Mr. Slope made one of the party.\n\n\"What!\" said the lady of the rectory. \"Was Mr. Slope there too?\"\n\nEleanor merely replied that such had been the case.\n\n\"Why, Eleanor, he must be very fond of you, I think; he seems to follow you everywhere.\"\n\nEven this did not open Eleanor's eyes. She merely laughed, and said that she imagined Mr. Slope found other attraction at Dr. Stanhope's. And so they parted. Mrs. Grantly felt quite convinced that the odious match would take place, and Mrs. Bold as convinced that that unfortunate chaplain, disagreeable as he must be allowed to be, was more sinned against than sinning.\n\nThe archdeacon of course heard before dinner that Eleanor had remained the day before in Barchester with the view of meeting Mr. Slope, and that she had so met him. He remembered how she had positively stated that there were to be no guests at the Stanhopes, and he did not hesitate to accuse her of deceit. Moreover, the fact, or rather presumed fact, of her being deceitful on such a matter spoke but too plainly in evidence against her as to her imputed crime of receiving Mr. Slope as a lover.\n\n\"I am afraid that anything we can do will be too late,\" said the archdeacon. \"I own I am fairly surprised. I never liked your sister's taste with regard to men, but still I did not give her credit for--ugh!\"\n\n\"And so soon, too,\" said Mrs. Grantly, who thought more, perhaps, of her sister's indecorum in having a lover before she had put off her weeds than her bad taste in having such a lover as Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Well, my dear, I shall be sorry to be harsh, or to do anything that can hurt your father; but, positively, neither that man nor his wife shall come within my doors.\"\n\nMrs. Grantly sighed, and then attempted to console herself and her lord by remarking that, after all, the thing was not accomplished yet. Now that Eleanor was at Plumstead, much might be done to wean her from her fatal passion. Poor Eleanor!\n\nThe evening passed off without anything to make it remarkable. Mr. Arabin discussed the parish of St. Ewold with the archdeacon, and Mrs. Grantly and Mr. Harding, who knew the personages of the parish, joined in. Eleanor also knew them, but she said little. Mr. Arabin did not apparently take much notice of her, and she was not in a humour to receive at that time with any special grace any special favourite of her brother-in-law. Her first idea on reaching her bedroom was that a much pleasanter family party might be met at Dr. Stanhope's than at the rectory. She began to think that she was getting tired of clergymen and their respectable, humdrum, wearisome mode of living, and that after all, people in the outer world, who had lived in Italy, London, or elsewhere, need not necessarily be regarded as atrocious and abominable. The Stanhopes, she had thought, were a giddy, thoughtless, extravagant set of people, but she had seen nothing wrong about them and had, on the other hand, found that they thoroughly knew how to make their house agreeable. It was a thousand pities, she thought, that the archdeacon should not have a little of the same savoir vivre. Mr. Arabin, as we have said, did not apparently take much notice of her, but yet he did not go to bed without feeling that he had been in company with a very pretty woman; and as is the case with most bachelors, and some married men, regarded the prospect of his month's visit at Plumstead in a pleasanter light when he learnt that a very pretty woman was to share it with him.\n\nBefore they all retired it was settled that the whole party should drive over on the following day to inspect the parsonage at St. Ewold. The three clergymen were to discuss dilapidations, and the two ladies were to lend their assistance in suggesting such changes as might be necessary for a bachelor's abode.\n\nAccordingly, soon after breakfast the carriage was at the door. There was only room for four inside, and the archdeacon got upon the box. Eleanor found herself opposite to Mr. Arabin, and was, therefore, in a manner forced into conversation with him. They were soon on comfortable terms together, and had she thought about it, she would have thought that, in spite of his black cloth, Mr. Arabin would not have been a bad addition to the Stanhope family party.\n\nNow that the archdeacon was away they could all trifle. Mr. Harding began by telling them in the most innocent manner imaginable an old legend about Mr. Arabin's new parish. There was, he said, in days of yore an illustrious priestess of St. Ewold, famed through the whole country for curing all manner of diseases. She had a well, as all priestesses have ever had, which well was extant to this day, and shared in the minds of many of the people the sanctity which belonged to the consecrated ground of the parish church. Mr. Arabin declared that he should look on such tenets on the part of his parishioners as anything but orthodox. And Mrs. Grantly replied that she so entirely disagreed with him as to think that no parish was in a proper state that had not its priestess as well as its priest. \"The duties are never well done,\" said she, \"unless they are so divided.\"\n\n\"I suppose, Papa,\" said Eleanor, \"that in the olden times the priestess bore all the sway herself. Mr. Arabin, perhaps, thinks that such might be too much the case now if a sacred lady were admitted within the parish.\"\n\n\"I think, at any rate,\" said he, \"that it is safer to run no such risk. No priestly pride has ever exceeded that of sacerdotal females. A very lowly curate I might, perhaps, essay to rule, but a curatess would be sure to get the better of me.\"\n\n\"There are certainly examples of such accidents happening,\" said Mrs. Grantly. \"They do say that there is a priestess at Barchester who is very imperious in all things touching the altar. Perhaps the fear of such a fate as that is before your eyes.\"\n\nWhen they were joined by the archdeacon on the gravel before the vicarage, they descended again to grave dullness. Not that Archdeacon Grantly was a dull man, but his frolic humours were of a cumbrous kind, and his wit, when he was witty, did not generally extend itself to his auditors. On the present occasion he was soon making speeches about wounded roofs and walls, which he declared to be in want of some surgeon's art. There was not a partition that he did not tap, nor a block of chimneys that he did not narrowly examine; all water-pipes, flues, cisterns, and sewers underwent an investigation; he even descended, in the care of his friend, so far as to bore sundry boards in the floors with a bradawl.\n\nMr. Arabin accompanied him through the rooms, trying to look wise in such domestic matters, and the other three also followed. Mrs. Grantly showed that she had not herself been priestess of a parish twenty years for nothing, and examined the bells and window-panes in a very knowing way.\n\n\"You will, at any rate, have a beautiful prospect out of your own window, if this is to be your private sanctum,\" said Eleanor. She was standing at the lattice of a little room upstairs, from which the view certainly was very lovely. It was from the back of the vicarage, and there was nothing to interrupt the eye between the house and the glorious gray pile of the cathedral. The intermediate ground, however, was beautifully studded with timber. In the immediate foreground ran the little river which afterwards skirted the city, and, just to the right of the cathedral, the pointed gables and chimneys of Hiram's Hospital peeped out of the elms which encompass it.\n\n\"Yes,\" said he, joining her. \"I shall have a beautifully complete view of my adversaries. I shall sit down before the hostile town and fire away at them at a very pleasant distance. I shall just be able to lodge a shot in the hospital, should the enemy ever get possession of it, and as for the palace, I have it within full range.\"\n\n\"I never saw anything like you clergymen,\" said Eleanor; \"You are always thinking of fighting each other.\"\n\n\"Either that,\" said he, \"or else supporting each other. The pity is that we cannot do the one without the other. But are we not here to fight? Is not ours a church militant? What is all our work but fighting, and hard fighting, if it be well done?\"\n\n\"But not with each other.\"\n\n\"That's as it may be. The same complaint which you make of me for battling with another clergyman of our own church, the Mohammedan would make against me for battling with the error of a priest of Rome. Yet, surely, you would not be inclined to say that I should be wrong to do battle with such as him. A pagan, too, with his multiplicity of gods, would think it equally odd that the Christian and the Mohammedan should disagree.\"\n\n\"Ah! But you wage your wars about trifles so bitterly.\"\n\n\"Wars about trifles,\" said he, \"are always bitter, especially among neighbours. When the differences are great, and the parties comparative strangers, men quarrel with courtesy. What combatants are ever so eager as two brothers?\"\n\n\"But do not such contentions bring scandal on the church?\"\n\n\"More scandal would fall on the church if there were no such contentions. We have but one way to avoid them--by that of acknowledging a common head of our church, whose word on all points of doctrine shall be authoritative. Such a termination of our difficulties is alluring enough. It has charms which are irresistible to many, and all but irresistible, I own, to me.\"\n\n\"You speak now of the Church of Rome?\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"No,\" said he, \"not necessarily of the Church of Rome; but of a church with a head. Had it pleased God to vouchsafe to us such a church our path would have been easy. But easy paths have not been thought good for us.\" He paused and stood silent for awhile, thinking of the time when he had so nearly sacrificed all he had, his powers of mind, his free agency, the fresh running waters of his mind's fountain, his very inner self, for an easy path in which no fighting would be needed; and then he continued: \"What you say is partly true: our contentions do bring on us some scandal. The outer world, though it constantly reviles us for our human infirmities and throws in our teeth the fact that being clergymen we are still no more than men, demands of us that we should do our work with godlike perfection. There is nothing god-like about us: we differ from each other with the acerbity common to man; we triumph over each other with human frailty; we allow differences on subjects of divine origin to produce among us antipathies and enmities which are anything but divine. This is all true. But what would you have in place of it? There is no infallible head for a church on earth. This dream of believing man has been tried, and we see in Italy and in Spain what has come of it. Grant that there are and have been no bickerings within the pale of the Pope's Church. Such an assumption would be utterly untrue, but let us grant it, and then let us say which church has incurred the heavier scandals.\"\n\nThere was a quiet earnestness about Mr. Arabin, as he half-acknowledged and half-defended himself from the charge brought against him, which surprised Eleanor. She had been used all her life to listen to clerical discussion, but the points at issue between the disputants had so seldom been of more than temporal significance as to have left on her mind no feeling of reverence for such subjects. There had always been a hard worldly leaven of the love either of income or of power in the strains she had heard; there had been no panting for the truth; no aspirations after religious purity. It had always been taken for granted by those around her that they were indubitably right; that there was no ground for doubt; that the hard uphill work of ascertaining what the duty of a clergyman should be had been already accomplished in full; and that what remained for an active militant parson to do was to hold his own against all comers. Her father, it is true, was an exception to this, but then he was so essentially anti-militant in all things that she classed him in her own mind apart from all others. She had never argued the matter within herself, or considered whether this common tone was or was not faulty; but she was sick of it without knowing that she was so. And now she found to her surprise, and not without a certain pleasurable excitement, that this new-comer among them spoke in a manner very different from that to which she was accustomed.\n\n\"It is so easy to condemn,\" said he, continuing the thread of his thoughts. \"I know no life that must be so delicious as that of a writer for newspapers, or a leading member of the opposition--to thunder forth accusations against men in power; to show up the worst side of everything that is produced; to pick holes in every coat; to be indignant, sarcastic, jocose, moral, or supercilious; to damn with faint praise, or crush with open calumny! What can be so easy as this when the critic has to be responsible for nothing? You condemn what I do, but put yourself in my position and do the reverse, and then see if I cannot condemn you.\"\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Arabin, I do not condemn you.\"\n\n\"Pardon me, you do, Mrs. Bold--you as one of the world; you are now the opposition member; you are now composing your leading article, and well and bitterly you do it. 'Let dogs delight to bark and bite'--you fitly begin with an elegant quotation--'but if we are to have a church at all, in heaven's name let the pastors who preside over it keep their hands from each other's throats. Lawyers can live without befouling each other's names; doctors do not fight duels. Why is it that clergymen alone should indulge themselves in such unrestrained liberty of abuse against each other?' and so you go on reviling us for our ungodly quarrels, our sectarian propensities, and scandalous differences. It will, however, give you no trouble to write another article next week in which we, or some of us, shall be twitted with an unseemly apathy in matters of our vocation. It will not fall on you to reconcile the discrepancy; your readers will never ask you how the poor parson is to be urgent in season and out of season and yet never come in contact with men who think widely differently from him. You, when you condemn this foreign treaty, or that official arrangement, will have to incur no blame for the graver faults of any different measure. It is so easy to condemn--and so pleasant too, for eulogy charms no listeners as detraction does.\"\n\nEleanor only half-followed him in his raillery, but she caught his meaning. \"I know I ought to apologize for presuming to criticize you,\" she said, \"but I was thinking with sorrow of the ill-will that has lately come among us at Barchester, and I spoke more freely than I should have done.\"\n\n\"Peace on earth and goodwill among men, are, like heaven, promises for the future;\" said he, following rather his own thoughts than hers. \"When that prophecy is accomplished, there will no longer be any need for clergymen.\"\n\nHere they were interrupted by the archdeacon, whose voice was heard from the cellar shouting to the vicar.\n\n\"Arabin, Arabin,\"--and then, turning to his wife, who was apparently at his elbow--\"where has he gone to? This cellar is perfectly abominable. It would be murder to put a bottle of wine into it till it has been roofed, walled, and floored. How on earth old Goodenough ever got on with it I cannot guess. But then Goodenough never had a glass of wine that any man could drink.\"\n\n\"What is it, Archdeacon?\" said the vicar, running downstairs and leaving Eleanor above to her meditations.\n\n\"This cellar must be roofed, walled, and floored,\" repeated the archdeacon. \"Now mind what I say, and don't let the architect persuade you that it will do; half of these fellows know nothing about wine. This place as it is now would be damp and cold in winter and hot and muggy in summer. I wouldn't give a straw for the best wine that ever was vinted, after it had lain here a couple of years.\"\n\nMr. Arabin assented and promised that the cellar should be reconstructed according to the archdeacon's receipt.\n\n\"And, Arabin, look here; was such an attempt at a kitchen grate ever seen?\"\n\n\"The grate is really very bad,\" said Mrs. Grantly. \"I am sure the priestess won't approve of it, when she is brought home to the scene of her future duties. Really, Mr. Arabin, no priestess accustomed to such an excellent well as that above could put up with such a grate as this.\"\n\n\"If there must be a priestess at St. Ewold's at all, Mrs. Grantly, I think we will leave her to her well and not call down her divine wrath on any of the imperfections rising from our human poverty. However, I own I am amenable to the attractions of a well-cooked dinner, and the grate shall certainly be changed.\"\n\nBy this time the archdeacon had again ascended, and was now in the dining-room. \"Arabin,\" said he, speaking in his usual loud, clear voice and with that tone of dictation which was so common to him, \"you must positively alter this dining-room--that is, remodel it altogether. Look here, it is just sixteen feet by fifteen; did any man ever hear of a dining-room of such proportions!\" The archdeacon stepped the room long-ways and cross-ways with ponderous steps, as though a certain amount of ecclesiastical dignity could be imparted even to such an occupation as that by the manner of doing it. \"Barely sixteen; you may call it a square.\"\n\n\"It would do very well for a round table,\" suggested the ex-warden.\n\nNow there was something peculiarly unorthodox, in the archdeacon's estimation, in the idea of a round table. He had always been accustomed to a goodly board of decent length, comfortably elongating itself according to the number of the guests, nearly black with perpetual rubbing, and as bright as a mirror. Now round dinner-tables are generally of oak, or else of such new construction as not to have acquired the peculiar hue which was so pleasing to him. He connected them with what he called the nasty newfangled method of leaving a cloth on the table, as though to warn people that they were not to sit long. In his eyes there was something democratic and parvenu in a round table. He imagined that dissenters and calico-printers chiefly used them, and perhaps a few literary lions more conspicuous for their wit than their gentility. He was a little flurried at the idea of such an article being introduced into the diocese by a protégé of his own, and at the instigation of his father-in-law.\n\n\"A round dinner-table,\" said he with some heat, \"is the most abominable article of furniture that ever was invented. I hope that Arabin has more taste than to allow such a thing in his house.\"\n\nPoor Mr. Harding felt himself completely snubbed, and of course said nothing further; but Mr. Arabin, who had yielded submissively in the small matters of the cellar and kitchen grate, found himself obliged to oppose reforms which might be of a nature too expensive for his pocket.\n\n\"But it seems to me, Archdeacon, that I can't very well lengthen the room without pulling down the wall, and if I pull down the wall, I must build it up again; then if I throw out a bow on this side, I must do the same on the other, and if I do it for the ground floor, I must carry it up to the floor above. That will be putting a new front to the house and will cost, I suppose, a couple of hundred pounds. The ecclesiastical commissioners will hardly assist me when they hear that my grievance consists in having a dining-room only sixteen feet long.\"\n\nThe archdeacon proceeded to explain that nothing would be easier than adding six feet to the front of the dining-room without touching any other room in the house. Such irregularities of construction in small country-houses were, he said, rather graceful than otherwise, and he offered to pay for the whole thing out of his own pocket if it cost more than forty pounds. Mr. Arabin, however, was firm, and, although the archdeacon fussed and fumed about it, would not give way. Forty pounds, he said, was a matter of serious moment to him, and his friends, if under such circumstances they would be good-natured enough to come to him at all, must put up with the misery of a square room. He was willing to compromise matters by disclaiming any intention of having a round table.\n\n\"But,\" said Mrs. Grantly, \"what if the priestess insists on having both the rooms enlarged?\"\n\n\"The priestess in that case must do it for herself, Mrs. Grantly.\"\n\n\"I have no doubt she will be well able to do so,\" replied the lady; \"to do that and many more wonderful things. I am quite sure that the priestess of St. Ewold, when she does come, won't come empty-handed.\"\n\nMr. Arabin, however, did not appear well inclined to enter into speculative expenses on such a chance as this, and therefore any material alterations in the house, the cost of which could not fairly be made to lie at the door either of the ecclesiastical commissioners or of the estate of the late incumbent, were tabooed. With this essential exception, the archdeacon ordered, suggested, and carried all points before him in a manner very much to his own satisfaction. A close observer, had there been one there, might have seen that his wife had been quite as useful in the matter as himself. No one knew better than Mrs. Grantly the appurtenances necessary to a comfortable house. She did not, however, think it necessary to lay claim to any of the glory which her lord and master was so ready to appropriate as his own.\n\nHaving gone through their work effectually and systematically, the party returned to Plumstead well satisfied with their expedition.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. The Thornes of Ullathorne\n\nOn the following Sunday Mr. Arabin was to read himself in at his new church. It was agreed at the rectory that the archdeacon should go over with him and assist at the reading desk, and that Mr. Harding should take the archdeacon's duty at Plumstead Church. Mrs. Grantly had her school and her buns to attend to, and professed that she could not be spared, but Mrs. Bold was to accompany them. It was further agreed also that they would lunch at the squire's house and return home after the afternoon service.\n\nWilfred Thorne, Esq., of Ullathorne, was the squire of St. Ewold's--or, rather, the squire of Ullathorne, for the domain of the modern landlord was of wider notoriety than the fame of the ancient saint. He was a fair specimen of what that race has come to in our days which, a century ago, was, as we are told, fairly represented by Squire Western. If that representation be a true one, few classes of men can have made faster strides in improvement. Mr. Thorne, however, was a man possessed of quite a sufficient number of foibles to lay him open to much ridicule. He was still a bachelor, being about fifty, and was not a little proud of his person. When living at home at Ullathorne, there was not much room for such pride, and there therefore he always looked like a gentleman and like that which he certainly was, the first man in his parish. But during the month or six weeks which he annually spent in London, he tried so hard to look like a great man there also, which he certainly was not, that he was put down as a fool by many at his club. He was a man of considerable literary attainment in a certain way and on certain subjects. His favourite authors were Montaigne and Burton, and he knew more perhaps than any other man in his own county and the next to it of the English essayists of the two last centuries. He possessed complete sets of the Idler, the Spectator, the Tatler, the Guardian, and the Rambler, and would discourse by hours together on the superiority of such publications to anything which has since been produced in our Edinburghs and Quarterlies. He was proficient in all questions of genealogy, and knew enough of almost every gentleman's family in England to say of what blood and lineage were descended all those who had any claim to be considered as possessors of any such luxuries. For blood and lineage he himself had a most profound respect. He counted back his own ancestors to some period long antecedent to the Conquest, and could tell you, if you would listen to him, how it had come to pass that they, like Cedric the Saxon, had been permitted to hold their own among the Norman barons. It was not, according to his showing, on account of any weak complaisance on the part of his family towards their Norman neighbours. Some Ealfried of Ullathorne once fortified his own castle and held out, not only that, but the then existing cathedral of Barchester also, against one Geoffrey De Burgh, in the time of King John; and Mr. Thorne possessed the whole history of the siege written on vellum and illuminated in a most costly manner. It little signified that no one could read the writing, as, had that been possible, no one could have understood the language. Mr. Thorne could, however, give you all the particulars in good English, and had no objection to do so.\n\nIt would be unjust to say that he looked down on men whose families were of recent date. He did not do so. He frequently consorted with such, and had chosen many of his friends from among them. But he looked on them as great millionaires are apt to look on those who have small incomes; as men who have Sophocles at their fingers' ends regard those who know nothing of Greek. They might doubtless be good sort of people, entitled to much praise for virtue, very admirable for talent, highly respectable in every way, but they were without the one great good gift. Such was Mr. Thorne's way of thinking on this matter; nothing could atone for the loss of good blood; nothing could neutralize its good effects. Few indeed were now possessed of it, but the possession was on that account the more precious. It was very pleasant to hear Mr. Thorne descant on this matter. Were you in your ignorance to surmise that such a one was of a good family because the head of his family was a baronet of an old date, he would open his eyes with a delightful look of affected surprise, and modestly remind you that baronetcies only dated from James I. He would gently sigh if you spoke of the blood of the Fitzgeralds and De Burghs; would hardly allow the claims of the Howards and Lowthers; and has before now alluded to the Talbots as a family who had hardly yet achieved the full honours of a pedigree.\n\nIn speaking once of a wide-spread race whose name had received the honours of three coronets, scions from which sat for various constituencies, some one of whose members had been in almost every cabinet formed during the present century, a brilliant race such as there are few in England, Mr. Thorne had called them all \"dirt.\" He had not intended any disrespect to these men. He admired them in many senses, and allowed them their privileges without envy. He had merely meant to express his feeling that the streams which ran through their veins were not yet purified by time to that perfection, had not become so genuine an ichor, as to be worthy of being called blood in the genealogical sense.\n\nWhen Mr. Arabin was first introduced to him, Mr. Thorne had immediately suggested that he was one of the Arabins of Uphill Stanton. Mr. Arabin replied that he was a very distant relative of the family alluded to. To this Mr. Thorne surmised that the relationship could not be very distant. Mr. Arabin assured him that it was so distant that the families knew nothing of each other. Mr. Thorne laughed his gentle laugh at this and told Mr. Arabin that there was now existing no branch of his family separated from the parent stock at an earlier date than the reign of Elizabeth, and that therefore Mr. Arabin could not call himself distant. Mr. Arabin himself was quite clearly an Arabin of Uphill Stanton.\n\n\"But,\" said the vicar, \"Uphill Stanton has been sold to the De Greys and has been in their hands for the last fifty years.\"\n\n\"And when it has been there one hundred and fifty, if it unluckily remain there so long,\" said Mr. Thorne, \"your descendants will not be a whit the less entitled to describe themselves as being of the family of Uphill Stanton. Thank God no De Grey can buy that--and thank God no Arabin, and no Thorne, can sell it.\"\n\nIn politics Mr. Thorne was an unflinching conservative. He looked on those fifty-three Trojans who, as Mr. Dod tells us, censured free trade in November, 1852, as the only patriots left among the public men of England. When that terrible crisis of free trade had arrived, when the repeal of the Corn Laws was carried by those very men whom Mr. Thorne had hitherto regarded as the only possible saviours of his country, he was for a time paralysed. His country was lost; but that was comparatively a small thing. Other countries had flourished and fallen, and the human race still went on improving under God's providence. But now all trust in human faith must forever be at an end. Not only must ruin come, but it must come through the apostasy of those who had been regarded as the truest of true believers. Politics in England, as a pursuit for gentlemen, must be at an end. Had Mr. Thorne been trodden under foot by a Whig, he could have borne it as a Tory and a martyr, but to be so utterly thrown over and deceived by those he had so earnestly supported, so thoroughly trusted, was more than he could endure and live. He therefore ceased to live as a politician, and refused to hold any converse with the world at large on the state of the country.\n\nSuch were Mr. Thorne's impressions for the first two or three years after Sir Robert Peel's apostasy, but by degrees his temper, as did that of others, cooled down. He began once more to move about, to frequent the bench and the market, and to be seen at dinners shoulder to shoulder with some of those who had so cruelly betrayed him. It was a necessity for him to live, and that plan of his for avoiding the world did not answer. He, however, and others around him who still maintained the same staunch principles of protection--men like himself who were too true to flinch at the cry of a mob--had their own way of consoling themselves. They were, and felt themselves to be, the only true depositaries left of certain Eleusinian mysteries, of certain deep and wondrous services of worship by which alone the gods could be rightly approached. To them and them only was it now given to know these things and to perpetuate them, if that might still be done, by the careful and secret education of their children.\n\nWe have read how private and peculiar forms of worship have been carried on from age to age in families which, to the outer world, have apparently adhered to the services of some ordinary church. And so by degrees it was with Mr. Thorne. He learnt at length to listen calmly while protection was talked of as a thing dead, although he knew within himself that it was still quick with a mystic life. Nor was he without a certain pleasure that such knowledge, though given to him, should be debarred from the multitude. He became accustomed to hear even among country gentlemen that free trade was after all not so bad, and to hear this without dispute, although conscious within himself that everything good in England had gone with his old palladium. He had within him something of the feeling of Cato, who gloried that he could kill himself because Romans were no longer worthy of their name. Mr. Thorne had no thought of killing himself, being a Christian and still possessing his £4000 a year, but the feeling was not on that account the less comfortable.\n\nMr. Thorne was a sportsman, and had been active though not outrageous in his sports. Previous to the great downfall of politics in his county, he had supported the hunt by every means in his power. He had preserved game till no goose or turkey could show a tail in the parish of St. Ewold's. He had planted gorse covers with more care than oaks and larches. He had been more anxious for the comfort of his foxes than of his ewes and lambs. No meet had been more popular than Ullathorne; no man's stables had been more liberally open to the horses of distant men than Mr. Thorne's; no man had said more, written more, or done more to keep the club up. The theory of protection could expand itself so thoroughly in the practices of a county hunt! But when the great ruin came; when the noble master of the Barsetshire hounds supported the recreant minister in the House of Lords and basely surrendered his truth, his manhood, his friends, and his honour for the hope of a garter, then Mr. Thorne gave up the hunt. He did not cut his covers, for that would not have been the act of a gentleman. He did not kill his foxes, for that according to his light would have been murder. He did not say that his covers should not be drawn, or his earths stopped, for that would have been illegal according to the by-laws prevailing among country gentlemen. But he absented himself from home on the occasion of every meet at Ullathorne, left the covers to their fate, and could not be persuaded to take his pink coat out of his press, or his hunters out of his stable. This lasted for two years, and then by degrees he came round. He first appeared at a neighbouring meet on a pony, dressed in his shooting-coat, as though he had trotted in by accident; then he walked up one morning on foot to see his favourite gorse drawn, and when his groom brought his mare out by chance, he did not refuse to mount her. He was next persuaded, by one of the immortal fifty-three, to bring his hunting materials over to the other side of the county and take a fortnight with the hounds there; and so gradually he returned to his old life. But in hunting as in other things he was only supported by an inward feeling of mystic superiority to those with whom he shared the common breath of outer life.\n\nMr. Thorne did not live in solitude at Ullathorne. He had a sister, who was ten years older than himself and who participated in his prejudices and feelings so strongly that she was a living caricature of all his foibles. She would not open a modern quarterly, did not choose to see a magazine in her drawing-room, and would not have polluted her fingers with a shred of the Times for any consideration. She spoke of Addison, Swift, and Steele as though they were still living, regarded Defoe as the best known novelist of his country, and thought of Fielding as a young but meritorious novice in the fields of romance. In poetry, she was familiar with names as late as Dryden, and had once been seduced into reading \"The Rape of the Lock;\" but she regarded Spenser as the purest type of her country's literature in this line. Genealogy was her favourite insanity. Those things which are the pride of most genealogists were to her contemptible. Arms and mottoes set her beside herself. Ealfried of Ullathorne had wanted no motto to assist him in cleaving to the brisket Geoffrey De Burgh, and Ealfried's great grandfather, the gigantic Ullafrid, had required no other arms than those which nature gave him to hurl from the top of his own castle a cousin of the base invading Norman. To her all modern English names were equally insignificant: Hengist, Horsa, and such like had for her ears the only true savour of nobility. She was not contented unless she could go beyond the Saxons, and would certainly have christened her children, had she had children, by the names of the ancient Britons. In some respects she was not unlike Scott's Ulrica, and had she been given to cursing, she would certainly have done so in the names of Mista, Skogula, and Zernebock. Not having submitted to the embraces of any polluting Norman, as poor Ulrica had done, and having assisted no parricide, the milk of human kindness was not curdled in her bosom. She never cursed therefore, but blessed rather. This, however, she did in a strange uncouth Saxon manner that would have been unintelligible to any peasants but her own.\n\nAs a politician, Miss Thorne had been so thoroughly disgusted with public life by base deeds long antecedent to the Corn Law question that that had but little moved her. In her estimation her brother had been a fast young man, hurried away by a too ardent temperament into democratic tendencies. Now happily he was brought to sounder views by seeing the iniquity of the world. She had not yet reconciled herself to the Reform Bill, and still groaned in spirit over the defalcations of the Duke as touching the Catholic Emancipation. If asked whom she thought the Queen should take as her counsellor, she would probably have named Lord Eldon, and when reminded that that venerable man was no longer present in the flesh to assist us, she would probably have answered with a sigh that none now could help us but the dead.\n\nIn religion Miss Thorne was a pure Druidess. We would not have it understood by that that she did actually in these latter days assist at any human sacrifices, or that she was in fact hostile to the Church of Christ. She had adopted the Christian religion as a milder form of the worship of her ancestors, and always appealed to her doing so as evidence that she had no prejudices against reform, when it could be shown that reform was salutary. This reform was the most modern of any to which she had as yet acceded, it being presumed that British ladies had given up their paint and taken to some sort of petticoats before the days of St. Augustine. That further feminine step in advance which combines paint and petticoats together had not found a votary in Miss Thorne.\n\nBut she was a Druidess in this, that she regretted she knew not what in the usages and practices of her Church. She sometimes talked and constantly thought of good things gone by, though she had but the faintest idea of what those good things had been. She imagined that a purity had existed which was now gone, that a piety had adorned our pastors and a simple docility our people, for which it may be feared history gave her but little true warrant. She was accustomed to speak of Cranmer as though he had been the firmest and most simple-minded of martyrs, and of Elizabeth as though the pure Protestant faith of her people had been the one anxiety of her life. It would have been cruel to undeceive her, had it been possible; but it would have been impossible to make her believe that the one was a time-serving priest, willing to go any length to keep his place, and that the other was in heart a papist, with this sole proviso, that she should be her own pope.\n\nAnd so Miss Thorne went on sighing and regretting, looking back to the divine right of kings as the ruling axiom of a golden age, and cherishing, low down in the bottom of her heart of hearts, a dear unmentioned wish for the restoration of some exiled Stuart. Who would deny her the luxury of her sighs, or the sweetness of her soft regrets!\n\nIn her person and her dress she was perfect, and well she knew her own perfection. She was a small, elegantly made old woman, with a face from which the glow of her youth had not departed without leaving some streaks of a roseate hue. She was proud of her colour, proud of her grey hair which she wore in short crisp curls peering out all around her face from her dainty white lace cap. To think of all the money that she spent in lace used to break the heart of poor Mrs. Quiverful with her seven daughters. She was proud of her teeth, which were still white and numerous, proud of her bright cheery eye, proud of her short jaunty step; and very proud of the neat, precise, small feet with which those steps were taken. She was proud also, ay, very proud, of the rich brocaded silk in which it was her custom to ruffle through her drawing-room.\n\nWe know what was the custom of the lady of Branksome--\n\nNine-and-twenty knights of fame Hung their shields in Branksome Hall.\n\nThe lady of Ullathorne was not so martial in her habits, but hardly less costly. She might have boasted that nine-and-twenty silken skirts might have been produced in her chamber, each fit to stand alone. The nine-and-twenty shields of the Scottish heroes were less independent and hardly more potent to withstand any attack that might be made on them. Miss Thorne when fully dressed might be said to have been armed cap-a-pie, and she was always fully dressed, as far as was ever known to mortal man.\n\nFor all this rich attire Miss Thorne was not indebted to the generosity of her brother. She had a very comfortable independence of her own, which she divided among juvenile relatives, the milliners, and the poor, giving much the largest share to the latter. It may be imagined, therefore, that with all her little follies she was not unpopular. All her follies have, we believe, been told. Her virtues were too numerous to describe, and not sufficiently interesting to deserve description.\n\nWhile we are on the subject of the Thornes, one word must be said of the house they lived in. It was not a large house, nor a fine house, nor perhaps to modern ideas a very commodious house, but by those who love the peculiar colour and peculiar ornaments of genuine Tudor architecture it was considered a perfect gem. We beg to own ourselves among the number, and therefore take this opportunity to express our surprise that so little is known by English men and women of the beauties of English architecture. The ruins of the Colosseum, the Campanile at Florence, St. Mark's, Cologne, the Bourse and Notre Dame are with our tourists as familiar as household words; but they know nothing of the glories of Wiltshire, Dorsetshire, and Somersetshire. Nay, we much question whether many noted travellers, men who have pitched their tents perhaps under Mount Sinai, are not still ignorant that there are glories in Wiltshire, Dorsetshire, and Somersetshire. We beg that they will go and see.\n\nMr. Thorne's house was called Ullathorne Court--and was properly so called, for the house itself formed two sides of a quadrangle, which was completed on the other two sides by a wall about twenty feet high. This wall was built of cut stone, rudely cut indeed, and now much worn, but of a beautiful, rich, tawny yellow colour, the effect of that stonecrop of minute growth which it had taken three centuries to produce. The top of this wall was ornamented by huge, round stone balls of the same colour as the wall itself. Entrance into the court was had through a pair of iron gates so massive that no one could comfortably open or close them--consequently, they were rarely disturbed. From the gateway two paths led obliquely across the court: that to the left reaching the hall-door, which was in the corner made by the angle of the house, and that to the right leading to the back entrance, which was at the further end of the longer portion of the building.\n\nWith those who are now adepts in contriving house accommodation, it will militate much against Ullathorne Court that no carriage could be brought to the hall-door. If you enter Ullathorne at all, you must do so, fair reader, on foot, or at least in a bath-chair. No vehicle drawn by horses ever comes within that iron gate. But this is nothing to the next horror that will encounter you. On entering the front door, which you do by no very grand portal, you find yourself immediately in the dining-room. What, no hall? exclaims my luxurious friend, accustomed to all the comfortable appurtenances of modern life. Yes, kind sir, a noble hall, if you will but observe it; a true old English hall of excellent dimensions for a country gentleman's family; but, if you please, no dining-parlour.\n\nBoth Mr. and Miss Thorne were proud of this peculiarity of their dwelling, though the brother was once all but tempted by his friends to alter it. They delighted in the knowledge that they, like Cedric, positively dined in their true hall, even though they so dined tête-à-tête. But though they had never owned, they had felt and endeavoured to remedy the discomfort of such an arrangement. A huge screen partitioned off the front door and a portion of the hall, and from the angle so screened off a second door led into a passage which ran along the larger side of the house next to the courtyard. Either my reader or I must be a bad hand at topography, if it be not clear that the great hall forms the ground-floor of the smaller portion of the mansion, that which was to your left as you entered the iron gate, and that it occupies the whole of this wing of the building. It must be equally clear that it looks out on a trim mown lawn, through three quadrangular windows with stone mullions, each window divided into a larger portion at the bottom, and a smaller portion at the top, and each portion again divided into five by perpendicular stone supporters. There may be windows which give a better light than such as these, and it may be, as my utilitarian friend observes, that the giving of light is the desired object of a window. I will not argue the point with him. Indeed I cannot. But I shall not the less die in the assured conviction that no sort or description of window is capable of imparting half so much happiness to mankind as that which had been adopted at Ullathorne Court. What, not an oriel? says Miss Diana de Midellage. No, Miss Diana, not even an oriel, beautiful as is an oriel window. It has not about it so perfect a feeling of quiet English homely comfort. Let oriel windows grace a college, or the half-public mansion of a potent peer, but for the sitting room of quiet country ladies, of ordinary homely folk, nothing can equal the square, mullioned windows of the Tudor architects.\n\nThe hall was hung round with family female insipidities by Lely and unprepossessing male Thornes in red coats by Kneller, each Thorne having been let into a panel in the wainscoting, in the proper manner. At the further end of the room was a huge fire-place, which afforded much ground of difference between the brother and sister. An antiquated grate that would hold about a hundredweight of coal, had been stuck on to the hearth by Mr. Thorne's father. This hearth had of course been intended for the consumption of wood faggots, and the iron dogs for the purpose were still standing, though half-buried in the masonry of the grate. Miss Thorne was very anxious to revert to the dogs. The dear good old creature was always glad to revert to anything, and had she been systematically indulged, would doubtless in time have reflected that fingers were made before forks and have reverted accordingly. But in the affairs of the fire-place Mr. Thorne would not revert. Country gentlemen around him all had comfortable grates in their dining-rooms. He was not exactly the man to have suggested a modern usage, but he was not so far prejudiced as to banish those which his father had prepared for his use. Mr. Thorne had indeed once suggested that with very little contrivance the front door might have been so altered as to open at least into the passage, but on hearing this, his sister Monica--such was Miss Thorne's name--had been taken ill and had remained so for a week. Before she came downstairs she received a pledge from her brother that the entrance should never be changed in her lifetime.\n\nAt the end of the hall opposite to the fire-place a door led into the drawing-room, which was of equal size, and lighted with precisely similar windows. But yet the aspect of the room was very different. It was papered, and the ceiling, which in the hall showed the old rafters, was whitened and finished with a modern cornice. Miss Thorne's drawing-room, or, as she always called it, withdrawing-room, was a beautiful apartment. The windows opened on to the full extent of the lovely trim garden; immediately before the windows were plots of flowers in stiff, stately, stubborn little beds, each bed surrounded by a stone coping of its own; beyond, there was a low parapet wall on which stood urns and images, fawns, nymphs, satyrs, and a whole tribe of Pan's followers; and then again, beyond that, a beautiful lawn sloped away to a sunk fence which divided the garden from the park. Mr. Thorne's study was at the end of the drawing-room, and beyond that were the kitchen and the offices. Doors opened into both Miss Thorne's withdrawing-room and Mr. Thorne's sanctum from the passage above alluded to, which, as it came to the latter room, widened itself so as to make space for the huge black oak stairs which led to the upper regions.\n\nSuch was the interior of Ullathorne Court. But having thus described it, perhaps somewhat too tediously, we beg to say that it is not the interior to which we wish to call the English tourist's attention, though we advise him to lose no legitimate opportunity of becoming acquainted with it in a friendly manner. It is the outside of Ullathorne that is so lovely. Let the tourist get admission at least into the garden and fling himself on that soft sward just opposite to the exterior angle of the house. He will there get the double frontage and enjoy that which is so lovely--the expanse of architectural beauty without the formal dullness of one long line.\n\nIt is the colour of Ullathorne that is so remarkable. It is of that delicious tawny hue which no stone can give, unless it has on it the vegetable richness of centuries. Strike the wall with your hand, and you will think that the stone has on it no covering, but rub it carefully, and you will find that the colour comes off upon your finger. No colourist that ever yet worked from a palette has been able to come up to this rich colouring of years crowding themselves on years.\n\nUllathorne is a high building for a country-house, for it possesses three stories, and in each story the windows are of the same sort as that described, though varying in size and varying also in their lines athwart the house. Those of the ground floor are all uniform in size and position. But those above are irregular both in size and place, and this irregularity gives a bizarre and not unpicturesque appearance to the building. Along the top, on every side, runs a low parapet, which nearly hides the roof, and at the corners are more figures of fawns and satyrs.\n\nSuch is Ullathorne House. But we must say one word of the approach to it, which shall include all the description which we mean to give of the church also. The picturesque old church of St. Ewold's stands immediately opposite to the iron gates which open into the court, and is all but surrounded by the branches of the lime-trees which form the avenue leading up to the house from both sides. This avenue is magnificent, but it would lose much of its value in the eyes of many proprietors by the fact that the road through it is not private property. It is a public lane between hedgerows, with a broad grass margin on each side of the road, from which the lime-trees spring. Ullathorne Court, therefore, does not stand absolutely surrounded by its own grounds, though Mr. Thorne is owner of all the adjacent land. This, however, is the source of very little annoyance to him. Men, when they are acquiring property, think much of such things, but they who live where their ancestors have lived for years do not feel the misfortune. It never occurred either to Mr. or Miss Thorne that they were not sufficiently private because the world at large might, if it so wished, walk or drive by their iron gates. That part of the world which availed itself of the privilege was however very small.\n\nSuch a year or two since were the Thornes of Ullathorne. Such, we believe, are the inhabitants of many an English country-home. May it be long before their number diminishes.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. Mr. Arabin Reads Himself in at St. Ewold's\n\nOn the Sunday morning the archdeacon with his sister-in-law and Mr. Arabin drove over to Ullathorne, as had been arranged. On their way thither the new vicar declared himself to be considerably disturbed in his mind at the idea of thus facing his parishioners for the first time. He had, he said, been always subject to mauvaise honte and an annoying degree of bashfulness, which often unfitted him for any work of a novel description; and now he felt this so strongly that he feared he should acquit himself badly in St. Ewold's reading-desk. He knew, he said, that those sharp little eyes of Miss Thorne would be on him, and that they would not approve. All this the archdeacon greatly ridiculed. He himself knew not, and had never known, what it was to be shy. He could not conceive that Miss Thorne, surrounded as she would be by the peasants of Ullathorne and a few of the poorer inhabitants of the suburbs of Barchester, could in any way affect the composure of a man well accustomed to address the learned congregation of St. Mary's at Oxford, and he laughed accordingly at the idea of Mr. Arabin's modesty.\n\nThereupon Mr. Arabin commenced to subtilize. The change, he said, from St. Mary's to St. Ewold's was quite as powerful on the spirits as would be that from St. Ewold's to St. Mary's. Would not a peer who, by chance of fortune, might suddenly be driven to herd among navvies be as afraid of the jeers of his companions as would any navvy suddenly exalted to a seat among the peers? Whereupon the archdeacon declared with a loud laugh that he would tell Miss Thorne that her new minister had likened her to a navvy. Eleanor, however, pronounced such a conclusion to be unfair; a comparison might be very just in its proportions which did not at all assimilate the things compared. But Mr. Arabin went on subtilizing, regarding neither the archdeacon's raillery nor Eleanor's defence. A young lady, he said, would execute with most perfect self-possession a difficult piece of music in a room crowded with strangers, who would not be able to express herself in intelligible language, even on any ordinary subject and among her most intimate friends, if she were required to do so standing on a box somewhat elevated among them. It was all an affair of education, and he at forty found it difficult to educate himself anew.\n\nEleanor dissented on the matter of the box, and averred she could speak very well about dresses, or babies, or legs of mutton from any box, provided it were big enough for her to stand upon without fear, even though all her friends were listening to her. The archdeacon was sure she would not be able to say a word, but this proved nothing in favour of Mr. Arabin. Mr. Arabin said that he would try the question out with Mrs. Bold, and get her on a box some day when the rectory might be full of visitors. To this Eleanor assented, making condition that the visitors should be of their own set, and the archdeacon cogitated in his mind whether by such a condition it was intended that Mr. Slope should be included, resolving also that, if so, the trial would certainly never take place in the rectory drawing-room at Plumstead.\n\nAnd so arguing, they drove up to the iron gates of Ullathorne Court.\n\nMr. and Miss Thorne were standing ready dressed for church in the hall, and greeted their clerical visitors with cordiality. The archdeacon was an old favourite. He was a clergyman of the old school, and this recommended him to the lady. He had always been an opponent of free trade as long as free trade was an open question, and now that it was no longer so, he, being a clergyman, had not been obliged, like most of his lay Tory companions, to read his recantation. He could therefore be regarded as a supporter of the immaculate fifty-three, and was on this account a favourite with Mr. Thorne. The little bell was tinkling, and the rural population of the parish were standing about the lane, leaning on the church-stile and against the walls of the old court, anxious to get a look at their new minister as he passed from the house to the rectory. The archdeacon's servant had already preceded them thither with the vestments.\n\nThey all went forth together, and when the ladies passed into the church, the three gentlemen tarried a moment in the lane, that Mr. Thorne might name to the vicar with some kind of one-sided introduction the most leading among his parishioners.\n\n\"Here are our churchwardens, Mr. Arabin--Farmer Greenacre and Mr. Stiles. Mr. Stiles has the mill as you go into Barchester; and very good churchwardens they are.\"\n\n\"Not very severe, I hope,\" said Mr. Arabin. The two ecclesiastical officers touched their hats, and each made a leg in the approved rural fashion, assuring the vicar that they were very glad to have the honour of seeing him, and adding that the weather was very good for the harvest. Mr. Stiles, being a man somewhat versed in town life, had an impression of his own dignity, and did not quite like leaving his pastor under the erroneous idea that he being a churchwarden kept the children in order during church time. 'Twas thus he understood Mr. Arabin's allusion to his severity and hastened to put matters right by observing that \"Sexton Clodheve looked to the younguns, and perhaps sometimes there may be a thought too much stick going on during sermon.\" Mr. Arabin's bright eye twinkled as he caught that of the archdeacon, and he smiled to himself as he observed how ignorant his officers were of the nature of their authority and of the surveillance which it was their duty to keep even over himself.\n\nMr. Arabin read the lessons and preached. It was enough to put a man a little out, let him have been ever so used to pulpit reading, to see the knowing way in which the farmers cocked their ears and set about a mental criticism as to whether their new minister did or did not fall short of the excellence of him who had lately departed from them. A mental and silent criticism it was for the existing moment, but soon to be made public among the elders of St. Ewold's over the green graves of their children and forefathers. The excellence, however, of poor old Mr. Goodenough had not been wonderful, and there were few there who did not deem that Mr. Arabin did his work sufficiently well, in spite of the slightly nervous affliction which at first impeded him, and which nearly drove the archdeacon beside himself.\n\nBut the sermon was the thing to try the man. It often surprises us that very young men can muster courage to preach for the first time to a strange congregation. Men who are as yet but little more than boys, who have but just left what indeed we may not call a school, but a seminary intended for their tuition as scholars, whose thoughts have been mostly of boating, cricketing, and wine-parties, ascend a rostrum high above the heads of the submissive crowd, not that they may read God's word to those below, but that they may preach their own word for the edification of their hearers. It seems strange to us that they are not stricken dumb by the new and awful solemnity of their position. \"How am I, just turned twenty-three, who have never yet passed ten thoughtful days since the power of thought first came to me, how am I to instruct these greybeards who, with the weary thinking of so many years, have approached so near the grave? Can I teach them their duty? Can I explain to them that which I so imperfectly understand, that which years of study may have made so plain to them? Has my newly acquired privilege as one of God's ministers imparted to me as yet any fitness for the wonderful work of a preacher?\"\n\nIt must be supposed that such ideas do occur to young clergymen, and yet they overcome, apparently with ease, this difficulty which to us appears to be all but insurmountable. We have never been subjected in the way of ordination to the power of a bishop's hands. It may be that there is in them something that sustains the spirit and banishes the natural modesty of youth. But for ourselves we must own that the deep affection which Dominie Sampson felt for his young pupils has not more endeared him to us than the bashful spirit which sent him mute and inglorious from the pulpit when he rose there with the futile attempt to preach God's gospel.\n\nThere is a rule in our church which forbids the younger order of our clergymen to perform a certain portion of the service. The absolution must be read by a minister in priest's orders. If there be no such minister present, the congregation can have the benefit of no absolution but that which each may succeed in administering to himself. The rule may be a good one, though the necessity for it hardly comes home to the general understanding. But this forbearance on the part of youth would be much more appreciated if it were extended likewise to sermons. The only danger would be that congregations would be too anxious to prevent their young clergymen from advancing themselves in the ranks of the ministry. Clergymen who could not preach would be such blessings that they would be bribed to adhere to their incompetence.\n\nMr. Arabin, however, had not the modesty of youth to impede him, and he succeeded with his sermon even better than with the lessons. He took for his text two verses out of the second epistle of St. John, \"Whosoever transgresseth, and abideth not in the doctrine of Christ, hath not God. He that abideth in the doctrine of Christ, he hath both the Father and the Son. If there come any unto you, and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into your house, neither bid him God-speed.\" He told them that the house of theirs to which he alluded was this their church, in which he now addressed them for the first time; that their most welcome and proper manner of bidding him God-speed would be their patient obedience to his teaching of the gospel; but that he could put forward no claim to such conduct on their part unless he taught them the great Christian doctrine of works and faith combined. On this he enlarged, but not very amply, and after twenty minutes succeeded in sending his new friends home to their baked mutton and pudding well pleased with their new minister.\n\nThen came the lunch at Ullathorne. As soon as they were in the hall Miss Thorne took Mr. Arabin's hand and assured him that she received him into her house, into the temple, she said, in which she worshipped, and bade him God-speed with all her heart. Mr. Arabin was touched and squeezed the spinster's hand without uttering a word in reply. Then Mr. Thorne expressed a hope that Mr. Arabin found the church well adapted for articulation, and Mr. Arabin having replied that he had no doubt he should as soon as he had learnt to pitch his voice to the building, they all sat down to the good things before them.\n\nMiss Thorne took special care of Mrs. Bold. Eleanor still wore her widow's weeds, and therefore had about her that air of grave and sad maternity which is the lot of recent widows. This opened the soft heart of Miss Thorne, and made her look on her young guest as though too much could not be done for her. She heaped chicken and ham upon her plate and poured out for her a full bumper of port wine. When Eleanor, who was not sorry to get it, had drunk a little of it, Miss Thorne at once essayed to fill it again. To this Eleanor objected, but in vain. Miss Thorne winked and nodded and whispered, saying that it was the proper thing and must be done, and that she knew all about it; and so she desired Mrs. Bold to drink it up and not mind anybody.\n\n\"It is your duty, you know, to support yourself,\" she said into the ear of the young mother; \"there's more than yourself depending on it;\" and thus she coshered up Eleanor with cold fowl and port wine. How it is that poor men's wives, who have no cold fowl and port wine on which to be coshered up, nurse their children without difficulty, whereas the wives of rich men, who eat and drink everything that is good, cannot do so, we will for the present leave to the doctors and the mothers to settle between them.\n\nAnd then Miss Thorne was great about teeth. Little Johnny Bold had been troubled for the last few days with his first incipient masticator, and with that freemasonry which exists among ladies, Miss Thorne became aware of the fact before Eleanor had half-finished her wing. The old lady prescribed at once a receipt which had been much in vogue in the young days of her grandmother, and warned Eleanor with solemn voice against the fallacies of modern medicine.\n\n\"Take his coral, my dear,\" said she, \"and rub it well with carrot-juice; rub it till the juice dries on it, and then give it him to play with--\"\n\n\"But he hasn't got a coral,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"Not got a coral!\" said Miss Thorne with almost angry vehemence. \"Not got a coral--how can you expect that he should cut his teeth? Have you got Daffy's Elixir?\"\n\nEleanor explained that she had not. It had not been ordered by Mr. Rerechild, the Barchester doctor whom she employed; and then the young mother mentioned some shockingly modern succedaneum which Mr. Rerechild's new lights had taught him to recommend.\n\nMiss Thorne looked awfully severe. \"Take care, my dear,\" said she, \"that the man knows what he's about; take care he doesn't destroy your little boy. But\"--and she softened into sorrow, as she said it, and spoke more in pity than in anger--\"but I don't know who there is in Barchester now that you can trust. Poor dear old Doctor Bumpwell, indeed--\"\n\n\"Why, Miss Thorne, he died when I was a little girl.\"\n\n\"Yes, my dear, he did, and an unfortunate day it was for Barchester. As to those young men that have come up since\"--Mr. Rerechild, by the by, was quite as old as Miss Thorne herself--\"one doesn't know where they came from or who they are, or whether they know anything about their business or not.\"\n\n\"I think there are very clever men in Barchester,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"Perhaps there may be; only I don't know them: and it's admitted on all sides that medical men aren't now what they used to be. They used to be talented, observing, educated men. But now any whipper-snapper out of an apothecary's shop can call himself a doctor. I believe no kind of education is now thought necessary.\"\n\nEleanor was herself the widow of a medical man and felt a little inclined to resent all these hard sayings. But Miss Thorne was so essentially good-natured that it was impossible to resent anything she said. She therefore sipped her wine and finished her chicken.\n\n\"At any rate, my dear, don't forget the carrot-juice, and by all means get him a coral at once. My grandmother Thorne had the best teeth in the county and carried them to the grave with her at eighty. I have heard her say it was all the carrot-juice. She couldn't bear the Barchester doctors. Even poor old Dr. Bumpwell didn't please her.\" It clearly never occurred to Miss Thorne that some fifty years ago Dr. Bumpwell was only a rising man and therefore as much in need of character in the eyes of the then ladies of Ullathorne as the present doctors were in her own.\n\nThe archdeacon made a very good lunch, and talked to his host about turnip-drillers and new machines for reaping, while the host, thinking it only polite to attend to a stranger, and fearing that perhaps he might not care about turnip crops on a Sunday, mooted all manner of ecclesiastical subjects.\n\n\"I never saw a heavier lot of wheat, Thorne, than you've got there in that field beyond the copse. I suppose that's guano,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Yes, guano. I get it from Bristol myself. You'll find you often have a tolerable congregation of Barchester people out here, Mr. Arabin. They are very fond of St. Ewold's, particularly of an afternoon when the weather is not too hot for the walk.\"\n\n\"I am under an obligation to them for staying away to-day, at any rate,\" said the vicar. \"The congregation can never be too small for a maiden sermon.\"\n\n\"I got a ton and a half at Bradley's in High Street,\" said the archdeacon, \"and it was a complete take in. I don't believe there was five hundredweight of guano in it.\"\n\n\"That Bradley never has anything good,\" said Miss Thorne, who had just caught the name during her whisperings with Eleanor. \"And such a nice shop as there used to be in that very house before he came. Wilfred, don't you remember what good things old Ambleoff used to have?\"\n\n\"There have been three men since Ambleoff's time,\" said the archdeacon, \"and each as bad as the other. But who gets it for you at Bristol, Thorne?\"\n\n\"I ran up myself this year and bought it out of the ship. I am afraid as the evenings get shorter, Mr. Arabin, you'll find the reading-desk too dark. I must send a fellow with an axe and make him lop off some of those branches.\"\n\nMr. Arabin declared that the morning light at any rate was perfect, and deprecated any interference with the lime-trees. And then they took a stroll out among the trim parterres, and Mr. Arabin explained to Mrs. Bold the difference between a naiad and a dryad, and dilated on vases and the shapes of urns. Miss Thorne busied herself among her pansies, and her brother, finding it quite impracticable to give anything of a peculiarly Sunday tone to the conversation, abandoned the attempt and had it out with the archdeacon about the Bristol guano.\n\nAt three o'clock they again went into church, and now Mr. Arabin read the service and the archdeacon preached. Nearly the same congregation was present, with some adventurous pedestrians from the city, who had not thought the heat of the midday August sun too great to deter them. The archdeacon took his text from the epistle to Philemon. \"I beseech thee for my son Onesimus, whom I have begotten in my bonds.\" From such a text it may be imagined the kind of sermon which Dr. Grantly preached, and on the whole it was neither dull, nor bad, nor out of place.\n\nHe told them that it had become his duty to look about for a pastor for them, to supply the place of one who had been long among them, and that in this manner he regarded as a son him whom he had selected, as St. Paul had regarded the young disciple whom he sent forth. Then he took a little merit to himself for having studiously provided the best man he could without reference to patronage or favour; but he did not say that the best man according to his views was he who was best able to subdue Mr. Slope, and make that gentleman's situation in Barchester too hot to be comfortable. As to the bonds, they had consisted in the exceeding struggle which he had made to get a good clergyman for them. He deprecated any comparison between himself and St. Paul, but said that he was entitled to beseech them for their goodwill towards Mr. Arabin, in the same manner that the apostle had besought Philemon and his household with regard to Onesimus.\n\nThe archdeacon's sermon--text, blessing, and all--was concluded within the half-hour. Then they shook hands with their Ullathorne friends and returned to Plumstead. 'Twas thus that Mr. Arabin read himself in at St. Ewold's.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. Mr. Slope Manages Matters Very Cleverly at Puddingdale\n\nThe next two weeks passed pleasantly enough at Plumstead. The whole party there assembled seemed to get on well together. Eleanor made the house agreeable, and the archdeacon and Mr. Grantly seemed to have forgotten her iniquity as regarded Mr. Slope. Mr. Harding had his violoncello, and played to them while his daughters accompanied him. Johnny Bold, by the help either of Mr. Rerechild or else by that of his coral and carrot-juice, got through his teething troubles. There had been gaieties, too, of all sorts. They had dined at Ullathorne, and the Thornes had dined at the rectory. Eleanor had been duly put to stand on her box, and in that position had found herself quite unable to express her opinion on the merits of flounces, such having been the subject given to try her elocution. Mr. Arabin had of course been much in his own parish, looking to the doings at his vicarage, calling on his parishioners, and taking on himself the duties of his new calling. But still he had been every evening at Plumstead, and Mrs. Grantly was partly willing to agree with her husband that he was a pleasant inmate in a house.\n\nThey had also been at a dinner-party at Dr. Stanhope's, of which Mr. Arabin had made one. He also, mothlike, burnt his wings in the flames of the signora's candle. Mrs. Bold, too, had been there, and had felt somewhat displeased with the taste--want of taste she called it--shown by Mr. Arabin in paying so much attention to Madame Neroni. It was as infallible that Madeline should displease and irritate the women as that she should charm and captivate the men. The one result followed naturally on the other. It was quite true that Mr. Arabin had been charmed. He thought her a very clever and a very handsome woman; he thought also that her peculiar affliction entitled her to the sympathy of all. He had never, he said, met so much suffering joined to such perfect beauty and so clear a mind. 'Twas thus he spoke of the signora, coming home in the archdeacon's carriage, and Eleanor by no means liked to hear the praise. It was, however, exceedingly unjust of her to be angry with Mr. Arabin, as she had herself spent a very pleasant evening with Bertie Stanhope, who had taken her down to dinner and had not left her side for one moment after the gentlemen came out of the dining-room. It was unfair that she should amuse herself with Bertie and yet begrudge her new friend his license of amusing himself with Bertie's sister. And yet she did so. She was half-angry with him in the carriage, and said something about meretricious manners. Mr. Arabin did not understand the ways of women very well, or else he might have flattered himself that Eleanor was in love with him.\n\nBut Eleanor was not in love with him. How many shades there are between love and indifference, and how little the graduated scale is understood! She had now been nearly three weeks in the same house with Mr. Arabin, and had received much of his attention and listened daily to his conversation. He had usually devoted at least some portion of his evening to her exclusively. At Dr. Stanhope's he had devoted himself exclusively to another. It does not require that a woman should be in love to be irritated at this; it does not require that she should even acknowledge to herself that it is unpleasant to her. Eleanor had no such self-knowledge. She thought in her own heart that it was only on Mr. Arabin's account that she regretted that he could condescend to be amused by the signora. \"I thought he had more mind,\" she said to herself as she sat watching her baby's cradle on her return from the party. \"After all, I believe Mr. Stanhope is the pleasanter man of the two.\" Alas for the memory of poor John Bold! Eleanor was not in love with Bertie Stanhope, nor was she in love with Mr. Arabin. But her devotion to her late husband was fast fading when she could revolve in her mind, over the cradle of his infant, the faults and failings of other aspirants to her favour.\n\nWill anyone blame my heroine for this? Let him or her rather thank God for all His goodness--for His mercy endureth forever.\n\nEleanor, in truth, was not in love; neither was Mr. Arabin. Neither indeed was Bertie Stanhope, though he had already found occasion to say nearly as much as that he was. The widow's cap had prevented him from making a positive declaration, when otherwise he would have considered himself entitled to do so on a third or fourth interview. It was, after all, but a small cap now, and had but little of the weeping willow left in its construction. It is singular how these emblems of grief fade away by unseen gradations. Each pretends to be the counterpart of the forerunner, and yet the last little bit of crimped white crape that sits so jauntily on the back of the head is as dissimilar to the first huge mountain of woe which disfigured the face of the weeper as the state of the Hindu is to the jointure of the English dowager.\n\nBut let it be clearly understood that Eleanor was in love with no one, and that no one was in love with Eleanor. Under these circumstances her anger against Mr. Arabin did not last long, and before two days were over they were both as good friends as ever. She could not but like him, for every hour spent in his company was spent pleasantly. And yet she could not quite like him, for there was always apparent in his conversation a certain feeling on his part that he hardly thought it worth his while to be in earnest. It was almost as though he were playing with a child. She knew well enough that he was in truth a sober, thoughtful man who, in some matters and on some occasions, could endure an agony of earnestness. And yet to her he was always gently playful. Could she have seen his brow once clouded, she might have learnt to love him.\n\nSo things went on at Plumstead, and on the whole not unpleasantly, till a huge storm darkened the horizon and came down upon the inhabitants of the rectory with all the fury of a water-spout. It was astonishing how in a few minutes the whole face of the heavens was changed. The party broke up from breakfast in perfect harmony, but fierce passions had arisen before the evening which did not admit of their sitting at the same board for dinner. To explain this it will be necessary to go back a little.\n\nIt will be remembered that the bishop expressed to Mr. Slope in his dressing-room his determination that Mr. Quiverful should be confirmed in his appointment to the hospital, and that his lordship requested Mr. Slope to communicate this decision to the archdeacon. It will also be remembered that the archdeacon had indignantly declined seeing Mr. Slope, and had instead written a strong letter to the bishop in which he all but demanded the situation of warden for Mr. Harding. To this letter the archdeacon received an immediate formal reply from Mr. Slope, in which it was stated that the bishop had received and would give his best consideration to the archdeacon's letter.\n\nThe archdeacon felt himself somewhat checkmated by this reply. What could he do with a man who would neither see him, nor argue with him by letter, and who had undoubtedly the power of appointing any clergyman he pleased? He had consulted with Mr. Arabin, who had suggested the propriety of calling in the aid of the Master of Lazarus. \"If,\" said he, \"you and Dr. Gwynne formally declare your intention of waiting upon the bishop, the bishop will not dare to refuse to see you; and if two such men as you are see him together, you will probably not leave him without carrying your point.\"\n\nThe archdeacon did not quite like admitting the necessity of his being backed by the Master of Lazarus before he could obtain admission into the episcopal palace of Barchester, but still he felt that the advice was good, and he resolved to take it. He wrote again to the bishop, expressing a hope that nothing further would be done in the matter of the hospital till the consideration promised by his lordship had been given, and then sent off a warm appeal to his friend the master, imploring him to come to Plumstead and assist in driving the bishop into compliance. The master had rejoined, raising some difficulty, but not declining, and the archdeacon had again pressed his point, insisting on the necessity for immediate action. Dr. Gwynne unfortunately had the gout, and could therefore name no immediate day, but still agreed to come, if it should be finally found necessary. So the matter stood, as regarded the party at Plumstead.\n\nBut Mr. Harding had another friend fighting his battle for him, quite as powerful as the Master of Lazarus, and this was Mr. Slope. Though the bishop had so pertinaciously insisted on giving way to his wife in the matter of the hospital, Mr. Slope did not think it necessary to abandon his object. He had, he thought, daily more and more reason to imagine that the widow would receive his overtures favourably, and he could not but feel that Mr. Harding at the hospital, and placed there by his means, would be more likely to receive him as a son-in-law than Mr. Harding growling in opposition and disappointment under the archdeacon's wing at Plumstead. Moreover, to give Mr. Slope due credit, he was actuated by greater motives even than these. He wanted a wife, and he wanted money, but he wanted power more than either. He had fully realized the fact that he must come to blows with Mrs. Proudie. He had no desire to remain in Barchester as her chaplain. Sooner than do so, he would risk the loss of his whole connexion with the diocese. What! Was he to feel within him the possession of no ordinary talents--was he to know himself to be courageous, firm, and, in matters where his conscience did not interfere, unscrupulous--and yet he contented to be the working factotum of a woman prelate? Mr. Slope had higher ideas of his own destiny. Either he or Mrs. Proudie must go to the wall, and now had come the time when he would try which it should be.\n\nThe bishop had declared that Mr. Quiverful should be the new warden. As Mr. Slope went downstairs, prepared to see the archdeacon, if necessary, but fully satisfied that no such necessity would arise, he declared to himself that Mr. Harding should be warden. With the object of carrying this point, he rode over to Puddingdale and had a further interview with the worthy expectant of clerical good things. Mr. Quiverful was on the whole a worthy man. The impossible task of bringing up as ladies and gentlemen fourteen children on an income which was insufficient to give them with decency the common necessaries of life, had had an effect upon him not beneficial either to his spirit or his keen sense of honour. Who can boast that he would have supported such a burden with a different result? Mr. Quiverful was an honest, painstaking, drudging man, anxious indeed for bread and meat, anxious for means to quiet his butcher and cover with returning smiles the now sour countenance of the baker's wife; but anxious also to be right with his own conscience. He was not careful, as another might be who sat on an easier worldly seat, to stand well with those around him, to shun a breath which might sully his name or a rumour which might affect his honour. He could not afford such niceties of conduct, such moral luxuries. It must suffice for him to be ordinarily honest according to the ordinary honesty of the world's ways, and to let men's tongues wag as they would.\n\nHe had felt that his brother clergymen, men whom he had known for the last twenty years, looked coldly on him from the first moment that he had shown himself willing to sit at the feet of Mr. Slope; he had seen that their looks grew colder still when it became bruited about that he was to be the bishop's new warden at Hiram's Hospital. This was painful enough, but it was the cross which he was doomed to bear. He thought of his wife, whose last new silk dress was six years in wear. He thought of all his young flock, whom he could hardly take to church with him on Sundays, for there were not decent shoes and stockings for them all to wear. He thought of the well-worn sleeves of his own black coat and of the stern face of the draper, from whom he would fain ask for cloth to make another, did he not know that the credit would be refused him. Then he thought of the comfortable house in Barchester, of the comfortable income, of his boys sent to school, of his girls with books in their hands instead of darning needles, of his wife's face again covered with smiles, and of his daily board again covered with plenty. He thought of these things; and do thou also, reader, think of them, and then wonder, if thou canst, that Mr. Slope had appeared to him to possess all those good gifts which could grace a bishop's chaplain. \"How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings.\"\n\nWhy, moreover, should the Barchester clergy have looked coldly on Mr. Quiverful? Had they not all shown that they regarded with complacency the loaves and fishes of their mother church? Had they not all, by some hook or crook, done better for themselves than he had done? They were not burdened as he was burdened. Dr. Grantly had five children and nearly as many thousands a year on which to feed them. It was very well for him to turn up his nose at a new bishop who could do nothing for him, and a chaplain who was beneath his notice; but it was cruel in a man so circumstanced to set the world against the father of fourteen children because he was anxious to obtain for them an honourable support! He, Mr. Quiverful, had not asked for the wardenship; he had not even accepted it till he had been assured that Mr. Harding had refused it. How hard then that he should be blamed for doing that which not to have done would have argued a most insane imprudence!\n\nThus in this matter of the hospital poor Mr. Quiverful had his trials, and he had also his consolations. On the whole the consolations were the more vivid of the two. The stern draper heard of the coming promotion, and the wealth of his warehouse was at Mr. Quiverful's disposal. Coming events cast their shadows before, and the coming event of Mr. Quiverful's transference to Barchester produced a delicious shadow in the shape of a new outfit for Mrs. Quiverful and her three elder daughters. Such consolations come home to the heart of a man, and quite home to the heart of a woman. Whatever the husband might feel, the wife cared nothing for frowns of dean, archdeacon, or prebendary. To her the outsides and insides of her husband and fourteen children were everything. In her bosom every other ambition had been swallowed up in that maternal ambition of seeing them and him and herself duly clad and properly fed. It had come to that with her that life had now no other purpose. She recked nothing of the imaginary rights of others. She had no patience with her husband when he declared to her that he could not accept the hospital unless he knew that Mr. Harding had refused it. Her husband had no right to be quixotic at the expense of fourteen children. The narrow escape of throwing away his good fortune which her lord had had, almost paralysed her. Now, indeed, they had received a full promise, not only from Mr. Slope, but also from Mrs. Proudie. Now, indeed, they might reckon with safety on their good fortune. But what if all had been lost? What if her fourteen bairns had been resteeped to the hips in poverty by the morbid sentimentality of their father? Mrs. Quiverful was just at present a happy woman, but yet it nearly took her breath away when she thought of the risk they had run.\n\n\"I don't know what your father means when he talks so much of what is due to Mr. Harding,\" she said to her eldest daughter. \"Does he think that Mr. Harding would give him £450 a year out of fine feeling? And what signifies it whom he offends, as long as he gets the place? He does not expect anything better. It passes me to think how your father can be so soft, while everybody around him is so griping.\"\n\nThus, while the outer world was accusing Mr. Quiverful of rapacity for promotion and of disregard to his honour, the inner world of his own household was falling foul of him, with equal vehemence, for his willingness to sacrifice their interests to a false feeling of sentimental pride. It is astonishing how much difference the point of view makes in the aspect of all that we look at!\n\nSuch were the feelings of the different members of the family at Puddingdale on the occasion of Mr. Slope's second visit. Mrs. Quiverful, as soon as she saw his horse coming up the avenue from the vicarage gate, hastily packed up her huge basket of needlework and hurried herself and her daughter out of the room in which she was sitting with her husband. \"It's Mr. Slope,\" she said. \"He's come to settle with you about the hospital. I do hope we shall now be able to move at once.\" And she hastened to bid the maid of all work go to the door, so that the welcome great man might not be kept waiting.\n\nMr. Slope thus found Mr. Quiverful alone. Mrs. Quiverful went off to her kitchen and back settlements with anxious beating heart, almost dreading that there might be some slip between the cup of her happiness and the lip of her fruition, but yet comforting herself with the reflexion that after what had taken place, any such slip could hardly be possible.\n\nMr. Slope was all smiles as he shook his brother clergyman's hand and said that he had ridden over because he thought it right at once to put Mr. Quiverful in possession of the facts of the matter regarding the wardenship of the hospital. As he spoke, the poor expectant husband and father saw at a glance that his brilliant hopes were to be dashed to the ground, and that his visitor was now there for the purpose of unsaying what on his former visit he had said. There was something in the tone of the voice, something in the glance of the eye, which told the tale. Mr. Quiverful knew it all at once. He maintained his self-possession, however, smiled with a slight unmeaning smile, and merely said that he was obliged to Mr. Slope for the trouble he was taking.\n\n\"It has been a troublesome matter from first to last,\" said Mr. Slope, \"and the bishop has hardly known how to act. Between ourselves--but mind this of course must go no further, Mr. Quiverful.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful said that of course it should not. \"The truth is that poor Mr. Harding has hardly known his own mind. You remember our last conversation, no doubt.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful assured him that he remembered it very well indeed.\n\n\"You will remember that I told you that Mr. Harding had refused to return to the hospital.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful declared that nothing could be more distinct on his memory.\n\n\"And acting on this refusal, I suggested that you should take the hospital,\" continued Mr. Slope.\n\n\"I understood you to say that the bishop had authorised you to offer it to me.\"\n\n\"Did I? Did I go so far as that? Well, perhaps it may be that in my anxiety in your behalf I did commit myself further than I should have done. So far as my own memory serves me, I don't think I did go quite so far as that. But I own I was very anxious that you should get it, and I may have said more than was quite prudent.\"\n\n\"But,\" said Mr. Quiverful in his deep anxiety to prove his case, \"my wife received as distinct a promise from Mrs. Proudie as one human being could give to another.\"\n\nMr. Slope smiled and gently shook his head. He meant the smile for a pleasant smile, but it was diabolical in the eyes of the man he was speaking to. \"Mrs. Proudie!\" he said. \"If we are to go to what passes between the ladies in these matters, we shall really be in a nest of troubles from which we shall never extricate ourselves. Mrs. Proudie is a most excellent lady, kind-hearted, charitable, pious, and in every way estimable. But, my dear Mr. Quiverful, the patronage of the diocese is not in her hands.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful for a moment sat panic-stricken and silent. \"Am I to understand, then, that I have received no promise?\" he said as soon as he had sufficiently collected his thoughts.\n\n\"If you will allow me, I will tell you exactly how the matter rests. You certainly did receive a promise conditional on Mr. Harding's refusal. I am sure you will do me the justice to remember that you yourself declared that you could accept the appointment on no other condition than the knowledge that Mr. Harding had declined it.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Mr. Quiverful; \"I did say that, certainly.\"\n\n\"Well, it now appears that he did not refuse it.\"\n\n\"But surely you told me, and repeated it more than once, that he had done so in your own hearing.\"\n\n\"So I understood him. But it seems I was in error. But don't for a moment, Mr. Quiverful, suppose that I mean to throw you over. No. Having held out my hand to a man in your position, with your large family and pressing claims, I am not now going to draw it back again. I only want you to act with me fairly and honestly.\"\n\n\"Whatever I do I shall endeavour at any rate to act fairly,\" said the poor man, feeling that he had to fall back for support on the spirit of martyrdom within him.\n\n\"I am sure you will,\" said the other. \"I am sure you have no wish to obtain possession of an income which belongs by all right to another. No man knows better than you do Mr. Harding's history, or can better appreciate his character. Mr. Harding is very desirous of returning to his old position, and the bishop feels that he is at the present moment somewhat hampered, though of course he is not bound, by the conversation which took place on the matter between you and me.\"\n\n\"Well,\" said Mr. Quiverful, dreadfully doubtful as to what his conduct under such circumstances should be, and fruitlessly striving to harden his nerves with some of that instinct of self-preservation which made his wife so bold.\n\n\"The wardenship of this little hospital is not the only thing in the bishop's gift, Mr. Quiverful, nor is it by many degrees the best. And his lordship is not the man to forget anyone whom he has once marked with approval. If you would allow me to advise you as a friend--\"\n\n\"Indeed, I shall be most grateful to you,\" said the poor vicar of Puddingdale.\n\n\"I should advise you to withdraw from any opposition to Mr. Harding's claims. If you persist in your demand, I do not think you will ultimately succeed. Mr. Harding has all but a positive right to the place. But if you will allow me to inform the bishop that you decline to stand in Mr. Harding's way, I think I may promise you--though, by the by, it must not be taken as a formal promise--that the bishop will not allow you to be a poorer man than you would have been had you become warden.\"\n\nMr. Quiverful sat in his armchair, silent, gazing at vacancy. What was he to say? All this that came from Mr. Slope was so true. Mr. Harding had a right to the hospital. The bishop had a great many good things to give away. Both the bishop and Mr. Slope would be excellent friends and terrible enemies to a man in his position. And then he had no proof of any promise; he could not force the bishop to appoint him.\n\n\"Well, Mr. Quiverful, what do you say about it?\"\n\n\"Oh, of course, whatever you think fit, Mr. Slope. It's a great disappointment, a very great disappointment. I won't deny that I am a very poor man, Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"In the end, Mr. Quiverful, you will find that it will have been better for you.\"\n\nThe interview ended in Mr. Slope receiving a full renunciation from Mr. Quiverful of any claim he might have to the appointment in question. It was only given verbally and without witnesses, but then the original promise was made in the same way.\n\nMr. Slope again assured him that he should not be forgotten, and then rode back to Barchester, satisfied that he would now be able to mould the bishop to his wishes.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV. Fourteen Arguments in Favour of Mr. Quiverful's Claims\n\nWe have most of us heard of the terrible anger of a lioness when, surrounded by her cubs, she guards her prey. Few of us wish to disturb the mother of a litter of puppies when mouthing a bone in the midst of her young family. Medea and her children are familiar to us, and so is the grief of Constance. Mrs. Quiverful, when she first heard from her husband the news which he had to impart, felt within her bosom all the rage of the lioness, the rapacity of the hound, the fury of the tragic queen, and the deep despair of the bereaved mother.\n\nDoubting, but yet hardly fearing, what might have been the tenor of Mr. Slope's discourse, she rushed back to her husband as soon as the front door was closed behind the visitor. It was well for Mr. Slope that he so escaped--the anger of such a woman, at such a moment, would have cowed even him. As a general rule, it is highly desirable that ladies should keep their temper: a woman when she storms always makes herself ugly, and usually ridiculous also. There is nothing so odious to man as a virago. Though Theseus loved an Amazon, he showed his love but roughly, and from the time of Theseus downward, no man ever wished to have his wife remarkable rather for forward prowess than retiring gentleness. A low voice \"is an excellent thing in woman.\"\n\nSuch may be laid down as a very general rule; and few women should allow themselves to deviate from it, and then only on rare occasions. But if there be a time when a woman may let her hair to the winds, when she may loose her arms, and scream out trumpet-tongued to the ears of men, it is when nature calls out within her not for her own wants, but for the wants of those whom her womb has borne, whom her breasts have suckled, for those who look to her for their daily bread as naturally as man looks to his Creator.\n\nThere was nothing poetic in the nature of Mrs. Quiverful. She was neither a Medea nor a Constance. When angry, she spoke out her anger in plain words, and in a tone which might have been modulated with advantage; but she did so, at any rate, without affectation. Now, without knowing it, she rose to a tragic vein.\n\n\"Well, my dear, we are not to have it.\" Such were the words with which her ears were greeted when she entered the parlour, still hot from the kitchen fire. And the face of her husband spoke even more plainly than his words:--\n\nE'en such a man, so faint, so spiritless, So dull, so dead in look, so woe-begone, Drew Priam's curtain in the dead of night.\n\n\"What!\" said she--and Mrs. Siddons could not have put more passion into a single syllable--\"What! Not have it? Who says so?\" And she sat opposite to her husband, with her elbows on the table, her hands clasped together, and her coarse, solid, but once handsome face stretched over it towards him.\n\nShe sat as silent as death while he told his story, and very dreadful to him her silence was. He told it very lamely and badly but still in such a manner that she soon understood the whole of it.\n\n\"And so you have resigned it?\" said she.\n\n\"I have had no opportunity of accepting it,\" he replied. \"I had no witnesses to Mr. Slope's offer, even if that offer would bind the bishop. It was better for me, on the whole, to keep on good terms with such men than to fight for what I should never get!\"\n\n\"Witnesses!\" she screamed, rising quickly to her feet and walking up and down the room. \"Do clergymen require witnesses to their words? He made the promise in the bishop's name, and if it is to be broken, I'll know the reason why. Did he not positively say that the bishop had sent him to offer you the place?\"\n\n\"He did, my dear. But that is now nothing to the purpose.\"\n\n\"It is everything to the purpose, Mr. Quiverful. Witnesses indeed! And then to talk of your honour being questioned because you wish to provide for fourteen children. It is everything to the purpose; and so they shall know, if I scream it into their ears from the town cross of Barchester.\"\n\n\"You forget, Letitia, that the bishop has so many things in his gift. We must wait a little longer. That is all.\"\n\n\"Wait! Shall we feed the children by waiting? Will waiting put George, and Tom, and Sam out into the world? Will it enable my poor girls to give up some of their drudgery? Will waiting make Bessy and Jane fit even to be governesses? Will waiting pay for the things we got in Barchester last week?\"\n\n\"It is all we can do, my dear. The disappointment is as much to me as to you; and yet, God knows, I feel it more for your sake than my own.\"\n\nMrs. Quiverful was looking full into her husband's face, and saw a small hot tear appear on each of those furrowed cheeks. This was too much for her woman's heart. He also had risen, and was standing with his back to the empty grate. She rushed towards him and, seizing him in her arms, sobbed aloud upon his bosom.\n\n\"You are too good, too soft, too yielding,\" she said at last. \"These men, when they want you, they use you like a cat's paw; and when they want you no longer, they throw you aside like an old shoe. This is twice they have treated you so.\"\n\n\"In one way this will be all for the better,\" argued he. \"It will make the bishop feel that he is bound to do something for me.\"\n\n\"At any rate he shall hear of it,\" said the lady, again reverting to her more angry mood. \"At any rate he shall hear of it, and that loudly; and so shall she. She little knows Letitia Quiverful, if she thinks I will sit down quietly with the loss after all that passed between us at the palace. If there's any feeling within her, I'll make her ashamed of herself,\"--and she paced the room again, stamping the floor as she went with her fat, heavy foot. \"Good heavens! What a heart she must have within her to treat in such a way as this the father of fourteen unprovided children!\"\n\nMr. Quiverful proceeded to explain that he didn't think that Mrs. Proudie had had anything to do with it.\n\n\"Don't tell me,\" said Mrs. Quiverful; \"I know more about it than that. Doesn't all the world know that Mrs. Proudie is bishop of Barchester and that Mr. Slope is merely her creature? Wasn't it she that made me the promise, just as though the thing was in her own particular gift? I tell you, it was that woman who sent him over here to-day, because, for some reason of her own, she wants to go back from her word.\"\n\n\"My dear, you're wrong--\"\n\n\"Now, Q., don't be so soft,\" she continued. \"Take my word for it, the bishop knows no more about it than Jemima does.\" Jemima was the two-year-old. \"And if you'll take my advice, you'll lose no time in going over and seeing him yourself.\"\n\nSoft, however, as Mr. Quiverful might be, he would not allow himself to be talked out of his opinion on this occasion, and proceeded with much minuteness to explain to his wife the tone in which Mr. Slope had spoken of Mrs. Proudie's interference in diocesan matters. As he did so, a new idea gradually instilled itself into the matron's head, and a new course of conduct presented itself to her judgement. What if, after all, Mrs. Proudie knew nothing of this visit of Mr. Slope's? In that case, might it not be possible that that lady would still be staunch to her in this matter, still stand her friend, and, perhaps, possibly carry her through in opposition to Mr. Slope? Mrs. Quiverful said nothing as this vague hope occurred to her, but listened with more than ordinary patience to what her husband had to say. While he was still explaining that in all probability the world was wrong in its estimation of Mrs. Proudie's power and authority, she had fully made up her mind as to her course of action. She did not, however, proclaim her intention. She shook her head ominously as he continued his narration, and when he had completed, she rose to go, merely observing that it was cruel, cruel treatment. She then asked him if he would mind waiting for a late dinner instead of dining at their usual hour of three; and, having received from him a concession on this point, she proceeded to carry her purpose into execution.\n\nShe determined that she would at once go to the palace, that she would do so, if possible, before Mrs. Proudie could have had an interview with Mr. Slope, and that she would be either submissive, piteous, and pathetic, or else indignant, violent, and exacting, according to the manner in which she was received.\n\nShe was quite confident in her own power. Strengthened as she was by the pressing wants of fourteen children, she felt that she could make her way through legions of episcopal servants and force herself, if need be, into the presence of the lady who had so wronged her. She had no shame about it, no mauvaise honte, no dread of archdeacons. She would, as she declared to her husband, make her wail heard in the market-place if she did not get redress and justice. It might be very well for an unmarried young curate to be shamefaced in such matters; it might be all right that a snug rector, really in want of nothing, but still looking for better preferment, should carry on his affairs decently under the rose. But Mrs. Quiverful, with fourteen children, had given over being shamefaced and, in some things, had given over being decent. If it were intended that she should be ill-used in the manner proposed by Mr. Slope, it should not be done under the rose. All the world should know of it.\n\nIn her present mood, Mrs. Quiverful was not over-careful about her attire. She tied her bonnet under her chin, threw her shawl over her shoulders, armed herself with the old family cotton umbrella, and started for Barchester. A journey to the palace was not quite so easy a thing for Mrs. Quiverful as for our friend at Plumstead. Plumstead is nine miles from Barchester, and Puddingdale is but four. But the archdeacon could order round his brougham, and his high-trotting fast bay gelding would take him into the city within the hour. There was no brougham in the coach-house of Puddingdale Vicarage, no bay horse in the stables. There was no method of locomotion for its inhabitants but that which nature has assigned to man.\n\nMrs. Quiverful was a broad, heavy woman, not young, nor given to walking. In her kitchen, and in the family dormitories, she was active enough, but her pace and gait were not adapted for the road. A walk into Barchester and back in the middle of an August day would be to her a terrible task, if not altogether impracticable. There was living in the parish, about half a mile from the vicarage on the road to the city, a decent, kindly farmer, well to do as regards this world and so far mindful of the next that he attended his parish church with decent regularity. To him Mrs. Quiverful had before now appealed in some of her more pressing family troubles, and had not appealed in vain. At his door she now presented herself, and, having explained to his wife that most urgent business required her to go at once to Barchester, begged that Farmer Subsoil would take her thither in his tax-cart. The farmer did not reject her plan, and, as soon as Prince could be got into his collar, they started on their journey.\n\nMrs. Quiverful did not mention the purpose of her business, nor did the farmer alloy his kindness by any unseemly questions. She merely begged to be put down at the bridge going into the city and to be taken up again at the same place in the course of two hours. The farmer promised to be punctual to his appointment, and the lady, supported by her umbrella, took the short cut to the close and, in a few minutes, was at the bishop's door.\n\nHitherto she had felt no dread with regard to the coming interview. She had felt nothing but an indignant longing to pour forth her claims, and declare her wrongs, if those claims were not fully admitted. But now the difficulty of her situation touched her a little. She had been at the palace once before, but then she went to give grateful thanks. Those who have thanks to return for favours received find easy admittance to the halls of the great. Such is not always the case with men, or even with women, who have favours to beg. Still less easy is access for those who demand the fulfilment of promises already made.\n\nMrs. Quiverful had not been slow to learn the ways of the world. She knew all this, and she knew also that her cotton umbrella and all but ragged shawl would not command respect in the eyes of the palatial servants. If she were too humble, she knew well that she would never succeed. To overcome by imperious overbearing with such a shawl as hers upon her shoulders and such a bonnet on her head would have required a personal bearing very superior to that with which nature had endowed her. Of this also Mrs. Quiverful was aware. She must make it known that she was the wife of a gentleman and a clergyman, and must yet condescend to conciliate.\n\nThe poor lady knew but one way to overcome these difficulties at the very threshold of her enterprise, and to this she resorted. Low as were the domestic funds at Puddingdale, she still retained possession of half a crown, and this she sacrificed to the avarice of Mrs. Proudie's metropolitan sesquipedalian serving-man. She was, she said, Mrs. Quiverful of Puddingdale, the wife of the Rev. Mr. Quiverful. She wished to see Mrs. Proudie. It was indeed quite indispensable that she should see Mrs. Proudie. James Fitzplush looked worse than dubious, did not know whether his lady were out, or engaged, or in her bedroom; thought it most probable she was subject to one of these or to some other cause that would make her invisible; but Mrs. Quiverful could sit down in the waiting-room while inquiry was being made of Mrs. Proudie's maid.\n\n\"Look here, my man,\" said Mrs. Quiverful; \"I must see her;\" and she put her card and half-crown--think of it, my reader, think of it; her last half-crown--into the man's hand and sat herself down on a chair in the waiting-room.\n\nWhether the bribe carried the day, or whether the bishop's wife really chose to see the vicar's wife, it boots not now to inquire. The man returned and, begging Mrs. Quiverful to follow him, ushered her into the presence of the mistress of the diocese.\n\nMrs. Quiverful at once saw that her patroness was in a smiling humour. Triumph sat throned upon her brow, and all the joys of dominion hovered about her curls. Her lord had that morning contested with her a great point. He had received an invitation to spend a couple of days with the archbishop. His soul longed for the gratification. Not a word, however, in his grace's note alluded to the fact of his being a married man; if he went at all, he must go alone. This necessity would have presented no insurmountable bar to the visit, or have militated much against the pleasure, had he been able to go without any reference to Mrs. Proudie. But this he could not do. He could not order his portmanteau to be packed and start with his own man, merely telling the lady of his heart that he would probably be back on Saturday. There are men--may we not rather say monsters?--who do such things, and there are wives--may we not rather say slaves?--who put up with such usage. But Dr. and Mrs. Proudie were not among the number.\n\nThe bishop, with some beating about the bush, made the lady understand that he very much wished to go. The lady, without any beating about the bush, made the bishop understand that she wouldn't hear of it. It would be useless here to repeat the arguments that were used on each side, and needless to record the result. Those who are married will understand very well how the battle was lost and won, and those who are single will never understand it till they learn the lesson which experience alone can give. When Mrs. Quiverful was shown into Mrs. Proudie's room, that lady had only returned a few minutes from her lord. But before she left him she had seen the answer to the archbishop's note written and sealed. No wonder that her face was wreathed with smiles as she received Mrs. Quiverful.\n\nShe instantly spoke of the subject which was so near the heart of her visitor. \"Well, Mrs. Quiverful,\" said she, \"is it decided yet when you are to move into Barchester?\"\n\n\"That woman,\" as she had an hour or two since been called, became instantly re-endowed with all the graces that can adorn a bishop's wife. Mrs. Quiverful immediately saw that her business was to be piteous, and that nothing was to be gained by indignation--nothing, indeed, unless she could be indignant in company with her patroness.\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie,\" she began, \"I fear we are not to move to Barchester at all.\"\n\n\"Why not?\" said that lady sharply, dropping at a moment's notice her smiles and condescension, and turning with her sharp quick way to business which she saw at a glance was important.\n\nAnd then Mrs. Quiverful told her tale. As she progressed in the history of her wrongs she perceived that the heavier she leant upon Mr. Slope the blacker became Mrs. Proudie's brow, but that such blackness was not injurious to her own case. When Mr. Slope was at Puddingdale Vicarage that morning she had regarded him as the creature of the lady-bishop; now she perceived that they were enemies. She admitted her mistake to herself without any pain or humiliation. She had but one feeling, and that was confined to her family. She cared little how she twisted and turned among these new-comers at the bishop's palace so long as she could twist her husband into the warden's house. She cared not which was her friend or which was her enemy, if only she could get this preferment which she so sorely wanted.\n\nShe told her tale, and Mrs. Proudie listened to it almost in silence. She told how Mr. Slope had cozened her husband into resigning his claim, and had declared that it was the bishop's will that none but Mr. Harding should be warden. Mrs. Proudie's brow became blacker and blacker. At last she started from her chair and, begging Mrs. Quiverful to sit and wait for her return, marched out of the room.\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie, it's for fourteen children--for fourteen children.\" Such was the burden that fell on her ear as she closed the door behind her.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI. Mrs. Proudie Wrestles and Gets a Fall\n\nIt was hardly an hour since Mrs. Proudie had left her husband's apartment victorious, and yet so indomitable was her courage that she now returned thither panting for another combat. She was greatly angry with what she thought was his duplicity. He had so clearly given her a promise on this matter of the hospital. He had been already so absolutely vanquished on that point. Mrs. Proudie began to feel that if every affair was to be thus discussed and battled about twice and even thrice, the work of the diocese would be too much even for her.\n\nWithout knocking at the door, she walked quickly into her husband's room and found him seated at his office table, with Mr. Slope opposite to him. Between his fingers was the very note which he had written to the archbishop in her presence--and it was open! Yes, he had absolutely violated the seal which had been made sacred by her approval. They were sitting in deep conclave, and it was too clear that the purport of the archbishop's invitation had been absolutely canvassed again, after it had been already debated and decided on in obedience to her behests! Mr. Slope rose from his chair and bowed slightly. The two opposing spirits looked each other fully in the face, and they knew that they were looking each at an enemy.\n\n\"What is this, Bishop, about Mr. Quiverful?\" said she, coming to the end of the table and standing there.\n\nMr. Slope did not allow the bishop to answer but replied himself. \"I have been out to Puddingdale this morning, ma'am, and have seen Mr. Quiverful. Mr. Quiverful has abandoned his claim to the hospital because he is now aware that Mr. Harding is desirous to fill his old place. Under these circumstances I have strongly advised his lordship to nominate Mr. Harding.\"\n\n\"Mr. Quiverful has not abandoned anything,\" said the lady, with a very imperious voice. \"His lordship's word has been pledged to him, and it must be respected.\"\n\nThe bishop still remained silent. He was anxiously desirous of making his old enemy bite the dust beneath his feet. His new ally had told him that nothing was more easy for him than to do so. The ally was there now at his elbow to help him, and yet his courage failed him. It is so hard to conquer when the prestige of former victories is all against one. It is so hard for the cock who has once been beaten out of his yard to resume his courage and again take a proud place upon a dunghill.\n\n\"Perhaps I ought not to interfere,\" said Mr. Slope, \"but yet--\"\n\n\"Certainly you ought not,\" said the infuriated dame.\n\n\"But yet,\" continued Mr. Slope, not regarding the interruption, \"I have thought it my imperative duty to recommend the bishop not to slight Mr. Harding's claims.\"\n\n\"Mr. Harding should have known his own mind,\" said the lady.\n\n\"If Mr. Harding be not replaced at the hospital, his lordship will have to encounter much ill-will, not only in the diocese, but in the world at large. Besides, taking a higher ground, his lordship, as I understand, feels it to be his duty to gratify, in this matter, so very worthy a man and so good a clergyman as Mr. Harding.\"\n\n\"And what is to become of the Sabbath-day school and of the Sunday services in the hospital?\" said Mrs. Proudie, with something very nearly approaching to a sneer on her face.\n\n\"I understand that Mr. Harding makes no objection to the Sabbath-day school,\" said Mr. Slope. \"And as to the hospital services, that matter will be best discussed after his appointment. If he has any permanent objection, then, I fear, the matter must rest.\"\n\n\"You have a very easy conscience in such matters, Mr. Slope,\" said she.\n\n\"I should not have an easy conscience,\" he rejoined, \"but a conscience very far from being easy, if anything said or done by me should lead the bishop to act unadvisedly in this matter. It is clear that in the interview I had with Mr. Harding I misunderstood him--\"\n\n\"And it is equally clear that you have misunderstood Mr. Quiverful,\" said she, now at the top of her wrath. \"What business have you at all with these interviews? Who desired you to go to Mr. Quiverful this morning? Who commissioned you to manage this affair? Will you answer me, sir? Who sent you to Mr. Quiverful this morning?\"\n\nThere was a dead pause in the room. Mr. Slope had risen from his chair, and was standing with his hand on the back of it, looking at first very solemn and now very black. Mrs. Proudie was standing as she had at first placed herself, at the end of the table, and as she interrogated her foe she struck her hand upon it with almost more than feminine vigour. The bishop was sitting in his easy chair twiddling his thumbs, turning his eyes now to his wife, and now to his chaplain, as each took up the cudgels. How comfortable it would be if they could fight it out between them without the necessity of any interference on his part; fight it out so that one should kill the other utterly, as far as diocesan life was concerned, so that he, the bishop, might know clearly by whom it behoved him to be led. There would be the comfort of quiet in either case; but if the bishop had a wish as to which might prove the victor, that wish was certainly not antagonistic to Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Better the d---- you know than the d---- you don't know,\" is an old saying, and perhaps a true one; but the bishop had not yet realized the truth of it.\n\n\"Will you answer me, sir?\" she repeated. \"Who instructed you to call on Mr. Quiverful this morning?\" There was another pause. \"Do you intend to answer me, sir?\"\n\n\"I think, Mrs. Proudie, that under all the circumstances it will be better for me not to answer such a question,\" said Mr. Slope. Mr. Slope had many tones in his voice, all duly under his command; among them was a sanctified low tone and a sanctified loud tone--he now used the former.\n\n\"Did anyone send you, sir?\"\n\n\"Mrs. Proudie,\" said Mr. Slope, \"I am quite aware how much I owe to your kindness. I am aware also what is due by courtesy from a gentleman to a lady. But there are higher considerations than either of those, and I hope I shall be forgiven if I now allow myself to be actuated solely by them. My duty in this matter is to his lordship, and I can admit of no questioning but from him. He has approved of what I have done, and you must excuse me if I say that, having that approval and my own, I want none other.\"\n\nWhat horrid words were these which greeted the ear of Mrs. Proudie? The matter was indeed too clear. There was premeditated mutiny in the camp. Not only had ill-conditioned minds become insubordinate by the fruition of a little power, but sedition had been overtly taught and preached. The bishop had not yet been twelve months in his chair, and rebellion had already reared her hideous head within the palace. Anarchy and misrule would quickly follow unless she took immediate and strong measures to put down the conspiracy which she had detected.\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" she said with slow and dignified voice, differing much from that which she had hitherto used, \"Mr. Slope, I will trouble you, if you please, to leave the apartment. I wish to speak to my lord alone.\"\n\nMr. Slope also felt that everything depended on the present interview. Should the bishop now be re-petticoated, his thraldom would be complete and forever. The present moment was peculiarly propitious for rebellion. The bishop had clearly committed himself by breaking the seal of the answer to the archbishop; he had therefore fear to influence him. Mr. Slope had told him that no consideration ought to induce him to refuse the archbishop's invitation; he had therefore hope to influence him. He had accepted Mr. Quiverful's resignation and therefore dreaded having to renew that matter with his wife. He had been screwed up to the pitch of asserting a will of his own, and might possibly be carried on till by an absolute success he should have been taught how possible it was to succeed. Now was the moment for victory or rout. It was now that Mr. Slope must make himself master of the diocese, or else resign his place and begin his search for fortune again. He saw all this plainly. After what had taken place any compromise between him and the lady was impossible. Let him once leave the room at her bidding and leave the bishop in her hands, and he might at once pack up his portmanteau and bid adieu to episcopal honours, Mrs. Bold, and the Signora Neroni.\n\nAnd yet it was not so easy to keep his ground when he was bidden by a lady to go, or to continue to make a third in a party between a husband and wife when the wife expressed a wish for a tête-à-tête with her husband.\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" she repeated, \"I wish to be alone with my lord.\"\n\n\"His lordship has summoned me on most important diocesan business,\" said Mr. Slope, glancing with uneasy eye at Dr. Proudie. He felt that he must trust something to the bishop, and yet that that trust was so woefully ill-placed. \"My leaving him at the present moment is, I fear, impossible.\"\n\n\"Do you bandy words with me, you ungrateful man?\" said she. \"My lord, will you do me the favour to beg Mr. Slope to leave the room?\"\n\nMy lord scratched his head, but for the moment said nothing. This was as much as Mr. Slope expected from him, and was on the whole, for him, an active exercise of marital rights.\n\n\"My lord,\" said the lady, \"is Mr. Slope to leave this room, or am I?\"\n\nHere Mrs. Proudie made a false step. She should not have alluded to the possibility of retreat on her part. She should not have expressed the idea that her order for Mr. Slope's expulsion could be treated otherwise than by immediate obedience. In answer to such a question the bishop naturally said in his own mind that, as it was necessary that one should leave the room, perhaps it might be as well that Mrs. Proudie did so. He did say so in his own mind, but externally he again scratched his head and again twiddled his thumbs.\n\nMrs. Proudie was boiling over with wrath. Alas, alas! Could she but have kept her temper as her enemy did, she would have conquered as she had ever conquered. But divine anger got the better of her, as it has done of other heroines, and she fell.\n\n\"My lord,\" said she, \"am I to be vouchsafed an answer or am I not?\"\n\nAt last he broke his deep silence and proclaimed himself a Slopeite. \"Why, my dear,\" said he, \"Mr. Slope and I are very busy.\"\n\nThat was all. There was nothing more necessary. He had gone to the battlefield, stood the dust and heat of the day, encountered the fury of the foe, and won the victory. How easy is success to those who will only be true to themselves!\n\nMr. Slope saw at once the full amount of his gain, and turned on the vanquished lady a look of triumph which she never forgot and never forgave. Here he was wrong. He should have looked humbly at her and, with meek entreating eye, have deprecated her anger. He should have said by his glance that he asked pardon for his success, and that he hoped forgiveness for the stand which he had been forced to make in the cause of duty. So might he perchance have somewhat mollified that imperious bosom and prepared the way for future terms. But Mr. Slope meant to rule without terms. Ah, forgetful, inexperienced man! Can you cause that little trembling victim to be divorced from the woman that possesses him? Can you provide that they shall be separated at bed and board? Is he not flesh of her flesh and bone of her bone, and must he not so continue? It is very well now for you to stand your ground and triumph as she is driven ignominiously from the room, but can you be present when those curtains are drawn, when that awful helmet of proof has been tied beneath the chin, when the small remnants of the bishop's prowess shall be cowed by the tassel above his head? Can you then intrude yourself when the wife wishes \"to speak to my lord alone?\"\n\nBut for the moment Mr. Slope's triumph was complete, for Mrs. Proudie without further parley left the room and did not forget to shut the door after her. Then followed a close conference between the new allies, in which was said much which it astonished Mr. Slope to say and the bishop to hear. And yet the one said it and the other heard it without ill-will. There was no mincing of matters now. The chaplain plainly told the bishop that the world gave him credit for being under the governance of his wife; that his credit and character in the diocese were suffering; that he would surely get himself in hot water if he allowed Mrs. Proudie to interfere in matters which were not suitable for a woman's powers; and in fact that he would become contemptible if he did not throw off the yoke under which he groaned. The bishop at first hummed and hawed and affected to deny the truth of what was said. But his denial was not stout and quickly broke down. He soon admitted by silence his state of vassalage and pledged himself, with Mr. Slope's assistance, to change his courses. Mr. Slope also did not make out a bad case for himself. He explained how it grieved him to run counter to a lady who had always been his patroness, who had befriended him in so many ways, who had, in fact, recommended him to the bishop's notice; but, as he stated, his duty was now imperative; he held a situation of peculiar confidence, and was immediately and especially attached to the bishop's person. In such a situation his conscience required that he should regard solely the bishop's interests, and therefore he had ventured to speak out.\n\nThe bishop took this for what it was worth, and Mr. Slope only intended that he should do so. It gilded the pill which Mr. Slope had to administer, and which the bishop thought would be less bitter than that other pill which he had so long been taking.\n\n\"My lord,\" had his immediate reward, like a good child. He was instructed to write and at once did write another note to the archbishop accepting his grace's invitation. This note Mr. Slope, more prudent than the lady, himself took away and posted with his own hands. Thus he made sure that this act of self-jurisdiction should be as nearly as possible a fait accompli. He begged, and coaxed, and threatened the bishop with a view of making him also write at once to Mr. Harding, but the bishop, though temporally emancipated from his wife, was not yet enthralled to Mr. Slope. He said, and probably said truly, that such an offer must be made in some official form; that he was not yet prepared to sign the form; and that he should prefer seeing Mr. Harding before he did so. Mr. Slope might, however, beg Mr. Harding to call upon him. Not disappointed with his achievement Mr. Slope went his way. He first posted the precious note which he had in his pocket, and then pursued other enterprises in which we must follow him in other chapters.\n\nMrs. Proudie, having received such satisfaction as was to be derived from slamming her husband's door, did not at once betake herself to Mrs. Quiverful. Indeed, for the first few moments after her repulse she felt that she could not again see that lady. She would have to own that she had been beaten, to confess that the diadem had passed from her brow, and the sceptre from her hand! No, she would send a message to her with a promise of a letter on the next day or the day after. Thus resolving, she betook herself to her bedroom, but here she again changed her mind. The air of that sacred enclosure somewhat restored her courage and gave her more heart. As Achilles warmed at the sight of his armour, as Don Quixote's heart grew strong when he grasped his lance, so did Mrs. Proudie look forward to fresh laurels, as her eye fell on her husband's pillow. She would not despair. Having so resolved, she descended with dignified mien and refreshed countenance to Mrs. Quiverful.\n\nThis scene in the bishop's study took longer in the acting than in the telling. We have not, perhaps, had the whole of the conversation. At any rate Mrs. Quiverful was beginning to be very impatient, and was thinking that Farmer Subsoil would be tired of waiting for her, when Mrs. Proudie returned. Oh, who can tell the palpitations of that maternal heart, as the suppliant looked into the face of the great lady to see written there either a promise of house, income, comfort and future competence, or else the doom of continued and ever-increasing poverty! Poor mother! Poor wife! There was little there to comfort you!\n\n\"Mrs. Quiverful,\" thus spoke the lady with considerable austerity, and without sitting down herself, \"I find that your husband has behaved in this matter in a very weak and foolish manner.\"\n\nMrs. Quiverful immediately rose upon her feet, thinking it disrespectful to remain sitting while the wife of the bishop stood. But she was desired to sit down again, and made to do so, so that Mrs. Proudie might stand and preach over her. It is generally considered an offensive thing for a gentleman to keep his seat while another is kept standing before him, and we presume the same law holds with regard to ladies. It often is so felt, but we are inclined to say that it never produces half the discomfort or half the feeling of implied inferiority that is shown by a great man who desires his visitor to be seated while he himself speaks from his legs. Such a solecism in good breeding, when construed into English, means this: \"The accepted rules of courtesy in the world require that I should offer you a seat; if I did not do so, you would bring a charge against me in the world of being arrogant and ill-mannered; I will obey the world, but, nevertheless, I will not put myself on an equality with you. You may sit down, but I won't sit with you. Sit, therefore, at my bidding, and I'll stand and talk at you!\"\n\nThis was just what Mrs. Proudie meant to say, and Mrs. Quiverful, though she was too anxious and too flurried thus to translate the full meaning of the manoeuvre, did not fail to feel its effect. She was cowed and uncomfortable, and a second time essayed to rise from her chair.\n\n\"Pray be seated, Mrs. Quiverful, pray keep your seat. Your husband, I say, has been most weak and most foolish. It is impossible, Mrs. Quiverful, to help people who will not help themselves. I much fear that I can now do nothing for you in this matter.\"\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie, don't say so,\" said the poor woman, again jumping up.\n\n\"Pray be seated, Mrs. Quiverful. I must fear that I can do nothing further for you in this matter. Your husband has, in a most unaccountable manner, taken upon himself to resign that which I was empowered to offer him. As a matter of course, the bishop expects that his clergy shall know their own minds. What he may ultimately do--what we may finally decide on doing--I cannot now say. Knowing the extent of your family--\"\n\n\"Fourteen children, Mrs. Proudie, fourteen of them! And barely bread--barely bread? It's hard for the children of a clergyman, it's hard for one who has always done his duty respectably!\" Not a word fell from her about herself, but the tears came streaming down her big, coarse cheeks, on which the dust of the August road had left its traces.\n\nMrs. Proudie has not been portrayed in these pages as an agreeable or an amiable lady. There has been no intention to impress the reader much in her favour. It is ordained that all novels should have a male and a female angel and a male and a female devil. If it be considered that this rule is obeyed in these pages, the latter character must be supposed to have fallen to the lot of Mrs. Proudie. But she was not all devil. There was a heart inside that stiff-ribbed bodice, though not, perhaps, of large dimensions, and certainly not easily accessible. Mrs. Quiverful, however, did gain access, and Mrs. Proudie proved herself a woman. Whether it was the fourteen children with their probable bare bread and their possible bare backs, or the respectability of the father's work, or the mingled dust and tears on the mother's face, we will not pretend to say. But Mrs. Proudie was touched.\n\nShe did not show it as other women might have done. She did not give Mrs. Quiverful eau-de-Cologne, or order her a glass of wine. She did not take her to her toilet table and offer her the use of brushes and combs, towels and water. She did not say soft little speeches and coax her kindly back to equanimity. Mrs. Quiverful, despite her rough appearance, would have been as amenable to such little tender cares as any lady in the land. But none such were forthcoming. Instead of this, Mrs. Proudie slapped one hand upon the other and declared--not with an oath, for, as a lady and a Sabbatarian and a she-bishop, she could not swear, but with an adjuration--that she \"wouldn't have it done.\"\n\nThe meaning of this was that she wouldn't have Mr. Quiverful's promised appointment cozened away by the treachery of Mr. Slope and the weakness of her husband. This meaning she very soon explained to Mrs. Quiverful.\n\n\"Why was your husband such a fool,\" said she, now dismounted from her high horse and sitting confidentially down close to her visitor, \"as to take the bait which that man threw to him? If he had not been so utterly foolish, nothing could have prevented your going to the hospital.\"\n\nPoor Mrs. Quiverful was ready enough with her own tongue in accusing her husband to his face of being soft, and perhaps did not always speak of him to her children quite so respectfully as she might have done. But she did not at all like to hear him abused by others, and began to vindicate him and to explain that of course he had taken Mr. Slope to be an emissary from Mrs. Proudie herself; that Mr. Slope was thought to be peculiarly her friend; and that, therefore, Mr. Quiverful would have been failing in respect to her had he assumed to doubt what Mr. Slope had said.\n\nThus mollified, Mrs. Proudie again declared that she \"would not have it done,\" and at last sent Mrs. Quiverful home with an assurance that, to the furthest stretch of her power and influence in the palace, the appointment of Mr. Quiverful should be insisted on. As she repeated the word \"insisted,\" she thought of the bishop in his night-cap and, with compressed lips, slightly shook her head. Oh, my aspiring pastors, divines to whose ears nolo episcopari are the sweetest of words, which of you would be a bishop on such terms as these?\n\nMrs. Quiverful got home in the farmer's cart, not indeed with a light heart, but satisfied that she had done right in making her visit.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII. A Love Scene\n\nMr. Slope, as we have said, left the palace with a feeling of considerable triumph. Not that he thought that his difficulties were all over--he did not so deceive himself--but he felt that he had played his first move well, as well as the pieces on the board would allow, and that he had nothing with which to reproach himself. He first of all posted the letter to the archbishop and, having made that sure, proceeded to push the advantage which he had gained. Had Mrs. Bold been at home, he would have called on her, but he knew that she was at Plumstead, so he wrote the following note. It was the beginning of what, he trusted, might be a long and tender series of epistles.\n\nYou will understand perfectly that I cannot at present correspond with your father. I heartily wish that I could, and hope the day may be not long distant when mists shall have been cleared away, and we may know each other. But I cannot preclude myself from the pleasure of sending you these few lines to say that Mr. Q. has to-day, in my presence, resigned any title that he ever had to the wardenship of the hospital, and that the bishop has assured me that it is his intention to offer it to your esteemed father.\n\nWill you, with my respectful compliments, ask him, who I believe is now a fellow-visitor with you, to call on the bishop either on Wednesday or Thursday, between ten and one. This is by the bishop's desire. If you will so far oblige me as to let me have a line naming either day, and the hour which will suit Mr. Harding, I will take care that the servants shall have orders to show him in without delay. Perhaps I should say no more--but still I wish you could make your father understand that no subject will be mooted between his lordship and him which will refer at all to the method in which he may choose to perform his duty. I for one am persuaded that no clergyman could perform it more satisfactorily than he did, or than he will do again.\n\nOn a former occasion I was indiscreet and much too impatient, considering your father's age and my own. I hope he will not now refuse my apology. I still hope also that with your aid and sweet pious labours we may live to attach such a Sabbath-school to the old endowment as may, by God's grace and furtherance, be a blessing to the poor of this city.\n\nYou will see at once that this letter is confidential. The subject, of course, makes it so. But, equally, of course, it is for your parent's eye as well as for your own, should you think proper to show it to him.\n\nI hope my darling little friend Johnny is as strong as ever--dear little fellow. Does he still continue his rude assaults on those beautiful long silken tresses?\n\nI can assure you your friends miss you from Barchester sorely, but it would be cruel to begrudge you your sojourn among flowers and fields during this truly sultry weather.\n\nPray believe me, my dear Mrs. Bold, Yours most sincerely, OBADIAH SLOPE\n\nBarchester, Friday.\n\nNow this letter, taken as a whole, and with the consideration that Mr. Slope wished to assume a great degree of intimacy with Eleanor, would not have been bad but for the allusion to the tresses. Gentlemen do not write to ladies about their tresses unless they are on very intimate terms indeed. But Mr. Slope could not be expected to be aware of this. He longed to put a little affection into his epistle, and yet he thought it injudicious, as the letter would, he knew, be shown to Mr. Harding. He would have insisted that the letter should be strictly private and seen by no eyes but Eleanor's own, had he not felt that such an injunction would have been disobeyed. He therefore restrained his passion, did not sign himself \"yours affectionately,\" and contented himself instead with the compliment to the tresses.\n\nHaving finished his letter, he took it to Mrs. Bold's house and, learning there, from the servant, that things were to be sent out to Plumstead that afternoon, left it, with many injunctions, in her hands.\n\nWe will now follow Mr. Slope so as to complete the day with him and then return to his letter and its momentous fate in the next chapter.\n\nThere is an old song which gives us some very good advice about courting:--\n\nIt's gude to be off with the auld luve Before ye be on wi' the new.\n\nOf the wisdom of this maxim Mr. Slope was ignorant, and accordingly, having written his letter to Mrs. Bold, he proceeded to call upon the Signora Neroni. Indeed, it was hard to say which was the old love and which the new, Mr. Slope having been smitten with both so nearly at the same time. Perhaps he thought it not amiss to have two strings to his bow. But two strings to Cupid's bow are always dangerous to him on whose behalf they are to be used. A man should remember that between two stools he may fall to the ground.\n\nBut in sooth Mr. Slope was pursuing Mrs. Bold in obedience to his better instincts, and the signora in obedience to his worser. Had he won the widow and worn her, no one could have blamed him. You, O reader, and I, and Eleanor's other friends would have received the story of such a winning with much disgust and disappointment, but we should have been angry with Eleanor, not with Mr. Slope. Bishop, male and female, dean and chapter and diocesan clergy in full congress could have found nothing to disapprove of in such an alliance. Convocation itself, that mysterious and mighty synod, could in no wise have fallen foul of it. The possession of £1000 a year and a beautiful wife would not at all have hurt the voice of the pulpit charmer, or lessened the grace and piety of the exemplary clergyman.\n\nBut not of such a nature were likely to be his dealings with the Signora Neroni. In the first place he knew that her husband was living, and therefore he could not woo her honestly. Then again she had nothing to recommend her to his honest wooing, had such been possible. She was not only portionless, but also from misfortune unfitted to be chosen as the wife of any man who wanted a useful mate. Mr. Slope was aware that she was a helpless, hopeless cripple.\n\nBut Mr. Slope could not help himself. He knew that he was wrong in devoting his time to the back drawing-room in Dr. Stanhope's house. He knew that what took place there would, if divulged, utterly ruin him with Mrs. Bold. He knew that scandal would soon come upon his heels and spread abroad among the black coats of Barchester some tidings, exaggerated tidings, of the sighs which he poured into the lady's ears. He knew that he was acting against the recognized principles of his life, against those laws of conduct by which he hoped to achieve much higher success. But, as we have said, he could not help himself. Passion, for the first time in his life, passion was too strong for him.\n\nAs for the signora, no such plea can be put forward for her, for in truth she cared no more for Mr. Slope than she did for twenty others who had been at her feet before him. She willingly, nay greedily, accepted his homage. He was the finest fly that Barchester had hitherto afforded to her web, and the signora was a powerful spider that made wondrous webs, and could in no way live without catching flies. Her taste in this respect was abominable, for she had no use for the victims when caught. She could not eat them matrimonially, as young lady flies do whose webs are most frequently of their mothers' weaving. Nor could she devour them by any escapade of a less legitimate description. Her unfortunate affliction precluded her from all hope of levanting with a lover. It would be impossible to run away with a lady who required three servants to move her from a sofa.\n\nThe signora was subdued by no passion. Her time for love was gone. She had lived out her heart, such heart as she had ever had, in her early years, at an age when Mr. Slope was thinking of the second book of Euclid and his unpaid bill at the buttery hatch. In age the lady was younger than the gentleman, but in feelings, in knowledge of the affairs of love, in intrigue, he was immeasurably her junior. It was necessary to her to have some man at her feet. It was the one customary excitement of her life. She delighted in the exercise of power which this gave her; it was now nearly the only food for her ambition; she would boast to her sister that she could make a fool of any man, and the sister, as little imbued with feminine delicacy as herself, good-naturedly thought it but fair that such amusement should be afforded to a poor invalid who was debarred from the ordinary pleasures of life.\n\nMr. Slope was madly in love but hardly knew it. The Signora spitted him, as a boy does a cockchafer on a cork, that she might enjoy the energetic agony of his gyrations. And she knew very well what she was doing.\n\nMr. Slope having added to his person all such adornments as are possible to a clergyman making a morning visit--such as a clean necktie, clean handkerchief, new gloves, and a soupçon of not unnecessary scent--called about three o'clock at the doctor's door. At about this hour the signora was almost always alone in the back drawing-room. The mother had not come down. The doctor was out or in his own room. Bertie was out, and Charlotte at any rate left the room if anyone called whose object was specially with her sister. Such was her idea of being charitable and sisterly.\n\nMr. Slope, as was his custom, asked for Mr. Stanhope, and was told, as was the servant's custom, that the signora was in the drawing-room. Upstairs he accordingly went. He found her, as he always did, lying on her sofa with a French volume before her and a beautiful little inlaid writing-case open on her table. At the moment of his entrance she was in the act of writing.\n\n\"Ah, my friend,\" said she, putting out her left hand to him across her desk, \"I did not expect you to-day and was this very instant writing to you--\"\n\nMr. Slope, taking the soft, fair, delicate hand in his--and very soft and fair and delicate it was--bowed over it his huge red head and kissed it. It was a sight to see, a deed to record if the author could fitly do it, a picture to put on canvas. Mr. Slope was big, awkward, cumbrous, and, having his heart in his pursuit, was ill at ease. The lady was fair, as we have said, and delicate; everything about her was fine and refined; her hand in his looked like a rose lying among carrots, and when he kissed it, he looked as a cow might do on finding such a flower among her food. She was graceful as a couchant goddess and, moreover, as self-possessed as Venus must have been when courting Adonis.\n\nOh, that such grace and such beauty should have condescended to waste itself on such a pursuit!\n\n\"I was in the act of writing to you,\" said she, \"but now my scrawl may go into the basket;\" and she raised the sheet of gilded note-paper from off her desk as though to tear it.\n\n\"Indeed it shall not,\" said he, laying the embargo of half a stone weight of human flesh and blood upon the devoted paper. \"Nothing that you write for my eyes, signora, shall be so desecrated,\" and he took up the letter, put that also among the carrots and fed on it, and then proceeded to read it.\n\n\"Gracious me! Mr. Slope,\" said she, \"I hope you don't mean to say you keep all the trash I write to you. Half my time I don't know what I write, and when I do, I know it is only fit for the back of the fire. I hope you have not that ugly trick of keeping letters.\"\n\n\"At any rate, I don't throw them into a waste-paper basket. If destruction is their doomed lot, they perish worthily, and are burnt on a pyre, as Dido was of old.\"\n\n\"With a steel pen stuck through them, of course,\" said she, \"to make the simile more complete. Of all the ladies of my acquaintance I think Lady Dido was the most absurd. Why did she not do as Cleopatra did? Why did she not take out her ships and insist on going with him? She could not bear to lose the land she had got by a swindle, and then she could not bear the loss of her lover. So she fell between two stools. Mr. Slope, whatever you do, never mingle love and business.\"\n\nMr. Slope blushed up to his eyes and over his mottled forehead to the very roots of his hair. He felt sure that the signora knew all about his intentions with reference to Mrs Bold. His conscience told him that he was detected. His doom was to be spoken; he was to be punished for his duplicity, and rejected by the beautiful creature before him. Poor man. He little dreamt that had all his intentions with reference to Mrs. Bold been known to the signora, it would only have added zest to that lady's amusement. It was all very well to have Mr. Slope at her feet, to show her power by making an utter fool of a clergyman, to gratify her own infidelity by thus proving the little strength which religion had in controlling the passions even of a religious man; but it would be an increased gratification if she could be made to understand that she was at the same time alluring her victim away from another, whose love if secured would be in every way beneficent and salutary.\n\nThe Signora had indeed discovered, with the keen instinct of such a woman, that Mr. Slope was bent on matrimony with Mrs. Bold, but in alluding to Dido she had not thought of it. She instantly perceived, however, from her lover's blushes, what was on his mind and was not slow in taking advantage of it.\n\nShe looked him full in the face, not angrily, nor yet with a smile, but with an intense and overpowering gaze; then, holding up her forefinger and slightly shaking her head, she said:--\n\n\"Whatever you do, my friend, do not mingle love and business. Either stick to your treasure and your city of wealth, or else follow your love like a true man. But never attempt both. If you do, you'll have to die with a broken heart as did poor Dido. Which is it to be with you, Mr. Slope, love or money?\"\n\nMr. Slope was not so ready with a pathetic answer as he usually was with touching episodes in his extempore sermons. He felt that he ought to say something pretty, something also that should remove the impression on the mind of his lady-love. But he was rather put about how to do it.\n\n\"Love,\" said he, \"true overpowering love, must be the strongest passion a man can feel; it must control every other wish, and put aside every other pursuit. But with me love will never act in that way unless it be returned;\" and he threw upon the signora a look of tenderness which was intended to make up for all the deficiencies of his speech.\n\n\"Take my advice,\" said she. \"Never mind love. After all, what is it? The dream of a few weeks. That is all its joy. The disappointment of a life is its Nemesis. Who was ever successful in true love? Success in love argues that the love is false. True love is always despondent or tragical. Juliet loved, Haidee loved, Dido loved, and what came of it? Troilus loved and ceased to be a man.\"\n\n\"Troilus loved and was fooled,\" said the more manly chaplain. \"A man may love and yet not be a Troilus. All women are not Cressidas.\"\n\n\"No, all women are not Cressidas. The falsehood is not always on the woman's side. Imogen was true, but how was she rewarded? Her lord believed her to be the paramour of the first he who came near her in his absence. Desdemona was true and was smothered. Ophelia was true and went mad. There is no happiness in love, except at the end of an English novel. But in wealth, money, houses, lands, goods, and chattels, in the good things of this world, yes, in them there is something tangible, something that can be retained and enjoyed.\"\n\n\"Oh, no,\" said Mr. Slope, feeling himself bound to enter some protest against so very unorthodox a doctrine, \"this world's wealth will make no one happy.\"\n\n\"And what will make you happy--you--you?\" said she, raising herself up and speaking to him with energy across the table. \"From what source do you look for happiness? Do not say that you look for none. I shall not believe you. It is a search in which every human being spends an existence.\"\n\n\"And the search is always in vain,\" said Mr. Slope. \"We look for happiness on earth, while we ought to be content to hope for it in heaven.\"\n\n\"Pshaw! You preach a doctrine which you know you don't believe. It is the way with you all. If you know that there is no earthly happiness, why do you long to be a bishop or a dean? Why do you want lands and income?\"\n\n\"I have the natural ambition of a man,\" said he.\n\n\"Of course you have, and the natural passions; and therefore I say that you don't believe the doctrine you preach. St. Paul was an enthusiast. He believed so that his ambition and passions did not war against his creed. So does the Eastern fanatic who passes half his life erect upon a pillar. As for me, I will believe in no belief that does not make itself manifest by outward signs. I will think no preaching sincere that is not recommended by the practice of the preacher.\"\n\nMr. Slope was startled and horrified, but he felt that he could not answer. How could he stand up and preach the lessons of his Master, being there, as he was, on the devil's business? He was a true believer, otherwise this would have been nothing to him. He had audacity for most things, but he had not audacity to make a plaything of the Lord's word. All this the signora understood, and felt much interest as she saw her cockchafer whirl round upon her pin.\n\n\"Your wit delights in such arguments,\" said he, \"but your heart and your reason do not go along with them.\"\n\n\"My heart!\" said she; \"you quite mistake the principles of my composition if you imagine that there is such a thing about me.\" After all, there was very little that was false in anything that the signora said. If Mr. Slope allowed himself to be deceived, it was his own fault. Nothing could have been more open than her declarations about herself.\n\nThe little writing-table with her desk was still standing before her, a barrier, as it were, against the enemy. She was sitting as nearly upright as she ever did, and he had brought a chair close to the sofa, so that there was only the corner of the table between him and her. It so happened that as she spoke her hand lay upon the table, and as Mr. Slope answered her he put his hand upon hers.\n\n\"No heart!\" said he. \"That is a heavy charge which you bring against yourself, and one of which I cannot find you guilty--\"\n\nShe withdrew her hand, not quickly and angrily, as though insulted by his touch, but gently and slowly.\n\n\"You are in no condition to give a verdict on the matter,\" said she, \"as you have not tried me. No, don't say that you intend doing so, for you know you have no intention of the kind; nor indeed have I, either. As for you, you will take your vows where they will result in something more substantial than the pursuit of such a ghostlike, ghastly love as mine--\"\n\n\"Your love should be sufficient to satisfy the dream of a monarch,\" said Mr. Slope, not quite clear as to the meaning of his words.\n\n\"Say an archbishop, Mr. Slope,\" said she. Poor fellow! She was very cruel to him. He went round again upon his cork on this allusion to his profession. He tried, however, to smile and gently accused her of joking on a matter, which was, he said, to him of such vital moment.\n\n\"Why--what gulls do you men make of us,\" she replied. \"How you fool us to the top of our bent; and of all men you clergymen are the most fluent of your honeyed, caressing words. Now look me in the face, Mr. Slope, boldly and openly.\"\n\nMr. Slope did look at her with a languishing loving eye, and as he did so he again put forth his hand to get hold of hers.\n\n\"I told you to look at me boldly, Mr. Slope, but confine your boldness to your eyes.\"\n\n\"Oh, Madeline!\" he sighed.\n\n\"Well, my name is Madeline,\" said she, \"but none except my own family usually call me so. Now look me in the face, Mr. Slope. Am I to understand that you say you love me?\"\n\nMr. Slope never had said so. If he had come there with any formed plan at all, his intention was to make love to the lady without uttering any such declaration. It was, however, quite impossible that he should now deny his love. He had, therefore, nothing for it but to go down on his knees distractedly against the sofa and swear that he did love her with a love passing the love of man.\n\nThe signora received the assurance with very little palpitation or appearance of surprise. \"And now answer me another question,\" said she. \"When are you to be married to my dear friend Eleanor Bold?\"\n\nPoor Mr. Slope went round and round in mortal agony. In such a condition as his it was really very hard for him to know what answer to give. And yet no answer would be his surest condemnation. He might as well at once plead guilty to the charge brought against him.\n\n\"And why do you accuse me of such dissimulation?\" said he.\n\n\"Dissimulation! I said nothing of dissimulation. I made no charge against you, and make none. Pray don't defend yourself to me. You swear that you are devoted to my beauty, and yet you are on the eve of matrimony with another. I feel this to be rather a compliment. It is to Mrs. Bold that you must defend yourself. That you may find difficult; unless, indeed, you can keep her in the dark. You clergymen are cleverer than other men.\"\n\n\"Signora, I have told you that I loved you, and now you rail at me.\"\n\n\"Rail at you. God bless the man; what would he have? Come, answer me this at your leisure--not without thinking now, but leisurely and with consideration--are you not going to be married to Mrs. Bold?\"\n\n\"I am not,\" said he. And as he said it he almost hated, with an exquisite hatred, the woman whom he could not help loving with an exquisite love.\n\n\"But surely you are a worshipper of hers?\"\n\n\"I am not,\" said Mr. Slope, to whom the word worshipper was peculiarly distasteful. The signora had conceived that it would be so.\n\n\"I wonder at that,\" said she. \"Do you not admire her? To my eye she is the perfection of English beauty. And then she is rich, too. I should have thought she was just the person to attract you. Come, Mr. Slope, let me give you advice on this matter. Marry the charming widow; she will be a good mother to your children and an excellent mistress of a clergyman's household.\"\n\n\"Oh, signora, how can you be so cruel?\"\n\n\"Cruel,\" said she, changing the voice of banter which she had been using for one which was expressively earnest in its tone; \"is that cruelty?\"\n\n\"How can I love another while my heart is entirely your own?\"\n\n\"If that were cruelty, Mr. Slope, what might you say of me if I were to declare that I returned your passion? What would you think if I bound you even by a lover's oath to do daily penance at this couch of mine? What can I give in return for a man's love? Ah, dear friend, you have not realized the conditions of my fate.\"\n\nMr. Slope was not on his knees all this time. After his declaration of love, he had risen from them as quickly as he thought consistent with the new position which he now filled, and as he stood was leaning on the back of his chair. This outburst of tenderness on the signora's part quite overcame him and made him feel for the moment that he could sacrifice everything to be assured of the love of the beautiful creature before him, maimed, lame, and already married as she was.\n\n\"And can I not sympathize with your lot?\" said he, now seating himself on her sofa and pushing away the table with his foot.\n\n\"Sympathy is so near to pity!\" said she. \"If you pity me, cripple as I am, I shall spurn you from me.\"\n\n\"Oh, Madeline, I will only love you,\" and again he caught her hand and devoured it with kisses. Now she did not draw it from him, but sat there as he kissed it, looking at him with her great eyes, just as a great spider would look at a great fly that was quite securely caught.\n\n\"Suppose Signor Neroni were to come to Barchester,\" said she. \"Would you make his acquaintance?\"\n\n\"Signor Neroni!\" said he.\n\n\"Would you introduce him to the bishop, and Mrs. Proudie, and the young ladies?\" said she, again having recourse to that horrid quizzing voice which Mr. Slope so particularly hated.\n\n\"Why do you ask such a question?\" said he.\n\n\"Because it is necessary that you should know that there is a Signor Neroni. I think you had forgotten it.\"\n\n\"If I thought that you retained for that wretch one particle of the love of which he was never worthy, I would die before I would distract you by telling you what I feel. No! Were your husband the master of your heart, I might perhaps love you, but you should never know it.\"\n\n\"My heart again! How you talk. And you consider then that if a husband be not master of his wife's heart, he has no right to her fealty; if a wife ceases to love, she may cease to be true. Is that your doctrine on this matter, as a minister of the Church of England?\"\n\nMr. Slope tried hard within himself to cast off the pollution with which he felt that he was defiling his soul. He strove to tear himself away from the noxious siren that had bewitched him. But he could not do it. He could not be again heart free. He had looked for rapturous joy in loving this lovely creature, and he already found that he met with little but disappointment and self-rebuke. He had come across the fruit of the Dead Sea, so sweet and delicious to the eye, so bitter and nauseous to the taste. He had put the apple to his mouth, and it had turned to ashes between his teeth. Yet he could not tear himself away. He knew, he could not but know, that she jeered at him, ridiculed his love, and insulted the weakness of his religion. But she half-permitted his adoration, and that half-permission added such fuel to his fire that all the fountain of his piety could not quench it. He began to feel savage, irritated, and revengeful. He meditated some severity of speech, some taunt that should cut her, as her taunts cut him. He reflected as he stood there for a moment, silent before her, that if he desired to quell her proud spirit, he should do so by being prouder even than herself; that if he wished to have her at his feet suppliant for his love, it behoved him to conquer her by indifference. All this passed through his mind. As far as dead knowledge went, he knew, or thought he knew, how a woman should be tamed. But when he essayed to bring his tactics to bear, he failed like a child. What chance has dead knowledge with experience in any of the transactions between man and man? What possible chance between man and woman? Mr. Slope loved furiously, insanely and truly, but he had never played the game of love. The signora did not love at all, but she was up to every move of the board. It was Philidor pitted against a schoolboy.\n\nAnd so she continued to insult him, and he continued to bear it.\n\n\"Sacrifice the world for love!\" she said in answer to some renewed vapid declaration of his passion. \"How often has the same thing been said, and how invariably with the same falsehood!\"\n\n\"Falsehood,\" said he. \"Do you say that I am false to you? Do you say that my love is not real?\"\n\n\"False? Of course it is false, false as the father of falsehood--if indeed falsehoods need a sire and are not self-begotten since the world began. You are ready to sacrifice the world for love? Come let us see what you will sacrifice. I care nothing for nuptial vows. The wretch, I think you were kind enough to call him so, whom I swore to love and obey is so base that he can only be thought of with repulsive disgust. In the council chamber of my heart I have divorced him. To me that is as good as though aged lords had gloated for months over the details of his licentious life. I care nothing for what the world can say. Will you be as frank? Will you take me to your home as your wife? Will you call me Mrs. Slope before bishop, dean, and prebendaries?\" The poor tortured wretch stood silent, not knowing what to say. \"What! You won't do that. Tell me, then, what part of the world is it that you will sacrifice for my charms?\"\n\n\"Were you free to marry, I would take you to my house to-morrow and wish no higher privilege.\"\n\n\"I am free,\" said she, almost starting up in her energy. For though there was no truth in her pretended regard for her clerical admirer, there was a mixture of real feeling in the scorn and satire with which she spoke of love and marriage generally. \"I am free--free as the winds. Come, will you take me as I am? Have your wish; sacrifice the world, and prove yourself a true man.\"\n\nMr. Slope should have taken her at her word. She would have drawn back, and he would have had the full advantage of the offer. But he did not. Instead of doing so, he stood wrapt in astonishment, passing his fingers through his lank red hair and thinking, as he stared upon her animated countenance, that her wondrous beauty grew more wonderful as he gazed on it. \"Ha! ha! ha!\" she laughed out loud. \"Come, Mr. Slope, don't talk of sacrificing the world again. People beyond one-and-twenty should never dream of such a thing. You and I, if we have the dregs of any love left in us, if we have the remnants of a passion remaining in our hearts, should husband our resources better. We are not in our première jeunesse. The world is a very nice place. Your world, at any rate, is so. You have all manner of fat rectories to get and possible bishoprics to enjoy. Come, confess; on second thoughts you would not sacrifice such things for the smiles of a lame lady?\"\n\nIt was impossible for him to answer this. In order to be in any way dignified, he felt that he must be silent.\n\n\"Come,\" said she, \"don't boody with me: don't be angry because I speak out some home truths. Alas, the world, as I have found it, has taught me bitter truths. Come, tell me that I am forgiven. Are we not to be friends?\" and she again put out her hand to him.\n\nHe sat himself down in the chair beside her, took her proffered hand, and leant over her.\n\n\"There,\" said she with her sweetest, softest smile--a smile to withstand which a man should be cased in triple steel, \"there; seal your forgiveness on it,\" and she raised it towards his face. He kissed it again and again, and stretched over her as though desirous of extending the charity of his pardon beyond the hand that was offered to him. She managed, however, to check his ardour. For one so easily allured as this poor chaplain, her hand was surely enough.\n\n\"Oh, Madeline!\" said he, \"tell me that you love me--do you--do you love me?\"\n\n\"Hush,\" said she. \"There is my mother's step. Our tête-à-tête has been of monstrous length. Now you had better go. But we shall see you soon again, shall we not?\"\n\nMr. Slope promised that he would call again on the following day.\n\n\"And, Mr. Slope,\" she continued, \"pray answer my note. You have it in your hand, though I declare during these two hours you have not been gracious enough to read it. It is about the Sabbath-school and the children. You know how anxious I am to have them here. I have been learning the catechism myself, on purpose. You must manage it for me next week. I will teach them, at any rate, to submit themselves to their spiritual pastors and masters.\"\n\nMr. Slope said but little on the subject of Sabbath-schools, but he made his adieu, and betook himself home with a sad heart, troubled mind, and uneasy conscience.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII. Mrs. Bold is Entertained by Dr. and Mrs. Grantly at Plumstead\n\nIt will be remembered that Mr. Slope, when leaving his billet-doux at the house of Mrs. Bold, had been informed that it would be sent out to her at Plumstead that afternoon. The archdeacon and Mr. Harding had in fact come into town together in the brougham, and it had been arranged that they should call for Eleanor's parcels as they left on their way home. Accordingly they did so call, and the maid, as she handed to the coachman a small basket and large bundle carefully and neatly packed, gave in at the carriage window Mr. Slope's epistle. The archdeacon, who was sitting next to the window, took it and immediately recognized the hand-writing of his enemy.\n\n\"Who left this?\" said he.\n\n\"Mr. Slope called with it himself, your Reverence,\" said the girl, \"and was very anxious that Missus should have it to-day.\"\n\nSo the brougham drove off, and the letter was left in the archdeacon's hand. He looked at it as though he held a basket of adders. He could not have thought worse of the document had he read it and discovered it to be licentious and atheistical. He did, moreover, what so many wise people are accustomed to do in similar circumstances; he immediately condemned the person to whom the letter was written, as though she were necessarily a particeps criminis.\n\nPoor Mr. Harding, though by no means inclined to forward Mr. Slope's intimacy with his daughter, would have given anything to have kept the letter from his son-in-law. But that was now impossible. There it was in his hand, and he looked as thoroughly disgusted as though he were quite sure that it contained all the rhapsodies of a favoured lover.\n\n\"It's very hard on me,\" said he after awhile, \"that this should go on under my roof.\"\n\nNow here the archdeacon was certainly most unreasonable. Having invited his sister-in-law to his house, it was a natural consequence that she should receive her letters there. And if Mr. Slope chose to write to her, his letter would, as a matter of course, be sent after her. Moreover, the very fact of an invitation to one's house implies confidence on the part of the inviter. He had shown that he thought Mrs. Bold to be a fit person to stay with him by his asking her to do so, and it was most cruel to her that he should complain of her violating the sanctity of his roof-tree, when the laches committed were none of her committing.\n\nMr. Harding felt this, and felt also that when the archdeacon talked thus about his roof, what he said was most offensive to himself as Eleanor's father. If Eleanor did receive a letter from Mr. Slope, what was there in that to pollute the purity of Dr. Grantly's household? He was indignant that his daughter should be so judged and so spoken of, and he made up his mind that even as Mrs. Slope she must be dearer to him than any other creature on God's earth. He almost broke out and said as much, but for the moment he restrained himself.\n\n\"Here,\" said the archdeacon, handing the offensive missile to his father-in-law, \"I am not going to be the bearer of his love-letters. You are her father and may do as you think fit with it.\"\n\nBy doing as he thought fit with it, the archdeacon certainly meant that Mr. Harding would be justified in opening and reading the letter, and taking any steps which might in consequence be necessary. To tell the truth, Dr. Grantly did feel rather a stronger curiosity than was justified by his outraged virtue to see the contents of the letter. Of course he could not open it himself, but he wished to make Mr. Harding understand that he, as Eleanor's father, would be fully justified in doing so. The idea of such a proceeding never occurred to Mr. Harding. His authority over Eleanor ceased when she became the wife of John Bold. He had not the slightest wish to pry into her correspondence. He consequently put the letter into his pocket, and only wished that he had been able to do so without the archdeacon's knowledge. They both sat silent during half the journey home, and then Dr. Grantly said, \"Perhaps Susan had better give it to her. She can explain to her sister better than either you or I can do how deep is the disgrace of such an acquaintance.\"\n\n\"I think you are very hard upon Eleanor,\" replied Mr. Harding. \"I will not allow that she has disgraced herself, nor do I think it likely that she will do so. She has a right to correspond with whom she pleases, and I shall not take upon myself to blame her because she gets a letter from Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"I suppose,\" said Dr. Grantly, \"you don't wish her to marry the man. I suppose you'll admit that she would disgrace herself if she did do so.\"\n\n\"I do not wish her to marry him,\" said the perplexed father. \"I do not like him, and do not think he would make a good husband. But if Eleanor chooses to do so, I shall certainly not think that she disgraces herself.\"\n\n\"Good heavens!\" exclaimed Dr. Grantly and threw himself back into the corner of his brougham. Mr. Harding said nothing more, but commenced playing a dirge with an imaginary fiddle bow upon an imaginary violoncello, for which there did not appear to be quite room enough in the carriage; he continued the tune, with sundry variations, till he arrived at the rectory door.\n\nThe archdeacon had been meditating sad things in his mind. Hitherto he had always looked on his father-in-law as a true partisan, though he knew him to be a man devoid of all the combative qualifications for that character. He had felt no fear that Mr. Harding would go over to the enemy, though he had never counted much on the ex-warden's prowess in breaking the hostile ranks. Now, however, it seemed that Eleanor, with her wiles, had completely trepanned and bewildered her father, cheated him out of his judgement, robbed him of the predilections and tastes of his life, and caused him to be tolerant of a man whose arrogance and vulgarity would, a few years since, have been unendurable to him. That the whole thing was as good as arranged between Eleanor and Mr. Slope there was no longer any room to doubt. That Mr. Harding knew that such was the case, even this could hardly be doubted. It was too manifest that he at any rate suspected it and was prepared to sanction it.\n\nAnd to tell the truth, such was the case. Mr. Harding disliked Mr. Slope as much as it was in his nature to dislike any man. Had his daughter wished to do her worst to displease him by a second marriage, she could hardly have succeeded better than by marrying Mr. Slope. But, as he said to himself now very often, what right had he to condemn her if she did nothing that was really wrong? If she liked Mr. Slope, it was her affair. It was indeed miraculous to him that a woman with such a mind, so educated, so refined, so nice in her tastes, should like such a man. Then he asked himself whether it was possible that she did so.\n\nAh, thou weak man; most charitable, most Christian, but weakest of men! Why couldn't thou not have asked herself? Was she not the daughter of thy loins, the child of thy heart, the best beloved to thee of all humanity? Had she not proved to thee, by years of closest affection, her truth and goodness and filial obedience? And yet, knowing and feeling all this, thou couldst endure to go groping in darkness, hearing her named in strains which wounded thy loving heart, and being unable to defend her as thou shouldst have done!\n\nMr. Harding had not believed, did not believe, that his daughter meant to marry this man, but he feared to commit himself to such an opinion. If she did do it there would be then no means of retreat. The wishes of his heart were: first, that there should be no truth in the archdeacon's surmises; and in this wish he would have fain trusted entirely, had he dared so to do; secondly, that the match might be prevented, if unfortunately, it had been contemplated by Eleanor; thirdly, that should she be so infatuated as to marry this man, he might justify his conduct and declare that no cause existed for his separating himself from her.\n\nHe wanted to believe her incapable of such a marriage; he wanted to show that he so believed of her; but he wanted also to be able to say hereafter that she had done nothing amiss, if she should unfortunately prove herself to be different from what he thought her to be.\n\nNothing but affection could justify such fickleness, but affection did justify it. There was but little of the Roman about Mr. Harding. He could not sacrifice his Lucretia even though she should be polluted by the accepted addresses of the clerical Tarquin at the palace. If Tarquin could be prevented, well and good, but if not, the father would still open his heart to his daughter and accept her as she presented herself, Tarquin and all.\n\nDr. Grantly's mind was of a stronger calibre, and he was by no means deficient in heart. He loved with an honest genuine love his wife and children and friends. He loved his father-in-law, and was quite prepared to love Eleanor too, if she would be one of his party, if she would be on his side, if she would regard the Slopes and the Proudies as the enemies of mankind and acknowledge and feel the comfortable merits of the Gwynnes and Arabins. He wished to be what he called \"safe\" with all those whom he had admitted to the penetralia of his house and heart. He could luxuriate in no society that was deficient in a certain feeling of faithful, staunch High Churchism, which to him was tantamount to freemasonry. He was not strict in his lines of definition. He endured without impatience many different shades of Anglo-church conservatism; but with the Slopes and Proudies he could not go on all fours.\n\nHe was wanting in, moreover, or perhaps it would be more correct to say, he was not troubled by that womanly tenderness which was so peculiar to Mr. Harding. His feelings towards his friends were that while they stuck to him, he would stick to them; that he would work with them shoulder and shoulder; that he would be faithful to the faithful. He knew nothing of that beautiful love which can be true to a false friend.\n\nAnd thus these two men, each miserable enough in his own way, returned to Plumstead.\n\nIt was getting late when they arrived there, and the ladies had already gone up to dress. Nothing more was said as the two parted in the hall. As Mr. Harding passed to his own room he knocked at Eleanor's door and handed in the letter. The archdeacon hurried to his own territory, there to unburden his heart to his faithful partner.\n\nWhat colloquy took place between the marital chamber and the adjoining dressing-room shall not be detailed. The reader, now intimate with the persons concerned, can well imagine it. The whole tenor of it also might be read in Mrs. Grantly's brow as she came down to dinner.\n\nEleanor, when she received the letter from her father's hand, had no idea from whom it came. She had never seen Mr. Slope's handwriting, or if so had forgotten it, and did not think of him as she twisted the letter as people do twist letters when they do not immediately recognize their correspondents either by the writing or the seal. She was sitting at her glass, brushing her hair and rising every other minute to play with her boy, who was sprawling on the bed and who engaged pretty nearly the whole attention of the maid as well as of his mother.\n\nAt last, sitting before her toilet-table, she broke the seal and, turning over the leaf, saw Mr. Slope's name. She first felt surprised, and then annoyed, and then anxious. As she read it she became interested. She was so delighted to find that all obstacles to her father's return to the hospital were apparently removed that she did not observe the fulsome language in which the tidings were conveyed. She merely perceived that she was commissioned to tell her father that such was the case, and she did not realize the fact that such a communication should not have been made, in the first instance, to her by an unmarried young clergyman. She felt, on the whole, grateful to Mr. Slope and anxious to get on her dress that she might run with the news to her father. Then she came to the allusion to her own pious labours, and she said in her heart that Mr. Slope was an affected ass. Then she went on again and was offended by her boy being called Mr. Slope's darling--he was nobody's darling but her own, or at any rate not the darling of a disagreeable stranger like Mr. Slope. Lastly she arrived at the tresses and felt a qualm of disgust. She looked up in the glass, and there they were before her, long and silken, certainly, and very beautiful. I will not say but that she knew them to be so, but she felt angry with them and brushed them roughly and carelessly. She crumpled the letter up with angry violence, and resolved, almost without thinking of it, that she would not show it to her father. She would merely tell him the contents of it. She then comforted herself again with her boy, had her dress fastened, and went down to dinner.\n\nAs she tripped down the stairs she began to ascertain that there was some difficulty in her situation. She could not keep from her father the news about the hospital, nor could she comfortably confess the letter from Mr. Slope before the Grantlys. Her father had already gone down. She had heard his step upon the lobby. She resolved therefore to take him aside and tell him her little bit of news. Poor girl! She had no idea how severely the unfortunate letter had already been discussed.\n\nWhen she entered the drawing-room, the whole party were there, including Mr. Arabin, and the whole party looked glum and sour. The two girls sat silent and apart as though they were aware that something was wrong. Even Mr. Arabin was solemn and silent. Eleanor had not seen him since breakfast. He had been the whole day at St. Ewold's, and such having been the case, it was natural that he should tell how matters were going on there. He did nothing of the kind, however, but remained solemn and silent. They were all solemn and silent. Eleanor knew in her heart that they had been talking about her, and her heart misgave her as she thought of Mr. Slope and his letter. At any rate she felt it to be quite impossible to speak to her father alone while matters were in this state.\n\nDinner was soon announced, and Dr. Grantly, as was his wont, gave Eleanor his arm. But he did so as though the doing it were an outrage on his feelings rendered necessary by sternest necessity. With quick sympathy Eleanor felt this, and hardly put her fingers on his coat-sleeve. It may be guessed in what way the dinner-hour was passed. Dr. Grantly said a few words to Mr. Arabin, Mr. Arabin said a few words to Mrs. Grantly, she said a few words to her father, and he tried to say a few words to Eleanor. She felt that she had been tried and found guilty of something, though she knew not what. She longed to say out to them all, \"Well, what is it that I have done; out with it, and let me know my crime; for heaven's sake let me hear the worst of it;\" but she could not. She could say nothing, but sat there silent, half-feeling that she was guilty, and trying in vain to pretend even to eat her dinner.\n\nAt last the cloth was drawn, and the ladies were not long following it. When they were gone, the gentlemen were somewhat more sociable but not much so. They could not of course talk over Eleanor's sins. The archdeacon had indeed so far betrayed his sister-in-law as to whisper into Mr. Arabin's ear in the study, as they met there before dinner, a hint of what he feared. He did so with the gravest and saddest of fears, and Mr. Arabin became grave and apparently sad enough as he heard it. He opened his eyes, and his mouth and said in a sort of whisper \"Mr. Slope!\" in the same way as he might have said \"The Cholera!\" had his friend told him that that horrid disease was in his nursery. \"I fear so, I fear so,\" said the archdeacon, and then together they left the room.\n\nWe will not accurately analyse Mr. Arabin's feelings on receipt of such astounding tidings. It will suffice to say that he was surprised, vexed, sorrowful, and ill at ease. He had not perhaps thought very much about Eleanor, but he had appreciated her influence, and had felt that close intimacy with her in a country-house was pleasant to him, and also beneficial. He had spoken highly of her intelligence to the archdeacon, and had walked about the shrubberies with her, carrying her boy on his back. When Mr. Arabin had called Johnny his darling, Eleanor was not angry.\n\nThus the three men sat over their wine, all thinking of the same subject, but unable to speak of it to each other. So we will leave them and follow the ladies into the drawing-room.\n\nMrs. Grantly had received a commission from her husband, and had undertaken it with some unwillingness. He had desired her to speak gravely to Eleanor and to tell her that, if she persisted in her adherence to Mr. Slope, she could no longer look for the countenance of her present friends. Mrs. Grantly probably knew her sister better than the doctor did, and assured him that it would be in vain to talk to her. The only course likely to be of any service in her opinion was to keep Eleanor away from Barchester. Perhaps she might have added, for she had a very keen eye in such things, that there might also be ground for hope in keeping Eleanor near Mr. Arabin. Of this, however, she said nothing. But the archdeacon would not be talked over; he spoke much of his conscience, and declared that, if Mrs. Grantly would not do it, he would. So instigated, the lady undertook the task, stating, however, her full conviction that her interference would be worse than useless. And so it proved.\n\nAs soon as they were in the drawing-room Mrs. Grantly found some excuse for sending her girls away, and then began her task. She knew well that she could exercise but very slight authority over her sister. Their various modes of life, and the distance between their residences, had prevented any very close confidence. They had hardly lived together since Eleanor was a child. Eleanor had, moreover, especially in latter years, resented in a quiet sort of way the dictatorial authority which the archdeacon seemed to exercise over her father, and on this account had been unwilling to allow the archdeacon's wife to exercise authority over herself.\n\n\"You got a note just before dinner, I believe,\" began the eldest sister.\n\nEleanor acknowledged that she had done so, and felt that she turned red as she acknowledged it. She would have given anything to have kept her colour, but the more she tried to do so the more signally she failed.\n\n\"Was it not from Mr. Slope?\"\n\nEleanor said that the letter was from Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Is he a regular correspondent of yours, Eleanor?\"\n\n\"Not exactly,\" said she, already beginning to feel angry at the cross-examination. She determined, and why it would be difficult to say, that nothing should induce her to tell her sister Susan what was the subject of the letter. Mrs. Grantly, she knew, was instigated by the archdeacon, and she would not plead to any arraignment made against her by him.\n\n\"But, Eleanor dear, why do you get letters from Mr. Slope at all, knowing, as you do, he is a person so distasteful to Papa, and to the archdeacon, and indeed to all your friends?\"\n\n\"In the first place, Susan, I don't get letters from him; and in the next place, as Mr. Slope wrote the one letter which I have got, and as I only received it, which I could not very well help doing, as Papa handed it to me, I think you had better ask Mr. Slope instead of me.\"\n\n\"What was his letter about, Eleanor?\"\n\n\"I cannot tell you,\" said she, \"because it was confidential. It was on business respecting a third person.\"\n\n\"It was in no way personal to yourself then?\"\n\n\"I won't exactly say that, Susan,\" said she, getting more and more angry at her sister's questions.\n\n\"Well, I must say it's rather singular,\" said Mrs. Grantly, affecting to laugh, \"that a young lady in your position should receive a letter from an unmarried gentleman of which she will not tell the contents and which she is ashamed to show to her sister.\"\n\n\"I am not ashamed,\" said Eleanor, blazing up. \"I am not ashamed of anything in the matter; only I do not choose to be cross-examined as to my letters by anyone.\"\n\n\"Well, dear,\" said the other, \"I cannot but tell you that I do not think Mr. Slope a proper correspondent for you.\"\n\n\"If he be ever so improper, how can I help his having written to me? But you are all prejudiced against him to such an extent that that which would be kind and generous in another man is odious and impudent in him. I hate a religion that teaches one to be so one-sided in one's charity.\"\n\n\"I am sorry, Eleanor, that you hate the religion you find here, but surely you should remember that in such matters the archdeacon must know more of the world than you do. I don't ask you to respect or comply with me, although I am, unfortunately, so many years your senior; but surely, in such a matter as this, you might consent to be guided by the archdeacon. He is most anxious to be your friend, if you will let him.\"\n\n\"In such a matter as what?\" said Eleanor very testily. \"Upon my word I don't know what this is all about.\"\n\n\"We all want you to drop Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"You all want me to be as illiberal as yourselves. That I shall never be. I see no harm in Mr. Slope's acquaintance, and I shall not insult the man by telling him that I do. He has thought it necessary to write to me, and I do not want the archdeacon's advice about the letter. If I did, I would ask it.\"\n\n\"Then, Eleanor, it is my duty to tell you,\" and now she spoke with a tremendous gravity, \"that the archdeacon thinks that such a correspondence is disgraceful, and that he cannot allow it to go on in his house.\"\n\nEleanor's eyes flashed fire as she answered her sister, jumping up from her seat as she did so. \"You may tell the archdeacon that wherever I am I shall receive what letters I please and from whom I please. And as for the word 'disgraceful,' if Dr. Grantly has used it of me, he has been unmanly and inhospitable,\" and she walked off to the door. \"When Papa comes from the dining-room I will thank you to ask him to step up to my bedroom. I will show him Mr. Slope's letter, but I will show it to no one else.\" And so saying, she retreated to her baby.\n\nShe had no conception of the crime with which she was charged. The idea that she could be thought by her friends to regard Mr. Slope as a lover had never flashed upon her. She conceived that they were all prejudiced and illiberal in their persecution of him, and therefore she would not join in the persecution, even though she greatly disliked the man.\n\nEleanor was very angry as she seated herself in a low chair by her open window at the foot of her child's bed. \"To dare to say I have disgraced myself,\" she repeated to herself more than once. \"How Papa can put up with that man's arrogance! I will certainly not sit down to dinner in his house again unless he begs my pardon for that word.\" And then a thought struck her that Mr. Arabin might perchance hear of her \"disgraceful\" correspondence with Mr. Slope, and she turned crimson with pure vexation. Oh, if she had known the truth! If she could have conceived that Mr. Arabin had been informed as a fact that she was going to marry Mr. Slope!\n\nShe had not been long in her room before her father joined her. As he left the drawing-room Mrs. Grantly took her husband into the recess of the window and told him how signally she had failed.\n\n\"I will speak to her myself before I go to bed,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Pray do no such thing,\" said she; \"you can do no good and will only make an unseemly quarrel in the house. You have no idea how headstrong she can be.\"\n\nThe archdeacon declared that as to that he was quite indifferent. He knew his duty and would do it. Mr. Harding was weak in the extreme in such matters. He would not have it hereafter on his conscience that he had not done all that in him lay to prevent so disgraceful an alliance. It was in vain that Mrs. Grantly assured him that speaking to Eleanor angrily would only hasten such a crisis and render it certain, if at present there were any doubt. He was angry, self-willed, and sore. The fact that a lady of his household had received a letter from Mr. Slope had wounded his pride in the sorest place, and nothing could control him.\n\nMr. Harding looked worn and woe-begone as he entered his daughter's room. These sorrows worried him sadly. He felt that if they were continued, he must go to the wall in the manner so kindly prophesied to him by the chaplain. He knocked gently at his daughter's door, waited till he was distinctly bade to enter, and then appeared as though he and not she were the suspected criminal.\n\nEleanor's arm was soon within his, and she had soon kissed his forehead and caressed him, not with joyous but with eager love. \"Oh, Papa,\" she said, \"I do so want to speak to you. They have been talking about me downstairs to-night--don't you know they have, Papa?\"\n\nMr. Harding confessed with a sort of murmur that the archdeacon had been speaking of her.\n\n\"I shall hate Dr. Grantly soon--\"\n\n\"Oh, my dear!\"\n\n\"Well, I shall. I cannot help it. He is so uncharitable, so unkind, so suspicious of everyone that does not worship himself: and then he is so monstrously arrogant to other people who have a right to their opinions as well as he has to his own.\"\n\n\"He is an earnest, eager man, my dear, but he never means to be unkind.\"\n\n\"He is unkind, Papa, most unkind. There, I got that letter from Mr. Slope before dinner. It was you yourself who gave it to me. There, pray read it. It is all for you. It should have been addressed to you. You know how they have been talking about it downstairs. You know how they behaved to me at dinner. And since dinner Susan has been preaching to me, till I could not remain in the room with her. Read it, Papa, and then say whether that is a letter that need make Dr. Grantly so outrageous.\"\n\nMr. Harding took his arm from his daughter's waist and slowly read the letter. She expected to see his countenance lit with joy as he learnt that his path back to the hospital was made so smooth; but she was doomed to disappointment, as had once been the case before on a somewhat similar occasion. His first feeling was one of unmitigated disgust that Mr. Slope should have chosen to interfere in his behalf. He had been anxious to get back to the hospital, but he would have infinitely sooner resigned all pretensions to the place than have owed it in any manner to Mr. Slope's influence in his favour. Then he thoroughly disliked the tone of Mr. Slope's letter; it was unctuous, false, and unwholesome, like the man. He saw, which Eleanor had failed to see, that much more had been intended than was expressed. The appeal to Eleanor's pious labours as separate from his own grated sadly against his feelings as a father. And then, when he came to the \"darling boy\" and the \"silken tresses,\" he slowly closed and folded the letter in despair. It was impossible that Mr. Slope should so write unless he had been encouraged. It was impossible Eleanor should have received such a letter, and have received it without annoyance, unless she were willing to encourage him. So at least Mr. Harding argued to himself.\n\nHow hard it is to judge accurately of the feelings of others. Mr. Harding, as he came to the close of the letter, in his heart condemned his daughter for indelicacy, and it made him miserable to do so. She was not responsible for what Mr. Slope might write. True. But then she expressed no disgust at it. She had rather expressed approval of the letter as a whole. She had given it to him to read, as a vindication for herself and also for him. The father's spirits sank within him as he felt that he could not acquit her.\n\nAnd yet it was the true feminine delicacy of Eleanor's mind which brought on her this condemnation. Listen to me, ladies, and I beseech you to acquit her. She thought of this man, this lover of whom she was so unconscious, exactly as her father did, exactly as the Grantlys did. At least she esteemed him personally as they did. But she believed him to be in the main an honest man, and one truly inclined to assist her father. She felt herself bound, after what had passed, to show this letter to Mr. Harding. She thought it necessary that he should know what Mr. Slope had to say. But she did not think it necessary to apologize for, or condemn, or even allude to the vulgarity of the man's tone, which arose, as does all vulgarity, from ignorance. It was nauseous to her to have a man like Mr. Slope commenting on her personal attractions, and she did not think it necessary to dilate with her father upon what was nauseous. She never supposed they could disagree on such a subject. It would have been painful for her to point it out, painful for her to speak strongly against a man of whom, on the whole, she was anxious to think and speak well. In encountering such a man she had encountered what was disagreeable, as she might do in walking the streets. But in such encounters she never thought it necessary to dwell on what disgusted her.\n\nAnd he, foolish, weak, loving man, would not say one word, though one word would have cleared up everything. There would have been a deluge of tears, and in ten minutes everyone in the house would have understood how matters really were. The father would have been delighted. The sister would have kissed her sister and begged a thousand pardons. The archdeacon would have apologized and wondered, and raised his eyebrows, and gone to bed a happy man. And Mr. Arabin--Mr. Arabin would have dreamt of Eleanor, have awoke in the morning with ideas of love, and retired to rest the next evening with schemes of marriage. But, alas, all this was not to be.\n\nMr. Harding slowly folded the letter, handed it back to her, kissed her forehead, and bade God bless her. He then crept slowly away to his own room.\n\nAs soon as he had left the passage, another knock was given at Eleanor's door, and Mrs. Grantly's very demure own maid, entering on tiptoe, wanted to know would Mrs. Bold be so kind as to speak to the archdeacon for two minutes in the archdeacon's study, if not disagreeable. The archdeacon's compliments, and he wouldn't detain her two minutes.\n\nEleanor thought it was very disagreeable; she was tired and fagged and sick at heart; her present feelings towards Dr. Grantly were anything but those of affection. She was, however, no coward, and therefore promised to be in the study in five minutes. So she arranged her hair, tied on her cap, and went down with a palpitating heart.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. A Serious Interview\n\nThere are people who delight in serious interviews, especially when to them appertains the part of offering advice or administering rebuke, and perhaps the archdeacon was one of these. Yet on this occasion he did not prepare himself for the coming conversation with much anticipation of pleasure. Whatever might be his faults he was not an inhospitable man, and he almost felt that he was sinning against hospitality in upbraiding Eleanor in his own house. Then, also, he was not quite sure that he would get the best of it. His wife had told him that he decidedly would not, and he usually gave credit to what his wife said. He was, however, so convinced of what he considered to be the impropriety of Eleanor's conduct, and so assured also of his own duty in trying to check it, that his conscience would not allow him to take his wife's advice and go to bed quietly.\n\nEleanor's face as she entered the room was not such as to reassure him. As a rule she was always mild in manner and gentle in conduct; but there was that in her eye which made it not an easy task to scold her. In truth she had been little used to scolding. No one since her childhood had tried it but the archdeacon, and he had generally failed when he did try it. He had never done so since her marriage; and now, when he saw her quiet, easy step as she entered his room, he almost wished that he had taken his wife's advice.\n\nHe began by apologizing for the trouble he was giving her. She begged him not to mention it, assured him that walking downstairs was no trouble to her at all, and then took a seat and waited patiently for him to begin his attack.\n\n\"My dear Eleanor,\" he said, \"I hope you believe me when I assure you that you have no sincerer friend than I am.\" To this Eleanor answered nothing, and therefore he proceeded. \"If you had a brother of your own, I should not probably trouble you with what I am going to say. But as it is I cannot but think that it must be a comfort to you to know that you have near you one who is as anxious for your welfare as any brother of your own could be.\"\n\n\"I never had a brother,\" said she.\n\n\"I know you never had, and it is therefore that I speak to you.\"\n\n\"I never had a brother,\" she repeated, \"but I have hardly felt the want. Papa has been to me both father and brother.\"\n\n\"Your father is the fondest and most affectionate of men. But--\"\n\n\"He is--the fondest and most affectionate of men, and the best of counsellors. While he lives I can never want advice.\"\n\nThis rather put the archdeacon out. He could not exactly contradict what his sister-in-law said about her father, and yet he did not at all agree with her. He wanted her to understand that he tendered his assistance because her father was a soft, good-natured gentleman not sufficiently knowing in the ways of the world; but he could not say this to her. So he had to rush into the subject-matter of his proffered counsel without any acknowledgement on her part that she could need it, or would be grateful for it.\n\n\"Susan tells me that you received a letter this evening from Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Yes; Papa brought it in the brougham. Did he not tell you?\"\n\n\"And Susan says that you objected to let her know what it was about.\"\n\n\"I don't think she asked me. But had she done so, I should not have told her. I don't think it nice to be asked about one's letters. If one wishes to show them, one does so without being asked.\"\n\n\"True. Quite so. What you say is quite true. But is not the fact of your receiving letters from Mr. Slope, which you do not wish to show to your friends, a circumstance which must excite some--some surprise--some suspicion--\"\n\n\"Suspicion!\" said she, not speaking above her usual voice, speaking still in a soft, womanly tone but yet with indignation. \"Suspicion! And who suspects me, and of what?\" And then there was a pause, for the archdeacon was not quite ready to explain the ground of his suspicion. \"No, Dr. Grantly, I did not choose to show Mr. Slope's letter to Susan. I could not show it to anyone till Papa had seen it. If you have any wish to read it now, you can do so,\" and she handed the letter to him over the table.\n\nThis was an amount of compliance which he had not at all expected, and which rather upset him in his tactics. However, he took the letter, perused it carefully, and then refolding it, kept it on the table under his hand. To him it appeared to be in almost every respect the letter of a declared lover; it seemed to corroborate his worst suspicions; and the fact of Eleanor's showing it to him was all but tantamount to a declaration on her part that it was her pleasure to receive love-letters from Mr. Slope. He almost entirely overlooked the real subject-matter of the epistle, so intent was he on the forthcoming courtship and marriage.\n\n\"I'll thank you to give it me back, if you please, Dr. Grantly.\"\n\nHe took it in his hand and held it up, but made no immediate overture to return it. \"And Mr. Harding has seen this?\" said he.\n\n\"Of course he has,\" said she; \"it was written that he might see it. It refers solely to his business--of course I showed it to him.\"\n\n\"And, Eleanor, do you think that that is a proper letter for you--for a person in your condition--to receive from Mr. Slope?\"\n\n\"Quite a proper letter,\" said she, speaking, perhaps, a little out of obstinacy, probably forgetting at the moment the objectionable mention of her silken curls.\n\n\"Then, Eleanor, it is my duty to tell you that I wholly differ from you.\"\n\n\"So I suppose,\" said she, instigated now by sheer opposition and determination not to succumb. \"You think Mr. Slope is a messenger direct from Satan. I think he is an industrious, well-meaning clergyman. It's a pity that we differ as we do. But, as we do differ, we had probably better not talk about it.\"\n\nHere Eleanor undoubtedly put herself in the wrong. She might probably have refused to talk to Dr. Grantly on the matter in dispute without any impropriety, but, having consented to listen to him, she had no business to tell him that he regarded Mr. Slope as an emissary from the evil one; nor was she justified in praising Mr. Slope, seeing that in her heart of hearts she did not think well of him. She was, however, wounded in spirit, and angry, and bitter. She had been subjected to contumely and cross-questioning and ill-usage through the whole evening. No one, not even Mr. Arabin, not even her father, had been kind to her. All this she attributed to the prejudice and conceit of the archdeacon, and therefore she resolved to set no bounds to her antagonism to him. She would neither give nor take quarter. He had greatly presumed in daring to question her about her correspondence, and she was determined to show that she thought so.\n\n\"Eleanor, you are forgetting yourself,\" said he, looking very sternly at her. \"Otherwise you would never tell me that I conceive any man to be a messenger from Satan.\"\n\n\"But you do,\" said she. \"Nothing is too bad for him. Give me that letter, if you please;\" and she stretched out her hand and took it from him. \"He has been doing his best to serve Papa, doing more than any of Papa's friends could do; and yet, because he is the chaplain of a bishop whom you don't like, you speak of him as though he had no right to the usage of a gentleman.\"\n\n\"He has done nothing for your father.\"\n\n\"I believe that he has done a great deal; and, as far as I am concerned, I am grateful to him. Nothing that you can say can prevent my being so. I judge people by their acts, and his, as far as I can see them, are good.\" She then paused for a moment. \"If you have nothing further to say, I shall be obliged by being permitted to say good night--I am very tired.\"\n\nDr. Grantly had, as he thought, done his best to be gracious to his sister-in-law. He had endeavoured not to be harsh to her, and had striven to pluck the sting from his rebuke. But he did not intend that she should leave him without hearing him.\n\n\"I have something to say, Eleanor, and I fear I must trouble you to hear it. You profess that it is quite proper that you should receive from Mr. Slope such letters as that you have in your hand. Susan and I think very differently. You are, of course, your own mistress, and much as we both must grieve should anything separate you from us, we have no power to prevent you from taking steps which may lead to such a separation. If you are so wilful as to reject the counsel of your friends, you must be allowed to cater for yourself. But, Eleanor, I may at any rate ask you this. Is it worth your while to break away from all those you have loved--from all who love you--for the sake of Mr. Slope?\"\n\n\"I don't know what you mean, Dr. Grantly; I don't know what you're talking about. I don't want to break away from anybody.\"\n\n\"But you will do so if you connect yourself with Mr. Slope. Eleanor, I must speak out to you. You must choose between your sister and myself and our friends, and Mr. Slope and his friends. I say nothing of your father, as you may probably understand his feelings better than I do.\"\n\n\"What do you mean, Dr. Grantly? What am I to understand? I never heard such wicked prejudice in my life.\"\n\n\"It is no prejudice, Eleanor. I have known the world longer than you have done. Mr. Slope is altogether beneath you. You ought to know and feel that he is so. Pray--pray think of this before it is too late.\"\n\n\"Too late!\"\n\n\"Or if you will not believe me, ask Susan; you cannot think she is prejudiced against you. Or even consult your father--he is not prejudiced against you. Ask Mr. Arabin--\"\n\n\"You haven't spoken to Mr. Arabin about this!\" said she, jumping up and standing before him.\n\n\"Eleanor, all the world in and about Barchester will be speaking of it soon.\"\n\n\"But have you spoken to Mr. Arabin about me and Mr. Slope?\"\n\n\"Certainly I have, and he quite agrees with me.\"\n\n\"Agrees with what?\" said she. \"I think you are trying to drive me mad.\"\n\n\"He agrees with me and Susan that it is quite impossible you should be received at Plumstead as Mrs. Slope.\"\n\nNot being favourites with the tragic muse, we do not dare to attempt any description of Eleanor's face when she first heard the name of Mrs. Slope pronounced as that which would or should or might at some time appertain to herself. The look, such as it was, Dr. Grantly did not soon forget. For a moment or two she could find no words to express her deep anger and deep disgust; indeed, at this conjuncture, words did not come to her very freely.\n\n\"How dare you be so impertinent?\" at last she said, and then she hurried out of the room without giving the archdeacon the opportunity of uttering another word. It was with difficulty she contained herself till she reached her own room; and then, locking the door, she threw herself on her bed and sobbed as though her heart would break.\n\nBut even yet she had no conception of the truth. She had no idea that her father and her sister had for days past conceived in sober earnest the idea that she was going to marry this man. She did not even then believe that the archdeacon thought that she would do so. By some manoeuvre of her brain she attributed the origin of the accusation to Mr. Arabin, and as she did so her anger against him was excessive, and the vexation of her spirit almost unendurable. She could not bring herself to think that the charge was made seriously. It appeared to her most probable that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin had talked over her objectionable acquaintance with Mr. Slope; that Mr. Arabin in his jeering, sarcastic way had suggested the odious match as being the severest way of treating with contumely her acquaintance with his enemy; and that the archdeacon, taking the idea from him, thought proper to punish her by the allusion. The whole night she lay awake thinking of what had been said, and this appeared to be the most probable solution.\n\nBut the reflexion that Mr. Arabin should have in any way mentioned her name in connexion with that of Mr. Slope was overpowering; and the spiteful ill-nature of the archdeacon in repeating the charge to her made her wish to leave his house almost before the day had broken. One thing was certain: nothing should make her stay there beyond the following morning, and nothing should make her sit down to breakfast in company with Dr. Grantly. When she thought of the man whose name had been linked with her own, she cried from sheer disgust. It was only because she would be thus disgusted, thus pained and shocked and cut to the quick, that the archdeacon had spoken the horrid word. He wanted to make her quarrel with Mr. Slope, and therefore he had outraged her by his abominable vulgarity. She determined that at any rate he should know that she appreciated it.\n\nNor was the archdeacon a bit better satisfied with the result of his serious interview than was Eleanor. He gathered from it, as indeed he could hardly fail to do, that she was very angry with him, but he thought that she was thus angry, not because she was suspected of an intention to marry Mr. Slope, but because such an intention was imputed to her as a crime. Dr. Grantly regarded this supposed union with disgust, but it never occurred to him that Eleanor was outraged because she looked at it exactly in the same light.\n\nHe returned to his wife, vexed and somewhat disconsolate, but nevertheless confirmed in his wrath against his sister-in-law. \"Her whole behaviour,\" said he, \"has been most objectionable. She handed me his love-letter to read as though she were proud of it. And she is proud of it. She is proud of having this slavering, greedy man at her feet. She will throw herself and John Bold's money into his lap; she will ruin her boy, disgrace her father and you, and be a wretched miserable woman.\"\n\nHis spouse, who was sitting at her toilet-table, continued her avocations, making no answer to all this. She had known that the archdeacon would gain nothing by interfering, but she was too charitable to provoke him by saying so while he was in such deep sorrow.\n\n\"This comes of a man making such a will as that of Bold's,\" he continued. \"Eleanor is no more fitted to be trusted with such an amount of money in her own hands than is a charity-school girl.\" Still Mrs. Grantly made no reply. \"But I have done my duty; I can do nothing further. I have told her plainly that she cannot be allowed to form a link of connexion between me and that man. From henceforward it will not be in my power to make her welcome at Plumstead. I cannot have Mr. Slope's love-letters coming here. Susan, I think you had better let her understand that, as her mind on this subject seems to be irrevocably fixed, it will be better for all parties that she should return to Barchester.\"\n\nNow Mrs. Grantly was angry with Eleanor--nearly as angry as her husband--but she had no idea of turning her sister out of the house. She therefore at length spoke out and explained to the archdeacon in her own mild, seducing way that he was fuming and fussing and fretting himself very unnecessarily. She declared that things, if left alone, would arrange themselves much better than he could arrange them, and at last succeeded in inducing him to go to bed in a somewhat less inhospitable state of mind.\n\nOn the following morning Eleanor's maid was commissioned to send word into the dining-room that her mistress was not well enough to attend prayers and that she would breakfast in her own room. Here she was visited by her father, and declared to him her intention of returning immediately to Barchester. He was hardly surprised by the announcement. All the household seemed to be aware that something had gone wrong. Everyone walked about with subdued feet, and people's shoes seemed to creak more than usual. There was a look of conscious intelligence on the faces of the women, and the men attempted, but in vain, to converse as though nothing were the matter. All this had weighed heavily on the heart of Mr. Harding, and when Eleanor told him that her immediate return to Barchester was a necessity, he merely sighed piteously and said that he would be ready to accompany her.\n\nBut here she objected strenuously. She had a great wish, she said, to go alone; a great desire that it might be seen that her father was not implicated in her quarrel with Dr. Grantly. To this at last he gave way; but not a word passed between them about Mr. Slope--not a word was said, not a question asked as to the serious interview on the preceding evening. There was, indeed, very little confidence between them, though neither of them knew why it should be so. Eleanor once asked him whether he would not call upon the bishop, but he answered rather tartly that he did not know--he did not think he should, but he could not say just at present. And so they parted. Each was miserably anxious for some show of affection, for some return of confidence, for some sign of the feeling that usually bound them together. But none was given. The father could not bring himself to question his daughter about her supposed lover, and the daughter would not sully her mouth by repeating the odious word with which Dr. Grantly had roused her wrath. And so they parted.\n\nThere was some trouble in arranging the method of Eleanor's return. She begged her father to send for a post-chaise, but when Mrs. Grantly heard of this, she objected strongly. If Eleanor would go away in dudgeon with the archdeacon, why should she let all the servants and all the neighbourhood know that she had done so? So at last Eleanor consented to make use of the Plumstead carriage, and as the archdeacon had gone out immediately after breakfast and was not to return till dinner-time, she also consented to postpone her journey till after lunch, and to join the family at that time. As to the subject of the quarrel not a word was said by anyone. The affair of the carriage was arranged by Mr. Harding, who acted as Mercury between the two ladies; they, when they met, kissed each other very lovingly and then sat down each to her crochet work as though nothing was amiss in all the world.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX. Another Love Scene\n\nBut there was another visitor at the rectory whose feelings in this unfortunate matter must be somewhat strictly analysed. Mr. Arabin had heard from his friend of the probability of Eleanor's marriage with Mr. Slope with amazement, but not with incredulity. It has been said that he was not in love with Eleanor, and up to this period this certainly had been true. But as soon as he heard that she loved someone else, he began to be very fond of her himself. He did not make up his mind that he wished to have her for his wife; he had never thought of her, and did not now think of her, in connexion with himself; but he experienced an inward, indefinable feeling of deep regret, a gnawing sorrow, an unconquerable depression of spirits, and also a species of self-abasement that he--he, Mr. Arabin--had not done something to prevent that other he, that vile he whom he so thoroughly despised, from carrying off this sweet prize.\n\nWhatever man may have reached the age of forty unmarried without knowing something of such feelings must have been very successful or else very cold-hearted.\n\nMr. Arabin had never thought of trimming the sails of his bark so that he might sail as convoy to this rich argosy. He had seen that Mrs. Bold was beautiful, but he had not dreamt of making her beauty his own. He knew that Mrs. Bold was rich, but he had had no more idea of appropriating her wealth than that of Dr. Grantly. He had discovered that Mrs. Bold was intelligent, warm-hearted, agreeable, sensible, all in fact that a man could wish his wife to be; but the higher were her attractions, the greater her claims to consideration, the less had he imagined that he might possibly become the possessor of them. Such had been his instinct rather than his thoughts, so humble and so diffident. Now his diffidence was to be rewarded by his seeing this woman, whose beauty was to his eyes perfect, whose wealth was such as to have deterred him from thinking of her, whose widowhood would have silenced him had he not been so deterred, by his seeing her become the prey of--Obadiah Slope!\n\nOn the morning of Mrs. Bold's departure he got on his horse to ride over to St. Ewold's. As he rode he kept muttering to himself a line from Van Artevelde,\n\nHow little flattering is woman's love.\n\nAnd then he strove to recall his mind and to think of other affairs--his parish, his college, his creed--but his thoughts would revert to Mr. Slope and the Flemish chieftain.\n\nWhen we think upon it, How little flattering is woman's love, Given commonly to whosoe'er is nearest And propped with most advantage.\n\nIt was not that Mrs. Bold should marry anyone but him--he had not put himself forward as a suitor--but that she should marry Mr. Slope; and so he repeated over again--\n\nOutward grace Nor inward light is needful--day by day Men wanting both are mated with the best And loftiest of God's feminine creation, Whose love takes no distinction but of gender, And ridicules the very name of choice.\n\nAnd so he went on, troubled much in his mind.\n\nHe had but an uneasy ride of it that morning, and little good did he do at St. Ewold's.\n\nThe necessary alterations in his house were being fast completed, and he walked through the rooms, and went up and down the stairs, and rambled through the garden, but he could not wake himself to much interest about them. He stood still at every window to look out and think upon Mr. Slope. At almost every window he had before stood and chatted with Eleanor. She and Mrs. Grantly had been there continually; and while Mrs. Grantly had been giving orders, and seeing that orders had been complied with, he and Eleanor had conversed on all things appertaining to a clergyman's profession. He thought how often he had laid down the law to her and how sweetly she had borne with his somewhat dictatorial decrees. He remembered her listening intelligence, her gentle but quick replies, her interest in all that concerned the church, in all that concerned him; and then he struck his riding-whip against the window-sill and declared to himself that it was impossible that Eleanor Bold should marry Mr. Slope.\n\nAnd yet he did not really believe, as he should have done, that it was impossible. He should have known her well enough to feel that it was truly impossible. He should have been aware that Eleanor had that within her which would surely protect her from such degradation. But he, like so many others, was deficient in confidence in woman. He said to himself over and over again that it was impossible that Eleanor Bold should become Mrs. Slope, and yet he believed that she would do so. And so he rambled about, and could do and think of nothing. He was thoroughly uncomfortable, thoroughly ill at ease, cross with himself and everybody else, and feeding in his heart on animosity towards Mr. Slope. This was not as it should be, as he knew and felt, but he could not help himself. In truth Mr. Arabin was now in love with Mrs. Bold, though ignorant of the fact himself. He was in love and, though forty years old, was in love without being aware of it. He fumed and fretted and did not know what was the matter, as a youth might do at one-and-twenty. And so having done no good at St. Ewold's, he rode back much earlier than was usual with him, instigated by some inward, unacknowledged hope that he might see Mrs. Bold before she left.\n\nEleanor had not passed a pleasant morning. She was irritated with everyone, and not least with herself. She felt that she had been hardly used, but she felt also that she had not played her own cards well. She should have held herself so far above suspicion as to have received her sister's innuendoes and the archdeacon's lecture with indifference. She had not done this, but had shown herself angry and sore, and was now ashamed of her own petulance, yet unable to discontinue it.\n\nThe greater part of the morning she had spent alone, but after awhile her father joined her. He had fully made up his mind that, come what come might, nothing should separate him from his younger daughter. It was a hard task for him to reconcile himself to the idea of seeing her at the head of Mr. Slope's table, but he got through it. Mr. Slope, as he argued to himself, was a respectable man and a clergyman, and he, as Eleanor's father, had no right even to endeavour to prevent her from marrying such a one. He longed to tell her how he had determined to prefer her to all the world, how he was prepared to admit that she was not wrong, how thoroughly he differed from Dr. Grantly; but he could not bring himself to mention Mr. Slope's name. There was yet a chance that they were all wrong in their surmise, and being thus in doubt, he could not bring himself to speak openly to her on the subject.\n\nHe was sitting with her in the drawing-room, with his arm round her waist, saying every now and then some little soft words of affection and working hard with his imaginary fiddle-bow, when Mr. Arabin entered the room. He immediately got up, and the two made some trite remarks to each other, neither thinking of what he was saying, while Eleanor kept her seat on the sofa, mute and moody. Mr. Arabin was included in the list of those against whom her anger was excited. He, too, had dared to talk about her acquaintance with Mr. Slope; he, too, had dared to blame her for not making an enemy of his enemy. She had not intended to see him before her departure, and was now but little inclined to be gracious.\n\nThere was a feeling through the whole house that something was wrong. Mr. Arabin, when he saw Eleanor, could not succeed in looking or in speaking as though he knew nothing of all this. He could not be cheerful and positive and contradictory with her, as was his wont. He had not been two minutes in the room before he felt that he had done wrong to return; and the moment he heard her voice, he thoroughly wished himself back at St. Ewold's. Why, indeed, should he have wished to have aught further to say to the future wife of Mr. Slope?\n\n\"I am sorry to hear that you are to leave us so soon,\" said he, striving in vain to use his ordinary voice. In answer to this she muttered something about the necessity of her being in Barchester, and betook herself most industriously to her crochet work.\n\nThen there was a little more trite conversation between Mr. Arabin and Mr. Harding--trite, and hard, and vapid, and senseless. Neither of them had anything to say to the other, and yet neither at such a moment liked to remain silent. At last Mr. Harding, taking advantage of a pause, escaped out of the room, and Eleanor and Mr. Arabin were left together.\n\n\"Your going will be a great break-up to our party,\" said he.\n\nShe again muttered something which was all but inaudible, but kept her eyes fixed upon her work.\n\n\"We have had a very pleasant month here,\" said he; \"at least I have; and I am sorry it should be so soon over.\"\n\n\"I have already been from home longer than I intended,\" said she, \"and it is time that I should return.\"\n\n\"Well, pleasant hours and pleasant days must come to an end. It is a pity that so few of them are pleasant; or perhaps, rather--\"\n\n\"It is a pity, certainly, that men and women do so much to destroy the pleasantness of their days,\" said she, interrupting him. \"It is a pity that there should be so little charity abroad.\"\n\n\"Charity should begin at home,\" said he, and he was proceeding to explain that he as a clergyman could not be what she would call charitable at the expense of those principles which he considered it his duty to teach, when he remembered that it would be worse than vain to argue on such a matter with the future wife of Mr. Slope. \"But you are just leaving us,\" he continued, \"and I will not weary your last hour with another lecture. As it is, I fear I have given you too many.\"\n\n\"You should practise as well as preach, Mr. Arabin.\"\n\n\"Undoubtedly I should. So should we all. All of us who presume to teach are bound to do our utmost towards fulfilling our own lessons. I thoroughly allow my deficiency in doing so, but I do not quite know now to what you allude. Have you any special reason for telling me now that I should practise as well as preach?\"\n\nEleanor made no answer. She longed to let him know the cause of her anger, to upbraid him for speaking of her disrespectfully, and then at last to forgive him, and so part friends. She felt that she would be unhappy to leave him in her present frame of mind, but yet she could hardly bring herself to speak to him of Mr. Slope. And how could she allude to the innuendo thrown out by the archdeacon, and thrown out, as she believed, at the instigation of Mr. Arabin? She wanted to make him know that he was wrong, to make him aware that he had ill-treated her, in order that the sweetness of her forgiveness might be enhanced. She felt that she liked him too well to be contented to part with him in displeasure, yet she could not get over her deep displeasure without some explanation, some acknowledgement on his part, some assurance that he would never again so sin against her.\n\n\"Why do you tell me that I should practise what I preach?\" continued he.\n\n\"All men should do so.\"\n\n\"Certainly. That is as it were understood and acknowledged. But you do not say so to all men, or to all clergymen. The advice, good as it is, is not given except in allusion to some special deficiency. If you will tell me my special deficiency, I will endeavour to profit by the advice.\"\n\nShe paused for awhile and then, looking full in his face, she said, \"You are not bold enough, Mr. Arabin, to speak out to me openly and plainly, and yet you expect me, a woman, to speak openly to you. Why did you speak calumny of me to Dr. Grantly behind my back?\"\n\n\"Calumny!\" said he, and his whole face became suffused with blood. \"What calumny? If I have spoken calumny of you, I will beg your pardon, and his to whom I spoke it, and God's pardon also. But what calumny have I spoken of you to Dr. Grantly?\"\n\nShe also blushed deeply. She could not bring herself to ask him whether he had not spoken of her as another man's wife. \"You know that best yourself,\" said she. \"But I ask you as a man of honour, if you have not spoken of me as you would not have spoken of your own sister--or rather I will not ask you,\" she continued, finding that he did not immediately answer her. \"I will not put you to the necessity of answering such a question. Dr. Grantly has told me what you said.\"\n\n\"Dr. Grantly certainly asked me for my advice, and I gave it. He asked me--\"\n\n\"I know he did, Mr. Arabin. He asked you whether he would be doing right to receive me at Plumstead if I continued my acquaintance with a gentleman who happens to be personally disagreeable to yourself and to him.\"\n\n\"You are mistaken, Mrs. Bold. I have no personal knowledge of Mr. Slope; I never met him in my life.\"\n\n\"You are not the less individually hostile to him. It is not for me to question the propriety of your enmity, but I had a right to expect that my name should not have been mixed up in your hostilities. This has been done, and been done by you in a manner the most injurious and the most distressing to me as a woman. I must confess, Mr. Arabin, that from you I expected a different sort of usage.\"\n\nAs she spoke she with difficulty restrained her tears--but she did restrain them. Had she given way and sobbed aloud, as in such cases a woman should do, he would have melted at once, implored her pardon, perhaps knelt at her feet and declared his love. Everything would have been explained, and Eleanor would have gone back to Barchester with a contented mind. How easily would she have forgiven and forgotten the archdeacon's suspicions had she but heard the whole truth from Mr. Arabin. But then where would have been my novel? She did not cry, and Mr. Arabin did not melt.\n\n\"You do me an injustice,\" said he. \"My advice was asked by Dr. Grantly, and I was obliged to give it.\"\n\n\"Dr. Grantly has been most officious, most impertinent. I have as complete a right to form my acquaintance as he has to form his. What would you have said had I consulted you as to the propriety of my banishing Dr. Grantly from my house because he knows Lord Tattenham Corner? I am sure Lord Tattenham is quite as objectionable an acquaintance for a clergyman as Mr. Slope is for a clergyman's daughter.\"\n\n\"I do not know Lord Tattenham Corner.\"\n\n\"No, but Dr. Grantly does. It is nothing to me if he knows all the young lords on every race-course in England. I shall not interfere with him, nor shall he with me.\"\n\n\"I am sorry to differ with you, Mrs. Bold, but as you have spoken to me on this matter, and especially as you blame me for what little I said on the subject, I must tell you that I do differ from you. Dr. Grantly's position as a man in the world gives him a right to choose his own acquaintances, subject to certain influences. If he chooses them badly, those influences will be used. If he consorts with persons unsuitable to him, his bishop will interfere. What the bishop is to Dr. Grantly, Dr. Grantly is to you.\"\n\n\"I deny it. I utterly deny it,\" said Eleanor, jumping from her seat and literally flashing before Mr. Arabin, as she stood on the drawing-room floor. He had never seen her so excited, he had never seen her look half so beautiful.\n\n\"I utterly deny it,\" said she. \"Dr. Grantly has no sort of jurisdiction over me whatsoever. Do you and he forget that I am not altogether alone in the world? Do you forget that I have a father? Dr. Grantly, I believe, always has forgotten it.\n\n\"From you, Mr. Arabin,\" she continued, \"I would have listened to advice because I should have expected it to have been given as one friend may advise another--not as a schoolmaster gives an order to a pupil. I might have differed from you--on this matter I should have done so--but had you spoken to me in your usual manner and with your usual freedom, I should not have been angry. But now--was it manly of you, Mr. Arabin, to speak of me in this way--so disrespectful--so--? I cannot bring myself to repeat what you said. You must understand what I feel. Was it just of you to speak of me in such a way and to advise my sister's husband to turn me out of my sister's house because I chose to know a man of whose doctrine you disapprove?\"\n\n\"I have no alternative left to me, Mrs. Bold,\" said he, standing with his back to the fire-place, looking down intently at the carpet pattern, and speaking with a slow, measured voice, \"but to tell you plainly what did take place between me and Dr. Grantly.\"\n\n\"Well,\" said she, finding that he paused for a moment.\n\n\"I am afraid that what I may say may pain you.\"\n\n\"It cannot well do so more than what you have already done,\" said she.\n\n\"Dr. Grantly asked me whether I thought it would be prudent for him to receive you in his house as the wife of Mr. Slope, and I told him that I thought it would be imprudent. Believing it to be utterly impossible that Mr. Slope and--\"\n\n\"Thank you, Mr. Arabin, that is sufficient. I do not want to know your reasons,\" said she, speaking with a terribly calm voice. \"I have shown to this gentleman the commonplace civility of a neighbour; and because I have done so, because I have not indulged against him in all the rancour and hatred which you and Dr. Grantly consider due to all clergymen who do not agree with yourselves, you conclude that I am to marry him; or rather you do not conclude so--no rational man could really come to such an outrageous conclusion without better ground; you have not thought so, but, as I am in a position in which such an accusation must be peculiarly painful, it is made in order that I may be terrified into hostility against this enemy of yours.\"\n\nAs she finished speaking, she walked to the drawing-room window and stepped out into the garden. Mr. Arabin was left in the room, still occupied in counting the pattern on the carpet. He had, however, distinctly heard and accurately marked every word that she had spoken. Was it not clear from what she had said that the archdeacon had been wrong in imputing to her any attachment to Mr. Slope? Was it not clear that Eleanor was still free to make another choice? It may seem strange that he should for a moment have had a doubt, and yet he did doubt. She had not absolutely denied the charge; she had not expressly said that it was untrue. Mr. Arabin understood little of the nature of a woman's feelings, or he would have known how improbable it was that she should make any clearer declaration than she had done. Few men do understand the nature of a woman's heart, till years have robbed such understanding of its value. And it is well that it should be so, or men would triumph too easily.\n\nMr. Arabin stood counting the carpet, unhappy, wretchedly unhappy, at the hard words that had been spoken to him, and yet happy, exquisitely happy, as he thought that after all the woman whom he so regarded was not to become the wife of the man whom he so much disliked. As he stood there he began to be aware that he was himself in love. Forty years had passed over his head, and as yet woman's beauty had never given him an uneasy hour. His present hour was very uneasy.\n\nNot that he remained there for half or a quarter of that time. In spite of what Eleanor had said, Mr. Arabin was, in truth, a manly man. Having ascertained that he loved this woman, and having now reason to believe that she was free to receive his love, at least if she pleased to do so, he followed her into the garden to make such wooing as he could.\n\nHe was not long in finding her. She was walking to and fro beneath the avenue of elms that stood in the archdeacon's grounds, skirting the churchyard. What had passed between her and Mr. Arabin had not, alas, tended to lessen the acerbity of her spirit. She was very angry--more angry with him than with anyone. How could he have so misunderstood her? She had been so intimate with him, had allowed him such latitude in what he had chosen to say to her, had complied with his ideas, cherished his views, fostered his precepts, cared for his comforts, made much of him in every way in which a pretty woman can make much of an unmarried man without committing herself or her feelings! She had been doing this, and while she had been doing it he had regarded her as the affianced wife of another man.\n\nAs she passed along the avenue, every now and then an unbidden tear would force itself on her cheek, and as she raised her hand to brush it away, she stamped with her little foot upon the sward with very spite to think that she had been so treated.\n\nMr. Arabin was very near to her when she first saw him, and she turned short round and retraced her steps down the avenue, trying to rid her cheeks of all trace of the tell-tale tears. It was a needless endeavour, for Mr. Arabin was in a state of mind that hardly allowed him to observe such trifles. He followed her down the walk and overtook her just as she reached the end of it.\n\nHe had not considered how he would address her; he had not thought what he would say. He had only felt that it was wretchedness to him to quarrel with her, and that it would be happiness to be allowed to love her. And yet he could not lower himself by asking her pardon. He had done her no wrong. He had not calumniated her, not injured her, as she had accused him of doing. He could not confess sins of which he had not been guilty. He could only let the past be past and ask her as to her and his hopes for the future.\n\n\"I hope we are not to part as enemies?\" said he.\n\n\"There shall be no enmity on my part,\" said Eleanor; \"I endeavour to avoid all enmities. It would be a hollow pretence were I to say that there can be true friendship between us, after what has just passed. People cannot make their friends of those whom they despise.\"\n\n\"And am I despised?\"\n\n\"I must have been so before you could have spoken of me as you did. And I was deceived, cruelly deceived. I believed that you thought well of me; I believed that you esteemed me.\"\n\n\"Thought well of you and esteemed you!\" said he. \"In justifying myself before you, I must use stronger words than those.\" He paused for a moment, and Eleanor's heart beat with painful violence within her bosom as she waited for him to go on. \"I have esteemed, do esteem you, as I never yet esteemed any woman. Think well of you! I never thought to think so well, so much of any human creature. Speak calumny of you! Insult you! Wilfully injure you! I wish it were my privilege to shield you from calumny, insult, and injury. Calumny! Ah me! 'Twere almost better that it were so. Better than to worship with a sinful worship; sinful and vain also.\" And then he walked along beside her, with his hands clasped behind his back, looking down on the grass beneath his feet and utterly at a loss how to express his meaning. And Eleanor walked beside him determined at least to give him no assistance.\n\n\"Ah me!\" he uttered at last, speaking rather to himself than to her. \"Ah me! These Plumstead walks were pleasant enough, if one could have but heart's ease, but without that the dull, dead stones of Oxford were far preferable--and St. Ewold's, too. Mrs. Bold, I am beginning to think that I mistook myself when I came hither. A Romish priest now would have escaped all this. Oh, Father of heaven, how good for us would it be if thou couldest vouchsafe to us a certain rule.\"\n\n\"And have we not a certain rule, Mr. Arabin?\"\n\n\"Yes--yes, surely; 'Lead us not into temptation but deliver us from evil.' But what is temptation? What is evil? Is this evil--is this temptation?\"\n\nPoor Mr. Arabin! It would not come out of him, that deep, true love of his. He could not bring himself to utter it in plain language that would require and demand an answer. He knew not how to say to the woman by his side, \"Since the fact is that you do not love that other man, that you are not to be his wife, can you love me, will you be my wife?\" These were the words which were in his heart, but with all his sighs he could not draw them to his lips. He would have given anything, everything for power to ask this simple question, but glib as was his tongue in pulpits and on platforms, now he could not find a word wherewith to express the plain wish of his heart.\n\nAnd yet Eleanor understood him as thoroughly as though he had declared his passion with all the elegant fluency of a practised Lothario. With a woman's instinct, she followed every bend of his mind as he spoke of the pleasantness of Plumstead and the stones of Oxford, as he alluded to the safety of the Romish priest and the hidden perils of temptation. She knew that it all meant love. She knew that this man at her side, this accomplished scholar, this practised orator, this great polemical combatant, was striving and striving in vain to tell her that his heart was no longer his own.\n\nShe knew this, and felt a sort of joy in knowing it; yet she would not come to his aid. He had offended her deeply, had treated her unworthily, the more unworthily seeing that he had learnt to love her, and Eleanor could not bring herself to abandon her revenge. She did not ask herself whether or no she would ultimately accept his love. She did not even acknowledge to herself that she now perceived it with pleasure. At the present moment it did not touch her heart; it merely appeased her pride and flattered her vanity. Mr. Arabin had dared to associate her name with that of Mr. Slope, and now her spirit was soothed by finding that he would fain associate it with his own. And so she walked on beside him, inhaling incense but giving out no sweetness in return.\n\n\"Answer me this,\" said Mr. Arabin, stopping suddenly in his walk and stepping forward so that he faced his companion. \"Answer me this one question. You do not love Mr. Slope? You do not intend to be his wife?\"\n\nMr. Arabin certainly did not go the right way to win such a woman as Eleanor Bold. Just as her wrath was evaporating, as it was disappearing before the true warmth of his untold love, he rekindled it by a most useless repetition of his original sin. Had he known what he was about, he should never have mentioned Mr. Slope's name before Eleanor Bold, till he had made her all his own. Then, and not till then, he might have talked of Mr. Slope with as much triumph as he chose.\n\n\"I shall answer no such question,\" said she; \"and what is more, I must tell you that nothing can justify your asking it. Good morning!\"\n\nAnd so saying, she stepped proudly across the lawn and, passing through the drawing-room window, joined her father and sister at lunch in the dining-room. Half an hour afterwards she was in the carriage, and so she left Plumstead without again seeing Mr. Arabin.\n\nHis walk was long and sad among the sombre trees that overshadowed the churchyard. He left the archdeacon's grounds that he might escape attention, and sauntered among the green hillocks under which lay at rest so many of the once loving swains and forgotten beauties of Plumstead. To his ears Eleanor's last words sounded like a knell never to be reversed. He could not comprehend that she might be angry with him, indignant with him, remorseless with him, and yet love him. He could not make up his mind whether or no Mr. Slope was in truth a favoured rival. If not, why should she not have answered his question?\n\nPoor Mr. Arabin--untaught, illiterate, boorish, ignorant man! That at forty years of age you should know so little of the workings of a woman's heart!\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI. The Bishop's Library\n\nAnd thus the pleasant party at Plumstead was broken up. It had been a very pleasant party as long as they had all remained in good humour with one another. Mrs. Grantly had felt her house to be gayer and brighter than it had been for many a long day, and the archdeacon had been aware that the month had passed pleasantly without attributing the pleasure to any other special merits than those of his own hospitality. Within three or four days of Eleanor's departure, Mr. Harding had also returned, and Mr. Arabin had gone to Oxford to spend one week there previous to his settling at the vicarage of St. Ewold's. He had gone laden with many messages to Dr. Gwynne touching the iniquity of the doings in Barchester palace and the peril in which it was believed the hospital still stood in spite of the assurances contained in Mr. Slope's inauspicious letter.\n\nDuring Eleanor's drive into Barchester she had not much opportunity of reflecting on Mr. Arabin. She had been constrained to divert her mind both from his sins and his love by the necessity of conversing with her sister and maintaining the appearance of parting with her on good terms. When the carriage reached her own door, and while she was in the act of giving her last kiss to her sister and nieces, Mary Bold ran out and exclaimed:\n\n\"Oh, Eleanor, have you heard? Oh, Mrs. Grantly, have you heard what has happened? The poor dean!\"\n\n\"Good heavens!\" said Mrs. Grantly. \"What--what has happened?\"\n\n\"This morning at nine he had a fit of apoplexy, and he has not spoken since. I very much fear that by this time he is no more.\"\n\nMrs. Grantly had been very intimate with the dean, and was therefore much shocked. Eleanor had not known him so well; nevertheless, she was sufficiently acquainted with his person and manners to feel startled and grieved also at the tidings she now received. \"I will go at once to the deanery,\" said Mrs. Grantly; \"the archdeacon, I am sure, will be there. If there is any news to send you, I will let Thomas call before he leaves town.\" And so the carriage drove off, leaving Eleanor and her baby with Mary Bold.\n\nMrs. Grantly had been quite right. The archdeacon was at the deanery. He had come into Barchester that morning by himself, not caring to intrude himself upon Eleanor, and he also immediately on his arrival had heard of the dean's fit. There was, as we have before said, a library or reading-room connecting the cathedral with the dean's house. This was generally called the bishop's library, because a certain bishop of Barchester was supposed to have added it to the cathedral. It was built immediately over a portion of the cloisters, and a flight of stairs descended from it into the room in which the cathedral clergymen put their surplices on and off. As it also opened directly into the dean's house, it was the passage through which that dignitary usually went to his public devotions. Who had or had not the right of entry into it, it might be difficult to say; but the people of Barchester believed that it belonged to the dean, and the clergymen of Barchester believed that it belonged to the chapter.\n\nOn the morning in question most of the resident clergymen who constituted the chapter, and some few others, were here assembled, and among them as usual the archdeacon towered with high authority. He had heard of the dean's fit before he was over the bridge which led into the town, and had at once come to the well-known clerical trysting place. He had been there by eleven o'clock, and had remained ever since. From time to time the medical men who had been called in came through from the deanery into the library, uttered little bulletins, and then returned. There was, it appears, very little hope of the old man's rallying, indeed no hope of anything like a final recovery. The only question was whether he must die at once speechless, unconscious, stricken to death by his first heavy fit, or whether by due aid of medical skill he might not be so far brought back to this world as to become conscious of his state and enabled to address one prayer to his Maker before he was called to meet Him face to face at the judgement seat.\n\nSir Omicron Pie had been sent for from London. That great man had shown himself a wonderful adept at keeping life still moving within an old man's heart in the case of good old Bishop Grantly, and it might be reasonably expected that he would be equally successful with a dean. In the meantime Dr. Fillgrave and Mr. Rerechild were doing their best, and poor Miss Trefoil sat at the head of her father's bed, longing, as in such cases daughters do long, to be allowed to do something to show her love--if it were only to chafe his feet with her hands, or wait in menial offices on those autocratic doctors--anything so that now in the time of need she might be of use.\n\nThe archdeacon alone of the attendant clergy had been admitted for a moment into the sick man's chamber. He had crept in with creaking shoes, had said with smothered voice a word of consolation to the sorrowing daughter, had looked on the distorted face of his old friend with solemn but yet eager scrutinising eye, as though he said in his heart \"and so some day it will probably be with me,\" and then, having whispered an unmeaning word or two to the doctors, had creaked his way back again into the library.\n\n\"He'll never speak again, I fear,\" said the archdeacon as he noiselessly closed the door, as though the unconscious dying man, from whom all sense had fled, would have heard in his distant chamber the spring of the lock which was now so carefully handled.\n\n\"Indeed! Indeed! Is he so bad?\" said the meagre little prebendary, turning over in his own mind all the probable candidates for the deanery and wondering whether the archdeacon would think it worth his while to accept it. \"The fit must have been very violent.\"\n\n\"When a man over seventy has a stroke of apoplexy, it seldom comes very lightly,\" said the burly chancellor.\n\n\"He was an excellent, sweet-tempered man,\" said one of the vicars choral. \"Heaven knows how we shall repair his loss.\"\n\n\"He was indeed,\" said a minor canon, \"and a great blessing to all those privileged to take a share in the services of our cathedral. I suppose the government will appoint, Mr. Archdeacon. I trust we may have no stranger.\"\n\n\"We will not talk about his successor,\" said the archdeacon, \"while there is yet hope.\"\n\n\"Oh, no, of course not,\" said the minor canon. \"It would be exceedingly indecorous; but--\"\n\n\"I know of no man,\" said the meagre little prebendary, \"who has better interest with the present government than Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" said two or three at once almost sotto voce. \"Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester!\"\n\n\"Pooh!\" exclaimed the burly chancellor.\n\n\"The bishop would do anything for him,\" said the little prebendary.\n\n\"And so would Mrs. Proudie,\" said the vicar choral.\n\n\"Pooh!\" said the chancellor.\n\nThe archdeacon had almost turned pale at the idea. What if Mr. Slope should become Dean of Barchester? To be sure there was no adequate ground, indeed no ground at all, for presuming that such a desecration could even be contemplated. But nevertheless it was on the cards. Dr. Proudie had interest with the government, and the man carried as it were Dr. Proudie in his pocket. How should they all conduct themselves if Mr. Slope were to become Dean of Barchester? The bare idea for a moment struck even Dr. Grantly dumb.\n\n\"It would certainly not be very pleasant for us to have Mr. Slope at the deanery,\" said the little prebendary, chuckling inwardly at the evident consternation which his surmise had created.\n\n\"About as pleasant and as probable as having you in the palace,\" said the chancellor.\n\n\"I should think such an appointment highly improbable,\" said the minor canon, \"and, moreover, extremely injudicious. Should not you, Mr. Archdeacon?\"\n\n\"I should presume such a thing to be quite out of the question,\" said the archdeacon, \"but at the present moment I am thinking rather of our poor friend who is lying so near us than of Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Of course, of course,\" said the vicar choral with a very solemn air; \"of course you are. So are we all. Poor Dr. Trefoil; the best of men, but--\"\n\n\"It's the most comfortable dean's residence in England,\" said a second prebendary. \"Fifteen acres in the grounds. It is better than many of the bishops' palaces.\"\n\n\"And full two thousand a year,\" said the meagre doctor.\n\n\"It is cut down to £1,200,\" said the chancellor.\n\n\"No,\" said the second prebendary. \"It is to be fifteen. A special case was made.\"\n\n\"No such thing,\" said the chancellor.\n\n\"You'll find I'm right,\" said the prebendary.\n\n\"I'm sure I read it in the report,\" said the minor canon.\n\n\"Nonsense,\" said the chancellor. \"They couldn't do it. There were to be no exceptions but London and Durham.\"\n\n\"And Canterbury and York,\" said the vicar choral modestly.\n\n\"What do you say, Grantly?\" said the meagre little doctor.\n\n\"Say about what?\" said the archdeacon, who had been looking as though he were thinking about his friend the dean, but who had in reality been thinking about Mr. Slope.\n\n\"What is the next dean to have, twelve or fifteen?\"\n\n\"Twelve,\" said the archdeacon authoritatively, thereby putting an end at once to all doubt and dispute among his subordinates as far as that subject was concerned.\n\n\"Well, I certainly thought it was fifteen,\" said the minor canon.\n\n\"Pooh!\" said the burly chancellor. At this moment the door opened and in came Dr. Fillgrave.\n\n\"How is he?\" \"Is he conscious?\" \"Can he speak?\" \"I hope not dead?\" \"No worse news, Doctor, I trust?\" \"I hope, I trust, something better, Doctor?\" said half a dozen voices all at once, each in a tone of extremest anxiety. It was pleasant to see how popular the good old dean was among his clergy.\n\n\"No change, gentlemen; not the slightest change. But a telegraphic message has arrived--Sir Omicron Pie will be here by the 9.15 P.M. train. If any man can do anything, Sir Omicron Pie will do it. But all that skill can do has been done.\"\n\n\"We are sure of that, Dr. Fillgrave,\" said the archdeacon; \"we are quite sure of that. But yet you know--\"\n\n\"Oh, quite right,\" said the doctor, \"quite right--I should have done just the same--I advised it at once. I said to Rerechild at once that with such a life and such a man, Sir Omicron should be summoned--of course I knew expense was nothing--so distinguished, you know, and so popular. Nevertheless, all that human skill can do has been done.\"\n\nJust at this period Mrs. Grantly's carriage drove into the close, and the archdeacon went down to confirm the news which she had heard before.\n\nBy the 9.15 P.M. train Sir Omicron Pie did arrive. And in the course of the night a sort of consciousness returned to the poor old dean. Whether this was due to Sir Omicron Pie is a question on which it may be well not to offer an opinion. Dr. Fillgrave was very clear in his own mind, but Sir Omicron himself is thought to have differed from that learned doctor. At any rate Sir Omicron expressed an opinion that the dean had yet some days to live.\n\nFor the eight or ten next days, accordingly, the poor dean remained in the same state, half-conscious and half-comatose; and the attendant clergy began to think that no new appointment would be necessary for some few months to come.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII. A New Candidate for Ecclesiastical Honours\n\nThe dean's illness occasioned much mental turmoil in other places besides the deanery and adjoining library, and the idea which occurred to the meagre little prebendary about Mr. Slope did not occur to him alone.\n\nThe bishop was sitting listlessly in his study when the news reached him of the dean's illness. It was brought to him by Mr. Slope, who of course was not the last person in Barchester to hear it. It was also not slow in finding its way to Mrs. Proudie's ears. It may be presumed that there was not just then much friendly intercourse between these two rival claimants for his lordship's obedience. Indeed, though living in the same house, they had not met since the stormy interview between them in the bishop's study on the preceding day.\n\nOn that occasion Mrs. Proudie had been defeated. That the prestige of continual victory should have been torn from her standards was a subject of great sorrow to that militant lady; but, though defeated, she was not overcome. She felt that she might yet recover her lost ground, that she might yet hurl Mr. Slope down to the dust from which she had picked him, and force her sinning lord to sue for pardon in sackcloth and ashes.\n\nOn that memorable day, memorable for his mutiny and rebellion against her high behests, he had carried his way with a high hand, and had really begun to think it possible that the days of his slavery were counted. He had begun to hope that he was now about to enter into a free land, a land delicious with milk which he himself might quaff and honey which would not tantalize him by being only honey to the eye. When Mrs. Proudie banged the door as she left his room, he felt himself every inch a bishop. To be sure, his spirit had been a little cowed by his chaplain's subsequent lecture, but on the whole he was highly pleased with himself, and he flattered himself that the worst was over. \"Ce n'est que le premier pas qui coûte,\" he reflected, and now that the first step had been so magnanimously taken, all the rest would follow easily.\n\nHe met his wife as a matter of course at dinner, where little or nothing was said that could ruffle the bishop's happiness. His daughters and the servants were present and protected him.\n\nHe made one or two trifling remarks on the subject of his projected visit to the archbishop, in order to show to all concerned that he intended to have his own way; the very servants, perceiving the change, transferred a little of their reverence from their mistress to their master. All which the master perceived, and so also did the mistress. But Mrs. Proudie bided her time.\n\nAfter dinner he returned to his study, where Mr. Slope soon found him, and there they had tea together and planned many things. For some few minutes the bishop was really happy; but as the clock on the chimney-piece warned him that the stilly hours of night were drawing on, as he looked at his chamber candlestick and knew that he must use it, his heart sank within him again. He was as a ghost, all whose power of wandering free through these upper regions ceases at cock-crow; or, rather, he was the opposite of the ghost, for till cock-crow he must again be a serf. And would that be all? Could he trust himself to come down to breakfast a free man in the morning?\n\nHe was nearly an hour later than usual when he betook himself to his rest. Rest! What rest? However, he took a couple of glasses of sherry and mounted the stairs. Far be it from us to follow him thither. There are some things which no novelist, no historian, should attempt; some few scenes in life's drama which even no poet should dare to paint. Let that which passed between Dr. Proudie and his wife on this night be understood to be among them.\n\nHe came down the following morning a sad and thoughtful man. He was attenuated in appearance--one might almost say emaciated. I doubt whether his now grizzled locks had not palpably become more grey than on the preceding evening. At any rate he had aged materially. Years do not make a man old gradually and at an even pace. Look through the world and see if this is not so always, except in those rare cases in which the human being lives and dies without joys and without sorrows, like a vegetable. A man shall be possessed of florid, youthful blooming health till, it matters not what age--thirty; forty; fifty--then comes some nipping frost, some period of agony, that robs the fibres of the body of their succulence, and the hale and hearty man is counted among the old.\n\nHe came down and breakfasted alone; Mrs. Proudie, being indisposed, took her coffee in her bedroom, and her daughters waited upon her there. He ate his breakfast alone, and then, hardly knowing what he did, he betook himself to his usual seat in his study. He tried to solace himself with his coming visit to the archbishop. That effort of his own free will at any rate remained to him as an enduring triumph. But somehow, now that he had achieved it, he did not seem to care so much about it. It was his ambition that had prompted him to take his place at the archiepiscopal table, and his ambition was now quite dead within him.\n\nHe was thus seated when Mr. Slope made his appearance, with breathless impatience.\n\n\"My lord, the dean is dead.\"\n\n\"Good heavens!\" exclaimed the bishop, startled out of his apathy by an announcement so sad and so sudden.\n\n\"He is either dead or now dying. He has had an apoplectic fit, and I am told that there is not the slightest hope; indeed, I do not doubt that by this time he is no more.\"\n\nBells were rung, and servants were immediately sent to inquire. In the course of the morning the bishop, leaning on his chaplain's arm, himself called at the deanery door. Mrs. Proudie sent to Miss Trefoil all manner of offers of assistance. The Misses Proudie sent also, and there was immense sympathy between the palace and the deanery. The answer to all inquiries was unvaried. The dean was just the same, and Sir Omicron Pie was expected down by the 9.15 P.M. train.\n\nAnd then Mr. Slope began to meditate, as others also had done, as to who might possibly be the new dean, and it occurred to him, as it had also occurred to others, that it might be possible that he should be the new dean himself. And then the question as to the twelve hundred, or fifteen hundred, or two thousand ran in his mind, as it had run through those of the other clergymen in the cathedral library.\n\nWhether it might be two thousand, or fifteen, or twelve hundred, it would in any case undoubtedly be a great thing for him, if he could get it. The gratification to his ambition would be greater even than that of his covetousness. How glorious to out-top the archdeacon in his own cathedral city; to sit above prebendaries and canons and have the cathedral pulpit and all the cathedral services altogether at his own disposal!\n\nBut it might be easier to wish for this than to obtain it. Mr. Slope, however, was not without some means of forwarding his views, and he at any rate did not let the grass grow under his feet. In the first place, he thought--and not vainly--that he could count upon what assistance the bishop could give him. He immediately changed his views with regard to his patron; he made up his mind that if he became dean, he would hand his lordship back again to his wife's vassalage; and he thought it possible that his lordship might not be sorry to rid himself of one of his mentors. Mr. Slope had also taken some steps towards making his name known to other men in power. There was a certain chief-commissioner of national schools, who at the present moment was presumed to stand especially high in the good graces of the government bigwigs, and with him Mr. Slope had contrived to establish a sort of epistolary intimacy. He thought that he might safely apply to Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, and he felt sure that if Sir Nicholas chose to exert himself, the promise of such a piece of preferment would be had for the asking.\n\nThen he also had the press at his bidding, or flattered himself that he had so. \"The Daily Jupiter\" had taken his part in a very thorough manner in those polemical contests of his with Mr. Arabin; he had on more than one occasion absolutely had an interview with a gentleman on the staff of that paper who, if not the editor, was as good as the editor; and he had long been in the habit of writing telling letters on all manner of ecclesiastical abuses, which he signed with his initials, and sent to his editorial friend with private notes signed in his own name. Indeed, he and Mr. Towers--such was the name of the powerful gentleman of the press with whom he was connected--were generally very amiable with each other. Mr. Slope's little productions were always printed and occasionally commented upon; and thus, in a small sort of way, he had become a literary celebrity. This public life had great charms for him, though it certainly also had its drawbacks. On one occasion, when speaking in the presence of reporters, he had failed to uphold and praise and swear by that special line of conduct which had been upheld and praised and sworn by in \"The Jupiter,\" and then he had been much surprised and at the moment not a little irritated to find himself lacerated most unmercifully by his old ally. He was quizzed and bespattered and made a fool of, just as though, or rather worse than if, he had been a constant enemy instead of a constant friend. He had hitherto not learnt that a man who aspires to be on the staff of \"The Jupiter\" must surrender all individuality. But ultimately this little castigation had broken no bones between him and his friend Mr. Towers. Mr. Slope was one of those who understood the world too well to show himself angry with such a potentate as \"The Jupiter.\" He had kissed the rod that scourged him, and now thought that he might fairly look for his reward. He determined that he would at once let Mr. Towers know that he was a candidate for the place which was about to become vacant. More than one piece of preferment had lately been given away much in accordance with advice tendered to the government in the columns of \"The Jupiter.\"\n\nBut it was incumbent on Mr. Slope first to secure the bishop. He specially felt that it behoved him to do this before the visit to the archbishop was made. It was really quite providential that the dean should have fallen ill just at the very nick of time. If Dr. Proudie could be instigated to take the matter up warmly, he might manage a good deal while staying at the archbishop's palace. Feeling this very strongly, Mr. Slope determined to sound the bishop that very afternoon. He was to start on the following morning to London, and therefore not a moment could be lost with safety.\n\nHe went into the bishop's study about five o'clock and found him still sitting alone. It might have been supposed that he had hardly moved since the little excitement occasioned by his walk to the dean's door. He still wore on his face that dull, dead look of half-unconscious suffering. He was doing nothing, reading nothing, thinking of nothing, but simply gazing on vacancy when Mr. Slope for the second time that day entered his room.\n\n\"Well, Slope,\" said he somewhat impatiently, for, to tell the truth, he was not anxious just at present to have much conversation with Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Your lordship will be sorry to hear that as yet the poor dean has shown no sign of amendment.\"\n\n\"Oh--ah--hasn't he? Poor man! I'm sure I'm very sorry. I suppose Sir Omicron has not arrived yet?\"\n\n\"No, not till the 9.15 P.M. train.\"\n\n\"I wonder they didn't have a special. They say Dr. Trefoil is very rich.\"\n\n\"Very rich, I believe,\" said Mr. Slope. \"But the truth is, all the doctors in London can do no good--no other good than to show that every possible care has been taken. Poor Dr. Trefoil is not long for this world, my lord.\"\n\n\"I suppose not--I suppose not.\"\n\n\"Oh, no; indeed, his best friends could not wish that he should outlive such a shock, for his intellects cannot possibly survive it.\"\n\n\"Poor man! Poor man!\" said the bishop.\n\n\"It will naturally be a matter of much moment to your lordship who is to succeed him,\" said Mr. Slope. \"It would be a great thing if you could secure the appointment for some person of your own way of thinking on important points. The party hostile to us are very strong here in Barchester--much too strong.\"\n\n\"Yes, yes. If poor Dr. Trefoil is to go, it will be a great thing to get a good man in his place.\"\n\n\"It will be everything to your lordship to get a man on whose co-operation you can reckon. Only think what trouble we might have if Dr. Grantly, or Dr. Hyandry, or any of that way of thinking were to get it.\"\n\n\"It is not very probable that Lord ---- will give it to any of that school; why should he?\"\n\n\"No. Not probable; certainly not; but it's possible. Great interest will probably be made. If I might venture to advise your lordship, I would suggest that you should discuss the matter with his grace next week. I have no doubt that your wishes, if made known and backed by his grace, would be paramount with Lord ----.\"\n\n\"Well, I don't know that; Lord ---- has always been very kind to me, very kind. But I am unwilling to interfere in such matters unless asked. And indeed if asked, I don't know whom, at this moment, I should recommend.\"\n\nMr. Slope, even Mr. Slope, felt at the present rather abashed. He hardly knew how to frame his little request in language sufficiently modest. He had recognized and acknowledged to himself the necessity of shocking the bishop in the first instance by the temerity of his application, and his difficulty was how best to remedy that by his adroitness and eloquence. \"I doubted myself,\" said he, \"whether your lordship would have anyone immediately in your eye, and it is on this account that I venture to submit to you an idea that I have been turning over in my own mind. If poor Dr. Trefoil must go, I really do not see why, with your lordship's assistance, I should not hold the preferment myself.\"\n\n\"You!\" exclaimed the bishop in a manner that Mr. Slope could hardly have considered complimentary.\n\nThe ice was now broken, and Mr. Slope became fluent enough. \"I have been thinking of looking for it. If your lordship will press the matter on the archbishop, I do not doubt but I shall succeed. You see I shall be the first to move, which is a great matter. Then I can count upon assistance from the public press: my name is known, I may say, somewhat favourably known, to that portion of the press which is now most influential with the government; and I have friends also in the government. But nevertheless it is to you, my lord, that I look for assistance. It is from your hands that I would most willingly receive the benefit. And, which should ever be the chief consideration in such matters, you must know better than any other person whatsoever what qualifications I possess.\"\n\nThe bishop sat for awhile dumbfounded. Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester! The idea of such a transformation of character would never have occurred to his own unaided intellect. At first he went on thinking why, for what reasons, on what account, Mr. Slope should be Dean of Barchester. But by degrees the direction of his thoughts changed, and he began to think why, for what reasons, on what account, Mr. Slope should not be Dean of Barchester. As far as he himself, the bishop, was concerned, he could well spare the services of his chaplain. That little idea of using Mr. Slope as a counterpoise to his wife had well nigh evaporated. He had all but acknowledged the futility of the scheme. If indeed he could have slept in his chaplain's bedroom instead of his wife's, there might have been something in it. But--. And thus as Mr. Slope was speaking, the bishop began to recognize the idea that that gentleman might become Dean of Barchester without impropriety--not moved, indeed, by Mr. Slope's eloquence, for he did not follow the tenor of his speech, but led thereto by his own cogitations.\n\n\"I need not say,\" continued Mr. Slope, \"that it would be my chief desire to act in all matters connected with the cathedral as far as possible in accordance with your views. I know your lordship so well (and I hope you know me well enough to have the same feelings) that I am satisfied that my being in that position would add materially to your own comfort, and enable you to extend the sphere of your useful influence. As I said before, it is most desirable that there should be but one opinion among the dignitaries of the same diocese. I doubt much whether I would accept such an appointment in any diocese in which I should be constrained to differ much from the bishop. In this case there would be a delightful uniformity of opinion.\"\n\nMr. Slope perfectly well perceived that the bishop did not follow a word that he said, but nevertheless he went on talking. He knew it was necessary that Dr. Proudie should recover from his surprise, and he knew also that he must give him the opportunity of appearing to have been persuaded by argument. So he went on and produced a multitude of fitting reasons all tending to show that no one on earth could make so good a Dean of Barchester as himself, that the government and the public would assuredly coincide in desiring that he, Mr. Slope, should be Dean of Barchester, but that for high considerations of ecclesiastical polity it would be especially desirable that this piece of preferment should be so bestowed through the instrumentality of the bishop of the diocese.\n\n\"But I really don't know what I could do in the matter,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"If you would mention it to the archbishop; if you could tell his grace that you consider such an appointment very desirable, that you have it much at heart with a view to putting an end to schism in the diocese; if you did this with your usual energy, you would probably find no difficulty in inducing his grace to promise that he would mention it to Lord ----. Of course you would let the archbishop know that I am not looking for the preferment solely through his intervention; that you do not exactly require him to ask it as a favour; that you expect that I shall get it through other sources, as is indeed the case; but that you are very anxious that his grace should express his approval of such an arrangement to Lord ----.\"\n\nIt ended in the bishop promising to do as he was bid. Not that he so promised without a stipulation. \"About that hospital,\" he said in the middle of the conference. \"I was never so troubled in my life\"--which was about the truth. \"You haven't spoken to Mr. Harding since I saw you?\"\n\nMr. Slope assured his patron that he had not.\n\n\"Ah well, then--I think upon the whole it will be better to let Quiverful have it. It has been half-promised to him, and he has a large family and is very poor. I think on the whole it will be better to make out the nomination for Mr. Quiverful.\"\n\n\"But, my lord,\" said Mr. Slope, still thinking that he was bound to make a fight for his own view on this matter, and remembering that it still behoved him to maintain his lately acquired supremacy over Mrs. Proudie, lest he should fail in his views regarding the deanery, \"but, my lord, I am really much afraid--\"\n\n\"Remember, Mr. Slope,\" said the bishop, \"I can hold out no sort of hope to you in this matter of succeeding poor Dr. Trefoil. I will certainly speak to the archbishop, as you wish it, but I cannot think--\"\n\n\"Well, my lord,\" said Mr. Slope, fully understanding the bishop and in his turn interrupting him, \"perhaps your lordship is right about Mr. Quiverful. I have no doubt I can easily arrange matters with Mr. Harding, and I will make out the nomination for your signature as you direct.\"\n\n\"Yes, Slope, I think that will be best; and you may be sure that any little that I can do to forward your views shall be done.\"\n\nAnd so they parted.\n\nMr. Slope had now much business on his hands. He had to make his daily visit to the signora. This common prudence should have now induced him to omit, but he was infatuated, and could not bring himself to be commonly prudent. He determined therefore that he would drink tea at the Stanhopes', and he determined also, or thought that he determined, that having done so he would go thither no more. He had also to arrange his matters with Mrs. Bold. He was of opinion that Eleanor would grace the deanery as perfectly as she would the chaplain's cottage, and he thought, moreover, that Eleanor's fortune would excellently repair any dilapidations and curtailments in the dean's stipend which might have been made by that ruthless ecclesiastical commission.\n\nTouching Mrs. Bold his hopes now soared high. Mr. Slope was one of that numerous multitude of swains who think that all is fair in love, and he had accordingly not refrained from using the services of Mrs. Bold's own maid. From her he had learnt much of what had taken place at Plumstead--not exactly with truth, for \"the own maid\" had not been able to divine the exact truth, but with some sort of similitude to it. He had been told that the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly and Mr. Harding and Mr. Arabin had all quarrelled with \"missus\" for having received a letter from Mr. Slope; that \"missus\" had positively refused to give the letter up; that she had received from the archdeacon the option of giving up either Mr. Slope and his letter, or else the society of Plumstead Rectory; and that \"missus\" had declared, with much indignation, that \"she didn't care a straw for the society of Plumstead Rectory,\" and that she wouldn't give up Mr. Slope for any of them.\n\nConsidering the source from whence this came, it was not quite so untrue as might have been expected. It showed pretty plainly what had been the nature of the conversation in the servants' hall; and, coupled as it was with the certainty of Eleanor's sudden return, it appeared to Mr. Slope to be so far worthy of credit as to justify him in thinking that the fair widow would in all human probability accept his offer.\n\nAll this work was therefore to be done. It was desirable, he thought, that he should make his offer before it was known that Mr. Quiverful was finally appointed to the hospital. In his letter to Eleanor he had plainly declared that Mr. Harding was to have the appointment. It would be very difficult to explain this away, and were he to write another letter to Eleanor, telling the truth and throwing the blame on the bishop, it would naturally injure him in her estimation. He determined therefore to let that matter disclose itself as it would, and to lose no time in throwing himself at her feet.\n\nThen he had to solicit the assistance of Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin and Mr. Towers, and he went directly from the bishop's presence to compose his letters to those gentlemen. As Mr. Slope was esteemed an adept at letter writing, they shall be given in full.\n\n(Private) Palace, Barchester, Sept. 185--\n\nI hope that the intercourse which has been between us will preclude you from regarding my present application as an intrusion. You cannot, I imagine, have yet heard that poor old Dr. Trefoil has been seized with apoplexy. It is a subject of profound grief to everyone in Barchester, for he has always been an excellent man--excellent as a man and as a clergyman. He is, however, full of years, and his life could not under any circumstances have been much longer spared. You may probably have known him.\n\nThere is, it appears, no probable chance of his recovery. Sir Omicron Pie is, I believe, at present with him. At any rate the medical men here have declared that one or two days more must limit the tether of his mortal coil. I sincerely trust that his soul may wing its flight to that haven where it may forever be at rest and forever be happy.\n\nThe bishop has been speaking to me about the preferment, and he is anxious that it should be conferred on me. I confess that I can hardly venture, at my age, to look for such advancement, but I am so far encouraged by his lordship that I believe I shall be induced to do so. His lordship goes to ---- to-morrow and is intent on mentioning the subject to the archbishop.\n\nI know well how deservedly great is your weight with the present government. In any matter touching church preferment you would of course be listened to. Now that the matter has been put into my head, I am of course anxious to be successful. If you can assist me by your good word, you will confer on me one additional favour.\n\nI had better add, that Lord ---- cannot as yet know of this piece of preferment having fallen in, or rather of its certainty of falling (for poor dear Dr. Trefoil is past hope). Should Lord ---- first hear it from you, that might probably be thought to give you a fair claim to express your opinion.\n\nOf course our grand object is that we should all be of one opinion in church matters. This is most desirable at Barchester; it is this that makes our good bishop so anxious about it. You may probably think it expedient to point this out to Lord ---- if it shall be in your power to oblige me by mentioning the subject to his lordship.\n\nBelieve me, My dear Sir Nicholas, Your most faithful servant,\n\nHis letter to Mr. Towers was written in quite a different strain. Mr. Slope conceived that he completely understood the difference in character and position of the two men whom he addressed. He knew that for such a man as Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin a little flummery was necessary, and that it might be of the easy, everyday description. Accordingly his letter to Sir Nicholas was written, currente calamo, with very little trouble. But to such a man as Mr. Towers it was not so easy to write a letter that should be effective and yet not offensive, that should carry its point without undue interference. It was not difficult to flatter Dr. Proudie or Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, but very difficult to flatter Mr. Towers without letting the flattery declare itself. This, however, had to be done. Moreover, this letter must, in appearance at least, be written without effort, and be fluent, unconstrained, and demonstrative of no doubt or fear on the part of the writer. Therefore the epistle to Mr. Towers was studied, and re-copied, and elaborated at the cost of so many minutes that Mr. Slope had hardly time to dress himself and reach Dr. Stanhope's that evening.\n\nWhen dispatched, it ran as follows:--\n\n(Private.) Barchester. Sept. 185--\n\n(He purposely omitted any allusion to the \"palace,\" thinking that Mr. Towers might not like it. A great man, he remembered, had been once much condemned for dating a letter from Windsor Castle.)\n\nWe were all a good deal shocked here this morning by hearing that poor old Dean Trefoil had been stricken with apoplexy. The fit took him about 9 A.M. I am writing now to save the post, and he is still alive, but past all hope or possibility, I believe, of living. Sir Omicron Pie is here, or will be very shortly, but all that even Sir Omicron can do is to ratify the sentence of his less distinguished brethren that nothing can be done. Poor Dr. Trefoil's race on this side the grave is run. I do not know whether you knew him. He was a good, quiet, charitable man, of the old school, of course, as any clergyman over seventy years of age must necessarily be.\n\nBut I do not write merely with the object of sending you such news as this: doubtless someone of your Mercuries will have seen and heard and reported so much; I write, as you usually do yourself, rather with a view to the future than to the past.\n\nRumour is already rife here as to Dr. Trefoil's successor, and among those named as possible future deans your humble servant is, I believe, not the least frequently spoken of; in short, I am looking for the preferment. You may probably know that since Bishop Proudie came to the diocese I have exerted myself here a good deal and, I may certainly say, not without some success. He and I are nearly always of the same opinion on points of doctrine as well as church discipline, and therefore I have had, as his confidential chaplain, very much in my own hands; but I confess to you that I have a higher ambition than to remain the chaplain of any bishop.\n\nThere are no positions in which more energy is now needed than those of our deans. The whole of our enormous cathedral establishments have been allowed to go to sleep--nay, they are all but dead and ready for the sepulchre! And yet of what prodigious moment they might be made if, as was intended, they were so managed as to lead the way and show an example for all our parochial clergy!\n\nThe bishop here is most anxious for my success; indeed, he goes to-morrow to press the matter on the archbishop. I believe also I may count on the support of at least one most effective member of the government. But I confess that the support of \"The Jupiter,\" if I be thought worthy of it, would be more gratifying to me than any other; more gratifying if by it I should be successful, and more gratifying also if, although so supported, I should be unsuccessful.\n\nThe time has, in fact, come in which no government can venture to fill up the high places of the Church in defiance of the public press. The age of honourable bishops and noble deans has gone by, and any clergyman however humbly born can now hope for success if his industry, talent, and character be sufficient to call forth the manifest opinion of the public in his favour.\n\nAt the present moment we all feel that any counsel given in such matters by \"The Jupiter\" has the greatest weight--is, indeed, generally followed; and we feel also--I am speaking of clergymen of my own age and standing--that it should be so. There can be no patron less interested than \"The Jupiter,\" and none that more thoroughly understands the wants of the people.\n\nI am sure you will not suspect me of asking from you any support which the paper with which you are connected cannot conscientiously give me. My object in writing is to let you know that I am a candidate for the appointment. It is for you to judge whether or no you can assist my views. I should not, of course, have written to you on such a matter had I not believed (and I have had good reason so to believe) that \"The Jupiter\" approves of my views on ecclesiastical polity.\n\nThe bishop expresses a fear that I may be considered too young for such a station, my age being thirty-six. I cannot think that at the present day any hesitation need be felt on such a point. The public has lost its love for antiquated servants. If a man will ever be fit to do good work, he will be fit at thirty-six years of age.\n\nBelieve me very faithfully yours, OBADIAH SLOPE\n\nT. TOWERS, ESQ., ---- Court, Middle Temple.\n\nHaving thus exerted himself, Mr. Slope posted his letters and passed the remainder of the evening at the feet of his mistress.\n\nMr. Slope will be accused of deceit in his mode of canvassing. It will be said that he lied in the application he made to each of his three patrons. I believe it must be owned that he did so. He could not hesitate on account of his youth and yet be quite assured that he was not too young. He could not count chiefly on the bishop's support and chiefly also on that of the newspaper. He did not think that the bishop was going to ---- to press the matter on the archbishop. It must be owned that in his canvassing Mr. Slope was as false as he well could be.\n\nLet it, however, be asked of those who are conversant with such matters, whether he was more false than men usually are on such occasions. We English gentlemen hate the name of a lie, but how often do we find public men who believe each other's words?\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII. Mrs. Proudie Victrix\n\nThe next week passed over at Barchester with much apparent tranquillity. The hearts, however, of some of the inhabitants were not so tranquil as the streets of the city. The poor old dean still continued to live, just as Sir Omicron Pie had prophesied that he would do, much to the amazement, and some thought disgust, of Dr. Fillgrave. The bishop still remained away. He had stayed a day or two in town and had also remained longer at the archbishop's than he had intended. Mr. Slope had as yet received no line in answer to either of his letters, but he had learnt the cause of this. Sir Nicholas was stalking a deer, or attending the Queen, in the Highlands, and even the indefatigable Mr. Towers had stolen an autumn holiday, and had made one of the yearly tribe who now ascend Mont Blanc. Mr. Slope learnt that he was not expected back till the last day of September.\n\nMrs. Bold was thrown much with the Stanhopes, of whom she became fonder and fonder. If asked, she would have said that Charlotte Stanhope was her especial friend, and so she would have thought. But, to tell the truth, she liked Bertie nearly as well; she had no more idea of regarding him as a lover than she would have had of looking at a big tame dog in such a light. Bertie had become very intimate with her, and made little speeches to her, and said little things of a sort very different from the speeches and sayings of other men. But then this was almost always done before his sisters; and he, with his long silken beard, his light blue eyes, and strange dress, was so unlike other men. She admitted him to a kind of familiarity which she had never known with anyone else, and of which she by no means understood the danger. She blushed once at finding that she had called him Bertie and, on the same day, only barely remembered her position in time to check herself from playing upon him some personal practical joke to which she was instigated by Charlotte.\n\nIn all this Eleanor was perfectly innocent, and Bertie Stanhope could hardly be called guilty. But every familiarity into which Eleanor was entrapped was deliberately planned by his sister. She knew well how to play her game, and played it without mercy; she knew, none so well, what was her brother's character, and she would have handed over to him the young widow, and the young widow's money, and the money of the widow's child, without remorse. With her pretended friendship and warm cordiality, she strove to connect Eleanor so closely with her brother as to make it impossible that she should go back even if she wished it. But Charlotte Stanhope knew really nothing of Eleanor's character, did not even understand that there were such characters. She did not comprehend that a young and pretty woman could be playful and familiar with a man such as Bertie Stanhope and yet have no idea in her head, no feeling in her heart, that she would have been ashamed to own to all the world. Charlotte Stanhope did not in the least conceive that her new friend was a woman whom nothing could entrap into an inconsiderate marriage, whose mind would have revolted from the slightest impropriety had she been aware that any impropriety existed.\n\nMiss Stanhope, however, had tact enough to make herself and her father's house very agreeable to Mrs. Bold. There was with them all an absence of stiffness and formality which was peculiarly agreeable to Eleanor after the great dose of clerical arrogance which she had lately been constrained to take. She played chess with them, walked with them, and drank tea with them; studied or pretended to study astronomy; assisted them in writing stories in rhyme, in turning prose tragedy into comic verse, or comic stories into would-be tragic poetry. She had no idea before that she had any such talents. She had not conceived the possibility of her doing such things as she now did. She found with the Stanhopes new amusements and employments, new pursuits, which in themselves could not be wrong, and which were exceedingly alluring.\n\nIs it not a pity that people who are bright and clever should so often be exceedingly improper, and that those who are never improper should so often be dull and heavy? Now Charlotte Stanhope was always bright and never heavy, but then her propriety was doubtful.\n\nBut during all this time Eleanor by no means forgot Mr. Arabin, nor did she forget Mr. Slope. She had parted from Mr. Arabin in her anger. She was still angry at what she regarded as his impertinent interference, but nevertheless she looked forward to meeting him again, and also looked forward to forgiving him. The words that Mr. Arabin had uttered still sounded in her ears. She knew that if not intended for a declaration of love, they did signify that he loved her, and she felt also that if he ever did make such a declaration, it might be that she should not receive it unkindly. She was still angry with him, very angry with him; so angry that she would bite her lip and stamp her foot as she thought of what he had said and done. Nevertheless, she yearned to let him know that he was forgiven; all that she required was that he should own that he had sinned.\n\nShe was to meet him at Ullathorne on the last day of the present month. Miss Thorne had invited all the country round to a breakfast on the lawn. There were to be tents, and archery, and dancing for the ladies on the lawn and for the swains and girls in the paddock. There were to be fiddlers and fifers, races for the boys, poles to be climbed, ditches full of water to be jumped over, horse-collars to be grinned through (this latter amusement was an addition of the stewards, and not arranged by Miss Thorne in the original programme), and every game to be played which, in a long course of reading, Miss Thorne could ascertain to have been played in the good days of Queen Elizabeth. Everything of more modern growth was to be tabooed, if possible. On one subject Miss Thorne was very unhappy. She had been turning in her mind the matter of a bull-ring, but could not succeed in making anything of it. She would not for the world have done, or allowed to be done, anything that was cruel; as to the promoting the torture of a bull for the amusement of her young neighbours, it need hardly be said that Miss Thorne would be the last to think of it. And yet there was something so charming in the name. A bull-ring, however, without a bull would only be a memento of the decadence of the times, and she felt herself constrained to abandon the idea. Quintains, however, she was determined to have, and had poles and swivels and bags of flour prepared accordingly. She would no doubt have been anxious for something small in the way of a tournament, but, as she said to her brother, that had been tried, and the age had proved itself too decidedly inferior to its forerunners to admit of such a pastime. Mr. Thorne did not seem to participate much in her regret, feeling perhaps that a full suit of chain-armour would have added but little to his own personal comfort.\n\nThis party at Ullathorne had been planned in the first place as a sort of welcoming to Mr. Arabin on his entrance into St. Ewold's parsonage; an intended harvest-home gala for the labourers and their wives and children had subsequently been amalgamated with it, and thus it had grown to its present dimensions. All the Plumstead party had of course been asked, and at the time of the invitation Eleanor had intended to have gone with her sister. Now her plans were altered, and she was going with the Stanhopes. The Proudies were also to be there, and, as Mr. Slope had not been included in the invitation to the palace, the signora, whose impudence never deserted her, asked permission of Miss Thorne to bring him.\n\nThis permission Miss Thorne gave, having no other alternative; but she did so with a trembling heart, fearing Mr. Arabin would be offended. Immediately on his return she apologized, almost with tears, so dire an enmity was presumed to rage between the two gentlemen. But Mr. Arabin comforted her by an assurance that he should meet Mr. Slope with the greatest pleasure imaginable and made her promise that she would introduce them to each other.\n\nBut this triumph of Mr. Slope's was not so agreeable to Eleanor, who since her return to Barchester had done her best to avoid him. She would not give way to the Plumstead folk when they so ungenerously accused her of being in love with this odious man; but, nevertheless, knowing that she was so accused, she was fully alive to the expediency of keeping out of his way and dropping him by degrees. She had seen very little of him since her return. Her servant had been instructed to say to all visitors that she was out. She could not bring herself to specify Mr. Slope particularly, and in order to avoid him she had thus debarred herself from all her friends. She had excepted Charlotte Stanhope and, by degrees, a few others also. Once she had met him at the Stanhopes', but as a rule, Mr. Slope's visits there were made in the morning and hers in the evening. On that one occasion Charlotte had managed to preserve her from any annoyance. This was very good-natured on the part of Charlotte, as Eleanor thought, and also very sharp-witted, as Eleanor had told her friend nothing of her reasons for wishing to avoid that gentleman. The fact, however, was that Charlotte had learnt from her sister that Mr. Slope would probably put himself forward as a suitor for the widow's hand, and she was consequently sufficiently alive to the expediency of guarding Bertie's future wife from any danger in that quarter.\n\nNevertheless the Stanhopes were pledged to take Mr. Slope with them to Ullathorne. An arrangement was therefore necessarily made, which was very disagreeable to Eleanor. Dr. Stanhope, with herself, Charlotte, and Mr. Slope, were to go together, and Bertie was to follow with his sister Madeline. It was clearly visible by Eleanor's face that this assortment was very disagreeable to her, and Charlotte, who was much encouraged thereby in her own little plan, made a thousand apologies.\n\n\"I see you don't like it, my dear,\" said she, \"but we could not manage otherwise. Bertie would give his eyes to go with you, but Madeline cannot possibly go without him. Nor could we possibly put Mr. Slope and Madeline in the same carriage without anyone else. They'd both be ruined forever, you know, and not admitted inside Ullathorne gates, I should imagine, after such an impropriety.\"\n\n\"Of course that wouldn't do,\" said Eleanor, \"but couldn't I go in the carriage with the signora and your brother?\"\n\n\"Impossible!\" said Charlotte. \"When she is there, there is only room for two.\" The Signora, in truth, did not care to do her travelling in the presence of strangers.\n\n\"Well, then,\" said Eleanor, \"you are all so kind, Charlotte, and so good to me that I am sure you won't be offended, but I think I'll not go at all.\"\n\n\"Not go at all!--what nonsense!--indeed you shall.\" It had been absolutely determined in family counsel that Bertie should propose on that very occasion.\n\n\"Or I can take a fly,\" said Eleanor. \"You know I am not embarrassed by so many difficulties as you young ladies; I can go alone.\"\n\n\"Nonsense, my dear! Don't think of such a thing; after all, it is only for an hour or so; and, to tell the truth, I don't know what it is you dislike so. I thought you and Mr. Slope were great friends. What is it you dislike?\"\n\n\"Oh, nothing particular,\" said Eleanor; \"only I thought it would be a family party.\"\n\n\"Of course it would be much nicer, much more snug, if Bertie could go with us. It is he that is badly treated. I can assure you he is much more afraid of Mr. Slope than you are. But you see Madeline cannot go out without him--and she, poor creature, goes out so seldom! I am sure you don't begrudge her this, though her vagary does knock about our own party a little.\"\n\nOf course Eleanor made a thousand protestations and uttered a thousand hopes that Madeline would enjoy herself. And of course she had to give way and undertake to go in the carriage with Mr. Slope. In fact, she was driven either to do this or to explain why she would not do so. Now she could not bring herself to explain to Charlotte Stanhope all that had passed at Plumstead.\n\nBut it was to her a sore necessity. She thought of a thousand little schemes for avoiding it; she would plead illness and not go at all; she would persuade Mary Bold to go, although not asked, and then make a necessity of having a carriage of her own to take her sister-in-law; anything, in fact, she could do, rather than be seen by Mr. Arabin getting out of the same carriage with Mr. Slope. However, when the momentous morning came, she had no scheme matured, and then Mr. Slope handed her into Dr. Stanhope's carriage and, following her steps, sat opposite to her.\n\nThe bishop returned on the eve of the Ullathorne party, and was received at home with radiant smiles by the partner of all his cares. On his arrival he crept up to his dressing-room with somewhat of a palpitating heart; he had overstayed his alloted time by three days, and was not without much fear of penalties. Nothing, however, could be more affectionately cordial than the greeting he received; the girls came out and kissed him in a manner that was quite soothing to his spirit; and Mrs. Proudie, \"albeit, unused to the melting mood,\" squeezed him in her arms and almost in words called him her dear, darling, good, pet, little bishop. All this was a very pleasant surprise.\n\nMrs. Proudie had somewhat changed her tactics; not that she had seen any cause to disapprove of her former line of conduct, but she had now brought matters to such a point that she calculated that she might safely do so. She had got the better of Mr. Slope, and she now thought well to show her husband that when allowed to get the better of everybody, when obeyed by him and permitted to rule over others, she would take care that he should have his reward. Mr. Slope had not a chance against her; not only could she stun the poor bishop by her midnight anger, but she could assuage and soothe him, if she so willed, by daily indulgences. She could furnish his room for him, turn him out as smart a bishop as any on the bench, give him good dinners, warm fires, and an easy life--all this she would do if he would but be quietly obedient. But, if not,--! To speak sooth, however, his sufferings on that dreadful night had been so poignant as to leave him little spirit for further rebellion.\n\nAs soon as he had dressed himself, she returned to his room. \"I hope you enjoyed yourself at ----,\" said she, seating herself on one side of the fire while he remained in his armchair on the other, stroking the calves of his legs. It was the first time he had had a fire in his room since the summer, and it pleased him, for the good bishop loved to be warm and cosy. Yes, he said, he had enjoyed himself very much. Nothing could be more polite than the archbishop, and Mrs. Archbishop had been equally charming.\n\nMrs. Proudie was delighted to hear it; nothing, she declared, pleased her so much as to think\n\nHer bairn respectit like the lave.\n\nShe did not put it precisely in these words, but what she said came to the same thing; and then, having petted and fondled her little man sufficiently, she proceeded to business.\n\n\"The poor dean is still alive,\" said she.\n\n\"So I hear, so I hear,\" said the bishop. \"I'll go to the deanery directly after breakfast to-morrow.\"\n\n\"We are going to this party at Ullathorne to-morrow morning, my dear; we must be there early, you know--by twelve o'clock I suppose.\"\n\n\"Oh--ah!\" said the bishop; \"then I'll certainly call the next day.\"\n\n\"Was much said about it at ----?\" asked Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"About what?\" said the bishop.\n\n\"Filling up the dean's place,\" said Mrs. Proudie. As she spoke, a spark of the wonted fire returned to her eye, and the bishop felt himself to be a little less comfortable than before.\n\n\"Filling up the dean's place; that is, if the dean dies? Very little, my dear. It was mentioned, just mentioned.\"\n\n\"And what did you say about it, Bishop?\"\n\n\"Why, I said that I thought that if, that is, should--should the dean die, that is, I said I thought--\" As he went on stammering and floundering, he saw that his wife's eye was fixed sternly on him. Why should he encounter such evil for a man whom he loved so slightly as Mr. Slope? Why should he give up his enjoyments and his ease and such dignity as might be allowed to him to fight a losing battle for a chaplain? The chaplain, after all, if successful, would be as great a tyrant as his wife. Why fight at all? Why contend? Why be uneasy? From that moment he determined to fling Mr. Slope to the winds and take the goods the gods provided.\n\n\"I am told,\" said Mrs. Proudie, speaking very slowly, \"that Mr. Slope is looking to be the new dean.\"\n\n\"Yes--certainly, I believe he is,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"And what does the archbishop say about that?\" asked Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"Well, my dear, to tell the truth, I promised Mr. Slope to speak to the archbishop. Mr. Slope spoke to me about it. It is very arrogant of him, I must say--but that is nothing to me.\"\n\n\"Arrogant!\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"it is the most impudent piece of pretension I ever heard of in my life. Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester, indeed! And what did you do in the matter, Bishop?\"\n\n\"Why, my dear, I did speak to the archbishop.\"\n\n\"You don't mean to tell me,\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"that you are going to make yourself ridiculous by lending your name to such a preposterous attempt as this? Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester, indeed!\" And she tossed her head and put her arms akimbo with an air of confident defiance that made her husband quite sure that Mr. Slope never would be Dean of Barchester. In truth, Mrs. Proudie was all but invincible; had she married Petruchio, it may be doubted whether that arch wife-tamer would have been able to keep her legs out of those garments which are presumed by men to be peculiarly unfitted for feminine use.\n\n\"It is preposterous, my dear.\"\n\n\"Then why have you endeavoured to assist him?\"\n\n\"Why--my dear, I haven't assisted him--much.\"\n\n\"But why have you done it at all? Why have you mixed your name up in anything so ridiculous? What was it you did say to the archbishop?\"\n\n\"Why, I just did mention it; I just did say that--that in the event of the poor dean's death, Mr. Slope would--would--\"\n\n\"Would what?\"\n\n\"I forget how I put it--would take it if he could get it; something of that sort. I didn't say much more than that.\"\n\n\"You shouldn't have said anything at all. And what did the archbishop say?\"\n\n\"He didn't say anything; he just bowed and rubbed his hands. Somebody else came up at the moment, and as we were discussing the new parochial universal school committee, the matter of the new dean dropped; after that I didn't think it wise to renew it.\"\n\n\"Renew it! I am very sorry you ever mentioned it. What will the archbishop think of you?\"\n\n\"You may be sure, my dear, the archbishop thought very little about it.\"\n\n\"But why did you think about it, Bishop? How could you think of making such a creature as that Dean of Barchester? Dean of Barchester! I suppose he'll be looking for a bishopric some of these days--a man that hardly knows who his own father was; a man that I found without bread to his mouth or a coat to his back. Dean of Barchester, indeed! I'll dean him.\"\n\nMrs. Proudie considered herself to be in politics a pure Whig; all her family belonged to the Whig party. Now, among all ranks of Englishmen and Englishwomen (Mrs. Proudie should, I think, be ranked among the former on the score of her great strength of mind), no one is so hostile to lowly born pretenders to high station as the pure Whig.\n\nThe bishop thought it necessary to exculpate himself. \"Why, my dear,\" said he, \"it appeared to me that you and Mr. Slope did not get on quite so well as you used to do!\"\n\n\"Get on!\" said Mrs. Proudie, moving her foot uneasily on the hearth-rug and compressing her lips in a manner that betokened much danger to the subject of their discourse.\n\n\"I began to find that he was objectionable to you\"--Mrs. Proudie's foot worked on the hearth-rug with great rapidity--\"and that you would be more comfortable if he was out of the palace\"--Mrs. Proudie smiled, as a hyena may probably smile before he begins his laugh--\"and therefore I thought that if he got this place, and so ceased to be my chaplain, you might be pleased at such an arrangement.\"\n\nAnd then the hyena laughed out. Pleased at such an arrangement! Pleased at having her enemy converted into a dean with twelve hundred a year! Medea, when she describes the customs of her native country (I am quoting from Robson's edition), assures her astonished auditor that in her land captives, when taken, are eaten.\n\n\"You pardon them?\" says Medea.\n\n\"We do indeed,\" says the mild Grecian.\n\n\"We eat them!\" says she of Colchis, with terrific energy.\n\nMrs. Proudie was the Medea of Barchester; she had no idea of not eating Mr. Slope. Pardon him! Merely get rid of him! Make a dean of him! It was not so they did with their captives in her country, among people of her sort! Mr. Slope had no such mercy to expect; she would pick him to the very last bone.\n\n\"Oh, yes, my dear, of course he'll cease to be your chaplain,\" said she. \"After what has passed, that must be a matter of course. I couldn't for a moment think of living in the same house with such a man. Besides, he has shown himself quite unfit for such a situation; making broils and quarrels among the clergy; getting you, my dear, into scrapes; and taking upon himself as though he were as good as bishop himself. Of course he'll go. But because he leaves the palace, that is no reason why he should get into the deanery.\"\n\n\"Oh, of course not!\" said the bishop; \"but to save appearances, you know, my dear--\"\n\n\"I don't want to save appearances; I want Mr. Slope to appear just what he is--a false, designing, mean, intriguing man. I have my eye on him; he little knows what I see. He is misconducting himself in the most disgraceful way with that lame Italian woman. That family is a disgrace to Barchester, and Mr. Slope is a disgrace to Barchester. If he doesn't look well to it, he'll have his gown stripped off his back instead of having a dean's hat on his head. Dean, indeed! The man has gone mad with arrogance.\"\n\nThe bishop said nothing further to excuse either himself or his chaplain, and having shown himself passive and docile, was again taken into favour. They soon went to dinner, and he spent the pleasantest evening he had had in his own house for a long time. His daughter played and sang to him as he sipped his coffee and read his newspaper, and Mrs. Proudie asked good-natured little questions about the archbishop; and then he went happily to bed and slept as quietly as though Mrs. Proudie had been Griselda herself. While shaving himself in the morning and preparing for the festivities of Ullathorne, he fully resolved to run no more tilts against a warrior so fully armed at all points as was Mrs. Proudie.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV. Oxford--The Master and Tutor of Lazarus\n\nMr. Arabin, as we have said, had but a sad walk of it under the trees of Plumstead churchyard. He did not appear to any of the family till dinner-time, and then he seemed, as far as their judgement went, to be quite himself. He had, as was his wont, asked himself a great many questions and given himself a great many answers; and the upshot of this was that he had sent himself down for an ass. He had determined that he was much too old and much too rusty to commence the manoeuvres of love-making; that he had let the time slip through his hands which should have been used for such purposes; and that now he must lie on his bed as he had made it. Then he asked himself whether in truth he did love this woman; and he answered himself, not without a long struggle, but at last honestly, that he certainly did love her. He then asked himself whether he did not also love her money, and he again answered himself that he did so. But here he did not answer honestly. It was and ever had been his weakness to look for impure motives for his own conduct. No doubt, circumstanced as he was, with a small living and a fellowship, accustomed as he had been to collegiate luxuries and expensive comforts, he might have hesitated to marry a penniless woman had he felt ever so strong a predilection for the woman herself; no doubt Eleanor's fortune put all such difficulties out of the question; but it was equally without doubt that his love for her had crept upon him without the slightest idea on his part that he could ever benefit his own condition by sharing her wealth.\n\nWhen he had stood on the hearth-rug, counting the pattern and counting also the future chances of his own life, the remembrances of Mrs. Bold's comfortable income had certainly not damped his first assured feeling of love for her. And why should it have done so? Need it have done so with the purest of men? Be that as it may, Mr. Arabin decided against himself; he decided that it had done so in his case, and that he was not the purest of men.\n\nHe also decided, which was more to his purpose, that Eleanor did not care a straw for him, and that very probably she did care a straw for his rival. Then he made up his mind not to think of her any more, and went on thinking of her till he was almost in a state to drown himself in the little brook which ran at the bottom of the archdeacon's grounds.\n\nAnd ever and again his mind would revert to the Signora Neroni, and he would make comparisons between her and Eleanor Bold, not always in favour of the latter. The signora had listened to him, and flattered him, and believed in him; at least she had told him so. Mrs. Bold had also listened to him, but had never flattered him; had not always believed in him; and now had broken from him in violent rage. The signora, too, was the more lovely woman of the two, and had also the additional attraction of her affliction--for to him it was an attraction.\n\nBut he never could have loved the Signora Neroni as he felt that he now loved Eleanor; and so he flung stones into the brook, instead of flinging in himself, and sat down on its margin as sad a gentleman as you shall meet in a summer's day.\n\nHe heard the dinner-bell ring from the churchyard, and he knew that it was time to recover his self-possession. He felt that he was disgracing himself in his own eyes, that he had been idling his time and neglecting the high duties which he had taken upon himself to perform. He should have spent this afternoon among the poor at St. Ewold's, instead of wandering about at Plumstead, an ancient, love-lorn swain, dejected and sighing, full of imaginary sorrows and Wertherian grief. He was thoroughly ashamed of himself, and determined to lose no time in retrieving his character, so damaged in his own eyes.\n\nThus when he appeared at dinner he was as animated as ever and was the author of most of the conversation which graced the archdeacon's board on that evening. Mr. Harding was ill at ease and sick at heart, and did not care to appear more comfortable than he really was; what little he did say was said to his daughter. He thought that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin had leagued together against Eleanor's comfort, and his wish now was to break away from the pair and undergo in his Barchester lodgings whatever Fate had in store for him. He hated the name of the hospital; his attempt to regain his lost inheritance there had brought upon him so much suffering. As far as he was concerned, Mr. Quiverful was now welcome to the place.\n\nAnd the archdeacon was not very lively. The poor dean's illness was of course discussed in the first place. Dr. Grantly did not mention Mr. Slope's name in connexion with the expected event of Dr. Trefoil's death; he did not wish to say anything about Mr. Slope just at present, nor did he wish to make known his sad surmises; but the idea that his enemy might possibly become Dean of Barchester made him very gloomy. Should such an event take place, such a dire catastrophe come about, there would be an end to his life as far as his life was connected with the city of Barchester. He must give up all his old haunts, all his old habits, and live quietly as a retired rector at Plumstead. It had been a severe trial for him to have Dr. Proudie in the palace, but with Mr. Slope also in the deanery he felt that he should be unable to draw his breath in Barchester close.\n\nThus it came to pass that in spite of the sorrow at his heart, Mr. Arabin was apparently the gayest of the party. Both Mr. Harding and Mrs. Grantly were in a slight degree angry with him on account of his want of gloom. To the one it appeared as though he were triumphing at Eleanor's banishment, and to the other that he was not affected as he should have been by all the sad circumstances of the day--Eleanor's obstinacy, Mr. Slope's success, and the poor dean's apoplexy. And so they were all at cross-purposes.\n\nMr. Harding left the room almost together with the ladies, and then the archdeacon opened his heart to Mr. Arabin. He still harped upon the hospital. \"What did that fellow mean,\" said he, \"by saying in his letter to Mrs. Bold that if Mr. Harding would call on the bishop, it would be all right? Of course I would not be guided by anything he might say, but still it may be well that Mr. Harding should see the bishop. It would be foolish to let the thing slip through our fingers because Mrs. Bold is determined to make a fool of herself.\"\n\nMr. Arabin hinted that he was not quite so sure that Mrs. Bold would make a fool of herself. He said that he was not convinced that she did regard Mr. Slope so warmly as she was supposed to do. The archdeacon questioned and cross-questioned him about this, but elicited nothing, and at last remained firm in his own conviction that he was destined, malgré lui, to be the brother-in-law of Mr. Slope. Mr. Arabin strongly advised that Mr. Harding should take no step regarding the hospital in connexion with, or in consequence of, Mr. Slope's letter. \"If the bishop really means to confer the appointment on Mr. Harding,\" argued Mr. Arabin, \"he will take care to let him have some other intimation than a message conveyed through a letter to a lady. Were Mr. Harding to present himself at the palace, he might merely be playing Mr. Slope's game;\" and thus it was settled that nothing should be done till the great Dr. Gwynne's arrival, or at any rate without that potentate's sanction.\n\nIt was droll to observe how these men talked of Mr. Harding as though he were a puppet, and planned their intrigues and small ecclesiastical manoeuvres in reference to Mr. Harding's future position without dreaming of taking him into their confidence. There was a comfortable house and income in question, and it was very desirable, and certainly very just, that Mr. Harding should have them; but that at present was not the main point; it was expedient to beat the bishop and, if possible, to smash Mr. Slope. Mr. Slope had set up, or was supposed to have set up, a rival candidate. Of all things the most desirable would have been to have had Mr. Quiverful's appointment published to the public and then annulled by the clamour of an indignant world, loud in the defence of Mr. Harding's rights. But of such an event the chance was small; a slight fraction only of the world would be indignant, and that fraction would be one not accustomed to loud speaking. And then the preferment had, in a sort of way, been offered to Mr. Harding and had, in a sort of way, been refused by him.\n\nMr. Slope's wicked, cunning hand had been peculiarly conspicuous in the way in which this had been brought to pass, and it was the success of Mr. Slope's cunning which was so painfully grating to the feelings of the archdeacon. That which of all things he most dreaded was that he should be outgeneralled by Mr. Slope; and just at present it appeared probable that Mr. Slope would turn his flank, steal a march on him, cut off his provisions, carry his strong town by a coup de main, and at last beat him thoroughly in a regular pitched battle. The archdeacon felt that his flank had been turned when desired to wait on Mr. Slope instead of the bishop, that a march had been stolen when Mr. Harding was induced to refuse the bishop's offer, that his provisions would be cut off when Mr. Quiverful got the hospital, that Eleanor was the strong town doomed to be taken, and that Mr. Slope, as Dean of Barchester, would be regarded by all the world as conqueror in the final conflict.\n\nDr. Gwynne was the Deus ex machina who was to come down upon the Barchester stage and bring about deliverance from these terrible evils. But how can melodramatic dénouements be properly brought about, how can vice and Mr. Slope be punished, and virtue and the archdeacon be rewarded, while the avenging god is laid up with the gout? In the mean time evil may be triumphant, and poor innocence, transfixed to the earth by an arrow from Dr. Proudie's quiver, may lie dead upon the ground, not to be resuscitated even by Dr. Gwynne.\n\nTwo or three days after Eleanor's departure, Mr. Arabin went to Oxford and soon found himself closeted with the august head of his college. It was quite clear that Dr. Gwynne was not very sanguine as to the effects of his journey to Barchester, and not over-anxious to interfere with the bishop. He had had the gout, but was very nearly convalescent, and Mr. Arabin at once saw that had the mission been one of which the master thoroughly approved, he would before this have been at Plumstead.\n\nAs it was, Dr. Gwynne was resolved on visiting his friend, and willingly promised to return to Barchester with Mr. Arabin. He could not bring himself to believe that there was any probability that Mr. Slope would be made Dean of Barchester. Rumour, he said, had reached even his ears, not at all favourable to that gentleman's character, and he expressed himself strongly of opinion that any such appointment was quite out of the question. At this stage of the proceedings, the master's right-hand man, Tom Staple, was called in to assist at the conference. Tom Staple was the Tutor of Lazarus and, moreover, a great man at Oxford. Though universally known by a species of nomenclature so very undignified, Tom Staple was one who maintained a high dignity in the university. He was, as it were, the leader of the Oxford tutors, a body of men who consider themselves collectively as being by very little, if at all, second in importance to the heads themselves. It is not always the case that the master, or warden, or provost, or principal can hit it off exactly with his tutor. A tutor is by no means indisposed to have a will of his own. But at Lazarus they were great friends and firm allies at the time of which we are writing.\n\nTom Staple was a hale, strong man of about forty-five, short in stature, swarthy in face, with strong, sturdy black hair and crisp black beard of which very little was allowed to show itself in shape of whiskers. He always wore a white neckcloth, clean indeed, but not tied with that scrupulous care which now distinguishes some of our younger clergy. He was, of course, always clothed in a seemly suit of solemn black. Mr. Staple was a decent cleanly liver, not over-addicted to any sensuality; but nevertheless a somewhat warmish hue was beginning to adorn his nose, the peculiar effect, as his friends averred, of a certain pipe of port introduced into the cellars of Lazarus the very same year in which the tutor entered it as a freshman. There was also, perhaps, a little redolence of port wine, as it were the slightest possible twang, in Mr. Staple's voice.\n\nIn these latter days Tom Staple was not a happy man; university reform had long been his bugbear, and now was his bane. It was not with him, as with most others, an affair of politics, respecting which, when the need existed, he could, for parties' sake or on behalf of principle, maintain a certain amount of necessary zeal; it was not with him a subject for dilettante warfare and courteous, commonplace opposition. To him it was life and death. The status quo of the university was his only idea of life, and any reformation was as bad to him as death. He would willingly have been a martyr in the cause, had the cause admitted of martyrdom.\n\nAt the present day, unfortunately, public affairs will allow of no martyrs, and therefore it is that there is such a deficiency of zeal. Could gentlemen of £10,000 a year have died on their own door-steps in defence of protection, no doubt some half-dozen glorious old baronets would have so fallen, and the school of protection would at this day have been crowded with scholars. Who can fight strenuously in any combat in which there is no danger? Tom Staple would have willingly been impaled before a Committee of the House, could he by such self-sacrifice have infused his own spirit into the component members of the hebdomadal board.\n\nTom Staple was one of those who in his heart approved of the credit system which had of old been in vogue between the students and tradesmen of the university. He knew and acknowledged to himself that it was useless in these degenerate days publicly to contend with \"The Jupiter\" on such a subject. \"The Jupiter\" had undertaken to rule the university, and Tom Staple was well aware that \"The Jupiter\" was too powerful for him. But in secret, and among his safe companions, he would argue that the system of credit was an ordeal good for young men to undergo.\n\nThe bad men, said he, the weak and worthless, blunder into danger and burn their feet; but the good men, they who have any character, they who have that within them which can reflect credit on their alma mater, they come through scatheless. What merit will there be to a young man to get through safely, if he be guarded and protected and restrained like a schoolboy? By so doing, the period of the ordeal is only postponed, and the manhood of the man will be deferred from the age of twenty to that of twenty-four. If you bind him with leading-strings at college, he will break loose while eating for the bar in London; bind him there, and he will break loose afterwards, when he is a married man. The wild oats must be sown somewhere. 'Twas thus that Tom Staple would argue of young men, not, indeed, with much consistency, but still with some practical knowledge of the subject gathered from long experience.\n\nAnd now Tom Staple proffered such wisdom as he had for the assistance of Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"Quite out of the question,\" said he, arguing that Mr. Slope could not possibly be made the new Dean of Barchester.\n\n\"So I think,\" said the master. \"He has no standing, and, if all I hear be true, very little character.\"\n\n\"As to character,\" said Tom Staple, \"I don't think much of that. They rather like loose parsons for deans; a little fast living, or a dash of infidelity, is no bad recommendation to a cathedral close. But they couldn't make Mr. Slope; the last two deans have been Cambridge men; you'll not show me an instance of their making three men running from the same university. We don't get our share and never shall, I suppose, but we must at least have one out of three.\"\n\n\"Those sort of rules are all gone by now,\" said Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"Everything has gone by, I believe,\" said Tom Staple. \"The cigar has been smoked out, and we are the ashes.\"\n\n\"Speak for yourself, Staple,\" said the master.\n\n\"I speak for all,\" said the tutor stoutly. \"It is coming to that, that there will be no life left anywhere in the country. No one is any longer fit to rule himself, or those belonging to him. The Government is to find us all in everything, and the press is to find the Government. Nevertheless, Mr. Slope won't be Dean of Barchester.\"\n\n\"And who will be warden of the hospital?\" said Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"I hear that Mr. Quiverful is already appointed,\" said Tom Staple.\n\n\"I think not,\" said the master. \"And I think, moreover, that Dr. Proudie will not be so short-sighted as to run against such a rock: Mr. Slope should himself have sense enough to prevent it.\"\n\n\"But perhaps Mr. Slope may have no objection to see his patron on a rock,\" said the suspicious tutor.\n\n\"What could he get by that?\" asked Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"It is impossible to see the doubles of such a man,\" said Mr. Staple. \"It seems quite clear that Bishop Proudie is altogether in his hands, and it is equally clear that he has been moving heaven and earth to get this Mr. Quiverful into the hospital, although he must know that such an appointment would be most damaging to the bishop. It is impossible to understand such a man, and dreadful to think,\" added Tom Staple, sighing deeply, \"that the welfare and fortunes of good men may depend on his intrigues.\"\n\nDr. Gwynne or Mr. Staple were not in the least aware, nor even was Mr. Arabin, that this Mr. Slope, of whom they were talking, had been using his utmost efforts to put their own candidate into the hospital, and that in lieu of being permanent in the palace, his own expulsion therefrom had been already decided on by the high powers of the diocese.\n\n\"I'll tell you what,\" said the tutor, \"if this Quiverful is thrust into the hospital and Dr. Trefoil does die, I should not wonder if the Government were to make Mr. Harding Dean of Barchester. They would feel bound to do something for him after all that was said when he resigned.\"\n\nDr. Gwynne at the moment made no reply to this suggestion, but it did not the less impress itself on his mind. If Mr. Harding could not be warden of the hospital, why should he not be Dean of Barchester?\n\nAnd so the conference ended without any very fixed resolution, and Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Arabin prepared for their journey to Plumstead on the morrow.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV. Miss Thorne's Fête Champêtre\n\nThe day of the Ullathorne party arrived, and all the world were there--or at least so much of the world as had been included in Miss Thorne's invitation. As we have said, the bishop returned home on the previous evening, and on the same evening and by the same train came Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Arabin from Oxford. The archdeacon with his brougham was in waiting for the Master of Lazarus, so that there was a goodly show of church dignitaries on the platform of the railway.\n\nThe Stanhope party was finally arranged in the odious manner already described, and Eleanor got into the doctor's carriage full of apprehension and presentiment of further misfortune, whereas Mr. Slope entered the vehicle elate with triumph.\n\nHe had received that morning a very civil note from Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, not promising much, indeed, but then Mr. Slope knew, or fancied that he knew, that it was not etiquette for government officers to make promises. Though Sir Nicholas promised nothing he implied a good deal, declared his conviction that Mr. Slope would make an excellent dean, and wished him every kind of success. To be sure he added that, not being in the cabinet, he was never consulted on such matters, and that even if he spoke on the subject, his voice would go for nothing. But all this Mr. Slope took for the prudent reserve of official life. To complete his anticipated triumphs, another letter was brought to him just as he was about to start to Ullathorne.\n\nMr. Slope also enjoyed the idea of handing Mrs. Bold out of Dr. Stanhope's carriage before the multitude at Ullathorne gate as much as Eleanor dreaded the same ceremony. He had fully made up his mind to throw himself and his fortune at the widow's feet, and had almost determined to select the present propitious morning for doing so. The signora had of late been less than civil to him. She had indeed admitted his visits and listened, at any rate without anger, to his love, but she had tortured him and reviled him, jeered at him and ridiculed him, while she allowed him to call her the most beautiful of living women, to kiss her hand, and to proclaim himself with reiterated oaths her adorer, her slave and worshipper.\n\nMiss Thorne was in great perturbation, yet in great glory, on the morning of the gala day. Mr. Thorne also, though the party was none of his giving, had much heavy work on his hands. But perhaps the most overtasked, the most anxious, and the most effective of all the Ullathorne household was Mr. Plomacy, the steward. This last personage had, in the time of Mr. Thorne's father, when the Directory held dominion in France, gone over to Paris with letters in his boot-heel for some of the royal party, and such had been his good luck that he had returned safe. He had then been very young and was now very old, but the exploit gave him a character for political enterprise and secret discretion which still availed him as thoroughly as it had done in its freshest gloss. Mr. Plomacy had been steward of Ullathorne for more than fifty years, and a very easy life he had had of it. Who could require much absolute work from a man who had carried safely at his heel that which, if discovered, would have cost him his head? Consequently Mr. Plomacy had never worked hard, and of latter years had never worked at all. He had a taste for timber, and therefore he marked the trees that were to be cut down; he had a taste for gardening, and would therefore allow no shrub to be planted or bed to be made without his express sanction. In these matters he was sometimes driven to run counter to his mistress, but he rarely allowed his mistress to carry the point against him.\n\nBut on occasions such as the present Mr. Plomacy came out strong. He had the honour of the family at heart; he thoroughly appreciated the duties of hospitality; and therefore, when gala doings were going on, he always took the management into his own hands and reigned supreme over master and mistress.\n\nTo give Mr. Plomacy his due, old as he was, he thoroughly understood such work as he had in hand, and did it well.\n\nThe order of the day was to be as follows. The quality, as the upper classes in rural districts are designated by the lower with so much true discrimination, were to eat a breakfast, and the non-quality were to eat a dinner. Two marquees had been erected for these two banquets: that for the quality on the esoteric or garden side of a certain deep ha-ha; and that for the non-quality on the exoteric or paddock side of the same. Both were of huge dimensions--that on the outer side was, one may say, on an egregious scale--but Mr. Plomacy declared that neither would be sufficient. To remedy this, an auxiliary banquet was prepared in the dining-room, and a subsidiary board was to be spread sub dio for the accommodation of the lower class of yokels on the Ullathorne property.\n\nNo one who has not had a hand in the preparation of such an affair can understand the manifold difficulties which Miss Thorne encountered in her project. Had she not been made throughout of the very finest whalebone, riveted with the best Yorkshire steel, she must have sunk under them. Had not Mr. Plomacy felt how much was justly expected from a man who at one time carried the destinies of Europe in his boot, he would have given way, and his mistress, so deserted, must have perished among her poles and canvas.\n\nIn the first place there was a dreadful line to be drawn. Who were to dispose themselves within the ha-ha, and who without? To this the unthinking will give an off-hand answer, as they will to every ponderous question. Oh, the bishop and such-like within the ha-ha, and Farmer Greenacre and such-like without. True, my unthinking friend, but who shall define these such-likes? It is in such definitions that the whole difficulty of society consists. To seat the bishop on an arm-chair on the lawn and place Farmer Greenacre at the end of a long table in the paddock is easy enough, but where will you put Mrs. Lookaloft, whose husband, though a tenant on the estate, hunts in a red coat, whose daughters go to a fashionable seminary in Barchester, who calls her farm-house Rosebank, and who has a pianoforte in her drawing-room? The Misses Lookaloft, as they call themselves, won't sit contented among the bumpkins. Mrs. Lookaloft won't squeeze her fine clothes on a bench and talk familiarly about cream and ducklings to good Mrs. Greenacre. And yet Mrs. Lookaloft is no fit companion and never has been the associate of the Thornes and the Grantlys. And if Mrs. Lookaloft be admitted within the sanctum of fashionable life, if she be allowed with her three daughters to leap the ha-ha, why not the wives and daughters of other families also? Mrs. Greenacre is at present well contented with the paddock, but she might cease to be so if she saw Mrs. Lookaloft on the lawn. And thus poor Miss Thorne had a hard time of it.\n\nAnd how was she to divide her guests between the marquee and the parlour? She had a countess coming, an Honourable John and an Honourable George, and a whole bevy of Ladies Amelia, Rosina, Margaretta, &c; she had a leash of baronets with their baronettes; and, as we all know, she had a bishop. If she put them on the lawn, no one would go into the parlour; if she put them into the parlour, no one would go into the tent. She thought of keeping the old people in the house and leaving the lawn to the lovers. She might as well have seated herself at once in a hornet's nest. Mr. Plomacy knew better than this. \"Bless your soul, ma'am,\" said he, \"there won't be no old ladies--not one, barring yourself and old Mrs. Clantantram.\"\n\nPersonally Miss Thorne accepted this distinction in her favour as a compliment to her good sense, but nevertheless she had no desire to be closeted on the coming occasion with Mrs. Clantantram. She gave up all idea of any arbitrary division of her guests and determined if possible to put the bishop on the lawn and the countess in the house, to sprinkle the baronets, and thus divide the attractions. What to do with the Lookalofts even Mr. Plomacy could not decide. They must take their chance. They had been specially told in the invitation that all the tenants had been invited, and they might probably have the good sense to stay away if they objected to mix with the rest of the tenantry.\n\nThen Mr. Plomacy declared his apprehension that the Honourable Johns and Honourable Georges would come in a sort of amphibious costume, half-morning, half-evening, satin neck-handkerchiefs, frock-coats, primrose gloves, and polished boots; and that, being so dressed, they would decline riding at the quintain, or taking part in any of the athletic games which Miss Thorne had prepared with so much fond care. If the Lord Johns and Lord Georges didn't ride at the quintain, Miss Thorne might be sure that nobody else would.\n\n\"But,\" said she in dolorous voice, all but overcome by her cares, \"it was specially signified that there were to be sports.\"\n\n\"And so there will be, of course,\" said Mr. Plomacy. \"They'll all be sporting with the young ladies in the laurel walks. Them's the sports they care most about now-a-days. If you gets the young men at the quintain, you'll have all the young women in the pouts.\"\n\n\"Can't they look on as their great grandmothers did before them?\" said Miss Thorne.\n\n\"It seems to me that the ladies ain't contented with looking now-a-days. Whatever the men do they'll do. If you'll have side-saddles on the nags; and let them go at the quintain too, it'll answer capital, no doubt.\"\n\nMiss Thorne made no reply. She felt that she had no good ground on which to defend her sex of the present generation from the sarcasm of Mr. Plomacy. She had once declared, in one of her warmer moments, \"that now-a-days the gentlemen were all women, and the ladies all men.\" She could not alter the debased character of the age. But, such being the case, why should she take on herself to cater for the amusement of people of such degraded tastes? This question she asked herself more than once, and she could only answer herself with a sigh. There was her own brother Wilfred, on whose shoulders rested all the ancient honours of Ullathorne house; it was very doubtful whether even he would consent to \"go at the quintain,\" as Mr. Plomacy not injudiciously expressed it.\n\nAnd now the morning arrived. The Ullathorne household was early on the move. Cooks were cooking in the kitchen long before daylight, and men were dragging out tables and hammering red baize on to benches at the earliest dawn. With what dread eagerness did Miss Thorne look out at the weather as soon as the parting veil of night permitted her to look at all! In this respect, at any rate, there was nothing to grieve her. The glass had been rising for the last three days, and the morning broke with that dull, chill, steady, grey haze which in autumn generally presages a clear and dry day. By seven she was dressed and down. Miss Thorne knew nothing of the modern luxury of déshabilles. She would as soon have thought of appearing before her brother without her stockings as without her stays--and Miss Thorne's stays were no trifle.\n\nAnd yet there was nothing for her to do when down. She fidgeted out to the lawn and then back into the kitchen. She put on her high-heeled clogs and fidgeted out into the paddock. Then she went into the small home park where the quintain was erected. The pole and cross-bar and the swivel and the target and the bag of flour were all complete. She got up on a carpenter's bench and touched the target with her hand; it went round with beautiful ease; the swivel had been oiled to perfection. She almost wished to take old Plomacy at his word, to get on a side-saddle and have a tilt at it herself. What must a young man be, thought she, who could prefer maundering among laurel trees with a wishy-washy school-girl to such fun as this? \"Well,\" said she aloud to herself, \"one man can take a horse to water, but a thousand can't make him drink. There it is. If they haven't the spirit to enjoy it, the fault shan't be mine;\" and so she returned to the house.\n\nAt a little after eight her brother came down, and they had a sort of scrap breakfast in his study. The tea was made without the customary urn, and they dispensed with the usual rolls and toast. Eggs also were missing, for every egg in the parish had been whipped into custards, baked into pies, or boiled into lobster salad. The allowance of fresh butter was short, and Mr. Thorne was obliged to eat the leg of a fowl without having it devilled in the manner he loved.\n\n\"I have been looking at the quintain, Wilfred,\" said she, \"and it appears to be quite right.\"\n\n\"Oh--ah, yes,\" said he. \"It seemed to be so yesterday when I saw it.\" Mr. Thorne was beginning to be rather bored by his sister's love of sports, and had especially no affection for this quintain post.\n\n\"I wish you'd just try it after breakfast,\" said she. \"You could have the saddle put on Mark Antony, and the pole is there all handy. You can take the flour bag off, you know, if you think Mark Antony won't be quick enough,\" added Miss Thorne, seeing that her brother's countenance was not indicative of complete accordance with her little proposition.\n\nNow Mark Antony was a valuable old hunter, excellently suited to Mr. Thorne's usual requirements, steady indeed at his fences, but extremely sure, very good in deep ground, and safe on the roads. But he had never yet been ridden at a quintain, and Mr. Thorne was not inclined to put him to the trial, either with or without the bag of flour. He hummed and hawed and finally declared that he was afraid Mark Antony would shy.\n\n\"Then try the cob,\" said the indefatigable Miss Thorne.\n\n\"He's in physic,\" said Wilfred.\n\n\"There's the Beelzebub colt,\" said his sister. \"I know he's in the stable because I saw Peter exercising him just now.\"\n\n\"My dear Monica, he's so wild that it's as much as I can do to manage him at all. He'd destroy himself and me, too, if I attempted to ride him at such a rattletrap as that.\"\n\nA rattletrap! The quintain that she had put up with so much anxious care; the game that she had prepared for the amusement of the stalwart yeomen of the country; the sport that had been honoured by the affection of so many of their ancestors! It cut her to the heart to hear it so denominated by her own brother. There were but the two of them left together in the world, and it had ever been one of the rules by which Miss Thorne had regulated her conduct through life to say nothing that could provoke her brother. She had often had to suffer from his indifference to time-honoured British customs, but she had always suffered in silence. It was part of her creed that the head of the family should never be upbraided in his own house, and Miss Thorne had lived up to her creed. Now, however, she was greatly tried. The colour mounted to her ancient cheek, and the fire blazed in her still bright eyes; but yet she said nothing. She resolved that, at any rate, to him nothing more should be said about the quintain that day.\n\nShe sipped her tea in silent sorrow and thought with painful regret of the glorious days when her great ancestor Ealfried had successfully held Ullathorne against a Norman invader. There was no such spirit now left in her family except that small useless spark which burnt in her own bosom. And she herself, was not she at this moment intent on entertaining a descendant of those very Normans, a vain proud countess with a Frenchified name who would only think that she graced Ullathorne too highly by entering its portals? Was it likely that an Honourable John, the son of an Earl De Courcy, should ride at a quintain in company with Saxon yeomen? And why should she expect her brother to do that which her brother's guests would decline to do?\n\nSome dim faint idea of the impracticability of her own views flitted across her brain. Perhaps it was necessary that races doomed to live on the same soil should give way to each other and adopt each other's pursuits. Perhaps it was impossible that after more than five centuries of close intercourse, Normans should remain Normans, and Saxons, Saxons. Perhaps, after all, her neighbours were wiser than herself. Such ideas did occasionally present themselves to Miss Thorne's mind and make her sad enough. But it never occurred to her that her favourite quintain was but a modern copy of a Norman knight's amusement, an adaptation of the noble tourney to the tastes and habits of the Saxon yeomen. Of this she was ignorant, and it would have been cruelty to instruct her.\n\nWhen Mr. Thorne saw the tear in her eye, he repented himself of his contemptuous expression. By him also it was recognized as a binding law that every whim of his sister was to be respected. He was not perhaps so firm in his observances to her as she was in hers to him. But his intentions were equally good, and whenever he found that he had forgotten them, it was matter of grief to him.\n\n\"My dear Monica,\" said he, \"I beg your pardon. I don't in the least mean to speak ill of the game. When I called it a rattletrap, I merely meant that it was so for a man of my age. You know you always forget that I an't a young man.\"\n\n\"I am quite sure you are not an old man, Wilfred,\" said she, accepting the apology in her heart and smiling at him with the tear still on her cheek.\n\n\"If I was five-and-twenty, or thirty,\" continued he, \"I should like nothing better than riding at the quintain all day.\"\n\n\"But you are not too old to hunt or to shoot,\" said she. \"If you can jump over a ditch and hedge, I am sure you could turn the quintain round.\"\n\n\"But when I ride over the hedges, my dear--and it isn't very often I do that--but when I do ride over the hedges, there isn't any bag of flour coming after me. Think how I'd look taking the countess out to breakfast with the back of my head all covered with meal.\"\n\nMiss Thorne said nothing further. She didn't like the allusion to the countess. She couldn't be satisfied with the reflection that the sports at Ullathorne should be interfered with by the personal attentions necessary for a Lady De Courcy. But she saw that it was useless for her to push the matter further. It was conceded that Mr. Thorne was to be spared the quintain, and Miss Thorne determined to trust wholly to a youthful knight of hers, an immense favourite, who, as she often declared, was a pattern to the young men of the age and an excellent sample of an English yeoman.\n\nThis was Farmer Greenacre's eldest son, who, to tell the truth, had from his earliest years taken the exact measure of Miss Thorne's foot. In his boyhood he had never failed to obtain from her apples, pocket-money, and forgiveness for his numerous trespasses; and now in his early manhood he got privileges and immunities which were equally valuable. He was allowed a day or two's shooting in September; he schooled the squire's horses; got slips of trees out of the orchard and roots of flowers out of the garden; and had the fishing of the little river altogether in his own hands. He had undertaken to come mounted on a nag of his father's and show the way at the quintain post. Whatever young Greenacre did the others would do after him. The juvenile Lookalofts might stand aloof, but the rest of the youth of Ullathorne would be sure to venture if Harry Greenacre showed the way. And so Miss Thorne made up her mind to dispense with the noble Johns and Georges and trust, as her ancestors had done before her, to the thews and sinews of native Ullathorne growth.\n\nAt about nine the lower orders began to congregate in the paddock and park, under the surveillance of Mr. Plomacy and the head gardener and head groom, who were sworn in as his deputies and were to assist him in keeping the peace and promoting the sports. Many of the younger inhabitants of the neighbourhood, thinking that they could not have too much of a good thing, had come at a very early hour, and the road between the house and the church had been thronged for some time before the gates were thrown open.\n\nAnd then another difficulty of huge dimensions arose, a difficulty which Mr. Plomacy had indeed foreseen and for which he was in some sort provided. Some of those who wished to share Miss Thorne's hospitality were not so particular as they should have been as to the preliminary ceremony of an invitation. They doubtless conceived that they had been overlooked by accident, and instead of taking this in dudgeon, as their betters would have done, they good-naturedly put up with the slight, and showed that they did so by presenting themselves at the gate in their Sunday best.\n\nMr. Plomacy, however, well-knew who were welcome and who were not. To some, even though uninvited, he allowed ingress. \"Don't be too particular, Plomacy,\" his mistress had said, \"especially with the children. If they live anywhere near, let them in.\"\n\nActing on this hint, Mr. Plomacy did let in many an eager urchin and a few tidily dressed girls with their swains who in no way belonged to the property. But to the denizens of the city he was inexorable. Many a Barchester apprentice made his appearance there that day and urged with piteous supplication that he had been working all the week in making saddles and boots for the use of Ullathorne, in compounding doses for the horses, or cutting up carcasses for the kitchen. No such claim was allowed. Mr. Plomacy knew nothing about the city apprentices; he was to admit the tenants and labourers on the estate; Miss Thorne wasn't going to take in the whole city of Barchester; and so on.\n\nNevertheless, before the day was half over, all this was found to be useless. Almost anybody who chose to come made his way into the park, and the care of the guardians was transferred to the tables on which the banquet was spread. Even here there was many an unauthorised claimant for a place, of whom it was impossible to get quit without more commotion than the place and food were worth.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI. Ullathorne Sports--Act I\n\nThe trouble in civilized life of entertaining company, as it is called too generally without much regard to strict veracity, is so great that it cannot but be matter of wonder that people are so fond of attempting it. It is difficult to ascertain what is the quid pro quo. If they who give such laborious parties, and who endure such toil and turmoil in the vain hope of giving them successfully, really enjoyed the parties given by others, the matter could be understood. A sense of justice would induce men and women to undergo, in behalf of others, those miseries which others had undergone in their behalf. But they all profess that going out is as great a bore as receiving, and to look at them when they are out, one cannot but believe them.\n\nEntertain! Who shall have sufficient self-assurance, who shall feel sufficient confidence in his own powers to dare to boast that he can entertain his company? A clown can sometimes do so, and sometimes a dancer in short petticoats and stuffed pink legs; occasionally, perhaps, a singer. But beyond these, success in this art of entertaining is not often achieved. Young men and girls linking themselves kind with kind, pairing like birds in spring because nature wills it, they, after a simple fashion, do entertain each other. Few others even try.\n\nLadies, when they open their houses, modestly confessing, it may be presumed, their own incapacity, mainly trust to wax candles and upholstery. Gentlemen seem to rely on their white waistcoats. To these are added, for the delight of the more sensual, champagne and such good things of the table as fashion allows to be still considered as comestible. Even in this respect the world is deteriorating. All the good soups are now tabooed, and at the houses of one's accustomed friends--small barristers, doctors, government clerks, and such-like (for we cannot all of us always live as grandees, surrounded by an elysium of livery servants)--one gets a cold potato handed to one as a sort of finale to one's slice of mutton. Alas for those happy days when one could say to one's neighbour, \"Jones, shall I give you some mashed turnip? May I trouble you for a little cabbage?\" And then the pleasure of drinking wine with Mrs. Jones and Miss Smith--with all the Joneses and all the Smiths! These latter-day habits are certainly more economical.\n\nMiss Thorne, however, boldly attempted to leave the modern, beaten track, and made a positive effort to entertain her guests. Alas! She did so with but moderate success. They had all their own way of going, and would not go her way. She piped to them, but they would not dance. She offered to them good, honest household cake made of currants and flour and eggs and sweetmeat, but they would feed themselves on trashy wafers from the shop of the Barchester pastry-cook, on chalk and gum and adulterated sugar. Poor Miss Thorne! Yours is not the first honest soul that has vainly striven to recall the glories of happy days gone by! If fashion suggests to a Lady De Courcy that, when invited to a déjeuner at twelve she ought to come at three, no eloquence of thine will teach her the advantage of a nearer approach to punctuality.\n\nShe had fondly thought that when she called on her friends to come at twelve, and specially begged them to believe that she meant it, she would be able to see them comfortably seated in their tents at two. Vain woman--or rather ignorant woman--ignorant of the advances of that civilization which the world had witnessed while she was growing old. At twelve she found herself alone, dressed in all the glory of the newest of her many suits of raiment--with strong shoes however, and a serviceable bonnet on her head, and a warm, rich shawl on her shoulders. Thus clad, she peered out into the tent, went to the ha-ha, and satisfied herself that at any rate the youngsters were amusing themselves, spoke a word to Mrs. Greenacre over the ditch, and took one look at the quintain. Three or four young farmers were turning the machine round and round and poking at the bag of flour in a manner not at all intended by the inventor of the game; but no mounted sportsmen were there. Miss Thorne looked at her watch. It was only fifteen minutes past twelve, and it was understood that Harry Greenacre was not to begin till the half-hour.\n\nMiss Thorne returned to her drawing-room rather quicker than was her wont, fearing that the countess might come and find none to welcome her. She need not have hurried, for no one was there. At half-past twelve she peeped into the kitchen; at a quarter to one she was joined by her brother; and just then the first fashionable arrival took place. Mrs. Clantantram was announced.\n\nNo announcement was necessary, indeed, for the good lady's voice was heard as she walked across the courtyard to the house, scolding the unfortunate postilion who had driven her from Barchester. At the moment Miss Thorne could not but be thankful that the other guests were more fashionable and were thus spared the fury of Mrs. Clantantram's indignation.\n\n\"Oh, Miss Thorne, look here!\" said she as soon as she found herself in the drawing-room; \"do look at my roque-laure. It's clean spoilt, and forever. I wouldn't but wear it because I knew you wished us all to be grand to-day, and yet I had my misgivings. Oh dear, oh dear! It was five-and-twenty shillings a yard.\"\n\nThe Barchester post-horses had misbehaved in some unfortunate manner just as Mrs. Clantantram was getting out of the chaise and had nearly thrown her under the wheel.\n\nMrs. Clantantram belonged to other days, and therefore, though she had but little else to recommend her, Miss Thorne was to a certain extent fond of her. She sent the roque-laure away to be cleaned, and lent her one of her best shawls out of her own wardrobe.\n\nThe next comer was Mr. Arabin, who was immediately informed of Mrs. Clantantram's misfortune and of her determination to pay neither master nor post-boy, although, as she remarked, she intended to get her lift home before she made known her mind upon that matter. Then a good deal of rustling was heard in the sort of lobby that was used for the ladies' outside cloaks, and the door having been thrown wide open, the servant announced, not in the most confident of voices, Mrs. Lookaloft, and the Miss Lookalofts, and Mr. Augustus Lookaloft.\n\nPoor man!--we mean the footman. He knew, none better, that Mrs. Lookaloft had no business there, that she was not wanted there, and would not be welcome. But he had not the courage to tell a stout lady with a low dress, short sleeves, and satin at eight shillings a yard that she had come to the wrong tent; he had not dared to hint to young ladies with white dancing shoes and long gloves that there was a place ready for them in the paddock. And thus Mrs. Lookaloft carried her point, broke through the guards, and made her way into the citadel. That she would have to pass an uncomfortable time there she had surmised before. But nothing now could rob her of the power of boasting that she had consorted on the lawn with the squire and Miss Thorne, with a countess, a bishop, and the county grandees, while Mrs. Greenacre and such-like were walking about with the ploughboys in the park. It was a great point gained by Mrs. Lookaloft, and it might be fairly expected that from this time forward the tradesmen of Barchester would, with undoubting pens, address her husband as T. Lookaloft, Esquire.\n\nMrs. Lookaloft's pluck carried her through everything, and she walked triumphant into the Ullathorne drawing-room; but her children did feel a little abashed at the sort of reception they met with. It was not in Miss Thorne's heart to insult her own guests, but neither was it in her disposition to overlook such effrontery.\n\n\"Oh, Mrs. Lookaloft, is this you?\" said she. \"And your daughters and son? Well, we're very glad to see you, but I'm sorry you've come in such low dresses, as we are all going out of doors. Could we lend you anything?\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no thank ye, Miss Thorne,\" said the mother; \"the girls and myself are quite used to low dresses, when we're out.\"\n\n\"Are you, indeed?\" said Miss Thorne shuddering--but the shudder was lost on Mrs. Lookaloft.\n\n\"And where's Lookaloft?\" said the master of the house, coming up to welcome his tenant's wife. Let the faults of the family be what they would, he could not but remember that their rent was well paid; he was therefore not willing to give them a cold shoulder.\n\n\"Such a headache, Mr. Thorne!\" said Mrs. Lookaloft. \"In fact he couldn't stir, or you may be certain on such a day he would not have absented hisself.\"\n\n\"Dear me,\" said Miss Thorne. \"If he is so ill, I'm sure you'd wish to be with him.\"\n\n\"Not at all!\" said Mrs. Lookaloft. \"Not at all, Miss Thorne. It is only bilious you know, and when he's that way, he can bear nobody nigh him.\"\n\nThe fact, however, was that Mr. Lookaloft, having either more sense or less courage than his wife, had not chosen to intrude on Miss Thorne's drawing-room, and as he could not very well have gone among the plebeians while his wife was with the patricians, he thought it most expedient to remain at Rosebank.\n\nMrs. Lookaloft soon found herself on a sofa, and the Miss Lookalofts on two chairs, while Mr. Augustus stood near the door; and here they remained till in due time they were seated, all four together, at the bottom of the dining-room table.\n\nThen the Grantlys came--the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly and the two girls, and Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Harding. As ill-luck would have it, they were closely followed by Dr. Stanhope's carriage. As Eleanor looked out of the carriage window, she saw her brother-in-law helping the ladies out and threw herself back into her seat, dreading to be discovered. She had had an odious journey. Mr. Slope's civility had been more than ordinarily greasy; and now, though he had not in fact said anything which she could notice, she had for the first time entertained a suspicion that he was intending to make love to her. Was it after all true that she had been conducting herself in a way that justified the world in thinking that she liked the man? After all, could it be possible that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin were right, and that she was wrong? Charlotte Stanhope had also been watching Mr. Slope and had come to the conclusion that it behoved her brother to lose no further time, if he meant to gain the widow. She almost regretted that it had not been contrived that Bertie should be at Ullathorne before them.\n\nDr. Grantly did not see his sister-in-law in company with Mr. Slope, but Mr. Arabin did. Mr. Arabin came out with Mr. Thorne to the front door to welcome Mrs. Grantly, and he remained in the courtyard till all their party had passed on. Eleanor hung back in the carriage as long as she well could, but she was nearest to the door, and when Mr. Slope, having alighted, offered her his hand, she had no alternative but to take it. Mr. Arabin, standing at the open door while Mrs. Grantly was shaking hands with someone within, saw a clergyman alight from the carriage whom he at once knew to be Mr. Slope, and then he saw this clergyman hand out Mrs. Bold. Having seen so much, Mr. Arabin, rather sick at heart, followed Mrs. Grantly into the house.\n\nEleanor was, however, spared any further immediate degradation, for Dr. Stanhope gave her his arm across the courtyard, and Mr. Slope was fain to throw away his attention upon Charlotte.\n\nThey had hardly passed into the house, and from the house to the lawn, when, with a loud rattle and such noise as great men and great women are entitled to make in their passage through the world, the Proudies drove up. It was soon apparent that no everyday comer was at the door. One servant whispered to another that it was the bishop, and the word soon ran through all the hangers-on and strange grooms and coachmen about the place. There was quite a little cortège to see the bishop and his \"lady\" walk across the courtyard, and the good man was pleased to see that the church was held in such respect in the parish of St. Ewold's.\n\nAnd now the guests came fast and thick, and the lawn began to be crowded, and the room to be full. Voices buzzed, silk rustled against silk, and muslin crumpled against muslin. Miss Thorne became more happy than she had been, and again bethought her of her sports. There were targets and bows and arrows prepared at the further end of the lawn. Here the gardens of the place encroached with a somewhat wide sweep upon the paddock and gave ample room for the doings of the toxophilites. Miss Thorne got together such daughters of Diana as could bend a bow and marshalled them to the targets. There were the Grantly girls and the Proudie girls and the Chadwick girls, and the two daughters of the burly chancellor, and Miss Knowle; and with them went Frederick and Augustus Chadwick, and young Knowle of Knowle Park, and Frank Foster of the Elms, and Mr. Vellem Deeds, the dashing attorney of the High Street, and the Rev. Mr. Green, and the Rev. Mr. Brown, and the Rev. Mr. White, all of whom, as in duty bound, attended the steps of the three Miss Proudies.\n\n\"Did you ever ride at the quintain, Mr. Foster?\" said Miss Thorne as she walked with her party across the lawn.\n\n\"The quintain?\" said young Foster, who considered himself a dab at horsemanship. \"Is it a sort of gate, Miss Thorne?\"\n\nMiss Thorne had to explain the noble game she spoke of, and Frank Foster had to own that he never had ridden at the quintain.\n\n\"Would you like to come and see?\" said Miss Thorne. \"There'll be plenty here you know without you, if you like it.\"\n\n\"Well, I don't mind,\" said Frank. \"I suppose the ladies can come too.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said Miss Thorne; \"those who like it. I have no doubt they'll go to see your prowess, if you'll ride, Mr. Foster.\"\n\nMr. Foster looked down at a most unexceptionable pair of pantaloons, which had arrived from London only the day before. They were the very things, at least he thought so, for a picnic or fête champêtre, but he was not prepared to ride in them. Nor was he more encouraged than had been Mr. Thorne by the idea of being attacked from behind by the bag of flour, which Miss Thorne had graphically described to him.\n\n\"Well, I don't know about riding, Miss Thorne,\" said he; \"I fear I'm not quite prepared.\"\n\nMiss Thorne sighed but said nothing further. She left the toxophilites to their bows and arrows and returned towards the house. But as she passed by the entrance to the small park, she thought that she might at any rate encourage the yeomen by her presence, as she could not induce her more fashionable guests to mix with them in their manly amusements. Accordingly she once more betook herself to the quintain post.\n\nHere to her great delight she found Harry Greenacre ready mounted, with his pole in his hand, and a lot of comrades standing round him, encouraging him to the assault. She stood at a little distance and nodded to him in token of her good pleasure.\n\n\"Shall I begin, ma'am?\" said Harry, fingering his long staff in a rather awkward way, while his horse moved uneasily beneath him, not accustomed to a rider armed with such a weapon.\n\n\"Yes, yes,\" said Miss Thorne, standing triumphant as the queen of beauty on an inverted tub which some chance had brought thither from the farmyard.\n\n\"Here goes then,\" said Harry as he wheeled his horse round to get the necessary momentum of a sharp gallop. The quintain post stood right before him, and the square board at which he was to tilt was fairly in his way. If he hit that duly in the middle, and maintained his pace as he did so, it was calculated that he would be carried out of reach of the flour bag, which, suspended at the other end of the cross-bar on the post, would swing round when the board was struck. It was also calculated that if the rider did not maintain his pace, he would get a blow from the flour bag just at the back of his head, and bear about him the signs of his awkwardness to the great amusement of the lookers-on.\n\nHarry Greenacre did not object to being powdered with flour in the service of his mistress and therefore gallantly touched his steed with his spur, having laid his lance in rest to the best of his ability. But his ability in this respect was not great, and his appurtenances probably not very good; consequently, he struck his horse with his pole unintentionally on the side of the head as he started. The animal swerved and shied and galloped off wide of the quintain. Harry, well-accustomed to manage a horse, but not to do so with a twelve-foot rod on his arm, lowered his right hand to the bridle, and thus the end of the lance came to the ground and got between the legs of the steed. Down came rider and steed and staff. Young Greenacre was thrown some six feet over the horse's head, and poor Miss Thorne almost fell off her tub in a swoon.\n\n\"Oh, gracious, he's killed,\" shrieked a woman who was near him when he fell.\n\n\"The Lord be good to him! His poor mother, his poor mother!\" said another.\n\n\"Well, drat them dangerous plays all the world over,\" said an old crone.\n\n\"He has broke his neck sure enough, if ever man did,\" said a fourth.\n\nPoor Miss Thorne. She heard all this and yet did not quite swoon. She made her way through the crowd as best she could, sick herself almost to death. Oh, his mother--his poor mother! How could she ever forgive herself. The agony of that moment was terrific. She could hardly get to the place where the poor lad was lying, as three or four men in front were about the horse, which had risen with some difficulty, but at last she found herself close to the young farmer.\n\n\"Has he marked himself? For heaven's sake tell me that: has he marked his knees?\" said Harry, slowly rising and rubbing his left shoulder with his right hand and thinking only of his horse's legs. Miss Thorne soon found that he had not broken his neck, nor any of his bones, nor been injured in any essential way. But from that time forth she never instigated anyone to ride at a quintain.\n\nEleanor left Dr. Stanhope as soon as she could do so civilly and went in quest of her father, whom she found on the lawn in company with Mr. Arabin. She was not sorry to find them together. She was anxious to disabuse at any rate her father's mind as to this report which had got abroad respecting her, and would have been well pleased to have been able to do the same with regard to Mr. Arabin. She put her own through her father's arm, coming up behind his back, and then tendered her hand also to the vicar of St. Ewold's.\n\n\"And how did you come?\" said Mr. Harding, when the first greeting was over.\n\n\"The Stanhopes brought me,\" said she; \"their carriage was obliged to come twice, and has now gone back for the signora.\" As she spoke she caught Mr. Arabin's eye and saw that he was looking pointedly at her with a severe expression. She understood at once the accusation contained in his glance. It said as plainly as an eye could speak, \"Yes, you came with the Stanhopes, but you did so in order that you might be in company with Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Our party,\" said she, still addressing her father, \"consisted of the doctor and Charlotte Stanhope, myself, and Mr. Slope.\" As she mentioned the last name she felt her father's arm quiver slightly beneath her touch. At the same moment Mr. Arabin turned away from them and, joining his hands behind his back, strolled slowly away by one of the paths.\n\n\"Papa,\" said she, \"it was impossible to help coming in the same carriage with Mr. Slope; it was quite impossible. I had promised to come with them before I dreamt of his coming, and afterwards I could not get out of it without explaining and giving rise to talk. You weren't at home, you know. I couldn't possibly help it.\" She said all this so quickly that by the time her apology was spoken she was quite out of breath.\n\n\"I don't know why you should have wished to help it, my dear,\" said her father.\n\n\"Yes, Papa, you do. You must know, you do know all the things they said at Plumstead. I am sure you do. You know all the archdeacon said. How unjust he was; and Mr. Arabin too. He's a horrid man, a horrid odious man, but--\"\n\n\"Who is an odious man, my dear? Mr. Arabin?\"\n\n\"No; but Mr. Slope. You know I mean Mr. Slope. He's the most odious man I ever met in my life, and it was most unfortunate my having to come here in the same carriage with him. But how could I help it?\"\n\nA great weight began to move itself off Mr. Harding's mind. So, after all, the archdeacon with all his wisdom, and Mrs. Grantly with all her tact, and Mr. Arabin with all his talent, were in the wrong. His own child, his Eleanor, the daughter of whom he was so proud, was not to become the wife of a Mr. Slope. He had been about to give his sanction to the marriage, so certified had he been of the fact, and now he learnt that this imputed lover of Eleanor's was at any rate as much disliked by her as by any one of the family. Mr. Harding, however, was by no means sufficiently a man of the world to conceal the blunder he had made. He could not pretend that he had entertained no suspicion; he could not make believe that he had never joined the archdeacon in his surmises. He was greatly surprised, and gratified beyond measure, and he could not help showing that such was the case.\n\n\"My darling girl,\" said he, \"I am so delighted, so overjoyed. My own child; you have taken such a weight off my mind.\"\n\n\"But surely, Papa, you didn't think--\"\n\n\"I didn't know what to think, my dear. The archdeacon told me that--\"\n\n\"The archdeacon!\" said Eleanor, her face lighting up with passion. \"A man like the archdeacon might, one would think, be better employed than in traducing his sister-in-law and creating bitterness between a father and his daughter!\"\n\n\"He didn't mean to do that, Eleanor.\"\n\n\"What did he mean then? Why did he interfere with me and fill your mind with such falsehood?\"\n\n\"Never mind it now, my child; never mind it now. We shall all know you better now.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, that you should have thought it! That you should have suspected me!\"\n\n\"I don't know what you mean by suspicion, Eleanor. There would be nothing disgraceful, you know, nothing wrong in such a marriage. Nothing that could have justified my interfering as your father.\" And Mr. Harding would have proceeded in his own defence to make out that Mr. Slope after all was a very good sort of man and a very fitting second husband for a young widow, had he not been interrupted by Eleanor's greater energy.\n\n\"It would be disgraceful,\" said she; \"it would be wrong; it would be abominable. Could I do such a horrid thing, I should expect no one to speak to me. Ugh--\" and she shuddered as she thought of the matrimonial torch which her friends had been so ready to light on her behalf. \"I don't wonder at Dr. Grantly; I don't wonder at Susan; but, oh, Papa, I do wonder at you. How could you, how could you believe it?\" Poor Eleanor, as she thought of her father's defalcation, could resist her tears no longer, and was forced to cover her face with her handkerchief.\n\nThe place was not very opportune for her grief. They were walking through the shrubberies, and there were many people near them. Poor Mr. Harding stammered out his excuse as best he could, and Eleanor with an effort controlled her tears and returned her handkerchief to her pocket. She did not find it difficult to forgive her father, nor could she altogether refuse to join him in the returning gaiety of spirit to which her present avowal gave rise. It was such a load off his heart to think that he should not be called on to welcome Mr. Slope as his son-in-law. It was such a relief to him to find that his daughter's feelings and his own were now, as they ever had been, in unison. He had been so unhappy for the last six weeks about this wretched Mr. Slope! He was so indifferent as to the loss of the hospital, so thankful for the recovery of his daughter, that, strong as was the ground for Eleanor's anger, she could not find it in her heart to be long angry with him.\n\n\"Dear Papa,\" she said, hanging closely to his arm, \"never suspect me again: promise me that you never will. Whatever I do you may be sure I shall tell you first; you may be sure I shall consult you.\"\n\nAnd Mr. Harding did promise, and owned his sin, and promised again. And so, while he promised amendment and she uttered forgiveness, they returned together to the drawing-room windows.\n\nAnd what had Eleanor meant when she declared that whatever she did, she would tell her father first? What was she thinking of doing?\n\nSo ended the first act of the melodrama which Eleanor was called on to perform this day at Ullathorne.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII. The Signora Neroni, the Countess De Courcy, and Mrs. Proudie Meet Each Other at Ullathorne\n\nAnd now there were new arrivals. Just as Eleanor reached the drawing-room the signora was being wheeled into it. She had been brought out of the carriage into the dining-room and there placed on a sofa, and was now in the act of entering the other room, by the joint aid of her brother and sister, Mr. Arabin, and two servants in livery. She was all in her glory, and looked so pathetically happy, so full of affliction and grace, was so beautiful, so pitiable, and so charming that it was almost impossible not to be glad she was there.\n\nMiss Thorne was unaffectedly glad to welcome her. In fact, the signora was a sort of lion; and though there was no drop of the Leohunter blood in Miss Thorne's veins, she nevertheless did like to see attractive people at her house. The signora was attractive, and on her first settlement in the dining-room she had whispered two or three soft feminine words into Miss Thorne's ear which, at the moment, had quite touched that lady's heart.\n\n\"Oh, Miss Thorne; where is Miss Thorne?\" she said as soon as her attendants had placed her in her position just before one of the windows, from whence she could see all that was going on upon the lawn. \"How am I to thank you for permitting a creature like me to be here? But if you knew the pleasure you give me, I am sure you would excuse the trouble I bring with me.\" And as she spoke she squeezed the spinster's little hand between her own.\n\n\"We are delighted to see you here,\" said Miss Thorne; \"you give us no trouble at all, and we think it a great favour conferred by you to come and see us--don't we, Wilfred?\"\n\n\"A very great favour indeed,\" said Mr. Thorne with a gallant bow but of a somewhat less cordial welcome than that conceded by his sister. Mr. Thorne had heard perhaps more of the antecedents of his guest than his sister had done, and had not as yet undergone the power of the signora's charms.\n\nBut while the mother of the last of the Neros was thus in her full splendour, with crowds of people gazing at her and the élite of the company standing round her couch, her glory was paled by the arrival of the Countess De Courcy. Miss Thorne had now been waiting three hours for the countess, and could not therefore but show very evident gratification when the arrival at last took place. She and her brother of course went off to welcome the titled grandees, and with them, alas, went many of the signora's admirers.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Thorne,\" said the countess, while in the act of being disrobed of her fur cloaks and rerobed in her gauze shawls, \"what dreadful roads you have; perfectly frightful.\"\n\nIt happened that Mr. Thorne was waywarden for the district and, not liking the attack, began to excuse his roads.\n\n\"Oh, yes, indeed they are,\" said the countess not minding him in the least; \"perfectly dreadful--are they not, Margaretta? Why, my dear Miss Thorne, we left Courcy Castle just at eleven; it was only just past eleven, was it not, George? And--\"\n\n\"Just past one I think you mean,\" said the Honourable George, turning from the group and eyeing the signora through his glass. The signora gave him back his own, as the saying is, and more with it, so that the young nobleman was forced to avert his glance and drop his glass.\n\n\"I say, Thorne,\" whispered he, \"who the deuce is that on the sofa?\"\n\n\"Dr. Stanhope's daughter,\" whispered back Mr. Thorne. \"Signora Neroni, she calls herself.\"\n\n\"Whew--ew--ew!\" whistled the Honourable George. \"The devil she is. I have heard no end of stories about that filly. You must positively introduce me, Thorne; you positively must.\"\n\nMr. Thorne, who was respectability itself, did not quite like having a guest about whom the Honourable George De Courcy had heard no end of stories, but he couldn't help himself. He merely resolved that before he went to bed he would let his sister know somewhat of the history of the lady she was so willing to welcome. The innocence of Miss Thorne at her time of life was perfectly charming, but even innocence may be dangerous.\n\n\"George may say what he likes,\" continued the countess, urging her excuses to Miss Thorne; \"I am sure we were past the castle gate before twelve--weren't we, Margaretta?\"\n\n\"Upon my word I don't know,\" said the Lady Margaretta, \"for I was half-asleep. But I do know that I was called some time in the middle of the night and was dressing myself before daylight.\"\n\nWise people, when they are in the wrong, always put themselves right by finding fault with the people against whom they have sinned. Lady De Courcy was a wise woman, and therefore, having treated Miss Thorne very badly by staying away till three o'clock, she assumed the offensive and attacked Mr. Thorne's roads. Her daughter, not less wise, attacked Miss Thorne's early hours. The art of doing this is among the most precious of those usually cultivated by persons who know how to live. There is no withstanding it. Who can go systematically to work and, having done battle with the primary accusation and settled that, then bring forward a countercharge and support that also? Life is not long enough for such labours. A man in the right relies easily on his rectitude and therefore goes about unarmed. His very strength is his weakness. A man in the wrong knows that he must look to his weapons; his very weakness is his strength. The one is never prepared for combat, the other is always ready. Therefore it is that in this world the man that is in the wrong almost invariably conquers the man that is in the right, and invariably despises him.\n\nA man must be an idiot or else an angel who, after the age of forty, shall attempt to be just to his neighbours. Many like the Lady Margaretta have learnt their lesson at a much earlier age. But this of course depends on the school in which they have been taught.\n\nPoor Miss Thorne was altogether overcome. She knew very well that she had been ill-treated, and yet she found herself making apologies to Lady De Courcy. To do her ladyship justice, she received them very graciously, and allowed herself, with her train of daughters, to be led towards the lawn.\n\nThere were two windows in the drawing-room wide open for the countess to pass through, but she saw that there was a woman on a sofa, at the third window, and that that woman had, as it were, a following attached to her. Her ladyship therefore determined to investigate the woman. The De Courcy's were hereditarily shortsighted, and had been so for thirty centuries at least. So Lady De Courcy, who when she entered the family had adopted the family habits, did as her son had done before her and, taking her glass to investigate the Signora Neroni, pressed in among the gentlemen who surrounded the couch, and bowed slightly to those whom she chose to honour by her acquaintance.\n\nIn order to get to the window she had to pass close to the front of the couch, and as she did so she stared hard at the occupant. The occupant, in return, stared hard at the countess. The countess, who, since her countess-ship commenced, had been accustomed to see all eyes not royal, ducal, or marquesal fall before her own, paused as she went on, raised her eyebrows, and stared even harder than before. But she had now to do with one who cared little for countesses. It was, one may say, impossible for mortal man or woman to abash Madeline Neroni. She opened her large, bright, lustrous eyes wider and wider, till she seemed to be all eyes. She gazed up into the lady's face, not as though she did it with an effort, but as if she delighted in doing it. She used no glass to assist her effrontery, and needed none. The faintest possible smile of derision played round her mouth, and her nostrils were slightly dilated, as if in sure anticipation of her triumph. And it was sure. The Countess De Courcy, in spite of her thirty centuries and De Courcy Castle, and the fact that Lord De Courcy was grand master of the ponies to the Prince of Wales, had not a chance with her. At first the little circlet of gold wavered in the countess's hand, then the hand shook, then the circlet fell, the countess's head tossed itself into the air, and the countess's feet shambled out to the lawn. She did not, however, go so fast but what she heard the signora's voice, asking:\n\n\"Who on earth is that woman, Mr. Slope?\"\n\n\"That is Lady De Courcy.\"\n\n\"Oh, ah. I might have supposed so. Ha, ha, ha. Well, that's as good as a play.\"\n\nIt was as good as a play to any there who had eyes to observe it and wit to comment on what they observed.\n\nBut the Lady De Courcy soon found a congenial spirit on the lawn. There she encountered Mrs. Proudie, and as Mrs. Proudie was not only the wife of a bishop but was also the cousin of an earl, Lady De Courcy considered her to be the fittest companion she was likely to meet in that assemblage. They were accordingly delighted to see each other. Mrs. Proudie by no means despised a countess, and as this countess lived in the county and within a sort of extensive visiting distance of Barchester, she was glad to have this opportunity of ingratiating herself.\n\n\"My dear Lady De Courcy, I am so delighted,\" said she, looking as little grim as it was in her nature to do. \"I hardly expected to see you here. It is such a distance, and then, you know, such a crowd.\"\n\n\"And such roads, Mrs. Proudie! I really wonder how the people ever get about. But I don't suppose they ever do.\"\n\n\"Well, I really don't know, but I suppose not. The Thornes don't, I know,\" said Mrs. Proudie. \"Very nice person, Miss Thorne, isn't she?\"\n\n\"Oh, delightful, and so queer; I've known her these twenty years. A great pet of mine is dear Miss Thorne. She is so very strange, you know. She always makes me think of the Eskimos and the Indians. Isn't her dress quite delightful?\"\n\n\"Delightful,\" said Mrs. Proudie. \"I wonder now whether she paints. Did you ever see such colour?\"\n\n\"Oh, of course,\" said Lady De Courcy; \"that is, I have no doubt she does. But, Mrs. Proudie, who is that woman on the sofa by the window? Just step this way and you'll see her, there--\" and the countess led her to a spot where she could plainly see the signora's well-remembered face and figure.\n\nShe did not however do so without being equally well seen by the signora. \"Look, look,\" said that lady to Mr. Slope, who was still standing near to her; \"see the high spiritualities and temporalities of the land in league together, and all against poor me. I'll wager my bracelet, Mr. Slope, against your next sermon that they've taken up their position there on purpose to pull me to pieces. Well, I can't rush to the combat, but I know how to protect myself if the enemy come near me.\"\n\nBut the enemy knew better. They could gain nothing by contact with the Signora Neroni, and they could abuse her as they pleased at a distance from her on the lawn.\n\n\"She's that horrid Italian woman, Lady De Courcy; you must have heard of her.\"\n\n\"What Italian woman?\" said her ladyship, quite alive to the coming story. \"I don't think I've heard of any Italian woman coming into the country. She doesn't look Italian, either.\"\n\n\"Oh, you must have heard of her,\" said Mrs. Proudie. \"No, she's not absolutely Italian. She is Dr. Stanhope's daughter--Dr. Stanhope the prebendary--and she calls herself the Signora Neroni.\"\n\n\"Oh-h-h-h!\" exclaimed the countess.\n\n\"I was sure you had heard of her,\" continued Mrs. Proudie. \"I don't know anything about her husband. They do say that some man named Neroni is still alive. I believe she did marry such a man abroad, but I do not at all know who or what he was.\"\n\n\"Oh-h-h-h!\" exclaimed the countess, shaking her head with much intelligence, as every additional \"h\" fell from her lips. \"I know all about it now. I have heard George mention her. George knows all about her. George heard about her in Rome.\"\n\n\"She's an abominable woman, at any rate,\" said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"Insufferable,\" said the countess.\n\n\"She made her way into the palace once, before I knew anything about her, and I cannot tell you how dreadfully indecent her conduct was.\"\n\n\"Was it?\" said the delighted countess.\n\n\"Insufferable,\" said the prelatess.\n\n\"But why does she lie on a sofa?\" asked Lady De Courcy.\n\n\"She has only one leg,\" replied Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"Only one leg!\" said Lady De Courcy, who felt to a certain degree dissatisfied that the signora was thus incapacitated. \"Was she born so?\"\n\n\"Oh, no,\" said Mrs. Proudie--and her ladyship felt some what recomforted by the assurance--\"she had two. But that Signor Neroni beat her, I believe, till she was obliged to have one amputated. At any rate, she entirely lost the use of it.\"\n\n\"Unfortunate creature!\" said the countess, who herself knew something of matrimonial trials.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"one would pity her in spite of her past bad conduct, if she now knew how to behave herself. But she does not. She is the most insolent creature I ever put my eyes on.\"\n\n\"Indeed she is,\" said Lady De Courcy.\n\n\"And her conduct with men is so abominable that she is not fit to be admitted into any lady's drawing-room.\"\n\n\"Dear me!\" said the countess, becoming again excited, happy and merciless.\n\n\"You saw that man standing near her--the clergyman with the red hair?\"\n\n\"Yes, yes.\"\n\n\"She has absolutely ruined that man. The bishop--or I should rather take the blame on myself, for it was I--I brought him down from London to Barchester. He is a tolerable preacher, an active young man, and I therefore introduced him to the bishop. That woman, Lady De Courcy, has got hold of him and has so disgraced him that I am forced to require that he shall leave the palace; and I doubt very much whether he won't lose his gown!\"\n\n\"Why, what an idiot the man must be!\" said the countess.\n\n\"You don't know the intriguing villainy of that woman,\" said Mrs. Proudie, remembering her torn flounces.\n\n\"But you say she has only got one leg!\"\n\n\"She is as full of mischief as tho' she had ten. Look at her eyes, Lady De Courcy. Did you ever see such eyes in a decent woman's head?\"\n\n\"Indeed, I never did, Mrs. Proudie.\"\n\n\"And her effrontery, and her voice! I quite pity her poor father, who is really a good sort of man.\"\n\n\"Dr. Stanhope, isn't he?\"\n\n\"Yes, Dr. Stanhope. He is one of our prebendaries--a good, quiet sort of man himself. But I am surprised that he should let his daughter conduct herself as she does.\"\n\n\"I suppose he can't help it,\" said the countess.\n\n\"But a clergyman, you know, Lady De Courcy! He should at any rate prevent her from exhibiting in public, if he cannot induce her to behave at home. But he is to be pitied. I believe he has a desperate life of it with the lot of them. That apish-looking man there, with the long beard and the loose trousers--he is the woman's brother. He is nearly as bad as she is. They are both of them infidels.\"\n\n\"Infidels!\" said Lady De Courcy, \"and their father a prebendary!\"\n\n\"Yes, and likely to be the new dean, too,\" said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"Oh, yes, poor dear Dr. Trefoil!\" said the countess, who had once in her life spoken to that gentleman. \"I was so distressed to hear it, Mrs. Proudie. And so Dr. Stanhope is to be the new dean! He comes of an excellent family, and I wish him success in spite of his daughter. Perhaps, Mrs. Proudie, when he is dean, they'll be better able to see the error of their ways.\"\n\nTo this Mrs. Proudie said nothing. Her dislike of the Signora Neroni was too deep to admit of her even hoping that that lady should see the error of her ways. Mrs. Proudie looked on the signora as one of the lost--one of those beyond the reach of Christian charity--and was therefore able to enjoy the luxury of hating her without the drawback of wishing her eventually well out of her sins.\n\nAny further conversation between these congenial souls was prevented by the advent of Mr. Thorne, who came to lead the countess to the tent. Indeed, he had been desired to do so some ten minutes since, but he had been delayed in the drawing-room by the signora. She had contrived to detain him, to get him near to her sofa, and at last to make him seat himself on a chair close to her beautiful arm. The fish took the bait, was hooked, and caught, and landed. Within that ten minutes he had heard the whole of the signora's history in such strains as she chose to use in telling it. He learnt from the lady's own lips the whole of that mysterious tale to which the Honourable George had merely alluded. He discovered that the beautiful creature lying before him had been more sinned against than sinning. She had owned to him that she had been weak, confiding, and indifferent to the world's opinion, and that she had therefore been ill-used, deceived, and evil spoken of. She had spoken to him of her mutilated limb, her youth destroyed in fullest bloom, her beauty robbed of its every charm, her life blighted, her hopes withered, and as she did so a tear dropped from her eye to her cheek. She had told him of these things and asked for his sympathy.\n\nWhat could a good-natured, genial, Anglo-Saxon Squire Thorne do but promise to sympathize with her? Mr. Thorne did promise to sympathize; promised also to come and see the last of the Neros, to hear more of those fearful Roman days, of those light and innocent but dangerous hours which flitted by so fast on the shores of Como, and to make himself the confidant of the signora's sorrows.\n\nWe need hardly say that he dropped all idea of warning his sister against the dangerous lady. He had been mistaken--never so much mistaken in his life. He had always regarded that Honourable George as a coarse, brutal-minded young man; now he was more convinced than ever that he was so. It was by such men as the Honourable George that the reputations of such women as Madeline Neroni were imperilled and damaged. He would go and see the lady in her own house; he was fully sure in his own mind of the soundness of his own judgement; if he found her, as he believed he should do, an injured, well-disposed, warm-hearted woman, he would get his sister Monica to invite her out to Ullathorne.\n\n\"No,\" said she, as at her instance he got up to leave her and declared that he himself would attend upon her wants; \"no, no, my friend; I positively put a veto upon your doing so. What, in your own house, with an assemblage round you such as there is here! Do you wish to make every woman hate me and every man stare at me? I lay a positive order on you not to come near me again to-day. Come and see me at home. It is only at home that I can talk, it is only at home that I really can live and enjoy myself. My days of going out, days such as these, are rare indeed. Come and see me at home, Mr. Thorne, and then I will not bid you to leave me.\"\n\nIt is, we believe, common with young men of five-and-twenty to look on their seniors--on men of, say, double their own age--as so many stocks and stones--stocks and stones, that is, in regard to feminine beauty. There never was a greater mistake. Women, indeed, generally know better, but on this subject men of one age are thoroughly ignorant of what is the very nature of mankind of other ages. No experience of what goes on in the world, no reading of history, no observation of life, has any effect in teaching the truth. Men of fifty don't dance mazurkas, being generally too fat and wheezy; nor do they sit for the hour together on river-banks at their mistresses' feet, being somewhat afraid of rheumatism. But for real true love--love at first sight, love to devotion, love that robs a man of his sleep, love that \"will gaze an eagle blind,\" love that \"will hear the lowest sound when the suspicious tread of theft is stopped,\" love that is \"like a Hercules, still climbing trees in the Hesperides\"--we believe the best age is from forty-five to seventy; up to that, men are generally given to mere flirting.\n\nAt the present moment Mr. Thorne, ætat. fifty, was over head and ears in love at first sight with the Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni, nata Stanhope.\n\nNevertheless, he was sufficiently master of himself to offer his arm with all propriety to Lady De Courcy, and the countess graciously permitted herself to be led to the tent. Such had been Miss Thorne's orders, as she had succeeded in inducing the bishop to lead old Lady Knowle to the top of the dining-room. One of the baronets was sent off in quest of Mrs. Proudie and found that lady on the lawn not in the best of humours. Mr. Thorne and the countess had left her too abruptly; she had in vain looked about for an attendant chaplain, or even a stray curate; they were all drawing long bows with the young ladies at the bottom of the lawn, or finding places for their graceful co-toxophilites in some snug corner of the tent. In such position Mrs. Proudie had been wont in earlier days to fall back upon Mr. Slope, but now she could never fall back upon him again. She gave her head one shake as she thought of her lone position, and that shake was as good as a week deducted from Mr. Slope's longer sojourn in Barchester. Sir Harkaway Gorse, however, relieved her present misery, though his doing so by no means mitigated the sinning chaplain's doom.\n\nAnd now the eating and drinking began in earnest. Dr. Grantly, to his great horror, found himself leagued to Mrs. Clantantram. Mrs. Clantantram had a great regard for the archdeacon, which was not cordially returned, and when she, coming up to him, whispered in his ear, \"Come, Archdeacon, I'm sure you won't begrudge an old friend the favour of your arm,\" and then proceeded to tell him the whole history of her roquelaure, he resolved that he would shake her off before he was fifteen minutes older. But latterly the archdeacon had not been successful in his resolutions, and on the present occasion Mrs. Clantantram stuck to him till the banquet was over.\n\nDr. Gwynne got a baronet's wife, and Mrs. Grantly fell to the lot of a baronet. Charlotte Stanhope attached herself to Mr. Harding in order to make room for Bertie, who succeeded in sitting down in the dining-room next to Mrs. Bold. To speak sooth, now that he had love in earnest to make, his heart almost failed him.\n\nEleanor had been right glad to avail herself of his arm, seeing that Mr. Slope was hovering nigh her. In striving to avoid that terrible Charybdis of a Slope she was in great danger of falling into an unseen Scylla on the other hand, that Scylla being Bertie Stanhope. Nothing could be more gracious than she was to Bertie. She almost jumped at his proffered arm. Charlotte perceived this from a distance and triumphed in her heart; Bertie felt it and was encouraged; Mr. Slope saw it and glowered with jealousy. Eleanor and Bertie sat down to table in the dining-room, and as she took her seat at his right hand she found that Mr. Slope was already in possession of the chair at her own.\n\nAs these things were going on in the dining-room, Mr. Arabin was hanging enraptured and alone over the signora's sofa, and Eleanor from her seat could look through the open door and see that he was doing so.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII. The Bishop Sits Down to Breakfast, and the Dean Dies\n\nThe Bishop of Barchester said grace over the well-spread board in the Ullathorne dining-room; while he did so, the last breath was flying from the Dean of Barchester as he lay in his sick room in the deanery. When the Bishop of Barchester raised his first glass of champagne to his lips, the deanship of Barchester was a good thing in the gift of the prime minister. Before the Bishop of Barchester had left the table, the minister of the day was made aware of the fact at his country-seat in Hampshire, and had already turned over in his mind the names of five very respectable aspirants for the preferment. It is at present only necessary to say that Mr. Slope's name was not among the five.\n\n\"'Twas merry in the hall when the beards wagged all,\" and the clerical beards wagged merrily in the hall of Ullathorne that day. It was not till after the last cork had been drawn, the last speech made, the last nut cracked, that tidings reached and were whispered about that the poor dean was no more. It was well for the happiness of the clerical beards that this little delay took place, as otherwise decency would have forbidden them to wag at all.\n\nBut there was one sad man among them that day. Mr. Arabin's beard did not wag as it should have done. He had come there hoping the best, striving to think the best, about Eleanor; turning over in his mind all the words he remembered to have fallen from her about Mr. Slope, and trying to gather from them a conviction unfavourable to his rival. He had not exactly resolved to come that day to some decisive proof as to the widow's intention, but he had meant, if possible, to recultivate his friendship with Eleanor, and in his present frame of mind any such recultivation must have ended in a declaration of love.\n\nHe had passed the previous night alone at his new parsonage, and it was the first night that he had so passed. It had been dull and sombre enough. Mrs. Grantly had been right in saying that a priestess would be wanting at St. Ewold's. He had sat there alone with his glass before him, and then with his tea-pot, thinking about Eleanor Bold. As is usual in such meditations, he did little but blame her; blame her for liking Mr. Slope, and blame her for not liking him; blame her for her cordiality to himself, and blame her for her want of cordiality; blame her for being stubborn, headstrong, and passionate; and yet the more he thought of her the higher she rose in his affection. If only it should turn out, if only it could be made to turn out, that she had defended Mr. Slope, not from love, but on principle, all would be right. Such principle in itself would be admirable, lovable, womanly; he felt that he could be pleased to allow Mr. Slope just so much favour as that. But if--And then Mr. Arabin poked his fire most unnecessarily, spoke crossly to his new parlour-maid who came in for the tea-things, and threw himself back in his chair determined to go to sleep. Why had she been so stiff-necked when asked a plain question? She could not but have known in what light he regarded her. Why had she not answered a plain question and so put an end to his misery? Then, instead of going to sleep in his armchair, Mr. Arabin walked about the room as though he had been possessed.\n\nOn the following morning, when he attended Miss Thorne's behests, he was still in a somewhat confused state. His first duty had been to converse with Mrs. Clantantram, and that lady had found it impossible to elicit the slightest sympathy from him on the subject of her roquelaure. Miss Thorne had asked him whether Mrs. Bold was coming with the Grantlys, and the two names of Bold and Grantly together had nearly made him jump from his seat.\n\nHe was in this state of confused uncertainty, hope, and doubt, when he saw Mr. Slope, with his most polished smile, handing Eleanor out of her carriage. He thought of nothing more. He never considered whether the carriage belonged to her or to Mr. Slope, or to anyone else to whom they might both be mutually obliged without any concert between themselves. This sight in his present state of mind was quite enough to upset him and his resolves. It was clear as noon-day. Had he seen her handed into a carriage by Mr. Slope at a church door with a white veil over her head, the truth could not be more manifest. He went into the house and, as we have seen, soon found himself walking with Mr. Harding. Shortly afterwards Eleanor came up, and then he had to leave his companion and either go about alone or find another. While in this state he was encountered by the archdeacon.\n\n\"I wonder,\" said Dr. Grantly, \"if it be true that Mr. Slope and Mrs. Bold came here together. Susan says she is almost sure she saw their faces in the same carriage as she got out of her own.\"\n\nMr. Arabin had nothing for it but to bear his testimony to the correctness of Mrs. Grantly's eyesight.\n\n\"It is perfectly shameful,\" said the archdeacon; \"or, I should rather say, shameless. She was asked here as my guest, and if she be determined to disgrace herself, she should have feeling enough not to do so before my immediate friends. I wonder how that man got himself invited. I wonder whether she had the face to bring him.\"\n\nTo this Mr. Arabin could answer nothing, nor did he wish to answer anything. Though he abused Eleanor to himself, he did not choose to abuse her to anyone else, nor was he well-pleased to hear anyone else speak ill of her. Dr. Grantly, however, was very angry and did not spare his sister-in-law. Mr. Arabin therefore left him as soon as he could and wandered back into the house.\n\nHe had not been there long when the signora was brought in. For some time he kept himself out of temptation, and merely hovered round her at a distance; but as soon as Mr. Thorne had left her, he yielded himself up to the basilisk and allowed himself to be made prey of.\n\nIt is impossible to say how the knowledge had been acquired, but the signora had a sort of instinctive knowledge that Mr. Arabin was an admirer of Mrs. Bold. Men hunt foxes by the aid of dogs, and are aware that they do so by the strong organ of smell with which the dog is endowed. They do not, however, in the least comprehend how such a sense can work with such acuteness. The organ by which women instinctively, as it were, know and feel how other women are regarded by men, and how also men are regarded by other women, is equally strong, and equally incomprehensible. A glance, a word, a motion, suffices: by some such acute exercise of her feminine senses the signora was aware that Mr. Arabin loved Eleanor Bold; therefore, by a further exercise of her peculiar feminine propensities, it was quite natural for her to entrap Mr. Arabin into her net.\n\nThe work was half-done before she came to Ullathorne, and when could she have a better opportunity of completing it? She had had almost enough of Mr. Slope, though she could not quite resist the fun of driving a very sanctimonious clergyman to madness by a desperate and ruinous passion. Mr. Thorne had fallen too easily to give much pleasure in the chase. His position as a man of wealth might make his alliance of value, but as a lover he was very second-rate. We may say that she regarded him somewhat as a sportsman does a pheasant. The bird is so easily shot that he would not be worth the shooting were it not for the very respectable appearance that he makes in a larder. The signora would not waste much time in shooting Mr. Thorne, but still he was worth bagging for family uses.\n\nBut Mr. Arabin was game of another sort. The signora was herself possessed of quite sufficient intelligence to know that Mr. Arabin was a man more than usually intellectual. She knew also that, as a clergyman, he was of a much higher stamp than Mr. Slope and that, as a gentleman, he was better educated than Mr. Thorne. She would never have attempted to drive Mr. Arabin into ridiculous misery as she did Mr. Slope, nor would she think it possible to dispose of him in ten minutes as she had done with Mr. Thorne.\n\nSuch were her reflexions about Mr. Arabin. As to Mr. Arabin, it cannot be said that he reflected at all about the signora. He knew that she was beautiful, and he felt that she was able to charm him. He required charming in his present misery, and therefore he went and stood at the head of her couch. She knew all about it. Such were her peculiar gifts. It was her nature to see that he required charming, and it was her province to charm him. As the Eastern idler swallows his dose of opium, as the London reprobate swallows his dose of gin, so with similar desires and for similar reasons did Mr. Arabin prepare to swallow the charms of the Signora Neroni.\n\n\"Why an't you shooting with bows and arrows, Mr. Arabin?\" said she, when they were nearly alone together in the drawing-room, \"or talking with young ladies in shady bowers, or turning your talents to account in some way? What was a bachelor like you asked here for? Don't you mean to earn your cold chicken and champagne? Were I you, I should be ashamed to be so idle.\"\n\nMr. Arabin murmured some sort of answer. Though he wished to be charmed, he was hardly yet in a mood to be playful in return.\n\n\"Why what ails you, Mr. Arabin?\" said she. \"Here you are in your own parish--Miss Thorne tells me that her party is given expressly in your honour--and yet you are the only dull man at it. Your friend Mr. Slope was with me a few minutes since, full of life and spirits; why don't you rival him?\"\n\nIt was not difficult for so acute an observer as Madeline Neroni to see that she had hit the nail on the head and driven the bolt home. Mr. Arabin winced visibly before her attack, and she knew at once that he was jealous of Mr. Slope.\n\n\"But I look on you and Mr. Slope as the very antipodes of men,\" said she. \"There is nothing in which you are not each the reverse of the other, except in belonging to the same profession--and even in that you are so unlike as perfectly to maintain the rule. He is gregarious; you are given to solitude. He is active; you are passive. He works; you think. He likes women; you despise them. He is fond of position and power; and so are you, but for directly different reasons. He loves to be praised; you very foolishly abhor it. He will gain his rewards, which will be an insipid, useful wife, a comfortable income, and a reputation for sanctimony; you will also gain yours.\"\n\n\"Well, and what will they be?\" said Mr. Arabin, who knew that he was being flattered and yet suffered himself to put up with it. \"What will be my rewards?\"\n\n\"The heart of some woman whom you will be too austere to own that you love, and the respect of some few friends which you will be too proud to own that you value.\"\n\n\"Rich rewards,\" said he; \"but of little worth, if they are to be so treated.\"\n\n\"Oh, you are not to look for such success as awaits Mr. Slope. He is born to be a successful man. He suggests to himself an object and then starts for it with eager intention. Nothing will deter him from his pursuit. He will have no scruples, no fears, no hesitation. His desire is to be a bishop with a rising family--the wife will come first, and in due time the apron. You will see all this, and then--\"\n\n\"Well, and what then?\"\n\n\"Then you will begin to wish that you had done the same.\"\n\nMr. Arabin looked placidly out at the lawn and, resting his shoulder on the head of the sofa, rubbed his chin with his hand. It was a trick he had when he was thinking deeply, and what the signora said made him think. Was it not all true? Would he not hereafter look back, if not at Mr. Slope, at some others, perhaps not equally gifted with himself, who had risen in the world while he had lagged behind, and then wish that he had done the same?\n\n\"Is not such the doom of all speculative men of talent?\" said she. \"Do they not all sit wrapt as you now are, cutting imaginary silken cords with their fine edges, while those not so highly tempered sever the everyday Gordian knots of the world's struggle and win wealth and renown? Steel too highly polished, edges too sharp, do not do for this world's work, Mr. Arabin.\"\n\nWho was this woman that thus read the secrets of his heart and re-uttered to him the unwelcome bodings of his own soul? He looked full into her face when she had done speaking and said, \"Am I one of those foolish blades, too sharp and too fine to do a useful day's work?\"\n\n\"Why do you let the Slopes of the world outdistance you?\" said she. \"Is not the blood in your veins as warm as his? Does not your pulse beat as fast? Has not God made you a man and intended you to do a man's work here, ay, and to take a man's wages also?\"\n\nMr. Arabin sat ruminating, rubbing his face, and wondering why these things were said to him, but he replied nothing. The signora went on:\n\n\"The greatest mistake any man ever made is to suppose that the good things of the world are not worth the winning. And it is a mistake so opposed to the religion which you preach! Why does God permit his bishops one after another to have their five thousands and ten thousands a year if such wealth be bad and not worth having? Why are beautiful things given to us, and luxuries and pleasant enjoyments, if they be not intended to be used? They must be meant for someone, and what is good for a layman surely cannot be bad for a clerk. You try to despise these good things, but you only try--you don't succeed.\"\n\n\"Don't I?\" said Mr. Arabin, still musing, not knowing what he said.\n\n\"I ask you the question: do you succeed?\"\n\nMr. Arabin looked at her piteously. It seemed to him as though he were being interrogated by some inner spirit of his own, to whom he could not refuse an answer, and to whom he did not dare to give a false reply.\n\n\"Come, Mr. Arabin, confess; do you succeed? Is money so contemptible? Is worldly power so worthless? Is feminine beauty a trifle to be so slightly regarded by a wise man?\"\n\n\"Feminine beauty!\" said he, gazing into her face, as though all the feminine beauty in the world were concentrated there. \"Why do you say I do not regard it?\"\n\n\"If you look at me like that, Mr. Arabin, I shall alter my opinion--or should do so, were I not of course aware that I have no beauty of my own worth regarding.\"\n\nThe gentleman blushed crimson, but the lady did not blush at all. A slightly increased colour animated her face, just so much so as to give her an air of special interest. She expected a compliment from her admirer, but she was rather gratified than otherwise by finding that he did not pay it to her. Messrs. Slope and Thorne, Messrs. Brown, Jones, and Robinson, they all paid her compliments. She was rather in hopes that she would ultimately succeed in inducing Mr. Arabin to abuse her.\n\n\"But your gaze,\" said she, \"is one of wonder, not of admiration. You wonder at my audacity in asking you such questions about yourself.\"\n\n\"Well, I do rather,\" said he.\n\n\"Nevertheless, I expect an answer, Mr. Arabin. Why were women made beautiful if men are not to regard them?\"\n\n\"But men do regard them,\" he replied.\n\n\"And why not you?\"\n\n\"You are begging the question, Madame Neroni.\"\n\n\"I am sure I shall beg nothing, Mr. Arabin, which you will not grant, and I do beg for an answer. Do you not as a rule think women below your notice as companions? Let us see. There is the Widow Bold looking round at you from her chair this minute. What would you say to her as a companion for life?\"\n\nMr. Arabin, rising from his position, leaned over the sofa and looked through the drawing-room door to the place where Eleanor was seated between Bertie Stanhope and Mr. Slope. She at once caught his glance and averted her own. She was not pleasantly placed in her present position. Mr. Slope was doing his best to attract her attention, and she was striving to prevent his doing so by talking to Mr. Stanhope, while her mind was intently fixed on Mr. Arabin and Madame Neroni. Bertie Stanhope endeavoured to take advantage of her favours, but he was thinking more of the manner in which he would by and by throw himself at her feet than of amusing her at the present moment.\n\n\"There,\" said the signora. \"She was stretching her beautiful neck to look at you, and now you have disturbed her. Well, I declare I believe I am wrong about you; I believe that you do think Mrs. Bold a charming woman. Your looks seem to say so, and by her looks I should say that she is jealous of me. Come, Mr. Arabin, confide in me, and if it is so, I'll do all in my power to make up the match.\"\n\nIt is needless to say that the signora was not very sincere in her offer. She was never sincere on such subjects. She never expected others to be so, nor did she expect others to think her so. Such matters were her playthings, her billiard table, her hounds and hunters, her waltzes and polkas, her picnics and summer-day excursions. She had little else to amuse her, and therefore played at love-making in all its forms. She was now playing at it with Mr. Arabin, and did not at all expect the earnestness and truth of his answer.\n\n\"All in your power would be nothing,\" said he, \"for Mrs. Bold is, I imagine, already engaged to another.\"\n\n\"Then you own the impeachment yourself.\"\n\n\"You cross-question me rather unfairly,\" he replied, \"and I do not know why I answer you at all. Mrs. Bold is a very beautiful woman, and as intelligent as beautiful. It is impossible to know her without admiring her.\"\n\n\"So you think the widow a very beautiful woman?\"\n\n\"Indeed I do.\"\n\n\"And one that would grace the parsonage of St. Ewold's.\"\n\n\"One that would well grace any man's house.\"\n\n\"And you really have the effrontery to tell me this,\" said she; \"to tell me, who, as you very well know, set up to be a beauty myself, and who am at this very moment taking such an interest in your affairs, you really have the effrontery to tell me that Mrs. Bold is the most beautiful woman you know.\"\n\n\"I did not say so,\" said Mr. Arabin; \"you are more beautiful--\"\n\n\"Ah, come now, that is something like. I thought you could not be so unfeeling.\"\n\n\"You are more beautiful, perhaps more clever.\"\n\n\"Thank you, thank you, Mr. Arabin. I knew that you and I should be friends.\"\n\n\"But--\"\n\n\"Not a word further. I will not hear a word further. If you talk till midnight you cannot improve what you have said.\"\n\n\"But Madame Neroni, Mrs. Bold--\"\n\n\"I will not hear a word about Mrs. Bold. Dread thoughts of strychnine did pass across my brain, but she is welcome to the second place.\"\n\n\"Her place--\"\n\n\"I won't hear anything about her or her place. I am satisfied, and that is enough. But Mr. Arabin, I am dying with hunger; beautiful and clever as I am, you know I cannot go to my food, and yet you do not bring it to me.\"\n\nThis at any rate was so true as to make it necessary that Mr. Arabin should act upon it, and he accordingly went into the dining-room and supplied the signora's wants.\n\n\"And yourself?\" said she.\n\n\"Oh,\" said he, \"I am not hungry. I never eat at this hour.\"\n\n\"Come, come, Mr. Arabin, don't let love interfere with your appetite. It never does with mine. Give me half a glass more champagne and then go to the table. Mrs. Bold will do me an injury if you stay talking to me any longer.\"\n\nMr. Arabin did as he was bid. He took her plate and glass from her and, going into the dining-room, helped himself to a sandwich from the crowded table and began munching it in a corner.\n\nAs he was doing so Miss Thorne, who had hardly sat down for a moment, came into the room and, seeing him standing, was greatly distressed.\n\n\"Oh, my dear Mr. Arabin,\" said she, \"have you never sat down yet? I am so distressed. You of all men, too.\"\n\nMr. Arabin assured her that he had only just come into the room.\n\n\"That is the very reason why you should lose no more time. Come, I'll make room for you. Thank'ee, my dear,\" she said, seeing that Mrs. Bold was making an attempt to move from her chair, \"but I would not for worlds see you stir, for all the ladies would think it necessary to follow. But, perhaps, if Mr. Stanhope has done--just for a minute, Mr. Stanhope, till I can get another chair.\"\n\nAnd so Bertie had to rise to make way for his rival. This he did, as he did everything, with an air of good-humoured pleasantry which made it impossible for Mr. Arabin to refuse the proffered seat.\n\n\"His bishopric let another take,\" said Bertie, the quotation being certainly not very appropriate either for the occasion or the person spoken to. \"I have eaten and am satisfied; Mr. Arabin, pray take my chair. I wish for your sake that it really was a bishop's seat.\"\n\nMr. Arabin did sit down, and as he did so Mrs. Bold got up as though to follow her neighbour.\n\n\"Pray, pray don't move,\" said Miss Thorne, almost forcing Eleanor back into her chair. \"Mr. Stanhope is not going to leave us. He will stand behind you like a true knight as he is. And now I think of it, Mr. Arabin, let me introduce you to Mr. Slope. Mr. Slope, Mr. Arabin.\" And the two gentlemen bowed stiffly to each other across the lady whom they both intended to marry, while the other gentleman who also intended to marry her stood behind, watching them.\n\nThe two had never met each other before, and the present was certainly not a good opportunity for much cordial conversation, even if cordial conversation between them had been possible. As it was, the whole four who formed the party seemed as though their tongues were tied. Mr. Slope, who was wide awake to what he hoped was his coming opportunity, was not much concerned in the interest of the moment. His wish was to see Eleanor move, that he might pursue her. Bertie was not exactly in the same frame of mind; the evil day was near enough; there was no reason why he should precipitate it. He had made up his mind to marry Eleanor Bold if he could, and was resolved to-day to take the first preliminary step towards doing so. But there was time enough before him. He was not going to make an offer of marriage over the table-cloth. Having thus good-naturedly made way for Mr. Arabin, he was willing also to let him talk to the future Mrs. Stanhope as long as they remained in their present position.\n\nMr. Arabin, having bowed to Mr. Slope, began eating his food without saying a word further. He was full of thought, and though he ate he did so unconsciously.\n\nBut poor Eleanor was the most to be pitied. The only friend on whom she thought she could rely was Bertie Stanhope, and he, it seemed, was determined to desert her. Mr. Arabin did not attempt to address her. She said a few words in reply to some remarks from Mr. Slope and then, feeling the situation too much for her, started from her chair in spite of Miss Thorne and hurried from the room. Mr. Slope followed her, and young Stanhope lost the occasion.\n\nMadeline Neroni, when she was left alone, could not help pondering much on the singular interview she had had with this singular man. Not a word that she had spoken to him had been intended by her to be received as true, and yet he had answered her in the very spirit of truth. He had done so, and she had been aware that he had so done. She had wormed from him his secret, and he, debarred as it would seem from man's usual privilege of lying, had innocently laid bare his whole soul to her. He loved Eleanor Bold, but Eleanor was not in his eye so beautiful as herself. He would fain have Eleanor for his wife, but yet he had acknowledged that she was the less gifted of the two. The man had literally been unable to falsify his thoughts when questioned, and had been compelled to be true malgré lui, even when truth must have been so disagreeable to him.\n\nThis teacher of men, this Oxford pundit, this double-distilled quintessence of university perfection, this writer of religious treatises, this speaker of ecclesiastical speeches, had been like a little child in her hands; she had turned him inside out and read his very heart as she might have done that of a young girl. She could not but despise him for his facile openness, and yet she liked him for it, too. It was a novelty to her, a new trait in a man's character. She felt also that she could never so completely make a fool of him as she did of the Slopes and Thornes. She felt that she never could induce Mr. Arabin to make protestations to her that were not true, or to listen to nonsense that was mere nonsense.\n\nIt was quite clear that Mr. Arabin was heartily in love with Mrs. Bold; and the signora, with very unwonted good nature, began to turn it over in her mind whether she could not do him a good turn. Of course Bertie was to have the first chance. It was an understood family arrangement that her brother was, if possible, to marry the Widow Bold. Madeline knew too well his necessities and what was due to her sister to interfere with so excellent a plan, as long as it might be feasible. But she had strong suspicion that it was not feasible. She did not think it likely that Mrs. Bold would accept a man in her brother's position, and she had frequently said so to Charlotte. She was inclined to believe that Mr. Slope had more chance of success, and with her it would be a labour of love to rob Mr. Slope of his wife.\n\nAnd so the signora resolved, should Bertie fail, to do a good-natured act for once in her life and give up Mr. Arabin to the woman whom he loved.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX. The Lookalofts and the Greenacres\n\nOn the whole, Miss Thorne's provision for the amusement and feeding of the outer classes in the exoteric paddock was not unsuccessful.\n\nTwo little drawbacks to the general happiness did take place, but they were of a temporary nature, and apparent rather than real. The first was the downfall of young Harry Greenacre, and the other the uprise of Mrs. Lookaloft and her family.\n\nAs to the quintain, it became more popular among the boys on foot than it would ever have been among the men on horseback, even had young Greenacre been more successful. It was twirled round and round till it was nearly twirled out of the ground, and the bag of flour was used with great gusto in powdering the backs and heads of all who could be coaxed within its vicinity.\n\nOf course it was reported all through the assemblage that Harry was dead, and there was a pathetic scene between him and his mother when it was found that he had escaped scatheless from the fall. A good deal of beer was drunk on the occasion, and the quintain was \"dratted\" and \"bothered,\" and very generally anathematized by all the mothers who had young sons likely to be placed in similar jeopardy. But the affair of Mrs. Lookaloft was of a more serious nature.\n\n\"I do tell 'ee plainly--face to face--she be there in madam's drawing-room; herself and Gussy, and them two walloping gals, dressed up to their very eyeses.\" This was said by a very positive, very indignant, and very fat farmer's wife, who was sitting on the end of a bench leaning on the handle of a huge, cotton umbrella.\n\n\"But: you didn't zee her, Dame Guffern?\" said Mrs. Greenacre, whom this information, joined to the recent peril undergone by her son, almost overpowered. Mr. Greenacre held just as much land as Mr. Lookaloft, paid his rent quite as punctually, and his opinion in the vestry room was reckoned to be every whit as good. Mrs. Lookaloft's rise in the world had been wormwood to Mrs. Greenacre. She had no taste herself for the sort of finery which had converted Barleystubb farm into Rosebank and which had occasionally graced Mr. Lookaloft's letters with the dignity of esquirehood. She had no wish to convert her own homestead into Violet Villa, or to see her goodman go about with a new-fangled handle to his name. But it was a mortal injury to her that Mrs. Lookaloft should be successful in her hunt after such honours. She had abused and ridiculed Mrs. Lookaloft to the extent of her little power. She had pushed against her going out of church, and had excused herself with all the easiness of equality. \"Ah, dame, I axes pardon, but you be grown so mortal stout these times.\" She had inquired with apparent cordiality of Mr. Lookaloft after \"the woman that owned him,\" and had, as she thought, been on the whole able to hold her own pretty well against her aspiring neighbour. Now, however, she found herself distinctly put into a separate and inferior class. Mrs. Lookaloft was asked into the Ullathorne drawing-room merely because she called her house Rosebank and had talked over her husband into buying pianos and silk dresses instead of putting his money by to stock farms for his sons.\n\nMrs. Greenacre, much as she reverenced Miss Thorne, and highly as she respected her husband's landlord, could not but look on this as an act of injustice done to her and hers. Hitherto the Lookalofts had never been recognized as being of a different class from the Greenacres. Their pretensions were all self-pretensions, their finery was all paid for by themselves and not granted to them by others. The local sovereigns of the vicinity, the district fountains of honour, had hitherto conferred on them the stamp of no rank. Hitherto their crinoline petticoats, late hours, and mincing gait had been a fair subject of Mrs. Greenacre's raillery, and this raillery had been a safety-valve for her envy. Now, however, and from henceforward, the case would be very different. Now the Lookalofts would boast that their aspirations had been sanctioned by the gentry of the country; now they would declare with some show of truth that their claims to peculiar consideration had been recognized. They had sat as equal guests in the presence of bishops and baronets; they had been curtseyed to by Miss Thorne on her own drawing-room carpet; they were about to sit down to table in company with a live countess! Bab Lookaloft, as she had always been called by the young Greenacres in the days of their juvenile equality, might possibly sit next to the Honourable George, and that wretched Gussy might be permitted to hand a custard to the Lady Margaretta De Courcy.\n\nThe fruition of those honours, or such of them as fell to the lot of the envied family, was not such as should have caused much envy. The attention paid to the Lookalofts by the De Courcys was very limited, and the amount of entertainment which they received from the bishop's society was hardly in itself a recompense for the dull monotony of their day. But of what they endured Mrs. Greenacre took no account; she thought only of what she considered they must enjoy, and of the dreadfully exalted tone of living which would be manifested by the Rosebank family, as the consequence of their present distinction.\n\n\"But did 'ee zee 'em there, dame, did 'ee zee 'em there with your own eyes?\" asked poor Mrs. Greenacre, still hoping that there might be some ground for doubt.\n\n\"And how could I do that, unless so be I was there myself?\" asked Mrs. Guffern. \"I didn't zet eyes on none of them this blessed morning, but I zee'd them as did. You know our John; well, he will be for keeping company with Betsey Rusk, madam's own maid, you know. And Betsey isn't none of your common kitchen wenches. So Betsey, she come out to our John, you know, and she's always vastly polite to me, is Betsey Rusk, I must say. So before she took so much as one turn with John she told me every ha'porth that was going on up in the house.\"\n\n\"Did she now?\" said Mrs. Greenacre.\n\n\"Indeed she did,\" said Mrs. Guffern.\n\n\"And she told you them people was up there in the drawing-room?\"\n\n\"She told me she zee'd 'em come in--that they was dressed finer by half nor any of the family, with all their neckses and buzoms stark naked as a born babby.\"\n\n\"The minxes!\" exclaimed Mrs. Greenacre, who felt herself more put about by this than any other mark of aristocratic distinction which her enemies had assumed.\n\n\"Yes, indeed,\" continued Mrs. Guffern, \"as naked as you please, while all the quality was dressed just as you and I be, Mrs. Greenacre.\"\n\n\"Drat their impudence,\" said Mrs. Greenacre, from whose well-covered bosom all milk of human kindness was receding, as far as the family of the Lookalofts were concerned.\n\n\"So says I,\" said Mrs. Guffern; \"and so says my goodman, Thomas Guffern, when he hear'd it. 'Molly,' says he to me, 'if ever you takes to going about o' mornings with yourself all naked in them ways, I begs you won't come back no more to the old house.' So says I, 'Thomas, no more I wull.' 'But,' says he, 'drat it, how the deuce does she manage with her rheumatiz, and she not a rag on her;'\" and Mrs. Guffern laughed loudly as she thought of Mrs. Lookaloft's probable sufferings from rheumatic attacks.\n\n\"But to liken herself that way to folk that ha' blood in their veins,\" said Mrs. Greenacre.\n\n\"Well, but that warn't all neither that Betsey told. There they all swelled into madam's drawing-room, like so many turkey cocks, as much as to say, 'and who dare say no to us?' and Gregory was thinking of telling of 'em to come down here, only his heart failed him 'cause of the grand way they was dressed. So in they went, but madam looked at them as glum as death.\"\n\n\"Well, now,\" said Mrs. Greenacre, greatly relieved, \"so they wasn't axed different from us at all then?\"\n\n\"Betsey says that Gregory says that madam wasn't a bit too well pleased to see them where they was, and that to his believing they was expected to come here just like the rest of us.\"\n\nThere was great consolation in this. Not that Mrs. Greenacre was altogether satisfied. She felt that justice to herself demanded that Mrs. Lookaloft should not only not be encouraged, but that she should also be absolutely punished. What had been done at that scriptural banquet, of which Mrs. Greenacre so often read the account to her family? Why had not Miss Thorne boldly gone to the intruder and said, \"Friend, thou hast come up hither to high places not fitted to thee. Go down lower, and thou wilt find thy mates.\" Let the Lookalofts be treated at the present moment with ever so cold a shoulder, they would still be enabled to boast hereafter of their position, their aspirations, and their honour.\n\n\"Well, with all her grandeur, I do wonder that she be so mean,\" continued Mrs. Greenacre, unable to dismiss the subject. \"Did you hear, goodman?\" she went on, about to repeat the whole story to her husband who then came up. \"There's Dame Lookaloft and Bab and Gussy and the lot of 'em all sitting as grand as fivepence in madam's drawing-room, and they not axed no more nor you nor me. Did you ever hear tell the like o' that?\"\n\n\"Well, and what for shouldn't they?\" said Farmer Greenacre.\n\n\"Likening theyselves to the quality, as though they was estated folk, or the like o' that!\" said Mrs. Guffern.\n\n\"Well, if they likes it, and madam likes it, they's welcome for me,\" said the farmer. \"Now I likes this place better, 'cause I be more at home-like, and don't have to pay for them fine clothes for the missus. Everyone to his taste, Mrs. Guffern, and if neighbour Lookaloft thinks that he has the best of it, he's welcome.\"\n\nMrs. Greenacre sat down by her husband's side to begin the heavy work of the banquet, and she did so in some measure with restored tranquillity, but nevertheless she shook her head at her gossip to show that in this instance she did not quite approve of her husband's doctrine.\n\n\"And I'll tell 'ee what, dames,\" continued he; \"if so be that we cannot enjoy the dinner that madam gives us because Mother Lookaloft is sitting up there on a grand sofa, I think we ought all to go home. If we greet at that, what'll we do when true sorrow comes across us? How would you be now, Dame, if the boy there had broke his neck when he got the tumble?\"\n\nMrs. Greenacre was humbled and said nothing further on the matter. But let prudent men such as Mr. Greenacre preach as they will, the family of the Lookalofts certainly does occasion a good deal of heart-burning in the world at large.\n\nIt was pleasant to see Mr. Plomacy as, leaning on his stout stick, he went about among the rural guests, acting as a sort of head constable as well as master of the revels. \"Now, young'un, if you can't manage to get along without that screeching, you'd better go to the other side of the twelve-acre field and take your dinner with you. Come, girls, what do you stand there for, twirling of your thumbs? Come out, and let the lads see you; you've no need to be so ashamed of your faces. Hollo there, who are you? How did you make your way in here?\"\n\nThis last disagreeable question was put to a young man of about twenty-four who did not, in Mr. Plomacy's eye, bear sufficient vestiges of a rural education and residence.\n\n\"If you please, your Worship, Master Barrell the coachman let me in at the church wicket, 'cause I do be working mostly al'ays for the family.\"\n\n\"Then Master Barrell the coachman may let you out again,\" said Mr. Plomacy, not even conciliated by the magisterial dignity which had been conceded to him. \"What's your name? And what trade are you? And who do you work for?\"\n\n\"I'm Stubbs, your worship, Bob Stubbs; and--and--and--\"\n\n\"And what's your trade, Stubbs?\"\n\n\"Plasterer, please your worship.\"\n\n\"I'll plaster you, and Barrell too; you'll just walk out of this 'ere field as quick as you walked in. We don't want no plasterers; when we do, we'll send for 'em. Come my buck, walk.\"\n\nStubbs the plasterer was much downcast at this dreadful edict. He was a sprightly fellow, and had contrived since his ingress into the Ullathorne elysium to attract to himself a forest nymph, to whom he was whispering a plasterer's usual soft nothings, when he was encountered by the great Mr. Plomacy. It was dreadful to be thus dissevered from his dryad and sent howling back to a Barchester pandemonium just as the nectar and ambrosia were about to descend on the fields of asphodel. He began to try what prayers would do, but city prayers were vain against the great rural potentate. Not only did Mr. Plomacy order his exit but, raising his stick to show the way which led to the gate that had been left in the custody of that false Cerberus Barrell, proceeded himself to see the edict of banishment carried out.\n\nThe goddess Mercy, however, the sweetest goddess that ever sat upon a cloud, and the dearest to poor, frail, erring man, appeared on the field in the person of Mr. Greenacre. Never was interceding goddess more welcome.\n\n\"Come, man,\" said Mr. Greenacre, \"never stick at trifles such a day as this. I know the lad well. Let him bide at my axing. Madam won't miss what he can eat and drink, I know.\"\n\nNow Mr. Plomacy and Mr. Greenacre were sworn friends. Mr. Plomacy had at his own disposal as comfortable a room as there was in Ullathorne House, but he was a bachelor, and alone there, and, moreover, smoking in the house was not allowed even to Mr. Plomacy. His moments of truest happiness were spent in a huge armchair in the warmest corner of Mrs. Greenacre's beautifully clean front kitchen. 'Twas there that the inner man dissolved itself and poured itself out in streams of pleasant chat; 'twas there that he was respected and yet at his ease; 'twas there, and perhaps there only, that he could unburden himself from the ceremonies of life without offending the dignity of those above him, or incurring the familiarity of those below. 'Twas there that his long pipe was always to be found on the accustomed chimney-board, not only permitted but encouraged.\n\nSuch being the state of the case, it was not to be supposed that Mr. Plomacy could refuse such a favour to Mr. Greenacre; but nevertheless he did not grant it without some further show of austere authority.\n\n\"Eat and drink, Mr. Greenacre! No. It's not what he eats and drinks, but the example such a chap shows, coming in where he's not invited--a chap of his age, too. He too that never did a day's work about Ullathorne since he was born. Plasterer! I'll plaster him!\"\n\n\"He worked long enough for me, then, Mr. Plomacy. And a good hand he is at setting tiles as any in Barchester,\" said the other, not sticking quite to veracity, as indeed mercy never should. \"Come, come, let him alone to-day and quarrel with him to-morrow. You wouldn't shame him before his lass there?\"\n\n\"It goes against the grain with me, then,\" said Mr. Plomacy. \"And take care, you Stubbs, and behave yourself. If I hear a row, I shall know where it comes from. I'm up to you Barchester journeymen; I know what stuff you're made of.\"\n\nAnd so Stubbs went off happy, pulling at the forelock of his shock head of hair in honour of the steward's clemency and giving another double pull at it in honour of the farmer's kindness. And as he went he swore within his grateful heart that if ever Farmer Greenacre wanted a day's work done for nothing, he was the lad to do it for him. Which promise it was not probable that he would ever be called on to perform.\n\nBut Mr. Plomacy was not quite happy in his mind, for he thought of the unjust steward and began to reflect whether he had not made for himself friends of the mammon of unrighteousness. This, however, did not interfere with the manner in which he performed his duties at the bottom of the long board; nor did Mr. Greenacre perform his the worse at the top on account of the good wishes of Stubbs the plasterer. Moreover the guests did not think it anything amiss when Mr. Plomacy, rising to say grace, prayed that God would make them all truly thankful for the good things which Madame Thorne in her great liberality had set before them!\n\nAll this time the quality in the tent on the lawn were getting on swimmingly--that is, if champagne without restriction can enable quality folk to swim. Sir Harkaway Gorse proposed the health of Miss Thorne, and likened her to a blood race-horse, always in condition and not to be tired down by any amount of work. Mr. Thorne returned thanks, saying he hoped his sister would always be found able to run when called upon, and then gave the health and prosperity of the De Courcy family. His sister was very much honoured by seeing so many of them at her poor board. They were all aware that important avocations made the absence of the earl necessary. As his duty to his prince had called him from his family hearth, he, Mr. Thorne, could not venture to regret that he did not see him at Ullathorne; but nevertheless he would venture to say--that was, to express a wish--an opinion, he meant to say--And so Mr. Thorne became somewhat gravelled, as country gentlemen in similar circumstances usually do; but he ultimately sat down, declaring that he had much satisfaction in drinking the noble earl's health, together with that of the countess, and all the family of De Courcy Castle.\n\nAnd then the Honourable George returned thanks. We will not follow him through the different periods of his somewhat irregular eloquence. Those immediately in his neighbourhood found it at first rather difficult to get him on his legs, but much greater difficulty was soon experienced in inducing him to resume his seat. One of two arrangements should certainly be made in these days: either let all speech-making on festive occasions be utterly tabooed and made as it were impossible; or else let those who are to exercise the privilege be first subjected to a competing examination before the civil-service examining commissioners. As it is now, the Honourable Georges do but little honour to our exertions in favour of British education.\n\nIn the dining-room the bishop went through the honours of the day with much more neatness and propriety. He also drank Miss Thorne's health, and did it in a manner becoming the bench which he adorned. The party there was perhaps a little more dull, a shade less lively than that in the tent. But what was lost in mirth was fully made up in decorum.\n\nAnd so the banquets passed off at the various tables with great éclat and universal delight.\n\n# CHAPTER XL. Ullathorne Sports--Act II\n\n\"That which has made them drunk has made me bold.\" 'Twas thus that Mr. Slope encouraged himself, as he left the dining-room in pursuit of Eleanor. He had not indeed seen in that room any person really intoxicated, but there had been a good deal of wine drunk, and Mr. Slope had not hesitated to take his share, in order to screw himself up to the undertaking which he had in hand. He is not the first man who has thought it expedient to call in the assistance of Bacchus on such an occasion.\n\nEleanor was out through the window and on the grass before she perceived that she was followed. Just at that moment the guests were nearly all occupied at the tables. Here and there were to be seen a constant couple or two, who preferred their own sweet discourse to the jingle of glasses or the charms of rhetoric which fell from the mouths of the Honourable George and the Bishop of Barchester; but the grounds were as nearly vacant as Mr. Slope could wish them to be.\n\nEleanor saw that she was pursued, and as a deer, when escape is no longer possible, will turn to bay and attack the hounds, so did she turn upon Mr. Slope.\n\n\"Pray don't let me take you from the room,\" said she, speaking with all the stiffness which she knew how to use. \"I have come out to look for a friend. I must beg of you, Mr. Slope, to go back.\"\n\nBut Mr. Slope would not be thus entreated. He had observed all day that Mrs. Bold was not cordial to him, and this had to a certain extent oppressed him. But he did not deduce from this any assurance that his aspirations were in vain. He saw that she was angry with him. Might she not be so because he had so long tampered with her feelings--might it not arise from his having, as he knew was the case, caused her name to be bruited about in conjunction with his own without having given her the opportunity of confessing to the world that henceforth their names were to be one and the same? Poor lady. He had within him a certain Christian conscience-stricken feeling of remorse on this head. It might be that he had wronged her by his tardiness. He had, however, at the present moment imbibed too much of Mr. Thorne's champagne to have any inward misgivings. He was right in repeating the boast of Lady Macbeth: he was not drunk, but he was bold enough for anything. It was a pity that in such a state he could not have encountered Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"You must permit me to attend you,\" said he; \"I could not think of allowing you to go alone.\"\n\n\"Indeed you must, Mr. Slope,\" said Eleanor still very stiffly, \"for it is my special wish to be alone.\"\n\nThe time for letting the great secret escape him had already come. Mr. Slope saw that it must be now or never, and he was determined that it should be now. This was not his first attempt at winning a fair lady. He had been on his knees, looked unutterable things with his eyes, and whispered honeyed words before this. Indeed, he was somewhat an adept at these things, and had only to adapt to the perhaps different taste of Mrs. Bold the well-remembered rhapsodies which had once so much gratified Olivia Proudie.\n\n\"Do not ask me to leave you, Mrs. Bold,\" said he with an impassioned look, impassioned and sanctified as well, with that sort of look which is not uncommon with gentlemen of Mr. Slope's school and which may perhaps be called the tender-pious. \"Do not ask me to leave you till I have spoken a few words with which my heart is full--which I have come hither purposely to say.\"\n\nEleanor saw how it was now. She knew directly what it was she was about to go through, and very miserable the knowledge made her. Of course she could refuse Mr. Slope, and there would be an end of that, one might say. But there would not be an end of it, as far as Eleanor was concerned. The very fact of Mr. Slope's making an offer to her would be a triumph to the archdeacon and, in a great measure, a vindication of Mr. Arabin's conduct. The widow could not bring herself to endure with patience the idea that she had been in the wrong. She had defended Mr. Slope, she had declared herself quite justified in admitting him among her acquaintance, had ridiculed the idea of his considering himself as more than an acquaintance, and had resented the archdeacon's caution in her behalf: now it was about to be proved to her in a manner sufficiently disagreeable that the archdeacon had been right, and she herself had been entirely wrong.\n\n\"I don't know what you can have to say to me, Mr. Slope, that you could not have said when we were sitting at table just now;\" and she closed her lips, and steadied her eyeballs, and looked at him in a manner that ought to have frozen him.\n\nBut gentlemen are not easily frozen when they are full of champagne, and it would not at any time have been easy to freeze Mr. Slope.\n\n\"There are things, Mrs. Bold, which a man cannot well say before a crowd; which perhaps he cannot well say at any time; which indeed he may most fervently desire to get spoken, and which he may yet find it almost impossible to utter. It is such things as these that I now wish to say to you;\" and then the tender-pious look was repeated, with a little more emphasis even than before.\n\nEleanor had not found it practicable to stand stock still before the dining-room window, there receive his offer in full view of Miss Thorne's guests. She had therefore in self-defence walked on, and thus Mr. Slope had gained his object of walking with her. He now offered her his arm.\n\n\"Thank you, Mr. Slope, I am much obliged to you; but for the very short time that I shall remain with you I shall prefer walking alone.\"\n\n\"And must it be so short?\" said he. \"Must it be--\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Eleanor, interrupting him, \"as short as possible, if you please, sir.\"\n\n\"I had hoped, Mrs. Bold--I had hoped--\"\n\n\"Pray hope nothing, Mr. Slope, as far as I am concerned; pray do not; I do not know and need not know what hope you mean. Our acquaintance is very slight, and will probably remain so. Pray, pray let that be enough; there is at any rate no necessity for us to quarrel.\"\n\nMrs. Bold was certainly treating Mr. Slope rather cavalierly, and he felt it so. She was rejecting him before he had offered himself, and informing him at the same time that he was taking a great deal too much on himself to be so familiar. She did not even make an attempt\n\nFrom such a sharp and waspish word as \"no\" To pluck the sting.\n\nHe was still determined to be very tender and very pious, seeing that, in spite of all Mrs. Bold had said to him, he had not yet abandoned hope; but he was inclined also to be somewhat angry. The widow was bearing herself, as he thought, with too high a hand, was speaking of herself in much too imperious a tone. She had clearly no idea that an honour was being conferred on her. Mr. Slope would be tender as long as he could, but he began to think if that failed it would not be amiss if he also mounted himself for awhile on his high horse. Mr. Slope could undoubtedly be very tender, but he could be very savage also, and he knew his own abilities.\n\n\"That is cruel,\" said he, \"and unchristian, too. The worst of us are still bidden to hope. What have I done that you should pass on me so severe a sentence?\" And then he paused a moment, during which the widow walked steadily on with measured steps, saying nothing further.\n\n\"Beautiful woman,\" at last he burst forth, \"beautiful woman, you cannot pretend to be ignorant that I adore you. Yes, Eleanor, yes, I love you. I love you with the truest affection which man can bear to woman. Next to my hopes of heaven are my hopes of possessing you.\" (Mr. Slope's memory here played him false, or he would not have omitted the deanery.) \"How sweet to walk to heaven with you by my side, with you for my guide, mutual guides. Say, Eleanor, dearest Eleanor, shall we walk that sweet path together?\"\n\nEleanor had no intention of ever walking together with Mr. Slope on any other path than that special one of Miss Thorne's which they now occupied, but as she had been unable to prevent the expression of Mr. Slope's wishes and aspirations, she resolved to hear him out to the end before she answered him.\n\n\"Ah, Eleanor,\" he continued, and it seemed to be his idea that as he had once found courage to pronounce her Christian name, he could not utter it often enough. \"Ah, Eleanor, will it not be sweet, with the Lord's assistance, to travel hand in hand through this mortal valley which His mercies will make pleasant to us, till hereafter we shall dwell together at the foot of His throne?\" And then a more tenderly pious glance than ever beamed from the lover's eyes. \"Ah, Eleanor--\"\n\n\"My name, Mr. Slope, is Mrs. Bold,\" said Eleanor, who, though determined to hear out the tale of his love, was too much disgusted by his blasphemy to be able to bear much more of it.\n\n\"Sweetest angel, be not so cold,\" said he, and as he said it the champagne broke forth, and he contrived to pass his arm round her waist. He did this with considerable cleverness, for up to this point Eleanor had contrived with tolerable success to keep her distance from him. They had got into a walk nearly enveloped by shrubs, and Mr. Slope therefore no doubt considered that as they were now alone it was fitting that he should give her some outward demonstration of that affection of which he talked so much. It may perhaps be presumed that the same stamp of measures had been found to succeed with Olivia Proudie. Be this as it may, it was not successful with Eleanor Bold.\n\nShe sprang from him as she would have jumped from an adder, but she did not spring far--not, indeed, beyond arm's length--and then, quick as thought, she raised her little hand and dealt him a box on the ear with such right goodwill that it sounded among the trees like a miniature thunderclap.\n\nAnd now it is to be feared that every well-bred reader of these pages will lay down the book with disgust, feeling that, after all, the heroine is unworthy of sympathy. She is a hoyden, one will say. At any rate she is not a lady, another will exclaim. I have suspected her all through, a third will declare; she has no idea of the dignity of a matron, or of the peculiar propriety which her position demands. At one moment she is romping with young Stanhope; then she is making eyes at Mr. Arabin; anon she comes to fisticuffs with a third lover--and all before she is yet a widow of two years' standing.\n\nShe cannot altogether be defended, and yet it may be averred that she is not a hoyden, not given to romping nor prone to boxing. It were to be wished devoutly that she had not struck Mr. Slope in the face. In doing so she derogated from her dignity and committed herself. Had she been educated in Belgravia, had she been brought up by any sterner mentor than that fond father, had she lived longer under the rule of a husband, she might, perhaps, have saved herself from this great fault. As it was, the provocation was too much for her, the temptation to instant resentment of the insult too strong. She was too keen in the feeling of independence, a feeling dangerous for a young woman, but one in which her position peculiarly tempted her to indulge. And then Mr. Slope's face, tinted with a deeper dye than usual by the wine he had drunk, simpering and puckering itself with pseudo-pity and tender grimaces, seemed specially to call for such punishment. She had, too, a true instinct as to the man; he was capable of rebuke in this way and in no other. To him the blow from her little hand was as much an insult as a blow from a man would have been to another. It went directly to his pride. He conceived himself lowered in his dignity and personally outraged. He could almost have struck at her again in his rage. Even the pain was a great annoyance to him, and the feeling that his clerical character had been wholly disregarded sorely vexed him.\n\nThere are such men: men who can endure no taint on their personal self-respect, even from a woman; men whose bodies are to themselves such sacred temples that a joke against them is desecration, and a rough touch downright sacrilege. Mr. Slope was such a man, and therefore the slap on the face that he got from Eleanor was, as far as he was concerned, the fittest rebuke which could have been administered to him.\n\nBut nevertheless, she should not have raised her hand against the man. Ladies' hands, so soft, so sweet, so delicious to the touch, so graceful to the eye, so gracious in their gentle doings, were not made to belabour men's faces. The moment the deed was done Eleanor felt that she had sinned against all propriety, and would have given little worlds to recall the blow. In her first agony of sorrow she all but begged the man's pardon. Her next impulse, however, and the one which she obeyed, was to run away.\n\n\"I never, never will speak another word to you,\" she said, gasping with emotion and the loss of breath which her exertion and violent feelings occasioned her, and so saying she put foot to the ground and ran quickly back along the path to the house.\n\nBut how shall I sing the divine wrath of Mr. Slope, or how invoke the tragic muse to describe the rage which swelled the celestial bosom of the bishop's chaplain? Such an undertaking by no means befits the low-heeled buskin of modern fiction. The painter put a veil over Agamemnon's face when called on to depict the father's grief at the early doom of his devoted daughter. The god, when he resolved to punish the rebellious winds, abstained from mouthing empty threats. We will not attempt to tell with what mighty surgings of the inner heart Mr. Slope swore to revenge himself on the woman who had disgraced him, nor will we vainly strive to depict his deep agony of soul.\n\nThere he is, however, alone in the garden walk, and we must contrive to bring him out of it. He was not willing to come forth quite at once. His cheek was stinging with the weight of Eleanor's fingers, and he fancied that everyone who looked at him would be able to see on his face the traces of what he had endured. He stood awhile, becoming redder and redder with rage. He stood motionless, undecided, glaring with his eyes, thinking of the pains and penalties of Hades, and meditating how he might best devote his enemy to the infernal gods with all the passion of his accustomed eloquence. He longed in his heart to be preaching at her. 'Twas thus that he was ordinarily avenged of sinning mortal men and women. Could he at once have ascended his Sunday rostrum and fulminated at her such denunciations as his spirit delighted in, his bosom would have been greatly eased.\n\nBut how preach to Mr. Thorne's laurels, or how preach indeed at all in such a vanity fair as this now going on at Ullathorne? And then he began to feel a righteous disgust at the wickedness of the doings around him. He had been justly chastised for lending, by his presence, a sanction to such worldly lures. The gaiety of society, the mirth of banquets, the laughter of the young, and the eating and drinking of the elders were, for awhile, without excuse in his sight. What had he now brought down upon himself by sojourning thus in the tents of the heathen? He had consorted with idolaters round the altars of Baal, and therefore a sore punishment had come upon him. He then thought of the Signora Neroni, and his soul within him was full of sorrow. He had an inkling--a true inkling--that he was a wicked, sinful man, but it led him in no right direction; he could admit no charity in his heart. He felt debasement coming on him, and he longed to shake it off, to rise up in his stirrup, to mount to high places and great power, that he might get up into a mighty pulpit and preach to the world a loud sermon against Mrs. Bold.\n\nThere he stood fixed to the gravel for about ten minutes. Fortune favoured him so far that no prying eyes came to look upon him in his misery. Then a shudder passed over his whole frame; he collected himself and slowly wound his way round to the lawn, advancing along the path and not returning in the direction which Eleanor had taken. When he reached the tent, he found the bishop standing there in conversation with the Master of Lazarus. His lordship had come out to air himself after the exertion of his speech.\n\n\"This is very pleasant--very pleasant, my lord, is it not?\" said Mr. Slope with his most gracious smile, pointing to the tent; \"very pleasant. It is delightful to see so many persons enjoying themselves so thoroughly.\"\n\nMr. Slope thought he might force the bishop to introduce him to Dr. Gwynne. A very great example had declared and practised the wisdom of being everything to everybody, and Mr. Slope was desirous of following it. His maxim was never to lose a chance. The bishop, however, at the present moment was not very anxious to increase Mr. Slope's circle of acquaintance among his clerical brethren. He had his own reasons for dropping any marked allusion to his domestic chaplain, and he therefore made his shoulder rather cold for the occasion.\n\n\"Very, very,\" said he without turning round, or even deigning to look at Mr. Slope. \"And therefore, Dr. Gwynne, I really think that you will find that the hebdomadal board will exercise as wide and as general an authority as at the present moment. I, for one, Dr. Gwynne--\"\n\n\"Dr. Gwynne,\" said Mr. Slope, raising his hat and resolving not to be outwitted by such an insignificant little goose as the Bishop of Barchester.\n\nThe Master of Lazarus also raised his hat and bowed very politely to Mr. Slope. There is not a more courteous gentleman in the queen's dominions than the Master of Lazarus.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Mr. Slope, \"pray do me the honour of introducing me to Dr. Gwynne. The opportunity is too much in my favour to be lost.\"\n\nThe bishop had no help for it. \"My chaplain, Dr. Gwynne,\" said he, \"my present chaplain, Mr. Slope.\" He certainly made the introduction as unsatisfactory to the chaplain as possible, and by the use of the word \"present\" seemed to indicate that Mr. Slope might probably not long enjoy the honour which he now held. But Mr. Slope cared nothing for this. He understood the innuendo, and disregarded it. It might probably come to pass that he would be in a situation to resign his chaplaincy before the bishop was in a situation to dismiss him from it. What need the future Dean of Barchester care for the bishop, or for the bishop's wife? Had not Mr. Slope, just as he was entering Dr. Stanhope's carriage, received an all-important note from Tom Towers of \"The Jupiter\"? Had he not that note this moment in his pocket?\n\nSo disregarding the bishop, he began to open out a conversation with the Master of Lazarus.\n\nBut suddenly an interruption came, not altogether unwelcome to Mr. Slope. One of the bishop's servants came up to his master's shoulder with a long, grave face and whispered into the bishop's ear.\n\n\"What is it, John?\" said the bishop.\n\n\"The dean, my lord; he is dead.\"\n\nMr. Slope had no further desire to converse with the Master of Lazarus, and was very soon on his road back to Barchester.\n\nEleanor, as we have said, having declared her intention of never holding further communication with Mr. Slope, ran hurriedly back towards the house. The thought, however, of what she had done grieved her greatly, and she could not abstain from bursting into tears. 'Twas thus she played the second act in that day's melodrama.\n\n# CHAPTER XLI. Mrs. Bold Confides Her Sorrow to Her Friend Miss Stanhope\n\nWhen Mrs. Bold came to the end of the walk and faced the lawn, she began to bethink herself what she should do. Was she to wait there till Mr. Slope caught her, or was she to go in among the crowd with tears in her eyes and passion in her face? She might in truth have stood there long enough without any reasonable fear of further immediate persecution from Mr. Slope, but we are all inclined to magnify the bugbears which frighten us. In her present state of dread she did not know of what atrocity he might venture to be guilty. Had anyone told her a week ago that he would have put his arm round her waist at this party of Miss Thorne's, she would have been utterly incredulous. Had she been informed that he would be seen on the following Sunday walking down the High Street in a scarlet coat and top boots, she would not have thought such a phenomenon more improbable.\n\nBut this improbable iniquity he had committed, and now there was nothing she could not believe of him. In the first place it was quite manifest that he was tipsy; in the next place it was to be taken as proved that all his religion was sheer hypocrisy; and finally the man was utterly shameless. She therefore stood watching for the sound of his footfall, not without some fear that he might creep out at her suddenly from among the bushes.\n\nAs she thus stood she saw Charlotte Stanhope at a little distance from her, walking quickly across the grass. Eleanor's handkerchief was in her hand, and putting it to her face so as to conceal her tears, she ran across the lawn and joined her friend.\n\n\"Oh, Charlotte,\" she said, almost too much out of breath to speak very plainly; \"I am so glad I have found you.\"\n\n\"Glad you have found me!\" said Charlotte, laughing; \"that's a good joke. Why Bertie and I have been looking for you everywhere. He swears that you have gone off with Mr. Slope, and is now on the point of hanging himself.\"\n\n\"Oh, Charlotte, don't,\" said Mrs. Bold.\n\n\"Why, my child, what on earth is the matter with you?\" said Miss Stanhope, perceiving that Eleanor's hand trembled on her own arm, and finding also that her companion was still half-choked by tears. \"Goodness heaven! Something has distressed you. What is it? What can I do for you?\"\n\nEleanor answered her only by a sort of spasmodic gurgle in her throat. She was a good deal upset, as people say, and could not at the moment collect herself.\n\n\"Come here, this way, Mrs. Bold; come this way, and we shall not be seen. What has happened to vex you so? What can I do for you? Can Bertie do anything?\"\n\n\"Oh, no, no, no, no,\" said Eleanor. \"There is nothing to be done. Only that horrid man--\"\n\n\"What horrid man?\" asked Charlotte.\n\nThere are some moments in life in which both men and women feel themselves imperatively called on to make a confidence, in which not to do so requires a disagreeable resolution and also a disagreeable suspicion. There are people of both sexes who never make confidences, who are never tempted by momentary circumstances to disclose their secrets, but such are generally dull, close, unimpassioned spirits, \"gloomy gnomes, who live in cold dark mines.\" There was nothing of the gnome about Eleanor, and she therefore resolved to tell Charlotte Stanhope the whole story about Mr. Slope.\n\n\"That horrid man; that Mr. Slope,\" said she. \"Did you not see that he followed me out of the dining-room?\"\n\n\"Of course I did, and was sorry enough, but I could not help it. I knew you would be annoyed. But you and Bertie managed it badly between you.\"\n\n\"It was not his fault nor mine either. You know how I disliked the idea of coming in the carriage with that man.\"\n\n\"I am sure I am very sorry if that has led to it.\"\n\n\"I don't know what has led to it,\" said Eleanor, almost crying again. \"But it has not been my fault.\"\n\n\"But what has he done, my dear?\"\n\n\"He's an abominable, horrid, hypocritical man, and it would serve him right to tell the bishop all about it.\"\n\n\"Believe me, if you want to do him an injury, you had far better tell Mrs. Proudie. But what did he do, Mrs. Bold?\"\n\n\"Ugh!\" exclaimed Eleanor.\n\n\"Well, I must confess he's not very nice,\" said Charlotte Stanhope.\n\n\"Nice!\" said Eleanor. \"He is the most fulsome, fawning, abominable man I ever saw. What business had he to come to me?--I that never gave him the slightest tittle of encouragement--I that always hated him, though I did take his part when others ran him down.\"\n\n\"That's just where it is, my dear. He has heard that and therefore fancied that of course you were in love with him.\"\n\nThis was wormwood to Eleanor. It was in fact the very thing which all her friends had been saying for the last month past--and which experience now proved to be true. Eleanor resolved within herself that she would never again take any man's part. The world, with all its villainy and all its ill-nature, might wag as it liked: she would not again attempt to set crooked things straight.\n\n\"But what did he do, my dear?\" said Charlotte, who was really rather interested in the subject.\n\n\"He--he--he--\"\n\n\"Well--come, it can't have been anything so very horrid, for the man was not tipsy.\"\n\n\"Oh, I am sure he was\" said Eleanor. \"I am sure he must have been tipsy.\"\n\n\"Well, I declare I didn't observe it. But what was it, my love?\"\n\n\"Why, I believe I can hardly tell you. He talked such horrid stuff that you never heard the like: about religion, and heaven, and love. Oh, dear--he is such a nasty man.\"\n\n\"I can easily imagine the sort of stuff he would talk. Well--and then--?\"\n\n\"And then--he took hold of me.\"\n\n\"Took hold of you?\"\n\n\"Yes--he somehow got close to me and took hold of me--\"\n\n\"By the waist?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Eleanor shuddering.\n\n\"And then--\"\n\n\"Then I jumped away from him, and gave him a slap on the face, and ran away along the path till I saw you.\"\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" Charlotte Stanhope laughed heartily at the finale to the tragedy. It was delightful to her to think that Mr. Slope had had his ears boxed. She did not quite appreciate the feeling which made her friend so unhappy at the result of the interview. To her thinking the matter had ended happily enough as regarded the widow, who indeed was entitled to some sort of triumph among her friends. Whereas to Mr. Slope would be due all those gibes and jeers which would naturally follow such an affair. His friends would ask him whether his ears tingled whenever he saw a widow, and he would be cautioned that beautiful things were made to be looked at and not to be touched.\n\nSuch were Charlotte Stanhope's views on such matters, but she did not at the present moment clearly explain them to Mrs. Bold. Her object was to endear herself to her friend, and therefore, having had her laugh, she was ready enough to offer sympathy. Could Bertie do anything? Should Bertie speak to the man and warn him that in future he must behave with more decorum? Bertie indeed, she declared, would be more angry than anyone else when he heard to what insult Mrs. Bold had been subjected.\n\n\"But you won't tell him?\" said Mrs. Bold with a look of horror.\n\n\"Not if you don't like it,\" said Charlotte; \"but considering everything, I would strongly advise it. If you had a brother, you know, it would be unnecessary. But it is very right that Mr. Slope should know that you have somebody by you that will and can protect you.\"\n\n\"But my father is here.\"\n\n\"Yes, but it is so disagreeable for clergymen to have to quarrel with each other; and circumstanced as your father is just at this moment, it would be very inexpedient that there should be anything unpleasant between him and Mr. Slope. Surely you and Bertie are intimate enough for you to permit him to take your part.\"\n\nCharlotte Stanhope was very anxious that her brother should at once on that very day settle matters with his future wife. Things had now come to that point between him and his father, and between him and his creditors, that he must either do so, or leave Barchester; either do that, or go back to his unwashed associates, dirty lodgings, and poor living at Carrara. Unless he could provide himself with an income, he must go to Carrara, or to ----. His father the prebendary had not said this in so many words, but had he done so, he could not have signified it more plainly.\n\nSuch being the state of the case it was very necessary that no more time should be lost. Charlotte had seen her brother's apathy, when he neglected to follow Mrs. Bold out of the room, with anger which she could hardly suppress. It was grievous to think that Mr. Slope should have so distanced him. Charlotte felt that she had played her part with sufficient skill. She had brought them together and induced such a degree of intimacy that her brother was really relieved from all trouble and labour in the matter. And moreover it was quite plain that Mrs. Bold was very fond of Bertie. And now it was plain enough also that he had nothing to fear from his rival, Mr. Slope.\n\nThere was certainly an awkwardness in subjecting Mrs. Bold to a second offer on the same day. It would have been well perhaps to have put the matter off for a week, could a week have been spared. But circumstances are frequently too peremptory to be arranged as we would wish to arrange them, and such was the case now. This being so, could not this affair of Mr. Slope's be turned to advantage? Could it not be made the excuse for bringing Bertie and Mrs. Bold into still closer connexion--into such close connexion that they could not fail to throw themselves into each other's arms? Such was the game which Miss Stanhope now at a moment's notice resolved to play.\n\nAnd very well she played it. In the first place it was arranged that Mr. Slope should not return in the Stanhopes' carriage to Barchester. It so happened that Mr. Slope was already gone, but of that of course they knew nothing. The signora should be induced to go first, with only the servants and her sister, and Bertie should take Mr. Slope's place in the second journey. Bertie was to be told in confidence of the whole affair, and when the carriage was gone off with its first load, Eleanor was to be left under Bertie's special protection, so as to insure her from any further aggression from Mr. Slope. While the carriage was getting ready, Bertie was to seek out that gentleman and make him understand that he must provide himself with another conveyance back to Barchester. Their immediate object should be to walk about together in search of Bertie. Bertie in short was to be the Pegasus on whose wings they were to ride out of their present dilemma.\n\nThere was a warmth of friendship and cordial kindliness in all this that was very soothing to the widow; but yet, though she gave way to it, she was hardly reconciled to doing so. It never occurred to her that, now that she had killed one dragon, another was about to spring up in her path; she had no remote idea that she would have to encounter another suitor in her proposed protector, but she hardly liked the thought of putting herself so much into the hands of young Stanhope. She felt that if she wanted protection, she should go to her father. She felt that she should ask him to provide a carriage for her back to Barchester. Mrs. Clantantram she knew would give her a seat. She knew that she should not throw herself entirely upon friends whose friendship dated, as it were, but from yesterday. But yet she could not say no to one who was so sisterly in her kindness, so eager in her good nature, so comfortably sympathetic as Charlotte Stanhope. And thus she gave way to all the propositions made to her.\n\nThey first went into the dining-room, looking for their champion, and from thence to the drawing-room. Here they found Mr. Arabin, still hanging over the signora's sofa; or rather they found him sitting near her head, as a physician might have sat had the lady been his patient. There was no other person in the room. The guests were some in the tent, some few still in the dining room, some at the bows and arrows, but most of them walking with Miss Thorne through the park and looking at the games that were going on.\n\nAll that had passed, and was passing between Mr. Arabin and the lady, it is unnecessary to give in detail. She was doing with him as she did with all others. It was her mission to make fools of men, and she was pursuing her mission with Mr. Arabin. She had almost got him to own his love for Mrs. Bold and had subsequently almost induced him to acknowledge a passion for herself. He, poor man, was hardly aware what he was doing or saying, hardly conscious whether he was in heaven or in hell. So little had he known of female attractions of that peculiar class which the signora owned, that he became affected with a kind of temporary delirium when first subjected to its power. He lost his head rather than this heart, and toppled about mentally, reeling in his ideas as a drunken man does on his legs. She had whispered to him words that really meant nothing but which, coming from such beautiful lips and accompanied by such lustrous glances, seemed to have a mysterious significance, which he felt though he could not understand.\n\nIn being thus besirened, Mr. Arabin behaved himself very differently from Mr. Slope. The signora had said truly that the two men were the contrasts of each other--that the one was all for action, the other all for thought. Mr. Slope, when this lady laid upon his senses the overpowering breath of her charms, immediately attempted to obtain some fruition, to achieve some mighty triumph. He began by catching at her hand and progressed by kissing it. He made vows of love and asked for vows in return. He promised everlasting devotion, knelt before her, and swore that had she been on Mount Ida, Juno would have had no cause to hate the offspring of Venus. But Mr. Arabin uttered no oaths, kept his hand mostly in his trousers pocket, and had no more thought of kissing Madame Neroni than of kissing the Countess De Courcy.\n\nAs soon as Mr. Arabin saw Mrs. Bold enter the room he blushed and rose from his chair; then he sat down again, and then again got up. The signora saw the blush at once and smiled at the poor victim, but Eleanor was too much confused to see anything.\n\n\"Oh, Madeline,\" said Charlotte, \"I want to speak to you particularly; we must arrange about the carriage, you know,\" and she stooped down to whisper to her sister. Mr. Arabin immediately withdrew to a little distance, and as Charlotte had in fact much to explain before she could make the new carriage arrangement intelligible, he had nothing to do but to talk to Mrs. Bold.\n\n\"We have had a very pleasant party,\" said he, using the tone he would have used had he declared that the sun was shining very brightly, or the rain falling very fast.\n\n\"Very,\" said Eleanor, who never in her life had passed a more unpleasant day.\n\n\"I hope Mr. Harding has enjoyed himself.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, very much,\" said Eleanor, who had not seen her father since she parted from him soon after her arrival.\n\n\"He returns to Barchester to-night, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Yes, I believe so--that is, I think he is staying at Plumstead.\"\n\n\"Oh, staying at Plumstead,\" said Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"He came from there this morning. I believe he is going back, he didn't exactly say, however.\"\n\n\"I hope Mrs. Grantly is quite well.\"\n\n\"She seemed to be quite well. She is here; that is, unless she has gone away.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, to be sure. I was talking to her. Looking very well indeed.\" Then there was a considerable pause; for Charlotte could not at once make Madeline understand why she was to be sent home in a hurry without her brother.\n\n\"Are you returning to Plumstead, Mrs. Bold?\" Mr. Arabin merely asked this by way of making conversation, but he immediately perceived that he was approaching dangerous ground.\n\n\"No,\" said Mrs. Bold very quietly; \"I am going home to Barchester.\"\n\n\"Oh, ah, yes. I had forgotten that you had returned.\" And then Mr. Arabin, finding it impossible to say anything further, stood silent till Charlotte had completed her plans, and Mrs. Bold stood equally silent, intently occupied as it appeared in the arrangement of her rings.\n\nAnd yet these two people were thoroughly in love with each other; and though one was a middle-aged clergyman, and the other a lady at any rate past the wishy-washy bread-and-butter period of life, they were as unable to tell their own minds to each other as any Damon and Phillis, whose united ages would not make up that to which Mr. Arabin had already attained.\n\nMadeline Neroni consented to her sister's proposal, and then the two ladies again went off in quest of Bertie Stanhope.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII. Ullathorne Sports--Act III\n\nAnd now Miss Thorne's guests were beginning to take their departure, and the amusement of those who remained was becoming slack. It was getting dark, and ladies in morning costumes were thinking that, if they were to appear by candlelight, they ought to readjust themselves. Some young gentlemen had been heard to talk so loud that prudent mammas determined to retire judiciously, and the more discreet of the male sex, whose libations had been moderate, felt that there was not much more left for them to do.\n\nMorning parties, as a rule, are failures. People never know how to get away from them gracefully. A picnic on an island or a mountain or in a wood may perhaps be permitted. There is no master of the mountain bound by courtesy to bid you stay while in his heart he is longing for your departure. But in a private house or in private grounds a morning party is a bore. One is called on to eat and drink at unnatural hours. One is obliged to give up the day, which is useful, and is then left without resource for the evening, which is useless. One gets home fagged and désoeuvré, and yet at an hour too early for bed. There is no comfortable resource left. Cards in these genteel days are among the things tabooed, and a rubber of whist is impracticable.\n\nAll this began now to be felt. Some young people had come with some amount of hope that they might get up a dance in the evening, and were unwilling to leave till all such hope was at an end. Others, fearful of staying longer than was expected, had ordered their carriages early, and were doing their best to go, solicitous for their servants and horses. The countess and her noble brood were among the first to leave, and as regarded the Hon. George, it was certainly time that he did so. Her ladyship was in a great fret and fume. Those horrid roads would, she was sure, be the death of her if unhappily she were caught in them by the dark night. The lamps she was assured were good, but no lamp could withstand the jolting of the roads of East Barsetshire. The De Courcy property lay in the western division of the county.\n\nMrs. Proudie could not stay when the countess was gone. So the bishop was searched for by the Revs. Messrs. Grey and Green and found in one corner of the tent enjoying himself thoroughly in a disquisition on the hebdomadal board. He obeyed, however, the behests of his lady without finishing the sentence in which he was promising to Dr. Gwynne that his authority at Oxford should remain unimpaired, and the episcopal horses turned their noses towards the palatial stables. Then the Grantlys went. Before they did so, Mr. Harding managed to whisper a word into his daughter's ear. Of course, he said, he would undeceive the Grantlys as to that foolish rumour about Mr. Slope.\n\n\"No, no, no,\" said Eleanor; \"pray do not--pray wait till I see you. You will be home in a day or two, and then I will explain to you everything.\"\n\n\"I shall be home to-morrow,\" said he.\n\n\"I am so glad,\" said Eleanor. \"You will come and dine with me, and then we shall be so comfortable.\"\n\nMr. Harding promised. He did not exactly know what there was to be explained, or why Dr. Grantly's mind should not be disabused of the mistake into which he had fallen, but nevertheless he promised. He owed some reparation to his daughter, and he thought that he might best make it by obedience.\n\nAnd thus the people were thinning off by degrees as Charlotte and Eleanor walked about in quest of Bertie. Their search might have been long had they not happened to hear his voice. He was comfortably ensconced in the ha-ha, with his back to the sloping side, smoking a cigar, and eagerly engaged in conversation with some youngster from the further side of the county, whom he had never met before, who was also smoking under Bertie's pupilage and listening with open ears to an account given by his companion of some of the pastimes of Eastern clime.\n\n\"Bertie, I am seeking you everywhere,\" said Charlotte. \"Come up here at once.\"\n\nBertie looked up out of the ha-ha and saw the two ladies before him. As there was nothing for him but to obey, he got up and threw away his cigar. From the first moment of his acquaintance with her he had liked Eleanor Bold. Had he been left to his own devices, had she been penniless, and had it then been quite out of the question that he should marry her, he would most probably have fallen violently in love with her. But now he could not help regarding her somewhat as he did the marble workshops at Carrara, as he had done his easel and palette, as he had done the lawyer's chambers in London--in fact, as he had invariably regarded everything by which it had been proposed to him to obtain the means of living. Eleanor Bold appeared before him, no longer as a beautiful woman, but as a new profession called matrimony. It was a profession indeed requiring but little labour, and one in which an income was insured to him. But nevertheless he had been as it were goaded on to it; his sister had talked to him of Eleanor, just as she had talked of busts and portraits. Bertie did not dislike money, but he hated the very thought of earning it. He was now called away from his pleasant cigar to earn it, by offering himself as a husband to Mrs. Bold. The work indeed was made easy enough, for in lieu of his having to seek the widow, the widow had apparently come to seek him.\n\nHe made some sudden absurd excuse to his auditor and then, throwing away his cigar, climbed up the wall of the ha-ha and joined the ladies on the lawn.\n\n\"Come and give Mrs. Bold an arm,\" said Charlotte, \"while I set you on a piece of duty which, as a preux chevalier, you must immediately perform. Your personal danger will, I fear, be insignificant, as your antagonist is a clergyman.\"\n\nBertie immediately gave his arm to Eleanor, walking between her and his sister. He had lived too long abroad to fall into the Englishman's habit of offering each an arm to two ladies at the same time--a habit, by the by, which foreigners regard as an approach to bigamy, or a sort of incipient Mormonism.\n\nThe little history of Mr. Slope's misconduct was then told to Bertie by his sister, Eleanor's ears tingling the while. And well they might tingle. If it were necessary to speak of the outrage at all, why should it be spoken of to such a person as Mr. Stanhope, and why in her own hearing? She knew she was wrong, and was unhappy and dispirited, yet she could think of no way to extricate herself, no way to set herself right. Charlotte spared her as much as she possibly could, spoke of the whole thing as though Mr. Slope had taken a glass of wine too much, said that of course there would be nothing more about it, but that steps must be taken to exclude Mr. Slope from the carriage.\n\n\"Mrs. Bold need be under no alarm about that,\" said Bertie, \"for Mr. Slope has gone this hour past. He told me that business made it necessary that he should start at once for Barchester.\"\n\n\"He is not so tipsy, at any rate, but what he knows his fault,\" said Charlotte. \"Well, my dear, that is one difficulty over. Now I'll leave you with your true knight and get Madeline off as quickly as I can. The carriage is here, I suppose, Bertie?\"\n\n\"It has been here for the last hour.\"\n\n\"That's well. Good-bye, my dear. Of course you'll come in to tea. I shall trust to you to bring her, Bertie, even by force if necessary.\" And so saying, Charlotte ran off across the lawn, leaving her brother alone with the widow.\n\nAs Miss Stanhope went off, Eleanor bethought herself that, as Mr. Slope had taken his departure, there no longer existed any necessity for separating Mr. Stanhope from his sister Madeline, who so much needed his aid. It had been arranged that he should remain so as to preoccupy Mr. Slope's place in the carriage, and act as a social policeman to effect the exclusion of that disagreeable gentleman. But Mr. Slope had effected his own exclusion, and there was no possible reason now why Bertie should not go with his sister--at least Eleanor saw none, and she said as much.\n\n\"Oh, let Charlotte have her own way,\" said he. \"She has arranged it, and there will be no end of confusion if we make another change. Charlotte always arranges everything in our house and rules us like a despot.\"\n\n\"But the signora?\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"Oh, the signora can do very well without me. Indeed, she will have to do without me,\" he added, thinking rather of his studies in Carrara than of his Barchester hymeneals.\n\n\"Why, you are not going to leave us?\" asked Eleanor.\n\nIt has been said that Bertie Stanhope was a man without principle. He certainly was so. He had no power of using active mental exertion to keep himself from doing evil. Evil had no ugliness in his eyes; virtue no beauty. He was void of any of these feelings which actuate men to do good. But he was perhaps equally void of those which actuate men to do evil. He got into debt with utter recklessness, thinking nothing as to whether the tradesmen would ever be paid or not. But he did not invent active schemes of deceit for the sake of extracting the goods of others. If a man gave him credit, that was the man's look-out; Bertie Stanhope troubled himself nothing further. In borrowing money he did the same; he gave people references to \"his governor;\" told them that the \"old chap\" had a good income; and agreed to pay sixty per cent for the accommodation. All this he did without a scruple of conscience; but then he never contrived active villainy.\n\nIn this affair of his marriage it had been represented to him as a matter of duty that he ought to put himself in possession of Mrs. Bold's hand and fortune, and at first he had so regarded it. About her he had thought but little. It was the customary thing for men situated as he was to marry for money, and there was no reason why he should not do what others around him did. And so he consented. But now he began to see the matter in another light. He was setting himself down to catch this woman, as a cat sits to catch a mouse. He was to catch her, and swallow her up, her and her child, and her houses and land, in order that he might live on her instead of on his father. There was a cold, calculating, cautious cunning about this quite at variance with Bertie's character. The prudence of the measure was quite as antagonistic to his feelings as the iniquity.\n\nAnd then, should he be successful, what would be the reward? Having satisfied his creditors with half of the widow's fortune, he would be allowed to sit down quietly at Barchester, keeping economical house with the remainder. His duty would be to rock the cradle of the late Mr. Bold's child, and his highest excitement a demure party at Plumstead Rectory, should it ultimately turn out that the archdeacon would be sufficiently reconciled to receive him.\n\nThere was very little in the programme to allure such a man as Bertie Stanhope. Would not the Carrara workshop, or whatever worldly career fortune might have in store for him, would not almost anything be better than this? The lady herself was undoubtedly all that was desirable, but the most desirable lady becomes nauseous when she has to be taken as a pill. He was pledged to his sister, however, and let him quarrel with whom he would, it behoved him not to quarrel with her. If she were lost to him, all would be lost that he could ever hope to derive henceforward from the paternal roof-tree. His mother was apparently indifferent to his weal or woe, to his wants or his warfare. His father's brow got blacker and blacker from day to day, as the old man looked at his hopeless son. And as for Madeline--poor Madeline, whom of all of them he liked the best--she had enough to do to shift for herself. No; come what might, he must cling to his sister and obey her behests, let them be ever so stern--or at the very least seem to obey them. Could not some happy deceit bring him through in this matter, so that he might save appearances with his sister and yet not betray the widow to her ruin? What if he made a confederate of Eleanor? 'Twas in this spirit that Bertie Stanhope set about his wooing.\n\n\"But you are not going to leave Barchester?\" asked Eleanor.\n\n\"I do not know,\" he replied; \"I hardly know yet what I am going to do. But it is at any rate certain that I must do something.\"\n\n\"You mean about your profession?\" said she.\n\n\"Yes, about my profession, if you can call it one.\"\n\n\"And is it not one?\" said Eleanor. \"Were I a man, I know none I should prefer to it, except painting. And I believe the one is as much in your power as the other.\"\n\n\"Yes, just about equally so,\" said Bertie with a little touch of inward satire directed at himself. He knew in his heart that he would never make a penny by either.\n\n\"I have often wondered, Mr. Stanhope, why you do not exert yourself more,\" said Eleanor, who felt a friendly fondness for the man with whom she was walking. \"But I know it is very impertinent in me to say so.\"\n\n\"Impertinent!\" said he. \"Not so, but much too kind. It is much too kind in you to take any interest in so idle a scamp.\"\n\n\"But you are not a scamp, though you are perhaps idle. And I do take an interest in you, a very great interest,\" she added in a voice which almost made him resolve to change his mind. \"And when I call you idle, I know you are only so for the present moment. Why can't you settle steadily to work here in Barchester?\"\n\n\"And make busts of the bishop, dean, and chapter? Or perhaps, if I achieve a great success, obtain a commission to put up an elaborate tombstone over a prebendary's widow, a dead lady with a Grecian nose, a bandeau, and an intricate lace veil; lying of course on a marble sofa from among the legs of which death will be creeping out and poking at his victim with a small toasting-fork.\"\n\nEleanor laughed, but yet she thought that if the surviving prebendary paid the bill, the object of the artist as a professional man would in a great measure be obtained.\n\n\"I don't know about the dean and chapter and the prebendary's widow,\" said Eleanor. \"Of course you must take them as they come. But the fact of your having a great cathedral in which such ornaments are required could not but be in your favour.\"\n\n\"No real artist could descend to the ornamentation of a cathedral,\" said Bertie, who had his ideas of the high ecstatic ambition of art, as indeed all artists have who are not in receipt of a good income. \"Buildings should be fitted to grace the sculpture, not the sculpture to grace the building.\"\n\n\"Yes, when the work of art is good enough to merit it. Do you, Mr. Stanhope, do something sufficiently excellent and we ladies of Barchester will erect for it a fitting receptacle. Come, what shall the subject be?\"\n\n\"I'll put you in your pony chair, Mrs. Bold, as Dannecker put Ariadne on her lion. Only you must promise to sit for me.\"\n\n\"My ponies are too tame, I fear, and my broad-brimmed straw hat will not look so well in marble as the lace veil of the prebendary's wife.\"\n\n\"If you will not consent to that, Mrs. Bold, I will consent to try no other subject in Barchester.\"\n\n\"You are determined then to push your fortune in other lands?\"\n\n\"I am determined,\" said Bertie slowly and significantly, as he tried to bring up his mind to a great resolve; \"I am determined in this matter to be guided wholly by you.\"\n\n\"Wholly by me?\" said Eleanor, astonished at, and not quite liking, his altered manner.\n\n\"Wholly by you,\" said Bertie, dropping his companion's arm and standing before her on the path. In their walk they had come exactly to the spot in which Eleanor had been provoked into slapping Mr. Slope's face. Could it be possible that this place was peculiarly unpropitious to her comfort? Could it be possible that she should here have to encounter yet another amorous swain?\n\n\"If you will be guided by me, Mr. Stanhope, you will set yourself down to steady and persevering work, and you will be ruled by your father as to the place in which it will be most advisable for you to do so.\"\n\n\"Nothing could be more prudent, if only it were practicable. But now, if you will let me, I will tell you how it is that I will be guided by you, and why. Will you let me tell you?\"\n\n\"I really do not know what you can have to tell.\"\n\n\"No, you cannot know. It is impossible that you should. But we have been very good friends, Mrs. Bold, have we not?\"\n\n\"Yes, I think we have,\" said she, observing in his demeanour an earnestness very unusual with him.\n\n\"You were kind enough to say just now that you took an interest in me, and I was perhaps vain enough to believe you.\"\n\n\"There is no vanity in that; I do so as your sister's brother--and as my own friend also.\"\n\n\"Well, I don't deserve that you should feel so kindly towards me,\" said Bertie, \"but upon my word I am very grateful for it,\" and he paused awhile, hardly knowing how to introduce the subject that he had in hand.\n\nAnd it was no wonder that he found it difficult. He had to make known to his companion the scheme that had been prepared to rob her of her wealth, he had to tell her that he had intended to marry her without loving her, or else that he loved her without intending to marry her; and he had also to bespeak from her not only his own pardon, but also that of his sister, and induce Mrs. Bold to protest in her future communion with Charlotte that an offer had been duly made to her and duly rejected.\n\nBertie Stanhope was not prone to be very diffident of his own conversational powers, but it did seem to him that he was about to tax them almost too far. He hardly knew where to begin, and he hardly knew where he should end.\n\nBy this time Eleanor was again walking on slowly by his side, not taking his arm as she had heretofore done but listening very intently for whatever Bertie might have to say to her.\n\n\"I wish to be guided by you,\" said he; \"indeed, in this matter there is no one else who can set me right.\"\n\n\"Oh, that must be nonsense,\" said she.\n\n\"Well, listen to me now, Mrs. Bold, and if you can help it, pray don't be angry with me.\"\n\n\"Angry!\" said she.\n\n\"Oh, indeed you will have cause to be so. You know how very much attached to you my sister Charlotte is.\"\n\nEleanor acknowledged that she did.\n\n\"Indeed she is; I never knew her to love anyone so warmly on so short an acquaintance. You know also how well she loves me?\"\n\nEleanor now made no answer, but she felt the blood tingle in her cheek as she gathered from what he said the probable result of this double-barrelled love on the part of Miss Stanhope.\n\n\"I am her only brother, Mrs. Bold, and it is not to be wondered at that she should love me. But you do not yet know Charlotte--you do not know how entirely the well-being of our family hangs on her. Without her to manage for us, I do not know how we should get on from day to day. You cannot yet have observed all this.\"\n\nEleanor had indeed observed a good deal of this; she did not, however, now say so, but allowed him to proceed with his story.\n\n\"You cannot therefore be surprised that Charlotte should be most anxious to do the best for us all.\"\n\nEleanor said that she was not at all surprised.\n\n\"And she has had a very difficult game to play, Mrs. Bold--a very difficult game. Poor Madeline's unfortunate marriage and terrible accident, my mother's ill-health, my father's absence from England, and last, and worse perhaps, my own roving, idle spirit have almost been too much for her. You cannot wonder if among all her cares one of the foremost is to see me settled in the world.\"\n\nEleanor on this occasion expressed no acquiescence. She certainly supposed that a formal offer was to be made and could not but think that so singular an exordium was never before made by a gentleman in a similar position. Mr. Slope had annoyed her by the excess of his ardour. It was quite clear that no such danger was to be feared from Mr. Stanhope. Prudential motives alone actuated him. Not only was he about to make love because his sister told him, but he also took the precaution of explaining all this before he began. 'Twas thus, we may presume, that the matter presented itself to Mrs. Bold.\n\nWhen he had got so far, Bertie began poking the gravel with a little cane which he carried. He still kept moving on, but very slowly, and his companion moved slowly by his side, not inclined to assist him in the task the performance of which appeared to be difficult to him.\n\n\"Knowing how fond she is of yourself, Mrs. Bold, cannot you imagine what scheme should have occurred to her?\"\n\n\"I can imagine no better scheme, Mr. Stanhope, than the one I proposed to you just now.\"\n\n\"No,\" said he somewhat lackadaisically; \"I suppose that would be the best, but Charlotte thinks another plan might be joined with it. She wants me to marry you.\"\n\nA thousand remembrances flashed across Eleanor's mind all in a moment--how Charlotte had talked about and praised her brother, how she had continually contrived to throw the two of them together, how she had encouraged all manner of little intimacies, how she had with singular cordiality persisted in treating Eleanor as one of the family. All this had been done to secure her comfortable income for the benefit of one of the family!\n\nSuch a feeling as this is very bitter when it first impresses itself on a young mind. To the old, such plots and plans, such matured schemes for obtaining the goods of this world without the trouble of earning them, such long-headed attempts to convert \"tuum\" into \"meum\" are the ways of life to which they are accustomed. 'Tis thus that many live, and it therefore behoves all those who are well-to-do in the world to be on their guard against those who are not. With them it is the success that disgusts, not the attempt. But Eleanor had not yet learnt to look on her money as a source of danger; she had not begun to regard herself as fair game to be hunted down by hungry gentlemen. She had enjoyed the society of the Stanhopes, she had greatly liked the cordiality of Charlotte, and had been happy in her new friends. Now she saw the cause of all this kindness, and her mind was opened to a new phase of human life.\n\n\"Miss Stanhope,\" said she haughtily, \"has been contriving for me a great deal of honour, but she might have saved herself the trouble. I am not sufficiently ambitious.\"\n\n\"Pray don't be angry with her, Mrs. Bold,\" said he, \"or with me either.\"\n\n\"Certainly not with you, Mr. Stanhope,\" said she with considerable sarcasm in her tone. \"Certainly not with you.\"\n\n\"No--nor with her,\" said he imploringly.\n\n\"And why, may I ask you, Mr. Stanhope, have you told me this singular story? For I may presume I may judge by your manner of telling it that--that--that you and your sister are not exactly of one mind on the subject.\"\n\n\"No, we are not.\"\n\n\"And if so,\" said Mrs. Bold, who was now really angry with the unnecessary insult which she thought had been offered to her. \"And if so, why has it been worth your while to tell me all this?\"\n\n\"I did once think, Mrs. Bold--that you--that you--\"\n\nThe widow now again became entirely impassive, and would not lend the slightest assistance to her companion.\n\n\"I did once think that you perhaps might--might have been taught to regard me as more than a friend.\"\n\n\"Never!\" said Mrs. Bold, \"never. If I have ever allowed myself to do anything to encourage such an idea, I have been very much to blame--very much to blame indeed.\"\n\n\"You never have,\" said Bertie, who really had a good-natured anxiety to make what he said as little unpleasant as possible. \"You never have, and I have seen for some time that I had no chance--but my sister's hopes ran higher. I have not mistaken you, Mrs. Bold, though perhaps she has.\"\n\n\"Then why have you said all this to me?\"\n\n\"Because I must not anger her.\"\n\n\"And will not this anger her? Upon my word, Mr. Stanhope, I do not understand the policy of your family. Oh, how I wish I was at home!\" And as she expressed the wish she could restrain herself no longer and burst out into a flood of tears.\n\nPoor Bertie was greatly moved. \"You shall have the carriage to yourself going home,\" said he; \"at least you and my father. As for me, I can walk, or for the matter of that it does not much signify what I do.\" He perfectly understood that part of Eleanor's grief arose from the apparent necessity of her going back to Barchester in the carriage with her second suitor.\n\nThis somewhat mollified her. \"Oh, Mr. Stanhope,\" said she, \"why should you have made me so miserable? What will you have gained by telling me all this?\"\n\nHe had not even yet explained to her the most difficult part of his proposition; he had not told her that she was to be a party to the little deception which he intended to play off upon his sister. This suggestion had still to be made, and as it was absolutely necessary, he proceeded to make it.\n\nWe need not follow him through the whole of his statement. At last, and not without considerable difficulty, he made Eleanor understand why he had let her into his confidence, seeing that he no longer intended her the honour of a formal offer. At last he made her comprehend the part which she was destined to play in this little family comedy.\n\nBut when she did understand it, she was only more angry with him than ever; more angry, not only with him, but with Charlotte also. Her fair name was to be bandied about between them in different senses, and each sense false. She was to be played off by the sister against the father, and then by the brother against the sister. Her dear friend Charlotte, with all her agreeable sympathy and affection, was striving to sacrifice her for the Stanhope family welfare; and Bertie, who, as he now proclaimed himself, was over head and ears in debt, completed the compliment of owning that he did not care to have his debts paid at so great a sacrifice of himself. Then she was asked to conspire together with this unwilling suitor for the sake of making the family believe that he had in obedience to their commands done his best to throw himself thus away!\n\nShe lifted up her face when he had finished, and looking at him with much dignity, even through her tears, she said:\n\n\"I regret to say it, Mr. Stanhope, but after what has passed I believe that all intercourse between your family and myself had better cease.\"\n\n\"Well, perhaps it had,\" said Bertie naïvely; \"perhaps that will be better at any rate for a time; and then Charlotte will think you are offended at what I have done.\"\n\n\"And now I will go back to the house, if you please,\" said Eleanor. \"I can find my way by myself, Mr. Stanhope: after what has passed,\" she added, \"I would rather go alone.\"\n\n\"But I must find the carriage for you, Mrs. Bold; and I must tell my father that you will return with him alone; and I must make some excuse to him for not going with you; and I must bid the servant put you down at your own house, for I suppose you will not now choose to see them again in the close.\"\n\nThere was a truth about this, and a perspicuity in making arrangements for lessening her immediate embarrassment, which had some effect in softening Eleanor's anger. So she suffered herself to walk by his side over the now deserted lawn, till they came to the drawing-room window. There was something about Bertie Stanhope which gave him, in the estimation of everyone, a different standing from that which any other man would occupy under similar circumstances. Angry as Eleanor was, and great as was her cause for anger, she was not half as angry with him as she would have been with anyone else. He was apparently so simple, so good-natured, so unaffected and easy to talk to, that she had already half-forgiven him before he was at the drawing-room window.\n\nWhen they arrived there, Dr. Stanhope was sitting nearly alone with Mr. and Miss Thorne; one or two other unfortunates were there, who from one cause or another were still delayed in getting away, but they were every moment getting fewer in number.\n\nAs soon as he had handed Eleanor over to his father, Bertie started off to the front gate in search of the carriage, and there he waited leaning patiently against the front wall, comfortably smoking a cigar, till it came up. When he returned to the room, Dr. Stanhope and Eleanor were alone with their hosts.\n\n\"At last, Miss Thorne,\" said he cheerily, \"I have come to relieve you. Mrs. Bold and my father are the last roses of the very delightful summer you have given us, and desirable as Mrs. Bold's society always is, now at least you must be glad to see the last flowers plucked from the tree.\"\n\nMiss Thorne declared that she was delighted to have Mrs. Bold and Dr. Stanhope still with her, and Mr. Thorne would have said the same, had he not been checked by a yawn, which he could not suppress.\n\n\"Father, will you give your arm to Mrs. Bold?\" said Bertie: and so the last adieux were made, and the prebendary led out Mrs. Bold, followed by his son.\n\n\"I shall be home soon after you,\" said he as the two got into the carriage.\n\n\"Are you not coming in the carriage?\" said the father.\n\n\"No, no; I have someone to see on the road, and shall walk. John, mind you drive to Mrs. Bold's house first.\"\n\nEleanor, looking out of the window, saw him with his hat in his hand, bowing to her with his usual gay smile, as though nothing had happened to mar the tranquillity of the day. It was many a long year before she saw him again. Dr. Stanhope hardly spoke to her on her way home, and she was safely deposited by John at her own hall-door before the carriage drove into the close.\n\nAnd thus our heroine played the last act of that day's melodrama.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. Mr. and Mrs. Quiverful Are Made Happy. Mr. Slope is Encouraged by the Press\n\nBefore she started for Ullathorne, Mrs. Proudie, careful soul, caused two letters to be written, one by herself and one by her lord, to the inhabitants of Puddingdale vicarage, which made happy the hearth of those within it.\n\nAs soon as the departure of the horses left the bishop's stable-groom free for other services, that humble denizen of the diocese started on the bishop's own pony with the two dispatches. We have had so many letters lately that we will spare ourselves these. That from the bishop was simply a request that Mr. Quiverful would wait upon his lordship the next morning at 11 A.M.; that from the lady was as simply a request that Mrs. Quiverful would do the same by her, though it was couched in somewhat longer and more grandiloquent phraseology.\n\nIt had become a point of conscience with Mrs. Proudie to urge the settlement of this great hospital question. She was resolved that Mr. Quiverful should have it. She was resolved that there should be no more doubt or delay, no more refusals and resignations, no more secret negotiations carried on by Mr. Slope on his own account in opposition to her behests.\n\n\"Bishop,\" she said immediately after breakfast on the morning of that eventful day, \"have you signed the appointment yet?\"\n\n\"No, my dear, not yet; it is not exactly signed as yet.\"\n\n\"Then do it,\" said the lady.\n\nThe bishop did it, and a very pleasant day indeed he spent at Ullathorne. And when he got home, he had a glass of hot negus in his wife's sitting-room, and read the last number of the Little Dorrit of the day with great inward satisfaction. Oh, husbands, oh, my marital friends, what great comfort is there to be derived from a wife well obeyed!\n\nMuch perturbation and flutter, high expectation and renewed hopes, were occasioned at Puddingdale, by the receipt of these episcopal dispatches. Mrs. Quiverful, whose careful ear caught the sound of the pony's feet as he trotted up to the vicarage kitchen door, brought them in hurriedly to her husband. She was at the moment concocting the Irish stew destined to satisfy the noonday wants of fourteen young birds, let alone the parent couple. She had taken the letters from the man's hands between the folds of her capacious apron so as to save them from the contamination of the stew, and in this guise she brought them to her husband's desk.\n\nThey at once divided the spoil, each taking that addressed to the other. \"Quiverful,\" said she with impressive voice, \"you are to be at the palace at eleven to-morrow.\"\n\n\"And so are you, my dear,\" said he, almost gasping with the importance of the tidings--and then they exchanged letters.\n\n\"She'd never have sent for me again,\" said the lady, \"if it wasn't all right.\"\n\n\"Oh, my dear, don't be too certain,\" said the gentleman, \"Only think if it should be wrong.\"\n\n\"She'd never have sent for me, Q., if it wasn't all right,\" again argued the lady. \"She's stiff and hard and proud as piecrust, but I think she's right at bottom.\" Such was Mrs. Quiverful's verdict about Mrs. Proudie, to which in after times she always adhered. People when they get their income doubled usually think that those through whose instrumentality this little ceremony is performed are right at bottom.\n\n\"Oh, Letty!\" said Mr. Quiverful, rising from his well-worn seat.\n\n\"Oh, Q.!\" said Mrs. Quiverful, and then the two, unmindful of the kitchen apron, the greasy fingers, and the adherent Irish stew, threw themselves warmly into each other's arms.\n\n\"For heaven's sake, don't let anyone cajole you out of it again,\" said the wife.\n\n\"Let me alone for that,\" said the husband with a look of almost fierce determination, pressing his fist as he spoke rigidly on his desk, as though he had Mr. Slope's head below his knuckles and meant to keep it there.\n\n\"I wonder how soon it will be?\" said she.\n\n\"I wonder whether it will be at all?\" said he, still doubtful.\n\n\"Well, I won't say too much,\" said the lady. \"The cup has slipped twice before, and it may fall altogether this time, but I'll not believe it. He'll give you the appointment to-morrow. You'll find he will.\"\n\n\"Heaven send he may,\" said Mr. Quiverful solemnly. And who that considers the weight of the burden on this man's back will say that the prayer was an improper one? There were fourteen of them--fourteen of them living--as Mrs. Quiverful had so powerfully urged in the presence of the bishop's wife. As long as promotion cometh from any human source, whether north or south, east or west, will not such a claim as this hold good, in spite of all our examination tests, detur digniori's, and optimist tendencies? It is fervently to be hoped that it may. Till we can become divine, we must be content to be human, lest in our hurry for a change we sink to something lower.\n\nAnd then the pair, sitting down lovingly together, talked over all their difficulties, as they so often did, and all their hopes, as they so seldom were enabled to do.\n\n\"You had better call on that man, Q., as you come away from the palace,\" said Mrs. Quiverful, pointing to an angry call for money from the Barchester draper, which the postman had left at the vicarage that morning. Cormorant that he was, unjust, hungry cormorant! When rumour first got abroad that the Quiverfuls were to go to the hospital, this fellow with fawning eagerness had pressed his goods upon the wants of the poor clergyman. He had done so, feeling that he should be paid from the hospital funds, and flattering himself that a man with fourteen children, and money wherewithal to clothe them, could not but be an excellent customer. As soon as the second rumour reached him, he applied for his money angrily.\n\nAnd \"the fourteen\"--or such of them as were old enough to hope and discuss their hopes--talked over their golden future. The tall grown girls whispered to each other of possible Barchester parties, of possible allowances for dress, of a possible piano--the one they had in the vicarage was so weather-beaten with the storms of years and children as to be no longer worthy of the name--of the pretty garden, and the pretty house. 'Twas of such things it most behoved them to whisper.\n\nAnd the younger fry, they did not content themselves with whispers, but shouted to each other of their new playground beneath our dear ex-warden's well-loved elms, of their future own gardens, of marbles to be procured in the wished-for city, and of the rumour which had reached them of a Barchester school.\n\n'Twas in vain that their cautious mother tried to instil into their breasts the very feeling she had striven to banish from that of their father; 'twas in vain that she repeated to the girls that \"there's many a slip 'twixt the cup and the lip;\" 'twas in vain she attempted to make the children believe that they were to live at Puddingdale all their lives. Hopes mounted high, and would not have themselves quelled. The neighbouring farmers heard the news and came in to congratulate them. 'Twas Mrs. Quiverful herself who had kindled the fire, and in the first outbreak of her renewed expectations she did it so thoroughly that it was quite past her power to put it out again.\n\nPoor matron! Good, honest matron, doing thy duty in the state to which thou hast been called, heartily if not contentedly; let the fire burn on; on this occasion the flames will not scorch; they shall warm thee and thine. 'Tis ordained that that husband of thine, that Q. of thy bosom, shall reign supreme for years to come over the bedesmen of Hiram's Hospital.\n\nAnd the last in all Barchester to mar their hopes, had he heard and seen all that passed at Puddingdale that day, would have been Mr. Harding. What wants had he to set in opposition to those of such a regiment of young ravens? There are fourteen of them living! With him, at any rate, let us say that that argument would have been sufficient for the appointment of Mr. Quiverful.\n\nIn the morning Q. and his wife kept their appointments with that punctuality which bespeaks an expectant mind. The friendly farmer's gig was borrowed, and in that they went, discussing many things by the way. They had instructed the household to expect them back by one, and injunctions were given to the eldest pledge to have ready by that accustomed hour the remainder of the huge stew which the provident mother had prepared on the previous day. The hands of the kitchen clock came round to two, three, four, before the farmer's gig wheels were again heard at the vicarage gate. With what palpitating hearts were the returning wanderers greeted!\n\n\"I suppose, children, you all thought we were never coming back any more?\" said the mother as she slowly let down her solid foot till it rested on the step of the gig. \"Well, such a day as we've had!\" and then leaning heavily on a big boy's shoulder, she stepped once more on terra firma.\n\nThere was no need for more than the tone of her voice to tell them that all was right. The Irish stew might burn itself to cinders now.\n\nThen there was such kissing and hugging, such crying and laughing. Mr. Quiverful could not sit still at all, but kept walking from room to room, then out into the garden, then down the avenue into the road, and then back again to his wife. She, however, lost no time so idly.\n\n\"We must go to work at once, girls, and that in earnest. Mrs. Proudie expects us to be in the hospital house on the 15th of October.\"\n\nHad Mrs. Proudie expressed a wish that they should all be there on the next morning, the girls would have had nothing to say against it.\n\n\"And when will the pay begin?\" asked the eldest boy.\n\n\"To-day, my dear,\" said the gratified mother.\n\n\"Oh, that is jolly,\" said the boy.\n\n\"Mrs. Proudie insisted on our going down to the house,\" continued the mother, \"and when there, I thought I might save a journey by measuring some of the rooms and windows; so I got a knot of tape from Bobbins. Bobbins is as civil as you please, now.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't thank him,\" said Letty the younger.\n\n\"Oh, it's the way of the world, my dear. They all do just the same. You might just as well be angry with the turkey cock for gobbling at you. It's the bird's nature.\" And as she enunciated to her bairns the upshot of her practical experience, she pulled from her pocket the portions of tape which showed the length and breadth of the various rooms at the hospital house.\n\nAnd so we will leave her happy in her toils.\n\nThe Quiverfuls had hardly left the palace, and Mrs. Proudie was still holding forth on the matter to her husband, when another visitor was announced in the person of Dr. Gwynne. The Master of Lazarus had asked for the bishop and not for Mrs. Proudie, and therefore when he was shown into the study, he was surprised rather than rejoiced to find the lady there.\n\nBut we must go back a little, and it shall be but a little, for a difficulty begins to make itself manifest in the necessity of disposing of all our friends in the small remainder of this one volume. Oh, that Mr. Longman would allow me a fourth! It should transcend the other three as the seventh heaven transcends all the lower stages of celestial bliss.\n\nGoing home in the carriage that evening from Ullathorne, Dr. Gwynne had not without difficulty brought round his friend the archdeacon to a line of tactics much less bellicose than that which his own taste would have preferred. \"It will be unseemly in us to show ourselves in a bad humour; moreover, we have no power in this matter, and it will therefore be bad policy to act as though we had.\" 'Twas thus the Master of Lazarus argued. \"If,\" he continued, \"the bishop be determined to appoint another to the hospital, threats will not prevent him, and threats should not be lightly used by an archdeacon to his bishop. If he will place a stranger in the hospital, we can only leave him to the indignation of others. It is probable that such a step may not eventually injure your father-in-law. I will see the bishop, if you will allow me--alone.\" At this the archdeacon winced visibly. \"Yes, alone; for so I shall be calmer; and then I shall at any rate learn what he does mean to do in the matter.\"\n\nThe archdeacon puffed and blew, put up the carriage window and then put it down again, argued the matter up to his own gate, and at last gave way. Everybody was against him, his own wife, Mr. Harding, and Dr. Gwynne.\n\n\"Pray keep him out of hot water, Dr. Gwynne,\" Mrs. Grantly had said to her guest.\n\n\"My dearest madam, I'll do my best,\" the courteous master had replied. 'Twas thus he did it and earned for himself the gratitude of Mrs. Grantly.\n\nAnd now we may return to the bishop's study.\n\nDr. Gwynne had certainly not foreseen the difficulty which here presented itself. He--together with all the clerical world of England--had heard it rumoured about that Mrs. Proudie did not confine herself to her wardrobes, still-rooms, and laundries; but yet it had never occurred to him that if he called on a bishop at one o'clock in the day, he could by any possibility find him closeted with his wife; or that if he did so, the wife would remain longer than necessary to make her curtsey. It appeared, however, as though in the present case Mrs. Proudie had no idea of retreating.\n\nThe bishop had been very much pleased with Dr. Gwynne on the preceding day, and of course thought that Dr. Gwynne had been as much pleased with him. He attributed the visit solely to compliment, and thought it an extremely gracious and proper thing for the Master of Lazarus to drive over from Plumstead specially to call at the palace so soon after his arrival in the country. The fact that they were not on the same side either in politics or doctrines made the compliment the greater. The bishop, therefore, was all smiles. And Mrs. Proudie, who liked people with good handles to their names, was also very well disposed to welcome the Master of Lazarus.\n\n\"We had a charming party at Ullathorne, Master, had we not?\" said she. \"I hope Mrs. Grantly got home without fatigue.\"\n\nDr. Gwynne said that they had all been a little tired, but were none the worse this morning.\n\n\"An excellent person, Miss Thorne,\" suggested the bishop.\n\n\"And an exemplary Christian, I am told,\" said Mrs. Proudie.\n\nDr. Gwynne declared that he was very glad to hear it.\n\n\"I have not seen her Sabbath-day schools yet,\" continued the lady, \"but I shall make a point of doing so before long.\"\n\nDr. Gwynne merely bowed at this intimation. He had heard something of Mrs. Proudie and her Sunday-schools, both from Dr. Grantly and Mr. Harding.\n\n\"By the by, Master,\" continued the lady, \"I wonder whether Mrs. Grantly would like me to drive over and inspect her Sabbath-day school. I hear that it is most excellently kept.\"\n\nDr. Gwynne really could not say. He had no doubt Mrs. Grantly would be most happy to see Mrs. Proudie any day Mrs. Proudie would do her the honour of calling: that was, of course, if Mrs. Grantly should happen to be at home.\n\nA slight cloud darkened the lady's brow. She saw that her offer was not taken in good part. This generation of unregenerated vipers was still perverse, stiff-necked, and hardened in their iniquity. \"The archdeacon, I know,\" said she, \"sets his face against these institutions.\"\n\nAt this Dr. Gwynne laughed slightly. It was but a smile. Had he given his cap for it he could not have helped it.\n\nMrs. Proudie frowned again. \"'Suffer little children, and forbid them not,'\" she said. \"Are we not to remember that, Dr. Gwynne? 'Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones.' Are we not to remember that, Dr. Gwynne?\" And at each of these questions she raised at him her menacing forefinger.\n\n\"Certainly, madam, certainly,\" said the master, \"and so does the archdeacon, I am sure, on weekdays as well as on Sundays.\"\n\n\"On weekdays you can't take heed not to despise them,\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"because then they are out in the fields. On weekdays they belong to their parents, but on Sundays they ought to belong to the clergyman.\" And the finger was again raised.\n\nThe master began to understand and to share the intense disgust which the archdeacon always expressed when Mrs. Proudie's name was mentioned. What was he to do with such a woman as this? To take his hat and go would have been his natural resource, but then he did not wish to be foiled in his object.\n\n\"My lord,\" said he, \"I wanted to ask you a question on business, if you could spare me one moment's leisure. I know I must apologize for so disturbing you, but in truth I will not detain you five minutes.\"\n\n\"Certainly, Master, certainly,\" said the bishop; \"my time is quite yours--pray make no apology, pray make no apology.\"\n\n\"You have a great deal to do just at the present moment, Bishop. Do not forget how extremely busy you are at present,\" said Mrs. Proudie, whose spirit was now up, for she was angry with her visitor.\n\n\"I will not delay his lordship much above a minute,\" said the Master of Lazarus, rising from his chair and expecting that Mrs. Proudie would now go, or else that the bishop would lead the way into another room.\n\nBut neither event seemed likely to occur, and Dr. Gwynne stood for a moment silent in the middle of the room.\n\n\"Perhaps it's about Hiram's Hospital?\" suggested Mrs. Proudie.\n\nDr. Gwynne, lost in astonishment, and not knowing what else on earth to do, confessed that his business with the bishop was connected with Hiram's Hospital.\n\n\"His lordship has finally conferred the appointment on Mr. Quiverful this morning,\" said the lady.\n\nDr. Gwynne made a simple reference to the bishop, and finding that the lady's statement was formally confirmed, he took his leave. \"That comes of the reform bill,\" he said to himself as he walked down the bishop's avenue. \"Well, at any rate the Greek play bishops were not so bad as that.\"\n\nIt has been said that Mr. Slope, as he started for Ullathorne, received a dispatch from his friend Mr. Towers, which had the effect of putting him in that high good humour which subsequent events somewhat untowardly damped. It ran as follows. Its shortness will be its sufficient apology.\n\nI wish you every success. I don't know that I can help you, but if I can, I will.\n\nYours ever, T. T.\n\n30/9/185--\n\nThere was more in this than in all Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin's flummery; more than in all the bishop's promises, even had they been ever so sincere; more than in any archbishop's good word, even had it been possible to obtain it. Tom Towers would do for him what he could.\n\nMr. Slope had from his youth upwards been a firm believer in the public press. He had dabbled in it himself ever since he had taken his degree, and he regarded it as the great arranger and distributor of all future British terrestrial affairs whatever. He had not yet arrived at the age, an age which sooner or later comes to most of us, which dissipates the golden dreams of youth. He delighted in the idea of wresting power from the hands of his country's magnates and placing it in a custody which was at any rate nearer to his own reach. Sixty thousand broadsheets dispersing themselves daily among his reading fellow citizens formed in his eyes a better depot for supremacy than a throne at Windsor, a cabinet in Downing Street, or even an assembly at Westminster. And on this subject we must not quarrel with Mr. Slope, for the feeling is too general to be met with disrespect.\n\nTom Towers was as good, if not better, than his promise. On the following morning \"The Jupiter,\" spouting forth public opinion with sixty thousand loud clarions, did proclaim to the world that Mr. Slope was the fitting man for the vacant post. It was pleasant for Mr. Slope to read the following lines in the Barchester news-room, which he did within thirty minutes after the morning train from London had reached the city.\n\nIt is just now five years since we called the attention of our readers to the quiet city of Barchester. From that day to this, we have in no way meddled with the affairs of that happy ecclesiastical community. Since then, an old bishop has died there, and a young bishop has been installed; but we believe we did not do more than give some customary record of the interesting event. Nor are we now about to meddle very deeply in the affairs of the diocese. If any of the chapter feel a qualm of conscience on reading thus far, let it be quieted. Above all, let the mind of the new bishop be at rest. We are now not armed for war, but approach the reverend towers of the old cathedral with an olive branch in our hands.\n\nIt will be remembered that at the time alluded to, now five years past, we had occasion to remark on the state of a charity in Barchester called Hiram's Hospital. We thought that it was maladministered, and that the very estimable and reverend gentleman who held the office of warden was somewhat too highly paid for duties which were somewhat too easily performed. This gentleman--and we say it in all sincerity and with no touch of sarcasm--had never looked on the matter in this light before. We do not wish to take praise to ourselves whether praise be due to us or not. But the consequence of our remark was that the warden did look into the matter, and finding on so doing that he himself could come to no other opinion than that expressed by us, he very creditably threw up the appointment. The then bishop as creditably declined to fill the vacancy till the affair was put on a better footing. Parliament then took it up, and we have now the satisfaction of informing our readers that Hiram's Hospital will be immediately reopened under new auspices. Heretofore, provision was made for the maintenance of twelve old men. This will now be extended to the fair sex, and twelve elderly women, if any such can be found in Barchester, will be added to the establishment. There will be a matron; there will, it is hoped, be schools attached for the poorest of the children of the poor, and there will be a steward. The warden, for there will still be a warden, will receive an income more in keeping with the extent of the charity than that heretofore paid. The stipend we believe will be £450. We may add that the excellent house which the former warden inhabited will still be attached to the situation.\n\nBarchester Hospital cannot perhaps boast a world-wide reputation, but as we adverted to its state of decadence, we think it right also to advert to its renaissance. May it go on and prosper. Whether the salutary reform which has been introduced within its walls has been carried as far as could have been desired may be doubtful. The important question of the school appears to be somewhat left to the discretion of the new warden. This might have been made the most important part of the establishment, and the new warden, whom we trust we shall not offend by the freedom of our remarks, might have been selected with some view to his fitness as schoolmaster. But we will not now look a gift-horse in the mouth. May the hospital go on and prosper! The situation of warden has of course been offered to the gentleman who so honourably vacated it five years since, but we are given to understand that he has declined it. Whether the ladies who have been introduced be in his estimation too much for his powers of control, whether it be that the diminished income does not offer to him sufficient temptation to resume his old place, or that he has in the meantime assumed other clerical duties, we do not know. We are, however, informed that he has refused the offer and that the situation has been accepted by Mr. Quiverful, the vicar of Puddingdale.\n\nSo much we think is due to Hiram redivivus. But while we are on the subject of Barchester, we will venture with all respectful humility to express our opinion on another matter connected with the ecclesiastical polity of that ancient city. Dr. Trefoil, the dean, died yesterday. A short record of his death, giving his age and the various pieces of preferment which he has at different times held, will be found in another column of this paper. The only fault we knew in him was his age, and as that is a crime of which we all hope to be guilty, we will not bear heavily on it. May he rest in peace! But though the great age of an expiring dean cannot be made matter of reproach, we are not inclined to look on such a fault as at all pardonable in a dean just brought to the birth. We do hope that the days of sexagenarian appointments are past. If we want deans, we must want them for some purpose. That purpose will necessarily be better fulfilled by a man of forty than by a man of sixty. If we are to pay deans at all, we are to pay them for some sort of work. That work, be it what it may, will be best performed by a workman in the prime of life. Dr. Trefoil, we see, was eighty when he died. As we have as yet completed no plan for pensioning superannuated clergymen, we do not wish to get rid of any existing deans of that age. But we prefer having as few such as possible. If a man of seventy be now appointed, we beg to point out to Lord ---- that he will be past all use in a year or two, if indeed he be not so at the present moment. His lordship will allow us to remind him that all men are not evergreens like himself.\n\nWe hear that Mr. Slope's name has been mentioned for this preferment. Mr. Slope is at present chaplain to the bishop. A better man could hardly be selected. He is a man of talent, young, active, and conversant with the affairs of the cathedral; he is moreover, we conscientiously believe, a truly pious clergyman. We know that his services in the city of Barchester have been highly appreciated. He is an eloquent preacher and a ripe scholar. Such a selection as this would go far to raise the confidence of the public in the present administration of church patronage and would teach men to believe that from henceforth the establishment of our church will not afford easy couches to worn-out clerical voluptuaries.\n\nStanding at a reading-desk in the Barchester news-room, Mr. Slope digested this article with considerable satisfaction. What was therein said as to the hospital was now comparatively a matter of indifference to him. He was certainly glad that he had not succeeded in restoring to the place the father of that virago who had so audaciously outraged all decency in his person, and was so far satisfied. But Mrs. Proudie's nominee was appointed, and he was so far dissatisfied. His mind, however, was now soaring above Mrs. Bold or Mrs. Proudie. He was sufficiently conversant with the tactics of \"The Jupiter\" to know that the pith of the article would lie in the last paragraph. The place of honour was given to him, and it was indeed as honourable as even he could have wished. He was very grateful to his friend Mr. Towers, and with full heart looked forward to the day when he might entertain him in princely style at his own full-spread board in the deanery dining-room.\n\nIt had been well for Mr. Slope that Dr. Trefoil had died in the autumn. Those caterers for our morning repast, the staff of \"The Jupiter,\" had been sorely put to it for the last month to find a sufficiency of proper pabulum. Just then there was no talk of a new American president. No wonderful tragedies had occurred on railway trains in Georgia, or elsewhere. There was a dearth of broken banks, and a dead dean with the necessity for a live one was a godsend. Had Dr. Trefoil died in June, Mr. Towers would probably not have known so much about the piety of Mr. Slope.\n\nAnd here we will leave Mr. Slope for awhile in his triumph, explaining, however, that his feelings were not altogether of a triumphant nature. His rejection by the widow, or rather the method of his rejection, galled him terribly. For days to come he positively felt the sting upon his cheek whenever he thought of what had been done to him. He could not refrain from calling her by harsh names, speaking to himself as he walked through the streets of Barchester. When he said his prayers, he could not bring himself to forgive her. When he strove to do so, his mind recoiled from the attempt, and in lieu of forgiving ran off in a double spirit of vindictiveness, dwelling on the extent of the injury he had received. And so his prayers dropped senseless from his lips.\n\nAnd then the signora--what would he not have given to be able to hate her also? As it was, he worshipped the very sofa on which she was ever lying.\n\nAnd thus it was not all rose colour with Mr. Slope, although his hopes ran high.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV. Mrs. Bold at Home\n\nPoor Mrs. Bold, when she got home from Ullathorne on the evening of Miss Thorne's party, was very unhappy and, moreover, very tired. Nothing fatigues the body so much as weariness of spirit, and Eleanor's spirit was indeed weary.\n\nDr. Stanhope had civilly but not very cordially asked her in to tea, and her manner of refusal convinced the worthy doctor that he need not repeat the invitation. He had not exactly made himself a party to the intrigue which was to convert the late Mr. Bold's patrimony into an income for his hopeful son, but he had been well aware what was going on. And he was well aware also, when he perceived that Bertie declined accompanying them home in the carriage, that the affair had gone off.\n\nEleanor was very much afraid that Charlotte would have darted out upon her, as the prebendary got out at his own door, but Bertie had thoughtfully saved her from this by causing the carriage to go round by her own house. This also Dr. Stanhope understood and allowed to pass by without remark.\n\nWhen she got home, she found Mary Bold in the drawing-room with the child in her lap. She rushed forward and, throwing herself on her knees, kissed the little fellow till she almost frightened him.\n\n\"Oh, Mary, I am so glad you did not go. It was an odious party.\"\n\nNow the question of Mary's going had been one greatly mooted between them. Mrs. Bold, when invited, had been the guest of the Grantlys, and Miss Thorne, who had chiefly known Eleanor at the hospital or at Plumstead Rectory, had forgotten all about Mary Bold. Her sister-in-law had implored her to go under her wing and had offered to write to Miss Thorne, or to call on her. But Miss Bold had declined. In fact, Mr. Bold had not been very popular with such people as the Thornes, and his sister would not go among them unless she were specially asked to do so.\n\n\"Well, then,\" said Mary cheerfully, \"I have the less to regret.\"\n\n\"You have nothing to regret; but oh! Mary, I have--so much--so much;\" and then she began kissing her boy, whom her caresses had roused from his slumbers. When she raised her head, Mary saw that the tears were running down her cheeks.\n\n\"Good heavens, Eleanor, what is the matter? What has happened to you--Eleanor--dearest Eleanor--what is the matter?\" and Mary got up with the boy still in her arms.\n\n\"Give him to me--give him to me,\" said the young mother. \"Give him to me, Mary,\" and she almost tore the child out of her sister's arms. The poor little fellow murmured somewhat at the disturbance but nevertheless nestled himself close into his mother's bosom.\n\n\"Here, Mary, take the cloak from me. My own, own darling, darling, darling jewel. You are not false to me. Everybody else is false; everybody else is cruel. Mamma will care for nobody, nobody, nobody, but her own, own, own little man;\" and she again kissed and pressed the baby and cried till the tears ran down over the child's face.\n\n\"Who has been cruel to you, Eleanor?\" said Mary. \"I hope I have not.\"\n\nNow in this matter Eleanor had great cause for mental uneasiness. She could not certainly accuse her loving sister-in-law of cruelty; but she had to do that which was more galling: she had to accuse herself of imprudence against which her sister-in-law had warned her. Miss Bold had never encouraged Eleanor's acquaintance with Mr. Slope, and she had positively discouraged the friendship of the Stanhopes, as far as her usual gentle mode of speaking had permitted. Eleanor had only laughed at her, however, when she said that she disapproved of married women who lived apart from their husbands and suggested that Charlotte Stanhope never went to church. Now, however, Eleanor must either hold her tongue, which was quite impossible, or confess herself to have been utterly wrong, which was nearly equally so. So she staved off the evil day by more tears, and consoled herself by inducing little Johnny to rouse himself sufficiently to return her caresses.\n\n\"He is a darling--as true as gold. What would mamma do without him? Mamma would lie down and die if she had not her own Johnny Bold to give her comfort.\" This and much more she said of the same kind, and for a time made no other answer to Mary's inquiries.\n\nThis kind of consolation from the world's deceit is very common. Mothers obtain it from their children, and men from their dogs. Some men even do so from their walking-sticks, which is just as rational. How is it that we can take joy to ourselves in that we are not deceived by those who have not attained the art to deceive us? In a true man, if such can be found, or a true woman, much consolation may indeed be taken.\n\nIn the caresses of her child, however, Eleanor did receive consolation, and may ill befall the man who would begrudge it to her. The evil day, however, was only postponed. She had to tell her disagreeable tale to Mary, and she had also to tell it to her father. Must it not, indeed, be told to the whole circle of her acquaintance before she could be made to stand all right with them? At the present moment there was no one to whom she could turn for comfort. She hated Mr. Slope; that was a matter of course; in that feeling she revelled. She hated and despised the Stanhopes; but that feeling distressed her greatly. She had, as it were, separated herself from her old friends to throw herself into the arms of this family; and then how had they intended to use her? She could hardly reconcile herself to her own father, who had believed ill of her. Mary Bold had turned Mentor. That she could have forgiven had the Mentor turned out to be in the wrong, but Mentors in the right are not to be pardoned. She could not but hate the archdeacon, and now she hated him worse than ever, for she must in some sort humble herself before him. She hated her sister, for she was part and parcel of the archdeacon. And she would have hated Mr. Arabin if she could. He had pretended to regard her, and yet before her face he had hung over that Italian woman as though there had been no beauty in the world but hers--no other woman worth a moment's attention. And Mr. Arabin would have to learn all this about Mr. Slope! She told herself that she hated him, and she knew that she was lying to herself as she did so. She had no consolation but her baby, and of that she made the most. Mary, though she could not surmise what it was that had so violently affected her sister-in-law, saw at once that her grief was too great to be kept under control and waited patiently till the child should be in his cradle.\n\n\"You'll have some tea, Eleanor,\" she said.\n\n\"Oh, I don't care,\" said she, though in fact she must have been very hungry, for she had eaten nothing at Ullathorne.\n\nMary quietly made the tea, and buttered the bread, laid aside the cloak, and made things look comfortable.\n\n\"He's fast asleep,\" said she; \"you're very tired; let me take him up to bed.\"\n\nBut Eleanor would not let her sister touch him. She looked wistfully at her baby's eyes, saw that they were lost in the deepest slumber, and then made a sort of couch for him on the sofa. She was determined that nothing should prevail upon her to let him out of her sight that night.\n\n\"Come, Nelly,\" said Mary, \"don't be cross with me. I at least have done nothing to offend you.\"\n\n\"I an't cross,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"Are you angry then? Surely you can't be angry with me.\"\n\n\"No, I an't angry--at least not with you.\"\n\n\"If you are not, drink the tea I have made for you. I am sure you must want it.\"\n\nEleanor did drink it, and allowed herself to be persuaded. She ate and drank, and as the inner woman was recruited she felt a little more charitable towards the world at large. At last she found words to begin her story, and before she went to bed she had made a clean breast of it and told everything--everything, that is, as to the lovers she had rejected; of Mr. Arabin she said not a word.\n\n\"I know I was wrong,\" said she, speaking of the blow she had given to Mr. Slope; \"but I didn't know what he might do, and I had to protect myself.\"\n\n\"He richly deserved it,\" said Mary.\n\n\"Deserved it!\" said Eleanor, whose mind as regarded Mr. Slope was almost bloodthirsty. \"Had I stabbed him with a dagger, he would have deserved it. But what will they say about it at Plumstead?\"\n\n\"I don't think I should tell them,\" said Mary. Eleanor began to think that she would not.\n\nThere could have been no kinder comforter than Mary Bold. There was not the slightest dash of triumph about her when she heard of the Stanhope scheme, nor did she allude to her former opinion when Eleanor called her late friend Charlotte a base, designing woman. She re-echoed all the abuse that was heaped on Mr. Slope's head and never hinted that she had said as much before. \"I told you so, I told you so!\" is the croak of a true Job's comforter. But Mary, when she found her friend lying in her sorrow and scraping herself with potsherds, forbore to argue and to exult. Eleanor acknowledged the merit of the forbearance, and at length allowed herself to be tranquilised.\n\nOn the next day she did not go out of the house. Barchester she thought would be crowded with Stanhopes and Slopes; perhaps also with Arabins and Grantlys. Indeed, there was hardly anyone among her friends whom she could have met without some cause of uneasiness.\n\nIn the course of the afternoon she heard that the dean was dead, and she also heard that Mr. Quiverful had been finally appointed to the hospital.\n\nIn the evening her father came to her, and then the story, or as much of it as she could bring herself to tell him, had to be repeated. He was not in truth much surprised at Mr. Slope's effrontery, but he was obliged to act as though he had been to save his daughter's feelings. He was, however, anything but skilful in his deceit, and she saw through it.\n\n\"I see,\" said she, \"that you think it only in the common course of things that Mr. Slope should have treated me in this way.\" She had said nothing to him about the embrace, nor yet of the way in which it had been met.\n\n\"I do not think it at all strange,\" said he, \"that anyone should admire my Eleanor.\"\n\n\"It is strange to me,\" said she, \"that any man should have so much audacity, without ever having received the slightest encouragement.\"\n\nTo this Mr. Harding answered nothing. With the archdeacon it would have been the text for a rejoinder which would not have disgraced Bildad the Shuhite.\n\n\"But you'll tell the archdeacon?\" asked Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Tell him what?\" said she sharply.\n\n\"Or Susan?\" continued Mr. Harding. \"You'll tell Susan; you'll let them know that they wronged you in supposing that this man's addresses would be agreeable to you.\"\n\n\"They may find that out their own way,\" said she; \"I shall not ever willingly mention Mr. Slope's name to either of them.\"\n\n\"But I may.\"\n\n\"I have no right to hinder you from doing anything that may be necessary to your own comfort, but pray do not do it for my sake. Dr. Grantly never thought well of me, and never will. I don't know now that I am even anxious that he should do so.\"\n\nAnd then they went to the affair of the hospital. \"But is it true, Papa?\"\n\n\"What, my dear?\" said he. \"About the dean? Yes, I fear quite true. Indeed I know there is no doubt about it.\"\n\n\"Poor Miss Trefoil, I am so sorry for her. But I did not mean that,\" said Eleanor. \"But about the hospital, Papa?\"\n\n\"Yes, my dear. I believe it is true that Mr. Quiverful is to have it.\"\n\n\"Oh, what a shame.\"\n\n\"No, my dear, not at all, not at all a shame: I am sure I hope it will suit him.\"\n\n\"But, Papa, you know it is a shame. After all your hopes, all your expectations to get back to your old house, to see it given away in this way to a perfect stranger!\"\n\n\"My dear, the bishop had a right to give it to whom he pleased.\"\n\n\"I deny that, Papa. He had no such right. It is not as though you were a candidate for a new piece of preferment. If the bishop has a grain of justice--\"\n\n\"The bishop offered it to me on his terms, and as I did not like the terms, I refused it. After that, I cannot complain.\"\n\n\"Terms! He had no right to make terms.\"\n\n\"I don't know about that; but it seems he had the power. But to tell you the truth, Nelly, I am as well satisfied as it is. When the affair became the subject of angry discussion, I thoroughly wished to be rid of it altogether.\"\n\n\"But you did want to go back to the old house, Papa. You told me so yourself.\"\n\n\"Yes, my dear, I did. For a short time I did wish it. And I was foolish in doing so. I am getting old now, and my chief worldly wish is for peace and rest. Had I gone back to the hospital, I should have had endless contentions with the bishop, contentions with his chaplain, and contentions with the archdeacon. I am not up to this now; I am not able to meet such troubles; and therefore I am not ill-pleased to find myself left to the little church of St. Cuthbert's. I shall never starve,\" added he, laughing, \"as long as you are here.\"\n\n\"But will you come and live with me, Papa?\" she said earnestly, taking him by both his hands. \"If you will do that, if you will promise that, I will own that you are right.\"\n\n\"I will dine with you to-day at any rate.\"\n\n\"No, but live here altogether. Give up that close, odious little room in High Street.\"\n\n\"My dear, it's a very nice little room, and you are really quite uncivil.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, don't joke. It's not a nice place for you. You say you are growing old, though I am sure you are not.\"\n\n\"Am not I, my dear?\"\n\n\"No, Papa, not old--not to say old. But you are quite old enough to feel the want of a decent room to sit in. You know how lonely Mary and I are here. You know nobody ever sleeps in the big front bedroom. It is really unkind of you to remain up there alone, when you are so much wanted here.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Nelly--thank you. But, my dear--\"\n\n\"If you had been living here, Papa, with us, as I really think you ought to have done, considering how lonely we are, there would have been none of all this dreadful affair about Mr. Slope.\"\n\nMr. Harding, however, did not allow himself to be talked over into giving up his own and only little pied à terre in the High Street. He promised to come and dine with his daughter, and stay with her, and visit her, and do everything but absolutely live with her. It did not suit the peculiar feelings of the man to tell his daughter that though she had rejected Mr. Slope, and been ready to reject Mr. Stanhope, some other more favoured suitor would probably soon appear, and that on the appearance of such a suitor the big front bedroom might perhaps be more frequently in requisition than at present. But doubtless such an idea crossed his mind, and added its weight to the other reasons which made him decide on still keeping the close, odious little room in High Street.\n\nThe evening passed over quietly and in comfort. Eleanor was always happier with her father than with anyone else. He had not, perhaps, any natural taste for baby-worship, but he was always ready to sacrifice himself, and therefore made an excellent third in a trio with his daughter and Mary Bold in singing the praises of the wonderful child.\n\nThey were standing together over their music in the evening, the baby having again been put to bed upon the sofa, when the servant brought in a very small note in a beautiful pink envelope. It quite filled the room with perfume as it lay upon the small salver. Mary Bold and Mrs. Bold were both at the piano, and Mr. Harding was sitting close to them, with the violoncello between his legs, so that the elegancy of the epistle was visible to them all.\n\n\"Please ma'am, Dr. Stanhope's coachman says he is to wait for an answer,\" said the servant.\n\nEleanor got very red in the face as she took the note in her hand. She had never seen the writing before. Charlotte's epistles, to which she was well accustomed, were of a very different style and kind. She generally wrote on large note-paper; she twisted up her letters into the shape and sometimes into the size of cocked hats; she addressed them in a sprawling, manly hand, and not unusually added a blot or a smudge, as though such were her own peculiar sign-manual. The address of this note was written in a beautiful female hand, and the gummed wafer bore on it an impress of a gilt coronet. Though Eleanor had never seen such a one before, she guessed that it came from the signora. Such epistles were very numerously sent out from any house in which the signora might happen to be dwelling, but they were rarely addressed to ladies. When the coachman was told by the lady's maid to take the letter to Mrs. Bold, he openly expressed his opinion that there was some mistake about it. Whereupon the lady's maid boxed the coachman's ears. Had Mr. Slope seen in how meek a spirit the coachman took the rebuke, he might have learnt a useful lesson, both in philosophy and religion.\n\nThe note was as follows. It may be taken as a faithful promise that no further letter whatever shall be transcribed at length in these pages.\n\nMay I ask you, as a great favour, to call on me to-morrow. You can say what hour will best suit you, but quite early, if you can. I need hardly say that if I could call upon you, I should not take this liberty with you.\n\nI partly know what occurred the other day, and I promise you that you shall meet with no annoyance if you will come to me. My brother leaves us for London to-day; from thence he goes to Italy.\n\nIt will probably occur to you that I should not thus intrude on you, unless I had that to say to you which may be of considerable moment. Pray therefore excuse me, even if you do not grant my request.\n\nAnd believe me, Very sincerely yours,\n\nThursday Evening\n\nThe three of them sat in consultation on this epistle for some ten or fifteen minutes, and then decided that Eleanor should write a line saying that she would see the signora the next morning at twelve o'clock.\n\n# CHAPTER XLV. The Stanhopes at Home\n\nWe must now return to the Stanhopes and see how they behaved themselves on their return from Ullathorne.\n\nCharlotte, who came back in the first homeward journey with her sister, waited in palpitating expectation till the carriage drove up to the door a second time. She did not run down, or stand at the window, or show in any outward manner that she looked for anything wonderful to occur; but when she heard the carriage wheels, she stood up with erect ears, listening for Eleanor's footfall on the pavement, or the cheery sound of Bertie's voice welcoming her in. Had she heard either, she would have felt that all was right; but neither sound was there for her to hear. She heard only her father's slow step as he ponderously let himself down from the carriage and slowly walked along the hall, till he got into his own private room on the ground floor. \"Send Miss Stanhope to me,\" he said to the servant.\n\n\"There's something wrong now,\" said Madeline, who was lying on her sofa in the back drawing-room.\n\n\"It's all up with Bertie,\" replied Charlotte. \"I know, I know,\" she said to the servant as he brought up the message. \"Tell my father I will be with him immediately.\"\n\n\"Bertie's wooing has gone astray,\" said Madeline. \"I knew it would.\"\n\n\"It has been his own fault then. She was ready enough, I am quite sure,\" said Charlotte with that sort of ill-nature which is not uncommon when one woman speaks of another.\n\n\"What will you say to him now?\" By \"him,\" the signora meant their father.\n\n\"That will be as I find him. He was ready to pay two hundred pounds for Bertie to stave off the worst of his creditors, if this marriage had gone on. Bertie must now have the money instead and go and take his chance.\"\n\n\"Where is he now?\"\n\n\"Heaven knows! Smoking in the bottom of Mr. Thorne's ha-ha, or philandering with some of those Miss Chadwicks. Nothing will ever make an impression on him. But he'll be furious if I don't go down.\"\n\n\"No, nothing ever will. But don't be long, Charlotte, for I want my tea.\"\n\nAnd so Charlotte went down to her father. There was a very black cloud on the old man's brow--blacker than his daughter could ever yet remember to have seen there. He was sitting in his own armchair, not comfortably over the fire, but in the middle of the room, waiting till she should come and listen to him.\n\n\"What has become of your brother?\" he said as soon as the door was shut.\n\n\"I should rather ask you,\" said Charlotte. \"I left you both at Ullathorne when I came away. What have you done with Mrs. Bold?\"\n\n\"Mrs. Bold! Nonsense. The woman has gone home as she ought to do. And heartily glad I am that she should not be sacrificed to so heartless a reprobate.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa!\"\n\n\"A heartless reprobate! Tell me now where he is and what he is going to do. I have allowed myself to be fooled between you. Marriage, indeed! Who on earth that has money, or credit, or respect in the world to lose would marry him?\"\n\n\"It is no use your scolding me, Papa. I have done the best I could for him and you.\"\n\n\"And Madeline is nearly as bad,\" said the prebendary, who was in truth very, very angry.\n\n\"Oh, I suppose we are all bad,\" replied Charlotte.\n\nThe old man emitted a huge, leonine sigh. If they were all bad, who had made them so? If they were unprincipled, selfish, and disreputable, who was to be blamed for the education which had had so injurious an effect?\n\n\"I know you'll ruin me among you,\" said he.\n\n\"Why, Papa, what nonsense that is. You are living within your income this minute, and if there are any new debts, I don't know of them. I am sure there ought to be none, for we are dull enough here.\"\n\n\"Are those bills of Madeline's paid?\"\n\n\"No, they are not. Who was to pay them?\"\n\n\"Her husband may pay them.\"\n\n\"Her husband! Would you wish me to tell her you say so? Do you wish to turn her out of your house?\"\n\n\"I wish she would know how to behave herself.\"\n\n\"Why, what on earth has she done now? Poor Madeline! To-day is only the second time she has gone out since we came to this vile town.\"\n\nHe then sat silent for a time, thinking in what shape he would declare his resolve. \"Well, Papa,\" said Charlotte, \"shall I stay here, or may I go upstairs and give Mamma her tea?\"\n\n\"You are in your brother's confidence. Tell me what he is going to do.\"\n\n\"Nothing, that I am aware of.\"\n\n\"Nothing--nothing! Nothing but eat and drink and spend every shilling of my money he can lay his hands upon. I have made up my mind, Charlotte. He shall eat and drink no more in this house.\"\n\n\"Very well. Then I suppose he must go back to Italy.\"\n\n\"He may go where he pleases.\"\n\n\"That's easily said, Papa, but what does it mean? You can't let him--\"\n\n\"It means this?\" said the doctor, speaking more loudly than was his wont and with wrath flashing from his eyes; \"that as sure as God rules in heaven I will not maintain him any longer in idleness.\"\n\n\"Oh, ruling in heaven!\" said Charlotte. \"It is no use talking about that. You must rule him here on earth; and the question is, how can you do it. You can't turn him out of the house penniless, to beg about the street.\"\n\n\"He may beg where he likes.\"\n\n\"He must go back to Carrara. That is the cheapest place he can live at, and nobody there will give him credit for above two or three hundred pauls. But you must let him have the means of going.\"\n\n\"As sure as--\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, don't swear. You know you must do it. You were ready to pay two hundred pounds for him if this marriage came off. Half that will start him to Carrara.\"\n\n\"What? Give him a hundred pounds?\"\n\n\"You know we are all in the dark, Papa,\" said she, thinking it expedient to change the conversation. \"For anything we know he may be at this moment engaged to Mrs. Bold.\"\n\n\"Fiddlestick,\" said the father, who had seen the way in which Mrs. Bold had got into the carriage while his son stood apart without even offering her his hand.\n\n\"Well, then, he must go to Carrara,\" said Charlotte.\n\nJust at this moment the lock of the front door was heard, and Charlotte's quick ears detected her brother's catlike step in the hall. She said nothing, feeling that for the present Bertie had better keep out of her father's way. But Dr. Stanhope also heard the sound of the lock.\n\n\"Who's that?\" he demanded. Charlotte made no reply, and he asked again, \"Who is that that has just come in? Open the door. Who is it?\"\n\n\"I suppose it is Bertie.\"\n\n\"Bid him come here,\" said the father. But Bertie, who was close to the door and heard the call, required no further bidding, but walked in with a perfectly unconcerned and cheerful air. It was this peculiar insouciance which angered Dr. Stanhope, even more than his son's extravagance.\n\n\"Well, sir?\" said the doctor.\n\n\"And how did you get home, sir, with your fair companion?\" said Bertie. \"I suppose she is not upstairs, Charlotte?\"\n\n\"Bertie,\" said Charlotte, \"Papa is in no humour for joking. He is very angry with you.\"\n\n\"Angry!\" said Bertie, raising his eyebrows as though he had never yet given his parent cause for a single moment's uneasiness.\n\n\"Sit down, if you please, sir,\" said Dr. Stanhope very sternly but not now very loudly. \"And I'll trouble you to sit down, too, Charlotte. Your mother can wait for her tea a few minutes.\"\n\nCharlotte sat down on the chair nearest to the door in somewhat of a perverse sort of manner, as much as though she would say--\"Well, here I am; you shan't say I don't do what I am bid; but I'll be whipped if I give way to you.\" And she was determined not to give way. She too was angry with Bertie, but she was not the less ready on that account to defend him from his father. Bertie also sat down. He drew his chair close to the library-table, upon which he put his elbow, and then resting his face comfortably on one hand, he began drawing little pictures on a sheet of paper with the other. Before the scene was over he had completed admirable figures of Miss Thorne, Mrs. Proudie, and Lady De Courcy, and begun a family piece to comprise the whole set of the Lookalofts.\n\n\"Would it suit you, sir,\" said the father, \"to give me some idea as to what your present intentions are? What way of living you propose to yourself?\"\n\n\"I'll do anything you can suggest, sir,\" replied Bertie.\n\n\"No, I shall suggest nothing further. My time for suggesting has gone by. I have only one order to give, and that is that you leave my house.\"\n\n\"To-night?\" said Bertie, and the simple tone of the question left the doctor without any adequately dignified method of reply.\n\n\"Papa does not quite mean to-night,\" said Charlotte; \"at least I suppose not.\"\n\n\"To-morrow, perhaps,\" suggested Bertie.\n\n\"Yes, sir, to-morrow,\" said the doctor. \"You shall leave this to-morrow.\"\n\n\"Very well, sir. Will the 4.30 P.M. train be soon enough?\" and Bertie, as he asked, put the finishing touch to Miss Thorne's high-heeled boots.\n\n\"You may go how and when and where you please, so that you leave my house to-morrow. You have disgraced me, sir; you have disgraced yourself, and me, and your sisters.\"\n\n\"I am glad at least, sir, that I have not disgraced my mother,\" said Bertie.\n\nCharlotte could hardly keep her countenance, but the doctor's brow grew still blacker than ever. Bertie was executing his chef d'oeuvre in the delineation of Mrs. Proudie's nose and mouth.\n\n\"You are a heartless reprobate, sir; a heartless, thankless, good-for-nothing reprobate. I have done with you. You are my son--that I cannot help--but you shall have no more part or parcel in me as my child, nor I in you as your father.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, Papa! You must not, shall not say so,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"I will say so, and do say so,\" said the father, rising from his chair. \"And now leave the room, sir.\"\n\n\"Stop, stop,\" said Charlotte. \"Why don't you speak, Bertie? Why don't you look up and speak? It is your manner that makes Papa so angry.\"\n\n\"He is perfectly indifferent to all decency, to all propriety,\" said the doctor; then he shouted out, \"Leave the room, sir! Do you hear what I say?\"\n\n\"Papa, Papa, I will not let you part so. I know you will be sorry for it.\" And then she added, getting up and whispering into his ear, \"Is he only to blame? Think of that. We have made our own bed, and, such as it is, we must lie on it. It is no use for us to quarrel among ourselves,\" and as she finished her whisper, Bertie finished off the countess's bustle, which was so well done that it absolutely seemed to be swaying to and fro on the paper with its usual lateral motion.\n\n\"My father is angry at the present time,\" said Bertie, looking up for a moment from his sketches, \"because I am not going to marry Mrs. Bold. What can I say on the matter? It is true that I am not going to marry her. In the first place--\"\n\n\"That is not true, sir,\" said Dr. Stanhope, \"but I will not argue with you.\"\n\n\"You were angry just this moment because I would not speak,\" said Bertie, going on with a young Lookaloft.\n\n\"Give over drawing,\" said Charlotte, going up to him and taking the paper from under his hand. The caricatures, however, she preserved and showed them afterwards to the friends of the Thornes, the Proudies, and De Courcys. Bertie, deprived of his occupation, threw himself back in his chair and waited further orders.\n\n\"I think it will certainly be for the best that Bertie should leave this at once; perhaps to-morrow,\" said Charlotte; \"but pray, Papa, let us arrange some scheme together.\"\n\n\"If he will leave this to-morrow, I will give him £10, and he shall be paid £5 a month by the banker at Carrara as long as he stays permanently in that place.\"\n\n\"Well, sir, it won't be long,\" said Bertie, \"for I shall be starved to death in about three months.\"\n\n\"He must have marble to work with,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"I have plenty there in the studio to last me three months,\" said Bertie. \"It will be no use attempting anything large in so limited a time--unless I do my own tombstone.\"\n\nTerms, however, were ultimately come to somewhat more liberal than those proposed, and the doctor was induced to shake hands with his son and bid him good night. Dr. Stanhope would not go up to tea, but had it brought to him in his study by his daughter.\n\nBut Bertie went upstairs and spent a pleasant evening. He finished the Lookalofts, greatly to the delight of his sisters, though the manner of portraying their décolleté dresses was not the most refined. Finding how matters were going, he by degrees allowed it to escape from him that he had not pressed his suit upon the widow in a very urgent way.\n\n\"I suppose, in point of fact, you never proposed at all?\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"Oh, she understood that she might have me if she wished,\" said he.\n\n\"And she didn't wish,\" said the Signora.\n\n\"You have thrown me over in the most shameful manner,\" said Charlotte. \"I suppose you told her all about my little plan?\"\n\n\"Well, it came out somehow--at least the most of it.\"\n\n\"There's an end of that alliance,\" said Charlotte, \"but it doesn't matter much. I suppose we shall all be back at Como soon.\"\n\n\"I am sure I hope so,\" said the signora. \"I'm sick of the sight of black coats. If that Mr. Slope comes here any more, he'll be the death of me.\"\n\n\"You've been the ruin of him, I think,\" said Charlotte.\n\n\"And as for a second black-coated lover of mine, I am going to make a present of him to another lady with most singular disinterestedness.\"\n\nThe next day, true to his promise, Bertie packed up and went off by the 4.30 P.M. train, with £20 in his pocket, bound for the marble quarries of Carrara. And so he disappears from our scene.\n\nAt twelve o'clock on the day following that on which Bertie went, Mrs. Bold, true also to her word, knocked at Dr. Stanhope's door with a timid hand and palpitating heart. She was at once shown up to the back drawing-room, the folding doors of which were closed, so that in visiting the signora Eleanor was not necessarily thrown into any communion with those in the front room. As she went up the stairs, she saw none of the family and was so far saved much of the annoyance which she had dreaded.\n\n\"This is very kind of you, Mrs. Bold; very kind, after what has happened,\" said the lady on the sofa with her sweetest smile.\n\n\"You wrote in such a strain that I could not but come to you.\"\n\n\"I did, I did; I wanted to force you to see me.\"\n\n\"Well, signora, I am here.\"\n\n\"How cold you are to me. But I suppose I must put up with that. I know you think you have reason to be displeased with us all. Poor Bertie; if you knew all, you would not be angry with him.\"\n\n\"I am not angry with your brother--not in the least. But I hope you did not send for me here to talk about him.\"\n\n\"If you are angry with Charlotte, that is worse, for you have no warmer friend in all Barchester. But I did not send for you to talk about this--pray bring your chair nearer, Mrs. Bold, so that I may look at you. It is so unnatural to see you keeping so far off from me.\"\n\nEleanor did as she was bid and brought her chair close to the sofa.\n\n\"And now, Mrs. Bold, I am going to tell you something which you may perhaps think indelicate, but yet I know that I am right in doing so.\"\n\nHereupon Mrs. Bold said nothing but felt inclined to shake in her chair. The signora, she knew, was not very particular, and that which to her appeared to be indelicate might to Mrs. Bold appear to be extremely indecent.\n\n\"I believe you know Mr. Arabin?\"\n\nMrs. Bold would have given the world not to blush, but her blood was not at her own command. She did blush up to her forehead, and the signora, who had made her sit in a special light in order that she might watch her, saw that she did so.\n\n\"Yes, I am acquainted with him. That is, slightly. He is an intimate friend of Dr. Grantly, and Dr. Grantly is my brother-in-law.\"\n\n\"Well, if you know Mr. Arabin, I am sure you must like him. I know and like him much. Everybody that knows him must like him.\"\n\nMrs. Bold felt it quite impossible to say anything in reply to this. Her blood was rushing about her body she knew not how or why. She felt as though she were swinging in her chair, and she knew that she was not only red in the face but also almost suffocated with heat. However, she sat still and said nothing.\n\n\"How stiff you are with me, Mrs. Bold,\" said the signora; \"and I the while am doing for you all that one woman can do to serve another.\"\n\nA kind of thought came over the widow's mind that perhaps the signora's friendship was real, and that at any rate it could not hurt her; and another kind of thought, a glimmering of a thought, came to her also--that Mr. Arabin was too precious to be lost. She despised the signora, but might she not stoop to conquer? It should be but the smallest fraction of a stoop!\n\n\"I don't want to be stiff,\" she said, \"but your questions are so very singular.\"\n\n\"Well, then, I will ask you one more singular still,\" said Madeline Neroni, raising herself on her elbow and turning her own face full upon her companion's. \"Do you love him, love him with all your heart and soul, with all the love your bosom can feel? For I can tell you that he loves you, adores you, worships you, thinks of you and nothing else, is now thinking of you as he attempts to write his sermon for next Sunday's preaching. What would I not give to be loved in such a way by such a man, that is, if I were an object fit for any man to love!\"\n\nMrs. Bold got up from her seat and stood speechless before the woman who was now addressing her in this impassioned way. When the signora thus alluded to herself, the widow's heart was softened, and she put her own hand, as though caressingly, on that of her companion, which was resting on the table. The signora grasped it and went on speaking.\n\n\"What I tell you is God's own truth; and it is for you to use it as may be best for your own happiness. But you must not betray me. He knows nothing of this. He knows nothing of my knowing his inmost heart. He is simple as a child in these matters. He told me his secret in a thousand ways because he could not dissemble, but he does not dream that he has told it. You know it now, and I advise you to use it.\"\n\nEleanor returned the pressure of the other's hand with an infinitesimal soupçon of a squeeze.\n\n\"And remember,\" continued the signora, \"he is not like other men. You must not expect him to come to you with vows and oaths and pretty presents, to kneel at your feet, and kiss your shoe-strings. If you want that, there are plenty to do it, but he won't be one of them.\" Eleanor's bosom nearly burst with a sigh, but Madeline, not heeding her, went on. \"With him, yea will stand for yea, and nay for nay. Though his heart should break for it, the woman who shall reject him once will have rejected him once and for all. Remember that. And now, Mrs. Bold, I will not keep you, for you are fluttered. I partly guess what use you will make of what I have said to you. If ever you are a happy wife in that man's house, we shall be far away, but I shall expect you to write me one line to say that you have forgiven the sins of the family.\"\n\nEleanor half-whispered that she would, and then, without uttering another word, crept out of the room and down the stairs, opened the front door for herself without hearing or seeing anyone, and found herself in the close.\n\nIt would be difficult to analyse Eleanor's feelings as she walked home. She was nearly stupefied by the things that had been said to her. She felt sore that her heart should have been so searched and riddled by a comparative stranger, by a woman whom she had never liked and never could like. She was mortified that the man whom she owned to herself that she loved should have concealed his love from her and shown it to another. There was much to vex her proud spirit. But there was, nevertheless, an under stratum of joy in all this which buoyed her up wondrously. She tried if she could disbelieve what Madame Neroni had said to her, but she found that she could not. It was true; it must be true. She could not, would not, did not doubt it.\n\nOn one point she fully resolved to follow the advice given her. If it should ever please Mr. Arabin to put such a question to her as that suggested, her \"yea\" should be \"yea.\" Would not all her miseries be at an end if she could talk of them to him openly, with her head resting on his shoulder?\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI. Mr. Slope's Parting Interview with the Signora\n\nOn the following day the signora was in her pride. She was dressed in her brightest of morning dresses, and had quite a levée round her couch. It was a beautifully bright October afternoon; all the gentlemen of the neighbourhood were in Barchester, and those who had the entry of Dr. Stanhope's house were in the signora's back drawing-room. Charlotte and Mrs. Stanhope were in the front room, and such of the lady's squires as could not for the moment get near the centre of attraction had to waste their fragrance on the mother and sister.\n\nThe first who came and the last to leave was Mr. Arabin. This was the second visit he had paid to Madame Neroni since he had met her at Ullathorne. He came, he knew not why, to talk about, he knew not what. But, in truth, the feelings which now troubled him were new to him, and he could not analyse them. It may seem strange that he should thus come dangling about Madame Neroni because he was in love with Mrs. Bold; but it was nevertheless the fact; and though he could not understand why he did so, Madame Neroni understood it well enough.\n\nShe had been gentle and kind to him and had encouraged his staying. Therefore he stayed on. She pressed his hand when he first greeted her; she made him remain near her and whispered to him little nothings. And then her eye, brilliant and bright, now mirthful, now melancholy, and invincible in either way! What man with warm feelings, blood unchilled, and a heart not guarded by a triple steel of experience could have withstood those eyes! The lady, it is true, intended to do him no mortal injury; she merely chose to inhale a slight breath of incense before she handed the casket over to another. Whether Mrs. Bold would willingly have spared even so much is another question.\n\nAnd then came Mr. Slope. All the world now knew that Mr. Slope was a candidate for the deanery and that he was generally considered to be the favourite. Mr. Slope, therefore, walked rather largely upon the earth. He gave to himself a portly air, such as might become a dean, spoke but little to other clergymen, and shunned the bishop as much as possible. How the meagre little prebendary, and the burly chancellor, and all the minor canons and vicars choral, ay, and all the choristers, too, cowered and shook and walked about with long faces when they read or heard of that article in \"The Jupiter.\" Now were coming the days when nothing would avail to keep the impure spirit from the cathedral pulpit. That pulpit would indeed be his own. Precentors, vicars, and choristers might hang up their harps on the willows. Ichabod! Ichabod! The glory of their house was departing from them.\n\nMr. Slope, great as he was with embryo grandeur, still came to see the signora. Indeed, he could not keep himself away. He dreamed of that soft hand which he had kissed so often, and of that imperial brow which his lips had once pressed; and he then dreamed also of further favours.\n\nAnd Mr. Thorne was there also. It was the first visit he had ever paid to the signora, and he made it not without due preparation. Mr. Thorne was a gentleman usually precise in his dress and prone to make the most of himself in an unpretending way. The grey hairs in his whiskers were eliminated perhaps once a month; those on his head were softened by a mixture which we will not call a dye--it was only a wash. His tailor lived in St. James's Street, and his bootmaker at the corner of that street and Piccadilly. He was particular in the article of gloves, and the getting up of his shirts was a matter not lightly thought of in the Ullathorne laundry. On the occasion of the present visit he had rather overdone his usual efforts, and caused some little uneasiness to his sister, who had not hitherto received very cordially the proposition for a lengthened visit from the signora at Ullathorne.\n\nThere were others also there--young men about the city who had not much to do and who were induced by the lady's charms to neglect that little--but all gave way to Mr. Thorne, who was somewhat of a grand signor, as a country gentleman always is in a provincial city.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Thorne, this is so kind of you!\" said the signora. \"You promised to come, but I really did not expect it. I thought you country gentlemen never kept your pledges.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, sometimes,\" said Mr. Thorne, looking rather sheepish and making his salutations a little too much in the style of the last century.\n\n\"You deceive none but your consti--stit--stit--what do you call the people that carry you about in chairs and pelt you with eggs and apples when they make you a member of Parliament?\"\n\n\"One another also, sometimes, signora,\" said Mr. Slope, with a very deanish sort of smirk on his face. \"Country gentlemen do deceive one another sometimes, don't they, Mr. Thorne?\"\n\nMr. Thorne gave him a look which undeaned him completely for the moment, but he soon remembered his high hopes and, recovering himself quickly, sustained his probable coming dignity by a laugh at Mr. Thorne's expense.\n\n\"I never deceive a lady, at any rate,\" said Mr. Thorne, \"especially when the gratification of my own wishes is so strong an inducement to keep me true, as it now is.\"\n\nMr. Thorne went on thus awhile with antediluvian grimaces and compliments which he had picked up from Sir Charles Grandison, and the signora at every grimace and at every bow smiled a little smile and bowed a little bow. Mr. Thorne, however, was kept standing at the foot of the couch, for the new dean sat in the seat of honour near the table. Mr. Arabin the while was standing with his back to the fire, his coat-tails under his arms, gazing at her with all his eyes--not quite in vain, for every now and again a glance came up at him, bright as a meteor out of heaven.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Thorne, you promised to let me introduce my little girl to you. Can you spare a moment--will you see her now?\"\n\nMr. Thorne assured her that he could and would see the young lady with the greatest pleasure in life. \"Mr. Slope, might I trouble you to ring the bell?\" said she, and when Mr. Slope got up, she looked at Mr. Thorne and pointed to the chair. Mr. Thorne, however, was much too slow to understand her, and Mr. Slope would have recovered his seat had not the signora, who never chose to be unsuccessful, somewhat summarily ordered him out of it.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Slope, I must ask you to let Mr. Thorne sit here just for a moment or two. I am sure you will pardon me. We can take a liberty with you this week. Next week, you know, when you move into the dean's house, we shall all be afraid of you.\"\n\nMr. Slope, with an air of much indifference, rose from his seat and, walking into the next room, became greatly interested in Mrs. Stanhope's worsted work.\n\nAnd then the child was brought in. She was a little girl, about eight years of age, like her mother, only that her enormous eyes were black, and her hair quite jet. Her complexion, too, was very dark and bespoke her foreign blood. She was dressed in the most outlandish and extravagant way in which clothes could be put on a child's back. She had great bracelets on her naked little arms, a crimson fillet braided with gold round her head, and scarlet shoes with high heels. Her dress was all flounces and stuck out from her as though the object were to make it lie off horizontally from her little hips. It did not nearly cover her knees, but this was atoned for by a loose pair of drawers, which seemed made throughout of lace; then she had on pink silk stockings. It was thus that the last of the Neros was habitually dressed at the hour when visitors were wont to call.\n\n\"Julia, my love,\" said the mother--Julia was ever a favourite name with the ladies of that family. \"Julia, my love, come here. I was telling you about the beautiful party poor Mamma went to. This is Mr. Thorne; will you give him a kiss, dearest?\"\n\nJulia put up her face to be kissed, as she did to all her mother's visitors, and then Mr. Thorne found that he had got her and, what was much more terrific to him, all her finery, into his arms. The lace and starch crumpled against his waistcoat and trousers, the greasy black curls hung upon his cheek, and one of the bracelet clasps scratched his ear. He did not at all know how to hold so magnificent a lady, nor holding her what to do with her. However, he had on other occasions been compelled to fondle little nieces and nephews, and now set about the task in the mode he always had used.\n\n\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle,\" said he, putting the child on one knee and working away with it as though he were turning a knife-grinder's wheel with his foot.\n\n\"Mamma, Mamma,\" said Julia crossly, \"I don't want to be diddle diddled. Let me go, you naughty old man, you.\"\n\nPoor Mr. Thorne put the child down quietly on the ground and drew back his chair; Mr. Slope, who had returned to the pole star that attracted him, laughed aloud; Mr. Arabin winced and shut his eyes; and the signora pretended not to hear her daughter.\n\n\"Go to Aunt Charlotte, lovey,\" said the mamma, \"and ask her if it is not time for you to go out.\"\n\nBut little Miss Julia, though she had not exactly liked the nature of Mr. Thorne's attention, was accustomed to be played with by gentlemen, and did not relish the idea of being sent so soon to her aunt.\n\n\"Julia, go when I tell you, my dear.\" But Julia still went pouting about the room. \"Charlotte, do come and take her,\" said the signora. \"She must go out, and the days get so short now.\" And thus ended the much-talked-of interview between Mr. Thorne and the last of the Neros.\n\nMr. Thorne recovered from the child's crossness sooner than from Mr. Slope's laughter. He could put up with being called an old man by an infant, but he did not like to be laughed at by the bishop's chaplain, even though that chaplain was about to become a dean. He said nothing, but he showed plainly enough that he was angry.\n\nThe signora was ready enough to avenge him. \"Mr. Slope,\" said she, \"I hear that you are triumphing on all sides.\"\n\n\"How so?\" said he, smiling. He did not dislike being talked to about the deanery, though, of course, he strongly denied the imputation.\n\n\"You carry the day both in love and war.\" Mr. Slope hereupon did not look quite so satisfied as he had done.\n\n\"Mr. Arabin,\" continued the signora, \"don't you think Mr. Slope is a very lucky man?\"\n\n\"Not more so than he deserves, I am sure,\" said Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"Only think, Mr. Thorne, he is to be our new dean; of course we all know that.\"\n\n\"Indeed, signora,\" said Mr. Slope, \"we all know nothing about it. I can assure you I myself--\"\n\n\"He is to be the new dean--there is no manner of doubt of it, Mr. Thorne.\"\n\n\"Hum!\" said Mr. Thorne.\n\n\"Passing over the heads of old men like my father and Archdeacon Grantly--\"\n\n\"Oh--oh!\" said Mr. Slope.\n\n\"The archdeacon would not accept it,\" said Mr. Arabin, whereupon Mr. Slope smiled abominably and said, as plainly as a look could speak, that the grapes were sour.\n\n\"Going over all our heads,\" continued the signora, \"for of course I consider myself one of the chapter.\"\n\n\"If I am ever dean,\" said Mr. Slope, \"that is, were I ever to become so, I should glory in such a canoness.\"\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Slope, stop; I haven't half done. There is another canoness for you to glory in. Mr. Slope is not only to have the deanery but a wife to put in it.\"\n\nMr. Slope again looked disconcerted.\n\n\"A wife with a large fortune, too. It never rains but it pours, does it, Mr. Thorne?\"\n\n\"No, never,\" said Mr. Thorne, who did not quite relish talking about Mr. Slope and his affairs.\n\n\"When will it be, Mr. Slope?\"\n\n\"When will what be?\" said he.\n\n\"Oh, we know when the affair of the dean will be: a week will settle that. The new hat, I have no doubt, has been already ordered. But when will the marriage come off?\"\n\n\"Do you mean mine or Mr. Arabin's?\" said he, striving to be facetious.\n\n\"Well, just then I meant yours, though, perhaps, after all, Mr. Arabin's may be first. But we know nothing of him. He is too close for any of us. Now all is open and above board with you--which, by the by, Mr. Arabin, I beg to tell you I like much the best. He who runs can read that Mr. Slope is a favoured lover. Come, Mr. Slope, when is the widow to be made Mrs. Dean?\"\n\nTo Mr. Arabin this badinage was peculiarly painful, and yet he could not tear himself away and leave it. He believed, still believed with that sort of belief which the fear of a thing engenders, that Mrs. Bold would probably become the wife of Mr. Slope. Of Mr. Slope's little adventure in the garden he knew nothing. For aught he knew, Mr. Slope might have had an adventure of quite a different character. He might have thrown himself at the widow's feet, been accepted, and then returned to town a jolly, thriving wooer. The signora's jokes were bitter enough to Mr. Slope, but they were quite as bitter to Mr. Arabin. He still stood leaning against the fire-place, fumbling with his hands in his trousers pockets.\n\n\"Come, come, Mr. Slope, don't be so bashful,\" continued the signora. \"We all know that you proposed to the lady the other day at Ullathorne. Tell us with what words she accepted you. Was it with a simple 'yes,' or with the two 'no no's' which make an affirmative? Or did silence give consent? Or did she speak out with that spirit which so well becomes a widow and say openly, 'By my troth, sir, you shall make me Mrs. Slope as soon as it is your pleasure to do so.'\"\n\nMr. Slope had seldom in his life felt himself less at his ease. There sat Mr. Thorne, laughing silently. There stood his old antagonist, Mr. Arabin, gazing at him with all his eyes. There round the door between the two rooms were clustered a little group of people, including Miss Stanhope and the Revs. Messrs. Grey and Green, all listening to his discomfiture. He knew that it depended solely on his own wit whether or no he could throw the joke back upon the lady. He knew that it stood him to do so if he possibly could, but he had not a word. \"'Tis conscience that makes cowards of us all.\" He felt on his cheek the sharp points of Eleanor's fingers, and did not know who might have seen the blow, who might have told the tale to this pestilent woman who took such delight in jeering him. He stood there, therefore, red as a carbuncle and mute as a fish; grinning sufficiently to show his teeth; an object of pity.\n\nBut the signora had no pity; she knew nothing of mercy. Her present object was to put Mr. Slope down, and she was determined to do it thoroughly, now that she had him in her power.\n\n\"What, Mr. Slope, no answer? Why it can't possibly be that the woman has been fool enough to refuse you? She can't surely be looking out after a bishop. But I see how it is, Mr. Slope. Widows are proverbially cautious. You should have let her alone till the new hat was on your head, till you could show her the key of the deanery.\"\n\n\"Signora,\" said he at last, trying to speak in a tone of dignified reproach, \"you really permit yourself to talk on solemn subjects in a very improper way.\"\n\n\"Solemn subjects--what solemn subject? Surely a dean's hat is not such a solemn subject.\"\n\n\"I have no aspirations such as those you impute to me. Perhaps you will drop the subject.\"\n\n\"Oh, certainly, Mr. Slope; but one word first. Go to her again with the prime minister's letter in your pocket. I'll wager my shawl to your shovel she does not refuse you then.\"\n\n\"I must say, signora, that I think you are speaking of the lady in a very unjustifiable manner.\"\n\n\"And one other piece of advice, Mr. Slope; I'll only offer you one other;\" and then she commenced singing--\n\n\"It's gude to be merry and wise, Mr. Slope; It's gude to be honest and true; It's gude to be off with the old love--Mr. Slope, Before you are on with the new.\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\"\n\nAnd the signora, throwing herself back on her sofa, laughed merrily. She little recked how those who heard her would, in their own imaginations, fill up the little history of Mr. Slope's first love. She little cared that some among them might attribute to her the honour of his earlier admiration. She was tired of Mr. Slope and wanted to get rid of him; she had ground for anger with him, and she chose to be revenged.\n\nHow Mr. Slope got out of that room he never himself knew. He did succeed ultimately, and probably with some assistance, in getting his hat and escaping into the air. At last his love for the signora was cured. Whenever he again thought of her in his dreams, it was not as of an angel with azure wings. He connected her rather with fire and brimstone, and though he could still believe her to be a spirit, he banished her entirely out of heaven and found a place for her among the infernal gods. When he weighed in the balance, as he not seldom did, the two women to whom he had attached himself in Barchester, the pre-eminent place in his soul's hatred was usually allotted to the signora.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII. The Dean Elect\n\nDuring the entire next week Barchester was ignorant who was to be its new dean. On Sunday morning Mr. Slope was decidedly the favourite, but he did not show himself in the cathedral, and then he sank a point or two in the betting. On Monday he got a scolding from the bishop in the hearing of the servants, and down he went till nobody would have him at any price; but on Tuesday he received a letter, in an official cover, marked private, by which he fully recovered his place in the public favour. On Wednesday he was said to be ill, and that did not look well; but on Thursday morning he went down to the railway station with a very jaunty air; and when it was ascertained that he had taken a first-class ticket for London, there was no longer any room for doubt on the matter.\n\nWhile matters were in this state of ferment at Barchester, there was not much mental comfort at Plumstead. Our friend the archdeacon had many grounds for inward grief. He was much displeased at the result of Dr. Gwynne's diplomatic mission to the palace, and did not even scruple to say to his wife that had he gone himself, he would have managed the affair much better. His wife did not agree with him, but that did not mend the matter.\n\nMr. Quiverful's appointment to the hospital was, however, a fait accompli, and Mr. Harding's acquiescence in that appointment was not less so. Nothing would induce Mr. Harding to make a public appeal against the bishop, and the Master of Lazarus quite approved of his not doing so.\n\n\"I don't know what has come to the master,\" said the archdeacon over and over again. \"He used to be ready enough to stand up for his order.\"\n\n\"My dear Archdeacon,\" Mrs. Grantly would say in reply, \"what is the use of always fighting? I really think the master is right.\" The master, however, had taken steps of his own of which neither the archdeacon nor his wife knew anything.\n\nThen Mr. Slope's successes were henbane to Dr. Grantly, and Mrs. Bold's improprieties were as bad. What would be all the world to Archdeacon Grantly if Mr. Slope should become Dean of Barchester and marry his wife's sister! He talked of it and talked of it till he was nearly ill. Mrs. Grantly almost wished that the marriage were done and over, so that she might hear no more about it.\n\nAnd there was yet another ground of misery which cut him to the quick nearly as closely as either of the others. That paragon of a clergyman whom he had bestowed upon St. Ewold's, that college friend of whom he had boasted so loudly, that ecclesiastical knight before whose lance Mr. Slope was to fall and bite the dust, that worthy bulwark of the church as it should be, that honoured representative of Oxford's best spirit, was--so at least his wife had told him half a dozen times--misconducting himself!\n\nNothing had been seen of Mr. Arabin at Plumstead for the last week, but a good deal had, unfortunately, been heard of him. As soon as Mrs. Grantly had found herself alone with the archdeacon, on the evening of the Ullathorne party, she had expressed herself very forcibly as to Mr. Arabin's conduct on that occasion. He had, she declared, looked and acted and talked very unlike a decent parish clergyman. At first the archdeacon had laughed at this, and assured her that she need not trouble herself--that Mr. Arabin would be found to be quite safe. But by degrees he began to find that his wife's eyes had been sharper than his own. Other people coupled the signora's name with that of Mr. Arabin. The meagre little prebendary who lived in the close told him to a nicety how often Mr. Arabin had visited at Dr. Stanhope's, and how long he had remained on the occasion of each visit. He had asked after Mr. Arabin at the cathedral library, and an officious little vicar choral had offered to go and see whether he could be found at Dr. Stanhope's. Rumour, when she has contrived to sound the first note on her trumpet, soon makes a loud peal audible enough. It was too clear that Mr. Arabin had succumbed to the Italian woman, and that the archdeacon's credit would suffer fearfully if something were not done to rescue the brand from the burning. Besides, to give the archdeacon his due, he was really attached to Mr. Arabin, and grieved greatly at his backsliding.\n\nThey were sitting, talking over their sorrows, in the drawing-room before dinner on the day after Mr. Slope's departure for London, and on this occasion Mrs. Grantly spoke out her mind freely. She had opinions of her own about parish clergymen, and now thought it right to give vent to them.\n\n\"If you would have been led by me, Archdeacon, you would never have put a bachelor into St. Ewold's.\"\n\n\"But my dear, you don't meant to say that all bachelor clergymen misbehave themselves.\"\n\n\"I don't know that clergymen are so much better than other men,\" said Mrs. Grantly. \"It's all very well with a curate, whom you have under your own eye and whom you can get rid of if he persists in improprieties.\"\n\n\"But Mr. Arabin was a fellow, and couldn't have had a wife.\"\n\n\"Then I would have found someone who could.\"\n\n\"But, my dear, are fellows never to get livings?\"\n\n\"Yes, to be sure they are, when they get engaged. I never would put a young man into a living unless he were married, or engaged to be married. Now, here is Mr. Arabin. The whole responsibility lies upon you.\"\n\n\"There is not at this moment a clergyman in all Oxford more respected for morals and conduct than Arabin.\"\n\n\"Oh, Oxford!\" said the lady, with a sneer. \"What men choose to do at Oxford nobody ever hears of. A man may do very well at Oxford who would bring disgrace on a parish; and to tell you the truth, it seems to me that Mr. Arabin is just such a man.\"\n\nThe archdeacon groaned deeply, but he had no further answer to make.\n\n\"You really must speak to him, Archdeacon. Only think what the Thornes will say if they hear that their parish clergyman spends his whole time philandering with this woman.\"\n\nThe archdeacon groaned again. He was a courageous man, and knew well enough how to rebuke the younger clergymen of the diocese, when necessary. But there was that about Mr. Arabin which made the doctor feel that it would be very difficult to rebuke him with good effect.\n\n\"You can advise him to find a wife for himself, and he will understand well enough what that means,\" said Mrs. Grantly.\n\nThe archdeacon had nothing for it but groaning. There was Mr. Slope: he was going to be made dean; he was going to take a wife; he was about to achieve respectability and wealth, an excellent family mansion, and a family carriage; he would soon be among the comfortable élite of the ecclesiastical world of Barchester; whereas his own protégé, the true scion of the true church, by whom he had sworn, would be still but a poor vicar, and that with a very indifferent character for moral conduct! It might be all very well recommending Mr. Arabin to marry, but how would Mr. Arabin, when married, support a wife?\n\nThings were ordering themselves thus in Plumstead drawing-room when Dr. and Mrs. Grantly were disturbed in their sweet discourse by the quick rattle of a carriage and pair of horses on the gravel sweep. The sound was not that of visitors, whose private carriages are generally brought up to country-house doors with demure propriety, but betokened rather the advent of some person or persons who were in a hurry to reach the house, and had no intention of immediately leaving it. Guests invited to stay a week, and who were conscious of arriving after the first dinner-bell, would probably approach in such a manner. So might arrive an attorney with the news of a granduncle's death, or a son from college with all the fresh honours of a double first. No one would have had himself driven up to the door of a country-house in such a manner who had the slightest doubt of his own right to force an entry.\n\n\"Who is it?\" said Mrs. Grantly, looking at her husband.\n\n\"Who on earth can it be?\" said the archdeacon to his wife. He then quietly got up and stood with the drawing-room door open in his hand. \"Why, it's your father!\"\n\nIt was indeed Mr. Harding, and Mr. Harding alone. He had come by himself in a post-chaise with a couple of horses from Barchester, arriving almost after dark, and evidently full of news. His visits had usually been made in the quietest manner; he had rarely presumed to come without notice, and had always been driven up in a modest old green fly, with one horse, that hardly made itself heard as it crawled up to the hall-door.\n\n\"Good gracious, Warden, is it you?\" said the archdeacon, forgetting in his surprise the events of the last few years. \"But come in; nothing the matter, I hope.\"\n\n\"We are very glad you are come, Papa,\" said his daughter. \"I'll go and get your room ready at once.\"\n\n\"I an't warden, Archdeacon,\" said Mr. Harding; \"Mr. Quiverful is warden.\"\n\n\"Oh, I know, I know,\" said the archdeacon petulantly. \"I forgot all about it at the moment. Is anything the matter?\"\n\n\"Don't go this moment, Susan,\" said Mr. Harding. \"I have something to tell you.\"\n\n\"The dinner-bell will ring in five minutes,\" said she.\n\n\"Will it?\" said Mr. Harding. \"Then perhaps I had better wait.\" He was big with news which he had come to tell, but which he knew could not be told without much discussion. He had hurried away to Plumstead as fast as two horses could bring him, and now, finding himself there, he was willing to accept the reprieve which dinner would give him.\n\n\"If you have anything of moment to tell us,\" said the archdeacon, \"pray let us hear it at once. Has Eleanor gone off?\"\n\n\"No, she has not,\" said Mr. Harding with a look of great displeasure.\n\n\"Has Slope been made dean?\"\n\n\"No, he has not, but--\"\n\n\"But what?\" said the archdeacon, who was becoming very impatient.\n\n\"They have--\"\n\n\"They have what?\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"They have offered it to me,\" said Mr. Harding, with a modesty which almost prevented his speaking.\n\n\"Good heavens!\" said the archdeacon, and sunk back exhausted in an easy chair.\n\n\"My dear, dear father,\" said Mrs. Grantly, and threw her arms round her father's neck.\n\n\"So I thought I had better come out and consult with you at once,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Consult!\" shouted the archdeacon. \"But, my dear Harding, I congratulate you with my whole heart--with my whole heart; I do indeed. I never heard anything in my life that gave me so much pleasure;\" and he got hold of both his father-in-law's hands, and shook them as though he were going to shake them off, and walked round and round the room, twirling a copy of \"The Jupiter\" over his head to show his extreme exultation.\n\n\"But--\" began Mr. Harding.\n\n\"But me no buts,\" said the archdeacon. \"I never was so happy in my life. It was just the proper thing to do. Upon my honour I'll never say another word against Lord ---- the longest day I have to live.\"\n\n\"That's Dr. Gwynne's doing, you may be sure,\" said Mrs. Grantly, who greatly liked the Master of Lazarus, he being an orderly married man with a large family.\n\n\"I suppose it is,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"Oh, Papa, I am so truly delighted!\" said Mrs. Grantly, getting up and kissing her father.\n\n\"But, my dear,\" said Mr. Harding. It was all in vain that he strove to speak; nobody would listen to him.\n\n\"Well, Mr. Dean,\" said the archdeacon, triumphing, \"the deanery gardens will be some consolation for the hospital elms. Well, poor Quiverful! I won't begrudge him his good fortune any longer.\"\n\n\"No, indeed,\" said Mrs. Grantly. \"Poor woman, she has fourteen children. I am sure I am very glad they have got it.\"\n\n\"So am I,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"I would give twenty pounds,\" said the archdeacon, \"to see how Mr. Slope will look when he hears it.\" The idea of Mr. Slope's discomfiture formed no small part of the archdeacon's pleasure.\n\nAt last Mr. Harding was allowed to go upstairs and wash his hands, having, in fact, said very little of all that he had come out to Plumstead on purpose to say. Nor could anything more be said till the servants were gone after dinner. The joy of Dr. Grantly was so uncontrollable that he could not refrain from calling his father-in-law Mr. Dean before the men, and therefore it was soon matter of discussion in the lower regions how Mr. Harding, instead of his daughter's future husband, was to be the new dean, and various were the opinions on the matter. The cook and butler, who were advanced in years, thought that it was just as it should be; but the footman and lady's maid, who were younger, thought it was a great shame that Mr. Slope should lose his chance.\n\n\"He's a mean chap all the same,\" said the footman, \"and it an't along of him that I says so. But I always did admire the missus's sister; and she'd well become the situation.\"\n\nWhile these were the ideas downstairs, a very great difference of opinion existed above. As soon as the cloth was drawn and the wine on the table, Mr. Harding made for himself an opportunity of speaking. It was, however, with much inward troubling that he said:\n\n\"It's very kind of Lord ----, very kind, and I feel it deeply, most deeply. I am, I must confess, gratified by the offer--\"\n\n\"I should think so,\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"But all the same I am afraid that I can't accept it.\"\n\nThe decanter almost fell from the archdeacon's hand upon the table, and the start he made was so great as to make his wife jump up from her chair. Not accept the deanship! If it really ended in this, there would be no longer any doubt that his father-in-law was demented. The question now was whether a clergyman with low rank and preferment amounting to less than £200 a year should accept high rank, £1,200 a year, and one of the most desirable positions which his profession had to afford!\n\n\"What!\" said the archdeacon, gasping for breath and staring at his guest as though the violence of his emotion had almost thrown him into a fit. \"What!\"\n\n\"I do not find myself fit for new duties,\" urged Mr. Harding.\n\n\"New duties! What duties?\" said the archdeacon with unintended sarcasm.\n\n\"Oh, Papa,\" said Mrs. Grantly, \"nothing can be easier than what a dean has to do. Surely you are more active than Dr. Trefoil.\"\n\n\"He won't have half as much to do as he has at present,\" said Dr. Grantly.\n\n\"Did you see what 'The Jupiter' said the other day about young men?\"\n\n\"Yes, and I saw that 'The Jupiter' said all that it could to induce the appointment of Mr. Slope. Perhaps you would wish to see Mr. Slope made dean.\"\n\nMr. Harding made no reply to this rebuke, though he felt it strongly. He had not come over to Plumstead to have further contention with his son-in-law about Mr. Slope, so he allowed it to pass by.\n\n\"I know I cannot make you understand my feeling,\" he said, \"for we have been cast in different moulds. I may wish that I had your spirit and energy and power of combatting; but I have not. Every day that is added to my life increases my wish for peace and rest.\"\n\n\"And where on earth can a man have peace and rest if not in a deanery!\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"People will say that I am too old for it.\"\n\n\"Good heavens! People! What people? What need you care for any people?\"\n\n\"But I think myself I am too old for any new place.\"\n\n\"Dear Papa,\" said Mrs. Grantly, \"men ten years older than you are appointed to new situations day after day.\"\n\n\"My dear,\" said he, \"it is impossible that I should make you understand my feelings, nor do I pretend to any great virtue in the matter. The truth is, I want the force of character which might enable me to stand against the spirit of the times. The call on all sides now is for young men, and I have not the nerve to put myself in opposition to the demand. Were 'The Jupiter,' when it hears of my appointment, to write article after article setting forth my incompetency, I am sure it would cost me my reason. I ought to be able to bear with such things, you will say. Well, my dear, I own that I ought. But I feel my weakness, and I know that I can't. And to tell you the truth, I know no more than a child what the dean has to do.\"\n\n\"Pshaw!\" exclaimed the archdeacon.\n\n\"Don't be angry with me, Archdeacon: don't let us quarrel about it, Susan. If you knew how keenly I feel the necessity of having to disoblige you in this matter, you would not be angry with me.\"\n\nThis was a dreadful blow to Dr. Grantly. Nothing could possibly have suited him better than having Mr. Harding in the deanery. Though he had never looked down on Mr. Harding on account of his recent poverty, he did fully recognize the satisfaction of having those belonging to him in comfortable positions. It would be much more suitable that Mr. Harding should be Dean of Barchester than vicar of St. Cuthbert's and precentor to boot. And then the great discomfiture of that arch-enemy of all that was respectable in Barchester, of that new Low Church clerical parvenu that had fallen amongst them, that alone would be worth more, almost, than the situation itself. It was frightful to think that such unhoped-for good fortune should be marred by the absurd crotchets and unwholesome hallucinations by which Mr. Harding allowed himself to be led astray. To have the cup so near his lips and then to lose the drinking of it was more than Dr. Grantly could endure.\n\nAnd yet it appeared as though he would have to endure it. In vain he threatened and in vain he coaxed. Mr. Harding did not indeed speak with perfect decision of refusing the proffered glory, but he would not speak with anything like decision of accepting it. When pressed again and again, he would again and again allege that he was wholly unfitted to new duties. It was in vain that the archdeacon tried to insinuate, though he could not plainly declare, that there were no new duties to perform. It was in vain he hinted that in all cases of difficulty he, he the archdeacon, was willing and able to guide a weak-minded dean. Mr. Harding seemed to have a foolish idea, not only that there were new duties to do, but that no one should accept the place who was not himself prepared to do them.\n\nThe conference ended in an understanding that Mr. Harding should at once acknowledge the letter he had received from the minister's private secretary, and should beg that he might be allowed two days to make up his mind; and that during those two days the matter should be considered.\n\nOn the following morning the archdeacon was to drive Mr. Harding back to Barchester.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII. Miss Thorne Shows Her Talent at Match-Making\n\nOn Mr. Harding's return to Barchester from Plumstead, which was effected by him in due course in company with the archdeacon, more tidings of a surprising nature met him. He was, during the journey, subjected to such a weight of unanswerable argument, all of which went to prove that it was his bounden duty not to interfere with the paternal Government that was so anxious to make him a dean, that when he arrived at the chemist's door in High Street, he hardly knew which way to turn himself in the matter. But, perplexed as he was, he was doomed to further perplexity. He found a note there from his daughter begging him most urgently to come to her immediately. But we must again go back a little in our story.\n\nMiss Thorne had not been slow to hear the rumours respecting Mr. Arabin which had so much disturbed the happiness of Mrs. Grantly. And she, also, was unhappy to think that her parish clergyman should be accused of worshipping a strange goddess. She, also, was of opinion that rectors and vicars should all be married, and with that good-natured energy which was characteristic of her, she put her wits to work to find a fitting match for Mr. Arabin. Mrs. Grantly, in this difficulty, could think of no better remedy than a lecture from the archdeacon. Miss Thorne thought that a young lady, marriageable and with a dowry, might be of more efficacy. In looking through the catalogue of her unmarried friends who might possibly be in want of a husband, and might also be fit for such promotion as a country parsonage affords, she could think of no one more eligible than Mrs. Bold; consequently, losing no time, she went into Barchester on the day of Mr. Slope's discomfiture, the same day that her brother had had his interesting interview with the last of the Neros, and invited Mrs. Bold to bring her nurse and baby to Ullathorne and make them a protracted visit.\n\nMiss Thorne suggested a month or two, intending to use her influence afterwards in prolonging it so as to last out the winter, in order that Mr. Arabin might have an opportunity of becoming fairly intimate with his intended bride. \"We'll have Mr. Arabin, too,\" said Miss Thorne to herself; \"and before the spring they'll know each other; and in twelve or eighteen months' time, if all goes well, Mrs. Bold will be domiciled at St. Ewold's;\" and then the kind-hearted lady gave herself some not undeserved praise for her match-making genius.\n\nEleanor was taken a little by surprise, but the matter ended in her promising to go to Ullathorne for at any rate a week or two; on the day previous to that on which her father drove out to Plumstead, she had had herself driven out to Ullathorne.\n\nMiss Thorne would not perplex her with her embryo lord on that same evening, thinking that she would allow her a few hours to make herself at home; but on the following morning Mr. Arabin arrived. \"And now,\" said Miss Thorne to herself, \"I must contrive to throw them in each other's way.\" That same day, after dinner, Eleanor, with an assumed air of dignity which she could not maintain, with tears which she could not suppress, with a flutter which she could not conquer, and a joy which she could not hide, told Miss Thorne that she was engaged to marry Mr. Arabin and that it behoved her to get back home to Barchester as quick as she could.\n\nTo say simply that Miss Thorne was rejoiced at the success of the scheme would give a very faint idea of her feelings on the occasion. My readers may probably have dreamt before now that they have had before them some terribly long walk to accomplish, some journey of twenty or thirty miles, an amount of labour frightful to anticipate, and that immediately on starting they have ingeniously found some accommodating short cut which has brought them without fatigue to their work's end in five minutes. Miss Thorne's waking feelings were somewhat of the same nature. My readers may perhaps have had to do with children, and may on some occasion have promised to their young charges some great gratification intended to come off, perhaps at the end of the winter, or at the beginning of summer. The impatient juveniles, however, will not wait, and clamorously demand their treat before they go to bed. Miss Thorne had a sort of feeling that her children were equally unreasonable. She was like an inexperienced gunner, who has ill-calculated the length of the train that he has laid. The gun-powder exploded much too soon, and poor Miss Thorne felt that she was blown up by the strength of her own petard.\n\nMiss Thorne had had lovers of her own, but they had been gentlemen of old-fashioned and deliberate habits. Miss Thorne's heart also had not always been hard, though she was still a virgin spinster; but it had never yielded in this way at the first assault. She had intended to bring together a middle-aged, studious clergyman and a discreet matron who might possibly be induced to marry again, and in doing so she had thrown fire among tinder. Well, it was all as it should be, but she did feel perhaps a little put out by the precipitancy of her own success, and perhaps a little vexed at the readiness of Mrs. Bold to be wooed.\n\nShe said, however, nothing about it to anyone, and ascribed it all to the altered manners of the new age. Their mothers and grandmothers were perhaps a little more deliberate, but it was admitted on all sides that things were conducted very differently now than in former times. For aught Miss Thorne knew of the matter, a couple of hours might be quite sufficient under the new régime to complete that for which she in her ignorance had allotted twelve months.\n\nBut we must not pass over the wooing so cavalierly. It has been told, with perhaps tedious accuracy, how Eleanor disposed of two of her lovers at Ullathorne; and it must also be told with equal accuracy, and if possible with less tedium, how she encountered Mr. Arabin.\n\nIt cannot be denied that when Eleanor accepted Miss Thorne's invitation she remembered that Ullathorne was in the parish of St. Ewold's. Since her interview with the signora she had done little else than think about Mr. Arabin and the appeal that had been made to her. She could not bring herself to believe, or try to bring herself to believe, that what she had been told was untrue. Think of it how she would, she could not but accept it as a fact that Mr. Arabin was fond of her; and then when she went further and asked herself the question, she could not but accept it as a fact also that she was fond of him. If it were destined for her to be the partner of his hopes and sorrows, to whom could she look for friendship so properly as to Miss Thorne? This invitation was like an ordained step towards the fulfilment of her destiny, and when she also heard that Mr. Arabin was expected to be at Ullathorne on the following day, it seemed as though all the world were conspiring in her favour. Well, did she not deserve it? In that affair of Mr. Slope had not all the world conspired against her?\n\nShe could not, however, make herself easy and at home. When, in the evening after dinner, Miss Thorne expatiated on the excellence of Mr. Arabin's qualities, and hinted that any little rumour which might be ill-naturedly spread abroad concerning him really meant nothing, Mrs. Bold found herself unable to answer. When Miss Thorne went a little further and declared that she did not know a prettier vicarage-house in the county than St. Ewold's, Mrs. Bold, remembering the projected bow-window and the projected priestess, still held her tongue, though her ears tingled with the conviction that all the world knew that she was in love with Mr. Arabin. Well, what would that matter if they could only meet and tell each other what each now longed to tell?\n\nAnd they did meet. Mr. Arabin came early in the day and found the two ladies together at work in the drawing-room. Miss Thorne, who, had she known all the truth, would have vanished into air at once, had no conception that her immediate absence would be a blessing, and remained chatting with them till luncheon-time. Mr. Arabin could talk about nothing but the Signora Neroni's beauty, would discuss no people but the Stanhopes. This was very distressing to Eleanor and not very satisfactory to Miss Thorne. But yet there was evidence of innocence in his open avowal of admiration.\n\nAnd then they had lunch, and then Mr. Arabin went out on parish duty, and Eleanor and Miss Thorne were left to take a walk together.\n\n\"Do you think the Signora Neroni is so lovely as people say?\" Eleanor asked as they were coming home.\n\n\"She is very beautiful, certainly, very beautiful,\" Miss Thorne answered; \"but I do not know that anyone considers her lovely. She is a woman all men would like to look at, but few, I imagine, would be glad to take her to their hearths, even were she unmarried and not afflicted as she is.\"\n\nThere was some little comfort in this. Eleanor made the most of it till she got back to the house. She was then left alone in the drawing-room, and just as it was getting dark Mr. Arabin came in.\n\nIt was a beautiful afternoon in the beginning of October, and Eleanor was sitting in the window to get the advantage of the last daylight for her novel. There was a fire in the comfortable room, but the weather was not cold enough to make it attractive; and as she could see the sun set from where she sat, she was not very attentive to her book.\n\nMr. Arabin, when he entered, stood awhile with his back to the fire in his usual way, merely uttering a few commonplace remarks about the beauty of the weather, while he plucked up courage for more interesting converse. It cannot probably be said that he had resolved then and there to make an offer to Eleanor. Men, we believe, seldom make such resolves. Mr. Slope and Mr. Stanhope had done so, it is true, but gentlemen generally propose without any absolutely defined determination as to their doing so. Such was now the case with Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"It is a lovely sunset,\" said Eleanor, answering him on the dreadfully trite subject which he had chosen.\n\nMr. Arabin could not see the sunset from the hearth-rug, so he had to go close to her.\n\n\"Very lovely,\" said he, standing modestly so far away from her as to avoid touching the flounces of her dress. Then it appeared that he had nothing further to say; so, after gazing for a moment in silence at the brightness of the setting sun, he returned to the fire.\n\nEleanor found that it was quite impossible for herself to commence a conversation. In the first place she could find nothing to say; words, which were generally plenty enough with her, would not come to her relief. And moreover, do what she would, she could hardly prevent herself from crying.\n\n\"Do you like Ullathorne?\" said Mr. Arabin, speaking from the safely distant position which he had assumed on the hearth-rug.\n\n\"Yes, indeed, very much!\"\n\n\"I don't mean Mr. and Miss Thorne--I know you like them--but the style of the house. There is something about old-fashioned mansions, built as this is, and old-fashioned gardens, that to me is especially delightful.\"\n\n\"I like everything old-fashioned,\" said Eleanor; \"old-fashioned things are so much the honestest.\"\n\n\"I don't know about that,\" said Mr. Arabin, gently laughing. \"That is an opinion on which very much may be said on either side. It is strange how widely the world is divided on a subject which so nearly concerns us all, and which is so close beneath our eyes. Some think that we are quickly progressing towards perfection, while others imagine that virtue is disappearing from the earth.\"\n\n\"And you, Mr. Arabin, what do you think?\" said Eleanor. She felt somewhat surprised at the tone which his conversation was taking, and yet she was relieved at his saying something which enabled herself to speak without showing her own emotion.\n\n\"What do I think, Mrs. Bold?\" and then he rumbled his money with his hands in his trousers pockets, and looked and spoke very little like a thriving lover. \"It is the bane of my life that on important subjects I acquire no fixed opinion. I think, and think, and go on thinking, and yet my thoughts are running ever in different directions. I hardly know whether or no we do lean more confidently than our fathers did on those high hopes to which we profess to aspire.\"\n\n\"I think the world grows more worldly every day,\" said Eleanor.\n\n\"That is because you see more of it than when you were younger. But we should hardly judge by what we see--we see so very, very little.\" There was then a pause for awhile, during which Mr. Arabin continued to turn over his shillings and half-crowns. \"If we believe in Scripture, we can hardly think that mankind in general will now be allowed to retrograde.\"\n\nEleanor, whose mind was certainly engaged otherwise than on the general state of mankind, made no answer to this. She felt thoroughly dissatisfied with herself. She could not force her thoughts away from the topic on which the signora had spoken to her in so strange a way, and yet she knew that she could not converse with Mr. Arabin in an unrestrained, natural tone till she did so. She was most anxious not to show to him any special emotion, and yet she felt that if he looked at her, he would at once see that she was not at ease.\n\nBut he did not look at her. Instead of doing so, he left the fire-place and began walking up and down the room. Eleanor took up her book resolutely, but she could not read, for there was a tear in her eye, and do what she would, it fell on her cheek. When Mr. Arabin's back was turned to her, she wiped it away; but another was soon coursing down her face in its place. They would come--not a deluge of tears that would have betrayed her at once, but one by one, single monitors. Mr. Arabin did not observe her closely, and they passed unseen.\n\nMr. Arabin, thus pacing up and down the room, took four or five turns before he spoke another word, and Eleanor sat equally silent with her face bent over her book. She was afraid that her tears would get the better of her, and was preparing for an escape from the room, when Mr. Arabin in his walk stood opposite to her. He did not come close up but stood exactly on the spot to which his course brought him, and then, with his hands under his coat-tails, thus made his confession.\n\n\"Mrs. Bold,\" said he, \"I owe you retribution for a great offence of which I have been guilty towards you.\" Eleanor's heart beat so that she could not trust herself to say that he had never been guilty of any offence. So Mr. Arabin thus went on.\n\n\"I have thought much of it since, and I am now aware that I was wholly unwarranted in putting to you a question which I once asked you. It was indelicate on my part, and perhaps unmanly. No intimacy which may exist between myself and your connexion, Dr. Grantly, could justify it. Nor could the acquaintance which existed between ourselves.\" This word acquaintance struck cold on Eleanor's heart. Was this to be her doom after all? \"I therefore think it right to beg your pardon in a humble spirit, and I now do so.\"\n\nWhat was Eleanor to say to him? She could not say much because she was crying, and yet she must say something. She was most anxious to say that something graciously, kindly, and yet not in such a manner as to betray herself. She had never felt herself so much at a loss for words.\n\n\"Indeed, I took no offence, Mr. Arabin.\"\n\n\"Oh, but you did! And had you not done so, you would not have been yourself. You were as right to be offended as I was wrong so to offend you. I have not forgiven myself, but I hope to hear that you forgive me.\"\n\nShe was now past speaking calmly, though she still continued to hide her tears; and Mr. Arabin, after pausing a moment in vain for her reply, was walking off towards the door. She felt that she could not allow him to go unanswered without grievously sinning against all charity; so, rising from her seat, she gently touched his arm and said, \"Oh, Mr. Arabin, do not go till I speak to you! I do forgive you. You know that I forgive you.\"\n\nHe took the hand that had so gently touched his arm and then gazed into her face as if he would peruse there, as though written in a book, the whole future destiny of his life; as he did so, there was a sober, sad seriousness in his own countenance which Eleanor found herself unable to sustain. She could only look down upon the carpet, let her tears trickle as they would, and leave her hand within his.\n\nIt was but for a minute that they stood so, but the duration of that minute was sufficient to make it ever memorable to them both. Eleanor was sure now that she was loved. No words, be their eloquence what it might, could be more impressive than that eager, melancholy gaze.\n\nWhy did he look so into her eyes? Why did he not speak to her? Could it be that he looked for her to make the first sign?\n\nAnd he, though he knew but little of women, even he knew that he was loved. He had only to ask, and it would be all his own, that inexpressible loveliness, those ever-speaking but yet now mute eyes, that feminine brightness and eager, loving spirit which had so attracted him since first he had encountered it at St. Ewold's. It might, must, all be his own now. On no other supposition was it possible that she should allow her hand to remain thus clasped within his own. He had only to ask. Ah, but that was the difficulty. Did a minute suffice for all this? Nay, perhaps it might be more than a minute.\n\n\"Mrs. Bold--\" at last he said and then stopped himself.\n\nIf he could not speak, how was she to do so? He had called her by her name, the same name that any merest stranger would have used! She withdrew her hand from his and moved as though to return to her seat. \"Eleanor!\" he then said in his softest tone, as though the courage of a lover were as yet but half-assumed, as though he were still afraid of giving offence by the freedom which he took. She looked slowly, gently, almost piteously up into his face. There was at any rate no anger there to deter him.\n\n\"Eleanor!\" he again exclaimed, and in a moment he had her clasped to his bosom. How this was done, whether the doing was with him or her, whether she had flown thither conquered by the tenderness of his voice, or he with a violence not likely to give offence had drawn her to his breast, neither of them knew; nor can I declare. There was now that sympathy between them which hardly admitted of individual motion. They were one and the same--one flesh--one spirit--one life.\n\n\"Eleanor, my own Eleanor, my own, my wife!\" She ventured to look up at him through her tears, and he, bowing his face down over hers, pressed his lips upon her brow--his virgin lips, which, since a beard first grew upon his chin, had never yet tasted the luxury of a woman's cheek.\n\nShe had been told that her yea must be yea, or her nay, nay, but she was called on for neither the one nor the other. She told Miss Thorne that she was engaged to Mr. Arabin, but no such words had passed between them, no promises had been asked or given.\n\n\"Oh, let me go,\" said she, \"let me go now. I am too happy to remain--let me go, that I may be alone.\" He did not try to hinder her; he did not repeat the kiss; he did not press another on her lips. He might have done so, had he been so minded. She was now all his own. He took his arm from round her waist, his arm that was trembling with a new delight, and let her go. She fled like a roe to her own chamber, and then, having turned the bolt, she enjoyed the full luxury of her love. She idolised, almost worshipped this man who had so meekly begged her pardon. And he was now her own. Oh, how she wept and cried and laughed as the hopes and fears and miseries of the last few weeks passed in remembrance through her mind.\n\nMr. Slope! That anyone should have dared to think that she who had been chosen by him could possibly have mated herself with Mr. Slope! That they should have dared to tell him, also, and subject her bright happiness to such needless risk! And then she smiled with joy as she thought of all the comforts that she could give him--not that he cared for comforts, but that it would be so delicious for her to give.\n\nShe got up and rang for her maid that she might tell her little boy of his new father, and in her own way she did tell him. She desired her maid to leave her, in order that she might be alone with her child; and then, while he lay sprawling on the bed, she poured forth the praises, all unmeaning to him, of the man she had selected to guard his infancy.\n\nShe could not be happy, however, till she had made Mr. Arabin take the child to himself and thus, as it were, adopt him as his own. The moment the idea struck her she took the baby up in her arms and, opening her door, ran quickly down to the drawing-room. She at once found, by his step still pacing on the floor, that he was there, and a glance within the room told her that he was alone. She hesitated a moment and then hurried in with her precious charge.\n\nMr. Arabin met her in the middle of the room. \"There,\" said she, breathless with her haste; \"there, take him--take him, and love him.\"\n\nMr. Arabin took the little fellow from her and, kissing him again and again, prayed God to bless him. \"He shall be all as my own--all as my own,\" said he. Eleanor, as she stooped to take back her child, kissed the hand that held him and then rushed back with her treasure to her chamber.\n\nIt was thus that Mr. Harding's younger daughter was won for the second time. At dinner neither she nor Mr. Arabin were very bright, but their silence occasioned no remark. In the drawing-room, as we have before said, she told Miss Thorne what had occurred. The next morning she returned to Barchester, and Mr. Arabin went over with his budget of news to the archdeacon. As Doctor Grantly was not there, he could only satisfy himself by telling Mrs. Grantly how that he intended himself the honour of becoming her brother-in-law. In the ecstasy of her joy at hearing such tidings Mrs. Grantly vouchsafed him a warmer welcome than any he had yet received from Eleanor.\n\n\"Good heavens!\" she exclaimed--it was the general exclamation of the rectory. \"Poor Eleanor! Dear Eleanor! What a monstrous injustice has been done her! Well, it shall all be made up now.\" And then she thought of the signora. \"What lies people tell,\" she said to herself.\n\nBut people in this matter had told no lies at all.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX. The Beelzebub Colt\n\nWhen Miss Thorne left the dining-room, Eleanor had formed no intention of revealing to her what had occurred, but when she was seated beside her hostess on the sofa, the secret dropped from her almost unawares. Eleanor was but a bad hypocrite, and she found herself quite unable to continue talking about Mr. Arabin as though he were a stranger while her heart was full of him. When Miss Thorne, pursuing her own scheme with discreet zeal, asked the young widow whether, in her opinion, it would not be a good thing for Mr. Arabin to get married, she had nothing for it but to confess the truth. \"I suppose it would,\" said Eleanor rather sheepishly. Whereupon Miss Thorne amplified on the idea. \"Oh, Miss Thorne,\" said Eleanor, \"he is going to be married: I am engaged to him.\"\n\nNow Miss Thorne knew very well that there had been no such engagement when she had been walking with Mrs. Bold in the morning. She had also heard enough to be tolerably sure that there had been no preliminaries to such an engagement. She was, therefore, as we have before described, taken a little by surprise. But nevertheless, she embraced her guest and cordially congratulated her.\n\nEleanor had no opportunity of speaking another word to Mr. Arabin that evening, except such words as all the world might hear; and these, as may be supposed, were few enough. Miss Thorne did her best to leave them in privacy, but Mr. Thorne, who knew nothing of what had occurred, and another guest, a friend of his, entirely interfered with her good intentions. So poor Eleanor had to go to bed without one sign of affection. Her state, nevertheless, was not to be pitied.\n\nThe next morning she was up early. It was probable, she thought, that by going down a little before the usual hour of breakfast she might find Mr. Arabin alone in the dining-room. Might it not be that he also would calculate that an interview would thus be possible? Thus thinking, Eleanor was dressed a full hour before the time fixed in the Ullathorne household for morning prayers. She did not at once go down. She was afraid to seem to be too anxious to meet her lover, though heaven knows her anxiety was intense enough. She therefore sat herself down at her window, and repeatedly looking at her watch, nursed her child till she thought she might venture forth.\n\nWhen she found herself at the dining-room door, she stood a moment, hesitating to turn the handle; but when she heard Mr. Thorne's voice inside she hesitated no longer. Her object was defeated, and she might now go in as soon as she liked without the slightest imputation on her delicacy. Mr. Thorne and Mr. Arabin were standing on the hearth-rug, discussing the merits of the Beelzebub colt; or rather, Mr. Thorne was discussing, and Mr. Arabin was listening. That interesting animal had rubbed the stump of his tail against the wall of his stable and occasioned much uneasiness to the Ullathorne master of the horse. Had Eleanor but waited another minute, Mr. Thorne would have been in the stables.\n\nMr. Thorne, when he saw his lady guest, repressed his anxiety. The Beelzebub colt must do without him. And so the three stood, saying little or nothing to each other, till at last the master of the house, finding that he could no longer bear his present state of suspense respecting his favourite young steed, made an elaborate apology to Mrs. Bold and escaped. As he shut the door behind him Eleanor almost wished that he had remained. It was not that she was afraid of Mr. Arabin, but she hardly yet knew how to address him.\n\nHe, however, soon relieved her from her embarrassment. He came up to her, and taking both her hands in his, he said, \"So, Eleanor, you and I are to be man and wife. Is it so?\"\n\nShe looked up into his face, and her lips formed themselves into a single syllable. She uttered no sound, but he could read the affirmative plainly in her face.\n\n\"It is a great trust,\" said he, \"a very great trust.\"\n\n\"It is--it is,\" said Eleanor, not exactly taking what he had said in the sense that he had meant. \"It is a very, very great trust, and I will do my utmost to deserve it.\"\n\n\"And I also will do my utmost to deserve it,\" said Mr. Arabin very solemnly. And then, winding his arm round her waist, he stood there gazing at the fire, and she, with her head leaning on his shoulder, stood by him, well satisfied with her position. They neither of them spoke, or found any want of speaking. All that was needful for them to say had been said. The yea, yea, had been spoken by Eleanor in her own way--and that way had been perfectly satisfactory to Mr. Arabin.\n\nAnd now it remained to them each to enjoy the assurance of the other's love. And how great that luxury is! How far it surpasses any other pleasure which God has allowed to his creatures! And to a woman's heart how doubly delightful!\n\nWhen the ivy has found its tower, when the delicate creeper has found its strong wall, we know how the parasite plants grow and prosper. They were not created to stretch forth their branches alone, and endure without protection the summer's sun and the winter's storm. Alone they but spread themselves on the ground and cower unseen in the dingy shade. But when they have found their firm supporters, how wonderful is their beauty; how all-pervading and victorious! What is the turret without its ivy, or the high garden wall without the jasmine which gives it its beauty and fragrance? The hedge without the honeysuckle is but a hedge.\n\nThere is a feeling still half-existing, but now half-conquered by the force of human nature, that a woman should be ashamed of her love till the husband's right to her compels her to acknowledge it. We would fain preach a different doctrine. A woman should glory in her love, but on that account let her take the more care that it be such as to justify her glory.\n\nEleanor did glory in hers, and she felt, and had cause to feel, that it deserved to be held as glorious. She could have stood there for hours with his arm round her, had fate and Mr. Thorne permitted it. Each moment she crept nearer to his bosom and felt more and more certain that there was her home. What now to her was the archdeacon's arrogance, her sister's coldness, or her dear father's weakness? What need she care for the duplicity of such friends as Charlotte Stanhope? She had found the strong shield that should guard her from all wrongs, the trusty pilot that should henceforward guide her through the shoals and rocks. She would give up the heavy burden of her independence, and once more assume the position of a woman and the duties of a trusting and loving wife.\n\nAnd he, too, stood there fully satisfied with his place. They were both looking intently on the fire, as though they could read there their future fate, till at last Eleanor turned her face towards his. \"How sad you are,\" she said, smiling; and indeed his face was, if not sad, at least serious. \"How sad you are, love!\"\n\n\"Sad,\" said he, looking down at her; \"no, certainly not sad.\" Her sweet, loving eyes were turned towards him, and she smiled softly as he answered her. The temptation was too strong even for the demure propriety of Mr. Arabin, and bending over her, he pressed his lips to hers.\n\nImmediately after this Mr. Thorne appeared, and they were both delighted to hear that the tail of the Beelzebub colt was not materially injured.\n\nIt had been Mr. Harding's intention to hurry over to Ullathorne as soon as possible after his return to Barchester, in order to secure the support of his daughter in his meditated revolt against the archdeacon as touching the deanery; but he was spared the additional journey by hearing that Mrs. Bold had returned unexpectedly home. As soon as he had read her note he started off, and found her waiting for him in her own house.\n\nHow much each of them had to tell the other, and how certain each was that the story which he or she had to tell would astonish the other!\n\n\"My dear, I am so anxious to see you,\" said Mr. Harding, kissing his daughter.\n\n\"Oh, Papa, I have so much to tell you!\" said the daughter, returning the embrace.\n\n\"My dear, they have offered me the deanery!\" said Mr. Harding, anticipating by the suddenness of the revelation the tidings which Eleanor had to give him.\n\n\"Oh, Papa,\" said she, forgetting her own love and happiness in her joy at the surprising news. \"Oh, Papa, can it be possible? Dear Papa, how thoroughly, thoroughly happy that makes me!\"\n\n\"But, my dear, I think it best to refuse it.\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa!\"\n\n\"I am sure you will agree with me, Eleanor, when I explain it to you. You know, my dear, how old I am. If I live I--\"\n\n\"But, Papa, I must tell you about myself.\"\n\n\"Well, my dear.\"\n\n\"I do so wonder how you'll take it.\"\n\n\"Take what?\"\n\n\"If you don't rejoice at it, if it doesn't make you happy, if you don't encourage me, I shall break my heart.\"\n\n\"If that be the case, Nelly, I certainly will encourage you.\"\n\n\"But I fear you won't. I do so fear you won't. And yet you can't but think I am the most fortunate woman living on God's earth.\"\n\n\"Are you, dearest? Then I certainly will rejoice with you. Come, Nelly, come to me and tell me what it is.\"\n\n\"I am going--\"\n\nHe led her to the sofa and, seating himself beside her, took both her hands in his. \"You are going to be married, Nelly. Is not that it?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said faintly. \"That is, if you will approve;\" and then she blushed as she remembered the promise which she had so lately volunteered to him and which she had so utterly forgotten in making her engagement with Mr. Arabin.\n\nMr. Harding thought for a moment who the man could be whom he was to be called upon to welcome as his son-in-law. A week since he would have had no doubt whom to name. In that case he would have been prepared to give his sanction, although he would have done so with a heavy heart. Now he knew that at any rate it would not be Mr. Slope, though he was perfectly at a loss to guess who could possibly have filled the place. For a moment he thought that the man might be Bertie Stanhope, and his very soul sank within him.\n\n\"Well, Nelly?\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, promise to me that, for my sake, you will love him.\"\n\n\"Come, Nelly, come; tell me who it is.\"\n\n\"But will you love him, Papa?\"\n\n\"Dearest, I must love anyone that you love.\" Then she turned her face to his and whispered into his ear the name of Mr. Arabin.\n\nNo man that she could have named could have more surprised or more delighted him. Had he looked round the world for a son-in-law to his taste, he could have selected no one whom he would have preferred to Mr. Arabin. He was a clergyman; he held a living in the neighbourhood; he was of a set to which all Mr. Harding's own partialities most closely adhered; he was the great friend of Dr. Grantly; and he was, moreover, a man of whom Mr. Harding knew nothing but what he approved. Nevertheless, his surprise was so great as to prevent the immediate expression of his joy. He had never thought of Mr. Arabin in connexion with his daughter; he had never imagined that they had any feeling in common. He had feared that his daughter had been made hostile to clergymen of Mr. Arabin's stamp by her intolerance of the archdeacon's pretensions. Had he been put to wish, he might have wished for Mr. Arabin for a son-in-law; but had he been put to guess, the name would never have occurred to him.\n\n\"Mr. Arabin!\" he exclaimed; \"impossible!\"\n\n\"Oh, Papa, for heaven's sake don't say anything against him! If you love me, don't say anything against him. Oh, Papa, it's done and mustn't be undone--oh, Papa!\"\n\nFickle Eleanor! Where was the promise that she would make no choice for herself without her father's approval? She had chosen, and now demanded his acquiescence. \"Oh, Papa, isn't he good? Isn't he noble? Isn't he religious, high-minded, everything that a good man possibly can be?\" She clung to her father, beseeching him for his consent.\n\n\"My Nelly, my child, my own daughter! He is; he is noble and good and high-minded; he is all that a woman can love and a man admire. He shall be my son, my own son. He shall be as close to my heart as you are. My Nelly, my child, my happy, happy child!\"\n\nWe need not pursue the interview any further. By degrees they returned to the subject of the new promotion. Eleanor tried to prove to him, as the Grantlys had done, that his age could be no bar to his being a very excellent dean, but those arguments had now even less weight on him than before. He said little or nothing but sat, meditative. Every now and then he would kiss his daughter and say \"yes,\" or \"no,\" or \"very true,\" or \"well, my dear, I can't quite agree with you there,\" but he could not be got to enter sharply into the question of \"to be, or not to be\" Dean of Barchester. Of her and her happiness, of Mr. Arabin and his virtues, he would talk as much as Eleanor desired--and to tell the truth, that was not a little--but about the deanery he would now say nothing further. He had got a new idea into his head--why should not Mr. Arabin be the new dean?\n\n# CHAPTER L. The Archdeacon Is Satisfied with the State of Affairs\n\nThe archdeacon, in his journey into Barchester, had been assured by Mr. Harding that all their prognostications about Mr. Slope and Eleanor were groundless. Mr. Harding, however, had found it very difficult to shake his son-in-law's faith in his own acuteness. The matter had, to Dr. Grantly, been so plainly corroborated by such patent evidence, borne out by such endless circumstances, that he at first refused to take as true the positive statement which Mr. Harding made to him of Eleanor's own disavowal of the impeachment. But at last he yielded in a qualified way. He brought himself to admit that he would at the present regard his past convictions as a mistake, but in doing this he so guarded himself that if, at any future time, Eleanor should come forth to the world as Mrs. Slope, he might still be able to say: \"There, I told you so. Remember what you said and what I said; and remember also for coming years, that I was right in this matter--as in all others.\"\n\nHe carried, however, his concession so far as to bring himself to undertake to call at Eleanor's house, and he did call accordingly, while the father and daughter were yet in the middle of their conference. Mr. Harding had had so much to hear and to say that he had forgotten to advise Eleanor of the honour that awaited her, and she heard her brother-in-law's voice in the hall while she was quite unprepared to see him.\n\n\"There's the archdeacon,\" she said, springing up.\n\n\"Yes, my dear. He told me to tell you that he would come and see you; but to tell the truth I had forgotten all about it.\"\n\nEleanor fled away, regardless of all her father's entreaties. She could not now, in the first hours of her joy, bring herself to bear all the archdeacon's retractions, apologies, and congratulations. He would have so much to say, and would be so tedious in saying it; consequently, the archdeacon, when he was shown into the drawing-room, found no one there but Mr. Harding.\n\n\"You must excuse Eleanor,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"Is anything the matter?\" asked the doctor, who at once anticipated that the whole truth about Mr. Slope had at last come out.\n\n\"Well, something is the matter. I wonder now whether you will be much surprised.\"\n\nThe archdeacon saw by his father-in-law's manner that after all he had nothing to tell him about Mr. Slope. \"No,\" said he, \"certainly not--nothing will ever surprise me again.\" Very many men now-a-days besides the archdeacon adopt or affect to adopt the nil admirari doctrine; but nevertheless, to judge from their appearance, they are just as subject to sudden emotions as their grandfathers and grandmothers were before them.\n\n\"What do you think Mr. Arabin has done?\"\n\n\"Mr. Arabin! It's nothing about that daughter of Stanhope's, I hope?\"\n\n\"No, not that woman,\" said Mr. Harding, enjoying his joke in his sleeve.\n\n\"Not that woman! Is he going to do anything about any woman? Why can't you speak out, if you have anything to say? There is nothing I hate so much as these sort of mysteries.\"\n\n\"There shall be no mystery with you, Archdeacon, though of course it must go no further at present.\"\n\n\"Well.\"\n\n\"Except Susan. You must promise me you'll tell no one else.\"\n\n\"Nonsense!\" exclaimed the archdeacon, who was becoming angry in his suspense. \"You can't have any secret about Mr. Arabin.\"\n\n\"Only this--that he and Eleanor are engaged.\"\n\nIt was quite clear to see, by the archdeacon's face, that he did not believe a word of it. \"Mr. Arabin! It's impossible!\"\n\n\"Eleanor, at any rate, has just now told me so.\"\n\n\"It's impossible,\" repeated the archdeacon.\n\n\"Well, I can't say I think it impossible. It certainly took me by surprise, but that does not make it impossible.\"\n\n\"She must be mistaken.\"\n\nMr. Harding assured him that there was no mistake; that he would find, on returning home, that Mr. Arabin had been at Plumstead with the express object of making the same declaration; that even Miss Thorne knew all about it; and that, in fact, the thing was as clearly settled as any such arrangement between a lady and a gentleman could well be.\n\n\"Good heavens!\" said the archdeacon, walking up and down Eleanor's drawing-room. \"Good heavens! Good heavens!\"\n\nNow these exclamations certainly betokened faith. Mr. Harding properly gathered from it that, at last, Dr. Grantly did believe the fact. The first utterance clearly evinced a certain amount of distaste at the information he had received; the second simply indicated surprise; in the tone of the third Mr. Harding fancied that he could catch a certain gleam of satisfaction.\n\nThe archdeacon had truly expressed the workings of his mind. He could not but be disgusted to find how utterly astray he had been in all his anticipations. Had he only been lucky enough to have suggested this marriage himself when he first brought Mr. Arabin into the country, his character for judgement and wisdom would have received an addition which would have classed him at any rate next to Solomon. And why had he not done so? Might he not have foreseen that Mr. Arabin would want a wife in his parsonage? He had foreseen that Eleanor would want a husband, but should he not also have perceived that Mr. Arabin was a man much more likely to attract her than Mr. Slope? The archdeacon found that he had been at fault and, of course, could not immediately get over his discomfiture.\n\nThen his surprise was intense. How sly this pair of young turtle-doves had been with him. How egregiously they had hoaxed him. He had preached to Eleanor against her fancied attachment to Mr. Slope at the very time that she was in love with his own protégé, Mr. Arabin, and had absolutely taken that same Mr. Arabin into his confidence with reference to his dread of Mr. Slope's alliance. It was very natural that the archdeacon should feel surprise.\n\nBut there was also great ground for satisfaction. Looking at the match by itself, it was the very thing to help the doctor out of his difficulties. In the first place, the assurance that he should never have Mr. Slope for his brother-in-law was in itself a great comfort. Then Mr. Arabin was, of all men, the one with whom it would best suit him to be so intimately connected. But the crowning comfort was the blow which this marriage would give to Mr. Slope. He had now certainly lost his wife; rumour was beginning to whisper that he might possibly lose his position in the palace; and if Mr. Harding would only be true, the great danger of all would be surmounted. In such case it might be expected that Mr. Slope would own himself vanquished, and take himself altogether away from Barchester. And so the archdeacon would again be able to breathe pure air.\n\n\"Well, well,\" said he. \"Good heavens! Good heavens!\" and the tone of the fifth exclamation made Mr. Harding fully aware that content was reigning in the archdeacon's bosom.\n\nAnd then slowly, gradually, and craftily Mr. Harding propounded his own new scheme. Why should not Mr. Arabin be the new dean?\n\nSlowly, gradually, and thoughtfully Dr. Grantly fell into his father-in-law's views. Much as he liked Mr. Arabin, sincere as was his admiration for that gentleman's ecclesiastical abilities, he would not have sanctioned a measure which would rob his father-in-law of his fairly earned promotion, were it at all practicable to induce his father-in-law to accept the promotion which he had earned. But the archdeacon had, on a former occasion, received proof of the obstinacy with which Mr. Harding could adhere to his own views in opposition to the advice of all his friends. He knew tolerably well that nothing would induce the meek, mild man before him to take the high place offered to him, if he thought it wrong to do so. Knowing this, he also said to himself more than once: \"Why should not Mr. Arabin be Dean of Barchester?\" It was at last arranged between them that they would together start to London by the earliest train on the following morning, making a little detour to Oxford on their journey. Dr. Gwynne's counsels, they imagined, might perhaps be of assistance to them.\n\nThese matters settled, the archdeacon hurried off, that he might return to Plumstead and prepare for his journey. The day was extremely fine, and he came into the city in an open gig. As he was driving up the High Street he encountered Mr. Slope at a crossing. Had he not pulled up rather sharply, he would have run over him. The two had never spoken to each other since they had met on a memorable occasion in the bishop's study. They did not speak now, but they looked each other full in the face, and Mr. Slope's countenance was as impudent, as triumphant, as defiant as ever. Had Dr. Grantly not known to the contrary, he would have imagined that his enemy had won the deanship, the wife, and all the rich honours for which he had been striving. As it was, he had lost everything that he had in the world, and had just received his congé from the bishop.\n\nIn leaving the town the archdeacon drove by the well-remembered entrance of Hiram's Hospital. There, at the gate, was a large, untidy farmer's wagon, laden with untidy-looking furniture; and there, inspecting the arrival, was good Mrs. Quiverful--not dressed in her Sunday best, not very clean in her apparel, not graceful as to her bonnet and shawl, or, indeed, with many feminine charms as to her whole appearance. She was busy at domestic work in her new house, and had just ventured out, expecting to see no one on the arrival of the family chattels. The archdeacon was down upon her before she knew where she was.\n\nHer acquaintance with Dr. Grantly or his family was very slight indeed. The archdeacon, as a matter of course, knew every clergyman in the archdeaconry--it may almost be said in the diocese--and had some acquaintance, more or less intimate, with their wives and families. With Mr. Quiverful he had been concerned on various matters of business, but of Mrs. Q. he had seen very little. Now, however, he was in too gracious a mood to pass her by unnoticed. The Quiverfuls, one and all, had looked for the bitterest hostility from Dr. Grantly; they knew his anxiety that Mr. Harding should return to his old home at the hospital, and they did not know that a new home had been offered to him at the deanery. Mrs. Quiverful was therefore not a little surprised, and not a little rejoiced also, at the tone in which she was addressed.\n\n\"How do you do, Mrs. Quiverful, how do you do?\" said he, stretching his left hand out of the gig as he spoke to her. \"I am very glad to see you employed in so pleasant and useful a manner; very glad indeed.\"\n\nMrs. Quiverful thanked him, and shook hands with him, and looked into his face suspiciously. She was not sure whether the congratulations and kindness were or were not ironical.\n\n\"Pray tell Mr. Quiverful from me,\" he continued, \"that I am rejoiced at his appointment. It's a comfortable place, Mrs. Quiverful, and a comfortable house, and I am very glad to see you in it. Good-bye--good-bye.\" And he drove on, leaving the lady well pleased and astonished at his good nature. On the whole things were going well with the archdeacon, and he could afford to be charitable to Mrs. Quiverful. He looked forth from his gig smilingly on all the world, and forgave everyone in Barchester their sins, excepting only Mrs. Proudie and Mr. Slope. Had he seen the bishop, he would have felt inclined to pat even him kindly on the head.\n\nHe determined to go home by St. Ewold's. This would take him some three miles out of his way, but he felt that he could not leave Plumstead comfortably without saying one word of good-fellowship to Mr. Arabin. When he reached the parsonage, the vicar was still out, but from what he had heard, he did not doubt but that he would meet him on the road between their two houses. He was right in this, for about half-way home, at a narrow turn, he came upon Mr. Arabin, who was on horseback.\n\n\"Well, well, well, well,\" said the archdeacon loudly, joyously, and with supreme good humour; \"well, well, well, well; so, after all, we have no further cause to fear Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"I hear from Mrs. Grantly that they have offered the deanery to Mr. Harding,\" said the other.\n\n\"Mr. Slope has lost more than the deanery I find,\" and then the archdeacon laughed jocosely. \"Come, come, Arabin, you have kept your secret well enough. I know all about it now.\"\n\n\"I have had no secret, Archdeacon,\" said the other with a quiet smile. \"None at all--not for a day. It was only yesterday that I knew my own good fortune, and to-day I went over to Plumstead to ask your approval. From what Mrs. Grantly has said to me, I am led to hope that I shall have it.\"\n\n\"With all my heart, with all my heart,\" said the archdeacon cordially, holding his friend fast by the hand. \"It's just as I would have it. She is an excellent young woman; she will not come to you empty-handed; and I think she will make you a good wife. If she does her duty by you as her sister does by me, you'll be a happy man; that's all I can say.\" And as he finished speaking a tear might have been observed in each of the doctor's eyes.\n\nMr. Arabin warmly returned the archdeacon's grasp, but he said little. His heart was too full for speaking, and he could not express the gratitude which he felt. Dr. Grantly understood him as well as though he had spoken for an hour.\n\n\"And mind, Arabin,\" said he, \"no one but myself shall tie the knot. We'll get Eleanor out to Plumstead, and it shall come off there. I'll make Susan stir herself, and we'll do it in style. I must be off to London to-morrow on special business. Harding goes with me. But I'll be back before your bride has got her wedding-dress ready.\" And so they parted.\n\nOn his journey home the archdeacon occupied his mind with preparations for the marriage festivities. He made a great resolve that he would atone to Eleanor for all the injury he had done her by the munificence of his future treatment. He would show her what was the difference in his eyes between a Slope and an Arabin. On one other thing also he decided with a firm mind: if the affair of the dean should not be settled in Mr. Arabin's favour, nothing should prevent him putting a new front and bow-window to the dining-room at St. Ewold's parsonage.\n\n\"So we're sold after all, Sue,\" said he to his wife, accosting her with a kiss as soon as he entered his house. He did not call his wife Sue above twice or thrice in a year, and these occasions were great high days.\n\n\"Eleanor has had more sense than we gave her credit for,\" said Mrs. Grantly.\n\nAnd there was great content in Plumstead Rectory that evening. Mrs. Grantly promised her husband that she would now open her heart and take Mr. Arabin into it. Hitherto she had declined to do so.\n\n# CHAPTER LI. Mr. Slope Bids Farewell to the Palace and Its Inhabitants\n\nWe must now take leave of Mr. Slope, and of the bishop also, and of Mrs. Proudie. These leave-takings in novels are as disagreeable as they are in real life; not so sad, indeed, for they want the reality of sadness; but quite as perplexing, and generally less satisfactory. What novelist, what Fielding, what Scott, what George Sand, or Sue, or Dumas, can impart an interest to the last chapter of his fictitious history? Promises of two children and superhuman happiness are of no avail, nor assurance of extreme respectability carried to an age far exceeding that usually allotted to mortals. The sorrows of our heroes and heroines, they are your delight, oh public!--their sorrows, or their sins, or their absurdities; not their virtues, good sense, and consequent rewards. When we begin to tint our final pages with couleur de rose, as in accordance with fixed rule we must do, we altogether extinguish our own powers of pleasing. When we become dull, we offend your intellect; and we must become dull or we should offend your taste. A late writer, wishing to sustain his interest to the last page, hung his hero at the end of the third volume. The consequence was that no one would read his novel. And who can apportion out and dovetail his incidents, dialogues, characters, and descriptive morsels so as to fit them all exactly into 930 pages, without either compressing them unnaturally, or extending them artificially at the end of his labour? Do I not myself know that I am at this moment in want of a dozen pages, and that I am sick with cudgelling my brains to find them? And then, when everything is done, the kindest-hearted critic of them all invariably twits us with the incompetency and lameness of our conclusion. We have either become idle and neglected it, or tedious and overlaboured it. It is insipid or unnatural, overstrained or imbecile. It means nothing, or attempts too much. The last scene of all, as all last scenes we fear must be,\n\nIs second childishness, and mere oblivion, Sans teeth, sans eyes, sans taste, sans everything.\n\nI can only say that if some critic who thoroughly knows his work, and has laboured on it till experience has made him perfect, will write the last fifty pages of a novel in the way they should be written, I, for one, will in future do my best to copy the example. Guided by my own lights only, I confess that I despair of success.\n\nFor the last week or ten days Mr. Slope had seen nothing of Mrs. Proudie, and very little of the bishop. He still lived in the palace, and still went through his usual routine work; but the confidential doings of the diocese had passed into other hands. He had seen this clearly and marked it well, but it had not much disturbed him. He had indulged in other hopes till the bishop's affairs had become dull to him, and he was moreover aware that, as regarded the diocese, Mrs. Proudie had checkmated him. It has been explained, in the beginning of these pages, how three or four were contending together as to who, in fact, should be Bishop of Barchester. Each of these had now admitted to himself (or boasted to herself) that Mrs. Proudie was victorious in the struggle. They had gone through a competitive examination of considerable severity, and she had come forth the winner, facile princeps. Mr. Slope had for a moment run her hard, but it was only for a moment. It had become, as it were, acknowledged that Hiram's Hospital should be the testing-point between them, and now Mr. Quiverful was already in the hospital, the proof of Mrs. Proudie's skill and courage.\n\nAll this did not break down Mr. Slope's spirit, because he had other hopes. But, alas, at last there came to him a note from his friend Sir Nicholas, informing him that the deanship was disposed of. Let us give Mr. Slope his due. He did not lie prostrate under this blow, or give himself up to vain lamentations; he did not henceforward despair of life and call upon gods above and gods below to carry him off. He sat himself down in his chair, counted out what monies he had in hand for present purposes and what others were coming in to him, bethought himself as to the best sphere for his future exertions, and at once wrote off a letter to a rich sugar-refiner's wife in Baker Street, who, as he well knew, was much given to the entertainment and encouragement of serious young evangelical clergymen. He was again, he said, \"upon the world, having found the air of a cathedral town, and the very nature of cathedral services, uncongenial to his spirit;\" and then he sat awhile, making firm resolves as to his manner of parting from the bishop, and also as to his future conduct.\n\nAt last he rose, and twitched his mantle blue (black), To-morrow to fresh woods and pastures new.\n\nHaving received a formal command to wait upon the bishop, he rose and proceeded to obey it. He rang the bell and desired the servant to inform his master that, if it suited his lordship, he, Mr. Slope, was ready to wait upon him. The servant, who well understood that Mr. Slope was no longer in the ascendant, brought back a message saying that \"his lordship desired that Mr. Slope would attend him immediately in his study.\" Mr. Slope waited about ten minutes more to prove his independence, and then he went into the bishop's room. There, as he had expected, he found Mrs. Proudie, together with her husband.\n\n\"Hum, ha--Mr. Slope, pray take a chair,\" said the gentleman bishop.\n\n\"Pray be seated, Mr. Slope,\" said the lady bishop.\n\n\"Thank ye, thank ye,\" said Mr. Slope, and walking round to the fire, he threw himself into one of the armchairs that graced the hearth-rug.\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" said the bishop, \"it has become necessary that I should speak to you definitively on a matter that has for some time been pressing itself on my attention.\"\n\n\"May I ask whether the subject is in any way connected with myself?\" said Mr. Slope.\n\n\"It is so--certainly--yes, it certainly is connected with yourself, Mr. Slope.\"\n\n\"Then, my lord, if I may be allowed to express a wish, I would prefer that no discussion on the subject should take place between us in the presence of a third person.\"\n\n\"Don't alarm yourself, Mr. Slope,\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"no discussion is at all necessary. The bishop merely intends to express his own wishes.\"\n\n\"I merely intend, Mr. Slope, to express my own wishes--no discussion will be at all necessary,\" said the bishop, reiterating his wife's words.\n\n\"That is more, my lord, than we any of us can be sure of,\" said Mr. Slope; \"I cannot, however, force Mrs. Proudie to leave the room; nor can I refuse to remain here if it be your lordship's wish that I should do so.\"\n\n\"It is his lordship's wish, certainly,\" said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n\"Mr. Slope,\" began the bishop in a solemn, serious voice, \"it grieves me to have to find fault. It grieves me much to have to find fault with a clergyman--but especially so with a clergyman in your position.\"\n\n\"Why, what have I done amiss, my lord?\" demanded Mr. Slope boldly.\n\n\"What have you done amiss, Mr. Slope?\" said Mrs. Proudie, standing erect before the culprit and raising that terrible forefinger. \"Do you dare to ask the bishop what you have done amiss? Does not your conscience--\"\n\n\"Mrs. Proudie, pray let it be understood, once for all, that I will have no words with you.\"\n\n\"Ah, sir, but you will have words,\" said she; \"you must have words. Why have you had so many words with that Signora Neroni? Why have you disgraced yourself, you a clergyman, too, by constantly consorting with such a woman as that--with a married woman--with one altogether unfit for a clergyman's society?\"\n\n\"At any rate I was introduced to her in your drawing-room,\" retorted Mr. Slope.\n\n\"And shamefully you behaved there,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"most shamefully. I was wrong to allow you to remain in the house a day after what I then saw. I should have insisted on your instant dismissal.\"\n\n\"I have yet to learn, Mrs. Proudie, that you have the power to insist either on my going from hence or on my staying here.\"\n\n\"What!\" said the lady. \"I am not to have the privilege of saying who shall and who shall not frequent my own drawing-room! I am not to save my servants and dependants from having their morals corrupted by improper conduct! I am not to save my own daughters from impurity! I will let you see, Mr. Slope, whether I have the power or whether I have not. You will have the goodness to understand that you no longer fill any situation about the bishop, and as your room will be immediately wanted in the palace for another chaplain, I must ask you to provide yourself with apartments as soon as may be convenient to you.\"\n\n\"My lord,\" said Mr. Slope, appealing to the bishop, and so turning his back completely on the lady, \"will you permit me to ask that I may have from your own lips any decision that you may have come to on this matter?\"\n\n\"Certainly, Mr. Slope, certainly,\" said the bishop; \"that is but reasonable. Well, my decision is that you had better look for some other preferment. For the situation which you have lately held I do not think that you are well suited.\"\n\n\"And what, my lord, has been my fault?\"\n\n\"That Signora Neroni is one fault,\" said Mrs. Proudie; \"and a very abominable fault she is; very abominable and very disgraceful. Fie, Mr. Slope, fie! You an evangelical clergyman indeed!\"\n\n\"My lord, I desire to know for what fault I am turned out of your lordship's house.\"\n\n\"You hear what Mrs. Proudie says,\" said the bishop.\n\n\"When I publish the history of this transaction, my lord, as I decidedly shall do in my own vindication, I presume you will not wish me to state that you have discarded me at your wife's bidding--because she has objected to my being acquainted with another lady, the daughter of one of the prebendaries of the chapter?\"\n\n\"You may publish what you please, sir,\" said Mrs. Proudie. \"But you will not be insane enough to publish any of your doings in Barchester. Do you think I have not heard of your kneelings at that creature's feet--that is, if she has any feet--and of your constant slobbering over her hand? I advise you to beware, Mr. Slope, of what you do and say. Clergymen have been unfrocked for less than what you have been guilty of.\"\n\n\"My lord, if this goes on I shall be obliged to indict this woman--Mrs. Proudie I mean--for defamation of character.\"\n\n\"I think, Mr. Slope, you had better now retire,\" said the bishop. \"I will enclose to you a cheque for any balance that may be due to you; under the present circumstances, it will of course be better for all parties that you should leave the palace at the earliest possible moment. I will allow you for your journey back to London and for your maintenance in Barchester for a week from this date.\"\n\n\"If, however, you wish to remain in this neighbourhood;\" said Mrs. Proudie, \"and will solemnly pledge yourself never again to see that woman, and will promise also to be more circumspect in your conduct, the bishop will mention your name to Mr. Quiverful, who now wants a curate at Puddingdale. The house is, I imagine, quite sufficient for your requirements, and there will moreover be a stipend of fifty pounds a year.\"\n\n\"May God forgive you, madam, for the manner in which you have treated me,\" said Mr. Slope, looking at her with a very heavenly look; \"and remember this, madam, that you yourself may still have a fall;\" and he looked at her with a very worldly look. \"As to the bishop, I pity him!\" And so saying, Mr. Slope left the room. Thus ended the intimacy of the Bishop of Barchester with his first confidential chaplain.\n\nMrs. Proudie was right in this; namely, that Mr. Slope was not insane enough to publish to the world any of his doings in Barchester. He did not trouble his friend Mr. Towers with any written statement of the iniquity of Mrs. Proudie, or the imbecility of her husband. He was aware that it would be wise in him to drop for the future all allusion to his doings in the cathedral city. Soon after the interview just recorded he left Barchester, shaking the dust off his feet as he entered the railway carriage; and he gave no longing, lingering look after the cathedral towers as the train hurried him quickly out of their sight.\n\nIt is well known that the family of the Slopes never starve: they always fall on their feet, like cats; and let them fall where they will, they live on the fat of the land. Our Mr. Slope did so. On his return to town he found that the sugar-refiner had died and that his widow was inconsolable--in other words, in want of consolation. Mr. Slope consoled her, and soon found himself settled with much comfort in the house in Baker Street. He possessed himself, also, before long, of a church in the vicinity of the Red Road, and became known to fame as one of the most eloquent preachers and pious clergymen in that part of the metropolis. There let us leave him.\n\nOf the bishop and his wife very little further need be said. From that time forth nothing material occurred to interrupt the even course of their domestic harmony. Very speedily, a further vacancy on the bench of bishops gave to Dr. Proudie the seat in the House of Lords, which he at first so anxiously longed for. But by this time he had become a wiser man. He did certainly take his seat, and occasionally registered a vote in favour of Government views on ecclesiastical matters. But he had thoroughly learnt that his proper sphere of action lay in close contiguity with Mrs. Proudie's wardrobe. He never again aspired to disobey, or seemed even to wish for autocratic diocesan authority. If ever he thought of freedom, he did so as men think of the millennium, as of a good time which may be coming, but which nobody expects to come in their day. Mrs. Proudie might be said still to bloom, and was, at any rate, strong, and the bishop had no reason to apprehend that he would be speedily visited with the sorrows of a widower's life.\n\nHe is still Bishop of Barchester. He has so graced that throne that the Government has been averse to translate him, even to higher dignities. There may he remain, under safe pupilage, till the newfangled manners of the age have discovered him to be superannuated and bestowed on him a pension. As for Mrs. Proudie, our prayers for her are that she may live forever.\n\n# CHAPTER LII. The New Dean Takes Possession of the Deanery, and the New Warden of the Hospital\n\nMr. Harding and the archdeacon together made their way to Oxford, and there, by dint of cunning argument, they induced the Master of Lazarus also to ask himself this momentous question: \"Why should not Mr. Arabin be Dean of Barchester?\" He, of course, for awhile tried his hand at persuading Mr. Harding that he was foolish, overscrupulous, self-willed, and weak-minded; but he tried in vain. If Mr. Harding would not give way to Dr. Grantly, it was not likely that he would give way to Dr. Gwynne, more especially now that so admirable a scheme as that of inducting Mr. Arabin into the deanery had been set on foot. When the master found that his eloquence was vain, and heard also that Mr. Arabin was about to become Mr. Harding's son-in-law, he confessed that he also would, under such circumstances, be glad to see his old friend and protégé, the fellow of his college, placed in the comfortable position that was going a-begging.\n\n\"It might be the means you know, Master, of keeping Mr. Slope out,\" said the archdeacon with grave caution.\n\n\"He has no more chance of it,\" said the master, \"than our college chaplain. I know more about it than that.\"\n\nMrs. Grantly had been right in her surmise. It was the Master of Lazarus who had been instrumental in representing in high places the claims which Mr. Harding had upon the Government, and he now consented to use his best endeavours towards getting the offer transferred to Mr. Arabin. The three of them went on to London together, and there they remained a week, to the great disgust of Mrs. Grantly, and most probably also of Mrs. Gwynne. The minister was out of town in one direction, and his private secretary in another. The clerks who remained could do nothing in such a matter as this, and all was difficulty and confusion. The two doctors seemed to have plenty to do; they bustled here and they bustled there, and complained at their club in the evenings that they had been driven off their legs; but Mr. Harding had no occupation. Once or twice he suggested that he might perhaps return to Barchester. His request, however, was peremptorily refused, and he had nothing for it but to while away his time in Westminster Abbey.\n\nAt length an answer from the great man came. The Master of Lazarus had made his proposition through the Bishop of Belgravia. Now this bishop, though but newly gifted with his diocesan honours, was a man of much weight in the clerico-political world. He was, if not as pious, at any rate as wise as St. Paul, and had been with so much effect all things to all men that, though he was great among the dons of Oxford, he had been selected for the most favourite seat on the bench by a Whig prime minister. To him Dr. Gwynne had made known his wishes and his arguments, and the bishop had made them known to the Marquis of Kensington-Gore. The marquis, who was Lord High Steward of the Pantry Board, and who by most men was supposed to hold the highest office out of the cabinet, trafficked much in affairs of this kind. He not only suggested the arrangement to the minister over a cup of coffee, standing on a drawing-room rug in Windsor Castle, but he also favourably mentioned Mr. Arabin's name in the ear of a distinguished person.\n\nAnd so the matter was arranged. The answer of the great man came, and Mr. Arabin was made Dean of Barchester. The three clergymen who had come up to town on this important mission dined together with great glee on the day on which the news reached them. In a silent, decent, clerical manner they toasted Mr. Arabin with full bumpers of claret. The satisfaction of all of them was supreme. The Master of Lazarus had been successful in his attempt, and success is dear to us all. The archdeacon had trampled upon Mr. Slope, and had lifted to high honours the young clergyman whom he had induced to quit the retirement and comfort of the university. So at least the archdeacon thought; though, to speak sooth, not he, but circumstances, had trampled on Mr. Slope. But the satisfaction of Mr. Harding was, of all, perhaps, the most complete. He laid aside his usual melancholy manner and brought forth little quiet jokes from the inmost mirth of his heart; he poked his fun at the archdeacon about Mr. Slope's marriage and quizzed him for his improper love for Mrs. Proudie. On the following day they all returned to Barchester.\n\nIt was arranged that Mr. Arabin should know nothing of what had been done till he received the minister's letter from the hands of his embryo father-in-law. In order that no time might be lost, a message had been sent to him by the preceding night's post, begging him to be at the deanery at the hour that the train from London arrived. There was nothing in this which surprised Mr. Arabin. It had somehow got about through all Barchester that Mr. Harding was the new dean, and all Barchester was prepared to welcome him with pealing bells and full hearts. Mr. Slope had certainly had a party; there had certainly been those in Barchester who were prepared to congratulate him on his promotion with assumed sincerity, but even his own party was not broken-hearted by his failure. The inhabitants of the city, even the high-souled, ecstatic young ladies of thirty-five, had begun to comprehend that their welfare, and the welfare of the place, was connected in some mysterious manner with daily chants and bi-weekly anthems. The expenditure of the palace had not added much to the popularity of the bishop's side of the question; and, on the whole, there was a strong reaction. When it became known to all the world that Mr. Harding was to be the new dean, all the world rejoiced heartily.\n\nMr. Arabin, we have said, was not surprised at the summons which called him to the deanery. He had not as yet seen Mr. Harding since Eleanor had accepted him, nor had he seen him since he had learnt his future father-in-law's preferment. There was nothing more natural, more necessary, than that they should meet each other at the earliest possible moment. Mr. Arabin was waiting in the deanery parlour when Mr. Harding and Dr. Grantly were driven up from the station.\n\nThere was some excitement in the bosoms of them all, as they met and shook hands; by far too much to enable either of them to begin his story and tell it in a proper equable style of narrative. Mr. Harding was some minutes quite dumbfounded, and Mr. Arabin could only talk in short, spasmodic sentences about his love and good fortune. He slipped in, as best he could, some sort of congratulation about the deanship, and then went on with his hopes and fears--hopes that he might be received as a son, and fears that he hardly deserved such good fortune. Then he went back to the dean; it was the most thoroughly satisfactory appointment, he said, of which he had ever heard.\n\n\"But! But! But--\" said Mr. Harding, and then, failing to get any further, he looked imploringly at the archdeacon.\n\n\"The truth is, Arabin,\" said the doctor, \"that, after all you are not destined to be son-in-law to a dean. Nor am I either: more's the pity.\"\n\nMr. Arabin looked at him for explanation. \"Is not Mr. Harding to be the new dean?\"\n\n\"It appears not,\" said the archdeacon. Mr. Arabin's face fell a little, and he looked from one to the other. It was plainly to be seen from them both that there was no cause of unhappiness in the matter, at least not of unhappiness to them; but there was as yet no elucidation of the mystery.\n\n\"Think how old I am,\" said Mr. Harding imploringly.\n\n\"Fiddlestick!\" said the archdeacon.\n\n\"That's all very well, but it won't make a young man of me,\" said Mr. Harding.\n\n\"And who is to be dean?\" asked Mr. Arabin.\n\n\"Yes, that's the question,\" said the archdeacon. \"Come, Mr. Precentor, since you obstinately refuse to be anything else, let us know who is to be the man. He has got the nomination in his pocket.\"\n\nWith eyes brim full of tears, Mr. Harding pulled out the letter and handed it to his future son-in-law. He tried to make a little speech but failed altogether. Having given up the document, he turned round to the wall, feigning to blow his nose, and then sat himself down on the old dean's dingy horsehair sofa. And here we find it necessary to bring our account of the interview to an end.\n\nNor can we pretend to describe the rapture with which Mr. Harding was received by his daughter. She wept with grief and wept with joy--with grief that her father should, in his old age, still be without that rank and worldly position which, according to her ideas, he had so well earned; and with joy in that he, her darling father, should have bestowed on that other dear one the good things of which he himself would not open his hand to take possession. And here Mr. Harding again showed his weakness. In the mêlée of this exposal of their loves and reciprocal affection, he found himself unable to resist the entreaties of all parties that the lodgings in the High Street should be given up. Eleanor would not live in the deanery, she said, unless her father lived there also. Mr. Arabin would not be dean, unless Mr. Harding would be co-dean with him. The archdeacon declared that his father-in-law should not have his own way in everything, and Mrs. Grantly carried him off to Plumstead, that he might remain there till Mr. and Mrs. Arabin were in a state to receive him in their own mansion.\n\nPressed by such arguments as these, what could a weak old man do but yield?\n\nBut there was yet another task which it behoved Mr. Harding to do before he could allow himself to be at rest. Little has been said in these pages of the state of those remaining old men who had lived under his sway at the hospital. But not on this account must it be presumed that he had forgotten them, or that in their state of anarchy and in their want of due government he had omitted to visit them. He visited them constantly, and had latterly given them to understand that they would soon be required to subscribe their adherence to a new master. There were now but five of them, one of them having been but quite lately carried to his rest--but five of the full number, which had hitherto been twelve, and which was now to be raised to twenty-four, including women. Of these, old Bunce, who for many years had been the favourite of the late warden, was one; and Abel Handy, who had been the humble means of driving that warden from his home, was another.\n\nMr. Harding now resolved that he himself would introduce the new warden to the hospital. He felt that many circumstances might conspire to make the men receive Mr. Quiverful with aversion and disrespect; he felt also that Mr. Quiverful might himself feel some qualms of conscience if he entered the hospital with an idea that he did so in hostility to his predecessor. Mr. Harding therefore determined to walk in, arm in arm with Mr. Quiverful, and to ask from these men their respectful obedience to their new master.\n\nOn returning to Barchester, he found that Mr. Quiverful had not yet slept in the hospital house, or entered on his new duties. He accordingly made known to that gentleman his wishes, and his proposition was not rejected.\n\nIt was a bright, clear morning, though in November, that Mr. Harding and Mr. Quiverful, arm in arm, walked through the hospital gate. It was one trait in our old friend's character that he did nothing with parade. He omitted, even in the more important doings of his life, that sort of parade by which most of us deem it necessary to grace our important doings. We have house-warmings, christenings, and gala days; we keep, if not our own birthdays, those of our children; we are apt to fuss ourselves if called upon to change our residences and have, almost all of us, our little state occasions. Mr. Harding had no state occasions. When he left his old house, he went forth from it with the same quiet composure as though he were merely taking his daily walk; now that he re-entered it with another warden under his wing, he did so with the same quiet step and calm demeanour. He was a little less upright than he had been five years, nay, it was now nearly six years ago; he walked perhaps a little slower; his footfall was perhaps a thought less firm; otherwise one might have said that he was merely returning with a friend under his arm.\n\nThis friendliness was everything to Mr. Quiverful. To him, even in his poverty, the thought that he was supplanting a brother clergyman so kind and courteous as Mr. Harding had been very bitter. Under his circumstances it had been impossible for him to refuse the proffered boon; he could not reject the bread that was offered to his children, or refuse to ease the heavy burden that had so long oppressed that poor wife of his; nevertheless, it had been very grievous to him to think that in going to the hospital he might encounter the ill-will of his brethren in the diocese. All this Mr. Harding had fully comprehended. It was for such feelings as these, for the nice comprehension of such motives, that his heart and intellect were peculiarly fitted. In most matters of worldly import the archdeacon set down his father-in-law as little better than a fool. And perhaps he was right. But in some other matters, equally important if they be rightly judged, Mr. Harding, had he been so minded, might with as much propriety have set down his son-in-law for a fool. Few men, however, are constituted as was Mr. Harding. He had that nice appreciation of the feelings of others which belongs of right exclusively to women.\n\nArm in arm they walked into the inner quadrangle of the building, and there the five old men met them. Mr. Harding shook hands with them all, and then Mr. Quiverful did the same. With Bunce Mr. Harding shook hands twice, and Mr. Quiverful was about to repeat the same ceremony, but the old man gave him no encouragement.\n\n\"I am very glad to know that at last you have a new warden,\" said Mr. Harding in a very cheery voice.\n\n\"We be very old for any change,\" said one of them, \"but we do suppose it be all for the best.\"\n\n\"Certainly--certainly it is for the best,\" said Mr. Harding. \"You will again have a clergyman of your own church under the same roof with you, and a very excellent clergyman you will have. It is a great satisfaction to me to know that so good a man is coming to take care of you, and that it is no stranger, but a friend of my own who will allow me from time to time to come in and see you.\"\n\n\"We be very thankful to your Reverence,\" said another of them.\n\n\"I need not tell you, my good friends,\" said Mr. Quiverful, \"how extremely grateful I am to Mr. Harding for his kindness to me--I must say his uncalled-for, unexpected kindness.\"\n\n\"He be always very kind,\" said a third.\n\n\"What I can do to fill the void which he left here I will do. For your sake and my own I will do so, and especially for his sake. But to you who have known him, I can never be the same well-loved friend and father that he has been.\"\n\n\"No, sir, no,\" said old Bunce, who hitherto had held his peace; \"no one can be that. Not if the new bishop sent a hangel to us out of heaven. We doesn't doubt you'll do your best, sir, but you'll not be like the old master--not to us old ones.\"\n\n\"Fie, Bunce, fie; how dare you talk in that way?\" said Mr. Harding; but as he scolded the old man he still held him by his arm and pressed it with warm affection.\n\nThere was no getting up any enthusiasm in the matter. How could five old men tottering away to their final resting place be enthusiastic on the reception of a stranger? What could Mr. Quiverful be to them, or they to Mr. Quiverful? Had Mr. Harding indeed come back to them, some last flicker of joyous light might have shone forth on their aged cheeks; but it was in vain to bid them rejoice because Mr. Quiverful was about to move his fourteen children from Puddingdale into the hospital house. In reality they did no doubt receive advantage, spiritual as well as corporal, but this they could neither anticipate nor acknowledge.\n\nIt was a dull affair enough, this introduction of Mr. Quiverful, but still it had its effect. The good which Mr. Harding intended did not fall to the ground. All the Barchester world, including the five old bedesmen, treated Mr. Quiverful with the more respect because Mr. Harding had thus walked in, arm in arm with him, on his first entrance to his duties.\n\nAnd here in their new abode we will leave Mr. and Mrs. Quiverful and their fourteen children. May they enjoy the good things which Providence has at length given to them!\n\n# CHAPTER LIII. Conclusion\n\nThe end of a novel, like the end of a children's dinner party, must be made up of sweetmeats and sugar-plums. There is now nothing else to be told but the gala doings of Mr. Arabin's marriage, nothing more to be described than the wedding-dresses, no further dialogue to be recorded than that which took place between the archdeacon, who married them, and Mr. Arabin and Eleanor, who were married.\n\n\"Wilt thou have this woman to thy wedded wife,\" and \"wilt thou have this man to thy wedded husband, to live together according to God's ordinance?\"\n\nMr. Arabin and Eleanor each answered, \"I will.\"\n\nWe have no doubt that they will keep their promises, the more especially as the Signora Neroni had left Barchester before the ceremony was performed.\n\nMrs. Bold had been somewhat more than two years a widow before she was married to her second husband, and little Johnny was then able with due assistance to walk on his own legs into the drawing-room to receive the salutations of the assembled guests. Mr. Harding gave away the bride, the archdeacon performed the service, and the two Miss Grantlys, who were joined in their labours by other young ladies of the neighbourhood, performed the duties of bridesmaids with equal diligence and grace. Mrs. Grantly superintended the breakfast and bouquets, and Mary Bold distributed the cards and cake. The archdeacon's three sons had also come home for the occasion. The elder was great with learning, being regarded by all who knew him as a certain future double first. The second, however, bore the palm on this occasion, being resplendent in a new uniform. The third was just entering the university, and was probably the proudest of the three.\n\nBut the most remarkable feature in the whole occasion was the excessive liberality of the archdeacon. He literally made presents to everybody. As Mr. Arabin had already moved out of the parsonage of St. Ewold's, that scheme of elongating the dining-room was of course abandoned; but he would have refurnished the whole deanery had he been allowed. He sent down a magnificent piano by Erard, gave Mr. Arabin a cob which any dean in the land might have been proud to bestride, and made a special present to Eleanor of a new pony chair that had gained a prize in the Exhibition. Nor did he even stay his hand here; he bought a set of cameos for his wife and a sapphire bracelet for Miss Bold; showered pearls and work-boxes on his daughters; and to each of his sons he presented a check for £20. On Mr. Harding he bestowed a magnificent violoncello with all the new-fashioned arrangements and expensive additions, which on account of these novelties that gentleman could never use with satisfaction to his audience or pleasure to himself.\n\nThose who knew the archdeacon well perfectly understood the causes of his extravagance. 'Twas thus that he sang his song of triumph over Mr. Slope. This was his pæan, his hymn of thanksgiving, his loud oration. He had girded himself with his sword and gone forth to the war; now he was returning from the field laden with the spoils of the foe. The cob and the cameos, the violoncello and the pianoforte, were all as it were trophies reft from the tent of his now-conquered enemy.\n\nThe Arabins after their marriage went abroad for a couple of months, according to the custom in such matters now duly established, and then commenced their deanery life under good auspices. And nothing can be more pleasant than the present arrangement of ecclesiastical affairs in Barchester. The titular bishop never interfered, and Mrs. Proudie not often. Her sphere is more extended, more noble, and more suited to her ambition than that of a cathedral city. As long as she can do what she pleases with the diocese, she is willing to leave the dean and chapter to themselves. Mr. Slope tried his hand at subverting the old-established customs of the close, and from his failure she had learnt experience. The burly chancellor and the meagre little prebendary are not teased by any application respecting Sabbath-day schools, the dean is left to his own dominions, and the intercourse between Mrs. Proudie and Mrs. Arabin is confined to a yearly dinner given by each to the other. At these dinners Dr. Grantly will not take a part, but he never fails to ask for and receive a full account of all that Mrs. Proudie either does or says.\n\nHis ecclesiastical authority has been greatly shorn since the palmy days in which he reigned supreme as mayor of the palace to his father, but nevertheless such authority as is now left to him he can enjoy without interference. He can walk down the High Street of Barchester without feeling that those who see him are comparing his claims with those of Mr. Slope. The intercourse between Plumstead and the deanery is of the most constant and familiar description. Since Eleanor has been married to a clergyman, and especially to a dignitary of the church, Mrs. Grantly has found many more points of sympathy with her sister; and on a coming occasion, which is much looked forward to by all parties, she intends to spend a month or two at the deanery. She never thought of spending a month in Barchester when little Johnny Bold was born!\n\nThe two sisters do not quite agree on matters of church doctrine, though their differences are of the most amicable description. Mrs. Arabin's church is two degrees higher than that of Mrs. Grantly. This may seem strange to those who will remember that Eleanor was once accused of partiality to Mr. Slope, but it is no less the fact. She likes her husband's silken vest, she likes his adherence to the rubric, she specially likes the eloquent philosophy of his sermons, and she likes the red letters in her own prayer-book. It must not be presumed that she has a taste for candles, or that she is at all astray about the real presence, but she has an inkling that way. She sent a handsome subscription towards certain very heavy ecclesiastical legal expenses which have lately been incurred in Bath, her name of course not appearing; she assumes a smile of gentle ridicule when the Archbishop of Canterbury is named; and she has put up a memorial window in the cathedral.\n\nMrs. Grantly, who belongs to the high and dry church, the High Church as it was some fifty years since, before tracts were written and young clergymen took upon themselves the highly meritorious duty of cleaning churches, rather laughs at her sister. She shrugs her shoulders and tells Miss Thorne that she supposes Eleanor will have an oratory in the deanery before she has done. But she is not on that account a whit displeased. A few High Church vagaries do not, she thinks, sit amiss on the shoulders of a young dean's wife. It shows at any rate that her heart is in the subject, and it shows moreover that she is removed, wide as the poles asunder, from that cesspool of abomination in which it was once suspected that she would wallow and grovel. Anathema maranatha! Let anything else be held as blessed, so that that be well cursed. Welcome kneelings and bowings, welcome matins and complines, welcome bell, book, and candle, so that Mr. Slope's dirty surplices and ceremonial Sabbaths be held in due execration!\n\nIf it be essentially and absolutely necessary to choose between the two, we are inclined to agree with Mrs. Grantly that the bell, book, and candle are the lesser evil of the two. Let it however be understood that no such necessity is admitted in these pages.\n\nDr. Arabin (we suppose he must have become a doctor when he became a dean) is more moderate and less outspoken on doctrinal points than his wife, as indeed in his station it behoves him to be. He is a studious, thoughtful, hard-working man. He lives constantly at the deanery and preaches nearly every Sunday. His time is spent in sifting and editing old ecclesiastical literature and in producing the same articles new. At Oxford he is generally regarded as the most promising clerical ornament of the age. He and his wife live together in perfect mutual confidence. There is but one secret in her bosom which he has not shared. He has never yet learned how Mr. Slope had his ears boxed.\n\nThe Stanhopes soon found that Mr. Slope's power need no longer operate to keep them from the delight of their Italian villa. Before Eleanor's marriage they had all migrated back to the shores of Como. They had not been resettled long before the signora received from Mrs. Arabin a very pretty though very short epistle, in which she was informed of the fate of the writer. This letter was answered by another--bright, charming, and witty, as the signora's letters always were--and so ended the friendship between Eleanor and the Stanhopes.\n\nOne word of Mr. Harding, and we have done. He is still precentor of Barchester and still pastor of the little church of St. Cuthbert's. In spite of what he has so often said himself, he is not even yet an old man. He does such duties as fall to his lot well and conscientiously, and is thankful that he has never been tempted to assume others for which he might be less fitted.\n\nThe author now leaves him in the hands of his readers: not as a hero, not as a man to be admired and talked of, not as a man who should be toasted at public dinners and spoken of with conventional absurdity as a perfect divine, but as a good man, without guile, believing humbly in the religion which he has striven to teach, and guided by the precepts which he has striven to learn.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-3409"]="# 제1장. 새 주교는 누가 될 것인가\n\n185X년 7월 하순, 대성당 도시 바체스터에서는 열흘 동안 시시각각 대단히 중대한 물음이 오갔고, 그 물음에는 매시간 갖가지 답이 나왔다 — 새 주교는 누가 될 것인가?\n\n오랜 세월 그 자리를 온화한 권위로 지켜 온 늙은 그랜틀리 박사의 죽음은, 하필 ---- 경의 내각이 ---- 경의 내각에 자리를 내주려던 바로 그때에 일어났다. 이 선량한 노인의 병환은 길고도 더디게 이어졌고, 마침내 관계된 이들에게는 새 임명이 보수당 정부의 손에서 이루어질 것인가 자유당 정부의 손에서 이루어질 것인가가 지극한 관심사가 되었다.\n\n물러나는 총리가 이미 사람을 골라 두었다는 것, 그리고 그 문제가 그의 손에 달려 있다면 주교의 관이 늙은 주교의 아들인 그랜틀리 부주교의 머리 위에 내려앉으리라는 것은 웬만큼 알려진 사실이었다. 부주교는 오래전부터 교구의 사무를 도맡아 왔고, 아버지가 세상을 뜨기 몇 달 전부터 소문은 아버지의 영예가 고스란히 그에게 물려지리라고 자신 있게 못 박고 있었다.\n\n그랜틀리 주교는 살아온 그대로, 평온하게, 천천히, 고통도 없고 동요도 없이 죽었다. 숨은 거의 알아채지 못할 만큼 조금씩 그에게서 빠져나갔고, 죽기 한 달 전부터는 그가 살아 있는지 죽었는지조차 물어야 할 지경이었다.\n\n부주교에게는 견디기 힘든 시기였다. 그때 주교좌를 나누어 줄 권한을 쥐고 있던 이들은 아버지의 교구를 그에게 물려줄 작정이었던 것이다. 나는 총리가 그랜틀리 박사에게 주교직을 말로 똑똑히 약속했다고 말하려는 것이 아니다. 그는 그러기에는 지나치게 조심스러운 사람이었다. 고양이를 죽이는 방법에 관한 속담이 하나 있거니와, 높든 낮든 관청의 사정을 조금이라도 아는 이라면 분명한 말 없이도 약속이 이루어질 수 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 입만 쳐다보고 있는 기대자를 더없이 고무된 상태로 올려놓는 데에는 그 위대한 인물이 “아무개 씨는 확실히 떠오르는 사람이지” 하고 속삭이는 것 이상이 필요치 않다는 것을 잘 알 것이다.\n\n그런 속삭임이 있었고, 그것을 들은 이들은 그 말이 바체스터 교구의 사목이 부주교의 손에서 빠져나가지 않으리라는 뜻임을 알았다. 당시의 총리는 옥스퍼드에서 절대적인 인물이었고, 얼마 전 라자러스 학장의 집에서 하룻밤을 묵은 일이 있었다. 그런데 라자러스의 학장은 — 덧붙여 두자면 라자러스 칼리지는 여러모로 옥스퍼드에서 가장 안락하고 또한 가장 부유한 칼리지다 — 부주교의 가장 가까운 벗이자 가장 미더운 조언자였다. 총리가 방문한 그 자리에 그랜틀리 박사도 물론 함께 있었고, 만남은 대단히 화기애애했다. 이튿날 아침 학장인 그윈 박사는 부주교에게 자기가 보기에 일은 이미 결정된 것이나 다름없다고 일러 주었다.\n\n이 무렵 주교는 완전히 마지막 고비에 있었다. 그러나 내각 또한 휘청거리고 있었다. 그랜틀리 박사는 옥스퍼드에서 흡족하고 들뜬 기분으로 돌아와 주교관에서 제자리를 되찾고, 아버지에게 아들로서의 마지막 도리를 이어서 다했는데, 공평하게 말하자면 그는 평소의 다소 세속적인 몸가짐에서 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 자상한 정성으로 그 일을 해냈다.\n\n한 달 전에 의사들은 죽어 가는 이의 몸 안에 숨이 붙어 있을 수 있는 기간을 길어야 4주라고 못 박았다. 그 한 달이 다 가자 의사들은 고개를 갸웃하며 다시 2주를 말했다. 노인은 포도주만으로 연명했으나 2주가 지나도 여전히 살아 있었고, 내각이 무너진다는 소식은 점점 잦아졌다. 런던의 두 명의(名醫) 램다 뮤뉴 경과 오미크론 파이 경이 이번으로 다섯 번째 내려와서는 학식 있는 고개를 저으며 일주일을 더 사는 것은 불가능하다고 선언했고, 주교관 식당에 점심을 들러 앉으면서는 내각이 닷새 안에 무너질 수밖에 없다는 자기들만의 정보를 부주교에게 귀띔했다. 아들은 아버지의 방으로 돌아가 제 손으로 기운을 돋우는 마데이라 포도주를 조금 떠 넣어 드린 뒤, 침대 곁에 앉아 자기 앞의 승산을 헤아려 보았다.\n\n내각은 닷새 안에 물러나게 된다. 아버지는 그 안에 돌아가시게 — 아니, 그는 그런 식으로 헤아리는 것을 물리쳤다. 내각은 물러나게 되고, 교구도 아마 같은 시기에 비게 될 것이다. 권력을 이어받을 사람들의 이름은 아직 매우 불확실했고, 내각이 꾸려지기까지는 일주일이 걸릴 터였다. 그 일주일 동안 물러나는 이들이 빈자리를 채우지 않겠는가? 그랜틀리 박사는 그렇게 되리라는 짐작이 어렴풋이 있었으나 확실히는 알지 못했고, 그러다 그런 문제에 자기가 이토록 무지하다는 데에 스스로 놀랐다.\n\n그는 그 생각에서 마음을 떼어 놓으려 애썼으나 그럴 수 없었다. 경주가 너무나 아슬아슬했고, 걸린 것이 너무나 컸다. 그러다 그는 죽어 가는 이의 무표정하고 평온한 얼굴을 바라보았다. 거기에는 죽음이나 병의 기색이 없었다. 예전보다 조금 여위고 얼마간 잿빛이 돌며 나이의 깊은 주름이 더 뚜렷하기는 했으나, 그가 보기에는 앞으로도 몇 주는 더 목숨이 걸려 있을 법했다. 램다 뮤뉴 경과 오미크론 파이 경은 이미 세 번 틀렸으니 앞으로 세 번 더 틀릴 수도 있었다. 늙은 주교는 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 스무 시간을 잠으로 보냈지만, 잠깐씩 깨어 있는 동안에는 아들도, 부주교의 장인인 오랜 벗 하딩 씨도 알아보았고 그 보살핌과 사랑에 다정히 고마움을 표하곤 했다. 지금 그는 갓난아기처럼 잠들어 편안히 반듯이 누워 있었고, 입은 조금 벌어졌으며, 몇 올 남지 않은 흰머리가 두건 밑으로 삐져나와 있었다. 숨소리는 조금도 나지 않았고, 이불 위에 얹힌 여위고 창백한 손은 미동도 없었다. 이 세상에서 저 세상으로 건너가는 노인의 걸음보다 더 수월한 것은 없을 듯했다.\n\n그러나 거기 앉아 지켜보는 이의 심사는 결코 수월하지 않았다. 그는 지금이 아니면 영영 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 이미 쉰이 넘었고, 이제 자리에서 물러나는 그의 벗들이 머지않아 되돌아올 가망은 별로 없었다. 지금 자리에 있는, 그리고 곧 물러날 그 사람 말고는, 앞으로 영국 총리가 될 만한 어느 누구도 그랜틀리 박사를 주교로 삼을 생각은 하지 않을 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 깊은 침묵 속에서 오래도록 서글피 생각에 잠겼다가, 아직 살아 있는 그 얼굴을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 마침내 스스로에게 감히 물었다 — 나는 정말로 아버지의 죽음을 바라고 있는가.\n\n그 물음을 던진 것은 이로운 일이었고, 답은 순식간에 나왔다. 오만하고 욕심 많고 세속적인 그 사람은 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 주교의 손을 제 손에 감싸 쥔 채, 자기 죄를 용서해 달라고 간절히 기도했다.\n\n침실 문이 소리 없이 열리고 하딩 씨가 벨벳 같은 걸음으로 들어섰을 때에도 그의 얼굴은 여전히 이불에 묻혀 있었다. 그 침대 곁을 지키는 일에서 하딩 씨의 발길은 부주교의 그것에 못지않게 잦았고, 그가 드나드는 것은 사위가 드나드는 것만큼이나 당연한 일이었다. 그는 부주교가 알아채기 전에 이미 그 곁에 바싹 다가와 서 있었고, 자기까지 무릎을 꿇으면 부주교가 화들짝 놀라 죽어 가는 이를 깨울까 두렵지만 않았다면 그 또한 무릎을 꿇고 기도했을 것이다. 그러나 그랜틀리 박사는 곧바로 그를 알아보고 무릎에서 일어났다. 그가 일어서자 하딩 씨는 그의 두 손을 잡고 따뜻하게 힘주어 쥐었다. 그 순간 두 사람 사이에는 일찍이 없던 정이 오갔고, 그 뒤에 벌어진 사정들이 마침 그 감정을 크게 지켜 주었다. 그렇게 서로의 손을 맞잡고 서 있는 동안 두 사람의 뺨으로는 눈물이 하염없이 흘러내렸다.\n\n“하느님이 너희를 축복하시기를, 얘들아.” 주교가 잠에서 깨어 힘없는 목소리로 말했다. “하느님이 너희를 축복하시기를 — 하느님이 너희 둘 다 축복하시기를, 사랑하는 내 아이들아.” 그리고 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었다.\n\n목에서 요란하게 가래 끓는 소리도 없었고, 끔찍한 몸부림도 없었으며, 눈에 띄는 죽음의 표시도 없었다. 다만 아래턱이 제자리에서 조금 처졌고, 그토록 줄곧 잠에 감겨 있던 두 눈이 이제 뜬 채로 고정되었다. 하딩 씨도 그랜틀리 박사도 목숨이 떠났음을 알지 못했으나, 둘 다 그런 것이 아닐까 짐작은 하고 있었다.\n\n“다 끝난 것 같군.” 하딩 씨가 여전히 상대의 두 손을 쥔 채 말했다. “그런 듯싶네 — 아니, 그러기를 바라네.”\n\n“벨을 울리겠습니다.” 상대가 거의 속삭임에 가까운 소리로 말했다. “필립스 부인이 와 있어야지요.”\n\n간병인 필립스 부인이 곧 방으로 들어와 곧바로 익숙한 손길로 그 뜬 눈을 감겨 주었다.\n\n“다 끝난 건가요, 필립스 부인?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“주교님께서는 이제 안 계십니다.” 필립스 부인이 몸을 돌려 엄숙한 얼굴로 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 말했다. “각하께서는 제가 여태 뵌 어느 분보다도 잠든 애기처럼 가셨습니다요.”\n\n“큰 다행일세, 부주교.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “참으로 큰 다행이야 — 사랑스럽고 선량하고 더없이 훌륭하신 노인이셨네. 아, 우리의 마지막 순간도 저분처럼 티 없고 평온하기를!”\n\n“그럼요.” 필립스 부인이 말했다. “주님의 온갖 자비를 찬양할지어다. 하오나 온유하고 부드럽고 말씨 고운 그리스도인으로 말하자면 각하께서야말로—” 그러고는 필립스 부인이 꾸밈없으면서도 수월한 슬픔에 젖어 흰 앞치마를 들어 흘러넘치는 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“끝났으니 기뻐하지 않을 수 없지.” 하딩 씨가 여전히 벗을 위로하며 말했다. 그러나 부주교의 마음은 벌써 임종의 방을 떠나 총리의 집무실에 가 있었다. 그는 아버지의 목숨을 위해 기도하기까지 했으나, 이제 그 목숨이 다한 마당에는 일 분 일 초가 아까웠다. 주교가 죽었다는 사실을 두고 우물쭈물하는 것은 이제 소용없는 짓이었다 — 어리석은 감상을 꾸며 보이느라 어쩌면 모든 것을 잃는 것도 소용없는 짓이었다.\n\n그러나 장인이 저기 서서 제 손을 잡고 있는데 어떻게 처신해야 한단 말인가? 어떻게 하면 매정해 보이지 않으면서 아버지를 잊고 주교직만 생각할 수 있단 말인가 — 잃은 것은 못 본 체하고 어쩌면 얻을 수 있는 것만 헤아릴 수 있단 말인가?\n\n“예, 그렇지 않을 수야 없지요.” 마침내 그가 하딩 씨의 말에 답하여 말했다. “우리 모두가 오래도록 예상해 온 일이니까요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 그의 팔을 잡고 방 밖으로 이끌었다. “내일 아침에 다시 뵙세.” 그가 말했다. “이제 방은 여자들에게 맡기는 편이 좋겠네.” 그러고서 두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n이미 저녁이었고 거의 어두워져 있었다. 그날 밤 안으로 총리가 그 교구가 비었음을 아는 것이 무엇보다 중요했다. 모든 것이 거기에 달려 있을지도 몰랐다. 그리하여 부주교는 하딩 씨의 거듭되는 위로에 답하는 대신, 지금 곧 런던으로 전보를 쳐야겠다고 말을 꺼냈다. 그랜틀리 박사가 자기가 보기에 저토록 마음 아파하는 것에 실은 적잖이 놀라고 있던 하딩 씨는 다소 당황했으나 이의를 달지는 않았다. 그는 부주교가 아버지의 자리를 잇기를 얼마간 바라고 있다는 것은 알고 있었으나, 그 바람이 얼마나 높이 부풀어 있는지는 전혀 알지 못했다.\n\n“그렇지요.” 그랜틀리 박사가 정신을 가다듬고 나약함을 떨쳐 내며 말했다. “지금 당장 소식을 보내야 합니다. 늦어져서 무슨 결과가 벌어질지 모르는 일이니까요. 좀 해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“내가! 아, 그럼세. 물론이지. 무엇이든 하겠네만, 자네가 원하는 게 정확히 무엇인지를 내가 모르겠구먼.”\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 필기용 탁자 앞에 앉아 펜과 잉크를 들고 종잇조각에 이렇게 썼다.\n\n전신으로. ---- 백작 앞, 다우닝가 또는 그 밖의 어디든. 바체스터 주교가 별세했음. 이 소식은 셉티머스 하딩 목사가 보냄.\n\n“자.” 그가 말했다. “이걸 철도역 전신국에 가져가서 그대로 접수시키십시오. 아마 자기들 용지에 옮겨 적으라고 할 겁니다. 하실 일은 그것뿐이고요. 그런 다음 반 크라운을 내시면 됩니다.” 그러고서 부주교는 주머니에 손을 넣어 필요한 돈을 꺼냈다.\n\n하딩 씨는 자기가 꼭 심부름꾼 아이가 된 것 같았고, 게다가 하필 이런 온당치 못한 때에 그 노릇을 하라는 부름을 받았다는 느낌도 들었으나, 아무 말도 하지 않고 종잇조각과 내미는 동전을 받아 들었다.\n\n“한데 부주교, 자네가 이 전보에다 내 이름을 적어 넣어 놓았군그래.”\n\n“그럼요.” 상대가 말했다. “아시다시피 성직자의 이름이 들어가야 하는데, 그토록 오랜 벗이신 장인어른의 이름보다 더 어울리는 이름이 어디 있겠습니까? 백작은 이름 따위 보지도 않을 겁니다, 그건 틀림없고요. 그러니 하딩 씨, 부디 잠시도 지체하지 마십시오.”\n\n하딩 씨는 역으로 가는 길에 서재 문께까지 갔다가, 가엾은 주교의 침실에 들어설 때 자기가 잔뜩 품고 있던 소식을 문득 떠올렸다. 그 순간이 세상일을 전하기에는 너무나 마땅치 않았던 터라 그는 혀끝까지 나온 말을 삼켰고, 그 직후에 벌어진 광경에 그 일에 대한 기억은 한동안 통째로 날아가 버렸던 것이다.\n\n“한데 부주교.” 그가 되돌아서며 말했다. “자네에게 말한다는 걸 잊었네 — 내각이 물러났다는군.”\n\n“물러나요!” 부주교가 소리쳤는데, 그 순간의 사정을 생각해 스스로를 다잡으려 애썼는데도 그 목소리에는 그의 불안과 낭패가 너무나 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다. “물러났다고요! 누가 그럽디까?”\n\n하딩 씨는 그런 취지의 소식이 전신으로 내려왔으며 그 기별을 채드윅 씨가 주교관 문간에 두고 갔다고 설명했다.\n\n부주교는 한동안 말없이 앉아 골똘히 생각했고, 하딩 씨는 선 채로 그를 바라보았다. “상관없습니다.” 마침내 부주교가 말했다. “그래도 전보는 그대로 보내십시오. 소식은 누군가에게는 알려야 하는데, 지금으로서는 그것을 받을 처지에 있는 사람이 달리 없으니까요. 지금 곧 해 주십시오, 장인어른. 제가 몸소 할 수 있는 형편이라면 이런 수고를 끼치지 않을 것을 아시지 않습니까. 몇 분이라도 더없이 소중합니다.”\n\n하딩 씨는 나가서 전보를 보냈는데, 우리도 그 전보를 따라 목적지까지 가 보는 편이 좋겠다. 그것은 바체스터를 떠난 지 30분도 되지 않아 안쪽 서재에 있던 ---- 백작에게 닿았다. 그런 순간에 그가 거기서 얼마나 공들인 편지를, 얼마나 유창한 호소를, 얼마나 분개에 찬 항의를 지어내야 했을지는 짐작할 수는 있어도 이루 다 적을 수는 없다! 그가 어떻게 석탄 난롯불에 등을 대고 영국 귀족답게 서서 바지 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 승리한 경쟁자들에게 퍼부을 벼락을 벼르고 있었는지 — 그의 고운 눈이 어떻게 분노로 타올랐고 이마가 어떻게 애국심으로 번쩍였는지 — 굼뜬 동료들을 생각하며 어떻게 발을 굴렀는지 — 그중 하나가 지나치게 영리했다는 것을 떠올리며 어떻게 하마터면 욕설을 내뱉을 뻔했는지는, 상상력 좋은 독자들이 알아서 그려 볼 일이다. 그런데 그가 정말 그러고 있었던가? 아니다. 역사와 진실이 나로 하여금 그것을 부인하게 만든다. 그는 안락의자에 편히 앉아 뉴마켓 경마표를 훑어보고 있었고, 팔꿈치 옆 탁자에는 아직 페이지도 자르지 않은 프랑스 소설이 펼쳐진 채 놓여 있어 그가 그것에 붙들려 있던 참이었다.\n\n그는 전보가 든 봉투를 열어 그것을 읽고 나서 펜을 들어 뒷면에 이렇게 썼다.\n\n---- 백작 앞. ---- 백작의 인사를 전함.\n\n그러고는 그것을 다시 길 위로 띄워 보냈다.\n\n우리의 불운한 벗이 주교직의 영광을 손에 넣을 가망은 이렇게 끝이 났다.\n\n신문마다 주교로 뽑힐 사람이라며 여러 성직자의 이름을 올렸다. 『브리티시 그랜드머더』는 물러나는 내각에 대한 예우로 그윈 박사가 그 사람이 되리라고 단언했다. 이는 그랜틀리 박사에게 큰 타격이었으나, 그는 제 벗에게 자리를 빼앗기는 꼴을 볼 운명은 아니었다. 『앵글리컨 디보티』는 엄격한 교리로 이름난 런던의 어느 위대한 설교자의 자격을 자신 있게 내세웠다. 그리고 관가의 사정에 밝다고 여겨지는 석간 『이스턴 헤미스피어』는 어느 저명한 박물학자를 지지한다고 선언했는데, 그는 암석과 광물에 관한 지식에 더없이 통달한 신사였으나 종교 문제에 관해서는 아무런 특별한 교리도 지니고 있지 않다고 많은 이가 짐작하는 인물이었다. 우리 모두가 알다시피 모든 주제에 관하여 결코 틀리는 법이 없는 정확한 정보의 유일한 참된 원천인 일간지 『주피터』는 한동안 잠자코 있다가 마침내 입을 열었다. 이 모든 후보의 자격이 논의되었고 다소 불경스럽게 처리되었으며, 그런 다음 『주피터』는 프라우디 박사가 그 사람이라고 선언했다.\n\n프라우디 박사가 바로 그 사람이었다. 선대 주교가 세상을 뜬 지 꼭 한 달 만에 프라우디 박사는 그 후임자로 뽑혀 여왕의 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n플럼스테드 에피스코피의 목사관 서재에 침울하고 서글픈 마음으로 앉아 있는 부주교의 슬픔에 대해서는, 우리에게 장막을 드리우도록 허락해 주기를 청해야겠다. 전보를 보낸 이튿날 그는 ---- 백작이 내각 구성을 맡기로 승낙했다는 소식을 들었고, 그 순간부터 자기 기회가 끝났음을 알았다. 많은 이는 그가 주교의 권세를 놓친 것을 슬퍼했다는 점에서, 그것을 탐냈다는 점에서, 아니 그때 그런 식으로 그것을 생각했다는 점 자체에서 사악했다고 여길 것이다.\n\n그런 비난에 내가 전적으로 동의한다고는 말하지 못하겠다. nolo episcopari 는 아직도 쓰이고는 있으나 모든 인간적 소망의 흐름과 너무나 정면으로 어긋나는 것이어서, 잉글랜드 교회에서 떠오르는 사제들의 참된 열망을 나타낸다고는 볼 수 없다. 법률가가 판사가 되기를 구하는 것은, 또 온갖 정직한 방법으로 그 소망을 이루려 하는 것은 죄가 아니다. 젊은 외교관이 일류 대사관의 주인이 되기를 내다본다면 그것은 온당한 야심이며, 가난한 소설가가 디킨스와 겨루어 보려 하거나 피츠제임스를 뛰어넘으려 한다 해도 어리석을지언정 잘못은 아니다. 시드니 스미스는 이 타락한 시대에 부목사의 사제복 아래에서 성 바울의 위엄을 찾기를 기대할 수는 없다고 참으로 옳게 말했다. 우리가 성직자에게 사람 이상이기를 바란다면, 우리는 아마 그들이 사람 이하라고 여기도록 스스로를 가르치게 될 것이며, 사람으로서 품을 열망의 권리를 목자에게서 빼앗는 방식으로 목자의 품격을 높이기를 기대하기는 어렵다.\n\n우리의 부주교는 세속적이었다 — 우리 가운데 그렇지 않은 이가 누구인가? 그는 야심가였다 — 우리 가운데 “고귀한 정신들의 마지막 약점”을 제 것이라 인정하기를 부끄러워할 이가 누구인가? 그는 탐욕스러웠다고 독자들은 말하리라. 아니다. 그가 바체스터의 주교가 되기를 바란 것은 결코 돈을 사랑해서가 아니었다. 그는 아버지의 외아들이었고 아버지는 그에게 큰 재산을 남겼다. 그가 받은 성직록은 연간 3,000파운드 가까이를 그에게 안겨 주었다. 주교직은 교회 재산 위원회의 삭감을 거쳐 겨우 5,000파운드였다. 그는 주교가 되는 것보다 부주교로 있는 편이 더 부유한 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 분명 제1바이올린을 켜기를 바랐고, 넓은 아마포 소매를 늘어뜨리고 왕국의 귀족들 사이에 앉기를 바랐으며, 굳이 진실을 밝혀야 한다면 동료 성직자들에게서 “각하”라 불리기를 바랐다.\n\n그러나 그의 희망은 그것이 죄 없는 것이었든 죄 많은 것이었든 이루어질 운명이 아니었고, 프라우디 박사가 바체스터의 주교로 축성되었다.\n\n# 제2장. 의회 법령에 따른 히램 요양원\n\n이 이야기가 시작되는 시점까지의 하딩 씨 이력을 여기서 세상에 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 거의 없을 것이다. 그 예민한 신사가 바체스터 시 히램 요양원의 워든으로서 받던 수입 때문에 『주피터』 지면에서 공격을 받고 얼마나 괴로워했는지를 세상이 잊었을 리 없다. 또한 그 자선 재산 문제로 존 볼드 씨가 그를 상대로 소송을 제기했던 일, 그리고 그 볼드 씨가 훗날 하딩 씨의 막내딸이자 당시 유일하게 미혼이던 딸과 결혼했다는 사실도 아직 잊히지 않았을 것이다. 이러한 공격에 눌린 나머지 하딩 씨는 워든 직을 사임했는데, 벗들도 변호사들도 그러지 말라고 한사코 만류하던 터였다. 그러나 그는 기어이 사임했고, 시내에 있는 자그마한 세인트 커스버트 교구의 사제로서 그 직무에 사내답게 몸을 던졌으며, 아울러 대성당 선창자의 직무도 계속 수행했다. 선창자 자리는 보수가 적어, 지금까지는 으레 앞서 말한 요양원의 워든 직에 딸려 오는 것으로 여겨져 왔다.\n\n그토록 무참하게 쫓겨나다시피 한 요양원을 떠나 바체스터의 하이 스트리트에 제 분수대로 소박하게 자리를 잡았을 때, 그는 자신이 그러려는 것보다 남들이 더 야단스럽게 굴리라고는 예상하지 않았다. 그의 바람이라야 기껏 이번 조치가 『주피터』에 더 이상의 기사가 실리지 않도록 제때 이루어졌기를 바라는 정도였다. 그러나 그의 일은 그렇게 조용히 가라앉도록 내버려 두어지지 않았고, 사람들은 전에 그의 탐욕을 나무라고 싶어 하던 것 못지않게 이제는 그가 치른 사심 없는 희생을 두고 입에 올리고 싶어 했다.\n\n가장 놀라운 일은 캔터베리 대주교에게서 친필 편지를 받은 것이었다. 그 편지에서 수좌 대주교는 그의 처신을 매우 따뜻하게 칭찬하고 앞으로의 뜻이 무엇인지 알려 달라고 청했다. 하딩 씨는 바체스터의 세인트 커스버트 교구 목사로 지낼 생각이라고 답했고, 그것으로 그 일은 끝났다. 이어 신문들이 그의 사건을 다루었는데 『주피터』도 그 축에 들었으며, 온 나라의 열람실마다 그의 이름을 찬사에 실어 날랐다. 또한 그가 저 위대한 음악 저작 『하딩의 교회 음악』의 저자라는 사실도 밝혀졌다. 개정판 이야기가 오갔으나 내가 아는 한 인쇄된 적은 없다. 다만 그 저작이 세인트 제임스의 왕실 예배당에 도입된 것은 분명하며, 『음악 심사관』에 긴 평이 실려, 이런 종류의 저작 가운데 그토록 깊은 연구와 그토록 드높은 음악적 역량이 결합된 예는 일찍이 없었다고 단언하고, 앞으로 하딩이라는 이름은 예술이 가꾸어지는 곳이면, 또 종교가 소중히 여겨지는 곳이면 어디서나 알려지리라고 주장한 것도 분명하다.\n\n이는 대단한 찬사였고, 하딩 씨가 그러한 칭송에 흡족해했다는 것을 나는 부인하지 않겠다. 하딩 씨가 어떤 일에서든 허영을 부린다면 그것은 음악에 관한 것이었으니까. 그러나 일은 거기서 멈추었다. 개정판은 설령 인쇄되었다 한들 팔린 적이 없었고, 왕실 예배당에 들어갔던 책들은 다시 자취를 감추어 비슷한 부류의 서적 더미와 함께 평화롭게 처박혔다. 『주피터』의 타워스 씨와 그 동료들은 다른 이름들에 매달렸고, 우리 벗에게 약속된 불멸의 명성은 아무래도 사후에나 오도록 예정된 것이 분명했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 많은 시간을 벗인 주교와 함께 보냈고, 또 많은 시간을 이제는 아아, 미망인이 된 딸 볼드 부인과 함께 보냈다. 그리고 거의 날마다 예전 자기 관하에 있던 이들의 가엾은 잔존자들, 곧 히램 요양원에 아직 남아 있는 몇 안 되는 구호 노인들을 찾아갔다. 그들 가운데 여섯이 아직 살아 있었다. 늙은 히램의 유언에 따르면 그 수는 언제나 12명이어야 했다. 그러나 워든이 물러난 뒤로 주교는 후임을 임명하지 않았고 자선의 새 수혜자도 지명되지 않았으니, 당국이 요양원을 다시 굴러가게 할 무슨 조치를 취하지 않는 한 바체스터의 요양원은 그대로 폐지 상태에 빠질 듯이 보였다.\n\n지난 5년 동안 당국이 바체스터 요양원을 못 본 체한 것은 아니어서, 여러 정치의 명의들이 이 문제를 손에 쥐어 보았다. 하딩 씨가 사임한 직후 『주피터』는 무엇을 해야 하는지를 아주 명료하게 밝혀 놓았다. 반 단 남짓한 지면에서 그 신문은 수입을 배분하고, 건물을 다시 짓고, 온갖 다툼을 끝내고, 훈훈한 정을 되살리고, 하딩 씨의 앞길을 마련해 주었으며, 이 모든 일을 바체스터 시와 바체스터 주교에게도, 나아가 온 나라에도 흡족할 수밖에 없는 기반 위에 올려놓았다. 이 방안이 얼마나 현명한지는 「상식」과 「베리타스」와 「공정한 겨룸을 사랑하는 사람」이 『주피터』에 보낸 편지의 수효로 입증되었으니, 하나같이 감탄을 표하며 제시된 세목을 부연하는 내용이었다. 반대하는 편지는 단 한 통도 실리지 않았다는 것은 참으로 기이한 일이며, 따라서 물론 그런 편지는 쓰인 적도 없었다.\n\n그러나 카산드라의 말은 믿기지 않는 법이고, 『주피터』의 지혜조차 때로는 귀먹은 자들에게 떨어진다. 『주피터』의 지면에 다른 방안이 나서지는 않았지만, 교회 자선 사업의 개혁가들은 히램 요양원을 다시 일으켜 세울 저마다의 비방을 여기저기서 알리는 데 게으르지 않았다. 어느 학식 있는 주교는 상원에서 기회를 잡아 이 문제를 언급하며, 바체스터의 존경하는 형제 주교와 이 사안을 두고 서신을 주고받았음을 내비쳤다. 스테일리브리지의 급진파 의원은 그 기금을 나라의 가난한 농민 교육에 전용해야 한다고 제안하고는, 문제의 그 농민들의 미신과 습속에 관한 일화 몇 가지로 의사당을 즐겁게 했다. 어느 정치 소책자 저자는 「존 히램의 상속인은 누구인가?」라는 제목으로 수십 쪽을 지어내어 그와 같은 모든 시설의 운영에 관한 어김없는 원칙을 제시하려 했다. 그리고 마침내 정부의 한 각료가 다음 회기에 바체스터와 그 비슷한 사안들을 규율할 짧은 법안을 제출하겠다고 약속했다.\n\n다음 회기가 왔고, 관례와는 달리 법안도 함께 왔다. 그때 사람들의 마음은 다른 데 쏠려 있었다. 거대한 전쟁의 첫 위협이 나라 위에 무겁게 드리워 있었고, 히램의 상속인 문제는 의사당 안에서나 밖에서나 그리 많은 이의 관심을 끄는 것 같지 않았다. 그렇지만 법안은 낭독되고 또 낭독되어, 이렇다 하게 눈에 띄는 일도 없이 이의도 반대도 없이 열한 단계를 거쳐 통과되었다. 신사 마흔다섯 명쯤이 나서서 제 유언의 취지를 통째로 바꾸는 법을 만들되 정작 만드는 그 순간에는 자기들이 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 조금도 알지 못하리라는 것을 존 히램이 미리 내다볼 수 있었다면, 그는 이 일을 두고 무어라 했을까? 그래도 내무부 차관만은 알고 있었기를 바랄 일이다. 이 사안은 그에게 맡겨져 있었으니까.\n\n어쨌든 법안은 통과되었고, 이 이야기가 시작된다고 하는 시점에는 다음과 같이 정해져 있었다. 바체스터 요양원에는 전과 같이 노인 12명을 두되 각자 하루 1실링 4펜스를 받고, 아울러 새로 지을 건물에 노파 12명을 두되 각자 하루 1실링 2펜스를 받으며, 사택과 연 70파운드를 받는 여사감을 두고, 연 150파운드를 받는 재산 관리인을 두며, 끝으로 연 450파운드를 받는 워든을 두어 두 시설 모두의 영적 지도와, 남자 쪽에 딸린 시설의 세속적 관리를 맡게 한다는 것이었다. 주교와 수석 사제와 워든은 예전처럼 번갈아 가며 자선의 수혜자를 지명하고, 직원은 주교가 임명하도록 되어 있었다. 워든 직을 대성당 선창자가 맡는다는 말은 한마디도 없었고, 하딩 씨가 그 자리에 대해 권리를 지닌다는 말도 한 자 없었다.\n\n그러나 개혁이 곧 시행되리라는 통지가 나온 것은 늙은 주교가 세상을 떠나고 여러 달이 지난 뒤, 그 후임자가 취임한 바로 다음의 일이었다. 새 법과 새 주교는 새 내각의, 아니 그보다는 한동안 정적들에게 자리를 내주었다가 다시 권좌에 돌아온 내각의 가장 이른 작품 축에 들었다. 그리고 그랜틀리 박사의 죽음은 앞서 보았듯이 바로 그 정권 교체의 시점에 일어났다.\n\n가엾은 엘리너 볼드! 저 미망인의 모자가 그이에게 얼마나 잘 어울리는지, 또 새로 맡은 소임에 몸을 바치는 그 엄숙한 진지함이 얼마나 잘 어울리는지. 가엾은 엘리너!\n\n가엾은 엘리너! 존 볼드가 나에게 언제고 마음에 드는 인물이었다고는 말할 수 없다. 나는 그가 제가 얻은 아내에 값하는 사람이라고 생각한 적이 없다. 그러나 그녀가 보기에 그는 더없이 값진 사람이었다. 그녀의 마음은 남편에게 매달리는 저 여성적인 마음 가운데 하나였으되 우상 숭배로 매달리는 것은 아니었으니, 숭배란 제 우상에게 어떤 흠도 용납하지 못하는 법이거니와, 그녀는 담쟁이의 저 완전한 끈질김으로 매달렸다. 기생하는 덩굴이 제가 껴안은 줄기의 뒤틀린 데까지 그대로 따라가듯, 엘리너도 남편의 결점 바로 그것에 매달리고 그것을 사랑했다. 그녀는 언젠가 아버지가 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 제 눈에는 옳으리라고 단언한 적이 있다. 그 뒤 그녀는 충성의 대상을 옮겨, 제 주인이자 지아비의 가장 못난 결함까지도 언제든 감싸고 나설 태세가 되었다.\n\n그리고 존 볼드는 여자에게 사랑받을 만한 남자였다. 그 자신이 다정했고, 사람을 믿었고, 사내다웠다. 그리고 일류의 능력이 뒷받침해 주지도 못하는 그 생각의 오만함, 이웃보다 더 나은 사람이 되어 보려는 그 안간힘, 아는 이들의 심사를 그토록 아프게 긁던 그것도 아내의 평가에는 아무런 해가 되지 않았다.\n\n설령 그녀가 그에게 결점이 있었음을 인정할 수 있었다 한들, 그의 이른 죽음이 그 기억을 지워 버렸을 것이다. 그녀는 죽어야 할 여인에게 주어진 적 있는 가장 완전한 보물을 잃기라도 한 듯이 울었다. 그가 떠난 뒤 몇 주 동안은 이 세상에서 앞으로 행복하리라는 생각 자체가 그녀에게 가증스러웠고, 이른바 위로라는 것은 견딜 수 없었으며, 눈물과 잠만이 유일한 위안이었다.\n\n그러나 하느님은 털 깎인 어린양에게 바람을 알맞게 하신다. 그녀는 제 안에 또 다른 근심의 살아 있는 원천을 품고 있음을 알았다. 하느님이 자비로이 허락하시는 대로 기쁨이 되든 화가 되든, 이루 말할 수 없는 환희가 되든 절망의 슬픔이 되든, 저를 위하여 또 하나의 대상이 지어지리라는 것을 알았다. 처음에는 그것이 슬픔을 더하기만 했다! 태어나기도 전에 아비를 잃은 가엾은 아기의 어미가 되어, 언제나 황량할 화롯가의 슬픔 속으로 그 아이를 낳아 놓고, 눈물과 통곡 가운데 길러, 그러고는 아비의 보살핌도 없이 세상에 떠밀어 보내야 하다니! 여기에 처음에는 아무런 기쁨도 없었다.\n\n그러나 차츰 그녀의 마음은 또 다른 대상을 향해 조바심을 내게 되었고, 태어나기도 전에 그 낯선 이는 애타는 어미의 온 갈망 속에 기다려졌다. 아버지가 죽은 지 꼭 여덟 달 만에 두 번째 존 볼드가 태어났다. 그리고 한 피조물이 다른 피조물을 숭배하는 일이 죄 없을 수 있다면, 아비 없는 아기의 요람 위에 바쳐진 그 경배만은 죄로 헤아려지지 않기를 바라기로 하자.\n\n그 아이의 성품을 규정하거나, 아비의 결점이 그 작은 가슴 안에서 어미의 미덕으로 얼마나 씻겼는지를 짚어 보는 일은 우리가 애쓸 값어치가 없을 것이다. 아기는 아기로서 더없이 사랑스러웠고, 그가 훗날 어떻게 살았는지의 사실들을 우리가 캐 볼 필요가 생기리라고는 나로서는 내다보이지 않는다. 바체스터에서 지금 우리가 볼 일은 길어 보아야 한두 해를 넘기지 않을 터이니, 어린 존 볼드의 전기는 굳이 필요하다면 다른 이의 붓에 맡겨 두겠다.\n\n그러나 아기로서 이 아기는 더 바랄 나위가 없었다. 이 사실만은 아무도 부인하려 들지 않았다. “정말 사랑스럽지 않아요?” 그녀는 무릎을 꿇은 채 아버지의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그렇게 말하곤 했다. 윤기 도는 두 눈에는 부드러운 눈물이 그렁그렁했고, 앳된 얼굴은 몸에 꼭 맞는 미망인의 모자에 둘러싸여 있었으며, 두 손은 제 보물이 잠들어 있는 요람 양쪽에 얹혀 있었다. 외할아버지는 그 보물이 참으로 사랑스럽다고 기꺼이 인정했고, 이모부인 부주교조차 동의했으며, 엘리너의 언니인 그랜틀리 부인은 참으로 자매다운 열의로 그 말을 되받아 울렸다. 그리고 메리 볼드는 — 아니, 메리 볼드는 같은 제단 앞의 두 번째 숭배자였다.\n\n아기는 정말로 사랑스러웠다. 젖을 힘차게 먹었고, 다리를 벗겨 놓기만 하면 발가락을 신나게 뻗어 댔으며, 경기를 일으키지 않았다. 이것들이 아기의 완전함을 이루는 가장 중요한 대목으로 여겨지는 터인데, 우리 아기는 그 모두에서 남달랐다.\n\n그리하여 미망인의 깊은 슬픔은 누그러졌고, 죽음 말고는 아무것도 아물게 하지 못하리라 여겼던 상처에 달콤한 향유가 부어졌다. 하느님은 우리가 우리 자신에게 기꺼이 베풀려는 것보다 얼마나 더 자애로우신가! 사랑하는 얼굴을 하나씩 잃을 때마다, 몹시 아끼던 이가 하나씩 마지막으로 떠날 때마다, 우리는 모두 스스로에게 영원한 슬픔을 선고하고 그침 없이 솟는 눈물의 샘 속에 저를 소진시키려 든다. 그러나 그런 슬픔이 오래가는 일이 얼마나 드문가! 그것이 오래가지 못하도록 막아 주는 선하심이 얼마나 복된가! “살아 있는 벗은 늘 기억하되, 죽은 벗은 죽는 즉시 잊게 하소서”라는 것이 하느님의 자비를 깨달은 어느 지혜로운 이의 기도였다. 그런 소원을 입 밖에 낼 용기를 지닌 사람은 아마 드물겠으나, 그리한다 한들 그것은 자애로운 창조주께서 거의 언제나 우리에게 베풀어 주시는 슬픔으로부터의 놓임을 구하는 것일 따름이다.\n\n그렇다고 볼드 부인이 남편을 잊었다고 여겨지기를 나는 바라지 않는다. 그녀는 날마다 아내다운 온 사랑으로 그를 생각했고, 그 기억을 제 마음 가장 깊은 한복판에 모셔 두었다. 그러면서도 아기가 있어 행복했다. 살아 있는 그 장난감을 가슴에 꼭 품고, 저에게 모든 것을 빚졌고 앞으로도 빚질 한 인간이 존재한다고 느끼는 일은 참으로 달콤했다. 그 아이의 날마다의 양식은 제 몸에서 나왔고, 그 작은 바람들은 죄다 제 손으로 채워 줄 수 있었으며, 그 작은 마음이 맨 처음 사랑하게 될 사람은 저이고 오직 저뿐일 터였고, 그 젖먹이의 혀가 맨 처음 애써 내뱉을 말은 여자가 들을 수 있는 가장 달콤한 이름으로 저를 부르는 소리일 터였다. 그리하여 엘리너의 가슴은 잔잔해졌고, 그녀는 새로 맡은 소임에 열심히, 또 고맙게 나섰다.\n\n세상살이의 형편으로 말하자면, 존 볼드는 미망인을 넉넉한 처지에 남겨 두고 갔다. 그는 제가 가진 것을 모두 그녀에게 물려주었는데, 그 재산에서 나오는 수입은 그녀에게든 그녀의 벗들에게든 필요하다고 여겨지는 정도를 훨씬 웃돌았다. 그것은 연 1,000파운드에 가까웠다. 그 규모를 헤아려 볼 때 그녀의 가장 간절한 소망은 그것을 축내지 않는 것은 물론이요 불려서 남편의 아들에게, 제가 사랑하는 아이에게, 저를 위해 쌓일 근심 따위는 복되게도 모른 채 지금 제 무릎에 잠들어 있는 그 조그만 사내에게 넘겨주는 일이었다.\n\n존 볼드가 죽었을 때 그녀는 아버지에게 와서 함께 살자고 간곡히 청했으나 하딩 씨는 사양했다. 다만 몇 주 동안은 손님으로 그녀와 함께 머물렀다. 그는 아무리 작아도 제 집이라 할 것을 지니는 일만은 끝내 포기하려 들지 않았고, 그리하여 바체스터 하이 스트리트의 약재상 위층에 처음 골라 든 하숙에 그대로 머물렀다.\n\n# 제3장. 프라우디 박사 내외\n\n이 이야기는 프라우디 박사의 취임식이 끝난 직후부터 시작되는 것으로 한다. 그 의식은 묘사하지 않겠는데, 나로서는 그 성격을 정확히 알지 못하기 때문이다. 주교가 국회의원처럼 가마에 떠메어지는지, 시장처럼 금박 마차에 실려 가는지, 치안판사처럼 선서를 하는지, 귀족처럼 상원에 소개되는지, 아니면 가터 훈작사처럼 두 형제 사이에 이끌려 나아가는지 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 모든 일이 마땅한 절차대로 치러졌다는 것, 그리고 젊은 주교에게 어울리고 걸맞은 것이라면 그날 하나도 빠짐없이 갖추어졌다는 것만은 안다.\n\n프라우디 박사는 자신의 새 위엄에 걸맞을 만한 것이라면 그 무엇도 빠뜨리게 둘 사람이 아니었다. 그는 격식의 가치를 잘 이해하고 있었고, 지위에 딸린 겉치레들이 응당한 존중을 받지 못하는 한 지위에 대한 예우도 유지될 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 상류 사회에서 움직이도록 태어난 사람이었다. 적어도 스스로는 그렇게 여겼고, 형편도 분명 그 생각을 뒷받침해 주었다. 그는 외가 쪽으로 아일랜드 남작의 조카였고, 그의 아내는 스코틀랜드 백작의 조카딸이었다. 그는 여러 해 동안 궁정 관련 업무에 딸린 성직을 맡아 왔는데, 덕분에 런던에 살면서 자기 교구는 부목사에게 맡겨 둘 수 있었다. 그는 왕실 근위병의 설교자였고, 교회 법정의 신학 사본 관리인이었으며, 여왕 직속 기병 근위대의 전속 사제였고, 라페블랑켄베르크 공 전하의 시혜관이었다.\n\n이렇듯 맡겨진 직무 때문에 부득이해진 수도 거주와, 그의 높은 연줄과, 이 사람 특유의 재능과 성품은 그를 권력을 쥔 이들에게 천거해 주었고, 그리하여 프라우디 박사는 쓸모 있고 앞길이 트인 성직자로 알려지게 되었다.\n\n몇 해 전만 해도, 아직 스스로를 늙었다고 부르고 싶지 않은 많은 이들의 기억 안에 있는 그 시절만 해도, 자유주의적인 성직자란 좀처럼 만나 볼 수 없는 사람이었다. 시드니 스미스가 그런 사람이었는데, 그는 무신론자보다 나을 것이 없다는 눈총을 받았다. 몇 사람 더 이름을 댈 수는 있겠으나 그들은 rarae aves였고, 동료 성직자들에게 의심과 불신의 눈길을 받았다. 시골 교구 목사만큼 확실한 토리당원은 없었고, 옥스퍼드만큼 기존의 권세가 떠받들어지는 곳도 없었다.\n\n그러나 웨이틀리 박사가 대주교가 되고 몇 해 뒤 햄프던 박사가 흠정 교수가 되자, 지각 있는 신학자들 다수는 사람들의 마음속에 변화가 일어나고 있으며 앞으로는 평신도뿐 아니라 성직자에게도 한결 자유주의적인 생각이 어울리게 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다. 한편으로 교황파를 저주하기를 그만두고 다른 한편으로 비국교도를 헐뜯기를 그만둔 성직자들의 이름이 들려오기 시작했다. 이른바 고교회파 원칙이 적어도 정치가들의 어느 한 부류에게는 더 이상 승진의 가장 확실한 자격이 못 된다는 것이 분명해 보였고, 프라우디 박사는 대부분의 신학적·종교적 문제에서 휘그당의 견해에 일찌감치 자신을 맞춘 이들 가운데 하나였다. 그는 로마의 우상숭배를 참아 주었고, 소치누스주의의 불신앙마저 용인했으며, 스코틀랜드와 얼스터의 장로교 노회들과도 허물없이 지냈다.\n\n그런 시절에 그런 사람은 쓸모가 있다는 것이 드러났고, 프라우디 박사의 이름이 신문에 오르내리기 시작했다. 그는 국가 교육위원회의 운영에 앞선 준비를 갖추러 아일랜드로 건너간 위원회의 한 사람이 되었고, 대성당 참사회의 수입을 조사하도록 임명된 또 다른 위원회의 명예 간사가 되었으며, regium donum과 메이누스 보조금 양쪽에도 얼마쯤 관여했다.\n\n그렇다고 해서 프라우디 박사가 대단한 지력의 소유자라거나 실무 능력이 뛰어난 사람이라는 것이 입증된 것으로 여겨서는 안 된다. 그에게 그런 자질이 요구된 적은 없었기 때문이다. 그가 관여한 교회 개혁을 마련하는 일에서, 해야 할 일에 대한 착상과 최초의 구상은 대개 당대의 자유주의 정치가들이 내놓았고, 세부를 다루는 수고는 그보다 낮은 직급의 관리들이 짊어졌다. 다만 그런 사안에는 어느 성직자의 이름이 하나 걸려 있는 편이 편리하다고들 여겼고, 프라우디 박사는 관용적인 신학자로 알려져 있었으므로 그의 이름이 이런 용도로 요긴하게 쓰였다. 그가 적극적으로 좋은 일을 많이 하지는 않았다 해도 해를 끼친 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그는 실권을 쥔 이들의 뜻에 순순히 따랐고, 자신이 속한 여러 위원회의 회의에서는 나름의 값어치가 있는 위엄 같은 것을 지켰다.\n\n그가 성가시게 굴지 않으면서도 자기에게 요구된 목적에 부응할 만한 눈치를 충분히 지녔던 것은 분명하다. 그러나 그렇다고 그가 자신의 역량을 의심했다거나, 제 차례가 오면 큰일에서 큰 몫을 스스로 맡을 수 있으리라고 믿지 못했다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 그는 때를 기다리고 있었으며, 자기가 어느 위원회의 상석에 권위 있게 앉아 말하고 지시하며 좌지우지하는 날, 그가 그토록 잘 익혀 온 그 순종을 이번에는 못한 별들이 빙 둘러앉아 하게 될 그날을 참을성 있게 고대하고 있었다.\n\n그의 보상과 그의 때가 마침내 왔다. 그는 비어 있는 주교좌에 선택되었고, 어느 교구에서든 다음 자리가 비면 상원에 자기 자리를 차지하게 될 터였으며, 국교회의 안녕에 관한 모든 사안에서 결코 말없는 표만 던지지는 않으리라 벼르고 있었다. 관용이 그가 싸움을 벌여 나갈 토대가 될 것이었고, 그는 그 정직한 담력으로, 엑서터와 옥스퍼드의 동료 주교들 같은 적수와 맞붙는다 해도 자기에게 나쁜 일이 생기지는 않으리라 생각했다.\n\n프라우디 박사는 야심 있는 사람이었으니, 바체스터 주교로 채 축성되기도 전에 벌써 대주교의 광휘와 램버스의 영광을, 하다못해 비숍스소프의 영광이라도 우러러보기 시작했다. 그는 비교적 젊었고, 스스로 흐뭇하게 자부하기로는, 이제 더 높은 무대가 열린 마당에 반드시 더 높은 주목을 끌어 줄 만한 천부의 재능과 후천의 재능을 지녔기에 뽑힌 것이었다. 그러므로 프라우디 박사는 이 나라의 신학적 사안 전반에서 두드러진 역할을 맡을 준비가 아주 잘 되어 있었고, 그런 포부를 지닌 터라 전임자처럼 바체스터에 파묻힐 생각은 조금도 없었다. 천만에! 런던이 여전히 그의 활동 무대가 되어야 했다. 지방 도시의 안락한 저택이야 한 해 중 죽은 듯한 달들에나 어울릴 만한 것이었다. 사실 프라우디 박사는 다른 훌륭하고 유행에 밝은 사람들이 런던을 뜰 때 자기도 물러나는 것이 자기 지위에 필요하다고 늘 여겨 왔다. 그러나 런던은 여전히 그의 붙박이 거처여야 했고, 사도 바울이 모든 주교에게 그토록 각별히 권한 그 손님 접대를 그가 베풀기로 작정한 곳도 런던이었다. 그러지 않고서야 어떻게 세상 앞에 자신을 계속 내세울 수 있겠는가? 달리 어떻게 신학적 사안에서 자신의 무게와 재능을 정부에 온전히 보태 줄 수 있겠는가?\n\n이 결심은 넓은 세상을 위해서는 의심할 바 없이 유익한 것이었겠으나, 바체스터의 성직자에게나 주민에게나 그를 인기 있는 사람으로 만들어 줄 것 같지는 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 늘 그곳에 살았다. 사실 그랜틀리 박사의 뒤를 이어 인기를 얻기란 주교로서 어려운 일이었다. 그의 수입은 연평균 9,000파운드였는데, 후임자는 5,000파운드로 엄격히 제한될 것이었다. 그는 돈을 쓸 자식이 하나뿐이었으나 프라우디 박사에게는 일고여덟이 있었다. 그는 개인적 씀씀이가 적은 사람이었고 그 씀씀이도 검소한 신사의 취향에 머물렀지만, 프라우디 박사는 유행하는 사교계에서 지위를 지켜야 했으며 그것도 비교적 적은 재력으로 해내야 했다. 그랜틀리 박사도 주교답게 마차를 갖추기는 했으나, 그의 마차와 말과 마부는 바체스터에서야 나무랄 데 없었을지 몰라도 웨스트민스터에서는 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없었을 것이다. 프라우디 부인은 남편의 마차 차림새가 자기에게 창피를 주게 두지 않겠다고 마음먹었고, 프라우디 부인이 마음먹은 일은 대개 이루어졌다.\n\n이 모든 사정으로 미루어 프라우디 박사가 바체스터에서 돈을 많이 쓰지는 않으리라는 결과가 나올 법했다. 반면 그의 전임자는 그 도시의 상인들을 그들로서는 매우 흡족하게 상대해 주었다. 아버지와 아들, 두 그랜틀리는 신사답게 돈을 썼으나, 프라우디 박사는 한정된 재력에서 최대한의 부유한 겉모습을 짜내는 저 알뜰한 방편들에 낯설지 않은 사람이라는 수군거림이 바체스터에 곧 돌기 시작했다.\n\n생김새로 말하자면 프라우디 박사는 잘생긴 사람이다. 말쑥하고 맵시 있고 아주 단정하다. 키는 중키에 조금 못 미쳐 5피트 4인치쯤 되지만, 모자라는 치수는 지닌 치수를 지니고 다니는 위엄으로 메운다. 그에게 사람을 압도하는 눈매가 없다면 그것은 그의 탓이 아니니, 그는 그런 눈매를 갖추려고 무던히도 애쓰기 때문이다. 이목구비는 반듯한데, 다만 코가 뾰족한 탓에 어떤 사람들 눈에는 그 얼굴이 대수롭지 않아 보일지도 모른다. 설령 그렇더라도 그가 자랑할 만도 한 입과 턱이 그 흠을 넉넉히 벌충해 준다.\n\n프라우디 박사는 운 좋은 사람이었다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 부유하게 태어나지 않았는데 이제 바체스터 주교가 되었으니 말이다. 그렇지만 그에게도 근심은 있다. 그는 식구가 많고, 그중 위로 셋은 딸인데 이제 모두 다 자라 사교계에 나설 만하다. 그리고 그에게는 아내가 있다. 나는 프라우디 부인의 인품에 대해 한마디라도 흠잡을 생각은 없다. 그러나 그 온갖 미덕에도 불구하고 그 부인이 남편의 행복에 크게 보태 준다고는 아무래도 생각할 수가 없다. 사실인즉 그 부인은 집안일에서 명목상의 주인인 남편 위에 군림하며, 그것도 쇠몽둥이로 다스린다. 그뿐이 아니다. 집안일이라면 프라우디 박사도, 자진해서는 아닐지언정 기꺼이 아내에게 넘겨주었을 것이다. 그러나 프라우디 부인은 그런 가정 안의 지배로는 만족하지 않아 남편의 모든 거동에까지 권세를 뻗치며, 영적인 일에서마저 손을 떼려 하지 않는다. 요컨대 이 주교는 아내에게 쥐여산다.\n\n부주교의 아내는 플럼스테드의 행복한 집에서 제 신분에 딸린 특권을 온전히 누릴 줄 알고, 걸맞은 어조로 걸맞은 자리에서 제 뜻을 밝힐 줄도 안다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인의 다스림은, 다스림이라 할 것이 있다면, 부드럽고 이롭다. 그 부인은 남편에게 결코 창피를 주지 않으며, 세상 앞에서는 순종의 본보기다. 목소리를 높이는 법도, 눈매가 매서워지는 법도 없다. 물론 권세를 소중히 여기고 그것을 손에 넣으려 애써 왔으며 그 애씀이 헛되지도 않았지만, 여자의 다스림이 어디까지여야 하는지 그 한계를 그 부인은 안다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 그렇지 않다. 이 부인은 누구에게나 늘 위압적이지만 가엾은 남편에게는 전제군주다. 세상의 눈에는 그의 이력이 성공적이었을지 몰라도, 아내의 눈에는 그가 옳은 적이 한 번도 없는 듯싶다. 자신을 변호해 보겠다는 희망은 그에게서 사라진 지 오래다. 실제로 그는 변명을 시도하는 일조차 드물거니와, 순순히 굽히는 것이야말로 제 집안이 도달할 수 있는 평화에 가장 가까운 길임을 알고 있다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 남편이 국가의 부름을 받아 나가는 위원회와 심의회 자리에 앉을 수는 없었고, 그가 자주 되새기는 바이지만 상원에서 제 목소리를 들리게 할 수도 없다. 어쩌면 그 부인은 주교 직무의 이 갈래에 남편이 관여하는 것을 허락하지 않을지도 모른다. 어쩌면 제 서재에나 붙어 있으라고 우길지도 모른다. 그는 이 문제를 두고 살아 있는 그 누구의 귀에도 한마디 속삭인 적이 없지만, 이미 마음속으로 굳게 작정해 두었다. 그런 시도가 있을진대 자기는 반역하리라고. 억압이 지나치게 가혹해지면 개도 주인에게 대들고 나폴리 사람들도 통치자에게 대드는 법이다. 그리고 프라우디 박사는 밧줄이 지나치게 조여진다면 자기 역시 용기를 그러모아 버틸 수 있으리라고 속으로 느끼고 있다.\n\n우리 주교가 아내에게 붙들려 지내는 이 예속 상태는 딸들의 눈에 아버지의 품위를 높여 주지 못했으니, 딸들은 아버지에게 말할 때 적어도 저희에게는 어울리지 않는 권위를 지나치게 부린다. 딸들은 대체로 훤칠하고 사람을 끄는 처녀들이다. 어머니를 닮아 키가 크고 튼튼하며, 어머니의 도드라진 광대뼈와 — 적갈색이라고 해 두자 — 그 머리칼을 셋 다 물려받았다. 딸들은 종조부들을 좀 과하게 떠받드는데, 종조부들 쪽에서는 이제껏 그만큼 저희를 떠받들어 답례한 적이 없다. 그러나 이제 아버지가 주교가 되었으니 친척의 정이 더 가까워질 법도 하다. 교회와 얽힌 집안인 것을 생각하면 딸들은 세상의 즐거움에 대해 편견이랄 것이 거의 없으며, 근래 너무 많은 영국 처녀들이 그랬듯 개신교 수녀원의 은둔에 몸을 바치겠다는 열띤 소망 따위로 부모의 애를 태운 적도 결코 없다. 프라우디 박사의 아들들은 아직 학교에 다닌다.\n\n주교 부인의 성격에서 두드러진 특징을 하나 더 짚어 두어야겠다. 세상의 교제와 몸가짐을 싫어하지는 않으면서도, 그 부인은 나름대로 신앙심이 깊은 여자다. 그리고 그 성향이 그 부인에게서 드러나는 형태는 안식일 규율의 엄격한 준수다. 주중의 방탕과 목이 파인 옷차림은, 그 부인의 관할 아래, 세 차례의 예배와 부인이 손수 읽는 저녁 설교와 주일에는 마음을 즐겁게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 완전히 삼가는 것으로 속량된다. 한 지붕 아래 있으면서도 그 방탕과 목이 파인 옷차림은 나누어 받지 못하는 이들, 곧 하인들과 남편에게는 불행하게도, 그 벌충으로서의 안식일 엄수만은 모두에게 적용된다. 슬로프 씨의 혼을 흔드는 저녁 강론 대신 리전트 파크에서 애인의 달콤한 소리에 귀를 기울인 것이 들통난 배교자 하녀에게는 화가 있을진저. 그 하녀는 쫓겨날 뿐 아니라, 반반한 자리를 다시 얻을 가망이 거의 없는 평판을 달고 쫓겨난다. 붉은 플러시 반바지 차림으로 프라우디 부인을 좌석까지 모시는 6피트짜리 장정이 제게 배정된 뒷자리에 앉는 대신 이웃 맥줏집으로 슬쩍 빠져나간다면, 그에게도 화가 있을진저. 그런 죄인을 살피는 데 프라우디 부인은 아르고스의 눈을 지녔다. 주중에 이따금 술에 취하는 것쯤은 눈감아 줄 수 있다. 품행을 늘 높은 수준으로 지키라고 하면 낮은 삯으로 6피트짜리를 구하기가 어려운 탓이다. 그러나 위엄을 위해서든 살림을 아끼기 위해서든, 안식일을 더럽히는 일만은 프라우디 부인이 결코 용서하지 않는다.\n\n그런 문제에서 프라우디 부인은 저 웅변에 능한 설교자 슬로프 목사의 인도를 곧잘 받아들이고, 프라우디 박사는 아내의 인도를 받으므로, 방금 이름을 댄 그 걸출한 인물이 종교에 관한 사안에서 프라우디 박사를 상당히 좌우하게 되었다는 결론이 자연히 따라 나온다. 슬로프 씨가 이제껏 얻은 성직이라고는 런던의 한 구역 교회에서 성경을 읽고 설교하는 자리뿐이었는데, 벗인 새 주교가 축성되자 그는 그 자리를 선뜻 내놓고 주교님의 가정 전속 사제라는 고되지만 제 마음에 꼭 맞는 직무를 떠맡았다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨를 처음 선보이면서 한 장의 꽁무니에 그를 세상 앞으로 끌어낼 수야 없는 노릇이다.\n\n# 제4장. 주교의 전속 사제\n\n슬로프 목사의 혈통에 관해서는 내가 말할 수 있는 바가 많지 않다. 나는 그가 T. 섄디 씨의 출생을 거들었던 저 저명한 의사의 직계 후손이며, 앞서 여러 위인들이 그리했듯 젊은 시절에 듣기 좋으라고 제 이름에 “e” 한 자를 덧붙였다는 말을 들은 적이 있다. 만일 그것이 사실이라면 그는 오바디아라는 이름으로 세례를 받았으리라 짐작되는데, 실제로 그것이 그의 이름이며, 이는 그 조상이 그토록 두각을 나타냈던 저 다툼을 기린 것이리라. 그러나 이 문제에 관한 나의 온갖 조사도, 그 집안이 언제 종교를 갈아탔는지 그 날짜를 확정하는 데는 끝내 이르지 못했다.\n\n그는 케임브리지의 급비생이었고, 그곳에서 적어도 성공적으로 처신하기는 했다. 때가 되자 문학 석사가 되어 제 밑에 대학생 제자들을 두었으니 말이다. 그는 거기서 런던으로 옮겨 가, 베이커 스트리트 어름에 새로 세워진 구역 교회의 설교자가 되었다. 종교 문제에 관한 견해가 서로 맞아떨어져 그가 프라우디 부인에게 천거된 것은 바로 이 자리에 있을 때였고, 두 사람의 왕래는 이내 긴밀하고 흉허물 없는 사이로 발전했다.\n\n이렇게 프라우디 집안 아가씨들 사이에 스스럼없이 섞이게 된 터라, 우정보다 조금 더 부드러운 감정이 싹튼 것도 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다. 그와 그 집의 맏딸 올리비아 사이에는 사랑의 곡절이 몇 차례 있었으나, 지금까지 이렇다 할 결실에는 이르지 못했다. 실인즉 슬로프 씨는 애정을 고백해 놓고서, 그 박사에게 제 딸에게 당장 떼어 줄 재산이 없다는 것을 알고는 그 고백을 도로 거두어들였던 것이다. 그러니 그런 통고를 받은 프라우디 양이 그 뒤로 그가 내미는 애정의 표시를 선뜻 받아들일 마음이 나지 않았으리라는 것은 쉬이 짐작할 수 있는 일이다. 그런데 프라우디 박사가 바체스터 주교로 임명되자 슬로프 씨의 견해는 사실 다소 달라졌다. 주교란 아무리 가난할지언정 성직에 몸담은 자식쯤은 넉넉히 앉힐 수 있는 자리인지라, 슬로프 씨는 자기가 좀 더 사심 없이 굴지 못한 것을 후회하기 시작했다. 그는 박사가 승진했다는 소식을 듣자마자 다시 공략을 개시했는데, 물론 거칠게가 아니라 공손하게, 또 적당한 거리를 두고서였다. 그러나 올리비아 프라우디는 기개 있는 처녀였다. 그 혈관에는 귀족 두 집안의 피가 흐르고 있었고, 더구나 장부에 올려 둔 다른 구혼자가 하나 더 있었다. 그리하여 슬로프 씨는 헛되이 한숨만 쉬었고, 두 사람은 이내 서로에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오의 유대를 맺어 두는 편이 편하다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그런 일이 있고서도 젊은 성직자에 대한 프라우디 부인의 우정이 흔들림 없이 남아 있다는 것이 이상하게 여겨질지 모르나, 실토하자면 부인은 그 일을 전혀 모르고 있었다. 부인 자신은 슬로프 씨를 몹시 아꼈으면서도, 제 딸 가운데 누가 그를 그런 눈으로 볼 수 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고, 딸들의 고귀한 태생과 사교계에서의 이점을 떠올리며 그들에게는 전혀 다른 종류의 혼처를 기대하고 있었던 것이다. 신사도 숙녀도 굳이 부인을 깨우쳐 줄 필요를 느끼지 않았다. 올리비아의 두 자매는 저마다 그 일을 알고 있었고, 하인들도 모조리 알고 있었으며, 양옆 이웃집에 사는 사람들까지 죄다 알고 있었으나, 프라우디 부인만은 감쪽같이 어둠 속에 남겨져 있었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 곧 이런 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다. 이미 주교의 전속 사제로 뽑힌 이상, 주교의 딸 때문에 애를 태우지 않고도 주교가 나눠 줄 좋은 것들을 손에 넣는 일쯤은 아마 제 힘으로 될 것이라고. 그러자 거절당한 사랑의 아픔쯤은 능히 견딜 만해졌다. 주교 내외가 바체스터로 첫 여행길에 오를 때, 그는 그들과 마주 보는 자리에 앉아 기차 객실에 몸을 묻고서 제 앞날의 계획을 마음속으로 짜기 시작했다. 그는 자기 후원자의 강점을 잘 알았지만, 약점 또한 그만큼 잘 알았다. 새 주교의 드높은 기개가 어떤 시도를 향해 날아오를지를 그는 꽤나 정확하게 헤아리고 있었으며, 그 위인의 취향에는 교구 사무의 자잘한 일보다 공적인 활동이 더 맞으리라고 옳게 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n그러니 그가, 바로 그 슬로프 씨가, 사실상 바체스터의 주교가 될 것이었다. 이것이 그의 결심이었고, 슬로프 씨에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 그에게는 그 결심을 밀고 나갈 만한 담력과 기개가 함께 있었다. 그는 힘겨운 싸움을 치러야 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 교구의 권세와 성직 임명권을 탐내는 또 하나의 위대한 정신이 있었기 때문이다. 프라우디 부인 또한 바체스터의 주교가 되기를 택할 터였다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 자기가 그 부인의 허를 찌를 수 있으리라고 자부했다. 부인은 런던에서 지내는 날이 많을 테지만 자기는 언제나 현장에 붙어 있을 것이었다. 부인은 필연코 모르는 일이 많을 테지만 자기는 교구에 속한 것이라면 무엇이든 알고 있을 것이었다. 물론 처음에는 아첨도 하고 비위도 맞춰야 할 테고, 더러는 양보도 해야 할 테지만, 결국은 이기리라는 것을 그는 의심하지 않았다. 다른 방책이 다 실패한다면 주교 편에 서서 그 아내에 맞설 수도 있었다. 그 가엾은 사내에게 용기를 불어넣고, 여인의 권세에 도끼를 대어 뿌리를 찍고, 남편을 해방시켜 주면 되는 것이다.\n\n기차 객실에서 잠든 두 사람을 바라보며 그가 품었던 생각이 이러했으니, 슬로프 씨는 그런 생각을 공연히 품고 앉아 있을 위인이 아니다. 그는 평균을 웃도는 능력을 지녔고 담력도 있다. 필요하다면 몸을 굽혀 알랑거릴 수도 있고 실로 아주 낮게까지 굽힐 수도 있지만, 그 안에는 폭군 노릇을 떠맡을 힘이 여전히 들어 있다. 그리고 그 힘과 더불어 그런 욕망 또한 분명히 지니고 있다. 그의 학식은 최상급이라 할 것은 못 되나, 있는 만큼은 완전히 제 통제 아래 두고 있으며 그것을 쓸 줄도 안다. 그는 일종의 강단 웅변술을 타고났는데, 남자들을 설득하기에는 그리 미덥지 못할지언정 연약한 성에게는 위력을 떨치는 종류의 것이다. 설교에서 그는 규탄을 아낌없이 퍼부어, 마음 약한 청중의 심령을 아주 불쾌하지만은 않은 공포로 뒤흔들어 놓고는, 온 인류가 위태로운 처지에 있으며 여인들 또한 그러하되 다만 베이커 스트리트의 저녁 강론에 꼬박꼬박 나오는 이들만은 예외라는 인상을 그들 마음에 남겨 놓는다. 그의 표정과 말씨는 지극히 준엄해서, 이 세상의 태반은 자기가 마음 쓰기에는 한없이 못돼먹었다고 그가 여기고 있구나 싶은 생각을 아니 할 도리가 없다. 거리를 걸어갈 때면 그 얼굴 자체가 세상의 사악함에 대한 그의 경악을 드러내며, 눈꼬리에는 언제나 저주의 말이 도사리고 있다.\n\n교리로 말하자면 그는 자기 후원자와 마찬가지로 비국교도에게 너그러운 편인데, 그토록 빡빡한 정신을 두고 무엇엔가 너그럽다는 말을 붙일 수 있다면 그렇다는 이야기다. 웨슬리파 감리교도와는 통하는 데가 얼마쯤 있으나, 퓨지파의 죄악상 앞에서는 그의 영혼이 고통스레 떤다. 그의 혐오는 안의 것에만이 아니라 겉의 것에까지 미친다. 지붕이 뾰족하게 솟은 새 교회를 보면 그는 부아가 치밀고, 앞가슴이 넓게 파인 검은 비단 조끼는 그에게 사탄의 상징이며, 붉은 글자로 찍히고 등에 십자가 장식이 박힌 기도서 한 권은 그가 보기에 불경한 농담집보다도 더 더럽게 그리스도인의 좌석을 욕되게 한다. 부지런한 성직자에게는 대개 저마다의 편벽이 있는 법인데, 그의 것은 주일 성수다. 그러나 “일요일”이라는 말이 그의 입을 더럽히는 일은 결코 없다. 그것은 언제나 “안식일”이다. 그가 즐겨 부르는 대로 하자면 “안식일의 모독”이야말로 그에게는 먹을 것이요 마실 것이다. 순경이 세상 사람들의 나쁜 버릇을 먹고 살듯 그는 그것을 먹고 산다. 그것은 저녁 강론마다 그가 사랑해 마지않는 주제요, 그의 온갖 웅변의 원천이며, 여인의 마음을 휘어잡는 그의 힘의 비결이다. 그에게 하나님의 계시란 유대인이 지키라고 주어진 그 한 가지 율법 속에서만 나타난다. 그에게는 우리 구주의 자비하심이 헛되이 말할 뿐이요, 저 거룩한 입술이 산 위에서 내려 주신 설교, 곧 “온유한 자는 복이 있나니 그들이 땅을 기업으로 받을 것임이요”, “긍휼히 여기는 자는 복이 있나니 그들이 긍휼히 여김을 받을 것임이요” 하신 그 말씀도 헛되이 전파되었을 따름이다. 그에게 신약성경은 비교적 대수롭지 않은 것인데, 이 땅에서 사람에게 배정된 시간의 적어도 7분의 1을 제 뜻대로 주무르기 좋아하는 그 지배권을 위해서는 거기서 아무런 새 권위도 끌어낼 수 없기 때문이다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 키가 크고 몸매도 나쁘지 않다. 손발이 큰 것은 그 집안 사람들이 예로부터 다 그러했던 바이지만, 그에게는 그 군더더기를 감당할 만한 넓은 가슴과 떡 벌어진 어깨가 있어서 전체적인 체구는 볼만하다. 그러나 얼굴 생김은 그리 호감을 주지 못한다. 머리칼은 힘없이 늘어졌고 빛깔은 흐릿하게 바랜 불그죽죽한 색이다. 그것은 언제나 꼿꼿하고 뭉툭한 세 덩어리로 지어져 있는데, 하나하나가 감탄스러울 만큼 정확하게 빗질된 뒤 기름으로 두껍게 굳혀져 있다. 그중 둘은 양쪽 뺨에 착 달라붙어 있고, 나머지 하나는 그 위에 직각으로 얹혀 있다. 구레나룻은 기르지 않으며 언제나 빈틈없이 면도를 한다. 낯빛은 머리칼과 거의 같은 색이되 어쩌면 조금 더 붉은데, 쇠고기와 그리 다르지 않다. 다만 질이 나쁜 쇠고기라고 해야 할 것이다. 이마는 널찍하고 높지만 네모지고 둔중하며 불쾌하게 번들거린다. 입은 크되 입술은 얇고 핏기가 없으며, 크고 툭 불거진 옅은 갈색 눈은 신뢰라고 할 만한 것과는 정반대의 감정을 불러일으킨다. 그래도 코만은 그를 구제해 주는 대목이다. 코는 두드러지고 곧으며 반듯하게 잘 빠졌다. 다만 나로서는, 붉은 코르크를 솜씨 좋게 깎아 만들어 붙인 듯 어딘가 스펀지처럼 물컹하고 구멍이 숭숭한 느낌만 없었더라면 훨씬 마음에 들었을 것이다.\n\n나는 슬로프 씨와 악수하는 일만은 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다. 그에게서는 언제나 차고 끈적한 땀이 배어 나오고, 이마에는 잔 땀방울이 늘 맺혀 있는 것이 보이며, 그 정다운 악력은 불쾌하다.\n\n이런 사람이 슬로프 씨다. 바체스터 대성당 경내 한복판에 난데없이 굴러떨어져, 지금껏 작고한 주교의 아들이 채우고 있던 자리를 그곳에서 떠맡게 될 운명을 진 사내가 바로 이런 사람이다. 생각해 보라, 사색을 즐기는 나의 독자여, 우리가 여기서 어떤 동료를 맞이하게 되었는가를. 저 안락한 참사회원들, 저 신사다운 성직 박사들, 그랜틀리 주교의 인자한 날개 아래 바체스터에서 자라난 저 행복하고 잘 대접받고 잘 먹은 하급 참사원들에게 말이다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨가 주교 내외와 함께 바체스터로 내려가는 것은 그저 그들의 동료가 되기 위해서가 아니다. 그는 그들의 주인까지는 아닐지라도 적어도 그들 가운데 으뜸이 될 작정이다. 그는 이끌 작정이며 따르는 자들을 거느릴 작정이다. 그는 교구의 돈줄을 틀어쥐고 가난하고 굶주린 형제들을 순종하는 무리로 제 주위에 끌어모을 작정이다.\n\n그리고 여기서 우리는 부주교와 우리의 새 전속 사제를 견주어 보지 않을 수 없는데, 앞사람의 허물이 하고많음에도 불구하고 그 비교가 그에게 여간 유리하게 나오지 않는다는 것 또한 부정하기 어렵다.\n\n두 사람 다 제 신분 계급의 권세를 떠받치고 키우는 데 열심이며, 지나칠 만큼 열심이다. 둘 다 세상이 사제의 다스림을 받기를 갈망하는데, 아마 그런 속내를 저 자신에게조차 털어놓아 본 적은 없을 것이다. 둘 다 사람이 사람 위에 서는 다른 종류의 지배는 무엇이든 못마땅해한다. 그랜틀리 박사는 영적인 일에서 여왕의 수장권을 인정하기는 하되, 그것은 대관식의 성별하는 효력을 통해 여왕에게 전해진 준사제적 성격에서 비롯된 것으로 볼 때에만 인정하는 것이며, 세속의 일이란 그 본성상 영적인 일에 종속된다고 여긴다. 사제의 지배에 관한 슬로프 씨의 생각은 이와는 전혀 다른 부류의 것이다. 그는 여왕의 수장권 따위에는 이렇든 저렇든 아무 관심이 없다. 그의 귀에 그런 것들은 아무 뜻도 없는 빈말일 뿐이다. 그는 형식이란 것을 별로 대수롭게 여기지 않으며, 수장권이니 성별이니 안수니 하는 명목상의 표현들은 그 자체로는 그에게 아무 의미도 전해 주지 않는다. 으뜸이 될 수 있는 자가 으뜸이 되면 그만이다. 세속의 왕이나 재판관이나 옥리는 몸뚱이에나 손을 댈 수 있을 뿐이다. 영적인 주인은 필요한 재능이 있고 그것을 제대로 쓸 줄만 안다면 훨씬 더 넓은 지배의 판도를 갖는다. 그는 영혼을 다루기 때문이다. 남들이 저를 믿게 만들 수만 있다면 그는 귀 기울이는 자들 위에 전능해질 수 있다. 지력이 너무 강한 자나 육신이 너무 약한 자와는 얽히지 않도록 조심하기만 하면 그는 실로 으뜸이 될 수 있는 것이다. 슬로프 씨의 야심이 바로 그러했다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 어떤 식으로든 제 아래 있는 이들의 세속적 처신에 거의 간섭하지 않았다. 그가 휘하 성직자의 비행이나 제 교구 안의 부도덕이나 제 집안의 소홀함을 못 본 체했다는 말은 아니다. 다만 굳이 그러지 않아도 될 자리에서는 애써 그러려 들지 않았다는 뜻이다. 그는 남의 일을 캐고 싶어 하는 성벽에 시달리지 않았으며, 제 주위 사람들이 비국교로 기우는 이단의 물이 들지 않은 한, 또 그들이 어머니 교회의 효력을 온전히 그리고 선선히 인정하는 한, 그 어머니가 자비롭고 다정하며 너그럽게 눈감아 주기를 즐기고 매를 들기는 꺼리는 편이기를 바랐다. 그 자신은 이 세상의 좋은 것들을 누렸고 자기가 그러고 있다는 사실이 알려지는 것도 좋아했다. 그는 만찬 모임을 해롭게 여기거나 클라레 한 병쯤의 위험을 겁내는 동료 교구 목사가 있으면 진심으로 경멸했다. 그리하여 만찬 모임과 클라레 병은 이 교구에서 흔한 것이 되었다. 그는 법을 세우고 그 법이 군말 없이 지켜지는 것을 좋아했지만, 자기가 내리는 규례가 사람이 감당할 만한 범위 안에 들고 신사의 비위에 거슬리지 않도록 애썼다. 그는 이제 여러 해째 이웃 성직자들 사이에서 군림해 왔는데, 인심을 잃지 않고서도 그 권세를 지켜 왔으니 얼마간의 지혜를 발휘해 온 것이라 짐작해도 좋을 것이다.\n\n슬로프 씨의 처신에 관해서는 그의 대단한 이력이 아직 시작되지도 않았으므로 할 말이 많지 않지만, 그 취향이 부주교의 것과는 아주 딴판이리라는 것쯤은 미리 말해 둘 수 있다. 그는 제게 맡겨진 양 떼의 사사로운 행실과 욕망을 죄다 아는 것이 제 직분이라고 여긴다. 가난한 계층에게서는 정해진 행실의 규칙에 대한 무조건적인 복종을 요구하며, 순종하지 않는 자가 있으면 그 위대한 조상이 그러했듯 에르눌푸스의 저주를 끌어다 쓴다. “너는 들어갈 때에도 저주를 받고 나올 때에도 저주를 받을 것이며, 먹을 때에도 마실 때에도 저주를 받으리라” 운운, 운운, 운운. 부유한 자들에게는 다른 방식이 필요하다는 것을 경험이 이미 그에게 가르쳐 주었다. 상류 사회를 걸어가는 남자들은 저주를 받아도 아랑곳하지 않고, 여자들은 그것이 고상한 말씨로 행해지기만 한다면 오히려 좋아하는 편이다. 그렇다고 그가 믿는 그리스도인들 가운데 그토록 중요한 몫을 포기해 버린 것은 아니다. 남자들과는 실로 대체로 사이가 틀어져 있다. 그들은 완악한 죄인들이라 사제라는 마술사의 목소리가 너무도 자주 헛되이 떨어지고 만다. 그러나 부인들이라면 늙었든 젊었든, 굳세든 여리든, 경건하든 방탕하든, 그는 제 생각에 전능하다. 그는 아첨을 잔뜩 섞어 잘못을 나무랄 줄 알고, 어루만지듯 하는 태도로 질책을 늘어놓을 줄 아는지라, 여인의 마음이란 저교회파의 감수성이 한 점이라도 타오르고 있는 한 그를 당해 낼 수 없다. 그리하여 그는 많은 집에서 흠모받는 손님이 된다. 남편들은 아내들을 위해 마지못해 그를 들이는데, 한번 들이고 나면 떼어 내기가 쉽지 않다. 그러나 그에게는 앞발로 더듬어 대듯 하는 기름진 구석이 있어서, 제 영혼 때문에 그를 귀히 여기는 처지가 아닌 사람들에게는 도무지 정이 붙지 않는다. 그러니 지금 바체스터에서 그를 둘러싸게 될 그런 넓은 사교 범위에서 대뜸 인기를 얻을 만한 위인은 못 된다.\n\n# 제5장. 아침 방문\n\n프라우디 박사가 앞서 언급한 의회 법령에 따라 요양원 워든 자리에 곧바로 다시 사람을 임명해야 하리라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었다. 그에게 무슨 선택의 여지가 남아 있으리라고 상상한 사람은 아무도 없었고, 하딩 씨 아닌 다른 누군가를 그가 임명할 수 있으리라고 잠시라도 생각한 사람은 없었다. 하딩 씨 자신도 일이 그렇게 정리되었다는 말을 들었을 때, 그 문제로 그리 마음을 쓰지도 않은 채, 자기가 그 정겨운 집과 정원으로 돌아가게 되는 것을 당연한 일로 여겼다. 그리고 그런 귀환에는 우울한, 아니 거의 가슴을 에는 구석이 적지 않을 터였지만, 그래도 그는 일이 그렇게 되는 것이 기뻤다. 딸도 아마 설득하면 함께 그리로 돌아갈 것이었다. 실제로 딸은 그러겠다고 약속하다시피 했다. 다만 그 죽어야 할 인간들 가운데 가장 위대한 자, 그 중대한 인류의 한 조각, 그 지상의 작은 신, 곧 제 아기 조니 볼드가 제 머리 위에 제 집을 이고 있어야 마땅하다는 생각을 아직 품고 있기는 했다.\n\n이 문제에 관한 하딩 씨의 마음이 그러하였으므로, 그는 프라우디 박사가 주교로 임명된 데 대해 개인적으로 각별한 관심을 느끼지 않았다. 그도 바체스터의 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로, 자기들과 생각이 다른 줄 뻔히 아는 사람이 자기들 가운데로 보내진 것을 유감스러워하기는 했다. 그러나 하딩 씨 자신은 교회 교리의 세목을 두고 완고하게 구는 사람이 아니었고, 프라우디 박사를 품위 있고 온당한 태도로 바체스터에 맞아들일 마음이 충분히 되어 있었다. 그는 구할 것도 없고 두려워할 것도 없었다. 자기 주교와 좋은 사이로 지내는 것이 도리라고 느꼈으며, 그것을 가로막을 장애가 있으리라고는 예상하지 않았다.\n\n그런 마음가짐으로 그는 주교와 그 전속 사제가 도착한 이튿날 주교관으로 인사를 드리러 갔다. 그러나 혼자 가지는 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사가 동행하겠다고 나섰고, 하딩 씨로서도 그런 면담에서 대화의 짐을 제 어깨에서 덜어 줄 동반자가 있는 것이 싫지 않았다. 취임식 자리에서 그랜틀리 박사는 주교에게 소개된 바 있었고, 하딩 씨도 그 자리에 있기는 했다. 그러나 그는 뒤로 물러나 있었으므로, 이제 처음으로 그 높으신 분에게 소개될 참이었다.\n\n부주교의 감정은 그보다 훨씬 격한 것이었다. 그는 무시당한 제 몫의 권리를 못 본 체하거나 남에게 돌아간 총애를 용서할 만한 인물이 결코 아니었다. 프라우디 박사는 그의 유노에 맞선 베누스 노릇을 하고 있는 셈이었고, 그는 그 탐나는 사과를 차지한 자와 그 모든 위성들, 곧 전속 사제며 그 밖의 것들을 상대로 끝장을 볼 때까지 싸울 각오가 되어 있었다.\n\n그렇더라도 그 침입자를 대하는 태도만큼은 오래 자리를 지킨 부주교가 새로 부임하는 주교를 대하듯 해야 마땅했다. 비국교도며 교회 개혁이며 대학 주간 평의회며 그런 따위 일들에 관한 프라우디 박사의 가증스러운 견해를 낱낱이 알고 있었음에도, 그 사람이 싫고 그 교리가 미웠음에도, 그는 주교라는 자리에는 경의를 표할 채비가 되어 있었다. 그리하여 그와 하딩 씨는 함께 주교관을 찾았다.\n\n주교님은 댁에 계셨고, 두 방문객은 익숙한 현관을 지나 선량한 노주교가 늘 앉아 있던 낯익은 방으로 안내되었다. 가구는 평가액대로 사들인 것이어서, 의자 하나 탁자 하나, 벽에 붙은 책장 하나하나, 양탄자의 무늬 한 칸 한 칸이 두 사람에게는 제 침실만큼이나 눈에 익었다. 그런데도 그들은 그 자리에서 대뜸 자기들이 그곳의 이방인임을 느꼈다. 가구는 대부분 그대로였으나 방은 딴것으로 탈바꿈해 있었다. 새 소파가 하나 들어와 있었는데, 끔찍한 사라사 천을 씌운 물건으로, 주교의 방에 어울리지 않기가 이를 데 없고 거의 불경스럽기까지 했다. 잉글랜드 국교회의 점잖은 고교회파 성직자의 서재에는 여태 한 번도 놓여 본 적이 없을 그런 소파였다. 옛 커튼도 물러났다. 하기야 그것은 빛이 바래 있었고, 본디 짙고 고왔던 루비색이 불그죽죽한 갈색으로 퇴색해 있었다. 그러나 하딩 씨는, 프라우디 부인이 지방 도시 바체스터에서 남편의 방에 쓰기에는 그만하면 되겠다고 여긴 그 야한 담황색 싸구려 모린 천보다는 옛 불그죽죽한 갈색이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했다.\n\n우리의 벗들은 프라우디 박사가 노주교의 의자에 앉아 새 앞치마 차림으로 아주 말쑥한 모습을 하고 있는 것을 보았다. 또 슬로프 씨가 예전에 부주교가 늘 서 있던 바로 그 자리, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 그럴듯한 낯빛으로 열을 올리고 있는 것도 보았다. 그런데 소파 위에는 프라우디 부인까지 있었으니, 바체스터 주교구의 온 연대기를 뒤져도 그 전례를 찾을 수 없을 파격이었다.\n\n그러나 부인은 거기 있었고, 그들로서는 그 사실을 감수하는 수밖에 없었다. 소개는 격식을 갖추어 이루어졌다. 부주교가 주교와 악수하고 하딩 씨를 소개하니, 하딩 씨는 주교가 선창자에게 베풀 만한 만큼의 인사를 받았다. 이어 주교님이 두 사람을 부인에게 소개했는데, 부주교가 먼저 부주교다운 예우를 받고, 그다음에 선창자가 한결 줄어든 격식으로 소개되었다. 그 뒤에 슬로프 씨가 스스로 나섰다. 주교가 그의 이름을 부르기는 했고 프라우디 부인도 더 큰 목소리로 그리하기는 했으나, 제 소개의 주된 짐은 슬로프 씨가 몸소 짊어졌다. 그는 그랜틀리 박사와 알고 지내게 되어 대단히 기쁘다고 했다. 부주교로서의 직무를 수행해 온 교구의 그 지역에서 부주교가 이룬 훌륭한 사업에 대해 많이 들었노라고 했다(이는 부주교가 여태 교구 전체에 걸쳐 휘둘러 온 무제한의 지배권을 일부러 못 본 체하는 말이었다). 주교님이 교구의 그 지역에서 그랜틀리 박사가 줄 수 있을 도움에 크게 기대고 계신 줄 안다고도 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 손을 쑥 내밀어 새 원수의 손을 움켜쥐고는 사정없이 축축하게 적셔 놓았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 답례로 고개를 숙이고, 뻣뻣한 얼굴로 눈썹을 찌푸린 다음, 주머니 손수건으로 손을 닦았다. 그래도 조금도 무안해하지 않고 슬로프 씨는 이제 선창자에게 눈길을 주며 하급 성직자의 등급으로 내려왔다. 그는 하딩 씨의 손을 한 번 쥐어 주었는데, 축축하기는 하되 다정한 악수였다. 그리고 이렇게 아무개 씨와, 아 그렇지, 하딩 씨와 알게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 했다. 이름을 미처 똑똑히 듣지 못했노라는 것이었다. “대성당의 선창자시지요.” 슬로프 씨가 짐작해 말했다. 하딩 씨는 제 일의 보잘것없는 영역이 그러하다고 시인했다. “교구 일도 좀 맡고 계시고요.” 슬로프 씨가 넌지시 말했다. 하딩 씨는 세인트 커스버트의 자그마한 성직을 맡고 있음을 인정했다. 그러자 슬로프 씨는 이만하면 충분히 몸을 낮추었다 싶었던지 그를 내버려 둔 채 높은 분들 사이의 대화에 끼어들었다.\n\n그 자리에는 네 사람이 있었고, 저마다 자기가 교구에서 가장 중요한 인물이라고 여기고 있었다. 그중 하나가 프라우디 부인이었으니 그 자신이라 해야 할지 그 부인 자신이라 해야 할지 모르겠으나 어쨌든 그러했다. 의견이 그렇게 갈리는 터에 넷이 사이좋게 지낼 성싶지는 않았다. 주교는 실제로 눈에 보이는 앞치마를 두르고 있었고 주로 그것에 기댔다. 그것과 제 직함에 기댔는데, 둘 다 못 본 체할 수 없는 사실이었다. 부주교는 사안을 알고 있었고 주교 노릇이라는 일이 무엇인지 정말로 이해하고 있었으니, 나머지 사람들은 그렇지 못했다. 이것이 그의 튼튼한 발판이었다. 프라우디 부인에게는 제 성별이 뒤를 받쳐 주었고 명령하는 습성이 있었으며, 그랜틀리 박사의 위압적인 얼굴과 풍채에도 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다. 슬로프 씨는 오직 제 자신과 제 배짱과 제 수완에 기댈 뿐이었으나, 그럼에도 완전히 자신만만했고, 주교와 부주교가 다 그러해 보이듯 겉치레에 그토록 기대는 약한 자들쯤은 머지않아 자기가 눌러 이기리라는 것을 의심치 않았다.\n\n“그랜틀리 박사, 바체스터에 사시오?” 부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 띠고 물었다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 자기가 도시에서 몇 마일 떨어진 제 교구 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 산다고 설명했다. 그러자 부인은 시골 방문을 다니기에 너무 먼 거리가 아니었으면 좋겠다고 했다. 그랜틀리 부인과 알고 지내게 되면 무척 기쁘겠다는 것이었다. 말들이 바체스터에 도착하는 대로 되도록 이른 기회에 찾아뵙겠노라 했다. 자기들 말은 지금 런던에 있는데, 주교가 며칠 뒤 다시 상경해야 할 터이므로 말들이 당장 내려오지는 않는다고 했다. 주교가 요즈음 「대학 개선 위원회」 일로 부름을 많이 받고 있는 것은 그랜틀리 박사도 필시 알고 있으리라고 했다. 실로 최종 보고서를 이제 작성해야 하는 마당이라 위원회가 그 없이는 제대로 굴러갈 수 없다는 것이었다. 게다가 주교는 「공장 도시 조석 주일학교 협회」의 계획안도 마련해야 하는데, 그가 그 후원자인지 회장인지 이사인지를 맡고 있다고 했다. 그러니 말들이 지금은 바체스터로 내려오지 않을 테지만, 언제고 말들이 내려오기만 하면 되도록 이른 기회에 플럼스테드 에피스코피로 찾아가겠다고, 시골 방문을 다니기에 너무 먼 거리만 아니라면 그러겠다고 했다.\n\n부주교는 다섯 번째로 절을 했다. 말 이야기가 나올 때마다 한 번씩 절을 했던 것이다. 그러고는 그랜틀리 부인이 조만간 주교관으로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리도록 하겠다고 약속했다. 프라우디 부인은 그러면 더없이 기쁘겠노라고 했다. 그랜틀리 부인에게 말이 있는지 확실히 알지 못해 차마 청하지 못했노라고, 게다가 거리가 어쩌면 운운하는 것이었다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 다시 고개를 숙였으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 프라우디 집안 전체의 재산을 하나하나 죄다 사들여 그것을 선물로 돌려주고도 손실을 별로 느끼지 않을 만한 사람이었고, 혼인한 날부터 오로지 아내만 쓰도록 따로 말 두 필을 두고 있었다. 반면 프라우디 부인은 여태껏 사교철에는 다달이 얼마씩을 치르고 빌린 말로 런던 거리를 돌아다녔고, 그 밖의 철에는 걷거나 삯마차집에서 말쑥한 이륜마차를 세내어 그럭저럭 다녔다.\n\n“부주교 관구의 안식일 학교 운영은 대체로 꽤 잘되고 있나요?” 슬로프 씨가 물었다.\n\n“안식일 학교라!” 부주교가 놀란 척하며 되뇌었다. “글쎄올시다, 나로서는 알 수가 없소. 그건 주로 교구 목사의 아내와 딸들에게 달린 일이지요. 플럼스테드에는 없소.”\n\n이는 부주교로서는 거의 거짓말에 가까운 말이었으니, 그랜틀리 부인은 아주 훌륭한 학교를 하나 두고 있기 때문이다. 하기야 그것이 오로지 주일학교인 것은 아니고 그렇게 불리지도 않지만, 그 모범적인 부인은 예배 전 한 시간씩 늘 그리로 나가 아이들이 교리문답 외는 것을 듣고, 아이들이 손을 씻고 신발 끈을 매어 예배에 갈 만큼 깨끗하고 단정한지를 살핀다. 그리고 두 딸 그리젤과 플로린다가 토요일 오후에 구워 낸 큼직한 빵을 바구니에 담아 그리로 나르고, 특별히 벌을 받고 있는 아이가 아니면 모두에게 나누어 준다. 그 빵은 예배가 끝난 뒤 흡족한 마음으로 집에 실려 가서, 반으로 갈라 구운 다음 차 시간에 따뜻하게 먹힌다. 플럼스테드의 아이들은 제 존경하는 목사가 제 교구에는 주일학교가 없다고 말하는 것을 들었다면 정말이지 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 것이다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 그저 큼직한 두 눈을 더 크게 뜨고 어깨를 조금 으쓱했을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그는 제가 애지중지하는 계획을 포기할 마음은 없었다.\n\n“이곳에는 안식일에 다니는 여행이 아주 많은 모양이더군요.” 그가 말했다. “『브래드쇼』를 보니 안식일마다 들어오는 기차가 세 편, 나가는 기차가 세 편 있습니다. 회사가 그것을 거두어들이도록 할 방도가 없을까요? 그랜틀리 박사님, 조금만 힘을 쓰면 그 악을 줄일 수 있으리라고 보지 않으십니까?”\n\n“이사가 아니어서 나로서는 정말 알 수가 없소. 다만 당신이 승객을 거두어들일 수 있다면 회사도 필시 기차를 거두어들일 것이오.” 박사가 말했다. “그저 배당금의 문제일 뿐이니까.”\n\n“하지만 그랜틀리 박사,” 부인이 말했다. “정말이지 우리는 그것을 달리 보아야 하지 않겠소. 이를테면 박사와 나 같은 처지에 있는 사람은 말이오. 그토록 통탄할 죄를 억누르기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 해야 하지 않겠소. 하딩 씨는 그렇게 생각지 않으시오?” 그러면서 부인은 말없이 딱한 낯으로 앉아 있는 선창자 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n하딩 씨는 짐꾼이며 화부며 차장이며 제동수며 전철수며 모두가 예배에 갈 기회를 가져야 마땅하다고 생각한다고, 그리고 그들 모두가 그 기회를 누리고 있기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n“하지만 정말이지, 정말이지,” 프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다. “그것만으로는 부족하지 않소. 그것으로는 안식일을 지키는 일이, 그저 마땅할 뿐 아니라 없어서는 안 될 것이라고 우리가 배운 그 준수가 확보되지 않을 것이오. 정말이지—”\n\n무슨 일이 있어도 그랜틀리 박사는 프라우디 부인과, 또한 슬로프 씨와 교리의 한 조목을 두고 논설을 벌이도록 끌려 들어갈 사람이 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 별다른 예의도 차리지 않은 채 소파 쪽으로 등을 돌리고는, 프라우디 박사께서 주교관 수리가 뜻에 맞게 되었다고 여기시기를 바란다는 말을 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n“그렇소, 그렇소.” 주교님이 말했다. 대체로 그렇게 여긴다고, 대체로 불평할 만한 거리가 그리 있는 줄 모르겠다고, 다만 건축가가 어쩌면— 그러나 그의 분신인 슬로프 씨가 어느새 주교의 의자 곁으로 슬금슬금 다가와서는, 주교님이 그 애매한 말을 끝맺도록 놓아두지 않았다.\n\n“부주교님, 한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 점이 있습니다. 주교님이 저더러 부지를 한 바퀴 둘러보라 하시기에 살펴보았더니, 두 번째 마구간의 칸이 온전하지 않더군요.”\n\n“아니—거기 말 열두 필은 세워 둘 자리가 있지 않소.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“그럴지도 모르지요.” 상대가 말했다. “실은 저도 그 점은 의심치 않습니다. 그렇지만 아시다시피 손님들은 흔히 자리를 아주 많이 차지하는 법이지요. 주교님 친척들 중에는 늘 제 말을 끌고 오는 분이 아주 많거든요.”\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 적어도 본디 마구간 건물의 규모가 허락하는 한도 안에서는 친척들의 말을 위해 마땅한 자리를 마련하겠다고 약속했다. 자기가 직접 건축가와 이야기하겠다는 것이었다.\n\n“그리고 마차 차고 말인데요, 그랜틀리 박사님.” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “큰 차고에는 두 번째 마차를 들일 자리가 정말이지 거의 없고, 작은 쪽은 물론 한 대밖에 못 들어갑니다.”\n\n“그리고 가스도요.” 부인이 끼어들었다. “집 안에 가스가 들어오지 않소. 부엌과 복도 말고는 아무 데도 없단 말이오. 정말이지 주교관이라면 가스관을, 온수관도 함께 집 전체에 놓았어야 하지 않겠소. 1층 위로는 어디에도 더운물이 나오지 않소. 부엌에서 주전자에 담아 나르지 않고도 침실에서 더운물을 얻을 방도가 정말이지 있어야 하지 않겠소.”\n\n주교는 온수관이 있어야 한다는 데 확고한 견해를 지니고 있었다. 더운물은 주교관의 안락에 매우 긴요한 것이었다. 실로 그것은 웬만한 신사의 집이라면 갖추어야 할 필수품이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 정원 담의 갓돌이 여러 군데 성치 않더라고 말했다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 하인들 방에서 큼직한 구멍을 하나 발견했는데, 보아하니 쥐가 낸 것이었다.\n\n주교는 쥐라면 몸서리가 쳐진다고 밝혔다. 이 세상에 쥐만큼 자기가 싫어하는 것은 없는 줄로 안다는 것이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 또한 헛간들의 자물쇠가 매우 부실하다는 것도 보아 두었다. 석탄광과 장작광을 예로 들 수 있다고 했다.\n\n프라우디 부인도 하인들 침실 문에 달린 자물쇠가 그에 못지않게 나쁜 상태임을 보아 두었다. 실로 온 집 안의 자물쇠가 죄다 구식이고 쓸 만하지 않았다.\n\n주교는 좋은 자물쇠에 달린 바가 크고 열쇠에 달린 바도 그에 못지않게 크다고 생각했다. 특히 열쇠 홈이 조금이라도 뒤틀려 있으면 흔히 열쇠 쪽에 탈이 있더라는 것을 그는 눈여겨보아 두었다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 불평 목록을 계속 읊어 나가려는데, 부주교가 다소 큰 소리로 그의 말을 끊었다. 부주교는 그런 일은 교구 건축가에게, 아니 그보다는 그 밑의 십장에게 말해야 할 사안이며, 자기 그랜틀리 박사가 주교관이 편안한지를 물은 것은 그저 예의를 차린 것일 뿐이라는 뜻을 그럭저럭 알아듣게 설명해 냈다. 그러나 잘못된 데가 그토록 많이 드러났다니 안됐다고 말한 다음, 그는 빠져나가려고 의자에서 일어났다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 주교관의 허물어진 데를 하나하나 헤아리는 일에 용케 거들면서도, 그렇다고 하딩 씨를 붙들고 있던 손을 놓지도 않았고 안식일의 오락이 얼마나 죄스러운가를 두고 그를 심문하는 일도 그치지 않았다. 부인은 하딩 씨의 그 헌신적인 머리 위로 “정말이지, 정말이지”를 몇 번이고 되풀이해 던졌고, 그 신사는 그 공격을 좀처럼 막아 내지 못했다.\n\n그는 여태껏 이런 성가심을 당해 본 적이 없었다. 지금까지 부인네들은 종교 문제로 그에게 의견을 구할 때면 그가 말하고자 하는 바를 얼마간 공손히 들었고, 생각이 다르더라도 잠자코 다를 뿐이었다. 그런데 프라우디 부인은 그를 심문하고 나서 훈계를 했다. “너나 네 아들이나 네 딸이나 네 남종이나 네 여종이나.” 부인은 하딩 씨가 그 말씀을 잊기라도 한 듯이, 힘주어, 그것도 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 이렇게 외웠다. 부인은 제가 아끼는 그 계명을 인용하면서, 벌을 내리겠다고 으르기라도 하듯 그를 향해 손가락을 흔들었다. 그러고는 안식일에 여행하는 것이 가증한 일이요 신성을 더럽히는 일이라고 생각지 않느냐고 딱 잘라 대답하라며 그를 몰아세웠다.\n\n하딩 씨는 평생 이토록 궁지에 몰려 본 적이 없었다. 저보다 여러 해 연상인 신사이자 성직자에게 감히 그런 식으로 말하는 부인을 꾸짖어야 마땅하다고 느꼈으나, 주교의 부인을, 그것도 주교가 보는 앞에서, 주교관에 처음 찾아온 날에 나무란다는 생각에는 몸이 움츠러들었다. 게다가 사실대로 말하자면 그는 그 부인이 다소 무섭기도 했다. 부인은 그가 말없이 넋을 놓고 앉아 있는 것을 보고도 공격을 조금도 늦추지 않았다.\n\n“하딩 씨,” 부인이 천천히 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. “당신이 안식일 여행을 옳게 여긴다고 내가 생각하도록 내버려 두지는 않으시겠지요.” 그러면서 부인은 이루 말할 수 없는 뜻을 담은 눈길로 그의 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n이쯤 되니 견딜 재간이 없었다. 이제 슬로프 씨가 그를 보고 있었고, 주교도 그러했으며, 방 저쪽에서 작별 인사를 마친 부주교마저 그러했기 때문이다. 그리하여 하딩 씨도 자리에서 일어나 프라우디 부인에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “언제 일요일에 세인트 커스버트로 오시면 제가 그 주제로 설교를 한 편 해 드리지요.”\n\n그리하여 부주교와 선창자는 부인에게 깊이 절하고 주교와 악수한 다음, 슬로프 씨한테서는 저마다 되도록 무난한 방법으로 빠져나오며 그 자리를 떠났다. 하딩 씨는 또 한 번 봉변을 당했으나, 그랜틀리 박사는 세상의 그 어떤 사정으로도 두 번 다시는 그 더럽고 불결한 짐승의 앞발을 만지지 않으리라고 마음속 깊은 곳에서 굳게 맹세했다.\n\n그리고 이제, 내게 위대한 시인의 붓이 있다면 나는 부주교의 그 고귀한 분노를 서사시로 노래하리라. 주교관 계단은 널따란 자갈밭으로 내려가고, 거기서 작은 문 하나가 거리로 열리는데, 대성당 경내로 통하는 지붕 덮인 문간에서 아주 가깝다. 주교관 문에서 난 길은 왼쪽으로 꺾여 널찍한 정원을 지나, 대성당에서 반 마일 떨어진 런던 가도에서 끝난다.\n\n두 사람이 그 작은 문을 지나 경내에 들어설 때까지 어느 쪽도 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 선창자는 동행의 얼굴에서 회오리바람이 몰아치리라는 것을 똑똑히 보았고, 그 자신도 그것을 말리고 싶은 마음이 없었다. 천성이 부주교보다 훨씬 덜 성마른 사람이었건만 그마저도 화가 나 있었다. 그 온화하고 정중한 사람마저도 도무지 정중하달 수 없는 말로 제 심정을 털어놓고 싶어졌던 것이다.\n\n# 제6장. 전쟁\n\n“맙소사!” 부주교는 대성당 경내의 자갈길에 발을 내디디며 이렇게 외쳤다. 한 손으로는 모자를 들어 올리고 다른 손으로는 이제 희끗희끗해진 머리칼을 다소 거칠게 쓸어 넘겼다. 들어 올린 비버 모자에서는 마치 분노의 구름과도 같은 김이 피어올랐으니, 노여움의 안전판이 열려 눈에 보이는 증기를 뿜어낸 덕에 실제 폭발과 있음직한 졸중이 면해진 셈이었다. “맙소사!” 그러고서 부주교는 대성당 탑의 잿빛 첨두를 올려다보며, 바체스터의 그 숱한 주교들이 저지른 일을 내려다보아 온 그 말 없는 산 증인에게 소리 없이 호소했다.\n\n“저 슬로프 씨라는 사람은 아무래도 끝내 좋아지지 않을 것 같네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“좋아진다니요!” 부주교는 목소리에 힘을 더 싣기 위해 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 고함쳤다. “좋아진다니요!” 경내의 까마귀들이 모두 그렇다고 까악까악 화답했다. 탑의 낡은 종들도 시각을 알리며 그 말을 되울렸고, 둥지에서 날아 나온 제비들도 말없이 같은 의견을 표했다. 슬로프 씨를 좋아한다니! 아니, 바체스터에서 나고 자란 살아 있는 것치고 슬로프 씨를 좋아할 만한 것은 도무지 있을 법하지 않았다.\n\n“프라우디 부인도 마찬가지고 말이야.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n이 대목에서 부주교는 그만 자제를 잃고 말았다. 나는 그의 본을 따르지 않을 것이며, 방금 이름이 나온 그 부인에 대해 그가 제 심경을 표현하며 쓴 말을 그대로 옮겨 적어 독자를 놀라게 하지도 않을 것이다. 까마귀들과 시계 종의 마지막 여운은 그만큼 조심스럽지 못하여, 그 심히 온당치 못한 감탄사를 화답하는 메아리로 되풀이했다. 부주교는 다시 모자를 들어 올렸고, 또 한 번 이로운 증기 배출이 이루어졌다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 선창자는 바체스터 주교의 부인이 다른 곳도 아닌 대성당 경내에서, 그것도 그 대성당 부주교의 입으로 그렇게 불렸다는 사실을 실감해 보려 애썼다. 그러나 아무리 해도 실감이 나지 않았다.\n\n“주교는 꽤 조용한 사람인 듯하던데.” 스스로 제 실패를 인정하고 나서 하딩 씨가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n“멍청이지요!” 박사가 외쳤다. 그는 당장으로서는 그렇게 발작하듯 한마디씩 내뱉는 것 이상은 해낼 수 없는 상태였다.\n\n“글쎄, 그리 명민해 보이지는 않더군.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “그런데도 늘 영리한 사람이라는 평판을 들어 오지 않았나. 아마 조심스러운 성품이라 속을 그리 시원하게 드러내지 않는 것이겠지.”\n\n바체스터의 새 주교는 그랜틀리 박사의 눈에 이미 너무나 하찮은 물건이어서, 그 인격을 논하는 일조차 그로서는 몸을 낮추는 짓이었다. 그는 남들이 놀리는 꼭두각시요, 앞치마와 삽 모양 모자를 씌워 옥좌든 어디든 앉혀 놓고 남들이 마음대로 줄을 당겨 흔드는 밀랍 인형에 지나지 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 프라우디 박사를 입에 올릴 만큼 자신을 낮출 생각이 없었으나, 그 집안의 다른 식구들, 곧 주교 각하를 말하자면 상자에 담아 내려보내고 이제 제 뜻대로 그 줄을 놀리려 드는 저 보좌 주교들에 대해서는 이야기하지 않을 수 없으리라는 것을 알아차렸다. 이것만으로도 부주교에게는 지독한 곤혹이었다. 전속 사제 따위는 무시하고 주교와 싸울 수만 있다면, 적어도 그런 싸움에는 품위를 잃을 일이 없었다. 여왕께서 누구를 바체스터 주교로 삼으시든 좋다. 사람이든 심지어 원숭이든, 일단 주교가 된 이상 제 스스로 싸우기만 한다면 존중할 만한 적수인 것이다. 그러나 슬로프 씨 같은 자가 적수랍시고 앞에 내세워졌을 때, 그랜틀리 박사 같은 사람은 대체 어찌해야 한단 말인가?\n\n만일 그가, 우리의 부주교가 그 싸움을 거부한다면, 슬로프 씨는 전장을 의기양양하게 활보하며 바체스터 교구를 제 발밑에 두게 될 것이었다.\n\n반대로 부주교가, 저 새 꼭두각시 주교가 자기 앞에 적으로 내세운 그 사람을 적으로 받아들인다면, 그는 슬로프 씨를 어느 정도 자기와 같은 지반에 선 존재로 여겨 슬로프 씨에 대해 말하고, 슬로프 씨에 대해 쓰고, 매사에 슬로프 씨와 상대해야 할 터였다. 슬로프 씨를 만나야 하고, 그리하여— 아이고! 생각만 해도 구역질이 났다. 그는 도저히 슬로프 씨와 상종할 마음을 낼 수 없었다.\n\n“내 평생 눈으로 본 것 중에 그렇게 철저히 짐승 같은 물건은 없었습니다.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“누구 말인가— 주교?” 상대가 순진하게 물었다.\n\n“주교라니요! 아닙니다, 주교 얘기가 아니에요. 대체 어떻게 저런 물건이 성직을 받았는지! 요즘은 아무나 안수해 준다는 걸 저도 압니다만, 저자는 교회에 몸담은 지 10년이 됐어요. 10년 전만 해도 그래도 좀 조심하는 편이었단 말입니다.”\n\n“아, 그러니까 자네는 슬로프 씨를 두고 하는 말이로군.”\n\n“저자만큼 신사와 거리가 먼 짐승을 보신 적이 있으십니까?” 그랜틀리 박사가 물었다.\n\n“나도 그 사람이 딱히 마음에 든다는 기분은 들지 않더라고 해야겠네.”\n\n“좋아진다니요!” 박사가 또 한 번 고함쳤고, 동의하는 까마귀들이 다시 메아리로 까악거렸다. “물론 좋아하실 리가 없지요. 좋고 싫고의 문제가 아닙니다. 문제는 저자를 어떻게 하느냐는 겁니다.”\n\n“어떻게 하다니?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그렇습니다, 저자를 어떻게 하느냐는 말입니다. 저자를 어떻게 대해야 합니까? 저자는 저기 있고, 저기 눌러앉을 겁니다. 저 주교관에 이미 발을 들여놓았으니 쫓겨나기 전에는 결코 발을 빼지 않을 거예요. 우리가 저자를 어떻게 떨쳐 냅니까?”\n\n“저 사람이 우리에게 그리 큰 해를 끼칠 수야 있겠나.”\n\n“해를 끼치지 못한다니요! 글쎄요, 한 달만 지나 보시면 생각이 달라지실 겁니다. 저자가 요양원 자리를 차지하고 앉는다면 뭐라 하시겠습니까? 그건 해가 아닙니까?”\n\n하딩 씨는 한동안 생각에 잠겼다가, 새 주교가 슬로프 씨를 요양원에 앉히지는 않으리라고 본다고 말했다.\n\n“거기 앉히지 않으면 다른 데, 그만큼이나 고약한 자리에 앉힐 겁니다. 제 말씀을 들으십시오. 저자야말로 실질적으로 바체스터의 주교가 될 것입니다!” 그러고서 그랜틀리 박사는 다시 모자를 들어 올리고 생각에 잠긴 채 서글피 머리를 쓸었다.\n\n“뻔뻔한 악당 같으니!” 그는 잠시 뒤에 말을 이었다. “감히 나를 붙들고 교구의 주일학교니 주일 여행이니 하는 것을 캐물어 대다니. 저렇게 순전히 뻔뻔하기만 한 인간은 내 평생 처음 봅니다. 저자는 우리를 안수받으러 온 지원자 둘쯤으로 여겼던 게 분명해요!”\n\n“나는 정말이지 둘 중에서는 프라우디 부인 쪽이 더하다고 생각했네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“여자가 무례하게 굴면 그저 참고 앞으로 그 앞을 피하는 수밖에 없지요. 하지만 슬로프 씨를 참아 줄 생각은 없습니다. ‘안식일 여행!’” 그러고서 박사는 제가 그토록 싫어하는 그 사람 특유의 느릿한 말투를 흉내 내려 애썼다. “‘안식일 여행!’ 저런 부류가 영국 교회를 망치고 성직자라는 직분을 우습게 만드는 자들입니다. 우리가 두려워할 것은 비국교도도 아니고 교황파도 아니에요. 우리 사이로 꿈틀꿈틀 기어들어 오는, 경건한 척 지껄이는 저 천박한 위선자 무리입니다. 정해진 원칙도 없고 신앙이나 교리에 대한 기준도 없이, 저 작자가 ‘안식일 여행’을 들고나온 것처럼 그때그때 사람들 입에 오르내리는 구호나 주워 드는 자들 말입니다.”\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 그 물음을 다시 소리 내어 되풀이하지는 않았으나, 속으로는 끊임없이 되뇌고 있었다. 슬로프 씨를 어떻게 해야 하는가? 어떻게 하면 세상 앞에서 드러내 놓고, 자기가 그런 인간을 철저히 못마땅해하며 혐오한다는 것을 보일 수 있는가?\n\n지금까지 바체스터는 교리상의 극단적 엄격주의에 물들지 않고 지내 왔다. 시내와 인근의 성직자들은 고교회파의 원칙과 특권과 대권을 밀어 올리는 데는 매우 적극적이었으나, 다소 헐겁게 퓨지파 관행이라 불리는 경향에 스스로를 내맡긴 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그들은 모두 선대가 그러했듯 검은 가운을 입고 설교했고, 평범한 검은 모직 조끼를 입었으며, 제단에는 촛불을 켜 놓지도 세워 두지도 않았다. 사사로이 무릎을 굽히는 일도 없었고, 지난 백 년 동안 통용되어 온 정도의 의례에 스스로를 가두는 것으로 만족했다. 예배는 각 교구 교회에서 단정하고 얌전하게 낭독되었고, 영창은 대성당에 국한되었으며, 영송이라는 기술은 알려져 있지 않았다. 옥스퍼드에서 곧장 플럼스테드로 부목사로 온 어느 젊은이가 두세 번의 주일이 지난 뒤 어설프게 그것을 시도해 보았다가, 회중 가운데 가난한 축을 어리둥절하게 만든 일이 있기는 했다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 자리에 없었으나, 이 문제에 대해 나름의 견해를 지닌 그랜틀리 부인이 예배가 끝나기 무섭게 그 젊은 신사가 혹 몸이 편찮은 것은 아니기를 바란다고 말하고, 인후통에 좋다는 온갖 약재를 보내 드리겠노라 청했다. 그 뒤로 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서 영송은 다시 들리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이제 부주교는 전면적인 반대를 위한 강경한 조치를 궁리하기 시작했다. 프라우디 박사와 그 패거리는 영국 교회 성직자 가운데 있을 수 있는 가장 낮은 축에 속했고, 따라서 그랜틀리 박사인 자기는 마땅히 가장 높은 축이 되어야 했다. 프라우디 박사가 모든 형식과 의례를 없애려 든다면, 그랜틀리 박사는 그것들을 늘려야 할 절박한 필요를 느꼈다. 프라우디 박사가 교회에서 집합적 권위와 통치를 죄다 앗아 가는 데 동의한다면, 그랜틀리 박사는 성직자 회의의 온전한 권한과 그 옛 특권의 부활을 위해 떨쳐 일어날 것이었다.\n\n그 자신이 예배를 영송할 수 없다는 것은 사실이었으나, 그 비법에 잘 훈련된 신사다운 부목사들의 협력이라면 얼마든지 얻어 낼 수 있었다. 제 옷차림을 기꺼이 바꿀 생각은 없었지만, 가장 긴 프록코트에 목까지 올라오는 실크 조끼를 입은 젊은 성직자들로 바체스터를 채울 수는 있었다. 성호를 긋거나 실체적 임재를 주창할 준비까지는 분명 되어 있지 않았으나, 거기까지 가지 않고도 채택하기만 하면 프라우디 박사나 슬로프 씨 같은 자들에 대한 반감을 뚜렷이 드러낼 수 있는 여러 관행이 있었다.\n\n하딩 씨와 함께 경내를 오르내리며 그의 머릿속으로 이 모든 것이 지나갔다. 전쟁, 전쟁, 서로를 죽이는 전쟁이 그의 가슴속에 있었다. 자기와 슬로프 씨로 말하자면 바체스터 시에 관한 한 둘 중 하나는 절멸되어야 한다고 그는 느꼈고, 자기가 딛고 설 땅이 한 치도 남지 않을 때까지는 물러설 생각이 없었다. 그는 여전히 바체스터를 슬로프 씨가 붙어 있지 못할 만큼 뜨거운 곳으로 만들 수 있으리라 자부했으며, 제 힘이 닿는 한 그 결말을 기어이 이루어 내는 것을 가로막을 심약함 따위는 그에게 없었다.\n\n“수전은 아무래도 주교관에 인사를 가야겠지.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“예, 가긴 갈 겁니다. 하지만 딱 한 번, 그 한 번뿐입니다. ‘말들’께서는 플럼스테드까지 나오시기가 그리 편치 않으실 테니, 그 한 번만 치르고 나면 그것으로 흐지부지되겠지요.”\n\n“엘리너는 갈 필요 없겠지. 엘리너가 프라우디 부인과 잘 지낼 성싶지가 않네.”\n\n“세상에 조금도 그럴 필요 없습니다.” 부주교가 대답했다. 제 아내에게는 꼭 필요한 예절이 존 볼드의 미망인에게는 전혀 구속력이 없을 수도 있다는 생각이 없지 않았던 것이다. “내키지 않는다면 그렇게 해야 할 이유가 이 세상에 털끝만큼도 없지요. 저로 말하자면, 반듯한 젊은 여자라면 누구든 그런 사내와 한방에 있는 곤욕을 치를 까닭이 없다고 봅니다.”\n\n그리하여 두 성직자는 헤어졌다. 하딩 씨는 딸의 집으로 갔고, 부주교는 제 사륜마차의 호젓함을 찾아 들어갔다.\n\n주교관의 새 거주자들이 손님들에 대해 품은 의견도 손님들이 그들에 대해 표한 의견보다 나을 것이 없었다. 그랜틀리 박사만큼 험한 말을 쓰지는 않았으나 그들도 그만큼이나 개인적인 반감을 느꼈고, 한바탕 싸움이 벌어지리라는 것, 그랜틀리주의가 우세한 한 바체스터에는 프라우디주의가 들어설 자리가 거의 없다는 것을 그 못지않게 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n실은 슬로프 씨가 이미 부주교보다 더 잘 짜인 전략 체계를, 더 정확하게 그어 놓은 적대 행동의 노선을 가슴속에 품고 있지 않았는지 의심해 볼 만하다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 사람이 밉다는 것을 알았기에 싸우려 들었다. 슬로프 씨는 그 사람과 싸워야 할 필요를 내다보았기에 미워하기로 미리 정해 두었다. 바체스터에 들어서기에 앞서 carte du pays를 처음 살폈을 때, 그에게는 부주교를 회유하여 구슬리고 추어올려 굴복시키고, 용기 대신 꾀로 우위를 차지한다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 조금 알아보고 나니, 그랜틀리 박사 같은 사람을 슬로프 씨가 취할 방식으로 끌어들이기에는 제 꾀가 아무 소용이 없으리라는 확신이 서기에 족했고, 그리하여 그는 용기 쪽으로 물러서기로 작정했다. 그는 그랜틀리 박사와 그 추종자 전부를 상대로 한 공공연한 싸움이 제 처지에 필연이라는 것을 대번에 알아보았고, 어떻게 하면 가장 효과적으로 남의 심기를 건드릴 수 있을지 신중하게 계획을 세웠다.\n\n부임한 지 얼마 되지 않아 주교는 수석 사제에게, 마침 당번을 맡은 참사원의 허락을 얻어 다음 주일에 제 전속 사제가 대성당에서 설교하게 하겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 그때 당번 참사원은 다름 아닌 존귀하신 베시 스탠호프 목사 박사였는데, 그는 이 무렵 코메 호수 기슭에서 그를 그토록 유명하게 만든 저 유례없는 나비 수집을 늘리느라 몹시 분주했다. 아니, 나비니 무어니 하는 여름날의 사정만 없었더라면 당번을 서고 있었을 터였다. 그리하여 그를 대신해 강단에 설 예정이던 성가대 사제는 슬로프 씨가 제 몫의 일을 대신 해 주는 데 조금도 이의가 없었다.\n\n그리하여 슬로프 씨는 설교를 했고, 설교자라는 것이 남들이 귀 기울여 들어 주는 데서 만족을 얻을 수 있는 존재라면 슬로프 씨는 흡족했어야 마땅했다. 나로서는 그가 실제로 흡족했으며, 강단에 올라설 때 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 해냈다는 확신을 품고 강단을 내려왔으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다.\n\n이날 새 주교는 자기에게 배정된 옥좌에 처음으로 앉았다. 새 진홍색 방석과 휘장이 마련되었고 금박 테두리와 술도 새것이었다. 성가대석 지붕까지 절반쯤 치솟은 수많은 기괴한 첨두 장식을 거느린 옥좌의 낡은 참나무 조각은 씻기고 먼지가 털리고 문질러져 온통 아주 말끔해 보였다. 아! 행복하던 어린 시절, 제단 앞의 그 낮은 걸상에 앉아, 저 나무 탑들 사이로 어떻게 길을 더듬어 올라가야 맨 꼭대기 첨두까지 무사히 기어오를 수 있을까 궁리하며 설교의 지루함을 달랜 적이 얼마나 많았던가!\n\n바체스터 사람들이 죄다 슬로프 씨의 설교를 들으러 왔다. 그게 아니면 새 주교를 구경하러 왔다. 시내에서 가장 좋은 보닛이 모두 거기 있었고, 게다가 가장 윤이 나는 성직자용 모자도 모두 거기 있었다. 참사원 좌석은 하나도 비지 않고 제 임자를 찾았으니, 참사회원 몇몇이 이탈리아니 어디니에 나가 있기는 했어도 그 자리는 이 기회에 바체스터로 몰려든 동료들로 채워졌다. 수석 사제도 나왔는데, 그는 이제 나이가 너무 많아 실은 제자리를 자주 지키지도 못하는 노인이었다. 부주교도 나왔다. 학무 참사원과 재무 참사원, 선창자와 여러 참사원과 하급 참사원들, 그리고 성가대의 평신도 단원 전원도 새 주교를 마땅한 가락과 거룩한 환영의 조화로운 표현으로 맞아들일 채비를 하고 나와 있었다.\n\n예배는 확실히 매우 훌륭하게 치러졌다. 바체스터에서는 늘 그러했으니, 성가대의 음악 교육이 좋았고 목소리들이 세심하게 골라졌기 때문이다. 시편은 아름답게 영창되었고, 테 데움은 장엄하게 불렸으며, 기도문은 지금도 바체스터에서는 들을 수 있으나 내 취향이 옳다면 다른 어디서도 들을 수 없는 방식으로 봉헌되었다. 바체스터 대성당의 이 기도문은 오래전부터 하딩 씨의 기량과 목소리가 바쳐지는 특별한 소임이었다. 대체로 청중이 붐비면 연주하는 이도 잘하는 법이라, 하딩 씨는 스스로 특별히 애를 썼다고 여기지 않았음에도 아마 평소의 수준을 조금 웃돌았을 것이다. 다들 제 최선을 다하고 있었으니 그가 동료들과 겨루려 한 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 그렇게 예배가 이어졌고, 마침내 슬로프 씨가 강단에 올랐다.\n\n그는 영적인 목자이자 인도자에게 필요한 처신에 관해 성 바울이 디모데에게 준 훈계 가운데 한 구절을 본문으로 골랐고, 바체스터의 선량한 성직자들이 한 수 배우게 되리라는 것이 대번에 분명해졌다.\n\n“너는 진리의 말씀을 옳게 분별하며 부끄러울 것이 없는 일꾼으로 인정된 자로 자신을 하나님 앞에 드리기를 힘쓰라.” 이것이 그가 택한 본문의 말씀이었고, 그런 자리에서 그런 주제를 들고나왔으니 그런 설교자가 그런 청중에게 경청되었으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다. 그는 숨죽인 주목 속에서, 그리고 적잖은 놀라움 속에서 끝까지 경청되었다. 그가 설교를 시작하기 전에 바체스터에서 슬로프 씨를 어떻게 여겼든 간에, 설교가 끝났을 때 그를 바보나 겁쟁이로 오인할 수 있는 청중은 하나도 없었다.\n\n내가 설교를 희화화하거나, 하다못해 그 표현을 소설 지면에 옮겨 적는 것은 온당치 못한 일이리라. 내가 쓰는 인물들의 성격을 그려 내려 하다 보니 어느 정도까지는 성스러운 것들을 입에 올릴 수밖에 없다. 그러나 더러는 내가 성직에 마땅한 경외를 다 갖추지 못했다고 여길지 모르나, 내가 강단을 조롱한다고는 생각지 말아 주기를 바란다. 나는 가르치는 자들의 무오류성을 의심할 수는 있으나, 그렇다고 가르쳐야 할 것 자체를 의심한다는 비난을 받지는 않기를 바란다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 설교를 시작하면서, 자기는 보잘것없는 사람이나 맞은편에 앉으신 저 걸출한 신학자의 대변자로서 이 자리에 섰다는 뜻을 모호하게 내비치는 데서 적잖은 수완을 보였다. 그렇게 운을 떼어 놓은 다음 그는, 이제 그 고위 성직자의 관할 아래 놓인 성직자들에게서 그분이 보고 기뻐하실 만한 처신이 어떤 것인지를 아주 정확하게 정의해 보였다. 그가 힘주어 짚은 특정 항목들이 하필 이 교구 성직자들에게 가장 역겨운 것들이자 그들의 관행과 견해에 가장 어긋나는 것들이었다는 것, 그리고 고교회파 사제들에게, 곧 요즘 망측하게도 “고답건조파”라 불리는 그 당파에게 언제나 소중했던 그 온갖 독특한 습속과 특권이 죄다 조롱당하고 매도당하고 저주받았다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠다. 그런데 바체스터 교구의 성직자들은 하나같이 고답건조파다.\n\n그리하여 제 나름의 견해에 따라, 성직자가 어떻게 하여야 부끄러울 것 없는 일꾼으로 하나님 앞에 인정된 자로 자신을 드릴 수 있는지를 설명하고 나서, 그는 진리의 말씀을 어떻게 옳게 분별할 것인지를 설명하는 데로 나아갔다. 그리고 여기서 그는 이 문제를 다소 좁게 보고 논거를 멀리서 끌어왔다. 그의 목적은 온갖 의례적인 발성 방식에 대한 제 혐오를 표하고, 말의 뜻이 아니라 소리에 의해 불러일으켜지는 종교적 감정이라면 무엇이든 깎아내리는 것, 요컨대 대성당의 관행을 모욕하는 것이었다. 성 바울이 진리의 말씀을 옳게 분별하라 하지 않고 옳게 발음하라 했더라면 설교의 이 대목이 더 걸맞았겠으나, 이 설교자의 당면 목적은 성 바울의 교리가 아니라 슬로프 씨의 교리를 설교하는 데 있었고, 그는 제법 솜씨 있게 본문을 필요한 만큼 비틀어 놓았다.\n\n대성당 강단에서 설교하면서 대성당 예배에서 영창을 폐지해야 한다고 대놓고 말할 수는 없었다. 그런 주장을 했다가는 과녁을 훌쩍 넘겨 스스로 우스워지고 청중을 기쁘게 할 뿐이었으리라. 그러나 교구 교회의 영송 관행에 대해서라면 얼마든지 준엄한 규탄을 곁들여 언급할 수 있었고 실제로 그렇게 했다. 이 교구에서는 그런 관행이 거의 알려져 있지도 않았는데 말이다. 그러고서 그는 방금 들은 저 아름다운 예배에서 음악이 뜻보다 지나치게 우세하다는 주장으로 넘어갔다. 그는 이렇게 말했다. 우리 조상들의 관행을 하루아침에 버릴 수 없다는 것은 저도 알고 있는 바입니다. 그리하면 연로하신 분들의 마음이 상할 것이요, 존경할 만한 이들의 심경이 충격을 받을 것입니다. 저도 알거니와, 사려의 그릇이 넉넉지 못하여 깨닫지 못하고 배움이 넉넉지 못하여 알지 못하는 이들이 많이 있습니다. 곧 겉으로 드러난 의식이 속마음보다 더 중하던 시절에는 유효하던 예배 방식이, 내면의 확신이 전부인 시대에 이르러, 목사의 입술에서 나오는 낱말 하나하나가 듣는 이의 마음에 알아들을 수 있게 떨어져야 하는 시대에 이르러서는 거의 야만에 가까운 것이 되고 말았다는 사실을 알지 못하는 이들 말입니다. 예전에 뭇사람의 종교는 상상의 문제였습니다. 그러나 이제 이 말세에 이르러서는 그리스도인이라면 제 믿음에 이유를 지녀야 하고, 믿을 뿐 아니라 소화해야 하며, 들을 뿐 아니라 이해해야 하게 되지 않았겠습니까. 우리 아침 예배의 말씀들, 소박하고 또렷한 품위로 낭독될 때 그 말씀들이 얼마나 아름답고 얼마나 적절하며 얼마나 알아듣기 쉬웠습니까! 그러나 그것이 온갖 겉만 번드르르한 선율의 매력을 걸치고 나올 때, 그 말씀의 뜻은 얼마나 많이 사라져 버리는 것이 아니겠습니까! 운운.\n\n이런 설교를 그랜틀리 부주교와 하딩 선창자와 그 나머지 사람들 앞에서 하다니! 제 대성당에 모인 수석 사제와 참사회 전원 앞에서! 제 나름의 예배가 그 의도한 모든 바에 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하다고 굳게 확신하며 그 예배를 집전하는 가운데 늙어 온 사람들 앞에서! 그것도 저런 인간, 성직계의 parvenu, 맡은 사목 하나 없는 자, 한낱 전속 사제, 그들 사이에 끼어든 침입자, 그랜틀리 박사의 말마따나 메릴본의 시궁창에서 긁어 올려진 자의 입에서 나온 설교를! 그들은 그것을 앉아서 끝까지 들어야 했다! 그들 중 누구도, 그랜틀리 박사조차도 귀를 막을 수 없었고 예배 시간에 하나님의 집을 떠날 수도 없었다. 그들은 들어야 할 의무 아래 있었고, 게다가 당장 대꾸할 힘도 없었다.\n\n오늘날 문명하고 자유로운 나라에서 인류에게 가해지는 고초 가운데 설교를 들어야 하는 일보다 더한 것은 아마 없을 것이다. 이 땅에서 설교하는 성직자 말고는 그 누구도 청중을 잠자코 앉혀 놓고 괴롭힐 권능을 가지지 못한다. 설교하는 성직자 말고는 그 누구도 상투어와 자명한 소리와 자명하지 않은 소리를 마음껏 늘어놓고도, 마치 그 입에서 열정적인 웅변이나 설득력 있는 논리가 흘러나오기라도 한 듯한 공손한 태도를 아무도 이의를 달지 못하는 제 특권으로 받아 누리지 못한다. 법학이나 물리학 교수가 강의실에 자리를 잡고 앉아 무미건조한 말과 쓸모없는 빈말을 쏟아 놓아 보라. 그는 텅 빈 걸상을 향해 쏟아 놓게 될 것이다. 법정 변호사가 잘하지도 못하면서 말을 늘어놓아 보라. 그가 말할 기회는 좀처럼 오지 않을 것이다. 판사의 논고는 배심원과 죄수와 간수 말고는 억지로 들어야 할 사람이 없다. 국회의원은 기침 소리에 눌려 앉거나 정족수 미달로 회의가 파해 버린다. 시의원은 따돌림을 당한다. 그러나 설교하는 성직자만은 누구도 떨쳐 낼 수가 없다. 그는 이 시대의 골칫거리요, 우리 신드바드들이 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 그 노인이요, 우리의 주일 안식을 어지럽히는 악몽이요, 우리의 신앙을 짓눌러 하나님 섬기는 일을 역겹게 만드는 몽마다. 우리는 억지로 교회에 끌려가는 것이 아니다! 그렇다. 하지만 우리가 바라는 것은 그 이상이다. 우리는 억지로 발길을 끊게 되지 않기를 바란다. 우리는 공적 예배의 위안을 누리기를 바라며, 아니, 누리기로 굳게 마음먹었으되, 보통의 인간 본성이 참을성 있게 견딜 수 없을 만큼의 지루함 없이 그리하기를 바란다. 흔한 설교의 흔한 결말인 저 도망치고 싶은 초조한 갈망 없이 하나님의 집을 나설 수 있기를 바라는 것이다.\n\n젊은 목사가 얼마나 흡족한 얼굴로 잘못 이해한 본문에서 그릇된 결론을 이끌어 내고는, 자기가 내린 지시를 따르지 않으면 지옥의 온갖 형벌을 받으리라 우리를 을러대는가! 그렇다, 자신감이 지나친 나의 젊은 벗이여, 나도 그대의 입에 그토록 흔히 오르내리는 그 신비들을 믿는다. 나도 그대가 손에 들고 있는 그 순전한 말씀을 믿는다. 다만 어떤 대목에서 그대의 해석을 의심하더라도 나를 용서해야 하리라. 성경도 좋고 기도서도 좋다. 아니, 우리의 위대한 신학자들이 그 역량이 무르익었을 때 다듬어 놓은 유서 깊은 강론의 한 대목이라도 내게 읽어 준다면 그대 자신도 받아들일 만하다. 그러나 부족한 나의 젊은 강사여, 그대의 미숙한 문장과 되풀이되는 상투구와 거짓 애상과 늘어지는 어조와 규탄, 그대의 흠흠거림과 어어거림, 그대의 오오 소리와 아아 소리, 그대의 검은 장갑과 흰 손수건 앞에서 내가 하품을 하더라도 부디 양해해 주기 바란다. 내게 그것은 다 아무 뜻도 없으며, 시간이란 그렇게 허비하기에는 너무 귀하다. 그것을 피할 수만 있다면 말이지만.\n\n그리고 여기서 나는, 현장에서 일하는 성직자들이 그토록 자주 내세우는 저 핑계, 곧 설교할 것이 너무 많아 짐이 무겁다는 주장에 항의를 해 두어야겠다. 우리는 모두 제 목소리를 지나치게 좋아하며, 설교자는 억지로 붙들린 청중이라는 특권 덕분에 제 목소리를 들려주려는 허영을 부추김받는다. 그의 설교는 그의 생애에서 달콤한 한 입이요, 스스로를 드높이는 감미로운 순간이다. “이번 주에 설교를 아홉 번 했지 뭔가.” 얼마 전 어느 젊은 벗이 내게 그렇게 말했다. 짐에 짓눌린 순교자의 형상 그대로, 나른하게 손을 이마에 얹은 채였다. “이번 주에 아홉 번, 지난주에 일곱 번, 그 전주에 네 번. 이번 달에만 스물세 번을 설교했네. 정말이지 너무 많아.”\n\n“정말 너무 많군.” 나는 몸서리치며 말했다. “누구의 기력으로도 감당하기 어려울 만큼 너무 많아.”\n\n“그렇지.” 그가 온순하게 대답했다. “정말 그렇다네. 요즘 들어 그게 아프도록 느껴지기 시작했어.”\n\n“느꼈으면 좋겠군.” 내가 말했다. “부디 그대가 그것을 느끼게 되었으면 좋겠어.” 그러나 그는 내 마음이 저 가엾은 청중들 때문에 아팠다는 것을 끝내 짐작하지 못했다.\n\n어쨌든 문제의 그날 슬로프 씨의 설교를 듣는 데 지루함을 느낀 사람은 없었다. 그의 주제는 청중에게 너무나 절실하게 와닿는 것이어서 따분할 수가 없었고, 사실대로 말하자면 슬로프 씨에게는 말을 힘 있게 부리는 재주가 있었다. 30분에 걸친 그의 웅변은 말없는 주목과 열린 귀로 끝까지 들렸으나, 눈만은 성난 목사에게서 또 다른 성난 목사에게로 번득이며 옮겨 다녔고, 벌름거리는 콧구멍에서는 이미 분노의 김이 뿜어져 나왔으며, 발을 여러 번 끌고 몸을 불편하게 뒤척이는 모양은 마음이 어지럽고 심사가 온 세상과 화평하지 못하다는 것을 말해 주고 있었다.\n\n마침내 온 회중 가운데 가장 크게 놀라 머리카락이 곤두설 지경이었던 주교가, 오랫동안 제 서재에서 연습해 온 것과는 전혀 딴판인 태도로 축도를 내렸고, 회중은 각자 제 갈 길을 갈 수 있게 되었다.\n\n# 제7장. 수석 사제와 참사회가 의논하다\n\n바체스터 전체가 발칵 뒤집혔다. 그랜틀리 박사는 대성당 현관을 채 벗어나기도 전에 분노를 터뜨렸다. 늙은 수석 사제는 말할 엄두조차 내지 못한 채 조용히 수석사제관으로 물러가, 반쯤 얼이 빠진 채 앉아 이런저런 생각을 부질없이 굴렸다. 하딩 씨는 홀로 쓸쓸하고 침울하게 걸어 나왔고, 경내의 느릅나무 아래를 천천히 지나면서도 자기가 들은 그 말들이 바체스터 대성당의 강단에서 나왔다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 또다시 시달려야 하는가? 자기 한평생이 쓸모없는 허울이었다고 두 번째로 까발려져야 하는가? 워든 자리를 내놓았듯이 선창자 자리도 내놓고, 열두 구호 노인을 포기했듯이 영창도 포기해야 하는가? 그런들 또 어떠랴! 또 다른 『주피터』가, 또 다른 슬로프 씨가 나타나 그를 세인트 커스버트 교회에서 몰아낼 것이다. 평생 해 온 대로 기도문을 영창해 온 것이 설마 그르지야 않았으리라! 그러나 그는 슬슬 의심이 들기 시작했다. 자기 자신을 의심하는 것이 하딩 씨의 약점이었다. 그러나 그것이 그가 속한 계층의 흔한 결점은 아니다.\n\n그렇다! 바체스터 전체가 발칵 뒤집혔다. 흔들린 것은 성직자들만이 아니었다. 평신도들도 슬로프 씨의 새로운 교리를 들었으니, 모두가 놀랐고 더러는 분개했으며, 더러는 설교자에 대한 반감이 그리 짙게 섞이지 않은 뒤섞인 심정으로 들었다. 늙은 주교와 그 전속 사제들, 수석 사제와 참사원들과 하급 참사원들, 오래된 성가대, 그중에서도 특히 그 우두머리인 하딩 씨는 모두 바체스터에서 인기가 있었다. 그들은 제 돈을 써 가며 좋은 일을 했고, 가난한 이들은 시달리지 않았으며, 사교계의 성직자들은 거만하지도 딱딱하지도 않았다. 그리고 이 도시의 명성이라는 것은 온통 그 교회적 비중에서 나온 것이었다. 그런데도 슬로프 씨의 설교를 흡족하게 들은 이들이 있었다.\n\n날마다 되풀이되는 따분한 일상에 시달리는 판에 자극이 한 번 톡 튀어 들어오는 것은 얼마나 유쾌한 일인가! 성가와 테 데움은 그 자체로 흐뭇한 것이었으나 너무 자주 들어 온 것이었다! 슬로프 씨는 결코 흐뭇한 인물이 아니었지만 새로웠고, 게다가 영리했다. 이제 바체스터 사람들 가운데 적잖은 수가 이렇게들 말했다. 바깥세상을 뒤흔드는 종교상의 변화에는 귀 한 번 기울이지 않은 채 예전의 그 단조로운 방식대로 지내 온 것이 진작부터 굼떠 보였다고. 시대를 앞서가는 이들은 이제 새로운 생각을 품고 있으니, 바체스터도 앞서 나갈 때가 되었다는 것이다. 슬로프 씨의 말이 옳을지도 모른다. 대성당 예배에 관한 한을 빼면, 바체스터에서 주일이 엄격히 지켜져 온 것은 분명 아니었다. 실제로 예배와 예배 사이의 두 시간은 오래전부터 오전 방문과 따뜻한 점심에 배정되어 있었다. 그리고 주일학교! 정말이지 주일학교에 대해서는 더 많은 일을 했어야 마땅했다 — 슬로프 씨는 그것을 안식일 학교라 불렀다. 선대 주교는 참으로 주일학교를 마땅히 생각해야 할 만큼 생각하지 않았다. (이런 사람들은 아마 이 점은 헤아려 보지 않았을 것이다. 어린 머리에게 교리문답과 기도문은 늙은 머리에게 부기가 그러하듯 고된 일이며, 그 두 가지 일에 깃드는 예배의 감정이라는 것도 서로 별반 다를 바 없이 적다는 점 말이다.) 그리고 또, 음악 예배라는 저 큰 문제로 말하자면, 그 문제에서 슬로프 씨 편에 서서 할 말이 많을지도 모른다. 사람들이 대성당에 음악을 들으러 간다는 것은 분명 사실이었으니, 등등 등등.\n\n그리하여 이 문제에서 슬로프 씨 편에 서는 무리가 바체스터에 실제로 형성되기에 이르렀다! 상류층 가운데서 이 무리를 이룬 것은 주로 부인들이었다. 남자라면 — 그러니까 신사라면 — 슬로프 씨에게 끌리거나 그토록 혐오스러운 가말리엘의 발치에 앉기를 승낙할 리가 없었다. 부인들은 때로 신체의 결격을 따지는 데 그리 까다롭지 않다. 남자가 자기들에게 말만 잘한다면, 그 입이 아무리 일그러지고 흉하더라도 귀를 기울여 준다. 윌크스는 연인으로서 더없이 운이 좋았고, 축축하고 모래빛 머리에 접시 같은 눈, 벌건 주먹을 한 슬로프 씨도 여자의 가슴에 대해서만은 위력을 발휘했다.\n\n그러나 이웃 성직자 가운데 한둘은 바체스터 교구의 떡과 물고기가 당분간 담겨 있는 그 바구니들을 소홀히 하는 것이 그리 안전하지 않다고 여겼다. 대성당 강단에서 그 공연을 마친 뒤 슬로프 씨를 찾아와 인사한 사람은 그들뿐이었다. 그중에 푸딩데일의 교구 목사 퀴버풀 씨가 있었는데, 그 부인은 해마다 그에게 사랑의 새 정표를 안겨 주기를 그치지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 근심을, 그리고 바라건대 그의 행복도 똑같이 늘려 주고 있었다. 살아 있는 자녀가 열넷이요 수입이라고는 한 해 400파운드가 고작인 신사가, 그 떡과 물고기가 설령 슬로프 씨 같은 자의 손아귀에 있다 한들 그것을 눈여겨보는 것을 누가 이상하다 하겠는가?\n\n그 설교가 행해진 주일이 지나고 얼마 되지 않아, 인근의 주요 성직자들이 한자리에 모여 슬로프 씨를 어떻게 눌러 놓을 것인가를 두고 성대한 토론을 벌였다. 우선 첫째로, 그자는 두 번 다시 바체스터 대성당의 강단에서 설교해서는 안 된다. 이것이 그랜틀리 박사가 맨 처음 내놓은 단정이었고, 다들 그 말에 찬동했다 — 다만 그자를 배제할 권한이 자기들에게 있기만 하다면 말이다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 권한이 수석 사제와 참사회에 있다고 단언하면서, 주교 자신을 제외하면 참사회 밖의 성직자는 그곳에서 설교할 권리가 없다고 지적했다. 수석 사제도 여기에는 동의했으나, 그런 문제로 다툼을 벌이는 것은 보기 흉하리라고 했다. 이에 대성당 참사회원 가운데 하나인 여윈 체구의 자그마한 박사가 응수하기를, 참사회원이 저마다 늘 그 자리에 대기하여 강단에서 제 몫을 맡기만 한다면 다툼은 온통 슬로프 씨 쪽에서나 벌어질 일이라 하였다. 바체스터 경내 안 제 아늑한 집에 사는 것이 더없이 마음에 맞고, 이탈리아의 별장이나 사람을 끄는 런던의 저택이 대성당의 성가대석과 사택보다 더 탐나서 자리를 비운 베시 스탠호프 박사 같은 이들에게 한마디 슬쩍 던져 놓고 흡족해하는, 교활하고 여윈 자그마한 박사여!\n\n이에 대해 몸집이 큰 학무 참사원가 대답하기를 — 말수는 확실히 적으나 매우 분별 있는 사람이었다 — 자리를 비운 참사회원에게는 대리 사제가 있고, 그런 경우 강단에 설 대리 사제의 권리는 윗자리 성직자의 권리와 같다고 하였다. 수석 사제는 이 말에도 동의하며 이 엄연한 사실 앞에서 깊이 신음했다. 그러나 그러자 여윈 박사가, 그리 되면 자기들이 하급 참사원들의 손에 맡겨지는 셈이며 그중 누구라도 언제든 그 신임을 저버릴 수 있다고 지적했다. 그러자 몸집 큰 학무 참사원에게서 “쯧, 쯧, 쯧!” 비슷하게 들리는 소리가 새어 나왔는데, 어쩌면 그 훌륭한 이는 그저 목구멍에 걸린 묵직한 숨을 내뿜은 것뿐인지도 몰랐다. 굳이 입을 막을 것까지야 있겠습니까? 하고 하딩 씨가 제안했다. 거짓 교리를 설교하는 것이 아닌 다음에야 어느 누가 무슨 설교를 하든 듣기를 부끄러워하지 맙시다. 거짓 교리라면 주교가 그 입을 막게 하면 될 일이고요. 우리의 벗은 그렇게 말했다. 부질없이. 인간의 목적은 인간의 수단으로 이루어야 하는 법이니. 그러나 수석 사제는 그 침침한 늙은 눈으로 한 줄기 희망을 보았다. 그렇지, 이 슬로프 씨가 자기들에게 얼마나 못마땅한 인물인지를 주교에게 알리자. 갓 자리에 오른 새 주교가, 첫 주교복 앞치마에 아직 윤이 가시지도 않았는데 제 휘하 성직자들을 모욕하고 싶어 할 리는 없을 테니.\n\n그러자 그랜틀리 박사가 몸을 일으켜, 동료들의 흩어진 지혜를 이렇게 그러모은 끝에 깊은 권위가 실린 말을 내놓았다. 부주교가 몸을 일으켰다고 할 때, 내가 말하는 것은 그의 내면의 사람이다. 그것이 그때 좀 더 즉각적인 행동으로 벌떡 일어섰던 것이다. 정작 육신으로 말하자면 박사는 내내 수석 사제의 불 꺼진 벽난로 쇠살대에 등을 대고 선 채, 프록코트 자락을 양팔에 걸치고 있었기 때문이다. 두 손은 반바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채였다.\n\n“이자가 이 대성당에서 다시 설교하도록 두어서는 안 된다는 것은 지극히 명백하오. 우리 모두 그 점을 알고 있소 — 여기 계신 우리의 다정한 벗 한 분만은 예외지만 말이오. 그분의 천성은 젖처럼 부드럽게 흐르는지라, 교황이 몸소 찾아와 강단을 좀 빌려 달라 청한다 해도 차마 거절하지 못할 어른이시오. 그러나 우리는 이 사람이 여기서 다시 설교하도록 두어서는 안 되오. 교회 문제에 관한 저자의 견해가 우리와 다를 수 있기 때문이 아니오. 그런 것을 두고서야 누가 다투겠소. 저자가 일부러 우리를 모욕했기 때문이오. 지난 주일 저 강단에 올라갔을 때 저자가 작정하고 노린 바는, 저가 감히 그토록 업신여겨 말한 그것들을 우러르며 늙어 온 사람들에게 모욕을 주자는 것이었소. 아니, 이곳에 낯선 자로, 젊고 알려진 바 없고 연고도 없는 낯선 자로 와서, 제 주인인 주교의 이름을 팔아, 우리더러 제 직분도 모르고 케케묵었고 쓸모없다고 말하다니! 저자의 담력에 더 감탄해야 할지 뻔뻔함에 더 감탄해야 할지 나는 모르겠소! 그리고 한 가지는 말해 두겠소. 그 설교는 오로지 저자 자신에게서 나온 것이오. 주교는 여기 계신 수석 사제님이 그러하듯 그 일에 아무 관여도 하지 않았소. 이 교구의 주교가 프라우디 박사로 하여금 세간의 눈에 띄게 만든 그 관용주의 사상을 품고 있는 것을 보며 내가 얼마나 개탄하는지는 여러분 모두 아시오. 그런 사람의 견해를 내가 얼마나 크게 불신하는지도 여러분 모두 아시오. 그러나 이 일에 관해서는 나는 그에게 아무 허물이 없다고 보오. 프라우디 박사는 신사들 틈에서 너무 오래 살아온 사람이라, 그토록 상스러운 행패를 스스로 저지르거나 남을 부추겨 저지르게 할 사람은 아니라고 나는 믿소. 아니오! 저 사람이 주교의 입을 대신하여 말하는 것처럼 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 그것은 사실이 아닌 말을 입에 올린 것이오. 우리에게 대뜸 도전장을 던지는 것, 우리 자신의 종교적 직무의 고요함 속에서 — 우리가 사랑하는 우리 대성당의 벽 안에서 — 여러 해 동안 분열 없이 좋은 평판을 들으며 우리의 사목을 행해 온 바로 이곳에서 대뜸 우리에게 맞서는 것이 저자의 야심찬 속셈에 맞았던 것이오. 그런 자리에서 나온 그런 공격은 가증스럽소.”\n\n“가증스럽소.” 수석 사제가 신음하듯 말했다. “가증스럽소.” 여윈 박사가 중얼거렸다. “가증스럽소.” 학무 참사원가 그 깊은 가슴 밑바닥에서 소리를 끌어내며 되받아 울렸다. “정말 그랬던 것 같습니다.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“더없이 가증스럽고 더없이 변명의 여지가 없는 일이오.” 부주교가 말을 이었다. “그러나 수석 사제님, 하느님께 감사하게도 저 강단은 아직 우리의 것이오. 아니, 사제님의 것이라 해야겠군요. 저 강단은 오로지 바체스터 대성당의 수석 사제와 참사회에 속한 것이고, 슬로프 씨는 아직 그 참사회의 일원이 아니오. 수석 사제님께서는 이 사람을 우리에게 떠맡기지 말아 달라고 주교에게 호소하자고 하셨소만, 주교가 제 전속 사제에게 휘둘리기로 든다면 어찌 되겠소? 내 생각에 이 일은 우리 손안에 있소. 슬로프 씨는 허락을 청하여 얻지 않고서는 그곳에서 설교할 수 없으니, 그 허락을 한결같이 거절합시다. 대성당 예배의 사목에 참여하는 일체를 저자에게 거절합시다. 그런 뒤에 주교가 굳이 끼어들겠다면, 우리는 주교에게 무어라 답해야 할지 알게 될 것이오. 여기 계신 내 벗께서는 이 사람이 사제님 휘하 하급 참사원 가운데 누군가의 직무를 대신 맡는 식으로 다시 강단에 오를 길을 찾을지 모른다고 하셨소만, 수석 사제께서 그런 대리를 반대하신다는 것이 알려지기만 하면 그분들이 우리를 지지해 주리라고 나는 온전히 믿어도 좋다고 보오.”\n\n“물론 그리하실 것이오.” 학무 참사원가 말했다.\n\n그 학식 있는 회의체 안에서는 훨씬 더 많은 논의가 오갔으나, 그 모든 것은 물론 부주교의 명령에 복종하는 것으로 끝났다. 그들은 너무 오랫동안 그의 지배에 길들어 있어 그것을 그리 쉽게 떨쳐 낼 수 없었고, 더구나 이번 일에서는 그가 그토록 눌러 놓고자 하는 사람을 편들고 싶은 마음이 아무에게도 없었다.\n\n우리가 방금 기록한 것과 같은 모임이 바체스터 같은 도시에서 아무도 모르게, 아무 말도 없이 지나갈 리는 없다. 모임이 있었다는 사실이 주교관을 포함하여 점잖은 집집마다 입에 오른 것은 물론이요, 수석 사제와 부주교와 학무 참사원가 한 말까지 그대로 옮겨졌다 — 전하는 이들의 취향과 견해에 따라 덧붙임과 지어낸 정황이 적잖이 곁들여지기는 했지만.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 대성당에서 입을 여는 것을 금지당하게 되었다는 데에는 모두의 말이 일치했다. 많은 이들은 성당지기들이 그에게 앉을 자리조차 내주지 말라는 지시를 받게 되었다고 믿었고, 강경책을 주장하는 이들 가운데 가장 멀리 나간 축은 그의 설교가 기소할 만한 죄로 여겨지고 있으며 교회 안에서 소란을 일으킨 죄로 그를 걸어 절차가 진행될 것이라고 단언했다.\n\n그를 두둔하려는 무리 — 종교에 열광하는 젊은 아가씨들과 무언가 변화를 바라는 중년의 노처녀들 — 는 물론 이 공격 때문에 한층 더 뜨겁게 그를 변호하고 나섰다. 대성당에서 슬로프 씨의 말을 들을 수 없다면 다른 데서라도 듣겠다는 것이었다. 따분한 수석 사제와 따분한 늙은 참사회원들과 그에 못지않게 따분한 젊은 하급 참사원들은 저희끼리 서로에게나 설교하라고 내버려 두고, 자기들은 슬로프 씨를 위해 실내화와 방석을 뜨고 띠에 단을 박아 주며, 그를 행복한 순교자로 만들어 어느 새로운 시온이나 베데스다에 모셔 세우고, 대성당은 아주 한물간 것으로 만들어 버리겠다는 것이었다.\n\n프라우디 박사 내외는 곧바로 런던으로 돌아갔다. 폭풍의 기세가 다 소진될 때까지는 그 설교에 관하여 수석 사제와 참사회로부터 직접 항의를 받는 일이 없도록 하는 편이 상책이라고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 그들은 슬로프 씨를 뒤에 남겨 두었고, 그는 조금도 주눅 들지 않은 채 열심히 제 일을 해 나갔다. 제 아첨에 귀를 기울여 줄 만한 이들에게는 아첨하고, 어리석은 여자들의 귀에는 신앙을 빙자한 허튼소리를 속삭이고, 자기를 받아 줄 몇 안 되는 성직자들에게는 비위를 맞추고, 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아다니고, 온갖 사람을 캐묻고, 온갖 일에 코를 들이밀고, 주교관의 허물어진 구석구석을 그 지독히도 세밀한 눈으로 뒤졌다. 그러나 당장에 대성당에서 다시 설교하려는 시도는 하지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 바체스터 전체가 서로 으르렁거리게 되었다.\n\n# 제8장. 전임 워든이 요양원에 돌아갈 일을 기뻐하다\n\n지금까지 바체스터에서 슬로프 씨를 제 영적 지도자로 인정한 부인들 가운데 미망인 볼드 부인과 그 시누이를 넣어서는 안 된다. 대성당 경내 사람들의 분노가 처음 터져 나왔을 때, 그 침입자에게 이 두 부인만큼 격렬히 맞선 이도 없었다. 그것은 당연한 일이었다. 선창자가 가장 아끼는 딸만큼 제 대성당의 음악적 명성을 자랑스러워할 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 오래된 성가대에 가해진 모욕을 그만큼 분하게 여길 사람이 또 누가 있겠는가? 그리고 그런 문제에서 볼드 양과 그 올케는 의견이 하나였다.\n\n그러나 이 분노는 어느 정도 누그러졌으니, 유감스럽게도 두 부인이 슬로프 씨로 하여금 스스로 변명하도록 내버려 두었다고 말해야겠다. 그 설교가 있고 두 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 단추 달린 제복을 입은 사환 아이가 볼드 부인의 응접실 문을 열며 슬로프 씨의 방문을 알리자 두 사람은 적잖이 놀랐다. 아닌 게 아니라 살아 있는 사람 가운데 아침 방문 하나로 그들을 이보다 더 놀라게 할 자가 누가 있었겠는가? 바체스터의 선한 모든 것에 맞선 대적이 자기들 응접실로 들어서는데, 곁에는 그들을 지켜 줄 억센 팔도 재빠른 혀도 없었다. 미망인은 요람에 있던 아기를 낚아채 무릎에 앉혔고, 메리 볼드는 어떤 사정으로든 그런 희생이 필요해진다면 그 아기를 위해 사내답게 죽을 각오로 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 그렇게 맞아들여졌다. 그러나 그가 물러갈 때에는 두 부인 모두 그가 제 손을 잡도록 허락했고, 그는 융숭한 대접을 받은 신사가 그러하듯 작별 인사를 했다! 그렇다, 그는 그들과 악수를 나누었고 정중한 무릎 인사를 받으며 배웅되었으며, 단추 달린 사환 아이는 참사원 가운데 가장 훌륭한 이에게 하듯 문을 열어 주었다. 그는 아기의 작은 손을 만지며 뜨거운 축복을 빌어 주었고, 미망인에게 일찍 찾아온 슬픔을 이야기했으나 엘리너의 말없는 눈물은 그를 나무라지 않았으며, 메리 볼드에게는 그 헌신이 언젠가 보답을 받으리라 말했건만 메리 볼드는 그 칭찬을 역겨워하지 않고 들었다. 그는 이 모든 일을 어떻게 해냈는가? 어떻게 그토록 빨리 반감을 적어도 안면 정도로는 바꾸어 놓았는가? 이 부인들이 그를 맞으려 벼르고 있던 적의를 어떻게 이겨 내고 그리도 손쉽게 화해했는가?\n\n내가 여기까지 쓴 것으로 미루어 독자들은 나 자신이 슬로프 씨를 좋아하지 않는다고 짐작할 것이다. 그러나 그가 재주 있는 사람이라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 그는 알맞은 자리에 부드러운 말 한마디를 놓을 줄 안다. 듣는 이의 귀에 맞추어 아첨을 매만질 줄 안다. 뱀의 간계를 알고, 또 그것을 쓴다. 슬로프 씨가 여자를 대하듯 남자도 대할 줄 알았더라면, 신사의 몸가짐을 끝내 익힐 수 있었더라면, 그는 크게 되었을지도 모른다.\n\n그는 엘리너의 아버지를 칭송하는 것으로 그녀와의 교분을 텄다. 아무리 높이 말해도 모자랄 분의 마음을 불행히도 상하게 했음을 알게 되었노라고 그는 말했다. 응접실에서 나눌 이야기로는 아마 너무 무거운 주제일 터이니 지금은 언급하지 않겠으나, 온 세상이, 적어도 성직자의 세상이 하딩 씨를 두고 그토록 높이 말하는 그런 분을 깎아내릴 말을 할 생각은 자기에게 결코 없었노라고 했다. 그런 식으로 그는 제 설교의 상당 부분을 도로 거두어들이면서 선창자의 음악적 재능에 더없는 감탄을 늘어놓고, 아버지와 딸과 시누이를 두루 기리며, 여자의 귀를 위해 늘 따로 마련해 두는 그 낮고 매끄러운 속삭임으로 이야기했고, 마침내 목적을 이루었다. 물러가면서 그는 다시 찾아뵈어도 좋겠느냐는 뜻을 비쳤고, 엘리너가 말로 승낙하지는 않았으나 싫다는 뜻도 내비치지 않았으니, 그렇게 하여 미망인의 집을 드나들 슬로프 씨의 권리가 세워졌다.\n\n그 방문이 있은 다음 날 엘리너는 아버지에게 그 일을 이야기하며, 슬로프 씨가 남들이 칠해 놓은 것만큼 그리 시커먼 사람은 아닌 듯하다는 뜻을 말했다. 하딩 씨는 무슨 일이 있었는지 듣고 평소보다 눈을 조금 크게 떴으나 별말은 하지 않았다. 그는 슬로프 씨를 칭찬하는 어떤 말에도 동의할 수 없었고, 그렇다고 누구의 험담을 길게 늘어놓는 것도 그의 버릇이 아니었다. 그러나 그는 그 방문이 마음에 들지 않았고, 사람이 순박하기는 해도 슬로프 씨에게 두 부인에게 부드러운 말을 건네는 즐거움 말고 무언가 더 깊은 속셈이 있으리라는 것만은 분명히 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 하딩 씨가 딸을 보러 온 것은 슬로프 씨를 좋게 말하려는 것도 나쁘게 말하려는 것도 아닌 다른 볼일 때문이었다. 그는 히램 요양원의 워든 자리가 다시 채워지게 되었으며 십중팔구 자신이 옛집과 열두 구호 노인에게로 다시 돌아가게 되리라는 소식을 딸에게 전하러 온 것이었다.\n\n“그런데 말이다.” 그가 웃으며 말했다. “예전의 영화는 크게 깎여 나가게 되었단다.”\n\n“어째서요, 아버지?”\n\n“우리를 모두 다시 일으켜 세워 준다는 그 새 의회 법령 말이다.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그게 내 수입을 한 해 450파운드로 정해 놓았구나.”\n\n“450파운드라니요.” 그녀가 말했다. “800파운드였는데 그것밖에 안 된다니! 그건 좀 인색하네요. 그래도 아버지, 그 정겨운 옛집과 정원은 그대로 갖게 되시는 거죠?”\n\n“얘야.” 그가 말했다. “그 집은 그 돈의 갑절 값어치가 있단다.” 그렇게 말하는 그의 말투와 몸짓에는, 그리고 엘리너의 응접실을 경쾌하고 기분 좋게 오가는 걸음걸이에는 어딘가 들뜬 만족감이 배어 있었다. “그 돈의 갑절 값어치야. 나는 그 집과 정원을 얻을 테고, 수입도 내가 쓰고 싶은 것보다 많을 게다.”\n\n“어차피 헤프게 쓰는 딸을 건사하실 일도 없잖아요.” 그렇게 말하며 젊은 미망인은 아버지의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 그를 곁의 소파에 앉혔다. “적어도 그 비용은 안 드실 테니까요.”\n\n“그렇지, 얘야. 그리고 그 애가 없으니 나는 좀 외롭겠지. 하지만 지금은 그런 생각일랑 하지 말자꾸나. 수입으로 말하자면 내가 바라는 모든 것에 넉넉하고도 남는다. 옛집도 다시 갖게 될 테고. 이제야 하는 말이다만, 셋방살이가 불편하다고 느낀 적이 이따금 있었단다. 셋방은 젊은 사람에게야 아주 좋지. 그러나 내 나이쯤 되면 뭐랄까, 무어라 불러야 할지 모르겠구나, 체면이라고까지 할 것은 아닐지 몰라도…”\n\n“어머, 아버지! 그런 일은 결코 없었어요. 아무도 그렇게 생각하지 않았는걸요. 아버지가 하이 스트리트에 그 방을 얻으신 뒤로 바체스터에서 아버지보다 더 존경받은 분은 없어요. 아무도요! 수석사제관의 수석 사제님도, 저 플럼스테드의 부주교님도 그렇지 못했어요.”\n\n“부주교가 그 말을 들었으면 너를 그리 고맙게 여기지는 않겠구나.” 딸이 예로 드는 사람을 바체스터 참사회의 고위 성직자들로만 한정하는 것이 우스워 그는 슬며시 웃으며 말했다. “어쨌든 옛집으로 돌아가게 되어 나는 기쁘다. 일이 다 정해졌다는 말을 들은 뒤로는 거실이 두 칸은 있어야 편하겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했으니 말이다.”\n\n“일이 정해질 때까지 저와 함께 지내세요, 아버지. 그래 주세요, 네?”\n\n“고맙다, 넬리. 하지만 그러지 않으련다. 그러면 짐을 두 번 옮겨야 하지 않겠니. 나는 그 노인들에게 다시 돌아가는 것이 무척 기쁘구나. 아아, 저런! 지난 몇 해 사이에 여섯이 세상을 떠났단다. 열둘 가운데 여섯이! 남은 이들도 거기서 고단하게 지냈을 게다. 가엾은 번스, 가엾은 늙은 번스!”\n\n번스는 히램의 자선을 받는 이들 가운데 아직 살아 있는 사람으로, 이제 아흔이 넘은 노인이었고 오래도록 하딩 씨가 아끼던 이였다.\n\n“번스 노인이 얼마나 기뻐할까요.” 볼드 부인이 부드러운 두 손을 가만히 마주치며 말했다. “아버지가 돌아오신다니 그분들 모두 얼마나 기뻐할까요. 아버지가 계시면 그분들 사이에도 곧 다시 정이 돌 거예요.”\n\n“그런데 말이다.” 그가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다. “나한테 무서운 새 골칫거리가 생기게 되었구나. 노파 열두 명에 여사감이 하나 온다는구나. 여자 열둘에 여사감까지 내가 어떻게 감당한단 말이냐!”\n\n“여사감이 노파들을 감당하겠지요, 당연히.”\n\n“그러면 여사감은 누가 감당하고?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그분은 감당해 줄 사람이 필요 없을 거예요. 스스로 대단한 마님이 되실 테니까요. 그런데 아버지, 여사감은 어디서 살아요? 설마 아버지와 함께 워든 사택에서 사는 건 아니겠죠?”\n\n“그러지 않기를 바란다, 얘야.”\n\n“어머, 아버지, 저는 분명히 말씀드려요. 여사감을 새어머니로 맞을 생각은 없어요.”\n\n“그럴 일은 없을 게다, 얘야. 적어도 내가 어찌해 볼 수 있는 한은 말이지. 여사감과 노파들이 지낼 집은 따로 짓는다고 하는데, 아직 그 건물을 어디 세울지도 정하지 못한 모양이더구나.”\n\n“그러면 여사감은 정해졌대요?” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“워든도 아직 정하지 못했단다.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“하지만 그건 의심할 여지가 없겠죠?” 딸이 말했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 의심할 여지가 없다고 생각한다며 그 까닭을 설명했다. 부주교가 그렇게까지 단언했다는 것이다. 주교와 그 전속 사제가 설령 다른 사람을 앉힐 뜻이 있고 그 뜻을 밀어붙일 만한 뻔뻔함이 있다 한들, 둘이 힘을 합쳐도 그럴 권한은 없다는 것이었다. 부주교의 견해로는, 하딩 씨가 워든 직을 내놓았고 그것도 아무 조건 없이 내놓았다고는 하나 그때의 사정으로 보아, 이제 요양원 문제가 의회 법령으로 새롭게 정리된 마당에 주교로서는 그를 다시 임명하는 것 말고 달리 택할 길이 없다는 것이었다. 부주교의 의견이 그러했고, 그의 장인은 한 점 의심 없이 그것을 받아들였다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 내놓는 데 처음부터 완강히 반대해 왔다. 그만두지 말라고 온 힘을 다해 말렸다. 그런 자선 기금에서 한 해 800파운드나 되는 큰 수입을 받는다고 세상이 떠들어 대며 공격할 때 하딩 씨는 마땅히 그 소란을 버텨 냈어야 한다는 것이 그의 생각이었고, 장인의 처신이 비겁하고 체통 없는 짓이 아니었다는 데에는 아직까지도 마음이 풀리지 않았다. 그는 또한 워든의 수입을 이렇게 깎은 것을, 신문이 몰아넣은 궁지에서 빠져나오려는 정부의 인색하고 옹색한 술책으로 보았다. 그랜틀리 박사가 말하기를, 정부에게는 히램의 유산에서 나오는 수입 가운데 한 해 900파운드를 처분할 권리가 없듯 450파운드를 처분할 권리도 없으며, 얼마를 지급할지 정할 권리는 분명 주교와 수석 사제와 참사회에 있다는 것이었다. 또 그는 정부에게 그 자선 시설에 노파 1,200명을 떠맡길 권리가 없듯 열두 명을 떠맡길 권리도 없다고 단언했으며, 그러므로 이 일에 몹시 분개했다. 그렇게 말할 때 그는 아마 잊고 있었을 것이다. 정부는 그런 짓을 한 적이 없고 그런 권세도 그런 권리도 자처한 적이 없다는 것을. 그는 흔히들 저지르는 착각을 했으니, 그런 일에서는 아무 힘도 없는 정부에게, 그런 일에서 전능한 의회가 한 일을 뒤집어씌운 것이었다.\n\n그러나 새 조치로 바체스터 요양원 워든 자리의 영광과 명예가 실로 깎여 나갔다고, 그 시설 전체가 휘그당 위원들의 손이 닿아 어느 정도 천해졌다고, 줄어든 수입에 노파들에 그 밖의 새 제도까지 딸린 그 자리가 예전의 요양원과는 아주 딴판이 되었다고 느끼면서도, 부주교는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람인지라, 지금 온갖 씀씀이를 통틀어 한 해 200파운드 남짓밖에 없는 장인이 아무리 더럽혀지고 체통 없고 위원회에 휘둘리는 자리라 한들 그것을 마다하기를 바랄 수는 없었다.\n\n그리하여 하딩 씨는 요양원의 옛집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 정했고, 사실대로 말하자면 그리하게 되었다는 생각에 거의 어린아이 같은 기쁨을 맛보고 있었다. 줄어든 수입은 그에게 잠깐의 아쉬움거리조차 되지 못했다. 여사감과 노파들은 아무래도 마음에 걸렸으나, 그런 조치가 결국 이 도시의 가난한 이들에게 참으로 도움이 될지도 모른다는 생각으로 스스로를 달랠 수 있었다. 자신의 재임명을 새 주교가 내리는 선물처럼, 그것도 아마 슬로프 씨의 손을 거쳐 받아야 한다는 생각은 그를 조금 언짢게 했으나, 그런 임명에는 은전이랄 것이 조금도 없다는 부주교의 장담에 마음이 놓였다. 옛 워든을 다시 임명하는 일은 온 세상이 당연한 일로 여길 터였다. 그리하여 하딩 씨는 자신이 옛 거처로 돌아가는 것을 정해진 일로 보아도 좋다고 딸에게 말하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었다.\n\n“그러면 아버지가 청하실 일은 없는 거죠?”\n\n“물론 없다, 얘야. 주교에게 무슨 은전을 청할 만한 근거도 내겐 없거니와, 사실 나는 그분을 거의 알지도 못한다. 그리고 베풀고 말고를 슬로프 씨가 판가름할 수도 있는 그런 은전이라면 나는 청하지 않겠다. 아니고말고.” 그는 잠시 제 성품과는 사뭇 다른 기운에 사로잡혀 말했다. “요양원으로 돌아가게 된다면 나야 참으로 기쁘겠지. 하지만 그러기 위해 슬로프 씨에게 청을 넣어야 한다면 나는 결코 그리로 가지 않을 게다.”\n\n아버지가 잠깐 내비친 이 노여움은 지금 엘리너의 마음결에 거슬렸다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨를 좋아하게 된 것은 아니었으나 그가 제 아버지를 무척 존경한다고 여기게 되었고, 그래서 두 사람 사이에 좋은 감정 비슷한 것이라도 생기게 하려고 기꺼이 애를 쓰려 했다.\n\n“아버지.” 그녀가 말했다. “아버지는 슬로프 씨의 사람됨을 조금 잘못 보고 계신 것 같아요.”\n\n“그러냐?” 그가 잔잔하게 말했다.\n\n“그런 것 같아요, 아버지. 부주교님과 수석 사제님을 그토록 노엽게 한 그 설교를 할 때에도 그분은 아버지 개인을 업신여길 뜻은 없었다고 생각해요!”\n\n“나도 그가 그랬으리라고는 생각한 적이 없다, 얘야. 그랬는지 안 그랬는지 마음속으로 따져 본 적조차 없기를 바란다. 그런 일은 따져 볼 값어치도 없거니와 참사회가 마음 쓸 값어치는 더더욱 없지. 다만 그가 잉글랜드 국교회의 법도에 따라 행해지는 하느님의 예배를 업신여길 뜻은 품었을까, 그것이 걱정이구나.”\n\n“하지만 아버지와 수석 사제님과 여기 우리 모두가 그토록 옳게 여기는 것에 대해, 그분은 자기 생각이 다르다고 밝히는 것이 도리라고 여겼을 수도 있지 않을까요?”\n\n“교회에서 손윗사람들의 신앙적 소신을 젊은 사람이 무례하게 들이받는 것이 도리일 수는 없다. 사랑도 겸손도 그를 잠잠하게 하지 못했다면 예의만으로라도 잠잠하게 했어야 했지.”\n\n“그렇지만 슬로프 씨라면 그런 문제에서는 하늘에 계신 주인의 분부가 잠잠히 있는 것을 허락지 않는다고 말할 거예요.”\n\n“예의를 지키는 것도 허락지 않는다더냐, 엘리너?”\n\n“그분이 그렇게 말한 건 아니에요, 아버지.”\n\n“내 말을 믿으렴, 얘야. 그리스도의 종은 형제들의 소신은 물론이요 그 편견조차 모욕하라고 하느님의 말씀에서 부름받은 일이 결코 없다. 그리고 신앙이란 사람이 붙잡을 수 있는 다른 어떤 공부 못지않게, 사람들 사이에서 점잖고 예의 바른 처신을 넉넉히 담아낼 수 있는 것이란다. 유감스럽지만 나는 대성당에서 있었던 슬로프 씨의 설교를 두둔할 수가 없구나. 그러나 얘야, 이제 그만하고 모자를 쓰렴. 요양원의 그 정겨운 옛 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아보자꾸나. 우리가 그곳을 떠난 뒤로 나는 아직 안뜰 너머로 발을 들일 엄두를 내지 못했단다. 이제는 들어가 볼 만하다는 생각이 드는구나.”\n\n엘리너는 종을 울려, 한 시간 남짓 두고 나가야 하는 것이 못내 마음에 걸리는 그 소중한 아기를 어떻게 돌봐야 하는지 이런저런 당부를 단단히 이르고는, 아버지와 함께 옛 요양원을 다시 보러 어슬렁어슬렁 나섰다. 그날 두 사람이 그 담장 안에서 나란히 걸어 나온 뒤로, 그곳은 아버지에게와 마찬가지로 그녀에게도 밟아서는 안 될 땅이었다.\n\n# 제9장. 스탠호프 일가\n\n프라우디 박사가 치세를 시작한 지도 어느덧 석 달이 지났고, 교구에는 이미 적어도 활동적인 정신의 기운만은 보여 주는 변화들이 이루어졌다. 그중에서도 자리를 비운 성직자들은 도저히 못 본 체할 수 없을 만큼 강한 암시를 받는 은혜를 입었다. 가엾고 인자하던 늙은 그랜틀리 주교는 이 문제에 지나치게 관대했고, 부주교 역시 그럴듯한 구실로 자리를 비우면서 제 직무는 후하게 남에게 맡겨 두는 이들에게 엄격하게 굴 마음이 조금도 없었다.\n\n이 점에서 교구의 죄인들 가운데 으뜸가는 축에 드는 이가 바로 베시 스탠호프 박사였다. 그가 하루치 직무라도 수행한 지는 이미 여러 해가 지났으나, 그가 직무를 수행하지 못할 이유라고는 본인에게 그럴 마음이 없다는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 그는 이 교구에 참사회원 좌석 하나와 대성당 경내에서 가장 좋은 주택 하나, 그리고 크래브트리 캐노니코럼과 스톡핑엄이라는 두 개의 큰 교구 목사직을 보유하고 있었다. 아니, 실은 세 교구의 사목을 맡고 있었으니, 아이더다운이 스톡핑엄에 병합되어 있었기 때문이다. 그는 12년째 이탈리아에 살고 있었다. 처음 그리로 간 것은 인후염 때문이라고들 했는데, 그 인후염은 그 뒤로 한 번도 심하게 도진 적이 없으면서도 그에게 어찌나 요긴했던지, 덕분에 그는 그때부터 줄곧 편안한 무위 속에서 지낼 수 있었다.\n\n이제 그에게 귀국하라는 부름이 떨어졌다. 물론 거칠게 윽박지르는 방식도, 무슨 단호한 명령의 형태도 아니었으나, 그로서는 도저히 무시할 수 없는 통첩이었다. 슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 받들어 그에게 편지를 썼던 것이다. 우선 주교는 교구 안에서 베시 스탠호프 박사의 값진 협력을 몹시 바라고 있다고 했다. 다음으로 주교는 교구에서 가장 이름난 성직자들과 몸소 안면을 트는 것이 자신의 지엄한 의무라 여긴다고 했다. 그다음으로 주교는 스탠호프 박사 자신의 이익을 위해서라도 박사가 적어도 얼마 동안은 바체스터로 돌아오는 것이 반드시 필요하다고 생각한다고 했다. 그리고 마지막으로, 교회의 고위 성직자들이 성직자의 부재라는 문제를 두고 지금 대단히 강경한 감정을 드러내고 있으니, 베시 스탠호프 박사로서는 몇 달 안에 나라의 의회에 제출될 법한 명단에 제 이름이 오르도록 내버려 두지 않는 편이 마땅하겠다는 말이 있었다.\n\n이 마지막 위협에는 무언가 모호하면서도 섬뜩한 데가 있었으므로, 스탠호프 박사는 여름의 두세 달을 바체스터의 제 사택에서 보내기로 마음먹었다. 그의 두 목사관에는 부목사들이 살고 있었고, 그 자신은 오래 손을 놓은 탓에 교구 직무에는 맞지 않는 사람이 되었다고 느꼈다. 그러나 참사회원 사택만은 그를 위해 비워 둔 채였으니, 어쩌면 이따금 참사회원으로서 설교 한 편쯤은 할 수 있으리라 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 온 식구를 데리고 바체스터에 당도했으며, 이제 그와 그 식구들을 독자 여러분께 소개해야겠다.\n\n스탠호프 집안 사람들의 큰 공통점은 아마 무정함이라 해야 할 것이나, 이 감정의 결핍은 그들 대부분에게서 워낙 넉넉한 붙임성과 짝을 이루고 있었던 까닭에 세상 사람들 눈에는 별로 띄지 않았다. 그들은 이웃의 편의를 봐주는 데 어찌나 선뜻했던지, 정작 이웃들은 주위 사람의 행복과 안녕이 그들에게 얼마나 아무래도 좋은 일인지를 알아채지 못했다. 스탠호프 집안 사람들은 당신이 앓아누우면 (전염병만 아니라면) 병문안을 와서 오렌지며 프랑스 소설이며 갓 나온 뒷소문 한 자락을 가져다줄 것이고, 그런 다음 당신이 죽었다는 소식이든 나았다는 소식이든 똑같이 무심한 낯빛으로 들을 것이다. 저희끼리 대하는 태도도 세상을 대하는 태도와 다르지 않았다. 그들은 참았고 또 봐주었으며, 앞으로 보게 되듯 봐주어야 할 일이 적지 않을 때도 있었다. 그러나 저희끼리의 애정이라는 것도 그 이상으로 올라가는 법은 좀처럼 없었다. 이 집안의 사람들이 저마다 나머지 네 사람의 안녕을 가로막기 위해 할 수 있는 일이 얼마나 많았는지, 또 실제로 얼마나 했는지는 놀라울 지경이다.\n\n식구는 모두 다섯이었으니, 곧 박사와 스탠호프 부인, 두 딸과 아들 하나였다. 박사는 아마 그중 가장 덜 별나고 가장 존경할 만한 사람이었겠으나, 그가 지닌 장점이라는 것은 하나같이 소극적인 것들이었다. 그는 예순 남짓 된, 풍채가 좋고 다소 혈색이 붉은 신사였다. 머리카락은 눈처럼 희고 숱이 아주 많았으며, 어딘가 더없이 고운 양털을 닮아 있었다. 구레나룻은 무척 크고 무척 희어서 그 얼굴을 자애롭고 졸린 늙은 사자처럼 보이게 했다. 옷차림은 언제나 나무랄 데가 없었다. 그토록 오랜 세월을 이탈리아에서 살았으면서도 그 옷은 한결같이 성직자다운 점잖은 빛깔이었고, 그러면서도 지나치게 성직자 티를 내는 법은 없었다. 그는 말이 많은 사람이 아니었으나, 어쩌다 하는 그 몇 마디는 대개 잘 고른 말이었다. 그의 독서는 좀처럼 연애소설과 시를 넘어서지 않았고, 그것도 가장 가벼운 축이었으며 늘 도덕적인 것도 아니었다. 그는 철저한 bon vivant였다. 술에 대해서는 조예 깊은 감식가이면서도 결코 과음하는 법이 없었고, 부엌에 관한 일이라면 무엇에도 추호의 용서가 없는 비평가였다. 슬하에 자식들이 자라난 뒤로 그는 제 집안에서 용서할 일이 많았고 또 모든 것을 용서해 왔다. 저녁 식사에 소홀한 것 하나만 빼고. 그 대목에서의 그의 약점은 이제 온 식구가 훤히 알고 있었으므로, 그의 성미가 시험대에 오르는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 스탠호프 박사가 성직자였으니 그의 종교적 신념이 그 인물됨의 적잖은 부분을 이루리라고 짐작할 법하지만, 실상은 그렇지 않았다. 그에게 종교적 신념이 있었으리라는 것이야 믿어 주어야 하겠으나, 그는 그것을 좀처럼 남에게, 심지어 제 자식들에게조차 내세우지 않았다. 이렇게 삼가는 태도는 무슨 방침에서 나온 것이 아니라 그 사람됨을 그대로 드러내는 것이었다. 자식들의 생각에 결코 영향을 끼치지 않겠다고 미리 정해 두었던 것이 아니라, 워낙 몸에 밴 게으름 탓에 그럴 때가 오기도 전에 그럴 기회가 영영 지나가 버렸던 것이다. 아버지에게 어떤 신념이 있었든 간에, 자식들은 어쨌거나 아버지가 수입을 끌어내는 그 교회의 무심한 신자에 지나지 않았다.\n\n스탠호프 박사는 그러한 사람이었다. 스탠호프 부인의 인물됨은 그 바깥양반보다도 윤곽이 더 흐릿했다. 이탈리아 생활의 far niente가 그 영혼 속까지 스며들어, 부인은 아무것도 하지 않는 상태야말로 이 땅의 유일한 선이라 여기게 되었다. 태도와 용모는 대단히 호감을 주었다. 한때 미인이었고, 쉰다섯이 된 지금도 아름다운 여인이었다. 옷차림은 언제나 완벽했다. 하루에 옷을 단 한 번만 갈아입었고 세 시에서 네 시 사이가 되기 전에는 결코 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 일단 나타났다 하면 가장 좋은 모습으로 나타났다. 그 수고가 얼마쯤은 부인 자신의 몫이었는지 아니면 온전히 하녀의 몫이었는지는, 글 쓰는 자 따위가 감히 짐작해 볼 바가 아니다. 그 의상의 짜임새는 언제나 공들인 것이면서도 결코 지나치게 공들인 티가 나지 않았다. 옷감은 풍족했으나 장신구로 요란하지는 않았고, 패물은 값지고 진귀하여 사람들의 눈길을 끌지 않을 수 없는 것들이었지만 그러라고 걸친 것처럼 보이지는 않았다. 부인은 제가 세운 구조물을 꾸민다는 저 위대한 건축의 비결을 잘 알고 있었고, 장식을 짓는 데까지 내려가는 일은 결코 없었다. 그러나 스탠호프 부인이 옷 입을 줄 알았고 그 앎을 날마다 써먹었다고 말하고 나면, 그로써 할 말은 다 한 셈이다. 그 밖에 인생의 목적이라고는 없었다. 하기야 남의 목적을 방해하지 않았다는 것만 해도 어디인가. 젊은 시절에는 박사의 저녁 식사 문제로 큰 시련을 겪었으나, 지난 10년 내지 12년 동안은 큰딸 샬럿이 그 수고를 대신 떠맡아 준 덕에 부인을 괴롭히는 일이라고는 거의 없었다. 거의 없었다는 것은 저 끔찍한 영국행 명령이 떨어지기 전까지의 이야기다. 그 뒤로는 과연 부인의 삶도 넉넉히 고달팠다. 그런 사람에게는 코모 호숫가에서 바체스터라는 도시까지 실려 가는 일이야말로 노역이라 하고도 남을 것이니, 나르는 이들이 아무리 조심스러웠다 한들 그렇다. 스탠호프 부인은 그 여행의 여파로 옷이란 옷은 죄다 품을 줄여야 했다.\n\n샬럿 스탠호프는 이때 서른다섯 살쯤이었는데, 그에게 어떤 결점이 있었든 간에 나이 든 처녀들에게 유독 따라붙는 결점만은 하나도 없었다. 젊게 차려입지도, 젊게 말하지도, 실은 젊어 보이지도 않았다. 제 나이를 온전히 흡족해하는 듯했고 젊음의 우아함을 흉내 내는 구석이 조금도 없었다. 그는 잘생긴 젊은 여자였고, 만일 남자로 태어났더라면 아주 잘생긴 젊은 남자가 되었을 것이다. 집 안에서 이루어지는 일 가운데 하인들이 하지 않는 일은 죄다 그의 손으로 이루어졌다. 분부를 내리고, 셈을 치르고, 하인을 들이고 내보내고, 차를 끓이고, 고기를 썰고, 스탠호프 집안의 모든 일을 건사하는 것이 그였다. 아버지로 하여금 제 세상살이 형편을 들여다보게 만들 수 있는 사람은 오직 그 하나뿐이었다. 동생의 터무니없는 짓을 그나마 조금이라도 억누를 수 있는 사람도 오직 그 하나뿐이었다. 온 집안이 아주 몰락하여 거지 신세가 되는 것을 막은 사람도 오직 그 하나뿐이었다. 그들이 지금 바체스터에서 이토록 거북한 처지에 놓이게 된 것도 그의 조언에 따른 일이었다.\n\n여기까지 보면 샬럿 스탠호프의 사람됨은 호감이 가지 않는 것도 아니다. 그러나 덧붙여 말해 두어야 할 것이 있으니, 그가 집안에서 지닌 영향력은 어느 정도까지 식구들의 세속적 안녕을 위해 쓰이기는 했을지언정 마음만 먹었다면 쓸 수 있었을 만큼 그들의 참된 이익을 위해 쓰이지는 않았다는 점이다. 그는 아버지가 성직의 의무에 무심한 것을 거들었고, 성직록이란 형님 되는 그 훌륭한 귀족의 영지가 그 귀족의 재산인 것과 똑같이 아버지 개인의 재산이라고 일러 주었다. 그는 박사가 이따금 내비치던, 영국으로 돌아가고 싶다는 자그마한 소망을 여러 해에 걸쳐 그때마다 눌러 버렸다. 어머니의 게으름은 부추겨 놓았으니, 그래야 스탠호프 집안의 안주인이자 살림꾼 노릇을 저 혼자 할 수 있었기 때문이다. 동생의 어리석은 짓 또한 부추기고 감싸 주었다. 그러면서도 그 짓의 있을 법한 결말로부터 동생을 지켜 줄 마음은 늘 있었고 지켜 줄 힘도 대개 있었다. 그리고 남동생을 버려 놓는 데에도 최선을 다하여 완전히 성공을 거두었으니, 직업도 없고 제 것이라 부를 만한 1실링도 없는 빈둥거리는 사내로 세상에 풀어놓은 것이다.\n\n스탠호프 양은 영리한 여자로, 웬만한 주제에 대해서는 다 이야기할 수 있었고 그 주제가 무엇이든 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그는 영국식 편견에서 자유롭다는 것을 자랑으로 삼았는데, 여기에 여자다운 조심성에서도 자유롭다는 말을 덧붙일 수 있었을 것이다. 종교에 관해서라면 그는 순전한 자유사상가였고, 참된 애정이 크게 모자란 탓에 아버지의 심란한 마음 앞에 제 견해를 늘어놓기를 즐겼다. 아버지에게 남아 있는 영국국교회 신앙을 뒤흔들어 놓을 수 있었다면 그로서는 무척 흡족했겠지만, 아버지가 교회에서 받는 성직록을 내던진다는 생각만은 단 한 번도 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 하기야 다른 데서 들어오는 수입이라고는 없는 터에 어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그러나 이 집안에서 가장 두드러진 두 사람은 아직 그려 내지 못했다. 둘째 아이는 세례명을 마들린이라 하였고 대단한 미인이었다. 미인이었다고 할 것도 없다. 우리가 지금 쓰고 있는 이 시점만큼 그가 아름다웠던 적은 없었기 때문이다. 다만 그 몸만은 여러 해 전부터 사고로 망가져 있었다. 마들린 스탠호프의 지난 내력을 낱낱이 늘어놓을 필요는 없다. 그는 열일곱 살쯤에 이탈리아로 갔고, 밀라노의 살롱에서, 또 코모 호숫가에 빽빽이 늘어선 별장들 사이에서 그 빼어난 아름다움을 마음껏 써먹도록 허락받았다. 그는 아슬아슬하게 평판을 지켜 낸 숱한 모험으로 이름을 날렸고, 정작 제 마음은 한 번도 다치지 않은 채 열두 명이나 되는 기사들의 심장을 결딴내 놓았다. 그의 매력을 두고 벌어진 다툼에서 피가 흘렀고, 그는 그런 결투의 소식을 즐거운 흥분과 함께 들었다. 한번은 그가 시동으로 변장하고 곁에 서서 제 연인이 쓰러지는 것을 지켜보았다는 이야기까지 있었다.\n\n흔히 그렇듯이, 그는 청혼자들 가운데 가장 못한 자와 결혼했다. 어째서 하필 파울로 네로니를 골랐는지는 지금 와서 이야기할 것도 없다. 가문도 재산도 없고 교황 근위대의 일개 대위에 지나지 않으며, 밀라노에 올라온 것이 그저 한몫 보러 온 것인지 아니면 밀정으로 온 것인지 알 수 없고, 성미는 사납고 태도는 느끼하며, 체구는 볼품없고 얼굴은 거무튀튀하고, 말이 어찌나 거짓되던지 시시각각 들통이 나던 그런 사내였다. 그럴 순간이 닥쳤을 때 그에게는 아마 달리 길이 없었을 것이다. 어쨌든 그 사내는 그의 남편이 되었고, 호수 지방에서 길게 신혼을 보낸 뒤 둘은 함께 로마로 갔다. 교황의 대위는 아내를 뒤에 남겨 두려고 애썼으나 헛일이었다.\n\n여섯 달 뒤 그는 불구가 된 몸으로, 그리고 어머니가 되어 아버지의 집에 당도했다. 기별 한 장 없이, 몸을 가릴 옷도 변변히 없이, 혼수를 빛내 주던 그 숱한 패물이라고는 하나도 지니지 않은 채였다. 아기는 밀라노에서 데려온 가난한 처녀의 품에 안겨 있었는데, 거기까지 함께 온 로마 하녀와 맞바꾼 아이였다. 그 하녀로 말하면, 여주인 말로는 향수병이 나서 돌아갔다는 것이었다. 이 부인이 제 로마 생활에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 이야기를 옮길 증인은 하나도 남기지 않겠다고 작정했음이 분명했다.\n\n그의 말로는 폐허에 오르다가 넘어져 무릎 힘줄을 결딴나게 다쳤다고 했다. 어찌나 심하게 다쳤던지 일어서면 제 키에서 8인치가 줄었고, 어찌나 심하게 다쳤던지 몸을 옮기려 들면 엉덩이를 내밀고 발을 뻗은 채 곱사등이보다도 볼품없는 모습으로 고통스럽게 몸을 끌 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 그는 두 번 다시 서지도 않고 스스로 몸을 옮기려 들지도 않겠다고 단번에 그리고 영영 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그를 뒤따라 이야기들이 도는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 네로니에게 모질게 학대를 당했으며 그 폭력 탓에 그런 사고를 당했다는 것이었다. 그야 어찌 되었든, 남편에 대해서는 별말이 없었으나 그 몇 마디만으로도 시뇨르 네로니를 다시 보거나 그 소식을 듣는 일은 없으리라는 것만은 집안 사람들에게 똑똑히 전해졌다. 가엾게 학대당한 이 미인을 옛 식구의 자리로 다시 받아들이는 데에도, 그 어린 딸을 스탠호프 집안의 지붕 아래 거두는 데에도 아무 이의가 없었다. 스탠호프 집안 사람들은 무정하기는 해도 이기적이지는 않았다. 모녀는 받아들여져 귀염을 받고 극진한 대접을 받았으며 한동안은 거의 떠받들리다시피 하다가, 이윽고 두 부모에게는 집 안의 큰 골칫거리로 여겨지게 되었다. 그러나 부인은 이미 그 집 안에 있었고 그대로 눌러앉아 제 뜻대로 살았다. 그 뜻이라는 것이 영국 성직자 집안의 관습과는 그리 어울리지 않았는데도 그러했다.\n\n마담 네로니는 세상에서 몸을 움직이는 일이라면 죄다 포기할 수밖에 없었으나, 세상 자체를 포기할 마음은 조금도 없었다. 얼굴의 아름다움은 조금도 상하지 않았고, 그 아름다움은 독특한 종류의 것이었다. 숱 많고 윤기 도는 갈색 머리는 그리스식으로 머리 둘레에 둘러 이마와 뺨을 될 수 있는 대로 많이 드러냈다. 이마는 좀 낮은 편이었으나 완벽한 윤곽과 진주 같은 흰빛 덕에 무척 아름다웠다. 눈은 길고 컸으며 놀랍도록 빛났으니, 루시퍼의 눈처럼 빛났다고 감히 말해도 된다면 그 광채의 깊이는 그렇게 말하는 것이 아마 가장 나으리라. 그것은 마주 보기에 무서운 눈이어서, 마음이 조용하고 심성이 무던한 남자라면 그런 적수와 창검을 겨루어 볼 엄두조차 아예 못 내게 만들 그런 눈이었다. 그 안에는 재기가 있었고 정념의 불길과 재치의 놀음이 있었으나 사랑은 없었다. 그 대신 잔인함이 있었고, 담대함과 남을 부리려는 욕심과 교활함과 남을 못되게 굴려는 마음이 있었다. 그러면서도 눈으로 치자면 그것은 대단히 아름다운 눈이었다. 속눈썹은 길고 흠잡을 데가 없었으며, 저를 흠모하는 사내의 얼굴을 오래도록 흔들림 없이 조금도 부끄러운 기색 없이 들여다보는 그 눈길은 그를 겁주는 동시에 홀렸다. 그는 아름다움을 뜨겁게 사랑하는 자라면 결코 벗어날 수 없는 바실리스크였다. 코와 입과 이와 턱과 목과 가슴은 완벽했으니, 열여덟 살 때보다 스물여덟 살인 지금이 훨씬 더 그러했다. 이런 매력이 여전히 그 얼굴에 이글거리고 있고 이런 기형이 그 몸매를 망쳐 놓았으니, 남들 앞에 나서되 오직 소파에 비스듬히 누운 모습으로만 나서기로 작정한 것이 무엇이 이상하겠는가.\n\n그 작정을 실행하는 일이 어렵지 않았던 것은 아니다. 그는 여전히 밀라노의 오페라를 드나들었고, 여전히 이따금 귀족들의 살롱에 모습을 드러냈다. 매력을 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 않고, 옷매무새를 흩뜨리지 않고, 흉한 곳을 드러내지도 않는 방식으로 마차에서 들려 나오고 들려 들어가게 했던 것이다. 언니가 늘 함께 다녔고 하녀가 하나, 하인이 또 하나, 그리고 격식을 차려야 하는 자리에는 둘이 따라붙었다. 이보다 적은 인원으로 그 목적을 이룬다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그러면서도 그는 가난한 형편에 기어이 그 목적을 이루어 냈다. 게다가 밀라노에서도 방탕한 축에 드는 이탈리아 젊은이들이 스탠호프 별장을 드나들며 그 긴 의자를 에워쌌으니, 아버지로서는 그리 흡족한 일이 아니었다. 이따금 그의 성미가 치밀어 올라 뺨에 검은 기운이 돌고 반기를 들 때도 있었으나, 그럴 때면 샬럿이 부엌 솜씨의 남다른 개가로 그를 달래 놓았고 그러면 한동안 만사가 다시 잔잔해졌다.\n\n마들린은 제 방과 제 몸, 그리고 제 여자다운 소지품을 꾸미는 데 온갖 화려하고 기발한 궁리를 부렸다. 이것이 무엇보다 잘 드러난 것이 저 쓰겠다고 만들어 둔 방문 명함이었다. 그런 물건이야 지금 같은 처지에는 별로 쓸 데가 없으리라고들 하겠으니, 아침 방문을 다닐 성싶지도 않은 까닭이다. 그러나 본인의 생각은 그렇지 않았다. 명함 가장자리에는 금박이 넓게 둘러져 있었고, 그 위에 석 줄로 이렇게 찍어 두었다.\n\nLa Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni. --Nata Stanhope.\n\n그리고 이름 위에는 번쩍이는 금박 소관을 얹어 두었는데, 과연 대단히 으리으리해 보였다. 어떻게 저를 두고 그런 이름을 지어낼 생각을 했는지는 설명하기 어렵다. 그 아버지는 다른 사람이 토머스라는 세례명을 받듯이 베시라는 세례명을 받았을 뿐이니, 그 이름을 제 것으로 삼을 권리로 말하면 조사이어 존스 씨의 딸이 스미스라는 성을 가진 남자와 결혼했다 하여 스스로 조사이어 스미스 부인이라 부를 권리나 매한가지였다. 금박 소관도 마찬가지로 어울리지 않았고, 어쩌면 그것을 박아 넣은 데에는 변명거리가 더더욱 없었다. 파울로 네로니는 이탈리아 귀족의 후예라 자칭할 만한 근거조차 손톱만큼도 없는 자였다. 그 두 사람이 영국에서 만났더라면 네로니는 아마 백작 행세를 했겠지만, 그들은 이탈리아에서 만났으니 그런 시늉을 했다가는 그저 우스운 꼴이 되었을 것이다. 그렇지만 소관이라는 것은 예쁜 장식이거니와, 가엾은 불구자가 제 명함에 그런 것을 얹어 놓고 위안을 삼는다면 누가 그것을 아까워하겠는가?\n\n남편에 대해서든 그 집안 사람들에 대해서든 그는 결코 입에 올리지 않았으나, 저를 흠모하는 이들 앞에서는 곧잘 결혼 생활과 홀로 남은 처지를 두고 알쏭달쏭하게 말을 흘렸고, 제 딸을 가리키며 황제들의 피를 이은 마지막 사람이라 부르곤 했다. 네로니의 혈통을 저 최악의 카이사르들이 나온 옛 로마 가문에 갖다 붙인 것이었다.\n\n이 “시뇨라”에게는 재능이 없지 않았고 어떤 종류의 부지런함도 없지 않았다. 그는 편지라면 지칠 줄 모르고 쓰는 사람이었고, 그 편지들은 우편 요금이 아깝지 않았다. 재치와 짓궂음과 풍자와 사랑과 관용주의 철학과 자유로운 종교관이, 그리고 때로는 아뿔싸, 걸쭉한 농지거리가 가득했던 것이다. 다만 무엇을 쓰느냐는 온전히 받는 사람에 달려 있었으니, 그는 도덕적인 젊은 처녀와 딱딱한 노부인만 아니라면 누구와도 편지를 주고받을 태세였다. 그는 시 비슷한 것도 썼는데 대개 이탈리아어였고, 짧은 이야기도 썼는데 대개 프랑스어였다. 잡다한 종류의 책을 많이 읽었고, 현대어에 관해서는 실로 크게 숙달해 있었다. 바체스터 남자들의 마음에 상처를 내러 이제 막 당도한 부인이 바로 이런 사람이었다.\n\n에설버트 스탠호프는 몇 가지 점에서 아래 누이를 닮았으나, 남자로서 그의 값어치는 여자로서 누이의 값어치만 못했다. 그의 큰 흠은, 재산 없는 사람의 아들이라면 마땅히 제 밥벌이를 해야 한다고 일러 주었을 그 원칙이 아예 없다는 것이었다. 그러도록 만들어 보려는 시도가 여러 번 있었으나 하나같이 무산되었는데, 그것은 그가 게으른 탓이라기보다 제 취향에 맞지 않는 일이라면 무엇에도 애쓰기 싫어하는 성미 탓이었다. 그는 이튼에서 교육을 받았고 성직자로 나설 예정이었으나, 케임브리지를 한 학기 만에 넌더리를 내며 떠났고 아버지에게는 법조계 공부를 하겠다고 알렸다. 그 준비로 독일의 대학에 다니는 것이 좋겠다고 생각하여 라이프치히로 갔다. 거기서 두 해를 머물며 독일어 지식과 미술 취향을 얻어 왔다. 그러면서도 여전히 법조계로 나갈 작정이어서 사무실을 얻고 어느 학식 있는 대가의 문하에 들기로 한 뒤 런던에서 한 철을 보냈다. 거기서 그는 제 소질이 죄다 예술가의 삶 쪽으로 기울어 있음을 깨닫고 그림으로 먹고살기로 마음먹었다. 그 목적으로 밀라노에 돌아가 로마로 갈 차림을 갖추었다. 화가로서라면 그는 밥벌이를 할 수도 있었을 것이니, 남달라지는 데 모자란 것은 오직 부지런함뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 로마에 이르자 그의 마음은 딴것에 팔려 버렸다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 집으로 돈을 부쳐 달라는 편지를 보내면서, 자기가 모교회로 개종했고 이미 예수회의 시자가 되었으며 유대인을 개종시키는 선교를 위해 다른 이들과 함께 팔레스타인으로 떠나려 한다고 알렸다. 그는 과연 유대 땅으로 갔으나, 유대인들을 개종시키기는커녕 도리어 그들에게 개종당했다. 그는 다시 집으로 편지를 보내, 모세야말로 세상에 완전한 율법을 준 유일한 사람이라는 것, 참된 메시아의 오심이 임박했다는 것, 팔레스타인에서 큰일이 벌어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 시도니아 집안의 한 사람을 만났는데 지극히 비범한 인물로서 지금 서유럽으로 가는 길이기에 자기가 권하여 길을 돌려 스탠호프 별장에 들르게 했다는 것을 알렸다. 그러고서 에설버트는 어머니와 두 누이가 부디 이 놀라운 예언자의 말에 귀 기울이기를 바란다고 했다. 아버지는 금전 사정상 그러실 수 없으리라는 것을 그도 알고 있었다. 그런데 이 시도니아는 예전에 그 집안의 다른 사람이 어느 젊은 영국 귀족을 어여삐 여겼던 것만큼 그를 어여삐 여기지는 않았다. 적어도 사자만 한 금덩이 무더기 따위는 안겨 주지 않았으므로, 유대교로 넘어간 에설버트는 또다시 그리스도 교회의 수입에 손을 벌릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n아버지가 다시는 돈을 부치지 않겠으며 유대인 따위는 들이지 않겠다고 어떻게 맹세했는지, 샬럿이 에설버트를 예루살렘에 무일푼으로 버려 둘 수는 없다고 어떻게 우겼는지, 그리고 “라 시뇨라 네로니”가 시도니아를 제 발치에 꿇리겠다고 어떻게 벼르었는지는 굳이 이야기할 것 없다. 돈은 부쳐졌고, 유대인은 정말로 왔다. 유대인은 정말로 왔으나 “라 시뇨라”의 취향에는 전혀 맞지 않았다. 그는 자그마하고 꾀죄죄한 노인이었고, 황금 사자를 마련해 준 적은 없었어도 젊은 스탠호프의 궁핍만은 어지간히 덜어 주었던 모양이다. 그는 박사에게서 런던 거래 은행 앞으로 끊은 어음을 받아 내기 전에는 별장을 떠나지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 버텼다.\n\n에설버트가 유대인으로 지낸 기간은 길지 않았다. 그는 이내 종교 문제에 아무 편견도 없는 몸으로, 그리고 조각가로 이름과 재산을 얻겠다는 굳은 결심을 품고 별장에 다시 나타났다. 로마에서 처음 빚어 본 모형 몇 점을 가지고 왔는데, 그것들이 정말이지 제법 가망을 보이는지라 아버지도 이 뜻을 밀어주느라 또 한 번 돈을 쓰게 되었다. 에설버트는 카라라에 공방을 차렸다, 아니 정확히는 셋방과 작업장을 얻었고, 거기서 대리석을 숱하게 망쳐 놓고 예쁘장한 조각상을 몇 점 만들었다. 그때 이후로, 그러니까 이제 4년째 그는 카라라와 별장을 오가며 지냈으나, 작업장에 머무는 기간은 점점 짧아지고 별장에 머무는 기간은 점점 길어졌다. 그도 그럴 것이 카라라란 영국 사람이 살고 싶어 할 만한 곳이 아니다.\n\n온 식구가 영국으로 떠날 때 그는 저 혼자 남지 않겠다고 작정했고, 큰누이의 도움을 얻어 아버지의 뜻을 꺾고 제 뜻을 관철했다. 자기는 주문을 받으러 영국에 가야 한다는 것이었다. 그러지 않고서야 어떻게 제 직업으로 돈을 만들겠느냐는 말이었다.\n\n생김새로 말하면 에설버트 스탠호프는 더없이 별난 사람이었다. 그가 아주 잘생겼다는 것만은 분명했다. 누이 마들린의 눈을 그대로 가졌으되, 쏘아보는 기운도 없고 그 딱딱하고 교활하고 잔인한 단단함도 없었다. 빛깔도 훨씬 옅어서, 어찌나 옅고 맑은 푸른빛인지 달리 눈에 띄는 것이 없더라도 그 눈 하나로 얼굴이 두드러졌다. 그와 함께 방에 들어서면 맨 먼저 보이는 것이 에설버트의 푸른 눈이었고, 방을 나서고 나서도 거의 맨 나중까지 잊히지 않는 것이 그 눈이었다. 옅은 빛깔 머리카락은 아주 길고 비단결 같아서 웃옷 위로 흘러내렸다. 수염은 성지에서 길러 온 것으로 족장처럼 덥수룩했다. 그는 결코 면도를 하지 않았고 다듬는 일도 드물었다. 그 수염은 윤이 나고 부드럽고 깨끗하여 도무지 밉지가 않았다. 부인네들이 명주실 대신 그것을 감아다가 제 수예에 놓고 싶어 할 만한 수염이었다. 낯빛은 희고 거의 발그레했다. 키는 작고 팔다리는 가늘었으나 몸매는 균형이 잡혔고, 목소리는 유난히 감미로웠다.\n\n태도와 옷차림도 그에 못지않게 남달랐다. 그에게는 영국 사람 특유의 mauvaise honte라고는 조금도 없었다. 누구에게든 상냥하게 굴기 위해 소개 따위가 필요하지 않았다. 그는 남자든 부인이든 처음 보는 사람에게 그런 격식 없이 말을 붙이는 것이 버릇이었고, 그러고도 면박을 당하는 일은 없는 듯했다. 그의 옷차림은 도무지 묘사할 수가 없으니 워낙 가지각색이었기 때문인데, 다만 그때그때 어울려 지내는 이들의 옷차림과는 빛깔로 보나 지음새로 보나 언제나 모든 원칙에서 정반대였다.\n\n그는 부인네들에게 사랑을 속삭이는 것이 몸에 밴 사람이었고, 그러면서 양심에 조금도 거리끼는 바가 없었으며 그런 짓이 잘못이라는 생각도 없었다. 제 마음이라는 것이 없어 흔들릴 것도 없었고, 인간이 그런 재앙을 겪는다는 사실을 말 그대로 알지 못했다. 그 문제를 깊이 생각해 본 적은 없지만, 만일 누가 물었더라면 그는 부인의 마음을 함부로 다룬다는 것은 그 부인이 세상에서 자리를 얻는 데 훼방을 놓는 일이라고 대답했을 것이다. 그러므로 그의 원칙은, 어떤 처녀가 결혼 상대로 삼음 직한 남자가 자리에 있다 싶으면 그 처녀에게 마음을 쏟지 못하게 막았다. 이런 식으로 그의 붙임성은 걸핏하면 그 자신의 재미를 가로막았으나, 눈에 드는 처녀마다 사랑을 끝까지 고백하지 않고 참는 데에는 그 밖의 다른 동기라고는 없었다.\n\n그러나 흔히들 부르는 대로 버티 스탠호프는 남녀 양쪽 모두에게 인기가 있었고, 이탈리아 사람들에게도 영국 사람들 못지않게 인기가 있었다. 그가 아는 사람의 범위는 무척 넓어서 온갖 부류를 아울렀다. 그는 신분을 우러르는 마음도 없었고 저보다 아래인 사람을 꺼리는 마음도 없었다. 영국 귀족과도, 독일 가게 주인과도, 로마 사제와도 스스럼없이 어울려 살아 왔다. 그에게는 사람이 다 거기서 거기였다. 그는 온갖 편견을 넘어서 있었다, 아니 그 아래에 있었다. 어떤 미덕도 그를 감동시키지 못했고 어떤 악덕도 그를 놀라게 하지 못했다. 그에게는 타고난 좋은 태도가 있어 가장 높은 자리에도 어울릴 사람처럼 보였으나, 그러면서 가장 낮은 자리에서도 결코 어색한 법이 없었다. 그에게는 원칙도, 남에 대한 배려도, 자존심도 없었고, 벌집 속의 수벌 노릇 말고 다른 무엇이 되고 싶은 마음도 없었다. 수벌로서 제게 넉넉할 만큼의 꿀만 얻을 수 있다면 말이다. 그런데 그 꿀로 말하면, 훗날의 그에게는 몫이 얼마 돌아오지 않으리라고 미리 점쳐 두어도 좋을 것이다.\n\n이것이 바로 이 무렵 바체스터 대성당 경내의 성직자 사교계에 느닷없이 끼어든 스탠호프 집안 사람들이었다. 이보다 더 기이한 결합은 아마 상상하기 어려울 것이다. 게다가 그들이 지금껏 알려지지도 않고 입에 오르내리지도 않던 채로 대성당 구내에 툭 떨어진 것도 아니었다. 만일 그랬더라면 새로 온 이들과 프라우디 패거리 사이든 그랜틀리 패거리 사이든 어우러지는 일은 도무지 있을 법하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 사정은 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 스탠호프 집안 사람들은 바체스터에서 이름만은 다들 알려져 있었고, 바체스터는 두 팔 벌려 그들을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다. 박사는 이 도시의 참사회원 가운데 하나요, 교구 목사 가운데 하나요, 기둥 가운데 하나였으며, 더구나 프라우디 쪽에서나 그랜틀리 쪽에서나 틀림없는 우군으로 셈해지고 있었다.\n\n그 자신은 어느 귀족의 아우였고 그 아내는 또 다른 귀족의 누이였는데, 두 귀족은 모두 휘그당 쪽으로 기운 사람들로서 새 주교와도 어떤 식으로든 연이 닿아 있었다. 슬로프 씨로서는 이만하면 적들이 자기를 앞질러 수를 쓰기 전에 스탠호프 박사를 제 편으로 끌어들일 수 있으리라는 큰 기대를 품기에 충분했다. 한편 늙은 수석 사제로 말하면, 아주 여러 해 전 박사에게 아직 성직자로서의 기력이 남아 있던 시절에 그의 승진 계획을 거들어 준 일이 있었다. 또한 역시 아주 여러 해 전, 스탠호프와 그랜틀리 두 박사는 젊은 성직자로서 옥스퍼드의 담화실에서 함께 즐거운 시절을 보낸 사이였다. 그러니 그랜틀리 박사도 이 새로 온 사람이 제 깃발 아래 설 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다.\n\n스탠호프 집안이 이제 어떤 재료들로 이루어져 있는지는 그들 가운데 누구도 꿈에도 몰랐다.\n\n# 제10장. 프라우디 부인의 접견회 — 상\n\n주교 내외는 첫 방문 때 바체스터에 사나흘밖에 머무르지 않았다. 앞서 보았듯이 주교는 자기 옥좌에 앉기는 했으나, 위계의 존엄을 잔뜩 불어넣으려 작정했던 그 자리에서의 태도는 전속 사제의 대담한 설교 탓에 적잖이 흐트러지고 말았다. 그는 감히 휘하 성직자들의 얼굴을 마주 보며, 제 심복이 자기를 대신해 하는 말이 실은 모두 자신의 뜻이라고 근엄한 표정으로 선언하지도 못했고, 그렇다고 슬로프 씨를 내던지고서 자기는 그 설교와 아무 상관이 없으며 그 일을 괘씸하게 여긴다고 주위 사람들에게 내보이지도 못했다.\n\n그리하여 그는 어정쩡한 태도로 신자들에게 축복을 내렸으니 스스로도 도무지 흡족하지 않았고, 그 문제를 두고 전속 사제에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못하는 마음으로 주교관까지 걸어 돌아왔다. 그러나 갈피를 잡지 못한 시간은 길지 않았다. 그가 미처 흰 삼베 소매 예복을 벗기도 전에 그의 온갖 수고를 함께 나누는 반려가 서재로 들어와, 자리에 앉기도 전에 이렇게 외쳤던 것이다.\n\n“주교님, 그보다 더 숭고하고 그보다 더 마음을 뒤흔들고 그보다 더 때에 들어맞는 강론을 평생에 들어 보신 적이 있으세요?”\n\n“글쎄, 여보. 하…흠…허!” 주교는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다.\n\n“주교님, 설마 그 설교가 못마땅하시다는 말씀을 하시려는 건 아니겠지요?”\n\n부인의 눈매에는 그 순간 주교가 못마땅해하는 것을 도무지 허락하지 않는 무엇이 있었다. 그는 못마땅해할 작정이라면 지금이 아니고서는 영영 기회가 없으리라고 느꼈으나, 동시에 그 지금이라는 것이 불가능하다는 것도 느꼈다. 슬로프 씨의 설교가 때에 맞지 않고 무례하며 성가신 것이었다고 품속의 아내에게 말할 만한 배포는 그에게 없었다.\n\n“아니, 아니오.” 주교가 대답했다. “못마땅하다고야 할 수 없지. 아주 영리한 설교였고 뜻도 아주 좋았고, 감히 말하건대 큰 유익을 끼칠 게요.” 이 마지막 칭찬은 앞서 한 말이 프라우디 부인을 조금도 만족시키지 못하는 것을 보고 덧붙인 것이었다.\n\n“그러기를 바라요.” 부인이 말했다. “그리고 그만한 소리를 들을 만했던 것도 분명하고요. 주교님, 하딩 씨가 기도문을 노래하는 그 꼴만큼 연극 놀음 같은 것을 평생에 들어 보신 적이 있으세요? 저는 슬로프 씨에게 그 모든 것이 바뀔 때까지 그 문제를 두고 설교를 이어 가 달라고 청할 작정이에요. 적어도 우리 대성당에서만큼은 단정하고 경건하고 겸손한 아침 예배를 보아야지요. 이제 여기서 연극 놀음은 더 이상 없어야 합니다.” 그러고서 부인은 종을 울려 점심을 청했다.\n\n주교는 대성당이며 수석 사제며 선창자며 예배에 관해 아내보다 더 많이 알았고, 주교의 권한에 대해서도 더 많이 알았다. 그러나 지금은 이 이야기를 그만 접어 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다.\n\n“여보,” 그가 말했다. “아무래도 화요일에는 런던으로 돌아가야 할 것 같소. 내가 여기 머물러 있으면 정부 쪽이 여간 불편하지 않을 모양이오.”\n\n주교는 이 제안에 아내가 반대하지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 또 이렇게 싸움터에서 물러나 있으면 자기가 없는 동안에 격전의 열기가 지나가 버릴지도 모른다고 느꼈다.\n\n“슬로프 씨는 물론 여기 남는 거지요?” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 물론이오.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n그리하여 주교는 제 주교관에 한 주도 채 머무르지 못한 채 바체스터에서 달아났고, 두 달 동안 그리로 돌아오지 않았으니, 그제야 런던의 사교 철이 끝난 것이었다. 그동안 슬로프 씨가 놀고 있었던 것은 아니지만, 대성당에서 다시 설교해 보려 들지는 않았다. 연속 설교를 하라고 권하는 프라우디 부인의 편지에 대해서는, 어쨌든 부인이 그 자리에 계셔서 직접 들으실 수 있게 될 때까지 그런 일은 미루고 싶다고 변명해 두었다.\n\n그는 그 시간을 프라우디·슬로프 당파를, 아니 차라리 슬로프·프라우디 당파를 다지는 데 썼고, 그 시간을 헛되이 쓰지 않았다. 그는 수석 사제와 참사회를 건드리지는 않았다. 다만 이 일에 관해서는 주교의 뜻이 이러하시다느니 저 일에 관해서는 주교의 심기가 저러하시다느니 하는 것을 슬쩍슬쩍 흘려 알려 주었을 뿐인데, 그 방식이란 그들에게는 충분히 짜증스러우면서도 화를 낼 구실은 되지 못하는 것이었다. 그는 시 변두리의 먼 교회에서 한두 번 설교를 했으나 대성당 예배에 대해서는 한마디도 언급하지 않았다. 그는 「주교 관하 바체스터 안식일 학교」 두 곳을 세우는 일에 착수했고, 「주교 관하 바체스터 청년 안식일 저녁 강연실」을 두겠다는 계획을 공고했으며, 바체스터 지선 철도 지배인에게 서너 통의 편지를 써서 주교께서 일요일 열차 운행이 중단되기를 얼마나 간절히 바라시는지를 알렸다.\n\n그러나 두 달이 지나자 주교와 부인이 다시 나타났고, 그 귀환의 기쁜 전조로서 최대 규모의 야회를 열겠다는 약속으로 자신들의 왕림을 미리 알렸다. 초대장은 런던에서 발송되었으니, 브루턴 스트리트 날짜가 찍혀 있었고, 저 가증스러운 안식일 파괴자인 철도 편에 커다란 갈색 종이 꾸러미로 슬로프 씨 앞으로 부쳐졌다. 바체스터 시내와 그 둘레 2마일 안에서 스스로 신사라 일컫는 자, 스스로 숙녀라 일컫는 자는 하나도 빠짐없이 포함되었다. 초대장은 교구의 모든 성직자에게 보내졌고, 그 밖에 성직계에서 이름 있는 여러 사람에게도 보내졌는데, 주교는, 적어도 주교의 부인은, 그들이 오지 않으리라는 데 상당한 자신이 있었다. 그러나 이 모임은 어디까지나 발 디딜 틈 없이 붐비고 남의 눈에 띄는 행사가 될 작정이었으므로, 수백 명을 맞을 채비가 갖추어졌다.\n\n그리하여 이제 그랜틀리파 사이에서는 주교의 부름에 응할 것인가 말 것인가를 두고 적잖은 소동이 일었다. 처음에는 다들 자기 몫은 물론 아내와 딸들 몫까지 가장 짤막한 사절의 말을 보내고 싶은 심정이었다. 그러나 차츰 정략이 격정을 이겼다. 부주교는 대성당 성직자들이 주교에게 노여워할 정당한 빌미를 주도록 내버려 둔다면 그것이야말로 제 실책이 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다. 그들은 다 함께 모여 의논한 끝에 가기로 뜻을 모았다. 사람은 아무리 싫더라도 그 직분만은 존중할 뜻이 있음을 보여 주자는 것이었다. 그들은 가기로 했다. 늙은 수석 사제도 반 시간만이라도 기어들어 갈 참이었다. 학무 참사원와 재무 참사원과 부주교와 참사회원들과 하급 참사원들이 모두 가고, 모두 아내를 데려가기로 했다. 하딩 씨에게는 특히 그리하라는 당부가 있었으니, 그는 프라우디 부인에게서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져 있으리라고 마음속으로 다짐했다. 그리고 볼드 부인도 가기로 마음을 굳혔는데, 아버지가 딸에게 그런 희생까지 치를 필요는 없다고 일러 주었건만 그러했다. 바체스터 사람이 다 간다는데 엘리너나 메리 볼드나 자기들이 빠질 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 자기들에게도 따로 초대장이 오지 않았던가? 게다가 전속 사제가 더할 나위 없이 정중한 말씨로 쓴 짤막한 별지가 그 커다란 주교의 초대 카드에 함께 들어 있지 않았던가?\n\n그리고 스탠호프 일가는 하나도 빠짐없이 갈 참이었다. 그 굼뜬 어머니조차 이런 자리라면 그만큼은 몸을 일으킬 것이었다. 그들은 이제 막 도착한 참이었다. 초대장은 사택에서 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 바체스터에서 그들을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 바체스터 사교계에 자신들을 내보이는 데 이보다 더 좋은 기회가 어디 있겠는가? 부주교 내외 같은 몇몇 옛 벗이 찾아와 박사와 그 맏딸을 만나 보기는 했으나, 이 집안의 정수라 할 이들은 아직 알려지지 않은 터였다.\n\n박사는 실은 속으로 시뇨라가 주교의 초대에 응하는 것을 막고 싶었으나, 정작 본인은 응하기로 단단히 마음을 정해 놓고 있었다. 제 딸이 주교관까지 들려 들어가는 것을 아버지가 부끄러워한다 한들, 그녀에게는 그런 감정이 없었다.\n\n“가고말고.” 오는 사람이라곤 죄다 목사와 목사의 아내들뿐일 거라는 말로 언니가 부드럽게 말리려 하자 그녀가 한 말이었다. “목사라 해도 그 검은 옷만 벗겨 놓으면 여느 남자와 별로 다를 게 없겠지. 그 아내들로 말하자면, 나를 성가시게 굴지는 못할걸. 아버지한테는 나는 집에 남아 있을 생각이 조금도 없다고 전해 줘.”\n\n아버지에게 그 말이 전해졌고, 그는 굽히는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없다고 느꼈다. 또한 이제 와서 제 자식들을 부끄러워해 봐야 소용없다는 것도 느꼈다. 그 애들이 그런 모양이 된 것은 제 밑에서 그리된 것이니, 제가 잠자리를 그렇게 폈으면 그 위에 누울 수밖에 없고, 제가 씨를 그렇게 뿌렸으면 그 곡식을 거둘 수밖에 없었다. 물론 그가 이런 말로 이런 생각을 입 밖에 낸 것은 아니지만, 그 생각의 요지는 그러했다. 그가 마들린이 주교의 손님 축에 끼는 것을 꺼린 까닭은 딸이 다리를 쓰지 못해서가 아니라, 딸이 늘 하던 대로 유혹의 수를 부려서 영국 부인네들의 예법으로는 도저히 못마땅해하지 않을 수 없는 짓을 하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 이런 일들은 이탈리아에서 그를 성가시게는 했어도 충격을 주지는 않았다. 거기서는 아무도 충격을 받지 않았다. 그러나 여기 바체스터에서, 여기 동료 목사들 틈에서는, 그런 것이 남의 눈에 띄는 것이 부끄러웠다. 그의 심정은 그러했으나 그는 그것을 눌러 두었다. 형제 성직자들이 충격을 받은들 어떠랴! 시집간 딸의 몸가짐이 너무 자유롭다고 해서 그들이 그의 성직록을 빼앗을 수는 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n어쨌든 시뇨라 네로니에게는 자기가 누구에게 충격을 줄까 봐 두려워하는 마음이 조금도 없었다. 그녀의 야심은 한바탕 소동을 일으키는 것, 바체스터의 남자라는 남자가 대개 목사인 터이니 그 목사들을 제 발치에 꿇리는 것, 그리고 될 수만 있다면 목사의 아내라는 아내는 하나도 남김없이 새파란 질투에 사로잡혀 집으로 돌아가게 하는 것이었다. 그녀에게 너무 늙은 남자란 없었고, 너무 젊은 남자도 거의 없었다. 너무 거룩한 남자도 없었고 너무 세속적인 남자도 없었다. 그녀는 주교 자신을 옭아맨 다음 주교의 부인 앞에서 코웃음을 칠 채비까지 완전히 되어 있었다. 그녀는 성공을 의심하지 않았으니, 여태 늘 성공해 온 까닭이었다. 다만 한 가지만은 절대로 필요했다. 소파 하나를 통째로 차지해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n스탠호프 박사 내외와 그 식구들에게 보낸 초대장은 겉봉에 슬로프 씨의 이름이 적힌 봉투에 담겨 왔다. 시뇨라는 프라우디 부인이 아직 주교관에 와 있지 않으며 전속 사제가 모든 일을 도맡아 처리하고 있다는 것을 곧 알아냈다. 부인보다는 그에게 청을 넣는 편이 훨씬 그녀의 방식에 맞았으므로, 그녀는 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 짤막한 쪽지를 그에게 써 보냈다. 다섯 줄 안에 그녀는 모든 것을 설명했으니, 바체스터의 주교와 그 부인 같은 분들과, 덧붙여 슬로프 씨와도 알고 지내고 싶지 않기란 자기로서는 얼마나 불가능한 일인지를 밝히고, 자신의 딱한 처지를 그려 보이고는, 소파까지 들려 가게 해 달라고 청하는 이 지나친 당돌함을 프라우디 부인께서 용서해 주시리라 믿는다는 말로 맺었다. 그러고는 자신의 그 아름다운 명함 한 장을 동봉했다. 답으로 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게서 그에 못지않게 정중한 답장을 받았다. 큰 계단 바로 위 널찍한 응접실에 오로지 그녀만을 위한 소파 하나를 마련해 두겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 이제 야회 날이 왔다. 주교와 부인은 그런 지체 높은 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 그 뜻깊은 날 아침에야 런던에서 내려왔으나, 슬로프 씨는 모든 것이 제대로 갖추어지도록 밤낮으로 애를 썼다. 할 일이 많았다. 주교관에서 손님을 치른 것이 언제였는지는 하늘이나 알 일이었다. 새 가구가 필요했고, 새 냄비와 새 팬이, 새 찻잔과 받침이, 새 접시와 그릇이 필요했다. 프라우디 부인은 처음에 먹고 마시는 따위 상스러운 일에까지 몸을 굽힐 생각은 없다고 선언했으나, 슬로프 씨가 말로, 아니 실은 편지로 그 절약심을 돌려놓았다. 주교는 마땅히 손님 접대에 힘써야 하며, 손님 접대란 곧 먹고 마시는 것이었다. 그리하여 야식은 허락되었다. 다만 손님들은 선 채로 먹어야 했다.\n\n그 집 2층에는 서로 통하는 방이 넷 있었는데, 응접실 둘과 접견실 하나와 프라우디 부인의 규방이라 불렸다. 옛날에는 그중 하나가 그랜틀리 주교의 침실이었고 또 하나는 그의 거실 겸 서재였다. 그러나 지금 주교는 뒤채의 작은 거실로 옮겨졌고, 성직자들이 제 좁은 골방에 다 들어가지 못할 만큼 떼 지어 몰려오거든 식당에서 맞으면 그만이라는 말을 들어야 했다. 그는 굽히기를 꺼렸으나, 잠깐 옥신각신한 끝에 굽혔다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 제 방들을 둘러보며 가슴이 한껏 부풀었다. 그 방들은 정말이지 대단히 웅장했다. 적어도 촛불을 켜 놓으면 그렇게 보일 것이었고, 게다가 칭찬할 만큼 알뜰하게 꾸며 놓은 것이었다. 큰 방은 사람이 가득하고 빛이 가득하면 보기가 좋은 법이니, 크고 가득 차고 밝기 때문이다. 값비싼 장식과 호사스러운 가구를 요구하는 것은 작은 방이다. 프라우디 부인은 이것을 알았고 그것을 최대한으로 활용했다. 그래서 그녀는 방마다 천장에 등구 열두 개짜리 커다란 가스등을 매달아 놓았다.\n\n손님들은 10시에 도착하고, 야식은 12시부터 1시까지 이어지고, 1시 반에는 모두 물러가기로 되어 있었다. 마차는 시내 쪽 문으로 들어와 바깥쪽 문으로 나가기로 했다. 마차더러 손님을 태우러 오라는 시각은 1시 15분 전이었다. 모든 것이 훌륭하게 처리되었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없이 요긴한 사람이었다.\n\n9시 반에 주교와 부인과 세 딸이 널찍한 접견실로 들어섰는데, 그 꼴이 여간 장중하고 여간 엄숙하지 않았다. 슬로프 씨는 아래층에서 포도주에 관한 마지막 지시를 내리고 있었다. 그는 부목사며 시골 교구 사제며 그 딸린 식구들에게는 대성당 경내의 고위 성직자들에게 내는 것만큼 후한 물건이 필요치 않다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 이런 일에는 요긴한 등급이라는 것이 있게 마련이니, 구석의 바깥쪽 보조 탁자에는 한 다스에 20실링짜리 마르살라면 아주 넉넉했다.\n\n“주교님.” 주교가 자리에 앉으려 하자 부인이 말했다. “미안하지만 그 소파에는 앉지 마세요. 그건 어떤 부인을 위해 따로 비워 두어야 하는 것이니까요.”\n\n주교는 벌떡 일어나 등나무 자리 의자에 옮겨 앉았다. “부인이라니?” 그가 순순히 물었다. “여보, 어떤 특정한 부인을 말하는 게요?”\n\n“그래요, 주교님, 어떤 특정한 부인이에요.” 아내는 설명하기를 마다하며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“그분은 다리가 없대요, 아버지.” 막내딸이 킥킥거리며 말했다.\n\n“다리가 없다고!” 주교가 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다.\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리, 네티, 무슨 그런 헛소리를 하니.” 올리비아가 말했다. “다리는 있는데 쓰지를 못하는 거야. 늘 누워 있어야 해서 어디를 가든 남자 서넛이 들고 다닌대.”\n\n“어머나, 별일이야!” 어거스타가 말했다. “늘 남자 넷한테 들려 다닌다니! 나 같으면 정말 싫을 것 같아. 어머니, 제 뒤가 제대로 되었나요? 벌어진 것 같은 느낌이 드는데요.” 그러면서 그녀는 근심 어린 어머니에게 등을 돌려 보였다.\n\n“벌어졌지! 그렇고말고.” 어머니가 말했다. “속치마 끈이 1야드나 빠져나와 있잖니. 네가 남에게 보일 만한 꼴인지 아닌지 살펴보는 수고조차 하지 않는다면, 내가 리처즈 부인에게 무엇 하러 그렇게 후한 삯을 주는지 모르겠구나.” 그러고서 프라우디 부인은 이쪽 끈을 쑤셔 넣고 저쪽 옷자락을 잡아당기고 딸을 떠밀었다 흔들었다 하고는, 이제 되었다고 선언했다.\n\n“그런데 말이오.” 그 알 수 없는 부인과 그 다리에 대한 궁금증으로 죽을 지경이 된 주교가 다시 입을 열었다. “대체 그 소파를 쓸 사람이 누구요? 네티야, 그 사람 이름이 무엇이냐?”\n\n현관문을 우레같이 두드리는 소리가 이야기를 끊었다. 프라우디 부인은 일어서서 몸을 가볍게 흔들어 매무새를 잡고, 거울을 들여다보며 두건의 양쪽을 매만졌다. 딸들은 저마다 발끝으로 서서 가슴에 단 리본을 고쳐 달았고, 슬로프 씨는 한 번에 세 계단씩 뛰어 위층으로 올라왔다.\n\n“그런데 그게 누구냐, 네티야?” 주교가 막내딸에게 속삭였다.\n\n“라 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니예요.” 딸이 속삭여 답했다. “그리고 아무도 그 소파에 앉지 못하게 하셔야 해요.”\n\n“라 시뇨라 마들린 비시니로니라!” 얼떨떨해진 고위 성직자는 혼자 중얼거렸다. 아우드의 베굼이 온다고 들었다 한들, 서쪽 섬나라의 포마라 여왕이 온다고 들었다 한들 이보다 더 놀라지는 않았을 것이다. 라 시뇨라 마들린 비시니로니라니, 딛고 설 다리가 없어서 제 응접실의 소파를 미리 청해 둔 여인이라니! 대체 누구란 말인가? 그러나 그는 이제 더 캐물을 수가 없었으니, 스탠호프 박사 내외가 왔다고 아뢰는 소리가 났기 때문이다. 그 내외는 시뇨라가 넉넉한 시간을 들여 편안하게 마차에 꾸려져 실릴 수 있도록, 시각보다 조금 일찍 앞질러 보내진 것이었다.\n\n주교는 참사회원의 부인에게 온통 미소였고, 주교의 부인은 참사회원에게 온통 미소였다. 슬로프 씨가 소개되었고, 말씀을 그토록 많이 들은 분과 알게 되어 기쁘다고 했다. 박사는 아주 깊이 허리를 굽혀 인사한 다음, 슬로프 씨에 관해서는 그 인사말을 되돌려 줄 수 없다는 듯한 표정을 지었으니, 실상 그는 슬로프 씨에 관해 들은 바가 하나도 없었던 것이다. 박사는 그토록 오래 나가 있었어도 영국 신사를 보면 알아보는 사람이었다.\n\n그러고 나자 손님들이 떼 지어 몰려들었다. 퀴버풀 씨 내외와 장성한 세 딸. 채드윅 씨 내외와 세 딸. 몸집 큰 학무 참사원와 그 부인과 옥스퍼드에서 온 성직자 아들. 딸린 식구 하나 없는 여윈 체구의 자그마한 박사. 엘리너와 볼드 양을 데리고 온 하딩 씨. 여윈 노처녀에게 몸을 기댄 수석 사제, 그 딸은 이제 그와 함께 사는 하나뿐인 자식으로, 돌과 양치식물과 초목과 벌레에 매우 밝은 부인이며 꽃잎에 관한 책을 한 권 쓴 이였다. 트리폴 양은 그 나름으로 놀라운 여인이었다. 변호사 피니 씨도 부인과 함께 눈에 띄었는데, 그를 응접실에서 마주친 적이 한 번도 없던 많은 이들이 여간 당황하지 않았다. 바체스터의 의사 다섯이 모두 왔고, 은퇴한 약제사 겸 이 뽑는 사람인 늙은 스칼펜도 왔으니, 그는 주교의 초대장을 받고서야 비로소 제가 상류층에 속한다고 여기는 법을 배운 사람이었다. 이어서 부주교 내외가 맏딸 그리젤다를 데리고 왔는데, 열일곱 살의 이 아가씨는 호리호리하고 창백하고 남 앞에 나서지 않는 성품이라 어머니 곁에 바짝 붙어 서서 조용히 살피는 눈으로 세상을 내다보았으니, 세월이 무르익히기만 하면 대단한 미인이 되리라는 조짐이 있었다.\n\n그리하여 방들은 가득 찼고, 여기저기 무리가 지어졌고, 새로 오는 이마다 주교께 인사를 올리고는 그 어른의 관심을 너무 오래 붙들어 두는 무례를 범하지 않으려 지나쳐 갔다. 부주교는 스탠호프 박사와 아주 정답게 악수를 나누었고, 그랜틀리 부인은 박사의 부인 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 그리고 프라우디 부인은 잘 절제된 우아함으로 이리저리 옮겨 다니며, 슬로프 씨가 포도주를 두고 하던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 손님의 격에 맞추어 제 호의의 분량을 되어 나누어 주었다. 그러나 소파는 여전히 비어 있었고, 스물다섯 명의 부인과 다섯 명의 신사가 빈틈없는 전속 사제에게 정중하게 저지당한 뒤였다.\n\n“어째서 오지 않는 게지?” 주교는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그의 마음이 온통 시뇨라에게 사로잡혀 있어서, 주교답게 처신하는 법조차 거의 잊을 지경이었다.\n\n마침내 마차 한 대가 현관 층계 앞으로 내달아 왔는데, 그날 저녁 그곳에 온 어떤 탈것과도 사뭇 다른 방식이었다. 그야말로 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다. 응접실에 서 있다가 그 소리를 들은 박사는 제 딸이 오는 줄을 알고, 딸이 들어서는 것을 보지 않아도 될 가장 먼 구석으로 물러났다. 프라우디 부인은 무언가 중대한 일이 벌어지고 있음을 느끼고 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다. 주교는 라 시뇨라 비시니로니가 드디어 왔다는 것을 본능적으로 알아차렸고, 슬로프 씨는 거들려고 현관으로 서둘러 나갔다.\n\n그러나 그는 현관 층계에서 마주친 그 행렬에 하마터면 떠밀려 넘어져 밟힐 뻔했다. 그는 그런대로 몸을 추슬러 일으키고는 그 행렬을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 시뇨라는 머리부터 앞세워 옮겨졌는데, 머리는 오라비와 이 일에 익숙한 이탈리아인 하인이 맡았고, 발은 하녀와 이탈리아인 시동이 맡았으며, 샬럿 스탠호프가 모든 일이 마땅한 품위와 예법에 맞게 이루어지는지 살피며 뒤따랐다. 이런 식으로 그들은 손쉽게 응접실까지 올라갔고, 사람들 사이로 넓은 길이 열리자 시뇨라는 무사히 제 자리에 놓였다. 그녀는 그것이 오른쪽 소파인지 왼쪽 소파인지 알아 오라고 미리 하인을 보내 두었으니, 그에 맞추어 옷을 차려입어야 했기 때문이고, 특히 팔찌가 그러했다.\n\n그리고 그 차림은 참으로 잘 어울렸다. 흰 벨벳이었고, 가슴께에 진주를 놓아 짠 값진 흰 레이스 말고는 다른 장식이 없었으며, 소매 언저리에도 같은 것을 둘렀다. 이마에는 붉은 벨벳 띠를 둘렀는데, 그 한복판에는 모자이크로 새긴 화려한 큐피드가 빛나고 있었으니, 날개의 빛깔은 더없이 고운 하늘빛이었고 통통한 두 볼의 빛깔은 더없이 맑은 분홍빛이었다. 그 자세 때문에 드러낼 수밖에 없는 한쪽 팔에는 저마다 돌이 다른 화려한 팔찌 셋을 끼고 있었다. 소파 위 그녀의 몸 아래로, 그리고 쿠션과 머리맡을 덮어, 진홍빛 비단 망토인지 숄인지가 펼쳐져 온몸 밑으로 들어가서는 발을 감추고 있었다. 그렇게 차려입고 그런 모습으로, 그토록 아름다우면서도 그토록 미동도 없이, 흰 옷의 티 없는 광채가 그 아래 깔린 빛깔에 도드라지고 힘을 얻은 채로, 그 사랑스러운 머리와 크고 대담하고 반짝이며 똑바로 응시하는 두 눈을 하고 있으니, 남자든 여자든 그녀를 바라보지 않고는 배길 도리가 없었다.\n\n몇 분 동안은 남자도 여자도 달리 어쩌지 못했다.\n\n그녀를 떠메고 온 이들도 눈여겨볼 만했다. 하인 셋은 이탈리아 사람이었는데, 제 나라에서라면 별날 것이 없을지 몰라도 바체스터의 주교관에서는 여간 별나지 않았다. 특히 사내가 사람들의 눈길을 끌었고, 그가 벗인지 하인인지 몇몇 사람의 마음에 의심을 불러일으켰다. 에설버트에 대해서도 같은 의심이 일었다. 사내는 헐렁하게 지은 흔한 검은 나사 모닝코트 차림이었다. 그는 경쾌하고 살집 있고 흡족해 보이는 말끔한 얼굴을 하고 있어서 수염이라고는 한 오라기도 보이지 않았고, 목에는 헐렁한 검은 비단 목수건을 두르고 있었다. 주교가 그에게 인사를 해 보려 했으나, 잘 훈련된 사내는 주교를 아랑곳하지 않고 아주 태연하게 방을 걸어 나갔으며, 여자와 소년이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n에설버트 스탠호프는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 하늘색으로 차려입었다. 그는 사냥복처럼 네모지게 재단한, 아주 짧고 더할 나위 없이 헐렁한 푸른 상의를 걸쳤는데, 안감은 하늘빛 비단이었다. 푸른 공단 조끼를 입었고, 목 아래를 산호 고리로 여민 푸른 목수건을 둘렀으며, 발이 거의 가려질 만큼 아주 헐렁한 푸른 바지를 입었다. 부드럽고 윤나는 그의 턱수염은 그 어느 때보다도 부드럽고 윤이 났다.\n\n한 번 실수를 저지른 주교는 이 사람도 하인인 줄 알고 그가 지나가도록 길을 비켜 주려 했다. 그러나 에설버트가 곧 그 오해를 바로잡아 주었다.\n\n# 제11장. 프라우디 부인의 접견회 — 하\n\n“바체스터 주교님이시겠지요?” 버티 스탠호프가 스스럼없이 손을 내밀며 말했다. “뵙게 되어 참으로 반갑습니다. 여기는 자리가 꽤나 비좁군요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n사실이 그러했다. 두 사람은 소파 머리맡 뒤쪽으로 밀려나 있었으니, 한쪽은 손님을 맞으려고 기다리다가, 다른 한쪽은 그 손님을 나르다가 그리된 것인데, 이제는 제 몸을 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다.\n\n주교는 얼른 손을 내밀고 늘 하던 대로 다듬어 둔 작은 인사를 하며, 뵙게 되어 반갑— 하고는 더 나아가지 못했다. 이 벗이 시뇨르인지 백작인지 공작인지 알 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n“누이 때문에 여러분이 정말 큰 수고를 하시는군요.” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“천만의 말씀입니다!” 주교는 라 시뇨라 비시니로니를 맞이하게 되어 더없이 기쁘다고 — 적어도 말은 그렇게 했다 — 하고는 소파 앞쪽으로 비집고 돌아가려 애썼다. 어쨌든 이 기이한 손님들이 남매라는 것만은 알아냈다. 남자 쪽은 시뇨르 비시니로니이거나, 아니면 사정에 따라 백작이나 공작이겠거니 짐작했다. 영어를 그토록 잘하는 것이 놀라웠다. 외국식 억양이 살짝 묻어날 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다.\n\n“대체로 보아 바체스터가 마음에 드십니까?” 버티가 물었다.\n\n주교는 위엄 있는 표정을 지으며, 바체스터가 마음에 든다고 말했다.\n\n“여기 오신 지는 얼마 되지 않으셨지요, 아마?” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“예, 얼마 되지 않았습니다.” 주교는 그렇게 말하고, 소파 등받이와 육중한 교구 목사 사이로 다시 한번 빠져나가 보려 했다. 그 목사는 소파 너머로 시뇨라의 갖가지 표정을 넋 놓고 바라보는 중이었다.\n\n“전에는 주교가 아니셨지요, 그렇지요?”\n\n프라우디 박사는 이번이 자신이 맡은 첫 교구라고 설명했다.\n\n“아, 그럴 줄 알았습니다.” 버티가 말했다. “그래도 이따금 자리를 옮기시기도 하지요, 안 그렇습니까?”\n\n“전임이 이따금 이루어지기는 합니다.” 프라우디 박사가 말했다. “그러나 예전만큼 잦지는 않지요.”\n\n“요즘은 그 자리들 값을 죄다 거의 같은 액수로 깎아 놓았다지요?” 버티가 말했다.\n\n여기에는 주교도 차마 아무 대답을 내놓을 수 없어, 다시 한번 그 목사를 밀어 보려 했다.\n\n“하지만 일이야 자리마다 다르겠지요?” 버티가 이어 말했다. “여기 바체스터는 할 일이 많습니까?” 이는 해군성의 젊은 서기가 재무성의 동료 신참에게 똑같은 질문을 던질 때 쓸 법한 바로 그 말투였다.\n\n“잉글랜드 국교회 주교의 일은,” 프라우디 박사가 자못 위엄을 갖추어 말했다. “쉽지 않습니다. 짊어져야 할 책임이 참으로 막중하지요.”\n\n“그렇습니까?” 버티가 그 놀라운 푸른 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다. “글쎄요, 저는 책임이라면 겁내 본 적이 없습니다. 저도 한때는 주교가 되어 볼까 생각한 적이 있지요.”\n\n“주교가 되어 볼까 생각을 하셨다니!” 프라우디 박사가 몹시 놀라 말했다.\n\n“그러니까 목사 말입니다. 아시다시피 먼저 목사가 되고 나중에 주교가 되는 것이지요. 한번 시작했더라면 끝까지 붙어 있었을 겁니다. 하지만 대체로 저는 로마 교회가 제일 마음에 듭니다.”\n\n주교로서는 그 문제를 논할 수 없었으므로 잠자코 있었다.\n\n“가령 저희 아버지를 보십시오.” 버티가 이어 말했다. “아버지는 끝까지 붙어 있지 않으셨지요. 같은 말을 그토록 자주 되풀이하는 것이 싫으셨던 모양입니다. 그건 그렇고 주교님, 저희 아버지를 뵌 적이 있으십니까?”\n\n주교는 어느 때보다도 더 놀랐다. 그의 아버지를 본 적이 있느냐고? “없습니다.” 그는 대답했다. 아직 그런 기쁨을 누리지 못했으며, 그리되기를 바란다고. 그렇게 말하면서 그는, 언젠가 그럴 힘이 생긴다면 저 뚱뚱하고 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 목사를 호되게 눌러 주리라 마음먹었다.\n\n“아버지는 이 방 어딘가에 계십니다.” 버티가 말했다. “곧 나타나시겠지요. 그건 그렇고, 유대인에 대해서는 잘 아십니까?”\n\n마침내 주교는 빠져나갈 길을 보았다. “실례하겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “아무래도 방을 한 바퀴 돌아야 해서요.”\n\n“그럼 저도 그 뒤를 따라가야겠군요.” 버티가 말했다. “지독하게 덥지 않습니까?” 이 말은 그가 바짝 붙어 서게 된 뚱뚱한 목사에게 건넨 것이었다. “이 소파를 방에서 가장 나쁜 자리에 놓아 두었군요. 우리가 좀 옮겨 봅시다. 조심해, 마들린.”\n\n과연 소파는 그 뒤에 있는 사람들이 빠져나오기가 여간 어렵지 않게 놓여 있었다. 통로라고는 한 사람이면 막을 수 있는 좁은 틈 하나뿐이었다. 이는 잘못된 배치였고, 버티가 보기에는 손을 좀 보는 편이 나을 배치였다.\n\n“조심해, 마들린.” 그가 말하고는 뚱뚱한 목사를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. “가볍게 한 번 미는 것만 좀 도와주십시오.”\n\n목사의 체중이 소파에 실려 있던 터라, 버티가 일부러 일으킨 그 움직임에 저도 모르게 온 힘을 보태어 속도와 기세를 더해 주었다. 소파는 제자리를 박차고 나가 방 한가운데까지 절반이나 달려 나갔다. 프라우디 부인은 슬로프 씨와 함께 시뇨라 앞에 서서 너그럽고 사근사근하게 굴어 보려던 참이었다. 그러나 기분이 그리 좋지는 않았으니, 자신이 이 부인에게 말을 걸 때마다 부인이 슬로프 씨를 향해 대답한다는 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다. 슬로프 씨가 총애를 받는 것이야 틀림없지만, 프라우디 부인으로서는 전속 사제보다 덜 대접받을 생각이 조금도 없었다. 그리하여 부인이 위엄을 세우고 뻣뻣해지며 언짢아지기 시작할 무렵, 불행히도 소파의 바퀴가 그 레이스 자락에 걸려, 얼마나 되는지 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼의 장식을 통째로 뜯어 갔다. 주름이 뜯기는 소리, 바느질이 터지는 소리, 겹주름이 활짝 벌어지는 소리가 들렸고, 프릴이 떨어지고 옷폭이 드러나는 것이 보였다. 찢긴 레이스의 긴 잔해가 양탄자를 흉하게 어지럽힌 채, 소파를 굴린 그 고약한 바퀴에 여전히 매달려 있었다.\n\n화강암 포대를 쌓아 올리면 싸움터의 사내들 눈에 흡족하여, 그 견고함과 균형이 찬탄을 받는 법이다. 그것은 여러 해에 걸친 공이다. 말끔한 총안, 야무지게 마무리된 흉벽, 포곽을 갖춘 층층은 근대 과학의 온갖 솜씨를 보여 준다. 그러나 이윽고 작은 불티 하나가 그 믿을 수 없는 도화선에 닿으면, 먼지구름이 하늘로 솟아오르고, 그다음에는 흙먼지와 흉한 파편밖에는 보이지 않는다.\n\n우리는 유노가 제 아름다움을 무시당했을 때 어떠한 분노에 사로잡혔는지 알고 있다. 천상의 마음조차 어떠한 격정의 폭풍에 굴복할 수 있는지도 알고 있다. 이다산에서 유노가 파리스를 바라보았을 법한 바로 그 눈으로, 프라우디 부인은 소파 다리를 제 레이스 자락에 밀어 넣은 에설버트 스탠호프를 바라보았다.\n\n“아이고, 이 멍청아, 버티!” 무슨 일이 벌어졌으며 그 결과가 어찌 될지를 대번에 알아본 시뇨라가 말했다.\n\n“멍청이라!” 프라우디 부인이 그 말로는 필요한 뜻을 절반도 담아내지 못한다는 듯 되받았다. “내 저자에게 톡톡히 일러 주겠—” 그러고는 한눈에 최악을 파악하려고 둘러보다가, 지금은 우선 흩어진 옷의 잔해를 주워 모으는 것이 제 도리임을 깨달았다.\n\n버티는 제가 저지른 일을 보고 소파를 뛰어넘어 달려가, 노한 부인 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇었다. 그의 목적은 두말할 것 없이 찢긴 레이스를 바퀴에서 풀어내려는 것이었으나, 보기에는 마치 여신에게 용서를 비는 것 같았다.\n\n“거기서 손을 떼시오!” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. 어느 희곡 나부랭이에서 그 말을 건져 냈는지는 알 길이 없으나, 어쨌든 기억에 남아 있다가 이 자리에 때맞추어 위엄 있게 여겨진 모양이었다.\n\n“용서만 해 주신다면 요정들의 베틀로 날아가 이 망가진 것을 고쳐 오겠습니다.” 에설버트가 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 말했다.\n\n“거기서 손을 떼시오!” 프라우디 부인이 갑절로 힘을 주어, 노기가 터지기 직전으로 말했다. 요정 운운하는 이 말은 대놓고 하는 조롱이요, 자기를 웃음거리로 만들려는 수작이었다. 적어도 부인에게는 그렇게 보였다. “거기서 손을 떼시오!” 부인은 거의 비명을 질렀다.\n\n“제가 아닙니다. 이 빌어먹을 소파 탓이지요.” 버티가 애원하듯 부인의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 자기는 부인의 물건에 손을 대고 있지 않다는 것을 보이려고 두 손을 들어 올린 채, 그러면서도 여전히 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 말했다.\n\n이때 시뇨라가 웃었다. 크게는 아니었으나 들릴 만큼은 웃었다. 그리고 새끼를 빼앗긴 암범이 손 닿는 것이면 무엇에든 똑같은 분노로 달려들 듯, 프라우디 부인도 그 여자 손님에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n“부인!” 프라우디 부인은 그렇게 말했다. 그 눈에서 번쩍인 불길이 어떠했는지는 산문의 힘으로는 도저히 전할 수 없다.\n\n시뇨라는 잠시 그 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보다가, 오라비를 돌아보며 장난스럽게 말했다. “버티, 이 멍청아, 일어나.”\n\n그사이 주교와 슬로프 씨와 세 딸이 부인을 둘러싸고, 그 장려함의 널따란 잔해를 그러모았다. 딸들은 어머니 뒤로 둥글게 열을 지어 섰고, 그리하여 어머니를 뒤따르며 그 조각들을 받쳐 들고서, 아주 위엄이 없지는 않은 모양새로 접견실을 빠져나갔다. 프라우디 부인은 물러가 옷을 다시 차려입어야 했다.\n\n그 성좌가 휩쓸고 지나가자마자 에설버트는 무릎에서 일어나, 뚱뚱한 목사를 향해 짐짓 노한 체하며 말했다. “따지고 보면 제가 아니라 선생 탓이었습니다. 하지만 성직 승진을 기다리시는 처지일 터이니, 제가 대신 뒤집어써 드린 것이지요.”\n\n이에 뚱뚱한 목사를 겨냥한 웃음이 일었고 주교와 전속 사제도 거기에 끼어들었으니, 그리하여 자리는 다시 제 모양을 찾았다.\n\n“아, 주교님, 이런 일이 벌어져 정말 죄송합니다.” 시뇨라가 주교로 하여금 잡지 않을 수 없게끔 손을 내밀며 말했다. “제 오라비가 워낙 생각이 없어서요. 부디 앉으세요. 인사를 나누는 기쁨을 제게 주시고요. 저야 소파가 있어야 하는 딱한 몸이지만, 소파를 통째로 차지할 만큼 이기적이지는 않답니다.” 마들린은 언제든 몸을 놀려 신사 한 사람 앉을 자리를 낼 수 있었다. 다만 본인 말로는, 여자 친구들의 크리놀린은 부피가 너무 커서 그렇게 앉힐 수가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n“오로지 주교님을 뵙는 기쁨 하나 때문에 이렇게 실려 온 것이랍니다.” 그가 말을 이었다. “물론 주교님 같은 소임을 지고서야 저희를 찾아오실 짬이 나기를 바랄 수조차 없겠지요, 그러니까 방문이라는 형식으로는요. 게다가 영국식 만찬이란 하나같이 따분하고 딱딱하지 않습니까. 주교님, 아시는지요, 제가 영국에 오면서 유일한 위안으로 삼은 것이 주교님을 알게 되리라는 생각이었답니다.” 그러고는 계집 악마의 눈길로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n그러나 주교는 그 모습이 천사와 꼭 같다고 생각했고, 내주는 자리를 받아 그 곁에 앉았다. 그는 이렇게 수고를 해 주셔서 참으로 송구하다는 따위의 상투적인 말을 늘어놓으면서, 이 여자가 대체 누구인지 점점 더 궁금해했다.\n\n“제 슬픈 사연이야 물론 알고 계시겠지요?” 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n주교는 그에 대해 한마디도 알지 못했다. 다만 이 여자가 남들처럼 제 발로 방에 걸어 들어올 수 없다는 것만은 알고 있었고, 아니 안다고 여겼고, 그것을 최대한 써먹었다. 그는 이루 말할 수 없이 애통한 표정을 짓고는, 하느님께서 이분에게 어떤 시련을 내리셨는지 잘 알고 있노라고 말했다.\n\n시뇨라는 더없이 어여쁜 손수건으로 눈가를 살짝 눌렀다. 그렇습니다, 하고 그는 말했다. 자신은 모진 시련을 겪었노라고, 사람이 흔히 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어선 시련이었다고 여긴다고. 그러나 아이가 곁에 남아 있는 한 모든 것이 남아 있는 것이라고. “아, 주교님.” 그가 외쳤다. “그 아이를 꼭 보셔야 합니다. 놀라운 나무에 마지막으로 맺힌 봉오리랍니다. 그 거룩한 손을 그 죄 없는 머리에 얹으시어 여자의 덕을 위해 축성해 주시리라고, 어미가 그렇게 바라도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다. 그리 바라도 될까요?” 그렇게 말하며 그는 주교의 눈을 들여다보고 제 손으로 주교의 팔을 짚었다.\n\n주교도 사내에 지나지 않았으므로 그리 바라셔도 좋다고 말했다. 따지고 보면 그 딸에게 견진성사를 베풀어 달라는 청 말고 무엇이겠는가. 사실 굳이 청할 것도 없는 일이었으니, 그 아가씨가 통상의 절차로 나오기만 하면 당연히 해 줄 일이었다.\n\n“티베리우스의 피랍니다.” 시뇨라가 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. “티베리우스의 피가 그 아이의 핏줄에 흐르고 있어요. 그 아이는 네로 가문의 마지막 후손이랍니다!”\n\n주교는 마지막 서고트인에 대해서는 들어 본 적이 있었고, 머릿속에 마지막 모히칸에 관한 어렴풋한 관념도 떠다니고 있었으나, 네로 가문의 마지막 후손이 이렇게 축복을 받겠다고 눈앞에 나서는 데에는 적잖이 아연했다. 그래도 이 부인은 마음에 들었다. 생각하는 품이 온당했고, 오라비보다 훨씬 예절 바르게 말했다. 그런데 대체 이들은 누구란 말인가? 비단결 같은 수염을 단 저 푸른 옷의 미치광이가 비시니로니 공작이 아니라는 것만은 이제 아주 분명해졌다. 부인은 혼인한 몸이니, 남편 쪽 연고로 비시니로니 집안 사람인 것이 당연했다. 그렇게 주교는 조금씩 알아 나갔다.\n\n“언제 그 아이를 보시겠어요?” 시뇨라가 문득 생각난 듯 말했다.\n\n“누구를 본다는 말씀입니까?” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“제 아이 말입니다.” 어미가 말했다.\n\n“그 아가씨는 나이가 어떻게 됩니까?” 주교가 물었다.\n\n“이제 막 일곱 살이랍니다.” 시뇨라가 말했다.\n\n“아.” 주교가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “너무 어립니다. 아주 많이 어리지요.”\n\n“하지만 볕 좋은 이탈리아에서는 나이를 햇수로 세지 않는답니다.” 그러고는 시뇨라가 주교에게 더없이 달콤한 미소 가운데 하나를 지어 보였다.\n\n“하지만 정말이지 너무나 어립니다.” 주교가 물러서지 않고 말했다. “저희는 결코 그 전에는 견진을—”\n\n“그래도 그 아이에게 말씀은 해 주실 수 있잖아요. 로마 사람이라 해서 버림받은 자가 아니라는 것을, 네로의 핏줄이면서도 그리스도인일 수 있다는 것을, 검은 머리채와 짙은 뺨은 이교도 카이사르들의 피에서 왔을지언정 그 자신은 은총의 자녀일 수 있다는 것을, 주교님의 축성받은 입술로 들려주실 수 있잖아요. 그렇게 말해 주시겠지요, 나의 벗이여?”\n\n그 벗은 그러겠노라 말하고는, 아이가 교리문답을 욀 줄 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n“아니요.” 시뇨라가 말했다. “사제들이 짓밟고 로마의 우상숭배가 더럽혀 놓은 땅에서 그런 것을 배우게 할 수는 없었어요. 그 아이가 그 거룩한 말들을 처음으로 더듬더듬 배워야 할 곳은 바로 여기, 이곳 바체스터랍니다. 아, 주교님이 그 아이의 스승이 되어 주실 수만 있다면!”\n\n프라우디 박사가 이 부인을 마음에 들어 한 것은 분명하지만, 명색이 주교인 이상 어린 계집아이에게 교리문답의 첫걸음을 가르칠 리는 없었다. 그래서 그는 선생을 하나 보내겠노라고 말했다.\n\n“그래도 주교님이 몸소 그 아이를 보러 오시겠지요?”\n\n주교는 그러겠다고 말했으나, 어디로 찾아가면 되겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n“아버지 댁이지요.” 시뇨라가 그 물음에 조금 놀랐다는 기색으로 말했다.\n\n주교는 실로 그 아버지가 누구냐고 물을 용기가 나지 않아, 끝내 수수께끼를 풀지 못한 채 자리를 뜰 수밖에 없었다. 프라우디 부인은 두 번째로 좋은 옷을 입고 이미 방으로 돌아와 있었고, 남편은 아내가 그토록 하찮게 여기는 듯한 여인과 너무 가까이 붙어 이야기를 이어 가지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 이윽고 그는 막내딸과 마주쳤다.\n\n“네티야.” 그가 말했다. “저 시뇨라 비시니로니의 아버지가 누구인지 아느냐?”\n\n“비시니로니가 아니에요, 아버지.” 네티가 말했다. “베시 네로니예요. 그리고 저분은 스탠호프 박사님의 따님이고요. 그런데 저는 가서 그리젤 그랜틀리에게 인사치레를 해야겠어요. 오늘 저녁 그 딱한 아이에게 아무도 말 한마디 걸지 않았지 뭐예요.”\n\n스탠호프 박사라니! 베시 스탠호프 박사라니! 방탕한 이탈리아 건달과 혼인했다는 이야기를 어디선가 들은 기억이 이제야 나는, 바로 그 베시 스탠호프 박사의 딸이라니! 그리고 주교로서의 처신을 이러니저러니 캐묻던 저 무엄한 푸른 옷의 애송이가 늙은 스탠호프의 아들이요, 제 아이에게 교리문답을 가르쳐 달라고 조르던 저 부인이 늙은 스탠호프의 딸이라니! 자기 밑에 있는 참사회원의 딸이라니! 이런 것들이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나가자, 그는 아내가 그랬던 것에 못지않게 화가 났다. 그럼에도 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 낳은 그 어미가 사람 마음에 드는 여자라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n프라우디 박사는 종종걸음으로 옆방으로 나갔다. 그 방에는 그랜틀리 편 성직자들이 한 무리 모여 있었고, 그 가운데 부주교가 우뚝 서 있었으며, 늙은 수석 사제는 벽난로 옆 커다란 안락의자에 파묻히다시피 앉아 있었다. 주교는 어떻게든 너그러운 태도를 보여, 될 수 있으면 제 전속 사제가 빚어 놓은 앙금을 덜어 내고 싶었다. fortiter in re는 슬로프 씨에게 맡기고, 자신은 suaviter in modo를 부어 넣으면 될 일이었다.\n\n“일어나지 마십시오, 수석 사제님, 부디 일어나지 마십시오.” 늙은이가 몸을 일으키려 하자 그가 말했다. “이런 omnium gatherum 같은 자리에 와 주신 것만으로도 큰 호의로 여깁니다. 아직 자리를 제대로 잡지 못한 터라 프라우디 부인도 바라는 만큼 벗들을 맞이하지 못했지요. 그런데 부주교, 따지고 보면 우리가 옥스퍼드에 그리 모질게 굴지는 않았지요.”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 부주교가 말했다. “우리 이빨을 뽑고 혀를 잘라 냈을 뿐이지요. 숨 쉬고 삼키는 것까지는 그래도 남겨 주셨습니다.”\n\n“하, 하, 하!” 주교가 웃었다. “옥스퍼드 유력자의 혀를 잘라 내기가 그리 쉬운 일은 아니지요. 이빨로 말하자면 — 하, 하, 하! 아니, 우리가 일을 이대로 남겨 둔 마당에, 각 칼리지 학장들이 대학 주간 평의회가 위세를 떨치던 때만큼이나 제 뜻대로 하지 못한다면 그것이 오히려 이상한 일이지요. 수석 사제님은 어떻게 보십니까?”\n\n“늙은 사람은 주교님, 변화라는 것을 도무지 좋아하지 않는 법입니다.” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“정말 그렇다면 여러분이 일을 지독히도 서투르게 처리한 것이겠지요.” 부주교가 말했다. “사실대로 말씀드리자면 저는 여러분이 일을 그르쳤다고 봅니다. 어쨌든 이것만은 인정하셔야 합니다. 하겠다고 큰소리치던 것의 절반도 해내지 못하셨지요.”\n\n“자, 여러분이 말하는 교수 제도로 말하자면—” 학무 참사원이 느릿느릿 말을 꺼냈다. 그는 그 첫머리 너머로 나아갈 운명이 못 되었다.\n\n“교수 이야기가 나왔으니 말입니다만.” 학무 참사원의 팔꿈치 바로 뒤에서 부드럽고 또렷한 목소리가 말했다. “영국 분들이 독일에서 배울 것이 얼마나 많은지요. 다만 여러분은 하나같이 자존심이 너무 세시지요.”\n\n주교가 돌아보니 그 고약한 젊은 스탠호프가 자기를 쫓아온 것이었다. 수석 사제는 이 세상 것이 아닌 유령이라도 본 듯 그를 빤히 바라보았고, 두엇의 참사회원과 하급 참사원들도 마찬가지였다. 부주교는 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“독일 교수들이 학식 있는 사람들인 것은 사실이오만,” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “그러나—”\n\n“독일 교수라니!” 학무 참사원이 신음하듯 내뱉었다. 옥스퍼드의 공기를 일주일은 쐬어야만 나을 충격을 신경 계통에 받은 것 같았다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 에설버트는 독일 교수가 어째서 옥스퍼드 학자의 눈에 하찮게 보여야 하는지 도무지 알지 못한 채 말을 이었다. “물론 그 이름값을 가장 톡톡히 하는 곳은 옥스퍼드지요. 독일에서는 교수들이 실제로 가르칩니다. 옥스퍼드에서는 가르치겠노라 공언만 할 뿐이고, 때로는 그 공언조차 하지 않는다더군요. 독일에서 한 수 배우기를 마다하신다면, 그 대학이란 것들이 머지않아 여러분 귀에다 대고 무너져 내릴 겁니다.”\n\n여기에는 대답할 말이 없었다. 예순 살 먹은 점잖은 성직자들이 저런 옷차림에 저런 수염을 한 젊은이와 그런 문제를 논하려고 몸을 낮출 수는 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n“플럼스테드는 물이 좋습니까, 부주교?” 주교가 화제를 돌리려고 말했다.\n\n“그런대로 좋습니다.” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 댁 포도주만큼 좋지는 않지요, 주교님.” 재치 있는 하급 참사원 하나가 말했다.\n\n“그만큼 널리 쓰이지도 않고요.” 또 다른 이가 말했다. “그러니까 몸 안에 들이는 용도로는 말입니다.”\n\n“하, 하, 하!” 주교가 웃었다. “좋은 포도주 광은 집에 두기에 참으로 흐뭇한 것이지요.”\n\n“그 독일 교수들은 말이오, 선생, 맥주를 더 좋아한다지요, 아마.” 비꼬기 좋아하는 자그맣고 여윈 참사회원이 말했다.\n\n“그들은 둘 다 대수롭게 여기지 않습니다.” 에설버트가 말했다. “아마 그래서 그들이 더 나은 것이겠지요. 그런데 유대인 교수로 말하자면—”\n\n모욕이 옥스퍼드의 정신으로 견디기에는 너무 깊어지고 있었으므로, 부주교는 이쪽으로 학무 참사원은 저쪽으로 걸어가 버렸고 그 제자들도 뒤를 따랐다. 그리하여 벽난로 앞 깔개에는 주교와 이 젊은 개혁가만 남았다.\n\n“저도 한때는 유대인이었습니다.” 버티가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n주교는 또 한 차례 심문을 당하거나 무슨 수를 써서든 팔레스타인으로 끌려 들어갈 마음이 조금도 없었으므로, 다시금 아주 긴히 할 일이 있다는 것을 떠올리고는 젊은 스탠호프를 수석 사제에게 떠맡겨 두고 갔다. 수석 사제로서는 손해 볼 것이 없었으니, 에설버트가 성지에서 벌인 놀라운 행적을 사실대로 들려주었기 때문이다.\n\n“아, 하딩 씨.” 주교가 전임 워든을 따라잡으며 말했다. “요양원 일로 한마디 드리고 싶었습니다. 그 자리를 채우게 되었다는 것은 물론 알고 계시겠지요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 가슴이 조금 뛰었고, 그렇게 들었노라고 말했다.\n\n“물론,” 주교가 말을 이었다. “그 자리에 앉는 것을 제가 보고 싶은 사람은 오직 한 분뿐이지요. 하딩 씨 본인의 생각이 어떠하신지는 모르겠습니다만—”\n\n“아주 간단히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 주교님.” 상대가 말했다. “제게 그 자리를 주신다면 받겠고, 다른 사람이 차지한다면 그것 없이 지내겠다는 것입니다.”\n\n주교는 그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘다고 했다. 하딩 씨는 다른 누구도 그 자리를 차지하지 않으리라 확실히 믿어도 좋다고 했다. 다만 직무의 성격을 얼마간 바꾸어 놓을 사정이 몇 가지 있다고 했다. 하딩 씨도 아마 그 점은 알고 계실 터이니, 그 문제를 슬로프 씨와 의논하는 데에 이의는 없으시리라고 했다. 슬로프 씨가 그 문제에 상당한 공을 들여 온 터였다.\n\n하딩 씨는 까닭 모르게 짓눌리고 언짢은 기분이 들었다. 슬로프 씨가 자기에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있단 말인가? 변화가 있으리라는 것은 그도 알고 있었다. 그 성격은 누군가를 통해 워든에게 전해져야 할 터이고, 주교의 전속 사제만큼 그 역할에 자연스러운 사람이 또 어디 있겠는가? 그는 이렇게 따져 가며 마음을 다시 편하게 돌려놓으려 했으나 헛일이었다.\n\n그사이 슬로프 씨는 주교가 비운 시뇨라의 소파 자리를 차지하고 앉아, 사람들을 저녁 식사 자리로 이끌 때가 되기까지 그 부인 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 프라우디 부인이 이를 흡족한 눈으로 보았을 리 없다. 저 여자가 자기의 낭패를 두고 웃지 않았던가, 그리고 슬로프 씨가 그 웃음을 듣지 않았던가? 저 여자야말로 술수에 능한 이탈리아 여인이요, 아내라 하기도 아니라 하기도 어려운 처지에 꾸밈과 허세와 뻔뻔함으로 가득 찬 자가 아닌가? 벨벳과 진주로 흉하게 치장한 자가 아닌가, 그것도 등에서 뜯겨 나가지 않은 벨벳과 진주로? 무엇보다도 제가 남들보다 아름다운 척하지 않는가? 프라우디 부인이 질투를 했다고 말한다면 그 심정을 잘못 전하는 것이 되리라. 부인은 슬로프 씨가 자기에게 반하기를 바라는 마음이 조금도 없었다. 다만 슬로프 씨가 영적으로나 세속적으로 바쳐 올리는 봉사의 향연은 원했고, 그것이 시뇨라 네로니 따위를 향해 방향을 틀도록 내버려 둘 생각은 없었다. 게다가 부인이 보기에 슬로프 씨는 마땅히 그 시뇨라를 미워해야 할 사람이었는데, 그 태도로 미루어 보건대 미워하는 것과는 거리가 아주 멀었다.\n\n“이보게, 슬로프 씨.” 부인이 제 심정을 고스란히 얼굴에 담고 옆을 스쳐 지나가며 말했다. “쓸모 있게 좀 굴 수 없겠나? 부디 그랜틀리 부인을 모시고 저녁 식사 자리로 내려가게.”\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 그 말을 듣고 달아났다. 프라우디 부인의 입에서 말이 채 떨어지기도 전에, 제물이 될 뻔한 이는 남편의 부목사 하나의 팔에 손을 꿰고 몸을 구했다. 슬로프 씨와 나란히 계단을 내려가는 아내를 보았더라면 부주교가 무어라 했겠는가?\n\n슬로프 씨 역시 그 말을 들었으나 기대만큼 고분고분하지는 않았다. 실상 슬로프 씨가 프라우디 부인에게 복종하던 시절은 저물어 가고 있었다. 그는 아직 부인과 틀어지고 싶지 않았고, 피할 수만 있다면 아예 틀어지지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 그는 저 주교관의 주인이 될 작정이었고, 부인도 똑같은 결심을 하고 있었으니 두 사람이 한판 붙게 되는 일도 없으란 법이 없었다.\n\n시뇨라 곁을 떠나기 전에 그는 그 앞에 작은 탁자를 놓아 주고, 무엇을 가져다드리면 좋겠느냐고 청했다. 아무래도 좋다고, 아무것도 필요 없다고, 무엇이든 상관없다고 그가 말했다. 홀로 남겨져야 하는 지금이야말로 제 처지의 비참함이 사무친다고 했다. 그럼, 닭고기 조금과 햄 약간과 샴페인 한 잔을 달라고 했다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 제 후원자를 대신해 얼굴을 붉히면서, 샴페인은 없다고 설명해야 했다.\n\n셰리로도 얼마든지 좋다고 했다. 그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는 학식 있는 트리폴 양을 팔에 끼고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 바셋셔의 고사리가 컴벌랜드의 고사리에 견줄 만한지 아시느냐고 그가 물었다. 그의 가장 강렬한 세속적 열정은 고사리에 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 그 여자가 대답을 하기도 전에, 문과 찬장 사이에 끼워 둔 채 자리를 떠 버렸다. 그가 거기서 빠져나오기까지 50분이 걸렸고, 그러고도 아무것도 먹지 못했다.\n\n“설마 우리를 두고 가는 것은 아니겠지, 슬로프 씨.” 제 종이 손님들 머리 위로 음식을 잔뜩 높이 쳐들고 문 쪽으로 빠져나가는 것을 보고, 빈틈없는 안주인이 말했다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 시뇨라 네로니가 저녁 식사를 기다리고 있다고 설명했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨, 그건 그 오라비더러 가져다주라고 하게.” 프라우디 부인이 아주 큰 소리로 말했다. “자네가 그런 일에 쓰이다니 있을 수 없는 일이야. 슬로프 씨, 부디 내 말을 들어주게. 스탠호프 씨가 제 누이를 돌볼 것이 분명하니까.”\n\n에설버트는 방의 가장 먼 구석에서 프라우디 부인의 막내딸에게 쓸모 있게도 굴고 살갑게도 굴면서 더없이 흐뭇하게 시간을 보내고 있었다.\n\n“마들린이 저녁을 굶어 죽는다 해도 저는 여기서 빠져나갈 수가 없습니다, 부인.” 그가 말했다. “날 수 있다면 모를까, 몸이 아주 박혀 버렸거든요.”\n\n제 딸까지 적진으로 넘어간 것을 보자 부인의 노여움은 한층 커졌고, 그토록 말렸음에도, 분명한 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 슬로프 씨가 응접실로 가 버리는 것을 보자, 분노의 잔이 넘쳐 스스로를 억누를 수 없었다. “저런 무례는 내 평생 본 적이 없다.” 부인은 중얼거리듯 말했다. “나는 그것을 용납할 수도 없고 용납하지도 않겠다.” 그러고는 몇 분 동안 부산을 떨며 씩씩거리다가, 사람들 틈을 헤치고 슬로프 씨를 뒤쫓았다.\n\n위층 방에 이르러 보니 그 죄 지은 두 사람 말고는 아무도 없이 텅 비어 있었다. 시뇨라는 아주 편안하게 앉아 저녁을 들고 있었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 시중을 들고 있었다. 두 사람은 안식일 학교의 이점을 논하던 참이었고, 부인은 자기가 도저히 아이들에게 갈 수 없으니 아이들을 자기에게 데려오게 해 준다면 마음속 소원을 이루어 주는 셈이 되겠다고 넌지시 말한 터였다.\n\n“그러면 언제로 할까요, 슬로프 씨?” 그가 말했다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 프라우디 부인이 들어서는 바람에 굳이 약속을 하지 않아도 되게 되었다. 부인은 죄 지은 두 사람과 맞서듯 소파 바로 앞까지 쓸어 오듯 다가와 잠시 그들을 똑바로 노려보더니, 옆방으로 지나가면서 말했다. “슬로프 씨, 주교님께서 아래층에 자네가 있어 주기를 각별히 바라시네. 내려가 준다면 내게도 큰 도움이 되겠네.” 그러고는 성큼성큼 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 무어라 웅얼거려 대답하고는 아래층으로 내려갈 채비를 했다. 주교가 자기를 찾는다는 말이야, 그 여자 후원자를 익히 아는 터라 그 말의 값어치가 어느 정도인지 짐작하고도 남았다. 그러나 그는 한바탕 소동의 주인공이 되고 싶지도 않았고, 자리에 필요한 것 이상으로 여자에게 헌신한다는 소리를 듣고 싶지도 않았다.\n\n“저분은 늘 저러신가요?” 시뇨라가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소, 늘 저렇소, 부인.” 프라우디 부인이 되돌아오며 말했다. “늘 한결같지요. 어떤 종류의 무례한 처신에도 늘 똑같이 단호하고말고요.” 그러고는 다시 방을 가로질러 성큼성큼 돌아가, 슬로프 씨를 뒤따라 문밖으로 나갔다.\n\n시뇨라는 따라갈 수가 없었으니, 갈 수만 있었다면 분명히 따라갔을 것이다. 대신 그는 크게 웃었고, 그 소리를 복도로 울려 보내 프라우디 부인의 발소리를 좇아 계단 아래까지 흘려보냈다. 그리말디만큼 몸이 재발랐더라도 그보다 나은 복수는 하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n“슬로프 씨.” 프라우디 부인이 문간에서 그 죄인을 붙잡고 말했다. “내 곁을 떠나 저따위 분칠한 이세벨의 시중을 들다니 놀랍기 짝이 없네.”\n\n“하지만 그분은 다리를 못 써서 움직이지 못하지 않습니까, 프라우디 부인. 누군가는 시중을 들어야 했지요.”\n\n“다리를 못 쓴다고?” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내 사람이었다면 내가 정말로 다리를 못 쓰게 만들어 놓았을 걸세. 애당초 여기 올 일이 무엇이었단 말인가? 그런 뻔뻔함이라니, 그런 꾸밈이라니.”\n\n현관과 그에 잇닿은 방들에서는 외투를 걸치고 숄을 두르는 온갖 소동이 벌어졌고, 바체스터 사람들은 하나둘 자리를 떴다. 프라우디 부인은 작별 인사를 하는 사람마다 애써 웃음을 지어 보였으나 거의 성공하지 못했다. 그날 부인의 성미는 무섭도록 시달린 터였다. 손님들은 더디게 하나씩 물러갔다.\n\n“마차를 얼른 돌려보내세요.” 스탠호프 박사 내외가 자리를 뜰 때 에설버트가 말했다.\n\n젊은 스탠호프 남매는 맨 마지막까지 남았고, 주교 가족과 어울려 여간 거북한 자리를 이루지 않았다. 모두 식당으로 들어갔다가, 주교가 “그 부인”이 응접실에 혼자 있다고 말하는 바람에 다들 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 프라우디 부인은 슬로프 씨와 딸들을 붙들고 이야기를 놓지 않았으니, 그가 제멋대로 굴지 못하게 하고 딸들도 물들지 않게 하려는 작정이었다. 주교는 버티와 유대인 이야기가 죽도록 두려워, 샬럿 스탠호프를 붙잡고 이탈리아의 기후에 대해 이야기해 보려 했다. 버티와 시뇨라에게는 서로 말고는 기댈 데가 없었다.\n\n“결국 저녁은 얻어먹었어, 마들린?” 뻔뻔한 것인지 짓궂은 것인지 모를 이 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n“응, 먹었지.” 마들린이 말했다. “슬로프 씨가 아주 친절하게도 가져다주셨어. 다만 내가 바란 것보다 더 큰 수고를 하신 것 같아 마음에 걸리는구나.”\n\n프라우디 부인은 그를 바라보기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그 눈길의 뜻은 이렇게 옮길 수 있었으리라. “네가 다시 이 집 담장 안에 발을 들이는 일이 있거든, 그때는 마음껏 뻔뻔하고 마음껏 꾸미고 마음껏 짓궂게 굴어도 좋다고 내 허락해 주마.”\n\n마침내 이탈리아인 하인 셋과 함께 마차가 돌아왔고, 라 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니는 실려 들어왔던 그대로 실려 나갔다.\n\n주교관의 안주인은 바체스터에서 연 첫 성대한 연회의 결과에 조금도 만족하지 못한 채 제 방으로 물러갔다.\n\n# 제12장. 슬로프 대 하딩\n\n접견회가 있고 이삼 일이 지났을 무렵, 하딩 씨는 이튿날 아침 이른 시각에 주교관으로 슬로프 씨를 찾아와 달라고 청하는 쪽지를 받았다. 그 글에 무례한 구석이라고는 없었으나, 그럼에도 어조는 처음부터 끝까지 불쾌했다. 내용은 이러했다.\n\n내일 아침 오전 9시 30분에 주교관으로 저를 찾아와 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까. 주교님께서 요양원 건으로 저더러 하딩 씨와 이야기를 나누라 하십니다. 이렇게 이른 시각을 지정하는 것을 부디 용서해 주시기 바랍니다. 제 시간이 워낙 빠듯한 탓에 그리하는 것입니다. 다만 그 시각이 정말로 불편하시다면 10시로 바꾸겠습니다. 수고스럽더라도 답장을 한 줄 보내 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\n\n그럼 이만 줄이겠습니다, 친애하는 하딩 씨, 선생의 변함없는 벗, 오바. 슬로프\n\n주교관에서, 월요일 아침, 185X년 8월 20일\n\n하딩 씨는 그런 따위를 믿을 수도 없었고 믿을 마음도 없었으며, 게다가 슬로프 씨가 스스로를 그런 이름으로 일컫는 것은 제법 뻔뻔한 노릇이라고 생각했다. 변함없는 벗이라니! 대체 이 세상에서 한 사람에게 변함없는 벗이라는 것이 몇이나 돌아온단 말인가? 또 그런 벗은 어떤 과정을 거쳐 만들어지는가? 그리고 하딩 씨와 슬로프 씨 사이에 그런 과정이 지금까지 얼마나 있었단 말인가? 하딩 씨는 눈앞의 쪽지를 읽고 또 읽으면서 이런 물음들을 스스로에게 던지지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 답장을 썼다.\n\n말씀하신 대로 내일 오전 9시 30분에 주교관으로 찾아가겠소.\n\n그럼 이만 줄이겠소,\n\n바체스터 하이 스트리트에서, 월요일\n\n그리하여 이튿날 아침, 정확히 9시 30분에 그는 주교관 문을 두드리고 슬로프 씨를 찾았다.\n\n주교에게는 1층에 작은 방 하나가 배정되어 있었고, 슬로프 씨에게도 또 하나가 있었다. 하딩 씨는 그중 뒤엣것으로 안내되어 앉으라는 권유를 받았다. 슬로프 씨는 아직 와 있지 않았다. 전임 워든은 정원이 내다보이는 창가에 서서, 이 집이 마치 그가 이 집에서 나고 자란 자식이기라도 한 듯 통째로 그에게 열려 있던 때로부터 얼마나 짧은 시간이 흘렀을 뿐인가를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 늙은 하인들이 문을 열어 주며 짓던 미소를 떠올렸고, 그가 여느 때보다 몇 시간 더 발길을 끊기라도 하면 낯익은 집사가 “하딩 씨를 뵈니 앓던 눈이 다 낫습니다요” 하고 말하던 것을 떠올렸으며, 부산스러운 가정부가 그가 저녁을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 아침을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 점심을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 하며 우기던 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 벗이 방에 들어설 때마다 늙은 주교의 얼굴에 늘 번지던 그 흐뭇한 속마음의 빛을 떠올렸다.\n\n이 모든 것이 사라졌음을 헤아리자 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 이제 와서 요양원이 그에게 무슨 소용이겠는가? 그는 세상에 홀로였고 늙어 가고 있었다. 머지않아, 아주 머지않아 그도 정든 옛 벗이 떠났듯 이 모든 것을 두고 떠나야 할 터였다. 요양원도, 대성당에서 늘 앉던 자리도, 즐겨 찾던 곳들과 소소한 낙도 모두 더 젊고 어쩌면 더 지혜로운 이들에게 넘겨주고 떠나야 할 터였다. 그가 그토록 아끼던 그 성가! 어쩌면 정말로 그 시절은 지나가 버렸는지도 몰랐다. 그는 발밑에서 세상이 꺼져 내리는 듯한 느낌을 받았고, 지금이야말로 남들에게 확신을 가지고 설교해 온 그 소망들에 스스로 확신을 가지고 기대야 할 때라는 느낌을 받았다. “사람의 신앙이 저물어 가는 나이의 그 당연한 서글픔조차 버텨 주지 못한다면 대체 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가.” 그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 흐려진 눈으로 주교관 정원의 환한 화단을 내다보면서, 그는 자기가 바라던 그 버팀을 이미 지니고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n그렇다고는 해도 이렇게 기다림을 당하는 것은 마음에 들지 않았다. 슬로프 씨가 정말로 9시 30분에 그를 만날 뜻이 아니었다면, 무엇하러 아침밥이 목에 걸린 채 하숙에서 나서게 만든단 말인가? 사실을 말하자면 그것은 슬로프 씨 쪽의 계책이었다. 슬로프 씨는 하딩 씨가 요양원을 비굴하게 굴복하며 받아들이든지 아니면 아예 거절하든지 둘 중 하나여야 한다고 마음을 정해 놓고 있었고, 하딩 씨가 언짢은 기분으로 그 이야기에 들어서게만 만들면 아마 후자 쪽으로 더 빨리 기울리라고 셈해 두었다. 어쩌면 슬로프 씨의 셈이 아주 틀리지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 방으로 급히 들어온 것은 10시가 다 되어서였다. 그는 주교와 교구 업무에 관해 무어라 중얼거리더니 하딩 씨의 손을 인정사정없이 흔들어 잡고는 앉으시라고 청했다.\n\n이 사람이 몸에 두른 우월한 태도는 과연 하딩 씨의 비위를 거슬렀지만, 그렇다고 그것에 어떻게 성을 내야 할지는 알지 못했다. 그의 마음과 기질은 통째로 자기 쪽에서 맞대고 위세를 부리는 일과는 어긋나 있었고, 부주교라면 했을 법하게 대놓고 정면으로 꾸짖어 건방진 허세를 눌러 버리는 데 필요한 세속의 기개도 재바름도 그에게는 없었다. 하딩 씨로서는 참고 받아들이는 수밖에 없었고, 그래서 그렇게 했다.\n\n“요양원 건입니다만, 하딩 씨.” 슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다. 케임브리지의 어느 칼리지 학장이 처리해야 할 급비생 자리 하나를 두고 말하기라도 하는 듯한 말투였다.\n\n하딩 씨는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 포개고, 그 위에 다시 한 손을 다른 손 위에 포갠 채 슬로프 씨의 얼굴을 마주 보았다. 그러나 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“그 자리를 다시 채우게 되었지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. 하딩 씨는 그렇게 알고 있다고 대답했다.\n\n“물론 아시겠지만 수입은 아주 많이 줄어들 겁니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “주교님께서는 후하게 하시려는 뜻이어서, 450파운드보다 적어서는 안 된다고 보신다고 정부에 말씀하셨어요. 저는 대체로 주교님이 옳으셨다고 봅니다. 요구되는 직무가 아주 무거운 성질의 것은 아니겠으나, 그래도 예전보다는 무거워질 테니까요. 그리고 대성당이 있는 도시에 바로 딸린 성직자들은 우리가 쓸 수 있는 교회 재원이 허락하는 한 넉넉하게 지내도록 해 주는 편이 아마 좋을 겁니다. 그것이 주교님의 생각이고, 제 생각도 그러하다고 말씀드려야겠군요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 한 손으로 다른 손을 문지르며 앉아 있을 뿐 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n“수입 이야기는 그쯤 해 두지요, 하딩 씨. 집은 물론 전과 마찬가지로 워든에게 남습니다. 다만 제 생각에는, 안쪽은 7년마다 바깥쪽은 3년마다 칠을 하고, 사망이든 그 밖의 사유든 자리를 비우게 될 경우 훼손분에 대한 책임을 지도록 조건을 달아 두어야 할 듯합니다. 그러나 이 문제는 아직 주교님과 상의를 드려야 하는 사안이지요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 여전히 두 손을 문지르며 말없이 앉은 채, 슬로프 씨의 호감 가지 않는 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었다.\n\n“그다음으로 직무 말입니다만,” 그가 말을 이었다. “제가 들은 바가 옳다면, 지금까지는 직무라 할 만한 것이 거의 없었다고 해야 할 듯한데요.” 그러고는 반쯤 웃는 듯한 소리를 내어, 그 힐난을 농담의 옷을 입혀 넘기려는 시늉을 했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 옛집에서 보낸 행복하고 편안했던 세월을 떠올렸고, 자기가 거두어 온 기력 다한 늙은이들을 떠올렸으며, 자기의 선한 뜻을 떠올렸고, 분명 더할 나위 없이 가벼웠던 자기 일을 떠올렸다. 그는 그런 것들을 떠올리면서, 자기가 저 빈정거림을 들어 마땅한지 아닌지 한순간 미심쩍어했다. 그는 그 미심쩍음을 적에게 유리한 쪽으로 돌려 두고 그를 꾸짖지 않았다. 그저 아주 차분하게, 그리고 어쩌면 지나칠 만큼 겸손하게, 그 자리의 직무란 것이 어떠했든 간에 선대 주교께서 흡족해하실 만큼은 해냈다고 믿는다고만 말했다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 다시 웃었다. 이번 웃음은 전임 워든의 열의보다는 선대 주교의 기억을 겨냥한 것이었고, 하딩 씨도 그렇게 알아들었다. 그의 뺨에 핏기가 올랐고, 그는 몹시 화가 나기 시작했다.\n\n“하딩 씨도 아시겠지만, 바체스터도 사정이 꽤 많이 달라졌지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 알고 있다고 말했다. “바체스터만이 아닙니다, 하딩 씨. 세상 전체가 그렇지요. 새 사람이 새 방책을 펴고 지난 세기들의 쓸모없는 쓰레기를 치워 내는 곳이 바체스터만은 아니에요. 같은 일이 온 나라에서 벌어지고 있습니다. 이제는 삯을 받는 사람이라면 누구에게나 일이 요구되고, 그 일이 되어 가는 것과 삯이 치러지는 것을 감독해야 하는 자리에 있는 사람들은 그 원칙이 지켜지는지 살펴야 할 의무가 있지요. 새 사람이, 하딩 씨, 다른 직업에서와 마찬가지로 교회에서도 지금 필요해졌고 또 지금 나오고 있는 것입니다.”\n\n이 모든 말이 우리의 오랜 벗에게는 쓰디쓴 쑥이었다. 그는 제 능력이나 활동력을 높이 친 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그의 마음이 품은 정은 온통 옛 성직자들 편에 있었고, 그의 마음이 품을 수 있는 반감이란 반감은 모조리 저 새롭고 부산스럽고 몰인정하고 제 자랑에 여념 없는 사람들, 곧 슬로프 씨가 그토록 좋은 본보기인 그런 부류를 향해 있었다.\n\n“어쩌면,” 그가 말했다. “주교님께서는 요양원에도 새 사람을 두시는 편을 더 바라시겠구려.”\n\n“천만의 말씀입니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 이 임명을 받아 주시기를 몹시 바라십니다. 다만 요구되는 직무가 무엇일지를 미리 헤아려 두시기를 바라시는 것이지요. 우선 요양원에 안식일 학교가 딸리게 됩니다.”\n\n“뭐라고요! 그 늙은이들을 위해서 말이오?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“아닙니다, 하딩 씨. 그 노인들을 위한 것이 아니라, 바체스터의 가난한 이들 가운데 형편이 맞는 집 아이들을 위한 것이지요. 주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 이 학교에 나오시고, 교사들이 하딩 씨의 감독과 보살핌 아래 있기를 바라실 겁니다.”\n\n하딩 씨는 위에 얹었던 손을 다른 손에서 미끄러뜨려 내리고는, 포개어 올린 쪽 다리의 종아리를 문지르기 시작했다.\n\n“그 노인들로 말하자면,” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “그리고 앞으로 요양원의 한 부분을 이루게 될 노파들로 말하자면, 주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 안식일마다 그 건물 안에서 아침 예배와 저녁 예배를 올리시고 주중에도 한 차례 예배를 올리시기를, 또 주일에는 적어도 한 번은 그들에게 설교를 하시기를, 그리고 아침 기도와 저녁 기도에는 요양원 전체가 언제나 모이도록 하시기를 바라십니다. 주교님께서는 그렇게 하면 요양원 거주자들을 위해 대성당에 따로 자리를 잡아 둘 필요가 없어지리라고 보십니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨가 말을 멈추었으나, 하딩 씨는 여전히 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“실은 그 노파들 자리를 찾아 주기도 어려울 겁니다. 대체로, 하딩 씨, 이참에 아주 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠군요. 저로서는 그런 계층의 사람들에게 대성당 예배가 가장 유익한 것으로는 보이지 않습니다. 설령 어느 계층엔가는 그러하다 치더라도 말이지요.”\n\n“미안하지만 그 문제는 논하지 않았으면 하오.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“저도 그러고 싶지 않습니다. 적어도 지금 이 자리에서는요. 다만 요양원을 새로 꾸리는 일에 관한 주교님의 뜻만은 하딩 씨께서 충분히 헤아리셨기를 바랍니다. 그리고 제가 의심치 않는 대로, 주교님의 견해에 동의하신다는 다짐을 하딩 씨께 받게 된다면, 주교님의 임명장을 하딩 씨께 전해 드리는 사람이 되는 것이 저로서는 대단히 기쁘겠습니다.”\n\n“그러나 내가 주교님의 견해에 동의하지 않는다면 어찌 되오?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그러실 리야 없기를 바랍니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그러나 만일 그렇다면?” 상대가 다시 물었다.\n\n“불행히도 그런 경우가 된다면, 저로서는 그런 일이 있으리라 좀처럼 짐작되지 않습니다만, 아마도 하딩 씨 자신의 심정이 임명을 사양하시는 것이 도리라고 일러 줄 테지요.”\n\n“그러나 내가 임명을 받아들이고서도 주교님과 뜻을 달리한다면, 그때는 어찌 되오?”\n\n이 물음은 슬로프 씨를 제법 난처하게 만들었다. 그가 이 일을 주교와 의논했고 주일학교와 요양원 예배 몇 가지가 마땅하다는 뜻을 하딩 씨에게 내비칠 권한 비슷한 것을 받아 두었던 것은 사실이지만, 그 제안들이 임명에 딸린 절대적인 조건이라고 말할 권한까지는 없었다. 주교의 생각은 하딩 씨가 당연히 승낙하리라는 것, 그리고 그 학교도 이 도시의 다른 새 시설들과 마찬가지로 제 아내와 제 전속 사제의 관할 아래 들어오리라는 것이었다. 슬로프 씨의 생각이 더 정확했다. 그는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 거절하고 자기 편 사람이 그 자리를 차지하게 할 작정이었으나, 하딩 씨가 임명은 드러내 놓고 받아들이면서 조건은 그만큼 드러내 놓고 물리칠 가능성까지는 미처 생각해 보지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“짐작건대 있을 법한 일은 아니겠지요.” 그가 말했다. “주교님의 손에서 성직 자리를 받으면서 거기 딸린 직무는 인정하지 않겠다고 미리 작정해 두신다는 것은 말입니다.”\n\n“내가 워든이 되고서,” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “제 직무를 게을리한다면, 주교님께는 그 폐단을 바로잡을 방도가 있소.”\n\n“하딩 씨에게서 그런 논리를, 아니 그런 처신을 내비치는 말씀을 듣게 되리라고는 미처 생각지 못했습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 덕을 상한 사람의 표정을 잔뜩 지으며 말했다.\n\n“나 또한 그런 제안을 받게 되리라고는 생각지 못했소.”\n\n“어찌 되었든 주교님께 어떤 답을 올려야 할지는 알았으면 합니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다.\n\n“머지않아 기회를 보아 내가 직접 주교님을 뵙겠소.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그런 식으로 하시는 것은,” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님을 흡족하게 해 드리지 못할 겁니다. 실상 주교님께서 교구의 성직자를 하나하나 만나 그때그때 생기는 성직 수여 문제를 일일이 상대하신다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이지요. 제가 알기로 주교님께서는 이 건으로 이미 하딩 씨를 만나 보셨습니다. 그런데 무엇 때문에 다시 그 수고를 하셔야 하는지 저로서는 정말 모르겠군요.”\n\n“슬로프 씨, 내가 이 도시에서 성직자로 봉직한 지 얼마나 되었는지 아시오?” 슬로프 씨의 바람은 이제 거의 이루어진 셈이었다. 하딩 씨가 화가 났으니, 말을 잘못 흘릴 공산이 컸다.\n\n“그것이 이 문제와 무슨 상관이 있는지 저로서는 정말 모르겠습니다. 하딩 씨께서 여러 해 동안 대성당에서 일하셨다는 이유만으로, 부지런한 사람을 필요로 하는 자리를 한직으로 여기시도록 주교님이 내버려 두시는 것이 온당하다고 여기시지는 않겠지요.”\n\n“그러나 내가 뵙기를 청한다면 주교님께서 나를 만나 주실 마음이 드실 수도 있지 않겠소. 이 일은 벗들과 상의해 보겠소, 슬로프 씨. 그러나 나는 어떤 얕은수도 부릴 생각이 없소. 주교님께 이렇게 전해 주시오. 요양원에 관한 주교님의 견해에 나는 전혀 동의할 수 없으니, 그대가 말한 것과 같은 조건이 그 자리에 딸려 있다는 것을 알게 된다면 나는 그 자리를 사양하겠다고 말이오.” 그렇게 말하고 하딩 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 만족했다. 그는 하딩 씨의 마지막 말을 임명에 대한 완전한 거절로 받아들여도 좋다고 여겼다. 적어도 주교와 프라우디 부인에게는 그렇게 전했다.\n\n“그것 참 놀라운 일이오.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“놀라울 것 없어요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 무리가 당신 권위에 맞서겠다고 얼마나 단단히 작정하고 있는지 당신은 도무지 모르시는군요.”\n\n“허나 하딩 씨는 그 자리를 그토록 바라지 않았소.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님의 관할권을 털끝만큼도 인정하지 않고 그 자리를 쥘 수 있다면 말입니다.”\n\n“그건 될 법하지 않은 이야기요.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“저도 정말 그러하리라고 생각합니다.” 전속 사제가 말했다.\n\n“당연히 그래야지요.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“나로서는 정말 안된 일이오.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“안타까워할 까닭이 그리 많은지는 모르겠군요.” 부인이 말했다. “퀴버풀 씨가 훨씬 더 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이고, 그 자리가 훨씬 더 아쉬운 사람이며, 주교관 바로 이웃에서 훨씬 더 쓸모 있게 처신할 사람이에요.”\n\n“제가 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보는 편이 낫겠지요?” 전속 사제가 말했다.\n\n“그러는 편이 낫겠소.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n# 제13장. 쓰레기 수레\n\n주교관의 오솔길을 걸어 내려와 대성당 경내로 나서는 하딩 씨는 행복한 사람이 아니었다. 성직과 안락한 집이 두 번째로 그에게서 떠나갔으나, 그것은 견딜 수 있었다. 아들뻘밖에 되지 않는 사내에게 훈계를 듣고 모욕을 당했으나, 그것도 참을 수 있었다. 그는 자기에게 가해진 바로 그 상처들에서, 순교자란 제가 겪는 부당한 고난으로부터 언제나 얼마간의 위안을 받는 법이라고 우리가 믿는 그 위안을 끌어낼 수조차 있었다. 그리고 그 위안의 힘은 대개 순교자가 받는 잔혹의 정도에 비례하는 법이다. 그는 옛집의 아늑함이 그립다고 딸에게 털어놓은 바 있었고, 그러면서도 그것이 전부였다면 하이 스트리트의 하숙으로 돌아가는 일을 의기양양까지는 아니어도 적어도 만족스럽게는 받아들일 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 전속 사제의 일장 연설에 담긴 독이 이미 그의 피 속으로 파고들어 그 달콤한 자족의 생기를 말려 놓았다.\n\n“새 사람들이 새 방책을 시행하고 있으며, 지난 세기들이 남긴 쓸모없는 쓰레기를 수레에 실어 치워 내고 있습니다!” 이 얼마나 잔인한 말이었던가. 그리고 오늘날 그런 말이 슬로프다운 몰인정한 잔혹함을 다 담은 채 얼마나 자주 쓰이는가! 정치에서든 종교에서든 어떤 사람이 최근 스무 해 안에 세워진 어느 새로운 학파에 속하지 않는다는 것만 보여 줄 수 있으면, 그를 단죄하기에는 그것으로 충분하다. 그러면 그는 스스로를 쓰레기로 여기고 수레에 실려 나갈 각오를 하는 편이 좋다. 오늘날 사람은 새 시대를 온전히 알아보는 눈을 제 안에 갖추지 못하면 아무것도 아니다. 그 시대란 정직도 진실도 그다지 바람직해 보이지 않고 오직 성공만이 가치를 재는 시금석인 시대다. 우리는 확립된 모든 것을 비웃어야 한다. 그 농담이 아무리 형편없고 농담의 참된 원리에 아무리 어긋난다 해도 상관없다. 그래도 우리는 웃어야 한다. 그러지 않으려거든 수레를 조심할 일이다. 우리는 시대의 정신에 맞추어 말하고 생각하고 살아야 하며, 글 쓰는 고질이 우리에게 들렸다면 그 정신에 맞추어 글도 써야 한다. 그러지 않으면 우리는 아무것도 아니다. 새 사람과 새 방책, 넉넉한 외상과 모자란 거리낌, 큰 성공 아니면 굉장한 파멸 — 이것이 사는 법을 아는 오늘날 영국인들의 취향이다. 아아, 슬프도다! 이런 형편에서 하딩 씨는 자신이 사는 법을 모르는 영국인이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 슬로프 씨와 쓰레기 수레의 이 새로운 교리는, 적어도 바체스터에서는 새로운 것이었는데, 그의 평정을 슬프도록 어지럽혀 놓았다.\n\n“나라 전체에 걸쳐 똑같은 일이 벌어지고 있습니다! 이제는 삯을 받는 사람이라면 누구에게나 일이 요구됩니다!” 그렇다면 그는 평생 삯만 받고 일은 하지 않으며 살아왔던가? 그는 정녕, 이제 늘그막에 이르러 어느 커다란 쓰레기 구덩이에 감추어 두기에나 알맞은 쓰레기로 헤아려지는 것이 마땅할 만큼 그렇게 살아왔던가? 그가 스스로 속한다고 공언하는 부류의 사람들, 곧 그랜틀리 같은 이들과 그윈 같은 이들, 그리고 옥스퍼드의 옛 고교회파 신학자 무리는 하딩 씨를 괴롭히는 이런 자기 고발 따위에 시달리지 않는다. 그들은 대체로, 어떤 슬로프 씨나 어떤 프라우디 박사가 제 처신을 두고 그러하듯이, 제 처신의 지혜와 온당함에 흡족해한다. 그러나 하딩 씨에게는 불행하게도 그런 자신감이 거의 없었다. 세상의 슬로프들에게서 자기가 쓰레기로 불리는 것을 들었을 때, 그는 그 호칭이 참인지를 제 가슴속에 물어보는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다. 아아, 슬프도다! 증거는 대체로 그에게 불리해 보였다.\n\n그는 주교의 응접실에서, 다가오는 이 노년의 슬픔의 원천들 속에서, 곧 생각할 줄 아는 사람이라면 그 만년에 좀처럼 면할 수 없는 저 서글픈 회한의 발작 속에서, 종교가 자신을 위로해 주기에 넉넉하리라고 스스로 공언한 바 있었다. 그렇다, 종교는 세속의 어떤 좋은 것을 잃더라도 그를 위로해 줄 수 있었다. 그러나 그의 종교는, 헛되이 보낸 세월을 뉘우쳐 남은 세월을 앞날의 소망 가운데 보내게 해 줄 만큼 그렇게 활발한 종류의 것이었던가? 그리고 그런 뉘우침이란 그 자체로 고뇌와 눈물의 노역이 아닌가? 회개를 입에 올리기는 아주 쉬우나, 사람이 그것을 끝맺기까지는 벌겋게 달군 보습 위를 걸어야 하고, 성 바르톨로메오처럼 산 채로 살가죽이 벗겨져야 하고, 성 세바스티아노처럼 온몸에 화살이 꽂혀야 하고, 성 로렌초처럼 석쇠 위에 누워 구워져야 한다! 만일 그의 지난 삶이 그만한 회개를 요구한다면 어찌할 것인가? 그에게 그것을 끝까지 겪어 낼 기력이 있었던가?\n\n하딩 씨는 주교관을 나선 뒤 한 시간 남짓 대성당 경내의 그늘진 느릅나무 아래를 천천히 거닐다가 딸의 집으로 발길을 옮겼다. 그는 어쨌든 플럼스테드로 나가 그랜틀리 박사와 의논하기로, 그리고 우선은 엘리너에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기하기로 마음을 정한 터였다.\n\n그런데 이제 그는 또 한 차례의 괴로움을 겪을 운명이었다. 슬로프 씨가 미망인의 집에 한발 앞서 다녀갔던 것이다. 그는 바로 전날 오후에 그 집에 들렀다. 아버지가 히램 요양원의 그 예쁜 집으로 돌아가시게 되었다는 소식을 볼드 부인에게 전하는 기쁨을 스스로 마다할 수는 없었노라고 그는 말했다. 임명이 이제 곧바로 이루어지리라는 것을 하딩 씨에게 알리라는 주교의 지시를 받았다고도 했다. 물론 주교로서는 하딩 씨가 그토록 오래 빛내던 그 성직을 그에게 되돌려 드리는 통로가 될 수 있어 더없이 기쁘다는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는 차차, 머지않아 요양원에 딸린 것으로 보게 되기를 바라는 그 예쁜 학교 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그림처럼 어여쁘고 유익하며 자비로운 그 부속 시설에 대한 그의 묘사에 볼드 부인은 완전히 홀려서, 아버지도 틀림없이 찬성하시리라고, 그리고 자기도 기꺼이 한 반을 맡겠다고까지 말했다.\n\n이 계획된 시설을 두고 슬로프 씨가 딸에게 말할 때와 아버지에게 말할 때 그 어조가 온통 딴판이고 태도 또한 온통 딴판이었음을 듣고 본 사람이라면 누구든, 슬로프 씨가 천재적인 인물임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 볼드 부인에게 요양원의 설교와 예배에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 노인들을 대성당에서 내보내는 일에 대해서도 한마디 없었으며, 퇴락과 도색에 대해서도, 쓰레기를 수레에 실어 치우는 일에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않았다. 엘리너는 속으로, 슬로프 씨가 사람됨으로는 마음에 들지 않는 것이 분명하지만 그가 매우 활동적이고 열성적인 성직자이며 바체스터에서 틀림없이 쓸모가 있으리라고 생각했다. 이 모든 것이 하딩 씨에게 한층 더한 괴로움이 닥칠 길을 닦아 놓았다.\n\n엘리너는 아버지가 계단을 올라오는 소리를 듣고 더없이 밝은 얼굴을 지었다. 축하만 드리면 되는 줄 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 아버지의 얼굴을 보는 순간 축하할 일이 별로 없다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그 지치고 시름겨운 표정을 그녀는 전에도 한두 번 본 적이 있었고 잘 기억하고 있었다. 결국 아버지가 요양원을 사직하게 만든 『주피터』의 그 공격 기사를 아버지가 처음 읽던 때에 그 얼굴을 보았고, 부주교가 아버지 자신의 온당함과 명예에 대한 감각을 거슬러 가면서까지 그 자리에 남으시라고 설득하던 때에도 그 얼굴을 보았다. 그녀는 한눈에 아버지의 마음이 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있음을 알았다.\n\n“어머, 아버지, 무슨 일이세요?” 그녀는 아들을 내려놓아 바닥을 기게 하며 말했다.\n\n“네게 말해 두려고 왔단다, 얘야.” 그가 말했다. “나는 플럼스테드로 나가 볼 참이다. 너는 같이 가지 않겠지?”\n\n“플럼스테드요, 아버지? 거기서 묵으시려고요?”\n\n“오늘 밤은 아무래도 그리되겠지. 이 성가신 요양원 일을 부주교와 의논해야 하니 말이다. 아아! 그 일을 다시 떠올리지나 말았더라면 좋았을 것을.”\n\n“아버지, 대체 무슨 일이 있으셨어요?”\n\n“슬로프 씨를 만나고 오는 길이란다, 얘야. 그런데 그 사람은 세상에서 가장 유쾌한 말벗은 못 되더구나. 적어도 나에게는 말이다.” 엘리너는 얼굴을 반쯤 붉혔으나, 아버지가 자기와 슬로프 씨의 교분을 조금이라도 두고 하는 말이라고 여겼다면 그것은 잘못이었다.\n\n“그런데요, 아버지.”\n\n“그 사람은 요양원을 주일학교와 설교소로 바꾸고 싶어 하는데, 아마 제 뜻대로 하겠지. 나는 그런 시설에 어울리는 사람이라는 생각이 도무지 들지 않는구나. 그러니 아무래도 그 임명을 사양해야 할 것 같다.”\n\n“학교가 무슨 해가 되나요, 아버지?”\n\n“제대로 된 교사가 없다는 것이 문제란다, 얘야.”\n\n“하지만 그거야 당연히 마련될 텐데요.”\n\n“슬로프 씨는 나를 제 교사로 삼아서 그것을 마련할 생각이더구나. 그러나 나는 그런 일에 도무지 맞지 않으니 사양할 작정이다.”\n\n“어머, 아버지! 슬로프 씨는 그런 뜻이 아니에요. 그분이 어제 여기 오셨는데, 그분 생각은—”\n\n“그 사람이 어제 여기 왔었다고?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“네, 아버지.”\n\n“요양원 이야기를 하면서 말이냐?”\n\n“아버지가 그곳으로 돌아가시는 것을 보게 되어 자기도 주교님도 얼마나 기쁘겠느냐고 하셨어요. 그러고는 주일학교 이야기를 하셨고요. 사실대로 말씀드리면 저는 그 말에 찬성했어요. 아버지도 그러시리라고 생각했고요. 슬로프 씨가 말한 학교는 요양원 안에 두는 것이 아니라 그저 거기에 딸린 학교인데, 아버지가 그 후원자이자 감독이 되시는 거예요. 저는 아버지가 그런 학교라면 마음에 들어 하시리라고 생각했어요. 그래서 제가 그 일을 돌보고 한 반을 맡겠다고 약속했고요. 그게 다 참 그렇게—. 아, 그런데 아버지! 제가 잘못한 것이라면 저는 정말이지 견딜 수 없이 괴로울 거예요.”\n\n“조금도 잘못한 것 없다, 얘야.” 그는 딸이 안겨 오는 것을 부드럽게, 아주 부드럽게 물리치며 말했다. “네가 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶어 하는 데에 잘못이 있을 리 있겠느냐. 아니, 무슨 수를 써서라도 마땅히 그래야 하고말고. 이제는 누구든 뒷전으로 밀려나기 싫다면 제힘을 다 써야 하는 법이니까.” 가엾은 하딩 씨는 이렇게 제 괴로움 속에서 그 새로운 교리를 제 자식에게 설교해 보려 들었다. “그가 됐든 그녀가 됐든 매한가지지.” 그가 말을 이었다. “얘야, 네가 그런 일을 하는 것은 아주 옳은 일이다. 다만—”\n\n“그런데요, 아버지.”\n\n“내가 너라면 슬로프 씨를 길잡이로 고르지는 않을 것 같구나.”\n\n“저는 한 번도 그런 적이 없고 앞으로도 그러지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그 사람을 나쁘게 말하는 것은 매우 못된 짓이겠지. 사실 나는 그에 대해 나쁜 것을 아는 바가 없으니 말이다. 다만 그가 정직한 사람인지는 잘 모르겠구나. 그의 태도가 신사답지 못하다는 것만은 아주 분명하다만.”\n\n“그분을 길잡이로 삼겠다는 생각은 해 본 적도 없어요, 아버지.”\n\n“나로 말하자면, 얘야,” 그가 말을 이었다. “우리가 아는 옛 속담이 있잖느냐. 늙은 개에게 재주를 가르치기는 어려운 법이라고. 나는 주일학교를 사양해야겠고, 따라서 아마 요양원도 사양하게 되겠지. 하지만 먼저 네 형부를 만나 보마.” 그러고서 그는 모자를 집어 들고 아기에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 물러갔으니, 엘리너는 그와 마찬가지로 가라앉은 마음으로 남겨졌다.\n\n이 모든 일이 그의 괴로움을 크게 더해 놓았다. 그에게는 마음을 나눌 사람이 워낙 적었으므로, 그중에서도 가장 값진 공감을 주던 사람과 사이가 끊기는 것을 감당할 형편이 못 되었다. 그런데도 그리될 성싶었다. 그는 딸이 슬로프 씨를 미워하기를 바란다고 스스로 인정하지는 않았으나, 딸이 그런 감정을 드러냈다면 그토록 매정한 마음가짐을 나무라는 그의 꾸지람에는 쓴맛이 거의 없었을 것이다. 그러나 사실인즉 딸은 슬로프 씨와 사이가 좋았고, 그의 견해에 동조했으며, 그의 계획을 곧바로 따랐고, 그의 가르침을 즐겁게 들었다. 하딩 씨가 딸이 그 사람을 미워하기를 바란 것은 아니었으나, 딸이 그를 사랑하느니 차라리 미워하는 편이 나았을 것이다.\n\n그는 여관으로 걸어가 삯마차를 부탁하고, 집으로 돌아가 여행 가방을 꾸린 뒤 플럼스테드로 떠났다. 어쨌거나 부주교가 슬로프 씨와 손을 잡을 위험은 없었다. 그러나 그 대신 부주교는 끝장을 보는 싸움과 공개 호소와 요란한 비난, 그리고 드러내 놓고 벌이는 전투의 온갖 장비를 권할 것이었다. 그런데 그 대안이라는 것도 하딩 씨의 취향에는 앞의 것보다 나을 것이 거의 없었다.\n\n하딩 씨가 목사관에 이르렀을 때 부주교는 밖에 나가 저녁 식사 때까지는 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알게 되었고, 그래서 그는 큰딸에게 하소연을 시작했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 슬로프 씨에게 남편 못지않게 강한 반감을 품고 있었다. 그녀 역시 프라우디 일파와 맞서 싸우고, 대성당 경내의 옛 교회 세력을 떠받치며, 응당 제 편에 속하는 떡고물은 제 편에 붙들어 두어야 한다는 필요를 남편만큼이나 절실히 느끼고 있었고, 자비를 베풀지도 구하지도 않은 채 싸움을 끌고 갈 채비 또한 그 주인만큼이나 잘 되어 있었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 다투기 좋아하는 여자라거나 성직자 이웃들과 화목하게 지내기를 꺼리는 여자라는 말은 아니다. 다만 그녀는 부주교와 마찬가지로, 슬로프 씨가 바체스터에 있다는 것 자체가 선대 주교와 인연 있는 모든 이에 대한 모욕이며 그가 교구에서 참칭하는 지배가 제 남편에게는 영적인 상해라고 느꼈던 것이다. 그때까지 사람들은 그랜틀리 부인이 얼마나 매서울 수 있는지 거의 짐작하지 못했다. 그녀는 주위의 교구 목사 부인들과 더없이 좋은 사이로 지냈다. 대성당 경내와 연이 닿는 부인이라면 누구에게나 인기가 있었다. 주(州)의 성직자 부인들 가운데 단연 가장 부유했으면서도, 제 마차와 말이 누구의 눈총도 받지 않도록 처신할 줄 알았다. 다른 성직자 부인들의 시샘을 살 만큼 주의 세도가들 틈에 끼어든 적도 없었다. 백작이니 백작부인이니 하는 이야기를 큰 소리로 늘어놓은 적도, 가정교사에게 한 해 60파운드를 주고 요리사에게 70파운드를 준다고 자랑한 적도 없었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 지혜롭고 신중하며 화평을 이루는 여인의 삶을 살아왔던 터라, 바체스터 사람들은 그녀가 그랜틀리파 여성 부대의 장군으로서 드러내 보이는 군사적 기백의 크기에 놀라고 말았다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 동생 엘리너가 요양원 학교 일에서 슬로프 씨를 돕겠다고 약속했다는 것을 이내 알게 되었고, 그녀의 주의가 맨 먼저 붙들린 것도 바로 그 대목이었다.\n\n“엘리너가 어떻게 그 사람을 견딜 수 있을까요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아주 교활한 사람이지.” 아버지가 말했다. “그리고 그 교활함이 먹혀들어, 엘리너는 그를 온유하고 자비로우며 선량한 성직자라고 여기게 된 게야. 내가 그를 잘못 보는 것이라면 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를. 하지만 내 생각에 그의 참된 성품은 그런 것이 아니다.”\n\n“참된 성품이라니요!” 아버지의 온건함을 거의 경멸하다시피 하며 그녀가 말했다. “저는 다만 그 사람이 엘리너로 하여금 제 자신과 제 처지를 잊게 만들 만큼 교활하지는 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.”\n\n“그 사람과 혼인이라도 한다는 말이냐?” 그토록 끔찍한 이야기가 느닷없이 튀어나오는 바람에 그는 놀라 평소의 몸가짐을 잃고 말했다.\n\n“그게 그토록 있을 법하지 않은 일인가요? 조금이라도 가망이 있다고 여긴다면 물론 그것이야말로 그 사람의 목적일 테지요. 엘리너에게는 온전히 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,000파운드가 있어요. 그런 재산을 제 손으로 넘겨 놓는 것보다 더 나은 행운이 슬로프 씨에게 굴러들 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“그렇지만 그 애가 그를 좋아한다고 여길 수야 없지 않으냐, 수전?”\n\n“왜 아니겠어요?” 수전이 말했다. “왜 그 애가 그를 좋아하지 않겠어요? 그 사람은 지금 그 애처럼 돌보아 줄 이 하나 없이 남겨진 여자에게 파고들기에 딱 알맞은 부류인걸요.”\n\n“돌보아 줄 이라니!” 불행한 아버지가 말했다. “우리가 그 애를 돌보지 않느냐?”\n\n“어머, 아버지, 어쩌면 그렇게 순진하세요! 엘리너가 다시 혼인하리라는 것이야 당연히 예상할 수 있는 일이었지요. 그 애가 마땅한 때를 기다렸다가 적어도 신사다운 사람과 혼인하기만 한다면, 저야말로 그것을 말릴 마음이 조금도 없고요.”\n\n“하지만 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 생각을 한 적이 있다고 정말로 말하려는 건 아니겠지? 아니, 볼드 군이 죽은 지 이제 겨우 한 해가 아니냐.”\n\n“열여덟 달이에요.” 딸이 말했다. “그렇지만 엘리너가 그런 생각을 해 보았으리라고는 저도 여기지 않아요. 다만 그 사람은 생각했을 법하고, 그 애도 그렇게 하도록 만들려 들 법하지요. 그리고 우리가 정신을 바짝 차리지 않으면 그가 뜻을 이루고 말 거예요.”\n\n이것은 가엾은 하딩 씨에게는 이 일의 아주 새로운 국면이었다. 세상에서 자기가 참으로 확실하게 싫어하는 단 하나의 사내를 사위로, 그것도 가장 아끼는 자식의 남편으로 떠맡게 된다는 것은, 어떻게 참을성 있게 견뎌 낼지 도무지 알 수 없는 불행이었다. 그러나 그토록 끔찍한 억측에 무슨 근거가 있기는 한 것일까? 세상일에 관해서라면 그는 큰딸의 의견을 대체로 온당하고 믿을 만한 것으로 여기는 편이었다. 사람의 성품이며 동기며 남자와 여자가 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지를 헤아리는 데서 그녀는 웬만해서는 크게 빗나가지 않았다. 그녀는 엘리너와 존 볼드의 혼인을 일찌감치 내다보았고, 새 주교와 그 전속 사제의 성품을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그렇다면 지금의 이 억측도 언젠가 사실로 드러나는 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가?\n\n“그래도 그 애가 그를 좋아한다고는 생각지 않겠지?” 하딩 씨가 다시 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 아버지, 그 애가 마땅히 싫어해야 할 만큼 그를 싫어한다고는 말씀드릴 수 없네요. 애초에 그 집 문턱도 넘어서는 안 되었을 사람이 어째서 허물없는 벗으로 드나드는 걸까요? 어째서 그 애가 그 사람에게 아버지의 안녕과 처지를 두고 이야기를 했을까요? 분명히 그랬는데 말이에요. 요전 날 밤 주교님 댁 모임에서는 그 애가 그 사람과 내리 반 시간이나 이야기하는 것을 제가 보았어요.”\n\n“내가 보기에 슬로프 씨는 거기서 스탠호프 댁 그 딸 말고는 아무하고도 이야기하지 않는 것 같더구나.” 하딩 씨가 제 자식을 두둔하려는 마음에서 말했다.\n\n“어머, 슬로프 씨는 아버지가 생각하시는 것보다 영리한 사람이에요, 아버지. 그리고 한 번에 한 가지 일만 벌여 놓는 사람도 아니고요.”\n\n엘리너를 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀가 슬로프 씨에게 조금이라도 마음이 기울었으리라는 의심은 그녀에게 부당한 것이었다. 그녀는 주교와 혼인할 생각이 없는 것과 마찬가지로 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 생각도 없었고, 슬로프 씨가 구혼자로 나서리라는 생각은 그녀에게 떠오른 적조차 없었다. 실로, 다시 한번 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀는 남편이 죽은 뒤로 구혼자라는 것을 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그랜틀리 편 사람들이 그토록 강하게 품고 있던 그 사람에 대한 혐오를 그녀가 다 이겨 냈다는 것만은 사실이었다. 그녀는 그의 설교를 용서했다. 그의 저교회파 성향과 안식일 학교와 청교도적 계율을 용서했다. 바리새인 같은 오만도, 심지어 번들거리는 얼굴과 기름기 도는 상스러운 태도까지 용서했다. 그만한 허물들을 눈감아 주기로 한 마당에, 그녀가 머지않아 슬로프 씨를 구혼자로 여기도록 길들여지지 못할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n그리고 그 사람으로 말하자면, 그 역시 그때까지는 그에게 씌워진 혐의에 대해 똑같이 결백했다고 밝혀 두어야 한다. 주위의 모든 것에 대개 그토록 눈이 밝은 사내가 이 젊은 미망인이 아름다울 뿐 아니라 부유하기도 하다는 것을 어찌하여 알아차리지 못했는지는 아마 이제 와서는 설명할 길이 없을 것이다. 그러나 사실이 그러했다. 슬로프 씨는 도시에서 제 세력을 굳히려는 뜻에서, 다른 부인들에게 그랬듯이 그저 그렇게 볼드 부인의 환심을 사 두었을 뿐이었다. 그는 나중에 제 잘못을 바로잡았지만, 그것은 그와 하딩 씨의 면담이 있은 뒤의 일이었다.\n\n# 제14장. 새로운 투사\n\n부주교는 저녁 식사 시각이 다 되어서야 목사관으로 돌아왔고, 따라서 그 중대한 의식을 치르기 전에 일을 의논할 겨를은 없었다. 그는 유난히 기분이 좋아 보였고, 마음에 두고 있던 일이 뜻대로 풀려 갈 때면 늘 그러하듯 유쾌하면서도 자못 진지한 태도로 장인을 맞이했다.\n\n“다 정리됐소, 여보.” 부주교는 옷방에서 손을 씻으며 아내에게 말했고, 아내는 늘 하던 대로 침실에 앉아 귀를 기울이고 있었다. “애러빈이 그 성직록을 받기로 했소. 다음 주면 이리로 올 거요.” 그러고서 부주교는 어찌나 기세 좋게 손을 문지르고 얼굴을 비벼 대는지, 애러빈이 온다는 것이 크게 얻어 낸 한 수임을 알 만했다.\n\n“여기 플럼스테드로 오시는 거예요?” 아내가 물었다.\n\n“한 달 우리 집에 묵기로 약속했소.” 부주교가 말했다. “제 교구가 어떤 곳인지 둘러볼 수 있게 말이오. 당신도 애러빈을 아주 마음에 들어 할 거요. 모든 면에서 신사인 데다 해학이 넘치는 사람이오.”\n\n“그분은 무척 별난 사람이라면서요?” 부인이 물었다.\n\n“글쎄—공상하는 대목이 좀 유별나기는 하오만, 당신이 싫어할 구석이라고는 하나도 없는 사람이오. 옥스퍼드를 통틀어 그만큼 신실한 교회인은 없소. 애러빈이 없었더라면 우리가 대체 어찌했을지 나는 정말이지 모르겠소. 그가 이렇게 가까이 온다는 것은 내게 큰일이고, 무엇이든 슬로프를 눌러 앉힐 수 있는 것이 있다면 그건 애러빈이 해낼 거요.”\n\n프랜시스 애러빈 목사는 라자러스 칼리지의 펠로이자 저 위대한 그윈 박사가 아끼는 문하생으로, 모든 점에서 고교회파였다—실로 어찌나 높았던지 한때는 로마라는 시궁창으로 넘어질 뻔한 적까지 있었다—시인이자 논쟁적인 저술가였고, 옥스퍼드 담화실의 큰 귀염둥이였으며, 언변이 뛰어난 성직자였고, 익살스럽고 별나고 해학이 넘치며 정력적이고 양심적인 사람이었고, 부주교가 자랑삼아 말한 대로 철저한 신사였다. 앞으로 그를 더 가까이 들여다볼 자리가 있을 터이니, 여기서는 다만 그가 방금 그랜틀리 박사에게서 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제직을 받았다는 것, 그리고 그 성직록의 수여권이 부주교인 그랜틀리 박사에게 있었다는 것만 덧붙여 두면 되겠다. 세인트 이월드는 바체스터 시 바로 바깥에 놓인 교구다. 실상 신시가지의 변두리는 일부가 그 경계 안에 들어와 있고, 아담한 교회와 목사관은 성문에서 1마일 남짓밖에 떨어져 있지 않다.\n\n세인트 이월드는 넉넉한 성직 자리가 아니다—많아야 한 해 300파운드나 400파운드 값어치이며, 대체로 대성당 성가대에 딸린 성직자가 맡아 왔다. 그러나 부주교는 이번에 그 성직록이 비게 되자, 세인트 이월드에 들어앉힐 만한 든든한 성채를 구할 수만 있다면 그런 성채로 제 진영의 힘을 보태는 것이 무엇보다 마땅한 도리라고 느꼈다. 그는 이 문제를 바체스터의 동료 성직자들과 의논했는데, 성직 수여권을 제 것이나 제 집안의 이익에 쓰려는 옹졸한 마음에서가 아니라, 그 온당한 시행에 교회의 안녕이 크게 달린 소임을 맡은 사람으로서 그리한 것이었다. 그는 마치 선택권이 회합에 모인 그들 모두에게 있기라도 한 듯 애러빈 씨의 이름을 내놓았고, 그들은 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드를 받아 준다면 그보다 나은 선택은 있을 수 없다고 이구동성으로 인정했다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드를 받아 준다면! 어려움은 바로 거기에 있었다. 애러빈 씨는 세상 앞에, 그러니까 영국 국교회의 세상 앞에 제법 두드러지게 서 있는 사람이었다. 부자가 아닌 것은 사실이니, 펠로직 말고는 아무 성직 자리도 지닌 것이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 재물을 지나치게 탐하는 사람이 아니었고, 물론 결혼도 하지 않았으며, 제 시간의 대부분을 인쇄물로든 강단에서든 제가 속한 교회의 특권과 관행을 논하는 데 쓰는 사람이었다. 부주교가 교회의 세속적 재산을 위해 싸워 왔듯 애러빈 씨는 교회의 영적 권능을 위해 싸워 왔으며, 둘 다 양심을 좇아 그리했다. 다시 말해 저마다 제 이익을 위해서라기보다 남의 이익을 위해 싸운 것이었다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 그런 위치에 있는 터라 그가 세인트 이월드의 교구 목사가 되기를 승낙할지는 크게 의심스러웠고, 그래서 그랜틀리 박사는 수고를 무릅쓰고 몸소 옥스퍼드까지 다녀왔던 것이다. 그윈 박사와 그랜틀리 박사가 힘을 합친 끝에 이 저명한 신학자에게 바체스터로 가는 것이 의무라고 설득해 냈다. 이 일에는 바퀴 안에 또 바퀴가 물려 있었다. 얼마 전부터 애러빈 씨는 다름 아닌 슬로프 씨와 사도 계승을 두고 엄청난 논쟁을 벌여 오던 참이었다. 두 신사는 서로 얼굴 한 번 본 적이 없었으나 지면에서는 지독히도 신랄했다. 슬로프 씨는 애러빈 씨를 올빼미라 부르는 것으로 제 편의 힘을 키우려 했고, 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨가 무신론자라고 넌지시 이르는 것으로 앙갚음을 했다. 이 싸움은 위세 등등한 신문 『주피터』의 지면에서 시작되었는데, 그 신문의 주간은 이 사안에 관한 슬로프 씨의 견해에 무척 우호적이었다. 그러나 이 문제는 『주피터』 독자들에게 너무나 지루해졌고, 그리하여 슬로프 씨가 쓴 가장 통쾌한 반박문 가운데 하나의 끝에 짧은 알림이 덧붙었으니, 두 목사님의 편지는 이제 광고로가 아니면 더는 실을 수 없다는 내용이었다.\n\n그러나 『주피터』에 광고를 싣는 것보다 돈이 덜 드는 다른 발표 방법들이 발견되었고, 전쟁은 신명나게 이어졌다. 슬로프 씨는 성직자를 성별하는 일의 요체가 속사람이 성직의 직무에 스스로를 바치는 데 있다고 단언했다. 애러빈 씨는, 어떤 주교의 안수를 받아 그리되지 않은 이상 그 사람은 애초에 성별된 것이 아니며 성직자의 속성이라고는 하나도 지니지 못한 것이라고, 게다가 그 주교 또한 다른 이의 안수를 받아 주교가 된 자여야 하고 그렇게 사도 가운데 한 사람에게까지 곧게 이어져야 한다고 맞섰다. 둘은 번갈아 상대를 딜레마의 뿔에 몇 번이고 꿰어 매달았으나 매달린 쪽이 조금이라도 축난 기색은 어느 쪽에도 없었다. 그리하여 전쟁은 신명나게 이어졌다.\n\n적이 가까이 있다는 사실이 애러빈 씨로 하여금 세인트 이월드의 성직록을 받아들이게 하는 데 얼마쯤 미끼 노릇을 했는지 어떤지, 우리는 아는 체하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 그가 그것을 받아들이리라는 것, 그리고 적을 바체스터에서 몰아내는 데, 적어도 그가 그곳에 머무는 동안 입을 다물게 하는 데 힘을 보태리라는 것은 라자러스에 있는 그윈 박사의 서재에서 이미 정해진 일이었다. 애러빈 씨는 옥스퍼드의 제 방을 그대로 두고 세인트 이월드에는 부목사를 두어 거들게 할 작정이었으나, 되도록 많은 시간을 바체스터 인근에서 보내겠다고 약속했다. 그만한 인물에게서 그만한 약속을 받았으니 그랜틀리 박사는 아주 흡족했다. 이 계획에서 얻어지는 만족 가운데 적지 않은 몫은, 프라우디 주교가 제가 아끼는 전속 사제의 원수를 제 코앞에 있는 성직록에 손수 취임시켜야만 하리라는 데 있었다.\n\n저녁 식사 내내 부주교의 좋은 기분은 얼굴에 환하게 드러났다. 그는 좋은 음식을 푸짐하게 먹었고, 아내와 딸과 더불어 포도주를 마셨고, 옥스퍼드에서 한 일을 유쾌하게 이야기했고, 장인에게 라자러스로 그윈 박사를 찾아뵈어야 한다고 일렀으며, 다시금 애러빈 씨에 대한 칭찬을 늘어놓았다.\n\n“애러빈 씨는 결혼하셨나요, 아버지?” 하고 그리젤다가 물었다.\n\n“아니다, 얘야. 칼리지의 펠로는 결혼하는 법이 없단다.”\n\n“젊은 분이신가요, 아버지?”\n\n“마흔쯤 되었을 게다.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“어머!” 하고 그리젤다가 말했다. 아버지가 여든이라고 했더라도 그리젤다에게 애러빈 씨가 그리 더 늙어 보이지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n두 신사만 남아 포도주를 앞에 두게 되었을 때, 하딩 씨는 제 딱한 사정을 털어놓았다. 그러나 사정이 딱하기는 해도 그 이야기가 부주교의 좋은 기분을 크게 덜어 내지는 못했으니, 다만 그의 호전성을 크게 북돋웠을 뿐이었다.\n\n“그자는 그렇게 못 합니다.” 장인이 요양원의 새 워든을 임명하는 조건을 설명하는 동안 그랜틀리 박사는 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. “그자는 그렇게 못 합니다. 그자가 하는 말은 귀 기울일 값어치도 없어요. 그 자리의 직무를 제멋대로 바꿀 수는 없습니다.”\n\n“누가 못 한다는 건가?” 전임 워든이 물었다.\n\n“주교도, 전속 사제도, 하물며 주교 부인도 못 합니다. 하기야 그런 일에 실제로 참견하는 몫은 그 부인이 나머지 둘을 합친 것보다 크겠습니다만. 주교관 식솔이 통째로 달려든다 해도 요양원 워든을 주일학교 교사로 둔갑시킬 권한은 없습니다.”\n\n“하지만 주교에게는 마음에 드는 사람을 임명할 권한이 있고, 또—”\n\n“그건 모를 일이지요. 저는 오히려 주교가 그런 권한이 없다는 걸 알게 되리라고 봅니다. 어디 한번 해 보라지요, 그러면 신문이 뭐라고 하는지 보게 될 겁니다. 이번만큼은 여론이 우리 편일 테니까요. 하지만 프라우디는 얼간이이긴 해도 세상 물정은 너무 잘 아는 사람이라, 제 귓가에 그런 말벌 집을 만들어 두지는 않을 겁니다.”\n\n하딩 씨는 신문 이야기에 몸이 움츠러들었다. 그런 종류의 세평이라면 겪을 만큼 겪었고, 괴물로든 순교자로든 다시 한번 구경거리가 되고 싶지는 않았다. 그는 신문이 제 이름을 다시 물고 늘어지지 않았으면 좋겠다고 조용히 말했고, 이어 어쩌면 이 일을 그만두는 편이 낫겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. “나도 늙어 가네.” 하고 그가 말했다. “게다가 새 직무를 맡을 만한 사람인지도 사실 의심스럽고 말일세.”\n\n“새 직무라니요?” 부주교가 말했다. “새 직무 같은 건 없을 거라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?”\n\n“아니면 옛 직무도 마찬가지겠지.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “나는 지금 이대로 만족하며 지낼까 하네.” 쓰레기를 수레에 실어 내가던 슬로프 씨의 모습이 아직도 그의 머릿속에 남아 있었다.\n\n부주교는 클라레 잔을 단숨에 비우고는 기운을 낼 채비를 했다. “저는 정말이지 바랍니다.” 그가 말했다. “장인어른께서 슬로프 씨 같은 자에게 겁을 먹고 마땅히 하셔야 할 일을 못 하실 만큼 약해지지는 않으시기를요. 의회가 봉급 문제를 그렇게 정리해 주어 애초에 사임하시게 만들었던 어려움이 사라진 지금, 요양원의 자리로 돌아가시는 것이 장인어른의 의무라는 것을 아시지 않습니까. 이건 부인하지 못하실 겁니다. 그리고 이제 와서 겁이 나 그리하지 못하신다면, 장인어른의 양심이 두고두고 스스로를 용서하지 않을 겁니다.” 그러고서 그는 이 대목의 말을 마치면서 술병을 상대 쪽으로 밀어 주었다.\n\n“양심이 결코 용서하지 않을 겁니다.” 그가 말을 이었다. “장인어른은 양심의 가책 때문에 그 자리를 내놓으셨지요. 저야 그 가책에 동의하지는 않았습니다만 크게 존경했습니다. 장인어른의 벗들이 모두 그것을 존경했고, 장인어른은 재산으로는 거덜이 난 만큼이나 명성으로는 넉넉해진 채 옛집을 떠나셨습니다. 이제 세상은 장인어른이 돌아가시기를 기대하고 있습니다. 그윈 박사도 바로 요전에 말씀하시기를—”\n\n“그윈 박사는 내가 그이를 마지막으로 본 때보다 얼마나 더 늙었는지를 헤아리지 못하는 걸세.”\n\n“늙기는 무슨, 당치 않은 말씀입니다.” 부주교가 말했다. “장인어른은 주교관에 있는 저 멋쟁이 얼간이의 뻔뻔한 헛소리를 듣기 전까지는 스스로를 늙었다고 여기신 적이 없지 않습니까.”\n\n“11월까지 살면 예순다섯이 되네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“10년 뒤 11월까지 사시면 일흔다섯이 되시겠지요.” 부주교가 말했다. “그리고 그때에도 10년 전만큼이나 정정하게 일하실 게 틀림없습니다. 하지만 제발이지 이 일에 핑계는 대지 맙시다. 늙었다는 말씀은 핑계입니다. 그런데 포도주를 통 안 드시는군요. 그것도 핑계일 뿐입니다. 사실인즉 장인어른은 이 슬로프라는 자가 반쯤 두려우신 거고, 가만두면 사람을 짓밟고 지나갈 자와 맞붙느니 차라리 궁색과 불편을 감수하시겠다는 겁니다.”\n\n“피할 수만 있다면 맞붙는 건 확실히 내키지 않네.”\n\n“저도 내키지 않습니다—하지만 피할 수 없을 때도 있지요. 저자의 목적은 장인어른이 요양원을 마다하게 만들어 제 수하 아무나 거기 앉히는 것입니다. 그리하여 제 힘을 과시하고, 그 처지와 인품이 참사회의 그것과 그토록 단단히 얽혀 있는 장인어른을 모욕함으로써 우리 모두를 모욕하려는 겁니다. 장인어른께서 스스로를 위할 마음이 없으시더라도, 우리 모두를 위해 이 일에서 저자에게 맞서 주셔야 할 도리가 있습니다. 그러나 무엇보다 장인어른 자신을 위해서라도, 저자가 미끼를 놓아 둔 이 덫에 겁쟁이처럼 걸려들어 입에 든 밥을 싸움 한 번 없이 빼앗기시는 일은 없어야 합니다.”\n\n하딩 씨는 겁쟁이라는 말을 듣는 것이 달갑지 않았고, 오히려 발끈하고 싶은 심정이었다. “돈을 두고 다투는 일에 참된 용기랄 것이 있을지 나는 의심스럽네.” 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n“이 사악한 세상에서 정직한 사람들이 돈을 두고 다투지 않는다면 부정직한 자들이 그것을 죄다 차지할 테고, 그렇다고 미덕의 명분이 크게 나아질 것 같지도 않습니다. 안 됩니다—우리는 우리에게 있는 수단을 써야 합니다. 장인어른의 논리를 끝까지 밀고 가자면 교회가 가진 수입을 한 실링까지 몽땅 내주어야 할 텐데, 그런 희생으로 교회가 튼튼해지리라고 말씀하실 준비는 안 되어 계시겠지요.” 부주교는 잔을 채운 다음 비웠으니, 제 영혼에 그토록 소중한 저 세속적 재산의 안녕과 항구한 보전을 위해 자못 경건하게 말없는 축배를 든 것이었다.\n\n“성직자와 그 주교 사이의 다툼은 무엇이든 피해야 한다고 나는 생각하네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“저도 그렇게 생각합니다. 하지만 그것을 살필 의무는 아랫사람 못지않게 주교에게도 있지요. 이렇게 하십시다, 장인어른. 이 일로 제가 주교를 만나 보겠습니다—허락해 주신다면 말이지요—그리고 장인어른께 누가 될 말은 결코 하지 않을 테니 안심하십시오. 제 생각으로는 주일학교니 설교니 하는 이 시시한 소리는 죄다 슬로프와 프라우디 부인에게서 나온 것이고, 주교는 아무것도 모르고 있습니다. 주교로서는 저를 만나지 않겠다고 하기가 어려울 테고, 저는 아내도 전속 사제도 곁에 없을 때를 골라 들이닥칠 겁니다. 결국 주교가 아무 조건도 붙이지 않고 임명장을 보내오는 것으로 끝나리라는 것을 아시게 될 겁니다. 그리고 대성당 좌석 문제는 수석 사제께 맡겨 두어도 무방합니다. 그 얼간이는 주교가 마음만 먹으면 대성당을 통째로 걸머지고 걸어 나갈 수도 있다고 정말로 믿는 모양이더군요.”\n\n그리하여 두 사람 사이에 일이 그렇게 정해졌다. 하딩 씨는 오로지 조언을 구하러 온 터라 주어진 조언을 따라야 한다고 스스로 여겼다. 게다가 그는 부주교가 이 일을 그만두겠다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않으리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었고, 따라서 제 생각을 참으로 진심에서 내놓기는 했으되 물러설 채비는 되어 있었다.\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 서로 기분 좋게 응접실로 건너갔고, 저녁 시간은 애러빈과 슬로프가 앞으로 벌일 전쟁을 점치는 이야기로 유쾌하게 흘러갔다. 개구리와 쥐의 싸움도 그에 견주면 아무것도 아닐 터이고, 아가멤논과 아킬레우스의 분노 또한 마찬가지일 터였다. 부주교는 제 마지막 한 수가 성공한 것을 두고 어찌나 손을 비비며 우쭐대던지. 그 자신은 슬로프와 더불어 투기장으로 내려갈 수 없는 몸이었으나 애러빈에게는 그런 거리낌이 없을 것이었다. 애러빈이야말로 그런 일에 꼭 맞는 사람이었고, 그가 아는 사람 가운데 그 일에 합당한 유일한 사람이었다.\n\n부주교의 좋은 기분과 드높은 흥은 베개에 몸을 누였을 때까지도 이어졌으나, 그때 그랜틀리 부인이 바체스터의 형편을 저는 어떻게 보는지 남편에게 들려주기 시작했다. 그러자 그는 과연 흠칫 놀랐다. 그날 밤 그가 마지막으로 한 말은 이러했다.\n\n“그 애가 정말 그런다면, 맹세코 나는 다시는 그 애와 말을 섞지 않겠소. 그 애가 나를 한 번 진창으로 끌고 들어갔지만, 그런 오물로 내 몸을 더럽히지는—” 그러고서 부주교는 몸서리를 쳤는데, 그때 그의 머릿속을 뒤흔든 생각에 어찌나 격하게 경련했던지 방 전체가 흔들릴 지경이었다.\n\n그런데 이 일에서 미망인 볼드 부인은 제 친척들에게 언어도단으로 부당한 대접을 받고 있었다. 그녀는 그 사내와 서너 번 말을 나누었고, 주일학교에서 가르쳐도 좋다는 뜻을 밝혔을 뿐이다. 슬로프 씨와 관련해 그녀가 지은 죄의 전부가 그것이었다. 가엾은 엘리너! 그러나 그 뒤는 시간이 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n이튿날 아침 하딩 씨는 바체스터로 돌아갔으니, 슬로프 씨와 제 작은딸의 친분에 관해서는 그가 듣는 데서 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 그러나 그는 아침 식탁에서 부주교가 전날 저녁보다 덜 다정하다는 것을 눈치챘다.\n\n# 제15장. 미망인의 구혼자들\n\n슬로프 씨는 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보아도 좋다는 주교의 허락을 지체 없이 써먹었는데, 이 훌륭한 목사와 마주 앉은 자리에서 그는 볼드 부인이 구애할 만한 값어치가 있는 사람이라는 사실을 처음으로 알게 되었다. 그는 푸딩데일까지 말을 몰고 나가, 장차 워든이 될 이 사람에게 주교가 그를 좋게 보고 있다는 뜻을 전했고, 그 일을 의논하는 동안 하딩 씨와 그 집안의 금전 형편이 이야깃거리로 떠오른 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다.\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 자녀가 열넷에 한 해 400파운드로 살아가는 몹시 가난한 사람이었으므로, 지금의 성직록과 겸해서 받게 될 이 새 자리의 전망이 여간 고마운 것이 아니었다. 그런 처지에 놓인 성직자라면 어느 누가 그런 전망을 고맙게 여기지 않겠는가? 그러나 퀴버풀 씨는 오래전부터 하딩 씨와 알고 지냈고 그의 손에서 호의를 받은 적도 있었으므로, 요양원에서 벗의 자리를 가로챈다는 생각에 마음이 켕겼다. 그럼에도 그는 슬로프 씨에게 지극히 공손했다. 굽실거릴 만큼 공손했다. 그를 아주 대단한 인물로 떠받들었고, 이 대단한 인물에게 셰리주를 한 잔 들어 주는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 간청했는데, 그것이 형편없는 마르살라였던 탓에 이제는 입이 높아진 슬로프는 코를 찡그렸다. 그리고 끝에 가서는 주교와 슬로프 씨에게 더없이 큰 은혜를 입었노라고, 요양원 자리를 몹시 맡고 싶노라고 밝혔다. 다만, 다만 하딩 씨가 정말로 그것을 거절한 것이 확실하다면 말이다.\n\n퀴버풀 씨만큼 궁한 처지에 놓인 사람 가운데 그보다 더 사심 없는 이가 어디 있었겠는가?\n\n“하딩 씨는 분명히 그것을 거절했습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 어딘가 자존심이 상했다는 투로 말했다. “이번 임명에 붙게 된 조건을 듣고 나서 말이지요. 물론 퀴버풀 씨, 똑같은 조건이 당신께도 그대로 부과되리라는 것은 알고 계시겠지요.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 조건 따위에는 조금도 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 슬로프 씨가 받아쓰라고 시키는 설교라면 몇 편이든 맡았을 것이고, 일요일에 남은 시간을 죄다 주일학교 담장 안에서 보내는 일도 마다하지 않았을 것이다. 수입이 그만큼 늘고 그런 집까지 생긴다는데, 무슨 희생인들, 아니 적어도 무슨 약속인들 지나치다 하겠는가! 그러나 그의 마음은 여전히 하딩 씨에게로 되돌아갔다.\n\n“하기야,” 그가 말했다. “하딩 씨 따님이 아주 부자이니, 그분이 요양원 일로 수고하실 까닭이야 없겠지요.”\n\n“그랜틀리 부인 말씀이군요.” 슬로프가 말했다.\n\n“저는 미망인이 되신 따님을 말씀드린 것입니다.” 상대가 말했다. “볼드 부인은 제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있고, 하딩 씨는 그 따님과 함께 지내실 생각인 듯합니다.”\n\n“제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드라!” 슬로프는 그렇게 말하고는 곧 자리를 떴다. 요양원 이야기는 더 꺼내지 않으려고 할 수 있는 데까지 피하면서 말이다. “한 해 1,200파운드!” 그는 말을 천천히 몰아 돌아가면서 혼잣말을 했다. 볼드 부인이 제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있는 것이 사실이라면, 그 아버지가 옛 자리로 돌아가는 일을 막고 나서는 자야말로 얼마나 어리석은 자이겠는가. 슬로프 씨의 생각이 어느 갈래로 뻗어 갔는지는 아마 내 독자 누구에게나 훤할 것이다. 그 한 해 1,200파운드를 제 것으로 만들지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가? 그리고 그렇게 한다면, 이 세상의 좋은 것들을 넉넉히 갖춘 장인을 두는 편이 좋지 않겠는가? 게다가 아비의 뜻을 이루어 주려고 힘닿는 데까지 애쓴다면 딸의 마음을 얻기가 훨씬 수월하지 않겠는가?\n\n이 물음들은 그에게 아주 강하게 밀려들었으나, 그래도 미심쩍은 대목이 한둘이 아니었다. 하딩 씨에게 옛 자리를 돌려주기로 마음을 정한다면 당장 그에 필요한 조처를 밟아야 했다. 주교를 곧바로 구슬려야 하고, 구슬릴 수 없다는 것을 스스로도 아는 프라우디 부인과는 이 일로 다투어야 하며, 퀴버풀 씨에게는 하딩 씨가 확실히 거절했다고 한 자기 말이 조금 성급했음을 알려야 했다. 이 모두를 해낼 수 있다는 것은 의심하지 않았으나, 아무 대가 없이 해내고 싶지는 않았다. 하딩 씨에게 양보해 놓고 그 딸에게 퇴짜를 맞고 싶지는 않았다. 세력 있는 벗 하나를 새로 얻기도 전에 다른 하나를 잃고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n그리하여 그는 여러 가지를 마음속으로 곱씹으며 말을 몰아 돌아갔다. 볼드 부인이 부주교의 처제라는 데에 생각이 미쳤고, 한 해 1,200파운드를 준다 해도 그 오만한 사내에게 굽히지는 않겠다는 데에도 생각이 미쳤다. 부유한 아내는 그에게 큰 소망거리였으나 성직에서의 출세는 그보다 더 큰 것이었다. 게다가 아내가 되어 줄 마음이 있을 만한 부유한 여자가 달리 없는 것도 아니었고, 무엇보다 이 한 해 1,200파운드라는 것도 막상 알아보면 그가 거들떠볼 것도 못 되는 푼돈으로 녹아 버릴지 몰랐다. 그러다 볼드 부인에게 아들이 있다는 것도 떠올랐다.\n\n또 한 가지 사정이 그에게 크게 작용했는데, 그것은 거의 그의 뜻을 거슬러 작용했다고 해야 할 것이었다. 시뇨라 네로니의 모습이 노상 그의 눈앞에 어른거렸다. 슬로프 씨가 사랑에 빠져 정신을 잃었다고까지 하면 지나친 말이겠으나, 그래도 그는 그토록 아름다운 여자는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했고 또 줄곧 그렇게 생각했다. 그는 본디 그런 충동에 쉽게 열리는 사람이었고, 이탈리아 물이 든 그 요염한 여자의 술수는 그의 머릿속을 차지하는 데 완전히 성공했다. 그의 마음에 대해서는 말하지 않기로 하자. 그에게 마음이 없어서가 아니라, 지금의 이 감정에 그의 마음이 관여한 바가 거의 없었기 때문이다. 그의 취향이 흡족했고, 눈이 홀렸으며, 허영이 채워졌다. 그는 여태 본 적 없는 종류의 아름다움에 눈이 부셨고, 그로서는 전혀 새로운, 스스럼없고 자유롭고 관능적인 태도에 사로잡혔다. 그렇게까지 유혹을 받아 본 적이 없었고, 이번 유혹은 뿌리칠 수 없었다. 그는 이 여자를 주위의 다른 여자들보다 더 마음에 두고 있다고 스스로 인정하지는 않았으나, 다음에 언제 그녀를 볼 수 있을지 자주 생각했고, 거의 저도 모르는 사이에 그녀를 자주 볼 수 있는 잔꾀를 이것저것 꾸며 두었다.\n\n그는 주교관 연회 다음 날 스탠호프 박사 댁을 찾아갔고, 그때 그의 뜨거운 흠모는 새 땔감을 얻었다. 주교의 소파에 누워 그 많은 눈길을 받고 있을 때에도 시뇨라의 태도가 다정하고 말이 사람을 우쭐하게 했다면, 제 어머니의 응접실에서 언니 말고는 아무도 없어 타고난 성정도 몸에 익힌 기교도 억누를 것이 없을 때에는 훨씬 더 그러했다. 슬로프 씨는 그렇게 얼이 빠진 채 그 집을 나섰으니, 이 부인과의 각별한 우정을 앞으로 아주 끊어야 한다는 대목이 한 자락이라도 들어 있는 계획이라면 어떤 것도 기꺼이 머릿속에 들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 그는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 천천히 말을 몰아갔다.\n\n여기서 글쓴이는 슬로프 씨가 매사에 나쁜 사람은 아니었다는 점을 기억해 주기를 청해야겠다. 그의 동기는 여느 사람들이 다 그러하듯 뒤섞여 있었고, 그의 행실이 대체로 우리가 칭찬하고 싶은 것과는 거리가 멀었다 해도, 그 행실을 움직인 것은 어쩌면 세상 사람 태반과 마찬가지로 제 본분을 다하려는 마음이었다. 그는 자기가 가르치는 종교를 믿었다. 그 종교가 아무리 모질고 거슬리고 인정 없는 것이었다 해도 그러했다. 그는 자기가 발밑에 두고자 하는 이들, 곧 교회의 그랜틀리와 그윈 같은 무리가 그 종교의 적이라고 믿었다. 그는 자신이 큰일을 하도록 정해진 튼튼한 기둥이라고 믿었으며, 사람이면 누구나 빠지기 쉬운 그 교묘하고 이기적이고 애매한 궤변으로, 제 잇속을 크게 도모하는 일이 곧 종교를 크게 도모하는 일이기도 하다고 스스로를 가르쳐 놓았다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 결코 부도덕한 사람이었던 적이 없다. 오히려 그는 부도덕으로 이끄는 유혹을 그로서는 칭찬받을 만한 굳은 뜻으로 물리쳐 왔다. 그는 일찍부터 젊음의 예사로운 즐거움과는 어울리지 않는 일에 몸을 바쳤고, 그런 즐거움을 버리기까지 나름의 싸움이 없지 않았다. 그러니 그가 남의 아내 된 여인의 아름다움을 뜨겁게 흠모한다고 스스로 인정하면서 양심의 가책을 뼈아프게 느끼지 않았을 리 없다고 보아야 한다. 그리고 그 양심을 달래기 위해 그는 제 흠모가 결백한 종류의 것이라고 스스로를 가르쳐야 했다.\n\n그리하여 그는 생각에 잠긴 채, 마음이 편치 않은 채 말을 몰아갔다. 그의 양심은 그가 미망인과 그 재산을 골랐다는 데 대해서는 한마디도 할 말이 없었다. 그 일은 오히려 경건한 일로 여겨졌으니, 끝까지 밀고 나간다면 그리스도인으로서의 명예를 높여 줄 행실이었다. 그쪽에는 뒷날의 뉘우침도, 잊어버려야 할 처신도, 마음속의 가책도 없었다. 볼드 부인이 정말로 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있는 것이 사실로 드러난다면, 슬로프 씨는 그 아내와 그 돈의 주인이 되는 일을 오히려 제 종교에 지고 있는 본분으로 여길 것이었다. 그것도 어느 정도의 자기희생이 따르는 본분으로 말이다. 시뇨라와의 우정을 버려야 할 것이고, 하딩 씨에게 맞서는 일도 버려야 할 것이며, 반감도 — 아니, 곰곰이 자신을 살펴본 끝에 그는 그랜틀리 박사에 대한 반감만은 도저히 버릴 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 그 부인이 그런 조건을 받아들인다면 그는 그녀의 형부에게 맞서는 적으로서 그녀와 혼인할 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면 부인은 남편감을 다른 데서 찾아야 할 것이었다.\n\n그런 결심을 품고 그는 바체스터에 다다랐다. 그는 우선 이 부인의 재산에 관한 진상이 무엇인지 알아보고, 그런 다음 요양원 문제에 관한 처신은 형편에 따라 정하기로 했다. 큰 자기희생 없이 방향을 돌려 그 자리를 하딩 씨에게 확보해 줄 수 있다면 그렇게 할 것이고, 그럴 수 없다면 아비에게 맞서면서 딸에게 구애할 것이었다. 그러나 어떤 경우에도 부주교에게 굽히지는 않을 것이었다.\n\n그는 말이 마구간으로 끌려가는 것을 보고 나서 곧바로 알아보러 나섰다. 슬로프 씨에게 공평하게 말하자면, 그는 제 발밑에 풀이 길게 자라도록 내버려 두는 사람이 결코 아니었다.\n\n가엾은 엘리너! 그녀는 하나가 아닌 여러 계략의 표적이 될 운명이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일의 교구 사제를 찾아가 있던 무렵, 대성당 경내의 스탠호프 박사 댁에서는 그녀의 아리따움과 재산을 두고 이야기가 오갔다. 그 집에는 아침 손님들이 다녀갔고, 사람들은 존 볼드가 남긴 재산에 관해 얼마쯤은 사실을, 또 얼마쯤은 헛소리를 늘어놓았다. 손님들이 하나둘 물러가고 박사도 그들과 함께 나가 버린 데다 박사의 부인은 아직 모습을 보이지 않았으므로, 샬럿 스탠호프와 그 동생만 남게 되었다. 그는 탁자 앞에 하릴없이 앉아 바체스터 명사들의 우스개 그림을 끼적이다가 하품을 하다가 책을 한두 권 뒤적이다가 하면서, 힘들이지 않고 시간을 죽일 방도를 못 찾아 딱한 꼴이었다.\n\n“버티, 주문 받는 일은 별로 못 했나 보네.” 누나가 말했다.\n\n“주문이라니!” 그가 말했다. “바체스터에 주문을 줄 사람이 대체 어디 있어? 여기 사람들 가운데 제 머리를 대리석으로 떠낼 값어치가 있다고 여길 자가 누가 있겠어?”\n\n“그럼 네 일을 아주 접겠다는 말이네.” 누나가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 그럴 생각 없어.” 그는 주교의 우스꽝스러운 초상을 계속 그려 가며 말했다. “로테, 이것 좀 봐. 앞치마까지 그 조그만 양반 그대로지? 아버지가 런던에 작업실이나 하나 차려 주신다면야 누나 말대로 당장이라도 일을 시작하겠어. 하지만 바체스터에서 조각이라니 — 여기 사람 절반은 토르소가 뭔지도 모를걸.”\n\n“아버지는 너를 런던에 자리 잡아 주려고 1실링도 내주지 않으실 거야.” 로테가 말했다. “아니, 넉넉히 줄 만한 것을 가지고 계시지도 않으니 주실 수가 없지. 하지만 네가 마음만 먹으면 너 스스로 얼마든지 시작할 수 있어.”\n\n“대체 나더러 어떻게 하라고?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“솔직히 말하면 버티, 너는 무슨 일을 하든 한 푼도 벌지 못할 거야.”\n\n“나도 자주 그렇게 생각해.” 그는 조금도 언짢아하지 않고 말했다. “어떤 사람은 돈 버는 재주는 크게 타고났는데 쓸 줄을 몰라. 또 어떤 사람은 2실링도 못 모으면서 온갖 씀씀이에는 대단한 재능이 있지. 아무래도 내 재능은 죄다 뒤쪽에만 몰려 있는 것 같아.”\n\n“그럼 앞으로 어떻게 살 작정인데?” 누나가 물었다.\n\n“나야 나를 어린 까마귀쯤으로 여기고 하늘에서 만나가 내리기를 기다려야지. 게다가 아버지가 가시고 나면 우리 모두 얼마쯤은 받을 것 아냐.”\n\n“그래 — 장갑이랑 구두나 사 신을 만큼은 되겠지. 그것도 유대인들이 죄다 차지해 버리지 않았다면 말이야. 내가 보기엔 벌써 태반이 그자들 손에 들어가 있어. 버티, 나는 네 그 태평함이 놀라워. 너 같은 재주와 인물을 가지고 어째서 한 번도 제 앞가림을 하려 들지 않는지. 나는 아버지가 가셔야 할 때가 오는 게 무서워. 어머니하고 마들린하고 나는 가난할 대로 가난해지겠지만, 너는 그야말로 아무것도 없을 거야.”\n\n“한 날의 괴로움은 그날로 족하니라.” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“내 말대로 하겠어?” 누나가 말했다.\n\n“Cela dépend.” 동생이 말했다.\n\n“돈 있는 여자와 혼인하겠느냐 말이야.”\n\n“적어도,” 그가 말했다. “돈 없는 여자와는 혼인하지 않을 거야. 요새는 돈 있는 아내를 얻기가 그리 쉽지 않아. 목사들이 죄다 채 가거든.”\n\n“그리고 네가 얼른 서두르지 않으면 내가 점찍어 둔 그 아내도 목사가 채 갈 거야. 내가 말하는 아내는 볼드 부인이야.”\n\n“휘이이익!” 버티가 휘파람을 불었다. “미망인이라!”\n\n“아주 아름다운 사람이야.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“아들에 상속자까지 통째로 딸려서 말이지.” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“십중팔구 죽을 갓난아이인걸.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“그건 모를 일이지.” 버티가 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 나를 위해 살아 준다면야 — 내가 그 아이를 죽이고 싶다는 건 아니니까. 다만 다 만들어진 식구가 딸린 건 흠이라고 해야겠지.”\n\n“그래 봐야 하나뿐이잖아.” 샬럿이 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n“게다가 그것도 아주 조그만 하나뿐이지 — 하녀가 했다는 말마따나.” 버티가 받아 말했다.\n\n“거지가 찬밥 더운밥 가리면 못써, 버티. 다 가질 수는 없는 법이야.”\n\n“하느님도 아시지만 나는 무리한 사람도 아니고 고집쟁이도 아니야.” 그가 말했다. “로테 누나가 다 알아서 마련해 준다면 나는 그 부인과 혼인하겠어. 다만 이것만은 새겨 둬. 돈은 확실해야 하고, 그 수입은 적어도 그 부인이 살아 있는 동안에는 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있어야 해.”\n\n샬럿은 이 일을 밀고 나갈 생각이라면 구애는 네가 직접 해야 한다고 동생에게 일러 주면서 엘리너의 아름다움을 한껏 추어올려 그를 부추기고 있었는데, 그때 시뇨라가 응접실로 옮겨져 들어왔다. 집에 있을 때, 그리고 제 식구 말고는 아무 눈길도 닿지 않을 때 그녀는 두 사람에게 몸을 맡겨 이리저리 옮겨 다녔거니와, 그 두 사람이 이제 그녀를 소파에 내려놓은 것이었다. 옷차림은 주교관 연회 때만큼 화려하지 않았으나 그래도 매우 공들여 차려입었고, 눈가에 근심과 아픔의 기색이 어려 있는데도 그녀는 대낮에 보아도 지극히 아름다웠다.\n\n“그래 마들린, 나 장가간대.” 하인들이 물러가자마자 버티가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“네가 아직 안 해 본 어리석은 짓이라곤 그것밖에 안 남았으니,” 마들린이 말했다. “그거라도 해 보는 게 옳지.”\n\n“아, 누나는 그게 어리석은 짓이라고 생각하는구나?” 그가 말했다. “로테 누나는 무슨 일이 있어도 혼인하라던데. 하지만 그런 문제라면 누나 의견이 제일 나을 수밖에 없지. 길잡이가 될 경험이 있으니까.”\n\n“그래, 있고말고.” 마들린이 어딘가 모질고 서글픈 어조로 말했는데, 그 어조는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. “내가 서글프든 말든 너와 무슨 상관이냐? 나는 네 동정을 구한 적이 없다.”\n\n버티는 제 말에 누나가 상했다는 것을 알고 미안해져서, 다가가 그 얼굴 바로 앞 바닥에 쭈그리고 앉아 화해를 청했다.\n\n“자, 매드, 농담이었어. 누나도 알잖아. 하지만 진담으로 말하면, 로테 누나가 나더러 혼인하라고 해. 이 볼드 부인이라는 사람과 맺어 주려는 거야. 돈은 잔뜩 있고, 아기는 튼튼하고, 살결은 곱고, 하이 스트리트에 조지앤드드래건 여관까지 딸린 미망인이지. 아이고 로테 누나, 내가 그 부인과 혼인하면 그 술집은 내가 직접 꾸릴 테야. 딱 내게 맞는 삶이잖아.”\n\n“뭐라고?” 마들린이 말했다. “미망인 모자를 쓴 그 밍밍하고 거무튀튀한 여자 말이니? 옷을 쇠스랑으로 등에 꽂아 놓은 것처럼 보이던 그 여자?” 시뇨라는 어떤 여자도 아름답다고 인정해 주는 법이 없었다.\n\n“밍밍하기는커녕,” 로테가 말했다. “나는 아주 사랑스러운 여자라고 보는데. 그날 밤 그 방에 있던 여자들 가운데 단연 제일 사랑스러웠어. 물론 마들린 너는 빼고.”\n\n그 칭찬조차 불구가 된 미인의 신랄함을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다. “로테 눈에는 여자란 여자가 다 매력적이지.” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 그렇게 볼 줄 모르는 눈은 처음 봤어. 우선 첫째로, 그 여자가 머리에 얹고 있던 그런 물건을 쓰고 어울릴 여자가 이 세상에 어디 있겠니.”\n\n“그야 미망인 모자를 쓰지. 하지만 버티가 혼인하면 벗을 거야.”\n\n“거기에 그야가 어디 있다는 건지 모르겠구나.” 마들린이 말했다. “남편이 스무 번 죽는다 해도 나라면 그런 고행은 하지 않겠어. 그건 남편의 시신을 태울 때 힌두 여자를 함께 바치는 것만큼이나 이교의 유물이야. 그만큼 피비린내가 나지는 않을지 몰라도 그만큼 야만스럽고 그만큼 쓸데없어.”\n\n“그렇다고 누나가 그 부인을 나무랄 건 아니잖아.” 버티가 말했다. “나라의 풍습이 그러니 그렇게 하는 거지. 안 그러면 사람들이 흉볼 테고.”\n\n“바로 그거야.” 마들린이 말했다. “그 여자는 딱 그런 영국식 껍데기들 가운데 하나야. 제 어머니와 할머니가 그렇게 했다면 여름마다 석 달씩 머리를 자루에 싸매고 다닐 사람이지. 그런 성가신 짓에 순순히 따르는 게 무슨 소용이 있는지 따져 볼 생각조차 못 할걸.”\n\n“영국 같은 나라에서 젊은 여자가 그런 편견에 맞서기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니야.” 조심성 있는 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“네 말은 곧 바보가 바보 아니기가 여간 어렵지 않다는 소리지.” 마들린이 말했다.\n\n버티 스탠호프는 어릴 적부터 워낙 세상을 굴러다닌 터라 영국 풍습의 근엄함 따위에는 존경심이 별로 남아 있지 않았다. 그러나 그런 그의 머리에도, 아내로 두기에는 어쩌면 진짜 영국식 편견이 영국·이탈리아 잡탕의 거리낌 없음보다 길게 보아 덜 마음에 들지는 않으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그는 그렇게 대놓고 말하지는 않고 다른 식으로 그 생각을 드러냈다.\n\n“내 생각엔,” 그가 말했다. “내가 죽어서 혼백이 되어 돌아다닌다면, 내 아내는 다른 어떤 머리쓰개보다 그런 모자를 쓴 편이 더 나아 보인다고 여길 것 같아.”\n\n“그래 — 그리고 아내가 방에 틀어박혀 너를 위해 우는 것보다, 아니면 제 몸을 태우는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없다고도 여기겠지. 하지만 아내 생각은 다를 거야. 그 끔찍한 계집 투구를 쓰기야 하겠지, 안 쓸 용기가 없을 테니까. 그러나 언제쯤 그것을 벗어 던져도 될까 애타게 기다리는 마음으로 쓰고 있을 거야. 나는 그런 얄팍한 거짓 시늉이 싫어. 나 같으면 세상이야 뭐라 하든 내버려 두고, 슬프지 않으면 슬픈 티를 내지 않겠어 — 슬프더라도 어쩌면 내지 않을 테고.”\n\n“그렇지만 미망인 모자를 쓴다고 재산이 줄지는 않잖아.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“늘지도 않지.” 마들린이 말했다. “그럼 대체 왜 그걸 쓰는 건데?”\n\n“로테 누나 속셈이 바로 그걸 벗기자는 거잖아.” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“정말로 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있고 몸가짐이 아주 천하기만 하지 않다면, 나는 너더러 그 여자와 혼인하라고 하겠어. 청하기만 하면 넘어올 것 같기도 하고. 게다가 너는 사랑 때문에 혼인하는 게 아니니 그 여자가 반반하든 말든 크게 상관없지. 네가 정말로 사랑 때문에 여자와 혼인하는 일로 말하자면, 나는 네가 그만큼 바보라고는 생각하지 않아.”\n\n“아니 마들린!” 언니가 소리쳤다.\n\n“아니 샬럿!” 다른 쪽이 말했다.\n\n“남자가 바보가 아니고서는 여자를 사랑할 수 없다는 말은 아니겠지?”\n\n“거의 그런 말이지 — 반반한 얼굴을 차지하겠다고 제 잇속을 기꺼이 내던지는 남자는 누구든 바보라는 말이야. 반반한 얼굴이야 그보다 싸게도 얻을 수 있어. 로테, 나는 네 그 느끼한 감상이 싫어. 남편과 아내가 대개 어떤 식으로 함께 사는지는 나만큼 너도 알잖아. 부부의 정이라는 온기가 형편없는 저녁상 앞에서, 비 오는 날 앞에서, 가난이 몰고 오는 아주 사소한 궁핍 앞에서 얼마나 버티는지도 알잖아. 남자가 제 몫으로 어떤 자유를 요구하는지, 할 수만 있다면 아내에게 어떤 종살이를 시키려 드는지도 알잖아! 그리고 아내들이 대개 어떻게 순종하는지도 알고. 혼인이란 한쪽의 폭정이고 다른 쪽의 속임수야. 나는 그런 흥정에 제 잇속을 내던지는 남자는 바보라고 말하는 거야. 여자야 대개 달리 살아갈 길이 없으니 그렇다 치고.”\n\n“하지만 버티도 달리 살아갈 길이 없잖아.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 하느님 이름으로, 볼드 부인과 혼인하라고 해.” 마들린이 말했다. 그리하여 그들 사이에서 그렇게 정해졌다.\n\n그러나 마음 여린 독자여, 조금도 걱정하지 마시라. 엘리너가 슬로프 씨나 버티 스탠호프와 혼인하도록 정해져 있지는 않다. 그리고 여기서 소설가에게, 이야기를 짓는 기술의 아주 중요한 대목에 관해 제 생각을 밝히도록 허락해 주어도 좋으리라. 그는 셋째 권 끝머리에 이르도록 독자가 아끼는 인물의 운명을 수수께끼로 묶어 두어 글쓴이와 독자 사이의 마땅한 신의를 크게 저버리는 그 방식을 감히 나무라고자 한다. 아니, 그보다 더한, 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 너무 자주 벌어진다. 독자의 바람을 어긋나게 하고, 헛된 희망과 헛된 두려움을 일으키고, 결코 이루어지지 않을 기대를 부풀리는 데에 천재의 가장 깊은 공력이 쓰인 적이 어디 한두 번이던가? 즐거운 무서움을 안겨 주겠다는 약속이나 다름없는 것을 해 놓고, 그 대신 마지막 장에서는 지극히 진부한 사실 말고는 아무것도 내놓지 않는 일이 어디 한두 번이던가? 그리고 여기에는 이 정직한 시대가 결코 편들어 주어서는 안 될 일종의 기만이 있지 않은가?\n\n그리고 셋째 권을 한 번 엿보는 것만으로 말끔히 흩어져 버리는 그 조바심이 대체 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가? 누리는 순간 여지없이 부서져 버리는 그 문학적 매력이 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가? 래드클리프 부인의 그 엄숙한 장막 뒤에 걸려 있던 그림이 무엇이었는지 한번 알고 나면, 우리는 그 틀에도 그 휘장에도 더는 관심이 가지 않는다. 그것들은 우리에게 그저 낡은 뼈다귀를 담아 두는 그릇, 어울리지 않는 관일 뿐이어서, 눈앞에서 치워 점잖게 묻어 버렸으면 싶어진다.\n\n그리고 먼저 읽은 사람이 경솔하게 뽐내는 바람에 제 소설의 즐거움을 잃는 일은 또 얼마나 억울한가. “아, 어거스타 걱정은 안 해도 돼. 끝에 가서는 물론 구스타부스를 받아들이거든.” “수전 언니는 어쩌면 그렇게 심술궂어요.” 키티가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해서 말한다. “이제 하나도 재미가 없잖아요.” 가엾은 키티여, 내 책을 읽는다면 그대는 언니의 심술 따위를 두려워하지 않아도 된다. 언니가 일러 줄 만한 비밀이라곤 하나도 없을 테니. 아니, 마음이 내키거든 셋째 권을 집어 들어도 좋다. 마지막 몇 쪽에서 이 파란 많은 이야기의 결말을 죄다 알아내도 좋다. 그래도 이야기는 그 재미를 조금도 잃지 않을 것이다 — 잃을 만한 재미가 조금이라도 있다면 말이지만.\n\n우리가 내세우는 바는 글쓴이와 독자가 서로를 온전히 믿으며 나란히 나아가야 한다는 것이다. 극중 인물들이야 저희끼리 아무리 완벽한 착각의 희극을 겪어도 좋으나, 구경하는 이만은 시라쿠사 사람을 에페소스 사람으로 잘못 보는 일이 없어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 그도 속아 넘어간 자들 가운데 하나가 되는데, 속아 넘어간 자의 배역이란 결코 품위 있는 것이 못 된다.\n\n나는 이 한 장의 값어치를 다 준다 해도, 내 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 마음을 먹을 수 있다거나 버티 스탠호프 따위에게 제물로 바쳐지리라고 단 한 사람의 독자에게라도 믿게 하고 싶지 않다. 그러나 바체스터의 선량한 사람들 가운데에는 이쪽도 저쪽도 다 그렇게 되리라 믿는 이가 많았다.\n\n# 제16장. 아기 숭배\n\n“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥.” 엘리너 볼드가 그렇게 말했다, 아니 노래했다.\n\n“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥.” 메리 볼드가 이 합주의 제2성부를 맡아 뒤를 이었다.\n\n이 연주회의 관객은 아기 하나뿐이었으나, 그 관객이 어찌나 우렁차게 갈채를 보냈던지 연주자들은 그것을 앙코르 요청으로 알아듣고 다시 시작했다.\n\n“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥. 얘 다리 좀 봐요, 어쩌면 이렇게 예뻐요?” 황홀경에 빠진 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“음 음 음 음 음.” 메리가 입술을 이 조그만 녀석의 통통한 목덜미에 파묻으며, 그것을 입맞춤 삼아 부글부글 소리를 냈다.\n\n“음 음 음 음 음.” 엄마도 부글거리며 이번에는 아기의 통통하고 동그랗고 짤막한 다리에 입술을 파묻었다. “우리 애기는 쪼꼬만 볼드네 귀염둥이지, 그럼 그렇고말고. 온 세상에서 제일 예쁜 쪼꼬만 분홍 다리를 가졌지, 그럼 그렇고말고.” 그러고는 부글거림과 입맞춤이 처음부터 다시 시작되었으니, 두 여인은 마치 몹시 배가 고파서 아기를 잡아먹기로 작정한 사람들 같았다.\n\n“오냐, 그래, 우리 아기는 제 엄마만의 귀염둥이지. 오냐, 그럼 우리 아기는… 어머, 어머… 메리, 메리, 저것 좀 봐요. 이걸 어쩌면 좋아요? 우리 못된, 못된, 못된, 못된 쪼꼬만 조니.” 이 기운찬 감탄사들은 모두, 아들이 제 어미의 머리카락을 모자 밑에서 죄다 끄집어낼 만큼 힘도 세고 장난도 심하다는 것을 알아차린 어미의 기쁨에서 터져 나온 것이었다. “엄마 머리를 죄다 끌어내렸네, 이 녀석이. 이 세상에 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이 못되고, 못되고, 못된 쪼꼬만 사내로구나…”\n\n정식으로 아기 숭배 예배가 진행되고 있었다. 메리 볼드는 등받이가 낮은 안락의자에 앉아 사내아이를 무릎에 올려놓고 있었고, 엘리너는 제 우상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 그녀가 길고 윤이 나는 짙은 갈색 머리채로 이 조그만 녀석의 얼굴을 덮으려 하면서 아기가 제멋대로 그 머리채를 이리저리 잡아당기도록 내버려 두고 있는 모습은, 아직도 쓰고 있는 미망인의 모자에도 불구하고 무척 아름다웠다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 조용하고 한결같고 감사할 줄 아는 다정함이 서려 있었는데, 그것이 그녀를 아는 사람들에게는 하도 강하게 자라나는 까닭에, 오랜 벗들의 입에서 나오는 그 대단한 미모 예찬이 그녀와 겨우 안면만 튼 사람들에게는 놀랍도록 과장된 말로 들릴 지경이었다. 그녀의 아름다움은 여러 풍경의 아름다움과 같아서, 자주 보아야만 온전히 누릴 수 있었다. 그녀의 눈에는 성급한 관찰자는 놓치고 마는 어둡고 맑은 광채의 깊이가 있었고, 그녀의 입가에는 허물없이 이야기를 나누는 사람에게만 드러나는 표정이 있었으며, 그 머리의 훌륭한 생김새는 완벽한 균형을 알아보는 데 예술가의 눈을 필요로 할 정도였다. 마들린 네로니에게 다가간 남자들을 바실리스크의 힘으로 단번에 홀려 버리는 그 눈부신 광휘, 그 관능적인 루벤스풍의 미모, 그 진주 같은 흰빛과 주홍빛 색조는 그녀에게 하나도 없었다. 시뇨라에게는 도저히 저항할 수 없었으나, 엘리너에게는 누구도 저항 따위를 요구받지 않았다. 그녀에게는 마치 제 누이에게 말을 걸듯 이야기를 시작해도 좋았고, 그러고는 베개에 머리를 누이고 나서야 비로소 그 아름다움의 진실함과 강렬함이 문득 떠오르고 그 목소리의 감미로움이 귀에 되살아나는 것이었다. 네로니와 보내는 갑작스러운 30분은 구덩이에 빠지는 일 같았고, 엘리너와 보내는 저녁 한때는 아스포델이 핀 고요한 들판을 뜻하지 않게 거니는 일 같았다.\n\n“우리 아기를 폭 덮어 버려서 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만 코가 손톱만큼도 안 보이게 해 줄 테야.” 어머니가 흘러내리는 머리채를 아기의 얼굴 위로 펼치며 말했다. 아기는 좋아서 소리를 질러 대며 발버둥을 쳤고, 메리 볼드는 아기를 붙들고 있기가 벅찰 지경이었다.\n\n바로 그때 문이 열리며 슬로프 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 있었다. 엘리너는 벌떡 일어나 손을 재빨리 놀려 머리를 어깨 뒤로 넘겼다. 아마도 그러지 않는 편이 그녀에게는 나았을 것이다. 그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 가만히 있었을 때보다 오히려 당황한 기색을 더 많이 드러냈기 때문이다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 그 자리에서 그녀의 아름다움을 알아보고, 재산을 떠나서도 저 여자라면 한 남자가 제 집에 들이고 싶어 할 만한 식구요, 가슴속 근심을 나눌 반려로서 그 근심을 가볍게 만들어 줄 자격이 충분하다고 속으로 생각했다. 엘리너는 아기를 핑계 삼아 필요도 없는 변명을 웅얼거리며 모자를 고쳐 쓰러 방을 나갔다. 그녀가 나가 있는 동안, 우리는 잠시 뒤로 돌아가 혼인 계획에 관한 슬로프 씨의 궁리가 그때까지 어떤 결실을 맺었는지 간략히 밝혀 두기로 하자.\n\n미망인의 수입에 관한 그의 탐문은 적어도 이 투기를 계속 밀고 나가기로 결심하게 할 만큼은 성공을 거두었다. 하딩 씨에 관해서도 그는 제 몸에 해가 되지 않는 한도에서 할 수 있는 일은 하기로 마음먹었다. 프라우디 부인에게는 이 문제를 말하지 않기로, 적어도 당분간은 말하지 않기로 작정했다. 그의 목적은 주교 편에서 작은 반란을 부추기는 데 있었다. 그는 그런 형세가 바람직하다고 보았는데, 하딩 씨와 퀴버풀 씨 문제에 관해서만이 아니라 교구의 사무 전반에 관해서도 그러했다. 슬로프 씨는 프라우디 박사가 다스릴 만한 인물이라고는 결코 생각하지 않았으나, 동료 성직자들이 치마폭 정치에 매여 지내는 것은 양심에 비추어 그르다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 주교에게 제 기백을 조금 불어넣되, 아내에게 맞설 만큼은 되고 아주 통제를 벗어날 만큼은 되지 않게 하기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n그래서 그는 기회를 보아 주교에게 요양원 이야기를 다시 꺼내어, 결국 하딩 씨를 이 자리에서 배제하는 것은 현명하지 못한 일이라는 쪽으로 몰아가려 애썼다. 그러나 슬로프 씨의 일은 그가 짐작했던 것보다 힘들었다. 프라우디 부인은 이 성직 수여의 공을 되도록 제 몫으로 돌리려는 마음에 퀴버풀 부인에게 편지를 보내 주교관으로 찾아오라 했고, 그러고는 그 부인에게 자못 은밀하고 겸손을 갖춘 위엄 어린 태도로 그녀와 그 자식들에게 어떤 복이 예비되어 있는지 설명해 주었다. 실은 프라우디 부인이 그 일을 하고 있던 바로 그때에 슬로프 씨는 푸딩데일 목사관에서 그 남편에게 같은 일을 하고 있었으니, 부인은 그렇게 해서 어느 정도 발을 담근 셈이었다. 퀴버풀 부인의 사례와 겸손과 감사와 놀라움은 실로 압도적이었다. 그녀는 은인의 무릎을 끌어안다시피 하며, 하느님이 보내 주신 후한 벗을 위해 앞길이 막막한 열넷 젖먹이들(퀴버풀 부인은 제 식구를 그렇게 말했는데, 그중 맏이는 스물세 살 난 튼실한 처녀였다)의 기도를 아침저녁으로 하늘에 올리겠노라 약속했다. 이만한 향불은 프라우디 부인에게 싫지 않았고, 부인은 그것을 한껏 누렸다. 그녀는 앞길이 막막한 그 열넷 젖먹이들이 — 물론 그러리라 믿어 의심치 않았지만 — 자격만 갖추었다면 두루 도와주겠노라 했고, 그중 맏이가 제 안식일 학교에서 가르치는 일을 맡을 만하기를 바란다고 말했으며, 요컨대 퀴버풀 부인이 보기에 스스로를 아주 대단한 귀부인으로 만들어 놓았다.\n\n이 일을 마치고 나자 그녀는 주교 앞에서 몇 마디 흘려 두는 편이 신중하겠다고 생각하여, 푸딩데일 식구들에게 그 경사를 알려 주었다는 사실을 남편이 알게 했다. 그리하여 그가 스스로 이 임명에 발이 묶였음을 깨닫게 하려는 것이었다. 남편은 아내의 술책을 훤히 알아보았으나 분해하지는 않았다. 그는 아내가 성직 수여권을 제 손에서 빼앗아 가고 있음을 알았고, 그 간섭을 끝장내고 제 권한을 되찾겠다고 결심하고 있었다. 그러나 그러기에는 지금이 가장 좋은 때가 아니라고 생각했다. 그는 비슷한 처지에 놓였던 숱한 남자들이 앞서 그랬듯이 그 고약한 시간을 뒤로 미루었다.\n\n사정이 이러했으므로 슬로프 씨는 주교를 구슬려 넘기는 데 자연히 어려움을 만났으니, 실로 주교관 전체가 한바탕 뒤집히는 값을 치르지 않고서는 넘어설 수 없는 어려움이었다. 지금 이 시점에서 주교관을 한바탕 뒤집는 것이 좋은 방책일 수도 있었으나 아닐 수도 있었다. 어느 쪽이든 가볍게 내디딜 걸음은 아니었다. 그는 하딩 씨가 요양원에 다시 나타나지 않으면 여론이 주교님께 불리하게 돌아설까 두렵다고 귀엣말을 하는 것으로 말문을 열었다. 주교는 다소 열을 내어, 퀴버풀 씨에게 그 자리를 약속한 것은 슬로프 씨가 권해서였다고 대답했다. “약속까지 하신 것은 아니겠지요?” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “아니오, 약속했소.” 주교가 대꾸했다. “게다가 프라우디 부인이 그 일로 퀴버풀 부인을 만났소.” 이는 슬로프 씨로서는 전혀 뜻밖이었으나, 그의 침착함은 무너지지 않았고 그는 그 말을 제 잇속에 맞게 돌려놓았다.\n\n“아, 주교님.” 그가 말했다. “부인들께서 끼어드시면 우리 모두 곤경에 빠지고 맙니다.”\n\n이 말은 주교님의 심정과 너무나 잘 맞아떨어져 아주 못마땅하다고만은 할 수 없었으나, 그렇게 간섭을 들먹이는 것은 마땅히 나무랄 일이었다. 또한 주교님은 아내와 전속 사제 사이에 의견이 갈리는 지점이 있음을 알고 다소 놀랐는데, 그렇다고 아주 언짢은 것만은 아니었다.\n\n“간섭이라니 무슨 말인지 모르겠소.” 주교가 부드럽게 말했다. “퀴버풀 씨가 임명되리라는 말을 듣고 프라우디 부인이 학교 일로 퀴버풀 부인을 만나 보고 싶어 한 것이야 이상할 게 없지 않소. 나로서는 도무지 간섭이라 할 것이 눈에 띄지 않는구려.”\n\n“저는 오직 주교님의 평안을 위해 말씀드릴 뿐입니다.” 슬로프가 말했다. “교구 안에서 주교님의 평안과 위엄을 위해서지요. 다른 뜻은 있을 수 없습니다. 개인적인 정으로 말하자면 프라우디 부인께서는 제게 더없이 좋은 벗이십니다. 그 점은 늘 잊지 말아야지요. 그러나 그렇다 해도 지금 제 자리에서 첫째가는 의무는 주교님께 있습니다.”\n\n“그 점은 나도 아오, 슬로프 씨. 나도 잘 알고 있소.” 주교가 누그러져 말했다. “그래, 정말로 하딩 씨가 요양원을 맡아야 한다고 생각하오?”\n\n“솔직히 말씀드려 저는 그런 쪽으로 기울고 있습니다. 퀴버풀 씨의 이름을 처음 꺼낸 허물은 얼마든지 제가 지겠습니다. 그러나 그 뒤로 살펴보니 교구 안에 하딩 씨를 지지하는 정서가 어찌나 강한지, 주교님께서 양보하시는 편이 좋겠다고 생각합니다. 또 하딩 씨가 처음에 주교님의 제안에 대해 품었던 반대를 누그러뜨렸다는 말도 들었습니다. 프라우디 부인과 퀴버풀 부인 사이에 오간 일로 말하자면, 사정이 다소 거북하기는 하겠으나 이만큼 중대한 문제에서 그것이 무게를 지녀서는 안 된다고 저는 정말로 생각합니다.”\n\n그리하여 가엾은 주교는 어떻게 해야 좋을지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못한 채로 남겨졌다. 그러나 그의 마음은 하딩 씨를 임명하는 쪽으로 조금 기울었으니, 그렇게 하면 프라우디 부인에게 맞서는 데 슬로프 씨의 도움을 얻으리라는 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 볼드 부인의 집을 찾아가 그녀가 아기와 놀고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 주교관의 형편은 그러했다. 그녀가 방에서 뛰어나가자 슬로프 씨는 메리 볼드에게 날씨를 칭찬하기 시작했고, 다음에는 아기를 칭찬하며 입을 맞추었고, 그다음에는 어머니를 칭찬했고, 그다음에는 볼드 양 본인을 칭찬했다. 그러나 볼드 부인은 오래지 않아 돌아왔다.\n\n“이렇게 이른 시각에 찾아온 것을 사과드려야겠어요.” 슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다. “꼭 뵙고 말씀드리고 싶은 일이 있어 마음이 급했던 것이니, 부인과 볼드 양께서 부디 너그러이 보아주셨으면 해요.”\n\n엘리너는 “그럼요”, “물론이지요”, “조금도 이르지 않아요” 하는 말이 겨우 알아들릴 만큼 무어라 웅얼거렸고, 그러고는 제 매무새에 대해 사과하며 웃는 얼굴로 아기가 어느새 이렇게 큰 사내가 되어 도무지 감당이 안 된다고 말했다.\n\n“이 녀석은 다 큰 개구쟁이랍니다.” 그녀가 아이에게 말했다. “그러니 크고 거칠고 시끄러운 학교로 멀리 보내 버려야겠어요. 거기에는 커다란 회초리가 있어서, 제 엄마 말을 안 듣는 못된 아이들한테 무시무시한 일을 한다지요.” 그러고는 다시 한바탕 입맞춤을 퍼붓기 시작했으니, 제 아이를 멀리 보낸다는 무서운 생각을 스스로의 상상이 그려 보인 탓이었다.\n\n“게다가 그곳 선생들은 헝클어뜨릴 만한 그리 아름다운 긴 머리도 없지요.” 슬로프 씨가 농담을 받으면서 동시에 찬사를 보냈다.\n\n엘리너는 그런 찬사는 그만두는 편이 나았으리라고 생각했으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았고 아무런 내색도 하지 않았으니, 마침 아기에게 매여 있던 참이었다.\n\n“내가 안을게요.” 메리가 말했다. “이렇게 뒹굴었으니 옷이 다 벗겨지겠어요.” 그러면서 그녀는 아이를 안고 방을 나갔다. 볼드 양은 슬로프 씨가 엘리너에게 긴히 할 말이 있다고 하는 것을 들었던 터라, 제가 있으면 거추장스럽겠다고 생각하고 이 기회에 방에서 빠져나간 것이었다.\n\n“오래 걸리지 말아요, 메리.” 볼드 양이 문을 닫는데 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“볼드 부인, 이렇게 단둘이 10분쯤 이야기 나눌 기회를 얻어 기쁩니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다. “솔직하게 한 가지만 여쭈어도 될까요?”\n\n“그러세요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 부인께서 솔직하고 숨김없는 대답을 주시리라 믿어요.”\n\n“그렇게 하든가, 아예 대답을 안 하든가겠지요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“제 물음은 이것입니다, 볼드 부인. 아버님께서는 정말로 요양원에 돌아가기를 간절히 바라고 계신가요?”\n\n“어째서 제게 물으시죠?” 그녀가 말했다. “어째서 아버지께 직접 여쭙지 않으시나요?”\n\n“볼드 부인, 그 까닭을 말씀드리지요. 바퀴 안에 또 바퀴가 있는 격이라 하나하나 다 설명해 드리고 싶지만, 시간이 없을까 걱정입니다. 저로서는 이 물음에 대한 답을 꼭 얻어야 해요. 그러지 않고서는 아버님의 뜻을 어떻게 밀어 드려야 할지 알 수 없으니까요. 그런데 아버님께 직접 여쭙는 것은 도무지 될 일이 아닙니다. 저만큼 아버님을 우러르는 사람도 없습니다만, 그 마음이 서로 오가는 것인지는 의심스럽군요.” 과연 그렇지 않았다. “하딩 씨께 몹시 해로울 결말을 피하자면 부인께 숨김없이 털어놓는 수밖에 없어요. 바체스터에는 저를 두고 어떤 감정이 — 편견이라고까지는 하지 않겠습니다만 — 있어서 제게 이롭지 않은 듯합니다. 그 설교를 기억하시겠지요…”\n\n“어머, 슬로프 씨, 그 이야기까지 되짚을 것은 없어요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“잠깐만이면 됩니다, 볼드 부인. 제 이야기를 하려는 것이 아니라, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 부인께서 아셔야 하기에 드리는 말씀이에요. 그 설교는 분별없는 짓이었을지도 모릅니다. 오해를 산 것만은 분명하지요. 그러나 그 일에 대해서는 지금 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요. 다만 이것만은 말씀드리지요. 그 일로 저를 향한 감정이 생겨났고, 아버님께서도 다른 이들과 더불어 그 감정을 지니고 계시다는 것입니다. 그럴 만한 까닭이 아버님께 있을지도 모르지요. 그러나 결과적으로 아버님은 저와 우호적으로 마주 앉을 마음이 없으십니다. 부인께서는 사정이 그렇다는 것을 모르고 계시느냐고, 저는 부인 자신께 여쭙고 싶군요.”\n\n엘리너는 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 슬로프 씨는 말에 열이 오른 나머지 제 의자를 미망인의 자리 쪽으로 조금 끌어당겼는데, 그녀는 알아채지 못했다.\n\n“사정이 그러하니,” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “부인께 여쭙듯이 아버님께 이 물음을 여쭐 수는 없습니다. 제가 바체스터에 온 뒤로 저지른 잘못에도 불구하고 부인께서는 제가 부인을 벗으로 여기도록 허락해 주셨지요.” 엘리너는 고개를 조금 움직였는데 그것이 그 말을 인정하는 몸짓이라고 보기는 어려웠으나, 슬로프 씨는 설령 알아챘더라도 알아챈 기색을 보이지 않았다. “부인께는 숨김없이 말씀드릴 수 있고 제 마음속을 풀어 보일 수 있어요. 아버님께서는 그것을 허락하지 않으시겠지요. 공교롭게도 주교님께서는 이 요양원 문제가 제 손을 거쳐 가는 것이 옳다고 여기셨습니다. 손수 수고하고 싶어 하지 않으시는 세세한 일들이 있었던 까닭이지요. 그래서 저는 어쩔 수 없이 이 일로 아버님과 마주 앉아야 했던 것입니다.”\n\n“그건 저도 알고 있어요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“물론 그러시겠지요.” 그가 말했다. “그 자리에서 하딩 씨는 요양원에 돌아가고 싶어 하지 않으신다는 인상을 제게 남기셨습니다.”\n\n“어떻게 그럴 수가 있죠?” 엘리너가 말했다. 마침내 마음이 움직여, 지키기로 작정했던 차가운 예의범절을 잊어버린 것이었다.\n\n“볼드 부인, 사정이 그러했다는 것은 제가 맹세할 수 있어요.” 그가 말하며 다시 조금 그녀 가까이 다가앉았다. “뿐만 아니라 제가 하딩 씨를 뵙기 전에, 주교관의 어떤 분들이 — 주교님을 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다 — 그것이 사실이라고 제게 일러 주었지요. 솔직히 저는 그 말을 좀처럼 믿지 않았습니다. 솔직히 저는, 아버님께서 무슨 이유로 보나, 양심을 위해서나, 그 노인들을 위해서나, 오랜 인연과 지나간 좋은 시절의 기억을 위해서나, 어느 모로 보나 옛 직분을 다시 맡고 싶어 하시리라 생각했어요. 그러나 그런 뜻이 아니시라는 말을 들었고, 아버님께서도 제가 들은 말이 참이라는 인상을 남기고 저와 헤어지셨습니다.”\n\n“그래서요!” 엘리너가 말했다. 이제 이 일에 마음이 한껏 동한 것이었다.\n\n“볼드 양의 발소리가 들리는군요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “너무 큰 청을 드리는 것이 될까요, 부인께 부탁드려도… 볼드 양이라면 부인 뜻대로 얼마든지 다루실 수 있을 텐데요.”\n\n엘리너는 다룬다는 말이 마음에 들지 않았으나, 그래도 밖으로 나가 메리에게 15분만 더 자리를 비켜 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n“고맙습니다, 볼드 부인. 이렇게 믿어 주시니 참으로 감사해요. 자, 저는 그런 인상을 안고 아버님과 헤어졌습니다. 실은 아버님께서 그 자리를 사양하신다는 뜻으로 저를 알아듣게 하셨다고 말씀드려도 좋을 정도예요.”\n\n“그 자리를 사양하신 게 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “아버지가 그 자리를 사양하셨을 리 없어요. 다만 동의할 수 없다고 하신 거지요. 그러니까 학교니 예배니 하는 그 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다고 하신 거예요. 아버지가 그 자리를 마다하고 싶다고 말씀하셨을 리는 결코 없어요.”\n\n“아, 볼드 부인!” 슬로프 씨가 거의 격정에 찬 투로 말했다. “그토록 훌륭한 따님께 그토록 훌륭한 아버님을 두고 한마디라도 나쁘게 말할 생각은 결코 없어요. 그러나 아버님을 위해서라도, 지금 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지 제가 보여 드리도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다. 학교에 대한 주교님의 뜻을 전해 드렸을 때 하딩 씨는 조금 흥분하셨어요. 부인께서 같은 문제에 대해 저와 그토록 완전히 뜻이 맞으셨던 터라, 제가 아마 조심을 덜 했던 모양입니다. 아버님은 조금 언짢아하시며 열을 내어 말씀하셨지요. ‘주교께 전해 주시오.’ 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. ‘나는 그분과 생각이 아주 다르며, 그런 조건이 붙어 있는 한 요양원에 돌아가지 않겠다고.’ 그런 취지의 말씀이었고, 실은 그 말씀이 이보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지는 않았어요. 저로서는 그것을 주교님께 그대로 옮겨 드리는 수밖에 없었고, 주교님께서는 그것을 거절로밖에 볼 수 없다고 하셨습니다. 주교님께서도 아버님이 그 자리를 원하지 않으신다는 소문을 들으셨던 터라, 이 모든 것을 함께 놓고 보시고는 다른 사람을 찾는 수밖에 없다고 생각하셨어요. 그래서 퀴버풀 씨에게 그 자리를 제안하신 것입니다.”\n\n“퀴버풀 씨에게 자리를 제안하셨다니요!” 엘리너가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 되뇌었다. “그렇다면 슬로프 씨, 이 일은 끝난 거네요.”\n\n“아니에요, 나의 벗이여, 그렇지 않습니다.” 그가 말했다. “일이 그렇게 끝나지 않도록 하려고 제가 지금 여기 와 있는 것이지요. 어쨌든 제 물음에 대한 답은 얻은 것으로, 그러니까 하딩 씨께서 돌아가기를 바라고 계신 것으로 여겨도 되겠군요.”\n\n“돌아가기를 바라시느냐고요. 물론 바라시지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “물론 아버지는 집과 수입과 세상에서의 자리를 되찾고 싶어 하세요. 그토록 자신을 버리는 정직함으로 내놓았던 것을 되찾고 싶어 하시고말고요. 그 연세에 도저히 따를 수 없는 굴레가 처신에 씌워지지만 않는다면요. 주교님은 어떻게 그 연세의 어른더러 아이들 한 무리를 앉혀 놓고 학교 선생 노릇을 하라 하실 수 있나요?”\n\n“당치도 않은 일이지요.” 슬로프 씨가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다. “물론 아버님께 그런 요구를 하는 일은 없을 겁니다. 적어도 그런 터무니없는 요구를 전하는 심부름꾼 노릇은 제가 하지 않겠다고 약속드릴 수 있어요. 저희는 아버님께서 요양원 안에서 설교해 주시기를 바랐습니다. 그 안에 계신 분들이 나이가 많아 밖으로 나오기 어려울 테니까요. 그러나 그것조차 고집하지 않겠어요. 또 저희는 요양원에 안식일 학교를 붙이고 싶었지요. 하딩 씨처럼 훌륭한 성직자의 보살핌 아래, 그리고 부인의 보살핌 아래라면 그런 시설이 유익하지 않을 리 없다고 생각했으니까요. 하지만 볼드 부인, 그 이야기는 지금 하지 맙시다. 한 가지는 분명해요. 주교님이 퀴버풀 씨에게 성급하게 내놓으신 그 제안을 무르도록 우리가 할 수 있는 일을 해야 합니다. 아버님께서 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보시지는 않겠지요? 퀴버풀 씨는 명예를 아는 사람이니 잠시라도 아버님 앞을 가로막지는 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“뭐라고요?” 엘리너가 말했다. “아이가 열넷인 사람더러 제게 온 성직을 내놓으라고 하라고요! 그분이 그럴 리 없다고 저는 확신해요.”\n\n“그러시겠지요.” 슬로프가 말하고는 다시 볼드 부인 가까이 다가앉았으니, 이제 둘은 아주 바싹 붙어 있었다. 엘리너는 그것을 깊이 생각하지 않았으나 본능적으로 조금 물러앉았다. 플럼스테드에서 자기를 두고 무슨 말이 오갔는지 짐작이라도 할 수 있었더라면 그 사이를 얼마나 더 벌려 놓았을 것인가. “그러시겠지요. 그러나 퀴버풀이 아버님을 밀어내고 그 자리에 앉는 일은 있을 수 없습니다. 결코 있을 수 없지요. 주교님께서 너무 성급하셨어요. 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠오르는데, 하느님의 도우심이 있다면 그것으로 일이 바로잡힐지도 모르겠습니다. 볼드 부인, 부인께서 손수 주교님을 만나 보시는 것은 어떠신지요?”\n\n“어째서 아버지가 만나 뵈면 안 되나요?” 엘리너가 말했다. 그녀는 전에 한 번 아버지의 일에 끼어든 적이 있었고, 그때 그리 좋은 결과를 얻지 못했다. 이제 나이가 들었으므로, 아버지에게 그토록 중대한 일에서는 그분의 승낙 없이 한 걸음도 내디뎌서는 안 된다고 느꼈다.\n\n“글쎄요, 사실대로 말씀드리자면,” 슬로프 씨가 마치 제 후원자의 인색한 마음씨를 몹시 애통해하기라도 하는 듯 서글픈 낯빛으로 말했다. “주교님께서는 아버님께 노여워할 까닭이 있다고 여기고 계세요. 만나 뵈면 도리어 감정이 더 나빠질까 걱정입니다.”\n\n“어머,” 엘리너가 말했다. “제 아버지는 세상에서 가장 순하고 가장 온화한 분인걸요.”\n\n“제가 아는 것은,” 슬로프가 말했다. “그분께 더없이 훌륭한 따님이 있다는 것뿐이에요. 그러면 주교님을 만나 보지 않으시겠다는 말씀이지요? 자리를 마련하는 일이라면 부인께 조금도 번거로움이 가지 않도록 제가 처리해 드릴 수 있습니다.”\n\n“슬로프 씨, 저는 아버지와 의논하지 않고는 아무것도 할 수 없어요.”\n\n“아!” 그가 말했다. “그러면 소용이 없지요. 그렇게 되면 부인은 아버님의 심부름꾼밖에 되지 못하니까요. 부인께 떠오르는 생각은 없으신가요? 무언가는 해야 합니다. 이토록 어처구니없는 오해로 아버님이 무너지시게 둘 수는 없어요.”\n\n엘리너는 떠오르는 것이 없다고, 다만 너무 딱한 일이라고 말했다. 눈에 눈물이 고여 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 슬로프 씨는 그 눈물을 닦아 줄 특권을 얻을 수만 있다면 많은 것을 내주었으련만, 볼드 부인에게 어떤 특권이든 바라 볼 수 있게 되기까지는 아직 할 일이 많다는 것쯤은 알아차릴 눈치가 있었다.\n\n“이렇게 슬퍼하시는 것을 보니 가슴이 저밉니다.” 그가 말했다. “그러나 제가 지킬 수만 있다면 아버님의 이익이 희생되는 일은 없으리라고 부디 믿어 주세요. 저는 주교님께 사실이 어떠한지 숨김없이 말씀드리겠습니다. 아버님 말고 다른 사람을 임명할 권리가 주교님께는 거의 없다는 것을 설명해 드리고, 그렇게 하신다면 큰 불의를 저지르는 것이 되리라는 점을 보여 드리겠어요. 그리고 볼드 부인, 부인께서도 적어도 저를 이만큼은 너그럽게 믿어 주시겠지요. 제가 아버님의 안녕을 진심으로 바라고 있다는 것을요. 아버님을 위해서, 그리고 부인 자신을 위해서 말입니다.”\n\n미망인은 무어라 대답해야 좋을지 알 수 없었다. 아버지가 슬로프 씨에게 조금도 고마워하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었고, 아버지의 마음을 함께하고 싶은 마음도 간절했다. 그러면서도 슬로프 씨가 참으로 친절하다는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 대체로 모든 사람에게 너그럽고 누구를 나쁘게 말하는 법이 거의 없는 아버지가 슬로프 씨를 조심하라고 일러 주었건만, 그녀는 그에게 고맙다는 말을 하지 않고 넘어갈 방법을 알지 못했다. 그가 이 일에서 스스로 밝힌 것 말고 무슨 다른 속셈을 가질 수 있단 말인가. 그런데도 그의 태도에는 그녀조차 미덥지 않게 여기는 무엇이 있었다. 왜인지는 몰라도 그에게는 마땅히 경계하게 만드는 무엇이 있다고 그녀는 느꼈다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 그녀가 머뭇거리는 태도에서 이 모든 것을, 마치 그녀가 제 마음을 활짝 열어 보이기라도 한 것처럼 훤히 읽어 냈다. 마주 앉아 이야기하는 여자의 속마음을 그렇게 읽어 내는 것이 이 사내의 재주였다. 그는 엘리너가 자기를 의심하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 고맙다는 말을 한다 해도 그것은 하지 않을 수 없어서 하는 것일 뿐임을 알았다. 그러나 그렇다고 화가 나거나 언짢아지지는 않았다. 로마는 하루아침에 세워지지 않았으니까.\n\n“저는 고맙다는 말을 들으러 온 것이 아니에요.” 그녀가 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었다. “적어도 그럴 만한 일을 하기 전에는 그런 말을 바라지도 않습니다. 다만 이것만은 바랍니다, 볼드 부인. 하느님께서 저를 가장 보잘것없는 목자의 하나로 불러 세우신 이 우리 안에서, 제가 스스로 벗을 얻을 수 있기를 바랍니다. 그러지 못한다면 이곳에서 제 소임은 참으로 서글픈 것이 되겠지요. 어쨌든 저는 그런 벗을 얻을 만한 사람이 되도록 애쓰겠습니다.”\n\n“그럼요,” 그녀가 말했다. “머지않아 벗을 많이 얻으시겠지요.” 무슨 말이든 해야 할 것 같았던 것이다.\n\n“제 마음에 공감해 줄 이들이 아니라면 그런 것은 아무것도 아니에요. 제가 우러르고 감탄할 수 있는, 그리고 사랑할 수 있는 이들이 아니라면 말이지요. 가장 훌륭하고 가장 순결한 이들이 제게서 등을 돌린다면, 그만 못한 이들의 우정으로 스스로를 만족시킬 수는 없습니다. 그런 경우라면 저는 홀로 살아야겠지요.”\n\n“어머, 설마 그러시겠어요, 슬로프 씨.” 엘리너는 아무 뜻 없이 한 말이었으나, 그로서는 무슨 각별한 뜻이 담긴 말로 여기는 척하는 편이 좋았다.\n\n“정말입니다, 볼드 부인. 제가 한마음으로 맺어지기를 갈망하는 이들이 제게서 등을 돌린다면, 저는 홀로 살게 될 거예요. 마음으로 말하자면 아주 홀로 말이지요. 그러나 이 이야기는 이만하겠습니다. 저는 부인을 벗이라 불렀으니, 부인께서 아니라고 하지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 언젠가는 아버님도 그렇게 부를 수 있는 날이 오기를 바라고요. 하느님의 복이 부인께, 그리고 부인의 사랑스러운 아드님께 함께하기를 빕니다. 그리고 아버님께 제 말이라고 전해 주세요. 아버님의 이익을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 하겠노라고요.”\n\n그러고서 그는 미망인의 손을 여느 때보다 좀 더 꼭 쥐고는 물러갔다. 그러나 그때의 사정이 그 행동을 그럴듯하게 만들어 주는 듯하여, 부인은 그것을 언짢게 여겨야 한다고는 느끼지 않았다.\n\n“저 사람을 알 수가 없어요.” 몇 분 뒤에 엘리너가 메리 볼드에게 말했다. “좋은 사람인지 나쁜 사람인지, 참된 사람인지 거짓된 사람인지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“그럼 의심스러운 쪽은 그이에게 유리하게 봐 주고,” 메리가 말했다. “가장 좋은 쪽으로 믿어요.”\n\n“대체로 그러고 있는 것 같아요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “저 사람이 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿고 있는 것 같아요. 그렇다면 우리와 함께 지내는 동안 저 사람을 헐뜯고 비참하게 만드는 것은 부끄러운 일이겠지요. 하지만 아, 메리, 아버지가 요양원 일로 실망하시게 될까 걱정이에요.”\n\n# 제17장. 누가 골목대장이 될 것인가\n\n그동안 주교관에서는 일이 어딘가 편치 않게 돌아가고 있었다. 슬로프 씨가 던져 놓은 한두 마디 암시는 결코 주교에게 헛되이 떨어지지 않았다. 이제 거의 견딜 수 없는 지경에 이른 아내의 전횡에 언젠가 맞설 생각이라면 더는 시간을 끌어서는 안 된다는 느낌이, 제 집안은 그만두고라도 제 교구에서만이라도 스스로 주인 노릇을 할 생각이라면 지금 당장 시작해야 한다는 느낌이 그에게 들었다. 주교로 축성을 받던 그날부터 그리했더라면 지금보다 쉬웠겠지만, 그래도 프라우디 부인이 교구의 세세한 사무까지 남김없이 장악하고 난 뒤보다는 지금이 쉬웠다. 그런 마당에 슬로프 씨가 자청한 도움은 그에게 대단한 것, 참으로 뜻밖이고 값을 매길 수 없는 원군이었다. 지금까지 그는 그 둘을 한편으로 묶인 세력으로 보았고, 그렇게 손을 잡은 이상 도저히 뚫을 수 없다고 여겨 왔다. 그로서 벗어날 길이라고는 슬로프 씨를 어디 멀고 넉넉한 자리로 영전시키는 것뿐이리라고 믿기 시작하던 참이었다. 그런데 이제 보니 그 적들 가운데 하나가, 분명 그중 힘이 가장 약한 쪽이기는 하나 그래도 매우 중요한 하나가, 제 진영을 버릴 뜻을 품고 있는 것이었다. 슬로프 씨의 도움을 받는다면 무엇인들 못 하랴? 그는 좁은 서재를 이리저리 거닐면서, 전임자가 늘 앉아 있던 위층의 그 큰 방을 제 것으로 삼을 날이 올지도 모른다는 생각마저 해 보았다.\n\n이런 일들을 마음속에서 굴리고 있는데 그랜틀리 부주교에게서 쪽지가 하나 전해졌다. 그 성직자는 그 글에서 내일 뵙는 영광을 허락해 주십사고 주교께 청하면서, 황송하오나 시각을 정해 주시겠느냐고 물었다. 그랜틀리 박사가 청한 방문은 하딩 씨를 바체스터 요양원의 워든 자리에 다시 임명하는 일에 관한 것이었다. 주교가 쪽지를 읽고 나자, 부주교의 하인이 답을 기다리고 있다는 전갈이 들어왔다.\n\n여기서 곧바로 주교에게는 제 책임으로 일을 처리해 볼 절호의 기회가 굴러들어 왔다. 그러나 그는 새 동맹자를 떠올리고 슬로프 씨를 부르려 종을 울렸다. 알고 보니 슬로프 씨는 집에 없었고, 그리하여 주교는 크게 마음먹고 누구의 도움도 없이 제 정신 하나로 부주교에게 만나겠노라는 답장을 써서 시각까지 정해 주었다. 서재 창으로 심부름꾼이 그 답장을 들고 무사히 집을 빠져나가는 것을 지켜본 다음, 그는 이제 다음으로 어떤 수를 두어야 할지 머릿속에서 이리저리 헤아리기 시작했다.\n\n내일이면 그는 부주교에게 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 맡게 되리라고 말하든지 맡지 못하리라고 말하든지, 둘 중 하나를 밝혀야 했다. 주교는 프라우디 부인에게 알리지도 않고 퀴버풀 내외를 떳떳하게 내칠 수는 없다고 느꼈고, 마침내 굴속의 암사자에게 맞서기로, 그리하여 사정이 이러이러하니 하딩 씨를 다시 임명하는 것이 마땅하다고 아내에게 말하기로 결심했다. 그러면서, 제 손에 들어오는 성직 자리 가운데 맨 처음 비는 것을 퀴버풀에게 주어 그가 입은 손해를 갚아 주겠노라고 프라우디 부인에게 약속한다 한들 새로 낸 용기가 조금이라도 깎이는 것은 아니라고 여겼다. 그런 미끼 한 조각으로 암사자를 달랠 수만 있다면, 첫 시도치고 얼마나 훌륭했다고 여기겠는가!\n\n그는 숱한 불안을 안은 채 프라우디 부인의 내실에 들어섰다. 처음에는 부인을 제 방으로 부를까도 생각했다. 그러나 부인이 그런 전갈을 언짢게 받아들이기로 마음먹을 가능성이 얼마든지 있었고, 또 딸들이 그 자리에 함께 있으면 그에게 얼마간 방패가 되어 줄지도 몰랐다. 가 보니 부인은 회계장부를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 연필 끝을 잘근거리며 앉아 있었는데, 보아하니 돈 문제에 깊이 빠져 주교관 살림의 온갖 비용과 주교의 위신을 지키는 데 드는 무거운 지출로 심사가 편치 않은 참이었다. 딸들이 그 둘레에 있었다. 올리비아는 소설을 읽고, 어거스타는 베이커 스트리트에 사는 단짝에게 보낼 편지를 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓰고 있었으며, 네타는 페티코트 자락에 달 자그마한 마차 바퀴 무늬를 뜨고 있었다. 지금 이 심기의 아내를 주교가 이겨 낼 수 있다면 그야말로 사내대장부라 할 만했다. 그때는 그 승리를 영원히 제 것으로 쳐도 좋을 터였다. 따지고 보면 이런 경우 부부 사이의 형편은 한 학교에 다니는 두 소년, 한 마당에 있는 두 마리 수탉, 한 대륙에 있는 두 군대 사이의 형편과 크게 다르지 않다. 한 번 이긴 자가 대개 그 뒤로도 내내 이기는 자가 된다. 승리의 위세가 전부인 것이다.\n\n“에헴, 여보,” 주교가 입을 열었다. “한가하거든 잠깐 얘기 좀 했으면 하오.” 프라우디 부인은 그때까지 더해 온 숫자의 자리에 조심스레 연필을 내려놓고, 지금까지 나온 합계를 머릿속에 새겨 둔 다음, 자못 떫은 얼굴로 남편의 낯을 올려다보았다. “바쁘거든 다음에 해도 좋소.” 주교가 말을 이었다. 밥 에이커스가 그러했듯이, 막상 싸움터에 발을 들여놓고 보니 그의 용기는 벌써 스르르 새어 나가 버렸던 것이다.\n\n“무슨 일인데요, 주교?” 부인이 물었다.\n\n“음, 저 퀴버풀 내외 일인데 말이오. 하지만 보아하니 바쁜 모양이니, 나야 다음에 해도 아무 상관 없소.”\n\n“퀴버풀 내외가 어쨌다는 거예요? 그 사람들이 요양원으로 들어가기로 한 건 이미 다 정해진 일 아닌가요. 거기에 무슨 의심할 여지가 있는 건 아니겠지요?” 그렇게 말하면서도 부인은 앞에 놓인 숫자 줄에 연필을 매섭고 억세게 고정한 채였다.\n\n“그게, 여보, 좀 곤란한 사정이 생겼소.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“곤란한 사정이라니요!” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “뭐가 곤란하다는 거예요? 그 자리는 퀴버풀 씨에게 주기로 약속한 것이고, 당연히 그 사람이 받아야지요. 그 사람은 벌써 만반의 채비를 다 해 놓았어요. 푸딩데일에 들일 부목사를 구하려고 편지를 냈고, 농장이며 말이며 소를 팔려고 경매인과 이야기를 마쳤고, 무엇으로 보나 그 자리를 제 것으로 알고 있단 말입니다. 당연히 그 사람이 받아야 해요.”\n\n자, 주교여, 그대 자신을 잘 살피고 그대 안에 있는 사내다움을 모조리 불러내라. 걸린 것이 얼마나 큰지 헤아려 보라. 이제 와서 그대가 제 대포를 지키지 못한다면, 앞으로 어떤 슬로프도 그대를 돕지 못하리라. 화약 냄새를 맡자마자 제 깃발을 버리는 자가 어찌 어떤 동맹에게서든 신의를 바라겠는가? 싸움터를 찾은 것은 그대 자신이니, 이미 그곳에 섰으면 사내답게 싸워 끝을 보라. 용기를 내라, 주교여, 용기를! 찡그린 낯이 사람을 죽이지 못하고, 날카로운 말이 뼈를 부러뜨리지도 못한다. 어차피 주교의 앞치마는 그대의 것이 아닌가. 그대가 그대 자신에게만 충실하다면, 저 여자는 워든을 하나도 임명하지 못하고 성직록을 하나도 내리지 못하며 전속 사제를 하나도 지명하지 못한다. 일어서라, 사내여, 한결같은 마음으로 저 여자에게 맞서 나아가라.\n\n주교의 가슴속 어느 작은 훈계자가 그에게 그렇게 말을 걸었다. 그러나 그곳에는 또 하나의 훈계자가 있어 이와 달리 이렇게 일렀다. 기억하라, 주교여, 저 사람은 여자요, 그것도 그대가 익히 아는 그런 여자다. 그런 여자와 말싸움을 벌이는 것은 그야말로 재앙이다. 굳이 이 전쟁을 치러야 한다면, 그대의 서재에서 그대의 탁자 뒤에 앉아 치르는 편이 낫지 않겠는가? 수탉은 누구나 제 두엄 위에서 가장 잘 싸우는 법이 아닌가? 게다가 이곳에는 그대의 딸들이, 그대의 사랑의 담보요 그대의 허리에서 난 열매들이 있다. 저희 어미를 그대가 이기는 순간을 저 아이들이 보는 것이 과연 좋은 일이겠는가? 아니, 그대가 질지도 모를 그 순간을 저 아이들이 보는 것은 또 좋은 일이겠는가? 게다가 그대는 때를 고르는 솜씨가 놀랍도록 서툴지 않았는가, 그대가 그토록 이름을 떨친 그 지혜를 한 자락도 쓰지 않고서 말이다. 결국 이 일에서는 그대가 그르고 그대의 적이 옳다고 판명되지 않겠는가. 요양원 문제에서 실은 그대 스스로 약속을 해 놓고서, 이제 와 아내가 그 약속을 지키라 요구할 뿐인데 그 아내에게 등을 돌리려는 것이 아니겠는가? 그대는 그리스도의 주교가 아닌가. 결과가 어떠하든 그대의 말은 거룩히 지켜야 하는 것이 아닌가? 돌아가라, 주교여, 아래층 그대의 성소로 돌아가, 적어도 승산이 이토록 끔찍하게 기울지는 않은 어느 때로 그 호전적인 기질을 미루어 두라.\n\n이 모든 것이 주교의 가슴속을 지나가는 동안 프라우디 부인은 여전히 연필을 고정한 채 앉아 있었고, 계산해 낸 숫자는 여전히 그 기억의 서판 위에 남아 있었다. “4파운드 17실링 7펜스.” 부인은 속으로 되뇌었다. “물론 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원을 맡아야지요.” 이번에는 제 주인을 향해 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 여보, 나는 그저 이런 말을 해 두고 싶었을 뿐이오. 슬로프 씨 생각으로는, 하딩 씨가 임명되지 않으면 이 일에 대한 세간의 감정이 우리에게 불리하게 돌아설 것이고, 어쩌면 신문이 이 일을 들고 나올지도 모른다는 것이오.”\n\n“슬로프 씨 생각으로는이라니요!” 프라우디 부인이 말했는데, 그 목소리의 어조만으로도 주교는 바로 그 방면에서 틈이 벌어지기를 기대한 것이 옳았음을 똑똑히 알 수 있었다. “대체 슬로프 씨가 이 일과 무슨 상관이랍니까? 주교님, 설마 전속 사제 하나에게 좌지우지되실 생각은 아니시겠지요.” 그러고는 흥분한 나머지 부인은 계산하던 자리를 놓치고 말았다.\n\n“그럴 리가 있소, 여보. 그보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 없다고 내 장담하리다. 하지만 그래도 바람이 어느 쪽에서 부는지 알아보는 데는 슬로프 씨가 쓸모가 있을 수 있고, 나는 정말이지 퀴버풀 내외에게 그만큼 좋은 다른 자리를 줄 수만 있다면——”\n\n“말도 안 됩니다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 사람들에게 그 절반이라도 걸맞은 다른 자리를 주려면 몇 해가 걸릴 거예요. 그리고 신문이니 세간이니 하는 것들로 말하자면, 이야기를 전하는 데는 두 가지 방식이 있다는 걸 잊지 마세요. 하딩 씨가 어리석게 제 사정을 떠벌린다면 우리도 우리 사정을 떠벌리면 그만입니다. 그 자리를 그에게 주겠다고 했는데 그가 거절했다, 그래서 이제 다른 사람에게 주었다, 그것으로 끝인 겁니다. 적어도 나는 그렇게 생각해요.”\n\n“글쎄, 여보, 아무래도 당신 말이 옳은 듯하오.” 주교는 그렇게 말하고 슬그머니 방을 빠져나와 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 내일 부주교를 어떻게 맞아야 할지 마음이 심란했다. 그는 지금 당장 몸이 그리 좋지 않다고 느꼈고, 이튿날 아침에는 담즙이 끓어올라 방에서 나오지 못하게 될 공산도 없지 않겠다는 생각을 하기 시작했다. 그는 불행히도 담즙 탈이 매우 잦은 사람이었다.\n\n“슬로프 씨가, 참! 그 슬로프는 내가 손봐 주지.” 분개한 안주인이 귀 기울이고 있는 제 소생들에게 말했다. “슬로프 씨가 대체 왜 저러는지 모르겠구나. 내가 손을 잡아 이끌어 주고 너희 아버지를 시켜 집안 전속 사제로 앉혀 주었더니, 제가 바체스터 주교라도 되는 줄 아는 모양이다.”\n\n“그 사람은 언제나 뻔뻔스러움이 넘쳤어요.” 올리비아가 말했다. “제가 전에도 한 번 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요, 어머니.” 그러나 올리비아는 언젠가 그가 자기를 슬로프 부인으로 만들겠노라 청했을 때에는 그를 그리 뻔뻔하다고 여기지 않았었다.\n\n“어머, 올리비아 언니, 나는 늘 언니가 그 사람을 좋아하는 줄 알았는데.” 그 순간 언니에게 무언가 앙심이 있던 어거스타가 말했다. “나는 그 사람이 늘 싫었어. 아주 천박한 사람이라고 생각하니까.”\n\n“거기서는 네가 틀렸다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 사람은 조금도 천박하지 않아. 게다가 영혼을 뒤흔드는 웅변가 설교자이기도 하고. 다만 이 집에 계속 머무르려거든 제 분수를 알도록 가르쳐 놓아야겠다.”\n\n“그 사람은 내가 남자 얼굴에서 본 눈 가운데 제일 끔찍한 눈을 가졌어요.” 네타가 말했다. “그리고 말이죠, 그 사람 먹성이 아주 지독해요. 어제 커런트 파이를 얼마나 먹어 치우던지 보셨어요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 집에 돌아오자마자, 주교의 말보다도 오히려 그 태도에서 요양원 일에 관해서는 프라우디 부인의 분부를 따르기로 되었음을 곧 알아차렸다. 프라우디 박사는 “이번만은”이라느니 “성직 임명권에 관한 일은 전부 오로지 제 손안에 두겠노라”느니 하는 말을 슬쩍 흘렸다. 그러나 하딩 씨 문제에서는 아주 단호했고, 슬로프 씨로서는 주교와 여주교를 한꺼번에 적으로 돌리고 싶지 않았으므로 지금으로서는 물러서는 것 말고 달리 할 수 있는 일이 있을 것 같지 않았다.\n\n그는 그저 물론 주교님의 뜻대로 처리하겠노라고, 그리고 주교님께서 주교님 자신의 판단을 믿고 따르신다면 이 교구의 일은 반드시 잘 다스려질 것이라 확신한다고만 말해 두었다. 슬로프 씨는 못이란 머리를 여러 번 두들기다 보면 결국은 박히고 만다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n바로 그날 저녁 그가 제 방에 혼자 앉아 있는데 문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 나더니, 그가 대답할 겨를도 없이 문이 열리고 그의 후원자가 나타났다. 그는 순식간에 온 얼굴이 웃음이 되었으나 부인은 그렇지 않았다. 그래도 부인은 권하는 의자에 앉았고, 그러고는 이렇게 항의를 시작했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨, 나는 요전 날 밤 자네가 그 이탈리아 여자에게 보인 처신을 조금도 좋게 보지 않네. 누가 봤어도 자네가 그 여자의 연인인 줄 알았을 걸세.”\n\n“저런, 부인, 그게 무슨 말씀이십니까.” 슬로프 씨가 소스라치는 낯빛으로 말했다. “아니, 그분은 남편이 있는 부인이 아닙니까.”\n\n“그거야 내가 알 바 아니고,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “어쨌든 본인은 그렇게 행세하기로 한 모양이더군. 하지만 남편이 있든 없든, 자네가 그 여자에게 쏟은 그런 관심은 온당치 못했네. 자네가 내 응접실에서 남의 눈에 거슬리는 짓을 하고 싶어 하리라고는 믿지 않네만, 슬로프 씨, 나 자신과 내 딸들을 위해서라도 자네 처신이 마땅치 않다고 일러 두어야겠어.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 크게 튀어나온 두 눈을 한껏 부릅뜨고, 감쪽같이 꾸며 낸 놀란 얼굴로 부인을 빤히 바라보았다. “아니, 프라우디 부인,” 그가 말했다. “저는 그분이 시장하다고 하시기에 먹을 것을 좀 가져다 드린 것뿐입니다.”\n\n“그리고 그 뒤에 그 여자를 찾아갔더군.” 부인이 말을 이으며, 이제야 제 정체를 밝히는 형사의 매서운 눈초리로 죄인을 바라보았다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 이 드센 여자에게 나는 내가 좋은 사람을 찾아가고 내가 좋은 대로 하겠다고 당장 말해 버리는 편이 나을지 마음속으로 저울질해 보았으나, 바체스터에서 제 발판이 아직 그만큼 단단하지 못하다는 것, 그러니 지금은 부인을 달래 두는 편이 낫다는 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n“그 뒤에 스탠호프 박사 댁에 들른 것은 사실이고, 네로니 부인을 뵌 것도 사실입니다.”\n\n“그렇지, 게다가 그 여자를 단둘이 만났지.” 주교관의 아르고스가 말했다.\n\n“그야 물론 그랬습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “하지만 그건 마침 방에 다른 사람이 아무도 없었기 때문입니다. 식구들이 다 나가고 없는 것이 설마 제 잘못은 아니겠지요.”\n\n“그야 아닐 테지. 하지만 슬로프 씨, 내 분명히 말해 두네만, 자네가 그 여자의 미끼에 걸려든 것을 내가 알게 되는 날에는 자네에 대한 내 평가는 크게 떨어질 걸세. 여자를 아는 데는 내가 자네보다 낫네, 슬로프 씨. 스스로 시뇨라라 부르는 그 여자는 엄격한 복음주의 신자인 미혼 젊은 성직자가 가까이할 만한 상대가 못 된다는 내 말을 믿어도 좋아.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면 부인의 면전에서 얼마나 웃어 주고 싶었으랴! 그러나 그는 감히 그러지 못했다. 그래서 그저 이렇게만 말했다. “프라우디 부인, 말씀하신 그 부인은 제게 아무것도 아니라고 장담드릴 수 있습니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 다행이네, 슬로프 씨. 그래도 이만한 주의는 주어야 하는 것이 내 도리라고 여겼네. 그리고 또 한 가지, 내가 말해 두어야겠다고 여기는 일이 있어. 주교님을 대하는 자네의 태도 말일세, 슬로프 씨.”\n\n“주교님을 대하는 제 태도라니요.” 그가 말했다. 이번에는 정말로 놀랐고, 부인이 무엇을 두고 하는 말인지 짐작조차 가지 않았다.\n\n“그래, 슬로프 씨, 주교님을 대하는 자네의 태도 말이야. 그건 내가 보고 싶은 모습과는 영 딴판이네.”\n\n“주교님께서 무슨 말씀이라도 하신 것입니까, 프라우디 부인?”\n\n“아니, 주교님은 아무 말씀도 하지 않으셨네. 아마 그 문제에 관한 말은 자네를 처음 주교님께 소개한 나에게서 나오는 편이 낫다고 생각하시는 게지. 사실은 이렇네, 슬로프 씨. 자네는 제 분수보다 조금 지나치게 나서는 구석이 있어.”\n\n슬로프 씨의 두 뺨에 성난 붉은 점이 돋아났고, 그는 간신히 제 자신을 다스렸다. 그러나 어쨌든 다스려 내어, 부인이 말을 이어 가는 동안 아주 잠자코 앉아 있었다.\n\n“자네 자리에 있는 젊은이들이 흔히 저지르는 잘못이니, 주교님께서도 지금으로서는 그것을 문제 삼을 뜻이 없으시네. 자네도 머지않아 무엇이 자네에게 요구되고 무엇이 요구되지 않는지 알게 되겠지. 그러나 내 충고를 받아들이겠거든, 성직 임명권에 관계된 일이라면 어떤 일에서든 주교님께 함부로 조언을 들이대지 않도록 조심하게. 주교님께서 조언이 필요하시면, 어디서 그것을 구해야 할지는 스스로 아시네.” 그러고서 부인은 엄격한 복음주의 신자인 미혼 젊은 성직자의 처신에서 무엇이 바람직하고 무엇이 바람직하지 않은가에 대한 상투적인 훈계를 한바탕 덧붙인 뒤 물러갔고, 전속 사제는 제 생각에 잠긴 채 남았다.\n\n그가 잠긴 생각의 결론은 이러했다. 이 교구에는 자신의 활력과 프라우디 부인의 활력이 함께 뻗을 자리가 결코 없다는 것, 그러니 자신의 활력이 이길 것인지 부인의 활력이 이길 것인지를 하루빨리 가려내야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n# 제18장. 미망인이 시달리다\n\n이튿날 아침 일찍 슬로프 씨는 주교의 옷방으로 불려 갔고, 주교가 아내에게 부추김을 받아 잔뜩 성이 난 채 전날 부인이 퍼부었던 질책을 되풀이하리라 단단히 예상하며 그리로 갔다. 슬로프 씨는 적어도 주교에게서만큼은 그런 꼴을 참고 있지 않겠다고 마음을 굳힌 터라 제법 싸울 태세를 갖추고 옷방에 들어섰다. 그러나 그가 본 주교는 더할 나위 없이 온화하고 부드러운 기분이었다. 각하는 몸이 좀 편치 않다고, 머리가 조금 아프고 속도 그리 좋지 않다고 하소연했으나, 성미로 말하자면 아무 탈이 없었다.\n\n“아, 슬로프.” 전속 사제가 내미는 손을 잡으며 그가 말했다. “오늘 아침에 그랜틀리 부주교가 나를 찾아오기로 되어 있는데, 도무지 그를 만날 형편이 못 되는구려. 미안하지만 나 대신 그를 좀 만나 주어야겠소.” 그러고 나서 프라우디 박사는 그랜틀리 박사에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 설명해 나갔다. 요컨대 하딩 씨가 워든 자리를 거절했으므로 그 자리는 퀴버풀 씨에게 제안되었고 그가 수락했다는 소식을, 그 소식을 전할 수 있는 가장 정중한 말로 그에게 알리라는 것이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 다시 한번 후원자에게 그 결정이 어쩌면 그리 현명하지 못한 듯싶다고 지적했고, 그것도 sotto voce로 했다. 그러나 그렇게 조심을 하고서도 많은 말을 하는 것은 안전하지 않았으니, 그가 그 몇 마디를 하는 동안 주교는 옷방에서 안쪽 성소로 통하는 문 쪽을 향해 엄지손가락으로 아주 희미하지만 그래도 아주 불길한 몸짓을 해 보였다. 슬로프 씨는 즉시 그 뜻을 알아채고 더는 말하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 자기와 후원자 사이에 밀약이 있게 되리라는 것, 자기가 바라던 동맹이 맺어지리라는 것, 그리고 이번 퀴버풀 씨의 임명이 부부간의 순종이라는 제단에 바쳐지는 마지막 제물이 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다. 이 모든 것을 슬로프 씨는 주교의 엄지손가락이 움직인 그 희미한 동작에서 읽어 냈고, 그가 읽어 낸 것은 옳았다. 양피지도 봉인도, 증서도 해명도 서약도 필요 없었다. 흥정은 두 사람 사이에 이해되었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 위에 주교에게 손을 내밀었다. 주교는 유난히 한 번 더 힘주어 쥔 그 손의 뜻을 알아들었고, 알아들었다는 표시의 빛이 그의 눈에서 반짝였다.\n\n“부주교에게 정중히 대하시오, 슬로프 씨.” 그가 소리를 높여 말했다. “다만 이 일에서는 하딩 씨 때문에 내가 그의 편의를 봐줄 도리가 없게 되었다는 것을 분명히 알아듣게 해 주시오.”\n\n이 면담이 오가는 동안 프라우디 부인이 침실에 앉아 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고 있었다고 말한다면 그것은 그 부인에 대한 중상이 될 것이다. 그녀 안에는 그런 비열함으로까지 내려가는 것을 막아 주는 체면의 정신이 있었다. 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대거나 문틈에 귀를 기울이는 것은 하녀나 하는 수작이었다. 프라우디 부인은 그것을 알았고, 그래서 그리하지 않았다. 다만 그녀는 될 수 있는 대로 문 가까이에 자리를 잡았으니, 하녀의 술책으로까지 내려가지 않고도 하녀가 얻었을 이점을 되도록 얻어 보자는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 들은 것은 얼마 되지 않았고, 그 얼마 안 되는 것은 그녀를 속이기에나 족했다. 그녀는 그 다정한 악수를 조금도 보지 못했고, 그 맺어진 흥정을 조금도 알아채지 못했다. 그 두 거짓된 사내가 함께 세운 배신의 결의를, 곧 그녀가 지위의 자랑에 겨워 있을 때 그녀를 뒤엎어 버리고, 그녀가 잔을 채 마시기도 전에 그 입술에서 잔을 쳐 내고, 그녀가 권력의 단맛을 맛보기도 전에 그 권력을 죄다 쓸어 가려는 결의를 그녀는 꿈에도 그리지 못했다! 배신자들이었다, 그 품속의 남편과, 그녀가 거두어 세상에서 가장 밝은 난롯가의 온기로 데려다 놓은 그 버림받은 자가! 그러나 둘 중 누구에게도 이 여인만 한 배포는 없었다. 두 사내가 이렇게 손을 잡고 그녀에게 맞섰다고는 하나, 싸움은 아직 진 것이 아니다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 그랜틀리 박사가 자기를 만나는 영광은 사양하리라고 꽤 확신했는데, 과연 그렇게 되었다. 부주교는 주교관 문이 열렸을 때 쪽지 한 장으로 영접을 받았다. 슬로프 씨가 삼가 인사를 올린다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고. 주교는 방에서 몸이 편치 않아 대단히 유감스럽게 여긴다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고. 슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 전하도록 위임받았으니 부주교만 좋다면 영광으로 알겠다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고. 그러나 부주교는 좋지 않았다. 그는 현관에서 그 쪽지를 읽고 손안에 구겨 쥐고는, 각하의 병환이 딱하다는 둥 무어라 중얼거리며 물러났으니, 슬로프 씨의 쪽지에 대한 답으로는 말 한마디조차 남기지 않았다.\n\n“병이라!” 부주교는 사륜마차에 몸을 던져 넣으며 혼잣말을 했다. “저 사람은 순전히 겁쟁이야. 나를 만나기가 무서운 게지. 병은 무슨 병.” 부주교는 자기가 앓아 본 적이 없었으므로 남이라고 정말로 병 때문에 약속을 못 지킬 수 있다는 것을 이해하지 못했다. 그는 그런 핑계란 모조리 발뺌이라고 여겼는데, 이번 경우에는 그리 틀린 것도 아니었다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 하이 스트리트에 있는 장인의 하숙으로 가자고 일렀고, 하인에게서 하딩 씨가 따님 댁에 있다는 말을 듣고는 볼드 부인의 집까지 그를 뒤쫓아 가 거기서 그를 찾아냈다. 부주교는 응접실에 들어설 무렵 분노로 씩씩거리고 있었으며, 이때쯤에는 전속 사제의 악랄함에 밀려 주교의 비겁함은 거의 잊어버린 참이었다.\n\n“이것 좀 보십시오.” 그는 슬로프 씨의 구겨진 쪽지를 하딩 씨에게 던지며 말했다. “내가 원한다면 슬로프 씨를 만나 뵙는 영광을 누려도 좋다는 소리를 듣게 생겼습니다. 그것도 주교와 분명하게 약속을 해 둔 다음에 말이지요.”\n\n“하지만 주교가 편찮다지 않은가.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“쳇! 설마 그따위 핑계에 넘어가셨다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요. 어제만 해도 멀쩡했습니다. 자, 제가 어찌할 작정인지 말씀드리지요. 저는 주교를 만날 겁니다. 그리고 그의 처신을 어떻게 생각하는지도 아주 분명하게 말해 줄 겁니다. 저는 기어이 그를 만나겠습니다. 그러지 않으면 바체스터가 곧 그가 발붙이기에 너무 뜨거운 곳이 되고 말 테니까요.”\n\n엘리너도 그 방에 앉아 있었으나 그랜틀리 박사는 성이 난 나머지 그녀를 거의 안중에 두지도 않았다. 그런데 엘리너가 이제 더없이 천진하게 그에게 말했다. “슬로프 씨를 만나 보셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 그랜틀리 박사님. 어쩌면 도움이 되었을지도 모르잖아요.”\n\n부주교는 거의 사나울 만큼 노해서 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다. 엘리너가 그 자리에서 슬로프 씨를 두 번째 남편으로 받아들이기로 했노라 실토했다 한들, 이런 소원을 입 밖에 내는 것을 듣고 지금 확신하게 된 것보다 더 굳게, 그녀가 몸과 마음을 다 바쳐 슬로프와 프라우디 편에 속해 있다고 믿기는 어려웠을 것이다. 가엾은 엘리너!\n\n“만나 보라고?” 부주교가 그녀를 노려보며 말했다. “대체 무엇 때문에 내가 그따위 자와 상대하여 세상의 평판에서도 내 자신의 눈에서도 스스로를 낮추어야 한단 말인가? 나는 여태 신사들 틈에서 살아왔고, 누가 되었든 나를 다른 부류 속으로 끌고 들어가게 둘 생각은 없네.”\n\n가엾은 하딩 씨는 부주교의 말뜻을 잘 알았으나, 엘리너는 제 아기만큼이나 천진했다. 아버지의 이익에 도움이 될 수 있는 마당에 슬로프 씨에게 몇 분 말을 붙여 주는 것이 어찌하여 부주교로서 스스로 나쁜 무리 속에 끌려 들어가는 일이 되는지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다.\n\n“저는 어제 슬로프 씨와 꼬박 한 시간을 이야기했어요.” 그녀가 짐짓 위엄을 조금 부리며 말했다. “그렇다고 제가 낮추어졌다고는 느끼지 않았고요.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 자네가 너그러이 허락해 준다면, 그런 일은 내가 알아서 판단하겠네. 그리고 내 한마디 하지, 엘리너. 자네를 벗으로 여기는 사람들의 충고에 자네도 좀 이끌리는 편이 훨씬 나을 걸세. 그러지 않으면 자네에게 충고해 줄 벗이 하나도 남지 않았다는 것을 알게 되기 쉬울 테니까.”\n\n엘리너는 머리카락 뿌리까지 붉어졌다. 그러나 그때까지도 부주교의 마음속에 무엇이 오가는지는 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 가엾은 존 볼드가 죽은 뒤로 사랑을 준다거나 받는다거나 하는 생각은 아직 그녀의 마음에 들어와 본 적이 없었고, 설령 그런 생각이 거기서 돋아날 수 있다 하더라도, 그것을 낳게 할 남자라면 슬로프 씨와는 아주 딴판인 사람이어야 했다.\n\n그럼에도 엘리너는 낯을 몹시 붉혔으니, 자기가 온당치 못한 처신을 했다고 책잡히고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이며, 게다가 아버지가 곧바로 제 편을 들어 주지 않았기에 속이 더욱 아팠다. 다름 아닌 그 아버지를 위해, 그 아버지가 사랑스러워서 슬로프 씨의 속내를 받아 담는 그릇 노릇까지 감수했던 것이 아닌가. 그녀는 오간 이야기를 낱낱이 아버지에게 들려주었고, 아버지가 요양원에 관한 슬로프 씨의 속셈을 두고 딸의 말에 온전히 동의하지는 않았다 해도, 그와 이야기를 나눈 것이 잘못이라고 여기게 할 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 너무나 화가 나서 형부 앞에 스스로를 낮출 수가 없었다. 사실 그녀는 형부 앞에서 그리 비굴하게 구는 데 길든 적이 없었고, 둘이 마음을 터놓는 한편이었던 적도 없었다. “저는 무슨 말씀이신지 조금도 모르겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님.” 그녀가 말했다. “제 벗들이 못마땅해할 만한 일을 제가 했다고는 생각하지 않아요. 슬로프 씨는 아버지가 요양원에 대해 어떤 뜻을 두고 계신지 물으려고 일부러 여기 온 것이고, 저는 그가 우호적인 뜻으로 왔다고 믿었기에 말해 준 거예요.”\n\n“우호적인 뜻이라!” 부주교가 비웃었다.\n\n“박사님은 슬로프 씨를 크게 잘못 보고 계신 것 같아요.” 엘리너가 말을 이었다. “하지만 이 일은 이미 아버지께 다 설명드렸어요. 그리고 제 말이 마음에 드시지 않는 모양이니, 그랜틀리 박사님, 허락해 주신다면 저는 물러가서 박사님과 아버지 두 분이 계시게 하겠어요.” 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 천천히 방에서 걸어 나갔다.\n\n이 모든 일이 하딩 씨를 몹시 언짢게 했다. 부주교와 그 아내가 엘리너는 슬로프 씨와 결혼할 작정이라고 마음을 굳혀 버렸다는 것은 아주 분명했다. 하딩 씨는 딸이 정말로 그러리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없었으나, 그러면서도 정황으로 보면 딸이 그 사내와 함께 있는 것을 싫어하지 않는 듯이 보인다는 사실만은 부인할 수 없었다. 딸은 요즘 노상 그를 만나면서도 다른 미혼 신사의 방문은 하나도 받지 않았다. 그의 처신이 도마에 오를 때면 딸은 늘 그의 편을 들었으니, 그 사내가 제 벗들에게 사람됨으로 얼마나 거슬리는지 알면서도 그러했다. 게다가 하딩 씨는 딸이 슬로프 부인이 되기로 마음먹는다 한들 자기로서는 그것을 나무랄 만한 정당한 근거가 하나도 없다고 느꼈다. 딸에게는 제 마음에 드는 대로 할 온전한 권리가 있었고, 그는 아버지로서, 슬로프 씨만큼 세상에 번듯하게 서 있는 성직자와 결혼한다고 해서 딸이 스스로를 욕되게 하는 것이라고는 말할 수 없었다. 그런 혼사를 두고 딸과 다투는 것, 부주교가 그리하겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것처럼 딸과 인연을 끊는 것으로 말하자면, 하딩 씨에게는 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 딸이 그 사내와 결혼하기로 굳게 정한다면 자기는 그 혐오감을 어떻게든 견뎌 넘기는 수밖에 없었다. 그의 엘리너, 그 옛 행복한 집에서 함께 지내던 오랜 길동무는 여전히 그의 가슴의 벗이요 마음의 자식이어야 했다. 누가 딸을 내치든 자기만은 내치지 않으리라. 만일 늘그막에 세상 모든 사람 가운데 가장 싫어하는 그 사내와 한 식탁에 앉아야 하는 것이 정해진 운명이라면, 그는 그 운명을 할 수 있는 한 잘 맞이할 참이었다. 그에게는 딸을 잃는 것보다 무엇이든 나았다.\n\n이러한 심경이었으므로 그는 엘리너 편에 서서 부주교에게 맞서기도, 부주교 편에 서서 엘리너에게 맞서기도 어려웠다. 애초에 딸을 의심하지 말았어야 했다고들 할 것이다. 아아, 정말이지 그러지 말았어야 했다. 그러나 하딩 씨는 결코 흠 없는 인물이 아니었다. 그 우유부단함, 그 나약함, 남에게 끌려가기 쉬운 성미, 자신에 대한 믿음의 부족에서 그는 흠 없는 것과는 거리가 멀었다. 그리고 이것도 기억해 두어야 하니, 부주교가 진저리를 치며 떠올린 그 혼사, 슬로프 씨를 익히 아는 우리라면 마찬가지로 진저리를 쳤을 그 혼사가 하딩 씨에게는 그토록 끔찍해 보이지 않았던 것은, 그가 마음이 너그러워 부주교처럼, 또 우리처럼 그 전속 사제를 미워하지는 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 딸이 방을 나갔을 때 그는 몹시 언짢았고, 그래서 슬플 때면 늘 하던 오랜 버릇에 기댔다. 그는 있지도 않은 첼로로 느린 곡조를 켜기 시작했으니, 한 손에 활을 쥔 양 천천히 앞뒤로 그으면서 다른 손으로는 있지도 않은 화음을 짚어 나갔다.\n\n“저 애는 그 사내와 결혼하고야 말 겁니다. 둘에 둘을 더하면 넷인 것만큼이나 확실하지요.” 현실적인 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“아니길 바라네, 아니길 바라.” 아버지가 말했다. “하지만 그 애가 그리한다면 내가 무슨 말을 하겠나? 나에게는 그를 반대할 권리가 없네.”\n\n“권리가 없다니요!” 그랜틀리 박사가 소리쳤다.\n\n“아비로서는 권리가 없지. 그는 나와 같은 직분에 있는 사람이고, 우리가 아는 한으로는 좋은 사람일세.”\n\n부주교는 여기에 결코 동의하려 들지 않았다. 그러나 엘리너의 응접실에서 그녀를 걸어 두고 따지는 것은 온당치 않았으므로, 두 사람은 함께 밖으로 나가 대성당 경내의 느릅나무 아래에서 이 일을 온갖 측면에서 논의했다. 하딩 씨는 또한 사위에게, 슬로프 씨가 지난번 그 미망인을 찾아간 목적이, 적어도 표방한 목적이 무엇이었는지를 설명해 주었다. 그러나 그는 슬로프 씨가 짐짓 내세운 것과 같은 진심 어린 염려를 조금이라도 품고 있다고는 도저히 믿을 수 없다고 말했다. “그자가 나를 대하던 태도를 잊을 수가 없네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “그리고 그 사람의 생각이 그렇게 빨리 바뀌었을 리도 없지.”\n\n“이제 다 알겠습니다.” 부주교가 말했다. “교활한 타르튀프 같으니! 아비에게 자리를 마련해 주어 딸을 사겠다는 심산이지요. 자기가 얼마나 힘이 있는지, 얼마나 착한 사람인지, 그 여자의 beaux yeux를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 마음이 있는지를 보여 주겠다는 겁니다. 그래요, 이제야 다 알겠습니다. 하지만 우리도 만만치 않다는 것을 보여 주지요, 하딩 씨.” 그는 다소 정색을 하고 길동무 쪽으로 돌아서서 그의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. “그런 조건으로 요양원을 얻느니 차라리 잃는 편이 나을지도 모릅니다.”\n\n“잃다니.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “나는 벌써 잃었네. 나는 그것을 바라지도 않아. 그것 없이 지내기로 마음을 굳혔네. 아주 물러나겠어. 지금 가서 주교에게 한 줄 적어 보내, 내 청을 아주 거두어들이겠다고 말하겠네.”\n\n그런 식으로 이 성가심과 곤경에서 벗어나도 좋다는 허락을 받는 것보다 그를 더 기쁘게 할 일은 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 지금 부주교에게는 너무 앞서 나가고 있었다.\n\n“안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 안 됩니다! 그런 짓은 하지 않을 겁니다.” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “우리는 여전히 요양원을 가질 겁니다. 우리가 그것을 얻으리라는 것은 거의 의심하지 않아요. 다만 슬로프 씨의 도움으로는 아니지요. 그것이 꼭 있어야 한다면 차라리 잃겠습니다. 하지만 될 수만 있다면 그자가 이를 갈든 말든 얻고야 말겠습니다. 애러빈이 내일 플럼스테드에 옵니다. 건너오셔서 그와 이야기를 나누셔야 합니다.”\n\n이제 두 사람은 대성당 도서관으로 들어갔다. 그곳은 경내의 성직자들이 설교문을, 이따금은 편지를 쓰는 곳으로, 또 신학 서적을, 이따금은 잡지와 신문을 읽는 곳으로, 말하자면 일종의 교회 클럽방처럼 쓰이는 데였다. 신학 서적들은 아마도 그 건물의 겉모양을 보고 바깥사람들이 짐작했을 만큼 자주 건드려지지는 않았을 것이다. 여기서 두 동지는 앞으로 취할 방침을 정했다. 부주교는 주교에게 편지를 한 통 썼는데, 말은 세게 썼으나 그래도 예의는 갖추었고, 그 안에 장인이 그 자리에 대해 지니는 권리를 내세우고 자기가 찾아갔을 때 각하를 뵙지 못한 것이 유감이라는 뜻을 밝혔다. 슬로프 씨에 대해서는 한마디도 언급하지 않았다. 이어서 하딩 씨가 이튿날 플럼스테드로 나가기로 정해졌고, 이 일을 두고 한참을 논의한 끝에 부주교는 엘리너도 그리로 부르자고 제안했으니, 될 수 있으면 슬로프 씨의 관심에서 그녀를 떼어 놓자는 것이었다. “한두 주면,” 그가 말했다. “그자가 어떤 사람인지 엘리너도 알게 되겠지요. 그리고 거기 있는 동안은 화를 입을 자리에서 벗어나 있는 셈입니다. 슬로프 씨가 거기까지 뒤쫓아 오지는 못할 테니까요.”\n\n엘리너는 형부가 돌아와서 아버지와 함께 플럼스테드로 나가자고 아주 정중하게 청하는 것을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다. 그녀는 아버지가 등 뒤에서 자기를 위해 싸워 주었다는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다. 그녀는 아버지가 고마웠고, 아버지를 생각해서라도 초대를 거절하는 식으로 부주교에게 노여움을 드러내지는 않으려 했다. 그러나 내일은 갈 수 없다고 그녀는 말했다. 스탠호프 댁에서 차를 마시자는 초대를 받았고 가겠다고 약속해 두었다는 것이었다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, 아버지가 기다려 주신다면 모레 함께 가겠고 그러지 않으면 뒤따라가겠다고 했다.\n\n“스탠호프 댁이라!” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “그 집안과 그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐군.”\n\n“저도 몰랐어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “어제 스탠호프 양이 찾아오기 전까지는요. 그렇지만 저는 그분이 아주 마음에 들고, 가서 그 댁 사람들과 체스를 두기로 약속했어요.”\n\n“거기 사람들이 모이기라도 하는가?” 여전히 슬로프 씨를 꺼리는 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “스탠호프 양이 아무도 오지 않을 거라고 했어요. 다만 메리가 몇 주 저를 떠나 있다는 말을 들었고, 제가 체스를 둘 줄 안다는 것도 누구한테선가 알게 되어서, 저더러 오라고 청하려고 일부러 건너온 거예요.”\n\n“그거참 다정하구나.” 전임 워든이 말했다. “그 사람들이 영국 사람보다 외국 사람처럼 보이는 것이야 확실하다만, 그렇다고 그만큼 못할 것도 없겠지.”\n\n부주교는 스탠호프 일가를 곱게 보는 편이었고 이 일에 대해서는 반대할 것이 없었다. 그리하여 하딩 씨가 플럼스테드 방문을 하루 미루었다가 엘리너와 아기와 유모를 데리고 가기로 정해졌다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 확실히 바체스터에서 제법 중요한 인물이 되어 가고 있다.\n\n# 제19장. 달빛 아래의 바체스터\n\n스탠호프 집안에는 슬퍼할 까닭도, 이따금 마음이 어지러워질 까닭도 적지 않았으나, 그럼에도 이들이 슬퍼하거나 흔들리는 모습은 좀처럼 보이지 않았다. 저마다 제 짐을 불평 없이, 그리고 아마도 동정도 받지 않은 채 지고 갈 줄 아는 것이 이 집안 사람들 하나하나의 남다른 재주였다. 이들은 담장 어느 쪽이든 볕뉘가 비치기만 하면 늘 볕 드는 쪽을 바라보았고, 어느 쪽에도 볕이 없으면 그늘을 무심히 견뎠는데, 그 무심함은 스토아적이라고까지는 못 해도 스토아학파가 목표로 삼던 바에는 넉넉히 닿아 있었다. 늙은 스탠호프는 아버지로서도 성직자로서도 제 본분을 제대로 해내지 못했음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었고, 제가 죽고 난 뒤 식구들이 놓이게 될 처지를 생각하면 슬픔 없이 제 죽음을 내다보기가 어려웠다. 여러 해 동안 그의 수입은 연 3,000파운드에 이르렀건만, 식구들에게 돌아갈 것이라고는 어머니의 재산 10,000파운드 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 그는 수입을 다 써 버렸을 뿐 아니라 빚까지 지고 있었다. 그런데도 그는 좀처럼 근심하는 기색을 겉으로 드러내지 않았다.\n\n어머니도 마찬가지였다. 그녀가 자식들의 즐거움에 보태는 것이 적었다면, 깎아내리는 것은 그보다도 적었다. 제 처지를 두고 투덜대지도 않았고 지난날이나 앞날의 고생을 입에 올리지도 않았다. 옷매무새를 만져 줄 하녀가 있고 그 옷들이 잘 지어져 있기만 하면, 그로써 그녀의 천성은 흡족했다. 자식들도 마찬가지였다. 샬럿은 앞으로 닥칠 가난을 들어 아버지를 나무란 적이 없었고, 저 혼자 이렇게 빨리 노처녀가 되어 가는 것을 서러워하는 기색도 없었다. 좀처럼 성을 내는 법이 없었고, 겉모습으로 미루어 판단해도 좋다면 그녀는 언제나 행복했다. 시뇨라는 그만큼 성정이 곱지는 않았으나 견디는 용기만은 대단했다. 좀처럼 하소연하지 않았고, 적어도 식구들 앞에서는 결코 하지 않았다. 그만큼 아름답고 그만큼 종교적 위안이라고는 없는 여자라면 대개 마음이 아주 무너져 내렸을 만한 고통거리를 안고 있으면서도, 그녀는 그 고통을 잠자코 견뎠고, 혹 그 이야기를 꺼낸다 해도 저와 시시덕거리는 남자들의 동정을 자아내고 찬탄을 부추기기 위해서일 뿐이었다. 버티로 말하자면, 그 목소리의 울림과 눈빛의 반짝임만 보아서는 이 세상에 슬픔도 근심도 하나 없는 사람이라고 여겼을 것이다. 실제로도 없었다. 그는 내일의 슬픔을 미리 당겨 걱정할 줄 모르는 사람이었다. 앞날의 궁핍이 그의 식욕을 어지럽히는 일은, 푸주한의 칼이 양의 식욕을 어지럽히는 일이나 매한가지로 없었다.\n\n그들이 살아가는 방식은 대체로 이러했으나, 드문 예외가 없지는 않았다. 이따금 아버지의 눈에서 성난 눈길이 번뜩였고, 사자는 무슨 피 보는 일이라도 궁리하는 듯 낮고 위험한 울음을 흘렸다. 이따금 네로니 부인 또한 사람이라는 것들에게 모질어져서, 여느 때보다도 세상의 체면치레에 각을 세우고, 이제 곧 밧줄을 끊고 제 감정의 물살에 실려 파멸과 난파로 떠내려가려는 듯이 보이곤 했다. 그러나 그녀 역시 나머지 식구들과 마찬가지로 진짜 감정이라는 것이 없었고, 참된 열정을 느낄 줄 몰랐다. 바로 그 점이 그녀의 안전판이었다. 무슨 일탈을 마음먹기 전에 그녀는 조그맣게 셈을 해 보았고, 그 셈의 결론은 대체로 스탠호프 저택이, 아니 바체스터 대성당 경내라 해도, 바깥 넓은 세상보다는 낫다는 것이었다.\n\n그들의 생활 시간은 몹시 제멋대로였다. 아침 식당에 제일 먼저 나오는 이는 대개 아버지였고, 곧이어 샬럿이 내려와 커피를 따라 주었으나, 나머지 식구들은 아무 데서나, 아무렇게나, 아무 때나 아침을 먹었다. 부주교가 주교관을 헛되이 다녀간 이튿날 아침, 스탠호프 박사는 눈썹께에 심상치 않은 그늘을 드리운 채 아래층으로 내려왔다. 흰 머리칼은 여느 때보다 헝클어져 있었고, 안락의자에 앉을 때는 숨소리가 굵고 크게 새어 나왔다. 손에는 뜯어 놓은 편지들이 들려 있었는데, 샬럿이 방에 들어왔을 때에도 그는 여전히 그것들을 읽고 있었다. 그녀가 다가가 늘 하던 대로 입을 맞추었으나 그는 그러거나 말거나 거의 알은체를 하지 않았고, 그녀는 곧바로 무슨 일이 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n“이건 대체 무슨 소리냐?” 그가 밀라노 소인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 식탁 너머로 던지며 말했다. 샬럿은 그것을 집어 들면서 조금 겁이 났으나, 그저 이탈리아 모자 가게에서 온 청구서임을 보고는 마음이 놓였다. 합계액이 크기는 컸으나, 큰 소동이 벌어질 만큼 크지는 않았다.\n\n“저희 옷값이에요, 아버지. 여기 오기 전 여섯 달 치예요. 셋이서 옷을 거저 지어 입을 수야 없잖아요.”\n\n“거저라니, 원!” 그가 밀라노 화폐 단위로 적힌 숫자들을 들여다보며 말했다. 과연 어마어마한 액수였다.\n\n“그 사람이 저한테 보냈어야 할 것을 그랬네요.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“나도 진심으로 그랬으면 싶구나. 네가 갚아 줄 생각이었다면 말이다. 이걸 보니 4분의 3은 마들린 몫이라는 걸 알겠다.”\n\n“마들린으로서는 달리 마음 붙일 것도 없잖아요, 아버지.” 샬럿이 진심 어린 너그러움으로 말했다.\n\n“그리고 저 녀석도 달리 마음 붙일 것이 없다는 말이렷다.” 박사가 딸에게 또 다른 편지 한 통을 던지며 말했다. 시도니아 집안의 누군가에게서 온 것으로, 에설버트 스탠호프 씨 앞으로 할인해 준 어음이 아홉 달째 연체되었으니 그 금액인 700파운드라는 하찮은 푼돈을 갚아 달라고 아버지에게 정중히 청하는 내용이었다.\n\n샬럿은 편지를 읽고 천천히 접어 찻쟁반 가장자리 밑에 밀어 넣었다.\n\n“저 녀석은 유대인들과 어음 할인 놀음을 하는 것 말고는 마음 붙일 데가 없다는 말이지. 내가 저걸 갚아 주리라 여기는 게냐?”\n\n“그런 생각은 결코 하지 않을 거예요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 누가 갚아 주리라 여긴다는 말이냐?”\n\n“정직성만 따진다면 끝내 갚지 않는다 해도 크게 상관없을 거예요.” 그녀가 말했다. “오라비가 손에 쥔 돈은 얼마 되지도 않았을 테니까요.”\n\n“상관없기야 마찬가지겠지.” 아버지가 말했다. “저놈이 감옥에 들어가 거기서 썩는다 해도 말이다. 내가 보기엔 그것이 나머지 한쪽 길이다.”\n\n스탠호프 박사는 제 젊은 시절의 관행을 두고 하는 말이었다. 그러나 딸은 그토록 오래 외국에 살았으면서도 영국 세상의 물정에 훨씬 밝았다. “그 사람이 오라비를 붙잡아 간다면,” 그녀가 말했다, “오라비는 파산 법정을 거치면 그만이에요.”\n\n위대한 시도니아 집안이여, 우리 이방인들은 그대들을 이렇게 대접하는도다. 우리가 더없이 궁한 처지에 몰렸을 때 그대와 그대의 식솔이 사자만 한 황금 산더미로, 더러는 포도주 인수증이며 화장 상자 열두 개짜리 주문서 따위로 우리를 도와주었건만.\n\n“뭐라고, 그래서 지불 불능자가 되라는 말이냐?” 박사가 말했다.\n\n“이미 그런걸요.” 샬럿이 말했다. 그녀는 언제나 어려운 대목은 얼른 넘어가고 싶어 했다.\n\n“꼴이 좋구나.” 박사가 말했다. “영국 국교회 성직자의 아들이라는 자가.”\n\n“성직자의 아들이라고 해서 다른 젊은이들보다 빚을 더 잘 갚아야 할 까닭이 있나요.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“학교를 나온 뒤로 내가 저에게 준 돈만 해도 어지간한 귀족 집 맏아들에게 넉넉하다고들 하는 액수는 되고도 남는다.” 성난 아버지가 말했다.\n\n“그러시더라도 아버지,” 샬럿이 말했다, “한 번만 더 기회를 주세요.”\n\n“뭐라고!” 박사가 말했다. “그 유대인에게 내가 돈을 갚으란 말이냐?”\n\n“어머, 아니에요! 저라도 그 사람에게는 안 갚아요. 그 사람은 제 운을 받아들여야죠. 그리고 정 안 되면 버티는 외국으로 나가야 하고요. 하지만 아버지가 버티에게 부드럽게 대해 주시고, 우리가 여기 머무는 동안은 이 집에 두어 주셨으면 해요. 오라비 머릿속에 계획이 하나 있는데, 그게 되면 그래도 제 발로 설 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n“제 직업을 이어 갈 계획이라도 있다더냐?”\n\n“아, 그것도 할 거예요. 다만 그건 나중 일이고요. 오라비는 결혼할 생각을 하고 있어요.”\n\n바로 그때 문이 열리고 버티가 휘파람을 불며 들어왔다. 박사는 즉시 제 달걀에 몰두하여, 버티가 휘파람을 불며 누이 곁으로 돌아가도록 알은체도 하지 않고 내버려 두었다.\n\n샬럿은 먼저 아버지 쪽을 흘긋 보고 다음에는 찻쟁반 밑으로 귀퉁이가 삐죽 나온 편지를 흘긋 보며 눈짓을 보냈다. 버티는 그것을 보고 알아차렸고, 고양이처럼 소리 없는 몸짓으로 편지를 빼내어 그 내용을 알아 두었다. 그러나 박사는 달걀 껍데기에 온통 빠져 있는 듯 보였어도 그것을 보고 있었고, 더할 나위 없이 거친 목소리로 말했다. “어떠냐, 네가 그 양반을 아느냐?”\n\n“압니다, 아버지.” 버티가 말했다. “얼마간 아는 사이이기는 하지만, 그렇다고 그 사람이 아버지를 성가시게 할 자격이 생기는 건 아니지요. 허락해 주신다면 제가 이 편지에 답장을 하겠어요.”\n\n“어쨌든 나는 안 쓴다.” 아버지가 말했다. 그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. “네가 그 사람에게 700파운드를 빚졌다는 것이 사실이냐?”\n\n“글쎄요,” 버티가 말했다, “제가 실제로 빚진 만큼이라도 갚을 형편이 된다면, 액수는 좀 따져 보고 싶은 마음이 있습니다.”\n\n“그 사람이 네 700파운드짜리 어음을 갖고 있느냐?” 아버지가 몹시 크고 몹시 성난 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“네, 갖고 있을 겁니다.” 버티가 말했다. “하지만 제가 그 사람한테서 받은 돈은 통틀어 150파운드뿐이에요.”\n\n“그럼 나머지 550파운드는 어찌 되었느냐?”\n\n“그게, 아버지, 수수료가 100파운드쯤 되었고요, 나머지는 포석과 흔들목마로 받았습니다.”\n\n“포석과 흔들목마라니!” 박사가 말했다. “그것들은 어디 있느냐?”\n\n“아, 그거야 런던 어딘가에 있겠지요. 원하시면 알아보겠습니다.”\n\n“저놈은 얼간이다.” 박사가 말했다. “저놈에게 돈을 더 쓰는 건 순전한 어리석음이야. 무엇으로도 저놈을 파멸에서 건져 내지 못한다.” 그렇게 말하고 이 가엾은 아버지는 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n“영감님이 그 포석을 갖고 싶으신 걸까?” 버티가 누이에게 말했다.\n\n“내 말 좀 들어 봐.” 그녀가 말했다. “조심하지 않으면 너는 머리 위에 지붕 하나 없이 세상에 내던져진 신세가 될 거야. 너는 아버지를 나만큼 몰라. 지금 몹시 화가 나셨어.”\n\n버티는 커다란 수염을 쓰다듬으며 차를 홀짝였고, 반은 우스개로 반은 진지하게 제 불운을 늘어놓다가, 마침내 미망인 볼드 부인의 마음에 들도록 힘껏 애써 보겠다고 누이에게 약속하는 것으로 끝을 맺었다. 그러고 나서 샬럿은 아버지를 따라 그의 방으로 가서 노여움을 누그러뜨리고, 적어도 몇 주 동안은 그 유대인 어음 할인업자 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않도록 아버지를 구슬렸다. 아버지는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 아들이 제 앞가림할 만한 살림을 확실히 마련하는 것이 눈에 보이기만 한다면 700파운드를 갚아 주겠다, 적어도 그 어음은 결말을 지어 주겠다고까지 말했다. 가엾은 엘리너에 대해서는 두 사람 사이에 아무 말도 드러내 놓고 오가지 않았으나, 아버지와 딸은 서로의 뜻을 알고 있었다.\n\n아홉 시에 온 식구가 응접실에 모였을 때는 서로 더할 나위 없이 기분이 좋았고, 그 무렵 볼드 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다. 그녀는 물론 인사차 들른 적은 있었으나 이 집에 들어와 본 적은 없었으므로, 여느 저녁 옷차림 그대로 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이라도 되는 양 이 낯선 사람들의 응접실에 격의 없이 다정하게 들어서고 있는 자신이 새삼 이상하게 느껴졌다. 그러나 삼 분도 지나지 않아 그들은 그녀를 편안하게 만들어 주었다. 샬럿이 가볍게 내려와 보닛을 받아 들었고, 버티가 다가와 숄을 벗겨 주었고, 시뇨라는 마음먹고 상냥해질 때 지을 수 있는 그 미소를 지어 보였으며, 늙은 박사는 마음에 곧장 와닿는 자애로운 축복 같은 태도로 그녀와 악수를 하여 이분은 틀림없이 좋은 사람이라는 느낌을 갖게 했다.\n\n자리에 앉은 지 오 분이 채 못 되어 문이 다시 열리고 슬로프 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 있었다. 그녀는 다른 사람은 아무도 오지 않는다고 들었던 터라 다소 놀랐고, 몇몇 사람의 태도로 보아 슬로프 씨가 뜻밖의 손님이 아님이 아주 분명했다. 그러나 그렇다고 대수로울 것도 없었다. 이런 초대 자리에서는 독신 남자 한둘쯤은 늘 아무도 아닌 사람으로 치기 마련이고, 엘리너 자신이 그렇듯 슬로프 씨라고 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 차를 마시지 못할 까닭도 없었다. 그러나 정작 그는 제 장래의 아내가 그 자리에 끼어 있는 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐고 그다지 반갑지도 않았다. 그는 네로니 부인의 아름다움을 바라보고 그녀의 아첨을 듣고 되돌려 주는 즐거움을 누리러 온 것이었다. 그리고 스스로 그만큼 인정하지는 않았을지언정, 애초에 작정한 대로 저녁을 보낸다면 볼드 부인에게 거는 구애가 진척되지는 않으리라는 것쯤은 느끼고 있었다.\n\n경쟁자가 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 시뇨라는 여느 때처럼 각별한 표시로 슬로프 씨를 맞았다. 그가 손을 잡자 그녀는 낮은 목소리로 무언가 은밀한 이야기를 건네며 차를 마신 뒤에 그에게 알려 줄 계획이 있다고 말했으니, 전속 사제를 사로잡힌 신세로 만드는 제 작업을 계속할 채비가 되어 있음이 역력했다. 가엾은 슬로프 씨는 어지간히 정신이 없었다. 그는 제 태도로 보아 엘리너가 자신이 그녀를 흠모하는 사람임을 모를 리 없다고 여겼고, 게다가 그 생각이 그녀에게 싫지만은 않으리라고 혼자 흐뭇해하던 참이었다. 그런 자신이 이제 남의 아내에게 매달린다면 그녀가 자신을 어찌 여기겠는가!\n\n그러나 엘리너는 그 점에 대해 그를 모질게 나무랄 마음이 없었고, 버티와 샬럿 스탠호프 사이에 앉게 되자 아무런 언짢음도 느끼지 않았다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨의 속셈을 의심하지 않았고, 남들이 그런 의심을 하고 있으리라는 것조차 의심하지 않았다. 그럼에도 슬로프 씨가 너무 가까이 앉지 않은 것이 은근히 흡족했다.\n\n그리고 버티 스탠호프가 곁에 있는 것도 싫지 않았다. 그가 처음 만나는 사람에게 호감을 주지 못하는 일이란 참으로 드물었다. 제 위엄을 크게 여기는 주교 앞이라면 실패할 수도 있었겠으나, 젊고 예쁜 여자 앞에서라면 그럴 일이 거의 없었다. 그는 무례하다는 두려움을 조금도 일으키지 않으면서 여자와 단박에 허물없어지는 재주를 지니고 있었다. 그에게는 길든 고양이의 성질 같은 것이 얼마간 있었다. 그가 귀염을 받고 쓰다듬어지고 스스럼없는 다정함으로 대접받는 것도, 그 답례로 그르렁대며 매끄럽고 우아하게 굴고 무엇보다 결코 발톱을 드러내지 않는 것도 아주 자연스러워 보였다. 그러나 다른 길든 고양이들과 마찬가지로 그에게도 발톱은 있었고, 때로는 그것을 위험하게 쓰기도 했다.\n\n차를 다 마시자 샬럿은 열린 창가로 가서 추수철 보름달이 너무 아름다워 그냥 지나칠 수 없다고 큰 소리로 외치며 모두를 불러 보게 했다. 사실을 말하자면 그 자리에서 달의 아름다움을 그리 마음에 두는 사람은 한 사람뿐이었고, 그 한 사람은 샬럿이 아니었다. 그러나 그녀는 저 정결한 여신이 제 목적에 얼마나 값진 도움이 될 수 있는지 알고 있었고, 그 순간의 목적을 위해 얼마쯤의 열의를 지어내는 일쯤은 어렵지 않았다. 엘리너와 버티가 곧 그녀 곁으로 왔다. 박사는 이제 안락의자에서 조용해졌고 스탠호프 부인도 제 의자에서 그러했으니, 둘 다 잠들 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n“볼드 부인은 휴얼파신가요, 브루스터파신가요, 아니면 또 다른 누구파신가요?” 샬럿이 말했다. 그녀는 무엇에 대해서든 조금씩은 알았고, 지금 들먹인 책들도 각각 3분의 1쯤 읽어 두었다.\n\n“어머!” 엘리너가 말했다. “그 책들은 하나도 읽지 못했어요. 하지만 달에는 적어도 한 사람은, 어쩌면 그보다 많은 사람이 살고 있다고 믿어요.”\n\n“말랑말랑한 아교질 물질은 믿지 않으시고요?” 버티가 말했다.\n\n“그 이야기는 저도 들었어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “그런 식으로 말하는 건 거의 불경한 일이 아닌가 싶어요. 이 별에서 우리를 다스리라고 하느님이 주신 법칙을 가지고 어떻게 다른 별들에서 발휘되는 그분의 권능을 따져 볼 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“정말이지 그렇지요!” 버티가 말했다. “금성에 도롱뇽 족속이 있으면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이겠어요? 목성에 물고기밖에 없다 한들, 그곳 물고기가 이곳 남자들이나 여자들만큼 총명하지 말라는 법은 또 어디 있고요?”\n\n“그건 그 물고기들을 아주 낮잡아 보는 말인걸요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “나는 휴얼 박사 편이에요. 남자와 여자가 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세계마다 되풀이될 만한 값어치가 있다고는 보지 않으니까요. 다른 별들에 영혼이 있을지는 몰라도 거기에 몸까지 딸려 있는지는 의심스러워요. 자, 볼드 부인, 우리 보닛을 쓰고 대성당 경내를 한 바퀴 돌아요. 별에 관한 문제를 논할 거라면 이 좁은 창가에 박혀 있느니 대성당 탑 아래에서 하는 편이 훨씬 나을 테니까요.”\n\n볼드 부인이 마다하지 않아 산책 나갈 무리가 꾸려졌다. 샬럿 스탠호프는 셋은 동행이 못 된다는 규칙을 잘 알고 있었으므로, 슬로프 씨가 함께 가도 좋다고 동생을 구슬려야 했다.\n\n“자, 슬로프 씨,” 그녀가 말했다, “저희와 함께 가 주시겠지요. 마들린, 우린 십오 분이면 돌아올 거야.”\n\n마들린은 언니의 눈에서 하려는 말을 다 읽어 냈고 그 속셈도 알았으며, 제 즐길 거리 대부분을 언니에게 기대고 사는 처지였으므로 물러서야 한다고 느꼈다. 또래의 다른 사람들이 밝은 밤의 부드러운 기운을 쐬러 나가는 동안 혼자 남겨진다는 것은 괴로운 일이었으나, 샬럿이 제 온갖 희롱과 술수에 얹어 주는 그 일종의 승인을 잃는 것은 그보다 더 괴로운 일이었다. 지금은 집안을 위해 양보해야 한다고 샬럿의 눈이 말하고 있었으므로, 마들린은 따랐다.\n\n그러나 샬럿의 눈은 슬로프 씨에게는 그런 말을 조금도 하지 않았다. 나머지 셋이 나가고 나면 시뇨라와 단둘이 마주 앉을 수 있을 터이니 그로서는 마다할 이유가 전혀 없었고, 그래서 그는 그녀에게 나직이 속삭였다. “저는 당신을 혼자 두지 않겠어요.”\n\n“아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “가세요. 제발 가세요, 부디 가세요, 저를 위해서요. 제가 그렇게 이기적인 사람이라고는 여기지 마세요. 저 때문에 누구도 집 안에 붙들려 있지 않는다는 것이 이 집의 정해진 이치랍니다. 저를 좀 더 아시게 되면 이것도 아시게 될 거예요. 저분들과 함께 가세요, 슬로프 씨. 다만 돌아오시거든 가시기 전에 오 분만 저와 이야기해 주세요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 자기가 가야 한다는 뜻임을 알아듣고 현관에서 일행에 합류했다. 볼드 부인의 팔짱을 낄 수만 있었다면 이 편성에도 조금도 불만이 없었을 것이나, 그것은 물론 어림없는 일이었다. 아닌 게 아니라 그의 운명은 아주 금세 정해졌으니, 현관문에 이르자마자 스탠호프 양이 그의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 버티는 엘리너가 이미 제 소유물이라도 되는 양 지극히 자연스럽게 그녀를 데리고 걸어 나갔던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔다. 먼저 공언한 대로 대성당 경내를 한 바퀴 돌았고, 다음에는 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회 아래 오래된 아치형 문을 지났으며, 그다음에는 주교관 뜰 뒤편으로 돌아 나가 마침내 마을 어귀의 다리에 이르렀다. 이 다리에서는 지나는 사람이 히램 요양원 뜰을 내려다볼 수 있었다. 앞서 걷던 샬럿과 슬로프 씨는 여기서 걸음을 멈추고 나머지 둘이 따라오기를 기다렸다. 달빛 속에 그토록 어여쁘게 솟아 있는 박공 끝과 오래된 벽돌 굴뚝이 하딩 씨가 얼마 전까지 살던 집의 것임을 슬로프 씨는 알고 있었고, 피할 수만 있었다면 그런 자리에서 그런 일행과 걸음을 멈추지 않았을 터이나, 스탠호프 양은 그가 넌지시 보내는 눈치를 받아 주지 않았다.\n\n“여긴 참 예쁜 곳이에요, 볼드 부인.” 샬럿이 말했다. “바체스터 근방에서는 단연 제일 예쁜 곳이지요. 아버님께서 왜 내놓으셨는지 모르겠어요.”\n\n과연 아주 예쁜 곳이었고, 지금은 달의 속임수 어린 빛을 받아 정직한 대낮에 보이는 것보다 두 배는 크고 두 배는 예쁘고 두 배는 예스럽게 그림 같아 보였다. 요양원이 그러했듯 좋은 나무들에 반쯤 둘러싸인 낡은 박공집에 달이 언제나 빌려주는 저 겹겹의 복잡한 기운과 신비롭고도 흥미로운 자태를 모르는 이가 어디 있으랴! 우리가 말하고 있는 그날 밤 다리 위에서 바라본 하딩 씨의 옛 거처는 참으로 사랑스러워 보였고, 엘리너는 아버지가 그곳을 떠난 것을 슬퍼하지는 않았으되 그 순간만은 아버지가 그리로 돌아갈 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n“곧 그리로 돌아가시게 된다지요, 아닌가요?” 버티가 물었다.\n\n엘리너는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그런 물음은 물은 사람도 알아채지 못한 채 대답 없이 지나가는 일이 흔하지만, 이번에는 그렇지 않았다. 다들 그녀가 대답하기를 기다리는 듯 잠자코 있었고, 한두 순간이 지나자 샬럿이 말했다. “하딩 씨가 요양원으로 돌아가시는 것으로 정해진 줄 알고 있는데요, 아닌가요?”\n\n“그 일은 아직 아무것도 정해지지 않은 것 같아요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그거야 당연한 일 아니겠어요.” 버티가 말했다. “아버님이 원하시기만 한다면 말이지요. 그런 일이 있고 난 뒤에 세상 어느 누가 그 자리를 맡을 수 있겠어요?”\n\n엘리너는 그것이 지금 이 자리에서는 이야기할 수 없는 문제임을 조용히 동행에게 알렸고, 그러고서 그들은 발길을 옮겼다. 샬럿이 대성당 탑들을 되돌아보게 마을 밖 언덕을 조금 올라가 보자고 하여, 엘리너는 걷는 데 힘이 되도록 버티의 팔에 기대면서 아버지와 주교 사이에 일이 어찌 되어 있는지를 그에게 이야기했다.\n\n“그러면 저 사람은,” 버티가 앞쪽의 슬로프 씨를 가리키며 말했다, “그 일에서 어떤 몫을 맡고 있나요?”\n\n엘리너는 슬로프 씨가 처음에는 아버지 위에 군림하려 들었으나 근래에는 마음을 돌려 하딩 씨에게 유리하도록 주교를 설득하는 데 힘을 다했다고 설명했다. “하지만 아버지는,” 그녀가 말했다, “그이를 그다지 믿으실 마음이 없으세요. 그이가 이 도시의 나이 든 성직자들에게 무척 오만하게 군다고들 하거든요.”\n\n“제 말을 믿으세요.” 버티가 말했다. “아버님이 옳으십니다. 제가 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저 사람은 오만하기도 하고 거짓되기도 한 사람이에요.”\n\n그들은 언덕 꼭대기까지 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔다가 들판을 가로지르는 오솔길로 돌아왔는데, 그 길은 강 위에 놓인 작은 나무다리, 아니 시골풍 난간을 붙인 널빤지 한 장을 건너 출발했던 쪽의 반대편 대성당 쪽으로 이어져 있었다. 그리하여 그들은 강이 가로질러 흐르는 주교관 뜰을 돌고, 대성당과 그 언저리 들판을 돌아 걸은 셈이었으니, 박사의 집 문 앞에 이르렀을 때는 열한 시가 넘어 있었다.\n\n“너무 늦었어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “이런 시각에 또 어머님을 성가시게 해서야 도리가 아니지요.”\n\n“어머,” 샬럿이 웃으며 말했다, “어머니를 성가시게 할 일은 없어요. 지금쯤은 벌써 잠자리에 드셨을걸요. 그리고 부인이 들어와서 얼굴을 보여 주지 않으면 마들린이 펄펄 뛸 거예요. 자, 버티, 볼드 부인 보닛을 받아 드려.”\n\n그들이 위층으로 올라가 보니 시뇨라가 혼자 책을 읽고 있었다. 어딘가 슬프고 우울해 보였으나, 슬로프 씨의 가슴에 관심을 한층 더 돋우는 데 필요한 정도를 넘어서지는 않았을 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 곧 그 행복한 신사와 속삭이는 대화에 깊이 빠져들었으니, 그에게는 그녀의 소파에 앉을 자리가 허락되었다. 시뇨라에게는 오로지 그녀만의 속삭이는 법이 있었는데, 그것은 이름난 비극 배우들 사이에 통용되는 방식과는 정반대였다. 위대한 비극 배우는 숨을 죽이고 혀끝으로 빚어낸 웅얼거림을 쉿쉿 소리 나게 내뱉어 또렷한 속삭임을 만들어 내면서도 극장 전체에 들리게 한다. 그러나 시뇨라는 쉿쉿 소리를 전혀 쓰지 않았고 모든 말을 맑고 은빛 나는 음색으로 냈으되, 그 말은 그것이 부어지는 귀에만 들릴 뿐이었다.\n\n샬럿은 이리저리 방 안을 부산히 돌아다니며 이런저런 일을 하거나 하는 척했고, 그러다 어머니를 보고 오겠다는 말을 남기고 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 그리하여 엘리너는 버티와 단둘이 남겨졌는데, 한 시간이 어떻게 지나갔는지도 모를 지경이었다. 버티의 공을 제대로 인정해 주자면, 그보다 더 패를 잘 놀릴 수는 없었을 것이다. 그는 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하지도, 한숨을 쉬지도, 애타는 눈길을 보내지도 않았고, 다만 재미있고 허물없으면서도 예의를 지켰다. 그리고 새벽 한 시에 그가 그녀를 집 문 앞까지 바래다주었을 때, 덧붙이자면 이제 질투가 난 슬로프의 거들음까지 받아 가며 그리했을 때, 그녀는 그가 여태 만나 본 가장 유쾌한 남자 가운데 하나이고 스탠호프 일가는 단연 가장 유쾌한 집안이라고 생각했다.\n\n# 제20장. 애러빈 씨\n\n라자러스의 펠로이자 전임 옥스퍼드 시학 교수이며 현재 바체스터 교구의 세인트 이월드 교구 사제인 프랜시스 애러빈 목사를, 이제는 독자에게 직접 소개해야 하겠다. 그는 새 권 하나를 통째로 얻을 만한 사람이고, 앞으로 이 이야기에서 눈에 띄는 자리를 차지할 터이니, 글쓴이가 그려 낼 수 있는 만큼의 초상화를 빌려서라도 그를 독자의 눈앞에 세워 두는 편이 바람직하겠다.\n\n사람의 성품을 오차 없이 정확한 서술로 글에 옮겨 문법에 맞는 언어로 담아낼 수 있는, 정신의 다게레오타이프나 사진 같은 방법이 아직 발견되지 않았다는 것은 유감스러운 일이다. 소설가는, 아니 역사가와 전기 작가 또한, 한 인물의 성격과 사람됨을 온전히 제 마음속에 품고 제 머릿속 서판에 정확히 그려 내었다고 여기면서도, 막상 그 초상을 영구히 남기려고 펜과 잉크로 달려가는 순간 말들이 저를 저버리고 빠져나가고 실망시키며 제멋대로 굴어, 열두어 쪽을 써 놓고 보면 묘사된 사람이 마음에 품었던 사람과 닮은 정도가 길모퉁이 간판이 케임브리지 공작을 닮은 정도밖에 되지 않는다는 것을 얼마나 자주 느끼는가.\n\n그렇다고 해서 그런 기계적인 묘사 솜씨가 독자에게 주는 만족이, 사랑하는 아이의 완전한 복제를 갖고 싶어 안달하는 어머니에게 사진사의 솜씨가 주는 만족보다 그리 크지도 않을 것이다. 닮기야 참으로 닮았지만, 그것은 둔하고 죽어 있고 아무 정감도 없고 상서롭지도 못한 닮음이다. 얼굴이 거기 있는 것은 분명하고, 그것을 들여다보는 사람들은 그것이 누구의 모습인지 대번에 알아보겠지만, 정작 그 얼굴의 임자는 그 닮음을 자랑스러워하지 않을 것이다.\n\n배움에 이르는 왕도는 없고, 값진 기예를 익히는 데 지름길이란 없다. 사진사들과 다게레오타이프 기술자들이 무슨 짓을 하든, 이미 솜씨로 이루어 놓은 것 위에 솜씨를 더 얹어 아무리 개량한들, 그들은 신이 빚은 인간의 얼굴을 담은 초상만은 결코 이루어 내지 못할 것이다. 전기 작가와 소설가와 그 밖의 우리 모두가, 제 어깨에 너무 무겁다고 걸핏하면 느끼는 그 짐 밑에서 아무리 신음한다 해도, 우리는 사내답게 그 짐을 져 올리거나 아니면 스스로 떠맡은 일에 우리가 너무 약하다고 자인하는 수밖에 없다. 잘 쓰면서 동시에 쉽게 쓰는 길이란 없다.\n\nLabor omnia vincit improbus. 이것이야말로 모든 일꾼이 골라 지녀야 할 좌우명이니, 어쩌면 그 수고가 넉넉히 오래 견디기만 한다면 프랜시스 애러빈 목사의 그리 어긋나지 않은 닮은꼴 하나쯤은 끝내 만들어 낼 수 있을지도 모른다.\n\n그가 세상에서 한 일과 그가 얻은 명성의 종류에 대해서는 이미 충분히 말했다. 그가 마흔 살이고 아직 독신이라는 것도 이미 말했다. 그는 잉글랜드 북부의 재산이 넉넉지 못한 시골 신사의 둘째 아들이었다. 어린 나이에 윈체스터로 보내졌고, 아버지는 그가 뉴 칼리지로 가기를 바랐다. 그러나 소년 시절 공부를 좋아하기는 했어도 정해진 테두리 안에서 좋아하지는 않았던 터라, 열여덟에 그는 재주 있다는 평판만 얻고 장학생 자리는 얻지 못한 채 학교를 떠났다. 그 평판이라는 이점 말고 그가 손에 쥔 것이라고는 영시로 받은 금메달 하나뿐이었고, 그리하여 그의 벗들 사이에서는 그가 영국 시인들의 불멸의 명단에 이름 하나를 더 보태도록 정해진 사람이라는 강한 짐작이 생겨났다.\n\n윈체스터에서 그는 옥스퍼드로 갔고, 베일리얼의 자비생으로 등록되었다. 여기서 그만의 남다른 이력이 곧 시작되었다. 그는 방탕한 무리와의 교제를 철저히 멀리했고, 술자리를 열지 않았고, 말을 기르지 않았고, 배를 젓지 않았고, 소동에 끼어들지 않아, 칼리지 지도교수의 자랑거리였다. 적어도 예비 시험을 치를 때까지의 그의 행로는 그러했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사람으로서 그에게 덜 명예로울 것은 없으나 지도교수의 취향에는 그다지 맞지 않는 행로를 시작했다. 그는 기세 좋은 토론회의 회원이 되어 거기서 익살스러운 기백으로 이름을 떨쳤다. 늘 진지하면서도 그의 진지함은 늘 우스꽝스러웠다. 생각이 옳고, 삼단논법이 반박할 수 없고, 지향이 정당한 것만으로는 그에게 부족했다. 상대의 논거를 터무니없는 것으로 몰아 재치와 이치 양쪽으로 이기지 못한다면, 그는 실패한 것이었다. 남들이 그를 알게 된 뒤에 남들이 보기에도 그러했거니와, 무엇보다 그 자신이 보기에 그러했다. 그의 목적이 웃음을 자아내는 데 있었다고 말한다면 그보다 큰 거짓은 없을 것이다. 그는 듣는 이들이 만족을 그렇게 흔하고 불필요한 방식으로 드러내는 것을 싫어했다. 웃어 주어야만 하는 농담은 그에게 입에 올릴 값어치가 없는 것이었다. 그는 제 재치가 통했는지를 귀보다 더 예민한 감각으로 알아챌 수 있었고, 자기가 이해되고 인정되었는지 아닌지를 듣는 이들의 눈에서 읽어 냈다.\n\n그는 학교를 떠나기 전부터 신앙심 깊은 소년이었다. 다시 말해 그는 종교의 한 당파에 스스로를 붙였고, 그렇게 한 뒤에는 그런 명분에 당파적으로 가담하는 대다수 사람이 얻는 이익을 얻었다. 우리는 우리 교회의 분열을 오로지 악한 것으로만 보려는 버릇이 너무 심하다. 온건한 분열이라는 것이 있을 수 있다면, 그런 분열은 어쨌든 그 주제로 사람들의 눈길을 돌리고, 그렇지 않았으면 그 문제에 무심했을 지지자들을 끌어들이며, 사람들에게 종교를 두고 생각하는 법을 가르친다. 프루드의 유고집 간행과 함께 시작된 잉글랜드 교회의 그 운동이 이런 종류의 선을 얼마나 크게 뒤따라 낳았던가!\n\n소년 시절 어린 애러빈은 트랙트파 편에 서서 몽둥이를 들었고, 옥스퍼드에서는 한동안 저 위대한 뉴먼의 발치에 앉았다. 이 명분에 그는 제 능력을 남김없이 바쳤다. 이를 위해 시를 지었고, 이를 위해 연설을 했고, 이를 위해 그의 조용한 재치가 낼 수 있는 가장 밝은 불꽃을 튀겼다. 이를 위해 그는 먹고 마시고 옷을 입고 존재했다. 때가 되어 그는 학위를 받고 스스로를 문학사라 적었으나, 학문상의 이렇다 할 광채와 더불어 그리한 것은 아니었다. 그는 고교회파의 문제와, 고교회파에 흔히 따라붙는 논쟁과 정치와 바깥으로의 과시에 너무 많이 매달린 나머지, 양과 최우등을 얻는 데 충분한 힘을 쏟지 못했다. 그는 양과 최우등도 아니었고 일등급조차 아니었다. 그러나 그해에는 일등급이니 양과 최우등이니 하는 것을 유행에서 몰아내고, 스물셋의 나이에 사람의 머릿속에 원뿔 곡선이나 그리스어 액센트보다 더 무거운 주제가 들어설 자리를 남기지 않는 그런 현학 취미를 웃음거리로 만들어 버림으로써 대학에 앙갚음을 했다.\n\n그러나 그리스어 액센트와 원뿔 곡선은 베일리얼에서 필수로 여겨지는 것이었으니, 그곳 펠로 명단 안에는 애러빈 씨가 들어설 자리가 없었다. 그러나 옥스퍼드 교수들의 거처 가운데 가장 부유하고 가장 안락한 라자러스가 이 싸우는 교회의 젊은 투사에게 품을 열어 주었다. 애러빈 씨는 서품을 받았고, 학위를 받은 지 얼마 되지 않아 펠로가 되었으며, 그 뒤 오래지 않아 시학 교수로 뽑혔다.\n\n그리고 이제 그의 큰 위기의 순간이 닥쳤다. 숱한 마음의 다툼과, 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있는 의심의 고뇌를 겪은 끝에, 트랙트파의 저 위대한 예언자가 스스로 로마 가톨릭 교도임을 고백한 것이다. 뉴먼 씨는 잉글랜드 교회를 떠났고 흔들리던 이들 여럿을 데리고 갔다. 애러빈 씨를 데려가지는 못했으나, 그 신사가 벗어난 것은 참으로 아슬아슬한 일이었다. 그는 자신에게 거의 피할 수 없어 보이는 그 한 걸음을 완전한 평온 속에서 곱씹어 보려고 한동안 옥스퍼드를 떠났고, 우리나라에서 가장 외진 주들 가운데 한 곳의 바닷가 작은 마을에 스스로를 가두었다. 제 영혼과 마주 이야기함으로써, 자기가 어머니 교회의 울타리 안에 양심을 상하지 않고 남아 있을 수 있는지 없는지를 배워 보려는 것이었다.\n\n그가 그곳에서 오로지 저 혼자에게만 맡겨졌더라면 사정은 나쁘게 흘러갔을 것이다. 모든 것이 그에게 불리했다. 그의 세속적 이해관계는 모조리 그가 프로테스탄트로 남아 있기를 요구했는데, 그는 제 세속적 이해관계를 한 무리의 적으로 보았고 그들을 이기는 것을 더없이 명예로운 일로 여겼다. 황홀한 고뇌에 잠긴 그때의 그로서는 그런 승리쯤 별 대가가 들지 않았을 것이고, 생계 전부를 내던지는 일쯤이야 쉽게 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 잉글랜드 교회를 택함으로써 자기가 그런 선택으로 이끌린 것은 떳떳지 못한 동기 때문이었다는 비난에 제 마음속에서 스스로 열려 있게 되리라는 생각, 그 생각을 넘어서는 데에는 큰 대가가 들었다. 그다음으로 그의 마음이 그에게 불리했다. 그는 그때까지 제 인도자였던 사람을 강하고 간절한 사랑으로 사랑했고, 그 발자취를 따르기를 애타게 바랐다. 그의 취향이 그에게 불리했다. 로마 교회의 예식과 화려함, 그 장엄한 축일과 엄숙한 금식은 그의 상상을 부르고 그의 눈을 즐겁게 했다. 그의 육신이 그에게 불리했다. 가난하고 약하고 흔들리는 사람에게, 규정이 분명하여 크고 뚜렷하고 명백한 죄를 짓지 않고서는 어길 수 없는 법으로 높은 도덕적 의무와 극기와 순종과 정결을 강제받는다는 것이 얼마나 큰 도움이 되겠는가! 그다음으로 그의 신앙이 그에게 불리했다. 그는 너무나 많은 것을 믿어야 했고, 제 믿음의 표를 내보이기를 그토록 간절히 바랐으며, 그저 요단강 물에 몸을 씻는 것만으로는 턱없이 부족하다고 여겼기에, 참된 교회를 위하여 모든 것을 버린다는 따위의 큰 행실에는 그로서는 거의 뿌리치기 어려운 유혹이 담겨 있었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 그때 아주 젊은 사람이었고, 저 먼 은거지로 가려고 옥스퍼드를 떠날 무렵에는 제 검술 실력을 너무 믿었고 보통 사람들의 보통 분별을 너무 얕보는 편이어서, 자기가 골라잡은 그 고장에 어쩌다 사는 이들에게서 싸움에 도움을 받으리라고는 기대하지 않았다. 그러나 섭리는 그에게 어지셨다. 그 황량하다시피 한 곳, 저 멀고 먼 바다의 폭풍에 시달리는 물가에서 그는 한 사람을 만났으니, 그이가 차츰 그의 마음을 가라앉히고 그의 상상을 잠재우며 그리스도인의 본분이 무엇인지를 얼마간 가르쳐 주었다. 애러빈 씨가 옥스퍼드를 떠날 때만 해도 그는 잉글랜드 대다수 교구의 시골 성직자들을 거의 경멸에 가까운 눈으로 보는 편이었다. 그가 그들 교회의 우리 안에 남는다면, 그들의 열등함을 얼마간 되살리고 바로잡는 데 힘을 보태며, 열정도 믿음도 별로 내보이지 않은 채 한평생을 지나는 것으로 곧잘 만족한다고 그가 생각하던 그리스도의 종들의 가슴에 열정과 믿음을 불어넣는 일을 거드는 것이 그의 포부였다.\n\n그런데도 애러빈 씨가 더없이 절박했을 때 그토록 절실히 필요로 하던 도움을 받은 것은 바로 그런 사람에게서였다. 그리스도인의 본분을 다스리는 가장 높은 법은 밖에서가 아니라 안에서 작용해야 한다는 것, 글로 적힌 명령에 복종하는 것만으로 쓸모 있는 종이 될 수 있는 사람은 없다는 것, 그리고 그가 로마의 성문 안에서 구하려 하던 그 안전이란 싸움 전날 밤에 꾀병을 부려 몸을 사리는 못난 병사가 얻어 내려는 이기적인 위험 모면과 다를 바 없다는 것을, 그는 콘월의 작은 교구의 가난한 부목사에게서 처음 배웠다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 더 겸손해진, 그러나 더 나아지고 더 행복해진 사람이 되어 옥스퍼드로 돌아왔고, 그때부터 자기가 그 안에서 교육받은 교회의 성직자로서 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대었다. 그가 스스럼없이 어울려 지내는 이들과의 교제는 그가 늘 속해 있던 그 교회 체계의 원칙에 그를 굳게 붙들어 두었다. 뉴먼 씨와 갈라선 뒤로 그에게 그토록 강한 영향을 끼친 사람은 그가 속한 칼리지의 수장 말고는 없었다. 그가 배교하리라 여겨지던 시절에 그윈 박사는 그 젊은 펠로를 그리 좋게 볼 마음이 없었다. 그윈 박사 자신도 온건한 테두리 안에서는 고교회파였으나, 39개조 신조로 제 믿음을 채우지 못하는 사람들에게는 아무 공감도 느끼지 않았다. 그는 뉴먼 같은 이들의 열광을 종교라기보다 광기에 더 가까운 정신 상태로 여겼고, 아주 젊은 사람들이 그런 열광을 내보이는 것을 볼 때면 그 상당 부분을 허영 탓으로 돌리는 편이었다. 그윈 박사 자신은 신앙심 있는 사람이면서도 철저히 실제적인 세상 사람이기도 했으니, 그 둘을 양립할 수 없는 것으로 보는 이의 주장이라면 어떤 것이든 곱게 보지 않았다. 애러빈 씨가 반쯤 로마 쪽 사람이라는 것을 알았을 때, 그는 그토록 자격 없는 이에게 펠로 자리를 주려고 자기가 애쓴 일을 죄다 후회하기 시작했다. 그리고 애러빈 씨가 아마도 로마까지 가는 길을 끝내 다 갈 모양이라는 말을 다시 들었을 때는, 그렇게 되면 그 펠로 자리가 다시 비게 되리라는 사실을 얼마간 흡족한 마음으로 바라보았다.\n\n그러나 애러빈 씨가 돌아와 스스로를 확고한 프로테스탄트라고 밝히자, 라자러스 학장은 다시 그에게 팔을 벌렸고, 그는 차츰 칼리지의 귀염둥이가 되었다. 얼마 동안 그는 침울하고 말이 없었으며 대학의 다툼에서 눈에 띄는 몫을 맡기를 꺼렸다. 그러나 차츰 그의 마음은 제 가락을 되찾았고, 아니 오히려 제 가락을 새로 빚어냈고, 그는 복음주의 냄새가 나는 것이라면 무엇에든 맞서 당장이라도 몽둥이를 들 채비가 되어 있는 사람으로 알려지게 되었다. 그는 설교에서 뛰어났고, 연단에서 뛰어났고, 만찬 뒤의 담소에서 뛰어났으며, 뛰어난 만큼 늘 유쾌하기도 했다. 그는 선거를 즐겼고, 위원회에 몸담았고, 대학 개혁이라는 계획이란 계획에는 이를 악물고 맞섰으며, 포트와인 잔을 기울이며 교회에 닥치리라 예상되는 파멸과 휘그당이 날마다 저지르는 신성 모독을 두고 흥겹게 떠들었다. 로마라는 귀부인의 온갖 유혹을 물리치면서 그가 겪은 그 시련은 분명 그의 성품을 굳히는 데 크게 이바지했다. 자잘하고 겉으로 드러나는 일에서는 그가 제법 자신만만했으나, 그럼에도 속사람에 관계된 일에서는 그 콘월 해안을 다녀오지 않았더라면 결코 마음이 끌리지 않았을 영혼의 겸손을 목표로 삼았다. 그 방문을 그는 이제 해마다 되풀이했다.\n\n세인트 이월드의 성직록을 받아들일 무렵의 애러빈 씨를 안에서 들여다보면 이러했다. 겉으로는 그는 눈에 띄는 사람이 아니었다. 중키보다 크고, 몸이 잘 짜였으며, 매우 활동적이었다. 새까맣던 머리털은 이제 잿빛이 섞였으나, 얼굴에는 세월의 자취가 없었다. 그를 잘생겼다고 하는 것은 아마 틀린 말이겠으나, 그럼에도 그의 얼굴은 바라보기에 좋았다. 광대뼈는 아름답다고 하기에는 좀 지나치게 높았고, 이마의 생김새는 지나치게 육중하고 무거웠다. 그러나 눈과 코와 입은 나무랄 데가 없었다. 그의 눈가에는 잔불이 끊임없이 어른거려, 그가 입을 열려 할 때마다 애틋함이든 익살이든 무언가를 약속했고, 그 약속이 깨지는 일은 드물었다. 입가에는 부드러운 움직임이 있어, 그의 재치가 결코 빈정거림으로 내려앉지 않으며 그의 응수에는 심술이 없다는 것을 말해 주었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 여자들에게 인기 있는 사람이었으나, 어느 한 사람의 총아라기보다는 두루 사랑받는 편이었다. 옥스퍼드에서 펠로로 사는 터라 그에게 결혼이란 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었고, 그가 제 마음을 누구에게 건드리게 허락한 적이 한 번이라도 있었는지는 의심스럽다. 성직자에게 독신이 요구되는 몫이 아닌 교회에 속해 있으면서도, 그는 스스로를 홀아비로 지내는 것이 거의 필수인 그런 성직자 가운데 하나로 여기게 되었다. 그는 교구의 직무를 바란 적이 없었고, 옥스퍼드에서의 그의 이력은 아내와 아이 방 같은 가정의 기쁨과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것이었다. 그러므로 그는 여자들을 로마 교회의 많은 사제들이 여자를 바라보는 것과 같은 눈으로 보았다. 그는 예쁘고 재미있는 것을 가까이 두기를 좋아했으나, 여자들이란 대체로 그에게 아이들보다 그리 나을 것이 없었다. 그는 제 힘을 다 내지 않은 채 그들과 이야기했고, 그들에게서 듣는 말이 제 행동을 움직이거나 제 의견에 영향을 주리라고는 생각조차 하지 않은 채 그들의 말을 들었다.\n\n세인트 이월드의 새 교구 사제이자 이제 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서 그랜틀리 일가와 함께 지내려 하는 애러빈 씨는 이러한 사람이었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨와 엘리너보다 하루 먼저 플럼스테드에 닿았고, 덕분에 그랜틀리 집안은 다른 손님들이 오기 전에 그와 인사를 나누고 그의 됨됨이를 두고 이야기할 수 있었다. 그리젤은 그가 그토록 젊어 보이는 것에 놀랐으나, 밤에 물러가 잠자리에 들면서 동생 플로린다에게 그가 조금도 젊은 사람처럼 말하지는 않는다고 일러 주었다. 그리고 열일곱이 열여섯에게 지니는 권위를 담아, 눈은 사랑스럽지만 그 사람은 조금도 근사하지 않다고 판정했다. 여느 때처럼 열여섯은 그런 문제에서 열일곱의 판결에 두말없이 따랐고, 그가 확실히 근사하지 않다고 말했다. 그러고 나서 둘은 이웃에 사는 다른 독신 성직자들의 상대적 장점으로 이야기를 옮겨 갔고, 서로 아무 시샘도 없이, 어거스터스 그린이라는 목사가 그 무리 가운데 단연 가장 높이 살 만한 사람이라고 뜻을 모았다. 문제의 신사에게는 유리한 점이 분명 많았으니, 아버지에게서 넉넉한 용돈을 받는 터라 부목사직에서 나오는 수입을 통째로 보랏빛 장갑과 나무랄 데 없는 넥타이에 쏟아부을 수 있었던 것이다. 이렇게 새로 온 사람에게는 그 드높은 그린의 우월함을 흔들 만한 구석이 하나도 없다고 굳게 결론을 내린 뒤, 두 소녀는 서로를 껴안은 채 저희 자신과 세상에 흡족해하며 잠이 들었다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인도 첫눈에 남편이 아끼는 이 사람에 대해 딸들과 거의 같은 결론에 이르렀다. 다만 그의 상대적 값어치를 재려 하면서 그를 그린 씨와 견주지는 않았고, 사실 그와 다른 누구의 이름을 대어 견주는 일 자체를 하지 않았다. 그저 남편에게 어떤 사람의 백조가 다른 사람에게는 흔히 거위이더라고 한마디 했을 뿐인데, 그것은 애러빈 씨가 아직 자기가 만족할 만큼 백조다움을 증명해 보이지 못했다는 뜻을 분명히 드러내는 말이었다.\n\n“이보시오, 수전.” 벗이 그렇게 함부로 이야기되는 것을 듣고 적잖이 언짢아진 그가 말했다. “당신이 애러빈 씨를 거위로 친다면, 나로서는 당신의 안목을 그리 높이 평가한다고 말할 수가 없구려.”\n\n“거위라뇨! 아뇨, 물론 그이는 거위가 아니지요. 아주 영리한 분인 것은 저도 의심하지 않아요. 하지만 부주교님은 필요하다 싶으면 어찌나 곧이곧대로 받아들이시는지, 말이라는 게 그저 façon de parler라는 걸 도무지 믿고 쓸 수가 없다니까요. 애러빈 씨가 아주 값진 사람인 것도, 옥스퍼드에서는 말이지요, 세인트 이월드의 좋은 교구 사제가 되리라는 것도 저는 의심하지 않아요. 제 말은 그저 하룻저녁을 함께 지내 본 것으로는 그이가 아주 완벽한 본보기라고까지는 느껴지지 않는다는 거예요. 우선 첫째로, 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그이는 좀 잘난 체하는 구석이 있어요.”\n\n“내가 가깝게 아는 사람들을 통틀어,” 부주교가 말했다. “애러빈만큼 자만의 티가 조금도 없는 사람은 없다고 나는 생각하오. 그의 흠이라면 지나치게 스스로를 낮춘다는 것이지.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지요.” 부인이 말했다. “다만 저는 오늘 저녁에 그걸 알아보지 못했다는 것만은 인정해야겠어요.”\n\n그에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 자기가 애러빈 씨를 칭찬했다는 그 이유만으로 아내가 그를 헐뜯는 것이라고 생각했고, 그랜틀리 부인은 부주교가 이미 강한 의견을 내놓은 사람을 두고 그를 편들어서든 거슬러서든 왈가왈부해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n사실 두 사람 다 옳았다. 애러빈 씨는 가깝게 알지 못하는 이들과 어울리는 자리에서는 스스로를 낮추는 사람이었다. 제가 맡아 채워야 할 자리에 놓이고 제가 마땅히 통달해 있어야 할 문제를 논할 때라면, 애러빈 씨는 몸에 밴 대로 얼굴빛 하나 바뀌지 않을 만큼 뻔뻔했다. 엑서터 홀의 연단에 설 때면 앞에 늘어선 군중의 눈길에 그만큼 덜 얼떨떨해할 사람도 없었으니, 그것이야말로 그의 직분이 그에게 요구해 온 일이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 여느 사교 자리에서 의견을 강하게 드러내기를 꺼렸고, 그렇게 하다 보니 그가 그 자리에 모인 사람들을 제 기운을 쏟을 만한 값어치가 없다고 여기는 것처럼 보이는 일이 드물지 않았다. 그는 그 자리가 단정 지어 말할 만하다고 여겨지지 않을 때 단정 지어 말하기를 꺼렸고, 사람들이 그의 말을 듣고 싶어 하는 주제란 하필 그가 늘 딱 잘라 다루어 오던 것들이었으므로, 그는 자기를 논쟁으로 꾀어내려고 놓인 덫을 대개 피해 다녔으며, 그리함으로써 그랜틀리 부인이 그에게 씌운 것과 같은 혐의를 심심찮게 뒤집어쓰곤 했다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 열어 둔 창가에 앉아 감미로운 달빛을 즐기며, 목사관 뜰 안이나 다름없는 자리에 서 있는 교회의 잿빛 탑들을 바라보면서, 자기가 그토록 여러 우호적이거나 비우호적인 품평의 대상이 되었으리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 않았다. 우리 모두가 남의 성품을 논하기를 얼마나 좋아하는지, 게다가 그것을 논할 때 자비의 정신을 그리 엄격히 지키지 않는 경우가 얼마나 잦은지를 생각하면, 남들도 우리를 심술궂게 말할 수 있다는 데에는 우리가 어쩌면 그토록 생각이 미치지 않는지, 그리고 그들이 그리했다는 증거가 우리 귀에 들어오면 우리가 어쩌면 그토록 성내고 상처받는지, 참으로 묘한 일이다. 우리는 누구나 이따금 가장 소중한 벗들을 두고 그 벗들이 제 이야기가 그렇게 오르내리는 것을 결코 달가워하지 않을 투로 말하면서도, 정작 그 가장 소중한 벗들은 우리 흠에는 죄다 눈이 멀고 우리 덕의 아무리 옅은 결에도 예민한 사람인 양 언제나 우리를 이야기해 주기를 기대한다고 말한들, 지나친 말은 아닐 것이다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 자기가 입에 오르내린다는 생각조차 하지 않았다. 자신을 이 집 주인과 견주어 볼 때, 그는 자기가 누구에게든 하도 대수롭지 않은 사람이라 아무의 말이나 생각을 받을 값어치도 없다고 여겨졌다. 그는 가정의 인연으로 보나, 남편과 아내나 부모와 자식이나 형제자매 사이가 아니고서는 거의 있을 수 없는 저 안쪽의 스스럼없는 관계로 보나, 이 세상에 철저히 혼자였다. 사람이 이 세상에서 행복하려면 그런 유대가 꼭 있어야 하는가를 그는 스스로와 자주 따져 보았고, 이 세상에서의 행복이란 꼭 있어야 하는 것이 아니라는 대답으로 대개 스스로를 달래 왔다. 이 점에서 그는 스스로를 속였다. 아니, 속이려고 애썼다는 편이 옳겠다. 그도 남들처럼 눈에 즐거워 보이는 것이면 무엇이든 누리고 싶어 애가 탔고, 그토록 많은 그리스도인이 지닌 저 현대의 극기로써 기쁨과 슬픔이란 이 세상에서 아주 대수롭지 않게 여겨야 할 문제라고 스스로 믿어 보려 애썼으나, 그런 것들은 그에게 대수롭지 않지 않았다. 그는 옥스퍼드의 제 방과 칼리지 생활에 지쳐 있었다. 그는 벗의 아내와 아이들을 부러움에 가까운 마음으로 바라보았고, 잔디밭과 화단으로 열린 예쁜 창이 달린 그 정겨운 응접실과, 안락한 집의 온갖 살림과, 무엇보다도 그 모든 것을 감싸고 있는 가정의 공기를 탐내다시피 했다.\n\n이제 막 시골 교구를, 들과 뜰 사이의 성직록을, 아내가 들어와 빛낼 만한 집을 손에 넣은 지금이야말로 그런 바람이 그에게 그토록 어울리는 때는 없었으리라고 사람들은 말할 것이다. 플럼스테드의 넉넉함과 세인트 이월드의 검소한 살림 사이에 차이가 있는 것은 사실이나, 애러빈 씨야 설마 재물을 두고 한숨지을 사람이던가! 모든 사람 가운데서도 그가 그럴 사람으로는 가장 거리가 멀다고 그의 벗들은 입을 모아 잘라 말했을 것이다. 그러나 우리 벗들이 우리를 아는 것이 얼마나 얕은가! 이 세상의 행복을 극기의 마음으로 물리치던 시절에 그는 재산에 대한 근심이라면 죄다 찌꺼기로 여겨 제게서 내던져 버렸다. 그는 이를테면 스스로 승진에 무심하다고 선언한 셈이었고, 그의 재능을 누구보다 아끼며 그 재능에 마땅한 보답이 돌아가도록 앞장서 애썼을 이들은 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다. 그런데 이제, 사실대로 밝혀야 한다면, 그는 스스로 실망하고 있었다. 그들에게 실망한 것이 아니라 자신에게 실망한 것이다. 젊은 날의 백일몽은 끝났고, 마흔의 나이에 그는 자기가 사도의 정신으로 일하기에 걸맞은 사람이 아니라고 느꼈다. 그는 스스로를 잘못 보았고, 그 잘못을 깨달았을 때는 이미 손쓸 수 없는 뒤였다. 그는 주교의 관에도, 수석사제관에도, 넉넉한 성직록과 기름진 교회 땅에도 무심하다고 공언해 왔건만, 이제는 제가 우쭐한 마음으로 감히 내려다보았던 다른 사람들의 그 좋은 것들을 두고 한숨짓고 있음을 스스로 인정해야 했다.\n\n속된 뜻의 재물을 그가 탐하여 한숨지은 적은 결코 없었고, 값진 물건을 누려 보겠다고 애태운 적도 결코 없었다. 다만 아내와 아이들과 행복한 가정이 그에게 줄 수 있었을 저 몫으로 정해진 세상의 복, 제게는 필요 없다며 감히 물리쳐 버렸던 저 남들만큼의 안락함, 그것만은 찾아 나섰더라면 더 지혜로웠으리라고 그는 이제 느끼고 있었다.\n\n그는 제 재능과 제 자리와 제 벗들이 있으니 승진을 얻어 낼 길로 스스로를 세우기만 했더라면 승진을 얻어 냈으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그런데 그리하는 대신 그는 설득에 넘어가, 장가를 들어 펠로 자리를 내놓을 경우 연 300파운드쯤의 수입을 안겨 줄 성직록을 받아들이고 말았다. 세상이 성공했다고 여기기로 한 그 수고의 세속적 결실이란 마흔의 나이에 그러했다. 세상은 또한 애러빈 씨가 제 생각으로는 넉넉히 보상받았다고 여겼다. 아아, 아아! 세상이 잘못 안 것이었고, 애러빈 씨는 사정이 그러하다는 것을 이제 막 알아차리기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n그리고 여기서 독자에게 청하거니와, 이 사람을 두고 모질게 판단하지는 말아 달라. 그가 이른 이 상태란 인간의 조건이 아닌 것에 닿아 보려는 노력에서 자연히 따라 나오는 결과가 아니겠는가? 현대의 극기는, 비록 그리스도교 위에 세워졌다고는 하나, 옛사람들의 극기가 그러했던 것만큼이나 인간 본성에 대한 큰 횡포가 아니겠는가? 제논의 철학은 참된 법칙 위에 세워졌으나, 잘못 이해되고 따라서 잘못 적용된 참된 법칙 위에 세워진 것이었다. 재물과 세상의 안락과 이 땅에서의 행복은 찾아 나설 값어치가 없다고 우리에게 가르치려 드는 이곳의 우리 스토아 학자들도 마찬가지다. 아아, 믿고 배울 제자도 없고 참되이 가르칠 스승도 없는 저 가르침이여!\n\n애러빈 씨의 경우는 더욱 유별났으니, 그가 속한 잉글랜드 교회의 갈래는 제 세속 재산을 드러내 놓고 호의로 바라보는 쪽이었고, 그 자신 세상의 안락을 넉넉히 누리는 데 익숙한 사람들과 늘 어울려 살아왔기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 기질이 유별난 탓에 바로 그런 사정이 도리어 그의 안에, 그것도 젊은 시절에, 그에게 본래 어울리지 않는 마음 상태를 낳고 말았다. 그는 고교회파가 되는 것에 만족하기는 하되 제 나름의 원칙 위에서 그리할 수 있고, 함께 어울리는 이들과 뚜렷이 다른 길을 스스로 내어 갈 수 있어야 했다. 그는 당파의 일원이 될 채비가 되어 있었으나 그것은 제 당파와 다른 행동과 생각의 길을 허락받는 동안뿐이었다. 그의 당파는 그를 너그러이 봐주었고, 그가 제 당파는 옳고 제가 그르다고 느끼기 시작한 것은 하필 그런 확신이 그에게 아무 도움도 되지 못할 만큼 때늦었을 무렵이었다. 그런 깨달음이 더는 쓸모없어진 뒤에야 그는 깨달았다. 이 세상에서 일에 매겨지는 여느 삯을 받으려고 일하여 아내와 아이들을, 그리고 그들을 태울 마차를 벌어 두는 편이, 벗들이 제 포도주를 마실 수 있는 정겨운 식당을 벌어 두고, 제 고장 읍의 큰길을 걸어갈 때 상인이란 상인이 죄다 제 가게 안으로 기꺼이 맞아들이리라는 것을 알고 걸을 수 있는 힘을 벌어 두는 편이 제게도 값어치 있는 일이었으리라고. 다른 사람들은 인생의 출발점에서 그런 확신에 이르러 그것을 향해 일해 나간다. 그에게 그 확신은 쓸모가 있기에는 너무 늦게 찾아왔다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 유쾌한 사람이라는 말을 앞에서 했거니와, 앞서 말한 것과 같은 마음 상태는 익살과 서로 어긋나는 것이라고 여겨질지도 모르겠다. 그러나 분명 그렇지 않다. 재치란 사람의 정신을 감싼 겉껍데기이니, 제단에 선 사제의 값진 비단 예복이 그 아래 거친 삼베옷에 살갗이 쓸리고 매질에 몸이 반쯤 벗겨진 은수자의 고행과 관계가 없는 것처럼, 재치도 생각과 감정이 자리한 속마음과는 관계가 없다. 아니, 그런 사람일수록 제 겉옷의 화려한 자태를 누구보다 더 기뻐하는 일이 잦지 않던가? 겉으로는 빛나면서 속으로는 신음하고 있다고 느끼는 것이 그의 자부심에 먹이가 되지 않던가? 사람이 기울이는 정신의 노력도 그와 같다. 그들이 날마다 세상에 내보이는 노력은 흔히 그 영혼 안쪽에서 일어나는 일의 정반대다.\n\n부주교의 응접실에서 애러빈 씨는 여느 때와 다름없이 꾸밈없는 광채로 반짝였다. 그러나 침실로 물러가 열어 둔 창가에 앉았을 때 그는 서글펐고, 자기에게도 아내가 없고 아이들이 없으며, 누워 볼 만하게 알맞추 깎아 놓은 부드러운 잔디밭도 없고, 곁에서 시중드는 부목사 떼도 없고, 은행원들의 굽실거림도 없고, 넉넉한 목사관도 없다는 것을 속으로 한탄했다. 그가 마음에 그리던 그 사도직은 그의 손아귀를 빠져나갔고, 이제 그는 주교의 관에 마음이 있으면서도 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제일 뿐이었다. 참으로 그는 두 걸상 사이에 주저앉은 꼴이었다.\n\n# 제21장. 세인트 이월드 목사관\n\n이튿날 아침 하딩 씨와 볼드 부인이 목사관에 이르렀을 때, 부주교와 그의 벗은 세인트 이월드에 가 있었다. 새 교구 사제가 제 교회를 둘러보고 지주에게 소개받도록 하려고 건너간 것이어서, 저녁 식사 전에는 돌아오지 않을 참이었다. 하딩 씨는 혼자 어슬렁어슬렁 나가 플럼스테드에서 늘 그러듯 잔디밭을 거닐고 교회 둘레를 돌았다. 그러는 사이 두 자매는 자연히 바체스터 이야기로 접어들었다.\n\n두 자매 사이에 속을 터놓는 정이 그리 두터운 것은 아니었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 엘리너보다 열 살이 위였고, 엘리너가 아직 어린아이였을 적에 이미 시집을 갔다. 그러므로 둘은 서로의 귀에 제 소망이며 사랑을 쏟아부어 본 적이 한 번도 없었으며, 이제 하나는 아내가 되고 하나는 미망인이 된 마당에 새삼 그런 일을 시작할 성싶지도 않았다. 자매 사이의 흉허물 없음을 거의 필연으로 만드는 그런 왕래를 나누기에는 둘은 너무 떨어져 살았다. 게다가 열여덟 살에는 그토록 쉬운 일이 스물여덟 살에는 흔히 몹시 어려운 법이다. 그랜틀리 부인도 그것을 알았으므로 동생에게서 속내를 기대하지는 않았다. 그러면서도 정말로 슬로프 씨가 동생의 마음에 드는 것인지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n화제를 슬로프 씨 쪽으로 돌리기란 조금도 어려운 일이 아니었다. 그 신사는 바체스터에서 어찌나 이름이 났는지, 그 도시에 연고를 둔 성직자란 성직자와는 모두 얽혀 있었고, 특히 하딩 씨의 일에는 유독 깊이 관여하고 있었으니, 하딩 씨의 딸들이 그 이야기를 하지 않았다면 오히려 이상했을 것이다. 그랜틀리 부인은 이내 그를 헐뜯기 시작했고 그것도 온 마음을 다해 헐뜯었으며, 볼드 부인은 그에 못지않게 열심히 그를 두둔했다. 엘리너는 그 사내가 분명히 싫었고, 그가 어디론가 사라져 다시는 볼 일이 없게 되었다는 소식을 들었다면 기뻐했을 것이며, 실은 그를 거의 두려워하기까지 했다. 그런데도 정신을 차려 보면 늘 그의 편을 들고 있었다. 남들이 그를 헐뜯는 것, 그것도 부당하다고 느껴지는 투로 헐뜯는 것이 엘리너에게 그런 노릇을 떠맡겼고, 마침내 슬로프 씨를 변호하는 일은 그의 입에 붙은 논법이 되고 말았다.\n\n슬로프 씨에서 이야기는 스탠호프 일가로 옮아갔다. 그랜틀리 부인이 그 집안에 대한 엘리너의 설명을 자못 흥미롭게 듣고 있는데, 그 자리에 슬로프 씨도 한몫 끼여 있었다는 말이 무심코 튀어나왔다.\n\n“뭐라고?” 목사관의 안주인이 말했다. “슬로프 씨도 거기 있었단 말이냐?”\n\n엘리너는 그저 그랬노라고만 대답했다.\n\n“얘, 엘리너, 내가 보기엔 그 사람이 너를 무척 좋아하는 게 틀림없구나. 네가 어디를 가든 따라다니는 것 같으니 말이다.”\n\n이런 말에도 엘리너의 눈은 뜨이지 않았다. 그저 웃으며, 슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 다른 끌림을 찾은 모양이더라고 말했을 뿐이다. 그리고 두 사람은 그렇게 헤어졌다. 그랜틀리 부인은 그 꼴사나운 혼사가 기어이 이루어지고야 말리라고 굳게 믿게 되었고, 볼드 부인은 그 딱한 전속 사제가 밉살스러운 인물임은 인정할 수밖에 없다 해도 지은 죄보다 뒤집어쓴 죄가 더 많은 사람이라고 그만큼 굳게 믿게 되었다.\n\n부주교는 물론 저녁 식사 전에 이미 들어 알고 있었다. 엘리너가 전날 바체스터에 남은 것이 슬로프 씨를 만날 요량에서였다는 것, 그리고 실제로 만났다는 것을. 그는 엘리너가 스탠호프 댁에는 손님이 없을 것이라고 딱 잘라 말하던 일을 떠올렸고, 서슴없이 그를 속임수로 몰았다. 더구나 그런 일로 속임수를 썼다는 사실은, 아니 썼으리라는 짐작은, 슬로프 씨를 연인으로 받아들였다는 그 죄목에 대하여 너무나도 뚜렷한 증거가 되어 그에게 불리하게 말하고 있었다.\n\n“우리가 무슨 수를 쓰든 이미 늦은 것이 아닌가 싶소.” 부주교가 말했다. “솔직히 말해 나도 적잖이 놀랐소. 당신 동생이 사내를 보는 눈이야 전부터 마음에 든 적이 없었지만, 그래도 설마 그 정도까지야 하고 보아 주었는데, 어휴!”\n\n“게다가 이렇게 빨리요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그는 어쩌면 동생이 슬로프 씨 같은 사내를 연인으로 삼은 그 안목보다, 상복을 채 벗기도 전에 연인을 두었다는 그 무례함을 더 마음에 두고 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n“여보, 나도 모질게 굴고 싶지는 않고, 장인어른 마음을 상하게 할 일은 더더욱 하고 싶지 않소. 그러나 분명히 말해 두거니와, 그 사내도 그 사내의 아내도 내 집 문턱은 넘지 못하오.”\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 한숨을 쉬고는, 어쨌든 아직 일이 다 된 것은 아니지 않으냐는 말로 제 자신과 지아비를 달래 보려 했다. 엘리너가 플럼스테드에 와 있는 지금, 그 치명적인 연정에서 동생을 떼어 놓을 방도야 얼마든지 있을 터였다. 가엾은 엘리너!\n\n그날 저녁은 이렇다 할 일 없이 지나갔다. 애러빈 씨는 부주교와 세인트 이월드 교구에 관해 이야기를 나누었고, 그 교구 사람들을 아는 그랜틀리 부인과 하딩 씨도 이야기에 끼어들었다. 엘리너도 그들을 알았으나 말수는 적었다. 애러빈 씨는 겉으로 보아 엘리너에게 그리 마음을 두지 않았고, 엘리너 역시 그때는 형부가 각별히 아끼는 사람을 각별히 상냥하게 대할 기분이 아니었다. 침실에 들어서면서 맨 먼저 든 생각은, 이 목사관보다 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 훨씬 즐거운 가족 모임을 만날 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 엘리너는 성직자들과 그 점잖고 단조롭고 지루한 살림살이가 슬슬 지겨워지고 있다는 것, 그리고 결국 이탈리아나 런던이나 그 밖의 어디에서 살아 본 바깥세상 사람들을 반드시 흉악하고 가증스럽다고 여길 것까지는 없다는 것을 생각하게 되었다. 스탠호프 일가는 경박하고 생각 없고 헤픈 사람들이라고 여겨 왔으나, 정작 그릇된 구석은 하나도 보지 못했고, 오히려 제 집을 즐겁게 만드는 법만은 훤히 알고 있음을 알게 되었다. 부주교에게 그런 savoir vivre가 조금이라도 있었더라면 좋았으련만, 하고 엘리너는 생각했다. 애러빈 씨는 앞서 말했듯 겉으로 보아 엘리너에게 그리 마음을 두지 않았으나, 그래도 무척 어여쁜 여인과 한자리에 있었다는 느낌 없이 잠자리에 들지는 않았다. 그리고 대개의 독신 남자들이, 또 더러는 아내 있는 남자들까지도 그러하듯, 무척 어여쁜 여인이 그 한 달을 함께 지내리라는 것을 알고 나자 플럼스테드에서 보낼 한 달의 방문을 한결 유쾌한 빛으로 바라보게 되었다.\n\n모두 물러가기 전에, 이튿날 일행 전부가 마차로 세인트 이월드의 목사관을 살펴보러 가기로 정해졌다. 세 성직자는 낡아 허물어진 데를 의논하고, 두 부인은 독신자의 거처에 필요할 법한 손질을 일러 주어 거들기로 했다.\n\n그리하여 아침을 마치고 얼마 지나지 않아 마차가 문 앞에 대령했다. 안에는 네 사람 자리밖에 없었으므로 부주교가 마부석에 올랐다. 엘리너는 애러빈 씨와 마주 앉게 되었고, 따라서 어쩔 수 없이 그와 이야기를 나누게 되었다. 두 사람은 이내 마음 편한 사이가 되었으니, 만일 엘리너가 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보았더라면 검은 성직자 옷을 입었을망정 애러빈 씨라면 스탠호프 댁 모임에 끼어도 나쁘지 않겠다고 여겼을 것이다.\n\n부주교가 자리에 없으니 다들 마음 놓고 실없는 소리를 할 수 있었다. 먼저 하딩 씨가 세상에 다시없이 천진한 태도로 애러빈 씨의 새 교구에 얽힌 옛 전설 하나를 들려주었다. 옛날 옛적에 세인트 이월드에는 온갖 병을 고쳐 준다 하여 온 고을에 이름이 자자하던 여사제가 있었다고 한다. 여사제라면 예로부터 다들 그러했듯 그에게도 우물이 하나 있었는데, 그 우물은 오늘날까지도 남아 있어서 여러 사람의 마음속에서는 교구 교회의 축성된 땅에 깃든 거룩함을 함께 나누어 지니고 있다는 것이었다. 애러빈 씨는 제 교구민들이 그런 믿음을 품고 있다면 도무지 정통이라고는 볼 수 없겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그러자 그랜틀리 부인은 자기는 생각이 전혀 달라서, 사제 말고 여사제까지 갖추지 못한 교구는 온전한 상태라 할 수 없다고 본다고 대답했다. “일이란 그렇게 나누어 맡지 않고서는 결코 제대로 되는 법이 없지요.” 부인은 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“아버지, 옛날에는 여사제가 권세를 죄다 혼자 쥐고 있었던 모양이지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “애러빈 씨는 아마 지금도 교구 안에 거룩한 여인을 들였다가는 너무 그렇게 되어 버릴까 봐 걱정하시는가 봐요.”\n\n“적어도 저로서는,” 그가 말했다. “그런 위험은 아예 무릅쓰지 않는 편이 안전하다고 봅니다. 사제의 교만이라 한들 여사제의 교만을 넘어선 적은 없었으니까요. 아주 미천한 부목사쯤이야 어찌어찌 다스려 볼 수 있겠지만, 여자 부목사라면 틀림없이 저를 눌러 이길 겁니다.”\n\n“그런 사고가 실제로 일어난 예가 분명히 있지요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “바체스터에도 제단에 관한 일이라면 무엇에나 서슬이 퍼런 여사제가 하나 있다고들 하더군요. 애러빈 씨 눈앞에 어른거리는 것도 어쩌면 그런 신세가 될까 하는 두려움인지 모르겠어요.”\n\n목사관 앞 자갈길에서 부주교와 합류하자 일행은 다시 근엄한 따분함으로 내려앉았다. 그랜틀리 부주교가 따분한 사람이라는 말은 아니다. 다만 그의 익살은 어딘가 묵직한 종류였고, 재치를 부릴 때에도 그 재치가 듣는 사람에게까지 미치는 일은 대체로 없었다. 이날도 그는 이내 상처 입은 지붕이며 벽에 관한 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작하여, 이것들이야말로 외과 의사의 손길이 필요하다고 단언했다. 그가 두드려 보지 않은 칸막이가 없었고, 꼼꼼히 살펴보지 않은 굴뚝 더미가 없었다. 물관이며 연통이며 물통이며 하수구가 모조리 조사를 받았고, 심지어 벗의 부축을 받아가며 몸을 굽혀 송곳으로 마루청 여기저기에 구멍까지 뚫어 보았다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 그런 살림살이 문제에도 아는 체하는 얼굴을 지어 보이며 방마다 그를 따라다녔고, 나머지 세 사람도 뒤를 따랐다. 그랜틀리 부인은 이십 년 동안 한 교구의 여사제 노릇을 괜히 해 온 것이 아님을 보여 주며, 초인종이며 창유리를 여간 밝은 눈으로 살피지 않았다.\n\n“어쨌든 이 방을 혼자 쓰는 서재로 삼으신다면 창밖으로 아름다운 경치를 내다보시겠어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. 엘리너는 이층의 조그만 방 격자창 앞에 서 있었는데, 거기서 내다보이는 경치는 과연 무척 아름다웠다. 목사관 뒤편이라 집과 저 웅장한 잿빛 대성당 덩어리 사이를 가로막는 것이 하나도 없었다. 그러면서도 그 사이의 땅에는 나무들이 아름답게 점점이 박혀 있었다. 바로 눈앞에는 뒤에 가서 도시를 끼고 도는 작은 강이 흘렀고, 대성당에서 조금 오른쪽으로는 히램 요양원의 뾰족한 박공과 굴뚝들이 그것을 둘러싼 느릅나무 사이로 빼꼼히 내다보였다.\n\n“그렇군요.” 그가 엘리너 곁으로 다가서며 말했다. “적들을 아주 남김없이 내다볼 수 있게 생겼습니다. 적의 성 앞에 진을 치고 앉아 퍽 기분 좋은 거리에서 포를 쏘아 대면 되겠군요. 요양원이 적의 손에 넘어가는 날이면 거기에도 한 방 먹일 수 있겠고, 주교관으로 말하자면 사정거리 안에 넉넉히 들어옵니다.”\n\n“성직자분들 같은 사람들은 정말 처음 봐요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “언제나 서로 싸울 궁리만 하시니 말이에요.”\n\n“그게 아니면,” 그가 말했다. “서로 떠받칠 궁리를 하지요. 딱한 것은 하나 없이 다른 하나만 할 수는 없다는 점입니다. 그러나 우리가 여기 있는 것은 싸우기 위함이 아닙니까? 우리 교회는 싸우는 교회가 아닙니까? 우리 일이라는 것이 싸움 말고 무엇이겠습니까. 제대로 하자면 고된 싸움이지요.”\n\n“하지만 서로를 상대로 싸우실 것까지야 없잖아요.”\n\n“그건 또 모를 일입니다. 같은 교회의 다른 성직자와 다툰다고 저를 나무라시는 그 말씀을, 마호메트교도라면 로마 사제의 오류와 다툰다고 저를 나무라는 데 그대로 쓸 겁니다. 그렇다고 제가 그런 자와 다투는 것이 그르다고야 설마 말씀하지 않으시겠지요. 신을 여럿 두고 섬기는 이교도라면 또 그리스도교도와 마호메트교도가 서로 어긋난다는 것을 똑같이 이상하게 여길 테고요.”\n\n“아! 하지만 대수롭지도 않은 일로 그렇게 모질게 싸우시잖아요.”\n\n“대수롭지 않은 일을 두고 벌이는 싸움은,” 그가 말했다. “늘 모진 법입니다. 특히 이웃끼리는 더 그렇지요. 차이가 크고 서로 사이가 먼 사람들끼리라면 다툴 때에도 예를 갖춥니다. 형제 둘만큼 기를 쓰고 싸우는 상대가 세상에 또 어디 있겠습니까?”\n\n“하지만 그런 다툼이 교회에 추문을 불러오지 않나요?”\n\n“그런 다툼이 없다면 오히려 더 큰 추문이 교회에 떨어질 겁니다. 다툼을 피할 길은 하나뿐입니다. 교리의 모든 점에 대하여 그 말씀이 곧 권위가 되는 공통의 머리를 우리 교회가 받든다고 인정하는 것이지요. 우리의 어려움이 그렇게 끝난다면 꽤나 마음이 끌리는 일입니다. 그 매력을 뿌리치지 못하는 사람이 많고, 저 역시 거의 뿌리치지 못한다는 것을 고백합니다.”\n\n“지금 로마 교회를 말씀하시는 건가요?” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. “꼭 로마 교회를 말하는 것은 아니고, 머리를 가진 교회를 말하는 겁니다. 하느님께서 우리에게 그런 교회를 베풀어 주시는 것이 그 뜻에 맞았다면 우리 길은 수월했겠지요. 그러나 수월한 길이 우리에게 좋다고 여겨지지는 않았던 모양입니다.” 그는 말을 멈추고 한동안 잠자코 서 있었다. 싸움이라고는 필요 없는 수월한 길 하나를 얻자고 제가 가진 모든 것을, 곧 제 정신의 힘과 제 자유로운 뜻과 제 마음의 샘에서 솟는 신선한 물줄기와 제 속 깊은 자기 자신을 하마터면 다 바칠 뻔했던 그때를 생각하고 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 말을 이었다. “하시는 말씀은 반은 옳습니다. 우리의 다툼이 우리에게 얼마간 추문을 불러오는 것은 사실이지요. 바깥세상은 우리가 인간으로서 약한 것을 두고 늘 우리를 헐뜯고, 성직자라 한들 결국 사람에 지나지 않는다는 사실을 우리 낯에다 내던지면서도, 정작 우리에게는 신과 같은 완전함으로 일을 해내라고 요구합니다. 우리에게 신과 같은 구석이라고는 없습니다. 우리는 사람에게 흔한 모진 마음으로 서로 갈라서고, 사람다운 나약함으로 서로를 이기고 우쭐대며, 신에게서 비롯된 주제를 두고 갈리면서 도무지 신다울 것이 없는 반감과 원한을 우리 사이에 빚어냅니다. 다 사실입니다. 그러나 그 자리에 무엇을 놓으시겠습니까? 이 땅 위에 그르침이 없는 교회의 머리란 없습니다. 믿는 사람들이 꾸어 온 이 꿈은 이미 시험을 거쳤고, 그 끝이 무엇이었는지는 이탈리아와 에스파냐에서 우리가 보고 있습니다. 교황의 교회 울타리 안에서는 아옹다옹 다툰 일이 지금도 없고 예전에도 없었다고 쳐 봅시다. 그런 가정이야 터무니없이 사실과 다르지만, 그래도 그렇다고 쳐 두고, 그러고 나서 어느 교회가 더 무거운 추문을 짊어졌는지 말해 봅시다.”\n\n제게 씌워진 혐의를 반쯤은 인정하고 반쯤은 변호하는 애러빈 씨에게는 조용한 진지함이 있었고, 엘리너는 거기에 놀랐다. 엘리너는 평생 성직자들의 논의를 들으며 자랐으나, 다투는 이들 사이의 쟁점이라는 것이 세속의 이해를 넘는 경우가 워낙 드물었던 터라, 그런 주제에 대해 아무런 경외심도 마음에 남아 있지 않았다. 엘리너가 들어 온 가락에는 언제나 수입 아니면 권세를 사랑하는 딱딱한 세속의 누룩이 들어 있었고, 진리를 향해 숨 가빠하는 일도, 종교적 순결을 향해 우러르는 마음도 없었다. 주위 사람들은 언제나 제 편이 의심할 여지 없이 옳다는 것, 미심쩍어할 근거란 없다는 것, 성직자의 본분이 무엇이어야 하는지를 알아내는 저 고되고 험한 일은 이미 남김없이 끝나 있다는 것, 그러니 활동하며 싸우는 목사에게 남은 일이란 덤벼드는 자마다 물리치고 제 자리를 지키는 것뿐이라는 것을 당연하게 여겼다. 물론 아버지는 그런 축이 아니었다. 그러나 아버지는 매사에 워낙 싸움과는 거리가 먼 사람이어서, 엘리너는 마음속으로 아버지를 다른 모든 사람과 따로 떼어 놓고 있었다. 엘리너는 이 문제를 스스로 따져 본 적도, 이 흔한 가락이 그릇된 것인지 아닌지 헤아려 본 적도 없었다. 다만 그런 줄도 모르는 채 그것에 물려 있었다. 그런데 이제 놀랍게도, 그리고 어느 만큼은 즐거운 설렘까지 느끼면서, 저희 가운데 새로 온 이 사람이 제가 익숙하던 것과는 사뭇 다르게 말한다는 것을 알아차린 것이었다.\n\n“나무라기란 참 쉬운 일이지요.” 그가 제 생각의 실마리를 이어 가며 말했다. “신문에 글을 쓰는 사람이나 야당의 앞장선 의원만큼 달콤한 삶은 없지 싶습니다. 권세를 쥔 자들을 향해 벼락같이 죄상을 늘어놓고, 세상에 나온 것마다 그 가장 나쁜 쪽을 들추어내고, 옷마다 흠집을 찾아내고, 분개하고 빈정대고 익살 부리고 도덕을 설하고 거들먹거리고, 밍밍한 칭찬으로 죽여 놓거나 대놓고 헐뜯어 짓뭉개고! 아무것도 책임질 것이 없는 비평가에게 이보다 쉬운 일이 무엇이겠습니까? 부인께서는 제가 하는 일을 나무라십니다만, 제 자리에 서서 그 반대로 해 보십시오. 그러고서도 제가 부인을 나무라지 못하는지 보십시오.”\n\n“어머, 애러빈 씨, 저는 나무라는 게 아니에요.”\n\n“죄송합니다만 나무라고 계십니다, 볼드 부인. 세상 사람의 하나로서 말이지요. 지금 부인은 야당 의원이십니다. 지금 부인은 사설을 쓰고 계시고, 그것도 아주 잘, 아주 모질게 쓰고 계십니다. ‘개들이야 짖고 물어뜯기를 즐긴다 하나’, 우아한 인용으로 서두를 여시는군요, ‘그래도 교회라는 것을 두려거든, 제발 그것을 이끄는 목자들만은 서로의 멱을 잡는 짓을 삼가게 하라. 법률가들도 서로의 이름을 더럽히지 않고 살아가며, 의사들은 결투를 벌이지 않는다. 어찌하여 유독 성직자들만이 서로를 헐뜯는 이 거리낌 없는 자유를 마음껏 누린단 말인가?’ 그렇게 부인은 우리의 불경한 다툼과 종파를 가르는 성벽과 추문거리인 견해차를 두고 우리를 꾸짖어 나가십니다. 그러나 다음 주에 또 한 편의 사설을 쓰시는 데에는 아무런 수고도 들지 않겠지요. 그 글에서는 우리가, 아니 우리 가운데 누군가가 제 소명에 관한 일에 볼썽사납게 무심하다고 놀림을 받을 겁니다. 그 어긋남을 맞춰 놓는 일은 부인 몫이 아닙니다. 독자들은 가련한 목사가 어떻게 때를 얻든 못 얻든 힘써 일하면서도 저와 생각이 크게 다른 사람들과는 결코 부딪치지 않을 수 있는지 부인께 묻는 법이 없으니까요. 부인은 이 외국 조약이나 저 관청의 조처를 나무라시면서도, 그와 다른 어떤 방책이 더 무거운 잘못을 저지른다 한들 아무 책망도 지지 않으실 겁니다. 나무라기란 참 쉬운 일입니다. 그리고 즐겁기도 하지요. 헐뜯는 말만큼 듣는 이를 홀리는 칭찬이란 없으니 말입니다.”\n\n엘리너는 그의 놀림을 절반밖에 따라가지 못했으나 뜻만은 알아들었다. “주제넘게 애러빈 씨를 비평했으니 사과를 드려야 마땅하겠지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “다만 저는 요즈음 바체스터에 들어앉은 나쁜 감정을 서글프게 생각하고 있던 참이라, 해야 할 말보다 좀 더 거리낌 없이 말했던 거예요.”\n\n“땅 위의 평화와 사람들 사이의 선의는 천국이 그러하듯 앞날에 대한 약속이지요.” 그는 엘리너의 생각보다는 제 생각을 좇아 말했다. “그 예언이 이루어지는 날에는 성직자란 것도 더는 있을 까닭이 없어질 겁니다.”\n\n여기서 두 사람의 이야기는 부주교에게 끊겼다. 지하 술광에서 교구 사제를 부르는 그의 목소리가 들려온 것이다.\n\n“애러빈, 애러빈!” 그러고는 곁에 있는 듯한 아내를 돌아보며 말했다. “이 사람이 어디로 갔소? 이 술광은 참으로 형편없구려. 지붕을 얹고 벽을 치고 바닥을 깔기 전에 여기에 포도주 한 병이라도 들여놓는다면 그건 살인이나 다름없소. 늙은 구드너프 영감이 대체 이런 데서 어떻게 지냈는지 도무지 짐작이 안 가오. 하기야 구드너프는 사람이 마실 만한 포도주라고는 한 잔도 가져 본 적이 없는 위인이었소.”\n\n“무슨 일이오, 부주교?” 교구 사제가 계단을 뛰어 내려가며 말했다. 엘리너는 위층에 남아 제 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n“이 술광은 지붕을 얹고 벽을 치고 바닥을 깔아야 하오.” 부주교가 되뇌었다. “내 말을 명심하시오. 이대로도 쓸 만하다는 건축업자의 말에 넘어가지 마시오. 그 작자들 절반은 포도주에 대해 아무것도 모르오. 이 자리는 지금 이대로라면 겨울에는 축축하고 차며 여름에는 덥고 눅눅할 거요. 세상에 빚어진 가장 좋은 술이라 한들 여기에 두어 해 뉘어 두었다면 나는 지푸라기 하나 값도 치르지 않겠소.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 그러겠노라 하고, 부주교의 처방대로 술광을 다시 짓겠다고 약속했다.\n\n“그리고 애러빈, 여기 좀 보시오. 부엌 화덕이랍시고 이렇게 만들어 놓은 것을 어디서 본 적이 있소?”\n\n“화덕이 정말 형편없기는 하네요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “여사제를 이 집에 맞아들여 앞으로 할 일을 맡기게 되면 분명 이 화덕을 마뜩잖아 할 거예요. 정말이지 애러빈 씨, 저 위의 그 훌륭한 우물에 길든 여사제라면 이런 화덕은 도저히 견디지 못할걸요.”\n\n“세인트 이월드에 여사제라는 것을 꼭 두어야 한다면, 그랜틀리 부인, 그저 그 우물이나 지키게 두고 우리네 가난에서 비롯한 이런저런 모자람에 대고 거룩한 노여움을 불러 내리지는 말았으면 합니다. 그렇지만 잘 지은 저녁상의 매력에 제가 약하다는 것만은 인정하니, 화덕은 반드시 갈겠습니다.”\n\n이때쯤 부주교는 다시 위층으로 올라와 이제는 식당에 들어와 있었다. “애러빈.” 그는 늘 그러하듯 크고 또렷한 목소리로, 또 그에게 그토록 흔한 명령조로 말했다. “이 식당은 반드시 손을 보아야 하오. 아니, 아예 통째로 다시 만들어야 하오. 여기 보시오, 꼭 16피트에 15피트밖에 안 되오. 이런 치수의 식당이라는 것을 세상 누가 들어나 보았겠소!” 부주교는 그런 일에조차 어떤 종류의 성직자다운 위엄을 실을 수 있기라도 한 듯한 몸가짐으로, 묵직한 걸음을 옮겨 방을 세로로 가로로 재어 보였다. “겨우 16피트요. 이만하면 네모반듯하다고 해야지.”\n\n“둥근 식탁을 놓으면 아주 알맞겠구먼.” 전임 워든이 넌지시 말했다.\n\n그런데 부주교가 보기에 둥근 식탁이라는 발상에는 유난히 정통에서 어긋나는 구석이 있었다. 그는 언제나 넉넉한 길이의 훌륭한 상, 손님 수에 따라 편안히 늘어나고, 하도 문질러 대어 거의 검게 되었으며, 거울처럼 반들거리는 상에 익숙해 있었다. 그런데 둥근 식탁이란 대개 참나무로 짜거나, 아니면 만든 지 얼마 되지 않아 그가 그토록 흡족해하는 그 특유의 빛깔을 아직 얻지 못한 것들이었다. 그는 둥근 식탁을 두고, 오래 앉아 있지 말라고 사람들에게 미리 일러 두기라도 하듯 상보를 그대로 깔아 두는, 그가 이르는바 고약한 신식 방식과 한데 묶어 생각했다. 그의 눈에 둥근 식탁에는 어딘가 민주주의적이고 벼락출세한 구석이 있었다. 그는 그런 것을 주로 비국교도나 옥양목에 무늬를 찍는 자들이 쓰리라고, 그리고 어쩌면 품위보다 재치로 더 이름난 몇몇 문단의 사자들이 쓰리라고 여겼다. 그런 물건이 제가 밀어 준 사람의 손으로, 그것도 장인의 부추김을 받아 이 교구 안에 들어온다는 생각에 그는 적잖이 마음이 어수선해졌다.\n\n“둥근 식탁이란,” 그는 얼마간 열을 내어 말했다. “여태 만들어진 가구 가운데 가장 가증스러운 물건이오. 애러빈에게는 그런 것을 제 집에 들이지 않을 만한 안목이 있으리라 믿소.”\n\n가엾은 하딩 씨는 완전히 면박을 당한 기분이 들어 물론 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 술광과 부엌 화덕 같은 사소한 문제에서는 고분고분 물러섰던 애러빈 씨는, 제 주머니 사정에 견주어 너무 값비쌀 법한 개조에는 맞서지 않을 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n“그렇지만 내가 보기에는, 부주교, 벽을 헐지 않고서야 이 방을 길게 늘일 도리가 없소. 그런데 벽을 헐면 다시 쌓아야 하고, 이쪽에 내닫이창을 내면 저쪽에도 똑같이 내야 하며, 아래층에 그렇게 하면 위층까지 올려 붙여야 하오. 그러면 집에 새 정면을 다는 셈이 되어 200파운드쯤은 들 것이오. 내 고충이라는 것이 식당 길이가 16피트밖에 안 된다는 것이라는 말을 듣고서 교회 재산 위원회가 나를 도와줄 성싶지 않소.”\n\n부주교는 집 안의 다른 방은 손끝 하나 대지 않고 식당 정면에 6피트를 덧붙이는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없다고 설명하기 시작했다. 시골의 자그마한 집에서 그 정도로 구조가 고르지 않은 것은 오히려 운치가 있으면 있었지 흠이 될 것은 없다고 그는 말했고, 40파운드가 넘게 들면 그 전부를 제 주머니에서 치르겠노라고까지 했다. 그러나 애러빈 씨는 단호했고, 부주교가 안달하고 씩씩거려도 물러서지 않았다. 40파운드는 제게 여간 중대한 문제가 아니며, 벗들이 그런 형편에도 마음 좋게 저를 찾아와 줄 생각이라면 네모반듯한 방의 괴로움쯤은 참아 주어야 한다고 그는 말했다. 다만 둥근 식탁을 들일 뜻은 전혀 없다고 밝히는 것으로 절충할 용의는 있었다.\n\n“그런데,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “여사제가 두 방을 다 넓혀 달라고 우기면 어떻게 하시겠어요?”\n\n“그렇다면 그 여사제가 제 손으로 해야지요, 그랜틀리 부인.”\n\n“그야 넉넉히 그럴 수 있을 거예요.” 부인이 대답했다. “그것뿐 아니라 더 놀라운 일도 얼마든지 해내겠지요. 세인트 이월드의 여사제는 정말이지 오게 되는 날에 빈손으로 오지는 않으리라고 저는 확신해요.”\n\n그러나 애러빈 씨는 그처럼 아득한 가망을 믿고 씀씀이를 벌일 마음이 별로 없어 보였다. 그리하여 그 비용을 교회 재산 위원회나 전임 성직자의 유산 어느 쪽에도 온당하게 떠넘길 수 없는 집안의 실질적인 개조는 모두 금기가 되었다. 이 중요한 예외만 빼면 부주교는 명령하고 제안하고 모든 사안을 제 뜻대로 밀어붙여, 스스로 여간 흡족해하지 않았다. 그 자리에 눈 밝은 사람이 하나라도 있었더라면, 이 일에서 부인이 남편 못지않게 쓸모가 있었음을 알아보았을 것이다. 편안한 집에 갖추어야 할 것들을 그랜틀리 부인만큼 잘 아는 사람은 없었다. 그러면서도 부인은 제 주인이자 지아비가 그토록 선뜻 제 것으로 챙기는 그 영광을 굳이 제 몫이라 주장할 필요를 느끼지 않았다.\n\n일을 효과적으로 또 체계적으로 마친 일행은 이번 나들이에 흡족해하며 플럼스테드로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제22장. 울라손의 손 남매\n\n다음 일요일에 애러빈 씨는 새 교회에서 취임 낭독을 하기로 되어 있었다. 목사관에서는 부주교가 그와 함께 건너가 독경대에서 거들고, 하딩 씨가 플럼스테드 교회에서 부주교의 직무를 맡기로 이야기가 되었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 학교와 빵을 돌보아야 하는 터라 도저히 몸을 뺄 수 없노라 하였으나, 볼드 부인은 그들과 동행하기로 하였다. 나아가 일행은 지주의 집에서 점심을 들고 오후 예배가 끝난 뒤에 돌아오기로 정하였다.\n\n울라손의 윌프레드 손 씨는 세인트 이월드의 지주였다. 아니, 차라리 울라손의 지주라 하는 편이 옳겠으니, 요즘 지주의 영지가 옛 성인의 명성보다 널리 알려져 있었던 까닭이다. 백 년 전에는 웨스턴 지주가 제법 알맞게 대변하였다고들 하는 그 족속이 오늘날에 이르러 어떤 모습이 되었는지를 그는 잘 보여 주는 본보기였다. 그 옛 대변자가 참으로 그러하였다면, 그만큼 빠르게 나아진 부류의 사람들도 흔치 않을 것이다. 그러나 손 씨 역시 남의 웃음거리가 되기에 충분할 만큼의 결점을 지니고 있었다. 나이 쉰 줄에 이르도록 그는 아직 독신이었고, 제 풍채를 적잖이 자랑스러워하였다. 울라손 제 집에 머물 때에는 그런 자랑을 부릴 자리가 그리 많지 않았으므로, 그곳에서 그는 언제나 신사답게, 그리고 그가 실제로 그러한 바 곧 제 교구에서 으뜸가는 사람답게 보였다. 그러나 해마다 런던에서 보내는 한 달 남짓 혹은 예닐곱 주 동안에는 거기서도 대단한 인물로 보이려고 어찌나 애를 쓰는지 — 그는 결코 대단한 인물이 아니었건만 — 클럽의 적잖은 사람들이 그를 얼간이로 치부하였다. 그는 어떤 방면, 어떤 주제에 관해서는 상당한 문필의 소양을 갖춘 사람이었다. 그가 아끼는 저자는 몽테뉴와 버턴이었고, 지난 두 세기 영국 수필가들에 관해서라면 아마 제 고을과 이웃 고을을 통틀어 그보다 더 아는 사람이 없었을 것이다. 그는 『아이들러』, 『스펙테이터』, 『태틀러』, 『가디언』, 『램블러』를 빠짐없이 갖추어 두고 있었으며, 그런 간행물이 그 뒤로 우리네 『에든버러』니 『쿼털리』니 하는 것들이 내놓은 어떤 글보다 얼마나 나은지를 몇 시간이고 늘어놓을 수 있었다. 족보에 관한 문제라면 무엇이든 능통하여, 영국의 웬만한 신사 집안은 거의 다 꿰고 있었으므로, 그런 호사를 누린다고 내세울 만한 이들이 어떤 피와 어떤 혈통에서 내려왔는지를 낱낱이 말해 줄 수 있었다. 피와 혈통에 대하여 그 자신은 더없이 깊은 경의를 품고 있었다. 그는 제 조상을 정복 시대보다 훨씬 앞선 어느 시절까지 거슬러 헤아렸고, 들어 줄 이만 있다면, 그의 조상이 색슨 사람 세드릭처럼 노르만 귀족들 틈에서 어떻게 제 것을 지킬 수 있었는지를 들려주었다. 그의 말에 따르면 그것은 결코 그의 집안이 노르만 이웃들에게 물러 터지게 굴었기 때문이 아니었다. 울라손의 어느 엘프리드는 존 왕 시절에 제 성을 굳게 지켰을 뿐 아니라 그때 있던 바체스터 대성당까지 아울러 지키며 제프리 드 버그라는 자에 맞서 버티었으니, 손 씨는 그 공성전의 내력을 통째로 담은 양피지 문서를 지니고 있었고 그 문서는 더없이 값지게 채색되어 있었다. 그 글씨를 읽어 낼 사람이 아무도 없다는 것은 그리 대수롭지 않았다. 설령 읽어 낸다 한들 그 말을 알아들을 사람 또한 없었을 터이므로. 그러나 손 씨는 그 자초지종을 훌륭한 영어로 들려줄 수 있었고, 또 그리하기를 마다하지 않았다.\n\n가문의 내력이 얼마 되지 않는 사람들을 그가 깔보았다고 한다면 온당치 못한 말이 될 것이다. 그는 그러지 않았다. 그런 이들과 자주 어울렸고, 벗들 가운데 여럿을 그런 이들 중에서 골랐다. 다만 그는 그들을 바라보기를, 큰 부자가 수입이 적은 이들을 바라보듯, 소포클레스를 손금 보듯 훤히 아는 사람이 그리스어라고는 한 자도 모르는 사람을 바라보듯 하였다. 그들도 물론 좋은 사람들일 수 있고, 덕으로 칭찬받아 마땅하고, 재주로 우러를 만하고, 모든 면에서 훌륭히 존경할 만할 수 있으나, 다만 그 크나큰 한 가지 선물을 갖추지 못한 이들이었다. 이 문제에 관한 손 씨의 생각이 그러하였다. 좋은 피를 잃은 것은 무엇으로도 메울 수 없고, 좋은 피가 미치는 좋은 효험은 무엇으로도 지울 수 없다. 이제 그것을 지닌 이가 참으로 드물어졌으나, 그런 까닭에 그 소유는 한층 더 귀하였다. 이 문제를 두고 손 씨가 늘어놓는 이야기를 듣는 것은 자못 즐거운 일이었다. 아무개 집안의 가장이 오래된 준남작이라 하여 그 집안이 좋은 가문이라고 무지한 채로 짐작하여 말하기라도 하면, 그는 짐짓 놀란 척하는 유쾌한 표정으로 눈을 크게 뜨고는, 준남작위란 제임스 1세 때에야 생긴 것임을 겸손히 일깨워 주었다. 피츠제럴드가와 드 버그가의 피를 입에 올리면 그는 가만히 한숨을 지었고, 하워드가와 라우더가의 주장은 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않았으며, 탤벗가를 두고서는 아직 족보의 온전한 영예에 이르지 못한 집안이라고 말한 적도 있었다.\n\n그 이름이 귀족의 관 셋을 받는 영예를 누렸고, 그 자손들이 여러 선거구를 대표하여 의석에 앉았으며, 그 일족 가운데 누군가는 금세기에 꾸려진 거의 모든 내각에 들어 있었던, 영국에도 몇 없는 그 찬란한 가문을 두고 이야기하던 자리에서, 손 씨는 그들을 통틀어 “흙”이라 불렀다. 그는 그 사람들을 업신여길 뜻이 조금도 없었다. 여러모로 그들을 우러렀고, 시샘 없이 그들의 특권을 인정하였다. 다만 그들의 핏줄을 흐르는 물줄기가 아직 세월에 씻겨 그만한 완전함에 이르지 못하였다는 것, 족보의 뜻으로 피라 불릴 만한 참된 이코르가 되지는 못하였다는 것, 그 느낌을 말하려 하였을 뿐이다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 처음 그에게 소개되었을 때, 손 씨는 대뜸 그가 업힐 스탠턴의 애러빈 가문 사람이리라고 짚었다. 애러빈 씨는 자기가 그 집안과는 아주 먼 친척이라고 답하였다. 이에 손 씨는 그 친족 관계가 그리 멀 리 없다고 짐작하였다. 애러빈 씨는 두 집안이 서로를 전혀 모를 만큼 먼 사이라고 딱 잘라 말하였다. 손 씨는 이 말에 특유의 부드러운 웃음을 웃고는, 애러빈 씨에게 이르기를, 지금 남아 있는 그 집안의 갈래 가운데 엘리자베스 여왕 치세보다 앞서 본줄기에서 갈라져 나온 것은 하나도 없으니, 그러므로 애러빈 씨가 스스로를 먼 친척이라 일컬을 수는 없다고 하였다. 애러빈 씨 자신이 분명 업힐 스탠턴의 애러빈이라는 것이었다.\n\n“그렇지만” 하고 교구 사제가 말하였다. “업힐 스탠턴은 드 그레이 가문에 팔려서 지난 50년 동안 그들 손에 있지 않소.”\n\n“그리고 그것이 150년 동안 그 손에 있는다 한들, 불행히도 그토록 오래 그리된다 한들” 하고 손 씨가 말하였다. “당신의 자손들이 스스로를 업힐 스탠턴 집안 사람이라 일컬을 자격은 털끝만큼도 줄지 않소. 다행히도 그것만은 드 그레이가 사들일 수 없고, 다행히도 그것만은 애러빈도 손도 팔아넘길 수 없는 것이오.”\n\n정치에서 손 씨는 물러섬 없는 보수주의자였다. 도드 씨가 일러 주는 바, 1852년 11월에 자유무역을 규탄한 그 쉰세 명의 트로이 용사들이야말로 영국의 공인들 가운데 남은 유일한 애국자라고 그는 여겼다. 자유무역이라는 저 무서운 고비가 닥쳤을 때, 그때까지 그가 조국을 구할 유일한 사람들로 여겨 온 바로 그자들의 손으로 곡물법 폐지가 관철되었을 때, 그는 한동안 얼이 나갔다. 조국은 무너졌다. 그러나 그것은 그나마 작은 일이었다. 다른 나라들도 흥하였다가 스러졌고, 인류는 하느님의 섭리 아래 그래도 나아져 왔다. 그러나 이제 사람의 신의에 대한 믿음은 영영 끝장이 나야 마땅하였다. 파멸이 닥칠 뿐 아니라, 그 파멸이 참된 신자 중의 참된 신자로 여겨지던 이들의 배교를 통하여 닥치는 것이었다. 신사가 몸담을 일로서의 영국 정치는 끝장이 나야 마땅하였다. 손 씨가 휘그당원의 발밑에 짓밟혔다면 토리당원으로서, 순교자로서 그것을 견딜 수 있었으련만, 그토록 열심히 떠받들고 그토록 철석같이 믿었던 이들에게 그처럼 송두리째 내던져지고 속아 넘어간 일은 견디고도 살아 있기에는 너무 벅찼다. 그리하여 그는 정치가로서 살기를 그만두었고, 나라 형편에 관하여 바깥 세상과 어떤 말도 섞기를 마다하였다.\n\n로버트 필 경의 배교가 있은 뒤 두어 해 동안 손 씨의 심경은 그러하였으나, 남들이 그러하였듯 그의 성미도 차츰 가라앉았다. 그는 다시금 나다니기 시작하여 치안판사석과 장터에 얼굴을 내밀었고, 그를 그토록 모질게 저버린 이들과 나란히 어깨를 겯고 만찬 자리에 앉은 모습을 보이기도 하였다. 그로서는 살아가야 하였고, 세상을 등지겠다는 그 계획은 소용에 닿지 않았다. 그러나 그를 비롯하여 여전히 보호무역의 굳은 원칙을 지키는 주위 사람들 — 군중의 아우성에 움츠러들기에는 너무 참된 그와 같은 사람들 — 에게는 저희끼리 마음을 달래는 방도가 있었다. 그들은 어떤 엘레우시스 비의(祕儀)의, 오직 그것을 통해서만 신들에게 바르게 다가갈 수 있는 어떤 깊고 놀라운 예배 의식의, 이제 남은 유일한 수호자였고 또 스스로 그렇게 느꼈다. 이제 그것들을 알고, 또 그것이 아직 가능하다면 제 자식들을 조심스럽고 은밀하게 가르쳐 그것을 이어 가는 일은 오직 그들에게만 주어진 몫이었다.\n\n바깥 세상에는 여느 교회의 예배를 좇는 듯이 보이던 집안들에서 사사롭고 유별난 예배의 형식이 대를 이어 지켜져 왔다는 이야기를 우리는 읽어 알고 있다. 손 씨의 경우도 차츰 그러하였다. 그는 마침내 보호무역이 죽은 것인 양 이야기되는 자리에서도 잠자코 듣고 있는 법을 익혔다. 속으로는 그것이 여전히 신비한 생명으로 살아 있음을 알고 있었으면서도. 게다가 그런 앎이 자기에게는 주어졌으되 뭇사람에게는 막혀 있다는 데에서 그는 얼마간의 즐거움을 느끼지 않은 것도 아니었다. 시골 신사들 사이에서조차 자유무역이 알고 보면 그리 나쁜 것도 아니라는 말이 나오는 것을 그는 예사로 듣게 되었고, 영국의 좋은 것이 죄다 저의 옛 팔라디움과 함께 사라졌음을 속으로 알면서도 그 말에 다투지 않고 듣게 되었다. 로마인들이 더는 그 이름에 값하지 못하는 까닭에 스스로 목숨을 끊을 수 있음을 자랑삼던 카토의 심정 같은 것이 그의 안에도 얼마간 있었다. 손 씨는 그리스도인인 데다 여전히 연 4000파운드의 수입을 지니고 있었으므로 목숨을 끊을 생각은 없었으나, 그렇다고 하여 그 심정이 덜 아늑하지는 않았다.\n\n손 씨는 사냥을 즐기는 사람이었고, 지나치다 할 정도는 아니어도 부지런히 사냥에 나섰다. 제 고을에서 정치가 크게 무너지기 전까지 그는 온갖 수를 다하여 사냥 모임을 뒷받침하였다. 사냥감을 어찌나 아껴 길렀던지 세인트 이월드 교구에서는 거위든 칠면조든 꽁지를 성히 달고 다니는 놈이 없을 지경이었다. 참나무와 낙엽송보다 가시금작화 덤불을 더 정성껏 심었다. 제 암양과 새끼 양보다 여우들의 안락을 더 걱정하였다. 울라손보다 사람이 많이 모이는 사냥 모임이 없었고, 손 씨의 마구간만큼 먼 데서 온 이들의 말에 후하게 열린 마구간이 없었으며, 사냥 클럽을 지탱하는 데에 그보다 더 많이 말하고 더 많이 쓰고 더 많이 애쓴 사람이 없었다. 보호무역의 이론이 고을 사냥의 관행 속에서 그토록 활짝 펼쳐질 수 있었던 것이다! 그러나 저 큰 파국이 닥쳤을 때, 바셋셔 사냥단의 고귀한 단장이 상원에서 그 변절한 대신을 편들며 가터 훈장을 바라는 마음에 제 진실과 사내다움과 벗들과 명예를 비굴하게 내던졌을 때, 손 씨는 사냥을 그만두었다. 그렇다고 제 덤불을 베어 내지는 않았으니, 그것은 신사가 할 짓이 아니었다. 제 여우들을 잡아 죽이지도 않았으니, 그가 보기에 그것은 살인이었다. 제 덤불을 뒤지지 말라거나 제 여우굴을 막지 말라고 이르지도 않았으니, 그것은 시골 신사들 사이에 통하는 규약에 어긋나는 일이었다. 다만 울라손에서 사냥 모임이 열릴 때마다 집을 비웠고, 덤불은 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었으며, 붉은 사냥복을 옷장에서 꺼내거나 사냥말을 마구간에서 끌어내라는 권유에는 도무지 넘어가지 않았다. 이것이 두 해 동안 이어졌고, 그런 뒤 그는 차츰 마음을 돌렸다. 그는 먼저 이웃 고을의 사냥 모임에 사냥용 저고리도 아닌 총 쏠 때 입는 웃옷 차림으로 조랑말을 타고 나타났으니, 마치 지나는 길에 우연히 들른 듯한 모양새였다. 다음에는 어느 아침 제가 아끼던 가시금작화 덤불을 뒤지는 것을 보러 걸어서 올라갔고, 마부가 마침 제 암말을 끌고 나오자 올라타기를 마다하지 않았다. 그다음에는 저 불멸의 쉰세 명 가운데 한 사람의 권유에 따라 사냥 채비를 고을 건너편으로 옮겨 그곳 사냥개들과 함께 보름을 지냈고, 그렇게 차츰 옛 생활로 돌아왔다. 그러나 사냥에서도 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그를 지탱한 것은 바깥세상의 공기를 함께 마시는 이들보다 자기가 신비롭게 우월하다는 속마음뿐이었다.\n\n손 씨는 울라손에서 홀로 살지 않았다. 그에게는 열 살 위의 누이가 있었는데, 이 누이는 그의 편견과 심정에 어찌나 깊이 함께하였던지 그의 온갖 결점을 살아 움직이는 희화로 만들어 놓은 듯하였다. 그녀는 요즘 나오는 계간지를 펼치는 법이 없었고, 제 응접실에 잡지가 놓이는 꼴을 보려 하지 않았으며, 무슨 사정이 있어도 『타임스』 조각 따위로 제 손가락을 더럽히려 들지 않았다. 애디슨과 스위프트와 스틸을 두고서는 그들이 아직 살아 있기라도 한 듯이 이야기하였고, 디포를 제 나라에서 가장 이름난 소설가로 여겼으며, 필딩은 이야기의 들판에 갓 들어선 젊고 기특한 신참쯤으로 생각하였다. 시로 말하자면 드라이든까지의 이름은 익히 알았고, 한 번은 꾐에 넘어가 『머리타래의 강탈』을 읽은 적도 있었으나, 이 갈래에서 제 나라 문학의 가장 순정한 본보기는 스펜서라고 여겼다. 족보야말로 그녀가 가장 아끼는 광증이었다. 웬만한 족보학자들이 자랑으로 삼는 것들이 그녀에게는 하찮았다. 문장(紋章)이니 표어니 하는 것은 그녀를 제정신이 아니게 만들었다. 울라손의 엘프리드가 제프리 드 버그의 가슴팍을 쪼개는 데에 무슨 표어의 도움이 필요하였던가. 엘프리드의 증조부인 거인 울라프리드가 그 비열한 노르만 침략자의 사촌을 제 성 꼭대기에서 내던질 적에 자연이 그에게 준 팔 말고 무슨 문장이 필요하였던가. 그녀에게는 요즘의 영국식 이름이란 죄다 하나같이 보잘것없었다. 헹기스트니 호르사니 하는 이름이라야 그녀의 귀에 고귀함의 참된 맛이 났다. 색슨 시대보다 더 거슬러 올라가지 않고서는 그녀는 흡족해하지 않았으니, 자식이 있었더라면 틀림없이 옛 브리튼 사람들의 이름으로 세례를 받게 하였을 것이다. 몇 가지 점에서 그녀는 스콧이 그린 울리카와 그리 다르지 않았고, 저주하는 버릇이 있었더라면 필시 미스타와 스코굴라와 체르네보크의 이름으로 저주하였을 것이다. 다만 가엾은 울리카처럼 더러운 노르만의 품에 안긴 일이 없고 어버이를 죽이는 일에 손을 보탠 적도 없었으므로, 사람다운 온정의 젖이 그 가슴에서 상하지는 않았다. 그리하여 그녀는 저주하는 대신 축복하였다. 다만 그 축복이라는 것이 예스럽고 투박한 색슨식이어서, 제 집 소작인들 말고는 어떤 농부도 알아듣지 못하였을 말투였다.\n\n정치를 두고 말하자면, 손 양은 곡물법 문제가 나오기 훨씬 전부터 저열한 짓거리들에 하도 진저리를 쳐 온 터라 그 일에는 별로 마음이 움직이지 않았다. 그녀가 보기에 제 오라비는 너무 뜨거운 기질에 떠밀려 민주주의로 기울었던 경솔한 젊은이였다. 이제 다행히도 세상의 사악함을 보고서 온당한 생각으로 돌아왔을 뿐이다. 그녀는 아직도 선거법 개정안과 화해하지 못하였고, 가톨릭 해방에 관하여 공작이 저지른 배신을 두고 여전히 속으로 탄식하였다. 여왕이 누구를 고문으로 삼아야 하겠느냐고 물었다면 그녀는 아마 엘던 경의 이름을 대었을 것이고, 그 존경할 어른은 이제 육신을 지니고 우리를 도울 수 없노라고 일러 주면 아마 한숨을 지으며 이제 우리를 도울 수 있는 이는 죽은 이들뿐이라고 대답하였을 것이다.\n\n종교로 말하자면 손 양은 순전한 드루이드 여사제였다. 그렇다고 하여 그녀가 요즘 세상에 사람을 제물로 바치는 자리에 실제로 거들었다거나, 실은 그리스도의 교회에 적대적이었다는 뜻으로 새기지는 말아 주기 바란다. 그녀는 그리스도교를 제 조상들이 드리던 예배의 좀 더 부드러운 형태로 받아들였고, 개혁이 이롭다는 것이 밝혀질 때에는 자기도 개혁에 아무 편견이 없다는 증거로 늘 그 사실을 내세웠다. 그녀가 여태 받아들인 개혁 가운데 이것이 가장 근래의 것이었으니, 성 아우구스티누스의 시절이 오기 전에 이미 브리튼의 부인들이 몸에 칠하기를 그만두고 무슨 속치마 같은 것을 입게 되었으리라 짐작한 것이다. 칠과 속치마를 아울러 갖추는, 여인들의 그다음 진보에 이르러서는 손 양이라는 신봉자를 얻지 못하였다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 드루이드였던 것은 이런 점에서였다. 곧 제 교회의 관습과 관례에서 무엇인지도 모를 무엇인가를 아쉬워하였다는 점이다. 그녀는 지나가 버린 좋은 것들을 이따금 입에 올렸고 늘 마음속으로 생각하였으나, 그 좋은 것들이 무엇이었는지에 대해서는 아주 흐릿한 짐작밖에 없었다. 지금은 사라진 순결함이 있었고, 우리 목자들을 꾸며 주던 경건과 우리 백성을 꾸며 주던 소박한 순종이 있었다고 그녀는 그렸으나, 역사가 그런 생각을 뒷받침해 주는 바는 유감스럽게도 별로 없었다. 그녀는 크랜머를 두고 순교자들 가운데 가장 굳세고 가장 순박한 이인 양 말하는 버릇이 있었고, 엘리자베스를 두고는 제 백성의 순수한 신교 신앙이 그 삶의 유일한 근심거리이기라도 하였던 양 말하곤 하였다. 할 수만 있다 한들 그 오해를 깨뜨리는 것은 잔인한 짓이었으리라. 그러나 앞의 이가 제자리를 지키기 위해서라면 어디까지든 갈 준비가 되어 있던 처세에 능한 성직자였고, 뒤의 이는 제가 제 교황이기만 하다면 마음으로는 교황파였다는 것을 그녀에게 믿게 하기란 애초에 불가능한 일이었다.\n\n그리하여 손 양은 한숨과 아쉬움 속에 살아가며, 왕권신수설을 황금시대의 으뜸가는 원리로 삼아 돌아보았고, 마음속 가장 깊은 자리에는 쫓겨난 어느 스튜어트 왕가 사람의 복위를 바라는, 입 밖에 내지 않는 은근한 소망을 품고 있었다. 누가 그녀에게서 그 한숨의 호사를, 그 부드러운 아쉬움의 달콤함을 앗아 가려 하겠는가!\n\n몸가짐과 차림새로 말하자면 그녀는 나무랄 데가 없었고, 자기가 나무랄 데 없다는 것을 그녀도 익히 알고 있었다. 그녀는 몸집이 작고 맵시 있게 다듬어진 노부인으로, 젊은 시절의 화색이 장밋빛 자취를 얼마간 남겨 두고 물러간 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 그녀는 제 안색을 자랑스러워하였고, 새하얀 레이스 실내모 가장자리로 얼굴을 빙 둘러 삐죽삐죽 내민 짧고 곱슬곱슬한 백발을 자랑스러워하였다. 그녀가 레이스에 쓰는 돈을 헤아리자면 딸 일곱을 둔 가엾은 퀴버풀 부인은 가슴이 미어지곤 하였다. 그녀는 아직도 희고 개수가 넉넉한 제 이를 자랑스러워하였고, 밝고 명랑한 눈을 자랑스러워하였으며, 짧고 경쾌한 걸음걸이를 자랑스러워하였고, 그 걸음을 옮기는 아담하고 반듯한 작은 발을 몹시 자랑스러워하였다. 또한 응접실을 사각사각 스치며 거닐 적에 늘 걸치던 값진 무늬 비단을, 참으로 몹시 자랑스러워하였다.\n\n우리는 브랭섬의 부인의 관례가 어떠하였는지를 알고 있다.\n\n이름 높은 스물아홉 기사가 브랭섬 홀에 제 방패를 걸어 두었더라.\n\n울라손의 부인은 그처럼 무인다운 버릇을 지니지는 않았으나 돈이 덜 든다고 하기도 어려웠다. 그녀는 제 방에서 비단 치마 스물아홉 벌을 꺼내 놓을 수 있으며 그 하나하나가 혼자서도 설 만큼 빳빳하다고 뽐낼 수 있었을 것이다. 스코틀랜드 용사들의 방패 스물아홉은 그만큼 홀로 설 힘도 없었고, 저희에게 닥칠 어떤 공격을 막아 내기에 더 나을 것도 별로 없었다. 손 양은 옷을 다 갖추어 입으면 머리끝에서 발끝까지 무장을 갖춘 셈이라 할 만하였고, 사람의 눈에 뜨이는 한에서 말하자면 그녀는 언제나 옷을 다 갖추어 입고 있었다.\n\n이 모든 값진 차림을 손 양이 제 오라비의 너그러움에 힘입어 얻은 것은 아니었다. 그녀에게는 아주 넉넉한 제 몫의 재산이 따로 있었고, 그것을 어린 친척들과 여성 모자 상인들과 가난한 이들에게 나누어 주되 마지막 쪽에 가장 큰 몫을 돌렸다. 그러니 자잘한 어리석음을 다 지녔으면서도 그녀가 사람들에게 미움을 사지 않았으리라는 것은 짐작할 만하다. 그녀의 어리석음이라면 이제 다 이야기한 셈이다. 그녀의 미덕은 낱낱이 그려 보이기에는 너무 많았고, 또 그려 보일 만큼 흥미롭지도 않았다.\n\n손 남매의 이야기가 나온 김에 그들이 살던 집에 관해서도 한마디 해 두어야겠다. 그것은 큰 집도 아니고 훌륭한 집도 아니었으며 요즘 생각으로는 그리 편리한 집도 아니었으나, 참된 튜더 양식 건축의 그 유별난 빛깔과 유별난 장식을 사랑하는 이들에게는 나무랄 데 없는 보석으로 여겨졌다. 우리도 그런 무리에 든다고 감히 밝혀 두거니와, 그런 까닭에 이 자리를 빌려 영국의 남녀가 영국 건축의 아름다움을 어찌 이리도 모르는지 놀라움을 말해 두려 한다. 콜로세움의 폐허며 피렌체의 종탑이며 산마르코며 쾰른이며 부르스며 노트르담은 우리 여행자들에게 집안 살림살이만큼이나 익숙한 이름이면서도, 그들은 윌트셔와 도싯셔와 서머싯셔의 영광에 관해서는 아무것도 모른다. 아니, 이름난 여행가들, 어쩌면 시나이산 아래에 천막을 쳐 본 적까지 있는 이들조차 윌트셔와 도싯셔와 서머싯셔에 영광이 있다는 것을 여태 모르고 있지는 않은지 우리는 자못 의심스럽다. 부디 가서 보시기를 청한다.\n\n손 씨의 집은 울라손 저택이라 불렸고, 그렇게 불리는 것이 온당하였으니, 집 자체가 사각 안뜰의 두 면을 이루고 나머지 두 면은 스무 자 남짓 되는 담이 채워 주고 있었던 까닭이다. 이 담은 다듬은 돌로 쌓았는데, 실은 거칠게 다듬은 데다 이제는 몹시 닳았으나, 아름답고 그윽한 황갈색을 띠고 있었으니, 세 세기가 걸려 자라난 자잘한 돌이끼가 빚어 낸 빛깔이었다. 담 꼭대기는 담과 같은 빛깔의 크고 둥근 돌공으로 꾸며져 있었다. 안뜰로 드는 길은 쇠문 한 쌍을 지나야 했는데, 그것이 어찌나 육중한지 누구도 편히 열거나 닫을 수 없었고, 그런 까닭에 좀처럼 건드리는 일이 없었다. 문간에서 두 갈래 길이 안뜰을 비스듬히 가로질렀으니, 왼편 길은 집이 꺾이는 모퉁이에 나 있는 현관에 닿았고, 오른편 길은 건물 가운데 긴 쪽의 저편 끝에 있는 뒷문으로 이어졌다.\n\n요즘 집을 편리하게 꾸미는 데에 이골이 난 이들에게는, 현관까지 마차를 댈 수 없다는 것이 울라손 저택에 크게 불리한 점으로 보일 것이다. 어여쁜 독자여, 울라손에 들어서려면 그대는 걸어서, 적어도 바퀴 달린 안락의자에 앉아서 들어가야만 한다. 말이 끄는 어떤 탈것도 저 쇠문 안으로는 들어오지 못한다. 그러나 이것은 그대를 맞이할 다음번 끔찍함에 견주면 아무것도 아니다. 그리 거창하달 것 없는 문간을 지나 현관문으로 들어서면, 그대는 대뜸 식당 안에 서 있게 된다. 뭐라, 홀도 없다니? 하고 근대 생활의 온갖 안락한 부속물에 익숙한 나의 호사스러운 벗이 소리친다. 아니오, 친절한 벗이여, 눈여겨보기만 한다면 훌륭한 홀이 여기 있소. 시골 신사의 집안에 알맞은 크기의 참되고 오래된 영국식 홀이 말이오. 다만 미안하게도 따로 식사할 방은 없소.\n\n손 씨도 손 양도 제 집의 이 유별난 점을 자랑스러워하였다. 오라비는 한때 벗들의 꾐에 넘어가 그것을 고칠 뻔하였지만 말이다. 남매는 저희가 세드릭처럼 참으로 제 홀에서 식사를 한다는 사실에 흐뭇해하였다. 비록 그 식사가 단둘이 마주 앉은 것일지언정. 그러나 남매는 결코 인정한 적은 없어도 그런 구조의 불편함을 느끼고 있었고 또 그것을 덜어 보려 애쓴 바 있었다. 커다란 가리개가 현관문과 홀의 한 부분을 막아 놓았고, 그렇게 가려진 모퉁이에서 두 번째 문이 안뜰에 면한 집의 긴 쪽을 따라 뻗은 복도로 이어졌다. 큰 홀이 저택의 작은 쪽 — 쇠문으로 들어설 때 그대의 왼편에 있던 그 부분 — 의 일층을 이루며 그 날개 전부를 차지한다는 것이 분명해지지 않는다면, 나의 독자나 나 둘 중 하나가 지형을 다루는 솜씨가 서툰 것이리라. 또한 그 홀이 말끔히 깎은 잔디밭을 내다보고 있다는 것, 그것도 돌 문설주가 세워진 네모난 창 셋을 통하여 그러하다는 것, 창마다 아래쪽의 큰 부분과 위쪽의 작은 부분으로 나뉘고 그 각 부분이 다시 세로로 선 돌 받침으로 다섯으로 나뉜다는 것도 분명해질 것이다. 이런 창보다 빛을 잘 들이는 창이 있을 수 있고, 나의 실용주의자 벗이 말하듯 빛을 들이는 것이야말로 창이 노리는 바일지도 모른다. 나는 그와 이 문제를 두고 다투지 않겠다. 사실 다툴 수도 없다. 그러나 그렇다 하여, 울라손 저택에 채택된 창만큼 사람에게 큰 행복을 나누어 줄 수 있는 창은 어떤 종류에도 어떤 모양에도 없다는 굳은 확신을 품은 채 죽으리라는 데에는 변함이 없다. 뭐라, 내닫이창도 아니라고? 하고 다이애나 드 미들리지 양이 말한다. 그렇소, 다이애나 양, 내닫이창조차 아니라오. 내닫이창이 아무리 아름답다 한들 말이오. 거기에는 고요하고 소박한 영국식 안락의 느낌이 그만큼 온전히 깃들어 있지 않소. 내닫이창일랑 대학이나 세도 있는 귀족의 반쯤 공적인 저택을 꾸미게 두시오. 조용한 시골 부인들, 평범하고 수수한 사람들이 앉아 지내는 방으로 말하자면, 튜더 시대 건축가들이 지은 저 네모나고 문설주로 나뉜 창에 견줄 것이 없소.\n\n홀에는 렐리가 그린 밋밋한 집안 여인들과 넬러가 그린 붉은 저고리 차림의 볼품없는 손 집안 남자들이 죽 걸려 있었는데, 손 집안 사람 하나하나가 격식대로 벽 판자의 칸 속에 끼워져 있었다. 방 저편 끝에는 커다란 벽난로가 있었으니, 이것이 남매 사이에 적잖은 다툼거리를 안겨 주었다. 석탄을 백 근쯤 담을 만한 구식 쇠 화격자를 손 씨의 아버지가 난로 바닥에 박아 놓았던 것이다. 이 난로 바닥은 물론 장작단을 때기 위해 만들어진 것이어서, 그 용도로 쓰던 쇠 받침대가 화격자의 돌 틈에 반쯤 파묻힌 채 아직도 서 있었다. 손 양은 그 쇠 받침대로 되돌아가기를 몹시 바랐다. 그 사랑스럽고 착한 노부인은 무엇으로든 되돌아가기를 언제나 반겼으니, 하자는 대로 다 해 주었더라면 머지않아 포크보다 손가락이 먼저 생겼다는 데에 생각이 미쳐 그에 걸맞게 되돌아갔을 것이 틀림없다. 그러나 벽난로에 관한 한 손 씨는 되돌아가려 하지 않았다. 이웃의 시골 신사들은 모두 제 식당에 편리한 화격자를 두고 있었다. 그가 요즘 방식을 먼저 제안할 사람은 아니었으나, 아버지가 제 쓰라고 마련해 둔 것을 몰아낼 만큼 편견에 사로잡히지도 않았다. 사실 손 씨는 조금만 손을 보면 현관문을 고쳐 적어도 복도로 열리게 할 수 있으리라고 한 번 말을 꺼낸 적이 있었는데, 그 말을 들은 누이 모니카는 — 손 양의 이름이 그러하였다 — 자리에 몸져누워 일주일을 그대로 있었다. 아래층으로 내려오기 전에 그녀는 제 살아생전에는 결코 출입구를 바꾸지 않겠다는 다짐을 오라비에게서 받아 냈다.\n\n벽난로 맞은편 홀 끝에 난 문은 응접실로 이어졌는데, 이 방은 홀과 크기가 같았고 꼭 같은 모양의 창으로 빛을 들였다. 그런데도 방의 느낌은 사뭇 달랐다. 벽에는 종이를 발랐고, 홀에서는 옛 서까래가 그대로 드러나 있던 천장이 여기서는 희게 칠해지고 요즘식 돌림띠로 마무리되어 있었다. 손 양의 응접실은 — 그녀는 늘 그것을 물러나 앉는 방이라 불렀다 — 아름다운 방이었다. 창은 어여쁘고 말끔한 정원 전부를 향하여 열렸다. 창 바로 앞에는 뻣뻣하고 위엄 있고 고집스러운 작은 화단들에 꽃이 심겨 있었고, 화단마다 제 몫의 돌 테두리가 둘려 있었다. 그 너머에는 낮은 난간 담이 있어 그 위에 항아리와 조각상들, 파우누스와 님프와 사티로스, 그리고 판을 따르는 온갖 무리가 서 있었다. 다시 그 너머로는 아름다운 잔디밭이 비스듬히 내려가 땅에 파묻힌 울타리에 닿았고, 그 울타리가 정원과 사냥터를 갈라 놓았다. 손 씨의 서재는 응접실 끝에 있었고, 그 너머에 부엌과 살림방들이 있었다. 손 양의 물러나 앉는 방으로도 손 씨의 성소로도 앞서 말한 복도에서 문이 열렸으며, 그 복도는 뒤쪽 방에 이르러 폭이 넓어지면서 위층으로 오르는 커다란 검은 참나무 계단이 들어설 자리를 내주었다.\n\n울라손 저택의 안은 이러하였다. 그러나 이렇게, 어쩌면 좀 지루하리만큼 늘어놓아 그려 보이고서 하는 말이지만, 우리가 영국 여행자의 눈길을 끌고 싶은 것은 이 안쪽이 아니다. 물론 이 안쪽과 정답게 사귈 마땅한 기회가 오거든 놓치지 말라고 권하기는 하겠다. 그토록 사랑스러운 것은 울라손의 바깥이다. 여행자는 적어도 정원에 드는 허락만이라도 얻어, 집이 바깥으로 꺾이는 모퉁이 바로 맞은편의 그 부드러운 잔디 위에 몸을 던져 보라. 그러면 그는 두 면의 앞모습을 한꺼번에 보게 되고, 그토록 사랑스러운 것 — 곧 한 줄로 길게 뻗은 격식 차린 밋밋함 없이 펼쳐진 건축의 아름다움 — 을 누리게 될 것이다.\n\n울라손에서 그토록 놀라운 것은 그 빛깔이다. 그것은 수백 년에 걸친 초목의 풍요를 제 몸에 얹지 않고서는 어떤 돌도 낼 수 없는, 그 감미로운 황갈색이다. 손으로 담을 쳐 보라. 그러면 돌 위에 아무것도 덮여 있지 않다고 여기게 될 것이다. 그러나 조심스레 문질러 보라. 그러면 그 빛깔이 손가락에 묻어 나오는 것을 알게 될 것이다. 팔레트를 놓고 작업한 어떤 색채가도 세월 위에 세월이 겹겹이 쌓여 이룬 이 풍요로운 빛깔에는 여태 미치지 못하였다.\n\n울라손은 시골집치고는 높은 건물이니, 삼층으로 되어 있고 층마다 앞서 말한 것과 같은 종류의 창이 나 있되 크기가 저마다 다르고 집을 가로지르는 줄도 저마다 다르다. 일층의 창들은 크기도 자리도 모두 한결같다. 그러나 그 위층의 창들은 크기도 자리도 들쭉날쭉하며, 이 들쭉날쭉함이 건물에 기이하면서도 그림 같지 않다고는 할 수 없는 모습을 준다. 꼭대기를 따라 사방으로 낮은 난간이 둘려 지붕을 거의 가리고 있고, 모퉁이마다 파우누스와 사티로스의 상이 또 서 있다.\n\n울라손 저택은 이러하다. 그러나 그리로 다가가는 길에 관하여 한마디 해 두어야겠으니, 여기에 교회에 관해 우리가 하려는 이야기도 모두 함께 담기게 될 것이다. 그림 같은 세인트 이월드의 옛 교회는 안뜰로 들어가는 쇠문 바로 맞은편에 서 있고, 양편에서 집으로 이어지는 가로수길을 이루는 보리수 가지에 거의 온통 둘러싸여 있다. 이 가로수길은 훌륭하지만, 그 사이로 난 길이 사유지가 아니라는 사실 때문에 많은 지주들의 눈에는 그 값어치가 크게 깎일 것이다. 그것은 산울타리 사이로 난 공용 소로이며, 길 양편에는 널찍한 풀밭 가장자리가 있고 그 풀밭에서 보리수가 솟아 있다. 그러므로 울라손 저택은 제 땅에 온전히 둘러싸여 있지 않은 셈이나, 인접한 땅은 모두 손 씨의 소유이다. 그러나 이 일로 그가 성가셔하는 바는 거의 없다. 사람이 재산을 늘려 가는 중에는 그런 것을 크게 마음에 두지만, 제 조상이 여러 해 살아온 곳에서 사는 이들은 그것을 불행으로 느끼지 않는다. 세상 사람들이 마음만 먹으면 제 쇠문 앞을 걷거나 마차로 지날 수 있다고 하여 저희가 충분히 사적이지 못하다는 생각은 손 씨에게도 손 양에게도 떠오른 적이 없다. 다만 그 특권을 실제로 누리는 세상 사람이란 아주 적었다.\n\n한두 해 전 울라손의 손 남매는 이러하였다. 우리가 믿기로는 영국의 숱한 시골집 사람들이 이러하다. 부디 그 수가 줄어들기까지 오랜 세월이 걸리기를.\n\n# 제23장. 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드에서 취임 낭독을 하다\n\n일요일 아침, 부주교는 처제와 애러빈 씨를 데리고 예정대로 울라손으로 마차를 몰았다. 가는 길에 새 교구 사제는 처음으로 제 교구민들 앞에 서야 한다는 생각에 마음이 적잖이 어지럽다고 털어놓았다. 자기는 늘 mauvaise honte에, 성가실 만큼 심한 수줍음에 시달려 왔고 그 탓에 처음 해 보는 종류의 일이라면 무엇이든 제대로 해내지 못하기 일쑤였는데, 지금이 바로 그러해서 세인트 이월드의 낭독대에서 스스로 망신을 당하지나 않을까 두렵다는 것이었다. 손 양의 그 날카롭고 작은 눈이 자기를 지켜볼 테고 그 눈이 흡족해하지 않으리라는 것도 안다고 그는 말했다. 부주교는 이 모든 말을 몹시 우스워했다. 그 자신은 수줍음이 무엇인지 알지 못했고 여태 알아 본 적도 없었다. 울라손의 소작인들과 바체스터 변두리의 가난한 주민 몇몇에게 둘러싸여 있을 손 양이, 옥스퍼드 세인트 메리 교회의 그 학식 높은 회중을 상대로 말하는 데 익숙한 사람의 침착함을 어떻게 흔들 수 있단 말인가. 그래서 그는 애러빈 씨의 겸양이라는 것을 두고 소리 내어 웃었다.\n\n그러자 애러빈 씨는 잘게 따지고 들기 시작했다. 세인트 메리에서 세인트 이월드로 옮겨 오는 변화는 세인트 이월드에서 세인트 메리로 옮겨 가는 변화만큼이나 사람의 마음을 뒤흔든다고 그는 말했다. 어쩌다 운수가 뒤집혀 갑자기 막노동꾼들 틈에 섞여 살게 된 귀족이라면, 별안간 귀족들 사이에 자리를 얻은 막노동꾼이 그러하듯, 제 동료들의 비웃음을 두려워하지 않겠느냐는 것이었다. 그 말에 부주교는 큰 소리로 웃으며, 새로 온 성직자가 손 양을 막노동꾼에 견주더라고 손 양에게 일러바치겠다고 선언했다. 그러나 엘리너는 그런 결론은 온당치 않다고 했다. 견주어진 두 가지를 조금도 같은 것으로 만들지 않으면서도 그 비례만은 아주 정확한 비유가 있을 수 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 애러빈 씨는 부주교의 놀림에도 엘리너의 두둔에도 아랑곳없이 계속 따지고 들었다. 어떤 젊은 아가씨는 낯선 사람들로 가득한 방에서 어려운 곡을 더할 나위 없이 침착하게 연주해 내면서도, 가장 가까운 벗들 사이에서 그저 평범한 이야기를 하는 것조차 사람들보다 조금 높은 상자 위에 올라서서 하라고 하면 알아들을 만한 말로 제 뜻을 밝히지 못한다고 그는 말했다. 그것은 전적으로 익힘의 문제인데, 마흔에 이른 자기로서는 스스로를 새로 익히기가 어렵다는 것이었다.\n\n엘리너는 상자 이야기에는 동의하지 않았다. 무서움 없이 올라설 만큼 넉넉히 크기만 하다면 어떤 상자 위에서든, 벗들이 모두 듣고 있다 하더라도 옷이나 아기나 양고기 다리에 대해서라면 아주 잘 이야기할 수 있노라고 그녀는 장담했다. 부주교는 그녀가 한마디도 하지 못하리라고 확신했지만 그것이 애러빈 씨에게 유리한 증거가 되지는 않는다고 했다. 애러빈 씨는 언젠가 목사관에 손님이 가득할 때 볼드 부인을 상자 위에 세워 이 문제를 끝까지 겨루어 보겠다고 말했다. 엘리너는 손님이 자기네 편 사람들이어야 한다는 조건을 달고 이에 응했고, 부주교는 그런 조건에 슬로프 씨도 포함하겠다는 뜻이 담긴 것은 아닌지 속으로 궁리하면서, 만약 그렇다면 그 시험은 플럼스테드 목사관 응접실에서는 결코 벌어지지 않으리라고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그렇게 옥신각신하는 사이 마차는 울라손 저택의 철문 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n손 씨와 손 양은 예배에 갈 채비를 다 갖추고 현관에 서 있다가 성직자 손님들을 살갑게 맞았다. 부주교는 오래된 총아였다. 그는 구식 성직자였고, 그 점이 이 댁 부인의 마음에 들었다. 자유 무역이 아직 논쟁거리로 열려 있는 동안 그는 줄곧 자유 무역의 반대자였고, 이제 그것이 더는 논쟁거리가 아니게 된 지금도 그는 성직자인 까닭에 세속의 토리 동료들 대부분과 달리 제 주장을 철회한다고 밝혀야 할 의무가 없었다. 그러므로 그는 흠 없는 53인의 지지자로 여겨질 수 있었고, 바로 그 점 때문에 손 씨의 총애를 받았다. 작은 종이 딸랑거리고 있었고, 교구의 시골 사람들은 새 성직자가 저택에서 목사관 쪽으로 지나가는 모습을 한번 보려고 골목에 서서 교회 층계문에 기대거나 오래된 저택 담벼락에 몸을 붙이고 있었다. 부주교의 하인은 예복을 들고 벌써 그리로 앞서 가 있었다.\n\n그들은 다 함께 밖으로 나섰고, 부인들이 교회 안으로 들어간 뒤 세 신사는 잠시 골목에 머물렀다. 손 씨가 교구민 가운데 가장 유력한 이들을 새 교구 사제에게 한쪽만 소개하는 식으로 알려 주기 위해서였다.\n\n“여기 우리 교회위원들이오, 애러빈 씨 — 그린에이커 농부와 스타일스 씨요. 스타일스 씨는 바체스터로 들어가는 길목에서 방앗간을 하고 있소. 두 사람 다 아주 훌륭한 교회위원이라오.”\n\n“너무 엄하지는 않으시기를 바랍니다.” 애러빈 씨가 말했다. 두 교회 직원은 모자에 손을 얹고 시골식 예법에 따라 각각 한쪽 다리를 뒤로 빼며 절을 했다. 그러고는 이렇게 뵙는 영광을 누리게 되어 매우 기쁘다고 교구 사제에게 인사한 뒤, 추수하기에 날씨가 아주 좋다고 덧붙였다. 도회지 물을 좀 먹은 축인 스타일스 씨는 제 위신에 대한 나름의 생각이 있어서, 교회위원인 자기가 예배 시간에 아이들을 단속한다는 잘못된 짐작을 목자가 그대로 품게 두고 싶지 않았다. 애러빈 씨가 제 엄함을 두고 한 말을 그는 그렇게 알아들었고, 그래서 서둘러 바로잡으려고 “애들은 클로디브 교회지기가 봅니다요. 설교 중에 매질이 좀 지나칠 때가 더러 있기도 합지요.” 하고 말했다. 부주교와 눈이 마주치자 애러빈 씨의 밝은 눈이 반짝였고, 그는 제 관리들이 제 권한의 성격을, 그리고 그들이 자기 자신까지도 감독해야 하는 그 직분을 얼마나 모르고 있는지를 보며 속으로 웃었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 성경 봉독을 하고 설교를 했다. 강단 낭독에 아무리 익숙한 사람이라도, 새로 온 성직자가 얼마 전 저희를 떠난 이의 훌륭함에 미치는지 못 미치는지를 속으로 견주어 보느라 농부들이 아는 체하며 귀를 쫑긋 세우는 꼴을 보면 조금은 마음이 흔들릴 만했다. 지금 이 순간에는 마음속의 소리 없는 비평이었지만, 머지않아 세인트 이월드의 노인들 사이에서, 제 자식들과 조상들의 푸른 무덤 위에서 공공연히 오갈 비평이었다. 그러나 가엾은 노 구드너프 씨의 훌륭함이라는 것도 그리 대단치는 않았던 터라, 처음에 그를 조금 더듬거리게 만들어 부주교를 거의 미치게 했던 그 가벼운 긴장에도 불구하고 애러빈 씨가 제 몫을 충분히 잘해 냈다고 여기지 않는 사람은 거기에 거의 없었다.\n\n그러나 사람을 시험하는 것은 역시 설교였다. 아주 젊은 사람들이 낯선 회중 앞에서 난생처음 설교할 용기를 낸다는 것은 우리에게 종종 놀라운 일이다. 아직 소년보다 조금 나은 정도인 사람들이, 학교라고 부를 수는 없으나 학자로 길러 내기 위한 배움터라 할 만한 곳을 갓 나온 사람들이, 그 머릿속이 대개 뱃놀이와 크리켓과 술자리로 채워져 있던 사람들이, 순종하는 무리의 머리보다 훨씬 높은 연단에 오른다. 아래 있는 이들에게 하나님의 말씀을 읽어 주기 위해서가 아니라, 듣는 이들을 교화하겠다며 제 말을 설교하기 위해서다. 그 새롭고 두려운 자리의 엄숙함에 그들이 벙어리가 되지 않는다는 것이 우리에게는 이상해 보인다. “생각하는 힘이 내게 처음 생긴 이래 사려 깊은 날이라고는 열흘도 보내 본 적이 없는, 이제 갓 스물셋이 된 내가 어떻게, 그 숱한 세월의 고단한 생각으로 무덤 가까이 다다른 저 백발의 노인들을 가르친단 말인가? 내가 그들에게 그들의 본분을 가르칠 수 있는가? 내가 그토록 어설프게 이해하고 있는 것을, 그들에게는 여러 해의 공부로 이미 환해졌을 그것을 내가 설명할 수 있는가? 하나님의 사역자 가운데 하나가 되었다는 이 갓 얻은 특권이, 설교자라는 저 놀라운 일에 걸맞은 자질을 내게 조금이라도 나누어 주었는가?”\n\n그런 생각이 젊은 성직자들에게 실제로 떠오르리라고 보아야 할 텐데, 그럼에도 그들은 우리에게는 거의 넘을 수 없어 보이는 이 어려움을 겉보기에는 손쉽게 이겨 낸다. 우리는 안수를 받느라 주교의 손 아래 놓여 본 적이 없다. 어쩌면 그 손 안에 정신을 떠받치고 젊음의 타고난 수줍음을 몰아내는 무언가가 있는지도 모른다. 그러나 우리로서는 이렇게 고백해야겠다. 도미니 샘슨이 어린 제자들에게 품었던 그 깊은 애정보다도, 하나님의 복음을 전해 보겠다는 헛된 시도로 강단에 올랐다가 한마디도 못 하고 초라하게 내려오게 만든 그 수줍은 심성이 우리에게는 더 정답다.\n\n우리 교회에는 아래 등급의 성직자가 예배의 어느 부분을 집전하지 못하도록 금하는 규칙이 있다. 사죄 선언은 사제 품계를 받은 성직자가 낭독해야 한다. 그런 성직자가 그 자리에 없으면 회중은 각자가 스스로에게 베풀 수 있는 사죄 말고는 어떤 사죄의 은혜도 누릴 수 없다. 그 규칙이 왜 필요한지는 보통의 이해로는 좀처럼 와닿지 않으나, 좋은 규칙일지도 모른다. 그러나 젊은이가 삼가는 이 미덕이 설교에까지 미친다면 훨씬 더 고맙게 여겨질 것이다. 다만 한 가지 위험이 있다면, 회중이 저희 젊은 성직자가 성직의 계단을 올라가는 것을 너무나도 막고 싶어 하리라는 점이다. 설교할 줄 모르는 성직자는 그야말로 복이어서, 사람들이 뇌물을 주어서라도 그 무능에 머물러 있게 하려 들 것이다.\n\n그러나 애러빈 씨에게는 그를 가로막을 젊음의 수줍음이 없었고, 그는 성경 봉독보다도 설교에서 한결 더 잘해 냈다. 그는 요한이서에서 두 구절을 본문으로 삼았다. “지내쳐 그리스도의 교훈 안에 거하지 아니하는 자는 다 하나님을 모시지 못하되 교훈 안에 거하는 그 사람은 아버지와 아들을 모시느니라. 누구든지 이 교훈을 가지지 않고 너희에게 나아가거든 그를 집에 들이지도 말고 인사도 말라.” 그는 자기가 가리킨 그들의 집이란 바로 이 그들의 교회, 자기가 오늘 처음으로 그들에게 말을 건네고 있는 이곳이라고 일렀다. 자기에게 인사하는 가장 반갑고 온당한 방법은 자기가 전하는 복음의 가르침에 참을성 있게 순종하는 것이겠으나, 믿음과 행함이 함께 간다는 저 위대한 그리스도교의 교리를 자기가 가르치지 않는 한 그들에게 그런 태도를 요구할 자격이 자기에게는 없다고 했다. 그는 이 대목을 부연했으나 그리 길게 늘이지는 않았고, 20분 만에 새 벗들을 구운 양고기와 푸딩이 기다리는 집으로, 새 성직자에게 흡족해하며 돌려보내는 데 성공했다.\n\n그러고 나서 울라손에서 점심이 있었다. 현관에 들어서자마자 손 양은 애러빈 씨의 손을 잡고, 자기가 그를 제 집으로, 자기가 예배드리는 성전으로 맞아들이는 것이라고 말하며 마음을 다해 그의 앞길을 축복한다고 했다. 애러빈 씨는 마음이 움직여 한마디 대답도 못 한 채 그 노처녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그러자 손 씨가 그 교회가 발성에 잘 맞더냐고 물었고, 애러빈 씨가 그 건물에 목소리를 맞추는 법만 익히면 틀림없이 그러할 것이라고 대답하자, 다들 차려진 좋은 음식 앞에 앉았다.\n\n손 양은 볼드 부인을 각별히 챙겼다. 엘리너는 아직 미망인의 상복을 입고 있었고, 그래서 얼마 전 남편을 잃은 여인들의 몫인 그 무겁고 슬픈 모성의 기운을 두르고 있었다. 그것이 손 양의 여린 마음을 열어 놓아, 이 젊은 손님에게는 무엇을 해 주어도 지나치지 않다는 듯 그녀를 바라보게 했다. 손 양은 엘리너의 접시에 닭고기와 햄을 수북이 얹어 주고 포트 와인을 잔이 넘치도록 따라 주었다. 마다할 것도 없었던 엘리너가 조금 마시자 손 양은 곧바로 그 잔을 다시 채우려 들었다. 엘리너가 이를 사양했으나 소용이 없었다. 손 양은 눈짓을 하고 고개를 끄덕이며, 이것이 마땅한 일이고 반드시 그리해야 하며 자기가 그 사정을 다 안다고 소곤거렸다. 그러고는 볼드 부인더러 남 눈치 볼 것 없이 다 마시라고 했다.\n\n“몸을 지탱하는 것이 댁의 도리요.” 그녀는 젊은 어미의 귀에 대고 말했다. “거기 매인 것이 댁 하나만이 아니잖소.” 그렇게 그녀는 차가운 닭고기와 포트 와인으로 엘리너를 어르고 먹였다. 어르고 먹일 차가운 닭고기도 포트 와인도 없는 가난한 이의 아내들은 아무 어려움 없이 제 아이를 젖 먹여 기르는데, 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 먹고 마시는 부자의 아내들은 어찌하여 그러지 못하는지, 그 문제는 당분간 의사들과 어머니들이 저희끼리 결말을 짓도록 남겨 두기로 하자.\n\n그다음으로 손 양이 열을 올린 것은 이였다. 어린 조니 볼드는 며칠 전부터 처음 돋아나려는 첫 이 때문에 보채고 있었는데, 부인네들 사이에 있는 그 비밀 결사 같은 통함으로 손 양은 엘리너가 닭 날개를 절반도 먹기 전에 그 사실을 알아차렸다. 노부인은 제 할머니의 젊은 시절에 크게 유행했던 처방을 당장 일러 주고는, 근래 의술의 헛됨을 두고 엄숙한 목소리로 엘리너에게 경고했다.\n\n“아기 산호를 가져다가,” 그녀가 말했다. “당근즙을 잘 문질러 바르시오. 즙이 그 위에서 마를 때까지 문지른 다음에 아기 손에 쥐여 주어 놀게 하면—”\n\n“그런데 그 애한테는 산호가 없어요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“산호가 없다고!” 손 양이 거의 노여워하다시피 목소리를 높였다. “산호가 없다니 — 그러고서 어떻게 아이 이가 나기를 바란단 말이오? 대피 영약은 갖고 있소?”\n\n엘리너는 그것도 없다고 설명했다. 자기가 부르는 바체스터 의사 리어차일드 씨가 처방해 준 적이 없다는 것이었다. 그러고서 젊은 어미는 리어차일드 씨가 새로 얻은 식견에 따라 권하게 되었다는, 듣기에 소름 끼치도록 현대적인 대용약의 이름을 입에 올렸다.\n\n손 양의 표정이 무섭도록 엄해졌다. “조심하시오.” 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람이 제가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지나 아는지 조심하란 말이오. 그자가 댁의 어린 아들을 망쳐 놓지 않도록 조심하시오. 하지만” — 그러면서 그녀는 슬픔으로 누그러졌고, 노여움보다는 안타까움을 실어 말했다 — “하지만 지금 바체스터에 믿을 만한 사람이 누가 있는지 나로서는 알 수가 없구려. 가엾은 범프웰 노 박사님이라면 그야—”\n\n“아니, 손 양, 그분은 제가 어린 여자아이일 때 돌아가셨는데요.”\n\n“그렇소, 이보오, 그분은 돌아가셨지. 바체스터로서는 불운한 날이었소. 그 뒤로 올라온 젊은 사람들로 말하면” — 그런데 리어차일드 씨는 손 양 자신과 나이가 엇비슷했다 — “어디서 왔는지, 누구인지, 제 일을 조금이라도 아는지 어떤지 도무지 알 수가 없단 말이오.”\n\n“바체스터에도 아주 훌륭한 분들이 계신다고 생각해요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“그럴지도 모르지. 다만 내가 그 사람들을 모를 뿐이오. 그리고 요즘 의원들이 예전 같지 않다는 것은 누구나 인정하는 바요. 예전에는 재주 있고 잘 살피고 배운 사람들이었소. 그런데 이제는 약재상 가게에서 굴러 나온 애송이라면 누구나 제 입으로 의사라 하지. 요즘은 어떤 배움도 필요치 않다고 여기는 모양이오.”\n\n엘리너 자신이 의사의 미망인이었으니 이런 모진 말들에 조금은 발끈할 만도 했다. 그러나 손 양은 워낙 천성이 좋은 사람이어서 그녀가 하는 말에는 무엇에도 발끈할 수가 없었다. 그래서 엘리너는 포도주를 홀짝이며 닭고기를 다 먹었다.\n\n“어쨌든 이보오, 당근즙은 잊지 말고, 무슨 수를 써서라도 아기 산호는 당장 구해 주시오. 우리 할머니 손 부인은 이 고을에서 가장 좋은 이를 지니셨고 여든에 그것을 그대로 지닌 채 무덤에 드셨소. 그게 다 당근즙 덕이라고 하시는 말씀을 나는 들었소. 할머니는 바체스터 의사들을 견디지 못하셨지. 가엾은 범프웰 노 박사님조차 할머니 마음에는 차지 않았다오.” 오십 년쯤 전에는 범프웰 박사도 이제 막 올라오는 사람이었을 뿐이고, 따라서 그 시절 울라손 부인네들의 눈에는 지금 의사들이 손 양의 눈에 그러하듯 신용이 아쉬운 처지였으리라는 생각은 손 양에게 끝내 떠오르지 않은 것이 분명했다.\n\n부주교는 아주 잘 먹었고, 주인에게 순무 파종기며 새로 나온 수확 기계 이야기를 했다. 한편 주인은 손님을 살피는 것이 예의라고만 여긴 데다 어쩌면 이 손님이 일요일에 순무 농사 이야기를 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다는 걱정에, 온갖 교회 이야기를 꺼내 들었다.\n\n“손, 저 잡목숲 너머 밭에 난 밀만큼 실한 것은 본 적이 없소. 구아노를 쓴 모양이구려.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소, 구아노요. 나는 브리스틀에서 직접 가져오지요. 애러빈 씨, 여기 바체스터 사람들이 제법 많이 예배에 나오는 것을 보게 될 거요. 사람들이 세인트 이월드를 아주 좋아하거든. 걷기에 너무 덥지 않은 오후면 더욱 그렇소.”\n\n“어쨌든 오늘 오지 않아 준 것만은 그분들께 고마울 따름입니다.” 교구 사제가 말했다. “첫 설교에는 회중이 적으면 적을수록 좋지요.”\n\n“하이 스트리트의 브래들리네에서 1.5톤을 샀는데,” 부주교가 말했다. “완전히 속아 넘어갔소. 그 속에 구아노가 5헌드레드웨이트나 들었을까 싶소.”\n\n“그 브래들리라는 사람은 좋은 물건이라고는 없지요.” 엘리너와 소곤거리다 그 이름만 얼핏 알아들은 손 양이 말했다. “그 사람이 오기 전에는 바로 그 집이 얼마나 좋은 가게였는데. 윌프레드, 늙은 앰블오프가 얼마나 좋은 물건을 두었는지 기억나지 않소?”\n\n“앰블오프 시절 이후로 사람이 셋이나 바뀌었는데,” 부주교가 말했다. “하나같이 서로 못지않게 형편없었소. 그런데 손, 브리스틀에서는 누가 그것을 대 주오?”\n\n“올해는 내가 직접 올라가서 배에서 바로 사 왔소. 애러빈 씨, 저녁이 짧아지면 낭독대가 너무 어두워서 곤란하실 거요. 사람을 시켜 도끼로 저 가지들을 좀 쳐 내게 해야겠소.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 아침 빛만은 더할 나위 없다고 하면서 보리수에 손대는 일에는 반대했다. 그러고서 그들은 잘 손질된 화단 사이를 거닐었고, 애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인에게 나이아드와 드리아드의 차이를 설명해 주고 화병과 항아리의 모양에 대해 길게 이야기했다. 손 양은 제 팬지꽃 사이에서 분주했고, 그녀의 오라비는 대화에 일요일다운 색조를 조금이라도 입히기가 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 깨닫고는 그 시도를 포기한 채 부주교와 브리스틀 구아노 이야기를 실컷 했다.\n\n세 시에 그들은 다시 교회에 들어갔고, 이번에는 애러빈 씨가 예배를 인도하고 부주교가 설교를 했다. 거의 같은 회중이 모였는데, 여기에 한낮 8월 볕의 열기쯤은 발길을 돌릴 만한 것이 못 된다고 여긴 도시의 겁 없는 도보객 몇이 더해졌다. 부주교는 빌레몬서에서 본문을 취했다. “갇힌 중에서 낳은 아들 오네시모를 위하여 네게 간구하노라.” 그런 본문에서 그랜틀리 박사가 어떤 설교를 했을지는 짐작할 수 있을 터인데, 대체로 그것은 지루하지도, 나쁘지도, 자리에 어울리지 않지도 않았다.\n\n그는 그들에게, 오랫동안 그들 가운데 있던 이의 자리를 메울 목자를 찾아보는 일이 제 소임이 되었고, 그리하여 바울이 자기가 보내는 젊은 제자를 여겼듯 자기도 제가 고른 사람을 아들로 여긴다고 말했다. 그러고는 후원이나 정실을 돌아보지 않고 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 사람을 애써 구해 놓았다며 제 공을 조금 내세웠다. 다만 제 생각에 가장 좋은 사람이란 슬로프 씨를 눌러 그자가 바체스터에서 편히 지내지 못하도록 만들 능력이 가장 뛰어난 사람이라는 말은 하지 않았다. 갇힘으로 말하자면, 그것은 그들에게 좋은 성직자를 구해 주려고 자기가 벌인 그 엄청난 분투에 있었다. 그는 자기를 바울에 견주는 일은 삼가야 한다고 하면서도, 사도가 오네시모를 두고 빌레몬과 그 집안 사람들에게 간구했던 것과 같은 방식으로 자기도 애러빈 씨를 향한 그들의 호의를 간구할 자격은 있다고 말했다.\n\n부주교의 설교는 본문이며 축도며 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않고도 반 시간 안에 끝났다. 그러고 나서 그들은 울라손의 벗들과 악수를 나누고 플럼스테드로 돌아갔다. 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드에서 취임 낭독을 한 것은 이러하였다.\n\n# 제24장. 슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일에서 일을 아주 능란하게 처리하다\n\n그 뒤 두 주는 플럼스테드에서 제법 유쾌하게 흘러갔다. 그곳에 모인 사람들은 다들 서로 잘 어울리는 듯했다. 엘리너는 집안 분위기를 화기애애하게 만들었고, 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인은 슬로프 씨에 관한 그녀의 죄를 잊은 것 같았다. 하딩 씨에게는 첼로가 있어서, 두 딸이 반주를 맡는 가운데 사람들에게 연주를 들려주었다. 조니 볼드는 리어차일드 씨 덕분인지 아니면 산호 노리개와 당근즙 덕분인지, 이앓이의 고생을 무사히 넘겼다. 온갖 즐거운 자리도 있었다. 그들은 울라손에서 만찬을 들었고, 손 남매도 목사관에서 만찬을 들었다. 엘리너는 어김없이 그 상자 위에 올라서야 했는데, 그 자세로는 주름 장식의 좋고 나쁨에 관해 제 의견을 도무지 밝힐 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다. 발성 시험 삼아 주어진 주제가 바로 그것이었기 때문이다. 애러빈 씨는 물론 제 교구에 머무는 때가 많아, 목사관의 공사를 살피고 교구민을 찾아보며 새 직분의 의무를 떠맡았다. 그래도 저녁마다 플럼스테드에 와 있었으므로, 그랜틀리 부인도 그가 한집에 두기 좋은 사람이라는 남편의 말에 어느 정도는 동의할 마음이 들었다.\n\n그들은 스탠호프 박사 댁 만찬에도 갔고, 애러빈 씨도 그 자리에 끼었다. 그 역시 불나방처럼 시뇨라의 촛불에 날개를 태웠다. 볼드 부인도 그 자리에 있었는데, 애러빈 씨가 네로니 부인에게 그토록 마음을 쏟는 데서 드러난 취향이 — 그녀는 그것을 취향의 결핍이라 불렀다 — 다소 못마땅했다. 마들린이 남자를 홀리고 사로잡는 것이 어김없듯, 여자를 언짢게 하고 약 오르게 하는 것도 어김없었다. 한쪽 결과는 다른 쪽 결과에서 자연히 따라 나왔다. 애러빈 씨가 홀린 것은 사실이었다. 그는 그녀가 무척 영리하고 무척 아름다운 여자라고 생각했고, 또 그녀의 남다른 불행이 모든 이의 동정을 받을 만하다고 여겼다. 그만한 고통이 그토록 완벽한 아름다움과 그토록 맑은 정신에 함께 깃든 경우는 여태 본 적이 없다고 그는 말했다. 부주교의 마차를 타고 돌아오는 길에 그는 시뇨라를 두고 그렇게 말했고, 엘리너는 그 찬사를 듣는 것이 조금도 달갑지 않았다. 그러나 애러빈 씨에게 화를 내는 것은 지극히 부당한 일이었다. 그녀 자신도 버티 스탠호프와 아주 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈기 때문이다. 버티는 그녀를 만찬석으로 안내했고, 남자들이 식당에서 나온 뒤로는 한순간도 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 제 딴에는 버티와 즐겨 놓고 새 벗이 버티의 누이와 즐기는 자유는 못마땅해하다니 공평하지 못한 일이었다. 그런데도 그녀는 그랬다. 마차 안에서 그녀는 그에게 반쯤 화가 나 있었고, 요염한 태도가 어떻다는 말을 했다. 애러빈 씨는 여자의 속내를 잘 알지 못했다. 그러지 않았더라면 엘리너가 자기를 사랑하는 모양이라고 우쭐해 볼 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 엘리너는 그를 사랑하지 않았다. 사랑과 무관심 사이에는 얼마나 많은 층이 있으며, 그 눈금은 또 얼마나 알려져 있지 않은가! 그녀는 애러빈 씨와 한집에서 지낸 지 벌써 세 주가 다 되어 갔고, 그의 관심을 많이 받았으며 날마다 그의 이야기를 들었다. 그는 대개 저녁 시간의 얼마쯤은 그녀에게만 바쳤다. 그런데 스탠호프 박사 댁에서는 다른 여자에게만 자신을 바쳤다. 이런 일에 약이 오르는 데에는 사랑에 빠져 있을 필요가 없다. 그것이 제게 불쾌하다고 스스로 인정할 필요조차 없다. 엘리너에게는 그만한 자기 인식이 없었다. 그가 시뇨라 따위에게 즐거워할 만큼 몸을 낮춘 것이 안타깝다면 그것은 오로지 애러빈 씨를 위해서라고 그녀는 마음속으로 생각했다. “그분에게 좀 더 깊은 정신이 있는 줄 알았는데.” 연회에서 돌아와 아기의 요람을 지켜보고 앉아 그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다. “결국 스탠호프 씨가 둘 중에서는 더 유쾌한 사람인 것 같아.” 가엾은 존 볼드의 기억이여! 엘리너는 버티 스탠호프를 사랑하지도, 애러빈 씨를 사랑하지도 않았다. 그러나 죽은 남편의 아이 요람 곁에서 제 환심을 노리는 다른 사내들의 허물과 결점을 이리저리 곱씹을 수 있게 되었다면, 남편을 향한 그녀의 헌신은 빠르게 스러져 가고 있는 것이었다.\n\n이 일로 내 여주인공을 나무랄 사람이 있겠는가? 그럴 바에는 남자든 여자든 하느님의 온갖 선하심에 감사드릴 일이다 — 그 인자하심이 영원함이로다.\n\n엘리너는 사실 사랑에 빠져 있지 않았다. 애러빈 씨도 마찬가지였다. 버티 스탠호프 역시 그러했으니, 다만 그는 벌써 그렇다는 말이나 다름없는 소리를 할 기회를 찾아냈을 뿐이다. 미망인의 모자가 그로 하여금 분명한 고백을 하지 못하게 막았을 뿐, 그렇지 않았다면 그는 세 번째나 네 번째 만남에서 그럴 자격이 제게 있다고 여겼을 것이다. 그것은 어차피 이제 자그마한 모자였고, 수양버들 같은 구석은 별로 남아 있지 않았다. 슬픔의 표지들이 눈에 띄지 않는 단계를 밟아 스러져 가는 모양은 참으로 묘하다. 저마다 앞선 것의 짝인 체하지만, 뒤통수에 사뿐히 얹힌 마지막 자잘한 주름 흰 크레이프 조각은 애도자의 얼굴을 망쳐 놓던 처음의 거대한 슬픔의 산과 딴판이다. 힌두 과부의 처지가 영국 미망인의 과부 재산과 딴판이듯이.\n\n그러나 엘리너가 아무도 사랑하지 않았고 아무도 엘리너를 사랑하지 않았다는 점은 분명히 해 두자. 사정이 이러했으므로 애러빈 씨에 대한 그녀의 노여움은 오래가지 않았고, 이틀이 지나기도 전에 둘은 예전과 다름없이 좋은 벗이 되었다. 그녀는 그를 좋아하지 않을 수 없었다. 그와 함께 보내는 시간은 매 시간이 유쾌했기 때문이다. 그러면서도 그를 온전히 좋아할 수는 없었다. 그의 이야기에는 진지해질 만큼의 값어치는 없다고 여기는 듯한 기색이 늘 배어 있었기 때문이다. 마치 어린아이와 놀아 주는 것 같았다. 그가 실은 진중하고 사려 깊은 사람이며, 어떤 일 어떤 자리에서는 진지함의 고통까지도 견뎌 낼 수 있는 사람임을 그녀는 잘 알았다. 그런데도 그는 그녀에게만은 늘 부드럽게 장난스러웠다. 그의 이마가 한 번이라도 흐려지는 것을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 그를 사랑하는 법을 배웠을지도 모른다.\n\n플럼스테드의 나날은 그렇게, 대체로 나쁘지 않게 흘러갔다. 그러다 거대한 폭풍이 지평선을 어둡게 하더니 용오름 같은 기세로 목사관 사람들 위에 쏟아져 내렸다. 단 몇 분 사이에 하늘의 낯빛이 온통 뒤바뀐 것은 놀라운 일이었다. 사람들은 더없이 화목하게 아침 식탁에서 일어섰으나, 저녁이 되기 전에 사나운 감정이 일어 한 상에 마주 앉아 만찬을 들 수 없는 지경이 되었다. 이를 설명하자면 조금 거슬러 올라가야 한다.\n\n주교가 옷방에서 슬로프 씨에게 퀴버풀 씨의 요양원 임명을 확정하겠다는 결심을 밝히고, 그 결정을 부주교에게 전하라고 슬로프 씨에게 이른 일을 독자는 기억할 것이다. 또 부주교가 슬로프 씨 만나기를 분개하여 거절하고, 그 대신 주교에게 강경한 편지를 써서 하딩 씨에게 워든 자리를 달라고 사실상 요구한 일도 기억할 것이다. 이 편지에 대해 부주교는 슬로프 씨에게서 곧바로 형식적인 답장을 받았는데, 주교가 부주교의 편지를 받았으며 그것을 잘 헤아려 보겠다는 내용이었다.\n\n부주교는 이 답장에 다소 외통수에 걸린 기분이었다. 만나 주지도 않고 편지로 따져 주지도 않으면서, 마음에 드는 성직자를 임명할 권한만은 분명히 쥐고 있는 사람을 어찌하겠는가? 그는 애러빈 씨와 의논했고, 애러빈 씨는 라자러스 학장의 도움을 청하는 것이 좋겠다고 제안했다. “당신과 그윈 박사가 주교를 찾아뵙겠다고 격식을 갖추어 통고하면” 하고 그가 말했다. “주교도 감히 만남을 거절하지는 못할 것이오. 그리고 두 분 같은 사람이 함께 마주 앉는다면, 뜻을 이루지 못한 채 물러 나오는 일은 아마 없을 것이오.”\n\n부주교로서는 바체스터의 주교관에 발을 들이는 데 라자러스 학장의 후원이 필요하다고 인정하기가 썩 내키지 않았으나, 그래도 그 조언이 옳다고 느껴 따르기로 했다. 그는 주교에게 다시 편지를 써서, 주교께서 약속한 숙고가 끝나기 전에는 요양원 문제에 더 이상의 조치가 취해지지 않기를 바란다고 밝혔다. 그런 다음 벗인 학장에게 간곡한 호소를 보내, 플럼스테드로 와서 주교를 굴복시키는 일을 거들어 달라고 청했다. 학장은 이런저런 어려움을 들면서도 거절하지는 않는 답을 보내왔고, 부주교는 즉각 행동해야 할 필요를 내세우며 다시 한번 다그쳤다. 그윈 박사는 공교롭게도 통풍을 앓고 있어서 당장 날을 잡을 수는 없었으나, 끝내 꼭 필요하다면 오겠다고는 했다. 플럼스테드 쪽 사정은 그렇게 되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 하딩 씨에게는 라자러스 학장 못지않게 힘 있는 또 한 사람의 벗이 있어 그를 위해 싸우고 있었으니, 바로 슬로프 씨였다. 주교가 요양원 문제에서 그토록 고집스레 아내에게 굽히기로 했지만, 슬로프 씨는 제 목적을 버릴 필요가 있다고 보지 않았다. 그 미망인이 자기 구애를 호의로 받아 주리라 여길 이유가 날마다 늘어난다고 그는 생각했으며, 하딩 씨가 제 손을 거쳐 요양원에 들어앉는다면 플럼스테드에서 부주교의 날개 아래 낙심하여 투덜대고 있을 때보다 자기를 사위로 맞을 마음이 더 있으리라고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 게다가 슬로프 씨의 공을 제대로 헤아리자면, 그를 움직인 것은 그런 것들보다 더 큰 동기였다. 그는 아내를 원했고 돈을 원했으나, 그 둘보다 권력을 더 원했다. 그는 프라우디 부인과 한판 붙어야 한다는 사실을 온전히 깨닫고 있었다. 그 여자의 전속 사제로 바체스터에 남을 마음은 없었다. 그럴 바에는 이 교구와의 인연을 통째로 잃는 위험을 무릅쓰겠다는 것이었다. 이 무슨 일인가! 제 안에 범상치 않은 재능이 있음을 느끼고, 제가 용기 있고 굳세며 양심이 끼어들지 않는 일에서는 거리낌조차 없는 사람임을 알면서, 그러고도 여자 고위 성직자의 잔심부름꾼 노릇에 만족한단 말인가? 슬로프 씨는 제 운명을 그보다 높이 보았다. 자기든 프라우디 부인이든 하나는 벽으로 밀려나야 했고, 이제 어느 쪽인지 겨루어 볼 때가 온 것이다.\n\n주교는 퀴버풀 씨가 새 워든이 될 것이라고 선언했다. 슬로프 씨는 필요하다면 부주교를 만날 각오로, 그러나 그런 필요는 생기지 않으리라 확신하며 아래층으로 내려가면서, 하딩 씨가 워든이 되어야 한다고 혼자 다짐했다. 이 뜻을 관철할 요량으로 그는 푸딩데일까지 말을 몰고 가, 성직의 좋은 것을 기다리고 있는 그 훌륭한 인물과 다시 한번 마주 앉았다. 퀴버풀 씨는 대체로 훌륭한 사람이었다. 자식 열넷을 신사 숙녀로 길러 내야 하는데 사람 사는 데 필요한 최소한의 것조차 남부끄럽지 않게 대 주기 어려운 수입뿐이라는 불가능한 과업은, 그의 기개에도 예민한 명예 감각에도 이롭지 않은 자국을 남겼다. 그런 짐을 지고서 다른 결과를 냈으리라고 장담할 사람이 누구인가? 퀴버풀 씨는 정직하고 부지런하고 고달프게 일하는 사람으로, 참으로 빵과 고기가 아쉬웠고 푸줏간 주인을 달래고 빵집 안주인의 시큰둥해진 얼굴에 다시 웃음을 씌울 방도가 아쉬웠으나, 제 양심 앞에 떳떳한 것 또한 아쉬웠다. 세상 자리가 더 편한 사람이라면 모를까, 그는 주위 사람들에게 좋게 보이려고, 제 이름을 더럽힐 입김이나 제 명예에 흠집 낼 소문을 피하려고 조심할 처지가 아니었다. 그에게 그런 행실의 섬세함, 그런 도덕의 사치는 감당할 수 없는 것이었다. 세상이 굴러가는 보통의 정직함만큼 보통으로 정직하고, 사람들의 혀는 놀리고 싶은 대로 놀리게 두는 것으로 족해야 했다.\n\n그는 슬로프 씨의 발치에 앉을 뜻을 내비친 첫 순간부터, 지난 이십 년 동안 알고 지낸 동료 성직자들이 저를 차갑게 바라본다고 느꼈다. 그가 주교의 새 히램 요양원 워든이 된다는 소문이 퍼지자 그 눈길이 한층 더 차가워지는 것도 보았다. 이는 충분히 쓰라린 일이었으나, 그것이 그가 짊어지도록 정해진 십자가였다. 그는 아내를 생각했다. 아내가 마지막으로 얻은 새 비단옷은 입은 지 육 년이 되었다. 어린 무리를 생각했다. 일요일이면 다 함께 교회에 데려가기도 어려웠으니, 제대로 된 신발과 양말이 모두에게 돌아가지 않았기 때문이다. 제 검은 상의의 닳아빠진 소매를 생각했고, 새로 한 벌 지을 옷감을 청하고 싶으나 외상은 거절당하리라는 것을 알기에 청하지 못하는, 그 포목상의 엄한 얼굴을 생각했다. 그러고는 바체스터의 안락한 집을 생각했고, 넉넉한 수입을, 학교에 보낼 아들들을, 기울 바늘 대신 책을 손에 든 딸들을, 다시 웃음이 번지는 아내의 얼굴을, 다시 그득해지는 제 밥상을 생각했다. 그는 이런 것들을 생각했다. 독자여, 그대도 이것들을 생각해 보라. 그러고도 슬로프 씨가 주교의 전속 사제를 빛낼 온갖 훌륭한 자질을 갖춘 사람으로 그의 눈에 비쳤다는 것이 이상하거든, 그때 가서 이상해하라. “좋은 소식을 전하는 자의 산을 넘는 발이 어찌 그리 아름다운가.”\n\n게다가 바체스터의 성직자들이 어째서 퀴버풀 씨를 차갑게 대해야 한단 말인가? 그들도 모두 어머니 교회의 떡과 물고기를 흐뭇하게 여긴다는 것을 보이지 않았던가? 그들도 모두 이런저런 수를 써서 그보다는 제 앞가림을 잘하지 않았던가? 그들은 그처럼 짐을 지고 있지 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 자식이 다섯이었고, 그들을 먹이는 데 쓸 돈은 해마다 그와 엇비슷한 수의 천 파운드였다. 자기에게 아무것도 해 줄 수 없는 새 주교와 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 전속 사제를 두고 코웃음 치는 것이야 그로서는 얼마든지 좋은 일이지만, 그런 처지에 있는 사람이 자식 열넷의 아비가 그들에게 떳떳한 살길을 마련해 주려 한다는 이유로 세상을 부추겨 그에게 등을 돌리게 하는 것은 잔인한 짓이었다. 그, 퀴버풀 씨는 워든 자리를 청한 적이 없었다. 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 거절했다는 확언을 듣기 전에는 받아들이지도 않았다. 그러니 하지 않았더라면 미치광이 같은 무분별이라는 소리를 들었을 그 일을 했다고 해서 비난을 받다니, 이 얼마나 가혹한가!\n\n이렇듯 요양원 문제에서 가엾은 퀴버풀 씨에게는 시련이 있었고, 위안도 있었다. 대체로는 위안 쪽이 더 생생했다. 그 엄하던 포목상이 다가올 승진 소식을 듣고 창고의 재물을 퀴버풀 씨 마음대로 쓰게 했다. 다가올 일은 미리 그림자를 드리우는 법이어서, 퀴버풀 씨가 바체스터로 옮겨 간다는 다가올 일은 퀴버풀 부인과 위의 세 딸을 위한 새 옷차림이라는 감미로운 그림자를 낳았다. 그런 위안은 남자의 가슴에 사무치고, 여자의 가슴에는 더욱 사무친다. 남편이야 어떻게 느끼든, 아내는 수석 사제든 부주교든 참사회원이든 그 찌푸린 얼굴 따위 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그녀에게는 남편과 열네 자식의 겉과 속이 전부였다. 그녀의 가슴속에서는 다른 모든 야심이, 그 아이들과 남편과 자신이 제대로 입고 제대로 먹는 것을 보겠다는 어머니의 야심에 삼켜져 버렸다. 그녀에게는 삶에 이제 다른 목적이 없는 지경에 이르러 있었다. 남의 상상 속 권리 따위는 안중에도 없었다. 하딩 씨가 거절한 줄을 알기 전에는 요양원을 받을 수 없다고 남편이 선언했을 때 그녀는 도무지 참을 수가 없었다. 남편에게는 열네 자식을 희생시켜 가며 기사도를 부릴 권리가 없었다. 제 주인이 굴러온 복을 하마터면 내던질 뻔했다는 사실은 그녀를 거의 마비시켰다. 이제는 슬로프 씨뿐 아니라 프라우디 부인에게서까지 온전한 약속을 받아 두었다. 이제는 그 복을 안심하고 셈에 넣어도 되었다. 그러나 그 모든 것이 날아갔더라면 어쩔 뻔했는가? 아비의 병적인 감상 탓에 열넷이나 되는 아이들이 다시 허리까지 가난에 잠겼더라면 어쩔 뻔했는가? 퀴버풀 부인은 지금이야 행복한 여자였지만, 그래도 자기들이 무릅쓴 위험을 떠올리면 숨이 막힐 지경이었다.\n\n“네 아버지가 하딩 씨에게 도리를 지켜야 한다고 그렇게 노래를 부르는 게 무슨 소린지 나는 모르겠구나.” 그녀가 큰딸에게 말했다. “하딩 씨가 고운 마음씨로 연 450파운드를 아버지한테 내주기라도 한다는 거냐? 자리만 얻는다면 누구 비위를 거스르든 그게 무슨 대수야? 아버지가 그보다 나은 걸 바랄 처지도 아니잖니. 둘레에서 다들 그렇게 움켜쥐려 드는데 어쩌면 네 아버지는 저리 무를 수가 있는지 나로서는 알 수가 없다.”\n\n이렇듯 바깥세상이 퀴버풀 씨를 두고 승진에 눈이 멀었다느니 제 명예를 돌보지 않는다느니 나무라는 동안, 제 집안이라는 안쪽 세상은 그가 감상적인 자존심이라는 헛된 감정에 식구들의 이익을 기꺼이 바치려 한다고 못지않게 사납게 그를 몰아세우고 있었다. 보는 자리가 어디냐에 따라 우리가 보는 모든 것의 모습이 얼마나 달라지는지 놀라울 따름이다!\n\n슬로프 씨가 두 번째로 찾아왔을 때 푸딩데일 집안 사람들의 심경은 이러했다. 퀴버풀 부인은 목사관 대문에서 들어오는 길로 그의 말이 올라오는 것을 보자마자 큼직한 바느질 바구니를 부랴부랴 챙기고는, 남편과 함께 앉아 있던 방에서 딸을 데리고 서둘러 나갔다. “슬로프 씨예요.” 그녀가 말했다. “요양원 일을 매듭지으러 오신 거예요. 이제는 정말 곧바로 옮겨 갈 수 있으면 좋겠어요.” 그러고는 그 반가운 귀인을 기다리게 하는 일이 없도록 잡일 하는 하녀를 어서 문간으로 보내라고 일렀다.\n\n그리하여 슬로프 씨는 퀴버풀 씨와 단둘이 마주하게 되었다. 퀴버풀 부인은 두근거리는 가슴을 안고 부엌과 뒤채로 물러가, 행복의 잔과 그것을 맛볼 입술 사이에 무슨 어긋남이라도 생기지 않을까 조마조마해하면서도, 그만한 일이 오간 뒤이니 그런 어긋남은 있을 수 없으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 다독였다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 동료 성직자의 손을 잡고 흔들며 연신 웃는 낯으로, 요양원 워든 자리에 관한 사실을 곧바로 퀴버풀 씨에게 알려 드리는 것이 옳다고 여겨 말을 달려왔노라고 말했다. 그가 말하는 사이, 기다리던 그 가엾은 남편이자 아비는 제 눈부신 희망이 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐지려 한다는 것을, 그리고 이 손님이 지난번 방문에서 한 말을 물리려고 지금 여기 와 있다는 것을 한눈에 알아보았다. 목소리의 어조에, 눈길의 빛에 그 사연이 다 들어 있었다. 퀴버풀 씨는 단번에 전부 알아차렸다. 그러나 그는 침착을 잃지 않고 뜻 모를 옅은 웃음을 지으며, 이렇게 수고해 주시니 슬로프 씨에게 신세를 진다고만 말했다.\n\n“처음부터 끝까지 성가신 일이었지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님께서도 어찌 처신하셔야 할지 몰라 하셨습니다. 우리끼리 이야기입니다만 — 물론 이건 여기서 그쳐야 합니다, 퀴버풀 씨.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 물론 그러겠다고 말했다. “실은 가엾은 하딩 씨가 제 마음을 좀처럼 정하지 못했어요. 지난번에 우리가 나눈 이야기는 기억하시겠지요.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 그 이야기를 아주 잘 기억하고 있노라고 다짐해 두었다.\n\n“하딩 씨가 요양원으로 돌아가기를 거절했다고 제가 말씀드린 것을 기억하실 겁니다.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 그보다 더 또렷이 남아 있는 기억은 없다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n“그리고 그 거절에 따라, 요양원을 맡으시라고 제가 권했지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n“주교님께서 그 자리를 저에게 제안할 권한을 선생께 주셨다고 말씀하신 걸로 알아들었는데요.”\n\n“제가요? 제가 그렇게까지 말했던가요? 글쎄요, 어쩌면 선생을 위하는 마음이 앞선 나머지 제가 해야 할 말보다 더 나아갔는지도 모르지요. 제 기억으로는 그렇게까지 말한 것 같지는 않습니다만. 하지만 선생이 그 자리를 얻으시기를 몹시 바랐던 것은 사실이니, 신중한 선을 넘는 말을 했을 수는 있겠습니다.”\n\n“하지만” 하고 퀴버풀 씨가 제 사정을 어떻게든 입증하려는 절박한 마음으로 말했다. “제 아내는 프라우디 부인께 사람이 사람에게 할 수 있는 가장 분명한 약속을 받았습니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 웃으며 가만히 고개를 저었다. 그는 그것을 다정한 웃음으로 지었으나, 마주 앉은 사람의 눈에는 악마 같은 웃음이었다. “프라우디 부인이라!” 그가 말했다. “이런 일에서 부인네들 사이에 오간 말까지 따지기 시작하면 우리는 정말이지 헤어날 수 없는 말썽의 소굴에 빠지고 말 겁니다. 프라우디 부인은 더없이 훌륭한 분이고, 마음이 어질고 자비롭고 경건하며 무엇 하나 나무랄 데가 없는 분이지요. 그러나 퀴버풀 씨, 이 교구의 성직 임명권은 그분 손에 있지 않습니다.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 한동안 얼이 빠진 채 말없이 앉아 있었다. “그러면 제가 아무 약속도 받은 게 없다는 말씀입니까?” 겨우 생각을 추스르고 그가 말했다.\n\n“허락하신다면 이 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지 말씀드리지요. 선생은 하딩 씨의 거절을 조건으로 하는 약속을 분명히 받으셨습니다. 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 사양했다는 사실을 아는 것 말고는 어떤 조건으로도 임명을 받아들일 수 없다고 선생 스스로 말씀하셨다는 것도, 선생은 공정하게 기억해 주시리라 믿습니다.”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 퀴버풀 씨가 말했다. “제가 그렇게 말한 것은 분명합니다.”\n\n“그런데 이제 와 보니 그분이 그 자리를 거절한 것이 아니더군요.”\n\n“하지만 선생께서는 그분이 직접 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었다고 저에게 분명히, 그것도 여러 번 되풀이해 말씀하시지 않았습니까.”\n\n“저는 그렇게 알아들었지요. 그런데 제가 잘못 안 모양입니다. 하지만 퀴버풀 씨, 제가 선생을 저버릴 셈이라고는 잠시도 생각하지 마십시오. 아닙니다. 선생 같은 처지에, 그 많은 식구에, 그토록 다급한 사정을 지닌 분에게 한번 손을 내민 이상, 이제 와서 그 손을 거둘 생각은 없습니다. 다만 선생께서 저와 함께 공정하고 정직하게 처신해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.”\n\n“무엇을 하든 저로서는 어쨌든 공정하게 처신하려 애쓰겠지요.” 가엾은 사내가 말했다. 제 안의 순교 정신에 기대어 버텨야겠다고 느끼면서였다.\n\n“그러시리라 믿습니다.” 상대가 말했다. “온전히 남의 몫인 수입을 차지하고 싶은 마음이야 선생에게 없으시겠지요. 하딩 씨의 내력을 선생보다 잘 아는 사람도, 그분의 인품을 선생보다 잘 헤아릴 사람도 없습니다. 하딩 씨는 옛 자리로 돌아가기를 몹시 바라고 계시고, 주교님께서는 선생과 저 사이에 오간 이야기 때문에 지금 다소 발이 묶인 느낌이라 하십니다. 물론 매인 몸은 아니십니다만.”\n\n“글쎄요.” 퀴버풀 씨가 말했다. 이런 마당에 제가 어떻게 처신해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 아내를 그토록 담대하게 만드는 그 자기 보존의 본능을 조금이나마 끌어와 마음을 단단히 해 보려 했으나 헛일이었다.\n\n“이 자그마한 요양원의 워든 자리가 주교님께서 내리실 수 있는 유일한 자리도 아니고, 가장 좋은 자리와는 한참 거리가 멉니다, 퀴버풀 씨. 게다가 주교님은 한번 마음에 들어 하신 사람을 잊으실 분이 아닙니다. 제가 벗으로서 한 말씀 드려도 괜찮다면 —”\n\n“정말이지 그래 주시면 더없이 고맙지요.” 푸딩데일의 가엾은 교구 사제가 말했다.\n\n“저라면 하딩 씨의 권리에 맞서는 일에서 물러나시라고 권하겠습니다. 요구를 굽히지 않으셔도 끝내 뜻을 이루지는 못하실 겁니다. 하딩 씨에게는 그 자리에 대한 권리가 사실상 있으니까요. 하지만 선생께서 하딩 씨의 앞길을 막지 않겠다고 하신다는 것을 제가 주교님께 전해 드려도 좋다면, 이렇게 약속드릴 수 있을 듯합니다 — 아, 물론 이것을 정식 약속으로 받아들이시면 안 됩니다만 — 주교님께서 선생을 워든이 되셨을 경우보다 가난한 처지에 두시지는 않을 겁니다.”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 말없이 허공을 바라보았다. 무슨 말을 하겠는가? 슬로프 씨의 입에서 나온 이 모든 말이 다 옳았다. 하딩 씨에게는 요양원에 대한 권리가 있었다. 주교에게는 나누어 줄 좋은 자리가 많았다. 주교도 슬로프 씨도 그 같은 처지의 사람에게는 더없이 훌륭한 벗이자 무서운 적이 될 수 있었다. 게다가 그에게는 약속을 받았다는 증거가 없었으니, 주교더러 자기를 임명하라고 억지를 부릴 수도 없었다.\n\n“자, 퀴버풀 씨, 어떻게 하시겠습니까?”\n\n“아, 물론 선생께서 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하지요, 슬로프 씨. 크게 낙심되는 일이기는 합니다, 아주 크게 낙심되는 일이지요. 제가 아주 가난한 사람이라는 건 부인하지 않겠습니다, 슬로프 씨.”\n\n“끝에 가서는, 퀴버풀 씨, 이편이 선생에게 더 나았다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다.”\n\n면담은 슬로프 씨가 문제의 임명에 대해 퀴버풀 씨가 지녔을 법한 일체의 권리 주장을 온전히 포기하겠다는 말을 받아 내는 것으로 끝났다. 그것은 증인도 없이 말로만 주어진 것이었으나, 애초의 약속 또한 같은 방식으로 이루어진 것이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 그를 잊지 않겠노라고 다시 한번 다짐하고는, 이제 주교를 제 뜻대로 주무를 수 있으리라 흡족해하며 바체스터로 말을 몰아 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제25장. 퀴버풀 씨의 자격을 뒷받침하는 열네 가지 논거\n\n우리 대부분은 새끼들에게 둘러싸인 채 제 먹이를 지키는 암사자의 무서운 노여움에 대해 들어 본 적이 있다. 어린 것들 한가운데서 뼈다귀를 물고 있는 어미 개를 굳이 건드리고 싶어 하는 사람도 별로 없다. 메데이아와 그 아이들 이야기는 우리에게 익숙하고, 콘스턴스의 비탄 또한 그러하다. 퀴버풀 부인은 남편이 전해야 할 소식을 처음 들었을 때 암사자의 분노와 사냥개의 탐욕과 비극 속 왕비의 격노와 자식을 잃은 어머니의 깊은 절망을 한꺼번에 제 가슴속에서 느꼈다.\n\n슬로프 씨의 이야기가 어떤 내용이었을지 미심쩍어하면서도 딱히 두려워하지는 않은 채, 그녀는 손님의 등 뒤로 현관문이 닫히자마자 남편에게로 달려갔다. 슬로프 씨로서는 그렇게 빠져나간 것이 다행이었다. 그런 순간에 그런 여자가 내는 노여움이라면 그조차도 기가 죽었을 것이다. 일반적인 규칙으로 말하자면, 부인네들은 성미를 다스리는 편이 대단히 바람직하다. 여자가 폭풍처럼 몰아칠 때는 언제나 제 얼굴을 추하게 만들고, 대개는 우스꽝스럽게까지 만든다. 사내에게 드센 여자만큼 질색인 것은 없다. 테세우스가 아마존 여인을 사랑하기는 했으되 그 사랑을 드러내는 방식은 거칠었고, 테세우스 이후로 제 아내가 물러서는 온순함보다 앞으로 나서는 기개로 이름나기를 바란 남자는 하나도 없었다. 낮은 목소리는 “여자에게 더없이 좋은 것”이다.\n\n이것은 아주 일반적인 규칙으로 세워 둘 만하다. 여자가 여기서 벗어나는 것을 스스로에게 허락하는 일은 드물어야 하고, 허락하더라도 아주 드문 경우에 한해야 한다. 그러나 여자가 머리카락을 바람에 풀어 헤치고 팔을 내던지듯 휘두르며 나팔 같은 목소리로 사내들의 귀에 대고 소리쳐도 좋을 때가 있다면, 그것은 제 안의 본성이 제 자신의 결핍을 위해서가 아니라 제 태가 낳고 제 젖이 먹여 기른 이들, 사람이 제 창조주를 우러르듯 그렇게 자연스럽게 제게 하루의 양식을 바라는 이들의 결핍을 위해 부르짖을 때다.\n\n퀴버풀 부인의 천성에는 시적인 구석이라곤 없었다. 그녀는 메데이아도 콘스턴스도 아니었다. 화가 나면 그 노여움을 꾸밈없는 말로, 조금 다스렸더라면 좋았을 어조로 그대로 쏟아 냈다. 그러나 어쨌든 그녀는 가식 없이 그렇게 했다. 지금 그녀는 스스로도 모르는 사이에 비극의 가락에 올라섰다.\n\n“여보, 우리 차지가 되지 않게 되었소.” 부엌 불기운이 아직 가시지 않은 몸으로 응접실에 들어선 그녀의 귀를 맞이한 말은 그러했다. 그리고 남편의 얼굴은 그 말보다도 더 분명하게 말하고 있었다.\n\n바로 그런 사람이, 그토록 맥이 풀리고 그토록 넋이 나가고, 그토록 흐리멍덩하고 그토록 죽은 낯빛에 그토록 비탄에 잠긴 자가, 한밤중에 프리아모스의 휘장을 걷었더라.\n\n“뭐라고요!” 그녀가 말했다. 시든스 부인이라 한들 한 음절에 그보다 더한 격정을 담지는 못했으리라. “뭐라고요! 우리 차지가 아니라고요? 누가 그럽디까?” 그러고는 남편 맞은편에 앉아 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 올리고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 거칠고 다부지지만 한때는 잘생겼던 얼굴을 탁자 너머로 남편에게 내밀었다.\n\n남편이 이야기를 하는 동안 그녀는 죽은 듯이 잠자코 앉아 있었고, 그 침묵이 그에게는 몹시도 무서웠다. 그는 아주 서투르고 어설프게 이야기했지만, 그래도 그녀가 곧 전모를 알아들을 만큼은 되었다.\n\n“그래서 당신, 그 자리를 내놓았단 말이에요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“받아들일 기회 자체가 없었소.” 그가 대답했다. “슬로프 씨가 그런 제안을 했다는 증인이 내겐 없었소. 설령 그 제안이 주교님을 얽어맬 수 있는 것이라 해도 말이오. 결국 얻지도 못할 것을 두고 다투느니 그런 사람들과 좋게 지내는 편이 나로서는 여러모로 나았소.”\n\n“증인이라니요!” 그녀가 소리치며 벌떡 일어나 방 안을 오갔다. “성직자가 제 말에 증인이 필요합디까? 그이는 주교님 이름으로 약속했어요. 그 약속을 깨겠다면 나는 그 까닭을 기어이 알아내고야 말겠어요. 주교님이 당신에게 그 자리를 주라고 자기를 보냈다고 그이가 똑똑히 말하지 않았어요?”\n\n“그랬소, 여보. 하지만 그건 이제 아무 소용도 없는 이야기요.”\n\n“아무 소용도 없다니요, 퀴버풀 씨. 증인이라니, 참! 그러고서 아이 열넷을 먹여 살릴 길을 찾는다는 이유로 당신 명예가 의심을 받는다는 소리를 한다는 거예요. 그건 소용이 아주 크고말고요. 바체스터 장터 십자가 앞에서 그 사람들 귀에 대고 악을 써서라도 그걸 알게 하고 말겠어요.”\n\n“당신 잊었구려, 러티샤. 주교님 손에는 내어 줄 자리가 아주 많소. 우리는 조금만 더 기다리면 되오. 그뿐이오.”\n\n“기다려요! 기다린다고 아이들을 먹일 수 있어요? 기다린다고 조지와 톰과 샘이 세상에 나가 자리를 잡아요? 기다린다고 우리 가엾은 딸애들이 그 고된 집안일을 조금이라도 덜 수 있어요? 기다린다고 베시와 제인이 가정교사 자리라도 맡을 만하게 돼요? 기다린다고 지난주에 바체스터에서 들여온 물건값이 치러져요?”\n\n“우리가 할 수 있는 건 그것뿐이오, 여보. 낙담이야 나도 당신 못지않소. 그런데도, 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 나 자신보다 당신을 생각하며 그것을 더 아프게 느끼오.”\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 남편의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 주름진 두 뺨에 뜨거운 눈물이 한 방울씩 맺히는 것을 보았다. 이것은 그녀의 여자다운 마음이 감당하기에는 너무한 일이었다. 그도 자리에서 일어나 불기 없는 벽난로를 등지고 서 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 달려들어 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 그 가슴에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 내어 흐느꼈다.\n\n“당신은 너무 착하고, 너무 무르고, 너무 잘 물러서요.” 마침내 그녀가 말했다. “저 사람들은 당신이 필요할 때는 고양이 발처럼 갖다 쓰고, 더 필요 없어지면 헌 신짝처럼 내던져요. 저이들이 당신을 이렇게 대접한 게 이번이 두 번째예요.”\n\n“한편으로 보면 이편이 도리어 잘된 일일 것이오.” 그가 따져 말했다. “주교님으로 하여금 나에게 무언가 해 주어야 한다는 마음이 들게 할 테니까.”\n\n“어쨌든 주교님 귀에는 들어가게 하겠어요.” 부인이 다시 아까의 노기 어린 기색으로 돌아가 말했다. “어쨌든 주교님 귀에 들어가게 하겠어요, 그것도 요란하게요. 그 부인 귀에도 마찬가지고요. 주교관에서 우리 둘 사이에 오간 그 모든 말이 있고서도 내가 이 손실을 얌전히 받아들이고 앉아 있으리라 여긴다면, 그 부인은 러티샤 퀴버풀을 너무 모르는 거예요. 그 속에 사람의 감정이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면, 나는 그 부인이 스스로 부끄러워하게 만들고야 말겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 살찌고 무거운 발로 마룻바닥을 쿵쿵 울리며 다시 방 안을 오갔다. “세상에! 아무 대책도 없는 아이 열넷의 아비를 이런 식으로 대접하다니, 대체 그 가슴속에 어떤 심장이 들어 있어야 그럴 수 있담!”\n\n퀴버풀 씨는 프라우디 부인이 이 일과 무슨 관계가 있으리라고는 생각하지 않는다고 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n“그런 말씀 마세요.” 퀴버풀 부인이 말했다. “나는 그보다는 더 잘 알아요. 프라우디 부인이 바체스터의 주교이고 슬로프 씨는 그저 그 부인이 부리는 사람일 뿐이라는 걸 온 세상이 다 알지 않아요? 마치 그 자리를 제 마음대로 줄 수 있기라도 한 양 나에게 약속한 사람이 바로 그 부인 아니었어요? 내 말 들어 보세요. 오늘 그이를 여기로 보낸 건 그 여자예요. 제 나름의 무슨 까닭이 있어서 제 말을 도로 물리고 싶은 거라고요.”\n\n“여보, 그건 당신이 잘못 알고 있는 거요. 프라우디 부인은—”\n\n“아이 참, Q, 그렇게 무르게 굴지 마세요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “내 말을 믿으세요. 주교님은 이 일에 대해 제미마만큼도 모르신다니까요.” 제미마는 두 살배기였다. “그리고 내 말대로 하시려거든, 지체 말고 건너가서 직접 주교님을 뵈세요.”\n\n그러나 퀴버풀 씨가 아무리 무른 사람이라 해도 이번만큼은 제 생각을 말로 꺾이도록 내버려 두지 않았다. 그는 프라우디 부인이 교구 일에 간섭하는 것을 두고 슬로프 씨가 어떤 어조로 말했는지 아주 세세하게 아내에게 설명해 나갔다. 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 이 안주인의 머릿속에는 새로운 생각이 차츰 스며들었고, 새로운 행동 방침이 그녀의 판단 앞에 떠올랐다. 만일 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨의 이번 방문을 정말로 전혀 모르고 있다면? 그렇다면 그 부인이 이 일에서 여전히 자기 편에 굳게 서 주고, 여전히 자기의 벗으로 남아 주고, 어쩌면 슬로프 씨에 맞서 자기를 끝까지 밀어 줄 수도 있지 않겠는가? 이 희미한 희망이 떠올랐을 때 퀴버풀 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않고, 남편이 하는 말을 여느 때보다 훨씬 참을성 있게 들었다. 프라우디 부인의 권세와 권위에 대한 세상의 평가가 아마도 틀렸을 것이라고 그가 아직 설명하고 있는 동안, 그녀는 이미 자기가 할 일을 완전히 정해 두었다. 그러나 그 뜻을 밖으로 알리지는 않았다. 그가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 그녀는 불길하게 고개를 저었고, 그가 다 마치자 잔인한, 참으로 잔인한 처사라고만 말하고서 자리에서 일어났다. 그러고는 늘 하던 대로 세 시에 식사하는 대신 저녁을 늦게 들어도 괜찮겠느냐고 남편에게 물었고, 그 점에 대해 양보를 받아 낸 뒤 제 목적을 실행에 옮기러 나섰다.\n\n그녀는 곧장 주교관으로 가되 될 수 있으면 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨와 만나기 전에 가기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 자기가 어떤 대접을 받느냐에 따라 순종적이고 애처롭고 가련한 태도를 취하든지, 아니면 분개하고 격렬하고 다그치는 태도를 취하든지 하기로 정했다.\n\n그녀는 제 힘을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 아이 열넷의 절박한 궁핍이 그녀를 떠받치고 있었으므로, 주교관 하인들의 군단을 뚫고 나아가 필요하다면 자기에게 그토록 부당한 일을 한 그 부인 앞으로 억지로라도 밀고 들어갈 수 있으리라 느꼈다. 그 일에 부끄러움 따위는 없었고, mauvaise honte도 없었으며, 부주교들에 대한 두려움도 없었다. 남편에게 선언한 대로, 바로잡음과 정의를 얻어 내지 못한다면 장터 한복판에서 제 통곡을 들리게 할 참이었다. 장가도 안 든 젊은 부목사라면 그런 일에 낯을 붉히는 것도 좋을 것이다. 아쉬울 것이라곤 정말 아무것도 없으면서 그래도 더 나은 자리를 노리는 안락한 교구 목사라면 제 일을 점잖게 남몰래 처리하는 것도 다 옳을 것이다. 그러나 아이 열넷을 둔 퀴버풀 부인은 낯 붉히기를 그만둔 지 오래였고, 어떤 일에서는 점잖기를 그만둔 지도 오래였다. 슬로프 씨가 내놓은 방식대로 자기가 부당한 대접을 받아야 한다면, 그 일이 남몰래 이루어지게 두지는 않을 작정이었다. 온 세상이 그것을 알아야 했다.\n\n지금 같은 심경에서 퀴버풀 부인은 옷차림에 그리 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 보닛 끈을 턱 밑에서 매고 숄을 어깨에 걸치고 집안 대대로 쓰던 낡은 무명 우산으로 무장하고서 바체스터를 향해 나섰다. 주교관까지 가는 길은 퀴버풀 부인에게 우리의 벗 플럼스테드 양반에게만큼 수월한 일이 아니었다. 플럼스테드는 바체스터에서 9마일 떨어져 있고 푸딩데일은 4마일밖에 되지 않는다. 그러나 부주교는 사륜마차를 대령시킬 수 있었고, 걸음이 빠른 밤색 거세마가 한 시간 안에 그를 시내로 데려다주었다. 푸딩데일 목사관의 마차 헛간에는 사륜마차가 없었고 마구간에는 밤색 말이 없었다. 그 집 식구들에게는 자연이 사람에게 배정해 준 것 말고는 이동할 방도가 없었다.\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 몸집이 넓고 무거운 여자였고 젊지도 않았으며 걷기를 즐기는 편도 아니었다. 부엌에서, 그리고 아이들이 자는 방에서라면 충분히 활발했지만 그 걸음걸이와 보폭은 길 위에 맞지 않았다. 8월 한낮에 바체스터까지 걸어갔다 돌아오는 일은 그녀에게 아예 못할 노릇까지는 아니라 해도 끔찍한 고역이었을 것이다. 목사관에서 시내로 가는 길로 반 마일쯤 되는 곳에, 이 세상 형편으로는 넉넉하고 저세상 일도 어지간히 마음에 두어 제 교구 교회에 꼬박꼬박 나오는 점잖고 인정 많은 농부 하나가 이 교구에 살고 있었다. 퀴버풀 부인은 집안의 더 급한 곤경 몇 번을 두고 전에도 그에게 부탁한 적이 있었고, 헛되이 부탁한 적은 없었다. 그녀는 이제 그 집 문 앞에 나타나, 아주 급한 볼일이 있어 당장 바체스터에 가야 한다고 그 아내에게 설명한 뒤, 서브소일 농부가 짐수레에 자기를 태워 그리로 데려다주기를 청했다. 농부는 그 청을 물리치지 않았고, 프린스에게 멍에를 씌우는 대로 두 사람은 길을 떠났다.\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 무슨 볼일인지 말하지 않았고, 농부도 주제넘은 물음 따위로 제 친절을 흐리지 않았다. 그녀는 그저 시내로 들어가는 다리에서 내려 달라고, 그리고 두 시간쯤 뒤에 같은 자리에서 다시 태워 달라고만 청했다. 농부는 약속 시간을 어기지 않겠다고 다짐했고, 우산에 몸을 의지한 부인은 대성당 경내로 가는 지름길을 잡아 몇 분 만에 주교 댁 문 앞에 이르렀다.\n\n여기까지 오는 동안 그녀는 앞으로 있을 면담에 대해 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않았다. 제 몫으로 요구할 것을 쏟아 놓고, 그 요구가 온전히 받아들여지지 않는다면 자기가 당한 부당함을 선언하고 싶다는 분에 찬 열망 말고는 아무것도 느끼지 않았다. 그러나 이제 제 처지의 난감함이 그녀의 마음을 조금 건드렸다. 그녀는 전에 한 번 주교관에 온 적이 있었지만, 그때는 감사 인사를 드리러 간 것이었다. 은혜를 입고 그 감사를 갚으러 오는 이들은 높은 분들의 저택에 쉽게 들어선다. 청을 드리러 오는 사내들, 아니 여자들의 경우에도 늘 그렇지는 않다. 이미 한 약속을 지키라고 요구하는 이들에게는 문이 더욱더 열리지 않는다.\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 세상 물정을 익히는 데 더디지 않았다. 그녀는 이 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 제 무명 우산과 거의 누더기가 된 숄이 주교관 하인들 눈에 존경을 불러일으키지는 못하리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 너무 굽실거리면 결코 뜻을 이루지 못하리라는 것도 잘 알았다. 그런 숄을 어깨에 걸치고 그런 보닛을 머리에 쓴 채 오만하게 위압해서 이겨 내려면 자연이 그녀에게 준 것보다 훨씬 뛰어난 몸가짐이 있어야 했을 것이다. 이 점 또한 퀴버풀 부인은 알고 있었다. 그녀는 자기가 신사이자 성직자인 사람의 아내임을 알려야 하면서도, 그러면서 몸을 낮추어 상대의 비위를 맞추어야 했다.\n\n이 가엾은 부인은 제 일의 문턱에서부터 마주친 이 난관을 넘을 방법을 단 하나밖에 알지 못했고, 그것에 의지했다. 푸딩데일의 살림 밑천이 아무리 바닥이라 해도 그녀에게는 아직 반 크라운 한 닢이 남아 있었으니, 그것을 프라우디 부인의 도회풍에 이름만 거창한 하인의 탐욕에 바쳤다. 자기는 푸딩데일의 퀴버풀 부인이며 퀴버풀 목사의 아내라고 그녀는 말했다. 프라우디 부인을 뵙고자 한다고 했다. 프라우디 부인을 뵙는 일이 정말이지 꼭 있어야 한다고 했다. 제임스 피츠플러시는 미심쩍은 것보다 더 나쁜 낯빛을 하고서, 마님이 외출 중이신지 다른 손님을 맞고 계신지 침실에 계신지 모르겠노라 했다. 그중 하나이거나 아니면 마님을 뵐 수 없게 만드는 다른 무슨 사정이 있을 공산이 크다고 여겨진다 했다. 다만 프라우디 부인의 하녀에게 여쭈어 보는 동안 퀴버풀 부인은 대기실에 앉아 계셔도 좋다고 했다.\n\n“이보게, 젊은이.” 퀴버풀 부인이 말했다. “나는 그분을 꼭 뵈어야 하네.” 그러고는 제 명함과 반 크라운을 — 독자여, 생각해 보라, 생각해 보라, 그것이 그녀의 마지막 반 크라운이었다 — 하인의 손에 쥐여 주고 대기실 의자에 앉았다.\n\n뇌물이 판가름을 낸 것인지, 아니면 주교의 부인이 정말로 교구 사제의 아내를 만나 볼 마음이 있었던 것인지는 이제 와서 따져 볼 일이 못 된다. 하인은 돌아와 따라오시라 청하고서 그녀를 이 교구의 여주인 앞으로 안내했다.\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 제 후원자가 웃음 띤 기분에 젖어 있음을 한눈에 알아보았다. 승리가 그 이마 위에 왕좌를 틀고 앉아 있었고, 지배의 온갖 기쁨이 그 곱슬머리 언저리를 맴돌고 있었다. 그날 아침 그 남편이 그녀와 큰 문제를 두고 겨루었던 것이다. 그는 대주교와 이틀을 지내자는 초대를 받았다. 그의 영혼은 그 흐뭇함을 간절히 바랐다. 그러나 대주교 각하의 쪽지에는 그가 아내 있는 몸이라는 사실을 언급하는 말이 한마디도 없었다. 가려면 혼자 가야 했다. 프라우디 부인에게 한마디도 알리지 않고 갈 수 있었다면 이 사정이 방문을 가로막는 넘지 못할 장벽이 되지도 않았을 것이고 그 즐거움을 크게 깎아내리지도 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그럴 수는 없었다. 그는 여행 가방을 꾸리라 이르고 제 하인을 데리고 떠나면서, 제 마음의 부인에게 아마 토요일쯤 돌아오겠노라고만 일러 둘 수 있는 사람이 못 되었다. 그런 짓을 하는 남자들이 — 차라리 괴물이라 불러야 하지 않을까? — 세상에 있고, 그런 대접을 참고 견디는 아내들이 — 차라리 노예라 불러야 하지 않을까? — 있기는 하다. 그러나 프라우디 박사 내외는 그런 축에 들지 않았다.\n\n주교는 이리저리 에두른 끝에 자기가 몹시 가고 싶어 한다는 뜻을 부인에게 알렸다. 부인은 에두르는 법 없이 그런 소리는 듣지 않겠다는 뜻을 주교에게 알렸다. 양쪽이 내세운 논거를 여기 되풀이하는 것은 쓸데없는 일이고, 그 결과를 적어 두는 것도 부질없는 일이다. 혼인한 이들은 그 싸움이 어떻게 지고 어떻게 이겼는지 아주 잘 알아볼 것이고, 혼자인 이들은 오직 경험만이 줄 수 있는 그 교훈을 배우기 전에는 끝내 알아보지 못할 것이다. 퀴버풀 부인이 프라우디 부인의 방으로 안내되었을 때 그 부인은 남편에게서 돌아온 지 몇 분밖에 되지 않은 참이었다. 그러나 남편 곁을 떠나기 전에 그녀는 대주교의 쪽지에 대한 답장이 쓰이고 봉해지는 것까지 보고 나왔다. 퀴버풀 부인을 맞이하는 그 얼굴에 웃음이 어려 있었던 것도 무리가 아니었다.\n\n그녀는 곧바로 손님의 마음에 그토록 가까이 놓여 있는 그 문제를 입에 올렸다. “그래, 퀴버풀 부인.” 그녀가 말했다. “바체스터로 언제 옮겨 올지 이제 정해졌나?”\n\n한두 시간 전만 해도 “그 여자”라고 불리던 이가, 주교의 부인을 꾸며 줄 수 있는 온갖 우아함을 순식간에 도로 갖추게 되었다. 퀴버풀 부인은 제가 할 일이 가련해 보이는 것이며 분개해서는 아무것도 얻을 수 없다는 것을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 아니, 제 후원자와 함께 분개할 수 있다면 또 모를까, 그렇지 않고서는 정말 아무것도 얻을 수 없었다.\n\n“아, 프라우디 부인.” 그녀가 입을 열었다. “저희는 아예 바체스터로 옮겨 가지 못하게 될까 두렵습니다.”\n\n“어째서?” 그 부인이 날카롭게 물으며, 웃음과 너그러움을 그 자리에서 내려놓고는 한눈에 중대한 일임을 알아본 용건 쪽으로 그 날카롭고 재빠른 태도를 돌렸다.\n\n그러고서 퀴버풀 부인은 제 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 제가 당한 부당함의 내력을 풀어 가면서 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 무겁게 기댈수록 프라우디 부인의 이마가 더 검어지는 것을, 그러나 그 검어짐이 제 쪽 사정에는 해롭지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그날 아침 슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일 목사관에 왔을 때 그녀는 그를 주교 노릇 하는 그 부인의 앞잡이로 여겼는데, 이제는 두 사람이 원수 사이임을 알아본 것이다. 그녀는 아무런 괴로움도 굴욕감도 없이 제 착오를 스스로 인정했다. 그녀에게는 감정이 하나뿐이었고, 그것은 제 식구들에게만 매여 있었다. 남편을 워든의 집으로 비틀어 넣을 수만 있다면, 주교관에 새로 온 이 사람들 사이에서 제가 어떻게 비틀리고 돌아서든 아무래도 좋았다. 그토록 절실히 바라는 이 자리만 얻을 수 있다면 누가 제 편이고 누가 제 원수인지는 상관없었다.\n\n그녀가 이야기를 늘어놓는 동안 프라우디 부인은 거의 잠자코 들었다. 슬로프 씨가 어떻게 제 남편을 구슬려 그 권리를 내놓게 만들었는지, 그리고 하딩 씨 말고는 아무도 워든이 되어서는 안 된다는 것이 주교의 뜻이라고 어떻게 잘라 말했는지를 그녀는 이야기했다. 프라우디 부인의 이마는 점점 더 검어졌다. 마침내 그 부인은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 퀴버풀 부인에게 앉아서 자기가 돌아올 때까지 기다리라 이르고는 성큼성큼 방을 나갔다.\n\n“아, 프라우디 부인, 아이가 열넷입니다. 아이가 열넷이나 됩니다.” 그녀가 등 뒤로 문을 닫을 때 그 귀에 얹힌 짐은 그런 말이었다.\n\n# 제26장. 프라우디 부인이 씨름을 하다 넘어지다\n\n프라우디 부인이 승리자로서 남편의 방을 떠난 지 채 한 시간도 되지 않았건만, 그 기개가 어찌나 꺾일 줄 모르는 것이었던지 그녀는 이제 또 한 번의 격돌을 갈망하며 그리로 돌아왔다. 그녀는 남편의 표리부동이라 여겨지는 것에 몹시 화가 나 있었다. 그는 요양원 문제에 관해 그토록 분명하게 약속을 해 주지 않았던가. 그 점에 관해서라면 그는 이미 그토록 완벽하게 굴복하지 않았던가. 프라우디 부인은 만사가 이런 식으로 두 번씩, 심지어 세 번씩 논의되고 다투어져야 한다면 교구의 일이 아무리 자기라 해도 감당하기 벅차리라는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n문을 두드리지도 않고 그녀는 남편의 방으로 성큼 들어섰고, 그가 집무 탁자 앞에 앉아 있고 그 맞은편에 슬로프 씨가 있는 것을 보았다. 그의 손가락 사이에는 자기가 보는 앞에서 대주교에게 써 보낸 바로 그 쪽지가 들려 있었다 — 그것도 뜯긴 채로! 그렇다, 그는 자신의 승인으로 신성해진 봉인을 기어이 훼손해 놓았던 것이다. 두 사람은 깊숙이 밀담을 나누고 있었고, 대주교의 초청이 지닌 뜻이 자기 명에 따라 이미 논의되고 결정된 뒤에 다시 한 번 처음부터 다시 따져졌다는 것이 너무나도 분명했다! 슬로프 씨가 의자에서 일어나 가볍게 고개를 숙였다. 서로 맞선 두 혼령은 정면으로 서로의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 자기가 지금 적을 보고 있음을 알았다.\n\n“주교님, 퀴버풀 씨 건은 대체 무슨 일이에요?” 탁자 끝으로 다가와 거기 선 채 그녀가 말했다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 주교가 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 자기가 대신 대답했다. “저는 오늘 아침 푸딩데일에 다녀왔고, 부인, 거기서 퀴버풀 씨를 만났습니다. 퀴버풀 씨는 요양원에 대한 자기 권리 주장을 거두었습니다. 하딩 씨가 옛 자리를 다시 맡고 싶어 한다는 것을 이제 알게 되었기 때문입니다. 그런 사정이니 저는 주교님께 하딩 씨를 지명하시라고 강력히 진언드렸습니다.”\n\n“퀴버풀 씨는 아무것도 거두지 않았네.” 부인이 몹시 위압적인 목소리로 말했다. “주교님의 약속은 이미 그이에게 주어졌고, 그 약속은 지켜져야 하네.”\n\n주교는 여전히 잠자코 있었다. 그는 오랜 적을 제 발밑에 무릎 꿇려 흙을 물게 하고 싶은 마음이 간절했다. 새 동맹자는 그에게 그보다 쉬운 일은 없다고 일러 주었다. 그 동맹자가 지금 바로 곁에 서서 거들어 주려 하건만, 그럼에도 그는 용기가 나지 않았다. 지난날의 패배가 남긴 위세가 온통 제게 불리하게 작용할 때, 이기기란 참으로 어려운 일이다. 한 번 제 마당에서 쫓겨난 수탉이 다시 기운을 차려 거름 더미 위의 자랑스러운 자리를 되찾기란 참으로 어려운 일이다.\n\n“제가 나설 일이 아닐지도 모르겠습니다만,” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “그래도—”\n\n“물론 나설 일이 아니지.” 격노한 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래도 말입니다,” 슬로프 씨는 말막음에 아랑곳하지 않고 이어 갔다. “저로서는 주교님께 하딩 씨의 자격을 가볍게 여기지 마시라고 진언드리는 것이 반드시 해야 할 의무라고 여겼습니다.”\n\n“하딩 씨는 제 마음쯤은 스스로 정해 두었어야지.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하딩 씨가 요양원에 복귀하지 못한다면, 주교님께서는 교구 안에서뿐 아니라 널리 세상에서도 많은 원성을 사게 되실 것입니다. 게다가 좀 더 높은 자리에서 보자면, 제가 알기로 주교님께서는 이 일에서 하딩 씨처럼 훌륭한 인물이자 그토록 선량한 성직자를 흡족하게 해 주는 것이 당신의 도리라고 여기고 계십니다.”\n\n“그럼 요양원의 안식일 학교와 주일 예배는 어떻게 되는 건가?” 프라우디 부인이 거의 비웃음에 가까운 것을 얼굴에 띠고 말했다.\n\n“하딩 씨는 안식일 학교에는 아무 이의도 없다고 알고 있습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “요양원 예배 문제라면, 그것은 그가 임명된 뒤에 논의하는 편이 가장 좋을 것입니다. 그가 끝내 이의를 굽히지 않는다면, 유감스럽지만 그 일은 그대로 두어야 하겠지요.”\n\n“자네는 그런 일에 양심이 참 편한 사람일세, 슬로프 씨.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“제 양심이 편할 리 없지요.” 그가 되받았다. “아니, 제가 말하거나 행한 무엇이 주교님으로 하여금 이 일에서 그릇된 처신을 하시게 만든다면 제 양심은 편하기는커녕 도무지 편할 수 없을 것입니다. 하딩 씨와 나눈 면담에서 제가 그를 잘못 알아들었던 것은 분명합니다—”\n\n“그리고 자네가 퀴버풀 씨를 잘못 알아들은 것도 그만큼 분명하지.” 이제 노여움이 꼭대기에 이른 그녀가 말했다. “대체 자네가 그런 면담들과 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가? 누가 자네더러 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨에게 가라고 했나? 누가 자네에게 이 일을 처리하라고 맡겼나? 대답해 보게, 이 사람아. 누가 자네를 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨에게 보냈나?”\n\n방 안에 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀다. 슬로프 씨는 의자에서 일어나 그 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 선 채, 처음에는 몹시 엄숙한, 이제는 몹시 험악한 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 프라우디 부인은 처음 자리를 잡은 그대로 탁자 끝에 서 있었고, 적을 다그치면서 여자의 힘이라 하기에는 지나칠 만큼 세게 탁자를 손으로 내리쳤다. 주교는 안락의자에 앉아 두 엄지를 맞돌리며, 둘이 번갈아 몽둥이를 집어 들 때마다 눈길을 아내에게로, 다시 전속 사제에게로 옮기고 있었다. 자기가 끼어들 것도 없이 저희끼리 이 일을 끝까지 싸워 준다면 얼마나 편할 것인가. 적어도 교구 생활에 관한 한 하나가 다른 하나를 완전히 죽여 없애도록 싸워 주어서, 자기가, 곧 주교가 누구에게 이끌려 다녀야 마땅한지를 분명히 알 수 있게 된다면 얼마나 편할 것인가. 어느 쪽이 이기든 조용해지는 편안함은 얻을 터였다. 그러나 주교에게 어느 쪽이 승자가 되기를 바라는 마음이 있었다면, 그 바람은 분명 슬로프 씨에게 적대적인 것은 아니었다.\n\n“모르는 마귀보다는 아는 마귀가 낫다”는 옛말이 있고 아마 참말이기도 하겠으나, 주교는 아직 그 말의 참됨을 깨닫지 못하고 있었다.\n\n“대답하겠나, 이 사람아?” 그녀가 되풀이했다. “누가 자네더러 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨를 찾아가라고 시켰나?” 또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다. “내게 대답할 생각이 있기는 한가?”\n\n“프라우디 부인, 저는 모든 사정을 고려할 때 그런 물음에는 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각합니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. 슬로프 씨의 목소리에는 여러 가락이 있었고 모두 그의 뜻대로 부릴 수 있었다. 그중에는 성스러운 낮은 가락과 성스러운 높은 가락이 있었는데, 그는 지금 앞엣것을 썼다.\n\n“누가 자네를 보냈나, 이 사람아?”\n\n“프라우디 부인,” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “제가 부인의 호의에 얼마나 큰 빚을 지고 있는지 저도 잘 알고 있습니다. 신사가 숙녀에게 갖추어야 할 예의가 무엇인지도 알고 있습니다. 그러나 그 둘보다 더 높은 고려가 있으며, 이제 제가 오로지 그것에만 움직이도록 스스로를 내맡긴다 해도 부디 용서받기를 바랍니다. 이 일에서 저의 의무는 주교님께 있으며, 저는 오직 그분의 물음에만 답할 수 있습니다. 그분께서 제가 한 일을 승인하셨으니, 그 승인과 저 자신의 승인이 있는 이상 다른 어떤 승인도 필요하지 않다고 말씀드려도 부인께서는 저를 너그러이 보아 주셔야 하겠습니다.”\n\n프라우디 부인의 귀를 때린 이 무시무시한 말은 대체 무엇이었던가? 사태는 참으로 명백했다. 진영 안에 미리 꾸며진 반란이 있었다. 못된 심보들이 손바닥만 한 권력을 맛보고 불복종해진 데 그치지 않고, 반역이 대놓고 가르쳐지고 설교되기까지 한 것이다. 주교가 그 자리에 앉은 지 아직 열두 달도 되지 않았는데 반란은 벌써 주교관 안에서 그 흉측한 머리를 쳐들었다. 자기가 찾아낸 이 음모를 당장 강력한 수단으로 눌러 놓지 않는다면 무정부와 난정이 곧 뒤따를 것이었다.\n\n“슬로프 씨,” 그녀가 지금까지 쓰던 것과는 사뭇 다른, 느리고 위엄 있는 목소리로 말했다. “슬로프 씨, 미안하지만 이 방에서 나가 주게. 나는 주교님과 단둘이 이야기하고 싶네.”\n\n슬로프 씨 또한 모든 것이 지금 이 자리에 달려 있음을 느꼈다. 주교가 여기서 다시 치마폭에 싸인다면 그의 예속은 완전해질 것이고 또 영영 풀리지 않을 것이었다. 지금 이 순간은 반란에 유난히 좋은 때였다. 주교는 대주교에게 보낼 답장의 봉인을 뜯음으로써 이미 스스로를 얽어매 놓았으니, 그를 움직일 두려움이 있었다. 슬로프 씨는 그에게 무슨 사정이 있어도 대주교의 초청을 거절해서는 안 된다고 일러 두었으니, 그를 움직일 희망이 있었다. 그는 퀴버풀 씨의 사임을 받아들였으니 그 문제를 아내와 다시 꺼내야 하는 것을 두려워하고 있었다. 그는 제 뜻이라는 것을 내세울 만큼 조여져 있었고, 어쩌면 완전한 성공에 이르러 성공이 얼마나 가능한 일인지를 배우게 될 때까지 계속 밀려갈 수도 있었다. 지금이야말로 승리 아니면 패주의 순간이었다. 슬로프 씨가 교구의 주인이 되느냐, 아니면 자리를 내놓고 다시 출셋길을 찾아 나서느냐가 지금 결정되는 것이었다. 그는 이 모든 것을 훤히 알고 있었다. 이미 벌어진 일이 있는 이상 그와 저 부인 사이에 타협이란 있을 수 없었다. 그녀의 분부대로 한 번 이 방을 나가 주교를 그녀의 손에 남겨 둔다면, 그는 그 길로 여행 가방을 꾸려 주교좌의 영예와 볼드 부인과 시뇨라 네로니에게 작별을 고하면 그만이었다.\n\n그러면서도 숙녀에게 나가라는 말을 듣고 그 자리를 지키기란, 또 아내가 남편과 단둘이 있는 tête-à-tête를 바란다고 밝히는 마당에 부부 사이에 제삼자로 계속 끼어 있기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다.\n\n“슬로프 씨,” 그녀가 되풀이했다. “나는 주교님과 단둘이 있고 싶네.”\n\n“주교님께서 지극히 중요한 교구 사무로 저를 부르셨습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 불안한 눈길로 프라우디 박사를 흘깃 보며 말했다. 그는 무언가를 주교에게 맡길 수밖에 없다고 느꼈으나, 그 믿음을 두기에 그처럼 딱한 자리도 없다는 것 또한 느꼈다. “지금 이 순간에 제가 자리를 뜨기란 유감스럽게도 불가능합니다.”\n\n“이 은혜도 모르는 사람이, 감히 나와 말을 겨루려 드는가?” 그녀가 말했다. “주교님, 부디 슬로프 씨에게 이 방에서 나가라고 일러 주세요.”\n\n주교 각하는 머리를 긁적였을 뿐 당장은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 이는 슬로프 씨가 그에게서 기대한 만큼은 되는 것이었고, 그로서는 대체로 남편의 권리를 자못 적극적으로 행사한 셈이었다.\n\n“주교님,” 부인이 말했다. “슬로프 씨가 이 방을 나가야 합니까, 아니면 제가 나가야 합니까?”\n\n여기서 프라우디 부인은 한 발을 헛디뎠다. 그녀는 제 쪽에서 물러날 가능성을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다. 슬로프 씨를 내보내라는 자기 명령이 즉각적인 복종 말고 다른 방식으로 처리될 수도 있다는 생각을 드러내서는 안 되었다. 그런 물음을 받고 보니 주교는 자연히 속으로, 어차피 누군가 하나는 방을 나가야 한다면 나가는 쪽이 프라우디 부인인 편이 나을지도 모르겠다고 생각했다. 속으로는 그렇게 말했으나 겉으로는 또다시 머리를 긁적이고 또다시 두 엄지를 맞돌렸다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 노여움으로 끓어 넘치고 있었다. 아아, 슬프도다! 적이 그리했듯 그녀도 성미를 다스릴 수만 있었더라면 늘 그래 왔듯 이겼을 것이다. 그러나 다른 여걸들이 그러했듯 그녀 또한 신들린 노여움에 사로잡혔고, 그리하여 넘어졌다.\n\n“주교님,” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 대답을 들을 수 있는 건가요, 없는 건가요?”\n\n마침내 그가 깊은 침묵을 깨고 자신이 슬로프 편임을 선언했다. “저, 여보,” 그가 말했다. “슬로프 씨와 나는 지금 아주 바쁘오.”\n\n그것이 전부였다. 그 이상은 필요하지 않았다. 그는 싸움터에 나아가 온종일의 먼지와 열기를 견디고 적의 격노를 마주하여 승리를 거두었다. 오직 제 자신에게 진실하기만 하다면 성공이란 얼마나 쉬운 것인가!\n\n슬로프 씨는 자기가 얻은 것의 크기를 대번에 알아보고, 패배한 부인에게 그녀가 결코 잊지도 용서하지도 않을 승리의 눈길을 던졌다. 여기서 그는 틀렸다. 그는 겸손하게 그녀를 바라보며 온순하게 애원하는 눈으로 그 노여움을 달래었어야 했다. 자기가 이긴 것을 용서해 달라고, 의무를 위해 어쩔 수 없이 버틴 것을 너그러이 보아 달라고 눈빛으로 말했어야 했다. 그랬더라면 그 위압적인 가슴을 얼마쯤 누그러뜨리고 훗날의 화해를 위한 길을 닦았을지도 모른다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 화해 없이 다스릴 작정이었다. 아, 잊어버리기도 잘하는, 세상 물정 모르는 사내여! 그대는 저 조그맣게 떨고 있는 제물을 그를 차지한 여자에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있는가? 두 사람을 잠자리와 밥상에서 갈라놓을 수 있는가? 그는 그녀의 살 중의 살이요 뼈 중의 뼈가 아닌가, 또 앞으로도 그러해야 하지 않겠는가? 그녀가 욕되게 방에서 쫓겨나는 지금이야 그대가 버티고 서서 개가를 올려도 좋겠으나, 저 휘장이 드리워질 때, 저 무시무시한 투구가 턱 밑에 매어질 때, 주교에게 남은 얼마 안 되는 용맹이 머리 위 술 장식에 눌려 기가 죽을 때, 그대가 거기 있을 수 있겠는가? 아내가 “주교님과 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다”고 할 때, 그대가 그 자리에 끼어들 수 있겠는가?\n\n그러나 당장으로는 슬로프 씨의 승리가 완전했으니, 프라우디 부인이 더 말을 섞지 않고 방을 나갔고 나가면서 문을 닫는 일도 잊지 않았기 때문이다. 그다음에는 새 동맹자들 사이에 긴밀한 회담이 이어졌는데, 그 자리에서는 슬로프 씨로서는 말하고 주교로서는 듣는 것이 스스로도 놀라울 만한 이야기가 숱하게 오갔다. 그런데도 하나는 그것을 말했고 다른 하나는 그것을 들었으되 서로 감정이 상하지는 않았다. 이제는 에두를 것이 없었다. 전속 사제는 주교에게 이렇게 대놓고 말했다. 세상은 각하께서 부인의 다스림 아래 계신다고 여기고 있으며, 교구 안에서 각하의 신망과 인격이 손상되고 있으며, 여자의 힘에 어울리지 않는 일에까지 프라우디 부인이 간섭하시게 두었다가는 반드시 곤경에 빠지실 것이며, 실은 그 신음하고 계신 멍에를 벗어 던지지 않으신다면 각하께서는 웃음거리가 되고 마시리라고. 주교는 처음에는 헛기침을 하고 우물거리며 그 말이 사실이 아닌 척했다. 그러나 그 부정은 굳세지 못했고 이내 무너졌다. 그는 곧 침묵으로써 제가 신하의 처지임을 인정했고, 슬로프 씨의 도움을 받아 방식을 바꾸겠노라고 약속했다. 슬로프 씨 또한 제 처지를 그럴싸하게 변호했다. 그는 늘 자기를 후원해 준 부인, 그토록 여러 모로 자기를 도와준 부인, 실은 자기를 주교의 눈에 들도록 천거해 준 바로 그 부인을 거스르는 것이 얼마나 마음 아픈 일인지를 설명했다. 그러나 그의 말대로 이제 그의 의무는 지엄한 것이었다. 그는 각별한 신임의 자리에 있고 주교의 신변에 직접 그리고 특별히 매여 있다는 것이었다. 그런 자리에 있는 만큼 그의 양심은 오직 주교의 이익만을 살필 것을 요구했고, 그래서 그는 감히 솔직히 말씀드린 것이라고 했다.\n\n주교는 이 말을 그 말값만큼만 받아들였고, 슬로프 씨도 딱 그만큼만 의도했다. 그것은 슬로프 씨가 먹여야 했던 알약에 금박을 입힌 것이었고, 주교는 그 약이 그동안 그토록 오래 먹어 온 다른 알약보다는 덜 쓰리라고 여겼다.\n\n“주교님”은 착한 아이처럼 곧바로 상을 받았다. 그는 편지를 쓰라는 지시를 받았고 즉시 대주교에게 그 초청을 받아들이겠다는 또 하나의 쪽지를 썼다. 이 쪽지는 부인보다 신중한 슬로프 씨가 몸소 들고 나가 제 손으로 부쳤다. 그리하여 그는 이 자주적 처분의 행위가 가능한 한 완전히 fait accompli 가 되도록 못을 박았다. 그는 주교가 하딩 씨에게도 당장 편지를 쓰게 하려고 빌기도 하고 구슬리기도 하고 으르기도 했으나, 주교는 잠시 아내에게서 풀려나기는 했어도 아직 슬로프 씨에게 사로잡히지는 않았다. 그는 그런 제안은 어떤 공식적인 형식을 갖추어야 하며, 자기는 아직 그 형식에 서명할 준비가 되지 않았고, 서명하기 전에 하딩 씨를 만나 보고 싶다고 말했는데, 아마 참말이었을 것이다. 다만 슬로프 씨가 하딩 씨더러 자기를 찾아와 달라고 청하는 것은 좋다고 했다. 제가 이룬 것에 실망하지 않은 채 슬로프 씨는 제 길을 갔다. 그는 먼저 주머니 속의 그 귀한 쪽지를 부쳤고, 그런 다음 다른 일들에 나섰으니 우리는 다른 장에서 그를 따라가 보아야 한다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 남편의 방문을 쾅 닫는 데서 얻을 수 있는 만큼의 만족을 얻은 뒤에도 곧바로 퀴버풀 부인에게로 가지는 않았다. 실은 물리침을 당한 뒤 처음 얼마 동안은 그 부인을 다시 볼 낯이 없다고 느꼈다. 자기가 졌음을 인정해야 하고, 왕관이 제 이마에서, 왕홀이 제 손에서 떠나갔음을 고백해야 할 터였다! 아니, 내일이나 모레 편지를 보내겠다는 약속과 함께 전갈이나 하나 넣어 두자. 그렇게 마음먹고 그녀는 침실로 갔으나, 여기서 다시 마음이 바뀌었다. 그 신성한 방의 공기가 그녀의 용기를 얼마쯤 되살려 주고 기운을 북돋아 주었다. 아킬레우스가 제 갑주를 보고 몸이 달아올랐듯이, 돈키호테의 가슴이 창을 움켜쥐자 굳세어졌듯이, 프라우디 부인도 남편의 베개에 눈길이 닿자 새로운 월계관을 내다보았다. 그녀는 절망하지 않으리라. 그렇게 마음을 정하고 그녀는 위엄 있는 몸가짐과 되살아난 낯빛으로 퀴버풀 부인에게 내려갔다.\n\n주교의 서재에서 벌어진 이 장면은 이야기하는 데보다 실제로 벌어지는 데 더 오래 걸렸다. 어쩌면 우리가 그 대화를 다 옮기지 못했는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 퀴버풀 부인은 몹시 초조해지기 시작해서, 서브소일 농부가 자기를 기다리다 지쳐 버리지나 않았을까 생각하고 있던 참에 프라우디 부인이 돌아왔다. 아, 저 어머니의 가슴이 얼마나 두근거렸을지 누가 말할 수 있으랴. 탄원하는 이는 저 대단한 부인의 얼굴을 들여다보며 거기에 집과 수입과 안락과 앞날의 넉넉함에 대한 약속이 적혀 있는지, 아니면 끝없이 이어지며 점점 깊어질 가난의 선고가 적혀 있는지를 읽으려 했으니! 가엾은 어머니여! 가엾은 아내여! 거기에는 그대를 위로할 것이 별로 없었다!\n\n“퀴버풀 부인,” 부인은 자기는 앉지도 않은 채 자못 엄한 어조로 이렇게 말했다. “내가 보기에 자네 남편은 이 일에서 아주 무르고 아주 어리석게 처신했네.”\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 주교의 부인이 서 있는데 자기가 앉아 있는 것은 무례한 일이라 여겨 즉시 일어섰다. 그러나 다시 앉으라는 분부를 받고 그대로 앉혀졌으니, 프라우디 부인이 선 채로 그녀 위에서 설교를 하려 함이었다. 신사가 다른 사람을 제 앞에 세워 둔 채 제 자리에 앉아 있는 것은 일반적으로 무례한 일로 여겨지는데, 짐작건대 부인네들에게도 같은 법이 적용될 것이다. 흔히 그렇게 느껴지기는 하나, 우리가 보기에 그것은 손님더러 앉으라고 청해 놓고 자기는 제 다리로 서서 말하는 대인물이 드러내는 불쾌함이나 아랫사람 취급을 당한다는 느낌에 견주면 절반에도 미치지 못한다. 예법에 어긋난 이런 짓을 우리말로 옮기면 이런 뜻이 된다. “세상에서 통용되는 예의의 규칙에 따르면 나는 그대에게 자리를 권해야 하오. 내가 그리하지 않는다면 그대는 세상에 대고 내가 오만하고 버릇없다고 나무랄 것이오. 나는 세상에 따르겠소. 그러나 그렇다고 내가 그대와 대등한 자리에 서지는 않겠소. 그대는 앉아도 좋으나 나는 그대와 함께 앉지는 않겠소. 그러니 내가 이르는 대로 앉으시오. 그러면 나는 서서 그대에게 대고 말하겠소!”\n\n프라우디 부인이 하려던 말이 바로 이것이었고, 퀴버풀 부인은 너무 애가 타고 너무 허둥대어 그 술책의 온전한 뜻까지 새기지는 못했으나 그 효과만은 어김없이 느꼈다. 그녀는 기가 죽고 마음이 불편해져서 두 번째로 의자에서 일어서려 했다.\n\n“앉아 있게, 퀴버풀 부인, 그대로 앉아 있게. 내 말은 자네 남편이 지극히 무르고 지극히 어리석었다는 것일세. 퀴버풀 부인, 제 스스로를 돕지 않는 사람을 돕기란 불가능한 일이야. 이 일에서 내가 이제 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이 아무것도 없지 않을까 몹시 걱정일세.”\n\n“아이고, 프라우디 부인, 그런 말씀 마십시오.” 가엾은 여인이 또다시 벌떡 일어서며 말했다.\n\n“앉아 있으래도, 퀴버풀 부인. 이 일에서 내가 자네를 위해 더 해 줄 수 있는 것이 없을까 걱정이라니까. 자네 남편은 참으로 알 수 없게도 내가 내어 줄 권한을 받은 그 자리를 제 손으로 내놓아 버렸네. 당연한 이야기지만 주교님께서는 당신 아래 성직자들이 제 마음을 스스로 알고 있기를 기대하시지. 그분이 끝내 어떻게 하실지, 우리가 마침내 무엇을 하기로 정할지 나는 지금 말할 수 없네. 자네 식구가 얼마나 되는지를 알고 있으니—”\n\n“아이가 열넷입니다, 프라우디 부인, 열넷이라고요! 그런데 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하는 형편이라니, 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하다니요! 성직자의 자식들에게는 가혹한 일입니다. 늘 제 직분을 번듯하게 다해 온 사람에게도 가혹한 일이고요!” 제 자신에 대해서는 한마디도 나오지 않았으나, 8월의 길바닥 먼지가 자국을 남긴 그 크고 거친 뺨을 타고 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 이 지면에서 상냥하거나 사랑스러운 부인으로 그려지지 않았다. 독자가 그녀에게 크게 호감을 갖게 하려는 의도도 없었다. 무릇 모든 소설에는 남자 천사와 여자 천사가 하나씩, 남자 악마와 여자 악마가 하나씩 있어야 한다는 것이 정해진 법도다. 이 지면에서도 그 법도가 지켜졌다고 본다면, 뒤엣것 가운데 여자 쪽 배역은 프라우디 부인에게 돌아갔다고 보아야 할 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 온통 악마이기만 했던 것은 아니다. 그 뻣뻣하게 살을 댄 몸판 안에도 심장이 하나 있기는 있었다. 크기가 대단하지는 않았을 테고 쉬이 닿을 수 있는 것도 분명 아니었지만 말이다. 그런데 퀴버풀 부인은 기어이 거기에 닿았고, 프라우디 부인은 스스로 여자임을 드러내 보였다. 그것이 열네 아이와 그들이 겨우 얻을 빵과 어쩌면 걸칠 것조차 없을 등짝 때문이었는지, 아버지 된 이가 해 온 일의 번듯함 때문이었는지, 아니면 어머니의 얼굴에 뒤섞인 먼지와 눈물 때문이었는지, 우리는 굳이 말하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 프라우디 부인의 마음이 움직였다.\n\n그녀는 다른 여자들이 했을 법한 방식으로 그것을 드러내지는 않았다. 퀴버풀 부인에게 오드콜로뉴를 건네지도, 포도주 한 잔을 내오라 이르지도 않았다. 화장대로 데려가 솔과 빗, 수건과 물을 쓰라고 권하지도 않았다. 부드러운 말을 조금씩 건네며 다정하게 달래어 마음을 가라앉혀 주지도 않았다. 퀴버풀 부인은 겉모습이 거칠기는 해도 이 나라 어느 귀부인 못지않게 그런 자잘한 다정한 보살핌을 반겼을 사람이었다. 그러나 그런 것은 하나도 나오지 않았다. 그 대신 프라우디 부인은 한 손으로 다른 손을 철썩 치며, 욕설을 곁들여서가 아니라 — 숙녀이자 안식일 엄수주의자이자 여자 주교인 그녀로서는 욕설을 할 수 없었으므로 — 엄숙한 다짐을 곁들여, 자기는 “그런 일이 되게 두지 않겠다”고 선언했다.\n\n이 말의 뜻인즉, 퀴버풀 씨에게 약속된 임명이 슬로프 씨의 배신과 제 남편의 무름으로 인해 감쪽같이 가로채이도록 두지 않겠다는 것이었다. 이 뜻을 그녀는 곧 퀴버풀 부인에게 설명해 주었다.\n\n“자네 남편은 어쩌자고 그런 바보짓을 했나.” 이제 높은 말에서 내려와 손님 곁에 바싹 다가앉으며 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람이 던진 미끼를 덥석 물다니. 그이가 그토록 어리석게 굴지만 않았더라면 자네들이 요양원에 들어가는 것을 아무것도 막지 못했을 걸세.”\n\n가엾은 퀴버풀 부인은 남편을 앞에 두고 물러 터졌다고 나무라는 데는 제 혀가 꽤나 잘 돌아가는 사람이었고, 아이들 앞에서 남편 이야기를 할 때도 늘 그리 공손하지만은 않았을 것이다. 그러나 남들이 남편을 헐뜯는 것은 조금도 듣기 좋지 않았으므로 그녀는 남편을 두둔하기 시작했다. 그이는 당연히 슬로프 씨를 프라우디 부인 본인이 보낸 사자로 여겼고, 슬로프 씨는 특별히 부인의 벗으로 알려져 있으니, 슬로프 씨가 한 말을 감히 의심했다면 퀴버풀 씨는 부인께 예를 잃는 것이 되었으리라고 설명했다.\n\n그렇게 마음이 누그러진 프라우디 부인은 다시 한 번 자기는 “그런 일이 되게 두지 않겠다”고 선언했고, 마침내 주교관 안에서 제 권세와 영향력이 미치는 끝까지 퀴버풀 씨의 임명을 관철하겠다는 다짐과 함께 퀴버풀 부인을 집으로 돌려보냈다. “관철”이라는 말을 되뇌면서 그녀는 잠자리 모자를 쓴 주교를 떠올리고는 입술을 꾹 다문 채 고개를 조금 저었다. 아, 출세를 바라는 목사들이여, nolo episcopari 라는 말이 그 귀에 가장 달콤하게 들리는 신학자들이여, 그대들 가운데 누가 이런 조건으로 주교가 되려 하겠는가?\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 농부의 수레를 타고 집으로 돌아갔다. 마음이 가볍지야 않았으나, 그래도 찾아가 보기를 잘했다는 데는 만족하고 있었다.\n\n# 제27장. 사랑의 한 장면\n\n슬로프 씨는, 앞서 말한 대로, 상당한 승리감을 안고 주교관을 나섰다. 어려움이 죄다 끝났다고 여긴 것은 아니었고 — 그렇게까지 스스로를 속이지는 않았다 — 다만 판 위의 말들이 허락하는 한 첫 수를 잘 두었으며 스스로를 탓할 것이 없다고 느꼈다. 그는 무엇보다 먼저 대주교에게 보내는 편지를 부쳤고, 그것을 확실히 해 둔 다음 제가 얻어 낸 이점을 밀어붙이러 나섰다. 볼드 부인이 집에 있었더라면 찾아갔겠지만 그녀가 플럼스테드에 있다는 것을 알았으므로 다음과 같은 짧은 편지를 썼다. 그것은 그가 길고 다정한 서한의 연속이 되리라 믿어 의심치 않은 것의 시작이었다.\n\n지금으로서는 제가 부인의 아버님과 서신을 주고받을 수 없다는 것을 부인께서 완벽히 이해하시리라 믿습니다. 그럴 수 있기를 진심으로 바라며, 안개가 걷히고 우리가 서로를 알게 될 날이 멀지 않기를 바랍니다. 그러나 오늘 Q 씨가 제가 있는 자리에서 요양원 워든 직에 대해 자신이 지녔던 모든 권리를 사임하였다는 것과, 주교께서 그 자리를 부인의 존경하는 아버님께 제안하실 뜻임을 제게 확언하셨다는 것을 몇 줄 적어 보내는 기쁨만은 스스로에게서 빼앗을 수가 없습니다.\n\n제 정중한 인사와 함께, 지금 부인과 함께 머물고 계실 그분께 수요일이나 목요일 열 시에서 한 시 사이에 주교를 찾아뵈어 달라고 청해 주시겠습니까. 이는 주교의 뜻입니다. 저를 위해 어느 날인지와 하딩 씨께 편하실 시각을 한 줄 적어 보내 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 하인들이 지체 없이 그분을 안으로 모시도록 일러 두겠습니다. 아마 더 말씀드리지 않는 편이 나을 테지만 — 그래도 저는 주교님과 그분 사이에서 그분이 제 직무를 수행하실 방식에 관하여는 어떤 문제도 거론되지 않으리라는 것을 부인께서 아버님께 알려 드리실 수 있기를 바랍니다. 저로서는 그분이 하셨던 것보다, 또 앞으로 다시 하실 것보다 더 만족스럽게 그 직무를 수행할 성직자는 없으리라 확신합니다.\n\n지난번에 저는 신중하지 못했고, 아버님의 연세와 제 나이를 헤아릴 때 지나치게 성급했습니다. 이제는 그분이 제 사과를 물리치지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 또한 부인의 도움과 어여쁘고 경건한 수고에 힘입어, 하느님의 은혜와 도우심으로 이 도시의 가난한 이들에게 축복이 될 만한 안식일 학교를 저 오래된 기부 재단에 붙여 놓을 날까지 살 수 있기를 여전히 바랍니다.\n\n이 편지가 비밀에 부쳐야 할 것임은 부인께서 곧바로 아시리라 봅니다. 주제가 주제이니 당연히 그렇습니다. 그러나 역시 당연하게도, 보여 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여기신다면 이는 부인의 눈뿐 아니라 아버님의 눈을 위한 것이기도 합니다.\n\n제가 아끼는 작은 벗 조니가 여전히 튼튼하기를 바랍니다 — 사랑스러운 꼬마 녀석. 그 아름답고 긴 비단결 머리채에 여전히 무례한 공격을 가하고 있는지요?\n\n바체스터에서 부인의 벗들이 부인을 몹시 그리워하고 있다고 장담할 수 있습니다. 그러나 이토록 무더운 날씨에 꽃과 들판 사이에서 지내시는 것을 아까워한다면 그야말로 잔인한 일이겠지요.\n\n부디 저를 믿어 주십시오, 친애하는 볼드 부인, 더없이 진실한 마음으로, 오바디아 슬로프\n\n바체스터에서, 금요일.\n\n그런데 이 편지는, 전체로 놓고 보고 또 슬로프 씨가 엘리너와 상당한 정도의 친밀함을 자처하고 싶어 했다는 점을 감안하면, 머리채에 대한 언급만 아니었더라면 나쁘지 않았을 것이다. 신사는 참으로 각별히 가까운 사이가 아니고서는 숙녀에게 그 머리채에 관하여 편지를 쓰지 않는 법이다. 그러나 슬로프 씨가 그것을 알고 있으리라 기대할 수는 없었다. 그는 제 서한에 애정을 조금 담고 싶어 못 견뎌 했으나, 그 편지가 하딩 씨에게 보이리라는 것을 알았으므로 그러는 것이 분별없다고 여겼다. 그런 당부가 지켜지지 않으리라고 느끼지만 않았더라면 그는 이 편지를 엄격히 사적인 것으로 하여 엘리너 자신의 눈 외에는 어떤 눈에도 보이지 말라고 못 박았을 것이다. 그리하여 그는 정념을 억눌러 “애정을 담아”라고 서명하지 않았고, 그 대신 머리채에 대한 찬사로 만족했다.\n\n편지를 다 쓴 그는 그것을 볼드 부인의 집으로 가져갔고, 그곳에서 하인에게 그날 오후에 플럼스테드로 물건을 보낼 참이라는 말을 듣고는 여러 차례 당부를 곁들여 그 하인의 손에 맡겨 두었다.\n\n이제 우리는 슬로프 씨를 따라가 그와 함께 하루를 마무리한 다음, 다음 장에서 그 편지와 그것의 중대한 운명으로 돌아오기로 하겠다.\n\n구애에 관하여 아주 훌륭한 조언을 들려주는 옛 노래가 하나 있다.\n\n새 사랑과 맺어지기 전에 옛 사랑과는 헤어져 두는 것이 좋으니라.\n\n이 격언의 지혜를 슬로프 씨는 알지 못했고, 따라서 볼드 부인에게 편지를 쓴 뒤 시뇨라 네로니를 찾아가기에 이르렀다. 실인즉 어느 쪽이 옛 사랑이고 어느 쪽이 새 사랑인지 말하기 어려웠으니, 슬로프 씨가 거의 같은 무렵에 둘 다에게 마음을 빼앗겼기 때문이다. 어쩌면 그는 활에 시위를 둘 걸어 두는 것이 나쁘지 않다고 여겼는지도 모른다. 그러나 큐피드의 활에 걸린 두 개의 시위는 그것을 쓰려는 자에게 언제나 위험하다. 사내는 두 걸상 사이에서 바닥에 주저앉을 수도 있다는 것을 기억해야 한다.\n\n그러나 실인즉 슬로프 씨는 제 안의 나은 본능에 순종하여 볼드 부인을 좇았고, 못한 본능에 순종하여 시뇨라를 좇았다. 그가 그 미망인을 얻어 아내로 삼았다 한들 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없었을 것이다. 독자여, 그대와 나와 엘리너의 다른 벗들은 그런 승리의 이야기를 몹시 역겹고 실망스럽게 받아들였겠지만, 우리가 노여워할 상대는 슬로프 씨가 아니라 엘리너였을 것이다. 남자 주교와 여자 주교, 수석 사제와 참사회와 교구 성직자들이 총회를 열어 앉아 있었다 한들 그런 결합에서 못마땅해할 구석은 하나도 찾아내지 못했을 것이다. 저 신비롭고 위력 있는 종교 회의인 성직자 회의 그 자체도 결코 그것을 헐뜯지 못했을 것이다. 연 1000파운드의 소유와 아름다운 아내는 저 강단의 매혹자가 지닌 목소리를 조금도 해치지 않았을 것이며, 그 모범적인 성직자의 품위와 경건함을 덜지도 않았을 것이다.\n\n그러나 시뇨라 네로니와의 거래는 그런 성질의 것이 될 리 없었다. 첫째로 그는 그녀의 남편이 살아 있다는 것을 알았고, 따라서 정직하게 구애할 수가 없었다. 또한 설령 그것이 가능했다 하더라도 그녀에게는 그의 정직한 구애를 받을 만한 조건이 하나도 없었다. 그녀는 지참금이 없을 뿐 아니라 불운으로 인하여, 쓸모 있는 반려를 원하는 어떤 사내의 아내로도 택할 수 없는 몸이었다. 슬로프 씨는 그녀가 어찌해 볼 도리 없고 가망도 없는 앉은뱅이라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨는 어쩔 수가 없었다. 그는 스탠호프 박사 집의 안쪽 응접실에 제 시간을 바치는 것이 잘못임을 알았다. 거기서 벌어지는 일이 새어 나가면 볼드 부인 앞에서 자신이 완전히 파멸하리라는 것도 알았다. 추문이 곧 제 뒤꿈치를 따라붙어, 그가 그 부인의 귀에 쏟아부은 한숨에 관한 소식이, 그것도 부풀려진 소식이 바체스터의 검은 옷들 사이에 퍼지리라는 것도 알았다. 자신이 삶의 공인된 원칙에, 훨씬 더 높은 성공을 이루고자 기대어 온 행동의 법칙에 어긋나게 굴고 있다는 것도 알았다. 그러나 앞서 말한 대로 그는 어쩔 수가 없었다. 정념이, 생애 처음으로 정념이 그에게는 너무 강했다.\n\n시뇨라로 말하자면 그런 변명조차 내세울 수 없으니, 실상 그녀는 제 앞에 먼저 무릎 꿇었던 스무 명의 다른 사내들만큼도 슬로프 씨를 아끼지 않았기 때문이다. 그녀는 기꺼이, 아니 게걸스럽게 그의 경배를 받아들였다. 그는 바체스터가 지금까지 그녀의 거미줄에 대어 준 것 가운데 가장 훌륭한 파리였고, 시뇨라는 놀라운 그물을 짜는 힘센 거미로서 파리를 잡지 않고서는 도무지 살 수가 없었다. 이 점에서 그녀의 취향은 고약했으니, 잡고 나면 그 먹이를 쓸 데가 없었기 때문이다. 어미가 짜 준 그물을 쓰기 마련인 젊은 처녀 파리들이 그러듯 혼인으로 그것을 먹어 치울 수도 없었다. 그렇다고 덜 떳떳한 종류의 탈선으로 삼켜 버릴 수도 없었다. 그 불운한 병약함이 정부와 함께 달아나리라는 희망을 죄다 막아 버렸다. 소파에서 옮기는 데만 하인이 셋이나 필요한 부인을 데리고 달아나기란 불가능한 일이었다.\n\n시뇨라는 어떤 정념에도 굴복하지 않았다. 그녀의 사랑의 시절은 지나갔다. 그녀는 제 심장을, 애초에 그녀에게 있었던 만큼의 심장을, 어린 시절에 이미 다 살아 버렸으니, 슬로프 씨가 유클리드 제2권과 식당 배급창구에 밀린 외상값을 생각하고 있던 나이의 일이었다. 나이로 치면 이 부인이 이 신사보다 어렸으나, 감정에서, 사랑의 사정에 대한 지식에서, 술수에서 그는 그녀보다 헤아릴 수 없이 어렸다. 그녀에게는 발치에 어떤 사내를 두는 일이 반드시 필요했다. 그것이 그녀 삶의 유일한 상례적 흥분거리였다. 그녀는 그것이 주는 권력의 행사를 즐겼다. 그것은 이제 그녀 야심의 거의 유일한 양식이었다. 그녀는 언니에게 자기는 어떤 사내든 바보로 만들 수 있다고 자랑하곤 했고, 언니 또한 그녀 못지않게 여자다운 섬세함이 부족한 사람이라 삶의 통상적인 즐거움을 빼앗긴 가엾은 병자에게 그만한 소일거리쯤은 마련해 주는 것이 공평하다고 사람 좋게 여겼다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠져 있었으나 그 사실을 거의 알지 못했다. 시뇨라는 소년이 코르크에 풍뎅이를 꿰듯 그를 꿰어 두고, 그가 몸부림치며 도는 기운찬 고통을 즐길 수 있게 해 두었다. 그리고 그녀는 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 아주 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 오전 방문에 나서는 성직자에게 가능한 온갖 치장 — 이를테면 깨끗한 넥타이, 깨끗한 손수건, 새 장갑, 그리고 아주 불필요하달 수는 없는 향수 한 soupçon — 을 제 몸에 더한 채 세 시쯤 박사네 문 앞을 찾았다. 이 시각이면 시뇨라는 거의 언제나 안쪽 응접실에 혼자 있었다. 어머니는 아직 내려오지 않았다. 박사는 밖에 나가 있거나 제 방에 있었다. 버티는 밖에 있었고, 샬럿은 어쨌든 용건이 특별히 제 동생에게 있는 손님이 찾아오면 방을 비켜 주었다. 그것이 그녀가 생각하는 자비이자 자매의 도리였다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 늘 하던 대로 스탠호프 씨를 찾았고, 하인은 늘 하던 대로 시뇨라가 응접실에 계시다고 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그는 위층으로 올라갔다. 그는 언제나처럼 그녀가 앞에 프랑스어 책을 펴 놓고 탁자에는 상감을 놓은 아름답고 작은 문갑을 열어 둔 채 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 들어서던 순간 그녀는 마침 무언가를 쓰고 있는 참이었다.\n\n“아, 나의 벗이여.” 그녀가 문갑 너머로 왼손을 내밀며 말했다. “오늘은 오시리라 생각지 않았는데, 바로 이 순간에 당신께 편지를 쓰고 있었답니다—”\n\n슬로프 씨는 그 부드럽고 희고 섬세한 손을 제 손에 잡고 — 참으로 부드럽고 희고 섬세한 손이었다 — 그 위로 커다랗고 붉은 머리를 숙여 입을 맞추었다. 그것은 볼 만한 광경이었고, 지은이가 제대로 해낼 수만 있다면 기록해 둘 만한 일이었으며, 화폭에 옮겨 둘 만한 그림이었다. 슬로프 씨는 덩치가 크고 어설프고 굼떴으며, 마음이 온통 그 추격에 가 있는 터라 편치가 않았다. 앞서 말한 대로 그 부인은 희고 섬세했다. 그녀에게 딸린 모든 것이 곱고 세련되었다. 그의 손안에 놓인 그녀의 손은 당근 사이에 놓인 장미 한 송이 같았고, 그가 거기에 입을 맞출 때 그는 제 먹이 사이에서 그런 꽃을 발견한 암소 같은 꼴이었다. 그녀는 엎드린 여신처럼 우아했고, 게다가 아도니스에게 구애하던 때의 비너스가 그러했을 만큼 침착했다.\n\n아, 그만한 우아함과 그만한 아름다움이 몸을 낮추어 그런 추격에 스스로를 허비하다니!\n\n“당신께 편지를 쓰고 있던 참이었어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 이제 이 끄적거림은 휴지통으로 가도 되겠군요.” 그러고는 찢기라도 할 듯이 문갑에서 금테 두른 편지지를 집어 들었다.\n\n“절대로 그리 되지 않을 겁니다.” 그가 말하며, 그 헌신적인 종이 위에 반 스톤 무게의 살과 피를 얹어 압류를 걸었다. “시뇨라, 당신이 제 눈을 위하여 쓴 것이라면 무엇도 그렇게 모독당해서는 안 됩니다.” 그러고는 그 편지를 집어 들어 그것 또한 당근 사이에 놓고 뜯어 먹더니, 이어서 그것을 읽어 내려가기 시작했다.\n\n“어머나, 슬로프 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “설마 제가 당신께 써 보낸 잡동사니를 죄다 간직하고 계신다는 말씀은 아니겠지요. 저는 절반쯤은 제가 무엇을 쓰는지도 모르고, 알 때에도 그것이 아궁이 뒤편에나 어울린다는 것을 압니다. 편지를 모아 두는 그 흉한 버릇만은 없으시기를 바라요.”\n\n“적어도 저는 그것들을 휴지통에 던지지는 않습니다. 스러지는 것이 그것들에 정해진 운명이라면, 그것들은 합당하게 스러지지요. 옛날의 디도가 그러했듯 장작더미 위에서 불태워집니다.”\n\n“물론 강철 펜을 하나 꽂아서 말이지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그래야 비유가 더 완전해질 테니까요. 제가 아는 부인네들 가운데 디도 여사만큼 어처구니없는 이는 없다고 봅니다. 어째서 클레오파트라처럼 하지 않았을까요? 어째서 제 배들을 끌어내어 그와 함께 가겠다고 우기지 않았을까요? 사기로 얻은 땅을 잃는 것도 견디지 못했고, 그러면서 연인을 잃는 것도 견디지 못했지요. 그래서 두 걸상 사이로 떨어져 버렸어요. 슬로프 씨, 무슨 일을 하시든 사랑과 사업만은 결코 섞지 마세요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 눈까지, 얼룩덜룩한 이마를 지나 머리카락 뿌리께까지 붉어졌다. 그는 시뇨라가 볼드 부인에 관한 제 속셈을 모조리 알고 있다고 확신했다. 양심이 그에게 들켰다고 일러 주었다. 이제 그의 선고가 내려질 참이었다. 그는 제 이중성 때문에 벌을 받고, 눈앞의 저 아름다운 피조물에게 거절당할 참이었다. 가엾은 사내. 그는 볼드 부인에 관한 제 속셈이 죄다 시뇨라에게 알려졌다 한들 그것은 그 부인의 소일거리에 흥취를 더해 줄 뿐이라는 것을 꿈에도 몰랐다. 슬로프 씨를 발치에 두는 것, 성직자 하나를 완전한 바보로 만들어 제 힘을 보이는 것, 신앙심 깊은 사내조차 그 정념을 다스리는 데 종교의 힘이 얼마나 보잘것없는지 그렇게 증명해 제 불신을 흡족하게 하는 것도 좋기는 했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 자기가 이 제물을 다른 여자에게서 꾀어내고 있으며, 그 다른 여자의 사랑이야말로 얻기만 하면 모든 면에서 이롭고 유익하리라는 것을 스스로 알 수 있게 된다면 그 흡족함은 한층 커질 터였다.\n\n시뇨라는 그런 여자 특유의 예리한 직감으로 슬로프 씨가 볼드 부인과의 혼인에 마음을 두고 있음을 진작 알아차렸으나, 디도를 들먹일 때에는 그것을 염두에 두지 않았다. 그러나 제 연인의 홍조를 보고 그의 마음에 무엇이 걸려 있는지 곧바로 알아차렸고, 그것을 이용하는 데 굼뜨지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 그의 얼굴을 정면으로, 성난 기색도 없고 그렇다고 미소도 없이, 그러나 강렬하고 압도적인 눈길로 바라보았다. 그런 다음 집게손가락을 들어 올리고 고개를 가볍게 저으며 말했다.\n\n“무슨 일을 하시든, 나의 벗이여, 사랑과 사업을 섞지 마세요. 당신의 보물과 당신의 부유한 도시에 붙어 있든지, 아니면 참된 사내답게 당신의 사랑을 좇든지 하세요. 그러나 둘 다를 노리려 들지는 마세요. 그러셨다가는 가엾은 디도가 그러했듯 가슴이 찢어진 채 죽으셔야 할 겁니다. 당신은 어느 쪽인가요, 슬로프 씨, 사랑인가요 돈인가요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 즉흥 설교에서 사람의 심금을 울리는 대목을 다룰 때처럼 애처로운 대답을 척척 내놓지 못했다. 그는 무언가 그럴듯한 말을, 그러면서 제 연인의 마음에 새겨진 인상을 지워 줄 말을 해야 한다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그것을 어떻게 해내야 할지 몰라 다소 쩔쩔맸다.\n\n“사랑은,” 그가 말했다. “참되고 압도적인 사랑은 사내가 느낄 수 있는 가장 강한 정념일 수밖에 없습니다. 그것은 다른 모든 소망을 다스리고 다른 모든 추구를 물리치게 마련이지요. 그러나 제게 사랑은 되돌아오지 않는 한 결코 그렇게 작동하지 않을 겁니다.” 그러고는 제 말의 부족함을 죄다 메워 보려는 뜻으로 시뇨라에게 다정한 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n“제 충고를 받아들이세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “사랑 따위는 마음 쓰지 마세요. 결국 그게 무엇인가요? 몇 주짜리 꿈이지요. 그것이 주는 기쁨의 전부예요. 한평생의 실망이 그 응보고요. 참된 사랑에 성공한 이가 누가 있던가요? 사랑에서의 성공은 그 사랑이 거짓이라는 뜻이에요. 참된 사랑은 언제나 낙담이거나 비극이지요. 줄리엣도 사랑했고, 하이디도 사랑했고, 디도도 사랑했는데, 그래서 어떻게 되었나요? 트로일로스는 사랑하다가 사내이기를 그쳤고요.”\n\n“트로일로스는 사랑하다가 우롱당한 것이지요.” 조금 더 사내다워진 전속 사제가 말했다. “사내가 사랑을 하고도 트로일로스가 되지 않을 수 있습니다. 모든 여자가 크레시다는 아니니까요.”\n\n“그래요, 모든 여자가 크레시다는 아니지요. 거짓이 언제나 여자 쪽에 있는 것도 아니고요. 이모진은 진실했는데, 그 보답이 무엇이었나요? 그 지아비는 자기가 없는 동안 아내 곁에 처음 다가온 사내의 정부가 되었다고 믿었지요. 데스데모나는 진실했다가 목이 졸렸어요. 오필리아는 진실했다가 미쳐 버렸고요. 영국 소설의 끝머리를 빼면 사랑에는 행복이란 것이 없습니다. 그러나 부와 돈과 집과 땅과 세간과 살림, 이 세상의 좋은 것들 안에는, 그래요, 그 안에는 무언가 손에 잡히는 것이, 붙들어 두고 누릴 수 있는 무언가가 있지요.”\n\n“아, 아닙니다.” 슬로프 씨가 그토록 정통에서 벗어난 교리에 무언가 항의는 해야 한다고 느끼며 말했다. “이 세상의 부는 누구도 행복하게 만들지 못합니다.”\n\n“그러면 무엇이 당신을, 당신을, 당신을 행복하게 만드나요?” 그녀가 몸을 일으켜 탁자 너머로 힘주어 말했다. “당신은 어느 샘에서 행복을 찾으시나요? 아무 데서도 찾지 않는다고는 말하지 마세요. 저는 믿지 않을 테니까요. 그것은 사람이라면 누구나 한평생을 들여 벌이는 수색이지요.”\n\n“그리고 그 수색은 언제나 헛되지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “우리는 하늘에서 그것을 바라는 것으로 만족해야 마땅한데도 땅에서 행복을 찾습니다.”\n\n“쳇! 당신은 스스로 믿지도 않는 교리를 설교하시는군요. 당신들은 다 그래요. 지상의 행복이란 없다는 것을 아신다면, 어째서 주교나 수석 사제가 되기를 갈망하시나요? 어째서 땅과 수입을 원하시나요?”\n\n“제게는 사내로서 타고난 야심이 있습니다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“물론 있으시겠지요. 타고난 정념도 있고요. 그러니까 저는 당신이 스스로 설교하시는 교리를 믿지 않는다고 말하는 거예요. 성 바울은 열광하는 사람이었어요. 그이는 제 야심과 정념이 신조와 다투지 않을 만큼 믿었지요. 한평생의 절반을 기둥 위에 곧추서서 보내는 저 동방의 광신자도 그렇고요. 저로 말하자면, 겉으로 드러난 표징으로 스스로를 나타내지 않는 믿음은 어떤 것도 믿지 않겠어요. 설교자 자신의 실행으로 뒷받침되지 않는 설교는 어떤 것도 진실하다고 여기지 않겠고요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 놀라고 소름이 끼쳤으나, 대답할 수 없다고 느꼈다. 지금 제가 그러하듯 악마의 심부름을 하고 있는 처지에 어찌 일어서서 제 주인의 가르침을 설교할 수 있겠는가? 그는 참된 신자였고, 그렇지 않았더라면 이런 것쯤은 그에게 아무것도 아니었을 것이다. 그는 웬만한 일에는 뻔뻔했으나 주님의 말씀을 장난감 삼을 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않았다. 이 모든 것을 시뇨라는 알고 있었고, 제 풍뎅이가 핀 위에서 빙글빙글 도는 것을 보며 자못 흥미를 느꼈다.\n\n“당신의 재치는 그런 논변을 즐기지요.” 그가 말했다. “그러나 당신의 심장과 당신의 이성은 그 논변과 함께 가지 않습니다.”\n\n“제 심장이라뇨!” 그녀가 말했다. “제게 그런 것이 있다고 여기신다면 제 구성 원리를 아주 잘못 아신 겁니다.” 실인즉 시뇨라가 하는 말 가운데 거짓인 것은 별로 없었다. 슬로프 씨가 스스로 속기를 허락했다면 그것은 그 자신의 잘못이었다. 제 자신에 관한 그녀의 언명만큼 숨김없는 것도 없었다.\n\n문갑을 얹은 작은 필기용 탁자가 여전히 그녀 앞에 서서, 말하자면 적을 막는 방벽 노릇을 하고 있었다. 그녀는 평소 그녀가 앉는 만큼은 거의 똑바로 앉아 있었고, 그는 의자를 소파 가까이 끌어다 놓은 터라 그와 그녀 사이에는 탁자 모서리밖에 없었다. 마침 그녀가 말할 때 그녀의 손이 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 슬로프 씨는 대답하면서 제 손을 그 위에 얹었다.\n\n“심장이 없다니요!” 그가 말했다. “그것은 당신이 스스로에게 씌우는 무거운 죄목이며, 저로서는 당신에게 유죄를 선고할 수 없는 죄목입니다—”\n\n그녀는 손을 거두었다. 그의 손길에 모욕이라도 당한 듯 재빠르고 성나게가 아니라, 부드럽고 천천히.\n\n“당신은 그 문제에 판결을 내리실 처지가 아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저를 시험해 보신 적이 없으니까요. 아뇨, 그러실 작정이라고는 말하지 마세요. 당신께 그럴 뜻이 없다는 것을 당신도 아시니까요. 실은 제게도 그럴 뜻이 없고요. 당신으로 말하자면, 당신은 저의 이 유령 같고 소름 끼치는 사랑을 좇는 것보다는 좀 더 실속 있는 결과가 나올 곳에 서약을 바치실 테지요—”\n\n“당신의 사랑이라면 군주의 꿈을 채우고도 남을 겁니다.” 슬로프 씨가 제 말뜻을 그리 분명히 알지도 못한 채 말했다.\n\n“대주교라고 하시지요, 슬로프 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. 가엾은 사내. 그녀는 그에게 몹시 잔인했다. 제 직업을 두고 하는 이 말에 그는 다시 코르크 위에서 한 바퀴 돌았다. 그러나 그는 미소를 지어 보이려 애쓰면서, 자기에게는 그토록 사활이 걸린 문제를 두고 농을 한다며 그녀를 부드럽게 나무랐다.\n\n“어쩌면 그렇게, 사내들은 우리를 얼간이로 만드시는지.” 그녀가 대꾸했다. “어쩌면 그렇게 우리를 마음껏 놀리시는지. 게다가 사내들 가운데서도 성직자들이 그 달콤하고 어르는 말솜씨가 가장 청산유수지요. 자, 제 얼굴을 보세요, 슬로프 씨, 담대하고 숨김없이.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 과연 애타는 사랑의 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 그러면서 다시 손을 뻗어 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다.\n\n“담대하게 저를 보시라고 했지요, 슬로프 씨. 그러나 그 담대함은 눈에만 두시라고 했어요.”\n\n“오, 마들린!” 그가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n“그래요, 제 이름은 마들린이지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 제 식구 말고는 보통 저를 그렇게 부르지 않아요. 자, 제 얼굴을 보세요, 슬로프 씨. 저를 사랑한다고 말씀하시는 것으로 알아들어도 되나요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 그렇게 말한 적이 결코 없었다. 그가 무슨 짜인 계획이라도 품고 그 자리에 왔다면, 그 속셈은 그런 선언은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않은 채 그 부인에게 사랑을 건네자는 것이었다. 그러나 이제 와서 제 사랑을 부인하기란 도무지 불가능한 일이었다. 그리하여 그에게는 정신없이 소파에 기대어 무릎을 꿇고, 사람의 사랑을 뛰어넘는 사랑으로 그녀를 사랑한다고 맹세하는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다.\n\n시뇨라는 그 다짐을 가슴 두근거림도 놀란 기색도 거의 없이 받아들였다. “이제 또 하나만 대답해 주세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “제 소중한 벗 엘리너 볼드와는 언제 혼인하시나요?”\n\n가엾은 슬로프 씨는 죽을 것 같은 고뇌 속에서 빙글빙글 돌았다. 그런 처지에서 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 알기란 정말이지 몹시 어려운 일이었다. 그렇다고 아무 대답도 하지 않는다면 그것이야말로 가장 확실한 유죄 판결이 될 터였다. 그럴 바에는 차라리 제게 씌워진 죄목을 곧바로 인정하는 편이 나았다.\n\n“어찌하여 저를 그런 표리부동으로 몰아세우십니까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“표리부동이라뇨! 저는 표리부동이라는 말은 한 적이 없어요. 당신께 아무 죄목도 씌운 적이 없고, 지금도 씌우지 않습니다. 제 앞에서 변명하려 들지 마세요. 당신은 제 아름다움에 헌신하겠다고 맹세하시면서 또 다른 이와 혼인을 코앞에 두고 계시지요. 저는 이것을 오히려 찬사로 여겨요. 변명하셔야 할 상대는 볼드 부인이지요. 그것이 어려우실 겁니다. 물론 그이를 어둠 속에 붙들어 둘 수 있다면 모르지만요. 성직자들은 다른 사내들보다 영리하니까요.”\n\n“시뇨라, 저는 당신을 사랑한다고 말씀드렸는데, 이제 당신은 저를 놀리시는군요.”\n\n“놀린다고요. 하느님이 이 사내를 굽어살피시기를. 대체 무엇을 바라시는 걸까요? 자, 이건 천천히 대답해 보세요. 지금 당장 생각 없이 말고, 느긋하게 곰곰이 헤아려서요. 당신은 볼드 부인과 혼인하실 것이 아닌가요?”\n\n“아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그는, 절묘한 사랑으로 사랑하지 않을 수 없는 그 여자를 절묘한 증오로 거의 미워했다.\n\n“하지만 분명 그이를 흠모하고 계시는 것이 아닌가요?”\n\n“아닙니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. 흠모라는 말이 그에게는 유난히 역겨웠다. 시뇨라는 그러리라 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n“그것 참 이상하군요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그이를 우러러보지 않으신다고요? 제 눈에 그이는 영국식 아름다움의 완성이에요. 게다가 부유하기까지 하고요. 저라면 그이야말로 당신을 끌어당길 만한 사람이라고 생각했을 텐데요. 자, 슬로프 씨, 이 문제에 관해 제가 충고를 하나 드리지요. 그 매력적인 미망인과 혼인하세요. 그이는 당신 아이들에게 좋은 어머니가 될 것이고, 성직자 집안의 훌륭한 안주인이 될 거예요.”\n\n“오, 시뇨라, 어찌 그리 잔인하실 수가 있습니까?”\n\n“잔인하다고요.” 그녀가 그때까지 쓰던 희롱조의 목소리를 바꾸어, 어조에 진심이 뚜렷이 배어나는 목소리로 말했다. “그게 잔인함인가요?”\n\n“제 심장이 온전히 당신의 것인데 제가 어찌 다른 이를 사랑할 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“그것이 잔인함이라면, 슬로프 씨, 제가 당신의 정념에 화답한다고 선언한다면 당신은 저를 무어라 하시겠어요? 제가 연인의 서약으로 당신을 묶어 이 소파 앞에서 날마다 고행을 하게 만든다면 어찌 생각하시겠어요? 사내의 사랑에 제가 무엇을 돌려드릴 수 있나요? 아, 소중한 벗이여, 당신은 제 운명의 조건을 헤아리지 못하셨어요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 그동안 내내 무릎을 꿇고 있지는 않았다. 사랑을 선언한 뒤 그는 이제 제가 차지한 새 지위에 어울린다고 여겨지는 만큼 재빨리 무릎에서 일어섰고, 선 채로 제 의자 등받이에 기대고 있었다. 시뇨라 쪽에서 터져 나온 이 다정함은 그를 완전히 압도하여, 불구이고 절름거리며 이미 혼인한 몸인 저 눈앞의 아름다운 피조물의 사랑을 확실히 얻을 수만 있다면 무엇이든 희생할 수 있겠다고 한순간 느끼게 만들었다.\n\n“그러면 제가 당신의 처지에 함께 마음 아파할 수도 없단 말입니까?” 그가 이제 그녀의 소파에 걸터앉아 발로 탁자를 밀어내며 말했다.\n\n“동정은 연민과 그리도 가깝지요!” 그녀가 말했다. “이런 앉은뱅이인 저를 가엾이 여기신다면, 저는 당신을 제게서 걷어차 버리겠어요.”\n\n“오, 마들린, 저는 오직 당신을 사랑할 뿐입니다.” 그러고는 그가 다시 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 입맞춤으로 삼켜 버렸다. 이번에 그녀는 손을 빼지 않고 그가 입 맞추는 동안 그대로 앉아, 커다란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 꼭 커다란 거미가 아주 단단히 걸려든 커다란 파리를 바라보듯이.\n\n“시뇨르 네로니가 바체스터에 온다고 해 보세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그이와 알고 지내시겠어요?”\n\n“시뇨르 네로니라니요!” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그이를 주교와 프라우디 부인과 그 따님들께 소개해 주시겠어요?” 그녀가 슬로프 씨가 유난히 싫어하는 그 끔찍한 놀림조의 목소리를 또다시 꺼내 들며 말했다.\n\n“어찌하여 그런 것을 물으십니까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“시뇨르 네로니라는 사람이 있다는 것을 당신이 아셔야 하기 때문이지요. 잊고 계셨던 듯해서요.”\n\n“당신이 그 가련한 자를 위하여, 그자가 결코 받을 자격이 없던 그 사랑을 한 조각이라도 간직하고 계신다고 생각했다면, 저는 제가 느끼는 바를 말씀드려 당신을 어지럽히느니 차라리 죽었을 겁니다. 아닙니다! 당신의 남편이 당신 심장의 주인이라면 저는 어쩌면 당신을 사랑했을지언정 당신은 결코 그것을 알지 못했을 겁니다.”\n\n“또 제 심장이군요! 어쩌면 그런 말씀을 하시는지. 그러면 당신은 남편이 아내 심장의 주인이 아니라면 그 아내의 정절을 요구할 권리가 없다고, 아내가 사랑하기를 그친다면 진실하기를 그쳐도 된다고 여기시는군요. 그것이 잉글랜드 교회의 성직자로서 이 문제에 관한 당신의 교리인가요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 제 영혼을 더럽히고 있다고 느껴지는 그 오염을 떨쳐 내려 속으로 몹시 애를 썼다. 그는 자기를 홀린 그 해로운 요부에게서 스스로를 뜯어내려 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었다. 다시 심장이 자유로워질 수가 없었다. 그는 이 사랑스러운 피조물을 사랑하는 데서 황홀한 기쁨을 기대했는데, 실망과 자책 말고는 얻는 것이 거의 없음을 이미 알아 가고 있었다. 그는 사해의 열매를 만난 것이었다. 눈에는 그토록 달고 맛깔스러우나 혀에는 그토록 쓰고 역겨운 열매를. 그는 그 사과를 입에 넣었고, 그것은 이 사이에서 재로 변했다. 그런데도 그는 스스로를 뜯어낼 수가 없었다. 그는 그녀가 자기를 비웃고 제 사랑을 조롱하며 제 신앙의 나약함을 모욕한다는 것을 알았고, 모를 수가 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 흠모를 반쯤 허락했고, 그 반쪽의 허락이 그의 불에 어찌나 기름을 부었는지 그의 경건함의 샘을 통틀어도 그것을 끌 수가 없었다. 그는 사납고 짜증스럽고 앙심 어린 기분이 들기 시작했다. 그는 무언가 매서운 말을, 그녀의 조롱이 자기를 베듯 그녀를 벨 만한 조롱을 궁리했다. 그녀 앞에 잠시 말없이 선 채로 그는 이렇게 헤아렸다. 그녀의 도도한 기개를 꺾고자 한다면 그녀보다 더 도도해짐으로써 그리해야 하며, 그녀를 제 발치에 두어 제 사랑을 구걸하게 만들고자 한다면 무관심으로 그녀를 정복하는 것이 마땅하다고. 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔다. 죽은 지식이 미치는 데까지는 그도 여자를 어떻게 길들여야 하는지 알았고, 아니 안다고 여겼다. 그러나 막상 제 전술을 펴 보려 하자 그는 어린아이처럼 실패했다. 사람과 사람 사이의 어떤 거래에서든 죽은 지식이 경험을 상대로 무슨 승산이 있겠는가? 하물며 남자와 여자 사이라면 대체 무슨 승산이 있겠는가? 슬로프 씨는 맹렬하게, 미친 듯이, 그리고 진실하게 사랑했으나 사랑의 노름을 해 본 적이 없었다. 시뇨라는 사랑이라고는 전혀 하지 않았으나 판 위의 온갖 수를 꿰고 있었다. 필리도르를 학생 하나와 맞붙여 놓은 격이었다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 계속 그를 모욕했고, 그는 계속 그것을 견뎠다.\n\n“사랑을 위해 세상을 희생한다니요!” 그가 또다시 늘어놓은 맹맹한 정념의 선언에 답하여 그녀가 말했다. “똑같은 말이 얼마나 자주 되풀이되었으며, 그러면서 얼마나 어김없이 똑같은 거짓이었던지요!”\n\n“거짓이라고요.” 그가 말했다. “제가 당신께 거짓되다고 말씀하시는 겁니까? 제 사랑이 진짜가 아니라고 말씀하시는 겁니까?”\n\n“거짓이냐고요? 물론 거짓이지요. 거짓의 아비만큼이나 거짓이에요 — 세상이 시작된 이래 거짓이란 것이 아비를 필요로 했고 스스로 태어난 것이 아니라면 말이지만요. 당신은 사랑을 위해 세상을 희생할 준비가 되셨다고요? 자, 당신이 무엇을 희생하실지 봅시다. 저는 혼인의 서약 따위는 조금도 마음에 두지 않아요. 당신이 친절히도 가련한 자라고 불러 주신 그 사람, 제가 사랑하고 순종하겠노라 맹세했던 그 사람은 어찌나 비루한지 역겨운 혐오로밖에는 떠올릴 수가 없어요. 제 심장의 회의실에서 저는 이미 그이와 이혼했습니다. 제게는 그것이 늙은 귀족들이 몇 달 동안 그이의 방탕한 삶을 낱낱이 뜯어보며 흡족해한 것이나 다름없어요. 저는 세상이 무어라 하든 조금도 마음에 두지 않습니다. 당신도 그만큼 솔직하시겠어요? 저를 아내로 삼아 댁으로 데려가시겠어요? 주교와 수석 사제와 참사회원들 앞에서 저를 슬로프 부인이라 부르시겠어요?” 그 가엾고 고문당하는 자는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 말없이 서 있었다. “뭐라고요! 그건 못 하신다고요. 그러면 말씀해 보세요, 제 매력을 위해 당신이 희생하실 세상이란 대체 어느 부분인가요?”\n\n“당신이 혼인할 수 있는 몸이라면, 저는 내일이라도 당신을 제 집으로 데려가겠고 그보다 더 높은 특권은 바라지도 않겠습니다.”\n\n“저는 자유로워요.” 그녀가 기운이 뻗쳐 거의 벌떡 일어설 듯이 말했다. 제 성직자 흠모자를 향한 그 꾸며 낸 애정에는 진실이라곤 없었으되, 사랑과 혼인 일반을 두고 말할 때의 그 경멸과 풍자에는 진짜 감정이 얼마간 섞여 있었기 때문이다. “저는 자유로워요 — 바람처럼 자유롭지요. 자, 저를 있는 그대로 거두시겠어요? 소원대로 하세요. 세상을 희생하고, 당신이 참된 사내임을 증명해 보이세요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 그녀의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였어야 했다. 그랬더라면 그녀가 물러섰을 테고, 그는 그 제안에서 온전한 이득을 보았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그러지 않았다. 그러기는커녕 그는 놀라움에 싸인 채 서서, 성긴 붉은 머리카락 사이로 손가락을 훑으며, 생기 도는 그녀의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보노라니 그 놀라운 아름다움이 보면 볼수록 더 놀랍게 자란다고 생각하고 있었다. “하! 하! 하!” 그녀가 소리 내어 웃었다. “자, 슬로프 씨, 세상을 희생한다는 말씀은 다시 꺼내지 마세요. 스물한 살 넘은 사람이라면 그런 것은 꿈에도 생각해서는 안 되지요. 당신과 저는, 우리 안에 사랑의 찌꺼기라도 남아 있다면, 우리 심장에 정념의 잔재라도 남아 있다면, 우리 밑천을 좀 더 알뜰히 아껴 써야 해요. 우리는 première jeunesse가 아니니까요. 세상은 아주 괜찮은 곳이에요. 적어도 당신의 세상은 그렇지요. 당신에게는 챙길 만한 온갖 기름진 교구가 있고 누릴 만한 주교 자리도 있을 수 있으니까요. 자, 실토해 보세요. 다시 생각해 보면 절름발이 부인의 미소를 얻자고 그런 것들을 희생하지는 않으시겠지요?”\n\n그로서는 여기에 대답하기가 불가능했다. 조금이라도 위엄을 지키려면 잠자코 있어야 한다고 그는 느꼈다.\n\n“자,” 그녀가 말했다. “저와 토라지지 마세요. 제가 뼈아픈 사실을 몇 마디 털어놓았다고 화내지 마시고요. 아, 제가 겪어 온 이 세상은 제게 쓰디쓴 진실을 가르쳐 주었답니다. 자, 저를 용서하셨다고 말씀해 주세요. 우리는 벗이 되어야 하지 않겠어요?” 그러고는 그녀가 그에게 다시 손을 내밀었다.\n\n그는 그녀 곁의 의자에 앉아 내밀어진 손을 잡고 그녀 위로 몸을 기울였다.\n\n“자,” 그녀가 더없이 달콤하고 부드러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. 사내라면 세 겹 강철을 두르고서야 견뎌 낼 만한 미소였다. “자, 여기에 당신의 용서를 새겨 두세요.” 그러면서 그녀는 그것을 그의 얼굴 쪽으로 들어 올렸다. 그는 몇 번이고 거듭 거기에 입을 맞추었고, 자기에게 내밀어진 손 너머로까지 제 용서의 자비를 넓히고 싶은 듯 그녀 위로 몸을 뻗었다. 그러나 그녀는 용케 그의 열기를 막아 냈다. 이 가엾은 전속 사제처럼 이토록 쉽게 홀리는 자에게라면 그녀의 손 하나로 충분하고도 남았다.\n\n“오, 마들린!” 그가 말했다. “저를 사랑한다고 말해 주세요 — 사랑하시나요, 사랑하시나요?”\n\n“쉿.” 그녀가 말했다. “어머니 발소리가 나요. 우리 tête-à-tête가 엄청나게 길어졌군요. 이제 가시는 편이 좋겠어요. 하지만 곧 다시 뵙는 거지요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 이튿날 다시 찾아오겠다고 약속했다.\n\n“그리고 슬로프 씨,” 그녀가 이어 말했다. “부디 제 쪽지에 답을 주세요. 그것을 손에 들고 계시면서도, 정말이지 이 두 시간 동안 읽어 주실 만큼 너그러우시지는 않더군요. 안식일 학교와 아이들에 관한 이야기예요. 제가 그 아이들을 여기 데려오고 싶어 얼마나 애가 타는지 아시잖아요. 저는 일부러 교리문답까지 익히고 있답니다. 다음 주에는 저를 위해 그 일을 성사시켜 주셔야 해요. 어쨌든 저는 그 아이들에게 제 영적인 목자와 스승에게 순종하도록 가르치겠어요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 안식일 학교라는 주제를 두고는 별말을 하지 않았고, 작별을 고한 다음 슬픈 마음과 어지러운 정신과 편치 않은 양심을 안고 집으로 향했다.\n\n# 제28장. 볼드 부인이 플럼스테드에서 그랜틀리 박사 내외의 대접을 받다\n\n독자도 기억하겠지만, 슬로프 씨는 볼드 부인의 집에 자신의 billet-doux를 맡기고 나오면서 그것이 그날 오후에 플럼스테드로 보내질 것이라는 말을 들었다. 실은 부주교와 하딩 씨가 사륜마차를 타고 함께 시내에 들어와 있었고, 집으로 돌아가는 길에 엘리너의 짐을 찾아 가기로 되어 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그들이 그 집에 들렀을 때, 하녀는 작고 정갈하게 꾸린 바구니 하나와 커다란 보따리 하나를 마부에게 건네면서 마차 창으로 슬로프 씨의 서한을 들이밀었다. 창가에 앉아 있던 부주교가 그것을 받아 들었고, 곧바로 자기 원수의 필적을 알아보았다.\n\n“이걸 누가 두고 갔느냐?” 부주교가 물었다.\n\n“슬로프 씨가 손수 가지고 오셨습니다, 부주교님.” 하녀가 말했다. “오늘 안으로 마님께서 꼭 받아 보셔야 한다고 몹시 신신당부를 하셨습니다.”\n\n그리하여 사륜마차는 떠났고, 편지는 부주교의 손에 남았다. 그는 독사가 가득 든 바구니라도 든 양 그것을 들여다보았다. 설령 그것을 읽고 그 안에서 음란하고 무신론적인 내용을 발견했다 한들, 그 문서를 이보다 더 나쁘게 여기지는 못했을 것이다. 게다가 그는 비슷한 처지에서 그토록 많은 현명한 사람들이 으레 하는 일을 그대로 했다. 곧 그 편지를 받는 사람이 반드시 particeps criminis이기라도 한 듯, 그 사람부터 즉시 단죄한 것이다.\n\n가엾은 하딩 씨는 슬로프 씨가 제 딸과 가까워지는 것을 거들 마음이 조금도 없었지만, 그 편지를 사위의 눈에 띄지 않게 할 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그것은 불가능했다. 편지는 저기 사위의 손에 들려 있었고, 사위는 그 안에 총애받는 연인의 온갖 황홀한 넋두리가 적혀 있음을 확신하기라도 한 듯 진저리 난 얼굴을 하고 있었다.\n\n“이런 일이 제 지붕 아래에서 벌어지다니, 저로서는 여간 가혹한 일이 아닙니다.” 잠시 후 부주교가 말했다.\n\n여기서 부주교는 분명 지극히 부당했다. 처제를 제 집으로 초대한 이상, 그녀가 그곳에서 제 편지를 받는 것은 자연스러운 결과였다. 그리고 슬로프 씨가 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 했다면, 그 편지는 당연히 그녀를 따라 부쳐질 터였다. 게다가 누군가를 제 집에 초대한다는 사실 자체가 초대하는 쪽의 신뢰를 뜻한다. 그는 볼드 부인에게 머물러 달라고 청함으로써 그녀를 제 집에 둘 만한 사람으로 여긴다는 것을 이미 보여 준 셈이었다. 그러니 저질러진 태만이 그녀의 소행이 전혀 아닌데도 그녀가 제 집안의 신성함을 더럽혔다고 탓하는 것은 더없이 잔인한 일이었다.\n\n하딩 씨도 그것을 느꼈고, 또한 부주교가 제 지붕을 두고 저렇게 말할 때 그 말이 엘리너의 아버지인 자신에게 더없이 모욕적이라는 것도 느꼈다. 엘리너가 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다 한들, 그것이 그랜틀리 박사네 집안의 순결을 더럽힐 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가? 그는 제 딸이 그렇게 재단되고 그렇게 입에 오르내리는 것이 분했고, 설령 딸이 슬로프 부인이 된다 하더라도 하느님의 땅 위 어떤 피조물보다 자기에게 더 소중한 사람일 것이라고 마음을 정했다. 그는 하마터면 터뜨리듯 그렇게 말할 뻔했으나, 그 순간만은 스스로를 억눌렀다.\n\n“자, 여기 있습니다.” 부주교가 그 불쾌한 물건을 장인에게 건네며 말했다. “저는 저 사람의 연애편지를 나르는 심부름꾼 노릇은 하지 않겠습니다. 아버님이 그 애의 아버지시니 좋으실 대로 하십시오.”\n\n좋을 대로 하라는 말로 부주교가 뜻한 바는, 하딩 씨가 그 편지를 뜯어 읽고 그에 따라 필요한 조치를 취하더라도 정당하리라는 것이었음이 분명하다. 사실을 말하자면, 그랜틀리 박사는 그 편지의 내용을 보고 싶은 호기심이 제 상처 입은 덕성이 허락하는 정도보다는 조금 더 강했다. 물론 자기 손으로 봉을 뜯을 수는 없었으나, 엘리너의 아버지인 하딩 씨라면 그리해도 아무 흠이 없다는 것을 그에게 납득시키고 싶었던 것이다. 하딩 씨에게는 그런 짓을 한다는 생각 자체가 떠오르지 않았다. 엘리너에 대한 그의 권한은 그 아이가 존 볼드의 아내가 된 순간에 끝났다. 그는 딸의 편지를 엿볼 마음이 티끌만큼도 없었다. 그리하여 그는 편지를 제 주머니에 넣었고, 다만 부주교가 모르는 사이에 그렇게 할 수 있었더라면 좋았겠다고 생각할 뿐이었다. 두 사람은 집으로 돌아가는 길의 절반 동안 말없이 앉아 있었고, 이윽고 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “수전이 그 애에게 건네는 편이 낫겠습니다. 그런 사귐이 얼마나 수치스러운 일인지는 아버님이나 저보다 그 사람이 제 동생에게 더 잘 설명할 수 있을 테니까요.”\n\n“자네는 엘리너에게 너무 가혹하네.” 하딩 씨가 대답했다. “나는 그 애가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 인정하지 않겠고, 앞으로 그럴 성싶지도 않네. 그 애에게는 제가 좋아하는 사람과 편지를 주고받을 권리가 있고, 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 이유로 내가 나서서 그 애를 나무라지는 않겠네.”\n\n“짐작건대,” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “아버님도 그 사람과 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시겠지요. 그렇게 된다면 처제가 스스로를 욕되게 하는 것이라는 점만은 인정하시겠지요.”\n\n“나는 그 애가 그 사람과 혼인하기를 바라지 않네.” 난처해진 아버지가 말했다. “나는 그 사람이 마음에 들지 않고, 좋은 남편이 되리라고 생각지도 않네. 그러나 엘리너가 그리하기로 한다면, 나는 결코 그 애가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 여기지는 않을 걸세.”\n\n“맙소사!” 그랜틀리 박사는 이렇게 외치고 제 사륜마차 구석에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다. 하딩 씨는 더 말이 없었고, 다만 마차 안에 자리가 넉넉지 않아 보이는데도 있지도 않은 첼로 위에 있지도 않은 활을 올려놓고 만가를 켜기 시작했다. 그는 이런저런 변주를 섞어 가며 목사관 문 앞에 이를 때까지 그 곡을 이어 갔다.\n\n부주교는 마음속으로 서글픈 것들을 곱씹고 있었다. 지금까지 그는 늘 장인을 진정한 같은 편으로 여겨 왔다. 그 노릇에 필요한 싸움의 기질이라고는 하나도 갖추지 못한 사람이라는 것은 알고 있었지만 말이다. 전임 워든이 적진을 무너뜨리는 무용을 보여 주리라고 크게 기대한 적은 없었으되, 하딩 씨가 적에게 넘어가리라는 두려움만은 품어 본 적이 없었다. 그런데 이제 보니 엘리너가 온갖 꾀로 제 아버지를 완전히 홀려 어지럽히고, 판단력을 빼앗고, 평생 지녀 온 취향과 애착을 앗아 가고, 몇 해 전이었다면 도저히 참아 내지 못했을 오만하고 천박한 사내를 너그럽게 보아 넘기게 만들어 놓은 것이었다. 엘리너와 슬로프 씨 사이에 모든 것이 이미 정해진 것이나 다름없다는 데는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었다. 하딩 씨가 사정이 그러함을 알고 있다는 것 또한 좀처럼 의심하기 어려웠다. 적어도 그가 그것을 짐작하면서도 묵인할 채비가 되어 있다는 것만은 너무나 뚜렷했다.\n\n그리고 사실을 말하자면, 정말 그러했다. 하딩 씨는 사람을 싫어할 수 있는 제 천성의 한계까지 슬로프 씨를 싫어했다. 딸이 두 번째 혼인으로 아버지를 최대한 언짢게 만들고자 했다면, 슬로프 씨를 택하는 것보다 더 잘해 낼 방법은 좀처럼 없었을 것이다. 그러나 요즘 그가 자주 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이, 딸이 정말로 그릇된 일을 하나도 하지 않았다면 자기에게 무슨 권리가 있어 딸을 단죄하겠는가? 딸이 슬로프 씨를 좋아한다면 그것은 딸의 일이었다. 그런 정신을 지녔고 그토록 교양 있고 그토록 세련되고 취향이 그토록 까다로운 여자가 그런 사내를 좋아한다는 것은 그에게 참으로 불가사의한 일이기는 했다. 그러다 그는 딸이 정말 그럴 수 있는 일인지 스스로에게 물었다.\n\n아, 그대 나약한 사람이여. 더없이 자애롭고 더없이 그리스도인답되 사내들 가운데 가장 약한 이여! 어찌하여 그대는 딸에게 직접 묻지 못하였는가? 그 아이는 그대의 몸에서 난 딸이요 그대 마음의 자식이며, 온 인류 가운데 그대가 가장 사랑하는 이가 아니던가? 그 아이는 여러 해에 걸친 더없이 가까운 정으로 제 진실함과 착함과 딸로서의 순종을 그대에게 증명하지 않았던가? 그런데도 이 모든 것을 알고 느끼면서, 그대는 어둠 속을 더듬어 다니며 사랑에 겨운 그대의 가슴을 찌르는 말투로 그 아이의 이름이 오르내리는 것을 듣고도 마땅히 그리했어야 할 만큼 그 아이를 지켜 주지 못한 채 견디고 있단 말인가!\n\n하딩 씨는 제 딸이 그 사내와 혼인할 뜻이 있다고 믿지 않았고 지금도 믿지 않았으나, 그런 견해를 입 밖에 내어 스스로를 묶는 것이 두려웠다. 만일 딸이 정말 그렇게 한다면 그때는 물러설 길이 없을 터였다. 그의 마음이 바라는 바는 이러했다. 첫째로 부주교의 억측에 아무런 진실도 없기를, 그리고 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면 그는 이 바람에 온전히 기대고 싶었다. 둘째로 불행히도 엘리너가 그런 혼인을 마음에 두었다면 그것이 막아지기를. 셋째로 딸이 그 사내와 혼인할 만큼 홀려 버린다 하더라도, 자기가 제 처신을 떳떳이 밝히고 딸과 갈라설 까닭이 조금도 없다고 말할 수 있기를.\n\n그는 딸이 그런 혼인을 할 수 있는 사람이 아니라고 믿고 싶었다. 자기가 딸을 그렇게 믿고 있음을 보이고도 싶었다. 그러나 불행히도 딸이 자기가 생각하던 사람과 다른 것으로 드러난다 하더라도, 훗날 그 아이는 잘못한 것이 하나도 없다고 말할 수 있기를 또한 바랐다.\n\n그런 변덕을 정당화할 수 있는 것은 애정뿐이었으나, 애정은 실제로 그것을 정당화했다. 하딩 씨에게는 로마인다운 구석이 거의 없었다. 그는 제 루크레티아가 주교관에 있는 저 성직자 타르퀴니우스의 구애를 받아들여 더럽혀진다 하더라도 그 아이를 제물로 바칠 수 없었다. 타르퀴니우스를 막을 수 있다면 더없이 좋은 일이겠으나, 막지 못한다면 아버지는 그래도 딸에게 제 가슴을 열고 그 아이가 나타나는 그대로, 타르퀴니우스까지 함께 받아들일 터였다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사의 정신은 더 단단한 재질이었고, 그렇다고 마음이 모자란 사람도 결코 아니었다. 그는 아내와 자식들과 벗들을 정직하고 진실한 사랑으로 사랑했다. 장인도 사랑했고, 엘리너 또한 얼마든지 사랑할 채비가 되어 있었다. 다만 그 아이가 제 편의 한 사람이 되어 준다면, 제 쪽에 서 준다면, 슬로프 무리와 프라우디 무리를 인류의 적으로 여기고 그윈 씨네와 애러빈 씨네의 든든한 미덕을 인정하고 느껴 준다면 말이다. 그는 제 집과 제 마음의 지성소에 들인 모든 이들과 이른바 “안전한” 사이이기를 바랐다. 그에게 프리메이슨의 결속이나 다름없었던 저 충실하고 흔들림 없는 고교회파의 정서가 모자란 자리에서는 조금도 흥겨울 수 없었다. 그는 경계를 긋는 데 그리 엄격하지는 않았다. 영국 국교회 보수주의의 여러 가지 색조를 짜증 없이 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 슬로프 무리, 프라우디 무리와는 도저히 네 발을 맞추어 걸을 수가 없었다.\n\n게다가 그에게는 하딩 씨 특유의 저 여성적인 부드러움이 없었다. 아니, 그런 부드러움 때문에 시달리는 일이 없었다고 하는 편이 더 정확할 것이다. 벗들을 향한 그의 마음은 이러했다. 그들이 자기에게 붙어 있는 한 자기도 그들에게 붙어 있겠다는 것, 그들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 일하겠다는 것, 충실한 자에게 충실하겠다는 것. 그는 거짓된 벗에게도 참될 수 있는 저 아름다운 사랑을 알지 못했다.\n\n그리하여 저마다의 방식으로 충분히 비참해진 두 사람은 플럼스테드로 돌아왔다.\n\n그들이 도착했을 때는 이미 늦은 시각이어서 부인들은 벌써 옷을 갈아입으러 올라간 뒤였다. 두 사람은 현관에서 헤어지면서 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 하딩 씨는 제 방으로 가는 길에 엘리너의 방문을 두드려 편지를 들이밀었다. 부주교는 제 영역으로 서둘러 가서 충실한 반려에게 마음을 털어놓았다.\n\n부부의 침실과 그에 딸린 옷방 사이에서 어떤 이야기가 오갔는지는 여기 적지 않겠다. 이제 관련된 사람들과 친해진 독자라면 넉넉히 그려 볼 수 있을 것이다. 그 대화의 전체 기조 또한 만찬에 내려오는 그랜틀리 부인의 이마에서 읽어 낼 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n엘리너는 아버지의 손에서 편지를 받았을 때 그것이 누구에게서 온 것인지 짐작조차 하지 못했다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨의 필적을 본 적이 없었고, 보았다 하더라도 잊어버린 터라, 사람들이 필적으로도 봉인으로도 상대를 얼른 알아보지 못할 때 흔히 그러듯 편지를 이리저리 돌려 보면서도 그를 떠올리지 않았다. 그녀는 거울 앞에 앉아 머리를 빗다가, 침대 위에 팔다리를 벌리고 누워 어미뿐 아니라 하녀의 주의까지 거의 다 차지하고 있는 아들과 놀아 주려고 잠깐씩 일어서곤 했다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 봉을 뜯었고, 장을 넘기다 슬로프 씨의 이름을 보았다. 처음에는 놀랐고, 다음에는 언짢았고, 그다음에는 마음이 조마조마했다. 읽어 가는 동안 그녀는 관심이 쏠렸다. 아버지가 요양원으로 돌아가는 데 걸림돌이 되던 것들이 모두 치워진 듯하다는 사실이 어찌나 반가웠던지, 그 소식을 전하는 낯간지러운 말투는 눈에 들어오지도 않았다. 그녀는 그저 사정이 그러하다는 것을 아버지에게 알리는 일을 자기가 맡았다고만 알아들었을 뿐, 그런 통지가 애초에 혼인하지 않은 젊은 성직자에게서 자기에게 먼저 와서는 안 되는 것이라는 사실은 깨닫지 못했다. 전체적으로 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 고마움을 느꼈고, 어서 옷을 입고 아버지에게 그 소식을 들고 달려가고 싶었다. 그러다 자신의 경건한 수고를 들먹인 대목에 이르자, 그녀는 슬로프 씨가 잘난 체하는 얼간이라고 속으로 말했다. 다시 읽어 나가다가 제 아들이 슬로프 씨의 귀염둥이라 불린 데서 기분이 상했다. 그 아이는 오직 제 어미의 귀염둥이일 뿐, 적어도 슬로프 씨같이 달갑지 않은 낯선 이의 귀염둥이는 아니었다. 마지막으로 그녀는 머릿단 이야기에 이르러 역겨움이 치미는 것을 느꼈다. 고개를 들어 거울을 보니 그것들이 눈앞에 있었다. 길고 비단결 같고 참으로 아름다웠다. 그녀가 그것을 몰랐다고 말하지는 않겠으나, 어쨌든 그녀는 그 머리카락에 화가 나서 거칠고 아무렇게나 빗어 내렸다. 그녀는 성난 손길로 편지를 구겨 버렸고, 거의 생각할 겨를도 없이 그것을 아버지에게 보이지 않기로 마음먹었다. 내용만 말씀드리면 되리라. 그러고는 다시 아들을 안고 마음을 달랜 뒤, 옷을 여미게 하고 만찬에 내려갔다.\n\n층계를 사뿐히 내려가면서 그녀는 제 처지에 얼마간 곤란한 데가 있음을 깨닫기 시작했다. 요양원 소식을 아버지에게 감출 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 그랜틀리 내외 앞에서 슬로프 씨의 편지를 마음 편히 고백할 수도 없었다. 아버지는 이미 내려간 뒤였다. 복도에서 그 발소리를 들었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 아버지를 따로 모셔다 그 자그마한 소식을 전하기로 마음먹었다. 가엾은 사람! 그녀는 그 불행한 편지가 이미 얼마나 매섭게 도마에 올랐는지 알지 못했다.\n\n그녀가 응접실에 들어섰을 때 애러빈 씨를 포함해 온 식구가 다 모여 있었고, 다들 시무룩하고 뾰로통한 얼굴이었다. 두 소녀는 무언가 잘못되었음을 아는 듯이 말없이 떨어져 앉아 있었다. 애러빈 씨마저 엄숙하고 말이 없었다. 엘리너는 아침 식사 이후로 그를 보지 못했다. 그는 온종일 세인트 이월드에 가 있었으니, 그렇다면 그곳 일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 이야기하는 것이 자연스러웠을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그런 이야기라고는 하지 않고 여전히 엄숙하게 입을 다물고 있었다. 다들 엄숙했고 말이 없었다. 엘리너는 그들이 제 이야기를 하고 있었음을 마음속으로 알았고, 슬로프 씨와 그 편지를 떠올리자 가슴이 켕겼다. 어쨌든 사정이 이러한데 아버지에게 단둘이 말을 건네기란 도저히 불가능하겠다고 그녀는 느꼈다.\n\n곧 만찬이 알려졌고, 그랜틀리 박사는 늘 하던 대로 엘리너에게 팔을 내주었다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도 그것이 지극한 필요에 떠밀려 어쩔 수 없이 제 감정을 짓밟는 일이라는 투였다. 엘리너는 예민한 마음으로 그것을 느끼고 손가락을 그의 소매에 겨우 얹는 둥 마는 둥 했다. 만찬 시간이 어떻게 흘러갔을지는 짐작이 갈 것이다. 그랜틀리 박사가 애러빈 씨에게 몇 마디 했고, 애러빈 씨가 그랜틀리 부인에게 몇 마디 했고, 그녀가 제 아버지에게 몇 마디 했고, 아버지는 엘리너에게 몇 마디 해 보려 애썼다. 그녀는 자기가 무언가로 재판을 받고 유죄가 되었다고 느꼈으나, 그것이 무엇인지는 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그들 모두에게 이렇게 외치고 싶었다. “자, 제가 무엇을 했다는 겁니까. 어서 말씀들 하시고 제 죄가 무엇인지 알려 주세요. 제발 가장 나쁜 것까지 다 듣게 해 주세요.” 그러나 그럴 수가 없었다. 그녀는 아무 말도 못 한 채 그저 말없이 앉아, 반쯤은 자기가 정말 죄인인 듯한 기분으로, 저녁을 먹는 시늉이라도 해 보려고 헛되이 애썼다.\n\n마침내 식탁보가 걷혔고, 부인들도 오래지 않아 자리를 떴다. 그들이 나가자 신사들은 조금 더 어울리는 편이 되었으나 그리 대단치는 않았다. 물론 그들이 엘리너의 죄를 놓고 이야기할 수는 없었다. 사실 부주교는 만찬 전에 서재에서 마주쳤을 때 자기가 두려워하는 바를 애러빈 씨의 귀에 속삭임으로써 그만큼 처제를 팔아넘긴 터였다. 그는 더없이 무겁고 더없이 서글픈 두려움을 담아 그렇게 했고, 그것을 들은 애러빈 씨의 낯빛도 그만큼 무겁고 서글퍼 보였다. 그는 눈을 크게 뜨고 입을 벌린 채 속삭이듯 “슬로프 씨라니!” 하고 말했다. 벗이 그 끔찍한 병이 제 아이 방에 들어왔다고 알려 주었다면 “콜레라라니!” 하고 말했을 그 투 그대로였다. “그런 것 같소, 그런 것 같소.” 부주교가 말했고, 두 사람은 함께 방을 나섰다.\n\n그토록 놀라운 소식을 들은 애러빈 씨의 심경을 우리가 정확히 헤아려 보지는 않겠다. 그가 놀랐고 언짢았고 서글펐고 마음이 편치 않았다고만 해 두면 충분하다. 그는 아마 엘리너에 대해 그리 많이 생각해 본 적은 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 미치는 영향을 소중히 여겼고, 시골집에서 그녀와 가깝게 지내는 것이 즐겁고 또 이롭기까지 하다고 느껴 왔다. 그는 부주교에게 그녀의 총명함을 높이 말했고, 그녀의 아들을 등에 업은 채 관목 숲길을 그녀와 함께 거닐기도 했다. 애러빈 씨가 조니를 제 귀염둥이라고 불렀을 때 엘리너는 화를 내지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 세 남자는 포도주를 앞에 두고 앉아 저마다 같은 일을 생각하면서도 서로 그것을 입에 올리지 못했다. 그러니 우리는 그들을 두고 부인들을 따라 응접실로 가 보기로 하자.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 남편에게서 임무를 받았고, 다소 내키지 않는 마음으로 그것을 떠맡았다. 남편은 그녀에게 엘리너와 진지하게 이야기하여, 슬로프 씨를 계속 가까이한다면 지금의 벗들에게서 더는 호의를 기대할 수 없으리라고 일러 주라 했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 아마 박사보다 제 동생을 더 잘 알았기에, 그 아이에게 말해 보아야 소용없으리라고 남편에게 장담했다. 그녀의 생각으로 조금이라도 도움이 될 만한 길은 엘리너를 바체스터에서 떼어 놓는 것뿐이었다. 그런 일에는 눈이 몹시 밝은 사람이었으니, 어쩌면 엘리너를 애러빈 씨 가까이에 두는 데도 기대를 걸 만한 구석이 있다고 덧붙일 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그 이야기는 입에 담지 않았다. 부주교는 설득에 넘어가지 않았다. 그는 제 양심을 길게 말했고, 그랜틀리 부인이 하지 않겠다면 자기가 하겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그렇게 떠밀린 부인은, 자기가 나서 봐야 소용없기는커녕 더 나빠지리라는 확신을 다 밝혀 두고서 그 일을 떠맡았다. 그리고 과연 그러했다.\n\n응접실에 들어서자마자 그랜틀리 부인은 딸들을 내보낼 구실을 찾아냈고, 그런 다음 제 임무에 착수했다. 그녀는 제가 동생에게 행사할 수 있는 권위가 아주 미미하다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 서로 사는 방식이 다르고 사는 곳이 멀리 떨어져 있어 그리 깊은 속내를 나눌 사이가 되지 못했던 것이다. 엘리너가 어린아이였던 시절 이후로 둘은 함께 산 적이 거의 없었다. 게다가 엘리너는 특히 근래에 들어, 부주교가 제 아버지에게 휘두르는 듯한 그 훈계조의 권위를 조용한 방식으로 못마땅해했고, 그런 까닭에 부주교의 아내가 제게 권위를 부리는 것도 받아들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n“만찬 직전에 쪽지를 한 통 받았다지.” 맏언니가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n엘리너는 그렇다고 인정했고, 인정하면서 제 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다. 낯빛을 지킬 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으련만, 그러려고 애쓸수록 보란 듯이 실패했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨한테서 온 것 아니었니?”\n\n엘리너는 그 편지가 슬로프 씨에게서 온 것이라고 말했다.\n\n“그 사람과 늘 편지를 주고받는 사이니, 엘리너?”\n\n“꼭 그렇지는 않아요.” 그녀가 말했다. 심문을 당하는 데 벌써 화가 나기 시작한 참이었다. 그 편지의 내용이 무엇인지는 무슨 일이 있어도 언니 수전에게 말하지 않겠다고 그녀는 마음을 정했는데, 왜 그랬는지는 말하기 어려울 것이다. 그랜틀리 부인이 부주교에게 떠밀려 나섰다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었고, 그가 자기에게 씌운 어떤 혐의에도 답변하지 않을 작정이었다.\n\n“하지만 엘리너, 그 사람이 아버지께도, 부주교께도, 아니 네 벗들 모두에게 그토록 거슬리는 사람인 줄 뻔히 알면서 어째서 그 사람한테서 편지를 받는 거니?”\n\n“우선 언니, 저는 그 사람한테서 편지를 받지 않아요. 그리고 다음으로, 제가 받은 그 한 통의 편지는 슬로프 씨가 쓴 것이고 저는 그저 받았을 뿐인데, 아버지가 손수 건네주시는 것을 제가 어떻게 받지 않을 수 있었겠어요. 그러니 저 말고 슬로프 씨에게 물어보시는 편이 나을 것 같은데요.”\n\n“그 사람 편지가 무슨 이야기였니, 엘리너?”\n\n“말씀드릴 수 없어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “비밀로 하기로 한 것이니까요. 제삼자에 관한 용무였어요.”\n\n“그럼 너 자신에 관한 이야기는 조금도 없었다는 거니?”\n\n“꼭 그렇다고는 하지 않겠어요, 언니.” 언니의 질문에 점점 더 화가 치밀어 오르며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 나로서는 좀 이상하다고 말할 수밖에 없구나.” 그랜틀리 부인이 웃는 시늉을 하며 말했다. “너 같은 처지의 젊은 부인이 혼인하지 않은 신사에게서 편지를 받고는, 그 내용을 말하려 하지도 않고 제 언니에게 보이기를 부끄러워하다니 말이다.”\n\n“부끄러워하는 게 아니에요.” 엘리너가 발끈해서 말했다. “이 일에 부끄러울 것이라고는 하나도 없어요. 다만 제 편지를 두고 누구에게든 심문을 받고 싶지 않을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“글쎄다.” 언니가 말했다. “그래도 나는 슬로프 씨가 너와 편지를 주고받기에 마땅한 사람이라고는 생각하지 않는다고 말해 두어야겠구나.”\n\n“그 사람이 아무리 마땅치 않다 한들, 그 사람이 제게 편지를 쓴 것을 제가 어떻게 막겠어요? 하지만 다들 그 사람에게 얼마나 편견을 품고 있는지, 다른 사람이 하면 친절하고 너그러운 일이 그 사람이 하면 밉살스럽고 뻔뻔한 일이 되어 버리잖아요. 저는 사람더러 자비를 그렇게 한쪽으로만 베풀라고 가르치는 종교라면 질색이에요.”\n\n“엘리너, 네가 이 집에서 만나는 종교를 질색한다니 유감이구나. 하지만 그런 일에서는 부주교께서 너보다 세상을 더 많이 아신다는 것쯤은 기억해야지. 나야 안됐지만 네 손위이기는 해도, 나를 존중하라거나 내 말을 따르라고는 하지 않으마. 그래도 이런 일에서만은 부주교의 인도를 받겠다고 해 줄 수 있지 않겠니. 그분은 네가 허락만 한다면 네 벗이 되어 주시려고 무척 애쓰고 계신단다.”\n\n“이런 일이라니 어떤 일 말인가요?” 엘리너가 몹시 까칠하게 말했다. “정말이지 이게 다 무슨 이야기인지 저는 모르겠어요.”\n\n“우리 모두는 네가 슬로프 씨를 끊었으면 한다.”\n\n“다들 저더러 언니들처럼 옹졸해지라는 거잖아요. 저는 결코 그렇게 되지 않을 거예요. 슬로프 씨와 아는 사이인 것에 아무 해로울 것이 없다고 저는 봐요. 해롭다고 그 사람에게 말해서 모욕을 줄 생각도 없고요. 그 사람은 제게 편지를 써야겠다고 여겼고, 저는 그 편지에 대해 부주교님의 조언을 바라지 않아요. 바랐다면 제가 여쭈었겠지요.”\n\n“그렇다면 엘리너, 이건 내가 네게 말해 두어야 할 일이구나.” 이제 그녀는 무시무시하게 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. “부주교께서는 그런 편지 왕래가 수치스러운 일이며, 그것이 당신 집 안에서 이어지도록 두고 볼 수는 없다고 여기신단다.”\n\n엘리너는 눈에서 불꽃을 튀기며 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 언니에게 대답했다. “부주교님께 이렇게 전해 주세요. 저는 어디에 있든 제가 원하는 편지를, 제가 원하는 사람에게서 받겠다고요. 그리고 ‘수치스럽다’는 말로 말하자면, 그랜틀리 박사가 저를 두고 그런 말을 쓰셨다면 그것은 사내답지 못하고 손님을 대접할 줄 모르는 처사예요.” 그러고는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. “아버지가 식당에서 나오시거든 제 침실로 올라와 주십사고 여쭈어 주시면 고맙겠어요. 아버지께는 슬로프 씨의 편지를 보여 드리겠어요. 하지만 다른 누구에게도 보여 드리지 않겠어요.” 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 제 아기에게로 물러갔다.\n\n그녀는 자기에게 씌워진 죄가 무엇인지 짐작조차 하지 못했다. 벗들이 자기가 슬로프 씨를 연인으로 여긴다고 생각할 수도 있으리라는 발상은 머리를 스친 적조차 없었다. 그녀는 그들이 편견에 사로잡혀 옹졸하게 그 사람을 몰아세우고 있다고 여겼으며, 그래서 자기가 그 사람을 몹시 싫어하면서도 그 몰이에 끼어들지 않으려 했던 것이다.\n\n엘리너는 아이의 침대 발치, 열린 창가의 낮은 의자에 앉으면서 몹시 화가 나 있었다. “내가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 감히 말하다니.” 그녀는 몇 번이나 혼잣말로 되뇌었다. “아버지는 저 사람의 오만을 어떻게 견디시는지! 그 말에 대해 용서를 빌기 전에는 다시는 저 사람 집 식탁에 앉지 않겠어.” 그러다 문득 애러빈 씨가 자기와 슬로프 씨의 그 “수치스러운” 편지 왕래에 대해 전해 들었을지도 모른다는 생각이 스치자, 그녀는 순전한 분함으로 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. 아, 그녀가 진실을 알았더라면! 애러빈 씨가 그녀와 슬로프 씨의 혼인을 이미 기정사실로 전해 들었다는 것을 짐작할 수 있었더라면!\n\n그녀가 제 방에 들어온 지 얼마 되지 않아 아버지가 왔다. 그랜틀리 부인은 응접실을 나서면서 남편을 창가의 후미진 자리로 데려가 자기가 얼마나 보란 듯이 실패했는지를 일러 주었다.\n\n“잠자리에 들기 전에 내가 직접 그 애에게 말하겠소.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“제발 그러지 마세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “좋을 것도 없고 집 안에 보기 흉한 다툼만 일으킬 거예요. 그 애가 얼마나 고집이 센지 당신은 모르세요.”\n\n부주교는 그런 것쯤은 조금도 상관없다고 잘라 말했다. 자기는 제 의무를 알고 있고 그것을 행하겠노라고 했다. 하딩 씨는 그런 일에 지나치게 물렀다. 그토록 수치스러운 혼인을 막기 위해 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하지 않았다는 것을 훗날 양심에 얹어 두고 싶지 않았다. 엘리너에게 성난 목소리로 말하는 것은 그런 위기를 앞당길 뿐이며, 지금 조금이라도 의심스러운 구석이 있다면 오히려 그것을 확실하게 만들 뿐이라고 그랜틀리 부인이 아무리 일러도 소용없었다. 그는 화가 나 있었고, 고집스러웠고, 속이 쓰렸다. 제 집안의 부인이 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 사실이 그의 자존심을 가장 아픈 자리에서 찔러 놓았으니, 무엇으로도 그를 다스릴 수 없었다.\n\n하딩 씨는 딸의 방에 들어설 때 지치고 수심에 잠긴 얼굴이었다. 이런 근심들이 그를 슬프도록 괴롭혔다. 이런 일이 계속된다면 전속 사제가 그토록 친절하게 예언해 준 대로 자기가 벽으로 밀려나고 말리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 그는 딸의 방문을 조용히 두드리고, 들어오라는 말이 또렷이 들릴 때까지 기다렸다가, 딸이 아니라 제가 혐의를 받는 죄인이기라도 한 듯한 모습으로 나타났다.\n\n엘리너의 팔은 이내 아버지의 팔에 감겼고, 그녀는 곧 아버지의 이마에 입을 맞추며 기쁨이 아니라 간절함이 담긴 사랑으로 그를 어루만졌다. “아버지,” 그녀가 말했다. “저 아버지께 꼭 드릴 말씀이 있어요. 오늘 밤 아래층에서 다들 제 이야기를 했지요. 그러셨죠, 아버지?”\n\n하딩 씨는 부주교가 그녀 이야기를 하기는 했다고 웅얼거리듯 털어놓았다.\n\n“저는 곧 그랜틀리 박사를 미워하게 될 거예요―”\n\n“오, 얘야!”\n\n“정말 그럴 거예요. 어쩔 수가 없어요. 그분은 어찌나 각박하고 어찌나 매정하고, 자기를 떠받들지 않는 사람은 누구든 어찌나 의심하시는지요. 게다가 그분이 제 의견을 가질 권리가 있듯 남들도 제 의견을 가질 권리가 있는데, 남들에게 어찌나 무섭도록 오만하신지요.”\n\n“그분은 진지하고 열심인 사람이란다, 얘야. 하지만 매정하게 굴 뜻은 결코 없는 사람이야.”\n\n“매정하세요, 아버지. 더없이 매정하세요. 자, 만찬 전에 슬로프 씨에게서 받은 편지가 이것이에요. 아버지가 손수 제게 주셨잖아요. 자, 어서 읽어 보세요. 온통 아버지에 관한 거예요. 애초에 아버지 앞으로 왔어야 할 편지고요. 아래층에서 다들 이 편지를 두고 어떤 이야기를 했는지 아시잖아요. 만찬 자리에서 저를 어떻게 대했는지도 아시잖아요. 게다가 만찬이 끝나고 수전 언니는 제가 그 방에 더 앉아 있을 수 없을 만큼 저를 붙들고 설교를 했어요. 읽어 보세요, 아버지. 그러고 나서 이게 그랜틀리 박사를 그토록 노발대발하게 만들어야 할 편지인지 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 딸의 허리에서 팔을 풀고 천천히 편지를 읽었다. 그녀는 아버지가 요양원으로 돌아가는 길이 이토록 평탄해졌음을 알고 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환해지리라 기대했다. 그러나 전에도 얼마쯤 비슷한 일에서 한 번 그랬듯이, 그녀는 실망할 운명이었다. 아버지가 처음 느낀 것은 슬로프 씨가 감히 제 일에 끼어들기로 했다는 데 대한 걷잡을 수 없는 역겨움이었다. 그는 요양원으로 돌아가기를 간절히 바라 왔으나, 그 자리를 슬로프 씨의 두둔에 조금이라도 빚지느니 그 자리에 대한 권리를 몽땅 내놓는 편이 한없이 나았다. 다음으로 그는 슬로프 씨의 편지가 풍기는 어조가 몹시 싫었다. 그것은 그 사람과 똑같이 느끼하고 거짓되고 병들어 있었다. 그는 엘리너가 보지 못한 것을 보았으니, 곧 적힌 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것이 뜻으로 담겨 있다는 사실이었다. 엘리너의 경건한 수고를 제 것과 따로 떼어 들먹인 대목은 아비 된 그의 감정을 슬프도록 긁었다. 그리고 “귀염둥이 아들”과 “비단 같은 머릿단”에 이르자, 그는 절망 속에서 천천히 편지를 닫아 접었다. 부추김을 받지 않았다면 슬로프 씨가 그렇게 쓸 리가 없었다. 엘리너가 그를 부추길 마음이 없었다면 그런 편지를 받고도 언짢아하지 않을 리가 없었다. 적어도 하딩 씨는 스스로에게 그렇게 따져 나갔다.\n\n남의 감정을 정확히 판단하기란 얼마나 어려운 일인가. 하딩 씨는 편지 끝에 이르면서 마음속으로 제 딸이 조심성 없다고 단죄했고, 그렇게 하는 것이 그를 비참하게 만들었다. 슬로프 씨가 무엇을 쓰든 그것은 딸의 책임이 아니다. 그렇다. 그러나 딸은 그것에 아무런 역겨움도 드러내지 않았다. 오히려 그 편지를 전체로 보아 괜찮다고 여기는 듯한 기색이었다. 딸은 자신을 위해서, 또 아버지를 위해서 그것을 변호의 근거로 삼아 읽어 보라고 건넸다. 아버지는 딸에게 무죄를 선고할 수 없다고 느끼면서 마음이 가라앉았다.\n\n그런데 이런 단죄를 그녀에게 불러온 것은 다름 아닌 엘리너의 참으로 여성다운 섬세함이었다. 부인들이여, 내 말을 들어 보시고 부디 그녀에게 무죄를 선고해 주시기를 청한다. 그녀는 자기가 전혀 알아채지 못한 이 사내, 이 연인을 두고 제 아버지가 생각한 것과 똑같이, 그랜틀리 내외가 생각한 것과 똑같이 생각했다. 적어도 사람됨에 대한 평가만은 그들과 같았다. 다만 그녀는 그가 대체로 정직한 사람이며 제 아버지를 진심으로 돕고자 하는 사람이라고 믿었다. 그녀는 그런 일이 있었던 이상 이 편지를 하딩 씨에게 보여야 할 의무가 있다고 느꼈다. 슬로프 씨가 무슨 말을 하는지 아버지가 아셔야 한다고 여겼다. 그러나 그 사내의 어조가 지닌 천박함에 대해서는 굳이 변명하거나 나무라거나 언급할 필요가 있다고 생각지 않았다. 모든 천박함이 그렇듯 그것 또한 무지에서 나온 것이었다. 슬로프 씨 같은 사내가 제 용모를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은 그녀에게 구역질 나는 일이었으나, 구역질 나는 것을 아버지 앞에서 길게 늘어놓을 필요가 있다고는 생각지 않았다. 그런 문제에서 두 사람의 생각이 갈릴 수 있으리라고는 상상해 본 적도 없었다. 그것을 짚어 보이는 일은 그녀에게 괴로웠을 것이고, 대체로 좋게 생각하고 좋게 말하고 싶은 사람을 두고 모질게 말하는 일도 괴로웠을 것이다. 그런 사내와 마주친 것은 길을 걷다 마주칠 법한 불쾌한 일과 다르지 않았다. 그리고 그런 마주침에서 자기를 역겹게 한 것을 굳이 곱씹을 필요가 있다고 그녀는 한 번도 생각한 적이 없었다.\n\n그리고 그는, 어리석고 나약하고 사랑이 많은 그 사람은, 한마디면 모든 것이 밝혀졌을 텐데도 그 한마디를 하지 않으려 했다. 눈물이 홍수처럼 쏟아졌을 것이고, 십 분이면 온 집안 사람이 사정이 실제로 어떠했는지 알게 되었을 것이다. 아버지는 기뻐했을 것이다. 언니는 동생에게 입을 맞추며 천 번이라도 용서를 빌었을 것이다. 부주교는 사과하고 놀라워하며 눈썹을 치켜올린 뒤 행복한 사람이 되어 잠자리에 들었을 것이다. 그리고 애러빈 씨는, 애러빈 씨는 엘리너의 꿈을 꾸었을 것이고, 아침에 사랑의 상념과 함께 깨어났을 것이며, 이튿날 저녁에는 혼인의 계획을 품고 잠자리에 들었을 것이다. 그러나 아아, 이 모든 것은 그렇게 되지 못했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 천천히 편지를 접어 딸에게 돌려주고, 그 이마에 입을 맞추고, 하느님의 축복을 빌어 주었다. 그러고는 천천히 제 방으로 물러갔다.\n\n그가 복도를 벗어나기가 무섭게 엘리너의 방문에 또 한 번 노크 소리가 났고, 그랜틀리 부인의 몹시 얌전한 몸종이 발끝으로 들어와, 언짢지 않으시다면 볼드 부인께서 부주교님의 서재로 오셔서 잠깐 2분만 말씀을 나누어 주실 수 있겠는지 여쭙는다고 했다. 부주교님의 인사를 전하며, 2분도 채 붙들지 않으시겠다고 했다.\n\n엘리너는 몹시 언짢았다. 그녀는 지치고 기진하고 가슴이 아팠으며, 그랜틀리 박사를 향한 지금의 심정은 애정과는 도무지 거리가 멀었다. 그러나 그녀는 겁쟁이가 아니었으므로 5분 뒤에 서재로 가겠다고 약속했다. 그리하여 그녀는 머리를 매만지고 모자를 매어 쓴 뒤, 두근거리는 가슴으로 아래층에 내려갔다.\n\n# 제29장. 심각한 면담\n\n심각한 면담을 즐기는 사람들이 있다. 특히 그 자리에서 충고를 베풀거나 훈계를 내리는 몫이 자기에게 돌아올 때 그러하거니와, 어쩌면 부주교도 그런 사람 축에 들었을 것이다. 그러나 이번만은 그도 다가올 대화를 앞두고 그리 즐거운 기대를 품지 못했다. 그에게 어떤 결점이 있든 손님을 박대하는 사람은 아니었으므로, 제 집에서 엘리너를 나무라는 것이 손님 대접의 도리를 저버리는 죄처럼 느껴지기까지 했다. 게다가 이 일에서 자기가 이기리라는 확신도 그리 서지 않았다. 아내는 결코 이기지 못하리라고 잘라 말했고, 그는 대개 아내의 말을 옳게 여기는 편이었다. 그러나 엘리너의 처신이 온당치 못하다는 확신이 워낙 굳었고 그것을 막아 보는 것이 제 의무라는 생각 또한 워낙 확고해서, 그의 양심은 아내의 충고를 받아들여 조용히 잠자리에 드는 것을 허락하지 않았다.\n\n방으로 들어서는 엘리너의 얼굴은 그를 안심시켜 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다. 평소 엘리너는 태도가 온화하고 행동이 부드러운 사람이었으나, 그 눈에는 함부로 꾸짖기 어렵게 만드는 무엇이 있었다. 실은 엘리너는 꾸중을 들어 본 일이 거의 없었다. 어린 시절 이후로 그에게 꾸중을 시도한 사람은 부주교뿐이었고, 부주교조차 시도할 때마다 대개 실패했다. 엘리너가 혼인한 뒤로는 한 번도 그런 일이 없었다. 그런데 이제 조용하고 편안한 걸음으로 방에 들어서는 처제를 보자, 그는 아내의 충고를 따를 것을 그랬다는 생각이 들 지경이었다.\n\n그는 수고를 끼쳐 미안하다는 말로 이야기를 시작했다. 엘리너는 그런 말씀 마시라고, 아래층으로 내려오는 것쯤은 조금도 수고랄 것이 없다고 대꾸하고는, 자리에 앉아 그가 공격을 시작하기를 참을성 있게 기다렸다.\n\n“엘리너,” 그가 말했다. “나만큼 처제를 진심으로 아끼는 벗은 없다고 내가 말하면, 그 말을 믿어 주리라 생각해요.” 이 말에 엘리너가 아무 대답도 하지 않았으므로 그는 말을 이었다. “처제에게 친오라비가 있었다면 아마 지금 하려는 이야기로 처제를 괴롭히지는 않았을 거예요. 그러나 사정이 이러하니, 친오라비 못지않게 처제의 안녕을 걱정하는 사람이 가까이 있다는 것이 처제에게 위안이 되리라고 생각할 수밖에 없군요.”\n\n“저는 오라비가 있어 본 적이 없어요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“없었다는 것을 나도 알아요. 그러니까 내가 이렇게 말을 꺼내는 것이지요.”\n\n“저는 오라비가 있어 본 적이 없어요.” 엘리너가 되풀이했다. “하지만 아쉬웠던 적도 별로 없었어요. 아버지가 제게는 아버지이자 오라비였으니까요.”\n\n“아버님은 세상에서 가장 자상하고 애정 깊은 분이지요. 그렇지만—”\n\n“그래요, 세상에서 가장 자상하고 애정 깊은 분이고, 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 조언자세요. 아버지가 살아 계신 동안에는 제가 조언이 아쉬울 일은 없어요.”\n\n이 말에 부주교는 다소 말문이 막혔다. 처제가 제 아버지를 두고 하는 말을 대놓고 반박할 수는 없었으나, 그렇다고 그 말에 조금이라도 동의하는 것은 아니었다. 그가 도움의 손길을 내미는 것은 그 아버지가 마음 여리고 사람 좋은 신사일 뿐 세상 물정에는 충분히 밝지 못한 까닭임을 처제가 알아주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그 말을 처제에게 할 수는 없었다. 그리하여 그는 자기 충고가 필요하다거나 고맙겠다는 기색을 상대에게서 조금도 얻어 내지 못한 채, 곧바로 충고의 본론으로 뛰어들어야 했다.\n\n“수전에게 들으니 오늘 저녁에 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다더군요.”\n\n“네, 아버지가 사륜마차 편에 가져다주셨어요. 아버지가 말씀 안 하시던가요?”\n\n“그리고 수전 말로는 무슨 내용인지 알려 주기를 처제가 꺼렸다고 하던데요.”\n\n“언니가 제게 물어본 것 같지는 않아요. 하지만 물었다 해도 말하지 않았을 거예요. 남의 편지를 두고 물어보는 것은 좋지 않다고 생각해요. 보여 주고 싶으면 묻지 않아도 보여 주는 법이니까요.”\n\n“옳은 말이에요. 그렇고말고요. 처제 말이 다 옳아요. 그렇지만 벗들에게 보이고 싶지 않은 편지를 슬로프 씨에게서 받는다는 사실 자체가 어느 정도의… 어느 정도의 놀라움을, 어느 정도의 의심을 불러일으킬 수밖에 없지 않겠어요…”\n\n“의심이라니요!” 엘리너가 말했다. 목소리를 평소보다 높이지도 않았고 여전히 부드럽고 여자다운 어조였으나, 거기에는 분노가 실려 있었다. “의심이라니요! 누가 저를 의심하고, 또 무엇을 의심한다는 말씀이에요?” 그러고는 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 부주교로서는 그 의심의 근거를 대뜸 설명할 채비가 되어 있지 않았던 것이다. “네, 그랜틀리 박사님, 저는 슬로프 씨의 편지를 언니에게 보이고 싶지 않았어요. 아버지가 보시기 전에는 누구에게도 보일 수 없었으니까요. 지금 읽어 보고 싶으시다면 그렇게 하세요.” 그러면서 엘리너는 탁자 너머로 편지를 그에게 건넸다.\n\n이것은 그가 전혀 예상하지 못한 순순함이었고, 도리어 그의 작전을 흔들어 놓았다. 어쨌든 그는 편지를 받아 꼼꼼히 읽고 나서 다시 접어 자기 손 밑 탁자 위에 두었다. 그가 보기에 그것은 거의 모든 점에서 사랑을 공언한 사내의 편지였다. 자기의 가장 나쁜 의심을 뒷받침해 주는 듯했다. 게다가 엘리너가 그것을 자기에게 보여 주었다는 사실은, 슬로프 씨에게서 연서를 받는 것이 제 기쁨이라고 스스로 선언한 것이나 진배없었다. 그는 앞으로 벌어질 구애와 혼인에 온통 정신이 팔린 나머지, 그 서한의 진짜 용건은 거의 완전히 지나쳐 버렸다.\n\n“이제 그 편지를 돌려주시면 고맙겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님.”\n\n그는 편지를 손에 들어 쳐들었으나 곧바로 돌려주려는 기색은 보이지 않았다. “하딩 씨도 이 편지를 보셨나요?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“물론 보셨지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “아버지가 보시라고 쓴 편지인걸요. 오로지 아버지 일만 적혀 있어요. 당연히 아버지께 보여 드렸지요.”\n\n“그런데 엘리너, 이런 편지가 처제 같은 사람이, 처제 같은 처지에 있는 사람이 슬로프 씨에게서 받기에 온당한 편지라고 생각해요?”\n\n“아주 온당한 편지예요.” 엘리너가 말했다. 어쩌면 순전한 고집에서 나온 말이었고, 아마 그 순간에는 제 비단결 같은 곱슬머리를 들먹인 그 못마땅한 대목을 잊고 있었을 것이다.\n\n“그렇다면 엘리너, 나는 처제와 생각이 전혀 다르다는 것을 말해 두는 것이 내 의무겠군요.”\n\n“그러시겠지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. 이제는 순전한 반발심과 지지 않겠다는 오기가 그를 부추기고 있었다. “박사님은 슬로프 씨가 사탄이 곧장 보낸 사자라고 생각하시지요. 저는 그분이 부지런하고 선의를 지닌 성직자라고 생각해요. 이렇게 생각이 다르다니 안타까운 일이에요. 하지만 이렇게 다른 이상, 이 이야기는 하지 않는 편이 나을 거예요.”\n\n여기서 엘리너는 분명 잘못을 저질렀다. 다투는 사안을 두고 그랜틀리 박사와 이야기하기를 거절했더라도 무례랄 것은 없었겠지만, 이미 그의 말을 듣기로 승낙한 이상 그가 슬로프 씨를 악한 자의 사자로 여긴다고 면박할 자격은 없었다. 또 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 슬로프 씨를 좋게 여기지 않으면서 그를 추어올린 것도 정당하지 못했다. 그러나 엘리너는 마음에 상처를 입었고, 화가 났고, 속이 쓰렸다. 저녁 내내 모욕과 심문과 박대를 당한 터였다. 애러빈 씨조차, 아버지조차 그에게 다정하지 않았다. 이 모든 것을 엘리너는 부주교의 편견과 자만 탓으로 돌렸고, 그리하여 그에 대한 적대에 아무 한계도 두지 않기로 마음먹었다. 봐주지도 않고 봐주기를 바라지도 않을 참이었다. 감히 제 서신을 두고 캐물었으니 이만저만 주제넘은 짓이 아니었고, 자기가 그렇게 여긴다는 것을 똑똑히 보여 주기로 작정했다.\n\n“엘리너, 지금 처제는 도리를 잊고 있어요.” 그가 몹시 준엄한 눈으로 엘리너를 보며 말했다. “그렇지 않고서야 내가 어떤 사람을 사탄의 사자로 여긴다는 말을 할 리가 없지요.”\n\n“하지만 정말 그러시잖아요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “그분에게라면 어떤 험담도 지나치지 않다고 여기시지요. 그 편지를 이리 주세요.” 그러면서 손을 뻗어 그의 손에서 편지를 가져갔다. “그분은 아버지를 돕느라 최선을 다하셨어요. 아버지의 어느 벗도 하지 못한 일을 하셨고요. 그런데도 박사님이 싫어하시는 주교의 전속 사제라는 이유만으로, 그분에게는 신사로 대접받을 자격조차 없다는 듯이 말씀하시는군요.”\n\n“그 사람은 처제 아버님을 위해 한 일이 아무것도 없어요.”\n\n“저는 그분이 아주 많은 일을 하셨다고 믿어요. 그리고 제게 관한 한, 저는 그분께 고마워하고 있어요. 박사님이 무슨 말씀을 하시든 그 마음을 막지는 못하실 거예요. 저는 사람을 그 행실로 판단하는데, 제가 볼 수 있는 한 그분의 행실은 훌륭해요.” 그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었다. “더 하실 말씀이 없으시면 이만 안녕히 주무시라는 인사를 드리게 해 주시면 고맙겠어요. 몹시 피곤하거든요.”\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 제 딴에는 처제에게 너그럽게 대하려고 최선을 다한 터였다. 모질게 굴지 않으려 애썼고, 제 훈계에서 가시를 뽑아내려고 힘썼다. 그러나 자기 말을 다 듣지도 않은 채 처제를 보내 줄 생각은 없었다.\n\n“할 말이 있어요, 엘리너. 미안하지만 그것만은 들어 주어야겠어요. 처제는 손에 든 그런 편지를 슬로프 씨에게서 받는 것이 아주 온당하다고 말하는군요. 수전과 나는 생각이 아주 다릅니다. 물론 처제는 제 몸의 주인이고, 무엇이든 처제를 우리에게서 갈라놓는 일이 생긴다면 우리 두 사람으로서는 몹시 슬프겠지만, 그런 이별로 이어질 수 있는 걸음을 처제가 내딛는 것을 막을 힘은 우리에게 없어요. 처제가 그토록 고집스럽게 벗들의 충고를 물리친다면, 제 앞가림은 제가 하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에요. 그렇지만 엘리너, 이것만은 물어도 되겠지요. 슬로프 씨 하나 때문에 처제가 사랑해 온 모든 사람에게서, 처제를 사랑하는 모든 사람에게서 등을 돌릴 만한 값어치가 있나요?”\n\n“무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님. 무슨 이야기를 하고 계신지 모르겠어요. 저는 누구에게서도 등을 돌리고 싶지 않아요.”\n\n“하지만 슬로프 씨와 인연을 맺는다면 그렇게 되고 말아요. 엘리너, 나는 터놓고 말해야겠어요. 처제는 언니와 나와 우리 벗들 쪽이든, 슬로프 씨와 그 벗들 쪽이든 어느 한쪽을 골라야 해요. 아버님에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요. 그분의 심경이야 나보다 처제가 더 잘 알 테니까요.”\n\n“무슨 말씀이세요, 그랜틀리 박사님? 제가 무엇을 알아야 한다는 거예요? 평생 이렇게 고약한 편견은 들어 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“편견이 아니에요, 엘리너. 나는 처제보다 세상을 오래 겪었어요. 슬로프 씨는 어느 모로 보나 처제에게 어울리지 않는 사람이에요. 처제도 그것을 알고 느껴야 마땅해요. 부디, 부디 너무 늦기 전에 이 일을 생각해 봐요.”\n\n“너무 늦기 전이라니요!”\n\n“내 말을 믿지 못하겠거든 수전에게 물어봐요. 언니가 처제에게 편견을 품었다고야 생각하지 못하겠지요. 아니면 아버님께 여쭤 봐도 좋아요. 그분이야 처제에게 편견이 있을 리 없으니까. 애러빈 씨에게 물어봐도—”\n\n“이 일을 애러빈 씨에게 이야기하신 건 아니겠지요!” 엘리너가 벌떡 일어나 그의 앞에 서며 말했다.\n\n“엘리너, 머지않아 바체스터 안팎의 온 세상이 이 이야기를 하게 될 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 저와 슬로프 씨 이야기를 애러빈 씨에게 하셨어요?”\n\n“물론 했지요. 그리고 그분도 내 생각에 전적으로 동의하더군요.”\n\n“무엇에 동의한다는 거예요?” 엘리너가 말했다. “저를 미치게 만드시려는 것 같군요.”\n\n“처제가 슬로프 부인이 되어 플럼스테드에 손님으로 오는 일은 도저히 있을 수 없다는 데에, 그분도 나와 수전과 같은 생각이더군요.”\n\n우리는 비극의 뮤즈에게 사랑받는 처지가 못 되므로, 슬로프 부인이라는 이름이 언젠가 제게 붙게 되리라거나 붙어야 한다거나 붙을지도 모른다는 뜻으로 발음되는 것을 처음 들었을 때 엘리너의 얼굴이 어떠했는지는 감히 묘사해 보려 하지 않겠다. 그것이 어떤 얼굴이었든, 그랜틀리 박사는 그 표정을 쉬이 잊지 못했다. 한두 순간 엘리너는 그 깊은 분노와 깊은 혐오를 담아낼 말을 찾지 못했다. 실로 이 대목에서 말이라는 것이 그에게 그리 수월하게 나와 주지 않았다.\n\n“어쩌면 그렇게 무례하실 수가 있어요?” 마침내 엘리너는 이렇게 말하고, 부주교에게 한마디 더 할 틈도 주지 않은 채 방을 뛰쳐나갔다. 제 방에 이를 때까지 마음을 다잡기가 힘겨웠다. 그러고는 문을 잠그고 침대에 몸을 던져 가슴이 미어지도록 흐느껴 울었다.\n\n그러나 엘리너는 아직도 진상을 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 아버지와 언니가 며칠 전부터 자기가 그 사내와 혼인하려 한다는 생각을 진지하게 품어 왔다는 것을 알지 못했다. 부주교조차 자기가 그러리라고 정말로 여긴다고는 그때까지도 믿지 않았다. 머릿속에서 무슨 조화가 일어났는지, 엘리너는 그 혐의의 발단을 애러빈 씨에게 돌렸다. 그러자 그에 대한 노여움이 걷잡을 수 없이 커졌고, 마음의 괴로움은 견디기 어려울 지경이 되었다. 그 힐난이 진지하게 나온 것이라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었다. 엘리너가 보기에 가장 그럴듯한 것은 이러했다. 부주교와 애러빈 씨가 슬로프 씨와의 못마땅한 교분을 두고 이야기를 나누었고, 애러빈 씨가 그 특유의 비웃는 듯한 빈정거림으로 제 원수와의 교분을 가장 매섭게 모욕하는 방법으로서 그 끔찍한 혼인을 들먹였으며, 부주교가 그 착상을 그에게서 얻어다 자기를 벌주기에 알맞다고 여겨 그 말을 꺼냈다는 것이다. 밤새도록 엘리너는 잠들지 못한 채 오간 말들을 곱씹었고, 이것이 가장 그럴듯한 답으로 보였다.\n\n그러나 애러빈 씨가 어떤 식으로든 제 이름을 슬로프 씨의 이름과 나란히 입에 올렸으리라는 생각은 견딜 수 없었다. 게다가 그 힐난을 굳이 제게 되풀이해 들려준 부주교의 짓궂은 심술 때문에, 엘리너는 날이 밝기도 전에 이 집을 떠나고 싶은 심정이었다. 한 가지는 분명했다. 무슨 일이 있어도 이튿날 아침을 넘겨 이 집에 머물지 않겠으며, 무슨 일이 있어도 그랜틀리 박사와 마주 앉아 아침을 먹지 않겠다는 것이었다. 제 이름과 나란히 놓인 그 사내를 떠올리면 순전한 역겨움에 눈물이 났다. 자기가 이렇게 역겨워하고 이렇게 아파하고 충격을 받고 뼈아프게 찔릴 줄 알았기에 부주교가 그 끔찍한 말을 입에 올린 것이었다. 그는 자기를 슬로프 씨와 다투게 만들고 싶었고, 그래서 그 역겨운 상스러움으로 자기를 모욕한 것이었다. 엘리너는 적어도 자기가 그 뜻을 똑똑히 알아들었다는 것만은 그에게 알려 주기로 마음먹었다.\n\n부주교라고 해서 이 심각한 면담의 결과에 엘리너보다 조금이라도 더 만족한 것은 아니었다. 그는 이 면담에서, 실로 모르려야 모를 수가 없었으므로, 처제가 자기에게 몹시 화가 났다는 것을 알아차렸다. 다만 처제가 화를 내는 까닭은 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 뜻을 품었다고 의심받아서가 아니라 그런 뜻이 죄인 양 씌워졌기 때문이라고 생각했다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 짝지음을 역겹게 여겼으나, 엘리너 역시 그것을 똑같은 눈으로 바라보았기에 모욕감을 느낀 것이라는 데에는 생각이 미치지 못했다.\n\n그는 속이 상하고 다소 낙심한 채 아내에게로 돌아갔으나, 그럼에도 처제에 대한 노여움은 오히려 굳어져 있었다. “그 애 태도가 하나부터 열까지,” 그가 말했다. “도무지 못마땅하기 짝이 없구려. 그자의 연서를 마치 자랑스럽다는 듯이 내게 건네 읽으라고 합디다. 실제로 자랑스러워하고 있소. 침이나 흘리는 그 탐욕스러운 사내가 제 발밑에 엎드린 것을 자랑스러워하는 게요. 그 애는 제 몸과 존 볼드의 돈을 그자의 무릎에 던져 줄 것이고, 제 아들을 망치고 제 아비와 당신을 욕보이고, 저는 비참하고 가엾은 여자가 되고 말 것이오.”\n\n화장대 앞에 앉아 있던 아내는 하던 일을 계속했을 뿐 이 모든 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 부주교가 끼어들어 봐야 얻을 것이 없으리라는 것을 그는 진작 알고 있었으나, 남편이 저토록 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있는 마당에 그 말을 꺼내 자극할 만큼 매정하지는 않았다.\n\n“볼드가 그따위 유언을 남기니 이런 일이 생기는 것이오.” 그가 말을 이었다. “엘리너에게 그만한 돈을 제 손에 맡겨 두는 것은 자선 학교 계집아이에게 맡기는 것이나 다를 바가 없소.” 그랜틀리 부인은 여전히 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. “그러나 나는 내 도리를 다했소. 더 할 수 있는 일이 없구려. 나와 그자 사이를 잇는 고리 노릇을 하도록 내버려 둘 수는 없다고 그 애에게 분명히 말해 두었소. 이제부터는 그 애를 플럼스테드에서 반가이 맞을 수가 없게 되었소. 슬로프 씨의 연서가 이 집으로 날아드는 것을 두고 볼 수는 없단 말이오. 수전, 이 문제에 대한 그 애 마음이 돌이킬 수 없이 굳은 듯하니, 모두를 위해서 바체스터로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 당신이 일러 주는 게 좋겠소.”\n\n그런데 그랜틀리 부인은 엘리너에게 화가 나 있기는 했고, 남편에 못지않게 화가 나 있었으나, 동생을 집에서 내보낼 생각은 조금도 없었다. 그리하여 마침내 입을 열어, 특유의 온화하고 사람을 홀리는 말투로 부주교에게 설명하기를, 당신은 지금 아무 까닭 없이 씩씩거리고 부산을 떨며 애를 태우고 있다고 했다. 내버려 두면 일이란 당신이 주무르는 것보다 훨씬 잘 풀리게 마련이라고 잘라 말했고, 마침내 남편을 어르는 데 성공하여 손님을 박대하려는 마음이 다소 누그러진 채로 잠자리에 들게 만들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 엘리너의 하녀는 마님이 기도에 나올 만큼 몸이 좋지 않아 자기 방에서 아침을 들겠다는 말을 식당으로 전하라는 분부를 받았다. 방으로 아버지가 찾아왔고, 엘리너는 당장 바체스터로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 아버지에게 밝혔다. 하딩 씨는 그 말에 별로 놀라지 않았다. 온 집안이 무언가 잘못되었다는 것을 아는 듯했다. 다들 발소리를 죽이고 다녔건만 사람들의 구두는 평소보다 더 삐걱거리는 것 같았다. 여자들의 얼굴에는 무언가 알고 있다는 기색이 어렸고, 남자들은 아무 일도 없다는 듯이 이야기를 나누어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 이 모든 것이 하딩 씨의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌러 온 터라, 엘리너가 당장 바체스터로 돌아가야만 하겠다고 말했을 때 그는 그저 가엾게 한숨을 쉬며 자기도 함께 갈 채비를 하겠다고만 했다.\n\n그러나 엘리너는 이 대목에서 완강히 반대했다. 혼자 가고 싶은 마음이 간절하다고, 아버지는 그랜틀리 박사와의 다툼에 얽혀 있지 않다는 것이 남들 눈에 보였으면 하는 마음이 간절하다고 했다. 하딩 씨는 마침내 뜻을 굽혔다. 그러나 두 사람 사이에 슬로프 씨에 관한 말은 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 전날 저녁의 그 심각한 면담에 대해서도 한마디 말이 없었고 한마디 물음도 없었다. 실로 두 사람 사이에는 서로 터놓는 마음이 거의 없었다. 어째서 그래야 하는지는 둘 다 알지 못했다. 엘리너가 한번 주교를 찾아뵙지 않겠느냐고 물었으나, 아버지는 다소 퉁명스럽게 모르겠다고, 그러지 않을 성싶다고, 지금 당장은 뭐라 말할 수 없다고 대답했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 저마다 애정의 표시를, 다시 터놓는 마음을, 늘 두 사람을 이어 주던 그 정의 어떤 기미를 애타게 갈망했다. 그러나 아무것도 주어지지 않았다. 아버지는 차마 딸에게 그 연인이라는 사람에 대해 물어볼 수 없었고, 딸은 그랜틀리 박사가 제 노여움을 돋우며 쓴 그 역겨운 말을 되풀이해 제 입을 더럽히려 하지 않았다. 그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n엘리너가 돌아가는 방법을 정하는 데에도 얼마간 말썽이 있었다. 엘리너는 아버지에게 역마차를 불러 달라고 청했으나, 그랜틀리 부인이 그 말을 듣고 강하게 반대했다. 엘리너가 부주교에게 골이 나서 떠날 참이라면, 어째서 하인들과 온 이웃에게 그 사실을 알려야 한단 말인가? 그리하여 마침내 엘리너는 플럼스테드의 마차를 쓰기로 승낙했고, 부주교가 아침 식사 뒤에 곧바로 나가 저녁 식사 때까지 돌아오지 않을 것이었으므로 길 떠나기를 점심 뒤로 미루고 그때 식구들과 자리를 함께하는 데에도 승낙했다. 다툼의 내용에 대해서는 누구도 한마디 하지 않았다. 마차 문제는 두 부인 사이에서 메르쿠리우스 노릇을 한 하딩 씨가 처리했다. 두 사람은 만나자 아주 다정하게 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 세상에 아무 탈도 없다는 듯이 저마다 코바늘뜨개질감을 들고 앉았다.\n\n# 제30장. 또 하나의 사랑의 장면\n\n그런데 목사관에는 이 딱한 사태를 두고 그 심경을 다소 엄밀하게 따져 보아야 할 손님이 또 한 사람 있었다. 애러빈 씨는 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 결혼할지도 모른다는 이야기를 벗에게서 듣고 경악했으나, 믿지 못한 것은 아니었다. 그가 엘리너를 사랑하고 있지 않았다는 말은 앞서 해 두었고, 이 시점까지는 그것이 분명 사실이었다. 그러나 그녀가 다른 누군가를 사랑한다는 말을 듣자마자, 그는 스스로 그녀를 몹시 좋아하게 되었다. 그녀를 아내로 삼고 싶다고 마음을 정한 것은 아니었다. 그는 그녀를 자신과 결부시켜 생각해 본 적이 없었고 지금도 그러지 않았다. 다만 그는 내면에서 형언할 수 없는 깊은 회한을, 갉아먹는 듯한 슬픔을, 도무지 이겨 낼 수 없는 의기소침을 느꼈고, 아울러 일종의 자기 비하를 느꼈다. 자기가, 다름 아닌 애러빈 씨 자신이, 그토록 철저히 경멸하는 저 사내가 이 어여쁜 상을 채어 가는 것을 막으려고 아무것도 하지 않았다는 자책이었다.\n\n마흔이 되도록 결혼하지 않은 남자가 이런 감정을 조금도 모른 채 지내 왔다면, 그는 매우 성공한 사람이었거나 아니면 매우 냉정한 사람이었을 것이다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 이 값진 상선단의 호위선으로 따라붙으려고 제 작은 배의 돛을 손볼 생각 따위는 해 본 적이 없었다. 볼드 부인이 아름답다는 것은 보아서 알았으나, 그 아름다움을 제 것으로 삼겠다는 꿈은 꾸어 본 적이 없었다. 볼드 부인이 부유하다는 것도 알았으나, 그 재산을 제 것으로 삼겠다는 생각은 그랜틀리 박사만큼이나 해 본 적이 없었다. 볼드 부인이 총명하고 마음이 따뜻하고 상냥하고 분별 있다는 것을, 요컨대 남자가 제 아내에게 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다. 그러나 그녀의 매력이 높으면 높을수록, 그녀가 존중받아 마땅한 까닭이 크면 클수록, 자기가 그것을 차지하는 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 상상은 그만큼 줄어들었다. 그것은 생각이라기보다 본능이었으니, 그만큼 그는 겸손하고 자신이 없었다. 이제 그 소심함이 받을 보상이란, 제 눈에 그 아름다움이 더할 나위 없이 완벽하고, 그 재산이 도리어 그로 하여금 마음을 접게 만들었으며, 그렇게 접지만 않았더라면 그 미망인 신세 때문에라도 그의 입을 다물게 했을 이 여인이 하필 오바디아 슬로프의 먹이가 되는 것을 지켜보는 일이었다.\n\n볼드 부인이 떠나는 날 아침, 그는 세인트 이월드로 가려고 말에 올랐다. 말을 몰면서 그는 반 아르테벨데의 한 구절을 계속 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n여자의 사랑이란 어찌 그리 우쭐할 것이 못 되는가.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 마음을 다잡아 다른 일들을, 제 교구를, 제 칼리지를, 제 신조를 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 그의 생각은 자꾸만 슬로프 씨와 저 플랑드르 수령에게로 되돌아갔다.\n\n가만히 헤아려 보면, 여자의 사랑이란 어찌 그리 우쭐할 것이 못 되는가. 흔히 가장 가까이 있는 자에게, 가장 유리하게 떠받쳐진 자에게 주어지는 것을.\n\n볼드 부인이 자기 아닌 다른 누구와 결혼한다는 것이 문제가 아니었다. 그는 구혼자로 나선 적조차 없었으니까. 문제는 그녀가 하필 슬로프 씨와 결혼한다는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 다시 한번 되뇌었다.\n\n겉으로 드러난 기품도, 안에 깃든 빛도 필요치 않다. 날마다 그 둘을 다 갖추지 못한 사내들이 하느님께서 지으신 여성 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 고결한 이들과 짝을 이룬다. 그 사랑은 남녀의 구별 말고는 아무 구별도 두지 않으며, 선택이라는 말 자체를 비웃는다.\n\n그렇게 그는 마음이 몹시 어지러운 채로 길을 갔다.\n\n그날 아침 그의 말길은 편치 않았고, 세인트 이월드에서도 이렇다 할 일을 하지 못했다.\n\n그의 집에 필요한 개축은 빠르게 마무리되어 가고 있었고, 그는 방들을 거닐고 계단을 오르내리고 정원을 어슬렁거렸으나, 그것들에 대해 이렇다 할 흥미를 불러일으킬 수가 없었다. 창마다 걸음을 멈추고 서서 밖을 내다보며 슬로프 씨를 생각했다. 거의 모든 창가에서 그는 예전에 엘리너와 나란히 서서 이야기를 나누었다. 그녀와 그랜틀리 부인이 노상 이곳에 와 있었고, 그랜틀리 부인이 이런저런 지시를 내리고 그 지시가 제대로 이행되었는지 살피는 동안, 그와 엘리너는 성직자의 직분에 딸린 온갖 이야기를 나누었던 것이다. 그는 자기가 그녀에게 얼마나 자주 법도를 늘어놓았던가를, 그리고 다소 독단적인 그 선언들을 그녀가 얼마나 곱게 받아 주었던가를 생각했다. 총명하게 귀 기울이던 모습, 부드러우면서도 재빠르던 대꾸, 교회에 관한 모든 일에, 그에게 관계된 모든 일에 보이던 관심이 떠올랐다. 그러고는 승마용 채찍으로 창틀을 내리치며, 엘리너 볼드가 슬로프 씨와 결혼한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 스스로에게 단언했다.\n\n그러면서도 그는 마땅히 그래야 했을 만큼 그것이 정말로 있을 수 없는 일이라고 믿지는 못했다. 그는 그것이 참으로 있을 수 없는 일임을 느낄 만큼 그녀를 잘 알았어야 했다. 엘리너에게는 그런 전락으로부터 스스로를 지켜 줄 무엇인가가 안에 있음을 알았어야 했다. 그러나 그도 다른 숱한 남자들처럼 여자를 믿는 마음이 모자랐다. 그는 엘리너 볼드가 슬로프 부인이 된다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 몇 번이고 되뇌면서도, 그녀가 그리하리라고 믿었다. 그리하여 그는 이리저리 서성거릴 뿐 아무것도 하지 못하고 아무 생각도 하지 못했다. 그는 철저히 불편했고 철저히 마음이 편치 않았으며, 자신에게도 남들에게도 짜증이 났고, 마음속으로는 슬로프 씨를 향한 적의를 곱씹고 있었다. 이것이 마땅한 모습이 아님을 그도 알고 느꼈으나 어찌할 도리가 없었다. 실인즉 애러빈 씨는 이제 볼드 부인을 사랑하고 있었으되, 정작 본인은 그 사실을 몰랐다. 그는 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 마흔 살이나 되어서 그것을 자각하지도 못한 채 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 그는 스물한 살 젊은이가 그러하듯 속을 끓이고 안달하면서도 무엇이 문제인지 알지 못했다. 그리하여 세인트 이월드에서 아무 일도 하지 못한 채, 볼드 부인이 떠나기 전에 한 번 볼 수 있을지도 모른다는 어떤 내밀하고 인정되지 않은 바람에 떠밀려, 그는 여느 때보다 훨씬 이른 시각에 말을 돌려 돌아왔다.\n\n엘리너도 유쾌한 아침을 보내지는 못했다. 그녀는 모두에게 짜증이 나 있었고, 무엇보다 자기 자신에게 짜증이 나 있었다. 자기가 부당한 대접을 받았다고 느꼈으나, 한편으로는 제 패를 잘 놀리지 못했다는 것도 느꼈다. 그녀는 언니의 비아냥과 부주교의 훈계를 무심하게 받아넘길 만큼 스스로를 의심 위에 두었어야 했다. 그러나 그러지 못하고 성내고 아파하는 모습을 보이고 말았으니, 이제 와 제 앙칼짐이 부끄러웠으면서도 그것을 그만두지 못했다.\n\n아침나절 대부분을 그녀는 혼자 보냈으나, 얼마 뒤 아버지가 곁에 왔다. 그는 무슨 일이 있더라도 작은딸과 갈라서지는 않겠다고 단단히 마음을 굳힌 터였다. 딸이 슬로프 씨의 식탁 상석에 앉아 있는 모습을 떠올리며 마음을 다잡기란 그에게 힘든 일이었으나, 그는 끝내 그것을 해냈다. 슬로프 씨도 어엿한 사람이고 성직자이니 엘리너의 아버지로서 딸이 그런 사람과 결혼하는 것을 막으려 들 권리조차 자기에게는 없다고 그는 스스로에게 따져 보았다. 그는 자기가 세상 무엇보다 딸을 먼저 두기로 정했다는 것을, 딸이 그르지 않다고 인정할 준비가 되어 있다는 것을, 그랜틀리 박사와는 생각이 전혀 다르다는 것을 딸에게 말해 주고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 그러나 슬로프 씨의 이름만은 차마 입에 올릴 수가 없었다. 그들 모두의 짐작이 어긋난 것일 가능성도 아직 남아 있었고, 그렇게 반신반의하는 터라 그 문제를 딸에게 터놓고 이야기할 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n그가 응접실에서 딸의 허리에 팔을 두르고 앉아 이따금 다정한 말을 나직이 건네며 보이지 않는 활을 열심히 켜고 있을 때, 애러빈 씨가 방으로 들어왔다. 하딩 씨는 곧바로 일어섰고, 두 사람은 서로 진부한 말을 몇 마디 주고받았는데 둘 다 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 헤아리지 않았다. 그동안 엘리너는 소파에 앉은 채 입을 다물고 시무룩해 있었다. 애러빈 씨도 그녀의 분노가 향한 사람들의 명단에 들어 있었다. 그 역시 감히 그녀와 슬로프 씨의 교분을 입에 올렸고, 그 역시 감히 제 원수를 원수로 삼지 않는다고 그녀를 나무랐던 것이다. 그녀는 떠나기 전에 그를 볼 생각이 없었고, 지금도 상냥하게 굴 마음이 별로 없었다.\n\n집안 전체에 무언가 잘못되었다는 기운이 감돌았다. 애러빈 씨는 엘리너를 보자, 이 모든 일을 전혀 모르는 사람인 양 표정을 짓거나 말을 할 수가 없었다. 여느 때처럼 그녀에게 쾌활하고 단호하고 짐짓 어깃장을 놓는 태도를 취할 수가 없었다. 방에 들어선 지 이 분도 되지 않아 그는 돌아온 것이 잘못이었다고 느꼈고, 그녀의 목소리를 듣는 순간 세인트 이월드로 돌아가 있었으면 하고 간절히 바랐다. 대체 무엇 하러 슬로프 씨의 장차 아내가 될 사람에게 무슨 말을 더 건네고 싶어 했더란 말인가?\n\n“이렇게 빨리 떠나신다니 섭섭합니다.” 그가 여느 때의 목소리를 내려 애썼으나 헛일이었다. 이 말에 그녀는 바체스터에 가 있어야 한다는 사정을 무어라 우물거리고는, 유난히 부지런히 코바늘 뜨개질에 몰두했다.\n\n그러고는 애러빈 씨와 하딩 씨 사이에 진부한 이야기가 조금 더 오갔다. 진부하고 딱딱하고 맥없고 알맹이 없는 이야기였다. 둘 다 서로에게 할 말이 없었으나, 그렇다고 이런 순간에 잠자코 있는 것도 둘 다 내키지 않았다. 마침내 하딩 씨는 말이 끊긴 틈을 타 방을 빠져나갔고, 엘리너와 애러빈 씨만 남게 되었다.\n\n“부인이 가시면 우리 모임도 크게 헐거워지겠지요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 다시 무어라 중얼거렸으나 거의 알아들을 수 없었고, 눈은 뜨개질감에 붙박아 두었다.\n\n“여기서 아주 즐거운 한 달을 보냈습니다.” 그가 말했다. “적어도 저는 그랬습니다. 이렇게 빨리 끝나 버린다니 아쉽군요.”\n\n“저는 생각했던 것보다 이미 오래 집을 비웠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “이제는 돌아가야 할 때예요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 즐거운 시간도 즐거운 날들도 끝이 나게 마련이지요. 그중 즐거운 날이 그리 적다는 것이 딱한 일이고요. 아니, 어쩌면 그보다는—”\n\n“사람들이 제 나날의 즐거움을 망가뜨리는 데 그토록 애쓴다는 것이야말로 딱한 일이지요.” 그녀가 그의 말을 자르며 말했다. “세상에 너그러움이 그리도 적다는 것이 딱한 일이고요.”\n\n“너그러움은 제 집안에서부터 시작되어야 하지요.” 그가 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 성직자로서 마땅히 가르쳐야 할 의무라 여기는 원칙들을 희생해 가면서까지 그녀가 말하는 그런 너그러움을 베풀 수는 없다고 설명하려다가, 슬로프 씨의 장차 아내가 될 사람과 그런 문제로 다투어 봐야 부질없다 못해 해롭기만 하리라는 데 생각이 미쳤다. “그러나 이제 곧 떠나실 텐데,” 그가 말을 이었다. “마지막 한 시간까지 또 훈계로 지치게 해 드리지는 않겠습니다. 그러잖아도 훈계를 너무 많이 해 드린 것 같아 걱정입니다.”\n\n“설교하시는 대로 몸소 행하셔야지요, 애러빈 씨.”\n\n“분명 그래야지요. 우리 모두가 그래야 합니다. 감히 남을 가르치겠다고 나서는 사람은 누구나 제가 한 말을 스스로 지키려 온 힘을 다할 의무가 있습니다. 제가 그 점에서 모자란다는 것은 기꺼이 인정합니다만, 지금 무엇을 두고 하시는 말씀인지는 잘 모르겠군요. 하필 지금 저더러 설교하는 대로 행하라고 말씀하시는 데에는 무슨 특별한 까닭이라도 있습니까?”\n\n엘리너는 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 제가 화난 까닭을 그에게 알리고, 자기를 함부로 말했다고 그를 나무라고, 그런 다음 마침내 용서하여 벗으로 헤어지고 싶은 마음이 간절했다. 지금 같은 심경으로 그를 떠나보내면 마음이 편치 않으리라는 것을 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 그에게 슬로프 씨 이야기를 꺼내기란 좀처럼 어려웠다. 게다가 부주교가 던진 그 비아냥을, 그것도 그녀가 믿기로는 애러빈 씨가 부추겨서 나온 그 비아냥을 어떻게 입에 올린단 말인가? 그녀는 그가 틀렸음을 알게 하고, 자기를 부당하게 대했음을 깨닫게 하여, 그리하여 용서의 달콤함이 한층 커지기를 바랐다. 노여운 채로 그와 헤어지고 말기에는 그를 너무 좋아한다는 것을 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 그 깊은 노여움을 그의 해명 없이, 그의 인정 없이, 다시는 자기에게 그런 잘못을 저지르지 않겠다는 다짐 없이 넘길 수도 없었다.\n\n“어찌하여 제가 설교하는 대로 행해야 한다고 말씀하십니까?” 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n“모든 사람이 그래야 하니까요.”\n\n“그렇지요. 그건 말하자면 다들 알고 인정하는 바입니다. 그러나 부인께서는 모든 사람에게, 또 모든 성직자에게 그렇게 말씀하시지는 않습니다. 그 충고는 옳은 말이기는 하지만, 어떤 특정한 결함을 두고서가 아니면 나오지 않는 법이지요. 제 결함이 무엇인지 말씀해 주시면, 그 충고로 나아지도록 힘써 보겠습니다.”\n\n그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들이더니, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. “애러빈 씨, 당신은 제게 터놓고 분명하게 말할 만큼 대담하지 못하시면서, 여자인 저더러는 당신께 터놓고 말하기를 바라시는군요. 어찌하여 제 등 뒤에서 그랜틀리 박사께 제 험담을 하셨습니까?”\n\n“험담이라니요!” 그가 말했고, 그의 얼굴이 온통 붉게 물들었다. “무슨 험담 말입니까? 제가 부인의 험담을 했다면, 부인께 용서를 빌고, 그 말을 들은 분께도 용서를 빌고, 하느님께도 용서를 빌겠습니다. 그러나 제가 그랜틀리 박사께 부인의 무슨 험담을 했다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n그녀 역시 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다. 자기를 다른 남자의 아내라고 말하지 않았느냐고 차마 물을 수가 없었다. “그건 당신이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “다만 명예를 아는 분으로서 여쭙습니다. 당신은 제 이야기를, 당신의 친누이를 두고서는 결코 하지 않았을 방식으로 하지 않으셨습니까? 아니, 여쭙지 않겠어요.” 그가 얼른 대답하지 않는 것을 보고 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그런 물음에 답해야 하는 처지로 몰지는 않겠습니다. 당신이 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지는 그랜틀리 박사께 들었으니까요.”\n\n“그랜틀리 박사께서 제게 조언을 구하신 것은 사실이고, 저는 그것을 드렸습니다. 그분이 제게 물으시기를—”\n\n“압니다, 애러빈 씨. 당신과 그분에게 개인적으로 마뜩잖은 어떤 신사와 제가 계속 알고 지낸다면 저를 플럼스테드에 맞아들이는 것이 옳겠느냐고 물으셨겠지요.”\n\n“오해십니다, 볼드 부인. 저는 슬로프 씨를 개인적으로 알지 못합니다. 평생 한 번도 만난 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“그렇다고 개인적으로 그분에게 적의를 품고 계시지 않은 것은 아니지요. 당신의 적의가 온당한지 아닌지를 따지는 것은 제 몫이 아닙니다만, 제 이름만은 당신의 반목에 끌려 들어가지 않기를 바랄 권리가 제게 있었습니다. 그런데 그 일이 벌어졌고, 그것도 여자인 제게 가장 해롭고 가장 괴로운 방식으로 다름 아닌 당신에 의해 벌어졌습니다. 솔직히 말씀드리자면 애러빈 씨, 저는 당신께만은 다른 대접을 기대했습니다.”\n\n이렇게 말하는 동안 그녀는 힘겹게 눈물을 참았다. 그러나 끝내 참아 냈다. 만약 그녀가 무너져 이런 경우에 여자라면 마땅히 그러하듯 소리 내어 흐느꼈더라면, 그는 그 자리에서 마음이 녹아 용서를 빌었을 것이고, 어쩌면 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 사랑을 고백했을 것이다. 모든 것이 밝혀졌을 터이고, 엘리너는 흡족한 마음으로 바체스터로 돌아갔을 터이다. 애러빈 씨에게서 온전한 진실을 듣기만 했다면 부주교의 의심쯤은 얼마나 쉽게 용서하고 잊어버렸겠는가. 그러나 그랬더라면 내 소설은 어찌 되었겠는가? 그녀는 울지 않았고, 애러빈 씨는 녹지 않았다.\n\n“저를 부당하게 보시는군요.” 그가 말했다. “그랜틀리 박사께서 제 조언을 구하셨으니, 저는 드리지 않을 수 없었습니다.”\n\n“그랜틀리 박사는 지나치게 참견이 심하고 지나치게 무례하셨어요. 그분이 그분의 지인을 사귈 권리가 있듯, 저도 제 지인을 사귈 권리가 온전히 있습니다. 그랜틀리 박사가 태튼햄 코너 경을 안다는 이유로 제 집에서 그분을 내치는 것이 옳겠느냐고 제가 당신께 여쭈었다면 뭐라고 하셨겠어요? 태튼햄 경이 성직자에게 마뜩잖은 지인인 것은, 슬로프 씨가 성직자의 딸에게 마뜩잖은 지인인 것과 꼭 같을 텐데요.”\n\n“저는 태튼햄 코너 경을 알지 못합니다.”\n\n“그러시겠지요. 하지만 그랜틀리 박사는 아십니다. 그분이 영국의 온갖 경마장에 있는 젊은 귀족을 죄다 안다 해도 제게는 아무 상관이 없어요. 저는 그분 일에 참견하지 않을 것이고, 그분도 제 일에 참견해서는 안 됩니다.”\n\n“생각이 달라 죄송합니다, 볼드 부인. 그러나 부인께서 이 문제를 제게 꺼내셨고, 더욱이 제가 이 일로 몇 마디 한 것을 나무라시니, 저는 부인과 생각이 다르다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다. 그랜틀리 박사께서는 세상 속의 한 남자로서 제 지인을 스스로 고를 권리가 있으시되, 거기에는 일정한 견제가 따릅니다. 그분이 그것을 잘못 고르시면 그 견제가 작동하지요. 그분이 어울리지 않는 사람들과 어울리시면 그분의 주교가 나설 것입니다. 주교가 그랜틀리 박사께 그러한 자리라면, 그랜틀리 박사는 부인께 그러한 자리입니다.”\n\n“저는 그것을 부인합니다. 전적으로 부인합니다.” 엘리너가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했고, 응접실 바닥에 선 그 모습은 애러빈 씨 앞에서 그야말로 번쩍였다. 그는 그녀가 그토록 격해진 것을 본 적이 없었고, 그녀가 그 반만큼이라도 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다.\n\n“전적으로 부인합니다.” 그녀가 말했다. “그랜틀리 박사는 제게 그 어떤 종류의 관할권도 갖고 계시지 않습니다. 당신도 그분도 제가 이 세상에 아주 혼자인 것은 아니라는 사실을 잊으셨나요? 제게 아버지가 계시다는 것을 잊으셨나요? 그랜틀리 박사는 늘 그 사실을 잊고 계셨던 듯합니다.\n\n“애러빈 씨, 당신에게서라면,” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “저는 조언을 들었을 거예요. 벗이 벗에게 건네듯 주어지는 조언이려니 여겼을 테니까요. 훈장이 학생에게 명령을 내리듯 하는 것이 아니라요. 당신과 생각이 달랐을 수도 있고, 이 문제라면 분명 달랐겠지요. 하지만 당신이 여느 때의 말투로, 여느 때의 스스럼없음으로 제게 말씀하셨더라면 저는 화내지 않았을 거예요. 그런데 지금은, 애러빈 씨, 저를 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것이 사내다운 일이었나요? 그토록 무례하게, 그토록—? 당신이 하신 말씀을 저는 차마 옮기지 못하겠어요. 제가 무엇을 느끼는지 아셔야 합니다. 제가 당신이 못마땅해하는 교리를 가진 사람과 알고 지내기로 했다는 이유로, 저를 그런 식으로 말하고 언니의 남편에게 저를 언니 집에서 내보내라고 조언하는 것이 온당한 일이었나요?”\n\n“달리 방도가 없군요, 볼드 부인.” 그가 벽난로를 등지고 서서, 눈은 융단의 무늬를 뚫어져라 내려다보며, 느릿하고 고른 목소리로 말했다. “저와 그랜틀리 박사 사이에 오간 일을 있는 그대로 말씀드리는 수밖에요.”\n\n“말씀하세요.” 그가 잠시 말을 멈추자 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“제가 드릴 말씀이 부인을 아프게 할까 걱정입니다.”\n\n“이미 하신 일보다 더 아프게 하기야 하겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그랜틀리 박사께서 제게, 부인을 슬로프 씨의 아내로서 댁에 맞아들이는 것이 분별 있는 일이겠느냐고 물으셨고, 저는 분별없는 일이라고 대답했습니다. 슬로프 씨와, 그런 일은 도저히 있을 수 없다고 믿었기에—”\n\n“고맙습니다, 애러빈 씨. 그만하면 됐습니다. 그 까닭까지는 알고 싶지 않아요.” 그녀가 무섭도록 차분한 목소리로 말했다. “저는 그 신사분께 이웃으로서의 예사로운 예의를 보였을 뿐입니다. 그런데 그렇게 했다는 이유로, 당신과 그랜틀리 박사께서 당신들과 뜻이 다른 성직자라면 누구에게나 마땅히 품어야 한다고 여기시는 그 원한과 증오를 제가 그분께 품지 않았다는 이유로, 당신들은 제가 그분과 결혼할 것이라고 단정하시는군요. 아니, 사실은 그렇게 단정하시는 것도 아니지요. 더 나은 근거도 없이 그런 터무니없는 결론에 이를 만큼 분별없는 사람은 없을 테니까요. 당신들은 그렇게 생각한 것이 아니라, 제가 그런 지목을 유난히 괴로워할 처지에 있으니 겁을 집어먹고 당신들의 저 적수에게 등을 돌리게 하려고 그런 말을 지어내신 겁니다.”\n\n말을 마치자 그녀는 응접실 창가로 걸어가 정원으로 나가 버렸다. 애러빈 씨는 방에 남아 여전히 융단의 무늬를 세고 있었다. 그러나 그는 그녀가 한 말을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 또렷이 듣고 정확히 새겨 두었다. 그녀가 한 말로 미루어, 부주교가 그녀에게 슬로프 씨를 향한 애정이 있다고 넘겨짚은 것이 틀렸음은 분명하지 않은가? 엘리너가 여전히 다른 선택을 할 수 있는 자유로운 몸임이 분명하지 않은가? 그가 한순간이라도 의심을 품었다는 것이 이상해 보일지 모르나, 그는 정말로 의심했다. 그녀는 그 혐의를 딱 잘라 부인하지 않았고, 그것이 사실이 아니라고 명시하여 말하지도 않았던 것이다. 애러빈 씨는 여자의 감정이 어떤 것인지 거의 알지 못했으니, 알았더라면 그녀가 이미 한 것보다 더 분명한 선언을 하리라고 기대하는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지 알았을 것이다. 여자의 마음이 어떤 것인지를 아는 남자는 드물고, 세월이 그 앎의 값어치를 앗아 간 뒤에야 겨우 알게 된다. 그리고 그러한 편이 다행이니, 그렇지 않았다면 남자들이 너무 쉽게 이겼을 것이다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 서서 융단을 세고 있었다. 자기에게 던져진 모진 말들 때문에 불행했다, 비참하도록 불행했다. 그러면서도 자기가 그토록 마음에 둔 여인이 자기가 그토록 싫어하는 남자의 아내가 되지 않으리라는 생각에 행복했다, 더없이 행복했다. 그렇게 서 있는 동안 그는 자기가 사랑에 빠져 있음을 깨닫기 시작했다. 마흔 해가 그의 머리 위로 지나가는 동안 여자의 아름다움이 그에게 불편한 한 시간을 안긴 적은 한 번도 없었다. 지금 이 시간은 몹시 불편했다.\n\n그렇다고 그가 그 시간의 절반이나 사분의 일이라도 거기 머물러 있었던 것은 아니다. 엘리너가 무어라 했든, 애러빈 씨는 사실 사내다운 사내였다. 자기가 이 여인을 사랑한다는 것을 확인하고, 이제 그녀가 적어도 마음만 있다면 제 사랑을 받아들일 수 있는 몸이라고 믿을 만한 근거가 생기자, 그는 힘닿는 대로 구애해 보려고 그녀를 따라 정원으로 나갔다.\n\n그녀를 찾는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그녀는 부주교의 땅에 서 있는, 교회 묘지와 맞닿은 느릅나무 가로수 아래를 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 그녀와 애러빈 씨 사이에 오간 일은 애석하게도 그녀의 격한 마음을 누그러뜨리는 데 조금도 도움이 되지 않았다. 그녀는 몹시 화가 나 있었고, 누구보다도 그에게 화가 나 있었다. 어떻게 그가 자기를 그토록 잘못 알 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 그와 그토록 스스럼없이 지냈고, 그가 하고 싶은 말을 무엇이든 하도록 그토록 너그럽게 두었고, 그의 생각에 맞추었고, 그의 견해를 아꼈고, 그의 가르침을 북돋웠고, 그의 편안함을 살폈으며, 아리따운 여자가 제 몸도 제 마음도 내주지 않으면서 미혼 남자를 떠받들 수 있는 온갖 방식으로 그를 떠받들었다! 그녀가 그렇게 하고 있는 동안, 그는 그녀를 다른 남자와 이미 약혼한 여자로 여기고 있었던 것이다.\n\n가로수길을 걷는 동안 이따금 부르지 않은 눈물이 뺨으로 밀려 나왔고, 손을 들어 그것을 훔쳐 내며 그녀는 이런 대접을 받았다는 생각에 분해서 작은 발로 잔디를 굴렀다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 눈에 들어왔을 때 그는 이미 아주 가까이 와 있었고, 그녀는 홱 돌아서서 속내를 들키는 눈물 자국을 뺨에서 지우려 애쓰며 가로수길을 되짚어 내려갔다. 부질없는 애씀이었으니, 애러빈 씨는 그런 사소한 것을 살필 수 있는 마음 상태가 아니었다. 그는 그녀를 따라 길을 내려갔고, 그녀가 길 끝에 다다른 바로 그때 따라잡았다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 어떻게 말을 붙일지 헤아려 보지 않았고, 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 두지도 않았다. 그저 그녀와 다투는 것이 자기에게 비참한 일이며, 그녀를 사랑하도록 허락받는 것이 행복이리라고 느꼈을 뿐이다. 그러면서도 용서를 구하며 스스로를 낮출 수는 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 잘못한 것이 없었다. 그녀가 나무란 것처럼 그녀를 험담하지도, 해치지도 않았다. 저지르지 않은 죄를 고백할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 지나간 일을 지나간 대로 두고, 그녀와 자기의 앞날에 대한 바람을 그녀에게 묻는 것뿐이었다.\n\n“우리가 원수로 헤어지는 것은 아니겠지요?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“제 쪽에서는 아무런 원한도 없을 겁니다.” 엘리너가 말했다. “저는 어떤 원한도 두지 않으려고 애씁니다. 다만 방금 있었던 일 뒤에 우리 사이에 참된 우정이 있을 수 있다고 말한다면 그건 속 빈 시늉이겠지요. 사람은 제가 업신여기는 이를 벗으로 삼지 못하는 법입니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 제가 업신여김을 받고 있습니까?”\n\n“그러셨으니 저를 두고 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있었겠지요. 그리고 저는 속았습니다, 모질게 속았어요. 저는 당신이 저를 좋게 여기신다고 믿었습니다. 저를 귀히 여기신다고 믿었어요.”\n\n“좋게 여기고 귀히 여긴다니요!” 그가 말했다. “부인 앞에서 저를 변명하자면 그보다 더 센 말을 써야겠습니다.” 그가 잠시 말을 멈추자, 그가 이어 말하기를 기다리는 동안 엘리너의 가슴속에서 심장이 아프도록 세차게 뛰었다. “저는 부인을 귀히 여겨 왔고 지금도 귀히 여깁니다. 여태 어떤 여인도 그렇게 귀히 여긴 적이 없습니다. 좋게 여기다니요! 저는 어떤 사람에 대해서도 그토록 좋게, 그토록 크게 여기게 되리라고는 생각조차 하지 못했습니다. 부인의 험담을 하다니요! 부인을 모욕하다니요! 짐짓 부인을 해치다니요! 저야말로 험담과 모욕과 해악으로부터 부인을 지켜 드리는 것이 제 몫이었으면 합니다. 험담이라니! 아, 나로서는! 차라리 그랬더라면 나았을 것을. 죄스러운 흠모로 흠모하는 것보다는요. 죄스러울 뿐 아니라 헛되기도 한 흠모로 말입니다.” 그러고서 그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡고 발밑의 풀을 내려다보며, 제 뜻을 어떻게 나타내야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 그녀 곁에서 걸었다. 그리고 엘리너는 적어도 그를 거들어 주지는 않겠다고 마음먹은 채 그의 곁에서 걸었다.\n\n“아, 나로서는!” 마침내 그가 그녀에게라기보다 제 자신에게 말하듯 내뱉었다. “아, 나로서는! 이 플럼스테드의 산책길도 마음이 편안하기만 하다면야 넉넉히 즐거운 곳이겠으나, 그렇지 못하다면 옥스퍼드의 그 칙칙하고 죽은 돌들이 훨씬 나을 것입니다. 세인트 이월드도 그렇고요. 볼드 부인, 저는 이곳에 올 때 제 자신을 잘못 알았다는 생각이 들기 시작합니다. 로마 교회의 사제라면 이 모든 것을 면했겠지요. 오, 하늘에 계신 아버지시여, 저희에게 확실한 규범을 내려 주실 수 있다면 그것이 저희에게 얼마나 좋겠나이까.”\n\n“우리에게 확실한 규범이 없나요, 애러빈 씨?”\n\n“있지요, 있고말고요. ‘우리를 시험에 들게 하지 마시옵고 다만 악에서 구하시옵소서.’ 그러나 무엇이 시험입니까? 무엇이 악입니까? 이것이 악입니까, 이것이 시험입니까?”\n\n가엾은 애러빈 씨! 그의 그 깊고 참된 사랑은 도무지 밖으로 나오지 않았다. 그는 그것을 답을 요구하고 요청하는 평범한 말로 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다. 그는 곁에 선 여인에게 이렇게 말할 줄을 몰랐다. “당신이 그 다른 남자를 사랑하지 않고 그의 아내가 되지 않을 것이 사실이라면, 나를 사랑해 줄 수 있겠습니까, 내 아내가 되어 주겠습니까?” 이것이 그의 마음속에 있는 말이었으나, 그 숱한 한숨으로도 그것을 입술까지 끌어올릴 수가 없었다. 이 간단한 물음을 던질 힘을 얻을 수만 있다면 그는 무엇이든, 모든 것을 내주었을 것이나, 강단에서도 연단에서도 그토록 매끄럽던 혀가 지금은 제 마음의 소박한 바람을 나타낼 말 한마디를 찾아내지 못했다.\n\n그런데도 엘리너는 그가 노련한 난봉꾼의 우아한 유창함으로 제 열정을 고백하기라도 한 것처럼 그를 온전히 알아들었다. 여자의 본능으로 그녀는 그가 플럼스테드의 즐거움과 옥스퍼드의 돌들을 말할 때, 로마 교회 사제의 안전함과 시험의 숨은 위험을 넌지시 비칠 때, 그 마음의 굽이굽이를 하나하나 따라갔다. 그녀는 그것이 모두 사랑을 뜻한다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 제 곁의 이 남자가, 이 뛰어난 학자가, 이 노련한 웅변가가, 이 대단한 논쟁의 투사가, 제 마음이 이제 제 것이 아님을 말하려고 애쓰고 또 애쓰다 헛되이 무너지고 있음을 알았다.\n\n그녀는 이것을 알았고, 알면서 일종의 기쁨을 느꼈다. 그러면서도 그를 거들어 주려 하지는 않았다. 그는 그녀를 깊이 언짢게 했고 합당치 않게 대했으며, 그녀를 사랑하게 되었다는 점을 생각하면 더욱 합당치 않게 대한 것이었으니, 엘리너는 제 앙갚음을 놓아 버릴 수가 없었다. 그녀는 끝내 그의 사랑을 받아들일 것인지 아닌지를 스스로에게 묻지 않았다. 지금 자기가 그것을 기쁘게 알아차리고 있다는 것조차 스스로에게 인정하지 않았다. 그 순간 그것은 그녀의 마음을 건드리지 못했고, 그저 그녀의 자존심을 달래고 허영을 흡족하게 했을 뿐이다. 애러빈 씨는 감히 제 이름을 슬로프 씨의 이름과 엮었는데, 이제 그가 그것을 제 이름과 엮고 싶어 한다는 것을 알게 되자 그녀의 마음이 누그러진 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 향을 들이마시면서도 그 대가로 아무런 향기도 내주지 않은 채 그의 곁에서 걸었다.\n\n“이것 하나만 대답해 주십시오.” 애러빈 씨가 걸음을 뚝 멈추고 앞으로 나서서 동행과 마주 보고 서며 말했다. “이 한 가지 물음에만 대답해 주십시오. 부인은 슬로프 씨를 사랑하지 않으시지요? 그의 아내가 될 뜻이 없으시지요?”\n\n애러빈 씨는 엘리너 볼드 같은 여자의 마음을 얻는 데 결코 옳은 길로 가고 있지 않았다. 그녀의 노여움이 막 걷혀 가던 참에, 말해지지 않은 그의 사랑이 뿜는 참된 온기 앞에서 막 사라져 가던 참에, 그는 제가 처음 지은 죄를 참으로 부질없이 되풀이하여 그것을 도로 지펴 놓았다. 제가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알았더라면 그는 엘리너 볼드를 온전히 제 사람으로 만들기 전에는 그녀 앞에서 슬로프 씨의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 것이다. 그때가 되면, 그때에야 비로소 그는 제가 원하는 만큼 의기양양하게 슬로프 씨 이야기를 할 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n“그런 물음에는 대답하지 않겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “게다가 말씀드리자면, 무엇으로도 당신이 그런 물음을 하는 것을 정당화할 수는 없습니다. 안녕히 계세요!”\n\n그러고는 그녀는 도도하게 잔디밭을 가로질러 응접실 창을 통해 들어가, 식당에서 점심을 들고 있던 아버지와 언니 곁에 앉았다. 반 시간 뒤 그녀는 마차 안에 있었고, 그리하여 애러빈 씨를 다시 보지 않은 채 플럼스테드를 떠났다.\n\n교회 묘지를 그늘로 덮은 침침한 나무들 사이로 그의 산책은 길고 서글펐다. 그는 남의 눈을 피하려고 부주교의 땅을 벗어나, 한때 사랑에 빠졌던 숱한 젊은이들과 잊힌 미녀들이 잠들어 있는 푸른 봉분들 사이를 어슬렁거렸다. 그의 귀에 엘리너의 마지막 말은 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 조종처럼 울렸다. 그녀가 자기에게 화가 나 있고 분개해 있고 매정할 수 있으면서도 동시에 자기를 사랑할 수 있다는 것을 그는 이해하지 못했다. 슬로프 씨가 정말로 총애받는 연적인지 아닌지 그는 마음을 정하지 못했다. 그렇지 않다면 어찌하여 그녀가 그의 물음에 대답하지 않았단 말인가?\n\n가엾은 애러빈 씨, 못 배우고 무식하고 촌스럽고 아무것도 모르는 사람이여! 마흔 살이나 되어서 여자의 마음이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그토록 모른다니!\n\n# 제31장. 주교의 서재\n\n플럼스테드의 그 즐거운 모임은 이렇게 해서 흩어졌다. 모두가 서로 기분 좋게 지내는 동안에는 참으로 즐거운 모임이었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 자기 집이 오랜만에 한결 흥겹고 환해졌다고 느꼈고, 부주교는 그 한 달이 유쾌하게 지나갔다는 것을 알고 있었으되 그 유쾌함을 제 접대 솜씨 말고 다른 어떤 특별한 미덕에 돌리지는 않았다. 엘리너가 떠난 지 사나흘 안에 하딩 씨도 돌아갔고, 애러빈 씨는 세인트 이월드 목사관에 자리를 잡기 전에 한 주를 보내려고 옥스퍼드로 떠났다. 그는 바체스터 주교관에서 벌어지는 일들의 부당함과, 슬로프 씨의 불길한 편지에 담긴 장담에도 불구하고 요양원이 여전히 처해 있다고들 믿는 위태로움에 관하여, 그윈 박사에게 전할 말을 잔뜩 짊어지고 갔다.\n\n엘리너는 마차로 바체스터까지 오는 동안 애러빈 씨를 두고 곰곰이 생각할 겨를이 별로 없었다. 언니와 이야기를 나누며 사이좋게 헤어지는 모양새를 지켜야 했던 탓에, 그의 죄과에서도 그의 사랑에서도 억지로 마음을 돌려놓아야 했던 것이다. 마차가 제 집 문 앞에 닿아 언니와 조카들에게 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 있는 참에, 메리 볼드가 달려 나오며 외쳤다.\n\n“아, 엘리너, 소식 들었어요? 아, 그랜틀리 부인, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨어요? 가엾은 수석 사제님!”\n\n“세상에!” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “대체 무슨, 무슨 일이 있었다는 게냐?”\n\n“오늘 아침 아홉 시에 졸중을 일으키셨는데, 그 뒤로 한마디도 못 하고 계세요. 지금쯤이면 이미 세상을 떠나셨을까 봐 몹시 걱정스러워요.”\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 수석 사제와 아주 가깝게 지내던 터라 크게 충격을 받았다. 엘리너는 그만큼 잘 아는 사이가 아니었으나, 그래도 그 인품과 몸가짐을 웬만큼은 알고 있었던지라 방금 들은 소식에 놀라고 슬퍼하기는 마찬가지였다. “나는 지금 곧장 수석사제관으로 가 봐야겠다.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “부주교도 분명 거기 있을 게다. 너희에게 보낼 소식이 있으면 토머스가 시내를 떠나기 전에 들르게 하마.” 그러고서 마차는 떠나갔고, 엘리너와 아기는 메리 볼드와 함께 남았다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인의 짐작은 옳았다. 부주교는 수석사제관에 있었다. 그는 그날 아침 혼자서 바체스터로 들어왔는데, 엘리너에게 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않았던 까닭이었고, 그 역시 도착하자마자 수석 사제가 쓰러졌다는 소식을 들었다. 앞서 말한 대로 대성당과 수석 사제의 집을 이어 주는 서재 겸 열람실이 하나 있었다. 이곳은 대개 주교의 서재라고 불렸는데, 바체스터의 어느 주교가 대성당에 이 방을 덧붙였다고 여겨지기 때문이었다. 회랑의 한 부분 바로 위에 지어져 있었고, 계단 하나가 이 방에서 아래로 내려가 대성당 성직자들이 중백의를 입고 벗는 방으로 이어졌다. 또한 수석 사제의 집으로 곧장 통해 있었으므로, 그 어른이 공식 예배에 나갈 때 늘 지나다니는 통로이기도 했다. 누구에게 이곳에 들어올 권리가 있고 누구에게 없는지는 말하기 어려운 노릇이었으나, 바체스터 사람들은 이 방이 수석 사제의 것이라고 믿었고, 바체스터의 성직자들은 이 방이 참사회의 것이라고 믿었다.\n\n문제의 그날 아침, 참사회를 이루는 상주 성직자 대부분과 그 밖의 몇몇이 이곳에 모여 있었고, 그 가운데서 여느 때처럼 부주교가 드높은 권위로 우뚝했다. 그는 시내로 들어오는 다리를 채 건너기도 전에 수석 사제가 쓰러졌다는 소식을 듣고는, 곧장 이 이름난 성직자들의 회합 장소로 온 것이었다. 열한 시부터 와 있었고 그 뒤로 줄곧 자리를 지켰다. 이따금 불려 온 의사들이 수석사제관 쪽에서 서재로 건너와 짤막한 소식을 전하고는 다시 돌아가곤 했다. 노인이 되살아날 가망은 거의 없어 보였고, 완전한 회복 같은 것은 실로 가망이 전혀 없었다. 문제는 오직, 첫 중한 발작에 쓰러져 말도 못 하고 의식도 없는 채 이대로 곧 숨을 거둘 것인가, 아니면 의술의 힘을 제대로 빌린 덕분에 제 처지를 알아볼 만큼은 이 세상으로 되돌아와, 심판대 앞에서 그분과 얼굴을 마주하도록 부름받기 전에 창조주께 기도 한마디를 올릴 수 있게 될 것인가 하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n오미크론 파이 경을 부르러 런던으로 사람을 보내 두었다. 그 위대한 인물은 선량한 노 그랜틀리 주교 때에 늙은이의 심장 속에서 목숨을 계속 움직이게 하는 데 놀라운 재간을 보여 준 바 있으니, 수석 사제를 두고도 똑같이 성공하리라고 기대해 봄 직했다. 그러는 동안 필그레이브 박사와 리어차일드 씨가 최선을 다하고 있었고, 가엾은 트리폴 양은 아버지의 머리맡에 앉아, 그런 처지의 딸들이 늘 그러하듯 제 사랑을 드러낼 무슨 일이든 하게 해 주기를 애타게 바라고 있었다. 제 손으로 아버지의 발을 문질러 드리는 일이든, 그 위세 등등한 의사들의 잔심부름을 하는 일이든, 이 절박한 때에 조금이라도 쓸모가 될 수만 있다면 무엇이든 좋았다.\n\n곁을 지키던 성직자들 가운데 부주교만이 잠깐 병자의 방에 들어가는 것을 허락받았다. 그는 삐걱대는 구두를 신은 채 살금살금 들어가, 슬픔에 잠긴 딸에게 낮게 죽인 목소리로 위로의 말을 한마디 건네고는, 일그러진 옛 벗의 얼굴을 엄숙하면서도 열심히 뜯어보는 눈길로 바라보았는데, 마음속으로는 마치 “언젠가는 나도 아마 이렇게 되겠지” 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러고 나서 의사들에게 뜻 모를 말을 한두 마디 속삭이고는 다시 삐걱삐걱 서재로 걸어 나왔다.\n\n“다시는 말을 못 하실 것 같소.” 부주교가 소리 나지 않게 문을 닫으며 말했다. 감각이라고는 죄다 달아나 버린 그 의식 없는 죽어 가는 이가, 저 멀찍한 방에서 지금 이토록 조심스레 다루어지는 자물쇠의 용수철 소리를 알아듣기라도 할 것처럼 말이다.\n\n“저런! 저런! 그렇게 나쁘단 말이오?” 야윈 자그마한 참사회원이 말하면서, 마음속으로는 수석사제직에 오를 만한 후보들을 하나하나 헤아려 보고 부주교가 그 자리를 받을 만하다고 여길지 궁금해했다. “발작이 어지간히 심했던 모양이오.”\n\n“일흔이 넘은 사람이 졸중을 맞으면 좀처럼 가볍게 지나가는 법이 없지요.” 몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“더할 나위 없이 훌륭하고 성품이 온화한 어른이셨소.” 성가대 사제 하나가 말했다. “그 빈자리를 무엇으로 메울지는 하늘이나 아실 일이오.”\n\n“참으로 그러셨소.” 하급 참사원이 말했다. “우리 대성당 예배에 몫을 나눠 받는 영광을 누린 모든 이에게 크나큰 축복이셨지요. 후임은 정부에서 임명하겠지요, 부주교님. 부디 낯선 사람이 오지 않기만을 바랄 뿐이오.”\n\n“후임 이야기는 하지 맙시다.” 부주교가 말했다. “아직 가망이 남아 있는 마당에.”\n\n“아, 그럼요, 물론이지요.” 하급 참사원이 말했다. “그런 이야기는 지극히 온당치 못한 노릇이오. 하지만…”\n\n“내가 아는 한,” 야윈 자그마한 참사회원이 말했다. “지금 정부와 슬로프 씨보다 더 두터운 연줄을 가진 사람은 없소.”\n\n“슬로프 씨라니.” 두세 사람이 거의 소리를 죽인 채 한꺼번에 말했다. “슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니!”\n\n“쳇!” 몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 내뱉었다.\n\n“주교는 그 사람을 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 할 게요.” 자그마한 참사회원이 말했다.\n\n“프라우디 부인도 마찬가지고.” 성가대 사제가 말했다.\n\n“쳇!” 학무 참사원이 말했다.\n\n부주교는 그 말에 하마터면 낯빛이 창백해질 뻔했다. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 된다면? 물론 그런 신성모독이 검토될 수 있으리라고 짐작할 만한 충분한 근거는 없었고, 실은 근거라 할 것이 아예 없었다. 그렇지만 그럼에도 있을 법한 일이기는 했다. 프라우디 박사는 정부에 연줄이 있었고, 그 사내는 이를테면 프라우디 박사를 제 주머니에 넣고 다녔다. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 된다면 다들 어떻게 처신해야 한단 말인가? 그 발상만으로도 그랜틀리 박사조차 한동안 말문이 막혔다.\n\n“슬로프 씨를 수석사제관에 두게 된다면 우리로서는 그리 유쾌한 노릇은 아니겠지요.” 자그마한 참사회원이 제 억측이 불러일으킨 뚜렷한 낭패를 보며 속으로 낄낄거리면서 말했다.\n\n“그대를 주교관에 들이는 것만큼이나 유쾌하고, 또 그만큼이나 있을 법한 일이겠소.” 학무 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“나는 그런 임명은 지극히 있을 법하지 않다고 보오.” 하급 참사원이 말했다. “게다가 더없이 사려 깊지 못한 처사이기도 하고. 부주교님께서도 그리 생각하지 않으시오?”\n\n“나로서는 그런 일은 아예 논할 거리도 못 된다고 봅니다.” 부주교가 말했다. “하지만 지금 이 순간 내가 생각하고 있는 것은 슬로프 씨가 아니라, 바로 저 가까이에 누워 계신 우리의 가엾은 벗이오.”\n\n“물론이지요, 물론이오.” 성가대 사제가 자못 엄숙한 낯빛으로 말했다. “물론 그러시겠지요. 우리도 다들 그렇소. 가엾은 트리폴 박사님, 더없이 훌륭한 분이신데, 하지만…”\n\n“영국에서 가장 안락한 수석 사제 사택이지요.” 또 다른 참사회원이 말했다. “대지가 15에이커요. 웬만한 주교관보다 낫소.”\n\n“게다가 꼬박 연 2,000파운드고.” 야윈 박사가 말했다.\n\n“1,200파운드로 깎였소.” 학무 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“아니오.” 두 번째 참사회원이 말했다. “1,500으로 정해질 게요. 특례가 만들어졌소.”\n\n“그런 일 없소.” 학무 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“내 말이 맞다는 걸 알게 될 게요.” 참사회원이 말했다.\n\n“나도 분명 보고서에서 그렇게 읽었소.” 하급 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“당치 않은 소리.” 학무 참사원이 말했다. “그렇게는 못 하오. 런던과 더럼 말고는 예외를 두지 않기로 되어 있었소.”\n\n“캔터베리와 요크도 있지요.” 성가대 사제가 조심스레 말했다.\n\n“그대는 어떻게 보시오, 그랜틀리?” 야윈 자그마한 박사가 말했다.\n\n“무엇을 말이오?” 부주교가 말했다. 그는 벗인 수석 사제를 생각하고 있는 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었으나, 실은 슬로프 씨를 생각하고 있던 참이었다.\n\n“다음 수석 사제는 얼마를 받게 되느냐 말이오, 1,200이오, 1,500이오?”\n\n“1,200이오.” 부주교가 권위 있게 말했고, 그리하여 적어도 그 문제에 관한 한 아랫사람들 사이의 온갖 의심과 다툼에 단번에 종지부를 찍었다.\n\n“글쎄, 나는 분명 1,500인 줄로 알았는데.” 하급 참사원이 말했다.\n\n“쳇!” 몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 말했다. 바로 그때 문이 열리며 필그레이브 박사가 들어왔다.\n\n“어떠십니까?” “의식은 돌아오셨습니까?” “말씀은 하실 수 있습니까?” “설마 돌아가신 건 아니겠지요?” “더 나쁜 소식은 아니겠지요, 박사님?” “뭔가 좀 나아졌기를 바랍니다만, 박사님?” 예닐곱 목소리가 저마다 더없이 애타는 어조로 한꺼번에 물었다. 이 선량한 노 수석 사제가 휘하 성직자들 사이에서 얼마나 인망이 두터웠는지를 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다.\n\n“변화는 없습니다, 여러분. 조금도 변화가 없습니다. 다만 전보가 한 통 왔습니다. 오미크론 파이 경이 밤 9시 15분 기차로 이곳에 닿을 것입니다. 누구든 손을 쓸 수 있는 사람이 있다면 오미크론 파이 경이 해낼 것입니다. 하지만 의술로 할 수 있는 일은 이미 다 해 놓았습니다.”\n\n“저희도 그 점은 잘 알고 있습니다, 필그레이브 박사님.” 부주교가 말했다. “그 점은 조금도 의심하지 않습니다. 하지만 그래도 아시다시피…”\n\n“아, 지당하십니다.” 박사가 말했다. “지당하십니다. 저라도 똑같이 했을 것입니다. 제가 곧바로 그렇게 하시라 권했지요. 이만한 목숨이고 이만한 어른이시니 오미크론 경을 모셔 와야 한다고 리어차일드에게 그 자리에서 말했습니다. 물론 비용 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었고요. 워낙 이름 높으신 분이고, 워낙 인망이 두터우신 분이니 말입니다. 그렇지만 사람의 의술로 할 수 있는 일은 이미 다 해 놓았습니다.”\n\n바로 이 무렵 그랜틀리 부인의 마차가 대성당 경내로 들어왔고, 부주교는 아래로 내려가 그녀가 앞서 들었던 소식을 확인해 주었다.\n\n밤 9시 15분 기차로 오미크론 파이 경은 정말로 도착했다. 그리고 밤이 깊어 가는 동안 가엾은 노 수석 사제에게 일종의 의식이 돌아왔다. 이것이 오미크론 파이 경 덕분이었는지는 견해를 내놓지 않는 편이 좋을 문제다. 필그레이브 박사는 제 나름으로 아주 분명한 생각을 지니고 있었으나, 오미크론 경 본인은 그 학식 높은 박사와 견해를 달리했다고 전해진다. 어쨌거나 오미크론 경은 수석 사제가 아직 며칠은 더 살 것이라는 소견을 밝혔다.\n\n그리하여 그 뒤 여드레에서 열흘 동안 가엾은 수석 사제는 반쯤 의식이 있고 반쯤 혼수에 빠진 그 상태 그대로였고, 곁을 지키던 성직자들은 앞으로 몇 달 동안은 새로 임명할 일이 없으리라고 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n# 제32장. 성직 영예를 노리는 새 후보\n\n수석 사제의 병환은 수석사제관과 그에 딸린 도서관 말고도 여러 곳에서 적잖은 심란함을 불러일으켰고, 그 여윈 자그마한 참사회원이 슬로프 씨를 두고 떠올린 생각을 떠올린 사람은 그 하나만이 아니었다.\n\n수석 사제가 병으로 쓰러졌다는 소식이 주교에게 닿았을 때 주교는 서재에 맥없이 앉아 있었다. 소식을 가져온 것은 슬로프 씨였는데, 물론 그는 바체스터에서 그 소식을 맨 나중에 들은 사람이 아니었다. 소식은 프라우디 부인의 귀에도 더디게 들어가지 않았다. 주교의 복종을 서로 차지하겠다고 다투는 이 두 사람 사이에 그 무렵 그리 다정한 왕래가 없었으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남는다. 실제로 두 사람은 한집에 살면서도 전날 주교의 서재에서 벌어진 그 험악한 담판 이후로 얼굴을 마주한 적이 없었다.\n\n그 자리에서 프라우디 부인은 패배했다. 늘 이겨 왔다는 위광이 제 군기에서 뜯겨 나갔다는 것은 그 호전적인 부인에게 크나큰 슬픔이었다. 그러나 패배했을 뿐 굴복한 것은 아니었다. 부인은 잃은 땅을 아직 되찾을 수 있다고, 제가 주워 올린 그 티끌 속으로 슬로프 씨를 다시 내던질 수 있다고, 그리고 죄를 지은 남편으로 하여금 굵은 베옷을 입고 재를 뒤집어쓴 채 용서를 빌게 만들 수 있다고 여겼다.\n\n그 잊지 못할 날, 부인의 지엄한 분부에 대들어 반란을 일으켰다는 점에서 잊지 못할 그날, 주교는 강경하게 제 뜻을 관철했고, 정말로 제 노예살이도 이제 날수가 얼마 남지 않았을지 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다. 이제 자유의 땅으로 들어서려는 참이라고, 제 손으로 들이켤 수 있는 젖이 흐르고 눈으로만 보라며 애를 태우지는 않을 꿀이 흐르는 그 감미로운 땅으로 들어서려는 참이라고 기대하기 시작했다. 프라우디 부인이 방을 나서며 문을 쾅 닫았을 때 주교는 스스로가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 주교라고 느꼈다. 물론 그 뒤에 이어진 전속 사제의 훈계에 기가 조금 죽기는 했으나, 대체로 주교는 제 자신이 몹시 흡족했고 최악은 지나갔다고 자위했다. “Ce n'est que le premier pas qui coûte.” 그는 그렇게 되뇌었다. 첫걸음을 그토록 통 크게 내디딘 이상 나머지는 수월하게 따라오리라는 것이었다.\n\n주교는 으레 그렇듯 만찬 자리에서 아내와 마주쳤는데, 그 자리에서는 주교의 행복을 어지럽힐 만한 말이 거의, 아니 전혀 오가지 않았다. 딸들과 하인들이 자리에 있어 주교를 지켜 주었다.\n\n주교는 대주교를 찾아가기로 한 계획을 두고 사소한 말을 한두 마디 던졌는데, 이는 제 뜻대로 하겠다는 것을 관계된 모두에게 보이기 위함이었다. 하인들까지도 그 변화를 알아채고 안주인에게 바치던 공경을 조금쯤 바깥주인 쪽으로 옮겼다. 바깥주인은 그것을 다 알아보았고, 안주인 또한 그러했다. 그러나 프라우디 부인은 때를 기다렸다.\n\n만찬을 마치고 주교는 서재로 돌아갔고, 곧 슬로프 씨가 그리로 찾아와 둘이 함께 차를 마시며 여러 가지를 궁리했다. 몇 분 동안 주교는 정말로 행복했다. 그러나 벽난로 선반 위의 시계가 밤의 고요한 시각이 다가온다고 일러 주자, 침실용 촛대를 바라보며 저것을 써야만 한다는 것을 깨닫자, 주교의 가슴은 다시 내려앉았다. 그는 닭이 울면 이 위쪽 세상을 마음대로 떠도는 힘이 끝나 버리는 유령과도 같았다. 아니, 차라리 유령의 반대였다. 닭이 울 때까지 그는 다시 종살이를 해야 했으니 말이다. 그리고 그것으로 끝일까? 아침에 자유로운 몸으로 조반상에 내려올 수 있으리라고 스스로를 믿어도 좋을까?\n\n주교가 잠자리에 들려고 몸을 일으킨 것은 여느 때보다 한 시간이나 늦어서였다. 잠자리라니! 무슨 잠자리란 말인가? 어쨌든 그는 셰리를 두어 잔 마시고 계단을 올라갔다. 우리로서는 거기까지 그를 따라갈 생각이 없다. 어떤 소설가도, 어떤 역사가도 손대서는 안 될 일이 있는 법이며, 인생이라는 극에는 어떤 시인도 감히 그려 내려 해서는 안 될 몇몇 장면이 있는 법이다. 이날 밤 프라우디 박사와 그 아내 사이에 오간 일도 그런 것들 가운데 하나로 알아 두면 되겠다.\n\n이튿날 아침 주교는 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 사람이 되어 내려왔다. 얼굴은 야위어 보였다. 수척해졌다고 해도 지나치지 않을 정도였다. 어느새 희끗해진 머리칼이 전날 저녁보다 눈에 띄게 더 세지 않았는지 의심스러울 지경이었다. 아무튼 그는 부쩍 늙었다. 세월이 사람을 늙게 만드는 것은 서서히, 고른 걸음으로 이루어지는 일이 아니다. 세상을 두루 살펴보고 언제나 그러한지 아닌지 보라. 다만 기쁨도 슬픔도 없이 초목처럼 살다 죽는 드문 경우만은 예외다. 사람은 혈색 좋고 젊고 활짝 핀 건강을 누리다가, 그 나이가 서른이든 마흔이든 쉰이든 상관없이 어느 날 살을 에는 서리가, 어떤 고통의 시절이 닥쳐와 육신의 결에서 물기를 앗아 가고, 그러면 그 정정하고 씩씩하던 사람이 노인 축에 들게 되는 것이다.\n\n주교는 내려와 혼자 조반을 들었다. 프라우디 부인은 몸이 편치 않아 침실에서 커피를 들었고 딸들이 거기서 시중을 들었다. 그는 혼자 조반을 먹고 나서, 제가 무엇을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 서재의 늘 앉던 자리로 갔다. 곧 있을 대주교 방문으로 마음을 달래 보려 했다. 그 일만큼은 제 자유로운 뜻으로 이루어 낸 것이니 오래 남을 승리로 그에게 남아 있었다. 그런데 어쩐 일인지, 막상 이루고 나니 그것이 그리 대수롭게 여겨지지 않았다. 대주교의 식탁에 자리를 잡도록 그를 부추긴 것은 야심이었는데, 그 야심이 이제 그의 안에서 아주 죽어 버린 것이다.\n\n그렇게 앉아 있는데 슬로프 씨가 숨이 턱에 닿아 안달하는 모습으로 나타났다.\n\n“주교님, 수석 사제께서 세상을 떠나셨습니다.”\n\n“맙소사!” 주교가 외쳤다. 그토록 슬프고 그토록 갑작스러운 소식에 무기력에서 화들짝 깨어난 것이다.\n\n“이미 돌아가셨거나 지금 돌아가시는 중입니다. 졸중 발작으로 쓰러지셨는데 조금의 가망도 없다고 합니다. 아니, 지금쯤이면 이미 세상에 계시지 않으리라는 것을 저는 의심하지 않습니다.”\n\n종이 울리고 하인들이 곧바로 알아보러 나갔다. 그날 오전에 주교는 전속 사제의 팔에 기대어 몸소 수석사제관 문 앞까지 찾아갔다. 프라우디 부인은 미스 트리폴에게 온갖 도움을 주겠다는 전갈을 보냈다. 프라우디 양들도 사람을 보냈으니, 주교관과 수석사제관 사이에는 어마어마한 동정이 오갔다. 어느 문의에나 돌아오는 대답은 한결같았다. 수석 사제는 그대로이고, 오미크론 파이 경이 밤 9시 15분 기차로 내려오기로 되어 있다는 것이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는, 다른 이들이 그러했듯이, 누가 새 수석 사제가 될 것인가를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했고, 다른 이들에게 그러했듯이 그에게도 자기 자신이 새 수석 사제가 될 수도 있지 않겠는가 하는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그리고 1,200이냐 1,500이냐 2,000이냐 하는 문제가, 대성당 도서관에 있던 다른 성직자들의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔던 것과 똑같이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다.\n\n2,000이든 1,500이든 1,200이든, 손에 넣기만 한다면 그에게는 어느 쪽이든 틀림없이 대단한 일이었다. 탐욕이 채워지는 것보다 야심이 채워지는 쪽이 더 흡족할 터였다. 부주교의 본거지인 그 대성당 도시에서 부주교를 내려다보고, 참사회원과 참사원들 위에 앉아, 대성당 설교단과 대성당의 모든 예식을 통째로 제 마음대로 주무른다니 이 얼마나 영광스러운 일인가!\n\n그러나 이것은 바라기는 쉬워도 얻기는 어려운 일일 수 있었다. 그렇지만 슬로프 씨에게 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 방도가 아주 없는 것은 아니었고, 적어도 그는 발밑에 풀이 자라도록 내버려 두는 사람이 아니었다. 우선 그는 주교가 줄 수 있는 도움만큼은 기대해도 좋다고 생각했는데, 그것은 헛된 생각이 아니었다. 그는 제 후원자를 보는 눈을 곧바로 바꾸었다. 수석 사제가 되기만 하면 주교를 그 아내의 종살이로 도로 넘겨주리라 마음먹은 것이다. 그리고 주교로서도 훈수꾼 하나를 덜어 내는 것을 굳이 아쉬워하지는 않으리라고 보았다. 슬로프 씨는 권세 있는 다른 이들에게 제 이름을 알려 두는 일에도 얼마간 손을 써 두었다. 국립학교 총감독관이라는 사람이 하나 있었는데, 당시 정부 요인들의 총애를 각별히 받고 있다고들 하는 인물이었다. 슬로프 씨는 그와 서신을 주고받는 사이쯤은 그럭저럭 맺어 두었다. 그는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경에게라면 마음 놓고 부탁해도 되겠다고 여겼고, 니컬러스 경이 나서 주기만 한다면 그만한 성직 승진의 약조쯤은 청하기만 해도 얻어지리라 확신했다.\n\n게다가 그에게는 언론이 제 손안에 있었다. 적어도 그는 그렇다고 자부했다. 애러빈 씨와 벌인 그 논쟁들에서 『데일리 주피터』는 아주 철저하게 그의 편을 들어 주었다. 그는 한 번 이상 그 신문사 직원 하나를 실제로 만난 적이 있었는데, 편집장은 아니라 해도 편집장이나 다름없는 사람이었다. 또 그는 오래전부터 온갖 교회의 폐단을 짚는 매서운 투고를 써서 제 이름 첫 글자로 서명해 그 편집자 친구에게 보내면서, 제 본명으로 서명한 사신을 함께 부치는 버릇이 있었다. 실제로 그와 타워스 씨는, 그가 연을 맺고 있던 그 세도 있는 언론인의 이름이 그러했는데, 대체로 서로 아주 살가운 사이였다. 슬로프 씨의 자잘한 글은 언제나 활자가 되었고 이따금 논평까지 얻었다. 그리하여 그는 소소하게나마 문필가로 이름이 났다. 이 공적인 생활은 그에게 크나큰 매력이 있었으나, 물론 그 나름의 폐단도 없지 않았다. 한번은 기자들이 있는 자리에서 말을 하다가 『주피터』가 떠받들고 칭송하며 맹세하던 그 특정한 노선을 함께 떠받들고 칭송하며 맹세하지 못한 일이 있었는데, 그러자 그는 옛 동지에게 무자비하게 난도질당하는 자신을 발견하고는 몹시 놀랐고 그 순간에는 적잖이 화도 났다. 그는 놀림감이 되고 흙탕물을 뒤집어쓰고 웃음거리가 되었다. 한결같은 벗이 아니라 한결같은 적이기라도 했던 것처럼, 아니 그보다 더 심하게 당했다. 『주피터』의 논객이 되기를 바라는 자는 제 개성을 죄다 내놓아야 한다는 것을 그는 그때까지 배우지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이 자그마한 매질이 결국 그와 벗 타워스 씨 사이의 뼈를 부러뜨리지는 않았다. 슬로프 씨는 『주피터』 같은 권세가에게 성을 내 보이기에는 세상 물정을 너무 잘 아는 사람이었다. 그는 자기를 때린 매에 입을 맞추었고, 이제는 그 보답을 바라도 좋으리라 여겼다. 그는 곧 비게 될 그 자리의 후보가 자기라는 것을 당장 타워스 씨에게 알리기로 마음먹었다. 근래에 한 자리 이상의 성직 승진이 『주피터』 지면에서 정부에 건넨 조언 그대로 주어진 바 있었다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨로서는 무엇보다 먼저 주교를 붙들어 두어야 했다. 그는 특히 대주교 방문이 이루어지기 전에 그 일을 해 두어야 한다고 느꼈다. 하필 이 절묘한 때에 수석 사제가 쓰러진 것은 참으로 하늘이 도운 일이었다. 프라우디 박사가 이 일을 열심히 떠맡도록 부추길 수만 있다면, 대주교의 관저에 머무는 동안 상당한 일을 해낼 수도 있었다. 이 점을 아주 절실하게 느낀 슬로프 씨는 바로 그날 오후에 주교의 속을 떠보기로 마음먹었다. 주교는 이튿날 아침 런던으로 떠날 참이었으니, 잠시도 마음 놓고 지체할 여유가 없었다.\n\n그는 다섯 시쯤 주교의 서재로 들어갔고, 주교가 여전히 혼자 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 수석 사제의 집 문 앞까지 걸어갔던 그 잠깐의 소동 이후로 거의 움직이지도 않은 듯싶었다. 얼굴에는 반쯤 넋이 나간 고통의 그 흐리고 죽은 빛이 그대로 서려 있었다. 그날 두 번째로 슬로프 씨가 방에 들어섰을 때, 주교는 아무것도 하지 않고 아무것도 읽지 않고 아무것도 생각하지 않은 채 그저 허공을 바라보고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n“왜 그러나, 슬로프.” 주교가 다소 짜증스레 말했다. 실은 그로서는 지금 슬로프 씨와 길게 이야기를 나누고 싶지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n“애석하게도 가엾은 수석 사제께서 아직 차도를 보이지 않으신다고 하니, 주교님께서 마음이 아프시겠습니다.”\n\n“오, 아, 그런가? 딱한 사람 같으니! 나도 정말 안타깝네. 오미크론 경은 아직 도착하지 않았겠지?”\n\n“예, 밤 9시 15분 기차라야 도착하십니다.”\n\n“특별 열차를 내지 않은 것이 이상하군. 트리폴 박사가 아주 부자라던데.”\n\n“대단한 부자시지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “그러나 사실을 말씀드리자면 런던의 의사들이 다 온들 아무 소용이 없습니다. 할 수 있는 조치는 모두 취했다는 것을 보여 주는 것 말고는요. 가엾은 트리폴 박사께서는 이 세상에 오래 계시지 못할 것입니다, 주교님.”\n\n“그렇겠지, 그렇겠지.”\n\n“예, 그렇습니다. 실은 아무리 가까운 벗이라도 그분이 이런 충격을 겪고 나서까지 살아 계시기를 바랄 수는 없을 것입니다. 지력이 도저히 온전할 수 없을 테니까요.”\n\n“딱한 사람! 딱한 사람!” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“뒤를 누가 잇느냐 하는 것은 당연히 주교님께 대단히 중요한 문제입니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “긴요한 문제에서 주교님과 생각을 같이하는 분에게 그 자리가 돌아가도록 해 두실 수 있다면 크나큰 일이 될 것입니다. 저희에게 적대적인 쪽이 여기 바체스터에서는 아주 강합니다. 지나치게 강합니다.”\n\n“그렇지, 그래. 가엾은 트리폴 박사가 가야 한다면, 그 자리에 좋은 사람을 앉히는 것이 큰일이지.”\n\n“주교님과 손발을 맞출 수 있는 사람을 얻으신다면 그야말로 전부를 얻으시는 셈입니다. 그랜틀리 박사나 하이앤드리 박사, 혹은 그런 부류의 누군가가 그 자리를 차지한다면 저희가 얼마나 곤란해질지 생각해 보십시오.”\n\n“—— 경께서 그 학파의 누군가에게 그 자리를 주실 것 같지는 않네. 무엇 하러 그러시겠나?”\n\n“예. 그럴 법하지는 않지요. 분명 그렇습니다. 그러나 있을 수는 있는 일입니다. 아마 대단한 청탁들이 오갈 것입니다. 감히 주교님께 말씀을 올려도 된다면, 다음 주에 대주교 각하와 이 일을 의논해 보시라고 권하고 싶습니다. 주교님의 뜻이 알려지고 각하께서 밀어 주신다면 —— 경께 결정적인 힘이 되리라는 것을 저는 의심하지 않습니다.”\n\n“글쎄, 그건 모를 일이지. —— 경은 늘 내게 아주 친절했네, 아주 친절했지. 하지만 청을 받지도 않았는데 그런 일에 끼어들기는 내키지 않아. 그리고 설령 청을 받는다 해도, 지금 이 순간 누구를 천거해야 할지 나는 모르겠네.”\n\n슬로프 씨는, 그 슬로프 씨조차도 이때만은 다소 머쓱해졌다. 제 자그마한 청을 어떻게 하면 충분히 겸손한 말에 담아낼 수 있을지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그는 이 뻔뻔한 청으로 처음에는 주교를 놀라게 할 수밖에 없다는 것을 스스로 알고 인정하고 있었으므로, 그 놀라움을 제 능란함과 언변으로 어떻게 가장 잘 수습하느냐가 문제였다. “저로서는 과연 주교님께서 당장 마음에 두신 분이 계실지 의심스러웠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “그래서 제가 혼자 이리저리 굴려 보던 생각을 감히 여쭙는 것입니다. 가엾은 트리폴 박사께서 정녕 가셔야 한다면, 주교님께서 도와주시기만 한다면, 그 자리를 제가 맡지 못할 까닭이 무엇인지 저로서는 정말 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“자네가!” 주교가 외쳤는데, 슬로프 씨로서는 그 말투를 도저히 칭찬으로 여길 수 없었다.\n\n이제 얼음이 깨졌고, 슬로프 씨는 제법 말이 술술 나왔다. “저는 그 자리를 노려 볼까 생각해 왔습니다. 주교님께서 대주교께 이 일을 밀어붙여 주신다면 제가 성공하리라는 것을 의심하지 않습니다. 보십시오, 제가 가장 먼저 움직이는 셈이 되는데 이것이 대단한 일입니다. 그리고 저는 언론의 도움도 기대할 수 있습니다. 제 이름은 지금 정부에 가장 영향력이 큰 그 언론 쪽에 알려져 있고, 다소 호의적으로 알려져 있다고 말씀드려도 좋을 것입니다. 정부 안에도 제게는 벗이 있습니다. 그러나 그럼에도 제가 도움을 바라보는 곳은 바로 주교님이십니다. 제가 가장 기꺼이 이 은혜를 받고자 하는 것은 주교님의 손에서입니다. 그리고 이런 일에서 언제나 으뜸으로 헤아려야 할 바이겠습니다만, 제게 어떤 자질이 있는지는 다른 어느 누구보다 주교님께서 잘 아실 것입니다.”\n\n주교는 한동안 어안이 벙벙해져 앉아 있었다. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제라니! 그런 인물의 탈바꿈은 주교 혼자의 머리로는 결코 떠오르지 않았을 생각이었다. 처음에 주교는 어째서, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 근거로 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어야 하는지를 따져 보았다. 그러나 차츰 생각의 방향이 바뀌어, 어째서, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 근거로 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어서는 안 되는지를 따지기 시작했다. 주교 자신으로 말하자면 전속 사제의 시중쯤은 없어도 그만이었다. 슬로프 씨를 아내에 맞서는 저울추로 쓴다는 그 자그마한 생각은 이미 거의 증발해 버렸다. 그 계책이 부질없다는 것을 그는 사실상 인정한 터였다. 정말이지 아내의 침실이 아니라 전속 사제의 침실에서 잘 수만 있다면 또 모를 일이었다. 그러나ㅡ. 그리하여 슬로프 씨가 말하는 동안 주교는 저 사람이 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어도 온당치 못할 것은 없겠다는 생각에 이르렀다. 슬로프 씨의 언변에 움직인 것은 물론 아니었으니, 주교는 그 말의 흐름을 따라가지도 않았다. 그저 제 궁리에 이끌려 그리로 간 것이다.\n\n“새삼 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠습니다만,” 슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “대성당에 관한 모든 일에서 되도록 주교님의 뜻에 맞추어 처리하는 것이 저의 으뜸가는 바람일 것입니다. 저는 주교님을 워낙 잘 알고 있으며, 주교님께서도 저를 그만큼 아셔서 같은 마음이시기를 바랍니다만, 그래서 제가 그 자리에 있게 되면 주교님의 마음이 한결 편해지고 주교님의 유익한 영향력이 미치는 범위도 넓어지리라 확신합니다. 앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 같은 교구의 고위 성직자들 사이에 의견이 하나뿐인 것이 가장 바람직합니다. 주교와 크게 어긋날 수밖에 없는 교구라면 저는 그런 자리를 과연 받아들일지 심히 의심스럽습니다. 이 경우에는 참으로 흐뭇한 의견의 일치가 있을 것입니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 주교가 제 말을 한마디도 따라오지 못한다는 것을 아주 잘 알아차렸으나, 그래도 계속 지껄였다. 프라우디 박사가 놀란 마음을 추스를 시간이 필요하다는 것을 알았고, 또 논변에 설득당한 것처럼 보일 기회를 주어야 한다는 것도 알았기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 말을 이어 가며 온갖 그럴듯한 이유를 늘어놓았는데, 하나같이 이 세상 누구도 자기만큼 훌륭한 바체스터 수석 사제가 될 수 없다는 것, 자기 슬로프가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되기를 정부도 대중도 반드시 한마음으로 바라리라는 것, 그러면서도 교회 정치의 드높은 고려에서 볼 때 이 성직 승진이 교구 주교의 손을 거쳐 주어지는 것이 특별히 바람직하다는 것을 보이려는 이야기였다.\n\n“허나 그 일에서 내가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 정말 모르겠네.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“대주교께 말씀만 꺼내 주신다면, 그런 임명이 매우 바람직하다고 여기신다고, 교구의 분열을 끝내기 위해 그 일을 마음에 깊이 두고 계신다고 각하께 말씀해 주신다면, 그리고 늘 그러시듯 힘차게 그리해 주신다면, 각하께서 —— 경께 그 말씀을 전하겠노라 약조하시게 하는 데 아마 아무 어려움도 없으실 것입니다. 물론 제가 오로지 각하의 주선만으로 이 자리를 노리는 것은 아니라는 것, 각하께 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하시는 것까지는 아니라는 것, 실제로 그러하듯 제가 다른 경로로도 이 자리를 얻으리라 보신다는 것을 대주교께 알려 주십시오. 다만 각하께서 —— 경께 그런 처사에 찬성한다고 표해 주시기를 주교님께서 몹시 바라신다고 말입니다.”\n\n결국 주교는 시키는 대로 하겠노라 약속하고 말았다. 그렇다고 조건 없이 약속한 것은 아니었다. “그 요양원 말인데.” 주교가 의논 도중에 말했다. “내 평생 그렇게 애를 태운 적이 없네.” 이는 사실에 가까운 말이었다. “나를 만난 뒤로 하딩 씨에게 이야기를 하지는 않았겠지?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 그런 일이 없다고 제 후원자에게 다짐했다.\n\n“아, 그렇다면ㅡ아무래도 전체로 보아 퀴버풀에게 주는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이네. 반쯤은 약속이 된 셈이고, 식솔은 많은데 몹시 가난하지 않은가. 아무래도 퀴버풀 씨 앞으로 임명장을 작성하는 편이 낫겠어.”\n\n“하지만 주교님,” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. 이 문제에서만큼은 제 뜻을 위해 싸워야 한다고 여전히 생각했고, 수석사제관 건에서 낭패를 보지 않으려면 근래 프라우디 부인에게서 얻어 낸 우위를 지켜야 한다는 것도 잊지 않았기 때문이다. “하지만 주교님, 저로서는 정말 몹시 염려스럽습니다ㅡ”\n\n“슬로프 씨, 기억해 두게.” 주교가 말했다. “가엾은 트리폴 박사의 뒤를 잇는 이 일에서 나는 자네에게 아무런 기대도 걸어 줄 수 없네. 자네가 바라니 대주교께 말씀은 분명히 드리겠네만, 아무래도 나는ㅡ”\n\n“알겠습니다, 주교님.” 슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 온전히 알아채고는 이번에는 제 쪽에서 말을 끊으며 말했다. “퀴버풀 씨 건은 아마 주교님 말씀이 옳으실 것입니다. 하딩 씨와는 제가 손쉽게 이야기를 매듭지을 수 있으리라 믿습니다. 분부하신 대로 주교님께서 서명하실 임명장을 작성해 두겠습니다.”\n\n“그래, 슬로프, 그게 가장 낫겠네. 그리고 자네 뜻을 밀어 주는 데 내가 할 수 있는 자잘한 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠으니 안심하게.”\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n이제 슬로프 씨에게는 처리할 일이 많았다. 시뇨라를 날마다 찾아가는 일이 있었다. 웬만한 분별이 있었다면 이제 그 일은 그만두어야 마땅했으나, 그는 홀려 있었고 웬만한 분별을 차릴 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 스탠호프 댁에서 차를 마시기로 마음먹었고, 그러고 나면 다시는 그리로 가지 않겠다고 마음먹었다. 아니, 마음먹었다고 스스로 여겼다. 볼드 부인과의 일도 매듭지어야 했다. 그가 보기에 엘리너는 전속 사제의 오두막을 빛내는 것 못지않게 수석사제관도 완벽하게 빛낼 것이었다. 게다가 그 무자비한 교회 재산 위원회가 수석 사제의 봉록을 헐어 내고 깎아 놓았을지도 모르니, 엘리너의 재산이 그것을 훌륭하게 메워 주리라고 생각했다.\n\n볼드 부인에 관해서라면 그의 희망은 이제 드높이 솟아 있었다. 슬로프 씨는 사랑에서는 무엇이든 정당하다고 여기는 그 숱한 구애자 무리 가운데 하나였으니, 볼드 부인의 하녀를 부리는 일도 마다하지 않았다. 그 하녀에게서 그는 플럼스테드에서 있었던 일을 많이 들었다. 정확히 사실 그대로는 아니었으니 그 “하녀”가 정확한 사실을 알아낼 수는 없었기 때문이나, 그래도 얼마쯤 사실과 닮은 이야기였다. 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인과 하딩 씨와 애러빈 씨가 “마님”이 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 이유로 모두 마님과 다투었다는 것, “마님”이 그 편지를 내놓기를 딱 잘라 거절했다는 것, 부주교가 마님에게 슬로프 씨와 그 편지를 버리든가 아니면 플럼스테드 목사관 식구들과의 왕래를 버리든가 하라고 했다는 것, 그러자 “마님”이 잔뜩 성이 나서 “플럼스테드 목사관 식구들과의 왕래 따위는 지푸라기만큼도 아깝지 않다”고, 그리고 그들 누구를 위해서도 슬로프 씨를 버리지 않겠다고 선언했다는 것이었다.\n\n이야기가 나온 출처를 생각하면 이것은 예상할 법한 것만큼 터무니없지는 않았다. 하인방에서 어떤 이야기가 오갔는지가 제법 뚜렷이 드러나는 이야기였다. 그리고 엘리너가 갑작스레 돌아왔다는 확실한 사실과 나란히 놓고 보니, 슬로프 씨에게는 이 이야기가 그 아리따운 미망인이 사람의 이치로 보아 십중팔구 제 청혼을 받아들이리라 여겨도 좋을 만큼 믿을 만하게 보였다.\n\n그러니 이 모든 일을 해내야 했다. 그가 보기에는 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원에 최종 임명되었다는 것이 알려지기 전에 청혼을 해 두는 것이 바람직했다. 엘리너에게 보낸 편지에서 그는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 맡게 되리라고 분명히 말해 두었던 것이다. 이것을 둘러대기란 몹시 어려운 일일 터였고, 그렇다고 엘리너에게 다시 편지를 써서 사실을 알리고 그 탓을 주교에게 돌린다면 자연히 그녀가 보는 그의 값이 떨어질 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 그 일은 저절로 드러나도록 내버려 두고, 한시도 지체하지 않고 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던지기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경과 타워스 씨의 도움을 청해야 했으므로, 주교 앞에서 물러나자마자 곧장 그 두 신사에게 보낼 편지를 짓기 시작했다. 슬로프 씨는 편지 쓰기의 명수로 통했으니, 그 편지들을 여기 온전히 옮겨 둔다.\n\n(친전) 바체스터 주교관, 185ㅡ년 9월\n\n그동안 저희 사이에 오간 교분을 생각하시어 이번 청을 주제넘은 참견으로 여기지 않아 주시기를 바랍니다. 가엾은 노인 트리폴 박사께서 졸중으로 쓰러지셨다는 소식은 아직 들으시지 못했으리라 짐작합니다. 바체스터의 모든 이에게 깊은 슬픔이 되는 일이니, 그분은 늘 훌륭한 분이셨기 때문입니다. 사람으로서도 성직자로서도 훌륭한 분이셨습니다. 다만 연세가 워낙 높으시니 어떤 경우에도 훨씬 더 오래 사시기는 어려웠을 것입니다. 아마 경께서도 그분을 아셨을 줄 압니다.\n\n회복의 가망은 도무지 없어 보입니다. 오미크론 파이 경이 지금 곁에 계신 줄로 압니다. 아무튼 이곳 의사들은 하루나 이틀이면 이승의 목숨줄이 다하리라고 단언했습니다. 그분의 영혼이 길이 안식하고 길이 복될 그 항구로 날아가기를 진심으로 빕니다.\n\n주교께서 이 성직 승진에 관하여 제게 말씀을 하셨는데, 그 자리가 제게 주어지기를 몹시 바라고 계십니다. 솔직히 제 나이에 그만한 승진을 감히 넘본다는 것이 저로서는 엄두가 나지 않습니다만, 주교님께서 하도 격려하시니 아무래도 그리하게 될 듯합니다. 주교님께서는 내일 ——로 떠나시며 대주교께 이 이야기를 꺼내실 작정이십니다.\n\n지금 정부에 대한 경의 무게가 얼마나 크고 또 얼마나 마땅한지 저는 잘 알고 있습니다. 교회의 성직 승진에 관한 일이라면 무엇이든 경의 말씀에는 당연히 귀를 기울일 것입니다. 이제 이 일이 제 머릿속에 들어온 이상 저로서는 당연히 성공하고 싶습니다. 경께서 좋은 말씀으로 도와주신다면 제게 은혜를 하나 더 베푸시는 것이 되겠습니다.\n\n한 가지 덧붙이는 편이 낫겠습니다. —— 경께서는 이 성직 자리가 비었다는 것을, 아니 비게 되리라는 것이 확실하다는 것을 아직 모르실 것입니다(가엾은 트리폴 박사께서는 이미 가망이 없으니 말입니다). —— 경께서 이 소식을 경에게서 먼저 들으신다면, 아마 경께서 의견을 말씀하실 정당한 자격이 있다고 여기실 것입니다.\n\n물론 저희의 큰 목표는 교회 일에서 모두가 한 의견이 되는 것입니다. 바체스터에서는 이것이 무엇보다 바람직하며, 저희 선량한 주교께서 이 일을 그토록 염려하시는 것도 그 때문입니다. 이 일을 —— 경께 말씀해 주시어 저를 도와주실 수 있게 된다면, 아마 이 점을 —— 경께 짚어 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여기실 것입니다.\n\n그럼 이만, 친애하는 니컬러스 경, 경의 가장 충실한 종으로부터,\n\n타워스 씨에게 보낸 편지는 사뭇 다른 가락으로 쓰였다. 슬로프 씨는 제가 붓을 든 두 사람의 성품과 처지의 차이를 완전히 꿰뚫고 있다고 여겼다. 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경 같은 사람에게는 얼마간의 입에 발린 말이 필요하며 그것도 손쉽고 흔해 빠진 종류면 된다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그리하여 니컬러스 경에게 보내는 편지는 currente calamo, 거의 힘들이지 않고 쓰였다. 그러나 타워스 씨 같은 사람에게는 효과가 있으면서도 비위를 거스르지 않는 편지, 지나치게 참견하지 않으면서도 뜻을 관철하는 편지를 쓰기가 그리 쉽지 않았다. 프라우디 박사나 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경에게 아첨하기는 어렵지 않았으나, 아첨을 아첨으로 드러내지 않으면서 타워스 씨에게 아첨하기란 몹시 어려웠다. 그러나 그렇게 해내야만 했다. 게다가 이 편지는 적어도 겉보기로는 힘들이지 않고 쓴 듯해야 하고, 술술 흐르고 거리낌이 없어야 하며, 쓴 사람에게 아무런 의심도 두려움도 없음을 드러내야 했다. 그리하여 타워스 씨에게 보내는 서한은 공을 들이고 다시 베끼고 다듬느라 어찌나 많은 시간을 잡아먹었는지, 슬로프 씨는 그날 저녁 옷을 갈아입고 스탠호프 박사 댁에 닿기에도 빠듯할 지경이었다.\n\n부쳐진 편지는 다음과 같았다.\n\n(친전) 바체스터, 185ㅡ년 9월\n\n(그는 “주교관”이라는 말은 일부러 빼놓았으니, 타워스 씨가 그것을 달갑게 여기지 않을지도 모른다고 생각한 까닭이었다. 어떤 대인물이 윈저 성에서 편지를 부쳤다 하여 크게 비난받은 일이 있었음을 그는 기억하고 있었다.)\n\n오늘 아침 이곳에서는 가엾은 노인 트리폴 수석 사제가 졸중으로 쓰러졌다는 소식에 다들 적잖이 놀랐소. 발작이 온 것은 오전 9시경이오. 우편 시간을 놓치지 않으려고 지금 쓰고 있는데, 그분은 아직 살아 있으나 살아날 가망도 여지도 전혀 없다고 보오. 오미크론 파이 경이 와 있거나 곧 올 것이나, 오미크론 경이라 한들 할 수 있는 일이라고는 손쓸 도리가 없다는 덜 이름난 동료들의 판결을 확인해 주는 것뿐이오. 가엾은 트리폴 박사가 무덤 이편에서 달릴 길은 이미 다 달렸소. 그대가 그분을 알았는지는 모르겠소. 선량하고 조용하고 자비로운 분이었고, 물론 구식이었소. 일흔이 넘은 성직자라면 누구나 그럴 수밖에 없듯이 말이오.\n\n그러나 내가 붓을 든 것은 단지 이런 소식을 전하려는 목적만은 아니오. 그대의 메르쿠리우스들 가운데 누군가가 이미 보고 듣고 그만큼은 알렸을 터이니 말이오. 나는 그대가 늘 그러하듯 지나간 일보다는 앞일을 염두에 두고 쓰는 것이오.\n\n트리폴 박사의 후임을 두고 이곳에는 벌써 소문이 자자하오. 앞으로 수석 사제가 될 만한 사람으로 거론되는 이들 가운데 이 몸도 결코 드물지 않게 입에 오르내리는 줄 아오. 요컨대 나는 그 자리를 노리고 있소. 프라우디 주교가 이 교구에 온 뒤로 내가 이곳에서 적잖이 애를 썼고 얼마간 성과도 없지 않았다고 말해도 좋으리라는 것을 그대도 아마 알 것이오. 그와 나는 교리 문제에서나 교회 규율 문제에서나 거의 언제나 의견이 같으며, 그리하여 나는 그의 심복 전속 사제로서 매우 많은 일을 내 손으로 처리해 왔소. 그러나 그대에게 털어놓거니와, 나에게는 어느 주교의 전속 사제로 머무는 것보다 더 높은 야심이 있소.\n\n지금 우리 수석 사제직만큼 정력이 절실한 자리도 없소. 우리의 저 거대한 대성당 조직 전체가 잠들도록 방치되어 왔소. 아니, 거의 죽어 무덤에 들어갈 채비를 하고 있소! 그러나 본디 뜻한 바대로 앞장서서 우리 모든 교구 성직자에게 본을 보이도록 운영되기만 한다면, 그것들이 얼마나 어마어마한 무게를 지닐 수 있겠소!\n\n이곳 주교는 내가 성공하기를 몹시 바라고 있소. 실로 그는 내일 대주교에게 이 일을 밀어붙이러 가오. 또한 정부의 가장 유력한 인사 가운데 적어도 한 사람의 지지도 기대할 수 있으리라 보오. 그러나 털어놓자면, 내가 그럴 만하다고 여겨진다면, 『주피터』의 지지야말로 다른 어떤 것보다 나를 흐뭇하게 할 것이오. 그 덕에 성공한다면 더 흐뭇할 것이고, 그런 지지를 받고도 실패한다 해도 역시 더 흐뭇할 것이오.\n\n사실 이제는 어떤 정부도 여론의 언론을 거스르며 교회의 높은 자리를 채울 엄두를 내지 못하는 때가 왔소. 귀족 주교와 명문 수석 사제의 시대는 지나갔고, 아무리 미천하게 태어난 성직자라도 그 근면과 재능과 인격이 대중의 뚜렷한 지지를 불러낼 만하다면 이제는 성공을 바랄 수 있소.\n\n지금 이 순간 우리는 이런 일에서 『주피터』가 내놓는 조언이 가장 큰 무게를 지니며 실로 대개 그대로 따라진다는 것을 다들 느끼고 있소. 그리고 우리는, 나와 나이도 처지도 비슷한 성직자들을 두고 하는 말이오만, 그것이 마땅히 그러해야 한다고도 느끼고 있소. 『주피터』보다 사심 없는 후견인은 없으며, 백성이 무엇을 바라는지를 그보다 더 속속들이 아는 후견인도 없소.\n\n그대와 연이 닿은 그 신문이 양심상 내게 줄 수 없는 지지를 내가 청하고 있다고 그대가 의심하지 않으리라 믿소. 내가 글을 쓰는 목적은 내가 그 자리의 후보라는 것을 그대에게 알리려는 데 있소. 내 뜻을 도울 수 있는지 없는지는 그대가 판단할 일이오. 『주피터』가 교회 정치에 관한 내 견해에 찬동한다고 믿지 않았다면(그렇게 믿을 만한 충분한 까닭이 있었소) 나는 물론 이런 일로 그대에게 편지를 쓰지 않았을 것이오.\n\n주교는 내가 그런 자리에 앉기에는 너무 젊다고 여겨질까 염려하오. 내 나이 서른여섯이오. 요즘 세상에 그런 점을 두고 망설일 필요가 있다고는 생각지 않소. 대중은 낡아 빠진 일꾼에 대한 애정을 잃었소. 언젠가 좋은 일을 해낼 만한 사람이라면 서른여섯에도 능히 해낼 만하오.\n\n그럼 이만, 그대의 충실한 벗, 오바디아 슬로프\n\nT. 타워스 귀하, —— 코트, 미들 템플.\n\n이렇게 애를 쓰고 나서 슬로프 씨는 편지를 부치고 그날 저녁의 나머지를 제 여인의 발치에서 보냈다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 이렇게 표를 구하는 방식을 두고 속임수를 썼다는 비난을 받을 것이다. 세 후원자 각각에게 낸 청원에서 그가 거짓말을 했다는 말이 나올 것이다. 그가 그리했음을 인정해야 한다고 나는 믿는다. 제 젊음을 두고 머뭇거리면서 동시에 자기가 너무 젊지 않다고 확신할 수는 없는 노릇이다. 주로 주교의 지지에 기대면서 동시에 주로 그 신문의 지지에도 기댈 수는 없는 노릇이다. 그는 주교가 대주교에게 이 일을 밀어붙이러 ——로 가리라고 생각하지 않았다. 표를 구함에 있어 슬로프 씨는 될 수 있는 한 거짓되었음을 인정해야만 한다.\n\n그러나 이런 일에 밝은 이들에게 물어보기로 하자. 그가 이런 자리에서 사람들이 으레 거짓된 것보다 더 거짓되었던가? 우리 영국 신사들은 거짓말이라는 이름을 몹시 싫어하지만, 서로의 말을 믿는 공인을 우리는 과연 얼마나 자주 보는가?\n\n# 제33장. 승리자 프라우디 부인\n\n다음 한 주는 바체스터에서 겉보기에는 몹시 평온하게 지나갔다. 그러나 이 도시 주민 가운데 더러는 그 마음이 거리만큼 평온하지 못했다. 가엾은 늙은 수석 사제는 오미크론 파이 경이 예언한 그대로 여전히 살아 있었으니, 필그레이브 박사에게는 그것이 몹시 놀라운 일이었고 더러는 못마땅한 일이기도 하리라고들 했다. 주교는 여전히 돌아오지 않고 있었다. 그는 런던에서 하루 이틀을 묵었고, 대주교 댁에도 애초 작정했던 것보다 오래 머물렀다. 슬로프 씨는 제가 보낸 두 통의 편지 어느 쪽에도 아직 한 줄의 답장을 받지 못했으나, 그 까닭만은 알아냈다. 니컬러스 경은 하일랜드에서 사슴을 몰거나 여왕을 수행하고 있었고, 지칠 줄 모르는 타워스 씨조차 가을 휴가를 훔쳐 내어 해마다 몽블랑에 오르는 무리 가운데 하나가 되어 있었다. 슬로프 씨는 그가 9월 마지막 날이나 되어야 돌아오리라는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n볼드 부인은 스탠호프 일가와 어울리는 일이 잦아졌고, 그럴수록 그들이 점점 더 좋아졌다. 누가 물었더라면 샬럿 스탠호프야말로 제 각별한 벗이라고 대답했을 터이고, 스스로도 그렇게 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 사실을 말하자면 버티도 그에 못지않게 좋아했다. 그를 연인으로 여긴다는 생각은, 덩치 크고 순한 개를 그런 눈으로 바라볼 생각만큼이나 머릿속에 없었다. 버티는 그와 아주 허물없는 사이가 되어 자잘한 말들을 건네었고, 여느 남자들의 말이며 이야기와는 사뭇 다른 자잘한 이야기들을 했다. 그러나 그런 일은 거의 언제나 누이들이 있는 자리에서 벌어졌고, 게다가 그 길고 비단결 같은 수염과 옅푸른 눈과 야릇한 옷차림을 한 그는 여느 남자들과 너무나 달랐다. 엘리너는 여태 누구에게도 허락한 적 없는 종류의 스스럼없음을 그에게 허락했으되, 그 위험은 조금도 알지 못했다. 한번은 제가 그를 버티라고 불렀음을 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔고, 바로 그날 샬럿의 부추김에 넘어가 그에게 몸으로 하는 짓궂은 장난을 걸려다가 겨우 때맞춰 제 처지를 떠올리고 그만두었다.\n\n이 모든 일에서 엘리너는 조금도 흠이 없었고, 버티 스탠호프 역시 죄가 있다고 하기는 어려웠다. 그러나 엘리너가 걸려든 스스럼없음은 하나하나 그 누이가 짜 놓은 것이었다. 샬럿은 제 판을 어떻게 놀려야 하는지 잘 알았고 인정사정없이 놀렸다. 제 오라비의 사람됨이 어떠한지는 누구보다 잘 알면서도, 젊은 미망인과 그 재산과 미망인의 아이 몫 돈까지 통째로 오라비에게 넘겨주고도 눈 하나 깜짝하지 않았을 것이다. 짐짓 꾸민 우정과 따스한 다정함으로, 샬럿은 엘리너를 제 오라비에게 바싹 붙들어 매어 설령 물러서고 싶어지더라도 물러설 수 없게 만들려 애썼다. 그러나 샬럿 스탠호프는 엘리너의 사람됨을 실은 조금도 알지 못했고, 세상에 그런 사람됨이 있다는 것조차 이해하지 못했다. 젊고 어여쁜 여자가 버티 스탠호프 같은 남자와 장난치고 허물없이 지내면서도 머릿속에 아무 생각이 없고 가슴속에 아무 감정이 없을 수 있다는 것을, 온 세상 앞에 내놓기 부끄러울 것이라고는 하나도 없을 수 있다는 것을 그는 헤아리지 못했다. 샬럿 스탠호프는 제 새 벗이 무엇으로도 경솔한 혼인에 끌어들일 수 없는 여자라는 것을, 조금이라도 온당치 못한 구석이 있음을 알아차렸다면 그 마음이 당장 뒷걸음질 쳤을 여자라는 것을 조금도 짐작하지 못했다.\n\n그렇지만 스탠호프 양에게는 저 자신과 제 아버지의 집을 볼드 부인에게 더없이 마음 편한 것으로 만들 만한 눈치가 있었다. 그 집 사람들에게는 뻣뻣함도 격식도 없었으니, 근래에 성직자들의 오만을 한꺼번에 잔뜩 들이켜야 했던 엘리너에게는 그것이 각별히 반가웠다. 엘리너는 그들과 체스를 두고, 그들과 거닐고, 그들과 차를 마셨다. 천문학을 공부하거나 공부하는 시늉을 했고, 운을 맞추어 이야기를 짓는 일이며 산문 비극을 우스운 시로 옮기고 우스운 이야기를 비극인 체하는 시로 옮기는 일을 거들었다. 저에게 그런 재주가 있는 줄은 여태 몰랐다. 지금 하고 있는 것 같은 일을 제가 할 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었다. 스탠호프 일가에게서 엘리너는 새로운 놀이와 소일거리를, 그 자체로는 나쁠 것이 없으면서도 몹시 마음을 끄는 새로운 즐길 거리를 찾아냈다.\n\n밝고 재기 있는 사람들은 어찌하여 그토록 자주 몹시 온당치 못하고, 결코 온당치 못한 법이 없는 사람들은 어찌하여 그토록 자주 따분하고 답답한지, 딱한 노릇이 아닌가? 그런데 샬럿 스탠호프는 언제나 밝았고 답답한 적이 없었으되, 그 온당함은 미덥지 못했다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 동안에도 엘리너는 애러빈 씨를 결코 잊지 않았고, 슬로프 씨 또한 잊지 않았다. 그는 애러빈 씨와 화가 난 채로 헤어졌다. 주제넘은 참견이라 여겨지는 그 일에 여전히 화가 나 있었으나, 그러면서도 그를 다시 만날 날을 기다렸고 그를 용서할 날도 기다렸다. 애러빈 씨가 뱉은 말들이 아직도 귓가에 울렸다. 사랑의 고백으로 뜻하고 한 말이 아니었다 하더라도 그 말이 곧 그가 저를 사랑한다는 뜻임을 엘리너는 알았고, 또 그가 언젠가 정말로 그런 고백을 한다면 저로서는 그것을 매정하게 받아들이지 않을지도 모른다고 느꼈다. 그는 여전히 애러빈 씨에게 화가 나 있었다. 몹시 화가 나 있었다. 어찌나 화가 났던지 그가 한 말과 한 짓을 떠올릴 때면 입술을 깨물고 발을 굴렀다. 그럼에도 그가 용서받았음을 알려 주고 싶어 애가 탔다. 다만 필요한 것은 그가 제 잘못을 스스로 인정하는 일뿐이었다.\n\n엘리너는 이달 마지막 날에 울라손에서 그를 만나기로 되어 있었다. 손 양은 잔디밭 조찬에 인근 온 고장을 초대해 두었다. 천막이 서고 활쏘기가 벌어지고, 숙녀들은 잔디밭에서 춤을 추고 시골 총각과 처녀들은 방목장에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있었다. 바이올린 악사와 피리 악사가 오고, 사내아이들의 달리기가 있고, 기어오를 장대와 뛰어넘을 물도랑과 얼굴을 들이밀고 우스꽝스레 찡그릴 말 목걸이도 마련되었으며(이 마지막 놀이는 진행을 맡은 이들이 보탠 것이지 손 양이 애초 순서에 넣은 것은 아니었다), 오랜 독서 끝에 손 양이 엘리자베스 여왕의 좋은 시절에 행해졌다고 확인할 수 있었던 놀이란 놀이는 모두 벌이기로 되어 있었다. 더 근래에 생겨난 것은 무엇이든 되도록 금하기로 했다. 다만 한 가지 일로 손 양은 몹시 마음이 언짢았다. 투우장을 두고 이리저리 궁리해 보았으나 도무지 방도를 찾을 수가 없었던 것이다. 잔인한 일이라면 무슨 일이 있어도 스스로 하지도, 남이 하도록 두지도 않을 사람이었다. 이웃 젊은이들을 즐겁게 하자고 황소를 괴롭히는 일을 부추긴다는 것은, 손 양이야말로 그런 생각을 할 사람이 결코 아니라고 굳이 말할 것도 없다. 그런데도 그 이름에는 어딘가 그토록 매혹적인 데가 있었다. 그러나 황소 없는 투우장이란 시대가 쇠락했음을 알리는 기념물에 지나지 않을 터라, 손 양은 그 생각을 접지 않을 수 없었다. 다만 퀸틴만은 기어이 갖추기로 하여 장대와 회전 축과 밀가루 자루를 그에 맞추어 마련해 두었다. 마상 창시합 비슷한 것을 조촐하게라도 벌이고 싶어 안달했을 법하나, 오라비에게 말한 대로 그것은 이미 시도해 본 바가 있었고, 이 시대는 앞선 시대들보다 너무나 확연히 못하다는 것이 드러나 그런 소일거리를 감당할 수 없었다. 손 씨는 누이의 그 아쉬움에 그다지 함께하는 기색이 없었으니, 아마도 사슬 갑옷을 온전히 한 벌 갖추어 입는다 한들 제 몸이 편안해질 것은 별로 없으리라 여긴 까닭이리라.\n\n울라손의 이 모임은 애초에 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드 목사관에 들어오는 것을 환영하는 자리로 계획된 것이었는데, 뒤에 일꾼들과 그 아내며 아이들을 위해 마련하려던 추수 잔치가 여기에 합쳐지면서 지금과 같은 규모로 커졌다. 플럼스테드 식구들도 물론 모두 초대를 받았고, 초대장이 왔을 무렵 엘리너는 언니와 함께 갈 작정이었다. 이제 계획이 바뀌어 스탠호프 일가와 함께 가게 되었다. 프라우디 내외도 오기로 되어 있었는데, 주교관으로 간 초대에 슬로프 씨는 들어 있지 않았으므로, 뻔뻔함이 한 번도 저를 저버린 적 없는 시뇨라가 손 양에게 그를 데려가도 좋겠느냐고 청했다.\n\n손 양은 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어 그 허락을 내주었으나, 애러빈 씨가 언짢아하지나 않을까 두려워 마음을 떨며 그리했다. 그가 돌아오자마자 손 양은 거의 눈물까지 글썽이며 사과했으니, 두 신사 사이에 그만큼 지독한 원한이 들끓고 있으리라 여겨졌던 것이다. 그러나 애러빈 씨는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨으로 슬로프 씨를 맞겠노라 다짐하여 손 양을 달래고는, 두 사람을 서로 소개해 주겠다는 약속까지 받아 냈다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨의 이 승리는 엘리너에게 그리 반갑지 않았다. 그는 바체스터로 돌아온 뒤로 그를 피하려 애써 온 터였다. 플럼스테드 사람들이 그토록 야박하게 저더러 그 역겨운 남자를 사랑한다고 몰아세울 때 엘리너는 물러서지 않았으나, 그럼에도 그런 말을 듣고 있는 처지인 만큼 그의 앞길에서 비켜서서 조금씩 그를 떼어 놓는 편이 낫다는 것쯤은 훤히 알고 있었다. 돌아온 뒤로 그를 만난 일은 거의 없었다. 하인에게는 찾아오는 사람이 누구든 마님은 나가고 없다고 하라 일러두었다. 슬로프 씨만 콕 집어 말할 수는 없는 노릇이어서, 그를 피하자고 제 벗들 모두에게 문을 닫아걸어 버린 셈이었다. 샬럿 스탠호프만은 예외로 두었고, 차차 몇몇 사람도 예외가 되었다. 한번은 스탠호프 댁에서 그와 마주친 적이 있었으나, 대개 슬로프 씨의 방문은 오전이었고 엘리너의 방문은 저녁이었다. 그 한 번의 자리에서는 샬럿이 용케 그를 성가신 일에서 지켜 주었다. 엘리너가 보기에 그것은 샬럿으로서 참으로 마음씨 고운 일이었고 또 눈치 빠른 일이기도 했으니, 엘리너는 그 신사를 피하고 싶은 까닭을 벗에게 한마디도 말한 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 실상인즉 샬럿은 슬로프 씨가 아마도 미망인의 손을 노리는 구혼자로 나서리라는 것을 제 동생에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 따라서 버티의 장래 아내를 그쪽에서 오는 위험으로부터 지켜 두는 편이 낫다는 것을 충분히 알고 있었다.\n\n그렇지만 스탠호프 일가는 슬로프 씨를 울라손에 데려가기로 약조가 되어 있었다. 그리하여 어쩔 수 없이 한 가지 채비가 정해졌는데, 엘리너에게는 몹시 언짢은 것이었다. 스탠호프 박사가 엘리너와 샬럿과 슬로프 씨를 데리고 함께 가고, 버티는 누이 마들린과 뒤따라오기로 한 것이다. 이 짝짓기가 저에게 몹시 언짢다는 것이 엘리너의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 드러났고, 그 덕에 제 조그만 계획에 크게 힘을 얻은 샬럿은 천 번이나 미안해했다.\n\n“마음에 안 드시는 줄 알아요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었어요. 버티는 눈이라도 내주고 엘리너와 함께 가려 들겠지만, 마들린이 그 애 없이는 도무지 나설 수가 없거든요. 그렇다고 슬로프 씨와 마들린을 다른 사람 하나 없이 같은 마차에 태울 수도 없는 노릇이고요. 그랬다간 둘 다 영영 신세를 망칠 테고, 그런 온당치 못한 일을 저지른 뒤에는 울라손 대문 안에도 못 들어가지 싶은걸요.”\n\n“그야 안 될 일이지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “하지만 제가 시뇨라와 동생분과 함께 마차를 타고 가면 안 될까요?”\n\n“그건 안 돼요!” 샬럿이 말했다. “그 애가 타면 자리가 둘밖에 나지 않거든요.” 실은 시뇨라가 낯선 사람 앞에서 길을 가는 것을 달가워하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“그렇다면,” 엘리너가 말했다, “샬럿, 다들 어찌나 친절하고 제게 잘해 주시는지 언짢아하지 않으시리라 믿고 말씀드리는데, 저는 아예 가지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n“아예 안 가겠다니, 무슨 그런 말씀을. 무슨 일이 있어도 가셔야죠.” 바로 그 자리에서 버티가 청혼하기로 집안 회의에서 이미 못 박아 둔 터였다.\n\n“아니면 삯마차를 부르면 돼요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “아시다시피 저는 아가씨들처럼 거리낄 것이 많은 처지가 아니니, 혼자서도 갈 수 있어요.”\n\n“무슨 말씀이에요. 그런 생각은 아예 마세요. 어차피 한 시간 남짓일 뿐이잖아요. 게다가 솔직히 말하면 무엇이 그리 싫으신지 저는 모르겠어요. 엘리너와 슬로프 씨는 아주 가까운 사이인 줄 알았는데요. 무엇이 그리 싫으신 거예요?”\n\n“아, 별것 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “그저 식구들끼리만 가는 자리인 줄 알았을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“버티가 우리와 함께 갈 수 있다면 훨씬 좋고 훨씬 오붓하겠지요. 딱한 대접을 받는 건 그 애예요. 장담하는데 그 애가 엘리너보다 슬로프 씨를 훨씬 더 무서워한답니다. 하지만 마들린이 그 애 없이는 나갈 수가 없으니 어쩌겠어요. 게다가 그 가엾은 것은 바깥나들이가 어찌나 드문지요. 그 애 변덕 때문에 우리 쪽 자리가 조금 흐트러지기는 하지만, 그 정도야 엘리너도 아까워하지 않으시리라 믿어요.”\n\n물론 엘리너는 천 번이나 아니라고 손사래를 쳤고 마들린이 부디 즐겁게 지내기를 천 번이나 빌었다. 그리고 물론 물러서서 슬로프 씨와 같은 마차를 타고 가겠노라 떠맡을 수밖에 없었다. 실상 그리하든지, 아니면 어찌하여 그리하지 않으려는지를 밝히든지 둘 중 하나로 몰린 셈이었다. 그런데 플럼스테드에서 있었던 일을 죄다 샬럿 스탠호프에게 털어놓을 마음은 도저히 나지 않았다.\n\n그러나 그것은 그에게 쓰라린 굴레였다. 그는 그 일을 면할 자잘한 꾀를 천 가지나 궁리했다. 몸이 아프다 핑계 대고 아예 가지 않을까, 초대받지도 않은 메리 볼드를 구슬려 함께 가게 한 다음 시누이를 태우자면 제 마차가 따로 있어야 한다고 우겨 볼까, 실로 애러빈 씨의 눈앞에서 슬로프 씨와 같은 마차에서 내리는 꼴을 보이느니 무슨 짓이든 다 하려 했다. 그러나 정작 그 중대한 아침이 왔을 때 무르익은 꾀는 하나도 없었고, 그리하여 슬로프 씨가 그를 스탠호프 박사의 마차에 태워 올리고는 뒤따라 올라 맞은편에 앉았다.\n\n주교는 울라손 모임 전날 저녁에 돌아왔고, 제 모든 근심을 나누는 반려에게서 환한 미소로 맞아들여졌다. 도착하자마자 그는 다소 가슴을 두근거리며 옷 갈아입는 방으로 살금살금 올라갔다. 정해진 기한을 사흘이나 넘긴 터라 벌이 두렵지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 그가 받은 인사는 더할 나위 없이 다정하고 살가웠다. 딸들이 나와서 그의 마음을 아주 누그러뜨리는 태도로 입을 맞추었고, 프라우디 부인은 “비록 마음이 녹는 일에는 익숙지 아니하나” 그를 두 팔로 꼭 껴안고는, 내 사랑 내 귀염둥이 착하고 어여쁜 우리 작은 주교님이라고 말로도 거의 그렇게 부르다시피 했다. 이 모두가 참으로 유쾌한 뜻밖의 일이었다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 전술을 얼마간 바꾸었다. 이전의 방침에 잘못이 있다고 여겨서가 아니라, 이제는 일을 그만한 지경에 이르게 해 놓았으니 그리해도 안전하리라고 셈했기 때문이다. 그는 슬로프 씨를 이겨 냈고, 이제는 남편에게 이런 것을 보여 주는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다. 곧 자기가 모두를 이기도록 내버려 두고 자기 말에 따르며 남들을 다스리도록 맡겨만 준다면, 남편이 그 상을 받도록 제가 마음 써 주리라는 것이었다. 슬로프 씨에게는 그를 당해 낼 가망이 없었다. 그는 한밤중의 노여움으로 가엾은 주교를 얼떨떨하게 만들 수도 있었거니와, 마음만 먹으면 날마다의 호사로 그를 달래고 어루만질 수도 있었다. 방을 꾸며 줄 수도 있고, 주교단의 어느 주교 못지않게 말쑥한 주교로 차려 내보낼 수도 있고, 좋은 만찬과 따뜻한 난롯불과 편안한 삶을 줄 수도 있었다. 남편이 조용히 순종하기만 한다면 이 모두를 해 줄 참이었다. 그러나 그러지 않는다면, 그때는! 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 그 무시무시한 밤에 겪은 고초가 어찌나 뼈아팠던지 주교에게는 더 반항할 기운이 거의 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n주교가 옷을 갈아입자마자 부인은 다시 그의 방으로 왔다. “거기서 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바라요.” 부인은 이렇게 말하며 난롯가 한쪽에 앉았고, 주교는 맞은편 안락의자에 앉은 채 제 종아리를 쓸어내리고 있었다. 여름이 지난 뒤로 그의 방에 불을 지핀 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 착한 주교는 따뜻하고 아늑한 것을 좋아하는 사람이라 그것이 마음에 들었다. 그럼, 아주 즐겁게 지냈지, 하고 그는 말했다. 대주교보다 더 정중할 수는 없었고, 대주교 부인 또한 그에 못지않게 사랑스러웠다고 했다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 그 말을 듣고 기뻐했다. 부인은 이렇게 생각하는 것만큼 저를 기쁘게 하는 일은 없다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n제 자식이 여느 사람들처럼 존중받는 것을.\n\n부인이 꼭 이런 말로 표현한 것은 아니었으나 한 말의 뜻은 같은 것이었다. 그러고 나서 제 작은 남자를 넉넉히 어루만지고 다독인 뒤에, 부인은 본론으로 들어갔다.\n\n“가엾은 수석 사제님은 아직 살아 계세요.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다더군, 그렇다더군.” 주교가 말했다. “내일 아침 식사가 끝나는 대로 곧장 수석사제관에 가 보리다.”\n\n“내일 아침에는 울라손 모임에 가야 해요, 여보. 일찍 가 있어야 하는 것 아시잖아요. 열두 시까지는 가야겠지요.”\n\n“아, 그렇지!” 주교가 말했다. “그럼 그다음 날에는 꼭 찾아가 보리다.”\n\n“거기서는 그 이야기가 많이 나왔나요?” 프라우디 부인이 물었다.\n\n“무엇에 대해서 말이오?” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“수석 사제 자리를 채우는 일 말이에요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. 그렇게 말하는 동안 부인의 눈에는 늘 있던 불꽃이 한 점 되돌아왔고, 주교는 조금 전보다 마음이 조금 덜 편안해지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“수석 사제 자리를 채우는 일이라, 그러니까 수석 사제가 세상을 떠난다면 말이오? 거의 없었소, 여보. 이야기가 나오기는 했지, 그저 나오기만 했소.”\n\n“그래서 그 일을 두고 뭐라고 하셨어요, 주교님?”\n\n“그게, 나는 이렇게 말했소, 만일, 그러니까, 혹시라도 수석 사제가 세상을 떠난다면, 그러니까, 내 생각에는, 하고 말했지…” 그가 이렇게 더듬거리며 갈팡질팡하는 동안, 아내의 눈이 저를 매섭게 쏘아보고 있음이 보였다. 슬로프 씨처럼 애정도 별로 없는 사람을 위하여 어찌하여 이런 재앙을 무릅써야 한단 말인가? 어찌하여 제 즐거움과 안락과 제게 허락된 그만한 위엄까지 내던지고 전속 사제 하나를 위하여 질 것이 뻔한 싸움을 벌여야 한단 말인가? 따지고 보면 그 전속 사제도 뜻을 이루고 나면 제 아내 못지않은 폭군이 될 터였다. 대체 무엇하러 싸운단 말인가? 무엇하러 겨룬단 말인가? 무엇하러 마음을 어지럽힌단 말인가? 그 순간부터 그는 슬로프 씨를 바람결에 날려 버리고 신들이 내려 주는 좋은 것이나 받기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“듣자 하니,” 프라우디 부인이 아주 천천히 말했다, “슬로프 씨가 새 수석 사제 자리를 노리고 있다더군요.”\n\n“그렇소, 아무렴, 그런 모양이오.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“그래서 대주교님은 그 일을 두고 뭐라고 하시던가요?” 프라우디 부인이 물었다.\n\n“글쎄, 여보, 사실대로 말하자면 내가 슬로프 씨에게 대주교께 말을 넣어 주겠다고 약속했소. 슬로프 씨가 나에게 그 이야기를 꺼냈지. 그 사람으로서는 참으로 주제넘은 짓이라 해야겠지만, 그거야 나와는 상관없는 일이오.”\n\n“주제넘은 짓이라고요!” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내 평생 들어 본 가운데 가장 뻔뻔스러운 참칭이에요. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원! 그래서 그 일을 어떻게 하셨어요, 주교님?”\n\n“그게, 여보, 대주교께 말씀을 드리기는 했소.”\n\n“설마 이런 터무니없는 수작에 당신 이름을 빌려주어 웃음거리가 되시겠다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원!” 그러고는 고개를 홱 젖히고 두 손을 허리에 얹었는데, 그 당당한 반항의 기색에 남편은 슬로프 씨가 결코 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되지 못하리라고 아주 확신하게 되었다. 참으로 프라우디 부인은 거의 무적이었다. 만일 그가 페트루치오와 혼인했더라면, 그 이름난 아내 길들이기의 명수라 한들 남자들이 여자에게는 유독 어울리지 않는다고 여기는 저 옷가지에 부인의 두 다리가 들어가는 것을 막을 수 있었을지는 의심스럽다.\n\n“터무니없는 일이오, 여보.”\n\n“그런데 어찌하여 그를 도우려고 애쓰셨어요?”\n\n“그게, 여보, 나는 그를 돕지 않았소, 그리 많이는.”\n\n“하지만 어찌하여 그런 일을 하시기는 하셨어요? 어찌하여 그토록 우스운 일에 당신 이름을 섞으셨어요? 대주교님께 무슨 말씀을 드리셨는데요?”\n\n“그게, 그저 말을 꺼냈을 뿐이오. 그저 이렇게 말했지, 가엾은 수석 사제가 세상을 떠나는 일이 생기면 슬로프 씨가, 그러니까, 그러니까…”\n\n“그러니까 뭐라고요?”\n\n“어떻게 말했는지는 잊었소. 얻을 수만 있다면 그 자리를 맡을 것이라고, 그런 식이었지. 그보다 더 한 말은 하지 않았소.”\n\n“애초에 아무 말씀도 하지 마셨어야죠. 그래서 대주교님은 뭐라고 하시던가요?”\n\n“아무 말씀도 없으셨소. 그저 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 비비셨지. 마침 그때 다른 사람이 다가왔고, 우리가 새 교구 통합 학교 위원회 이야기를 하는 사이에 새 수석 사제 이야기는 흐지부지되었소. 그 뒤로는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내는 것이 현명해 보이지 않았고.”\n\n“다시 꺼내다니요! 저는 그 이야기를 꺼내신 것부터가 몹시 유감스러워요. 대주교님이 당신을 어떻게 생각하시겠어요?”\n\n“여보, 대주교께서는 그 일을 거의 마음에 두지 않으셨을 것이 틀림없소.”\n\n“하지만 당신은 어찌하여 그 일을 마음에 두셨어요, 주교님? 어떻게 그런 자를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 삼을 생각을 하실 수가 있어요? 바체스터의 수석 사제라니! 그러다 머지않아 주교 자리까지 노리겠군요. 제 아비가 누구인지도 제대로 모르는 자, 입에 넣을 빵도 등에 걸칠 웃옷도 없던 것을 제가 거두어 준 자가 말이에요. 바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원! 내가 수석 사제로 만들어 주고말고요.”\n\n프라우디 부인은 스스로를 정치에서 순수한 휘그라고 여겼고, 집안사람들이 모두 휘그당에 속해 있었다. 그런데 영국 남자와 영국 여자의 온갖 계층 가운데(프라우디 부인은 그 대단한 정신력을 보아 앞쪽에 넣어야 마땅하리라고 나는 생각한다), 낮은 데서 태어나 높은 자리를 넘보는 자에게 순수한 휘그만큼 적대적인 부류는 없다.\n\n주교는 제 결백을 밝힐 필요가 있다고 생각했다. “그게, 여보,” 그가 말했다, “내가 보기에는 당신과 슬로프 씨가 예전만큼 사이좋게 지내지 못하는 것 같았소!”\n\n“사이좋게 지내다니요!” 프라우디 부인은 난롯가 깔개 위에서 발을 불편하게 놀리고, 두 사람이 입에 올린 그 인물에게 큰 위험이 닥쳤음을 알리는 모양새로 입술을 꾹 다물며 말했다.\n\n“나는 그 사람이 당신에게 못마땅한 존재라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했고” — 프라우디 부인의 발이 난롯가 깔개 위에서 몹시 빠르게 놀았다 — “그가 주교관에서 나가면 당신이 한결 편안하리라는 것도 알게 되었소” — 프라우디 부인은, 하이에나가 웃음을 터뜨리기에 앞서 짓는다는 그런 미소를 지었다 — “그래서 그가 이 자리를 얻어 내 전속 사제 노릇을 그만두게 되면 당신도 그런 처사를 반기리라 생각했던 것이오.”\n\n그러자 하이에나가 소리 내어 웃었다. 그런 처사를 반긴다니! 제 원수가 한 해 1,200파운드를 받는 수석 사제로 둔갑하는 것을 반긴다니! 메데이아는 제 고향의 풍습을 이야기하면서(나는 롭슨판에서 인용하고 있다), 놀라서 듣고 있는 상대에게 제 나라에서는 사로잡은 포로를 잡아먹는다고 일러 준다.\n\n“그들을 용서한다고요?” 메데이아가 말한다.\n\n“그렇고말고요.” 온순한 그리스 사람이 말한다.\n\n“우리는 그들을 잡아먹지요!” 콜키스의 여인이 무시무시한 기세로 말한다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 바체스터의 메데이아였다. 슬로프 씨를 잡아먹지 않는다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 아예 없었다. 그를 용서한다니! 그저 치워 버리기만 한다니! 그를 수석 사제로 만들어 준다니! 그의 고장에서, 그와 같은 부류의 사람들 사이에서 사로잡은 자들을 다루는 방식은 그런 것이 아니었다. 슬로프 씨는 그런 자비를 기대할 처지가 못 되었다. 부인은 그를 마지막 뼈 한 조각까지 발라먹을 참이었다.\n\n“아, 그럼요, 여보, 물론 그 사람은 당신의 전속 사제 노릇을 그만두게 되겠지요.” 부인이 말했다. “그동안 있었던 일을 생각하면 그거야 당연한 일이에요. 그런 사람과 한집에 사는 것은 잠시라도 생각할 수 없어요. 게다가 그 사람은 그런 자리에 도무지 어울리지 않는다는 것을 스스로 드러냈어요. 성직자들 사이에 다툼과 싸움을 만들어 내고, 여보, 당신을 곤경에 빠뜨리고, 제가 주교나 다름없다는 듯이 나서지 않았어요. 물론 나가야지요. 하지만 주교관을 떠난다고 해서 그것이 수석사제관에 들어갈 이유가 되지는 않아요.”\n\n“아, 물론 아니지!” 주교가 말했다. “하지만 체면을 지키자면, 알잖소, 여보…”\n\n“저는 체면을 지킬 생각이 없어요. 저는 슬로프 씨가 꼭 생긴 그대로 드러나기를 바라요. 거짓되고 음흉하고 비열하고 술수를 부리는 사람으로 말이에요. 저는 그를 눈여겨보고 있어요. 제가 무엇을 보고 있는지 그 사람은 조금도 모르지요. 그는 그 다리 못 쓰는 이탈리아 여자와 더할 나위 없이 창피스러운 짓을 저지르고 있어요. 그 집안이 바체스터의 수치이고, 슬로프 씨도 바체스터의 수치예요. 몸조심하지 않으면 머리에 수석 사제의 모자를 쓰기는커녕 등에 걸친 성직복이 벗겨질 거예요. 수석 사제라니, 원! 그 사람은 오만이 지나쳐 미쳤어요.”\n\n주교는 저를 위해서든 제 전속 사제를 위해서든 더는 변명하지 않았고, 고분고분 순종하는 모습을 보인 덕에 다시 총애를 얻었다. 두 사람은 곧 만찬 자리로 갔고, 그는 제 집에서 오랜만에 가장 즐거운 저녁을 보냈다. 그가 커피를 홀짝이며 신문을 읽는 동안 딸이 연주를 하고 노래를 불러 주었고, 프라우디 부인은 대주교에 관하여 상냥하게 이런저런 것을 물었다. 그러고 나서 그는 흐뭇한 마음으로 잠자리에 들어, 프라우디 부인이 그리젤다 본인이기라도 한 듯 고요히 잠들었다. 아침에 면도를 하며 울라손 잔치를 채비하는 동안, 그는 프라우디 부인처럼 온몸을 빈틈없이 무장한 전사를 상대로 다시는 창을 겨누지 않겠노라 굳게 마음먹었다.\n\n# 제34장. 옥스퍼드 — 라자러스의 학장과 지도교수\n\n애러빈 씨는, 앞서 말한 대로, 플럼스테드 교회 묘지의 나무 아래를 서글프게 거닐었을 뿐이다. 그는 만찬 시간이 될 때까지 식구들 가운데 누구 앞에도 나타나지 않았고, 그때가 되자 그들이 판단하기로는 여느 때의 그와 조금도 다름없어 보였다. 그는 늘 하던 대로 스스로에게 숱한 질문을 던지고 숱한 대답을 내놓았다. 그리고 그 결말은 자기 자신을 얼간이로 판정해 내려보내는 것이었다. 그는 이렇게 단정했다. 자신은 연애의 술책을 이제 와서 시작하기에는 너무 늙었고 너무 녹슬었으며, 그런 일에 썼어야 할 시절을 손가락 사이로 흘려보냈으니, 이제는 제가 깔아 놓은 자리에 그대로 누울 수밖에 없다고. 그다음 그는 자신에게 물었다. 과연 자기가 정말로 이 여인을 사랑하는가? 그리고 그는 스스로에게 대답했다. 오랜 씨름 끝이기는 했으나 마침내 정직하게, 자신이 분명 그녀를 사랑한다고. 이어 그는 자기가 그녀의 재산도 아울러 사랑하는 것은 아닌지 물었고, 다시 그렇다고 대답했다. 그러나 여기서는 정직하게 대답한 것이 아니었다. 제 자신의 행위에서 불순한 동기를 굳이 찾아내려 드는 것은 예나 지금이나 그의 약점이었다. 물론 그가 처한 형편으로 보아 — 성직록은 보잘것없고 가진 것이라곤 펠로 자리뿐이며, 칼리지의 호사와 값비싼 안락에 길들어 있는 처지였으니 — 설령 그 여인 자체에 아무리 강한 끌림을 느꼈다 한들 무일푼의 여자와 혼인하기는 망설였을지 모른다. 물론 엘리너의 재산은 그런 난처함을 죄다 문제 밖으로 치워 주었다. 그러나 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑이, 그녀의 재산을 나누어 가짐으로써 제 처지를 낫게 할 수 있으리라는 생각은 털끝만큼도 없이 슬며시 그에게 스며들었다는 것 또한 똑같이 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n그가 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 그 무늬를 세고 아울러 제 앞날의 승산을 헤아리고 있었을 때, 볼드 부인의 넉넉한 수입에 대한 기억이 그녀를 향해 처음으로 확신하게 된 사랑을 식히지 않았던 것은 분명하다. 그리고 어찌하여 그것이 사랑을 식혔어야 하는가? 사내 가운데 가장 순결한 자라 한들 그래야 할 까닭이 있는가? 어떻든 간에 애러빈 씨는 자신에게 불리하게 판결을 내렸다. 그는 제 경우에는 그 기억이 사랑을 식혔다고, 그러므로 자신은 사내 가운데 가장 순결한 자가 아니라고 판결했다.\n\n그는 또한, 이쪽이 그로서는 더 요긴한 결론이었는데, 엘리너가 자기 따위는 지푸라기만큼도 마음에 두지 않으며 아마도 제 연적에게라면 지푸라기만큼은 마음을 두고 있으리라고 판단했다. 그러고는 다시는 그녀를 생각하지 않기로 마음을 정한 뒤, 부주교네 땅 아래쪽으로 흐르는 작은 개울에 몸을 던지고 싶어질 지경이 되도록 줄곧 그녀만을 생각했다.\n\n그리고 이따금 그의 마음은 시뇨라 네로니에게로 되돌아가곤 했고, 그는 그녀와 엘리너 볼드를 견주어 보았는데 언제나 후자에게 유리한 쪽으로만 견준 것은 아니었다. 시뇨라는 그의 말에 귀를 기울였고, 그를 추어올렸으며, 그를 믿었다. 적어도 그렇다고 그에게 말해 주기는 했다. 볼드 부인 역시 그의 말에 귀를 기울이기는 했으나 결코 그를 추어올리지 않았고, 늘 그를 믿어 주지도 않았으며, 이제는 격한 노여움 속에 그와 갈라섰다. 게다가 시뇨라는 둘 가운데 더 아리따운 여인이었고, 제 불구라는 덤의 매력까지 갖추고 있었다. 그에게는 그것이 매력이었으므로.\n\n그러나 그는 지금 엘리너를 사랑한다고 느끼는 그만큼 시뇨라 네로니를 사랑할 수는 결코 없었을 것이다. 그리하여 그는 개울에 제 몸을 던지는 대신 돌을 던졌고, 여름날에 만날 수 있는 가장 서글픈 신사의 모습으로 그 물가에 주저앉았다.\n\n그는 교회 묘지에서 만찬을 알리는 종소리를 들었고, 이제 평정을 되찾아야 할 때임을 알았다. 그는 제 눈에 스스로가 부끄러운 꼴이 되었다고, 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내며 스스로 떠맡은 높은 소임을 소홀히 했다고 느꼈다. 이 오후를 그는 플럼스테드를 서성이며 보낼 것이 아니라 세인트 이월드의 가난한 이들 사이에서 보냈어야 했다. 늙어 가는 상사병 든 시골 총각처럼, 풀이 죽어 한숨을 쉬며, 지어낸 슬픔과 베르테르식 비탄으로 가득 찬 채 서성이고 있을 것이 아니라 말이다. 그는 자신이 몹시 부끄러웠고, 제 눈에 그토록 훼손된 제 인격을 되찾는 데 한시도 지체하지 않으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n그리하여 만찬 자리에 나타났을 때 그는 여느 때만큼이나 활기찼고, 그날 저녁 부주교의 식탁을 빛낸 대화의 태반은 그가 이끌어 낸 것이었다. 하딩 씨는 마음이 편치 않고 속이 쓰려서, 실제보다 편안해 보이려 애쓸 마음도 없었다. 그가 한 얼마 안 되는 말은 딸에게 한 것이었다. 그는 부주교와 애러빈 씨가 한편이 되어 엘리너의 안락을 해쳤다고 생각했고, 이제 그의 바람은 그 두 사람에게서 벗어나 바체스터의 제 하숙에서 운명이 자기를 위해 마련해 둔 무엇이든 감당하는 것이었다. 그는 요양원이라는 이름 자체가 미웠다. 그곳에서 잃었던 제 몫을 되찾으려던 시도가 그에게 그토록 많은 괴로움을 안겨 주었던 것이다. 그로서는 이제 퀴버풀 씨가 그 자리를 차지해도 좋았다.\n\n그리고 부주교도 그리 흥이 나 있지 않았다. 물론 가엾은 수석 사제의 병환이 맨 먼저 화제에 올랐다. 그랜틀리 박사는 트리폴 박사의 죽음이라는 예상되는 사태와 관련해 슬로프 씨의 이름을 입에 올리지 않았다. 그는 지금 당장은 슬로프 씨에 관해 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않았고, 제 서글픈 억측을 알리고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 제 원수가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 될지도 모른다는 생각은 그를 몹시 침울하게 만들었다. 그런 일이 벌어진다면, 그런 참담한 재앙이 닥친다면, 그의 삶은 바체스터라는 도시와 얽혀 있는 한에서는 끝장이 날 것이었다. 그는 오랜 걸음길도 오랜 습관도 모조리 버리고 플럼스테드에서 은퇴한 교구 목사로 조용히 살아야 하리라. 프라우디 박사가 주교관에 들어앉은 것만도 그에게는 혹독한 시련이었는데, 슬로프 씨까지 수석사제관에 들어앉는다면 바체스터 대성당 경내에서는 숨조차 쉴 수 없으리라고 그는 느꼈다.\n\n그리하여 마음에는 슬픔을 품고 있으면서도 애러빈 씨가 그 자리에서 겉보기로는 가장 유쾌한 사람이 되는 일이 벌어졌다. 하딩 씨도 그랜틀리 부인도 그가 침울해하지 않는다는 이유로 그에게 조금씩 화가 나 있었다. 한 사람에게는 그가 엘리너의 추방을 두고 개가를 올리는 듯 보였고, 다른 한 사람에게는 그가 그날의 온갖 서글픈 사정들 — 엘리너의 고집, 슬로프 씨의 성공, 가엾은 수석 사제의 졸중 — 에 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 마음이 흔들리지 않은 듯 보였다. 그렇게 그들은 모두 서로 어긋나 있었다.\n\n하딩 씨는 부인들과 거의 함께 방을 나섰고, 그러자 부주교는 애러빈 씨에게 속내를 털어놓았다. 그는 여전히 요양원 이야기를 되풀이했다. “그 작자가 볼드 부인에게 보낸 편지에서, 하딩 씨가 주교를 찾아뵙기만 하면 만사가 잘될 것이라고 한 것은 대체 무슨 속셈이겠소?” 그가 말했다. “물론 나야 그자가 하는 말에 이끌려 움직일 생각은 없소만, 그래도 하딩 씨가 주교를 만나 뵙는 편이 좋을지도 모르지요. 볼드 부인이 기어이 제 앞길을 그르치려 든다고 해서 이 일까지 손가락 사이로 흘려보낸다면 그야말로 어리석은 짓일 테니.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인이 제 앞길을 그르치리라고는 그리 확신하지 않는다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다. 그는 그녀가 사람들이 짐작하는 만큼 슬로프 씨를 살갑게 여기고 있다고는 믿기지 않는다고 말했다. 부주교는 이 점을 두고 그에게 묻고 또 캐물었으나 아무것도 끌어내지 못했고, 결국 자신은 malgré lui 슬로프 씨의 손위 처남이 될 운명이라는 제 확신을 굳게 지켰다. 애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨가 슬로프 씨의 편지와 관련해서든 그 편지의 결과로서든 요양원에 관해 아무 조치도 취하지 말아야 한다고 힘주어 권했다. “주교가 정말로 하딩 씨에게 그 자리를 내줄 뜻이라면,” 애러빈 씨가 논했다. “한 부인에게 보낸 편지에 실려 전해지는 전갈 말고 다른 방식으로 그 뜻을 알리도록 마음을 쓸 것이오. 하딩 씨가 주교관에 몸소 나타난다면 그저 슬로프 씨의 판에 놀아나는 꼴이 될 수도 있소.” 그리하여 위대한 그윈 박사가 도착할 때까지, 아니 적어도 그 권세가의 재가 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않기로 정해졌다.\n\n이 사내들이 하딩 씨를 마치 꼭두각시인 양 이야기하며, 정작 그를 저희 속내에 끌어들일 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않은 채 하딩 씨의 앞날의 자리를 두고 음모와 자잘한 교회 정치의 술책을 꾸미는 꼴은 우스꽝스러웠다. 문제가 되는 것은 안락한 집 한 채와 수입이었고, 하딩 씨가 그것을 갖는 것은 매우 바람직하고 또한 분명 매우 온당한 일이었다. 그러나 지금으로서는 그것이 요점이 아니었다. 요긴한 것은 주교를 이기는 일이었고, 될 수만 있다면 슬로프 씨를 짓뭉개는 일이었다. 슬로프 씨는 맞수 후보를 내세웠거나, 내세웠다고 여겨지고 있었다. 무엇보다도 가장 바람직한 일은 퀴버풀 씨의 임명이 세상에 공표되었다가 하딩 씨의 권리를 지키자고 목청을 높이는 분개한 세상의 아우성에 파기되는 것이었으리라. 그러나 그런 일이 벌어질 가망은 적었다. 세상의 아주 작은 조각만이 분개할 터인데, 그 조각은 목청을 높이는 데 익숙한 부류가 아니었다. 게다가 그 자리는 어떻게 보면 하딩 씨에게 제안되었던 것이고, 또 어떻게 보면 그가 물리친 것이었다.\n\n이 일이 그렇게 되도록 만든 방식에서 슬로프 씨의 사악하고 교활한 손길이 유난히 두드러졌고, 부주교의 심사를 그토록 아프게 긁은 것이 바로 그 슬로프 씨의 간계가 거둔 성공이었다. 그가 무엇보다도 두려워한 것은 슬로프 씨에게 지략으로 밀리는 일이었다. 그런데 지금으로서는 슬로프 씨가 그의 옆구리를 돌아 치고, 앞질러 진군하고, 병참을 끊고, coup de main으로 그의 견고한 성읍을 빼앗고, 마침내 정면으로 벌어진 회전에서 그를 철저히 쳐부술 성싶었다. 부주교는 주교 아닌 슬로프 씨를 찾아가라는 말을 들었을 때 제 옆구리가 돌파당했다고 느꼈고, 하딩 씨가 주교의 제안을 물리치도록 꾀임을 당했을 때 앞질러 진군당했다고 느꼈으며, 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원을 차지하면 병참이 끊길 것이고, 엘리너야말로 함락될 운명에 놓인 그 견고한 성읍이며, 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되면 온 세상이 그를 마지막 싸움의 승자로 여기리라고 느꼈다.\n\n그윈 박사는 바체스터라는 무대 위로 내려와 이 끔찍한 악들로부터 구원을 가져다줄 Deus ex machina였다. 그러나 복수의 신이 통풍으로 드러누워 있는 판에 어떻게 통속극의 dénouement이 제대로 이루어지겠으며, 어떻게 악덕과 슬로프 씨가 벌을 받고 미덕과 부주교가 상을 받겠는가? 그러는 동안 악이 개가를 올릴 수도 있고, 가엾은 무고함은 프라우디 박사의 화살통에서 날아온 화살에 땅에 꿰뚫린 채 죽어 널브러져 그윈 박사조차 되살리지 못할 수도 있는 것이다.\n\n엘리너가 떠난 지 이삼일 뒤, 애러빈 씨는 옥스퍼드로 가서 이내 제 칼리지의 존엄한 수장과 단둘이 마주 앉게 되었다. 그윈 박사가 바체스터행이 가져올 효과에 대해 그리 낙관하지 않으며 주교의 일에 끼어들기를 그다지 달가워하지 않는다는 것은 아주 분명했다. 그는 통풍을 앓았으나 이제 거의 회복된 상태였고, 애러빈 씨는 만일 이번 임무가 학장이 진심으로 찬동하는 종류의 것이었더라면 그가 진작에 플럼스테드에 와 있었으리라는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다.\n\n그렇기는 해도 그윈 박사는 제 벗을 찾아가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 애러빈 씨와 함께 바체스터로 돌아가겠노라 선선히 약속했다. 그는 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 될 가망이 조금이라도 있다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다. 그 신사의 인품에 대해 조금도 좋지 않은 소문이 제 귀에까지 들어왔노라고 그는 말했고, 그런 임명이란 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이라는 의견을 힘주어 밝혔다. 일이 이 대목에 이르자 학장의 오른팔인 톰 스테이플이 이 의논에 거들라고 불려 들어왔다. 톰 스테이플은 라자러스의 지도교수였고, 나아가 옥스퍼드의 거물이었다. 그토록 위엄이라고는 없는 호칭으로 두루 불리기는 했으나, 톰 스테이플은 대학 안에서 높은 위엄을 지키는 사람이었다. 그는 말하자면 옥스퍼드 지도교수들의 우두머리였는데, 이 무리는 스스로를 통틀어 놓고 보면 칼리지 수장들보다 중요성에서 조금도, 아니 설령 뒤진다 해도 아주 조금밖에 뒤지지 않는다고 여기는 사람들이었다. 학장이든 워든이든 학감이든 총장이든 제 지도교수와 늘 사이가 척척 맞는 것은 아니다. 지도교수란 제 나름의 뜻을 지니기를 결코 꺼리지 않는 존재다. 그러나 우리가 쓰고 있는 이 시절의 라자러스에서는 두 사람이 더없이 가까운 벗이자 굳건한 동맹이었다.\n\n톰 스테이플은 마흔다섯쯤 된 다부지고 건장한 사내로, 키는 작고 얼굴빛은 거무스름했으며, 뻣뻣하고 억센 검은 머리에 곱슬거리는 검은 수염을 길렀는데 그 수염이 구레나룻 꼴로 드러나는 것은 아주 조금밖에 허용하지 않았다. 그는 늘 흰 목도리를 둘렀는데, 깨끗하기는 했으나 요즘 우리 젊은 성직자 몇몇을 돋보이게 하는 저 꼼꼼한 정성으로 매어져 있지는 않았다. 그는 물론 언제나 격식에 맞는 근엄한 검은 옷을 갖추어 입었다. 스테이플 씨는 점잖고 깔끔하게 사는 사람이었고 어떤 육욕에도 지나치게 빠지지 않았다. 그럼에도 어쩐지 불그레한 빛이 그의 코를 물들이기 시작하고 있었는데, 그의 벗들이 단언하기로 이는 그 지도교수가 신입생으로 라자러스에 들어오던 바로 그해에 라자러스의 지하 술광에 들여놓은 어떤 포트 술통이 낸 특유의 효험이었다. 또한 스테이플 씨의 목소리에도 아마 포트 포도주의 향내가, 말하자면 아주 희미한 울림 같은 것이 조금은 배어 있었다.\n\n요즈음 들어 톰 스테이플은 행복한 사람이 아니었다. 대학 개혁은 오랫동안 그의 골칫거리였고 이제는 그의 재앙이 되었다. 그에게 그것은 대다수 사람들에게 그렇듯 정치의 문제, 즉 필요할 때면 당파를 위해서나 원칙을 위해서 얼마간 마땅한 열의를 내보일 수 있는 그런 문제가 아니었다. 그에게 그것은 호사가의 전쟁놀이나 정중하고 상투적인 반대의 소재가 아니었다. 그에게 그것은 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 문제였다. 대학의 status quo만이 그가 아는 유일한 삶이었고, 어떠한 개혁이든 그에게는 죽음만큼이나 나쁜 것이었다. 그 대의가 순교라는 것을 허락하기만 했더라면 그는 기꺼이 그 대의의 순교자가 되었으리라.\n\n불행히도 오늘날 공적인 사안이란 순교자를 허락하지 않으며, 그래서 열의가 그토록 모자란 것이다. 연 수입 10,000파운드의 신사들이 보호 관세를 지키자고 제집 문간에서 죽을 수 있었더라면, 필시 대여섯 명의 영광스러운 늙은 준남작들이 그렇게 쓰러졌을 터이고 보호 관세 학파는 오늘날 제자들로 붐볐을 것이다. 아무런 위험도 없는 싸움에서 누가 힘껏 싸우겠는가? 톰 스테이플은 그런 자기희생으로써 대학 주간 평의회 위원들에게 제 정신을 불어넣을 수만 있었다면 기꺼이 의회 위원회 앞에서 꼬챙이에 꿰였을 것이다.\n\n톰 스테이플은 예로부터 대학의 학생들과 상인들 사이에 성행해 온 외상 제도를 속으로 찬성하는 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 그는 이 타락한 시절에 그런 문제를 두고 『주피터』와 공공연히 겨루어 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알고 있었고 스스로에게도 인정했다. 『주피터』는 대학을 다스리는 일을 떠맡고 나섰으며, 톰 스테이플은 『주피터』가 제게는 너무나 강대하다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 남모르게, 그리고 마음 놓을 만한 동무들 사이에서는, 외상 제도야말로 젊은이가 한번 겪어 볼 만한 시련이라고 그는 주장하곤 했다.\n\n못난 자들은, 하고 그는 말했다, 약하고 쓸모없는 자들은 위험 속으로 잘못 들어가 제 발을 데지만, 잘난 자들은, 인격이라 할 것을 지닌 자들은, 제 모교에 명예를 돌려줄 무엇을 안에 지닌 자들은 상처 하나 없이 빠져나온다는 것이다. 젊은이가 학동처럼 지켜지고 보호되고 단속받은 끝에 무사히 빠져나온다면 거기에 무슨 공이 있겠는가? 그렇게 해 보아야 시련의 시기가 미루어질 뿐이고, 사내가 사내다워지는 일이 스무 살에서 스물네 살로 늦추어질 따름이다. 칼리지에서 걸음마 끈으로 묶어 두면 그는 런던에서 법정 변호사가 되려고 만찬 횟수를 채우는 동안에 고삐를 끊을 것이요, 거기서 묶어 두면 나중에 혼인한 사내가 된 뒤에 고삐를 끊을 것이다. 젊은 날의 방종은 어디에선가는 뿌려져야 한다. 톰 스테이플은 젊은이들을 두고 이렇게 주장하곤 했는데, 사실 그리 앞뒤가 맞지는 않았으나 그래도 오랜 경험에서 얻은 이 문제에 대한 실질적인 지식이 조금은 담겨 있었다.\n\n그리고 이제 톰 스테이플은 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨를 돕고자 제가 지닌 지혜를 내놓았다.\n\n“전혀 있을 수 없는 일이오.” 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 새 수석 사제가 될 리는 없다고 논하면서 그가 말했다.\n\n“나도 그리 생각하오.” 학장이 말했다. “그자에게는 내세울 지위가 없고, 내가 들은 것이 죄다 사실이라면 인품이라 할 것도 거의 없소.”\n\n“인품으로 말하자면,” 톰 스테이플이 말했다. “나는 그것을 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않소. 수석 사제 자리에는 오히려 헤픈 목사를 좋아하는 법이지요. 조금 방탕하게 산다거나 불신앙의 기미가 조금 있다거나 하는 것은 대성당 경내에 들어가는 데 나쁜 천거가 아니외다. 그러나 그들이 슬로프 씨를 앉힐 수는 없을 것이오. 최근 두 수석 사제가 케임브리지 사람이었소. 같은 대학에서 내리 세 사람을 앉힌 전례를 그대는 내게 보여 주지 못할 것이오. 우리는 우리 몫을 못 받고 있고 앞으로도 못 받겠지만, 그래도 셋에 하나쯤은 가져야 하지 않겠소.”\n\n“그런 규칙 따위야 이제 다 지나간 옛일이오.” 애러빈 씨가 말했다.\n\n“내가 보기엔 모든 것이 다 지나가 버렸소.” 톰 스테이플이 말했다. “여송연은 다 타 버렸고, 우리는 그 재요.”\n\n“그건 그대 한 사람 이야기로 해 두시오, 스테이플.” 학장이 말했다.\n\n“나는 모두를 대신해 말하는 것이오.” 지도교수가 굳세게 말했다. “이 나라 어디에도 생기라고는 남지 않는 지경으로 가고 있소. 이제 아무도 제 자신을, 또 제게 딸린 이들을 다스릴 만한 자격이 없다는 것이오. 정부가 우리 모두에게 무엇이든 다 대 주어야 하고, 신문이 그 정부에게 무엇이든 다 대 주어야 한다는 것이지요. 그렇더라도 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되지는 않을 것이오.”\n\n“그러면 요양원의 워든은 누가 되겠소?” 애러빈 씨가 말했다.\n\n“퀴버풀 씨가 이미 임명되었다고 나는 들었소.” 톰 스테이플이 말했다.\n\n“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않소.” 학장이 말했다. “그리고 더욱이 프라우디 박사가 그런 암초를 향해 배를 몰 만큼 앞을 못 보지는 않으리라 생각하오. 슬로프 씨 자신도 그것을 막을 만한 분별은 있어야 할 테고.”\n\n“그러나 슬로프 씨로서는 제 후원자가 암초에 걸리는 꼴을 보아도 개의치 않을지 모르지요.” 의심 많은 지도교수가 말했다.\n\n“그런다고 그자가 무엇을 얻겠소?” 애러빈 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그런 사내의 속임수는 도무지 헤아릴 길이 없소.” 스테이플 씨가 말했다. “프라우디 주교가 완전히 그자의 손아귀에 있다는 것은 아주 분명해 보이오. 그리고 그자가 이 퀴버풀 씨를 요양원에 넣으려고 하늘과 땅을 뒤흔들어 왔다는 것 또한 똑같이 분명하지요. 그런 임명이 주교에게 더없이 해로우리라는 것을 그자가 모를 리 없는데도 말이오. 그런 사내는 도무지 이해할 수가 없고,” 톰 스테이플이 깊이 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. “선한 사람들의 안녕과 운수가 그자의 음모에 달려 있을지 모른다고 생각하면 참으로 끔찍하오.”\n\n그윈 박사도 스테이플 씨도, 아니 애러빈 씨조차도 조금도 알지 못했다. 그들이 이야기하고 있는 이 슬로프 씨야말로 바로 저희 편 후보를 요양원에 앉히려고 온 힘을 다해 애써 왔다는 것을, 그리고 그가 주교관에 눌러앉기는커녕 그의 축출이 이미 교구의 높은 권세에 의해 결정되어 있다는 것을.\n\n“내 말해 두겠소만,” 지도교수가 말했다. “만일 이 퀴버풀이 요양원에 밀어 넣어지고 트리폴 박사가 정말로 죽는다면, 정부가 하딩 씨를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 앉힌다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 것이오. 그가 사임했을 때 그토록 말이 많았으니, 그들로서는 그를 위해 무언가 해야만 한다고 느낄 테니 말이오.”\n\n그윈 박사는 그 자리에서는 이 제안에 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그렇다고 그것이 그의 마음에 덜 새겨진 것은 아니었다. 하딩 씨가 요양원의 워든이 될 수 없다면, 어찌하여 바체스터의 수석 사제는 되지 못하겠는가?\n\n그리하여 의논은 이렇다 할 결론 없이 끝났고, 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨는 이튿날 플럼스테드로 떠날 채비를 했다.\n\n# 제35장. 손 양의 야외 연회\n\n울라손 잔치의 날이 밝았고, 온 세상이 그 자리에 모였다 — 적어도 손 양의 초대장에 포함된 만큼의 세상은 다 모였다. 앞서 말했듯 주교는 전날 저녁에 집으로 돌아왔고, 같은 날 저녁 같은 기차로 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 내려왔다. 부주교는 사륜마차를 대령한 채 라자러스의 학장을 기다리고 있었으니, 철도 승강장에는 교회의 고위 인사들이 제법 볼만하게 늘어선 셈이었다.\n\n스탠호프 일행의 자리 배정은 앞서 이야기한 그 얄궂은 방식으로 결국 굳어졌고, 엘리너는 앞으로 닥칠 불행에 대한 불안과 예감으로 가득한 채 박사의 마차에 올랐다. 반면 슬로프 씨는 승리감에 들떠 그 마차에 올라탔다.\n\n그는 그날 아침 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경으로부터 대단히 정중한 짧은 편지를 받았다. 사실 약속이라 할 만한 것은 별로 없었으나, 슬로프 씨는 정부 관리가 약속을 하는 것은 예법에 어긋난다는 사실을 알고 있었다 — 혹은 안다고 여겼다. 니컬러스 경은 아무것도 약속하지 않으면서 많은 것을 암시했고, 슬로프 씨가 훌륭한 수석 사제가 되리라 확신한다고 밝혔으며, 온갖 종류의 성공을 빌어 주었다. 물론 그는 자신이 내각에 속해 있지 않아 그런 문제에 대해서는 한 번도 자문을 받은 일이 없으며, 설령 그 일에 대해 입을 연다 해도 자기 목소리는 아무 무게도 없으리라는 말을 덧붙이기는 했다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 이 모두를 관직 생활의 신중한 자제로 받아들였다. 예상된 승리를 완성이라도 하려는 듯, 그가 막 울라손으로 떠나려는 참에 또 한 통의 편지가 그에게 전해졌다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 또한 울라손 대문 앞의 군중 앞에서 볼드 부인을 스탠호프 박사의 마차에서 손잡아 내려 주는 장면을 흐뭇하게 그려 보고 있었으니, 그 흐뭇함은 엘리너가 바로 그 절차를 두려워하는 정도와 꼭 같았다. 그는 자신과 자신의 앞날을 저 미망인의 발치에 던지리라 이미 마음을 굳혔고, 그 일을 치를 날로 상서로운 오늘 아침을 고르기로 거의 정해 놓은 터였다. 시뇨라는 근래 그에게 정중하달 수 없는 태도를 보였다. 그의 방문을 받아들이기는 했고 적어도 노여워하지는 않으면서 그의 사랑 고백을 들어 주기는 했으나, 그를 못살게 굴고 헐뜯고 비웃고 조롱했다. 그러면서도 그가 자기를 살아 있는 여인 가운데 가장 아름답다 부르고, 자기 손에 입 맞추고, 거듭 맹세하며 스스로를 그대의 숭배자요 노예요 예배자라 선언하는 것은 내버려 두었다.\n\n손 양은 축제 당일 아침 크나큰 동요 속에, 그러면서도 크나큰 영광 속에 있었다. 손 씨 또한 자기가 여는 잔치는 아니었으되 손에 걸린 궂은일이 많았다. 그러나 울라손 집안 전체에서 가장 혹사당하고, 가장 노심초사하고, 가장 큰 몫을 해낸 사람은 아마도 집사 플로머시 씨였을 것이다. 이 마지막 인물은 손 씨의 부친 시절, 프랑스에 총재 정부가 위세를 떨치던 무렵에 왕당파 몇 사람에게 보내는 편지를 장화 뒤축에 숨기고 파리로 건너간 적이 있었는데, 운이 좋아 무사히 돌아왔다. 그때 그는 아주 젊었고 지금은 아주 늙었으나, 그 무용담은 그에게 정치적 담력과 은밀한 분별이라는 평판을 안겨 주었고, 그 평판은 광이 가장 새로웠던 시절과 다름없이 지금도 여전히 그에게 쓸모가 있었다. 플로머시 씨는 50년 넘게 울라손의 집사로 있었고, 그동안 대단히 편한 삶을 누렸다. 발각되었더라면 제 목이 달아났을 물건을 뒤축에 넣고 무사히 날랐던 사람에게 누가 감히 절대적인 노동을 많이 요구할 수 있었겠는가? 그리하여 플로머시 씨는 한 번도 고되게 일한 적이 없었고, 근년에는 아예 일이라는 것을 하지 않았다. 그는 목재에 취미가 있었으므로 베어 낼 나무에 표를 했고, 원예에 취미가 있었으므로 자기가 명시적으로 승낙하지 않고서는 관목 한 그루도 심지 못하게 하고 화단 한 뙈기도 만들지 못하게 했다. 이런 일들에서 그는 이따금 여주인과 정면으로 맞서야 했지만, 여주인이 자기를 이기게 내버려 두는 일은 좀처럼 없었다.\n\n그러나 오늘 같은 자리에서는 플로머시 씨의 진가가 드러났다. 그는 가문의 명예를 마음에 품고 있었고, 손님을 맞는 도리를 속속들이 알고 있었다. 그러므로 축제가 벌어질 때면 그는 언제나 지휘권을 제 손에 거두어 쥐고 주인과 여주인 위에 군림했다.\n\n플로머시 씨에게 공정을 기해 말하자면, 그는 늙었으되 제 손에 맡겨진 그런 일을 속속들이 알고 있었고, 또 그 일을 잘해 냈다.\n\n그날의 차례는 이러했다. 시골에서 아랫사람들이 참으로 정확한 안목으로 윗계층을 일컬어 '귀인'이라 하거니와, 그 귀인들은 조찬을 들고 귀인 아닌 이들은 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었다. 이 두 잔치를 위해 대형 천막 두 채가 세워졌다. 귀인용 천막은 어느 깊은 하하 도랑의 안쪽, 곧 정원 편에 있었고, 귀인 아닌 이들의 천막은 같은 도랑의 바깥쪽, 곧 방목장 편에 있었다. 둘 다 엄청난 크기였다 — 바깥쪽 것은 터무니없는 규모라 해도 좋을 정도였다 — 그런데도 플로머시 씨는 둘 다 모자라리라고 단언했다. 이를 보완하기 위해 식당에 보조 연회석이 차려졌고, 울라손 영지의 더 낮은 계층 촌사람들을 위해 sub dio 곁상이 하나 더 펼쳐지게 되었다.\n\n이런 일을 준비하는 데 손을 대 보지 않은 사람은 손 양이 제 계획에서 부딪힌 갖가지 난관을 이해할 수 없다. 그가 최상급 고래수염으로 온몸이 짜여 요크셔 최고의 강철로 대갈못까지 박혀 있지 않았더라면 그 난관들 아래 주저앉고 말았으리라. 한때 유럽의 운명을 제 장화에 넣고 날랐던 사람에게 세상이 응당 무엇을 기대하는지를 플로머시 씨가 절감하고 있지 않았더라면 그 역시 손을 놓았을 터이고, 그렇게 버림받은 그의 여주인은 장대와 천막 천 사이에서 스러지고 말았을 것이다.\n\n무엇보다 먼저, 무시무시한 선 하나를 그어야 했다. 누구를 하하 도랑 안에 앉히고 누구를 밖에 앉힐 것인가? 무겁기 짝이 없는 물음에 늘 그러하듯, 생각 없는 이들은 이 물음에도 대뜸 답을 내놓을 것이다. 아, 주교와 그런 부류는 도랑 안이고, 농부 그린에이커와 그런 부류는 도랑 밖이지요. 옳은 말이오, 생각 없는 나의 벗이여. 그러나 그 '그런 부류'를 대체 누가 규정한단 말인가? 사교라는 것의 온갖 어려움은 바로 그런 규정에 있다. 주교를 잔디밭 안락의자에 앉히고 농부 그린에이커를 방목장의 긴 식탁 끝자리에 앉히는 일이야 어렵지 않지만, 룩어로프트 부인은 어디에 앉힐 것인가? 그 남편은 영지의 소작인이면서도 붉은 사냥옷을 입고 사냥을 나가고, 그 딸들은 바체스터의 유행하는 여학교에 다니며, 그는 제 농가를 로즈뱅크라 부르고 응접실에는 피아노포르테를 들여놓은 사람이 아닌가. 스스로를 '룩어로프트 아가씨들'이라 일컫는 그 딸들이 촌뜨기들 틈에 흡족히 앉아 있을 리 없다. 룩어로프트 부인은 고운 옷을 구겨 가며 긴 걸상에 몸을 밀어 넣고 착한 그린에이커 부인과 크림이며 오리 새끼며를 두고 허물없이 이야기할 사람이 아니다. 그러면서도 룩어로프트 부인은 손 집안과 그랜틀리 집안의 벗이 될 만한 사람이 못 되고, 여태 그들과 어울려 본 적도 없다. 그리고 만일 룩어로프트 부인이 상류 사교의 성역 안에 들어오도록 허락된다면, 세 딸을 거느리고 하하 도랑을 건너뛰도록 허락된다면, 다른 집안의 아내와 딸들은 어찌 안 된단 말인가? 그린에이커 부인은 지금이야 방목장으로 만족하고 있지만, 룩어로프트 부인이 잔디밭에 있는 것을 본다면 더는 만족하지 않을지도 모른다. 그리하여 가엾은 손 양은 참으로 고된 시간을 보냈다.\n\n그리고 손님들을 대형 천막과 응접실에 어떻게 나누어 배치할 것인가? 백작부인이 오기로 되어 있었고, 아너러블 존과 아너러블 조지가 있었으며, 레이디 어밀리아니 로지나니 마거리타니 하는 이들이 한 무리였다. 준남작이 세 사람 각각 준남작 부인을 데리고 왔고, 다들 알다시피 주교도 있었다. 그들을 잔디밭에 두면 아무도 응접실로 가지 않을 것이고, 응접실에 두면 아무도 천막으로 가지 않을 것이었다. 그는 나이 든 이들을 집 안에 붙들어 두고 잔디밭은 젊은 연인들에게 내줄까 생각했다. 그럴 바에는 차라리 당장 말벌집에 주저앉는 편이 나았을 것이다. 플로머시 씨는 그보다 사정을 잘 알고 있었다. “원, 마님도 참,” 그가 말했다. “나이 드신 부인네는 한 분도 안 오십니다. 마님과 늙은 클랜탠트럼 부인 말고는 한 분도 없습지요.”\n\n손 양은 개인적으로는 자기를 따로 떼어 준 이 구분을 제 분별력에 대한 찬사로 받아들였으나, 그렇다고 이번 자리에서 클랜탠트럼 부인과 단둘이 갇혀 있고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없었다. 그는 손님을 제멋대로 갈라놓겠다는 생각을 아주 버리고, 되도록 주교는 잔디밭에 백작부인은 집 안에 두고 준남작들은 여기저기 흩뿌려 매력을 고루 나누기로 마음먹었다. 룩어로프트네를 어찌할지는 플로머시 씨조차 결정하지 못했다. 그들은 운에 맡기는 수밖에 없었다. 초대장에는 소작인 전원을 초대했다는 말이 따로 적혀 있었으니, 나머지 소작인들과 섞이는 것이 싫다면 아마 오지 않을 만한 분별쯤은 있으리라.\n\n이어 플로머시 씨는 아너러블 존이니 아너러블 조지니 하는 이들이 일종의 물뭍 양쪽에 걸친 차림으로, 반은 낮 예복이고 반은 밤 예복인 채로, 새틴 목수건에 프록코트에 앵초빛 장갑에 반질반질한 장화 차림으로 나타나리라는 우려를 내놓았다. 그렇게 차려입고 온 이상 그들은 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 것도, 손 양이 그토록 애틋한 정성으로 마련한 어떤 경기에 끼는 것도 마다하리라는 것이었다. 그리고 존 나리들과 조지 나리들이 퀸틴을 향해 달리지 않는다면 다른 누구도 그러지 않으리라는 것은 손 양도 확신해도 좋았다.\n\n“하지만,” 근심에 짓눌릴 대로 짓눌린 그가 서글픈 목소리로 말했다. “경기가 있으리라고 각별히 알려 두었단 말이오.”\n\n“물론 있고말고요,” 플로머시 씨가 말했다. “다들 월계수 길에서 젊은 아가씨들과 어울려 노느라 바쁠 겁니다. 요새 젊은것들이 제일 좋아하는 놀이가 바로 그것 아니겠습니까. 젊은 사내들을 퀸틴 앞에 붙들어다 놓으시면 젊은 여자들은 모조리 뾰로통해질 겁니다.”\n\n“증조모들이 예전에 그리했듯 구경하고 있으면 될 것 아니오?” 손 양이 말했다.\n\n“요새 부인네들은 구경만으로는 만족을 못 하는 모양입니다. 사내들이 하는 것이면 저희도 하겠다는 거지요. 말들에 여자용 안장을 얹어 주시고 그이들도 퀸틴을 향해 달리게 하시면, 그야 더할 나위 없이 잘될 겁니다요.”\n\n손 양은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 플로머시 씨의 빈정거림에서 이 시대의 제 성(性)을 변호할 만한 근거가 없음을 느꼈다. 그는 언젠가 좀 격해진 순간에 “요새는 신사가 죄다 여자고 숙녀가 죄다 남자”라고 단언한 적이 있었다. 그는 이 시대의 타락한 성정을 바꿀 수 없었다. 그러나 사정이 그러하다면, 어찌하여 그런 저열한 취향을 지닌 이들의 오락을 제 손으로 차려 주는 수고를 떠맡는단 말인가? 이 물음을 그는 한 번 이상 스스로에게 던졌고, 그때마다 한숨으로밖에는 답할 수 없었다. 울라손 저택의 온갖 유서 깊은 명예를 어깨에 짊어진 제 친오라비 윌프레드조차, 플로머시 씨가 제법 그럴듯하게 표현한 대로 '퀸틴을 향해 달리는' 데 동의할지 대단히 의심스러웠다.\n\n그리하여 아침이 왔다. 울라손 집안은 일찌감치 움직이기 시작했다. 날이 밝기 한참 전부터 부엌에서는 요리사들이 요리를 하고 있었고, 동트기 무섭게 남자들은 식탁을 끌어내고 긴 걸상에 붉은 나사 천을 못질해 붙이고 있었다. 밤의 장막이 걷혀 무엇이든 내다볼 수 있게 되자마자 손 양은 얼마나 두려운 열심으로 날씨를 살폈던가! 적어도 이 점에서는 그를 슬프게 할 것이 없었다. 기압계는 사흘째 오르고 있었고, 아침은 가을이면 대개 맑고 마른 하루를 예고하는 그 흐릿하고 차고 한결같은 잿빛 안개와 함께 밝았다. 7시에 그는 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 아래층에 내려와 있었다. 손 양은 déshabilles 라는 근대의 사치를 조금도 알지 못했다. 오라비 앞에 코르셋 없이 나서는 일이나 양말 없이 나서는 일이나 그에게는 똑같이 생각조차 할 수 없는 노릇이었다 — 게다가 손 양의 코르셋은 결코 만만한 물건이 아니었다.\n\n그러면서도 막상 내려와 보니 할 일이 없었다. 그는 안절부절못하며 잔디밭으로 나갔다가 다시 부엌으로 들어왔다. 굽 높은 나막신을 신고 안달하며 방목장으로 나갔다. 그러고는 퀸틴을 세워 둔, 저택에 딸린 작은 공원으로 들어갔다. 기둥과 가로대와 회전축과 표적과 밀가루 자루가 모두 갖추어져 있었다. 그는 목수의 작업대 위에 올라서서 표적을 손으로 건드려 보았다. 표적은 놀랍도록 매끄럽게 돌아갔다. 회전축에는 기름이 완벽하게 먹여져 있었다. 그는 늙은 플로머시의 말을 곧이곧대로 따라 여자용 안장에 올라앉아 제 손으로 한번 겨루어 보고 싶은 마음이 들 지경이었다. 이런 재미를 두고 밍밍한 여학생과 월계수 나무 사이나 어슬렁거리는 편을 택하는 젊은이란 대체 어떤 인간이란 말인가, 하고 그는 생각했다. “좋아,” 그는 소리 내어 혼잣말을 했다. “한 사람이 말을 물가로 끌고 갈 수는 있어도 천 사람이 그 말에게 물을 먹일 수는 없는 법이지. 사정이 그러한 것을. 저들에게 이것을 즐길 기개가 없다면 그건 내 잘못이 아니오.” 그러고는 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n8시가 조금 지나 오라비가 내려왔고, 남매는 그의 서재에서 대충 때우는 아침을 먹었다. 차는 늘 쓰던 차 항아리 없이 우려냈고, 여느 때의 롤빵과 토스트도 없이 넘어갔다. 달걀도 없었으니, 이 교구의 달걀이란 달걀은 모조리 커스터드로 저어지거나 파이에 구워 넣어지거나 바닷가재 샐러드용으로 삶아진 까닭이었다. 신선한 버터도 넉넉지 못했고, 손 씨는 좋아하는 방식대로 맵게 구워 내지 않은 닭다리를 그냥 먹어야 했다.\n\n“퀸틴을 살펴보았는데, 윌프레드,” 그가 말했다. “아주 멀쩡한 것 같더이다.”\n\n“오 — 아, 그렇더군요,” 그가 말했다. “어제 내가 보았을 때도 그런 것 같더이다.” 손 씨는 누이의 경기 애호에 슬슬 넌더리가 나던 참이었고, 특히 이 퀸틴 기둥에는 아무 애정도 없었다.\n\n“아침을 들고 나서 한번 시험해 보시면 좋겠소,” 그가 말했다. “마크 안토니에 안장을 얹으면 되고, 창대도 거기 다 갖추어져 있소. 마크 안토니가 그리 빠르지 못하겠다 싶으면 밀가루 자루는 떼어 놓아도 되고 말이오.” 오라비의 낯빛이 이 자그마한 제안에 온전히 찬동하는 기색은 아님을 보고 손 양이 이렇게 덧붙인 것이었다.\n\n그런데 마크 안토니로 말하자면 값나가는 늙은 사냥말로서, 손 씨가 평소에 바라는 바에는 더할 나위 없이 알맞은 말이었다. 울타리 앞에서 서두르는 법이 없었고 발은 지극히 확실했으며, 진창에서도 잘 달렸고 길 위에서도 안전했다. 그러나 이 말은 여태 퀸틴을 향해 달려 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 손 씨는 밀가루 자루가 있건 없건 그런 시험에 말을 내몰 마음이 없었다. 그는 이러쿵저러쿵 얼버무리다가 결국 마크 안토니가 놀라 옆으로 뛸까 걱정된다고 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그 땅딸막한 승용마를 타 보시오.” 지칠 줄 모르는 손 양이 말했다.\n\n“그놈은 약을 먹이는 중이오.” 윌프레드가 말했다.\n\n“베엘제붑 망아지가 있잖소.” 누이가 말했다. “방금 피터가 그놈을 운동시키는 것을 보았으니 마구간에 있는 줄은 내가 아오.”\n\n“모니카, 그놈은 어찌나 사나운지 내가 다루는 것만도 벅차오. 그런 덜컹거리는 고물을 향해 그놈을 몰았다가는 제 몸도 내 몸도 결딴내고 말 거요.”\n\n덜컹거리는 고물이라니! 그토록 노심초사하며 세워 놓은 퀸틴이, 이 고장의 건장한 자영농들을 즐겁게 하려고 마련한 그 놀이가, 그토록 많은 선조들의 애호로 영예를 입어 온 그 경기가! 다른 사람도 아닌 제 오라비의 입에서 그런 이름으로 불리는 것을 듣자니 가슴이 미어졌다. 이 세상에 남은 것은 남매 둘뿐이었고, 오라비를 노엽게 할 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것은 손 양이 평생 제 처신을 다스려 온 규칙 가운데 하나였다. 유서 깊은 영국의 관습에 대한 오라비의 무심함 때문에 그는 여러 번 속을 썩였으나, 언제나 잠자코 썩였다. 가문의 어른을 그 집 안에서 나무라서는 안 된다는 것이 그의 신조의 일부였고, 손 양은 제 신조대로 살아왔다. 그러나 이번만은 시험이 컸다. 늙은 뺨에 홍조가 오르고 여전히 빛나는 눈에는 불길이 일었으나, 그래도 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 적어도 오라비에게만은 그날 다시 퀸틴 이야기를 꺼내지 않으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n그는 말없이 슬픔에 잠긴 채 차를 홀짝이며, 위대한 선조 엘프리드가 노르만 침입자에 맞서 울라손을 지켜 낸 영광의 나날을 아프게 그리워했다. 이제 그 집안에는 그런 기개가 남아 있지 않았다. 오직 제 가슴속에서 타는 작고 쓸모없는 불티 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 그 자신은, 바로 이 순간 그 노르만인들의 후손을, 프랑스식 이름을 지녔으며 울라손의 문지방을 넘어 주는 것만으로도 이 집에 과분한 영광을 베푼다고 여길 허영 많고 오만한 백작부인을 대접하는 데 골몰하고 있지 않은가? 드 코시 백작의 아들인 아너러블 존이 색슨 자영농들과 나란히 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 일이 있을 법한가? 그리고 제 오라비의 손님들이 하지 않으려는 일을 어찌 제 오라비에게 기대한단 말인가?\n\n제 생각이 실행 불가능하다는 어렴풋하고 희미한 관념이 그의 머리를 스쳐 갔다. 같은 흙 위에서 살도록 운명 지어진 종족들이 서로에게 자리를 내주고 서로의 소일거리를 받아들이는 것이 어쩌면 필연인지도 몰랐다. 다섯 세기가 넘도록 가까이 부대끼고서도 노르만인이 노르만인으로, 색슨인이 색슨인으로 남아 있기란 어쩌면 불가능한 일인지도 몰랐다. 어쩌면 결국 이웃들이 제 자신보다 지혜로운지도 몰랐다. 이런 생각이 이따금 손 양의 마음에 떠올라 그를 제법 서글프게 했다. 그러나 그가 그토록 아끼는 퀸틴이란 것이 실은 노르만 기사의 놀이를 근래에 본뜬 것이요, 고귀한 마상 시합을 색슨 자영농의 취향과 습속에 맞추어 고친 것임은 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 이 점을 그는 알지 못했고, 그것을 가르쳐 주는 것은 잔인한 일이었으리라.\n\n손 씨는 누이의 눈에 맺힌 눈물을 보고 제 경멸조의 표현을 뉘우쳤다. 누이의 모든 변덕을 존중해야 한다는 것은 그에게도 구속력 있는 법이었다. 그가 누이를 위해 지키는 정도는 누이가 그를 위해 지키는 정도만큼 굳지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그 뜻만은 똑같이 좋았고, 그것을 잊었음을 깨달을 때마다 그것은 그에게 슬픔거리였다.\n\n“모니카,” 그가 말했다. “내가 미안하오. 그 놀이를 헐뜯을 뜻은 조금도 없었소. 덜컹거리는 고물이라 한 것은 내 나이의 사람에게 그렇다는 말이었을 뿐이오. 내가 젊은 사람이 아니라는 걸 그대는 늘 잊어버리지 않소.”\n\n“그대가 늙은이가 아니라는 건 내가 잘 아오, 윌프레드.” 그가 사과를 마음으로 받아들이고 뺨에 눈물이 아직 남은 채로 오라비에게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n“내가 스물다섯이나 서른이라면,” 그가 말을 이었다. “하루 종일 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 것보다 더 좋은 일은 없을 거요.”\n\n“하지만 그대는 사냥이나 총질을 못 할 만큼 늙지는 않았잖소.” 그가 말했다. “도랑과 산울타리를 뛰어넘을 수 있다면 퀸틴쯤이야 능히 돌려놓을 수 있을 것이오.”\n\n“하지만 내가 산울타리를 넘어갈 때는 말이오 — 그것도 그리 자주 하는 일은 아니오만 — 아무튼 산울타리를 넘어갈 때는 밀가루 자루가 뒤에서 쫓아오지는 않소. 내가 뒤통수에 온통 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 채 백작부인을 모시고 조찬에 들어가는 꼴을 한번 생각해 보시오.”\n\n손 양은 더 말하지 않았다. 백작부인을 들먹이는 것이 마음에 들지 않았다. 울라손의 경기가 레이디 드 코시 한 사람에게 바쳐야 할 사사로운 시중 때문에 방해받아야 한다는 생각으로는 도저히 흡족할 수 없었다. 그러나 그는 이 문제를 더 밀어붙여야 아무 소용이 없음을 알았다. 손 씨는 퀸틴을 면제받는 것으로 정해졌고, 손 양은 제가 몹시 아끼는 젊은 기사 하나에게 모든 것을 걸기로 마음먹었다. 그가 늘 말하기를, 그 젊은이야말로 이 시대 젊은이들의 본보기요 영국 자영농의 훌륭한 표본이었다.\n\n이 젊은이는 농부 그린에이커의 맏아들이었는데, 사실을 말하자면 어린 시절부터 손 양의 마음을 손바닥 들여다보듯 정확히 재고 있었다. 소년 시절 그는 손 양에게서 사과와 용돈과, 수없이 저지른 무단 침입에 대한 용서를 얻어 내는 데 한 번도 실패한 적이 없었다. 그리고 이제 청년이 된 지금은 그에 못지않게 값진 특권과 면제를 누렸다. 9월이면 하루 이틀 사냥이 허락되었고, 지주의 말들을 길들였으며, 과수원에서 묘목을 얻고 정원에서 꽃뿌리를 얻었으며, 작은 강의 낚시는 통째로 제 손아귀에 있었다. 그는 제 아버지의 말을 타고 나와 퀸틴 기둥 앞에서 본을 보이기로 약속해 둔 터였다. 젊은 그린에이커가 무엇을 하든 다른 이들은 그를 따라 할 것이었다. 어린 룩어로프트네야 멀찍이 물러서 있을지 몰라도, 해리 그린에이커가 본을 보이면 울라손의 나머지 젊은이들은 반드시 나서고야 말 것이었다. 그리하여 손 양은 귀하신 존이며 조지며 하는 이들을 단념하고, 선조들이 그러했듯 울라손 땅에서 자란 토박이의 힘줄과 근육에 기대기로 마음먹었다.\n\n9시쯤 되자 아래 계층 사람들이 방목장과 공원에 모여들기 시작했다. 플로머시 씨와, 그의 대리로 선서를 하고 치안 유지와 경기 진행을 돕게 된 수석 정원사와 수석 마부의 감독 아래였다. 이웃 마을의 젊은 축 가운데 여럿은 좋은 일은 아무리 많아도 지나치지 않다고 여겼는지 아주 이른 시각에 와 있었고, 저택과 교회 사이의 길은 대문이 열리기 한참 전부터 사람들로 붐볐다.\n\n그러자 또 하나의 커다란 난관이 일어났는데, 플로머시 씨가 진작에 내다보고 나름대로 대비해 둔 난관이었다. 손 양의 환대에 끼고자 하는 이들 가운데 더러는 초대라는 예비 절차에 대해 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 까다롭지 못했다. 그들은 필시 자기들이 실수로 빠뜨려졌으려니 여겼고, 윗사람들이라면 그것을 분개할 일로 삼았겠지만 그들은 사람 좋게 그 홀대를 참아 넘겼으며, 참아 넘겼다는 표시로 나들이옷을 차려입고 대문 앞에 나타났다.\n\n그러나 플로머시 씨는 누가 환영받을 사람이고 누가 아닌지를 훤히 알고 있었다. 초대받지 않은 이들 가운데서도 더러는 들여보내 주었다. “너무 까다롭게 굴지 마시오, 플로머시,” 여주인이 그에게 이렇게 말해 두었던 것이다. “아이들에게는 특히 그러하오. 근처에 사는 아이들이거든 들여보내 주시오.”\n\n이 언질에 따라 플로머시 씨는 눈이 반짝이는 개구쟁이 여럿과, 이 영지와는 아무 상관도 없는 말끔한 차림의 처녀들과 그 짝들을 더러 들여보내 주었다. 그러나 도시 사람들에게는 요지부동이었다. 그날 바체스터의 도제들이 여럿 그곳에 나타나 애처롭게 사정하기를, 자기는 한 주 내내 울라손에서 쓸 안장과 장화를 만들었노라고, 말들에게 먹일 약을 지었노라고, 부엌에 들어갈 고기를 발랐노라고 호소했다. 그런 주장은 하나도 통하지 않았다. 플로머시 씨는 도시의 도제 따위는 알지 못했다. 그가 들일 사람은 이 영지의 소작인과 일꾼들이었다. 손 양이 바체스터 시 전체를 들일 생각은 없으시다. 그런 식이었다.\n\n그럼에도 하루가 절반도 지나기 전에 이 모든 것이 소용없는 일임이 드러났다. 오려고 마음먹은 사람이면 거의 누구나 공원 안으로 들어왔고, 문지기들의 소임은 잔치 음식이 차려진 식탁 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 거기서도 자격 없이 자리를 요구하는 이가 많았는데, 그 자리와 음식이 지닌 값어치보다 더 큰 소동을 일으키지 않고서는 그들을 떼어 낼 도리가 없었다.\n\n# 제36장. 울라손 운동회 — 제1막\n\n문명 생활에서 이른바 손님을 접대한다는 일 — 엄밀한 진실성은 별로 따지지 않고 흔히들 그렇게 부른다 — 이 얼마나 성가신지를 헤아려 보면, 사람들이 그토록 그 일에 열을 올린다는 것은 놀랍기 그지없다. 그 quid pro quo가 대체 무엇인지를 알아내기가 어렵다. 그토록 공들인 잔치를 베푸는 이들이, 그리고 그것을 성공적으로 치러 내겠다는 헛된 희망 속에서 그토록 애쓰고 법석을 떠는 이들이, 정말로 남이 베푼 잔치를 즐긴다면 그 일은 이해할 만하다. 공평하다는 감각이 남녀로 하여금 남을 위하여, 남이 저를 위하여 겪었던 그 고초를 마찬가지로 겪게 만들 것이다. 그러나 그들은 하나같이 남의 집에 나가는 것도 손님을 맞는 것 못지않게 지겨운 일이라고 공언하며, 정작 나가 있는 그들을 보고 있노라면 그 말을 믿지 않을 수 없다.\n\n접대라니! 누가 감히 제 힘을 그토록 자신하고 제 능력을 그토록 믿어 의심치 않기에, 제 손님을 접대할 수 있노라고 큰소리칠 것인가? 광대라면 더러 해내고, 짧은 치마에 살을 채운 분홍 다리를 한 무희도 더러 해내며, 어쩌면 가수도 이따금은 해낼 것이다. 그러나 그 밖에는 이 접대라는 기예에서 성공을 거두는 일이 흔치 않다. 젊은 사내와 처녀들이 끼리끼리 짝을 지어, 자연이 그리 시키는 까닭에 봄철의 새들처럼 짝을 이루는 것 — 그들은 소박한 방식으로나마 서로를 정말로 접대한다. 그 밖에는 시도라도 해 보는 이가 드물다.\n\n부인네들은 제 집을 열 때, 아마도 저의 무능을 겸손히 시인하는 뜻이겠으나, 주로 밀랍 초와 실내 장식에 기댄다. 신사들은 제 흰 조끼를 믿는 듯하다. 여기에 좀 더 감각적인 이들의 즐거움을 위하여 샴페인과, 유행이 아직 먹을 것으로 쳐 주는 몇 가지 좋은 음식이 더해진다. 이 점에서조차 세상은 나빠지고 있다. 훌륭한 수프는 이제 모두 금기가 되었고, 늘 드나드는 벗들의 집 — 별 볼 일 없는 법정 변호사, 의사, 관청 서기 따위의 집(우리가 모두 언제나 제복 입은 하인들의 낙원에 둘러싸인 귀인으로 살 수는 없는 노릇이니) — 에 가면 양고기 한 점 뒤에 마무리랍시고 식은 감자 한 알을 건네받는다. 아, 옆자리 사람에게 “존스, 순무 으깬 것을 좀 드릴까요? 양배추를 조금 청해도 되겠습니까?” 하고 말할 수 있던 그 행복한 시절이여! 그리고 존스 부인, 스미스 양과 — 존스 집안 전부, 스미스 집안 전부와 — 잔을 나누던 그 즐거움이여! 요즈음의 이런 습속이 더 알뜰한 것만은 분명하다.\n\n그러나 손 양은 현대의 닳고 닳은 길에서 벗어나 보려고 대담하게 시도했고, 제 손님들을 정말로 접대해 보겠다는 적극적인 노력을 기울였다. 아아! 그 성과는 그저 그런 정도였다. 손님들에게는 저마다 제가 가는 길이 있었고, 그들은 그 여자의 길로 가려 하지 않았다. 그 여자가 피리를 불었으나 그들은 춤추지 않았다. 그 여자는 건포도와 밀가루와 달걀과 설탕절임으로 만든 정직하고 좋은 집 케이크를 내놓았으나, 그들은 바체스터 제과점에서 파는 값싼 웨이퍼 과자를, 백묵과 고무와 불순물 섞인 설탕을 먹고자 했다. 가엾은 손 양이여! 지나간 행복한 날들의 영광을 되불러 오려다 헛되이 애쓴 정직한 영혼이 그대가 처음은 아니로다! 유행이 레이디 드 코시에게 12시 déjeuner에 초대받았거든 3시에 가는 것이 마땅하다고 일러 준다면, 그대의 어떤 웅변으로도 시간을 좀 더 지키는 편이 낫다는 것을 그 여자에게 가르치지는 못하리라.\n\n그 여자는 벗들에게 12시에 오라고 청하면서 그것이 진심임을 특별히 믿어 달라고 당부해 두었으니 2시면 그들이 천막 안에 편안히 자리 잡은 것을 볼 수 있으리라고 다정하게 생각해 두었다. 헛된 여자여 — 아니 차라리 무지한 여자여 — 제가 늙어 가는 동안 세상이 목격한 저 문명의 진보를 알지 못하는 여자여. 12시가 되자 그 여자는 혼자였다. 여러 벌 되는 옷 가운데 가장 새것을 갖추어 화려하게 차려입되 신만은 튼튼한 것을 신고, 머리에는 실용적인 보닛을 쓰고, 어깨에는 따뜻하고 값진 숄을 걸친 차림이었다. 그렇게 차려입고 그 여자는 천막을 들여다보고, 하하 도랑까지 나가 적어도 젊은것들만은 저희끼리 즐기고 있음을 확인하고, 도랑 너머로 그린에이커 부인과 한마디 나누고, 퀸틴을 한 번 살펴보았다. 젊은 농부 서넛이 그 장치를 빙빙 돌려 가며, 이 놀이를 고안한 사람이 전혀 의도하지 않은 방식으로 밀가루 자루를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있었다. 그러나 말에 오른 선수는 하나도 없었다. 손 양은 시계를 보았다. 아직 12시 15분이었고, 해리 그린에이커는 12시 반이 되기 전에는 시작하지 않기로 되어 있었다.\n\n손 양은 백작부인이 왔는데 맞아 줄 사람이 아무도 없을까 두려워, 평소보다는 조금 빠른 걸음으로 응접실로 돌아왔다. 서두를 것도 없었다. 거기에는 아무도 없었으니까. 12시 반에 그 여자는 부엌을 들여다보았고, 1시 15분 전에는 오라비가 곁에 와 주었으며, 바로 그때 첫 번째 상류층 손님이 도착했다. 클랜탠트럼 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 있었다.\n\n사실 전갈은 필요하지도 않았다. 그 착한 부인이 안뜰을 가로질러 집으로 걸어오면서, 바체스터에서 자기를 태우고 온 가엾은 마부를 나무라는 목소리가 다 들렸던 것이다. 그 순간 손 양은 다른 손님들이 더 상류층이어서 클랜탠트럼 부인의 분노가 퍼붓는 것을 면했다는 사실에 감사하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“오, 손 양, 이것 좀 보시오!” 응접실에 들어서기가 무섭게 부인이 말했다. “내 로클로르 좀 보시오. 아주 못 쓰게 되었소, 그것도 영영. 오늘은 우리 모두 잘 차려입기를 바라시는 줄 알았기에 굳이 이걸 입고 나선 것인데, 그러면서도 어쩐지 꺼림칙하기는 했다오. 아이고, 아이고! 야드당 25실링짜리였는데.”\n\n바체스터의 역마들이 클랜탠트럼 부인이 마차에서 내리려는 바로 그 순간에 어떤 불운한 방식으로 말썽을 부려, 하마터면 부인을 바퀴 밑으로 넘어뜨릴 뻔했던 것이다.\n\n클랜탠트럼 부인은 지나간 시절에 속한 사람이었고, 그래서 그 밖에는 딱히 내세울 것이 없었음에도 손 양은 그 여자를 어느 정도 좋아했다. 손 양은 로클로르를 손질하도록 내보내고, 제 옷장에서 가장 좋은 숄 하나를 꺼내 빌려 주었다.\n\n다음에 온 사람은 애러빈 씨였는데, 그는 곧장 클랜탠트럼 부인의 불운과, 마차 주인에게도 마부 아이에게도 삯을 치르지 않겠다는 부인의 결심을 전해 들었다. 물론 부인의 말인즉, 그 마음을 밝히기 전에 우선 집까지 태워다 달라고 할 작정이라는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 부인들의 겉옷을 두는 현관방 쪽에서 옷자락 스치는 소리가 한바탕 들리더니, 문이 활짝 열리며 하인이 그리 자신 있지 못한 목소리로 룩어로프트 부인과 룩어로프트 양들과 오거스터스 룩어로프트 씨의 도착을 알렸다.\n\n가엾은 사람! — 우리가 말하는 것은 그 하인이다. 그는 룩어로프트 부인이 거기 올 자격이 없다는 것, 아무도 그 여자를 원하지 않는다는 것, 환영받지 못하리라는 것을 누구보다 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 목이 깊이 파인 옷에 소매가 짧고 야드당 8실링짜리 새틴을 걸친 뚱뚱한 부인에게 천막을 잘못 찾아왔다고 말할 용기가 그에게는 없었다. 흰 무도화를 신고 긴 장갑을 낀 아가씨들에게 방목장에 그대들 자리가 마련되어 있다고 넌지시 일러 줄 엄두도 내지 못했다. 그리하여 룩어로프트 부인은 뜻을 이루었고, 파수를 뚫었으며, 성채 안으로 들어섰다. 거기서 편치 못한 시간을 보내야 하리라는 것쯤은 이미 짐작하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 그 무엇도, 그린에이커 부인 따위가 공원에서 쟁기꾼 아이들과 어울려 거니는 동안 저는 잔디밭에서 지주와 손 양과, 백작부인과 주교와 이 고장 유지들과 나란히 어울렸노라고 자랑할 권리를 그 여자에게서 빼앗지 못할 것이었다. 룩어로프트 부인이 크게 한 점 딴 셈이었으니, 이제부터 바체스터의 상인들이 그 남편에게 조금도 망설이지 않는 필치로 T. 룩어로프트 귀하라고 써 보내리라고 기대해도 무방했다.\n\n룩어로프트 부인의 뱃심은 무슨 일이든 뚫고 나아가게 했고, 그 여자는 의기양양하게 울라손 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다. 그러나 그 자식들은 저희가 받은 대접의 종류에 다소 기가 죽었다. 제 손님을 모욕하는 것은 손 양의 마음에 없는 일이었으나, 그런 뻔뻔함을 못 본 체 넘기는 것 또한 그 성미에 없는 일이었다.\n\n“오, 룩어로프트 부인, 부인이었구려.” 손 양이 말했다. “따님들과 아드님까지 함께요? 그래요, 와 주어 매우 반갑소만, 우리가 모두 바깥으로 나갈 참인데 그렇게 목이 파인 옷을 입고 오셨으니 안됐구려. 무엇이든 빌려 드릴까요?”\n\n“아이고, 아닙니다요, 고맙습니다, 손 양.” 어미가 말했다. “딸애들이나 저나 바깥에 나갈 적에는 목이 파인 옷에 아주 익숙합지요.”\n\n“정말 그러시오?” 손 양이 몸서리를 치며 말했다. 그러나 그 몸서리는 룩어로프트 부인에게 가닿지 못했다.\n\n“그런데 룩어로프트는 어디 있소?” 집주인이 제 소작인의 아내를 맞으러 다가오며 말했다. 그 집안의 허물이 무엇이든 간에, 그는 그들이 소작료만은 잘 치른다는 것을 잊을 수 없었다. 그러니 그들을 쌀쌀맞게 대할 마음이 없었다.\n\n“어찌나 머리가 아프다는지요, 손 씨!” 룩어로프트 부인이 말했다. “정말이지 꼼짝을 못 하지 뭡니까요. 안 그랬으면 이런 날에 빠질 사람이 아닌 줄은 잘 아시지 않습니까요.”\n\n“저런.” 손 양이 말했다. “그렇게 편찮으시다면 부인도 곁에 있고 싶겠구려.”\n\n“천만에요!” 룩어로프트 부인이 말했다. “천만의 말씀입니다, 손 양. 그저 담증일 뿐이고요, 그이는 그럴 적엔 곁에 아무도 못 오게 합지요.”\n\n그러나 사실인즉, 룩어로프트 씨는 아내보다 분별이 더 있었는지 아니면 용기가 덜했는지, 손 양의 응접실에 밀고 들어가지 않기로 한 것이었다. 그리고 아내가 귀족들 틈에 끼어 있는 마당에 저 혼자 평민들 사이로 갈 수도 없는 노릇이었으므로, 그는 로즈뱅크에 남아 있는 것이 가장 상책이라고 생각했다.\n\n룩어로프트 부인은 곧 소파 하나를 차지했고 룩어로프트 양들은 의자 둘을 차지했으며, 오거스터스 씨는 문 가까이 서 있었다. 그들은 때가 되어 넷이 함께 식당 식탁의 맨 끄트머리에 앉게 될 때까지 그렇게 있었다.\n\n그다음에 그랜틀리 일행이 왔다 — 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인과 두 딸, 그리고 그윈 박사와 하딩 씨였다. 운 나쁘게도 그들 바로 뒤를 스탠호프 박사의 마차가 따라왔다. 엘리너는 마차 창밖을 내다보다가 형부가 부인들이 내리는 것을 거들고 있는 것을 보고, 들킬까 겁이 나서 자리 안쪽으로 몸을 물렸다. 오는 길이 지긋지긋했던 것이다. 슬로프 씨의 정중함은 여느 때보다도 더 느끼했고, 이제 와 보니, 딱히 트집 잡을 만한 말을 그가 실제로 한 것도 아닌데, 그 여자는 난생처음으로 그가 자기에게 구애할 작정이라는 의심을 품게 되었다. 자기가 저 사내를 좋아한다고 세상이 여겨도 할 말이 없을 만한 처신을 해 왔다는 것이 결국 사실이었단 말인가? 결국 부주교와 애러빈 씨가 옳고 제가 틀렸을 수도 있단 말인가? 샬럿 스탠호프 또한 슬로프 씨를 지켜보고 있었고, 제 오라비가 그 미망인을 차지할 작정이라면 더는 시간을 끌어서는 안 되겠다는 결론에 이르렀다. 버티가 자기들보다 먼저 울라손에 가 있도록 손을 써 두지 않은 것이 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 처제가 슬로프 씨와 함께 있는 것을 보지 못했으나, 애러빈 씨는 보았다. 애러빈 씨는 그랜틀리 부인을 맞으려고 손 씨와 함께 현관까지 나왔고, 그 일행이 모두 지나가고 나서도 안뜰에 남아 있었다. 엘리너는 그럴듯하게 버틸 수 있는 데까지 마차 안에 뭉그적거리고 있었으나, 문에 가장 가까이 앉은 사람이 자기였고, 슬로프 씨가 먼저 내려서 손을 내밀자 그 손을 잡는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다. 그랜틀리 부인이 안쪽의 누군가와 악수를 나누는 동안 열린 문가에 서 있던 애러빈 씨는, 한 성직자가 마차에서 내리는 것을 보고 그가 슬로프 씨임을 대번에 알아보았으며, 이어 그 성직자가 볼드 부인을 부축해 내리는 것을 보았다. 그만큼을 보고 나서 애러빈 씨는 속이 몹시 상한 채 그랜틀리 부인을 따라 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n그러나 엘리너는 당장 더 창피를 당하는 일만은 면했다. 스탠호프 박사가 안뜰을 가로지르는 동안 팔을 내주었고, 슬로프 씨는 하는 수 없이 제 정성을 샬럿에게 허비할 수밖에 없었던 것이다.\n\n그들이 집 안으로 들어가고 집을 지나 잔디밭으로 나서기가 무섭게, 요란한 바퀴 소리와 함께, 큰 어른과 큰 부인이 세상을 지나가면서 낼 자격이 있는 그런 소음을 일으키며 프라우디 내외가 마차를 몰고 들어섰다. 문간에 온 것이 예사 손님이 아니라는 것이 곧 드러났다. 하인 하나가 다른 하인에게 주교님이라고 속삭였고, 그 말은 이내 그곳에 얼쩡거리던 모든 사람과 낯선 마부와 마차꾼들에게 퍼졌다. 주교와 그 “부인”이 안뜰을 가로질러 걸어가는 것을 구경하려고 제법 작은 행렬이 이루어졌으니, 이 착한 어른은 세인트 이월드 교구에서 교회가 이토록 존경받는 것을 보고 흐뭇해했다.\n\n이제 손님들이 빠르게 몰려들어 잔디밭이 붐비기 시작하고 방이 가득 차기 시작했다. 목소리들이 웅웅거리고, 비단이 비단에 스치고, 모슬린이 모슬린에 구겨졌다. 손 양은 아까보다 기분이 좋아져서 다시 제 운동회를 떠올렸다. 잔디밭 저편 끝에는 과녁과 활과 화살이 마련되어 있었다. 그곳에서는 이 저택의 정원이 제법 넓게 방목장 쪽으로 밀고 들어가 있어 궁술 애호가들이 놀기에 넉넉한 자리를 내주었다. 손 양은 활을 당길 줄 아는 디아나의 딸들을 그러모아 과녁 앞으로 이끌었다. 그랜틀리 집안 딸들과 프라우디 집안 딸들과 채드윅 집안 딸들이 있었고, 덩치 큰 학무 참사원의 두 딸과 놀 양이 있었다. 그들과 함께 프레더릭 채드윅과 오거스터스 채드윅, 놀 파크의 젊은 놀, 엘름스의 프랭크 포스터, 하이 스트리트의 잘나가는 사무 변호사 벨럼 디즈 씨, 그리고 그린 목사와 브라운 목사와 화이트 목사가 따라나섰는데, 이 세 사람은 마땅한 도리로서 프라우디 양 세 자매의 걸음을 뒤따랐다.\n\n“포스터 씨는 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 본 적이 있소?” 손 양이 일행과 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 걸으며 말했다.\n\n“퀸틴이요?” 스스로 말타기에는 일가견이 있다고 여기는 젊은 포스터가 말했다. “그게 무슨 문 같은 것입니까, 손 양?”\n\n손 양은 자기가 말한 그 고귀한 놀이를 설명해 주어야 했고, 프랭크 포스터는 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 본 적이 한 번도 없노라고 실토해야 했다.\n\n“가서 구경하겠소?” 손 양이 말했다. “그대가 빠져도 여기는 사람이 넉넉하니, 마음이 내킨다면 말이오.”\n\n“뭐, 저는 상관없습니다.” 프랭크가 말했다. “부인들도 함께 가도 되는 것이겠지요.”\n\n“오, 그럼요.” 손 양이 말했다. “가고 싶은 이들은요. 포스터 씨가 말을 타겠다면 다들 그 솜씨를 보러 갈 것이 분명하오.”\n\n포스터 씨는 바로 어제 런던에서 도착한, 나무랄 데 없는 바지를 내려다보았다. 적어도 그의 생각으로는 그것이야말로 피크닉이나 야외 연회에 꼭 맞는 물건이었으나, 그것을 입고 말을 탈 준비까지는 되어 있지 않았다. 게다가 손 양이 생생하게 묘사해 준 밀가루 자루에 뒤에서 얻어맞는다는 발상 앞에서 그도 손 씨보다 더 마음이 동하지는 않았다.\n\n“글쎄요, 말타기는 어떨지 모르겠습니다, 손 양.” 그가 말했다. “아무래도 채비가 덜 된 듯합니다.”\n\n손 양은 한숨을 쉬었으나 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그 여자는 궁술 애호가들을 활과 화살에 맡겨 두고 집 쪽으로 돌아왔다. 그러나 작은 공원으로 들어가는 어귀를 지나면서, 더 상류한 손님들을 저 사내다운 놀이에 끌어들일 수 없다면 적어도 제가 가 있음으로써 자작농들에게 힘을 실어 줄 수는 있으리라고 생각했다. 그리하여 그 여자는 다시 한번 퀸틴 기둥으로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n거기서 그 여자는 크게 기뻐하며, 해리 그린에이커가 장대를 손에 쥐고 말에 올라 채비를 갖춘 채 동무들에게 둘러싸여 어서 달려 보라는 부추김을 받고 있는 것을 보았다. 그 여자는 조금 떨어져 서서 흡족하다는 표시로 그에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n“이제 시작해도 되겠습니까요, 마님?” 해리가 제법 어설픈 손놀림으로 긴 장대를 만지작거리며 말했다. 그동안 그 밑의 말은 그런 무기를 든 기수가 익숙하지 않아 불안하게 몸을 움직였다.\n\n“그래요, 그래요.” 어쩌다 농가 마당에서 굴러 나온 통을 뒤집어 놓은 그 위에 미의 여왕처럼 의기양양하게 올라선 손 양이 말했다.\n\n“그럼 갑니다요.” 해리가 힘차게 달려 나갈 기세를 얻으려고 말을 빙 돌리며 말했다. 퀸틴 기둥은 바로 그의 앞에 서 있었고, 그가 창으로 찔러야 할 네모난 판자도 마침 가는 길목에 놓여 있었다. 그 한복판을 제대로 맞히고 그러면서도 속도를 늦추지 않는다면, 기둥의 가로대 반대편 끝에 매달려 있다가 판자가 맞는 순간 휙 돌아오는 밀가루 자루가 닿지 않는 데까지 그가 빠져나가게 되어 있었다. 반대로 기수가 속도를 늦추면 바로 뒤통수에 밀가루 자루를 한 대 얻어맞고, 구경꾼들의 큰 즐거움을 위하여 제 서투름의 표를 온몸에 뒤집어쓰게 되어 있었다.\n\n해리 그린에이커는 제 여주인을 섬기다가 밀가루를 뒤집어쓰는 것쯤은 마다하지 않았고, 그리하여 나름대로 최선을 다해 창을 겨드랑이에 끼고서 씩씩하게 말에 박차를 댔다. 그러나 이 방면의 그의 솜씨는 대단치 않았고 장비도 아마 그리 좋지 못했다. 그 결과 출발하면서 뜻하지 않게 장대로 제 말의 옆머리를 치고 말았다. 짐승은 옆으로 비켜서며 놀라 뛰더니 퀸틴을 한참 벗어나 내달렸다. 말을 다루는 데는 익숙했으나 12피트짜리 장대를 팔에 낀 채 그리하는 데는 익숙하지 않았던 해리는 오른손을 내려 고삐를 잡았고, 그 바람에 창끝이 땅에 닿아 말의 다리 사이로 끼어들었다. 기수와 말과 장대가 함께 굴렀다. 젊은 그린에이커는 말 머리 너머로 6피트쯤 나가떨어졌고, 가엾은 손 양은 하마터면 통에서 굴러떨어져 혼절할 뻔했다.\n\n“어머나, 저 사람 죽었네.” 그가 떨어질 때 가까이 있던 여자가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n“주여 저이를 굽어살피소서! 가엾은 어미, 가엾은 어미!” 또 다른 여자가 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 세상천지 어디서나 저런 위험한 놀음은 다 몹쓸 것이지.” 한 늙은 노파가 말했다.\n\n“목이 부러진 게 틀림없어. 사람이 목을 부러뜨린 적이 있다면 딱 저 꼴이지.” 네 번째 사람이 말했다.\n\n가엾은 손 양. 그 여자는 이 말을 다 들으면서도 아주 혼절하지는 않았다. 스스로도 죽을 만큼 속이 메스꺼운 채, 되는대로 사람들 틈을 헤치고 나아갔다. 아, 저 아이의 어미 — 가엾은 어미! 자신을 무슨 수로 용서한단 말인가. 그 순간의 고뇌는 끔찍했다. 앞쪽에서 서너 사람이 겨우 몸을 일으킨 말을 둘러싸고 있어 가엾은 젊은이가 누워 있는 자리까지 가기도 어려웠으나, 마침내 그 여자는 그 젊은 농부 곁에 이르렀다.\n\n“말이 상했습니까요? 제발 그것만 말씀해 주십시오. 무릎이 까졌습니까요?” 해리가 천천히 몸을 일으켜 오른손으로 왼쪽 어깨를 문지르며, 오직 제 말의 다리만 생각하며 말했다. 손 양은 그가 목도 부러지지 않았고 뼈 하나 부러지지 않았으며 크게 다친 데도 없다는 것을 이내 알게 되었다. 그러나 그때부터 그 여자는 두 번 다시 누구에게도 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 보라고 부추기지 않았다.\n\n엘리너는 예의에 어긋나지 않을 만큼만 있다가 곧 스탠호프 박사 곁을 떠나 아버지를 찾아 나섰고, 잔디밭에서 애러빈 씨와 함께 있는 아버지를 만났다. 두 사람이 함께 있는 것이 싫지 않았다. 그 여자는 적어도 아버지의 마음에서만은 자기를 두고 퍼진 그 소문을 씻어 내고 싶었고, 애러빈 씨에 대해서도 그럴 수 있다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다. 그 여자는 등 뒤로 다가가 제 팔을 아버지의 팔에 끼고, 이어 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제에게도 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“그래, 너는 어떻게 왔느냐?” 첫인사가 끝나자 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“스탠호프 댁에서 데려다주었어요.” 그 여자가 말했다. “마차가 두 번 오가야 해서, 지금은 시뇨라를 데리러 되돌아갔고요.” 이렇게 말하다가 그 여자는 애러빈 씨와 눈이 마주쳤고, 그가 엄한 표정으로 자기를 빤히 바라보고 있음을 알았다. 그 눈길에 담긴 비난을 그 여자는 대번에 알아들었다. 그 눈은 눈이 말할 수 있는 한 가장 또렷하게 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “그래요, 스탠호프 댁 사람들과 함께 왔지요. 그러나 그렇게 한 것은 슬로프 씨와 한자리에 있기 위해서였고요.”\n\n“우리 일행은요,” 그 여자가 여전히 아버지를 향해 말했다. “스탠호프 박사님과 샬럿 스탠호프와 저와 슬로프 씨였어요.” 마지막 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 그 여자는 제 손 밑에서 아버지의 팔이 살짝 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 바로 그때 애러빈 씨가 그들에게서 몸을 돌리더니, 두 손을 등 뒤로 마주 잡고는 길 하나를 따라 천천히 거닐어 가 버렸다.\n\n“아버지,” 그 여자가 말했다. “슬로프 씨와 같은 마차를 타는 것은 피할 도리가 없었어요. 정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그이가 온다는 것은 꿈에도 모른 채 그 댁과 함께 가기로 이미 약속해 둔 터였고, 나중에는 이러쿵저러쿵 설명하고 말거리를 만들지 않고서는 빠져나올 수가 없었고요. 아버지는 그때 집에 안 계셨잖아요. 정말 어쩔 도리가 없었어요.” 그 여자가 어찌나 빨리 말했던지, 변명이 끝났을 때는 숨이 다 차 있었다.\n\n“네가 왜 그것을 피하고 싶어 했는지 모르겠구나, 얘야.” 아버지가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 아버지, 아시잖아요. 아시는 게 틀림없어요. 플럼스테드에서 그이들이 한 말을 다 아시잖아요. 분명히 아세요. 부주교가 한 말을 다 아시잖아요. 얼마나 부당했는지요. 애러빈 씨도 그랬고요. 그이는 끔찍한 사람이에요, 끔찍하고 역겨운 사람이에요, 하지만—”\n\n“누가 역겨운 사람이라는 게냐, 얘야? 애러빈 씨 말이냐?”\n\n“아니요, 슬로프 씨요. 제가 슬로프 씨를 말하는 거 아시잖아요. 그이는 제가 평생 만난 사람 가운데 가장 역겨운 사람이에요. 하필 그이와 같은 마차를 타고 여기 와야 했다니 정말이지 재수가 없었어요. 하지만 어쩔 수가 있었나요?”\n\n하딩 씨의 마음에서 큰 짐 하나가 스스로 움직여 내려가기 시작했다. 그러니까 결국 그 모든 지혜를 갖춘 부주교도, 그 모든 요령을 갖춘 그랜틀리 부인도, 그 모든 재능을 갖춘 애러빈 씨도 틀렸던 것이다. 제 자식, 제 엘리너, 그토록 자랑스러워하던 딸이 슬로프 씨 따위의 아내가 되는 일은 없을 것이었다. 그는 그것을 기정사실로 굳게 믿은 나머지 그 혼사에 동의를 내주려던 참이었는데, 이제 와서 알고 보니 엘리너의 연인이라던 그 사람은 적어도 집안의 그 누구 못지않게 딸 자신에게 미움받고 있었다. 그러나 하딩 씨는 제가 저지른 실수를 감출 만큼 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이 결코 아니었다. 아무 의심도 품지 않았던 척할 수 없었고, 부주교의 억측에 한 번도 동조한 적이 없다고 시치미를 뗄 수도 없었다. 그는 몹시 놀랐고 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻤으며, 그러하다는 것을 겉으로 드러내지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“내 사랑하는 딸아,” 그가 말했다. “나는 참으로 기쁘구나, 참으로 반갑구나. 내 자식아, 네가 내 마음에서 이렇게 큰 짐을 덜어 주었다.”\n\n“하지만 아버지, 설마 아버지까지 그렇게 생각하신 건—”\n\n“나는 무엇을 생각해야 할지 몰랐단다, 얘야. 부주교가 나에게 말하기를—”\n\n“부주교가요!” 엘리너가 격정으로 얼굴이 달아오른 채 말했다. “부주교쯤 되는 사람이라면 처제를 헐뜯고 아비와 딸 사이에 앙금을 만드는 것보다는 더 나은 일에 힘을 쓸 수도 있었을 텐데요!”\n\n“그이는 그럴 뜻으로 그런 것이 아니란다, 엘리너.”\n\n“그럼 무슨 뜻이었대요? 왜 제 일에 끼어들어 아버지 마음을 그런 거짓으로 채웠대요?”\n\n“이제 그만 잊자꾸나, 얘야. 이제 그만 잊으렴. 우리 모두 이제부터는 너를 더 잘 알게 될 게다.”\n\n“아, 아버지, 아버지가 그런 생각을 하시다니요! 아버지가 저를 의심하시다니요!”\n\n“의심이라니 무슨 말인지 모르겠구나, 엘리너. 그런 혼인이라고 해서 부끄러울 것이야 없지 않으냐, 잘못될 것도 없고 말이다. 아비로서 끼어들 만한 일도 못 되지.” 그리고 하딩 씨는 제 변명을 이어, 슬로프 씨도 알고 보면 꽤 괜찮은 사람이고 젊은 미망인의 두 번째 남편으로 아주 적합하다는 데까지 늘어놓을 참이었으나, 엘리너의 더 거센 기세에 말이 막혔다.\n\n“부끄러운 일이에요.” 그 여자가 말했다. “잘못된 일이고요, 끔찍한 일이에요. 제가 그런 흉한 짓을 한다면 아무도 저와 말을 섞지 않는대도 할 말이 없어요. 어휴—” 그러면서 그 여자는 벗들이 그토록 선뜻 자기를 위해 밝혀 주려 했던 그 혼인의 횃불을 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤다. “그랜틀리 박사님이야 그렇다 쳐요. 언니야 그렇다 쳐요. 하지만 아버지, 아버지는 정말 뜻밖이에요. 어떻게 그러실 수가, 어떻게 그걸 믿으실 수가 있어요?” 가엾은 엘리너는 아버지가 저를 저버린 일을 생각하니 더는 눈물을 참을 수 없어, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그 여자의 슬픔에 그 자리는 그리 알맞지 못했다. 그들은 관목 숲 사이를 걷고 있었고 가까이에 사람이 많았다. 가엾은 하딩 씨는 되는대로 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓았고, 엘리너는 애써 눈물을 다스리고 손수건을 주머니에 도로 넣었다. 아버지를 용서하는 것은 어렵지 않았고, 이제 제 고백에서 되살아난 아버지의 유쾌한 기분에 함께 어울리는 것을 아주 뿌리칠 수도 없었다. 슬로프 씨를 사위로 맞아들이라는 요구를 받을 일이 없으리라는 생각은 그의 가슴에서 그토록 큰 짐을 덜어 주었다. 딸의 마음과 제 마음이 이제 언제나 그러했듯 하나라는 것을 알게 된 것은 그토록 큰 안도였다. 그는 지난 6주 동안 그 몹쓸 슬로프 씨 때문에 그토록 마음이 편치 못했던 것이다! 요양원을 잃은 일에는 그토록 무심하고 딸을 되찾은 일에는 그토록 감사해하니, 엘리너로서는 화를 낼 근거가 아무리 든든하다 해도 도무지 그를 오래 미워할 마음이 들지 않았다.\n\n“아버지,” 그 여자가 아버지의 팔에 바싹 매달리며 말했다. “다시는 저를 의심하지 마세요. 절대 그러지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요. 제가 무슨 일을 하든 아버지께 가장 먼저 말씀드릴 테니까요. 아버지와 반드시 의논할 테니까요.”\n\n그리하여 하딩 씨는 약속했고, 제 죄를 인정했고, 다시 약속했다. 그렇게 그는 고치겠노라 다짐하고 그 여자는 용서한다고 말하면서, 둘은 함께 응접실 창가로 돌아왔다.\n\n그런데 엘리너가 무슨 일을 하든 아버지께 가장 먼저 말하겠노라고 선언했을 때, 그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까? 그 여자는 무슨 일을 할 생각이었을까?\n\n이리하여 엘리너가 이날 울라손에서 연기해야 했던 멜로드라마의 제1막이 끝났다.\n\n# 제37장. 시뇨라 네로니와 드 코시 백작부인과 프라우디 부인이 울라손에서 마주치다\n\n그리고 이제 새로 도착하는 이들이 있었다. 엘리너가 응접실에 다다른 바로 그때 시뇨라가 그 방으로 실려 들어오고 있었다. 그녀는 마차에서 들려 나와 식당으로 옮겨졌고 거기서 소파에 뉘어졌다가, 이제 남동생과 언니와 애러빈 씨와 제복 입은 하인 둘의 합동 부축을 받아 다른 방으로 들어서는 참이었다. 그녀는 온통 제 광채에 싸여 있었고, 어찌나 애처롭도록 행복해 보이고 어찌나 비탄과 우아함으로 가득하며 어찌나 아름답고 어찌나 가엾고 어찌나 매혹적인지, 그녀가 그 자리에 있는 것을 반기지 않기란 거의 불가능했다.\n\n손 양은 꾸밈없이 그녀를 반겼다. 사실 시뇨라는 일종의 명사였다. 손 양의 핏줄에는 명사 사냥꾼의 피가 한 방울도 섞여 있지 않았으나, 그럼에도 그녀는 매력 있는 사람들이 제 집에 오는 것을 보기 좋아하기는 했다. 시뇨라는 매력이 있었고, 식당에 처음 자리를 잡았을 때 손 양의 귀에 부드럽고 여자다운 말 두어 마디를 속삭였는데 그 말이 그 순간 그 노처녀의 마음을 제법 건드려 놓았던 것이다.\n\n“아, 손 양은요. 손 양은 어디 계신가요?” 시중드는 이들이 잔디밭에서 벌어지는 일을 죄다 내다볼 수 있는 창 하나 바로 앞에 그녀를 자리 잡아 주자마자 그녀가 말했다. “저 같은 것이 여기 있도록 허락해 주신 데 대해 무어라 감사를 드려야 할까요? 하지만 제가 얼마나 기쁜지 아신다면, 제가 몰고 다니는 이 성가심쯤은 분명 눈감아 주실 거예요.” 그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 그 노처녀의 작은 손을 제 두 손 사이에 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n“이렇게 와 주시니 우리는 더없이 반갑소이다.” 손 양이 말했다. “성가시기는커녕 조금도 그렇지 않고, 이렇게 찾아와 주신 것을 우리는 도리어 큰 은혜로 여기고 있소 — 그렇지 않소, 윌프레드?”\n\n“참으로 크나큰 은혜지요.” 손 씨가 정중히 허리를 굽히며 말했으나, 그 환대는 누이가 베푼 것보다는 다소 덜 다정했다. 손 씨는 아마 제 누이보다 이 손님의 내력을 더 많이 들었을 터였고, 아직 시뇨라의 매력에 굴복해 본 일이 없었다.\n\n그러나 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 낳은 어머니가 이렇듯 한창 광채를 뿜으며, 사람들이 무리 지어 그녀를 바라보고 그 자리의 정예 인사들이 그녀의 소파를 둘러싸고 서 있는 동안, 그 영광은 드 코시 백작부인의 도착으로 빛이 바랬다. 손 양은 그때까지 세 시간째 백작부인을 기다리던 터라, 마침내 그 도착이 이루어지자 아주 뚜렷한 만족을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀와 그 오라비는 물론 작위 있는 귀인들을 맞으러 나갔고, 아, 그들과 함께 시뇨라의 숭배자들도 여럿 떠나갔다.\n\n“아, 손 씨,” 백작부인이 모피 외투를 벗기우고 얇은 사 숄을 다시 걸치는 중에 말했다, “댁의 도로는 어찌 그리 끔찍하오. 아주 소름이 끼칠 지경이더군.”\n\n마침 손 씨는 그 구역의 도로 관리 위원이었던지라, 그 공격이 달갑지 않아 제 도로를 변호하기 시작했다.\n\n“아, 그렇고말고, 정말 그렇다오.” 백작부인은 그의 말을 조금도 귀담아듣지 않고 말했다. “아주 끔찍했소 — 안 그러냐, 마거리타? 아니 글쎄, 손 양, 우리는 열한 시에 드 코시 성을 떠났다오. 열한 시가 막 지났을 뿐이었지, 그렇지 않으냐, 조지? 그리고—”\n\n“한 시가 막 지났다는 말씀이시겠지요.” 아너러블 조지가 무리에서 몸을 돌려 외알 안경 너머로 시뇨라를 훑어보며 말했다. 시뇨라는 흔히들 말하듯 받은 것을 고스란히 되돌려 주었고 거기에 덤까지 얹어 주었으므로, 그 젊은 귀족은 눈길을 돌리고 안경을 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“이보게, 손,” 그가 속삭였다, “소파에 있는 저 여자는 대체 누군가?”\n\n“스탠호프 박사의 딸이오.” 손 씨가 속삭여 답했다. “스스로 시뇨라 네로니라 부르지요.”\n\n“휘 — 휴 — 휘!” 아너러블 조지가 휘파람을 불었다. “저 여자가 바로 그 여자였군. 저 계집에 대해서라면 나도 이야기를 끝도 없이 들었네. 손, 자네 나를 꼭 소개해 주어야 하네. 꼭 그래 주어야 해.”\n\n점잖음 그 자체였던 손 씨로서는 아너러블 조지 드 코시가 끝도 없이 이야기를 들었다는 그런 손님을 집에 두는 것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았으나, 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그는 다만 잠자리에 들기 전에 누이에게 그토록 기꺼이 맞아들인 그 부인의 내력을 얼마간은 알려 주리라 마음먹었다. 그 나이에 이르러서도 손 양이 지닌 순진함은 더없이 사랑스러운 것이었으나, 순진함이라 해도 위험할 수 있는 법이었다.\n\n“조지야 무어라 하든 상관없소.” 백작부인이 손 양에게 변명을 늘어놓으며 말을 이었다. “우리가 열두 시 전에 성문을 지난 것만은 분명하다오 — 그렇지 않으냐, 마거리타?”\n\n“글쎄요, 저는 정말 모르겠어요.” 레이디 마거리타가 말했다. “반쯤 잠들어 있었으니까요. 하지만 한밤중 어느 무렵엔가 누가 저를 깨웠고 제가 동트기 전에 옷을 입고 있었던 것만은 분명히 알아요.”\n\n지혜로운 사람들은 자기가 그른 편에 섰을 때면 언제나 자기가 잘못을 저지른 그 상대의 흠을 잡아 스스로를 옳은 편에 세운다. 레이디 드 코시는 지혜로운 여인이었으므로, 세 시까지 나타나지 않아 손 양을 몹시 홀대해 놓고는 도리어 공세로 돌아서서 손 씨의 도로를 공격했다. 못지않게 지혜로운 그 딸은 손 양의 이른 시각을 공격했다. 이런 재주야말로 사는 법을 아는 이들이 흔히 갈고닦는 기술 가운데 가장 값진 축에 든다. 이것을 당해 낼 도리는 없다. 누가 조목조목 따져 들어가 첫 번째 고발과 맞싸워 그것을 매듭짓고, 그런 다음 다시 맞고발을 꺼내 그것까지 뒷받침할 수 있겠는가? 인생은 그런 수고를 감당하기에 충분히 길지 않다. 옳은 편에 선 사람은 제 올바름에 태평히 기대는 까닭에 무장하지 않은 채 다닌다. 바로 그 힘이 그의 약점이다. 그른 편에 선 사람은 제 무기를 챙겨야 함을 안다. 바로 그 약함이 그의 힘이다. 한쪽은 결코 싸울 채비가 되어 있지 않고, 다른 쪽은 언제나 준비가 되어 있다. 그러므로 이 세상에서는 그른 편에 선 사람이 거의 어김없이 옳은 편에 선 사람을 이기며, 또 어김없이 그를 경멸한다.\n\n마흔을 넘긴 뒤에도 제 이웃에게 공정하기를 꾀하는 사람은 바보이거나 아니면 천사임에 틀림없다. 레이디 마거리타 같은 이들은 그보다 훨씬 이른 나이에 그 교훈을 익혀 두었다. 물론 이는 그들이 어떤 학교에서 배웠느냐에 달린 문제이기는 하다.\n\n가엾은 손 양은 완전히 압도당하고 말았다. 자기가 홀대를 받았다는 것을 그녀는 아주 잘 알고 있었으면서도, 어느새 레이디 드 코시에게 사과를 하고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 백작부인의 명예를 위해 말해 두자면, 그 사과를 그녀는 아주 너그럽게 받아들였고, 딸들을 줄줄이 거느린 채 잔디밭 쪽으로 인도되는 데 몸을 맡겼다.\n\n응접실에는 백작부인이 지나갈 수 있도록 창이 둘 활짝 열려 있었으나, 그녀는 세 번째 창가 소파에 한 여자가 있고 그 여자에게 이를테면 한 무리의 추종자가 붙어 있다는 것을 알아보았다. 그리하여 백작부인은 그 여자를 조사해 보기로 마음먹었다. 드 코시 집안 사람들은 대대로 근시였고, 적어도 서른 세기 동안 그러했다. 그러니 그 집안에 들어오면서 그 집안의 습속을 받아들인 레이디 드 코시는 앞서 아들이 한 그대로, 시뇨라 네로니를 살펴보려고 외알 안경을 들고서 소파를 둘러싼 신사들 틈으로 밀고 들어갔고, 알은체를 해 주기로 마음먹은 이들에게는 가볍게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n창가에 이르려면 소파 앞을 바짝 지나야 했고, 지나가면서 그녀는 소파에 누운 이를 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다. 누운 이도 답례로 백작부인을 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다. 백작부인이 된 이래로 왕가나 공작가나 후작가에 속하지 않은 눈은 모조리 제 눈앞에서 내리깔리는 것을 보아 온 백작부인은, 가던 걸음을 멈추고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 아까보다 한층 더 뚫어져라 노려보았다. 그러나 이번에 그녀가 상대한 것은 백작부인 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 사람이었다. 마들린 네로니를 무안하게 만드는 일은, 이렇게 말해도 좋다면, 인간으로 난 남자든 여자든 해낼 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그녀는 크고 밝고 윤이 흐르는 눈을 점점 더 크게 뜨더니 마침내 온몸이 눈뿐인 듯이 보였다. 그녀는 애를 써서 그리하는 것이 아니라 그리하는 것이 즐거워 못 견디겠다는 듯이 그 귀부인의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그녀는 제 뻔뻔함을 거들 안경 따위는 쓰지 않았고 쓸 필요도 없었다. 더할 나위 없이 희미한 조롱의 미소가 입가에 어렸고, 승리를 확신하기라도 하는 듯 콧구멍이 살짝 벌어졌다. 그리고 그 승리는 확실했다. 드 코시 백작부인은 서른 세기와 드 코시 성을 등에 업고, 드 코시 경이 웨일스 공의 조랑말 관리 총책이라는 사실까지 있었음에도, 그녀 앞에서는 승산이 없었다. 처음에는 백작부인의 손에 들린 그 작은 금테가 흔들렸고, 다음에는 손이 떨렸고, 다음에는 그 금테가 떨어졌으며, 백작부인의 머리는 홱 치켜들려 허공을 향했고, 백작부인의 발은 잔디밭으로 허둥허둥 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 그렇게까지 빨리 나가지는 못한 까닭에 그녀는 시뇨라의 목소리가 이렇게 묻는 것을 듣고야 말았다.\n\n“대체 저 여자는 누구예요, 슬로프 씨?”\n\n“드 코시 백작부인입니다.”\n\n“아, 그렇군요. 그럴 줄 알았어요. 하, 하, 하. 그것참 연극 한 편만큼이나 볼만하군요.”\n\n그리고 그것은 볼 눈이 있고 본 것을 두고 한마디 할 재치가 있는 이라면 누구에게나 연극 한 편만큼이나 볼만한 일이었다.\n\n그러나 레이디 드 코시는 곧 잔디밭에서 마음 맞는 영혼을 하나 찾아냈다. 거기서 그녀는 프라우디 부인과 마주쳤는데, 프라우디 부인은 주교의 아내일 뿐 아니라 어느 백작의 사촌이기도 했으므로, 레이디 드 코시는 이 모임에서 만날 법한 이들 가운데 가장 어울리는 동반자가 그녀라고 여겼다. 그리하여 두 사람은 서로를 보고 기뻐했다. 프라우디 부인은 백작부인을 결코 하찮게 여기지 않았고, 이 백작부인이 같은 주에 살며 바체스터에서 왕래할 만한 거리 안에 있었으므로 이 기회에 환심을 사 둘 수 있게 된 것을 반겼다.\n\n“드 코시 백작부인, 이렇게 뵈니 정말 반갑습니다.” 그녀는 제 천성이 허락하는 한 최대로 험상궂지 않은 얼굴을 하고 말했다. “여기서 뵈리라고는 생각지도 못했습니다. 거리도 이렇게 멀고, 게다가 아시다시피 이렇게 사람이 많으니 말입니다.”\n\n“게다가 그 도로라니, 프라우디 부인! 이 고장 사람들이 대체 어떻게 돌아다니는지 참으로 모르겠소. 하기야 아예 돌아다니지 않는 모양이지.”\n\n“글쎄요, 저도 정말 모르겠습니다만, 아마 다니지 않겠지요. 손 남매가 다니지 않는다는 것만은 제가 압니다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “손 양은 아주 좋은 분이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“아, 더없이 유쾌한 사람이지, 게다가 어찌나 별난지. 나는 그이와 이십 년째 알고 지낸다오. 저 손 양으로 말하면 내가 아주 아끼는 사람이오. 알다시피 참으로 이상한 사람이지. 나는 그이를 볼 때마다 늘 에스키모와 인디언이 떠오른다니까. 저 옷차림이 정말 유쾌하지 않소?”\n\n“유쾌하고말고요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “저분이 얼굴에 분칠을 하는지 어떤지 저는 궁금하군요. 저런 낯빛을 보신 일이 있으신가요?”\n\n“아, 물론이지.” 레이디 드 코시가 말했다. “아니, 내 말은 틀림없이 하리라는 것이오. 그런데 프라우디 부인, 저기 창가 소파에 있는 저 여자는 누구요? 이쪽으로 한 걸음만 와 보시오, 그럼 보일 게요, 저기—” 그러고는 백작부인이 그녀를, 시뇨라의 잊으려야 잊히지 않는 얼굴과 자태가 훤히 보이는 자리로 데려갔다.\n\n그러나 그렇게 하면서 그녀들 역시 시뇨라의 눈에 똑같이 훤히 띄지 않을 도리는 없었다. “보세요, 저것 좀 보세요.” 그 부인이 아직 제 곁에 서 있던 슬로프 씨에게 말했다. “이 나라의 지엄한 영적 권세와 세속 권세가 한통속이 되어 죄다 가엾은 저 하나를 겨누고 있는 꼴을 좀 보세요. 슬로프 씨, 제 팔찌를 걸고 당신의 다음 설교와 내기를 해도 좋아요. 저 두 사람은 저를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려고 일부러 저기다 진을 친 거예요. 뭐, 저야 싸움터로 달려나갈 수는 없지요. 하지만 적이 제 가까이 오기만 한다면 제 몸 지키는 법쯤은 알고 있답니다.”\n\n그러나 적은 그보다는 영리했다. 시뇨라 네로니와 맞붙어서는 얻을 것이 없었고, 잔디밭에서 그녀와 떨어져 있는 한 마음껏 그녀를 헐뜯을 수 있었다.\n\n“저 여자가 바로 그 끔찍한 이탈리아 여자입니다, 드 코시 백작부인. 백작부인께서도 이야기를 들으셨을 겁니다.”\n\n“이탈리아 여자라니, 무슨 이탈리아 여자 말이오?” 백작부인이 다가올 이야기에 대뜸 생기가 돌아 말했다. “이 고장에 이탈리아 여자가 들어왔다는 소리는 들은 적이 없는 것 같은데. 게다가 저 여자는 이탈리아 사람으로 보이지도 않소.”\n\n“아, 분명 들으셨을 겁니다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “아니요, 저 여자가 순전한 이탈리아 사람인 것은 아니지요. 스탠호프 박사의 딸입니다 — 참사회원인 스탠호프 박사 말입니다 — 그러면서 스스로를 시뇨라 네로니라 부른답니다.”\n\n“오오오오!” 백작부인이 외쳤다.\n\n“들으셨을 줄 알았습니다.” 프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다. “그 남편이라는 자에 대해서는 저도 아무것도 모릅니다. 네로니라는 이름의 사내가 아직 살아 있다는 말들은 하더군요. 저 여자가 외국에서 그런 사내와 혼인한 것은 사실인 모양인데, 그자가 대체 누구인지 무엇 하는 자였는지는 저도 도무지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“오오오오!” 백작부인이 외치며, 입술에서 그 소리가 한 자락씩 더 떨어질 때마다 모든 것을 다 알겠다는 듯 고개를 주억거렸다. “이제야 죄다 알겠소. 조지가 저 여자 이야기를 하는 것을 내가 들은 적이 있소. 조지는 저 여자에 대해 죄다 알고 있지. 조지가 로마에서 저 여자 이야기를 들었다오.”\n\n“어쨌든 참으로 가증스러운 여자입니다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“도저히 못 견딜 노릇이지.” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“제가 저 여자에 대해 아무것도 모르던 시절에 한번은 저 여자가 주교관에 발을 들인 적이 있는데, 그때 그 행실이 얼마나 끔찍하게 망측했는지는 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다.”\n\n“그랬소?” 신이 난 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“도저히 못 견딜 노릇이었지요.” 주교 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그런데 저 여자는 어째서 소파에 누워 있는 게요?” 레이디 드 코시가 물었다.\n\n“다리가 하나뿐이니까요.” 프라우디 부인이 대답했다.\n\n“다리가 하나뿐이라니!” 레이디 드 코시가 말했는데, 시뇨라가 그렇게 성치 못한 몸이라는 데에 그녀는 어느 정도 불만스러운 기분이 들었다. “나면서부터 그랬소?”\n\n“아, 아니지요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했고 — 그 장담에 백작부인은 다소 마음이 놓였다 — “다리가 둘 다 있었답니다. 그런데 그 네로니라는 자가 저 여자를 하도 때려서 결국 한쪽을 잘라 내야 했다지 뭡니까. 어쨌든 저 여자는 그 다리를 아주 못 쓰게 되고 말았지요.”\n\n“가엾은 것 같으니!” 백작부인이 말했으니, 그녀 자신도 부부 사이의 시련이라면 얼마간 아는 바가 있었던 것이다.\n\n“그렇지요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “저 여자가 지금이라도 처신할 줄만 안다면 지난날의 못된 행실에도 불구하고 딱하게 여겨 줄 만도 하겠지요. 그러나 저 여자는 그러지를 않습니다. 제 눈으로 본 것 가운데 가장 뻔뻔한 것이 저 여자입니다.”\n\n“정말 그렇구려.” 레이디 드 코시가 말했다.\n\n“게다가 사내들을 대하는 행실이 어찌나 가증스러운지, 점잖은 부인네의 응접실에 들일 만한 여자가 못 됩니다.”\n\n“저런 저런!” 백작부인이 말했는데, 그녀는 다시금 흥이 올라 행복하고도 무자비해져 있었다.\n\n“저 여자 곁에 서 있는 저 남자를 보셨습니까 — 머리가 붉은 저 성직자 말입니다.”\n\n“보았지, 보았고말고.”\n\n“저 여자가 저 사람을 아주 망쳐 놓았습니다. 주교가 — 아니, 그 탓은 차라리 제가 저 자신에게 돌려야겠군요, 그게 저였으니까요 — 제가 저 사람을 런던에서 바체스터로 데려왔습니다. 설교는 그런대로 하는 편이고 부지런한 젊은이라 제가 주교에게 소개했지요. 그런데 드 코시 백작부인, 저 여자가 저 사람을 손아귀에 넣고는 어찌나 망신을 시켜 놓았는지, 저로서는 저 사람더러 주교관을 떠나라고 요구하지 않을 수 없게 되었습니다. 게다가 저 사람이 성직 예복을 벗게 되지나 않을지 저는 몹시 의심스럽습니다!”\n\n“아니, 그 사람은 대체 얼마나 얼간이란 말이오!” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“저 여자가 얼마나 음흉하고 간악한지 백작부인께서는 모르십니다.” 프라우디 부인이 찢겨 나간 제 옷자락 주름 장식을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n“하지만 다리가 하나뿐이라 하지 않았소!”\n\n“다리가 열 개 있다 한들 저보다 더 말썽을 부리지는 못할 겁니다. 저 눈을 좀 보십시오, 드 코시 백작부인. 점잖은 여자의 얼굴에서 저런 눈을 보신 적이 있으십니까?”\n\n“정말이지 나도 본 적이 없소, 프라우디 부인.”\n\n“게다가 저 뻔뻔함이며 저 목소리라니요! 저는 저 여자의 가엾은 아비가 참으로 딱합니다. 그이는 정말 좋은 축에 드는 사람이거든요.”\n\n“스탠호프 박사라 하였소?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 스탠호프 박사지요. 우리 참사회원 가운데 한 사람인데, 사람 자체는 착하고 조용한 편입니다. 다만 제 딸이 저렇게 처신하도록 내버려 둔다는 것이 저로서는 놀라울 따름입니다.”\n\n“어쩔 도리가 없나 보구려.” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 성직자가 아닙니까, 드 코시 백작부인! 집 안에서 얌전히 굴게 만들지는 못한다 하더라도, 적어도 사람들 앞에 저렇게 나서서 구경거리가 되는 것만은 막아야지요. 그래도 딱하기는 딱한 사람입니다. 저 식구들을 죄다 데리고 사느라 아주 절망스러운 나날을 보내는 모양이니까요. 저기 저 원숭이처럼 생긴 사내, 수염이 길고 헐렁한 바지를 입은 저자가 저 여자의 오라비입니다. 저자도 누이 못지않게 못됐지요. 둘 다 신앙이라고는 없는 자들입니다.”\n\n“신앙이 없다니!” 레이디 드 코시가 말했다. “그런데 그 아비가 참사회원이라니!”\n\n“그렇습니다, 게다가 새 수석 사제가 될 가망까지 있는 사람이지요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇지, 가엾은 트리폴 박사!” 평생에 딱 한 번 그 신사와 말을 나누어 본 일이 있는 백작부인이 말했다. “그 소식을 듣고 나도 무척 마음이 아팠소, 프라우디 부인. 그래서 스탠호프 박사가 새 수석 사제가 된다는 말이구려! 훌륭한 집안 출신이니, 그 딸이 저렇긴 해도 나는 그이가 잘되기를 바라오. 프라우디 부인, 어쩌면 그이가 수석 사제가 되고 나면 저 사람들도 제 잘못된 길을 좀 더 잘 깨닫게 될지 모르지 않겠소.”\n\n이 말에 프라우디 부인은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 시뇨라 네로니를 향한 그녀의 혐오는 너무나 깊어서, 그 부인이 제 잘못된 길을 깨닫게 되기를 바라는 것조차 그녀에게는 허락되지 않았다. 프라우디 부인은 시뇨라를 이미 버림받은 자 가운데 하나로, 그리스도인의 사랑이 미치지 못하는 곳에 있는 자로 여겼고, 그러므로 언젠가 그 죄에서 벗어나 잘되기를 빌어 주어야 하는 부담 없이 그녀를 미워하는 호사를 마음껏 누릴 수 있었다.\n\n이 뜻이 맞는 두 영혼 사이의 대화가 더 이어지지 못한 것은 손 씨가 나타난 탓이었으니, 그는 백작부인을 천막으로 안내하러 온 것이었다. 실은 십 분쯤 전에 이미 그리하도록 부탁을 받았으나 응접실에서 시뇨라에게 붙들려 늦어졌다. 그녀는 용케 그를 붙들어 두었고, 제 소파 가까이로 끌어들였으며, 마침내는 제 아름다운 팔 바로 곁의 의자에 그를 앉히기에 이르렀다. 물고기는 미끼를 물었고, 바늘에 꿰였고, 걸려들었고, 뭍으로 끌어올려졌다. 그 십 분 사이에 그는 시뇨라의 내력을 그녀가 골라 쓰는 어조 그대로 죄다 들었다. 아너러블 조지가 그저 넌지시 비추기만 했던 그 알 수 없는 이야기를 그는 당사자의 입에서 남김없이 들었다. 그는 자기 앞에 누워 있는 이 아름다운 사람이 죄를 지었다기보다는 죄를 당한 쪽이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 자기가 약했고 남을 잘 믿었고 세상의 평판에 무심했으며, 그런 까닭에 학대당하고 속임을 당하고 나쁜 소문에 시달렸노라고 그에게 털어놓았다. 그녀는 잘려 나간 제 다리에 대해, 한창 꽃필 때에 무너진 제 젊음에 대해, 온갖 매력을 빼앗긴 제 아름다움에 대해, 시들어 버린 제 삶과 말라 버린 제 희망에 대해 그에게 이야기했고, 그러는 사이 눈에서 눈물 한 방울이 뺨으로 굴러떨어졌다. 그녀는 그에게 이런 것들을 이야기하고 나서 그의 동정을 구했다.\n\n마음씨 좋고 유쾌한 앵글로색슨 지주 손 씨로서 그녀에게 동정을 약속하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 손 씨는 동정하겠노라 약속했다. 아울러 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 보러 가겠노라고, 그 무시무시하던 로마 시절 이야기를, 코메 호숫가에서 그토록 빨리 스쳐 지나간 그 가볍고 순진하나 위태롭던 시간들의 이야기를 더 듣겠노라고, 그리하여 시뇨라의 슬픔을 들어 주는 사람이 되겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n그가 그 위험한 부인에 대해 누이에게 경고하겠다는 생각을 죄다 버렸다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라. 그는 잘못 알고 있었던 것이다 — 평생에 그토록 잘못 안 적이 없을 만큼. 그는 늘 저 아너러블 조지를 거칠고 심성이 야비한 젊은이라 여겨 왔는데, 이제는 그것이 사실임을 그 어느 때보다 굳게 확신하게 되었다. 마들린 네로니 같은 여인의 명예를 위태롭게 하고 더럽히는 것이 바로 아너러블 조지 같은 사내들이었다. 그는 그 부인의 집으로 찾아가 볼 참이었다. 제 판단이 옳다는 것을 그는 마음속으로 온전히 확신하고 있었다. 만일 자기가 믿는 대로 그녀가 상처 입고 마음씨 곧고 정 많은 여인임을 확인하게 된다면, 그는 누이 모니카에게 부탁하여 그녀를 울라손으로 초대하게 할 작정이었다.\n\n“안 돼요.” 그가 그녀의 권유에 따라 자리에서 일어나며 자기가 직접 그녀의 시중을 들겠노라 선언하자 그녀가 말했다. “안 돼요, 안 됩니다, 나의 벗이여. 저는 그러시는 데 단호히 거부권을 놓겠어요. 아니, 당신 댁에서, 이렇게 사람들이 잔뜩 모여 있는 자리에서 그러시겠다고요! 여자란 여자는 죄다 저를 미워하게 만들고 남자란 남자는 죄다 저를 빤히 쳐다보게 만들고 싶으신가요? 오늘은 다시 제 곁에 오지 마시라고 저는 분명히 명을 내리겠어요. 저희 집으로 찾아오세요. 제가 이야기라는 것을 할 수 있는 것은 집에서뿐이고, 제가 정말로 살아 있고 즐거울 수 있는 것도 집에서뿐이랍니다. 이렇게 바깥나들이를 하는 날은, 오늘 같은 날은 저에게 참으로 드물어요. 저희 집으로 찾아오세요, 손 씨. 그러면 그때는 제가 물러가시라는 말은 하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n스물다섯 먹은 젊은이들은 흔히 제 윗세대를 — 이를테면 제 나이의 갑절이 되는 사내들을 — 한낱 나무토막이나 돌덩이로 여기곤 한다고 우리는 알고 있다. 여자의 아름다움에 관해서라면 나무토막이요 돌덩이라는 말이다. 이보다 더 큰 착각은 없다. 여자들은 대체로 더 잘 알고 있으나, 이 문제에 관해서라면 어느 한 나이의 남자들은 다른 나이의 인간이 지닌 바로 그 본성에 대해 철저히 무지하다. 세상 돌아가는 일을 아무리 겪어 보아도, 역사를 아무리 읽어도, 삶을 아무리 살펴보아도 그 진실을 가르치는 데는 아무 소용이 없다. 쉰 먹은 남자들은 마주르카를 추지 않으니, 대개 너무 살이 찌고 숨이 가쁜 까닭이다. 또 연인의 발치에서 몇 시간씩 강둑에 앉아 있지도 않으니, 류머티즘이 조금 두려운 까닭이다. 그러나 진짜 참된 사랑으로 말하자면 — 첫눈에 빠지는 사랑, 헌신에 이르는 사랑, 사람에게서 잠을 앗아 가는 사랑, “독수리의 눈마저 멀게 할 만큼 응시하는” 사랑, “도둑의 수상쩍은 발소리가 멎는 그 가장 낮은 소리까지 듣는” 사랑, “헤스페리데스의 나무를 여전히 기어오르는 헤라클레스와 같은” 사랑으로 말하자면 — 우리가 보기에 가장 좋은 나이는 마흔다섯에서 일흔까지다. 그 전까지 남자들은 대개 한낱 희롱에 머물 뿐이다.\n\n지금 이 순간 손 씨는, ætat. 쉰의 몸으로, 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니, nata Stanhope에게 첫눈에 반해 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠져 있었다.\n\n그럼에도 그는 예법에 어긋남 없이 레이디 드 코시에게 팔을 내밀 만큼은 자기를 다스리고 있었고, 백작부인은 너그러이 그의 인도를 받아 천막으로 갔다. 손 양의 지시가 그러했으니, 그녀는 주교를 구슬려 늙은 레이디 놀을 식당 상석으로 안내하게 하는 데 성공한 터였다. 준남작 가운데 하나가 프라우디 부인을 찾으러 보내졌는데, 그는 그 부인이 잔디밭에서 그리 좋지 못한 기분으로 있는 것을 발견했다. 손 씨와 백작부인이 너무 갑작스레 그녀를 두고 가 버린 것이다. 시중들어 줄 전속 사제나, 하다못해 떠도는 부목사라도 없나 하고 그녀는 헛되이 둘러보았다. 그들은 죄다 잔디밭 아래쪽에서 젊은 아가씨들과 함께 긴 활을 당기고 있거나, 천막 어느 아늑한 구석에 제 우아한 활쏘기 동무를 위한 자리를 마련해 주고 있었다. 그런 처지에 놓이면 프라우디 부인은 예전에는 으레 슬로프 씨에게 기대곤 했으나, 이제는 다시는 그에게 기댈 수 없었다. 자신의 외로운 처지를 생각하며 그녀는 고개를 한 번 저었는데, 그 한 번의 고갯짓은 슬로프 씨가 바체스터에 더 머무를 날에서 일주일을 깎아 낸 것이나 다름없었다. 하카웨이 고스 경이 당장의 곤경은 덜어 주었으나, 그렇다고 해서 그 죄지은 전속 사제의 운명이 조금이라도 누그러진 것은 결코 아니었다.\n\n이제 먹고 마시는 일이 본격적으로 시작되었다. 그랜틀리 박사는 대경실색하게도 자기가 클랜탠트럼 부인과 짝지어진 것을 알게 되었다. 클랜탠트럼 부인은 부주교를 몹시 좋아했으나 그 마음이 살갑게 되돌아온 적은 없었는데, 그녀가 그에게 다가와 귀에 대고 “자, 부주교님, 오랜 벗에게 팔 하나 빌려주시는 것쯤이야 아까워하지 않으시겠지요” 하고 속삭인 다음 제 로클로르에 얽힌 사연을 처음부터 끝까지 늘어놓기 시작하자, 그는 십오 분이 지나기 전에 그녀를 떼어 놓으리라 마음먹었다. 그러나 근래 들어 부주교는 제 결심을 이루는 데 성공한 적이 없었고, 이번에도 클랜탠트럼 부인은 잔치가 끝날 때까지 그에게 착 달라붙어 있었다.\n\n그윈 박사에게는 어느 준남작의 부인이 돌아갔고, 그랜틀리 부인은 어느 준남작의 몫이 되었다. 샬럿 스탠호프는 버티에게 자리를 내주려고 하딩 씨에게 붙었고, 버티는 식당에서 볼드 부인 옆자리에 앉는 데 성공했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이제 진짜로 구애를 해야 하는 마당이 되고 보니 그는 거의 용기를 잃을 지경이었다.\n\n엘리너는 슬로프 씨가 제 가까이에서 맴도는 것을 보았던 터라 기꺼이 버티의 팔을 잡았다. 슬로프라는 저 무서운 카리브디스를 피하려다가 그녀는 다른 쪽에 있는 보이지 않는 스킬라에 빠질 위험에 크게 처해 있었으니, 그 스킬라란 곧 버티 스탠호프였다. 그녀가 버티에게 보인 태도보다 더 다정할 수는 없었다. 그녀는 그가 내민 팔에 거의 뛰어들다시피 했다. 샬럿은 멀리서 이것을 알아보고 속으로 개가를 올렸고, 버티는 그것을 느끼고 용기를 얻었으며, 슬로프 씨는 그것을 보고 질투로 눈을 부라렸다. 엘리너와 버티는 식당에서 식탁 앞에 앉았는데, 그의 오른편에 자리를 잡고 보니 그녀는 슬로프 씨가 이미 제 오른편 의자를 차지하고 있는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n식당에서 이런 일들이 벌어지고 있는 동안 애러빈 씨는 시뇨라의 소파 위로 홀린 듯 홀로 몸을 기울이고 있었고, 엘리너는 제 자리에서 열린 문 너머로 그가 그러고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n# 제38장. 주교는 아침 식탁에 앉고 수석 사제는 죽다\n\n바체스터의 주교는 울라손 식당에 푸짐하게 차려진 식탁 앞에서 감사 기도를 올렸다. 그가 그러고 있는 동안, 바체스터의 수석 사제는 수석사제관 병실에 누운 채 마지막 숨을 내쉬고 있었다. 바체스터의 주교가 첫 잔의 샴페인을 입술로 가져갔을 때, 바체스터의 수석 사제 자리는 총리가 내려 줄 수 있는 좋은 자리가 되어 있었다. 바체스터의 주교가 식탁을 뜨기도 전에, 그날의 총리는 햄프셔의 시골 저택에서 그 사실을 전해 듣고, 그 성직 승진을 노리는 대단히 존경할 만한 지망자 다섯 사람의 이름을 벌써 머릿속에 굴려 보고 있었다. 지금으로서는 그 다섯 안에 슬로프 씨의 이름이 없었다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠다.\n\n“수염이 모두 흔들릴 때 홀은 흥겨웠네”라 하거니와, 그날 울라손의 홀에서는 성직자들의 수염이 흥겹게 흔들렸다. 마지막 코르크가 뽑히고, 마지막 연설이 끝나고, 마지막 견과가 깨어진 뒤에야 비로소 가엾은 수석 사제가 세상을 떠났다는 소식이 당도해 수군수군 퍼졌다. 이 짧은 지체가 있었던 것은 성직자들의 수염의 행복을 위해 다행한 일이었다. 그러지 않았더라면 체면상 수염은 아예 흔들리지도 못했을 터이기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그날 그들 가운데 슬픈 사람이 하나 있었다. 애러빈 씨의 수염은 마땅히 흔들려야 할 만큼 흔들리지 않았다. 그는 엘리너에 대해 최선을 바라며, 최선을 생각해 보려 애쓰며 그곳에 왔다. 그녀가 슬로프 씨에 대해 흘렸던 말이라면 무엇이든 기억을 더듬어 머릿속에 굴려 보며, 거기에서 제 연적에게 불리한 확신을 그러모으려 애썼던 것이다. 그날 그 미망인의 뜻에 관해 결정적인 증거를 얻고야 말겠다고 딱히 작정한 것은 아니었으나, 되도록이면 엘리너와의 우정을 다시 가꾸어 볼 생각이었고, 지금 같은 마음 상태로는 그런 재배가 무엇이든 결국 사랑의 고백으로 끝날 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그는 지난밤을 새 목사관에서 혼자 보냈는데, 그렇게 보낸 것은 그 밤이 처음이었다. 그 밤은 어지간히 따분하고 침울했다. 세인트 이월드에는 여사제가 하나 아쉬우리라던 그랜틀리 부인의 말은 옳았다. 그는 앞에 잔을 놓고, 이어서 찻주전자를 놓고 홀로 앉아 엘리너 볼드를 생각했다. 그런 종류의 명상이 대개 그렇듯, 그가 한 일이라고는 그녀를 탓하는 것뿐이었다. 슬로프 씨를 좋아한다고 탓하고, 자기를 좋아하지 않는다고 탓하고, 자기에게 다정했다고 탓하고, 다정하지 않았다고 탓하고, 고집스럽고 제멋대로이고 격정적이라고 탓했다. 그러면서도 그녀를 생각하면 할수록 그녀는 그의 애정 속에서 점점 더 높이 떠올랐다. 만일 그녀가 슬로프 씨를 두둔한 것이 사랑에서가 아니라 원칙에서였다는 것이 드러나기만 한다면, 그렇게 드러나도록 만들 수만 있다면, 모든 것이 괜찮아질 터였다. 그런 원칙이라면 그 자체로 훌륭하고 사랑스럽고 여자다운 것이니, 슬로프 씨에게 딱 그만큼의 호의는 기꺼이 허락해도 좋겠다고 그는 느꼈다. 그러나 만일— 그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 아무 까닭도 없이 난롯불을 쑤셔 대고, 찻그릇을 치우러 들어온 새 응접실 하녀에게 퉁명스럽게 말하고, 잠이나 자야겠다고 마음먹으며 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 뻔한 물음을 받고 어째서 그토록 목을 뻣뻣이 세웠단 말인가? 자기가 그녀를 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 그녀가 모를 리 없었다. 어째서 뻔한 물음에 답해 그의 비참함을 끝내 주지 않았단 말인가? 그러고는 안락의자에서 잠들기는커녕, 애러빈 씨는 무엇에 씐 사람처럼 방 안을 서성거렸다.\n\n이튿날 아침 손 양의 분부를 받들 때에도 그는 여전히 다소 어수선한 상태였다. 그의 첫 임무는 클랜탠트럼 부인과 이야기를 나누는 것이었는데, 그 부인은 제 로클로르 이야기를 두고 그에게서 손톱만 한 동정도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 손 양이 볼드 부인은 그랜틀리 댁 사람들과 함께 오느냐고 묻자, 볼드와 그랜틀리라는 두 이름이 나란히 놓인 것만으로 그는 하마터면 자리에서 벌떡 일어설 뻔했다.\n\n희망과 의심이 뒤엉킨 그 어수선한 불확실 속에 있을 때, 그는 슬로프 씨가 더없이 세련된 미소를 띠고 엘리너를 마차에서 부축해 내리는 것을 보았다. 그는 더 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 그 마차가 그녀의 것인지 슬로프 씨의 것인지, 아니면 둘 사이에 아무 약속이 없어도 둘 다 신세를 질 만한 제삼자의 것인지 따위는 따져 보지도 않았다. 지금 같은 마음 상태에서 그 광경은 그와 그의 결심을 뒤엎기에 충분하고도 남았다. 한낮처럼 명백했다. 머리에 흰 면사포를 쓴 그녀가 교회 문간에서 슬로프 씨의 손에 이끌려 마차에 오르는 것을 보았다 한들, 진실이 그보다 더 뚜렷할 수는 없었을 것이다. 그는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 앞서 본 대로 곧 하딩 씨와 나란히 걷고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 조금 뒤 엘리너가 다가왔고, 그리하여 그는 동행을 두고 물러나 혼자 다니거나 다른 동행을 찾아야 했다. 그러던 참에 부주교와 마주쳤다.\n\n“나는 궁금하오.” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “슬로프 씨와 볼드 부인이 함께 왔다는 것이 정말이오? 수전은 제 마차에서 내리다가 같은 마차 안에 있는 두 사람의 얼굴을 본 것이 거의 틀림없다고 하는구려.”\n\n애러빈 씨로서는 그랜틀리 부인의 눈이 정확했다고 증언하는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“참으로 수치스러운 일이오.” 부주교가 말했다. “아니, 차라리 수치를 모르는 짓이라 해야겠구려. 그 사람은 내 손님으로 초대받아 온 거요. 제 몸을 망치기로 작정했다면, 적어도 내 가까운 벗들 앞에서만은 그러지 않을 만한 분별은 있어야 하지 않겠소. 대체 저 사내가 어떻게 초대를 얻어 냈는지 모르겠구려. 설마 그 사람이 낯짝 좋게 데려온 것은 아니겠지.”\n\n여기에 애러빈 씨는 아무 대답도 할 수 없었고, 무슨 말이든 하고 싶지도 않았다. 그는 혼자서는 엘리너를 헐뜯었으나 남 앞에서 그녀를 헐뜯을 마음은 없었고, 남이 그녀를 나쁘게 말하는 것을 듣는 일도 달갑지 않았다. 그러나 그랜틀리 박사는 몹시 화가 나 있어 처제를 조금도 봐주지 않았다. 그래서 애러빈 씨는 되도록 빨리 그에게서 벗어나 다시 집 안으로 흘러들어 갔다.\n\n그가 들어간 지 얼마 되지 않아 시뇨라가 실려 들어왔다. 한동안 그는 유혹을 피해 멀찍이서 그 언저리를 맴돌기만 했다. 그러나 손 씨가 그녀 곁을 떠나자마자, 그는 그 바실리스크에게 자신을 내맡기고 순순히 먹이가 되었다.\n\n그 앎이 어떻게 얻어진 것인지는 말할 도리가 없으나, 시뇨라는 애러빈 씨가 볼드 부인을 흠모한다는 것을 일종의 본능으로 알고 있었다. 사람은 개의 도움을 받아 여우를 사냥하며, 개에게 주어진 강력한 후각 기관 덕분에 그리한다는 것을 안다. 그러면서도 그런 감각이 어떻게 그토록 예민하게 작동하는지는 조금도 이해하지 못한다. 다른 여자가 남자들에게 어떻게 여겨지는지, 또 남자들이 다른 여자들에게 어떻게 여겨지는지를 여자가 이를테면 본능적으로 알고 느끼게 하는 그 기관도 그와 똑같이 강력하고 그와 똑같이 헤아리기 어렵다. 눈길 하나, 말 한마디, 몸짓 하나면 족하다. 그런 식으로 제 여성적 감각을 예리하게 놀린 끝에 시뇨라는 애러빈 씨가 엘리너 볼드를 사랑한다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그러므로 그 특유의 여성적 성향을 한 번 더 발휘하여 애러빈 씨를 제 그물에 얽어 넣는 것은 그녀로서는 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다.\n\n그 일은 그녀가 울라손에 오기 전에 이미 절반은 되어 있었으니, 그것을 마무리하기에 이보다 나은 기회가 또 어디 있겠는가? 슬로프 씨는 이제 거의 물릴 만큼 겪었다. 물론 성인군자인 척하는 성직자 하나를 절망적이고 파멸적인 정열로 몰아 미치게 하는 재미만은 아주 뿌리치지 못했지만 말이다. 손 씨는 너무 쉽게 넘어와 사냥의 즐거움이 별로 없었다. 재산가라는 그의 처지는 인연을 맺어 둘 값어치가 있을지 모르나, 연인으로서는 몹시 이류였다. 그녀가 그를 두고 사냥꾼이 꿩을 대하듯 했다고 해도 좋겠다. 그 새는 너무 쉽게 맞아 떨어져서, 식품 저장실에 걸어 두었을 때 제법 그럴듯해 보이지만 않는다면 쏠 값어치조차 없다. 시뇨라는 손 씨를 쏘는 데 많은 시간을 들이지 않겠지만, 그래도 집안에 쓸모가 있으니 자루에 넣어 둘 만은 했다.\n\n그러나 애러빈 씨는 종류가 다른 사냥감이었다. 시뇨라 자신도 애러빈 씨가 보통 이상으로 지적인 사람임을 알아볼 만큼은 충분히 영리했다. 그녀는 또한 그가 성직자로서 슬로프 씨보다 훨씬 격이 높고, 신사로서 손 씨보다 교양이 낫다는 것도 알았다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 하듯 애러빈 씨를 우스꽝스러운 비참으로 몰아넣으려 들지는 않았을 것이며, 손 씨를 처리하듯 10분 만에 그를 처리할 수 있으리라고 여기지도 않았을 것이다.\n\n애러빈 씨에 대한 그녀의 생각은 그러했다. 애러빈 씨로 말하자면, 그가 시뇨라에 대해 무엇이든 생각했다고는 할 수 없었다. 그는 그녀가 아름답다는 것을 알았고, 그녀가 자기를 홀릴 수 있으리라고 느꼈다. 지금의 비참함 속에서 그는 홀림이 필요했고, 그래서 가서 그녀의 긴 의자 머리맡에 섰다. 그녀는 그 사정을 죄다 알고 있었다. 그런 것이 그녀의 남다른 재능이었다. 그가 홀림을 필요로 한다는 것을 알아보는 것이 그녀의 천성이었고, 그를 홀리는 것이 그녀의 소임이었다. 동방의 게으름뱅이가 제 몫의 아편을 삼키듯, 런던의 난봉꾼이 제 몫의 진을 삼키듯, 애러빈 씨도 그와 같은 욕구로 그와 같은 이유에서 시뇨라 네로니의 매혹을 삼킬 채비를 했다.\n\n“애러빈 씨는 어째서 활을 쏘고 계시지 않나요?” 응접실에 둘만 남다시피 했을 때 그녀가 말했다. “아니면 그늘진 정자에서 젊은 아가씨들과 이야기라도 하시든지, 어떻게든 그 재주를 좀 쓰시든지요. 당신 같은 독신 남자가 대체 무엇 때문에 여기 초대를 받았겠어요? 차가운 닭고기와 샴페인 값은 하실 생각이 없으신가요? 나 같으면 그렇게 빈둥거리는 것이 부끄러울 텐데요.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 대답 비슷한 것을 우물거렸다. 홀리고 싶은 마음은 있었으나, 아직은 장난스럽게 되받을 기분이 되지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“대체 무슨 일이 있으신 거예요, 애러빈 씨?” 그녀가 말했다. “여기는 당신의 교구고, 손 양 말로는 이 잔치가 오로지 당신을 기려 베풀어진 것이라던데, 그런데도 이 자리에서 시무룩한 사람은 당신 하나뿐이군요. 당신 친구 슬로프 씨는 조금 전까지 나와 함께 있었는데 활기와 기운이 넘치더군요. 어째서 그분과 겨루어 보지 않으세요?”\n\n마들린 네로니처럼 예리한 관찰자가 자기가 정곡을 찔렀고 빗장을 끝까지 밀어 넣었음을 알아채기란 어렵지 않았다. 애러빈 씨는 그 공격 앞에서 눈에 띄게 움찔했고, 그녀는 그가 슬로프 씨를 질투하고 있음을 대번에 알았다.\n\n“하지만 나는 당신과 슬로프 씨를 사람의 양극이라고 보고 있어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “두 분이 서로 정반대가 아닌 데라고는 한 군데도 없어요. 같은 직업에 몸담고 있다는 것만 빼면요. 게다가 그 점에서도 두 분은 어찌나 다른지 오히려 그 규칙을 완벽하게 지켜 주고 계시죠. 그분은 무리를 좋아하고, 당신은 홀로 있기를 좋아해요. 그분은 활동적이고, 당신은 가만있죠. 그분은 일하고, 당신은 생각해요. 그분은 여자를 좋아하고, 당신은 여자를 업신여기죠. 그분은 지위와 권력을 좋아하는데, 당신도 그렇답니다. 다만 이유가 정반대일 뿐이죠. 그분은 칭찬받기를 사랑하고, 당신은 어리석게도 그것을 질색하고요. 그분은 제 보상을 얻을 거예요. 밋밋하지만 쓸모 있는 아내와 넉넉한 수입과 경건하다는 평판을 말이에요. 당신도 당신 몫의 보상을 얻을 테고요.”\n\n“그래, 그것이 무엇이겠소?” 애러빈 씨가 말했다. 그는 자기가 아첨을 받고 있음을 알면서도 그것을 참고 받아들였다. “내가 받을 보상이란 무엇이오?”\n\n“당신이 사랑한다고 차마 인정하지 못할 만큼 준엄한 어느 여인의 마음, 그리고 당신이 소중히 여긴다고 차마 인정하지 못할 만큼 자존심 센 몇몇 벗들의 존경이죠.”\n\n“푸짐한 보상이구려.” 그가 말했다. “그렇게 다루어질 것이라면 값어치는 별로 없겠소만.”\n\n“오, 당신은 슬로프 씨를 기다리는 그런 성공을 바라서는 안 돼요. 그분은 성공하도록 태어난 사람이거든요. 스스로 목표를 세우고 열망을 품은 채 그리로 달려가죠. 무엇도 그분을 그 추구에서 물러서게 하지 못해요. 그분에게는 거리낌도 두려움도 망설임도 없을 거예요. 그분의 소망은 식구가 불어나는 주교가 되는 것이고, 아내가 먼저 오고 때가 되면 앞치마가 오겠죠. 당신은 그 모든 것을 보게 될 거고, 그러면—”\n\n“그래, 그러면 어떻게 된단 말이오?”\n\n“그러면 당신도 똑같이 해 둘걸 하고 바라기 시작하겠죠.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 잔디밭을 태연히 내다보며 어깨를 소파 등받이에 기댄 채 손으로 턱을 문질렀다. 깊이 생각에 잠길 때면 나오는 버릇이었는데, 시뇨라의 말이 그를 생각에 잠기게 한 것이다. 그것이 다 사실이 아닌가? 훗날 그는, 슬로프 씨는 아닐지언정 자기만큼 재능을 타고나지도 못한 어떤 이들이 자기가 뒤처져 있는 동안 세상에서 올라선 것을 돌아보며, 나도 똑같이 할걸 하고 바라게 되지 않겠는가?\n\n“재주 있는 사색가들이 다 그런 운명 아니겠어요?” 그녀가 말했다. “그런 사람들은 다들 지금 당신처럼 골똘히 앉아 그 고운 날로 있지도 않은 비단실을 자르고 있고, 그동안 그만큼 벼려지지 못한 이들은 세상살이 싸움의 저 평범한 고르디우스 매듭을 끊어 내고 부와 명성을 얻지 않던가요? 너무 곱게 벼린 강철, 너무 날카로운 날은 이 세상 일에는 쓸모가 없답니다, 애러빈 씨.”\n\n제 마음속 비밀을 그렇게 읽어 내고 제 영혼이 품은 달갑지 않은 예감을 그대로 되뇌어 주는 이 여인은 대체 누구인가? 그녀가 말을 마치자 그는 그 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다. “나도 하루치 쓸모 있는 일을 하기에는 너무 날카롭고 너무 고운 그 어리석은 칼날 가운데 하나요?”\n\n“어째서 세상의 슬로프 같은 이들이 당신을 앞지르도록 두시나요?” 그녀가 말했다. “당신 핏줄 속의 피가 그분 것만큼 뜨겁지 않던가요? 당신 맥이 그만큼 빠르게 뛰지 않던가요? 하느님이 당신을 남자로 지으시고 여기서 남자의 일을 하라고, 그래요, 남자의 삯도 받으라고 뜻하지 않으셨던가요?”\n\n애러빈 씨는 얼굴을 문지르며 곰곰이 앉아, 어째서 이런 말들이 자기에게 던져지는지 의아해했으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 시뇨라는 말을 이었다.\n\n“사람이 저지르는 가장 큰 잘못은 세상의 좋은 것들이 얻을 값어치가 없다고 여기는 거예요. 게다가 그건 당신이 설교하는 종교에도 그토록 어긋나는 잘못이고요! 재물이 나쁘고 가질 값어치도 없는 것이라면, 하느님은 어째서 주교들에게 대대로 연 5,000파운드니 1만 파운드니 하는 것을 허락하시겠어요? 쓰라고 주신 것이 아니라면 어째서 아름다운 것들이, 사치와 즐거운 향락이 우리에게 주어졌겠어요? 그것들은 누군가를 위한 것일 텐데, 속인에게 좋은 것이 성직자에게 나쁠 리야 없지 않겠어요. 당신은 그 좋은 것들을 업신여기려 애쓰지만, 애쓸 뿐이지 성공하지는 못하고 있어요.”\n\n“내가 성공하지 못하고 있소?” 애러빈 씨가 여전히 골똘한 채, 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르고 말했다.\n\n“내가 여쭙는 거예요. 당신은 성공하고 계신가요?”\n\n애러빈 씨는 애처로운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 마치 제 안에 있는 어떤 영에게 심문을 받는 것 같았다. 대답을 거절할 수도 없고, 거짓으로 대답할 엄두도 나지 않는 그런 영에게 말이다.\n\n“자, 애러빈 씨, 고백해 보세요. 성공하고 계신가요? 돈이 그렇게 하찮은가요? 세속의 권력이 그렇게 값없는가요? 여자의 아름다움이란 지혜로운 사람이 그토록 가볍게 여겨도 좋을 하찮은 것인가요?”\n\n“여자의 아름다움이라!” 세상의 모든 여성적 아름다움이 거기 모여 있기라도 한 듯 그녀의 얼굴을 응시하며 그가 말했다. “어째서 내가 그것을 눈여겨보지 않는다고 하시오?”\n\n“나를 그런 눈으로 보신다면, 애러빈 씨, 나는 생각을 바꾸겠어요. 아니, 내게는 눈여겨볼 만한 아름다움이 없다는 것을 물론 잘 알고 있으니, 바꾸어야 마땅하겠지요.”\n\n신사는 얼굴이 새빨개졌으나 부인은 조금도 붉어지지 않았다. 낯빛이 살짝 더 살아났을 뿐이고, 그것도 딱 각별한 관심을 품은 듯한 기색을 줄 만큼이었다. 그녀는 흠모하는 남자에게서 찬사가 나오기를 기다렸으나, 그가 찬사를 바치지 않는 것을 알고는 서운하기는커녕 오히려 흡족했다. 슬로프 씨며 손 씨며, 브라운이며 존스며 로빈슨이며, 하나같이 그녀에게 찬사를 바쳤다. 그녀는 끝내 애러빈 씨가 자기를 헐뜯게 만들 수 있으리라고 은근히 기대하고 있었다.\n\n“하지만 당신의 그 눈길은 놀라움이지 흠모가 아니로군요.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신 자신에 대해 내가 그런 것을 묻는 그 대담함에 놀라신 거예요.”\n\n“그렇소, 좀 그렇구려.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그래도 나는 대답을 기다리겠어요, 애러빈 씨. 남자들이 여자를 눈여겨보지 않을 것이라면 여자는 어째서 아름답게 지어졌겠어요?”\n\n“남자들은 여자를 눈여겨보고 있소.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“그런데 당신은 어째서 아니죠?”\n\n“그것은 논점을 구걸하는 것이오, 마담 네로니.”\n\n“나는 당신이 들어주지 않을 것은 무엇도 구걸하지 않을 거예요, 애러빈 씨. 그리고 대답만은 정말로 구걸하겠어요. 당신은 대체로 여자를 벗으로 삼기에는 눈 아래로 보시지 않나요? 어디 봅시다. 저기 볼드 미망인이 지금 이 순간 제 의자에서 당신을 돌아보고 있네요. 저분을 평생의 반려로 삼는다면 뭐라고 하시겠어요?”\n\n애러빈 씨는 앉은 자리에서 몸을 일으켜 소파 위로 기대며 응접실 문 너머로, 엘리너가 버티 스탠호프와 슬로프 씨 사이에 앉아 있는 자리를 바라보았다. 그녀는 곧바로 그 눈길을 알아채고 제 눈길을 돌렸다. 지금 앉은 자리는 그녀에게 편안하지 않았다. 슬로프 씨는 그녀의 주의를 끌려고 있는 힘을 다하고 있었고, 그녀는 스탠호프 씨와 이야기함으로써 그것을 막으려 애쓰고 있었으며, 그러면서도 마음은 온통 애러빈 씨와 마담 네로니에게 쏠려 있었다. 버티 스탠호프는 그녀가 베푸는 호의를 이용해 보려 했으나, 지금 이 순간 그녀를 즐겁게 하는 일보다는 이따가 어떤 식으로 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던질까 하는 궁리를 더 하고 있었다.\n\n“보세요.” 시뇨라가 말했다. “저분이 그 고운 목을 늘여 당신을 보고 있었는데, 이제 당신이 저분을 흔들어 놓았군요. 어머, 나는 정말이지 당신에 대해 잘못 알고 있었나 봐요. 당신은 참으로 볼드 부인이 매력적인 여인이라고 여기고 계시는군요. 당신 눈빛이 그렇다고 말하고, 저분 눈빛으로 보아서는 나를 질투하고 있다고 해야겠어요. 자, 애러빈 씨, 내게 털어놓으세요. 정말 그러하다면 내가 힘닿는 데까지 그 혼사를 이루어 드리죠.”\n\n시뇨라가 그 제안에 그리 진심이 아니었다는 말은 할 필요도 없다. 그녀는 그런 문제에서 결코 진심인 적이 없었다. 남들이 진심이리라고 기대한 적도 없고, 남들이 자기를 진심이라 여겨 주기를 바란 적도 없었다. 그런 일들은 그녀의 장난감이었고, 당구대였고, 사냥개와 사냥말이었고, 왈츠와 폴카였고, 소풍과 여름날의 나들이였다. 달리 즐길 거리가 별로 없었으므로 그녀는 온갖 형태로 연애놀이를 했다. 지금은 애러빈 씨를 상대로 그 놀이를 하고 있었으니, 그의 대답이 그토록 진지하고 진실하리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다.\n\n“힘닿는 데까지라 해 봐야 아무 소용 없을 것이오.” 그가 말했다. “볼드 부인은 이미 다른 사람과 약혼했다고 나는 짐작하고 있으니 말이오.”\n\n“그럼 당신 입으로 그 혐의를 인정하시는군요.”\n\n“나를 너무 부당하게 추궁하시는구려.” 그가 대답했다. “내가 어째서 대답을 하고 있는지조차 모르겠소. 볼드 부인은 대단히 아름다운 여인이고, 아름다운 만큼 총명하오. 그 사람을 알고서 흠모하지 않기란 불가능하오.”\n\n“그러니까 그 미망인이 대단히 아름다운 여인이라고 여기시는군요?”\n\n“정말 그렇게 여기오.”\n\n“그리고 세인트 이월드 목사관을 빛내 줄 만한 사람이라고요.”\n\n“어느 남자의 집이든 넉넉히 빛내 줄 사람이오.”\n\n“그런데도 정말 뻔뻔스럽게 내게 그런 말씀을 하시는군요.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신도 잘 아시다시피 스스로 미인 행세를 하는 나에게, 게다가 바로 이 순간 당신 일에 이토록 관심을 쏟고 있는 나에게, 볼드 부인이 당신이 아는 가장 아름다운 여인이라고 정말 뻔뻔스럽게 말씀하시는군요.”\n\n“그렇게 말하지는 않았소.” 애러빈 씨가 말했다. “당신이 더 아름답소—”\n\n“아, 이제야 좀 말이 되는군요. 나는 당신이 그렇게 무정할 리 없다고 생각했어요.”\n\n“당신이 더 아름답고, 어쩌면 더 영리하기도 하오.”\n\n“고마워요, 고마워요, 애러빈 씨. 당신과 내가 벗이 되리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었어요.”\n\n“하지만—”\n\n“한 마디도 더 하지 마세요. 한 마디도 더 듣지 않겠어요. 자정까지 말씀하셔도 방금 하신 말씀보다 나아질 수는 없으니까요.”\n\n“하지만 마담 네로니, 볼드 부인은—”\n\n“볼드 부인에 대해서는 한 마디도 듣지 않겠어요. 스트리크닌이라는 무시무시한 생각이 머리를 스쳐 가기는 했지만, 그분에게 둘째 자리쯤은 기꺼이 내드리죠.”\n\n“그 사람의 자리는—”\n\n“그분에 대해서도 그분 자리에 대해서도 아무것도 듣지 않겠어요. 나는 만족했고, 그것으로 되었어요. 그런데 애러빈 씨, 나는 배가 고파 죽을 지경이랍니다. 아름답고 영리하기는 해도 내가 음식이 있는 데로 갈 수 없다는 것을 아시면서, 그런데도 내게 음식을 가져다주지 않으시는군요.”\n\n이것만은 어쨌든 사실이어서 애러빈 씨는 그에 따라 움직이지 않을 수 없었고, 그리하여 식당으로 가서 시뇨라가 원하는 것을 챙겨 왔다.\n\n“당신 것은요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아,” 그가 말했다. “나는 시장하지 않소. 이 시각에는 원래 먹지 않소.”\n\n“자, 자, 애러빈 씨, 사랑 때문에 입맛을 잃으시면 못써요. 내 입맛은 한 번도 그런 적이 없답니다. 샴페인을 반 잔만 더 주시고, 그러고는 식탁으로 가세요. 당신이 여기서 나와 더 오래 이야기하고 있으면 볼드 부인이 나를 해코지할 거예요.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 시키는 대로 했다. 그는 그녀에게서 접시와 잔을 받아 들고 식당으로 가서, 사람으로 붐비는 식탁에서 샌드위치 하나를 집어 구석에서 우물거리기 시작했다.\n\n그가 그러고 있을 때, 잠시도 앉아 있지 못하던 손 양이 방으로 들어왔다가 그가 서 있는 것을 보고 몹시 난처해했다.\n\n“어머나, 애러빈 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “아직도 자리에 앉지 못하셨소? 나는 참으로 민망하구려. 하필이면 다른 분도 아니고 당신이.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 방금 막 방에 들어왔을 뿐이라고 그녀를 안심시켰다.\n\n“그러니 더더욱 한시도 지체하시면 아니 되지요. 자, 내가 자리를 마련해 드리리다. 고맙구려, 그대여.” 볼드 부인이 제 의자에서 일어나려는 기색을 보이자 그녀가 말했다. “허나 그대가 몸을 움직이는 것만은 무슨 일이 있어도 보고 싶지 않소. 그리하면 부인들이 모두 따라 일어나야 한다고 여길 터이니. 하지만 혹시 스탠호프 씨가 다 드셨다면, 잠깐이면 되오, 스탠호프 씨, 내가 의자를 하나 더 구해 올 동안만.”\n\n그리하여 버티는 제 연적에게 자리를 내주려 일어서야 했다. 그는 무슨 일이든 그러하듯 그 일도 사람 좋게 유쾌한 태도로 해치웠고, 그 바람에 애러빈 씨는 내미는 자리를 마다할 수 없게 되었다.\n\n“그의 직분은 타인이 취하게 하소서.” 버티가 말했다. 그 인용은 이 자리에도 이 상대에게도 도무지 어울리는 것이 아니었다. “저는 먹었고 만족했습니다. 애러빈 씨, 부디 제 의자에 앉으시지요. 당신을 위해서라면 이것이 정말 주교의 자리였으면 좋겠군요.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 자리에 앉았고, 그가 앉자 볼드 부인은 옆 사람을 따라가려는 듯 일어섰다.\n\n“제발, 제발 움직이지 마시오.” 손 양이 엘리너를 거의 억지로 의자에 도로 앉히며 말했다. “스탠호프 씨는 우리를 떠나지 않을 것이오. 진짜 기사답게 그대 뒤에 서 있을 테니. 그러고 보니 생각났소만, 애러빈 씨, 슬로프 씨를 소개해 드리리다. 슬로프 씨, 애러빈 씨요.” 그리하여 두 신사는 저희가 나란히 아내로 맞으려는 부인을 사이에 두고 서로 뻣뻣하게 고개를 숙였고, 그 부인을 아내로 맞으려는 또 한 신사는 뒤에 서서 그 광경을 지켜보았다.\n\n두 사람은 전에 서로 만난 적이 없었고, 설령 그들 사이에 정다운 대화가 가능했다 할지라도 지금은 결코 그럴 만한 자리가 아니었다. 사정이 그러하니, 그 자리를 이룬 네 사람은 하나같이 혀가 묶인 것 같았다. 슬로프 씨는 곧 다가오리라 바라 마지않는 기회에 온 신경이 깨어 있었으므로 지금 이 순간의 흥취에는 별 관심이 없었다. 그의 바람은 엘리너가 자리를 뜨는 것을 보고 그 뒤를 쫓는 것이었다. 버티는 마음가짐이 꼭 그렇지는 않았다. 궂은 날은 이미 충분히 가까웠으니 그것을 앞당길 까닭이 없었다. 그는 할 수만 있다면 엘리너 볼드와 결혼하기로 마음을 정해 두었고, 오늘 그리로 가는 첫 준비 걸음을 떼기로 작정한 터였다. 그러나 시간은 넉넉했다. 식탁보를 사이에 두고 청혼을 할 생각은 없었다. 이렇게 사람 좋게 애러빈 씨에게 자리를 내준 김에, 그는 두 사람이 지금 자리에 그대로 있는 동안은 애러빈 씨가 장차의 스탠호프 부인과 이야기하도록 내버려 둘 마음도 있었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨에게 고개를 숙인 뒤 더는 한 마디도 없이 음식을 먹기 시작했다. 그는 생각으로 가득 차 있었고, 먹기는 하되 저도 모르게 먹었다.\n\n그러나 가장 딱한 것은 가엾은 엘리너였다. 기댈 수 있으리라 여겼던 단 하나의 벗이 버티 스탠호프였는데, 그마저 그녀를 저버리기로 작정한 듯했다. 애러빈 씨는 그녀에게 말을 붙이려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨의 몇 마디에 몇 마디로 답하다가, 그 자리를 더는 견딜 수 없어 손 양이 말리는데도 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 방을 빠져나갔다. 슬로프 씨가 그 뒤를 따랐고, 젊은 스탠호프는 기회를 놓쳤다.\n\n마들린 네로니는 혼자 남게 되자, 그 남다른 남자와 나눈 남다른 면담을 두고 곰곰 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그에게 한 말 가운데 진실로 받아들여지기를 바라고 한 말은 한 마디도 없었건만, 그는 그녀에게 진실 그 자체의 정신으로 대답했다. 그는 그렇게 했고, 그녀도 그가 그렇게 했음을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 그에게서 비밀을 캐냈고, 그는 거짓말이라는 남자의 흔한 특권을 빼앗기기라도 한 듯 순진하게 제 영혼을 통째로 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 그는 엘리너 볼드를 사랑하지만, 그의 눈에 엘리너는 그녀만큼 아름답지 않았다. 그는 엘리너를 아내로 삼고 싶어 하면서도 그녀가 둘 중 덜 뛰어난 쪽이라고 인정했다. 그 남자는 질문을 받자 문자 그대로 제 생각을 거짓으로 꾸미지 못했고, 진실이 그토록 껄끄러울 수밖에 없는 자리에서조차 malgré lui 진실할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n사람을 가르치는 이 스승, 이 옥스퍼드의 석학, 대학이 이룬 완성의 이 두 번 걸러 낸 정수, 종교 논설의 이 저술가, 교회 연설의 이 웅변가가 그녀의 손안에서는 어린아이 같았다. 그녀는 그를 뒤집어 놓고, 어린 처녀의 마음을 읽듯 그의 마음속을 그대로 읽어 냈다. 그 손쉬운 솔직함 때문에 그를 얕보지 않을 수 없었으나, 또 그 때문에 그가 마음에 들기도 했다. 그것은 그녀에게 새로운 것이었고, 남자의 성품에서 여태 보지 못한 새로운 결이었다. 그녀는 또한 슬로프나 손 같은 이들에게 하듯 그를 완전히 바보로 만들 수는 결코 없으리라고 느꼈다. 애러빈 씨에게 진실이 아닌 언약을 늘어놓게 하거나, 순전한 헛소리를 헛소리인 줄 알면서 듣고 있게 만들 수는 결코 없으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 볼드 부인을 진심으로 사랑하고 있다는 것은 아주 분명했다. 그리하여 시뇨라는 평소답지 않게 인정을 발휘하여, 그에게 좋은 일을 하나 해 줄 수는 없을까 하고 마음속으로 굴려 보기 시작했다. 물론 첫 기회는 버티의 것이었다. 될 수만 있다면 아우가 볼드 미망인과 결혼해야 한다는 것은 집안에서 이미 합의된 일이었다. 마들린은 아우의 궁핍한 사정도, 언니에게 마땅히 돌아가야 할 도리도 너무 잘 알고 있었으므로, 그 훌륭한 계획이 될 법한 동안에는 끼어들 마음이 없었다. 그러나 그 계획이 될 법하지 않다는 강한 의심을 품고 있었다. 그녀는 볼드 부인이 제 아우 같은 처지의 남자를 받아들일 성싶지 않다고 여겼고, 샬럿에게도 그렇게 여러 번 말한 터였다. 오히려 슬로프 씨 쪽이 성공할 가망이 더 크다고 보았는데, 그녀로서는 슬로프 씨에게서 아내를 빼앗는 일이라면 사랑으로 하는 수고였다.\n\n그리하여 시뇨라는, 버티가 실패한다면 평생 한 번쯤 인정 있는 짓을 해서 애러빈 씨를 그가 사랑하는 여인에게 넘겨주기로 마음먹었다.\n\n# 제39장. 룩어로프트 일가와 그린에이커 일가\n\n대체로 보아, 바깥 방목장에 모인 바깥 계층 사람들을 즐겁게 하고 먹이는 일에 대한 손 양의 채비는 실패라 할 수 없었다.\n\n전반적인 즐거움에 자그마한 흠 두 가지가 생기기는 했으나, 그것은 일시적인 것이었고 실재라기보다 겉보기의 것이었다. 첫째는 젊은 해리 그린에이커의 추락이었고, 다른 하나는 룩어로프트 부인과 그 식구들의 상승이었다.\n\n퀸틴으로 말하자면, 그것은 젊은 그린에이커가 훨씬 잘해 냈다 한들 말 탄 사내들 사이에서 누렸을 인기보다 더 큰 인기를 걸어 다니는 소년들 사이에서 누리게 되었다. 그것은 땅에서 거의 뽑혀 나갈 지경이 되도록 뱅뱅 돌아갔고, 밀가루 자루는 그 근처로 꾀어들일 수 있는 모든 이의 등과 머리에 분칠을 하는 데 신나게 쓰였다.\n\n물론 해리가 죽었다는 소문이 모인 사람들 사이에 두루 퍼졌고, 그가 그 낙마에서 아무 상처 없이 벗어났음이 밝혀졌을 때 그와 그 어머니 사이에 애처로운 장면이 벌어졌다. 그 통에 맥주가 꽤 많이 소비되었고, 퀸틴은 “빌어먹을 것”이니 “성가신 것”이니 하는 소리를 들으며, 비슷한 위험에 처할 법한 어린 아들을 둔 모든 어머니에게서 두루 저주를 받았다. 그러나 룩어로프트 부인의 일은 그보다 훨씬 심각한 성질의 것이었다.\n\n“내 자네한테 똑똑히 말하네. 얼굴을 맞대고 말일세. 그 여편네가 마님네 응접실에 가 있다니까. 저하고 거시하고 그 덩치 큰 계집애 둘까지, 눈두덩까지 치장을 하고서 말이야.” 이렇게 말한 사람은 매우 단호하고 매우 분개하고 매우 살진 어느 농부의 아내였는데, 그는 긴 의자 끝에 앉아 커다란 무명 우산 손잡이에 몸을 기대고 있었다.\n\n“하지만 자네가 직접 본 건 아니잖은가, 구펀 댁?” 그린에이커 부인이 말했다. 아들이 방금 겪은 위험에 이 소식까지 겹쳐 그는 거의 정신을 못 차릴 지경이었다. 그린에이커 씨는 룩어로프트 씨와 꼭 같은 넓이의 땅을 부치고 있었고, 소작료도 꼭 같이 꼬박꼬박 냈으며, 교구 회의실에서 그의 의견은 조금도 못하지 않게 대접받았다. 룩어로프트 부인이 세상에서 올라선 일은 그린에이커 부인에게 쓰디쓴 노릇이었다. 그 자신은 발리스터브 농장을 로즈뱅크로 바꾸어 놓고 이따금 룩어로프트 씨의 편지에 향사라는 위엄을 얹어 준 그런 종류의 겉치레에는 아무 취미가 없었다. 제 살림집을 바이올릿 빌라로 바꾸고 싶은 마음도, 제 바깥양반이 이름 뒤에 새로 지어낸 꼬리표를 달고 다니는 꼴을 보고 싶은 마음도 없었다. 그러나 룩어로프트 부인이 그런 영예를 사냥해 손에 넣는 데 성공한다는 것은 그에게 뼈에 사무치는 상처였다. 그는 제 작은 힘이 닿는 데까지 룩어로프트 부인을 헐뜯고 비웃어 왔다. 교회에서 나오면서 일부러 그를 밀치고는 대등한 사이다운 스스럼없는 태도로 사과하곤 했다. “아이고 댁네, 미안하우. 근데 요새 댁네가 어찌나 몸이 나셨는지 원.” 그는 룩어로프트 씨를 보면 겉으로는 살갑게 “댁을 데리고 사는 그 여편네”의 안부를 묻곤 했고, 스스로 생각하기에 대체로 저 잘난 이웃을 상대로 제 몫을 꽤 잘 지켜 왔다. 그러나 이제 그는 자기가 뚜렷이 따로 떨어진 낮은 계층으로 분류되었음을 알게 되었다. 룩어로프트 부인이 울라손 응접실로 청함을 받은 것은 오로지 제 집을 로즈뱅크라 부르고, 남편을 구슬려 아들들에게 농장을 마련해 줄 돈을 모으는 대신 피아노와 비단옷을 사게 만들었기 때문이었다.\n\n그린에이커 부인은 손 양을 아무리 우러러보고 제 남편의 지주를 아무리 존경한다 해도, 이 일만은 자기와 제 식구들에게 저질러진 부당한 처사로 볼 수밖에 없었다. 지금까지 룩어로프트네가 그린에이커네와 다른 계층이라고 인정받은 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그들의 잘난 체는 모조리 제가 저를 잘났다 하는 것이었고, 그들의 겉치레는 모조리 제 돈으로 산 것이지 남이 내려 준 것이 아니었다. 이 근방의 지역 군주들, 곧 이 고장에서 영예가 흘러나오는 샘들은 지금까지 그들에게 어떤 신분의 도장도 찍어 준 적이 없었다. 지금까지 그들의 크리놀린 속치마며 늦도록 자지 않는 버릇이며 종종거리는 걸음걸이는 그린에이커 부인이 놀려 먹기 좋은 재료였고, 그 놀림은 그의 시샘을 빼내는 안전판 노릇을 했다. 그러나 이제, 그리고 이제부터는 사정이 아주 달라질 것이었다. 이제 룩어로프트네는 제 야심이 이 고장 상류층의 재가를 받았노라고 뽐낼 것이고, 이제 그들은 저희가 각별한 대접을 받을 자격이 있다는 주장을 얼마간 사실인 양 내세울 것이었다. 그들은 주교와 준남작들 앞에 대등한 손님으로 앉았고, 손 양이 제 응접실 양탄자 위에서 그들에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했으며, 이제 살아 있는 백작부인과 한 상에 앉으려는 참이었다! 어린 시절 대등하던 때에 그린에이커네 아이들이 늘 그렇게 부르던 밥 룩어로프트가 어쩌면 아너러블 조지 옆에 앉을지도 몰랐고, 그 못난 거시가 레이디 마거리타 드 코시에게 커스터드를 건네도 좋다는 허락을 받을지도 몰랐다.\n\n그 영예의 열매는, 시샘받는 그 집안에 실제로 돌아간 몫으로 말하자면, 그다지 시샘할 만한 것이 못 되었다. 드 코시 집안 사람들이 룩어로프트네에게 베푼 관심은 지극히 적었고, 주교 일행과 어울려 얻은 즐거움의 양도 그들이 보낸 하루의 따분한 단조로움에 대한 보상이 되기에는 어림없었다. 그러나 그들이 견뎌 낸 것에 대해서는 그린에이커 부인은 조금도 셈에 넣지 않았다. 그는 오로지 그들이 틀림없이 누리고 있으리라 여기는 것만을, 그리고 이번 영광의 결과로 로즈뱅크 집안이 드러내 보일 무시무시하게 드높아진 생활의 어조만을 생각했다.\n\n“그래도 자네가 그이들을 거기서 봤는가, 댁네? 자네 두 눈으로 그이들이 거기 있는 걸 봤느냔 말일세.” 가엾은 그린에이커 부인이 물었다. 아직도 의심해 볼 만한 구석이 남아 있기를 바라면서였다.\n\n“아니, 내가 거기 있지 않고서야 어찌 그럴 수 있겠나?” 구펀 부인이 말했다. “나야 이 좋은 아침나절에 그이들 얼굴 한 번 못 봤지. 하지만 본 사람을 내가 봤네. 우리 존 알지? 그 녀석이 마님 몸종인 벳시 러스크하고 눈이 맞아 붙어 다니잖은가. 그 벳시가 여느 부엌데기 같은 아이가 아닐세. 그래 벳시가 우리 존한테 나오지 않았겠나. 그 애가 나한테는 늘 어찌나 깍듯한지 모르네, 벳시 러스크 그 애가 말이야. 그래 존하고 한 바퀴 돌기도 전에 나한테 저 위 저택에서 벌어지는 일을 반 푼어치까지 죄다 일러 주더군.”\n\n“그 애가 그러던가?” 그린에이커 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그럼, 그러고말고.” 구펀 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래 그 애가 그 사람들이 저 위 응접실에 있다고 하던가?”\n\n“들어오는 걸 제 눈으로 봤다더군. 그 집 식구 누구보다도 갑절은 곱게 차려입고서, 목이며 가슴이며를 갓난애처럼 홀랑 드러내 놓고 말일세.”\n\n“저 발칙한 것들!” 그린에이커 부인이 외쳤다. 원수들이 걸친 온갖 귀족 흉내 가운데 이것이 그를 가장 심란하게 했다.\n\n“암, 그렇고말고.” 구펀 부인이 말을 이었다. “아주 벗을 대로 벗고서 말일세. 정작 귀인들은 자네나 나나 다름없이 차려입고 있었다지 뭔가, 그린에이커 댁.”\n\n“뻔뻔스럽기도 하지.” 그린에이커 부인이 말했다. 잘 여며 덮인 그의 가슴에서는 룩어로프트 집안에 관한 한 인정의 젖이 죄다 말라 가고 있었다.\n\n“내 말이 그 말일세.” 구펀 부인이 말했다. “우리 바깥양반 토머스 구펀도 그 얘기를 듣더니 똑같은 소리를 하더군. ‘몰리,’ 그이가 나더러 그러데. ‘자네가 만에 하나 아침나절에 그런 꼴로 홀랑 벗고 나다니게 되거든, 다시는 이 낡은 집으로 돌아오지 말게.’ 그래 내가 그랬지. ‘토머스, 다시는 그럴 일 없으리다.’ 그랬더니 그이가 또, ‘그건 그렇고, 대체 그 여편네는 실오라기 하나 안 걸치고서 그 신경통을 어떻게 견딘담?’ 하더군.” 그러고서 구펀 부인은 룩어로프트 부인이 신경통 발작으로 겪을 법한 고생을 떠올리며 큰 소리로 웃었다.\n\n“그래도 그렇지, 핏줄에 진짜 피가 흐르는 사람들한테 저를 그렇게 견주다니.” 그린에이커 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그런데 벳시가 한 얘기가 그것뿐이 아닐세. 그이들이 죄다 칠면조 수컷처럼 부풀어서 마님네 응접실로 밀고 들어갔다는군. ‘누가 감히 우리더러 안 된다 하겠나?’ 하는 낯짝으로 말이야. 그레고리가 그이들더러 이리 내려가라고 이를까 하다가, 워낙 거창하게 차려입은 통에 기가 죽어 못 그랬다지 뭔가. 그래 그대로 들어갔는데, 마님이 죽음처럼 침울한 낯으로 그이들을 쳐다보시더라네.”\n\n“그것 보게.” 그린에이커 부인이 크게 안도하며 말했다. “그러니까 그이들도 우리하고 다르게 청함을 받은 게 아니었단 말이지?”\n\n“벳시 말이, 그레고리가 그러는데 마님이 그이들이 거기 있는 걸 보고 조금도 달가워하지 않으셨다더군. 그레고리 생각에는 그이들도 우리 나머지처럼 이리로 오게 되어 있었다는 거야.”\n\n여기에는 큰 위로가 있었다. 그렇다고 그린에이커 부인이 아주 흡족해진 것은 아니었다. 자기에게 정의가 이루어지려면 룩어로프트 부인이 부추김을 받지 않는 것만으로는 부족하고 아주 벌을 받아야 마땅하다고 그는 느꼈다. 그린에이커 부인이 식구들에게 그토록 자주 읽어 주던 그 성경 속 잔치에서는 어떻게 하였던가? 어찌하여 손 양은 당당히 그 침입자에게 걸어가 이렇게 말하지 않았단 말인가. “벗이여, 그대는 그대에게 맞지 아니한 높은 자리에 올라왔도다. 더 낮은 데로 내려가라, 그리하면 그대의 짝들을 만나리라.” 지금 이 순간 룩어로프트네가 아무리 냉대를 받는다 한들, 그들은 앞으로도 제 자리와 제 야심과 제 영예를 두고 뽐낼 수 있을 것이었다.\n\n“그런 거창한 티를 다 내면서 어찌 그리 상스러울 수 있는지 나는 참 모르겠네.” 그린에이커 부인이 이 이야기를 떨쳐 내지 못하고 말을 이었다. “들었수, 영감?” 그는 마침 다가온 남편에게 이 이야기를 죄다 되풀이할 참으로 말을 걸었다. “룩어로프트 댁네하고 밥이하고 거시하고 그 집 식구들이 죄다 마님네 응접실에 앉아 오 펜스짜리 동전처럼 거들먹거리고 있다지 뭐요. 우리보다 더 청함을 받은 것도 아니면서 말이오. 그런 소리 들어 본 적 있수?”\n\n“그래, 그이들이라고 그러지 말란 법이 어디 있소?” 농부 그린에이커가 말했다.\n\n“귀인들에게 저를 견주다니요. 무슨 땅마지기나 가진 사람들이라도 되는 것처럼 말이에요!” 구펀 부인이 말했다.\n\n“뭐, 그이들이 좋다 하고 마님도 좋다 하시면 나야 얼마든지 좋소.” 농부가 말했다. “나로 말하면 여기가 더 좋소. 여기가 더 내 집 같고, 마누라한테 그런 좋은 옷을 사 입힐 일도 없으니 말이오. 사람마다 제 취향이 있는 법이오, 구펀 댁. 이웃 룩어로프트가 저희 쪽이 더 낫다고 여긴다면 그것도 얼마든지 좋소.”\n\n그린에이커 부인은 남편 옆에 앉아 잔치의 고된 일을 시작했고, 어느 정도는 마음의 평온을 되찾은 채로 그리했다. 그러면서도 그는 이 일에서만은 남편의 지론에 썩 동의하지 않는다는 뜻으로 제 수다 동무를 향해 고개를 저었다.\n\n“내 한마디 하리다, 아주머니들.” 그가 말을 이었다. “룩어로프트 어멈이 저 위 훌륭한 소파에 앉아 있다고 해서 마님이 차려 주신 이 만찬을 즐길 수 없다면, 우리는 다 같이 집으로 돌아가는 게 옳소. 그런 일에 운다면 참된 슬픔이 닥칠 때는 어쩌려고 그러오? 임자, 아까 그 애가 굴러떨어지면서 목이라도 부러졌다면 지금 어떻겠소?”\n\n그린에이커 부인은 기가 꺾여 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그린에이커 씨 같은 사려 깊은 사람들이 아무리 설교를 한다 한들, 룩어로프트 같은 집안이 이 넓은 세상에 적잖은 속앓이를 일으키는 것만은 분명하다.\n\n플로머시 씨가 튼튼한 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 시골 손님들 사이를 돌아다니며 잔치의 사회자 노릇과 순경 우두머리 노릇을 겸하는 모습은 볼만했다. “이봐, 애송이, 그렇게 꽥꽥거리지 않고는 못 배기겠거든 열두 에이커 밭 저쪽으로 밥그릇을 들고 건너가는 게 좋을 걸세. 자, 처녀들, 손가락이나 배배 꼬면서 거기 왜들 서 있는가? 나와서 총각들한테 얼굴을 보이게. 그렇게 부끄러워할 낯짝도 아니지 않은가. 여봐, 거기. 자네는 누구지? 여기는 어떻게 들어왔나?”\n\n이 마지막의 달갑잖은 물음은 스물네 살쯤 된 젊은이에게 던져진 것이었는데, 플로머시 씨가 보기에 그 젊은이에게는 시골에서 배우고 시골에서 살아온 흔적이 넉넉하지 않았다.\n\n“황송합니다요, 나리. 마부 배럴 어른이 교회 쪽 쪽문으로 저를 들여보내 주셨습니다요. 제가 거의 늘 이 댁 일을 해 온 터라서요.”\n\n“그렇다면 마부 배럴 어른더러 도로 내보내 달라고 하게.” 플로머시 씨가 말했다. 자기에게 바쳐진 치안판사 같은 위엄에도 그는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다. “이름이 무엇인가? 무슨 일을 하는 자인가? 그리고 누구 밑에서 일하는가?”\n\n“저는 스텁스입니다요, 나리. 밥 스텁스입니다요. 그리고, 그리고, 그리고…”\n\n“그래 무슨 일을 하느냐고 물었네, 스텁스.”\n\n“미장이입니다요, 나리.”\n\n“내가 자네를 발라 주지, 배럴까지 같이 발라 주지. 자네는 들어온 걸음 그대로 냉큼 이 밭에서 걸어 나가게. 우리는 미장이 따위 필요 없네. 필요할 때는 사람을 보내 부를 걸세. 자, 이보게, 걸으라니까.”\n\n미장이 스텁스는 이 무서운 포고에 크게 풀이 죽었다. 그는 발랄한 사내였고, 울라손의 낙원에 들어선 이래 숲의 요정 하나를 제 곁으로 끌어들이는 데 성공하여 미장이들이 으레 하는 달콤하고 실없는 소리를 속삭이던 참이었는데, 하필 그때 위대한 플로머시 씨와 맞닥뜨린 것이었다. 신들의 술과 신들의 음식이 아스포델 들판 위로 막 내려오려는 참에 제 나무 요정에게서 떼어져 울부짖으며 바체스터의 아수라장으로 쫓겨 간다는 것은 참혹한 일이었다. 그는 애원이 통할지 어떨지 시험해 보기 시작했으나, 도시의 애원은 이 위대한 시골 권세가 앞에서 헛일이었다. 플로머시 씨는 퇴장을 명했을 뿐 아니라, 저 사이비 케르베로스 배럴이 지키도록 맡겨 둔 대문으로 가는 길을 지팡이를 들어 가리키고는, 손수 그 추방령이 집행되는 것을 지켜보려고 따라나섰다.\n\n그러나 구름 위에 앉은 여신 가운데 가장 다정하고, 가엾고 연약하고 허물 많은 인간에게 가장 소중한 여신인 자비의 여신이 그린에이커 씨의 모습으로 그 밭에 나타났다. 중재에 나선 여신이 이보다 더 반가운 적은 없었다.\n\n“여보시오, 이 사람아.” 그린에이커 씨가 말했다. “이런 날에 사소한 일로 걸고넘어지지 마시오. 내가 저 아이를 잘 아오. 내가 청하는 것이니 그냥 두시구려. 저 아이가 먹고 마셔야 얼마나 되겠소. 마님이 아쉬워하실 리 없다는 걸 나는 아오.”\n\n그런데 플로머시 씨와 그린에이커 씨는 둘도 없는 벗이었다. 플로머시 씨는 울라손 저택에서 가장 안락한 방 하나를 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있었으나, 그는 독신이었고 그 방에 혼자였으며, 게다가 저택 안에서는 플로머시 씨라 해도 담배를 피울 수 없었다. 그가 참된 행복을 누리는 순간은 그린에이커 부인의 티 없이 깨끗한 앞부엌, 그중에서도 가장 따뜻한 구석에 놓인 커다란 안락의자에서 흘러갔다. 속에 든 사람이 녹아내려 유쾌한 이야기의 냇물이 되어 흘러나오는 곳이 바로 거기였고, 존경을 받으면서도 마음이 편안한 곳이 거기였으며, 윗사람의 위엄을 거스르지도 않고 아랫사람의 허물없음을 불러들이지도 않으면서 삶의 온갖 격식에서 짐을 벗을 수 있는 곳이 거기, 어쩌면 오직 거기뿐이었다. 그의 긴 담뱃대가 늘 정해진 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있는 곳도 거기였는데, 그것은 허락받은 정도가 아니라 권장되기까지 했다.\n\n사정이 이러하였으니 플로머시 씨가 그린에이커 씨에게 그만한 부탁을 거절하리라고는 생각할 수 없었다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 그는 준엄한 권위를 조금 더 내보이지 않고서는 그 부탁을 들어주지 않았다.\n\n“먹고 마시는 것이라니요, 그린에이커 씨! 아니지요. 저 녀석이 먹고 마시는 게 문제가 아니라, 청하지도 않은 자리에 들어와서 저런 녀석이 보이는 본보기가 문제요. 그것도 저만한 나이를 먹은 녀석이 말이오. 게다가 태어난 뒤로 울라손에서 하루도 일해 본 적 없는 녀석이오. 미장이라! 내가 발라 주지!”\n\n“그러면 우리 집 일은 오래도록 해 주었소, 플로머시 씨. 기와 얹는 솜씨로 말하면 바체스터에서 저만한 사람이 없소.” 상대가 말했다. 진실에 그리 충실한 말은 아니었으나, 자비란 본디 그래야 하는 법이다. “자, 자, 오늘은 그냥 두고 싸움은 내일 하시구려. 저기 저 처녀 앞에서 저 아이를 망신 주고 싶지는 않을 것 아니오?”\n\n“내 성미에는 영 맞지 않는 일이오만.” 플로머시 씨가 말했다. “그리고 이봐 스텁스, 조심해서 얌전히 굴게. 소란이 났다는 소리가 들리면 그게 어디서 난 것인지 내가 다 알 걸세. 나는 자네 같은 바체스터 직공들을 훤히 꿰고 있네. 자네들이 어떤 물건으로 만들어졌는지 다 알고 있네.”\n\n그리하여 스텁스는 기분 좋게 물러갔다. 집사의 관대함에 경의를 표하며 제 텁수룩한 머리의 앞머리를 잡아당겼고, 농부의 친절에 경의를 표하며 한 번 더, 그것도 두 배로 잡아당겼다. 그리고 걸어가면서 그는 고마움에 겨운 마음속으로 맹세하기를, 언제든 농부 그린에이커가 품삯 없이 하루치 일을 시키고 싶어 하거든 그 일을 해 줄 사람은 바로 저라고 했다. 그러나 그 약속을 실제로 이행하라는 부름을 받을 일은 아마 없을 것이었다.\n\n그러나 플로머시 씨는 마음이 아주 편하지는 않았으니, 불의한 청지기가 떠올랐고 자기가 불의의 재물로 친구를 사귄 것은 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작한 까닭이었다. 그렇더라도 그 때문에 긴 식탁의 아래쪽 끝에서 제 소임을 다하는 그의 태도가 흐트러지지는 않았다. 미장이 스텁스의 축원을 받았다 하여 그린에이커 씨가 식탁 위쪽 끝에서 제 소임을 더 못하게 된 것도 아니었다. 게다가 플로머시 씨가 일어나 식전 감사 기도를 올리면서, 손 마님께서 그 크신 너그러움으로 우리 앞에 차려 주신 좋은 것들에 대하여 우리 모두가 참으로 감사하게 하여 주시기를 하느님께 빌었을 때에도, 손님들은 그것을 조금도 잘못이라 여기지 않았다!\n\n그동안 잔디밭 천막 안의 귀인들은 술술 잘 헤엄쳐 나가고 있었다. 물론 무제한으로 나오는 샴페인이 귀인들을 헤엄치게 할 수 있다면 말이다. 하커웨이 고스 경이 손 양의 건강을 위해 잔을 들자고 제안하면서, 그를 언제나 몸이 성하고 아무리 일을 시켜도 지치는 법이 없는 순종 경주마에 비겼다. 손 씨가 답사를 하며, 제 누이가 부름을 받을 때면 언제나 달릴 수 있는 사람으로 남기를 바란다고 말한 뒤 드 코시 집안의 건강과 번영을 위해 잔을 들자고 했다. 제 누이는 그 초라한 식탁에 그 집안 분들이 이토록 많이 와 주신 것을 크나큰 영광으로 여긴다고 했다. 중요한 공무 때문에 백작께서 부득이 자리를 비우실 수밖에 없었음은 여러분이 다 아시는 바라고 했다. 임금을 섬기는 도리가 그분을 제 집 화롯가에서 불러낸 것이니, 자기로서는 그분을 울라손에서 뵙지 못한 것을 감히 유감이라 할 수 없으나, 그럼에도 감히 말씀드리자면, 그러니까 한 가지 바람을 말씀드리자면, 아니 의견을 말씀드리려던 것인데. 이리하여 손 씨는 이런 처지에 놓인 시골 신사들이 으레 그러하듯 다소 말이 막히고 말았다. 그러나 마침내 그는 저 고귀한 백작과 백작부인과 드 코시 성의 온 집안의 건강을 위해 잔을 드는 것이 참으로 흡족하다고 선언하며 자리에 앉았다.\n\n그다음에는 아너러블 조지가 답사를 했다. 다소 고르지 못한 그의 웅변이 거쳐 간 여러 대목을 우리는 하나하나 따라가지 않으려 한다. 바로 곁에 있던 이들은 처음에는 그를 일으켜 세우기가 꽤 어렵다고 여겼으나, 곧이어 그를 다시 자리에 앉히는 데에서 훨씬 더 큰 어려움을 겪었다. 요즘 같은 시대에는 둘 중 하나를 반드시 정해 두어야 한다. 잔치 자리에서의 연설을 아예 금기로 삼아 사실상 불가능하게 만들거나, 아니면 그 특권을 행사할 사람들에게 먼저 문관 임용 시험 위원들 앞에서 경쟁 시험을 치르게 하거나. 지금 형편으로는 아너러블 조지 같은 이들이 영국 교육을 위한 우리의 노고에 명예를 거의 돌려주지 못한다.\n\n식당에서는 주교가 훨씬 더 말끔하고 격에 맞게 그날의 예를 치렀다. 그 역시 손 양의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었는데, 자기가 빛내고 있는 주교석에 어울리는 태도로 그리했다. 그곳의 자리는 아마도 천막 쪽보다 조금 더 지루하고 한 겹쯤 덜 활기찼을 것이다. 그러나 흥에서 잃은 것은 예법에서 남김없이 채워졌다.\n\n이리하여 여러 식탁에서 잔치는 큰 성황과 두루 미치는 기쁨 속에 끝이 났다.\n\n# 제40장. 울라손 운동회 — 제2막\n\n“그들을 취하게 한 것이 나를 담대하게 하였도다.” 엘리너를 뒤쫓아 식당을 나서면서 슬로프 씨는 이런 말로 스스로를 북돋웠다. 사실 그 방에서 정말로 취한 사람을 본 것은 아니었으나 포도주가 꽤 많이 비워졌고, 슬로프 씨도 제 몫을 사양하지 않았다. 손에 쥔 일을 치를 만큼 스스로를 조여 두기 위해서였다. 이런 자리에서 바쿠스의 도움을 청하는 편이 상책이라 여긴 사람이 그가 처음은 아니다.\n\n엘리너는 누군가 뒤따라오고 있다는 것을 알아채기도 전에 이미 창문을 지나 잔디밭에 나와 있었다. 마침 그때 손님들은 거의 모두 식탁에 붙들려 있었다. 여기저기 다정한 남녀 한두 쌍이 눈에 띄기는 했으니, 잔 부딪는 소리나 아너러블 조지와 바체스터 주교의 입에서 흘러나오는 웅변의 매력보다 자기들끼리의 달콤한 이야기를 더 좋아하는 이들이었다. 그러나 정원은 슬로프 씨가 바랄 수 있는 만큼 거의 비어 있었다.\n\n엘리너는 자기가 쫓기고 있음을 알았고, 더는 달아날 수 없게 된 사슴이 몸을 돌려 사냥개들에게 맞서듯 슬로프 씨에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n“저 때문에 방에서 나오시게 할 마음은 없어요.” 그녀가 자기가 낼 수 있는 온갖 뻣뻣함을 실어 말했다. “저는 벗을 찾으러 나온 것이랍니다. 슬로프 씨, 부디 돌아가 주시기를 청합니다.”\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨는 그만한 청에 물러설 사람이 아니었다. 그는 하루 종일 볼드 부인이 자기에게 살갑지 않다는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 그 때문에 어느 정도 마음이 눌려 있기는 했다. 그러나 그 사실에서 제 소망이 헛되다는 확신을 끌어내지는 않았다. 그가 보기에 그녀는 자기에게 화가 나 있었다. 그런데 그것은 그가 그토록 오래 그녀의 마음을 건드려 놓기만 한 탓이 아니겠는가—이제부터 두 사람의 이름이 하나가 되리라고 세상에 고백할 기회는 주지 않은 채, 스스로도 알고 있듯이 그녀의 이름을 제 이름과 나란히 사람들 입에 오르내리게 만든 탓에서 온 것이 아니겠는가? 가엾은 부인. 이 대목에 관해서라면 그의 속에도 그리스도인다운 양심의 가책 비슷한 것이 없지는 않았다. 자기가 꾸물거린 탓에 그녀에게 못할 짓을 했는지도 몰랐다. 그러나 지금 이 순간 그는 손 씨의 샴페인을 너무 많이 들이켠 터라 속으로 흔들릴 여지가 없었다. 레이디 맥베스의 호언을 되뇐 것은 옳았다. 그는 취하지 않았으되 무슨 짓이든 할 만큼 담대했다. 그런 상태로 프라우디 부인과 마주치지 못한 것이 애석할 따름이다.\n\n“제가 모시고 가도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다.” 그가 말했다. “혼자 가시게 두는 일은 생각조차 할 수 없어요.”\n\n“아니요, 그리하셔야 해요, 슬로프 씨.” 엘리너가 여전히 몹시 뻣뻣하게 말했다. “혼자 있고 싶다는 것이 제 각별한 바람이니까요.”\n\n큰 비밀을 입 밖에 낼 때가 이미 와 있었다. 슬로프 씨는 지금이 아니면 영영 없다는 것을 알았고, 지금으로 하기로 마음을 굳혔다. 아리따운 여인의 마음을 얻어 보려 나선 것이 이번이 처음은 아니었다. 그는 전에도 무릎을 꿇어 보았고, 말로 옮길 수 없는 것들을 눈빛에 담아 보았으며, 꿀 바른 말을 속삭여도 보았다. 실상 이런 일에는 제법 이력이 난 사람이라, 한때 올리비아 프라우디를 그토록 흡족하게 했고 아직도 또렷이 기억하고 있는 열띤 사설들을 볼드 부인의 다소 다를지 모를 취향에 맞추어 손보기만 하면 되었다.\n\n“가라고 하지 마세요, 볼드 부인.” 그가 격정에 찬 눈길로, 격정에 차 있으면서 동시에 거룩하기도 한 눈길로 말했다. 슬로프 씨 부류의 신사들에게 그리 드물지 않은, 이를테면 다정경건형이라 불러도 좋을 눈길이었다. “제 마음에 가득 찬 몇 마디를, 바로 그것을 아뢰려고 일부러 여기까지 온 그 몇 마디를 말씀드리기 전에는 가라고 하지 마세요.”\n\n엘리너는 이제 사정이 어떠한지 알아차렸다. 자기가 무엇을 겪게 될지 대번에 알았고, 그 앎이 그녀를 몹시 비참하게 만들었다. 물론 슬로프 씨를 거절하면 그것으로 끝이라고 말할 사람도 있을 것이다. 그러나 엘리너로서는 그것으로 끝이 아니었다. 슬로프 씨가 자기에게 청혼했다는 사실 자체가 부주교에게는 승리요, 애러빈 씨의 처신을 상당 부분 옳았다고 증명해 주는 일이 될 터였다. 이 미망인은 자기가 틀렸었다는 생각을 참을성 있게 견뎌 낼 수가 없었다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨를 두둔했고, 그를 아는 사이로 받아들인 것은 조금도 나무랄 데 없는 일이라고 잘라 말했으며, 그가 스스로를 그저 아는 사이 이상으로 여긴다는 생각을 비웃었고, 자기를 위한다며 부주교가 늘어놓은 경고에 분개했다. 그런데 이제 부주교가 옳았고 자기가 온통 틀렸다는 것이 충분히 불쾌한 방식으로 그녀에게 증명되려는 참이었다.\n\n“조금 전 식탁에 앉아 있을 때는 하지 못하고 이제야 제게 하셔야 할 말씀이 무엇인지 저는 모르겠어요.” 그러고는 입을 다물고 눈길을 굳힌 채, 사람을 얼어붙게 만들었어야 마땅할 태도로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n그러나 신사란 샴페인이 가득 차 있을 때는 좀처럼 얼어붙지 않는 법이고, 슬로프 씨로 말하면 어느 때든 얼어붙게 만들기가 쉬운 사람이 아니었다.\n\n“볼드 부인, 사람이 여럿 앞에서는 좀처럼 하지 못하는 말이 있습니다. 어쩌면 어느 때든 좀처럼 하지 못할 말이, 실은 입 밖으로 내고 싶어 애가 타면서도 도무지 발설하기 어려운 말이 있지요. 제가 지금 부인께 하고자 하는 것이 바로 그런 말입니다.” 그러고는 그 다정경건한 눈길이 아까보다 조금 더 힘을 실어 되풀이되었다.\n\n엘리너로서는 식당 창 앞에 못 박힌 듯 서서 손 양의 손님들이 훤히 보는 앞에서 그의 청혼을 받는 것이 될 일이 아니었다. 그래서 스스로를 지키느라 걸음을 옮겼고, 그 바람에 슬로프 씨는 그녀와 나란히 걷는다는 목적을 이루었다. 그는 이제 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n“고맙습니다, 슬로프 씨. 그 마음은 감사히 받겠어요. 다만 제가 함께 있을 아주 짧은 동안은 혼자 걷는 편이 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그토록 짧아야만 하나요?” 그가 말했다. “꼭 그래야만—”\n\n“네.” 엘리너가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. “되도록 짧게 부탁드려요.”\n\n“저는 바랐습니다, 볼드 부인. 저는 바랐어요—”\n\n“저에 관한 일이라면 아무것도 바라지 마세요, 슬로프 씨. 부디 그러지 마세요. 무슨 바람을 말씀하시는지 저는 모르고, 알 필요도 없어요. 우리 사이는 아주 얕은 아는 사이일 뿐이고 아마 앞으로도 그럴 거예요. 부디, 부디 그것으로 족하다고 여겨 주세요. 어차피 우리가 다툴 까닭은 조금도 없으니까요.”\n\n볼드 부인이 슬로프 씨를 다소 매몰차게 다루고 있는 것은 분명했고, 그도 그렇게 느꼈다. 그녀는 그가 청혼을 내놓기도 전에 그를 물리치고 있었으며, 동시에 그가 이토록 스스럼없이 구는 것은 분수에 넘치게 자기를 높이는 짓이라고 일러 주고 있었다. 그녀는 이런 시늉조차 하지 않았다\n\n“아니오”라는 그 날카롭고 쏘아붙이는 말에서 독침을 뽑아내는 일 따위는.\n\n그는 여전히 몹시 다정하고 몹시 경건하기로 작정하고 있었다. 볼드 부인이 그렇게까지 말했는데도 아직 희망을 버리지 않았던 것이다. 그러면서도 한편으로는 슬며시 화가 나기도 했다. 그가 보기에 이 미망인은 지나치게 높은 데서 굴었고, 제 이야기를 하는 말투가 너무 위압적이었다. 자기에게 영예가 내려지고 있다는 것을 도무지 모르는 눈치였다. 슬로프 씨는 할 수 있는 데까지는 다정하게 굴 작정이었으나, 그것이 통하지 않는다면 자기도 잠시 높은 말 위에 올라앉는 편이 나쁘지 않겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 슬로프 씨는 두말할 것 없이 아주 다정해질 수 있는 사람이었지만 아주 사나워질 수도 있는 사람이었고, 제 재주를 스스로 알고 있었다.\n\n“그것은 잔인한 말씀이에요.” 그가 말했다. “그리스도인답지도 않고요. 우리 가운데 가장 못난 자에게도 희망을 가지라는 분부가 내려져 있습니다. 제가 무슨 일을 했기에 그토록 준엄한 선고를 내리시는 겁니까?” 그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었는데, 그동안 미망인은 아무 말 없이 고른 걸음으로 꾸준히 걸어갈 뿐이었다.\n\n“아름다운 분이여.” 마침내 그가 터뜨렸다. “아름다운 분이여, 제가 당신을 흠모한다는 것을 모르는 체하실 수는 없을 겁니다. 그렇습니다, 엘리너, 그래요, 저는 당신을 사랑합니다. 남자가 여자에게 품을 수 있는 가장 참된 애정으로 당신을 사랑합니다. 천국을 바라는 소망 다음가는 것이 당신을 얻으려는 소망이에요.” (여기서 슬로프 씨의 기억이 그를 속였다. 그러지 않았다면 수석사제관을 빠뜨릴 리 없었을 것이다.) “당신을 곁에 두고, 당신을 길잡이 삼아, 서로가 서로의 길잡이가 되어 천국까지 걸어간다면 얼마나 달겠습니까. 말해 주세요, 엘리너, 더없이 사랑하는 엘리너, 우리 그 달콤한 길을 함께 걸을까요?”\n\n엘리너로서는 지금 두 사람이 밟고 있는 손 양의 그 길 말고는 어떤 길도 슬로프 씨와 함께 걸을 마음이 없었으나, 슬로프 씨가 제 소망과 포부를 늘어놓는 것을 막을 수 없었던 터라 대답하기 전에 끝까지 들어 주기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“아, 엘리너.” 그가 말을 이었다. 한번 용기를 내어 그녀의 세례명을 불러 본 이상 아무리 자주 불러도 모자란다고 여기는 모양이었다. “아, 엘리너, 주님의 도우심으로 손을 맞잡고 이 죽을 자들의 골짜기를 지나가되 주님의 자비가 그 길을 우리에게 즐겁게 하여 주시고, 마침내 저 너머에서 그분의 보좌 앞에 함께 거하게 된다면 얼마나 달겠습니까?” 그러고는 그 어느 때보다도 다정하고 경건한 눈빛이 이 연인의 눈에서 뿜어 나왔다. “아, 엘리너—”\n\n“제 이름은 볼드 부인입니다, 슬로프 씨.” 엘리너가 말했다. 그의 사랑 타령을 끝까지 들어 주기로 작정하기는 했으나, 그 불경한 말투에 넌더리가 나서 더는 견딜 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n“더없이 다정한 천사여, 그리 차갑게 굴지 마세요.” 그가 말했고, 그 말과 함께 샴페인이 터져 나왔으니, 그는 용케 팔을 둘러 그녀의 허리를 감았다. 여기까지 엘리너가 제법 성공적으로 거리를 지켜 온 것을 생각하면 상당히 솜씨 좋은 짓이었다. 두 사람은 관목에 거의 파묻힌 오솔길로 접어들어 있었고, 그러니 슬로프 씨로서는 이제 단둘이 되었으니 그토록 입에 올리던 애정을 겉으로도 얼마쯤 보여 주는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼음이 틀림없다. 어쩌면 똑같은 종류의 방책이 올리비아 프라우디에게는 잘 먹혔던 것이라고 짐작해도 좋겠다. 그야 어떻든 엘리너 볼드에게는 먹히지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 살무사에게서 뛰어 물러나듯 그에게서 뛰어 물러났으나 멀리 물러나지는 않았다. 실은 팔 길이를 벗어나지 않았다. 그러고는 생각보다도 빠르게 그 작은 손을 들어 그의 뺨을 후려쳤는데, 어찌나 시원스러운 손길이었던지 나무들 사이로 조그마한 벼락 소리처럼 울렸다.\n\n이제는 이 책장을 넘기던 교양 있는 독자마다 넌더리를 내며 책을 내려놓지 않을까 걱정스럽다. 결국 이 여주인공은 동정할 값어치가 없구나 하고 여기면서 말이다. 저 여자는 말괄량이라고 누군가는 말할 것이다. 적어도 숙녀는 아니라고 또 누군가는 소리칠 것이다. 나는 처음부터 저 여자가 미심쩍었다고 세 번째 사람은 단언할 것이다. 부인다운 품위도, 제 처지가 요구하는 각별한 몸가짐도 도무지 모르는 여자라고. 한때는 젊은 스탠호프와 어울려 뒹굴며 장난을 치더니, 다음에는 애러빈 씨에게 눈길을 보내고, 이내 세 번째 구애자와 주먹다짐에 이르렀으니, 그것도 아직 미망인이 된 지 두 해도 되지 않아서 말이다.\n\n그녀를 온전히 두둔할 수는 없다. 그러나 그녀가 말괄량이가 아니며 뒹굴며 장난치는 버릇도, 주먹을 휘두르는 성미도 없다는 것만은 단언해 두어도 좋겠다. 그녀가 슬로프 씨의 얼굴을 치지 않았더라면 참으로 좋았을 것이다. 그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 제 품위를 깎았고 스스로를 곤란하게 만들었다. 벨그레이비아에서 자랐더라면, 그 자애로운 아버지보다 좀 더 엄한 스승 밑에서 컸더라면, 남편의 다스림 아래 좀 더 오래 살았더라면 아마 이 큰 허물만은 면했을지도 모른다. 그러나 실상은 도발이 너무 심했고, 모욕을 그 자리에서 되갚고 싶은 유혹이 너무 강했다. 그녀는 독립의 감정을 지나치게 예민하게 느끼는 사람이었으니, 젊은 여인에게는 위험한 감정이로되 그녀의 처지가 유독 그것에 빠져들도록 부추기는 감정이었다. 게다가 마신 술 때문에 여느 때보다 짙은 빛으로 물든 슬로프 씨의 얼굴이, 거짓 연민과 다정한 찡그림으로 히죽거리며 주름을 잡던 그 얼굴이 유독 그런 징벌을 불러들이는 듯싶었다. 또한 그녀는 이 사내에 대해 참으로 옳은 직감을 지니고 있었다. 이 사람은 오직 이런 식으로만 꾸짖을 수 있는 자였다. 그 작은 손에서 날아온 일격은 다른 사람이라면 사내에게서 받았을 주먹만큼이나 그에게는 모욕이었다. 그것은 곧바로 그의 자존심을 찔렀다. 그는 제 품위가 낮아지고 제 몸이 능욕당했다고 여겼다. 분한 나머지 그녀를 되받아 칠 뻔했다. 아픔조차 그에게는 큰 성가심이었고, 제 성직자 신분이 깡그리 무시당했다는 느낌이 그를 몹시 언짢게 했다.\n\n그런 사내들이 있다. 제 자존심에 흠이 나는 것을 여자에게서조차 견디지 못하는 사내들, 제 몸이 스스로에게는 어찌나 거룩한 성전인지 저를 두고 하는 농담은 신성모독이요 거친 손길은 아예 성물 훼손이 되는 사내들 말이다. 슬로프 씨가 바로 그런 사내였고, 따라서 엘리너에게 얻어맞은 따귀는 그로 말하자면 그에게 내려질 수 있는 가장 알맞은 꾸짖음이었다.\n\n그렇다 하더라도 그녀가 그 사내에게 손을 들어서는 안 되었다. 그토록 부드럽고 그토록 곱고 손끝에 닿기에 그토록 감미로우며 눈에 그토록 우아하고 그 얌전한 놀림이 그토록 사랑스러운 숙녀의 손은 사내의 얼굴을 두들기라고 만들어진 것이 아니다. 일을 저지른 순간 엘리너는 자기가 온갖 예법에 죄를 지었다고 느꼈고, 그 일격을 도로 거두어들일 수만 있다면 작은 세상 하나쯤은 내주었을 것이다. 처음의 애타는 뉘우침 속에서 그녀는 하마터면 그 사내에게 용서를 빌 뻔했다. 그러나 뒤이어 밀려온 충동, 그리고 그녀가 따른 충동은 달아나는 것이었다.\n\n“저는 다시는, 다시는 당신께 한마디도 하지 않겠어요.” 그녀가 격한 감정과, 힘을 쓴 데다 감정이 사나워진 탓에 가빠진 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. 그러고는 땅을 박차고 오솔길을 따라 집 쪽으로 재빠르게 달려갔다.\n\n그러나 슬로프 씨의 신적인 진노를 내 어찌 노래하며, 주교의 전속 사제의 천상 같은 가슴을 부풀린 그 분노를 그려 내고자 비극의 뮤즈를 어찌 부르랴? 그런 일은 근대 소설의 굽 낮은 비극 장화에는 도무지 어울리지 않는다. 화가는 제 몸을 바친 딸의 이른 운명 앞에서 아버지가 느낀 슬픔을 그리라는 청을 받았을 때 아가멤논의 얼굴에 너울을 덮었다. 신은 거역하는 바람들을 벌하기로 마음먹었을 때 빈 위협을 입에 올리지 않았다. 슬로프 씨가 자기를 욕보인 여인에게 복수하리라 맹세하며 속마음이 얼마나 크게 소용돌이쳤는지 우리는 이야기하려 들지 않을 것이며, 그의 깊은 영혼의 고통을 그려 내려 헛되이 애쓰지도 않을 것이다.\n\n어쨌든 그는 저기 정원 오솔길에 홀로 서 있고, 우리는 어떻게든 그를 거기서 데리고 나와야 한다. 그는 당장 나설 마음이 없었다. 뺨은 엘리너의 손가락이 남긴 무게로 얼얼했고, 자기를 쳐다보는 사람이면 누구나 제 얼굴에서 방금 당한 일의 자취를 읽어 내리라 여겨졌다. 그는 한동안 서서 분노로 점점 더 붉어졌다. 꼼짝 않고 서서, 어찌할 바를 정하지 못한 채 눈을 부라리며, 하데스의 고통과 형벌을 떠올리고, 제 익숙한 웅변의 온갖 열정을 다해 원수를 저승의 신들에게 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 바쳐 넘길 수 있을지 궁리했다. 그의 마음은 그녀를 향해 설교하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 죄지은 사내와 여인들에게 그가 늘 복수하는 방식이 바로 그것이었다. 당장 주일 강단에 올라 제 영이 즐거워하는 그런 규탄을 그녀에게 퍼부을 수만 있었다면 가슴이 크게 후련해졌을 것이다.\n\n그러나 손 씨네 월계수를 앞에 두고 어떻게 설교하며, 지금 울라손에서 벌어지고 있는 이런 허영의 시장에서 대체 무슨 설교를 할 수 있단 말인가? 이윽고 그는 제 둘레에서 벌어지는 일들의 사악함에 의로운 역겨움을 느끼기 시작했다. 제가 그 자리에 있음으로써 그런 세속의 유혹에 인정을 보태 주었으니 마땅히 벌을 받은 것이었다. 사교의 흥겨움, 잔치의 즐거움, 젊은이들의 웃음, 나이 든 이들의 먹고 마심이 한동안 그의 눈에는 변명의 여지가 없는 것으로 비쳤다. 이렇게 이방인의 장막에 머무름으로써 그는 대체 무엇을 제 머리 위에 불러들였는가? 그는 바알의 제단 둘레에서 우상 숭배자들과 어울렸으니, 그리하여 쓰라린 형벌이 그에게 임한 것이었다. 이어 그는 시뇨라 네로니를 떠올렸고, 그의 속 영혼은 슬픔으로 가득했다. 그는 제가 사악하고 죄 많은 사람이라는 낌새를, 참으로 옳은 낌새를 어렴풋이 느끼고 있었으나 그것이 그를 바른 길로 이끌지는 못했다. 그의 마음속에는 사랑이 들어설 자리가 없었다. 그는 자기가 낮아지고 있음을 느꼈고, 그것을 떨쳐 버리기를, 등자를 딛고 일어서기를, 높은 자리와 큰 권세에 올라 웅장한 강단에 서서 볼드 부인을 규탄하는 설교를 세상에 우렁차게 외치기를 갈망했다.\n\n그는 자갈 위에 붙박인 듯 10분쯤 서 있었다. 그나마 운이 따라 주어 캐묻는 눈길이 그의 비참을 들여다보러 오지는 않았다. 이윽고 온몸에 전율이 지나갔고, 그는 마음을 추스른 뒤 천천히 돌아 잔디밭 쪽으로 길을 잡았는데, 엘리너가 간 쪽으로 되돌아가지 않고 오솔길을 따라 나아갔다. 천막에 이르렀을 때 그는 주교가 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 나누며 서 있는 것을 보았다. 주교 각하는 연설의 노고를 치른 뒤 바람을 쐬러 나와 있었던 것이다.\n\n“참으로 흥겹습니다, 참으로 흥겹지 않습니까, 주교님?” 슬로프 씨가 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 천막을 가리키고 말했다. “참으로 흥겹습니다. 저리 많은 사람이 저토록 흠뻑 즐거워하는 것을 보니 기쁩니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 주교를 몰아붙여 그윈 박사에게 자기를 소개하게 할 수 있으리라 생각했다. 아주 위대한 본보기 한 분이 여러 사람에게 여러 모양이 되는 지혜를 선포하고 몸소 행하신 바 있으니, 슬로프 씨도 그것을 따르고 싶었던 것이다. 기회를 놓치지 않는다는 것이 그의 좌우명이었다. 그러나 주교는 지금 이 순간 슬로프 씨가 성직자 형제들 사이에서 아는 이를 늘리는 데 그다지 열의가 없었다. 제 전속 사제를 눈에 띄게 언급하는 일을 삼갈 나름의 사정이 있었던 터라, 이 자리에서는 어깨를 제법 쌀쌀하게 굴렸다.\n\n“참으로, 참으로.” 그는 돌아보지도 않고, 슬로프 씨에게 눈길 한 번 주는 수고조차 하지 않은 채 말했다. “그러니 그윈 박사, 나는 대학 주간 평의회가 지금과 다름없이 넓고 두루 미치는 권한을 행사하게 되리라고 정말로 생각하오. 나로 말하자면, 그윈 박사—”\n\n“그윈 박사님.” 슬로프 씨가 모자를 들어 올리며 말했다. 바체스터 주교 따위의 하잘것없는 얼간이에게 허를 찔릴 수는 없다고 작정한 것이었다.\n\n라자러스 학장도 모자를 들어 올리며 슬로프 씨에게 아주 정중하게 인사했다. 여왕의 영토를 통틀어 라자러스 학장보다 더 예의 바른 신사는 없다.\n\n“주교님.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “부디 그윈 박사께 저를 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주십시오. 이런 기회는 제게 너무나 유리한 것이라 놓칠 수가 없습니다.”\n\n주교로서는 어찌할 도리가 없었다. “내 전속 사제요, 그윈 박사.” 그가 말했다. “지금의 내 전속 사제, 슬로프 씨요.” 그는 분명 그 소개를 전속 사제에게 되도록 흡족하지 않게 해 주었으니, ‘지금의’라는 말을 씀으로써 슬로프 씨가 현재 누리는 영예를 아마 오래 누리지는 못하리라는 뜻을 내비친 셈이었다. 그러나 슬로프 씨는 그런 것에 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그는 그 빈정거림을 알아들었으되 무시했다. 주교가 그를 전속 사제 자리에서 내칠 형편이 되기도 전에 그가 먼저 그 자리를 내놓을 형편이 될 공산이 컸다. 장차 바체스터 수석 사제가 될 사람이 주교를, 또는 주교의 부인을 무엇 때문에 마음에 두겠는가? 슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 박사의 마차에 오르려던 참에 『주피터』의 톰 타워스에게서 더없이 중요한 쪽지를 받지 않았던가? 바로 이 순간 그 쪽지가 제 주머니 속에 들어 있지 않은가?\n\n그리하여 주교를 개의치 않고 그는 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 트기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 갑자기 이야기가 끊겼는데, 슬로프 씨에게 아주 달갑지 않은 것만은 아니었다. 주교의 하인 하나가 길고 엄숙한 얼굴로 주인의 어깨께로 다가와 주교의 귀에 대고 속삭였던 것이다.\n\n“무슨 일인가, 존?” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“수석 사제님 일입니다, 주교님. 돌아가셨습니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 더 이상 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 없었고, 아주 이내 바체스터로 돌아가는 길에 올라 있었다.\n\n엘리너는, 앞서 말한 대로, 다시는 슬로프 씨와 말을 섞지 않겠다고 선언하고서 서둘러 집 쪽으로 달려갔다. 그러나 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하니 몹시 마음이 아팠고, 끝내 눈물이 터지는 것을 참을 수 없었다. 그날의 멜로드라마에서 그녀가 제2막을 연기한 것은 이러했다.\n\n# 제41장. 볼드 부인이 벗 스탠호프 양에게 근심을 털어놓다\n\n볼드 부인은 오솔길 끝에 이르러 잔디밭을 마주하고서야 이제 어떻게 해야 하나 생각하기 시작했다. 슬로프 씨가 뒤따라와 붙잡을 때까지 여기 서 있어야 하는가, 아니면 눈에는 눈물이 어리고 얼굴에는 노기가 서린 채로 사람들 틈에 들어가야 하는가? 사실을 말하자면 그 자리에 아무리 오래 서 있었더라도 슬로프 씨에게 당장 또 시달릴 염려는 딱히 없었으나, 우리는 누구나 저를 겁주는 도깨비를 실제보다 크게 부풀려 보게 마련이다. 이렇듯 겁에 질린 상태에서 그는 저 사람이 무슨 흉악한 짓을 감히 저지를지 알 수 없었다. 일주일 전에 누가 그에게, 저 사람이 손 양의 이 연회에서 당신 허리에 팔을 두르리라고 말했더라면 그는 도무지 믿지 않았을 것이다. 저 사람이 다음 일요일에 주홍색 상의에 장화 차림으로 하이 스트리트를 걸어 내려가는 모습이 목격되리라는 말을 들었더라도, 그런 괴변이 그보다 더 있을 법하지 않다고 여기지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그 있을 법하지 않은 악행을 슬로프 씨는 기어이 저질렀고, 이제 그 사람에 대해서라면 무엇이든 못 믿을 것이 없었다. 우선 그가 술에 취했다는 것은 아주 명백했다. 다음으로 그의 신앙이라는 것이 죄다 순전한 위선임은 이미 입증된 것으로 쳐야 했다. 그리고 끝으로 그 사람은 염치라고는 조금도 없는 자였다. 그리하여 볼드 부인은 그자가 덤불 사이에서 불쑥 튀어나오지나 않을까 하는 두려움이 없지 않은 채로, 그 발소리가 들려오지는 않는지 살피며 서 있었다.\n\n그렇게 서 있다가 그는 조금 떨어진 곳에서 샬럿 스탠호프가 빠른 걸음으로 잔디를 가로질러 오는 것을 보았다. 엘리너는 손에 손수건을 쥐고 있었고, 그것을 얼굴에 갖다 대어 눈물을 가리면서 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 벗에게 갔다.\n\n“오, 샬럿.” 숨이 차서 또렷이 말하기도 어려운 채로 그가 말했다. “당신을 찾아냈으니 정말 다행이에요.”\n\n“나를 찾아냈으니 다행이라고요!” 샬럿이 웃으며 말했다. “그거 참 좋은 농담이네요. 아니, 버티와 내가 사방으로 당신을 찾아다녔는걸요. 그 애는 당신이 슬로프 씨와 달아나 버렸다고 장담하면서 지금 막 목이라도 맬 참이라니까요.”\n\n“오, 샬럿, 그런 말 마세요.” 볼드 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아니, 대체 무슨 일이에요?” 스탠호프 양은 엘리너의 손이 제 팔 위에서 떨리는 것을 느끼고, 또 그 벗이 아직도 눈물에 반쯤 목이 메어 있음을 알아채고서 말했다. “어머나 세상에! 무슨 일로 이렇게 상심했군요. 무슨 일이에요? 내가 뭘 해 드리면 될까요?”\n\n엘리너는 목구멍에서 발작처럼 새어 나오는 흐느낌 소리로만 대답했다. 흔히들 말하듯 그는 몹시 상해 있었고, 그 순간에는 도무지 마음을 추스를 수가 없었다.\n\n“이리로 와요, 볼드 부인. 이쪽으로 오면 아무도 우리를 못 봐요. 무슨 일이 있었기에 이토록 속이 상했어요? 내가 뭘 해 드릴까요? 버티가 뭐라도 할 수 있을까요?”\n\n“아, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “어쩔 수 있는 일이 아니에요. 다만 그 끔찍한 사람이—”\n\n“끔찍한 사람이라니, 누구요?” 샬럿이 물었다.\n\n살다 보면 남자든 여자든 속을 털어놓지 않고는 못 배기겠는 순간이 있다. 그럴 때 털어놓지 않으려면 불쾌한 결심이 있어야 하고 또한 불쾌한 의심도 있어야 한다. 남녀를 통틀어 결코 속을 털어놓는 법이 없는 사람들, 순간의 사정에 이끌려 제 비밀을 내놓는 일이 결코 없는 사람들도 있기는 하다. 그러나 그런 이들은 대개 둔하고 닫혀 있고 정열이 없는 영혼, “차고 어두운 굴속에 사는 침침한 땅난쟁이들”이다. 엘리너에게는 그런 땅난쟁이 구석이라고는 없었고, 그래서 그는 슬로프 씨에 관한 이야기를 죄다 샬럿 스탠호프에게 털어놓기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“그 끔찍한 사람, 그 슬로프 씨 말이에요.” 그가 말했다. “그 사람이 나를 따라 식당에서 나오는 걸 못 봤어요?”\n\n“물론 봤죠. 딱하다고 여기기도 했고요. 하지만 나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었어요. 당신이 성가셔하리라는 건 알았죠. 하지만 당신과 버티가 둘이서 일을 서투르게 처리했어요.”\n\n“그건 그이 잘못도 내 잘못도 아니에요. 내가 그 사람과 한 마차를 타고 오는 걸 얼마나 싫어했는지 당신도 알잖아요.”\n\n“그 일이 이런 결과를 불렀다면 정말이지 미안해요.”\n\n“무엇이 이런 결과를 불렀는지는 나도 모르겠어요.” 다시 울음이 터질 듯한 목소리로 엘리너가 말했다. “하지만 내 잘못은 아니었어요.”\n\n“그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거예요?”\n\n“그 사람은 밉살스럽고 끔찍하고 위선적인 인간이에요. 주교님께 죄다 일러바쳐도 싼 사람이라고요.”\n\n“내 말을 믿어요. 그 사람에게 해를 입히고 싶거든 프라우디 부인에게 이르는 편이 훨씬 나아요. 그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거예요, 볼드 부인?”\n\n“으!” 엘리너가 소리쳤다.\n\n“하긴, 그리 좋은 사람이 아니라는 건 나도 인정해요.” 샬럿 스탠호프가 말했다.\n\n“좋은 사람이라니요!” 엘리너가 말했다. “내가 여태 본 중에 가장 역겹고 알랑거리고 밉살스러운 인간이에요. 무슨 염치로 나한테 온단 말이에요? 나는 그 사람에게 티끌만 한 곁도 준 적이 없어요. 남들이 그 사람을 헐뜯을 때 내가 편을 들어 준 적은 있어도, 나는 늘 그 사람이 싫었단 말이에요.”\n\n“바로 거기예요. 그 사람이 그 말을 듣고서, 당연히 당신이 자기를 사랑하는 줄로 여긴 거죠.”\n\n이 말은 엘리너에게 쓸개즙 같았다. 그것이야말로 지난 한 달 내내 그의 벗들이 죄다 해 온 말이었고, 이제 경험이 그 말이 옳았음을 증명해 보인 것이다. 엘리너는 다시는 어떤 남자의 편도 들지 않겠다고 속으로 다짐했다. 세상이 그 온갖 간악함과 온갖 심술궂음을 안고 제멋대로 굴러가든 말든, 그는 다시는 굽은 것을 바로잡으려 들지 않으리라.\n\n“그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했어요?” 샬럿이 말했다. 그는 사실 이 이야기에 자못 흥미가 동해 있었다.\n\n“그 사람이—그 사람이—그 사람이—”\n\n“자, 어서요. 그리 끔찍한 일이었을 리는 없어요. 그 사람은 취하지도 않았는걸요.”\n\n“아, 나는 분명히 취했다고 봐요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “틀림없이 취해 있었을 거예요.”\n\n“글쎄, 나는 그런 낌새를 못 봤는걸요. 그래서 무슨 일이었어요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 나로서는 도저히 말 못 하겠어요. 그 사람이 늘어놓은 그런 끔찍한 소리는 당신도 들어 본 적 없을 거예요. 신앙이니 천국이니 사랑이니 하는 소리를요. 아이참, 정말 고약한 사람이에요.”\n\n“그 사람이 무슨 소리를 늘어놓았을지는 쉽게 짐작이 가요. 그래서, 그다음엔요?”\n\n“그러고는—나를 붙잡았어요.”\n\n“붙잡았다고요?”\n\n“네—어느새 바짝 다가와서는 나를 붙잡았어요—”\n\n“허리를요?”\n\n“네.” 엘리너가 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n“그래서요—”\n\n“그래서 나는 그 사람에게서 펄쩍 물러나 뺨을 한 대 올려붙이고, 길을 따라 달아나다가 당신을 본 거예요.”\n\n“하하하!” 샬럿 스탠호프는 그 비극의 대단원에 마음껏 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 슬로프 씨가 따귀를 얻어맞았구나 생각하니 그로서는 유쾌하기 짝이 없었다. 그 만남의 결말에 벗이 그토록 괴로워하는 심정을 그는 그리 깊이 헤아리지 못했다. 그가 보기에 이 일은 미망인 쪽으로 보자면 충분히 흡족하게 끝난 셈이었고, 실상 그 미망인은 벗들 사이에서 얼마간 개선의 대접을 받을 자격이 있었다. 반면 슬로프 씨에게는 그런 사건 뒤에 으레 따라붙게 마련인 온갖 비웃음과 놀림이 돌아갈 터였다. 그의 벗들은 미망인만 보면 귀가 얼얼하지 않으냐고 물을 것이고, 아름다운 것은 보라고 있는 것이지 만지라고 있는 것이 아니라는 훈계를 들을 것이다.\n\n그런 일들에 대한 샬럿 스탠호프의 생각은 이러했으나, 그는 그것을 지금 이 자리에서 볼드 부인에게 또렷이 설명하지는 않았다. 그의 목적은 벗의 마음을 제게로 끌어당기는 데 있었으므로, 한바탕 웃고 난 뒤에는 기꺼이 동정을 내놓을 채비가 되어 있었다. 버티가 무언가 할 수 있지 않을까? 버티를 시켜 그 사람에게 앞으로는 좀 더 예의를 갖추라고 경고하게 할까? 실로 버티야말로 볼드 부인이 어떤 모욕을 당했는지 들으면 누구보다도 화를 낼 것이라고 그는 단언했다.\n\n“하지만 그이에게 말하지는 않을 거죠?” 볼드 부인이 질겁한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“당신이 싫다면 안 하죠.” 샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 이것저것 헤아려 보면 나로서는 꼭 말하기를 권하고 싶어요. 당신에게 오라비가 있다면야 그럴 필요도 없겠지요. 하지만 슬로프 씨로서는 당신 곁에 당신을 지켜 줄 마음도 힘도 있는 사람이 있다는 걸 아는 편이 아주 마땅해요.”\n\n“하지만 우리 아버지가 여기 계신걸요.”\n\n“그렇지요. 하지만 성직자끼리 다투는 건 여간 거북한 일이 아니에요. 게다가 지금 당장 아버님이 처하신 형편으로 보면, 아버님과 슬로프 씨 사이에 불편한 일이 생기는 건 아주 이롭지 못해요. 당신과 버티는 그 애더러 당신 편을 들라고 해도 될 만큼은 가까운 사이잖아요.”\n\n샬럿 스탠호프는 제 동생이 바로 그날 당장 장래의 아내와 일을 매듭짓기를 몹시 바라고 있었다. 동생과 아버지 사이, 그리고 동생과 빚쟁이들 사이의 일이 이제 그 지경에 이른 터라, 그는 그렇게 하든지 아니면 바체스터를 떠나든지 해야 했다. 그렇게 하든지, 아니면 씻지 않는 패거리와 지저분한 하숙과 궁핍한 살림이 기다리는 카라라로 돌아가든지 해야 했다. 스스로 수입을 마련하지 못하면 카라라로 가거나, 아니면 —— 로 가야 했다. 참사회원인 아버지가 그렇게 말로 못 박은 것은 아니었으나, 설령 말로 했더라도 그보다 더 분명히 뜻을 전할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n사정이 이러했으니 더는 시간을 허비해서는 안 되었다. 샬럿은 동생이 볼드 부인을 따라 방을 나서지 않고 굼뜨게 구는 꼴을 좀처럼 억누르기 어려운 노기를 품고 지켜본 터였다. 슬로프 씨가 동생을 그토록 앞질렀다는 것은 생각만 해도 통탄할 노릇이었다. 샬럿은 제 몫의 일은 충분히 솜씨 있게 해냈다고 여겼다. 그는 둘을 맺어 주었고, 동생이 이 일로 어떤 수고도 애도 들일 필요가 없을 만큼 두 사람을 가깝게 만들어 놓았다. 게다가 볼드 부인이 버티를 몹시 마음에 들어 한다는 것도 아주 뚜렷했다. 그리고 이제는 동생이 경쟁자인 슬로프 씨를 겁낼 것 없다는 사실 또한 뚜렷해졌다.\n\n볼드 부인에게 같은 날 두 번째 청혼을 받게 하는 데에는 물론 어색한 구석이 있었다. 한 주쯤 미룰 여유가 있었다면 아마 그러는 편이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 사정이란 우리가 바라는 대로 다룰 수 없을 만큼 성급할 때가 잦은 법이고, 지금이 바로 그러했다. 그렇다면 이 슬로프 씨 사건을 되레 이롭게 돌려쓸 수는 없을까? 이것을 구실로 삼아 버티와 볼드 부인을 한층 더 가까이 붙여 놓을 수는, 서로의 품에 뛰어들지 않고는 못 배길 만큼 바싹 붙여 놓을 수는 없을까? 스탠호프 양이 그 자리에서 즉각 벌이기로 작정한 노름은 바로 이런 것이었다.\n\n그리고 그는 그 노름을 아주 잘 해냈다. 우선 슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 집안의 마차로 바체스터에 돌아가지 못하도록 정해졌다. 마침 슬로프 씨는 이미 떠나고 없었으나, 물론 그들은 그런 줄을 알지 못했다. 시뇨라를 구슬려 하인들과 제 언니만 데리고 먼저 가게 하고, 두 번째 편에 버티가 슬로프 씨의 자리를 차지하기로 했다. 버티에게는 이 일의 전말을 은밀히 일러 두고, 첫 짐을 싣고 마차가 떠나고 나면 엘리너를 버티의 각별한 보호 아래 남겨 슬로프 씨가 더는 침범하지 못하도록 하기로 했다. 마차를 채비하는 동안 버티는 그 신사를 찾아내어, 바체스터로 돌아갈 다른 탈것을 스스로 마련해야 한다는 것을 알아듣게 하기로 했다. 그러니 두 사람이 당장 할 일은 함께 거닐며 버티를 찾는 것이어야 했다. 요컨대 버티는 그들이 지금의 곤경에서 타고 날아오를 페가수스인 셈이었다.\n\n이 모든 것에는 미망인의 마음을 매우 누그러뜨리는 따뜻한 우정과 살가운 친절이 담겨 있었다. 그러나 그는 거기에 몸을 맡기면서도 그렇게 하는 것이 썩 마음 편하지는 않았다. 용 하나를 잡고 나니 또 다른 용이 제 앞길에 솟아오르려 한다는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 보호자로 내세운 사람에게서 또 하나의 구혼자를 만나게 되리라고는 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 다만 그는 젊은 스탠호프의 손에 저를 그토록 많이 내맡긴다는 것이 그리 내키지 않았다. 보호가 필요하다면 아버지에게 가야 한다고 그는 느꼈다. 아버지에게 바체스터로 돌아갈 마차를 마련해 달라고 청해야 한다고 느꼈다. 클랜탠트럼 부인이라면 자리 하나쯤 내주리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 말하자면 어제부터 사귄 것이나 다름없는 벗들에게 저를 온통 내던져서는 안 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그러나 샬럿 스탠호프처럼 자매같이 다정하고, 그토록 열심히 호의를 베풀고, 그토록 마음 편히 동정해 주는 사람에게 그는 아니라고 말할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 제게 건네진 모든 제안에 몸을 맡겼다.\n\n두 사람은 저희 투사를 찾아 먼저 식당으로 갔다가, 거기서 응접실로 갔다. 거기서 그들은 애러빈 씨가 여전히 시뇨라의 소파 위로 몸을 기울이고 있는 것을, 아니 그보다는 그 부인이 제 환자이기라도 한 양 의사가 앉듯 그 머리맡에 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다. 방에는 다른 사람이 아무도 없었다. 손님들은 더러는 천막에, 더러는 아직 식당에, 더러는 활쏘기 자리에 있었으나, 대부분은 손 양을 따라 정원을 거닐며 벌어지고 있는 놀이들을 구경하는 중이었다.\n\n애러빈 씨와 그 부인 사이에 오갔고 또 오가고 있던 이야기를 낱낱이 옮길 필요는 없다. 시뇨라는 남들에게 하던 대로 그에게도 하고 있었다. 남자들을 바보로 만드는 것이 그의 사명이었고, 그는 애러빈 씨를 상대로 그 사명을 수행하는 중이었다. 그는 애러빈 씨에게서 볼드 부인을 사랑한다는 고백을 거의 받아 냈고, 뒤이어 제게 연정을 품었다는 시인까지 거의 이끌어 냈다. 가엾게도 그 사람은 제가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못했고, 제가 천국에 있는지 지옥에 있는지도 거의 의식하지 못했다. 시뇨라가 지닌 그 독특한 부류의 여성적 매력을 그는 워낙 몰랐던 터라, 그 힘에 처음으로 내맡겨지자 일종의 일시적인 착란에 걸리고 말았다. 그는 마음보다 정신을 잃었고, 술 취한 사람이 다리로 비틀거리듯 머릿속에서 생각들이 휘청거리며 이리저리 넘어졌다. 시뇨라가 그에게 속삭인 말들은 실상 아무 뜻도 없는 것이었으나, 그토록 아름다운 입술에서 나오고 그토록 빛나는 눈길이 곁들여지니 무언가 신비로운 의미를 띤 듯했고, 그는 그것을 이해하지는 못하면서도 느꼈다.\n\n이렇듯 세이렌에게 홀리면서도 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨와는 아주 다르게 처신했다. 두 남자가 서로의 대조라고, 하나는 온통 행동이요 다른 하나는 온통 사색이라고 한 시뇨라의 말은 옳았다. 슬로프 씨는 이 부인이 제 감각 위에 그 매력의 숨결을 압도적으로 끼얹자 곧바로 무언가 결실을 얻으려, 무언가 대단한 승리를 거두려 들었다. 그는 그 손을 붙잡는 것으로 시작해 거기에 입 맞추는 것으로 나아갔다. 사랑을 맹세하고 그 대가로 맹세를 요구했다. 영원한 헌신을 약속하고 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으며, 만일 그 부인이 이다산에 있었더라면 유노가 베누스의 자식을 미워할 까닭이 없었으리라고 장담했다. 그러나 애러빈 씨는 아무 맹세도 입에 올리지 않았고, 손은 대개 바지 주머니에 넣어 두었으며, 네로니 부인에게 입 맞출 생각이라고는 드 코시 백작부인에게 입 맞출 생각만큼이나 없었다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인이 방에 들어오는 것을 보자마자 얼굴을 붉히며 의자에서 일어섰다가, 다시 앉았다가, 또다시 일어섰다. 시뇨라는 그 홍조를 대번에 알아채고 가엾은 제물을 향해 미소 지었으나, 엘리너는 너무 심란한 나머지 아무것도 보지 못했다.\n\n“오, 마들린.” 샬럿이 말했다. “너한테 꼭 할 이야기가 있어. 마차 일을 정해야 하잖아.” 그러고는 몸을 굽혀 동생에게 귓속말을 했다. 애러빈 씨는 곧바로 조금 떨어진 곳으로 물러났는데, 실상 샬럿이 새로운 마차 배정을 알아듣게 하려면 설명할 것이 많았으므로 그로서는 볼드 부인과 이야기하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다.\n\n“아주 즐거운 모임이었지요.” 그가 말했다. 해가 매우 밝게 비친다거나 비가 매우 세차게 내린다고 말할 때 썼을 법한 어조였다.\n\n“네, 그렇네요.” 살면서 이보다 더 불쾌한 하루를 보낸 적이 없는 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“하딩 씨께서도 즐겁게 지내셨으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“아, 네, 아주 즐거우셨어요.” 도착하고 얼마 안 되어 헤어진 뒤로 아버지를 보지 못한 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“오늘 밤 바체스터로 돌아가시겠지요.”\n\n“네, 그러실 거예요. 아니, 그러니까 플럼스테드에 묵으시는 것 같아요.”\n\n“아, 플럼스테드에 묵으시는군요.” 애러빈 씨가 말했다.\n\n“오늘 아침에 거기서 오셨어요. 돌아가실 것 같은데, 딱히 말씀은 없으셨어요.”\n\n“그랜틀리 부인도 안녕하시겠지요.”\n\n“아주 잘 지내시는 것 같았어요. 여기 와 계세요. 아니, 벌써 가시지 않았다면요.”\n\n“아, 네, 그렇지요. 나도 이야기를 나눴어요. 정말 좋아 보이시더군요.” 그러고는 한참 동안 말이 끊겼다. 샬럿이 마들린에게 어째서 버티도 없이 서둘러 먼저 돌아가야 하는지를 대번에 납득시키지 못한 까닭이었다.\n\n“볼드 부인, 플럼스테드로 돌아가시나요?” 애러빈 씨는 그저 말을 잇느라 이렇게 물었을 뿐이었으나, 제가 위험한 땅에 다가서고 있음을 곧바로 알아차렸다.\n\n“아니요.” 볼드 부인이 아주 조용히 말했다. “바체스터의 집으로 돌아가요.”\n\n“아, 아, 네. 돌아오신 걸 깜빡했군요.” 그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 더 할 말을 찾지 못한 채 샬럿이 계획을 다 짤 때까지 말없이 서 있었고, 볼드 부인 또한 제 반지들을 매만지는 데 골똘히 빠진 듯한 모습으로 마찬가지로 말없이 서 있었다.\n\n그런데도 이 두 사람은 서로에게 흠뻑 빠져 있었다. 한 사람은 중년의 성직자였고 다른 한 사람은 아무리 보아도 밍밍한 빵과 버터 같은 시절은 지난 부인이었건만, 둘은 나이를 합쳐도 애러빈 씨가 이미 이른 나이에 미치지 못할 어떤 다몬과 필리스 못지않게 제 마음을 서로에게 말할 줄 몰랐다.\n\n마들린 네로니는 언니의 제안에 동의했고, 그러고 나서 두 부인은 다시 버티 스탠호프를 찾아 나섰다.\n\n# 제42장. 울라손 운동회 — 제3막\n\n이제 손 양의 손님들은 하나둘 자리를 뜨기 시작했고, 남은 이들의 흥도 시들해져 갔다. 날이 저물어 가고 있었고, 낮 나들이옷 차림의 부인들은 촛불 아래에 모습을 드러낼 요량이라면 매무새를 고쳐야 하리라 생각하는 중이었다. 몇몇 젊은 신사가 목소리를 하도 높여 떠드는 통에 조심성 있는 어머니들은 슬기롭게 물러나기로 마음을 정했고, 술을 절제한 축에 드는 신중한 남자들은 이제 더 할 일이 별로 남지 않았다고 느꼈다.\n\n낮 연회란 대체로 실패하기 마련이다. 사람들은 거기서 우아하게 빠져나오는 법을 도무지 알지 못한다. 섬이나 산이나 숲에서 벌이는 소풍이라면 혹 봐줄 만하다. 산에는 속으로는 당신이 떠나 주기를 간절히 바라면서도 예의상 더 머무르라 청해야 하는 주인이 없기 때문이다. 그러나 남의 집이나 남의 정원에서 열리는 낮 연회는 지겨운 노릇이다. 어정쩡한 시각에 먹고 마시기를 강요당한다. 쓸모 있는 낮을 통째로 내주고 나면, 정작 쓸모없는 저녁에는 아무 방도가 남지 않는다. 지친 몸에 désoeuvré 한 기분으로 집에 돌아오지만 잠자리에 들기에는 아직 이른 시각이다. 편안하게 기댈 만한 것이 아무것도 없다. 이 점잖은 시대에 카드놀이는 금기시되는 것 가운데 하나요, 휘스트 한 판은 어림도 없는 일이다.\n\n이제 이 모든 것이 느껴지기 시작했다. 젊은 축 가운데 더러는 저녁에 춤판이라도 벌어질까 하는 기대를 품고 왔던 터라 그런 희망이 완전히 사라지기 전에는 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았다. 또 더러는 예상보다 오래 머무르게 될까 저어하여 일찌감치 마차를 대령시켜 놓고, 하인과 말을 염려하며 어떻게든 떠나려고 애를 썼다. 백작부인과 그 고귀한 자식들은 가장 먼저 떠난 축에 들었는데, 아너러블 조지로 말하자면 그럴 때가 되고도 남았다. 백작부인은 몹시 초조하고 안달이 나 있었다. 그 끔찍한 길들은, 불행히도 캄캄한 밤에 그 길에 갇히기라도 하면 틀림없이 자기 목숨을 앗아 가고야 말리라는 것이었다. 마차 등이 좋다는 말은 들었지만, 동부 바셋셔의 길이 흔들어 대는 데야 어떤 등인들 견디겠는가. 드 코시 가문의 영지는 이 주의 서부에 있었다.\n\n백작부인이 떠난 마당에 프라우디 부인이 남아 있을 수는 없었다. 그리하여 그레이 목사와 그린 목사가 주교를 찾아 나섰고, 천막 한구석에서 주간 평의회를 논하며 한껏 즐거워하고 있는 그를 찾아냈다. 그러나 그는 옥스퍼드에서 그윈 박사의 권위가 조금도 손상되지 않게 하겠노라 약속하던 문장을 채 맺지도 못한 채 부인의 분부에 따랐고, 주교의 말들은 주교관 마구간 쪽으로 코를 돌렸다. 이어서 그랜틀리 내외가 떠났다. 떠나기 전에 하딩 씨는 딸의 귀에 한마디를 속삭일 짬을 냈다. 물론 슬로프 씨에 관한 그 어리석은 소문에 대해서는 그랜틀리 내외의 오해를 풀어 주겠노라고 그는 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “제발 그러지 마세요 — 부디 제가 뵐 때까지 기다려 주세요. 하루 이틀이면 집에 오실 테니, 그때 제가 아버지께 모든 것을 설명해 드릴게요.”\n\n“나는 내일이면 집에 돌아간단다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“정말 잘됐어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “저희 집에 오셔서 저녁을 드세요. 그러면 우리 참 편안하게 지낼 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n하딩 씨는 그러겠노라 약속했다. 그는 무엇을 설명해야 한다는 것인지, 어째서 그랜틀리 박사가 빠져 있는 오해를 풀어 주면 안 된다는 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으나 그래도 약속했다. 그는 딸에게 갚아야 할 빚이 얼마간 있었고, 순순히 따르는 것이 그것을 갚는 가장 좋은 길이라고 생각했다.\n\n그렇게 사람들이 차츰 빠져나가는 동안 샬럿과 엘리너는 버티를 찾아 이리저리 거닐었다. 그의 목소리가 마침 들려오지 않았더라면 두 사람의 수색은 꽤 길어졌을 것이다. 그는 하하 도랑 안에 편안히 자리 잡고 비스듬한 벽에 등을 기댄 채 시가를 피우면서, 전에 한 번도 만난 적 없는, 이 주 저편에서 온 어떤 젊은이와 열심히 이야기를 나누는 중이었다. 그 젊은이 역시 버티의 가르침을 받아 담배를 피우면서, 동방 나라들의 여러 소일거리에 관해 동행이 들려주는 이야기에 귀를 활짝 열고 듣고 있었다.\n\n“버티, 온 데를 다 뒤지며 널 찾고 있잖아.” 샬럿이 말했다. “당장 이리로 올라와.”\n\n버티는 하하 도랑 밖으로 고개를 들어 두 부인이 앞에 서 있는 것을 보았다. 따르는 수밖에 없었으므로 그는 일어나 시가를 던져 버렸다. 그는 엘리너 볼드와 알게 된 첫 순간부터 그녀가 마음에 들었다. 제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두었더라면, 그리고 그녀가 무일푼이어서 그녀와 결혼한다는 것이 애초에 말도 안 되는 일이었더라면, 그는 십중팔구 그녀에게 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠졌을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 그녀를 카라라의 대리석 공방을 보듯이, 이젤과 팔레트를 보듯이, 런던의 변호사 사무실을 보듯이 — 요컨대 먹고살 방편을 마련하라며 제 앞에 내밀어진 모든 것을 늘 보아 온 그 눈으로 보지 않을 수 없었다. 엘리너 볼드는 이제 그의 앞에 아름다운 여인이 아니라 혼인이라는 이름의 새 직업으로 나타났다. 그것은 과연 품이 별로 들지 않으면서도 수입이 보장되는 직업이었다. 그럼에도 그는 말하자면 등을 떠밀려 그리로 몰려온 것이었다. 누이는 흉상과 초상화를 이야기하듯 그에게 엘리너를 이야기했다. 버티는 돈을 싫어하지는 않았으나 그것을 벌어야 한다는 생각만은 질색이었다. 그런데 지금 그는 볼드 부인에게 남편이 되겠노라 자청함으로써 돈을 벌기 위해 그 즐거운 시가에서 불려 나온 참이었다. 게다가 그 일은 아주 수월하게 마련되어 있었으니, 그가 미망인을 찾아 나설 것도 없이 미망인 쪽에서 그를 찾아온 모양새였기 때문이다.\n\n그는 제 이야기를 듣던 젊은이에게 불쑥 터무니없는 핑계를 대고는 시가를 던져 버리고 하하 도랑의 벽을 기어올라 잔디밭의 두 부인에게 합류했다.\n\n“이리 와서 볼드 부인께 팔을 내드려.” 샬럿이 말했다. “그동안 내가 너에게 임무를 하나 맡길 테니, preux chevalier로서 즉시 수행하도록 해. 다만 네가 몸소 무릅쓸 위험은 유감스럽게도 보잘것없을 거야. 상대가 성직자니까.”\n\n버티는 곧바로 엘리너에게 팔을 내주고 그녀와 누이 사이에 서서 걸었다. 그는 외국에서 너무 오래 산 탓에 두 부인에게 양쪽 팔을 동시에 내주는 영국식 습관에는 물들지 않았다 — 말이 나왔으니 말이지만, 외국인들은 그 습관을 중혼에 가까운 것, 혹은 일종의 초기 모르몬교쯤으로 여긴다.\n\n이어 누이는 슬로프 씨의 무례한 행실에 관한 짧은 내력을 버티에게 들려주었고, 그러는 동안 엘리너의 귀는 화끈거렸다. 화끈거릴 만도 했다. 그 봉변을 굳이 입에 올려야 한다면, 어째서 하필 스탠호프 씨 같은 사람에게, 그것도 어째서 제가 듣는 자리에서 이야기해야 한단 말인가? 그녀는 자기가 잘못했다는 것을 알았고, 불행하고 풀이 죽었으나, 이 자리에서 빠져나갈 길도 제 처지를 바로잡을 길도 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. 샬럿은 될 수 있는 대로 그녀를 감싸 주어, 마치 슬로프 씨가 포도주를 한 잔 과하게 마셨을 뿐인 것처럼 그 일을 이야기했고, 물론 더 문제 삼을 것은 없겠지만 슬로프 씨를 마차에서 빼기 위한 조치는 취해야 한다고 말했다.\n\n“볼드 부인께서 그 일로 걱정하실 것 없어요.” 버티가 말했다. “슬로프 씨는 한 시간 전에 벌써 갔으니까요. 볼일이 있어서 곧장 바체스터로 출발해야 한다고 저에게 말하더군요.”\n\n“어쨌든 제 잘못을 모를 만큼 취하지는 않았군요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “자, 그럼 볼드 부인, 걱정거리 하나는 넘긴 셈이에요. 이제 부인을 이 충실한 기사에게 맡기고 저는 얼른 마들린을 데리고 나가야겠어요. 마차는 와 있겠지, 버티?”\n\n“한 시간 전부터 와서 기다리고 있는걸.”\n\n“됐네. 그럼 안녕히 계세요. 물론 차 마시러 저희 집에 오셔야 해요. 버티, 필요하면 억지로라도 부인을 모셔 오는 일은 너에게 맡길게.” 그렇게 말하고 샬럿은 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 남동생을 미망인과 단둘이 남겨 두었다.\n\n스탠호프 양이 가 버리자 엘리너는 문득, 슬로프 씨가 이미 떠났으니 스탠호프 씨를 그의 도움이 그토록 필요한 누이 마들린에게서 떼어 놓을 이유가 더는 없다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 그가 남기로 한 것은 마차에서 슬로프 씨의 자리를 미리 차지하고 앉아, 그 불쾌한 신사를 배제하는 일종의 사교적 순경 노릇을 하기 위해서였다. 그런데 슬로프 씨가 스스로 알아서 물러났으니 이제 버티가 누이와 함께 가지 못할 까닭은 조금도 없었다 — 적어도 엘리너의 눈에는 그랬고, 그녀는 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“아, 샬럿이 하자는 대로 두세요.” 그가 말했다. “누이가 그렇게 정해 놓았으니, 우리가 또 바꾸면 혼란이 끝이 없을 거예요. 샬럿은 우리 집안일을 늘 혼자서 다 정하고, 우리를 폭군처럼 부린답니다.”\n\n“그렇지만 시뇨라는요?” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“아, 시뇨라는 저 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어요. 아니, 앞으로는 저 없이 지내야 할 테고요.” 그는 바체스터의 혼사보다 카라라에서의 공부를 염두에 두고 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n“아니, 저희 곁을 떠나실 생각이신가요?” 엘리너가 물었다.\n\n버티 스탠호프는 원칙이라고는 없는 사람이라고 앞에서 말한 바 있다. 실제로 그는 그러했다. 그는 악을 저지르지 않으려고 정신을 다잡아 애쓰는 힘이 없었다. 그의 눈에는 악이 추해 보이지 않았고 덕이 아름다워 보이지도 않았다. 그는 사람으로 하여금 선을 행하게 하는 감정이라고는 하나도 갖추지 못했다. 그러나 아마 사람으로 하여금 악을 행하게 하는 감정 또한 똑같이 갖추지 못했을 것이다. 그는 상인들이 언제 돈을 받게 될지 어떨지는 조금도 헤아리지 않고 그야말로 무모하게 빚을 졌다. 그러나 남의 재물을 우려내려고 능동적으로 속임수를 꾸미지는 않았다. 누가 그에게 외상을 준다면 그것은 그 사람이 알아서 할 일이었고, 버티 스탠호프는 그 이상 마음 쓰지 않았다. 돈을 빌릴 때도 마찬가지였다. 그는 사람들에게 ‘우리 영감’을 보증인으로 대고, 그 ‘노친네’가 수입이 넉넉하다고 일러 준 다음, 편의를 봐주는 값으로 60퍼센트를 치르기로 합의했다. 이 모든 일을 그는 양심의 거리낌 없이 했다. 그러나 그는 능동적인 악행을 꾸민 적만은 결코 없었다.\n\n이 혼사라는 문제에서도 그는 볼드 부인의 손과 재산을 제 것으로 만드는 것이 마땅한 도리라는 말을 들었고, 처음에는 그 자신도 그렇게 여겼다. 그녀에 대해서는 별로 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그와 같은 처지에 놓인 남자가 돈을 보고 결혼하는 것은 흔한 일이었고, 주위 사람들이 다 하는 일을 그라고 하지 못할 까닭이 없었다. 그래서 그는 동의했다. 그러나 이제 그는 그 일을 다른 빛 아래에서 보기 시작했다. 그는 고양이가 쥐를 잡으려고 앉아 있듯 이 여인을 잡으려고 앉아 있는 셈이었다. 그는 그녀를 잡아 삼켜야 했으니, 그녀와 그녀의 아이와 그녀의 집들과 땅까지 통째로 삼켜, 아버지에게 얹혀사는 대신 그녀에게 얹혀살자는 것이었다. 여기에는 버티의 천성과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 차갑고 계산적이고 조심스러운 교활함이 있었다. 이 계책의 신중함은 그 부정함 못지않게 그의 감정에 거슬렸다.\n\n그리고 설령 성공한다 한들 그 보상은 무엇인가? 미망인 재산의 절반으로 채권자들을 달래고 나면, 그는 남은 절반으로 알뜰하게 살림을 꾸리며 바체스터에 조용히 눌러앉도록 허락받을 것이다. 그의 소임은 고인이 된 볼드 씨의 아이 요람을 흔드는 일이 될 터이고, 그가 누릴 최고의 흥분이란, 부주교가 마침내 그를 맞아들일 만큼 마음을 풀어 준다면, 플럼스테드 목사관에서 열리는 얌전한 모임 정도일 것이다.\n\n버티 스탠호프 같은 사람의 마음을 끌 만한 것이 이 계획서에는 거의 없었다. 카라라의 공방이든, 운명이 그를 위해 마련해 둔 어떤 세속의 진로든, 아니 거의 무엇이든 이것보다는 낫지 않겠는가? 여인 자체야 두말할 나위 없이 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었지만, 아무리 바랄 만한 여인이라도 환약처럼 삼켜야 한다면 구역질이 나는 법이다. 그러나 그는 누이에게 약속을 한 몸이었고, 누구와 다투든 누이와만은 다투지 않는 것이 옳았다. 누이를 잃는다면 앞으로 아버지의 지붕 아래서 그가 얻어 낼 수 있으리라 기대하던 모든 것을 잃는 셈이었다. 어머니는 그의 안녕에도 불행에도, 그가 무엇이 아쉬운지 무엇과 싸우는지에도 아랑곳하지 않는 듯했다. 아버지의 이마는 가망 없는 아들을 바라볼수록 나날이 더 어두워져 갔다. 마들린으로 말하자면 — 온 식구 가운데 그가 가장 좋아하는 가엾은 마들린은 제 앞가림만으로도 벅찼다. 아니, 어떤 일이 있어도 그는 누이에게 매달려 그 분부를 따라야 했다. 그것이 아무리 모질더라도 — 아니면 적어도 따르는 시늉이라도 해야 했다. 어떤 그럴듯한 속임수로 이 일을 헤쳐 나가, 누이 앞에서는 체면을 지키면서도 미망인을 파멸에 넘기지 않을 수는 없을까? 엘리너를 한패로 끌어들이면 어떨까? 버티 스탠호프가 구애에 나선 것은 바로 이런 심정에서였다.\n\n“그렇지만 바체스터를 떠나실 건 아니지요?” 엘리너가 물었다.\n\n“모르겠어요.” 그가 대답했다. “제가 무엇을 하게 될지 아직 저도 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 무엇이든 해야 한다는 것만은 어쨌든 분명하고요.”\n\n“직업 말씀이신가요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“네, 제 직업 말입니다. 그것을 직업이라고 부를 수 있다면 말이지요.”\n\n“직업이 아닌가요?” 엘리너가 말했다. “제가 남자라면 그림 말고는 그보다 더 하고 싶은 일이 없을 것 같아요. 그리고 제가 보기에는 그 두 가지가 다 스탠호프 씨께서 하실 수 있는 일이고요.”\n\n“네, 딱 그만큼씩 그렇지요.” 버티는 제 자신을 겨눈 얼마간의 속내 섞인 야유를 담아 말했다. 그는 어느 쪽으로도 한 푼 벌지 못하리라는 것을 마음속으로 알고 있었다.\n\n“스탠호프 씨, 저는 어째서 좀 더 애쓰지 않으시는지 늘 궁금했어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. 그녀는 함께 걷고 있는 이 남자에게 다정한 호감을 느끼고 있었다. “그런 말을 하는 게 무척 주제넘은 짓인 줄은 알지만요.”\n\n“주제넘다뇨!” 그가 말했다. “그런 게 아니라 너무 과분하게 친절하신 겁니다. 저 같은 게으른 건달에게 조금이라도 관심을 두시는 것만도 너무 과분한 친절이에요.”\n\n“게으르실지는 몰라도 건달은 아니시잖아요. 그리고 저는 정말로 관심이 있어요, 아주 큰 관심이요.” 그가 마음을 고쳐먹을 뻔할 만한 목소리로 그녀가 덧붙였다. “그리고 게으르다고 말했지만, 그건 지금 이 순간만 그러신 거라는 걸 알아요. 어째서 여기 바체스터에 자리를 잡고 꾸준히 일하실 수는 없나요?”\n\n“그래서 주교와 수석 사제와 참사회의 흉상이나 만들라고요? 아니면 크게 성공한다면 참사회원의 미망인 무덤 위에 공들인 묘비를 세우는 일감을 얻을 수도 있겠지요. 그리스식 콧날에 머리띠를 두르고 정교한 레이스 베일을 쓴 죽은 부인이, 물론 대리석 소파에 누워 있고, 그 다리 사이에서는 죽음이 기어 나와 작은 고기 굽는 포크로 제 희생자를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있는 그런 묘비 말입니다.”\n\n엘리너는 웃었다. 그러면서도 살아남은 참사회원이 값을 치러 주기만 한다면 직업인으로서 예술가의 목적은 상당 부분 이루어지는 셈이라고 생각했다.\n\n“수석 사제니 참사회니 참사회원의 미망인이니 하는 건 저야 모르겠어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “물론 오는 대로 받으셔야 하겠지요. 하지만 그런 장식이 필요한 큰 대성당이 곁에 있다는 사실은 스탠호프 씨께 유리할 수밖에 없잖아요.”\n\n“진짜 예술가라면 대성당을 장식하는 일 따위로 몸을 낮출 수는 없어요.” 버티가 말했다. 그에게는 예술의 드높고 황홀한 야심에 관한 나름의 생각이 있었으니, 실은 벌이가 시원치 않은 예술가라면 누구나 그런 생각을 품는 법이다. “조각이 건물을 돋보이게 할 것이 아니라, 건물이 조각을 돋보이게 하도록 지어져야 하는 겁니다.”\n\n“그야 예술 작품이 그럴 만큼 훌륭할 때 이야기지요. 스탠호프 씨께서 충분히 뛰어난 것을 하나 만들어 내시면, 저희 바체스터 부인들이 그것을 담을 알맞은 자리를 마련해 드리겠어요. 자, 무엇을 소재로 하실까요?”\n\n“단네커가 아리아드네를 사자 위에 앉혔듯이, 저는 볼드 부인을 조랑말 마차에 앉히겠습니다. 다만 저를 위해 모델로 앉아 주시겠다고 약속하셔야 해요.”\n\n“제 조랑말들은 너무 순해서 안 될 것 같고, 챙 넓은 제 밀짚모자는 대리석으로 옮겨 놓으면 참사회원 부인의 레이스 베일만큼 보기 좋지도 않을 거예요.”\n\n“볼드 부인께서 거기에 응해 주지 않으신다면, 저도 바체스터에서 다른 소재는 시도하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“그러면 다른 나라에 가서 운을 개척하시겠다고 마음을 굳히신 건가요?”\n\n“마음을 굳혔습니다.” 버티는 큰 결심에 이르려고 애쓰면서 천천히 의미심장하게 말했다. “이 일에 관해서는 전적으로 부인의 인도를 따르기로 마음을 굳혔어요.”\n\n“전적으로 제 인도를요?” 엘리너가 말했다. 달라진 그의 태도에 놀랐고, 또 그 태도가 그다지 마음에 들지도 않았다.\n\n“전적으로 부인의 인도를요.” 버티는 동행의 팔을 놓고 오솔길 위에서 그녀 앞에 마주 섰다. 걷다 보니 두 사람은 하필 엘리너가 참다못해 슬로프 씨의 뺨을 후려친 바로 그 자리에 이르러 있었다. 이 자리는 그녀의 평온에 유독 불길한 곳이란 말인가? 그녀가 여기서 또 한 사람의 사랑에 들뜬 구혼자를 마주해야 한다는 말인가?\n\n“제 인도를 따르시겠다면, 스탠호프 씨, 꾸준하고 끈기 있는 일에 자리를 잡고 앉으세요. 그리고 어디서 그렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지는 아버님의 뜻을 따르시고요.”\n\n“그보다 더 사려 깊은 말씀은 없겠지요, 실행할 수만 있다면요. 하지만 이제 허락하신다면, 제가 어떤 식으로 부인의 인도를 따르겠다는 것인지, 또 어째서 그런지 말씀드리겠습니다. 말씀드려도 될까요?”\n\n“저에게 무슨 하실 말씀이 있는지 정말 모르겠네요.”\n\n“그러시겠지요, 아실 리가 없습니다. 아실 수가 없는 일이니까요. 그런데 볼드 부인, 우리는 아주 좋은 벗이었지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“네, 그랬다고 생각해요.” 그녀는 그의 태도에서 그답지 않은 진지함을 알아채고 이렇게 말했다.\n\n“조금 전에 부인께서는 친절하게도 저에게 관심이 있다고 말씀하셨고, 저는 어쩌면 주제넘게도 그 말을 믿었습니다.”\n\n“거기에 주제넘을 것은 없어요. 저는 스탠호프 씨를 제 벗의 남동생으로서, 또 제 자신의 벗으로서도 그렇게 여기고 있으니까요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 저는 부인께서 그렇게 다정하게 여겨 주실 만한 사람이 못 됩니다.” 버티가 말했다. “그렇지만 맹세코 정말 고맙게 생각해요.” 그러고는 손에 쥔 이야기를 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 몰라 잠시 말을 멈추었다.\n\n그가 어려워한 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그는 동행에게 그녀의 재산을 빼앗으려고 마련된 계획을 알려야 했다. 그녀를 사랑하지 않으면서 결혼하려 했노라고, 아니면 결혼할 뜻도 없으면서 그녀를 사랑했노라고 말해야 했다. 게다가 자기 자신에 대한 용서만이 아니라 누이에 대한 용서까지 구해야 했고, 앞으로 샬럿과 이야기를 나눌 때 정식 청혼을 분명히 받았고 분명히 거절했노라고 볼드 부인이 말해 주도록 만들어야 했다.\n\n버티 스탠호프는 제 말솜씨를 그리 의심하는 편이 아니었으나, 이번만은 그 솜씨를 거의 지나칠 만큼 시험하게 되리라는 생각이 들었다. 그는 어디서 시작해야 할지도, 어디서 끝맺어야 할지도 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n이때쯤 엘리너는 다시 그의 곁에서 천천히 걷고 있었으나, 여태처럼 그의 팔을 잡지는 않은 채 버티가 무슨 말을 하려는지 몹시 골똘히 귀를 기울이고 있었다.\n\n“저는 부인의 인도를 받고 싶습니다.” 그가 말했다. “실은 이 일에서 저를 바로잡아 줄 수 있는 사람은 달리 아무도 없거든요.”\n\n“아유, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리예요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 이제 제 말을 들어 주십시오, 볼드 부인. 그리고 참으실 수 있다면 부디 저에게 화를 내지 말아 주세요.”\n\n“화라니요!” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아니요, 화를 내실 만한 까닭이 정말 있을 겁니다. 제 누이 샬럿이 부인을 얼마나 각별히 따르는지는 아시지요.”\n\n엘리너는 그렇다고 인정했다.\n\n“정말 그렇습니다. 그렇게 짧게 알고 지낸 사이에 누이가 누구를 그토록 따뜻하게 아끼는 것은 저도 처음 봤어요. 그리고 누이가 저를 얼마나 아끼는지도 아시지요?”\n\n엘리너는 이번에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 스탠호프 양의 이 쌍발총 같은 애정이 어떤 결말로 이어질지를 그의 말에서 헤아리자 뺨에 피가 확 도는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“저는 누이의 하나뿐인 남동생이니, 볼드 부인, 누이가 저를 아끼는 것은 이상할 게 없지요. 하지만 부인께서는 아직 샬럿을 모르십니다 — 우리 집안의 안녕이 얼마나 오롯이 누이 한 사람에게 매달려 있는지 모르세요. 누이가 우리 대신 살림을 꾸려 주지 않는다면 우리가 하루하루를 어떻게 넘길지 저는 모르겠습니다. 부인께서 아직 이런 것까지 다 보셨을 리는 없지요.”\n\n엘리너는 실은 그런 것을 꽤 많이 보아 온 터였다. 그러나 지금은 그렇다고 말하지 않고 그가 이야기를 계속하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n“그러니 샬럿이 우리 모두를 위해 가장 좋은 길을 찾으려고 그토록 애쓰는 것이 놀랍지는 않으실 겁니다.”\n\n엘리너는 조금도 놀랍지 않다고 말했다.\n\n“그런데 누이는 아주 어려운 판을 벌여 왔습니다, 볼드 부인 — 아주 어려운 판이지요. 가엾은 마들린의 불행한 결혼과 끔찍한 사고, 어머니의 병약함, 아버지가 영국을 떠나 계셨던 일, 그리고 마지막으로, 어쩌면 그 무엇보다 고약하게도 떠돌기만 하는 저의 게으른 성정까지, 누이에게는 거의 감당하기 벅찬 것들이었어요. 그 숱한 걱정거리 가운데 저를 세상에 자리 잡게 하는 일이 첫손에 꼽힌다 해도 이상할 게 없지요.”\n\n이번에는 엘리너도 맞장구를 치지 않았다. 그녀는 정식 청혼이 있으리라고 짐작했고, 비슷한 처지에 놓인 신사가 이토록 별난 머리말을 늘어놓은 적은 일찍이 없었으리라 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 슬로프 씨는 지나친 열정으로 그녀를 성가시게 했다. 스탠호프 씨에게서는 그런 위험을 걱정할 필요가 전혀 없다는 것이 아주 분명했다. 그를 움직이는 것은 오로지 타산이었다. 누이가 시켜서 구애하려는 것도 모자라, 그는 시작하기도 전에 그 사정을 미리 다 설명해 두는 조심성까지 부렸다. 우리가 짐작하기로는 볼드 부인의 눈에 이 일이 그렇게 비쳤을 것이다.\n\n여기까지 이야기하고 나서 버티는 들고 있던 작은 지팡이로 자갈을 쿡쿡 쑤시기 시작했다. 그는 여전히 걸음을 옮겼으나 아주 느렸고, 동행도 그 곁에서 느릿느릿 걸으면서 그가 어려워하는 듯한 그 일을 도와줄 마음은 조금도 없었다.\n\n“누이가 부인을 얼마나 좋아하는지 아신다면, 볼드 부인, 누이 머리에 어떤 계책이 떠올랐을지 짐작하지 못하시겠습니까?”\n\n“스탠호프 씨, 저는 조금 전에 제가 말씀드린 것보다 더 나은 계책은 떠올릴 수가 없네요.”\n\n“그렇지요.” 그가 다소 맥없이 말했다. “그게 최선이기는 하겠지요. 하지만 샬럿은 거기에 다른 계획을 하나 더 붙일 수 있다고 생각한답니다. 누이는 제가 부인과 결혼하기를 바라요.”\n\n천 가지 기억이 한순간에 엘리너의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 샬럿이 남동생 이야기를 꺼내며 칭찬하던 일, 끊임없이 두 사람을 한자리에 몰아넣던 일, 온갖 자잘한 친밀함을 부추기던 일, 유별난 살가움으로 엘리너를 한 식구처럼 대하기를 고집하던 일. 그 모든 것이 식구 하나의 이익을 위해 그녀의 넉넉한 수입을 확보하려고 벌인 짓이었다!\n\n이런 느낌은 젊은 마음에 처음 새겨질 때 몹시 쓰라리다. 나이 든 이들에게는 그런 음모와 계획, 애써 벌지 않고도 이 세상의 재물을 손에 넣으려는 그런 무르익은 계책, “tuum”을 “meum”으로 바꿔 놓으려는 그런 약삭빠른 시도야말로 익숙한 삶의 방식이다. 많은 이가 그렇게 살아가니, 세상에서 넉넉한 사람은 누구나 넉넉하지 못한 이들을 경계해야 마땅하다. 그런 이들에게 역겨운 것은 시도가 아니라 성공이다. 그러나 엘리너는 아직 제 돈을 위험의 원천으로 보는 법을 배우지 못했다. 굶주린 신사들에게 사냥당하기 좋은 먹잇감으로 스스로를 여기기 시작하지도 않았다. 그녀는 스탠호프 일가와 어울리는 것이 즐거웠고, 샬럿의 살가움이 무척 좋았으며, 새로 사귄 벗들 덕에 행복했다. 이제 그녀는 그 모든 친절의 까닭을 알게 되었고, 그녀의 마음은 인간 삶의 새로운 국면 앞에 열렸다.\n\n“스탠호프 양께서는,” 그녀가 도도하게 말했다. “저를 위해 크나큰 영광을 마련해 주셨군요. 그렇지만 그런 수고는 하지 않으셔도 좋았을 것을. 저는 그만한 야심이 없답니다.”\n\n“부디 누이에게 화내지 마십시오, 볼드 부인.” 그가 말했다. “저에게도 화내지 마시고요.”\n\n“스탠호프 씨께야 물론 화내지 않지요.” 그녀가 상당한 빈정거림을 담은 어조로 말했다. “스탠호프 씨께야 물론 화내지 않고말고요.”\n\n“아니요 — 누이에게도 내지 마십시오.” 그가 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n“그러면 여쭈어도 될까요, 스탠호프 씨. 어째서 이 별난 이야기를 저에게 하신 건가요? 이야기하시는 태도로 미루어 보건대 — 그러니까 — 그러니까 이 문제에서 스탠호프 씨와 누이님의 뜻이 꼭 같지는 않은 모양이니 말이에요.”\n\n“네, 같지 않습니다.”\n\n“그렇다면,” 볼드 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 자신이 굳이 받지 않아도 될 모욕을 당했다는 생각에 이제 정말로 화가 나 있었다. “그렇다면 어째서 저에게 이 모든 이야기를 하실 만한 값어치가 있었던 건가요?”\n\n“저는 한때 이런 생각을 했습니다, 볼드 부인 — 부인께서 — 부인께서 —”\n\n미망인은 이제 다시 완전히 무표정해져서 동행에게 조금의 도움도 주려 하지 않았다.\n\n“저는 한때 부인께서 어쩌면 — 어쩌면 저를 벗 이상으로 여기게 되실 수도 있으리라 생각했습니다.”\n\n“결코 그럴 일 없어요!” 볼드 부인이 말했다. “결코요. 제가 그런 생각을 부추길 만한 일을 조금이라도 스스로에게 허락한 적이 있다면 그건 제 잘못이 아주 큽니다 — 정말이지 아주 큰 잘못이고말고요.”\n\n“그러신 적 없습니다.” 버티가 말했다. 그는 제 말이 되도록 덜 언짢게 들리기를 진심으로 인정 있게 걱정하고 있었다. “그러신 적 없으시고, 저도 얼마 전부터 제게 가망이 없다는 것을 알고 있었습니다 — 다만 누이의 기대가 더 컸던 거지요. 저는 부인을 오해하지 않았습니다, 볼드 부인. 누이는 어쩌면 오해했을지 몰라도요.”\n\n“그러면 어째서 저에게 이 모든 말씀을 하신 건가요?”\n\n“누이를 화나게 해서는 안 되기 때문입니다.”\n\n“그럼 이 일은 누이님을 화나게 하지 않는다는 건가요? 정말이지, 스탠호프 씨, 저는 댁 식구들의 방침을 이해할 수가 없네요. 아, 집에 있었으면 얼마나 좋을까!” 그렇게 바람을 입 밖에 내면서 그녀는 더는 스스로를 억누르지 못하고 눈물을 왈칵 쏟아 냈다.\n\n가엾은 버티는 크게 마음이 움직였다. “돌아가실 때 마차는 부인 혼자 쓰십시오.” 그가 말했다. “적어도 부인과 제 아버지만요. 저야 걸어가면 되고, 사실 제가 어떻게 하든 대수로울 것도 없으니까요.” 엘리너의 슬픔이 얼마간은 두 번째 구혼자와 함께 마차를 타고 바체스터로 돌아가야 할 듯싶은 사정에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그는 훤히 알고 있었다.\n\n이 말에 그녀의 마음이 얼마간 누그러졌다. “아, 스탠호프 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “어째서 저를 이토록 비참하게 만드셔야 했나요? 이 모든 말씀을 하셔서 무엇을 얻으시겠어요?”\n\n그는 제 제안에서 가장 까다로운 대목을 아직 그녀에게 설명하지도 않은 터였다. 누이에게 부려 볼 작정인 그 자그마한 속임수에 그녀도 한몫 끼어야 한다는 말은 아직 하지 않았던 것이다. 이 제안은 여태 남아 있었고 반드시 필요한 것이었으므로, 그는 그것을 꺼내는 일로 넘어갔다.\n\n그의 진술을 처음부터 끝까지 따라갈 필요는 없다. 마침내, 그리고 적잖은 애를 먹은 끝에 그는 엘리너에게, 이제는 정식 청혼이라는 영광을 바칠 뜻이 없으면서도 어째서 그녀를 제 속내에 끌어들였는지를 납득시켰다. 마침내 그는 이 자그마한 집안 희극에서 그녀가 맡도록 정해진 배역을 그녀에게 이해시켰다.\n\n그러나 막상 그것을 이해하고 나자 그녀는 어느 때보다 그에게 화가 났을 뿐이었다. 그에게만이 아니라 샬럿에게도 화가 났다. 그녀의 깨끗한 이름이 두 사람 사이에서 서로 다른 뜻으로 이리저리 오갈 참이었는데, 그 뜻이 어느 쪽이나 거짓이었다. 그녀는 누이가 아버지를 상대로 써먹는 패가 되고, 다시 남동생이 누이를 상대로 써먹는 패가 될 참이었다. 그 다정한 공감과 애정을 다 갖춘 소중한 벗 샬럿은 스탠호프 집안의 안녕을 위해 그녀를 제물로 바치려 애쓰고 있었다. 그리고 버티는, 스스로 이제 밝힌 대로 빚에 목까지 잠겨 있으면서도, 제 몸을 그렇게까지 크게 희생해 가며 빚을 갚고 싶지는 않다고 인정함으로써 그 찬사를 완성했다. 그러고는 식구들에게 그가 그들의 분부를 좇아 제 몸을 그렇게 내던지려고 최선을 다했다고 믿게 하려는 목적으로, 이 내키지 않는 구혼자와 한통속이 되어 달라는 부탁까지 받은 것이다!\n\n그가 말을 마치자 그녀는 얼굴을 들었고, 눈물 너머로도 자못 위엄 있게 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“이런 말씀을 드리게 되어 유감입니다만, 스탠호프 씨, 지금까지 있었던 일을 생각하면 댁 식구들과 저 사이의 왕래는 모두 끊는 편이 좋겠어요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 그러는 편이 낫겠지요.” 버티가 천진하게 말했다. “어쨌든 한동안은 그편이 나을 겁니다. 그러면 샬럿도 부인께서 제가 한 일에 노하신 줄로 알 테고요.”\n\n“이제 괜찮으시다면 저는 집 쪽으로 돌아가겠어요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “길은 혼자서도 찾을 수 있습니다, 스탠호프 씨. 지금까지 있었던 일을 생각하면,” 그녀가 덧붙였다. “혼자 가는 편이 좋겠어요.”\n\n“하지만 부인을 위해 마차를 찾아 드려야 합니다, 볼드 부인. 그리고 아버지께 부인이 아버지와 단둘이 돌아가시게 되었다고 말씀드려야 하고, 제가 함께 가지 않는 데 대해 무슨 핑계든 대야 하고요. 또 하인더러 부인 댁 앞에 내려 드리라고 일러 두어야 합니다. 이제는 부인께서 대성당 경내에서 저희 식구를 다시 보고 싶어 하지 않으실 테니까요.”\n\n이 말에는 진실함이 있었고, 당장의 곤란을 덜어 줄 조치를 챙기는 데는 명민함이 있었으니, 그것이 엘리너의 노여움을 누그러뜨리는 데 얼마간 효과가 있었다. 그리하여 그녀는 이제 텅 빈 잔디밭을 지나 응접실 창가에 이르기까지 그의 곁에서 걷도록 스스로를 내맡겼다. 버티 스탠호프에게는 누구의 눈에나 그를, 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 어떤 남자와도 다른 자리에 세워 주는 무언가가 있었다. 엘리너가 아무리 화가 났다 한들, 화를 낼 까닭이 아무리 컸다 한들, 그녀는 다른 사람에게였다면 냈을 화의 절반도 그에게는 내지 못했다. 그는 보기에 하도 단순하고 하도 사람 좋고 하도 꾸밈없고 말 붙이기 편해서, 그녀는 그가 응접실 창가에 이르기도 전에 이미 반쯤 그를 용서해 버린 뒤였다.\n\n두 사람이 그리로 갔을 때 스탠호프 박사는 손 씨와 손 양과 함께 거의 단둘이라 할 만하게 앉아 있었다. 이런저런 사정으로 아직 떠나지 못하고 붙들려 있는 불운한 이가 한둘 더 있기는 했으나, 그 수는 시시각각 줄어들고 있었다.\n\n엘리너를 아버지에게 넘겨주자마자 버티는 마차를 찾아 정문 쪽으로 나갔고, 거기서 마차가 올 때까지 앞담에 느긋이 기대선 채 편안히 시가를 피우며 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 그가 방으로 돌아왔을 때 스탠호프 박사와 엘리너는 주인 남매와만 남아 있었다.\n\n“드디어 왔습니다, 손 양.” 그가 쾌활하게 말했다. “이제 짐을 덜어 드리러 왔어요. 볼드 부인과 제 아버지는 손 양께서 저희에게 베풀어 주신 그 더없이 즐거운 여름의 마지막 장미인데, 볼드 부인과 함께하는 자리야 늘 바랄 만한 것이라 해도, 적어도 지금만은 마지막 꽃이 나무에서 꺾여 나가는 것을 반가워하셔야 할 테니까요.”\n\n손 양은 볼드 부인과 스탠호프 박사가 아직 곁에 남아 있어 기쁘기 그지없다고 잘라 말했다. 손 씨도 같은 말을 했을 터이나, 참을 수 없는 하품에 가로막히고 말았다.\n\n“아버지, 볼드 부인께 팔을 내드리시겠어요?” 버티가 말했다. 그리하여 마지막 작별 인사가 오갔고, 참사회원이 볼드 부인을 이끌고 나가는 뒤를 그 아들이 따랐다.\n\n“저도 곧 뒤따라 집에 가겠습니다.” 두 사람이 마차에 오를 때 그가 말했다.\n\n“마차에 안 타느냐?” 아버지가 말했다.\n\n“아뇨, 아뇨. 가는 길에 만날 사람이 있어서 걸어가겠어요. 존, 볼드 부인 댁으로 먼저 가는 것을 잊지 말게.”\n\n엘리너가 창밖을 내다보니 그는 모자를 손에 든 채, 그날의 평온을 흐트러뜨린 일이라고는 아무것도 없었다는 듯 여느 때처럼 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 절을 하고 있었다. 그녀가 그를 다시 보기까지는 기나긴 세월이 흘렀다. 스탠호프 박사는 돌아가는 길에 그녀에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았고, 그녀는 마차가 대성당 경내로 들어서기 전에 존의 손으로 무사히 제 집 현관 앞에 내려졌다.\n\n이리하여 우리의 여주인공은 그날 멜로드라마의 마지막 막을 연기했다.\n\n# 제43장. 퀴버풀 씨 내외가 행복해지고 슬로프 씨는 신문의 격려를 받다\n\n울라손으로 떠나기에 앞서, 매사에 빈틈이 없는 프라우디 부인은 편지 두 통을 쓰게 했다. 한 통은 자기가 쓰고 한 통은 남편이 쓴 것으로, 둘 다 푸딩데일 목사관에 사는 이들에게 보내는 것이었으며, 그 집 화롯가를 행복으로 채워 놓았다.\n\n말들이 떠나 주교의 마부가 다른 일에 손이 비게 되자마자, 이 교구의 미천한 주민은 주교의 조랑말에 올라 두 통의 급보를 들고 출발했다. 근래 편지가 하도 많았으니 이번 것들은 우리끼리 생략하기로 하자. 주교가 쓴 것은 이튿날 아침 11시에 퀴버풀 씨가 주교를 찾아뵈어 달라는 요청에 지나지 않았고, 부인이 쓴 것 역시 퀴버풀 부인이 자기에게 그리해 달라는 요청에 지나지 않았다. 다만 그쪽은 훨씬 길고 훨씬 거창한 문투로 짜여 있었다.\n\n프라우디 부인에게 이 중대한 요양원 문제를 매듭짓는 일은 이제 양심의 문제가 되어 있었다. 그녀는 퀴버풀 씨가 그 자리를 차지해야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다. 더 이상의 의심도 지체도, 더 이상의 거절도 사임도, 자기 명령을 거슬러 슬로프 씨가 제 잇속을 챙기려 벌이는 밀약도 다시는 없어야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n“주교님,” 그 뜻깊은 날 아침, 그녀는 식사가 끝나기가 무섭게 말했다. “임명장에 서명은 하셨어요?”\n\n“아니오, 여보, 아직이오. 아직 정확히 서명이 된 것은 아니라오.”\n\n“그럼 지금 하세요.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n주교는 그대로 했고, 과연 울라손에서 더없이 즐거운 하루를 보냈다. 그리고 집에 돌아와서는 아내의 거실에서 따뜻한 니거스 한 잔을 마시며, 그 시절 유행하던 『리틀 도릿』의 최신 호를 속으로 대단히 흡족해하며 읽었다. 아아, 남편들이여, 아아, 나의 혼인한 벗들이여, 잘 순종한 아내에게서 길어 올릴 수 있는 위안이란 얼마나 큰 것인가!\n\n이 주교관 급보가 도착하자 푸딩데일에는 큰 소동과 설렘이, 드높은 기대와 되살아난 희망이 일어났다. 조랑말이 목사관 부엌문으로 총총 다가오는 발굽 소리를 놓치지 않고 잡아낸 퀴버풀 부인은 그것들을 부랴부랴 남편에게 가져갔다. 그때 그녀는 어린 새 열넷과 그 부모까지 점심때의 허기를 달래 줄 아일랜드 스튜를 젓고 있던 참이었다. 그녀는 스튜가 묻지 않도록 자기 널찍한 앞치마 자락 사이로 심부름꾼의 손에서 편지를 받아 들었고, 그 꼴 그대로 남편의 책상까지 들고 온 것이었다.\n\n두 사람은 곧바로 전리품을 나누었는데, 각자 상대에게 온 것을 집어 들었다. “퀴버풀,” 부인이 자못 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다. “내일 열한 시에 주교관에 가 있어야 한대요.”\n\n“당신도 그렇다는구려, 여보.” 남편은 소식의 무게에 숨이 막힐 지경이 되어 말했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 편지를 맞바꾸었다.\n\n“일이 제대로 되지 않았다면 나를 다시 부르실 리가 없어요.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“여보, 너무 장담하지는 마시오.” 남편이 말했다. “일이 어그러지기라도 하면 어쩌겠소.”\n\n“일이 제대로 되지 않았다면 나를 부르실 리가 없다니까요, Q.” 부인이 다시 우겼다. “그분이야 파이 껍질처럼 뻣뻣하고 딱딱하고 도도하지만, 속은 바른 분이라고 나는 봐요.” 프라우디 부인에 대한 퀴버풀 부인의 판정은 그러했고, 그녀는 뒷날에도 늘 이 판정을 굽히지 않았다. 사람이란 제 수입이 갑절이 되면, 그 자그마한 의식을 치러 준 사람은 대개 속이 바른 사람이라고 여기게 마련이다.\n\n“아, 레티!” 퀴버풀 씨가 닳고 닳은 의자에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n“아, Q.!” 퀴버풀 부인이 말했다. 그러고는 두 사람이, 부엌 앞치마도 기름투성이 손가락도 거기 들러붙은 아일랜드 스튜도 아랑곳하지 않고, 서로의 품에 뜨겁게 몸을 던졌다.\n\n“제발 부탁이니 이번엔 누구한테도 구슬려서 빼앗기지 말아요.” 아내가 말했다.\n\n“그 일은 내게 맡겨 두시오.” 남편이 사나울 만큼 단호한 낯빛으로 말하며, 마치 슬로프 씨의 머리통이 제 주먹 밑에 깔려 있고 그것을 그대로 눌러 두려는 사람처럼 책상을 뻣뻣하게 내리눌렀다.\n\n“얼마나 있으면 될까요?” 부인이 말했다.\n\n“되기는 될까 모르겠소.” 남편은 여전히 미심쩍어하며 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 너무 큰소리는 안 할래요.” 부인이 말했다. “잔이 입에 닿기 전에 두 번이나 미끄러졌으니, 이번엔 아주 엎어질지도 모르지요. 그래도 나는 그렇게는 안 믿어요. 그분이 내일 당신에게 그 자리를 주실 거예요. 두고 보세요, 꼭 그러실 테니.”\n\n“하늘이 그리 보내 주시기를.” 퀴버풀 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. 그런데 이 사람의 등에 얹힌 짐의 무게를 헤아리는 이라면 누가 그 기도를 온당치 못하다 하겠는가? 열넷이었다. 살아 있는 아이가 열넷이었다. 퀴버풀 부인이 주교 부인 앞에서 그토록 힘주어 내세웠던 그대로다. 승진이 남에서든 북에서든 동에서든 서에서든 어차피 사람의 손에서 나오는 것인 한, 우리의 온갖 시험과 심사와 detur digniori's와 낙관적인 성향에도 불구하고, 이런 청구야말로 유효한 것이 아니겠는가? 그러하기를 간절히 바랄 뿐이다. 우리가 신이 되기 전까지는 사람으로 있는 데 만족해야 한다. 서둘러 바꾸려다가 도리어 더 낮은 것으로 떨어지지 않으려면.\n\n그러고 나서 부부는 다정하게 나란히 앉아, 늘 그러했듯이 온갖 어려움을 이야기했고, 좀처럼 그럴 기회가 없었던 온갖 희망도 이야기했다.\n\n“주교관에서 나오는 길에 그 사람한테 들르는 게 좋겠어요, Q.” 퀴버풀 부인이 말하며, 그날 아침 우편배달부가 목사관에 놓고 간 바체스터 포목상의 성난 독촉장을 가리켰다. 가마우지 같은 자, 부당하고 게걸스러운 가마우지! 퀴버풀 일가가 요양원으로 가게 되었다는 소문이 처음 퍼졌을 때, 이 사내는 알랑거리는 열의로 이 가난한 성직자에게 필요하다 싶은 물건을 앞다투어 들이밀었다. 요양원 재산에서 돈을 받게 되리라 여겼고, 아이가 열넷인 데다 그 아이들을 입힐 돈까지 생긴 사람이라면 더없이 훌륭한 손님일 수밖에 없다며 혼자 흐뭇해했던 것이다. 그러다 두 번째 소문이 귀에 들어오자마자, 그는 성이 나서 대금을 청구했다.\n\n그리고 “그 열넷”은, 아니 그중에서 희망을 품고 그 희망을 논할 만큼 자란 아이들은, 저희의 황금빛 앞날을 두고 이야기꽃을 피웠다. 다 자란 큰 딸들은 바체스터에서 열릴 법한 모임이며, 옷값으로 받게 될 법한 용돈이며, 어쩌면 생길지도 모를 피아노며 — 목사관에 있는 것은 세월과 아이들의 풍파에 하도 시달려 이제 피아노라는 이름을 붙이기도 민망했다 — 그 예쁜 정원과 예쁜 집을 두고 서로 소곤거렸다. 큰 딸들이 소곤거릴 만한 것이라면 마땅히 그런 것들이었다.\n\n그리고 어린 것들은 소곤거리는 데서 그치지 않고, 우리의 정다운 전임 워든이 그토록 아끼던 느릅나무 아래에 생길 새 놀이터며, 앞으로 저마다 갖게 될 뜰이며, 그토록 바라던 도시에 가면 사게 될 구슬이며, 바체스터에 학교가 있다더라는 소문을 두고 서로 소리를 질러 댔다.\n\n조심성 많은 어머니가 아이들 가슴에는 정작 아버지 가슴에서 몰아내려 애쓰던 바로 그 감정을 심어 보려 한 것도 헛일이었고, 딸들에게 “잔이 입에 닿기까지는 미끄러질 일이 숱하다”고 되뇐 것도 헛일이었으며, 아이들에게 우리는 평생 푸딩데일에서 살 거라고 믿게 하려 한 것도 헛일이었다. 희망은 높이 솟구쳐 도무지 가라앉으려 하지 않았다. 이웃 농부들이 소식을 듣고 축하하러 찾아왔다. 애초에 불을 지핀 것은 다름 아닌 퀴버풀 부인 자신이었으니, 되살아난 기대가 처음 터져 나오던 순간에 어찌나 야무지게 지펴 놓았던지 이제는 제힘으로 도로 끌 수가 없게 되어 버린 것이다.\n\n가엾은 안주인! 착하고 정직한 안주인이여, 그대가 부르심을 받은 처지에서 흡족하지는 못할지언정 진심으로 제 본분을 다하는 이여. 불은 그대로 타게 두라. 이번만은 그 불길이 그대를 태우지 않으리라. 그것은 그대와 그대의 식구를 따뜻하게 데워 주리라. 그대의 그 남편, 그대 가슴에 품은 그 Q.가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 히램 요양원의 구호 노인들 위에 군림하도록 이미 정해진 것이다.\n\n그리고 그날 푸딩데일에서 오간 일을 모두 듣고 보았던들, 온 바체스터에서 그들의 희망을 꺾을 마음이 가장 없었을 사람은 하딩 씨였을 것이다. 저 어린 갈까마귀 한 부대의 필요에 맞세울 만한 무슨 필요가 그에게 있었겠는가? 살아 있는 아이가 열넷이다! 적어도 그에게만은 그 한 가지가 퀴버풀 씨를 임명할 충분한 논거가 되었으리라고 말해 두자.\n\n아침이 되자 Q.와 그 아내는 기대에 부푼 마음을 그대로 드러내는 정확함으로 약속 시간을 지켰다. 친절한 농부의 이륜마차를 빌려 그것을 타고 갔는데, 가는 길에 온갖 이야기를 나누었다. 두 사람은 집안 식구들에게 한 시까지는 돌아오겠다고 일러 두었고, 맏이에게는 그 익숙한 시각에 맞춰, 알뜰한 어머니가 전날 끓여 둔 큰 솥 스튜의 남은 것을 차려 놓으라고 단단히 당부해 두었다. 부엌 시계 바늘이 두 시, 세 시, 네 시를 돌아 지나서야 농부의 마차 바퀴 소리가 다시 목사관 문간에 들렸다. 돌아온 나그네들은 얼마나 두근거리는 가슴들의 맞이를 받았던가!\n\n“얘들아, 너희는 아마 엄마 아빠가 영영 안 돌아오는 줄 알았지?” 어머니가 그 튼실한 발을 천천히 내려 마차 발판에 딛으며 말했다. “아이고, 오늘 같은 날이 또 있을까!” 그러고는 큰아이의 어깨에 몸을 실은 채 다시 한번 단단한 땅을 밟았다.\n\n그 목소리의 가락 하나면 족했으니, 모든 일이 잘되었다는 것을 알리는 데는 더 필요한 것이 없었다. 아일랜드 스튜쯤이야 이제 새까맣게 타든 말든 상관없었다.\n\n이어 어찌나 입을 맞추고 얼싸안았는지, 어찌나 울고 웃었는지. 퀴버풀 씨는 도무지 가만히 앉아 있지를 못하고 이 방 저 방을 돌아다니다가, 정원으로 나갔다가, 가로수길을 따라 큰길까지 내려갔다가, 다시 아내에게로 돌아오기를 되풀이했다. 그러나 아내는 그렇게 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내지 않았다.\n\n“얘들아, 당장 일을 시작해야 한다, 그것도 단단히 마음먹고 말이야. 프라우디 부인께서 10월 15일까지는 우리가 요양원 집에 들어가 있기를 바라신다.”\n\n프라우디 부인이 이튿날 아침까지 모두 그리로 들어와 있으라고 했더라도, 딸들은 아무 불평이 없었을 것이다.\n\n“그럼 돈은 언제부터 나와요?” 맏아들이 물었다.\n\n“오늘부터란다, 얘야.” 흐뭇해진 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“야, 신난다.” 아이가 말했다.\n\n“프라우디 부인께서 그 집에 꼭 내려가 봐야 한다고 하시더구나.” 어머니가 말을 이었다. “가 본 김에 방이랑 창문을 좀 재 두면 걸음을 하나 덜겠다 싶어서, 보빈스한테 줄자 한 뭉치를 얻었지. 보빈스가 요새는 어찌나 굽실거리는지 모른다.”\n\n“나 같으면 고맙단 소리 안 할 텐데.” 작은 레티가 말했다.\n\n“얘야, 세상 이치가 다 그런 거란다. 다들 똑같이 그런다. 칠면조가 꼬르륵거린다고 화를 내 봐야 무슨 소용이겠니. 그게 그 새의 천성인걸.” 그러고는 제 살아온 경험의 결론을 아이들에게 그렇게 일러 주면서, 그녀는 주머니에서 요양원 집 방들의 길이와 너비를 재어 둔 줄자 토막들을 꺼냈다.\n\n그러니 우리는 그녀를 그 고단하고도 행복한 일거리 속에 남겨 두기로 하자.\n\n퀴버풀 내외가 주교관을 나선 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 프라우디 부인이 아직 그 일을 두고 남편에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있는데, 또 한 사람의 손님이 왔다고 알려 왔으니 그윈 박사였다. 라자러스의 학장은 프라우디 부인이 아니라 주교를 찾은 것이었으므로, 서재로 안내되어 들어가 부인이 거기 있는 것을 보고는 기뻐하기보다 오히려 놀랐다.\n\n그러나 우리는 조금 뒤로 되돌아가야 한다. 아주 조금만이다. 이 한 권의 얼마 남지 않은 지면 안에 우리의 벗들을 모조리 처리해야 한다는 데서 슬슬 난처함이 드러나기 시작하는 까닭이다. 아아, 롱맨 씨가 네 번째 권을 허락해 주었더라면! 일곱째 하늘이 그 아래 온갖 천상의 복락의 단계들을 뛰어넘듯, 그 권은 앞의 세 권을 뛰어넘었을 것을.\n\n그날 저녁 울라손에서 마차를 타고 돌아오는 길에, 그윈 박사는 적잖은 애를 쓴 끝에 벗인 부주교를 그의 취향에 맞는 것보다 훨씬 덜 호전적인 방책 쪽으로 끌어당겨 놓았다. “우리가 언짢은 낯을 드러내는 것은 볼썽사나운 일이오. 게다가 이 문제에 우리에게는 아무 권한이 없으니, 있는 척 굴어 봐야 서투른 계책일 뿐이오.” 라자러스의 학장은 이렇게 주장했다. “주교가 요양원에 다른 사람을 앉히기로 작정했다면, 으름장으로 막을 수는 없소. 게다가 부주교가 제 주교에게 으름장을 가벼이 놓을 일도 아니오. 그가 요양원에 낯선 사람을 들여앉힌다면, 우리로서는 다른 이들의 분노에 그를 맡겨 두는 수밖에 없소. 그런 처사가 결국에 가서는 장인어른께 해가 되지 않을 공산도 크오. 허락해 주신다면 내가 주교를 만나 보겠소 — 혼자서.” 이 대목에서 부주교는 눈에 띄게 움찔했다. “그렇소, 혼자서요. 그래야 내가 더 침착할 테니. 그러면 적어도 그가 이 일을 어찌할 작정인지는 알아낼 수 있을 것이오.”\n\n부주교는 씩씩거리며 숨을 몰아쉬었고, 마차 창을 올렸다가 다시 내렸으며, 제 집 대문에 이르도록 그 문제를 두고 따지다가 마침내 물러섰다. 모두가 그에게 맞서고 있었다. 제 아내도, 하딩 씨도, 그윈 박사도.\n\n“부디 그이가 곤경에 빠지지 않게 해 주세요, 그윈 박사님.” 그랜틀리 부인이 손님에게 그렇게 말해 두었던 것이다.\n\n“부인, 힘닿는 데까지 해 보겠습니다.” 정중한 학장은 그렇게 대답했다. 그가 일을 해낸 방식이 바로 그러했고, 그리하여 그는 그랜틀리 부인의 고마움을 얻었다.\n\n이제 우리는 주교의 서재로 돌아가도 좋겠다.\n\n그윈 박사가 여기서 맞닥뜨린 난처함은 그로서도 미처 내다보지 못한 것이었다. 그도 — 잉글랜드의 온 성직자 사회와 마찬가지로 — 프라우디 부인이 옷장과 저장실과 세탁장에만 머물러 있지 않는다는 소문쯤은 들어 알고 있었다. 그러나 대낮 한 시에 주교를 찾아갔다가 그가 아내와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있는 광경을 마주할 수도 있으리라고는, 설령 그렇더라도 그 아내가 인사를 하고 물러가는 데 필요한 시간 이상으로 남아 있으리라고는 여태 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그런데 지금 이 경우에는 프라우디 부인이 물러날 뜻이 조금도 없어 보였다.\n\n주교는 전날 그윈 박사가 여간 마음에 들지 않았고, 그러니 당연히 그윈 박사도 자기가 그만큼 마음에 들었으리라 여기고 있었다. 그는 이번 방문을 순전히 예를 갖춘 것으로 여겼고, 라자러스의 학장이 이 고장에 내려오자마자 이렇게 일찍 플럼스테드에서 마차를 몰아 일부러 주교관에 들러 주었으니 참으로 은혜롭고도 온당한 처사라고 생각했다. 두 사람이 정치로 보나 교리로 보나 같은 편이 아니라는 사실이 그 예를 한층 더 값지게 만들었다. 그리하여 주교는 온통 웃음이었다. 그리고 이름 뒤에 그럴듯한 직함이 붙은 사람을 좋아하는 프라우디 부인 역시 라자러스의 학장을 맞이할 마음이 아주 흡족했다.\n\n“울라손 모임은 참 즐거웠지요, 학장님, 그렇지 않던가요?” 부인이 말했다. “그랜틀리 부인은 지치지 않고 잘 돌아가셨기를 바라요.”\n\n그윈 박사는 다들 조금 고단하기는 했으나 오늘 아침에는 아무렇지도 않더라고 말했다.\n\n“손 양은 훌륭한 분이지요.” 주교가 한마디 거들었다.\n\n“그리고 본받을 만한 기독교인이라고들 하더군요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n그윈 박사는 그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘다고 밝혔다.\n\n“그 댁 안식일 학교는 아직 못 보았소만,” 부인이 말을 이었다. “머지않아 꼭 한번 가 볼 작정이오.”\n\n그윈 박사는 그 말에 그저 고개를 숙여 보였다. 그는 프라우디 부인과 그 주일학교에 관한 이야기를 그랜틀리 박사와 하딩 씨에게서 익히 들은 바 있었다.\n\n“그런데 학장님,” 부인이 말을 이었다. “내가 마차를 몰고 가서 그랜틀리 부인의 안식일 학교를 살펴보면 그이가 반가워할지 모르겠소. 아주 훌륭하게 꾸려 나간다는 말을 들었는데.”\n\n그윈 박사로서는 정말이지 뭐라 말할 수가 없었다. 프라우디 부인이 왕림해 주시는 영광을 베푸신다면 그랜틀리 부인은 어느 날이든 더없이 기꺼이 맞이할 것이라고 그는 믿어 의심치 않았다. 물론 그랜틀리 부인이 마침 집에 있기만 하다면 말이지만.\n\n부인의 이마에 옅은 구름이 드리웠다. 그녀는 제 호의가 좋게 받아들여지지 않았음을 알아차렸다. 거듭나지 못한 이 독사의 무리는 여전히 비뚤어지고 목이 뻣뻣하며 제 죄악 속에 굳어 있는 것이다. “부주교가 이런 시설에 낯을 찌푸린다는 건 나도 알고 있소.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n이 말에 그윈 박사는 슬며시 웃었다. 웃음이랄 것도 없이 미소에 지나지 않았다. 그것을 참을 수만 있다면 제 모자라도 내주었으련만, 그로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었다.\n\n프라우디 부인이 다시 이맛살을 찌푸렸다. “‘어린아이들을 용납하고 금하지 말라.’” 그녀가 말했다. “우리가 그 말씀을 기억해야 하지 않겠소, 그윈 박사? ‘삼가 이 작은 자 중의 하나도 업신여기지 말라.’ 우리가 그 말씀을 기억해야 하지 않겠소, 그윈 박사?” 그러고는 그 물음을 던질 때마다 그를 향해 으르는 집게손가락을 치켜들었다.\n\n“여부가 있겠습니까, 부인, 여부가 있겠습니까.” 학장이 말했다. “그리고 부주교 역시 그러하리라 확신합니다. 주일에만이 아니라 주중에도 말입니다.”\n\n“주중에는 그 아이들을 업신여기지 않으려야 않을 수가 없소.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그때는 아이들이 들에 나가 있으니까. 주중에 아이들은 제 부모의 것이지만, 주일에는 성직자의 것이 되어야 마땅하오.” 그러고는 다시 손가락이 올라갔다.\n\n학장은 프라우디 부인의 이름이 나올 때마다 부주교가 늘 드러내던 그 지독한 혐오를 이제야 알아들었고 또 함께 느끼기 시작했다. 이런 여자를 대체 어찌해야 한단 말인가? 모자를 집어 들고 나가 버리는 것이 그로서는 자연스러운 방책이었겠으나, 그러자니 제 볼일을 그르치고 싶지가 않았다.\n\n“주교님,” 그가 말했다. “한순간만 짬을 내주실 수 있다면 볼일로 여쭐 것이 하나 있습니다. 이렇게 방해를 놓으니 사과부터 드려야 마땅한 줄 압니다만, 정말이지 5분도 붙들지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“여부가 있겠습니까, 학장님, 여부가 있겠습니까.” 주교가 말했다. “내 시간은 온전히 학장님 것입니다. 부디 사과는 마십시오, 부디 사과는 마십시오.”\n\n“주교님, 지금은 하실 일이 아주 많으시잖아요. 요즘 얼마나 바쁘신지 잊지 마세요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. 손님에게 성이 나서 이제 그녀의 기세가 오른 참이었다.\n\n“주교님을 1분 남짓 이상은 붙들지 않겠습니다.” 라자러스의 학장이 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. 이제는 프라우디 부인이 나가든지, 아니면 주교가 앞장서서 다른 방으로 데려가든지 하리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그러나 어느 쪽도 일어날 낌새가 없었고, 그윈 박사는 잠시 말없이 방 한가운데에 서 있었다.\n\n“히램 요양원 일이 아니오?” 프라우디 부인이 넌지시 물었다.\n\n그윈 박사는 어이가 없어 넋을 놓은 채, 달리 무엇을 어찌해야 좋을지 몰라, 주교에게 여쭐 볼일이란 히램 요양원에 관한 것이라고 실토하고 말았다.\n\n“주교님께서는 오늘 아침에 그 자리를 퀴버풀 씨에게 최종적으로 내리셨소.” 부인이 말했다.\n\n그윈 박사는 주교 쪽을 향해 짤막하게 확인을 구했고, 부인의 말이 격식대로 확인되는 것을 보고는 자리를 떴다. “저것이 선거법 개정안이 낳은 결과로군.” 주교관의 가로수길을 걸어 내려가며 그는 혼잣말을 했다. “뭐, 어쨌든 그리스 희곡을 읽던 주교들이 저것보다야 나빴겠나.”\n\n앞서 이야기한 대로, 슬로프 씨는 울라손으로 떠나던 참에 벗인 타워스 씨에게서 급보를 한 통 받았고, 그 덕분에 몹시 기분이 좋아졌던 것인데 그 뒤에 벌어진 일들이 얄궂게도 그 기분에 찬물을 끼얹었다. 편지는 다음과 같았다. 짧다는 것 자체가 넉넉한 변명이 되어 주리라.\n\n부디 만사가 뜻대로 되시기를 빕니다. 내가 도움이 될지는 모르겠으나, 될 수만 있다면 돕겠습니다.\n\n언제나 그대의 벗, T. T.\n\n185-년 9월 30일\n\n여기에는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경의 온갖 입에 발린 소리를 다 합친 것보다 더한 것이 담겨 있었다. 주교의 온갖 약속을 다 합친 것보다, 설령 그 약속이 아무리 진심이었다 한들 그보다 더한 것이. 대주교의 좋은 말 한마디보다, 설령 그것을 얻어 낼 수 있었다 한들 그보다 더한 것이. 톰 타워스가 그를 위해 할 수 있는 일을 해 주겠다는 것이다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 젊은 시절부터 줄곧 신문의 힘을 굳게 믿는 사람이었다. 학위를 받은 뒤로 줄곧 자신도 거기에 손을 담가 왔고, 그것이야말로 앞으로 영국의 지상사를 모조리 정리하고 분배할 위대한 기관이라 여겼다. 그는 아직, 우리 대부분에게 늦든 이르든 찾아오고야 마는 그 나이, 젊은 날의 황금빛 꿈이 흩어져 버리는 그 나이에 이르지 않았다. 그는 제 나라 권세가들의 손에서 권력을 빼앗아, 적어도 제 손이 닿기에는 더 가까운 곳에 맡겨 둔다는 발상에 신이 났다. 날마다 6만 장의 신문이 글 읽는 동포들 사이로 흩어져 나가는 것이야말로, 그가 보기에는 윈저의 왕좌보다도, 다우닝가의 내각보다도, 심지어 웨스트민스터의 의회보다도 더 나은 최고 권력의 보관소였다. 그리고 이 문제를 두고 우리가 슬로프 씨와 다툴 일은 아니다. 그런 생각은 너무나 널리 퍼져 있어 함부로 업신여길 것이 못 되기 때문이다.\n\n톰 타워스는 제 약속만큼은, 아니 그 이상으로 해냈다. 이튿날 아침 『주피터』는 6만 개의 우렁찬 나팔로 여론을 뿜어내며, 슬로프 씨야말로 비어 있는 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이라고 온 세상에 알렸다. 슬로프 씨는 런던발 아침 기차가 이 도시에 닿은 지 30분 만에 바체스터 신문 열람실에서 다음과 같은 대목을 읽었으니, 그로서는 흐뭇한 일이었다.\n\n우리가 조용한 도시 바체스터에 독자 여러분의 주의를 돌린 지 이제 꼭 5년이 되었다. 그날 이후 오늘까지 우리는 그 행복한 성직 사회의 일에 조금도 참견한 바가 없다. 그동안 그곳에서는 늙은 주교가 죽고 젊은 주교가 취임하였으나, 우리는 그 흥미로운 사건에 관하여 관례적인 기록을 남긴 것 이상은 하지 않았다고 믿는다. 지금도 우리는 그 교구의 일에 깊이 참견할 뜻이 없다. 참사회의 어느 분이든 여기까지 읽고 양심에 거리끼는 바가 있거든 부디 마음을 놓으시라. 무엇보다 새 주교의 마음이 편안하시기를. 우리는 지금 싸울 무장을 갖춘 것이 아니라, 손에 올리브 가지를 들고 저 유서 깊은 대성당의 존엄한 탑들에 다가서는 것이다.\n\n이제 5년 전인 그 시절에 우리가 바체스터의 히램 요양원이라 불리는 자선 시설의 형편을 두고 논평할 일이 있었음을 기억하실 것이다. 우리는 그것이 잘못 운영되고 있으며, 워든의 직을 맡고 있던 그 대단히 존경스럽고 경건한 신사가 지나치게 쉽게 수행되는 직무에 견주어 지나치게 후한 보수를 받고 있다고 보았다. 이 신사는 — 그리고 우리는 이 말을 아무런 빈정거림 없이 온전한 진심으로 하거니와 — 그때까지 이 문제를 그런 눈으로 바라본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 우리는 그 공이 우리에게 돌아올 것이든 아니든 스스로 공을 취할 뜻이 없다. 다만 우리의 논평이 낳은 결과는, 워든이 실제로 그 문제를 들여다보았고, 들여다본 끝에 자신도 우리가 말한 것 말고는 달리 어떤 견해에도 이를 수 없음을 깨닫고는 매우 훌륭하게도 그 직을 내던졌다는 것이다. 당시의 주교 또한 그에 못지않게 훌륭하게도, 이 일이 더 나은 기틀 위에 놓이기 전까지는 그 빈자리를 채우기를 사양하였다. 그 뒤 의회가 이 문제를 다루었으며, 우리는 이제 히램 요양원이 곧 새로운 후원 아래 다시 문을 열게 되었음을 독자 여러분께 알리게 되어 흡족하다. 종래에는 노인 열두 사람을 부양할 몫이 마련되어 있었다. 이것이 이제는 여성에게까지 넓혀져, 바체스터에 그런 분들이 있기만 하다면 나이 든 여인 열두 사람이 그 시설에 더해질 것이다. 여사감이 한 사람 있을 것이고, 바라건대 가난한 이들 가운데서도 가장 가난한 아이들을 위한 학교가 딸려 있을 것이며, 재산 관리인도 한 사람 있을 것이다. 워든은 — 워든은 여전히 있을 것이므로 — 종래에 지급되던 것보다 이 자선 시설의 규모에 한결 걸맞은 수입을 받게 될 것이다. 그 봉급은 450파운드가 되리라 우리는 알고 있다. 아울러 전임 워든이 살던 그 훌륭한 집도 여전히 이 직에 딸려 있으리라는 것을 덧붙여 둔다.\n\n바체스터 요양원이 세계에 이름을 떨친다고 뽐낼 수야 없겠으나, 우리가 그 쇠락한 형편을 언급한 바 있으니 그 부흥에도 언급하는 것이 옳다고 여긴다. 부디 나아가고 번창하기를. 그 담장 안에 들여온 유익한 개혁이 바랄 만한 데까지 이르렀는지는 의심스러울 수도 있다. 학교라는 저 중요한 문제가 다소간 새 워든의 재량에 맡겨진 듯 보이는 까닭이다. 이것이야말로 이 시설에서 가장 중요한 부분이 될 수도 있었으니, 새 워든은 — 우리의 거리낌 없는 논평에 그가 부디 언짢아하지 않기를 바라거니와 — 교사로서의 자질을 어느 정도 염두에 두고 뽑을 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 지금 얻은 말의 이빨을 들여다보지는 않겠다. 부디 이 요양원이 나아가고 번창하기를! 물론 워든 자리는 5년 전 그토록 명예롭게 그것을 내놓은 그 신사에게 제안되었으나, 우리가 듣기로 그는 이를 사양하였다고 한다. 새로 들어오게 된 여인들이 그의 눈에는 감당하기 벅차 보였던 것인지, 줄어든 수입이 옛 자리로 돌아갈 만한 유인이 되지 못했던 것인지, 아니면 그사이에 다른 성직 임무를 맡게 된 것인지 우리로서는 알 수 없다. 다만 그가 그 제안을 거절하였고 그 자리는 푸딩데일의 교구 사제 퀴버풀 씨가 받아들였다는 소식은 전해 들었다.\n\n되살아난 히램에게 이만큼은 마땅히 돌려야 할 몫이라고 우리는 생각한다. 그러나 바체스터 이야기가 나온 김에, 이 유서 깊은 도시의 교회 정치와 관련한 또 한 가지 문제를 두고 정중하고 겸허하게 우리의 의견을 밝혀 볼까 한다. 수석 사제 트리폴 박사가 어제 세상을 떠났다. 그의 별세에 관한 짧은 기록은 나이와 그가 여러 시기에 맡았던 여러 성직을 담아 본지의 다른 난에 실릴 것이다. 우리가 그에게서 알고 있는 유일한 흠은 나이였는데, 그것은 우리 모두가 저지르게 되기를 바라 마지않는 죄인 만큼 무겁게 나무라지는 않겠다. 부디 평안히 잠드시기를! 그러나 세상을 떠나는 수석 사제의 많은 나이는 나무랄 거리가 못 된다 하더라도, 이제 막 태어나는 수석 사제에게 그런 흠이 조금이라도 용납될 만하다고는 우리는 생각하지 않는다. 예순 넘은 이를 임명하던 시절은 이제 지나갔기를 우리는 진심으로 바란다. 우리에게 수석 사제가 필요하다면, 그것은 어떤 목적을 위해서일 터이다. 그 목적은 예순 된 사람보다 마흔 된 사람이 반드시 더 잘 이루어 낼 것이다. 수석 사제에게 보수를 준다면, 그것은 어떤 종류의 일에 대한 보수일 터이다. 그 일이 무엇이든 한창때의 일꾼이 가장 잘해 낼 것이다. 보아하니 트리폴 박사는 죽을 때 여든이었다. 우리는 늙은 성직자를 위한 연금 제도를 아직 아무것도 마련해 두지 못했으니, 그 나이의 현직 수석 사제들을 내치자는 뜻은 아니다. 다만 그런 이가 되도록 적기를 바랄 뿐이다. 지금 일흔 된 이를 임명한다면, 그는 한두 해 안에 아무 쓸모가 없어질 것임을 — 지금 당장 이미 그렇지 않다면 말이지만 — ○○ 경께 삼가 지적해 두는 바이다. 모든 사람이 각하처럼 사철 푸른 나무는 아니라는 점을 일깨워 드려도 각하께서는 너그러이 받아 주시리라.\n\n슬로프 씨의 이름이 이 성직 자리에 거론되고 있다는 말을 우리는 듣고 있다. 슬로프 씨는 현재 주교의 전속 사제이다. 이보다 나은 사람을 뽑기란 좀처럼 어려울 것이다. 그는 재능 있는 사람이며, 젊고 활동적이고 대성당의 사정에 밝다. 게다가 우리가 양심을 걸고 믿는 바로는 참으로 경건한 성직자이다. 그가 바체스터시에서 해 온 봉사가 높이 평가받아 왔음을 우리는 알고 있다. 그는 뛰어난 설교자이며 원숙한 학자이다. 이런 선임이라면 교회 성직 수여의 현 운영에 대한 대중의 신뢰를 크게 끌어올릴 것이며, 이제부터 우리 국교회의 제도가 낡아 빠진 성직 향락가들에게 안락한 자리를 내주지 않으리라는 것을 사람들이 믿게 만들 것이다.\n\n바체스터 신문 열람실의 독서대 앞에 선 채로 슬로프 씨는 이 기사를 상당한 만족감 속에 소화했다. 요양원에 관하여 거기 적힌 내용은 이제 그에게 비교적 아무래도 좋은 문제였다. 그의 인격을 향해 그토록 뻔뻔스럽게 온갖 예의를 짓밟은 그 악녀의 아버지를 그 자리에 되돌려 놓는 데 실패한 것은 물론 다행스러웠고, 그런 점에서는 만족스러웠다. 그러나 프라우디 부인이 미는 사람이 임명되었으니, 그런 점에서는 불만스러웠다. 다만 그의 마음은 이제 볼드 부인이나 프라우디 부인 따위보다 훨씬 높이 날아오르고 있었다. 그는 『주피터』의 수법에 익히 밝았으므로 기사의 핵심이 마지막 문단에 놓인다는 것을 알고 있었다. 명예로운 그 자리가 자기에게 주어졌고, 그것은 참으로 그가 바랄 수 있었던 만큼이나 명예로웠다. 그는 벗 타워스 씨에게 무척 고마웠고, 언젠가 수석사제관 식당의 제 성찬 차린 식탁에서 왕후처럼 그를 대접할 날을 마음 가득 그려 보았다.\n\n트리폴 박사가 가을에 죽어 준 것은 슬로프 씨로서는 다행한 일이었다. 우리의 아침 식탁에 먹을거리를 대는 저 『주피터』 편집진은 지난 한 달 동안 넉넉한 꼴을 구하느라 몹시 애를 먹던 참이었다. 마침 새 미국 대통령 이야기도 없었다. 조지아든 어디든 철도 열차에서 놀라운 참사가 일어나지도 않았다. 파산한 은행도 씨가 말랐으니, 죽은 수석 사제 하나와 산 수석 사제 하나가 필요하다는 사정은 하늘이 내린 선물이었다. 트리폴 박사가 6월에 죽었더라면 타워스 씨는 아마 슬로프 씨의 경건함에 관하여 그토록 소상히 알지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그리고 여기서 우리는 승리에 젖은 슬로프 씨를 잠시 남겨 두기로 한다. 다만 그의 심경이 온통 승리감뿐이었던 것은 아니라는 점은 밝혀 두어야겠다. 그 미망인에게 거절당한 일, 아니 그보다도 거절당한 그 방식이 그의 속을 지독히도 긁어 놓았다. 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 그는 자기가 당한 일을 떠올릴 때마다 뺨에 그 얼얼함을 실제로 느꼈다. 바체스터 거리를 걸으며 혼잣말로 그녀를 험한 이름으로 부르지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다. 기도를 올릴 때에도 그녀를 용서할 마음이 도무지 들지 않았다. 억지로라도 그리해 보려 하면 마음이 그 시도에서 움츠러들었고, 용서하기는커녕 두 배의 앙심으로 내달아 제가 입은 상처가 얼마나 큰지를 곱씹었다. 그리하여 그의 기도는 아무 뜻 없이 입술에서 굴러떨어졌다.\n\n그리고 시뇨라 — 그녀까지 미워할 수만 있다면 그는 무엇인들 내주지 않았으랴? 그러기는커녕 그는 그녀가 늘 누워 있는 그 소파마저 떠받들고 있었다.\n\n그리하여 슬로프 씨에게도 모든 것이 장밋빛만은 아니었으니, 그의 희망만은 드높이 치솟고 있었지만 말이다.\n\n# 제44장. 집에 있는 볼드 부인\n\n손 양의 연회가 있던 날 저녁, 울라손에서 집으로 돌아왔을 때 가엾은 볼드 부인은 몹시 불행했고, 게다가 몹시 지쳐 있었다. 마음의 고달픔만큼 몸을 지치게 하는 것은 없거니와, 엘리너의 마음은 참으로 고달팠다.\n\n스탠호프 박사는 정중하게, 그러나 그리 살갑지는 않게 그녀에게 들어와 차를 들라고 청했고, 그녀가 사양하는 태도를 보고 이 훌륭한 박사는 그 청을 되풀이할 필요가 없겠다고 확신했다. 그는 고인이 된 볼드 씨의 유산을 제 촉망받는 아들의 수입으로 바꾸어 놓으려던 그 음모에 딱히 한몫 낀 것은 아니었으나, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 훤히 알고 있었다. 그리고 버티가 마차로 함께 집에 돌아가기를 마다하는 것을 보았을 때, 그 일이 틀어졌다는 것 또한 훤히 알아차렸다.\n\n엘리너는 참사회원이 제 집 문 앞에서 내릴 때 샬럿이 튀어나와 저를 덮치지나 않을까 몹시 두려웠으나, 버티가 사려 깊게도 마차를 그녀의 집 쪽으로 돌아가게 하여 그 일을 면하게 해 주었다. 이것 역시 스탠호프 박사는 알아차렸으되 아무 말 없이 넘어가 주었다.\n\n집에 이르렀을 때 그녀는 메리 볼드가 아기를 무릎에 앉힌 채 응접실에 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 달려들어 무릎을 꿇고는, 그 조그만 녀석이 놀랄 지경이 되도록 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“아, 메리, 함께 가지 않기를 정말 잘했어요. 지긋지긋한 파티였어요.”\n\n그런데 메리가 가느냐 마느냐 하는 문제는 두 사람 사이에서 크게 논의되었던 일이다. 볼드 부인은 초대를 받았을 때 그랜틀리 집안의 손님으로 가 있었고, 엘리너를 주로 요양원이나 플럼스테드 목사관에서 보아 온 손 양은 메리 볼드에 대해서는 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 올케는 제 날개 밑에 들어와 함께 가자고 간청하며 손 양에게 편지를 쓰거나 찾아가 보겠다고까지 했다. 그러나 볼드 양은 사양했다. 실은 볼드 씨가 손 집안 같은 사람들에게 그리 인기가 있지 않았던 터라, 그 누이는 특별히 청을 받지 않는 한 그들 틈에 끼려 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“그렇다면요,” 메리가 밝게 말했다. “아쉬워할 일이 그만큼 줄어든 셈이네요.”\n\n“아쉬워할 일이야 하나도 없지요. 하지만 아! 메리, 나는… 너무 많아요, 너무 많아요.” 그러고서 그녀는 제 어루만짐에 잠에서 깨어난 아들에게 입을 맞추기 시작했다. 고개를 들었을 때, 메리는 그녀의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 보았다.\n\n“세상에, 엘리너, 무슨 일이에요? 무슨 일이 있었기에… 엘리너, 가엾은 엘리너, 대체 무슨 일이에요?” 그러면서 메리는 아이를 여전히 품에 안은 채 일어섰다.\n\n“이리 줘요, 이리 줘요.” 젊은 어머니가 말했다. “이리 줘요, 메리.” 그러면서 그녀는 시누이의 팔에서 아이를 거의 빼앗다시피 했다. 가엾은 조그만 녀석은 방해를 받고 무어라 칭얼거렸으나, 그래도 제 어머니의 품속으로 파고들었다.\n\n“여기, 메리, 이 외투 좀 받아 줘요. 내 것, 오직 내 것인 귀염둥이, 귀염둥이, 귀염둥이 보석아. 너는 나를 속이지 않지. 다른 사람들은 다 거짓되고, 다른 사람들은 다 모질단다. 엄마는 아무도, 아무도, 아무도 아끼지 않을 거야. 오직 제 것, 제 것, 제 것인 조그만 사내만 빼고.” 그러면서 그녀는 다시 아기에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안았고, 아이의 얼굴 위로 눈물이 흘러내릴 때까지 울었다.\n\n“누가 그렇게 모질게 굴었어요, 엘리너?” 메리가 말했다. “나는 아니었으면 좋겠는데요.”\n\n그런데 이 문제에 관해서는 엘리너에게도 마음이 편치 않을 큰 까닭이 있었다. 다정한 시누이를 모질다고 나무랄 수야 없는 노릇이었으나, 그보다 더 속 쓰린 일을 해야 했으니, 곧 시누이가 미리 일러 주었던 그 경솔함을 두고 자기 자신을 나무라야 했던 것이다. 볼드 양은 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 가까이 지내는 것을 한 번도 부추긴 적이 없었고, 스탠호프 집안과의 우정에 대해서는 그 부드러운 말투가 허락하는 한도 안에서 분명하게 말렸다. 그러나 남편과 떨어져 사는 유부녀는 못마땅하다고 하고 샬럿 스탠호프가 도무지 교회에 나가지 않는다고 짚었을 때, 엘리너는 그저 웃어넘겼을 뿐이었다. 그런데 이제 엘리너로서는 입을 다물고 있거나 — 이는 도무지 될 수 없는 일이었다 — 아니면 제가 아주 틀렸음을 고백하거나 해야 했는데, 이 또한 거의 마찬가지로 될 수 없는 일이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 눈물을 더 흘려 그 궂은 날을 미루었고, 조그만 조니를 흔들어 깨워 제 어루만짐에 응하게 함으로써 스스로를 달랬다.\n\n“얘는 귀염둥이야. 금처럼 참되지. 얘가 없으면 엄마는 어떻게 살까? 저를 위로해 줄 제 조니 볼드가 없으면 엄마는 그냥 드러누워 죽어 버릴 거야.” 이런 말과 이와 비슷한 말을 그녀는 훨씬 더 많이 했고, 한동안 메리가 묻는 말에는 그 밖의 다른 대답을 하지 않았다.\n\n세상의 속임수에서 이런 식으로 위안을 얻는 일은 아주 흔하다. 어머니는 제 아이에게서 그것을 얻고, 사내들은 제 개에게서 얻는다. 어떤 이들은 제 지팡이에게서까지 그것을 얻는데, 이 또한 꼭 그만큼은 사리에 맞는 일이다. 우리를 속이는 재주를 아직 익히지 못한 것들에게 속지 않았다 하여 우리가 어찌 스스로 기뻐할 수 있단 말인가? 참된 사내가 — 그런 것이 있기나 하다면 — 혹은 참된 여인이 있다면, 거기서는 실로 큰 위안을 얻을 수 있으리라.\n\n그러나 제 아이를 어루만지는 가운데 엘리너는 참으로 위안을 얻었으니, 그것을 그녀에게 아까워하는 자에게는 화가 있을진저. 그러나 궂은 날은 미루어졌을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 그 언짢은 이야기를 메리에게 해야 했고, 아버지에게도 해야 했다. 아니, 아는 이들 앞에서 온전히 떳떳해지려면 그 무리 전체에게 그 이야기를 해야 하지 않겠는가? 지금 이 순간 그녀가 위로를 구할 만한 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨를 미워했다. 그것은 두말할 나위가 없었고, 그 감정 속에서는 오히려 마음이 후련했다. 그녀는 스탠호프 집안을 미워하고 경멸했다. 그러나 그 감정은 그녀를 몹시 괴롭혔다. 그녀는 말하자면 옛 벗들에게서 저를 떼어 내어 이 집안의 품에 몸을 던졌던 것인데, 그러고 나서 그들은 그녀를 어찌 이용하려 들었던가? 저를 나쁘게 여겼던 제 아버지와도 마음을 풀기가 쉽지 않았다. 메리 볼드는 훈계자 노릇을 했다. 그 훈계자가 틀린 것으로 드러났다면 용서할 수도 있었으련만, 옳은 훈계자는 용서받을 수 없는 법이다. 부주교를 미워하지 않을 수 없었고, 이제는 어느 만큼 그 앞에서 스스로를 낮추어야 하는 까닭에 전보다 더 미웠다. 언니도 미웠으니, 언니는 부주교와 한 몸이나 다름없었기 때문이다. 그리고 할 수만 있었다면 애러빈 씨도 미워했을 것이다. 그는 저를 마음에 두는 척하고서도, 그녀가 보는 앞에서 세상에 그 여자 말고는 아름다운 이가 없다는 듯, 잠깐의 눈길이라도 받을 만한 여자가 달리 없다는 듯 그 이탈리아 여자에게 몸을 기울이고 있지 않았던가. 그런데 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨에 관한 이 모든 일을 알게 될 터였다! 그녀는 스스로에게 그를 미워한다고 말했고, 그렇게 말하면서 스스로에게 거짓말을 하고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 아기 말고는 위안이 없었으므로, 그녀는 그것을 한껏 누렸다. 메리는 무엇이 올케를 이토록 격렬하게 뒤흔들어 놓았는지 짐작할 수 없었으나, 그 슬픔이 억눌러 둘 수 있기에는 너무 크다는 것을 이내 알아보고 아이가 요람에 들 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다.\n\n“차 좀 들어요, 엘리너.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아무래도 좋아요.” 그녀가 말했으나, 실은 울라손에서 아무것도 먹지 않았으므로 몹시 시장했을 것이 틀림없었다.\n\n메리는 조용히 차를 끓이고 빵에 버터를 발랐으며, 외투를 치우고 방 안을 편안하게 꾸며 놓았다.\n\n“푹 잠들었네요.” 그녀가 말했다. “많이 지쳤을 텐데, 내가 안아다 침대에 뉘어 올게요.”\n\n그러나 엘리너는 시누이가 아이에게 손을 대게 하려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 아기의 눈을 그윽이 들여다보고 그 눈이 가장 깊은 잠에 잠겨 있음을 확인한 뒤, 소파 위에 아이가 누울 자리를 마련해 주었다. 그날 밤만은 무슨 일이 있어도 아이를 제 눈에서 떼어 놓지 않겠다고 그녀는 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n“자, 넬리,” 메리가 말했다. “나한테 성내지 말아요. 적어도 나는 엘리너 기분을 상하게 한 일이 없잖아요.”\n\n“성내는 거 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 화가 났어요? 설마 나한테 화가 났을 리는 없을 텐데요.”\n\n“아니에요, 화 안 났어요. 적어도 메리한테는요.”\n\n“화가 안 났으면 내가 끓여 놓은 차를 마셔요. 분명 마시고 싶을 텐데요.”\n\n엘리너는 정말로 그것을 마셨고, 설득당해 주었다. 그녀는 먹고 마셨으며, 속사람이 기운을 되찾자 세상 전반에 대해 조금은 너그러워졌다. 마침내 그녀는 제 이야기를 시작할 말을 찾아냈고, 잠자리에 들기 전에 속을 죄다 털어놓고 모든 것을 이야기했다. 모든 것이라고는 하나, 자기가 물리친 구혼자들에 관해서만이었다. 애러빈 씨에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n“내가 잘못한 건 알아요.” 슬로프 씨에게 날린 손찌검을 두고 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 그이가 무슨 짓을 할지 알 수 없었고, 나는 나를 지켜야 했어요.”\n\n“그런 꼴을 당하고도 남을 사람이지요.” 메리가 말했다.\n\n“당하고도 남다니요!” 슬로프 씨에 관한 한 마음이 거의 피에 굶주려 있던 엘리너가 말했다. “내가 단검으로 찔렀더라도 그이는 당해도 쌌을 거예요. 그런데 플럼스테드에서는 이 일을 두고 뭐라고 할까요?”\n\n“나 같으면 그쪽에는 말하지 않겠어요.” 메리가 말했다. 엘리너도 말하지 말아야겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n메리 볼드보다 더 다정한 위로자는 없었을 것이다. 스탠호프 집안의 꿍꿍이를 전해 들었을 때 그녀에게는 우쭐한 기색이라고는 티끌만큼도 없었고, 엘리너가 얼마 전까지 벗이었던 샬럿을 비열하고 음흉한 여자라고 불렀을 때에도 전에 제가 했던 말을 들먹이지 않았다. 슬로프 씨의 머리 위로 퍼부어지는 온갖 욕설을 그녀는 그대로 받아 되뇌었을 뿐, 자기가 진작 그렇게 말했노라고는 한 번도 내비치지 않았다. “내가 그랬잖아, 내가 그랬잖아!” 하는 것은 참된 욥의 친구가 내는 까마귀 울음소리다. 그러나 메리는 제 벗이 슬픔 가운데 누워 질그릇 조각으로 제 몸을 긁고 있는 것을 보고는, 따지지도 우쭐대지도 않고 삼갔다. 엘리너는 그 삼감의 고마움을 알아보았고, 마침내 스스로 마음을 가라앉히도록 저를 놓아두었다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 집 밖으로 나가지 않았다. 바체스터는 스탠호프네와 슬로프네 사람들로, 어쩌면 애러빈네와 그랜틀리네 사람들로까지 그득할 것 같았다. 실로 그녀의 벗들 가운데는 마음이 켕기지 않고 마주칠 수 있는 사람이 거의 없었다.\n\n그날 오후에 그녀는 수석 사제가 죽었다는 소식을 들었고, 퀴버풀 씨가 마침내 요양원에 임명되었다는 소식도 들었다.\n\n저녁에 아버지가 찾아왔고, 그리하여 그 이야기를, 적어도 아버지에게 차마 말할 수 있는 만큼은 다시 되풀이해야 했다. 그는 사실 슬로프 씨의 뻔뻔함에 그리 놀라지 않았으나, 딸의 마음을 다치지 않게 하려고 놀란 척해야만 했다. 그러나 그는 속이는 데에는 도무지 서툴렀으므로, 그녀는 그것을 훤히 알아보았다.\n\n“보아하니,” 그녀가 말했다. “아버지는 슬로프 씨가 저를 그런 식으로 대한 것이 지극히 흔한 일이라고 여기시는군요.” 그녀는 그 포옹에 대해서도, 그것을 어떻게 맞받았는지에 대해서도 아버지에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“나는 조금도 이상하다고 생각하지 않는단다.” 그가 말했다. “누구든 우리 엘리너를 흠모한다는 게 말이다.”\n\n“저로서는 이상해요.” 그녀가 말했다. “털끝만큼도 부추김을 받은 일이 없으면서 어떻게 그런 뻔뻔함을 부릴 수 있는지요.”\n\n이에 대해 하딩 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 부주교였다면 이는 수아 사람 빌닷에게도 부끄럽지 않을 반박의 실마리가 되었을 것이다.\n\n“그렇지만 부주교에게는 말할 테지?” 하딩 씨가 물었다.\n\n“무얼 말이에요?” 그녀가 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n“아니면 수전에게라도.” 하딩 씨가 말을 이었다. “수전에게는 말하려무나. 그 사람의 구애가 네게 반가운 것이리라고 짐작한 것이 너를 그르치는 일이었음을 알게 해 주려무나.”\n\n“그건 그분들이 알아서 알아내시겠지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 두 분 어느 쪽에게도 슬로프 씨의 이름을 제 입으로 꺼내는 일은 없을 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 내가 하마.”\n\n“아버지 마음이 편해지시는 데 필요한 일이라면 무엇이든 제가 막을 권리는 없지요. 하지만 저를 위해서라면 부디 하지 마세요. 그랜틀리 박사는 저를 좋게 여긴 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 거예요. 그리고 이제는 제가 그분에게 좋게 여겨지기를 바라는지조차 잘 모르겠어요.”\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 요양원 일로 넘어갔다. “그런데 그게 사실인가요, 아버지?”\n\n“무엇이 말이냐, 얘야?” 그가 말했다. “수석 사제 말이냐? 그래, 아무래도 사실인 듯싶구나. 아니, 그 일에 의심할 여지가 없다는 것을 나는 알고 있단다.”\n\n“가엾은 트리폴 양, 정말 안됐어요. 하지만 제가 여쭌 건 그게 아니에요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “요양원 말이에요, 아버지.”\n\n“그래, 얘야. 퀴버풀 씨가 그것을 맡게 되었다는 게 사실인 모양이다.”\n\n“아, 너무해요.”\n\n“아니다, 얘야, 조금도, 조금도 너무한 일이 아니란다. 나는 그 자리가 그에게 잘 맞기를 진심으로 바라고 있다.”\n\n“하지만 아버지, 너무한 일인 줄 아시잖아요. 그토록 바라시고 그토록 기다리시며 옛집으로 돌아가려 하셨는데, 그것이 이런 식으로 생판 남에게 넘어가다니요!”\n\n“얘야, 주교에게는 제 뜻에 드는 사람에게 그것을 줄 권리가 있단다.”\n\n“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요, 아버지. 그런 권리는 없어요. 아버지가 새로 무슨 성직 자리를 노리는 후보인 것도 아니잖아요. 주교에게 티끌만큼이라도 공정함이 있다면…”\n\n“주교는 제 조건을 붙여 그것을 내게 내주었고, 나는 그 조건이 마음에 들지 않아 거절했다. 그러고 나서 불평할 수는 없는 노릇이지.”\n\n“조건이라니요! 조건을 붙일 권리는 없었어요.”\n\n“그건 나도 모르겠다만, 그럴 힘은 있었던 모양이더구나. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 넬리, 나는 지금 이대로도 흡족하단다. 그 일이 노여운 논쟁거리가 되었을 때, 나는 아예 그것을 다 털어 버리고 싶었다.”\n\n“하지만 옛집으로 돌아가고 싶어 하셨잖아요, 아버지. 저한테 그렇게 말씀하셨어요.”\n\n“그래, 얘야, 그랬지. 잠시 동안은 그러기를 바랐다. 그리고 그건 어리석은 일이었다. 나는 이제 늙어 가고 있고, 이 세상에서 바라는 것이라고는 무엇보다 평온과 안식이다. 요양원으로 돌아갔더라면 주교와 끝없이 다투고, 그 전속 사제와 다투고, 부주교와 다투었을 게다. 나는 이제 그럴 만한 사람이 못 된다. 그런 성가신 일들을 감당할 수가 없다. 그러니 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회에 남겨진 것이 내게는 그리 언짢지 않구나. 굶어 죽을 일이야 결코 없을 게다.” 그가 웃으며 덧붙였다. “네가 여기 있는 한은 말이다.”\n\n“그럼 저와 함께 와서 사시겠어요, 아버지?” 그녀가 아버지의 두 손을 잡으며 간절하게 말했다. “그렇게 해 주신다면, 그렇게 하겠다고 약속해 주신다면, 아버지 말씀이 옳다고 인정하겠어요.”\n\n“오늘은 어쨌든 너와 함께 저녁을 먹으마.”\n\n“그게 아니라 아주 여기서 사시라고요. 하이 스트리트의 그 답답하고 지긋지긋한 조그만 방은 버리시고요.”\n\n“얘야, 그건 아주 근사한 조그만 방이란다. 너는 정말이지 무례하기 짝이 없구나.”\n\n“아, 아버지, 농담하지 마세요. 아버지가 계실 만한 데가 못 돼요. 늙어 가신다고 말씀하시지만, 저는 아버지가 늙지 않으셨다고 믿어요.”\n\n“늙지 않았다고, 얘야?”\n\n“네, 아버지, 늙지 않으셨어요. 늙었다고 할 정도는 아니에요. 하지만 앉아 지낼 만한 반반한 방이 아쉬울 만큼은 넉넉히 나이 드셨지요. 메리와 제가 여기서 얼마나 쓸쓸한지 아시잖아요. 앞쪽 큰 침실에서는 아무도 자는 사람이 없다는 것도 아시고요. 여기서 이토록 아버지를 바라는데 저 위에 혼자 계신다는 건 정말 매정한 일이에요.”\n\n“고맙구나, 넬리. 고맙다. 하지만 얘야…”\n\n“아버지가 여기 저희와 함께 사셨더라면, 저희가 얼마나 쓸쓸한지를 생각하면 정말 그러셨어야 했다고 저는 여기는데요, 슬로프 씨에 관한 이 끔찍한 일도 하나도 없었을 거예요.”\n\n그러나 하딩 씨는 하이 스트리트에 있는 자기만의, 하나뿐인 조그만 pied à terre를 포기하도록 설득당하지는 않았다. 그는 딸과 함께 저녁을 먹고, 딸의 집에 머물고, 딸을 찾아오고, 아주 함께 사는 것 말고는 무엇이든 하겠노라고 약속했다. 딸이 슬로프 씨를 물리쳤고 스탠호프 씨도 물리칠 참이었다고는 하나 머지않아 더 마음에 드는 다른 구혼자가 나타날 성싶으며, 그런 구혼자가 나타나는 날에는 앞쪽 큰 침실이 지금보다 더 자주 쓰이게 될지도 모른다는 말을 딸에게 하는 것은 이 사람의 유별난 심성에 맞지 않는 일이었다. 그러나 그런 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳐 지나간 것은 틀림없으며, 그것이 하이 스트리트의 답답하고 지긋지긋한 조그만 방을 그대로 두기로 마음먹게 한 다른 이유들에 무게를 보태었을 것이다.\n\n저녁은 조용하고 편안하게 흘러갔다. 엘리너는 언제나 다른 누구와 있을 때보다 아버지와 있을 때 더 행복했다. 그에게 아기 숭배를 향한 타고난 취향이 있었다고는 할 수 없겠으나, 그는 언제나 제 몸을 기꺼이 바칠 채비가 되어 있었으므로, 딸과 메리 볼드와 더불어 그 놀라운 아이를 찬미하는 삼중창에서 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 제3성부를 맡았다.\n\n저녁에 아기를 다시 소파에 뉘어 재워 놓고 세 사람이 함께 악보 앞에 서 있는데, 하녀가 아주 조그만 쪽지 한 장을 어여쁜 분홍빛 봉투에 담아 들여왔다. 그것은 조그만 쟁반 위에 놓인 채로 방 안을 온통 향내로 채웠다. 메리 볼드와 볼드 부인은 둘 다 피아노 앞에 있었고, 하딩 씨는 두 다리 사이에 첼로를 끼고 그 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있었으므로, 그 서한의 우아함은 세 사람 모두의 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n“저, 마님, 스탠호프 박사님 댁 마부가 답장을 기다리라는 분부를 받았다고 합니다.” 하녀가 말했다.\n\n엘리너는 그 쪽지를 손에 받아 들면서 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌다. 그 글씨는 전에 본 적이 없는 것이었다. 익히 보아 온 샬럿의 편지는 아주 다른 투와 모양새였다. 샬럿은 대개 큰 편지지에 썼고, 편지를 삼각모 모양으로, 때로는 그 크기만 하게 비틀어 접었으며, 겉봉은 사내처럼 제멋대로 뻗은 글씨로 썼고, 마치 그것이 제 고유한 서명이라도 되는 양 잉크 방울이나 얼룩을 하나 더하는 일도 드물지 않았다. 이 쪽지의 겉봉은 어여쁜 여자 글씨로 쓰여 있었고, 붙여 놓은 봉함지에는 금빛 작은 관이 도장처럼 찍혀 있었다. 엘리너는 그런 것을 전에 본 적이 없었으나, 그것이 시뇨라에게서 온 것이리라 짐작했다. 그런 서한은 시뇨라가 머무는 집이면 어디서든 수없이 많이 나갔으되, 부인네들에게 보내지는 일은 드물었다. 마부는 시녀에게서 그 편지를 볼드 부인에게 가져가라는 말을 듣고, 무언가 잘못된 것이 아니냐는 뜻을 대놓고 내비쳤다. 그러자 시녀는 마부의 따귀를 올려붙였다. 마부가 그 꾸지람을 얼마나 온순한 마음으로 받아들이는지를 슬로프 씨가 보았더라면, 철학에서도 종교에서도 쓸모 있는 교훈을 하나 배웠을 것이다.\n\n쪽지의 내용은 아래와 같다. 이 책장 안에 그 어떤 편지도 다시는 전문 그대로 옮겨 적지 않겠노라는 미더운 약속으로 여겨 주셔도 좋다.\n\n큰 호의로 여기시고, 내일 저를 찾아와 주십사 청해도 되겠는지요. 어느 시간이 가장 편하신지 말씀해 주시면 좋겠으나, 되도록 이른 시간이었으면 합니다. 제가 부인을 찾아뵐 수 있는 몸이라면 이런 무례를 범하지 않으리라는 것은 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지요.\n\n요전 날 무슨 일이 있었는지 저는 얼마쯤 알고 있으며, 부인께서 저를 찾아와 주신다면 언짢은 일은 조금도 겪지 않으시리라 약속드립니다. 제 오라비는 오늘 저희 곁을 떠나 런던으로 가고, 거기서 이탈리아로 갑니다.\n\n적잖이 중요한 말씀을 드릴 것이 없고서야 제가 이렇게 부인을 성가시게 하지는 않으리라는 생각이 아마 드실 것입니다. 그러니 제 청을 들어주지 않으시더라도 부디 저를 너그러이 보아 주십시오.\n\n그러면 부디 저를 믿어 주시기를, 진심을 다하여,\n\n목요일 저녁\n\n세 사람은 이 서한을 두고 10분이나 15분쯤 의논하며 앉아 있다가, 엘리너가 이튿날 아침 12시에 시뇨라를 찾아가겠다는 한 줄을 써 보내기로 정했다.\n\n# 제45장. 집에 있는 스탠호프 일가\n\n이제 우리는 스탠호프 일가로 돌아가, 그들이 울라손에서 돌아온 뒤 어떻게 처신했는지를 보아야 한다.\n\n동생과 함께 첫 번째 귀갓길에 돌아온 샬럿은 마차가 두 번째로 문 앞에 닿을 때까지 가슴을 두근거리며 기다렸다. 아래층으로 뛰어 내려가지도, 창가에 서지도, 무언가 놀라운 일이 벌어지기를 기대한다는 기색을 겉으로 드러내지도 않았다. 그러나 마차 바퀴 소리가 들리자 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 귀를 곤두세우고, 포도를 밟는 엘리너의 발소리나 그녀를 맞아들이는 버티의 쾌활한 목소리가 나기를 기다렸다. 둘 중 하나라도 들렸다면 만사가 잘되었다고 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 귀에는 어느 쪽도 들려오지 않았다. 들린 것은 아버지의 느린 발소리뿐이었다. 아버지는 육중하게 마차에서 내려 복도를 천천히 걸어, 1층에 있는 자기 방으로 들어갔다. “스탠호프 양을 들여보내라.” 그가 하인에게 말했다.\n\n“뭔가 잘못됐어.” 뒤쪽 응접실 소파에 누운 채로 마들린이 말했다.\n\n“버티는 이제 끝났어.” 샬럿이 대꾸했다. “알았어, 알았다니까.” 전갈을 가지고 올라온 하인에게 그녀가 말했다. “곧 가겠다고 아버지께 전해라.”\n\n“버티의 구애가 어긋난 모양이야.” 마들린이 말했다. “그럴 줄 알았어.”\n\n“그렇다면 제 잘못이지. 그 여자 쪽은 마음이 충분히 있었을 거야, 난 확신해.” 샬럿이 말했다. 한 여자가 다른 여자를 두고 말할 때 드물지 않게 나오는 그런 심술궂은 어조였다.\n\n“이제 그분께는 뭐라고 할 거야?” ‘그분’이란 시뇨라가 아버지를 두고 한 말이었다.\n\n“그건 아버지가 어떤 상태이신가에 달렸지. 이 혼사가 성사되기만 했으면 아버지는 버티의 급한 빚쟁이들을 막아 주려고 200파운드를 낼 참이셨어. 이제는 그 돈을 버티가 대신 받아서 나가 제 운을 시험해 봐야지.”\n\n“지금 걔는 어디 있어?”\n\n“하늘이나 알겠지! 손 씨 댁 정원 도랑 바닥에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있거나, 채드윅 양들 가운데 누구랑 시시덕거리고 있겠지. 저 애한테는 무슨 말을 해도 새겨지는 법이 없어. 하지만 내가 내려가지 않으면 아버지가 노발대발하실 거야.”\n\n“그래, 무슨 말을 해도 새겨지지 않지. 하지만 오래 끌지는 마, 샬럿. 나 차 마시고 싶으니까.”\n\n그리하여 샬럿은 아버지에게로 내려갔다. 노인의 이마에는 아주 검은 구름이 드리워 있었다. 딸이 여태껏 보았던 기억이 있는 어떤 것보다도 검은 구름이었다. 그는 자기 안락의자에 앉아 있었으되, 난롯가에 편안히 자리 잡은 것이 아니라 방 한가운데에 의자를 놓고 앉아 딸이 와서 자기 말을 들을 때까지 기다리고 있었다.\n\n“네 동생은 어떻게 된 거냐?” 문이 닫히자마자 그가 말했다.\n\n“제가 오히려 여쭤봐야 할 것 같은데요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “저는 먼저 오면서 두 분을 울라손에 두고 왔잖아요. 볼드 부인은 어떻게 하셨어요?”\n\n“볼드 부인이라니! 말도 안 되는 소리. 그 부인은 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 집으로 돌아갔다. 그런 무정한 난봉꾼에게 제물로 바쳐지지 않았으니 나로서는 진심으로 다행한 일이지.”\n\n“어머, 아버지도!”\n\n“무정한 난봉꾼이고말고! 자, 그 애가 지금 어디 있고 앞으로 무얼 할 작정인지 말해 보아라. 내가 너희들 사이에서 놀아나도록 나 자신을 내버려 두었구나. 결혼이라니, 원! 잃을 돈이나 신용이나 세상의 존경을 조금이라도 지닌 사람이라면 대체 누가 그 애와 결혼하겠느냐?”\n\n“저를 나무라셔야 소용없어요, 아버지. 저는 그 애를 위해서도 아버지를 위해서도 할 수 있는 만큼은 다 했어요.”\n\n“마들린도 그 애 못지않게 못됐지.” 참사회원이 말했다. 그는 사실 몹시, 몹시 화가 나 있었다.\n\n“아, 저희는 다들 못된 것들이겠지요.” 샬럿이 대꾸했다.\n\n노인은 사자 같은 커다란 한숨을 내뱉었다. 그들이 모두 못되었다면, 그들을 그렇게 만든 것은 누구인가? 그들이 줏대 없고 이기적이고 볼썽사납다면, 그토록 해로운 결과를 낳은 그 교육의 책임은 누구에게 물어야 하는가?\n\n“너희가 한통속이 되어 나를 파산시키고 말 게다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 아버지, 그게 무슨 터무니없는 말씀이세요. 지금 이 순간에도 아버지는 수입 안에서 사시잖아요. 새 빚이 있다면 저는 모르는 일이고요. 새 빚이 있을 리도 없어요. 여기 사는 게 어지간히 심심하니까요.”\n\n“마들린의 그 청구서들은 다 치렀느냐?”\n\n“아뇨, 안 치렀어요. 그걸 누가 치를 수 있었겠어요?”\n\n“그 애 남편더러 치르라고 해라.”\n\n“남편이라뇨! 아버지가 그러셨다고 제가 그 애한테 전하기를 바라세요? 그 애를 이 집에서 내쫓고 싶으신 거예요?”\n\n“나는 그 애가 제 몸가짐을 좀 알았으면 싶을 뿐이다.”\n\n“아니, 그 애가 대체 이번엔 무얼 했다는 거예요? 가엾은 마들린! 이 고약한 도시에 온 뒤로 그 애가 바깥에 나간 것은 오늘로 겨우 두 번째예요.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 자기 결심을 어떤 형태로 선언할까 궁리하며 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. “그래서요, 아버지.” 샬럿이 말했다. “제가 여기 더 있을까요, 아니면 위층에 올라가 어머니 차 시중을 들어도 될까요?”\n\n“너는 네 동생이 속을 털어놓는 상대가 아니냐. 그 애가 무얼 할 작정인지 말해 보아라.”\n\n“제가 아는 한, 아무것도 하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“아무것도, 아무것도라니! 먹고 마시고, 손에 닿는 대로 내 돈을 한 실링까지 다 써 버리는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않겠지. 나는 마음을 정했다, 샬럿. 그 애는 이 집에서 더는 먹지도 마시지도 못한다.”\n\n“좋아요. 그렇다면 그 애는 이탈리아로 돌아가야겠네요.”\n\n“제가 가고 싶은 데로 가라지.”\n\n“말씀이야 쉽지요, 아버지. 그런데 그게 무슨 뜻이에요? 설마 그 애를…”\n\n“이런 뜻이다.” 평소보다 목소리를 높이고 눈에서 노기를 번뜩이며 박사가 말했다. “하나님이 하늘에서 다스리심이 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게, 나는 그 애를 더 이상 놀고먹도록 거두어 주지 않겠다는 뜻이다.”\n\n“아유, 하늘에서 다스리시기는요!” 샬럿이 말했다. “그런 이야기는 해 봐야 소용없어요. 아버지가 다스리셔야 할 곳은 여기 땅 위이고, 문제는 그것을 어떻게 하시느냐는 것이에요. 그 애를 한 푼도 없이 집 밖으로 내몰아 거리에서 구걸하게 하실 수는 없잖아요.”\n\n“구걸이야 제가 하고 싶은 데서 하라지.”\n\n“그 애는 카라라로 돌아가야 해요. 그 애가 살 수 있는 곳 가운데 값이 가장 싸고, 거기서는 아무도 그 애한테 200~300파울 넘게 외상을 주지 않을 테니까요. 하지만 갈 수 있는 노자만은 마련해 주셔야 해요.”\n\n“하늘에 맹세코…”\n\n“아유, 아버지, 맹세는 하지 마세요. 아버지도 그렇게 하셔야 한다는 걸 아시잖아요. 이 혼사가 성사되었더라면 그 애를 위해 200파운드를 내실 참이셨어요. 그 절반이면 그 애를 카라라로 떠나보낼 수 있어요.”\n\n“뭐라고? 그 애한테 100파운드를 주라고?”\n\n“저희는 아무것도 모르고 있잖아요, 아버지.” 화제를 돌리는 편이 낫겠다고 여기며 그녀가 말했다. “저희가 모르는 사이에 그 애가 지금 이 순간 볼드 부인과 약혼했을지도 모르는 일이고요.”\n\n“허튼소리.” 아버지가 말했다. 그는 아들이 손 한 번 내밀지도 않고 멀찍이 떨어져 서 있는 동안 볼드 부인이 마차에 오르던 모양을 보았던 것이다.\n\n“그럼 그 애는 카라라로 가야지요.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n바로 그때 현관문 자물쇠 소리가 났고, 샬럿의 밝은 귀는 복도에서 나는 동생의 고양이 같은 발소리를 알아챘다. 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 당분간은 버티가 아버지 눈에 띄지 않는 편이 낫다고 여긴 것이다. 그러나 스탠호프 박사에게도 자물쇠 소리는 들렸다.\n\n“누구냐?” 그가 캐물었다. 샬럿이 아무 대답도 하지 않자 그가 다시 물었다. “방금 들어온 게 누구냐? 문을 열어라. 누구야?”\n\n“버티겠지요.”\n\n“들어오라고 일러라.” 아버지가 말했다. 그러나 문 가까이에 있다가 그 부름을 들은 버티는 더 이를 것도 없이, 조금도 개의치 않는 쾌활한 낯빛으로 걸어 들어왔다. 스탠호프 박사를 아들의 낭비벽보다도 더 화나게 하는 것이 바로 이 독특한 insouciance였다.\n\n“그래, 어떻게 된 거냐?” 박사가 말했다.\n\n“그런데 아버지, 그 아리따운 동행분과는 어떻게 돌아오셨어요?” 버티가 말했다. “설마 위층에 계시지는 않겠지, 샬럿?”\n\n“버티.” 샬럿이 말했다. “아버지는 농담을 받으실 기분이 아니야. 너한테 몹시 화가 나셨어.”\n\n“화가 나셨다고!” 여태껏 부모에게 단 한순간의 불편함도 끼친 적이 없다는 듯 눈썹을 치켜올리며 버티가 말했다.\n\n“앉거라.” 스탠호프 박사가 매우 준엄하게, 그러나 이번에는 그리 큰 소리가 아니게 말했다. “샬럿, 너도 좀 앉아 주었으면 좋겠구나. 네 어미는 차를 몇 분쯤 더 기다려도 된다.”\n\n샬럿은 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 어딘가 심사가 뒤틀린 태도로 앉았다. 이렇게 말하는 것이나 다름없는 태도였다. “자, 앉았어요. 시키시는 대로 하지 않는다는 말씀은 못 하시겠지요. 하지만 제가 아버지께 굽히고 들어간다면 매를 맞아도 좋아요.” 실제로 그녀는 굽히지 않기로 작정하고 있었다. 그녀 역시 버티에게 화가 나 있었지만, 그렇다고 해서 아버지로부터 그를 감싸 줄 태세가 조금이라도 덜한 것은 아니었다. 버티도 자리에 앉았다. 그는 의자를 서재 탁자 가까이 끌어다 놓고 탁자 위에 팔꿈치를 올린 다음, 한 손에 얼굴을 편안히 괴고 다른 손으로 종이 한 장에 자잘한 그림들을 그리기 시작했다. 이 장면이 끝나기 전에 그는 손 양과 프라우디 부인과 레이디 드 코시의 훌륭한 초상을 완성했고, 룩어로프트 일가 전원을 담을 가족화까지 시작해 놓았다.\n\n“네 지금 속셈이 무엇인지 나에게 조금이나마 일러 줄 마음이 있느냐?” 아버지가 말했다. “앞으로 어떤 방식으로 살아갈 작정인지 말이다.”\n\n“아버지가 무엇을 이르시든 그대로 하겠어요.” 버티가 대답했다.\n\n“아니, 나는 이제 아무것도 이르지 않겠다. 내가 이를 때는 이미 지나갔어. 내릴 명령은 하나뿐이다. 내 집에서 나가라는 것이다.”\n\n“오늘 밤에요?” 버티가 말했다. 그 물음의 담백한 어조 앞에서 박사는 위엄을 갖추어 대답할 방도를 찾지 못했다.\n\n“아버지가 꼭 오늘 밤을 말씀하시는 건 아니야.” 샬럿이 말했다. “적어도 내 생각엔 그래.”\n\n“그럼 내일쯤일까요.” 버티가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n“그래, 내일이다.” 박사가 말했다. “내일 이 집을 나가야 한다.”\n\n“알겠어요, 아버지. 오후 4시 30분 기차면 충분히 이르겠지요?” 그렇게 물으면서 버티는 손 양의 굽 높은 장화에 마지막 손질을 넣었다.\n\n“내일 내 집을 나가기만 한다면 어떻게 언제 어디로 가든 네 마음대로 해라. 너는 나를 욕보였다. 너 자신을, 나를, 그리고 네 누이들을 욕보였어.”\n\n“적어도 어머니는 욕보이지 않았으니 다행이에요, 아버지.” 버티가 말했다.\n\n샬럿은 웃음을 참느라 얼굴을 가누기가 어려웠으나 박사의 이마는 전보다 한층 더 검어졌다. 버티는 프라우디 부인의 코와 입을 그려 내며 자신의 chef d’oeuvre를 완성하는 중이었다.\n\n“너는 무정한 난봉꾼이다. 무정하고 은혜도 모르고 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 난봉꾼이야. 나는 너와는 끝이다. 네가 내 아들인 것이야 어쩔 수 없다만, 이제 너는 내 자식으로서 나와 아무 상관이 없고, 나도 네 아비로서 너와 아무 상관이 없다.”\n\n“어머, 아버지, 아버지! 그런 말씀 하시면 안 돼요, 하셔서도 안 되고요.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“하겠다, 그리고 지금 하고 있다.” 아버지가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. “자, 이제 방에서 나가거라.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 잠깐만요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “버티, 왜 말을 안 해? 왜 고개를 들고 말을 안 하느냐고. 아버지를 저렇게 화나게 만드는 건 바로 네 그 태도야.”\n\n“저 애는 예의도 체면도 조금도 아랑곳하지 않는구나.” 박사가 말했다. 그러고는 소리를 질렀다. “방에서 나가! 내 말이 들리지 않느냐?”\n\n“아버지, 아버지, 이렇게 갈라서시게 두지는 않겠어요. 아버지가 나중에 후회하실 걸 저는 알아요.” 그러고는 자리에서 일어나 아버지의 귀에 대고 속삭이며 덧붙였다. “잘못이 저 애한테만 있을까요? 그것을 생각해 보세요. 우리 잠자리는 우리가 편 것이고, 그것이 어떤 자리든 우리는 그 위에 누워야 해요. 우리끼리 다투어 봐야 아무 소용이 없어요.” 그녀가 속삭임을 마쳤을 때 버티는 백작부인의 허리받이를 마무리했는데, 어찌나 잘 그렸던지 종이 위에서 그것이 평소의 그 좌우 흔들림으로 실제로 살랑거리는 듯이 보였다.\n\n“아버지가 지금 화가 나신 건.” 스케치에서 잠깐 눈을 들며 버티가 말했다. “제가 볼드 부인과 결혼하지 않을 것이기 때문이에요. 그 일에 대해 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요? 제가 그분과 결혼하지 않으리라는 건 사실이에요. 우선 첫째로…”\n\n“그것은 사실이 아니다.” 스탠호프 박사가 말했다. “허나 너와 입씨름을 하지는 않겠다.”\n\n“방금 전에는 제가 말을 하지 않는다고 화를 내셨잖아요.” 어린 룩어로프트를 계속 그려 나가며 버티가 말했다.\n\n“그림은 그만 그려.” 샬럿이 그에게 다가가 손 밑에서 종이를 빼내며 말했다. 그러나 그 풍자화들은 잘 간직해 두었다가 나중에 손 씨네와 프라우디 씨네와 드 코시 씨네의 지인들에게 보여 주었다. 소일거리를 빼앗긴 버티는 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 다음 분부를 기다렸다.\n\n“버티가 곧바로, 어쩌면 내일이라도 이곳을 떠나는 편이 가장 좋으리라는 데는 저도 분명 동의해요.” 샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 아버지, 부디 우리 함께 무슨 방도를 마련하기로 해요.”\n\n“내일 이곳을 떠난다면 10파운드를 주겠다. 그리고 그곳에 눌러 있는 동안에는 카라라의 은행가를 통해 다달이 5파운드씩 지급하도록 하마.”\n\n“그럼 오래가지는 않겠네요, 아버지.” 버티가 말했다. “석 달쯤이면 굶어 죽을 테니까요.”\n\n“작업할 대리석은 있어야지요.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“석 달 버틸 만큼은 그곳 작업실에 넉넉히 있어.” 버티가 말했다. “그렇게 한정된 기간에 큰 작품을 시도해 봐야 소용없겠지. 내 묘비를 만드는 것이라면 몰라도.”\n\n그러나 결국 처음 제시된 것보다는 얼마간 후한 조건으로 합의가 이루어졌고, 박사는 아들과 악수를 하고 잘 자라는 인사를 건네기에 이르렀다. 스탠호프 박사는 차를 마시러 위층에 올라가지 않고, 딸에게 서재로 차를 가져오게 했다.\n\n그러나 버티는 위층으로 올라가 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈다. 그는 룩어로프트 일가의 그림을 완성해 누이들을 크게 즐겁게 했는데, 그들의 décolleté 드레스를 묘사한 방식은 그리 고상하다고 할 수 없었다. 일이 어떻게 되어 가는지를 알아차린 그는 자기가 그 미망인에게 그리 다급하게 구애를 밀어붙이지는 않았다는 사실을 조금씩 흘려 놓았다.\n\n“실은 청혼이란 걸 아예 하지도 않은 거지?” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“아, 원하기만 하면 나를 가질 수 있다는 건 그분도 알아들었어.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그런데 원하지 않았던 거고.” 시뇨라가 말했다.\n\n“너는 참으로 창피스러운 방식으로 나를 저버렸어.” 샬럿이 말했다. “내 자그마한 계획도 죄다 그분한테 말해 버렸겠지?”\n\n“뭐, 어쩌다 보니 나와 버렸어. 적어도 그 대부분은 말이야.”\n\n“그 혼담도 이걸로 끝이네.” 샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 그리 대수로울 것도 없지. 우리 모두 머잖아 코메 호수로 돌아가게 될 테니까.”\n\n“정말이지 나도 그러기를 바라.” 시뇨라가 말했다. “검은 상의는 이제 보기만 해도 신물이 나. 그 슬로프 씨가 여기 한 번만 더 오면 나는 그이 때문에 죽고 말 거야.”\n\n“내가 보기엔 네가 그이를 망쳐 놓았지 싶은데.” 샬럿이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 검은 상의를 입은 내 두 번째 연인으로 말하자면, 나는 그이를 지극히 유별난 무욕으로 다른 부인께 선물해 드릴 참이야.”\n\n이튿날 버티는 약속대로 짐을 꾸려 오후 4시 30분 기차로 떠났다. 주머니에 20파운드를 넣고 카라라의 대리석 채석장을 향해서였다. 그리하여 그는 우리 무대에서 사라진다.\n\n버티가 떠난 다음 날 열두 시에, 볼드 부인 역시 자기 말대로 겁먹은 손과 두근거리는 가슴으로 스탠호프 박사 댁 문을 두드렸다. 그녀는 곧바로 뒤쪽 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그 접이문이 닫혀 있었으므로 시뇨라를 찾아온 엘리너가 앞쪽 방에 있는 사람들과 굳이 얼굴을 맞대야 할 일은 없었다. 계단을 올라가는 동안 그녀는 식구 가운데 아무도 마주치지 않았고, 그만큼 그녀가 두려워하던 곤혹스러움을 많이 면할 수 있었다.\n\n“참으로 고마워요, 볼드 부인. 그런 일이 있고 나서도 이렇게 와 주시다니 참으로 고마워요.” 소파에 누운 부인이 더없이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n“그런 어조로 편지를 쓰셨으니 제가 오지 않을 수가 없었어요.”\n\n“그랬지요, 그랬어요. 억지로라도 나를 만나시게 하고 싶었으니까요.”\n\n“그래서 이렇게 왔어요, 시뇨라.”\n\n“어쩌면 그렇게 나에게 쌀쌀맞으실까. 하기야 그건 내가 참아야겠지요. 우리 식구 모두에게 언짢아할 까닭이 있다고 여기시는 걸 나도 알아요. 가엾은 버티. 사정을 다 아신다면 그 애한테 화를 내지는 않으실 텐데.”\n\n“저는 동생분께 화가 나지 않았어요. 조금도요. 하지만 그 이야기를 하시려고 저를 부르신 것은 아니기를 바라요.”\n\n“샬럿에게 화가 나셨다면 그것이 더 나쁜 일이에요. 바체스터를 통틀어 그보다 더 따뜻한 벗은 부인께 없으니까요. 하지만 이 이야기를 하려고 부른 건 아니에요. 볼드 부인, 부디 의자를 좀 더 가까이 끌어당겨서 내가 부인을 볼 수 있게 해 주세요. 그렇게 멀찍이 떨어져 계시는 걸 보고 있자니 어쩐지 부자연스러워요.”\n\n엘리너는 시키는 대로 의자를 소파 가까이 끌어다 놓았다.\n\n“자, 볼드 부인, 이제 나는 어쩌면 부인이 무례하다고 여기실 만한 이야기를 하려고 해요. 그래도 이렇게 하는 것이 옳다는 걸 나는 알고 있어요.”\n\n이 말에 볼드 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 의자에 앉은 채 몸이 떨릴 것만 같았다. 시뇨라가 그리 가리는 것이 없는 사람임을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 시뇨라에게 무례한 정도로 보이는 것이 볼드 부인에게는 지극히 상스러운 것으로 보일 수도 있었다.\n\n“애러빈 씨를 아시지요?”\n\n볼드 부인은 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수만 있다면 온 세상이라도 내주었을 테지만, 피는 그녀의 뜻대로 움직여 주지 않았다. 그녀는 이마까지 붉어졌고, 그 모습을 살피려고 일부러 빛이 특정하게 드는 자리에 그녀를 앉혀 놓았던 시뇨라는 그것을 놓치지 않고 보았다.\n\n“네, 아는 사이예요. 그러니까 조금 아는 정도지요. 그분은 그랜틀리 박사님과 절친한 벗이고, 그랜틀리 박사님은 제 형부시니까요.”\n\n“그러면, 애러빈 씨를 아신다면 분명 그분을 좋아하실 거예요. 나도 그분을 알고, 아주 좋아하고 있어요. 그분을 아는 사람이라면 누구든 좋아하지 않을 수 없지요.”\n\n볼드 부인은 여기에 무어라 대답한다는 것이 도무지 불가능하게 느껴졌다. 어찌 된 영문인지도 모르는 채 피가 온몸을 내달렸다. 그녀는 자기가 의자에 앉은 채 흔들리고 있는 것만 같았고, 얼굴이 붉어졌을 뿐 아니라 열기에 숨이 막힐 지경이라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 가만히 앉아 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“나에게 어쩌면 그렇게 뻣뻣하실까요, 볼드 부인.” 시뇨라가 말했다. “그러는 동안 나는 한 여자가 다른 여자를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일을 모조리 해 드리고 있는데요.”\n\n어쩌면 시뇨라의 우정이 진짜일지도 모르고, 어쨌든 그것이 자기에게 해가 될 리는 없으리라는 생각이 미망인의 마음을 스쳤다. 그리고 또 하나의 생각, 어렴풋이 밝아 오는 생각도 함께 떠올랐다. 애러빈 씨는 잃어버리기에는 너무나 귀한 사람이라는 생각이었다. 그녀는 시뇨라를 경멸했다. 그러나 이기기 위해서 조금 몸을 굽히지 못할 것도 없지 않은가? 굽히더라도 아주 지극히 조금이면 될 터였다!\n\n“뻣뻣하게 굴 생각은 없어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 물으시는 것이 하도 유별나서요.”\n\n“그렇다면 더 유별난 것을 하나 더 물어보지요.” 마들린 네로니가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키고 자기 얼굴을 상대의 얼굴 쪽으로 온전히 돌리며 말했다. “그분을 사랑하시나요? 온 마음과 온 영혼을 다해, 가슴이 느낄 수 있는 온 사랑을 다해 그분을 사랑하시나요? 그분이 부인을 사랑하고, 흠모하고, 떠받들고, 부인 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않는다고 내가 말씀드릴 수 있으니 하는 말이에요. 다음 주일 설교를 쓰려고 애쓰고 있는 지금 이 순간에도 그분은 부인을 생각하고 있어요. 그런 남자에게 그런 식으로 사랑받을 수만 있다면 나는 무엇인들 내주지 않겠어요. 하기야 내가 어느 남자에게든 사랑받기에 어울리는 존재이기라도 하다면 말이지만!”\n\n볼드 부인은 자리에서 일어나, 이렇게 격정적인 어조로 자기에게 말을 건네는 여인 앞에 아무 말도 못 한 채 서 있었다. 시뇨라가 그렇게 자기 자신을 빗대어 말하자 미망인의 마음은 누그러졌고, 그녀는 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 상대의 손 위에 쓰다듬듯이 자기 손을 얹었다. 시뇨라는 그 손을 붙들고 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n“내가 하는 말은 하나님의 진리 그대로예요. 그것을 부인 자신의 행복에 가장 이롭도록 쓰는 것은 부인의 몫이고요. 다만 나를 발설해서는 안 돼요. 그분은 이 일을 전혀 몰라요. 내가 그분의 속마음을 다 알고 있다는 것도 전혀 모르지요. 이런 일에서 그분은 어린아이처럼 단순해요. 그분은 시치미를 뗄 줄 몰라서 천 가지 방식으로 자기 비밀을 나에게 말해 버렸지만, 자기가 그것을 말했다는 것은 꿈에도 모르고 있어요. 이제 부인이 그것을 아셨으니, 그것을 쓰시라고 나는 권해요.”\n\n엘리너는 상대가 쥔 손에, 지극히 미세한 soupçon만큼 힘을 주어 답했다.\n\n“그리고 기억해 두세요.” 시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “그분은 다른 남자들과 달라요. 그분이 맹세와 서약과 예쁜 선물을 들고 부인께 와서 발치에 무릎 꿇고 신발 끈에 입 맞추리라고 기대해서는 안 돼요. 그런 것을 원하신다면 그리해 줄 사람은 얼마든지 있지만, 그분은 그런 사람이 아니에요.” 엘리너의 가슴은 한숨으로 터질 듯했으나, 마들린은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다. “그분에게는 예가 예이고, 아니오가 아니오예요. 설령 그 때문에 심장이 부서진다 해도, 한 번 그분을 물리친 여자는 영영 그분을 물리친 것이 되고 말지요. 그것을 기억해 두세요. 자, 볼드 부인, 마음이 설레실 테니 붙들어 두지는 않겠어요. 내가 드린 말씀을 부인이 어떻게 쓰실지는 어렴풋이 짐작이 가요. 언젠가 그분의 집에서 행복한 아내가 되신다면, 그때 우리는 멀리 떠나 있겠지만, 이 집안의 죄를 용서했노라고 한 줄만 적어 보내 주시기를 나는 기다리고 있겠어요.”\n\n엘리너는 그러겠다고 반쯤 속삭이듯 대답하고는, 더 한마디도 하지 않은 채 방을 빠져나와 계단을 내려갔고, 아무도 보지 못하고 아무 소리도 듣지 못한 채 스스로 현관문을 열고 나와 어느새 대성당 경내에 서 있었다.\n\n집으로 걸어가는 동안 엘리너의 심정을 헤아려 분석하기란 어려운 일이었을 것이다. 그녀는 자기에게 건네진 말들에 거의 넋이 나가 있었다. 자기 마음이 거의 남이나 다름없는 사람에게, 그것도 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없고 앞으로도 좋아할 수 없을 여자에게 그토록 샅샅이 뒤져지고 파헤쳐졌다는 것이 쓰라렸다. 스스로 사랑한다고 인정하는 그 남자가 자기에게는 사랑을 감추고 다른 여자에게 그것을 내보였다는 것도 분했다. 그녀의 자존심을 상하게 할 것이 많았다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 모든 것 밑에는 기쁨의 지층이 하나 깔려 있어서 그녀를 신기하리만큼 떠받쳐 주었다. 그녀는 마담 네로니가 한 말을 믿지 않을 수 있을지 시험해 보았으나, 그럴 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 그것은 사실이었다. 사실일 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 그것을 의심할 수 없었고, 의심하려 하지도 않았고, 의심하지도 않았다.\n\n한 가지 점에서 그녀는 자기에게 주어진 충고를 그대로 따르기로 온전히 결심했다. 만일 언젠가 애러빈 씨가 그 암시된 것과 같은 물음을 그녀에게 던져 주기를 마음먹는다면, 그녀의 “예”는 “예”가 될 터였다. 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 자기의 온갖 괴로움을 그에게 툭 터놓고 이야기할 수 있게 된다면, 그 괴로움이 죄다 끝나 버리지 않겠는가?\n\n# 제46장. 슬로프 씨가 시뇨라와 마지막으로 마주 앉다\n\n이튿날 시뇨라는 한껏 우쭐해 있었다. 그녀는 가진 것 가운데 가장 화사한 아침 옷을 차려입고서, 제 소파 둘레에 그야말로 접견회를 벌여 놓고 있었다. 아름답고 화창한 10월의 오후였다. 인근의 신사라는 신사는 죄다 바체스터에 있었고, 그 가운데 스탠호프 박사 집에 드나들 자격이 있는 이들은 시뇨라의 안쪽 응접실에 있었다. 샬럿과 스탠호프 부인은 앞쪽 방에 있었고, 이 부인을 모시는 기사들 가운데 당장은 관심의 중심에 다가가지 못한 이들은 그 어머니와 언니를 상대로 제 향기를 허비해야만 했다.\n\n가장 먼저 와서 가장 늦게까지 남은 것은 애러빈 씨였다. 울라손에서 그녀를 만난 이래 그가 마담 네로니를 찾은 것은 이번이 두 번째였다. 그는 왜인지도 모르는 채 찾아와서, 무엇인지도 모를 것에 관하여 이야기했다. 그러나 실상 지금 그를 어지럽히는 감정은 그에게 낯선 것이었고, 그는 그것을 헤아려 볼 수가 없었다. 볼드 부인을 사랑하기 때문에 마담 네로니의 주위를 이렇게 맴돈다는 것이 이상하게 보일지도 모르나, 그럼에도 그것은 사실이었다. 그리고 그는 제가 어째서 그러는지 알지 못했으되, 마담 네로니는 그것을 넉넉히 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 그에게 다정하고 상냥하게 굴며 더 머물기를 부추겼다. 그래서 그는 그대로 머물렀다. 그가 처음 인사를 건넬 때 그녀는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그녀는 그를 제 곁에 붙들어 두고 아무것도 아닌 말들을 속삭였다. 그리고 그 눈, 광채가 돌고 빛나는, 어느 때는 즐겁고 어느 때는 우울하며, 어느 쪽이든 당해 낼 도리가 없는 그 눈! 따뜻한 감정을 지니고, 피가 아직 식지 않았으며, 심장이 세 겹 강철 같은 경험으로 지켜지지 않은 사내라면 그 눈을 어찌 견뎌 낼 수 있었겠는가! 하기야 이 부인은 그에게 치명적인 상처를 입힐 뜻은 없었다. 그저 보석함을 다른 이에게 넘겨주기 전에 향내를 한 줄기 살짝 들이마시기로 했을 뿐이었다. 볼드 부인이 그만한 것이나마 기꺼이 내주었을지는 또 다른 문제다.\n\n그리고 이어서 슬로프 씨가 왔다. 이제 온 세상이 슬로프 씨가 수석 사제 자리의 후보이며 대체로 가장 유력한 사람으로 여겨진다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리하여 슬로프 씨는 땅을 제법 넓게 밟고 다녔다. 그는 수석 사제에게나 어울릴 법한 위엄 있는 태도를 스스로에게 부여했고, 다른 성직자들에게는 말을 별로 걸지 않았으며, 주교는 될 수 있는 대로 피했다. 『주피터』의 그 기사를 읽거나 전해 들었을 때, 그 빼빼 마른 자그마한 참사회원과 우람한 학무 참사원과 모든 하급 참사원과 성가대 사제들, 그렇다, 성가대원들까지도 어찌나 움츠러들고 떨며 긴 얼굴을 하고 돌아다녔던지. 이제 그 부정한 영을 대성당 설교단에서 막아 낼 길이 아무것도 없는 날들이 오고 있었다. 그 설교단은 참으로 그의 것이 될 터였다. 선창자도 사제도 성가대원도 제 수금을 버드나무에 걸어 두는 수밖에 없었다. 이가봇! 이가봇! 그 집의 영광이 그들에게서 떠나고 있었다.\n\n슬로프 씨는 아직 껍질을 벗지 않은 위엄으로 그토록 대단해졌으면서도 여전히 시뇨라를 보러 왔다. 아니, 그는 스스로를 떼어 놓을 수가 없었다. 그는 그토록 자주 입 맞추었던 그 부드러운 손을 꿈꾸었고, 제 입술이 한 번 눌러 보았던 그 제왕 같은 이마를 꿈꾸었으며, 그러고는 더 나아간 은총까지도 꿈꾸었다.\n\n그리고 손 씨 또한 그 자리에 있었다. 그가 시뇨라를 찾은 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 그는 마땅한 채비 없이 그 걸음을 하지는 않았다. 손 씨는 평소에도 옷차림이 꼼꼼하고, 나서지 않는 방식으로 제 몸을 최대한 돋보이게 하는 데 마음을 쓰는 신사였다. 구레나룻의 센 털은 아마도 한 달에 한 번쯤 뽑혀 나갔고, 머리의 센 털은 우리가 염색약이라고는 부르지 않을 어떤 혼합물로 누그러뜨렸으니 — 그것은 그저 물약이었을 뿐이다. 그의 재봉사는 세인트 제임스 스트리트에 살았고, 구두장이는 그 거리와 피커딜리가 만나는 모퉁이에 살았다. 그는 장갑이라는 품목에 까다로웠고, 그의 셔츠를 매만지는 일은 울라손의 세탁실에서 가볍게 여겨지는 일이 아니었다. 이번 방문에 즈음하여 그는 평소의 공을 다소 지나치게 들였고, 그 바람에 누이가 적잖이 불안해졌으니, 누이는 시뇨라를 울라손에 오래 묵게 하자는 제안을 여태 그다지 살갑게 받아들이지 않고 있던 터였다.\n\n그 자리에는 다른 이들도 있었다 — 딱히 할 일이 많지 않은 데다 이 부인의 매력에 이끌려 그 얼마 안 되는 일마저 팽개친 시내의 젊은이들이었다 — 그러나 모두가 손 씨에게 자리를 내주었으니, 그는 지방 도시에서 시골 신사가 늘 그러하듯 일종의 대감 같은 인물이었기 때문이다.\n\n“어머, 손 씨, 이렇게 와 주시다니 참으로 고마우셔라!” 시뇨라가 말했다. “오시겠다고 약속은 하셨지만 정말로 기대하지는 않았답니다. 시골 신사분들은 약속이란 걸 지키는 법이 없다고 여겼거든요.”\n\n“아, 그야 더러는 지키지요.” 손 씨가 다소 수줍은 낯빛으로, 인사하는 몸짓을 지난 세기의 투로 조금 지나치게 차리며 말했다.\n\n“속이시는 건 오직 당신네 유권—권—권—, 아니, 당신을 의회 의원으로 만들어 줄 때 의자에 태워 메고 다니며 달걀이며 사과를 던지는 그 사람들, 그 사람들을 뭐라고 부르던가요? 그 사람들뿐이겠지요.”\n\n“저희끼리도 더러 속이지요, 시뇨라.” 슬로프 씨가 자못 수석 사제다운 히죽거림을 얼굴에 띠고 말했다. “시골 신사들은 저희끼리도 더러 속이지 않습니까, 손 씨?”\n\n손 씨는 그에게 눈길을 한 번 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 잠시나마 그에게서 수석 사제다움을 깡그리 벗겨 버렸다. 그러나 그는 곧 제 드높은 희망을 떠올렸고, 얼른 정신을 추슬러 손 씨를 비웃는 웃음으로 머지않아 다가올 제 위엄을 떠받쳤다.\n\n“저는 어쨌거나 숙녀를 속이는 일은 없습니다.” 손 씨가 말했다. “더욱이 제 소원을 이루는 것이 지금처럼 저를 진실하게 붙들어 두는 강한 유인이 될 때에는 말이지요.”\n\n손 씨는 한동안 그런 식으로 『찰스 그랜디슨 경』에서 주워 온 태곳적 표정과 인사말을 이어 갔고, 시뇨라는 그 표정 하나마다 그 절 하나마다 작은 미소를 짓고 작은 목례를 하였다. 그러나 손 씨는 소파 발치에 선 채로 있어야 했으니, 탁자 곁의 상석에는 새 수석 사제가 앉아 있었기 때문이다. 그동안 애러빈 씨는 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 연미복 자락을 팔 밑에 끼운 채 온 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었는데 — 아주 헛된 일만은 아니었으니, 이따금 하늘에서 떨어지는 유성처럼 밝은 눈길 하나가 그에게로 올라왔기 때문이다.\n\n“참, 손 씨, 제 어린 딸아이를 소개해 드리게 해 주마고 약속하셨지요. 잠깐 짬을 내 주시겠어요 — 지금 그 아이를 보시겠어요?”\n\n손 씨는 세상에 더없는 기쁨으로 그 어린 아가씨를 볼 수 있고 또 보겠노라고 다짐했다. “슬로프 씨, 수고스럽지만 종을 좀 울려 주시겠어요?” 그녀가 말했고, 슬로프 씨가 일어서자 그녀는 손 씨를 바라보며 그 의자를 가리켰다. 그러나 손 씨는 그 뜻을 알아채기에는 너무나 굼떴고, 결코 실패하기를 택하는 법이 없는 시뇨라가 다소 대뜸 그를 그 자리에서 내보내지 않았더라면 슬로프 씨는 제 자리를 도로 차지했을 것이었다.\n\n“아, 슬로프 씨, 잠깐만, 아주 잠깐만 손 씨더러 여기 앉으시라고 해야겠어요. 너그러이 봐주시리라 믿어요. 이번 주까지는 당신께 이만한 무례를 부려도 되겠지요. 다음 주에, 아시다시피 수석사제관으로 옮겨 가시고 나면 우리 모두 당신을 두려워하게 될 테니까요.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 자못 무심한 태도로 자리에서 일어나 옆방으로 걸어가더니 스탠호프 부인의 털실 자수에 크나큰 관심을 보이기 시작했다.\n\n그러고 나서 아이가 들려 들어왔다. 여덟 살쯤 된 계집아이로, 제 어미를 닮았으되 그 커다란 눈은 검고 머리카락은 아주 새까맸다. 살빛 또한 매우 짙어 이국의 피를 드러냈다. 아이는 어린아이의 몸에 옷을 걸칠 수 있는 방식 가운데 가장 괴상하고 가장 야단스러운 차림을 하고 있었다. 맨살이 드러난 자그마한 팔에는 커다란 팔찌를 끼웠고, 머리에는 금실을 섞어 땋은 진홍색 머리띠를 둘렀으며, 굽 높은 다홍색 신을 신었다. 옷은 온통 주름 장식이어서, 마치 그 작은 엉덩이에서 옷자락이 수평으로 뻗어 나가게 하는 것이 목적이기라도 한 양 몸에서 툭 불거져 있었다. 옷은 무릎을 채 덮지 못했으나, 그 대신 온통 레이스로만 지은 듯한 헐렁한 속바지가 그것을 메워 주었다. 게다가 아이는 분홍빛 비단 양말을 신고 있었다. 네로 가문의 마지막 후손은 손님이 찾아오기 마련인 시각이면 늘 이렇게 차려입혀졌다.\n\n“줄리아, 아가.” 어미가 말했다 — 줄리아는 그 집안 여자들에게 예로부터 늘 사랑받아 온 이름이었다. “줄리아, 아가, 이리 오렴. 가엾은 엄마가 갔던 아름다운 잔치 이야기를 해 주고 있었지. 이분이 손 씨란다. 아가야, 이분께 입 맞춰 드리겠니?”\n\n줄리아는 제 어미의 손님 모두에게 그리하듯 얼굴을 들어 입맞춤을 받았고, 그러자 손 씨는 아이를, 그리고 그에게 훨씬 더 끔찍하게도 그 아이의 온갖 치장을 제 팔에 안게 되었음을 깨달았다. 레이스와 풀 먹인 천이 그의 조끼와 바지에 눌려 구겨졌고, 기름진 검은 곱슬머리가 그의 뺨에 늘어졌으며, 팔찌 고리 하나가 그의 귀를 긁었다. 그는 그토록 대단한 숙녀를 어떻게 안아야 할지도, 안은 다음 그 숙녀를 어찌해야 할지도 도무지 알지 못했다. 그러나 그도 다른 자리에서 어린 조카들을 어르지 않을 수 없었던 적이 있었으므로, 이제 늘 쓰던 방식으로 그 일에 착수했다.\n\n“디들, 디들, 디들, 디들.” 그가 아이를 한쪽 무릎에 앉히고, 마치 칼갈이의 숫돌 바퀴를 발로 돌리기라도 하듯 아이를 흔들어 대며 말했다.\n\n“엄마, 엄마.” 줄리아가 짜증스레 말했다. “나 디들디들 당하기 싫어. 놔줘요, 이 못된 늙은 아저씨.”\n\n가엾은 손 씨는 아이를 조용히 바닥에 내려놓고 제 의자를 뒤로 물렸다. 저를 끌어당기는 북극성 곁으로 돌아와 있던 슬로프 씨는 소리 내어 웃었다. 애러빈 씨는 움찔하며 눈을 감았다. 그리고 시뇨라는 제 딸의 말을 못 들은 척했다.\n\n“샬럿 이모께 가 보렴, 아가.” 어미가 말했다. “이모께 이제 나갈 시간이 되지 않았는지 여쭈어 보고.”\n\n그러나 어린 줄리아 양은 손 씨가 보인 관심의 성격이 딱히 마음에 들지는 않았으되 신사들에게 놀림받는 데 익숙한 터라, 이렇게 빨리 이모에게 보내진다는 생각이 달갑지 않았다.\n\n“줄리아, 가라고 하면 가야지, 아가.” 그래도 줄리아는 여전히 입을 삐죽인 채 방 안을 돌아다녔다. “샬럿, 와서 이 아이 좀 데려가.” 시뇨라가 말했다. “나가야 하는데 요새는 해가 어찌나 짧은지.” 그리하여 그토록 말이 많던 손 씨와 네로 가문의 마지막 후손 사이의 면담은 끝이 났다.\n\n손 씨는 아이의 짜증에서보다 슬로프 씨의 웃음에서 더 늦게 회복되었다. 그는 젖먹이에게 늙은이 소리를 듣는 것쯤은 참을 수 있었으나, 주교의 전속 사제에게 비웃음을 사는 것은, 설령 그 전속 사제가 곧 수석 사제가 될 참이라 하더라도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 화가 났다는 것만은 넉넉히 드러내 보였다.\n\n시뇨라는 기꺼이 그의 원수를 갚아 줄 참이었다. “슬로프 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신이 사방에서 개가를 올리고 계시다고 들었어요.”\n\n“어째서 그런 말씀을?” 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그는 수석 사제직에 관한 이야기를 듣는 것이 싫지 않았으나, 물론 그런 혐의는 강하게 부인했다.\n\n“사랑에서도 전쟁에서도 이기고 계시잖아요.” 이 말에 슬로프 씨는 아까만큼 흡족한 낯빛은 아니게 되었다.\n\n“애러빈 씨.” 시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “슬로프 씨는 참으로 운 좋은 분이라고 생각지 않으세요?”\n\n“그가 마땅히 누릴 만한 것 이상은 아니라고 나는 믿소.” 애러빈 씨가 말했다.\n\n“생각 좀 해 보세요, 손 씨. 이분이 우리의 새 수석 사제가 되신답니다. 물론 우리야 다들 아는 일이지만요.”\n\n“원, 시뇨라.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “우리 모두 그런 일은 전혀 알지 못합니다. 저 자신도 정말이지 장담하건대—”\n\n“이분이 새 수석 사제가 되실 겁니다 — 그 점은 조금도 의심할 여지가 없어요, 손 씨.”\n\n“흠!” 손 씨가 말했다.\n\n“제 아버지나 그랜틀리 부주교 같은 늙은 어른들의 머리 위를 훌쩍 넘어서 말이지요—”\n\n“원, 원!” 슬로프 씨가 말했다.\n\n“부주교라면 그 자리를 받지 않을 것이오.” 애러빈 씨가 말했고, 그러자 슬로프 씨는 밉살스럽게 미소 지으며, 눈빛만으로 말할 수 있는 한 또렷하게 저 포도는 실 것이라고 말했다.\n\n“우리 모두의 머리 위를 넘어서 말이에요.” 시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “저야 물론 스스로를 참사회의 한 사람으로 여기고 있으니까요.”\n\n“제가 언젠가 수석 사제가 된다면,” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “아니, 설령 제가 그리된다 한들, 저는 그런 여참사원을 모시는 것을 영광으로 알겠습니다.”\n\n“어머, 슬로프 씨, 잠깐만요. 아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았답니다. 당신이 영광으로 알 여참사원이 하나 더 있으니까요. 슬로프 씨는 수석사제관만 얻는 것이 아니라 그 안에 들여앉힐 아내까지 얻으신답니다.”\n\n슬로프 씨는 또다시 당황한 낯빛이었다.\n\n“게다가 큰 재산을 지닌 아내지요. 비가 왔다 하면 퍼붓는 법이지요, 그렇지 않나요, 손 씨?”\n\n“예, 결코 그러는 법이 없지요.” 손 씨가 말했으나, 그는 슬로프 씨와 그 신상에 관해 이야기하는 것이 그다지 달갑지 않았다.\n\n“그게 언제쯤이 될까요, 슬로프 씨?”\n\n“무엇이 언제쯤이라는 말씀입니까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아, 수석 사제 일이야 언제일지 우리도 알지요. 일주일이면 결판이 날 테고요. 새 모자도 벌써 맞추어 놓으셨을 테지요, 그건 의심할 것도 없어요. 그런데 혼인은 언제 치르시나요?”\n\n“제 것 말씀입니까, 아니면 애러빈 씨 것 말씀입니까?” 그가 익살을 부려 보려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 방금은 당신 것을 두고 한 말이었지만, 어쩌면 결국 애러빈 씨 쪽이 먼저일지도 모르지요. 그렇지만 우리는 저분에 대해서는 아무것도 모른답니다. 저분은 우리 누구에게도 너무 입이 무거우세요. 그런데 당신은 모든 것이 훤히 드러나 있고 떳떳하지요 — 그런데 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 애러빈 씨, 나는 그편이 훨씬 낫다고 말해 두고 싶소. 슬로프 씨가 사랑받는 구혼자라는 것쯤은 달려가면서도 읽을 수 있는 일이지요. 자, 슬로프 씨, 그 미망인은 언제 수석 사제 부인이 되나요?”\n\n애러빈 씨에게 이 희롱은 유독 괴로웠고, 그러면서도 그는 몸을 떼어 그 자리를 떠날 수가 없었다. 그는 여전히, 무언가를 두려워하는 마음이 낳는 그런 종류의 믿음으로, 볼드 부인이 아마도 슬로프 씨의 아내가 되리라고 믿고 있었다. 정원에서 있었던 슬로프 씨의 자그마한 사건에 대해서 그는 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그가 아는 한 슬로프 씨는 전혀 다른 성격의 일을 겪었을 수도 있었다. 그는 미망인의 발치에 몸을 던져 승낙을 얻고, 그런 뒤에 흥겹고 잘나가는 구혼자가 되어 시내로 돌아왔을 수도 있었다. 시뇨라의 농담은 슬로프 씨에게 충분히 쓰라렸으나, 애러빈 씨에게도 꼭 그만큼 쓰라렸다. 그는 여전히 벽난로에 기대선 채 바지 주머니에 손을 넣고 만지작거리고 있었다.\n\n“자, 자, 슬로프 씨, 그렇게 수줍어하지 마세요.” 시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “요전 날 울라손에서 당신이 그 부인께 청혼하셨다는 것쯤은 우리 모두 알고 있답니다. 그분이 어떤 말로 승낙하셨는지 들려주세요. 그저 ‘네’ 한마디였나요, 아니면 둘이 모여 긍정이 되는 그 ‘아니요, 아니요’였나요? 아니면 침묵이 승낙을 대신했나요? 아니면 미망인에게 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 기개로 대놓고 말씀하시기를, ‘맹세코, 선생님, 당신이 원하시는 때가 되면 언제든 저를 슬로프 부인으로 만들어 주세요.’ 하셨나요?”\n\n슬로프 씨는 평생 그때만큼 마음이 편치 않았던 적이 별로 없었다. 저기에는 손 씨가 앉아 소리 없이 웃고 있었다. 저기에는 그의 오랜 적수 애러빈 씨가 서서 온 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다. 두 방 사이의 문간 언저리에는 스탠호프 양과 그레이 목사와 그린 목사를 비롯한 사람들이 작은 무리를 이루어 모여, 하나같이 그가 낭패 보는 꼴에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그는 이 농담을 저 부인에게 되던질 수 있느냐 없느냐가 오로지 제 재치에 달려 있음을 알았다. 될 수만 있다면 그리하는 편이 제게 이롭다는 것도 알았다. 그러나 그에게는 말 한마디가 없었다. “양심이라는 것이 우리 모두를 겁쟁이로 만드는구나.” 그는 제 뺨에 닿았던 엘리너의 날카로운 손가락 끝을 느꼈고, 누가 그 매질을 보았을지, 누가 저를 조롱하는 데 그토록 재미를 붙인 이 고약한 여자에게 그 이야기를 전했을지 알 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 거기 홍옥처럼 붉고 물고기처럼 말없이 서서, 이를 드러낼 만큼만 히죽거리는, 가엾다고밖에 할 수 없는 물건이 되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 시뇨라에게는 연민이 없었다. 그녀는 자비라는 것을 알지 못했다. 지금 그녀의 목적은 슬로프 씨를 눌러 놓는 것이었고, 이제 그를 제 손아귀에 넣은 마당에 그것을 철저히 해치우기로 작정하고 있었다.\n\n“아니, 슬로프 씨, 대답이 없으세요? 설마 그 여자가 당신을 물리칠 만큼 어리석었을 리는 없잖아요? 그분이 설마 주교 자리를 노리고 있는 건 아닐 테고요. 아, 하지만 어찌 된 일인지 알겠어요, 슬로프 씨. 미망인은 조심스럽기로 이름난 사람들이지요. 새 모자가 당신 머리에 얹힐 때까지, 수석사제관 열쇠를 그분께 보여 드릴 수 있을 때까지 그분을 가만히 두셨어야 했어요.”\n\n“시뇨라.” 마침내 그가 위엄 있게 나무라는 어조로 말해 보려 애쓰며 말했다. “참으로 엄숙한 주제를 두고 몹시 온당치 못한 방식으로 말씀하고 계십니다.”\n\n“엄숙한 주제라니 — 무슨 엄숙한 주제 말인가요? 수석 사제의 모자가 그렇게까지 엄숙한 주제일 리는 없잖아요.”\n\n“저에게는 당신이 저에게 씌우시는 그런 야심 따위는 없습니다. 그 이야기는 그만두시는 것이 좋겠습니다.”\n\n“아, 그러지요, 슬로프 씨. 그렇지만 한마디만 먼저 하겠어요. 총리의 편지를 주머니에 넣고 그분께 다시 가 보세요. 그때는 그분이 당신을 물리치지 않는다는 데에 제 숄을 걸고 당신 삽 모자와 내기하겠어요.”\n\n“시뇨라, 제가 말씀드려야겠습니다만, 당신은 그 부인에 관해 참으로 변명의 여지가 없는 방식으로 말씀하고 계십니다.”\n\n“그리고 한 가지 더 충고를 드리지요, 슬로프 씨. 딱 한 가지만 더 드리겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 노래를 부르기 시작했다 —\n\n“유쾌하고 슬기롭게 지내는 것이 좋지요, 슬로프 씨. 정직하고 참되게 지내는 것이 좋지요. 새 사랑과 맺어지기 전에, 슬로프 씨, 옛 사랑과는 헤어져 두는 것이 좋지요.”\n\n“하, 하, 하!”\n\n그리고 시뇨라는 소파에 몸을 뒤로 던지며 즐겁게 웃었다. 제 말을 들은 이들이 저마다의 상상 속에서 슬로프 씨의 첫사랑에 관한 자그마한 내력을 어떻게 채워 넣을지 그녀는 조금도 헤아리지 않았다. 그들 가운데 몇몇이 그가 더 일찍 흠모했던 상대라는 영예를 제게 돌릴지도 모른다는 것 또한 그녀는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 싫증이 났고 그를 떨쳐 버리고 싶었다. 그녀에게는 그에게 화를 낼 만한 까닭이 있었고, 그리하여 그녀는 앙갚음하기를 택한 것이었다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 어떻게 그 방을 빠져나왔는지는 그 자신도 끝내 알지 못했다. 그는 마침내, 아마도 누군가의 도움을 받아 제 모자를 챙겨 바깥 공기 속으로 달아나는 데 성공했다. 시뇨라를 향한 그의 사랑은 마침내 나았다. 그 뒤로 꿈속에서 그녀를 다시 떠올릴 때, 그것은 하늘빛 날개를 단 천사의 모습이 아니었다. 그는 차라리 그녀를 불과 유황에 결부시켰고, 여전히 그녀를 하나의 영으로 믿을 수는 있었으되 그녀를 하늘에서 아주 내쫓아 지옥의 신들 사이에 자리를 마련해 주었다. 바체스터에서 제 마음을 붙였던 두 여자를 저울에 달아 볼 때 — 그는 그런 일을 드물지 않게 했다 — 그의 영혼이 품은 증오에서 으뜸가는 자리는 대개 시뇨라에게 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제47장. 수석 사제 당선자\n\n그 뒤 한 주일 내내 바체스터는 누가 제 도시의 새 수석 사제가 될지 알지 못했다. 일요일 아침만 해도 슬로프 씨가 단연 유력한 후보였으나, 그가 대성당에 얼굴을 내밀지 않자 그의 배당은 한두 점 내려앉았다. 월요일에는 하인들이 다 듣는 자리에서 주교에게 꾸지람을 들었고, 그리하여 값을 아무리 낮추어도 그를 사겠다는 사람이 없을 지경까지 떨어졌다. 그러나 화요일에 관용 봉투에 담겨 친전이라 적힌 편지 한 통을 받으면서 그는 세간의 지지를 온전히 되찾았다. 수요일에는 그가 앓아누웠다는 말이 돌아 형세가 좋아 보이지 않았으나, 목요일 아침 그가 자못 의기양양한 태도로 철도역에 내려갔고 런던행 일등석 표를 끊었다는 것이 확인되자, 이 문제에는 더 이상 의심이 들어설 자리가 없었다.\n\n바체스터가 이렇듯 술렁이고 있는 동안 플럼스테드에서도 마음이 그리 편치는 않았다. 우리의 벗 부주교에게는 속으로 끙끙 앓을 까닭이 여럿 있었다. 그는 그윈 박사가 주교관에 가서 벌인 담판의 결과가 몹시 언짢았고, 자기가 몸소 갔더라면 일을 훨씬 잘 처리했으리라고 아내에게 말하기까지 서슴지 않았다. 아내는 그 말에 동의하지 않았으나, 그렇다고 사정이 나아지는 것도 아니었다.\n\n그러나 퀴버풀 씨의 요양원 임명은 이미 fait accompli였고, 하딩 씨가 그 임명을 순순히 받아들인 것 또한 그에 못지않게 돌이킬 수 없는 일이었다. 무슨 수를 써도 하딩 씨가 주교를 상대로 공개적인 항의에 나설 리는 없었고, 라자러스 학장 또한 그가 그러지 않는 편이 옳다고 전적으로 여겼다.\n\n“학장께 무슨 일이 생긴 것인지 도무지 모르겠소.” 부주교는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말했다. “예전에는 제 신분의 권리를 지키겠다고 얼마든지 나서던 분이었는데.”\n\n“부주교님,” 그랜틀리 부인은 그럴 때마다 이렇게 대답했다. “늘 싸움만 해서 무슨 소용이 있어요? 저는 정말이지 학장님이 옳다고 생각해요.” 그러나 그 학장은 부주교도 그 아내도 전혀 모르는 나름의 조치를 이미 취해 둔 터였다.\n\n게다가 슬로프 씨가 잇달아 거두는 성공은 그랜틀리 박사에게 독초와도 같았고, 볼드 부인의 온당치 못한 처신도 그에 못지않았다. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되고 게다가 제 아내의 여동생과 혼인까지 한다면, 그랜틀리 부주교에게 이 세상 전부가 다 무슨 소용이겠는가! 그는 그 이야기를 하고 또 하다가 거의 앓아누울 지경이 되었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 차라리 그 혼사가 얼른 치러져 끝나 버려서 더는 그 말을 듣지 않게 되기를 바랄 지경이었다.\n\n그리고 앞의 두 가지 못지않게 그의 가슴을 후벼 파는 괴로움의 근거가 하나 더 있었다. 그가 세인트 이월드에 앉혀 준 그 성직자의 귀감, 그가 그토록 소리 높여 자랑하던 그 대학 시절의 벗, 그 창끝 앞에서 슬로프 씨가 쓰러져 흙을 물게 되어 있던 그 교회의 기사, 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습의 교회를 떠받치는 그 훌륭한 보루, 옥스퍼드 최고의 정신을 대표하는 그 영예로운 인물이 — 적어도 아내가 예닐곱 번은 일러 준 바에 따르면 — 처신을 그르치고 있다는 것이었다!\n\n지난 한 주 동안 애러빈 씨는 플럼스테드에 코빼기도 보이지 않았으나, 유감스럽게도 그에 관한 말은 적잖이 들려왔다. 울라손 연회가 있던 날 저녁 부주교와 단둘이 남게 되자마자 그랜틀리 부인은 그날 애러빈 씨의 행실에 대해 아주 힘주어 제 생각을 밝혔다. 그가 짓던 낯빛이며 몸가짐이며 말투가 점잖은 교구 성직자답기는커녕 영 딴판이었다는 것이었다. 처음에 부주교는 그 말을 웃어넘기며, 그런 걱정은 할 것 없다고, 애러빈 씨는 조금도 위태로울 것 없는 사람으로 판명되리라고 아내를 안심시켰다. 그러나 차츰 그는 아내의 눈이 제 눈보다 밝았음을 깨닫기 시작했다. 다른 사람들도 시뇨라의 이름을 애러빈 씨의 이름과 나란히 놓았다. 경내에 사는 그 비쩍 마른 자그마한 참사회원은 애러빈 씨가 스탠호프 박사 댁을 몇 번이나 찾아갔는지, 또 갈 때마다 얼마나 오래 머물렀는지를 낱낱이 일러 주었다. 그가 대성당 도서관에서 애러빈 씨의 행방을 묻자, 유난히 설쳐 대는 자그마한 성가대 사제 하나가 스탠호프 박사 댁에 가 계신지 알아보고 오겠노라 나서기까지 했다. 소문이란 제 나팔로 첫 음을 울려 내는 데 한번 성공하고 나면 이내 제법 크게 울려 퍼지는 법이다. 애러빈 씨가 그 이탈리아 여자에게 넘어가고 말았다는 것, 그리고 불붙은 나무를 그 불에서 끄집어내기 위해 무슨 수를 쓰지 않는다면 부주교의 체면이 무섭게 상하리라는 것은 너무나 분명했다. 게다가 부주교에게 공평을 기하자면, 그는 애러빈 씨에게 참으로 정을 붙이고 있었으므로 그가 미끄러지는 것을 크게 슬퍼하기도 했다.\n\n슬로프 씨가 런던으로 떠난 이튿날, 두 사람은 만찬 전 응접실에 앉아 이런 근심거리들을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 이 자리에서 그랜틀리 부인은 마음속 생각을 거리낌 없이 털어놓았다. 그녀에게는 교구 성직자들에 대한 나름의 견해가 있었고, 이제야말로 그것을 쏟아 놓아도 좋을 때라고 여겼다.\n\n“제 말대로 하셨더라면, 부주교님, 세인트 이월드에 독신 남자를 앉히지는 않으셨을 거예요.”\n\n“허나 여보, 독신인 성직자는 죄다 처신을 그르친다는 말은 아닐 테지.”\n\n“성직자라고 해서 다른 남자들보다 그리 나을 게 있는지 저는 모르겠어요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “부목사라면야 괜찮지요. 늘 제 눈앞에 두고 볼 수 있고, 온당치 못한 짓을 고집하면 내보낼 수도 있으니까요.”\n\n“허나 애러빈 씨는 펠로였으니 아내를 둘 수가 없었소.”\n\n“그렇다면 아내를 둘 수 있는 사람으로 찾아보셨어야지요.”\n\n“그럼 여보, 펠로들은 성직록을 영영 받아서는 안 된다는 말이오?”\n\n“아니지요, 물론 받아야지요. 약혼을 하고 나서 말이에요. 저라면 혼인을 했거나 혼인을 약속한 젊은이가 아니고서는 결코 성직록 자리에 앉히지 않겠어요. 그런데 여기 애러빈 씨를 보세요. 책임은 온통 당신께 있는 거예요.”\n\n“지금 이 순간 옥스퍼드를 통틀어 애러빈만큼 품행과 몸가짐으로 존경받는 성직자는 없소.”\n\n“어머, 옥스퍼드요!” 부인은 코웃음을 치며 말했다. “옥스퍼드에서 남자들이 무슨 짓을 하고 다니든 아무도 듣지 못하는걸요. 교구에 나오면 망신을 살 사람도 옥스퍼드에서라면 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있어요. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 제 눈에는 애러빈 씨가 꼭 그런 사람으로 보여요.”\n\n부주교는 깊이 신음했으나, 더 내놓을 대답이 없었다.\n\n“정말이지 그이에게 말씀을 하셔야 해요, 부주교님. 자기네 교구 성직자가 그 여자와 시시덕거리는 데 온 시간을 다 쓰고 있다는 말을 손 남매가 듣기라도 하면 뭐라고 하겠는지 좀 생각해 보세요.”\n\n부주교는 또 한 번 신음했다. 그는 담대한 사람이었고, 필요할 때 교구의 젊은 성직자들을 어떻게 나무라야 하는지도 훤히 알았다. 그러나 애러빈 씨에게는 어딘지 모르게, 그를 나무라 봐야 이렇다 할 효험을 보기가 몹시 어려우리라는 느낌을 이 박사에게 안겨 주는 데가 있었다.\n\n“아내를 하나 얻으라고 권해 보세요. 그러면 그이도 그게 무슨 뜻인지 넉넉히 알아들을 거예요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n부주교로서는 신음하는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다. 저기 슬로프 씨가 있었다. 그는 수석 사제가 될 참이었고, 아내를 맞이할 참이었으며, 남부럽지 않은 지체와 재산과 훌륭한 저택 한 채와 집안 마차 한 대를 손에 넣을 참이었다. 머지않아 그는 바체스터 성직 사회의 안락한 élite 축에 들 것이었다. 그런데 정작 제가 밀어 준 protégé, 참된 교회의 참된 후예, 그를 걸고 맹세하다시피 했던 그 사람은 여전히 가난한 교구 사제 신세를 면치 못할 것이요, 그나마도 품행이 매우 미덥지 못하다는 평판까지 달고 있을 것이었다! 애러빈 씨에게 혼인을 권하는 것이야 얼마든지 좋은 일이겠으나, 애러빈 씨가 혼인을 한들 무엇으로 아내를 먹여 살린단 말인가?\n\n플럼스테드 응접실에서 일이 이렇게 정리되어 가고 있을 때, 그랜틀리 박사 내외의 이 달콤한 담론은 자갈 깔린 진입로 위를 요란하게 굴러 들어오는 쌍두마차 소리에 방해를 받았다. 그것은 방문객의 소리가 아니었다. 방문객의 자가용 마차라면 대개 얌전하고 예절 바르게 시골 저택의 문 앞에 대는 법이다. 그 소리는 오히려 이 집에 서둘러 닿으려 하며, 닿고 나서는 당장 떠날 뜻이 없는 누군가의 도착을 알리는 소리였다. 한 주 묵으라는 초대를 받은 손님이 만찬을 알리는 첫 종이 울린 뒤에야 당도했음을 의식하고 있다면 아마 그런 식으로 다가올 것이다. 종조부의 부고를 들고 오는 변호사나, 두 과목 최우등의 갓 딴 영예를 안고 대학에서 돌아오는 아들도 그렇게 도착할 것이다. 제게 그 문을 밀고 들어설 권리가 있는지 조금이라도 의심하는 사람이라면, 결코 그런 식으로 시골 저택 현관에 마차를 몰아 들이대지 않을 것이었다.\n\n“누굴까요?” 그랜틀리 부인이 남편을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“대체 누구란 말이오?” 부주교가 아내에게 말했다. 그러고는 조용히 일어나 응접실 문을 열고 손잡이를 잡은 채 섰다. “아니, 장인어른이시잖소!”\n\n과연 하딩 씨였고, 그것도 하딩 씨 혼자였다. 그는 바체스터에서 말 두 필이 끄는 역마차를 혼자 타고 와 거의 날이 저문 뒤에 닿았는데, 보아하니 전할 소식이 가득한 모양이었다. 그가 이곳을 찾을 때는 늘 더없이 조용한 방식이었다. 미리 기별 없이 오는 일은 좀처럼 없었고, 언제나 말 한 필이 끄는 수수한 낡은 초록색 삯마차를 타고 왔는데, 그 마차는 현관 앞까지 기어오다시피 하는 동안 소리 하나 제대로 내지 못했다.\n\n“아이고 이런, 워든, 장인어른이십니까?” 부주교는 놀란 나머지 지난 몇 해 사이의 일들을 깜빡 잊고 말했다. “어서 들어오십시오. 별일은 없으시겠지요.”\n\n“오셔서 정말 기뻐요, 아버지.” 딸이 말했다. “제가 지금 곧장 아버지 방을 준비해 놓을게요.”\n\n“나는 워든이 아닐세, 부주교.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “이제 퀴버풀 씨가 워든이지.”\n\n“아, 압니다, 알아요.” 부주교가 짜증스럽게 말했다. “잠깐 깜빡했을 뿐입니다. 무슨 일이라도 생겼습니까?”\n\n“지금 가지 말거라, 수전.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “너희에게 할 이야기가 있단다.”\n\n“5분 뒤면 만찬 종이 울릴 텐데요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그런가?” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “그럼 좀 기다리는 편이 낫겠구나.” 그는 전하러 온 소식으로 잔뜩 부풀어 있었으나, 그 소식이 긴 의논 없이는 전해질 수 없다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그는 말 두 필이 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 걸음으로 플럼스테드까지 달려왔건만, 막상 와 놓고 보니 만찬이 벌어 줄 유예를 기꺼이 받아들이고 싶어졌다.\n\n“중대한 이야기가 있으시다면,” 부주교가 말했다. “부디 지금 당장 들려주십시오. 엘리너가 달아나기라도 했습니까?”\n\n“아닐세, 그러지 않았네.” 하딩 씨가 몹시 언짢은 낯빛으로 말했다.\n\n“슬로프가 수석 사제가 되었습니까?”\n\n“아닐세, 그것도 아니야. 다만―”\n\n“다만 무엇입니까?” 몹시 조바심이 나기 시작한 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“그쪽에서 말이지―”\n\n“그쪽에서 무엇을 어쨌다는 말씀입니까?” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“그 자리를 나에게 주겠다고 하네.” 하딩 씨는 겸연쩍은 나머지 거의 말을 잇지 못할 지경으로 말했다.\n\n“맙소사!” 부주교는 이렇게 말하고는 기진하여 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n“아버지, 사랑하는 우리 아버지.” 그랜틀리 부인은 이렇게 말하며 아버지의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n“그래서 곧장 나와 자네들과 의논하는 편이 낫겠다 싶었네.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“의논이라니요!” 부주교가 소리쳤다. “아니 장인어른, 진심으로 축하드립니다. 온 마음을 다해서 말입니다. 정말 그렇습니다. 제 평생 이토록 기쁜 소식을 들어 본 적이 없습니다.” 그러고는 장인의 두 손을 붙들고 그 손이 떨어져 나가기라도 할 듯이 흔들어 대더니, 더할 나위 없는 기쁨을 드러내려는 듯 『주피터』 한 부를 머리 위로 빙빙 돌리며 방 안을 몇 바퀴고 돌았다.\n\n“허나―” 하딩 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n“허나라는 말씀은 마십시오.” 부주교가 말했다. “제 평생 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없습니다. 이야말로 마땅히 그리 되었어야 할 일이지요. 명예를 걸고 말씀드리거니와, 저는 살아 있는 동안 다시는 ○○ 경을 두고 험담 한마디 하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“그건 틀림없이 그윈 박사님이 애쓰신 덕이에요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 라자러스 학장을 무척 좋아했는데, 그가 대가족을 거느린 반듯한 기혼 남자였기 때문이다.\n\n“그렇겠지.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아버지, 저는 정말 너무나 기뻐요!” 그랜틀리 부인은 이렇게 말하며 일어나 아버지에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“허나 얘야.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. 말을 해 보려 애써도 다 헛일이었다. 아무도 그의 말에 귀를 기울이지 않았다.\n\n“자, 수석 사제님.” 부주교가 의기양양하게 말했다. “요양원 느릅나무를 잃으신 것쯤은 수석사제관 정원이 얼마간 달래 줄 겁니다. 자, 가엾은 퀴버풀! 이제는 그 사람 복을 더 이상 시샘하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“그럼요, 정말이지요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “가엾은 부인, 아이가 열넷이나 되잖아요. 그 자리가 그 댁에 돌아간 것이 저는 참으로 다행스러워요.”\n\n“나도 그렇단다.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨가 그 소식을 들을 때 어떤 낯빛이 되는지 볼 수만 있다면 20파운드라도 내겠습니다.” 부주교가 말했다. 슬로프 씨가 낭패를 볼 광경을 그려 보는 것은 부주교의 기쁨에서 적잖은 몫을 차지했다.\n\n마침내 하딩 씨는 이층에 올라가 손을 씻어도 좋다는 허락을 받았는데, 실인즉 플럼스테드까지 일부러 나와서 하려던 이야기는 거의 한마디도 하지 못한 뒤였다. 그리고 만찬이 끝나고 하인들이 물러가기 전에는 더 이야기할 수도 없었다. 그랜틀리 박사의 기쁨은 어찌나 걷잡을 수 없었던지 그는 하인들 앞에서 장인을 수석 사제님이라 부르는 것을 삼가지 못했고, 그리하여 아래층에서는 곧 따님의 미래 남편이 아니라 하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 될 모양이라는 것이 화제에 올랐으며, 그에 대한 의견도 갖가지였다. 나이가 지긋한 요리사와 집사는 그렇게 되는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼으나, 더 젊은 하인과 몸종은 슬로프 씨가 기회를 놓친 것이 참으로 애석한 일이라고 여겼다.\n\n“그래도 그 양반은 쩨쩨한 사람입지요.” 하인이 말했다. “제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 건 그 양반 때문이 아닙니다요. 다만 저야 늘 마님 동생분을 우러러 뵈었는데, 그분이라면 그 자리에 아주 잘 어울리셨을 겁니다요.”\n\n아래층 생각이 이러한 동안, 위층에서는 아주 크게 다른 의견이 자리 잡고 있었다. 식탁보가 걷히고 포도주가 식탁에 오르자마자 하딩 씨는 말을 꺼낼 기회를 스스로 만들었다. 그러나 그가 입을 뗀 것은 속으로 몹시 마음을 졸이면서였다.\n\n“○○ 경께서 참으로 고맙게 대해 주셨네. 참으로 고마운 일이고, 나도 그것을 깊이, 아주 깊이 느끼고 있네. 고백하자면 나도 그 제안이 기쁘기는 했어―”\n\n“그러셨겠지요.” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“허나 그래도 나는 아무래도 그것을 받아들일 수 없을 것 같네.”\n\n술병이 부주교의 손에서 식탁 위로 떨어질 뻔했고, 그가 어찌나 크게 몸을 움찔했던지 아내가 의자에서 벌떡 일어날 정도였다. 수석 사제 자리를 받지 않겠다니! 정말로 일이 이렇게 끝난다면, 장인이 실성했다는 데에 더는 의심의 여지가 없을 것이었다. 이제 문제는, 지체도 낮고 성직 승진이라야 한 해 200파운드에도 못 미치는 성직자가 높은 지체와 한 해 1,200파운드와 제 직업이 내줄 수 있는 가장 탐나는 자리 가운데 하나를 받아야 하느냐 마느냐 하는 것이었다!\n\n“무어라고요!” 부주교는 숨을 몰아쉬며, 격한 감정에 못 이겨 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 듯한 얼굴로 손님을 빤히 바라보며 말했다. “무어라고요!”\n\n“나는 새 직무를 감당할 만한 사람이 못 되네.” 하딩 씨가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n“새 직무라니요! 무슨 직무 말씀입니까?” 부주교는 본의 아니게 빈정거리는 말을 했다.\n\n“아, 아버지.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “수석 사제가 할 일보다 쉬운 일이 어디 있겠어요. 아버지는 분명 트리폴 박사님보다야 정정하시잖아요.”\n\n“지금 하고 계신 일의 절반도 되지 않을 겁니다.” 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다.\n\n“요전에 『주피터』가 젊은 사람들을 두고 뭐라고 썼는지 보았는가?”\n\n“예, 그리고 『주피터』가 슬로프 씨를 임명시키려고 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다는 것도 보았습니다. 혹시 슬로프 씨가 수석 사제가 되는 꼴을 보고 싶으신 겁니까.”\n\n하딩 씨는 이 책망을 뼈아프게 느꼈으면서도 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 슬로프 씨를 두고 사위와 또 다투려고 플럼스테드까지 건너온 것이 아니었으므로 그냥 흘려보냈다.\n\n“내 심정을 자네에게 이해시킬 수 없다는 것은 나도 아네.” 그가 말했다. “우리는 서로 다른 거푸집에서 나온 사람들이니 말일세. 나에게도 자네 같은 기백과 기운과 맞서 싸우는 힘이 있었으면 싶기는 하네만, 나에게는 그것이 없어. 살아온 날이 하루씩 늘어 갈수록 나는 평온과 안식을 더 바라게 된다네.”\n\n“아니, 수석사제관이 아니라면 대체 어디서 평온과 안식을 누린단 말씀입니까!” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“사람들은 내가 그 자리에 앉기에는 너무 늙었다고 할 걸세.”\n\n“맙소사! 사람들이라니요! 어떤 사람들 말씀입니까? 그깟 사람들을 무엇 하러 신경 쓰십니까?”\n\n“허나 나 스스로도 어떤 새 자리든 맡기에는 너무 늙었다고 생각하네.”\n\n“아버지.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “아버지보다 열 살이나 위인 분들도 날마다 새 자리에 임명되고 있어요.”\n\n“얘야.” 그가 말했다. “내 심정을 너희에게 이해시키기란 불가능한 일이고, 나도 이 일에 무슨 대단한 덕이 있다고 내세울 생각은 없다. 사실인즉 나에게는 시대의 기풍에 맞설 만한 기질의 힘이 없구나. 요즈음은 사방에서 젊은 사람을 부르짖고 있는데, 나에게는 그 요구에 맞서 나설 배짱이 없어. 만일 『주피터』가 내 임명 소식을 듣고서 내가 그 자리에 걸맞지 않다는 논설을 쓰고 또 쓴다면, 나는 그 때문에 정신을 놓고 말 게다. 그런 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어야 한다고 너희는 말하겠지. 그래, 얘야, 나도 견뎌야 마땅하다는 것은 인정한다. 허나 나는 내 약함을 느끼고, 내가 그러지 못하리라는 것을 안다. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 수석 사제가 무슨 일을 해야 하는지 나는 어린아이만큼도 알지 못한다.”\n\n“쯧!” 부주교가 소리쳤다.\n\n“나에게 화내지 말게, 부주교. 그리고 수전, 이 일로 우리 다투지는 말자꾸나. 이 문제에서 너희 뜻을 거스를 수밖에 없다는 것을 내가 얼마나 사무치게 느끼는지 안다면, 너희도 나에게 화내지는 못할 게다.”\n\n이것은 그랜틀리 박사에게 끔찍한 타격이었다. 하딩 씨가 수석사제관에 들어앉는 것보다 그에게 더 흡족할 일은 도무지 있을 수 없었다. 그는 하딩 씨가 근래 궁색해졌다 하여 그를 얕본 적은 한 번도 없었으나, 제 사람들이 편안한 자리에 앉아 있는 데서 오는 흡족함만큼은 온전히 알아보는 사람이었다. 하딩 씨로 말하면 세인트 커스버트 교구 사제에 선창자까지 겸하고 있느니 바체스터 수석 사제가 되는 편이 훨씬 걸맞은 일이었다. 게다가 바체스터의 점잖은 모든 것을 적대하는 저 원수, 그들 틈에 굴러떨어진 저 저교회파 성직 벼락출세자가 크게 낭패를 보는 것만 해도, 그것만으로도 그 자리 자체보다 거의 더 값진 일일 터였다. 바라지도 못했던 이런 행운이, 하딩 씨가 스스로 끌려다니도록 내버려 두는 터무니없는 옹고집과 병적인 망상 때문에 망가진다고 생각하니 소름이 끼쳤다. 잔을 입술에 그토록 가까이 대어 놓고서 그것을 마시지 못한다는 것은 그랜틀리 박사가 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어섰다.\n\n그런데도 아무래도 그는 그것을 견뎌야 할 듯싶었다. 으름장을 놓아도 헛일이었고 구슬려도 헛일이었다. 하딩 씨가 제의된 그 영예를 딱 잘라 거절한다고 말한 것은 아니었으나, 받아들이겠다고 딱 잘라 말하려 하지도 않았다. 거듭거듭 다그침을 받으면 그는 거듭거듭 제가 새 직무에는 전혀 맞지 않는 사람이라고 우겼다. 부주교가, 드러내 놓고 말하지는 못한 채, 새로 할 직무 따위는 없다고 넌지시 흘려 보아도 헛일이었다. 어려운 일이 생길 때마다 자기가, 이 부주교가 기꺼이 나서서 마음 약한 수석 사제를 이끌어 줄 수 있노라고 넌지시 비쳐 보아도 헛일이었다. 하딩 씨는 새로 할 직무가 있다는 어리석은 생각뿐 아니라, 그 직무를 몸소 감당할 각오가 되어 있지 않은 사람이라면 그 자리를 맡아서는 안 된다는 생각까지 품고 있는 듯했다.\n\n이 회담은, 하딩 씨가 장관의 개인 비서에게서 받은 편지에 곧바로 잘 받았다는 회신을 보내되 마음을 정할 시간을 이틀만 달라고 청하고, 그 이틀 동안 이 문제를 함께 생각해 보기로 한다는 양해로 끝이 났다.\n\n이튿날 아침에 부주교가 하딩 씨를 마차로 바체스터까지 데려다주기로 되어 있었다.\n\n# 제48장. 손 양이 중매 솜씨를 발휘하다\n\n하딩 씨가 플럼스테드에서 바체스터로 돌아왔을 때 — 물론 부주교와 동행하여 돌아온 것이었다 — 또 한 번 놀랄 만한 소식이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 오는 길 내내 그는 반박할 수 없는 논변의 무게에 짓눌렸는데, 그 논변이 한결같이 증명하는 바는 자기를 수석 사제로 만들어 주려 그토록 애쓰는 자애로운 정부의 뜻에 끼어들지 않는 것이 그의 마땅한 도리라는 것이었다. 그리하여 하이 스트리트의 약방 문 앞에 이르렀을 무렵 그는 이 일을 어느 쪽으로 돌려놓아야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그렇게 갈피를 못 잡는 그에게 또 다른 갈피 없음이 예정되어 있었다. 거기서 그는 딸이 남긴 쪽지를 발견했으니, 당장 자기에게 와 달라고 몹시도 다급하게 청하는 내용이었다. 그러나 우리는 이야기를 다시 조금 뒤로 돌려야겠다.\n\n손 양은 그랜틀리 부인의 마음을 그토록 어지럽혔던 애러빈 씨에 관한 소문을 진작에 들어 알고 있었다. 그리고 그 역시 제 교구의 성직자가 낯선 여신을 섬긴다는 혐의를 받는다는 것이 마음 아팠다. 그 역시 교구 목사든 교구 사제든 모두 혼인을 해야 한다는 견해를 지녔던지라, 특유의 선량한 열의를 발휘하여 애러빈 씨에게 어울릴 짝을 찾아내려고 머리를 굴리기 시작했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 이 난처한 일에 부주교의 훈계 말고는 더 나은 처방을 생각해 내지 못했다. 손 양은 혼기가 찼고 지참금도 있는 젊은 부인이 더 효험이 있으리라고 보았다. 남편을 필요로 할 법하고 시골 목사관이 줄 수 있는 정도의 영전을 감당할 만한 미혼의 벗들을 하나하나 헤아려 보았으나, 볼드 부인보다 더 알맞은 이는 떠오르지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 지체 없이, 슬로프 씨가 낭패를 본 바로 그날, 곧 제 오라비가 네로 가문의 마지막 후손과 그 흥미로운 면담을 나눈 바로 그날, 바체스터로 들어가 볼드 부인에게 유모와 아기를 데리고 울라손으로 와서 오래 머물다 가라고 청했다.\n\n손 양은 한두 달을 제안했으나, 속으로는 나중에 제 영향력을 써서 그 기간을 겨울이 다 가도록 늘려 볼 작정이었다. 애러빈 씨가 장차 신부가 될 사람과 제법 친해질 기회를 얻게 하자는 것이었다. “애러빈 씨도 오시게 해야지.” 손 양은 혼자 이렇게 생각했다. “봄이 오기 전에 서로 알게 될 테고, 열두 달이나 열여덟 달쯤 지나 모든 일이 잘 풀리면 볼드 부인은 세인트 이월드에 자리를 잡고 계실 게야.” 그러고 나서 이 마음씨 고운 부인은 제 중매 솜씨에 대해 스스로에게 과분하지 않은 칭찬을 얼마간 베풀었다.\n\n엘리너는 조금 놀랐으나, 일은 아무튼 한두 주쯤은 울라손에 가 있겠다고 약속하는 것으로 마무리되었다. 그리하여 그의 아버지가 플럼스테드로 마차를 몰고 나가기 전날, 그는 마차를 불러 울라손으로 나갔다.\n\n손 양은 그날 저녁만큼은 아직 싹도 트지 않은 낭군감으로 그를 성가시게 하지 않으려 했으니, 몇 시간쯤은 편히 자리를 잡게 두자는 생각에서였다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 애러빈 씨가 당도했다. “자.” 손 양은 혼자 중얼거렸다. “이제 어떻게든 두 사람이 서로 마주치도록 손을 써야겠구먼.” 바로 그날, 만찬이 끝난 뒤 엘리너는 지어 보이려 했으나 지켜 내지 못한 위엄과, 참으려 했으나 참지 못한 눈물과, 이겨 내려 했으나 이기지 못한 설렘과, 감추려 했으나 감추지 못한 기쁨을 안고서, 자기가 애러빈 씨와 혼인을 약속했으며 되도록 빨리 바체스터의 집으로 돌아가야 하겠다고 손 양에게 말했다.\n\n손 양이 제 계략의 성공을 기뻐했다고만 말한다면 그때 그의 심경을 아주 희미하게밖에 전하지 못할 것이다. 독자 여러분도 아마 이런 꿈을 꾸어 본 적이 있으리라. 앞에 무시무시하게 긴 도보 여정이, 20마일이나 30마일쯤 되는 길이, 생각만 해도 끔찍한 노고가 놓여 있는데, 막상 출발하자마자 용케 편리한 지름길을 찾아내어 피로 한 점 없이 5분 만에 그 일의 끝에 다다랐던 꿈 말이다. 손 양이 깨어서 느낀 심경도 얼마간 그런 종류였다. 또 독자 여러분은 아이들을 상대해 본 적이 있을 터인즉, 어느 땐가 어린것들에게 굉장한 즐길 거리를 하나 약속하되 이를테면 겨울이 끝날 무렵이나 여름이 시작될 무렵에 있을 일이라고 일러 준 적이 있을 것이다. 그러나 성마른 아이들은 기다리지 않고, 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 대접을 내놓으라고 아우성을 친다. 손 양은 제 아이들도 그만큼이나 막무가내라는 느낌이 들었다. 그는 제가 깔아 둔 도화선의 길이를 잘못 헤아린 서투른 포수와도 같았다. 화약은 너무 일찍 터졌고, 가엾은 손 양은 제가 놓은 화약의 힘에 제가 날아간 꼴이라고 느꼈다.\n\n손 양에게도 한때 구애하는 이들이 있었으나, 그들은 하나같이 예스럽고 신중한 습성의 신사들이었다. 손 양의 심장도 늘 굳어만 있었던 것은 아니다 — 비록 그가 여태 처녀의 몸으로 늙어 가는 노처녀이기는 하지만. 그러나 그 심장이 첫 번째 공격에 이런 식으로 무너진 적은 결코 없었다. 그는 중년의 학구적인 성직자와, 어쩌면 재혼할 마음을 품게 될지도 모를 신중한 부인을 맺어 주려 했던 것인데, 그러다 보니 부싯깃 더미에 불씨를 던져 넣은 셈이 되었다. 그야 다 마땅히 그리될 일이었다마는, 그래도 그는 제 성공이 너무 성급했던 것에 얼마간 마음이 어수선했고, 볼드 부인이 구애를 그토록 선뜻 받아들인 데에도 얼마간 언짢은 마음이 들었던 듯하다.\n\n그러나 그는 그 일을 두고 누구에게도 아무 말을 하지 않았고, 모든 것을 달라진 새 시대의 풍속 탓으로 돌렸다. 그들의 어머니와 할머니 세대는 어쩌면 조금 더 느긋했을 터이나, 요즘 일이 옛날과는 사뭇 다르게 굴러간다는 것만은 어느 쪽에서나 인정하는 바였다. 손 양이 이 문제에 대해 아는 바로는, 새로운 régime 아래서는 두 시간이면 넉넉히 마칠 수 있는 일을 그는 아무것도 모르고 열두 달이나 잡아 두었던 것인지도 몰랐다.\n\n그러나 우리가 이 구애를 이렇게 대충 지나쳐 버려서는 안 되겠다. 엘리너가 울라손에서 제 구혼자 둘을 어떻게 처리했는지는 어쩌면 지루하리만큼 정확하게 이야기했으니, 그가 애러빈 씨와 어떻게 마주하게 되었는지도 그에 못지않게 정확하게, 그러면서도 되도록이면 덜 지루하게 이야기해야 마땅하다.\n\n엘리너가 손 양의 초대를 받아들일 때 울라손이 세인트 이월드 교구에 속해 있다는 사실을 떠올렸다는 것은 부인할 수 없다. 시뇨라와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그는 애러빈 씨와 자기에게 던져진 그 호소 말고는 거의 아무것도 생각하지 못했다. 그는 자기가 들은 말이 사실이 아니라고 믿을 수도, 믿어 보려 애쓸 수도 없었다. 아무리 이리저리 생각해 보아도 애러빈 씨가 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 사실로 받아들이지 않을 도리가 없었다. 그리고 거기서 한 걸음 더 나아가 스스로에게 물었을 때에는, 자기 또한 그를 좋아한다는 것 역시 사실로 받아들이지 않을 도리가 없었다. 만일 그가 그이의 희망과 슬픔을 함께 나눌 사람이 되도록 정해져 있다면, 손 양 말고 또 누구에게 이만큼 걸맞은 우정을 구할 수 있겠는가? 이 초대는 제 운명의 성취를 향해 내디디도록 정해진 한 걸음 같았고, 게다가 애러빈 씨가 이튿날 울라손에 오리라는 말까지 듣고 나니 온 세상이 자기 편을 들어 짜고 도는 것만 같았다. 하기야 그럴 만하지 않은가? 슬로프 씨의 그 일에서는 온 세상이 그를 거슬러 짜고 돌지 않았던가?\n\n그러나 그는 마음을 놓고 편히 지낼 수가 없었다. 만찬이 끝난 저녁에 손 양이 애러빈 씨의 여러 미덕을 늘어놓으며, 그를 두고 짓궂게 퍼진 자잘한 소문 따위는 아무 뜻도 없는 것이라고 넌지시 일러 주었을 때, 볼드 부인은 무어라 대답할 수가 없었다. 손 양이 조금 더 나아가, 이 주에서 세인트 이월드보다 더 어여쁜 교구 사제관은 알지 못한다고 잘라 말했을 때에도, 볼드 부인은 계획된 퇴창과 계획된 여사제를 떠올리며 여전히 입을 다물고 있었다. 다만 온 세상이 자기가 애러빈 씨를 사랑한다는 것을 다 안다는 확신에 두 귀가 화끈거렸다. 하기야 두 사람이 만나서 지금 서로에게 말하고 싶어 못 견디는 그 말을 주고받을 수만 있다면, 그런 것이 무슨 대수이겠는가?\n\n그리고 두 사람은 정말로 만났다. 애러빈 씨는 그날 이른 시각에 와서 두 부인이 응접실에서 함께 바느질을 하고 있는 것을 보았다. 손 양은, 만일 사정을 죄다 알았더라면 그 자리에서 공기 속으로 사라져 버렸을 사람인데도, 제가 당장 자리를 비켜 주는 것이 얼마나 큰 은혜인지는 짐작도 못 한 채 점심 시간까지 두 사람과 이야기를 나누며 남아 있었다. 애러빈 씨는 시뇨라 네로니의 아름다움 말고는 아무 이야기도 하지 못했고, 스탠호프 일가 말고는 아무도 화제에 올리려 하지 않았다. 이는 엘리너에게 몹시 괴로운 일이었고 손 양에게도 그리 흡족한 일이 아니었다. 그러나 그가 그렇게 대놓고 감탄을 털어놓는 데에는 그래도 결백의 증거가 있었다.\n\n그러고 나서 점심을 들었고, 그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 교구 일로 나갔으며, 엘리너와 손 양만 남아 함께 산책을 하게 되었다.\n\n“시뇨라 네로니가 사람들 말대로 그렇게 어여쁜가요?” 돌아오는 길에 엘리너가 물었다.\n\n“아주 아름답지요, 분명히, 아주 아름다워요.” 손 양이 대답했다. “허나 누구든 그이를 사랑스럽다고 여기는지는 모르겠구려. 사내라면 누구나 한 번 바라보고 싶어 할 여인이기는 하나, 설령 혼인한 몸이 아니고 지금처럼 몸이 성치 못한 것도 아니라 한들, 제 집 화롯가에 기꺼이 들이려 할 사내는 몇 없지 싶소.”\n\n이 말에는 얼마간 위로가 있었다. 엘리너는 집에 돌아올 때까지 그 위로를 한껏 곱씹었다. 그러고는 응접실에 홀로 남겨졌는데, 막 어둑어둑해질 무렵 애러빈 씨가 들어왔다.\n\n10월 초의 고운 오후였고, 엘리너는 마지막 햇빛을 소설 읽는 데 쓰려고 창가에 앉아 있었다. 아늑한 방에는 불이 피워져 있었으나 날씨가 그 앞으로 끌릴 만큼 춥지는 않았고, 앉은 자리에서 해가 지는 것이 보였으므로 그는 책에 그리 마음을 두고 있지 않았다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 들어오자 늘 하던 대로 잠시 난롯불을 등지고 서서, 더 흥미로운 이야기를 꺼낼 용기를 그러모으는 동안 날씨가 좋다는 따위의 평범한 말만 몇 마디 늘어놓았다. 그가 그때 그 자리에서 엘리너에게 청혼하기로 마음을 굳혔다고는 아마 말할 수 없을 것이다. 남자란 대개 그런 결심을 하지 않는 법이라고 우리는 생각한다. 슬로프 씨와 스탠호프 씨는 과연 그런 결심을 했다마는, 신사들은 대개 하겠다는 뚜렷한 작정도 없이 청혼을 한다. 애러빈 씨의 경우도 지금 그러했다.\n\n“해 지는 빛이 곱네요.” 엘리너가 그가 골라 놓은 그 지독히도 진부한 화제에 응하며 말했다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 난로 앞 깔개에서는 지는 해가 보이지 않았으므로 그의 곁으로 다가가야만 했다.\n\n“참으로 곱군요.” 그는 이렇게 말하며, 그의 옷자락 주름에 닿지 않을 만큼 얌전히 멀찍이 떨어져 섰다. 그러고 나니 더는 할 말이 없는 모양이었다. 그리하여 잠시 말없이 저무는 해의 밝음을 바라보고 나서 그는 난로 쪽으로 돌아갔다.\n\n엘리너는 자기 쪽에서 먼저 이야기를 꺼내기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다. 우선 무슨 말을 해야 할지 찾을 수가 없었다. 평소에는 넉넉하고도 남던 말이 도와주러 나와 주지 않았다. 게다가 아무리 애를 써도 눈물이 나오는 것을 좀처럼 막을 수가 없었다.\n\n“울라손이 마음에 드시나요?” 애러빈 씨가 안전하게 멀찍한 자리, 곧 난로 앞 깔개에 자리를 잡고 서서 말했다.\n\n“네, 정말이지 아주 마음에 들어요!”\n\n“손 씨와 손 양을 말하는 것이 아니라 — 두 분을 좋아하시는 건 저도 알지요 — 이 집의 양식 말입니다. 이렇게 지은 예스러운 저택과 예스러운 정원에는 저로서는 유난히 마음이 끌리는 무언가가 있어요.”\n\n“저는 예스러운 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “예스러운 것들은 그만큼 더 정직하니까요.”\n\n“그건 어떨지 모르겠습니다.” 애러빈 씨가 나직이 웃으며 말했다. “그건 이쪽으로든 저쪽으로든 할 말이 아주 많은 문제지요. 우리 모두에게 그토록 가까이 걸린 문제, 우리 눈앞에 그토록 바싹 놓여 있는 문제를 두고 세상이 그토록 넓게 갈라져 있다는 것이 이상합니다. 어떤 이들은 우리가 완전함을 향해 빠르게 나아가고 있다고 여기는데, 또 어떤 이들은 미덕이 이 땅에서 사라져 가고 있다고 여기니까요.”\n\n“그러면 애러빈 씨는 어떻게 생각하세요?” 엘리너가 말했다. 그는 이야기가 흘러가는 어조에 얼마간 놀라면서도, 자기 감정을 드러내지 않고 말할 수 있게 해 주는 무언가를 그가 꺼내 준 것이 다행스러웠다.\n\n“제가 어떻게 생각하느냐고요, 볼드 부인?” 그러고서 그는 바지 주머니에 손을 넣고 돈을 짤랑거렸는데, 그 꼴이나 말투나 일이 잘 풀리는 구애자와는 도무지 닮은 데가 없었다. “중요한 문제에 대해서는 도무지 굳은 견해를 얻지 못한다는 것이 제 삶의 골칫거리랍니다. 생각하고, 생각하고, 계속 생각하는데도 제 생각은 늘 서로 다른 방향으로 달려가지요. 우리가 우러러 바란다고 공언하는 그 높은 소망들에 우리가 우리 아버지들보다 더 든든히 기대고 있는지 어떤지, 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“저는 세상이 날마다 더 세속적으로 되어 간다고 생각해요.” 엘리너가 말했다.\n\n“그건 부인이 젊으셨을 때보다 세상을 더 많이 보시기 때문이지요. 허나 우리가 보는 것으로 판단해서는 안 될 겁니다 — 우리가 보는 것은 참으로, 참으로 적으니까요.” 그러고서 얼마간 말이 끊겼고, 그동안 애러빈 씨는 계속 실링과 반 크라운 은화를 뒤적였다. “성경을 믿는다면야, 인류가 전체로서 이제 와 뒷걸음질하도록 허락되리라고는 좀처럼 생각할 수 없지요.”\n\n엘리너는 마음이 인류의 일반적 형편 따위와는 사뭇 다른 데에 붙들려 있었으므로 이 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신이 철저히 못마땅했다. 시뇨라가 그토록 이상한 방식으로 자기에게 꺼낸 그 이야기에서 생각을 억지로 떼어 낼 수가 없었고, 그러면서도 그 이야기를 매듭짓기 전에는 애러빈 씨와 거리낌 없는 자연스러운 어조로 말을 나눌 수 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 그에게 어떤 특별한 감정도 내보이지 않기를 몹시 바랐으나, 그가 자기를 바라보기만 하면 자기가 편치 않다는 것을 대번에 알아채리라는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n그러나 그는 그를 바라보지 않았다. 바라보는 대신 그는 난롯가를 떠나 방 안을 오르내리며 거닐기 시작했다. 엘리너는 마음을 다잡고 책을 집어 들었으나 읽을 수가 없었다. 눈에 눈물이 고여 있었고, 아무리 애를 써도 그것이 뺨으로 흘러내렸다. 애러빈 씨가 자기에게 등을 돌렸을 때 그는 그것을 닦아 냈다. 그러나 이내 또 한 줄기가 그 자리를 대신해 얼굴을 타고 흘렀다. 눈물은 계속 나왔다 — 단번에 그를 들키게 만들 홍수 같은 눈물이 아니라, 하나씩 하나씩, 홀로 오는 파수꾼들처럼. 애러빈 씨는 그를 유심히 살피지 않았으므로 눈물은 들키지 않고 지나갔다.\n\n이렇게 방 안을 오르내리던 애러빈 씨는 네댓 번을 돌고서야 다음 말을 꺼냈고, 엘리너도 똑같이 말없이 책 위로 얼굴을 숙이고 앉아 있었다. 그는 눈물에 지고 말까 봐 두려워 방에서 빠져나갈 채비를 하던 참이었는데, 그때 애러빈 씨가 거닐다가 그의 맞은편에 멈춰 섰다. 그는 바싹 다가오지는 않고 걸음이 데려다 놓은 바로 그 자리에 선 채, 두 손을 웃옷 자락 밑에 넣고서 이렇게 고백을 시작했다.\n\n“볼드 부인.” 그가 말했다. “저는 부인께 저지른 큰 잘못에 대해 갚아야 할 빚이 있습니다.” 엘리너는 심장이 어찌나 뛰던지 그가 아무 잘못도 저지른 적이 없다는 말조차 제 입으로 할 자신이 없었다. 그리하여 애러빈 씨는 이렇게 말을 이었다.\n\n“그 뒤로 그 일을 많이 생각해 보았고, 이제는 언젠가 제가 부인께 여쭈었던 그 물음이 도무지 아무 근거도 없는 것이었음을 압니다. 제 쪽에서 무례한 일이었고, 어쩌면 사내답지 못한 일이었지요. 저와 부인의 인척이신 그랜틀리 박사 사이에 어떤 친분이 있다 한들 그것이 정당화될 수는 없습니다. 우리 두 사람 사이에 있었던 아는 사이라는 것으로도 마찬가지고요.” 이 ‘아는 사이’라는 말이 엘리너의 가슴을 서늘하게 쳤다. 결국 이것이 자기에게 정해진 운명이란 말인가? “그래서 저는 겸허한 마음으로 부인께 용서를 구하는 것이 옳다고 생각하며, 지금 그리하는 것입니다.”\n\n엘리너는 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 했을까? 울고 있었으므로 많은 말을 할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 무슨 말이든 해야 했다. 그는 그 무슨 말을 정중하게, 다정하게, 그러면서도 제 속을 드러내지 않는 방식으로 하기를 몹시 바랐다. 그는 여태 그토록 말이 궁했던 적이 없었다.\n\n“정말이지 저는 아무 언짢음도 느끼지 않았어요, 애러빈 씨.”\n\n“아니, 느끼셨습니다! 그리고 느끼지 않으셨다면 부인은 부인이 아니었겠지요. 제가 그렇게 언짢게 해 드린 것이 그른 만큼 부인이 언짢아하신 것은 옳았습니다. 저는 아직 제 자신을 용서하지 못했습니다만, 부인께서 저를 용서하신다는 말은 듣고 싶습니다.”\n\n이제 그는 차분히 말할 수 있는 지경을 넘어서 있었으나, 그래도 여전히 눈물은 감추고 있었다. 애러빈 씨는 잠시 대답을 기다렸으나 헛일이었으므로 문 쪽으로 걸어가려 했다. 그는 그를 이렇게 대답 없이 보내는 것이야말로 온갖 자애로움을 크게 거스르는 죄라고 느꼈다. 그래서 자리에서 일어나 살며시 그의 팔에 손을 대고 말했다. “아, 애러빈 씨, 제 말을 들으시기 전에는 가지 마세요! 저는 용서해요. 제가 용서하는 걸 아시잖아요.”\n\n그는 그토록 살며시 제 팔에 닿았던 그 손을 잡고서, 마치 책에 씌어 있기라도 한 것처럼 제 남은 생의 온 운명을 거기서 읽어 내려는 듯 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그렇게 바라보는 그의 얼굴에는 가라앉은 슬픈 진지함이 서려 있었는데, 엘리너는 그것을 마주 감당할 수가 없었다. 그는 그저 융단만 내려다보며, 눈물은 흐르는 대로 내버려 두고, 제 손도 그의 손 안에 그대로 두고 있을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n두 사람이 그렇게 서 있었던 것은 겨우 1분이었지만, 그 1분의 길이는 두 사람 모두에게 그것을 영원히 잊지 못할 것으로 만들기에 넉넉했다. 엘리너는 이제 자기가 사랑받고 있음을 확신했다. 그 어떤 말도, 그 웅변이 어떠하든, 저 간절하고 우수 어린 눈길보다 더 깊이 사무칠 수는 없었다.\n\n어찌하여 그는 그렇게 그의 눈을 들여다보았을까? 어찌하여 그는 그에게 말을 하지 않았을까? 혹시 그가 먼저 신호를 보내 주기를 바라고 있었던 것일까?\n\n그리고 그 또한, 여자에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없는 그조차도, 자기가 사랑받고 있음을 알았다. 청하기만 하면 그 모든 것이 다 제 것이 될 터였다. 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 사랑스러움, 늘 말을 하지만 지금은 말이 없는 저 두 눈, 세인트 이월드에서 처음 마주친 뒤로 그토록 그를 끌어당겼던 저 여인다운 환함과 간절하고 정 많은 마음이. 그것이 이제 다 제 것이 될 수 있었고, 되어야만 했다. 그렇지 않고서야 그가 제 손을 그의 손 안에 이렇게 잡힌 채로 두었을 리가 없었다. 청하기만 하면 되었다. 아, 그러나 그것이 바로 어려운 일이었다. 이 모든 것에 1분이면 넉넉했을까? 아니, 어쩌면 1분이 넘었는지도 모른다.\n\n“볼드 부인 —” 마침내 그가 이렇게 말하고는 스스로 말을 멈추었다.\n\n그가 말을 하지 못한다면 그가 어떻게 말을 하겠는가? 그는 그를 성으로 불렀다. 생판 남이라도 썼을 바로 그 호칭으로! 그는 그의 손에서 제 손을 빼내고 제자리로 돌아가려는 듯 몸을 움직였다. “엘리너!” 그러자 그가 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했으니, 마치 연인의 용기를 아직 절반밖에 꾸어 오지 못한 듯이, 마치 제가 취한 그 스스럼없음으로 그를 언짢게 할까 아직도 두려운 듯이. 그는 천천히, 부드럽게, 거의 애처로울 만큼 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 거기에는 적어도 그를 물러서게 할 노여움은 없었다.\n\n“엘리너!” 그가 다시 소리쳐 부르더니 순식간에 그를 제 품에 끌어안았다. 어떻게 그리되었는지, 그리한 것이 그인지 그인지, 그가 그 목소리의 다정함에 사로잡혀 그리로 날아든 것인지, 아니면 그가 언짢게 할 성싶지 않은 힘으로 그를 제 가슴으로 끌어당긴 것인지, 두 사람 중 누구도 알지 못했고, 나 또한 단언할 수 없다. 이제 두 사람 사이에는 각자 따로 움직인다는 것이 거의 있을 수 없는 그런 공명이 있었다. 두 사람은 하나요 같은 것이었다 — 한 몸, 한 영, 한 생명이었다.\n\n“엘리너, 나의 엘리너, 나의 사람, 나의 아내!” 그는 눈물 너머로 그를 올려다볼 용기를 냈고, 그는 제 얼굴을 그의 얼굴 위로 숙여 그의 이마에 입술을 대었다 — 턱에 처음 수염이 난 이래 여태 여인의 뺨이라는 호사를 맛본 적이 없는 그 숫된 입술을.\n\n그는 제 ‘예’는 ‘예’여야 하고 제 ‘아니오’는 ‘아니오’여야 한다고 배워 왔으나, 이번에는 그 어느 쪽도 요구받지 않았다. 그는 손 양에게 자기가 애러빈 씨와 혼인을 약속했다고 말했지만, 두 사람 사이에 그런 말이 오간 적은 없었고 약조를 청하거나 준 일도 없었다.\n\n“아, 저를 보내 주세요.” 그가 말했다. “이제 저를 보내 주세요. 너무 행복해서 여기 있을 수가 없어요 — 혼자 있게 저를 보내 주세요.” 그는 그를 붙들려 하지 않았다. 입맞춤을 되풀이하지도 않았고, 그의 입술에 또 하나를 누르지도 않았다. 마음만 먹었다면 그리할 수도 있었을 것이다. 이제 그는 온전히 그의 사람이었다. 그는 그의 허리를 감았던 팔을, 새로운 기쁨으로 떨리던 그 팔을 거두고 그를 놓아주었다. 그는 노루처럼 제 방으로 달아났고, 빗장을 지른 다음 제 사랑의 호사를 남김없이 누렸다. 그는 그토록 온순하게 제게 용서를 빌던 이 사내를 우러렀고, 거의 떠받들다시피 했다. 그리고 이제 그는 제 사람이었다. 아, 지난 몇 주의 희망과 두려움과 비참함이 기억을 스쳐 지나갈 때 그는 얼마나 울고 흐느끼고 또 웃었던가.\n\n슬로프 씨라니! 그이에게 택함을 받은 이 사람이 슬로프 씨와 짝이 되었을 수도 있다고 감히 생각한 자가 있었다니! 게다가 그런 말을 감히 그이에게까지 전하여, 제 눈부신 행복을 그런 부질없는 위험에 내던졌다니! 그러다가 그는 자기가 그이에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 안락을 떠올리며 기쁘게 미소를 지었다 — 그이가 안락 따위에 마음을 쓰는 사람이어서가 아니라, 베푸는 일이 저로서는 그토록 달콤할 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n그는 일어나 종을 울려 하녀를 불렀으니, 어린 아들에게 새 아버지가 생긴 일을 일러 주기 위해서였다. 그리고 제 나름의 방식으로 정말 그렇게 일러 주었다. 그는 아이와 단둘이 있으려고 하녀에게 물러가라 이르고는, 아이가 침대 위에서 팔다리를 뻗고 뒹구는 동안, 이 아이의 어린 시절을 지켜 주도록 자기가 골라 놓은 사내에 대한 찬사를 아이로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말로 쏟아 놓았다.\n\n그러나 그는 애러빈 씨가 이 아이를 제 품에 받아들여 말하자면 제 자식으로 삼게 하기 전에는 행복할 수가 없었다. 그 생각이 떠오른 순간 그는 아기를 두 팔에 안아 들고 문을 열어 잰걸음으로 응접실까지 뛰어 내려갔다. 여전히 방바닥을 오가는 그의 발소리로 그가 거기 있음을 대번에 알았고, 방 안을 한 번 들여다보고서 그가 혼자임을 알았다. 그는 잠시 망설이다가 제 귀한 짐을 안고 서둘러 들어갔다.\n\n애러빈 씨가 방 한가운데서 그를 맞았다. “여기요.” 그가 급히 달려오느라 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. “여기, 이 아이를 받으세요 — 받아서 사랑해 주세요.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 그에게서 이 작은 사내아이를 받아 들고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추며 하느님께 이 아이를 축복해 주십사 기도했다. “이 아이는 온전히 내 자식이 될 겁니다 — 온전히 내 자식이.” 그가 말했다. 엘리너는 아이를 도로 받으려 몸을 숙이면서 아이를 안고 있던 그 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 제 보물을 안고 다시 제 방으로 달려갔다.\n\n하딩 씨의 작은딸이 두 번째로 얻어진 것은 이러하였다. 만찬 자리에서 그도 애러빈 씨도 그리 밝지 못했으나, 두 사람의 말없음은 아무 말거리도 되지 않았다. 앞서 말한 대로 응접실에서 그는 손 양에게 있었던 일을 이야기했다. 이튿날 아침 그는 바체스터로 돌아갔고, 애러빈 씨는 소식 보따리를 들고 부주교에게 건너갔다. 그랜틀리 박사가 자리에 없었으므로 그는 그랜틀리 부인에게 자기가 처형의 매제가 되는 영광을 누리고자 한다고 말하는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 그런 소식을 듣고 기쁨에 황홀해진 그랜틀리 부인은 그가 엘리너에게서 여태 받았던 그 어떤 환영보다 더 따뜻한 환영을 그에게 베풀어 주었다.\n\n“어머나 세상에!” 그가 소리쳤다 — 이것은 목사관 전체의 한결같은 외침이었다. “가엾은 엘리너! 사랑스러운 엘리너! 그 애가 얼마나 터무니없는 억울한 일을 당했는지! 그래, 이제 다 보상받게 될 거예요.” 그러고 나서 그는 시뇨라를 떠올렸다. “사람들이 무슨 거짓말을 지어내는 건지.” 그는 혼자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 일에서 사람들은 거짓말이라고는 조금도 하지 않았다.\n\n# 제49장. 베엘제붑이라는 망아지\n\n손 양이 식당을 나설 때만 해도 엘리너는 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀에게 털어놓을 생각이 조금도 없었다. 그러나 소파에 안주인과 나란히 앉고 보니 그 비밀은 저도 모르는 사이에 입 밖으로 떨어져 나왔다. 엘리너는 위선에 도무지 서툰 사람이었고, 마음이 온통 애러빈 씨로 가득한 판에 그를 남 이야기하듯 계속 이야기한다는 것은 도저히 할 수 없는 일이었다. 손 양이 조심스러운 열의로 제 계획을 밀고 나가며 애러빈 씨가 혼인을 하는 것이 좋지 않겠느냐고 젊은 미망인의 의견을 물었을 때, 엘리너로서는 사실을 고백하는 수밖에 없었다. “그렇겠지요.” 엘리너가 조금 겸연쩍게 말했다. 그러자 손 양은 그 생각을 한층 넓혀 이야기했다. “아, 손 양.” 엘리너가 말했다. “그분은 혼인을 하실 거예요. 제가 그분과 약혼했답니다.”\n\n그런데 손 양은 그날 아침 볼드 부인과 함께 거닐 때만 해도 그런 약혼 따위는 없었다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다. 또한 그런 약혼에 이르는 사전 절차조차 없었으리라는 것을 어지간히 확신할 만큼은 들어 알고 있었다. 그러니 앞서 우리가 적어 둔 대로 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐던 것이다. 그럼에도 그녀는 손님을 끌어안고 진심으로 축하해 주었다.\n\n엘리너는 그날 저녁 애러빈 씨에게 다른 말을 한마디도 건넬 기회가 없었다. 온 세상이 들어도 좋을 말이라면 몰라도, 그런 말이 몇 마디나 되었겠는가. 손 양은 두 사람을 단둘이 있게 해 주려고 애를 썼으나, 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 모르는 손 씨와 또 한 사람의 손님, 곧 그의 벗이 그 갸륵한 뜻을 아주 망쳐 놓았다. 그리하여 가엾은 엘리너는 애정의 표시 하나 받지 못한 채 잠자리에 들어야 했다. 그러나 그녀의 처지를 딱하다고 할 것은 아니었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그녀는 일찍 일어났다. 평소의 아침 식사 시간보다 조금 일찍 내려가면 식당에서 애러빈 씨를 혼자 만날 수 있을지도 모른다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그이 역시 그렇게 하면 마주할 수 있으리라고 헤아리고 있지는 않을까? 이런 생각으로 엘리너는 울라손 집안의 아침 기도 시각보다 꼬박 한 시간이나 먼저 옷을 갖추어 입었다. 그러고도 곧바로 내려가지는 않았다. 연인을 만나고 싶어 안달하는 것처럼 보일까 겁이 났던 것이다. 하기야 그녀의 안달이 얼마나 대단했는지는 하늘이 알 일이었다. 그래서 그녀는 창가에 앉아 몇 번이고 시계를 들여다보면서, 이제는 나서도 되겠다 싶어질 때까지 아이에게 젖을 물리고 있었다.\n\n식당 문 앞에 이르러 그녀는 손잡이를 돌리기를 망설이며 잠시 서 있었다. 그러나 안에서 손 씨의 목소리가 들리자 더는 망설이지 않았다. 목적은 어그러졌고, 이제는 조심스러운 처신에 조금도 흠이 갈 염려 없이 아무 때나 들어가도 좋았다. 손 씨와 애러빈 씨는 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 베엘제붑 망아지의 됨됨이를 논하고 있었다. 아니, 정확히는 손 씨가 논하고 애러빈 씨는 듣고 있었다. 그 흥미로운 짐승은 마구간 벽에 꼬리 밑동을 문질러 대어 울라손의 말 관리 책임자에게 적잖은 불안을 안겨 준 참이었다. 엘리너가 1분만 더 기다렸던들 손 씨는 마구간에 가 있었을 것이다.\n\n손 씨는 여자 손님을 보자 제 근심을 눌러 두었다. 베엘제붑 망아지는 그 없이 견뎌야 했다. 그리하여 세 사람은 서로 이렇다 할 말도 없이 서 있었는데, 마침내 주인은 아끼는 어린 말을 두고 조마조마해하는 지금의 상태를 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 볼드 부인에게 정성 들여 양해를 구하고 빠져나갔다. 그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자 엘리너는 차라리 그가 남아 있었더라면 싶었다. 애러빈 씨가 두려워서가 아니라, 그에게 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지 아직 알 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧 그녀를 그 난처함에서 건져 주었다. 그가 다가와 그녀의 두 손을 제 손에 쥐고 말했다. “자, 엘리너, 당신과 나는 부부가 되는 것이지요. 그렇지요?”\n\n그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그녀의 입술은 한 음절을 지어 보였다. 소리는 내지 않았으나 그는 그 얼굴에서 그렇다는 대답을 또렷이 읽을 수 있었다.\n\n“이것은 큰 소임입니다.” 그가 말했다. “참으로 큰 소임이지요.”\n\n“그래요, 그래요.” 엘리너가 말했다. 그가 한 말을 그가 뜻한 그대로의 뜻으로 받아들인 것은 아니었다. “참으로, 참으로 큰 소임이지요. 저는 그에 값하도록 온 힘을 다하겠어요.”\n\n“나 또한 그에 값하도록 온 힘을 다하겠습니다.” 애러빈 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다. 그러고는 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두른 채 그 자리에 서서 불을 바라보았고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 그 곁에 서서 제 자리에 흡족해했다. 둘 다 말이 없었고, 말이 아쉽지도 않았다. 서로에게 해야 할 말은 이미 다 한 뒤였다. 그렇다, 그렇다는 대답을 엘리너는 제 나름의 방식으로 했고, 그 방식은 애러빈 씨에게 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 것이었다.\n\n그리고 이제 두 사람에게는 서로의 사랑을 확신하는 그 기쁨을 누리는 일이 남아 있었다. 그 호사가 얼마나 큰 것인가! 하느님이 그 피조물에게 허락하신 다른 어떤 즐거움도 그것에 얼마나 미치지 못하는가! 그리고 여자의 마음에는 그것이 곱절로 감미로운 것이다!\n\n담쟁이가 제 탑을 찾았을 때, 여린 덩굴이 제 튼튼한 담을 찾았을 때, 그 붙어사는 식물들이 어떻게 자라고 번성하는지를 우리는 안다. 그것들은 홀로 가지를 뻗어 여름의 볕과 겨울의 폭풍을 보호도 없이 견디도록 지어진 것이 아니다. 홀로 있으면 그것들은 땅바닥에 퍼져 침침한 그늘 속에 눈에 띄지 않게 웅크릴 뿐이다. 그러나 든든한 버팀목을 찾고 나면 그 아름다움이 얼마나 놀라운가. 얼마나 온 사방에 뻗치며 얼마나 당당한가! 담쟁이 없는 망루가 무엇이며, 아름다움과 향기를 주는 재스민 없는 높다란 정원 담이 무엇이겠는가? 인동덩굴 없는 산울타리는 그저 산울타리일 뿐이다.\n\n아직도 반쯤은 남아 있으나 이제 인간 본성의 힘에 반쯤은 꺾인 생각이 하나 있다. 여자는 남편의 권리가 그것을 인정하도록 강요하기 전까지는 제 사랑을 부끄러워해야 한다는 생각이다. 우리는 기꺼이 그와 다른 교리를 설파하고자 한다. 여자는 제 사랑을 자랑스러워해야 한다. 다만 그런 까닭에, 그 사랑이 제 자랑에 값하는 것이 되도록 더욱 조심할 일이다.\n\n엘리너는 실로 제 사랑을 자랑스러워했고, 그것이 자랑스러운 것으로 여겨질 만하다고 느꼈으며 또 그렇게 느낄 만한 근거가 있었다. 운명과 손 씨가 허락하기만 했다면 그녀는 그의 팔에 안긴 채 몇 시간이라도 그렇게 서 있을 수 있었을 것이다. 순간순간 그녀는 그의 품으로 더 가까이 파고들었고, 제 집이 바로 여기라는 것을 점점 더 확신했다. 이제 그녀에게 부주교의 오만함이며 언니의 냉랭함이며 사랑하는 아버지의 유약함이 무엇이겠는가? 샬럿 스탠호프 같은 벗들의 표리부동을 무엇 하러 마음에 두겠는가? 그녀는 온갖 부당함에서 자기를 지켜 줄 튼튼한 방패를, 이제부터 여울과 암초 사이로 자기를 이끌어 줄 믿음직한 키잡이를 찾아낸 것이다. 그녀는 홀로 서는 무거운 짐을 내려놓고, 다시 한번 여자의 자리와 믿고 사랑하는 아내의 본분을 떠맡으려 했다.\n\n그리고 그 역시 제 자리에 온전히 만족하여 그곳에 서 있었다. 두 사람은 마치 거기서 제 앞날의 운명을 읽어 낼 수 있기라도 한 듯 불을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 마침내 엘리너가 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸다. “어쩌면 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요.” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. 실로 그의 얼굴은 슬프다고까지는 못 해도 적어도 심각하기는 했다. “어쩌면 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요, 내 사랑.”\n\n“슬프다니요.” 그가 그녀를 내려다보며 말했다. “아니오, 결코 슬프지 않습니다.” 그녀의 달콤하고 사랑스러운 눈이 그를 향하고 있었고, 그가 대답하는 동안 그녀는 부드럽게 미소 지었다. 그 유혹은 애러빈 씨의 조신한 예법으로도 당해 낼 수 없을 만큼 강했고, 그는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혀 제 입술을 그녀의 입술에 갖다 대었다.\n\n이 일이 있고 곧바로 손 씨가 나타났다. 그리고 두 사람은 베엘제붑 망아지의 꼬리가 크게 상하지는 않았다는 소식을 듣고 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다.\n\n하딩 씨는 바체스터로 돌아오는 대로 되도록 빨리 울라손으로 달려갈 작정이었다. 수석사제직을 두고 부주교에게 반기를 들려는 참이라 딸의 지지를 얻어 두기 위해서였다. 그러나 볼드 부인이 뜻밖에 집으로 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고 그 걸음을 덜게 되었다. 딸의 쪽지를 읽자마자 그는 길을 나섰고, 제 집에서 자기를 기다리고 있는 딸을 만났다.\n\n두 사람이 서로에게 들려줄 이야기가 얼마나 많았으며, 제가 들려줄 이야기가 상대를 깜짝 놀라게 하리라고 두 사람 다 얼마나 확신하고 있었던가!\n\n“얘야, 어찌나 너를 보고 싶었는지 모른다.” 하딩 씨가 딸에게 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n“아버지, 저도 드릴 말씀이 얼마나 많은지 몰라요!” 딸이 그 포옹에 답하며 말했다.\n\n“얘야, 저쪽에서 나에게 수석사제 자리를 주겠다는구나!” 하딩 씨가 말했다. 그 느닷없는 고백으로 그는 엘리너가 전할 소식보다 앞서 나가 버렸다.\n\n“어머, 아버지.” 그녀가 말했다. 놀라운 소식이 안겨 준 기쁨에 제 사랑과 행복은 잊어버린 것이었다. “어머, 아버지, 그게 정말인가요? 아버지, 그 소식에 저는 얼마나, 얼마나 마음이 온통 기쁜지 몰라요!”\n\n“그런데 얘야, 나는 그 자리를 사양하는 것이 좋겠다고 생각한단다.”\n\n“어머, 아버지!”\n\n“내가 설명해 주면 너도 내 생각에 동의할 게다, 엘리너. 얘야, 내가 몇 살인지 너도 알지 않느냐. 앞으로 산다 한들 나는—”\n\n“하지만 아버지, 제 이야기도 말씀드려야 해요.”\n\n“그래, 얘야.”\n\n“아버지가 이 일을 어떻게 받아들이실지 정말 궁금해요.”\n\n“무엇을 받아들인단 말이냐?”\n\n“아버지가 기뻐해 주지 않으시면, 그 일이 아버지를 행복하게 해 드리지 못하면, 아버지가 저를 북돋아 주지 않으시면, 저는 가슴이 무너지고 말 거예요.”\n\n“그런 일이라면 넬리, 나야 반드시 너를 북돋아 주고말고.”\n\n“하지만 그러지 않으실까 봐 겁이 나요. 정말이지 그러지 않으실까 봐 겁이 나요. 그렇지만 아버지도 제가 하느님의 땅 위에 사는 여자 가운데 가장 복 받은 여자라고 여기지 않으실 수 없을 거예요.”\n\n“정말 그러냐, 우리 딸? 그렇다면 나도 반드시 너와 함께 기뻐하마. 자, 넬리, 이리 오너라. 그게 무슨 일인지 아비에게 말해 보렴.”\n\n“저는 이제—”\n\n그는 딸을 소파로 이끌고 그 곁에 앉아 딸의 두 손을 제 손에 쥐었다. “너 혼인을 하려는 게로구나, 넬리. 그렇지 않으냐?”\n\n“네.” 그녀가 가냘프게 말했다. “아버지가 허락해 주신다면요.” 그러고는 얼마 전에 제 입으로 아버지께 드린 약속, 애러빈 씨와 약혼하면서 까맣게 잊어버렸던 그 약속이 떠올라 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n하딩 씨는 사위로 맞아들이라고 청함을 받게 될 사내가 대체 누구일까 잠시 생각해 보았다. 일주일 전이었다면 누구의 이름을 댈지 의심할 것도 없었으리라. 그런 경우라면 그는 무거운 마음으로일망정 승낙할 채비가 되어 있었을 것이다. 이제 그는 적어도 슬로프 씨는 아니라는 것을 알고 있었으나, 그렇다면 그 자리를 채울 만한 사람이 대체 누구일지 도무지 짐작할 수가 없었다. 한순간 그는 그 사내가 버티 스탠호프일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었고, 그러자 그의 넋이 아주 내려앉았다.\n\n“그래, 넬리?”\n\n“아버지, 저를 봐서라도 그이를 사랑해 주시겠다고 약속해 주세요.”\n\n“자, 넬리, 어서. 그이가 누구인지 말해 보렴.”\n\n“그렇지만 그이를 사랑해 주실 거지요, 아버지?”\n\n“얘야, 네가 사랑하는 사람이라면 나도 사랑할 수밖에 없지 않겠느냐.” 그러자 그녀는 아버지 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려 그 귀에 애러빈 씨의 이름을 속삭였다.\n\n그녀가 댈 수 있었을 어떤 이름도 그를 그보다 더 놀라게 하거나 더 기쁘게 할 수는 없었을 것이다. 제 마음에 드는 사위를 찾아 온 세상을 둘러보았다 한들 애러빈 씨보다 더 마음에 드는 사람은 하나도 고를 수 없었으리라. 그는 성직자였다. 그는 이웃에 성직록을 가지고 있었다. 그는 하딩 씨 자신의 온갖 애착이 가장 가까이 붙어 있는 부류에 속했다. 그는 그랜틀리 박사의 둘도 없는 벗이었다. 게다가 그는 하딩 씨가 그에 관하여 마땅찮게 여길 것이라고는 아무것도 알지 못하는 사람이었다. 그럼에도 그의 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지 기쁨을 곧바로 드러내지 못할 지경이었다. 그는 애러빈 씨를 제 딸과 결부해 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 두 사람에게 무슨 공통된 감정이 있으리라고는 상상조차 해 본 적이 없었다. 그는 딸이 부주교의 허세를 못 견뎌 하는 통에 애러빈 씨 같은 유의 성직자에게 적의를 품게 되었으리라고 걱정하고 있었다. 바라 보라고 했다면 그는 애러빈 씨를 사위로 바랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 알아맞혀 보라고 했다면 그 이름은 결코 떠오르지 않았을 것이다.\n\n“애러빈 씨라니!” 그가 외쳤다. “그럴 리가 없다!”\n\n“아, 아버지, 부디 그이를 나쁘게 말씀하지 말아 주세요! 저를 사랑하신다면 그이를 나쁘게 말씀하지 말아 주세요. 아, 아버지, 이미 다 된 일이고 되돌려서도 안 되는 일이에요. 아, 아버지!”\n\n변덕스러운 엘리너여! 아버지의 승낙 없이는 혼자서 아무것도 정하지 않겠다던 그 약속은 어디로 갔는가? 그녀는 이미 정해 놓고서 이제 와 아버지의 묵인을 요구하고 있었다. “아, 아버지, 그이는 훌륭하지 않아요? 고결하지 않아요? 신앙 깊고 높은 뜻을 지녔고, 좋은 사람이 갖출 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추지 않았어요?” 그녀는 아버지에게 매달려 승낙해 달라고 애원했다.\n\n“내 넬리, 내 아이, 내 귀한 딸아! 그렇고말고, 그이는 고결하고 훌륭하고 높은 뜻을 지녔지. 여자가 사랑할 만하고 남자가 우러러볼 만한 것을 다 갖추었지. 그이는 내 아들이 될 게다, 내 친아들이. 그이는 너만큼이나 내 마음에 가까운 사람이 될 게다. 내 넬리, 내 아이, 행복한, 행복한 내 아이야!”\n\n이 만남을 더 좇을 필요는 없겠다. 두 사람은 차츰 새 승진 이야기로 돌아갔다. 엘리너는 그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 그랬듯이 나이가 아주 훌륭한 수석 사제가 되는 데 걸림돌이 될 수는 없다는 것을 아버지에게 입증해 보이려 했으나, 그런 논거는 이제 전보다도 그에게 힘을 쓰지 못했다. 그는 이렇다 할 말 없이 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 이따금 딸에게 입을 맞추고 “그래”라거나 “아니다”라거나 “참으로 그렇구나”라거나 “글쎄다, 얘야, 그 점은 나도 썩 동의하지는 못하겠구나” 하고 말할 뿐, 바체스터 수석 사제가 “될 것이냐 말 것이냐” 하는 문제를 놓고 딱 부러지게 이야기하도록 그를 끌어들일 수는 없었다. 딸과 딸의 행복에 관하여, 애러빈 씨와 그의 미덕에 관하여라면 그는 엘리너가 바라는 만큼 얼마든지 이야기했고 — 사실대로 말하자면 그 바라는 바가 적지 않았다 — 그러나 수석사제직에 관해서는 이제 더 아무 말도 하지 않으려 했다. 그의 머릿속에 새로운 생각 하나가 들어앉은 것이다. 애러빈 씨가 새 수석 사제가 되면 어떻겠는가?\n\n# 제50장. 부주교가 사태에 만족하다\n\n부주교는 바체스터로 가는 길에 하딩 씨로부터 슬로프 씨와 엘리너에 관한 그들의 온갖 예측이 근거 없는 것이라는 확언을 들은 터였다. 그러나 하딩 씨로서는 제 통찰력에 대한 사위의 믿음을 흔들어 놓기가 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다. 그랜틀리 박사에게는 그 일이 너무나도 훤한 증거로 뒷받침되고 끝도 없는 정황으로 입증된 것이었던 터라, 그는 처음에는 엘리너가 그 혐의를 제 입으로 부인하더라는 하딩 씨의 단호한 진술을 사실로 받아들이기를 거부했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 단서를 붙여 물러섰다. 그는 지금으로서는 지난날의 확신을 착오로 여기겠노라고 인정하기에 이르렀는데, 그러면서도 어찌나 빠져나갈 구멍을 남겨 두었던지, 훗날 언제고 엘리너가 슬로프 부인이 되어 세상에 나서는 일이 생긴다면 여전히 이렇게 말할 수 있을 정도였다. “거 보십시오, 제가 그러지 않았습니까. 그때 뭐라고 하셨고 제가 뭐라고 했는지 기억해 두십시오. 그리고 앞으로 두고두고 기억해 두십시오, 이 일에서도 제가 옳았다는 것을 — 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 말입니다.”\n\n그러나 그는 양보를 밀고 나가 엘리너의 집에 들르겠노라고 스스로 나서기까지 했고, 실제로 아버지와 딸이 아직 이야기를 한창 나누고 있는 중에 찾아왔다. 하딩 씨는 들을 말도 할 말도 너무 많았던 터라 그런 영광이 기다리고 있다는 것을 엘리너에게 미리 일러 주는 것을 잊고 있었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그를 맞을 채비가 전혀 되지 않은 채로 현관에서 형부의 목소리를 들었다.\n\n“부주교님이 오셨어요.” 그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 얘야. 너를 보러 오겠다고 나더러 전하라 하더구나. 그런데 솔직히 말하면 나는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었단다.”\n\n엘리너는 아버지가 아무리 붙드는데도 아랑곳없이 달아나 버렸다. 기쁨에 젖은 그 첫 시간에 그녀로서는 부주교의 온갖 철회와 사과와 축하를 도저히 견뎌 낼 수가 없었던 것이다. 그는 할 말이 무척 많을 터였고, 그것을 말하는 품이 어지간히 지루할 터였다. 그리하여 부주교는 응접실로 안내되었을 때 거기서 하딩 씨 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다.\n\n“엘리너는 좀 양해해 주시게.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“무슨 일이라도 있습니까?” 박사가 물었다. 그는 대뜸 슬로프 씨에 관한 진상이 마침내 죄다 드러난 모양이라고 넘겨짚었던 것이다.\n\n“그래, 일이 좀 있네. 자네가 얼마나 놀랄지 나는 그것이 궁금하구먼.”\n\n부주교는 장인의 태도로 미루어 결국 슬로프 씨에 관해서는 자기에게 들려줄 이야기가 없다는 것을 알아차렸다. “아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. “결코 놀라지 않을 겁니다. 이제 저를 놀라게 할 일은 아무것도 없을 테니까요.” 요즈음은 부주교 말고도 아주 많은 사람들이 nil admirari 신조를 받아들이거나 받아들이는 척한다. 그럼에도 그 낯빛으로 판단하건대 그들 역시 저희 할아버지 할머니가 그러했던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 갑작스러운 감정에 사로잡히기 마련이다.\n\n“애러빈 씨가 무슨 일을 했는지 자네는 짐작이나 하겠나?”\n\n“애러빈 씨라니요! 설마 그 스탠호프네 딸에 관한 일은 아니겠지요?”\n\n“아니, 그 여자는 아닐세.” 하딩 씨가 남몰래 제 농담을 즐기며 말했다.\n\n“그 여자가 아니라니요! 그럼 그가 어떤 여자에 관해 무슨 일이라도 저지른답니까? 하실 말씀이 있으시면 어째서 툭 털어놓지 않으십니까? 저는 이런 식으로 알쏭달쏭하게 구는 것을 세상에서 제일 싫어합니다.”\n\n“자네에게 수수께끼를 낼 생각은 없네, 부주교. 물론 지금으로서는 더 퍼져 나가서는 안 될 이야기이네만.”\n\n“말씀해 보시지요.”\n\n“수전은 예외로 하고 말일세. 그 밖의 누구에게도 말하지 않겠다고 나에게 약속해 주게.”\n\n“당치 않은 말씀입니다!” 부주교가 소리쳤다. 애를 태우다 못해 슬슬 화가 나던 참이었다. “애러빈 씨에 관해서 무슨 비밀이 있을 리가 없지 않습니까.”\n\n“다름이 아니라, 그와 엘리너가 약혼을 했다네.”\n\n부주교의 얼굴을 보면 그가 그 말을 한마디도 믿지 않는다는 것이 훤히 드러났다. “애러빈 씨가요! 그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다.”\n\n“어쨌거나 엘리너가 방금 나에게 그렇게 말했네.”\n\n“있을 수 없는 일입니다.” 부주교가 되풀이했다.\n\n“글쎄, 나로서는 그것이 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말하지는 못하겠네. 나도 분명 놀라기는 했네만, 그렇다고 있을 수 없는 일이 되는 것은 아니지 않은가.”\n\n“그 사람이 뭔가 잘못 안 것입니다.”\n\n하딩 씨는 잘못 안 것이 결코 아니라고 잘라 말했다. 집에 돌아가 보면 애러빈 씨가 바로 그 이야기를 하겠다는 뚜렷한 목적으로 플럼스테드에 다녀갔다는 것을 알게 될 터이며, 손 양까지도 그 일을 죄다 알고 있고, 실상 그 일은 숙녀와 신사 사이의 그런 약속이 정해질 수 있는 한 더없이 분명하게 정해진 일이라고 했다.\n\n“맙소사!” 부주교가 엘리너의 응접실을 이리저리 오가며 말했다. “맙소사! 맙소사!”\n\n그런데 이 감탄사들은 분명 믿음을 나타내는 것이었다. 하딩 씨는 마침내 그랜틀리 박사가 그 사실을 믿게 되었다고 제대로 헤아렸다. 첫 번째 감탄은 자기가 들은 소식에 대한 어느 정도의 언짢음을 뚜렷이 드러냈고, 두 번째는 그저 놀라움을 나타냈으며, 세 번째의 어조에서는 어떤 만족의 기색이 언뜻 비치는 것을 하딩 씨가 알아챌 수 있을 듯싶었다.\n\n부주교는 참으로 제 마음속에서 일어나는 바를 그대로 드러내 보였던 것이다. 그는 자기 예상이 온통 얼마나 빗나갔는지를 깨닫고 언짢아하지 않을 수 없었다. 애러빈 씨를 처음 이 고장으로 데려왔을 때 이 혼사를 제 입으로 제안할 만한 복이 있었더라면, 판단력과 지혜에 관한 그의 명성은 적어도 그를 솔로몬 다음가는 자리에 올려놓을 만큼 더해졌으리라. 그런데 어째서 그러지 못했던가? 애러빈 씨가 목사관에 안주인을 두고 싶어 하리라는 것쯤은 내다볼 수 있지 않았던가? 엘리너에게 남편이 필요하리라는 것은 내다보았으면서, 애러빈 씨가 슬로프 씨보다 그녀의 마음을 끌기에 훨씬 알맞은 사람이라는 것 또한 알아차렸어야 하지 않았던가? 부주교는 자기에게 잘못이 있었음을 깨달았고, 당연하게도 그 낭패감을 대뜸 떨쳐 버리지는 못했다.\n\n그다음으로 그의 놀라움이 대단했다. 이 한 쌍의 젊은 멧비둘기가 자기를 상대로 어찌나 시치미를 뗐던지. 어찌나 감쪽같이 자기를 속여 넘겼던지. 그는 엘리너가 실은 제 후원을 받는 애러빈 씨를 사랑하고 있던 바로 그때에 슬로프 씨에게 마음을 두었다는 억측을 놓고 그녀에게 설교를 늘어놓았으며, 게다가 슬로프 씨와의 결합이 두렵다며 다름 아닌 그 애러빈 씨에게 속을 죄다 털어놓기까지 했던 것이다. 부주교가 놀라는 것도 지극히 당연한 일이었다.\n\n그러나 흡족해할 근거 또한 대단히 컸다. 이 혼사만 따로 놓고 보면 그것은 박사를 곤경에서 건져 내기에 더없이 알맞은 일이었다. 우선 슬로프 씨를 동서로 맞을 일이 결코 없으리라는 확신 자체가 큰 위안이었다. 다음으로 애러빈 씨는 그토록 가까운 사이로 얽히기에 그에게 가장 잘 맞는 사람이었다. 그러나 무엇보다 큰 위안은 이 혼사가 슬로프 씨에게 안길 타격이었다. 그는 이제 아내를 잃은 것이 분명했다. 어쩌면 주교관에서의 자리마저 잃을지 모른다는 소문이 수군수군 돌기 시작하고 있었다. 그리고 하딩 씨가 뜻만 굽히지 않는다면 가장 큰 위험도 넘어서게 될 터였다. 그리 되면 슬로프 씨가 스스로 졌음을 인정하고 바체스터를 아주 떠나 버리리라 기대해 봄 직했다. 그러면 부주교는 다시 맑은 공기를 마실 수 있을 것이었다.\n\n“그것 참, 그것 참.” 그가 말했다. “맙소사! 맙소사!” 그리고 이 다섯 번째 감탄의 어조에서 하딩 씨는 부주교의 가슴속에 흡족함이 자리 잡았음을 온전히 알아차렸다.\n\n그러고 나서 하딩 씨는 천천히, 차근차근, 그리고 슬며시 자기 나름의 새 계책을 내놓았다. 애러빈 씨를 새 수석 사제로 앉히면 어떻겠는가?\n\n천천히, 차근차근, 그리고 곰곰이 헤아리며 그랜틀리 박사는 장인의 견해로 기울었다. 그가 애러빈 씨를 아무리 좋아한다 한들, 그 신사의 교회 관련 역량에 대한 그의 감탄이 아무리 진심이라 한들, 장인이 정당하게 얻어 낸 승진을 빼앗는 조치를 그가 승인하지는 않았을 것이다. 장인으로 하여금 그 얻어 낸 승진을 받아들이게 하는 일이 조금이라도 될 법한 일이기만 했다면 말이다. 그러나 부주교는 예전에 한 번, 하딩 씨가 모든 벗들의 권고를 거스르면서까지 제 뜻을 고집스레 지킬 수 있다는 증거를 받아 든 적이 있었다. 눈앞의 이 온순하고 순한 사람이 그것을 그르다고 여기는 한 어떤 수를 써도 제게 주어진 높은 자리를 맡지 않으리라는 것을 그는 어지간히 잘 알고 있었다. 그것을 알기에 그 역시 속으로 몇 번이나 이렇게 되뇌었다. “애러빈 씨를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 앉히면 어떤가?” 마침내 두 사람 사이에 이야기가 정해져, 이튿날 아침 가장 이른 기차로 함께 런던을 향해 떠나되 가는 길에 옥스퍼드에 잠깐 들르기로 했다. 그윈 박사의 조언이 어쩌면 도움이 되리라고 그들은 생각했던 것이다.\n\n이런 일들이 정해지자 부주교는 플럼스테드로 돌아가 여행 채비를 하려고 서둘러 자리를 떴다. 날이 몹시 좋았던 터라 그는 포장 없는 이륜마차를 타고 시내로 들어와 있었다. 하이 스트리트를 몰아 올라가다가 그는 건널목에서 슬로프 씨와 맞닥뜨렸다. 고삐를 꽤 급히 당기지 않았더라면 그를 치고 지나갈 뻔했다. 두 사람은 주교의 서재에서 잊지 못할 마주침을 가진 이래 서로 한마디도 나눈 적이 없었다. 지금도 말은 하지 않았으나 서로 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 슬로프 씨의 낯빛은 여전히 뻔뻔스럽고 여전히 의기양양하며 여전히 도전적이었다. 그랜틀리 박사가 사정을 달리 알고 있지 않았더라면, 제 적수가 수석 사제 자리며 아내며 그가 얻으려 애쓰던 온갖 값진 영예를 죄다 손에 넣었으려니 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 실상 그는 이 세상에서 가진 것을 몽땅 잃었고, 방금 주교에게서 congé를 받아 든 참이었다.\n\n시내를 벗어나면서 부주교는 눈에 익은 히램 요양원 입구를 지나 마차를 몰았다. 거기 문간에는 어수선한 세간을 잔뜩 실은 크고 어수선한 농가 짐마차가 서 있었고, 그 도착을 살펴보고 있는 이는 다름 아닌 착한 퀴버풀 부인이었다. 나들이옷을 차려입지도 않았고, 옷차림이 그리 깨끗하지도 않았으며, 보닛과 숄이 맵시 있지도 않았고, 실상 온몸 어디를 보아도 여자다운 매력이랄 것이 별로 없었다. 그녀는 새집에서 집안일에 바빴고, 세간이 닿을 때 아무도 마주치지 않으려니 여기고 막 밖으로 나와 본 참이었다. 부주교는 그녀가 정신을 차리기도 전에 그녀 앞으로 들이닥쳤다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사나 그 집안과 그녀의 친분은 실로 아주 얕았다. 부주교는 으레 그렇듯 부주교 관구 안의, 거의 교구 전체라고 해도 좋을 성직자를 모조리 알고 있었고, 그 아내들과 식솔들과도 깊고 얕은 차이는 있으나 얼마간 안면이 있었다. 퀴버풀 씨와는 이런저런 일로 얽힌 적이 있었으나 퀴버풀 부인은 거의 본 일이 없었다. 그러나 지금 그는 그녀를 못 본 체 지나치기에는 너무나 너그러운 기분이었다. 퀴버풀 집안 사람들은 하나같이 그랜틀리 박사에게서 더없이 모진 적대를 예상하고 있었다. 그들은 하딩 씨가 요양원의 옛집으로 돌아가기를 그가 얼마나 바라는지 알고 있었고, 하딩 씨에게 수석사제관이라는 새 거처가 제안되었다는 것은 알지 못했다. 그러니 퀴버풀 부인이 자기에게 건네진 말투에 적잖이 놀라고 또 적잖이 기뻐한 것도 무리가 아니었다.\n\n“안녕하시오, 퀴버풀 부인, 그동안 안녕하셨소?” 그가 말을 건네며 마차 밖으로 왼손을 내밀었다. “이렇게 즐겁고 쓸모 있는 일에 힘쓰고 계신 것을 보니 참으로 반갑소이다. 참으로 반갑소.”\n\n퀴버풀 부인은 고맙다고 인사하고 그와 악수를 나누며 미심쩍은 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그 축하와 친절이 비아냥인지 아닌지 분간할 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n“퀴버풀 씨에게 내 말이라고 꼭 전해 주시오.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그가 그 자리에 임명된 것이 나는 기쁘다고 말이오. 편안한 자리요, 퀴버풀 부인. 집도 편안하고. 부인이 여기 들어와 있는 것을 보니 참으로 반갑소. 잘 있으시오, 잘 있으시오.” 그러고는 마차를 몰아 가 버렸고, 부인은 그의 후한 마음씨에 흐뭇해하면서도 어리둥절해했다. 대체로 부주교에게는 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있었으니 퀴버풀 부인에게 너그러움을 베풀 여유쯤은 있었던 것이다. 그는 마차에서 온 세상을 향해 웃음 띤 얼굴로 내다보며, 오직 프라우디 부인과 슬로프 씨만 빼고 바체스터의 모든 사람의 죄를 용서해 주었다. 주교를 만났더라면 그 주교의 머리마저 다정하게 쓰다듬어 주고 싶은 마음이 들었으리라.\n\n그는 세인트 이월드를 거쳐 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 그리 하면 길을 3마일쯤 돌게 되지만, 애러빈 씨에게 우정 어린 말 한마디 건네지 않고서는 마음 편히 플럼스테드를 떠날 수 없을 것 같았다. 목사관에 이르러 보니 교구 사제는 아직 나가 있었으나, 들은 바로 미루어 두 집 사이의 길에서 그를 만나게 되리라는 것을 그는 의심하지 않았다. 과연 그의 짐작이 맞아서, 집으로 가는 길 절반쯤 되는 좁은 모퉁이에서 그는 말을 타고 오는 애러빈 씨와 마주쳤다.\n\n“허, 그것 참, 그것 참, 그것 참.” 부주교가 큰 소리로, 유쾌하게, 더없이 좋은 기분으로 말했다. “허, 그것 참, 그것 참, 그것 참. 그러니까 결국 우리가 더는 슬로프 씨를 두려워할 까닭이 없게 된 것이오.”\n\n“그랜틀리 부인께 들으니 하딩 씨에게 수석 사제 자리를 제안했다더구려.” 상대가 말했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨는 수석 사제 자리보다 더한 것을 잃은 모양이오.” 그러고 나서 부주교는 익살스럽게 웃었다. “자, 자, 애러빈, 비밀 하나는 야무지게도 지켰구려. 이제 나도 죄다 알고 있소.”\n\n“나는 비밀이랄 것이 없었소, 부주교.” 상대가 조용히 웃으며 말했다. “도무지 없었소이다. 하루도 없었소. 내 행운을 안 것이 바로 어제요, 오늘은 당신의 승낙을 청하러 플럼스테드에 다녀왔소. 그랜틀리 부인이 내게 해 준 말로 미루어 그 승낙을 받으리라는 기대를 품게 되었소.”\n\n“진심으로, 진심으로 그러겠소.” 부주교가 다정하게 말하며 벗의 손을 굳게 붙들었다. “내가 바라던 바로 그대로요. 그 사람은 참으로 훌륭한 젊은 부인이오. 빈손으로 그대에게 가지도 않을 것이고, 좋은 아내가 되어 주리라고 나는 생각하오. 그 언니가 나에게 하는 만큼 그 사람이 그대에게 도리를 다한다면 그대는 행복한 사람이 될 것이오. 내가 할 수 있는 말은 그뿐이오.” 그리고 말을 마칠 즈음 박사의 두 눈에는 눈물이 한 방울씩 맺혀 있는 것이 보였을 법도 하다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 부주교가 잡은 손을 따뜻하게 마주 잡았으나 말은 별로 하지 않았다. 가슴이 너무 벅차 말이 나오지 않았고, 자기가 느끼는 고마움을 표현할 수가 없었던 것이다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그가 한 시간을 이야기한 것이나 다름없이 그를 알아들었다.\n\n“그리고 잘 들어 두시오, 애러빈.” 그가 말했다. “나 아닌 그 누구도 그 매듭을 묶지 못하오. 엘리너를 플럼스테드로 데려올 테니 식은 거기서 치릅시다. 내가 수전을 다그쳐 놓을 테니 격식을 갖추어 제대로 해 봅시다. 나는 내일 특별한 볼일로 런던에 가야 하오. 하딩 씨가 나와 함께 가오. 그러나 그대의 신부가 혼례복을 갖추기 전에 돌아와 있을 것이오.” 그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n집으로 돌아가는 길에 부주교는 혼례 잔치 준비로 마음을 채웠다. 그는 앞으로 후하게 대접함으로써 자기가 엘리너에게 끼친 온갖 상처를 갚아 주리라고 크게 결심했다. 슬로프 같은 자와 애러빈 같은 이가 제 눈에 얼마나 다른지를 그녀에게 보여 줄 참이었다. 또 한 가지도 그는 단단히 마음을 정했다. 수석 사제 일이 애러빈 씨에게 유리하게 매듭지어지지 않는다면, 세인트 이월드 목사관 식당에 새 정면과 내닫이창을 내는 것을 무엇으로도 막지 못하리라는 것이었다.\n\n“그래서 우리는 결국 한 방 먹은 셈이오, 수.” 그가 집에 들어서자마자 아내에게 입을 맞추며 말을 건넸다. 그가 아내를 수라고 부르는 일은 일 년에 두세 번을 넘지 않았고, 그런 날은 크게 경사스러운 날이었다.\n\n“엘리너는 우리가 여겼던 것보다 분별이 있었던 게지요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n그리고 그날 저녁 플럼스테드 목사관에는 큰 흡족함이 감돌았다. 그랜틀리 부인은 이제 마음을 열어 애러빈 씨를 그 안에 받아들이겠노라고 남편에게 약속했다. 그때까지 그녀는 그러기를 마다해 왔던 것이다.\n\n# 제51장. 슬로프 씨가 주교관과 그 식구들에게 작별을 고하다\n\n이제 우리는 슬로프 씨와, 또한 주교와, 그리고 프라우디 부인과도 작별해야 한다. 소설에서의 이런 작별은 실제 삶에서의 그것만큼이나 거북한 일이다. 슬픔의 실감이 없으니 실은 그만큼 슬프지야 않지만, 곤혹스럽기로는 조금도 덜하지 않고 대개는 만족스럽지도 못하다. 어떤 소설가가, 어떤 필딩이, 어떤 스콧이, 어떤 조르주 상드나 쉬나 뒤마가 제가 지어낸 이야기의 마지막 장에 흥미를 불어넣을 수 있겠는가? 아이 둘과 인간의 한계를 넘어선 행복을 약속해 보아야 아무 소용이 없고, 필멸자에게 흔히 허락된 세월을 훌쩍 넘도록 더없이 점잖게 살았노라 장담해 보아야 마찬가지다. 오, 대중이여, 그대들을 기쁘게 하는 것은 우리 주인공과 여주인공의 슬픔이다. 그들의 슬픔이거나 그들의 죄이거나 그들의 어리석음이지, 그들의 미덕이나 분별이나 그에 따라 주어지는 보상이 아니다. 정해진 규칙에 따라 마땅히 그리해야 하는 대로 우리가 마지막 몇 쪽을 couleur de rose 로 물들이기 시작하는 순간, 우리는 남을 즐겁게 하는 우리 자신의 능력을 아주 꺼 버리고 만다. 우리가 지루해지면 그대들의 지성을 거스르는 것이요, 그러나 지루해지지 않으면 그대들의 취향을 거스르게 되니 지루해질 수밖에 없다. 근래의 어느 작가는 마지막 쪽까지 흥미를 이어 가고 싶은 마음에 제3권 끝에서 제 주인공을 목매달았다. 그 결과 아무도 그의 소설을 읽으려 들지 않았다. 게다가 누가 제 사건과 대화와 인물과 묘사의 토막들을, 마지막에 이르러 부자연스럽게 눌러 담거나 억지로 늘여 붙이지 않고서, 정확히 930쪽에 꼭 들어맞도록 배분하고 짜 맞출 수 있겠는가? 나 자신도 바로 이 순간 열두 쪽이 모자라, 그것을 어디서 찾아낼까 머리를 쥐어짜다 진력이 나 있음을 내가 모르겠는가? 그러고 나서 모든 것이 끝나고 나면, 그들 가운데 마음이 가장 너그러운 비평가조차 어김없이 우리 결말의 무능함과 어설픔을 들어 우리를 놀린다. 우리가 게을러져서 결말을 소홀히 했거나, 아니면 지루하게 지나친 공을 들였다는 것이다. 결말은 맹탕이거나 부자연스럽고, 지나치게 힘이 들어갔거나 얼이 빠져 있다. 아무 뜻도 없거나 너무 많은 것을 시도한다. 그리고 모든 장면 가운데 마지막 장면은, 우리가 염려하기로 모든 마지막 장면이 그러할 수밖에 없듯이,\n\n두 번째 어린 시절이요 그저 망각일 뿐, 이도 없고 눈도 없고 맛도 없고 아무것도 없느니.\n\n내가 할 수 있는 말이라고는 이것뿐이다. 제 일을 속속들이 아는 어떤 비평가가, 경험이 그를 완벽하게 만들어 줄 때까지 그 일에 공을 들인 끝에, 소설의 마지막 쉰 쪽을 마땅히 쓰여야 할 방식대로 써 보인다면, 나로서는 앞으로 그 본을 힘껏 본뜨는 데 최선을 다하겠노라고. 오직 내 눈에 비치는 빛만을 길잡이로 삼는 한, 나는 성공을 단념하고 있음을 고백한다.\n\n지난 한 주나 열흘 동안 슬로프 씨는 프라우디 부인을 전혀 보지 못했고 주교도 거의 보지 못했다. 그는 여전히 주교관에 살면서 여전히 늘 하던 일과를 해 나갔으나, 교구의 은밀한 일들은 이미 다른 이의 손으로 넘어가 있었다. 그도 이를 똑똑히 보았고 잘 새겨 두었지만, 그 일로 크게 마음이 흔들리지는 않았다. 주교의 일이 그에게 시들해질 무렵 그는 다른 희망에 빠져 있었고, 게다가 교구에 관한 한 프라우디 부인이 자기를 외통수에 몰아넣었다는 것도 알고 있었다. 이 이야기의 첫머리에서, 대체 누가 바체스터의 주교가 될 것인가를 두고 서넛이 어떻게 다투었는지는 이미 설명한 바 있다. 그들 각자는 이제 프라우디 부인이 그 싸움의 승자라는 것을 스스로 인정하거나, 혹은 스스로 자랑하거나 하게 되었다. 그들은 꽤나 혹독한 경쟁 시험을 치렀고, 부인이 facile princeps 로 승자가 되어 나왔다. 슬로프 씨가 한때 부인을 바짝 몰아붙이기는 했으나 그것도 잠시뿐이었다. 히램 요양원이 두 사람 사이의 시금석이 되어야 한다는 것은 말하자면 서로 인정된 바였는데, 이제 퀴버풀 씨가 이미 요양원에 들어앉았으니 그것이 곧 프라우디 부인의 수완과 배짱을 증명하는 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 일도 슬로프 씨의 기운을 꺾지는 못했으니, 그에게는 다른 희망이 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 아아, 마침내 그의 벗 니컬러스 경에게서 수석 사제 자리가 이미 정해졌음을 알리는 쪽지가 날아들었다. 슬로프 씨에게도 마땅한 몫은 돌려주기로 하자. 그는 이 일격을 맞고 넋을 놓은 채 엎어지지도 않았고, 부질없는 탄식에 제 몸을 내맡기지도 않았다. 그때부터 삶에 절망하여 위의 신들과 아래의 신들을 불러 자기를 데려가 달라고 하지도 않았다. 그는 제 의자에 앉아 당장 쓸 돈이 얼마나 손에 있고 앞으로 들어올 돈이 얼마나 되는지 세어 보고, 앞으로 힘을 쏟기에 가장 좋은 무대가 어디일지 궁리한 뒤, 곧바로 베이커 스트리트에 사는 어느 부유한 제당업자의 아내에게 편지를 써 보냈다. 그가 익히 알기로 그 부인은 진지한 젊은 복음주의 성직자를 대접하고 북돋우는 일에 몹시 열심인 사람이었다. 그는 다시금, 제 말로는, “대성당 도시의 공기와 대성당 예배의 성격 그 자체가 제 정신에 맞지 않음을 알게 되어 세상에 나선” 처지였다. 그러고는 한동안 앉아, 주교와 어떤 식으로 갈라설지, 또 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지 굳게 마음을 정했다.\n\n마침내 그는 일어나 푸른(검은) 겉옷을 여미었으니, 내일이면 새로운 숲과 새로운 풀밭으로.\n\n주교를 뵈러 오라는 정식 명령을 받고서 그는 자리에서 일어나 그 명을 따르러 갔다. 그는 종을 울려, 주교님만 괜찮으시다면 자기, 곧 슬로프 씨가 뵈러 갈 준비가 되어 있다고 주인에게 전하라 하인에게 일렀다. 슬로프 씨가 이제 더는 세도를 부리지 못한다는 것을 잘 아는 하인은 “주교님께서 슬로프 씨더러 지금 곧 서재로 오라 하십니다” 하는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다. 슬로프 씨는 제 독립을 내보이려고 십 분쯤 더 기다렸다가 주교의 방으로 들어갔다. 거기에는 그가 예상한 대로 프라우디 부인이 남편과 함께 있었다.\n\n“흠, 하 — 슬로프 씨, 자, 앉으시오.” 신사 주교가 말했다.\n\n“앉게, 슬로프 씨.” 여자 주교가 말했다.\n\n“고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.” 슬로프 씨는 이렇게 말하고는 난롯가로 돌아가, 난로 앞 깔개를 장식하고 있던 안락의자 가운데 하나에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n“슬로프 씨.” 주교가 말했다. “얼마 전부터 내 마음을 눌러 온 문제를 두고 이제는 그대에게 분명히 말해야 할 필요가 생겼소.”\n\n“그 문제가 저와 어떤 식으로든 관계가 있는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?” 슬로프 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소 — 물론이오 — 그렇소, 그것은 분명 그대와 관계된 일이오, 슬로프 씨.”\n\n“그렇다면 주교님, 제가 바람을 하나 말씀드려도 된다면, 저는 그 문제에 관한 논의가 제삼자가 있는 자리에서 우리 사이에 오가지 않기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“놀랄 것 없네, 슬로프 씨.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “논의 따위는 조금도 필요치 않아. 주교님은 그저 당신의 뜻을 밝히려 하실 뿐이니까.”\n\n“나는 그저 내 뜻을 밝히려 할 뿐이오, 슬로프 씨. 논의 따위는 조금도 필요치 않소.” 주교가 아내의 말을 그대로 되풀이하며 말했다.\n\n“그것은, 주교님, 우리 가운데 누구도 장담할 수 있는 일이 아닙니다.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다. “그러나 저로서는 프라우디 부인을 이 방에서 나가시게 할 수도 없고, 제가 여기 남아 있기를 주교님께서 바라신다면 그것을 마다할 수도 없습니다.”\n\n“주교님의 뜻이 그러하시네, 물론.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“슬로프 씨.” 주교가 엄숙하고 진지한 목소리로 입을 열었다. “잘못을 나무라야 한다는 것은 나로서는 괴로운 일이오. 성직자의 잘못을 나무라야 한다는 것은 더욱 괴로운 일이고, 더구나 그대와 같은 자리에 있는 성직자라면 유난히 그러하오.”\n\n“아니, 제가 무엇을 잘못했다는 말씀입니까, 주교님?” 슬로프 씨가 당돌하게 따져 물었다.\n\n“무엇을 잘못했느냐고, 슬로프 씨?” 프라우디 부인이 죄인 앞에 꼿꼿이 서서 그 무시무시한 집게손가락을 치켜들며 말했다. “무엇을 잘못했느냐고 감히 주교님께 여쭙는 겐가? 자네 양심이 —”\n\n“프라우디 부인, 저는 부인과 한마디도 나누지 않겠다는 것을 이 자리에서 아주 분명히 해 두고 싶습니다.”\n\n“아니지, 이 사람아, 자네는 말을 나누게 될 걸세.” 부인이 말했다. “나누어야만 하고말고. 어째서 자네는 그 시뇨라 네로니와 그토록 많은 말을 나누었는가? 어째서 자네는, 그것도 성직자인 자네가, 그런 여자와, 남편이 있는 여자와, 성직자가 어울리기에는 도무지 마땅치 않은 여자와 노상 어울려 다니며 스스로를 욕되게 하였는가?”\n\n“어쨌든 저는 그분을 부인의 응접실에서 소개받았습니다.” 슬로프 씨가 되받았다.\n\n“그리고 자네는 거기서 부끄러운 짓을 했지.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “참으로 부끄러운 짓을. 그때 내가 본 것을 보고도 자네를 이 집에 하루라도 더 두었던 것이 내 잘못이었네. 그 자리에서 당장 내보내라고 우겼어야 했어.”\n\n“프라우디 부인, 부인께 제가 여기서 나가라거나 여기 머무르라거나 우기실 권한이 있다는 것은 저로서는 아직 배우지 못했습니다.”\n\n“뭐라고!” 부인이 말했다. “내 응접실에 누가 드나들어도 되고 누가 안 되는지를 내가 말할 권리가 없다고! 내 하인과 내게 딸린 사람들이 옳지 못한 행실로 그 품행을 물들이지 않도록 지킬 권리가 없다고! 내 딸들을 더러움에서 지킬 권리가 없다고! 슬로프 씨, 내게 그 권한이 있는지 없는지 자네에게 똑똑히 보여 주겠네. 자네는 이제 주교님 곁에서 아무 직임도 맡고 있지 않다는 것을 알아 두는 게 좋을 걸세. 그리고 자네가 쓰던 방은 다른 전속 사제를 위해 주교관에서 곧 필요하게 될 터이니, 형편이 닿는 대로 되도록 빨리 거처를 마련하기 바라네.”\n\n“주교님.” 슬로프 씨가 주교에게 호소하며, 그리하여 부인에게는 완전히 등을 돌린 채 말했다. “이 일을 두고 주교님께서 내리신 결정이 있다면 그것을 주교님 입으로 직접 듣기를 청해도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“물론이오, 슬로프 씨, 물론이오.” 주교가 말했다. “그것은 당연한 요구요. 그래, 내 결정은 그대가 다른 성직 승진 자리를 찾아보는 편이 낫겠다는 것이오. 그대가 근래에 맡아 온 자리에는 그대가 잘 맞지 않는다고 나는 생각하오.”\n\n“그러면 주교님, 제 잘못이 무엇이었습니까?”\n\n“그 시뇨라 네로니가 한 가지 잘못일세.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “참으로 언어도단인 잘못이지. 아주 언어도단이고 아주 망측한 일이야. 저런, 슬로프 씨, 저런! 자네가 복음주의 성직자라니, 참으로 대단하이!”\n\n“주교님, 저는 제가 무슨 잘못으로 주교님 댁에서 쫓겨나는지 알고 싶습니다.”\n\n“프라우디 부인이 하는 말을 그대도 들었잖소.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“주교님, 제가 이 일의 전말을 세상에 밝힐 때에는, 제 자신을 변호하기 위해 반드시 그리하겠습니다만, 주교님께서도 제가 이렇게 적기를 바라지는 않으실 테지요. 곧 주교님께서 사모님의 분부에 따라 저를 내치셨다고, 그것도 제가 참사회의 어느 참사회원의 딸인 다른 부인과 알고 지낸다는 이유로 사모님께서 반대하셨기 때문이라고 말입니다.”\n\n“쓰고 싶은 대로 쓰게, 이 사람.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “허나 자네가 바체스터에서 한 짓을 세상에 알릴 만큼 제정신이 아니지는 않을 걸세. 자네가 그 계집의 발치에 무릎 꿇던 일을, 그 여자에게 발이란 게 있기나 하다면 말이지만, 그리고 그 손등에 노상 침을 발라 대던 일을 내가 못 들었을 성싶은가? 슬로프 씨, 자네가 하는 짓과 하는 말을 삼가라고 충고하네. 자네가 저지른 것보다 못한 일로도 성직을 박탈당한 성직자들이 있었으니까.”\n\n“주교님, 이대로 계속된다면 저는 이 여자를, 프라우디 부인 말입니다, 명예 훼손으로 고발할 수밖에 없겠습니다.”\n\n“슬로프 씨, 이제 그만 물러가는 편이 좋겠소.” 주교가 말했다. “그대에게 치러야 할 잔액이 있다면 수표를 넣어 보내겠소. 지금 형편으로는 그대가 되도록 이른 시각에 주교관을 떠나는 편이 물론 모두에게 나을 것이오. 런던으로 돌아갈 여비와 오늘부터 한 주 동안 바체스터에서 지낼 비용은 내가 대 주겠소.”\n\n“그러나 만일 자네가 이 근처에 남아 있고 싶다면,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 다시는 그 여자를 만나지 않겠다고 엄숙히 서약하고, 또 앞으로 처신을 더 조심하겠다고 약속한다면, 주교님께서 자네 이름을 퀴버풀 씨에게 말씀해 주실 걸세. 그이가 지금 푸딩데일에서 부목사를 구하고 있으니까. 집은 내 짐작에 자네가 쓰기에 넉넉할 테고, 게다가 한 해 50파운드의 봉급도 나올 것이네.”\n\n“부인, 부인께서 저를 대하신 그 방식을 하느님께서 용서하시기를 빕니다.” 슬로프 씨가 지극히 천상적인 눈빛으로 부인을 바라보며 말했다. “그리고 부인, 이것을 기억하십시오. 부인께서도 아직 넘어지실 날이 있을지 모른다는 것을.” 그러고는 지극히 세속적인 눈빛으로 부인을 바라보았다. “주교님으로 말하자면, 저는 그분이 가엾습니다!” 이렇게 말하고 슬로프 씨는 방을 나갔다. 이렇게 하여 바체스터 주교와 그의 첫 심복 전속 사제 사이의 친분은 끝이 났다.\n\n프라우디 부인의 말은 이 점에서 옳았다. 곧 슬로프 씨는 바체스터에서 자기가 한 짓을 세상에 알릴 만큼 제정신이 아니지는 않았다는 것이다. 그는 프라우디 부인의 부당함이나 그 남편의 무능함에 관한 글 한 줄로도 제 벗 타워스 씨를 성가시게 하지 않았다. 앞으로는 그 대성당 도시에서 자기가 한 일에 대한 언급을 모두 접어 두는 것이 현명하리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 방금 적은 면담이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 바체스터를 떠났는데, 기차에 오르면서 제 발의 먼지를 털어 냈다. 그리고 기차가 그를 서둘러 실어 내어 대성당의 탑들이 시야에서 사라질 때에도, 그는 그것을 그리워하며 아쉬운 눈길로 돌아보는 일이 없었다.\n\n슬로프 집안 사람들이 결코 굶는 법이 없다는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이다. 그들은 고양이처럼 언제나 제 발로 떨어지고, 어디에 떨어지든 그 땅의 기름진 것을 먹고 산다. 우리의 슬로프 씨도 그러했다. 도시로 돌아온 그는 그 제당업자가 죽었고 그 미망인이 위로받을 길 없는 처지임을, 달리 말해 위로가 필요한 처지임을 알게 되었다. 슬로프 씨가 그 부인을 위로했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 베이커 스트리트의 그 집에 아주 안락하게 자리를 잡은 자신을 발견했다. 그는 또한 오래지 않아 레드 로드 부근의 교회 하나를 손에 넣었고, 그 도시의 그 일대에서 가장 언변이 뛰어나고 가장 경건한 성직자의 한 사람으로 이름을 떨치게 되었다. 그를 거기에 두고 떠나기로 하자.\n\n주교와 그 부인에 대해서는 더 할 말이 별로 없다. 그때부터 그들의 가정의 화목이 순탄하게 흘러가는 것을 가로막을 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 아주 이내 주교단에 자리가 또 하나 비면서 프라우디 박사는 처음에 그토록 애타게 바라던 상원 의석을 얻었다. 그러나 그때쯤 그는 더 지혜로운 사람이 되어 있었다. 그는 분명 그 자리에 앉았고 이따금 교회 문제에 관한 정부의 견해에 찬성표를 던지기도 했다. 그러나 제가 힘을 쓸 알맞은 무대는 프라우디 부인의 옷장에 바짝 붙은 자리에 있다는 것을 그는 확실히 배웠다. 그는 다시는 거스르려 들지 않았고, 교구를 홀로 다스리는 권위를 바라는 기색조차 보이지 않았다. 혹시라도 자유를 생각할 때면, 사람들이 천년왕국을 생각하듯 그리하였다. 곧 언젠가 올지도 모르나 제 살아생전에 오리라고는 아무도 기대하지 않는 좋은 시절로 여긴 것이다. 프라우디 부인은 여전히 한창때라 할 만하였고 어쨌든 정정했으니, 주교로서는 머잖아 홀아비의 설움을 겪게 되리라고 걱정할 이유가 없었다.\n\n그는 아직도 바체스터의 주교다. 그가 그 자리를 어찌나 훌륭히 빛냈던지 정부는 그를 더 높은 지위로라도 옮기기를 꺼려 왔다. 이 시대의 새삼스러운 풍조가 그를 노후한 사람으로 판정하여 연금을 내려 줄 때까지, 부디 그가 안전한 후견 아래 거기 머물기를. 프라우디 부인으로 말하자면, 우리가 그 부인을 위해 드리는 기도는 부디 영원히 살아 주십사 하는 것이다.\n\n# 제52장. 새 수석 사제가 수석사제관에, 새 워든이 요양원에 들어서다\n\n하딩 씨와 부주교는 함께 옥스퍼드로 향했고, 거기서 교묘한 논변을 거듭한 끝에 라자러스 학장으로 하여금 스스로 이 중대한 물음을 던지게 만들었다. “애러빈 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되어서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?” 물론 학장도 한동안은 하딩 씨를 향해, 당신은 어리석고 지나치게 결벽하며 고집스럽고 마음이 여리다고 설득해 보려 애썼다. 그러나 헛수고였다. 하딩 씨가 그랜틀리 박사에게 굽히지 않았거늘, 하물며 그윈 박사에게 굽힐 리가 있겠는가. 더구나 애러빈 씨를 수석사제관에 들여앉힌다는 그토록 훌륭한 계획이 이미 첫발을 뗀 마당이었다. 학장은 자신의 웅변이 소용없음을 깨닫고, 게다가 애러빈 씨가 곧 하딩 씨의 사위가 된다는 소식까지 듣게 되자, 사정이 그러하다면 자기 또한 오랜 벗이자 후원의 대상이며 제 칼리지의 펠로인 그 사람이 지금 주인 없이 굴러다니는 저 안락한 자리에 앉는 것을 기꺼이 보고 싶노라고 고백했다.\n\n“그렇게 되면 슬로프 씨를 막아 내는 방편도 되겠지요, 학장.” 부주교가 자못 신중한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“그자에게 그럴 가망이 있다면 우리 칼리지 전속 사제에게도 있겠소.” 학장이 말했다. “나는 그 일에 관해서라면 그보다는 사정을 잘 알고 있소.”\n\n그랜틀리 부인의 짐작은 옳았다. 하딩 씨가 정부에 대해 지닌 자격을 높은 자리에 있는 이들에게 알린 장본인이 바로 라자러스 학장이었고, 그는 이제 그 제안을 애러빈 씨에게로 옮겨 놓기 위해 힘닿는 데까지 애써 보겠노라 승낙했다. 세 사람은 함께 런던으로 올라가 일주일을 머물렀는데, 이는 그랜틀리 부인에게 크나큰 불만이었고 필시 그윈 부인에게도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 장관은 이쪽으로 출타 중이었고 그 개인 비서는 저쪽으로 출타 중이었다. 남아 있는 서기들이야 이런 일에 아무것도 할 수 없었으니, 모든 것이 난감하고 어수선했다. 두 박사는 할 일이 넘치는 듯 보였다. 이리 부산을 떨고 저리 부산을 떨고는, 저녁이면 클럽에서 다리가 부러지도록 끌려다녔노라고 투덜거렸다. 그러나 하딩 씨에게는 아무 소임이 없었다. 그는 한두 번, 자기는 바체스터로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다고 제안해 보았다. 그러나 그 청은 단칼에 거절당했고, 그로서는 웨스트민스터 사원에서 시간을 흘려보내는 수밖에 달리 도리가 없었다.\n\n마침내 그 높으신 어른에게서 회답이 왔다. 라자러스 학장은 벨그레이비아 주교를 통해 자신의 제안을 넣어 두었던 터였다. 그런데 이 주교로 말하자면, 교구를 다스리는 영예를 얻은 것은 얼마 되지 않았으나 성직과 정치가 뒤엉킨 세계에서는 무게가 자못 대단한 인물이었다. 그는 성 바울만큼 경건하지는 않았을지 몰라도 적어도 성 바울만큼 지혜로웠고, 여러 사람에게 여러 모습이 되어 주는 재주를 어찌나 요긴하게 부렸던지, 옥스퍼드의 학감들 사이에서 큰 인물이면서도 휘그당 총리의 손에 뽑혀 주교석 가운데 가장 탐나는 자리를 차지하기에 이르렀다. 그윈 박사는 이 주교에게 자신의 소원과 논거를 알렸고, 주교는 그것을 다시 켄싱턴고어 후작의 귀에 넣었다. 후작은 식품국 대집사장이자, 내각 바깥의 직위 중에서는 가장 높은 자리에 앉아 있다고 세상 사람 대다수가 여기는 인물이었으며, 이런 종류의 일이라면 거래에 이골이 나 있었다. 그는 윈저 성의 응접실 양탄자 위에 선 채 커피 한 잔을 들며 장관에게 그 조처를 넌지시 권했을 뿐 아니라, 어느 지체 높은 분의 귀에다 애러빈 씨의 이름을 좋게 흘려 넣어 두기까지 했다.\n\n그리하여 일은 매듭지어졌다. 높으신 어른의 회답이 도착했고, 애러빈 씨는 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되었다. 이 중대한 사명을 띠고 런던에 올라왔던 세 성직자는 소식이 당도한 그날 함께 저녁을 들며 크게 흡족해했다. 그들은 조용하고 점잖은, 성직자다운 태도로 클라레를 가득 채운 잔을 들어 애러빈 씨를 위해 건배했다. 세 사람 모두 더할 나위 없이 만족스러웠다. 라자러스 학장은 자신의 시도에 성공을 거두었으니, 성공이란 누구에게나 달콤한 법이다. 부주교는 슬로프 씨를 짓밟았고, 대학의 은거와 안락에서 끌어낸 그 젊은 성직자를 높은 영예의 자리에 올려놓았다. 적어도 부주교는 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면 슬로프 씨를 짓밟은 것은 부주교가 아니라 정황이었다. 그렇지만 그 가운데 아마도 가장 온전한 만족을 누린 사람은 하딩 씨였을 것이다. 그는 늘 몸에 지니고 다니던 우울한 태도를 잠시 내려놓고 마음속 가장 깊은 흥에서 길어 올린 조용한 농담을 몇 마디 내놓았다. 슬로프 씨의 혼사를 두고 부주교를 놀렸고, 프라우디 부인을 향한 부주교의 온당치 못한 연정을 들먹이며 그를 골렸다. 이튿날 그들은 모두 바체스터로 돌아갔다.\n\n애러빈 씨는 장인이 될 사람의 손에서 장관의 서한을 건네받기 전까지는 그동안 벌어진 일을 아무것도 모르고 있어야 한다는 것이 그들의 계획이었다. 시간을 조금도 허비하지 않으려고 전날 밤 우편으로 그에게 전갈을 띄워, 런던발 기차가 닿는 시각에 수석사제관에 와 있어 달라고 청해 두었다. 애러빈 씨로서는 이 일에 놀랄 구석이 없었다. 어느 결엔가 하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제라는 말이 바체스터 전역에 퍼져 있었고, 바체스터 전체가 종을 울리고 마음을 다해 그를 맞이할 채비를 하고 있었다. 슬로프 씨에게도 물론 제 편이 있기는 했다. 그의 승진을 두고 진심인 척 축하할 태세를 갖춘 자들이 바체스터에 없지는 않았다. 그러나 그의 실패에 가슴이 미어진 사람은 그 편에서조차 없었다. 이 도시의 주민들은, 심지어 마음이 드높고 감격에 잘 젖는 서른다섯 살 처녀들까지도, 자기네의 복락과 이 고장의 복락이 날마다의 성가와 주 두 번의 찬송가에 어떤 신비로운 방식으로 얽혀 있음을 차츰 깨닫기 시작하던 참이었다. 주교관의 씀씀이는 주교 쪽 주장의 인기를 그다지 높여 주지 못했다. 요컨대 여론은 크게 되돌아서 있었다. 하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 된다는 것이 온 세상에 알려지자, 온 세상이 진심으로 기뻐했다.\n\n앞서 말했듯 애러빈 씨는 자기를 수석사제관으로 부르는 전갈에 놀라지 않았다. 그는 엘리너가 자기를 받아들인 뒤로 아직 하딩 씨를 만난 적이 없었고, 장차 장인이 될 이의 서임 소식을 들은 뒤로도 마찬가지였다. 두 사람이 되도록 이른 시각에 서로 얼굴을 마주하는 것보다 더 자연스럽고 더 긴요한 일은 없었다. 하딩 씨와 그랜틀리 박사가 역에서 마차로 실려 왔을 때 애러빈 씨는 수석사제관 응접실에서 기다리고 있었다.\n\n세 사람이 만나 악수를 나눌 때 그 가슴속에는 저마다 적잖은 흥분이 일었다. 누구든 제 이야기를 꺼내어 차분하고 조리 있는 화법으로 늘어놓기에는 지나치게 큰 흥분이었다. 하딩 씨는 몇 분 동안 아예 말문이 막혀 있었고, 애러빈 씨는 자신의 사랑과 행운에 관해 짧고 발작하듯 끊기는 문장으로밖에 말하지 못했다. 그는 수석 사제직에 대한 축하의 말을 나름대로 애써 끼워 넣고는, 다시 제 희망과 두려움으로 돌아갔다. 아들로 받아들여지기를 바란다는 희망과, 이만한 행운을 누릴 자격이 자기에게 있을까 하는 두려움이었다. 그러고는 또 수석 사제 이야기로 돌아와, 이제껏 들어 본 것 중 가장 흡족한 임명이라고 말했다.\n\n“하지만! 하지만! 하지만…….” 하딩 씨는 그렇게 말하고는 더 나아가지 못한 채 애원하는 눈으로 부주교를 바라보았다.\n\n“사실은 이렇소, 애러빈.” 박사가 말했다. “결국 그대는 수석 사제의 사위가 될 운명이 아니오. 나 또한 아니고. 그 점이 더욱 애석한 일이지.”\n\n애러빈 씨는 설명을 구하는 눈길로 그를 보았다. “하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 되시는 것이 아니오?”\n\n“아무래도 아닌 모양이오.” 부주교가 말했다. 애러빈 씨의 얼굴이 조금 어두워졌고, 그는 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보았다. 이 일에 슬퍼할 까닭이 없다는 것은, 적어도 저 두 사람에게는 슬퍼할 까닭이 없다는 것은 그들의 표정에서 훤히 읽을 수 있었다. 그러나 수수께끼는 아직 풀리지 않은 채였다.\n\n“내 나이가 몇인지 좀 생각해 보게.” 하딩 씨가 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n“당치 않은 말씀이십니다!” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“그렇게 말하면야 좋지. 하지만 그런다고 내가 젊은이가 되는 것은 아니지 않은가.” 하딩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 수석 사제는 누가 되는 것이오?” 애러빈 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그렇지, 바로 그것이 문제요.” 부주교가 말했다. “자, 선창자님, 다른 것은 무엇이든 한사코 마다하시니, 그러면 그 사람이 누구인지나 알려 주시지요. 임명장은 저분 주머니 속에 들어 있소.”\n\n눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로 하딩 씨는 편지를 꺼내어 장차 사위가 될 사람에게 건넸다. 그는 짧은 말을 한마디 하려다가 끝내 하지 못했다. 서한을 넘겨준 뒤 그는 코를 푸는 시늉을 하며 벽 쪽으로 돌아섰고, 이윽고 전임 수석 사제가 쓰던 낡고 칙칙한 말총 소파에 앉았다. 그리고 이 자리에서 이 만남에 대한 우리의 서술을 끝맺는 것이 마땅하리라 여겨진다.\n\n하딩 씨가 딸에게서 어떤 환희로 맞아들여졌는지는 우리로서도 감히 그려 낼 재간이 없다. 그녀는 슬픔으로 울고 또 기쁨으로 울었다. 아버지가 그 노년에 이르러서도, 제 생각으로는 그토록 마땅히 얻으신 지위와 세속의 자리를 여전히 갖지 못하신 것이 슬퍼서 울었고, 사랑하는 그 아버지가 정작 당신은 손을 펴서 가지려 하지 않으신 그 좋은 것을 또 하나의 소중한 이에게 내어 주신 것이 기뻐서 울었다. 그리고 여기서 하딩 씨는 다시 한번 자신의 여림을 드러냈다. 서로의 사랑과 애정을 털어놓느라 벌어진 이 북새통 속에서 그는 하이 스트리트의 셋방을 정리하라는 모두의 간청을 끝내 물리치지 못했다. 엘리너는 아버지가 함께 사시지 않는다면 수석사제관에서 살지 않겠다고 했다. 애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨가 자기와 더불어 공동 수석 사제가 되어 주지 않으신다면 수석 사제 노릇을 하지 않겠다고 했다. 부주교는 장인이 매사를 제 뜻대로 하시게 둘 수는 없다고 선언했고, 그랜틀리 부인은 애러빈 씨 내외가 제 저택에서 아버지를 모실 형편이 될 때까지 머무시게 하려고 그를 플럼스테드로 데려갔다.\n\n이런 논거들에 밀렸으니, 여린 노인이 굽히는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그러나 하딩 씨가 마음 놓고 쉬기 전에 아직 치러야 할 일이 하나 더 남아 있었다. 요양원에서 그의 다스림 아래 살던 노인들이 그 뒤로 어찌 지내는지에 관해서는 이 지면에서 별로 이야기하지 않았다. 그렇다고 해서 그가 그들을 잊었다거나, 다스리는 이 없이 어지러운 그 처지에 놓인 그들을 찾아보지 않았다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 그는 꾸준히 그들을 찾아갔고, 근래에는 머지않아 새 주인에게 복종을 서약해야 할 것이라는 뜻을 그들에게 알려 두었다. 이제 남은 사람은 다섯뿐이었다. 그중 하나는 바로 얼마 전에 안식의 자리로 실려 간 터였다. 본래 정원은 열두 명이었으나 남은 이는 다섯뿐이었고, 이제 그 정원은 여자들까지 넣어 스물네 명으로 늘어나게 되어 있었다. 남은 다섯 중에는 오랜 세월 전임 워든의 각별한 사랑을 받아 온 늙은 번스가 있었고, 그 워든을 제집에서 몰아내는 데 보잘것없는 앞잡이 노릇을 했던 에이블 핸디도 있었다.\n\n하딩 씨는 이제 자기가 손수 새 워든을 요양원에 소개하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 여러 사정이 겹쳐 노인들이 퀴버풀 씨를 못마땅해하고 업신여기며 맞을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 또한 퀴버풀 씨 자신도 전임자를 밀어내고 들어간다는 생각을 품은 채 요양원에 들어선다면 양심에 거리끼는 바가 없지 않으리라고 여겼다. 그리하여 하딩 씨는 퀴버풀 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 함께 걸어 들어가, 그 노인들에게 새 주인을 삼가 따르라고 당부하기로 작정했다.\n\n바체스터로 돌아온 그는 퀴버풀 씨가 아직 요양원 사택에서 하룻밤도 자지 않았고 새 소임을 시작하지도 않았음을 알게 되었다. 그래서 그는 자신의 뜻을 그 신사에게 알렸고, 그의 제안은 거절당하지 않았다.\n\n11월이었으나 밝고 맑은 아침에 하딩 씨와 퀴버풀 씨는 팔짱을 낀 채 요양원 문을 걸어 들어갔다. 무슨 일이든 요란을 떨지 않는 것이 우리 오랜 벗의 한 가지 됨됨이였다. 그는 제 인생의 자못 중대한 일에서조차, 우리 대다수가 중대한 일에는 으레 격을 갖춰야 한다고 여기는 그런 요란을 생략했다. 우리는 집들이를 하고 세례식을 하고 경축일을 벌인다. 제 생일은 아니더라도 자식들의 생일은 챙긴다. 사는 집을 옮겨야 할 일이라도 생기면 곧잘 부산을 떨며, 거의 누구나 저마다 자그마한 예식의 날을 지닌다. 하딩 씨에게는 그런 예식의 날이 없었다. 옛집을 떠날 때 그는 그저 날마다의 산책을 나서는 사람처럼 조용하고 담담하게 그곳을 나섰고, 이제 또 한 사람의 워든을 팔에 끼고 그 집으로 되돌아 들어가면서도 똑같이 조용한 걸음과 잔잔한 몸가짐이었다. 그는 5년 전, 아니 이제는 6년이 다 되었으니, 그때보다 허리가 조금 덜 곧았다. 걸음도 아마 조금은 느려졌을 것이고, 발소리도 아마 어딘가 덜 단단해졌을 것이다. 그 밖에는, 그저 벗을 팔에 끼고 돌아오는 길이라 해도 좋았을 법했다.\n\n이 다정함이 퀴버풀 씨에게는 무엇보다 큰 것이었다. 그토록 가난한 처지에 있으면서도, 하딩 씨처럼 친절하고 정중한 동료 성직자를 밀어내고 그 자리에 들어앉는다는 생각은 그에게 몹시 쓰라린 것이었다. 형편이 형편이니만큼 그로서는 내밀어진 은혜를 마다할 도리가 없었다. 자식들에게 돌아올 빵을 물리칠 수도 없었고, 그토록 오래 짓눌려 온 가엾은 아내의 무거운 짐을 덜어 주기를 마다할 수도 없었다. 그럼에도 요양원에 들어감으로써 교구 안 형제들의 미움을 사게 될지 모른다는 생각은 그에게 몹시 괴로웠다. 하딩 씨는 이 모든 것을 남김없이 헤아리고 있었다. 그런 감정들, 그런 속마음의 결을 섬세하게 알아채는 데에는 그의 마음과 지성이 유별나게 잘 맞았다. 세상살이에 속하는 일이라면 부주교는 제 장인을 바보나 다름없다고 여겼다. 그리고 그 판단은 어쩌면 옳았을 것이다. 그러나 제대로 헤아리기만 한다면 그에 못지않게 중요한 다른 몇 가지 일에서는, 하딩 씨가 마음만 먹었다면 제 사위를 바보로 여겨도 그만큼 무리가 없었을 것이다. 그러나 하딩 씨처럼 생겨먹은 사람은 드물다. 그는 본디 여자에게만 오롯이 허락된 저 섬세한 감수성, 남의 마음을 알아보는 그 감각을 지니고 있었다.\n\n두 사람은 팔짱을 낀 채 건물 안쪽 안뜰로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기서 다섯 노인이 그들을 맞았다. 하딩 씨는 그들 모두와 악수를 나누었고, 이어 퀴버풀 씨도 그렇게 했다. 번스와는 하딩 씨가 두 번 악수를 했고, 퀴버풀 씨도 같은 예를 되풀이하려 했으나 노인은 그에게 그럴 틈을 주지 않았다.\n\n“드디어 자네들에게 새 워든이 생겼다니 나는 참으로 기쁘네.” 하딩 씨가 아주 밝은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“저희야 이제 무슨 변화든 겪기에는 너무 늙었습죠.” 그중 한 사람이 말했다. “그래도 다 잘되자고 하는 일이겠습니다요.”\n\n“그렇고말고, 그렇고말고, 다 잘되자는 일이지.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “자네들은 다시 한 지붕 아래에서 자네들 교회의 성직자를 모시게 되었고, 그것도 아주 훌륭한 분을 모시게 되었네. 저렇게 좋은 분이 자네들을 돌보러 오신다는 것, 그것도 낯선 사람이 아니라 내 벗이어서 내가 이따금 들러 자네들을 보고 갈 수 있게 해 주리라는 것이 나에게는 큰 기쁨일세.”\n\n“목사님께 참으로 감사드립니다요.” 그중 또 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n“여러분, 하딩 씨가 저에게 베풀어 주신 친절에, 그것도 청하지도 않았고 뜻하지도 않았던 그 친절에 제가 얼마나 깊이 감사하고 있는지는 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지요.” 퀴버풀 씨가 말했다.\n\n“저분은 늘 인정이 깊으셨습죠.” 세 번째 사람이 말했다.\n\n“저분이 여기 남기고 가신 빈자리를 메우기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠습니다. 여러분을 위해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서 그리하겠고, 무엇보다 저분을 위해서 그리하겠습니다. 그러나 저분을 겪어 아시는 여러분께 제가 저분처럼 사랑받는 벗이자 아버지가 될 수는 결코 없을 것입니다.”\n\n“아니올시다, 나리, 아니올시다.” 여태 잠자코 있던 늙은 번스가 말했다. “그리될 사람은 아무도 없습죠. 새 주교님이 하늘에서 천사를 하나 내려보내 주신대도 안 될 일입니다요. 나리께서 힘껏 하시리라는 것이야 저희도 의심치 않습니다만, 나리는 옛 주인님 같지는 않으실 겁니다요. 적어도 저희 늙은것들에게는요.”\n\n“쯧쯧, 번스, 쯧쯧. 어찌 그런 말을 하는가?” 하딩 씨가 그렇게 나무랐으나, 노인을 꾸짖으면서도 그는 여전히 그의 팔을 붙든 채 따뜻한 정을 담아 꼭 눌러 주었다.\n\n이 일에서 열띤 기색이라고는 도무지 끌어낼 수가 없었다. 마지막 안식처를 향해 비틀비틀 걸어가고 있는 다섯 노인이 낯선 사람을 맞아들이는 일에 어찌 열을 낼 수 있겠는가? 퀴버풀 씨가 그들에게 무엇이며, 그들이 퀴버풀 씨에게 또 무엇이겠는가? 하딩 씨가 정말로 그들에게 돌아왔더라면 늙은 뺨 위에 기쁨의 빛이 마지막으로 한 번쯤 반짝였을지도 모른다. 그러나 퀴버풀 씨가 자녀 열넷을 푸딩데일에서 요양원 사택으로 옮겨 온다고 해서 기뻐하라고 하는 것은 부질없는 노릇이었다. 사실 그들이 몸으로나 영으로나 이로움을 얻게 되리라는 것은 의심할 바 없었으나, 그것은 그들이 미리 헤아릴 수도 없었고 인정할 수도 없는 것이었다.\n\n퀴버풀 씨의 이 소개는 어지간히도 밋밋한 일이었으나, 그래도 효험이 없지는 않았다. 하딩 씨가 뜻한 선의는 땅에 떨어지지 않았다. 다섯 구호 노인을 비롯한 바체스터의 온 세상은, 하딩 씨가 그렇게 퀴버풀 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 그의 첫 취임 길에 함께 걸어 들어갔다는 이유로 그를 한결 더 존중하게 되었다.\n\n그리고 우리는 이 새 거처에 퀴버풀 씨 내외와 그 열네 자녀를 남겨 두고 떠나기로 한다. 섭리가 마침내 그들에게 내려 준 좋은 것들을 부디 마음껏 누리기를!\n\n# 제53장. 맺음말\n\n소설의 끝은 아이들 만찬회의 끝이 그러하듯 과자와 사탕으로 채워져야 하는 법이다. 이제 더 들려줄 것이라고는 애러빈 씨의 혼례에 얽힌 잔치 이야기뿐이고, 더 그려 볼 것이라고는 혼례복밖에 없으며, 더 적어 둘 대화라고는 두 사람을 혼인시킨 부주교와 혼인한 애러빈 씨와 엘리너 사이에 오간 말뿐이다.\n\n“그대는 이 여자를 아내로 맞아들이겠는가”, 그리고 “그대는 이 남자를 남편으로 맞아들여 하느님이 정하신 법도를 따라 함께 살겠는가?”\n\n애러빈 씨와 엘리너는 저마다 “그리하겠나이다” 하고 대답했다.\n\n두 사람이 그 약속을 지키리라는 데에는 우리로서도 의심할 바가 없다. 더구나 시뇨라 네로니는 예식이 치러지기 전에 이미 바체스터를 떠난 뒤였으니 더욱 그러하다.\n\n볼드 부인은 두 번째 남편과 혼인하기까지 이태 남짓 미망인으로 지냈고, 그 무렵 어린 조니는 부축만 받으면 제 다리로 응접실까지 걸어 들어와 모여 있는 손님들의 인사를 받을 만큼 자라 있었다. 하딩 씨가 신부를 넘겨주었고, 부주교가 예식을 집전했으며, 이웃의 다른 젊은 아가씨들까지 거들어 함께 나선 그랜틀리 집안의 두 딸이 부지런하고도 우아하게 들러리 소임을 다했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 피로연 음식과 꽃다발을 감독했고, 메리 볼드는 청첩 카드와 케이크를 나누어 주었다. 부주교의 세 아들도 이 일을 맞아 집에 돌아와 있었다. 맏이는 학문이 대단하여 그를 아는 이라면 누구나 장차 반드시 양과 최우등을 받으리라 여겼다. 그러나 이날 으뜸가는 영광은 둘째의 몫이었으니, 새 군복 차림으로 눈부셨던 까닭이다. 셋째는 이제 막 대학에 들어가는 참이었는데, 아마 셋 가운데 가장 우쭐한 것이 그였을 것이다.\n\n그러나 이날의 모든 일 가운데 가장 볼만한 대목은 부주교의 지나칠 만큼 후한 인심이었다. 그는 말 그대로 아무에게나 선물을 안겼다. 애러빈 씨가 이미 세인트 이월드 목사관에서 나온 뒤였으므로 식당을 늘려 짓겠다던 계획은 물론 없던 일이 되었지만, 허락만 받았더라면 그는 수석사제관의 세간을 죄다 새로 들여놓았을 것이다. 그는 에라르제 훌륭한 피아노를 내려보냈고, 이 나라 어느 수석 사제가 올라타도 자랑스러웠을 만한 승용마를 애러빈 씨에게 주었으며, 엘리너에게는 박람회에서 상을 받은 새 조랑말 마차를 따로 선물했다. 그는 거기서 손을 멈추지도 않았다. 아내에게는 카메오 한 벌을, 볼드 양에게는 사파이어 팔찌를 사 주었고, 딸들에게는 진주와 반짇고리를 쏟아부었으며, 아들들에게는 저마다 20파운드짜리 수표를 한 장씩 안겼다. 하딩 씨에게는 새로운 방식의 장치와 값비싼 부속을 모조리 갖춘 훌륭한 첼로를 내렸는데, 정작 그 새로운 것들 때문에 이 신사는 듣는 이를 흡족하게 하지도 못하고 스스로 즐겁지도 못하여 끝내 그 악기를 제대로 쓰지 못했다.\n\n부주교를 잘 아는 이들은 그 씀씀이의 까닭을 훤히 알고 있었다. 그것이야말로 그가 슬로프 씨를 두고 부르는 개선의 노래였다. 그것이 그의 찬가요, 감사의 송가요, 목청 높은 연설이었다. 그는 허리에 칼을 차고 싸움터로 나아갔고, 이제 적에게서 빼앗은 전리품을 잔뜩 지고 싸움터에서 돌아오는 길이었다. 승용마와 카메오, 첼로와 피아노는 하나같이 말하자면 이제는 정복당한 적의 천막에서 앗아 온 전승 기념물이었다.\n\n애러빈 부부는 혼인한 뒤 요즈음 이런 일에 으레 굳어진 관례를 좇아 두어 달 외국에 나가 있다가, 좋은 조짐 속에 수석사제관의 살림을 시작했다. 그리고 바체스터의 교회 사정이 지금처럼 흐뭇하게 정돈된 적은 없다. 이름뿐인 주교는 결코 참견하는 법이 없고, 프라우디 부인도 자주는 참견하지 않는다. 부인의 활동 무대는 대성당이 있는 도시 하나보다 더 넓고 더 드높으며 그 야심에도 더 걸맞다. 교구를 제 뜻대로 주무를 수만 있다면 부인은 수석 사제와 참사회쯤은 저희끼리 하도록 기꺼이 내버려 둔다. 슬로프 씨가 대성당 경내의 오랜 관습을 뒤엎어 보려다 실패한 일에서 부인은 교훈을 얻었던 것이다. 몸집 좋은 학무 참사원과 여윈 자그마한 참사회원은 안식일 학교 문제로 이래라저래라 하는 청을 받아 시달리는 일이 없고, 수석 사제는 제 영역을 제 뜻대로 다스리도록 맡겨져 있으며, 프라우디 부인과 애러빈 부인의 왕래는 서로 한 해에 한 번씩 베푸는 만찬에 그친다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 만찬에는 끼지 않으나, 프라우디 부인이 무슨 짓을 하고 무슨 말을 했는지 낱낱이 묻고 들어 두는 일만은 한 번도 거른 적이 없다.\n\n그의 교회 내 권위는 아버지를 대신해 주교관의 궁재로 군림하던 전성기에 견주면 크게 깎여 나갔으나, 그럼에도 이제 그에게 남은 만큼의 권위는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 누릴 수 있다. 그는 자기를 보는 이들이 자기의 자격을 슬로프 씨의 그것과 견주고 있다는 느낌 없이 바체스터의 하이 스트리트를 걸어 내려갈 수 있다. 플럼스테드와 수석사제관 사이의 왕래는 더할 나위 없이 잦고 허물없다. 엘리너가 성직자와, 그것도 교회의 고위 성직자와 혼인한 뒤로 그랜틀리 부인은 동생과 마음이 맞는 구석을 훨씬 더 많이 찾아냈고, 모두가 자못 기다리고 있는 다가올 일을 맞아 수석사제관에서 한두 달 지낼 작정이다. 어린 조니 볼드가 태어났을 때에는 바체스터에서 한 달을 지낼 생각 따위는 해 본 적도 없던 사람이!\n\n두 자매는 교리 문제에서는 그리 뜻이 맞지 않으나, 그 견해차는 더없이 정다운 종류의 것이다. 애러빈 부인의 신앙은 그랜틀리 부인의 그것보다 두 단계쯤 높다. 엘리너가 한때 슬로프 씨에게 마음이 기울었다는 혐의를 받았던 일을 기억하는 이에게는 이것이 이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 사실은 그래도 사실이다. 부인은 남편의 비단 조끼를 좋아하고, 남편이 예식 규정을 지키는 것을 좋아하며, 특히 남편의 설교에 담긴 유려한 사색을 좋아하고, 제 기도서에 붉은 글씨로 적힌 대목을 좋아한다. 그렇다고 부인이 촛불을 즐긴다거나 실재 임재를 두고 갈피를 못 잡고 있다고 지레짐작해서는 안 되겠지만, 그쪽으로 어렴풋이 마음이 기울어 있기는 하다. 부인은 근래 배스에서 치러진 어떤 몹시 무거운 교회 소송 비용에 넉넉한 기부금을 보냈는데, 물론 이름은 내지 않았다. 캔터베리 대주교의 이름이 나오면 부드럽게 비웃는 미소를 지으며, 대성당에는 기념 창을 하나 세워 두었다.\n\n소책자들이 쓰이고 젊은 성직자들이 교회를 청소하는 지극히 갸륵한 소임을 떠맡기 이전, 그러니까 오십 년쯤 전의 고교회파에 해당하는 고답건조파에 속하는 그랜틀리 부인은 동생을 두고 적잖이 웃는다. 부인은 어깨를 으쓱하며 손 양에게, 엘리너가 저러다 끝내는 수석사제관 안에 기도실까지 두게 될 모양이라고 말한다. 그렇다고 그 때문에 조금이라도 언짢아하는 것은 아니다. 고교회파다운 별난 짓 몇 가지쯤은 젊은 수석 사제 부인의 어깨에 얹혀도 어울리지 않을 것이 없다고 부인은 생각한다. 그것은 어쨌든 동생이 그 문제에 진심을 두고 있다는 표시요, 나아가 한때 동생이 그 안에서 뒹굴고 기게 되리라 의심받았던 그 가증스러운 시궁창에서 하늘과 땅만큼 멀리 떨어져 나왔다는 표시이기도 하다. Anathema maranatha! 저것만 제대로 저주받는다면 다른 무엇이든 복되다 하여도 좋다. 무릎 꿇음도 좋고 절함도 좋고, 아침기도도 좋고 끝기도도 좋고, 종과 책과 촛불도 다 좋으니, 다만 슬로프 씨의 더러운 중백의와 형식뿐인 안식일이 마땅한 저주를 받기만 한다면!\n\n굳이 둘 가운데 하나를 반드시 골라야 한다면, 우리로서도 종과 책과 촛불 쪽이 그래도 덜한 해악이라는 그랜틀리 부인의 말에 손을 들어 주고 싶다. 다만 이 책장 안에서는 그렇게 골라야 할 필요 따위는 인정하지 않는다는 점을 밝혀 두기로 하자.\n\n애러빈 박사는(수석 사제가 되었으니 박사도 되었으려니 우리는 짐작한다) 교리 문제에서 아내보다 온건하고 말수도 적은데, 실상 그 자리에 있는 사람으로서는 마땅히 그래야 한다. 그는 학구적이고 사려 깊으며 부지런히 일하는 사람이다. 수석사제관에 늘 머물러 살며 거의 주일마다 설교한다. 그의 시간은 옛 교회 문헌을 가려 뽑아 엮는 일과 같은 것을 새로 써내는 일에 바쳐진다. 옥스퍼드에서 그는 이 시대 성직자 가운데 가장 앞날이 밝은 자랑거리로 여겨진다. 그와 아내는 서로를 온전히 믿으며 함께 산다. 아내의 가슴속에는 남편과 나누지 않은 비밀이 단 하나 있다. 그는 슬로프 씨가 어쩌다 따귀를 맞았는지를 아직껏 알지 못한다.\n\n스탠호프 일가는 이제 슬로프 씨의 힘이 저희를 이탈리아 별장의 즐거움에서 더는 떼어 놓을 수 없게 되었음을 이내 알아차렸다. 엘리너가 혼인하기 전에 그들은 벌써 모두 코메 호숫가로 돌아가 있었다. 다시 자리를 잡은 지 얼마 되지 않아 시뇨라는 애러빈 부인에게서 아주 예쁘장하되 아주 짧은 편지를 한 통 받았고, 그것으로 쓴 이의 신상에 일어난 일을 알게 되었다. 이 편지에는 또 한 통의 답장이 갔으니, 시뇨라의 편지가 언제나 그러하듯 반짝이고 매혹적이고 재치 있는 것이었다. 그리고 그것으로 엘리너와 스탠호프 일가의 우정은 끝이 났다.\n\n하딩 씨에 관해 한마디만 하면 우리 일은 끝난다. 그는 여전히 바체스터의 선창자이며, 여전히 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회를 맡은 목자다. 스스로 그토록 자주 말해 온 것과는 달리, 그는 아직 노인도 아니다. 제 몫으로 떨어진 소임을 훌륭하고 양심껏 해내며, 자기에게 덜 어울렸을 법한 다른 소임을 떠맡고 싶은 유혹에 한 번도 빠져 본 적이 없음을 고맙게 여긴다.\n\n이제 글쓴이는 그를 독자의 손에 맡긴다. 영웅으로서가 아니고, 우러르고 입에 올릴 만한 인물로서도 아니며, 공식 만찬 자리에서 축배의 대상이 되고 완전무결한 성직자라는 상투적인 허튼소리로 일컬어질 만한 인물로서도 아니라, 다만 거짓 없는 선한 사람으로서, 자기가 가르치려 애써 온 종교를 겸손히 믿고 자기가 배우려 애써 온 계율을 따라 살아온 사람으로서 맡긴다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-3409"]=[[["# CHAPTER I. Who Will Be the New Bishop?", "# 제1장. 새 주교는 누가 될 것인가"]], [["In the latter days of July in the year 185--, a most important question was for ten days hourly asked in the cathedral city of Barchester, and answered every hour in various ways--Who was to be the new bishop?", "185X년 7월 하순, 대성당 도시 바체스터에서는 열흘 동안 시시각각 대단히 중대한 물음이 오갔고, 그 물음에는 매시간 갖가지 답이 나왔다 — 새 주교는 누가 될 것인가?"]], [["The death of old Dr. Grantly, who had for many years filled that chair with meek authority, took place exactly as the ministry of Lord ---- was going to give place to that of Lord ----.", "오랜 세월 그 자리를 온화한 권위로 지켜 온 늙은 그랜틀리 박사의 죽음은, 하필 ---- 경의 내각이 ---- 경의 내각에 자리를 내주려던 바로 그때에 일어났다."], ["The illness of the good old man was long and lingering, and it became at last a matter of intense interest to those concerned whether the new appointment should be made by a conservative or liberal government.", "이 선량한 노인의 병환은 길고도 더디게 이어졌고, 마침내 관계된 이들에게는 새 임명이 보수당 정부의 손에서 이루어질 것인가 자유당 정부의 손에서 이루어질 것인가가 지극한 관심사가 되었다."]], [["It was pretty well understood that the outgoing premier had made his selection and that if the question rested with him, the mitre would descend on the head of Archdeacon Grantly, the old bishop's son.", "물러나는 총리가 이미 사람을 골라 두었다는 것, 그리고 그 문제가 그의 손에 달려 있다면 주교의 관이 늙은 주교의 아들인 그랜틀리 부주교의 머리 위에 내려앉으리라는 것은 웬만큼 알려진 사실이었다."], ["The archdeacon had long managed the affairs of the diocese, and for some months previous to the demise of his father rumour had confidently assigned to him the reversion of his father's honours.", "부주교는 오래전부터 교구의 사무를 도맡아 왔고, 아버지가 세상을 뜨기 몇 달 전부터 소문은 아버지의 영예가 고스란히 그에게 물려지리라고 자신 있게 못 박고 있었다."]], [["Bishop Grantly died as he had lived, peaceably, slowly, without pain and without excitement.", "그랜틀리 주교는 살아온 그대로, 평온하게, 천천히, 고통도 없고 동요도 없이 죽었다."], ["The breath ebbed from him almost imperceptibly, and for a month before his death it was a question whether he were alive or dead.", "숨은 거의 알아채지 못할 만큼 조금씩 그에게서 빠져나갔고, 죽기 한 달 전부터는 그가 살아 있는지 죽었는지조차 물어야 할 지경이었다."]], [["A trying time was this for the archdeacon, for whom was designed the reversion of his father's see by those who then had the giving away of episcopal thrones.", "부주교에게는 견디기 힘든 시기였다. 그때 주교좌를 나누어 줄 권한을 쥐고 있던 이들은 아버지의 교구를 그에게 물려줄 작정이었던 것이다."], ["I would not be understood to say that the prime minister had in so many words promised the bishopric to Dr.", "나는 총리가 그랜틀리 박사에게 주교직을 말로 똑똑히 약속했다고 말하려는 것이 아니다."], ["Grantly. He was too discreet a man for that.", "그는 그러기에는 지나치게 조심스러운 사람이었다."], ["There is a proverb with reference to the killing of cats, and those who know anything either of high or low government places will be well aware that a promise may be made without positive words and that an expectant may be put into the highest state of encouragement, though the great man on whose breath he hangs may have done no more than whisper that \"Mr.", "고양이를 죽이는 방법에 관한 속담이 하나 있거니와, 높든 낮든 관청의 사정을 조금이라도 아는 이라면 분명한 말 없이도 약속이 이루어질 수 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 입만 쳐다보고 있는 기대자를 더없이 고무된 상태로 올려놓는 데에는 그 위대한 인물이 “아무개 씨는 확실히 떠오르는 사람이지”"], ["So-and-So is certainly a rising man.\"", "하고 속삭이는 것 이상이 필요치 않다는 것을 잘 알 것이다."]], [["Such a whisper had been made, and was known by those who heard it to signify that the cures of the diocese of Barchester should not be taken out of the hands of the archdeacon.", "그런 속삭임이 있었고, 그것을 들은 이들은 그 말이 바체스터 교구의 사목이 부주교의 손에서 빠져나가지 않으리라는 뜻임을 알았다."], ["The then prime minister was all in all at Oxford, and had lately passed a night at the house of the Master of Lazarus.", "당시의 총리는 옥스퍼드에서 절대적인 인물이었고, 얼마 전 라자러스 학장의 집에서 하룻밤을 묵은 일이 있었다."], ["Now the Master of Lazarus--which is, by the by, in many respects the most comfortable as well as the richest college at Oxford--was the archdeacon's most intimate friend and most trusted counsellor.", "그런데 라자러스의 학장은 — 덧붙여 두자면 라자러스 칼리지는 여러모로 옥스퍼드에서 가장 안락하고 또한 가장 부유한 칼리지다 — 부주교의 가장 가까운 벗이자 가장 미더운 조언자였다."], ["On the occasion of the prime minister's visit, Dr. Grantly was of course present, and the meeting was very gracious.", "총리가 방문한 그 자리에 그랜틀리 박사도 물론 함께 있었고, 만남은 대단히 화기애애했다."], ["On the following morning Dr. Gwynne, the master, told the archdeacon that in his opinion the thing was settled.", "이튿날 아침 학장인 그윈 박사는 부주교에게 자기가 보기에 일은 이미 결정된 것이나 다름없다고 일러 주었다."]], [["At this time the bishop was quite on his last legs; but the ministry also were tottering.", "이 무렵 주교는 완전히 마지막 고비에 있었다. 그러나 내각 또한 휘청거리고 있었다."], ["Dr. Grantly returned from Oxford, happy and elated, to resume his place in the palace and to continue to perform for the father the last duties of a son, which, to give him his due, he performed with more tender care than was to be expected from his usual somewhat worldly manners.", "그랜틀리 박사는 옥스퍼드에서 흡족하고 들뜬 기분으로 돌아와 주교관에서 제자리를 되찾고, 아버지에게 아들로서의 마지막 도리를 이어서 다했는데, 공평하게 말하자면 그는 평소의 다소 세속적인 몸가짐에서 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 자상한 정성으로 그 일을 해냈다."]], [["A month since, the physicians had named four weeks as the outside period during which breath could be supported within the body of the dying man.", "한 달 전에 의사들은 죽어 가는 이의 몸 안에 숨이 붙어 있을 수 있는 기간을 길어야 4주라고 못 박았다."], ["At the end of the month the physicians wondered, and named another fortnight.", "그 한 달이 다 가자 의사들은 고개를 갸웃하며 다시 2주를 말했다."], ["The old man lived on wine alone, but at the end of the fortnight he still lived, and the tidings of the fall of the ministry became more frequent.", "노인은 포도주만으로 연명했으나 2주가 지나도 여전히 살아 있었고, 내각이 무너진다는 소식은 점점 잦아졌다."], ["Sir Lamda Mewnew and Sir Omicron Pie, the two great London doctors, now came down for the fifth time and declared, shaking their learned heads, that another week of life was impossible; and as they sat down to lunch in the episcopal dining-room, whispered to the archdeacon their own private knowledge that the ministry must fall within five days.", "런던의 두 명의(名醫) 램다 뮤뉴 경과 오미크론 파이 경이 이번으로 다섯 번째 내려와서는 학식 있는 고개를 저으며 일주일을 더 사는 것은 불가능하다고 선언했고, 주교관 식당에 점심을 들러 앉으면서는 내각이 닷새 안에 무너질 수밖에 없다는 자기들만의 정보를 부주교에게 귀띔했다."], ["The son returned to his father's room and, after administering with his own hands the sustaining modicum of madeira, sat down by the bedside to calculate his chances.", "아들은 아버지의 방으로 돌아가 제 손으로 기운을 돋우는 마데이라 포도주를 조금 떠 넣어 드린 뒤, 침대 곁에 앉아 자기 앞의 승산을 헤아려 보았다."]], [["The ministry were to be out within five days: his father was to be dead within--no, he rejected that view of the subject.", "내각은 닷새 안에 물러나게 된다. 아버지는 그 안에 돌아가시게 — 아니, 그는 그런 식으로 헤아리는 것을 물리쳤다."], ["The ministry were to be out, and the diocese might probably be vacant at the same period.", "내각은 물러나게 되고, 교구도 아마 같은 시기에 비게 될 것이다."], ["There was much doubt as to the names of the men who were to succeed to power, and a week must elapse before a cabinet was formed.", "권력을 이어받을 사람들의 이름은 아직 매우 불확실했고, 내각이 꾸려지기까지는 일주일이 걸릴 터였다."], ["Would not vacancies be filled by the outgoing men during this week?", "그 일주일 동안 물러나는 이들이 빈자리를 채우지 않겠는가?"], ["Dr. Grantly had a kind of idea that such would be the case but did not know, and then he wondered at his own ignorance on such a question.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그렇게 되리라는 짐작이 어렴풋이 있었으나 확실히는 알지 못했고, 그러다 그런 문제에 자기가 이토록 무지하다는 데에 스스로 놀랐다."]], [["He tried to keep his mind away from the subject, but he could not.", "그는 그 생각에서 마음을 떼어 놓으려 애썼으나 그럴 수 없었다."], ["The race was so very close, and the stakes were so very high.", "경주가 너무나 아슬아슬했고, 걸린 것이 너무나 컸다."], ["He then looked at the dying man's impassive, placid face.", "그러다 그는 죽어 가는 이의 무표정하고 평온한 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["There was no sign there of death or disease; it was something thinner than of yore, somewhat grayer, and the deep lines of age more marked; but, as far as he could judge, life might yet hang there for weeks to come.", "거기에는 죽음이나 병의 기색이 없었다. 예전보다 조금 여위고 얼마간 잿빛이 돌며 나이의 깊은 주름이 더 뚜렷하기는 했으나, 그가 보기에는 앞으로도 몇 주는 더 목숨이 걸려 있을 법했다."], ["Sir Lamda Mewnew and Sir Omicron Pie had thrice been wrong, and might yet be wrong thrice again.", "램다 뮤뉴 경과 오미크론 파이 경은 이미 세 번 틀렸으니 앞으로 세 번 더 틀릴 수도 있었다."], ["The old bishop slept during twenty of the twenty-four hours, but during the short periods of his waking moments, he knew both his son and his dear old friend, Mr. Harding, the archdeacon's father-in-law, and would thank them tenderly for their care and love.", "늙은 주교는 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 스무 시간을 잠으로 보냈지만, 잠깐씩 깨어 있는 동안에는 아들도, 부주교의 장인인 오랜 벗 하딩 씨도 알아보았고 그 보살핌과 사랑에 다정히 고마움을 표하곤 했다."], ["Now he lay sleeping like a baby, resting easily on his back, his mouth just open, and his few gray hairs straggling from beneath his cap; his breath was perfectly noiseless, and his thin, wan hand, which lay above the coverlid, never moved.", "지금 그는 갓난아기처럼 잠들어 편안히 반듯이 누워 있었고, 입은 조금 벌어졌으며, 몇 올 남지 않은 흰머리가 두건 밑으로 삐져나와 있었다. 숨소리는 조금도 나지 않았고, 이불 위에 얹힌 여위고 창백한 손은 미동도 없었다."], ["Nothing could be easier than the old man's passage from this world to the next.", "이 세상에서 저 세상으로 건너가는 노인의 걸음보다 더 수월한 것은 없을 듯했다."]], [["But by no means easy were the emotions of him who sat there watching.", "그러나 거기 앉아 지켜보는 이의 심사는 결코 수월하지 않았다."], ["He knew it must be now or never.", "그는 지금이 아니면 영영 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He was already over fifty, and there was little chance that his friends who were now leaving office would soon return to it.", "그는 이미 쉰이 넘었고, 이제 자리에서 물러나는 그의 벗들이 머지않아 되돌아올 가망은 별로 없었다."], ["No probable British prime minister but he who was now in, he who was so soon to be out, would think of making a bishop of Dr. Grantly.", "지금 자리에 있는, 그리고 곧 물러날 그 사람 말고는, 앞으로 영국 총리가 될 만한 어느 누구도 그랜틀리 박사를 주교로 삼을 생각은 하지 않을 것이었다."], ["Thus he thought long and sadly, in deep silence, and then gazed at that still living face, and then at last dared to ask himself whether he really longed for his father's death.", "그리하여 그는 깊은 침묵 속에서 오래도록 서글피 생각에 잠겼다가, 아직 살아 있는 그 얼굴을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 마침내 스스로에게 감히 물었다 — 나는 정말로 아버지의 죽음을 바라고 있는가."]], [["The effort was a salutary one, and the question was answered in a moment.", "그 물음을 던진 것은 이로운 일이었고, 답은 순식간에 나왔다."], ["The proud, wishful, worldly man sank on his knees by the bedside and, taking the bishop's hand within his own, prayed eagerly that his sins might be forgiven him.", "오만하고 욕심 많고 세속적인 그 사람은 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 주교의 손을 제 손에 감싸 쥔 채, 자기 죄를 용서해 달라고 간절히 기도했다."]], [["His face was still buried in the clothes when the door of the bedroom opened noiselessly and Mr. Harding entered with a velvet step.", "침실 문이 소리 없이 열리고 하딩 씨가 벨벳 같은 걸음으로 들어섰을 때에도 그의 얼굴은 여전히 이불에 묻혀 있었다."], ["Mr. Harding's attendance at that bedside had been nearly as constant as that of the archdeacon, and his ingress and egress was as much a matter of course as that of his son-in-law.", "그 침대 곁을 지키는 일에서 하딩 씨의 발길은 부주교의 그것에 못지않게 잦았고, 그가 드나드는 것은 사위가 드나드는 것만큼이나 당연한 일이었다."], ["He was standing close beside the archdeacon before he was perceived, and would also have knelt in prayer had he not feared that his doing so might have caused some sudden start and have disturbed the dying man.", "그는 부주교가 알아채기 전에 이미 그 곁에 바싹 다가와 서 있었고, 자기까지 무릎을 꿇으면 부주교가 화들짝 놀라 죽어 가는 이를 깨울까 두렵지만 않았다면 그 또한 무릎을 꿇고 기도했을 것이다."], ["Dr. Grantly, however, instantly perceived him and rose from his knees.", "그러나 그랜틀리 박사는 곧바로 그를 알아보고 무릎에서 일어났다."], ["As he did so Mr. Harding took both his hands and pressed them warmly.", "그가 일어서자 하딩 씨는 그의 두 손을 잡고 따뜻하게 힘주어 쥐었다."], ["There was more fellowship between them at that moment than there had ever been before, and it so happened that after circumstances greatly preserved the feeling.", "그 순간 두 사람 사이에는 일찍이 없던 정이 오갔고, 그 뒤에 벌어진 사정들이 마침 그 감정을 크게 지켜 주었다."], ["As they stood there pressing each other's hands, the tears rolled freely down their cheeks.", "그렇게 서로의 손을 맞잡고 서 있는 동안 두 사람의 뺨으로는 눈물이 하염없이 흘러내렸다."]], [["\"God bless you, my dears,\"", "“하느님이 너희를 축복하시기를, 얘들아.”"], ["said the bishop with feeble voice as he woke.", "주교가 잠에서 깨어 힘없는 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"God bless you--may God bless you both, my dear children.\"", "“하느님이 너희를 축복하시기를 — 하느님이 너희 둘 다 축복하시기를, 사랑하는 내 아이들아.”"], ["And so he died.", "그리고 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었다."]], [["There was no loud rattle in the throat, no dreadful struggle, no palpable sign of death, but the lower jaw fell a little from its place, and the eyes which had been so constantly closed in sleep now remained fixed and open.", "목에서 요란하게 가래 끓는 소리도 없었고, 끔찍한 몸부림도 없었으며, 눈에 띄는 죽음의 표시도 없었다."], ["Neither Mr. Harding nor Dr.", "다만 아래턱이 제자리에서 조금 처졌고, 그토록 줄곧 잠에 감겨 있던 두 눈이 이제 뜬 채로 고정되었다."], ["Grantly knew that life was gone, though both suspected it.", "하딩 씨도 그랜틀리 박사도 목숨이 떠났음을 알지 못했으나, 둘 다 그런 것이 아닐까 짐작은 하고 있었다."]], [["\"I believe it's all over,\"", "“다 끝난 것 같군.”"], ["said Mr. Harding, still pressing the other's hands.", "하딩 씨가 여전히 상대의 두 손을 쥔 채 말했다."], ["\"I think--nay, I hope it is.\"", "“그런 듯싶네 — 아니, 그러기를 바라네.”"]], [["\"I will ring the bell,\"", "“벨을 울리겠습니다.”"], ["said the other, speaking all but in a whisper.", "상대가 거의 속삭임에 가까운 소리로 말했다."], ["\"Mrs. Phillips should be here.\"", "“필립스 부인이 와 있어야지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Phillips, the nurse, was soon in the room, and immediately, with practised hand, closed those staring eyes.", "간병인 필립스 부인이 곧 방으로 들어와 곧바로 익숙한 손길로 그 뜬 눈을 감겨 주었다."]], [["\"It's all over, Mrs. Phillips?\"", "“다 끝난 건가요, 필립스 부인?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"My lord's no more,\" said Mrs.", "“주교님께서는 이제 안 계십니다.”"], ["Phillips, turning round and curtseying low with solemn face; \"his lordship's gone more like a sleeping babby than any that I ever saw.\"", "필립스 부인이 몸을 돌려 엄숙한 얼굴로 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 말했다. “각하께서는 제가 여태 뵌 어느 분보다도 잠든 애기처럼 가셨습니다요.”"]], [["\"It's a great relief, Archdeacon,\"", "“큰 다행일세, 부주교.” 하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Harding, \"a great relief--dear, good, excellent old man.", "“참으로 큰 다행이야 — 사랑스럽고 선량하고 더없이 훌륭하신 노인이셨네."], ["Oh that our last moments may be as innocent and as peaceful as his!\"", "아, 우리의 마지막 순간도 저분처럼 티 없고 평온하기를!”"]], [["\"Surely,\"", "“그럼요.”"], ["said Mrs. Phillips.", "필립스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"The Lord be praised for all his mercies; but, for a meek, mild, gentle-spoken Christian, his lordship was--\"", "“주님의 온갖 자비를 찬양할지어다. 하오나 온유하고 부드럽고 말씨 고운 그리스도인으로 말하자면 각하께서야말로—”"], ["and Mrs. Phillips, with unaffected but easy grief, put up her white apron to her flowing eyes.", "그러고는 필립스 부인이 꾸밈없으면서도 수월한 슬픔에 젖어 흰 앞치마를 들어 흘러넘치는 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["\"You cannot but rejoice that it is over,\"", "“끝났으니 기뻐하지 않을 수 없지.”"], ["said Mr. Harding, still consoling his friend.", "하딩 씨가 여전히 벗을 위로하며 말했다."], ["The archdeacon's mind, however, had already travelled from the death chamber to the closet of the prime minister.", "그러나 부주교의 마음은 벌써 임종의 방을 떠나 총리의 집무실에 가 있었다."], ["He had brought himself to pray for his father's life, but now that that life was done, minutes were too precious to be lost.", "그는 아버지의 목숨을 위해 기도하기까지 했으나, 이제 그 목숨이 다한 마당에는 일 분 일 초가 아까웠다."], ["It was now useless to dally with the fact of the bishop's death--useless to lose perhaps everything for the pretence of a foolish sentiment.", "주교가 죽었다는 사실을 두고 우물쭈물하는 것은 이제 소용없는 짓이었다 — 어리석은 감상을 꾸며 보이느라 어쩌면 모든 것을 잃는 것도 소용없는 짓이었다."]], [["But how was he to act while his father-in-law stood there holding his hand?", "그러나 장인이 저기 서서 제 손을 잡고 있는데 어떻게 처신해야 한단 말인가?"], ["How, without appearing unfeeling, was he to forget his father in the bishop--to overlook what he had lost, and think only of what he might possibly gain?", "어떻게 하면 매정해 보이지 않으면서 아버지를 잊고 주교직만 생각할 수 있단 말인가 — 잃은 것은 못 본 체하고 어쩌면 얻을 수 있는 것만 헤아릴 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"No, I suppose not,\"", "“예, 그렇지 않을 수야 없지요.”"], ["said he, at last, in answer to Mr.", "마침내 그가 하딩 씨의 말에 답하여 말했다."], ["Harding. \"We have all expected it so long.\"", "“우리 모두가 오래도록 예상해 온 일이니까요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding took him by the arm and led him from the room.", "하딩 씨는 그의 팔을 잡고 방 밖으로 이끌었다."], ["\"We will see him again to-morrow morning,\"", "“내일 아침에 다시 뵙세.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; \"we had better leave the room now to the women.\"", "“이제 방은 여자들에게 맡기는 편이 좋겠네.”"], ["And so they went downstairs.", "그러고서 두 사람은 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["It was already evening and nearly dark.", "이미 저녁이었고 거의 어두워져 있었다."], ["It was most important that the prime minister should know that night that the diocese was vacant.", "그날 밤 안으로 총리가 그 교구가 비었음을 아는 것이 무엇보다 중요했다."], ["Everything might depend on it; and so, in answer to Mr.", "모든 것이 거기에 달려 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["Harding's further consolation, the archdeacon suggested that a telegraph message should be immediately sent off to London. Mr.", "그리하여 부주교는 하딩 씨의 거듭되는 위로에 답하는 대신, 지금 곧 런던으로 전보를 쳐야겠다고 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Harding, who had really been somewhat surprised to find Dr. Grantly, as he thought, so much affected, was rather taken aback, but he made no objection.", "그랜틀리 박사가 자기가 보기에 저토록 마음 아파하는 것에 실은 적잖이 놀라고 있던 하딩 씨는 다소 당황했으나 이의를 달지는 않았다."], ["He knew that the archdeacon had some hope of succeeding to his father's place, though he by no means knew how highly raised that hope had been.", "그는 부주교가 아버지의 자리를 잇기를 얼마간 바라고 있다는 것은 알고 있었으나, 그 바람이 얼마나 높이 부풀어 있는지는 전혀 알지 못했다."]], [["\"Yes,\" said Dr.", "“그렇지요.”"], ["Grantly, collecting himself and shaking off his weakness, \"we must send a message at once; we don't know what might be the consequence of delay.", "그랜틀리 박사가 정신을 가다듬고 나약함을 떨쳐 내며 말했다. “지금 당장 소식을 보내야 합니다."], ["Will you do it?'", "늦어져서 무슨 결과가 벌어질지 모르는 일이니까요. 좀 해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"I!", "“내가!"], ["Oh, yes; certainly.", "아, 그럼세. 물론이지."], ["I'll do anything, only I don't know exactly what it is you want.\"", "무엇이든 하겠네만, 자네가 원하는 게 정확히 무엇인지를 내가 모르겠구먼.”"]], [["Dr. Grantly sat down before a writing-table and, taking pen and ink, wrote on a slip of paper as follows:--", "그랜틀리 박사는 필기용 탁자 앞에 앉아 펜과 잉크를 들고 종잇조각에 이렇게 썼다."]], [["By Electric Telegraph.", "전신으로."], ["For the Earl of ----, Downing Street, or elsewhere.", "---- 백작 앞, 다우닝가 또는 그 밖의 어디든."], ["The Bishop of Barchester is dead.", "바체스터 주교가 별세했음."], ["Message sent by the Rev. Septimus Harding.", "이 소식은 셉티머스 하딩 목사가 보냄."]], [["\"There,\"", "“자.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다. “이걸 철도역 전신국에 가져가서 그대로 접수시키십시오."], ["\"Just take that to the telegraph office at the railway station and give it in as it is; they'll probably make you copy it on to one of their own slips; that's all you'll have to do; then you'll have to pay them half a crown.\"", "아마 자기들 용지에 옮겨 적으라고 할 겁니다. 하실 일은 그것뿐이고요."], ["And the archdeacon put his hand in his pocket and pulled out the necessary sum.", "그런 다음 반 크라운을 내시면 됩니다.” 그러고서 부주교는 주머니에 손을 넣어 필요한 돈을 꺼냈다."]], [["Mr. Harding felt very much like an errand-boy, and also felt that he was called on to perform his duties as such at rather an unseemly time, but he said nothing, and took the slip of paper and the proffered coin.", "하딩 씨는 자기가 꼭 심부름꾼 아이가 된 것 같았고, 게다가 하필 이런 온당치 못한 때에 그 노릇을 하라는 부름을 받았다는 느낌도 들었으나, 아무 말도 하지 않고 종잇조각과 내미는 동전을 받아 들었다."]], [["\"But you've put my name into it, Archdeacon.\"", "“한데 부주교, 자네가 이 전보에다 내 이름을 적어 넣어 놓았군그래.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그럼요.”"], ["said the other, \"there should be the name of some clergyman, you know, and what name so proper as that of so old a friend as yourself?", "상대가 말했다. “아시다시피 성직자의 이름이 들어가야 하는데, 그토록 오랜 벗이신 장인어른의 이름보다 더 어울리는 이름이 어디 있겠습니까?"], ["The earl won't look at the name, you may be sure of that; but my dear Mr.", "백작은 이름 따위 보지도 않을 겁니다, 그건 틀림없고요."], ["Harding, pray don't lose any time.\"", "그러니 하딩 씨, 부디 잠시도 지체하지 마십시오.”"]], [["Mr. Harding got as far as the library door on his way to the station, when he suddenly remembered the news with which he was fraught when he entered the poor bishop's bedroom.", "하딩 씨는 역으로 가는 길에 서재 문께까지 갔다가, 가엾은 주교의 침실에 들어설 때 자기가 잔뜩 품고 있던 소식을 문득 떠올렸다."], ["He had found the moment so inopportune for any mundane tidings, that he had repressed the words which were on his tongue, and immediately afterwards all recollection of the circumstance was for the time banished by the scene which had occurred.", "그 순간이 세상일을 전하기에는 너무나 마땅치 않았던 터라 그는 혀끝까지 나온 말을 삼켰고, 그 직후에 벌어진 광경에 그 일에 대한 기억은 한동안 통째로 날아가 버렸던 것이다."]], [["\"But, Archdeacon,\"", "“한데 부주교.”"], ["said he, turning back, \"I forgot to tell you--the ministry are out.\"", "그가 되돌아서며 말했다. “자네에게 말한다는 걸 잊었네 — 내각이 물러났다는군.”"]], [["\"Out!\"", "“물러나요!”"], ["ejaculated the archdeacon, in a tone which too plainly showed his anxiety and dismay, although under the circumstances of the moment he endeavoured to control himself.", "부주교가 소리쳤는데, 그 순간의 사정을 생각해 스스로를 다잡으려 애썼는데도 그 목소리에는 그의 불안과 낭패가 너무나 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다. “물러났다고요!"], ["\"Out! Who told you so?\"", "누가 그럽디까?”"]], [["Mr. Harding explained that news to this effect had come down by electric telegraph, and that the tidings had been left at the palace door by Mr. Chadwick.", "하딩 씨는 그런 취지의 소식이 전신으로 내려왔으며 그 기별을 채드윅 씨가 주교관 문간에 두고 갔다고 설명했다."]], [["The archdeacon sat silent for awhile meditating, and Mr. Harding stood looking at him.", "부주교는 한동안 말없이 앉아 골똘히 생각했고, 하딩 씨는 선 채로 그를 바라보았다."], ["\"Never mind,\"", "“상관없습니다.”"], ["said the archdeacon at last; \"send the message all the same.", "마침내 부주교가 말했다. “그래도 전보는 그대로 보내십시오."], ["The news must be sent to someone, and there is at present no one else in a position to receive it.", "소식은 누군가에게는 알려야 하는데, 지금으로서는 그것을 받을 처지에 있는 사람이 달리 없으니까요."], ["Do it at once, my dear friend; you know I would not trouble you, were I in a state to do it myself.", "지금 곧 해 주십시오, 장인어른. 제가 몸소 할 수 있는 형편이라면 이런 수고를 끼치지 않을 것을 아시지 않습니까."], ["A few minutes' time is of the greatest importance.\"", "몇 분이라도 더없이 소중합니다.”"]], [["Mr. Harding went out and sent the message, and it may be as well that we should follow it to its destination.", "하딩 씨는 나가서 전보를 보냈는데, 우리도 그 전보를 따라 목적지까지 가 보는 편이 좋겠다."], ["Within thirty minutes of its leaving Barchester it reached the Earl of ---- in his inner library.", "그것은 바체스터를 떠난 지 30분도 되지 않아 안쪽 서재에 있던 ---- 백작에게 닿았다."], ["What elaborate letters, what eloquent appeals, what indignant remonstrances he might there have to frame, at such a moment, may be conceived but not described!", "그런 순간에 그가 거기서 얼마나 공들인 편지를, 얼마나 유창한 호소를, 얼마나 분개에 찬 항의를 지어내야 했을지는 짐작할 수는 있어도 이루 다 적을 수는 없다!"], ["How he was preparing his thunder for successful rivals, standing like a British peer with his back to the sea-coal fire, and his hands in his breeches pockets--how his fine eye was lit up with anger, and his forehead gleamed with patriotism--how he stamped his foot as he thought of his heavy associates--how he all but swore as he remembered how much too clever one of them had been--my creative readers may imagine.", "그가 어떻게 석탄 난롯불에 등을 대고 영국 귀족답게 서서 바지 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 승리한 경쟁자들에게 퍼부을 벼락을 벼르고 있었는지 — 그의 고운 눈이 어떻게 분노로 타올랐고 이마가 어떻게 애국심으로 번쩍였는지 — 굼뜬 동료들을 생각하며 어떻게 발을 굴렀는지 — 그중 하나가 지나치게 영리했다는 것을 떠올리며 어떻게 하마터면 욕설을 내뱉을 뻔했는지는, 상상력 좋은 독자들이 알아서 그려 볼 일이다. 그런데 그가 정말 그러고 있었던가?"], ["But was he so engaged?", "아니다."], ["No: history and truth compel me to deny it.", "역사와 진실이 나로 하여금 그것을 부인하게 만든다."], ["He was sitting easily in a lounging chair, conning over a Newmarket list, and by his elbow on the table was lying open an uncut French novel on which he was engaged.", "그는 안락의자에 편히 앉아 뉴마켓 경마표를 훑어보고 있었고, 팔꿈치 옆 탁자에는 아직 페이지도 자르지 않은 프랑스 소설이 펼쳐진 채 놓여 있어 그가 그것에 붙들려 있던 참이었다."]], [["He opened the cover in which the message was enclosed and, having read it, he took his pen and wrote on the back of it--", "그는 전보가 든 봉투를 열어 그것을 읽고 나서 펜을 들어 뒷면에 이렇게 썼다."]], [["For the Earl of ----, With the Earl of ----'s compliments", "---- 백작 앞. ---- 백작의 인사를 전함."]], [["and sent it off again on its journey.", "그러고는 그것을 다시 길 위로 띄워 보냈다."]], [["Thus terminated our unfortunate friend's chances of possessing the glories of a bishopric.", "우리의 불운한 벗이 주교직의 영광을 손에 넣을 가망은 이렇게 끝이 났다."]], [["The names of many divines were given in the papers as that of the bishop-elect. \"The British Grandmother\"", "신문마다 주교로 뽑힐 사람이라며 여러 성직자의 이름을 올렸다."], ["declared that Dr. Gwynne was to be the man, in compliment to the late ministry.", "『브리티시 그랜드머더』는 물러나는 내각에 대한 예우로 그윈 박사가 그 사람이 되리라고 단언했다."], ["This was a heavy blow to Dr. Grantly, but he was not doomed to see himself superseded by his friend.", "이는 그랜틀리 박사에게 큰 타격이었으나, 그는 제 벗에게 자리를 빼앗기는 꼴을 볼 운명은 아니었다."], ["\"The Anglican Devotee\" put forward confidently the claims of a great London preacher of austere doctrines; and \"The Eastern Hemisphere,\"", "『앵글리컨 디보티』는 엄격한 교리로 이름난 런던의 어느 위대한 설교자의 자격을 자신 있게 내세웠다."], ["an evening paper supposed to possess much official knowledge, declared in favour of an eminent naturalist, a gentleman most completely versed in the knowledge of rocks and minerals, but supposed by many to hold on religious subjects no special doctrines whatever. \"The Jupiter,\"", "그리고 관가의 사정에 밝다고 여겨지는 석간 『이스턴 헤미스피어』는 어느 저명한 박물학자를 지지한다고 선언했는데, 그는 암석과 광물에 관한 지식에 더없이 통달한 신사였으나 종교 문제에 관해서는 아무런 특별한 교리도 지니고 있지 않다고 많은 이가 짐작하는 인물이었다."], ["that daily paper which, as we all know, is the only true source of infallibly correct information on all subjects, for awhile was silent, but at last spoke out. The merits of all these candidates were discussed and somewhat irreverently disposed of, and then \"The Jupiter\"", "우리 모두가 알다시피 모든 주제에 관하여 결코 틀리는 법이 없는 정확한 정보의 유일한 참된 원천인 일간지 『주피터』는 한동안 잠자코 있다가 마침내 입을 열었다."], ["declared that Dr. Proudie was to be the man.", "이 모든 후보의 자격이 논의되었고 다소 불경스럽게 처리되었으며, 그런 다음 『주피터』는 프라우디 박사가 그 사람이라고 선언했다."]], [["Dr. Proudie was the man.", "프라우디 박사가 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["Just a month after the demise of the late bishop, Dr. Proudie kissed the Queen's hand as his successor-elect.", "선대 주교가 세상을 뜬 지 꼭 한 달 만에 프라우디 박사는 그 후임자로 뽑혀 여왕의 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["We must beg to be allowed to draw a curtain over the sorrows of the archdeacon as he sat, sombre and sad at heart, in the study of his parsonage at Plumstead Episcopi.", "플럼스테드 에피스코피의 목사관 서재에 침울하고 서글픈 마음으로 앉아 있는 부주교의 슬픔에 대해서는, 우리에게 장막을 드리우도록 허락해 주기를 청해야겠다."], ["On the day subsequent to the dispatch of the message he heard that the Earl of ---- had consented to undertake the formation of a ministry, and from that moment he knew that his chance was over.", "전보를 보낸 이튿날 그는 ---- 백작이 내각 구성을 맡기로 승낙했다는 소식을 들었고, 그 순간부터 자기 기회가 끝났음을 알았다."], ["Many will think that he was wicked to grieve for the loss of episcopal power, wicked to have coveted it, nay, wicked even to have thought about it, in the way and at the moments he had done so.", "많은 이는 그가 주교의 권세를 놓친 것을 슬퍼했다는 점에서, 그것을 탐냈다는 점에서, 아니 그때 그런 식으로 그것을 생각했다는 점 자체에서 사악했다고 여길 것이다."]], [["With such censures I cannot profess that I completely agree.", "그런 비난에 내가 전적으로 동의한다고는 말하지 못하겠다."], ["The nolo episcopari, though still in use, is so directly at variance with the tendency of all human wishes, that it cannot be thought to express the true aspirations of rising priests in the Church of England.", "nolo episcopari 는 아직도 쓰이고는 있으나 모든 인간적 소망의 흐름과 너무나 정면으로 어긋나는 것이어서, 잉글랜드 교회에서 떠오르는 사제들의 참된 열망을 나타낸다고는 볼 수 없다."], ["A lawyer does not sin in seeking to be a judge, or in compassing his wishes by all honest means.", "법률가가 판사가 되기를 구하는 것은, 또 온갖 정직한 방법으로 그 소망을 이루려 하는 것은 죄가 아니다."], ["A young diplomat entertains a fair ambition when he looks forward to be the lord of a first-rate embassy; and a poor novelist, when he attempts to rival Dickens or rise above Fitzjeames, commits no fault, though he may be foolish.", "젊은 외교관이 일류 대사관의 주인이 되기를 내다본다면 그것은 온당한 야심이며, 가난한 소설가가 디킨스와 겨루어 보려 하거나 피츠제임스를 뛰어넘으려 한다 해도 어리석을지언정 잘못은 아니다."], ["Sydney Smith truly said that in these recreant days we cannot expect to find the majesty of St. Paul beneath the cassock of a curate.", "시드니 스미스는 이 타락한 시대에 부목사의 사제복 아래에서 성 바울의 위엄을 찾기를 기대할 수는 없다고 참으로 옳게 말했다."], ["If we look to our clergymen to be more than men, we shall probably teach ourselves to think that they are less, and can hardly hope to raise the character of the pastor by denying to him the right to entertain the aspirations of a man.", "우리가 성직자에게 사람 이상이기를 바란다면, 우리는 아마 그들이 사람 이하라고 여기도록 스스로를 가르치게 될 것이며, 사람으로서 품을 열망의 권리를 목자에게서 빼앗는 방식으로 목자의 품격을 높이기를 기대하기는 어렵다."]], [["Our archdeacon was worldly--who among us is not so?", "우리의 부주교는 세속적이었다 — 우리 가운데 그렇지 않은 이가 누구인가?"], ["He was ambitious--who among us is ashamed to own that \"last infirmity of noble minds!\"", "그는 야심가였다 — 우리 가운데 “고귀한 정신들의 마지막 약점”을 제 것이라 인정하기를 부끄러워할 이가 누구인가?"], ["He was avaricious, my readers will say.", "그는 탐욕스러웠다고 독자들은 말하리라."], ["No;--it was for no love of lucre that he wished to be Bishop of Barchester.", "아니다. 그가 바체스터의 주교가 되기를 바란 것은 결코 돈을 사랑해서가 아니었다."], ["He was his father's only child, and his father had left him great wealth.", "그는 아버지의 외아들이었고 아버지는 그에게 큰 재산을 남겼다."], ["His preferment brought him in nearly three thousand a year.", "그가 받은 성직록은 연간 3,000파운드 가까이를 그에게 안겨 주었다."], ["The bishopric, as cut down by the Ecclesiastical Commission, was only five.", "주교직은 교회 재산 위원회의 삭감을 거쳐 겨우 5,000파운드였다."], ["He would be a richer man as archdeacon than he could be as bishop.", "그는 주교가 되는 것보다 부주교로 있는 편이 더 부유한 사람이었다."], ["But he certainly did desire to play first fiddle; he did desire to sit in full lawn sleeves among the peers of the realm; and he did desire, if the truth must out, to be called \"My lord\" by his reverend brethren.", "그러나 그는 분명 제1바이올린을 켜기를 바랐고, 넓은 아마포 소매를 늘어뜨리고 왕국의 귀족들 사이에 앉기를 바랐으며, 굳이 진실을 밝혀야 한다면 동료 성직자들에게서 “각하”라 불리기를 바랐다."]], [["His hopes, however, were they innocent or sinful, were not fated to be realized, and Dr. Proudie was consecrated Bishop of Barchester.", "그러나 그의 희망은 그것이 죄 없는 것이었든 죄 많은 것이었든 이루어질 운명이 아니었고, 프라우디 박사가 바체스터의 주교로 축성되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER II. Hiram's Hospital According to Act of Parliament", "# 제2장. 의회 법령에 따른 히램 요양원"]], [["It is hardly necessary that I should here give to the public any lengthened biography of Mr. Harding up to the period of the commencement of this tale.", "이 이야기가 시작되는 시점까지의 하딩 씨 이력을 여기서 세상에 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 거의 없을 것이다."], ["The public cannot have forgotten how ill that sensitive gentleman bore the attack that was made on him in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" with reference to the income which he received as warden of Hiram's Hospital, in the city of Barchester.", "그 예민한 신사가 바체스터 시 히램 요양원의 워든으로서 받던 수입 때문에 『주피터』 지면에서 공격을 받고 얼마나 괴로워했는지를 세상이 잊었을 리 없다."], ["Nor can it yet be forgotten that a lawsuit was instituted against him on the matter of that charity by Mr. John Bold, who afterwards married his, Mr.", "또한 그 자선 재산 문제로 존 볼드 씨가 그를 상대로 소송을 제기했던 일, 그리고 그 볼드 씨가 훗날 하딩 씨의 막내딸이자 당시 유일하게 미혼이던 딸과 결혼했다는 사실도 아직 잊히지 않았을 것이다."], ["Harding's, younger and then only unmarried daughter. Under pressure of these attacks, Mr.", "이러한 공격에 눌린 나머지 하딩 씨는 워든 직을 사임했는데, 벗들도 변호사들도 그러지 말라고 한사코 만류하던 터였다."], ["Harding had resigned his wardenship, though strongly recommended to abstain from doing so both by his friends and by his lawyers. He did, however, resign it, and betook himself manfully to the duties of the small parish of St.", "그러나 그는 기어이 사임했고, 시내에 있는 자그마한 세인트 커스버트 교구의 사제로서 그 직무에 사내답게 몸을 던졌으며, 아울러 대성당 선창자의 직무도 계속 수행했다."], ["Cuthbert's, in the city, of which he was vicar, continuing also to perform those of precentor of the cathedral, a situation of small emolument which had hitherto been supposed to be joined, as a matter of course, to the wardenship of the hospital above spoken of.", "선창자 자리는 보수가 적어, 지금까지는 으레 앞서 말한 요양원의 워든 직에 딸려 오는 것으로 여겨져 왔다."]], [["When he left the hospital from which he had been so ruthlessly driven, and settled himself down in his own modest manner in the High Street of Barchester, he had not expected that others would make more fuss about it than he was inclined to do himself; and the extent of his hope was, that the movement might have been made in time to prevent any further paragraphs in \"The Jupiter.\"", "그토록 무참하게 쫓겨나다시피 한 요양원을 떠나 바체스터의 하이 스트리트에 제 분수대로 소박하게 자리를 잡았을 때, 그는 자신이 그러려는 것보다 남들이 더 야단스럽게 굴리라고는 예상하지 않았다. 그의 바람이라야 기껏 이번 조치가 『주피터』에 더 이상의 기사가 실리지 않도록 제때 이루어졌기를 바라는 정도였다."], ["His affairs, however, were not allowed to subside thus quietly, and people were quite as much inclined to talk about the disinterested sacrifice he had made, as they had before been to upbraid him for his cupidity.", "그러나 그의 일은 그렇게 조용히 가라앉도록 내버려 두어지지 않았고, 사람들은 전에 그의 탐욕을 나무라고 싶어 하던 것 못지않게 이제는 그가 치른 사심 없는 희생을 두고 입에 올리고 싶어 했다."]], [["The most remarkable thing that occurred was the receipt of an autographed letter from the Archbishop of Canterbury, in which the primate very warmly praised his conduct, and begged to know what his intentions were for the future. Mr.", "가장 놀라운 일은 캔터베리 대주교에게서 친필 편지를 받은 것이었다."], ["Harding replied that he intended to be rector of St. Cuthbert's, in Barchester, and so that matter dropped.", "그 편지에서 수좌 대주교는 그의 처신을 매우 따뜻하게 칭찬하고 앞으로의 뜻이 무엇인지 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["Then the newspapers took up his case, \"The Jupiter\" among the rest, and wafted his name in eulogistic strains through every reading-room in the nation.", "하딩 씨는 바체스터의 세인트 커스버트 교구 목사로 지낼 생각이라고 답했고, 그것으로 그 일은 끝났다."], ["It was discovered also that he was the author of that great musical work, Harding's Church Music,--and a new edition was spoken of, though, I believe, never printed.", "이어 신문들이 그의 사건을 다루었는데 『주피터』도 그 축에 들었으며, 온 나라의 열람실마다 그의 이름을 찬사에 실어 날랐다."], ["It is, however, certain that the work was introduced into the Royal Chapel at St.", "또한 그가 저 위대한 음악 저작 『하딩의 교회 음악』의 저자라는 사실도 밝혀졌다."], ["James's, and that a long criticism appeared in the \"Musical Scrutator,\"", "개정판 이야기가 오갔으나 내가 아는 한 인쇄된 적은 없다."], ["declaring that in no previous work of the kind had so much research been joined with such exalted musical ability, and asserting that the name of Harding would henceforward be known wherever the arts were cultivated, or religion valued.", "다만 그 저작이 세인트 제임스의 왕실 예배당에 도입된 것은 분명하며, 『음악 심사관』에 긴 평이 실려, 이런 종류의 저작 가운데 그토록 깊은 연구와 그토록 드높은 음악적 역량이 결합된 예는 일찍이 없었다고 단언하고, 앞으로 하딩이라는 이름은 예술이 가꾸어지는 곳이면, 또 종교가 소중히 여겨지는 곳이면 어디서나 알려지리라고 주장한 것도 분명하다."]], [["This was high praise, and I will not deny that Mr. Harding was gratified by such flattery; for if Mr.", "이는 대단한 찬사였고, 하딩 씨가 그러한 칭송에 흡족해했다는 것을 나는 부인하지 않겠다."], ["Harding was vain on any subject, it was on that of music.", "하딩 씨가 어떤 일에서든 허영을 부린다면 그것은 음악에 관한 것이었으니까."], ["But here the matter rested.", "그러나 일은 거기서 멈추었다."], ["The second edition, if printed, was never purchased; the copies which had been introduced into the Royal Chapel disappeared again, and were laid by in peace, with a load of similar literature. Mr.", "개정판은 설령 인쇄되었다 한들 팔린 적이 없었고, 왕실 예배당에 들어갔던 책들은 다시 자취를 감추어 비슷한 부류의 서적 더미와 함께 평화롭게 처박혔다."], ["Towers of \"The Jupiter\" and his brethren occupied themselves with other names, and the undying fame promised to our friend was clearly intended to be posthumous.", "『주피터』의 타워스 씨와 그 동료들은 다른 이름들에 매달렸고, 우리 벗에게 약속된 불멸의 명성은 아무래도 사후에나 오도록 예정된 것이 분명했다."]], [["Mr. Harding had spent much of his time with his friend the bishop; much with his daughter Mrs.", "하딩 씨는 많은 시간을 벗인 주교와 함께 보냈고, 또 많은 시간을 이제는 아아, 미망인이 된 딸 볼드 부인과 함께 보냈다."], ["Bold, now, alas, a widow; and had almost daily visited the wretched remnant of his former subjects, the few surviving bedesmen now left at Hiram's Hospital.", "그리고 거의 날마다 예전 자기 관하에 있던 이들의 가엾은 잔존자들, 곧 히램 요양원에 아직 남아 있는 몇 안 되는 구호 노인들을 찾아갔다."], ["Six of them were still living.", "그들 가운데 여섯이 아직 살아 있었다."], ["The number, according to old Hiram's will, should always have been twelve.", "늙은 히램의 유언에 따르면 그 수는 언제나 12명이어야 했다."], ["But after the abdication of their warden, the bishop had appointed no successor to him, no new occupants of the charity had been nominated, and it appeared as though the hospital at Barchester would fall into abeyance, unless the powers that be should take some steps towards putting it once more into working order.", "그러나 워든이 물러난 뒤로 주교는 후임을 임명하지 않았고 자선의 새 수혜자도 지명되지 않았으니, 당국이 요양원을 다시 굴러가게 할 무슨 조치를 취하지 않는 한 바체스터의 요양원은 그대로 폐지 상태에 빠질 듯이 보였다."]], [["During the past five years, the powers that be had not overlooked Barchester Hospital, and sundry political doctors had taken the matter in hand. Shortly after Mr. Harding's resignation, \"The Jupiter\" had very clearly shown what ought to be done. In about half a column it had distributed the income, rebuilt the buildings, put an end to all bickerings, regenerated kindly feeling, provided for Mr. Harding, and placed the whole thing on a footing which could not but be satisfactory to the city and Bishop of Barchester, and to the nation at large. The wisdom of this scheme was testified by the number of letters which \"Common Sense,\" \"Veritas,\" and \"One that loves fair play\" sent to \"The Jupiter\", all expressing admiration and amplifying on the details given. It is singular enough that no adverse letter appeared at all, and, therefore, none of course was written.", "지난 5년 동안 당국이 바체스터 요양원을 못 본 체한 것은 아니어서, 여러 정치의 명의들이 이 문제를 손에 쥐어 보았다. 하딩 씨가 사임한 직후 『주피터』는 무엇을 해야 하는지를 아주 명료하게 밝혀 놓았다. 반 단 남짓한 지면에서 그 신문은 수입을 배분하고, 건물을 다시 짓고, 온갖 다툼을 끝내고, 훈훈한 정을 되살리고, 하딩 씨의 앞길을 마련해 주었으며, 이 모든 일을 바체스터 시와 바체스터 주교에게도, 나아가 온 나라에도 흡족할 수밖에 없는 기반 위에 올려놓았다. 이 방안이 얼마나 현명한지는 「상식」과 「베리타스」와 「공정한 겨룸을 사랑하는 사람」이 『주피터』에 보낸 편지의 수효로 입증되었으니, 하나같이 감탄을 표하며 제시된 세목을 부연하는 내용이었다. 반대하는 편지는 단 한 통도 실리지 않았다는 것은 참으로 기이한 일이며, 따라서 물론 그런 편지는 쓰인 적도 없었다."]], [["But Cassandra was not believed, and even the wisdom of \"The Jupiter\" sometimes falls on deaf ears.", "그러나 카산드라의 말은 믿기지 않는 법이고, 『주피터』의 지혜조차 때로는 귀먹은 자들에게 떨어진다."], ["Though other plans did not put themselves forward in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" reformers of church charities were not slack to make known in various places their different nostrums for setting Hiram's Hospital on its feet again.", "『주피터』의 지면에 다른 방안이 나서지는 않았지만, 교회 자선 사업의 개혁가들은 히램 요양원을 다시 일으켜 세울 저마다의 비방을 여기저기서 알리는 데 게으르지 않았다."], ["A learned bishop took occasion, in the Upper House, to allude to the matter, intimating that he had communicated on the subject with his right reverend brother of Barchester.", "어느 학식 있는 주교는 상원에서 기회를 잡아 이 문제를 언급하며, 바체스터의 존경하는 형제 주교와 이 사안을 두고 서신을 주고받았음을 내비쳤다."], ["The radical member for Staleybridge had suggested that the funds should be alienated for the education of the agricultural poor of the country, and he amused the house by some anecdotes touching the superstition and habits of the agriculturists in question. A political pamphleteer had produced a few dozen pages, which he called \"Who are John Hiram's heirs?\"", "스테일리브리지의 급진파 의원은 그 기금을 나라의 가난한 농민 교육에 전용해야 한다고 제안하고는, 문제의 그 농민들의 미신과 습속에 관한 일화 몇 가지로 의사당을 즐겁게 했다."], ["intending to give an infallible rule for the governance of all such establishments; and, at last, a member of the government promised that in the next session a short bill should be introduced for regulating the affairs of Barchester and other kindred concerns.", "어느 정치 소책자 저자는 「존 히램의 상속인은 누구인가?」라는 제목으로 수십 쪽을 지어내어 그와 같은 모든 시설의 운영에 관한 어김없는 원칙을 제시하려 했다. 그리고 마침내 정부의 한 각료가 다음 회기에 바체스터와 그 비슷한 사안들을 규율할 짧은 법안을 제출하겠다고 약속했다."]], [["The next session came, and, contrary to custom, the bill came also.", "다음 회기가 왔고, 관례와는 달리 법안도 함께 왔다."], ["Men's minds were then intent on other things.", "그때 사람들의 마음은 다른 데 쏠려 있었다."], ["The first threatenings of a huge war hung heavily over the nation, and the question as to Hiram's heirs did not appear to interest very many people either in or out of the house.", "거대한 전쟁의 첫 위협이 나라 위에 무겁게 드리워 있었고, 히램의 상속인 문제는 의사당 안에서나 밖에서나 그리 많은 이의 관심을 끄는 것 같지 않았다."], ["The bill, however, was read and re-read, and in some undistinguished manner passed through its eleven stages without appeal or dissent.", "그렇지만 법안은 낭독되고 또 낭독되어, 이렇다 하게 눈에 띄는 일도 없이 이의도 반대도 없이 열한 단계를 거쳐 통과되었다."], ["What would John Hiram have said in the matter, could he have predicted that some forty-five gentlemen would take on themselves to make a law altering the whole purport of his will, without in the least knowing at the moment of their making it, what it was that they were doing?", "신사 마흔다섯 명쯤이 나서서 제 유언의 취지를 통째로 바꾸는 법을 만들되 정작 만드는 그 순간에는 자기들이 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 조금도 알지 못하리라는 것을 존 히램이 미리 내다볼 수 있었다면, 그는 이 일을 두고 무어라 했을까?"], ["It is however to be hoped that the under-secretary for the Home Office knew, for to him had the matter been confided.", "그래도 내무부 차관만은 알고 있었기를 바랄 일이다. 이 사안은 그에게 맡겨져 있었으니까."]], [["The bill, however, did pass, and at the time at which this history is supposed to commence, it had been ordained that there should be, as heretofore, twelve old men in Barchester Hospital, each with 1s. 4d.", "어쨌든 법안은 통과되었고, 이 이야기가 시작된다고 하는 시점에는 다음과 같이 정해져 있었다."], ["a day; that there should also be twelve old women to be located in a house to be built, each with 1s. 2d.", "바체스터 요양원에는 전과 같이 노인 12명을 두되 각자 하루 1실링 4펜스를 받고, 아울러 새로 지을 건물에 노파 12명을 두되 각자 하루 1실링 2펜스를 받으며, 사택과 연 70파운드를 받는 여사감을 두고, 연 150파운드를 받는 재산 관리인을 두며, 끝으로 연 450파운드를 받는 워든을 두어 두 시설 모두의 영적 지도와, 남자 쪽에 딸린 시설의 세속적 관리를 맡게 한다는 것이었다."], ["a day; that there should be a matron, with a house and £70 a year; a steward with £150 a year; and latterly, a warden with £450 a year, who should have the spiritual guidance of both establishments, and the temporal guidance of that appertaining to the male sex. The bishop, dean, and warden were, as formerly, to appoint in turn the recipients of the charity, and the bishop was to appoint the officers.", "주교와 수석 사제와 워든은 예전처럼 번갈아 가며 자선의 수혜자를 지명하고, 직원은 주교가 임명하도록 되어 있었다."], ["There was nothing said as to the wardenship being held by the precentor of the cathedral, nor a word as to Mr. Harding's right to the situation.", "워든 직을 대성당 선창자가 맡는다는 말은 한마디도 없었고, 하딩 씨가 그 자리에 대해 권리를 지닌다는 말도 한 자 없었다."]], [["It was not, however, till some months after the death of the old bishop, and almost immediately consequent on the installation of his successor, that notice was given that the reform was about to be carried out.", "그러나 개혁이 곧 시행되리라는 통지가 나온 것은 늙은 주교가 세상을 떠나고 여러 달이 지난 뒤, 그 후임자가 취임한 바로 다음의 일이었다."], ["The new law and the new bishop were among the earliest works of a new ministry, or rather of a ministry who, having for awhile given place to their opponents, had then returned to power; and the death of Dr.", "새 법과 새 주교는 새 내각의, 아니 그보다는 한동안 정적들에게 자리를 내주었다가 다시 권좌에 돌아온 내각의 가장 이른 작품 축에 들었다."], ["Grantly occurred, as we have seen, exactly at the period of the change.", "그리고 그랜틀리 박사의 죽음은 앞서 보았듯이 바로 그 정권 교체의 시점에 일어났다."]], [["Poor Eleanor Bold!", "가엾은 엘리너 볼드!"], ["How well does that widow's cap become her, and the solemn gravity with which she devotes herself to her new duties.", "저 미망인의 모자가 그이에게 얼마나 잘 어울리는지, 또 새로 맡은 소임에 몸을 바치는 그 엄숙한 진지함이 얼마나 잘 어울리는지."], ["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"]], [["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"], ["I cannot say that with me John Bold was ever a favourite.", "존 볼드가 나에게 언제고 마음에 드는 인물이었다고는 말할 수 없다."], ["I never thought him worthy of the wife he had won.", "나는 그가 제가 얻은 아내에 값하는 사람이라고 생각한 적이 없다."], ["But in her estimation he was most worthy.", "그러나 그녀가 보기에 그는 더없이 값진 사람이었다."], ["Hers was one of those feminine hearts which cling to a husband, not with idolatry, for worship can admit of no defect in its idol, but with the perfect tenacity of ivy.", "그녀의 마음은 남편에게 매달리는 저 여성적인 마음 가운데 하나였으되 우상 숭배로 매달리는 것은 아니었으니, 숭배란 제 우상에게 어떤 흠도 용납하지 못하는 법이거니와, 그녀는 담쟁이의 저 완전한 끈질김으로 매달렸다."], ["As the parasite plant will follow even the defects of the trunk which it embraces, so did Eleanor cling to and love the very faults of her husband.", "기생하는 덩굴이 제가 껴안은 줄기의 뒤틀린 데까지 그대로 따라가듯, 엘리너도 남편의 결점 바로 그것에 매달리고 그것을 사랑했다."], ["She had once declared that whatever her father did should in her eyes be right.", "그녀는 언젠가 아버지가 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 제 눈에는 옳으리라고 단언한 적이 있다."], ["She then transferred her allegiance, and became ever ready to defend the worst failings of her lord and master.", "그 뒤 그녀는 충성의 대상을 옮겨, 제 주인이자 지아비의 가장 못난 결함까지도 언제든 감싸고 나설 태세가 되었다."]], [["And John Bold was a man to be loved by a woman; he was himself affectionate; he was confiding and manly; and that arrogance of thought, unsustained by first-rate abilities, that attempt at being better than his neighbours which jarred so painfully on the feelings of his acquaintance, did not injure him in the estimation of his wife.", "그리고 존 볼드는 여자에게 사랑받을 만한 남자였다. 그 자신이 다정했고, 사람을 믿었고, 사내다웠다. 그리고 일류의 능력이 뒷받침해 주지도 못하는 그 생각의 오만함, 이웃보다 더 나은 사람이 되어 보려는 그 안간힘, 아는 이들의 심사를 그토록 아프게 긁던 그것도 아내의 평가에는 아무런 해가 되지 않았다."]], [["Could she even have admitted that he had a fault, his early death would have blotted out the memory of it.", "설령 그녀가 그에게 결점이 있었음을 인정할 수 있었다 한들, 그의 이른 죽음이 그 기억을 지워 버렸을 것이다."], ["She wept as for the loss of the most perfect treasure with which mortal woman had ever been endowed; for weeks after he was gone the idea of future happiness in this world was hateful to her; consolation, as it is called, was insupportable, and tears and sleep were her only relief.", "그녀는 죽어야 할 여인에게 주어진 적 있는 가장 완전한 보물을 잃기라도 한 듯이 울었다. 그가 떠난 뒤 몇 주 동안은 이 세상에서 앞으로 행복하리라는 생각 자체가 그녀에게 가증스러웠고, 이른바 위로라는 것은 견딜 수 없었으며, 눈물과 잠만이 유일한 위안이었다."]], [["But God tempers the wind to the shorn lamb.", "그러나 하느님은 털 깎인 어린양에게 바람을 알맞게 하신다."], ["She knew that she had within her the living source of other cares.", "그녀는 제 안에 또 다른 근심의 살아 있는 원천을 품고 있음을 알았다."], ["She knew that there was to be created for her another subject of weal or woe, of unutterable joy or despairing sorrow, as God in his mercy might vouchsafe to her.", "하느님이 자비로이 허락하시는 대로 기쁨이 되든 화가 되든, 이루 말할 수 없는 환희가 되든 절망의 슬픔이 되든, 저를 위하여 또 하나의 대상이 지어지리라는 것을 알았다."], ["At first this did but augment her grief!", "처음에는 그것이 슬픔을 더하기만 했다!"], ["To be the mother of a poor infant, orphaned before it was born, brought forth to the sorrows of an ever desolate hearth, nurtured amidst tears and wailing, and then turned adrift into the world without the aid of a father's care!", "태어나기도 전에 아비를 잃은 가엾은 아기의 어미가 되어, 언제나 황량할 화롯가의 슬픔 속으로 그 아이를 낳아 놓고, 눈물과 통곡 가운데 길러, 그러고는 아비의 보살핌도 없이 세상에 떠밀어 보내야 하다니!"], ["There was at first no joy in this.", "여기에 처음에는 아무런 기쁨도 없었다."]], [["By degrees, however, her heart became anxious for another object, and, before its birth, the stranger was expected with all the eagerness of a longing mother.", "그러나 차츰 그녀의 마음은 또 다른 대상을 향해 조바심을 내게 되었고, 태어나기도 전에 그 낯선 이는 애타는 어미의 온 갈망 속에 기다려졌다."], ["Just eight months after the father's death a second John Bold was born, and if the worship of one creature can be innocent in another, let us hope that the adoration offered over the cradle of the fatherless infant may not be imputed as a sin.", "아버지가 죽은 지 꼭 여덟 달 만에 두 번째 존 볼드가 태어났다. 그리고 한 피조물이 다른 피조물을 숭배하는 일이 죄 없을 수 있다면, 아비 없는 아기의 요람 위에 바쳐진 그 경배만은 죄로 헤아려지지 않기를 바라기로 하자."]], [["It will not be worth our while to define the character of the child, or to point out in how far the faults of the father were redeemed within that little breast by the virtues of the mother.", "그 아이의 성품을 규정하거나, 아비의 결점이 그 작은 가슴 안에서 어미의 미덕으로 얼마나 씻겼는지를 짚어 보는 일은 우리가 애쓸 값어치가 없을 것이다."], ["The baby, as a baby, was all that was delightful, and I cannot foresee that it will be necessary for us to inquire into the facts of his after-life.", "아기는 아기로서 더없이 사랑스러웠고, 그가 훗날 어떻게 살았는지의 사실들을 우리가 캐 볼 필요가 생기리라고는 나로서는 내다보이지 않는다."], ["Our present business at Barchester will not occupy us above a year or two at the furthest, and I will leave it to some other pen to produce, if necessary, the biography of John Bold the Younger.", "바체스터에서 지금 우리가 볼 일은 길어 보아야 한두 해를 넘기지 않을 터이니, 어린 존 볼드의 전기는 굳이 필요하다면 다른 이의 붓에 맡겨 두겠다."]], [["But, as a baby, this baby was all that could be desired.", "그러나 아기로서 이 아기는 더 바랄 나위가 없었다."], ["This fact no one attempted to deny.", "이 사실만은 아무도 부인하려 들지 않았다."], ["\"Is he not delightful?\"", "“정말 사랑스럽지 않아요?”"], ["she would say to her father, looking up into his face from her knees, her lustrous eyes overflowing with soft tears, her young face encircled by her close widow's cap, and her hands on each side of the cradle in which her treasure was sleeping.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇은 채 아버지의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그렇게 말하곤 했다. 윤기 도는 두 눈에는 부드러운 눈물이 그렁그렁했고, 앳된 얼굴은 몸에 꼭 맞는 미망인의 모자에 둘러싸여 있었으며, 두 손은 제 보물이 잠들어 있는 요람 양쪽에 얹혀 있었다."], ["The grandfather would gladly admit that the treasure was delightful, and the uncle archdeacon himself would agree, and Mrs.", "외할아버지는 그 보물이 참으로 사랑스럽다고 기꺼이 인정했고, 이모부인 부주교조차 동의했으며, 엘리너의 언니인 그랜틀리 부인은 참으로 자매다운 열의로 그 말을 되받아 울렸다."], ["Grantly, Eleanor's sister, would re-echo the word with true sisterly energy; and Mary Bold--but Mary Bold was a second worshipper at the same shrine.", "그리고 메리 볼드는 — 아니, 메리 볼드는 같은 제단 앞의 두 번째 숭배자였다."]], [["The baby was really delightful; he took his food with a will, struck out his toes merrily whenever his legs were uncovered, and did not have fits.", "아기는 정말로 사랑스러웠다. 젖을 힘차게 먹었고, 다리를 벗겨 놓기만 하면 발가락을 신나게 뻗어 댔으며, 경기를 일으키지 않았다."], ["These are supposed to be the strongest points of baby perfection, and in all these our baby excelled.", "이것들이 아기의 완전함을 이루는 가장 중요한 대목으로 여겨지는 터인데, 우리 아기는 그 모두에서 남달랐다."]], [["And thus the widow's deep grief was softened, and a sweet balm was poured into the wound which she had thought nothing but death could heal.", "그리하여 미망인의 깊은 슬픔은 누그러졌고, 죽음 말고는 아무것도 아물게 하지 못하리라 여겼던 상처에 달콤한 향유가 부어졌다."], ["How much kinder is God to us than we are willing to be to ourselves!", "하느님은 우리가 우리 자신에게 기꺼이 베풀려는 것보다 얼마나 더 자애로우신가!"], ["At the loss of every dear face, at the last going of every well-beloved one, we all doom ourselves to an eternity of sorrow, and look to waste ourselves away in an ever-running fountain of tears.", "사랑하는 얼굴을 하나씩 잃을 때마다, 몹시 아끼던 이가 하나씩 마지막으로 떠날 때마다, 우리는 모두 스스로에게 영원한 슬픔을 선고하고 그침 없이 솟는 눈물의 샘 속에 저를 소진시키려 든다."], ["How seldom does such grief endure!", "그러나 그런 슬픔이 오래가는 일이 얼마나 드문가!"], ["How blessed is the goodness which forbids it to do so!", "그것이 오래가지 못하도록 막아 주는 선하심이 얼마나 복된가!"], ["\"Let me ever remember my living friends, but forget them as soon as dead,\" was the prayer of a wise man who understood the mercy of God.", "“살아 있는 벗은 늘 기억하되, 죽은 벗은 죽는 즉시 잊게 하소서”라는 것이 하느님의 자비를 깨달은 어느 지혜로운 이의 기도였다."], ["Few perhaps would have the courage to express such a wish, and yet to do so would only be to ask for that release from sorrow which a kind Creator almost always extends to us.", "그런 소원을 입 밖에 낼 용기를 지닌 사람은 아마 드물겠으나, 그리한다 한들 그것은 자애로운 창조주께서 거의 언제나 우리에게 베풀어 주시는 슬픔으로부터의 놓임을 구하는 것일 따름이다."]], [["I would not, however, have it imagined that Mrs. Bold forgot her husband.", "그렇다고 볼드 부인이 남편을 잊었다고 여겨지기를 나는 바라지 않는다."], ["She daily thought of him with all conjugal love, and enshrined his memory in the innermost centre of her heart.", "그녀는 날마다 아내다운 온 사랑으로 그를 생각했고, 그 기억을 제 마음 가장 깊은 한복판에 모셔 두었다."], ["But yet she was happy in her baby.", "그러면서도 아기가 있어 행복했다."], ["It was so sweet to press the living toy to her breast, and feel that a human being existed who did owe, and was to owe, everything to her; whose daily food was drawn from herself; whose little wants could all be satisfied by her; whose little heart would first love her and her only; whose infant tongue would make its first effort in calling her by the sweetest name a woman can hear.", "살아 있는 그 장난감을 가슴에 꼭 품고, 저에게 모든 것을 빚졌고 앞으로도 빚질 한 인간이 존재한다고 느끼는 일은 참으로 달콤했다. 그 아이의 날마다의 양식은 제 몸에서 나왔고, 그 작은 바람들은 죄다 제 손으로 채워 줄 수 있었으며, 그 작은 마음이 맨 처음 사랑하게 될 사람은 저이고 오직 저뿐일 터였고, 그 젖먹이의 혀가 맨 처음 애써 내뱉을 말은 여자가 들을 수 있는 가장 달콤한 이름으로 저를 부르는 소리일 터였다."], ["And so Eleanor's bosom became tranquil, and she set about her new duties eagerly and gratefully.", "그리하여 엘리너의 가슴은 잔잔해졌고, 그녀는 새로 맡은 소임에 열심히, 또 고맙게 나섰다."]], [["As regards the concerns of the world, John Bold had left his widow in prosperous circumstances.", "세상살이의 형편으로 말하자면, 존 볼드는 미망인을 넉넉한 처지에 남겨 두고 갔다."], ["He had bequeathed to her all that he possessed, and that comprised an income much exceeding what she or her friends thought necessary for her.", "그는 제가 가진 것을 모두 그녀에게 물려주었는데, 그 재산에서 나오는 수입은 그녀에게든 그녀의 벗들에게든 필요하다고 여겨지는 정도를 훨씬 웃돌았다."], ["It amounted to nearly a thousand a year; when she reflected on its extent, her dearest hope was to hand it over, not only unimpaired but increased, to her husband's son, to her own darling, to the little man who now lay sleeping on her knee, happily ignorant of the cares which were to be accumulated in his behalf.", "그것은 연 1,000파운드에 가까웠다. 그 규모를 헤아려 볼 때 그녀의 가장 간절한 소망은 그것을 축내지 않는 것은 물론이요 불려서 남편의 아들에게, 제가 사랑하는 아이에게, 저를 위해 쌓일 근심 따위는 복되게도 모른 채 지금 제 무릎에 잠들어 있는 그 조그만 사내에게 넘겨주는 일이었다."]], [["When John Bold died, she earnestly implored her father to come and live with her, but this Mr.", "존 볼드가 죽었을 때 그녀는 아버지에게 와서 함께 살자고 간곡히 청했으나 하딩 씨는 사양했다."], ["Harding declined, though for some weeks he remained with her as a visitor.", "다만 몇 주 동안은 손님으로 그녀와 함께 머물렀다."], ["He could not be prevailed upon to forego the possession of some small home of his own, and so remained in the lodgings he had first selected over a chemist's shop in the High Street of Barchester.", "그는 아무리 작아도 제 집이라 할 것을 지니는 일만은 끝내 포기하려 들지 않았고, 그리하여 바체스터 하이 스트리트의 약재상 위층에 처음 골라 든 하숙에 그대로 머물렀다."]], [["# CHAPTER III. Dr. and Mrs. Proudie", "# 제3장. 프라우디 박사 내외"]], [["This narrative is supposed to commence immediately after the installation of Dr.", "이 이야기는 프라우디 박사의 취임식이 끝난 직후부터 시작되는 것으로 한다."], ["Proudie. I will not describe the ceremony, as I do not precisely understand its nature.", "그 의식은 묘사하지 않겠는데, 나로서는 그 성격을 정확히 알지 못하기 때문이다."], ["I am ignorant whether a bishop be chaired like a member of Parliament, or carried in a gilt coach like a lord mayor, or sworn like a justice of peace, or introduced like a peer to the upper house, or led between two brethren like a knight of the garter; but I do know that everything was properly done, and that nothing fit or becoming to a young bishop was omitted on the occasion.", "주교가 국회의원처럼 가마에 떠메어지는지, 시장처럼 금박 마차에 실려 가는지, 치안판사처럼 선서를 하는지, 귀족처럼 상원에 소개되는지, 아니면 가터 훈작사처럼 두 형제 사이에 이끌려 나아가는지 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 모든 일이 마땅한 절차대로 치러졌다는 것, 그리고 젊은 주교에게 어울리고 걸맞은 것이라면 그날 하나도 빠짐없이 갖추어졌다는 것만은 안다."]], [["Dr. Proudie was not the man to allow anything to be omitted that might be becoming to his new dignity.", "프라우디 박사는 자신의 새 위엄에 걸맞을 만한 것이라면 그 무엇도 빠뜨리게 둘 사람이 아니었다."], ["He understood well the value of forms, and knew that the due observance of rank could not be maintained unless the exterior trappings belonging to it were held in proper esteem.", "그는 격식의 가치를 잘 이해하고 있었고, 지위에 딸린 겉치레들이 응당한 존중을 받지 못하는 한 지위에 대한 예우도 유지될 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He was a man born to move in high circles; at least so he thought himself, and circumstances had certainly sustained him in this view.", "그는 상류 사회에서 움직이도록 태어난 사람이었다. 적어도 스스로는 그렇게 여겼고, 형편도 분명 그 생각을 뒷받침해 주었다."], ["He was the nephew of an Irish baron by his mother's side, and his wife was the niece of a Scotch earl.", "그는 외가 쪽으로 아일랜드 남작의 조카였고, 그의 아내는 스코틀랜드 백작의 조카딸이었다."], ["He had for years held some clerical office appertaining to courtly matters, which had enabled him to live in London, and to entrust his parish to his curate.", "그는 여러 해 동안 궁정 관련 업무에 딸린 성직을 맡아 왔는데, 덕분에 런던에 살면서 자기 교구는 부목사에게 맡겨 둘 수 있었다."], ["He had been preacher to the royal beefeaters, curator of theological manuscripts in the Ecclesiastical Courts, chaplain to the Queen's yeomanry guard, and almoner to his Royal Highness the Prince of Rappe-Blankenberg.", "그는 왕실 근위병의 설교자였고, 교회 법정의 신학 사본 관리인이었으며, 여왕 직속 기병 근위대의 전속 사제였고, 라페블랑켄베르크 공 전하의 시혜관이었다."]], [["His residence in the metropolis, rendered necessary by duties thus entrusted to him, his high connexions, and the peculiar talents and nature of the man, recommended him to persons in power, and Dr. Proudie became known as a useful and rising clergyman.", "이렇듯 맡겨진 직무 때문에 부득이해진 수도 거주와, 그의 높은 연줄과, 이 사람 특유의 재능과 성품은 그를 권력을 쥔 이들에게 천거해 주었고, 그리하여 프라우디 박사는 쓸모 있고 앞길이 트인 성직자로 알려지게 되었다."]], [["Some few years since, even within the memory of many who are not yet willing to call themselves old, a liberal clergyman was a person not frequently to be met.", "몇 해 전만 해도, 아직 스스로를 늙었다고 부르고 싶지 않은 많은 이들의 기억 안에 있는 그 시절만 해도, 자유주의적인 성직자란 좀처럼 만나 볼 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["Sydney Smith was such and was looked on as little better than an infidel; a few others also might be named, but they were rarae aves and were regarded with doubt and distrust by their brethren.", "시드니 스미스가 그런 사람이었는데, 그는 무신론자보다 나을 것이 없다는 눈총을 받았다. 몇 사람 더 이름을 댈 수는 있겠으나 그들은 rarae aves였고, 동료 성직자들에게 의심과 불신의 눈길을 받았다."], ["No man was so surely a Tory as a country rector--nowhere were the powers that be so cherished as at Oxford.", "시골 교구 목사만큼 확실한 토리당원은 없었고, 옥스퍼드만큼 기존의 권세가 떠받들어지는 곳도 없었다."]], [["When, however, Dr. Whately was made an archbishop, and Dr.", "그러나 웨이틀리 박사가 대주교가 되고 몇 해 뒤 햄프던 박사가 흠정 교수가 되자, 지각 있는 신학자들 다수는 사람들의 마음속에 변화가 일어나고 있으며 앞으로는 평신도뿐 아니라 성직자에게도 한결 자유주의적인 생각이 어울리게 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Hampden some years afterwards regius professor, many wise divines saw that a change was taking place in men's minds, and that more liberal ideas would henceforward be suitable to the priests as well as to the laity. Clergymen began to be heard of who had ceased to anathematize papists on the one hand, or vilify dissenters on the other.", "한편으로 교황파를 저주하기를 그만두고 다른 한편으로 비국교도를 헐뜯기를 그만둔 성직자들의 이름이 들려오기 시작했다."], ["It appeared clear that High Church principles, as they are called, were no longer to be surest claims to promotion with at any rate one section of statesmen, and Dr. Proudie was one among those who early in life adapted himself to the views held by the Whigs on most theological and religious subjects.", "이른바 고교회파 원칙이 적어도 정치가들의 어느 한 부류에게는 더 이상 승진의 가장 확실한 자격이 못 된다는 것이 분명해 보였고, 프라우디 박사는 대부분의 신학적·종교적 문제에서 휘그당의 견해에 일찌감치 자신을 맞춘 이들 가운데 하나였다."], ["He bore with the idolatry of Rome, tolerated even the infidelity of Socinianism, and was hand and glove with the Presbyterian Synods of Scotland and Ulster.", "그는 로마의 우상숭배를 참아 주었고, 소치누스주의의 불신앙마저 용인했으며, 스코틀랜드와 얼스터의 장로교 노회들과도 허물없이 지냈다."]], [["Such a man at such a time was found to be useful, and Dr. Proudie's name began to appear in the newspapers.", "그런 시절에 그런 사람은 쓸모가 있다는 것이 드러났고, 프라우디 박사의 이름이 신문에 오르내리기 시작했다."], ["He was made one of a commission who went over to Ireland to arrange matters preparative to the working of the national board; he became honorary secretary to another commission nominated to inquire into the revenues of cathedral chapters; he had had something to do with both the regium donum and the Maynooth grant.", "그는 국가 교육위원회의 운영에 앞선 준비를 갖추러 아일랜드로 건너간 위원회의 한 사람이 되었고, 대성당 참사회의 수입을 조사하도록 임명된 또 다른 위원회의 명예 간사가 되었으며, regium donum과 메이누스 보조금 양쪽에도 얼마쯤 관여했다."]], [["It must not on this account be taken as proved that Dr. Proudie was a man of great mental powers, or even of much capacity for business, for such qualities had not been required in him.", "그렇다고 해서 프라우디 박사가 대단한 지력의 소유자라거나 실무 능력이 뛰어난 사람이라는 것이 입증된 것으로 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["In the arrangement of those church reforms with which he was connected, the ideas and original conception of the work to be done were generally furnished by the liberal statesmen of the day, and the labour of the details was borne by officials of a lower rank.", "그에게 그런 자질이 요구된 적은 없었기 때문이다. 그가 관여한 교회 개혁을 마련하는 일에서, 해야 할 일에 대한 착상과 최초의 구상은 대개 당대의 자유주의 정치가들이 내놓았고, 세부를 다루는 수고는 그보다 낮은 직급의 관리들이 짊어졌다."], ["It was, however, thought expedient that the name of some clergyman should appear in such matters, and as Dr. Proudie had become known as a tolerating divine, great use of this sort was made of his name.", "다만 그런 사안에는 어느 성직자의 이름이 하나 걸려 있는 편이 편리하다고들 여겼고, 프라우디 박사는 관용적인 신학자로 알려져 있었으므로 그의 이름이 이런 용도로 요긴하게 쓰였다."], ["If he did not do much active good, he never did any harm; he was amenable to those who were really in authority and, at the sittings of the various boards to which he belonged, maintained a kind of dignity which had its value.", "그가 적극적으로 좋은 일을 많이 하지는 않았다 해도 해를 끼친 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그는 실권을 쥔 이들의 뜻에 순순히 따랐고, 자신이 속한 여러 위원회의 회의에서는 나름의 값어치가 있는 위엄 같은 것을 지켰다."]], [["He was certainly possessed of sufficient tact to answer the purpose for which he was required without making himself troublesome; but it must not therefore be surmised that he doubted his own power, or failed to believe that he could himself take a high part in high affairs when his own turn came.", "그가 성가시게 굴지 않으면서도 자기에게 요구된 목적에 부응할 만한 눈치를 충분히 지녔던 것은 분명하다. 그러나 그렇다고 그가 자신의 역량을 의심했다거나, 제 차례가 오면 큰일에서 큰 몫을 스스로 맡을 수 있으리라고 믿지 못했다고 짐작해서는 안 된다."], ["He was biding his time, and patiently looking forward to the days when he himself would sit authoritative at some board, and talk and direct, and rule the roost, while lesser stars sat round and obeyed, as he had so well accustomed himself to do.", "그는 때를 기다리고 있었으며, 자기가 어느 위원회의 상석에 권위 있게 앉아 말하고 지시하며 좌지우지하는 날, 그가 그토록 잘 익혀 온 그 순종을 이번에는 못한 별들이 빙 둘러앉아 하게 될 그날을 참을성 있게 고대하고 있었다."]], [["His reward and his time had now come.", "그의 보상과 그의 때가 마침내 왔다."], ["He was selected for the vacant bishopric and, on the next vacancy which might occur in any diocese, would take his place in the House of Lords, prepared to give not a silent vote in all matters concerning the weal of the church establishment.", "그는 비어 있는 주교좌에 선택되었고, 어느 교구에서든 다음 자리가 비면 상원에 자기 자리를 차지하게 될 터였으며, 국교회의 안녕에 관한 모든 사안에서 결코 말없는 표만 던지지는 않으리라 벼르고 있었다."], ["Toleration was to be the basis on which he was to fight his battles, and in the honest courage of his heart he thought no evil would come to him in encountering even such foes as his brethren of Exeter and Oxford.", "관용이 그가 싸움을 벌여 나갈 토대가 될 것이었고, 그는 그 정직한 담력으로, 엑서터와 옥스퍼드의 동료 주교들 같은 적수와 맞붙는다 해도 자기에게 나쁜 일이 생기지는 않으리라 생각했다."]], [["Dr. Proudie was an ambitious man, and before he was well consecrated Bishop of Barchester, he had begun to look up to archiepiscopal splendour, and the glories of Lambeth, or at any rate of Bishopsthorpe.", "프라우디 박사는 야심 있는 사람이었으니, 바체스터 주교로 채 축성되기도 전에 벌써 대주교의 광휘와 램버스의 영광을, 하다못해 비숍스소프의 영광이라도 우러러보기 시작했다."], ["He was comparatively young, and had, as he fondly flattered himself, been selected as possessing such gifts, natural and acquired, as must be sure to recommend him to a yet higher notice, now that a higher sphere was opened to him.", "그는 비교적 젊었고, 스스로 흐뭇하게 자부하기로는, 이제 더 높은 무대가 열린 마당에 반드시 더 높은 주목을 끌어 줄 만한 천부의 재능과 후천의 재능을 지녔기에 뽑힌 것이었다. 그러므로 프라우디 박사는 이 나라의 신학적 사안 전반에서 두드러진 역할을 맡을 준비가 아주 잘 되어 있었고, 그런 포부를 지닌 터라 전임자처럼 바체스터에 파묻힐 생각은 조금도 없었다."], ["Dr.", "천만에!"], ["Proudie was, therefore, quite prepared to take a conspicuous part in all theological affairs appertaining to these realms; and having such views, by no means intended to bury himself at Barchester as his predecessor had done.", "런던이 여전히 그의 활동 무대가 되어야 했다. 지방 도시의 안락한 저택이야 한 해 중 죽은 듯한 달들에나 어울릴 만한 것이었다."], ["No! London should still be his ground: a comfortable mansion in a provincial city might be well enough for the dead months of the year.", "사실 프라우디 박사는 다른 훌륭하고 유행에 밝은 사람들이 런던을 뜰 때 자기도 물러나는 것이 자기 지위에 필요하다고 늘 여겨 왔다."], ["Indeed, Dr. Proudie had always felt it necessary to his position to retire from London when other great and fashionable people did so; but London should still be his fixed residence, and it was in London that he resolved to exercise that hospitality so peculiarly recommended to all bishops by St.", "그러나 런던은 여전히 그의 붙박이 거처여야 했고, 사도 바울이 모든 주교에게 그토록 각별히 권한 그 손님 접대를 그가 베풀기로 작정한 곳도 런던이었다."], ["Paul. How otherwise could he keep himself before the world?", "그러지 않고서야 어떻게 세상 앞에 자신을 계속 내세울 수 있겠는가?"], ["How else give to the government, in matters theological, the full benefit of his weight and talents?", "달리 어떻게 신학적 사안에서 자신의 무게와 재능을 정부에 온전히 보태 줄 수 있겠는가?"]], [["This resolution was no doubt a salutary one as regarded the world at large, but was not likely to make him popular either with the clergy or people of Barchester. Dr.", "이 결심은 넓은 세상을 위해서는 의심할 바 없이 유익한 것이었겠으나, 바체스터의 성직자에게나 주민에게나 그를 인기 있는 사람으로 만들어 줄 것 같지는 않았다."], ["Grantly had always lived there--in truth, it was hard for a bishop to be popular after Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사는 늘 그곳에 살았다."], ["His income had averaged £9,000 a year; his successor was to be rigidly limited to £5,000.", "사실 그랜틀리 박사의 뒤를 이어 인기를 얻기란 주교로서 어려운 일이었다."], ["He had but one child on whom to spend his money; Dr. Proudie had seven or eight.", "그의 수입은 연평균 9,000파운드였는데, 후임자는 5,000파운드로 엄격히 제한될 것이었다."], ["He had been a man of few personal expenses, and they had been confined to the tastes of a moderate gentleman; but Dr.", "그는 돈을 쓸 자식이 하나뿐이었으나 프라우디 박사에게는 일고여덟이 있었다."], ["Proudie had to maintain a position in fashionable society, and had that to do with comparatively small means. Dr.", "그는 개인적 씀씀이가 적은 사람이었고 그 씀씀이도 검소한 신사의 취향에 머물렀지만, 프라우디 박사는 유행하는 사교계에서 지위를 지켜야 했으며 그것도 비교적 적은 재력으로 해내야 했다."], ["Grantly had certainly kept his carriage as became a bishop, but his carriage, horses, and coachman, though they did very well for Barchester, would have been almost ridiculous at Westminster. Mrs.", "그랜틀리 박사도 주교답게 마차를 갖추기는 했으나, 그의 마차와 말과 마부는 바체스터에서야 나무랄 데 없었을지 몰라도 웨스트민스터에서는 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없었을 것이다."], ["Proudie determined that her husband's equipage should not shame her, and things on which Mrs. Proudie resolved were generally accomplished.", "프라우디 부인은 남편의 마차 차림새가 자기에게 창피를 주게 두지 않겠다고 마음먹었고, 프라우디 부인이 마음먹은 일은 대개 이루어졌다."]], [["From all this it was likely to result that Dr. Proudie would not spend much money at Barchester, whereas his predecessor had dealt with the tradesmen of the city in a manner very much to their satisfaction.", "이 모든 사정으로 미루어 프라우디 박사가 바체스터에서 돈을 많이 쓰지는 않으리라는 결과가 나올 법했다."], ["The Grantlys, father and son, had spent their money like gentlemen, but it soon became whispered in Barchester that Dr.", "반면 그의 전임자는 그 도시의 상인들을 그들로서는 매우 흡족하게 상대해 주었다."], ["Proudie was not unacquainted with those prudent devices by which the utmost show of wealth is produced from limited means.", "아버지와 아들, 두 그랜틀리는 신사답게 돈을 썼으나, 프라우디 박사는 한정된 재력에서 최대한의 부유한 겉모습을 짜내는 저 알뜰한 방편들에 낯설지 않은 사람이라는 수군거림이 바체스터에 곧 돌기 시작했다."]], [["In person Dr. Proudie is a good-looking man; spruce and dapper, and very tidy.", "생김새로 말하자면 프라우디 박사는 잘생긴 사람이다."], ["He is somewhat below middle height, being about five feet four; but he makes up for the inches which he wants by the dignity with which he carries those which he has.", "말쑥하고 맵시 있고 아주 단정하다. 키는 중키에 조금 못 미쳐 5피트 4인치쯤 되지만, 모자라는 치수는 지닌 치수를 지니고 다니는 위엄으로 메운다."], ["It is no fault of his own if he has not a commanding eye, for he studies hard to assume it.", "그에게 사람을 압도하는 눈매가 없다면 그것은 그의 탓이 아니니, 그는 그런 눈매를 갖추려고 무던히도 애쓰기 때문이다."], ["His features are well formed, though perhaps the sharpness of his nose may give to his face in the eyes of some people an air of insignificance.", "이목구비는 반듯한데, 다만 코가 뾰족한 탓에 어떤 사람들 눈에는 그 얼굴이 대수롭지 않아 보일지도 모른다."], ["If so, it is greatly redeemed by his mouth and chin, of which he is justly proud.", "설령 그렇더라도 그가 자랑할 만도 한 입과 턱이 그 흠을 넉넉히 벌충해 준다."]], [["Dr. Proudie may well be said to have been a fortunate man, for he was not born to wealth, and he is now Bishop of Barchester; nevertheless, he has his cares.", "프라우디 박사는 운 좋은 사람이었다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["He has a large family, of whom the three eldest are daughters, now all grown up and fit for fashionable life;--and he has a wife.", "부유하게 태어나지 않았는데 이제 바체스터 주교가 되었으니 말이다. 그렇지만 그에게도 근심은 있다."], ["It is not my intention to breathe a word against the character of Mrs.", "그는 식구가 많고, 그중 위로 셋은 딸인데 이제 모두 다 자라 사교계에 나설 만하다."], ["Proudie, but still I cannot think that with all her virtues she adds much to her husband's happiness.", "그리고 그에게는 아내가 있다. 나는 프라우디 부인의 인품에 대해 한마디라도 흠잡을 생각은 없다."], ["The truth is that in matters domestic she rules supreme over her titular lord, and rules with a rod of iron.", "그러나 그 온갖 미덕에도 불구하고 그 부인이 남편의 행복에 크게 보태 준다고는 아무래도 생각할 수가 없다. 사실인즉 그 부인은 집안일에서 명목상의 주인인 남편 위에 군림하며, 그것도 쇠몽둥이로 다스린다."], ["Nor is this all.", "그뿐이 아니다."], ["Things domestic Dr. Proudie might have abandoned to her, if not voluntarily, yet willingly.", "집안일이라면 프라우디 박사도, 자진해서는 아닐지언정 기꺼이 아내에게 넘겨주었을 것이다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie is not satisfied with such home dominion, and stretches her power over all his movements, and will not even abstain from things spiritual.", "그러나 프라우디 부인은 그런 가정 안의 지배로는 만족하지 않아 남편의 모든 거동에까지 권세를 뻗치며, 영적인 일에서마저 손을 떼려 하지 않는다."], ["In fact, the bishop is hen-pecked.", "요컨대 이 주교는 아내에게 쥐여산다."]], [["The archdeacon's wife, in her happy home at Plumstead, knows how to assume the full privileges of her rank and express her own mind in becoming tone and place.", "부주교의 아내는 플럼스테드의 행복한 집에서 제 신분에 딸린 특권을 온전히 누릴 줄 알고, 걸맞은 어조로 걸맞은 자리에서 제 뜻을 밝힐 줄도 안다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인의 다스림은, 다스림이라 할 것이 있다면, 부드럽고 이롭다."], ["But Mrs. Grantly's sway, if sway she has, is easy and beneficent.", "그 부인은 남편에게 결코 창피를 주지 않으며, 세상 앞에서는 순종의 본보기다."], ["She never shames her husband; before the world she is a pattern of obedience; her voice is never loud, nor her looks sharp: doubtless she values power, and has not unsuccessfully striven to acquire it; but she knows what should be the limits of a woman's rule.", "목소리를 높이는 법도, 눈매가 매서워지는 법도 없다. 물론 권세를 소중히 여기고 그것을 손에 넣으려 애써 왔으며 그 애씀이 헛되지도 않았지만, 여자의 다스림이 어디까지여야 하는지 그 한계를 그 부인은 안다."]], [["Not so Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인은 그렇지 않다."], ["This lady is habitually authoritative to all, but to her poor husband she is despotic.", "이 부인은 누구에게나 늘 위압적이지만 가엾은 남편에게는 전제군주다."], ["Successful as has been his career in the eyes of the world, it would seem that in the eyes of his wife he is never right.", "세상의 눈에는 그의 이력이 성공적이었을지 몰라도, 아내의 눈에는 그가 옳은 적이 한 번도 없는 듯싶다."], ["All hope of defending himself has long passed from him; indeed he rarely even attempts self-justification, and is aware that submission produces the nearest approach to peace which his own house can ever attain.", "자신을 변호해 보겠다는 희망은 그에게서 사라진 지 오래다. 실제로 그는 변명을 시도하는 일조차 드물거니와, 순순히 굽히는 것이야말로 제 집안이 도달할 수 있는 평화에 가장 가까운 길임을 알고 있다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie has not been able to sit at the boards and committees to which her husband has been called by the State, nor, as he often reflects, can she make her voice heard in the House of Lords.", "프라우디 부인은 남편이 국가의 부름을 받아 나가는 위원회와 심의회 자리에 앉을 수는 없었고, 그가 자주 되새기는 바이지만 상원에서 제 목소리를 들리게 할 수도 없다."], ["It may be that she will refuse to him permission to attend to this branch of a bishop's duties; it may be that she will insist on his close attendance to his own closet.", "어쩌면 그 부인은 주교 직무의 이 갈래에 남편이 관여하는 것을 허락하지 않을지도 모른다. 어쩌면 제 서재에나 붙어 있으라고 우길지도 모른다."], ["He has never whispered a word on the subject to living ears, but he has already made his fixed resolve.", "그는 이 문제를 두고 살아 있는 그 누구의 귀에도 한마디 속삭인 적이 없지만, 이미 마음속으로 굳게 작정해 두었다."], ["Should such attempt be made he will rebel.", "그런 시도가 있을진대 자기는 반역하리라고."], ["Dogs have turned against their masters, and even Neapolitans against their rulers, when oppression has been too severe.", "억압이 지나치게 가혹해지면 개도 주인에게 대들고 나폴리 사람들도 통치자에게 대드는 법이다."], ["And Dr. Proudie feels within himself that if the cord be drawn too tight, he also can muster courage and resist.", "그리고 프라우디 박사는 밧줄이 지나치게 조여진다면 자기 역시 용기를 그러모아 버틸 수 있으리라고 속으로 느끼고 있다."]], [["The state of vassalage in which our bishop has been kept by his wife has not tended to exalt his character in the eyes of his daughters, who assume in addressing their father too much of that authority which is not properly belonging, at any rate, to them.", "우리 주교가 아내에게 붙들려 지내는 이 예속 상태는 딸들의 눈에 아버지의 품위를 높여 주지 못했으니, 딸들은 아버지에게 말할 때 적어도 저희에게는 어울리지 않는 권위를 지나치게 부린다."], ["They are, on the whole, fine engaging young ladies.", "딸들은 대체로 훤칠하고 사람을 끄는 처녀들이다."], ["They are tall and robust like their mother, whose high cheek-bones, and--we may say auburn hair they all inherit.", "어머니를 닮아 키가 크고 튼튼하며, 어머니의 도드라진 광대뼈와 — 적갈색이라고 해 두자 — 그 머리칼을 셋 다 물려받았다."], ["They think somewhat too much of their grand-uncles, who have not hitherto returned the compliment by thinking much of them.", "딸들은 종조부들을 좀 과하게 떠받드는데, 종조부들 쪽에서는 이제껏 그만큼 저희를 떠받들어 답례한 적이 없다."], ["But now that their father is a bishop, it is probable that family ties will be drawn closer.", "그러나 이제 아버지가 주교가 되었으니 친척의 정이 더 가까워질 법도 하다."], ["Considering their connexion with the church, they entertain but few prejudices against the pleasures of the world, and have certainly not distressed their parents, as too many English girls have lately done, by any enthusiastic wish to devote themselves to the seclusion of a Protestant nunnery.", "교회와 얽힌 집안인 것을 생각하면 딸들은 세상의 즐거움에 대해 편견이랄 것이 거의 없으며, 근래 너무 많은 영국 처녀들이 그랬듯 개신교 수녀원의 은둔에 몸을 바치겠다는 열띤 소망 따위로 부모의 애를 태운 적도 결코 없다."], ["Dr. Proudie's sons are still at school.", "프라우디 박사의 아들들은 아직 학교에 다닌다."]], [["One other marked peculiarity in the character of the bishop's wife must be mentioned.", "주교 부인의 성격에서 두드러진 특징을 하나 더 짚어 두어야겠다."], ["Though not averse to the society and manners of the world, she is in her own way a religious woman; and the form in which this tendency shows itself in her is by a strict observance of Sabbatarian rule.", "세상의 교제와 몸가짐을 싫어하지는 않으면서도, 그 부인은 나름대로 신앙심이 깊은 여자다. 그리고 그 성향이 그 부인에게서 드러나는 형태는 안식일 규율의 엄격한 준수다."], ["Dissipation and low dresses during the week are, under her control, atoned for by three services, an evening sermon read by herself, and a perfect abstinence from any cheering employment on the Sunday.", "주중의 방탕과 목이 파인 옷차림은, 그 부인의 관할 아래, 세 차례의 예배와 부인이 손수 읽는 저녁 설교와 주일에는 마음을 즐겁게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 완전히 삼가는 것으로 속량된다."], ["Unfortunately for those under her roof to whom the dissipation and low dresses are not extended, her servants namely and her husband, the compensating strictness of the Sabbath includes all.", "한 지붕 아래 있으면서도 그 방탕과 목이 파인 옷차림은 나누어 받지 못하는 이들, 곧 하인들과 남편에게는 불행하게도, 그 벌충으로서의 안식일 엄수만은 모두에게 적용된다."], ["Woe betide the recreant housemaid who is found to have been listening to the honey of a sweetheart in the Regent's park instead of the soul-stirring evening discourse of Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨의 혼을 흔드는 저녁 강론 대신 리전트 파크에서 애인의 달콤한 소리에 귀를 기울인 것이 들통난 배교자 하녀에게는 화가 있을진저."], ["Not only is she sent adrift, but she is so sent with a character which leaves her little hope of a decent place.", "그 하녀는 쫓겨날 뿐 아니라, 반반한 자리를 다시 얻을 가망이 거의 없는 평판을 달고 쫓겨난다."], ["Woe betide the six-foot hero who escorts Mrs. Proudie to her pew in red plush breeches, if he slips away to the neighbouring beer-shop, instead of falling into the back seat appropriated to his use.", "붉은 플러시 반바지 차림으로 프라우디 부인을 좌석까지 모시는 6피트짜리 장정이 제게 배정된 뒷자리에 앉는 대신 이웃 맥줏집으로 슬쩍 빠져나간다면, 그에게도 화가 있을진저."], ["Mrs. Proudie has the eyes of Argus for such offenders.", "그런 죄인을 살피는 데 프라우디 부인은 아르고스의 눈을 지녔다."], ["Occasional drunkenness in the week may be overlooked, for six feet on low wages are hardly to be procured if the morals are always kept at a high pitch, but not even for grandeur or economy will Mrs.", "주중에 이따금 술에 취하는 것쯤은 눈감아 줄 수 있다. 품행을 늘 높은 수준으로 지키라고 하면 낮은 삯으로 6피트짜리를 구하기가 어려운 탓이다."], ["Proudie forgive a desecration of the Sabbath.", "그러나 위엄을 위해서든 살림을 아끼기 위해서든, 안식일을 더럽히는 일만은 프라우디 부인이 결코 용서하지 않는다."]], [["In such matters Mrs. Proudie allows herself to be often guided by that eloquent preacher, the Rev. Mr. Slope, and as Dr. Proudie is guided by his wife, it necessarily follows that the eminent man we have named has obtained a good deal of control over Dr. Proudie in matters concerning religion. Mr. Slope's only preferment has hitherto been that of reader and preacher in a London district church; and on the consecration of his friend the new bishop, he readily gave this up to undertake the onerous but congenial duties of domestic chaplain to his lordship.", "그런 문제에서 프라우디 부인은 저 웅변에 능한 설교자 슬로프 목사의 인도를 곧잘 받아들이고, 프라우디 박사는 아내의 인도를 받으므로, 방금 이름을 댄 그 걸출한 인물이 종교에 관한 사안에서 프라우디 박사를 상당히 좌우하게 되었다는 결론이 자연히 따라 나온다. 슬로프 씨가 이제껏 얻은 성직이라고는 런던의 한 구역 교회에서 성경을 읽고 설교하는 자리뿐이었는데, 벗인 새 주교가 축성되자 그는 그 자리를 선뜻 내놓고 주교님의 가정 전속 사제라는 고되지만 제 마음에 꼭 맞는 직무를 떠맡았다."]], [["Mr. Slope, however, on his first introduction must not be brought before the public at the tail of a chapter.", "그러나 슬로프 씨를 처음 선보이면서 한 장의 꽁무니에 그를 세상 앞으로 끌어낼 수야 없는 노릇이다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV. The Bishop's Chaplain", "# 제4장. 주교의 전속 사제"]], [["Of the Rev. Mr.", "슬로프 목사의 혈통에 관해서는 내가 말할 수 있는 바가 많지 않다."], ["Slope's parentage I am not able to say much.", "나는 그가 T."], ["I have heard it asserted that he is lineally descended from that eminent physician who assisted at the birth of Mr. T.", "섄디 씨의 출생을 거들었던 저 저명한 의사의 직계 후손이며, 앞서 여러 위인들이 그리했듯 젊은 시절에 듣기 좋으라고 제 이름에 “e”"], ["Shandy, and that in early years he added an \"e\"", "한 자를 덧붙였다는 말을 들은 적이 있다."], ["to his name, for the sake of euphony, as other great men have done before him. If this be so, I presume he was christened Obadiah, for that is his name, in commemoration of the conflict in which his ancestor so distinguished himself.", "만일 그것이 사실이라면 그는 오바디아라는 이름으로 세례를 받았으리라 짐작되는데, 실제로 그것이 그의 이름이며, 이는 그 조상이 그토록 두각을 나타냈던 저 다툼을 기린 것이리라."], ["All my researches on the subject have, however, failed in enabling me to fix the date on which the family changed its religion.", "그러나 이 문제에 관한 나의 온갖 조사도, 그 집안이 언제 종교를 갈아탔는지 그 날짜를 확정하는 데는 끝내 이르지 못했다."]], [["He had been a sizar at Cambridge, and had there conducted himself at any rate successfully, for in due process of time he was an M.A., having university pupils under his care.", "그는 케임브리지의 급비생이었고, 그곳에서 적어도 성공적으로 처신하기는 했다. 때가 되자 문학 석사가 되어 제 밑에 대학생 제자들을 두었으니 말이다."], ["From thence he was transferred to London, and became preacher at a new district church built on the confines of Baker Street.", "그는 거기서 런던으로 옮겨 가, 베이커 스트리트 어름에 새로 세워진 구역 교회의 설교자가 되었다."], ["He was in this position when congenial ideas on religious subjects recommended him to Mrs. Proudie, and the intercourse had become close and confidential.", "종교 문제에 관한 견해가 서로 맞아떨어져 그가 프라우디 부인에게 천거된 것은 바로 이 자리에 있을 때였고, 두 사람의 왕래는 이내 긴밀하고 흉허물 없는 사이로 발전했다."]], [["Having been thus familiarly thrown among the Misses Proudie, it was no more than natural that some softer feeling than friendship should be engendered.", "이렇게 프라우디 집안 아가씨들 사이에 스스럼없이 섞이게 된 터라, 우정보다 조금 더 부드러운 감정이 싹튼 것도 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["There have been some passages of love between him and the eldest hope, Olivia, but they have hitherto resulted in no favourable arrangement. In truth, Mr.", "그와 그 집의 맏딸 올리비아 사이에는 사랑의 곡절이 몇 차례 있었으나, 지금까지 이렇다 할 결실에는 이르지 못했다."], ["Slope, having made a declaration of affection, afterwards withdrew it on finding that the doctor had no immediate worldly funds with which to endow his child, and it may easily be conceived that Miss Proudie, after such an announcement on his part, was not readily disposed to receive any further show of affection.", "실인즉 슬로프 씨는 애정을 고백해 놓고서, 그 박사에게 제 딸에게 당장 떼어 줄 재산이 없다는 것을 알고는 그 고백을 도로 거두어들였던 것이다. 그러니 그런 통고를 받은 프라우디 양이 그 뒤로 그가 내미는 애정의 표시를 선뜻 받아들일 마음이 나지 않았으리라는 것은 쉬이 짐작할 수 있는 일이다."], ["On the appointment of Dr. Proudie to the bishopric of Barchester, Mr.", "그런데 프라우디 박사가 바체스터 주교로 임명되자 슬로프 씨의 견해는 사실 다소 달라졌다."], ["Slope's views were in truth somewhat altered. Bishops, even though they be poor, can provide for clerical children, and Mr.", "주교란 아무리 가난할지언정 성직에 몸담은 자식쯤은 넉넉히 앉힐 수 있는 자리인지라, 슬로프 씨는 자기가 좀 더 사심 없이 굴지 못한 것을 후회하기 시작했다."], ["Slope began to regret that he had not been more disinterested. He no sooner heard the tidings of the doctor's elevation than he recommenced his siege, not violently, indeed, but respectfully, and at a distance.", "그는 박사가 승진했다는 소식을 듣자마자 다시 공략을 개시했는데, 물론 거칠게가 아니라 공손하게, 또 적당한 거리를 두고서였다."], ["Olivia Proudie, however, was a girl of spirit: she had the blood of two peers in her veins, and better still she had another lover on her books, so Mr.", "그러나 올리비아 프라우디는 기개 있는 처녀였다. 그 혈관에는 귀족 두 집안의 피가 흐르고 있었고, 더구나 장부에 올려 둔 다른 구혼자가 하나 더 있었다."], ["Slope sighed in vain, and the pair soon found it convenient to establish a mutual bond of inveterate hatred.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨는 헛되이 한숨만 쉬었고, 두 사람은 이내 서로에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오의 유대를 맺어 두는 편이 편하다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["It may be thought singular that Mrs. Proudie's friendship for the young clergyman should remain firm after such an affair, but, to tell the truth, she had known nothing of it.", "그런 일이 있고서도 젊은 성직자에 대한 프라우디 부인의 우정이 흔들림 없이 남아 있다는 것이 이상하게 여겨질지 모르나, 실토하자면 부인은 그 일을 전혀 모르고 있었다."], ["Though very fond of Mr. Slope herself, she had never conceived the idea that either of her daughters would become so, and remembering their high birth and social advantages, expected for them matches of a different sort.", "부인 자신은 슬로프 씨를 몹시 아꼈으면서도, 제 딸 가운데 누가 그를 그런 눈으로 볼 수 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고, 딸들의 고귀한 태생과 사교계에서의 이점을 떠올리며 그들에게는 전혀 다른 종류의 혼처를 기대하고 있었던 것이다."], ["Neither the gentleman nor the lady found it necessary to enlighten her.", "신사도 숙녀도 굳이 부인을 깨우쳐 줄 필요를 느끼지 않았다."], ["Olivia's two sisters had each known of the affair, as had all the servants, as had all the people living in the adjoining houses on either side, but Mrs. Proudie had been kept in the dark.", "올리비아의 두 자매는 저마다 그 일을 알고 있었고, 하인들도 모조리 알고 있었으며, 양옆 이웃집에 사는 사람들까지 죄다 알고 있었으나, 프라우디 부인만은 감쪽같이 어둠 속에 남겨져 있었다."]], [["Mr. Slope soon comforted himself with the reflexion that, as he had been selected as chaplain to the bishop, it would probably be in his power to get the good things in the bishop's gift without troubling himself with the bishop's daughter, and he found himself able to endure the pangs of rejected love.", "슬로프 씨는 곧 이런 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다."], ["As he sat himself down in the railway carriage, confronting the bishop and Mrs.", "이미 주교의 전속 사제로 뽑힌 이상, 주교의 딸 때문에 애를 태우지 않고도 주교가 나눠 줄 좋은 것들을 손에 넣는 일쯤은 아마 제 힘으로 될 것이라고."], ["Proudie as they started on their first journey to Barchester, he began to form in his own mind a plan of his future life.", "그러자 거절당한 사랑의 아픔쯤은 능히 견딜 만해졌다. 주교 내외가 바체스터로 첫 여행길에 오를 때, 그는 그들과 마주 보는 자리에 앉아 기차 객실에 몸을 묻고서 제 앞날의 계획을 마음속으로 짜기 시작했다."], ["He knew well his patron's strong points, but he knew the weak ones as well.", "그는 자기 후원자의 강점을 잘 알았지만, 약점 또한 그만큼 잘 알았다."], ["He understood correctly enough to what attempts the new bishop's high spirit would soar, and he rightly guessed that public life would better suit the great man's taste than the small details of diocesan duty.", "새 주교의 드높은 기개가 어떤 시도를 향해 날아오를지를 그는 꽤나 정확하게 헤아리고 있었으며, 그 위인의 취향에는 교구 사무의 자잘한 일보다 공적인 활동이 더 맞으리라고 옳게 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["He, therefore,--he, Mr. Slope,--would in effect be Bishop of Barchester.", "그러니 그가, 바로 그 슬로프 씨가, 사실상 바체스터의 주교가 될 것이었다."], ["Such was his resolve, and to give Mr. Slope his due, he had both courage and spirit to bear him out in his resolution.", "이것이 그의 결심이었고, 슬로프 씨에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 그에게는 그 결심을 밀고 나갈 만한 담력과 기개가 함께 있었다."], ["He knew that he should have a hard battle to fight, for the power and patronage of the see would be equally coveted by another great mind--Mrs.", "그는 힘겨운 싸움을 치러야 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 교구의 권세와 성직 임명권을 탐내는 또 하나의 위대한 정신이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Proudie would also choose to be Bishop of Barchester. Mr.", "프라우디 부인 또한 바체스터의 주교가 되기를 택할 터였다."], ["Slope, however, flattered himself that he could outmanoeuvre the lady.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 자기가 그 부인의 허를 찌를 수 있으리라고 자부했다."], ["She must live much in London, while he would always be on the spot.", "부인은 런던에서 지내는 날이 많을 테지만 자기는 언제나 현장에 붙어 있을 것이었다."], ["She would necessarily remain ignorant of much, while he would know everything belonging to the diocese.", "부인은 필연코 모르는 일이 많을 테지만 자기는 교구에 속한 것이라면 무엇이든 알고 있을 것이었다."], ["At first, doubtless, he must flatter and cajole, perhaps yield in some things, but he did not doubt of ultimate triumph.", "물론 처음에는 아첨도 하고 비위도 맞춰야 할 테고, 더러는 양보도 해야 할 테지만, 결국은 이기리라는 것을 그는 의심하지 않았다."], ["If all other means failed, he could join the bishop against his wife, inspire courage into the unhappy man, lay an axe to the root of the woman's power, and emancipate the husband.", "다른 방책이 다 실패한다면 주교 편에 서서 그 아내에 맞설 수도 있었다. 그 가엾은 사내에게 용기를 불어넣고, 여인의 권세에 도끼를 대어 뿌리를 찍고, 남편을 해방시켜 주면 되는 것이다."]], [["Such were his thoughts as he sat looking at the sleeping pair in the railway carriage, and Mr. Slope is not the man to trouble himself with such thoughts for nothing.", "기차 객실에서 잠든 두 사람을 바라보며 그가 품었던 생각이 이러했으니, 슬로프 씨는 그런 생각을 공연히 품고 앉아 있을 위인이 아니다."], ["He is possessed of more than average abilities, and is of good courage.", "그는 평균을 웃도는 능력을 지녔고 담력도 있다."], ["Though he can stoop to fawn, and stoop low indeed, if need be, he has still within him the power to assume the tyrant;--and with the power he has certainly the wish.", "필요하다면 몸을 굽혀 알랑거릴 수도 있고 실로 아주 낮게까지 굽힐 수도 있지만, 그 안에는 폭군 노릇을 떠맡을 힘이 여전히 들어 있다. 그리고 그 힘과 더불어 그런 욕망 또한 분명히 지니고 있다."], ["His acquirements are not of the highest order, but such as they are, they are completely under control, and he knows the use of them.", "그의 학식은 최상급이라 할 것은 못 되나, 있는 만큼은 완전히 제 통제 아래 두고 있으며 그것을 쓸 줄도 안다."], ["He is gifted with a certain kind of pulpit eloquence, not likely indeed to be persuasive with men, but powerful with the softer sex.", "그는 일종의 강단 웅변술을 타고났는데, 남자들을 설득하기에는 그리 미덥지 못할지언정 연약한 성에게는 위력을 떨치는 종류의 것이다."], ["In his sermons he deals greatly in denunciations, excites the minds of his weaker hearers with a not unpleasant terror, and leaves an impression on their minds that all mankind are in a perilous state, and all womankind, too, except those who attend regularly to the evening lectures in Baker Street.", "설교에서 그는 규탄을 아낌없이 퍼부어, 마음 약한 청중의 심령을 아주 불쾌하지만은 않은 공포로 뒤흔들어 놓고는, 온 인류가 위태로운 처지에 있으며 여인들 또한 그러하되 다만 베이커 스트리트의 저녁 강론에 꼬박꼬박 나오는 이들만은 예외라는 인상을 그들 마음에 남겨 놓는다."], ["His looks and tones are extremely severe, so much so that one cannot but fancy that he regards the greater part of the world as being infinitely too bad for his care.", "그의 표정과 말씨는 지극히 준엄해서, 이 세상의 태반은 자기가 마음 쓰기에는 한없이 못돼먹었다고 그가 여기고 있구나 싶은 생각을 아니 할 도리가 없다."], ["As he walks through the streets his very face denotes his horror of the world's wickedness, and there is always an anathema lurking in the corner of his eye.", "거리를 걸어갈 때면 그 얼굴 자체가 세상의 사악함에 대한 그의 경악을 드러내며, 눈꼬리에는 언제나 저주의 말이 도사리고 있다."]], [["In doctrine he, like his patron, is tolerant of dissent, if so strict a mind can be called tolerant of anything.", "교리로 말하자면 그는 자기 후원자와 마찬가지로 비국교도에게 너그러운 편인데, 그토록 빡빡한 정신을 두고 무엇엔가 너그럽다는 말을 붙일 수 있다면 그렇다는 이야기다."], ["With Wesleyan-Methodists he has something in common, but his soul trembles in agony at the iniquities of the Puseyites.", "웨슬리파 감리교도와는 통하는 데가 얼마쯤 있으나, 퓨지파의 죄악상 앞에서는 그의 영혼이 고통스레 떤다."], ["His aversion is carried to things outward as well as inward.", "그의 혐오는 안의 것에만이 아니라 겉의 것에까지 미친다."], ["His gall rises at a new church with a high-pitched roof; a full-breasted black silk waistcoat is with him a symbol of Satan; and a profane jest-book would not, in his view, more foully desecrate the church seat of a Christian than a book of prayer printed with red letters and ornamented with a cross on the back.", "지붕이 뾰족하게 솟은 새 교회를 보면 그는 부아가 치밀고, 앞가슴이 넓게 파인 검은 비단 조끼는 그에게 사탄의 상징이며, 붉은 글자로 찍히고 등에 십자가 장식이 박힌 기도서 한 권은 그가 보기에 불경한 농담집보다도 더 더럽게 그리스도인의 좌석을 욕되게 한다."], ["Most active clergymen have their hobby, and Sunday observances are his.", "부지런한 성직자에게는 대개 저마다의 편벽이 있는 법인데, 그의 것은 주일 성수다."], ["Sunday, however, is a word which never pollutes his mouth--it is always \"the Sabbath.\"", "그러나 “일요일”이라는 말이 그의 입을 더럽히는 일은 결코 없다."], ["The \"desecration of the Sabbath,\"", "그것은 언제나 “안식일”이다."], ["as he delights to call it, is to him meat and drink: he thrives upon that as policemen do on the general evil habits of the community.", "그가 즐겨 부르는 대로 하자면 “안식일의 모독”이야말로 그에게는 먹을 것이요 마실 것이다. 순경이 세상 사람들의 나쁜 버릇을 먹고 살듯 그는 그것을 먹고 산다."], ["It is the loved subject of all his evening discourses, the source of all his eloquence, the secret of all his power over the female heart.", "그것은 저녁 강론마다 그가 사랑해 마지않는 주제요, 그의 온갖 웅변의 원천이며, 여인의 마음을 휘어잡는 그의 힘의 비결이다."], ["To him the revelation of God appears only in that one law given for Jewish observance.", "그에게 하나님의 계시란 유대인이 지키라고 주어진 그 한 가지 율법 속에서만 나타난다."], ["To him the mercies of our Saviour speak in vain, to him in vain has been preached that sermon which fell from divine lips on the mountain--\"Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth\"--\"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy.\"", "그에게는 우리 구주의 자비하심이 헛되이 말할 뿐이요, 저 거룩한 입술이 산 위에서 내려 주신 설교, 곧 “온유한 자는 복이 있나니 그들이 땅을 기업으로 받을 것임이요”, “긍휼히 여기는 자는 복이 있나니 그들이 긍휼히 여김을 받을 것임이요” 하신 그 말씀도 헛되이 전파되었을 따름이다."], ["To him the New Testament is comparatively of little moment, for from it can he draw no fresh authority for that dominion which he loves to exercise over at least a seventh part of man's allotted time here below.", "그에게 신약성경은 비교적 대수롭지 않은 것인데, 이 땅에서 사람에게 배정된 시간의 적어도 7분의 1을 제 뜻대로 주무르기 좋아하는 그 지배권을 위해서는 거기서 아무런 새 권위도 끌어낼 수 없기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Slope is tall, and not ill-made.", "슬로프 씨는 키가 크고 몸매도 나쁘지 않다."], ["His feet and hands are large, as has ever been the case with all his family, but he has a broad chest and wide shoulders to carry off these excrescences, and on the whole his figure is good.", "손발이 큰 것은 그 집안 사람들이 예로부터 다 그러했던 바이지만, 그에게는 그 군더더기를 감당할 만한 넓은 가슴과 떡 벌어진 어깨가 있어서 전체적인 체구는 볼만하다."], ["His countenance, however, is not specially prepossessing.", "그러나 얼굴 생김은 그리 호감을 주지 못한다."], ["His hair is lank and of a dull pale reddish hue.", "머리칼은 힘없이 늘어졌고 빛깔은 흐릿하게 바랜 불그죽죽한 색이다."], ["It is always formed into three straight, lumpy masses, each brushed with admirable precision and cemented with much grease; two of them adhere closely to the sides of his face, and the other lies at right angles above them.", "그것은 언제나 꼿꼿하고 뭉툭한 세 덩어리로 지어져 있는데, 하나하나가 감탄스러울 만큼 정확하게 빗질된 뒤 기름으로 두껍게 굳혀져 있다. 그중 둘은 양쪽 뺨에 착 달라붙어 있고, 나머지 하나는 그 위에 직각으로 얹혀 있다."], ["He wears no whiskers, and is always punctiliously shaven.", "구레나룻은 기르지 않으며 언제나 빈틈없이 면도를 한다."], ["His face is nearly of the same colour as his hair, though perhaps a little redder: it is not unlike beef--beef, however, one would say, of a bad quality.", "낯빛은 머리칼과 거의 같은 색이되 어쩌면 조금 더 붉은데, 쇠고기와 그리 다르지 않다. 다만 질이 나쁜 쇠고기라고 해야 할 것이다."], ["His forehead is capacious and high, but square and heavy and unpleasantly shining.", "이마는 널찍하고 높지만 네모지고 둔중하며 불쾌하게 번들거린다."], ["His mouth is large, though his lips are thin and bloodless; and his big, prominent, pale-brown eyes inspire anything but confidence.", "입은 크되 입술은 얇고 핏기가 없으며, 크고 툭 불거진 옅은 갈색 눈은 신뢰라고 할 만한 것과는 정반대의 감정을 불러일으킨다. 그래도 코만은 그를 구제해 주는 대목이다."], ["His nose, however, is his redeeming feature: it is pronounced, straight and well-formed; though I myself should have liked it better did it not possess a somewhat spongy, porous appearance, as though it had been cleverly formed out of a red-coloured cork.", "코는 두드러지고 곧으며 반듯하게 잘 빠졌다. 다만 나로서는, 붉은 코르크를 솜씨 좋게 깎아 만들어 붙인 듯 어딘가 스펀지처럼 물컹하고 구멍이 숭숭한 느낌만 없었더라면 훨씬 마음에 들었을 것이다."]], [["I never could endure to shake hands with Mr. Slope.", "나는 슬로프 씨와 악수하는 일만은 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["A cold, clammy perspiration always exudes from him, the small drops are ever to be seen standing on his brow, and his friendly grasp is unpleasant.", "그에게서는 언제나 차고 끈적한 땀이 배어 나오고, 이마에는 잔 땀방울이 늘 맺혀 있는 것이 보이며, 그 정다운 악력은 불쾌하다."]], [["Such is Mr.", "이런 사람이 슬로프 씨다."], ["Slope--such is the man who has suddenly fallen into the midst of Barchester Close, and is destined there to assume the station which has heretofore been filled by the son of the late bishop.", "바체스터 대성당 경내 한복판에 난데없이 굴러떨어져, 지금껏 작고한 주교의 아들이 채우고 있던 자리를 그곳에서 떠맡게 될 운명을 진 사내가 바로 이런 사람이다."], ["Think, oh, my meditative reader, what an associate we have here for those comfortable prebendaries, those gentlemanlike clerical doctors, those happy, well-used, well-fed minor canons who have grown into existence at Barchester under the kindly wings of Bishop Grantly!", "생각해 보라, 사색을 즐기는 나의 독자여, 우리가 여기서 어떤 동료를 맞이하게 되었는가를. 저 안락한 참사회원들, 저 신사다운 성직 박사들, 그랜틀리 주교의 인자한 날개 아래 바체스터에서 자라난 저 행복하고 잘 대접받고 잘 먹은 하급 참사원들에게 말이다."]], [["But not as a mere associate for these does Mr. Slope travel down to Barchester with the bishop and his wife.", "그러나 슬로프 씨가 주교 내외와 함께 바체스터로 내려가는 것은 그저 그들의 동료가 되기 위해서가 아니다."], ["He intends to be, if not their master, at least the chief among them.", "그는 그들의 주인까지는 아닐지라도 적어도 그들 가운데 으뜸이 될 작정이다."], ["He intends to lead and to have followers; he intends to hold the purse-strings of the diocese and draw round him an obedient herd of his poor and hungry brethren.", "그는 이끌 작정이며 따르는 자들을 거느릴 작정이다. 그는 교구의 돈줄을 틀어쥐고 가난하고 굶주린 형제들을 순종하는 무리로 제 주위에 끌어모을 작정이다."]], [["And here we can hardly fail to draw a comparison between the archdeacon and our new private chaplain, and despite the manifold faults of the former, one can hardly fail to make it much to his advantage.", "그리고 여기서 우리는 부주교와 우리의 새 전속 사제를 견주어 보지 않을 수 없는데, 앞사람의 허물이 하고많음에도 불구하고 그 비교가 그에게 여간 유리하게 나오지 않는다는 것 또한 부정하기 어렵다."]], [["Both men are eager, much too eager, to support and increase the power of their order.", "두 사람 다 제 신분 계급의 권세를 떠받치고 키우는 데 열심이며, 지나칠 만큼 열심이다."], ["Both are anxious that the world should be priest-governed, though they have probably never confessed so much, even to themselves.", "둘 다 세상이 사제의 다스림을 받기를 갈망하는데, 아마 그런 속내를 저 자신에게조차 털어놓아 본 적은 없을 것이다."], ["Both begrudge any other kind of dominion held by man over man. Dr.", "둘 다 사람이 사람 위에 서는 다른 종류의 지배는 무엇이든 못마땅해한다."], ["Grantly, if he admits the Queen's supremacy in things spiritual, only admits it as being due to the quasi-priesthood conveyed in the consecrating qualities of her coronation, and he regards things temporal as being by their nature subject to those which are spiritual.", "그랜틀리 박사는 영적인 일에서 여왕의 수장권을 인정하기는 하되, 그것은 대관식의 성별하는 효력을 통해 여왕에게 전해진 준사제적 성격에서 비롯된 것으로 볼 때에만 인정하는 것이며, 세속의 일이란 그 본성상 영적인 일에 종속된다고 여긴다."], ["Mr. Slope's ideas of sacerdotal rule are of quite a different class.", "사제의 지배에 관한 슬로프 씨의 생각은 이와는 전혀 다른 부류의 것이다."], ["He cares nothing, one way or the other, for the Queen's supremacy; these to his ears are empty words, meaning nothing.", "그는 여왕의 수장권 따위에는 이렇든 저렇든 아무 관심이 없다. 그의 귀에 그런 것들은 아무 뜻도 없는 빈말일 뿐이다."], ["Forms he regards but little, and such titular expressions as supremacy, consecration, ordination, and the like convey of themselves no significance to him.", "그는 형식이란 것을 별로 대수롭게 여기지 않으며, 수장권이니 성별이니 안수니 하는 명목상의 표현들은 그 자체로는 그에게 아무 의미도 전해 주지 않는다."], ["Let him be supreme who can.", "으뜸이 될 수 있는 자가 으뜸이 되면 그만이다."], ["The temporal king, judge, or gaoler can work but on the body.", "세속의 왕이나 재판관이나 옥리는 몸뚱이에나 손을 댈 수 있을 뿐이다."], ["The spiritual master, if he have the necessary gifts and can duly use them, has a wider field of empire.", "영적인 주인은 필요한 재능이 있고 그것을 제대로 쓸 줄만 안다면 훨씬 더 넓은 지배의 판도를 갖는다."], ["He works upon the soul.", "그는 영혼을 다루기 때문이다."], ["If he can make himself be believed, he can be all powerful over those who listen.", "남들이 저를 믿게 만들 수만 있다면 그는 귀 기울이는 자들 위에 전능해질 수 있다."], ["If he be careful to meddle with none who are too strong in intellect, or too weak in flesh, he may indeed be supreme.", "지력이 너무 강한 자나 육신이 너무 약한 자와는 얽히지 않도록 조심하기만 하면 그는 실로 으뜸이 될 수 있는 것이다."], ["And such was the ambition of Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨의 야심이 바로 그러했다."]], [["Dr. Grantly interfered very little with the worldly doings of those who were in any way subject to him.", "그랜틀리 박사는 어떤 식으로든 제 아래 있는 이들의 세속적 처신에 거의 간섭하지 않았다."], ["I do not mean to say that he omitted to notice misconduct among his clergy, immorality in his parish, or omissions in his family, but he was not anxious to do so where the necessity could be avoided.", "그가 휘하 성직자의 비행이나 제 교구 안의 부도덕이나 제 집안의 소홀함을 못 본 체했다는 말은 아니다. 다만 굳이 그러지 않아도 될 자리에서는 애써 그러려 들지 않았다는 뜻이다."], ["He was not troubled with a propensity to be curious, and as long as those around him were tainted with no heretical leaning towards dissent, as long as they fully and freely admitted the efficacy of Mother Church, he was willing that that mother should be merciful and affectionate, prone to indulgence, and unwilling to chastise.", "그는 남의 일을 캐고 싶어 하는 성벽에 시달리지 않았으며, 제 주위 사람들이 비국교로 기우는 이단의 물이 들지 않은 한, 또 그들이 어머니 교회의 효력을 온전히 그리고 선선히 인정하는 한, 그 어머니가 자비롭고 다정하며 너그럽게 눈감아 주기를 즐기고 매를 들기는 꺼리는 편이기를 바랐다."], ["He himself enjoyed the good things of this world and liked to let it be known that he did so.", "그 자신은 이 세상의 좋은 것들을 누렸고 자기가 그러고 있다는 사실이 알려지는 것도 좋아했다."], ["He cordially despised any brother rector who thought harm of dinner-parties, or dreaded the dangers of a moderate claret-jug; consequently, dinner-parties and claret-jugs were common in the diocese.", "그는 만찬 모임을 해롭게 여기거나 클라레 한 병쯤의 위험을 겁내는 동료 교구 목사가 있으면 진심으로 경멸했다. 그리하여 만찬 모임과 클라레 병은 이 교구에서 흔한 것이 되었다."], ["He liked to give laws and to be obeyed in them implicitly, but he endeavoured that his ordinances should be within the compass of the man and not unpalatable to the gentleman.", "그는 법을 세우고 그 법이 군말 없이 지켜지는 것을 좋아했지만, 자기가 내리는 규례가 사람이 감당할 만한 범위 안에 들고 신사의 비위에 거슬리지 않도록 애썼다."], ["He had ruled among his clerical neighbours now for sundry years, and as he had maintained his power without becoming unpopular, it may be presumed that he had exercised some wisdom.", "그는 이제 여러 해째 이웃 성직자들 사이에서 군림해 왔는데, 인심을 잃지 않고서도 그 권세를 지켜 왔으니 얼마간의 지혜를 발휘해 온 것이라 짐작해도 좋을 것이다."]], [["Of Mr. Slope's conduct much cannot be said, as his grand career is yet to commence, but it may be premised that his tastes will be very different from those of the archdeacon.", "슬로프 씨의 처신에 관해서는 그의 대단한 이력이 아직 시작되지도 않았으므로 할 말이 많지 않지만, 그 취향이 부주교의 것과는 아주 딴판이리라는 것쯤은 미리 말해 둘 수 있다."], ["He conceives it to be his duty to know all the private doings and desires of the flock entrusted to his care.", "그는 제게 맡겨진 양 떼의 사사로운 행실과 욕망을 죄다 아는 것이 제 직분이라고 여긴다."], ["From the poorer classes he exacts an unconditional obedience to set rules of conduct, and if disobeyed he has recourse, like his great ancestor, to the fulminations of an Ernulfus: \"Thou shalt be damned in thy going in and in thy coming out--in thy eating and thy drinking,\"", "가난한 계층에게서는 정해진 행실의 규칙에 대한 무조건적인 복종을 요구하며, 순종하지 않는 자가 있으면 그 위대한 조상이 그러했듯 에르눌푸스의 저주를 끌어다 쓴다. “너는 들어갈 때에도 저주를 받고 나올 때에도 저주를 받을 것이며, 먹을 때에도 마실 때에도 저주를 받으리라”"], ["&c. &c.", "운운, 운운, 운운."], ["&c. With the rich, experience has already taught him that a different line of action is necessary.", "부유한 자들에게는 다른 방식이 필요하다는 것을 경험이 이미 그에게 가르쳐 주었다."], ["Men in the upper walks of life do not mind being cursed, and the women, presuming that it be done in delicate phrase, rather like it.", "상류 사회를 걸어가는 남자들은 저주를 받아도 아랑곳하지 않고, 여자들은 그것이 고상한 말씨로 행해지기만 한다면 오히려 좋아하는 편이다."], ["But he has not, therefore, given up so important a portion of believing Christians.", "그렇다고 그가 믿는 그리스도인들 가운데 그토록 중요한 몫을 포기해 버린 것은 아니다. 남자들과는 실로 대체로 사이가 틀어져 있다."], ["With the men, indeed, he is generally at variance; they are hardened sinners, on whom the voice of the priestly charmer too often falls in vain; but with the ladies, old and young, firm and frail, devout and dissipated, he is, as he conceives, all powerful.", "그들은 완악한 죄인들이라 사제라는 마술사의 목소리가 너무도 자주 헛되이 떨어지고 만다. 그러나 부인들이라면 늙었든 젊었든, 굳세든 여리든, 경건하든 방탕하든, 그는 제 생각에 전능하다."], ["He can reprove faults with so much flattery and utter censure in so caressing a manner that the female heart, if it glow with a spark of Low Church susceptibility, cannot withstand him.", "그는 아첨을 잔뜩 섞어 잘못을 나무랄 줄 알고, 어루만지듯 하는 태도로 질책을 늘어놓을 줄 아는지라, 여인의 마음이란 저교회파의 감수성이 한 점이라도 타오르고 있는 한 그를 당해 낼 수 없다."], ["In many houses he is thus an admired guest: the husbands, for their wives'", "그리하여 그는 많은 집에서 흠모받는 손님이 된다."], ["sake, are fain to admit him; and when once admitted it is not easy to shake him off.", "남편들은 아내들을 위해 마지못해 그를 들이는데, 한번 들이고 나면 떼어 내기가 쉽지 않다."], ["He has, however, a pawing, greasy way with him, which does not endear him to those who do not value him for their souls'", "그러나 그에게는 앞발로 더듬어 대듯 하는 기름진 구석이 있어서, 제 영혼 때문에 그를 귀히 여기는 처지가 아닌 사람들에게는 도무지 정이 붙지 않는다."], ["sake, and he is not a man to make himself at once popular in a large circle such as is now likely to surround him at Barchester.", "그러니 지금 바체스터에서 그를 둘러싸게 될 그런 넓은 사교 범위에서 대뜸 인기를 얻을 만한 위인은 못 된다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. A Morning Visit", "# 제5장. 아침 방문"]], [["It was known that Dr.", "프라우디 박사가 앞서 언급한 의회 법령에 따라 요양원 워든 자리에 곧바로 다시 사람을 임명해야 하리라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었다."], ["Proudie would immediately have to reappoint to the wardenship of the hospital under the act of Parliament to which allusion has been made; no one imagined that any choice was left to him--no one for a moment thought that he could appoint any other than Mr. Harding.", "그에게 무슨 선택의 여지가 남아 있으리라고 상상한 사람은 아무도 없었고, 하딩 씨 아닌 다른 누군가를 그가 임명할 수 있으리라고 잠시라도 생각한 사람은 없었다."], ["Mr. Harding himself, when he heard how the matter had been settled, without troubling himself much on the subject, considered it as certain that he would go back to his pleasant house and garden.", "하딩 씨 자신도 일이 그렇게 정리되었다는 말을 들었을 때, 그 문제로 그리 마음을 쓰지도 않은 채, 자기가 그 정겨운 집과 정원으로 돌아가게 되는 것을 당연한 일로 여겼다."], ["And though there would be much that was melancholy, nay, almost heartrending, in such a return, he still was glad that it was to be so.", "그리고 그런 귀환에는 우울한, 아니 거의 가슴을 에는 구석이 적지 않을 터였지만, 그래도 그는 일이 그렇게 되는 것이 기뻤다."], ["His daughter might probably be persuaded to return there with him.", "딸도 아마 설득하면 함께 그리로 돌아갈 것이었다."], ["She had, indeed, all but promised to do so, though she still entertained an idea that that greatest of mortals, that important atom of humanity, that little god upon earth, Johnny Bold her baby, ought to have a house of his own over his head.", "실제로 딸은 그러겠다고 약속하다시피 했다. 다만 그 죽어야 할 인간들 가운데 가장 위대한 자, 그 중대한 인류의 한 조각, 그 지상의 작은 신, 곧 제 아기 조니 볼드가 제 머리 위에 제 집을 이고 있어야 마땅하다는 생각을 아직 품고 있기는 했다."]], [["Such being the state of Mr. Harding's mind in the matter, he did not feel any peculiar personal interest in the appointment of Dr.", "이 문제에 관한 하딩 씨의 마음이 그러하였으므로, 그는 프라우디 박사가 주교로 임명된 데 대해 개인적으로 각별한 관심을 느끼지 않았다."], ["Proudie to the bishopric. He, as well as others at Barchester, regretted that a man should be sent among them who, they were aware, was not of their way of thinking; but Mr.", "그도 바체스터의 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로, 자기들과 생각이 다른 줄 뻔히 아는 사람이 자기들 가운데로 보내진 것을 유감스러워하기는 했다."], ["Harding himself was not a bigoted man on points of church doctrine, and he was quite prepared to welcome Dr. Proudie to Barchester in a graceful and becoming manner.", "그러나 하딩 씨 자신은 교회 교리의 세목을 두고 완고하게 구는 사람이 아니었고, 프라우디 박사를 품위 있고 온당한 태도로 바체스터에 맞아들일 마음이 충분히 되어 있었다."], ["He had nothing to seek and nothing to fear; he felt that it behoved him to be on good terms with his bishop, and he did not anticipate any obstacle that would prevent it.", "그는 구할 것도 없고 두려워할 것도 없었다. 자기 주교와 좋은 사이로 지내는 것이 도리라고 느꼈으며, 그것을 가로막을 장애가 있으리라고는 예상하지 않았다."]], [["In such a frame of mind he proceeded to pay his respects at the palace the second day after the arrival of the bishop and his chaplain.", "그런 마음가짐으로 그는 주교와 그 전속 사제가 도착한 이튿날 주교관으로 인사를 드리러 갔다."], ["But he did not go alone. Dr.", "그러나 혼자 가지는 않았다."], ["Grantly proposed to accompany him, and Mr. Harding was not sorry to have a companion, who would remove from his shoulders the burden of the conversation in such an interview.", "그랜틀리 박사가 동행하겠다고 나섰고, 하딩 씨로서도 그런 면담에서 대화의 짐을 제 어깨에서 덜어 줄 동반자가 있는 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["In the affair of the consecration Dr. Grantly had been introduced to the bishop, and Mr.", "취임식 자리에서 그랜틀리 박사는 주교에게 소개된 바 있었고, 하딩 씨도 그 자리에 있기는 했다."], ["Harding had also been there. He had, however, kept himself in the background, and he was now to be presented to the great man for the first time.", "그러나 그는 뒤로 물러나 있었으므로, 이제 처음으로 그 높으신 분에게 소개될 참이었다."]], [["The archdeacon's feelings were of a much stronger nature.", "부주교의 감정은 그보다 훨씬 격한 것이었다."], ["He was not exactly the man to overlook his own slighted claims, or to forgive the preference shown to another.", "그는 무시당한 제 몫의 권리를 못 본 체하거나 남에게 돌아간 총애를 용서할 만한 인물이 결코 아니었다."], ["Dr. Proudie was playing Venus to his Juno, and he was prepared to wage an internecine war against the owner of the wished-for apple, and all his satellites, private chaplains, and others.", "프라우디 박사는 그의 유노에 맞선 베누스 노릇을 하고 있는 셈이었고, 그는 그 탐나는 사과를 차지한 자와 그 모든 위성들, 곧 전속 사제며 그 밖의 것들을 상대로 끝장을 볼 때까지 싸울 각오가 되어 있었다."]], [["Nevertheless, it behoved him also to conduct himself towards the intruder as an old archdeacon should conduct himself to an incoming bishop; and though he was well aware of all Dr.", "그렇더라도 그 침입자를 대하는 태도만큼은 오래 자리를 지킨 부주교가 새로 부임하는 주교를 대하듯 해야 마땅했다."], ["Proudie's abominable opinions as regarded dissenters, church reform, the hebdomadal council, and such like; though he disliked the man, and hated the doctrines, still he was prepared to show respect to the station of the bishop.", "비국교도며 교회 개혁이며 대학 주간 평의회며 그런 따위 일들에 관한 프라우디 박사의 가증스러운 견해를 낱낱이 알고 있었음에도, 그 사람이 싫고 그 교리가 미웠음에도, 그는 주교라는 자리에는 경의를 표할 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["So he and Mr. Harding called together at the palace.", "그리하여 그와 하딩 씨는 함께 주교관을 찾았다."]], [["His lordship was at home, and the two visitors were shown through the accustomed hall into the well-known room where the good old bishop used to sit.", "주교님은 댁에 계셨고, 두 방문객은 익숙한 현관을 지나 선량한 노주교가 늘 앉아 있던 낯익은 방으로 안내되었다."], ["The furniture had been bought at a valuation, and every chair and table, every bookshelf against the wall, and every square in the carpet was as well known to each of them as their own bedrooms.", "가구는 평가액대로 사들인 것이어서, 의자 하나 탁자 하나, 벽에 붙은 책장 하나하나, 양탄자의 무늬 한 칸 한 칸이 두 사람에게는 제 침실만큼이나 눈에 익었다."], ["Nevertheless they at once felt that they were strangers there.", "그런데도 그들은 그 자리에서 대뜸 자기들이 그곳의 이방인임을 느꼈다."], ["The furniture was for the most part the same, yet the place had been metamorphosed.", "가구는 대부분 그대로였으나 방은 딴것으로 탈바꿈해 있었다."], ["A new sofa had been introduced, a horrid chintz affair, most unprelatical and almost irreligious; such a sofa as never yet stood in the study of any decent High Church clergyman of the Church of England.", "새 소파가 하나 들어와 있었는데, 끔찍한 사라사 천을 씌운 물건으로, 주교의 방에 어울리지 않기가 이를 데 없고 거의 불경스럽기까지 했다. 잉글랜드 국교회의 점잖은 고교회파 성직자의 서재에는 여태 한 번도 놓여 본 적이 없을 그런 소파였다."], ["The old curtains had also given way.", "옛 커튼도 물러났다."], ["They had, to be sure, become dingy, and that which had been originally a rich and goodly ruby had degenerated into a reddish brown. Mr.", "하기야 그것은 빛이 바래 있었고, 본디 짙고 고왔던 루비색이 불그죽죽한 갈색으로 퇴색해 있었다."], ["Harding, however, thought the old reddish-brown much preferable to the gaudy buff-coloured trumpery moreen which Mrs. Proudie had deemed good enough for her husband's own room in the provincial city of Barchester.", "그러나 하딩 씨는, 프라우디 부인이 지방 도시 바체스터에서 남편의 방에 쓰기에는 그만하면 되겠다고 여긴 그 야한 담황색 싸구려 모린 천보다는 옛 불그죽죽한 갈색이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했다."]], [["Our friends found Dr. Proudie sitting on the old bishop's chair, looking very nice in his new apron; they found, too, Mr.", "우리의 벗들은 프라우디 박사가 노주교의 의자에 앉아 새 앞치마 차림으로 아주 말쑥한 모습을 하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Slope standing on the hearth-rug, persuasive and eager, just as the archdeacon used to stand; but on the sofa they also found Mrs.", "또 슬로프 씨가 예전에 부주교가 늘 서 있던 바로 그 자리, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 그럴듯한 낯빛으로 열을 올리고 있는 것도 보았다."], ["Proudie, an innovation for which a precedent might in vain be sought in all the annals of the Barchester bishopric!", "그런데 소파 위에는 프라우디 부인까지 있었으니, 바체스터 주교구의 온 연대기를 뒤져도 그 전례를 찾을 수 없을 파격이었다."]], [["There she was, however, and they could only make the best of her.", "그러나 부인은 거기 있었고, 그들로서는 그 사실을 감수하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["The introductions were gone through in much form.", "소개는 격식을 갖추어 이루어졌다."], ["The archdeacon shook hands with the bishop, and named Mr. Harding, who received such an amount of greeting as was due from a bishop to a precentor.", "부주교가 주교와 악수하고 하딩 씨를 소개하니, 하딩 씨는 주교가 선창자에게 베풀 만한 만큼의 인사를 받았다."], ["His lordship then presented them to his lady wife; the archdeacon first, with archidiaconal honours, and then the precentor with diminished parade.", "이어 주교님이 두 사람을 부인에게 소개했는데, 부주교가 먼저 부주교다운 예우를 받고, 그다음에 선창자가 한결 줄어든 격식으로 소개되었다."], ["After this Mr. Slope presented himself.", "그 뒤에 슬로프 씨가 스스로 나섰다."], ["The bishop, it is true, did mention his name, and so did Mrs. Proudie too, in a louder tone, but Mr.", "주교가 그의 이름을 부르기는 했고 프라우디 부인도 더 큰 목소리로 그리하기는 했으나, 제 소개의 주된 짐은 슬로프 씨가 몸소 짊어졌다."], ["Slope took upon himself the chief burden of his own introduction.", "그는 그랜틀리 박사와 알고 지내게 되어 대단히 기쁘다고 했다."], ["He had great pleasure in making himself acquainted with Dr. Grantly; he had heard much of the archdeacon's good works in that part of the diocese in which his duties as archdeacon had been exercised (thus purposely ignoring the archdeacon's hitherto unlimited dominion over the diocese at large).", "부주교로서의 직무를 수행해 온 교구의 그 지역에서 부주교가 이룬 훌륭한 사업에 대해 많이 들었노라고 했다(이는 부주교가 여태 교구 전체에 걸쳐 휘둘러 온 무제한의 지배권을 일부러 못 본 체하는 말이었다)."], ["He was aware that his lordship depended greatly on the assistance which Dr. Grantly would be able to give him in that portion of his diocese.", "주교님이 교구의 그 지역에서 그랜틀리 박사가 줄 수 있을 도움에 크게 기대고 계신 줄 안다고도 했다."], ["He then thrust out his hand and, grasping that of his new foe, bedewed it unmercifully. Dr.", "그러고 나서 그는 손을 쑥 내밀어 새 원수의 손을 움켜쥐고는 사정없이 축축하게 적셔 놓았다."], ["Grantly in return bowed, looked stiff, contracted his eyebrows, and wiped his hand with his pocket-handkerchief.", "그랜틀리 박사는 답례로 고개를 숙이고, 뻣뻣한 얼굴로 눈썹을 찌푸린 다음, 주머니 손수건으로 손을 닦았다."], ["Nothing abashed, Mr. Slope then noticed the precentor and descended to the grade of the lower clergy.", "그래도 조금도 무안해하지 않고 슬로프 씨는 이제 선창자에게 눈길을 주며 하급 성직자의 등급으로 내려왔다."], ["He gave him a squeeze of the hand, damp indeed, but affectionate, and was very glad to make the acquaintance of Mr.--oh yes, Mr.", "그는 하딩 씨의 손을 한 번 쥐어 주었는데, 축축하기는 하되 다정한 악수였다. 그리고 이렇게 아무개 씨와, 아 그렇지, 하딩 씨와 알게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 했다."], ["Harding; he had not exactly caught the name.", "이름을 미처 똑똑히 듣지 못했노라는 것이었다."], ["\"Precentor in the cathedral,\"", "“대성당의 선창자시지요.”"], ["surmised Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 짐작해 말했다."], ["Mr. Harding confessed that such was the humble sphere of his work.", "하딩 씨는 제 일의 보잘것없는 영역이 그러하다고 시인했다."], ["\"Some parish duty as well,\" suggested Mr.", "“교구 일도 좀 맡고 계시고요.”"], ["Slope. Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 넌지시 말했다."], ["Harding acknowledged the diminutive incumbency of St. Cuthbert's.", "하딩 씨는 세인트 커스버트의 자그마한 성직을 맡고 있음을 인정했다."], ["Mr. Slope then left him alone, having condescended sufficiently, and joined the conversation among the higher powers.", "그러자 슬로프 씨는 이만하면 충분히 몸을 낮추었다 싶었던지 그를 내버려 둔 채 높은 분들 사이의 대화에 끼어들었다."]], [["There were four persons there, each of whom considered himself the most important personage in the diocese--himself, indeed, or herself, as Mrs.", "그 자리에는 네 사람이 있었고, 저마다 자기가 교구에서 가장 중요한 인물이라고 여기고 있었다."], ["Proudie was one of them--and with such a difference of opinion it was not probable that they would get on pleasantly together.", "그중 하나가 프라우디 부인이었으니 그 자신이라 해야 할지 그 부인 자신이라 해야 할지 모르겠으나 어쨌든 그러했다."], ["The bishop himself actually wore the visible apron, and trusted mainly to that--to that and his title, both being facts which could not be overlooked.", "의견이 그렇게 갈리는 터에 넷이 사이좋게 지낼 성싶지는 않았다. 주교는 실제로 눈에 보이는 앞치마를 두르고 있었고 주로 그것에 기댔다."], ["The archdeacon knew his subject and really understood the business of bishoping, which the others did not, and this was his strong ground.", "그것과 제 직함에 기댔는데, 둘 다 못 본 체할 수 없는 사실이었다."], ["Mrs. Proudie had her sex to back her, and her habit of command, and was nothing daunted by the high tone of Dr.", "부주교는 사안을 알고 있었고 주교 노릇이라는 일이 무엇인지 정말로 이해하고 있었으니, 나머지 사람들은 그렇지 못했다."], ["Grantly's face and figure. Mr.", "이것이 그의 튼튼한 발판이었다."], ["Slope had only himself and his own courage and tact to depend on, but he nevertheless was perfectly self-assured, and did not doubt but that he should soon get the better of weak men who trusted so much to externals, as both bishop and archdeacon appeared to do.", "프라우디 부인에게는 제 성별이 뒤를 받쳐 주었고 명령하는 습성이 있었으며, 그랜틀리 박사의 위압적인 얼굴과 풍채에도 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다. 슬로프 씨는 오직 제 자신과 제 배짱과 제 수완에 기댈 뿐이었으나, 그럼에도 완전히 자신만만했고, 주교와 부주교가 다 그러해 보이듯 겉치레에 그토록 기대는 약한 자들쯤은 머지않아 자기가 눌러 이기리라는 것을 의심치 않았다."]], [["\"Do you reside in Barchester, Dr. Grantly?\"", "“그랜틀리 박사, 바체스터에 사시오?”"], ["asked the lady with her sweetest smile.", "부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 띠고 물었다."]], [["Dr. Grantly explained that he lived in his own parish of Plumstead Episcopi, a few miles out of the city.", "그랜틀리 박사는 자기가 도시에서 몇 마일 떨어진 제 교구 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 산다고 설명했다."], ["Whereupon the lady hoped that the distance was not too great for country visiting, as she would be so glad to make the acquaintance of Mrs. Grantly.", "그러자 부인은 시골 방문을 다니기에 너무 먼 거리가 아니었으면 좋겠다고 했다."], ["She would take the earliest opportunity, after the arrival of her horses at Barchester; their horses were at present in London; their horses were not immediately coming down, as the bishop would be obliged, in a few days, to return to town.", "그랜틀리 부인과 알고 지내게 되면 무척 기쁘겠다는 것이었다. 말들이 바체스터에 도착하는 대로 되도록 이른 기회에 찾아뵙겠노라 했다."], ["Dr. Grantly was no doubt aware that the bishop was at present much called upon by the \"University Improvement Committee:\"", "자기들 말은 지금 런던에 있는데, 주교가 며칠 뒤 다시 상경해야 할 터이므로 말들이 당장 내려오지는 않는다고 했다."], ["indeed, the committee could not well proceed without him, as their final report had now to be drawn up.", "주교가 요즈음 「대학 개선 위원회」 일로 부름을 많이 받고 있는 것은 그랜틀리 박사도 필시 알고 있으리라고 했다."], ["The bishop had also to prepare a scheme for the \"Manufacturing Towns Morning and Evening Sunday School Society,\"", "실로 최종 보고서를 이제 작성해야 하는 마당이라 위원회가 그 없이는 제대로 굴러갈 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["of which he was a patron, or president, or director, and therefore the horses would not come down to Barchester at present; but whenever the horses did come down, she would take the earliest opportunity of calling at Plumstead Episcopi, providing the distance was not too great for country visiting.", "게다가 주교는 「공장 도시 조석 주일학교 협회」의 계획안도 마련해야 하는데, 그가 그 후원자인지 회장인지 이사인지를 맡고 있다고 했다. 그러니 말들이 지금은 바체스터로 내려오지 않을 테지만, 언제고 말들이 내려오기만 하면 되도록 이른 기회에 플럼스테드 에피스코피로 찾아가겠다고, 시골 방문을 다니기에 너무 먼 거리만 아니라면 그러겠다고 했다."]], [["The archdeacon made his fifth bow--he had made one at each mention of the horses--and promised that Mrs.", "부주교는 다섯 번째로 절을 했다. 말 이야기가 나올 때마다 한 번씩 절을 했던 것이다."], ["Grantly would do herself the honour of calling at the palace on an early day. Mrs.", "그러고는 그랜틀리 부인이 조만간 주교관으로 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리도록 하겠다고 약속했다."], ["Proudie declared that she would be delighted: she hadn't liked to ask, not being quite sure whether Mrs.", "프라우디 부인은 그러면 더없이 기쁘겠노라고 했다."], ["Grantly had horses; besides, the distance might have been, &c. &c.", "그랜틀리 부인에게 말이 있는지 확실히 알지 못해 차마 청하지 못했노라고, 게다가 거리가 어쩌면 운운하는 것이었다."]], [["Dr. Grantly again bowed but said nothing.", "그랜틀리 박사는 다시 고개를 숙였으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He could have bought every individual possession of the whole family of the Proudies and have restored them as a gift, without much feeling the loss; and had kept a separate pair of horses for the exclusive use of his wife since the day of his marriage, whereas Mrs.", "그는 프라우디 집안 전체의 재산을 하나하나 죄다 사들여 그것을 선물로 돌려주고도 손실을 별로 느끼지 않을 만한 사람이었고, 혼인한 날부터 오로지 아내만 쓰도록 따로 말 두 필을 두고 있었다."], ["Proudie had been hitherto jobbed about the streets of London at so much a month, during the season, and at other times had managed to walk, or hire a smart fly from the livery stables.", "반면 프라우디 부인은 여태껏 사교철에는 다달이 얼마씩을 치르고 빌린 말로 런던 거리를 돌아다녔고, 그 밖의 철에는 걷거나 삯마차집에서 말쑥한 이륜마차를 세내어 그럭저럭 다녔다."]], [["\"Are the arrangements with reference to the Sabbath-day schools generally pretty good in your archdeaconry?\"", "“부주교 관구의 안식일 학교 운영은 대체로 꽤 잘되고 있나요?”"], ["asked Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"Sabbath-day schools!\"", "“안식일 학교라!”"], ["repeated the archdeacon with an affectation of surprise.", "부주교가 놀란 척하며 되뇌었다. “글쎄올시다, 나로서는 알 수가 없소."], ["\"Upon my word, I can't tell; it depends mainly on the parson's wife and daughters.", "그건 주로 교구 목사의 아내와 딸들에게 달린 일이지요."], ["There is none at Plumstead.\"", "플럼스테드에는 없소.”"]], [["This was almost a fib on the part of the archdeacon, for Mrs. Grantly has a very nice school.", "이는 부주교로서는 거의 거짓말에 가까운 말이었으니, 그랜틀리 부인은 아주 훌륭한 학교를 하나 두고 있기 때문이다."], ["To be sure it is not a Sunday-school exclusively, and is not so designated, but that exemplary lady always attends there for an hour before church, and hears the children say their catechism, and sees that they are clean and tidy for church, with their hands washed and their shoes tied; and Grisel and Florinda, her daughters, carry thither a basket of large buns, baked on the Saturday afternoon, and distribute them to all the children not especially under disgrace, which buns are carried home after church with considerable content, and eaten hot at tea, being then split and toasted.", "하기야 그것이 오로지 주일학교인 것은 아니고 그렇게 불리지도 않지만, 그 모범적인 부인은 예배 전 한 시간씩 늘 그리로 나가 아이들이 교리문답 외는 것을 듣고, 아이들이 손을 씻고 신발 끈을 매어 예배에 갈 만큼 깨끗하고 단정한지를 살핀다. 그리고 두 딸 그리젤과 플로린다가 토요일 오후에 구워 낸 큼직한 빵을 바구니에 담아 그리로 나르고, 특별히 벌을 받고 있는 아이가 아니면 모두에게 나누어 준다."], ["The children of Plumstead would indeed open their eyes if they heard their venerated pastor declare that there was no Sunday-school in his parish.", "그 빵은 예배가 끝난 뒤 흡족한 마음으로 집에 실려 가서, 반으로 갈라 구운 다음 차 시간에 따뜻하게 먹힌다. 플럼스테드의 아이들은 제 존경하는 목사가 제 교구에는 주일학교가 없다고 말하는 것을 들었다면 정말이지 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Slope merely opened his wide eyes wider and slightly shrugged his shoulders.", "슬로프 씨는 그저 큼직한 두 눈을 더 크게 뜨고 어깨를 조금 으쓱했을 뿐이었다."], ["He was not, however, prepared to give up his darling project.", "그러나 그는 제가 애지중지하는 계획을 포기할 마음은 없었다."]], [["\"I fear there is a great deal of Sabbath travelling here,\"", "“이곳에는 안식일에 다니는 여행이 아주 많은 모양이더군요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"On looking at the 'Bradshaw,' I see that there are three trains in and three out every Sabbath.", "“『브래드쇼』를 보니 안식일마다 들어오는 기차가 세 편, 나가는 기차가 세 편 있습니다."], ["Could nothing be done to induce the company to withdraw them?", "회사가 그것을 거두어들이도록 할 방도가 없을까요?"], ["Don't you think, Dr. Grantly, that a little energy might diminish the evil?\"", "그랜틀리 박사님, 조금만 힘을 쓰면 그 악을 줄일 수 있으리라고 보지 않으십니까?”"]], [["\"Not being a director, I really can't say.", "“이사가 아니어서 나로서는 정말 알 수가 없소."], ["But if you can withdraw the passengers, the company I dare say will withdraw the trains,\"", "다만 당신이 승객을 거두어들일 수 있다면 회사도 필시 기차를 거두어들일 것이오.”"], ["said the doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["\"It's merely a question of dividends.\"", "“그저 배당금의 문제일 뿐이니까.”"]], [["\"But surely, Dr. Grantly,\"", "“하지만 그랜틀리 박사,”"], ["said the lady; \"surely we should look at it differently.", "부인이 말했다. “정말이지 우리는 그것을 달리 보아야 하지 않겠소."], ["You and I, for instance, in our position: surely we should do all that we can to control so grievous a sin.", "이를테면 박사와 나 같은 처지에 있는 사람은 말이오. 그토록 통탄할 죄를 억누르기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 해야 하지 않겠소."], ["Don't you think so, Mr. Harding?\"", "하딩 씨는 그렇게 생각지 않으시오?”"], ["and she turned to the precentor, who was sitting mute and unhappy.", "그러면서 부인은 말없이 딱한 낯으로 앉아 있는 선창자 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Mr. Harding thought that all porters and stokers, guards, brakesmen, and pointsmen ought to have an opportunity of going to church, and he hoped that they all had.", "하딩 씨는 짐꾼이며 화부며 차장이며 제동수며 전철수며 모두가 예배에 갈 기회를 가져야 마땅하다고 생각한다고, 그리고 그들 모두가 그 기회를 누리고 있기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["\"But surely, surely,\" continued Mrs.", "“하지만 정말이지, 정말이지,”"], ["Proudie, \"surely that is not enough.", "프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다. “그것만으로는 부족하지 않소."], ["Surely that will not secure such an observance of the Sabbath as we are taught to conceive is not only expedient but indispensable; surely--\"", "그것으로는 안식일을 지키는 일이, 그저 마땅할 뿐 아니라 없어서는 안 될 것이라고 우리가 배운 그 준수가 확보되지 않을 것이오. 정말이지—”"]], [["Come what come might, Dr. Grantly was not to be forced into a dissertation on a point of doctrine with Mrs. Proudie, nor yet with Mr. Slope, so without much ceremony he turned his back upon the sofa and began to hope that Dr. Proudie had found that the palace repairs had been such as to meet his wishes.", "무슨 일이 있어도 그랜틀리 박사는 프라우디 부인과, 또한 슬로프 씨와 교리의 한 조목을 두고 논설을 벌이도록 끌려 들어갈 사람이 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 별다른 예의도 차리지 않은 채 소파 쪽으로 등을 돌리고는, 프라우디 박사께서 주교관 수리가 뜻에 맞게 되었다고 여기시기를 바란다는 말을 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["\"Yes, yes,\" said his lordship; upon the whole he thought so--upon the whole, he didn't know that there was much ground for complaint; the architect, perhaps, might have--but his double, Mr.", "“그렇소, 그렇소.”"], ["Slope, who had sidled over to the bishop's chair, would not allow his lordship to finish his ambiguous speech.", "주교님이 말했다. 대체로 그렇게 여긴다고, 대체로 불평할 만한 거리가 그리 있는 줄 모르겠다고, 다만 건축가가 어쩌면— 그러나 그의 분신인 슬로프 씨가 어느새 주교의 의자 곁으로 슬금슬금 다가와서는, 주교님이 그 애매한 말을 끝맺도록 놓아두지 않았다."]], [["\"There is one point I would like to mention, Mr. Archdeacon.", "“부주교님, 한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 점이 있습니다."], ["His lordship asked me to step through the premises, and I see that the stalls in the second stable are not perfect.\"", "주교님이 저더러 부지를 한 바퀴 둘러보라 하시기에 살펴보았더니, 두 번째 마구간의 칸이 온전하지 않더군요.”"]], [["\"Why--there's standing there for a dozen horses,\"", "“아니—거기 말 열두 필은 세워 둘 자리가 있지 않소.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps so,\"", "“그럴지도 모르지요.”"], ["said the other; \"indeed, I've no doubt of it; but visitors, you know, often require so much accommodation.", "상대가 말했다. “실은 저도 그 점은 의심치 않습니다."], ["There are so many of the bishop's relatives who always bring their own horses.\"", "그렇지만 아시다시피 손님들은 흔히 자리를 아주 많이 차지하는 법이지요. 주교님 친척들 중에는 늘 제 말을 끌고 오는 분이 아주 많거든요.”"]], [["Dr. Grantly promised that due provision for the relatives'", "그랜틀리 박사는 적어도 본디 마구간 건물의 규모가 허락하는 한도 안에서는 친척들의 말을 위해 마땅한 자리를 마련하겠다고 약속했다."], ["horses should be made, as far at least as the extent of the original stable building would allow. He would himself communicate with the architect.", "자기가 직접 건축가와 이야기하겠다는 것이었다."]], [["\"And the coach-house, Dr.", "“그리고 마차 차고 말인데요, 그랜틀리 박사님.”"], ["Grantly,\" continued Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Slope; \"there is really hardly room for a second carriage in the large coach-house, and the smaller one, of course, holds only one.\"", "“큰 차고에는 두 번째 마차를 들일 자리가 정말이지 거의 없고, 작은 쪽은 물론 한 대밖에 못 들어갑니다.”"]], [["\"And the gas,\"", "“그리고 가스도요.”"], ["chimed in the lady; \"there is no gas through the house, none whatever, but in the kitchen and passages.", "부인이 끼어들었다. “집 안에 가스가 들어오지 않소."], ["Surely the palace should have been fitted through with pipes for gas, and hot water too.", "부엌과 복도 말고는 아무 데도 없단 말이오. 정말이지 주교관이라면 가스관을, 온수관도 함께 집 전체에 놓았어야 하지 않겠소."], ["There is no hot water laid on anywhere above the ground-floor; surely there should be the means of getting hot water in the bedrooms without having it brought in jugs from the kitchen.\"", "1층 위로는 어디에도 더운물이 나오지 않소. 부엌에서 주전자에 담아 나르지 않고도 침실에서 더운물을 얻을 방도가 정말이지 있어야 하지 않겠소.”"]], [["The bishop had a decided opinion that there should be pipes for hot water.", "주교는 온수관이 있어야 한다는 데 확고한 견해를 지니고 있었다."], ["Hot water was very essential for the comfort of the palace.", "더운물은 주교관의 안락에 매우 긴요한 것이었다."], ["It was, indeed, a requisite in any decent gentleman's house.", "실로 그것은 웬만한 신사의 집이라면 갖추어야 할 필수품이었다."]], [["Mr. Slope had remarked that the coping on the garden wall was in many places imperfect.", "슬로프 씨는 정원 담의 갓돌이 여러 군데 성치 않더라고 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie had discovered a large hole, evidently the work of rats, in the servants' hall.", "프라우디 부인은 하인들 방에서 큼직한 구멍을 하나 발견했는데, 보아하니 쥐가 낸 것이었다."]], [["The bishop expressed an utter detestation of rats.", "주교는 쥐라면 몸서리가 쳐진다고 밝혔다."], ["There was nothing, he believed, in this world that he so much hated as a rat.", "이 세상에 쥐만큼 자기가 싫어하는 것은 없는 줄로 안다는 것이었다."]], [["Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 또한 헛간들의 자물쇠가 매우 부실하다는 것도 보아 두었다."], ["Slope had, moreover, observed that the locks of the outhouses were very imperfect: he might specify the coal-cellar and the woodhouse.", "석탄광과 장작광을 예로 들 수 있다고 했다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie had also seen that those on the doors of the servants'", "프라우디 부인도 하인들 침실 문에 달린 자물쇠가 그에 못지않게 나쁜 상태임을 보아 두었다."], ["bedrooms were in an-equally bad condition; indeed, the locks all through the house were old-fashioned and unserviceable.", "실로 온 집 안의 자물쇠가 죄다 구식이고 쓸 만하지 않았다."]], [["The bishop thought that a great deal depended on a good lock and quite as much on the key.", "주교는 좋은 자물쇠에 달린 바가 크고 열쇠에 달린 바도 그에 못지않게 크다고 생각했다."], ["He had observed that the fault very often lay with the key, especially if the wards were in any way twisted.", "특히 열쇠 홈이 조금이라도 뒤틀려 있으면 흔히 열쇠 쪽에 탈이 있더라는 것을 그는 눈여겨보아 두었다."]], [["Mr. Slope was going on with his catalogue of grievances, when he was somewhat loudly interrupted by the archdeacon, who succeeded in explaining that the diocesan architect, or rather his foreman, was the person to be addressed on such subjects, and that he, Dr.", "슬로프 씨가 불평 목록을 계속 읊어 나가려는데, 부주교가 다소 큰 소리로 그의 말을 끊었다."], ["Grantly, had inquired as to the comfort of the palace merely as a point of compliment.", "부주교는 그런 일은 교구 건축가에게, 아니 그보다는 그 밑의 십장에게 말해야 할 사안이며, 자기 그랜틀리 박사가 주교관이 편안한지를 물은 것은 그저 예의를 차린 것일 뿐이라는 뜻을 그럭저럭 알아듣게 설명해 냈다."], ["He was sorry, however, that so many things had been found amiss: and then he rose from his chair to escape.", "그러나 잘못된 데가 그토록 많이 드러났다니 안됐다고 말한 다음, 그는 빠져나가려고 의자에서 일어났다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie, though she had contrived to lend her assistance in recapitulating the palatial dilapidations, had not on that account given up her hold of Mr. Harding, nor ceased from her cross-examinations as to the iniquity of Sabbatical amusements. Over and over again had she thrown out her \"Surely, surely,\" at Mr. Harding's devoted head, and ill had that gentleman been able to parry the attack.", "프라우디 부인은 주교관의 허물어진 데를 하나하나 헤아리는 일에 용케 거들면서도, 그렇다고 하딩 씨를 붙들고 있던 손을 놓지도 않았고 안식일의 오락이 얼마나 죄스러운가를 두고 그를 심문하는 일도 그치지 않았다. 부인은 하딩 씨의 그 헌신적인 머리 위로 “정말이지, 정말이지”를 몇 번이고 되풀이해 던졌고, 그 신사는 그 공격을 좀처럼 막아 내지 못했다."]], [["He had never before found himself subjected to such a nuisance.", "그는 여태껏 이런 성가심을 당해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Ladies hitherto, when they had consulted him on religious subjects, had listened to what he might choose to say with some deference, and had differed, if they differed, in silence.", "지금까지 부인네들은 종교 문제로 그에게 의견을 구할 때면 그가 말하고자 하는 바를 얼마간 공손히 들었고, 생각이 다르더라도 잠자코 다를 뿐이었다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie interrogated him and then lectured.", "그런데 프라우디 부인은 그를 심문하고 나서 훈계를 했다."], ["\"Neither thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, thy manservant, nor thy maidservant,\"", "“너나 네 아들이나 네 딸이나 네 남종이나 네 여종이나.”"], ["said she impressively, and more than once, as though Mr. Harding had forgotten the words.", "부인은 하딩 씨가 그 말씀을 잊기라도 한 듯이, 힘주어, 그것도 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 이렇게 외웠다."], ["She shook her finger at him as she quoted the favourite law, as though menacing him with punishment, and then called upon him categorically to state whether he did not think that travelling on the Sabbath was an abomination and a desecration.", "부인은 제가 아끼는 그 계명을 인용하면서, 벌을 내리겠다고 으르기라도 하듯 그를 향해 손가락을 흔들었다. 그러고는 안식일에 여행하는 것이 가증한 일이요 신성을 더럽히는 일이라고 생각지 않느냐고 딱 잘라 대답하라며 그를 몰아세웠다."]], [["Mr. Harding had never been so hard pressed in his life.", "하딩 씨는 평생 이토록 궁지에 몰려 본 적이 없었다."], ["He felt that he ought to rebuke the lady for presuming so to talk to a gentleman and a clergyman many years her senior, but he recoiled from the idea of scolding the bishop's wife, in the bishop's presence, on his first visit to the palace; moreover, to tell the truth, he was somewhat afraid of her.", "저보다 여러 해 연상인 신사이자 성직자에게 감히 그런 식으로 말하는 부인을 꾸짖어야 마땅하다고 느꼈으나, 주교의 부인을, 그것도 주교가 보는 앞에서, 주교관에 처음 찾아온 날에 나무란다는 생각에는 몸이 움츠러들었다. 게다가 사실대로 말하자면 그는 그 부인이 다소 무섭기도 했다."], ["She, seeing him sit silent and absorbed, by no means refrained from the attack.", "부인은 그가 말없이 넋을 놓고 앉아 있는 것을 보고도 공격을 조금도 늦추지 않았다."]], [["\"I hope, Mr. Harding,\"", "“하딩 씨,”"], ["said she, shaking her head slowly and solemnly, \"I hope you will not leave me to think that you approve of Sabbath travelling,\"", "부인이 천천히 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. “당신이 안식일 여행을 옳게 여긴다고 내가 생각하도록 내버려 두지는 않으시겠지요.”"], ["and she looked a look of unutterable meaning into his eyes.", "그러면서 부인은 이루 말할 수 없는 뜻을 담은 눈길로 그의 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["There was no standing this, for Mr.", "이쯤 되니 견딜 재간이 없었다."], ["Slope was now looking at him, and so was the bishop, and so was the archdeacon, who had completed his adieux on that side of the room.", "이제 슬로프 씨가 그를 보고 있었고, 주교도 그러했으며, 방 저쪽에서 작별 인사를 마친 부주교마저 그러했기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Harding therefore got up also and, putting out his hand to Mrs.", "그리하여 하딩 씨도 자리에서 일어나 프라우디 부인에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["Proudie, said: \"If you will come to St. Cuthbert's some Sunday, I will preach you a sermon on that subject.\"", "“언제 일요일에 세인트 커스버트로 오시면 제가 그 주제로 설교를 한 편 해 드리지요.”"]], [["And so the archdeacon and the precentor took their departure, bowing low to the lady, shaking hands with the lord, and escaping from Mr. Slope in the best manner each could. Mr. Harding was again maltreated, but Dr. Grantly swore deeply in the bottom of his heart, that no earthly consideration should ever again induce him to touch the paw of that impure and filthy animal.", "그리하여 부주교와 선창자는 부인에게 깊이 절하고 주교와 악수한 다음, 슬로프 씨한테서는 저마다 되도록 무난한 방법으로 빠져나오며 그 자리를 떠났다. 하딩 씨는 또 한 번 봉변을 당했으나, 그랜틀리 박사는 세상의 그 어떤 사정으로도 두 번 다시는 그 더럽고 불결한 짐승의 앞발을 만지지 않으리라고 마음속 깊은 곳에서 굳게 맹세했다."]], [["And now, had I the pen of a mighty poet, would I sing in epic verse the noble wrath of the archdeacon.", "그리고 이제, 내게 위대한 시인의 붓이 있다면 나는 부주교의 그 고귀한 분노를 서사시로 노래하리라."], ["The palace steps descend to a broad gravel sweep, from whence a small gate opens out into the street, very near the covered gateway leading into the close.", "주교관 계단은 널따란 자갈밭으로 내려가고, 거기서 작은 문 하나가 거리로 열리는데, 대성당 경내로 통하는 지붕 덮인 문간에서 아주 가깝다."], ["The road from the palace door turns to the left, through the spacious gardens, and terminates on the London road, half a mile from the cathedral.", "주교관 문에서 난 길은 왼쪽으로 꺾여 널찍한 정원을 지나, 대성당에서 반 마일 떨어진 런던 가도에서 끝난다."]], [["Till they had both passed this small gate and entered the close, neither of them spoke a word, but the precentor clearly saw from his companion's face that a tornado was to be expected, nor was he himself inclined to stop it.", "두 사람이 그 작은 문을 지나 경내에 들어설 때까지 어느 쪽도 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 선창자는 동행의 얼굴에서 회오리바람이 몰아치리라는 것을 똑똑히 보았고, 그 자신도 그것을 말리고 싶은 마음이 없었다."], ["Though by nature far less irritable than the archdeacon, even he was angry: he even--that mild and courteous man--was inclined to express himself in anything but courteous terms.", "천성이 부주교보다 훨씬 덜 성마른 사람이었건만 그마저도 화가 나 있었다. 그 온화하고 정중한 사람마저도 도무지 정중하달 수 없는 말로 제 심정을 털어놓고 싶어졌던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. War", "# 제6장. 전쟁"]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“맙소사!” 부주교는 대성당 경내의 자갈길에 발을 내디디며 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the archdeacon, as he placed his foot on the gravel walk of the close, and raising his hat with one hand, passed the other somewhat violently over his now grizzled locks; smoke issued forth from the uplifted beaver as it were a cloud of wrath, and the safety valve of his anger opened, and emitted a visible steam, preventing positive explosion and probable apoplexy.", "한 손으로는 모자를 들어 올리고 다른 손으로는 이제 희끗희끗해진 머리칼을 다소 거칠게 쓸어 넘겼다. 들어 올린 비버 모자에서는 마치 분노의 구름과도 같은 김이 피어올랐으니, 노여움의 안전판이 열려 눈에 보이는 증기를 뿜어낸 덕에 실제 폭발과 있음직한 졸중이 면해진 셈이었다."], ["\"Good heavens!\"--and the archdeacon looked up to the gray pinnacles of the cathedral tower, making a mute appeal to that still living witness which had looked down on the doings of so many bishops of Barchester.", "“맙소사!” 그러고서 부주교는 대성당 탑의 잿빛 첨두를 올려다보며, 바체스터의 그 숱한 주교들이 저지른 일을 내려다보아 온 그 말 없는 산 증인에게 소리 없이 호소했다."]], [["\"I don't think I shall ever like that Mr. Slope,\"", "“저 슬로프 씨라는 사람은 아무래도 끝내 좋아지지 않을 것 같네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Like him!\"", "“좋아진다니요!”"], ["roared the archdeacon, standing still for a moment to give more force to his voice; \"like him!\"", "부주교는 목소리에 힘을 더 싣기 위해 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 고함쳤다. “좋아진다니요!”"], ["All the ravens of the close cawed their assent.", "경내의 까마귀들이 모두 그렇다고 까악까악 화답했다."], ["The old bells of the tower, in chiming the hour, echoed the words, and the swallows flying out from their nests mutely expressed a similar opinion.", "탑의 낡은 종들도 시각을 알리며 그 말을 되울렸고, 둥지에서 날아 나온 제비들도 말없이 같은 의견을 표했다."], ["Like Mr. Slope!", "슬로프 씨를 좋아한다니!"], ["Why no, it was not very probable that any Barchester-bred living thing should like Mr. Slope!", "아니, 바체스터에서 나고 자란 살아 있는 것치고 슬로프 씨를 좋아할 만한 것은 도무지 있을 법하지 않았다."]], [["\"Nor Mrs. Proudie either,\"", "“프라우디 부인도 마찬가지고 말이야.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["The archdeacon hereupon forgot himself.", "이 대목에서 부주교는 그만 자제를 잃고 말았다."], ["I will not follow his example, nor shock my readers by transcribing the term in which he expressed his feeling as to the lady who had been named.", "나는 그의 본을 따르지 않을 것이며, 방금 이름이 나온 그 부인에 대해 그가 제 심경을 표현하며 쓴 말을 그대로 옮겨 적어 독자를 놀라게 하지도 않을 것이다."], ["The ravens and the last lingering notes of the clock bells were less scrupulous and repeated in correspondent echoes the very improper exclamation.", "까마귀들과 시계 종의 마지막 여운은 그만큼 조심스럽지 못하여, 그 심히 온당치 못한 감탄사를 화답하는 메아리로 되풀이했다."], ["The archdeacon again raised his hat, and another salutary escape of steam was effected.", "부주교는 다시 모자를 들어 올렸고, 또 한 번 이로운 증기 배출이 이루어졌다."]], [["There was a pause, during which the precentor tried to realize the fact that the wife of a Bishop of Barchester had been thus designated, in the close of the cathedral, by the lips of its own archdeacon; but he could not do it.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 선창자는 바체스터 주교의 부인이 다른 곳도 아닌 대성당 경내에서, 그것도 그 대성당 부주교의 입으로 그렇게 불렸다는 사실을 실감해 보려 애썼다. 그러나 아무리 해도 실감이 나지 않았다."]], [["\"The bishop seems to be a quiet man enough,\"", "“주교는 꽤 조용한 사람인 듯하던데.”"], ["suggested Mr. Harding, having acknowledged to himself his own failure.", "스스로 제 실패를 인정하고 나서 하딩 씨가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["\"Idiot!\"", "“멍청이지요!”"], ["exclaimed the doctor, who for the nonce was not capable of more than such spasmodic attempts at utterance.", "박사가 외쳤다. 그는 당장으로서는 그렇게 발작하듯 한마디씩 내뱉는 것 이상은 해낼 수 없는 상태였다."]], [["\"Well, he did not seem very bright,\" said Mr.", "“글쎄, 그리 명민해 보이지는 않더군.”"], ["Harding, \"and yet he has always had the reputation of a clever man.", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “그런데도 늘 영리한 사람이라는 평판을 들어 오지 않았나."], ["I suppose he's cautious and not inclined to express himself very freely.\"", "아마 조심스러운 성품이라 속을 그리 시원하게 드러내지 않는 것이겠지.”"]], [["The new Bishop of Barchester was already so contemptible a creature in Dr. Grantly's eyes that he could not condescend to discuss his character.", "바체스터의 새 주교는 그랜틀리 박사의 눈에 이미 너무나 하찮은 물건이어서, 그 인격을 논하는 일조차 그로서는 몸을 낮추는 짓이었다."], ["He was a puppet to be played by others; a mere wax doll, done up in an apron and a shovel hat, to be stuck on a throne or elsewhere, and pulled about by wires as others chose. Dr.", "그는 남들이 놀리는 꼭두각시요, 앞치마와 삽 모양 모자를 씌워 옥좌든 어디든 앉혀 놓고 남들이 마음대로 줄을 당겨 흔드는 밀랍 인형에 지나지 않았다."], ["Grantly did not choose to let himself down low enough to talk about Dr. Proudie, but he saw that he would have to talk about the other members of his household, the coadjutor bishops, who had brought his lordship down, as it were, in a box, and were about to handle the wires as they willed.", "그랜틀리 박사는 프라우디 박사를 입에 올릴 만큼 자신을 낮출 생각이 없었으나, 그 집안의 다른 식구들, 곧 주교 각하를 말하자면 상자에 담아 내려보내고 이제 제 뜻대로 그 줄을 놀리려 드는 저 보좌 주교들에 대해서는 이야기하지 않을 수 없으리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["This in itself was a terrible vexation to the archdeacon.", "이것만으로도 부주교에게는 지독한 곤혹이었다."], ["Could he have ignored the chaplain and have fought the bishop, there would have been, at any rate, nothing degrading in such a contest.", "전속 사제 따위는 무시하고 주교와 싸울 수만 있다면, 적어도 그런 싸움에는 품위를 잃을 일이 없었다. 여왕께서 누구를 바체스터 주교로 삼으시든 좋다."], ["Let the Queen make whom she would Bishop of Barchester; a man, or even an ape, when once a bishop, would be a respectable adversary, if he would but fight, himself. But what was such a person as Dr.", "사람이든 심지어 원숭이든, 일단 주교가 된 이상 제 스스로 싸우기만 한다면 존중할 만한 적수인 것이다."], ["Grantly to do when such another person as Mr. Slope was put forward as his antagonist?", "그러나 슬로프 씨 같은 자가 적수랍시고 앞에 내세워졌을 때, 그랜틀리 박사 같은 사람은 대체 어찌해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["If he, our archdeacon, refused the combat, Mr. Slope would walk triumphant over the field, and have the diocese of Barchester under his heel.", "만일 그가, 우리의 부주교가 그 싸움을 거부한다면, 슬로프 씨는 전장을 의기양양하게 활보하며 바체스터 교구를 제 발밑에 두게 될 것이었다."]], [["If, on the other hand, the archdeacon accepted as his enemy the man whom the new puppet bishop put before him as such, he would have to talk about Mr. Slope, and write about Mr. Slope, and in all matters treat with Mr. Slope, as a being standing, in some degree, on ground similar to his own. He would have to meet Mr. Slope, to--Bah! the idea was sickening. He could not bring himself to have to do with Mr. Slope.", "반대로 부주교가, 저 새 꼭두각시 주교가 자기 앞에 적으로 내세운 그 사람을 적으로 받아들인다면, 그는 슬로프 씨를 어느 정도 자기와 같은 지반에 선 존재로 여겨 슬로프 씨에 대해 말하고, 슬로프 씨에 대해 쓰고, 매사에 슬로프 씨와 상대해야 할 터였다. 슬로프 씨를 만나야 하고, 그리하여— 아이고! 생각만 해도 구역질이 났다. 그는 도저히 슬로프 씨와 상종할 마음을 낼 수 없었다."]], [["\"He is the most thoroughly bestial creature that ever I set my eyes upon,\"", "“내 평생 눈으로 본 것 중에 그렇게 철저히 짐승 같은 물건은 없었습니다.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Who--the bishop?\"", "“누구 말인가— 주교?”"], ["asked the other innocently.", "상대가 순진하게 물었다."]], [["\"Bishop!", "“주교라니요!"], ["no--I'm not talking about the bishop.", "아닙니다, 주교 얘기가 아니에요. 대체 어떻게 저런 물건이 성직을 받았는지!"], ["How on earth such a creature got ordained!--they'll ordain anybody now, I know, but he's been in the church these ten years, and they used to be a little careful ten years ago.\"", "요즘은 아무나 안수해 준다는 걸 저도 압니다만, 저자는 교회에 몸담은 지 10년이 됐어요. 10년 전만 해도 그래도 좀 조심하는 편이었단 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Oh! You mean Mr. Slope.\"", "“아, 그러니까 자네는 슬로프 씨를 두고 하는 말이로군.”"]], [["\"Did you ever see any animal less like a gentleman?\"", "“저자만큼 신사와 거리가 먼 짐승을 보신 적이 있으십니까?”"], ["asked Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 물었다."]], [["\"I can't say I felt myself much disposed to like him.\"", "“나도 그 사람이 딱히 마음에 든다는 기분은 들지 않더라고 해야겠네.”"]], [["\"Like him!\"", "“좋아진다니요!”"], ["again shouted the doctor, and the assenting ravens again cawed an echo; \"of course, you don't like him: it's not a question of liking.", "박사가 또 한 번 고함쳤고, 동의하는 까마귀들이 다시 메아리로 까악거렸다. “물론 좋아하실 리가 없지요."], ["But what are we to do with him?\"", "좋고 싫고의 문제가 아닙니다. 문제는 저자를 어떻게 하느냐는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Do with him?\"", "“어떻게 하다니?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes--what are we to do with him?", "“그렇습니다, 저자를 어떻게 하느냐는 말입니다."], ["How are we to treat him?", "저자를 어떻게 대해야 합니까?"], ["There he is, and there he'll stay.", "저자는 저기 있고, 저기 눌러앉을 겁니다."], ["He has put his foot in that palace, and he'll never take it out again till he's driven.", "저 주교관에 이미 발을 들여놓았으니 쫓겨나기 전에는 결코 발을 빼지 않을 거예요."], ["How are we to get rid of him?\"", "우리가 저자를 어떻게 떨쳐 냅니까?”"]], [["\"I don't suppose he can do us much harm.\"", "“저 사람이 우리에게 그리 큰 해를 끼칠 수야 있겠나.”"]], [["\"Not do harm!--Well, I think you'll find yourself of a different opinion before a month is gone.", "“해를 끼치지 못한다니요! 글쎄요, 한 달만 지나 보시면 생각이 달라지실 겁니다."], ["What would you say now, if he got himself put into the hospital?", "저자가 요양원 자리를 차지하고 앉는다면 뭐라 하시겠습니까?"], ["Would that be harm?\"", "그건 해가 아닙니까?”"]], [["Mr. Harding mused awhile and then said he didn't think the new bishop would put Mr. Slope into the hospital.", "하딩 씨는 한동안 생각에 잠겼다가, 새 주교가 슬로프 씨를 요양원에 앉히지는 않으리라고 본다고 말했다."]], [["\"If he doesn't put him there, he'll put him somewhere else where he'll be as bad.", "“거기 앉히지 않으면 다른 데, 그만큼이나 고약한 자리에 앉힐 겁니다."], ["I tell you that that man, to all intents and purposes, will be Bishop of Barchester!\"", "제 말씀을 들으십시오. 저자야말로 실질적으로 바체스터의 주교가 될 것입니다!”"], ["And again Dr. Grantly raised his hat and rubbed his hand thoughtfully and sadly over his head.", "그러고서 그랜틀리 박사는 다시 모자를 들어 올리고 생각에 잠긴 채 서글피 머리를 쓸었다."]], [["\"Impudent scoundrel!\"", "“뻔뻔한 악당 같으니!”"], ["he continued after a while.", "그는 잠시 뒤에 말을 이었다."], ["\"To dare to cross-examine me about the Sunday-schools in the diocese, and Sunday travelling too: I never in my life met his equal for sheer impudence.", "“감히 나를 붙들고 교구의 주일학교니 주일 여행이니 하는 것을 캐물어 대다니. 저렇게 순전히 뻔뻔하기만 한 인간은 내 평생 처음 봅니다."], ["Why, he must have thought we were two candidates for ordination!\"", "저자는 우리를 안수받으러 온 지원자 둘쯤으로 여겼던 게 분명해요!”"]], [["\"I declare I thought Mrs. Proudie was the worst of the two,\"", "“나는 정말이지 둘 중에서는 프라우디 부인 쪽이 더하다고 생각했네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"When a woman is impertinent, one must only put up with it, and keep out of her way in future, but I am not inclined to put up with Mr.", "“여자가 무례하게 굴면 그저 참고 앞으로 그 앞을 피하는 수밖에 없지요. 하지만 슬로프 씨를 참아 줄 생각은 없습니다."], ["Slope. 'Sabbath travelling!'\"", "‘안식일 여행!’”"], ["and the doctor attempted to imitate the peculiar drawl of the man he so much disliked: \"'Sabbath travelling!'", "그러고서 박사는 제가 그토록 싫어하는 그 사람 특유의 느릿한 말투를 흉내 내려 애썼다."], ["Those are the sort of men who will ruin the Church of England and make the profession of a clergyman disreputable.", "“‘안식일 여행!’ 저런 부류가 영국 교회를 망치고 성직자라는 직분을 우습게 만드는 자들입니다. 우리가 두려워할 것은 비국교도도 아니고 교황파도 아니에요."], ["It is not the dissenters or the papists that we should fear, but the set of canting, low-bred hypocrites who are wriggling their way in among us; men who have no fixed principle, no standard ideas of religion or doctrine, but who take up some popular cry, as this fellow has done about 'Sabbath travelling.'\"", "우리 사이로 꿈틀꿈틀 기어들어 오는, 경건한 척 지껄이는 저 천박한 위선자 무리입니다. 정해진 원칙도 없고 신앙이나 교리에 대한 기준도 없이, 저 작자가 ‘안식일 여행’을 들고나온 것처럼 그때그때 사람들 입에 오르내리는 구호나 주워 드는 자들 말입니다.”"]], [["Dr. Grantly did not again repeat the question aloud, but he did so constantly to himself: What were they to do with Mr.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그 물음을 다시 소리 내어 되풀이하지는 않았으나, 속으로는 끊임없이 되뇌고 있었다."], ["Slope?", "슬로프 씨를 어떻게 해야 하는가?"], ["How was he openly, before the world, to show that he utterly disapproved of and abhorred such a man?", "어떻게 하면 세상 앞에서 드러내 놓고, 자기가 그런 인간을 철저히 못마땅해하며 혐오한다는 것을 보일 수 있는가?"]], [["Hitherto Barchester had escaped the taint of any extreme rigour of church doctrine.", "지금까지 바체스터는 교리상의 극단적 엄격주의에 물들지 않고 지내 왔다."], ["The clergymen of the city and neighbourhood, though very well inclined to promote High Church principles, privileges, and prerogatives, had never committed themselves to tendencies which are somewhat too loosely called Puseyite practices.", "시내와 인근의 성직자들은 고교회파의 원칙과 특권과 대권을 밀어 올리는 데는 매우 적극적이었으나, 다소 헐겁게 퓨지파 관행이라 불리는 경향에 스스로를 내맡긴 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["They all preached in their black gowns, as their fathers had done before them; they wore ordinary black cloth waistcoats; they had no candles on their altars, either lighted or unlighted; they made no private genuflexions, and were contented to confine themselves to such ceremonial observances as had been in vogue for the last hundred years.", "그들은 모두 선대가 그러했듯 검은 가운을 입고 설교했고, 평범한 검은 모직 조끼를 입었으며, 제단에는 촛불을 켜 놓지도 세워 두지도 않았다. 사사로이 무릎을 굽히는 일도 없었고, 지난 백 년 동안 통용되어 온 정도의 의례에 스스로를 가두는 것으로 만족했다."], ["The services were decently and demurely read in their parish churches, chanting was confined to the cathedral, and the science of intoning was unknown.", "예배는 각 교구 교회에서 단정하고 얌전하게 낭독되었고, 영창은 대성당에 국한되었으며, 영송이라는 기술은 알려져 있지 않았다."], ["One young man who had come direct from Oxford as a curate to Plumstead had, after the lapse of two or three Sundays, made a faint attempt, much to the bewilderment of the poorer part of the congregation. Dr.", "옥스퍼드에서 곧장 플럼스테드로 부목사로 온 어느 젊은이가 두세 번의 주일이 지난 뒤 어설프게 그것을 시도해 보았다가, 회중 가운데 가난한 축을 어리둥절하게 만든 일이 있기는 했다."], ["Grantly had not been present on the occasion, but Mrs. Grantly, who had her own opinion on the subject, immediately after the service expressed a hope that the young gentleman had not been taken ill, and offered to send him all kinds of condiments supposed to be good for a sore throat.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그 자리에 없었으나, 이 문제에 대해 나름의 견해를 지닌 그랜틀리 부인이 예배가 끝나기 무섭게 그 젊은 신사가 혹 몸이 편찮은 것은 아니기를 바란다고 말하고, 인후통에 좋다는 온갖 약재를 보내 드리겠노라 청했다."], ["After that there had been no more intoning at Plumstead Episcopi.", "그 뒤로 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서 영송은 다시 들리지 않았다."]], [["But now the archdeacon began to meditate on some strong measures of absolute opposition. Dr. Proudie and his crew were of the lowest possible order of Church of England clergymen, and therefore it behoved him, Dr. Grantly, to be of the very highest. Dr. Proudie would abolish all forms and ceremonies, and therefore Dr. Grantly felt the sudden necessity of multiplying them. Dr. Proudie would consent to deprive the church of all collective authority and rule, and therefore Dr. Grantly would stand up for the full power of convocation and the renewal of all its ancient privileges.", "그러나 이제 부주교는 전면적인 반대를 위한 강경한 조치를 궁리하기 시작했다. 프라우디 박사와 그 패거리는 영국 교회 성직자 가운데 있을 수 있는 가장 낮은 축에 속했고, 따라서 그랜틀리 박사인 자기는 마땅히 가장 높은 축이 되어야 했다. 프라우디 박사가 모든 형식과 의례를 없애려 든다면, 그랜틀리 박사는 그것들을 늘려야 할 절박한 필요를 느꼈다. 프라우디 박사가 교회에서 집합적 권위와 통치를 죄다 앗아 가는 데 동의한다면, 그랜틀리 박사는 성직자 회의의 온전한 권한과 그 옛 특권의 부활을 위해 떨쳐 일어날 것이었다."]], [["It was true that he could not himself intone the service, but he could procure the co-operation of any number of gentlemanlike curates well trained in the mystery of doing so.", "그 자신이 예배를 영송할 수 없다는 것은 사실이었으나, 그 비법에 잘 훈련된 신사다운 부목사들의 협력이라면 얼마든지 얻어 낼 수 있었다."], ["He would not willingly alter his own fashion of dress, but he could people Barchester with young clergymen dressed in the longest frocks and in the highest-breasted silk waistcoats. He certainly was not prepared to cross himself, or to advocate the real presence, but without going this length there were various observances, by adopting which he could plainly show his antipathy to such men as Dr.", "제 옷차림을 기꺼이 바꿀 생각은 없었지만, 가장 긴 프록코트에 목까지 올라오는 실크 조끼를 입은 젊은 성직자들로 바체스터를 채울 수는 있었다."], ["Proudie and Mr. Slope.", "성호를 긋거나 실체적 임재를 주창할 준비까지는 분명 되어 있지 않았으나, 거기까지 가지 않고도 채택하기만 하면 프라우디 박사나 슬로프 씨 같은 자들에 대한 반감을 뚜렷이 드러낼 수 있는 여러 관행이 있었다."]], [["All these things passed through his mind as he paced up and down the close with Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨와 함께 경내를 오르내리며 그의 머릿속으로 이 모든 것이 지나갔다."], ["War, war, internecine war was in his heart. He felt that, as regarded himself and Mr.", "전쟁, 전쟁, 서로를 죽이는 전쟁이 그의 가슴속에 있었다."], ["Slope, one of the two must be annihilated as far as the city of Barchester was concerned, and he did not intend to give way until there was not left to him an inch of ground on which he could stand.", "자기와 슬로프 씨로 말하자면 바체스터 시에 관한 한 둘 중 하나는 절멸되어야 한다고 그는 느꼈고, 자기가 딛고 설 땅이 한 치도 남지 않을 때까지는 물러설 생각이 없었다."], ["He still flattered himself that he could make Barchester too hot to hold Mr. Slope, and he had no weakness of spirit to prevent his bringing about such a consummation if it were in his power.", "그는 여전히 바체스터를 슬로프 씨가 붙어 있지 못할 만큼 뜨거운 곳으로 만들 수 있으리라 자부했으며, 제 힘이 닿는 한 그 결말을 기어이 이루어 내는 것을 가로막을 심약함 따위는 그에게 없었다."]], [["\"I suppose Susan must call at the palace,\"", "“수전은 아무래도 주교관에 인사를 가야겠지.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, she shall call there, but it shall be once and once only.", "“예, 가긴 갈 겁니다. 하지만 딱 한 번, 그 한 번뿐입니다."], ["I dare say 'the horses' won't find it convenient to come out to Plumstead very soon, and when that once is done the matter may drop.\"", "‘말들’께서는 플럼스테드까지 나오시기가 그리 편치 않으실 테니, 그 한 번만 치르고 나면 그것으로 흐지부지되겠지요.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose Eleanor need call.", "“엘리너는 갈 필요 없겠지."], ["I don't think Eleanor would get on at all well with Mrs. Proudie.\"", "엘리너가 프라우디 부인과 잘 지낼 성싶지가 않네.”"]], [["\"Not the least necessity in life,\"", "“세상에 조금도 그럴 필요 없습니다.”"], ["replied the archdeacon, not without the reflexion that a ceremony which was necessary for his wife might not be at all binding on the widow of John Bold.", "부주교가 대답했다. 제 아내에게는 꼭 필요한 예절이 존 볼드의 미망인에게는 전혀 구속력이 없을 수도 있다는 생각이 없지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"Not the slightest reason on earth why she should do so, if she doesn't like it.", "“내키지 않는다면 그렇게 해야 할 이유가 이 세상에 털끝만큼도 없지요."], ["For myself, I don't think that any decent young woman should be subjected to the nuisance of being in the same room with that man.\"", "저로 말하자면, 반듯한 젊은 여자라면 누구든 그런 사내와 한방에 있는 곤욕을 치를 까닭이 없다고 봅니다.”"]], [["And so the two clergymen parted, Mr.", "그리하여 두 성직자는 헤어졌다."], ["Harding going to his daughter's house, and the archdeacon seeking the seclusion of his brougham.", "하딩 씨는 딸의 집으로 갔고, 부주교는 제 사륜마차의 호젓함을 찾아 들어갔다."]], [["The new inhabitants of the palace did not express any higher opinion of their visitors than their visitors had expressed of them.", "주교관의 새 거주자들이 손님들에 대해 품은 의견도 손님들이 그들에 대해 표한 의견보다 나을 것이 없었다."], ["Though they did not use quite such strong language as Dr. Grantly had done, they felt as much personal aversion, and were quite as well aware as he was that there would be a battle to be fought, and that there was hardly room for Proudieism in Barchester as long as Grantlyism was predominant.", "그랜틀리 박사만큼 험한 말을 쓰지는 않았으나 그들도 그만큼이나 개인적인 반감을 느꼈고, 한바탕 싸움이 벌어지리라는 것, 그랜틀리주의가 우세한 한 바체스터에는 프라우디주의가 들어설 자리가 거의 없다는 것을 그 못지않게 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["Indeed, it may be doubted whether Mr. Slope had not already within his breast a better prepared system of strategy, a more accurately defined line of hostile conduct than the archdeacon. Dr. Grantly was going to fight because he found that he hated the man. Mr. Slope had predetermined to hate the man because he foresaw the necessity of fighting him. When he had first reviewed the carte du pays previous to his entry into Barchester, the idea had occurred to him of conciliating the archdeacon, of cajoling and flattering him into submission, and of obtaining the upper hand by cunning instead of courage. A little inquiry, however, sufficed to convince him that all his cunning would fail to win over such a man as Dr. Grantly to such a mode of action as that to be adopted by Mr. Slope, and then he determined to fall back upon his courage. He at once saw that open battle against Dr. Grantly and all Dr. Grantly's adherents was a necessity of his position, and he deliberately planned the most expedient methods of giving offence.", "실은 슬로프 씨가 이미 부주교보다 더 잘 짜인 전략 체계를, 더 정확하게 그어 놓은 적대 행동의 노선을 가슴속에 품고 있지 않았는지 의심해 볼 만하다. 그랜틀리 박사는 그 사람이 밉다는 것을 알았기에 싸우려 들었다. 슬로프 씨는 그 사람과 싸워야 할 필요를 내다보았기에 미워하기로 미리 정해 두었다. 바체스터에 들어서기에 앞서 carte du pays를 처음 살폈을 때, 그에게는 부주교를 회유하여 구슬리고 추어올려 굴복시키고, 용기 대신 꾀로 우위를 차지한다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 조금 알아보고 나니, 그랜틀리 박사 같은 사람을 슬로프 씨가 취할 방식으로 끌어들이기에는 제 꾀가 아무 소용이 없으리라는 확신이 서기에 족했고, 그리하여 그는 용기 쪽으로 물러서기로 작정했다. 그는 그랜틀리 박사와 그 추종자 전부를 상대로 한 공공연한 싸움이 제 처지에 필연이라는 것을 대번에 알아보았고, 어떻게 하면 가장 효과적으로 남의 심기를 건드릴 수 있을지 신중하게 계획을 세웠다."]], [["Soon after his arrival the bishop had intimated to the dean that, with the permission of the canon then in residence, his chaplain would preach in the cathedral on the next Sunday.", "부임한 지 얼마 되지 않아 주교는 수석 사제에게, 마침 당번을 맡은 참사원의 허락을 얻어 다음 주일에 제 전속 사제가 대성당에서 설교하게 하겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["The canon in residence happened to be the Hon. and Rev.", "그때 당번 참사원은 다름 아닌 존귀하신 베시 스탠호프 목사 박사였는데, 그는 이 무렵 코메 호수 기슭에서 그를 그토록 유명하게 만든 저 유례없는 나비 수집을 늘리느라 몹시 분주했다."], ["Dr. Vesey Stanhope, who at this time was very busy on the shores of the Lake of Como, adding to that unique collection of butterflies for which he is so famous.", "아니, 나비니 무어니 하는 여름날의 사정만 없었더라면 당번을 서고 있었을 터였다."], ["Or rather, he would have been in residence but for the butterflies and other such summer-day considerations; and the vicar-choral, who was to take his place in the pulpit, by no means objected to having his work done for him by Mr. Slope.", "그리하여 그를 대신해 강단에 설 예정이던 성가대 사제는 슬로프 씨가 제 몫의 일을 대신 해 주는 데 조금도 이의가 없었다."]], [["Mr. Slope accordingly preached, and if a preacher can have satisfaction in being listened to, Mr.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨는 설교를 했고, 설교자라는 것이 남들이 귀 기울여 들어 주는 데서 만족을 얻을 수 있는 존재라면 슬로프 씨는 흡족했어야 마땅했다."], ["Slope ought to have been gratified. I have reason to think that he was gratified, and that he left the pulpit with the conviction that he had done what he intended to do when he entered it.", "나로서는 그가 실제로 흡족했으며, 강단에 올라설 때 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 해냈다는 확신을 품고 강단을 내려왔으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 있다."]], [["On this occasion the new bishop took his seat for the first time in the throne alloted to him.", "이날 새 주교는 자기에게 배정된 옥좌에 처음으로 앉았다."], ["New scarlet cushions and drapery had been prepared, with new gilt binding and new fringe.", "새 진홍색 방석과 휘장이 마련되었고 금박 테두리와 술도 새것이었다."], ["The old carved oak-wood of the throne, ascending with its numerous grotesque pinnacles half-way up to the roof of the choir, had been washed, and dusted, and rubbed, and it all looked very smart.", "성가대석 지붕까지 절반쯤 치솟은 수많은 기괴한 첨두 장식을 거느린 옥좌의 낡은 참나무 조각은 씻기고 먼지가 털리고 문질러져 온통 아주 말끔해 보였다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["how often sitting there, in happy early days, on those lowly benches in front of the altar, have I whiled away the tedium of a sermon in considering how best I might thread my way up amidst those wooden towers and climb safely to the topmost pinnacle!", "행복하던 어린 시절, 제단 앞의 그 낮은 걸상에 앉아, 저 나무 탑들 사이로 어떻게 길을 더듬어 올라가야 맨 꼭대기 첨두까지 무사히 기어오를 수 있을까 궁리하며 설교의 지루함을 달랜 적이 얼마나 많았던가!"]], [["All Barchester went to hear Mr.", "바체스터 사람들이 죄다 슬로프 씨의 설교를 들으러 왔다."], ["Slope; either for that or to gaze at the new bishop.", "그게 아니면 새 주교를 구경하러 왔다."], ["All the best bonnets of the city were there, and moreover all the best glossy clerical hats.", "시내에서 가장 좋은 보닛이 모두 거기 있었고, 게다가 가장 윤이 나는 성직자용 모자도 모두 거기 있었다."], ["Not a stall but had its fitting occupant, for though some of the prebendaries might be away in Italy or elsewhere, their places were filled by brethren who flocked into Barchester on the occasion.", "참사원 좌석은 하나도 비지 않고 제 임자를 찾았으니, 참사회원 몇몇이 이탈리아니 어디니에 나가 있기는 했어도 그 자리는 이 기회에 바체스터로 몰려든 동료들로 채워졌다."], ["The dean was there, a heavy old man, now too old, indeed, to attend frequently in his place, and so was the archdeacon.", "수석 사제도 나왔는데, 그는 이제 나이가 너무 많아 실은 제자리를 자주 지키지도 못하는 노인이었다. 부주교도 나왔다."], ["So also were the chancellor, the treasurer, the precentor, sundry canons and minor canons, and every lay member of the choir, prepared to sing the new bishop in with due melody and harmonious expression of sacred welcome.", "학무 참사원과 재무 참사원, 선창자와 여러 참사원과 하급 참사원들, 그리고 성가대의 평신도 단원 전원도 새 주교를 마땅한 가락과 거룩한 환영의 조화로운 표현으로 맞아들일 채비를 하고 나와 있었다."]], [["The service was certainly very well performed.", "예배는 확실히 매우 훌륭하게 치러졌다."], ["Such was always the case at Barchester, as the musical education of the choir had been good, and the voices had been carefully selected.", "바체스터에서는 늘 그러했으니, 성가대의 음악 교육이 좋았고 목소리들이 세심하게 골라졌기 때문이다."], ["The psalms were beautifully chanted; the Te Deum was magnificently sung; and the litany was given in a manner which is still to be found at Barchester, but, if my taste be correct, is to be found nowhere else.", "시편은 아름답게 영창되었고, 테 데움은 장엄하게 불렸으며, 기도문은 지금도 바체스터에서는 들을 수 있으나 내 취향이 옳다면 다른 어디서도 들을 수 없는 방식으로 봉헌되었다."], ["The litany in Barchester cathedral has long been the special task to which Mr. Harding's skill and voice have been devoted.", "바체스터 대성당의 이 기도문은 오래전부터 하딩 씨의 기량과 목소리가 바쳐지는 특별한 소임이었다."], ["Crowded audiences generally make good performers, and though Mr. Harding was not aware of any extraordinary exertion on his part, yet probably he rather exceeded his usual mark.", "대체로 청중이 붐비면 연주하는 이도 잘하는 법이라, 하딩 씨는 스스로 특별히 애를 썼다고 여기지 않았음에도 아마 평소의 수준을 조금 웃돌았을 것이다."], ["Others were doing their best, and it was natural that he should emulate his brethren.", "다들 제 최선을 다하고 있었으니 그가 동료들과 겨루려 한 것도 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["So the service went on, and at last Mr. Slope got into the pulpit.", "그렇게 예배가 이어졌고, 마침내 슬로프 씨가 강단에 올랐다."]], [["He chose for his text a verse from the precepts addressed by St. Paul to Timothy, as to the conduct necessary in a spiritual pastor and guide, and it was immediately evident that the good clergy of Barchester were to have a lesson.", "그는 영적인 목자이자 인도자에게 필요한 처신에 관해 성 바울이 디모데에게 준 훈계 가운데 한 구절을 본문으로 골랐고, 바체스터의 선량한 성직자들이 한 수 배우게 되리라는 것이 대번에 분명해졌다."]], [["\"Study to show thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth.\"", "“너는 진리의 말씀을 옳게 분별하며 부끄러울 것이 없는 일꾼으로 인정된 자로 자신을 하나님 앞에 드리기를 힘쓰라.”"], ["These were the words of his text, and with such a subject in such a place, it may be supposed that such a preacher would be listened to by such an audience.", "이것이 그가 택한 본문의 말씀이었고, 그런 자리에서 그런 주제를 들고나왔으니 그런 설교자가 그런 청중에게 경청되었으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다."], ["He was listened to with breathless attention and not without considerable surprise.", "그는 숨죽인 주목 속에서, 그리고 적잖은 놀라움 속에서 끝까지 경청되었다."], ["Whatever opinion of Mr. Slope might have been held in Barchester before he commenced his discourse, none of his hearers, when it was over, could mistake him either for a fool or a coward.", "그가 설교를 시작하기 전에 바체스터에서 슬로프 씨를 어떻게 여겼든 간에, 설교가 끝났을 때 그를 바보나 겁쟁이로 오인할 수 있는 청중은 하나도 없었다."]], [["It would not be becoming were I to travesty a sermon, or even to repeat the language of it in the pages of a novel.", "내가 설교를 희화화하거나, 하다못해 그 표현을 소설 지면에 옮겨 적는 것은 온당치 못한 일이리라."], ["In endeavouring to depict the characters of the persons of whom I write, I am to a certain extent forced to speak of sacred things.", "내가 쓰는 인물들의 성격을 그려 내려 하다 보니 어느 정도까지는 성스러운 것들을 입에 올릴 수밖에 없다."], ["I trust, however, that I shall not be thought to scoff at the pulpit, though some may imagine that I do not feel all the reverence that is due to the cloth.", "그러나 더러는 내가 성직에 마땅한 경외를 다 갖추지 못했다고 여길지 모르나, 내가 강단을 조롱한다고는 생각지 말아 주기를 바란다."], ["I may question the infallibility of the teachers, but I hope that I shall not therefore be accused of doubt as to the thing to be taught.", "나는 가르치는 자들의 무오류성을 의심할 수는 있으나, 그렇다고 가르쳐야 할 것 자체를 의심한다는 비난을 받지는 않기를 바란다."]], [["Mr. Slope, in commencing his sermon, showed no slight tact in his ambiguous manner of hinting that, humble as he was himself, he stood there as the mouth-piece of the illustrious divine who sat opposite to him; and having premised so much, he gave forth a very accurate definition of the conduct which that prelate would rejoice to see in the clergymen now brought under his jurisdiction.", "슬로프 씨는 설교를 시작하면서, 자기는 보잘것없는 사람이나 맞은편에 앉으신 저 걸출한 신학자의 대변자로서 이 자리에 섰다는 뜻을 모호하게 내비치는 데서 적잖은 수완을 보였다."], ["It is only necessary to say that the peculiar points insisted upon were exactly those which were most distasteful to the clergy of the diocese, and most averse to their practice and opinions, and that all those peculiar habits and privileges which have always been dear to High Church priests, to that party which is now scandalously called the \"high and dry church,\"", "그렇게 운을 떼어 놓은 다음 그는, 이제 그 고위 성직자의 관할 아래 놓인 성직자들에게서 그분이 보고 기뻐하실 만한 처신이 어떤 것인지를 아주 정확하게 정의해 보였다. 그가 힘주어 짚은 특정 항목들이 하필 이 교구 성직자들에게 가장 역겨운 것들이자 그들의 관행과 견해에 가장 어긋나는 것들이었다는 것, 그리고 고교회파 사제들에게, 곧 요즘 망측하게도 “고답건조파”라 불리는 그 당파에게 언제나 소중했던 그 온갖 독특한 습속과 특권이 죄다 조롱당하고 매도당하고 저주받았다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠다."], ["were ridiculed, abused, and anathematized. Now, the clergymen of the diocese of Barchester are all of the high and dry church.", "그런데 바체스터 교구의 성직자들은 하나같이 고답건조파다."]], [["Having thus, according to his own opinion, explained how a clergyman should show himself approved unto God, as a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, he went on to explain how the word of truth should be divided; and here he took a rather narrow view of the question and fetched his arguments from afar.", "그리하여 제 나름의 견해에 따라, 성직자가 어떻게 하여야 부끄러울 것 없는 일꾼으로 하나님 앞에 인정된 자로 자신을 드릴 수 있는지를 설명하고 나서, 그는 진리의 말씀을 어떻게 옳게 분별할 것인지를 설명하는 데로 나아갔다."], ["His object was to express his abomination of all ceremonious modes of utterance, to cry down any religious feeling which might be excited, not by the sense, but by the sound of words, and in fact to insult cathedral practices.", "그리고 여기서 그는 이 문제를 다소 좁게 보고 논거를 멀리서 끌어왔다."], ["Had St. Paul spoken of rightly pronouncing, instead of rightly dividing the word of truth, this part of his sermon would have been more to the purpose, but the preacher's immediate object was to preach Mr.", "그의 목적은 온갖 의례적인 발성 방식에 대한 제 혐오를 표하고, 말의 뜻이 아니라 소리에 의해 불러일으켜지는 종교적 감정이라면 무엇이든 깎아내리는 것, 요컨대 대성당의 관행을 모욕하는 것이었다."], ["Slope's doctrine, and not St. Paul's, and he contrived to give the necessary twist to the text with some skill.", "성 바울이 진리의 말씀을 옳게 분별하라 하지 않고 옳게 발음하라 했더라면 설교의 이 대목이 더 걸맞았겠으나, 이 설교자의 당면 목적은 성 바울의 교리가 아니라 슬로프 씨의 교리를 설교하는 데 있었고, 그는 제법 솜씨 있게 본문을 필요한 만큼 비틀어 놓았다."]], [["He could not exactly say, preaching from a cathedral pulpit, that chanting should be abandoned in cathedral services.", "대성당 강단에서 설교하면서 대성당 예배에서 영창을 폐지해야 한다고 대놓고 말할 수는 없었다. 그런 주장을 했다가는 과녁을 훌쩍 넘겨 스스로 우스워지고 청중을 기쁘게 할 뿐이었으리라."], ["By such an assertion he would have overshot his mark and rendered himself absurd, to the delight of his hearers.", "그러나 교구 교회의 영송 관행에 대해서라면 얼마든지 준엄한 규탄을 곁들여 언급할 수 있었고 실제로 그렇게 했다. 이 교구에서는 그런 관행이 거의 알려져 있지도 않았는데 말이다."], ["He could, however, and did, allude with heavy denunciations to the practice of intoning in parish churches, although the practice was all but unknown in the diocese; and from thence he came round to the undue preponderance which, he asserted, music had over meaning in the beautiful service which they had just heard.", "그러고서 그는 방금 들은 저 아름다운 예배에서 음악이 뜻보다 지나치게 우세하다는 주장으로 넘어갔다. 그는 이렇게 말했다."], ["He was aware, he said, that the practices of our ancestors could not be abandoned at a moment's notice; the feelings of the aged would be outraged, and the minds of respectable men would be shocked.", "우리 조상들의 관행을 하루아침에 버릴 수 없다는 것은 저도 알고 있는 바입니다. 그리하면 연로하신 분들의 마음이 상할 것이요, 존경할 만한 이들의 심경이 충격을 받을 것입니다."], ["There were many, he was aware, of not sufficient calibre of thought to perceive, of not sufficient education to know, that a mode of service which was effective when outward ceremonies were of more moment than inward feelings, had become all but barbarous at a time when inward conviction was everything, when each word of the minister's lips should fall intelligibly into the listener's heart.", "저도 알거니와, 사려의 그릇이 넉넉지 못하여 깨닫지 못하고 배움이 넉넉지 못하여 알지 못하는 이들이 많이 있습니다. 곧 겉으로 드러난 의식이 속마음보다 더 중하던 시절에는 유효하던 예배 방식이, 내면의 확신이 전부인 시대에 이르러, 목사의 입술에서 나오는 낱말 하나하나가 듣는 이의 마음에 알아들을 수 있게 떨어져야 하는 시대에 이르러서는 거의 야만에 가까운 것이 되고 말았다는 사실을 알지 못하는 이들 말입니다."], ["Formerly the religion of the multitude had been an affair of the imagination: now, in these latter days, it had become necessary that a Christian should have a reason for his faith--should not only believe, but digest--not only hear, but understand.", "예전에 뭇사람의 종교는 상상의 문제였습니다. 그러나 이제 이 말세에 이르러서는 그리스도인이라면 제 믿음에 이유를 지녀야 하고, 믿을 뿐 아니라 소화해야 하며, 들을 뿐 아니라 이해해야 하게 되지 않았겠습니까."], ["The words of our morning service, how beautiful, how apposite, how intelligible they were, when read with simple and distinct decorum!", "우리 아침 예배의 말씀들, 소박하고 또렷한 품위로 낭독될 때 그 말씀들이 얼마나 아름답고 얼마나 적절하며 얼마나 알아듣기 쉬웠습니까!"], ["But how much of the meaning of the words was lost when they were produced with all the meretricious charms of melody!", "그러나 그것이 온갖 겉만 번드르르한 선율의 매력을 걸치고 나올 때, 그 말씀의 뜻은 얼마나 많이 사라져 버리는 것이 아니겠습니까!"], ["&c. &c.", "운운."]], [["Here was a sermon to be preached before Mr. Archdeacon Grantly, Mr.", "이런 설교를 그랜틀리 부주교와 하딩 선창자와 그 나머지 사람들 앞에서 하다니!"], ["Precentor Harding, and the rest of them! Before a whole dean and chapter assembled in their own cathedral!", "제 대성당에 모인 수석 사제와 참사회 전원 앞에서!"], ["Before men who had grown old in the exercise of their peculiar services, with a full conviction of their excellence for all intended purposes!", "제 나름의 예배가 그 의도한 모든 바에 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하다고 굳게 확신하며 그 예배를 집전하는 가운데 늙어 온 사람들 앞에서!"], ["This too from such a man, a clerical parvenu, a man without a cure, a mere chaplain, an intruder among them; a fellow raked up, so said Dr. Grantly, from the gutters of Marylebone!", "그것도 저런 인간, 성직계의 parvenu, 맡은 사목 하나 없는 자, 한낱 전속 사제, 그들 사이에 끼어든 침입자, 그랜틀리 박사의 말마따나 메릴본의 시궁창에서 긁어 올려진 자의 입에서 나온 설교를!"], ["They had to sit through it! None of them, not even Dr.", "그들은 그것을 앉아서 끝까지 들어야 했다!"], ["Grantly, could close his ears, nor leave the house of God during the hours of service.", "그들 중 누구도, 그랜틀리 박사조차도 귀를 막을 수 없었고 예배 시간에 하나님의 집을 떠날 수도 없었다."], ["They were under an obligation of listening, and that too without any immediate power of reply.", "그들은 들어야 할 의무 아래 있었고, 게다가 당장 대꾸할 힘도 없었다."]], [["There is, perhaps, no greater hardship at present inflicted on mankind in civilized and free countries than the necessity of listening to sermons.", "오늘날 문명하고 자유로운 나라에서 인류에게 가해지는 고초 가운데 설교를 들어야 하는 일보다 더한 것은 아마 없을 것이다."], ["No one but a preaching clergyman has, in these realms, the power of compelling an audience to sit silent and be tormented.", "이 땅에서 설교하는 성직자 말고는 그 누구도 청중을 잠자코 앉혀 놓고 괴롭힐 권능을 가지지 못한다."], ["No one but a preaching clergyman can revel in platitudes, truisms, and untruisms, and yet receive, as his undisputed privilege, the same respectful demeanour as though words of impassioned eloquence, or persuasive logic, fell from his lips.", "설교하는 성직자 말고는 그 누구도 상투어와 자명한 소리와 자명하지 않은 소리를 마음껏 늘어놓고도, 마치 그 입에서 열정적인 웅변이나 설득력 있는 논리가 흘러나오기라도 한 듯한 공손한 태도를 아무도 이의를 달지 못하는 제 특권으로 받아 누리지 못한다."], ["Let a professor of law or physics find his place in a lecture-room, and there pour forth jejune words and useless empty phrases, and he will pour them forth to empty benches.", "법학이나 물리학 교수가 강의실에 자리를 잡고 앉아 무미건조한 말과 쓸모없는 빈말을 쏟아 놓아 보라. 그는 텅 빈 걸상을 향해 쏟아 놓게 될 것이다."], ["Let a barrister attempt to talk without talking well, and he will talk but seldom.", "법정 변호사가 잘하지도 못하면서 말을 늘어놓아 보라. 그가 말할 기회는 좀처럼 오지 않을 것이다."], ["A judge's charge need be listened to perforce by none but the jury, prisoner, and gaoler.", "판사의 논고는 배심원과 죄수와 간수 말고는 억지로 들어야 할 사람이 없다."], ["A member of Parliament can be coughed down or counted out.", "국회의원은 기침 소리에 눌려 앉거나 정족수 미달로 회의가 파해 버린다."], ["Town-councillors can be tabooed.", "시의원은 따돌림을 당한다."], ["But no one can rid himself of the preaching clergyman.", "그러나 설교하는 성직자만은 누구도 떨쳐 낼 수가 없다."], ["He is the bore of the age, the old man whom we Sindbads cannot shake off, the nightmare that disturbs our Sunday's rest, the incubus that overloads our religion and makes God's service distasteful.", "그는 이 시대의 골칫거리요, 우리 신드바드들이 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 그 노인이요, 우리의 주일 안식을 어지럽히는 악몽이요, 우리의 신앙을 짓눌러 하나님 섬기는 일을 역겹게 만드는 몽마다."], ["We are not forced into church!", "우리는 억지로 교회에 끌려가는 것이 아니다!"], ["No: but we desire more than that.", "그렇다. 하지만 우리가 바라는 것은 그 이상이다."], ["We desire not to be forced to stay away.", "우리는 억지로 발길을 끊게 되지 않기를 바란다."], ["We desire, nay, we are resolute, to enjoy the comfort of public worship, but we desire also that we may do so without an amount of tedium which ordinary human nature cannot endure with patience; that we may be able to leave the house of God without that anxious longing for escape which is the common consequence of common sermons.", "우리는 공적 예배의 위안을 누리기를 바라며, 아니, 누리기로 굳게 마음먹었으되, 보통의 인간 본성이 참을성 있게 견딜 수 없을 만큼의 지루함 없이 그리하기를 바란다. 흔한 설교의 흔한 결말인 저 도망치고 싶은 초조한 갈망 없이 하나님의 집을 나설 수 있기를 바라는 것이다."]], [["With what complacency will a young parson deduce false conclusions from misunderstood texts, and then threaten us with all the penalties of Hades if we neglect to comply with the injunctions he has given us!", "젊은 목사가 얼마나 흡족한 얼굴로 잘못 이해한 본문에서 그릇된 결론을 이끌어 내고는, 자기가 내린 지시를 따르지 않으면 지옥의 온갖 형벌을 받으리라 우리를 을러대는가! 그렇다, 자신감이 지나친 나의 젊은 벗이여, 나도 그대의 입에 그토록 흔히 오르내리는 그 신비들을 믿는다."], ["Yes, my too self-confident juvenile friend, I do believe in those mysteries which are so common in your mouth; I do believe in the unadulterated word which you hold there in your hand; but you must pardon me if, in some things, I doubt your interpretation.", "나도 그대가 손에 들고 있는 그 순전한 말씀을 믿는다. 다만 어떤 대목에서 그대의 해석을 의심하더라도 나를 용서해야 하리라."], ["The Bible is good, the prayer-book is good, nay, you yourself would be acceptable, if you would read to me some portion of those time-honoured discourses which our great divines have elaborated in the full maturity of their powers.", "성경도 좋고 기도서도 좋다. 아니, 우리의 위대한 신학자들이 그 역량이 무르익었을 때 다듬어 놓은 유서 깊은 강론의 한 대목이라도 내게 읽어 준다면 그대 자신도 받아들일 만하다."], ["But you must excuse me, my insufficient young lecturer, if I yawn over your imperfect sentences, your repeated phrases, your false pathos, your drawlings and denouncings, your humming and hawing, your oh-ing and ah-ing, your black gloves and your white handkerchief.", "그러나 부족한 나의 젊은 강사여, 그대의 미숙한 문장과 되풀이되는 상투구와 거짓 애상과 늘어지는 어조와 규탄, 그대의 흠흠거림과 어어거림, 그대의 오오 소리와 아아 소리, 그대의 검은 장갑과 흰 손수건 앞에서 내가 하품을 하더라도 부디 양해해 주기 바란다."], ["To me, it all means nothing; and hours are too precious to be so wasted--if one could only avoid it.", "내게 그것은 다 아무 뜻도 없으며, 시간이란 그렇게 허비하기에는 너무 귀하다. 그것을 피할 수만 있다면 말이지만."]], [["And here I must make a protest against the pretence, so often put forward by the working clergy, that they are overburdened by the multitude of sermons to be preached.", "그리고 여기서 나는, 현장에서 일하는 성직자들이 그토록 자주 내세우는 저 핑계, 곧 설교할 것이 너무 많아 짐이 무겁다는 주장에 항의를 해 두어야겠다."], ["We are all too fond of our own voices, and a preacher is encouraged in the vanity of making his heard by the privilege of a compelled audience.", "우리는 모두 제 목소리를 지나치게 좋아하며, 설교자는 억지로 붙들린 청중이라는 특권 덕분에 제 목소리를 들려주려는 허영을 부추김받는다."], ["His sermon is the pleasant morsel of his life, his delicious moment of self-exaltation.", "그의 설교는 그의 생애에서 달콤한 한 입이요, 스스로를 드높이는 감미로운 순간이다."], ["\"I have preached nine sermons this week,\"", "“이번 주에 설교를 아홉 번 했지 뭔가.”"], ["said a young friend to me the other day, with hand languidly raised to his brow, the picture of an overburdened martyr.", "얼마 전 어느 젊은 벗이 내게 그렇게 말했다. 짐에 짓눌린 순교자의 형상 그대로, 나른하게 손을 이마에 얹은 채였다."], ["\"Nine this week, seven last week, four the week before.", "“이번 주에 아홉 번, 지난주에 일곱 번, 그 전주에 네 번."], ["I have preached twenty-three sermons this month.", "이번 달에만 스물세 번을 설교했네."], ["It is really too much.\"", "정말이지 너무 많아.”"]], [["\"Too much, indeed,\"", "“정말 너무 많군.”"], ["said I, shuddering; \"too much for the strength of any one.\"", "나는 몸서리치며 말했다. “누구의 기력으로도 감당하기 어려울 만큼 너무 많아.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그렇지.” 그가 온순하게 대답했다."], ["he answered meekly, \"indeed it is; I am beginning to feel it painfully.\"", "“정말 그렇다네. 요즘 들어 그게 아프도록 느껴지기 시작했어.”"]], [["\"Would,\"", "“느꼈으면 좋겠군.”"], ["said I, \"you could feel it--would that you could be made to feel it.\"", "내가 말했다. “부디 그대가 그것을 느끼게 되었으면 좋겠어.”"], ["But he never guessed that my heart was wrung for the poor listeners.", "그러나 그는 내 마음이 저 가엾은 청중들 때문에 아팠다는 것을 끝내 짐작하지 못했다."]], [["There was, at any rate, no tedium felt in listening to Mr. Slope on the occasion in question.", "어쨌든 문제의 그날 슬로프 씨의 설교를 듣는 데 지루함을 느낀 사람은 없었다."], ["His subject came too home to his audience to be dull, and, to tell the truth, Mr. Slope had the gift of using words forcibly.", "그의 주제는 청중에게 너무나 절실하게 와닿는 것이어서 따분할 수가 없었고, 사실대로 말하자면 슬로프 씨에게는 말을 힘 있게 부리는 재주가 있었다."], ["He was heard through his thirty minutes of eloquence with mute attention and open ears, but with angry eyes, which glared round from one enraged parson to another, with wide-spread nostrils from which already burst forth fumes of indignation, and with many shufflings of the feet and uneasy motions of the body, which betokened minds disturbed, and hearts not at peace with all the world.", "30분에 걸친 그의 웅변은 말없는 주목과 열린 귀로 끝까지 들렸으나, 눈만은 성난 목사에게서 또 다른 성난 목사에게로 번득이며 옮겨 다녔고, 벌름거리는 콧구멍에서는 이미 분노의 김이 뿜어져 나왔으며, 발을 여러 번 끌고 몸을 불편하게 뒤척이는 모양은 마음이 어지럽고 심사가 온 세상과 화평하지 못하다는 것을 말해 주고 있었다."]], [["At last the bishop, who, of all the congregation, had been most surprised, and whose hair almost stood on end with terror, gave the blessing in a manner not at all equal to that in which he had long been practising it in his own study, and the congregation was free to go their way.", "마침내 온 회중 가운데 가장 크게 놀라 머리카락이 곤두설 지경이었던 주교가, 오랫동안 제 서재에서 연습해 온 것과는 전혀 딴판인 태도로 축도를 내렸고, 회중은 각자 제 갈 길을 갈 수 있게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII. The Dean and Chapter Take Counsel", "# 제7장. 수석 사제와 참사회가 의논하다"]], [["All Barchester was in a tumult. Dr.", "바체스터 전체가 발칵 뒤집혔다."], ["Grantly could hardly get himself out of the cathedral porch before he exploded in his wrath.", "그랜틀리 박사는 대성당 현관을 채 벗어나기도 전에 분노를 터뜨렸다."], ["The old dean betook himself silently to his deanery, afraid to speak, and there sat, half-stupefied, pondering many things in vain. Mr.", "늙은 수석 사제는 말할 엄두조차 내지 못한 채 조용히 수석사제관으로 물러가, 반쯤 얼이 빠진 채 앉아 이런저런 생각을 부질없이 굴렸다."], ["Harding crept forth solitary and unhappy; and, slowly passing beneath the elms of the close, could scarcely bring himself to believe that the words which he had heard had proceeded from the pulpit of Barchester cathedral.", "하딩 씨는 홀로 쓸쓸하고 침울하게 걸어 나왔고, 경내의 느릅나무 아래를 천천히 지나면서도 자기가 들은 그 말들이 바체스터 대성당의 강단에서 나왔다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다."], ["Was he again to be disturbed?", "또다시 시달려야 하는가?"], ["Was his whole life to be shown up as a useless sham a second time?", "자기 한평생이 쓸모없는 허울이었다고 두 번째로 까발려져야 하는가?"], ["Would he have to abdicate his precentorship, as he had his wardenship, and to give up chanting, as he had given up his twelve old bedesmen?", "워든 자리를 내놓았듯이 선창자 자리도 내놓고, 열두 구호 노인을 포기했듯이 영창도 포기해야 하는가?"], ["And what if he did!", "그런들 또 어떠랴!"], ["Some other Jupiter, some other Mr. Slope, would come and turn him out of St.", "또 다른 『주피터』가, 또 다른 슬로프 씨가 나타나 그를 세인트 커스버트 교회에서 몰아낼 것이다."], ["Cuthbert's. Surely he could not have been wrong all his life in chanting the litany as he had done!", "평생 해 온 대로 기도문을 영창해 온 것이 설마 그르지야 않았으리라!"], ["He began, however, to have his doubts.", "그러나 그는 슬슬 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["Doubting himself was Mr. Harding's weakness.", "자기 자신을 의심하는 것이 하딩 씨의 약점이었다."], ["It is not, however, the usual fault of his order.", "그러나 그것이 그가 속한 계층의 흔한 결점은 아니다."]], [["Yes! All Barchester was in a tumult.", "그렇다!"], ["It was not only the clergy who were affected.", "바체스터 전체가 발칵 뒤집혔다."], ["The laity also had listened to Mr.", "흔들린 것은 성직자들만이 아니었다."], ["Slope's new doctrine, all with surprise, some with indignation, and some with a mixed feeling, in which dislike of the preacher was not so strongly blended.", "평신도들도 슬로프 씨의 새로운 교리를 들었으니, 모두가 놀랐고 더러는 분개했으며, 더러는 설교자에 대한 반감이 그리 짙게 섞이지 않은 뒤섞인 심정으로 들었다."], ["The old bishop and his chaplains, the dean and his canons and minor canons, the old choir, and especially Mr. Harding who was at the head of it, had all been popular in Barchester.", "늙은 주교와 그 전속 사제들, 수석 사제와 참사원들과 하급 참사원들, 오래된 성가대, 그중에서도 특히 그 우두머리인 하딩 씨는 모두 바체스터에서 인기가 있었다."], ["They had spent their money and done good; the poor had not been ground down; the clergy in society had neither been overbearing nor austere; and the whole repute of the city was due to its ecclesiastical importance.", "그들은 제 돈을 써 가며 좋은 일을 했고, 가난한 이들은 시달리지 않았으며, 사교계의 성직자들은 거만하지도 딱딱하지도 않았다. 그리고 이 도시의 명성이라는 것은 온통 그 교회적 비중에서 나온 것이었다."], ["Yet there were those who had heard Mr. Slope with satisfaction.", "그런데도 슬로프 씨의 설교를 흡족하게 들은 이들이 있었다."]], [["It is so pleasant to receive a fillip of excitement when suffering from the dull routine of everyday life!", "날마다 되풀이되는 따분한 일상에 시달리는 판에 자극이 한 번 톡 튀어 들어오는 것은 얼마나 유쾌한 일인가!"], ["The anthems and Te Deums were in themselves delightful, but they had been heard so often! Mr.", "성가와 테 데움은 그 자체로 흐뭇한 것이었으나 너무 자주 들어 온 것이었다!"], ["Slope was certainly not delightful, but he was new, and, moreover, clever.", "슬로프 씨는 결코 흐뭇한 인물이 아니었지만 새로웠고, 게다가 영리했다."], ["They had long thought it slow, so said now many of the Barchesterians, to go on as they had done in their old humdrum way, giving ear to none of the religious changes which were moving the world without.", "이제 바체스터 사람들 가운데 적잖은 수가 이렇게들 말했다. 바깥세상을 뒤흔드는 종교상의 변화에는 귀 한 번 기울이지 않은 채 예전의 그 단조로운 방식대로 지내 온 것이 진작부터 굼떠 보였다고."], ["People in advance of the age now had new ideas, and it was quite time that Barchester should go in advance.", "시대를 앞서가는 이들은 이제 새로운 생각을 품고 있으니, 바체스터도 앞서 나갈 때가 되었다는 것이다."], ["Mr. Slope might be right.", "슬로프 씨의 말이 옳을지도 모른다."], ["Sunday had certainly not been strictly kept in Barchester, except as regarded the cathedral services.", "대성당 예배에 관한 한을 빼면, 바체스터에서 주일이 엄격히 지켜져 온 것은 분명 아니었다."], ["Indeed the two hours between services had long been appropriated to morning calls and hot luncheons.", "실제로 예배와 예배 사이의 두 시간은 오래전부터 오전 방문과 따뜻한 점심에 배정되어 있었다."], ["Then, Sunday-schools!", "그리고 주일학교!"], ["Really more ought to have been done as to Sunday-schools--Sabbath-day schools Mr. Slope had called them.", "정말이지 주일학교에 대해서는 더 많은 일을 했어야 마땅했다 — 슬로프 씨는 그것을 안식일 학교라 불렀다."], ["The late bishop had really not thought of Sunday-schools as he should have done.", "선대 주교는 참으로 주일학교를 마땅히 생각해야 할 만큼 생각하지 않았다. (이런 사람들은 아마 이 점은 헤아려 보지 않았을 것이다."], ["(These people probably did not reflect that catechisms and collects are quite as hard work to the young mind as bookkeeping is to the elderly, and that quite as little feeling of worship enters into the one task as the other.) And then, as regarded that great question of musical services, there might be much to be said on Mr. Slope's side of the question.", "어린 머리에게 교리문답과 기도문은 늙은 머리에게 부기가 그러하듯 고된 일이며, 그 두 가지 일에 깃드는 예배의 감정이라는 것도 서로 별반 다를 바 없이 적다는 점 말이다.) 그리고 또, 음악 예배라는 저 큰 문제로 말하자면, 그 문제에서 슬로프 씨 편에 서서 할 말이 많을지도 모른다."], ["It certainly was the fact that people went to the cathedral to hear the music, &c. &c", "사람들이 대성당에 음악을 들으러 간다는 것은 분명 사실이었으니, 등등 등등."]], [["And so a party absolutely formed itself in Barchester on Mr. Slope's side of the question!", "그리하여 이 문제에서 슬로프 씨 편에 서는 무리가 바체스터에 실제로 형성되기에 이르렀다!"], ["This consisted, among the upper classes, chiefly of ladies.", "상류층 가운데서 이 무리를 이룬 것은 주로 부인들이었다."], ["No man--that is, no gentleman--could possibly be attracted by Mr. Slope, or consent to sit at the feet of so abhorrent a Gamaliel.", "남자라면 — 그러니까 신사라면 — 슬로프 씨에게 끌리거나 그토록 혐오스러운 가말리엘의 발치에 앉기를 승낙할 리가 없었다."], ["Ladies are sometimes less nice in their appreciation of physical disqualification; provided that a man speak to them well, they will listen, though he speak from a mouth never so deformed and hideous.", "부인들은 때로 신체의 결격을 따지는 데 그리 까다롭지 않다. 남자가 자기들에게 말만 잘한다면, 그 입이 아무리 일그러지고 흉하더라도 귀를 기울여 준다."], ["Wilkes was most fortunate as a lover, and the damp, sandy-haired, saucer-eyed, red-fisted Mr. Slope was powerful only over the female breast.", "윌크스는 연인으로서 더없이 운이 좋았고, 축축하고 모래빛 머리에 접시 같은 눈, 벌건 주먹을 한 슬로프 씨도 여자의 가슴에 대해서만은 위력을 발휘했다."]], [["There were, however, one or two of the neighbouring clergy who thought it not quite safe to neglect the baskets in which for the nonce were stored the loaves and fishes of the diocese of Barchester.", "그러나 이웃 성직자 가운데 한둘은 바체스터 교구의 떡과 물고기가 당분간 담겨 있는 그 바구니들을 소홀히 하는 것이 그리 안전하지 않다고 여겼다."], ["They, and they only, came to call on Mr. Slope after his performance in the cathedral pulpit.", "대성당 강단에서 그 공연을 마친 뒤 슬로프 씨를 찾아와 인사한 사람은 그들뿐이었다."], ["Among these Mr. Quiverful, the rector of Puddingdale, whose wife still continued to present him from year to year with fresh pledges of her love, and so to increase his cares and, it is to be hoped, his happiness equally.", "그중에 푸딩데일의 교구 목사 퀴버풀 씨가 있었는데, 그 부인은 해마다 그에게 사랑의 새 정표를 안겨 주기를 그치지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 근심을, 그리고 바라건대 그의 행복도 똑같이 늘려 주고 있었다."], ["Who can wonder that a gentleman with fourteen living children and a bare income of £400 a year should look after the loaves and fishes, even when they are under the thumb of a Mr. Slope?", "살아 있는 자녀가 열넷이요 수입이라고는 한 해 400파운드가 고작인 신사가, 그 떡과 물고기가 설령 슬로프 씨 같은 자의 손아귀에 있다 한들 그것을 눈여겨보는 것을 누가 이상하다 하겠는가?"]], [["Very soon after the Sunday on which the sermon was preached, the leading clergy of the neighbourhood held high debate together as to how Mr. Slope should be put down.", "그 설교가 행해진 주일이 지나고 얼마 되지 않아, 인근의 주요 성직자들이 한자리에 모여 슬로프 씨를 어떻게 눌러 놓을 것인가를 두고 성대한 토론을 벌였다."], ["In the first place, he should never again preach from the pulpit of Barchester cathedral. This was Dr.", "우선 첫째로, 그자는 두 번 다시 바체스터 대성당의 강단에서 설교해서는 안 된다."], ["Grantly's earliest dictum, and they all agreed, providing only that they had the power to exclude him. Dr.", "이것이 그랜틀리 박사가 맨 처음 내놓은 단정이었고, 다들 그 말에 찬동했다 — 다만 그자를 배제할 권한이 자기들에게 있기만 하다면 말이다."], ["Grantly declared that the power rested with the dean and chapter, observing that no clergyman out of the chapter had a claim to preach there, saving only the bishop himself.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그 권한이 수석 사제와 참사회에 있다고 단언하면서, 주교 자신을 제외하면 참사회 밖의 성직자는 그곳에서 설교할 권리가 없다고 지적했다. 수석 사제도 여기에는 동의했으나, 그런 문제로 다툼을 벌이는 것은 보기 흉하리라고 했다."], ["To this the dean assented, but alleged that contests on such a subject would be unseemly; to which rejoined a meagre little doctor, one of the cathedral prebendaries, that the contest must be all on the side of Mr. Slope if every prebendary were always there ready to take his own place in the pulpit.", "이에 대성당 참사회원 가운데 하나인 여윈 체구의 자그마한 박사가 응수하기를, 참사회원이 저마다 늘 그 자리에 대기하여 강단에서 제 몫을 맡기만 한다면 다툼은 온통 슬로프 씨 쪽에서나 벌어질 일이라 하였다."], ["Cunning little meagre doctor, whom it suits well to live in his own cosy house within Barchester close, and who is well content to have his little fling at Dr. Vesey Stanhope and other absentees, whose Italian villas, or enticing London homes, are more tempting than cathedral stalls and residences!", "바체스터 경내 안 제 아늑한 집에 사는 것이 더없이 마음에 맞고, 이탈리아의 별장이나 사람을 끄는 런던의 저택이 대성당의 성가대석과 사택보다 더 탐나서 자리를 비운 베시 스탠호프 박사 같은 이들에게 한마디 슬쩍 던져 놓고 흡족해하는, 교활하고 여윈 자그마한 박사여!"]], [["To this answered the burly chancellor, a man rather silent indeed, but very sensible, that absent prebendaries had their vicars, and that in such case the vicar's right to the pulpit was the same as that of the higher order.", "이에 대해 몸집이 큰 학무 참사원가 대답하기를 — 말수는 확실히 적으나 매우 분별 있는 사람이었다 — 자리를 비운 참사회원에게는 대리 사제가 있고, 그런 경우 강단에 설 대리 사제의 권리는 윗자리 성직자의 권리와 같다고 하였다."], ["To which the dean assented, groaning deeply at these truths.", "수석 사제는 이 말에도 동의하며 이 엄연한 사실 앞에서 깊이 신음했다."], ["Thereupon, however, the meagre doctor remarked that they would be in the hands of their minor canons, one of whom might at any hour betray his trust.", "그러나 그러자 여윈 박사가, 그리 되면 자기들이 하급 참사원들의 손에 맡겨지는 셈이며 그중 누구라도 언제든 그 신임을 저버릴 수 있다고 지적했다."], ["Whereon was heard from the burly chancellor an ejaculation sounding somewhat like \"Pooh, pooh, pooh!\"", "그러자 몸집 큰 학무 참사원에게서 “쯧, 쯧, 쯧!”"], ["but it might be that the worthy man was but blowing out the heavy breath from his windpipe.", "비슷하게 들리는 소리가 새어 나왔는데, 어쩌면 그 훌륭한 이는 그저 목구멍에 걸린 묵직한 숨을 내뿜은 것뿐인지도 몰랐다."], ["Why silence him at all?", "굳이 입을 막을 것까지야 있겠습니까?"], ["suggested Mr. Harding.", "하고 하딩 씨가 제안했다."], ["Let them not be ashamed to hear what any man might have to preach to them, unless he preached false doctrine; in which case, let the bishop silence him.", "거짓 교리를 설교하는 것이 아닌 다음에야 어느 누가 무슨 설교를 하든 듣기를 부끄러워하지 맙시다."], ["So spoke our friend; vainly; for human ends must be attained by human means.", "거짓 교리라면 주교가 그 입을 막게 하면 될 일이고요. 우리의 벗은 그렇게 말했다."], ["But the dean saw a ray of hope out of those purblind old eyes of his.", "부질없이. 인간의 목적은 인간의 수단으로 이루어야 하는 법이니."], ["Yes, let them tell the bishop how distasteful to them was this Mr.", "그러나 수석 사제는 그 침침한 늙은 눈으로 한 줄기 희망을 보았다."], ["Slope: a new bishop just come to his seat could not wish to insult his clergy while the gloss was yet fresh on his first apron.", "그렇지, 이 슬로프 씨가 자기들에게 얼마나 못마땅한 인물인지를 주교에게 알리자. 갓 자리에 오른 새 주교가, 첫 주교복 앞치마에 아직 윤이 가시지도 않았는데 제 휘하 성직자들을 모욕하고 싶어 할 리는 없을 테니."]], [["Then up rose Dr. Grantly and, having thus collected the scattered wisdom of his associates, spoke forth with words of deep authority.", "그러자 그랜틀리 박사가 몸을 일으켜, 동료들의 흩어진 지혜를 이렇게 그러모은 끝에 깊은 권위가 실린 말을 내놓았다."], ["When I say up rose the archdeacon, I speak of the inner man, which then sprang up to more immediate action, for the doctor had bodily been standing all along with his back to the dean's empty fire-grate, and the tails of his frock coat supported over his two arms.", "부주교가 몸을 일으켰다고 할 때, 내가 말하는 것은 그의 내면의 사람이다. 그것이 그때 좀 더 즉각적인 행동으로 벌떡 일어섰던 것이다."], ["His hands were in his breeches pockets.", "정작 육신으로 말하자면 박사는 내내 수석 사제의 불 꺼진 벽난로 쇠살대에 등을 대고 선 채, 프록코트 자락을 양팔에 걸치고 있었기 때문이다. 두 손은 반바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채였다."]], [["\"It is quite clear that this man must not be allowed to preach again in this cathedral.", "“이자가 이 대성당에서 다시 설교하도록 두어서는 안 된다는 것은 지극히 명백하오."], ["We all see that, except our dear friend here, the milk of whose nature runs so softly that he would not have the heart to refuse the Pope the loan of his pulpit, if the Pope would come and ask it.", "우리 모두 그 점을 알고 있소 — 여기 계신 우리의 다정한 벗 한 분만은 예외지만 말이오. 그분의 천성은 젖처럼 부드럽게 흐르는지라, 교황이 몸소 찾아와 강단을 좀 빌려 달라 청한다 해도 차마 거절하지 못할 어른이시오."], ["We must not, however, allow the man to preach again here.", "그러나 우리는 이 사람이 여기서 다시 설교하도록 두어서는 안 되오."], ["It is not because his opinion on church matters may be different from ours--with that one would not quarrel.", "교회 문제에 관한 저자의 견해가 우리와 다를 수 있기 때문이 아니오. 그런 것을 두고서야 누가 다투겠소."], ["It is because he has purposely insulted us.", "저자가 일부러 우리를 모욕했기 때문이오."], ["When he went up into that pulpit last Sunday, his studied object was to give offence to men who had grown old in reverence of those things of which he dared to speak so slightingly. What!", "지난 주일 저 강단에 올라갔을 때 저자가 작정하고 노린 바는, 저가 감히 그토록 업신여겨 말한 그것들을 우러르며 늙어 온 사람들에게 모욕을 주자는 것이었소."], ["To come here a stranger, a young, unknown, and unfriended stranger, and tell us, in the name of the bishop his master, that we are ignorant of our duties, old-fashioned, and useless!", "아니, 이곳에 낯선 자로, 젊고 알려진 바 없고 연고도 없는 낯선 자로 와서, 제 주인인 주교의 이름을 팔아, 우리더러 제 직분도 모르고 케케묵었고 쓸모없다고 말하다니!"], ["I don't know whether most to admire his courage or his impudence!", "저자의 담력에 더 감탄해야 할지 뻔뻔함에 더 감탄해야 할지 나는 모르겠소!"], ["And one thing I will tell you: that sermon originated solely with the man himself.", "그리고 한 가지는 말해 두겠소. 그 설교는 오로지 저자 자신에게서 나온 것이오."], ["The bishop was no more a party to it than was the dean here.", "주교는 여기 계신 수석 사제님이 그러하듯 그 일에 아무 관여도 하지 않았소."], ["You all know how grieved I am to see a bishop in this diocese holding the latitudinarian ideas by which Dr. Proudie has made himself conspicuous.", "이 교구의 주교가 프라우디 박사로 하여금 세간의 눈에 띄게 만든 그 관용주의 사상을 품고 있는 것을 보며 내가 얼마나 개탄하는지는 여러분 모두 아시오."], ["You all know how greatly I should distrust the opinion of such a man.", "그런 사람의 견해를 내가 얼마나 크게 불신하는지도 여러분 모두 아시오."], ["But in this matter I hold him to be blameless. I believe Dr.", "그러나 이 일에 관해서는 나는 그에게 아무 허물이 없다고 보오."], ["Proudie has lived too long among gentlemen to be guilty, or to instigate another to be guilty, of so gross an outrage.", "프라우디 박사는 신사들 틈에서 너무 오래 살아온 사람이라, 그토록 상스러운 행패를 스스로 저지르거나 남을 부추겨 저지르게 할 사람은 아니라고 나는 믿소. 아니오!"], ["No! That man uttered what was untrue when he hinted that he was speaking as the mouthpiece of the bishop.", "저 사람이 주교의 입을 대신하여 말하는 것처럼 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 그것은 사실이 아닌 말을 입에 올린 것이오."], ["It suited his ambitious views at once to throw down the gauntlet to us--at once to defy us here in the quiet of our own religious duties--here within the walls of our own loved cathedral--here where we have for so many years exercised our ministry without schism and with good repute.", "우리에게 대뜸 도전장을 던지는 것, 우리 자신의 종교적 직무의 고요함 속에서 — 우리가 사랑하는 우리 대성당의 벽 안에서 — 여러 해 동안 분열 없이 좋은 평판을 들으며 우리의 사목을 행해 온 바로 이곳에서 대뜸 우리에게 맞서는 것이 저자의 야심찬 속셈에 맞았던 것이오."], ["Such an attack upon us, coming from such a quarter, is abominable.\"", "그런 자리에서 나온 그런 공격은 가증스럽소.”"]], [["\"Abominable,\"", "“가증스럽소.”"], ["groaned the dean.", "수석 사제가 신음하듯 말했다."], ["\"Abominable,\"", "“가증스럽소.”"], ["muttered the meagre doctor.", "여윈 박사가 중얼거렸다."], ["\"Abominable,\"", "“가증스럽소.”"], ["re-echoed the chancellor, uttering the sound from the bottom of his deep chest.", "학무 참사원가 그 깊은 가슴 밑바닥에서 소리를 끌어내며 되받아 울렸다."], ["\"I really think it was,\"", "“정말 그랬던 것 같습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Most abominable and most unjustifiable,\"", "“더없이 가증스럽고 더없이 변명의 여지가 없는 일이오.”"], ["continued the archdeacon. \"But, Mr.", "부주교가 말을 이었다."], ["Dean, thank God, that pulpit is still our own: your own, I should say.", "“그러나 수석 사제님, 하느님께 감사하게도 저 강단은 아직 우리의 것이오. 아니, 사제님의 것이라 해야겠군요."], ["That pulpit belongs solely to the dean and chapter of Barchester Cathedral, and as yet Mr. Slope is no part of that chapter.", "저 강단은 오로지 바체스터 대성당의 수석 사제와 참사회에 속한 것이고, 슬로프 씨는 아직 그 참사회의 일원이 아니오."], ["You, Mr. Dean, have suggested that we should appeal to the bishop to abstain from forcing this man on us; but what if the bishop allow himself to be ruled by his chaplain?", "수석 사제님께서는 이 사람을 우리에게 떠맡기지 말아 달라고 주교에게 호소하자고 하셨소만, 주교가 제 전속 사제에게 휘둘리기로 든다면 어찌 되겠소?"], ["In my opinion the matter is in our own hands.", "내 생각에 이 일은 우리 손안에 있소."], ["Mr. Slope cannot preach there without permission asked and obtained, and let that permission be invariably refused.", "슬로프 씨는 허락을 청하여 얻지 않고서는 그곳에서 설교할 수 없으니, 그 허락을 한결같이 거절합시다."], ["Let all participation in the ministry of the cathedral service be refused to him.", "대성당 예배의 사목에 참여하는 일체를 저자에게 거절합시다."], ["Then, if the bishop choose to interfere, we shall know what answer to make to the bishop.", "그런 뒤에 주교가 굳이 끼어들겠다면, 우리는 주교에게 무어라 답해야 할지 알게 될 것이오."], ["My friend here has suggested that this man may again find his way into the pulpit by undertaking the duty of some of your minor canons, but I am sure that we may fully trust to these gentlemen to support us, when it is known that the dean objects to any such transfer.\"", "여기 계신 내 벗께서는 이 사람이 사제님 휘하 하급 참사원 가운데 누군가의 직무를 대신 맡는 식으로 다시 강단에 오를 길을 찾을지 모른다고 하셨소만, 수석 사제께서 그런 대리를 반대하신다는 것이 알려지기만 하면 그분들이 우리를 지지해 주리라고 나는 온전히 믿어도 좋다고 보오.”"]], [["\"Of course you may,\"", "“물론 그리하실 것이오.”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원가 말했다."]], [["There was much more discussion among the learned conclave, all of which, of course, ended in obedience to the archdeacon's commands.", "그 학식 있는 회의체 안에서는 훨씬 더 많은 논의가 오갔으나, 그 모든 것은 물론 부주교의 명령에 복종하는 것으로 끝났다."], ["They had too long been accustomed to his rule to shake it off so soon, and in this particular case they had none of them a wish to abet the man whom he was so anxious to put down.", "그들은 너무 오랫동안 그의 지배에 길들어 있어 그것을 그리 쉽게 떨쳐 낼 수 없었고, 더구나 이번 일에서는 그가 그토록 눌러 놓고자 하는 사람을 편들고 싶은 마음이 아무에게도 없었다."]], [["Such a meeting as that we have just recorded is not held in such a city as Barchester unknown and untold of.", "우리가 방금 기록한 것과 같은 모임이 바체스터 같은 도시에서 아무도 모르게, 아무 말도 없이 지나갈 리는 없다."], ["Not only was the fact of the meeting talked of in every respectable house, including the palace, but the very speeches of the dean, the archdeacon, and chancellor were repeated; not without many additions and imaginary circumstances, according to the tastes and opinions of the relaters.", "모임이 있었다는 사실이 주교관을 포함하여 점잖은 집집마다 입에 오른 것은 물론이요, 수석 사제와 부주교와 학무 참사원가 한 말까지 그대로 옮겨졌다 — 전하는 이들의 취향과 견해에 따라 덧붙임과 지어낸 정황이 적잖이 곁들여지기는 했지만."]], [["All, however, agreed in saying that Mr.", "그러나 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 대성당에서 입을 여는 것을 금지당하게 되었다는 데에는 모두의 말이 일치했다."], ["Slope was to be debarred from opening his mouth in the cathedral of Barchester; many believed that the vergers were to be ordered to refuse him even the accommodation of a seat; and some of the most far-going advocates for strong measures declared that his sermon was looked upon as an indictable offence, and that proceedings were to be taken against him for brawling.", "많은 이들은 성당지기들이 그에게 앉을 자리조차 내주지 말라는 지시를 받게 되었다고 믿었고, 강경책을 주장하는 이들 가운데 가장 멀리 나간 축은 그의 설교가 기소할 만한 죄로 여겨지고 있으며 교회 안에서 소란을 일으킨 죄로 그를 걸어 절차가 진행될 것이라고 단언했다."]], [["The party who were inclined to defend him--the enthusiastically religious young ladies and the middle-aged spinsters desirous of a move--of course took up his defence the more warmly on account of this attack.", "그를 두둔하려는 무리 — 종교에 열광하는 젊은 아가씨들과 무언가 변화를 바라는 중년의 노처녀들 — 는 물론 이 공격 때문에 한층 더 뜨겁게 그를 변호하고 나섰다."], ["If they could not hear Mr.", "대성당에서 슬로프 씨의 말을 들을 수 없다면 다른 데서라도 듣겠다는 것이었다."], ["Slope in the cathedral, they would hear him elsewhere; they would leave the dull dean, the dull old prebendaries, and the scarcely less dull young minor canons to preach to each other; they would work slippers and cushions and hem bands for Mr. Slope, make him a happy martyr, and stick him up in some new Sion or Bethesda, and put the cathedral quite out of fashion.", "따분한 수석 사제와 따분한 늙은 참사회원들과 그에 못지않게 따분한 젊은 하급 참사원들은 저희끼리 서로에게나 설교하라고 내버려 두고, 자기들은 슬로프 씨를 위해 실내화와 방석을 뜨고 띠에 단을 박아 주며, 그를 행복한 순교자로 만들어 어느 새로운 시온이나 베데스다에 모셔 세우고, 대성당은 아주 한물간 것으로 만들어 버리겠다는 것이었다."]], [["Dr. and Mrs.", "프라우디 박사 내외는 곧바로 런던으로 돌아갔다."], ["Proudie at once returned to London. They thought it expedient not to have to encounter any personal application from the dean and chapter respecting the sermon till the violence of the storm had expended itself; but they left Mr.", "폭풍의 기세가 다 소진될 때까지는 그 설교에 관하여 수석 사제와 참사회로부터 직접 항의를 받는 일이 없도록 하는 편이 상책이라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["Slope behind them nothing daunted, and he went about his work zealously, flattering such as would listen to his flattery, whispering religious twaddle into the ears of foolish women, ingratiating himself with the few clergy who would receive him, visiting the houses of the poor, inquiring into all people, prying into everything, and searching with his minutest eye into all palatial dilapidations.", "그러나 그들은 슬로프 씨를 뒤에 남겨 두었고, 그는 조금도 주눅 들지 않은 채 열심히 제 일을 해 나갔다. 제 아첨에 귀를 기울여 줄 만한 이들에게는 아첨하고, 어리석은 여자들의 귀에는 신앙을 빙자한 허튼소리를 속삭이고, 자기를 받아 줄 몇 안 되는 성직자들에게는 비위를 맞추고, 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아다니고, 온갖 사람을 캐묻고, 온갖 일에 코를 들이밀고, 주교관의 허물어진 구석구석을 그 지독히도 세밀한 눈으로 뒤졌다."], ["He did not, however, make any immediate attempt to preach again in the cathedral.", "그러나 당장에 대성당에서 다시 설교하려는 시도는 하지 않았다."]], [["And so all Barchester was by the ears.", "그리하여 바체스터 전체가 서로 으르렁거리게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. The Ex-warden Rejoices in His Probable Return to the Hospital", "# 제8장. 전임 워든이 요양원에 돌아갈 일을 기뻐하다"]], [["Among the ladies in Barchester who have hitherto acknowledged Mr. Slope as their spiritual director must not be reckoned either the Widow Bold or her sister-in-law.", "지금까지 바체스터에서 슬로프 씨를 제 영적 지도자로 인정한 부인들 가운데 미망인 볼드 부인과 그 시누이를 넣어서는 안 된다."], ["On the first outbreak of the wrath of the denizens of the close, none had been more animated against the intruder than these two ladies.", "대성당 경내 사람들의 분노가 처음 터져 나왔을 때, 그 침입자에게 이 두 부인만큼 격렬히 맞선 이도 없었다."], ["And this was natural.", "그것은 당연한 일이었다."], ["Who could be so proud of the musical distinction of their own cathedral as the favourite daughter of the precentor?", "선창자가 가장 아끼는 딸만큼 제 대성당의 음악적 명성을 자랑스러워할 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Who would be so likely to resent an insult offered to the old choir?", "오래된 성가대에 가해진 모욕을 그만큼 분하게 여길 사람이 또 누가 있겠는가?"], ["And in such matters Miss Bold and her sister-in-law had but one opinion.", "그리고 그런 문제에서 볼드 양과 그 올케는 의견이 하나였다."]], [["This wrath, however, has in some degree been mitigated, and I regret to say that these ladies allowed Mr. Slope to be his own apologist.", "그러나 이 분노는 어느 정도 누그러졌으니, 유감스럽게도 두 부인이 슬로프 씨로 하여금 스스로 변명하도록 내버려 두었다고 말해야겠다."], ["About a fortnight after the sermon had been preached, they were both of them not a little surprised by hearing Mr. Slope announced, as the page in buttons opened Mrs.", "그 설교가 있고 두 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 단추 달린 제복을 입은 사환 아이가 볼드 부인의 응접실 문을 열며 슬로프 씨의 방문을 알리자 두 사람은 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Bold's drawing-room door. Indeed, what living man could, by a mere morning visit, have surprised them more?", "아닌 게 아니라 살아 있는 사람 가운데 아침 방문 하나로 그들을 이보다 더 놀라게 할 자가 누가 있었겠는가?"], ["Here was the great enemy of all that was good in Barchester coming into their own drawing-room, and they had no strong arm, no ready tongue, near at hand for their protection.", "바체스터의 선한 모든 것에 맞선 대적이 자기들 응접실로 들어서는데, 곁에는 그들을 지켜 줄 억센 팔도 재빠른 혀도 없었다."], ["The widow snatched her baby out of its cradle into her lap, and Mary Bold stood up ready to die manfully in that baby's behalf, should, under any circumstances, such a sacrifice become necessary.", "미망인은 요람에 있던 아기를 낚아채 무릎에 앉혔고, 메리 볼드는 어떤 사정으로든 그런 희생이 필요해진다면 그 아기를 위해 사내답게 죽을 각오로 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["In this manner was Mr. Slope received.", "슬로프 씨는 그렇게 맞아들여졌다."], ["But when he left, he was allowed by each lady to take her hand and to make his adieux as gentlemen do who have been graciously entertained!", "그러나 그가 물러갈 때에는 두 부인 모두 그가 제 손을 잡도록 허락했고, 그는 융숭한 대접을 받은 신사가 그러하듯 작별 인사를 했다!"], ["Yes, he shook hands with them, and was curtseyed out courteously, the buttoned page opening the door as he would have done for the best canon of them all.", "그렇다, 그는 그들과 악수를 나누었고 정중한 무릎 인사를 받으며 배웅되었으며, 단추 달린 사환 아이는 참사원 가운데 가장 훌륭한 이에게 하듯 문을 열어 주었다."], ["He had touched the baby's little hand and blessed him with a fervid blessing; he had spoken to the widow of her early sorrows, and Eleanor's silent tears had not rebuked him; he had told Mary Bold that her devotion would be rewarded, and Mary Bold had heard the praise without disgust.", "그는 아기의 작은 손을 만지며 뜨거운 축복을 빌어 주었고, 미망인에게 일찍 찾아온 슬픔을 이야기했으나 엘리너의 말없는 눈물은 그를 나무라지 않았으며, 메리 볼드에게는 그 헌신이 언젠가 보답을 받으리라 말했건만 메리 볼드는 그 칭찬을 역겨워하지 않고 들었다."], ["And how had he done all this?", "그는 이 모든 일을 어떻게 해냈는가?"], ["How had he so quickly turned aversion into, at any rate, acquaintance?", "어떻게 그토록 빨리 반감을 적어도 안면 정도로는 바꾸어 놓았는가?"], ["How had he over-come the enmity with which these ladies had been ready to receive him, and made his peace with them so easily?", "이 부인들이 그를 맞으려 벼르고 있던 적의를 어떻게 이겨 내고 그리도 손쉽게 화해했는가?"]], [["My readers will guess from what I have written that I myself do not like Mr.", "내가 여기까지 쓴 것으로 미루어 독자들은 나 자신이 슬로프 씨를 좋아하지 않는다고 짐작할 것이다. 그러나 그가 재주 있는 사람이라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["Slope, but I am constrained to admit that he is a man of parts.", "그는 알맞은 자리에 부드러운 말 한마디를 놓을 줄 안다."], ["He knows how to say a soft word in the proper place; he knows how to adapt his flattery to the ears of his hearers; he knows the wiles of the serpent, and he uses them.", "듣는 이의 귀에 맞추어 아첨을 매만질 줄 안다. 뱀의 간계를 알고, 또 그것을 쓴다."], ["Could Mr. Slope have adapted his manners to men as well as to women, could he ever have learnt the ways of a gentleman, he might have risen to great things.", "슬로프 씨가 여자를 대하듯 남자도 대할 줄 알았더라면, 신사의 몸가짐을 끝내 익힐 수 있었더라면, 그는 크게 되었을지도 모른다."]], [["He commenced his acquaintance with Eleanor by praising her father.", "그는 엘리너의 아버지를 칭송하는 것으로 그녀와의 교분을 텄다."], ["He had, he said, become aware that he had unfortunately offended the feelings of a man of whom he could not speak too highly; he would not now allude to a subject which was probably too serious for drawing-room conversation, but he would say that it had been very far from him to utter a word in disparagement of a man of whom all the world, at least the clerical world, spoke so highly as it did of Mr.", "아무리 높이 말해도 모자랄 분의 마음을 불행히도 상하게 했음을 알게 되었노라고 그는 말했다. 응접실에서 나눌 이야기로는 아마 너무 무거운 주제일 터이니 지금은 언급하지 않겠으나, 온 세상이, 적어도 성직자의 세상이 하딩 씨를 두고 그토록 높이 말하는 그런 분을 깎아내릴 말을 할 생각은 자기에게 결코 없었노라고 했다."], ["Harding. And so he went on, unsaying a great deal of his sermon, expressing his highest admiration for the precentor's musical talents, eulogizing the father and the daughter and the sister-in-law, speaking in that low silky whisper which he always had specially prepared for feminine ears, and, ultimately, gaining his object.", "그런 식으로 그는 제 설교의 상당 부분을 도로 거두어들이면서 선창자의 음악적 재능에 더없는 감탄을 늘어놓고, 아버지와 딸과 시누이를 두루 기리며, 여자의 귀를 위해 늘 따로 마련해 두는 그 낮고 매끄러운 속삭임으로 이야기했고, 마침내 목적을 이루었다."], ["When he left, he expressed a hope that he might again be allowed to call; and though Eleanor gave no verbal assent to this, she did not express dissent: and so Mr. Slope's right to visit at the widow's house was established.", "물러가면서 그는 다시 찾아뵈어도 좋겠느냐는 뜻을 비쳤고, 엘리너가 말로 승낙하지는 않았으나 싫다는 뜻도 내비치지 않았으니, 그렇게 하여 미망인의 집을 드나들 슬로프 씨의 권리가 세워졌다."]], [["The day after this visit Eleanor told her father of it and expressed an opinion that Mr. Slope was not quite so black as he had been painted.", "그 방문이 있은 다음 날 엘리너는 아버지에게 그 일을 이야기하며, 슬로프 씨가 남들이 칠해 놓은 것만큼 그리 시커먼 사람은 아닌 듯하다는 뜻을 말했다."], ["Mr. Harding opened his eyes rather wider than usual when he heard what had occurred, but he said little; he could not agree in any praise of Mr.", "하딩 씨는 무슨 일이 있었는지 듣고 평소보다 눈을 조금 크게 떴으나 별말은 하지 않았다."], ["Slope, and it was not his practice to say much evil of anyone.", "그는 슬로프 씨를 칭찬하는 어떤 말에도 동의할 수 없었고, 그렇다고 누구의 험담을 길게 늘어놓는 것도 그의 버릇이 아니었다."], ["He did not, however, like the visit, and simple-minded as he was, he felt sure that Mr. Slope had some deeper motive than the mere pleasure of making soft speeches to two ladies.", "그러나 그는 그 방문이 마음에 들지 않았고, 사람이 순박하기는 해도 슬로프 씨에게 두 부인에게 부드러운 말을 건네는 즐거움 말고 무언가 더 깊은 속셈이 있으리라는 것만은 분명히 느꼈다."]], [["Mr. Harding, however, had come to see his daughter with other purpose than that of speaking either good or evil of Mr.", "그러나 하딩 씨가 딸을 보러 온 것은 슬로프 씨를 좋게 말하려는 것도 나쁘게 말하려는 것도 아닌 다른 볼일 때문이었다."], ["Slope. He had come to tell her that the place of warden in Hiram's Hospital was again to be filled up, and that in all probability he would once more return to his old home and his twelve bedesmen.", "그는 히램 요양원의 워든 자리가 다시 채워지게 되었으며 십중팔구 자신이 옛집과 열두 구호 노인에게로 다시 돌아가게 되리라는 소식을 딸에게 전하러 온 것이었다."]], [["\"But,\"", "“그런데 말이다.”"], ["said he, laughing, \"I shall be greatly shorn of my ancient glory.\"", "그가 웃으며 말했다. “예전의 영화는 크게 깎여 나가게 되었단다.”"]], [["\"Why so, Papa?\"", "“어째서요, 아버지?”"]], [["\"This new act of Parliament that is to put us all on our feet again,\"", "“우리를 모두 다시 일으켜 세워 준다는 그 새 의회 법령 말이다.”"], ["continued he, \"settles my income at four hundred and fifty pounds per annum.\"", "그가 말을 이었다. “그게 내 수입을 한 해 450파운드로 정해 놓았구나.”"]], [["\"Four hundred and fifty,\"", "“450파운드라니요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"instead of eight hundred!", "“800파운드였는데 그것밖에 안 된다니!"], ["Well, that is rather shabby.", "그건 좀 인색하네요."], ["But still, Papa, you'll have the dear old house and the garden?\"", "그래도 아버지, 그 정겨운 옛집과 정원은 그대로 갖게 되시는 거죠?”"]], [["\"My dear,\"", "“얘야.”"], ["said he, \"it's worth twice the money;\"", "그가 말했다. “그 집은 그 돈의 갑절 값어치가 있단다.”"], ["and as he spoke he showed a jaunty kind of satisfaction in his tone and manner and in the quick, pleasant way in which he paced Eleanor's drawing-room.", "그렇게 말하는 그의 말투와 몸짓에는, 그리고 엘리너의 응접실을 경쾌하고 기분 좋게 오가는 걸음걸이에는 어딘가 들뜬 만족감이 배어 있었다."], ["\"It's worth twice the money.", "“그 돈의 갑절 값어치야."], ["I shall have the house and the garden and a larger income than I can possibly want.\"", "나는 그 집과 정원을 얻을 테고, 수입도 내가 쓰고 싶은 것보다 많을 게다.”"]], [["\"At any rate, you'll have no extravagant daughter to provide for;\"", "“어차피 헤프게 쓰는 딸을 건사하실 일도 없잖아요.”"], ["and as she spoke, the young widow put her arm within his, and made him sit on the sofa beside her; \"at any rate, you'll not have that expense.\"", "그렇게 말하며 젊은 미망인은 아버지의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 그를 곁의 소파에 앉혔다. “적어도 그 비용은 안 드실 테니까요.”"]], [["\"No, my dear, and I shall be rather lonely without her; but we won't think of that now.", "“그렇지, 얘야. 그리고 그 애가 없으니 나는 좀 외롭겠지."], ["As regards income, I shall have plenty for all I want.", "하지만 지금은 그런 생각일랑 하지 말자꾸나. 수입으로 말하자면 내가 바라는 모든 것에 넉넉하고도 남는다."], ["I shall have my old house, and I don't mind owning now that I have felt sometimes the inconvenience of living in a lodging.", "옛집도 다시 갖게 될 테고. 이제야 하는 말이다만, 셋방살이가 불편하다고 느낀 적이 이따금 있었단다."], ["Lodgings are very nice for young men, but at my time of life there is a want of--I hardly know what to call it, perhaps not respectability--\"", "셋방은 젊은 사람에게야 아주 좋지. 그러나 내 나이쯤 되면 뭐랄까, 무어라 불러야 할지 모르겠구나, 체면이라고까지 할 것은 아닐지 몰라도…”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa!", "“어머, 아버지!"], ["I'm sure there's been nothing like that.", "그런 일은 결코 없었어요."], ["Nobody has thought it; nobody in all Barchester has been more respected than you have been since you took those rooms in High Street.", "아무도 그렇게 생각하지 않았는걸요. 아버지가 하이 스트리트에 그 방을 얻으신 뒤로 바체스터에서 아버지보다 더 존경받은 분은 없어요."], ["Nobody!", "아무도요!"], ["Not the dean in his deanery, or the archdeacon out at Plumstead.\"", "수석사제관의 수석 사제님도, 저 플럼스테드의 부주교님도 그렇지 못했어요.”"]], [["\"The archdeacon would not be much obliged to you if he heard you,\"", "“부주교가 그 말을 들었으면 너를 그리 고맙게 여기지는 않겠구나.”"], ["said he, smiling somewhat at the exclusive manner in which his daughter confined her illustration to the church dignitaries of the chapter of Barchester; \"but at any rate I shall be glad to get back to the old house.", "딸이 예로 드는 사람을 바체스터 참사회의 고위 성직자들로만 한정하는 것이 우스워 그는 슬며시 웃으며 말했다. “어쨌든 옛집으로 돌아가게 되어 나는 기쁘다."], ["Since I heard that it was all settled, I have begun to fancy that I can't be comfortable without my two sitting-rooms.\"", "일이 다 정해졌다는 말을 들은 뒤로는 거실이 두 칸은 있어야 편하겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했으니 말이다.”"]], [["\"Come and stay with me, Papa, till it is settled--there's a dear Papa.\"", "“일이 정해질 때까지 저와 함께 지내세요, 아버지. 그래 주세요, 네?”"]], [["\"Thank ye, Nelly.", "“고맙다, 넬리."], ["But no, I won't do that.", "하지만 그러지 않으련다."], ["It would make two movings.", "그러면 짐을 두 번 옮겨야 하지 않겠니."], ["I shall be very glad to get back to my old men again.", "나는 그 노인들에게 다시 돌아가는 것이 무척 기쁘구나."], ["Alas! alas!", "아아, 저런!"], ["There have six of them gone in these few last years.", "지난 몇 해 사이에 여섯이 세상을 떠났단다."], ["Six out of twelve!", "열둘 가운데 여섯이!"], ["And the others I fear have had but a sorry life of it there.", "남은 이들도 거기서 고단하게 지냈을 게다."], ["Poor Bunce, poor old Bunce!\"", "가엾은 번스, 가엾은 늙은 번스!”"]], [["Bunce was one of the surviving recipients of Hiram's charity, an old man, now over ninety, who had long been a favourite of Mr. Harding's.", "번스는 히램의 자선을 받는 이들 가운데 아직 살아 있는 사람으로, 이제 아흔이 넘은 노인이었고 오래도록 하딩 씨가 아끼던 이였다."]], [["\"How happy old Bunce will be,\"", "“번스 노인이 얼마나 기뻐할까요.”"], ["said Mrs. Bold, clapping her soft hands softly.", "볼드 부인이 부드러운 두 손을 가만히 마주치며 말했다."], ["\"How happy they all will be to have you back again.", "“아버지가 돌아오신다니 그분들 모두 얼마나 기뻐할까요."], ["You may be sure there will soon be friendship among them again when you are there.\"", "아버지가 계시면 그분들 사이에도 곧 다시 정이 돌 거예요.”"]], [["\"But,\"", "“그런데 말이다.”"], ["said he, half-laughing, \"I am to have new troubles, which will be terrible to me.", "그가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다. “나한테 무서운 새 골칫거리가 생기게 되었구나."], ["There are to be twelve old women, and a matron.", "노파 열두 명에 여사감이 하나 온다는구나."], ["How shall I manage twelve women and a matron!\"", "여자 열둘에 여사감까지 내가 어떻게 감당한단 말이냐!”"]], [["\"The matron will manage the women, of course.\"", "“여사감이 노파들을 감당하겠지요, 당연히.”"]], [["\"And who'll manage the matron?\"", "“그러면 여사감은 누가 감당하고?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"She won't want to be managed.", "“그분은 감당해 줄 사람이 필요 없을 거예요."], ["She'll be a great lady herself, I suppose.", "스스로 대단한 마님이 되실 테니까요."], ["But, Papa, where will the matron live?", "그런데 아버지, 여사감은 어디서 살아요?"], ["She is not to live in the warden's house with you, is she?\"", "설마 아버지와 함께 워든 사택에서 사는 건 아니겠죠?”"]], [["\"Well, I hope not, my dear.\"", "“그러지 않기를 바란다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, I tell you fairly, I won't have a matron for a new stepmother.\"", "“어머, 아버지, 저는 분명히 말씀드려요. 여사감을 새어머니로 맞을 생각은 없어요.”"]], [["\"You shan't, my dear; that is, if I can help it.", "“그럴 일은 없을 게다, 얘야."], ["But they are going to build another house for the matron and the women, and I believe they haven't even fixed yet on the site of the building.\"", "적어도 내가 어찌해 볼 수 있는 한은 말이지. 여사감과 노파들이 지낼 집은 따로 짓는다고 하는데, 아직 그 건물을 어디 세울지도 정하지 못한 모양이더구나.”"]], [["\"And have they appointed the matron?\"", "“그러면 여사감은 정해졌대요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"They haven't appointed the warden yet,\"", "“워든도 아직 정하지 못했단다.”"], ["replied he.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["\"But there's no doubt about that, I suppose,\"", "“하지만 그건 의심할 여지가 없겠죠?”"], ["said his daughter.", "딸이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Harding explained that he thought there was no doubt; that the archdeacon had declared as much, saying that the bishop and his chaplain between them had not the power to appoint anyone else, even if they had the will to do so, and sufficient impudence to carry out such a will.", "하딩 씨는 의심할 여지가 없다고 생각한다며 그 까닭을 설명했다."], ["The archdeacon was of opinion that, though Mr.", "부주교가 그렇게까지 단언했다는 것이다."], ["Harding had resigned his wardenship, and had done so unconditionally, he had done so under circumstances which left the bishop no choice as to his reappointment, now that the affair of the hospital had been settled on a new basis by act of Parliament.", "주교와 그 전속 사제가 설령 다른 사람을 앉힐 뜻이 있고 그 뜻을 밀어붙일 만한 뻔뻔함이 있다 한들, 둘이 힘을 합쳐도 그럴 권한은 없다는 것이었다. 부주교의 견해로는, 하딩 씨가 워든 직을 내놓았고 그것도 아무 조건 없이 내놓았다고는 하나 그때의 사정으로 보아, 이제 요양원 문제가 의회 법령으로 새롭게 정리된 마당에 주교로서는 그를 다시 임명하는 것 말고 달리 택할 길이 없다는 것이었다."], ["Such was the archdeacon's opinion, and his father-in-law received it without a shadow of doubt.", "부주교의 의견이 그러했고, 그의 장인은 한 점 의심 없이 그것을 받아들였다."]], [["Dr. Grantly had always been strongly opposed to Mr.", "그랜틀리 박사는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 내놓는 데 처음부터 완강히 반대해 왔다."], ["Harding's resignation of the place. He had done all in his power to dissuade him from it.", "그만두지 말라고 온 힘을 다해 말렸다."], ["He had considered that Mr. Harding was bound to withstand the popular clamour with which he was attacked for receiving so large an income as eight hundred a year from such a charity, and was not even yet satisfied that his father-in-law's conduct had not been pusillanimous and undignified.", "그런 자선 기금에서 한 해 800파운드나 되는 큰 수입을 받는다고 세상이 떠들어 대며 공격할 때 하딩 씨는 마땅히 그 소란을 버텨 냈어야 한다는 것이 그의 생각이었고, 장인의 처신이 비겁하고 체통 없는 짓이 아니었다는 데에는 아직까지도 마음이 풀리지 않았다."], ["He looked also on this reduction of the warden's income as a shabby, paltry scheme on the part of government for escaping from a difficulty into which it had been brought by the public press.", "그는 또한 워든의 수입을 이렇게 깎은 것을, 신문이 몰아넣은 궁지에서 빠져나오려는 정부의 인색하고 옹색한 술책으로 보았다."], ["Dr. Grantly observed that the government had no more right to dispose of a sum of four hundred and fifty pounds a year out of the income of Hiram's legacy than of nine hundred; whereas, as he said, the bishop, dean, and chapter clearly had a right to settle what sum should be paid.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말하기를, 정부에게는 히램의 유산에서 나오는 수입 가운데 한 해 900파운드를 처분할 권리가 없듯 450파운드를 처분할 권리도 없으며, 얼마를 지급할지 정할 권리는 분명 주교와 수석 사제와 참사회에 있다는 것이었다."], ["He also declared that the government had no more right to saddle the charity with twelve old women than with twelve hundred; and he was, therefore, very indignant on the matter.", "또 그는 정부에게 그 자선 시설에 노파 1,200명을 떠맡길 권리가 없듯 열두 명을 떠맡길 권리도 없다고 단언했으며, 그러므로 이 일에 몹시 분개했다."], ["He probably forgot when so talking that government had done nothing of the kind, and had never assumed any such might or any such right.", "그렇게 말할 때 그는 아마 잊고 있었을 것이다. 정부는 그런 짓을 한 적이 없고 그런 권세도 그런 권리도 자처한 적이 없다는 것을."], ["He made the common mistake of attributing to the government, which in such matters is powerless, the doings of Parliament, which in such matters is omnipotent.", "그는 흔히들 저지르는 착각을 했으니, 그런 일에서는 아무 힘도 없는 정부에게, 그런 일에서 전능한 의회가 한 일을 뒤집어씌운 것이었다."]], [["But though he felt that the glory and honour of the situation of warden of Barchester Hospital were indeed curtailed by the new arrangement; that the whole establishment had to a certain degree been made vile by the touch of Whig commissioners; that the place, with its lessened income, its old women, and other innovations, was very different from the hospital of former days; still the archdeacon was too practical a man of the world to wish that his father-in-law, who had at present little more than £200 per annum for all his wants, should refuse the situation, defiled, undignified, and commission-ridden as it was.", "그러나 새 조치로 바체스터 요양원 워든 자리의 영광과 명예가 실로 깎여 나갔다고, 그 시설 전체가 휘그당 위원들의 손이 닿아 어느 정도 천해졌다고, 줄어든 수입에 노파들에 그 밖의 새 제도까지 딸린 그 자리가 예전의 요양원과는 아주 딴판이 되었다고 느끼면서도, 부주교는 세상 물정에 밝은 사람인지라, 지금 온갖 씀씀이를 통틀어 한 해 200파운드 남짓밖에 없는 장인이 아무리 더럽혀지고 체통 없고 위원회에 휘둘리는 자리라 한들 그것을 마다하기를 바랄 수는 없었다."]], [["Mr. Harding had, accordingly, made up his mind that he would return to his old home at the hospital, and, to tell the truth, had experienced almost a childish pleasure in the idea of doing so.", "그리하여 하딩 씨는 요양원의 옛집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 정했고, 사실대로 말하자면 그리하게 되었다는 생각에 거의 어린아이 같은 기쁨을 맛보고 있었다."], ["The diminished income was to him not even the source of momentary regret.", "줄어든 수입은 그에게 잠깐의 아쉬움거리조차 되지 못했다."], ["The matron and the old women did rather go against the grain, but he was able to console himself with the reflection that, after all, such an arrangement might be of real service to the poor of the city.", "여사감과 노파들은 아무래도 마음에 걸렸으나, 그런 조치가 결국 이 도시의 가난한 이들에게 참으로 도움이 될지도 모른다는 생각으로 스스로를 달랠 수 있었다."], ["The thought that he must receive his reappointment as the gift of the new bishop, and probably through the hands of Mr. Slope, annoyed him a little, but his mind was set at rest by the assurance of the archdeacon that there would be no favour in such a presentation.", "자신의 재임명을 새 주교가 내리는 선물처럼, 그것도 아마 슬로프 씨의 손을 거쳐 받아야 한다는 생각은 그를 조금 언짢게 했으나, 그런 임명에는 은전이랄 것이 조금도 없다는 부주교의 장담에 마음이 놓였다."], ["The reappointment of the old warden would be regarded by all the world as a matter of course.", "옛 워든을 다시 임명하는 일은 온 세상이 당연한 일로 여길 터였다."], ["Mr. Harding, therefore, felt no hesitation in telling his daughter that they might look upon his return to his old quarters as a settled matter.", "그리하여 하딩 씨는 자신이 옛 거처로 돌아가는 것을 정해진 일로 보아도 좋다고 딸에게 말하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었다."]], [["\"And you won't have to ask for it, Papa?\"", "“그러면 아버지가 청하실 일은 없는 거죠?”"]], [["\"Certainly not, my dear.", "“물론 없다, 얘야."], ["There is no ground on which I could ask for any favour from the bishop, whom, indeed, I hardly know.", "주교에게 무슨 은전을 청할 만한 근거도 내겐 없거니와, 사실 나는 그분을 거의 알지도 못한다. 그리고 베풀고 말고를 슬로프 씨가 판가름할 수도 있는 그런 은전이라면 나는 청하지 않겠다."], ["Nor would I ask a favour, the granting of which might possibly be made a question to be settled by Mr.", "아니고말고.” 그는 잠시 제 성품과는 사뭇 다른 기운에 사로잡혀 말했다."], ["Slope. No,\"", "“요양원으로 돌아가게 된다면 나야 참으로 기쁘겠지."], ["said he, moved for a moment by a spirit very unlike his own, \"I certainly shall be very glad to go back to the hospital; but I should never go there if it were necessary that my doing so should be the subject of a request to Mr. Slope.\"", "하지만 그러기 위해 슬로프 씨에게 청을 넣어야 한다면 나는 결코 그리로 가지 않을 게다.”"]], [["This little outbreak of her father's anger jarred on the present tone of Eleanor's mind.", "아버지가 잠깐 내비친 이 노여움은 지금 엘리너의 마음결에 거슬렸다."], ["She had not learnt to like Mr. Slope, but she had learnt to think that he had much respect for her father; and she would, therefore, willingly use her efforts to induce something like good feeling between them.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨를 좋아하게 된 것은 아니었으나 그가 제 아버지를 무척 존경한다고 여기게 되었고, 그래서 두 사람 사이에 좋은 감정 비슷한 것이라도 생기게 하려고 기꺼이 애를 쓰려 했다."]], [["\"Papa,\"", "“아버지.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"I think you somewhat mistake Mr. Slope's character.\"", "“아버지는 슬로프 씨의 사람됨을 조금 잘못 보고 계신 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"Do I?\"", "“그러냐?”"], ["said he placidly.", "그가 잔잔하게 말했다."]], [["\"I think you do, Papa.", "“그런 것 같아요, 아버지."], ["I think he intended no personal disrespect to you when he preached the sermon which made the archdeacon and the dean so angry!\"", "부주교님과 수석 사제님을 그토록 노엽게 한 그 설교를 할 때에도 그분은 아버지 개인을 업신여길 뜻은 없었다고 생각해요!”"]], [["\"I never supposed he did, my dear.", "“나도 그가 그랬으리라고는 생각한 적이 없다, 얘야."], ["I hope I never inquired within myself whether he did or no.", "그랬는지 안 그랬는지 마음속으로 따져 본 적조차 없기를 바란다."], ["Such a matter would be unworthy of any inquiry, and very unworthy of the consideration of the chapter.", "그런 일은 따져 볼 값어치도 없거니와 참사회가 마음 쓸 값어치는 더더욱 없지."], ["But I fear he intended disrespect to the ministration of God's services, as conducted in conformity with the rules of the Church of England.\"", "다만 그가 잉글랜드 국교회의 법도에 따라 행해지는 하느님의 예배를 업신여길 뜻은 품었을까, 그것이 걱정이구나.”"]], [["\"But might it not be that he thought it his duty to express his dissent from that which you, and the dean, and all of us here so much approve?\"", "“하지만 아버지와 수석 사제님과 여기 우리 모두가 그토록 옳게 여기는 것에 대해, 그분은 자기 생각이 다르다고 밝히는 것이 도리라고 여겼을 수도 있지 않을까요?”"]], [["\"It can hardly be the duty of a young man rudely to assail the religious convictions of his elders in the church.", "“교회에서 손윗사람들의 신앙적 소신을 젊은 사람이 무례하게 들이받는 것이 도리일 수는 없다."], ["Courtesy should have kept him silent, even if neither charity nor modesty could do so.\"", "사랑도 겸손도 그를 잠잠하게 하지 못했다면 예의만으로라도 잠잠하게 했어야 했지.”"]], [["\"But Mr. Slope would say that on such a subject the commands of his heavenly Master do not admit of his being silent.\"", "“그렇지만 슬로프 씨라면 그런 문제에서는 하늘에 계신 주인의 분부가 잠잠히 있는 것을 허락지 않는다고 말할 거예요.”"]], [["\"Nor of his being courteous, Eleanor?\"", "“예의를 지키는 것도 허락지 않는다더냐, 엘리너?”"]], [["\"He did not say that, Papa.\"", "“그분이 그렇게 말한 건 아니에요, 아버지.”"]], [["\"Believe me, my child, that Christian ministers are never called on by God's word to insult the convictions, or even the prejudices of their brethren, and that religion is at any rate not less susceptible of urbane and courteous conduct among men than any other study which men may take up.", "“내 말을 믿으렴, 얘야. 그리스도의 종은 형제들의 소신은 물론이요 그 편견조차 모욕하라고 하느님의 말씀에서 부름받은 일이 결코 없다."], ["I am sorry to say that I cannot defend Mr. Slope's sermon in the cathedral.", "그리고 신앙이란 사람이 붙잡을 수 있는 다른 어떤 공부 못지않게, 사람들 사이에서 점잖고 예의 바른 처신을 넉넉히 담아낼 수 있는 것이란다."], ["But come, my dear, put on your bonnet and let us walk round the dear old gardens at the hospital.", "유감스럽지만 나는 대성당에서 있었던 슬로프 씨의 설교를 두둔할 수가 없구나. 그러나 얘야, 이제 그만하고 모자를 쓰렴."], ["I have never yet had the heart to go beyond the courtyard since we left the place.", "요양원의 그 정겨운 옛 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아보자꾸나. 우리가 그곳을 떠난 뒤로 나는 아직 안뜰 너머로 발을 들일 엄두를 내지 못했단다."], ["Now I think I can venture to enter.\"", "이제는 들어가 볼 만하다는 생각이 드는구나.”"]], [["Eleanor rang the bell and gave a variety of imperative charges as to the welfare of the precious baby, whom, all but unwillingly, she was about to leave for an hour or so, and then sauntered forth with her father to revisit the old hospital.", "엘리너는 종을 울려, 한 시간 남짓 두고 나가야 하는 것이 못내 마음에 걸리는 그 소중한 아기를 어떻게 돌봐야 하는지 이런저런 당부를 단단히 이르고는, 아버지와 함께 옛 요양원을 다시 보러 어슬렁어슬렁 나섰다."], ["It had been forbidden ground to her as well as to him since the day on which they had walked forth together from its walls.", "그날 두 사람이 그 담장 안에서 나란히 걸어 나온 뒤로, 그곳은 아버지에게와 마찬가지로 그녀에게도 밟아서는 안 될 땅이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX. The Stanhope Family", "# 제9장. 스탠호프 일가"]], [["It is now three months since Dr. Proudie began his reign, and changes have already been effected in the diocese which show at least the energy of an active mind.", "프라우디 박사가 치세를 시작한 지도 어느덧 석 달이 지났고, 교구에는 이미 적어도 활동적인 정신의 기운만은 보여 주는 변화들이 이루어졌다."], ["Among other things absentee clergymen have been favoured with hints much too strong to be overlooked.", "그중에서도 자리를 비운 성직자들은 도저히 못 본 체할 수 없을 만큼 강한 암시를 받는 은혜를 입었다."], ["Poor dear old Bishop Grantly had on this matter been too lenient, and the archdeacon had never been inclined to be severe with those who were absent on reputable pretences, and who provided for their duties in a liberal way.", "가엾고 인자하던 늙은 그랜틀리 주교는 이 문제에 지나치게 관대했고, 부주교 역시 그럴듯한 구실로 자리를 비우면서 제 직무는 후하게 남에게 맡겨 두는 이들에게 엄격하게 굴 마음이 조금도 없었다."]], [["Among the greatest of the diocesan sinners in this respect was Dr. Vesey Stanhope.", "이 점에서 교구의 죄인들 가운데 으뜸가는 축에 드는 이가 바로 베시 스탠호프 박사였다."], ["Years had now passed since he had done a day's duty, and yet there was no reason against his doing duty except a want of inclination on his own part.", "그가 하루치 직무라도 수행한 지는 이미 여러 해가 지났으나, 그가 직무를 수행하지 못할 이유라고는 본인에게 그럴 마음이 없다는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다."], ["He held a prebendal stall in the diocese, one of the best residences in the close, and the two large rectories of Crabtree Canonicorum and Stogpingum.", "그는 이 교구에 참사회원 좌석 하나와 대성당 경내에서 가장 좋은 주택 하나, 그리고 크래브트리 캐노니코럼과 스톡핑엄이라는 두 개의 큰 교구 목사직을 보유하고 있었다."], ["Indeed, he had the cure of three parishes, for that of Eiderdown was joined to Stogpingum.", "아니, 실은 세 교구의 사목을 맡고 있었으니, 아이더다운이 스톡핑엄에 병합되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["He had resided in Italy for twelve years.", "그는 12년째 이탈리아에 살고 있었다."], ["His first going there had been attributed to a sore throat, and that sore throat, though never repeated in any violent manner, had stood him in such stead that it had enabled him to live in easy idleness ever since.", "처음 그리로 간 것은 인후염 때문이라고들 했는데, 그 인후염은 그 뒤로 한 번도 심하게 도진 적이 없으면서도 그에게 어찌나 요긴했던지, 덕분에 그는 그때부터 줄곧 편안한 무위 속에서 지낼 수 있었다."]], [["He had now been summoned home--not, indeed, with rough violence, or by any peremptory command, but by a mandate which he found himself unable to disregard.", "이제 그에게 귀국하라는 부름이 떨어졌다. 물론 거칠게 윽박지르는 방식도, 무슨 단호한 명령의 형태도 아니었으나, 그로서는 도저히 무시할 수 없는 통첩이었다."], ["Mr. Slope had written to him by the bishop's desire.", "슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 받들어 그에게 편지를 썼던 것이다."], ["In the first place, the bishop much wanted the valuable co-operation of Dr.", "우선 주교는 교구 안에서 베시 스탠호프 박사의 값진 협력을 몹시 바라고 있다고 했다."], ["Vesey Stanhope in the diocese; in the next, the bishop thought it his imperative duty to become personally acquainted with the most conspicuous of his diocesan clergy; then the bishop thought it essentially necessary for Dr. Stanhope's own interests that Dr.", "다음으로 주교는 교구에서 가장 이름난 성직자들과 몸소 안면을 트는 것이 자신의 지엄한 의무라 여긴다고 했다."], ["Stanhope should, at any rate for a time, return to Barchester; and lastly, it was said that so strong a feeling was at the present moment evinced by the hierarchs of the church with reference to the absence of its clerical members, that it behoved Dr.", "그다음으로 주교는 스탠호프 박사 자신의 이익을 위해서라도 박사가 적어도 얼마 동안은 바체스터로 돌아오는 것이 반드시 필요하다고 생각한다고 했다."], ["Vesey Stanhope not to allow his name to stand among those which would probably in a few months be submitted to the councils of the nation.", "그리고 마지막으로, 교회의 고위 성직자들이 성직자의 부재라는 문제를 두고 지금 대단히 강경한 감정을 드러내고 있으니, 베시 스탠호프 박사로서는 몇 달 안에 나라의 의회에 제출될 법한 명단에 제 이름이 오르도록 내버려 두지 않는 편이 마땅하겠다는 말이 있었다."]], [["There was something so ambiguously frightful in this last threat that Dr. Stanhope determined to spend two or three summer months at his residence in Barchester.", "이 마지막 위협에는 무언가 모호하면서도 섬뜩한 데가 있었으므로, 스탠호프 박사는 여름의 두세 달을 바체스터의 제 사택에서 보내기로 마음먹었다."], ["His rectories were inhabited by his curates, and he felt himself from disuse to be unfit for parochial duty; but his prebendal home was kept empty for him, and he thought it probable that he might be able now and again to preach a prebendal sermon.", "그의 두 목사관에는 부목사들이 살고 있었고, 그 자신은 오래 손을 놓은 탓에 교구 직무에는 맞지 않는 사람이 되었다고 느꼈다. 그러나 참사회원 사택만은 그를 위해 비워 둔 채였으니, 어쩌면 이따금 참사회원으로서 설교 한 편쯤은 할 수 있으리라 여겼다."], ["He arrived, therefore, with all his family at Barchester, and he and they must be introduced to my readers.", "그리하여 그는 온 식구를 데리고 바체스터에 당도했으며, 이제 그와 그 식구들을 독자 여러분께 소개해야겠다."]], [["The great family characteristic of the Stanhopes might probably be said to be heartlessness, but this want of feeling was, in most of them, accompanied by so great an amount of good nature as to make itself but little noticeable to the world.", "스탠호프 집안 사람들의 큰 공통점은 아마 무정함이라 해야 할 것이나, 이 감정의 결핍은 그들 대부분에게서 워낙 넉넉한 붙임성과 짝을 이루고 있었던 까닭에 세상 사람들 눈에는 별로 띄지 않았다."], ["They were so prone to oblige their neighbours that their neighbours failed to perceive how indifferent to them was the happiness and well-being of those around them.", "그들은 이웃의 편의를 봐주는 데 어찌나 선뜻했던지, 정작 이웃들은 주위 사람의 행복과 안녕이 그들에게 얼마나 아무래도 좋은 일인지를 알아채지 못했다."], ["The Stanhopes would visit you in your sickness (provided it were not contagious), would bring you oranges, French novels, and the last new bit of scandal, and then hear of your death or your recovery with an equally indifferent composure.", "스탠호프 집안 사람들은 당신이 앓아누우면 (전염병만 아니라면) 병문안을 와서 오렌지며 프랑스 소설이며 갓 나온 뒷소문 한 자락을 가져다줄 것이고, 그런 다음 당신이 죽었다는 소식이든 나았다는 소식이든 똑같이 무심한 낯빛으로 들을 것이다. 저희끼리 대하는 태도도 세상을 대하는 태도와 다르지 않았다."], ["Their conduct to each other was the same as to the world; they bore and forbore; and there was sometimes, as will be seen, much necessity for forbearing; but their love among themselves rarely reached above this.", "그들은 참았고 또 봐주었으며, 앞으로 보게 되듯 봐주어야 할 일이 적지 않을 때도 있었다. 그러나 저희끼리의 애정이라는 것도 그 이상으로 올라가는 법은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["It is astonishing how much each of the family was able to do, and how much each did, to prevent the well-being of the other four.", "이 집안의 사람들이 저마다 나머지 네 사람의 안녕을 가로막기 위해 할 수 있는 일이 얼마나 많았는지, 또 실제로 얼마나 했는지는 놀라울 지경이다."]], [["For there were five in all; the doctor, namely, and Mrs. Stanhope, two daughters, and one son.", "식구는 모두 다섯이었으니, 곧 박사와 스탠호프 부인, 두 딸과 아들 하나였다."], ["The doctor, perhaps, was the least singular and most estimable of them all, and yet such good qualities as he possessed were all negative.", "박사는 아마 그중 가장 덜 별나고 가장 존경할 만한 사람이었겠으나, 그가 지닌 장점이라는 것은 하나같이 소극적인 것들이었다."], ["He was a good-looking rather plethoric gentleman of about sixty years of age.", "그는 예순 남짓 된, 풍채가 좋고 다소 혈색이 붉은 신사였다."], ["His hair was snow-white, very plentiful, and somewhat like wool of the finest description.", "머리카락은 눈처럼 희고 숱이 아주 많았으며, 어딘가 더없이 고운 양털을 닮아 있었다."], ["His whiskers were very large and very white, and gave to his face the appearance of a benevolent, sleepy old lion.", "구레나룻은 무척 크고 무척 희어서 그 얼굴을 자애롭고 졸린 늙은 사자처럼 보이게 했다."], ["His dress was always unexceptionable.", "옷차림은 언제나 나무랄 데가 없었다."], ["Although he had lived so many years in Italy it was invariably of a decent clerical hue, but it never was hyperclerical.", "그토록 오랜 세월을 이탈리아에서 살았으면서도 그 옷은 한결같이 성직자다운 점잖은 빛깔이었고, 그러면서도 지나치게 성직자 티를 내는 법은 없었다."], ["He was a man not given to much talking, but what little he did say was generally well said.", "그는 말이 많은 사람이 아니었으나, 어쩌다 하는 그 몇 마디는 대개 잘 고른 말이었다."], ["His reading seldom went beyond romances and poetry of the lightest and not always most moral description.", "그의 독서는 좀처럼 연애소설과 시를 넘어서지 않았고, 그것도 가장 가벼운 축이었으며 늘 도덕적인 것도 아니었다."], ["He was thoroughly a bon vivant; an accomplished judge of wine, though he never drank to excess; and a most inexorable critic in all affairs touching the kitchen.", "그는 철저한 bon vivant였다. 술에 대해서는 조예 깊은 감식가이면서도 결코 과음하는 법이 없었고, 부엌에 관한 일이라면 무엇에도 추호의 용서가 없는 비평가였다."], ["He had had much to forgive in his own family, since a family had grown up around him, and had forgiven everything--except inattention to his dinner.", "슬하에 자식들이 자라난 뒤로 그는 제 집안에서 용서할 일이 많았고 또 모든 것을 용서해 왔다. 저녁 식사에 소홀한 것 하나만 빼고."], ["His weakness in that respect was now fully understood, and his temper but seldom tried. As Dr.", "그 대목에서의 그의 약점은 이제 온 식구가 훤히 알고 있었으므로, 그의 성미가 시험대에 오르는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Stanhope was a clergyman, it may be supposed that his religious convictions made up a considerable part of his character, but this was not so.", "스탠호프 박사가 성직자였으니 그의 종교적 신념이 그 인물됨의 적잖은 부분을 이루리라고 짐작할 법하지만, 실상은 그렇지 않았다."], ["That he had religious convictions must be believed, but he rarely obtruded them, even on his children.", "그에게 종교적 신념이 있었으리라는 것이야 믿어 주어야 하겠으나, 그는 그것을 좀처럼 남에게, 심지어 제 자식들에게조차 내세우지 않았다."], ["This abstinence on his part was not systematic, but very characteristic of the man.", "이렇게 삼가는 태도는 무슨 방침에서 나온 것이 아니라 그 사람됨을 그대로 드러내는 것이었다."], ["It was not that he had predetermined never to influence their thoughts, but he was so habitually idle that his time for doing so had never come till the opportunity for doing so was gone forever.", "자식들의 생각에 결코 영향을 끼치지 않겠다고 미리 정해 두었던 것이 아니라, 워낙 몸에 밴 게으름 탓에 그럴 때가 오기도 전에 그럴 기회가 영영 지나가 버렸던 것이다."], ["Whatever conviction the father may have had, the children were at any rate but indifferent members of the church from which he drew his income.", "아버지에게 어떤 신념이 있었든 간에, 자식들은 어쨌거나 아버지가 수입을 끌어내는 그 교회의 무심한 신자에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Such was Dr. Stanhope.", "스탠호프 박사는 그러한 사람이었다."], ["The features of Mrs. Stanhope's character were even less plainly marked than those of her lord.", "스탠호프 부인의 인물됨은 그 바깥양반보다도 윤곽이 더 흐릿했다."], ["The far niente of her Italian life had entered into her very soul, and brought her to regard a state of inactivity as the only earthly good.", "이탈리아 생활의 far niente가 그 영혼 속까지 스며들어, 부인은 아무것도 하지 않는 상태야말로 이 땅의 유일한 선이라 여기게 되었다."], ["In manner and appearance she was exceedingly prepossessing.", "태도와 용모는 대단히 호감을 주었다."], ["She had been a beauty, and even now, at fifty-five, she was a handsome woman.", "한때 미인이었고, 쉰다섯이 된 지금도 아름다운 여인이었다. 옷차림은 언제나 완벽했다."], ["Her dress was always perfect: she never dressed but once in the day, and never appeared till between three and four; but when she did appear, she appeared at her best.", "하루에 옷을 단 한 번만 갈아입었고 세 시에서 네 시 사이가 되기 전에는 결코 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 일단 나타났다 하면 가장 좋은 모습으로 나타났다."], ["Whether the toil rested partly with her, or wholly with her handmaid, it is not for such a one as the author even to imagine.", "그 수고가 얼마쯤은 부인 자신의 몫이었는지 아니면 온전히 하녀의 몫이었는지는, 글 쓰는 자 따위가 감히 짐작해 볼 바가 아니다."], ["The structure of her attire was always elaborate and yet never over-laboured.", "그 의상의 짜임새는 언제나 공들인 것이면서도 결코 지나치게 공들인 티가 나지 않았다."], ["She was rich in apparel but not bedizened with finery; her ornaments were costly, rare, and such as could not fail to attract notice, but they did not look as though worn with that purpose.", "옷감은 풍족했으나 장신구로 요란하지는 않았고, 패물은 값지고 진귀하여 사람들의 눈길을 끌지 않을 수 없는 것들이었지만 그러라고 걸친 것처럼 보이지는 않았다."], ["She well knew the great architectural secret of decorating her constructions, and never descended to construct a decoration.", "부인은 제가 세운 구조물을 꾸민다는 저 위대한 건축의 비결을 잘 알고 있었고, 장식을 짓는 데까지 내려가는 일은 결코 없었다."], ["But when we have said that Mrs. Stanhope knew how to dress and used her knowledge daily, we have said all.", "그러나 스탠호프 부인이 옷 입을 줄 알았고 그 앎을 날마다 써먹었다고 말하고 나면, 그로써 할 말은 다 한 셈이다."], ["Other purpose in life she had none.", "그 밖에 인생의 목적이라고는 없었다."], ["It was something, indeed, that she did not interfere with the purposes of others.", "하기야 남의 목적을 방해하지 않았다는 것만 해도 어디인가."], ["In early life she had undergone great trials with reference to the doctor's dinners, but for the last ten or twelve years her elder daughter Charlotte had taken that labour off her hands, and she had had little to trouble her--little, that is, till the edict for this terrible English journey had gone forth: since then, indeed, her life had been laborious enough.", "젊은 시절에는 박사의 저녁 식사 문제로 큰 시련을 겪었으나, 지난 10년 내지 12년 동안은 큰딸 샬럿이 그 수고를 대신 떠맡아 준 덕에 부인을 괴롭히는 일이라고는 거의 없었다. 거의 없었다는 것은 저 끔찍한 영국행 명령이 떨어지기 전까지의 이야기다."], ["For such a one, the toil of being carried from the shores of Como to the city of Barchester is more than labour enough, let the care of the carriers be ever so vigilant.", "그 뒤로는 과연 부인의 삶도 넉넉히 고달팠다. 그런 사람에게는 코모 호숫가에서 바체스터라는 도시까지 실려 가는 일이야말로 노역이라 하고도 남을 것이니, 나르는 이들이 아무리 조심스러웠다 한들 그렇다."], ["Mrs. Stanhope had been obliged to have every one of her dresses taken in from the effects of the journey.", "스탠호프 부인은 그 여행의 여파로 옷이란 옷은 죄다 품을 줄여야 했다."]], [["Charlotte Stanhope was at this time about thirty-five years old, and whatever may have been her faults, she had none of those which belong particularly to old young ladies.", "샬럿 스탠호프는 이때 서른다섯 살쯤이었는데, 그에게 어떤 결점이 있었든 간에 나이 든 처녀들에게 유독 따라붙는 결점만은 하나도 없었다."], ["She neither dressed young, nor talked young, nor indeed looked young.", "젊게 차려입지도, 젊게 말하지도, 실은 젊어 보이지도 않았다."], ["She appeared to be perfectly content with her time of life, and in no way affected the graces of youth.", "제 나이를 온전히 흡족해하는 듯했고 젊음의 우아함을 흉내 내는 구석이 조금도 없었다."], ["She was a fine young woman, and had she been a man, would have been a very fine young man.", "그는 잘생긴 젊은 여자였고, 만일 남자로 태어났더라면 아주 잘생긴 젊은 남자가 되었을 것이다."], ["All that was done in the house, and that was not done by servants, was done by her.", "집 안에서 이루어지는 일 가운데 하인들이 하지 않는 일은 죄다 그의 손으로 이루어졌다."], ["She gave the orders, paid the bills, hired and dismissed the domestics, made the tea, carved the meat, and managed everything in the Stanhope household.", "분부를 내리고, 셈을 치르고, 하인을 들이고 내보내고, 차를 끓이고, 고기를 썰고, 스탠호프 집안의 모든 일을 건사하는 것이 그였다."], ["She, and she alone, could ever induce her father to look into the state of his worldly concerns.", "아버지로 하여금 제 세상살이 형편을 들여다보게 만들 수 있는 사람은 오직 그 하나뿐이었다."], ["She, and she alone, could in any degree control the absurdities of her sister.", "동생의 터무니없는 짓을 그나마 조금이라도 억누를 수 있는 사람도 오직 그 하나뿐이었다."], ["She, and she alone, prevented the whole family from falling into utter disrepute and beggary.", "온 집안이 아주 몰락하여 거지 신세가 되는 것을 막은 사람도 오직 그 하나뿐이었다."], ["It was by her advice that they now found themselves very unpleasantly situated in Barchester.", "그들이 지금 바체스터에서 이토록 거북한 처지에 놓이게 된 것도 그의 조언에 따른 일이었다."]], [["So far, the character of Charlotte Stanhope is not unprepossessing.", "여기까지 보면 샬럿 스탠호프의 사람됨은 호감이 가지 않는 것도 아니다."], ["But it remains to be said that the influence which she had in her family, though it had been used to a certain extent for their worldly well-being, had not been used to their real benefit, as it might have been.", "그러나 덧붙여 말해 두어야 할 것이 있으니, 그가 집안에서 지닌 영향력은 어느 정도까지 식구들의 세속적 안녕을 위해 쓰이기는 했을지언정 마음만 먹었다면 쓸 수 있었을 만큼 그들의 참된 이익을 위해 쓰이지는 않았다는 점이다."], ["She had aided her father in his indifference to his professional duties, counselling him that his livings were as much his individual property as the estates of his elder brother were the property of that worthy peer.", "그는 아버지가 성직의 의무에 무심한 것을 거들었고, 성직록이란 형님 되는 그 훌륭한 귀족의 영지가 그 귀족의 재산인 것과 똑같이 아버지 개인의 재산이라고 일러 주었다."], ["She had for years past stifled every little rising wish for a return to England which the doctor had from time to time expressed.", "그는 박사가 이따금 내비치던, 영국으로 돌아가고 싶다는 자그마한 소망을 여러 해에 걸쳐 그때마다 눌러 버렸다."], ["She had encouraged her mother in her idleness, in order that she herself might be mistress and manager of the Stanhope household.", "어머니의 게으름은 부추겨 놓았으니, 그래야 스탠호프 집안의 안주인이자 살림꾼 노릇을 저 혼자 할 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["She had encouraged and fostered the follies of her sister, though she was always willing, and often able, to protect her from their probable result.", "동생의 어리석은 짓 또한 부추기고 감싸 주었다. 그러면서도 그 짓의 있을 법한 결말로부터 동생을 지켜 줄 마음은 늘 있었고 지켜 줄 힘도 대개 있었다."], ["She had done her best, and had thoroughly succeeded in spoiling her brother, and turning him loose upon the world an idle man without a profession and without a shilling that he could call his own.", "그리고 남동생을 버려 놓는 데에도 최선을 다하여 완전히 성공을 거두었으니, 직업도 없고 제 것이라 부를 만한 1실링도 없는 빈둥거리는 사내로 세상에 풀어놓은 것이다."]], [["Miss Stanhope was a clever woman, able to talk on most subjects, and quite indifferent as to what the subject was.", "스탠호프 양은 영리한 여자로, 웬만한 주제에 대해서는 다 이야기할 수 있었고 그 주제가 무엇이든 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["She prided herself on her freedom from English prejudice, and, she might have added, from feminine delicacy.", "그는 영국식 편견에서 자유롭다는 것을 자랑으로 삼았는데, 여기에 여자다운 조심성에서도 자유롭다는 말을 덧붙일 수 있었을 것이다."], ["On religion she was a pure free-thinker, and with much want of true affection, delighted to throw out her own views before the troubled mind of her father.", "종교에 관해서라면 그는 순전한 자유사상가였고, 참된 애정이 크게 모자란 탓에 아버지의 심란한 마음 앞에 제 견해를 늘어놓기를 즐겼다."], ["To have shaken what remained of his Church of England faith would have gratified her much, but the idea of his abandoning his preferment in the church had never once presented itself to her mind.", "아버지에게 남아 있는 영국국교회 신앙을 뒤흔들어 놓을 수 있었다면 그로서는 무척 흡족했겠지만, 아버지가 교회에서 받는 성직록을 내던진다는 생각만은 단 한 번도 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["How could he indeed, when he had no income from any other source?", "하기야 다른 데서 들어오는 수입이라고는 없는 터에 어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["But the two most prominent members of the family still remain to be described.", "그러나 이 집안에서 가장 두드러진 두 사람은 아직 그려 내지 못했다."], ["The second child had been christened Madeline and had been a great beauty.", "둘째 아이는 세례명을 마들린이라 하였고 대단한 미인이었다. 미인이었다고 할 것도 없다."], ["We need not say had been, for she was never more beautiful than at the time of which we write, though her person for many years had been disfigured by an accident.", "우리가 지금 쓰고 있는 이 시점만큼 그가 아름다웠던 적은 없었기 때문이다. 다만 그 몸만은 여러 해 전부터 사고로 망가져 있었다."], ["It is unnecessary that we should give in detail the early history of Madeline Stanhope.", "마들린 스탠호프의 지난 내력을 낱낱이 늘어놓을 필요는 없다."], ["She had gone to Italy when about seventeen years of age, and had been allowed to make the most of her surpassing beauty in the salons of Milan and among the crowded villas along the shores of the Lake of Como.", "그는 열일곱 살쯤에 이탈리아로 갔고, 밀라노의 살롱에서, 또 코모 호숫가에 빽빽이 늘어선 별장들 사이에서 그 빼어난 아름다움을 마음껏 써먹도록 허락받았다."], ["She had become famous for adventures in which her character was just not lost, and had destroyed the hearts of a dozen cavaliers without once being touched in her own.", "그는 아슬아슬하게 평판을 지켜 낸 숱한 모험으로 이름을 날렸고, 정작 제 마음은 한 번도 다치지 않은 채 열두 명이나 되는 기사들의 심장을 결딴내 놓았다."], ["Blood had flowed in quarrels about her charms, and she had heard of these encounters with pleasurable excitement.", "그의 매력을 두고 벌어진 다툼에서 피가 흘렀고, 그는 그런 결투의 소식을 즐거운 흥분과 함께 들었다."], ["It had been told of her that on one occasion she had stood by in the disguise of a page and had seen her lover fall.", "한번은 그가 시동으로 변장하고 곁에 서서 제 연인이 쓰러지는 것을 지켜보았다는 이야기까지 있었다."]], [["As is so often the case, she had married the very worst of those who sought her hand.", "흔히 그렇듯이, 그는 청혼자들 가운데 가장 못한 자와 결혼했다."], ["Why she had chosen Paulo Neroni, a man of no birth and no property, a mere captain in the Pope's guard, one who had come up to Milan either simply as an adventurer or else as a spy, a man of harsh temper and oily manners, mean in figure, swarthy in face, and so false in words as to be hourly detected, need not now be told.", "어째서 하필 파울로 네로니를 골랐는지는 지금 와서 이야기할 것도 없다. 가문도 재산도 없고 교황 근위대의 일개 대위에 지나지 않으며, 밀라노에 올라온 것이 그저 한몫 보러 온 것인지 아니면 밀정으로 온 것인지 알 수 없고, 성미는 사납고 태도는 느끼하며, 체구는 볼품없고 얼굴은 거무튀튀하고, 말이 어찌나 거짓되던지 시시각각 들통이 나던 그런 사내였다."], ["When the moment for doing so came, she had probably no alternative.", "그럴 순간이 닥쳤을 때 그에게는 아마 달리 길이 없었을 것이다."], ["He, at any rate, had become her husband, and after a prolonged honeymoon among the lakes, they had gone together to Rome, the papal captain having vainly endeavoured to induce his wife to remain behind him.", "어쨌든 그 사내는 그의 남편이 되었고, 호수 지방에서 길게 신혼을 보낸 뒤 둘은 함께 로마로 갔다. 교황의 대위는 아내를 뒤에 남겨 두려고 애썼으나 헛일이었다."]], [["Six months afterwards she arrived at her father's house a cripple, and a mother.", "여섯 달 뒤 그는 불구가 된 몸으로, 그리고 어머니가 되어 아버지의 집에 당도했다."], ["She had arrived without even notice, with hardly clothes to cover her, and without one of those many ornaments which had graced her bridal trousseau.", "기별 한 장 없이, 몸을 가릴 옷도 변변히 없이, 혼수를 빛내 주던 그 숱한 패물이라고는 하나도 지니지 않은 채였다."], ["Her baby was in the arms of a poor girl from Milan, whom she had taken in exchange for the Roman maid who had accompanied her thus far, and who had then, as her mistress said, become homesick and had returned.", "아기는 밀라노에서 데려온 가난한 처녀의 품에 안겨 있었는데, 거기까지 함께 온 로마 하녀와 맞바꾼 아이였다. 그 하녀로 말하면, 여주인 말로는 향수병이 나서 돌아갔다는 것이었다."], ["It was clear that the lady had determined that there should be no witness to tell stories of her life in Rome.", "이 부인이 제 로마 생활에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 이야기를 옮길 증인은 하나도 남기지 않겠다고 작정했음이 분명했다."]], [["She had fallen, she said, in ascending a ruin, and had fatally injured the sinews of her knee; so fatally that when she stood, she lost eight inches of her accustomed height; so fatally that when she essayed to move, she could only drag herself painfully along, with protruded hip and extended foot, in a manner less graceful than that of a hunchback.", "그의 말로는 폐허에 오르다가 넘어져 무릎 힘줄을 결딴나게 다쳤다고 했다. 어찌나 심하게 다쳤던지 일어서면 제 키에서 8인치가 줄었고, 어찌나 심하게 다쳤던지 몸을 옮기려 들면 엉덩이를 내밀고 발을 뻗은 채 곱사등이보다도 볼품없는 모습으로 고통스럽게 몸을 끌 수밖에 없었다."], ["She had consequently made up her mind, once and forever, that she would never stand and never attempt to move herself.", "그리하여 그는 두 번 다시 서지도 않고 스스로 몸을 옮기려 들지도 않겠다고 단번에 그리고 영영 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Stories were not slow to follow her, averring that she had been cruelly ill-used by Neroni, and that to his violence had she owed her accident.", "그를 뒤따라 이야기들이 도는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 네로니에게 모질게 학대를 당했으며 그 폭력 탓에 그런 사고를 당했다는 것이었다."], ["Be that as it may, little had been said about her husband, but that little had made it clearly intelligible to the family that Signor Neroni was to be seen and heard of no more.", "그야 어찌 되었든, 남편에 대해서는 별말이 없었으나 그 몇 마디만으로도 시뇨르 네로니를 다시 보거나 그 소식을 듣는 일은 없으리라는 것만은 집안 사람들에게 똑똑히 전해졌다."], ["There was no question as to readmitting the poor, ill-used beauty to her old family rights, no question as to adopting her infant daughter beneath the Stanhope roof-tree.", "가엾게 학대당한 이 미인을 옛 식구의 자리로 다시 받아들이는 데에도, 그 어린 딸을 스탠호프 집안의 지붕 아래 거두는 데에도 아무 이의가 없었다."], ["Though heartless, the Stanhopes were not selfish.", "스탠호프 집안 사람들은 무정하기는 해도 이기적이지는 않았다."], ["The two were taken in, petted, made much of, for a time all but adored, and then felt by the two parents to be great nuisances in the house.", "모녀는 받아들여져 귀염을 받고 극진한 대접을 받았으며 한동안은 거의 떠받들리다시피 하다가, 이윽고 두 부모에게는 집 안의 큰 골칫거리로 여겨지게 되었다."], ["But in the house the lady was, and there she remained, having her own way, though that way was not very conformable with the customary usages of an English clergyman.", "그러나 부인은 이미 그 집 안에 있었고 그대로 눌러앉아 제 뜻대로 살았다. 그 뜻이라는 것이 영국 성직자 집안의 관습과는 그리 어울리지 않았는데도 그러했다."]], [["Madame Neroni, though forced to give up all motion in the world, had no intention whatever of giving up the world itself.", "마담 네로니는 세상에서 몸을 움직이는 일이라면 죄다 포기할 수밖에 없었으나, 세상 자체를 포기할 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["The beauty of her face was uninjured, and that beauty was of a peculiar kind.", "얼굴의 아름다움은 조금도 상하지 않았고, 그 아름다움은 독특한 종류의 것이었다."], ["Her copious rich brown hair was worn in Grecian bandeaux round her head, displaying as much as possible of her forehead and cheeks.", "숱 많고 윤기 도는 갈색 머리는 그리스식으로 머리 둘레에 둘러 이마와 뺨을 될 수 있는 대로 많이 드러냈다."], ["Her forehead, though rather low, was very beautiful from its perfect contour and pearly whiteness.", "이마는 좀 낮은 편이었으나 완벽한 윤곽과 진주 같은 흰빛 덕에 무척 아름다웠다."], ["Her eyes were long and large, and marvellously bright; might I venture to say bright as Lucifer's, I should perhaps best express the depth of their brilliancy.", "눈은 길고 컸으며 놀랍도록 빛났으니, 루시퍼의 눈처럼 빛났다고 감히 말해도 된다면 그 광채의 깊이는 그렇게 말하는 것이 아마 가장 나으리라."], ["They were dreadful eyes to look at, such as would absolutely deter any man of quiet mind and easy spirit from attempting a passage of arms with such foes.", "그것은 마주 보기에 무서운 눈이어서, 마음이 조용하고 심성이 무던한 남자라면 그런 적수와 창검을 겨루어 볼 엄두조차 아예 못 내게 만들 그런 눈이었다."], ["There was talent in them, and the fire of passion and the play of wit, but there was no love.", "그 안에는 재기가 있었고 정념의 불길과 재치의 놀음이 있었으나 사랑은 없었다."], ["Cruelty was there instead, and courage, a desire of masterhood, cunning, and a wish for mischief.", "그 대신 잔인함이 있었고, 담대함과 남을 부리려는 욕심과 교활함과 남을 못되게 굴려는 마음이 있었다."], ["And yet, as eyes, they were very beautiful.", "그러면서도 눈으로 치자면 그것은 대단히 아름다운 눈이었다."], ["The eyelashes were long and perfect, and the long, steady, unabashed gaze with which she would look into the face of her admirer fascinated while it frightened him.", "속눈썹은 길고 흠잡을 데가 없었으며, 저를 흠모하는 사내의 얼굴을 오래도록 흔들림 없이 조금도 부끄러운 기색 없이 들여다보는 그 눈길은 그를 겁주는 동시에 홀렸다."], ["She was a basilisk from whom an ardent lover of beauty could make no escape.", "그는 아름다움을 뜨겁게 사랑하는 자라면 결코 벗어날 수 없는 바실리스크였다."], ["Her nose and mouth and teeth and chin and neck and bust were perfect, much more so at twenty-eight than they had been at eighteen.", "코와 입과 이와 턱과 목과 가슴은 완벽했으니, 열여덟 살 때보다 스물여덟 살인 지금이 훨씬 더 그러했다."], ["What wonder that with such charms still glowing in her face, and with such deformity destroying her figure, she should resolve to be seen, but only to be seen reclining on a sofa.", "이런 매력이 여전히 그 얼굴에 이글거리고 있고 이런 기형이 그 몸매를 망쳐 놓았으니, 남들 앞에 나서되 오직 소파에 비스듬히 누운 모습으로만 나서기로 작정한 것이 무엇이 이상하겠는가."]], [["Her resolve had not been carried out without difficulty.", "그 작정을 실행하는 일이 어렵지 않았던 것은 아니다."], ["She had still frequented the opera at Milan; she had still been seen occasionally in the salons of the noblesse; she had caused herself to be carried in and out from her carriage, and that in such a manner as in no wise to disturb her charms, disarrange her dress, or expose her deformities.", "그는 여전히 밀라노의 오페라를 드나들었고, 여전히 이따금 귀족들의 살롱에 모습을 드러냈다. 매력을 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 않고, 옷매무새를 흩뜨리지 않고, 흉한 곳을 드러내지도 않는 방식으로 마차에서 들려 나오고 들려 들어가게 했던 것이다."], ["Her sister always accompanied her and a maid, a manservant also, and on state occasions, two.", "언니가 늘 함께 다녔고 하녀가 하나, 하인이 또 하나, 그리고 격식을 차려야 하는 자리에는 둘이 따라붙었다."], ["It was impossible that her purpose could have been achieved with less; and yet, poor as she was, she had achieved her purpose.", "이보다 적은 인원으로 그 목적을 이룬다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그러면서도 그는 가난한 형편에 기어이 그 목적을 이루어 냈다."], ["And then again the more dissolute Italian youths of Milan frequented the Stanhope villa and surrounded her couch, not greatly to her father's satisfaction.", "게다가 밀라노에서도 방탕한 축에 드는 이탈리아 젊은이들이 스탠호프 별장을 드나들며 그 긴 의자를 에워쌌으니, 아버지로서는 그리 흡족한 일이 아니었다."], ["Sometimes his spirit would rise, a dark spot would show itself on his cheek, and he would rebel, but Charlotte would assuage him with some peculiar triumph of her culinary art and all again would be smooth for awhile.", "이따금 그의 성미가 치밀어 올라 뺨에 검은 기운이 돌고 반기를 들 때도 있었으나, 그럴 때면 샬럿이 부엌 솜씨의 남다른 개가로 그를 달래 놓았고 그러면 한동안 만사가 다시 잔잔해졌다."]], [["Madeline affected all manner of rich and quaint devices in the garniture of her room, her person, and her feminine belongings.", "마들린은 제 방과 제 몸, 그리고 제 여자다운 소지품을 꾸미는 데 온갖 화려하고 기발한 궁리를 부렸다."], ["In nothing was this more apparent than in the visiting card which she had prepared for her use.", "이것이 무엇보다 잘 드러난 것이 저 쓰겠다고 만들어 둔 방문 명함이었다."], ["For such an article one would say that she, in her present state, could have but small need, seeing how improbable it was that she should make a morning call: but not such was her own opinion.", "그런 물건이야 지금 같은 처지에는 별로 쓸 데가 없으리라고들 하겠으니, 아침 방문을 다닐 성싶지도 않은 까닭이다. 그러나 본인의 생각은 그렇지 않았다."], ["Her card was surrounded by a deep border of gilding; on this she had imprinted, in three lines", "명함 가장자리에는 금박이 넓게 둘러져 있었고, 그 위에 석 줄로 이렇게 찍어 두었다."]], [["La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni.", "La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni."], ["--Nata Stanhope.", "--Nata Stanhope."]], [["And over the name she had a bright gilt coronet, which certainly looked very magnificent.", "그리고 이름 위에는 번쩍이는 금박 소관을 얹어 두었는데, 과연 대단히 으리으리해 보였다."], ["How she had come to concoct such a name for herself it would be difficult to explain.", "어떻게 저를 두고 그런 이름을 지어낼 생각을 했는지는 설명하기 어렵다."], ["Her father had been christened Vesey as another man is christened Thomas, and she had no more right to assume it than would have the daughter of a Mr. Josiah Jones to call herself Mrs.", "그 아버지는 다른 사람이 토머스라는 세례명을 받듯이 베시라는 세례명을 받았을 뿐이니, 그 이름을 제 것으로 삼을 권리로 말하면 조사이어 존스 씨의 딸이 스미스라는 성을 가진 남자와 결혼했다 하여 스스로 조사이어 스미스 부인이라 부를 권리나 매한가지였다."], ["Josiah Smith, on marrying a man of the latter name. The gold coronet was equally out of place, and perhaps inserted with even less excuse.", "금박 소관도 마찬가지로 어울리지 않았고, 어쩌면 그것을 박아 넣은 데에는 변명거리가 더더욱 없었다."], ["Paulo Neroni had had not the faintest title to call himself a scion of even Italian nobility.", "파울로 네로니는 이탈리아 귀족의 후예라 자칭할 만한 근거조차 손톱만큼도 없는 자였다."], ["Had the pair met in England Neroni would probably have been a count, but they had met in Italy, and any such pretence on his part would have been simply ridiculous.", "그 두 사람이 영국에서 만났더라면 네로니는 아마 백작 행세를 했겠지만, 그들은 이탈리아에서 만났으니 그런 시늉을 했다가는 그저 우스운 꼴이 되었을 것이다."], ["A coronet, however, was a pretty ornament, and if it could solace a poor cripple to have such on her card, who would begrudge it to her?", "그렇지만 소관이라는 것은 예쁜 장식이거니와, 가엾은 불구자가 제 명함에 그런 것을 얹어 놓고 위안을 삼는다면 누가 그것을 아까워하겠는가?"]], [["Of her husband, or of his individual family, she never spoke, but with her admirers she would often allude in a mysterious way to her married life and isolated state, and, pointing to her daughter, would call her the last of the blood of the emperors, thus referring Neroni's extraction to the old Roman family from which the worst of the Caesars sprang.", "남편에 대해서든 그 집안 사람들에 대해서든 그는 결코 입에 올리지 않았으나, 저를 흠모하는 이들 앞에서는 곧잘 결혼 생활과 홀로 남은 처지를 두고 알쏭달쏭하게 말을 흘렸고, 제 딸을 가리키며 황제들의 피를 이은 마지막 사람이라 부르곤 했다. 네로니의 혈통을 저 최악의 카이사르들이 나온 옛 로마 가문에 갖다 붙인 것이었다."]], [["The \"signora\" was not without talent and not without a certain sort of industry; she was an indomitable letter-writer, and her letters were worth the postage: they were full of wit, mischief, satire, love, latitudinarian philosophy, free religion, and, sometimes, alas, loose ribaldry.", "이 “시뇨라”에게는 재능이 없지 않았고 어떤 종류의 부지런함도 없지 않았다."], ["The subject, however, depended entirely on the recipient, and she was prepared to correspond with anyone but moral young ladies or stiff old women.", "그는 편지라면 지칠 줄 모르고 쓰는 사람이었고, 그 편지들은 우편 요금이 아깝지 않았다. 재치와 짓궂음과 풍자와 사랑과 관용주의 철학과 자유로운 종교관이, 그리고 때로는 아뿔싸, 걸쭉한 농지거리가 가득했던 것이다."], ["She wrote also a kind of poetry, generally in Italian, and short romances, generally in French.", "다만 무엇을 쓰느냐는 온전히 받는 사람에 달려 있었으니, 그는 도덕적인 젊은 처녀와 딱딱한 노부인만 아니라면 누구와도 편지를 주고받을 태세였다."], ["She read much of a desultory sort of literature, and as a modern linguist had really made great proficiency.", "그는 시 비슷한 것도 썼는데 대개 이탈리아어였고, 짧은 이야기도 썼는데 대개 프랑스어였다. 잡다한 종류의 책을 많이 읽었고, 현대어에 관해서는 실로 크게 숙달해 있었다."], ["Such was the lady who had now come to wound the hearts of the men of Barchester.", "바체스터 남자들의 마음에 상처를 내러 이제 막 당도한 부인이 바로 이런 사람이었다."]], [["Ethelbert Stanhope was in some respects like his younger sister, but he was less inestimable as a man than she as a woman.", "에설버트 스탠호프는 몇 가지 점에서 아래 누이를 닮았으나, 남자로서 그의 값어치는 여자로서 누이의 값어치만 못했다."], ["His great fault was an entire absence of that principle which should have induced him, as the son of a man without fortune, to earn his own bread.", "그의 큰 흠은, 재산 없는 사람의 아들이라면 마땅히 제 밥벌이를 해야 한다고 일러 주었을 그 원칙이 아예 없다는 것이었다."], ["Many attempts had been made to get him to do so, but these had all been frustrated, not so much by idleness on his part as by a disinclination to exert himself in any way not to his taste.", "그러도록 만들어 보려는 시도가 여러 번 있었으나 하나같이 무산되었는데, 그것은 그가 게으른 탓이라기보다 제 취향에 맞지 않는 일이라면 무엇에도 애쓰기 싫어하는 성미 탓이었다."], ["He had been educated at Eton and had been intended for the Church, but he had left Cambridge in disgust after a single term, and notified to his father his intention to study for the bar.", "그는 이튼에서 교육을 받았고 성직자로 나설 예정이었으나, 케임브리지를 한 학기 만에 넌더리를 내며 떠났고 아버지에게는 법조계 공부를 하겠다고 알렸다."], ["Preparatory to that, he thought it well that he should attend a German university, and consequently went to Leipzig.", "그 준비로 독일의 대학에 다니는 것이 좋겠다고 생각하여 라이프치히로 갔다. 거기서 두 해를 머물며 독일어 지식과 미술 취향을 얻어 왔다."], ["There he remained two years and brought away a knowledge of German and a taste for the fine arts.", "그러면서도 여전히 법조계로 나갈 작정이어서 사무실을 얻고 어느 학식 있는 대가의 문하에 들기로 한 뒤 런던에서 한 철을 보냈다."], ["He still, however, intended himself for the bar, took chambers, engaged himself to sit at the feet of a learned pundit, and spent a season in London.", "거기서 그는 제 소질이 죄다 예술가의 삶 쪽으로 기울어 있음을 깨닫고 그림으로 먹고살기로 마음먹었다. 그 목적으로 밀라노에 돌아가 로마로 갈 차림을 갖추었다."], ["He there found that all his aptitudes inclined him to the life of an artist, and he determined to live by painting.", "화가로서라면 그는 밥벌이를 할 수도 있었을 것이니, 남달라지는 데 모자란 것은 오직 부지런함뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["With this object he returned to Milan, and had himself rigged out for Rome.", "그러나 로마에 이르자 그의 마음은 딴것에 팔려 버렸다."], ["As a painter he might have earned his bread, for he wanted only diligence to excel, but when at Rome his mind was carried away by other things: he soon wrote home for money, saying that he had been converted to the Mother Church, that he was already an acolyte of the Jesuits, and that he was about to start with others to Palestine on a mission for converting Jews.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그는 집으로 돈을 부쳐 달라는 편지를 보내면서, 자기가 모교회로 개종했고 이미 예수회의 시자가 되었으며 유대인을 개종시키는 선교를 위해 다른 이들과 함께 팔레스타인으로 떠나려 한다고 알렸다."], ["He did go to Judea, but being unable to convert the Jews, was converted by them.", "그는 과연 유대 땅으로 갔으나, 유대인들을 개종시키기는커녕 도리어 그들에게 개종당했다."], ["He again wrote home, to say that Moses was the only giver of perfect laws to the world, that the coming of the true Messiah was at hand, that great things were doing in Palestine, and that he had met one of the family of Sidonia, a most remarkable man, who was now on his way to western Europe, and whom he had induced to deviate from his route with the object of calling at the Stanhope villa.", "그는 다시 집으로 편지를 보내, 모세야말로 세상에 완전한 율법을 준 유일한 사람이라는 것, 참된 메시아의 오심이 임박했다는 것, 팔레스타인에서 큰일이 벌어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 시도니아 집안의 한 사람을 만났는데 지극히 비범한 인물로서 지금 서유럽으로 가는 길이기에 자기가 권하여 길을 돌려 스탠호프 별장에 들르게 했다는 것을 알렸다."], ["Ethelbert then expressed his hope that his mother and sisters would listen to this wonderful prophet.", "그러고서 에설버트는 어머니와 두 누이가 부디 이 놀라운 예언자의 말에 귀 기울이기를 바란다고 했다."], ["His father he knew could not do so from pecuniary considerations.", "아버지는 금전 사정상 그러실 수 없으리라는 것을 그도 알고 있었다."], ["This Sidonia, however, did not take so strong a fancy to him as another of that family once did to a young English nobleman.", "그런데 이 시도니아는 예전에 그 집안의 다른 사람이 어느 젊은 영국 귀족을 어여삐 여겼던 것만큼 그를 어여삐 여기지는 않았다."], ["At least he provided him with no heaps of gold as large as lions, so that the Judaized Ethelbert was again obliged to draw on the revenues of the Christian Church.", "적어도 사자만 한 금덩이 무더기 따위는 안겨 주지 않았으므로, 유대교로 넘어간 에설버트는 또다시 그리스도 교회의 수입에 손을 벌릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["It is needless to tell how the father swore that he would send no more money and receive no Jew, nor how Charlotte declared that Ethelbert could not be left penniless in Jerusalem, and how \"La Signora Neroni\" resolved to have Sidonia at her feet.", "아버지가 다시는 돈을 부치지 않겠으며 유대인 따위는 들이지 않겠다고 어떻게 맹세했는지, 샬럿이 에설버트를 예루살렘에 무일푼으로 버려 둘 수는 없다고 어떻게 우겼는지, 그리고 “라 시뇨라 네로니”가 시도니아를 제 발치에 꿇리겠다고 어떻게 벼르었는지는 굳이 이야기할 것 없다."], ["The money was sent, and the Jew did come.", "돈은 부쳐졌고, 유대인은 정말로 왔다."], ["The Jew did come, but he was not at all to the taste of \"La Signora.\"", "유대인은 정말로 왔으나 “라 시뇨라”의 취향에는 전혀 맞지 않았다."], ["He was a dirty little old man, and though he had provided no golden lions, he had, it seems, relieved young Stanhope's necessities.", "그는 자그마하고 꾀죄죄한 노인이었고, 황금 사자를 마련해 준 적은 없었어도 젊은 스탠호프의 궁핍만은 어지간히 덜어 주었던 모양이다."], ["He positively refused to leave the villa till he had got a bill from the doctor on his London bankers.", "그는 박사에게서 런던 거래 은행 앞으로 끊은 어음을 받아 내기 전에는 별장을 떠나지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 버텼다."]], [["Ethelbert did not long remain a Jew.", "에설버트가 유대인으로 지낸 기간은 길지 않았다."], ["He soon reappeared at the villa without prejudices on the subject of his religion, and with a firm resolve to achieve fame and fortune as a sculptor.", "그는 이내 종교 문제에 아무 편견도 없는 몸으로, 그리고 조각가로 이름과 재산을 얻겠다는 굳은 결심을 품고 별장에 다시 나타났다."], ["He brought with him some models which he had originated at Rome and which really gave such fair promise that his father was induced to go to further expense in furthering these views.", "로마에서 처음 빚어 본 모형 몇 점을 가지고 왔는데, 그것들이 정말이지 제법 가망을 보이는지라 아버지도 이 뜻을 밀어주느라 또 한 번 돈을 쓰게 되었다."], ["Ethelbert opened an establishment, or rather took lodgings and a workshop, at Carrara, and there spoilt much marble and made some few pretty images.", "에설버트는 카라라에 공방을 차렸다, 아니 정확히는 셋방과 작업장을 얻었고, 거기서 대리석을 숱하게 망쳐 놓고 예쁘장한 조각상을 몇 점 만들었다."], ["Since that period, now four years ago, he had alternated between Carrara and the villa, but his sojourns at the workshop became shorter and shorter and those at the villa longer and longer.", "그때 이후로, 그러니까 이제 4년째 그는 카라라와 별장을 오가며 지냈으나, 작업장에 머무는 기간은 점점 짧아지고 별장에 머무는 기간은 점점 길어졌다."], ["'Twas no wonder, for Carrara is not a spot in which an Englishman would like to dwell.", "그도 그럴 것이 카라라란 영국 사람이 살고 싶어 할 만한 곳이 아니다."]], [["When the family started for England, he had resolved not to be left behind, and, with the assistance of his elder sister, had carried his point against his father's wishes.", "온 식구가 영국으로 떠날 때 그는 저 혼자 남지 않겠다고 작정했고, 큰누이의 도움을 얻어 아버지의 뜻을 꺾고 제 뜻을 관철했다."], ["It was necessary, he said, that he should come to England for orders.", "자기는 주문을 받으러 영국에 가야 한다는 것이었다."], ["How otherwise was he to bring his profession to account?", "그러지 않고서야 어떻게 제 직업으로 돈을 만들겠느냐는 말이었다."]], [["In personal appearance Ethelbert Stanhope was the most singular of beings.", "생김새로 말하면 에설버트 스탠호프는 더없이 별난 사람이었다."], ["He was certainly very handsome.", "그가 아주 잘생겼다는 것만은 분명했다."], ["He had his sister Madeline's eyes, without their stare and without their hard, cunning, cruel firmness.", "누이 마들린의 눈을 그대로 가졌으되, 쏘아보는 기운도 없고 그 딱딱하고 교활하고 잔인한 단단함도 없었다."], ["They were also very much lighter, and of so light and clear a blue as to make his face remarkable, if nothing else did so.", "빛깔도 훨씬 옅어서, 어찌나 옅고 맑은 푸른빛인지 달리 눈에 띄는 것이 없더라도 그 눈 하나로 얼굴이 두드러졌다."], ["On entering a room with him, Ethelbert's blue eyes would be the first thing you would see, and on leaving it almost the last you would forget.", "그와 함께 방에 들어서면 맨 먼저 보이는 것이 에설버트의 푸른 눈이었고, 방을 나서고 나서도 거의 맨 나중까지 잊히지 않는 것이 그 눈이었다."], ["His light hair was very long and silky, coming down over his coat.", "옅은 빛깔 머리카락은 아주 길고 비단결 같아서 웃옷 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["His beard had been prepared in holy land, and was patriarchal.", "수염은 성지에서 길러 온 것으로 족장처럼 덥수룩했다."], ["He never shaved and rarely trimmed it.", "그는 결코 면도를 하지 않았고 다듬는 일도 드물었다."], ["It was glossy, soft, clean, and altogether not unprepossessing.", "그 수염은 윤이 나고 부드럽고 깨끗하여 도무지 밉지가 않았다."], ["It was such that ladies might desire to reel it off and work it into their patterns in lieu of floss silk.", "부인네들이 명주실 대신 그것을 감아다가 제 수예에 놓고 싶어 할 만한 수염이었다."], ["His complexion was fair and almost pink; he was small in height and slender in limb, but well-made; and his voice was of peculiar sweetness.", "낯빛은 희고 거의 발그레했다. 키는 작고 팔다리는 가늘었으나 몸매는 균형이 잡혔고, 목소리는 유난히 감미로웠다."]], [["In manner and dress he was equally remarkable.", "태도와 옷차림도 그에 못지않게 남달랐다."], ["He had none of the mauvaise honte of an Englishman.", "그에게는 영국 사람 특유의 mauvaise honte라고는 조금도 없었다."], ["He required no introduction to make himself agreeable to any person.", "누구에게든 상냥하게 굴기 위해 소개 따위가 필요하지 않았다."], ["He habitually addressed strangers, ladies as well as men, without any such formality, and in doing so never seemed to meet with rebuke.", "그는 남자든 부인이든 처음 보는 사람에게 그런 격식 없이 말을 붙이는 것이 버릇이었고, 그러고도 면박을 당하는 일은 없는 듯했다."], ["His costume cannot be described because it was so various, but it was always totally opposed in every principle of colour and construction to the dress of those with whom he for the time consorted.", "그의 옷차림은 도무지 묘사할 수가 없으니 워낙 가지각색이었기 때문인데, 다만 그때그때 어울려 지내는 이들의 옷차림과는 빛깔로 보나 지음새로 보나 언제나 모든 원칙에서 정반대였다."]], [["He was habitually addicted to making love to ladies, and did so without any scruples of conscience, or any idea that such a practice was amiss.", "그는 부인네들에게 사랑을 속삭이는 것이 몸에 밴 사람이었고, 그러면서 양심에 조금도 거리끼는 바가 없었으며 그런 짓이 잘못이라는 생각도 없었다."], ["He had no heart to touch himself, and was literally unaware that humanity was subject to such an infliction.", "제 마음이라는 것이 없어 흔들릴 것도 없었고, 인간이 그런 재앙을 겪는다는 사실을 말 그대로 알지 못했다."], ["He had not thought much about it, but, had he been asked, would have said that ill-treating a lady's heart meant injuring her promotion in the world.", "그 문제를 깊이 생각해 본 적은 없지만, 만일 누가 물었더라면 그는 부인의 마음을 함부로 다룬다는 것은 그 부인이 세상에서 자리를 얻는 데 훼방을 놓는 일이라고 대답했을 것이다."], ["His principles therefore forbade him to pay attention to a girl if he thought any man was present whom it might suit her to marry.", "그러므로 그의 원칙은, 어떤 처녀가 결혼 상대로 삼음 직한 남자가 자리에 있다 싶으면 그 처녀에게 마음을 쏟지 못하게 막았다."], ["In this manner his good nature frequently interfered with his amusement, but he had no other motive in abstaining from the fullest declarations of love to every girl that pleased his eye.", "이런 식으로 그의 붙임성은 걸핏하면 그 자신의 재미를 가로막았으나, 눈에 드는 처녀마다 사랑을 끝까지 고백하지 않고 참는 데에는 그 밖의 다른 동기라고는 없었다."]], [["Bertie Stanhope, as he was generally called, was, however, popular with both sexes--and with Italians as well as English.", "그러나 흔히들 부르는 대로 버티 스탠호프는 남녀 양쪽 모두에게 인기가 있었고, 이탈리아 사람들에게도 영국 사람들 못지않게 인기가 있었다."], ["His circle of acquaintance was very large and embraced people of all sorts.", "그가 아는 사람의 범위는 무척 넓어서 온갖 부류를 아울렀다."], ["He had no respect for rank, and no aversion to those below him.", "그는 신분을 우러르는 마음도 없었고 저보다 아래인 사람을 꺼리는 마음도 없었다."], ["He had lived on familiar terms with English peers, German shopkeepers, and Roman priests.", "영국 귀족과도, 독일 가게 주인과도, 로마 사제와도 스스럼없이 어울려 살아 왔다."], ["All people were nearly alike to him.", "그에게는 사람이 다 거기서 거기였다."], ["He was above, or rather below, all prejudices.", "그는 온갖 편견을 넘어서 있었다, 아니 그 아래에 있었다."], ["No virtue could charm him, no vice shock him.", "어떤 미덕도 그를 감동시키지 못했고 어떤 악덕도 그를 놀라게 하지 못했다."], ["He had about him a natural good manner, which seemed to qualify him for the highest circles, and yet he was never out of place in the lowest.", "그에게는 타고난 좋은 태도가 있어 가장 높은 자리에도 어울릴 사람처럼 보였으나, 그러면서 가장 낮은 자리에서도 결코 어색한 법이 없었다."], ["He had no principle, no regard for others, no self-respect, no desire to be other than a drone in the hive, if only he could, as a drone, get what honey was sufficient for him.", "그에게는 원칙도, 남에 대한 배려도, 자존심도 없었고, 벌집 속의 수벌 노릇 말고 다른 무엇이 되고 싶은 마음도 없었다. 수벌로서 제게 넉넉할 만큼의 꿀만 얻을 수 있다면 말이다."], ["Of honey, in his latter days, it may probably be presaged, that he will have but short allowance.", "그런데 그 꿀로 말하면, 훗날의 그에게는 몫이 얼마 돌아오지 않으리라고 미리 점쳐 두어도 좋을 것이다."]], [["Such was the family of the Stanhopes, who, at this period, suddenly joined themselves to the ecclesiastical circle of Barchester close.", "이것이 바로 이 무렵 바체스터 대성당 경내의 성직자 사교계에 느닷없이 끼어든 스탠호프 집안 사람들이었다."], ["Any stranger union it would be impossible perhaps to conceive.", "이보다 더 기이한 결합은 아마 상상하기 어려울 것이다."], ["And it was not as though they all fell down into the cathedral precincts hitherto unknown and untalked of.", "게다가 그들이 지금껏 알려지지도 않고 입에 오르내리지도 않던 채로 대성당 구내에 툭 떨어진 것도 아니었다."], ["In such case, no amalgamation would have been at all probable between the new-comers and either the Proudie set or the Grantly set.", "만일 그랬더라면 새로 온 이들과 프라우디 패거리 사이든 그랜틀리 패거리 사이든 어우러지는 일은 도무지 있을 법하지 않았을 것이다."], ["But such was far from being the case.", "그러나 사정은 전혀 그렇지 않았다."], ["The Stanhopes were all known by name in Barchester, and Barchester was prepared to receive them with open arms.", "스탠호프 집안 사람들은 바체스터에서 이름만은 다들 알려져 있었고, 바체스터는 두 팔 벌려 그들을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["The doctor was one of her prebendaries, one of her rectors, one of her pillars of strength; and was, moreover, counted on as a sure ally both by Proudies and Grantlys.", "박사는 이 도시의 참사회원 가운데 하나요, 교구 목사 가운데 하나요, 기둥 가운데 하나였으며, 더구나 프라우디 쪽에서나 그랜틀리 쪽에서나 틀림없는 우군으로 셈해지고 있었다."]], [["He himself was the brother of one peer, and his wife was the sister of another--and both these peers were lords of Whiggish tendency, with whom the new bishop had some sort of alliance. This was sufficient to give to Mr.", "그 자신은 어느 귀족의 아우였고 그 아내는 또 다른 귀족의 누이였는데, 두 귀족은 모두 휘그당 쪽으로 기운 사람들로서 새 주교와도 어떤 식으로든 연이 닿아 있었다."], ["Slope high hope that he might enlist Dr. Stanhope on his side, before his enemies could outmanoeuvre him.", "슬로프 씨로서는 이만하면 적들이 자기를 앞질러 수를 쓰기 전에 스탠호프 박사를 제 편으로 끌어들일 수 있으리라는 큰 기대를 품기에 충분했다."], ["On the other hand, the old dean had many many years ago, in the days of the doctor's clerical energies, been instrumental in assisting him in his views as to preferment; and many many years ago also, the two doctors, Stanhope and Grantly, had, as young parsons, been joyous together in the common-rooms of Oxford.", "한편 늙은 수석 사제로 말하면, 아주 여러 해 전 박사에게 아직 성직자로서의 기력이 남아 있던 시절에 그의 승진 계획을 거들어 준 일이 있었다. 또한 역시 아주 여러 해 전, 스탠호프와 그랜틀리 두 박사는 젊은 성직자로서 옥스퍼드의 담화실에서 함께 즐거운 시절을 보낸 사이였다."], ["Dr. Grantly, consequently, did not doubt but that the newcomer would range himself under his banners.", "그러니 그랜틀리 박사도 이 새로 온 사람이 제 깃발 아래 설 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다."]], [["Little did any of them dream of what ingredients the Stanhope family was now composed.", "스탠호프 집안이 이제 어떤 재료들로 이루어져 있는지는 그들 가운데 누구도 꿈에도 몰랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER X. Mrs. Proudie's Reception--Commenced", "# 제10장. 프라우디 부인의 접견회 — 상"]], [["The bishop and his wife had spent only three or four days in Barchester on the occasion of their first visit.", "주교 내외는 첫 방문 때 바체스터에 사나흘밖에 머무르지 않았다."], ["His lordship had, as we have seen, taken his seat on his throne, but his demeanour there, into which it had been his intention to infuse much hierarchal dignity, had been a good deal disarranged by the audacity of his chaplain's sermon.", "앞서 보았듯이 주교는 자기 옥좌에 앉기는 했으나, 위계의 존엄을 잔뜩 불어넣으려 작정했던 그 자리에서의 태도는 전속 사제의 대담한 설교 탓에 적잖이 흐트러지고 말았다."], ["He had hardly dared to look his clergy in the face, and to declare by the severity of his countenance that in truth he meant all that his factotum was saying on his behalf; nor yet did he dare to throw Mr. Slope over, and show to those around him that he was no party to the sermon, and would resent it.", "그는 감히 휘하 성직자들의 얼굴을 마주 보며, 제 심복이 자기를 대신해 하는 말이 실은 모두 자신의 뜻이라고 근엄한 표정으로 선언하지도 못했고, 그렇다고 슬로프 씨를 내던지고서 자기는 그 설교와 아무 상관이 없으며 그 일을 괘씸하게 여긴다고 주위 사람들에게 내보이지도 못했다."]], [["He had accordingly blessed his people in a shambling manner, not at all to his own satisfaction, and had walked back to his palace with his mind very doubtful as to what he would say to his chaplain on the subject.", "그리하여 그는 어정쩡한 태도로 신자들에게 축복을 내렸으니 스스로도 도무지 흡족하지 않았고, 그 문제를 두고 전속 사제에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못하는 마음으로 주교관까지 걸어 돌아왔다."], ["He did not remain long in doubt.", "그러나 갈피를 잡지 못한 시간은 길지 않았다."], ["He had hardly doffed his lawn when the partner of all his toils entered his study and exclaimed even before she had seated herself:", "그가 미처 흰 삼베 소매 예복을 벗기도 전에 그의 온갖 수고를 함께 나누는 반려가 서재로 들어와, 자리에 앉기도 전에 이렇게 외쳤던 것이다."]], [["\"Bishop, did you ever hear a more sublime, more spirit-moving, more appropriate discourse than that?\"", "“주교님, 그보다 더 숭고하고 그보다 더 마음을 뒤흔들고 그보다 더 때에 들어맞는 강론을 평생에 들어 보신 적이 있으세요?”"]], [["\"Well, my love; ha--hum--he!\"", "“글쎄, 여보. 하…흠…허!”"], ["The bishop did not know what to say.", "주교는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다."]], [["\"I hope, my lord, you don't mean to say you disapprove?\"", "“주교님, 설마 그 설교가 못마땅하시다는 말씀을 하시려는 건 아니겠지요?”"]], [["There was a look about the lady's eye which did not admit of my lord's disapproving at that moment.", "부인의 눈매에는 그 순간 주교가 못마땅해하는 것을 도무지 허락하지 않는 무엇이 있었다."], ["He felt that if he intended to disapprove, it must be now or never; but he also felt that it could not be now.", "그는 못마땅해할 작정이라면 지금이 아니고서는 영영 기회가 없으리라고 느꼈으나, 동시에 그 지금이라는 것이 불가능하다는 것도 느꼈다."], ["It was not in him to say to the wife of his bosom that Mr. Slope's sermon was ill-timed, impertinent, and vexatious.", "슬로프 씨의 설교가 때에 맞지 않고 무례하며 성가신 것이었다고 품속의 아내에게 말할 만한 배포는 그에게 없었다."]], [["\"No, no,\"", "“아니, 아니오.”"], ["replied the bishop.", "주교가 대답했다."], ["\"No, I can't say I disapprove--a very clever sermon and very well intended, and I dare say will do a great deal of good.\"", "“못마땅하다고야 할 수 없지. 아주 영리한 설교였고 뜻도 아주 좋았고, 감히 말하건대 큰 유익을 끼칠 게요.”"], ["This last praise was added, seeing that what he had already said by no means satisfied Mrs. Proudie.", "이 마지막 칭찬은 앞서 한 말이 프라우디 부인을 조금도 만족시키지 못하는 것을 보고 덧붙인 것이었다."]], [["\"I hope it will,\"", "“그러기를 바라요.”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"And I am sure it was well deserved.", "“그리고 그만한 소리를 들을 만했던 것도 분명하고요."], ["Did you ever in your life, bishop, hear anything so like play-acting as the way in which Mr. Harding sings the litany?", "주교님, 하딩 씨가 기도문을 노래하는 그 꼴만큼 연극 놀음 같은 것을 평생에 들어 보신 적이 있으세요?"], ["I shall beg Mr. Slope to continue a course of sermons on the subject till all that is altered.", "저는 슬로프 씨에게 그 모든 것이 바뀔 때까지 그 문제를 두고 설교를 이어 가 달라고 청할 작정이에요."], ["We will have at any rate, in our cathedral, a decent, godly, modest morning service.", "적어도 우리 대성당에서만큼은 단정하고 경건하고 겸손한 아침 예배를 보아야지요."], ["There must be no more play-acting here now;\"", "이제 여기서 연극 놀음은 더 이상 없어야 합니다.”"], ["and so the lady rang for lunch.", "그러고서 부인은 종을 울려 점심을 청했다."]], [["The bishop knew more about cathedrals and deans and precentors and church services than his wife did, and also more of a bishop's powers.", "주교는 대성당이며 수석 사제며 선창자며 예배에 관해 아내보다 더 많이 알았고, 주교의 권한에 대해서도 더 많이 알았다."], ["But he thought it better at present to let the subject drop.", "그러나 지금은 이 이야기를 그만 접어 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."]], [["\"My dear,\"", "“여보,”"], ["said he, \"I think we must go back to London on Tuesday.", "그가 말했다. “아무래도 화요일에는 런던으로 돌아가야 할 것 같소."], ["I find my staying here will be very inconvenient to the Government.\"", "내가 여기 머물러 있으면 정부 쪽이 여간 불편하지 않을 모양이오.”"]], [["The bishop knew that to this proposal his wife would not object, and he also felt that by thus retreating from the ground of battle the heat of the fight might be got over in his absence.", "주교는 이 제안에 아내가 반대하지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 또 이렇게 싸움터에서 물러나 있으면 자기가 없는 동안에 격전의 열기가 지나가 버릴지도 모른다고 느꼈다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope will remain here, of course?\"", "“슬로프 씨는 물론 여기 남는 거지요?”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, of course,\"", "“아, 물론이오.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["Thus, after less than a week's sojourn in his palace, did the bishop fly from Barchester; nor did he return to it for two months, the London season being then over.", "그리하여 주교는 제 주교관에 한 주도 채 머무르지 못한 채 바체스터에서 달아났고, 두 달 동안 그리로 돌아오지 않았으니, 그제야 런던의 사교 철이 끝난 것이었다."], ["During that time Mr. Slope was not idle, but he did not again essay to preach in the cathedral.", "그동안 슬로프 씨가 놀고 있었던 것은 아니지만, 대성당에서 다시 설교해 보려 들지는 않았다."], ["In answer to Mrs. Proudie's letters advising a course of sermons, he had pleaded that he would at any rate wish to put off such an undertaking till she was there to hear them.", "연속 설교를 하라고 권하는 프라우디 부인의 편지에 대해서는, 어쨌든 부인이 그 자리에 계셔서 직접 들으실 수 있게 될 때까지 그런 일은 미루고 싶다고 변명해 두었다."]], [["He had employed his time in consolidating a Proudie and Slope party--or rather a Slope and Proudie party, and he had not employed his time in vain.", "그는 그 시간을 프라우디·슬로프 당파를, 아니 차라리 슬로프·프라우디 당파를 다지는 데 썼고, 그 시간을 헛되이 쓰지 않았다."], ["He did not meddle with the dean and chapter, except by giving them little teasing intimations of the bishop's wishes about this and the bishop's feelings about that, in a manner which was to them sufficiently annoying, but which they could not resent.", "그는 수석 사제와 참사회를 건드리지는 않았다. 다만 이 일에 관해서는 주교의 뜻이 이러하시다느니 저 일에 관해서는 주교의 심기가 저러하시다느니 하는 것을 슬쩍슬쩍 흘려 알려 주었을 뿐인데, 그 방식이란 그들에게는 충분히 짜증스러우면서도 화를 낼 구실은 되지 못하는 것이었다."], ["He preached once or twice in a distant church in the suburbs of the city, but made no allusion to the cathedral service.", "그는 시 변두리의 먼 교회에서 한두 번 설교를 했으나 대성당 예배에 대해서는 한마디도 언급하지 않았다."], ["He commenced the establishment of two \"Bishop's Barchester Sabbath-day schools,\"", "그는 「주교 관하 바체스터 안식일 학교」"], ["gave notice of a proposed \"Bishop's Barchester Young Men's Sabbath Evening Lecture Room,\" and wrote three or four letters to the manager of the Barchester branch railway, informing him how anxious the bishop was that the Sunday trains should be discontinued.", "두 곳을 세우는 일에 착수했고, 「주교 관하 바체스터 청년 안식일 저녁 강연실」을 두겠다는 계획을 공고했으며, 바체스터 지선 철도 지배인에게 서너 통의 편지를 써서 주교께서 일요일 열차 운행이 중단되기를 얼마나 간절히 바라시는지를 알렸다."]], [["At the end of two months, however, the bishop and the lady reappeared, and as a happy harbinger of their return, heralded their advent by the promise of an evening party on the largest scale.", "그러나 두 달이 지나자 주교와 부인이 다시 나타났고, 그 귀환의 기쁜 전조로서 최대 규모의 야회를 열겠다는 약속으로 자신들의 왕림을 미리 알렸다."], ["The tickets of invitation were sent out from London--they were dated from Bruton Street, and were dispatched by the odious Sabbath-breaking railway, in a huge brown paper parcel to Mr. Slope.", "초대장은 런던에서 발송되었으니, 브루턴 스트리트 날짜가 찍혀 있었고, 저 가증스러운 안식일 파괴자인 철도 편에 커다란 갈색 종이 꾸러미로 슬로프 씨 앞으로 부쳐졌다."], ["Everybody calling himself a gentleman, or herself a lady, within the city of Barchester, and a circle of two miles round it, was included.", "바체스터 시내와 그 둘레 2마일 안에서 스스로 신사라 일컫는 자, 스스로 숙녀라 일컫는 자는 하나도 빠짐없이 포함되었다."], ["Tickets were sent to all the diocesan clergy, and also to many other persons of priestly note, of whose absence the bishop, or at least the bishop's wife, felt tolerably confident.", "초대장은 교구의 모든 성직자에게 보내졌고, 그 밖에 성직계에서 이름 있는 여러 사람에게도 보내졌는데, 주교는, 적어도 주교의 부인은, 그들이 오지 않으리라는 데 상당한 자신이 있었다."], ["It was intended, however, to be a thronged and noticeable affair, and preparations were made for receiving some hundreds.", "그러나 이 모임은 어디까지나 발 디딜 틈 없이 붐비고 남의 눈에 띄는 행사가 될 작정이었으므로, 수백 명을 맞을 채비가 갖추어졌다."]], [["And now there arose considerable agitation among the Grantlyites whether or no they would attend the episcopal bidding.", "그리하여 이제 그랜틀리파 사이에서는 주교의 부름에 응할 것인가 말 것인가를 두고 적잖은 소동이 일었다."], ["The first feeling with them all was to send the briefest excuses both for themselves and their wives and daughters.", "처음에는 다들 자기 몫은 물론 아내와 딸들 몫까지 가장 짤막한 사절의 말을 보내고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["But by degrees policy prevailed over passion.", "그러나 차츰 정략이 격정을 이겼다."], ["The archdeacon perceived that he would be making a false step if he allowed the cathedral clergy to give the bishop just ground of umbrage.", "부주교는 대성당 성직자들이 주교에게 노여워할 정당한 빌미를 주도록 내버려 둔다면 그것이야말로 제 실책이 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["They all met in conclave and agreed to go.", "그들은 다 함께 모여 의논한 끝에 가기로 뜻을 모았다."], ["They would show that they were willing to respect the office, much as they might dislike the man.", "사람은 아무리 싫더라도 그 직분만은 존중할 뜻이 있음을 보여 주자는 것이었다."], ["They agreed to go.", "그들은 가기로 했다."], ["The old dean would crawl in, if it were but for half an hour.", "늙은 수석 사제도 반 시간만이라도 기어들어 갈 참이었다."], ["The chancellor, treasurer, archdeacon, prebendaries, and minor canons would all go, and would all take their wives. Mr.", "학무 참사원와 재무 참사원과 부주교와 참사회원들과 하급 참사원들이 모두 가고, 모두 아내를 데려가기로 했다."], ["Harding was especially bidden to do so, resolving in his heart to keep himself far removed from Mrs. Proudie.", "하딩 씨에게는 특히 그리하라는 당부가 있었으니, 그는 프라우디 부인에게서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져 있으리라고 마음속으로 다짐했다."], ["And Mrs. Bold was determined to go, though assured by her father that there was no necessity for such a sacrifice on her part.", "그리고 볼드 부인도 가기로 마음을 굳혔는데, 아버지가 딸에게 그런 희생까지 치를 필요는 없다고 일러 주었건만 그러했다."], ["When all Barchester was to be there, neither Eleanor nor Mary Bold understood why they should stay away.", "바체스터 사람이 다 간다는데 엘리너나 메리 볼드나 자기들이 빠질 까닭을 알 수 없었다."], ["Had they not been invited separately?", "자기들에게도 따로 초대장이 오지 않았던가?"], ["And had not a separate little note from the chaplain, couched in the most respectful language, been enclosed with the huge episcopal card?", "게다가 전속 사제가 더할 나위 없이 정중한 말씨로 쓴 짤막한 별지가 그 커다란 주교의 초대 카드에 함께 들어 있지 않았던가?"]], [["And the Stanhopes would be there, one and all.", "그리고 스탠호프 일가는 하나도 빠짐없이 갈 참이었다."], ["Even the lethargic mother would so far bestir herself on such an occasion.", "그 굼뜬 어머니조차 이런 자리라면 그만큼은 몸을 일으킬 것이었다."], ["They had only just arrived.", "그들은 이제 막 도착한 참이었다."], ["The card was at the residence waiting for them.", "초대장은 사택에서 그들을 기다리고 있었다."], ["No one in Barchester had seen them.", "바체스터에서 그들을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["What better opportunity could they have of showing themselves to the Barchester world?", "바체스터 사교계에 자신들을 내보이는 데 이보다 더 좋은 기회가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Some few old friends, such as the archdeacon and his wife, had called and had found the doctor and his eldest daughter, but the élite of the family were not yet known.", "부주교 내외 같은 몇몇 옛 벗이 찾아와 박사와 그 맏딸을 만나 보기는 했으나, 이 집안의 정수라 할 이들은 아직 알려지지 않은 터였다."]], [["The doctor indeed wished in his heart to prevent the signora from accepting the bishop's invitation, but she herself had fully determined that she would accept it.", "박사는 실은 속으로 시뇨라가 주교의 초대에 응하는 것을 막고 싶었으나, 정작 본인은 응하기로 단단히 마음을 정해 놓고 있었다."], ["If her father was ashamed of having his daughter carried into a bishop's palace, she had no such feeling.", "제 딸이 주교관까지 들려 들어가는 것을 아버지가 부끄러워한다 한들, 그녀에게는 그런 감정이 없었다."]], [["\"Indeed, I shall,\"", "“가고말고.”"], ["she had said to her sister who had gently endeavoured to dissuade her, by saying that the company would consist wholly of parsons and parsons' wives.", "오는 사람이라곤 죄다 목사와 목사의 아내들뿐일 거라는 말로 언니가 부드럽게 말리려 하자 그녀가 한 말이었다."], ["\"Parsons, I suppose, are much the same as other men, if you strip them of their black coats; and as to their wives, I dare say they won't trouble me.", "“목사라 해도 그 검은 옷만 벗겨 놓으면 여느 남자와 별로 다를 게 없겠지. 그 아내들로 말하자면, 나를 성가시게 굴지는 못할걸."], ["You may tell Papa I don't at all mean to be left at home.\"", "아버지한테는 나는 집에 남아 있을 생각이 조금도 없다고 전해 줘.”"]], [["Papa was told, and felt that he could do nothing but yield.", "아버지에게 그 말이 전해졌고, 그는 굽히는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없다고 느꼈다."], ["He also felt that it was useless for him now to be ashamed of his children.", "또한 이제 와서 제 자식들을 부끄러워해 봐야 소용없다는 것도 느꼈다."], ["Such as they were, they had become such under his auspices; as he had made his bed, so he must lie upon it; as he had sown his seed, so must he reap his corn.", "그 애들이 그런 모양이 된 것은 제 밑에서 그리된 것이니, 제가 잠자리를 그렇게 폈으면 그 위에 누울 수밖에 없고, 제가 씨를 그렇게 뿌렸으면 그 곡식을 거둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["He did not indeed utter such reflexions in such language, but such was the gist of his thought.", "물론 그가 이런 말로 이런 생각을 입 밖에 낸 것은 아니지만, 그 생각의 요지는 그러했다."], ["It was not because Madeline was a cripple that he shrank from seeing her made one of the bishop's guests, but because he knew that she would practise her accustomed lures, and behave herself in a way that could not fail of being distasteful to the propriety of Englishwomen.", "그가 마들린이 주교의 손님 축에 끼는 것을 꺼린 까닭은 딸이 다리를 쓰지 못해서가 아니라, 딸이 늘 하던 대로 유혹의 수를 부려서 영국 부인네들의 예법으로는 도저히 못마땅해하지 않을 수 없는 짓을 하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["These things had annoyed but not shocked him in Italy.", "이런 일들은 이탈리아에서 그를 성가시게는 했어도 충격을 주지는 않았다."], ["There they had shocked no one; but here in Barchester, here among his fellow parsons, he was ashamed that they should be seen.", "거기서는 아무도 충격을 받지 않았다. 그러나 여기 바체스터에서, 여기 동료 목사들 틈에서는, 그런 것이 남의 눈에 띄는 것이 부끄러웠다."], ["Such had been his feelings, but he repressed them.", "그의 심정은 그러했으나 그는 그것을 눌러 두었다."], ["What if his brother clergymen were shocked!", "형제 성직자들이 충격을 받은들 어떠랴!"], ["They could not take from him his preferment because the manners of his married daughter were too free.", "시집간 딸의 몸가짐이 너무 자유롭다고 해서 그들이 그의 성직록을 빼앗을 수는 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["La Signora Neroni had, at any rate, no fear that she would shock anybody.", "어쨌든 시뇨라 네로니에게는 자기가 누구에게 충격을 줄까 봐 두려워하는 마음이 조금도 없었다."], ["Her ambition was to create a sensation, to have parsons at her feet, seeing that the manhood of Barchester consisted mainly of parsons, and to send, if possible, every parson's wife home with a green fit of jealousy.", "그녀의 야심은 한바탕 소동을 일으키는 것, 바체스터의 남자라는 남자가 대개 목사인 터이니 그 목사들을 제 발치에 꿇리는 것, 그리고 될 수만 있다면 목사의 아내라는 아내는 하나도 남김없이 새파란 질투에 사로잡혀 집으로 돌아가게 하는 것이었다."], ["None could be too old for her, and hardly any too young.", "그녀에게 너무 늙은 남자란 없었고, 너무 젊은 남자도 거의 없었다."], ["None too sanctified, and none too worldly.", "너무 거룩한 남자도 없었고 너무 세속적인 남자도 없었다."], ["She was quite prepared to entrap the bishop himself, and then to turn up her nose at the bishop's wife.", "그녀는 주교 자신을 옭아맨 다음 주교의 부인 앞에서 코웃음을 칠 채비까지 완전히 되어 있었다. 그녀는 성공을 의심하지 않았으니, 여태 늘 성공해 온 까닭이었다."], ["She did not doubt of success, for she had always succeeded; but one thing was absolutely necessary; she must secure the entire use of a sofa.", "다만 한 가지만은 절대로 필요했다. 소파 하나를 통째로 차지해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["The card sent to Dr. and Mrs.", "스탠호프 박사 내외와 그 식구들에게 보낸 초대장은 겉봉에 슬로프 씨의 이름이 적힌 봉투에 담겨 왔다."], ["Stanhope and family had been so sent in an envelope having on the cover Mr. Slope's name.", "시뇨라는 프라우디 부인이 아직 주교관에 와 있지 않으며 전속 사제가 모든 일을 도맡아 처리하고 있다는 것을 곧 알아냈다."], ["The signora soon learnt that Mrs. Proudie was not yet at the palace and that the chaplain was managing everything.", "부인보다는 그에게 청을 넣는 편이 훨씬 그녀의 방식에 맞았으므로, 그녀는 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 짤막한 쪽지를 그에게 써 보냈다."], ["It was much more in her line to apply to him than to the lady, and she accordingly wrote him the prettiest little billet in the world. In five lines she explained everything, declared how impossible it was for her not to be desirous to make the acquaintance of such persons as the Bishop of Barchester and his wife, and she might add also of Mr.", "다섯 줄 안에 그녀는 모든 것을 설명했으니, 바체스터의 주교와 그 부인 같은 분들과, 덧붙여 슬로프 씨와도 알고 지내고 싶지 않기란 자기로서는 얼마나 불가능한 일인지를 밝히고, 자신의 딱한 처지를 그려 보이고는, 소파까지 들려 가게 해 달라고 청하는 이 지나친 당돌함을 프라우디 부인께서 용서해 주시리라 믿는다는 말로 맺었다."], ["Slope, depicted her own grievous state, and concluded by being assured that Mrs. Proudie would forgive her extreme hardihood in petitioning to be allowed to be carried to a sofa.", "그러고는 자신의 그 아름다운 명함 한 장을 동봉했다."], ["She then enclosed one of her beautiful cards. In return she received as polite an answer from Mr.", "답으로 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게서 그에 못지않게 정중한 답장을 받았다."], ["Slope--a sofa should be kept in the large drawing-room, immediately at the top of the grand stairs, especially for her use.", "큰 계단 바로 위 널찍한 응접실에 오로지 그녀만을 위한 소파 하나를 마련해 두겠다는 것이었다."]], [["And now the day of the party had arrived.", "그리하여 이제 야회 날이 왔다."], ["The bishop and his wife came down from town only on the morning of the eventful day, as behoved such great people to do, but Mr. Slope had toiled day and night to see that everything should be in right order.", "주교와 부인은 그런 지체 높은 사람들이 으레 그러하듯 그 뜻깊은 날 아침에야 런던에서 내려왔으나, 슬로프 씨는 모든 것이 제대로 갖추어지도록 밤낮으로 애를 썼다."], ["There had been much to do.", "할 일이 많았다."], ["No company had been seen in the palace since heaven knows when.", "주교관에서 손님을 치른 것이 언제였는지는 하늘이나 알 일이었다."], ["New furniture had been required, new pots and pans, new cups and saucers, new dishes and plates. Mrs.", "새 가구가 필요했고, 새 냄비와 새 팬이, 새 찻잔과 받침이, 새 접시와 그릇이 필요했다."], ["Proudie had at first declared that she would condescend to nothing so vulgar as eating and drinking, but Mr. Slope had talked, or rather written her out of economy.", "프라우디 부인은 처음에 먹고 마시는 따위 상스러운 일에까지 몸을 굽힐 생각은 없다고 선언했으나, 슬로프 씨가 말로, 아니 실은 편지로 그 절약심을 돌려놓았다."], ["Bishops should be given to hospitality, and hospitality meant eating and drinking.", "주교는 마땅히 손님 접대에 힘써야 하며, 손님 접대란 곧 먹고 마시는 것이었다."], ["So the supper was conceded; the guests, however, were to stand as they consumed it.", "그리하여 야식은 허락되었다. 다만 손님들은 선 채로 먹어야 했다."]], [["There were four rooms opening into each other on the first floor of the house, which were denominated the drawing-rooms, the reception-room, and Mrs.", "그 집 2층에는 서로 통하는 방이 넷 있었는데, 응접실 둘과 접견실 하나와 프라우디 부인의 규방이라 불렸다."], ["Proudie's boudoir. In olden days one of these had been Bishop Grantly's bedroom, and another his common sitting-room and study.", "옛날에는 그중 하나가 그랜틀리 주교의 침실이었고 또 하나는 그의 거실 겸 서재였다."], ["The present bishop, however, had been moved down into a back parlour and had been given to understand that he could very well receive his clergy in the dining-room, should they arrive in too large a flock to be admitted into his small sanctum.", "그러나 지금 주교는 뒤채의 작은 거실로 옮겨졌고, 성직자들이 제 좁은 골방에 다 들어가지 못할 만큼 떼 지어 몰려오거든 식당에서 맞으면 그만이라는 말을 들어야 했다."], ["He had been unwilling to yield, but after a short debate had yielded.", "그는 굽히기를 꺼렸으나, 잠깐 옥신각신한 끝에 굽혔다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie's heart beat high as she inspected her suite of rooms.", "프라우디 부인은 제 방들을 둘러보며 가슴이 한껏 부풀었다."], ["They were really very magnificent, or at least would be so by candlelight, and they had nevertheless been got up with commendable economy.", "그 방들은 정말이지 대단히 웅장했다. 적어도 촛불을 켜 놓으면 그렇게 보일 것이었고, 게다가 칭찬할 만큼 알뜰하게 꾸며 놓은 것이었다."], ["Large rooms when full of people and full of light look well, because they are large, and are full, and are light.", "큰 방은 사람이 가득하고 빛이 가득하면 보기가 좋은 법이니, 크고 가득 차고 밝기 때문이다."], ["Small rooms are those which require costly fittings and rich furniture. Mrs.", "값비싼 장식과 호사스러운 가구를 요구하는 것은 작은 방이다."], ["Proudie knew this, and made the most of it; she had therefore a huge gas lamp with a dozen burners hanging from each of the ceilings.", "프라우디 부인은 이것을 알았고 그것을 최대한으로 활용했다. 그래서 그녀는 방마다 천장에 등구 열두 개짜리 커다란 가스등을 매달아 놓았다."]], [["People were to arrive at ten, supper was to last from twelve till one, and at half-past one everybody was to be gone.", "손님들은 10시에 도착하고, 야식은 12시부터 1시까지 이어지고, 1시 반에는 모두 물러가기로 되어 있었다."], ["Carriages were to come in at the gate in the town and depart at the gate outside.", "마차는 시내 쪽 문으로 들어와 바깥쪽 문으로 나가기로 했다."], ["They were desired to take up at a quarter before one.", "마차더러 손님을 태우러 오라는 시각은 1시 15분 전이었다."], ["It was managed excellently, and Mr. Slope was invaluable.", "모든 것이 훌륭하게 처리되었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없이 요긴한 사람이었다."]], [["At half-past nine the bishop and his wife and their three daughters entered the great reception-room, and very grand and very solemn they were.", "9시 반에 주교와 부인과 세 딸이 널찍한 접견실로 들어섰는데, 그 꼴이 여간 장중하고 여간 엄숙하지 않았다."], ["Mr. Slope was downstairs giving the last orders about the wine.", "슬로프 씨는 아래층에서 포도주에 관한 마지막 지시를 내리고 있었다."], ["He well understood that curates and country vicars with their belongings did not require so generous an article as the dignitaries of the close.", "그는 부목사며 시골 교구 사제며 그 딸린 식구들에게는 대성당 경내의 고위 성직자들에게 내는 것만큼 후한 물건이 필요치 않다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["There is a useful gradation in such things, and Marsala at 20s. a dozen did very well for the exterior supplementary tables in the corner.", "이런 일에는 요긴한 등급이라는 것이 있게 마련이니, 구석의 바깥쪽 보조 탁자에는 한 다스에 20실링짜리 마르살라면 아주 넉넉했다."]], [["\"Bishop,\"", "“주교님.” 주교가 자리에 앉으려 하자 부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady, as his lordship sat himself down, \"don't sit on that sofa, if you please; it is to be kept separate for a lady.\"", "“미안하지만 그 소파에는 앉지 마세요. 그건 어떤 부인을 위해 따로 비워 두어야 하는 것이니까요.”"]], [["The bishop jumped up and seated himself on a cane-bottomed chair.", "주교는 벌떡 일어나 등나무 자리 의자에 옮겨 앉았다."], ["\"A lady?\"", "“부인이라니?”"], ["he inquired meekly; \"do you mean one particular lady, my dear?\"", "그가 순순히 물었다. “여보, 어떤 특정한 부인을 말하는 게요?”"]], [["\"Yes, Bishop, one particular lady,\"", "“그래요, 주교님, 어떤 특정한 부인이에요.”"], ["said his wife, disdaining to explain.", "아내는 설명하기를 마다하며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"She has got no legs, Papa,\"", "“그분은 다리가 없대요, 아버지.”"], ["said the youngest daughter, tittering.", "막내딸이 킥킥거리며 말했다."]], [["\"No legs!\"", "“다리가 없다고!”"], ["said the bishop, opening his eyes.", "주교가 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다."]], [["\"Nonsense, Netta, what stuff you talk,\" said Olivia.", "“말도 안 되는 소리, 네티, 무슨 그런 헛소리를 하니.”"], ["\"She has got legs, but she can't use them.", "올리비아가 말했다. “다리는 있는데 쓰지를 못하는 거야."], ["She has always to be kept lying down, and three or four men carry her about everywhere.\"", "늘 누워 있어야 해서 어디를 가든 남자 서넛이 들고 다닌대.”"]], [["\"Laws, how odd!\"", "“어머나, 별일이야!”"], ["said Augusta.", "어거스타가 말했다."], ["\"Always carried about by four men!", "“늘 남자 넷한테 들려 다닌다니!"], ["I'm sure I shouldn't like it.", "나 같으면 정말 싫을 것 같아."], ["Am I right behind, Mamma?", "어머니, 제 뒤가 제대로 되었나요?"], ["I feel as if I was open;\"", "벌어진 것 같은 느낌이 드는데요.”"], ["and she turned her back to her anxious parent.", "그러면서 그녀는 근심 어린 어머니에게 등을 돌려 보였다."]], [["\"Open!", "“벌어졌지!"], ["To be sure you are,\"", "그렇고말고.”"], ["said she, \"and a yard of petticoat strings hanging out.", "어머니가 말했다. “속치마 끈이 1야드나 빠져나와 있잖니."], ["I don't know why I pay such high wages to Mrs. Richards if she can't take the trouble to see whether or no you are fit to be looked at,\"", "네가 남에게 보일 만한 꼴인지 아닌지 살펴보는 수고조차 하지 않는다면, 내가 리처즈 부인에게 무엇 하러 그렇게 후한 삯을 주는지 모르겠구나.”"], ["and Mrs. Proudie poked the strings here, and twitched the dress there, and gave her daughter a shove and a shake, and then pronounced it all right.", "그러고서 프라우디 부인은 이쪽 끈을 쑤셔 넣고 저쪽 옷자락을 잡아당기고 딸을 떠밀었다 흔들었다 하고는, 이제 되었다고 선언했다."]], [["\"But,\"", "“그런데 말이오.”"], ["rejoined the bishop, who was dying with curiosity about the mysterious lady and her legs, \"who is it that is to have the sofa?", "그 알 수 없는 부인과 그 다리에 대한 궁금증으로 죽을 지경이 된 주교가 다시 입을 열었다. “대체 그 소파를 쓸 사람이 누구요?"], ["What's her name, Netta?\"", "네티야, 그 사람 이름이 무엇이냐?”"]], [["A thundering rap at the front door interrupted the conversation.", "현관문을 우레같이 두드리는 소리가 이야기를 끊었다."], ["Mrs. Proudie stood up and shook herself gently, and touched her cap on each side as she looked in the mirror.", "프라우디 부인은 일어서서 몸을 가볍게 흔들어 매무새를 잡고, 거울을 들여다보며 두건의 양쪽을 매만졌다."], ["Each of the girls stood on tiptoe and rearranged the bows on their bosoms, and Mr. Slope rushed upstairs three steps at a time.", "딸들은 저마다 발끝으로 서서 가슴에 단 리본을 고쳐 달았고, 슬로프 씨는 한 번에 세 계단씩 뛰어 위층으로 올라왔다."]], [["\"But who is it, Netta?\"", "“그런데 그게 누구냐, 네티야?”"], ["whispered the bishop to his youngest daughter.", "주교가 막내딸에게 속삭였다."]], [["\"La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni,\"", "“라 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니예요.”"], ["whispered back the daughter; \"and mind you don't let anyone sit upon the sofa.\"", "딸이 속삭여 답했다. “그리고 아무도 그 소파에 앉지 못하게 하셔야 해요.”"]], [["\"La Signora Madeline Vicinironi!\"", "“라 시뇨라 마들린 비시니로니라!”"], ["muttered to himself the bewildered prelate.", "얼떨떨해진 고위 성직자는 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["Had he been told that the Begum of Oude was to be there, or Queen Pomara of the Western Isles, he could not have been more astonished.", "아우드의 베굼이 온다고 들었다 한들, 서쪽 섬나라의 포마라 여왕이 온다고 들었다 한들 이보다 더 놀라지는 않았을 것이다."], ["La Signora Madeline Vicinironi, who, having no legs to stand on, had bespoken a sofa in his drawing-room!", "라 시뇨라 마들린 비시니로니라니, 딛고 설 다리가 없어서 제 응접실의 소파를 미리 청해 둔 여인이라니!"], ["Who could she be?", "대체 누구란 말인가?"], ["He however could now make no further inquiry, as Dr. and Mrs.", "그러나 그는 이제 더 캐물을 수가 없었으니, 스탠호프 박사 내외가 왔다고 아뢰는 소리가 났기 때문이다."], ["Stanhope were announced. They had been sent on out of the way a little before the time, in order that the signora might have plenty of time to get herself conveniently packed into the carriage.", "그 내외는 시뇨라가 넉넉한 시간을 들여 편안하게 마차에 꾸려져 실릴 수 있도록, 시각보다 조금 일찍 앞질러 보내진 것이었다."]], [["The bishop was all smiles for the prebendary's wife, and the bishop's wife was all smiles for the prebendary. Mr.", "주교는 참사회원의 부인에게 온통 미소였고, 주교의 부인은 참사회원에게 온통 미소였다."], ["Slope was presented and was delighted to make the acquaintance of one of whom he had heard so much.", "슬로프 씨가 소개되었고, 말씀을 그토록 많이 들은 분과 알게 되어 기쁘다고 했다."], ["The doctor bowed very low, and then looked as though he could not return the compliment as regarded Mr. Slope, of whom, indeed, he had heard nothing.", "박사는 아주 깊이 허리를 굽혀 인사한 다음, 슬로프 씨에 관해서는 그 인사말을 되돌려 줄 수 없다는 듯한 표정을 지었으니, 실상 그는 슬로프 씨에 관해 들은 바가 하나도 없었던 것이다."], ["The doctor, in spite of his long absence, knew an English gentleman when he saw him.", "박사는 그토록 오래 나가 있었어도 영국 신사를 보면 알아보는 사람이었다."]], [["And then the guests came in shoals: Mr. and Mrs.", "그러고 나자 손님들이 떼 지어 몰려들었다."], ["Quiverful and their three grown daughters. Mr.", "퀴버풀 씨 내외와 장성한 세 딸."], ["and Mrs. Chadwick and their three daughters.", "채드윅 씨 내외와 세 딸."], ["The burly chancellor and his wife and clerical son from Oxford.", "몸집 큰 학무 참사원와 그 부인과 옥스퍼드에서 온 성직자 아들."], ["The meagre little doctor without incumbrance.", "딸린 식구 하나 없는 여윈 체구의 자그마한 박사."], ["Mr. Harding with Eleanor and Miss Bold.", "엘리너와 볼드 양을 데리고 온 하딩 씨."], ["The dean leaning on a gaunt spinster, his only child now living with him, a lady very learned in stones, ferns, plants, and vermin, and who had written a book about petals.", "여윈 노처녀에게 몸을 기댄 수석 사제, 그 딸은 이제 그와 함께 사는 하나뿐인 자식으로, 돌과 양치식물과 초목과 벌레에 매우 밝은 부인이며 꽃잎에 관한 책을 한 권 쓴 이였다."], ["A wonderful woman in her way was Miss Trefoil.", "트리폴 양은 그 나름으로 놀라운 여인이었다."], ["Mr. Finnie, the attorney, with his wife, was to be seen, much to the dismay of many who had never met him in a drawing-room before.", "변호사 피니 씨도 부인과 함께 눈에 띄었는데, 그를 응접실에서 마주친 적이 한 번도 없던 많은 이들이 여간 당황하지 않았다."], ["The five Barchester doctors were all there, and old Scalpen, the retired apothecary and tooth-drawer, who was first taught to consider himself as belonging to the higher orders by the receipt of the bishop's card.", "바체스터의 의사 다섯이 모두 왔고, 은퇴한 약제사 겸 이 뽑는 사람인 늙은 스칼펜도 왔으니, 그는 주교의 초대장을 받고서야 비로소 제가 상류층에 속한다고 여기는 법을 배운 사람이었다."], ["Then came the archdeacon and his wife with their elder daughter Griselda, a slim, pale, retiring girl of seventeen who kept close to her mother, and looked out on the world with quiet watchful eyes, one who gave promise of much beauty when time should have ripened it.", "이어서 부주교 내외가 맏딸 그리젤다를 데리고 왔는데, 열일곱 살의 이 아가씨는 호리호리하고 창백하고 남 앞에 나서지 않는 성품이라 어머니 곁에 바짝 붙어 서서 조용히 살피는 눈으로 세상을 내다보았으니, 세월이 무르익히기만 하면 대단한 미인이 되리라는 조짐이 있었다."]], [["And so the rooms became full, and knots were formed, and every newcomer paid his respects to my lord and passed on, not presuming to occupy too much of the great man's attention. The archdeacon shook hands very heartily with Dr.", "그리하여 방들은 가득 찼고, 여기저기 무리가 지어졌고, 새로 오는 이마다 주교께 인사를 올리고는 그 어른의 관심을 너무 오래 붙들어 두는 무례를 범하지 않으려 지나쳐 갔다."], ["Stanhope, and Mrs. Grantly seated herself by the doctor's wife.", "부주교는 스탠호프 박사와 아주 정답게 악수를 나누었고, 그랜틀리 부인은 박사의 부인 곁에 자리를 잡았다."], ["And Mrs. Proudie moved about with well-regulated grace, measuring out the quantity of her favours to the quality of her guests, just as Mr.", "그리고 프라우디 부인은 잘 절제된 우아함으로 이리저리 옮겨 다니며, 슬로프 씨가 포도주를 두고 하던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 손님의 격에 맞추어 제 호의의 분량을 되어 나누어 주었다."], ["Slope had been doing with the wine. But the sofa was still empty, and five-and-twenty ladies and five gentlemen had been courteously warned off it by the mindful chaplain.", "그러나 소파는 여전히 비어 있었고, 스물다섯 명의 부인과 다섯 명의 신사가 빈틈없는 전속 사제에게 정중하게 저지당한 뒤였다."]], [["\"Why doesn't she come?\"", "“어째서 오지 않는 게지?”"], ["said the bishop to himself.", "주교는 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["His mind was so preoccupied with the signora that he hardly remembered how to behave himself en bishop.", "그의 마음이 온통 시뇨라에게 사로잡혀 있어서, 주교답게 처신하는 법조차 거의 잊을 지경이었다."]], [["At last a carriage dashed up to the hall steps with a very different manner of approach from that of any other vehicle that had been there that evening.", "마침내 마차 한 대가 현관 층계 앞으로 내달아 왔는데, 그날 저녁 그곳에 온 어떤 탈것과도 사뭇 다른 방식이었다."], ["A perfect commotion took place.", "그야말로 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌다."], ["The doctor, who heard it as he was standing in the drawing-room, knew that his daughter was coming, and retired into the furthest corner, where he might not see her entrance.", "응접실에 서 있다가 그 소리를 들은 박사는 제 딸이 오는 줄을 알고, 딸이 들어서는 것을 보지 않아도 될 가장 먼 구석으로 물러났다."], ["Mrs. Proudie perked herself up, feeling that some important piece of business was in hand.", "프라우디 부인은 무언가 중대한 일이 벌어지고 있음을 느끼고 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다."], ["The bishop was instinctively aware that La Signora Vicinironi was come at last, and Mr. Slope hurried into the hall to give his assistance.", "주교는 라 시뇨라 비시니로니가 드디어 왔다는 것을 본능적으로 알아차렸고, 슬로프 씨는 거들려고 현관으로 서둘러 나갔다."]], [["He was, however, nearly knocked down and trampled on by the cortège that he encountered on the hall steps.", "그러나 그는 현관 층계에서 마주친 그 행렬에 하마터면 떠밀려 넘어져 밟힐 뻔했다."], ["He got himself picked up, as well as he could, and followed the cortège upstairs.", "그는 그런대로 몸을 추슬러 일으키고는 그 행렬을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The signora was carried head foremost, her head being the care of her brother and an Italian manservant who was accustomed to the work; her feet were in the care of the lady's maid and the lady's Italian page; and Charlotte Stanhope followed to see that all was done with due grace and decorum.", "시뇨라는 머리부터 앞세워 옮겨졌는데, 머리는 오라비와 이 일에 익숙한 이탈리아인 하인이 맡았고, 발은 하녀와 이탈리아인 시동이 맡았으며, 샬럿 스탠호프가 모든 일이 마땅한 품위와 예법에 맞게 이루어지는지 살피며 뒤따랐다."], ["In this manner they climbed easily into the drawing-room, and a broad way through the crowd having been opened, the signora rested safely on her couch.", "이런 식으로 그들은 손쉽게 응접실까지 올라갔고, 사람들 사이로 넓은 길이 열리자 시뇨라는 무사히 제 자리에 놓였다."], ["She had sent a servant beforehand to learn whether it was a right- or a left-hand sofa, for it required that she should dress accordingly, particularly as regarded her bracelets.", "그녀는 그것이 오른쪽 소파인지 왼쪽 소파인지 알아 오라고 미리 하인을 보내 두었으니, 그에 맞추어 옷을 차려입어야 했기 때문이고, 특히 팔찌가 그러했다."]], [["And very becoming her dress was.", "그리고 그 차림은 참으로 잘 어울렸다."], ["It was white velvet, without any other garniture than rich white lace worked with pearls across her bosom, and the same round the armlets of her dress.", "흰 벨벳이었고, 가슴께에 진주를 놓아 짠 값진 흰 레이스 말고는 다른 장식이 없었으며, 소매 언저리에도 같은 것을 둘렀다."], ["Across her brow she wore a band of red velvet, on the centre of which shone a magnificent Cupid in mosaic, the tints of whose wings were of the most lovely azure, and the colour of his chubby cheeks the clearest pink.", "이마에는 붉은 벨벳 띠를 둘렀는데, 그 한복판에는 모자이크로 새긴 화려한 큐피드가 빛나고 있었으니, 날개의 빛깔은 더없이 고운 하늘빛이었고 통통한 두 볼의 빛깔은 더없이 맑은 분홍빛이었다."], ["On the one arm which her position required her to expose she wore three magnificent bracelets, each of different stones.", "그 자세 때문에 드러낼 수밖에 없는 한쪽 팔에는 저마다 돌이 다른 화려한 팔찌 셋을 끼고 있었다."], ["Beneath her on the sofa, and over the cushion and head of it, was spread a crimson silk mantle or shawl, which went under her whole body and concealed her feet.", "소파 위 그녀의 몸 아래로, 그리고 쿠션과 머리맡을 덮어, 진홍빛 비단 망토인지 숄인지가 펼쳐져 온몸 밑으로 들어가서는 발을 감추고 있었다."], ["Dressed as she was and looking as she did, so beautiful and yet so motionless, with the pure brilliancy of her white dress brought out and strengthened by the colour beneath it, with that lovely head, and those large, bold, bright, staring eyes, it was impossible that either man or woman should do other than look at her.", "그렇게 차려입고 그런 모습으로, 그토록 아름다우면서도 그토록 미동도 없이, 흰 옷의 티 없는 광채가 그 아래 깔린 빛깔에 도드라지고 힘을 얻은 채로, 그 사랑스러운 머리와 크고 대담하고 반짝이며 똑바로 응시하는 두 눈을 하고 있으니, 남자든 여자든 그녀를 바라보지 않고는 배길 도리가 없었다."]], [["Neither man nor woman for some minutes did do other.", "몇 분 동안은 남자도 여자도 달리 어쩌지 못했다."]], [["Her bearers too were worthy of note.", "그녀를 떠메고 온 이들도 눈여겨볼 만했다."], ["The three servants were Italian, and though perhaps not peculiar in their own country, were very much so in the palace at Barchester.", "하인 셋은 이탈리아 사람이었는데, 제 나라에서라면 별날 것이 없을지 몰라도 바체스터의 주교관에서는 여간 별나지 않았다."], ["The man especially attracted notice and created a doubt in the mind of some whether he were a friend or a domestic.", "특히 사내가 사람들의 눈길을 끌었고, 그가 벗인지 하인인지 몇몇 사람의 마음에 의심을 불러일으켰다."], ["The same doubt was felt as to Ethelbert.", "에설버트에 대해서도 같은 의심이 일었다."], ["The man was attired in a loose-fitting, common, black-cloth morning-coat.", "사내는 헐렁하게 지은 흔한 검은 나사 모닝코트 차림이었다."], ["He had a jaunty, fat, well-pleased, clean face on which no atom of beard appeared, and he wore round his neck a loose, black silk neck-handkerchief.", "그는 경쾌하고 살집 있고 흡족해 보이는 말끔한 얼굴을 하고 있어서 수염이라고는 한 오라기도 보이지 않았고, 목에는 헐렁한 검은 비단 목수건을 두르고 있었다."], ["The bishop essayed to make him a bow, but the man, who was well trained, took no notice of him and walked out of the room quite at his ease, followed by the woman and the boy.", "주교가 그에게 인사를 해 보려 했으나, 잘 훈련된 사내는 주교를 아랑곳하지 않고 아주 태연하게 방을 걸어 나갔으며, 여자와 소년이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Ethelbert Stanhope was dressed in light blue from head to foot.", "에설버트 스탠호프는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 하늘색으로 차려입었다."], ["He had on the loosest possible blue coat, cut square like a shooting coat, and very short. It was lined with silk of azure blue.", "그는 사냥복처럼 네모지게 재단한, 아주 짧고 더할 나위 없이 헐렁한 푸른 상의를 걸쳤는데, 안감은 하늘빛 비단이었다."], ["He had on a blue satin waistcoat, a blue neck-handkerchief which was fastened beneath his throat with a coral ring, and very loose blue trousers which almost concealed his feet.", "푸른 공단 조끼를 입었고, 목 아래를 산호 고리로 여민 푸른 목수건을 둘렀으며, 발이 거의 가려질 만큼 아주 헐렁한 푸른 바지를 입었다."], ["His soft, glossy beard was softer and more glossy than ever.", "부드럽고 윤나는 그의 턱수염은 그 어느 때보다도 부드럽고 윤이 났다."]], [["The bishop, who had made one mistake, thought that he also was a servant and therefore tried to make way for him to pass.", "한 번 실수를 저지른 주교는 이 사람도 하인인 줄 알고 그가 지나가도록 길을 비켜 주려 했다."], ["But Ethelbert soon corrected the error.", "그러나 에설버트가 곧 그 오해를 바로잡아 주었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI. Mrs. Proudie's Reception--Concluded", "# 제11장. 프라우디 부인의 접견회 — 하"]], [["\"Bishop of Barchester, I presume?\"", "“바체스터 주교님이시겠지요?”"], ["said Bertie Stanhope, putting out his hand frankly; \"I am delighted to make your acquaintance.", "버티 스탠호프가 스스럼없이 손을 내밀며 말했다. “뵙게 되어 참으로 반갑습니다."], ["We are in rather close quarters here, a'nt we?\"", "여기는 자리가 꽤나 비좁군요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["In truth they were.", "사실이 그러했다."], ["They had been crowded up behind the head of the sofa--the bishop in waiting to receive his guest, and the other in carrying her--and they now had hardly room to move themselves.", "두 사람은 소파 머리맡 뒤쪽으로 밀려나 있었으니, 한쪽은 손님을 맞으려고 기다리다가, 다른 한쪽은 그 손님을 나르다가 그리된 것인데, 이제는 제 몸을 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다."]], [["The bishop gave his hand quickly, made his little studied bow, and was delighted to make--He couldn't go on, for he did not know whether his friend was a signor, or a count or a prince.", "주교는 얼른 손을 내밀고 늘 하던 대로 다듬어 둔 작은 인사를 하며, 뵙게 되어 반갑— 하고는 더 나아가지 못했다. 이 벗이 시뇨르인지 백작인지 공작인지 알 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["\"My sister really puts you all to great trouble,\"", "“누이 때문에 여러분이 정말 큰 수고를 하시는군요.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"Not at all!\"", "“천만의 말씀입니다!”"], ["The bishop was delighted to have the opportunity of welcoming La Signora Vicinironi--so at least he said--and attempted to force his way round to the front of the sofa.", "주교는 라 시뇨라 비시니로니를 맞이하게 되어 더없이 기쁘다고 — 적어도 말은 그렇게 했다 — 하고는 소파 앞쪽으로 비집고 돌아가려 애썼다."], ["He had, at any rate, learnt that his strange guests were brother and sister.", "어쨌든 이 기이한 손님들이 남매라는 것만은 알아냈다."], ["The man, he presumed, must be Signor Vicinironi--or count, or prince, as it might be.", "남자 쪽은 시뇨르 비시니로니이거나, 아니면 사정에 따라 백작이나 공작이겠거니 짐작했다."], ["It was wonderful what good English he spoke.", "영어를 그토록 잘하는 것이 놀라웠다."], ["There was just a twang of foreign accent, and no more.", "외국식 억양이 살짝 묻어날 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다."]], [["\"Do you like Barchester, on the whole?\"", "“대체로 보아 바체스터가 마음에 드십니까?”"], ["asked Bertie.", "버티가 물었다."]], [["The bishop, looking dignified, said that he did like Barchester.", "주교는 위엄 있는 표정을 지으며, 바체스터가 마음에 든다고 말했다."]], [["\"You've not been here very long, I believe,\"", "“여기 오신 지는 얼마 되지 않으셨지요, 아마?”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"No--not long,\"", "“예, 얼마 되지 않았습니다.”"], ["said the bishop and tried again to make his way between the back of the sofa and a heavy rector, who was staring over it at the grimaces of the signora.", "주교는 그렇게 말하고, 소파 등받이와 육중한 교구 목사 사이로 다시 한번 빠져나가 보려 했다. 그 목사는 소파 너머로 시뇨라의 갖가지 표정을 넋 놓고 바라보는 중이었다."]], [["\"You weren't a bishop before, were you?\"", "“전에는 주교가 아니셨지요, 그렇지요?”"]], [["Dr. Proudie explained that this was the first diocese he had held.", "프라우디 박사는 이번이 자신이 맡은 첫 교구라고 설명했다."]], [["\"Ah--I thought so,\"", "“아, 그럴 줄 알았습니다.”"], ["said Bertie, \"but you are changed about sometimes, a'nt you?\"", "버티가 말했다. “그래도 이따금 자리를 옮기시기도 하지요, 안 그렇습니까?”"]], [["\"Translations are occasionally made,\" said Dr.", "“전임이 이따금 이루어지기는 합니다.”"], ["Proudie, \"but not so frequently as in former days.\"", "프라우디 박사가 말했다. “그러나 예전만큼 잦지는 않지요.”"]], [["\"They've cut them all down to pretty nearly the same figure, haven't they?\"", "“요즘은 그 자리들 값을 죄다 거의 같은 액수로 깎아 놓았다지요?”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["To this the bishop could not bring himself to make any answer, but again attempted to move the rector.", "여기에는 주교도 차마 아무 대답을 내놓을 수 없어, 다시 한번 그 목사를 밀어 보려 했다."]], [["\"But the work, I suppose, is different?\"", "“하지만 일이야 자리마다 다르겠지요?”"], ["continued Bertie.", "버티가 이어 말했다."], ["\"Is there much to do here, at Barchester?\"", "“여기 바체스터는 할 일이 많습니까?”"], ["This was said exactly in the tone that a young Admiralty clerk might use in asking the same question of a brother acolyte at the Treasury.", "이는 해군성의 젊은 서기가 재무성의 동료 신참에게 똑같은 질문을 던질 때 쓸 법한 바로 그 말투였다."]], [["\"The work of a bishop of the Church of England,\"", "“잉글랜드 국교회 주교의 일은,”"], ["said Dr. Proudie with considerable dignity, \"is not easy.", "프라우디 박사가 자못 위엄을 갖추어 말했다."], ["The responsibility which he has to bear is very great indeed.\"", "“쉽지 않습니다. 짊어져야 할 책임이 참으로 막중하지요.”"]], [["\"Is it?\"", "“그렇습니까?”"], ["said Bertie, opening wide his wonderful blue eyes.", "버티가 그 놀라운 푸른 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다."], ["\"Well, I never was afraid of responsibility.", "“글쎄요, 저는 책임이라면 겁내 본 적이 없습니다."], ["I once had thoughts of being a bishop, myself.\"", "저도 한때는 주교가 되어 볼까 생각한 적이 있지요.”"]], [["\"Had thoughts of being a bishop!\"", "“주교가 되어 볼까 생각을 하셨다니!”"], ["said Dr. Proudie, much amazed.", "프라우디 박사가 몹시 놀라 말했다."]], [["\"That is, a parson--a parson first, you know, and a bishop afterwards.", "“그러니까 목사 말입니다. 아시다시피 먼저 목사가 되고 나중에 주교가 되는 것이지요."], ["If I had once begun, I'd have stuck to it.", "한번 시작했더라면 끝까지 붙어 있었을 겁니다."], ["But, on the whole, I like the Church of Rome the best.\"", "하지만 대체로 저는 로마 교회가 제일 마음에 듭니다.”"]], [["The bishop could not discuss the point, so he remained silent.", "주교로서는 그 문제를 논할 수 없었으므로 잠자코 있었다."]], [["\"Now, there's my father,\"", "“가령 저희 아버지를 보십시오.”"], ["continued Bertie; \"he hasn't stuck to it.", "버티가 이어 말했다. “아버지는 끝까지 붙어 있지 않으셨지요."], ["I fancy he didn't like saying the same thing over so often.", "같은 말을 그토록 자주 되풀이하는 것이 싫으셨던 모양입니다."], ["By the by, Bishop, have you seen my father?\"", "그건 그렇고 주교님, 저희 아버지를 뵌 적이 있으십니까?”"]], [["The bishop was more amazed than ever.", "주교는 어느 때보다도 더 놀랐다."], ["Had he seen his father?", "그의 아버지를 본 적이 있느냐고? “없습니다.”"], ["\"No,\"", "그는 대답했다."], ["he replied; he had not yet had the pleasure: he hoped he might; and, as he said so, he resolved to bear heavy on that fat, immovable rector, if ever he had the power of doing so.", "아직 그런 기쁨을 누리지 못했으며, 그리되기를 바란다고. 그렇게 말하면서 그는, 언젠가 그럴 힘이 생긴다면 저 뚱뚱하고 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 목사를 호되게 눌러 주리라 마음먹었다."]], [["\"He's in the room somewhere,\"", "“아버지는 이 방 어딘가에 계십니다.”"], ["said Bertie, \"and he'll turn up soon.", "버티가 말했다. “곧 나타나시겠지요."], ["By the by, do you know much about the Jews?\"", "그건 그렇고, 유대인에 대해서는 잘 아십니까?”"]], [["At last the bishop saw a way out.", "마침내 주교는 빠져나갈 길을 보았다."], ["\"I beg your pardon,\"", "“실례하겠습니다.”"], ["said he, \"but I'm forced to go round the room.\"", "그가 말했다. “아무래도 방을 한 바퀴 돌아야 해서요.”"]], [["\"Well--I believe I'll follow in your wake,\"", "“그럼 저도 그 뒤를 따라가야겠군요.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"Terribly hot--isn't it?\"", "“지독하게 덥지 않습니까?”"], ["This he addressed to the fat rector with whom he had brought himself into the closest contact.", "이 말은 그가 바짝 붙어 서게 된 뚱뚱한 목사에게 건넨 것이었다."], ["\"They've got this sofa into the worst possible part of the room; suppose we move it.", "“이 소파를 방에서 가장 나쁜 자리에 놓아 두었군요. 우리가 좀 옮겨 봅시다."], ["Take care, Madeline.\"", "조심해, 마들린.”"]], [["The sofa had certainly been so placed that those who were behind it found great difficulty in getting out; there was but a narrow gangway, which one person could stop.", "과연 소파는 그 뒤에 있는 사람들이 빠져나오기가 여간 어렵지 않게 놓여 있었다. 통로라고는 한 사람이면 막을 수 있는 좁은 틈 하나뿐이었다."], ["This was a bad arrangement, and one which Bertie thought it might be well to improve.", "이는 잘못된 배치였고, 버티가 보기에는 손을 좀 보는 편이 나을 배치였다."]], [["\"Take care, Madeline,\"", "“조심해, 마들린.”"], ["said he, and turning to the fat rector, added, \"Just help me with a slight push.\"", "그가 말하고는 뚱뚱한 목사를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. “가볍게 한 번 미는 것만 좀 도와주십시오.”"]], [["The rector's weight was resting on the sofa and unwittingly lent all its impetus to accelerate and increase the motion which Bertie intentionally originated.", "목사의 체중이 소파에 실려 있던 터라, 버티가 일부러 일으킨 그 움직임에 저도 모르게 온 힘을 보태어 속도와 기세를 더해 주었다."], ["The sofa rushed from its moorings and ran half-way into the middle of the room.", "소파는 제자리를 박차고 나가 방 한가운데까지 절반이나 달려 나갔다."], ["Mrs. Proudie was standing with Mr.", "프라우디 부인은 슬로프 씨와 함께 시뇨라 앞에 서서 너그럽고 사근사근하게 굴어 보려던 참이었다."], ["Slope in front of the signora, and had been trying to be condescending and sociable; but she was not in the very best of tempers, for she found that, whenever she spoke to the lady, the lady replied by speaking to Mr. Slope.", "그러나 기분이 그리 좋지는 않았으니, 자신이 이 부인에게 말을 걸 때마다 부인이 슬로프 씨를 향해 대답한다는 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Slope was a favourite, no doubt, but Mrs.", "슬로프 씨가 총애를 받는 것이야 틀림없지만, 프라우디 부인으로서는 전속 사제보다 덜 대접받을 생각이 조금도 없었다."], ["Proudie had no idea of being less thought of than the chaplain. She was beginning to be stately, stiff, and offended, when unfortunately the castor of the sofa caught itself in her lace train, and carried away there is no saying how much of her garniture.", "그리하여 부인이 위엄을 세우고 뻣뻣해지며 언짢아지기 시작할 무렵, 불행히도 소파의 바퀴가 그 레이스 자락에 걸려, 얼마나 되는지 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼의 장식을 통째로 뜯어 갔다."], ["Gathers were heard to go, stitches to crack, plaits to fly open, flounces were seen to fall, and breadths to expose themselves; a long ruin of rent lace disfigured the carpet, and still clung to the vile wheel on which the sofa moved.", "주름이 뜯기는 소리, 바느질이 터지는 소리, 겹주름이 활짝 벌어지는 소리가 들렸고, 프릴이 떨어지고 옷폭이 드러나는 것이 보였다. 찢긴 레이스의 긴 잔해가 양탄자를 흉하게 어지럽힌 채, 소파를 굴린 그 고약한 바퀴에 여전히 매달려 있었다."]], [["So, when a granite battery is raised, excellent to the eyes of warfaring men, is its strength and symmetry admired.", "화강암 포대를 쌓아 올리면 싸움터의 사내들 눈에 흡족하여, 그 견고함과 균형이 찬탄을 받는 법이다."], ["It is the work of years.", "그것은 여러 해에 걸친 공이다."], ["Its neat embrasures, its finished parapets, its casemated stories show all the skill of modern science.", "말끔한 총안, 야무지게 마무리된 흉벽, 포곽을 갖춘 층층은 근대 과학의 온갖 솜씨를 보여 준다."], ["But, anon, a small spark is applied to the treacherous fusee--a cloud of dust arises to the heavens--and then nothing is to be seen but dirt and dust and ugly fragments.", "그러나 이윽고 작은 불티 하나가 그 믿을 수 없는 도화선에 닿으면, 먼지구름이 하늘로 솟아오르고, 그다음에는 흙먼지와 흉한 파편밖에는 보이지 않는다."]], [["We know what was the wrath of Juno when her beauty was despised.", "우리는 유노가 제 아름다움을 무시당했을 때 어떠한 분노에 사로잡혔는지 알고 있다."], ["We know to what storms of passion even celestial minds can yield.", "천상의 마음조차 어떠한 격정의 폭풍에 굴복할 수 있는지도 알고 있다."], ["As Juno may have looked at Paris on Mount Ida, so did Mrs. Proudie look on Ethelbert Stanhope when he pushed the leg of the sofa into her lace train.", "이다산에서 유노가 파리스를 바라보았을 법한 바로 그 눈으로, 프라우디 부인은 소파 다리를 제 레이스 자락에 밀어 넣은 에설버트 스탠호프를 바라보았다."]], [["\"Oh, you idiot, Bertie!\"", "“아이고, 이 멍청아, 버티!”"], ["said the signora, seeing what had been done and what were to be the consequences.", "무슨 일이 벌어졌으며 그 결과가 어찌 될지를 대번에 알아본 시뇨라가 말했다."]], [["\"Idiot!\" re-echoed Mrs.", "“멍청이라!”"], ["Proudie, as though the word were not half strong enough to express the required meaning; \"I'll let him know--\"", "프라우디 부인이 그 말로는 필요한 뜻을 절반도 담아내지 못한다는 듯 되받았다. “내 저자에게 톡톡히 일러 주겠—”"], ["and then looking round to learn, at a glance, the worst, she saw that at present it behoved her to collect the scattered débris of her dress.", "그러고는 한눈에 최악을 파악하려고 둘러보다가, 지금은 우선 흩어진 옷의 잔해를 주워 모으는 것이 제 도리임을 깨달았다."]], [["Bertie, when he saw what he had done, rushed over the sofa and threw himself on one knee before the offended lady.", "버티는 제가 저지른 일을 보고 소파를 뛰어넘어 달려가, 노한 부인 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["His object, doubtless, was to liberate the torn lace from the castor, but he looked as though he were imploring pardon from a goddess.", "그의 목적은 두말할 것 없이 찢긴 레이스를 바퀴에서 풀어내려는 것이었으나, 보기에는 마치 여신에게 용서를 비는 것 같았다."]], [["\"Unhand it, sir!\"", "“거기서 손을 떼시오!”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["From what scrap of dramatic poetry she had extracted the word cannot be said, but it must have rested on her memory, and now seemed opportunely dignified for the occasion.", "어느 희곡 나부랭이에서 그 말을 건져 냈는지는 알 길이 없으나, 어쨌든 기억에 남아 있다가 이 자리에 때맞추어 위엄 있게 여겨진 모양이었다."]], [["\"I'll fly to the looms of the fairies to repair the damage, if you'll only forgive me,\"", "“용서만 해 주신다면 요정들의 베틀로 날아가 이 망가진 것을 고쳐 오겠습니다.”"], ["said Ethelbert, still on his knees.", "에설버트가 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 말했다."]], [["\"Unhand it, sir!\" said Mrs.", "“거기서 손을 떼시오!”"], ["Proudie with redoubled emphasis, and all but furious wrath.", "프라우디 부인이 갑절로 힘을 주어, 노기가 터지기 직전으로 말했다."], ["This allusion to the fairies was a direct mockery and intended to turn her into ridicule.", "요정 운운하는 이 말은 대놓고 하는 조롱이요, 자기를 웃음거리로 만들려는 수작이었다."], ["So at least it seemed to her.", "적어도 부인에게는 그렇게 보였다."], ["\"Unhand it, sir!\"", "“거기서 손을 떼시오!”"], ["she almost screamed.", "부인은 거의 비명을 질렀다."]], [["\"It's not me; it's the cursed sofa,\"", "“제가 아닙니다. 이 빌어먹을 소파 탓이지요.”"], ["said Bertie, looking imploringly in her face and holding up both his hands to show that he was not touching her belongings, but still remaining on his knees.", "버티가 애원하듯 부인의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 자기는 부인의 물건에 손을 대고 있지 않다는 것을 보이려고 두 손을 들어 올린 채, 그러면서도 여전히 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 말했다."]], [["Hereupon the Signora laughed; not loud, indeed, but yet audibly.", "이때 시뇨라가 웃었다. 크게는 아니었으나 들릴 만큼은 웃었다."], ["And as the tigress bereft of her young will turn with equal anger on any within reach, so did Mrs. Proudie turn upon her female guest.", "그리고 새끼를 빼앗긴 암범이 손 닿는 것이면 무엇에든 똑같은 분노로 달려들 듯, 프라우디 부인도 그 여자 손님에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["\"Madam!\"", "“부인!”"], ["she said--and it is beyond the power of prose to tell of the fire which flashed from her eyes.", "프라우디 부인은 그렇게 말했다. 그 눈에서 번쩍인 불길이 어떠했는지는 산문의 힘으로는 도저히 전할 수 없다."]], [["The signora stared her full in the face for a moment, and then turning to her brother said playfully, \"Bertie, you idiot, get up.\"", "시뇨라는 잠시 그 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보다가, 오라비를 돌아보며 장난스럽게 말했다. “버티, 이 멍청아, 일어나.”"]], [["By this time the bishop, and Mr. Slope, and her three daughters were around her, and had collected together the wide ruins of her magnificence.", "그사이 주교와 슬로프 씨와 세 딸이 부인을 둘러싸고, 그 장려함의 널따란 잔해를 그러모았다."], ["The girls fell into circular rank behind their mother, and thus following her and carrying out the fragments, they left the reception-rooms in a manner not altogether devoid of dignity.", "딸들은 어머니 뒤로 둥글게 열을 지어 섰고, 그리하여 어머니를 뒤따르며 그 조각들을 받쳐 들고서, 아주 위엄이 없지는 않은 모양새로 접견실을 빠져나갔다."], ["Mrs. Proudie had to retire and re-array herself.", "프라우디 부인은 물러가 옷을 다시 차려입어야 했다."]], [["As soon as the constellation had swept by, Ethelbert rose from his knees and, turning with mock anger to the fat rector, said: \"After all it was your doing, sir--not mine.", "그 성좌가 휩쓸고 지나가자마자 에설버트는 무릎에서 일어나, 뚱뚱한 목사를 향해 짐짓 노한 체하며 말했다. “따지고 보면 제가 아니라 선생 탓이었습니다."], ["But perhaps you are waiting for preferment, and so I bore it.\"", "하지만 성직 승진을 기다리시는 처지일 터이니, 제가 대신 뒤집어써 드린 것이지요.”"]], [["Whereupon there was a laugh against the fat rector, in which both the bishop and the chaplain joined, and thus things got themselves again into order.", "이에 뚱뚱한 목사를 겨냥한 웃음이 일었고 주교와 전속 사제도 거기에 끼어들었으니, 그리하여 자리는 다시 제 모양을 찾았다."]], [["\"Oh! my lord, I am so sorry for this accident,\"", "“아, 주교님, 이런 일이 벌어져 정말 죄송합니다.”"], ["said the signora, putting out her hand so as to force the bishop to take it.", "시뇨라가 주교로 하여금 잡지 않을 수 없게끔 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["\"My brother is so thoughtless.", "“제 오라비가 워낙 생각이 없어서요."], ["Pray sit down, and let me have the pleasure of making your acquaintance.", "부디 앉으세요. 인사를 나누는 기쁨을 제게 주시고요."], ["Though I am so poor a creature as to want a sofa, I am not so selfish as to require it all.\"", "저야 소파가 있어야 하는 딱한 몸이지만, 소파를 통째로 차지할 만큼 이기적이지는 않답니다.”"], ["Madeline could always dispose herself so as to make room for a gentleman, though, as she declared, the crinoline of her lady friends was much too bulky to be so accommodated.", "마들린은 언제든 몸을 놀려 신사 한 사람 앉을 자리를 낼 수 있었다. 다만 본인 말로는, 여자 친구들의 크리놀린은 부피가 너무 커서 그렇게 앉힐 수가 없다는 것이었다."]], [["\"It was solely for the pleasure of meeting you that I have had myself dragged here,\"", "“오로지 주교님을 뵙는 기쁨 하나 때문에 이렇게 실려 온 것이랍니다.”"], ["she continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Of course, with your occupation, one cannot even hope that you should have time to come to us, that is, in the way of calling.", "“물론 주교님 같은 소임을 지고서야 저희를 찾아오실 짬이 나기를 바랄 수조차 없겠지요, 그러니까 방문이라는 형식으로는요."], ["And at your English dinner-parties all is so dull and so stately.", "게다가 영국식 만찬이란 하나같이 따분하고 딱딱하지 않습니까."], ["Do you know, my lord, that in coming to England my only consolation has been the thought that I should know you;\"", "주교님, 아시는지요, 제가 영국에 오면서 유일한 위안으로 삼은 것이 주교님을 알게 되리라는 생각이었답니다.”"], ["and she looked at him with the look of a she-devil.", "그러고는 계집 악마의 눈길로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["The bishop, however, thought that she looked very like an angel and, accepting the proffered seat, sat down beside her.", "그러나 주교는 그 모습이 천사와 꼭 같다고 생각했고, 내주는 자리를 받아 그 곁에 앉았다."], ["He uttered some platitude as to his deep obligation for the trouble she had taken, and wondered more and more who she was.", "그는 이렇게 수고를 해 주셔서 참으로 송구하다는 따위의 상투적인 말을 늘어놓으면서, 이 여자가 대체 누구인지 점점 더 궁금해했다."]], [["\"Of course you know my sad story?\"", "“제 슬픈 사연이야 물론 알고 계시겠지요?”"], ["she continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["The bishop didn't know a word of it.", "주교는 그에 대해 한마디도 알지 못했다."], ["He knew, however, or thought he knew, that she couldn't walk into a room like other people, and so made the most of that.", "다만 이 여자가 남들처럼 제 발로 방에 걸어 들어올 수 없다는 것만은 알고 있었고, 아니 안다고 여겼고, 그것을 최대한 써먹었다."], ["He put on a look of ineffable distress and said that he was aware how God had afflicted her.", "그는 이루 말할 수 없이 애통한 표정을 짓고는, 하느님께서 이분에게 어떤 시련을 내리셨는지 잘 알고 있노라고 말했다."]], [["The signora just touched the corner of her eyes with the most lovely of pocket-handkerchiefs.", "시뇨라는 더없이 어여쁜 손수건으로 눈가를 살짝 눌렀다. 그렇습니다, 하고 그는 말했다."], ["Yes, she said--she had been sorely tried--tried, she thought, beyond the common endurance of humanity; but while her child was left to her, everything was left.", "자신은 모진 시련을 겪었노라고, 사람이 흔히 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어선 시련이었다고 여긴다고. 그러나 아이가 곁에 남아 있는 한 모든 것이 남아 있는 것이라고."], ["\"Oh!", "“아, 주교님.”"], ["my lord,\"", "그가 외쳤다. “그 아이를 꼭 보셔야 합니다."], ["she exclaimed, \"you must see that infant--the last bud of a wondrous tree: you must let a mother hope that you will lay your holy hands on her innocent head and consecrate her for female virtues.", "놀라운 나무에 마지막으로 맺힌 봉오리랍니다. 그 거룩한 손을 그 죄 없는 머리에 얹으시어 여자의 덕을 위해 축성해 주시리라고, 어미가 그렇게 바라도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다."], ["May I hope it?\"", "그리 바라도 될까요?”"], ["said she, looking into the bishop's eye and touching the bishop's arm with her hand.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 주교의 눈을 들여다보고 제 손으로 주교의 팔을 짚었다."]], [["The bishop was but a man and said she might.", "주교도 사내에 지나지 않았으므로 그리 바라셔도 좋다고 말했다."], ["After all, what was it but a request that he would confirm her daughter?--a request, indeed, very unnecessary to make, as he should do so as a matter of course if the young lady came forward in the usual way.", "따지고 보면 그 딸에게 견진성사를 베풀어 달라는 청 말고 무엇이겠는가. 사실 굳이 청할 것도 없는 일이었으니, 그 아가씨가 통상의 절차로 나오기만 하면 당연히 해 줄 일이었다."]], [["\"The blood of Tiberius,\"", "“티베리우스의 피랍니다.”"], ["said the signora in all but a whisper; \"the blood of Tiberius flows in her veins.", "시뇨라가 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. “티베리우스의 피가 그 아이의 핏줄에 흐르고 있어요."], ["She is the last of the Neros!\"", "그 아이는 네로 가문의 마지막 후손이랍니다!”"]], [["The bishop had heard of the last of the Visigoths, and had floating in his brain some indistinct idea of the last of the Mohicans, but to have the last of the Neros thus brought before him for a blessing was very staggering.", "주교는 마지막 서고트인에 대해서는 들어 본 적이 있었고, 머릿속에 마지막 모히칸에 관한 어렴풋한 관념도 떠다니고 있었으나, 네로 가문의 마지막 후손이 이렇게 축복을 받겠다고 눈앞에 나서는 데에는 적잖이 아연했다. 그래도 이 부인은 마음에 들었다."], ["Still he liked the lady: she had a proper way of thinking and talked with more propriety than her brother.", "생각하는 품이 온당했고, 오라비보다 훨씬 예절 바르게 말했다. 그런데 대체 이들은 누구란 말인가?"], ["But who were they? It was now quite clear that that blue madman with the silky beard was not a Prince Vicinironi.", "비단결 같은 수염을 단 저 푸른 옷의 미치광이가 비시니로니 공작이 아니라는 것만은 이제 아주 분명해졌다."], ["The lady was married and was of course one of the Vicinironi's by right of the husband.", "부인은 혼인한 몸이니, 남편 쪽 연고로 비시니로니 집안 사람인 것이 당연했다."], ["So the bishop went on learning.", "그렇게 주교는 조금씩 알아 나갔다."]], [["\"When will you see her?", "“언제 그 아이를 보시겠어요?”"], ["said the signora with a start.", "시뇨라가 문득 생각난 듯 말했다."]], [["\"See whom?\"", "“누구를 본다는 말씀입니까?”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"My child,\"", "“제 아이 말입니다.”"], ["said the mother.", "어미가 말했다."]], [["\"What is the young lady's age?\"", "“그 아가씨는 나이가 어떻게 됩니까?”"], ["asked the bishop.", "주교가 물었다."]], [["\"She is just seven,\"", "“이제 막 일곱 살이랍니다.”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh,\"", "“아.” 주교가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["said the bishop, shaking his head; \"she is much too young--very much too young.\"", "“너무 어립니다. 아주 많이 어리지요.”"]], [["\"But in sunny Italy, you know, we do not count by years,\"", "“하지만 볕 좋은 이탈리아에서는 나이를 햇수로 세지 않는답니다.”"], ["and the signora gave the bishop one of her very sweetest smiles.", "그러고는 시뇨라가 주교에게 더없이 달콤한 미소 가운데 하나를 지어 보였다."]], [["\"But indeed, she is a great deal too young,\"", "“하지만 정말이지 너무나 어립니다.”"], ["persisted the bishop; \"we never confirm before--\"", "주교가 물러서지 않고 말했다. “저희는 결코 그 전에는 견진을—”"]], [["\"But you might speak to her; you might let her hear from your consecrated lips that she is not a castaway because she is a Roman; that she may be a Nero and yet a Christian; that she may owe her black locks and dark cheeks to the blood of the pagan Caesars, and yet herself be a child of grace; you will tell her this, won't you, my friend?\"", "“그래도 그 아이에게 말씀은 해 주실 수 있잖아요. 로마 사람이라 해서 버림받은 자가 아니라는 것을, 네로의 핏줄이면서도 그리스도인일 수 있다는 것을, 검은 머리채와 짙은 뺨은 이교도 카이사르들의 피에서 왔을지언정 그 자신은 은총의 자녀일 수 있다는 것을, 주교님의 축성받은 입술로 들려주실 수 있잖아요. 그렇게 말해 주시겠지요, 나의 벗이여?”"]], [["The friend said he would, and asked if the child could say her catechism.", "그 벗은 그러겠노라 말하고는, 아이가 교리문답을 욀 줄 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["\"No,\"", "“아니요.”"], ["said the signora, \"I would not allow her to learn lessons such as those in a land ridden over by priests and polluted by the idolatry of Rome.", "시뇨라가 말했다. “사제들이 짓밟고 로마의 우상숭배가 더럽혀 놓은 땅에서 그런 것을 배우게 할 수는 없었어요."], ["It is here, here in Barchester, that she must first be taught to lisp those holy words.", "그 아이가 그 거룩한 말들을 처음으로 더듬더듬 배워야 할 곳은 바로 여기, 이곳 바체스터랍니다."], ["Oh, that you could be her instructor!\"", "아, 주교님이 그 아이의 스승이 되어 주실 수만 있다면!”"]], [["Now, Dr.", "프라우디 박사가 이 부인을 마음에 들어 한 것은 분명하지만, 명색이 주교인 이상 어린 계집아이에게 교리문답의 첫걸음을 가르칠 리는 없었다."], ["Proudie certainly liked the lady, but, seeing that he was a bishop, it was not probable that he was going to instruct a little girl in the first rudiments of her catechism; so he said he'd send a teacher.", "그래서 그는 선생을 하나 보내겠노라고 말했다."]], [["\"But you'll see her yourself, my lord?\"", "“그래도 주교님이 몸소 그 아이를 보러 오시겠지요?”"]], [["The bishop said he would, but where should he call.", "주교는 그러겠다고 말했으나, 어디로 찾아가면 되겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["\"At Papa's house,\"", "“아버지 댁이지요.”"], ["said the Signora with an air of some little surprise at the question.", "시뇨라가 그 물음에 조금 놀랐다는 기색으로 말했다."]], [["The bishop actually wanted the courage to ask her who was her papa, so he was forced at last to leave her without fathoming the mystery.", "주교는 실로 그 아버지가 누구냐고 물을 용기가 나지 않아, 끝내 수수께끼를 풀지 못한 채 자리를 뜰 수밖에 없었다."], ["Mrs. Proudie, in her second best, had now returned to the rooms, and her husband thought it as well that he should not remain in too close conversation with the lady whom his wife appeared to hold in such slight esteem.", "프라우디 부인은 두 번째로 좋은 옷을 입고 이미 방으로 돌아와 있었고, 남편은 아내가 그토록 하찮게 여기는 듯한 여인과 너무 가까이 붙어 이야기를 이어 가지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["Presently he came across his youngest daughter.", "이윽고 그는 막내딸과 마주쳤다."]], [["\"Netta,\"", "“네티야.”"], ["said he, \"do you know who is the father of that Signora Vicinironi?\"", "그가 말했다. “저 시뇨라 비시니로니의 아버지가 누구인지 아느냐?”"]], [["\"It isn't Vicinironi, Papa,\"", "“비시니로니가 아니에요, 아버지.” 네티가 말했다."], ["said Netta; \"but Vesey Neroni, and she's Doctor Stanhope's daughter.", "“베시 네로니예요. 그리고 저분은 스탠호프 박사님의 따님이고요."], ["But I must go and do the civil to Griselda Grantly; I declare nobody has spoken a word to the poor girl this evening.\"", "그런데 저는 가서 그리젤 그랜틀리에게 인사치레를 해야겠어요. 오늘 저녁 그 딱한 아이에게 아무도 말 한마디 걸지 않았지 뭐예요.”"]], [["Dr. Stanhope!", "스탠호프 박사라니!"], ["Dr. Vesey Stanhope!", "베시 스탠호프 박사라니!"], ["Dr. Vesey Stanhope's daughter, of whose marriage with a dissolute Italian scamp he now remembered to have heard something!", "방탕한 이탈리아 건달과 혼인했다는 이야기를 어디선가 들은 기억이 이제야 나는, 바로 그 베시 스탠호프 박사의 딸이라니!"], ["And that impertinent blue cub who had examined him as to his episcopal bearings was old Stanhope's son, and the lady who had entreated him to come and teach her child the catechism was old Stanhope's daughter!", "그리고 주교로서의 처신을 이러니저러니 캐묻던 저 무엄한 푸른 옷의 애송이가 늙은 스탠호프의 아들이요, 제 아이에게 교리문답을 가르쳐 달라고 조르던 저 부인이 늙은 스탠호프의 딸이라니!"], ["The daughter of one of his own prebendaries!", "자기 밑에 있는 참사회원의 딸이라니!"], ["As these things flashed across his mind, he was nearly as angry as his wife had been.", "이런 것들이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나가자, 그는 아내가 그랬던 것에 못지않게 화가 났다."], ["Nevertheless, he could not but own that the mother of the last of the Neros was an agreeable woman.", "그럼에도 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 낳은 그 어미가 사람 마음에 드는 여자라는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Dr. Proudie tripped out into the adjoining room, in which were congregated a crowd of Grantlyite clergymen, among whom the archdeacon was standing pre-eminent, while the old dean was sitting nearly buried in a huge arm chair by the fire-place.", "프라우디 박사는 종종걸음으로 옆방으로 나갔다."], ["The bishop was very anxious to be gracious, and, if possible, to diminish the bitterness which his chaplain had occasioned.", "그 방에는 그랜틀리 편 성직자들이 한 무리 모여 있었고, 그 가운데 부주교가 우뚝 서 있었으며, 늙은 수석 사제는 벽난로 옆 커다란 안락의자에 파묻히다시피 앉아 있었다. 주교는 어떻게든 너그러운 태도를 보여, 될 수 있으면 제 전속 사제가 빚어 놓은 앙금을 덜어 내고 싶었다."], ["Let Mr. Slope do the fortiter in re, he himself would pour in the suaviter in modo.", "fortiter in re는 슬로프 씨에게 맡기고, 자신은 suaviter in modo를 부어 넣으면 될 일이었다."]], [["\"Pray don't stir, Mr. Dean, pray don't stir,\"", "“일어나지 마십시오, 수석 사제님, 부디 일어나지 마십시오.”"], ["he said as the old man essayed to get up; \"I take it as a great kindness, your coming to such an omnium gatherum as this.", "늙은이가 몸을 일으키려 하자 그가 말했다. “이런 omnium gatherum 같은 자리에 와 주신 것만으로도 큰 호의로 여깁니다."], ["But we have hardly got settled yet, and Mrs. Proudie has not been able to see her friends as she would wish to do.", "아직 자리를 제대로 잡지 못한 터라 프라우디 부인도 바라는 만큼 벗들을 맞이하지 못했지요."], ["Well, Mr. Archdeacon, after all, we have not been so hard upon you at Oxford.\"", "그런데 부주교, 따지고 보면 우리가 옥스퍼드에 그리 모질게 굴지는 않았지요.”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“그렇습니다.” 부주교가 말했다."], ["said the archdeacon, \"you've only drawn our teeth and cut out our tongues; you've allowed us still to breathe and swallow.\"", "“우리 이빨을 뽑고 혀를 잘라 냈을 뿐이지요. 숨 쉬고 삼키는 것까지는 그래도 남겨 주셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하!”"], ["laughed the bishop; \"it's not quite so easy to cut out the tongue of an Oxford magnate--and as for teeth--ha, ha, ha!", "주교가 웃었다. “옥스퍼드 유력자의 혀를 잘라 내기가 그리 쉬운 일은 아니지요."], ["Why, in the way we've left the matter, it's very odd if the heads of colleges don't have their own way quite as fully as when the hebdomadal board was in all its glory; what do you say, Mr.", "이빨로 말하자면 — 하, 하, 하! 아니, 우리가 일을 이대로 남겨 둔 마당에, 각 칼리지 학장들이 대학 주간 평의회가 위세를 떨치던 때만큼이나 제 뜻대로 하지 못한다면 그것이 오히려 이상한 일이지요."], ["Dean?\"", "수석 사제님은 어떻게 보십니까?”"]], [["\"An old man, my lord, never likes changes,\"", "“늙은 사람은 주교님, 변화라는 것을 도무지 좋아하지 않는 법입니다.”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["\"You must have been sad bunglers if it is so,\"", "“정말 그렇다면 여러분이 일을 지독히도 서투르게 처리한 것이겠지요.”"], ["said the archdeacon; \"and indeed, to tell the truth, I think you have bungled it.", "부주교가 말했다. “사실대로 말씀드리자면 저는 여러분이 일을 그르쳤다고 봅니다."], ["At any rate, you must own this; you have not done the half what you boasted you would do.\"", "어쨌든 이것만은 인정하셔야 합니다. 하겠다고 큰소리치던 것의 절반도 해내지 못하셨지요.”"]], [["\"Now, as regards your system of professors--\"", "“자, 여러분이 말하는 교수 제도로 말하자면—”"], ["began the chancellor slowly.", "학무 참사원이 느릿느릿 말을 꺼냈다."], ["He was never destined to get beyond such beginning.", "그는 그 첫머리 너머로 나아갈 운명이 못 되었다."]], [["\"Talking of professors,\"", "“교수 이야기가 나왔으니 말입니다만.” 학무 참사원의 팔꿈치 바로 뒤에서 부드럽고 또렷한 목소리가 말했다."], ["said a soft clear voice, close behind the chancellor's elbow; \"how much you Englishmen might learn from Germany; only you are all too proud.\"", "“영국 분들이 독일에서 배울 것이 얼마나 많은지요. 다만 여러분은 하나같이 자존심이 너무 세시지요.”"]], [["The bishop, looking round, perceived that that abominable young Stanhope had pursued him.", "주교가 돌아보니 그 고약한 젊은 스탠호프가 자기를 쫓아온 것이었다."], ["The dean stared at him as though he were some unearthly apparition; so also did two or three prebendaries and minor canons.", "수석 사제는 이 세상 것이 아닌 유령이라도 본 듯 그를 빤히 바라보았고, 두엇의 참사회원과 하급 참사원들도 마찬가지였다."], ["The archdeacon laughed.", "부주교는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"The German professors are men of learning,\" said Mr.", "“독일 교수들이 학식 있는 사람들인 것은 사실이오만,”"], ["Harding, \"but--\"", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “그러나—”"]], [["\"German professors!\"", "“독일 교수라니!”"], ["groaned out the chancellor, as though his nervous system had received a shock which nothing but a week of Oxford air could cure.", "학무 참사원이 신음하듯 내뱉었다. 옥스퍼드의 공기를 일주일은 쐬어야만 나을 충격을 신경 계통에 받은 것 같았다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["continued Ethelbert, not at all understanding why a German professor should be contemptible in the eyes of an Oxford don.", "에설버트는 독일 교수가 어째서 옥스퍼드 학자의 눈에 하찮게 보여야 하는지 도무지 알지 못한 채 말을 이었다."], ["\"Not but what the name is best earned at Oxford.", "“물론 그 이름값을 가장 톡톡히 하는 곳은 옥스퍼드지요."], ["In Germany the professors do teach; at Oxford, I believe, they only profess to do so, and sometimes not even that.", "독일에서는 교수들이 실제로 가르칩니다. 옥스퍼드에서는 가르치겠노라 공언만 할 뿐이고, 때로는 그 공언조차 하지 않는다더군요."], ["You'll have those universities of yours about your ears soon, if you don't consent to take a lesson from Germany.\"", "독일에서 한 수 배우기를 마다하신다면, 그 대학이란 것들이 머지않아 여러분 귀에다 대고 무너져 내릴 겁니다.”"]], [["There was no answering this.", "여기에는 대답할 말이 없었다."], ["Dignified clergymen of sixty years of age could not condescend to discuss such a matter with a young man with such clothes and such a beard.", "예순 살 먹은 점잖은 성직자들이 저런 옷차림에 저런 수염을 한 젊은이와 그런 문제를 논하려고 몸을 낮출 수는 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["\"Have you got good water out at Plumstead, Mr. Archdeacon?\"", "“플럼스테드는 물이 좋습니까, 부주교?”"], ["said the bishop by way of changing the conversation.", "주교가 화제를 돌리려고 말했다."]], [["\"Pretty good,\"", "“그런대로 좋습니다.”"], ["said Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."]], [["\"But by no means so good as his wine, my lord,\"", "“하지만 그 댁 포도주만큼 좋지는 않지요, 주교님.”"], ["said a witty minor canon.", "재치 있는 하급 참사원 하나가 말했다."]], [["\"Nor so generally used,\"", "“그만큼 널리 쓰이지도 않고요.”"], ["said another; \"that is, for inward application.\"", "또 다른 이가 말했다. “그러니까 몸 안에 들이는 용도로는 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하!”"], ["laughed the bishop, \"a good cellar of wine is a very comfortable thing in a house.\"", "주교가 웃었다. “좋은 포도주 광은 집에 두기에 참으로 흐뭇한 것이지요.”"]], [["\"Your German professors, Sir, prefer beer, I believe,\"", "“그 독일 교수들은 말이오, 선생, 맥주를 더 좋아한다지요, 아마.”"], ["said the sarcastic little meagre prebendary.", "비꼬기 좋아하는 자그맣고 여윈 참사회원이 말했다."]], [["\"They don't think much of either,\"", "“그들은 둘 다 대수롭게 여기지 않습니다.”"], ["said Ethelbert, \"and that perhaps accounts for their superiority.", "에설버트가 말했다. “아마 그래서 그들이 더 나은 것이겠지요."], ["Now the Jewish professor--\"", "그런데 유대인 교수로 말하자면—”"]], [["The insult was becoming too deep for the spirit of Oxford to endure, so the archdeacon walked off one way and the chancellor another, followed by their disciples, and the bishop and the young reformer were left together on the hearth-rug.", "모욕이 옥스퍼드의 정신으로 견디기에는 너무 깊어지고 있었으므로, 부주교는 이쪽으로 학무 참사원은 저쪽으로 걸어가 버렸고 그 제자들도 뒤를 따랐다. 그리하여 벽난로 앞 깔개에는 주교와 이 젊은 개혁가만 남았다."]], [["\"I was a Jew once myself,\"", "“저도 한때는 유대인이었습니다.”"], ["began Bertie.", "버티가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["The bishop was determined not to stand another examination, or be led on any terms into Palestine, so he again remembered that he had to do something very particular, and left young Stanhope with the dean.", "주교는 또 한 차례 심문을 당하거나 무슨 수를 써서든 팔레스타인으로 끌려 들어갈 마음이 조금도 없었으므로, 다시금 아주 긴히 할 일이 있다는 것을 떠올리고는 젊은 스탠호프를 수석 사제에게 떠맡겨 두고 갔다."], ["The dean did not get the worst of it for Ethelbert gave him a true account of his remarkable doings in the Holy Land.", "수석 사제로서는 손해 볼 것이 없었으니, 에설버트가 성지에서 벌인 놀라운 행적을 사실대로 들려주었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Harding,\"", "“아, 하딩 씨.”"], ["said the bishop, overtaking the ci-devant warden; \"I wanted to say one word about the hospital.", "주교가 전임 워든을 따라잡으며 말했다. “요양원 일로 한마디 드리고 싶었습니다."], ["You know, of course, that it is to be filled up.\"", "그 자리를 채우게 되었다는 것은 물론 알고 계시겠지요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding's heart beat a little, and he said that he had heard so.", "하딩 씨는 가슴이 조금 뛰었고, 그렇게 들었노라고 말했다."]], [["\"Of course,\"", "“물론,”"], ["continued the bishop; \"there can be only one man whom I could wish to see in that situation.", "주교가 말을 이었다. “그 자리에 앉는 것을 제가 보고 싶은 사람은 오직 한 분뿐이지요."], ["I don't know what your own views may be, Mr. Harding--\"", "하딩 씨 본인의 생각이 어떠하신지는 모르겠습니다만—”"]], [["\"They are very simply told, my lord,\"", "“아주 간단히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 주교님.”"], ["said the other; \"to take the place if it be offered me, and to put up with the want of it should another man get it.\"", "상대가 말했다. “제게 그 자리를 주신다면 받겠고, 다른 사람이 차지한다면 그것 없이 지내겠다는 것입니다.”"]], [["The bishop professed himself delighted to hear it; Mr.", "주교는 그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘다고 했다."], ["Harding might be quite sure that no other man would get it.", "하딩 씨는 다른 누구도 그 자리를 차지하지 않으리라 확실히 믿어도 좋다고 했다."], ["There were some few circumstances which would in a slight degree change the nature of the duties. Mr.", "다만 직무의 성격을 얼마간 바꾸어 놓을 사정이 몇 가지 있다고 했다."], ["Harding was probably aware of this, and would, perhaps, not object to discuss the matter with Mr. Slope.", "하딩 씨도 아마 그 점은 알고 계실 터이니, 그 문제를 슬로프 씨와 의논하는 데에 이의는 없으시리라고 했다."], ["It was a subject to which Mr. Slope had given a good deal of attention.", "슬로프 씨가 그 문제에 상당한 공을 들여 온 터였다."]], [["Mr. Harding felt, he knew not why, oppressed and annoyed.", "하딩 씨는 까닭 모르게 짓눌리고 언짢은 기분이 들었다."], ["What could Mr. Slope do to him?", "슬로프 씨가 자기에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He knew that there were to be changes.", "변화가 있으리라는 것은 그도 알고 있었다."], ["The nature of them must be communicated to the warden through somebody, and through whom so naturally as the bishop's chaplain?", "그 성격은 누군가를 통해 워든에게 전해져야 할 터이고, 주교의 전속 사제만큼 그 역할에 자연스러운 사람이 또 어디 있겠는가?"], ["'Twas thus he tried to argue himself back to an easy mind, but in vain.", "그는 이렇게 따져 가며 마음을 다시 편하게 돌려놓으려 했으나 헛일이었다."]], [["Mr. Slope in the meantime had taken the seat which the bishop had vacated on the signora's sofa, and remained with that lady till it was time to marshal the folk to supper.", "그사이 슬로프 씨는 주교가 비운 시뇨라의 소파 자리를 차지하고 앉아, 사람들을 저녁 식사 자리로 이끌 때가 되기까지 그 부인 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["Not with contented eyes had Mrs. Proudie seen this.", "프라우디 부인이 이를 흡족한 눈으로 보았을 리 없다."], ["Had not this woman laughed at her distress, and had not Mr. Slope heard it?", "저 여자가 자기의 낭패를 두고 웃지 않았던가, 그리고 슬로프 씨가 그 웃음을 듣지 않았던가?"], ["Was she not an intriguing Italian woman, half wife and half not, full of affectation, airs, and impudence?", "저 여자야말로 술수에 능한 이탈리아 여인이요, 아내라 하기도 아니라 하기도 어려운 처지에 꾸밈과 허세와 뻔뻔함으로 가득 찬 자가 아닌가?"], ["Was she not horribly bedizened with velvet and pearls, with velvet and pearls, too, which had not been torn off her back?", "벨벳과 진주로 흉하게 치장한 자가 아닌가, 그것도 등에서 뜯겨 나가지 않은 벨벳과 진주로?"], ["Above all, did she not pretend to be more beautiful than her neighbours?", "무엇보다도 제가 남들보다 아름다운 척하지 않는가?"], ["To say that Mrs. Proudie was jealous would give a wrong idea of her feelings.", "프라우디 부인이 질투를 했다고 말한다면 그 심정을 잘못 전하는 것이 되리라."], ["She had not the slightest desire that Mr. Slope should be in love with herself.", "부인은 슬로프 씨가 자기에게 반하기를 바라는 마음이 조금도 없었다."], ["But she desired the incense of Mr. Slope's spiritual and temporal services, and did not choose that they should be turned out of their course to such an object as Signora Neroni.", "다만 슬로프 씨가 영적으로나 세속적으로 바쳐 올리는 봉사의 향연은 원했고, 그것이 시뇨라 네로니 따위를 향해 방향을 틀도록 내버려 둘 생각은 없었다."], ["She considered also that Mr. Slope ought in duty to hate the signora, and it appeared from his manner that he was very far from hating her.", "게다가 부인이 보기에 슬로프 씨는 마땅히 그 시뇨라를 미워해야 할 사람이었는데, 그 태도로 미루어 보건대 미워하는 것과는 거리가 아주 멀었다."]], [["\"Come, Mr. Slope,\"", "“이보게, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["she said, sweeping by and looking all that she felt, \"can't you make yourself useful?", "부인이 제 심정을 고스란히 얼굴에 담고 옆을 스쳐 지나가며 말했다. “쓸모 있게 좀 굴 수 없겠나?"], ["Do pray take Mrs. Grantly down to supper.\"", "부디 그랜틀리 부인을 모시고 저녁 식사 자리로 내려가게.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly heard and escaped.", "그랜틀리 부인은 그 말을 듣고 달아났다."], ["The words were hardly out of Mrs. Proudie's mouth before the intended victim had stuck her hand through the arm of one of her husband's curates and saved herself.", "프라우디 부인의 입에서 말이 채 떨어지기도 전에, 제물이 될 뻔한 이는 남편의 부목사 하나의 팔에 손을 꿰고 몸을 구했다."], ["What would the archdeacon have said had he seen her walking downstairs with Mr. Slope?", "슬로프 씨와 나란히 계단을 내려가는 아내를 보았더라면 부주교가 무어라 했겠는가?"]], [["Mr. Slope heard also, but was by no means so obedient as was expected.", "슬로프 씨 역시 그 말을 들었으나 기대만큼 고분고분하지는 않았다."], ["Indeed, the period of Mr. Slope's obedience to Mrs.", "실상 슬로프 씨가 프라우디 부인에게 복종하던 시절은 저물어 가고 있었다."], ["Proudie was drawing to a close. He did not wish yet to break with her, nor to break with her at all, if it could be avoided.", "그는 아직 부인과 틀어지고 싶지 않았고, 피할 수만 있다면 아예 틀어지지 않기를 바랐다."], ["But he intended to be master in that palace, and as she had made the same resolution it was not improbable that they might come to blows.", "그러나 그는 저 주교관의 주인이 될 작정이었고, 부인도 똑같은 결심을 하고 있었으니 두 사람이 한판 붙게 되는 일도 없으란 법이 없었다."]], [["Before leaving the signora he arranged a little table before her and begged to know what he should bring her.", "시뇨라 곁을 떠나기 전에 그는 그 앞에 작은 탁자를 놓아 주고, 무엇을 가져다드리면 좋겠느냐고 청했다."], ["She was quite indifferent, she said--nothing--anything.", "아무래도 좋다고, 아무것도 필요 없다고, 무엇이든 상관없다고 그가 말했다."], ["It was now she felt the misery of her position, now that she must be left alone.", "홀로 남겨져야 하는 지금이야말로 제 처지의 비참함이 사무친다고 했다."], ["Well, a little chicken, some ham, and a glass of champagne.", "그럼, 닭고기 조금과 햄 약간과 샴페인 한 잔을 달라고 했다."]], [["Mr. Slope had to explain, not without blushing for his patron, that there was no champagne.", "슬로프 씨는 제 후원자를 대신해 얼굴을 붉히면서, 샴페인은 없다고 설명해야 했다."]], [["Sherry would do just as well.", "셰리로도 얼마든지 좋다고 했다."], ["And then Mr. Slope descended with the learned Miss Trefoil on his arm.", "그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는 학식 있는 트리폴 양을 팔에 끼고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Could she tell him, he asked, whether the ferns of Barsetshire were equal to those of Cumberland?", "바셋셔의 고사리가 컴벌랜드의 고사리에 견줄 만한지 아시느냐고 그가 물었다."], ["His strongest worldly passion was for ferns--and before she could answer him he left her wedged between the door and the sideboard.", "그의 가장 강렬한 세속적 열정은 고사리에 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 그 여자가 대답을 하기도 전에, 문과 찬장 사이에 끼워 둔 채 자리를 떠 버렸다."], ["It was fifty minutes before she escaped, and even then unfed.", "그가 거기서 빠져나오기까지 50분이 걸렸고, 그러고도 아무것도 먹지 못했다."]], [["\"You are not leaving us, Mr. Slope,\"", "“설마 우리를 두고 가는 것은 아니겠지, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["said the watchful lady of the house, seeing her slave escaping towards the door, with stores of provisions held high above the heads of the guests.", "제 종이 손님들 머리 위로 음식을 잔뜩 높이 쳐들고 문 쪽으로 빠져나가는 것을 보고, 빈틈없는 안주인이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Slope explained that the Signora Neroni was in want of her supper.", "슬로프 씨는 시뇨라 네로니가 저녁 식사를 기다리고 있다고 설명했다."]], [["\"Pray, Mr. Slope, let her brother take it to her,\"", "“슬로프 씨, 그건 그 오라비더러 가져다주라고 하게.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, quite out loud.", "프라우디 부인이 아주 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["\"It is out of the question that you should be so employed.", "“자네가 그런 일에 쓰이다니 있을 수 없는 일이야."], ["Pray, Mr. Slope, oblige me; I am sure Mr.", "슬로프 씨, 부디 내 말을 들어주게."], ["Stanhope will wait upon his sister.\"", "스탠호프 씨가 제 누이를 돌볼 것이 분명하니까.”"]], [["Ethelbert was most agreeably occupied in the furthest corner of the room, making himself both useful and agreeable to Mrs. Proudie's youngest daughter.", "에설버트는 방의 가장 먼 구석에서 프라우디 부인의 막내딸에게 쓸모 있게도 굴고 살갑게도 굴면서 더없이 흐뭇하게 시간을 보내고 있었다."]], [["\"I couldn't get out, madam, if Madeline were starving for her supper,\"", "“마들린이 저녁을 굶어 죽는다 해도 저는 여기서 빠져나갈 수가 없습니다, 부인.”"], ["said he; \"I'm physically fixed, unless I could fly.\"", "그가 말했다. “날 수 있다면 모를까, 몸이 아주 박혀 버렸거든요.”"]], [["The lady's anger was increased by seeing that her daughter also had gone over to the enemy, and when she saw, that in spite of her remonstrances, in the teeth of her positive orders, Mr.", "제 딸까지 적진으로 넘어간 것을 보자 부인의 노여움은 한층 커졌고, 그토록 말렸음에도, 분명한 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 슬로프 씨가 응접실로 가 버리는 것을 보자, 분노의 잔이 넘쳐 스스로를 억누를 수 없었다."], ["Slope went off to the drawing-room, the cup of her indignation ran over, and she could not restrain herself.", "“저런 무례는 내 평생 본 적이 없다.”"], ["\"Such manners I never saw,\"", "부인은 중얼거리듯 말했다."], ["she said, muttering. \"I cannot and will not permit it;\"", "“나는 그것을 용납할 수도 없고 용납하지도 않겠다.”"], ["and then, after fussing and fuming for a few minutes, she pushed her way through the crowd and followed Mr. Slope.", "그러고는 몇 분 동안 부산을 떨며 씩씩거리다가, 사람들 틈을 헤치고 슬로프 씨를 뒤쫓았다."]], [["When she reached the room above, she found it absolutely deserted, except by the guilty pair.", "위층 방에 이르러 보니 그 죄 지은 두 사람 말고는 아무도 없이 텅 비어 있었다."], ["The signora was sitting very comfortably up to her supper, and Mr. Slope was leaning over her and administering to her wants.", "시뇨라는 아주 편안하게 앉아 저녁을 들고 있었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 시중을 들고 있었다."], ["They had been discussing the merits of Sabbath-day schools, and the lady had suggested that as she could not possibly go to the children, she might be indulged in the wish of her heart by having the children brought to her.", "두 사람은 안식일 학교의 이점을 논하던 참이었고, 부인은 자기가 도저히 아이들에게 갈 수 없으니 아이들을 자기에게 데려오게 해 준다면 마음속 소원을 이루어 주는 셈이 되겠다고 넌지시 말한 터였다."]], [["\"And when shall it be, Mr. Slope?\"", "“그러면 언제로 할까요, 슬로프 씨?”"], ["said she.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Slope was saved the necessity of committing himself to a promise by the entry of Mrs.", "슬로프 씨는 프라우디 부인이 들어서는 바람에 굳이 약속을 하지 않아도 되게 되었다."], ["Proudie. She swept close up to the sofa so as to confront the guilty pair, stared full at them for a moment, and then said, as she passed on to the next room, \"Mr.", "부인은 죄 지은 두 사람과 맞서듯 소파 바로 앞까지 쓸어 오듯 다가와 잠시 그들을 똑바로 노려보더니, 옆방으로 지나가면서 말했다."], ["Slope, his lordship is especially desirous of your attendance below; you will greatly oblige me if you will join him.\"", "“슬로프 씨, 주교님께서 아래층에 자네가 있어 주기를 각별히 바라시네. 내려가 준다면 내게도 큰 도움이 되겠네.”"], ["And so she stalked on.", "그러고는 성큼성큼 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["Mr. Slope muttered something in reply, and prepared to go downstairs.", "슬로프 씨는 무어라 웅얼거려 대답하고는 아래층으로 내려갈 채비를 했다."], ["As for the bishop's wanting him, he knew his lady patroness well enough to take that assertion at what it was worth; but he did not wish to make himself the hero of a scene, or to become conspicuous for more gallantry than the occasion required.", "주교가 자기를 찾는다는 말이야, 그 여자 후원자를 익히 아는 터라 그 말의 값어치가 어느 정도인지 짐작하고도 남았다. 그러나 그는 한바탕 소동의 주인공이 되고 싶지도 않았고, 자리에 필요한 것 이상으로 여자에게 헌신한다는 소리를 듣고 싶지도 않았다."]], [["\"Is she always like this?\"", "“저분은 늘 저러신가요?”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes--always--madam,\" said Mrs.", "“그렇소, 늘 저렇소, 부인.”"], ["Proudie, returning; \"always the same--always equally adverse to impropriety of conduct of every description;\"", "프라우디 부인이 되돌아오며 말했다. “늘 한결같지요."], ["and she stalked back through the room again, following Mr.", "어떤 종류의 무례한 처신에도 늘 똑같이 단호하고말고요.”"], ["Slope out of the door.", "그러고는 다시 방을 가로질러 성큼성큼 돌아가, 슬로프 씨를 뒤따라 문밖으로 나갔다."]], [["The signora couldn't follow her, or she certainly would have done so.", "시뇨라는 따라갈 수가 없었으니, 갈 수만 있었다면 분명히 따라갔을 것이다."], ["But she laughed loud, and sent the sound of it ringing through the lobby and down the stairs after Mrs. Proudie's feet.", "대신 그는 크게 웃었고, 그 소리를 복도로 울려 보내 프라우디 부인의 발소리를 좇아 계단 아래까지 흘려보냈다."], ["Had she been as active as Grimaldi, she could probably have taken no better revenge.", "그리말디만큼 몸이 재발랐더라도 그보다 나은 복수는 하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["\"Mr.", "“슬로프 씨.”"], ["Slope,\" said Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 문간에서 그 죄인을 붙잡고 말했다."], ["Proudie, catching the delinquent at the door, \"I am surprised you should leave my company to attend on such a painted Jezebel as that.\"", "“내 곁을 떠나 저따위 분칠한 이세벨의 시중을 들다니 놀랍기 짝이 없네.”"]], [["\"But she's lame, Mrs. Proudie, and cannot move.", "“하지만 그분은 다리를 못 써서 움직이지 못하지 않습니까, 프라우디 부인."], ["Somebody must have waited upon her.\"", "누군가는 시중을 들어야 했지요.”"]], [["\"Lame,\" said Mrs.", "“다리를 못 쓴다고?”"], ["Proudie; \"I'd lame her if she belonged to me.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내 사람이었다면 내가 정말로 다리를 못 쓰게 만들어 놓았을 걸세."], ["What business had she here at all?--such impertinence--such affectation.\"", "애당초 여기 올 일이 무엇이었단 말인가? 그런 뻔뻔함이라니, 그런 꾸밈이라니.”"]], [["In the hall and adjacent rooms all manner of cloaking and shawling was going on, and the Barchester folk were getting themselves gone. Mrs.", "현관과 그에 잇닿은 방들에서는 외투를 걸치고 숄을 두르는 온갖 소동이 벌어졌고, 바체스터 사람들은 하나둘 자리를 떴다."], ["Proudie did her best to smirk at each and every one as they made their adieux, but she was hardly successful.", "프라우디 부인은 작별 인사를 하는 사람마다 애써 웃음을 지어 보였으나 거의 성공하지 못했다."], ["Her temper had been tried fearfully.", "그날 부인의 성미는 무섭도록 시달린 터였다."], ["By slow degrees the guests went.", "손님들은 더디게 하나씩 물러갔다."]], [["\"Send back the carriage quick,\" said Ethelbert, as Dr.", "“마차를 얼른 돌려보내세요.”"], ["and Mrs. Stanhope took their departure.", "스탠호프 박사 내외가 자리를 뜰 때 에설버트가 말했다."]], [["The younger Stanhopes were left to the very last, and an uncomfortable party they made with the bishop's family. They all went into the dining-room, and then the bishop observing that \"the lady\"", "젊은 스탠호프 남매는 맨 마지막까지 남았고, 주교 가족과 어울려 여간 거북한 자리를 이루지 않았다."], ["was alone in the drawing-room, they followed him up. Mrs.", "모두 식당으로 들어갔다가, 주교가 “그 부인”이 응접실에 혼자 있다고 말하는 바람에 다들 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Proudie kept Mr. Slope and her daughters in close conversation, resolving that he should not be indulged, nor they polluted.", "프라우디 부인은 슬로프 씨와 딸들을 붙들고 이야기를 놓지 않았으니, 그가 제멋대로 굴지 못하게 하고 딸들도 물들지 않게 하려는 작정이었다."], ["The bishop, in mortal dread of Bertie and the Jews, tried to converse with Charlotte Stanhope about the climate of Italy.", "주교는 버티와 유대인 이야기가 죽도록 두려워, 샬럿 스탠호프를 붙잡고 이탈리아의 기후에 대해 이야기해 보려 했다."], ["Bertie and the signora had no resource but in each other.", "버티와 시뇨라에게는 서로 말고는 기댈 데가 없었다."]], [["\"Did you get your supper at last, Madeline?\"", "“결국 저녁은 얻어먹었어, 마들린?”"], ["said the impudent or else mischievous young man.", "뻔뻔한 것인지 짓궂은 것인지 모를 이 젊은이가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“응, 먹었지.”"], ["said Madeline; \"Mr.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["Slope was so very kind as to bring it me.", "“슬로프 씨가 아주 친절하게도 가져다주셨어."], ["I fear, however, he put himself to more inconvenience than I wished.\"", "다만 내가 바란 것보다 더 큰 수고를 하신 것 같아 마음에 걸리는구나.”"]], [["Mrs. Proudie looked at her but said nothing.", "프라우디 부인은 그를 바라보기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["The meaning of her look might have been thus translated; \"If ever you find yourself within these walls again, I'll give you leave to be as impudent and affected and as mischievous as you please.\"", "그 눈길의 뜻은 이렇게 옮길 수 있었으리라. “네가 다시 이 집 담장 안에 발을 들이는 일이 있거든, 그때는 마음껏 뻔뻔하고 마음껏 꾸미고 마음껏 짓궂게 굴어도 좋다고 내 허락해 주마.”"]], [["At last the carriage returned with the three Italian servants, and La Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni was carried out, as she had been carried in.", "마침내 이탈리아인 하인 셋과 함께 마차가 돌아왔고, 라 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니는 실려 들어왔던 그대로 실려 나갔다."]], [["The lady of the palace retired to her chamber by no means contented with the result of her first grand party at Barchester.", "주교관의 안주인은 바체스터에서 연 첫 성대한 연회의 결과에 조금도 만족하지 못한 채 제 방으로 물러갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. Slope versus Harding", "# 제12장. 슬로프 대 하딩"]], [["Two or three days after the party, Mr. Harding received a note begging him to call on Mr.", "접견회가 있고 이삼 일이 지났을 무렵, 하딩 씨는 이튿날 아침 이른 시각에 주교관으로 슬로프 씨를 찾아와 달라고 청하는 쪽지를 받았다."], ["Slope, at the palace, at an early hour on the following morning. There was nothing uncivil in the communication, and yet the tone of it was thoroughly displeasing.", "그 글에 무례한 구석이라고는 없었으나, 그럼에도 어조는 처음부터 끝까지 불쾌했다."], ["It was as follows:", "내용은 이러했다."]], [["Will you favour me by calling on me at the palace to-morrow morning at 9:30 A.M.", "내일 아침 오전 9시 30분에 주교관으로 저를 찾아와 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까."], ["The bishop wishes me to speak to you touching the hospital.", "주교님께서 요양원 건으로 저더러 하딩 씨와 이야기를 나누라 하십니다."], ["I hope you will excuse my naming so early an hour.", "이렇게 이른 시각을 지정하는 것을 부디 용서해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["I do so as my time is greatly occupied.", "제 시간이 워낙 빠듯한 탓에 그리하는 것입니다."], ["If, however, it is positively inconvenient to you, I will change it to 10.", "다만 그 시각이 정말로 불편하시다면 10시로 바꾸겠습니다."], ["You will, perhaps, be kind enough to let me have a note in reply.", "수고스럽더라도 답장을 한 줄 보내 주시면 고맙겠습니다."]], [["Believe me to be, My dear Mr. Harding, Your assured friend, OBH.", "그럼 이만 줄이겠습니다, 친애하는 하딩 씨, 선생의 변함없는 벗, 오바."], ["SLOPE", "슬로프"]], [["The Palace, Monday morning, 20th August, 185--", "주교관에서, 월요일 아침, 185X년 8월 20일"]], [["Mr. Harding neither could nor would believe anything of the sort, and he thought, moreover, that Mr.", "하딩 씨는 그런 따위를 믿을 수도 없었고 믿을 마음도 없었으며, 게다가 슬로프 씨가 스스로를 그런 이름으로 일컫는 것은 제법 뻔뻔한 노릇이라고 생각했다."], ["Slope was rather impertinent to call himself by such a name. His assured friend, indeed!", "변함없는 벗이라니!"], ["How many assured friends generally fall to the lot of a man in this world? And by what process are they made?", "대체 이 세상에서 한 사람에게 변함없는 벗이라는 것이 몇이나 돌아온단 말인가?"], ["And how much of such process had taken place as yet between Mr.", "또 그런 벗은 어떤 과정을 거쳐 만들어지는가?"], ["Harding and Mr. Slope?", "그리고 하딩 씨와 슬로프 씨 사이에 그런 과정이 지금까지 얼마나 있었단 말인가?"], ["Mr. Harding could not help asking himself these questions as he read and re-read the note before him.", "하딩 씨는 눈앞의 쪽지를 읽고 또 읽으면서 이런 물음들을 스스로에게 던지지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He answered it, however, as follows:", "그러나 그는 이렇게 답장을 썼다."]], [["I will call at the palace to-morrow at 9:30 A.M. as you desire.", "말씀하신 대로 내일 오전 9시 30분에 주교관으로 찾아가겠소."]], [["Truly yours,", "그럼 이만 줄이겠소,"]], [["High Street, Barchester, Monday", "바체스터 하이 스트리트에서, 월요일"]], [["And on the following morning, punctually at half-past nine, he knocked at the palace door and asked for Mr. Slope.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침, 정확히 9시 30분에 그는 주교관 문을 두드리고 슬로프 씨를 찾았다."]], [["The bishop had one small room allotted to him on the ground-floor, and Mr. Slope had another.", "주교에게는 1층에 작은 방 하나가 배정되어 있었고, 슬로프 씨에게도 또 하나가 있었다."], ["Into this latter Mr. Harding was shown and asked to sit down.", "하딩 씨는 그중 뒤엣것으로 안내되어 앉으라는 권유를 받았다."], ["Mr. Slope was not yet there.", "슬로프 씨는 아직 와 있지 않았다."], ["The ex-warden stood up at the window looking into the garden, and could not help thinking how very short a time had passed since the whole of that house had been open to him, as though he had been a child of the family, born and bred in it.", "전임 워든은 정원이 내다보이는 창가에 서서, 이 집이 마치 그가 이 집에서 나고 자란 자식이기라도 한 듯 통째로 그에게 열려 있던 때로부터 얼마나 짧은 시간이 흘렀을 뿐인가를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He remembered how the old servants used to smile as they opened the door to him; how the familiar butler would say, when he had been absent a few hours longer than usual, \"A sight of you, Mr.", "그는 늙은 하인들이 문을 열어 주며 짓던 미소를 떠올렸고, 그가 여느 때보다 몇 시간 더 발길을 끊기라도 하면 낯익은 집사가 “하딩 씨를 뵈니 앓던 눈이 다 낫습니다요”"], ["Harding, is good for sore eyes;\" how the fussy housekeeper would swear that he couldn't have dined, or couldn't have breakfasted, or couldn't have lunched.", "하고 말하던 것을 떠올렸으며, 부산스러운 가정부가 그가 저녁을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 아침을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 점심을 못 자셨을 것이라느니 하며 우기던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["And then, above all, he remembered the pleasant gleam of inward satisfaction which always spread itself over the old bishop's face whenever his friend entered his room.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 벗이 방에 들어설 때마다 늙은 주교의 얼굴에 늘 번지던 그 흐뭇한 속마음의 빛을 떠올렸다."]], [["A tear came into each eye as he reflected that all this was gone.", "이 모든 것이 사라졌음을 헤아리자 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 이제 와서 요양원이 그에게 무슨 소용이겠는가?"], ["What use would the hospital be to him now?", "그는 세상에 홀로였고 늙어 가고 있었다."], ["He was alone in the world, and getting old; he would soon, very soon have to go and leave it all, as his dear old friend had gone; go, and leave the hospital, and his accustomed place in the cathedral, and his haunts and pleasures, to younger and perhaps wiser men.", "머지않아, 아주 머지않아 그도 정든 옛 벗이 떠났듯 이 모든 것을 두고 떠나야 할 터였다. 요양원도, 대성당에서 늘 앉던 자리도, 즐겨 찾던 곳들과 소소한 낙도 모두 더 젊고 어쩌면 더 지혜로운 이들에게 넘겨주고 떠나야 할 터였다."], ["That chanting of his!", "그가 그토록 아끼던 그 성가!"], ["Perhaps, in truth, the time for it was gone by.", "어쩌면 정말로 그 시절은 지나가 버렸는지도 몰랐다."], ["He felt as though the world were sinking from his feet; as though this, this was the time for him to turn with confidence to those hopes which he had preached with confidence to others. \"What,\"", "그는 발밑에서 세상이 꺼져 내리는 듯한 느낌을 받았고, 지금이야말로 남들에게 확신을 가지고 설교해 온 그 소망들에 스스로 확신을 가지고 기대야 할 때라는 느낌을 받았다."], ["said he to himself, \"can a man's religion be worth if it does not support him against the natural melancholy of declining years?\"", "“사람의 신앙이 저물어 가는 나이의 그 당연한 서글픔조차 버텨 주지 못한다면 대체 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가.”"], ["And as he looked out through his dimmed eyes into the bright parterres of the bishop's garden, he felt that he had the support which he wanted.", "그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 흐려진 눈으로 주교관 정원의 환한 화단을 내다보면서, 그는 자기가 바라던 그 버팀을 이미 지니고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["Nevertheless, he did not like to be thus kept waiting. If Mr.", "그렇다고는 해도 이렇게 기다림을 당하는 것은 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["Slope did not really wish to see him at half-past nine o'clock, why force him to come away from his lodgings with his breakfast in his throat? To tell the truth, it was policy on the part of Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 정말로 9시 30분에 그를 만날 뜻이 아니었다면, 무엇하러 아침밥이 목에 걸린 채 하숙에서 나서게 만든단 말인가?"], ["Slope. Mr.", "사실을 말하자면 그것은 슬로프 씨 쪽의 계책이었다."], ["Slope had made up his mind that Mr. Harding should either accept the hospital with abject submission, or else refuse it altogether, and had calculated that he would probably be more quick to do the latter, if he could be got to enter upon the subject in an ill-humour.", "슬로프 씨는 하딩 씨가 요양원을 비굴하게 굴복하며 받아들이든지 아니면 아예 거절하든지 둘 중 하나여야 한다고 마음을 정해 놓고 있었고, 하딩 씨가 언짢은 기분으로 그 이야기에 들어서게만 만들면 아마 후자 쪽으로 더 빨리 기울리라고 셈해 두었다."], ["Perhaps Mr. Slope was not altogether wrong in his calculation.", "어쩌면 슬로프 씨의 셈이 아주 틀리지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["It was nearly ten when Mr. Slope hurried into the room and, muttering something about the bishop and diocesan duties, shook Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 방으로 급히 들어온 것은 10시가 다 되어서였다."], ["Harding's hand ruthlessly and begged him to be seated.", "그는 주교와 교구 업무에 관해 무어라 중얼거리더니 하딩 씨의 손을 인정사정없이 흔들어 잡고는 앉으시라고 청했다."]], [["Now the air of superiority which this man assumed did go against the grain with Mr. Harding, and yet he did not know how to resent it.", "이 사람이 몸에 두른 우월한 태도는 과연 하딩 씨의 비위를 거슬렀지만, 그렇다고 그것에 어떻게 성을 내야 할지는 알지 못했다."], ["The whole tendency of his mind and disposition was opposed to any contra-assumption of grandeur on his own part, and he hadn't the worldly spirit or quickness necessary to put down insolent pretensions by downright and open rebuke, as the archdeacon would have done.", "그의 마음과 기질은 통째로 자기 쪽에서 맞대고 위세를 부리는 일과는 어긋나 있었고, 부주교라면 했을 법하게 대놓고 정면으로 꾸짖어 건방진 허세를 눌러 버리는 데 필요한 세속의 기개도 재바름도 그에게는 없었다."], ["There was nothing for Mr. Harding but to submit, and he accordingly did so.", "하딩 씨로서는 참고 받아들이는 수밖에 없었고, 그래서 그렇게 했다."]], [["\"About the hospital, Mr.", "“요양원 건입니다만, 하딩 씨.”"], ["Harding?\" began Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다."], ["Slope, speaking of it as the head of a college at Cambridge might speak of some sizarship which had to be disposed of.", "케임브리지의 어느 칼리지 학장이 처리해야 할 급비생 자리 하나를 두고 말하기라도 하는 듯한 말투였다."]], [["Mr. Harding crossed one leg over another, and then one hand over the other on the top of them, and looked Mr.", "하딩 씨는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 포개고, 그 위에 다시 한 손을 다른 손 위에 포갠 채 슬로프 씨의 얼굴을 마주 보았다."], ["Slope in the face; but he said nothing.", "그러나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"It's to be filled up again,\"", "“그 자리를 다시 채우게 되었지요.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Mr. Harding said that he had understood so.", "하딩 씨는 그렇게 알고 있다고 대답했다."]], [["\"Of course, you know, the income will be very much reduced,\"", "“물론 아시겠지만 수입은 아주 많이 줄어들 겁니다.”"], ["continued Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["\"The bishop wished to be liberal, and he therefore told the government that he thought it ought to be put at not less than £450.", "“주교님께서는 후하게 하시려는 뜻이어서, 450파운드보다 적어서는 안 된다고 보신다고 정부에 말씀하셨어요."], ["I think on the whole the bishop was right, for though the services required will not be of a very onerous nature, they will be more so than they were before.", "저는 대체로 주교님이 옳으셨다고 봅니다. 요구되는 직무가 아주 무거운 성질의 것은 아니겠으나, 그래도 예전보다는 무거워질 테니까요."], ["And it is, perhaps, well that the clergy immediately attached to the cathedral town should be made as comfortable as the extent of the ecclesiastical means at our disposal will allow.", "그리고 대성당이 있는 도시에 바로 딸린 성직자들은 우리가 쓸 수 있는 교회 재원이 허락하는 한 넉넉하게 지내도록 해 주는 편이 아마 좋을 겁니다."], ["Those are the bishop's ideas, and I must say mine also.\"", "그것이 주교님의 생각이고, 제 생각도 그러하다고 말씀드려야겠군요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding sat rubbing one hand on the other, but said not a word.", "하딩 씨는 한 손으로 다른 손을 문지르며 앉아 있을 뿐 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"So much for the income, Mr. Harding.", "“수입 이야기는 그쯤 해 두지요, 하딩 씨."], ["The house will, of course, remain to the warden, as before.", "집은 물론 전과 마찬가지로 워든에게 남습니다."], ["It should, however, I think, be stipulated that he should paint inside every seven years, and outside every three years, and be subject to dilapidations, in the event of vacating, either by death or otherwise.", "다만 제 생각에는, 안쪽은 7년마다 바깥쪽은 3년마다 칠을 하고, 사망이든 그 밖의 사유든 자리를 비우게 될 경우 훼손분에 대한 책임을 지도록 조건을 달아 두어야 할 듯합니다."], ["But this is a matter on which the bishop must yet be consulted.\"", "그러나 이 문제는 아직 주교님과 상의를 드려야 하는 사안이지요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding still rubbed his hands and still sat silent, gazing up into Mr. Slope's unprepossessing face.", "하딩 씨는 여전히 두 손을 문지르며 말없이 앉은 채, 슬로프 씨의 호감 가지 않는 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었다."]], [["\"Then, as to the duties,\"", "“그다음으로 직무 말입니다만,”"], ["continued he, \"I believe, if I am rightly informed, there can hardly be said to have been any duties hitherto,\"", "그가 말을 이었다. “제가 들은 바가 옳다면, 지금까지는 직무라 할 만한 것이 거의 없었다고 해야 할 듯한데요.”"], ["and he gave a sort of half-laugh, as though to pass off the accusation in the guise of a pleasantry.", "그러고는 반쯤 웃는 듯한 소리를 내어, 그 힐난을 농담의 옷을 입혀 넘기려는 시늉을 했다."]], [["Mr. Harding thought of the happy, easy years he had passed in his old home; of the worn-out, aged men whom he had succoured; of his good intentions; and of his work, which had certainly been of the lightest.", "하딩 씨는 옛집에서 보낸 행복하고 편안했던 세월을 떠올렸고, 자기가 거두어 온 기력 다한 늙은이들을 떠올렸으며, 자기의 선한 뜻을 떠올렸고, 분명 더할 나위 없이 가벼웠던 자기 일을 떠올렸다."], ["He thought of these things, doubting for a moment whether he did or did not deserve the sarcasm.", "그는 그런 것들을 떠올리면서, 자기가 저 빈정거림을 들어 마땅한지 아닌지 한순간 미심쩍어했다."], ["He gave his enemy the benefit of the doubt, and did not rebuke him.", "그는 그 미심쩍음을 적에게 유리한 쪽으로 돌려 두고 그를 꾸짖지 않았다."], ["He merely observed, very tranquilly, and perhaps with too much humility, that the duties of the situation, such as they were, had, he believed, been done to the satisfaction of the late bishop.", "그저 아주 차분하게, 그리고 어쩌면 지나칠 만큼 겸손하게, 그 자리의 직무란 것이 어떠했든 간에 선대 주교께서 흡족해하실 만큼은 해냈다고 믿는다고만 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 다시 웃었다."], ["Slope again smiled, and this time the smile was intended to operate against the memory of the late bishop rather than against the energy of the ex-warden; so it was understood by Mr.", "이번 웃음은 전임 워든의 열의보다는 선대 주교의 기억을 겨냥한 것이었고, 하딩 씨도 그렇게 알아들었다."], ["Harding. The colour rose to his cheeks, and he began to feel very angry.", "그의 뺨에 핏기가 올랐고, 그는 몹시 화가 나기 시작했다."]], [["\"You must be aware, Mr. Harding, that things are a good deal changed in Barchester,\"", "“하딩 씨도 아시겠지만, 바체스터도 사정이 꽤 많이 달라졌지요.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Harding said that he was aware of it.", "하딩 씨는 알고 있다고 말했다."], ["\"And not only in Barchester, Mr.", "“바체스터만이 아닙니다, 하딩 씨."], ["Harding, but in the world at large.", "세상 전체가 그렇지요."], ["It is not only in Barchester that a new man is carrying out new measures and casting away the useless rubbish of past centuries.", "새 사람이 새 방책을 펴고 지난 세기들의 쓸모없는 쓰레기를 치워 내는 곳이 바체스터만은 아니에요."], ["The same thing is going on throughout the country.", "같은 일이 온 나라에서 벌어지고 있습니다."], ["Work is now required from every man who receives wages, and they who have to superintend the doing of work, and the paying of wages, are bound to see that this rule is carried out.", "이제는 삯을 받는 사람이라면 누구에게나 일이 요구되고, 그 일이 되어 가는 것과 삯이 치러지는 것을 감독해야 하는 자리에 있는 사람들은 그 원칙이 지켜지는지 살펴야 할 의무가 있지요."], ["New men, Mr. Harding, are now needed and are now forthcoming in the church, as well as in other professions.\"", "새 사람이, 하딩 씨, 다른 직업에서와 마찬가지로 교회에서도 지금 필요해졌고 또 지금 나오고 있는 것입니다.”"]], [["All this was wormwood to our old friend.", "이 모든 말이 우리의 오랜 벗에게는 쓰디쓴 쑥이었다."], ["He had never rated very high his own abilities or activity, but all the feelings of his heart were with the old clergy, and any antipathies of which his heart was susceptible were directed against those new, busy, uncharitable, self-lauding men, of whom Mr. Slope was so good an example.", "그는 제 능력이나 활동력을 높이 친 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그의 마음이 품은 정은 온통 옛 성직자들 편에 있었고, 그의 마음이 품을 수 있는 반감이란 반감은 모조리 저 새롭고 부산스럽고 몰인정하고 제 자랑에 여념 없는 사람들, 곧 슬로프 씨가 그토록 좋은 본보기인 그런 부류를 향해 있었다."]], [["\"Perhaps,\"", "“어쩌면,”"], ["said he, \"the bishop will prefer a new man at the hospital?\"", "그가 말했다. “주교님께서는 요양원에도 새 사람을 두시는 편을 더 바라시겠구려.”"]], [["\"By no means,\"", "“천만의 말씀입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"The bishop is very anxious that you should accept the appointment, but he wishes you should understand beforehand what will be the required duties.", "“주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 이 임명을 받아 주시기를 몹시 바라십니다. 다만 요구되는 직무가 무엇일지를 미리 헤아려 두시기를 바라시는 것이지요."], ["In the first place, a Sabbath-day school will be attached to the hospital.\"", "우선 요양원에 안식일 학교가 딸리게 됩니다.”"]], [["\"What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["For the old men?\"", "그 늙은이들을 위해서 말이오?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"No, Mr.", "“아닙니다, 하딩 씨."], ["Harding, not for the old men, but for the benefit of the children of such of the poor of Barchester as it may suit.", "그 노인들을 위한 것이 아니라, 바체스터의 가난한 이들 가운데 형편이 맞는 집 아이들을 위한 것이지요."], ["The bishop will expect that you shall attend this school, and that the teachers shall be under your inspection and care.\"", "주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 이 학교에 나오시고, 교사들이 하딩 씨의 감독과 보살핌 아래 있기를 바라실 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr. Harding slipped his topmost hand off the other and began to rub the calf of the leg which was supported.", "하딩 씨는 위에 얹었던 손을 다른 손에서 미끄러뜨려 내리고는, 포개어 올린 쪽 다리의 종아리를 문지르기 시작했다."]], [["\"As to the old men,\" continued Mr.", "“그 노인들로 말하자면,”"], ["Slope, \"and the old women who are to form a part of the hospital, the bishop is desirous that you shall have morning and evening service on the premises every Sabbath, and one weekday service; that you shall preach to them once at least on Sundays; and that the whole hospital be always collected for morning and evening prayer.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “그리고 앞으로 요양원의 한 부분을 이루게 될 노파들로 말하자면, 주교님께서는 하딩 씨께서 안식일마다 그 건물 안에서 아침 예배와 저녁 예배를 올리시고 주중에도 한 차례 예배를 올리시기를, 또 주일에는 적어도 한 번은 그들에게 설교를 하시기를, 그리고 아침 기도와 저녁 기도에는 요양원 전체가 언제나 모이도록 하시기를 바라십니다."], ["The bishop thinks that this will render it unnecessary that any separate seats in the cathedral should be reserved for the hospital inmates.\"", "주교님께서는 그렇게 하면 요양원 거주자들을 위해 대성당에 따로 자리를 잡아 둘 필요가 없어지리라고 보십니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope paused, but Mr. Harding still said nothing.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 멈추었으나, 하딩 씨는 여전히 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"Indeed, it would be difficult to find seats for the women; on the whole, Mr.", "“실은 그 노파들 자리를 찾아 주기도 어려울 겁니다. 대체로, 하딩 씨, 이참에 아주 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠군요."], ["Harding, I may as well say at once, that for people of that class the cathedral service does not appear to me the most useful--even if it be so for any class of people.\"", "저로서는 그런 계층의 사람들에게 대성당 예배가 가장 유익한 것으로는 보이지 않습니다. 설령 어느 계층엔가는 그러하다 치더라도 말이지요.”"]], [["\"We will not discuss that, if you please,\"", "“미안하지만 그 문제는 논하지 않았으면 하오.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I am not desirous of doing so; at least, not at the present moment.", "“저도 그러고 싶지 않습니다. 적어도 지금 이 자리에서는요."], ["I hope, however, you fully understand the bishop's wishes about the new establishment of the hospital; and if, as I do not doubt, I shall receive from you an assurance that you accord with his lordship's views, it will give me very great pleasure to be the bearer from his lordship to you of the presentation to the appointment.\"", "다만 요양원을 새로 꾸리는 일에 관한 주교님의 뜻만은 하딩 씨께서 충분히 헤아리셨기를 바랍니다. 그리고 제가 의심치 않는 대로, 주교님의 견해에 동의하신다는 다짐을 하딩 씨께 받게 된다면, 주교님의 임명장을 하딩 씨께 전해 드리는 사람이 되는 것이 저로서는 대단히 기쁘겠습니다.”"]], [["\"But if I disagree with his lordship's views?\"", "“그러나 내가 주교님의 견해에 동의하지 않는다면 어찌 되오?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"But I hope you do not,\"", "“그러실 리야 없기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"But if I do?\"", "“그러나 만일 그렇다면?”"], ["again asked the other.", "상대가 다시 물었다."]], [["\"If such unfortunately should be the case, which I can hardly conceive, I presume your own feelings will dictate to you the propriety of declining the appointment.\"", "“불행히도 그런 경우가 된다면, 저로서는 그런 일이 있으리라 좀처럼 짐작되지 않습니다만, 아마도 하딩 씨 자신의 심정이 임명을 사양하시는 것이 도리라고 일러 줄 테지요.”"]], [["\"But if I accept the appointment and yet disagree with the bishop, what then?\"", "“그러나 내가 임명을 받아들이고서도 주교님과 뜻을 달리한다면, 그때는 어찌 되오?”"]], [["This question rather bothered Mr. Slope. It was true that he had talked the matter over with the bishop and had received a sort of authority for suggesting to Mr. Harding the propriety of a Sunday school and certain hospital services, but he had no authority for saying that these propositions were to be made peremptory conditions attached to the appointment. The bishop's idea had been that Mr. Harding would of course consent and that the school would become, like the rest of those new establishments in the city, under the control of his wife and his chaplain. Mr. Slope's idea had been more correct. He intended that Mr. Harding should refuse the situation, and that an ally of his own should get it, but he had not conceived the possibility of Mr. Harding openly accepting the appointment and as openly rejecting the conditions.", "이 물음은 슬로프 씨를 제법 난처하게 만들었다. 그가 이 일을 주교와 의논했고 주일학교와 요양원 예배 몇 가지가 마땅하다는 뜻을 하딩 씨에게 내비칠 권한 비슷한 것을 받아 두었던 것은 사실이지만, 그 제안들이 임명에 딸린 절대적인 조건이라고 말할 권한까지는 없었다. 주교의 생각은 하딩 씨가 당연히 승낙하리라는 것, 그리고 그 학교도 이 도시의 다른 새 시설들과 마찬가지로 제 아내와 제 전속 사제의 관할 아래 들어오리라는 것이었다. 슬로프 씨의 생각이 더 정확했다. 그는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 거절하고 자기 편 사람이 그 자리를 차지하게 할 작정이었으나, 하딩 씨가 임명은 드러내 놓고 받아들이면서 조건은 그만큼 드러내 놓고 물리칠 가능성까지는 미처 생각해 보지 못했던 것이다."]], [["\"It is not, I presume, probable,\"", "“짐작건대 있을 법한 일은 아니겠지요.”"], ["said he, \"that you will accept from the hands of the bishop a piece of preferment with a fixed predetermination to disacknowledge the duties attached to it.\"", "그가 말했다. “주교님의 손에서 성직 자리를 받으면서 거기 딸린 직무는 인정하지 않겠다고 미리 작정해 두신다는 것은 말입니다.”"]], [["\"If I become warden,\" said Mr.", "“내가 워든이 되고서,”"], ["Harding, \"and neglect my duty, the bishop has means by which he can remedy the grievance.\"", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “제 직무를 게을리한다면, 주교님께는 그 폐단을 바로잡을 방도가 있소.”"]], [["\"I hardly expected such an argument from you, or I may say the suggestion of such a line of conduct,\"", "“하딩 씨에게서 그런 논리를, 아니 그런 처신을 내비치는 말씀을 듣게 되리라고는 미처 생각지 못했습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope with a great look of injured virtue.", "슬로프 씨가 덕을 상한 사람의 표정을 잔뜩 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"Nor did I expect such a proposition.\"", "“나 또한 그런 제안을 받게 되리라고는 생각지 못했소.”"]], [["\"I shall be glad at any rate to know what answer I am to make to his lordship,\"", "“어찌 되었든 주교님께 어떤 답을 올려야 할지는 알았으면 합니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I will take an early opportunity of seeing his lordship myself,\"", "“머지않아 기회를 보아 내가 직접 주교님을 뵙겠소.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Such an arrangement,\" said Mr.", "“그런 식으로 하시는 것은,”"], ["Slope, \"will hardly give his lordship satisfaction.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님을 흡족하게 해 드리지 못할 겁니다."], ["Indeed, it is impossible that the bishop should himself see every clergyman in the diocese on every subject of patronage that may arise.", "실상 주교님께서 교구의 성직자를 하나하나 만나 그때그때 생기는 성직 수여 문제를 일일이 상대하신다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이지요."], ["The bishop, I believe, did see you on the matter, and I really cannot see why he should be troubled to do so again.\"", "제가 알기로 주교님께서는 이 건으로 이미 하딩 씨를 만나 보셨습니다. 그런데 무엇 때문에 다시 그 수고를 하셔야 하는지 저로서는 정말 모르겠군요.”"]], [["\"Do you know, Mr. Slope, how long I have been officiating as a clergyman in this city?\"", "“슬로프 씨, 내가 이 도시에서 성직자로 봉직한 지 얼마나 되었는지 아시오?”"], ["Mr. Slope's wish was now nearly fulfilled.", "슬로프 씨의 바람은 이제 거의 이루어진 셈이었다."], ["Mr. Harding had become angry, and it was probable that he might commit himself.", "하딩 씨가 화가 났으니, 말을 잘못 흘릴 공산이 컸다."]], [["\"I really do not see what that has to do with the question.", "“그것이 이 문제와 무슨 상관이 있는지 저로서는 정말 모르겠습니다."], ["You cannot think the bishop would be justified in allowing you to regard as a sinecure a situation that requires an active, man merely because you have been employed for many years in the cathedral.\"", "하딩 씨께서 여러 해 동안 대성당에서 일하셨다는 이유만으로, 부지런한 사람을 필요로 하는 자리를 한직으로 여기시도록 주교님이 내버려 두시는 것이 온당하다고 여기시지는 않겠지요.”"]], [["\"But it might induce the bishop to see me, if I asked him to do so.", "“그러나 내가 뵙기를 청한다면 주교님께서 나를 만나 주실 마음이 드실 수도 있지 않겠소. 이 일은 벗들과 상의해 보겠소, 슬로프 씨."], ["I shall consult my friends in this matter, Mr.", "그러나 나는 어떤 얕은수도 부릴 생각이 없소."], ["Slope; but I mean to be guilty of no subterfuge--you may tell the bishop that as I altogether disagree with his views about the hospital, I shall decline the situation if I find that any such conditions are attached to it as those you have suggested;\"", "주교님께 이렇게 전해 주시오. 요양원에 관한 주교님의 견해에 나는 전혀 동의할 수 없으니, 그대가 말한 것과 같은 조건이 그 자리에 딸려 있다는 것을 알게 된다면 나는 그 자리를 사양하겠다고 말이오.”"], ["and so saying, Mr. Harding took his hat and went his way.", "그렇게 말하고 하딩 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["Mr. Slope was contented.", "슬로프 씨는 만족했다."], ["He considered himself at liberty to accept Mr. Harding's last speech as an absolute refusal of the appointment.", "그는 하딩 씨의 마지막 말을 임명에 대한 완전한 거절로 받아들여도 좋다고 여겼다."], ["At least, he so represented it to the bishop and to Mrs. Proudie.", "적어도 주교와 프라우디 부인에게는 그렇게 전했다."]], [["\"That is very surprising,\"", "“그것 참 놀라운 일이오.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Not at all,\" said Mrs.", "“놀라울 것 없어요.”"], ["Proudie; \"you little know how determined the whole set of them are to withstand your authority.\"", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 무리가 당신 권위에 맞서겠다고 얼마나 단단히 작정하고 있는지 당신은 도무지 모르시는군요.”"]], [["\"But Mr. Harding was so anxious for it,\"", "“허나 하딩 씨는 그 자리를 그토록 바라지 않았소.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes,\" said Mr.", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["Slope, \"if he can hold it without the slightest acknowledgement of your lordship's jurisdiction.\"", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. “주교님의 관할권을 털끝만큼도 인정하지 않고 그 자리를 쥘 수 있다면 말입니다.”"]], [["\"That is out of the question,\"", "“그건 될 법하지 않은 이야기요.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"I should imagine it to be quite so,\"", "“저도 정말 그러하리라고 생각합니다.”"], ["said the chaplain.", "전속 사제가 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed, I should think so,\"", "“당연히 그래야지요.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I really am sorry for it,\"", "“나로서는 정말 안된 일이오.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know that there is much cause for sorrow,\"", "“안타까워할 까닭이 그리 많은지는 모르겠군요.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"Mr. Quiverful is a much more deserving man, more in need of it, and one who will make himself much more useful in the close neighbourhood of the palace.\"", "“퀴버풀 씨가 훨씬 더 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이고, 그 자리가 훨씬 더 아쉬운 사람이며, 주교관 바로 이웃에서 훨씬 더 쓸모 있게 처신할 사람이에요.”"]], [["\"I suppose I had better see Quiverful?\"", "“제가 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보는 편이 낫겠지요?”"], ["said the chaplain.", "전속 사제가 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose you had,\"", "“그러는 편이 낫겠소.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. The Rubbish Cart", "# 제13장. 쓰레기 수레"]], [["Mr. Harding was not a happy man as he walked down the palace pathway and stepped out into the close.", "주교관의 오솔길을 걸어 내려와 대성당 경내로 나서는 하딩 씨는 행복한 사람이 아니었다."], ["His preferment and pleasant house were a second time gone from him, but that he could endure.", "성직과 안락한 집이 두 번째로 그에게서 떠나갔으나, 그것은 견딜 수 있었다."], ["He had been schooled and insulted by a man young enough to be his son, but that he could put up with.", "아들뻘밖에 되지 않는 사내에게 훈계를 듣고 모욕을 당했으나, 그것도 참을 수 있었다."], ["He could even draw from the very injuries which had been inflicted on him some of that consolation which we may believe martyrs always receive from the injustice of their own sufferings, and which is generally proportioned in its strength to the extent of cruelty with which martyrs are treated.", "그는 자기에게 가해진 바로 그 상처들에서, 순교자란 제가 겪는 부당한 고난으로부터 언제나 얼마간의 위안을 받는 법이라고 우리가 믿는 그 위안을 끌어낼 수조차 있었다. 그리고 그 위안의 힘은 대개 순교자가 받는 잔혹의 정도에 비례하는 법이다."], ["He had admitted to his daughter that he wanted the comfort of his old home, and yet he could have returned to his lodgings in the High Street, if not with exaltation, at least with satisfaction, had that been all.", "그는 옛집의 아늑함이 그립다고 딸에게 털어놓은 바 있었고, 그러면서도 그것이 전부였다면 하이 스트리트의 하숙으로 돌아가는 일을 의기양양까지는 아니어도 적어도 만족스럽게는 받아들일 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But the venom of the chaplain's harangue had worked into his blood, and sapped the life of his sweet contentment.", "그러나 전속 사제의 일장 연설에 담긴 독이 이미 그의 피 속으로 파고들어 그 달콤한 자족의 생기를 말려 놓았다."]], [["\"New men are carrying out new measures and are carting away the useless rubbish of past centuries!\"", "“새 사람들이 새 방책을 시행하고 있으며, 지난 세기들이 남긴 쓸모없는 쓰레기를 수레에 실어 치워 내고 있습니다!”"], ["What cruel words these had been; and how often are they now used with all the heartless cruelty of a Slope!", "이 얼마나 잔인한 말이었던가. 그리고 오늘날 그런 말이 슬로프다운 몰인정한 잔혹함을 다 담은 채 얼마나 자주 쓰이는가!"], ["A man is sufficiently condemned if it can only be shown that either in politics or religion he does not belong to some new school established within the last score of years.", "정치에서든 종교에서든 어떤 사람이 최근 스무 해 안에 세워진 어느 새로운 학파에 속하지 않는다는 것만 보여 줄 수 있으면, 그를 단죄하기에는 그것으로 충분하다."], ["He may then regard himself as rubbish and expect to be carted away.", "그러면 그는 스스로를 쓰레기로 여기고 수레에 실려 나갈 각오를 하는 편이 좋다."], ["A man is nothing now unless he has within him a full appreciation of the new era, an era in which it would seem that neither honesty nor truth is very desirable, but in which success is the only touchstone of merit.", "오늘날 사람은 새 시대를 온전히 알아보는 눈을 제 안에 갖추지 못하면 아무것도 아니다. 그 시대란 정직도 진실도 그다지 바람직해 보이지 않고 오직 성공만이 가치를 재는 시금석인 시대다."], ["We must laugh at everything that is established.", "우리는 확립된 모든 것을 비웃어야 한다."], ["Let the joke be ever so bad, ever so untrue to the real principles of joking; nevertheless we must laugh--or else beware the cart.", "그 농담이 아무리 형편없고 농담의 참된 원리에 아무리 어긋난다 해도 상관없다. 그래도 우리는 웃어야 한다."], ["We must talk, think, and live up to the spirit of the times, and write up to it too, if that cacoethes be upon us, or else we are nought.", "그러지 않으려거든 수레를 조심할 일이다. 우리는 시대의 정신에 맞추어 말하고 생각하고 살아야 하며, 글 쓰는 고질이 우리에게 들렸다면 그 정신에 맞추어 글도 써야 한다."], ["New men and new measures, long credit and few scruples, great success or wonderful ruin, such are now the tastes of Englishmen who know how to live.", "그러지 않으면 우리는 아무것도 아니다. 새 사람과 새 방책, 넉넉한 외상과 모자란 거리낌, 큰 성공 아니면 굉장한 파멸 — 이것이 사는 법을 아는 오늘날 영국인들의 취향이다."], ["Alas, alas! Under such circumstances Mr.", "아아, 슬프도다!"], ["Harding could not but feel that he was an Englishman who did not know how to live.", "이런 형편에서 하딩 씨는 자신이 사는 법을 모르는 영국인이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["This new doctrine of Mr. Slope and the rubbish cart, new at least at Barchester, sadly disturbed his equanimity.", "슬로프 씨와 쓰레기 수레의 이 새로운 교리는, 적어도 바체스터에서는 새로운 것이었는데, 그의 평정을 슬프도록 어지럽혀 놓았다."]], [["\"The same thing is going on throughout the whole country!", "“나라 전체에 걸쳐 똑같은 일이 벌어지고 있습니다!"], ["Work is now required from every man who receives wages!\"", "이제는 삯을 받는 사람이라면 누구에게나 일이 요구됩니다!”"], ["And had he been living all his life receiving wages and doing no work?", "그렇다면 그는 평생 삯만 받고 일은 하지 않으며 살아왔던가?"], ["Had he in truth so lived as to be now in his old age justly reckoned as rubbish fit only to be hidden away in some huge dust-hole?", "그는 정녕, 이제 늘그막에 이르러 어느 커다란 쓰레기 구덩이에 감추어 두기에나 알맞은 쓰레기로 헤아려지는 것이 마땅할 만큼 그렇게 살아왔던가?"], ["The school of men to whom he professes to belong, the Grantlys, the Gwynnes, and the old high set of Oxford divines, are afflicted with no such self-accusations as these which troubled Mr. Harding.", "그가 스스로 속한다고 공언하는 부류의 사람들, 곧 그랜틀리 같은 이들과 그윈 같은 이들, 그리고 옥스퍼드의 옛 고교회파 신학자 무리는 하딩 씨를 괴롭히는 이런 자기 고발 따위에 시달리지 않는다."], ["They, as a rule, are as satisfied with the wisdom and propriety of their own conduct as can be any Mr. Slope, or any Dr.", "그들은 대체로, 어떤 슬로프 씨나 어떤 프라우디 박사가 제 처신을 두고 그러하듯이, 제 처신의 지혜와 온당함에 흡족해한다."], ["Proudie, with his own. But unfortunately for himself Mr.", "그러나 하딩 씨에게는 불행하게도 그런 자신감이 거의 없었다."], ["Harding had little of this self-reliance. When he heard himself designated as rubbish by the Slopes of the world, he had no other resource than to make inquiry within his own bosom as to the truth of the designation.", "세상의 슬로프들에게서 자기가 쓰레기로 불리는 것을 들었을 때, 그는 그 호칭이 참인지를 제 가슴속에 물어보는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다."], ["Alas, alas!", "아아, 슬프도다!"], ["The evidence seemed generally to go against him.", "증거는 대체로 그에게 불리해 보였다."]], [["He had professed to himself in the bishop's parlour that in these coming sources of the sorrow of age, in these fits of sad regret from which the latter years of few reflecting men can be free, religion would suffice to comfort him.", "그는 주교의 응접실에서, 다가오는 이 노년의 슬픔의 원천들 속에서, 곧 생각할 줄 아는 사람이라면 그 만년에 좀처럼 면할 수 없는 저 서글픈 회한의 발작 속에서, 종교가 자신을 위로해 주기에 넉넉하리라고 스스로 공언한 바 있었다."], ["Yes, religion could console him for the loss of any worldly good, but was his religion of that active sort which would enable him so to repent of misspent years as to pass those that were left to him in a spirit of hope for the future?", "그렇다, 종교는 세속의 어떤 좋은 것을 잃더라도 그를 위로해 줄 수 있었다. 그러나 그의 종교는, 헛되이 보낸 세월을 뉘우쳐 남은 세월을 앞날의 소망 가운데 보내게 해 줄 만큼 그렇게 활발한 종류의 것이었던가?"], ["And such repentance itself, is it not a work of agony and of tears?", "그리고 그런 뉘우침이란 그 자체로 고뇌와 눈물의 노역이 아닌가?"], ["It is very easy to talk of repentance, but a man has to walk over hot ploughshares before he can complete it; to be skinned alive as was St. Bartholomew; to be stuck full of arrows as was St.", "회개를 입에 올리기는 아주 쉬우나, 사람이 그것을 끝맺기까지는 벌겋게 달군 보습 위를 걸어야 하고, 성 바르톨로메오처럼 산 채로 살가죽이 벗겨져야 하고, 성 세바스티아노처럼 온몸에 화살이 꽂혀야 하고, 성 로렌초처럼 석쇠 위에 누워 구워져야 한다!"], ["Sebastian; to lie broiling on a gridiron like St. Lorenzo!", "만일 그의 지난 삶이 그만한 회개를 요구한다면 어찌할 것인가?"], ["How if his past life required such repentance as this? Had he the energy to go through with it?", "그에게 그것을 끝까지 겪어 낼 기력이 있었던가?"]], [["Mr. Harding, after leaving the palace, walked slowly for an hour or so beneath the shady elms of the close and then betook himself to his daughter's house.", "하딩 씨는 주교관을 나선 뒤 한 시간 남짓 대성당 경내의 그늘진 느릅나무 아래를 천천히 거닐다가 딸의 집으로 발길을 옮겼다."], ["He had at any rate made up his mind that he would go out to Plumstead to consult Dr. Grantly, and that he would in the first instance tell Eleanor what had occurred.", "그는 어쨌든 플럼스테드로 나가 그랜틀리 박사와 의논하기로, 그리고 우선은 엘리너에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기하기로 마음을 정한 터였다."]], [["And now he was doomed to undergo another misery. Mr.", "그런데 이제 그는 또 한 차례의 괴로움을 겪을 운명이었다."], ["Slope had forestalled him at the widow's house.", "슬로프 씨가 미망인의 집에 한발 앞서 다녀갔던 것이다."], ["He had called there on the preceding afternoon.", "그는 바로 전날 오후에 그 집에 들렀다."], ["He could not, he had said, deny himself the pleasure of telling Mrs. Bold that her father was about to return to the pretty house at Hiram's Hospital.", "아버지가 히램 요양원의 그 예쁜 집으로 돌아가시게 되었다는 소식을 볼드 부인에게 전하는 기쁨을 스스로 마다할 수는 없었노라고 그는 말했다."], ["He had been instructed by the bishop to inform Mr. Harding that the appointment would now be made at once.", "임명이 이제 곧바로 이루어지리라는 것을 하딩 씨에게 알리라는 주교의 지시를 받았다고도 했다."], ["The bishop was of course only too happy to be able to be the means of restoring to Mr. Harding the preferment which he had so long adorned.", "물론 주교로서는 하딩 씨가 그토록 오래 빛내던 그 성직을 그에게 되돌려 드리는 통로가 될 수 있어 더없이 기쁘다는 것이었다."], ["And then by degrees Mr. Slope had introduced the subject of the pretty school which he hoped before long to see attached to the hospital.", "그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는 차차, 머지않아 요양원에 딸린 것으로 보게 되기를 바라는 그 예쁜 학교 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["He had quite fascinated Mrs. Bold by his description of this picturesque, useful, and charitable appendage, and she had gone so far as to say that she had no doubt her father would approve, and that she herself would gladly undertake a class.", "그림처럼 어여쁘고 유익하며 자비로운 그 부속 시설에 대한 그의 묘사에 볼드 부인은 완전히 홀려서, 아버지도 틀림없이 찬성하시리라고, 그리고 자기도 기꺼이 한 반을 맡겠다고까지 말했다."]], [["Anyone who had heard the entirely different tone and seen the entirely different manner in which Mr. Slope had spoken of this projected institution to the daughter and to the father could not have failed to own that Mr. Slope was a man of genius. He said nothing to Mrs. Bold about the hospital sermons and services, nothing about the exclusion of the old men from the cathedral, nothing about dilapidation and painting, nothing about carting away the rubbish. Eleanor had said to herself that certainly she did not like Mr. Slope personally, but that he was a very active, zealous clergyman and would no doubt be useful in Barchester. All this paved the way for much additional misery to Mr. Harding.", "이 계획된 시설을 두고 슬로프 씨가 딸에게 말할 때와 아버지에게 말할 때 그 어조가 온통 딴판이고 태도 또한 온통 딴판이었음을 듣고 본 사람이라면 누구든, 슬로프 씨가 천재적인 인물임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 볼드 부인에게 요양원의 설교와 예배에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 노인들을 대성당에서 내보내는 일에 대해서도 한마디 없었으며, 퇴락과 도색에 대해서도, 쓰레기를 수레에 실어 치우는 일에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않았다. 엘리너는 속으로, 슬로프 씨가 사람됨으로는 마음에 들지 않는 것이 분명하지만 그가 매우 활동적이고 열성적인 성직자이며 바체스터에서 틀림없이 쓸모가 있으리라고 생각했다. 이 모든 것이 하딩 씨에게 한층 더한 괴로움이 닥칠 길을 닦아 놓았다."]], [["Eleanor put on her happiest face as she heard her father on the stairs, for she thought she had only to congratulate him; but directly she saw his face she knew that there was but little matter for congratulation.", "엘리너는 아버지가 계단을 올라오는 소리를 듣고 더없이 밝은 얼굴을 지었다. 축하만 드리면 되는 줄 알았기 때문이다."], ["She had seen him with the same weary look of sorrow on one or two occasions before, and remembered it well.", "그러나 아버지의 얼굴을 보는 순간 축하할 일이 별로 없다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["She had seen him when he first read that attack upon himself in \"The Jupiter\"", "그 지치고 시름겨운 표정을 그녀는 전에도 한두 번 본 적이 있었고 잘 기억하고 있었다."], ["which had ultimately caused him to resign the hospital, and she had seen him also when the archdeacon had persuaded him to remain there against his own sense of propriety and honour.", "결국 아버지가 요양원을 사직하게 만든 『주피터』의 그 공격 기사를 아버지가 처음 읽던 때에 그 얼굴을 보았고, 부주교가 아버지 자신의 온당함과 명예에 대한 감각을 거슬러 가면서까지 그 자리에 남으시라고 설득하던 때에도 그 얼굴을 보았다."], ["She knew at a glance that his spirit was in deep trouble.", "그녀는 한눈에 아버지의 마음이 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있음을 알았다."]], [["\"Oh, Papa, what is it?\"", "“어머, 아버지, 무슨 일이세요?”"], ["said she, putting down her boy to crawl upon the floor.", "그녀는 아들을 내려놓아 바닥을 기게 하며 말했다."]], [["\"I came to tell you, my dear,\"", "“네게 말해 두려고 왔단다, 얘야.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"that I am going out to Plumstead: you won't come with me, I suppose?\"", "“나는 플럼스테드로 나가 볼 참이다. 너는 같이 가지 않겠지?”"]], [["\"To Plumstead, Papa?", "“플럼스테드요, 아버지?"], ["Shall you stay there?\"", "거기서 묵으시려고요?”"]], [["\"I suppose I shall, to-night: I must consult the archdeacon about this weary hospital.", "“오늘 밤은 아무래도 그리되겠지. 이 성가신 요양원 일을 부주교와 의논해야 하니 말이다."], ["Ah me!", "아아!"], ["I wish I had never thought of it again.\"", "그 일을 다시 떠올리지나 말았더라면 좋았을 것을.”"]], [["\"Why, Papa, what is the matter?\"", "“아버지, 대체 무슨 일이 있으셨어요?”"]], [["\"I've been with Mr.", "“슬로프 씨를 만나고 오는 길이란다, 얘야."], ["Slope, my dear, and he isn't the pleasantest companion in the world, at least not to me.\"", "그런데 그 사람은 세상에서 가장 유쾌한 말벗은 못 되더구나. 적어도 나에게는 말이다.”"], ["Eleanor gave a sort of half-blush, but she was wrong if she imagined that her father in any way alluded to her acquaintance with Mr. Slope.", "엘리너는 얼굴을 반쯤 붉혔으나, 아버지가 자기와 슬로프 씨의 교분을 조금이라도 두고 하는 말이라고 여겼다면 그것은 잘못이었다."]], [["\"Well, Papa.\"", "“그런데요, 아버지.”"]], [["\"He wants to turn the hospital into a Sunday-school and a preaching-house, and I suppose he will have his way.", "“그 사람은 요양원을 주일학교와 설교소로 바꾸고 싶어 하는데, 아마 제 뜻대로 하겠지."], ["I do not feel myself adapted for such an establishment, and therefore, I suppose, I must refuse the appointment.\"", "나는 그런 시설에 어울리는 사람이라는 생각이 도무지 들지 않는구나. 그러니 아무래도 그 임명을 사양해야 할 것 같다.”"]], [["\"What would be the harm of the school, Papa?\"", "“학교가 무슨 해가 되나요, 아버지?”"]], [["\"The want of a proper schoolmaster, my dear.\"", "“제대로 된 교사가 없다는 것이 문제란다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"But that would of course be supplied.\"", "“하지만 그거야 당연히 마련될 텐데요.”"]], [["\"Mr. Slope wishes to supply it by making me his schoolmaster.", "“슬로프 씨는 나를 제 교사로 삼아서 그것을 마련할 생각이더구나."], ["But as I am hardly fit for such work, I intend to decline.\"", "그러나 나는 그런 일에 도무지 맞지 않으니 사양할 작정이다.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa! Mr.", "“어머, 아버지!"], ["Slope doesn't intend that.", "슬로프 씨는 그런 뜻이 아니에요."], ["He was here yesterday, and what he intends--\"", "그분이 어제 여기 오셨는데, 그분 생각은—”"]], [["\"He was here yesterday, was he?\"", "“그 사람이 어제 여기 왔었다고?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, Papa.\"", "“네, 아버지.”"]], [["\"And talking about the hospital?\"", "“요양원 이야기를 하면서 말이냐?”"]], [["\"He was saying how glad he would be, and the bishop too, to see you back there again.", "“아버지가 그곳으로 돌아가시는 것을 보게 되어 자기도 주교님도 얼마나 기쁘겠느냐고 하셨어요."], ["And then he spoke about the Sunday-school; and to tell the truth I agreed with him; and I thought you would have done so too.", "그러고는 주일학교 이야기를 하셨고요. 사실대로 말씀드리면 저는 그 말에 찬성했어요."], ["Mr.", "아버지도 그러시리라고 생각했고요."], ["Slope spoke of a school, not inside the hospital, but just connected with it, of which you would be the patron and visitor; and I thought you would have liked such a school as that; and I promised to look after it and to take a class--and it all seemed so very--.", "슬로프 씨가 말한 학교는 요양원 안에 두는 것이 아니라 그저 거기에 딸린 학교인데, 아버지가 그 후원자이자 감독이 되시는 거예요. 저는 아버지가 그런 학교라면 마음에 들어 하시리라고 생각했어요."], ["But, oh, Papa!", "그래서 제가 그 일을 돌보고 한 반을 맡겠다고 약속했고요. 그게 다 참 그렇게—."], ["I shall be so miserable if I find I have done wrong.\"", "아, 그런데 아버지! 제가 잘못한 것이라면 저는 정말이지 견딜 수 없이 괴로울 거예요.”"]], [["\"Nothing wrong at all, my dear,\"", "“조금도 잘못한 것 없다, 얘야.”"], ["said he gently, very gently rejecting his daughter's caress.", "그는 딸이 안겨 오는 것을 부드럽게, 아주 부드럽게 물리치며 말했다."], ["\"There can be nothing wrong in your wishing to make yourself useful; indeed, you ought to do so by all means.", "“네가 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶어 하는 데에 잘못이 있을 리 있겠느냐. 아니, 무슨 수를 써서라도 마땅히 그래야 하고말고."], ["Everyone must now exert himself who would not choose to go to the wall.\"", "이제는 누구든 뒷전으로 밀려나기 싫다면 제힘을 다 써야 하는 법이니까.”"], ["Poor Mr. Harding thus attempted in his misery to preach the new doctrine to his child.", "가엾은 하딩 씨는 이렇게 제 괴로움 속에서 그 새로운 교리를 제 자식에게 설교해 보려 들었다."], ["\"Himself or herself, it's all the same,\"", "“그가 됐든 그녀가 됐든 매한가지지.” 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued; \"you will be quite right, my dear, to do something of this sort; but--\"", "“얘야, 네가 그런 일을 하는 것은 아주 옳은 일이다. 다만—”"]], [["\"Well, Papa.\"", "“그런데요, 아버지.”"]], [["\"I am not quite sure that if I were you I would select Mr. Slope for my guide.\"", "“내가 너라면 슬로프 씨를 길잡이로 고르지는 않을 것 같구나.”"]], [["\"But I never have done so and never shall.\"", "“저는 한 번도 그런 적이 없고 앞으로도 그러지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"It would be very wicked of me to speak evil of him, for to tell the truth I know no evil of him; but I am not quite sure that he is honest.", "“그 사람을 나쁘게 말하는 것은 매우 못된 짓이겠지. 사실 나는 그에 대해 나쁜 것을 아는 바가 없으니 말이다."], ["That he is not gentlemanlike in his manners, of that I am quite sure.\"", "다만 그가 정직한 사람인지는 잘 모르겠구나. 그의 태도가 신사답지 못하다는 것만은 아주 분명하다만.”"]], [["\"I never thought of taking him for my guide, Papa.\"", "“그분을 길잡이로 삼겠다는 생각은 해 본 적도 없어요, 아버지.”"]], [["\"As for myself, my dear,\"", "“나로 말하자면, 얘야,”"], ["continued he, \"we know the old proverb--'It's bad teaching an old dog tricks.'", "그가 말을 이었다. “우리가 아는 옛 속담이 있잖느냐."], ["I must decline the Sunday-school, and shall therefore probably decline the hospital also.", "늙은 개에게 재주를 가르치기는 어려운 법이라고. 나는 주일학교를 사양해야겠고, 따라서 아마 요양원도 사양하게 되겠지."], ["But I will first see your brother-in-law.\"", "하지만 먼저 네 형부를 만나 보마.”"], ["So he took up his hat, kissed the baby, and withdrew, leaving Eleanor in as low spirits as himself.", "그러고서 그는 모자를 집어 들고 아기에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 물러갔으니, 엘리너는 그와 마찬가지로 가라앉은 마음으로 남겨졌다."]], [["All this was a great aggravation to his misery.", "이 모든 일이 그의 괴로움을 크게 더해 놓았다."], ["He had so few with whom to sympathize that he could not afford to be cut off from the one whose sympathy was of the most value to him.", "그에게는 마음을 나눌 사람이 워낙 적었으므로, 그중에서도 가장 값진 공감을 주던 사람과 사이가 끊기는 것을 감당할 형편이 못 되었다."], ["And yet it seemed probable that this would be the case.", "그런데도 그리될 성싶었다."], ["He did not own to himself that he wished his daughter to hate Mr. Slope, yet had she expressed such a feeling there would have been very little bitterness in the rebuke he would have given her for so uncharitable a state of mind.", "그는 딸이 슬로프 씨를 미워하기를 바란다고 스스로 인정하지는 않았으나, 딸이 그런 감정을 드러냈다면 그토록 매정한 마음가짐을 나무라는 그의 꾸지람에는 쓴맛이 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["The fact, however, was that she was on friendly terms with Mr. Slope, that she coincided with his views, adhered at once to his plans, and listened with delight to his teaching.", "그러나 사실인즉 딸은 슬로프 씨와 사이가 좋았고, 그의 견해에 동조했으며, 그의 계획을 곧바로 따랐고, 그의 가르침을 즐겁게 들었다."], ["Mr. Harding hardly wished his daughter to hate the man, but he would have preferred that to her loving him.", "하딩 씨가 딸이 그 사람을 미워하기를 바란 것은 아니었으나, 딸이 그를 사랑하느니 차라리 미워하는 편이 나았을 것이다."]], [["He walked away to the inn to order a fly, went home to put up his carpet-bag, and then started for Plumstead.", "그는 여관으로 걸어가 삯마차를 부탁하고, 집으로 돌아가 여행 가방을 꾸린 뒤 플럼스테드로 떠났다."], ["There was, at any rate, no danger that the archdeacon would fraternize with Mr.", "어쨌거나 부주교가 슬로프 씨와 손을 잡을 위험은 없었다."], ["Slope; but then he would recommend internecine war, public appeals, loud reproaches, and all the paraphernalia of open battle.", "그러나 그 대신 부주교는 끝장을 보는 싸움과 공개 호소와 요란한 비난, 그리고 드러내 놓고 벌이는 전투의 온갖 장비를 권할 것이었다."], ["Now that alternative was hardly more to Mr. Harding's taste than the other.", "그런데 그 대안이라는 것도 하딩 씨의 취향에는 앞의 것보다 나을 것이 거의 없었다."]], [["When Mr. Harding reached the parsonage, he found that the archdeacon was out, and would not be home till dinnertime, so he began his complaint to his elder daughter.", "하딩 씨가 목사관에 이르렀을 때 부주교는 밖에 나가 저녁 식사 때까지는 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알게 되었고, 그래서 그는 큰딸에게 하소연을 시작했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly entertained quite as strong an antagonism to Mr.", "그랜틀리 부인은 슬로프 씨에게 남편 못지않게 강한 반감을 품고 있었다."], ["Slope as did her husband; she was also quite as alive to the necessity of combating the Proudie faction, of supporting the old church interest of the close, of keeping in her own set such of the loaves and fishes as duly belonged to it; and was quite as well prepared as her lord to carry on the battle without giving or taking quarter.", "그녀 역시 프라우디 일파와 맞서 싸우고, 대성당 경내의 옛 교회 세력을 떠받치며, 응당 제 편에 속하는 떡고물은 제 편에 붙들어 두어야 한다는 필요를 남편만큼이나 절실히 느끼고 있었고, 자비를 베풀지도 구하지도 않은 채 싸움을 끌고 갈 채비 또한 그 주인만큼이나 잘 되어 있었다."], ["Not that she was a woman prone to quarrelling, or ill-inclined to live at peace with her clerical neighbours; but she felt, as did the archdeacon, that the presence of Mr.", "그렇다고 그녀가 다투기 좋아하는 여자라거나 성직자 이웃들과 화목하게 지내기를 꺼리는 여자라는 말은 아니다."], ["Slope in Barchester was an insult to everyone connected with the late bishop, and that his assumed dominion in the diocese was a spiritual injury to her husband.", "다만 그녀는 부주교와 마찬가지로, 슬로프 씨가 바체스터에 있다는 것 자체가 선대 주교와 인연 있는 모든 이에 대한 모욕이며 그가 교구에서 참칭하는 지배가 제 남편에게는 영적인 상해라고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["Hitherto people had little guessed how bitter Mrs. Grantly could be.", "그때까지 사람들은 그랜틀리 부인이 얼마나 매서울 수 있는지 거의 짐작하지 못했다."], ["She lived on the best of terms with all the rectors' wives around her.", "그녀는 주위의 교구 목사 부인들과 더없이 좋은 사이로 지냈다."], ["She had been popular with all the ladies connected with the close.", "대성당 경내와 연이 닿는 부인이라면 누구에게나 인기가 있었다."], ["Though much the wealthiest of the ecclesiastical matrons of the county, she had so managed her affairs that her carriage and horses had given umbrage to none.", "주(州)의 성직자 부인들 가운데 단연 가장 부유했으면서도, 제 마차와 말이 누구의 눈총도 받지 않도록 처신할 줄 알았다."], ["She had never thrown herself among the county grandees so as to excite the envy of other clergymen's wives.", "다른 성직자 부인들의 시샘을 살 만큼 주의 세도가들 틈에 끼어든 적도 없었다."], ["She never talked too loudly of earls and countesses, or boasted that she gave her governess sixty pounds a year, or her cook seventy. Mrs.", "백작이니 백작부인이니 하는 이야기를 큰 소리로 늘어놓은 적도, 가정교사에게 한 해 60파운드를 주고 요리사에게 70파운드를 준다고 자랑한 적도 없었다."], ["Grantly had lived the life of a wise, discreet, peace-making woman, and the people of Barchester were surprised at the amount of military vigour she displayed as general of the feminine Grantlyite forces.", "그랜틀리 부인은 지혜롭고 신중하며 화평을 이루는 여인의 삶을 살아왔던 터라, 바체스터 사람들은 그녀가 그랜틀리파 여성 부대의 장군으로서 드러내 보이는 군사적 기백의 크기에 놀라고 말았다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly soon learned that her sister Eleanor had promised to assist Mr. Slope in the affairs of the hospital school, and it was on this point that her attention first fixed itself.", "그랜틀리 부인은 동생 엘리너가 요양원 학교 일에서 슬로프 씨를 돕겠다고 약속했다는 것을 이내 알게 되었고, 그녀의 주의가 맨 먼저 붙들린 것도 바로 그 대목이었다."]], [["\"How can Eleanor endure him?\"", "“엘리너가 어떻게 그 사람을 견딜 수 있을까요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"He is a very crafty man,\"", "“아주 교활한 사람이지.”"], ["said her father, \"and his craft has been successful in making Eleanor think that he is a meek, charitable, good clergyman.", "아버지가 말했다. “그리고 그 교활함이 먹혀들어, 엘리너는 그를 온유하고 자비로우며 선량한 성직자라고 여기게 된 게야."], ["God forgive me, if I wrong him, but such is not his true character in my opinion.\"", "내가 그를 잘못 보는 것이라면 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를. 하지만 내 생각에 그의 참된 성품은 그런 것이 아니다.”"]], [["\"His true character, indeed!\"", "“참된 성품이라니요!”"], ["said she, with something approaching scorn for her father's moderation.", "아버지의 온건함을 거의 경멸하다시피 하며 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I only hope he won't have craft enough to make Eleanor forget herself and her position.\"", "“저는 다만 그 사람이 엘리너로 하여금 제 자신과 제 처지를 잊게 만들 만큼 교활하지는 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"Do you mean marry him?\"", "“그 사람과 혼인이라도 한다는 말이냐?”"], ["said he, startled out of his usual demeanour by the abruptness and horror of so dreadful a proposition.", "그토록 끔찍한 이야기가 느닷없이 튀어나오는 바람에 그는 놀라 평소의 몸가짐을 잃고 말했다."]], [["\"What is there so improbable in it?", "“그게 그토록 있을 법하지 않은 일인가요?"], ["Of course that would be his own object if he thought he had any chance of success.", "조금이라도 가망이 있다고 여긴다면 물론 그것이야말로 그 사람의 목적일 테지요."], ["Eleanor has a thousand a year entirely at her own disposal, and what better fortune could fall to Mr.", "엘리너에게는 온전히 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,000파운드가 있어요."], ["Slope's lot than the transferring of the disposal of such a fortune to himself?\"", "그런 재산을 제 손으로 넘겨 놓는 것보다 더 나은 행운이 슬로프 씨에게 굴러들 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"But you can't think she likes him, Susan?\"", "“그렇지만 그 애가 그를 좋아한다고 여길 수야 없지 않으냐, 수전?”"]], [["\"Why not?\"", "“왜 아니겠어요?”"], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."], ["\"Why shouldn't she like him?", "“왜 그 애가 그를 좋아하지 않겠어요?"], ["He's just the sort of man to get on with a woman left, as she is, with no one to look after her.\"", "그 사람은 지금 그 애처럼 돌보아 줄 이 하나 없이 남겨진 여자에게 파고들기에 딱 알맞은 부류인걸요.”"]], [["\"Look after her!\"", "“돌보아 줄 이라니!”"], ["said the unhappy father; \"don't we look after her?\"", "불행한 아버지가 말했다. “우리가 그 애를 돌보지 않느냐?”"]], [["\"Ah, Papa, how innocent you are!", "“어머, 아버지, 어쩌면 그렇게 순진하세요!"], ["Of course it was to be expected that Eleanor should marry again.", "엘리너가 다시 혼인하리라는 것이야 당연히 예상할 수 있는 일이었지요."], ["I should be the last to advise her against it, if she would only wait the proper time, and then marry at least a gentleman.\"", "그 애가 마땅한 때를 기다렸다가 적어도 신사다운 사람과 혼인하기만 한다면, 저야말로 그것을 말릴 마음이 조금도 없고요.”"]], [["\"But you don't really mean to say that you suppose Eleanor has ever thought of marrying Mr. Slope?", "“하지만 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 생각을 한 적이 있다고 정말로 말하려는 건 아니겠지?"], ["Why, Mr. Bold has only been dead a year.\"", "아니, 볼드 군이 죽은 지 이제 겨우 한 해가 아니냐.”"]], [["\"Eighteen months,\"", "“열여덟 달이에요.”"], ["said his daughter.", "딸이 말했다."], ["\"But I don't suppose Eleanor has ever thought about it.", "“그렇지만 엘리너가 그런 생각을 해 보았으리라고는 저도 여기지 않아요."], ["It is very probable, though, that he has; and that he will try and make her do so; and that he will succeed too, if we don't take care what we are about.\"", "다만 그 사람은 생각했을 법하고, 그 애도 그렇게 하도록 만들려 들 법하지요. 그리고 우리가 정신을 바짝 차리지 않으면 그가 뜻을 이루고 말 거예요.”"]], [["This was quite a new phase of the affair to poor Mr. Harding.", "이것은 가엾은 하딩 씨에게는 이 일의 아주 새로운 국면이었다."], ["To have thrust upon him as his son-in-law, as the husband of his favourite child, the only man in the world whom he really positively disliked, would be a misfortune which he felt he would not know how to endure patiently.", "세상에서 자기가 참으로 확실하게 싫어하는 단 하나의 사내를 사위로, 그것도 가장 아끼는 자식의 남편으로 떠맡게 된다는 것은, 어떻게 참을성 있게 견뎌 낼지 도무지 알 수 없는 불행이었다."], ["But then, could there be any ground for so dreadful a surmise?", "그러나 그토록 끔찍한 억측에 무슨 근거가 있기는 한 것일까?"], ["In all worldly matters he was apt to look upon the opinion of his eldest daughter as one generally sound and trustworthy.", "세상일에 관해서라면 그는 큰딸의 의견을 대체로 온당하고 믿을 만한 것으로 여기는 편이었다."], ["In her appreciation of character, of motives, and the probable conduct both of men and women, she was usually not far wrong.", "사람의 성품이며 동기며 남자와 여자가 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지를 헤아리는 데서 그녀는 웬만해서는 크게 빗나가지 않았다."], ["She had early foreseen the marriage of Eleanor and John Bold; she had at a glance deciphered the character of the new bishop and his chaplain; could it possibly be that her present surmise should ever come forth as true?", "그녀는 엘리너와 존 볼드의 혼인을 일찌감치 내다보았고, 새 주교와 그 전속 사제의 성품을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그렇다면 지금의 이 억측도 언젠가 사실로 드러나는 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"But you don't think that she likes him?\"", "“그래도 그 애가 그를 좋아한다고는 생각지 않겠지?”"], ["said Mr. Harding again.", "하딩 씨가 다시 말했다."]], [["\"Well, Papa, I can't say that I think she dislikes him as she ought to do.", "“글쎄요, 아버지, 그 애가 마땅히 싫어해야 할 만큼 그를 싫어한다고는 말씀드릴 수 없네요."], ["Why is he visiting there as a confidential friend, when he never ought to have been admitted inside the house?", "애초에 그 집 문턱도 넘어서는 안 되었을 사람이 어째서 허물없는 벗으로 드나드는 걸까요?"], ["Why is it that she speaks to him about your welfare and your position, as she clearly has done?", "어째서 그 애가 그 사람에게 아버지의 안녕과 처지를 두고 이야기를 했을까요? 분명히 그랬는데 말이에요."], ["At the bishop's party the other night I saw her talking to him for half an hour at the stretch.\"", "요전 날 밤 주교님 댁 모임에서는 그 애가 그 사람과 내리 반 시간이나 이야기하는 것을 제가 보았어요.”"]], [["\"I thought Mr. Slope seemed to talk to nobody there but that daughter of Stanhope's,\"", "“내가 보기에 슬로프 씨는 거기서 스탠호프 댁 그 딸 말고는 아무하고도 이야기하지 않는 것 같더구나.”"], ["said Mr. Harding, wishing to defend his child.", "하딩 씨가 제 자식을 두둔하려는 마음에서 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr.", "“어머, 슬로프 씨는 아버지가 생각하시는 것보다 영리한 사람이에요, 아버지."], ["Slope is a cleverer man than you think of, Papa, and keeps more than one iron in the fire.\"", "그리고 한 번에 한 가지 일만 벌여 놓는 사람도 아니고요.”"]], [["To give Eleanor her due, any suspicion as to the slightest inclination on her part towards Mr. Slope was a wrong to her.", "엘리너를 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀가 슬로프 씨에게 조금이라도 마음이 기울었으리라는 의심은 그녀에게 부당한 것이었다."], ["She had no more idea of marrying Mr. Slope than she had of marrying the bishop, and the idea that Mr.", "그녀는 주교와 혼인할 생각이 없는 것과 마찬가지로 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 생각도 없었고, 슬로프 씨가 구혼자로 나서리라는 생각은 그녀에게 떠오른 적조차 없었다."], ["Slope would present himself as a suitor had never occurred to her. Indeed, to give her her due again, she had never thought about suitors since her husband's death.", "실로, 다시 한번 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀는 남편이 죽은 뒤로 구혼자라는 것을 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["But nevertheless it was true that she had overcome all that repugnance to the man which was so strongly felt for him by the rest of the Grantly faction.", "그러나 그럼에도 그랜틀리 편 사람들이 그토록 강하게 품고 있던 그 사람에 대한 혐오를 그녀가 다 이겨 냈다는 것만은 사실이었다."], ["She had forgiven him his sermon.", "그녀는 그의 설교를 용서했다."], ["She had forgiven him his Low Church tendencies, his Sabbath-schools, and puritanical observances.", "그의 저교회파 성향과 안식일 학교와 청교도적 계율을 용서했다."], ["She had forgiven his pharisaical arrogance, and even his greasy face and oily, vulgar manners.", "바리새인 같은 오만도, 심지어 번들거리는 얼굴과 기름기 도는 상스러운 태도까지 용서했다."], ["Having agreed to overlook such offences as these, why should she not in time be taught to regard Mr. Slope as a suitor?", "그만한 허물들을 눈감아 주기로 한 마당에, 그녀가 머지않아 슬로프 씨를 구혼자로 여기도록 길들여지지 못할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["And as to him, it must also be affirmed that he was hitherto equally innocent of the crime imputed to him.", "그리고 그 사람으로 말하자면, 그 역시 그때까지는 그에게 씌워진 혐의에 대해 똑같이 결백했다고 밝혀 두어야 한다."], ["How it had come to pass that a man whose eyes were generally so widely open to everything around him had not perceived that this young widow was rich as well as beautiful, cannot probably now be explained.", "주위의 모든 것에 대개 그토록 눈이 밝은 사내가 이 젊은 미망인이 아름다울 뿐 아니라 부유하기도 하다는 것을 어찌하여 알아차리지 못했는지는 아마 이제 와서는 설명할 길이 없을 것이다."], ["But such was the fact. Mr.", "그러나 사실이 그러했다."], ["Slope had ingratiated himself with Mrs. Bold, merely as he had done with other ladies, in order to strengthen his party in the city.", "슬로프 씨는 도시에서 제 세력을 굳히려는 뜻에서, 다른 부인들에게 그랬듯이 그저 그렇게 볼드 부인의 환심을 사 두었을 뿐이었다."], ["He subsequently amended his error, but it was not till after the interview between him and Mr. Harding.", "그는 나중에 제 잘못을 바로잡았지만, 그것은 그와 하딩 씨의 면담이 있은 뒤의 일이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV. The New Champion", "# 제14장. 새로운 투사"]], [["The archdeacon did not return to the parsonage till close upon the hour of dinner, and there was therefore no time to discuss matters before that important ceremony.", "부주교는 저녁 식사 시각이 다 되어서야 목사관으로 돌아왔고, 따라서 그 중대한 의식을 치르기 전에 일을 의논할 겨를은 없었다."], ["He seemed to be in an especial good humour, and welcomed his father-in-law with a sort of jovial earnestness that was usual with him when things on which he was intent were going on as he would have them.", "그는 유난히 기분이 좋아 보였고, 마음에 두고 있던 일이 뜻대로 풀려 갈 때면 늘 그러하듯 유쾌하면서도 자못 진지한 태도로 장인을 맞이했다."]], [["\"It's all settled, my dear,\"", "“다 정리됐소, 여보.”"], ["said he to his wife as he washed his hands in his dressing-room, while she, according to her wont, sat listening in the bedroom; \"Arabin has agreed to accept the living.", "부주교는 옷방에서 손을 씻으며 아내에게 말했고, 아내는 늘 하던 대로 침실에 앉아 귀를 기울이고 있었다. “애러빈이 그 성직록을 받기로 했소."], ["He'll be here next week.\"", "다음 주면 이리로 올 거요.”"], ["And the archdeacon scrubbed his hands and rubbed his face with a violent alacrity, which showed that Arabin's coming was a great point gained.", "그러고서 부주교는 어찌나 기세 좋게 손을 문지르고 얼굴을 비벼 대는지, 애러빈이 온다는 것이 크게 얻어 낸 한 수임을 알 만했다."]], [["\"Will he come here to Plumstead?\"", "“여기 플럼스테드로 오시는 거예요?”"], ["said the wife.", "아내가 물었다."]], [["\"He has promised to stay a month with us,\"", "“한 달 우리 집에 묵기로 약속했소.”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"so that he may see what his parish is like.", "부주교가 말했다. “제 교구가 어떤 곳인지 둘러볼 수 있게 말이오."], ["You'll like Arabin very much.", "당신도 애러빈을 아주 마음에 들어 할 거요."], ["He's a gentleman in every respect, and full of humour.\"", "모든 면에서 신사인 데다 해학이 넘치는 사람이오.”"]], [["\"He's very queer, isn't he?\"", "“그분은 무척 별난 사람이라면서요?”"], ["asked the lady.", "부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Well--he is a little odd in some of his fancies, but there's nothing about him you won't like.", "“글쎄—공상하는 대목이 좀 유별나기는 하오만, 당신이 싫어할 구석이라고는 하나도 없는 사람이오."], ["He is as staunch a churchman as there is at Oxford.", "옥스퍼드를 통틀어 그만큼 신실한 교회인은 없소."], ["I really don't know what we should do without Arabin.", "애러빈이 없었더라면 우리가 대체 어찌했을지 나는 정말이지 모르겠소."], ["It's a great thing for me to have him so near me, and if anything can put Slope down, Arabin will do it.\"", "그가 이렇게 가까이 온다는 것은 내게 큰일이고, 무엇이든 슬로프를 눌러 앉힐 수 있는 것이 있다면 그건 애러빈이 해낼 거요.”"]], [["The Reverend Francis Arabin was a fellow of Lazarus, the favoured disciple of the great Dr. Gwynne, a High Churchman at all points--so high, indeed, that at one period of his career he had all but toppled over into the cesspool of Rome--a poet and also a polemical writer, a great pet in the common-rooms at Oxford, an eloquent clergyman, a droll, odd, humorous, energetic, conscientious man, and, as the archdeacon had boasted of him, a thorough gentleman.", "프랜시스 애러빈 목사는 라자러스 칼리지의 펠로이자 저 위대한 그윈 박사가 아끼는 문하생으로, 모든 점에서 고교회파였다—실로 어찌나 높았던지 한때는 로마라는 시궁창으로 넘어질 뻔한 적까지 있었다—시인이자 논쟁적인 저술가였고, 옥스퍼드 담화실의 큰 귀염둥이였으며, 언변이 뛰어난 성직자였고, 익살스럽고 별나고 해학이 넘치며 정력적이고 양심적인 사람이었고, 부주교가 자랑삼아 말한 대로 철저한 신사였다."], ["As he will hereafter be brought more closely to our notice, it is now only necessary to add that he had just been presented to the vicarage of St. Ewold by Dr.", "앞으로 그를 더 가까이 들여다볼 자리가 있을 터이니, 여기서는 다만 그가 방금 그랜틀리 박사에게서 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제직을 받았다는 것, 그리고 그 성직록의 수여권이 부주교인 그랜틀리 박사에게 있었다는 것만 덧붙여 두면 되겠다."], ["Grantly, in whose gift as archdeacon the living lay. St.", "세인트 이월드는 바체스터 시 바로 바깥에 놓인 교구다."], ["Ewold is a parish lying just without the city of Barchester. The suburbs of the new town, indeed, are partly within its precincts, and the pretty church and parsonage are not much above a mile distant from the city gate.", "실상 신시가지의 변두리는 일부가 그 경계 안에 들어와 있고, 아담한 교회와 목사관은 성문에서 1마일 남짓밖에 떨어져 있지 않다."]], [["St. Ewold is not a rich piece of preferment--it is worth some three or four hundred a year at most, and has generally been held by a clergyman attached to the cathedral choir. The archdeacon, however, felt, when the living on this occasion became vacant, that it imperatively behoved him to aid the force of his party with some tower of strength, if any such tower could be got to occupy St. Ewold's. He had discussed the matter with his brethren in Barchester, not in any weak spirit as the holder of patronage to be used for his own or his family's benefit, but as one to whom was committed a trust on the due administration of which much of the church's welfare might depend. He had submitted to them the name of Mr. Arabin, as though the choice had rested with them all in conclave, and they had unanimously admitted that, if Mr. Arabin would accept St. Ewold's, no better choice could possibly be made.", "세인트 이월드는 넉넉한 성직 자리가 아니다—많아야 한 해 300파운드나 400파운드 값어치이며, 대체로 대성당 성가대에 딸린 성직자가 맡아 왔다. 그러나 부주교는 이번에 그 성직록이 비게 되자, 세인트 이월드에 들어앉힐 만한 든든한 성채를 구할 수만 있다면 그런 성채로 제 진영의 힘을 보태는 것이 무엇보다 마땅한 도리라고 느꼈다. 그는 이 문제를 바체스터의 동료 성직자들과 의논했는데, 성직 수여권을 제 것이나 제 집안의 이익에 쓰려는 옹졸한 마음에서가 아니라, 그 온당한 시행에 교회의 안녕이 크게 달린 소임을 맡은 사람으로서 그리한 것이었다. 그는 마치 선택권이 회합에 모인 그들 모두에게 있기라도 한 듯 애러빈 씨의 이름을 내놓았고, 그들은 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드를 받아 준다면 그보다 나은 선택은 있을 수 없다고 이구동성으로 인정했다."]], [["If Mr. Arabin would accept St.", "애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드를 받아 준다면!"], ["Ewold's! There lay the difficulty.", "어려움은 바로 거기에 있었다."], ["Mr. Arabin was a man standing somewhat prominently before the world, that is, before the Church of England world.", "애러빈 씨는 세상 앞에, 그러니까 영국 국교회의 세상 앞에 제법 두드러지게 서 있는 사람이었다."], ["He was not a rich man, it is true, for he held no preferment but his fellowship; but he was a man not over-anxious for riches, not married of course, and one whose time was greatly taken up in discussing, both in print and on platforms, the privileges and practices of the church to which he belonged.", "부자가 아닌 것은 사실이니, 펠로직 말고는 아무 성직 자리도 지닌 것이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 재물을 지나치게 탐하는 사람이 아니었고, 물론 결혼도 하지 않았으며, 제 시간의 대부분을 인쇄물로든 강단에서든 제가 속한 교회의 특권과 관행을 논하는 데 쓰는 사람이었다."], ["As the archdeacon had done battle for its temporalities, so did Mr.", "부주교가 교회의 세속적 재산을 위해 싸워 왔듯 애러빈 씨는 교회의 영적 권능을 위해 싸워 왔으며, 둘 다 양심을 좇아 그리했다."], ["Arabin do battle for its spiritualities, and both had done so conscientiously; that is, not so much each for his own benefit as for that of others.", "다시 말해 저마다 제 이익을 위해서라기보다 남의 이익을 위해 싸운 것이었다."]], [["Holding such a position as Mr. Arabin did, there was much reason to doubt whether he would consent to become the parson of St. Ewold's, and Dr. Grantly had taken the trouble to go himself to Oxford on the matter. Dr. Gwynne and Dr. Grantly together had succeeded in persuading this eminent divine that duty required him to go to Barchester. There were wheels within wheels in this affair. For some time past Mr. Arabin had been engaged in a tremendous controversy with no less a person than Mr. Slope, respecting the apostolic succession. These two gentlemen had never seen each other, but they had been extremely bitter in print. Mr. Slope had endeavoured to strengthen his cause by calling Mr. Arabin an owl, and Mr. Arabin had retaliated by hinting that Mr. Slope was an infidel. This battle had been commenced in the columns of \"The Jupiter,\" a powerful newspaper, the manager of which was very friendly to Mr. Slope's view of the case. The matter, however, had become too tedious for the readers of \"The Jupiter,\" and a little note had therefore been appended to one of Mr. Slope's most telling rejoinders, in which it had been stated that no further letters from the reverend gentlemen could be inserted except as advertisements.", "애러빈 씨가 그런 위치에 있는 터라 그가 세인트 이월드의 교구 목사가 되기를 승낙할지는 크게 의심스러웠고, 그래서 그랜틀리 박사는 수고를 무릅쓰고 몸소 옥스퍼드까지 다녀왔던 것이다. 그윈 박사와 그랜틀리 박사가 힘을 합친 끝에 이 저명한 신학자에게 바체스터로 가는 것이 의무라고 설득해 냈다. 이 일에는 바퀴 안에 또 바퀴가 물려 있었다. 얼마 전부터 애러빈 씨는 다름 아닌 슬로프 씨와 사도 계승을 두고 엄청난 논쟁을 벌여 오던 참이었다. 두 신사는 서로 얼굴 한 번 본 적이 없었으나 지면에서는 지독히도 신랄했다. 슬로프 씨는 애러빈 씨를 올빼미라 부르는 것으로 제 편의 힘을 키우려 했고, 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨가 무신론자라고 넌지시 이르는 것으로 앙갚음을 했다. 이 싸움은 위세 등등한 신문 『주피터』의 지면에서 시작되었는데, 그 신문의 주간은 이 사안에 관한 슬로프 씨의 견해에 무척 우호적이었다. 그러나 이 문제는 『주피터』 독자들에게 너무나 지루해졌고, 그리하여 슬로프 씨가 쓴 가장 통쾌한 반박문 가운데 하나의 끝에 짧은 알림이 덧붙었으니, 두 목사님의 편지는 이제 광고로가 아니면 더는 실을 수 없다는 내용이었다."]], [["Other methods of publication were, however, found, less expensive than advertisements in \"The Jupiter,\" and the war went on merrily.", "그러나 『주피터』에 광고를 싣는 것보다 돈이 덜 드는 다른 발표 방법들이 발견되었고, 전쟁은 신명나게 이어졌다."], ["Mr. Slope declared that the main part of the consecration of a clergyman was the self-devotion of the inner man to the duties of the ministry.", "슬로프 씨는 성직자를 성별하는 일의 요체가 속사람이 성직의 직무에 스스로를 바치는 데 있다고 단언했다."], ["Mr. Arabin contended that a man was not consecrated at all, had, indeed, no single attribute of a clergyman, unless he became so through the imposition of some bishop's hands, who had become a bishop through the imposition of other hands, and so on in a direct line to one of the apostles.", "애러빈 씨는, 어떤 주교의 안수를 받아 그리되지 않은 이상 그 사람은 애초에 성별된 것이 아니며 성직자의 속성이라고는 하나도 지니지 못한 것이라고, 게다가 그 주교 또한 다른 이의 안수를 받아 주교가 된 자여야 하고 그렇게 사도 가운데 한 사람에게까지 곧게 이어져야 한다고 맞섰다."], ["Each had repeatedly hung the other on the horns of a dilemma, but neither seemed to be a whit the worse for the hanging; and so the war went on merrily.", "둘은 번갈아 상대를 딜레마의 뿔에 몇 번이고 꿰어 매달았으나 매달린 쪽이 조금이라도 축난 기색은 어느 쪽에도 없었다. 그리하여 전쟁은 신명나게 이어졌다."]], [["Whether or no the near neighbourhood of the foe may have acted in any way as an inducement to Mr. Arabin to accept the living of St.", "적이 가까이 있다는 사실이 애러빈 씨로 하여금 세인트 이월드의 성직록을 받아들이게 하는 데 얼마쯤 미끼 노릇을 했는지 어떤지, 우리는 아는 체하지 않겠다."], ["Ewold, we will not pretend to say; but it had at any rate been settled in Dr. Gwynne's library, at Lazarus, that he would accept it, and that he would lend his assistance towards driving the enemy out of Barchester, or, at any rate, silencing him while he remained there.", "어쨌든 그가 그것을 받아들이리라는 것, 그리고 적을 바체스터에서 몰아내는 데, 적어도 그가 그곳에 머무는 동안 입을 다물게 하는 데 힘을 보태리라는 것은 라자러스에 있는 그윈 박사의 서재에서 이미 정해진 일이었다."], ["Mr. Arabin intended to keep his rooms at Oxford and to have the assistance of a curate at St.", "애러빈 씨는 옥스퍼드의 제 방을 그대로 두고 세인트 이월드에는 부목사를 두어 거들게 할 작정이었으나, 되도록 많은 시간을 바체스터 인근에서 보내겠다고 약속했다."], ["Ewold, but he promised to give as much time as possible to the neighbourhood of Barchester, and from so great a man Dr.", "그만한 인물에게서 그만한 약속을 받았으니 그랜틀리 박사는 아주 흡족했다."], ["Grantly was quite satisfied with such a promise. It was no small part of the satisfaction derivable from such an arrangement that Bishop Proudie would be forced to institute into a living immediately under his own nose the enemy of his favourite chaplain.", "이 계획에서 얻어지는 만족 가운데 적지 않은 몫은, 프라우디 주교가 제가 아끼는 전속 사제의 원수를 제 코앞에 있는 성직록에 손수 취임시켜야만 하리라는 데 있었다."]], [["All through dinner the archdeacon's good humour shone brightly in his face. He ate of the good things heartily, he drank wine with his wife and daughter, he talked pleasantly of his doings at Oxford, told his father-in-law that he ought to visit Dr.", "저녁 식사 내내 부주교의 좋은 기분은 얼굴에 환하게 드러났다."], ["Gwynne at Lazarus, and launched out again in praise of Mr. Arabin.", "그는 좋은 음식을 푸짐하게 먹었고, 아내와 딸과 더불어 포도주를 마셨고, 옥스퍼드에서 한 일을 유쾌하게 이야기했고, 장인에게 라자러스로 그윈 박사를 찾아뵈어야 한다고 일렀으며, 다시금 애러빈 씨에 대한 칭찬을 늘어놓았다."]], [["\"Is Mr. Arabin married, Papa?\"", "“애러빈 씨는 결혼하셨나요, 아버지?”"], ["asked Griselda.", "하고 그리젤다가 물었다."]], [["\"No, my dear, the fellow of a college is never married.\"", "“아니다, 얘야. 칼리지의 펠로는 결혼하는 법이 없단다.”"]], [["\"Is he a young man, Papa?\"", "“젊은 분이신가요, 아버지?”"]], [["\"About forty, I believe,\"", "“마흔쯤 되었을 게다.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh!\"", "“어머!”"], ["said Griselda.", "하고 그리젤다가 말했다."], ["Had her father said eighty, Mr. Arabin would not have appeared to her to be very much older.", "아버지가 여든이라고 했더라도 그리젤다에게 애러빈 씨가 그리 더 늙어 보이지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["When the two gentlemen were left alone over their wine, Mr. Harding told his tale of woe.", "두 신사만 남아 포도주를 앞에 두게 되었을 때, 하딩 씨는 제 딱한 사정을 털어놓았다."], ["But even this, sad as it was, did not much diminish the archdeacon's good humour, though it greatly added to his pugnacity.", "그러나 사정이 딱하기는 해도 그 이야기가 부주교의 좋은 기분을 크게 덜어 내지는 못했으니, 다만 그의 호전성을 크게 북돋웠을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"He can't do it,\" said Dr.", "“그자는 그렇게 못 합니다.”"], ["Grantly over and over again, as his father-in-law explained to him the terms on which the new warden of the hospital was to be appointed; \"he can't do it.", "장인이 요양원의 새 워든을 임명하는 조건을 설명하는 동안 그랜틀리 박사는 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. “그자는 그렇게 못 합니다."], ["What he says is not worth the trouble of listening to.", "그자가 하는 말은 귀 기울일 값어치도 없어요."], ["He can't alter the duties of the place.\"", "그 자리의 직무를 제멋대로 바꿀 수는 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Who can't?\"", "“누가 못 한다는 건가?”"], ["asked the ex-warden.", "전임 워든이 물었다."]], [["\"Neither the bishop nor the chaplain, nor yet the bishop's wife, who, I take it, has really more to say to such matters than either of the other two.", "“주교도, 전속 사제도, 하물며 주교 부인도 못 합니다. 하기야 그런 일에 실제로 참견하는 몫은 그 부인이 나머지 둘을 합친 것보다 크겠습니다만."], ["The whole body corporate of the palace together have no power to turn the warden of the hospital into a Sunday-schoolmaster.\"", "주교관 식솔이 통째로 달려든다 해도 요양원 워든을 주일학교 교사로 둔갑시킬 권한은 없습니다.”"]], [["\"But the bishop has the power to appoint whom he pleases, and--\"", "“하지만 주교에게는 마음에 드는 사람을 임명할 권한이 있고, 또—”"]], [["\"I don't know that; I rather think he'll find he has no such power.", "“그건 모를 일이지요. 저는 오히려 주교가 그런 권한이 없다는 걸 알게 되리라고 봅니다."], ["Let him try it, and see what the press will say.", "어디 한번 해 보라지요, 그러면 신문이 뭐라고 하는지 보게 될 겁니다."], ["For once we shall have the popular cry on our side.", "이번만큼은 여론이 우리 편일 테니까요."], ["But Proudie, ass as he is, knows the world too well to get such a hornet's nest about his ears.\"", "하지만 프라우디는 얼간이이긴 해도 세상 물정은 너무 잘 아는 사람이라, 제 귓가에 그런 말벌 집을 만들어 두지는 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr. Harding winced at the idea of the press.", "하딩 씨는 신문 이야기에 몸이 움츠러들었다."], ["He had had enough of that sort of publicity, and was unwilling to be shown up a second time either as a monster or as a martyr.", "그런 종류의 세평이라면 겪을 만큼 겪었고, 괴물로든 순교자로든 다시 한번 구경거리가 되고 싶지는 않았다."], ["He gently remarked that he hoped the newspapers would not get hold of his name again, and then suggested that perhaps it would be better that he should abandon his object.", "그는 신문이 제 이름을 다시 물고 늘어지지 않았으면 좋겠다고 조용히 말했고, 이어 어쩌면 이 일을 그만두는 편이 낫겠다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. “나도 늙어 가네.”"], ["\"I am getting old,\"", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"and after all I doubt whether I am fit to undertake new duties.\"", "“게다가 새 직무를 맡을 만한 사람인지도 사실 의심스럽고 말일세.”"]], [["\"New duties?\"", "“새 직무라니요?”"], ["said the archdeacon; \"don't I tell you there shall be no new duties?\"", "부주교가 말했다. “새 직무 같은 건 없을 거라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?”"]], [["\"Or perhaps old duties either,\" said Mr.", "“아니면 옛 직무도 마찬가지겠지.”"], ["Harding; \"I think I will remain content as I am.\"", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “나는 지금 이대로 만족하며 지낼까 하네.”"], ["The picture of Mr. Slope carting away the rubbish was still present to his mind.", "쓰레기를 수레에 실어 내가던 슬로프 씨의 모습이 아직도 그의 머릿속에 남아 있었다."]], [["The archdeacon drank off his glass of claret and prepared himself to be energetic.", "부주교는 클라레 잔을 단숨에 비우고는 기운을 낼 채비를 했다. “저는 정말이지 바랍니다.”"], ["\"I do hope,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"that you are not going to be so weak as to allow such a man as Mr. Slope to deter you from doing what you know it is your duty to do.", "“장인어른께서 슬로프 씨 같은 자에게 겁을 먹고 마땅히 하셔야 할 일을 못 하실 만큼 약해지지는 않으시기를요."], ["You know it is your duty to resume your place at the hospital now that parliament has so settled the stipend as to remove those difficulties which induced you to resign it.", "의회가 봉급 문제를 그렇게 정리해 주어 애초에 사임하시게 만들었던 어려움이 사라진 지금, 요양원의 자리로 돌아가시는 것이 장인어른의 의무라는 것을 아시지 않습니까."], ["You cannot deny this, and should your timidity now prevent you from doing so, your conscience will hereafter never forgive you,\"", "이건 부인하지 못하실 겁니다. 그리고 이제 와서 겁이 나 그리하지 못하신다면, 장인어른의 양심이 두고두고 스스로를 용서하지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["and as he finished this clause of his speech, he pushed over the bottle to his companion.", "그러고서 그는 이 대목의 말을 마치면서 술병을 상대 쪽으로 밀어 주었다."]], [["\"Your conscience will never forgive you,\"", "“양심이 결코 용서하지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"You resigned the place from conscientious scruples, scruples which I greatly respected, though I did not share them.", "“장인어른은 양심의 가책 때문에 그 자리를 내놓으셨지요. 저야 그 가책에 동의하지는 않았습니다만 크게 존경했습니다."], ["All your friends respected them, and you left your old house as rich in reputation as you were ruined in fortune.", "장인어른의 벗들이 모두 그것을 존경했고, 장인어른은 재산으로는 거덜이 난 만큼이나 명성으로는 넉넉해진 채 옛집을 떠나셨습니다."], ["It is now expected that you will return. Dr.", "이제 세상은 장인어른이 돌아가시기를 기대하고 있습니다."], ["Gwynne was saying only the other day--\"", "그윈 박사도 바로 요전에 말씀하시기를—”"]], [["\"Dr. Gwynne does not reflect how much older a man I am now than when he last saw me.\"", "“그윈 박사는 내가 그이를 마지막으로 본 때보다 얼마나 더 늙었는지를 헤아리지 못하는 걸세.”"]], [["\"Old--nonsense,\"", "“늙기는 무슨, 당치 않은 말씀입니다.”"], ["said the archdeacon; \"you never thought yourself old till you listened to the impudent trash of that coxcomb at the palace.\"", "부주교가 말했다. “장인어른은 주교관에 있는 저 멋쟁이 얼간이의 뻔뻔한 헛소리를 듣기 전까지는 스스로를 늙었다고 여기신 적이 없지 않습니까.”"]], [["\"I shall be sixty-five if I live till November,\"", "“11월까지 살면 예순다섯이 되네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"And seventy-five, if you live till November ten years,\"", "“10년 뒤 11월까지 사시면 일흔다섯이 되시겠지요.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"And you bid fair to be as efficient then as you were ten years ago.", "“그리고 그때에도 10년 전만큼이나 정정하게 일하실 게 틀림없습니다."], ["But for heaven's sake let us have no pretence in this matter.", "하지만 제발이지 이 일에 핑계는 대지 맙시다."], ["Your plea of old age is a pretence.", "늙었다는 말씀은 핑계입니다."], ["But you're not drinking your wine.", "그런데 포도주를 통 안 드시는군요."], ["It is only a pretence.", "그것도 핑계일 뿐입니다."], ["The fact is, you are half-afraid of this Slope, and would rather subject yourself to comparative poverty and discomfort than come to blows with a man who will trample on you, if you let him.\"", "사실인즉 장인어른은 이 슬로프라는 자가 반쯤 두려우신 거고, 가만두면 사람을 짓밟고 지나갈 자와 맞붙느니 차라리 궁색과 불편을 감수하시겠다는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"I certainly don't like coming to blows, if I can help it.\"", "“피할 수만 있다면 맞붙는 건 확실히 내키지 않네.”"]], [["\"Nor I neither--but sometimes we can't help it.", "“저도 내키지 않습니다—하지만 피할 수 없을 때도 있지요."], ["This man's object is to induce you to refuse the hospital, that he may put some creature of his own into it; that he may show his power and insult us all by insulting you, whose cause and character are so intimately bound up with that of the chapter.", "저자의 목적은 장인어른이 요양원을 마다하게 만들어 제 수하 아무나 거기 앉히는 것입니다. 그리하여 제 힘을 과시하고, 그 처지와 인품이 참사회의 그것과 그토록 단단히 얽혀 있는 장인어른을 모욕함으로써 우리 모두를 모욕하려는 겁니다."], ["You owe it to us all to resist him in this, even if you have no solicitude for yourself.", "장인어른께서 스스로를 위할 마음이 없으시더라도, 우리 모두를 위해 이 일에서 저자에게 맞서 주셔야 할 도리가 있습니다."], ["But surely, for your own sake, you will not be so lily-livered as to fall into this trap which he has baited for you and let him take the very bread out of your mouth without a struggle.\"", "그러나 무엇보다 장인어른 자신을 위해서라도, 저자가 미끼를 놓아 둔 이 덫에 겁쟁이처럼 걸려들어 입에 든 밥을 싸움 한 번 없이 빼앗기시는 일은 없어야 합니다.”"]], [["Mr. Harding did not like being called lily-livered, and was rather inclined to resent it.", "하딩 씨는 겁쟁이라는 말을 듣는 것이 달갑지 않았고, 오히려 발끈하고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["\"I doubt there is any true courage,\"", "“돈을 두고 다투는 일에 참된 용기랄 것이 있을지 나는 의심스럽네.”"], ["said he, \"in squabbling for money.\"", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["\"If honest men did not squabble for money, in this wicked world of ours, the dishonest men would get it all, and I do not see that the cause of virtue would be much improved.", "“이 사악한 세상에서 정직한 사람들이 돈을 두고 다투지 않는다면 부정직한 자들이 그것을 죄다 차지할 테고, 그렇다고 미덕의 명분이 크게 나아질 것 같지도 않습니다."], ["No--we must use the means which we have.", "안 됩니다—우리는 우리에게 있는 수단을 써야 합니다."], ["If we were to carry your argument home, we might give away every shilling of revenue which the church has, and I presume you are not prepared to say that the church would be strengthened by such a sacrifice.\"", "장인어른의 논리를 끝까지 밀고 가자면 교회가 가진 수입을 한 실링까지 몽땅 내주어야 할 텐데, 그런 희생으로 교회가 튼튼해지리라고 말씀하실 준비는 안 되어 계시겠지요.”"], ["The archdeacon filled his glass and then emptied it, drinking with much reverence a silent toast to the well-being and permanent security of those temporalities which were so dear to his soul.", "부주교는 잔을 채운 다음 비웠으니, 제 영혼에 그토록 소중한 저 세속적 재산의 안녕과 항구한 보전을 위해 자못 경건하게 말없는 축배를 든 것이었다."]], [["\"I think all quarrels between a clergyman and his bishop should be avoided,\"", "“성직자와 그 주교 사이의 다툼은 무엇이든 피해야 한다고 나는 생각하네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I think so too, but it is quite as much the duty of the bishop to look to that as of his inferior.", "“저도 그렇게 생각합니다. 하지만 그것을 살필 의무는 아랫사람 못지않게 주교에게도 있지요."], ["I tell you what, my friend; I'll see the bishop in this matter--that is, if you will allow me--and you may be sure I will not compromise you.", "이렇게 하십시다, 장인어른. 이 일로 제가 주교를 만나 보겠습니다—허락해 주신다면 말이지요—그리고 장인어른께 누가 될 말은 결코 하지 않을 테니 안심하십시오."], ["My opinion is that all this trash about the Sunday-schools and the sermons has originated wholly with Slope and Mrs. Proudie, and that the bishop knows nothing about it.", "제 생각으로는 주일학교니 설교니 하는 이 시시한 소리는 죄다 슬로프와 프라우디 부인에게서 나온 것이고, 주교는 아무것도 모르고 있습니다."], ["The bishop can't very well refuse to see me, and I'll come upon him when he has neither his wife nor his chaplain by him.", "주교로서는 저를 만나지 않겠다고 하기가 어려울 테고, 저는 아내도 전속 사제도 곁에 없을 때를 골라 들이닥칠 겁니다."], ["I think you'll find that it will end in his sending you the appointment without any condition whatever.", "결국 주교가 아무 조건도 붙이지 않고 임명장을 보내오는 것으로 끝나리라는 것을 아시게 될 겁니다."], ["And as to the seats in the cathedral, we may safely leave that to Mr.", "그리고 대성당 좌석 문제는 수석 사제께 맡겨 두어도 무방합니다."], ["Dean. I believe the fool positively thinks that the bishop could walk away with the cathedral if he pleased.\"", "그 얼간이는 주교가 마음만 먹으면 대성당을 통째로 걸머지고 걸어 나갈 수도 있다고 정말로 믿는 모양이더군요.”"]], [["And so the matter was arranged between them. Mr.", "그리하여 두 사람 사이에 일이 그렇게 정해졌다."], ["Harding had come expressly for advice, and therefore felt himself bound to take the advice given him.", "하딩 씨는 오로지 조언을 구하러 온 터라 주어진 조언을 따라야 한다고 스스로 여겼다."], ["He had known, moreover, beforehand that the archdeacon would not hear of his giving the matter up, and accordingly, though he had in perfect good faith put forward his own views, he was prepared to yield.", "게다가 그는 부주교가 이 일을 그만두겠다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않으리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었고, 따라서 제 생각을 참으로 진심에서 내놓기는 했으되 물러설 채비는 되어 있었다."]], [["They therefore went into the drawing-room in good humour with each other, and the evening passed pleasantly in prophetic discussions on the future wars of Arabin and Slope.", "그리하여 두 사람은 서로 기분 좋게 응접실로 건너갔고, 저녁 시간은 애러빈과 슬로프가 앞으로 벌일 전쟁을 점치는 이야기로 유쾌하게 흘러갔다."], ["The frogs and the mice would be nothing to them, nor the angers of Agamemnon and Achilles.", "개구리와 쥐의 싸움도 그에 견주면 아무것도 아닐 터이고, 아가멤논과 아킬레우스의 분노 또한 마찬가지일 터였다."], ["How the archdeacon rubbed his hands and plumed himself on the success of his last move.", "부주교는 제 마지막 한 수가 성공한 것을 두고 어찌나 손을 비비며 우쭐대던지."], ["He could not himself descend into the arena with Slope, but Arabin would have no such scruples.", "그 자신은 슬로프와 더불어 투기장으로 내려갈 수 없는 몸이었으나 애러빈에게는 그런 거리낌이 없을 것이었다."], ["Arabin was exactly the man for such work, and the only man whom he knew that was fit for it.", "애러빈이야말로 그런 일에 꼭 맞는 사람이었고, 그가 아는 사람 가운데 그 일에 합당한 유일한 사람이었다."]], [["The archdeacon's good humour and high buoyancy continued till, when reclining on his pillow, Mrs. Grantly commenced to give him her view of the state of affairs at Barchester.", "부주교의 좋은 기분과 드높은 흥은 베개에 몸을 누였을 때까지도 이어졌으나, 그때 그랜틀리 부인이 바체스터의 형편을 저는 어떻게 보는지 남편에게 들려주기 시작했다."], ["And then certainly he was startled.", "그러자 그는 과연 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["The last words he said that night were as follows:", "그날 밤 그가 마지막으로 한 말은 이러했다."]], [["\"If she does, by heaven I'll never speak to her again.", "“그 애가 정말 그런다면, 맹세코 나는 다시는 그 애와 말을 섞지 않겠소."], ["She dragged me into the mire once, but I'll not pollute myself with such filth as that--\"", "그 애가 나를 한 번 진창으로 끌고 들어갔지만, 그런 오물로 내 몸을 더럽히지는—”"], ["And the archdeacon gave a shudder which shook the whole room, so violently was he convulsed with the thought which then agitated his mind.", "그러고서 부주교는 몸서리를 쳤는데, 그때 그의 머릿속을 뒤흔든 생각에 어찌나 격하게 경련했던지 방 전체가 흔들릴 지경이었다."]], [["Now in this matter the widow Bold was scandalously ill-treated by her relatives.", "그런데 이 일에서 미망인 볼드 부인은 제 친척들에게 언어도단으로 부당한 대접을 받고 있었다."], ["She had spoken to the man three or four times, and had expressed her willingness to teach in a Sunday-school.", "그녀는 그 사내와 서너 번 말을 나누었고, 주일학교에서 가르쳐도 좋다는 뜻을 밝혔을 뿐이다."], ["Such was the full extent of her sins in the matter of Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨와 관련해 그녀가 지은 죄의 전부가 그것이었다."], ["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"], ["But time will show.", "그러나 그 뒤는 시간이 보여 줄 것이다."]], [["The next morning Mr. Harding returned to Barchester, no further word having been spoken in his hearing respecting Mr.", "이튿날 아침 하딩 씨는 바체스터로 돌아갔으니, 슬로프 씨와 제 작은딸의 친분에 관해서는 그가 듣는 데서 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."], ["Slope's acquaintance with his younger daughter. But he observed that the archdeacon at breakfast was less cordial than he had been on the preceding evening.", "그러나 그는 아침 식탁에서 부주교가 전날 저녁보다 덜 다정하다는 것을 눈치챘다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV. The Widow's Suitors", "# 제15장. 미망인의 구혼자들"]], [["Mr. Slope lost no time in availing himself of the bishop's permission to see Mr. Quiverful, and it was in his interview with this worthy pastor that he first learned that Mrs. Bold was worth the wooing. He rode out to Puddingdale to communicate to the embryo warden the goodwill of the bishop in his favour, and during the discussion on the matter it was not unnatural that the pecuniary resources of Mr. Harding and his family should become the subject of remark.", "슬로프 씨는 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보아도 좋다는 주교의 허락을 지체 없이 써먹었는데, 이 훌륭한 목사와 마주 앉은 자리에서 그는 볼드 부인이 구애할 만한 값어치가 있는 사람이라는 사실을 처음으로 알게 되었다. 그는 푸딩데일까지 말을 몰고 나가, 장차 워든이 될 이 사람에게 주교가 그를 좋게 보고 있다는 뜻을 전했고, 그 일을 의논하는 동안 하딩 씨와 그 집안의 금전 형편이 이야깃거리로 떠오른 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다."]], [["Mr. Quiverful, with his fourteen children and his four hundred a year, was a very poor man, and the prospect of this new preferment, which was to be held together with his living, was very grateful to him.", "퀴버풀 씨는 자녀가 열넷에 한 해 400파운드로 살아가는 몹시 가난한 사람이었으므로, 지금의 성직록과 겸해서 받게 될 이 새 자리의 전망이 여간 고마운 것이 아니었다."], ["To what clergyman so circumstanced would not such a prospect be very grateful? But Mr.", "그런 처지에 놓인 성직자라면 어느 누가 그런 전망을 고맙게 여기지 않겠는가?"], ["Quiverful had long been acquainted with Mr. Harding, and had received kindness at his hands, so that his heart misgave him as he thought of supplanting a friend at the hospital.", "그러나 퀴버풀 씨는 오래전부터 하딩 씨와 알고 지냈고 그의 손에서 호의를 받은 적도 있었으므로, 요양원에서 벗의 자리를 가로챈다는 생각에 마음이 켕겼다."], ["Nevertheless, he was extremely civil, cringingly civil, to Mr.", "그럼에도 그는 슬로프 씨에게 지극히 공손했다. 굽실거릴 만큼 공손했다."], ["Slope; treated him quite as the great man; entreated this great man to do him the honour to drink a glass of sherry, at which, as it was very poor Marsala, the now pampered Slope turned up his nose; and ended by declaring his extreme obligation to the bishop and Mr.", "그를 아주 대단한 인물로 떠받들었고, 이 대단한 인물에게 셰리주를 한 잔 들어 주는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 간청했는데, 그것이 형편없는 마르살라였던 탓에 이제는 입이 높아진 슬로프는 코를 찡그렸다. 그리고 끝에 가서는 주교와 슬로프 씨에게 더없이 큰 은혜를 입었노라고, 요양원 자리를 몹시 맡고 싶노라고 밝혔다."], ["Slope and his great desire to accept the hospital, if--if it were certainly the case that Mr. Harding had refused it.", "다만, 다만 하딩 씨가 정말로 그것을 거절한 것이 확실하다면 말이다."]], [["What man as needy as Mr. Quiverful would have been more disinterested?", "퀴버풀 씨만큼 궁한 처지에 놓인 사람 가운데 그보다 더 사심 없는 이가 어디 있었겠는가?"]], [["\"Mr. Harding did positively refuse it,\"", "“하딩 씨는 분명히 그것을 거절했습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope with a certain air of offended dignity, \"when he heard of the conditions to which the appointment is now subjected.", "슬로프 씨가 어딘가 자존심이 상했다는 투로 말했다."], ["Of course you understand, Mr.", "“이번 임명에 붙게 된 조건을 듣고 나서 말이지요."], ["Quiverful, that the same conditions will be imposed on yourself.\"", "물론 퀴버풀 씨, 똑같은 조건이 당신께도 그대로 부과되리라는 것은 알고 계시겠지요.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful cared nothing for the conditions.", "퀴버풀 씨는 조건 따위에는 조금도 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["He would have undertaken to preach any number of sermons Mr. Slope might have chosen to dictate, and to pass every remaining hour of his Sundays within the walls of a Sunday-school.", "슬로프 씨가 받아쓰라고 시키는 설교라면 몇 편이든 맡았을 것이고, 일요일에 남은 시간을 죄다 주일학교 담장 안에서 보내는 일도 마다하지 않았을 것이다."], ["What sacrifices, or at any rate, what promises would have been too much to make for such an addition to his income, and for such a house!", "수입이 그만큼 늘고 그런 집까지 생긴다는데, 무슨 희생인들, 아니 적어도 무슨 약속인들 지나치다 하겠는가!"], ["But his mind still recurred to Mr. Harding.", "그러나 그의 마음은 여전히 하딩 씨에게로 되돌아갔다."]], [["\"To be sure,\"", "“하기야,”"], ["said he; \"Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Harding's daughter is very rich, and why should he trouble himself with the hospital?\"", "“하딩 씨 따님이 아주 부자이니, 그분이 요양원 일로 수고하실 까닭이야 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"You mean Mrs. Grantly,\"", "“그랜틀리 부인 말씀이군요.”"], ["said Slope.", "슬로프가 말했다."]], [["\"I meant his widowed daughter,\"", "“저는 미망인이 되신 따님을 말씀드린 것입니다.”"], ["said the other. \"Mrs.", "상대가 말했다."], ["Bold has twelve hundred a year of her own, and I suppose Mr. Harding means to live with her.\"", "“볼드 부인은 제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있고, 하딩 씨는 그 따님과 함께 지내실 생각인 듯합니다.”"]], [["\"Twelve hundred a year of her own!\"", "“제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드라!”"], ["said Slope, and very shortly afterwards took his leave, avoiding, as far as it was possible for him to do, any further allusion to the hospital.", "슬로프는 그렇게 말하고는 곧 자리를 떴다. 요양원 이야기는 더 꺼내지 않으려고 할 수 있는 데까지 피하면서 말이다."], ["\"Twelve hundred a year!\"", "“한 해 1,200파운드!”"], ["said he to himself as he rode slowly home.", "그는 말을 천천히 몰아 돌아가면서 혼잣말을 했다."], ["If it were the fact that Mrs. Bold had twelve hundred a year of her own, what a fool would he be to oppose her father's return to his old place.", "볼드 부인이 제 몫으로 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있는 것이 사실이라면, 그 아버지가 옛 자리로 돌아가는 일을 막고 나서는 자야말로 얼마나 어리석은 자이겠는가."], ["The train of Mr. Slope's ideas will probably be plain to all my readers.", "슬로프 씨의 생각이 어느 갈래로 뻗어 갔는지는 아마 내 독자 누구에게나 훤할 것이다."], ["Why should he not make the twelve hundred a year his own?", "그 한 해 1,200파운드를 제 것으로 만들지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?"], ["And if he did so, would it not be well for him to have a father-in-law comfortably provided with the good things of this world?", "그리고 그렇게 한다면, 이 세상의 좋은 것들을 넉넉히 갖춘 장인을 두는 편이 좋지 않겠는가?"], ["Would it not, moreover, be much more easy for him to gain the daughter if he did all in his power to forward the father's views?", "게다가 아비의 뜻을 이루어 주려고 힘닿는 데까지 애쓴다면 딸의 마음을 얻기가 훨씬 수월하지 않겠는가?"]], [["These questions presented themselves to him in a very forcible way, and yet there were many points of doubt.", "이 물음들은 그에게 아주 강하게 밀려들었으나, 그래도 미심쩍은 대목이 한둘이 아니었다."], ["If he resolved to restore to Mr. Harding his former place, he must take the necessary steps for doing so at once; he must immediately talk over the bishop, quarrel on the matter with Mrs.", "하딩 씨에게 옛 자리를 돌려주기로 마음을 정한다면 당장 그에 필요한 조처를 밟아야 했다."], ["Proudie, whom he knew he could not talk over, and let Mr. Quiverful know that he had been a little too precipitate as to Mr.", "주교를 곧바로 구슬려야 하고, 구슬릴 수 없다는 것을 스스로도 아는 프라우디 부인과는 이 일로 다투어야 하며, 퀴버풀 씨에게는 하딩 씨가 확실히 거절했다고 한 자기 말이 조금 성급했음을 알려야 했다."], ["Harding's positive refusal. That he could effect all this he did not doubt, but he did not wish to effect it for nothing.", "이 모두를 해낼 수 있다는 것은 의심하지 않았으나, 아무 대가 없이 해내고 싶지는 않았다."], ["He did not wish to give way to Mr. Harding and then be rejected by the daughter.", "하딩 씨에게 양보해 놓고 그 딸에게 퇴짜를 맞고 싶지는 않았다."], ["He did not wish to lose one influential friend before he had gained another.", "세력 있는 벗 하나를 새로 얻기도 전에 다른 하나를 잃고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["And thus he rode home, meditating many things in his mind.", "그리하여 그는 여러 가지를 마음속으로 곱씹으며 말을 몰아 돌아갔다."], ["It occurred to him that Mrs. Bold was sister-in-law to the archdeacon, and that not even for twelve hundred a year would he submit to that imperious man.", "볼드 부인이 부주교의 처제라는 데에 생각이 미쳤고, 한 해 1,200파운드를 준다 해도 그 오만한 사내에게 굽히지는 않겠다는 데에도 생각이 미쳤다."], ["A rich wife was a great desideratum to him, but success in his profession was still greater; there were, moreover, other rich women who might be willing to become wives; and after all, this twelve hundred a year might, when inquired into, melt away into some small sum utterly beneath his notice.", "부유한 아내는 그에게 큰 소망거리였으나 성직에서의 출세는 그보다 더 큰 것이었다. 게다가 아내가 되어 줄 마음이 있을 만한 부유한 여자가 달리 없는 것도 아니었고, 무엇보다 이 한 해 1,200파운드라는 것도 막상 알아보면 그가 거들떠볼 것도 못 되는 푼돈으로 녹아 버릴지 몰랐다."], ["Then also he remembered that Mrs. Bold had a son.", "그러다 볼드 부인에게 아들이 있다는 것도 떠올랐다."]], [["Another circumstance also much influenced him, though it was one which may almost be said to have influenced him against his will.", "또 한 가지 사정이 그에게 크게 작용했는데, 그것은 거의 그의 뜻을 거슬러 작용했다고 해야 할 것이었다."], ["The vision of the Signora Neroni was perpetually before his eyes.", "시뇨라 네로니의 모습이 노상 그의 눈앞에 어른거렸다."], ["It would be too much to say that Mr. Slope was lost in love, but yet he thought, and kept continually thinking, that he had never seen so beautiful a woman.", "슬로프 씨가 사랑에 빠져 정신을 잃었다고까지 하면 지나친 말이겠으나, 그래도 그는 그토록 아름다운 여자는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했고 또 줄곧 그렇게 생각했다."], ["He was a man whose nature was open to such impulses, and the wiles of the Italianized charmer had been thoroughly successful in imposing upon his thoughts.", "그는 본디 그런 충동에 쉽게 열리는 사람이었고, 이탈리아 물이 든 그 요염한 여자의 술수는 그의 머릿속을 차지하는 데 완전히 성공했다."], ["We will not talk about his heart: not that he had no heart, but because his heart had little to do with his present feelings.", "그의 마음에 대해서는 말하지 않기로 하자. 그에게 마음이 없어서가 아니라, 지금의 이 감정에 그의 마음이 관여한 바가 거의 없었기 때문이다."], ["His taste had been pleased, his eyes charmed, and his vanity gratified.", "그의 취향이 흡족했고, 눈이 홀렸으며, 허영이 채워졌다."], ["He had been dazzled by a sort of loveliness which he had never before seen, and had been caught by an easy, free, voluptuous manner which was perfectly new to him.", "그는 여태 본 적 없는 종류의 아름다움에 눈이 부셨고, 그로서는 전혀 새로운, 스스럼없고 자유롭고 관능적인 태도에 사로잡혔다."], ["He had never been so tempted before, and the temptation was now irresistible.", "그렇게까지 유혹을 받아 본 적이 없었고, 이번 유혹은 뿌리칠 수 없었다."], ["He had not owned to himself that he cared for this woman more than for others around him, but yet he thought often of the time when he might see her next, and made, almost unconsciously, little cunning plans for seeing her frequently.", "그는 이 여자를 주위의 다른 여자들보다 더 마음에 두고 있다고 스스로 인정하지는 않았으나, 다음에 언제 그녀를 볼 수 있을지 자주 생각했고, 거의 저도 모르는 사이에 그녀를 자주 볼 수 있는 잔꾀를 이것저것 꾸며 두었다."]], [["He had called at Dr. Stanhope's house the day after the bishop's party, and then the warmth of his admiration had been fed with fresh fuel.", "그는 주교관 연회 다음 날 스탠호프 박사 댁을 찾아갔고, 그때 그의 뜨거운 흠모는 새 땔감을 얻었다."], ["If the signora had been kind in her manner and flattering in her speech when lying upon the bishop's sofa, with the eyes of so many on her, she had been much more so in her mother's drawing-room, with no one present but her sister to repress either her nature or her art.", "주교의 소파에 누워 그 많은 눈길을 받고 있을 때에도 시뇨라의 태도가 다정하고 말이 사람을 우쭐하게 했다면, 제 어머니의 응접실에서 언니 말고는 아무도 없어 타고난 성정도 몸에 익힌 기교도 억누를 것이 없을 때에는 훨씬 더 그러했다."], ["Mr. Slope had thus left her quite bewildered, and could not willingly admit into his brain any scheme a part of which would be the necessity of his abandoning all further special friendship with this lady.", "슬로프 씨는 그렇게 얼이 빠진 채 그 집을 나섰으니, 이 부인과의 각별한 우정을 앞으로 아주 끊어야 한다는 대목이 한 자락이라도 들어 있는 계획이라면 어떤 것도 기꺼이 머릿속에 들이려 하지 않았다."]], [["And so he slowly rode along, very meditative.", "그리하여 그는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 천천히 말을 몰아갔다."]], [["And here the author must beg it to be remembered that Mr. Slope was not in all things a bad man.", "여기서 글쓴이는 슬로프 씨가 매사에 나쁜 사람은 아니었다는 점을 기억해 주기를 청해야겠다."], ["His motives, like those of most men, were mixed, and though his conduct was generally very different from that which we would wish to praise, it was actuated perhaps as often as that of the majority of the world by a desire to do his duty.", "그의 동기는 여느 사람들이 다 그러하듯 뒤섞여 있었고, 그의 행실이 대체로 우리가 칭찬하고 싶은 것과는 거리가 멀었다 해도, 그 행실을 움직인 것은 어쩌면 세상 사람 태반과 마찬가지로 제 본분을 다하려는 마음이었다."], ["He believed in the religion which he taught, harsh, unpalatable, uncharitable as that religion was.", "그는 자기가 가르치는 종교를 믿었다. 그 종교가 아무리 모질고 거슬리고 인정 없는 것이었다 해도 그러했다."], ["He believed those whom he wished to get under his hoof, the Grantlys and Gwynnes of the church, to be the enemies of that religion.", "그는 자기가 발밑에 두고자 하는 이들, 곧 교회의 그랜틀리와 그윈 같은 무리가 그 종교의 적이라고 믿었다."], ["He believed himself to be a pillar of strength, destined to do great things, and with that subtle, selfish, ambiguous sophistry to which the minds of all men are so subject, he had taught himself to think that in doing much for the promotion of his own interests, he was doing much also for the promotion of religion.", "그는 자신이 큰일을 하도록 정해진 튼튼한 기둥이라고 믿었으며, 사람이면 누구나 빠지기 쉬운 그 교묘하고 이기적이고 애매한 궤변으로, 제 잇속을 크게 도모하는 일이 곧 종교를 크게 도모하는 일이기도 하다고 스스로를 가르쳐 놓았다."], ["But Mr. Slope had never been an immoral man.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 결코 부도덕한 사람이었던 적이 없다."], ["Indeed, he had resisted temptations to immorality with a strength of purpose that was creditable to him.", "오히려 그는 부도덕으로 이끄는 유혹을 그로서는 칭찬받을 만한 굳은 뜻으로 물리쳐 왔다."], ["He had early in life devoted himself to works which were not compatible with the ordinary pleasures of youth, and he had abandoned such pleasures not without a struggle.", "그는 일찍부터 젊음의 예사로운 즐거움과는 어울리지 않는 일에 몸을 바쳤고, 그런 즐거움을 버리기까지 나름의 싸움이 없지 않았다."], ["It must therefore be conceived that he did not admit to himself that he warmly admired the beauty of a married woman without heart-felt stings of conscience; and to pacify that conscience he had to teach himself that the nature of his admiration was innocent.", "그러니 그가 남의 아내 된 여인의 아름다움을 뜨겁게 흠모한다고 스스로 인정하면서 양심의 가책을 뼈아프게 느끼지 않았을 리 없다고 보아야 한다. 그리고 그 양심을 달래기 위해 그는 제 흠모가 결백한 종류의 것이라고 스스로를 가르쳐야 했다."]], [["And thus he rode along meditative and ill at ease.", "그리하여 그는 생각에 잠긴 채, 마음이 편치 않은 채 말을 몰아갔다."], ["His conscience had not a word to say against his choosing the widow and her fortune.", "그의 양심은 그가 미망인과 그 재산을 골랐다는 데 대해서는 한마디도 할 말이 없었다."], ["That he looked upon as a godly work rather than otherwise; as a deed which, if carried through, would redound to his credit as a Christian.", "그 일은 오히려 경건한 일로 여겨졌으니, 끝까지 밀고 나간다면 그리스도인으로서의 명예를 높여 줄 행실이었다."], ["On that side lay no future remorse, no conduct which he might probably have to forget, no inward stings. If it should turn out to be really the fact that Mrs.", "그쪽에는 뒷날의 뉘우침도, 잊어버려야 할 처신도, 마음속의 가책도 없었다."], ["Bold had twelve hundred a year at her own disposal, Mr. Slope would rather look upon it as a duty which he owed his religion to make himself the master of the wife and the money; as a duty too, in which some amount of self-sacrifice would be necessary.", "볼드 부인이 정말로 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있는 것이 사실로 드러난다면, 슬로프 씨는 그 아내와 그 돈의 주인이 되는 일을 오히려 제 종교에 지고 있는 본분으로 여길 것이었다."], ["He would have to give up his friendship with the signora, his resistance to Mr.", "그것도 어느 정도의 자기희생이 따르는 본분으로 말이다."], ["Harding, his antipathy--no, he found on mature self-examination that he could not bring himself to give up his antipathy to Dr. Grantly.", "시뇨라와의 우정을 버려야 할 것이고, 하딩 씨에게 맞서는 일도 버려야 할 것이며, 반감도 — 아니, 곰곰이 자신을 살펴본 끝에 그는 그랜틀리 박사에 대한 반감만은 도저히 버릴 수 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["He would marry the lady as the enemy of her brother-in-law if such an arrangement suited her; if not, she must look elsewhere for a husband.", "그 부인이 그런 조건을 받아들인다면 그는 그녀의 형부에게 맞서는 적으로서 그녀와 혼인할 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면 부인은 남편감을 다른 데서 찾아야 할 것이었다."]], [["It was with such resolve as this that he reached Barchester.", "그런 결심을 품고 그는 바체스터에 다다랐다."], ["He would at once ascertain what the truth might be as to the lady's wealth, and having done this he would be ruled by circumstances in his conduct respecting the hospital.", "그는 우선 이 부인의 재산에 관한 진상이 무엇인지 알아보고, 그런 다음 요양원 문제에 관한 처신은 형편에 따라 정하기로 했다."], ["If he found that he could turn round and secure the place for Mr. Harding without much self-sacrifice, he would do so; but if not, he would woo the daughter in opposition to the father.", "큰 자기희생 없이 방향을 돌려 그 자리를 하딩 씨에게 확보해 줄 수 있다면 그렇게 할 것이고, 그럴 수 없다면 아비에게 맞서면서 딸에게 구애할 것이었다."], ["But in no case would he succumb to the archdeacon.", "그러나 어떤 경우에도 부주교에게 굽히지는 않을 것이었다."]], [["He saw his horse taken round to the stable, and immediately went forth to commence his inquiries.", "그는 말이 마구간으로 끌려가는 것을 보고 나서 곧바로 알아보러 나섰다."], ["To give Mr. Slope his due, he was not a man who ever let much grass grow under his feet.", "슬로프 씨에게 공평하게 말하자면, 그는 제 발밑에 풀이 길게 자라도록 내버려 두는 사람이 결코 아니었다."]], [["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"], ["She was doomed to be the intended victim of more schemes than one.", "그녀는 하나가 아닌 여러 계략의 표적이 될 운명이었다."]], [["About the time that Mr. Slope was visiting the vicar of Puddingdale, a discussion took place respecting her charms and wealth at Dr.", "슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일의 교구 사제를 찾아가 있던 무렵, 대성당 경내의 스탠호프 박사 댁에서는 그녀의 아리따움과 재산을 두고 이야기가 오갔다."], ["Stanhope's house in the close. There had been morning callers there, and people had told some truth and also some falsehood respecting the property which John Bold had left behind him.", "그 집에는 아침 손님들이 다녀갔고, 사람들은 존 볼드가 남긴 재산에 관해 얼마쯤은 사실을, 또 얼마쯤은 헛소리를 늘어놓았다."], ["By degrees the visitors went, and as the doctor went with them, and as the doctor's wife had not made her appearance, Charlotte Stanhope and her brother were left together.", "손님들이 하나둘 물러가고 박사도 그들과 함께 나가 버린 데다 박사의 부인은 아직 모습을 보이지 않았으므로, 샬럿 스탠호프와 그 동생만 남게 되었다."], ["He was sitting idly at the table, scrawling caricatures of Barchester notables, then yawning, then turning over a book or two, and evidently at a loss how to kill his time without much labour.", "그는 탁자 앞에 하릴없이 앉아 바체스터 명사들의 우스개 그림을 끼적이다가 하품을 하다가 책을 한두 권 뒤적이다가 하면서, 힘들이지 않고 시간을 죽일 방도를 못 찾아 딱한 꼴이었다."]], [["\"You haven't done much, Bertie, about getting any orders,\"", "“버티, 주문 받는 일은 별로 못 했나 보네.”"], ["said his sister.", "누나가 말했다."]], [["\"Orders!\"", "“주문이라니!”"], ["said he; \"who on earth is there at Barchester to give one orders?", "그가 말했다. “바체스터에 주문을 줄 사람이 대체 어디 있어?"], ["Who among the people here could possibly think it worth his while to have his head done into marble?\"", "여기 사람들 가운데 제 머리를 대리석으로 떠낼 값어치가 있다고 여길 자가 누가 있겠어?”"]], [["\"Then you mean to give up your profession,\"", "“그럼 네 일을 아주 접겠다는 말이네.”"], ["said she.", "누나가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I don't,\"", "“아니, 그럴 생각 없어.”"], ["said he, going on with some absurd portrait of the bishop.", "그는 주교의 우스꽝스러운 초상을 계속 그려 가며 말했다."], ["\"Look at that, Lotte; isn't it the little man all over, apron and all?", "“로테, 이것 좀 봐. 앞치마까지 그 조그만 양반 그대로지?"], ["I'd go on with my profession at once, as you call it, if the governor would set me up with a studio in London; but as to sculpture at Barchester--I suppose half the people here don't know what a torso means.\"", "아버지가 런던에 작업실이나 하나 차려 주신다면야 누나 말대로 당장이라도 일을 시작하겠어. 하지만 바체스터에서 조각이라니 — 여기 사람 절반은 토르소가 뭔지도 모를걸.”"]], [["\"The governor will not give you a shilling to start you in London,\"", "“아버지는 너를 런던에 자리 잡아 주려고 1실링도 내주지 않으실 거야.”"], ["said Lotte.", "로테가 말했다."], ["\"Indeed, he can't give you what would be sufficient, for he has not got it.", "“아니, 넉넉히 줄 만한 것을 가지고 계시지도 않으니 주실 수가 없지."], ["But you might start yourself very well, if you pleased.\"", "하지만 네가 마음만 먹으면 너 스스로 얼마든지 시작할 수 있어.”"]], [["\"How the deuce am I to do it?\"", "“대체 나더러 어떻게 하라고?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"To tell you the truth, Bertie, you'll never make a penny by any profession.\"", "“솔직히 말하면 버티, 너는 무슨 일을 하든 한 푼도 벌지 못할 거야.”"]], [["\"That's what I often think myself,\"", "“나도 자주 그렇게 생각해.”"], ["said he, not in the least offended.", "그는 조금도 언짢아하지 않고 말했다."], ["\"Some men have a great gift of making money, but they can't spend it.", "“어떤 사람은 돈 버는 재주는 크게 타고났는데 쓸 줄을 몰라."], ["Others can't put two shillings together, but they have a great talent for all sorts of outlay.", "또 어떤 사람은 2실링도 못 모으면서 온갖 씀씀이에는 대단한 재능이 있지."], ["I begin to think that my genius is wholly in the latter line.\"", "아무래도 내 재능은 죄다 뒤쪽에만 몰려 있는 것 같아.”"]], [["\"How do you mean to live then?\"", "“그럼 앞으로 어떻게 살 작정인데?”"], ["asked the sister.", "누나가 물었다."]], [["\"I suppose I must regard myself as a young raven and look for heavenly manna; besides, we have all got something when the governor goes.\"", "“나야 나를 어린 까마귀쯤으로 여기고 하늘에서 만나가 내리기를 기다려야지. 게다가 아버지가 가시고 나면 우리 모두 얼마쯤은 받을 것 아냐.”"]], [["\"Yes--you'll have enough to supply yourself with gloves and boots; that is, if the Jews have not got the possession of it all.", "“그래 — 장갑이랑 구두나 사 신을 만큼은 되겠지. 그것도 유대인들이 죄다 차지해 버리지 않았다면 말이야."], ["I believe they have the most of it already.", "내가 보기엔 벌써 태반이 그자들 손에 들어가 있어."], ["I wonder, Bertie, at your indifference; that you, with your talents and personal advantages, should never try to settle yourself in life.", "버티, 나는 네 그 태평함이 놀라워. 너 같은 재주와 인물을 가지고 어째서 한 번도 제 앞가림을 하려 들지 않는지."], ["I look forward with dread to the time when the governor must go.", "나는 아버지가 가셔야 할 때가 오는 게 무서워."], ["Mother, and Madeline, and I--we shall be poor enough, but you will have absolutely nothing.\"", "어머니하고 마들린하고 나는 가난할 대로 가난해지겠지만, 너는 그야말로 아무것도 없을 거야.”"]], [["\"Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof,\"", "“한 날의 괴로움은 그날로 족하니라.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"Will you take my advice?\"", "“내 말대로 하겠어?”"], ["said his sister.", "누나가 말했다."]], [["\"Cela dépend,\"", "“Cela dépend.”"], ["said the brother.", "동생이 말했다."]], [["\"Will you marry a wife with money?\"", "“돈 있는 여자와 혼인하겠느냐 말이야.”"]], [["\"At any rate,\" said he, \"I won't marry one without; wives with money a'nt so easy to get now-a-days; the parsons pick them all up.\"", "“적어도,” 그가 말했다. “돈 없는 여자와는 혼인하지 않을 거야. 요새는 돈 있는 아내를 얻기가 그리 쉽지 않아. 목사들이 죄다 채 가거든.”"]], [["\"And a parson will pick up the wife I mean for you, if you do not look quickly about it; the wife I mean is Mrs.", "“그리고 네가 얼른 서두르지 않으면 내가 점찍어 둔 그 아내도 목사가 채 갈 거야."], ["Bold.\"", "내가 말하는 아내는 볼드 부인이야.”"]], [["\"Whew-w-w-w!\"", "“휘이이익!”"], ["whistled Bertie, \"a widow!\"", "버티가 휘파람을 불었다. “미망인이라!”"]], [["\"She is very beautiful,\"", "“아주 아름다운 사람이야.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"With a son and heir all ready to my hand,\"", "“아들에 상속자까지 통째로 딸려서 말이지.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"A baby that will very likely die,\"", "“십중팔구 죽을 갓난아이인걸.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't see that,\"", "“그건 모를 일이지.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"But however, he may live for me--I don't wish to kill him; only, it must be owned that a ready-made family is a drawback.\"", "“하지만 어쨌든 나를 위해 살아 준다면야 — 내가 그 아이를 죽이고 싶다는 건 아니니까. 다만 다 만들어진 식구가 딸린 건 흠이라고 해야겠지.”"]], [["\"There is only one after all,\"", "“그래 봐야 하나뿐이잖아.”"], ["pleaded Charlotte.", "샬럿이 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["\"And that a very little one, as the maidservant said,\"", "“게다가 그것도 아주 조그만 하나뿐이지 — 하녀가 했다는 말마따나.”"], ["rejoined Bertie.", "버티가 받아 말했다."]], [["\"Beggars mustn't be choosers, Bertie; you can't have everything.\"", "“거지가 찬밥 더운밥 가리면 못써, 버티. 다 가질 수는 없는 법이야.”"]], [["\"God knows I am not unreasonable,\"", "“하느님도 아시지만 나는 무리한 사람도 아니고 고집쟁이도 아니야.”"], ["said he, \"nor yet opinionated, and if you'll arrange it all for me, Lotte, I'll marry the lady.", "그가 말했다. “로테 누나가 다 알아서 마련해 준다면 나는 그 부인과 혼인하겠어."], ["Only mark this: the money must be sure, and the income at my own disposal, at any rate for the lady's life.\"", "다만 이것만은 새겨 둬. 돈은 확실해야 하고, 그 수입은 적어도 그 부인이 살아 있는 동안에는 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있어야 해.”"]], [["Charlotte was explaining to her brother that he must make love for himself if he meant to carry on the matter, and was encouraging him to do so by warm eulogiums on Eleanor's beauty, when the signora was brought into the drawing-room.", "샬럿은 이 일을 밀고 나갈 생각이라면 구애는 네가 직접 해야 한다고 동생에게 일러 주면서 엘리너의 아름다움을 한껏 추어올려 그를 부추기고 있었는데, 그때 시뇨라가 응접실로 옮겨져 들어왔다."], ["When at home, and subject to the gaze of none but her own family, she allowed herself to be dragged about by two persons, and her two bearers now deposited her on her sofa.", "집에 있을 때, 그리고 제 식구 말고는 아무 눈길도 닿지 않을 때 그녀는 두 사람에게 몸을 맡겨 이리저리 옮겨 다녔거니와, 그 두 사람이 이제 그녀를 소파에 내려놓은 것이었다."], ["She was not quite so grand in her apparel as she had been at the bishop's party, but yet she was dressed with much care, and though there was a look of care and pain about her eyes, she was, even by daylight, extremely beautiful.", "옷차림은 주교관 연회 때만큼 화려하지 않았으나 그래도 매우 공들여 차려입었고, 눈가에 근심과 아픔의 기색이 어려 있는데도 그녀는 대낮에 보아도 지극히 아름다웠다."]], [["\"Well, Madeline, so I'm going to be married,\"", "“그래 마들린, 나 장가간대.”"], ["Bertie began as soon as the servants had withdrawn.", "하인들이 물러가자마자 버티가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"There's no other foolish thing left that you haven't done,\"", "“네가 아직 안 해 본 어리석은 짓이라곤 그것밖에 안 남았으니,”"], ["said Madeline, \"and therefore you are quite right to try that.\"", "마들린이 말했다. “그거라도 해 보는 게 옳지.”"]], [["\"Oh, you think it's a foolish thing, do you?\"", "“아, 누나는 그게 어리석은 짓이라고 생각하는구나?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"There's Lotte advising me to marry by all means.", "“로테 누나는 무슨 일이 있어도 혼인하라던데."], ["But on such a subject your opinion ought to be the best; you have experience to guide you.\"", "하지만 그런 문제라면 누나 의견이 제일 나을 수밖에 없지. 길잡이가 될 경험이 있으니까.”"]], [["\"Yes, I have,\"", "“그래, 있고말고.”"], ["said Madeline with a sort of harsh sadness in her tone, which seemed to say--\"What is it to you if I am sad?", "마들린이 어딘가 모질고 서글픈 어조로 말했는데, 그 어조는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. “내가 서글프든 말든 너와 무슨 상관이냐?"], ["I have never asked your sympathy.\"", "나는 네 동정을 구한 적이 없다.”"]], [["Bertie was sorry when he saw that she was hurt by what he said, and he came and squatted on the floor close before her face to make his peace with her.", "버티는 제 말에 누나가 상했다는 것을 알고 미안해져서, 다가가 그 얼굴 바로 앞 바닥에 쭈그리고 앉아 화해를 청했다."]], [["\"Come, Mad, I was only joking; you know that.", "“자, 매드, 농담이었어. 누나도 알잖아."], ["But in sober earnest, Lotte is advising me to marry.", "하지만 진담으로 말하면, 로테 누나가 나더러 혼인하라고 해."], ["She wants me to marry this Mrs. Bold.", "이 볼드 부인이라는 사람과 맺어 주려는 거야."], ["She's a widow with lots of tin, a fine baby, a beautiful complexion, and the George and Dragon hotel up in the High Street.", "돈은 잔뜩 있고, 아기는 튼튼하고, 살결은 곱고, 하이 스트리트에 조지앤드드래건 여관까지 딸린 미망인이지."], ["By Jove, Lotte, if I marry her, I'll keep the public-house myself--it's just the life to suit me.\"", "아이고 로테 누나, 내가 그 부인과 혼인하면 그 술집은 내가 직접 꾸릴 테야. 딱 내게 맞는 삶이잖아.”"]], [["\"What,\"", "“뭐라고?”"], ["said Madeline, \"that vapid, swarthy creature in the widow's cap, who looked as though her clothes had been stuck on her back with a pitchfork!\"", "마들린이 말했다. “미망인 모자를 쓴 그 밍밍하고 거무튀튀한 여자 말이니?"], ["The signora never allowed any woman to be beautiful.", "옷을 쇠스랑으로 등에 꽂아 놓은 것처럼 보이던 그 여자?” 시뇨라는 어떤 여자도 아름답다고 인정해 주는 법이 없었다."]], [["\"Instead of being vapid,\"", "“밍밍하기는커녕,” 로테가 말했다."], ["said Lotte, \"I call her a very lovely woman.", "“나는 아주 사랑스러운 여자라고 보는데."], ["She was by far the loveliest woman in the rooms the other night; that is, excepting you, Madeline.\"", "그날 밤 그 방에 있던 여자들 가운데 단연 제일 사랑스러웠어. 물론 마들린 너는 빼고.”"]], [["Even the compliment did not soften the asperity of the maimed beauty.", "그 칭찬조차 불구가 된 미인의 신랄함을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다."], ["\"Every woman is charming according to Lotte,\"", "“로테 눈에는 여자란 여자가 다 매력적이지.”"], ["she said; \"I never knew an eye with so little true appreciation.", "그녀가 말했다. “나는 그렇게 볼 줄 모르는 눈은 처음 봤어."], ["In the first place, what woman on earth could look well in such a thing as that she had on her head.\"", "우선 첫째로, 그 여자가 머리에 얹고 있던 그런 물건을 쓰고 어울릴 여자가 이 세상에 어디 있겠니.”"]], [["\"Of course she wears a widow's cap, but she'll put that off when Bertie marries her.\"", "“그야 미망인 모자를 쓰지. 하지만 버티가 혼인하면 벗을 거야.”"]], [["\"I don't see any of course in it,\"", "“거기에 그야가 어디 있다는 건지 모르겠구나.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["\"The death of twenty husbands should not make me undergo such a penance.", "“남편이 스무 번 죽는다 해도 나라면 그런 고행은 하지 않겠어."], ["It is as much a relic of paganism as the sacrifice of a Hindu woman at the burning of her husband's body.", "그건 남편의 시신을 태울 때 힌두 여자를 함께 바치는 것만큼이나 이교의 유물이야."], ["If not so bloody, it is quite as barbarous, and quite as useless.\"", "그만큼 피비린내가 나지는 않을지 몰라도 그만큼 야만스럽고 그만큼 쓸데없어.”"]], [["\"But you don't blame her for that,\" said Bertie.", "“그렇다고 누나가 그 부인을 나무랄 건 아니잖아.”"], ["\"She does it because it's the custom of the country.", "버티가 말했다. “나라의 풍습이 그러니 그렇게 하는 거지."], ["People would think ill of her if she didn't do it.\"", "안 그러면 사람들이 흉볼 테고.”"]], [["\"Exactly,\"", "“바로 그거야.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["\"She is just one of those English nonentities who would tie her head up in a bag for three months every summer, if her mother and her grandmother had tied up their heads before her.", "“그 여자는 딱 그런 영국식 껍데기들 가운데 하나야. 제 어머니와 할머니가 그렇게 했다면 여름마다 석 달씩 머리를 자루에 싸매고 다닐 사람이지."], ["It would never occur to her to think whether there was any use in submitting to such a nuisance.\"", "그런 성가신 짓에 순순히 따르는 게 무슨 소용이 있는지 따져 볼 생각조차 못 할걸.”"]], [["\"It's very hard in a country like England, for a young woman to set herself in opposition to prejudices of that sort,\"", "“영국 같은 나라에서 젊은 여자가 그런 편견에 맞서기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니야.”"], ["said the prudent Charlotte.", "조심성 있는 샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"What you mean is that it's very hard for a fool not to be a fool,\"", "“네 말은 곧 바보가 바보 아니기가 여간 어렵지 않다는 소리지.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."]], [["Bertie Stanhope had been so much knocked about the world from his earliest years that he had not retained much respect for the gravity of English customs; but even to his mind an idea presented itself that, perhaps in a wife, true British prejudice would not in the long run be less agreeable than Anglo-Italian freedom from restraint.", "버티 스탠호프는 어릴 적부터 워낙 세상을 굴러다닌 터라 영국 풍습의 근엄함 따위에는 존경심이 별로 남아 있지 않았다. 그러나 그런 그의 머리에도, 아내로 두기에는 어쩌면 진짜 영국식 편견이 영국·이탈리아 잡탕의 거리낌 없음보다 길게 보아 덜 마음에 들지는 않으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["He did not exactly say so, but he expressed the idea in another way.", "그는 그렇게 대놓고 말하지는 않고 다른 식으로 그 생각을 드러냈다."]], [["\"I fancy,\"", "“내 생각엔,”"], ["said he, \"that if I were to die, and then walk, I should think that my widow looked better in one of those caps than any other kind of head-dress.\"", "그가 말했다. “내가 죽어서 혼백이 되어 돌아다닌다면, 내 아내는 다른 어떤 머리쓰개보다 그런 모자를 쓴 편이 더 나아 보인다고 여길 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Yes--and you'd fancy also that she could do nothing better than shut herself up and cry for you, or else burn herself.", "“그래 — 그리고 아내가 방에 틀어박혀 너를 위해 우는 것보다, 아니면 제 몸을 태우는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없다고도 여기겠지."], ["But she would think differently.", "하지만 아내 생각은 다를 거야."], ["She'd probably wear one of those horrid she-helmets, because she'd want the courage not to do so; but she'd wear it with a heart longing for the time when she might be allowed to throw it off.", "그 끔찍한 계집 투구를 쓰기야 하겠지, 안 쓸 용기가 없을 테니까. 그러나 언제쯤 그것을 벗어 던져도 될까 애타게 기다리는 마음으로 쓰고 있을 거야."], ["I hate such shallow false pretences.", "나는 그런 얄팍한 거짓 시늉이 싫어."], ["For my part I would let the world say what it pleased, and show no grief if I felt none--and perhaps not, if I did.\"", "나 같으면 세상이야 뭐라 하든 내버려 두고, 슬프지 않으면 슬픈 티를 내지 않겠어 — 슬프더라도 어쩌면 내지 않을 테고.”"]], [["\"But wearing a widow's cap won't lessen her fortune,\"", "“그렇지만 미망인 모자를 쓴다고 재산이 줄지는 않잖아.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"Or increase it,\"", "“늘지도 않지.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["\"Then why on earth does she do it?\"", "“그럼 대체 왜 그걸 쓰는 건데?”"]], [["\"But Lotte's object is to make her put it off,\"", "“로테 누나 속셈이 바로 그걸 벗기자는 거잖아.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"If it be true that she has got twelve hundred a year quite at her own disposal, and she be not utterly vulgar in her manners, I would advise you to marry her.", "“정말로 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 한 해 1,200파운드를 가지고 있고 몸가짐이 아주 천하기만 하지 않다면, 나는 너더러 그 여자와 혼인하라고 하겠어."], ["I dare say she's to be had for the asking: and as you are not going to marry her for love, it doesn't much matter whether she is good-looking or not.", "청하기만 하면 넘어올 것 같기도 하고. 게다가 너는 사랑 때문에 혼인하는 게 아니니 그 여자가 반반하든 말든 크게 상관없지."], ["As to your really marrying a woman for love, I don't believe you are fool enough for that.\"", "네가 정말로 사랑 때문에 여자와 혼인하는 일로 말하자면, 나는 네가 그만큼 바보라고는 생각하지 않아.”"]], [["\"Oh, Madeline!\"", "“아니 마들린!”"], ["exclaimed her sister.", "언니가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"And oh, Charlotte!\"", "“아니 샬럿!”"], ["said the other.", "다른 쪽이 말했다."]], [["\"You don't mean to say that no man can love a woman unless he be a fool?\"", "“남자가 바보가 아니고서는 여자를 사랑할 수 없다는 말은 아니겠지?”"]], [["\"I mean very much the same thing--that any man who is willing to sacrifice his interest to get possession of a pretty face is a fool.", "“거의 그런 말이지 — 반반한 얼굴을 차지하겠다고 제 잇속을 기꺼이 내던지는 남자는 누구든 바보라는 말이야. 반반한 얼굴이야 그보다 싸게도 얻을 수 있어."], ["Pretty faces are to be had cheaper than that.", "로테, 나는 네 그 느끼한 감상이 싫어."], ["I hate your mawkish sentimentality, Lotte.", "남편과 아내가 대개 어떤 식으로 함께 사는지는 나만큼 너도 알잖아."], ["You know as well as I do in what way husbands and wives generally live together; you know how far the warmth of conjugal affection can withstand the trial of a bad dinner, of a rainy day, or of the least privation which poverty brings with it; you know what freedom a man claims for himself, what slavery he would exact from his wife if he could!", "부부의 정이라는 온기가 형편없는 저녁상 앞에서, 비 오는 날 앞에서, 가난이 몰고 오는 아주 사소한 궁핍 앞에서 얼마나 버티는지도 알잖아. 남자가 제 몫으로 어떤 자유를 요구하는지, 할 수만 있다면 아내에게 어떤 종살이를 시키려 드는지도 알잖아!"], ["And you know also how wives generally obey.", "그리고 아내들이 대개 어떻게 순종하는지도 알고."], ["Marriage means tyranny on one side and deceit on the other.", "혼인이란 한쪽의 폭정이고 다른 쪽의 속임수야."], ["I say that a man is a fool to sacrifice his interests for such a bargain.", "나는 그런 흥정에 제 잇속을 내던지는 남자는 바보라고 말하는 거야."], ["A woman, too generally, has no other way of living.\"", "여자야 대개 달리 살아갈 길이 없으니 그렇다 치고.”"]], [["\"But Bertie has no other way of living,\"", "“하지만 버티도 달리 살아갈 길이 없잖아.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"Then, in God's name, let him marry Mrs. Bold,\"", "“그렇다면 하느님 이름으로, 볼드 부인과 혼인하라고 해.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["And so it was settled between them.", "그리하여 그들 사이에서 그렇게 정해졌다."]], [["But let the gentle-hearted reader be under no apprehension whatsoever.", "그러나 마음 여린 독자여, 조금도 걱정하지 마시라."], ["It is not destined that Eleanor shall marry Mr. Slope or Bertie Stanhope.", "엘리너가 슬로프 씨나 버티 스탠호프와 혼인하도록 정해져 있지는 않다."], ["And here perhaps it may be allowed to the novelist to explain his views on a very important point in the art of telling tales.", "그리고 여기서 소설가에게, 이야기를 짓는 기술의 아주 중요한 대목에 관해 제 생각을 밝히도록 허락해 주어도 좋으리라."], ["He ventures to reprobate that system which goes so far to violate all proper confidence between the author and his readers by maintaining nearly to the end of the third volume a mystery as to the fate of their favourite personage.", "그는 셋째 권 끝머리에 이르도록 독자가 아끼는 인물의 운명을 수수께끼로 묶어 두어 글쓴이와 독자 사이의 마땅한 신의를 크게 저버리는 그 방식을 감히 나무라고자 한다."], ["Nay, more, and worse than this, is too frequently done.", "아니, 그보다 더한, 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 너무 자주 벌어진다."], ["Have not often the profoundest efforts of genius been used to baffle the aspirations of the reader, to raise false hopes and false fears, and to give rise to expectations which are never to be realized?", "독자의 바람을 어긋나게 하고, 헛된 희망과 헛된 두려움을 일으키고, 결코 이루어지지 않을 기대를 부풀리는 데에 천재의 가장 깊은 공력이 쓰인 적이 어디 한두 번이던가?"], ["Are not promises all but made of delightful horrors, in lieu of which the writer produces nothing but most commonplace realities in his final chapter?", "즐거운 무서움을 안겨 주겠다는 약속이나 다름없는 것을 해 놓고, 그 대신 마지막 장에서는 지극히 진부한 사실 말고는 아무것도 내놓지 않는 일이 어디 한두 번이던가?"], ["And is there not a species of deceit in this to which the honesty of the present age should lend no countenance?", "그리고 여기에는 이 정직한 시대가 결코 편들어 주어서는 안 될 일종의 기만이 있지 않은가?"]], [["And what can be the worth of that solicitude which a peep into the third volume can utterly dissipate?", "그리고 셋째 권을 한 번 엿보는 것만으로 말끔히 흩어져 버리는 그 조바심이 대체 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가?"], ["What the value of those literary charms which are absolutely destroyed by their enjoyment?", "누리는 순간 여지없이 부서져 버리는 그 문학적 매력이 무슨 값어치가 있겠는가?"], ["When we have once learnt what was that picture before which was hung Mrs. Ratcliffe's solemn curtain, we feel no further interest about either the frame or the veil.", "래드클리프 부인의 그 엄숙한 장막 뒤에 걸려 있던 그림이 무엇이었는지 한번 알고 나면, 우리는 그 틀에도 그 휘장에도 더는 관심이 가지 않는다."], ["They are to us merely a receptacle for old bones, an inappropriate coffin, which we would wish to have decently buried out of our sight.", "그것들은 우리에게 그저 낡은 뼈다귀를 담아 두는 그릇, 어울리지 않는 관일 뿐이어서, 눈앞에서 치워 점잖게 묻어 버렸으면 싶어진다."]], [["And then how grievous a thing it is to have the pleasure of your novel destroyed by the ill-considered triumph of a previous reader.", "그리고 먼저 읽은 사람이 경솔하게 뽐내는 바람에 제 소설의 즐거움을 잃는 일은 또 얼마나 억울한가."], ["\"Oh, you needn't be alarmed for Augusta; of course she accepts Gustavus in the end.\"", "“아, 어거스타 걱정은 안 해도 돼. 끝에 가서는 물론 구스타부스를 받아들이거든.”"], ["\"How very ill-natured you are, Susan,\"", "“수전 언니는 어쩌면 그렇게 심술궂어요.”"], ["says Kitty with tears in her eyes: \"I don't care a bit about it now.\"", "키티가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해서 말한다. “이제 하나도 재미가 없잖아요.”"], ["Dear Kitty, if you will read my book, you may defy the ill-nature of your sister.", "가엾은 키티여, 내 책을 읽는다면 그대는 언니의 심술 따위를 두려워하지 않아도 된다. 언니가 일러 줄 만한 비밀이라곤 하나도 없을 테니."], ["There shall be no secret that she can tell you.", "아니, 마음이 내키거든 셋째 권을 집어 들어도 좋다."], ["Nay, take the third volume if you please--learn from the last pages all the results of our troubled story, and the story shall have lost none of its interest, if indeed there be any interest in it to lose.", "마지막 몇 쪽에서 이 파란 많은 이야기의 결말을 죄다 알아내도 좋다. 그래도 이야기는 그 재미를 조금도 잃지 않을 것이다 — 잃을 만한 재미가 조금이라도 있다면 말이지만."]], [["Our doctrine is that the author and the reader should move along together in full confidence with each other.", "우리가 내세우는 바는 글쓴이와 독자가 서로를 온전히 믿으며 나란히 나아가야 한다는 것이다."], ["Let the personages of the drama undergo ever so complete a comedy of errors among themselves, but let the spectator never mistake the Syracusan for the Ephesian; otherwise he is one of the dupes, and the part of a dupe is never dignified.", "극중 인물들이야 저희끼리 아무리 완벽한 착각의 희극을 겪어도 좋으나, 구경하는 이만은 시라쿠사 사람을 에페소스 사람으로 잘못 보는 일이 없어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 그도 속아 넘어간 자들 가운데 하나가 되는데, 속아 넘어간 자의 배역이란 결코 품위 있는 것이 못 된다."]], [["I would not for the value of this chapter have it believed by a single reader that my Eleanor could bring herself to marry Mr. Slope, or that she should be sacrificed to a Bertie Stanhope.", "나는 이 한 장의 값어치를 다 준다 해도, 내 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 마음을 먹을 수 있다거나 버티 스탠호프 따위에게 제물로 바쳐지리라고 단 한 사람의 독자에게라도 믿게 하고 싶지 않다."], ["But among the good folk of Barchester many believed both the one and the other.", "그러나 바체스터의 선량한 사람들 가운데에는 이쪽도 저쪽도 다 그렇게 되리라 믿는 이가 많았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. Baby Worship", "# 제16장. 아기 숭배"]], [["\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum,\"", "“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥.”"], ["said or sung Eleanor Bold.", "엘리너 볼드가 그렇게 말했다, 아니 노래했다."]], [["\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum,\"", "“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥.”"], ["continued Mary Bold, taking up the second part in this concerted piece.", "메리 볼드가 이 합주의 제2성부를 맡아 뒤를 이었다."]], [["The only audience at the concert was the baby, who however gave such vociferous applause that the performers, presuming it to amount to an encore, commenced again.", "이 연주회의 관객은 아기 하나뿐이었으나, 그 관객이 어찌나 우렁차게 갈채를 보냈던지 연주자들은 그것을 앙코르 요청으로 알아듣고 다시 시작했다."]], [["\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle, dum, dum, dum: hasn't he got lovely legs?\"", "“둥가 둥가 둥가 둥가, 둥 둥 둥. 얘 다리 좀 봐요, 어쩌면 이렇게 예뻐요?”"], ["said the rapturous mother.", "황홀경에 빠진 어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"H'm 'm 'm 'm 'm,\"", "“음 음 음 음 음.”"], ["simmered Mary, burying her lips in the little fellow's fat neck, by way of kissing him.", "메리가 입술을 이 조그만 녀석의 통통한 목덜미에 파묻으며, 그것을 입맞춤 삼아 부글부글 소리를 냈다."]], [["\"H'm 'm 'm 'm 'm,\"", "“음 음 음 음 음.”"], ["simmered the mamma, burying her lips also in his fat, round, short legs.", "엄마도 부글거리며 이번에는 아기의 통통하고 동그랗고 짤막한 다리에 입술을 파묻었다."], ["\"He's a dawty little bold darling, so he is; and he has the nicest little pink legs in all the world, so he has;\"", "“우리 애기는 쪼꼬만 볼드네 귀염둥이지, 그럼 그렇고말고. 온 세상에서 제일 예쁜 쪼꼬만 분홍 다리를 가졌지, 그럼 그렇고말고.”"], ["and the simmering and the kissing went on over again, as though the ladies were very hungry and determined to eat him.", "그러고는 부글거림과 입맞춤이 처음부터 다시 시작되었으니, 두 여인은 마치 몹시 배가 고파서 아기를 잡아먹기로 작정한 사람들 같았다."]], [["\"Well, then, he's his own mother's own darling: well, he shall--oh, oh--Mary, Mary--did you ever see?", "“오냐, 그래, 우리 아기는 제 엄마만의 귀염둥이지. 오냐, 그럼 우리 아기는…"], ["What am I to do?", "어머, 어머…"], ["My naughty, naughty, naughty, naughty little Johnny.\"", "메리, 메리, 저것 좀 봐요. 이걸 어쩌면 좋아요?"], ["All these energetic exclamations were elicited by the delight of the mother in finding that her son was strong enough and mischievous enough to pull all her hair out from under her cap.", "우리 못된, 못된, 못된, 못된 쪼꼬만 조니.” 이 기운찬 감탄사들은 모두, 아들이 제 어미의 머리카락을 모자 밑에서 죄다 끄집어낼 만큼 힘도 세고 장난도 심하다는 것을 알아차린 어미의 기쁨에서 터져 나온 것이었다."], ["\"He's been and pulled down all Mamma's hair, and he's the naughtiest, naughtiest, naughtiest little man that ever, ever, ever, ever, ever--\"", "“엄마 머리를 죄다 끌어내렸네, 이 녀석이. 이 세상에 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이, 다시없이 못되고, 못되고, 못된 쪼꼬만 사내로구나…”"]], [["A regular service of baby worship was going on.", "정식으로 아기 숭배 예배가 진행되고 있었다."], ["Mary Bold was sitting on a low easy chair, with the boy in her lap, and Eleanor was kneeling before the object of her idolatry.", "메리 볼드는 등받이가 낮은 안락의자에 앉아 사내아이를 무릎에 올려놓고 있었고, 엘리너는 제 우상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다."], ["As she tried to cover up the little fellow's face with her long, glossy, dark brown locks, and permitted him to pull them hither and thither as he would, she looked very beautiful in spite of the widow's cap which she still wore.", "그녀가 길고 윤이 나는 짙은 갈색 머리채로 이 조그만 녀석의 얼굴을 덮으려 하면서 아기가 제멋대로 그 머리채를 이리저리 잡아당기도록 내버려 두고 있는 모습은, 아직도 쓰고 있는 미망인의 모자에도 불구하고 무척 아름다웠다."], ["There was a quiet, enduring, grateful sweetness about her face which grew so strongly upon those who knew her, as to make the great praise of her beauty which came from her old friends appear marvellously exaggerated to those who were only slightly acquainted with her.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 조용하고 한결같고 감사할 줄 아는 다정함이 서려 있었는데, 그것이 그녀를 아는 사람들에게는 하도 강하게 자라나는 까닭에, 오랜 벗들의 입에서 나오는 그 대단한 미모 예찬이 그녀와 겨우 안면만 튼 사람들에게는 놀랍도록 과장된 말로 들릴 지경이었다."], ["Her loveliness was like that of many landscapes, which require to be often seen to be fully enjoyed.", "그녀의 아름다움은 여러 풍경의 아름다움과 같아서, 자주 보아야만 온전히 누릴 수 있었다."], ["There was a depth of dark clear brightness in her eyes which was lost upon a quick observer, a character about her mouth which only showed itself to those with whom she familiarly conversed, a glorious form of head the perfect symmetry of which required the eye of an artist for its appreciation.", "그녀의 눈에는 성급한 관찰자는 놓치고 마는 어둡고 맑은 광채의 깊이가 있었고, 그녀의 입가에는 허물없이 이야기를 나누는 사람에게만 드러나는 표정이 있었으며, 그 머리의 훌륭한 생김새는 완벽한 균형을 알아보는 데 예술가의 눈을 필요로 할 정도였다."], ["She had none of that dazzling brilliancy, of that voluptuous Rubens beauty, of that pearly whiteness, and those vermilion tints which immediately entranced with the power of a basilisk men who came within reach of Madeline Neroni.", "마들린 네로니에게 다가간 남자들을 바실리스크의 힘으로 단번에 홀려 버리는 그 눈부신 광휘, 그 관능적인 루벤스풍의 미모, 그 진주 같은 흰빛과 주홍빛 색조는 그녀에게 하나도 없었다."], ["It was all but impossible to resist the signora, but no one was called upon for any resistance towards Eleanor.", "시뇨라에게는 도저히 저항할 수 없었으나, 엘리너에게는 누구도 저항 따위를 요구받지 않았다."], ["You might begin to talk to her as though she were your sister, and it would not be till your head was on your pillow that the truth and intensity of her beauty would flash upon you, that the sweetness of her voice would come upon your ear.", "그녀에게는 마치 제 누이에게 말을 걸듯 이야기를 시작해도 좋았고, 그러고는 베개에 머리를 누이고 나서야 비로소 그 아름다움의 진실함과 강렬함이 문득 떠오르고 그 목소리의 감미로움이 귀에 되살아나는 것이었다."], ["A sudden half-hour with the Neroni was like falling into a pit, an evening spent with Eleanor like an unexpected ramble in some quiet fields of asphodel.", "네로니와 보내는 갑작스러운 30분은 구덩이에 빠지는 일 같았고, 엘리너와 보내는 저녁 한때는 아스포델이 핀 고요한 들판을 뜻하지 않게 거니는 일 같았다."]], [["\"We'll cover him up till there shan't be a morsel of his little 'ittle 'ittle 'ittle nose to be seen,\"", "“우리 아기를 폭 덮어 버려서 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만, 쪼꼬만 코가 손톱만큼도 안 보이게 해 줄 테야.”"], ["said the mother, stretching her streaming locks over the infant's face.", "어머니가 흘러내리는 머리채를 아기의 얼굴 위로 펼치며 말했다."], ["The child screamed with delight, and kicked till Mary Bold was hardly able to hold him.", "아기는 좋아서 소리를 질러 대며 발버둥을 쳤고, 메리 볼드는 아기를 붙들고 있기가 벅찰 지경이었다."]], [["At this moment the door opened, and Mr. Slope was announced.", "바로 그때 문이 열리며 슬로프 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 있었다."], ["Up jumped Eleanor and, with a sudden quick motion of her hands, pushed back her hair over her shoulders.", "엘리너는 벌떡 일어나 손을 재빨리 놀려 머리를 어깨 뒤로 넘겼다."], ["It would have been perhaps better for her that she had not, for she thus showed more of her confusion than she would have done had she remained as she was.", "아마도 그러지 않는 편이 그녀에게는 나았을 것이다. 그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 가만히 있었을 때보다 오히려 당황한 기색을 더 많이 드러냈기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Slope, however, immediately recognized her loveliness and thought to himself that, irrespective of her fortune, she would be an inmate that a man might well desire for his house, a partner for his bosom's care very well qualified to make care lie easy.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 그 자리에서 그녀의 아름다움을 알아보고, 재산을 떠나서도 저 여자라면 한 남자가 제 집에 들이고 싶어 할 만한 식구요, 가슴속 근심을 나눌 반려로서 그 근심을 가볍게 만들어 줄 자격이 충분하다고 속으로 생각했다."], ["Eleanor hurried out of the room to readjust her cap, muttering some unnecessary apology about her baby.", "엘리너는 아기를 핑계 삼아 필요도 없는 변명을 웅얼거리며 모자를 고쳐 쓰러 방을 나갔다."], ["And while she is gone, we will briefly go back and state what had been hitherto the results of Mr. Slope's meditations on his scheme of matrimony.", "그녀가 나가 있는 동안, 우리는 잠시 뒤로 돌아가 혼인 계획에 관한 슬로프 씨의 궁리가 그때까지 어떤 결실을 맺었는지 간략히 밝혀 두기로 하자."]], [["His inquiries as to the widow's income had at any rate been so far successful as to induce him to determine to go on with the speculation. As regarded Mr.", "미망인의 수입에 관한 그의 탐문은 적어도 이 투기를 계속 밀고 나가기로 결심하게 할 만큼은 성공을 거두었다."], ["Harding, he had also resolved to do what he could without injury to himself. To Mrs.", "하딩 씨에 관해서도 그는 제 몸에 해가 되지 않는 한도에서 할 수 있는 일은 하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Proudie he determined not to speak on the matter, at least not at present. His object was to instigate a little rebellion on the part of the bishop.", "프라우디 부인에게는 이 문제를 말하지 않기로, 적어도 당분간은 말하지 않기로 작정했다."], ["He thought that such a state of things would be advisable, not only in respect to Messrs.", "그의 목적은 주교 편에서 작은 반란을 부추기는 데 있었다."], ["Harding and Quiverful, but also in the affairs of the diocese generally. Mr.", "그는 그런 형세가 바람직하다고 보았는데, 하딩 씨와 퀴버풀 씨 문제에 관해서만이 아니라 교구의 사무 전반에 관해서도 그러했다."], ["Slope was by no means of opinion that Dr. Proudie was fit to rule, but he conscientiously thought it wrong that his brother clergy should be subjected to petticoat government.", "슬로프 씨는 프라우디 박사가 다스릴 만한 인물이라고는 결코 생각하지 않았으나, 동료 성직자들이 치마폭 정치에 매여 지내는 것은 양심에 비추어 그르다고 여겼다."], ["He therefore made up his mind to infuse a little of his spirit into the bishop, sufficient to induce him to oppose his wife, though not enough to make him altogether insubordinate.", "그리하여 그는 주교에게 제 기백을 조금 불어넣되, 아내에게 맞설 만큼은 되고 아주 통제를 벗어날 만큼은 되지 않게 하기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["He had therefore taken an opportunity of again speaking to his lordship about the hospital, and had endeavoured to make it appear that after all it would be unwise to exclude Mr. Harding from the appointment. Mr. Slope, however, had a harder task than he had imagined. Mrs. Proudie, anxious to assume to herself as much as possible of the merit of patronage, had written to Mrs. Quiverful, requesting her to call at the palace, and had then explained to that matron, with much mystery, condescension, and dignity, the good that was in store for her and her progeny. Indeed, Mrs. Proudie had been so engaged at the very time that Mr. Slope had been doing the same with the husband at Puddingdale Vicarage, and had thus in a measure committed herself. The thanks, the humility, the gratitude, the surprise of Mrs. Quiverful had been very overpowering; she had all but embraced the knees of her patroness, and had promised that the prayers of fourteen unprovided babes (so Mrs. Quiverful had described her own family, the eldest of which was a stout young woman of three-and-twenty) should be put up to heaven morning and evening for the munificent friend whom God had sent to them. Such incense as this was not unpleasing to Mrs. Proudie, and she made the most of it. She offered her general assistance to the fourteen unprovided babes, if, as she had no doubt, she should find them worthy; expressed a hope that the eldest of them would be fit to undertake tuition in her Sabbath-schools; and altogether made herself a very great lady in the estimation of Mrs. Quiverful.", "그래서 그는 기회를 보아 주교에게 요양원 이야기를 다시 꺼내어, 결국 하딩 씨를 이 자리에서 배제하는 것은 현명하지 못한 일이라는 쪽으로 몰아가려 애썼다. 그러나 슬로프 씨의 일은 그가 짐작했던 것보다 힘들었다. 프라우디 부인은 이 성직 수여의 공을 되도록 제 몫으로 돌리려는 마음에 퀴버풀 부인에게 편지를 보내 주교관으로 찾아오라 했고, 그러고는 그 부인에게 자못 은밀하고 겸손을 갖춘 위엄 어린 태도로 그녀와 그 자식들에게 어떤 복이 예비되어 있는지 설명해 주었다. 실은 프라우디 부인이 그 일을 하고 있던 바로 그때에 슬로프 씨는 푸딩데일 목사관에서 그 남편에게 같은 일을 하고 있었으니, 부인은 그렇게 해서 어느 정도 발을 담근 셈이었다. 퀴버풀 부인의 사례와 겸손과 감사와 놀라움은 실로 압도적이었다. 그녀는 은인의 무릎을 끌어안다시피 하며, 하느님이 보내 주신 후한 벗을 위해 앞길이 막막한 열넷 젖먹이들(퀴버풀 부인은 제 식구를 그렇게 말했는데, 그중 맏이는 스물세 살 난 튼실한 처녀였다)의 기도를 아침저녁으로 하늘에 올리겠노라 약속했다. 이만한 향불은 프라우디 부인에게 싫지 않았고, 부인은 그것을 한껏 누렸다. 그녀는 앞길이 막막한 그 열넷 젖먹이들이 — 물론 그러리라 믿어 의심치 않았지만 — 자격만 갖추었다면 두루 도와주겠노라 했고, 그중 맏이가 제 안식일 학교에서 가르치는 일을 맡을 만하기를 바란다고 말했으며, 요컨대 퀴버풀 부인이 보기에 스스로를 아주 대단한 귀부인으로 만들어 놓았다."]], [["Having done this, she thought it prudent to drop a few words before the bishop, letting him know that she had acquainted the Puddingdale family with their good fortune; so that he might perceive that he stood committed to the appointment.", "이 일을 마치고 나자 그녀는 주교 앞에서 몇 마디 흘려 두는 편이 신중하겠다고 생각하여, 푸딩데일 식구들에게 그 경사를 알려 주었다는 사실을 남편이 알게 했다. 그리하여 그가 스스로 이 임명에 발이 묶였음을 깨닫게 하려는 것이었다."], ["The husband well understood the ruse of his wife, but he did not resent it.", "남편은 아내의 술책을 훤히 알아보았으나 분해하지는 않았다."], ["He knew that she was taking the patronage out of his hands; he was resolved to put an end to her interference and reassume his powers.", "그는 아내가 성직 수여권을 제 손에서 빼앗아 가고 있음을 알았고, 그 간섭을 끝장내고 제 권한을 되찾겠다고 결심하고 있었다."], ["But then he thought this was not the best time to do it.", "그러나 그러기에는 지금이 가장 좋은 때가 아니라고 생각했다."], ["He put off the evil hour, as many a man in similar circumstances has done before him.", "그는 비슷한 처지에 놓였던 숱한 남자들이 앞서 그랬듯이 그 고약한 시간을 뒤로 미루었다."]], [["Such having been the case, Mr. Slope naturally encountered a difficulty in talking over the bishop, a difficulty indeed which he found could not be overcome except at the cost of a general outbreak at the palace.", "사정이 이러했으므로 슬로프 씨는 주교를 구슬려 넘기는 데 자연히 어려움을 만났으니, 실로 주교관 전체가 한바탕 뒤집히는 값을 치르지 않고서는 넘어설 수 없는 어려움이었다."], ["A general outbreak at the present moment might be good policy, but it also might not.", "지금 이 시점에서 주교관을 한바탕 뒤집는 것이 좋은 방책일 수도 있었으나 아닐 수도 있었다."], ["It was at any rate not a step to be lightly taken.", "어느 쪽이든 가볍게 내디딜 걸음은 아니었다."], ["He began by whispering to the bishop that he feared that public opinion would be against him if Mr. Harding did not reappear at the hospital.", "그는 하딩 씨가 요양원에 다시 나타나지 않으면 여론이 주교님께 불리하게 돌아설까 두렵다고 귀엣말을 하는 것으로 말문을 열었다."], ["The bishop answered with some warmth that Mr. Quiverful had been promised the appointment on Mr.", "주교는 다소 열을 내어, 퀴버풀 씨에게 그 자리를 약속한 것은 슬로프 씨가 권해서였다고 대답했다."], ["Slope's advice. \"Not promised?\"", "“약속까지 하신 것은 아니겠지요?”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Yes, promised,\" replied the bishop, \"and Mrs.", "“아니오, 약속했소.”"], ["Proudie has seen Mrs.", "주교가 대꾸했다."], ["Quiverful on the subject.\" This was quite unexpected on the part of Mr.", "“게다가 프라우디 부인이 그 일로 퀴버풀 부인을 만났소.”"], ["Slope, but his presence of mind did not fail him, and he turned the statement to his own account.", "이는 슬로프 씨로서는 전혀 뜻밖이었으나, 그의 침착함은 무너지지 않았고 그는 그 말을 제 잇속에 맞게 돌려놓았다."]], [["\"Ah, my lord,\"", "“아, 주교님.”"], ["said he, \"we shall all be in scrapes if the ladies interfere.\"", "그가 말했다. “부인들께서 끼어드시면 우리 모두 곤경에 빠지고 맙니다.”"]], [["This was too much in unison with my lord's feelings to be altogether unpalatable, and yet such an allusion to interference demanded a rebuke.", "이 말은 주교님의 심정과 너무나 잘 맞아떨어져 아주 못마땅하다고만은 할 수 없었으나, 그렇게 간섭을 들먹이는 것은 마땅히 나무랄 일이었다."], ["My lord was somewhat astounded also, though not altogether made miserable, by finding that there was a point of difference between his wife and his chaplain.", "또한 주교님은 아내와 전속 사제 사이에 의견이 갈리는 지점이 있음을 알고 다소 놀랐는데, 그렇다고 아주 언짢은 것만은 아니었다."]], [["\"I don't know what you mean by interference,\"", "“간섭이라니 무슨 말인지 모르겠소.”"], ["said the bishop mildly. \"When Mrs.", "주교가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["Proudie heard that Mr. Quiverful was to be appointed, it was not unnatural that she should wish to see Mrs.", "“퀴버풀 씨가 임명되리라는 말을 듣고 프라우디 부인이 학교 일로 퀴버풀 부인을 만나 보고 싶어 한 것이야 이상할 게 없지 않소."], ["Quiverful about the schools. I really cannot say that I see any interference.\"", "나로서는 도무지 간섭이라 할 것이 눈에 띄지 않는구려.”"]], [["\"I only speak, my lord, for your own comfort,\"", "“저는 오직 주교님의 평안을 위해 말씀드릴 뿐입니다.”"], ["said Slope; \"for your own comfort and dignity in the diocese.", "슬로프가 말했다. “교구 안에서 주교님의 평안과 위엄을 위해서지요."], ["I can have no other motive.", "다른 뜻은 있을 수 없습니다."], ["As far as personal feelings go, Mrs. Proudie is the best friend I have.", "개인적인 정으로 말하자면 프라우디 부인께서는 제게 더없이 좋은 벗이십니다."], ["I must always remember that.", "그 점은 늘 잊지 말아야지요."], ["But still, in my present position, my first duty is to your lordship.\"", "그러나 그렇다 해도 지금 제 자리에서 첫째가는 의무는 주교님께 있습니다.”"]], [["\"I'm sure of that, Mr.", "“그 점은 나도 아오, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope; I am quite sure of that;\"", "나도 잘 알고 있소.” 주교가 누그러져 말했다."], ["said the bishop, mollified: \"and you really think that Mr. Harding should have the hospital?\"", "“그래, 정말로 하딩 씨가 요양원을 맡아야 한다고 생각하오?”"]], [["\"Upon my word, I'm inclined to think so. I am quite prepared to take upon myself the blame of first suggesting Mr.", "“솔직히 말씀드려 저는 그런 쪽으로 기울고 있습니다."], ["Quiverful's name. But since doing so, I have found that there is so strong a feeling in the diocese in favour of Mr.", "퀴버풀 씨의 이름을 처음 꺼낸 허물은 얼마든지 제가 지겠습니다."], ["Harding that I think your lordship should give way. I hear also that Mr.", "그러나 그 뒤로 살펴보니 교구 안에 하딩 씨를 지지하는 정서가 어찌나 강한지, 주교님께서 양보하시는 편이 좋겠다고 생각합니다."], ["Harding has modified the objections he first felt to your lordship's propositions. And as to what has passed between Mrs.", "또 하딩 씨가 처음에 주교님의 제안에 대해 품었던 반대를 누그러뜨렸다는 말도 들었습니다."], ["Proudie and Mrs. Quiverful, the circumstance may be a little inconvenient, but I really do not think that that should weigh in a matter of so much moment.\"", "프라우디 부인과 퀴버풀 부인 사이에 오간 일로 말하자면, 사정이 다소 거북하기는 하겠으나 이만큼 중대한 문제에서 그것이 무게를 지녀서는 안 된다고 저는 정말로 생각합니다.”"]], [["And thus the poor bishop was left in a dreadfully undecided step as to what he should do. His mind, however, slightly inclined itself to the appointment of Mr. Harding, seeing that by such a step he should have the assistance of Mr. Slope in opposing Mrs. Proudie.", "그리하여 가엾은 주교는 어떻게 해야 좋을지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못한 채로 남겨졌다. 그러나 그의 마음은 하딩 씨를 임명하는 쪽으로 조금 기울었으니, 그렇게 하면 프라우디 부인에게 맞서는 데 슬로프 씨의 도움을 얻으리라는 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다."]], [["Such was the state of affairs at the palace, when Mr. Slope called at Mrs. Bold's house and found her playing with her baby. When she ran out of the room, Mr. Slope began praising the weather to Mary Bold, then he praised the baby and kissed him, and then he praised the mother, and then he praised Miss Bold herself. Mrs. Bold, however, was not long before she came back.", "슬로프 씨가 볼드 부인의 집을 찾아가 그녀가 아기와 놀고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 주교관의 형편은 그러했다. 그녀가 방에서 뛰어나가자 슬로프 씨는 메리 볼드에게 날씨를 칭찬하기 시작했고, 다음에는 아기를 칭찬하며 입을 맞추었고, 그다음에는 어머니를 칭찬했고, 그다음에는 볼드 양 본인을 칭찬했다. 그러나 볼드 부인은 오래지 않아 돌아왔다."]], [["\"I have to apologize for calling at so very early an hour,\"", "“이렇게 이른 시각에 찾아온 것을 사과드려야겠어요.” 슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다."], ["began Mr. Slope, \"but I was really so anxious to speak to you that I hope you and Miss Bold will excuse me.\"", "“꼭 뵙고 말씀드리고 싶은 일이 있어 마음이 급했던 것이니, 부인과 볼드 양께서 부디 너그러이 보아주셨으면 해요.”"]], [["Eleanor muttered something in which the words \"certainly,\" and \"of course,\"", "엘리너는 “그럼요”, “물론이지요”, “조금도 이르지 않아요”"], ["and \"not early at all,\" were just audible, and then apologized for her own appearance, declaring, with a smile, that her baby was becoming such a big boy that he was quite unmanageable.", "하는 말이 겨우 알아들릴 만큼 무어라 웅얼거렸고, 그러고는 제 매무새에 대해 사과하며 웃는 얼굴로 아기가 어느새 이렇게 큰 사내가 되어 도무지 감당이 안 된다고 말했다."]], [["\"He's a great big naughty boy,\"", "“이 녀석은 다 큰 개구쟁이랍니다.” 그녀가 아이에게 말했다."], ["said she to the child, \"and we must send him away to a great big rough romping school, where they have great big rods and do terrible things to naughty boys who don't do what their own mammas tell them;\"", "“그러니 크고 거칠고 시끄러운 학교로 멀리 보내 버려야겠어요. 거기에는 커다란 회초리가 있어서, 제 엄마 말을 안 듣는 못된 아이들한테 무시무시한 일을 한다지요.”"], ["and she then commenced another course of kissing, being actuated thereto by the terrible idea of sending her child away which her own imagination had depicted.", "그러고는 다시 한바탕 입맞춤을 퍼붓기 시작했으니, 제 아이를 멀리 보낸다는 무서운 생각을 스스로의 상상이 그려 보인 탓이었다."]], [["\"And where the masters don't have such beautiful long hair to be dishevelled,\" said Mr.", "“게다가 그곳 선생들은 헝클어뜨릴 만한 그리 아름다운 긴 머리도 없지요.”"], ["Slope, taking up the joke and paying a compliment at the same time.", "슬로프 씨가 농담을 받으면서 동시에 찬사를 보냈다."]], [["Eleanor thought he might as well have left the compliment alone, but she said nothing and looked nothing, being occupied as she was with the baby.", "엘리너는 그런 찬사는 그만두는 편이 나았으리라고 생각했으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았고 아무런 내색도 하지 않았으니, 마침 아기에게 매여 있던 참이었다."]], [["\"Let me take him,\"", "“내가 안을게요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["\"His clothes are nearly off his back with his romping,\"", "“이렇게 뒹굴었으니 옷이 다 벗겨지겠어요.”"], ["and so saying she left the room with the child.", "그러면서 그녀는 아이를 안고 방을 나갔다."], ["Miss Bold had heard Mr. Slope say he had something pressing to say to Eleanor, and thinking that she might be de trop, took this opportunity of getting herself out of the room.", "볼드 양은 슬로프 씨가 엘리너에게 긴히 할 말이 있다고 하는 것을 들었던 터라, 제가 있으면 거추장스럽겠다고 생각하고 이 기회에 방에서 빠져나간 것이었다."]], [["\"Don't be long, Mary,\"", "“오래 걸리지 말아요, 메리.”"], ["said Eleanor as Miss Bold shut the door.", "볼드 양이 문을 닫는데 엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"I am glad, Mrs. Bold, to have the opportunity of having ten minutes'", "“볼드 부인, 이렇게 단둘이 10분쯤 이야기 나눌 기회를 얻어 기쁩니다.”"], ["conversation with you alone,\" began Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말문을 열었다."], ["Slope. \"Will you let me openly ask you a plain question?\"", "“솔직하게 한 가지만 여쭈어도 될까요?”"]], [["\"Certainly,\"", "“그러세요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"And I am sure you will give me a plain and open answer.\"", "“그리고 부인께서 솔직하고 숨김없는 대답을 주시리라 믿어요.”"]], [["\"Either that, or none at all,\"", "“그렇게 하든가, 아예 대답을 안 하든가겠지요.”"], ["said she, laughing.", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"My question is this, Mrs.", "“제 물음은 이것입니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold: is your father really anxious to go back to the hospital?\"", "아버님께서는 정말로 요양원에 돌아가기를 간절히 바라고 계신가요?”"]], [["\"Why do you ask me?\"", "“어째서 제게 물으시죠?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Why don't you ask himself?\"", "“어째서 아버지께 직접 여쭙지 않으시나요?”"]], [["\"My dear Mrs. Bold, I'll tell you why.", "“볼드 부인, 그 까닭을 말씀드리지요."], ["There are wheels within wheels, all of which I would explain to you, only I fear that there is not time.", "바퀴 안에 또 바퀴가 있는 격이라 하나하나 다 설명해 드리고 싶지만, 시간이 없을까 걱정입니다. 저로서는 이 물음에 대한 답을 꼭 얻어야 해요."], ["It is essentially necessary that I should have an answer to this question, otherwise I cannot know how to advance your father's wishes; and it is quite impossible that I should ask himself.", "그러지 않고서는 아버님의 뜻을 어떻게 밀어 드려야 할지 알 수 없으니까요. 그런데 아버님께 직접 여쭙는 것은 도무지 될 일이 아닙니다."], ["No one can esteem your father more than I do, but I doubt if this feeling is reciprocal.\"", "저만큼 아버님을 우러르는 사람도 없습니다만, 그 마음이 서로 오가는 것인지는 의심스럽군요.”"], ["It certainly was not.", "과연 그렇지 않았다."], ["\"I must be candid with you as the only means of avoiding ultimate consequences, which may be most injurious to Mr. Harding.", "“하딩 씨께 몹시 해로울 결말을 피하자면 부인께 숨김없이 털어놓는 수밖에 없어요."], ["I fear there is a feeling--I will not even call it a prejudice--with regard to myself in Barchester, which is not in my favour.", "바체스터에는 저를 두고 어떤 감정이 — 편견이라고까지는 하지 않겠습니다만 — 있어서 제게 이롭지 않은 듯합니다."], ["You remember that sermon--\"", "그 설교를 기억하시겠지요…”"]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Slope, we need not go back to that,\"", "“어머, 슬로프 씨, 그 이야기까지 되짚을 것은 없어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"For one moment, Mrs.", "“잠깐만이면 됩니다, 볼드 부인. 제 이야기를 하려는 것이 아니라, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 부인께서 아셔야 하기에 드리는 말씀이에요."], ["Bold.", "그 설교는 분별없는 짓이었을지도 모릅니다."], ["It is not that I may talk of myself, but because it is so essential that you should understand how matters stand.", "오해를 산 것만은 분명하지요. 그러나 그 일에 대해서는 지금 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요."], ["That sermon may have been ill-judged--it was certainly misunderstood; but I will say nothing about that now; only this, that it did give rise to a feeling against myself which your father shares with others.", "다만 이것만은 말씀드리지요. 그 일로 저를 향한 감정이 생겨났고, 아버님께서도 다른 이들과 더불어 그 감정을 지니고 계시다는 것입니다."], ["It may be that he has proper cause, but the result is that he is not inclined to meet me on friendly terms.", "그럴 만한 까닭이 아버님께 있을지도 모르지요. 그러나 결과적으로 아버님은 저와 우호적으로 마주 앉을 마음이 없으십니다."], ["I put it to yourself whether you do not know this to be the case.\"", "부인께서는 사정이 그렇다는 것을 모르고 계시느냐고, 저는 부인 자신께 여쭙고 싶군요.”"]], [["Eleanor made no answer, and Mr. Slope, in the eagerness of his address, edged his chair a little nearer to the widow's seat, unperceived by her.", "엘리너는 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 슬로프 씨는 말에 열이 오른 나머지 제 의자를 미망인의 자리 쪽으로 조금 끌어당겼는데, 그녀는 알아채지 못했다."]], [["\"Such being so,\" continued Mr.", "“사정이 그러하니,”"], ["Slope, \"I cannot ask him this question as I can ask it of you.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “부인께 여쭙듯이 아버님께 이 물음을 여쭐 수는 없습니다."], ["In spite of my delinquencies since I came to Barchester you have allowed me to regard you as a friend.\"", "제가 바체스터에 온 뒤로 저지른 잘못에도 불구하고 부인께서는 제가 부인을 벗으로 여기도록 허락해 주셨지요.”"], ["Eleanor made a little motion with her head which was hardly confirmatory, but Mr. Slope if he noticed it, did not appear to do so.", "엘리너는 고개를 조금 움직였는데 그것이 그 말을 인정하는 몸짓이라고 보기는 어려웠으나, 슬로프 씨는 설령 알아챘더라도 알아챈 기색을 보이지 않았다."], ["\"To you I can speak openly and explain the feelings of my heart.", "“부인께는 숨김없이 말씀드릴 수 있고 제 마음속을 풀어 보일 수 있어요."], ["This your father would not allow.", "아버님께서는 그것을 허락하지 않으시겠지요."], ["Unfortunately, the bishop has thought it right that this matter of the hospital should pass through my hands.", "공교롭게도 주교님께서는 이 요양원 문제가 제 손을 거쳐 가는 것이 옳다고 여기셨습니다."], ["There have been some details to get up with which he would not trouble himself, and thus it has come to pass that I was forced to have an interview with your father on the matter.\"", "손수 수고하고 싶어 하지 않으시는 세세한 일들이 있었던 까닭이지요. 그래서 저는 어쩔 수 없이 이 일로 아버님과 마주 앉아야 했던 것입니다.”"]], [["\"I am aware of that,\"", "“그건 저도 알고 있어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"Of course,\" said he.", "“물론 그러시겠지요.”"], ["\"In that interview Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Harding left the impression on my mind that he did not wish to return to the hospital.\"", "“그 자리에서 하딩 씨는 요양원에 돌아가고 싶어 하지 않으신다는 인상을 제게 남기셨습니다.”"]], [["\"How could that be?\"", "“어떻게 그럴 수가 있죠?”"], ["said Eleanor, at last stirred up to forget the cold propriety of demeanour which she had determined to maintain.", "엘리너가 말했다. 마침내 마음이 움직여, 지키기로 작정했던 차가운 예의범절을 잊어버린 것이었다."]], [["\"My dear Mrs. Bold, I give you my word that such was the case,\"", "“볼드 부인, 사정이 그러했다는 것은 제가 맹세할 수 있어요.”"], ["said he, again getting a little nearer to her.", "그가 말하며 다시 조금 그녀 가까이 다가앉았다."], ["\"And what is more than that, before my interview with Mr. Harding, certain persons at the palace--I do not mean the bishop--had told me that such was the fact.", "“뿐만 아니라 제가 하딩 씨를 뵙기 전에, 주교관의 어떤 분들이 — 주교님을 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다 — 그것이 사실이라고 제게 일러 주었지요."], ["I own, I hardly believed it; I own, I thought that your father would wish on every account, for conscience'", "솔직히 저는 그 말을 좀처럼 믿지 않았습니다."], ["sake, for the sake of those old men, for old association and the memory of dear days long gone by, on every account I thought that he would wish to resume his duties.", "솔직히 저는, 아버님께서 무슨 이유로 보나, 양심을 위해서나, 그 노인들을 위해서나, 오랜 인연과 지나간 좋은 시절의 기억을 위해서나, 어느 모로 보나 옛 직분을 다시 맡고 싶어 하시리라 생각했어요."], ["But I was told that such was not his wish, and he certainly left me with the impression that I had been told the truth.\"", "그러나 그런 뜻이 아니시라는 말을 들었고, 아버님께서도 제가 들은 말이 참이라는 인상을 남기고 저와 헤어지셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Well!\"", "“그래서요!”"], ["said Eleanor, now sufficiently roused on the matter.", "엘리너가 말했다. 이제 이 일에 마음이 한껏 동한 것이었다."]], [["\"I hear Miss Bold's step,\"", "“볼드 양의 발소리가 들리는군요.”"], ["said Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Slope; \"would it be asking too great a favour to beg you to--I know you can manage anything with Miss Bold.\"", "“너무 큰 청을 드리는 것이 될까요, 부인께 부탁드려도… 볼드 양이라면 부인 뜻대로 얼마든지 다루실 수 있을 텐데요.”"]], [["Eleanor did not like the word manage, but still she went out and asked Mary to leave them alone for another quarter of an hour.", "엘리너는 다룬다는 말이 마음에 들지 않았으나, 그래도 밖으로 나가 메리에게 15분만 더 자리를 비켜 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["\"Thank you, Mrs.", "“고맙습니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold--I am so very grateful for this confidence.", "이렇게 믿어 주시니 참으로 감사해요."], ["Well, I left your father with this impression.", "자, 저는 그런 인상을 안고 아버님과 헤어졌습니다."], ["Indeed, I may say that he made me understand that he declined the appointment.\"", "실은 아버님께서 그 자리를 사양하신다는 뜻으로 저를 알아듣게 하셨다고 말씀드려도 좋을 정도예요.”"]], [["\"Not the appointment,\" said Eleanor.", "“그 자리를 사양하신 게 아니에요.”"], ["\"I am sure he did not decline the appointment.", "엘리너가 말했다. “아버지가 그 자리를 사양하셨을 리 없어요."], ["But he said that he would not agree--that is, that he did not like the scheme about the schools and the services and all that.", "다만 동의할 수 없다고 하신 거지요. 그러니까 학교니 예배니 하는 그 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다고 하신 거예요."], ["I am quite sure he never said that he wished to refuse the place.\"", "아버지가 그 자리를 마다하고 싶다고 말씀하셨을 리는 결코 없어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Bold!\"", "“아, 볼드 부인!”"], ["said Mr. Slope in a manner almost impassioned.", "슬로프 씨가 거의 격정에 찬 투로 말했다."], ["\"I would not for the world say to so good a daughter a word against so good a father.", "“그토록 훌륭한 따님께 그토록 훌륭한 아버님을 두고 한마디라도 나쁘게 말할 생각은 결코 없어요."], ["But you must, for his sake, let me show you exactly how the matter stands at present. Mr.", "그러나 아버님을 위해서라도, 지금 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지 제가 보여 드리도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다."], ["Harding was a little flurried when I told him of the bishop's wishes about the school.", "학교에 대한 주교님의 뜻을 전해 드렸을 때 하딩 씨는 조금 흥분하셨어요."], ["I did so perhaps with the less caution because you yourself had so perfectly agreed with me on the same subject.", "부인께서 같은 문제에 대해 저와 그토록 완전히 뜻이 맞으셨던 터라, 제가 아마 조심을 덜 했던 모양입니다. 아버님은 조금 언짢아하시며 열을 내어 말씀하셨지요."], ["He was a little put out and spoke warmly. 'Tell the bishop,'", "‘주교께 전해 주시오.’ 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["said he, 'that I quite disagree with him--and shall not return to the hospital as such conditions are attached to it.' What he said was to that effect; indeed, his words were, if anything, stronger than those.", "‘나는 그분과 생각이 아주 다르며, 그런 조건이 붙어 있는 한 요양원에 돌아가지 않겠다고.’ 그런 취지의 말씀이었고, 실은 그 말씀이 이보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지는 않았어요."], ["I had no alternative but to repeat them to his lordship, who said that he could look on them in no other light than a refusal.", "저로서는 그것을 주교님께 그대로 옮겨 드리는 수밖에 없었고, 주교님께서는 그것을 거절로밖에 볼 수 없다고 하셨습니다."], ["He also had heard the report that your father did not wish for the appointment, and putting all these things together, he thought he had no choice but to look for someone else.", "주교님께서도 아버님이 그 자리를 원하지 않으신다는 소문을 들으셨던 터라, 이 모든 것을 함께 놓고 보시고는 다른 사람을 찾는 수밖에 없다고 생각하셨어요."], ["He has consequently offered the place to Mr. Quiverful.\"", "그래서 퀴버풀 씨에게 그 자리를 제안하신 것입니다.”"]], [["\"Offered the place to Mr. Quiverful!\"", "“퀴버풀 씨에게 자리를 제안하셨다니요!”"], ["repeated Eleanor, her eyes suffused with tears.", "엘리너가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 되뇌었다."], ["\"Then, Mr. Slope, there is an end of it.\"", "“그렇다면 슬로프 씨, 이 일은 끝난 거네요.”"]], [["\"No, my friend--not so,\" said he.", "“아니에요, 나의 벗이여, 그렇지 않습니다.”"], ["\"It is to prevent such being the end of it that I am now here.", "그가 말했다. “일이 그렇게 끝나지 않도록 하려고 제가 지금 여기 와 있는 것이지요."], ["I may at any rate presume that I have got an answer to my question, and that Mr. Harding is desirous of returning.\"", "어쨌든 제 물음에 대한 답은 얻은 것으로, 그러니까 하딩 씨께서 돌아가기를 바라고 계신 것으로 여겨도 되겠군요.”"]], [["\"Desirous of returning--of course he is,\" said Eleanor; \"of course he wishes to have back his house and his income and his place in the world; to have back what he gave up with such self-denying honesty, if he can have them without restraints on his conduct to which at his age it would be impossible that he should submit. How can the bishop ask a man of his age to turn schoolmaster to a pack of children?\"", "“돌아가기를 바라시느냐고요. 물론 바라시지요.” 엘리너가 말했다. “물론 아버지는 집과 수입과 세상에서의 자리를 되찾고 싶어 하세요. 그토록 자신을 버리는 정직함으로 내놓았던 것을 되찾고 싶어 하시고말고요. 그 연세에 도저히 따를 수 없는 굴레가 처신에 씌워지지만 않는다면요. 주교님은 어떻게 그 연세의 어른더러 아이들 한 무리를 앉혀 놓고 학교 선생 노릇을 하라 하실 수 있나요?”"]], [["\"Out of the question,\" said Mr.", "“당치도 않은 일이지요.”"], ["Slope, laughing slightly; \"of course no such demand shall be made on your father.", "슬로프 씨가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다. “물론 아버님께 그런 요구를 하는 일은 없을 겁니다."], ["I can at any rate promise you that I will not be the medium of any so absurd a requisition.", "적어도 그런 터무니없는 요구를 전하는 심부름꾼 노릇은 제가 하지 않겠다고 약속드릴 수 있어요. 저희는 아버님께서 요양원 안에서 설교해 주시기를 바랐습니다."], ["We wished your father to preach in the hospital, as the inmates may naturally be too old to leave it, but even that shall not be insisted on.", "그 안에 계신 분들이 나이가 많아 밖으로 나오기 어려울 테니까요. 그러나 그것조차 고집하지 않겠어요."], ["We wished also to attach a Sabbath-day school to the hospital, thinking that such an establishment could not but be useful under the surveillance of so good a clergyman as Mr.", "또 저희는 요양원에 안식일 학교를 붙이고 싶었지요. 하딩 씨처럼 훌륭한 성직자의 보살핌 아래, 그리고 부인의 보살핌 아래라면 그런 시설이 유익하지 않을 리 없다고 생각했으니까요."], ["Harding, and also under your own. But, dear Mrs.", "하지만 볼드 부인, 그 이야기는 지금 하지 맙시다."], ["Bold, we won't talk of these things now.", "한 가지는 분명해요."], ["One thing is clear: we must do what we can to annul this rash offer the bishop has made to Mr. Quiverful.", "주교님이 퀴버풀 씨에게 성급하게 내놓으신 그 제안을 무르도록 우리가 할 수 있는 일을 해야 합니다."], ["Your father wouldn't see Quiverful, would he?", "아버님께서 퀴버풀 씨를 만나 보시지는 않겠지요?"], ["Quiverful is an honourable man, and would not for a moment stand in your father's way.\"", "퀴버풀 씨는 명예를 아는 사람이니 잠시라도 아버님 앞을 가로막지는 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"What?\"", "“뭐라고요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"Ask a man with fourteen children to give up his preferment!", "“아이가 열넷인 사람더러 제게 온 성직을 내놓으라고 하라고요!"], ["I am quite sure he will do no such thing.\"", "그분이 그럴 리 없다고 저는 확신해요.”"]], [["\"I suppose not,\"", "“그러시겠지요.”"], ["said Slope, and he again drew near to Mrs. Bold, so that now they were very close to each other.", "슬로프가 말하고는 다시 볼드 부인 가까이 다가앉았으니, 이제 둘은 아주 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["Eleanor did not think much about it but instinctively moved away a little.", "엘리너는 그것을 깊이 생각하지 않았으나 본능적으로 조금 물러앉았다."], ["How greatly would she have increased the distance could she have guessed what had been said about her at Plumstead!", "플럼스테드에서 자기를 두고 무슨 말이 오갔는지 짐작이라도 할 수 있었더라면 그 사이를 얼마나 더 벌려 놓았을 것인가."], ["\"I suppose not.", "“그러시겠지요."], ["But it is out of the question that Quiverful should supersede your father--quite out of the question.", "그러나 퀴버풀이 아버님을 밀어내고 그 자리에 앉는 일은 있을 수 없습니다. 결코 있을 수 없지요."], ["The bishop has been too rash.", "주교님께서 너무 성급하셨어요."], ["An idea occurs to me which may perhaps, with God's blessing, put us right.", "문득 한 가지 생각이 떠오르는데, 하느님의 도우심이 있다면 그것으로 일이 바로잡힐지도 모르겠습니다."], ["My dear Mrs. Bold, would you object to seeing the bishop yourself?\"", "볼드 부인, 부인께서 손수 주교님을 만나 보시는 것은 어떠신지요?”"]], [["\"Why should not my father see him?\"", "“어째서 아버지가 만나 뵈면 안 되나요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["She had once before in her life interfered in her father's affairs, and then not to much advantage.", "그녀는 전에 한 번 아버지의 일에 끼어든 적이 있었고, 그때 그리 좋은 결과를 얻지 못했다."], ["She was older now and felt that she should take no step in a matter so vital to him without his consent.", "이제 나이가 들었으므로, 아버지에게 그토록 중대한 일에서는 그분의 승낙 없이 한 걸음도 내디뎌서는 안 된다고 느꼈다."]], [["\"Why, to tell the truth,\" said Mr.", "“글쎄요, 사실대로 말씀드리자면,”"], ["Slope with a look of sorrow, as though he greatly bewailed the want of charity in his patron, \"the bishop fancies that he has cause of anger against your father.", "슬로프 씨가 마치 제 후원자의 인색한 마음씨를 몹시 애통해하기라도 하는 듯 서글픈 낯빛으로 말했다. “주교님께서는 아버님께 노여워할 까닭이 있다고 여기고 계세요."], ["I fear an interview would lead to further ill-will.\"", "만나 뵈면 도리어 감정이 더 나빠질까 걱정입니다.”"]], [["\"Why,\"", "“어머,”"], ["said Eleanor, \"my father is the mildest, the gentlest man living.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “제 아버지는 세상에서 가장 순하고 가장 온화한 분인걸요.”"]], [["\"I only know,\"", "“제가 아는 것은,”"], ["said Slope, \"that he has the best of daughters.", "슬로프가 말했다. “그분께 더없이 훌륭한 따님이 있다는 것뿐이에요."], ["So you would not see the bishop?", "그러면 주교님을 만나 보지 않으시겠다는 말씀이지요?"], ["As to getting an interview, I could manage that for you without the slightest annoyance to yourself.\"", "자리를 마련하는 일이라면 부인께 조금도 번거로움이 가지 않도록 제가 처리해 드릴 수 있습니다.”"]], [["\"I could do nothing, Mr. Slope, without consulting my father.\"", "“슬로프 씨, 저는 아버지와 의논하지 않고는 아무것도 할 수 없어요.”"]], [["\"Ah!\"", "“아!”"], ["said he, \"that would be useless; you would then only be your father's messenger.", "그가 말했다. “그러면 소용이 없지요."], ["Does anything occur to yourself?", "그렇게 되면 부인은 아버님의 심부름꾼밖에 되지 못하니까요."], ["Something must be done.", "부인께 떠오르는 생각은 없으신가요?"], ["Your father shall not be ruined by so ridiculous a misunderstanding.\"", "무언가는 해야 합니다. 이토록 어처구니없는 오해로 아버님이 무너지시게 둘 수는 없어요.”"]], [["Eleanor said that nothing occurred to her, but that it was very hard; the tears came to her eyes and rolled down her cheeks.", "엘리너는 떠오르는 것이 없다고, 다만 너무 딱한 일이라고 말했다."], ["Mr.", "눈에 눈물이 고여 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["Slope would have given much to have had the privilege of drying them, but he had tact enough to know that he had still a great deal to do before he could even hope for any privilege with Mrs. Bold.", "슬로프 씨는 그 눈물을 닦아 줄 특권을 얻을 수만 있다면 많은 것을 내주었으련만, 볼드 부인에게 어떤 특권이든 바라 볼 수 있게 되기까지는 아직 할 일이 많다는 것쯤은 알아차릴 눈치가 있었다."]], [["\"It cuts me to the heart to see you so grieved,\"", "“이렇게 슬퍼하시는 것을 보니 가슴이 저밉니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"But pray let me assure you that your father's interests shall not be sacrificed if it be possible for me to protect them.", "“그러나 제가 지킬 수만 있다면 아버님의 이익이 희생되는 일은 없으리라고 부디 믿어 주세요."], ["I will tell the bishop openly what are the facts.", "저는 주교님께 사실이 어떠한지 숨김없이 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["I will explain to him that he has hardly the right to appoint any other than your father, and will show him that if he does so he will be guilty of great injustice--and you, Mrs.", "아버님 말고 다른 사람을 임명할 권리가 주교님께는 거의 없다는 것을 설명해 드리고, 그렇게 하신다면 큰 불의를 저지르는 것이 되리라는 점을 보여 드리겠어요. 그리고 볼드 부인, 부인께서도 적어도 저를 이만큼은 너그럽게 믿어 주시겠지요."], ["Bold, you will have the charity at any rate to believe this of me, that I am truly anxious for your father's welfare--for his and for your own.\"", "제가 아버님의 안녕을 진심으로 바라고 있다는 것을요. 아버님을 위해서, 그리고 부인 자신을 위해서 말입니다.”"]], [["The widow hardly knew what answer to make.", "미망인은 무어라 대답해야 좋을지 알 수 없었다."], ["She was quite aware that her father would not be at all thankful to Mr.", "아버지가 슬로프 씨에게 조금도 고마워하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었고, 아버지의 마음을 함께하고 싶은 마음도 간절했다."], ["Slope; she had a strong wish to share her father's feelings; and yet she could not but acknowledge that Mr. Slope was very kind.", "그러면서도 슬로프 씨가 참으로 친절하다는 것만은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Her father, who was generally so charitable to all men, who seldom spoke ill of anyone, had warned her against Mr. Slope, and yet she did not know how to abstain from thanking him.", "대체로 모든 사람에게 너그럽고 누구를 나쁘게 말하는 법이 거의 없는 아버지가 슬로프 씨를 조심하라고 일러 주었건만, 그녀는 그에게 고맙다는 말을 하지 않고 넘어갈 방법을 알지 못했다."], ["What interest could he have in the matter but that which he professed?", "그가 이 일에서 스스로 밝힌 것 말고 무슨 다른 속셈을 가질 수 있단 말인가."], ["Nevertheless there was that in his manner which even she distrusted.", "그런데도 그의 태도에는 그녀조차 미덥지 않게 여기는 무엇이 있었다."], ["She felt, she did not know why, that there was something about him which ought to put her on her guard.", "왜인지는 몰라도 그에게는 마땅히 경계하게 만드는 무엇이 있다고 그녀는 느꼈다."]], [["Mr. Slope read all this in her hesitating manner just as plainly as though she had opened her heart to him.", "슬로프 씨는 그녀가 머뭇거리는 태도에서 이 모든 것을, 마치 그녀가 제 마음을 활짝 열어 보이기라도 한 것처럼 훤히 읽어 냈다."], ["It was the talent of the man that he could so read the inward feelings of women with whom he conversed.", "마주 앉아 이야기하는 여자의 속마음을 그렇게 읽어 내는 것이 이 사내의 재주였다."], ["He knew that Eleanor was doubting him, and that, if she thanked him, she would only do so because she could not help it, but yet this did not make him angry or even annoy him.", "그는 엘리너가 자기를 의심하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 고맙다는 말을 한다 해도 그것은 하지 않을 수 없어서 하는 것일 뿐임을 알았다. 그러나 그렇다고 화가 나거나 언짢아지지는 않았다."], ["Rome was not built in a day.", "로마는 하루아침에 세워지지 않았으니까."]], [["\"I did not come for thanks,\"", "“저는 고맙다는 말을 들으러 온 것이 아니에요.”"], ["continued he, seeing her hesitation, \"and do not want them--at any rate before they are merited.", "그녀가 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었다. “적어도 그럴 만한 일을 하기 전에는 그런 말을 바라지도 않습니다."], ["But this I do want, Mrs.", "다만 이것만은 바랍니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold, that I may make to myself friends in this fold to which it has pleased God to call me as one of the humblest of his shepherds.", "하느님께서 저를 가장 보잘것없는 목자의 하나로 불러 세우신 이 우리 안에서, 제가 스스로 벗을 얻을 수 있기를 바랍니다."], ["If I cannot do so, my task here must indeed be a sad one.", "그러지 못한다면 이곳에서 제 소임은 참으로 서글픈 것이 되겠지요."], ["I will at any rate endeavour to deserve them.\"", "어쨌든 저는 그런 벗을 얻을 만한 사람이 되도록 애쓰겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I'm sure,\"", "“그럼요,”"], ["said she, \"you will soon make plenty of friends.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “머지않아 벗을 많이 얻으시겠지요.”"], ["She felt herself obliged to say something.", "무슨 말이든 해야 할 것 같았던 것이다."]], [["\"That will be nothing unless they are such as will sympathize with my feelings; unless they are such as I can reverence and admire--and love.", "“제 마음에 공감해 줄 이들이 아니라면 그런 것은 아무것도 아니에요. 제가 우러르고 감탄할 수 있는, 그리고 사랑할 수 있는 이들이 아니라면 말이지요."], ["If the best and purest turn away from me, I cannot bring myself to be satisfied with the friendship of the less estimable.", "가장 훌륭하고 가장 순결한 이들이 제게서 등을 돌린다면, 그만 못한 이들의 우정으로 스스로를 만족시킬 수는 없습니다."], ["In such case I must live alone.\"", "그런 경우라면 저는 홀로 살아야겠지요.”"]], [["\"Oh, I'm sure you will not do that, Mr.", "“어머, 설마 그러시겠어요, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Slope.\" Eleanor meant nothing, but it suited him to appear to think some special allusion had been intended.", "엘리너는 아무 뜻 없이 한 말이었으나, 그로서는 무슨 각별한 뜻이 담긴 말로 여기는 척하는 편이 좋았다."]], [["\"Indeed, Mrs.", "“정말입니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold, I shall live alone, quite alone as far as the heart is concerned, if those with whom I yearn to ally myself turn away from me.", "제가 한마음으로 맺어지기를 갈망하는 이들이 제게서 등을 돌린다면, 저는 홀로 살게 될 거예요. 마음으로 말하자면 아주 홀로 말이지요."], ["But enough of this; I have called you my friend, and I hope you will not contradict me.", "그러나 이 이야기는 이만하겠습니다. 저는 부인을 벗이라 불렀으니, 부인께서 아니라고 하지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["I trust the time may come when I may also call your father so.", "언젠가는 아버님도 그렇게 부를 수 있는 날이 오기를 바라고요."], ["May God bless you, Mrs. Bold, you and your darling boy.", "하느님의 복이 부인께, 그리고 부인의 사랑스러운 아드님께 함께하기를 빕니다."], ["And tell your father from me that what can be done for his interest shall be done.\"", "그리고 아버님께 제 말이라고 전해 주세요. 아버님의 이익을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 하겠노라고요.”"]], [["And so he took his leave, pressing the widow's hand rather more closely than usual.", "그러고서 그는 미망인의 손을 여느 때보다 좀 더 꼭 쥐고는 물러갔다."], ["Circumstances, however, seemed just then to make this intelligible, and the lady did not feel called on to resent it.", "그러나 그때의 사정이 그 행동을 그럴듯하게 만들어 주는 듯하여, 부인은 그것을 언짢게 여겨야 한다고는 느끼지 않았다."]], [["\"I cannot understand him,\"", "“저 사람을 알 수가 없어요.”"], ["said Eleanor to Mary Bold a few minutes afterwards.", "몇 분 뒤에 엘리너가 메리 볼드에게 말했다."], ["\"I do not know whether he is a good man or a bad man--whether he is true or false.\"", "“좋은 사람인지 나쁜 사람인지, 참된 사람인지 거짓된 사람인지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"Then give him the benefit of the doubt,\"", "“그럼 의심스러운 쪽은 그이에게 유리하게 봐 주고,”"], ["said Mary, \"and believe the best.\"", "메리가 말했다. “가장 좋은 쪽으로 믿어요.”"]], [["\"On the whole, I think I do,\"", "“대체로 그러고 있는 것 같아요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"I think I do believe that he means well--and if so, it is a shame that we should revile him and make him miserable while he is among us.", "“저 사람이 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿고 있는 것 같아요. 그렇다면 우리와 함께 지내는 동안 저 사람을 헐뜯고 비참하게 만드는 것은 부끄러운 일이겠지요."], ["But, oh, Mary, I fear Papa will be disappointed in the hospital.\"", "하지만 아, 메리, 아버지가 요양원 일로 실망하시게 될까 걱정이에요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XVII. Who Shall Be Cock of the Walk?", "# 제17장. 누가 골목대장이 될 것인가"]], [["All this time things were going somewhat uneasily at the palace.", "그동안 주교관에서는 일이 어딘가 편치 않게 돌아가고 있었다."], ["The hint or two which Mr. Slope had given was by no means thrown away upon the bishop.", "슬로프 씨가 던져 놓은 한두 마디 암시는 결코 주교에게 헛되이 떨어지지 않았다."], ["He had a feeling that if he ever meant to oppose the now almost unendurable despotism of his wife, he must lose no further time in doing so; that if he ever meant to be himself master in his own diocese, let alone his own house, he should begin at once.", "이제 거의 견딜 수 없는 지경에 이른 아내의 전횡에 언젠가 맞설 생각이라면 더는 시간을 끌어서는 안 된다는 느낌이, 제 집안은 그만두고라도 제 교구에서만이라도 스스로 주인 노릇을 할 생각이라면 지금 당장 시작해야 한다는 느낌이 그에게 들었다."], ["It would have been easier to have done so from the day of his consecration than now, but easier now than when Mrs. Proudie should have succeeded in thoroughly mastering the diocesan details.", "주교로 축성을 받던 그날부터 그리했더라면 지금보다 쉬웠겠지만, 그래도 프라우디 부인이 교구의 세세한 사무까지 남김없이 장악하고 난 뒤보다는 지금이 쉬웠다."], ["Then the proffered assistance of Mr. Slope was a great thing for him, a most unexpected and invaluable aid.", "그런 마당에 슬로프 씨가 자청한 도움은 그에게 대단한 것, 참으로 뜻밖이고 값을 매길 수 없는 원군이었다."], ["Hitherto he had looked on the two as allied forces and had considered that, as allies, they were impregnable.", "지금까지 그는 그 둘을 한편으로 묶인 세력으로 보았고, 그렇게 손을 잡은 이상 도저히 뚫을 수 없다고 여겨 왔다."], ["He had begun to believe that his only chance of escape would be by the advancement of Mr. Slope to some distant and rich preferment.", "그로서 벗어날 길이라고는 슬로프 씨를 어디 멀고 넉넉한 자리로 영전시키는 것뿐이리라고 믿기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["But now it seemed that one of his enemies, certainly the least potent of them, but nevertheless one very important, was willing to desert his own camp.", "그런데 이제 보니 그 적들 가운데 하나가, 분명 그중 힘이 가장 약한 쪽이기는 하나 그래도 매우 중요한 하나가, 제 진영을 버릴 뜻을 품고 있는 것이었다."], ["Assisted by Mr. Slope what might he not do?", "슬로프 씨의 도움을 받는다면 무엇인들 못 하랴?"], ["He walked up and down his little study, almost thinking that the time might come when he would be able to appropriate to his own use the big room upstairs in which his predecessor had always sat.", "그는 좁은 서재를 이리저리 거닐면서, 전임자가 늘 앉아 있던 위층의 그 큰 방을 제 것으로 삼을 날이 올지도 모른다는 생각마저 해 보았다."]], [["As he revolved these things in his mind a note was brought to him from Archdeacon Grantly, in which that divine begged his lordship to do him the honour of seeing him on the morrow--would his lordship have the kindness to name an hour?", "이런 일들을 마음속에서 굴리고 있는데 그랜틀리 부주교에게서 쪽지가 하나 전해졌다. 그 성직자는 그 글에서 내일 뵙는 영광을 허락해 주십사고 주교께 청하면서, 황송하오나 시각을 정해 주시겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Dr. Grantly's proposed visit would have reference to the reappointment of Mr.", "그랜틀리 박사가 청한 방문은 하딩 씨를 바체스터 요양원의 워든 자리에 다시 임명하는 일에 관한 것이었다."], ["Harding to the wardenship of Barchester Hospital. The bishop having read his note was informed that the archdeacon's servant was waiting for an answer.", "주교가 쪽지를 읽고 나자, 부주교의 하인이 답을 기다리고 있다는 전갈이 들어왔다."]], [["Here at once a great opportunity offered itself to the bishop of acting on his own responsibility.", "여기서 곧바로 주교에게는 제 책임으로 일을 처리해 볼 절호의 기회가 굴러들어 왔다."], ["He bethought himself however of his new ally and rang the bell for Mr. Slope.", "그러나 그는 새 동맹자를 떠올리고 슬로프 씨를 부르려 종을 울렸다."], ["It turned out that Mr. Slope was not in the house, and then, greatly daring, the bishop with his own unassisted spirit wrote a note to the archdeacon saying that he would see him, and naming an hour for doing so.", "알고 보니 슬로프 씨는 집에 없었고, 그리하여 주교는 크게 마음먹고 누구의 도움도 없이 제 정신 하나로 부주교에게 만나겠노라는 답장을 써서 시각까지 정해 주었다."], ["Having watched from his study-window that the messenger got safely off from the premises with this dispatch, he began to turn over in his mind what step he should next take.", "서재 창으로 심부름꾼이 그 답장을 들고 무사히 집을 빠져나가는 것을 지켜본 다음, 그는 이제 다음으로 어떤 수를 두어야 할지 머릿속에서 이리저리 헤아리기 시작했다."]], [["To-morrow he would have to declare to the archdeacon either that Mr. Harding should have the appointment, or that he should not have it.", "내일이면 그는 부주교에게 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 맡게 되리라고 말하든지 맡지 못하리라고 말하든지, 둘 중 하나를 밝혀야 했다."], ["The bishop felt that he could not honestly throw over the Quiverfuls without informing Mrs. Proudie, and he resolved at last to brave the lioness in her den and tell her that circumstances were such that it behoved him to reappoint Mr.", "주교는 프라우디 부인에게 알리지도 않고 퀴버풀 내외를 떳떳하게 내칠 수는 없다고 느꼈고, 마침내 굴속의 암사자에게 맞서기로, 그리하여 사정이 이러이러하니 하딩 씨를 다시 임명하는 것이 마땅하다고 아내에게 말하기로 결심했다."], ["Harding. He did not feel that he should at all derogate from his new courage by promising Mrs.", "그러면서, 제 손에 들어오는 성직 자리 가운데 맨 처음 비는 것을 퀴버풀에게 주어 그가 입은 손해를 갚아 주겠노라고 프라우디 부인에게 약속한다 한들 새로 낸 용기가 조금이라도 깎이는 것은 아니라고 여겼다."], ["Proudie that the very first piece of available preferment at his disposal should be given to Quiverful to atone for the injury done to him. If he could mollify the lioness with such a sop, how happy would he think his first efforts to have been!", "그런 미끼 한 조각으로 암사자를 달랠 수만 있다면, 첫 시도치고 얼마나 훌륭했다고 여기겠는가!"]], [["Not without many misgivings did he find himself in Mrs. Proudie's boudoir.", "그는 숱한 불안을 안은 채 프라우디 부인의 내실에 들어섰다."], ["He had at first thought of sending for her.", "처음에는 부인을 제 방으로 부를까도 생각했다."], ["But it was not at all impossible that she might choose to take such a message amiss, and then also it might be some protection to him to have his daughters present at the interview.", "그러나 부인이 그런 전갈을 언짢게 받아들이기로 마음먹을 가능성이 얼마든지 있었고, 또 딸들이 그 자리에 함께 있으면 그에게 얼마간 방패가 되어 줄지도 몰랐다."], ["He found her sitting with her account-books before her, nibbling the end of her pencil, evidently immersed in pecuniary difficulties, and harassed in mind by the multiplicity of palatial expenses and the heavy cost of episcopal grandeur.", "가 보니 부인은 회계장부를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 연필 끝을 잘근거리며 앉아 있었는데, 보아하니 돈 문제에 깊이 빠져 주교관 살림의 온갖 비용과 주교의 위신을 지키는 데 드는 무거운 지출로 심사가 편치 않은 참이었다."], ["Her daughters were around her.", "딸들이 그 둘레에 있었다."], ["Olivia was reading a novel, Augusta was crossing a note to her bosom friend in Baker Street, and Netta was working diminutive coach wheels for the bottom of a petticoat.", "올리비아는 소설을 읽고, 어거스타는 베이커 스트리트에 사는 단짝에게 보낼 편지를 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓰고 있었으며, 네타는 페티코트 자락에 달 자그마한 마차 바퀴 무늬를 뜨고 있었다."], ["If the bishop could get the better of his wife in her present mood, he would be a man indeed.", "지금 이 심기의 아내를 주교가 이겨 낼 수 있다면 그야말로 사내대장부라 할 만했다."], ["He might then consider the victory his own forever.", "그때는 그 승리를 영원히 제 것으로 쳐도 좋을 터였다."], ["After all, in such cases the matter between husband and wife stands much the same as it does between two boys at the same school, two cocks in the same yard, or two armies on the same continent.", "따지고 보면 이런 경우 부부 사이의 형편은 한 학교에 다니는 두 소년, 한 마당에 있는 두 마리 수탉, 한 대륙에 있는 두 군대 사이의 형편과 크게 다르지 않다."], ["The conqueror once is generally the conqueror forever after.", "한 번 이긴 자가 대개 그 뒤로도 내내 이기는 자가 된다."], ["The prestige of victory is everything.", "승리의 위세가 전부인 것이다."]], [["\"Ahem--my dear,\"", "“에헴, 여보,”"], ["began the bishop, \"if you are disengaged, I wished to speak to you.\"", "주교가 입을 열었다. “한가하거든 잠깐 얘기 좀 했으면 하오.”"], ["Mrs. Proudie put her pencil down carefully at the point to which she had totted her figures, marked down in her memory the sum she had arrived at, and then looked up, sourly enough, into her helpmate's face.", "프라우디 부인은 그때까지 더해 온 숫자의 자리에 조심스레 연필을 내려놓고, 지금까지 나온 합계를 머릿속에 새겨 둔 다음, 자못 떫은 얼굴로 남편의 낯을 올려다보았다."], ["\"If you are busy, another time will do as well,\"", "“바쁘거든 다음에 해도 좋소.” 주교가 말을 이었다."], ["continued the bishop, whose courage, like Bob Acres', had oozed out now that he found himself on the ground of battle.", "밥 에이커스가 그러했듯이, 막상 싸움터에 발을 들여놓고 보니 그의 용기는 벌써 스르르 새어 나가 버렸던 것이다."]], [["\"What is it about, Bishop?\"", "“무슨 일인데요, 주교?”"], ["asked the lady.", "부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Well--it was about those Quiverfuls--but I see you are engaged.", "“음, 저 퀴버풀 내외 일인데 말이오."], ["Another time will do just as well for me.\"", "하지만 보아하니 바쁜 모양이니, 나야 다음에 해도 아무 상관 없소.”"]], [["\"What about the Quiverfuls?", "“퀴버풀 내외가 어쨌다는 거예요?"], ["It is quite understood, I believe, that they are to come to the hospital.", "그 사람들이 요양원으로 들어가기로 한 건 이미 다 정해진 일 아닌가요."], ["There is to be no doubt about that, is there?\"", "거기에 무슨 의심할 여지가 있는 건 아니겠지요?”"], ["and as she spoke she kept her pencil sternly and vigorously fixed on the column of figures before her.", "그렇게 말하면서도 부인은 앞에 놓인 숫자 줄에 연필을 매섭고 억세게 고정한 채였다."]], [["\"Why, my dear, there is a difficulty,\"", "“그게, 여보, 좀 곤란한 사정이 생겼소.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"A difficulty!\" said Mrs.", "“곤란한 사정이라니요!”"], ["Proudie, \"what difficulty?", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["The place has been promised to Mr.", "“뭐가 곤란하다는 거예요?"], ["Quiverful, and of course he must have it. He has made all his arrangements.", "그 자리는 퀴버풀 씨에게 주기로 약속한 것이고, 당연히 그 사람이 받아야지요."], ["He has written for a curate for Puddingdale, he has spoken to the auctioneer about selling his farm, horses, and cows, and in all respects considers the place as his own.", "그 사람은 벌써 만반의 채비를 다 해 놓았어요. 푸딩데일에 들일 부목사를 구하려고 편지를 냈고, 농장이며 말이며 소를 팔려고 경매인과 이야기를 마쳤고, 무엇으로 보나 그 자리를 제 것으로 알고 있단 말입니다."], ["Of course he must have it.\"", "당연히 그 사람이 받아야 해요.”"]], [["Now, Bishop, look well to thyself and call up all the manhood that is in thee.", "자, 주교여, 그대 자신을 잘 살피고 그대 안에 있는 사내다움을 모조리 불러내라."], ["Think how much is at stake.", "걸린 것이 얼마나 큰지 헤아려 보라."], ["If now thou art not true to thy guns, no Slope can hereafter aid thee.", "이제 와서 그대가 제 대포를 지키지 못한다면, 앞으로 어떤 슬로프도 그대를 돕지 못하리라."], ["How can he who deserts his own colours at the first smell of gunpowder expect faith in any ally?", "화약 냄새를 맡자마자 제 깃발을 버리는 자가 어찌 어떤 동맹에게서든 신의를 바라겠는가?"], ["Thou thyself hast sought the battle-field: fight out the battle manfully now thou art there.", "싸움터를 찾은 것은 그대 자신이니, 이미 그곳에 섰으면 사내답게 싸워 끝을 보라."], ["Courage, Bishop, courage!", "용기를 내라, 주교여, 용기를!"], ["Frowns cannot kill, nor can sharp words break any bones.", "찡그린 낯이 사람을 죽이지 못하고, 날카로운 말이 뼈를 부러뜨리지도 못한다."], ["After all, the apron is thine own.", "어차피 주교의 앞치마는 그대의 것이 아닌가."], ["She can appoint no wardens, give away no benefices, nominate no chaplains, an' thou art but true to thyself.", "그대가 그대 자신에게만 충실하다면, 저 여자는 워든을 하나도 임명하지 못하고 성직록을 하나도 내리지 못하며 전속 사제를 하나도 지명하지 못한다."], ["Up, man, and at her with a constant heart.", "일어서라, 사내여, 한결같은 마음으로 저 여자에게 맞서 나아가라."]], [["Some little monitor within the bishop's breast so addressed him.", "주교의 가슴속 어느 작은 훈계자가 그에게 그렇게 말을 걸었다."], ["But then there was another monitor there which advised him differently, and as follows.", "그러나 그곳에는 또 하나의 훈계자가 있어 이와 달리 이렇게 일렀다."], ["Remember, Bishop, she is a woman, and such a woman too as thou well knowest: a battle of words with such a woman is the very mischief.", "기억하라, 주교여, 저 사람은 여자요, 그것도 그대가 익히 아는 그런 여자다. 그런 여자와 말싸움을 벌이는 것은 그야말로 재앙이다."], ["Were it not better for thee to carry on this war, if it must be waged, from behind thine own table in thine own study?", "굳이 이 전쟁을 치러야 한다면, 그대의 서재에서 그대의 탁자 뒤에 앉아 치르는 편이 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["Does not every cock fight best on his own dunghill?", "수탉은 누구나 제 두엄 위에서 가장 잘 싸우는 법이 아닌가?"], ["Thy daughters also are here, the pledges of thy love, the fruits of thy loins: is it well that they should see thee in the hour of thy victory over their mother?", "게다가 이곳에는 그대의 딸들이, 그대의 사랑의 담보요 그대의 허리에서 난 열매들이 있다. 저희 어미를 그대가 이기는 순간을 저 아이들이 보는 것이 과연 좋은 일이겠는가?"], ["Nay, is it well that they should see thee in the possible hour of thy defeat?", "아니, 그대가 질지도 모를 그 순간을 저 아이들이 보는 것은 또 좋은 일이겠는가?"], ["Besides, hast thou not chosen thy opportunity with wonderful little skill, indeed with no touch of that sagacity for which thou art famous?", "게다가 그대는 때를 고르는 솜씨가 놀랍도록 서툴지 않았는가, 그대가 그토록 이름을 떨친 그 지혜를 한 자락도 쓰지 않고서 말이다."], ["Will it not turn out that thou art wrong in this matter and thine enemy right; that thou hast actually pledged thyself in this matter of the hospital, and that now thou wouldest turn upon thy wife because she requires from thee but the fulfilment of thy promise?", "결국 이 일에서는 그대가 그르고 그대의 적이 옳다고 판명되지 않겠는가. 요양원 문제에서 실은 그대 스스로 약속을 해 놓고서, 이제 와 아내가 그 약속을 지키라 요구할 뿐인데 그 아내에게 등을 돌리려는 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["Art thou not a Christian bishop, and is not thy word to be held sacred whatever be the result?", "그대는 그리스도의 주교가 아닌가. 결과가 어떠하든 그대의 말은 거룩히 지켜야 하는 것이 아닌가?"], ["Return, Bishop, to thy sanctum on the lower floor and postpone thy combative propensities for some occasion in which at least thou mayest fight the battle against odds less tremendously against thee.", "돌아가라, 주교여, 아래층 그대의 성소로 돌아가, 적어도 승산이 이토록 끔찍하게 기울지는 않은 어느 때로 그 호전적인 기질을 미루어 두라."]], [["All this passed within the bishop's bosom while Mrs. Proudie still sat with her fixed pencil, and the figures of her sum still enduring on the tablets of her memory.", "이 모든 것이 주교의 가슴속을 지나가는 동안 프라우디 부인은 여전히 연필을 고정한 채 앉아 있었고, 계산해 낸 숫자는 여전히 그 기억의 서판 위에 남아 있었다."], ["\"£4 17s. 7d.\"", "“4파운드 17실링 7펜스.”"], ["she said to herself.", "부인은 속으로 되뇌었다."], ["\"Of course Mr. Quiverful must have the hospital,\"", "“물론 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원을 맡아야지요.”"], ["she said out loud to her lord.", "이번에는 제 주인을 향해 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["\"Well, my dear, I merely wanted to suggest to you that Mr.", "“글쎄, 여보, 나는 그저 이런 말을 해 두고 싶었을 뿐이오."], ["Slope seems to think that if Mr. Harding be not appointed, public feeling in the matter would be against us, and that the press might perhaps take it up.\"", "슬로프 씨 생각으로는, 하딩 씨가 임명되지 않으면 이 일에 대한 세간의 감정이 우리에게 불리하게 돌아설 것이고, 어쩌면 신문이 이 일을 들고 나올지도 모른다는 것이오.”"]], [["\"Mr. Slope seems to think!\"", "“슬로프 씨 생각으로는이라니요!”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie in a tone of voice which plainly showed the bishop that he was right in looking for a breach in that quarter.", "프라우디 부인이 말했는데, 그 목소리의 어조만으로도 주교는 바로 그 방면에서 틈이 벌어지기를 기대한 것이 옳았음을 똑똑히 알 수 있었다."], ["\"And what has Mr. Slope to do with it?", "“대체 슬로프 씨가 이 일과 무슨 상관이랍니까?"], ["I hope, my lord, you are not going to allow yourself to be governed by a chaplain.\"", "주교님, 설마 전속 사제 하나에게 좌지우지되실 생각은 아니시겠지요.”"], ["And now in her eagerness the lady lost her place in her account.", "그러고는 흥분한 나머지 부인은 계산하던 자리를 놓치고 말았다."]], [["\"Certainly not, my dear.", "“그럴 리가 있소, 여보."], ["Nothing I can assure you is less probable.", "그보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 없다고 내 장담하리다."], ["But still, Mr. Slope may be useful in finding how the wind blows, and I really thought that if we could give something else as good to the Quiverfuls--\"", "하지만 그래도 바람이 어느 쪽에서 부는지 알아보는 데는 슬로프 씨가 쓸모가 있을 수 있고, 나는 정말이지 퀴버풀 내외에게 그만큼 좋은 다른 자리를 줄 수만 있다면——”"]], [["\"Nonsense,\" said Mrs.", "“말도 안 됩니다.”"], ["Proudie; \"it would be years before you could give them anything else that could suit them half as well, and as for the press and the public and all that, remember there are two ways of telling a story.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 사람들에게 그 절반이라도 걸맞은 다른 자리를 주려면 몇 해가 걸릴 거예요."], ["If Mr. Harding is fool enough to tell his tale, we can also tell ours.", "그리고 신문이니 세간이니 하는 것들로 말하자면, 이야기를 전하는 데는 두 가지 방식이 있다는 걸 잊지 마세요."], ["The place was offered to him, and he refused it.", "하딩 씨가 어리석게 제 사정을 떠벌린다면 우리도 우리 사정을 떠벌리면 그만입니다."], ["It has now been given to someone else, and there's an end of it.", "그 자리를 그에게 주겠다고 했는데 그가 거절했다, 그래서 이제 다른 사람에게 주었다, 그것으로 끝인 겁니다."], ["At least I should think so.\"", "적어도 나는 그렇게 생각해요.”"]], [["\"Well, my dear, I rather believe you are right,\"", "“글쎄, 여보, 아무래도 당신 말이 옳은 듯하오.”"], ["said the bishop, and sneaking out of the room, he went downstairs, troubled in his mind as to how he should receive the archdeacon on the morrow.", "주교는 그렇게 말하고 슬그머니 방을 빠져나와 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 내일 부주교를 어떻게 맞아야 할지 마음이 심란했다."], ["He felt himself not very well just at present, and began to consider that he might, not improbably, be detained in his room the next morning by an attack of bile.", "그는 지금 당장 몸이 그리 좋지 않다고 느꼈고, 이튿날 아침에는 담즙이 끓어올라 방에서 나오지 못하게 될 공산도 없지 않겠다는 생각을 하기 시작했다."], ["He was, unfortunately, very subject to bilious annoyances.", "그는 불행히도 담즙 탈이 매우 잦은 사람이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope, indeed!", "“슬로프 씨가, 참!"], ["I'll Slope him,\"", "그 슬로프는 내가 손봐 주지.”"], ["said the indignant matron to her listening progeny.", "분개한 안주인이 귀 기울이고 있는 제 소생들에게 말했다."], ["\"I don't know what has come to Mr. Slope.", "“슬로프 씨가 대체 왜 저러는지 모르겠구나."], ["I believe he thinks he is to be Bishop of Barchester himself, because I've taken him by the hand and got your father to make him his domestic chaplain.\"", "내가 손을 잡아 이끌어 주고 너희 아버지를 시켜 집안 전속 사제로 앉혀 주었더니, 제가 바체스터 주교라도 되는 줄 아는 모양이다.”"]], [["\"He was always full of impudence,\"", "“그 사람은 언제나 뻔뻔스러움이 넘쳤어요.”"], ["said Olivia; \"I told you so once before, Mamma.\"", "올리비아가 말했다. “제가 전에도 한 번 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요, 어머니.”"], ["Olivia, however, had not thought him too impudent when once before he had proposed to make her Mrs. Slope.", "그러나 올리비아는 언젠가 그가 자기를 슬로프 부인으로 만들겠노라 청했을 때에는 그를 그리 뻔뻔하다고 여기지 않았었다."]], [["\"Well, Olivia, I always thought you liked him,\"", "“어머, 올리비아 언니, 나는 늘 언니가 그 사람을 좋아하는 줄 알았는데.”"], ["said Augusta, who at that moment had some grudge against her sister.", "그 순간 언니에게 무언가 앙심이 있던 어거스타가 말했다."], ["\"I always disliked the man, because I think him thoroughly vulgar.\"", "“나는 그 사람이 늘 싫었어. 아주 천박한 사람이라고 생각하니까.”"]], [["\"There you're wrong,\"", "“거기서는 네가 틀렸다.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs.", "“그 사람은 조금도 천박하지 않아."], ["Proudie; \"he's not vulgar at all; and what is more, he is a soul-stirring, eloquent preacher; but he must be taught to know his place if he is to remain in this house.\"", "게다가 영혼을 뒤흔드는 웅변가 설교자이기도 하고. 다만 이 집에 계속 머무르려거든 제 분수를 알도록 가르쳐 놓아야겠다.”"]], [["\"He has the horridest eyes I ever saw in a man's head,\"", "“그 사람은 내가 남자 얼굴에서 본 눈 가운데 제일 끔찍한 눈을 가졌어요.” 네타가 말했다."], ["said Netta; \"and I tell you what, he's terribly greedy; did you see all the currant pie he ate yesterday?\"", "“그리고 말이죠, 그 사람 먹성이 아주 지독해요. 어제 커런트 파이를 얼마나 먹어 치우던지 보셨어요?”"]], [["When Mr. Slope got home he soon learnt from the bishop, as much from his manner as his words, that Mrs. Proudie's behests in the matter of the hospital were to be obeyed. Dr. Proudie let fall something as to \"this occasion only\" and \"keeping all affairs about patronage exclusively in his own hands.\" But he was quite decided about Mr. Harding; and as Mr. Slope did not wish to have both the prelate and the prelatess against him, he did not at present see that he could do anything but yield.", "슬로프 씨는 집에 돌아오자마자, 주교의 말보다도 오히려 그 태도에서 요양원 일에 관해서는 프라우디 부인의 분부를 따르기로 되었음을 곧 알아차렸다. 프라우디 박사는 “이번만은”이라느니 “성직 임명권에 관한 일은 전부 오로지 제 손안에 두겠노라”느니 하는 말을 슬쩍 흘렸다. 그러나 하딩 씨 문제에서는 아주 단호했고, 슬로프 씨로서는 주교와 여주교를 한꺼번에 적으로 돌리고 싶지 않았으므로 지금으로서는 물러서는 것 말고 달리 할 수 있는 일이 있을 것 같지 않았다."]], [["He merely remarked that he would of course carry out the bishop's views and that he was quite sure that if the bishop trusted to his own judgement things in the diocese would certainly be well ordered.", "그는 그저 물론 주교님의 뜻대로 처리하겠노라고, 그리고 주교님께서 주교님 자신의 판단을 믿고 따르신다면 이 교구의 일은 반드시 잘 다스려질 것이라 확신한다고만 말해 두었다."], ["Mr. Slope knew that if you hit a nail on the head often enough, it will penetrate at last.", "슬로프 씨는 못이란 머리를 여러 번 두들기다 보면 결국은 박히고 만다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["He was sitting alone in his room on the same evening when a light knock was made on his door, and before he could answer it the door was opened, and his patroness appeared.", "바로 그날 저녁 그가 제 방에 혼자 앉아 있는데 문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 나더니, 그가 대답할 겨를도 없이 문이 열리고 그의 후원자가 나타났다."], ["He was all smiles in a moment, but so was not she also.", "그는 순식간에 온 얼굴이 웃음이 되었으나 부인은 그렇지 않았다."], ["She took, however, the chair that was offered to her, and thus began her expostulation:", "그래도 부인은 권하는 의자에 앉았고, 그러고는 이렇게 항의를 시작했다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope, I did not at all approve your conduct the other night with that Italian woman.", "“슬로프 씨, 나는 요전 날 밤 자네가 그 이탈리아 여자에게 보인 처신을 조금도 좋게 보지 않네."], ["Anyone would have thought that you were her lover.\"", "누가 봤어도 자네가 그 여자의 연인인 줄 알았을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Good gracious, my dear madam,\"", "“저런, 부인, 그게 무슨 말씀이십니까.”"], ["said Mr. Slope with a look of horror.", "슬로프 씨가 소스라치는 낯빛으로 말했다."], ["\"Why, she is a married woman.\"", "“아니, 그분은 남편이 있는 부인이 아닙니까.”"]], [["\"That's more than I know,\" said Mrs.", "“그거야 내가 알 바 아니고,”"], ["Proudie; \"however she chooses to pass for such.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “어쨌든 본인은 그렇게 행세하기로 한 모양이더군."], ["But married or not married, such attention as you paid to her was improper.", "하지만 남편이 있든 없든, 자네가 그 여자에게 쏟은 그런 관심은 온당치 못했네."], ["I cannot believe that you would wish to give offence in my drawing-room, Mr. Slope, but I owe it to myself and my daughters to tell you that I disapprove of your conduct.\"", "자네가 내 응접실에서 남의 눈에 거슬리는 짓을 하고 싶어 하리라고는 믿지 않네만, 슬로프 씨, 나 자신과 내 딸들을 위해서라도 자네 처신이 마땅치 않다고 일러 두어야겠어.”"]], [["Mr. Slope opened wide his huge protruding eyes and stared out of them with a look of well-feigned surprise.", "슬로프 씨는 크게 튀어나온 두 눈을 한껏 부릅뜨고, 감쪽같이 꾸며 낸 놀란 얼굴로 부인을 빤히 바라보았다."], ["\"Why, Mrs. Proudie,\"", "“아니, 프라우디 부인,”"], ["said he, \"I did but fetch her something to eat when she said she was hungry.\"", "그가 말했다. “저는 그분이 시장하다고 하시기에 먹을 것을 좀 가져다 드린 것뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"And you have called on her since,\"", "“그리고 그 뒤에 그 여자를 찾아갔더군.”"], ["continued she, looking at the culprit with the stern look of a detective policeman in the act of declaring himself.", "부인이 말을 이으며, 이제야 제 정체를 밝히는 형사의 매서운 눈초리로 죄인을 바라보았다."]], [["Mr. Slope turned over in his mind whether it would be well for him to tell this termagant at once that he should call on whom he liked and do what he liked, but he remembered that his footing in Barchester was not yet sufficiently firm, and that it would be better for him to pacify her.", "슬로프 씨는 이 드센 여자에게 나는 내가 좋은 사람을 찾아가고 내가 좋은 대로 하겠다고 당장 말해 버리는 편이 나을지 마음속으로 저울질해 보았으나, 바체스터에서 제 발판이 아직 그만큼 단단하지 못하다는 것, 그러니 지금은 부인을 달래 두는 편이 낫다는 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"I certainly called since at Dr. Stanhope's house, and certainly saw Madame Neroni.\"", "“그 뒤에 스탠호프 박사 댁에 들른 것은 사실이고, 네로니 부인을 뵌 것도 사실입니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, and you saw her alone,\"", "“그렇지, 게다가 그 여자를 단둘이 만났지.”"], ["said the episcopal Argus.", "주교관의 아르고스가 말했다."]], [["\"Undoubtedly, I did,\" said Mr.", "“그야 물론 그랬습니다.”"], ["Slope, \"but that was because nobody else happened to be in the room.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. “하지만 그건 마침 방에 다른 사람이 아무도 없었기 때문입니다."], ["Surely it was no fault of mine if the rest of the family were out.\"", "식구들이 다 나가고 없는 것이 설마 제 잘못은 아니겠지요.”"]], [["\"Perhaps not, but I assure you, Mr.", "“그야 아닐 테지."], ["Slope, you will fall greatly in my estimation if I find that you allow yourself to be caught by the lures of that woman.", "하지만 슬로프 씨, 내 분명히 말해 두네만, 자네가 그 여자의 미끼에 걸려든 것을 내가 알게 되는 날에는 자네에 대한 내 평가는 크게 떨어질 걸세."], ["I know women better than you do, Mr.", "여자를 아는 데는 내가 자네보다 낫네, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope, and you may believe me that that signora, as she calls herself, is not a fitting companion for a strict evangelical unmarried young clergyman.\"", "스스로 시뇨라라 부르는 그 여자는 엄격한 복음주의 신자인 미혼 젊은 성직자가 가까이할 만한 상대가 못 된다는 내 말을 믿어도 좋아.”"]], [["How Mr. Slope would have liked to laugh at her, had he dared!", "슬로프 씨는 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면 부인의 면전에서 얼마나 웃어 주고 싶었으랴!"], ["But he did not dare.", "그러나 그는 감히 그러지 못했다."], ["So he merely said, \"I can assure you, Mrs.", "그래서 그저 이렇게만 말했다."], ["Proudie, the lady in question is nothing to me.\"", "“프라우디 부인, 말씀하신 그 부인은 제게 아무것도 아니라고 장담드릴 수 있습니다.”"]], [["\"Well, I hope not, Mr. Slope.", "“그렇다면 다행이네, 슬로프 씨."], ["But I have considered it my duty to give you this caution.", "그래도 이만한 주의는 주어야 하는 것이 내 도리라고 여겼네. 그리고 또 한 가지, 내가 말해 두어야겠다고 여기는 일이 있어."], ["And now there is another thing I feel myself called on to speak about: it is your conduct to the bishop, Mr. Slope.\"", "주교님을 대하는 자네의 태도 말일세, 슬로프 씨.”"]], [["\"My conduct to the bishop,\"", "“주교님을 대하는 제 태도라니요.”"], ["said he, now truly surprised and ignorant what the lady alluded to.", "그가 말했다. 이번에는 정말로 놀랐고, 부인이 무엇을 두고 하는 말인지 짐작조차 가지 않았다."]], [["\"Yes, Mr. Slope, your conduct to the bishop.", "“그래, 슬로프 씨, 주교님을 대하는 자네의 태도 말이야."], ["It is by no means what I would wish to see it.\"", "그건 내가 보고 싶은 모습과는 영 딴판이네.”"]], [["\"Has the bishop said anything, Mrs. Proudie?\"", "“주교님께서 무슨 말씀이라도 하신 것입니까, 프라우디 부인?”"]], [["\"No, the bishop has said nothing.", "“아니, 주교님은 아무 말씀도 하지 않으셨네."], ["He probably thinks that any remarks on the matter will come better from me, who first introduced you to his lordship's notice.", "아마 그 문제에 관한 말은 자네를 처음 주교님께 소개한 나에게서 나오는 편이 낫다고 생각하시는 게지. 사실은 이렇네, 슬로프 씨."], ["The fact is, Mr. Slope, you are a little inclined to take too much upon yourself.\"", "자네는 제 분수보다 조금 지나치게 나서는 구석이 있어.”"]], [["An angry spot showed itself on Mr. Slope's cheeks, and it was with difficulty that he controlled himself.", "슬로프 씨의 두 뺨에 성난 붉은 점이 돋아났고, 그는 간신히 제 자신을 다스렸다."], ["But he did do so, and sat quite silent while the lady went on.", "그러나 어쨌든 다스려 내어, 부인이 말을 이어 가는 동안 아주 잠자코 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"It is the fault of many young men in your position, and therefore the bishop is not inclined at present to resent it.", "“자네 자리에 있는 젊은이들이 흔히 저지르는 잘못이니, 주교님께서도 지금으로서는 그것을 문제 삼을 뜻이 없으시네."], ["You will, no doubt, soon learn what is required from you and what is not.", "자네도 머지않아 무엇이 자네에게 요구되고 무엇이 요구되지 않는지 알게 되겠지."], ["If you will take my advice, however, you will be careful not to obtrude advice upon the bishop in any matter touching patronage.", "그러나 내 충고를 받아들이겠거든, 성직 임명권에 관계된 일이라면 어떤 일에서든 주교님께 함부로 조언을 들이대지 않도록 조심하게."], ["If his lordship wants advice, he knows where to look for it.\"", "주교님께서 조언이 필요하시면, 어디서 그것을 구해야 할지는 스스로 아시네.”"], ["And then having added to her counsel a string of platitudes as to what was desirable and what not desirable in the conduct of a strictly evangelical unmarried young clergyman, Mrs. Proudie retreated, leaving the chaplain to his thoughts.", "그러고서 부인은 엄격한 복음주의 신자인 미혼 젊은 성직자의 처신에서 무엇이 바람직하고 무엇이 바람직하지 않은가에 대한 상투적인 훈계를 한바탕 덧붙인 뒤 물러갔고, 전속 사제는 제 생각에 잠긴 채 남았다."]], [["The upshot of his thoughts was this, that there certainly was not room in the diocese for the energies of both himself and Mrs.", "그가 잠긴 생각의 결론은 이러했다."], ["Proudie, and that it behoved him quickly to ascertain whether his energies or hers were to prevail.", "이 교구에는 자신의 활력과 프라우디 부인의 활력이 함께 뻗을 자리가 결코 없다는 것, 그러니 자신의 활력이 이길 것인지 부인의 활력이 이길 것인지를 하루빨리 가려내야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII. The Widow's Persecution", "# 제18장. 미망인이 시달리다"]], [["Early on the following morning Mr. Slope was summoned to the bishop's dressing-room, and went there fully expecting that he should find his lordship very indignant and spirited up by his wife to repeat the rebuke which she had administered on the previous day.", "이튿날 아침 일찍 슬로프 씨는 주교의 옷방으로 불려 갔고, 주교가 아내에게 부추김을 받아 잔뜩 성이 난 채 전날 부인이 퍼부었던 질책을 되풀이하리라 단단히 예상하며 그리로 갔다."], ["Mr. Slope had resolved that at any rate from him he would not stand it, and entered the dressing-room in rather a combative disposition; but he found the bishop in the most placid and gentlest of humours.", "슬로프 씨는 적어도 주교에게서만큼은 그런 꼴을 참고 있지 않겠다고 마음을 굳힌 터라 제법 싸울 태세를 갖추고 옷방에 들어섰다."], ["His lordship complained of being rather unwell, had a slight headache, and was not quite the thing in his stomach; but there was nothing the matter with his temper.", "그러나 그가 본 주교는 더할 나위 없이 온화하고 부드러운 기분이었다. 각하는 몸이 좀 편치 않다고, 머리가 조금 아프고 속도 그리 좋지 않다고 하소연했으나, 성미로 말하자면 아무 탈이 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, Slope,\"", "“아, 슬로프.”"], ["said he, taking the chaplain's proffered hand, \"Archdeacon Grantly is to call on me this morning, and I really am not fit to see him.", "전속 사제가 내미는 손을 잡으며 그가 말했다."], ["I fear I must trouble you to see him for me;\" and then Dr.", "“오늘 아침에 그랜틀리 부주교가 나를 찾아오기로 되어 있는데, 도무지 그를 만날 형편이 못 되는구려."], ["Proudie proceeded to explain what it was that must be said to Dr.", "미안하지만 나 대신 그를 좀 만나 주어야겠소.”"], ["Grantly. He was to be told in fact, in the civilest words in which the tidings could be conveyed, that Mr.", "그러고 나서 프라우디 박사는 그랜틀리 박사에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 설명해 나갔다."], ["Harding having refused the wardenship, the appointment had been offered to Mr. Quiverful and accepted by him.", "요컨대 하딩 씨가 워든 자리를 거절했으므로 그 자리는 퀴버풀 씨에게 제안되었고 그가 수락했다는 소식을, 그 소식을 전할 수 있는 가장 정중한 말로 그에게 알리라는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Slope again pointed out to his patron that he thought he was perhaps not quite wise in his decision, and this he did sotto voce.", "슬로프 씨는 다시 한번 후원자에게 그 결정이 어쩌면 그리 현명하지 못한 듯싶다고 지적했고, 그것도 sotto voce로 했다."], ["But even with this precaution it was not safe to say much, and during the little that he did say, the bishop made a very slight, but still a very ominous gesture with his thumb towards the door which opened from his dressing-room to some inner sanctuary.", "그러나 그렇게 조심을 하고서도 많은 말을 하는 것은 안전하지 않았으니, 그가 그 몇 마디를 하는 동안 주교는 옷방에서 안쪽 성소로 통하는 문 쪽을 향해 엄지손가락으로 아주 희미하지만 그래도 아주 불길한 몸짓을 해 보였다. 슬로프 씨는 즉시 그 뜻을 알아채고 더는 말하지 않았다."], ["Mr. Slope at once took the hint and said no more, but he perceived that there was to be confidence between him and his patron, that the league desired by him was to be made, and that this appointment of Mr.", "그러나 그는 자기와 후원자 사이에 밀약이 있게 되리라는 것, 자기가 바라던 동맹이 맺어지리라는 것, 그리고 이번 퀴버풀 씨의 임명이 부부간의 순종이라는 제단에 바쳐지는 마지막 제물이 되리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Quiverful was to be the last sacrifice offered on the altar of conjugal obedience. All this Mr.", "이 모든 것을 슬로프 씨는 주교의 엄지손가락이 움직인 그 희미한 동작에서 읽어 냈고, 그가 읽어 낸 것은 옳았다."], ["Slope read in the slight motion of the bishop's thumb, and he read it correctly. There was no need of parchments and seals, of attestations, explanations, and professions.", "양피지도 봉인도, 증서도 해명도 서약도 필요 없었다."], ["The bargain was understood between them, and Mr. Slope gave the bishop his hand upon it.", "흥정은 두 사람 사이에 이해되었고, 슬로프 씨는 그 위에 주교에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["The bishop understood the little extra squeeze, and an intelligible gleam of assent twinkled in his eye.", "주교는 유난히 한 번 더 힘주어 쥔 그 손의 뜻을 알아들었고, 알아들었다는 표시의 빛이 그의 눈에서 반짝였다."]], [["\"Pray be civil to the archdeacon, Mr.", "“부주교에게 정중히 대하시오, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Slope,\" said he out loud, \"but make him quite understand that in this matter Mr.", "그가 소리를 높여 말했다."], ["Harding has put it out of my power to oblige him.\"", "“다만 이 일에서는 하딩 씨 때문에 내가 그의 편의를 봐줄 도리가 없게 되었다는 것을 분명히 알아듣게 해 주시오.”"]], [["It would be a calumny on Mrs. Proudie to suggest that she was sitting in her bedroom with her ear at the keyhole during this interview.", "이 면담이 오가는 동안 프라우디 부인이 침실에 앉아 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고 있었다고 말한다면 그것은 그 부인에 대한 중상이 될 것이다."], ["She had within her a spirit of decorum which prevented her from descending to such baseness.", "그녀 안에는 그런 비열함으로까지 내려가는 것을 막아 주는 체면의 정신이 있었다."], ["To put her ear to a keyhole, or to listen at a chink, was a trick for a housemaid. Mrs.", "열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대거나 문틈에 귀를 기울이는 것은 하녀나 하는 수작이었다."], ["Proudie knew this, and therefore did not do it; but she stationed herself as near to the door as she well could, that she might, if possible, get the advantage which the housemaid would have had, without descending to the housemaid's artifice.", "프라우디 부인은 그것을 알았고, 그래서 그리하지 않았다. 다만 그녀는 될 수 있는 대로 문 가까이에 자리를 잡았으니, 하녀의 술책으로까지 내려가지 않고도 하녀가 얻었을 이점을 되도록 얻어 보자는 것이었다."]], [["It was little, however, that she heard, and that little was only sufficient to deceive her.", "그러나 그녀가 들은 것은 얼마 되지 않았고, 그 얼마 안 되는 것은 그녀를 속이기에나 족했다."], ["She saw nothing of that friendly pressure, perceived nothing of that concluded bargain; she did not even dream of the treacherous resolves which those two false men had made together to upset her in the pride of her station, to dash the cup from her lip before she had drunk of it, to sweep away all her power before she had tasted its sweets!", "그녀는 그 다정한 악수를 조금도 보지 못했고, 그 맺어진 흥정을 조금도 알아채지 못했다. 그 두 거짓된 사내가 함께 세운 배신의 결의를, 곧 그녀가 지위의 자랑에 겨워 있을 때 그녀를 뒤엎어 버리고, 그녀가 잔을 채 마시기도 전에 그 입술에서 잔을 쳐 내고, 그녀가 권력의 단맛을 맛보기도 전에 그 권력을 죄다 쓸어 가려는 결의를 그녀는 꿈에도 그리지 못했다!"], ["Traitors that they were, the husband of her bosom and the outcast whom she had fostered and brought to the warmth of the world's brightest fireside!", "배신자들이었다, 그 품속의 남편과, 그녀가 거두어 세상에서 가장 밝은 난롯가의 온기로 데려다 놓은 그 버림받은 자가!"], ["But neither of them had the magnanimity of this woman.", "그러나 둘 중 누구에게도 이 여인만 한 배포는 없었다."], ["Though two men have thus leagued themselves together against her, even yet the battle is not lost.", "두 사내가 이렇게 손을 잡고 그녀에게 맞섰다고는 하나, 싸움은 아직 진 것이 아니다."]], [["Mr. Slope felt pretty sure that Dr.", "슬로프 씨는 그랜틀리 박사가 자기를 만나는 영광은 사양하리라고 꽤 확신했는데, 과연 그렇게 되었다."], ["Grantly would decline the honour of seeing him, and such turned out to be the case. The archdeacon, when the palace door was opened to him, was greeted by a note.", "부주교는 주교관 문이 열렸을 때 쪽지 한 장으로 영접을 받았다."], ["Mr. Slope presented his compliments, &c.", "슬로프 씨가 삼가 인사를 올린다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고."], ["&c. The bishop was ill in his room and very greatly regretted, &c.", "주교는 방에서 몸이 편치 않아 대단히 유감스럽게 여긴다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고."], ["&c. Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 전하도록 위임받았으니 부주교만 좋다면 영광으로 알겠다는 둥 어쩌고저쩌고."], ["Slope had been charged with the bishop's views, and if agreeable to the archdeacon, would do himself the honour, &c. &c.", "그러나 부주교는 좋지 않았다."], ["The archdeacon, however, was not agreeable, and having read his note in the hall, crumpled it up in his hand, and muttering something about sorrow for his lordship's illness, took his leave, without sending as much as a verbal message in answer to Mr. Slope's note.", "그는 현관에서 그 쪽지를 읽고 손안에 구겨 쥐고는, 각하의 병환이 딱하다는 둥 무어라 중얼거리며 물러났으니, 슬로프 씨의 쪽지에 대한 답으로는 말 한마디조차 남기지 않았다."]], [["\"Ill!\"", "“병이라!”"], ["said the archdeacon to himself as he flung himself into his brougham.", "부주교는 사륜마차에 몸을 던져 넣으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"The man is absolutely a coward.", "“저 사람은 순전히 겁쟁이야."], ["He is afraid to see me. Ill, indeed!\"", "나를 만나기가 무서운 게지."], ["The archdeacon was never ill himself, and did not therefore understand that anyone else could in truth be prevented by illness from keeping an appointment.", "병은 무슨 병.” 부주교는 자기가 앓아 본 적이 없었으므로 남이라고 정말로 병 때문에 약속을 못 지킬 수 있다는 것을 이해하지 못했다."], ["He regarded all such excuses as subterfuges, and in the present instance he was not far wrong.", "그는 그런 핑계란 모조리 발뺌이라고 여겼는데, 이번 경우에는 그리 틀린 것도 아니었다."]], [["Dr. Grantly desired to be driven to his father-in-law's lodgings in the High Street, and hearing from the servant that Mr. Harding was at his daughter's, followed him to Mrs. Bold's house, and there found him. The archdeacon was fuming with rage when he got into the drawing-room, and had by this time nearly forgotten the pusillanimity of the bishop in the villainy of the chaplain.", "그랜틀리 박사는 하이 스트리트에 있는 장인의 하숙으로 가자고 일렀고, 하인에게서 하딩 씨가 따님 댁에 있다는 말을 듣고는 볼드 부인의 집까지 그를 뒤쫓아 가 거기서 그를 찾아냈다. 부주교는 응접실에 들어설 무렵 분노로 씩씩거리고 있었으며, 이때쯤에는 전속 사제의 악랄함에 밀려 주교의 비겁함은 거의 잊어버린 참이었다."]], [["\"Look at that,\"", "“이것 좀 보십시오.”"], ["said he, throwing Mr. Slope's crumpled note to Mr.", "그는 슬로프 씨의 구겨진 쪽지를 하딩 씨에게 던지며 말했다."], ["Harding. \"I am to be told that if I choose I may have the honour of seeing Mr.", "“내가 원한다면 슬로프 씨를 만나 뵙는 영광을 누려도 좋다는 소리를 듣게 생겼습니다."], ["Slope, and that too after a positive engagement with the bishop.\"", "그것도 주교와 분명하게 약속을 해 둔 다음에 말이지요.”"]], [["\"But he says the bishop is ill,\"", "“하지만 주교가 편찮다지 않은가.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Pshaw!", "“쳇!"], ["You don't mean to say that you are deceived by such an excuse as that.", "설마 그따위 핑계에 넘어가셨다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요. 어제만 해도 멀쩡했습니다."], ["He was well enough yesterday.", "자, 제가 어찌할 작정인지 말씀드리지요."], ["Now I tell you what, I will see the bishop, and I will tell him also very plainly what I think of his conduct.", "저는 주교를 만날 겁니다. 그리고 그의 처신을 어떻게 생각하는지도 아주 분명하게 말해 줄 겁니다."], ["I will see him, or else Barchester will soon be too hot to hold him.\"", "저는 기어이 그를 만나겠습니다. 그러지 않으면 바체스터가 곧 그가 발붙이기에 너무 뜨거운 곳이 되고 말 테니까요.”"]], [["Eleanor was sitting in the room, but Dr. Grantly had hardly noticed her in his anger.", "엘리너도 그 방에 앉아 있었으나 그랜틀리 박사는 성이 난 나머지 그녀를 거의 안중에 두지도 않았다."], ["Eleanor now said to him with the greatest innocence, \"I wish you had seen Mr.", "그런데 엘리너가 이제 더없이 천진하게 그에게 말했다. “슬로프 씨를 만나 보셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 그랜틀리 박사님."], ["Slope, Dr. Grantly, because I think perhaps it might have done good.\"", "어쩌면 도움이 되었을지도 모르잖아요.”"]], [["The archdeacon turned on her with almost brutal wrath.", "부주교는 거의 사나울 만큼 노해서 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["Had she at once owned that she had accepted Mr. Slope for her second husband, he could hardly have felt more convinced of her belonging body and soul to the Slope and Proudie party than he now did on hearing her express such a wish as this.", "엘리너가 그 자리에서 슬로프 씨를 두 번째 남편으로 받아들이기로 했노라 실토했다 한들, 이런 소원을 입 밖에 내는 것을 듣고 지금 확신하게 된 것보다 더 굳게, 그녀가 몸과 마음을 다 바쳐 슬로프와 프라우디 편에 속해 있다고 믿기는 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"]], [["\"See him!\"", "“만나 보라고?”"], ["said the archdeacon glaring at her.", "부주교가 그녀를 노려보며 말했다."], ["\"And why am I to be called on to lower myself in the world's esteem and my own by coming in contact with such a man as that?", "“대체 무엇 때문에 내가 그따위 자와 상대하여 세상의 평판에서도 내 자신의 눈에서도 스스로를 낮추어야 한단 말인가?"], ["I have hitherto lived among gentlemen, and do not mean to be dragged into other company by anybody.\"", "나는 여태 신사들 틈에서 살아왔고, 누가 되었든 나를 다른 부류 속으로 끌고 들어가게 둘 생각은 없네.”"]], [["Poor Mr. Harding well knew what the archdeacon meant, but Eleanor was as innocent as her own baby.", "가엾은 하딩 씨는 부주교의 말뜻을 잘 알았으나, 엘리너는 제 아기만큼이나 천진했다."], ["She could not understand how the archdeacon could consider himself to be dragged into bad company by condescending to speak to Mr. Slope for a few minutes when the interests of her father might be served by his doing so.", "아버지의 이익에 도움이 될 수 있는 마당에 슬로프 씨에게 몇 분 말을 붙여 주는 것이 어찌하여 부주교로서 스스로 나쁜 무리 속에 끌려 들어가는 일이 되는지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."]], [["\"I was talking for a full hour yesterday to Mr. Slope,\"", "“저는 어제 슬로프 씨와 꼬박 한 시간을 이야기했어요.”"], ["said she with some little assumption of dignity, \"and I did not find myself lowered by it.\"", "그녀가 짐짓 위엄을 조금 부리며 말했다. “그렇다고 제가 낮추어졌다고는 느끼지 않았고요.”"]], [["\"Perhaps not,\" said he.", "“그럴지도 모르지.”"], ["\"But if you'll be good enough to allow me, I shall judge for myself in such matters.", "그가 말했다. “하지만 자네가 너그러이 허락해 준다면, 그런 일은 내가 알아서 판단하겠네."], ["And I tell you what, Eleanor; it will be much better for you if you will allow yourself to be guided also by the advice of those who are your friends.", "그리고 내 한마디 하지, 엘리너. 자네를 벗으로 여기는 사람들의 충고에 자네도 좀 이끌리는 편이 훨씬 나을 걸세."], ["If you do not, you will be apt to find that you have no friends left who can advise you.\"", "그러지 않으면 자네에게 충고해 줄 벗이 하나도 남지 않았다는 것을 알게 되기 쉬울 테니까.”"]], [["Eleanor blushed up to the roots of her hair.", "엘리너는 머리카락 뿌리까지 붉어졌다."], ["But even now she had not the slightest idea of what was passing in the archdeacon's mind.", "그러나 그때까지도 부주교의 마음속에 무엇이 오가는지는 조금도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["No thought of love-making or love-receiving had yet found its way to her heart since the death of poor John Bold, and if it were possible that such a thought should spring there, the man must be far different from Mr. Slope that could give it birth.", "가엾은 존 볼드가 죽은 뒤로 사랑을 준다거나 받는다거나 하는 생각은 아직 그녀의 마음에 들어와 본 적이 없었고, 설령 그런 생각이 거기서 돋아날 수 있다 하더라도, 그것을 낳게 할 남자라면 슬로프 씨와는 아주 딴판인 사람이어야 했다."]], [["Nevertheless Eleanor blushed deeply, for she felt she was charged with improper conduct, and she did so with the more inward pain because her father did not instantly rally to her side--that father for whose sake and love she had submitted to be the receptacle of Mr. Slope's confidence.", "그럼에도 엘리너는 낯을 몹시 붉혔으니, 자기가 온당치 못한 처신을 했다고 책잡히고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이며, 게다가 아버지가 곧바로 제 편을 들어 주지 않았기에 속이 더욱 아팠다."], ["She had given a detailed account of all that had passed to her father, and though he had not absolutely agreed with her about Mr.", "다름 아닌 그 아버지를 위해, 그 아버지가 사랑스러워서 슬로프 씨의 속내를 받아 담는 그릇 노릇까지 감수했던 것이 아닌가."], ["Slope's views touching the hospital, yet he had said nothing to make her think that she had been wrong in talking to him.", "그녀는 오간 이야기를 낱낱이 아버지에게 들려주었고, 아버지가 요양원에 관한 슬로프 씨의 속셈을 두고 딸의 말에 온전히 동의하지는 않았다 해도, 그와 이야기를 나눈 것이 잘못이라고 여기게 할 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["She was far too angry to humble herself before her brother-in-law.", "그녀는 너무나 화가 나서 형부 앞에 스스로를 낮출 수가 없었다."], ["Indeed, she had never accustomed herself to be very abject before him, and they had never been confidential allies.", "사실 그녀는 형부 앞에서 그리 비굴하게 구는 데 길든 적이 없었고, 둘이 마음을 터놓는 한편이었던 적도 없었다."], ["\"I do not the least understand what you mean, Dr.", "“저는 무슨 말씀이신지 조금도 모르겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님.”"], ["Grantly,\" said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I do not know that I can accuse myself of doing anything that my friends should disapprove.", "“제 벗들이 못마땅해할 만한 일을 제가 했다고는 생각하지 않아요."], ["Mr. Slope called here expressly to ask what Papa's wishes were about the hospital, and as I believe he called with friendly intentions, I told him.\"", "슬로프 씨는 아버지가 요양원에 대해 어떤 뜻을 두고 계신지 물으려고 일부러 여기 온 것이고, 저는 그가 우호적인 뜻으로 왔다고 믿었기에 말해 준 거예요.”"]], [["\"Friendly intentions!\"", "“우호적인 뜻이라!”"], ["sneered the archdeacon.", "부주교가 비웃었다."]], [["\"I believe you greatly wrong Mr. Slope,\"", "“박사님은 슬로프 씨를 크게 잘못 보고 계신 것 같아요.”"], ["continued Eleanor, \"but I have explained this to Papa already; and as you do not seem to approve of what I say, Dr.", "엘리너가 말을 이었다. “하지만 이 일은 이미 아버지께 다 설명드렸어요."], ["Grantly, I will with your permission leave you and Papa together;\"", "그리고 제 말이 마음에 드시지 않는 모양이니, 그랜틀리 박사님, 허락해 주신다면 저는 물러가서 박사님과 아버지 두 분이 계시게 하겠어요.”"], ["so saying, she walked slowly out of the room.", "그렇게 말하고 그녀는 천천히 방에서 걸어 나갔다."]], [["All this made Mr. Harding very unhappy.", "이 모든 일이 하딩 씨를 몹시 언짢게 했다."], ["It was quite clear that the archdeacon and his wife had made up their minds that Eleanor was going to marry Mr. Slope.", "부주교와 그 아내가 엘리너는 슬로프 씨와 결혼할 작정이라고 마음을 굳혀 버렸다는 것은 아주 분명했다."], ["Mr. Harding could not really bring himself to think that she would do so, but yet he could not deny that circumstances made it appear that the man's company was not disagreeable to her.", "하딩 씨는 딸이 정말로 그러리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없었으나, 그러면서도 정황으로 보면 딸이 그 사내와 함께 있는 것을 싫어하지 않는 듯이 보인다는 사실만은 부인할 수 없었다."], ["She was now constantly seeing him, and yet she received visits from no other unmarried gentleman.", "딸은 요즘 노상 그를 만나면서도 다른 미혼 신사의 방문은 하나도 받지 않았다."], ["She always took his part when his conduct was canvassed, although she was aware how personally objectionable he was to her friends. Then, again, Mr.", "그의 처신이 도마에 오를 때면 딸은 늘 그의 편을 들었으니, 그 사내가 제 벗들에게 사람됨으로 얼마나 거슬리는지 알면서도 그러했다."], ["Harding felt that if she should choose to become Mrs. Slope, he had nothing that he could justly urge against her doing so.", "게다가 하딩 씨는 딸이 슬로프 부인이 되기로 마음먹는다 한들 자기로서는 그것을 나무랄 만한 정당한 근거가 하나도 없다고 느꼈다."], ["She had full right to please herself, and he, as a father, could not say that she would disgrace herself by marrying a clergyman who stood so well before the world as Mr. Slope did.", "딸에게는 제 마음에 드는 대로 할 온전한 권리가 있었고, 그는 아버지로서, 슬로프 씨만큼 세상에 번듯하게 서 있는 성직자와 결혼한다고 해서 딸이 스스로를 욕되게 하는 것이라고는 말할 수 없었다."], ["As for quarrelling with his daughter on account of such a marriage, and separating himself from her as the archdeacon had threatened to do, that, with Mr. Harding, would be out of the question.", "그런 혼사를 두고 딸과 다투는 것, 부주교가 그리하겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것처럼 딸과 인연을 끊는 것으로 말하자면, 하딩 씨에게는 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["If she should determine to marry this man, he must get over his aversion as best he could.", "딸이 그 사내와 결혼하기로 굳게 정한다면 자기는 그 혐오감을 어떻게든 견뎌 넘기는 수밖에 없었다."], ["His Eleanor, his own old companion in their old happy home, must still be the friend of his bosom, the child of his heart.", "그의 엘리너, 그 옛 행복한 집에서 함께 지내던 오랜 길동무는 여전히 그의 가슴의 벗이요 마음의 자식이어야 했다."], ["Let who would cast her off, he would not.", "누가 딸을 내치든 자기만은 내치지 않으리라."], ["If it were fated that he should have to sit in his old age at the same table with that man whom of all men he disliked the most, he would meet his fate as best he might.", "만일 늘그막에 세상 모든 사람 가운데 가장 싫어하는 그 사내와 한 식탁에 앉아야 하는 것이 정해진 운명이라면, 그는 그 운명을 할 수 있는 한 잘 맞이할 참이었다."], ["Anything to him would be preferable to the loss of his daughter.", "그에게는 딸을 잃는 것보다 무엇이든 나았다."]], [["Such being his feelings, he hardly knew how to take part with Eleanor against the archdeacon, or with the archdeacon against Eleanor.", "이러한 심경이었으므로 그는 엘리너 편에 서서 부주교에게 맞서기도, 부주교 편에 서서 엘리너에게 맞서기도 어려웠다."], ["It will be said that he should never have suspected her.--Alas!", "애초에 딸을 의심하지 말았어야 했다고들 할 것이다."], ["he never should have done so. But Mr.", "아아, 정말이지 그러지 말았어야 했다."], ["Harding was by no means a perfect character.", "그러나 하딩 씨는 결코 흠 없는 인물이 아니었다."], ["In his indecision, his weakness, his proneness to be led by others, his want of self-confidence, he was very far from being perfect. And then it must be remembered that such a marriage as that which the archdeacon contemplated with disgust, which we who know Mr.", "그 우유부단함, 그 나약함, 남에게 끌려가기 쉬운 성미, 자신에 대한 믿음의 부족에서 그는 흠 없는 것과는 거리가 멀었다."], ["Slope so well would regard with equal disgust, did not appear so monstrous to Mr. Harding because in his charity he did not hate the chaplain as the archdeacon did, and as we do.", "그리고 이것도 기억해 두어야 하니, 부주교가 진저리를 치며 떠올린 그 혼사, 슬로프 씨를 익히 아는 우리라면 마찬가지로 진저리를 쳤을 그 혼사가 하딩 씨에게는 그토록 끔찍해 보이지 않았던 것은, 그가 마음이 너그러워 부주교처럼, 또 우리처럼 그 전속 사제를 미워하지는 않았기 때문이다."]], [["He was, however, very unhappy when his daughter left the room, and he had recourse to an old trick of his that was customary to him in his times of sadness.", "그러나 딸이 방을 나갔을 때 그는 몹시 언짢았고, 그래서 슬플 때면 늘 하던 오랜 버릇에 기댔다."], ["He began playing some slow tune upon an imaginary violoncello, drawing one hand slowly backwards and forwards as though he held a bow in it, and modulating the unreal chords with the other.", "그는 있지도 않은 첼로로 느린 곡조를 켜기 시작했으니, 한 손에 활을 쥔 양 천천히 앞뒤로 그으면서 다른 손으로는 있지도 않은 화음을 짚어 나갔다."]], [["\"She'll marry that man as sure as two and two make four,\"", "“저 애는 그 사내와 결혼하고야 말 겁니다. 둘에 둘을 더하면 넷인 것만큼이나 확실하지요.”"], ["said the practical archdeacon.", "현실적인 부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"I hope not, I hope not,\"", "“아니길 바라네, 아니길 바라.”"], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["\"But if she does, what can I say to her?", "“하지만 그 애가 그리한다면 내가 무슨 말을 하겠나?"], ["I have no right to object to him.\"", "나에게는 그를 반대할 권리가 없네.”"]], [["\"No right!\"", "“권리가 없다니요!”"], ["exclaimed Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"No right as her father.", "“아비로서는 권리가 없지."], ["He is in my own profession and, for aught we know, a good man.\"", "그는 나와 같은 직분에 있는 사람이고, 우리가 아는 한으로는 좋은 사람일세.”"]], [["To this the archdeacon would by no means assent.", "부주교는 여기에 결코 동의하려 들지 않았다."], ["It was not well, however, to argue the case against Eleanor in her own drawing-room, and so they both walked forth and discussed the matter in all its bearings under the elm-trees of the close. Mr.", "그러나 엘리너의 응접실에서 그녀를 걸어 두고 따지는 것은 온당치 않았으므로, 두 사람은 함께 밖으로 나가 대성당 경내의 느릅나무 아래에서 이 일을 온갖 측면에서 논의했다."], ["Harding also explained to his son-in-law what had been the purport, at any rate the alleged purport, of Mr. Slope's last visit to the widow.", "하딩 씨는 또한 사위에게, 슬로프 씨가 지난번 그 미망인을 찾아간 목적이, 적어도 표방한 목적이 무엇이었는지를 설명해 주었다."], ["He, however, stated that he could not bring himself to believe that Mr. Slope had any real anxiety such as that he had pretended.", "그러나 그는 슬로프 씨가 짐짓 내세운 것과 같은 진심 어린 염려를 조금이라도 품고 있다고는 도저히 믿을 수 없다고 말했다."], ["\"I cannot forget his demeanour to myself,\" said Mr.", "“그자가 나를 대하던 태도를 잊을 수가 없네.”"], ["Harding, \"and it is not possible that his ideas should have changed so soon.\"", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “그리고 그 사람의 생각이 그렇게 빨리 바뀌었을 리도 없지.”"]], [["\"I see it all,\"", "“이제 다 알겠습니다.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"The sly tartuffe!", "“교활한 타르튀프 같으니!"], ["He thinks to buy the daughter by providing for the father.", "아비에게 자리를 마련해 주어 딸을 사겠다는 심산이지요."], ["He means to show how powerful he is, how good he is, and how much he is willing to do for her beaux yeux; yes, I see it all now.", "자기가 얼마나 힘이 있는지, 얼마나 착한 사람인지, 그 여자의 beaux yeux를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 마음이 있는지를 보여 주겠다는 겁니다. 그래요, 이제야 다 알겠습니다."], ["But we'll be too many for him yet, Mr. Harding;\"", "하지만 우리도 만만치 않다는 것을 보여 주지요, 하딩 씨.”"], ["he said, turning to his companion with some gravity and pressing his hand upon the other's arm.", "그는 다소 정색을 하고 길동무 쪽으로 돌아서서 그의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["\"It would, perhaps, be better for you to lose the hospital than get it on such terms.\"", "“그런 조건으로 요양원을 얻느니 차라리 잃는 편이 나을지도 모릅니다.”"]], [["\"Lose it!\" said Mr.", "“잃다니.”"], ["Harding; \"why I've lost it already.", "하딩 씨가 말했다. “나는 벌써 잃었네."], ["I don't want it.", "나는 그것을 바라지도 않아."], ["I've made up my mind to do without it.", "그것 없이 지내기로 마음을 굳혔네."], ["I'll withdraw altogether.", "아주 물러나겠어."], ["I'll just go and write a line to the bishop and tell him that I withdraw my claim altogether.\"", "지금 가서 주교에게 한 줄 적어 보내, 내 청을 아주 거두어들이겠다고 말하겠네.”"]], [["Nothing would have pleased him better than to be allowed to escape from the trouble and difficulty in such a manner.", "그런 식으로 이 성가심과 곤경에서 벗어나도 좋다는 허락을 받는 것보다 그를 더 기쁘게 할 일은 없었을 것이다."], ["But he was now going too fast for the archdeacon.", "그러나 그는 지금 부주교에게는 너무 앞서 나가고 있었다."]], [["\"No--no--no!", "“안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 안 됩니다!"], ["We'll do no such thing,\"", "그런 짓은 하지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["said Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."], ["\"We'll still have the hospital.", "“우리는 여전히 요양원을 가질 겁니다."], ["I hardly doubt but that we'll have it.", "우리가 그것을 얻으리라는 것은 거의 의심하지 않아요."], ["But not by Mr. Slope's assistance.", "다만 슬로프 씨의 도움으로는 아니지요."], ["If that be necessary, we'll lose it; but we'll have it, spite of his teeth, if we can.", "그것이 꼭 있어야 한다면 차라리 잃겠습니다. 하지만 될 수만 있다면 그자가 이를 갈든 말든 얻고야 말겠습니다."], ["Arabin will be at Plumstead to-morrow; you must come over and talk to him.\"", "애러빈이 내일 플럼스테드에 옵니다. 건너오셔서 그와 이야기를 나누셔야 합니다.”"]], [["The two now turned into the cathedral library, which was used by the clergymen of the close as a sort of ecclesiastical club-room, for writing sermons and sometimes letters; also for reading theological works and sometimes magazines and newspapers.", "이제 두 사람은 대성당 도서관으로 들어갔다. 그곳은 경내의 성직자들이 설교문을, 이따금은 편지를 쓰는 곳으로, 또 신학 서적을, 이따금은 잡지와 신문을 읽는 곳으로, 말하자면 일종의 교회 클럽방처럼 쓰이는 데였다."], ["The theological works were not disturbed, perhaps, quite as often as from the appearance of the building the outside public might have been led to expect.", "신학 서적들은 아마도 그 건물의 겉모양을 보고 바깥사람들이 짐작했을 만큼 자주 건드려지지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Here the two allies settled on their course of action.", "여기서 두 동지는 앞으로 취할 방침을 정했다."], ["The archdeacon wrote a letter to the bishop, strongly worded, but still respectful, in which he put forward his father-in-law's claim to the appointment and expressed his own regret that he had not been able to see his lordship when he called.", "부주교는 주교에게 편지를 한 통 썼는데, 말은 세게 썼으나 그래도 예의는 갖추었고, 그 안에 장인이 그 자리에 대해 지니는 권리를 내세우고 자기가 찾아갔을 때 각하를 뵙지 못한 것이 유감이라는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Of Mr. Slope he made no mention whatsoever.", "슬로프 씨에 대해서는 한마디도 언급하지 않았다."], ["It was then settled that Mr. Harding should go out to Plumstead on the following day, and after considerable discussion on the matter the archdeacon proposed to ask Eleanor there also, so as to withdraw her, if possible, from Mr.", "이어서 하딩 씨가 이튿날 플럼스테드로 나가기로 정해졌고, 이 일을 두고 한참을 논의한 끝에 부주교는 엘리너도 그리로 부르자고 제안했으니, 될 수 있으면 슬로프 씨의 관심에서 그녀를 떼어 놓자는 것이었다."], ["Slope's attentions.", "“한두 주면,”"], ["\"A week or two,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"may teach her what he is, and while she is there she will be out of harm's way.", "“그자가 어떤 사람인지 엘리너도 알게 되겠지요. 그리고 거기 있는 동안은 화를 입을 자리에서 벗어나 있는 셈입니다."], ["Mr. Slope won't come there after her.\"", "슬로프 씨가 거기까지 뒤쫓아 오지는 못할 테니까요.”"]], [["Eleanor was not a little surprised when her brother-in-law came back and very civilly pressed her to go out to Plumstead with her father.", "엘리너는 형부가 돌아와서 아버지와 함께 플럼스테드로 나가자고 아주 정중하게 청하는 것을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["She instantly perceived that her father had been fighting her battles for her behind her back.", "그녀는 아버지가 등 뒤에서 자기를 위해 싸워 주었다는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다."], ["She felt thankful to him, and for his sake she would not show her resentment to the archdeacon by refusing his invitation.", "그녀는 아버지가 고마웠고, 아버지를 생각해서라도 초대를 거절하는 식으로 부주교에게 노여움을 드러내지는 않으려 했다."], ["But she could not, she said, go on the morrow; she had an invitation to drink tea at the Stanhopes, which she had promised to accept.", "그러나 내일은 갈 수 없다고 그녀는 말했다. 스탠호프 댁에서 차를 마시자는 초대를 받았고 가겠다고 약속해 두었다는 것이었다."], ["She would, she added, go with her father on the next day, if he would wait; or she would follow him.", "그러면서 덧붙이기를, 아버지가 기다려 주신다면 모레 함께 가겠고 그러지 않으면 뒤따라가겠다고 했다."]], [["\"The Stanhopes!\"", "“스탠호프 댁이라!”"], ["said Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."], ["\"I did not know you were so intimate with them.\"", "“그 집안과 그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐군.”"]], [["\"I did not know it myself,\"", "“저도 몰랐어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"till Miss Stanhope called yesterday.", "“어제 스탠호프 양이 찾아오기 전까지는요."], ["However, I like her very much, and I have promised to go and play chess with some of them.\"", "그렇지만 저는 그분이 아주 마음에 들고, 가서 그 댁 사람들과 체스를 두기로 약속했어요.”"]], [["\"Have they a party there?\"", "“거기 사람들이 모이기라도 하는가?”"], ["said the archdeacon, still fearful of Mr. Slope.", "여전히 슬로프 씨를 꺼리는 부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, no,\"", "“아, 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"Miss Stanhope said there was to be nobody at all.", "엘리너가 말했다. “스탠호프 양이 아무도 오지 않을 거라고 했어요."], ["But she had heard that Mary had left me for a few weeks, and she had learnt from someone that I play chess, and so she came over on purpose to ask me to go in.\"", "다만 메리가 몇 주 저를 떠나 있다는 말을 들었고, 제가 체스를 둘 줄 안다는 것도 누구한테선가 알게 되어서, 저더러 오라고 청하려고 일부러 건너온 거예요.”"]], [["\"Well, that's very friendly,\"", "“그거참 다정하구나.”"], ["said the ex-warden.", "전임 워든이 말했다."], ["\"They certainly do look more like foreigners than English people, but I dare say they are none the worse for that.\"", "“그 사람들이 영국 사람보다 외국 사람처럼 보이는 것이야 확실하다만, 그렇다고 그만큼 못할 것도 없겠지.”"]], [["The archdeacon was inclined to look upon the Stanhopes with favourable eyes, and had nothing to object on the matter.", "부주교는 스탠호프 일가를 곱게 보는 편이었고 이 일에 대해서는 반대할 것이 없었다."], ["It was therefore arranged that Mr. Harding should postpone his visit to Plumstead for one day and then take with him Eleanor, the baby, and the nurse.", "그리하여 하딩 씨가 플럼스테드 방문을 하루 미루었다가 엘리너와 아기와 유모를 데리고 가기로 정해졌다."]], [["Mr. Slope is certainly becoming of some importance in Barchester.", "슬로프 씨는 확실히 바체스터에서 제법 중요한 인물이 되어 가고 있다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. Barchester by Moonlight", "# 제19장. 달빛 아래의 바체스터"]], [["There was much cause for grief and occasional perturbation of spirits in the Stanhope family, but yet they rarely seemed to be grieved or to be disturbed.", "스탠호프 집안에는 슬퍼할 까닭도, 이따금 마음이 어지러워질 까닭도 적지 않았으나, 그럼에도 이들이 슬퍼하거나 흔들리는 모습은 좀처럼 보이지 않았다."], ["It was the peculiar gift of each of them that each was able to bear his or her own burden without complaint, and perhaps without sympathy.", "저마다 제 짐을 불평 없이, 그리고 아마도 동정도 받지 않은 채 지고 갈 줄 아는 것이 이 집안 사람들 하나하나의 남다른 재주였다."], ["They habitually looked on the sunny side of the wall, if there was a gleam on either side for them to look at; if there was none, they endured the shade with an indifference which, if not stoical, answered the end at which the Stoics aimed.", "이들은 담장 어느 쪽이든 볕뉘가 비치기만 하면 늘 볕 드는 쪽을 바라보았고, 어느 쪽에도 볕이 없으면 그늘을 무심히 견뎠는데, 그 무심함은 스토아적이라고까지는 못 해도 스토아학파가 목표로 삼던 바에는 넉넉히 닿아 있었다."], ["Old Stanhope could not but feel that he had ill-performed his duties as a father and a clergyman, and could hardly look forward to his own death without grief at the position in which he would leave his family.", "늙은 스탠호프는 아버지로서도 성직자로서도 제 본분을 제대로 해내지 못했음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었고, 제가 죽고 난 뒤 식구들이 놓이게 될 처지를 생각하면 슬픔 없이 제 죽음을 내다보기가 어려웠다."], ["His income for many years had been as high as £3,000 a year, and yet they had among them no other provision than their mother's fortune of £10,000.", "여러 해 동안 그의 수입은 연 3,000파운드에 이르렀건만, 식구들에게 돌아갈 것이라고는 어머니의 재산 10,000파운드 말고는 아무것도 없었다."], ["He had not only spent his income, but was in debt.", "그는 수입을 다 써 버렸을 뿐 아니라 빚까지 지고 있었다."], ["Yet with all this he seldom showed much outward sign of trouble.", "그런데도 그는 좀처럼 근심하는 기색을 겉으로 드러내지 않았다."]], [["It was the same with the mother.", "어머니도 마찬가지였다. 그녀가 자식들의 즐거움에 보태는 것이 적었다면, 깎아내리는 것은 그보다도 적었다."], ["If she added little to the pleasures of her children, she detracted still less: she neither grumbled at her lot, nor spoke much of her past or future sufferings; as long as she had a maid to adjust her dress, and had those dresses well made, nature with her was satisfied.", "제 처지를 두고 투덜대지도 않았고 지난날이나 앞날의 고생을 입에 올리지도 않았다. 옷매무새를 만져 줄 하녀가 있고 그 옷들이 잘 지어져 있기만 하면, 그로써 그녀의 천성은 흡족했다."], ["It was the same with the children.", "자식들도 마찬가지였다."], ["Charlotte never rebuked her father with the prospect of their future poverty, nor did it seem to grieve her that she was becoming an old maid so quickly; her temper was rarely ruffled, and, if we might judge by her appearance, she was always happy.", "샬럿은 앞으로 닥칠 가난을 들어 아버지를 나무란 적이 없었고, 저 혼자 이렇게 빨리 노처녀가 되어 가는 것을 서러워하는 기색도 없었다. 좀처럼 성을 내는 법이 없었고, 겉모습으로 미루어 판단해도 좋다면 그녀는 언제나 행복했다."], ["The signora was not so sweet-tempered, but she possessed much enduring courage; she seldom complained--never, indeed, to her family.", "시뇨라는 그만큼 성정이 곱지는 않았으나 견디는 용기만은 대단했다. 좀처럼 하소연하지 않았고, 적어도 식구들 앞에서는 결코 하지 않았다."], ["Though she had a cause for affliction which would have utterly broken down the heart of most women as beautiful as she and as devoid of all religious support, yet she bore her suffering in silence, or alluded to it only to elicit the sympathy and stimulate the admiration of the men with whom she flirted.", "그만큼 아름답고 그만큼 종교적 위안이라고는 없는 여자라면 대개 마음이 아주 무너져 내렸을 만한 고통거리를 안고 있으면서도, 그녀는 그 고통을 잠자코 견뎠고, 혹 그 이야기를 꺼낸다 해도 저와 시시덕거리는 남자들의 동정을 자아내고 찬탄을 부추기기 위해서일 뿐이었다."], ["As to Bertie, one would have imagined from the sound of his voice and the gleam of his eye that he had not a sorrow nor a care in the world.", "버티로 말하자면, 그 목소리의 울림과 눈빛의 반짝임만 보아서는 이 세상에 슬픔도 근심도 하나 없는 사람이라고 여겼을 것이다. 실제로도 없었다."], ["Nor had he. He was incapable of anticipating to-morrow's griefs.", "그는 내일의 슬픔을 미리 당겨 걱정할 줄 모르는 사람이었다."], ["The prospect of future want no more disturbed his appetite than does that of the butcher's knife disturb the appetite of the sheep.", "앞날의 궁핍이 그의 식욕을 어지럽히는 일은, 푸주한의 칼이 양의 식욕을 어지럽히는 일이나 매한가지로 없었다."]], [["Such was the usual tenor of their way; but there were rare exceptions.", "그들이 살아가는 방식은 대체로 이러했으나, 드문 예외가 없지는 않았다."], ["Occasionally the father would allow an angry glance to fall from his eye, and the lion would send forth a low dangerous roar as though he meditated some deed of blood.", "이따금 아버지의 눈에서 성난 눈길이 번뜩였고, 사자는 무슨 피 보는 일이라도 궁리하는 듯 낮고 위험한 울음을 흘렸다."], ["Occasionally also Madame Neroni would become bitter against mankind, more than usually antagonistic to the world's decencies, and would seem as though she was about to break from her moorings and allow herself to be carried forth by the tide of her feelings to utter ruin and shipwreck.", "이따금 네로니 부인 또한 사람이라는 것들에게 모질어져서, 여느 때보다도 세상의 체면치레에 각을 세우고, 이제 곧 밧줄을 끊고 제 감정의 물살에 실려 파멸과 난파로 떠내려가려는 듯이 보이곤 했다."], ["She, however, like the rest of them, had no real feelings, could feel no true passion. In that was her security.", "그러나 그녀 역시 나머지 식구들과 마찬가지로 진짜 감정이라는 것이 없었고, 참된 열정을 느낄 줄 몰랐다."], ["Before she resolved on any contemplated escapade she would make a small calculation, and generally summed up that the Stanhope villa or even Barchester close was better than the world at large.", "바로 그 점이 그녀의 안전판이었다. 무슨 일탈을 마음먹기 전에 그녀는 조그맣게 셈을 해 보았고, 그 셈의 결론은 대체로 스탠호프 저택이, 아니 바체스터 대성당 경내라 해도, 바깥 넓은 세상보다는 낫다는 것이었다."]], [["They were most irregular in their hours.", "그들의 생활 시간은 몹시 제멋대로였다."], ["The father was generally the earliest in the breakfast-parlour, and Charlotte would soon follow and give him his coffee, but the others breakfasted anywhere, anyhow, and at any time. On the morning after the archdeacon's futile visit to the palace, Dr.", "아침 식당에 제일 먼저 나오는 이는 대개 아버지였고, 곧이어 샬럿이 내려와 커피를 따라 주었으나, 나머지 식구들은 아무 데서나, 아무렇게나, 아무 때나 아침을 먹었다."], ["Stanhope came downstairs with an ominously dark look about his eyebrows; his white locks were rougher than usual, and he breathed thickly and loudly as he took his seat in his armchair.", "부주교가 주교관을 헛되이 다녀간 이튿날 아침, 스탠호프 박사는 눈썹께에 심상치 않은 그늘을 드리운 채 아래층으로 내려왔다. 흰 머리칼은 여느 때보다 헝클어져 있었고, 안락의자에 앉을 때는 숨소리가 굵고 크게 새어 나왔다."], ["He had open letters in his hand, and when Charlotte came into the room, he was still reading them.", "손에는 뜯어 놓은 편지들이 들려 있었는데, 샬럿이 방에 들어왔을 때에도 그는 여전히 그것들을 읽고 있었다."], ["She went up and kissed him as was her wont, but he hardly noticed her as she did so, and she knew at once that something was the matter.", "그녀가 다가가 늘 하던 대로 입을 맞추었으나 그는 그러거나 말거나 거의 알은체를 하지 않았고, 그녀는 곧바로 무슨 일이 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["\"What's the meaning of that?\"", "“이건 대체 무슨 소리냐?”"], ["said he, throwing over the table a letter with a Milan postmark.", "그가 밀라노 소인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 식탁 너머로 던지며 말했다."], ["Charlotte was a little frightened as she took it up, but her mind was relieved when she saw that it was merely the bill of their Italian milliner.", "샬럿은 그것을 집어 들면서 조금 겁이 났으나, 그저 이탈리아 모자 가게에서 온 청구서임을 보고는 마음이 놓였다."], ["The sum total was certainly large, but not so large as to create an important row.", "합계액이 크기는 컸으나, 큰 소동이 벌어질 만큼 크지는 않았다."]], [["\"It's for our clothes, Papa, for six months before we came here.", "“저희 옷값이에요, 아버지. 여기 오기 전 여섯 달 치예요."], ["The three of us can't dress for nothing, you know.\"", "셋이서 옷을 거저 지어 입을 수야 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"Nothing, indeed!\"", "“거저라니, 원!”"], ["said he, looking at the figures which, in Milanese denominations, were certainly monstrous.", "그가 밀라노 화폐 단위로 적힌 숫자들을 들여다보며 말했다. 과연 어마어마한 액수였다."]], [["\"The man should have sent it to me,\"", "“그 사람이 저한테 보냈어야 할 것을 그랬네요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"I wish he had with all my heart--if you would have paid it.", "“나도 진심으로 그랬으면 싶구나. 네가 갚아 줄 생각이었다면 말이다."], ["I see enough in it to know that three quarters of it are for Madeline.\"", "이걸 보니 4분의 3은 마들린 몫이라는 걸 알겠다.”"]], [["\"She has little else to amuse her, sir,\"", "“마들린으로서는 달리 마음 붙일 것도 없잖아요, 아버지.”"], ["said Charlotte with true good nature.", "샬럿이 진심 어린 너그러움으로 말했다."]], [["\"And I suppose he has nothing else to amuse him,\"", "“그리고 저 녀석도 달리 마음 붙일 것이 없다는 말이렷다.”"], ["said the doctor, throwing over another letter to his daughter.", "박사가 딸에게 또 다른 편지 한 통을 던지며 말했다."], ["It was from some member of the family of Sidonia, and politely requested the father to pay a small trifle of £700, being the amount of a bill discounted in favour of Mr. Ethelbert Stanhope and now overdue for a period of nine months.", "시도니아 집안의 누군가에게서 온 것으로, 에설버트 스탠호프 씨 앞으로 할인해 준 어음이 아홉 달째 연체되었으니 그 금액인 700파운드라는 하찮은 푼돈을 갚아 달라고 아버지에게 정중히 청하는 내용이었다."]], [["Charlotte read the letter, slowly folded it up, and put it under the edge of the tea-tray.", "샬럿은 편지를 읽고 천천히 접어 찻쟁반 가장자리 밑에 밀어 넣었다."]], [["\"I suppose he has nothing to amuse him but discounting bills with Jews.", "“저 녀석은 유대인들과 어음 할인 놀음을 하는 것 말고는 마음 붙일 데가 없다는 말이지."], ["Does he think I'll pay that?\"", "내가 저걸 갚아 주리라 여기는 게냐?”"]], [["\"I am sure he thinks no such thing,\"", "“그런 생각은 결코 하지 않을 거예요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"And who does he think will pay it?\"", "“그럼 누가 갚아 주리라 여긴다는 말이냐?”"]], [["\"As far as honesty goes I suppose it won't much matter if it is never paid,\"", "“정직성만 따진다면 끝내 갚지 않는다 해도 크게 상관없을 거예요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she. \"I dare say he got very little of it.\"", "“오라비가 손에 쥔 돈은 얼마 되지도 않았을 테니까요.”"]], [["\"I suppose it won't much matter either,\"", "“상관없기야 마찬가지겠지.” 아버지가 말했다."], ["said the father, \"if he goes to prison and rots there.", "“저놈이 감옥에 들어가 거기서 썩는다 해도 말이다."], ["It seems to me that that's the other alternative.\"", "내가 보기엔 그것이 나머지 한쪽 길이다.”"]], [["Dr. Stanhope spoke of the custom of his youth.", "스탠호프 박사는 제 젊은 시절의 관행을 두고 하는 말이었다."], ["But his daughter, though she had lived so long abroad, was much more completely versed in the ways of the English world.", "그러나 딸은 그토록 오래 외국에 살았으면서도 영국 세상의 물정에 훨씬 밝았다. “그 사람이 오라비를 붙잡아 간다면,”"], ["\"If the man arrests him,\" said she, \"he must go through the court.\"", "그녀가 말했다, “오라비는 파산 법정을 거치면 그만이에요.”"]], [["It is thus, thou great family of Sidonia--it is thus that we Gentiles treat thee, when, in our extremest need, thou and thine have aided us with mountains of gold as big as lions--and occasionally with wine-warrants and orders for dozens of dressing-cases.", "위대한 시도니아 집안이여, 우리 이방인들은 그대들을 이렇게 대접하는도다. 우리가 더없이 궁한 처지에 몰렸을 때 그대와 그대의 식솔이 사자만 한 황금 산더미로, 더러는 포도주 인수증이며 화장 상자 열두 개짜리 주문서 따위로 우리를 도와주었건만."]], [["\"What, and become an insolvent?\"", "“뭐라고, 그래서 지불 불능자가 되라는 말이냐?”"], ["said the doctor.", "박사가 말했다."]], [["\"He's that already,\"", "“이미 그런걸요.”"], ["said Charlotte, wishing always to get over a difficulty.", "샬럿이 말했다. 그녀는 언제나 어려운 대목은 얼른 넘어가고 싶어 했다."]], [["\"What a condition,\"", "“꼴이 좋구나.”"], ["said the doctor, \"for the son of a clergyman of the Church of England.\"", "박사가 말했다. “영국 국교회 성직자의 아들이라는 자가.”"]], [["\"I don't see why clergymen's sons should pay their debts more than other young men,\"", "“성직자의 아들이라고 해서 다른 젊은이들보다 빚을 더 잘 갚아야 할 까닭이 있나요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"He's had as much from me since he left school as is held sufficient for the eldest son of many a nobleman,\"", "“학교를 나온 뒤로 내가 저에게 준 돈만 해도 어지간한 귀족 집 맏아들에게 넉넉하다고들 하는 액수는 되고도 남는다.”"], ["said the angry father.", "성난 아버지가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, sir,\"", "“그러시더라도 아버지,”"], ["said Charlotte, \"give him another chance.\"", "샬럿이 말했다, “한 번만 더 기회를 주세요.”"]], [["\"What!\"", "“뭐라고!”"], ["said the doctor, \"do you mean that I am to pay that Jew?\"", "박사가 말했다. “그 유대인에게 내가 돈을 갚으란 말이냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, no!", "“어머, 아니에요!"], ["I wouldn't pay him, he must take his chance; and if the worst comes to the worst, Bertie must go abroad.", "저라도 그 사람에게는 안 갚아요. 그 사람은 제 운을 받아들여야죠."], ["But I want you to be civil to Bertie and let him remain here as long as we stop.", "그리고 정 안 되면 버티는 외국으로 나가야 하고요. 하지만 아버지가 버티에게 부드럽게 대해 주시고, 우리가 여기 머무는 동안은 이 집에 두어 주셨으면 해요."], ["He has a plan in his head that may put him on his feet after all.\"", "오라비 머릿속에 계획이 하나 있는데, 그게 되면 그래도 제 발로 설 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Has he any plan for following up his profession?\"", "“제 직업을 이어 갈 계획이라도 있다더냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, he'll do that too; but that must follow.", "“아, 그것도 할 거예요. 다만 그건 나중 일이고요."], ["He's thinking of getting married.\"", "오라비는 결혼할 생각을 하고 있어요.”"]], [["Just at that moment the door opened, and Bertie came in whistling.", "바로 그때 문이 열리고 버티가 휘파람을 불며 들어왔다."], ["The doctor immediately devoted himself to his egg and allowed Bertie to whistle himself round to his sister's side without noticing him.", "박사는 즉시 제 달걀에 몰두하여, 버티가 휘파람을 불며 누이 곁으로 돌아가도록 알은체도 하지 않고 내버려 두었다."]], [["Charlotte gave a sign to him with her eye, first glancing at her father, and then at the letter, the corner of which peeped out from under the tea-tray.", "샬럿은 먼저 아버지 쪽을 흘긋 보고 다음에는 찻쟁반 밑으로 귀퉁이가 삐죽 나온 편지를 흘긋 보며 눈짓을 보냈다."], ["Bertie saw and understood, and with the quiet motion of a cat he abstracted the letter and made himself acquainted with its contents.", "버티는 그것을 보고 알아차렸고, 고양이처럼 소리 없는 몸짓으로 편지를 빼내어 그 내용을 알아 두었다."], ["The doctor, however, had seen him, deep as he appeared to be mersed in his egg-shell, and said in his harshest voice, \"Well, sir, do you know that gentleman?\"", "그러나 박사는 달걀 껍데기에 온통 빠져 있는 듯 보였어도 그것을 보고 있었고, 더할 나위 없이 거친 목소리로 말했다. “어떠냐, 네가 그 양반을 아느냐?”"]], [["\"Yes, sir,\"", "“압니다, 아버지.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"I have a sort of acquaintance with him, but none that can justify him in troubling you.", "“얼마간 아는 사이이기는 하지만, 그렇다고 그 사람이 아버지를 성가시게 할 자격이 생기는 건 아니지요."], ["If you will allow me, sir, I will answer this.\"", "허락해 주신다면 제가 이 편지에 답장을 하겠어요.”"]], [["\"At any rate I shan't,\"", "“어쨌든 나는 안 쓴다.” 아버지가 말했다."], ["said the father, and then he added, after a pause, \"Is it true, sir, that you owe the man £700?\"", "그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. “네가 그 사람에게 700파운드를 빚졌다는 것이 사실이냐?”"]], [["\"Well,\"", "“글쎄요,”"], ["said Bertie, \"I think I should be inclined to dispute the amount, if I were in a condition to pay him such of it as I really do owe him.\"", "버티가 말했다, “제가 실제로 빚진 만큼이라도 갚을 형편이 된다면, 액수는 좀 따져 보고 싶은 마음이 있습니다.”"]], [["\"Has he your bill for £700?\"", "“그 사람이 네 700파운드짜리 어음을 갖고 있느냐?”"], ["said the father, speaking very loudly and very angrily.", "아버지가 몹시 크고 몹시 성난 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I believe he has,\"", "“네, 갖고 있을 겁니다.”"], ["said Bertie, \"but all the money I ever got from him was £150.\"", "버티가 말했다. “하지만 제가 그 사람한테서 받은 돈은 통틀어 150파운드뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"And what became of the £550?\"", "“그럼 나머지 550파운드는 어찌 되었느냐?”"]], [["\"Why, sir, the commission was £100 or so, and I took the remainder in paving-stones and rocking-horses.\"", "“그게, 아버지, 수수료가 100파운드쯤 되었고요, 나머지는 포석과 흔들목마로 받았습니다.”"]], [["\"Paving-stones and rocking-horses!\"", "“포석과 흔들목마라니!”"], ["said the doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["\"Where are they?\"", "“그것들은 어디 있느냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, sir, I suppose they are in London somewhere--but I'll inquire if you wish for them.\"", "“아, 그거야 런던 어딘가에 있겠지요. 원하시면 알아보겠습니다.”"]], [["\"He's an idiot,\"", "“저놈은 얼간이다.”"], ["said the doctor, \"and it's sheer folly to waste more money on him.", "박사가 말했다. “저놈에게 돈을 더 쓰는 건 순전한 어리석음이야."], ["Nothing can save him from ruin,\"", "무엇으로도 저놈을 파멸에서 건져 내지 못한다.”"], ["and so saying, the unhappy father walked out of the room.", "그렇게 말하고 이 가엾은 아버지는 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["\"Would the governor like to have the paving-stones?\"", "“영감님이 그 포석을 갖고 싶으신 걸까?”"], ["said Bertie to his sister.", "버티가 누이에게 말했다."]], [["\"I'll tell you what,\"", "“내 말 좀 들어 봐.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"If you don't take care, you will find yourself loose upon the world without even a house over your head; you don't know him as well as I do.", "“조심하지 않으면 너는 머리 위에 지붕 하나 없이 세상에 내던져진 신세가 될 거야. 너는 아버지를 나만큼 몰라."], ["He's very angry.\"", "지금 몹시 화가 나셨어.”"]], [["Bertie stroked his big beard, sipped his tea, chatted over his misfortunes in a half-comic, half-serious tone, and ended by promising his sister that he would do his very best to make himself agreeable to the Widow Bold.", "버티는 커다란 수염을 쓰다듬으며 차를 홀짝였고, 반은 우스개로 반은 진지하게 제 불운을 늘어놓다가, 마침내 미망인 볼드 부인의 마음에 들도록 힘껏 애써 보겠다고 누이에게 약속하는 것으로 끝을 맺었다."], ["Then Charlotte followed her father to his own room, softened down his wrath, and persuaded him to say nothing more about the Jew bill discounter, at any rate for a few weeks.", "그러고 나서 샬럿은 아버지를 따라 그의 방으로 가서 노여움을 누그러뜨리고, 적어도 몇 주 동안은 그 유대인 어음 할인업자 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않도록 아버지를 구슬렸다."], ["He even went so far as to say he would pay the £700, or at any rate settle the bill, if he saw a certainty of his son's securing for himself anything like a decent provision in life.", "아버지는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 아들이 제 앞가림할 만한 살림을 확실히 마련하는 것이 눈에 보이기만 한다면 700파운드를 갚아 주겠다, 적어도 그 어음은 결말을 지어 주겠다고까지 말했다."], ["Nothing was said openly between them about poor Eleanor, but the father and the daughter understood each other.", "가엾은 엘리너에 대해서는 두 사람 사이에 아무 말도 드러내 놓고 오가지 않았으나, 아버지와 딸은 서로의 뜻을 알고 있었다."]], [["They all met together in the drawing-room at nine o'clock, in perfect good humour with each other, and about that hour Mrs. Bold was announced.", "아홉 시에 온 식구가 응접실에 모였을 때는 서로 더할 나위 없이 기분이 좋았고, 그 무렵 볼드 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다."], ["She had never been in the house before, though she had of course called, and now she felt it strange to find herself there in her usual evening dress, entering the drawing-room of these strangers in this friendly, unceremonious way, as though she had known them all her life.", "그녀는 물론 인사차 들른 적은 있었으나 이 집에 들어와 본 적은 없었으므로, 여느 저녁 옷차림 그대로 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이라도 되는 양 이 낯선 사람들의 응접실에 격의 없이 다정하게 들어서고 있는 자신이 새삼 이상하게 느껴졌다."], ["But in three minutes they made her at home.", "그러나 삼 분도 지나지 않아 그들은 그녀를 편안하게 만들어 주었다."], ["Charlotte tripped downstairs and took her bonnet from her, and Bertie came to relieve her from her shawl, and the signora smiled on her as she could smile when she chose to be gracious, and the old doctor shook hands with her in a kind benedictory manner that went to her heart at once and made her feel that he must be a good man.", "샬럿이 가볍게 내려와 보닛을 받아 들었고, 버티가 다가와 숄을 벗겨 주었고, 시뇨라는 마음먹고 상냥해질 때 지을 수 있는 그 미소를 지어 보였으며, 늙은 박사는 마음에 곧장 와닿는 자애로운 축복 같은 태도로 그녀와 악수를 하여 이분은 틀림없이 좋은 사람이라는 느낌을 갖게 했다."]], [["She had not been seated for above five minutes when the door again opened and Mr. Slope was announced.", "자리에 앉은 지 오 분이 채 못 되어 문이 다시 열리고 슬로프 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 있었다."], ["She felt rather surprised, because she was told that nobody was to be there, and it was very evident from the manner of some of them that Mr. Slope was not unexpected.", "그녀는 다른 사람은 아무도 오지 않는다고 들었던 터라 다소 놀랐고, 몇몇 사람의 태도로 보아 슬로프 씨가 뜻밖의 손님이 아님이 아주 분명했다."], ["But still there was not much in it.", "그러나 그렇다고 대수로울 것도 없었다."], ["In such invitations a bachelor or two more or less are always spoken of as nobodies, and there was no reason why Mr.", "이런 초대 자리에서는 독신 남자 한둘쯤은 늘 아무도 아닌 사람으로 치기 마련이고, 엘리너 자신이 그렇듯 슬로프 씨라고 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 차를 마시지 못할 까닭도 없었다."], ["Slope should not drink tea at Dr. Stanhope's as well as Eleanor herself.", "그러나 정작 그는 제 장래의 아내가 그 자리에 끼어 있는 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐고 그다지 반갑지도 않았다."], ["He, however, was very much surprised and not very much gratified at finding that his own embryo spouse made one of the party.", "그는 네로니 부인의 아름다움을 바라보고 그녀의 아첨을 듣고 되돌려 주는 즐거움을 누리러 온 것이었다."], ["He had come there to gratify himself by gazing on Madame Neroni's beauty and listening to and returning her flattery: and though he had not owned as much to himself, he still felt that if he spent the evening as he had intended to do, he might probably not thereby advance his suit with Mrs. Bold.", "그리고 스스로 그만큼 인정하지는 않았을지언정, 애초에 작정한 대로 저녁을 보낸다면 볼드 부인에게 거는 구애가 진척되지는 않으리라는 것쯤은 느끼고 있었다."]], [["The signora, who had no idea of a rival, received Mr. Slope with her usual marks of distinction.", "경쟁자가 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못한 시뇨라는 여느 때처럼 각별한 표시로 슬로프 씨를 맞았다."], ["As he took her hand, she made some confidential communication to him in a low voice, declaring that she had a plan to communicate to him after tea, and was evidently prepared to go on with her work of reducing the chaplain to a state of captivity.", "그가 손을 잡자 그녀는 낮은 목소리로 무언가 은밀한 이야기를 건네며 차를 마신 뒤에 그에게 알려 줄 계획이 있다고 말했으니, 전속 사제를 사로잡힌 신세로 만드는 제 작업을 계속할 채비가 되어 있음이 역력했다."], ["Poor Mr. Slope was rather beside himself.", "가엾은 슬로프 씨는 어지간히 정신이 없었다."], ["He thought that Eleanor could not but have learnt from his demeanour that he was an admirer of her own, and he had also flattered himself that the idea was not unacceptable to her.", "그는 제 태도로 보아 엘리너가 자신이 그녀를 흠모하는 사람임을 모를 리 없다고 여겼고, 게다가 그 생각이 그녀에게 싫지만은 않으리라고 혼자 흐뭇해하던 참이었다."], ["What would she think of him if he now devoted himself to a married woman!", "그런 자신이 이제 남의 아내에게 매달린다면 그녀가 자신을 어찌 여기겠는가!"]], [["But Eleanor was not inclined to be severe in her criticisms on him in this respect, and felt no annoyance of any kind, when she found herself seated between Bertie and Charlotte Stanhope. She had no suspicion of Mr.", "그러나 엘리너는 그 점에 대해 그를 모질게 나무랄 마음이 없었고, 버티와 샬럿 스탠호프 사이에 앉게 되자 아무런 언짢음도 느끼지 않았다."], ["Slope's intentions; she had no suspicion even of the suspicion of other people; but still she felt well-pleased not to have Mr.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨의 속셈을 의심하지 않았고, 남들이 그런 의심을 하고 있으리라는 것조차 의심하지 않았다."], ["Slope too near to her.", "그럼에도 슬로프 씨가 너무 가까이 앉지 않은 것이 은근히 흡족했다."]], [["And she was not ill-pleased to have Bertie Stanhope near her.", "그리고 버티 스탠호프가 곁에 있는 것도 싫지 않았다."], ["It was rarely indeed that he failed to make an agreeable impression on strangers.", "그가 처음 만나는 사람에게 호감을 주지 못하는 일이란 참으로 드물었다."], ["With a bishop indeed who thought much of his own dignity it was possible that he might fail, but hardly with a young and pretty woman.", "제 위엄을 크게 여기는 주교 앞이라면 실패할 수도 있었겠으나, 젊고 예쁜 여자 앞에서라면 그럴 일이 거의 없었다."], ["He possessed the tact of becoming instantly intimate with women without giving rise to any fear of impertinence.", "그는 무례하다는 두려움을 조금도 일으키지 않으면서 여자와 단박에 허물없어지는 재주를 지니고 있었다."], ["He had about him somewhat of the propensities of a tame cat.", "그에게는 길든 고양이의 성질 같은 것이 얼마간 있었다."], ["It seemed quite natural that he should be petted, caressed, and treated with familiar good nature, and that in return he should purr, and be sleek and graceful, and above all never show his claws.", "그가 귀염을 받고 쓰다듬어지고 스스럼없는 다정함으로 대접받는 것도, 그 답례로 그르렁대며 매끄럽고 우아하게 굴고 무엇보다 결코 발톱을 드러내지 않는 것도 아주 자연스러워 보였다."], ["Like other tame cats, however, he had his claws, and sometimes made them dangerous.", "그러나 다른 길든 고양이들과 마찬가지로 그에게도 발톱은 있었고, 때로는 그것을 위험하게 쓰기도 했다."]], [["When tea was over, Charlotte went to the open window and declared loudly that the full harvest moon was much too beautiful to be disregarded, and called them all to look at it.", "차를 다 마시자 샬럿은 열린 창가로 가서 추수철 보름달이 너무 아름다워 그냥 지나칠 수 없다고 큰 소리로 외치며 모두를 불러 보게 했다."], ["To tell the truth there was but one there who cared much about the moon's beauty, and that one was not Charlotte, but she knew how valuable an aid to her purpose the chaste goddess might become, and could easily create a little enthusiasm for the purpose of the moment.", "사실을 말하자면 그 자리에서 달의 아름다움을 그리 마음에 두는 사람은 한 사람뿐이었고, 그 한 사람은 샬럿이 아니었다. 그러나 그녀는 저 정결한 여신이 제 목적에 얼마나 값진 도움이 될 수 있는지 알고 있었고, 그 순간의 목적을 위해 얼마쯤의 열의를 지어내는 일쯤은 어렵지 않았다."], ["Eleanor and Bertie were soon with her.", "엘리너와 버티가 곧 그녀 곁으로 왔다."], ["The doctor was now quiet in his armchair, and Mrs. Stanhope in hers, both prepared for slumber.", "박사는 이제 안락의자에서 조용해졌고 스탠호프 부인도 제 의자에서 그러했으니, 둘 다 잠들 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["\"Are you a Whewellite or a Brewsterite, or a t'othermanite, Mrs. Bold?\"", "“볼드 부인은 휴얼파신가요, 브루스터파신가요, 아니면 또 다른 누구파신가요?”"], ["said Charlotte, who knew a little about everything, and had read about a third of each of the books to which she alluded.", "샬럿이 말했다. 그녀는 무엇에 대해서든 조금씩은 알았고, 지금 들먹인 책들도 각각 3분의 1쯤 읽어 두었다."]], [["\"Oh!\"", "“어머!” 엘리너가 말했다."], ["said Eleanor; \"I have not read any of the books, but I feel sure that there is one man in the moon at least, if not more.\"", "“그 책들은 하나도 읽지 못했어요. 하지만 달에는 적어도 한 사람은, 어쩌면 그보다 많은 사람이 살고 있다고 믿어요.”"]], [["\"You don't believe in the pulpy gelatinous matter?\"", "“말랑말랑한 아교질 물질은 믿지 않으시고요?”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["\"I heard about that,\"", "“그 이야기는 저도 들었어요.”"], ["said Eleanor, \"and I really think it's almost wicked to talk in such a manner.", "엘리너가 말했다. “그런 식으로 말하는 건 거의 불경한 일이 아닌가 싶어요."], ["How can we argue about God's power in the other stars from the laws which he has given for our rule in this one?\"", "이 별에서 우리를 다스리라고 하느님이 주신 법칙을 가지고 어떻게 다른 별들에서 발휘되는 그분의 권능을 따져 볼 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"How indeed!\"", "“정말이지 그렇지요!”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"Why shouldn't there be a race of salamanders in Venus?", "“금성에 도롱뇽 족속이 있으면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이겠어요?"], ["And even if there be nothing but fish in Jupiter, why shouldn't the fish there be as wide awake as the men and women here?\"", "목성에 물고기밖에 없다 한들, 그곳 물고기가 이곳 남자들이나 여자들만큼 총명하지 말라는 법은 또 어디 있고요?”"]], [["\"That would be saying very little for them,\" said Charlotte.", "“그건 그 물고기들을 아주 낮잡아 보는 말인걸요.”"], ["\"I am for Dr.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["Whewell myself, for I do not think that men and women are worth being repeated in such countless worlds.", "“나는 휴얼 박사 편이에요. 남자와 여자가 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세계마다 되풀이될 만한 값어치가 있다고는 보지 않으니까요."], ["There may be souls in other stars, but I doubt their having any bodies attached to them.", "다른 별들에 영혼이 있을지는 몰라도 거기에 몸까지 딸려 있는지는 의심스러워요."], ["But come, Mrs. Bold, let us put our bonnets on and walk round the close.", "자, 볼드 부인, 우리 보닛을 쓰고 대성당 경내를 한 바퀴 돌아요."], ["If we are to discuss sidereal questions, we shall do so much better under the towers of the cathedral than stuck in this narrow window.\"", "별에 관한 문제를 논할 거라면 이 좁은 창가에 박혀 있느니 대성당 탑 아래에서 하는 편이 훨씬 나을 테니까요.”"]], [["Mrs. Bold made no objection, and a party was made to walk out.", "볼드 부인이 마다하지 않아 산책 나갈 무리가 꾸려졌다."], ["Charlotte Stanhope well knew the rule as to three being no company, and she had therefore to induce her sister to allow Mr. Slope to accompany them.", "샬럿 스탠호프는 셋은 동행이 못 된다는 규칙을 잘 알고 있었으므로, 슬로프 씨가 함께 가도 좋다고 동생을 구슬려야 했다."]], [["\"Come, Mr. Slope,\"", "“자, 슬로프 씨,”"], ["she said, \"I'm sure you'll join us.", "그녀가 말했다, “저희와 함께 가 주시겠지요."], ["We shall be in again in a quarter of an hour, Madeline.\"", "마들린, 우린 십오 분이면 돌아올 거야.”"]], [["Madeline read in her eye all that she had to say, knew her object, and as she had to depend on her sister for so many of her amusements, she felt that she must yield.", "마들린은 언니의 눈에서 하려는 말을 다 읽어 냈고 그 속셈도 알았으며, 제 즐길 거리 대부분을 언니에게 기대고 사는 처지였으므로 물러서야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["It was hard to be left alone while others of her own age walked out to feel the soft influence of the bright night, but it would be harder still to be without the sort of sanction which Charlotte gave to all her flirtations and intrigues.", "또래의 다른 사람들이 밝은 밤의 부드러운 기운을 쐬러 나가는 동안 혼자 남겨진다는 것은 괴로운 일이었으나, 샬럿이 제 온갖 희롱과 술수에 얹어 주는 그 일종의 승인을 잃는 것은 그보다 더 괴로운 일이었다."], ["Charlotte's eye told her that she must give up just at present for the good of the family, and so Madeline obeyed.", "지금은 집안을 위해 양보해야 한다고 샬럿의 눈이 말하고 있었으므로, 마들린은 따랐다."]], [["But Charlotte's eyes said nothing of the sort to Mr. Slope.", "그러나 샬럿의 눈은 슬로프 씨에게는 그런 말을 조금도 하지 않았다."], ["He had no objection at all to the tête-à-tête with the signora which the departure of the other three would allow him, and gently whispered to her, \"I shall not leave you alone.\"", "나머지 셋이 나가고 나면 시뇨라와 단둘이 마주 앉을 수 있을 터이니 그로서는 마다할 이유가 전혀 없었고, 그래서 그는 그녀에게 나직이 속삭였다. “저는 당신을 혼자 두지 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“아니에요.”"], ["said she; \"go--pray go, pray go, for my sake.", "그녀가 말했다. “가세요."], ["Do not think that I am so selfish.", "제발 가세요, 부디 가세요, 저를 위해서요."], ["It is understood that nobody is kept within for me.", "제가 그렇게 이기적인 사람이라고는 여기지 마세요."], ["You will understand this too when you know me better.", "저 때문에 누구도 집 안에 붙들려 있지 않는다는 것이 이 집의 정해진 이치랍니다."], ["Pray join them, Mr.", "저를 좀 더 아시게 되면 이것도 아시게 될 거예요."], ["Slope, but when you come in speak to me for five minutes before you leave us.\"", "저분들과 함께 가세요, 슬로프 씨. 다만 돌아오시거든 가시기 전에 오 분만 저와 이야기해 주세요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope understood that he was to go, and he therefore joined the party in the hall.", "슬로프 씨는 자기가 가야 한다는 뜻임을 알아듣고 현관에서 일행에 합류했다."], ["He would have had no objection at all to this arrangement, if he could have secured Mrs. Bold's arm; but this of course was out of the question.", "볼드 부인의 팔짱을 낄 수만 있었다면 이 편성에도 조금도 불만이 없었을 것이나, 그것은 물론 어림없는 일이었다."], ["Indeed, his fate was very soon settled, for no sooner had he reached the hall-door than Miss Stanhope put her hand within his arm, and Bertie walked off with Eleanor just as naturally as though she were already his own property.", "아닌 게 아니라 그의 운명은 아주 금세 정해졌으니, 현관문에 이르자마자 스탠호프 양이 그의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 버티는 엘리너가 이미 제 소유물이라도 되는 양 지극히 자연스럽게 그녀를 데리고 걸어 나갔던 것이다."]], [["And so they sauntered forth: first they walked round the close, according to their avowed intent; then they went under the old arched gateway below St.", "그리하여 그들은 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔다."], ["Cuthbert's little church, and then they turned behind the grounds of the bishop's palace, and so on till they came to the bridge just at the edge of the town, from which passers-by can look down into the gardens of Hiram's Hospital; and here Charlotte and Mr.", "먼저 공언한 대로 대성당 경내를 한 바퀴 돌았고, 다음에는 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회 아래 오래된 아치형 문을 지났으며, 그다음에는 주교관 뜰 뒤편으로 돌아 나가 마침내 마을 어귀의 다리에 이르렀다."], ["Slope, who were in advance, stopped till the other two came up to them. Mr.", "이 다리에서는 지나는 사람이 히램 요양원 뜰을 내려다볼 수 있었다."], ["Slope knew that the gable-ends and old brick chimneys which stood up so prettily in the moonlight were those of Mr.", "앞서 걷던 샬럿과 슬로프 씨는 여기서 걸음을 멈추고 나머지 둘이 따라오기를 기다렸다."], ["Harding's late abode, and would not have stopped on such a spot, in such company, if he could have avoided it; but Miss Stanhope would not take the hint which he tried to give.", "달빛 속에 그토록 어여쁘게 솟아 있는 박공 끝과 오래된 벽돌 굴뚝이 하딩 씨가 얼마 전까지 살던 집의 것임을 슬로프 씨는 알고 있었고, 피할 수만 있었다면 그런 자리에서 그런 일행과 걸음을 멈추지 않았을 터이나, 스탠호프 양은 그가 넌지시 보내는 눈치를 받아 주지 않았다."]], [["\"This is a very pretty place, Mrs. Bold,\"", "“여긴 참 예쁜 곳이에요, 볼드 부인.”"], ["said Charlotte; \"by far the prettiest place near Barchester.", "샬럿이 말했다. “바체스터 근방에서는 단연 제일 예쁜 곳이지요."], ["I wonder your father gave it up.\"", "아버님께서 왜 내놓으셨는지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["It was a very pretty place, and now by the deceitful light of the moon looked twice larger, twice prettier, twice more antiquely picturesque than it would have done in truth-telling daylight.", "과연 아주 예쁜 곳이었고, 지금은 달의 속임수 어린 빛을 받아 정직한 대낮에 보이는 것보다 두 배는 크고 두 배는 예쁘고 두 배는 예스럽게 그림 같아 보였다."], ["Who does not know the air of complex multiplicity and the mysterious interesting grace which the moon always lends to old gabled buildings half-surrounded, as was the hospital, by fine trees!", "요양원이 그러했듯 좋은 나무들에 반쯤 둘러싸인 낡은 박공집에 달이 언제나 빌려주는 저 겹겹의 복잡한 기운과 신비롭고도 흥미로운 자태를 모르는 이가 어디 있으랴!"], ["As seen from the bridge on the night of which we are speaking, Mr. Harding's late abode did look very lovely, and though Eleanor did not grieve at her father's having left it, she felt at the moment an intense wish that he might be allowed to return.", "우리가 말하고 있는 그날 밤 다리 위에서 바라본 하딩 씨의 옛 거처는 참으로 사랑스러워 보였고, 엘리너는 아버지가 그곳을 떠난 것을 슬퍼하지는 않았으되 그 순간만은 아버지가 그리로 돌아갈 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["\"He is going to return to it almost immediately, is he not?\"", "“곧 그리로 돌아가시게 된다지요, 아닌가요?”"], ["asked Bertie.", "버티가 물었다."]], [["Eleanor made no immediate reply.", "엘리너는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다."], ["Many such a question passes unanswered without the notice of the questioner, but such was not now the case.", "그런 물음은 물은 사람도 알아채지 못한 채 대답 없이 지나가는 일이 흔하지만, 이번에는 그렇지 않았다."], ["They all remained silent as though expecting her to reply, and after a moment or two, Charlotte said, \"I believe it is settled that Mr.", "다들 그녀가 대답하기를 기다리는 듯 잠자코 있었고, 한두 순간이 지나자 샬럿이 말했다."], ["Harding returns to the hospital, is it not?\"", "“하딩 씨가 요양원으로 돌아가시는 것으로 정해진 줄 알고 있는데요, 아닌가요?”"]], [["\"I don't think anything about it is settled yet,\"", "“그 일은 아직 아무것도 정해지지 않은 것 같아요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"But it must be a matter of course,\"", "“하지만 그거야 당연한 일 아니겠어요.”"], ["said Bertie; \"that is, if your father wishes it.", "버티가 말했다. “아버님이 원하시기만 한다면 말이지요."], ["Who else on earth could hold it after what has occurred?\"", "그런 일이 있고 난 뒤에 세상 어느 누가 그 자리를 맡을 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["Eleanor quietly made her companion understand that the matter was one which she could not discuss in the present company, and then they passed on.", "엘리너는 그것이 지금 이 자리에서는 이야기할 수 없는 문제임을 조용히 동행에게 알렸고, 그러고서 그들은 발길을 옮겼다."], ["Charlotte said she would go a short way up the hill out of the town so as to look back upon the towers of the cathedral, and as Eleanor leant upon Bertie's arm for assistance in the walk, she told him how the matter stood between her father and the bishop.", "샬럿이 대성당 탑들을 되돌아보게 마을 밖 언덕을 조금 올라가 보자고 하여, 엘리너는 걷는 데 힘이 되도록 버티의 팔에 기대면서 아버지와 주교 사이에 일이 어찌 되어 있는지를 그에게 이야기했다."]], [["\"And, he,\"", "“그러면 저 사람은,”"], ["said Bertie, pointing on to Mr. Slope, \"what part does he take in it?\"", "버티가 앞쪽의 슬로프 씨를 가리키며 말했다, “그 일에서 어떤 몫을 맡고 있나요?”"]], [["Eleanor explained how Mr. Slope had at first endeavoured to tyrannize over her father, but how he had latterly come round and done all he could to talk the bishop over in Mr.", "엘리너는 슬로프 씨가 처음에는 아버지 위에 군림하려 들었으나 근래에는 마음을 돌려 하딩 씨에게 유리하도록 주교를 설득하는 데 힘을 다했다고 설명했다."], ["Harding's favour. \"But my father,\"", "“하지만 아버지는,”"], ["she said, \"is hardly inclined to trust him; they all say he is so arrogant to the old clergymen of the city.\"", "그녀가 말했다, “그이를 그다지 믿으실 마음이 없으세요. 그이가 이 도시의 나이 든 성직자들에게 무척 오만하게 군다고들 하거든요.”"]], [["\"Take my word for it,\"", "“제 말을 믿으세요.”"], ["said Bertie, \"your father is right.", "버티가 말했다. “아버님이 옳으십니다."], ["If I am not very much mistaken, that man is both arrogant and false.\"", "제가 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저 사람은 오만하기도 하고 거짓되기도 한 사람이에요.”"]], [["They strolled up to the top of the hill and then returned through the fields by a foot-path which leads by a small wooden bridge, or rather a plank with a rustic rail to it, over the river to the other side of the cathedral from that at which they had started.", "그들은 언덕 꼭대기까지 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔다가 들판을 가로지르는 오솔길로 돌아왔는데, 그 길은 강 위에 놓인 작은 나무다리, 아니 시골풍 난간을 붙인 널빤지 한 장을 건너 출발했던 쪽의 반대편 대성당 쪽으로 이어져 있었다."], ["They had thus walked round the bishop's grounds, through which the river runs, and round the cathedral and adjacent fields, and it was past eleven before they reached the doctor's door.", "그리하여 그들은 강이 가로질러 흐르는 주교관 뜰을 돌고, 대성당과 그 언저리 들판을 돌아 걸은 셈이었으니, 박사의 집 문 앞에 이르렀을 때는 열한 시가 넘어 있었다."]], [["\"It is very late,\"", "“너무 늦었어요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"it will be a shame to disturb your mother again at such an hour.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “이런 시각에 또 어머님을 성가시게 해서야 도리가 아니지요.”"]], [["\"Oh\"'", "“어머,”"], ["said Charlotte, laughing, \"you won't disturb Mamma; I dare say she is in bed by this time, and Madeline would be furious if you did not come in and see her.", "샬럿이 웃으며 말했다, “어머니를 성가시게 할 일은 없어요. 지금쯤은 벌써 잠자리에 드셨을걸요."], ["Come, Bertie, take Mrs.", "그리고 부인이 들어와서 얼굴을 보여 주지 않으면 마들린이 펄펄 뛸 거예요."], ["Bold's bonnet from her.\"", "자, 버티, 볼드 부인 보닛을 받아 드려.”"]], [["They went upstairs and found the signora alone, reading.", "그들이 위층으로 올라가 보니 시뇨라가 혼자 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["She looked somewhat sad and melancholy, but not more so perhaps than was sufficient to excite additional interest in the bosom of Mr.", "어딘가 슬프고 우울해 보였으나, 슬로프 씨의 가슴에 관심을 한층 더 돋우는 데 필요한 정도를 넘어서지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Slope; and she was soon deep in whispered intercourse with that happy gentleman, who was allowed to find a resting-place on her sofa.", "그리고 그녀는 곧 그 행복한 신사와 속삭이는 대화에 깊이 빠져들었으니, 그에게는 그녀의 소파에 앉을 자리가 허락되었다."], ["The signora had a way of whispering that was peculiarly her own, and was exactly the reverse of that which prevails among great tragedians.", "시뇨라에게는 오로지 그녀만의 속삭이는 법이 있었는데, 그것은 이름난 비극 배우들 사이에 통용되는 방식과는 정반대였다."], ["The great tragedian hisses out a positive whisper, made with bated breath, and produced by inarticulated tongue-formed sounds, but yet he is audible through the whole house.", "위대한 비극 배우는 숨을 죽이고 혀끝으로 빚어낸 웅얼거림을 쉿쉿 소리 나게 내뱉어 또렷한 속삭임을 만들어 내면서도 극장 전체에 들리게 한다."], ["The signora, however, used no hisses and produced all her words in a clear, silver tone, but they could only be heard by the ear into which they were poured.", "그러나 시뇨라는 쉿쉿 소리를 전혀 쓰지 않았고 모든 말을 맑고 은빛 나는 음색으로 냈으되, 그 말은 그것이 부어지는 귀에만 들릴 뿐이었다."]], [["Charlotte hurried and scurried about the room hither and thither, doing, or pretending to do many things; then, saying something about seeing her mother, ran upstairs.", "샬럿은 이리저리 방 안을 부산히 돌아다니며 이런저런 일을 하거나 하는 척했고, 그러다 어머니를 보고 오겠다는 말을 남기고 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["Eleanor was thus left alone with Bertie, and she hardly felt an hour fly by her.", "그리하여 엘리너는 버티와 단둘이 남겨졌는데, 한 시간이 어떻게 지나갔는지도 모를 지경이었다."], ["To give Bertie his due credit, he could not have played his cards better.", "버티의 공을 제대로 인정해 주자면, 그보다 더 패를 잘 놀릴 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["He did not make love to her, nor sigh, nor look languishing, but he was amusing and familiar, yet respectful; and when he left Eleanor at her own door at one o'clock, which he did by the by with the assistance of the now jealous Slope, she thought that he was one of the most agreeable men and the Stanhopes decidedly the most agreeable family that she had ever met.", "그는 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하지도, 한숨을 쉬지도, 애타는 눈길을 보내지도 않았고, 다만 재미있고 허물없으면서도 예의를 지켰다. 그리고 새벽 한 시에 그가 그녀를 집 문 앞까지 바래다주었을 때, 덧붙이자면 이제 질투가 난 슬로프의 거들음까지 받아 가며 그리했을 때, 그녀는 그가 여태 만나 본 가장 유쾌한 남자 가운데 하나이고 스탠호프 일가는 단연 가장 유쾌한 집안이라고 생각했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX. Mr. Arabin", "# 제20장. 애러빈 씨"]], [["The Rev. Francis Arabin, fellow of Lazarus, late professor of poetry at Oxford, and present vicar of St.", "라자러스의 펠로이자 전임 옥스퍼드 시학 교수이며 현재 바체스터 교구의 세인트 이월드 교구 사제인 프랜시스 애러빈 목사를, 이제는 독자에게 직접 소개해야 하겠다."], ["Ewold, in the diocese of Barchester, must now be introduced personally to the reader. He is worthy of a new volume, and as he will fill a conspicuous place in it, it is desirable that he should be made to stand before the reader's eye by the aid of such portraiture as the author is able to produce.", "그는 새 권 하나를 통째로 얻을 만한 사람이고, 앞으로 이 이야기에서 눈에 띄는 자리를 차지할 터이니, 글쓴이가 그려 낼 수 있는 만큼의 초상화를 빌려서라도 그를 독자의 눈앞에 세워 두는 편이 바람직하겠다."]], [["It is to be regretted that no mental method of daguerreotype or photography has yet been discovered by which the characters of men can be reduced to writing and put into grammatical language with an unerring precision of truthful description.", "사람의 성품을 오차 없이 정확한 서술로 글에 옮겨 문법에 맞는 언어로 담아낼 수 있는, 정신의 다게레오타이프나 사진 같은 방법이 아직 발견되지 않았다는 것은 유감스러운 일이다."], ["How often does the novelist feel, ay, and the historian also and the biographer, that he has conceived within his mind and accurately depicted on the tablet of his brain the full character and personage of a man, and that nevertheless, when he flies to pen and ink to perpetuate the portrait, his words forsake, elude, disappoint, and play the deuce with him, till at the end of a dozen pages the man described has no more resemblance to the man conceived than the sign-board at the corner of the street has to the Duke of Cambridge.", "소설가는, 아니 역사가와 전기 작가 또한, 한 인물의 성격과 사람됨을 온전히 제 마음속에 품고 제 머릿속 서판에 정확히 그려 내었다고 여기면서도, 막상 그 초상을 영구히 남기려고 펜과 잉크로 달려가는 순간 말들이 저를 저버리고 빠져나가고 실망시키며 제멋대로 굴어, 열두어 쪽을 써 놓고 보면 묘사된 사람이 마음에 품었던 사람과 닮은 정도가 길모퉁이 간판이 케임브리지 공작을 닮은 정도밖에 되지 않는다는 것을 얼마나 자주 느끼는가."]], [["And yet such mechanical descriptive skill would hardly give more satisfaction to the reader than the skill of the photographer does to the anxious mother desirous to possess an absolute duplicate of her beloved child.", "그렇다고 해서 그런 기계적인 묘사 솜씨가 독자에게 주는 만족이, 사랑하는 아이의 완전한 복제를 갖고 싶어 안달하는 어머니에게 사진사의 솜씨가 주는 만족보다 그리 크지도 않을 것이다."], ["The likeness is indeed true, but it is a dull, dead, unfeeling, inauspicious likeness.", "닮기야 참으로 닮았지만, 그것은 둔하고 죽어 있고 아무 정감도 없고 상서롭지도 못한 닮음이다."], ["The face is indeed there, and those looking at it will know at once whose image it is, but the owner of the face will not be proud of the resemblance.", "얼굴이 거기 있는 것은 분명하고, 그것을 들여다보는 사람들은 그것이 누구의 모습인지 대번에 알아보겠지만, 정작 그 얼굴의 임자는 그 닮음을 자랑스러워하지 않을 것이다."]], [["There is no royal road to learning, no short cut to the acquirement of any valuable art.", "배움에 이르는 왕도는 없고, 값진 기예를 익히는 데 지름길이란 없다."], ["Let photographers and daguerreotypers do what they will, and improve as they may with further skill on that which skill has already done, they will never achieve a portrait of the human face divine.", "사진사들과 다게레오타이프 기술자들이 무슨 짓을 하든, 이미 솜씨로 이루어 놓은 것 위에 솜씨를 더 얹어 아무리 개량한들, 그들은 신이 빚은 인간의 얼굴을 담은 초상만은 결코 이루어 내지 못할 것이다."], ["Let biographers, novelists, and the rest of us groan as we may under the burdens which we so often feel too heavy for our shoulders; we must either bear them up like men, or own ourselves too weak for the work we have undertaken.", "전기 작가와 소설가와 그 밖의 우리 모두가, 제 어깨에 너무 무겁다고 걸핏하면 느끼는 그 짐 밑에서 아무리 신음한다 해도, 우리는 사내답게 그 짐을 져 올리거나 아니면 스스로 떠맡은 일에 우리가 너무 약하다고 자인하는 수밖에 없다."], ["There is no way of writing well and also of writing easily.", "잘 쓰면서 동시에 쉽게 쓰는 길이란 없다."]], [["Labor omnia vincit improbus.", "Labor omnia vincit improbus."], ["Such should be the chosen motto of every labourer, and it may be that labour, if adequately enduring, may suffice at last to produce even some not untrue resemblance of the Rev. Francis Arabin.", "이것이야말로 모든 일꾼이 골라 지녀야 할 좌우명이니, 어쩌면 그 수고가 넉넉히 오래 견디기만 한다면 프랜시스 애러빈 목사의 그리 어긋나지 않은 닮은꼴 하나쯤은 끝내 만들어 낼 수 있을지도 모른다."]], [["Of his doings in the world, and of the sort of fame which he has achieved, enough has been already said.", "그가 세상에서 한 일과 그가 얻은 명성의 종류에 대해서는 이미 충분히 말했다."], ["It has also been said that he is forty years of age, and still unmarried.", "그가 마흔 살이고 아직 독신이라는 것도 이미 말했다."], ["He was the younger son of a country gentleman of small fortune in the north of England.", "그는 잉글랜드 북부의 재산이 넉넉지 못한 시골 신사의 둘째 아들이었다."], ["At an early age he went to Winchester, and was intended by his father for New College; but though studious as a boy, he was not studious within the prescribed limits, and at the age of eighteen he left school with a character for talent, but without a scholarship.", "어린 나이에 윈체스터로 보내졌고, 아버지는 그가 뉴 칼리지로 가기를 바랐다. 그러나 소년 시절 공부를 좋아하기는 했어도 정해진 테두리 안에서 좋아하지는 않았던 터라, 열여덟에 그는 재주 있다는 평판만 얻고 장학생 자리는 얻지 못한 채 학교를 떠났다."], ["All that he had obtained, over and above the advantage of his character, was a gold medal for English verse, and hence was derived a strong presumption on the part of his friends that he was destined to add another name to the imperishable list of English poets.", "그 평판이라는 이점 말고 그가 손에 쥔 것이라고는 영시로 받은 금메달 하나뿐이었고, 그리하여 그의 벗들 사이에서는 그가 영국 시인들의 불멸의 명단에 이름 하나를 더 보태도록 정해진 사람이라는 강한 짐작이 생겨났다."]], [["From Winchester he went to Oxford, and was entered as a commoner at Balliol.", "윈체스터에서 그는 옥스퍼드로 갔고, 베일리얼의 자비생으로 등록되었다."], ["Here his special career very soon commenced.", "여기서 그만의 남다른 이력이 곧 시작되었다."], ["He utterly eschewed the society of fast men, gave no wine-parties, kept no horses, rowed no boats, joined no rows, and was the pride of his college tutor.", "그는 방탕한 무리와의 교제를 철저히 멀리했고, 술자리를 열지 않았고, 말을 기르지 않았고, 배를 젓지 않았고, 소동에 끼어들지 않아, 칼리지 지도교수의 자랑거리였다."], ["Such at least was his career till he had taken his little go, and then he commenced a course of action which, though not less creditable to himself as a man, was hardly so much to the taste of the tutor.", "적어도 예비 시험을 치를 때까지의 그의 행로는 그러했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사람으로서 그에게 덜 명예로울 것은 없으나 지도교수의 취향에는 그다지 맞지 않는 행로를 시작했다."], ["He became a member of a vigorous debating society, and rendered himself remarkable there for humorous energy.", "그는 기세 좋은 토론회의 회원이 되어 거기서 익살스러운 기백으로 이름을 떨쳤다."], ["Though always in earnest, yet his earnestness was always droll.", "늘 진지하면서도 그의 진지함은 늘 우스꽝스러웠다."], ["To be true in his ideas, unanswerable in his syllogisms, and just in his aspirations was not enough for him.", "생각이 옳고, 삼단논법이 반박할 수 없고, 지향이 정당한 것만으로는 그에게 부족했다."], ["He had failed, failed in his own opinion as well as that of others when others came to know him, if he could not reduce the arguments of his opponents to an absurdity and conquer both by wit and reason.", "상대의 논거를 터무니없는 것으로 몰아 재치와 이치 양쪽으로 이기지 못한다면, 그는 실패한 것이었다. 남들이 그를 알게 된 뒤에 남들이 보기에도 그러했거니와, 무엇보다 그 자신이 보기에 그러했다."], ["To say that his object was ever to raise a laugh would be most untrue.", "그의 목적이 웃음을 자아내는 데 있었다고 말한다면 그보다 큰 거짓은 없을 것이다."], ["He hated such common and unnecessary evidence of satisfaction on the part of his hearers.", "그는 듣는 이들이 만족을 그렇게 흔하고 불필요한 방식으로 드러내는 것을 싫어했다."], ["A joke that required to be laughed at was, with him, not worth uttering.", "웃어 주어야만 하는 농담은 그에게 입에 올릴 값어치가 없는 것이었다."], ["He could appreciate by a keener sense than that of his ears the success of his wit, and would see in the eyes of his auditors whether or no he was understood and appreciated.", "그는 제 재치가 통했는지를 귀보다 더 예민한 감각으로 알아챌 수 있었고, 자기가 이해되고 인정되었는지 아닌지를 듣는 이들의 눈에서 읽어 냈다."]], [["He had been a religious lad before he left school.", "그는 학교를 떠나기 전부터 신앙심 깊은 소년이었다."], ["That is, he had addicted himself to a party in religion, and having done so had received that benefit which most men do who become partisans in such a cause.", "다시 말해 그는 종교의 한 당파에 스스로를 붙였고, 그렇게 한 뒤에는 그런 명분에 당파적으로 가담하는 대다수 사람이 얻는 이익을 얻었다."], ["We are much too apt to look at schism in our church as an unmitigated evil.", "우리는 우리 교회의 분열을 오로지 악한 것으로만 보려는 버릇이 너무 심하다."], ["Moderate schism, if there may be such a thing, at any rate calls attention to the subject, draws in supporters who would otherwise have been inattentive to the matter, and teaches men to think upon religion.", "온건한 분열이라는 것이 있을 수 있다면, 그런 분열은 어쨌든 그 주제로 사람들의 눈길을 돌리고, 그렇지 않았으면 그 문제에 무심했을 지지자들을 끌어들이며, 사람들에게 종교를 두고 생각하는 법을 가르친다."], ["How great an amount of good of this description has followed that movement in the Church of England which commenced with the publication of Froude's Remains!", "프루드의 유고집 간행과 함께 시작된 잉글랜드 교회의 그 운동이 이런 종류의 선을 얼마나 크게 뒤따라 낳았던가!"]], [["As a boy young Arabin took up the cudgels on the side of the Tractarians, and at Oxford he sat for a while at the feet of the great Newman.", "소년 시절 어린 애러빈은 트랙트파 편에 서서 몽둥이를 들었고, 옥스퍼드에서는 한동안 저 위대한 뉴먼의 발치에 앉았다."], ["To this cause he lent all his faculties.", "이 명분에 그는 제 능력을 남김없이 바쳤다."], ["For it he concocted verses, for it he made speeches, for it he scintillated the brightest sparks of his quiet wit.", "이를 위해 시를 지었고, 이를 위해 연설을 했고, 이를 위해 그의 조용한 재치가 낼 수 있는 가장 밝은 불꽃을 튀겼다."], ["For it he ate and drank and dressed and had his being.", "이를 위해 그는 먹고 마시고 옷을 입고 존재했다."], ["In due process of time he took his degree and wrote himself B.A., but he did not do so with any remarkable amount of academical éclat.", "때가 되어 그는 학위를 받고 스스로를 문학사라 적었으나, 학문상의 이렇다 할 광채와 더불어 그리한 것은 아니었다."], ["He had occupied himself too much with High Church matters and the polemics, politics, and outward demonstrations usually concurrent with High Churchmanship to devote himself with sufficient vigour to the acquisition of a double first.", "그는 고교회파의 문제와, 고교회파에 흔히 따라붙는 논쟁과 정치와 바깥으로의 과시에 너무 많이 매달린 나머지, 양과 최우등을 얻는 데 충분한 힘을 쏟지 못했다. 그는 양과 최우등도 아니었고 일등급조차 아니었다."], ["He was not a double first, nor even a first class man, but he revenged himself on the university by putting firsts and double firsts out of fashion for the year and laughing down a species of pedantry which, at the age of twenty-three, leaves no room in a man's mind for graver subjects than conic sections or Greek accents.", "그러나 그해에는 일등급이니 양과 최우등이니 하는 것을 유행에서 몰아내고, 스물셋의 나이에 사람의 머릿속에 원뿔 곡선이나 그리스어 액센트보다 더 무거운 주제가 들어설 자리를 남기지 않는 그런 현학 취미를 웃음거리로 만들어 버림으로써 대학에 앙갚음을 했다."]], [["Greek accents, however, and conic sections were esteemed necessaries at Balliol, and there was no admittance there for Mr. Arabin within the list of its fellows.", "그러나 그리스어 액센트와 원뿔 곡선은 베일리얼에서 필수로 여겨지는 것이었으니, 그곳 펠로 명단 안에는 애러빈 씨가 들어설 자리가 없었다."], ["Lazarus, however, the richest and most comfortable abode of Oxford dons, opened its bosom to the young champion of a church militant.", "그러나 옥스퍼드 교수들의 거처 가운데 가장 부유하고 가장 안락한 라자러스가 이 싸우는 교회의 젊은 투사에게 품을 열어 주었다."], ["Mr. Arabin was ordained, and became a fellow soon after taking his degree, and shortly after that was chosen professor of poetry.", "애러빈 씨는 서품을 받았고, 학위를 받은 지 얼마 되지 않아 펠로가 되었으며, 그 뒤 오래지 않아 시학 교수로 뽑혔다."]], [["And now came the moment of his great danger.", "그리고 이제 그의 큰 위기의 순간이 닥쳤다."], ["After many mental struggles, and an agony of doubt which may be well surmised, the great prophet of the Tractarians confessed himself a Roman Catholic.", "숱한 마음의 다툼과, 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있는 의심의 고뇌를 겪은 끝에, 트랙트파의 저 위대한 예언자가 스스로 로마 가톨릭 교도임을 고백한 것이다."], ["Mr. Newman left the Church of England and with him carried many a waverer.", "뉴먼 씨는 잉글랜드 교회를 떠났고 흔들리던 이들 여럿을 데리고 갔다."], ["He did not carry off Mr. Arabin, but the escape which that gentleman had was a very narrow one.", "애러빈 씨를 데려가지는 못했으나, 그 신사가 벗어난 것은 참으로 아슬아슬한 일이었다."], ["He left Oxford for awhile that he might meditate in complete peace on the step which appeared to him to be all but unavoidable, and shut himself up in a little village on the sea-shore of one of our remotest counties, that he might learn by communing with his own soul whether or no he could with a safe conscience remain within the pale of his mother church.", "그는 자신에게 거의 피할 수 없어 보이는 그 한 걸음을 완전한 평온 속에서 곱씹어 보려고 한동안 옥스퍼드를 떠났고, 우리나라에서 가장 외진 주들 가운데 한 곳의 바닷가 작은 마을에 스스로를 가두었다. 제 영혼과 마주 이야기함으로써, 자기가 어머니 교회의 울타리 안에 양심을 상하지 않고 남아 있을 수 있는지 없는지를 배워 보려는 것이었다."]], [["Things would have gone badly with him there had he been left entirely to himself.", "그가 그곳에서 오로지 저 혼자에게만 맡겨졌더라면 사정은 나쁘게 흘러갔을 것이다."], ["Everything was against him: all his worldly interests required him to remain a Protestant, and he looked on his worldly interests as a legion of foes, to get the better of whom was a point of extremest honour.", "모든 것이 그에게 불리했다. 그의 세속적 이해관계는 모조리 그가 프로테스탄트로 남아 있기를 요구했는데, 그는 제 세속적 이해관계를 한 무리의 적으로 보았고 그들을 이기는 것을 더없이 명예로운 일로 여겼다."], ["In his then state of ecstatic agony such a conquest would have cost him little; he could easily have thrown away all his livelihood; but it cost him much to get over the idea that by choosing the Church of England he should be open in his own mind to the charge that he had been led to such a choice by unworthy motives.", "황홀한 고뇌에 잠긴 그때의 그로서는 그런 승리쯤 별 대가가 들지 않았을 것이고, 생계 전부를 내던지는 일쯤이야 쉽게 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 잉글랜드 교회를 택함으로써 자기가 그런 선택으로 이끌린 것은 떳떳지 못한 동기 때문이었다는 비난에 제 마음속에서 스스로 열려 있게 되리라는 생각, 그 생각을 넘어서는 데에는 큰 대가가 들었다."], ["Then his heart was against him: he loved with a strong and eager love the man who had hitherto been his guide, and yearned to follow his footsteps.", "그다음으로 그의 마음이 그에게 불리했다. 그는 그때까지 제 인도자였던 사람을 강하고 간절한 사랑으로 사랑했고, 그 발자취를 따르기를 애타게 바랐다."], ["His tastes were against him: the ceremonies and pomps of the Church of Rome, their august feasts and solemn fasts, invited his imagination and pleased his eye.", "그의 취향이 그에게 불리했다. 로마 교회의 예식과 화려함, 그 장엄한 축일과 엄숙한 금식은 그의 상상을 부르고 그의 눈을 즐겁게 했다."], ["His flesh was against him: how great an aid would it be to a poor, weak, wavering man to be constrained to high moral duties, self-denial, obedience, and chastity by laws which were certain in their enactments, and not to be broken without loud, palpable, unmistakable sin!", "그의 육신이 그에게 불리했다. 가난하고 약하고 흔들리는 사람에게, 규정이 분명하여 크고 뚜렷하고 명백한 죄를 짓지 않고서는 어길 수 없는 법으로 높은 도덕적 의무와 극기와 순종과 정결을 강제받는다는 것이 얼마나 큰 도움이 되겠는가!"], ["Then his faith was against him: he required to believe so much; panted so eagerly to give signs of his belief; deemed it so insufficient to wash himself simply in the waters of Jordan; that some great deed, such as that of forsaking everything for a true Church, had for him allurements almost past withstanding.", "그다음으로 그의 신앙이 그에게 불리했다. 그는 너무나 많은 것을 믿어야 했고, 제 믿음의 표를 내보이기를 그토록 간절히 바랐으며, 그저 요단강 물에 몸을 씻는 것만으로는 턱없이 부족하다고 여겼기에, 참된 교회를 위하여 모든 것을 버린다는 따위의 큰 행실에는 그로서는 거의 뿌리치기 어려운 유혹이 담겨 있었다."]], [["Mr. Arabin was at this time a very young man, and when he left Oxford for his far retreat was much too confident in his powers of fence, and too apt to look down on the ordinary sense of ordinary people, to expect aid in the battle that he had to fight from any chance inhabitants of the spot which he had selected.", "애러빈 씨는 그때 아주 젊은 사람이었고, 저 먼 은거지로 가려고 옥스퍼드를 떠날 무렵에는 제 검술 실력을 너무 믿었고 보통 사람들의 보통 분별을 너무 얕보는 편이어서, 자기가 골라잡은 그 고장에 어쩌다 사는 이들에게서 싸움에 도움을 받으리라고는 기대하지 않았다."], ["But Providence was good to him; there, in that all but desolate place, on the storm-beat shore of that distant sea, he met one who gradually calmed his mind, quieted his imagination, and taught him something of a Christian's duty.", "그러나 섭리는 그에게 어지셨다. 그 황량하다시피 한 곳, 저 멀고 먼 바다의 폭풍에 시달리는 물가에서 그는 한 사람을 만났으니, 그이가 차츰 그의 마음을 가라앉히고 그의 상상을 잠재우며 그리스도인의 본분이 무엇인지를 얼마간 가르쳐 주었다."], ["When Mr. Arabin left Oxford, he was inclined to look upon the rural clergymen of most English parishes almost with contempt.", "애러빈 씨가 옥스퍼드를 떠날 때만 해도 그는 잉글랜드 대다수 교구의 시골 성직자들을 거의 경멸에 가까운 눈으로 보는 편이었다."], ["It was his ambition, should he remain within the fold of their church, to do somewhat towards redeeming and rectifying their inferiority and to assist in infusing energy and faith into the hearts of Christian ministers, who were, as he thought, too often satisfied to go through life without much show of either.", "그가 그들 교회의 우리 안에 남는다면, 그들의 열등함을 얼마간 되살리고 바로잡는 데 힘을 보태며, 열정도 믿음도 별로 내보이지 않은 채 한평생을 지나는 것으로 곧잘 만족한다고 그가 생각하던 그리스도의 종들의 가슴에 열정과 믿음을 불어넣는 일을 거드는 것이 그의 포부였다."]], [["And yet it was from such a one that Mr. Arabin in his extremest need received that aid which he so much required.", "그런데도 애러빈 씨가 더없이 절박했을 때 그토록 절실히 필요로 하던 도움을 받은 것은 바로 그런 사람에게서였다."], ["It was from the poor curate of a small Cornish parish that he first learnt to know that the highest laws for the governance of a Christian's duty must act from within and not from without; that no man can become a serviceable servant solely by obedience to written edicts; and that the safety which he was about to seek within the gates of Rome was no other than the selfish freedom from personal danger which the bad soldier attempts to gain who counterfeits illness on the eve of battle.", "그리스도인의 본분을 다스리는 가장 높은 법은 밖에서가 아니라 안에서 작용해야 한다는 것, 글로 적힌 명령에 복종하는 것만으로 쓸모 있는 종이 될 수 있는 사람은 없다는 것, 그리고 그가 로마의 성문 안에서 구하려 하던 그 안전이란 싸움 전날 밤에 꾀병을 부려 몸을 사리는 못난 병사가 얻어 내려는 이기적인 위험 모면과 다를 바 없다는 것을, 그는 콘월의 작은 교구의 가난한 부목사에게서 처음 배웠다."]], [["Mr. Arabin returned to Oxford a humbler but a better and a happier man, and from that time forth he put his shoulder to the wheel as a clergyman of the Church for which he had been educated.", "애러빈 씨는 더 겸손해진, 그러나 더 나아지고 더 행복해진 사람이 되어 옥스퍼드로 돌아왔고, 그때부터 자기가 그 안에서 교육받은 교회의 성직자로서 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대었다."], ["The intercourse of those among whom he familiarly lived kept him staunch to the principles of that system of the Church to which he had always belonged.", "그가 스스럼없이 어울려 지내는 이들과의 교제는 그가 늘 속해 있던 그 교회 체계의 원칙에 그를 굳게 붙들어 두었다."], ["Since his severance from Mr. Newman, no one had had so strong an influence over him as the head of his college.", "뉴먼 씨와 갈라선 뒤로 그에게 그토록 강한 영향을 끼친 사람은 그가 속한 칼리지의 수장 말고는 없었다."], ["During the time of his expected apostasy Dr. Gwynne had not felt much predisposition in favour of the young fellow.", "그가 배교하리라 여겨지던 시절에 그윈 박사는 그 젊은 펠로를 그리 좋게 볼 마음이 없었다."], ["Though a High Churchman himself within moderate limits, Dr. Gwynne felt no sympathy with men who could not satisfy their faiths with the Thirty-nine Articles.", "그윈 박사 자신도 온건한 테두리 안에서는 고교회파였으나, 39개조 신조로 제 믿음을 채우지 못하는 사람들에게는 아무 공감도 느끼지 않았다."], ["He regarded the enthusiasm of such as Newman as a state of mind more nearly allied to madness than to religion, and when he saw it evinced by very young men, he was inclined to attribute a good deal of it to vanity. Dr.", "그는 뉴먼 같은 이들의 열광을 종교라기보다 광기에 더 가까운 정신 상태로 여겼고, 아주 젊은 사람들이 그런 열광을 내보이는 것을 볼 때면 그 상당 부분을 허영 탓으로 돌리는 편이었다."], ["Gwynne himself, though a religious man, was also a thoroughly practical man of the world, and he regarded with no favourable eye the tenets of anyone who looked on the two things as incompatible.", "그윈 박사 자신은 신앙심 있는 사람이면서도 철저히 실제적인 세상 사람이기도 했으니, 그 둘을 양립할 수 없는 것으로 보는 이의 주장이라면 어떤 것이든 곱게 보지 않았다."], ["When he found that Mr. Arabin was a half Roman, he began to regret all he had done towards bestowing a fellowship on so unworthy a recipient; and when again he learnt that Mr.", "애러빈 씨가 반쯤 로마 쪽 사람이라는 것을 알았을 때, 그는 그토록 자격 없는 이에게 펠로 자리를 주려고 자기가 애쓴 일을 죄다 후회하기 시작했다."], ["Arabin would probably complete his journey to Rome, he regarded with some satisfaction the fact that in such case the fellowship would be again vacant.", "그리고 애러빈 씨가 아마도 로마까지 가는 길을 끝내 다 갈 모양이라는 말을 다시 들었을 때는, 그렇게 되면 그 펠로 자리가 다시 비게 되리라는 사실을 얼마간 흡족한 마음으로 바라보았다."]], [["When, however, Mr. Arabin returned and professed himself a confirmed Protestant, the Master of Lazarus again opened his arms to him, and gradually he became the pet of the college.", "그러나 애러빈 씨가 돌아와 스스로를 확고한 프로테스탄트라고 밝히자, 라자러스 학장은 다시 그에게 팔을 벌렸고, 그는 차츰 칼리지의 귀염둥이가 되었다."], ["For some little time he was saturnine, silent, and unwilling to take any prominent part in university broils, but gradually his mind recovered, or rather made its tone, and he became known as a man always ready at a moment's notice to take up the cudgels in opposition to anything that savoured of an evangelical bearing.", "얼마 동안 그는 침울하고 말이 없었으며 대학의 다툼에서 눈에 띄는 몫을 맡기를 꺼렸다. 그러나 차츰 그의 마음은 제 가락을 되찾았고, 아니 오히려 제 가락을 새로 빚어냈고, 그는 복음주의 냄새가 나는 것이라면 무엇에든 맞서 당장이라도 몽둥이를 들 채비가 되어 있는 사람으로 알려지게 되었다."], ["He was great in sermons, great on platforms, great at after-dinner conversations, and always pleasant as well as great.", "그는 설교에서 뛰어났고, 연단에서 뛰어났고, 만찬 뒤의 담소에서 뛰어났으며, 뛰어난 만큼 늘 유쾌하기도 했다."], ["He took delight in elections, served on committees, opposed tooth and nail all projects of university reform, and talked jovially over his glass of port of the ruin to be anticipated by the Church and of the sacrilege daily committed by the Whigs.", "그는 선거를 즐겼고, 위원회에 몸담았고, 대학 개혁이라는 계획이란 계획에는 이를 악물고 맞섰으며, 포트와인 잔을 기울이며 교회에 닥치리라 예상되는 파멸과 휘그당이 날마다 저지르는 신성 모독을 두고 흥겹게 떠들었다."], ["The ordeal through which he had gone in resisting the blandishments of the lady of Rome had certainly done much towards the strengthening of his character.", "로마라는 귀부인의 온갖 유혹을 물리치면서 그가 겪은 그 시련은 분명 그의 성품을 굳히는 데 크게 이바지했다."], ["Although in small and outward matters he was self-confident enough, nevertheless in things affecting the inner man he aimed at a humility of spirit which would never have been attractive to him but for that visit to the coast of Cornwall.", "자잘하고 겉으로 드러나는 일에서는 그가 제법 자신만만했으나, 그럼에도 속사람에 관계된 일에서는 그 콘월 해안을 다녀오지 않았더라면 결코 마음이 끌리지 않았을 영혼의 겸손을 목표로 삼았다."], ["This visit he now repeated every year.", "그 방문을 그는 이제 해마다 되풀이했다."]], [["Such is an interior view of Mr. Arabin at the time when he accepted the living of St.", "세인트 이월드의 성직록을 받아들일 무렵의 애러빈 씨를 안에서 들여다보면 이러했다."], ["Ewold. Exteriorly, he was not a remarkable person.", "겉으로는 그는 눈에 띄는 사람이 아니었다."], ["He was above the middle height, well-made, and very active.", "중키보다 크고, 몸이 잘 짜였으며, 매우 활동적이었다."], ["His hair, which had been jet black, was now tinged with gray, but his face bore no sign of years.", "새까맣던 머리털은 이제 잿빛이 섞였으나, 얼굴에는 세월의 자취가 없었다."], ["It would perhaps be wrong to say that he was handsome, but his face was nevertheless pleasant to look upon.", "그를 잘생겼다고 하는 것은 아마 틀린 말이겠으나, 그럼에도 그의 얼굴은 바라보기에 좋았다."], ["The cheek-bones were rather too high for beauty, and the formation of the forehead too massive and heavy: but the eyes, nose, and mouth were perfect.", "광대뼈는 아름답다고 하기에는 좀 지나치게 높았고, 이마의 생김새는 지나치게 육중하고 무거웠다. 그러나 눈과 코와 입은 나무랄 데가 없었다."], ["There was a continual play of lambent fire about his eyes, which gave promise of either pathos or humour whenever he essayed to speak, and that promise was rarely broken.", "그의 눈가에는 잔불이 끊임없이 어른거려, 그가 입을 열려 할 때마다 애틋함이든 익살이든 무언가를 약속했고, 그 약속이 깨지는 일은 드물었다."], ["There was a gentle play about his mouth which declared that his wit never descended to sarcasm, and that there was no ill-nature in his repartee.", "입가에는 부드러운 움직임이 있어, 그의 재치가 결코 빈정거림으로 내려앉지 않으며 그의 응수에는 심술이 없다는 것을 말해 주었다."]], [["Mr. Arabin was a popular man among women, but more so as a general than a special favourite.", "애러빈 씨는 여자들에게 인기 있는 사람이었으나, 어느 한 사람의 총아라기보다는 두루 사랑받는 편이었다."], ["Living as a fellow at Oxford, marriage with him had been out of the question, and it may be doubted whether he had ever allowed his heart to be touched.", "옥스퍼드에서 펠로로 사는 터라 그에게 결혼이란 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었고, 그가 제 마음을 누구에게 건드리게 허락한 적이 한 번이라도 있었는지는 의심스럽다."], ["Though belonging to a church in which celibacy is not the required lot of its ministers, he had come to regard himself as one of those clergymen to whom to be a bachelor is almost a necessity.", "성직자에게 독신이 요구되는 몫이 아닌 교회에 속해 있으면서도, 그는 스스로를 홀아비로 지내는 것이 거의 필수인 그런 성직자 가운데 하나로 여기게 되었다."], ["He had never looked for parochial duty, and his career at Oxford was utterly incompatible with such domestic joys as a wife and nursery.", "그는 교구의 직무를 바란 적이 없었고, 옥스퍼드에서의 그의 이력은 아내와 아이 방 같은 가정의 기쁨과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것이었다."], ["He looked on women, therefore, in the same light that one sees them regarded by many Romish priests.", "그러므로 그는 여자들을 로마 교회의 많은 사제들이 여자를 바라보는 것과 같은 눈으로 보았다."], ["He liked to have near him that which was pretty and amusing, but women generally were little more to him than children.", "그는 예쁘고 재미있는 것을 가까이 두기를 좋아했으나, 여자들이란 대체로 그에게 아이들보다 그리 나을 것이 없었다."], ["He talked to them without putting out all his powers, and listened to them without any idea that what he should hear from them could either actuate his conduct or influence his opinion.", "그는 제 힘을 다 내지 않은 채 그들과 이야기했고, 그들에게서 듣는 말이 제 행동을 움직이거나 제 의견에 영향을 주리라고는 생각조차 하지 않은 채 그들의 말을 들었다."]], [["Such was Mr. Arabin, the new vicar of St. Ewold, who is going to stay with the Grantlys at Plumstead Episcopi.", "세인트 이월드의 새 교구 사제이자 이제 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서 그랜틀리 일가와 함께 지내려 하는 애러빈 씨는 이러한 사람이었다."]], [["Mr. Arabin reached Plumstead the day before Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨와 엘리너보다 하루 먼저 플럼스테드에 닿았고, 덕분에 그랜틀리 집안은 다른 손님들이 오기 전에 그와 인사를 나누고 그의 됨됨이를 두고 이야기할 수 있었다."], ["Harding and Eleanor, and the Grantly family were thus enabled to make his acquaintance and discuss his qualifications before the arrival of the other guests.", "그리젤은 그가 그토록 젊어 보이는 것에 놀랐으나, 밤에 물러가 잠자리에 들면서 동생 플로린다에게 그가 조금도 젊은 사람처럼 말하지는 않는다고 일러 주었다."], ["Griselda was surprised to find that he looked so young, but she told Florinda her younger sister, when they had retired for the night, that he did not talk at all like a young man: and she decided with the authority that seventeen has over sixteen that he was not at all nice, although his eyes were lovely.", "그리고 열일곱이 열여섯에게 지니는 권위를 담아, 눈은 사랑스럽지만 그 사람은 조금도 근사하지 않다고 판정했다."], ["As usual, sixteen implicitly acceded to the dictum of seventeen in such a matter, and said that he certainly was not nice.", "여느 때처럼 열여섯은 그런 문제에서 열일곱의 판결에 두말없이 따랐고, 그가 확실히 근사하지 않다고 말했다."], ["They then branched off on the relative merits of other clerical bachelors in the vicinity, and both determined without any feeling of jealousy between them that a certain Rev. Augustus Green was by many degrees the most estimable of the lot.", "그러고 나서 둘은 이웃에 사는 다른 독신 성직자들의 상대적 장점으로 이야기를 옮겨 갔고, 서로 아무 시샘도 없이, 어거스터스 그린이라는 목사가 그 무리 가운데 단연 가장 높이 살 만한 사람이라고 뜻을 모았다."], ["The gentleman in question had certainly much in his favour, as, having a comfortable allowance from his father, he could devote the whole proceeds of his curacy to violet gloves and unexceptionable neck ties.", "문제의 신사에게는 유리한 점이 분명 많았으니, 아버지에게서 넉넉한 용돈을 받는 터라 부목사직에서 나오는 수입을 통째로 보랏빛 장갑과 나무랄 데 없는 넥타이에 쏟아부을 수 있었던 것이다."], ["Having thus fixedly resolved that the new-comer had nothing about him to shake the pre-eminence of the exalted Green, the two girls went to sleep in each other's arms, contented with themselves and the world.", "이렇게 새로 온 사람에게는 그 드높은 그린의 우월함을 흔들 만한 구석이 하나도 없다고 굳게 결론을 내린 뒤, 두 소녀는 서로를 껴안은 채 저희 자신과 세상에 흡족해하며 잠이 들었다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly at first sight came to much the same conclusion about her husband's favourite as her daughters had done, though, in seeking to measure his relative value, she did not compare him to Mr.", "그랜틀리 부인도 첫눈에 남편이 아끼는 이 사람에 대해 딸들과 거의 같은 결론에 이르렀다."], ["Green; indeed, she made no comparison by name between him and anyone else; but she remarked to her husband that one person's swans were very often another person's geese, thereby clearly showing that Mr.", "다만 그의 상대적 값어치를 재려 하면서 그를 그린 씨와 견주지는 않았고, 사실 그와 다른 누구의 이름을 대어 견주는 일 자체를 하지 않았다."], ["Arabin had not yet proved his qualifications in swanhood to her satisfaction.", "그저 남편에게 어떤 사람의 백조가 다른 사람에게는 흔히 거위이더라고 한마디 했을 뿐인데, 그것은 애러빈 씨가 아직 자기가 만족할 만큼 백조다움을 증명해 보이지 못했다는 뜻을 분명히 드러내는 말이었다."]], [["\"Well, Susan,\"", "“이보시오, 수전.”"], ["said he, rather offended at hearing his friend spoken of so disrespectfully, \"if you take Mr.", "벗이 그렇게 함부로 이야기되는 것을 듣고 적잖이 언짢아진 그가 말했다."], ["Arabin for a goose, I cannot say that I think very highly of your discrimination.\"", "“당신이 애러빈 씨를 거위로 친다면, 나로서는 당신의 안목을 그리 높이 평가한다고 말할 수가 없구려.”"]], [["\"A goose!", "“거위라뇨!"], ["No, of course, he's not a goose.", "아뇨, 물론 그이는 거위가 아니지요."], ["I've no doubt he's a very clever man.", "아주 영리한 분인 것은 저도 의심하지 않아요."], ["But you're so matter-of-fact, Archdeacon, when it suits your purpose, that one can't trust oneself to any façon de parler.", "하지만 부주교님은 필요하다 싶으면 어찌나 곧이곧대로 받아들이시는지, 말이라는 게 그저 façon de parler라는 걸 도무지 믿고 쓸 수가 없다니까요."], ["I've no doubt Mr. Arabin is a very valuable man--at Oxford--and that he'll be a good vicar at St.", "애러빈 씨가 아주 값진 사람인 것도, 옥스퍼드에서는 말이지요, 세인트 이월드의 좋은 교구 사제가 되리라는 것도 저는 의심하지 않아요."], ["Ewold. All I mean is that, having passed one evening with him, I don't find him to be absolutely a paragon.", "제 말은 그저 하룻저녁을 함께 지내 본 것으로는 그이가 아주 완벽한 본보기라고까지는 느껴지지 않는다는 거예요."], ["In the first place, if I am not mistaken, he is a little inclined to be conceited.\"", "우선 첫째로, 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그이는 좀 잘난 체하는 구석이 있어요.”"]], [["\"Of all the men that I know intimately,\"", "“내가 가깝게 아는 사람들을 통틀어,”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"Arabin is, in my opinion, the most free from any taint of self-conceit.", "부주교가 말했다. “애러빈만큼 자만의 티가 조금도 없는 사람은 없다고 나는 생각하오."], ["His fault is that he's too diffident.\"", "그의 흠이라면 지나치게 스스로를 낮춘다는 것이지.”"]], [["\"Perhaps so,\"", "“그럴지도 모르지요.”"], ["said the lady; \"only I must own I did not find it out this evening.\"", "부인이 말했다. “다만 저는 오늘 저녁에 그걸 알아보지 못했다는 것만은 인정해야겠어요.”"]], [["Nothing further was said about him. Dr. Grantly thought that his wife was abusing Mr. Arabin merely because he had praised him, and Mrs. Grantly knew that it was useless arguing for or against any person in favour of or in opposition to whom the archdeacon had already pronounced a strong opinion.", "그에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 그랜틀리 박사는 자기가 애러빈 씨를 칭찬했다는 그 이유만으로 아내가 그를 헐뜯는 것이라고 생각했고, 그랜틀리 부인은 부주교가 이미 강한 의견을 내놓은 사람을 두고 그를 편들어서든 거슬러서든 왈가왈부해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["In truth, they were both right.", "사실 두 사람 다 옳았다."], ["Mr. Arabin was a diffident man in social intercourse with those whom he did not intimately know; when placed in situations which it was his business to fill, and discussing matters with which it was his duty to be conversant, Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 가깝게 알지 못하는 이들과 어울리는 자리에서는 스스로를 낮추는 사람이었다."], ["Arabin was from habit brazen-faced enough.", "제가 맡아 채워야 할 자리에 놓이고 제가 마땅히 통달해 있어야 할 문제를 논할 때라면, 애러빈 씨는 몸에 밴 대로 얼굴빛 하나 바뀌지 않을 만큼 뻔뻔했다."], ["When standing on a platform in Exeter Hall, no man would be less mazed than he by the eyes of the crowd before him, for such was the work which his profession had called on him to perform; but he shrank from a strong expression of opinion in general society, and his doing so not uncommonly made it appear that he considered the company not worth the trouble of his energy.", "엑서터 홀의 연단에 설 때면 앞에 늘어선 군중의 눈길에 그만큼 덜 얼떨떨해할 사람도 없었으니, 그것이야말로 그의 직분이 그에게 요구해 온 일이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 여느 사교 자리에서 의견을 강하게 드러내기를 꺼렸고, 그렇게 하다 보니 그가 그 자리에 모인 사람들을 제 기운을 쏟을 만한 값어치가 없다고 여기는 것처럼 보이는 일이 드물지 않았다."], ["He was averse to dictate when the place did not seem to him to justify dictation, and as those subjects on which people wished to hear him speak were such as he was accustomed to treat with decision, he generally shunned the traps there were laid to allure him into discussion, and, by doing so, not infrequently subjected himself to such charges as those brought against him by Mrs. Grantly.", "그는 그 자리가 단정 지어 말할 만하다고 여겨지지 않을 때 단정 지어 말하기를 꺼렸고, 사람들이 그의 말을 듣고 싶어 하는 주제란 하필 그가 늘 딱 잘라 다루어 오던 것들이었으므로, 그는 자기를 논쟁으로 꾀어내려고 놓인 덫을 대개 피해 다녔으며, 그리함으로써 그랜틀리 부인이 그에게 씌운 것과 같은 혐의를 심심찮게 뒤집어쓰곤 했다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, as he sat at his open window, enjoying the delicious moonlight and gazing at the gray towers of the church, which stood almost within the rectory grounds, little dreamed that he was the subject of so many friendly or unfriendly criticisms.", "애러빈 씨는 열어 둔 창가에 앉아 감미로운 달빛을 즐기며, 목사관 뜰 안이나 다름없는 자리에 서 있는 교회의 잿빛 탑들을 바라보면서, 자기가 그토록 여러 우호적이거나 비우호적인 품평의 대상이 되었으리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 않았다."], ["Considering how much we are all given to discuss the characters of others, and discuss them often not in the strictest spirit of charity, it is singular how little we are inclined to think that others can speak ill-naturedly of us, and how angry and hurt we are when proof reaches us that they have done so.", "우리 모두가 남의 성품을 논하기를 얼마나 좋아하는지, 게다가 그것을 논할 때 자비의 정신을 그리 엄격히 지키지 않는 경우가 얼마나 잦은지를 생각하면, 남들도 우리를 심술궂게 말할 수 있다는 데에는 우리가 어쩌면 그토록 생각이 미치지 않는지, 그리고 그들이 그리했다는 증거가 우리 귀에 들어오면 우리가 어쩌면 그토록 성내고 상처받는지, 참으로 묘한 일이다."], ["It is hardly too much to say that we all of us occasionally speak of our dearest friends in a manner in which those dearest friends would very little like to hear themselves mentioned, and that we nevertheless expect that our dearest friends shall invariably speak of us as though they were blind to all our faults, but keenly alive to every shade of our virtues.", "우리는 누구나 이따금 가장 소중한 벗들을 두고 그 벗들이 제 이야기가 그렇게 오르내리는 것을 결코 달가워하지 않을 투로 말하면서도, 정작 그 가장 소중한 벗들은 우리 흠에는 죄다 눈이 멀고 우리 덕의 아무리 옅은 결에도 예민한 사람인 양 언제나 우리를 이야기해 주기를 기대한다고 말한들, 지나친 말은 아닐 것이다."]], [["It did not occur to Mr. Arabin that he was spoken of at all.", "애러빈 씨는 자기가 입에 오르내린다는 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["It seemed to him, when he compared himself with his host, that he was a person of so little consequence to any, that he was worth no one's words or thoughts.", "자신을 이 집 주인과 견주어 볼 때, 그는 자기가 누구에게든 하도 대수롭지 않은 사람이라 아무의 말이나 생각을 받을 값어치도 없다고 여겨졌다."], ["He was utterly alone in the world as regarded domestic ties and those inner familiar relations which are hardly possible between others than husbands and wives, parents and children, or brothers and sisters.", "그는 가정의 인연으로 보나, 남편과 아내나 부모와 자식이나 형제자매 사이가 아니고서는 거의 있을 수 없는 저 안쪽의 스스럼없는 관계로 보나, 이 세상에 철저히 혼자였다."], ["He had often discussed with himself the necessity of such bonds for a man's happiness in this world, and had generally satisfied himself with the answer that happiness in this world is not a necessity.", "사람이 이 세상에서 행복하려면 그런 유대가 꼭 있어야 하는가를 그는 스스로와 자주 따져 보았고, 이 세상에서의 행복이란 꼭 있어야 하는 것이 아니라는 대답으로 대개 스스로를 달래 왔다. 이 점에서 그는 스스로를 속였다."], ["Herein he deceived himself, or rather tried to do so.", "아니, 속이려고 애썼다는 편이 옳겠다."], ["He, like others, yearned for the enjoyment of whatever he saw enjoyable, and though he attempted, with the modern stoicism of so many Christians, to make himself believe that joy and sorrow were matters which here should be held as perfectly indifferent, these things were not indifferent to him.", "그도 남들처럼 눈에 즐거워 보이는 것이면 무엇이든 누리고 싶어 애가 탔고, 그토록 많은 그리스도인이 지닌 저 현대의 극기로써 기쁨과 슬픔이란 이 세상에서 아주 대수롭지 않게 여겨야 할 문제라고 스스로 믿어 보려 애썼으나, 그런 것들은 그에게 대수롭지 않지 않았다."], ["He was tired of his Oxford rooms and his college life.", "그는 옥스퍼드의 제 방과 칼리지 생활에 지쳐 있었다."], ["He regarded the wife and children of his friend with something like envy; he all but coveted the pleasant drawing-room, with its pretty windows opening on to lawns and flower-beds, the apparel of the comfortable house, and--above all--the air of home which encompassed it all.", "그는 벗의 아내와 아이들을 부러움에 가까운 마음으로 바라보았고, 잔디밭과 화단으로 열린 예쁜 창이 달린 그 정겨운 응접실과, 안락한 집의 온갖 살림과, 무엇보다도 그 모든 것을 감싸고 있는 가정의 공기를 탐내다시피 했다."]], [["It will be said that no time can have been so fitted for such desires on his part as this, when he had just possessed himself of a country parish, of a living among fields and gardens, of a house which a wife would grace.", "이제 막 시골 교구를, 들과 뜰 사이의 성직록을, 아내가 들어와 빛낼 만한 집을 손에 넣은 지금이야말로 그런 바람이 그에게 그토록 어울리는 때는 없었으리라고 사람들은 말할 것이다."], ["It is true there was a difference between the opulence of Plumstead and the modest economy of St. Ewold, but surely Mr.", "플럼스테드의 넉넉함과 세인트 이월드의 검소한 살림 사이에 차이가 있는 것은 사실이나, 애러빈 씨야 설마 재물을 두고 한숨지을 사람이던가!"], ["Arabin was not a man to sigh after wealth! Of all men, his friends would have unanimously declared he was the last to do so.", "모든 사람 가운데서도 그가 그럴 사람으로는 가장 거리가 멀다고 그의 벗들은 입을 모아 잘라 말했을 것이다."], ["But how little our friends know us!", "그러나 우리 벗들이 우리를 아는 것이 얼마나 얕은가!"], ["In his period of stoical rejection of this world's happiness, he had cast from him as utter dross all anxiety as to fortune.", "이 세상의 행복을 극기의 마음으로 물리치던 시절에 그는 재산에 대한 근심이라면 죄다 찌꺼기로 여겨 제게서 내던져 버렸다."], ["He had, as it were, proclaimed himself to be indifferent to promotion, and those who chiefly admired his talents, and would mainly have exerted themselves to secure to them their deserved reward, had taken him at his word.", "그는 이를테면 스스로 승진에 무심하다고 선언한 셈이었고, 그의 재능을 누구보다 아끼며 그 재능에 마땅한 보답이 돌아가도록 앞장서 애썼을 이들은 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였다."], ["And now, if the truth must out, he felt himself disappointed--disappointed not by them but by himself.", "그런데 이제, 사실대로 밝혀야 한다면, 그는 스스로 실망하고 있었다. 그들에게 실망한 것이 아니라 자신에게 실망한 것이다."], ["The daydream of his youth was over, and at the age of forty he felt that he was not fit to work in the spirit of an apostle.", "젊은 날의 백일몽은 끝났고, 마흔의 나이에 그는 자기가 사도의 정신으로 일하기에 걸맞은 사람이 아니라고 느꼈다."], ["He had mistaken himself, and learned his mistake when it was past remedy.", "그는 스스로를 잘못 보았고, 그 잘못을 깨달았을 때는 이미 손쓸 수 없는 뒤였다."], ["He had professed himself indifferent to mitres and diaconal residences, to rich livings and pleasant glebes, and now he had to own to himself that he was sighing for the good things of other men on whom, in his pride, he had ventured to look down.", "그는 주교의 관에도, 수석사제관에도, 넉넉한 성직록과 기름진 교회 땅에도 무심하다고 공언해 왔건만, 이제는 제가 우쭐한 마음으로 감히 내려다보았던 다른 사람들의 그 좋은 것들을 두고 한숨짓고 있음을 스스로 인정해야 했다."]], [["Not for wealth, in its vulgar sense, had he ever sighed; not for the enjoyment of rich things had he ever longed; but for the allotted share of worldly bliss which a wife, and children, and happy home could give him, for that usual amount of comfort which he had ventured to reject as unnecessary for him, he did now feel that he would have been wiser to have searched.", "속된 뜻의 재물을 그가 탐하여 한숨지은 적은 결코 없었고, 값진 물건을 누려 보겠다고 애태운 적도 결코 없었다. 다만 아내와 아이들과 행복한 가정이 그에게 줄 수 있었을 저 몫으로 정해진 세상의 복, 제게는 필요 없다며 감히 물리쳐 버렸던 저 남들만큼의 안락함, 그것만은 찾아 나섰더라면 더 지혜로웠으리라고 그는 이제 느끼고 있었다."]], [["He knew that his talents, his position, and his friends would have won for him promotion, had he put himself in the way of winning it.", "그는 제 재능과 제 자리와 제 벗들이 있으니 승진을 얻어 낼 길로 스스로를 세우기만 했더라면 승진을 얻어 냈으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Instead of doing so, he had allowed himself to be persuaded to accept a living which would give him an income of some £300 a year should he, by marrying, throw up his fellowship.", "그런데 그리하는 대신 그는 설득에 넘어가, 장가를 들어 펠로 자리를 내놓을 경우 연 300파운드쯤의 수입을 안겨 줄 성직록을 받아들이고 말았다."], ["Such, at the age of forty, was the worldly result of labour which the world had chosen to regard as successful.", "세상이 성공했다고 여기기로 한 그 수고의 세속적 결실이란 마흔의 나이에 그러했다."], ["The world also thought that Mr. Arabin was, in his own estimation, sufficiently paid.", "세상은 또한 애러빈 씨가 제 생각으로는 넉넉히 보상받았다고 여겼다."], ["Alas! Alas!", "아아, 아아!"], ["The world was mistaken, and Mr. Arabin was beginning to ascertain that such was the case.", "세상이 잘못 안 것이었고, 애러빈 씨는 사정이 그러하다는 것을 이제 막 알아차리기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["And here may I beg the reader not to be hard in his judgement upon this man.", "그리고 여기서 독자에게 청하거니와, 이 사람을 두고 모질게 판단하지는 말아 달라."], ["Is not the state at which he has arrived the natural result of efforts to reach that which is not the condition of humanity?", "그가 이른 이 상태란 인간의 조건이 아닌 것에 닿아 보려는 노력에서 자연히 따라 나오는 결과가 아니겠는가?"], ["Is not modern stoicism, built though it be on Christianity, as great an outrage on human nature as was the stoicism of the ancients?", "현대의 극기는, 비록 그리스도교 위에 세워졌다고는 하나, 옛사람들의 극기가 그러했던 것만큼이나 인간 본성에 대한 큰 횡포가 아니겠는가?"], ["The philosophy of Zeno was built on true laws, but on true laws misunderstood and therefore misapplied.", "제논의 철학은 참된 법칙 위에 세워졌으나, 잘못 이해되고 따라서 잘못 적용된 참된 법칙 위에 세워진 것이었다."], ["It is the same with our Stoics here, who would teach us that wealth and worldly comfort and happiness on earth are not worth the search.", "재물과 세상의 안락과 이 땅에서의 행복은 찾아 나설 값어치가 없다고 우리에게 가르치려 드는 이곳의 우리 스토아 학자들도 마찬가지다."], ["Alas, for a doctrine which can find no believing pupils and no true teachers!", "아아, 믿고 배울 제자도 없고 참되이 가르칠 스승도 없는 저 가르침이여!"]], [["The case of Mr. Arabin was the more singular, as he belonged to a branch of the Church of England well inclined to regard its temporalities with avowed favour, and had habitually lived with men who were accustomed to much worldly comfort.", "애러빈 씨의 경우는 더욱 유별났으니, 그가 속한 잉글랜드 교회의 갈래는 제 세속 재산을 드러내 놓고 호의로 바라보는 쪽이었고, 그 자신 세상의 안락을 넉넉히 누리는 데 익숙한 사람들과 늘 어울려 살아왔기 때문이다."], ["But such was his idiosyncrasy that these very facts had produced within him, in early life, a state of mind that was not natural to him.", "그러나 그의 기질이 유별난 탓에 바로 그런 사정이 도리어 그의 안에, 그것도 젊은 시절에, 그에게 본래 어울리지 않는 마음 상태를 낳고 말았다."], ["He was content to be a High Churchman, if he could be so on principles of his own and could strike out a course showing a marked difference from those with whom he consorted.", "그는 고교회파가 되는 것에 만족하기는 하되 제 나름의 원칙 위에서 그리할 수 있고, 함께 어울리는 이들과 뚜렷이 다른 길을 스스로 내어 갈 수 있어야 했다."], ["He was ready to be a partisan as long as he was allowed to have a course of action and of thought unlike that of his party.", "그는 당파의 일원이 될 채비가 되어 있었으나 그것은 제 당파와 다른 행동과 생각의 길을 허락받는 동안뿐이었다."], ["His party had indulged him, and he began to feel that his party was right and himself wrong, just when such a conviction was too late to be of service to him.", "그의 당파는 그를 너그러이 봐주었고, 그가 제 당파는 옳고 제가 그르다고 느끼기 시작한 것은 하필 그런 확신이 그에게 아무 도움도 되지 못할 만큼 때늦었을 무렵이었다."], ["He discovered, when such discovery was no longer serviceable, that it would have been worth his while to have worked for the usual pay assigned to work in this world and have earned a wife and children, with a carriage for them to sit in; to have earned a pleasant dining-room, in which his friends could drink his wine, and the power of walking up the high street of his country town, with the knowledge that all its tradesmen would have gladly welcomed him within their doors.", "그런 깨달음이 더는 쓸모없어진 뒤에야 그는 깨달았다. 이 세상에서 일에 매겨지는 여느 삯을 받으려고 일하여 아내와 아이들을, 그리고 그들을 태울 마차를 벌어 두는 편이, 벗들이 제 포도주를 마실 수 있는 정겨운 식당을 벌어 두고, 제 고장 읍의 큰길을 걸어갈 때 상인이란 상인이 죄다 제 가게 안으로 기꺼이 맞아들이리라는 것을 알고 걸을 수 있는 힘을 벌어 두는 편이 제게도 값어치 있는 일이었으리라고."], ["Other men arrived at those convictions in their start in life and so worked up to them.", "다른 사람들은 인생의 출발점에서 그런 확신에 이르러 그것을 향해 일해 나간다."], ["To him they had come when they were too late to be of use.", "그에게 그 확신은 쓸모가 있기에는 너무 늦게 찾아왔다."]], [["It has been said that Mr. Arabin was a man of pleasantry, and it may be thought that such a state of mind as that described would be antagonistic to humour.", "애러빈 씨가 유쾌한 사람이라는 말을 앞에서 했거니와, 앞서 말한 것과 같은 마음 상태는 익살과 서로 어긋나는 것이라고 여겨질지도 모르겠다."], ["But surely such is not the case.", "그러나 분명 그렇지 않다."], ["Wit is the outward mental casing of the man, and has no more to do with the inner mind of thoughts and feelings than have the rich brocaded garments of the priest at the altar with the asceticism of the anchorite below them, whose skin is tormented with sackcloth and whose body is half-flayed with rods.", "재치란 사람의 정신을 감싼 겉껍데기이니, 제단에 선 사제의 값진 비단 예복이 그 아래 거친 삼베옷에 살갗이 쓸리고 매질에 몸이 반쯤 벗겨진 은수자의 고행과 관계가 없는 것처럼, 재치도 생각과 감정이 자리한 속마음과는 관계가 없다."], ["Nay, will not such a one often rejoice more than any other in the rich show of his outer apparel?", "아니, 그런 사람일수록 제 겉옷의 화려한 자태를 누구보다 더 기뻐하는 일이 잦지 않던가?"], ["Will it not be food for his pride to feel that he groans inwardly while he shines outwardly?", "겉으로는 빛나면서 속으로는 신음하고 있다고 느끼는 것이 그의 자부심에 먹이가 되지 않던가?"], ["So it is with the mental efforts which men make.", "사람이 기울이는 정신의 노력도 그와 같다."], ["Those which they show forth daily to the world are often the opposites of the inner workings of the spirit.", "그들이 날마다 세상에 내보이는 노력은 흔히 그 영혼 안쪽에서 일어나는 일의 정반대다."]], [["In the archdeacon's drawing-room, Mr.", "부주교의 응접실에서 애러빈 씨는 여느 때와 다름없이 꾸밈없는 광채로 반짝였다."], ["Arabin had sparkled with his usual unaffected brilliancy, but when he retired to his bedroom, he sat there sad, at his open window, repining within himself that he also had no wife, no bairns, no soft sward of lawn duly mown for him to lie on, no herd of attendant curates, no bowings from the banker's clerks, no rich rectory.", "그러나 침실로 물러가 열어 둔 창가에 앉았을 때 그는 서글펐고, 자기에게도 아내가 없고 아이들이 없으며, 누워 볼 만하게 알맞추 깎아 놓은 부드러운 잔디밭도 없고, 곁에서 시중드는 부목사 떼도 없고, 은행원들의 굽실거림도 없고, 넉넉한 목사관도 없다는 것을 속으로 한탄했다."], ["That apostleship that he had thought of had evaded his grasp, and he was now only vicar of St. Ewold's, with a taste for a mitre.", "그가 마음에 그리던 그 사도직은 그의 손아귀를 빠져나갔고, 이제 그는 주교의 관에 마음이 있으면서도 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제일 뿐이었다."], ["Truly he had fallen between two stools.", "참으로 그는 두 걸상 사이에 주저앉은 꼴이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI. St. Ewold's Parsonage", "# 제21장. 세인트 이월드 목사관"]], [["When Mr. Harding and Mrs.", "이튿날 아침 하딩 씨와 볼드 부인이 목사관에 이르렀을 때, 부주교와 그의 벗은 세인트 이월드에 가 있었다."], ["Bold reached the rectory on the following morning, the archdeacon and his friend were at St. Ewold's.", "새 교구 사제가 제 교회를 둘러보고 지주에게 소개받도록 하려고 건너간 것이어서, 저녁 식사 전에는 돌아오지 않을 참이었다."], ["They had gone over that the new vicar might inspect his church and be introduced to the squire, and were not expected back before dinner.", "하딩 씨는 혼자 어슬렁어슬렁 나가 플럼스테드에서 늘 그러듯 잔디밭을 거닐고 교회 둘레를 돌았다."], ["Mr. Harding rambled out by himself and strolled, as was his wont at Plumstead, about the lawn and round the church; and as he did so, the two sisters naturally fell into conversation about Barchester.", "그러는 사이 두 자매는 자연히 바체스터 이야기로 접어들었다."]], [["There was not much sisterly confidence between them. Mrs.", "두 자매 사이에 속을 터놓는 정이 그리 두터운 것은 아니었다."], ["Grantly was ten years older than Eleanor, and had been married while Eleanor was yet a child.", "그랜틀리 부인은 엘리너보다 열 살이 위였고, 엘리너가 아직 어린아이였을 적에 이미 시집을 갔다."], ["They had never, therefore, poured into each other's ears their hopes and loves; and now that one was a wife and the other a widow, it was not probable that they would begin to do so.", "그러므로 둘은 서로의 귀에 제 소망이며 사랑을 쏟아부어 본 적이 한 번도 없었으며, 이제 하나는 아내가 되고 하나는 미망인이 된 마당에 새삼 그런 일을 시작할 성싶지도 않았다."], ["They lived too much asunder to be able to fall into that kind of intercourse which makes confidence between sisters almost a necessity; moreover, that which is so easy at eighteen is often very difficult at twenty-eight.", "자매 사이의 흉허물 없음을 거의 필연으로 만드는 그런 왕래를 나누기에는 둘은 너무 떨어져 살았다. 게다가 열여덟 살에는 그토록 쉬운 일이 스물여덟 살에는 흔히 몹시 어려운 법이다."], ["Mrs. Grantly knew this, and did not, therefore, expect confidence from her sister; yet she longed to ask her whether in real truth Mr.", "그랜틀리 부인도 그것을 알았으므로 동생에게서 속내를 기대하지는 않았다."], ["Slope was agreeable to her.", "그러면서도 정말로 슬로프 씨가 동생의 마음에 드는 것인지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["It was by no means difficult to turn the conversation to Mr. Slope.", "화제를 슬로프 씨 쪽으로 돌리기란 조금도 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["That gentleman had become so famous at Barchester, had so much to do with all clergymen connected with the city, and was so specially concerned in the affairs of Mr.", "그 신사는 바체스터에서 어찌나 이름이 났는지, 그 도시에 연고를 둔 성직자란 성직자와는 모두 얽혀 있었고, 특히 하딩 씨의 일에는 유독 깊이 관여하고 있었으니, 하딩 씨의 딸들이 그 이야기를 하지 않았다면 오히려 이상했을 것이다."], ["Harding, that it would have been odd if Mr. Harding's daughters had not talked about him.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이내 그를 헐뜯기 시작했고 그것도 온 마음을 다해 헐뜯었으며, 볼드 부인은 그에 못지않게 열심히 그를 두둔했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly was soon abusing him, which she did with her whole heart, and Mrs.", "엘리너는 그 사내가 분명히 싫었고, 그가 어디론가 사라져 다시는 볼 일이 없게 되었다는 소식을 들었다면 기뻐했을 것이며, 실은 그를 거의 두려워하기까지 했다."], ["Bold was nearly as eager to defend him. She positively disliked the man, would have been delighted to learn that he had taken himself off so that she should never see him again, had indeed almost a fear of him, and yet she constantly found herself taking his part.", "그런데도 정신을 차려 보면 늘 그의 편을 들고 있었다."], ["The abuse of other people, and abuse of a nature that she felt to be unjust, imposed this necessity on her, and at last made Mr. Slope's defence an habitual course of argument with her.", "남들이 그를 헐뜯는 것, 그것도 부당하다고 느껴지는 투로 헐뜯는 것이 엘리너에게 그런 노릇을 떠맡겼고, 마침내 슬로프 씨를 변호하는 일은 그의 입에 붙은 논법이 되고 말았다."]], [["From Mr. Slope the conversation turned to the Stanhopes, and Mrs.", "슬로프 씨에서 이야기는 스탠호프 일가로 옮아갔다."], ["Grantly was listening with some interest to Eleanor's account of the family, when it dropped out that Mr. Slope made one of the party.", "그랜틀리 부인이 그 집안에 대한 엘리너의 설명을 자못 흥미롭게 듣고 있는데, 그 자리에 슬로프 씨도 한몫 끼여 있었다는 말이 무심코 튀어나왔다."]], [["\"What!\"", "“뭐라고?”"], ["said the lady of the rectory.", "목사관의 안주인이 말했다."], ["\"Was Mr. Slope there too?\"", "“슬로프 씨도 거기 있었단 말이냐?”"]], [["Eleanor merely replied that such had been the case.", "엘리너는 그저 그랬노라고만 대답했다."]], [["\"Why, Eleanor, he must be very fond of you, I think; he seems to follow you everywhere.\"", "“얘, 엘리너, 내가 보기엔 그 사람이 너를 무척 좋아하는 게 틀림없구나. 네가 어디를 가든 따라다니는 것 같으니 말이다.”"]], [["Even this did not open Eleanor's eyes. She merely laughed, and said that she imagined Mr.", "이런 말에도 엘리너의 눈은 뜨이지 않았다."], ["Slope found other attraction at Dr. Stanhope's.", "그저 웃으며, 슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 다른 끌림을 찾은 모양이더라고 말했을 뿐이다."], ["And so they parted. Mrs.", "그리고 두 사람은 그렇게 헤어졌다."], ["Grantly felt quite convinced that the odious match would take place, and Mrs. Bold as convinced that that unfortunate chaplain, disagreeable as he must be allowed to be, was more sinned against than sinning.", "그랜틀리 부인은 그 꼴사나운 혼사가 기어이 이루어지고야 말리라고 굳게 믿게 되었고, 볼드 부인은 그 딱한 전속 사제가 밉살스러운 인물임은 인정할 수밖에 없다 해도 지은 죄보다 뒤집어쓴 죄가 더 많은 사람이라고 그만큼 굳게 믿게 되었다."]], [["The archdeacon of course heard before dinner that Eleanor had remained the day before in Barchester with the view of meeting Mr.", "부주교는 물론 저녁 식사 전에 이미 들어 알고 있었다. 엘리너가 전날 바체스터에 남은 것이 슬로프 씨를 만날 요량에서였다는 것, 그리고 실제로 만났다는 것을."], ["Slope, and that she had so met him. He remembered how she had positively stated that there were to be no guests at the Stanhopes, and he did not hesitate to accuse her of deceit.", "그는 엘리너가 스탠호프 댁에는 손님이 없을 것이라고 딱 잘라 말하던 일을 떠올렸고, 서슴없이 그를 속임수로 몰았다."], ["Moreover, the fact, or rather presumed fact, of her being deceitful on such a matter spoke but too plainly in evidence against her as to her imputed crime of receiving Mr. Slope as a lover.", "더구나 그런 일로 속임수를 썼다는 사실은, 아니 썼으리라는 짐작은, 슬로프 씨를 연인으로 받아들였다는 그 죄목에 대하여 너무나도 뚜렷한 증거가 되어 그에게 불리하게 말하고 있었다."]], [["\"I am afraid that anything we can do will be too late,\"", "“우리가 무슨 수를 쓰든 이미 늦은 것이 아닌가 싶소.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"I own I am fairly surprised.", "“솔직히 말해 나도 적잖이 놀랐소."], ["I never liked your sister's taste with regard to men, but still I did not give her credit for--ugh!\"", "당신 동생이 사내를 보는 눈이야 전부터 마음에 든 적이 없었지만, 그래도 설마 그 정도까지야 하고 보아 주었는데, 어휴!”"]], [["\"And so soon, too,\"", "“게다가 이렇게 빨리요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["Grantly, who thought more, perhaps, of her sister's indecorum in having a lover before she had put off her weeds than her bad taste in having such a lover as Mr. Slope.", "그는 어쩌면 동생이 슬로프 씨 같은 사내를 연인으로 삼은 그 안목보다, 상복을 채 벗기도 전에 연인을 두었다는 그 무례함을 더 마음에 두고 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["\"Well, my dear, I shall be sorry to be harsh, or to do anything that can hurt your father; but, positively, neither that man nor his wife shall come within my doors.\"", "“여보, 나도 모질게 굴고 싶지는 않고, 장인어른 마음을 상하게 할 일은 더더욱 하고 싶지 않소. 그러나 분명히 말해 두거니와, 그 사내도 그 사내의 아내도 내 집 문턱은 넘지 못하오.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly sighed, and then attempted to console herself and her lord by remarking that, after all, the thing was not accomplished yet.", "그랜틀리 부인은 한숨을 쉬고는, 어쨌든 아직 일이 다 된 것은 아니지 않으냐는 말로 제 자신과 지아비를 달래 보려 했다."], ["Now that Eleanor was at Plumstead, much might be done to wean her from her fatal passion.", "엘리너가 플럼스테드에 와 있는 지금, 그 치명적인 연정에서 동생을 떼어 놓을 방도야 얼마든지 있을 터였다."], ["Poor Eleanor!", "가엾은 엘리너!"]], [["The evening passed off without anything to make it remarkable. Mr.", "그날 저녁은 이렇다 할 일 없이 지나갔다."], ["Arabin discussed the parish of St. Ewold with the archdeacon, and Mrs.", "애러빈 씨는 부주교와 세인트 이월드 교구에 관해 이야기를 나누었고, 그 교구 사람들을 아는 그랜틀리 부인과 하딩 씨도 이야기에 끼어들었다."], ["Grantly and Mr. Harding, who knew the personages of the parish, joined in.", "엘리너도 그들을 알았으나 말수는 적었다."], ["Eleanor also knew them, but she said little. Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 겉으로 보아 엘리너에게 그리 마음을 두지 않았고, 엘리너 역시 그때는 형부가 각별히 아끼는 사람을 각별히 상냥하게 대할 기분이 아니었다."], ["Arabin did not apparently take much notice of her, and she was not in a humour to receive at that time with any special grace any special favourite of her brother-in-law. Her first idea on reaching her bedroom was that a much pleasanter family party might be met at Dr.", "침실에 들어서면서 맨 먼저 든 생각은, 이 목사관보다 스탠호프 박사 댁에서 훨씬 즐거운 가족 모임을 만날 수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["Stanhope's than at the rectory. She began to think that she was getting tired of clergymen and their respectable, humdrum, wearisome mode of living, and that after all, people in the outer world, who had lived in Italy, London, or elsewhere, need not necessarily be regarded as atrocious and abominable.", "엘리너는 성직자들과 그 점잖고 단조롭고 지루한 살림살이가 슬슬 지겨워지고 있다는 것, 그리고 결국 이탈리아나 런던이나 그 밖의 어디에서 살아 본 바깥세상 사람들을 반드시 흉악하고 가증스럽다고 여길 것까지는 없다는 것을 생각하게 되었다."], ["The Stanhopes, she had thought, were a giddy, thoughtless, extravagant set of people, but she had seen nothing wrong about them and had, on the other hand, found that they thoroughly knew how to make their house agreeable.", "스탠호프 일가는 경박하고 생각 없고 헤픈 사람들이라고 여겨 왔으나, 정작 그릇된 구석은 하나도 보지 못했고, 오히려 제 집을 즐겁게 만드는 법만은 훤히 알고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["It was a thousand pities, she thought, that the archdeacon should not have a little of the same savoir vivre. Mr.", "부주교에게 그런 savoir vivre가 조금이라도 있었더라면 좋았으련만, 하고 엘리너는 생각했다."], ["Arabin, as we have said, did not apparently take much notice of her, but yet he did not go to bed without feeling that he had been in company with a very pretty woman; and as is the case with most bachelors, and some married men, regarded the prospect of his month's visit at Plumstead in a pleasanter light when he learnt that a very pretty woman was to share it with him.", "애러빈 씨는 앞서 말했듯 겉으로 보아 엘리너에게 그리 마음을 두지 않았으나, 그래도 무척 어여쁜 여인과 한자리에 있었다는 느낌 없이 잠자리에 들지는 않았다. 그리고 대개의 독신 남자들이, 또 더러는 아내 있는 남자들까지도 그러하듯, 무척 어여쁜 여인이 그 한 달을 함께 지내리라는 것을 알고 나자 플럼스테드에서 보낼 한 달의 방문을 한결 유쾌한 빛으로 바라보게 되었다."]], [["Before they all retired it was settled that the whole party should drive over on the following day to inspect the parsonage at St. Ewold.", "모두 물러가기 전에, 이튿날 일행 전부가 마차로 세인트 이월드의 목사관을 살펴보러 가기로 정해졌다."], ["The three clergymen were to discuss dilapidations, and the two ladies were to lend their assistance in suggesting such changes as might be necessary for a bachelor's abode.", "세 성직자는 낡아 허물어진 데를 의논하고, 두 부인은 독신자의 거처에 필요할 법한 손질을 일러 주어 거들기로 했다."]], [["Accordingly, soon after breakfast the carriage was at the door.", "그리하여 아침을 마치고 얼마 지나지 않아 마차가 문 앞에 대령했다."], ["There was only room for four inside, and the archdeacon got upon the box.", "안에는 네 사람 자리밖에 없었으므로 부주교가 마부석에 올랐다."], ["Eleanor found herself opposite to Mr. Arabin, and was, therefore, in a manner forced into conversation with him.", "엘리너는 애러빈 씨와 마주 앉게 되었고, 따라서 어쩔 수 없이 그와 이야기를 나누게 되었다."], ["They were soon on comfortable terms together, and had she thought about it, she would have thought that, in spite of his black cloth, Mr. Arabin would not have been a bad addition to the Stanhope family party.", "두 사람은 이내 마음 편한 사이가 되었으니, 만일 엘리너가 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보았더라면 검은 성직자 옷을 입었을망정 애러빈 씨라면 스탠호프 댁 모임에 끼어도 나쁘지 않겠다고 여겼을 것이다."]], [["Now that the archdeacon was away they could all trifle. Mr.", "부주교가 자리에 없으니 다들 마음 놓고 실없는 소리를 할 수 있었다."], ["Harding began by telling them in the most innocent manner imaginable an old legend about Mr. Arabin's new parish.", "먼저 하딩 씨가 세상에 다시없이 천진한 태도로 애러빈 씨의 새 교구에 얽힌 옛 전설 하나를 들려주었다."], ["There was, he said, in days of yore an illustrious priestess of St. Ewold, famed through the whole country for curing all manner of diseases.", "옛날 옛적에 세인트 이월드에는 온갖 병을 고쳐 준다 하여 온 고을에 이름이 자자하던 여사제가 있었다고 한다."], ["She had a well, as all priestesses have ever had, which well was extant to this day, and shared in the minds of many of the people the sanctity which belonged to the consecrated ground of the parish church. Mr.", "여사제라면 예로부터 다들 그러했듯 그에게도 우물이 하나 있었는데, 그 우물은 오늘날까지도 남아 있어서 여러 사람의 마음속에서는 교구 교회의 축성된 땅에 깃든 거룩함을 함께 나누어 지니고 있다는 것이었다."], ["Arabin declared that he should look on such tenets on the part of his parishioners as anything but orthodox.", "애러빈 씨는 제 교구민들이 그런 믿음을 품고 있다면 도무지 정통이라고는 볼 수 없겠다고 잘라 말했다."], ["And Mrs. Grantly replied that she so entirely disagreed with him as to think that no parish was in a proper state that had not its priestess as well as its priest.", "그러자 그랜틀리 부인은 자기는 생각이 전혀 달라서, 사제 말고 여사제까지 갖추지 못한 교구는 온전한 상태라 할 수 없다고 본다고 대답했다."], ["\"The duties are never well done,\"", "“일이란 그렇게 나누어 맡지 않고서는 결코 제대로 되는 법이 없지요.”"], ["said she, \"unless they are so divided.\"", "부인은 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose, Papa,\"", "“아버지, 옛날에는 여사제가 권세를 죄다 혼자 쥐고 있었던 모양이지요.”"], ["said Eleanor, \"that in the olden times the priestess bore all the sway herself.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["Mr. Arabin, perhaps, thinks that such might be too much the case now if a sacred lady were admitted within the parish.\"", "“애러빈 씨는 아마 지금도 교구 안에 거룩한 여인을 들였다가는 너무 그렇게 되어 버릴까 봐 걱정하시는가 봐요.”"]], [["\"I think, at any rate,\"", "“적어도 저로서는,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"that it is safer to run no such risk.", "“그런 위험은 아예 무릅쓰지 않는 편이 안전하다고 봅니다."], ["No priestly pride has ever exceeded that of sacerdotal females.", "사제의 교만이라 한들 여사제의 교만을 넘어선 적은 없었으니까요."], ["A very lowly curate I might, perhaps, essay to rule, but a curatess would be sure to get the better of me.\"", "아주 미천한 부목사쯤이야 어찌어찌 다스려 볼 수 있겠지만, 여자 부목사라면 틀림없이 저를 눌러 이길 겁니다.”"]], [["\"There are certainly examples of such accidents happening,\"", "“그런 사고가 실제로 일어난 예가 분명히 있지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"They do say that there is a priestess at Barchester who is very imperious in all things touching the altar.", "“바체스터에도 제단에 관한 일이라면 무엇에나 서슬이 퍼런 여사제가 하나 있다고들 하더군요."], ["Perhaps the fear of such a fate as that is before your eyes.\"", "애러빈 씨 눈앞에 어른거리는 것도 어쩌면 그런 신세가 될까 하는 두려움인지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["When they were joined by the archdeacon on the gravel before the vicarage, they descended again to grave dullness.", "목사관 앞 자갈길에서 부주교와 합류하자 일행은 다시 근엄한 따분함으로 내려앉았다."], ["Not that Archdeacon Grantly was a dull man, but his frolic humours were of a cumbrous kind, and his wit, when he was witty, did not generally extend itself to his auditors.", "그랜틀리 부주교가 따분한 사람이라는 말은 아니다. 다만 그의 익살은 어딘가 묵직한 종류였고, 재치를 부릴 때에도 그 재치가 듣는 사람에게까지 미치는 일은 대체로 없었다."], ["On the present occasion he was soon making speeches about wounded roofs and walls, which he declared to be in want of some surgeon's art.", "이날도 그는 이내 상처 입은 지붕이며 벽에 관한 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작하여, 이것들이야말로 외과 의사의 손길이 필요하다고 단언했다."], ["There was not a partition that he did not tap, nor a block of chimneys that he did not narrowly examine; all water-pipes, flues, cisterns, and sewers underwent an investigation; he even descended, in the care of his friend, so far as to bore sundry boards in the floors with a bradawl.", "그가 두드려 보지 않은 칸막이가 없었고, 꼼꼼히 살펴보지 않은 굴뚝 더미가 없었다. 물관이며 연통이며 물통이며 하수구가 모조리 조사를 받았고, 심지어 벗의 부축을 받아가며 몸을 굽혀 송곳으로 마루청 여기저기에 구멍까지 뚫어 보았다."]], [["Mr. Arabin accompanied him through the rooms, trying to look wise in such domestic matters, and the other three also followed.", "애러빈 씨는 그런 살림살이 문제에도 아는 체하는 얼굴을 지어 보이며 방마다 그를 따라다녔고, 나머지 세 사람도 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Mrs. Grantly showed that she had not herself been priestess of a parish twenty years for nothing, and examined the bells and window-panes in a very knowing way.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이십 년 동안 한 교구의 여사제 노릇을 괜히 해 온 것이 아님을 보여 주며, 초인종이며 창유리를 여간 밝은 눈으로 살피지 않았다."]], [["\"You will, at any rate, have a beautiful prospect out of your own window, if this is to be your private sanctum,\"", "“어쨌든 이 방을 혼자 쓰는 서재로 삼으신다면 창밖으로 아름다운 경치를 내다보시겠어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["She was standing at the lattice of a little room upstairs, from which the view certainly was very lovely.", "엘리너는 이층의 조그만 방 격자창 앞에 서 있었는데, 거기서 내다보이는 경치는 과연 무척 아름다웠다."], ["It was from the back of the vicarage, and there was nothing to interrupt the eye between the house and the glorious gray pile of the cathedral.", "목사관 뒤편이라 집과 저 웅장한 잿빛 대성당 덩어리 사이를 가로막는 것이 하나도 없었다."], ["The intermediate ground, however, was beautifully studded with timber.", "그러면서도 그 사이의 땅에는 나무들이 아름답게 점점이 박혀 있었다."], ["In the immediate foreground ran the little river which afterwards skirted the city, and, just to the right of the cathedral, the pointed gables and chimneys of Hiram's Hospital peeped out of the elms which encompass it.", "바로 눈앞에는 뒤에 가서 도시를 끼고 도는 작은 강이 흘렀고, 대성당에서 조금 오른쪽으로는 히램 요양원의 뾰족한 박공과 굴뚝들이 그것을 둘러싼 느릅나무 사이로 빼꼼히 내다보였다."]], [["\"Yes,\" said he, joining her.", "“그렇군요.”"], ["\"I shall have a beautifully complete view of my adversaries.", "그가 엘리너 곁으로 다가서며 말했다. “적들을 아주 남김없이 내다볼 수 있게 생겼습니다."], ["I shall sit down before the hostile town and fire away at them at a very pleasant distance.", "적의 성 앞에 진을 치고 앉아 퍽 기분 좋은 거리에서 포를 쏘아 대면 되겠군요."], ["I shall just be able to lodge a shot in the hospital, should the enemy ever get possession of it, and as for the palace, I have it within full range.\"", "요양원이 적의 손에 넘어가는 날이면 거기에도 한 방 먹일 수 있겠고, 주교관으로 말하자면 사정거리 안에 넉넉히 들어옵니다.”"]], [["\"I never saw anything like you clergymen,\"", "“성직자분들 같은 사람들은 정말 처음 봐요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"You are always thinking of fighting each other.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “언제나 서로 싸울 궁리만 하시니 말이에요.”"]], [["\"Either that,\"", "“그게 아니면,”"], ["said he, \"or else supporting each other.", "그가 말했다. “서로 떠받칠 궁리를 하지요."], ["The pity is that we cannot do the one without the other.", "딱한 것은 하나 없이 다른 하나만 할 수는 없다는 점입니다."], ["But are we not here to fight?", "그러나 우리가 여기 있는 것은 싸우기 위함이 아닙니까?"], ["Is not ours a church militant?", "우리 교회는 싸우는 교회가 아닙니까?"], ["What is all our work but fighting, and hard fighting, if it be well done?\"", "우리 일이라는 것이 싸움 말고 무엇이겠습니까. 제대로 하자면 고된 싸움이지요.”"]], [["\"But not with each other.\"", "“하지만 서로를 상대로 싸우실 것까지야 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"That's as it may be.", "“그건 또 모를 일입니다."], ["The same complaint which you make of me for battling with another clergyman of our own church, the Mohammedan would make against me for battling with the error of a priest of Rome.", "같은 교회의 다른 성직자와 다툰다고 저를 나무라시는 그 말씀을, 마호메트교도라면 로마 사제의 오류와 다툰다고 저를 나무라는 데 그대로 쓸 겁니다."], ["Yet, surely, you would not be inclined to say that I should be wrong to do battle with such as him.", "그렇다고 제가 그런 자와 다투는 것이 그르다고야 설마 말씀하지 않으시겠지요."], ["A pagan, too, with his multiplicity of gods, would think it equally odd that the Christian and the Mohammedan should disagree.\"", "신을 여럿 두고 섬기는 이교도라면 또 그리스도교도와 마호메트교도가 서로 어긋난다는 것을 똑같이 이상하게 여길 테고요.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["But you wage your wars about trifles so bitterly.\"", "하지만 대수롭지도 않은 일로 그렇게 모질게 싸우시잖아요.”"]], [["\"Wars about trifles,\"", "“대수롭지 않은 일을 두고 벌이는 싸움은,”"], ["said he, \"are always bitter, especially among neighbours.", "그가 말했다. “늘 모진 법입니다."], ["When the differences are great, and the parties comparative strangers, men quarrel with courtesy.", "특히 이웃끼리는 더 그렇지요. 차이가 크고 서로 사이가 먼 사람들끼리라면 다툴 때에도 예를 갖춥니다."], ["What combatants are ever so eager as two brothers?\"", "형제 둘만큼 기를 쓰고 싸우는 상대가 세상에 또 어디 있겠습니까?”"]], [["\"But do not such contentions bring scandal on the church?\"", "“하지만 그런 다툼이 교회에 추문을 불러오지 않나요?”"]], [["\"More scandal would fall on the church if there were no such contentions.", "“그런 다툼이 없다면 오히려 더 큰 추문이 교회에 떨어질 겁니다."], ["We have but one way to avoid them--by that of acknowledging a common head of our church, whose word on all points of doctrine shall be authoritative.", "다툼을 피할 길은 하나뿐입니다. 교리의 모든 점에 대하여 그 말씀이 곧 권위가 되는 공통의 머리를 우리 교회가 받든다고 인정하는 것이지요."], ["Such a termination of our difficulties is alluring enough.", "우리의 어려움이 그렇게 끝난다면 꽤나 마음이 끌리는 일입니다."], ["It has charms which are irresistible to many, and all but irresistible, I own, to me.\"", "그 매력을 뿌리치지 못하는 사람이 많고, 저 역시 거의 뿌리치지 못한다는 것을 고백합니다.”"]], [["\"You speak now of the Church of Rome?\"", "“지금 로마 교회를 말씀하시는 건가요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"No,\"", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said he, \"not necessarily of the Church of Rome; but of a church with a head.", "그가 말했다. “꼭 로마 교회를 말하는 것은 아니고, 머리를 가진 교회를 말하는 겁니다."], ["Had it pleased God to vouchsafe to us such a church our path would have been easy.", "하느님께서 우리에게 그런 교회를 베풀어 주시는 것이 그 뜻에 맞았다면 우리 길은 수월했겠지요. 그러나 수월한 길이 우리에게 좋다고 여겨지지는 않았던 모양입니다.”"], ["But easy paths have not been thought good for us.\"", "그는 말을 멈추고 한동안 잠자코 서 있었다."], ["He paused and stood silent for awhile, thinking of the time when he had so nearly sacrificed all he had, his powers of mind, his free agency, the fresh running waters of his mind's fountain, his very inner self, for an easy path in which no fighting would be needed; and then he continued: \"What you say is partly true: our contentions do bring on us some scandal.", "싸움이라고는 필요 없는 수월한 길 하나를 얻자고 제가 가진 모든 것을, 곧 제 정신의 힘과 제 자유로운 뜻과 제 마음의 샘에서 솟는 신선한 물줄기와 제 속 깊은 자기 자신을 하마터면 다 바칠 뻔했던 그때를 생각하고 있었던 것이다. 그러고는 말을 이었다."], ["The outer world, though it constantly reviles us for our human infirmities and throws in our teeth the fact that being clergymen we are still no more than men, demands of us that we should do our work with godlike perfection.", "“하시는 말씀은 반은 옳습니다. 우리의 다툼이 우리에게 얼마간 추문을 불러오는 것은 사실이지요."], ["There is nothing god-like about us: we differ from each other with the acerbity common to man; we triumph over each other with human frailty; we allow differences on subjects of divine origin to produce among us antipathies and enmities which are anything but divine.", "바깥세상은 우리가 인간으로서 약한 것을 두고 늘 우리를 헐뜯고, 성직자라 한들 결국 사람에 지나지 않는다는 사실을 우리 낯에다 내던지면서도, 정작 우리에게는 신과 같은 완전함으로 일을 해내라고 요구합니다. 우리에게 신과 같은 구석이라고는 없습니다."], ["This is all true. But what would you have in place of it?", "우리는 사람에게 흔한 모진 마음으로 서로 갈라서고, 사람다운 나약함으로 서로를 이기고 우쭐대며, 신에게서 비롯된 주제를 두고 갈리면서 도무지 신다울 것이 없는 반감과 원한을 우리 사이에 빚어냅니다."], ["There is no infallible head for a church on earth.", "다 사실입니다. 그러나 그 자리에 무엇을 놓으시겠습니까?"], ["This dream of believing man has been tried, and we see in Italy and in Spain what has come of it.", "이 땅 위에 그르침이 없는 교회의 머리란 없습니다. 믿는 사람들이 꾸어 온 이 꿈은 이미 시험을 거쳤고, 그 끝이 무엇이었는지는 이탈리아와 에스파냐에서 우리가 보고 있습니다."], ["Grant that there are and have been no bickerings within the pale of the Pope's Church.", "교황의 교회 울타리 안에서는 아옹다옹 다툰 일이 지금도 없고 예전에도 없었다고 쳐 봅시다."], ["Such an assumption would be utterly untrue, but let us grant it, and then let us say which church has incurred the heavier scandals.\"", "그런 가정이야 터무니없이 사실과 다르지만, 그래도 그렇다고 쳐 두고, 그러고 나서 어느 교회가 더 무거운 추문을 짊어졌는지 말해 봅시다.”"]], [["There was a quiet earnestness about Mr. Arabin, as he half-acknowledged and half-defended himself from the charge brought against him, which surprised Eleanor.", "제게 씌워진 혐의를 반쯤은 인정하고 반쯤은 변호하는 애러빈 씨에게는 조용한 진지함이 있었고, 엘리너는 거기에 놀랐다."], ["She had been used all her life to listen to clerical discussion, but the points at issue between the disputants had so seldom been of more than temporal significance as to have left on her mind no feeling of reverence for such subjects.", "엘리너는 평생 성직자들의 논의를 들으며 자랐으나, 다투는 이들 사이의 쟁점이라는 것이 세속의 이해를 넘는 경우가 워낙 드물었던 터라, 그런 주제에 대해 아무런 경외심도 마음에 남아 있지 않았다."], ["There had always been a hard worldly leaven of the love either of income or of power in the strains she had heard; there had been no panting for the truth; no aspirations after religious purity.", "엘리너가 들어 온 가락에는 언제나 수입 아니면 권세를 사랑하는 딱딱한 세속의 누룩이 들어 있었고, 진리를 향해 숨 가빠하는 일도, 종교적 순결을 향해 우러르는 마음도 없었다."], ["It had always been taken for granted by those around her that they were indubitably right; that there was no ground for doubt; that the hard uphill work of ascertaining what the duty of a clergyman should be had been already accomplished in full; and that what remained for an active militant parson to do was to hold his own against all comers.", "주위 사람들은 언제나 제 편이 의심할 여지 없이 옳다는 것, 미심쩍어할 근거란 없다는 것, 성직자의 본분이 무엇이어야 하는지를 알아내는 저 고되고 험한 일은 이미 남김없이 끝나 있다는 것, 그러니 활동하며 싸우는 목사에게 남은 일이란 덤벼드는 자마다 물리치고 제 자리를 지키는 것뿐이라는 것을 당연하게 여겼다."], ["Her father, it is true, was an exception to this, but then he was so essentially anti-militant in all things that she classed him in her own mind apart from all others.", "물론 아버지는 그런 축이 아니었다. 그러나 아버지는 매사에 워낙 싸움과는 거리가 먼 사람이어서, 엘리너는 마음속으로 아버지를 다른 모든 사람과 따로 떼어 놓고 있었다."], ["She had never argued the matter within herself, or considered whether this common tone was or was not faulty; but she was sick of it without knowing that she was so.", "엘리너는 이 문제를 스스로 따져 본 적도, 이 흔한 가락이 그릇된 것인지 아닌지 헤아려 본 적도 없었다. 다만 그런 줄도 모르는 채 그것에 물려 있었다."], ["And now she found to her surprise, and not without a certain pleasurable excitement, that this new-comer among them spoke in a manner very different from that to which she was accustomed.", "그런데 이제 놀랍게도, 그리고 어느 만큼은 즐거운 설렘까지 느끼면서, 저희 가운데 새로 온 이 사람이 제가 익숙하던 것과는 사뭇 다르게 말한다는 것을 알아차린 것이었다."]], [["\"It is so easy to condemn,\"", "“나무라기란 참 쉬운 일이지요.”"], ["said he, continuing the thread of his thoughts.", "그가 제 생각의 실마리를 이어 가며 말했다."], ["\"I know no life that must be so delicious as that of a writer for newspapers, or a leading member of the opposition--to thunder forth accusations against men in power; to show up the worst side of everything that is produced; to pick holes in every coat; to be indignant, sarcastic, jocose, moral, or supercilious; to damn with faint praise, or crush with open calumny!", "“신문에 글을 쓰는 사람이나 야당의 앞장선 의원만큼 달콤한 삶은 없지 싶습니다. 권세를 쥔 자들을 향해 벼락같이 죄상을 늘어놓고, 세상에 나온 것마다 그 가장 나쁜 쪽을 들추어내고, 옷마다 흠집을 찾아내고, 분개하고 빈정대고 익살 부리고 도덕을 설하고 거들먹거리고, 밍밍한 칭찬으로 죽여 놓거나 대놓고 헐뜯어 짓뭉개고!"], ["What can be so easy as this when the critic has to be responsible for nothing?", "아무것도 책임질 것이 없는 비평가에게 이보다 쉬운 일이 무엇이겠습니까?"], ["You condemn what I do, but put yourself in my position and do the reverse, and then see if I cannot condemn you.\"", "부인께서는 제가 하는 일을 나무라십니다만, 제 자리에 서서 그 반대로 해 보십시오. 그러고서도 제가 부인을 나무라지 못하는지 보십시오.”"]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Arabin, I do not condemn you.\"", "“어머, 애러빈 씨, 저는 나무라는 게 아니에요.”"]], [["\"Pardon me, you do, Mrs.", "“죄송합니다만 나무라고 계십니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold--you as one of the world; you are now the opposition member; you are now composing your leading article, and well and bitterly you do it.", "세상 사람의 하나로서 말이지요. 지금 부인은 야당 의원이십니다."], ["'Let dogs delight to bark and bite'--you fitly begin with an elegant quotation--'but if we are to have a church at all, in heaven's name let the pastors who preside over it keep their hands from each other's throats.", "지금 부인은 사설을 쓰고 계시고, 그것도 아주 잘, 아주 모질게 쓰고 계십니다. ‘개들이야 짖고 물어뜯기를 즐긴다 하나’, 우아한 인용으로 서두를 여시는군요, ‘그래도 교회라는 것을 두려거든, 제발 그것을 이끄는 목자들만은 서로의 멱을 잡는 짓을 삼가게 하라."], ["Lawyers can live without befouling each other's names; doctors do not fight duels.", "법률가들도 서로의 이름을 더럽히지 않고 살아가며, 의사들은 결투를 벌이지 않는다."], ["Why is it that clergymen alone should indulge themselves in such unrestrained liberty of abuse against each other?' and so you go on reviling us for our ungodly quarrels, our sectarian propensities, and scandalous differences.", "어찌하여 유독 성직자들만이 서로를 헐뜯는 이 거리낌 없는 자유를 마음껏 누린단 말인가?’ 그렇게 부인은 우리의 불경한 다툼과 종파를 가르는 성벽과 추문거리인 견해차를 두고 우리를 꾸짖어 나가십니다."], ["It will, however, give you no trouble to write another article next week in which we, or some of us, shall be twitted with an unseemly apathy in matters of our vocation.", "그러나 다음 주에 또 한 편의 사설을 쓰시는 데에는 아무런 수고도 들지 않겠지요. 그 글에서는 우리가, 아니 우리 가운데 누군가가 제 소명에 관한 일에 볼썽사납게 무심하다고 놀림을 받을 겁니다."], ["It will not fall on you to reconcile the discrepancy; your readers will never ask you how the poor parson is to be urgent in season and out of season and yet never come in contact with men who think widely differently from him.", "그 어긋남을 맞춰 놓는 일은 부인 몫이 아닙니다. 독자들은 가련한 목사가 어떻게 때를 얻든 못 얻든 힘써 일하면서도 저와 생각이 크게 다른 사람들과는 결코 부딪치지 않을 수 있는지 부인께 묻는 법이 없으니까요."], ["You, when you condemn this foreign treaty, or that official arrangement, will have to incur no blame for the graver faults of any different measure.", "부인은 이 외국 조약이나 저 관청의 조처를 나무라시면서도, 그와 다른 어떤 방책이 더 무거운 잘못을 저지른다 한들 아무 책망도 지지 않으실 겁니다. 나무라기란 참 쉬운 일입니다."], ["It is so easy to condemn--and so pleasant too, for eulogy charms no listeners as detraction does.\"", "그리고 즐겁기도 하지요. 헐뜯는 말만큼 듣는 이를 홀리는 칭찬이란 없으니 말입니다.”"]], [["Eleanor only half-followed him in his raillery, but she caught his meaning.", "엘리너는 그의 놀림을 절반밖에 따라가지 못했으나 뜻만은 알아들었다."], ["\"I know I ought to apologize for presuming to criticize you,\"", "“주제넘게 애러빈 씨를 비평했으니 사과를 드려야 마땅하겠지요.”"], ["she said, \"but I was thinking with sorrow of the ill-will that has lately come among us at Barchester, and I spoke more freely than I should have done.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “다만 저는 요즈음 바체스터에 들어앉은 나쁜 감정을 서글프게 생각하고 있던 참이라, 해야 할 말보다 좀 더 거리낌 없이 말했던 거예요.”"]], [["\"Peace on earth and goodwill among men, are, like heaven, promises for the future;\"", "“땅 위의 평화와 사람들 사이의 선의는 천국이 그러하듯 앞날에 대한 약속이지요.”"], ["said he, following rather his own thoughts than hers.", "그는 엘리너의 생각보다는 제 생각을 좇아 말했다."], ["\"When that prophecy is accomplished, there will no longer be any need for clergymen.\"", "“그 예언이 이루어지는 날에는 성직자란 것도 더는 있을 까닭이 없어질 겁니다.”"]], [["Here they were interrupted by the archdeacon, whose voice was heard from the cellar shouting to the vicar.", "여기서 두 사람의 이야기는 부주교에게 끊겼다. 지하 술광에서 교구 사제를 부르는 그의 목소리가 들려온 것이다."]], [["\"Arabin, Arabin,\"--and then, turning to his wife, who was apparently at his elbow--\"where has he gone to?", "“애러빈, 애러빈!” 그러고는 곁에 있는 듯한 아내를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["This cellar is perfectly abominable.", "“이 사람이 어디로 갔소? 이 술광은 참으로 형편없구려."], ["It would be murder to put a bottle of wine into it till it has been roofed, walled, and floored.", "지붕을 얹고 벽을 치고 바닥을 깔기 전에 여기에 포도주 한 병이라도 들여놓는다면 그건 살인이나 다름없소."], ["How on earth old Goodenough ever got on with it I cannot guess.", "늙은 구드너프 영감이 대체 이런 데서 어떻게 지냈는지 도무지 짐작이 안 가오."], ["But then Goodenough never had a glass of wine that any man could drink.\"", "하기야 구드너프는 사람이 마실 만한 포도주라고는 한 잔도 가져 본 적이 없는 위인이었소.”"]], [["\"What is it, Archdeacon?\"", "“무슨 일이오, 부주교?”"], ["said the vicar, running downstairs and leaving Eleanor above to her meditations.", "교구 사제가 계단을 뛰어 내려가며 말했다. 엘리너는 위층에 남아 제 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["\"This cellar must be roofed, walled, and floored,\"", "“이 술광은 지붕을 얹고 벽을 치고 바닥을 깔아야 하오.” 부주교가 되뇌었다."], ["repeated the archdeacon.", "“내 말을 명심하시오."], ["\"Now mind what I say, and don't let the architect persuade you that it will do; half of these fellows know nothing about wine.", "이대로도 쓸 만하다는 건축업자의 말에 넘어가지 마시오. 그 작자들 절반은 포도주에 대해 아무것도 모르오."], ["This place as it is now would be damp and cold in winter and hot and muggy in summer.", "이 자리는 지금 이대로라면 겨울에는 축축하고 차며 여름에는 덥고 눅눅할 거요."], ["I wouldn't give a straw for the best wine that ever was vinted, after it had lain here a couple of years.\"", "세상에 빚어진 가장 좋은 술이라 한들 여기에 두어 해 뉘어 두었다면 나는 지푸라기 하나 값도 치르지 않겠소.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin assented and promised that the cellar should be reconstructed according to the archdeacon's receipt.", "애러빈 씨는 그러겠노라 하고, 부주교의 처방대로 술광을 다시 짓겠다고 약속했다."]], [["\"And, Arabin, look here; was such an attempt at a kitchen grate ever seen?\"", "“그리고 애러빈, 여기 좀 보시오. 부엌 화덕이랍시고 이렇게 만들어 놓은 것을 어디서 본 적이 있소?”"]], [["\"The grate is really very bad,\"", "“화덕이 정말 형편없기는 하네요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I am sure the priestess won't approve of it, when she is brought home to the scene of her future duties.", "“여사제를 이 집에 맞아들여 앞으로 할 일을 맡기게 되면 분명 이 화덕을 마뜩잖아 할 거예요."], ["Really, Mr. Arabin, no priestess accustomed to such an excellent well as that above could put up with such a grate as this.\"", "정말이지 애러빈 씨, 저 위의 그 훌륭한 우물에 길든 여사제라면 이런 화덕은 도저히 견디지 못할걸요.”"]], [["\"If there must be a priestess at St. Ewold's at all, Mrs.", "“세인트 이월드에 여사제라는 것을 꼭 두어야 한다면, 그랜틀리 부인, 그저 그 우물이나 지키게 두고 우리네 가난에서 비롯한 이런저런 모자람에 대고 거룩한 노여움을 불러 내리지는 말았으면 합니다."], ["Grantly, I think we will leave her to her well and not call down her divine wrath on any of the imperfections rising from our human poverty. However, I own I am amenable to the attractions of a well-cooked dinner, and the grate shall certainly be changed.\"", "그렇지만 잘 지은 저녁상의 매력에 제가 약하다는 것만은 인정하니, 화덕은 반드시 갈겠습니다.”"]], [["By this time the archdeacon had again ascended, and was now in the dining-room.", "이때쯤 부주교는 다시 위층으로 올라와 이제는 식당에 들어와 있었다."], ["\"Arabin,\"", "“애러빈.”"], ["said he, speaking in his usual loud, clear voice and with that tone of dictation which was so common to him, \"you must positively alter this dining-room--that is, remodel it altogether.", "그는 늘 그러하듯 크고 또렷한 목소리로, 또 그에게 그토록 흔한 명령조로 말했다. “이 식당은 반드시 손을 보아야 하오."], ["Look here, it is just sixteen feet by fifteen; did any man ever hear of a dining-room of such proportions!\"", "아니, 아예 통째로 다시 만들어야 하오. 여기 보시오, 꼭 16피트에 15피트밖에 안 되오."], ["The archdeacon stepped the room long-ways and cross-ways with ponderous steps, as though a certain amount of ecclesiastical dignity could be imparted even to such an occupation as that by the manner of doing it.", "이런 치수의 식당이라는 것을 세상 누가 들어나 보았겠소!” 부주교는 그런 일에조차 어떤 종류의 성직자다운 위엄을 실을 수 있기라도 한 듯한 몸가짐으로, 묵직한 걸음을 옮겨 방을 세로로 가로로 재어 보였다."], ["\"Barely sixteen; you may call it a square.\"", "“겨우 16피트요. 이만하면 네모반듯하다고 해야지.”"]], [["\"It would do very well for a round table,\"", "“둥근 식탁을 놓으면 아주 알맞겠구먼.”"], ["suggested the ex-warden.", "전임 워든이 넌지시 말했다."]], [["Now there was something peculiarly unorthodox, in the archdeacon's estimation, in the idea of a round table.", "그런데 부주교가 보기에 둥근 식탁이라는 발상에는 유난히 정통에서 어긋나는 구석이 있었다."], ["He had always been accustomed to a goodly board of decent length, comfortably elongating itself according to the number of the guests, nearly black with perpetual rubbing, and as bright as a mirror.", "그는 언제나 넉넉한 길이의 훌륭한 상, 손님 수에 따라 편안히 늘어나고, 하도 문질러 대어 거의 검게 되었으며, 거울처럼 반들거리는 상에 익숙해 있었다."], ["Now round dinner-tables are generally of oak, or else of such new construction as not to have acquired the peculiar hue which was so pleasing to him.", "그런데 둥근 식탁이란 대개 참나무로 짜거나, 아니면 만든 지 얼마 되지 않아 그가 그토록 흡족해하는 그 특유의 빛깔을 아직 얻지 못한 것들이었다."], ["He connected them with what he called the nasty newfangled method of leaving a cloth on the table, as though to warn people that they were not to sit long.", "그는 둥근 식탁을 두고, 오래 앉아 있지 말라고 사람들에게 미리 일러 두기라도 하듯 상보를 그대로 깔아 두는, 그가 이르는바 고약한 신식 방식과 한데 묶어 생각했다."], ["In his eyes there was something democratic and parvenu in a round table.", "그의 눈에 둥근 식탁에는 어딘가 민주주의적이고 벼락출세한 구석이 있었다."], ["He imagined that dissenters and calico-printers chiefly used them, and perhaps a few literary lions more conspicuous for their wit than their gentility.", "그는 그런 것을 주로 비국교도나 옥양목에 무늬를 찍는 자들이 쓰리라고, 그리고 어쩌면 품위보다 재치로 더 이름난 몇몇 문단의 사자들이 쓰리라고 여겼다."], ["He was a little flurried at the idea of such an article being introduced into the diocese by a protégé of his own, and at the instigation of his father-in-law.", "그런 물건이 제가 밀어 준 사람의 손으로, 그것도 장인의 부추김을 받아 이 교구 안에 들어온다는 생각에 그는 적잖이 마음이 어수선해졌다."]], [["\"A round dinner-table,\"", "“둥근 식탁이란,”"], ["said he with some heat, \"is the most abominable article of furniture that ever was invented.", "그는 얼마간 열을 내어 말했다. “여태 만들어진 가구 가운데 가장 가증스러운 물건이오."], ["I hope that Arabin has more taste than to allow such a thing in his house.\"", "애러빈에게는 그런 것을 제 집에 들이지 않을 만한 안목이 있으리라 믿소.”"]], [["Poor Mr. Harding felt himself completely snubbed, and of course said nothing further; but Mr.", "가엾은 하딩 씨는 완전히 면박을 당한 기분이 들어 물론 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Arabin, who had yielded submissively in the small matters of the cellar and kitchen grate, found himself obliged to oppose reforms which might be of a nature too expensive for his pocket.", "그러나 술광과 부엌 화덕 같은 사소한 문제에서는 고분고분 물러섰던 애러빈 씨는, 제 주머니 사정에 견주어 너무 값비쌀 법한 개조에는 맞서지 않을 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["\"But it seems to me, Archdeacon, that I can't very well lengthen the room without pulling down the wall, and if I pull down the wall, I must build it up again; then if I throw out a bow on this side, I must do the same on the other, and if I do it for the ground floor, I must carry it up to the floor above.", "“그렇지만 내가 보기에는, 부주교, 벽을 헐지 않고서야 이 방을 길게 늘일 도리가 없소. 그런데 벽을 헐면 다시 쌓아야 하고, 이쪽에 내닫이창을 내면 저쪽에도 똑같이 내야 하며, 아래층에 그렇게 하면 위층까지 올려 붙여야 하오."], ["That will be putting a new front to the house and will cost, I suppose, a couple of hundred pounds.", "그러면 집에 새 정면을 다는 셈이 되어 200파운드쯤은 들 것이오."], ["The ecclesiastical commissioners will hardly assist me when they hear that my grievance consists in having a dining-room only sixteen feet long.\"", "내 고충이라는 것이 식당 길이가 16피트밖에 안 된다는 것이라는 말을 듣고서 교회 재산 위원회가 나를 도와줄 성싶지 않소.”"]], [["The archdeacon proceeded to explain that nothing would be easier than adding six feet to the front of the dining-room without touching any other room in the house.", "부주교는 집 안의 다른 방은 손끝 하나 대지 않고 식당 정면에 6피트를 덧붙이는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없다고 설명하기 시작했다."], ["Such irregularities of construction in small country-houses were, he said, rather graceful than otherwise, and he offered to pay for the whole thing out of his own pocket if it cost more than forty pounds. Mr.", "시골의 자그마한 집에서 그 정도로 구조가 고르지 않은 것은 오히려 운치가 있으면 있었지 흠이 될 것은 없다고 그는 말했고, 40파운드가 넘게 들면 그 전부를 제 주머니에서 치르겠노라고까지 했다."], ["Arabin, however, was firm, and, although the archdeacon fussed and fumed about it, would not give way.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 단호했고, 부주교가 안달하고 씩씩거려도 물러서지 않았다."], ["Forty pounds, he said, was a matter of serious moment to him, and his friends, if under such circumstances they would be good-natured enough to come to him at all, must put up with the misery of a square room.", "40파운드는 제게 여간 중대한 문제가 아니며, 벗들이 그런 형편에도 마음 좋게 저를 찾아와 줄 생각이라면 네모반듯한 방의 괴로움쯤은 참아 주어야 한다고 그는 말했다."], ["He was willing to compromise matters by disclaiming any intention of having a round table.", "다만 둥근 식탁을 들일 뜻은 전혀 없다고 밝히는 것으로 절충할 용의는 있었다."]], [["\"But,\" said Mrs.", "“그런데,”"], ["Grantly, \"what if the priestess insists on having both the rooms enlarged?\"", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “여사제가 두 방을 다 넓혀 달라고 우기면 어떻게 하시겠어요?”"]], [["\"The priestess in that case must do it for herself, Mrs. Grantly.\"", "“그렇다면 그 여사제가 제 손으로 해야지요, 그랜틀리 부인.”"]], [["\"I have no doubt she will be well able to do so,\"", "“그야 넉넉히 그럴 수 있을 거예요.” 부인이 대답했다."], ["replied the lady; \"to do that and many more wonderful things.", "“그것뿐 아니라 더 놀라운 일도 얼마든지 해내겠지요."], ["I am quite sure that the priestess of St. Ewold, when she does come, won't come empty-handed.\"", "세인트 이월드의 여사제는 정말이지 오게 되는 날에 빈손으로 오지는 않으리라고 저는 확신해요.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin, however, did not appear well inclined to enter into speculative expenses on such a chance as this, and therefore any material alterations in the house, the cost of which could not fairly be made to lie at the door either of the ecclesiastical commissioners or of the estate of the late incumbent, were tabooed.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 그처럼 아득한 가망을 믿고 씀씀이를 벌일 마음이 별로 없어 보였다."], ["With this essential exception, the archdeacon ordered, suggested, and carried all points before him in a manner very much to his own satisfaction.", "그리하여 그 비용을 교회 재산 위원회나 전임 성직자의 유산 어느 쪽에도 온당하게 떠넘길 수 없는 집안의 실질적인 개조는 모두 금기가 되었다. 이 중요한 예외만 빼면 부주교는 명령하고 제안하고 모든 사안을 제 뜻대로 밀어붙여, 스스로 여간 흡족해하지 않았다."], ["A close observer, had there been one there, might have seen that his wife had been quite as useful in the matter as himself.", "그 자리에 눈 밝은 사람이 하나라도 있었더라면, 이 일에서 부인이 남편 못지않게 쓸모가 있었음을 알아보았을 것이다."], ["No one knew better than Mrs. Grantly the appurtenances necessary to a comfortable house.", "편안한 집에 갖추어야 할 것들을 그랜틀리 부인만큼 잘 아는 사람은 없었다."], ["She did not, however, think it necessary to lay claim to any of the glory which her lord and master was so ready to appropriate as his own.", "그러면서도 부인은 제 주인이자 지아비가 그토록 선뜻 제 것으로 챙기는 그 영광을 굳이 제 몫이라 주장할 필요를 느끼지 않았다."]], [["Having gone through their work effectually and systematically, the party returned to Plumstead well satisfied with their expedition.", "일을 효과적으로 또 체계적으로 마친 일행은 이번 나들이에 흡족해하며 플럼스테드로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII. The Thornes of Ullathorne", "# 제22장. 울라손의 손 남매"]], [["On the following Sunday Mr. Arabin was to read himself in at his new church.", "다음 일요일에 애러빈 씨는 새 교회에서 취임 낭독을 하기로 되어 있었다."], ["It was agreed at the rectory that the archdeacon should go over with him and assist at the reading desk, and that Mr. Harding should take the archdeacon's duty at Plumstead Church.", "목사관에서는 부주교가 그와 함께 건너가 독경대에서 거들고, 하딩 씨가 플럼스테드 교회에서 부주교의 직무를 맡기로 이야기가 되었다."], ["Mrs. Grantly had her school and her buns to attend to, and professed that she could not be spared, but Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인은 학교와 빵을 돌보아야 하는 터라 도저히 몸을 뺄 수 없노라 하였으나, 볼드 부인은 그들과 동행하기로 하였다."], ["Bold was to accompany them. It was further agreed also that they would lunch at the squire's house and return home after the afternoon service.", "나아가 일행은 지주의 집에서 점심을 들고 오후 예배가 끝난 뒤에 돌아오기로 정하였다."]], [["Wilfred Thorne, Esq., of Ullathorne, was the squire of St.", "울라손의 윌프레드 손 씨는 세인트 이월드의 지주였다."], ["Ewold's--or, rather, the squire of Ullathorne, for the domain of the modern landlord was of wider notoriety than the fame of the ancient saint.", "아니, 차라리 울라손의 지주라 하는 편이 옳겠으니, 요즘 지주의 영지가 옛 성인의 명성보다 널리 알려져 있었던 까닭이다."], ["He was a fair specimen of what that race has come to in our days which, a century ago, was, as we are told, fairly represented by Squire Western.", "백 년 전에는 웨스턴 지주가 제법 알맞게 대변하였다고들 하는 그 족속이 오늘날에 이르러 어떤 모습이 되었는지를 그는 잘 보여 주는 본보기였다."], ["If that representation be a true one, few classes of men can have made faster strides in improvement. Mr.", "그 옛 대변자가 참으로 그러하였다면, 그만큼 빠르게 나아진 부류의 사람들도 흔치 않을 것이다."], ["Thorne, however, was a man possessed of quite a sufficient number of foibles to lay him open to much ridicule.", "그러나 손 씨 역시 남의 웃음거리가 되기에 충분할 만큼의 결점을 지니고 있었다."], ["He was still a bachelor, being about fifty, and was not a little proud of his person.", "나이 쉰 줄에 이르도록 그는 아직 독신이었고, 제 풍채를 적잖이 자랑스러워하였다."], ["When living at home at Ullathorne, there was not much room for such pride, and there therefore he always looked like a gentleman and like that which he certainly was, the first man in his parish.", "울라손 제 집에 머물 때에는 그런 자랑을 부릴 자리가 그리 많지 않았으므로, 그곳에서 그는 언제나 신사답게, 그리고 그가 실제로 그러한 바 곧 제 교구에서 으뜸가는 사람답게 보였다."], ["But during the month or six weeks which he annually spent in London, he tried so hard to look like a great man there also, which he certainly was not, that he was put down as a fool by many at his club.", "그러나 해마다 런던에서 보내는 한 달 남짓 혹은 예닐곱 주 동안에는 거기서도 대단한 인물로 보이려고 어찌나 애를 쓰는지 — 그는 결코 대단한 인물이 아니었건만 — 클럽의 적잖은 사람들이 그를 얼간이로 치부하였다."], ["He was a man of considerable literary attainment in a certain way and on certain subjects.", "그는 어떤 방면, 어떤 주제에 관해서는 상당한 문필의 소양을 갖춘 사람이었다."], ["His favourite authors were Montaigne and Burton, and he knew more perhaps than any other man in his own county and the next to it of the English essayists of the two last centuries.", "그가 아끼는 저자는 몽테뉴와 버턴이었고, 지난 두 세기 영국 수필가들에 관해서라면 아마 제 고을과 이웃 고을을 통틀어 그보다 더 아는 사람이 없었을 것이다."], ["He possessed complete sets of the Idler, the Spectator, the Tatler, the Guardian, and the Rambler, and would discourse by hours together on the superiority of such publications to anything which has since been produced in our Edinburghs and Quarterlies.", "그는 『아이들러』, 『스펙테이터』, 『태틀러』, 『가디언』, 『램블러』를 빠짐없이 갖추어 두고 있었으며, 그런 간행물이 그 뒤로 우리네 『에든버러』니 『쿼털리』니 하는 것들이 내놓은 어떤 글보다 얼마나 나은지를 몇 시간이고 늘어놓을 수 있었다."], ["He was proficient in all questions of genealogy, and knew enough of almost every gentleman's family in England to say of what blood and lineage were descended all those who had any claim to be considered as possessors of any such luxuries.", "족보에 관한 문제라면 무엇이든 능통하여, 영국의 웬만한 신사 집안은 거의 다 꿰고 있었으므로, 그런 호사를 누린다고 내세울 만한 이들이 어떤 피와 어떤 혈통에서 내려왔는지를 낱낱이 말해 줄 수 있었다."], ["For blood and lineage he himself had a most profound respect.", "피와 혈통에 대하여 그 자신은 더없이 깊은 경의를 품고 있었다."], ["He counted back his own ancestors to some period long antecedent to the Conquest, and could tell you, if you would listen to him, how it had come to pass that they, like Cedric the Saxon, had been permitted to hold their own among the Norman barons.", "그는 제 조상을 정복 시대보다 훨씬 앞선 어느 시절까지 거슬러 헤아렸고, 들어 줄 이만 있다면, 그의 조상이 색슨 사람 세드릭처럼 노르만 귀족들 틈에서 어떻게 제 것을 지킬 수 있었는지를 들려주었다."], ["It was not, according to his showing, on account of any weak complaisance on the part of his family towards their Norman neighbours.", "그의 말에 따르면 그것은 결코 그의 집안이 노르만 이웃들에게 물러 터지게 굴었기 때문이 아니었다."], ["Some Ealfried of Ullathorne once fortified his own castle and held out, not only that, but the then existing cathedral of Barchester also, against one Geoffrey De Burgh, in the time of King John; and Mr. Thorne possessed the whole history of the siege written on vellum and illuminated in a most costly manner.", "울라손의 어느 엘프리드는 존 왕 시절에 제 성을 굳게 지켰을 뿐 아니라 그때 있던 바체스터 대성당까지 아울러 지키며 제프리 드 버그라는 자에 맞서 버티었으니, 손 씨는 그 공성전의 내력을 통째로 담은 양피지 문서를 지니고 있었고 그 문서는 더없이 값지게 채색되어 있었다."], ["It little signified that no one could read the writing, as, had that been possible, no one could have understood the language.", "그 글씨를 읽어 낼 사람이 아무도 없다는 것은 그리 대수롭지 않았다. 설령 읽어 낸다 한들 그 말을 알아들을 사람 또한 없었을 터이므로."], ["Mr. Thorne could, however, give you all the particulars in good English, and had no objection to do so.", "그러나 손 씨는 그 자초지종을 훌륭한 영어로 들려줄 수 있었고, 또 그리하기를 마다하지 않았다."]], [["It would be unjust to say that he looked down on men whose families were of recent date.", "가문의 내력이 얼마 되지 않는 사람들을 그가 깔보았다고 한다면 온당치 못한 말이 될 것이다."], ["He did not do so.", "그는 그러지 않았다."], ["He frequently consorted with such, and had chosen many of his friends from among them.", "그런 이들과 자주 어울렸고, 벗들 가운데 여럿을 그런 이들 중에서 골랐다."], ["But he looked on them as great millionaires are apt to look on those who have small incomes; as men who have Sophocles at their fingers' ends regard those who know nothing of Greek.", "다만 그는 그들을 바라보기를, 큰 부자가 수입이 적은 이들을 바라보듯, 소포클레스를 손금 보듯 훤히 아는 사람이 그리스어라고는 한 자도 모르는 사람을 바라보듯 하였다."], ["They might doubtless be good sort of people, entitled to much praise for virtue, very admirable for talent, highly respectable in every way, but they were without the one great good gift. Such was Mr.", "그들도 물론 좋은 사람들일 수 있고, 덕으로 칭찬받아 마땅하고, 재주로 우러를 만하고, 모든 면에서 훌륭히 존경할 만할 수 있으나, 다만 그 크나큰 한 가지 선물을 갖추지 못한 이들이었다."], ["Thorne's way of thinking on this matter; nothing could atone for the loss of good blood; nothing could neutralize its good effects.", "이 문제에 관한 손 씨의 생각이 그러하였다. 좋은 피를 잃은 것은 무엇으로도 메울 수 없고, 좋은 피가 미치는 좋은 효험은 무엇으로도 지울 수 없다."], ["Few indeed were now possessed of it, but the possession was on that account the more precious.", "이제 그것을 지닌 이가 참으로 드물어졌으나, 그런 까닭에 그 소유는 한층 더 귀하였다."], ["It was very pleasant to hear Mr. Thorne descant on this matter.", "이 문제를 두고 손 씨가 늘어놓는 이야기를 듣는 것은 자못 즐거운 일이었다."], ["Were you in your ignorance to surmise that such a one was of a good family because the head of his family was a baronet of an old date, he would open his eyes with a delightful look of affected surprise, and modestly remind you that baronetcies only dated from James I.", "아무개 집안의 가장이 오래된 준남작이라 하여 그 집안이 좋은 가문이라고 무지한 채로 짐작하여 말하기라도 하면, 그는 짐짓 놀란 척하는 유쾌한 표정으로 눈을 크게 뜨고는, 준남작위란 제임스 1세 때에야 생긴 것임을 겸손히 일깨워 주었다."], ["He would gently sigh if you spoke of the blood of the Fitzgeralds and De Burghs; would hardly allow the claims of the Howards and Lowthers; and has before now alluded to the Talbots as a family who had hardly yet achieved the full honours of a pedigree.", "피츠제럴드가와 드 버그가의 피를 입에 올리면 그는 가만히 한숨을 지었고, 하워드가와 라우더가의 주장은 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않았으며, 탤벗가를 두고서는 아직 족보의 온전한 영예에 이르지 못한 집안이라고 말한 적도 있었다."]], [["In speaking once of a wide-spread race whose name had received the honours of three coronets, scions from which sat for various constituencies, some one of whose members had been in almost every cabinet formed during the present century, a brilliant race such as there are few in England, Mr. Thorne had called them all \"dirt.\"", "그 이름이 귀족의 관 셋을 받는 영예를 누렸고, 그 자손들이 여러 선거구를 대표하여 의석에 앉았으며, 그 일족 가운데 누군가는 금세기에 꾸려진 거의 모든 내각에 들어 있었던, 영국에도 몇 없는 그 찬란한 가문을 두고 이야기하던 자리에서, 손 씨는 그들을 통틀어 “흙”이라 불렀다."], ["He had not intended any disrespect to these men.", "그는 그 사람들을 업신여길 뜻이 조금도 없었다."], ["He admired them in many senses, and allowed them their privileges without envy.", "여러모로 그들을 우러렀고, 시샘 없이 그들의 특권을 인정하였다."], ["He had merely meant to express his feeling that the streams which ran through their veins were not yet purified by time to that perfection, had not become so genuine an ichor, as to be worthy of being called blood in the genealogical sense.", "다만 그들의 핏줄을 흐르는 물줄기가 아직 세월에 씻겨 그만한 완전함에 이르지 못하였다는 것, 족보의 뜻으로 피라 불릴 만한 참된 이코르가 되지는 못하였다는 것, 그 느낌을 말하려 하였을 뿐이다."]], [["When Mr. Arabin was first introduced to him, Mr. Thorne had immediately suggested that he was one of the Arabins of Uphill Stanton. Mr. Arabin replied that he was a very distant relative of the family alluded to. To this Mr. Thorne surmised that the relationship could not be very distant. Mr. Arabin assured him that it was so distant that the families knew nothing of each other. Mr. Thorne laughed his gentle laugh at this and told Mr. Arabin that there was now existing no branch of his family separated from the parent stock at an earlier date than the reign of Elizabeth, and that therefore Mr. Arabin could not call himself distant. Mr. Arabin himself was quite clearly an Arabin of Uphill Stanton.", "애러빈 씨가 처음 그에게 소개되었을 때, 손 씨는 대뜸 그가 업힐 스탠턴의 애러빈 가문 사람이리라고 짚었다. 애러빈 씨는 자기가 그 집안과는 아주 먼 친척이라고 답하였다. 이에 손 씨는 그 친족 관계가 그리 멀 리 없다고 짐작하였다. 애러빈 씨는 두 집안이 서로를 전혀 모를 만큼 먼 사이라고 딱 잘라 말하였다. 손 씨는 이 말에 특유의 부드러운 웃음을 웃고는, 애러빈 씨에게 이르기를, 지금 남아 있는 그 집안의 갈래 가운데 엘리자베스 여왕 치세보다 앞서 본줄기에서 갈라져 나온 것은 하나도 없으니, 그러므로 애러빈 씨가 스스로를 먼 친척이라 일컬을 수는 없다고 하였다. 애러빈 씨 자신이 분명 업힐 스탠턴의 애러빈이라는 것이었다."]], [["\"But,\"", "“그렇지만”"], ["said the vicar, \"Uphill Stanton has been sold to the De Greys and has been in their hands for the last fifty years.\"", "하고 교구 사제가 말하였다. “업힐 스탠턴은 드 그레이 가문에 팔려서 지난 50년 동안 그들 손에 있지 않소.”"]], [["\"And when it has been there one hundred and fifty, if it unluckily remain there so long,\" said Mr.", "“그리고 그것이 150년 동안 그 손에 있는다 한들, 불행히도 그토록 오래 그리된다 한들”"], ["Thorne, \"your descendants will not be a whit the less entitled to describe themselves as being of the family of Uphill Stanton.", "하고 손 씨가 말하였다. “당신의 자손들이 스스로를 업힐 스탠턴 집안 사람이라 일컬을 자격은 털끝만큼도 줄지 않소."], ["Thank God no De Grey can buy that--and thank God no Arabin, and no Thorne, can sell it.\"", "다행히도 그것만은 드 그레이가 사들일 수 없고, 다행히도 그것만은 애러빈도 손도 팔아넘길 수 없는 것이오.”"]], [["In politics Mr. Thorne was an unflinching conservative.", "정치에서 손 씨는 물러섬 없는 보수주의자였다."], ["He looked on those fifty-three Trojans who, as Mr. Dod tells us, censured free trade in November, 1852, as the only patriots left among the public men of England.", "도드 씨가 일러 주는 바, 1852년 11월에 자유무역을 규탄한 그 쉰세 명의 트로이 용사들이야말로 영국의 공인들 가운데 남은 유일한 애국자라고 그는 여겼다."], ["When that terrible crisis of free trade had arrived, when the repeal of the Corn Laws was carried by those very men whom Mr. Thorne had hitherto regarded as the only possible saviours of his country, he was for a time paralysed.", "자유무역이라는 저 무서운 고비가 닥쳤을 때, 그때까지 그가 조국을 구할 유일한 사람들로 여겨 온 바로 그자들의 손으로 곡물법 폐지가 관철되었을 때, 그는 한동안 얼이 나갔다."], ["His country was lost; but that was comparatively a small thing.", "조국은 무너졌다. 그러나 그것은 그나마 작은 일이었다."], ["Other countries had flourished and fallen, and the human race still went on improving under God's providence.", "다른 나라들도 흥하였다가 스러졌고, 인류는 하느님의 섭리 아래 그래도 나아져 왔다."], ["But now all trust in human faith must forever be at an end.", "그러나 이제 사람의 신의에 대한 믿음은 영영 끝장이 나야 마땅하였다."], ["Not only must ruin come, but it must come through the apostasy of those who had been regarded as the truest of true believers.", "파멸이 닥칠 뿐 아니라, 그 파멸이 참된 신자 중의 참된 신자로 여겨지던 이들의 배교를 통하여 닥치는 것이었다."], ["Politics in England, as a pursuit for gentlemen, must be at an end.", "신사가 몸담을 일로서의 영국 정치는 끝장이 나야 마땅하였다."], ["Had Mr. Thorne been trodden under foot by a Whig, he could have borne it as a Tory and a martyr, but to be so utterly thrown over and deceived by those he had so earnestly supported, so thoroughly trusted, was more than he could endure and live.", "손 씨가 휘그당원의 발밑에 짓밟혔다면 토리당원으로서, 순교자로서 그것을 견딜 수 있었으련만, 그토록 열심히 떠받들고 그토록 철석같이 믿었던 이들에게 그처럼 송두리째 내던져지고 속아 넘어간 일은 견디고도 살아 있기에는 너무 벅찼다."], ["He therefore ceased to live as a politician, and refused to hold any converse with the world at large on the state of the country.", "그리하여 그는 정치가로서 살기를 그만두었고, 나라 형편에 관하여 바깥 세상과 어떤 말도 섞기를 마다하였다."]], [["Such were Mr. Thorne's impressions for the first two or three years after Sir Robert Peel's apostasy, but by degrees his temper, as did that of others, cooled down.", "로버트 필 경의 배교가 있은 뒤 두어 해 동안 손 씨의 심경은 그러하였으나, 남들이 그러하였듯 그의 성미도 차츰 가라앉았다."], ["He began once more to move about, to frequent the bench and the market, and to be seen at dinners shoulder to shoulder with some of those who had so cruelly betrayed him.", "그는 다시금 나다니기 시작하여 치안판사석과 장터에 얼굴을 내밀었고, 그를 그토록 모질게 저버린 이들과 나란히 어깨를 겯고 만찬 자리에 앉은 모습을 보이기도 하였다."], ["It was a necessity for him to live, and that plan of his for avoiding the world did not answer.", "그로서는 살아가야 하였고, 세상을 등지겠다는 그 계획은 소용에 닿지 않았다."], ["He, however, and others around him who still maintained the same staunch principles of protection--men like himself who were too true to flinch at the cry of a mob--had their own way of consoling themselves.", "그러나 그를 비롯하여 여전히 보호무역의 굳은 원칙을 지키는 주위 사람들 — 군중의 아우성에 움츠러들기에는 너무 참된 그와 같은 사람들 — 에게는 저희끼리 마음을 달래는 방도가 있었다."], ["They were, and felt themselves to be, the only true depositaries left of certain Eleusinian mysteries, of certain deep and wondrous services of worship by which alone the gods could be rightly approached.", "그들은 어떤 엘레우시스 비의(祕儀)의, 오직 그것을 통해서만 신들에게 바르게 다가갈 수 있는 어떤 깊고 놀라운 예배 의식의, 이제 남은 유일한 수호자였고 또 스스로 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["To them and them only was it now given to know these things and to perpetuate them, if that might still be done, by the careful and secret education of their children.", "이제 그것들을 알고, 또 그것이 아직 가능하다면 제 자식들을 조심스럽고 은밀하게 가르쳐 그것을 이어 가는 일은 오직 그들에게만 주어진 몫이었다."]], [["We have read how private and peculiar forms of worship have been carried on from age to age in families which, to the outer world, have apparently adhered to the services of some ordinary church.", "바깥 세상에는 여느 교회의 예배를 좇는 듯이 보이던 집안들에서 사사롭고 유별난 예배의 형식이 대를 이어 지켜져 왔다는 이야기를 우리는 읽어 알고 있다."], ["And so by degrees it was with Mr. Thorne.", "손 씨의 경우도 차츰 그러하였다."], ["He learnt at length to listen calmly while protection was talked of as a thing dead, although he knew within himself that it was still quick with a mystic life.", "그는 마침내 보호무역이 죽은 것인 양 이야기되는 자리에서도 잠자코 듣고 있는 법을 익혔다. 속으로는 그것이 여전히 신비한 생명으로 살아 있음을 알고 있었으면서도."], ["Nor was he without a certain pleasure that such knowledge, though given to him, should be debarred from the multitude.", "게다가 그런 앎이 자기에게는 주어졌으되 뭇사람에게는 막혀 있다는 데에서 그는 얼마간의 즐거움을 느끼지 않은 것도 아니었다."], ["He became accustomed to hear even among country gentlemen that free trade was after all not so bad, and to hear this without dispute, although conscious within himself that everything good in England had gone with his old palladium.", "시골 신사들 사이에서조차 자유무역이 알고 보면 그리 나쁜 것도 아니라는 말이 나오는 것을 그는 예사로 듣게 되었고, 영국의 좋은 것이 죄다 저의 옛 팔라디움과 함께 사라졌음을 속으로 알면서도 그 말에 다투지 않고 듣게 되었다."], ["He had within him something of the feeling of Cato, who gloried that he could kill himself because Romans were no longer worthy of their name. Mr.", "로마인들이 더는 그 이름에 값하지 못하는 까닭에 스스로 목숨을 끊을 수 있음을 자랑삼던 카토의 심정 같은 것이 그의 안에도 얼마간 있었다."], ["Thorne had no thought of killing himself, being a Christian and still possessing his £4000 a year, but the feeling was not on that account the less comfortable.", "손 씨는 그리스도인인 데다 여전히 연 4000파운드의 수입을 지니고 있었으므로 목숨을 끊을 생각은 없었으나, 그렇다고 하여 그 심정이 덜 아늑하지는 않았다."]], [["Mr. Thorne was a sportsman, and had been active though not outrageous in his sports.", "손 씨는 사냥을 즐기는 사람이었고, 지나치다 할 정도는 아니어도 부지런히 사냥에 나섰다."], ["Previous to the great downfall of politics in his county, he had supported the hunt by every means in his power.", "제 고을에서 정치가 크게 무너지기 전까지 그는 온갖 수를 다하여 사냥 모임을 뒷받침하였다."], ["He had preserved game till no goose or turkey could show a tail in the parish of St. Ewold's.", "사냥감을 어찌나 아껴 길렀던지 세인트 이월드 교구에서는 거위든 칠면조든 꽁지를 성히 달고 다니는 놈이 없을 지경이었다."], ["He had planted gorse covers with more care than oaks and larches.", "참나무와 낙엽송보다 가시금작화 덤불을 더 정성껏 심었다."], ["He had been more anxious for the comfort of his foxes than of his ewes and lambs.", "제 암양과 새끼 양보다 여우들의 안락을 더 걱정하였다."], ["No meet had been more popular than Ullathorne; no man's stables had been more liberally open to the horses of distant men than Mr. Thorne's; no man had said more, written more, or done more to keep the club up.", "울라손보다 사람이 많이 모이는 사냥 모임이 없었고, 손 씨의 마구간만큼 먼 데서 온 이들의 말에 후하게 열린 마구간이 없었으며, 사냥 클럽을 지탱하는 데에 그보다 더 많이 말하고 더 많이 쓰고 더 많이 애쓴 사람이 없었다."], ["The theory of protection could expand itself so thoroughly in the practices of a county hunt!", "보호무역의 이론이 고을 사냥의 관행 속에서 그토록 활짝 펼쳐질 수 있었던 것이다!"], ["But when the great ruin came; when the noble master of the Barsetshire hounds supported the recreant minister in the House of Lords and basely surrendered his truth, his manhood, his friends, and his honour for the hope of a garter, then Mr. Thorne gave up the hunt.", "그러나 저 큰 파국이 닥쳤을 때, 바셋셔 사냥단의 고귀한 단장이 상원에서 그 변절한 대신을 편들며 가터 훈장을 바라는 마음에 제 진실과 사내다움과 벗들과 명예를 비굴하게 내던졌을 때, 손 씨는 사냥을 그만두었다."], ["He did not cut his covers, for that would not have been the act of a gentleman.", "그렇다고 제 덤불을 베어 내지는 않았으니, 그것은 신사가 할 짓이 아니었다."], ["He did not kill his foxes, for that according to his light would have been murder.", "제 여우들을 잡아 죽이지도 않았으니, 그가 보기에 그것은 살인이었다."], ["He did not say that his covers should not be drawn, or his earths stopped, for that would have been illegal according to the by-laws prevailing among country gentlemen.", "제 덤불을 뒤지지 말라거나 제 여우굴을 막지 말라고 이르지도 않았으니, 그것은 시골 신사들 사이에 통하는 규약에 어긋나는 일이었다."], ["But he absented himself from home on the occasion of every meet at Ullathorne, left the covers to their fate, and could not be persuaded to take his pink coat out of his press, or his hunters out of his stable.", "다만 울라손에서 사냥 모임이 열릴 때마다 집을 비웠고, 덤불은 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었으며, 붉은 사냥복을 옷장에서 꺼내거나 사냥말을 마구간에서 끌어내라는 권유에는 도무지 넘어가지 않았다."], ["This lasted for two years, and then by degrees he came round.", "이것이 두 해 동안 이어졌고, 그런 뒤 그는 차츰 마음을 돌렸다."], ["He first appeared at a neighbouring meet on a pony, dressed in his shooting-coat, as though he had trotted in by accident; then he walked up one morning on foot to see his favourite gorse drawn, and when his groom brought his mare out by chance, he did not refuse to mount her.", "그는 먼저 이웃 고을의 사냥 모임에 사냥용 저고리도 아닌 총 쏠 때 입는 웃옷 차림으로 조랑말을 타고 나타났으니, 마치 지나는 길에 우연히 들른 듯한 모양새였다. 다음에는 어느 아침 제가 아끼던 가시금작화 덤불을 뒤지는 것을 보러 걸어서 올라갔고, 마부가 마침 제 암말을 끌고 나오자 올라타기를 마다하지 않았다."], ["He was next persuaded, by one of the immortal fifty-three, to bring his hunting materials over to the other side of the county and take a fortnight with the hounds there; and so gradually he returned to his old life.", "그다음에는 저 불멸의 쉰세 명 가운데 한 사람의 권유에 따라 사냥 채비를 고을 건너편으로 옮겨 그곳 사냥개들과 함께 보름을 지냈고, 그렇게 차츰 옛 생활로 돌아왔다."], ["But in hunting as in other things he was only supported by an inward feeling of mystic superiority to those with whom he shared the common breath of outer life.", "그러나 사냥에서도 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그를 지탱한 것은 바깥세상의 공기를 함께 마시는 이들보다 자기가 신비롭게 우월하다는 속마음뿐이었다."]], [["Mr. Thorne did not live in solitude at Ullathorne.", "손 씨는 울라손에서 홀로 살지 않았다."], ["He had a sister, who was ten years older than himself and who participated in his prejudices and feelings so strongly that she was a living caricature of all his foibles.", "그에게는 열 살 위의 누이가 있었는데, 이 누이는 그의 편견과 심정에 어찌나 깊이 함께하였던지 그의 온갖 결점을 살아 움직이는 희화로 만들어 놓은 듯하였다."], ["She would not open a modern quarterly, did not choose to see a magazine in her drawing-room, and would not have polluted her fingers with a shred of the Times for any consideration.", "그녀는 요즘 나오는 계간지를 펼치는 법이 없었고, 제 응접실에 잡지가 놓이는 꼴을 보려 하지 않았으며, 무슨 사정이 있어도 『타임스』 조각 따위로 제 손가락을 더럽히려 들지 않았다."], ["She spoke of Addison, Swift, and Steele as though they were still living, regarded Defoe as the best known novelist of his country, and thought of Fielding as a young but meritorious novice in the fields of romance.", "애디슨과 스위프트와 스틸을 두고서는 그들이 아직 살아 있기라도 한 듯이 이야기하였고, 디포를 제 나라에서 가장 이름난 소설가로 여겼으며, 필딩은 이야기의 들판에 갓 들어선 젊고 기특한 신참쯤으로 생각하였다."], ["In poetry, she was familiar with names as late as Dryden, and had once been seduced into reading \"The Rape of the Lock;\" but she regarded Spenser as the purest type of her country's literature in this line.", "시로 말하자면 드라이든까지의 이름은 익히 알았고, 한 번은 꾐에 넘어가 『머리타래의 강탈』을 읽은 적도 있었으나, 이 갈래에서 제 나라 문학의 가장 순정한 본보기는 스펜서라고 여겼다."], ["Genealogy was her favourite insanity.", "족보야말로 그녀가 가장 아끼는 광증이었다."], ["Those things which are the pride of most genealogists were to her contemptible.", "웬만한 족보학자들이 자랑으로 삼는 것들이 그녀에게는 하찮았다."], ["Arms and mottoes set her beside herself.", "문장(紋章)이니 표어니 하는 것은 그녀를 제정신이 아니게 만들었다."], ["Ealfried of Ullathorne had wanted no motto to assist him in cleaving to the brisket Geoffrey De Burgh, and Ealfried's great grandfather, the gigantic Ullafrid, had required no other arms than those which nature gave him to hurl from the top of his own castle a cousin of the base invading Norman.", "울라손의 엘프리드가 제프리 드 버그의 가슴팍을 쪼개는 데에 무슨 표어의 도움이 필요하였던가. 엘프리드의 증조부인 거인 울라프리드가 그 비열한 노르만 침략자의 사촌을 제 성 꼭대기에서 내던질 적에 자연이 그에게 준 팔 말고 무슨 문장이 필요하였던가."], ["To her all modern English names were equally insignificant: Hengist, Horsa, and such like had for her ears the only true savour of nobility.", "그녀에게는 요즘의 영국식 이름이란 죄다 하나같이 보잘것없었다. 헹기스트니 호르사니 하는 이름이라야 그녀의 귀에 고귀함의 참된 맛이 났다."], ["She was not contented unless she could go beyond the Saxons, and would certainly have christened her children, had she had children, by the names of the ancient Britons.", "색슨 시대보다 더 거슬러 올라가지 않고서는 그녀는 흡족해하지 않았으니, 자식이 있었더라면 틀림없이 옛 브리튼 사람들의 이름으로 세례를 받게 하였을 것이다."], ["In some respects she was not unlike Scott's Ulrica, and had she been given to cursing, she would certainly have done so in the names of Mista, Skogula, and Zernebock.", "몇 가지 점에서 그녀는 스콧이 그린 울리카와 그리 다르지 않았고, 저주하는 버릇이 있었더라면 필시 미스타와 스코굴라와 체르네보크의 이름으로 저주하였을 것이다."], ["Not having submitted to the embraces of any polluting Norman, as poor Ulrica had done, and having assisted no parricide, the milk of human kindness was not curdled in her bosom.", "다만 가엾은 울리카처럼 더러운 노르만의 품에 안긴 일이 없고 어버이를 죽이는 일에 손을 보탠 적도 없었으므로, 사람다운 온정의 젖이 그 가슴에서 상하지는 않았다."], ["She never cursed therefore, but blessed rather.", "그리하여 그녀는 저주하는 대신 축복하였다."], ["This, however, she did in a strange uncouth Saxon manner that would have been unintelligible to any peasants but her own.", "다만 그 축복이라는 것이 예스럽고 투박한 색슨식이어서, 제 집 소작인들 말고는 어떤 농부도 알아듣지 못하였을 말투였다."]], [["As a politician, Miss Thorne had been so thoroughly disgusted with public life by base deeds long antecedent to the Corn Law question that that had but little moved her.", "정치를 두고 말하자면, 손 양은 곡물법 문제가 나오기 훨씬 전부터 저열한 짓거리들에 하도 진저리를 쳐 온 터라 그 일에는 별로 마음이 움직이지 않았다."], ["In her estimation her brother had been a fast young man, hurried away by a too ardent temperament into democratic tendencies.", "그녀가 보기에 제 오라비는 너무 뜨거운 기질에 떠밀려 민주주의로 기울었던 경솔한 젊은이였다."], ["Now happily he was brought to sounder views by seeing the iniquity of the world.", "이제 다행히도 세상의 사악함을 보고서 온당한 생각으로 돌아왔을 뿐이다."], ["She had not yet reconciled herself to the Reform Bill, and still groaned in spirit over the defalcations of the Duke as touching the Catholic Emancipation.", "그녀는 아직도 선거법 개정안과 화해하지 못하였고, 가톨릭 해방에 관하여 공작이 저지른 배신을 두고 여전히 속으로 탄식하였다."], ["If asked whom she thought the Queen should take as her counsellor, she would probably have named Lord Eldon, and when reminded that that venerable man was no longer present in the flesh to assist us, she would probably have answered with a sigh that none now could help us but the dead.", "여왕이 누구를 고문으로 삼아야 하겠느냐고 물었다면 그녀는 아마 엘던 경의 이름을 대었을 것이고, 그 존경할 어른은 이제 육신을 지니고 우리를 도울 수 없노라고 일러 주면 아마 한숨을 지으며 이제 우리를 도울 수 있는 이는 죽은 이들뿐이라고 대답하였을 것이다."]], [["In religion Miss Thorne was a pure Druidess.", "종교로 말하자면 손 양은 순전한 드루이드 여사제였다."], ["We would not have it understood by that that she did actually in these latter days assist at any human sacrifices, or that she was in fact hostile to the Church of Christ.", "그렇다고 하여 그녀가 요즘 세상에 사람을 제물로 바치는 자리에 실제로 거들었다거나, 실은 그리스도의 교회에 적대적이었다는 뜻으로 새기지는 말아 주기 바란다."], ["She had adopted the Christian religion as a milder form of the worship of her ancestors, and always appealed to her doing so as evidence that she had no prejudices against reform, when it could be shown that reform was salutary.", "그녀는 그리스도교를 제 조상들이 드리던 예배의 좀 더 부드러운 형태로 받아들였고, 개혁이 이롭다는 것이 밝혀질 때에는 자기도 개혁에 아무 편견이 없다는 증거로 늘 그 사실을 내세웠다."], ["This reform was the most modern of any to which she had as yet acceded, it being presumed that British ladies had given up their paint and taken to some sort of petticoats before the days of St. Augustine.", "그녀가 여태 받아들인 개혁 가운데 이것이 가장 근래의 것이었으니, 성 아우구스티누스의 시절이 오기 전에 이미 브리튼의 부인들이 몸에 칠하기를 그만두고 무슨 속치마 같은 것을 입게 되었으리라 짐작한 것이다."], ["That further feminine step in advance which combines paint and petticoats together had not found a votary in Miss Thorne.", "칠과 속치마를 아울러 갖추는, 여인들의 그다음 진보에 이르러서는 손 양이라는 신봉자를 얻지 못하였다."]], [["But she was a Druidess in this, that she regretted she knew not what in the usages and practices of her Church.", "그러나 그녀가 드루이드였던 것은 이런 점에서였다. 곧 제 교회의 관습과 관례에서 무엇인지도 모를 무엇인가를 아쉬워하였다는 점이다."], ["She sometimes talked and constantly thought of good things gone by, though she had but the faintest idea of what those good things had been.", "그녀는 지나가 버린 좋은 것들을 이따금 입에 올렸고 늘 마음속으로 생각하였으나, 그 좋은 것들이 무엇이었는지에 대해서는 아주 흐릿한 짐작밖에 없었다."], ["She imagined that a purity had existed which was now gone, that a piety had adorned our pastors and a simple docility our people, for which it may be feared history gave her but little true warrant.", "지금은 사라진 순결함이 있었고, 우리 목자들을 꾸며 주던 경건과 우리 백성을 꾸며 주던 소박한 순종이 있었다고 그녀는 그렸으나, 역사가 그런 생각을 뒷받침해 주는 바는 유감스럽게도 별로 없었다."], ["She was accustomed to speak of Cranmer as though he had been the firmest and most simple-minded of martyrs, and of Elizabeth as though the pure Protestant faith of her people had been the one anxiety of her life.", "그녀는 크랜머를 두고 순교자들 가운데 가장 굳세고 가장 순박한 이인 양 말하는 버릇이 있었고, 엘리자베스를 두고는 제 백성의 순수한 신교 신앙이 그 삶의 유일한 근심거리이기라도 하였던 양 말하곤 하였다."], ["It would have been cruel to undeceive her, had it been possible; but it would have been impossible to make her believe that the one was a time-serving priest, willing to go any length to keep his place, and that the other was in heart a papist, with this sole proviso, that she should be her own pope.", "할 수만 있다 한들 그 오해를 깨뜨리는 것은 잔인한 짓이었으리라. 그러나 앞의 이가 제자리를 지키기 위해서라면 어디까지든 갈 준비가 되어 있던 처세에 능한 성직자였고, 뒤의 이는 제가 제 교황이기만 하다면 마음으로는 교황파였다는 것을 그녀에게 믿게 하기란 애초에 불가능한 일이었다."]], [["And so Miss Thorne went on sighing and regretting, looking back to the divine right of kings as the ruling axiom of a golden age, and cherishing, low down in the bottom of her heart of hearts, a dear unmentioned wish for the restoration of some exiled Stuart.", "그리하여 손 양은 한숨과 아쉬움 속에 살아가며, 왕권신수설을 황금시대의 으뜸가는 원리로 삼아 돌아보았고, 마음속 가장 깊은 자리에는 쫓겨난 어느 스튜어트 왕가 사람의 복위를 바라는, 입 밖에 내지 않는 은근한 소망을 품고 있었다."], ["Who would deny her the luxury of her sighs, or the sweetness of her soft regrets!", "누가 그녀에게서 그 한숨의 호사를, 그 부드러운 아쉬움의 달콤함을 앗아 가려 하겠는가!"]], [["In her person and her dress she was perfect, and well she knew her own perfection.", "몸가짐과 차림새로 말하자면 그녀는 나무랄 데가 없었고, 자기가 나무랄 데 없다는 것을 그녀도 익히 알고 있었다."], ["She was a small, elegantly made old woman, with a face from which the glow of her youth had not departed without leaving some streaks of a roseate hue.", "그녀는 몸집이 작고 맵시 있게 다듬어진 노부인으로, 젊은 시절의 화색이 장밋빛 자취를 얼마간 남겨 두고 물러간 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["She was proud of her colour, proud of her grey hair which she wore in short crisp curls peering out all around her face from her dainty white lace cap.", "그녀는 제 안색을 자랑스러워하였고, 새하얀 레이스 실내모 가장자리로 얼굴을 빙 둘러 삐죽삐죽 내민 짧고 곱슬곱슬한 백발을 자랑스러워하였다."], ["To think of all the money that she spent in lace used to break the heart of poor Mrs. Quiverful with her seven daughters.", "그녀가 레이스에 쓰는 돈을 헤아리자면 딸 일곱을 둔 가엾은 퀴버풀 부인은 가슴이 미어지곤 하였다."], ["She was proud of her teeth, which were still white and numerous, proud of her bright cheery eye, proud of her short jaunty step; and very proud of the neat, precise, small feet with which those steps were taken.", "그녀는 아직도 희고 개수가 넉넉한 제 이를 자랑스러워하였고, 밝고 명랑한 눈을 자랑스러워하였으며, 짧고 경쾌한 걸음걸이를 자랑스러워하였고, 그 걸음을 옮기는 아담하고 반듯한 작은 발을 몹시 자랑스러워하였다."], ["She was proud also, ay, very proud, of the rich brocaded silk in which it was her custom to ruffle through her drawing-room.", "또한 응접실을 사각사각 스치며 거닐 적에 늘 걸치던 값진 무늬 비단을, 참으로 몹시 자랑스러워하였다."]], [["We know what was the custom of the lady of Branksome--", "우리는 브랭섬의 부인의 관례가 어떠하였는지를 알고 있다."]], [["Nine-and-twenty knights of fame Hung their shields in Branksome Hall.", "이름 높은 스물아홉 기사가 브랭섬 홀에 제 방패를 걸어 두었더라."]], [["The lady of Ullathorne was not so martial in her habits, but hardly less costly.", "울라손의 부인은 그처럼 무인다운 버릇을 지니지는 않았으나 돈이 덜 든다고 하기도 어려웠다."], ["She might have boasted that nine-and-twenty silken skirts might have been produced in her chamber, each fit to stand alone.", "그녀는 제 방에서 비단 치마 스물아홉 벌을 꺼내 놓을 수 있으며 그 하나하나가 혼자서도 설 만큼 빳빳하다고 뽐낼 수 있었을 것이다."], ["The nine-and-twenty shields of the Scottish heroes were less independent and hardly more potent to withstand any attack that might be made on them.", "스코틀랜드 용사들의 방패 스물아홉은 그만큼 홀로 설 힘도 없었고, 저희에게 닥칠 어떤 공격을 막아 내기에 더 나을 것도 별로 없었다."], ["Miss Thorne when fully dressed might be said to have been armed cap-a-pie, and she was always fully dressed, as far as was ever known to mortal man.", "손 양은 옷을 다 갖추어 입으면 머리끝에서 발끝까지 무장을 갖춘 셈이라 할 만하였고, 사람의 눈에 뜨이는 한에서 말하자면 그녀는 언제나 옷을 다 갖추어 입고 있었다."]], [["For all this rich attire Miss Thorne was not indebted to the generosity of her brother.", "이 모든 값진 차림을 손 양이 제 오라비의 너그러움에 힘입어 얻은 것은 아니었다."], ["She had a very comfortable independence of her own, which she divided among juvenile relatives, the milliners, and the poor, giving much the largest share to the latter.", "그녀에게는 아주 넉넉한 제 몫의 재산이 따로 있었고, 그것을 어린 친척들과 여성 모자 상인들과 가난한 이들에게 나누어 주되 마지막 쪽에 가장 큰 몫을 돌렸다."], ["It may be imagined, therefore, that with all her little follies she was not unpopular.", "그러니 자잘한 어리석음을 다 지녔으면서도 그녀가 사람들에게 미움을 사지 않았으리라는 것은 짐작할 만하다."], ["All her follies have, we believe, been told.", "그녀의 어리석음이라면 이제 다 이야기한 셈이다."], ["Her virtues were too numerous to describe, and not sufficiently interesting to deserve description.", "그녀의 미덕은 낱낱이 그려 보이기에는 너무 많았고, 또 그려 보일 만큼 흥미롭지도 않았다."]], [["While we are on the subject of the Thornes, one word must be said of the house they lived in.", "손 남매의 이야기가 나온 김에 그들이 살던 집에 관해서도 한마디 해 두어야겠다."], ["It was not a large house, nor a fine house, nor perhaps to modern ideas a very commodious house, but by those who love the peculiar colour and peculiar ornaments of genuine Tudor architecture it was considered a perfect gem.", "그것은 큰 집도 아니고 훌륭한 집도 아니었으며 요즘 생각으로는 그리 편리한 집도 아니었으나, 참된 튜더 양식 건축의 그 유별난 빛깔과 유별난 장식을 사랑하는 이들에게는 나무랄 데 없는 보석으로 여겨졌다."], ["We beg to own ourselves among the number, and therefore take this opportunity to express our surprise that so little is known by English men and women of the beauties of English architecture.", "우리도 그런 무리에 든다고 감히 밝혀 두거니와, 그런 까닭에 이 자리를 빌려 영국의 남녀가 영국 건축의 아름다움을 어찌 이리도 모르는지 놀라움을 말해 두려 한다."], ["The ruins of the Colosseum, the Campanile at Florence, St. Mark's, Cologne, the Bourse and Notre Dame are with our tourists as familiar as household words; but they know nothing of the glories of Wiltshire, Dorsetshire, and Somersetshire.", "콜로세움의 폐허며 피렌체의 종탑이며 산마르코며 쾰른이며 부르스며 노트르담은 우리 여행자들에게 집안 살림살이만큼이나 익숙한 이름이면서도, 그들은 윌트셔와 도싯셔와 서머싯셔의 영광에 관해서는 아무것도 모른다."], ["Nay, we much question whether many noted travellers, men who have pitched their tents perhaps under Mount Sinai, are not still ignorant that there are glories in Wiltshire, Dorsetshire, and Somersetshire.", "아니, 이름난 여행가들, 어쩌면 시나이산 아래에 천막을 쳐 본 적까지 있는 이들조차 윌트셔와 도싯셔와 서머싯셔에 영광이 있다는 것을 여태 모르고 있지는 않은지 우리는 자못 의심스럽다."], ["We beg that they will go and see.", "부디 가서 보시기를 청한다."]], [["Mr. Thorne's house was called Ullathorne Court--and was properly so called, for the house itself formed two sides of a quadrangle, which was completed on the other two sides by a wall about twenty feet high.", "손 씨의 집은 울라손 저택이라 불렸고, 그렇게 불리는 것이 온당하였으니, 집 자체가 사각 안뜰의 두 면을 이루고 나머지 두 면은 스무 자 남짓 되는 담이 채워 주고 있었던 까닭이다."], ["This wall was built of cut stone, rudely cut indeed, and now much worn, but of a beautiful, rich, tawny yellow colour, the effect of that stonecrop of minute growth which it had taken three centuries to produce.", "이 담은 다듬은 돌로 쌓았는데, 실은 거칠게 다듬은 데다 이제는 몹시 닳았으나, 아름답고 그윽한 황갈색을 띠고 있었으니, 세 세기가 걸려 자라난 자잘한 돌이끼가 빚어 낸 빛깔이었다."], ["The top of this wall was ornamented by huge, round stone balls of the same colour as the wall itself.", "담 꼭대기는 담과 같은 빛깔의 크고 둥근 돌공으로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Entrance into the court was had through a pair of iron gates so massive that no one could comfortably open or close them--consequently, they were rarely disturbed.", "안뜰로 드는 길은 쇠문 한 쌍을 지나야 했는데, 그것이 어찌나 육중한지 누구도 편히 열거나 닫을 수 없었고, 그런 까닭에 좀처럼 건드리는 일이 없었다."], ["From the gateway two paths led obliquely across the court: that to the left reaching the hall-door, which was in the corner made by the angle of the house, and that to the right leading to the back entrance, which was at the further end of the longer portion of the building.", "문간에서 두 갈래 길이 안뜰을 비스듬히 가로질렀으니, 왼편 길은 집이 꺾이는 모퉁이에 나 있는 현관에 닿았고, 오른편 길은 건물 가운데 긴 쪽의 저편 끝에 있는 뒷문으로 이어졌다."]], [["With those who are now adepts in contriving house accommodation, it will militate much against Ullathorne Court that no carriage could be brought to the hall-door.", "요즘 집을 편리하게 꾸미는 데에 이골이 난 이들에게는, 현관까지 마차를 댈 수 없다는 것이 울라손 저택에 크게 불리한 점으로 보일 것이다."], ["If you enter Ullathorne at all, you must do so, fair reader, on foot, or at least in a bath-chair.", "어여쁜 독자여, 울라손에 들어서려면 그대는 걸어서, 적어도 바퀴 달린 안락의자에 앉아서 들어가야만 한다."], ["No vehicle drawn by horses ever comes within that iron gate.", "말이 끄는 어떤 탈것도 저 쇠문 안으로는 들어오지 못한다."], ["But this is nothing to the next horror that will encounter you.", "그러나 이것은 그대를 맞이할 다음번 끔찍함에 견주면 아무것도 아니다."], ["On entering the front door, which you do by no very grand portal, you find yourself immediately in the dining-room.", "그리 거창하달 것 없는 문간을 지나 현관문으로 들어서면, 그대는 대뜸 식당 안에 서 있게 된다."], ["What, no hall?", "뭐라, 홀도 없다니?"], ["exclaims my luxurious friend, accustomed to all the comfortable appurtenances of modern life.", "하고 근대 생활의 온갖 안락한 부속물에 익숙한 나의 호사스러운 벗이 소리친다. 아니오, 친절한 벗이여, 눈여겨보기만 한다면 훌륭한 홀이 여기 있소."], ["Yes, kind sir, a noble hall, if you will but observe it; a true old English hall of excellent dimensions for a country gentleman's family; but, if you please, no dining-parlour.", "시골 신사의 집안에 알맞은 크기의 참되고 오래된 영국식 홀이 말이오. 다만 미안하게도 따로 식사할 방은 없소."]], [["Both Mr. and Miss Thorne were proud of this peculiarity of their dwelling, though the brother was once all but tempted by his friends to alter it.", "손 씨도 손 양도 제 집의 이 유별난 점을 자랑스러워하였다."], ["They delighted in the knowledge that they, like Cedric, positively dined in their true hall, even though they so dined tête-à-tête.", "오라비는 한때 벗들의 꾐에 넘어가 그것을 고칠 뻔하였지만 말이다. 남매는 저희가 세드릭처럼 참으로 제 홀에서 식사를 한다는 사실에 흐뭇해하였다."], ["But though they had never owned, they had felt and endeavoured to remedy the discomfort of such an arrangement.", "비록 그 식사가 단둘이 마주 앉은 것일지언정. 그러나 남매는 결코 인정한 적은 없어도 그런 구조의 불편함을 느끼고 있었고 또 그것을 덜어 보려 애쓴 바 있었다."], ["A huge screen partitioned off the front door and a portion of the hall, and from the angle so screened off a second door led into a passage which ran along the larger side of the house next to the courtyard.", "커다란 가리개가 현관문과 홀의 한 부분을 막아 놓았고, 그렇게 가려진 모퉁이에서 두 번째 문이 안뜰에 면한 집의 긴 쪽을 따라 뻗은 복도로 이어졌다."], ["Either my reader or I must be a bad hand at topography, if it be not clear that the great hall forms the ground-floor of the smaller portion of the mansion, that which was to your left as you entered the iron gate, and that it occupies the whole of this wing of the building.", "큰 홀이 저택의 작은 쪽 — 쇠문으로 들어설 때 그대의 왼편에 있던 그 부분 — 의 일층을 이루며 그 날개 전부를 차지한다는 것이 분명해지지 않는다면, 나의 독자나 나 둘 중 하나가 지형을 다루는 솜씨가 서툰 것이리라."], ["It must be equally clear that it looks out on a trim mown lawn, through three quadrangular windows with stone mullions, each window divided into a larger portion at the bottom, and a smaller portion at the top, and each portion again divided into five by perpendicular stone supporters.", "또한 그 홀이 말끔히 깎은 잔디밭을 내다보고 있다는 것, 그것도 돌 문설주가 세워진 네모난 창 셋을 통하여 그러하다는 것, 창마다 아래쪽의 큰 부분과 위쪽의 작은 부분으로 나뉘고 그 각 부분이 다시 세로로 선 돌 받침으로 다섯으로 나뉜다는 것도 분명해질 것이다."], ["There may be windows which give a better light than such as these, and it may be, as my utilitarian friend observes, that the giving of light is the desired object of a window.", "이런 창보다 빛을 잘 들이는 창이 있을 수 있고, 나의 실용주의자 벗이 말하듯 빛을 들이는 것이야말로 창이 노리는 바일지도 모른다."], ["I will not argue the point with him.", "나는 그와 이 문제를 두고 다투지 않겠다."], ["Indeed I cannot.", "사실 다툴 수도 없다."], ["But I shall not the less die in the assured conviction that no sort or description of window is capable of imparting half so much happiness to mankind as that which had been adopted at Ullathorne Court.", "그러나 그렇다 하여, 울라손 저택에 채택된 창만큼 사람에게 큰 행복을 나누어 줄 수 있는 창은 어떤 종류에도 어떤 모양에도 없다는 굳은 확신을 품은 채 죽으리라는 데에는 변함이 없다. 뭐라, 내닫이창도 아니라고?"], ["What, not an oriel? says Miss Diana de Midellage.", "하고 다이애나 드 미들리지 양이 말한다."], ["No, Miss Diana, not even an oriel, beautiful as is an oriel window.", "그렇소, 다이애나 양, 내닫이창조차 아니라오. 내닫이창이 아무리 아름답다 한들 말이오."], ["It has not about it so perfect a feeling of quiet English homely comfort.", "거기에는 고요하고 소박한 영국식 안락의 느낌이 그만큼 온전히 깃들어 있지 않소."], ["Let oriel windows grace a college, or the half-public mansion of a potent peer, but for the sitting room of quiet country ladies, of ordinary homely folk, nothing can equal the square, mullioned windows of the Tudor architects.", "내닫이창일랑 대학이나 세도 있는 귀족의 반쯤 공적인 저택을 꾸미게 두시오. 조용한 시골 부인들, 평범하고 수수한 사람들이 앉아 지내는 방으로 말하자면, 튜더 시대 건축가들이 지은 저 네모나고 문설주로 나뉜 창에 견줄 것이 없소."]], [["The hall was hung round with family female insipidities by Lely and unprepossessing male Thornes in red coats by Kneller, each Thorne having been let into a panel in the wainscoting, in the proper manner.", "홀에는 렐리가 그린 밋밋한 집안 여인들과 넬러가 그린 붉은 저고리 차림의 볼품없는 손 집안 남자들이 죽 걸려 있었는데, 손 집안 사람 하나하나가 격식대로 벽 판자의 칸 속에 끼워져 있었다."], ["At the further end of the room was a huge fire-place, which afforded much ground of difference between the brother and sister.", "방 저편 끝에는 커다란 벽난로가 있었으니, 이것이 남매 사이에 적잖은 다툼거리를 안겨 주었다."], ["An antiquated grate that would hold about a hundredweight of coal, had been stuck on to the hearth by Mr. Thorne's father.", "석탄을 백 근쯤 담을 만한 구식 쇠 화격자를 손 씨의 아버지가 난로 바닥에 박아 놓았던 것이다."], ["This hearth had of course been intended for the consumption of wood faggots, and the iron dogs for the purpose were still standing, though half-buried in the masonry of the grate.", "이 난로 바닥은 물론 장작단을 때기 위해 만들어진 것이어서, 그 용도로 쓰던 쇠 받침대가 화격자의 돌 틈에 반쯤 파묻힌 채 아직도 서 있었다."], ["Miss Thorne was very anxious to revert to the dogs.", "손 양은 그 쇠 받침대로 되돌아가기를 몹시 바랐다."], ["The dear good old creature was always glad to revert to anything, and had she been systematically indulged, would doubtless in time have reflected that fingers were made before forks and have reverted accordingly.", "그 사랑스럽고 착한 노부인은 무엇으로든 되돌아가기를 언제나 반겼으니, 하자는 대로 다 해 주었더라면 머지않아 포크보다 손가락이 먼저 생겼다는 데에 생각이 미쳐 그에 걸맞게 되돌아갔을 것이 틀림없다."], ["But in the affairs of the fire-place Mr. Thorne would not revert.", "그러나 벽난로에 관한 한 손 씨는 되돌아가려 하지 않았다."], ["Country gentlemen around him all had comfortable grates in their dining-rooms.", "이웃의 시골 신사들은 모두 제 식당에 편리한 화격자를 두고 있었다."], ["He was not exactly the man to have suggested a modern usage, but he was not so far prejudiced as to banish those which his father had prepared for his use.", "그가 요즘 방식을 먼저 제안할 사람은 아니었으나, 아버지가 제 쓰라고 마련해 둔 것을 몰아낼 만큼 편견에 사로잡히지도 않았다."], ["Mr. Thorne had indeed once suggested that with very little contrivance the front door might have been so altered as to open at least into the passage, but on hearing this, his sister Monica--such was Miss Thorne's name--had been taken ill and had remained so for a week.", "사실 손 씨는 조금만 손을 보면 현관문을 고쳐 적어도 복도로 열리게 할 수 있으리라고 한 번 말을 꺼낸 적이 있었는데, 그 말을 들은 누이 모니카는 — 손 양의 이름이 그러하였다 — 자리에 몸져누워 일주일을 그대로 있었다."], ["Before she came downstairs she received a pledge from her brother that the entrance should never be changed in her lifetime.", "아래층으로 내려오기 전에 그녀는 제 살아생전에는 결코 출입구를 바꾸지 않겠다는 다짐을 오라비에게서 받아 냈다."]], [["At the end of the hall opposite to the fire-place a door led into the drawing-room, which was of equal size, and lighted with precisely similar windows.", "벽난로 맞은편 홀 끝에 난 문은 응접실로 이어졌는데, 이 방은 홀과 크기가 같았고 꼭 같은 모양의 창으로 빛을 들였다."], ["But yet the aspect of the room was very different.", "그런데도 방의 느낌은 사뭇 달랐다."], ["It was papered, and the ceiling, which in the hall showed the old rafters, was whitened and finished with a modern cornice.", "벽에는 종이를 발랐고, 홀에서는 옛 서까래가 그대로 드러나 있던 천장이 여기서는 희게 칠해지고 요즘식 돌림띠로 마무리되어 있었다."], ["Miss Thorne's drawing-room, or, as she always called it, withdrawing-room, was a beautiful apartment.", "손 양의 응접실은 — 그녀는 늘 그것을 물러나 앉는 방이라 불렀다 — 아름다운 방이었다. 창은 어여쁘고 말끔한 정원 전부를 향하여 열렸다."], ["The windows opened on to the full extent of the lovely trim garden; immediately before the windows were plots of flowers in stiff, stately, stubborn little beds, each bed surrounded by a stone coping of its own; beyond, there was a low parapet wall on which stood urns and images, fawns, nymphs, satyrs, and a whole tribe of Pan's followers; and then again, beyond that, a beautiful lawn sloped away to a sunk fence which divided the garden from the park.", "창 바로 앞에는 뻣뻣하고 위엄 있고 고집스러운 작은 화단들에 꽃이 심겨 있었고, 화단마다 제 몫의 돌 테두리가 둘려 있었다. 그 너머에는 낮은 난간 담이 있어 그 위에 항아리와 조각상들, 파우누스와 님프와 사티로스, 그리고 판을 따르는 온갖 무리가 서 있었다."], ["Mr. Thorne's study was at the end of the drawing-room, and beyond that were the kitchen and the offices.", "다시 그 너머로는 아름다운 잔디밭이 비스듬히 내려가 땅에 파묻힌 울타리에 닿았고, 그 울타리가 정원과 사냥터를 갈라 놓았다."], ["Doors opened into both Miss Thorne's withdrawing-room and Mr.", "손 씨의 서재는 응접실 끝에 있었고, 그 너머에 부엌과 살림방들이 있었다."], ["Thorne's sanctum from the passage above alluded to, which, as it came to the latter room, widened itself so as to make space for the huge black oak stairs which led to the upper regions.", "손 양의 물러나 앉는 방으로도 손 씨의 성소로도 앞서 말한 복도에서 문이 열렸으며, 그 복도는 뒤쪽 방에 이르러 폭이 넓어지면서 위층으로 오르는 커다란 검은 참나무 계단이 들어설 자리를 내주었다."]], [["Such was the interior of Ullathorne Court.", "울라손 저택의 안은 이러하였다."], ["But having thus described it, perhaps somewhat too tediously, we beg to say that it is not the interior to which we wish to call the English tourist's attention, though we advise him to lose no legitimate opportunity of becoming acquainted with it in a friendly manner.", "그러나 이렇게, 어쩌면 좀 지루하리만큼 늘어놓아 그려 보이고서 하는 말이지만, 우리가 영국 여행자의 눈길을 끌고 싶은 것은 이 안쪽이 아니다. 물론 이 안쪽과 정답게 사귈 마땅한 기회가 오거든 놓치지 말라고 권하기는 하겠다."], ["It is the outside of Ullathorne that is so lovely.", "그토록 사랑스러운 것은 울라손의 바깥이다."], ["Let the tourist get admission at least into the garden and fling himself on that soft sward just opposite to the exterior angle of the house.", "여행자는 적어도 정원에 드는 허락만이라도 얻어, 집이 바깥으로 꺾이는 모퉁이 바로 맞은편의 그 부드러운 잔디 위에 몸을 던져 보라."], ["He will there get the double frontage and enjoy that which is so lovely--the expanse of architectural beauty without the formal dullness of one long line.", "그러면 그는 두 면의 앞모습을 한꺼번에 보게 되고, 그토록 사랑스러운 것 — 곧 한 줄로 길게 뻗은 격식 차린 밋밋함 없이 펼쳐진 건축의 아름다움 — 을 누리게 될 것이다."]], [["It is the colour of Ullathorne that is so remarkable.", "울라손에서 그토록 놀라운 것은 그 빛깔이다."], ["It is of that delicious tawny hue which no stone can give, unless it has on it the vegetable richness of centuries.", "그것은 수백 년에 걸친 초목의 풍요를 제 몸에 얹지 않고서는 어떤 돌도 낼 수 없는, 그 감미로운 황갈색이다. 손으로 담을 쳐 보라."], ["Strike the wall with your hand, and you will think that the stone has on it no covering, but rub it carefully, and you will find that the colour comes off upon your finger.", "그러면 돌 위에 아무것도 덮여 있지 않다고 여기게 될 것이다. 그러나 조심스레 문질러 보라."], ["No colourist that ever yet worked from a palette has been able to come up to this rich colouring of years crowding themselves on years.", "그러면 그 빛깔이 손가락에 묻어 나오는 것을 알게 될 것이다. 팔레트를 놓고 작업한 어떤 색채가도 세월 위에 세월이 겹겹이 쌓여 이룬 이 풍요로운 빛깔에는 여태 미치지 못하였다."]], [["Ullathorne is a high building for a country-house, for it possesses three stories, and in each story the windows are of the same sort as that described, though varying in size and varying also in their lines athwart the house.", "울라손은 시골집치고는 높은 건물이니, 삼층으로 되어 있고 층마다 앞서 말한 것과 같은 종류의 창이 나 있되 크기가 저마다 다르고 집을 가로지르는 줄도 저마다 다르다."], ["Those of the ground floor are all uniform in size and position.", "일층의 창들은 크기도 자리도 모두 한결같다."], ["But those above are irregular both in size and place, and this irregularity gives a bizarre and not unpicturesque appearance to the building.", "그러나 그 위층의 창들은 크기도 자리도 들쭉날쭉하며, 이 들쭉날쭉함이 건물에 기이하면서도 그림 같지 않다고는 할 수 없는 모습을 준다."], ["Along the top, on every side, runs a low parapet, which nearly hides the roof, and at the corners are more figures of fawns and satyrs.", "꼭대기를 따라 사방으로 낮은 난간이 둘려 지붕을 거의 가리고 있고, 모퉁이마다 파우누스와 사티로스의 상이 또 서 있다."]], [["Such is Ullathorne House.", "울라손 저택은 이러하다."], ["But we must say one word of the approach to it, which shall include all the description which we mean to give of the church also. The picturesque old church of St.", "그러나 그리로 다가가는 길에 관하여 한마디 해 두어야겠으니, 여기에 교회에 관해 우리가 하려는 이야기도 모두 함께 담기게 될 것이다."], ["Ewold's stands immediately opposite to the iron gates which open into the court, and is all but surrounded by the branches of the lime-trees which form the avenue leading up to the house from both sides.", "그림 같은 세인트 이월드의 옛 교회는 안뜰로 들어가는 쇠문 바로 맞은편에 서 있고, 양편에서 집으로 이어지는 가로수길을 이루는 보리수 가지에 거의 온통 둘러싸여 있다."], ["This avenue is magnificent, but it would lose much of its value in the eyes of many proprietors by the fact that the road through it is not private property.", "이 가로수길은 훌륭하지만, 그 사이로 난 길이 사유지가 아니라는 사실 때문에 많은 지주들의 눈에는 그 값어치가 크게 깎일 것이다."], ["It is a public lane between hedgerows, with a broad grass margin on each side of the road, from which the lime-trees spring.", "그것은 산울타리 사이로 난 공용 소로이며, 길 양편에는 널찍한 풀밭 가장자리가 있고 그 풀밭에서 보리수가 솟아 있다."], ["Ullathorne Court, therefore, does not stand absolutely surrounded by its own grounds, though Mr. Thorne is owner of all the adjacent land.", "그러므로 울라손 저택은 제 땅에 온전히 둘러싸여 있지 않은 셈이나, 인접한 땅은 모두 손 씨의 소유이다."], ["This, however, is the source of very little annoyance to him.", "그러나 이 일로 그가 성가셔하는 바는 거의 없다."], ["Men, when they are acquiring property, think much of such things, but they who live where their ancestors have lived for years do not feel the misfortune.", "사람이 재산을 늘려 가는 중에는 그런 것을 크게 마음에 두지만, 제 조상이 여러 해 살아온 곳에서 사는 이들은 그것을 불행으로 느끼지 않는다."], ["It never occurred either to Mr. or Miss Thorne that they were not sufficiently private because the world at large might, if it so wished, walk or drive by their iron gates.", "세상 사람들이 마음만 먹으면 제 쇠문 앞을 걷거나 마차로 지날 수 있다고 하여 저희가 충분히 사적이지 못하다는 생각은 손 씨에게도 손 양에게도 떠오른 적이 없다."], ["That part of the world which availed itself of the privilege was however very small.", "다만 그 특권을 실제로 누리는 세상 사람이란 아주 적었다."]], [["Such a year or two since were the Thornes of Ullathorne.", "한두 해 전 울라손의 손 남매는 이러하였다."], ["Such, we believe, are the inhabitants of many an English country-home.", "우리가 믿기로는 영국의 숱한 시골집 사람들이 이러하다."], ["May it be long before their number diminishes.", "부디 그 수가 줄어들기까지 오랜 세월이 걸리기를."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII. Mr. Arabin Reads Himself in at St. Ewold's", "# 제23장. 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드에서 취임 낭독을 하다"]], [["On the Sunday morning the archdeacon with his sister-in-law and Mr. Arabin drove over to Ullathorne, as had been arranged.", "일요일 아침, 부주교는 처제와 애러빈 씨를 데리고 예정대로 울라손으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["On their way thither the new vicar declared himself to be considerably disturbed in his mind at the idea of thus facing his parishioners for the first time.", "가는 길에 새 교구 사제는 처음으로 제 교구민들 앞에 서야 한다는 생각에 마음이 적잖이 어지럽다고 털어놓았다."], ["He had, he said, been always subject to mauvaise honte and an annoying degree of bashfulness, which often unfitted him for any work of a novel description; and now he felt this so strongly that he feared he should acquit himself badly in St. Ewold's reading-desk.", "자기는 늘 mauvaise honte에, 성가실 만큼 심한 수줍음에 시달려 왔고 그 탓에 처음 해 보는 종류의 일이라면 무엇이든 제대로 해내지 못하기 일쑤였는데, 지금이 바로 그러해서 세인트 이월드의 낭독대에서 스스로 망신을 당하지나 않을까 두렵다는 것이었다."], ["He knew, he said, that those sharp little eyes of Miss Thorne would be on him, and that they would not approve.", "손 양의 그 날카롭고 작은 눈이 자기를 지켜볼 테고 그 눈이 흡족해하지 않으리라는 것도 안다고 그는 말했다."], ["All this the archdeacon greatly ridiculed.", "부주교는 이 모든 말을 몹시 우스워했다."], ["He himself knew not, and had never known, what it was to be shy.", "그 자신은 수줍음이 무엇인지 알지 못했고 여태 알아 본 적도 없었다."], ["He could not conceive that Miss Thorne, surrounded as she would be by the peasants of Ullathorne and a few of the poorer inhabitants of the suburbs of Barchester, could in any way affect the composure of a man well accustomed to address the learned congregation of St.", "울라손의 소작인들과 바체스터 변두리의 가난한 주민 몇몇에게 둘러싸여 있을 손 양이, 옥스퍼드 세인트 메리 교회의 그 학식 높은 회중을 상대로 말하는 데 익숙한 사람의 침착함을 어떻게 흔들 수 있단 말인가."], ["Mary's at Oxford, and he laughed accordingly at the idea of Mr. Arabin's modesty.", "그래서 그는 애러빈 씨의 겸양이라는 것을 두고 소리 내어 웃었다."]], [["Thereupon Mr. Arabin commenced to subtilize.", "그러자 애러빈 씨는 잘게 따지고 들기 시작했다."], ["The change, he said, from St. Mary's to St.", "세인트 메리에서 세인트 이월드로 옮겨 오는 변화는 세인트 이월드에서 세인트 메리로 옮겨 가는 변화만큼이나 사람의 마음을 뒤흔든다고 그는 말했다."], ["Ewold's was quite as powerful on the spirits as would be that from St. Ewold's to St.", "어쩌다 운수가 뒤집혀 갑자기 막노동꾼들 틈에 섞여 살게 된 귀족이라면, 별안간 귀족들 사이에 자리를 얻은 막노동꾼이 그러하듯, 제 동료들의 비웃음을 두려워하지 않겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["Mary's. Would not a peer who, by chance of fortune, might suddenly be driven to herd among navvies be as afraid of the jeers of his companions as would any navvy suddenly exalted to a seat among the peers?", "그 말에 부주교는 큰 소리로 웃으며, 새로 온 성직자가 손 양을 막노동꾼에 견주더라고 손 양에게 일러바치겠다고 선언했다."], ["Whereupon the archdeacon declared with a loud laugh that he would tell Miss Thorne that her new minister had likened her to a navvy.", "그러나 엘리너는 그런 결론은 온당치 않다고 했다."], ["Eleanor, however, pronounced such a conclusion to be unfair; a comparison might be very just in its proportions which did not at all assimilate the things compared. But Mr.", "견주어진 두 가지를 조금도 같은 것으로 만들지 않으면서도 그 비례만은 아주 정확한 비유가 있을 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["Arabin went on subtilizing, regarding neither the archdeacon's raillery nor Eleanor's defence.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 부주교의 놀림에도 엘리너의 두둔에도 아랑곳없이 계속 따지고 들었다."], ["A young lady, he said, would execute with most perfect self-possession a difficult piece of music in a room crowded with strangers, who would not be able to express herself in intelligible language, even on any ordinary subject and among her most intimate friends, if she were required to do so standing on a box somewhat elevated among them.", "어떤 젊은 아가씨는 낯선 사람들로 가득한 방에서 어려운 곡을 더할 나위 없이 침착하게 연주해 내면서도, 가장 가까운 벗들 사이에서 그저 평범한 이야기를 하는 것조차 사람들보다 조금 높은 상자 위에 올라서서 하라고 하면 알아들을 만한 말로 제 뜻을 밝히지 못한다고 그는 말했다."], ["It was all an affair of education, and he at forty found it difficult to educate himself anew.", "그것은 전적으로 익힘의 문제인데, 마흔에 이른 자기로서는 스스로를 새로 익히기가 어렵다는 것이었다."]], [["Eleanor dissented on the matter of the box, and averred she could speak very well about dresses, or babies, or legs of mutton from any box, provided it were big enough for her to stand upon without fear, even though all her friends were listening to her.", "엘리너는 상자 이야기에는 동의하지 않았다."], ["The archdeacon was sure she would not be able to say a word, but this proved nothing in favour of Mr. Arabin.", "무서움 없이 올라설 만큼 넉넉히 크기만 하다면 어떤 상자 위에서든, 벗들이 모두 듣고 있다 하더라도 옷이나 아기나 양고기 다리에 대해서라면 아주 잘 이야기할 수 있노라고 그녀는 장담했다."], ["Mr. Arabin said that he would try the question out with Mrs.", "부주교는 그녀가 한마디도 하지 못하리라고 확신했지만 그것이 애러빈 씨에게 유리한 증거가 되지는 않는다고 했다."], ["Bold, and get her on a box some day when the rectory might be full of visitors.", "애러빈 씨는 언젠가 목사관에 손님이 가득할 때 볼드 부인을 상자 위에 세워 이 문제를 끝까지 겨루어 보겠다고 말했다."], ["To this Eleanor assented, making condition that the visitors should be of their own set, and the archdeacon cogitated in his mind whether by such a condition it was intended that Mr. Slope should be included, resolving also that, if so, the trial would certainly never take place in the rectory drawing-room at Plumstead.", "엘리너는 손님이 자기네 편 사람들이어야 한다는 조건을 달고 이에 응했고, 부주교는 그런 조건에 슬로프 씨도 포함하겠다는 뜻이 담긴 것은 아닌지 속으로 궁리하면서, 만약 그렇다면 그 시험은 플럼스테드 목사관 응접실에서는 결코 벌어지지 않으리라고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["And so arguing, they drove up to the iron gates of Ullathorne Court.", "그렇게 옥신각신하는 사이 마차는 울라손 저택의 철문 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["Mr. and Miss Thorne were standing ready dressed for church in the hall, and greeted their clerical visitors with cordiality.", "손 씨와 손 양은 예배에 갈 채비를 다 갖추고 현관에 서 있다가 성직자 손님들을 살갑게 맞았다."], ["The archdeacon was an old favourite.", "부주교는 오래된 총아였다."], ["He was a clergyman of the old school, and this recommended him to the lady.", "그는 구식 성직자였고, 그 점이 이 댁 부인의 마음에 들었다."], ["He had always been an opponent of free trade as long as free trade was an open question, and now that it was no longer so, he, being a clergyman, had not been obliged, like most of his lay Tory companions, to read his recantation.", "자유 무역이 아직 논쟁거리로 열려 있는 동안 그는 줄곧 자유 무역의 반대자였고, 이제 그것이 더는 논쟁거리가 아니게 된 지금도 그는 성직자인 까닭에 세속의 토리 동료들 대부분과 달리 제 주장을 철회한다고 밝혀야 할 의무가 없었다."], ["He could therefore be regarded as a supporter of the immaculate fifty-three, and was on this account a favourite with Mr. Thorne.", "그러므로 그는 흠 없는 53인의 지지자로 여겨질 수 있었고, 바로 그 점 때문에 손 씨의 총애를 받았다."], ["The little bell was tinkling, and the rural population of the parish were standing about the lane, leaning on the church-stile and against the walls of the old court, anxious to get a look at their new minister as he passed from the house to the rectory.", "작은 종이 딸랑거리고 있었고, 교구의 시골 사람들은 새 성직자가 저택에서 목사관 쪽으로 지나가는 모습을 한번 보려고 골목에 서서 교회 층계문에 기대거나 오래된 저택 담벼락에 몸을 붙이고 있었다."], ["The archdeacon's servant had already preceded them thither with the vestments.", "부주교의 하인은 예복을 들고 벌써 그리로 앞서 가 있었다."]], [["They all went forth together, and when the ladies passed into the church, the three gentlemen tarried a moment in the lane, that Mr.", "그들은 다 함께 밖으로 나섰고, 부인들이 교회 안으로 들어간 뒤 세 신사는 잠시 골목에 머물렀다."], ["Thorne might name to the vicar with some kind of one-sided introduction the most leading among his parishioners.", "손 씨가 교구민 가운데 가장 유력한 이들을 새 교구 사제에게 한쪽만 소개하는 식으로 알려 주기 위해서였다."]], [["\"Here are our churchwardens, Mr.", "“여기 우리 교회위원들이오, 애러빈 씨 — 그린에이커 농부와 스타일스 씨요."], ["Arabin--Farmer Greenacre and Mr. Stiles.", "스타일스 씨는 바체스터로 들어가는 길목에서 방앗간을 하고 있소."], ["Mr. Stiles has the mill as you go into Barchester; and very good churchwardens they are.\"", "두 사람 다 아주 훌륭한 교회위원이라오.”"]], [["\"Not very severe, I hope,\"", "“너무 엄하지는 않으시기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다."], ["The two ecclesiastical officers touched their hats, and each made a leg in the approved rural fashion, assuring the vicar that they were very glad to have the honour of seeing him, and adding that the weather was very good for the harvest.", "두 교회 직원은 모자에 손을 얹고 시골식 예법에 따라 각각 한쪽 다리를 뒤로 빼며 절을 했다. 그러고는 이렇게 뵙는 영광을 누리게 되어 매우 기쁘다고 교구 사제에게 인사한 뒤, 추수하기에 날씨가 아주 좋다고 덧붙였다."], ["Mr. Stiles, being a man somewhat versed in town life, had an impression of his own dignity, and did not quite like leaving his pastor under the erroneous idea that he being a churchwarden kept the children in order during church time.", "도회지 물을 좀 먹은 축인 스타일스 씨는 제 위신에 대한 나름의 생각이 있어서, 교회위원인 자기가 예배 시간에 아이들을 단속한다는 잘못된 짐작을 목자가 그대로 품게 두고 싶지 않았다."], ["'Twas thus he understood Mr.", "애러빈 씨가 제 엄함을 두고 한 말을 그는 그렇게 알아들었고, 그래서 서둘러 바로잡으려고 “애들은 클로디브 교회지기가 봅니다요."], ["Arabin's allusion to his severity and hastened to put matters right by observing that \"Sexton Clodheve looked to the younguns, and perhaps sometimes there may be a thought too much stick going on during sermon.\"", "설교 중에 매질이 좀 지나칠 때가 더러 있기도 합지요.” 하고 말했다."], ["Mr. Arabin's bright eye twinkled as he caught that of the archdeacon, and he smiled to himself as he observed how ignorant his officers were of the nature of their authority and of the surveillance which it was their duty to keep even over himself.", "부주교와 눈이 마주치자 애러빈 씨의 밝은 눈이 반짝였고, 그는 제 관리들이 제 권한의 성격을, 그리고 그들이 자기 자신까지도 감독해야 하는 그 직분을 얼마나 모르고 있는지를 보며 속으로 웃었다."]], [["Mr. Arabin read the lessons and preached.", "애러빈 씨는 성경 봉독을 하고 설교를 했다."], ["It was enough to put a man a little out, let him have been ever so used to pulpit reading, to see the knowing way in which the farmers cocked their ears and set about a mental criticism as to whether their new minister did or did not fall short of the excellence of him who had lately departed from them. A mental and silent criticism it was for the existing moment, but soon to be made public among the elders of St.", "강단 낭독에 아무리 익숙한 사람이라도, 새로 온 성직자가 얼마 전 저희를 떠난 이의 훌륭함에 미치는지 못 미치는지를 속으로 견주어 보느라 농부들이 아는 체하며 귀를 쫑긋 세우는 꼴을 보면 조금은 마음이 흔들릴 만했다."], ["Ewold's over the green graves of their children and forefathers. The excellence, however, of poor old Mr.", "지금 이 순간에는 마음속의 소리 없는 비평이었지만, 머지않아 세인트 이월드의 노인들 사이에서, 제 자식들과 조상들의 푸른 무덤 위에서 공공연히 오갈 비평이었다."], ["Goodenough had not been wonderful, and there were few there who did not deem that Mr. Arabin did his work sufficiently well, in spite of the slightly nervous affliction which at first impeded him, and which nearly drove the archdeacon beside himself.", "그러나 가엾은 노 구드너프 씨의 훌륭함이라는 것도 그리 대단치는 않았던 터라, 처음에 그를 조금 더듬거리게 만들어 부주교를 거의 미치게 했던 그 가벼운 긴장에도 불구하고 애러빈 씨가 제 몫을 충분히 잘해 냈다고 여기지 않는 사람은 거기에 거의 없었다."]], [["But the sermon was the thing to try the man.", "그러나 사람을 시험하는 것은 역시 설교였다."], ["It often surprises us that very young men can muster courage to preach for the first time to a strange congregation.", "아주 젊은 사람들이 낯선 회중 앞에서 난생처음 설교할 용기를 낸다는 것은 우리에게 종종 놀라운 일이다."], ["Men who are as yet but little more than boys, who have but just left what indeed we may not call a school, but a seminary intended for their tuition as scholars, whose thoughts have been mostly of boating, cricketing, and wine-parties, ascend a rostrum high above the heads of the submissive crowd, not that they may read God's word to those below, but that they may preach their own word for the edification of their hearers.", "아직 소년보다 조금 나은 정도인 사람들이, 학교라고 부를 수는 없으나 학자로 길러 내기 위한 배움터라 할 만한 곳을 갓 나온 사람들이, 그 머릿속이 대개 뱃놀이와 크리켓과 술자리로 채워져 있던 사람들이, 순종하는 무리의 머리보다 훨씬 높은 연단에 오른다. 아래 있는 이들에게 하나님의 말씀을 읽어 주기 위해서가 아니라, 듣는 이들을 교화하겠다며 제 말을 설교하기 위해서다."], ["It seems strange to us that they are not stricken dumb by the new and awful solemnity of their position.", "그 새롭고 두려운 자리의 엄숙함에 그들이 벙어리가 되지 않는다는 것이 우리에게는 이상해 보인다."], ["\"How am I, just turned twenty-three, who have never yet passed ten thoughtful days since the power of thought first came to me, how am I to instruct these greybeards who, with the weary thinking of so many years, have approached so near the grave?", "“생각하는 힘이 내게 처음 생긴 이래 사려 깊은 날이라고는 열흘도 보내 본 적이 없는, 이제 갓 스물셋이 된 내가 어떻게, 그 숱한 세월의 고단한 생각으로 무덤 가까이 다다른 저 백발의 노인들을 가르친단 말인가? 내가 그들에게 그들의 본분을 가르칠 수 있는가?"], ["Can I teach them their duty? Can I explain to them that which I so imperfectly understand, that which years of study may have made so plain to them?", "내가 그토록 어설프게 이해하고 있는 것을, 그들에게는 여러 해의 공부로 이미 환해졌을 그것을 내가 설명할 수 있는가?"], ["Has my newly acquired privilege as one of God's ministers imparted to me as yet any fitness for the wonderful work of a preacher?\"", "하나님의 사역자 가운데 하나가 되었다는 이 갓 얻은 특권이, 설교자라는 저 놀라운 일에 걸맞은 자질을 내게 조금이라도 나누어 주었는가?”"]], [["It must be supposed that such ideas do occur to young clergymen, and yet they overcome, apparently with ease, this difficulty which to us appears to be all but insurmountable.", "그런 생각이 젊은 성직자들에게 실제로 떠오르리라고 보아야 할 텐데, 그럼에도 그들은 우리에게는 거의 넘을 수 없어 보이는 이 어려움을 겉보기에는 손쉽게 이겨 낸다."], ["We have never been subjected in the way of ordination to the power of a bishop's hands.", "우리는 안수를 받느라 주교의 손 아래 놓여 본 적이 없다."], ["It may be that there is in them something that sustains the spirit and banishes the natural modesty of youth.", "어쩌면 그 손 안에 정신을 떠받치고 젊음의 타고난 수줍음을 몰아내는 무언가가 있는지도 모른다."], ["But for ourselves we must own that the deep affection which Dominie Sampson felt for his young pupils has not more endeared him to us than the bashful spirit which sent him mute and inglorious from the pulpit when he rose there with the futile attempt to preach God's gospel.", "그러나 우리로서는 이렇게 고백해야겠다. 도미니 샘슨이 어린 제자들에게 품었던 그 깊은 애정보다도, 하나님의 복음을 전해 보겠다는 헛된 시도로 강단에 올랐다가 한마디도 못 하고 초라하게 내려오게 만든 그 수줍은 심성이 우리에게는 더 정답다."]], [["There is a rule in our church which forbids the younger order of our clergymen to perform a certain portion of the service.", "우리 교회에는 아래 등급의 성직자가 예배의 어느 부분을 집전하지 못하도록 금하는 규칙이 있다."], ["The absolution must be read by a minister in priest's orders.", "사죄 선언은 사제 품계를 받은 성직자가 낭독해야 한다."], ["If there be no such minister present, the congregation can have the benefit of no absolution but that which each may succeed in administering to himself.", "그런 성직자가 그 자리에 없으면 회중은 각자가 스스로에게 베풀 수 있는 사죄 말고는 어떤 사죄의 은혜도 누릴 수 없다."], ["The rule may be a good one, though the necessity for it hardly comes home to the general understanding.", "그 규칙이 왜 필요한지는 보통의 이해로는 좀처럼 와닿지 않으나, 좋은 규칙일지도 모른다."], ["But this forbearance on the part of youth would be much more appreciated if it were extended likewise to sermons.", "그러나 젊은이가 삼가는 이 미덕이 설교에까지 미친다면 훨씬 더 고맙게 여겨질 것이다."], ["The only danger would be that congregations would be too anxious to prevent their young clergymen from advancing themselves in the ranks of the ministry.", "다만 한 가지 위험이 있다면, 회중이 저희 젊은 성직자가 성직의 계단을 올라가는 것을 너무나도 막고 싶어 하리라는 점이다."], ["Clergymen who could not preach would be such blessings that they would be bribed to adhere to their incompetence.", "설교할 줄 모르는 성직자는 그야말로 복이어서, 사람들이 뇌물을 주어서라도 그 무능에 머물러 있게 하려 들 것이다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, however, had not the modesty of youth to impede him, and he succeeded with his sermon even better than with the lessons.", "그러나 애러빈 씨에게는 그를 가로막을 젊음의 수줍음이 없었고, 그는 성경 봉독보다도 설교에서 한결 더 잘해 냈다."], ["He took for his text two verses out of the second epistle of St.", "그는 요한이서에서 두 구절을 본문으로 삼았다."], ["John, \"Whosoever transgresseth, and abideth not in the doctrine of Christ, hath not God. He that abideth in the doctrine of Christ, he hath both the Father and the Son.", "“지내쳐 그리스도의 교훈 안에 거하지 아니하는 자는 다 하나님을 모시지 못하되 교훈 안에 거하는 그 사람은 아버지와 아들을 모시느니라."], ["If there come any unto you, and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into your house, neither bid him God-speed.\"", "누구든지 이 교훈을 가지지 않고 너희에게 나아가거든 그를 집에 들이지도 말고 인사도 말라.”"], ["He told them that the house of theirs to which he alluded was this their church, in which he now addressed them for the first time; that their most welcome and proper manner of bidding him God-speed would be their patient obedience to his teaching of the gospel; but that he could put forward no claim to such conduct on their part unless he taught them the great Christian doctrine of works and faith combined.", "그는 자기가 가리킨 그들의 집이란 바로 이 그들의 교회, 자기가 오늘 처음으로 그들에게 말을 건네고 있는 이곳이라고 일렀다. 자기에게 인사하는 가장 반갑고 온당한 방법은 자기가 전하는 복음의 가르침에 참을성 있게 순종하는 것이겠으나, 믿음과 행함이 함께 간다는 저 위대한 그리스도교의 교리를 자기가 가르치지 않는 한 그들에게 그런 태도를 요구할 자격이 자기에게는 없다고 했다."], ["On this he enlarged, but not very amply, and after twenty minutes succeeded in sending his new friends home to their baked mutton and pudding well pleased with their new minister.", "그는 이 대목을 부연했으나 그리 길게 늘이지는 않았고, 20분 만에 새 벗들을 구운 양고기와 푸딩이 기다리는 집으로, 새 성직자에게 흡족해하며 돌려보내는 데 성공했다."]], [["Then came the lunch at Ullathorne. As soon as they were in the hall Miss Thorne took Mr. Arabin's hand and assured him that she received him into her house, into the temple, she said, in which she worshipped, and bade him God-speed with all her heart. Mr. Arabin was touched and squeezed the spinster's hand without uttering a word in reply. Then Mr. Thorne expressed a hope that Mr. Arabin found the church well adapted for articulation, and Mr. Arabin having replied that he had no doubt he should as soon as he had learnt to pitch his voice to the building, they all sat down to the good things before them.", "그러고 나서 울라손에서 점심이 있었다. 현관에 들어서자마자 손 양은 애러빈 씨의 손을 잡고, 자기가 그를 제 집으로, 자기가 예배드리는 성전으로 맞아들이는 것이라고 말하며 마음을 다해 그의 앞길을 축복한다고 했다. 애러빈 씨는 마음이 움직여 한마디 대답도 못 한 채 그 노처녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그러자 손 씨가 그 교회가 발성에 잘 맞더냐고 물었고, 애러빈 씨가 그 건물에 목소리를 맞추는 법만 익히면 틀림없이 그러할 것이라고 대답하자, 다들 차려진 좋은 음식 앞에 앉았다."]], [["Miss Thorne took special care of Mrs.", "손 양은 볼드 부인을 각별히 챙겼다."], ["Bold. Eleanor still wore her widow's weeds, and therefore had about her that air of grave and sad maternity which is the lot of recent widows.", "엘리너는 아직 미망인의 상복을 입고 있었고, 그래서 얼마 전 남편을 잃은 여인들의 몫인 그 무겁고 슬픈 모성의 기운을 두르고 있었다."], ["This opened the soft heart of Miss Thorne, and made her look on her young guest as though too much could not be done for her.", "그것이 손 양의 여린 마음을 열어 놓아, 이 젊은 손님에게는 무엇을 해 주어도 지나치지 않다는 듯 그녀를 바라보게 했다."], ["She heaped chicken and ham upon her plate and poured out for her a full bumper of port wine.", "손 양은 엘리너의 접시에 닭고기와 햄을 수북이 얹어 주고 포트 와인을 잔이 넘치도록 따라 주었다."], ["When Eleanor, who was not sorry to get it, had drunk a little of it, Miss Thorne at once essayed to fill it again.", "마다할 것도 없었던 엘리너가 조금 마시자 손 양은 곧바로 그 잔을 다시 채우려 들었다."], ["To this Eleanor objected, but in vain.", "엘리너가 이를 사양했으나 소용이 없었다."], ["Miss Thorne winked and nodded and whispered, saying that it was the proper thing and must be done, and that she knew all about it; and so she desired Mrs.", "손 양은 눈짓을 하고 고개를 끄덕이며, 이것이 마땅한 일이고 반드시 그리해야 하며 자기가 그 사정을 다 안다고 소곤거렸다."], ["Bold to drink it up and not mind anybody.", "그러고는 볼드 부인더러 남 눈치 볼 것 없이 다 마시라고 했다."]], [["\"It is your duty, you know, to support yourself,\"", "“몸을 지탱하는 것이 댁의 도리요.”"], ["she said into the ear of the young mother; \"there's more than yourself depending on it;\"", "그녀는 젊은 어미의 귀에 대고 말했다. “거기 매인 것이 댁 하나만이 아니잖소.”"], ["and thus she coshered up Eleanor with cold fowl and port wine.", "그렇게 그녀는 차가운 닭고기와 포트 와인으로 엘리너를 어르고 먹였다."], ["How it is that poor men's wives, who have no cold fowl and port wine on which to be coshered up, nurse their children without difficulty, whereas the wives of rich men, who eat and drink everything that is good, cannot do so, we will for the present leave to the doctors and the mothers to settle between them.", "어르고 먹일 차가운 닭고기도 포트 와인도 없는 가난한 이의 아내들은 아무 어려움 없이 제 아이를 젖 먹여 기르는데, 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 먹고 마시는 부자의 아내들은 어찌하여 그러지 못하는지, 그 문제는 당분간 의사들과 어머니들이 저희끼리 결말을 짓도록 남겨 두기로 하자."]], [["And then Miss Thorne was great about teeth.", "그다음으로 손 양이 열을 올린 것은 이였다."], ["Little Johnny Bold had been troubled for the last few days with his first incipient masticator, and with that freemasonry which exists among ladies, Miss Thorne became aware of the fact before Eleanor had half-finished her wing.", "어린 조니 볼드는 며칠 전부터 처음 돋아나려는 첫 이 때문에 보채고 있었는데, 부인네들 사이에 있는 그 비밀 결사 같은 통함으로 손 양은 엘리너가 닭 날개를 절반도 먹기 전에 그 사실을 알아차렸다."], ["The old lady prescribed at once a receipt which had been much in vogue in the young days of her grandmother, and warned Eleanor with solemn voice against the fallacies of modern medicine.", "노부인은 제 할머니의 젊은 시절에 크게 유행했던 처방을 당장 일러 주고는, 근래 의술의 헛됨을 두고 엄숙한 목소리로 엘리너에게 경고했다."]], [["\"Take his coral, my dear,\"", "“아기 산호를 가져다가,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"and rub it well with carrot-juice; rub it till the juice dries on it, and then give it him to play with--\"", "“당근즙을 잘 문질러 바르시오. 즙이 그 위에서 마를 때까지 문지른 다음에 아기 손에 쥐여 주어 놀게 하면—”"]], [["\"But he hasn't got a coral,\"", "“그런데 그 애한테는 산호가 없어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"Not got a coral!\"", "“산호가 없다고!”"], ["said Miss Thorne with almost angry vehemence.", "손 양이 거의 노여워하다시피 목소리를 높였다."], ["\"Not got a coral--how can you expect that he should cut his teeth?", "“산호가 없다니 — 그러고서 어떻게 아이 이가 나기를 바란단 말이오?"], ["Have you got Daffy's Elixir?\"", "대피 영약은 갖고 있소?”"]], [["Eleanor explained that she had not.", "엘리너는 그것도 없다고 설명했다."], ["It had not been ordered by Mr.", "자기가 부르는 바체스터 의사 리어차일드 씨가 처방해 준 적이 없다는 것이었다."], ["Rerechild, the Barchester doctor whom she employed; and then the young mother mentioned some shockingly modern succedaneum which Mr. Rerechild's new lights had taught him to recommend.", "그러고서 젊은 어미는 리어차일드 씨가 새로 얻은 식견에 따라 권하게 되었다는, 듣기에 소름 끼치도록 현대적인 대용약의 이름을 입에 올렸다."]], [["Miss Thorne looked awfully severe.", "손 양의 표정이 무섭도록 엄해졌다."], ["\"Take care, my dear,\"", "“조심하시오.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"that the man knows what he's about; take care he doesn't destroy your little boy.", "“그 사람이 제가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지나 아는지 조심하란 말이오. 그자가 댁의 어린 아들을 망쳐 놓지 않도록 조심하시오."], ["But\"--and she softened into sorrow, as she said it, and spoke more in pity than in anger--\"but I don't know who there is in Barchester now that you can trust.", "하지만” — 그러면서 그녀는 슬픔으로 누그러졌고, 노여움보다는 안타까움을 실어 말했다 — “하지만 지금 바체스터에 믿을 만한 사람이 누가 있는지 나로서는 알 수가 없구려."], ["Poor dear old Doctor Bumpwell, indeed--\"", "가엾은 범프웰 노 박사님이라면 그야—”"]], [["\"Why, Miss Thorne, he died when I was a little girl.\"", "“아니, 손 양, 그분은 제가 어린 여자아이일 때 돌아가셨는데요.”"]], [["\"Yes, my dear, he did, and an unfortunate day it was for Barchester.", "“그렇소, 이보오, 그분은 돌아가셨지. 바체스터로서는 불운한 날이었소."], ["As to those young men that have come up since\"--Mr.", "그 뒤로 올라온 젊은 사람들로 말하면”"], ["Rerechild, by the by, was quite as old as Miss Thorne herself--\"one doesn't know where they came from or who they are, or whether they know anything about their business or not.\"", "— 그런데 리어차일드 씨는 손 양 자신과 나이가 엇비슷했다 — “어디서 왔는지, 누구인지, 제 일을 조금이라도 아는지 어떤지 도무지 알 수가 없단 말이오.”"]], [["\"I think there are very clever men in Barchester,\"", "“바체스터에도 아주 훌륭한 분들이 계신다고 생각해요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps there may be; only I don't know them: and it's admitted on all sides that medical men aren't now what they used to be.", "“그럴지도 모르지. 다만 내가 그 사람들을 모를 뿐이오."], ["They used to be talented, observing, educated men.", "그리고 요즘 의원들이 예전 같지 않다는 것은 누구나 인정하는 바요."], ["But now any whipper-snapper out of an apothecary's shop can call himself a doctor.", "예전에는 재주 있고 잘 살피고 배운 사람들이었소. 그런데 이제는 약재상 가게에서 굴러 나온 애송이라면 누구나 제 입으로 의사라 하지."], ["I believe no kind of education is now thought necessary.\"", "요즘은 어떤 배움도 필요치 않다고 여기는 모양이오.”"]], [["Eleanor was herself the widow of a medical man and felt a little inclined to resent all these hard sayings.", "엘리너 자신이 의사의 미망인이었으니 이런 모진 말들에 조금은 발끈할 만도 했다."], ["But Miss Thorne was so essentially good-natured that it was impossible to resent anything she said.", "그러나 손 양은 워낙 천성이 좋은 사람이어서 그녀가 하는 말에는 무엇에도 발끈할 수가 없었다."], ["She therefore sipped her wine and finished her chicken.", "그래서 엘리너는 포도주를 홀짝이며 닭고기를 다 먹었다."]], [["\"At any rate, my dear, don't forget the carrot-juice, and by all means get him a coral at once.", "“어쨌든 이보오, 당근즙은 잊지 말고, 무슨 수를 써서라도 아기 산호는 당장 구해 주시오."], ["My grandmother Thorne had the best teeth in the county and carried them to the grave with her at eighty.", "우리 할머니 손 부인은 이 고을에서 가장 좋은 이를 지니셨고 여든에 그것을 그대로 지닌 채 무덤에 드셨소."], ["I have heard her say it was all the carrot-juice.", "그게 다 당근즙 덕이라고 하시는 말씀을 나는 들었소."], ["She couldn't bear the Barchester doctors.", "할머니는 바체스터 의사들을 견디지 못하셨지."], ["Even poor old Dr. Bumpwell didn't please her.\"", "가엾은 범프웰 노 박사님조차 할머니 마음에는 차지 않았다오.”"], ["It clearly never occurred to Miss Thorne that some fifty years ago Dr. Bumpwell was only a rising man and therefore as much in need of character in the eyes of the then ladies of Ullathorne as the present doctors were in her own.", "오십 년쯤 전에는 범프웰 박사도 이제 막 올라오는 사람이었을 뿐이고, 따라서 그 시절 울라손 부인네들의 눈에는 지금 의사들이 손 양의 눈에 그러하듯 신용이 아쉬운 처지였으리라는 생각은 손 양에게 끝내 떠오르지 않은 것이 분명했다."]], [["The archdeacon made a very good lunch, and talked to his host about turnip-drillers and new machines for reaping, while the host, thinking it only polite to attend to a stranger, and fearing that perhaps he might not care about turnip crops on a Sunday, mooted all manner of ecclesiastical subjects.", "부주교는 아주 잘 먹었고, 주인에게 순무 파종기며 새로 나온 수확 기계 이야기를 했다. 한편 주인은 손님을 살피는 것이 예의라고만 여긴 데다 어쩌면 이 손님이 일요일에 순무 농사 이야기를 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다는 걱정에, 온갖 교회 이야기를 꺼내 들었다."]], [["\"I never saw a heavier lot of wheat, Thorne, than you've got there in that field beyond the copse.", "“손, 저 잡목숲 너머 밭에 난 밀만큼 실한 것은 본 적이 없소."], ["I suppose that's guano,\"", "구아노를 쓴 모양이구려.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, guano.", "“그렇소, 구아노요."], ["I get it from Bristol myself.", "나는 브리스틀에서 직접 가져오지요."], ["You'll find you often have a tolerable congregation of Barchester people out here, Mr.", "애러빈 씨, 여기 바체스터 사람들이 제법 많이 예배에 나오는 것을 보게 될 거요."], ["Arabin. They are very fond of St.", "사람들이 세인트 이월드를 아주 좋아하거든."], ["Ewold's, particularly of an afternoon when the weather is not too hot for the walk.\"", "걷기에 너무 덥지 않은 오후면 더욱 그렇소.”"]], [["\"I am under an obligation to them for staying away to-day, at any rate,\"", "“어쨌든 오늘 오지 않아 준 것만은 그분들께 고마울 따름입니다.”"], ["said the vicar.", "교구 사제가 말했다."], ["\"The congregation can never be too small for a maiden sermon.\"", "“첫 설교에는 회중이 적으면 적을수록 좋지요.”"]], [["\"I got a ton and a half at Bradley's in High Street,\"", "“하이 스트리트의 브래들리네에서 1.5톤을 샀는데,”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"and it was a complete take in.", "부주교가 말했다. “완전히 속아 넘어갔소."], ["I don't believe there was five hundredweight of guano in it.\"", "그 속에 구아노가 5헌드레드웨이트나 들었을까 싶소.”"]], [["\"That Bradley never has anything good,\"", "“그 브래들리라는 사람은 좋은 물건이라고는 없지요.”"], ["said Miss Thorne, who had just caught the name during her whisperings with Eleanor.", "엘리너와 소곤거리다 그 이름만 얼핏 알아들은 손 양이 말했다."], ["\"And such a nice shop as there used to be in that very house before he came.", "“그 사람이 오기 전에는 바로 그 집이 얼마나 좋은 가게였는데."], ["Wilfred, don't you remember what good things old Ambleoff used to have?\"", "윌프레드, 늙은 앰블오프가 얼마나 좋은 물건을 두었는지 기억나지 않소?”"]], [["\"There have been three men since Ambleoff's time,\"", "“앰블오프 시절 이후로 사람이 셋이나 바뀌었는데,”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"and each as bad as the other.", "부주교가 말했다. “하나같이 서로 못지않게 형편없었소."], ["But who gets it for you at Bristol, Thorne?\"", "그런데 손, 브리스틀에서는 누가 그것을 대 주오?”"]], [["\"I ran up myself this year and bought it out of the ship.", "“올해는 내가 직접 올라가서 배에서 바로 사 왔소."], ["I am afraid as the evenings get shorter, Mr. Arabin, you'll find the reading-desk too dark.", "애러빈 씨, 저녁이 짧아지면 낭독대가 너무 어두워서 곤란하실 거요."], ["I must send a fellow with an axe and make him lop off some of those branches.\"", "사람을 시켜 도끼로 저 가지들을 좀 쳐 내게 해야겠소.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin declared that the morning light at any rate was perfect, and deprecated any interference with the lime-trees.", "애러빈 씨는 아침 빛만은 더할 나위 없다고 하면서 보리수에 손대는 일에는 반대했다."], ["And then they took a stroll out among the trim parterres, and Mr. Arabin explained to Mrs.", "그러고서 그들은 잘 손질된 화단 사이를 거닐었고, 애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인에게 나이아드와 드리아드의 차이를 설명해 주고 화병과 항아리의 모양에 대해 길게 이야기했다."], ["Bold the difference between a naiad and a dryad, and dilated on vases and the shapes of urns. Miss Thorne busied herself among her pansies, and her brother, finding it quite impracticable to give anything of a peculiarly Sunday tone to the conversation, abandoned the attempt and had it out with the archdeacon about the Bristol guano.", "손 양은 제 팬지꽃 사이에서 분주했고, 그녀의 오라비는 대화에 일요일다운 색조를 조금이라도 입히기가 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 깨닫고는 그 시도를 포기한 채 부주교와 브리스틀 구아노 이야기를 실컷 했다."]], [["At three o'clock they again went into church, and now Mr. Arabin read the service and the archdeacon preached.", "세 시에 그들은 다시 교회에 들어갔고, 이번에는 애러빈 씨가 예배를 인도하고 부주교가 설교를 했다."], ["Nearly the same congregation was present, with some adventurous pedestrians from the city, who had not thought the heat of the midday August sun too great to deter them.", "거의 같은 회중이 모였는데, 여기에 한낮 8월 볕의 열기쯤은 발길을 돌릴 만한 것이 못 된다고 여긴 도시의 겁 없는 도보객 몇이 더해졌다."], ["The archdeacon took his text from the epistle to Philemon.", "부주교는 빌레몬서에서 본문을 취했다."], ["\"I beseech thee for my son Onesimus, whom I have begotten in my bonds.\"", "“갇힌 중에서 낳은 아들 오네시모를 위하여 네게 간구하노라.”"], ["From such a text it may be imagined the kind of sermon which Dr. Grantly preached, and on the whole it was neither dull, nor bad, nor out of place.", "그런 본문에서 그랜틀리 박사가 어떤 설교를 했을지는 짐작할 수 있을 터인데, 대체로 그것은 지루하지도, 나쁘지도, 자리에 어울리지 않지도 않았다."]], [["He told them that it had become his duty to look about for a pastor for them, to supply the place of one who had been long among them, and that in this manner he regarded as a son him whom he had selected, as St. Paul had regarded the young disciple whom he sent forth.", "그는 그들에게, 오랫동안 그들 가운데 있던 이의 자리를 메울 목자를 찾아보는 일이 제 소임이 되었고, 그리하여 바울이 자기가 보내는 젊은 제자를 여겼듯 자기도 제가 고른 사람을 아들로 여긴다고 말했다."], ["Then he took a little merit to himself for having studiously provided the best man he could without reference to patronage or favour; but he did not say that the best man according to his views was he who was best able to subdue Mr.", "그러고는 후원이나 정실을 돌아보지 않고 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 사람을 애써 구해 놓았다며 제 공을 조금 내세웠다."], ["Slope, and make that gentleman's situation in Barchester too hot to be comfortable. As to the bonds, they had consisted in the exceeding struggle which he had made to get a good clergyman for them.", "다만 제 생각에 가장 좋은 사람이란 슬로프 씨를 눌러 그자가 바체스터에서 편히 지내지 못하도록 만들 능력이 가장 뛰어난 사람이라는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["He deprecated any comparison between himself and St. Paul, but said that he was entitled to beseech them for their goodwill towards Mr.", "갇힘으로 말하자면, 그것은 그들에게 좋은 성직자를 구해 주려고 자기가 벌인 그 엄청난 분투에 있었다."], ["Arabin, in the same manner that the apostle had besought Philemon and his household with regard to Onesimus.", "그는 자기를 바울에 견주는 일은 삼가야 한다고 하면서도, 사도가 오네시모를 두고 빌레몬과 그 집안 사람들에게 간구했던 것과 같은 방식으로 자기도 애러빈 씨를 향한 그들의 호의를 간구할 자격은 있다고 말했다."]], [["The archdeacon's sermon--text, blessing, and all--was concluded within the half-hour.", "부주교의 설교는 본문이며 축도며 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않고도 반 시간 안에 끝났다."], ["Then they shook hands with their Ullathorne friends and returned to Plumstead. 'Twas thus that Mr.", "그러고 나서 그들은 울라손의 벗들과 악수를 나누고 플럼스테드로 돌아갔다."], ["Arabin read himself in at St. Ewold's.", "애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드에서 취임 낭독을 한 것은 이러하였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV. Mr. Slope Manages Matters Very Cleverly at Puddingdale", "# 제24장. 슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일에서 일을 아주 능란하게 처리하다"]], [["The next two weeks passed pleasantly enough at Plumstead. The whole party there assembled seemed to get on well together.", "그 뒤 두 주는 플럼스테드에서 제법 유쾌하게 흘러갔다."], ["Eleanor made the house agreeable, and the archdeacon and Mr.", "그곳에 모인 사람들은 다들 서로 잘 어울리는 듯했다."], ["Grantly seemed to have forgotten her iniquity as regarded Mr. Slope.", "엘리너는 집안 분위기를 화기애애하게 만들었고, 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인은 슬로프 씨에 관한 그녀의 죄를 잊은 것 같았다."], ["Mr. Harding had his violoncello, and played to them while his daughters accompanied him.", "하딩 씨에게는 첼로가 있어서, 두 딸이 반주를 맡는 가운데 사람들에게 연주를 들려주었다."], ["Johnny Bold, by the help either of Mr. Rerechild or else by that of his coral and carrot-juice, got through his teething troubles.", "조니 볼드는 리어차일드 씨 덕분인지 아니면 산호 노리개와 당근즙 덕분인지, 이앓이의 고생을 무사히 넘겼다."], ["There had been gaieties, too, of all sorts.", "온갖 즐거운 자리도 있었다."], ["They had dined at Ullathorne, and the Thornes had dined at the rectory.", "그들은 울라손에서 만찬을 들었고, 손 남매도 목사관에서 만찬을 들었다."], ["Eleanor had been duly put to stand on her box, and in that position had found herself quite unable to express her opinion on the merits of flounces, such having been the subject given to try her elocution.", "엘리너는 어김없이 그 상자 위에 올라서야 했는데, 그 자세로는 주름 장식의 좋고 나쁨에 관해 제 의견을 도무지 밝힐 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다. 발성 시험 삼아 주어진 주제가 바로 그것이었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Arabin had of course been much in his own parish, looking to the doings at his vicarage, calling on his parishioners, and taking on himself the duties of his new calling.", "애러빈 씨는 물론 제 교구에 머무는 때가 많아, 목사관의 공사를 살피고 교구민을 찾아보며 새 직분의 의무를 떠맡았다."], ["But still he had been every evening at Plumstead, and Mrs. Grantly was partly willing to agree with her husband that he was a pleasant inmate in a house.", "그래도 저녁마다 플럼스테드에 와 있었으므로, 그랜틀리 부인도 그가 한집에 두기 좋은 사람이라는 남편의 말에 어느 정도는 동의할 마음이 들었다."]], [["They had also been at a dinner-party at Dr. Stanhope's, of which Mr.", "그들은 스탠호프 박사 댁 만찬에도 갔고, 애러빈 씨도 그 자리에 끼었다."], ["Arabin had made one. He also, mothlike, burnt his wings in the flames of the signora's candle.", "그 역시 불나방처럼 시뇨라의 촛불에 날개를 태웠다."], ["Mrs. Bold, too, had been there, and had felt somewhat displeased with the taste--want of taste she called it--shown by Mr.", "볼드 부인도 그 자리에 있었는데, 애러빈 씨가 네로니 부인에게 그토록 마음을 쏟는 데서 드러난 취향이 — 그녀는 그것을 취향의 결핍이라 불렀다 — 다소 못마땅했다."], ["Arabin in paying so much attention to Madame Neroni. It was as infallible that Madeline should displease and irritate the women as that she should charm and captivate the men.", "마들린이 남자를 홀리고 사로잡는 것이 어김없듯, 여자를 언짢게 하고 약 오르게 하는 것도 어김없었다."], ["The one result followed naturally on the other.", "한쪽 결과는 다른 쪽 결과에서 자연히 따라 나왔다."], ["It was quite true that Mr. Arabin had been charmed.", "애러빈 씨가 홀린 것은 사실이었다."], ["He thought her a very clever and a very handsome woman; he thought also that her peculiar affliction entitled her to the sympathy of all.", "그는 그녀가 무척 영리하고 무척 아름다운 여자라고 생각했고, 또 그녀의 남다른 불행이 모든 이의 동정을 받을 만하다고 여겼다."], ["He had never, he said, met so much suffering joined to such perfect beauty and so clear a mind.", "그만한 고통이 그토록 완벽한 아름다움과 그토록 맑은 정신에 함께 깃든 경우는 여태 본 적이 없다고 그는 말했다."], ["'Twas thus he spoke of the signora, coming home in the archdeacon's carriage, and Eleanor by no means liked to hear the praise.", "부주교의 마차를 타고 돌아오는 길에 그는 시뇨라를 두고 그렇게 말했고, 엘리너는 그 찬사를 듣는 것이 조금도 달갑지 않았다."], ["It was, however, exceedingly unjust of her to be angry with Mr.", "그러나 애러빈 씨에게 화를 내는 것은 지극히 부당한 일이었다."], ["Arabin, as she had herself spent a very pleasant evening with Bertie Stanhope, who had taken her down to dinner and had not left her side for one moment after the gentlemen came out of the dining-room.", "그녀 자신도 버티 스탠호프와 아주 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈기 때문이다. 버티는 그녀를 만찬석으로 안내했고, 남자들이 식당에서 나온 뒤로는 한순간도 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["It was unfair that she should amuse herself with Bertie and yet begrudge her new friend his license of amusing himself with Bertie's sister.", "제 딴에는 버티와 즐겨 놓고 새 벗이 버티의 누이와 즐기는 자유는 못마땅해하다니 공평하지 못한 일이었다."], ["And yet she did so.", "그런데도 그녀는 그랬다."], ["She was half-angry with him in the carriage, and said something about meretricious manners. Mr.", "마차 안에서 그녀는 그에게 반쯤 화가 나 있었고, 요염한 태도가 어떻다는 말을 했다."], ["Arabin did not understand the ways of women very well, or else he might have flattered himself that Eleanor was in love with him.", "애러빈 씨는 여자의 속내를 잘 알지 못했다. 그러지 않았더라면 엘리너가 자기를 사랑하는 모양이라고 우쭐해 볼 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["But Eleanor was not in love with him.", "그러나 엘리너는 그를 사랑하지 않았다."], ["How many shades there are between love and indifference, and how little the graduated scale is understood!", "사랑과 무관심 사이에는 얼마나 많은 층이 있으며, 그 눈금은 또 얼마나 알려져 있지 않은가!"], ["She had now been nearly three weeks in the same house with Mr. Arabin, and had received much of his attention and listened daily to his conversation.", "그녀는 애러빈 씨와 한집에서 지낸 지 벌써 세 주가 다 되어 갔고, 그의 관심을 많이 받았으며 날마다 그의 이야기를 들었다."], ["He had usually devoted at least some portion of his evening to her exclusively.", "그는 대개 저녁 시간의 얼마쯤은 그녀에게만 바쳤다."], ["At Dr. Stanhope's he had devoted himself exclusively to another.", "그런데 스탠호프 박사 댁에서는 다른 여자에게만 자신을 바쳤다."], ["It does not require that a woman should be in love to be irritated at this; it does not require that she should even acknowledge to herself that it is unpleasant to her.", "이런 일에 약이 오르는 데에는 사랑에 빠져 있을 필요가 없다. 그것이 제게 불쾌하다고 스스로 인정할 필요조차 없다."], ["Eleanor had no such self-knowledge.", "엘리너에게는 그만한 자기 인식이 없었다."], ["She thought in her own heart that it was only on Mr. Arabin's account that she regretted that he could condescend to be amused by the signora.", "그가 시뇨라 따위에게 즐거워할 만큼 몸을 낮춘 것이 안타깝다면 그것은 오로지 애러빈 씨를 위해서라고 그녀는 마음속으로 생각했다."], ["\"I thought he had more mind,\"", "“그분에게 좀 더 깊은 정신이 있는 줄 알았는데.”"], ["she said to herself as she sat watching her baby's cradle on her return from the party.", "연회에서 돌아와 아기의 요람을 지켜보고 앉아 그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["\"After all, I believe Mr. Stanhope is the pleasanter man of the two.\"", "“결국 스탠호프 씨가 둘 중에서는 더 유쾌한 사람인 것 같아.”"], ["Alas for the memory of poor John Bold!", "가엾은 존 볼드의 기억이여!"], ["Eleanor was not in love with Bertie Stanhope, nor was she in love with Mr.", "엘리너는 버티 스탠호프를 사랑하지도, 애러빈 씨를 사랑하지도 않았다."], ["Arabin. But her devotion to her late husband was fast fading when she could revolve in her mind, over the cradle of his infant, the faults and failings of other aspirants to her favour.", "그러나 죽은 남편의 아이 요람 곁에서 제 환심을 노리는 다른 사내들의 허물과 결점을 이리저리 곱씹을 수 있게 되었다면, 남편을 향한 그녀의 헌신은 빠르게 스러져 가고 있는 것이었다."]], [["Will anyone blame my heroine for this?", "이 일로 내 여주인공을 나무랄 사람이 있겠는가?"], ["Let him or her rather thank God for all His goodness--for His mercy endureth forever.", "그럴 바에는 남자든 여자든 하느님의 온갖 선하심에 감사드릴 일이다 — 그 인자하심이 영원함이로다."]], [["Eleanor, in truth, was not in love; neither was Mr.", "엘리너는 사실 사랑에 빠져 있지 않았다. 애러빈 씨도 마찬가지였다."], ["Arabin. Neither indeed was Bertie Stanhope, though he had already found occasion to say nearly as much as that he was.", "버티 스탠호프 역시 그러했으니, 다만 그는 벌써 그렇다는 말이나 다름없는 소리를 할 기회를 찾아냈을 뿐이다."], ["The widow's cap had prevented him from making a positive declaration, when otherwise he would have considered himself entitled to do so on a third or fourth interview.", "미망인의 모자가 그로 하여금 분명한 고백을 하지 못하게 막았을 뿐, 그렇지 않았다면 그는 세 번째나 네 번째 만남에서 그럴 자격이 제게 있다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["It was, after all, but a small cap now, and had but little of the weeping willow left in its construction.", "그것은 어차피 이제 자그마한 모자였고, 수양버들 같은 구석은 별로 남아 있지 않았다."], ["It is singular how these emblems of grief fade away by unseen gradations.", "슬픔의 표지들이 눈에 띄지 않는 단계를 밟아 스러져 가는 모양은 참으로 묘하다."], ["Each pretends to be the counterpart of the forerunner, and yet the last little bit of crimped white crape that sits so jauntily on the back of the head is as dissimilar to the first huge mountain of woe which disfigured the face of the weeper as the state of the Hindu is to the jointure of the English dowager.", "저마다 앞선 것의 짝인 체하지만, 뒤통수에 사뿐히 얹힌 마지막 자잘한 주름 흰 크레이프 조각은 애도자의 얼굴을 망쳐 놓던 처음의 거대한 슬픔의 산과 딴판이다. 힌두 과부의 처지가 영국 미망인의 과부 재산과 딴판이듯이."]], [["But let it be clearly understood that Eleanor was in love with no one, and that no one was in love with Eleanor.", "그러나 엘리너가 아무도 사랑하지 않았고 아무도 엘리너를 사랑하지 않았다는 점은 분명히 해 두자."], ["Under these circumstances her anger against Mr. Arabin did not last long, and before two days were over they were both as good friends as ever.", "사정이 이러했으므로 애러빈 씨에 대한 그녀의 노여움은 오래가지 않았고, 이틀이 지나기도 전에 둘은 예전과 다름없이 좋은 벗이 되었다."], ["She could not but like him, for every hour spent in his company was spent pleasantly.", "그녀는 그를 좋아하지 않을 수 없었다. 그와 함께 보내는 시간은 매 시간이 유쾌했기 때문이다."], ["And yet she could not quite like him, for there was always apparent in his conversation a certain feeling on his part that he hardly thought it worth his while to be in earnest.", "그러면서도 그를 온전히 좋아할 수는 없었다. 그의 이야기에는 진지해질 만큼의 값어치는 없다고 여기는 듯한 기색이 늘 배어 있었기 때문이다."], ["It was almost as though he were playing with a child.", "마치 어린아이와 놀아 주는 것 같았다."], ["She knew well enough that he was in truth a sober, thoughtful man who, in some matters and on some occasions, could endure an agony of earnestness.", "그가 실은 진중하고 사려 깊은 사람이며, 어떤 일 어떤 자리에서는 진지함의 고통까지도 견뎌 낼 수 있는 사람임을 그녀는 잘 알았다."], ["And yet to her he was always gently playful.", "그런데도 그는 그녀에게만은 늘 부드럽게 장난스러웠다."], ["Could she have seen his brow once clouded, she might have learnt to love him.", "그의 이마가 한 번이라도 흐려지는 것을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 그를 사랑하는 법을 배웠을지도 모른다."]], [["So things went on at Plumstead, and on the whole not unpleasantly, till a huge storm darkened the horizon and came down upon the inhabitants of the rectory with all the fury of a water-spout.", "플럼스테드의 나날은 그렇게, 대체로 나쁘지 않게 흘러갔다. 그러다 거대한 폭풍이 지평선을 어둡게 하더니 용오름 같은 기세로 목사관 사람들 위에 쏟아져 내렸다."], ["It was astonishing how in a few minutes the whole face of the heavens was changed.", "단 몇 분 사이에 하늘의 낯빛이 온통 뒤바뀐 것은 놀라운 일이었다."], ["The party broke up from breakfast in perfect harmony, but fierce passions had arisen before the evening which did not admit of their sitting at the same board for dinner.", "사람들은 더없이 화목하게 아침 식탁에서 일어섰으나, 저녁이 되기 전에 사나운 감정이 일어 한 상에 마주 앉아 만찬을 들 수 없는 지경이 되었다."], ["To explain this it will be necessary to go back a little.", "이를 설명하자면 조금 거슬러 올라가야 한다."]], [["It will be remembered that the bishop expressed to Mr. Slope in his dressing-room his determination that Mr. Quiverful should be confirmed in his appointment to the hospital, and that his lordship requested Mr. Slope to communicate this decision to the archdeacon. It will also be remembered that the archdeacon had indignantly declined seeing Mr. Slope, and had instead written a strong letter to the bishop in which he all but demanded the situation of warden for Mr. Harding. To this letter the archdeacon received an immediate formal reply from Mr. Slope, in which it was stated that the bishop had received and would give his best consideration to the archdeacon's letter.", "주교가 옷방에서 슬로프 씨에게 퀴버풀 씨의 요양원 임명을 확정하겠다는 결심을 밝히고, 그 결정을 부주교에게 전하라고 슬로프 씨에게 이른 일을 독자는 기억할 것이다. 또 부주교가 슬로프 씨 만나기를 분개하여 거절하고, 그 대신 주교에게 강경한 편지를 써서 하딩 씨에게 워든 자리를 달라고 사실상 요구한 일도 기억할 것이다. 이 편지에 대해 부주교는 슬로프 씨에게서 곧바로 형식적인 답장을 받았는데, 주교가 부주교의 편지를 받았으며 그것을 잘 헤아려 보겠다는 내용이었다."]], [["The archdeacon felt himself somewhat checkmated by this reply.", "부주교는 이 답장에 다소 외통수에 걸린 기분이었다."], ["What could he do with a man who would neither see him, nor argue with him by letter, and who had undoubtedly the power of appointing any clergyman he pleased?", "만나 주지도 않고 편지로 따져 주지도 않으면서, 마음에 드는 성직자를 임명할 권한만은 분명히 쥐고 있는 사람을 어찌하겠는가? 그는 애러빈 씨와 의논했고, 애러빈 씨는 라자러스 학장의 도움을 청하는 것이 좋겠다고 제안했다."], ["He had consulted with Mr. Arabin, who had suggested the propriety of calling in the aid of the Master of Lazarus.", "“당신과 그윈 박사가 주교를 찾아뵙겠다고 격식을 갖추어 통고하면”"], ["\"If,\" said he, \"you and Dr.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["Gwynne formally declare your intention of waiting upon the bishop, the bishop will not dare to refuse to see you; and if two such men as you are see him together, you will probably not leave him without carrying your point.\"", "“주교도 감히 만남을 거절하지는 못할 것이오. 그리고 두 분 같은 사람이 함께 마주 앉는다면, 뜻을 이루지 못한 채 물러 나오는 일은 아마 없을 것이오.”"]], [["The archdeacon did not quite like admitting the necessity of his being backed by the Master of Lazarus before he could obtain admission into the episcopal palace of Barchester, but still he felt that the advice was good, and he resolved to take it.", "부주교로서는 바체스터의 주교관에 발을 들이는 데 라자러스 학장의 후원이 필요하다고 인정하기가 썩 내키지 않았으나, 그래도 그 조언이 옳다고 느껴 따르기로 했다."], ["He wrote again to the bishop, expressing a hope that nothing further would be done in the matter of the hospital till the consideration promised by his lordship had been given, and then sent off a warm appeal to his friend the master, imploring him to come to Plumstead and assist in driving the bishop into compliance.", "그는 주교에게 다시 편지를 써서, 주교께서 약속한 숙고가 끝나기 전에는 요양원 문제에 더 이상의 조치가 취해지지 않기를 바란다고 밝혔다. 그런 다음 벗인 학장에게 간곡한 호소를 보내, 플럼스테드로 와서 주교를 굴복시키는 일을 거들어 달라고 청했다."], ["The master had rejoined, raising some difficulty, but not declining, and the archdeacon had again pressed his point, insisting on the necessity for immediate action.", "학장은 이런저런 어려움을 들면서도 거절하지는 않는 답을 보내왔고, 부주교는 즉각 행동해야 할 필요를 내세우며 다시 한번 다그쳤다."], ["Dr. Gwynne unfortunately had the gout, and could therefore name no immediate day, but still agreed to come, if it should be finally found necessary.", "그윈 박사는 공교롭게도 통풍을 앓고 있어서 당장 날을 잡을 수는 없었으나, 끝내 꼭 필요하다면 오겠다고는 했다."], ["So the matter stood, as regarded the party at Plumstead.", "플럼스테드 쪽 사정은 그렇게 되어 있었다."]], [["But Mr. Harding had another friend fighting his battle for him, quite as powerful as the Master of Lazarus, and this was Mr.", "그러나 하딩 씨에게는 라자러스 학장 못지않게 힘 있는 또 한 사람의 벗이 있어 그를 위해 싸우고 있었으니, 바로 슬로프 씨였다."], ["Slope. Though the bishop had so pertinaciously insisted on giving way to his wife in the matter of the hospital, Mr.", "주교가 요양원 문제에서 그토록 고집스레 아내에게 굽히기로 했지만, 슬로프 씨는 제 목적을 버릴 필요가 있다고 보지 않았다."], ["Slope did not think it necessary to abandon his object. He had, he thought, daily more and more reason to imagine that the widow would receive his overtures favourably, and he could not but feel that Mr.", "그 미망인이 자기 구애를 호의로 받아 주리라 여길 이유가 날마다 늘어난다고 그는 생각했으며, 하딩 씨가 제 손을 거쳐 요양원에 들어앉는다면 플럼스테드에서 부주교의 날개 아래 낙심하여 투덜대고 있을 때보다 자기를 사위로 맞을 마음이 더 있으리라고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Harding at the hospital, and placed there by his means, would be more likely to receive him as a son-in-law than Mr.", "게다가 슬로프 씨의 공을 제대로 헤아리자면, 그를 움직인 것은 그런 것들보다 더 큰 동기였다."], ["Harding growling in opposition and disappointment under the archdeacon's wing at Plumstead.", "그는 아내를 원했고 돈을 원했으나, 그 둘보다 권력을 더 원했다."], ["Moreover, to give Mr. Slope due credit, he was actuated by greater motives even than these.", "그는 프라우디 부인과 한판 붙어야 한다는 사실을 온전히 깨닫고 있었다."], ["He wanted a wife, and he wanted money, but he wanted power more than either.", "그 여자의 전속 사제로 바체스터에 남을 마음은 없었다."], ["He had fully realized the fact that he must come to blows with Mrs. Proudie.", "그럴 바에는 이 교구와의 인연을 통째로 잃는 위험을 무릅쓰겠다는 것이었다."], ["He had no desire to remain in Barchester as her chaplain. Sooner than do so, he would risk the loss of his whole connexion with the diocese.", "이 무슨 일인가!"], ["What! Was he to feel within him the possession of no ordinary talents--was he to know himself to be courageous, firm, and, in matters where his conscience did not interfere, unscrupulous--and yet he contented to be the working factotum of a woman prelate?", "제 안에 범상치 않은 재능이 있음을 느끼고, 제가 용기 있고 굳세며 양심이 끼어들지 않는 일에서는 거리낌조차 없는 사람임을 알면서, 그러고도 여자 고위 성직자의 잔심부름꾼 노릇에 만족한단 말인가?"], ["Mr. Slope had higher ideas of his own destiny.", "슬로프 씨는 제 운명을 그보다 높이 보았다."], ["Either he or Mrs. Proudie must go to the wall, and now had come the time when he would try which it should be.", "자기든 프라우디 부인이든 하나는 벽으로 밀려나야 했고, 이제 어느 쪽인지 겨루어 볼 때가 온 것이다."]], [["The bishop had declared that Mr. Quiverful should be the new warden.", "주교는 퀴버풀 씨가 새 워든이 될 것이라고 선언했다."], ["As Mr. Slope went downstairs, prepared to see the archdeacon, if necessary, but fully satisfied that no such necessity would arise, he declared to himself that Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 필요하다면 부주교를 만날 각오로, 그러나 그런 필요는 생기지 않으리라 확신하며 아래층으로 내려가면서, 하딩 씨가 워든이 되어야 한다고 혼자 다짐했다."], ["Harding should be warden. With the object of carrying this point, he rode over to Puddingdale and had a further interview with the worthy expectant of clerical good things.", "이 뜻을 관철할 요량으로 그는 푸딩데일까지 말을 몰고 가, 성직의 좋은 것을 기다리고 있는 그 훌륭한 인물과 다시 한번 마주 앉았다."], ["Mr. Quiverful was on the whole a worthy man.", "퀴버풀 씨는 대체로 훌륭한 사람이었다."], ["The impossible task of bringing up as ladies and gentlemen fourteen children on an income which was insufficient to give them with decency the common necessaries of life, had had an effect upon him not beneficial either to his spirit or his keen sense of honour.", "자식 열넷을 신사 숙녀로 길러 내야 하는데 사람 사는 데 필요한 최소한의 것조차 남부끄럽지 않게 대 주기 어려운 수입뿐이라는 불가능한 과업은, 그의 기개에도 예민한 명예 감각에도 이롭지 않은 자국을 남겼다."], ["Who can boast that he would have supported such a burden with a different result?", "그런 짐을 지고서 다른 결과를 냈으리라고 장담할 사람이 누구인가?"], ["Mr. Quiverful was an honest, painstaking, drudging man, anxious indeed for bread and meat, anxious for means to quiet his butcher and cover with returning smiles the now sour countenance of the baker's wife; but anxious also to be right with his own conscience.", "퀴버풀 씨는 정직하고 부지런하고 고달프게 일하는 사람으로, 참으로 빵과 고기가 아쉬웠고 푸줏간 주인을 달래고 빵집 안주인의 시큰둥해진 얼굴에 다시 웃음을 씌울 방도가 아쉬웠으나, 제 양심 앞에 떳떳한 것 또한 아쉬웠다."], ["He was not careful, as another might be who sat on an easier worldly seat, to stand well with those around him, to shun a breath which might sully his name or a rumour which might affect his honour.", "세상 자리가 더 편한 사람이라면 모를까, 그는 주위 사람들에게 좋게 보이려고, 제 이름을 더럽힐 입김이나 제 명예에 흠집 낼 소문을 피하려고 조심할 처지가 아니었다."], ["He could not afford such niceties of conduct, such moral luxuries.", "그에게 그런 행실의 섬세함, 그런 도덕의 사치는 감당할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["It must suffice for him to be ordinarily honest according to the ordinary honesty of the world's ways, and to let men's tongues wag as they would.", "세상이 굴러가는 보통의 정직함만큼 보통으로 정직하고, 사람들의 혀는 놀리고 싶은 대로 놀리게 두는 것으로 족해야 했다."]], [["He had felt that his brother clergymen, men whom he had known for the last twenty years, looked coldly on him from the first moment that he had shown himself willing to sit at the feet of Mr.", "그는 슬로프 씨의 발치에 앉을 뜻을 내비친 첫 순간부터, 지난 이십 년 동안 알고 지낸 동료 성직자들이 저를 차갑게 바라본다고 느꼈다."], ["Slope; he had seen that their looks grew colder still when it became bruited about that he was to be the bishop's new warden at Hiram's Hospital.", "그가 주교의 새 히램 요양원 워든이 된다는 소문이 퍼지자 그 눈길이 한층 더 차가워지는 것도 보았다."], ["This was painful enough, but it was the cross which he was doomed to bear.", "이는 충분히 쓰라린 일이었으나, 그것이 그가 짊어지도록 정해진 십자가였다. 그는 아내를 생각했다."], ["He thought of his wife, whose last new silk dress was six years in wear.", "아내가 마지막으로 얻은 새 비단옷은 입은 지 육 년이 되었다."], ["He thought of all his young flock, whom he could hardly take to church with him on Sundays, for there were not decent shoes and stockings for them all to wear.", "어린 무리를 생각했다. 일요일이면 다 함께 교회에 데려가기도 어려웠으니, 제대로 된 신발과 양말이 모두에게 돌아가지 않았기 때문이다."], ["He thought of the well-worn sleeves of his own black coat and of the stern face of the draper, from whom he would fain ask for cloth to make another, did he not know that the credit would be refused him.", "제 검은 상의의 닳아빠진 소매를 생각했고, 새로 한 벌 지을 옷감을 청하고 싶으나 외상은 거절당하리라는 것을 알기에 청하지 못하는, 그 포목상의 엄한 얼굴을 생각했다."], ["Then he thought of the comfortable house in Barchester, of the comfortable income, of his boys sent to school, of his girls with books in their hands instead of darning needles, of his wife's face again covered with smiles, and of his daily board again covered with plenty.", "그러고는 바체스터의 안락한 집을 생각했고, 넉넉한 수입을, 학교에 보낼 아들들을, 기울 바늘 대신 책을 손에 든 딸들을, 다시 웃음이 번지는 아내의 얼굴을, 다시 그득해지는 제 밥상을 생각했다."], ["He thought of these things; and do thou also, reader, think of them, and then wonder, if thou canst, that Mr.", "그는 이런 것들을 생각했다. 독자여, 그대도 이것들을 생각해 보라."], ["Slope had appeared to him to possess all those good gifts which could grace a bishop's chaplain.", "그러고도 슬로프 씨가 주교의 전속 사제를 빛낼 온갖 훌륭한 자질을 갖춘 사람으로 그의 눈에 비쳤다는 것이 이상하거든, 그때 가서 이상해하라."], ["\"How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings.\"", "“좋은 소식을 전하는 자의 산을 넘는 발이 어찌 그리 아름다운가.”"]], [["Why, moreover, should the Barchester clergy have looked coldly on Mr. Quiverful?", "게다가 바체스터의 성직자들이 어째서 퀴버풀 씨를 차갑게 대해야 한단 말인가?"], ["Had they not all shown that they regarded with complacency the loaves and fishes of their mother church?", "그들도 모두 어머니 교회의 떡과 물고기를 흐뭇하게 여긴다는 것을 보이지 않았던가?"], ["Had they not all, by some hook or crook, done better for themselves than he had done?", "그들도 모두 이런저런 수를 써서 그보다는 제 앞가림을 잘하지 않았던가?"], ["They were not burdened as he was burdened.", "그들은 그처럼 짐을 지고 있지 않았다."], ["Dr. Grantly had five children and nearly as many thousands a year on which to feed them.", "그랜틀리 박사는 자식이 다섯이었고, 그들을 먹이는 데 쓸 돈은 해마다 그와 엇비슷한 수의 천 파운드였다."], ["It was very well for him to turn up his nose at a new bishop who could do nothing for him, and a chaplain who was beneath his notice; but it was cruel in a man so circumstanced to set the world against the father of fourteen children because he was anxious to obtain for them an honourable support! He, Mr.", "자기에게 아무것도 해 줄 수 없는 새 주교와 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 전속 사제를 두고 코웃음 치는 것이야 그로서는 얼마든지 좋은 일이지만, 그런 처지에 있는 사람이 자식 열넷의 아비가 그들에게 떳떳한 살길을 마련해 주려 한다는 이유로 세상을 부추겨 그에게 등을 돌리게 하는 것은 잔인한 짓이었다."], ["Quiverful, had not asked for the wardenship; he had not even accepted it till he had been assured that Mr.", "그, 퀴버풀 씨는 워든 자리를 청한 적이 없었다. 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 거절했다는 확언을 듣기 전에는 받아들이지도 않았다."], ["Harding had refused it. How hard then that he should be blamed for doing that which not to have done would have argued a most insane imprudence!", "그러니 하지 않았더라면 미치광이 같은 무분별이라는 소리를 들었을 그 일을 했다고 해서 비난을 받다니, 이 얼마나 가혹한가!"]], [["Thus in this matter of the hospital poor Mr. Quiverful had his trials, and he had also his consolations.", "이렇듯 요양원 문제에서 가엾은 퀴버풀 씨에게는 시련이 있었고, 위안도 있었다."], ["On the whole the consolations were the more vivid of the two.", "대체로는 위안 쪽이 더 생생했다."], ["The stern draper heard of the coming promotion, and the wealth of his warehouse was at Mr. Quiverful's disposal.", "그 엄하던 포목상이 다가올 승진 소식을 듣고 창고의 재물을 퀴버풀 씨 마음대로 쓰게 했다."], ["Coming events cast their shadows before, and the coming event of Mr. Quiverful's transference to Barchester produced a delicious shadow in the shape of a new outfit for Mrs.", "다가올 일은 미리 그림자를 드리우는 법이어서, 퀴버풀 씨가 바체스터로 옮겨 간다는 다가올 일은 퀴버풀 부인과 위의 세 딸을 위한 새 옷차림이라는 감미로운 그림자를 낳았다."], ["Quiverful and her three elder daughters. Such consolations come home to the heart of a man, and quite home to the heart of a woman.", "그런 위안은 남자의 가슴에 사무치고, 여자의 가슴에는 더욱 사무친다."], ["Whatever the husband might feel, the wife cared nothing for frowns of dean, archdeacon, or prebendary.", "남편이야 어떻게 느끼든, 아내는 수석 사제든 부주교든 참사회원이든 그 찌푸린 얼굴 따위 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["To her the outsides and insides of her husband and fourteen children were everything.", "그녀에게는 남편과 열네 자식의 겉과 속이 전부였다."], ["In her bosom every other ambition had been swallowed up in that maternal ambition of seeing them and him and herself duly clad and properly fed.", "그녀의 가슴속에서는 다른 모든 야심이, 그 아이들과 남편과 자신이 제대로 입고 제대로 먹는 것을 보겠다는 어머니의 야심에 삼켜져 버렸다."], ["It had come to that with her that life had now no other purpose.", "그녀에게는 삶에 이제 다른 목적이 없는 지경에 이르러 있었다."], ["She recked nothing of the imaginary rights of others.", "남의 상상 속 권리 따위는 안중에도 없었다."], ["She had no patience with her husband when he declared to her that he could not accept the hospital unless he knew that Mr. Harding had refused it.", "하딩 씨가 거절한 줄을 알기 전에는 요양원을 받을 수 없다고 남편이 선언했을 때 그녀는 도무지 참을 수가 없었다."], ["Her husband had no right to be quixotic at the expense of fourteen children.", "남편에게는 열네 자식을 희생시켜 가며 기사도를 부릴 권리가 없었다."], ["The narrow escape of throwing away his good fortune which her lord had had, almost paralysed her.", "제 주인이 굴러온 복을 하마터면 내던질 뻔했다는 사실은 그녀를 거의 마비시켰다."], ["Now, indeed, they had received a full promise, not only from Mr. Slope, but also from Mrs.", "이제는 슬로프 씨뿐 아니라 프라우디 부인에게서까지 온전한 약속을 받아 두었다."], ["Proudie. Now, indeed, they might reckon with safety on their good fortune.", "이제는 그 복을 안심하고 셈에 넣어도 되었다."], ["But what if all had been lost?", "그러나 그 모든 것이 날아갔더라면 어쩔 뻔했는가?"], ["What if her fourteen bairns had been resteeped to the hips in poverty by the morbid sentimentality of their father?", "아비의 병적인 감상 탓에 열넷이나 되는 아이들이 다시 허리까지 가난에 잠겼더라면 어쩔 뻔했는가?"], ["Mrs. Quiverful was just at present a happy woman, but yet it nearly took her breath away when she thought of the risk they had run.", "퀴버풀 부인은 지금이야 행복한 여자였지만, 그래도 자기들이 무릅쓴 위험을 떠올리면 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."]], [["\"I don't know what your father means when he talks so much of what is due to Mr. Harding,\"", "“네 아버지가 하딩 씨에게 도리를 지켜야 한다고 그렇게 노래를 부르는 게 무슨 소린지 나는 모르겠구나.”"], ["she said to her eldest daughter.", "그녀가 큰딸에게 말했다."], ["\"Does he think that Mr. Harding would give him £450 a year out of fine feeling?", "“하딩 씨가 고운 마음씨로 연 450파운드를 아버지한테 내주기라도 한다는 거냐?"], ["And what signifies it whom he offends, as long as he gets the place?", "자리만 얻는다면 누구 비위를 거스르든 그게 무슨 대수야?"], ["He does not expect anything better.", "아버지가 그보다 나은 걸 바랄 처지도 아니잖니."], ["It passes me to think how your father can be so soft, while everybody around him is so griping.\"", "둘레에서 다들 그렇게 움켜쥐려 드는데 어쩌면 네 아버지는 저리 무를 수가 있는지 나로서는 알 수가 없다.”"]], [["Thus, while the outer world was accusing Mr. Quiverful of rapacity for promotion and of disregard to his honour, the inner world of his own household was falling foul of him, with equal vehemence, for his willingness to sacrifice their interests to a false feeling of sentimental pride.", "이렇듯 바깥세상이 퀴버풀 씨를 두고 승진에 눈이 멀었다느니 제 명예를 돌보지 않는다느니 나무라는 동안, 제 집안이라는 안쪽 세상은 그가 감상적인 자존심이라는 헛된 감정에 식구들의 이익을 기꺼이 바치려 한다고 못지않게 사납게 그를 몰아세우고 있었다."], ["It is astonishing how much difference the point of view makes in the aspect of all that we look at!", "보는 자리가 어디냐에 따라 우리가 보는 모든 것의 모습이 얼마나 달라지는지 놀라울 따름이다!"]], [["Such were the feelings of the different members of the family at Puddingdale on the occasion of Mr. Slope's second visit.", "슬로프 씨가 두 번째로 찾아왔을 때 푸딩데일 집안 사람들의 심경은 이러했다."], ["Mrs. Quiverful, as soon as she saw his horse coming up the avenue from the vicarage gate, hastily packed up her huge basket of needlework and hurried herself and her daughter out of the room in which she was sitting with her husband.", "퀴버풀 부인은 목사관 대문에서 들어오는 길로 그의 말이 올라오는 것을 보자마자 큼직한 바느질 바구니를 부랴부랴 챙기고는, 남편과 함께 앉아 있던 방에서 딸을 데리고 서둘러 나갔다."], ["\"It's Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨예요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"He's come to settle with you about the hospital.", "“요양원 일을 매듭지으러 오신 거예요."], ["I do hope we shall now be able to move at once.\"", "이제는 정말 곧바로 옮겨 갈 수 있으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["And she hastened to bid the maid of all work go to the door, so that the welcome great man might not be kept waiting.", "그러고는 그 반가운 귀인을 기다리게 하는 일이 없도록 잡일 하는 하녀를 어서 문간으로 보내라고 일렀다."]], [["Mr. Slope thus found Mr. Quiverful alone. Mrs. Quiverful went off to her kitchen and back settlements with anxious beating heart, almost dreading that there might be some slip between the cup of her happiness and the lip of her fruition, but yet comforting herself with the reflexion that after what had taken place, any such slip could hardly be possible.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨는 퀴버풀 씨와 단둘이 마주하게 되었다. 퀴버풀 부인은 두근거리는 가슴을 안고 부엌과 뒤채로 물러가, 행복의 잔과 그것을 맛볼 입술 사이에 무슨 어긋남이라도 생기지 않을까 조마조마해하면서도, 그만한 일이 오간 뒤이니 그런 어긋남은 있을 수 없으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 다독였다."]], [["Mr. Slope was all smiles as he shook his brother clergyman's hand and said that he had ridden over because he thought it right at once to put Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 동료 성직자의 손을 잡고 흔들며 연신 웃는 낯으로, 요양원 워든 자리에 관한 사실을 곧바로 퀴버풀 씨에게 알려 드리는 것이 옳다고 여겨 말을 달려왔노라고 말했다."], ["Quiverful in possession of the facts of the matter regarding the wardenship of the hospital. As he spoke, the poor expectant husband and father saw at a glance that his brilliant hopes were to be dashed to the ground, and that his visitor was now there for the purpose of unsaying what on his former visit he had said.", "그가 말하는 사이, 기다리던 그 가엾은 남편이자 아비는 제 눈부신 희망이 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐지려 한다는 것을, 그리고 이 손님이 지난번 방문에서 한 말을 물리려고 지금 여기 와 있다는 것을 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["There was something in the tone of the voice, something in the glance of the eye, which told the tale.", "목소리의 어조에, 눈길의 빛에 그 사연이 다 들어 있었다."], ["Mr. Quiverful knew it all at once.", "퀴버풀 씨는 단번에 전부 알아차렸다."], ["He maintained his self-possession, however, smiled with a slight unmeaning smile, and merely said that he was obliged to Mr. Slope for the trouble he was taking.", "그러나 그는 침착을 잃지 않고 뜻 모를 옅은 웃음을 지으며, 이렇게 수고해 주시니 슬로프 씨에게 신세를 진다고만 말했다."]], [["\"It has been a troublesome matter from first to last,\"", "“처음부터 끝까지 성가신 일이었지요.” 슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Slope, \"and the bishop has hardly known how to act.", "“주교님께서도 어찌 처신하셔야 할지 몰라 하셨습니다."], ["Between ourselves--but mind this of course must go no further, Mr. Quiverful.\"", "우리끼리 이야기입니다만 — 물론 이건 여기서 그쳐야 합니다, 퀴버풀 씨.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful said that of course it should not.", "퀴버풀 씨는 물론 그러겠다고 말했다."], ["\"The truth is that poor Mr. Harding has hardly known his own mind.", "“실은 가엾은 하딩 씨가 제 마음을 좀처럼 정하지 못했어요."], ["You remember our last conversation, no doubt.\"", "지난번에 우리가 나눈 이야기는 기억하시겠지요.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful assured him that he remembered it very well indeed.", "퀴버풀 씨는 그 이야기를 아주 잘 기억하고 있노라고 다짐해 두었다."]], [["\"You will remember that I told you that Mr. Harding had refused to return to the hospital.\"", "“하딩 씨가 요양원으로 돌아가기를 거절했다고 제가 말씀드린 것을 기억하실 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful declared that nothing could be more distinct on his memory.", "퀴버풀 씨는 그보다 더 또렷이 남아 있는 기억은 없다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["\"And acting on this refusal, I suggested that you should take the hospital,\"", "“그리고 그 거절에 따라, 요양원을 맡으시라고 제가 권했지요.”"], ["continued Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"I understood you to say that the bishop had authorised you to offer it to me.\"", "“주교님께서 그 자리를 저에게 제안할 권한을 선생께 주셨다고 말씀하신 걸로 알아들었는데요.”"]], [["\"Did I?", "“제가요?"], ["Did I go so far as that?", "제가 그렇게까지 말했던가요?"], ["Well, perhaps it may be that in my anxiety in your behalf I did commit myself further than I should have done.", "글쎄요, 어쩌면 선생을 위하는 마음이 앞선 나머지 제가 해야 할 말보다 더 나아갔는지도 모르지요."], ["So far as my own memory serves me, I don't think I did go quite so far as that.", "제 기억으로는 그렇게까지 말한 것 같지는 않습니다만."], ["But I own I was very anxious that you should get it, and I may have said more than was quite prudent.\"", "하지만 선생이 그 자리를 얻으시기를 몹시 바랐던 것은 사실이니, 신중한 선을 넘는 말을 했을 수는 있겠습니다.”"]], [["\"But,\" said Mr.", "“하지만”"], ["Quiverful in his deep anxiety to prove his case, \"my wife received as distinct a promise from Mrs.", "하고 퀴버풀 씨가 제 사정을 어떻게든 입증하려는 절박한 마음으로 말했다."], ["Proudie as one human being could give to another.\"", "“제 아내는 프라우디 부인께 사람이 사람에게 할 수 있는 가장 분명한 약속을 받았습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope smiled and gently shook his head.", "슬로프 씨는 웃으며 가만히 고개를 저었다."], ["He meant the smile for a pleasant smile, but it was diabolical in the eyes of the man he was speaking to.", "그는 그것을 다정한 웃음으로 지었으나, 마주 앉은 사람의 눈에는 악마 같은 웃음이었다. “프라우디 부인이라!”"], ["\"Mrs. Proudie!\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"If we are to go to what passes between the ladies in these matters, we shall really be in a nest of troubles from which we shall never extricate ourselves.", "“이런 일에서 부인네들 사이에 오간 말까지 따지기 시작하면 우리는 정말이지 헤어날 수 없는 말썽의 소굴에 빠지고 말 겁니다."], ["Mrs. Proudie is a most excellent lady, kind-hearted, charitable, pious, and in every way estimable.", "프라우디 부인은 더없이 훌륭한 분이고, 마음이 어질고 자비롭고 경건하며 무엇 하나 나무랄 데가 없는 분이지요."], ["But, my dear Mr. Quiverful, the patronage of the diocese is not in her hands.\"", "그러나 퀴버풀 씨, 이 교구의 성직 임명권은 그분 손에 있지 않습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful for a moment sat panic-stricken and silent.", "퀴버풀 씨는 한동안 얼이 빠진 채 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["\"Am I to understand, then, that I have received no promise?\"", "“그러면 제가 아무 약속도 받은 게 없다는 말씀입니까?”"], ["he said as soon as he had sufficiently collected his thoughts.", "겨우 생각을 추스르고 그가 말했다."]], [["\"If you will allow me, I will tell you exactly how the matter rests.", "“허락하신다면 이 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지 말씀드리지요."], ["You certainly did receive a promise conditional on Mr. Harding's refusal.", "선생은 하딩 씨의 거절을 조건으로 하는 약속을 분명히 받으셨습니다."], ["I am sure you will do me the justice to remember that you yourself declared that you could accept the appointment on no other condition than the knowledge that Mr. Harding had declined it.\"", "하딩 씨가 그 자리를 사양했다는 사실을 아는 것 말고는 어떤 조건으로도 임명을 받아들일 수 없다고 선생 스스로 말씀하셨다는 것도, 선생은 공정하게 기억해 주시리라 믿습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes,\" said Mr.", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["Quiverful; \"I did say that, certainly.\"", "퀴버풀 씨가 말했다. “제가 그렇게 말한 것은 분명합니다.”"]], [["\"Well, it now appears that he did not refuse it.\"", "“그런데 이제 와 보니 그분이 그 자리를 거절한 것이 아니더군요.”"]], [["\"But surely you told me, and repeated it more than once, that he had done so in your own hearing.\"", "“하지만 선생께서는 그분이 직접 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었다고 저에게 분명히, 그것도 여러 번 되풀이해 말씀하시지 않았습니까.”"]], [["\"So I understood him. But it seems I was in error.", "“저는 그렇게 알아들었지요."], ["But don't for a moment, Mr.", "그런데 제가 잘못 안 모양입니다."], ["Quiverful, suppose that I mean to throw you over. No.", "하지만 퀴버풀 씨, 제가 선생을 저버릴 셈이라고는 잠시도 생각하지 마십시오."], ["Having held out my hand to a man in your position, with your large family and pressing claims, I am not now going to draw it back again.", "아닙니다. 선생 같은 처지에, 그 많은 식구에, 그토록 다급한 사정을 지닌 분에게 한번 손을 내민 이상, 이제 와서 그 손을 거둘 생각은 없습니다."], ["I only want you to act with me fairly and honestly.\"", "다만 선생께서 저와 함께 공정하고 정직하게 처신해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"Whatever I do I shall endeavour at any rate to act fairly,\"", "“무엇을 하든 저로서는 어쨌든 공정하게 처신하려 애쓰겠지요.”"], ["said the poor man, feeling that he had to fall back for support on the spirit of martyrdom within him.", "가엾은 사내가 말했다. 제 안의 순교 정신에 기대어 버텨야겠다고 느끼면서였다."]], [["\"I am sure you will,\"", "“그러시리라 믿습니다.”"], ["said the other.", "상대가 말했다."], ["\"I am sure you have no wish to obtain possession of an income which belongs by all right to another. No man knows better than you do Mr.", "“온전히 남의 몫인 수입을 차지하고 싶은 마음이야 선생에게 없으시겠지요."], ["Harding's history, or can better appreciate his character. Mr.", "하딩 씨의 내력을 선생보다 잘 아는 사람도, 그분의 인품을 선생보다 잘 헤아릴 사람도 없습니다."], ["Harding is very desirous of returning to his old position, and the bishop feels that he is at the present moment somewhat hampered, though of course he is not bound, by the conversation which took place on the matter between you and me.\"", "하딩 씨는 옛 자리로 돌아가기를 몹시 바라고 계시고, 주교님께서는 선생과 저 사이에 오간 이야기 때문에 지금 다소 발이 묶인 느낌이라 하십니다. 물론 매인 몸은 아니십니다만.”"]], [["\"Well,\" said Mr.", "“글쎄요.”"], ["Quiverful, dreadfully doubtful as to what his conduct under such circumstances should be, and fruitlessly striving to harden his nerves with some of that instinct of self-preservation which made his wife so bold.", "퀴버풀 씨가 말했다. 이런 마당에 제가 어떻게 처신해야 할지 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 아내를 그토록 담대하게 만드는 그 자기 보존의 본능을 조금이나마 끌어와 마음을 단단히 해 보려 했으나 헛일이었다."]], [["\"The wardenship of this little hospital is not the only thing in the bishop's gift, Mr. Quiverful, nor is it by many degrees the best.", "“이 자그마한 요양원의 워든 자리가 주교님께서 내리실 수 있는 유일한 자리도 아니고, 가장 좋은 자리와는 한참 거리가 멉니다, 퀴버풀 씨."], ["And his lordship is not the man to forget anyone whom he has once marked with approval.", "게다가 주교님은 한번 마음에 들어 하신 사람을 잊으실 분이 아닙니다."], ["If you would allow me to advise you as a friend--\"", "제가 벗으로서 한 말씀 드려도 괜찮다면 —”"]], [["\"Indeed, I shall be most grateful to you,\"", "“정말이지 그래 주시면 더없이 고맙지요.”"], ["said the poor vicar of Puddingdale.", "푸딩데일의 가엾은 교구 사제가 말했다."]], [["\"I should advise you to withdraw from any opposition to Mr. Harding's claims.", "“저라면 하딩 씨의 권리에 맞서는 일에서 물러나시라고 권하겠습니다."], ["If you persist in your demand, I do not think you will ultimately succeed.", "요구를 굽히지 않으셔도 끝내 뜻을 이루지는 못하실 겁니다."], ["Mr. Harding has all but a positive right to the place.", "하딩 씨에게는 그 자리에 대한 권리가 사실상 있으니까요."], ["But if you will allow me to inform the bishop that you decline to stand in Mr. Harding's way, I think I may promise you--though, by the by, it must not be taken as a formal promise--that the bishop will not allow you to be a poorer man than you would have been had you become warden.\"", "하지만 선생께서 하딩 씨의 앞길을 막지 않겠다고 하신다는 것을 제가 주교님께 전해 드려도 좋다면, 이렇게 약속드릴 수 있을 듯합니다 — 아, 물론 이것을 정식 약속으로 받아들이시면 안 됩니다만 — 주교님께서 선생을 워든이 되셨을 경우보다 가난한 처지에 두시지는 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful sat in his armchair, silent, gazing at vacancy.", "퀴버풀 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 말없이 허공을 바라보았다."], ["What was he to say?", "무슨 말을 하겠는가?"], ["All this that came from Mr. Slope was so true.", "슬로프 씨의 입에서 나온 이 모든 말이 다 옳았다."], ["Mr. Harding had a right to the hospital.", "하딩 씨에게는 요양원에 대한 권리가 있었다."], ["The bishop had a great many good things to give away.", "주교에게는 나누어 줄 좋은 자리가 많았다."], ["Both the bishop and Mr. Slope would be excellent friends and terrible enemies to a man in his position.", "주교도 슬로프 씨도 그 같은 처지의 사람에게는 더없이 훌륭한 벗이자 무서운 적이 될 수 있었다."], ["And then he had no proof of any promise; he could not force the bishop to appoint him.", "게다가 그에게는 약속을 받았다는 증거가 없었으니, 주교더러 자기를 임명하라고 억지를 부릴 수도 없었다."]], [["\"Well, Mr. Quiverful, what do you say about it?\"", "“자, 퀴버풀 씨, 어떻게 하시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"Oh, of course, whatever you think fit, Mr. Slope.", "“아, 물론 선생께서 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하지요, 슬로프 씨."], ["It's a great disappointment, a very great disappointment.", "크게 낙심되는 일이기는 합니다, 아주 크게 낙심되는 일이지요."], ["I won't deny that I am a very poor man, Mr. Slope.\"", "제가 아주 가난한 사람이라는 건 부인하지 않겠습니다, 슬로프 씨.”"]], [["\"In the end, Mr. Quiverful, you will find that it will have been better for you.\"", "“끝에 가서는, 퀴버풀 씨, 이편이 선생에게 더 나았다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["The interview ended in Mr. Slope receiving a full renunciation from Mr.", "면담은 슬로프 씨가 문제의 임명에 대해 퀴버풀 씨가 지녔을 법한 일체의 권리 주장을 온전히 포기하겠다는 말을 받아 내는 것으로 끝났다."], ["Quiverful of any claim he might have to the appointment in question. It was only given verbally and without witnesses, but then the original promise was made in the same way.", "그것은 증인도 없이 말로만 주어진 것이었으나, 애초의 약속 또한 같은 방식으로 이루어진 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Slope again assured him that he should not be forgotten, and then rode back to Barchester, satisfied that he would now be able to mould the bishop to his wishes.", "슬로프 씨는 그를 잊지 않겠노라고 다시 한번 다짐하고는, 이제 주교를 제 뜻대로 주무를 수 있으리라 흡족해하며 바체스터로 말을 몰아 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV. Fourteen Arguments in Favour of Mr. Quiverful's Claims", "# 제25장. 퀴버풀 씨의 자격을 뒷받침하는 열네 가지 논거"]], [["We have most of us heard of the terrible anger of a lioness when, surrounded by her cubs, she guards her prey.", "우리 대부분은 새끼들에게 둘러싸인 채 제 먹이를 지키는 암사자의 무서운 노여움에 대해 들어 본 적이 있다."], ["Few of us wish to disturb the mother of a litter of puppies when mouthing a bone in the midst of her young family.", "어린 것들 한가운데서 뼈다귀를 물고 있는 어미 개를 굳이 건드리고 싶어 하는 사람도 별로 없다."], ["Medea and her children are familiar to us, and so is the grief of Constance. Mrs.", "메데이아와 그 아이들 이야기는 우리에게 익숙하고, 콘스턴스의 비탄 또한 그러하다."], ["Quiverful, when she first heard from her husband the news which he had to impart, felt within her bosom all the rage of the lioness, the rapacity of the hound, the fury of the tragic queen, and the deep despair of the bereaved mother.", "퀴버풀 부인은 남편이 전해야 할 소식을 처음 들었을 때 암사자의 분노와 사냥개의 탐욕과 비극 속 왕비의 격노와 자식을 잃은 어머니의 깊은 절망을 한꺼번에 제 가슴속에서 느꼈다."]], [["Doubting, but yet hardly fearing, what might have been the tenor of Mr.", "슬로프 씨의 이야기가 어떤 내용이었을지 미심쩍어하면서도 딱히 두려워하지는 않은 채, 그녀는 손님의 등 뒤로 현관문이 닫히자마자 남편에게로 달려갔다."], ["Slope's discourse, she rushed back to her husband as soon as the front door was closed behind the visitor.", "슬로프 씨로서는 그렇게 빠져나간 것이 다행이었다. 그런 순간에 그런 여자가 내는 노여움이라면 그조차도 기가 죽었을 것이다."], ["It was well for Mr. Slope that he so escaped--the anger of such a woman, at such a moment, would have cowed even him.", "일반적인 규칙으로 말하자면, 부인네들은 성미를 다스리는 편이 대단히 바람직하다."], ["As a general rule, it is highly desirable that ladies should keep their temper: a woman when she storms always makes herself ugly, and usually ridiculous also.", "여자가 폭풍처럼 몰아칠 때는 언제나 제 얼굴을 추하게 만들고, 대개는 우스꽝스럽게까지 만든다."], ["There is nothing so odious to man as a virago.", "사내에게 드센 여자만큼 질색인 것은 없다."], ["Though Theseus loved an Amazon, he showed his love but roughly, and from the time of Theseus downward, no man ever wished to have his wife remarkable rather for forward prowess than retiring gentleness.", "테세우스가 아마존 여인을 사랑하기는 했으되 그 사랑을 드러내는 방식은 거칠었고, 테세우스 이후로 제 아내가 물러서는 온순함보다 앞으로 나서는 기개로 이름나기를 바란 남자는 하나도 없었다."], ["A low voice \"is an excellent thing in woman.\"", "낮은 목소리는 “여자에게 더없이 좋은 것”이다."]], [["Such may be laid down as a very general rule; and few women should allow themselves to deviate from it, and then only on rare occasions.", "이것은 아주 일반적인 규칙으로 세워 둘 만하다. 여자가 여기서 벗어나는 것을 스스로에게 허락하는 일은 드물어야 하고, 허락하더라도 아주 드문 경우에 한해야 한다."], ["But if there be a time when a woman may let her hair to the winds, when she may loose her arms, and scream out trumpet-tongued to the ears of men, it is when nature calls out within her not for her own wants, but for the wants of those whom her womb has borne, whom her breasts have suckled, for those who look to her for their daily bread as naturally as man looks to his Creator.", "그러나 여자가 머리카락을 바람에 풀어 헤치고 팔을 내던지듯 휘두르며 나팔 같은 목소리로 사내들의 귀에 대고 소리쳐도 좋을 때가 있다면, 그것은 제 안의 본성이 제 자신의 결핍을 위해서가 아니라 제 태가 낳고 제 젖이 먹여 기른 이들, 사람이 제 창조주를 우러르듯 그렇게 자연스럽게 제게 하루의 양식을 바라는 이들의 결핍을 위해 부르짖을 때다."]], [["There was nothing poetic in the nature of Mrs. Quiverful.", "퀴버풀 부인의 천성에는 시적인 구석이라곤 없었다."], ["She was neither a Medea nor a Constance.", "그녀는 메데이아도 콘스턴스도 아니었다."], ["When angry, she spoke out her anger in plain words, and in a tone which might have been modulated with advantage; but she did so, at any rate, without affectation.", "화가 나면 그 노여움을 꾸밈없는 말로, 조금 다스렸더라면 좋았을 어조로 그대로 쏟아 냈다. 그러나 어쨌든 그녀는 가식 없이 그렇게 했다."], ["Now, without knowing it, she rose to a tragic vein.", "지금 그녀는 스스로도 모르는 사이에 비극의 가락에 올라섰다."]], [["\"Well, my dear, we are not to have it.\"", "“여보, 우리 차지가 되지 않게 되었소.”"], ["Such were the words with which her ears were greeted when she entered the parlour, still hot from the kitchen fire.", "부엌 불기운이 아직 가시지 않은 몸으로 응접실에 들어선 그녀의 귀를 맞이한 말은 그러했다."], ["And the face of her husband spoke even more plainly than his words:--", "그리고 남편의 얼굴은 그 말보다도 더 분명하게 말하고 있었다."]], [["E'en such a man, so faint, so spiritless, So dull, so dead in look, so woe-begone, Drew Priam's curtain in the dead of night.", "바로 그런 사람이, 그토록 맥이 풀리고 그토록 넋이 나가고, 그토록 흐리멍덩하고 그토록 죽은 낯빛에 그토록 비탄에 잠긴 자가, 한밤중에 프리아모스의 휘장을 걷었더라."]], [["\"What!\"", "“뭐라고요!”"], ["said she--and Mrs.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Siddons could not have put more passion into a single syllable--\"What!", "시든스 부인이라 한들 한 음절에 그보다 더한 격정을 담지는 못했으리라. “뭐라고요!"], ["Not have it? Who says so?\"", "우리 차지가 아니라고요?"], ["And she sat opposite to her husband, with her elbows on the table, her hands clasped together, and her coarse, solid, but once handsome face stretched over it towards him.", "누가 그럽디까?” 그러고는 남편 맞은편에 앉아 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 올리고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 거칠고 다부지지만 한때는 잘생겼던 얼굴을 탁자 너머로 남편에게 내밀었다."]], [["She sat as silent as death while he told his story, and very dreadful to him her silence was.", "남편이 이야기를 하는 동안 그녀는 죽은 듯이 잠자코 앉아 있었고, 그 침묵이 그에게는 몹시도 무서웠다."], ["He told it very lamely and badly but still in such a manner that she soon understood the whole of it.", "그는 아주 서투르고 어설프게 이야기했지만, 그래도 그녀가 곧 전모를 알아들을 만큼은 되었다."]], [["\"And so you have resigned it?\"", "“그래서 당신, 그 자리를 내놓았단 말이에요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I have had no opportunity of accepting it,\"", "“받아들일 기회 자체가 없었소.” 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied. \"I had no witnesses to Mr.", "“슬로프 씨가 그런 제안을 했다는 증인이 내겐 없었소."], ["Slope's offer, even if that offer would bind the bishop.", "설령 그 제안이 주교님을 얽어맬 수 있는 것이라 해도 말이오."], ["It was better for me, on the whole, to keep on good terms with such men than to fight for what I should never get!\"", "결국 얻지도 못할 것을 두고 다투느니 그런 사람들과 좋게 지내는 편이 나로서는 여러모로 나았소.”"]], [["\"Witnesses!\"", "“증인이라니요!”"], ["she screamed, rising quickly to her feet and walking up and down the room.", "그녀가 소리치며 벌떡 일어나 방 안을 오갔다."], ["\"Do clergymen require witnesses to their words?", "“성직자가 제 말에 증인이 필요합디까?"], ["He made the promise in the bishop's name, and if it is to be broken, I'll know the reason why.", "그이는 주교님 이름으로 약속했어요. 그 약속을 깨겠다면 나는 그 까닭을 기어이 알아내고야 말겠어요."], ["Did he not positively say that the bishop had sent him to offer you the place?\"", "주교님이 당신에게 그 자리를 주라고 자기를 보냈다고 그이가 똑똑히 말하지 않았어요?”"]], [["\"He did, my dear.", "“그랬소, 여보."], ["But that is now nothing to the purpose.\"", "하지만 그건 이제 아무 소용도 없는 이야기요.”"]], [["\"It is everything to the purpose, Mr. Quiverful.", "“아무 소용도 없다니요, 퀴버풀 씨."], ["Witnesses indeed!", "증인이라니, 참!"], ["And then to talk of your honour being questioned because you wish to provide for fourteen children.", "그러고서 아이 열넷을 먹여 살릴 길을 찾는다는 이유로 당신 명예가 의심을 받는다는 소리를 한다는 거예요."], ["It is everything to the purpose; and so they shall know, if I scream it into their ears from the town cross of Barchester.\"", "그건 소용이 아주 크고말고요. 바체스터 장터 십자가 앞에서 그 사람들 귀에 대고 악을 써서라도 그걸 알게 하고 말겠어요.”"]], [["\"You forget, Letitia, that the bishop has so many things in his gift.", "“당신 잊었구려, 러티샤. 주교님 손에는 내어 줄 자리가 아주 많소."], ["We must wait a little longer.", "우리는 조금만 더 기다리면 되오."], ["That is all.\"", "그뿐이오.”"]], [["\"Wait!", "“기다려요!"], ["Shall we feed the children by waiting?", "기다린다고 아이들을 먹일 수 있어요?"], ["Will waiting put George, and Tom, and Sam out into the world?", "기다린다고 조지와 톰과 샘이 세상에 나가 자리를 잡아요?"], ["Will it enable my poor girls to give up some of their drudgery?", "기다린다고 우리 가엾은 딸애들이 그 고된 집안일을 조금이라도 덜 수 있어요?"], ["Will waiting make Bessy and Jane fit even to be governesses?", "기다린다고 베시와 제인이 가정교사 자리라도 맡을 만하게 돼요?"], ["Will waiting pay for the things we got in Barchester last week?\"", "기다린다고 지난주에 바체스터에서 들여온 물건값이 치러져요?”"]], [["\"It is all we can do, my dear.", "“우리가 할 수 있는 건 그것뿐이오, 여보."], ["The disappointment is as much to me as to you; and yet, God knows, I feel it more for your sake than my own.\"", "낙담이야 나도 당신 못지않소. 그런데도, 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 나 자신보다 당신을 생각하며 그것을 더 아프게 느끼오.”"]], [["Mrs. Quiverful was looking full into her husband's face, and saw a small hot tear appear on each of those furrowed cheeks.", "퀴버풀 부인은 남편의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 주름진 두 뺨에 뜨거운 눈물이 한 방울씩 맺히는 것을 보았다."], ["This was too much for her woman's heart.", "이것은 그녀의 여자다운 마음이 감당하기에는 너무한 일이었다."], ["He also had risen, and was standing with his back to the empty grate.", "그도 자리에서 일어나 불기 없는 벽난로를 등지고 서 있었다."], ["She rushed towards him and, seizing him in her arms, sobbed aloud upon his bosom.", "그녀는 그에게 달려들어 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 그 가슴에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 내어 흐느꼈다."]], [["\"You are too good, too soft, too yielding,\" she said at last.", "“당신은 너무 착하고, 너무 무르고, 너무 잘 물러서요.”"], ["\"These men, when they want you, they use you like a cat's paw; and when they want you no longer, they throw you aside like an old shoe.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. “저 사람들은 당신이 필요할 때는 고양이 발처럼 갖다 쓰고, 더 필요 없어지면 헌 신짝처럼 내던져요."], ["This is twice they have treated you so.\"", "저이들이 당신을 이렇게 대접한 게 이번이 두 번째예요.”"]], [["\"In one way this will be all for the better,\"", "“한편으로 보면 이편이 도리어 잘된 일일 것이오.”"], ["argued he.", "그가 따져 말했다."], ["\"It will make the bishop feel that he is bound to do something for me.\"", "“주교님으로 하여금 나에게 무언가 해 주어야 한다는 마음이 들게 할 테니까.”"]], [["\"At any rate he shall hear of it,\"", "“어쨌든 주교님 귀에는 들어가게 하겠어요.”"], ["said the lady, again reverting to her more angry mood.", "부인이 다시 아까의 노기 어린 기색으로 돌아가 말했다."], ["\"At any rate he shall hear of it, and that loudly; and so shall she.", "“어쨌든 주교님 귀에 들어가게 하겠어요, 그것도 요란하게요. 그 부인 귀에도 마찬가지고요."], ["She little knows Letitia Quiverful, if she thinks I will sit down quietly with the loss after all that passed between us at the palace.", "주교관에서 우리 둘 사이에 오간 그 모든 말이 있고서도 내가 이 손실을 얌전히 받아들이고 앉아 있으리라 여긴다면, 그 부인은 러티샤 퀴버풀을 너무 모르는 거예요."], ["If there's any feeling within her, I'll make her ashamed of herself,\"--and she paced the room again, stamping the floor as she went with her fat, heavy foot.", "그 속에 사람의 감정이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면, 나는 그 부인이 스스로 부끄러워하게 만들고야 말겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 살찌고 무거운 발로 마룻바닥을 쿵쿵 울리며 다시 방 안을 오갔다."], ["\"Good heavens!", "“세상에!"], ["What a heart she must have within her to treat in such a way as this the father of fourteen unprovided children!\"", "아무 대책도 없는 아이 열넷의 아비를 이런 식으로 대접하다니, 대체 그 가슴속에 어떤 심장이 들어 있어야 그럴 수 있담!”"]], [["Mr. Quiverful proceeded to explain that he didn't think that Mrs. Proudie had had anything to do with it.", "퀴버풀 씨는 프라우디 부인이 이 일과 무슨 관계가 있으리라고는 생각하지 않는다고 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["\"Don't tell me,\" said Mrs.", "“그런 말씀 마세요.”"], ["Quiverful; \"I know more about it than that.", "퀴버풀 부인이 말했다."], ["Doesn't all the world know that Mrs.", "“나는 그보다는 더 잘 알아요."], ["Proudie is bishop of Barchester and that Mr. Slope is merely her creature?", "프라우디 부인이 바체스터의 주교이고 슬로프 씨는 그저 그 부인이 부리는 사람일 뿐이라는 걸 온 세상이 다 알지 않아요?"], ["Wasn't it she that made me the promise, just as though the thing was in her own particular gift?", "마치 그 자리를 제 마음대로 줄 수 있기라도 한 양 나에게 약속한 사람이 바로 그 부인 아니었어요? 내 말 들어 보세요."], ["I tell you, it was that woman who sent him over here to-day, because, for some reason of her own, she wants to go back from her word.\"", "오늘 그이를 여기로 보낸 건 그 여자예요. 제 나름의 무슨 까닭이 있어서 제 말을 도로 물리고 싶은 거라고요.”"]], [["\"My dear, you're wrong--\"", "“여보, 그건 당신이 잘못 알고 있는 거요. 프라우디 부인은—”"]], [["\"Now, Q., don't be so soft,\"", "“아이 참, Q, 그렇게 무르게 굴지 마세요.”"], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Take my word for it, the bishop knows no more about it than Jemima does.\"", "“내 말을 믿으세요. 주교님은 이 일에 대해 제미마만큼도 모르신다니까요.”"], ["Jemima was the two-year-old.", "제미마는 두 살배기였다."], ["\"And if you'll take my advice, you'll lose no time in going over and seeing him yourself.\"", "“그리고 내 말대로 하시려거든, 지체 말고 건너가서 직접 주교님을 뵈세요.”"]], [["Soft, however, as Mr. Quiverful might be, he would not allow himself to be talked out of his opinion on this occasion, and proceeded with much minuteness to explain to his wife the tone in which Mr.", "그러나 퀴버풀 씨가 아무리 무른 사람이라 해도 이번만큼은 제 생각을 말로 꺾이도록 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["Slope had spoken of Mrs. Proudie's interference in diocesan matters.", "그는 프라우디 부인이 교구 일에 간섭하는 것을 두고 슬로프 씨가 어떤 어조로 말했는지 아주 세세하게 아내에게 설명해 나갔다."], ["As he did so, a new idea gradually instilled itself into the matron's head, and a new course of conduct presented itself to her judgement. What if, after all, Mrs.", "그가 그렇게 하는 동안 이 안주인의 머릿속에는 새로운 생각이 차츰 스며들었고, 새로운 행동 방침이 그녀의 판단 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["Proudie knew nothing of this visit of Mr. Slope's?", "만일 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨의 이번 방문을 정말로 전혀 모르고 있다면?"], ["In that case, might it not be possible that that lady would still be staunch to her in this matter, still stand her friend, and, perhaps, possibly carry her through in opposition to Mr. Slope?", "그렇다면 그 부인이 이 일에서 여전히 자기 편에 굳게 서 주고, 여전히 자기의 벗으로 남아 주고, 어쩌면 슬로프 씨에 맞서 자기를 끝까지 밀어 줄 수도 있지 않겠는가?"], ["Mrs. Quiverful said nothing as this vague hope occurred to her, but listened with more than ordinary patience to what her husband had to say.", "이 희미한 희망이 떠올랐을 때 퀴버풀 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않고, 남편이 하는 말을 여느 때보다 훨씬 참을성 있게 들었다."], ["While he was still explaining that in all probability the world was wrong in its estimation of Mrs. Proudie's power and authority, she had fully made up her mind as to her course of action.", "프라우디 부인의 권세와 권위에 대한 세상의 평가가 아마도 틀렸을 것이라고 그가 아직 설명하고 있는 동안, 그녀는 이미 자기가 할 일을 완전히 정해 두었다."], ["She did not, however, proclaim her intention.", "그러나 그 뜻을 밖으로 알리지는 않았다."], ["She shook her head ominously as he continued his narration, and when he had completed, she rose to go, merely observing that it was cruel, cruel treatment.", "그가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 그녀는 불길하게 고개를 저었고, 그가 다 마치자 잔인한, 참으로 잔인한 처사라고만 말하고서 자리에서 일어났다."], ["She then asked him if he would mind waiting for a late dinner instead of dining at their usual hour of three; and, having received from him a concession on this point, she proceeded to carry her purpose into execution.", "그러고는 늘 하던 대로 세 시에 식사하는 대신 저녁을 늦게 들어도 괜찮겠느냐고 남편에게 물었고, 그 점에 대해 양보를 받아 낸 뒤 제 목적을 실행에 옮기러 나섰다."]], [["She determined that she would at once go to the palace, that she would do so, if possible, before Mrs. Proudie could have had an interview with Mr.", "그녀는 곧장 주교관으로 가되 될 수 있으면 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨와 만나기 전에 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["Slope, and that she would be either submissive, piteous, and pathetic, or else indignant, violent, and exacting, according to the manner in which she was received.", "그리고 자기가 어떤 대접을 받느냐에 따라 순종적이고 애처롭고 가련한 태도를 취하든지, 아니면 분개하고 격렬하고 다그치는 태도를 취하든지 하기로 정했다."]], [["She was quite confident in her own power.", "그녀는 제 힘을 조금도 의심하지 않았다."], ["Strengthened as she was by the pressing wants of fourteen children, she felt that she could make her way through legions of episcopal servants and force herself, if need be, into the presence of the lady who had so wronged her.", "아이 열넷의 절박한 궁핍이 그녀를 떠받치고 있었으므로, 주교관 하인들의 군단을 뚫고 나아가 필요하다면 자기에게 그토록 부당한 일을 한 그 부인 앞으로 억지로라도 밀고 들어갈 수 있으리라 느꼈다."], ["She had no shame about it, no mauvaise honte, no dread of archdeacons.", "그 일에 부끄러움 따위는 없었고, mauvaise honte도 없었으며, 부주교들에 대한 두려움도 없었다."], ["She would, as she declared to her husband, make her wail heard in the market-place if she did not get redress and justice.", "남편에게 선언한 대로, 바로잡음과 정의를 얻어 내지 못한다면 장터 한복판에서 제 통곡을 들리게 할 참이었다."], ["It might be very well for an unmarried young curate to be shamefaced in such matters; it might be all right that a snug rector, really in want of nothing, but still looking for better preferment, should carry on his affairs decently under the rose.", "장가도 안 든 젊은 부목사라면 그런 일에 낯을 붉히는 것도 좋을 것이다. 아쉬울 것이라곤 정말 아무것도 없으면서 그래도 더 나은 자리를 노리는 안락한 교구 목사라면 제 일을 점잖게 남몰래 처리하는 것도 다 옳을 것이다."], ["But Mrs. Quiverful, with fourteen children, had given over being shamefaced and, in some things, had given over being decent.", "그러나 아이 열넷을 둔 퀴버풀 부인은 낯 붉히기를 그만둔 지 오래였고, 어떤 일에서는 점잖기를 그만둔 지도 오래였다."], ["If it were intended that she should be ill-used in the manner proposed by Mr. Slope, it should not be done under the rose.", "슬로프 씨가 내놓은 방식대로 자기가 부당한 대접을 받아야 한다면, 그 일이 남몰래 이루어지게 두지는 않을 작정이었다."], ["All the world should know of it.", "온 세상이 그것을 알아야 했다."]], [["In her present mood, Mrs. Quiverful was not over-careful about her attire.", "지금 같은 심경에서 퀴버풀 부인은 옷차림에 그리 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["She tied her bonnet under her chin, threw her shawl over her shoulders, armed herself with the old family cotton umbrella, and started for Barchester.", "보닛 끈을 턱 밑에서 매고 숄을 어깨에 걸치고 집안 대대로 쓰던 낡은 무명 우산으로 무장하고서 바체스터를 향해 나섰다."], ["A journey to the palace was not quite so easy a thing for Mrs. Quiverful as for our friend at Plumstead.", "주교관까지 가는 길은 퀴버풀 부인에게 우리의 벗 플럼스테드 양반에게만큼 수월한 일이 아니었다."], ["Plumstead is nine miles from Barchester, and Puddingdale is but four.", "플럼스테드는 바체스터에서 9마일 떨어져 있고 푸딩데일은 4마일밖에 되지 않는다."], ["But the archdeacon could order round his brougham, and his high-trotting fast bay gelding would take him into the city within the hour.", "그러나 부주교는 사륜마차를 대령시킬 수 있었고, 걸음이 빠른 밤색 거세마가 한 시간 안에 그를 시내로 데려다주었다."], ["There was no brougham in the coach-house of Puddingdale Vicarage, no bay horse in the stables.", "푸딩데일 목사관의 마차 헛간에는 사륜마차가 없었고 마구간에는 밤색 말이 없었다."], ["There was no method of locomotion for its inhabitants but that which nature has assigned to man.", "그 집 식구들에게는 자연이 사람에게 배정해 준 것 말고는 이동할 방도가 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Quiverful was a broad, heavy woman, not young, nor given to walking.", "퀴버풀 부인은 몸집이 넓고 무거운 여자였고 젊지도 않았으며 걷기를 즐기는 편도 아니었다."], ["In her kitchen, and in the family dormitories, she was active enough, but her pace and gait were not adapted for the road.", "부엌에서, 그리고 아이들이 자는 방에서라면 충분히 활발했지만 그 걸음걸이와 보폭은 길 위에 맞지 않았다."], ["A walk into Barchester and back in the middle of an August day would be to her a terrible task, if not altogether impracticable.", "8월 한낮에 바체스터까지 걸어갔다 돌아오는 일은 그녀에게 아예 못할 노릇까지는 아니라 해도 끔찍한 고역이었을 것이다."], ["There was living in the parish, about half a mile from the vicarage on the road to the city, a decent, kindly farmer, well to do as regards this world and so far mindful of the next that he attended his parish church with decent regularity.", "목사관에서 시내로 가는 길로 반 마일쯤 되는 곳에, 이 세상 형편으로는 넉넉하고 저세상 일도 어지간히 마음에 두어 제 교구 교회에 꼬박꼬박 나오는 점잖고 인정 많은 농부 하나가 이 교구에 살고 있었다."], ["To him Mrs. Quiverful had before now appealed in some of her more pressing family troubles, and had not appealed in vain.", "퀴버풀 부인은 집안의 더 급한 곤경 몇 번을 두고 전에도 그에게 부탁한 적이 있었고, 헛되이 부탁한 적은 없었다."], ["At his door she now presented herself, and, having explained to his wife that most urgent business required her to go at once to Barchester, begged that Farmer Subsoil would take her thither in his tax-cart.", "그녀는 이제 그 집 문 앞에 나타나, 아주 급한 볼일이 있어 당장 바체스터에 가야 한다고 그 아내에게 설명한 뒤, 서브소일 농부가 짐수레에 자기를 태워 그리로 데려다주기를 청했다."], ["The farmer did not reject her plan, and, as soon as Prince could be got into his collar, they started on their journey.", "농부는 그 청을 물리치지 않았고, 프린스에게 멍에를 씌우는 대로 두 사람은 길을 떠났다."]], [["Mrs. Quiverful did not mention the purpose of her business, nor did the farmer alloy his kindness by any unseemly questions.", "퀴버풀 부인은 무슨 볼일인지 말하지 않았고, 농부도 주제넘은 물음 따위로 제 친절을 흐리지 않았다."], ["She merely begged to be put down at the bridge going into the city and to be taken up again at the same place in the course of two hours.", "그녀는 그저 시내로 들어가는 다리에서 내려 달라고, 그리고 두 시간쯤 뒤에 같은 자리에서 다시 태워 달라고만 청했다."], ["The farmer promised to be punctual to his appointment, and the lady, supported by her umbrella, took the short cut to the close and, in a few minutes, was at the bishop's door.", "농부는 약속 시간을 어기지 않겠다고 다짐했고, 우산에 몸을 의지한 부인은 대성당 경내로 가는 지름길을 잡아 몇 분 만에 주교 댁 문 앞에 이르렀다."]], [["Hitherto she had felt no dread with regard to the coming interview.", "여기까지 오는 동안 그녀는 앞으로 있을 면담에 대해 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않았다."], ["She had felt nothing but an indignant longing to pour forth her claims, and declare her wrongs, if those claims were not fully admitted.", "제 몫으로 요구할 것을 쏟아 놓고, 그 요구가 온전히 받아들여지지 않는다면 자기가 당한 부당함을 선언하고 싶다는 분에 찬 열망 말고는 아무것도 느끼지 않았다."], ["But now the difficulty of her situation touched her a little.", "그러나 이제 제 처지의 난감함이 그녀의 마음을 조금 건드렸다."], ["She had been at the palace once before, but then she went to give grateful thanks.", "그녀는 전에 한 번 주교관에 온 적이 있었지만, 그때는 감사 인사를 드리러 간 것이었다."], ["Those who have thanks to return for favours received find easy admittance to the halls of the great.", "은혜를 입고 그 감사를 갚으러 오는 이들은 높은 분들의 저택에 쉽게 들어선다."], ["Such is not always the case with men, or even with women, who have favours to beg.", "청을 드리러 오는 사내들, 아니 여자들의 경우에도 늘 그렇지는 않다."], ["Still less easy is access for those who demand the fulfilment of promises already made.", "이미 한 약속을 지키라고 요구하는 이들에게는 문이 더욱더 열리지 않는다."]], [["Mrs. Quiverful had not been slow to learn the ways of the world.", "퀴버풀 부인은 세상 물정을 익히는 데 더디지 않았다."], ["She knew all this, and she knew also that her cotton umbrella and all but ragged shawl would not command respect in the eyes of the palatial servants.", "그녀는 이 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 제 무명 우산과 거의 누더기가 된 숄이 주교관 하인들 눈에 존경을 불러일으키지는 못하리라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["If she were too humble, she knew well that she would never succeed.", "너무 굽실거리면 결코 뜻을 이루지 못하리라는 것도 잘 알았다."], ["To overcome by imperious overbearing with such a shawl as hers upon her shoulders and such a bonnet on her head would have required a personal bearing very superior to that with which nature had endowed her.", "그런 숄을 어깨에 걸치고 그런 보닛을 머리에 쓴 채 오만하게 위압해서 이겨 내려면 자연이 그녀에게 준 것보다 훨씬 뛰어난 몸가짐이 있어야 했을 것이다."], ["Of this also Mrs. Quiverful was aware.", "이 점 또한 퀴버풀 부인은 알고 있었다."], ["She must make it known that she was the wife of a gentleman and a clergyman, and must yet condescend to conciliate.", "그녀는 자기가 신사이자 성직자인 사람의 아내임을 알려야 하면서도, 그러면서 몸을 낮추어 상대의 비위를 맞추어야 했다."]], [["The poor lady knew but one way to overcome these difficulties at the very threshold of her enterprise, and to this she resorted.", "이 가엾은 부인은 제 일의 문턱에서부터 마주친 이 난관을 넘을 방법을 단 하나밖에 알지 못했고, 그것에 의지했다."], ["Low as were the domestic funds at Puddingdale, she still retained possession of half a crown, and this she sacrificed to the avarice of Mrs.", "푸딩데일의 살림 밑천이 아무리 바닥이라 해도 그녀에게는 아직 반 크라운 한 닢이 남아 있었으니, 그것을 프라우디 부인의 도회풍에 이름만 거창한 하인의 탐욕에 바쳤다."], ["Proudie's metropolitan sesquipedalian serving-man. She was, she said, Mrs.", "자기는 푸딩데일의 퀴버풀 부인이며 퀴버풀 목사의 아내라고 그녀는 말했다."], ["Quiverful of Puddingdale, the wife of the Rev. Mr.", "프라우디 부인을 뵙고자 한다고 했다."], ["Quiverful. She wished to see Mrs.", "프라우디 부인을 뵙는 일이 정말이지 꼭 있어야 한다고 했다."], ["Proudie. It was indeed quite indispensable that she should see Mrs.", "제임스 피츠플러시는 미심쩍은 것보다 더 나쁜 낯빛을 하고서, 마님이 외출 중이신지 다른 손님을 맞고 계신지 침실에 계신지 모르겠노라 했다."], ["Proudie. James Fitzplush looked worse than dubious, did not know whether his lady were out, or engaged, or in her bedroom; thought it most probable she was subject to one of these or to some other cause that would make her invisible; but Mrs.", "그중 하나이거나 아니면 마님을 뵐 수 없게 만드는 다른 무슨 사정이 있을 공산이 크다고 여겨진다 했다."], ["Quiverful could sit down in the waiting-room while inquiry was being made of Mrs. Proudie's maid.", "다만 프라우디 부인의 하녀에게 여쭈어 보는 동안 퀴버풀 부인은 대기실에 앉아 계셔도 좋다고 했다."]], [["\"Look here, my man,\"", "“이보게, 젊은이.”"], ["said Mrs. Quiverful; \"I must see her;\"", "퀴버풀 부인이 말했다."], ["and she put her card and half-crown--think of it, my reader, think of it; her last half-crown--into the man's hand and sat herself down on a chair in the waiting-room.", "“나는 그분을 꼭 뵈어야 하네.” 그러고는 제 명함과 반 크라운을 — 독자여, 생각해 보라, 생각해 보라, 그것이 그녀의 마지막 반 크라운이었다 — 하인의 손에 쥐여 주고 대기실 의자에 앉았다."]], [["Whether the bribe carried the day, or whether the bishop's wife really chose to see the vicar's wife, it boots not now to inquire. The man returned and, begging Mrs.", "뇌물이 판가름을 낸 것인지, 아니면 주교의 부인이 정말로 교구 사제의 아내를 만나 볼 마음이 있었던 것인지는 이제 와서 따져 볼 일이 못 된다."], ["Quiverful to follow him, ushered her into the presence of the mistress of the diocese.", "하인은 돌아와 따라오시라 청하고서 그녀를 이 교구의 여주인 앞으로 안내했다."]], [["Mrs. Quiverful at once saw that her patroness was in a smiling humour.", "퀴버풀 부인은 제 후원자가 웃음 띤 기분에 젖어 있음을 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["Triumph sat throned upon her brow, and all the joys of dominion hovered about her curls.", "승리가 그 이마 위에 왕좌를 틀고 앉아 있었고, 지배의 온갖 기쁨이 그 곱슬머리 언저리를 맴돌고 있었다."], ["Her lord had that morning contested with her a great point.", "그날 아침 그 남편이 그녀와 큰 문제를 두고 겨루었던 것이다."], ["He had received an invitation to spend a couple of days with the archbishop.", "그는 대주교와 이틀을 지내자는 초대를 받았다."], ["His soul longed for the gratification.", "그의 영혼은 그 흐뭇함을 간절히 바랐다."], ["Not a word, however, in his grace's note alluded to the fact of his being a married man; if he went at all, he must go alone.", "그러나 대주교 각하의 쪽지에는 그가 아내 있는 몸이라는 사실을 언급하는 말이 한마디도 없었다. 가려면 혼자 가야 했다."], ["This necessity would have presented no insurmountable bar to the visit, or have militated much against the pleasure, had he been able to go without any reference to Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인에게 한마디도 알리지 않고 갈 수 있었다면 이 사정이 방문을 가로막는 넘지 못할 장벽이 되지도 않았을 것이고 그 즐거움을 크게 깎아내리지도 않았을 것이다."], ["But this he could not do.", "그러나 그럴 수는 없었다."], ["He could not order his portmanteau to be packed and start with his own man, merely telling the lady of his heart that he would probably be back on Saturday.", "그는 여행 가방을 꾸리라 이르고 제 하인을 데리고 떠나면서, 제 마음의 부인에게 아마 토요일쯤 돌아오겠노라고만 일러 둘 수 있는 사람이 못 되었다."], ["There are men--may we not rather say monsters?--who do such things, and there are wives--may we not rather say slaves?--who put up with such usage.", "그런 짓을 하는 남자들이 — 차라리 괴물이라 불러야 하지 않을까? — 세상에 있고, 그런 대접을 참고 견디는 아내들이 — 차라리 노예라 불러야 하지 않을까?"], ["But Dr. and Mrs.", "— 있기는 하다."], ["Proudie were not among the number.", "그러나 프라우디 박사 내외는 그런 축에 들지 않았다."]], [["The bishop, with some beating about the bush, made the lady understand that he very much wished to go.", "주교는 이리저리 에두른 끝에 자기가 몹시 가고 싶어 한다는 뜻을 부인에게 알렸다."], ["The lady, without any beating about the bush, made the bishop understand that she wouldn't hear of it.", "부인은 에두르는 법 없이 그런 소리는 듣지 않겠다는 뜻을 주교에게 알렸다."], ["It would be useless here to repeat the arguments that were used on each side, and needless to record the result.", "양쪽이 내세운 논거를 여기 되풀이하는 것은 쓸데없는 일이고, 그 결과를 적어 두는 것도 부질없는 일이다."], ["Those who are married will understand very well how the battle was lost and won, and those who are single will never understand it till they learn the lesson which experience alone can give. When Mrs.", "혼인한 이들은 그 싸움이 어떻게 지고 어떻게 이겼는지 아주 잘 알아볼 것이고, 혼자인 이들은 오직 경험만이 줄 수 있는 그 교훈을 배우기 전에는 끝내 알아보지 못할 것이다."], ["Quiverful was shown into Mrs. Proudie's room, that lady had only returned a few minutes from her lord.", "퀴버풀 부인이 프라우디 부인의 방으로 안내되었을 때 그 부인은 남편에게서 돌아온 지 몇 분밖에 되지 않은 참이었다."], ["But before she left him she had seen the answer to the archbishop's note written and sealed.", "그러나 남편 곁을 떠나기 전에 그녀는 대주교의 쪽지에 대한 답장이 쓰이고 봉해지는 것까지 보고 나왔다."], ["No wonder that her face was wreathed with smiles as she received Mrs. Quiverful.", "퀴버풀 부인을 맞이하는 그 얼굴에 웃음이 어려 있었던 것도 무리가 아니었다."]], [["She instantly spoke of the subject which was so near the heart of her visitor.", "그녀는 곧바로 손님의 마음에 그토록 가까이 놓여 있는 그 문제를 입에 올렸다."], ["\"Well, Mrs. Quiverful,\"", "“그래, 퀴버풀 부인.”"], ["said she, \"is it decided yet when you are to move into Barchester?\"", "그녀가 말했다. “바체스터로 언제 옮겨 올지 이제 정해졌나?”"]], [["\"That woman,\"", "한두 시간 전만 해도 “그 여자”라고 불리던 이가, 주교의 부인을 꾸며 줄 수 있는 온갖 우아함을 순식간에 도로 갖추게 되었다."], ["as she had an hour or two since been called, became instantly re-endowed with all the graces that can adorn a bishop's wife.", "퀴버풀 부인은 제가 할 일이 가련해 보이는 것이며 분개해서는 아무것도 얻을 수 없다는 것을 곧바로 알아차렸다."], ["Mrs. Quiverful immediately saw that her business was to be piteous, and that nothing was to be gained by indignation--nothing, indeed, unless she could be indignant in company with her patroness.", "아니, 제 후원자와 함께 분개할 수 있다면 또 모를까, 그렇지 않고서는 정말 아무것도 얻을 수 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie,\"", "“아, 프라우디 부인.”"], ["she began, \"I fear we are not to move to Barchester at all.\"", "그녀가 입을 열었다. “저희는 아예 바체스터로 옮겨 가지 못하게 될까 두렵습니다.”"]], [["\"Why not?\"", "“어째서?”"], ["said that lady sharply, dropping at a moment's notice her smiles and condescension, and turning with her sharp quick way to business which she saw at a glance was important.", "그 부인이 날카롭게 물으며, 웃음과 너그러움을 그 자리에서 내려놓고는 한눈에 중대한 일임을 알아본 용건 쪽으로 그 날카롭고 재빠른 태도를 돌렸다."]], [["And then Mrs. Quiverful told her tale.", "그러고서 퀴버풀 부인은 제 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["As she progressed in the history of her wrongs she perceived that the heavier she leant upon Mr. Slope the blacker became Mrs.", "제가 당한 부당함의 내력을 풀어 가면서 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 무겁게 기댈수록 프라우디 부인의 이마가 더 검어지는 것을, 그러나 그 검어짐이 제 쪽 사정에는 해롭지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Proudie's brow, but that such blackness was not injurious to her own case. When Mr.", "그날 아침 슬로프 씨가 푸딩데일 목사관에 왔을 때 그녀는 그를 주교 노릇 하는 그 부인의 앞잡이로 여겼는데, 이제는 두 사람이 원수 사이임을 알아본 것이다."], ["Slope was at Puddingdale Vicarage that morning she had regarded him as the creature of the lady-bishop; now she perceived that they were enemies.", "그녀는 아무런 괴로움도 굴욕감도 없이 제 착오를 스스로 인정했다."], ["She admitted her mistake to herself without any pain or humiliation.", "그녀에게는 감정이 하나뿐이었고, 그것은 제 식구들에게만 매여 있었다."], ["She had but one feeling, and that was confined to her family. She cared little how she twisted and turned among these new-comers at the bishop's palace so long as she could twist her husband into the warden's house.", "남편을 워든의 집으로 비틀어 넣을 수만 있다면, 주교관에 새로 온 이 사람들 사이에서 제가 어떻게 비틀리고 돌아서든 아무래도 좋았다."], ["She cared not which was her friend or which was her enemy, if only she could get this preferment which she so sorely wanted.", "그토록 절실히 바라는 이 자리만 얻을 수 있다면 누가 제 편이고 누가 제 원수인지는 상관없었다."]], [["She told her tale, and Mrs. Proudie listened to it almost in silence. She told how Mr. Slope had cozened her husband into resigning his claim, and had declared that it was the bishop's will that none but Mr. Harding should be warden. Mrs. Proudie's brow became blacker and blacker. At last she started from her chair and, begging Mrs. Quiverful to sit and wait for her return, marched out of the room.", "그녀가 이야기를 늘어놓는 동안 프라우디 부인은 거의 잠자코 들었다. 슬로프 씨가 어떻게 제 남편을 구슬려 그 권리를 내놓게 만들었는지, 그리고 하딩 씨 말고는 아무도 워든이 되어서는 안 된다는 것이 주교의 뜻이라고 어떻게 잘라 말했는지를 그녀는 이야기했다. 프라우디 부인의 이마는 점점 더 검어졌다. 마침내 그 부인은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 퀴버풀 부인에게 앉아서 자기가 돌아올 때까지 기다리라 이르고는 성큼성큼 방을 나갔다."]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie, it's for fourteen children--for fourteen children.\"", "“아, 프라우디 부인, 아이가 열넷입니다."], ["Such was the burden that fell on her ear as she closed the door behind her.", "아이가 열넷이나 됩니다.” 그녀가 등 뒤로 문을 닫을 때 그 귀에 얹힌 짐은 그런 말이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI. Mrs. Proudie Wrestles and Gets a Fall", "# 제26장. 프라우디 부인이 씨름을 하다 넘어지다"]], [["It was hardly an hour since Mrs. Proudie had left her husband's apartment victorious, and yet so indomitable was her courage that she now returned thither panting for another combat.", "프라우디 부인이 승리자로서 남편의 방을 떠난 지 채 한 시간도 되지 않았건만, 그 기개가 어찌나 꺾일 줄 모르는 것이었던지 그녀는 이제 또 한 번의 격돌을 갈망하며 그리로 돌아왔다."], ["She was greatly angry with what she thought was his duplicity.", "그녀는 남편의 표리부동이라 여겨지는 것에 몹시 화가 나 있었다."], ["He had so clearly given her a promise on this matter of the hospital.", "그는 요양원 문제에 관해 그토록 분명하게 약속을 해 주지 않았던가."], ["He had been already so absolutely vanquished on that point. Mrs.", "그 점에 관해서라면 그는 이미 그토록 완벽하게 굴복하지 않았던가."], ["Proudie began to feel that if every affair was to be thus discussed and battled about twice and even thrice, the work of the diocese would be too much even for her.", "프라우디 부인은 만사가 이런 식으로 두 번씩, 심지어 세 번씩 논의되고 다투어져야 한다면 교구의 일이 아무리 자기라 해도 감당하기 벅차리라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["Without knocking at the door, she walked quickly into her husband's room and found him seated at his office table, with Mr. Slope opposite to him.", "문을 두드리지도 않고 그녀는 남편의 방으로 성큼 들어섰고, 그가 집무 탁자 앞에 앉아 있고 그 맞은편에 슬로프 씨가 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Between his fingers was the very note which he had written to the archbishop in her presence--and it was open!", "그의 손가락 사이에는 자기가 보는 앞에서 대주교에게 써 보낸 바로 그 쪽지가 들려 있었다 — 그것도 뜯긴 채로!"], ["Yes, he had absolutely violated the seal which had been made sacred by her approval.", "그렇다, 그는 자신의 승인으로 신성해진 봉인을 기어이 훼손해 놓았던 것이다."], ["They were sitting in deep conclave, and it was too clear that the purport of the archbishop's invitation had been absolutely canvassed again, after it had been already debated and decided on in obedience to her behests!", "두 사람은 깊숙이 밀담을 나누고 있었고, 대주교의 초청이 지닌 뜻이 자기 명에 따라 이미 논의되고 결정된 뒤에 다시 한 번 처음부터 다시 따져졌다는 것이 너무나도 분명했다!"], ["Mr. Slope rose from his chair and bowed slightly.", "슬로프 씨가 의자에서 일어나 가볍게 고개를 숙였다."], ["The two opposing spirits looked each other fully in the face, and they knew that they were looking each at an enemy.", "서로 맞선 두 혼령은 정면으로 서로의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 자기가 지금 적을 보고 있음을 알았다."]], [["\"What is this, Bishop, about Mr. Quiverful?\"", "“주교님, 퀴버풀 씨 건은 대체 무슨 일이에요?”"], ["said she, coming to the end of the table and standing there.", "탁자 끝으로 다가와 거기 선 채 그녀가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Slope did not allow the bishop to answer but replied himself.", "슬로프 씨는 주교가 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 자기가 대신 대답했다."], ["\"I have been out to Puddingdale this morning, ma'am, and have seen Mr. Quiverful.", "“저는 오늘 아침 푸딩데일에 다녀왔고, 부인, 거기서 퀴버풀 씨를 만났습니다."], ["Mr. Quiverful has abandoned his claim to the hospital because he is now aware that Mr.", "퀴버풀 씨는 요양원에 대한 자기 권리 주장을 거두었습니다."], ["Harding is desirous to fill his old place.", "하딩 씨가 옛 자리를 다시 맡고 싶어 한다는 것을 이제 알게 되었기 때문입니다."], ["Under these circumstances I have strongly advised his lordship to nominate Mr. Harding.\"", "그런 사정이니 저는 주교님께 하딩 씨를 지명하시라고 강력히 진언드렸습니다.”"]], [["\"Mr. Quiverful has not abandoned anything,\"", "“퀴버풀 씨는 아무것도 거두지 않았네.”"], ["said the lady, with a very imperious voice.", "부인이 몹시 위압적인 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"His lordship's word has been pledged to him, and it must be respected.\"", "“주교님의 약속은 이미 그이에게 주어졌고, 그 약속은 지켜져야 하네.”"]], [["The bishop still remained silent.", "주교는 여전히 잠자코 있었다."], ["He was anxiously desirous of making his old enemy bite the dust beneath his feet.", "그는 오랜 적을 제 발밑에 무릎 꿇려 흙을 물게 하고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."], ["His new ally had told him that nothing was more easy for him than to do so.", "새 동맹자는 그에게 그보다 쉬운 일은 없다고 일러 주었다."], ["The ally was there now at his elbow to help him, and yet his courage failed him.", "그 동맹자가 지금 바로 곁에 서서 거들어 주려 하건만, 그럼에도 그는 용기가 나지 않았다."], ["It is so hard to conquer when the prestige of former victories is all against one.", "지난날의 패배가 남긴 위세가 온통 제게 불리하게 작용할 때, 이기기란 참으로 어려운 일이다."], ["It is so hard for the cock who has once been beaten out of his yard to resume his courage and again take a proud place upon a dunghill.", "한 번 제 마당에서 쫓겨난 수탉이 다시 기운을 차려 거름 더미 위의 자랑스러운 자리를 되찾기란 참으로 어려운 일이다."]], [["\"Perhaps I ought not to interfere,\" said Mr.", "“제가 나설 일이 아닐지도 모르겠습니다만,”"], ["Slope, \"but yet--\"", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. “그래도—”"]], [["\"Certainly you ought not,\"", "“물론 나설 일이 아니지.”"], ["said the infuriated dame.", "격노한 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But yet,\"", "“그래도 말입니다,”"], ["continued Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 말막음에 아랑곳하지 않고 이어 갔다."], ["Slope, not regarding the interruption, \"I have thought it my imperative duty to recommend the bishop not to slight Mr. Harding's claims.\"", "“저로서는 주교님께 하딩 씨의 자격을 가볍게 여기지 마시라고 진언드리는 것이 반드시 해야 할 의무라고 여겼습니다.”"]], [["\"Mr. Harding should have known his own mind,\"", "“하딩 씨는 제 마음쯤은 스스로 정해 두었어야지.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"If Mr. Harding be not replaced at the hospital, his lordship will have to encounter much ill-will, not only in the diocese, but in the world at large.", "“하딩 씨가 요양원에 복귀하지 못한다면, 주교님께서는 교구 안에서뿐 아니라 널리 세상에서도 많은 원성을 사게 되실 것입니다."], ["Besides, taking a higher ground, his lordship, as I understand, feels it to be his duty to gratify, in this matter, so very worthy a man and so good a clergyman as Mr. Harding.\"", "게다가 좀 더 높은 자리에서 보자면, 제가 알기로 주교님께서는 이 일에서 하딩 씨처럼 훌륭한 인물이자 그토록 선량한 성직자를 흡족하게 해 주는 것이 당신의 도리라고 여기고 계십니다.”"]], [["\"And what is to become of the Sabbath-day school and of the Sunday services in the hospital?\"", "“그럼 요양원의 안식일 학교와 주일 예배는 어떻게 되는 건가?”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, with something very nearly approaching to a sneer on her face.", "프라우디 부인이 거의 비웃음에 가까운 것을 얼굴에 띠고 말했다."]], [["\"I understand that Mr. Harding makes no objection to the Sabbath-day school,\"", "“하딩 씨는 안식일 학교에는 아무 이의도 없다고 알고 있습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"And as to the hospital services, that matter will be best discussed after his appointment.", "“요양원 예배 문제라면, 그것은 그가 임명된 뒤에 논의하는 편이 가장 좋을 것입니다."], ["If he has any permanent objection, then, I fear, the matter must rest.\"", "그가 끝내 이의를 굽히지 않는다면, 유감스럽지만 그 일은 그대로 두어야 하겠지요.”"]], [["\"You have a very easy conscience in such matters, Mr.", "“자네는 그런 일에 양심이 참 편한 사람일세, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Slope,\" said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I should not have an easy conscience,\"", "“제 양심이 편할 리 없지요.” 그가 되받았다."], ["he rejoined, \"but a conscience very far from being easy, if anything said or done by me should lead the bishop to act unadvisedly in this matter.", "“아니, 제가 말하거나 행한 무엇이 주교님으로 하여금 이 일에서 그릇된 처신을 하시게 만든다면 제 양심은 편하기는커녕 도무지 편할 수 없을 것입니다."], ["It is clear that in the interview I had with Mr. Harding I misunderstood him--\"", "하딩 씨와 나눈 면담에서 제가 그를 잘못 알아들었던 것은 분명합니다—”"]], [["\"And it is equally clear that you have misunderstood Mr. Quiverful,\"", "“그리고 자네가 퀴버풀 씨를 잘못 알아들은 것도 그만큼 분명하지.”"], ["said she, now at the top of her wrath.", "이제 노여움이 꼭대기에 이른 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"What business have you at all with these interviews?", "“대체 자네가 그런 면담들과 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가?"], ["Who desired you to go to Mr. Quiverful this morning?", "누가 자네더러 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨에게 가라고 했나?"], ["Who commissioned you to manage this affair?", "누가 자네에게 이 일을 처리하라고 맡겼나?"], ["Will you answer me, sir?", "대답해 보게, 이 사람아."], ["Who sent you to Mr. Quiverful this morning?\"", "누가 자네를 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨에게 보냈나?”"]], [["There was a dead pause in the room. Mr.", "방 안에 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀다."], ["Slope had risen from his chair, and was standing with his hand on the back of it, looking at first very solemn and now very black. Mrs.", "슬로프 씨는 의자에서 일어나 그 등받이에 한 손을 얹고 선 채, 처음에는 몹시 엄숙한, 이제는 몹시 험악한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["Proudie was standing as she had at first placed herself, at the end of the table, and as she interrogated her foe she struck her hand upon it with almost more than feminine vigour.", "프라우디 부인은 처음 자리를 잡은 그대로 탁자 끝에 서 있었고, 적을 다그치면서 여자의 힘이라 하기에는 지나칠 만큼 세게 탁자를 손으로 내리쳤다."], ["The bishop was sitting in his easy chair twiddling his thumbs, turning his eyes now to his wife, and now to his chaplain, as each took up the cudgels.", "주교는 안락의자에 앉아 두 엄지를 맞돌리며, 둘이 번갈아 몽둥이를 집어 들 때마다 눈길을 아내에게로, 다시 전속 사제에게로 옮기고 있었다. 자기가 끼어들 것도 없이 저희끼리 이 일을 끝까지 싸워 준다면 얼마나 편할 것인가."], ["How comfortable it would be if they could fight it out between them without the necessity of any interference on his part; fight it out so that one should kill the other utterly, as far as diocesan life was concerned, so that he, the bishop, might know clearly by whom it behoved him to be led.", "적어도 교구 생활에 관한 한 하나가 다른 하나를 완전히 죽여 없애도록 싸워 주어서, 자기가, 곧 주교가 누구에게 이끌려 다녀야 마땅한지를 분명히 알 수 있게 된다면 얼마나 편할 것인가. 어느 쪽이 이기든 조용해지는 편안함은 얻을 터였다."], ["There would be the comfort of quiet in either case; but if the bishop had a wish as to which might prove the victor, that wish was certainly not antagonistic to Mr. Slope.", "그러나 주교에게 어느 쪽이 승자가 되기를 바라는 마음이 있었다면, 그 바람은 분명 슬로프 씨에게 적대적인 것은 아니었다."]], [["\"Better the d---- you know than the d---- you don't know,\" is an old saying, and perhaps a true one; but the bishop had not yet realized the truth of it.", "“모르는 마귀보다는 아는 마귀가 낫다”는 옛말이 있고 아마 참말이기도 하겠으나, 주교는 아직 그 말의 참됨을 깨닫지 못하고 있었다."]], [["\"Will you answer me, sir?\"", "“대답하겠나, 이 사람아?”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되풀이했다."], ["\"Who instructed you to call on Mr. Quiverful this morning?\"", "“누가 자네더러 오늘 아침 퀴버풀 씨를 찾아가라고 시켰나?”"], ["There was another pause.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["\"Do you intend to answer me, sir?\"", "“내게 대답할 생각이 있기는 한가?”"]], [["\"I think, Mrs. Proudie, that under all the circumstances it will be better for me not to answer such a question,\"", "“프라우디 부인, 저는 모든 사정을 고려할 때 그런 물음에는 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각합니다.”"], ["said Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Slope. Mr.", "슬로프 씨의 목소리에는 여러 가락이 있었고 모두 그의 뜻대로 부릴 수 있었다."], ["Slope had many tones in his voice, all duly under his command; among them was a sanctified low tone and a sanctified loud tone--he now used the former.", "그중에는 성스러운 낮은 가락과 성스러운 높은 가락이 있었는데, 그는 지금 앞엣것을 썼다."]], [["\"Did anyone send you, sir?\"", "“누가 자네를 보냈나, 이 사람아?”"]], [["\"Mrs. Proudie,\"", "“프라우디 부인,”"], ["said Mr. Slope, \"I am quite aware how much I owe to your kindness.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["I am aware also what is due by courtesy from a gentleman to a lady.", "“제가 부인의 호의에 얼마나 큰 빚을 지고 있는지 저도 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["But there are higher considerations than either of those, and I hope I shall be forgiven if I now allow myself to be actuated solely by them.", "신사가 숙녀에게 갖추어야 할 예의가 무엇인지도 알고 있습니다. 그러나 그 둘보다 더 높은 고려가 있으며, 이제 제가 오로지 그것에만 움직이도록 스스로를 내맡긴다 해도 부디 용서받기를 바랍니다."], ["My duty in this matter is to his lordship, and I can admit of no questioning but from him.", "이 일에서 저의 의무는 주교님께 있으며, 저는 오직 그분의 물음에만 답할 수 있습니다."], ["He has approved of what I have done, and you must excuse me if I say that, having that approval and my own, I want none other.\"", "그분께서 제가 한 일을 승인하셨으니, 그 승인과 저 자신의 승인이 있는 이상 다른 어떤 승인도 필요하지 않다고 말씀드려도 부인께서는 저를 너그러이 보아 주셔야 하겠습니다.”"]], [["What horrid words were these which greeted the ear of Mrs. Proudie?", "프라우디 부인의 귀를 때린 이 무시무시한 말은 대체 무엇이었던가?"], ["The matter was indeed too clear.", "사태는 참으로 명백했다."], ["There was premeditated mutiny in the camp.", "진영 안에 미리 꾸며진 반란이 있었다."], ["Not only had ill-conditioned minds become insubordinate by the fruition of a little power, but sedition had been overtly taught and preached.", "못된 심보들이 손바닥만 한 권력을 맛보고 불복종해진 데 그치지 않고, 반역이 대놓고 가르쳐지고 설교되기까지 한 것이다."], ["The bishop had not yet been twelve months in his chair, and rebellion had already reared her hideous head within the palace.", "주교가 그 자리에 앉은 지 아직 열두 달도 되지 않았는데 반란은 벌써 주교관 안에서 그 흉측한 머리를 쳐들었다."], ["Anarchy and misrule would quickly follow unless she took immediate and strong measures to put down the conspiracy which she had detected.", "자기가 찾아낸 이 음모를 당장 강력한 수단으로 눌러 놓지 않는다면 무정부와 난정이 곧 뒤따를 것이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨,”"], ["she said with slow and dignified voice, differing much from that which she had hitherto used, \"Mr.", "그녀가 지금까지 쓰던 것과는 사뭇 다른, 느리고 위엄 있는 목소리로 말했다."], ["Slope, I will trouble you, if you please, to leave the apartment.", "“슬로프 씨, 미안하지만 이 방에서 나가 주게."], ["I wish to speak to my lord alone.\"", "나는 주교님과 단둘이 이야기하고 싶네.”"]], [["Mr. Slope also felt that everything depended on the present interview.", "슬로프 씨 또한 모든 것이 지금 이 자리에 달려 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Should the bishop now be re-petticoated, his thraldom would be complete and forever.", "주교가 여기서 다시 치마폭에 싸인다면 그의 예속은 완전해질 것이고 또 영영 풀리지 않을 것이었다."], ["The present moment was peculiarly propitious for rebellion.", "지금 이 순간은 반란에 유난히 좋은 때였다."], ["The bishop had clearly committed himself by breaking the seal of the answer to the archbishop; he had therefore fear to influence him. Mr.", "주교는 대주교에게 보낼 답장의 봉인을 뜯음으로써 이미 스스로를 얽어매 놓았으니, 그를 움직일 두려움이 있었다."], ["Slope had told him that no consideration ought to induce him to refuse the archbishop's invitation; he had therefore hope to influence him.", "슬로프 씨는 그에게 무슨 사정이 있어도 대주교의 초청을 거절해서는 안 된다고 일러 두었으니, 그를 움직일 희망이 있었다."], ["He had accepted Mr. Quiverful's resignation and therefore dreaded having to renew that matter with his wife.", "그는 퀴버풀 씨의 사임을 받아들였으니 그 문제를 아내와 다시 꺼내야 하는 것을 두려워하고 있었다."], ["He had been screwed up to the pitch of asserting a will of his own, and might possibly be carried on till by an absolute success he should have been taught how possible it was to succeed.", "그는 제 뜻이라는 것을 내세울 만큼 조여져 있었고, 어쩌면 완전한 성공에 이르러 성공이 얼마나 가능한 일인지를 배우게 될 때까지 계속 밀려갈 수도 있었다."], ["Now was the moment for victory or rout.", "지금이야말로 승리 아니면 패주의 순간이었다."], ["It was now that Mr. Slope must make himself master of the diocese, or else resign his place and begin his search for fortune again.", "슬로프 씨가 교구의 주인이 되느냐, 아니면 자리를 내놓고 다시 출셋길을 찾아 나서느냐가 지금 결정되는 것이었다."], ["He saw all this plainly.", "그는 이 모든 것을 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["After what had taken place any compromise between him and the lady was impossible.", "이미 벌어진 일이 있는 이상 그와 저 부인 사이에 타협이란 있을 수 없었다."], ["Let him once leave the room at her bidding and leave the bishop in her hands, and he might at once pack up his portmanteau and bid adieu to episcopal honours, Mrs. Bold, and the Signora Neroni.", "그녀의 분부대로 한 번 이 방을 나가 주교를 그녀의 손에 남겨 둔다면, 그는 그 길로 여행 가방을 꾸려 주교좌의 영예와 볼드 부인과 시뇨라 네로니에게 작별을 고하면 그만이었다."]], [["And yet it was not so easy to keep his ground when he was bidden by a lady to go, or to continue to make a third in a party between a husband and wife when the wife expressed a wish for a tête-à-tête with her husband.", "그러면서도 숙녀에게 나가라는 말을 듣고 그 자리를 지키기란, 또 아내가 남편과 단둘이 있는 tête-à-tête를 바란다고 밝히는 마당에 부부 사이에 제삼자로 계속 끼어 있기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨,”"], ["she repeated, \"I wish to be alone with my lord.\"", "그녀가 되풀이했다. “나는 주교님과 단둘이 있고 싶네.”"]], [["\"His lordship has summoned me on most important diocesan business,\"", "“주교님께서 지극히 중요한 교구 사무로 저를 부르셨습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope, glancing with uneasy eye at Dr.", "슬로프 씨가 불안한 눈길로 프라우디 박사를 흘깃 보며 말했다."], ["Proudie. He felt that he must trust something to the bishop, and yet that that trust was so woefully ill-placed.", "그는 무언가를 주교에게 맡길 수밖에 없다고 느꼈으나, 그 믿음을 두기에 그처럼 딱한 자리도 없다는 것 또한 느꼈다."], ["\"My leaving him at the present moment is, I fear, impossible.\"", "“지금 이 순간에 제가 자리를 뜨기란 유감스럽게도 불가능합니다.”"]], [["\"Do you bandy words with me, you ungrateful man?\"", "“이 은혜도 모르는 사람이, 감히 나와 말을 겨루려 드는가?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"My lord, will you do me the favour to beg Mr. Slope to leave the room?\"", "“주교님, 부디 슬로프 씨에게 이 방에서 나가라고 일러 주세요.”"]], [["My lord scratched his head, but for the moment said nothing.", "주교 각하는 머리를 긁적였을 뿐 당장은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["This was as much as Mr. Slope expected from him, and was on the whole, for him, an active exercise of marital rights.", "이는 슬로프 씨가 그에게서 기대한 만큼은 되는 것이었고, 그로서는 대체로 남편의 권리를 자못 적극적으로 행사한 셈이었다."]], [["\"My lord,\"", "“주교님,”"], ["said the lady, \"is Mr.", "부인이 말했다."], ["Slope to leave this room, or am I?\"", "“슬로프 씨가 이 방을 나가야 합니까, 아니면 제가 나가야 합니까?”"]], [["Here Mrs. Proudie made a false step.", "여기서 프라우디 부인은 한 발을 헛디뎠다."], ["She should not have alluded to the possibility of retreat on her part.", "그녀는 제 쪽에서 물러날 가능성을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다."], ["She should not have expressed the idea that her order for Mr. Slope's expulsion could be treated otherwise than by immediate obedience.", "슬로프 씨를 내보내라는 자기 명령이 즉각적인 복종 말고 다른 방식으로 처리될 수도 있다는 생각을 드러내서는 안 되었다."], ["In answer to such a question the bishop naturally said in his own mind that, as it was necessary that one should leave the room, perhaps it might be as well that Mrs. Proudie did so.", "그런 물음을 받고 보니 주교는 자연히 속으로, 어차피 누군가 하나는 방을 나가야 한다면 나가는 쪽이 프라우디 부인인 편이 나을지도 모르겠다고 생각했다."], ["He did say so in his own mind, but externally he again scratched his head and again twiddled his thumbs.", "속으로는 그렇게 말했으나 겉으로는 또다시 머리를 긁적이고 또다시 두 엄지를 맞돌렸다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie was boiling over with wrath.", "프라우디 부인은 노여움으로 끓어 넘치고 있었다."], ["Alas, alas!", "아아, 슬프도다!"], ["Could she but have kept her temper as her enemy did, she would have conquered as she had ever conquered.", "적이 그리했듯 그녀도 성미를 다스릴 수만 있었더라면 늘 그래 왔듯 이겼을 것이다."], ["But divine anger got the better of her, as it has done of other heroines, and she fell.", "그러나 다른 여걸들이 그러했듯 그녀 또한 신들린 노여움에 사로잡혔고, 그리하여 넘어졌다."]], [["\"My lord,\"", "“주교님,”"], ["said she, \"am I to be vouchsafed an answer or am I not?\"", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 대답을 들을 수 있는 건가요, 없는 건가요?”"]], [["At last he broke his deep silence and proclaimed himself a Slopeite.", "마침내 그가 깊은 침묵을 깨고 자신이 슬로프 편임을 선언했다."], ["\"Why, my dear,\"", "“저, 여보,”"], ["said he, \"Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Slope and I are very busy.\"", "“슬로프 씨와 나는 지금 아주 바쁘오.”"]], [["That was all.", "그것이 전부였다."], ["There was nothing more necessary.", "그 이상은 필요하지 않았다."], ["He had gone to the battlefield, stood the dust and heat of the day, encountered the fury of the foe, and won the victory.", "그는 싸움터에 나아가 온종일의 먼지와 열기를 견디고 적의 격노를 마주하여 승리를 거두었다."], ["How easy is success to those who will only be true to themselves!", "오직 제 자신에게 진실하기만 하다면 성공이란 얼마나 쉬운 것인가!"]], [["Mr. Slope saw at once the full amount of his gain, and turned on the vanquished lady a look of triumph which she never forgot and never forgave.", "슬로프 씨는 자기가 얻은 것의 크기를 대번에 알아보고, 패배한 부인에게 그녀가 결코 잊지도 용서하지도 않을 승리의 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Here he was wrong.", "여기서 그는 틀렸다."], ["He should have looked humbly at her and, with meek entreating eye, have deprecated her anger.", "그는 겸손하게 그녀를 바라보며 온순하게 애원하는 눈으로 그 노여움을 달래었어야 했다."], ["He should have said by his glance that he asked pardon for his success, and that he hoped forgiveness for the stand which he had been forced to make in the cause of duty.", "자기가 이긴 것을 용서해 달라고, 의무를 위해 어쩔 수 없이 버틴 것을 너그러이 보아 달라고 눈빛으로 말했어야 했다."], ["So might he perchance have somewhat mollified that imperious bosom and prepared the way for future terms.", "그랬더라면 그 위압적인 가슴을 얼마쯤 누그러뜨리고 훗날의 화해를 위한 길을 닦았을지도 모른다."], ["But Mr. Slope meant to rule without terms.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 화해 없이 다스릴 작정이었다."], ["Ah, forgetful, inexperienced man!", "아, 잊어버리기도 잘하는, 세상 물정 모르는 사내여!"], ["Can you cause that little trembling victim to be divorced from the woman that possesses him?", "그대는 저 조그맣게 떨고 있는 제물을 그를 차지한 여자에게서 떼어 놓을 수 있는가?"], ["Can you provide that they shall be separated at bed and board?", "두 사람을 잠자리와 밥상에서 갈라놓을 수 있는가?"], ["Is he not flesh of her flesh and bone of her bone, and must he not so continue?", "그는 그녀의 살 중의 살이요 뼈 중의 뼈가 아닌가, 또 앞으로도 그러해야 하지 않겠는가?"], ["It is very well now for you to stand your ground and triumph as she is driven ignominiously from the room, but can you be present when those curtains are drawn, when that awful helmet of proof has been tied beneath the chin, when the small remnants of the bishop's prowess shall be cowed by the tassel above his head?", "그녀가 욕되게 방에서 쫓겨나는 지금이야 그대가 버티고 서서 개가를 올려도 좋겠으나, 저 휘장이 드리워질 때, 저 무시무시한 투구가 턱 밑에 매어질 때, 주교에게 남은 얼마 안 되는 용맹이 머리 위 술 장식에 눌려 기가 죽을 때, 그대가 거기 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Can you then intrude yourself when the wife wishes \"to speak to my lord alone?\"", "아내가 “주교님과 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다”고 할 때, 그대가 그 자리에 끼어들 수 있겠는가?"]], [["But for the moment Mr. Slope's triumph was complete, for Mrs.", "그러나 당장으로는 슬로프 씨의 승리가 완전했으니, 프라우디 부인이 더 말을 섞지 않고 방을 나갔고 나가면서 문을 닫는 일도 잊지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Proudie without further parley left the room and did not forget to shut the door after her. Then followed a close conference between the new allies, in which was said much which it astonished Mr.", "그다음에는 새 동맹자들 사이에 긴밀한 회담이 이어졌는데, 그 자리에서는 슬로프 씨로서는 말하고 주교로서는 듣는 것이 스스로도 놀라울 만한 이야기가 숱하게 오갔다."], ["Slope to say and the bishop to hear. And yet the one said it and the other heard it without ill-will.", "그런데도 하나는 그것을 말했고 다른 하나는 그것을 들었으되 서로 감정이 상하지는 않았다."], ["There was no mincing of matters now.", "이제는 에두를 것이 없었다."], ["The chaplain plainly told the bishop that the world gave him credit for being under the governance of his wife; that his credit and character in the diocese were suffering; that he would surely get himself in hot water if he allowed Mrs.", "전속 사제는 주교에게 이렇게 대놓고 말했다. 세상은 각하께서 부인의 다스림 아래 계신다고 여기고 있으며, 교구 안에서 각하의 신망과 인격이 손상되고 있으며, 여자의 힘에 어울리지 않는 일에까지 프라우디 부인이 간섭하시게 두었다가는 반드시 곤경에 빠지실 것이며, 실은 그 신음하고 계신 멍에를 벗어 던지지 않으신다면 각하께서는 웃음거리가 되고 마시리라고."], ["Proudie to interfere in matters which were not suitable for a woman's powers; and in fact that he would become contemptible if he did not throw off the yoke under which he groaned.", "주교는 처음에는 헛기침을 하고 우물거리며 그 말이 사실이 아닌 척했다. 그러나 그 부정은 굳세지 못했고 이내 무너졌다."], ["The bishop at first hummed and hawed and affected to deny the truth of what was said.", "그는 곧 침묵으로써 제가 신하의 처지임을 인정했고, 슬로프 씨의 도움을 받아 방식을 바꾸겠노라고 약속했다."], ["But his denial was not stout and quickly broke down.", "슬로프 씨 또한 제 처지를 그럴싸하게 변호했다."], ["He soon admitted by silence his state of vassalage and pledged himself, with Mr. Slope's assistance, to change his courses.", "그는 늘 자기를 후원해 준 부인, 그토록 여러 모로 자기를 도와준 부인, 실은 자기를 주교의 눈에 들도록 천거해 준 바로 그 부인을 거스르는 것이 얼마나 마음 아픈 일인지를 설명했다."], ["Mr. Slope also did not make out a bad case for himself.", "그러나 그의 말대로 이제 그의 의무는 지엄한 것이었다."], ["He explained how it grieved him to run counter to a lady who had always been his patroness, who had befriended him in so many ways, who had, in fact, recommended him to the bishop's notice; but, as he stated, his duty was now imperative; he held a situation of peculiar confidence, and was immediately and especially attached to the bishop's person.", "그는 각별한 신임의 자리에 있고 주교의 신변에 직접 그리고 특별히 매여 있다는 것이었다."], ["In such a situation his conscience required that he should regard solely the bishop's interests, and therefore he had ventured to speak out.", "그런 자리에 있는 만큼 그의 양심은 오직 주교의 이익만을 살필 것을 요구했고, 그래서 그는 감히 솔직히 말씀드린 것이라고 했다."]], [["The bishop took this for what it was worth, and Mr. Slope only intended that he should do so.", "주교는 이 말을 그 말값만큼만 받아들였고, 슬로프 씨도 딱 그만큼만 의도했다."], ["It gilded the pill which Mr. Slope had to administer, and which the bishop thought would be less bitter than that other pill which he had so long been taking.", "그것은 슬로프 씨가 먹여야 했던 알약에 금박을 입힌 것이었고, 주교는 그 약이 그동안 그토록 오래 먹어 온 다른 알약보다는 덜 쓰리라고 여겼다."]], [["\"My lord,\" had his immediate reward, like a good child.", "“주교님”은 착한 아이처럼 곧바로 상을 받았다."], ["He was instructed to write and at once did write another note to the archbishop accepting his grace's invitation. This note Mr.", "그는 편지를 쓰라는 지시를 받았고 즉시 대주교에게 그 초청을 받아들이겠다는 또 하나의 쪽지를 썼다."], ["Slope, more prudent than the lady, himself took away and posted with his own hands.", "이 쪽지는 부인보다 신중한 슬로프 씨가 몸소 들고 나가 제 손으로 부쳤다."], ["Thus he made sure that this act of self-jurisdiction should be as nearly as possible a fait accompli. He begged, and coaxed, and threatened the bishop with a view of making him also write at once to Mr.", "그리하여 그는 이 자주적 처분의 행위가 가능한 한 완전히 fait accompli 가 되도록 못을 박았다."], ["Harding, but the bishop, though temporally emancipated from his wife, was not yet enthralled to Mr. Slope.", "그는 주교가 하딩 씨에게도 당장 편지를 쓰게 하려고 빌기도 하고 구슬리기도 하고 으르기도 했으나, 주교는 잠시 아내에게서 풀려나기는 했어도 아직 슬로프 씨에게 사로잡히지는 않았다."], ["He said, and probably said truly, that such an offer must be made in some official form; that he was not yet prepared to sign the form; and that he should prefer seeing Mr. Harding before he did so.", "그는 그런 제안은 어떤 공식적인 형식을 갖추어야 하며, 자기는 아직 그 형식에 서명할 준비가 되지 않았고, 서명하기 전에 하딩 씨를 만나 보고 싶다고 말했는데, 아마 참말이었을 것이다."], ["Mr. Slope might, however, beg Mr.", "다만 슬로프 씨가 하딩 씨더러 자기를 찾아와 달라고 청하는 것은 좋다고 했다."], ["Harding to call upon him. Not disappointed with his achievement Mr.", "제가 이룬 것에 실망하지 않은 채 슬로프 씨는 제 길을 갔다."], ["Slope went his way. He first posted the precious note which he had in his pocket, and then pursued other enterprises in which we must follow him in other chapters.", "그는 먼저 주머니 속의 그 귀한 쪽지를 부쳤고, 그런 다음 다른 일들에 나섰으니 우리는 다른 장에서 그를 따라가 보아야 한다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie, having received such satisfaction as was to be derived from slamming her husband's door, did not at once betake herself to Mrs.", "프라우디 부인은 남편의 방문을 쾅 닫는 데서 얻을 수 있는 만큼의 만족을 얻은 뒤에도 곧바로 퀴버풀 부인에게로 가지는 않았다."], ["Quiverful. Indeed, for the first few moments after her repulse she felt that she could not again see that lady.", "실은 물리침을 당한 뒤 처음 얼마 동안은 그 부인을 다시 볼 낯이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["She would have to own that she had been beaten, to confess that the diadem had passed from her brow, and the sceptre from her hand!", "자기가 졌음을 인정해야 하고, 왕관이 제 이마에서, 왕홀이 제 손에서 떠나갔음을 고백해야 할 터였다!"], ["No, she would send a message to her with a promise of a letter on the next day or the day after.", "아니, 내일이나 모레 편지를 보내겠다는 약속과 함께 전갈이나 하나 넣어 두자."], ["Thus resolving, she betook herself to her bedroom, but here she again changed her mind.", "그렇게 마음먹고 그녀는 침실로 갔으나, 여기서 다시 마음이 바뀌었다."], ["The air of that sacred enclosure somewhat restored her courage and gave her more heart.", "그 신성한 방의 공기가 그녀의 용기를 얼마쯤 되살려 주고 기운을 북돋아 주었다."], ["As Achilles warmed at the sight of his armour, as Don Quixote's heart grew strong when he grasped his lance, so did Mrs. Proudie look forward to fresh laurels, as her eye fell on her husband's pillow.", "아킬레우스가 제 갑주를 보고 몸이 달아올랐듯이, 돈키호테의 가슴이 창을 움켜쥐자 굳세어졌듯이, 프라우디 부인도 남편의 베개에 눈길이 닿자 새로운 월계관을 내다보았다."], ["She would not despair.", "그녀는 절망하지 않으리라."], ["Having so resolved, she descended with dignified mien and refreshed countenance to Mrs. Quiverful.", "그렇게 마음을 정하고 그녀는 위엄 있는 몸가짐과 되살아난 낯빛으로 퀴버풀 부인에게 내려갔다."]], [["This scene in the bishop's study took longer in the acting than in the telling.", "주교의 서재에서 벌어진 이 장면은 이야기하는 데보다 실제로 벌어지는 데 더 오래 걸렸다."], ["We have not, perhaps, had the whole of the conversation. At any rate Mrs.", "어쩌면 우리가 그 대화를 다 옮기지 못했는지도 모른다."], ["Quiverful was beginning to be very impatient, and was thinking that Farmer Subsoil would be tired of waiting for her, when Mrs. Proudie returned.", "어쨌든 퀴버풀 부인은 몹시 초조해지기 시작해서, 서브소일 농부가 자기를 기다리다 지쳐 버리지나 않았을까 생각하고 있던 참에 프라우디 부인이 돌아왔다."], ["Oh, who can tell the palpitations of that maternal heart, as the suppliant looked into the face of the great lady to see written there either a promise of house, income, comfort and future competence, or else the doom of continued and ever-increasing poverty!", "아, 저 어머니의 가슴이 얼마나 두근거렸을지 누가 말할 수 있으랴. 탄원하는 이는 저 대단한 부인의 얼굴을 들여다보며 거기에 집과 수입과 안락과 앞날의 넉넉함에 대한 약속이 적혀 있는지, 아니면 끝없이 이어지며 점점 깊어질 가난의 선고가 적혀 있는지를 읽으려 했으니!"], ["Poor mother!", "가엾은 어머니여!"], ["Poor wife!", "가엾은 아내여!"], ["There was little there to comfort you!", "거기에는 그대를 위로할 것이 별로 없었다!"]], [["\"Mrs. Quiverful,\"", "“퀴버풀 부인,”"], ["thus spoke the lady with considerable austerity, and without sitting down herself, \"I find that your husband has behaved in this matter in a very weak and foolish manner.\"", "부인은 자기는 앉지도 않은 채 자못 엄한 어조로 이렇게 말했다. “내가 보기에 자네 남편은 이 일에서 아주 무르고 아주 어리석게 처신했네.”"]], [["Mrs. Quiverful immediately rose upon her feet, thinking it disrespectful to remain sitting while the wife of the bishop stood.", "퀴버풀 부인은 주교의 부인이 서 있는데 자기가 앉아 있는 것은 무례한 일이라 여겨 즉시 일어섰다."], ["But she was desired to sit down again, and made to do so, so that Mrs. Proudie might stand and preach over her.", "그러나 다시 앉으라는 분부를 받고 그대로 앉혀졌으니, 프라우디 부인이 선 채로 그녀 위에서 설교를 하려 함이었다."], ["It is generally considered an offensive thing for a gentleman to keep his seat while another is kept standing before him, and we presume the same law holds with regard to ladies.", "신사가 다른 사람을 제 앞에 세워 둔 채 제 자리에 앉아 있는 것은 일반적으로 무례한 일로 여겨지는데, 짐작건대 부인네들에게도 같은 법이 적용될 것이다. 흔히 그렇게 느껴지기는 하나, 우리가 보기에 그것은 손님더러 앉으라고 청해 놓고 자기는 제 다리로 서서 말하는 대인물이 드러내는 불쾌함이나 아랫사람 취급을 당한다는 느낌에 견주면 절반에도 미치지 못한다."], ["It often is so felt, but we are inclined to say that it never produces half the discomfort or half the feeling of implied inferiority that is shown by a great man who desires his visitor to be seated while he himself speaks from his legs.", "예법에 어긋난 이런 짓을 우리말로 옮기면 이런 뜻이 된다. “세상에서 통용되는 예의의 규칙에 따르면 나는 그대에게 자리를 권해야 하오."], ["Such a solecism in good breeding, when construed into English, means this: \"The accepted rules of courtesy in the world require that I should offer you a seat; if I did not do so, you would bring a charge against me in the world of being arrogant and ill-mannered; I will obey the world, but, nevertheless, I will not put myself on an equality with you.", "내가 그리하지 않는다면 그대는 세상에 대고 내가 오만하고 버릇없다고 나무랄 것이오. 나는 세상에 따르겠소."], ["You may sit down, but I won't sit with you.", "그러나 그렇다고 내가 그대와 대등한 자리에 서지는 않겠소. 그대는 앉아도 좋으나 나는 그대와 함께 앉지는 않겠소."], ["Sit, therefore, at my bidding, and I'll stand and talk at you!\"", "그러니 내가 이르는 대로 앉으시오. 그러면 나는 서서 그대에게 대고 말하겠소!”"]], [["This was just what Mrs. Proudie meant to say, and Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 하려던 말이 바로 이것이었고, 퀴버풀 부인은 너무 애가 타고 너무 허둥대어 그 술책의 온전한 뜻까지 새기지는 못했으나 그 효과만은 어김없이 느꼈다."], ["Quiverful, though she was too anxious and too flurried thus to translate the full meaning of the manoeuvre, did not fail to feel its effect. She was cowed and uncomfortable, and a second time essayed to rise from her chair.", "그녀는 기가 죽고 마음이 불편해져서 두 번째로 의자에서 일어서려 했다."]], [["\"Pray be seated, Mrs. Quiverful, pray keep your seat.", "“앉아 있게, 퀴버풀 부인, 그대로 앉아 있게."], ["Your husband, I say, has been most weak and most foolish.", "내 말은 자네 남편이 지극히 무르고 지극히 어리석었다는 것일세."], ["It is impossible, Mrs. Quiverful, to help people who will not help themselves.", "퀴버풀 부인, 제 스스로를 돕지 않는 사람을 돕기란 불가능한 일이야."], ["I much fear that I can now do nothing for you in this matter.\"", "이 일에서 내가 이제 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이 아무것도 없지 않을까 몹시 걱정일세.”"]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Proudie, don't say so,\"", "“아이고, 프라우디 부인, 그런 말씀 마십시오.”"], ["said the poor woman, again jumping up.", "가엾은 여인이 또다시 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."]], [["\"Pray be seated, Mrs. Quiverful.", "“앉아 있으래도, 퀴버풀 부인."], ["I must fear that I can do nothing further for you in this matter.", "이 일에서 내가 자네를 위해 더 해 줄 수 있는 것이 없을까 걱정이라니까."], ["Your husband has, in a most unaccountable manner, taken upon himself to resign that which I was empowered to offer him.", "자네 남편은 참으로 알 수 없게도 내가 내어 줄 권한을 받은 그 자리를 제 손으로 내놓아 버렸네."], ["As a matter of course, the bishop expects that his clergy shall know their own minds.", "당연한 이야기지만 주교님께서는 당신 아래 성직자들이 제 마음을 스스로 알고 있기를 기대하시지."], ["What he may ultimately do--what we may finally decide on doing--I cannot now say.", "그분이 끝내 어떻게 하실지, 우리가 마침내 무엇을 하기로 정할지 나는 지금 말할 수 없네."], ["Knowing the extent of your family--\"", "자네 식구가 얼마나 되는지를 알고 있으니—”"]], [["\"Fourteen children, Mrs.", "“아이가 열넷입니다, 프라우디 부인, 열넷이라고요!"], ["Proudie, fourteen of them! And barely bread--barely bread?", "그런데 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하는 형편이라니, 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하다니요!"], ["It's hard for the children of a clergyman, it's hard for one who has always done his duty respectably!\"", "성직자의 자식들에게는 가혹한 일입니다. 늘 제 직분을 번듯하게 다해 온 사람에게도 가혹한 일이고요!”"], ["Not a word fell from her about herself, but the tears came streaming down her big, coarse cheeks, on which the dust of the August road had left its traces.", "제 자신에 대해서는 한마디도 나오지 않았으나, 8월의 길바닥 먼지가 자국을 남긴 그 크고 거친 뺨을 타고 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie has not been portrayed in these pages as an agreeable or an amiable lady.", "프라우디 부인은 이 지면에서 상냥하거나 사랑스러운 부인으로 그려지지 않았다."], ["There has been no intention to impress the reader much in her favour.", "독자가 그녀에게 크게 호감을 갖게 하려는 의도도 없었다."], ["It is ordained that all novels should have a male and a female angel and a male and a female devil.", "무릇 모든 소설에는 남자 천사와 여자 천사가 하나씩, 남자 악마와 여자 악마가 하나씩 있어야 한다는 것이 정해진 법도다."], ["If it be considered that this rule is obeyed in these pages, the latter character must be supposed to have fallen to the lot of Mrs.", "이 지면에서도 그 법도가 지켜졌다고 본다면, 뒤엣것 가운데 여자 쪽 배역은 프라우디 부인에게 돌아갔다고 보아야 할 것이다."], ["Proudie. But she was not all devil.", "그러나 그녀가 온통 악마이기만 했던 것은 아니다."], ["There was a heart inside that stiff-ribbed bodice, though not, perhaps, of large dimensions, and certainly not easily accessible. Mrs.", "그 뻣뻣하게 살을 댄 몸판 안에도 심장이 하나 있기는 있었다."], ["Quiverful, however, did gain access, and Mrs. Proudie proved herself a woman.", "크기가 대단하지는 않았을 테고 쉬이 닿을 수 있는 것도 분명 아니었지만 말이다."], ["Whether it was the fourteen children with their probable bare bread and their possible bare backs, or the respectability of the father's work, or the mingled dust and tears on the mother's face, we will not pretend to say.", "그런데 퀴버풀 부인은 기어이 거기에 닿았고, 프라우디 부인은 스스로 여자임을 드러내 보였다. 그것이 열네 아이와 그들이 겨우 얻을 빵과 어쩌면 걸칠 것조차 없을 등짝 때문이었는지, 아버지 된 이가 해 온 일의 번듯함 때문이었는지, 아니면 어머니의 얼굴에 뒤섞인 먼지와 눈물 때문이었는지, 우리는 굳이 말하지 않겠다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie was touched.", "어쨌든 프라우디 부인의 마음이 움직였다."]], [["She did not show it as other women might have done.", "그녀는 다른 여자들이 했을 법한 방식으로 그것을 드러내지는 않았다."], ["She did not give Mrs. Quiverful eau-de-Cologne, or order her a glass of wine.", "퀴버풀 부인에게 오드콜로뉴를 건네지도, 포도주 한 잔을 내오라 이르지도 않았다."], ["She did not take her to her toilet table and offer her the use of brushes and combs, towels and water.", "화장대로 데려가 솔과 빗, 수건과 물을 쓰라고 권하지도 않았다."], ["She did not say soft little speeches and coax her kindly back to equanimity.", "부드러운 말을 조금씩 건네며 다정하게 달래어 마음을 가라앉혀 주지도 않았다."], ["Mrs. Quiverful, despite her rough appearance, would have been as amenable to such little tender cares as any lady in the land.", "퀴버풀 부인은 겉모습이 거칠기는 해도 이 나라 어느 귀부인 못지않게 그런 자잘한 다정한 보살핌을 반겼을 사람이었다."], ["But none such were forthcoming.", "그러나 그런 것은 하나도 나오지 않았다."], ["Instead of this, Mrs. Proudie slapped one hand upon the other and declared--not with an oath, for, as a lady and a Sabbatarian and a she-bishop, she could not swear, but with an adjuration--that she \"wouldn't have it done.\"", "그 대신 프라우디 부인은 한 손으로 다른 손을 철썩 치며, 욕설을 곁들여서가 아니라 — 숙녀이자 안식일 엄수주의자이자 여자 주교인 그녀로서는 욕설을 할 수 없었으므로 — 엄숙한 다짐을 곁들여, 자기는 “그런 일이 되게 두지 않겠다”고 선언했다."]], [["The meaning of this was that she wouldn't have Mr. Quiverful's promised appointment cozened away by the treachery of Mr. Slope and the weakness of her husband. This meaning she very soon explained to Mrs. Quiverful.", "이 말의 뜻인즉, 퀴버풀 씨에게 약속된 임명이 슬로프 씨의 배신과 제 남편의 무름으로 인해 감쪽같이 가로채이도록 두지 않겠다는 것이었다. 이 뜻을 그녀는 곧 퀴버풀 부인에게 설명해 주었다."]], [["\"Why was your husband such a fool,\"", "“자네 남편은 어쩌자고 그런 바보짓을 했나.”"], ["said she, now dismounted from her high horse and sitting confidentially down close to her visitor, \"as to take the bait which that man threw to him?", "이제 높은 말에서 내려와 손님 곁에 바싹 다가앉으며 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람이 던진 미끼를 덥석 물다니."], ["If he had not been so utterly foolish, nothing could have prevented your going to the hospital.\"", "그이가 그토록 어리석게 굴지만 않았더라면 자네들이 요양원에 들어가는 것을 아무것도 막지 못했을 걸세.”"]], [["Poor Mrs. Quiverful was ready enough with her own tongue in accusing her husband to his face of being soft, and perhaps did not always speak of him to her children quite so respectfully as she might have done. But she did not at all like to hear him abused by others, and began to vindicate him and to explain that of course he had taken Mr. Slope to be an emissary from Mrs. Proudie herself; that Mr. Slope was thought to be peculiarly her friend; and that, therefore, Mr. Quiverful would have been failing in respect to her had he assumed to doubt what Mr. Slope had said.", "가엾은 퀴버풀 부인은 남편을 앞에 두고 물러 터졌다고 나무라는 데는 제 혀가 꽤나 잘 돌아가는 사람이었고, 아이들 앞에서 남편 이야기를 할 때도 늘 그리 공손하지만은 않았을 것이다. 그러나 남들이 남편을 헐뜯는 것은 조금도 듣기 좋지 않았으므로 그녀는 남편을 두둔하기 시작했다. 그이는 당연히 슬로프 씨를 프라우디 부인 본인이 보낸 사자로 여겼고, 슬로프 씨는 특별히 부인의 벗으로 알려져 있으니, 슬로프 씨가 한 말을 감히 의심했다면 퀴버풀 씨는 부인께 예를 잃는 것이 되었으리라고 설명했다."]], [["Thus mollified, Mrs. Proudie again declared that she \"would not have it done,\" and at last sent Mrs. Quiverful home with an assurance that, to the furthest stretch of her power and influence in the palace, the appointment of Mr. Quiverful should be insisted on. As she repeated the word \"insisted,\" she thought of the bishop in his night-cap and, with compressed lips, slightly shook her head. Oh, my aspiring pastors, divines to whose ears nolo episcopari are the sweetest of words, which of you would be a bishop on such terms as these?", "그렇게 마음이 누그러진 프라우디 부인은 다시 한 번 자기는 “그런 일이 되게 두지 않겠다”고 선언했고, 마침내 주교관 안에서 제 권세와 영향력이 미치는 끝까지 퀴버풀 씨의 임명을 관철하겠다는 다짐과 함께 퀴버풀 부인을 집으로 돌려보냈다. “관철”이라는 말을 되뇌면서 그녀는 잠자리 모자를 쓴 주교를 떠올리고는 입술을 꾹 다문 채 고개를 조금 저었다. 아, 출세를 바라는 목사들이여, nolo episcopari 라는 말이 그 귀에 가장 달콤하게 들리는 신학자들이여, 그대들 가운데 누가 이런 조건으로 주교가 되려 하겠는가?"]], [["Mrs.", "퀴버풀 부인은 농부의 수레를 타고 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Quiverful got home in the farmer's cart, not indeed with a light heart, but satisfied that she had done right in making her visit.", "마음이 가볍지야 않았으나, 그래도 찾아가 보기를 잘했다는 데는 만족하고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII. A Love Scene", "# 제27장. 사랑의 한 장면"]], [["Mr. Slope, as we have said, left the palace with a feeling of considerable triumph.", "슬로프 씨는, 앞서 말한 대로, 상당한 승리감을 안고 주교관을 나섰다."], ["Not that he thought that his difficulties were all over--he did not so deceive himself--but he felt that he had played his first move well, as well as the pieces on the board would allow, and that he had nothing with which to reproach himself.", "어려움이 죄다 끝났다고 여긴 것은 아니었고 — 그렇게까지 스스로를 속이지는 않았다 — 다만 판 위의 말들이 허락하는 한 첫 수를 잘 두었으며 스스로를 탓할 것이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["He first of all posted the letter to the archbishop and, having made that sure, proceeded to push the advantage which he had gained.", "그는 무엇보다 먼저 대주교에게 보내는 편지를 부쳤고, 그것을 확실히 해 둔 다음 제가 얻어 낸 이점을 밀어붙이러 나섰다."], ["Had Mrs. Bold been at home, he would have called on her, but he knew that she was at Plumstead, so he wrote the following note.", "볼드 부인이 집에 있었더라면 찾아갔겠지만 그녀가 플럼스테드에 있다는 것을 알았으므로 다음과 같은 짧은 편지를 썼다."], ["It was the beginning of what, he trusted, might be a long and tender series of epistles.", "그것은 그가 길고 다정한 서한의 연속이 되리라 믿어 의심치 않은 것의 시작이었다."]], [["You will understand perfectly that I cannot at present correspond with your father. I heartily wish that I could, and hope the day may be not long distant when mists shall have been cleared away, and we may know each other.", "지금으로서는 제가 부인의 아버님과 서신을 주고받을 수 없다는 것을 부인께서 완벽히 이해하시리라 믿습니다."], ["But I cannot preclude myself from the pleasure of sending you these few lines to say that Mr. Q.", "그럴 수 있기를 진심으로 바라며, 안개가 걷히고 우리가 서로를 알게 될 날이 멀지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["has to-day, in my presence, resigned any title that he ever had to the wardenship of the hospital, and that the bishop has assured me that it is his intention to offer it to your esteemed father.", "그러나 오늘 Q 씨가 제가 있는 자리에서 요양원 워든 직에 대해 자신이 지녔던 모든 권리를 사임하였다는 것과, 주교께서 그 자리를 부인의 존경하는 아버님께 제안하실 뜻임을 제게 확언하셨다는 것을 몇 줄 적어 보내는 기쁨만은 스스로에게서 빼앗을 수가 없습니다."]], [["Will you, with my respectful compliments, ask him, who I believe is now a fellow-visitor with you, to call on the bishop either on Wednesday or Thursday, between ten and one.", "제 정중한 인사와 함께, 지금 부인과 함께 머물고 계실 그분께 수요일이나 목요일 열 시에서 한 시 사이에 주교를 찾아뵈어 달라고 청해 주시겠습니까."], ["This is by the bishop's desire.", "이는 주교의 뜻입니다."], ["If you will so far oblige me as to let me have a line naming either day, and the hour which will suit Mr. Harding, I will take care that the servants shall have orders to show him in without delay.", "저를 위해 어느 날인지와 하딩 씨께 편하실 시각을 한 줄 적어 보내 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 하인들이 지체 없이 그분을 안으로 모시도록 일러 두겠습니다."], ["Perhaps I should say no more--but still I wish you could make your father understand that no subject will be mooted between his lordship and him which will refer at all to the method in which he may choose to perform his duty.", "아마 더 말씀드리지 않는 편이 나을 테지만 — 그래도 저는 주교님과 그분 사이에서 그분이 제 직무를 수행하실 방식에 관하여는 어떤 문제도 거론되지 않으리라는 것을 부인께서 아버님께 알려 드리실 수 있기를 바랍니다."], ["I for one am persuaded that no clergyman could perform it more satisfactorily than he did, or than he will do again.", "저로서는 그분이 하셨던 것보다, 또 앞으로 다시 하실 것보다 더 만족스럽게 그 직무를 수행할 성직자는 없으리라 확신합니다."]], [["On a former occasion I was indiscreet and much too impatient, considering your father's age and my own.", "지난번에 저는 신중하지 못했고, 아버님의 연세와 제 나이를 헤아릴 때 지나치게 성급했습니다."], ["I hope he will not now refuse my apology.", "이제는 그분이 제 사과를 물리치지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["I still hope also that with your aid and sweet pious labours we may live to attach such a Sabbath-school to the old endowment as may, by God's grace and furtherance, be a blessing to the poor of this city.", "또한 부인의 도움과 어여쁘고 경건한 수고에 힘입어, 하느님의 은혜와 도우심으로 이 도시의 가난한 이들에게 축복이 될 만한 안식일 학교를 저 오래된 기부 재단에 붙여 놓을 날까지 살 수 있기를 여전히 바랍니다."]], [["You will see at once that this letter is confidential.", "이 편지가 비밀에 부쳐야 할 것임은 부인께서 곧바로 아시리라 봅니다."], ["The subject, of course, makes it so.", "주제가 주제이니 당연히 그렇습니다."], ["But, equally, of course, it is for your parent's eye as well as for your own, should you think proper to show it to him.", "그러나 역시 당연하게도, 보여 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여기신다면 이는 부인의 눈뿐 아니라 아버님의 눈을 위한 것이기도 합니다."]], [["I hope my darling little friend Johnny is as strong as ever--dear little fellow.", "제가 아끼는 작은 벗 조니가 여전히 튼튼하기를 바랍니다 — 사랑스러운 꼬마 녀석."], ["Does he still continue his rude assaults on those beautiful long silken tresses?", "그 아름답고 긴 비단결 머리채에 여전히 무례한 공격을 가하고 있는지요?"]], [["I can assure you your friends miss you from Barchester sorely, but it would be cruel to begrudge you your sojourn among flowers and fields during this truly sultry weather.", "바체스터에서 부인의 벗들이 부인을 몹시 그리워하고 있다고 장담할 수 있습니다. 그러나 이토록 무더운 날씨에 꽃과 들판 사이에서 지내시는 것을 아까워한다면 그야말로 잔인한 일이겠지요."]], [["Pray believe me, my dear Mrs. Bold, Yours most sincerely, OBADIAH SLOPE", "부디 저를 믿어 주십시오, 친애하는 볼드 부인, 더없이 진실한 마음으로, 오바디아 슬로프"]], [["Barchester, Friday.", "바체스터에서, 금요일."]], [["Now this letter, taken as a whole, and with the consideration that Mr. Slope wished to assume a great degree of intimacy with Eleanor, would not have been bad but for the allusion to the tresses.", "그런데 이 편지는, 전체로 놓고 보고 또 슬로프 씨가 엘리너와 상당한 정도의 친밀함을 자처하고 싶어 했다는 점을 감안하면, 머리채에 대한 언급만 아니었더라면 나쁘지 않았을 것이다."], ["Gentlemen do not write to ladies about their tresses unless they are on very intimate terms indeed.", "신사는 참으로 각별히 가까운 사이가 아니고서는 숙녀에게 그 머리채에 관하여 편지를 쓰지 않는 법이다."], ["But Mr. Slope could not be expected to be aware of this.", "그러나 슬로프 씨가 그것을 알고 있으리라 기대할 수는 없었다."], ["He longed to put a little affection into his epistle, and yet he thought it injudicious, as the letter would, he knew, be shown to Mr. Harding.", "그는 제 서한에 애정을 조금 담고 싶어 못 견뎌 했으나, 그 편지가 하딩 씨에게 보이리라는 것을 알았으므로 그러는 것이 분별없다고 여겼다."], ["He would have insisted that the letter should be strictly private and seen by no eyes but Eleanor's own, had he not felt that such an injunction would have been disobeyed.", "그런 당부가 지켜지지 않으리라고 느끼지만 않았더라면 그는 이 편지를 엄격히 사적인 것으로 하여 엘리너 자신의 눈 외에는 어떤 눈에도 보이지 말라고 못 박았을 것이다."], ["He therefore restrained his passion, did not sign himself \"yours affectionately,\" and contented himself instead with the compliment to the tresses.", "그리하여 그는 정념을 억눌러 “애정을 담아”라고 서명하지 않았고, 그 대신 머리채에 대한 찬사로 만족했다."]], [["Having finished his letter, he took it to Mrs. Bold's house and, learning there, from the servant, that things were to be sent out to Plumstead that afternoon, left it, with many injunctions, in her hands.", "편지를 다 쓴 그는 그것을 볼드 부인의 집으로 가져갔고, 그곳에서 하인에게 그날 오후에 플럼스테드로 물건을 보낼 참이라는 말을 듣고는 여러 차례 당부를 곁들여 그 하인의 손에 맡겨 두었다."]], [["We will now follow Mr. Slope so as to complete the day with him and then return to his letter and its momentous fate in the next chapter.", "이제 우리는 슬로프 씨를 따라가 그와 함께 하루를 마무리한 다음, 다음 장에서 그 편지와 그것의 중대한 운명으로 돌아오기로 하겠다."]], [["There is an old song which gives us some very good advice about courting:--", "구애에 관하여 아주 훌륭한 조언을 들려주는 옛 노래가 하나 있다."]], [["It's gude to be off with the auld luve Before ye be on wi' the new.", "새 사랑과 맺어지기 전에 옛 사랑과는 헤어져 두는 것이 좋으니라."]], [["Of the wisdom of this maxim Mr. Slope was ignorant, and accordingly, having written his letter to Mrs.", "이 격언의 지혜를 슬로프 씨는 알지 못했고, 따라서 볼드 부인에게 편지를 쓴 뒤 시뇨라 네로니를 찾아가기에 이르렀다."], ["Bold, he proceeded to call upon the Signora Neroni. Indeed, it was hard to say which was the old love and which the new, Mr.", "실인즉 어느 쪽이 옛 사랑이고 어느 쪽이 새 사랑인지 말하기 어려웠으니, 슬로프 씨가 거의 같은 무렵에 둘 다에게 마음을 빼앗겼기 때문이다."], ["Slope having been smitten with both so nearly at the same time. Perhaps he thought it not amiss to have two strings to his bow.", "어쩌면 그는 활에 시위를 둘 걸어 두는 것이 나쁘지 않다고 여겼는지도 모른다."], ["But two strings to Cupid's bow are always dangerous to him on whose behalf they are to be used.", "그러나 큐피드의 활에 걸린 두 개의 시위는 그것을 쓰려는 자에게 언제나 위험하다."], ["A man should remember that between two stools he may fall to the ground.", "사내는 두 걸상 사이에서 바닥에 주저앉을 수도 있다는 것을 기억해야 한다."]], [["But in sooth Mr. Slope was pursuing Mrs.", "그러나 실인즉 슬로프 씨는 제 안의 나은 본능에 순종하여 볼드 부인을 좇았고, 못한 본능에 순종하여 시뇨라를 좇았다."], ["Bold in obedience to his better instincts, and the signora in obedience to his worser.", "그가 그 미망인을 얻어 아내로 삼았다 한들 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Had he won the widow and worn her, no one could have blamed him. You, O reader, and I, and Eleanor's other friends would have received the story of such a winning with much disgust and disappointment, but we should have been angry with Eleanor, not with Mr.", "독자여, 그대와 나와 엘리너의 다른 벗들은 그런 승리의 이야기를 몹시 역겹고 실망스럽게 받아들였겠지만, 우리가 노여워할 상대는 슬로프 씨가 아니라 엘리너였을 것이다."], ["Slope. Bishop, male and female, dean and chapter and diocesan clergy in full congress could have found nothing to disapprove of in such an alliance.", "남자 주교와 여자 주교, 수석 사제와 참사회와 교구 성직자들이 총회를 열어 앉아 있었다 한들 그런 결합에서 못마땅해할 구석은 하나도 찾아내지 못했을 것이다."], ["Convocation itself, that mysterious and mighty synod, could in no wise have fallen foul of it.", "저 신비롭고 위력 있는 종교 회의인 성직자 회의 그 자체도 결코 그것을 헐뜯지 못했을 것이다."], ["The possession of £1000 a year and a beautiful wife would not at all have hurt the voice of the pulpit charmer, or lessened the grace and piety of the exemplary clergyman.", "연 1000파운드의 소유와 아름다운 아내는 저 강단의 매혹자가 지닌 목소리를 조금도 해치지 않았을 것이며, 그 모범적인 성직자의 품위와 경건함을 덜지도 않았을 것이다."]], [["But not of such a nature were likely to be his dealings with the Signora Neroni.", "그러나 시뇨라 네로니와의 거래는 그런 성질의 것이 될 리 없었다."], ["In the first place he knew that her husband was living, and therefore he could not woo her honestly.", "첫째로 그는 그녀의 남편이 살아 있다는 것을 알았고, 따라서 정직하게 구애할 수가 없었다."], ["Then again she had nothing to recommend her to his honest wooing, had such been possible.", "또한 설령 그것이 가능했다 하더라도 그녀에게는 그의 정직한 구애를 받을 만한 조건이 하나도 없었다."], ["She was not only portionless, but also from misfortune unfitted to be chosen as the wife of any man who wanted a useful mate.", "그녀는 지참금이 없을 뿐 아니라 불운으로 인하여, 쓸모 있는 반려를 원하는 어떤 사내의 아내로도 택할 수 없는 몸이었다."], ["Mr. Slope was aware that she was a helpless, hopeless cripple.", "슬로프 씨는 그녀가 어찌해 볼 도리 없고 가망도 없는 앉은뱅이라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["But Mr. Slope could not help himself.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 어쩔 수가 없었다."], ["He knew that he was wrong in devoting his time to the back drawing-room in Dr. Stanhope's house.", "그는 스탠호프 박사 집의 안쪽 응접실에 제 시간을 바치는 것이 잘못임을 알았다."], ["He knew that what took place there would, if divulged, utterly ruin him with Mrs. Bold.", "거기서 벌어지는 일이 새어 나가면 볼드 부인 앞에서 자신이 완전히 파멸하리라는 것도 알았다."], ["He knew that scandal would soon come upon his heels and spread abroad among the black coats of Barchester some tidings, exaggerated tidings, of the sighs which he poured into the lady's ears.", "추문이 곧 제 뒤꿈치를 따라붙어, 그가 그 부인의 귀에 쏟아부은 한숨에 관한 소식이, 그것도 부풀려진 소식이 바체스터의 검은 옷들 사이에 퍼지리라는 것도 알았다."], ["He knew that he was acting against the recognized principles of his life, against those laws of conduct by which he hoped to achieve much higher success.", "자신이 삶의 공인된 원칙에, 훨씬 더 높은 성공을 이루고자 기대어 온 행동의 법칙에 어긋나게 굴고 있다는 것도 알았다."], ["But, as we have said, he could not help himself.", "그러나 앞서 말한 대로 그는 어쩔 수가 없었다."], ["Passion, for the first time in his life, passion was too strong for him.", "정념이, 생애 처음으로 정념이 그에게는 너무 강했다."]], [["As for the signora, no such plea can be put forward for her, for in truth she cared no more for Mr. Slope than she did for twenty others who had been at her feet before him.", "시뇨라로 말하자면 그런 변명조차 내세울 수 없으니, 실상 그녀는 제 앞에 먼저 무릎 꿇었던 스무 명의 다른 사내들만큼도 슬로프 씨를 아끼지 않았기 때문이다."], ["She willingly, nay greedily, accepted his homage.", "그녀는 기꺼이, 아니 게걸스럽게 그의 경배를 받아들였다."], ["He was the finest fly that Barchester had hitherto afforded to her web, and the signora was a powerful spider that made wondrous webs, and could in no way live without catching flies.", "그는 바체스터가 지금까지 그녀의 거미줄에 대어 준 것 가운데 가장 훌륭한 파리였고, 시뇨라는 놀라운 그물을 짜는 힘센 거미로서 파리를 잡지 않고서는 도무지 살 수가 없었다."], ["Her taste in this respect was abominable, for she had no use for the victims when caught.", "이 점에서 그녀의 취향은 고약했으니, 잡고 나면 그 먹이를 쓸 데가 없었기 때문이다."], ["She could not eat them matrimonially, as young lady flies do whose webs are most frequently of their mothers' weaving.", "어미가 짜 준 그물을 쓰기 마련인 젊은 처녀 파리들이 그러듯 혼인으로 그것을 먹어 치울 수도 없었다."], ["Nor could she devour them by any escapade of a less legitimate description.", "그렇다고 덜 떳떳한 종류의 탈선으로 삼켜 버릴 수도 없었다."], ["Her unfortunate affliction precluded her from all hope of levanting with a lover.", "그 불운한 병약함이 정부와 함께 달아나리라는 희망을 죄다 막아 버렸다."], ["It would be impossible to run away with a lady who required three servants to move her from a sofa.", "소파에서 옮기는 데만 하인이 셋이나 필요한 부인을 데리고 달아나기란 불가능한 일이었다."]], [["The signora was subdued by no passion.", "시뇨라는 어떤 정념에도 굴복하지 않았다."], ["Her time for love was gone.", "그녀의 사랑의 시절은 지나갔다."], ["She had lived out her heart, such heart as she had ever had, in her early years, at an age when Mr. Slope was thinking of the second book of Euclid and his unpaid bill at the buttery hatch.", "그녀는 제 심장을, 애초에 그녀에게 있었던 만큼의 심장을, 어린 시절에 이미 다 살아 버렸으니, 슬로프 씨가 유클리드 제2권과 식당 배급창구에 밀린 외상값을 생각하고 있던 나이의 일이었다."], ["In age the lady was younger than the gentleman, but in feelings, in knowledge of the affairs of love, in intrigue, he was immeasurably her junior.", "나이로 치면 이 부인이 이 신사보다 어렸으나, 감정에서, 사랑의 사정에 대한 지식에서, 술수에서 그는 그녀보다 헤아릴 수 없이 어렸다. 그녀에게는 발치에 어떤 사내를 두는 일이 반드시 필요했다."], ["It was necessary to her to have some man at her feet.", "그것이 그녀 삶의 유일한 상례적 흥분거리였다."], ["It was the one customary excitement of her life.", "그녀는 그것이 주는 권력의 행사를 즐겼다."], ["She delighted in the exercise of power which this gave her; it was now nearly the only food for her ambition; she would boast to her sister that she could make a fool of any man, and the sister, as little imbued with feminine delicacy as herself, good-naturedly thought it but fair that such amusement should be afforded to a poor invalid who was debarred from the ordinary pleasures of life.", "그것은 이제 그녀 야심의 거의 유일한 양식이었다. 그녀는 언니에게 자기는 어떤 사내든 바보로 만들 수 있다고 자랑하곤 했고, 언니 또한 그녀 못지않게 여자다운 섬세함이 부족한 사람이라 삶의 통상적인 즐거움을 빼앗긴 가엾은 병자에게 그만한 소일거리쯤은 마련해 주는 것이 공평하다고 사람 좋게 여겼다."]], [["Mr. Slope was madly in love but hardly knew it.", "슬로프 씨는 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠져 있었으나 그 사실을 거의 알지 못했다."], ["The Signora spitted him, as a boy does a cockchafer on a cork, that she might enjoy the energetic agony of his gyrations.", "시뇨라는 소년이 코르크에 풍뎅이를 꿰듯 그를 꿰어 두고, 그가 몸부림치며 도는 기운찬 고통을 즐길 수 있게 해 두었다."], ["And she knew very well what she was doing.", "그리고 그녀는 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 아주 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Slope having added to his person all such adornments as are possible to a clergyman making a morning visit--such as a clean necktie, clean handkerchief, new gloves, and a soupçon of not unnecessary scent--called about three o'clock at the doctor's door.", "슬로프 씨는 오전 방문에 나서는 성직자에게 가능한 온갖 치장 — 이를테면 깨끗한 넥타이, 깨끗한 손수건, 새 장갑, 그리고 아주 불필요하달 수는 없는 향수 한 soupçon — 을 제 몸에 더한 채 세 시쯤 박사네 문 앞을 찾았다."], ["At about this hour the signora was almost always alone in the back drawing-room.", "이 시각이면 시뇨라는 거의 언제나 안쪽 응접실에 혼자 있었다."], ["The mother had not come down.", "어머니는 아직 내려오지 않았다."], ["The doctor was out or in his own room.", "박사는 밖에 나가 있거나 제 방에 있었다."], ["Bertie was out, and Charlotte at any rate left the room if anyone called whose object was specially with her sister.", "버티는 밖에 있었고, 샬럿은 어쨌든 용건이 특별히 제 동생에게 있는 손님이 찾아오면 방을 비켜 주었다."], ["Such was her idea of being charitable and sisterly.", "그것이 그녀가 생각하는 자비이자 자매의 도리였다."]], [["Mr. Slope, as was his custom, asked for Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 늘 하던 대로 스탠호프 씨를 찾았고, 하인은 늘 하던 대로 시뇨라가 응접실에 계시다고 일러 주었다."], ["Stanhope, and was told, as was the servant's custom, that the signora was in the drawing-room. Upstairs he accordingly went.", "그리하여 그는 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["He found her, as he always did, lying on her sofa with a French volume before her and a beautiful little inlaid writing-case open on her table.", "그는 언제나처럼 그녀가 앞에 프랑스어 책을 펴 놓고 탁자에는 상감을 놓은 아름답고 작은 문갑을 열어 둔 채 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["At the moment of his entrance she was in the act of writing.", "그가 들어서던 순간 그녀는 마침 무언가를 쓰고 있는 참이었다."]], [["\"Ah, my friend,\"", "“아, 나의 벗이여.”"], ["said she, putting out her left hand to him across her desk, \"I did not expect you to-day and was this very instant writing to you--\"", "그녀가 문갑 너머로 왼손을 내밀며 말했다. “오늘은 오시리라 생각지 않았는데, 바로 이 순간에 당신께 편지를 쓰고 있었답니다—”"]], [["Mr. Slope, taking the soft, fair, delicate hand in his--and very soft and fair and delicate it was--bowed over it his huge red head and kissed it.", "슬로프 씨는 그 부드럽고 희고 섬세한 손을 제 손에 잡고 — 참으로 부드럽고 희고 섬세한 손이었다 — 그 위로 커다랗고 붉은 머리를 숙여 입을 맞추었다."], ["It was a sight to see, a deed to record if the author could fitly do it, a picture to put on canvas. Mr.", "그것은 볼 만한 광경이었고, 지은이가 제대로 해낼 수만 있다면 기록해 둘 만한 일이었으며, 화폭에 옮겨 둘 만한 그림이었다."], ["Slope was big, awkward, cumbrous, and, having his heart in his pursuit, was ill at ease.", "슬로프 씨는 덩치가 크고 어설프고 굼떴으며, 마음이 온통 그 추격에 가 있는 터라 편치가 않았다. 앞서 말한 대로 그 부인은 희고 섬세했다."], ["The lady was fair, as we have said, and delicate; everything about her was fine and refined; her hand in his looked like a rose lying among carrots, and when he kissed it, he looked as a cow might do on finding such a flower among her food.", "그녀에게 딸린 모든 것이 곱고 세련되었다. 그의 손안에 놓인 그녀의 손은 당근 사이에 놓인 장미 한 송이 같았고, 그가 거기에 입을 맞출 때 그는 제 먹이 사이에서 그런 꽃을 발견한 암소 같은 꼴이었다."], ["She was graceful as a couchant goddess and, moreover, as self-possessed as Venus must have been when courting Adonis.", "그녀는 엎드린 여신처럼 우아했고, 게다가 아도니스에게 구애하던 때의 비너스가 그러했을 만큼 침착했다."]], [["Oh, that such grace and such beauty should have condescended to waste itself on such a pursuit!", "아, 그만한 우아함과 그만한 아름다움이 몸을 낮추어 그런 추격에 스스로를 허비하다니!"]], [["\"I was in the act of writing to you,\"", "“당신께 편지를 쓰고 있던 참이었어요.”"], ["said she, \"but now my scrawl may go into the basket;\"", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 이제 이 끄적거림은 휴지통으로 가도 되겠군요.”"], ["and she raised the sheet of gilded note-paper from off her desk as though to tear it.", "그러고는 찢기라도 할 듯이 문갑에서 금테 두른 편지지를 집어 들었다."]], [["\"Indeed it shall not,\"", "“절대로 그리 되지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["said he, laying the embargo of half a stone weight of human flesh and blood upon the devoted paper.", "그가 말하며, 그 헌신적인 종이 위에 반 스톤 무게의 살과 피를 얹어 압류를 걸었다."], ["\"Nothing that you write for my eyes, signora, shall be so desecrated,\"", "“시뇨라, 당신이 제 눈을 위하여 쓴 것이라면 무엇도 그렇게 모독당해서는 안 됩니다.”"], ["and he took up the letter, put that also among the carrots and fed on it, and then proceeded to read it.", "그러고는 그 편지를 집어 들어 그것 또한 당근 사이에 놓고 뜯어 먹더니, 이어서 그것을 읽어 내려가기 시작했다."]], [["\"Gracious me!", "“어머나, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Mr. Slope,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"I hope you don't mean to say you keep all the trash I write to you.", "“설마 제가 당신께 써 보낸 잡동사니를 죄다 간직하고 계신다는 말씀은 아니겠지요."], ["Half my time I don't know what I write, and when I do, I know it is only fit for the back of the fire.", "저는 절반쯤은 제가 무엇을 쓰는지도 모르고, 알 때에도 그것이 아궁이 뒤편에나 어울린다는 것을 압니다."], ["I hope you have not that ugly trick of keeping letters.\"", "편지를 모아 두는 그 흉한 버릇만은 없으시기를 바라요.”"]], [["\"At any rate, I don't throw them into a waste-paper basket.", "“적어도 저는 그것들을 휴지통에 던지지는 않습니다."], ["If destruction is their doomed lot, they perish worthily, and are burnt on a pyre, as Dido was of old.\"", "스러지는 것이 그것들에 정해진 운명이라면, 그것들은 합당하게 스러지지요. 옛날의 디도가 그러했듯 장작더미 위에서 불태워집니다.”"]], [["\"With a steel pen stuck through them, of course,\"", "“물론 강철 펜을 하나 꽂아서 말이지요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"to make the simile more complete.", "“그래야 비유가 더 완전해질 테니까요."], ["Of all the ladies of my acquaintance I think Lady Dido was the most absurd.", "제가 아는 부인네들 가운데 디도 여사만큼 어처구니없는 이는 없다고 봅니다."], ["Why did she not do as Cleopatra did?", "어째서 클레오파트라처럼 하지 않았을까요?"], ["Why did she not take out her ships and insist on going with him?", "어째서 제 배들을 끌어내어 그와 함께 가겠다고 우기지 않았을까요?"], ["She could not bear to lose the land she had got by a swindle, and then she could not bear the loss of her lover.", "사기로 얻은 땅을 잃는 것도 견디지 못했고, 그러면서 연인을 잃는 것도 견디지 못했지요."], ["So she fell between two stools. Mr.", "그래서 두 걸상 사이로 떨어져 버렸어요."], ["Slope, whatever you do, never mingle love and business.\"", "슬로프 씨, 무슨 일을 하시든 사랑과 사업만은 결코 섞지 마세요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope blushed up to his eyes and over his mottled forehead to the very roots of his hair.", "슬로프 씨는 눈까지, 얼룩덜룩한 이마를 지나 머리카락 뿌리께까지 붉어졌다."], ["He felt sure that the signora knew all about his intentions with reference to Mrs Bold.", "그는 시뇨라가 볼드 부인에 관한 제 속셈을 모조리 알고 있다고 확신했다."], ["His conscience told him that he was detected.", "양심이 그에게 들켰다고 일러 주었다."], ["His doom was to be spoken; he was to be punished for his duplicity, and rejected by the beautiful creature before him.", "이제 그의 선고가 내려질 참이었다. 그는 제 이중성 때문에 벌을 받고, 눈앞의 저 아름다운 피조물에게 거절당할 참이었다."], ["Poor man. He little dreamt that had all his intentions with reference to Mrs.", "가엾은 사내."], ["Bold been known to the signora, it would only have added zest to that lady's amusement. It was all very well to have Mr.", "그는 볼드 부인에 관한 제 속셈이 죄다 시뇨라에게 알려졌다 한들 그것은 그 부인의 소일거리에 흥취를 더해 줄 뿐이라는 것을 꿈에도 몰랐다."], ["Slope at her feet, to show her power by making an utter fool of a clergyman, to gratify her own infidelity by thus proving the little strength which religion had in controlling the passions even of a religious man; but it would be an increased gratification if she could be made to understand that she was at the same time alluring her victim away from another, whose love if secured would be in every way beneficent and salutary.", "슬로프 씨를 발치에 두는 것, 성직자 하나를 완전한 바보로 만들어 제 힘을 보이는 것, 신앙심 깊은 사내조차 그 정념을 다스리는 데 종교의 힘이 얼마나 보잘것없는지 그렇게 증명해 제 불신을 흡족하게 하는 것도 좋기는 했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 자기가 이 제물을 다른 여자에게서 꾀어내고 있으며, 그 다른 여자의 사랑이야말로 얻기만 하면 모든 면에서 이롭고 유익하리라는 것을 스스로 알 수 있게 된다면 그 흡족함은 한층 커질 터였다."]], [["The Signora had indeed discovered, with the keen instinct of such a woman, that Mr. Slope was bent on matrimony with Mrs.", "시뇨라는 그런 여자 특유의 예리한 직감으로 슬로프 씨가 볼드 부인과의 혼인에 마음을 두고 있음을 진작 알아차렸으나, 디도를 들먹일 때에는 그것을 염두에 두지 않았다."], ["Bold, but in alluding to Dido she had not thought of it. She instantly perceived, however, from her lover's blushes, what was on his mind and was not slow in taking advantage of it.", "그러나 제 연인의 홍조를 보고 그의 마음에 무엇이 걸려 있는지 곧바로 알아차렸고, 그것을 이용하는 데 굼뜨지 않았다."]], [["She looked him full in the face, not angrily, nor yet with a smile, but with an intense and overpowering gaze; then, holding up her forefinger and slightly shaking her head, she said:--", "그녀는 그의 얼굴을 정면으로, 성난 기색도 없고 그렇다고 미소도 없이, 그러나 강렬하고 압도적인 눈길로 바라보았다. 그런 다음 집게손가락을 들어 올리고 고개를 가볍게 저으며 말했다."]], [["\"Whatever you do, my friend, do not mingle love and business.", "“무슨 일을 하시든, 나의 벗이여, 사랑과 사업을 섞지 마세요."], ["Either stick to your treasure and your city of wealth, or else follow your love like a true man.", "당신의 보물과 당신의 부유한 도시에 붙어 있든지, 아니면 참된 사내답게 당신의 사랑을 좇든지 하세요."], ["But never attempt both.", "그러나 둘 다를 노리려 들지는 마세요."], ["If you do, you'll have to die with a broken heart as did poor Dido.", "그러셨다가는 가엾은 디도가 그러했듯 가슴이 찢어진 채 죽으셔야 할 겁니다."], ["Which is it to be with you, Mr. Slope, love or money?\"", "당신은 어느 쪽인가요, 슬로프 씨, 사랑인가요 돈인가요?”"]], [["Mr. Slope was not so ready with a pathetic answer as he usually was with touching episodes in his extempore sermons.", "슬로프 씨는 즉흥 설교에서 사람의 심금을 울리는 대목을 다룰 때처럼 애처로운 대답을 척척 내놓지 못했다."], ["He felt that he ought to say something pretty, something also that should remove the impression on the mind of his lady-love.", "그는 무언가 그럴듯한 말을, 그러면서 제 연인의 마음에 새겨진 인상을 지워 줄 말을 해야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["But he was rather put about how to do it.", "그러나 그것을 어떻게 해내야 할지 몰라 다소 쩔쩔맸다."]], [["\"Love,\"", "“사랑은,”"], ["said he, \"true overpowering love, must be the strongest passion a man can feel; it must control every other wish, and put aside every other pursuit.", "그가 말했다. “참되고 압도적인 사랑은 사내가 느낄 수 있는 가장 강한 정념일 수밖에 없습니다."], ["But with me love will never act in that way unless it be returned;\"", "그것은 다른 모든 소망을 다스리고 다른 모든 추구를 물리치게 마련이지요."], ["and he threw upon the signora a look of tenderness which was intended to make up for all the deficiencies of his speech.", "그러나 제게 사랑은 되돌아오지 않는 한 결코 그렇게 작동하지 않을 겁니다.” 그러고는 제 말의 부족함을 죄다 메워 보려는 뜻으로 시뇨라에게 다정한 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["\"Take my advice,\" said she.", "“제 충고를 받아들이세요.”"], ["\"Never mind love.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["After all, what is it?", "“사랑 따위는 마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["The dream of a few weeks.", "결국 그게 무엇인가요?"], ["That is all its joy.", "몇 주짜리 꿈이지요."], ["The disappointment of a life is its Nemesis.", "그것이 주는 기쁨의 전부예요. 한평생의 실망이 그 응보고요."], ["Who was ever successful in true love?", "참된 사랑에 성공한 이가 누가 있던가요?"], ["Success in love argues that the love is false.", "사랑에서의 성공은 그 사랑이 거짓이라는 뜻이에요."], ["True love is always despondent or tragical.", "참된 사랑은 언제나 낙담이거나 비극이지요."], ["Juliet loved, Haidee loved, Dido loved, and what came of it?", "줄리엣도 사랑했고, 하이디도 사랑했고, 디도도 사랑했는데, 그래서 어떻게 되었나요?"], ["Troilus loved and ceased to be a man.\"", "트로일로스는 사랑하다가 사내이기를 그쳤고요.”"]], [["\"Troilus loved and was fooled,\"", "“트로일로스는 사랑하다가 우롱당한 것이지요.”"], ["said the more manly chaplain.", "조금 더 사내다워진 전속 사제가 말했다."], ["\"A man may love and yet not be a Troilus.", "“사내가 사랑을 하고도 트로일로스가 되지 않을 수 있습니다."], ["All women are not Cressidas.\"", "모든 여자가 크레시다는 아니니까요.”"]], [["\"No, all women are not Cressidas.", "“그래요, 모든 여자가 크레시다는 아니지요."], ["The falsehood is not always on the woman's side.", "거짓이 언제나 여자 쪽에 있는 것도 아니고요."], ["Imogen was true, but how was she rewarded?", "이모진은 진실했는데, 그 보답이 무엇이었나요?"], ["Her lord believed her to be the paramour of the first he who came near her in his absence.", "그 지아비는 자기가 없는 동안 아내 곁에 처음 다가온 사내의 정부가 되었다고 믿었지요."], ["Desdemona was true and was smothered.", "데스데모나는 진실했다가 목이 졸렸어요."], ["Ophelia was true and went mad.", "오필리아는 진실했다가 미쳐 버렸고요."], ["There is no happiness in love, except at the end of an English novel.", "영국 소설의 끝머리를 빼면 사랑에는 행복이란 것이 없습니다."], ["But in wealth, money, houses, lands, goods, and chattels, in the good things of this world, yes, in them there is something tangible, something that can be retained and enjoyed.\"", "그러나 부와 돈과 집과 땅과 세간과 살림, 이 세상의 좋은 것들 안에는, 그래요, 그 안에는 무언가 손에 잡히는 것이, 붙들어 두고 누릴 수 있는 무언가가 있지요.”"]], [["\"Oh, no,\" said Mr.", "“아, 아닙니다.”"], ["Slope, feeling himself bound to enter some protest against so very unorthodox a doctrine, \"this world's wealth will make no one happy.\"", "슬로프 씨가 그토록 정통에서 벗어난 교리에 무언가 항의는 해야 한다고 느끼며 말했다. “이 세상의 부는 누구도 행복하게 만들지 못합니다.”"]], [["\"And what will make you happy--you--you?\"", "“그러면 무엇이 당신을, 당신을, 당신을 행복하게 만드나요?”"], ["said she, raising herself up and speaking to him with energy across the table.", "그녀가 몸을 일으켜 탁자 너머로 힘주어 말했다."], ["\"From what source do you look for happiness?", "“당신은 어느 샘에서 행복을 찾으시나요?"], ["Do not say that you look for none.", "아무 데서도 찾지 않는다고는 말하지 마세요."], ["I shall not believe you.", "저는 믿지 않을 테니까요."], ["It is a search in which every human being spends an existence.\"", "그것은 사람이라면 누구나 한평생을 들여 벌이는 수색이지요.”"]], [["\"And the search is always in vain,\"", "“그리고 그 수색은 언제나 헛되지요.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"We look for happiness on earth, while we ought to be content to hope for it in heaven.\"", "“우리는 하늘에서 그것을 바라는 것으로 만족해야 마땅한데도 땅에서 행복을 찾습니다.”"]], [["\"Pshaw!", "“쳇!"], ["You preach a doctrine which you know you don't believe.", "당신은 스스로 믿지도 않는 교리를 설교하시는군요."], ["It is the way with you all.", "당신들은 다 그래요."], ["If you know that there is no earthly happiness, why do you long to be a bishop or a dean?", "지상의 행복이란 없다는 것을 아신다면, 어째서 주교나 수석 사제가 되기를 갈망하시나요?"], ["Why do you want lands and income?\"", "어째서 땅과 수입을 원하시나요?”"]], [["\"I have the natural ambition of a man,\"", "“제게는 사내로서 타고난 야심이 있습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Of course you have, and the natural passions; and therefore I say that you don't believe the doctrine you preach. St.", "“물론 있으시겠지요."], ["Paul was an enthusiast.", "타고난 정념도 있고요."], ["He believed so that his ambition and passions did not war against his creed.", "그러니까 저는 당신이 스스로 설교하시는 교리를 믿지 않는다고 말하는 거예요."], ["So does the Eastern fanatic who passes half his life erect upon a pillar.", "성 바울은 열광하는 사람이었어요. 그이는 제 야심과 정념이 신조와 다투지 않을 만큼 믿었지요."], ["As for me, I will believe in no belief that does not make itself manifest by outward signs.", "한평생의 절반을 기둥 위에 곧추서서 보내는 저 동방의 광신자도 그렇고요. 저로 말하자면, 겉으로 드러난 표징으로 스스로를 나타내지 않는 믿음은 어떤 것도 믿지 않겠어요."], ["I will think no preaching sincere that is not recommended by the practice of the preacher.\"", "설교자 자신의 실행으로 뒷받침되지 않는 설교는 어떤 것도 진실하다고 여기지 않겠고요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope was startled and horrified, but he felt that he could not answer.", "슬로프 씨는 놀라고 소름이 끼쳤으나, 대답할 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["How could he stand up and preach the lessons of his Master, being there, as he was, on the devil's business?", "지금 제가 그러하듯 악마의 심부름을 하고 있는 처지에 어찌 일어서서 제 주인의 가르침을 설교할 수 있겠는가?"], ["He was a true believer, otherwise this would have been nothing to him.", "그는 참된 신자였고, 그렇지 않았더라면 이런 것쯤은 그에게 아무것도 아니었을 것이다."], ["He had audacity for most things, but he had not audacity to make a plaything of the Lord's word.", "그는 웬만한 일에는 뻔뻔했으나 주님의 말씀을 장난감 삼을 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않았다."], ["All this the signora understood, and felt much interest as she saw her cockchafer whirl round upon her pin.", "이 모든 것을 시뇨라는 알고 있었고, 제 풍뎅이가 핀 위에서 빙글빙글 도는 것을 보며 자못 흥미를 느꼈다."]], [["\"Your wit delights in such arguments,\"", "“당신의 재치는 그런 논변을 즐기지요.”"], ["said he, \"but your heart and your reason do not go along with them.\"", "그가 말했다. “그러나 당신의 심장과 당신의 이성은 그 논변과 함께 가지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"My heart!\"", "“제 심장이라뇨!”"], ["said she; \"you quite mistake the principles of my composition if you imagine that there is such a thing about me.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “제게 그런 것이 있다고 여기신다면 제 구성 원리를 아주 잘못 아신 겁니다.”"], ["After all, there was very little that was false in anything that the signora said.", "실인즉 시뇨라가 하는 말 가운데 거짓인 것은 별로 없었다."], ["If Mr. Slope allowed himself to be deceived, it was his own fault.", "슬로프 씨가 스스로 속기를 허락했다면 그것은 그 자신의 잘못이었다."], ["Nothing could have been more open than her declarations about herself.", "제 자신에 관한 그녀의 언명만큼 숨김없는 것도 없었다."]], [["The little writing-table with her desk was still standing before her, a barrier, as it were, against the enemy.", "문갑을 얹은 작은 필기용 탁자가 여전히 그녀 앞에 서서, 말하자면 적을 막는 방벽 노릇을 하고 있었다."], ["She was sitting as nearly upright as she ever did, and he had brought a chair close to the sofa, so that there was only the corner of the table between him and her.", "그녀는 평소 그녀가 앉는 만큼은 거의 똑바로 앉아 있었고, 그는 의자를 소파 가까이 끌어다 놓은 터라 그와 그녀 사이에는 탁자 모서리밖에 없었다."], ["It so happened that as she spoke her hand lay upon the table, and as Mr. Slope answered her he put his hand upon hers.", "마침 그녀가 말할 때 그녀의 손이 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 슬로프 씨는 대답하면서 제 손을 그 위에 얹었다."]], [["\"No heart!\" said he.", "“심장이 없다니요!”"], ["\"That is a heavy charge which you bring against yourself, and one of which I cannot find you guilty--\"", "그가 말했다. “그것은 당신이 스스로에게 씌우는 무거운 죄목이며, 저로서는 당신에게 유죄를 선고할 수 없는 죄목입니다—”"]], [["She withdrew her hand, not quickly and angrily, as though insulted by his touch, but gently and slowly.", "그녀는 손을 거두었다. 그의 손길에 모욕이라도 당한 듯 재빠르고 성나게가 아니라, 부드럽고 천천히."]], [["\"You are in no condition to give a verdict on the matter,\"", "“당신은 그 문제에 판결을 내리실 처지가 아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"as you have not tried me.", "“저를 시험해 보신 적이 없으니까요."], ["No, don't say that you intend doing so, for you know you have no intention of the kind; nor indeed have I, either.", "아뇨, 그러실 작정이라고는 말하지 마세요. 당신께 그럴 뜻이 없다는 것을 당신도 아시니까요."], ["As for you, you will take your vows where they will result in something more substantial than the pursuit of such a ghostlike, ghastly love as mine--\"", "실은 제게도 그럴 뜻이 없고요. 당신으로 말하자면, 당신은 저의 이 유령 같고 소름 끼치는 사랑을 좇는 것보다는 좀 더 실속 있는 결과가 나올 곳에 서약을 바치실 테지요—”"]], [["\"Your love should be sufficient to satisfy the dream of a monarch,\"", "“당신의 사랑이라면 군주의 꿈을 채우고도 남을 겁니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope, not quite clear as to the meaning of his words.", "슬로프 씨가 제 말뜻을 그리 분명히 알지도 못한 채 말했다."]], [["\"Say an archbishop, Mr. Slope,\"", "“대주교라고 하시지요, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사내."], ["She was very cruel to him.", "그녀는 그에게 몹시 잔인했다."], ["He went round again upon his cork on this allusion to his profession.", "제 직업을 두고 하는 이 말에 그는 다시 코르크 위에서 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["He tried, however, to smile and gently accused her of joking on a matter, which was, he said, to him of such vital moment.", "그러나 그는 미소를 지어 보이려 애쓰면서, 자기에게는 그토록 사활이 걸린 문제를 두고 농을 한다며 그녀를 부드럽게 나무랐다."]], [["\"Why--what gulls do you men make of us,\"", "“어쩌면 그렇게, 사내들은 우리를 얼간이로 만드시는지.”"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["\"How you fool us to the top of our bent; and of all men you clergymen are the most fluent of your honeyed, caressing words.", "“어쩌면 그렇게 우리를 마음껏 놀리시는지. 게다가 사내들 가운데서도 성직자들이 그 달콤하고 어르는 말솜씨가 가장 청산유수지요."], ["Now look me in the face, Mr. Slope, boldly and openly.\"", "자, 제 얼굴을 보세요, 슬로프 씨, 담대하고 숨김없이.”"]], [["Mr. Slope did look at her with a languishing loving eye, and as he did so he again put forth his hand to get hold of hers.", "슬로프 씨는 과연 애타는 사랑의 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 그러면서 다시 손을 뻗어 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다."]], [["\"I told you to look at me boldly, Mr.", "“담대하게 저를 보시라고 했지요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope, but confine your boldness to your eyes.\"", "그러나 그 담대함은 눈에만 두시라고 했어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, Madeline!\"", "“오, 마들린!”"], ["he sighed.", "그가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["\"Well, my name is Madeline,\"", "“그래요, 제 이름은 마들린이지요.”"], ["said she, \"but none except my own family usually call me so.", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 제 식구 말고는 보통 저를 그렇게 부르지 않아요."], ["Now look me in the face, Mr. Slope.", "자, 제 얼굴을 보세요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Am I to understand that you say you love me?\"", "저를 사랑한다고 말씀하시는 것으로 알아들어도 되나요?”"]], [["Mr. Slope never had said so.", "슬로프 씨는 그렇게 말한 적이 결코 없었다."], ["If he had come there with any formed plan at all, his intention was to make love to the lady without uttering any such declaration.", "그가 무슨 짜인 계획이라도 품고 그 자리에 왔다면, 그 속셈은 그런 선언은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않은 채 그 부인에게 사랑을 건네자는 것이었다."], ["It was, however, quite impossible that he should now deny his love.", "그러나 이제 와서 제 사랑을 부인하기란 도무지 불가능한 일이었다."], ["He had, therefore, nothing for it but to go down on his knees distractedly against the sofa and swear that he did love her with a love passing the love of man.", "그리하여 그에게는 정신없이 소파에 기대어 무릎을 꿇고, 사람의 사랑을 뛰어넘는 사랑으로 그녀를 사랑한다고 맹세하는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다."]], [["The signora received the assurance with very little palpitation or appearance of surprise.", "시뇨라는 그 다짐을 가슴 두근거림도 놀란 기색도 거의 없이 받아들였다."], ["\"And now answer me another question,\"", "“이제 또 하나만 대답해 주세요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"When are you to be married to my dear friend Eleanor Bold?\"", "“제 소중한 벗 엘리너 볼드와는 언제 혼인하시나요?”"]], [["Poor Mr. Slope went round and round in mortal agony.", "가엾은 슬로프 씨는 죽을 것 같은 고뇌 속에서 빙글빙글 돌았다."], ["In such a condition as his it was really very hard for him to know what answer to give.", "그런 처지에서 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 알기란 정말이지 몹시 어려운 일이었다."], ["And yet no answer would be his surest condemnation.", "그렇다고 아무 대답도 하지 않는다면 그것이야말로 가장 확실한 유죄 판결이 될 터였다."], ["He might as well at once plead guilty to the charge brought against him.", "그럴 바에는 차라리 제게 씌워진 죄목을 곧바로 인정하는 편이 나았다."]], [["\"And why do you accuse me of such dissimulation?\"", "“어찌하여 저를 그런 표리부동으로 몰아세우십니까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Dissimulation!", "“표리부동이라뇨!"], ["I said nothing of dissimulation.", "저는 표리부동이라는 말은 한 적이 없어요."], ["I made no charge against you, and make none.", "당신께 아무 죄목도 씌운 적이 없고, 지금도 씌우지 않습니다."], ["Pray don't defend yourself to me.", "제 앞에서 변명하려 들지 마세요."], ["You swear that you are devoted to my beauty, and yet you are on the eve of matrimony with another.", "당신은 제 아름다움에 헌신하겠다고 맹세하시면서 또 다른 이와 혼인을 코앞에 두고 계시지요."], ["I feel this to be rather a compliment.", "저는 이것을 오히려 찬사로 여겨요."], ["It is to Mrs. Bold that you must defend yourself.", "변명하셔야 할 상대는 볼드 부인이지요."], ["That you may find difficult; unless, indeed, you can keep her in the dark.", "그것이 어려우실 겁니다. 물론 그이를 어둠 속에 붙들어 둘 수 있다면 모르지만요."], ["You clergymen are cleverer than other men.\"", "성직자들은 다른 사내들보다 영리하니까요.”"]], [["\"Signora, I have told you that I loved you, and now you rail at me.\"", "“시뇨라, 저는 당신을 사랑한다고 말씀드렸는데, 이제 당신은 저를 놀리시는군요.”"]], [["\"Rail at you.", "“놀린다고요."], ["God bless the man; what would he have?", "하느님이 이 사내를 굽어살피시기를. 대체 무엇을 바라시는 걸까요?"], ["Come, answer me this at your leisure--not without thinking now, but leisurely and with consideration--are you not going to be married to Mrs.", "자, 이건 천천히 대답해 보세요. 지금 당장 생각 없이 말고, 느긋하게 곰곰이 헤아려서요."], ["Bold?\"", "당신은 볼드 부인과 혼인하실 것이 아닌가요?”"]], [["\"I am not,\" said he.", "“아닙니다.”"], ["And as he said it he almost hated, with an exquisite hatred, the woman whom he could not help loving with an exquisite love.", "그가 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그는, 절묘한 사랑으로 사랑하지 않을 수 없는 그 여자를 절묘한 증오로 거의 미워했다."]], [["\"But surely you are a worshipper of hers?\"", "“하지만 분명 그이를 흠모하고 계시는 것이 아닌가요?”"]], [["\"I am not,\" said Mr.", "“아닙니다.”"], ["Slope, to whom the word worshipper was peculiarly distasteful.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. 흠모라는 말이 그에게는 유난히 역겨웠다."], ["The signora had conceived that it would be so.", "시뇨라는 그러리라 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["\"I wonder at that,\"", "“그것 참 이상하군요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Do you not admire her?", "“그이를 우러러보지 않으신다고요?"], ["To my eye she is the perfection of English beauty.", "제 눈에 그이는 영국식 아름다움의 완성이에요."], ["And then she is rich, too.", "게다가 부유하기까지 하고요."], ["I should have thought she was just the person to attract you.", "저라면 그이야말로 당신을 끌어당길 만한 사람이라고 생각했을 텐데요."], ["Come, Mr. Slope, let me give you advice on this matter.", "자, 슬로프 씨, 이 문제에 관해 제가 충고를 하나 드리지요."], ["Marry the charming widow; she will be a good mother to your children and an excellent mistress of a clergyman's household.\"", "그 매력적인 미망인과 혼인하세요. 그이는 당신 아이들에게 좋은 어머니가 될 것이고, 성직자 집안의 훌륭한 안주인이 될 거예요.”"]], [["\"Oh, signora, how can you be so cruel?\"", "“오, 시뇨라, 어찌 그리 잔인하실 수가 있습니까?”"]], [["\"Cruel,\"", "“잔인하다고요.”"], ["said she, changing the voice of banter which she had been using for one which was expressively earnest in its tone; \"is that cruelty?\"", "그녀가 그때까지 쓰던 희롱조의 목소리를 바꾸어, 어조에 진심이 뚜렷이 배어나는 목소리로 말했다. “그게 잔인함인가요?”"]], [["\"How can I love another while my heart is entirely your own?\"", "“제 심장이 온전히 당신의 것인데 제가 어찌 다른 이를 사랑할 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["\"If that were cruelty, Mr. Slope, what might you say of me if I were to declare that I returned your passion?", "“그것이 잔인함이라면, 슬로프 씨, 제가 당신의 정념에 화답한다고 선언한다면 당신은 저를 무어라 하시겠어요?"], ["What would you think if I bound you even by a lover's oath to do daily penance at this couch of mine?", "제가 연인의 서약으로 당신을 묶어 이 소파 앞에서 날마다 고행을 하게 만든다면 어찌 생각하시겠어요?"], ["What can I give in return for a man's love?", "사내의 사랑에 제가 무엇을 돌려드릴 수 있나요?"], ["Ah, dear friend, you have not realized the conditions of my fate.\"", "아, 소중한 벗이여, 당신은 제 운명의 조건을 헤아리지 못하셨어요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope was not on his knees all this time.", "슬로프 씨는 그동안 내내 무릎을 꿇고 있지는 않았다."], ["After his declaration of love, he had risen from them as quickly as he thought consistent with the new position which he now filled, and as he stood was leaning on the back of his chair.", "사랑을 선언한 뒤 그는 이제 제가 차지한 새 지위에 어울린다고 여겨지는 만큼 재빨리 무릎에서 일어섰고, 선 채로 제 의자 등받이에 기대고 있었다."], ["This outburst of tenderness on the signora's part quite overcame him and made him feel for the moment that he could sacrifice everything to be assured of the love of the beautiful creature before him, maimed, lame, and already married as she was.", "시뇨라 쪽에서 터져 나온 이 다정함은 그를 완전히 압도하여, 불구이고 절름거리며 이미 혼인한 몸인 저 눈앞의 아름다운 피조물의 사랑을 확실히 얻을 수만 있다면 무엇이든 희생할 수 있겠다고 한순간 느끼게 만들었다."]], [["\"And can I not sympathize with your lot?\"", "“그러면 제가 당신의 처지에 함께 마음 아파할 수도 없단 말입니까?”"], ["said he, now seating himself on her sofa and pushing away the table with his foot.", "그가 이제 그녀의 소파에 걸터앉아 발로 탁자를 밀어내며 말했다."]], [["\"Sympathy is so near to pity!\"", "“동정은 연민과 그리도 가깝지요!”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"If you pity me, cripple as I am, I shall spurn you from me.\"", "“이런 앉은뱅이인 저를 가엾이 여기신다면, 저는 당신을 제게서 걷어차 버리겠어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, Madeline, I will only love you,\"", "“오, 마들린, 저는 오직 당신을 사랑할 뿐입니다.”"], ["and again he caught her hand and devoured it with kisses.", "그러고는 그가 다시 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 입맞춤으로 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Now she did not draw it from him, but sat there as he kissed it, looking at him with her great eyes, just as a great spider would look at a great fly that was quite securely caught.", "이번에 그녀는 손을 빼지 않고 그가 입 맞추는 동안 그대로 앉아, 커다란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 꼭 커다란 거미가 아주 단단히 걸려든 커다란 파리를 바라보듯이."]], [["\"Suppose Signor Neroni were to come to Barchester,\"", "“시뇨르 네로니가 바체스터에 온다고 해 보세요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Would you make his acquaintance?\"", "“그이와 알고 지내시겠어요?”"]], [["\"Signor Neroni!\"", "“시뇨르 네로니라니요!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Would you introduce him to the bishop, and Mrs. Proudie, and the young ladies?\"", "“그이를 주교와 프라우디 부인과 그 따님들께 소개해 주시겠어요?”"], ["said she, again having recourse to that horrid quizzing voice which Mr. Slope so particularly hated.", "그녀가 슬로프 씨가 유난히 싫어하는 그 끔찍한 놀림조의 목소리를 또다시 꺼내 들며 말했다."]], [["\"Why do you ask such a question?\"", "“어찌하여 그런 것을 물으십니까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Because it is necessary that you should know that there is a Signor Neroni.", "“시뇨르 네로니라는 사람이 있다는 것을 당신이 아셔야 하기 때문이지요."], ["I think you had forgotten it.\"", "잊고 계셨던 듯해서요.”"]], [["\"If I thought that you retained for that wretch one particle of the love of which he was never worthy, I would die before I would distract you by telling you what I feel.", "“당신이 그 가련한 자를 위하여, 그자가 결코 받을 자격이 없던 그 사랑을 한 조각이라도 간직하고 계신다고 생각했다면, 저는 제가 느끼는 바를 말씀드려 당신을 어지럽히느니 차라리 죽었을 겁니다. 아닙니다!"], ["No! Were your husband the master of your heart, I might perhaps love you, but you should never know it.\"", "당신의 남편이 당신 심장의 주인이라면 저는 어쩌면 당신을 사랑했을지언정 당신은 결코 그것을 알지 못했을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"My heart again! How you talk.", "“또 제 심장이군요!"], ["And you consider then that if a husband be not master of his wife's heart, he has no right to her fealty; if a wife ceases to love, she may cease to be true.", "어쩌면 그런 말씀을 하시는지. 그러면 당신은 남편이 아내 심장의 주인이 아니라면 그 아내의 정절을 요구할 권리가 없다고, 아내가 사랑하기를 그친다면 진실하기를 그쳐도 된다고 여기시는군요."], ["Is that your doctrine on this matter, as a minister of the Church of England?\"", "그것이 잉글랜드 교회의 성직자로서 이 문제에 관한 당신의 교리인가요?”"]], [["Mr. Slope tried hard within himself to cast off the pollution with which he felt that he was defiling his soul.", "슬로프 씨는 제 영혼을 더럽히고 있다고 느껴지는 그 오염을 떨쳐 내려 속으로 몹시 애를 썼다."], ["He strove to tear himself away from the noxious siren that had bewitched him.", "그는 자기를 홀린 그 해로운 요부에게서 스스로를 뜯어내려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["But he could not do it.", "그러나 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["He could not be again heart free.", "다시 심장이 자유로워질 수가 없었다."], ["He had looked for rapturous joy in loving this lovely creature, and he already found that he met with little but disappointment and self-rebuke.", "그는 이 사랑스러운 피조물을 사랑하는 데서 황홀한 기쁨을 기대했는데, 실망과 자책 말고는 얻는 것이 거의 없음을 이미 알아 가고 있었다."], ["He had come across the fruit of the Dead Sea, so sweet and delicious to the eye, so bitter and nauseous to the taste.", "그는 사해의 열매를 만난 것이었다. 눈에는 그토록 달고 맛깔스러우나 혀에는 그토록 쓰고 역겨운 열매를."], ["He had put the apple to his mouth, and it had turned to ashes between his teeth.", "그는 그 사과를 입에 넣었고, 그것은 이 사이에서 재로 변했다."], ["Yet he could not tear himself away.", "그런데도 그는 스스로를 뜯어낼 수가 없었다."], ["He knew, he could not but know, that she jeered at him, ridiculed his love, and insulted the weakness of his religion.", "그는 그녀가 자기를 비웃고 제 사랑을 조롱하며 제 신앙의 나약함을 모욕한다는 것을 알았고, 모를 수가 없었다."], ["But she half-permitted his adoration, and that half-permission added such fuel to his fire that all the fountain of his piety could not quench it.", "그러나 그녀는 그의 흠모를 반쯤 허락했고, 그 반쪽의 허락이 그의 불에 어찌나 기름을 부었는지 그의 경건함의 샘을 통틀어도 그것을 끌 수가 없었다."], ["He began to feel savage, irritated, and revengeful.", "그는 사납고 짜증스럽고 앙심 어린 기분이 들기 시작했다."], ["He meditated some severity of speech, some taunt that should cut her, as her taunts cut him.", "그는 무언가 매서운 말을, 그녀의 조롱이 자기를 베듯 그녀를 벨 만한 조롱을 궁리했다."], ["He reflected as he stood there for a moment, silent before her, that if he desired to quell her proud spirit, he should do so by being prouder even than herself; that if he wished to have her at his feet suppliant for his love, it behoved him to conquer her by indifference.", "그녀 앞에 잠시 말없이 선 채로 그는 이렇게 헤아렸다. 그녀의 도도한 기개를 꺾고자 한다면 그녀보다 더 도도해짐으로써 그리해야 하며, 그녀를 제 발치에 두어 제 사랑을 구걸하게 만들고자 한다면 무관심으로 그녀를 정복하는 것이 마땅하다고."], ["All this passed through his mind.", "이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔다."], ["As far as dead knowledge went, he knew, or thought he knew, how a woman should be tamed.", "죽은 지식이 미치는 데까지는 그도 여자를 어떻게 길들여야 하는지 알았고, 아니 안다고 여겼다."], ["But when he essayed to bring his tactics to bear, he failed like a child.", "그러나 막상 제 전술을 펴 보려 하자 그는 어린아이처럼 실패했다."], ["What chance has dead knowledge with experience in any of the transactions between man and man?", "사람과 사람 사이의 어떤 거래에서든 죽은 지식이 경험을 상대로 무슨 승산이 있겠는가?"], ["What possible chance between man and woman? Mr.", "하물며 남자와 여자 사이라면 대체 무슨 승산이 있겠는가?"], ["Slope loved furiously, insanely and truly, but he had never played the game of love.", "슬로프 씨는 맹렬하게, 미친 듯이, 그리고 진실하게 사랑했으나 사랑의 노름을 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["The signora did not love at all, but she was up to every move of the board.", "시뇨라는 사랑이라고는 전혀 하지 않았으나 판 위의 온갖 수를 꿰고 있었다."], ["It was Philidor pitted against a schoolboy.", "필리도르를 학생 하나와 맞붙여 놓은 격이었다."]], [["And so she continued to insult him, and he continued to bear it.", "그리하여 그녀는 계속 그를 모욕했고, 그는 계속 그것을 견뎠다."]], [["\"Sacrifice the world for love!\"", "“사랑을 위해 세상을 희생한다니요!”"], ["she said in answer to some renewed vapid declaration of his passion.", "그가 또다시 늘어놓은 맹맹한 정념의 선언에 답하여 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"How often has the same thing been said, and how invariably with the same falsehood!\"", "“똑같은 말이 얼마나 자주 되풀이되었으며, 그러면서 얼마나 어김없이 똑같은 거짓이었던지요!”"]], [["\"Falsehood,\"", "“거짓이라고요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Do you say that I am false to you?", "“제가 당신께 거짓되다고 말씀하시는 겁니까?"], ["Do you say that my love is not real?\"", "제 사랑이 진짜가 아니라고 말씀하시는 겁니까?”"]], [["\"False?", "“거짓이냐고요?"], ["Of course it is false, false as the father of falsehood--if indeed falsehoods need a sire and are not self-begotten since the world began.", "물론 거짓이지요. 거짓의 아비만큼이나 거짓이에요 — 세상이 시작된 이래 거짓이란 것이 아비를 필요로 했고 스스로 태어난 것이 아니라면 말이지만요."], ["You are ready to sacrifice the world for love?", "당신은 사랑을 위해 세상을 희생할 준비가 되셨다고요?"], ["Come let us see what you will sacrifice.", "자, 당신이 무엇을 희생하실지 봅시다."], ["I care nothing for nuptial vows.", "저는 혼인의 서약 따위는 조금도 마음에 두지 않아요."], ["The wretch, I think you were kind enough to call him so, whom I swore to love and obey is so base that he can only be thought of with repulsive disgust.", "당신이 친절히도 가련한 자라고 불러 주신 그 사람, 제가 사랑하고 순종하겠노라 맹세했던 그 사람은 어찌나 비루한지 역겨운 혐오로밖에는 떠올릴 수가 없어요."], ["In the council chamber of my heart I have divorced him.", "제 심장의 회의실에서 저는 이미 그이와 이혼했습니다."], ["To me that is as good as though aged lords had gloated for months over the details of his licentious life.", "제게는 그것이 늙은 귀족들이 몇 달 동안 그이의 방탕한 삶을 낱낱이 뜯어보며 흡족해한 것이나 다름없어요."], ["I care nothing for what the world can say.", "저는 세상이 무어라 하든 조금도 마음에 두지 않습니다."], ["Will you be as frank?", "당신도 그만큼 솔직하시겠어요?"], ["Will you take me to your home as your wife?", "저를 아내로 삼아 댁으로 데려가시겠어요?"], ["Will you call me Mrs. Slope before bishop, dean, and prebendaries?\"", "주교와 수석 사제와 참사회원들 앞에서 저를 슬로프 부인이라 부르시겠어요?”"], ["The poor tortured wretch stood silent, not knowing what to say.", "그 가엾고 고문당하는 자는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 말없이 서 있었다."], ["\"What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["You won't do that.", "그건 못 하신다고요."], ["Tell me, then, what part of the world is it that you will sacrifice for my charms?\"", "그러면 말씀해 보세요, 제 매력을 위해 당신이 희생하실 세상이란 대체 어느 부분인가요?”"]], [["\"Were you free to marry, I would take you to my house to-morrow and wish no higher privilege.\"", "“당신이 혼인할 수 있는 몸이라면, 저는 내일이라도 당신을 제 집으로 데려가겠고 그보다 더 높은 특권은 바라지도 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I am free,\"", "“저는 자유로워요.”"], ["said she, almost starting up in her energy.", "그녀가 기운이 뻗쳐 거의 벌떡 일어설 듯이 말했다."], ["For though there was no truth in her pretended regard for her clerical admirer, there was a mixture of real feeling in the scorn and satire with which she spoke of love and marriage generally.", "제 성직자 흠모자를 향한 그 꾸며 낸 애정에는 진실이라곤 없었으되, 사랑과 혼인 일반을 두고 말할 때의 그 경멸과 풍자에는 진짜 감정이 얼마간 섞여 있었기 때문이다."], ["\"I am free--free as the winds.", "“저는 자유로워요 — 바람처럼 자유롭지요."], ["Come, will you take me as I am?", "자, 저를 있는 그대로 거두시겠어요?"], ["Have your wish; sacrifice the world, and prove yourself a true man.\"", "소원대로 하세요. 세상을 희생하고, 당신이 참된 사내임을 증명해 보이세요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope should have taken her at her word.", "슬로프 씨는 그녀의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였어야 했다."], ["She would have drawn back, and he would have had the full advantage of the offer. But he did not.", "그랬더라면 그녀가 물러섰을 테고, 그는 그 제안에서 온전한 이득을 보았을 것이다."], ["Instead of doing so, he stood wrapt in astonishment, passing his fingers through his lank red hair and thinking, as he stared upon her animated countenance, that her wondrous beauty grew more wonderful as he gazed on it.", "그러나 그는 그러지 않았다. 그러기는커녕 그는 놀라움에 싸인 채 서서, 성긴 붉은 머리카락 사이로 손가락을 훑으며, 생기 도는 그녀의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보노라니 그 놀라운 아름다움이 보면 볼수록 더 놀랍게 자란다고 생각하고 있었다."], ["\"Ha!", "“하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!\"", "하!”"], ["she laughed out loud.", "그녀가 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["\"Come, Mr. Slope, don't talk of sacrificing the world again.", "“자, 슬로프 씨, 세상을 희생한다는 말씀은 다시 꺼내지 마세요."], ["People beyond one-and-twenty should never dream of such a thing.", "스물한 살 넘은 사람이라면 그런 것은 꿈에도 생각해서는 안 되지요."], ["You and I, if we have the dregs of any love left in us, if we have the remnants of a passion remaining in our hearts, should husband our resources better.", "당신과 저는, 우리 안에 사랑의 찌꺼기라도 남아 있다면, 우리 심장에 정념의 잔재라도 남아 있다면, 우리 밑천을 좀 더 알뜰히 아껴 써야 해요."], ["We are not in our première jeunesse.", "우리는 première jeunesse가 아니니까요."], ["The world is a very nice place.", "세상은 아주 괜찮은 곳이에요."], ["Your world, at any rate, is so.", "적어도 당신의 세상은 그렇지요."], ["You have all manner of fat rectories to get and possible bishoprics to enjoy.", "당신에게는 챙길 만한 온갖 기름진 교구가 있고 누릴 만한 주교 자리도 있을 수 있으니까요."], ["Come, confess; on second thoughts you would not sacrifice such things for the smiles of a lame lady?\"", "자, 실토해 보세요. 다시 생각해 보면 절름발이 부인의 미소를 얻자고 그런 것들을 희생하지는 않으시겠지요?”"]], [["It was impossible for him to answer this.", "그로서는 여기에 대답하기가 불가능했다."], ["In order to be in any way dignified, he felt that he must be silent.", "조금이라도 위엄을 지키려면 잠자코 있어야 한다고 그는 느꼈다."]], [["\"Come,\"", "“자,”"], ["said she, \"don't boody with me: don't be angry because I speak out some home truths.", "그녀가 말했다. “저와 토라지지 마세요."], ["Alas, the world, as I have found it, has taught me bitter truths.", "제가 뼈아픈 사실을 몇 마디 털어놓았다고 화내지 마시고요. 아, 제가 겪어 온 이 세상은 제게 쓰디쓴 진실을 가르쳐 주었답니다."], ["Come, tell me that I am forgiven.", "자, 저를 용서하셨다고 말씀해 주세요."], ["Are we not to be friends?\"", "우리는 벗이 되어야 하지 않겠어요?”"], ["and she again put out her hand to him.", "그러고는 그녀가 그에게 다시 손을 내밀었다."]], [["He sat himself down in the chair beside her, took her proffered hand, and leant over her.", "그는 그녀 곁의 의자에 앉아 내밀어진 손을 잡고 그녀 위로 몸을 기울였다."]], [["\"There,\"", "“자,”"], ["said she with her sweetest, softest smile--a smile to withstand which a man should be cased in triple steel, \"there; seal your forgiveness on it,\"", "그녀가 더없이 달콤하고 부드러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. 사내라면 세 겹 강철을 두르고서야 견뎌 낼 만한 미소였다."], ["and she raised it towards his face.", "“자, 여기에 당신의 용서를 새겨 두세요.”"], ["He kissed it again and again, and stretched over her as though desirous of extending the charity of his pardon beyond the hand that was offered to him.", "그러면서 그녀는 그것을 그의 얼굴 쪽으로 들어 올렸다. 그는 몇 번이고 거듭 거기에 입을 맞추었고, 자기에게 내밀어진 손 너머로까지 제 용서의 자비를 넓히고 싶은 듯 그녀 위로 몸을 뻗었다."], ["She managed, however, to check his ardour.", "그러나 그녀는 용케 그의 열기를 막아 냈다."], ["For one so easily allured as this poor chaplain, her hand was surely enough.", "이 가엾은 전속 사제처럼 이토록 쉽게 홀리는 자에게라면 그녀의 손 하나로 충분하고도 남았다."]], [["\"Oh, Madeline!\"", "“오, 마들린!”"], ["said he, \"tell me that you love me--do you--do you love me?\"", "그가 말했다. “저를 사랑한다고 말해 주세요 — 사랑하시나요, 사랑하시나요?”"]], [["\"Hush,\"", "“쉿.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"There is my mother's step.", "“어머니 발소리가 나요."], ["Our tête-à-tête has been of monstrous length.", "우리 tête-à-tête가 엄청나게 길어졌군요."], ["Now you had better go.", "이제 가시는 편이 좋겠어요."], ["But we shall see you soon again, shall we not?\"", "하지만 곧 다시 뵙는 거지요?”"]], [["Mr. Slope promised that he would call again on the following day.", "슬로프 씨는 이튿날 다시 찾아오겠다고 약속했다."]], [["\"And, Mr. Slope,\"", "“그리고 슬로프 씨,”"], ["she continued, \"pray answer my note.", "그녀가 이어 말했다. “부디 제 쪽지에 답을 주세요."], ["You have it in your hand, though I declare during these two hours you have not been gracious enough to read it.", "그것을 손에 들고 계시면서도, 정말이지 이 두 시간 동안 읽어 주실 만큼 너그러우시지는 않더군요. 안식일 학교와 아이들에 관한 이야기예요."], ["It is about the Sabbath-school and the children.", "제가 그 아이들을 여기 데려오고 싶어 얼마나 애가 타는지 아시잖아요."], ["You know how anxious I am to have them here.", "저는 일부러 교리문답까지 익히고 있답니다."], ["I have been learning the catechism myself, on purpose.", "다음 주에는 저를 위해 그 일을 성사시켜 주셔야 해요."], ["You must manage it for me next week. I will teach them, at any rate, to submit themselves to their spiritual pastors and masters.\"", "어쨌든 저는 그 아이들에게 제 영적인 목자와 스승에게 순종하도록 가르치겠어요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope said but little on the subject of Sabbath-schools, but he made his adieu, and betook himself home with a sad heart, troubled mind, and uneasy conscience.", "슬로프 씨는 안식일 학교라는 주제를 두고는 별말을 하지 않았고, 작별을 고한 다음 슬픈 마음과 어지러운 정신과 편치 않은 양심을 안고 집으로 향했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII. Mrs. Bold is Entertained by Dr. and Mrs. Grantly at Plumstead", "# 제28장. 볼드 부인이 플럼스테드에서 그랜틀리 박사 내외의 대접을 받다"]], [["It will be remembered that Mr. Slope, when leaving his billet-doux at the house of Mrs.", "독자도 기억하겠지만, 슬로프 씨는 볼드 부인의 집에 자신의 billet-doux를 맡기고 나오면서 그것이 그날 오후에 플럼스테드로 보내질 것이라는 말을 들었다."], ["Bold, had been informed that it would be sent out to her at Plumstead that afternoon. The archdeacon and Mr.", "실은 부주교와 하딩 씨가 사륜마차를 타고 함께 시내에 들어와 있었고, 집으로 돌아가는 길에 엘리너의 짐을 찾아 가기로 되어 있었던 것이다."], ["Harding had in fact come into town together in the brougham, and it had been arranged that they should call for Eleanor's parcels as they left on their way home. Accordingly they did so call, and the maid, as she handed to the coachman a small basket and large bundle carefully and neatly packed, gave in at the carriage window Mr.", "그리하여 그들이 그 집에 들렀을 때, 하녀는 작고 정갈하게 꾸린 바구니 하나와 커다란 보따리 하나를 마부에게 건네면서 마차 창으로 슬로프 씨의 서한을 들이밀었다."], ["Slope's epistle. The archdeacon, who was sitting next to the window, took it and immediately recognized the hand-writing of his enemy.", "창가에 앉아 있던 부주교가 그것을 받아 들었고, 곧바로 자기 원수의 필적을 알아보았다."]], [["\"Who left this?\"", "“이걸 누가 두고 갔느냐?”"], ["said he.", "부주교가 물었다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope called with it himself, your Reverence,\"", "“슬로프 씨가 손수 가지고 오셨습니다, 부주교님.”"], ["said the girl, \"and was very anxious that Missus should have it to-day.\"", "하녀가 말했다. “오늘 안으로 마님께서 꼭 받아 보셔야 한다고 몹시 신신당부를 하셨습니다.”"]], [["So the brougham drove off, and the letter was left in the archdeacon's hand.", "그리하여 사륜마차는 떠났고, 편지는 부주교의 손에 남았다."], ["He looked at it as though he held a basket of adders.", "그는 독사가 가득 든 바구니라도 든 양 그것을 들여다보았다."], ["He could not have thought worse of the document had he read it and discovered it to be licentious and atheistical.", "설령 그것을 읽고 그 안에서 음란하고 무신론적인 내용을 발견했다 한들, 그 문서를 이보다 더 나쁘게 여기지는 못했을 것이다."], ["He did, moreover, what so many wise people are accustomed to do in similar circumstances; he immediately condemned the person to whom the letter was written, as though she were necessarily a particeps criminis.", "게다가 그는 비슷한 처지에서 그토록 많은 현명한 사람들이 으레 하는 일을 그대로 했다. 곧 그 편지를 받는 사람이 반드시 particeps criminis이기라도 한 듯, 그 사람부터 즉시 단죄한 것이다."]], [["Poor Mr. Harding, though by no means inclined to forward Mr.", "가엾은 하딩 씨는 슬로프 씨가 제 딸과 가까워지는 것을 거들 마음이 조금도 없었지만, 그 편지를 사위의 눈에 띄지 않게 할 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었을 것이다."], ["Slope's intimacy with his daughter, would have given anything to have kept the letter from his son-in-law. But that was now impossible.", "그러나 이제 그것은 불가능했다."], ["There it was in his hand, and he looked as thoroughly disgusted as though he were quite sure that it contained all the rhapsodies of a favoured lover.", "편지는 저기 사위의 손에 들려 있었고, 사위는 그 안에 총애받는 연인의 온갖 황홀한 넋두리가 적혀 있음을 확신하기라도 한 듯 진저리 난 얼굴을 하고 있었다."]], [["\"It's very hard on me,\"", "“이런 일이 제 지붕 아래에서 벌어지다니, 저로서는 여간 가혹한 일이 아닙니다.”"], ["said he after awhile, \"that this should go on under my roof.\"", "잠시 후 부주교가 말했다."]], [["Now here the archdeacon was certainly most unreasonable.", "여기서 부주교는 분명 지극히 부당했다."], ["Having invited his sister-in-law to his house, it was a natural consequence that she should receive her letters there.", "처제를 제 집으로 초대한 이상, 그녀가 그곳에서 제 편지를 받는 것은 자연스러운 결과였다."], ["And if Mr. Slope chose to write to her, his letter would, as a matter of course, be sent after her.", "그리고 슬로프 씨가 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 했다면, 그 편지는 당연히 그녀를 따라 부쳐질 터였다."], ["Moreover, the very fact of an invitation to one's house implies confidence on the part of the inviter. He had shown that he thought Mrs.", "게다가 누군가를 제 집에 초대한다는 사실 자체가 초대하는 쪽의 신뢰를 뜻한다."], ["Bold to be a fit person to stay with him by his asking her to do so, and it was most cruel to her that he should complain of her violating the sanctity of his roof-tree, when the laches committed were none of her committing.", "그는 볼드 부인에게 머물러 달라고 청함으로써 그녀를 제 집에 둘 만한 사람으로 여긴다는 것을 이미 보여 준 셈이었다. 그러니 저질러진 태만이 그녀의 소행이 전혀 아닌데도 그녀가 제 집안의 신성함을 더럽혔다고 탓하는 것은 더없이 잔인한 일이었다."]], [["Mr. Harding felt this, and felt also that when the archdeacon talked thus about his roof, what he said was most offensive to himself as Eleanor's father.", "하딩 씨도 그것을 느꼈고, 또한 부주교가 제 지붕을 두고 저렇게 말할 때 그 말이 엘리너의 아버지인 자신에게 더없이 모욕적이라는 것도 느꼈다."], ["If Eleanor did receive a letter from Mr. Slope, what was there in that to pollute the purity of Dr.", "엘리너가 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다 한들, 그것이 그랜틀리 박사네 집안의 순결을 더럽힐 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["Grantly's household? He was indignant that his daughter should be so judged and so spoken of, and he made up his mind that even as Mrs.", "그는 제 딸이 그렇게 재단되고 그렇게 입에 오르내리는 것이 분했고, 설령 딸이 슬로프 부인이 된다 하더라도 하느님의 땅 위 어떤 피조물보다 자기에게 더 소중한 사람일 것이라고 마음을 정했다."], ["Slope she must be dearer to him than any other creature on God's earth. He almost broke out and said as much, but for the moment he restrained himself.", "그는 하마터면 터뜨리듯 그렇게 말할 뻔했으나, 그 순간만은 스스로를 억눌렀다."]], [["\"Here,\"", "“자, 여기 있습니다.”"], ["said the archdeacon, handing the offensive missile to his father-in-law, \"I am not going to be the bearer of his love-letters.", "부주교가 그 불쾌한 물건을 장인에게 건네며 말했다. “저는 저 사람의 연애편지를 나르는 심부름꾼 노릇은 하지 않겠습니다."], ["You are her father and may do as you think fit with it.\"", "아버님이 그 애의 아버지시니 좋으실 대로 하십시오.”"]], [["By doing as he thought fit with it, the archdeacon certainly meant that Mr. Harding would be justified in opening and reading the letter, and taking any steps which might in consequence be necessary.", "좋을 대로 하라는 말로 부주교가 뜻한 바는, 하딩 씨가 그 편지를 뜯어 읽고 그에 따라 필요한 조치를 취하더라도 정당하리라는 것이었음이 분명하다."], ["To tell the truth, Dr. Grantly did feel rather a stronger curiosity than was justified by his outraged virtue to see the contents of the letter.", "사실을 말하자면, 그랜틀리 박사는 그 편지의 내용을 보고 싶은 호기심이 제 상처 입은 덕성이 허락하는 정도보다는 조금 더 강했다."], ["Of course he could not open it himself, but he wished to make Mr. Harding understand that he, as Eleanor's father, would be fully justified in doing so.", "물론 자기 손으로 봉을 뜯을 수는 없었으나, 엘리너의 아버지인 하딩 씨라면 그리해도 아무 흠이 없다는 것을 그에게 납득시키고 싶었던 것이다."], ["The idea of such a proceeding never occurred to Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨에게는 그런 짓을 한다는 생각 자체가 떠오르지 않았다."], ["His authority over Eleanor ceased when she became the wife of John Bold.", "엘리너에 대한 그의 권한은 그 아이가 존 볼드의 아내가 된 순간에 끝났다."], ["He had not the slightest wish to pry into her correspondence.", "그는 딸의 편지를 엿볼 마음이 티끌만큼도 없었다."], ["He consequently put the letter into his pocket, and only wished that he had been able to do so without the archdeacon's knowledge.", "그리하여 그는 편지를 제 주머니에 넣었고, 다만 부주교가 모르는 사이에 그렇게 할 수 있었더라면 좋았겠다고 생각할 뿐이었다."], ["They both sat silent during half the journey home, and then Dr.", "두 사람은 집으로 돌아가는 길의 절반 동안 말없이 앉아 있었고, 이윽고 그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."], ["Grantly said, \"Perhaps Susan had better give it to her.", "“수전이 그 애에게 건네는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["She can explain to her sister better than either you or I can do how deep is the disgrace of such an acquaintance.\"", "그런 사귐이 얼마나 수치스러운 일인지는 아버님이나 저보다 그 사람이 제 동생에게 더 잘 설명할 수 있을 테니까요.”"]], [["\"I think you are very hard upon Eleanor,\"", "“자네는 엘리너에게 너무 가혹하네.”"], ["replied Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 대답했다."], ["\"I will not allow that she has disgraced herself, nor do I think it likely that she will do so.", "“나는 그 애가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 인정하지 않겠고, 앞으로 그럴 성싶지도 않네."], ["She has a right to correspond with whom she pleases, and I shall not take upon myself to blame her because she gets a letter from Mr. Slope.\"", "그 애에게는 제가 좋아하는 사람과 편지를 주고받을 권리가 있고, 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 이유로 내가 나서서 그 애를 나무라지는 않겠네.”"]], [["\"I suppose,\" said Dr.", "“짐작건대,”"], ["Grantly, \"you don't wish her to marry the man.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다. “아버님도 그 사람과 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시겠지요."], ["I suppose you'll admit that she would disgrace herself if she did do so.\"", "그렇게 된다면 처제가 스스로를 욕되게 하는 것이라는 점만은 인정하시겠지요.”"]], [["\"I do not wish her to marry him,\"", "“나는 그 애가 그 사람과 혼인하기를 바라지 않네.”"], ["said the perplexed father.", "난처해진 아버지가 말했다."], ["\"I do not like him, and do not think he would make a good husband.", "“나는 그 사람이 마음에 들지 않고, 좋은 남편이 되리라고 생각지도 않네."], ["But if Eleanor chooses to do so, I shall certainly not think that she disgraces herself.\"", "그러나 엘리너가 그리하기로 한다면, 나는 결코 그 애가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 여기지는 않을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Good heavens!\" exclaimed Dr.", "“맙소사!”"], ["Grantly and threw himself back into the corner of his brougham. Mr.", "그랜틀리 박사는 이렇게 외치고 제 사륜마차 구석에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다."], ["Harding said nothing more, but commenced playing a dirge with an imaginary fiddle bow upon an imaginary violoncello, for which there did not appear to be quite room enough in the carriage; he continued the tune, with sundry variations, till he arrived at the rectory door.", "하딩 씨는 더 말이 없었고, 다만 마차 안에 자리가 넉넉지 않아 보이는데도 있지도 않은 첼로 위에 있지도 않은 활을 올려놓고 만가를 켜기 시작했다. 그는 이런저런 변주를 섞어 가며 목사관 문 앞에 이를 때까지 그 곡을 이어 갔다."]], [["The archdeacon had been meditating sad things in his mind.", "부주교는 마음속으로 서글픈 것들을 곱씹고 있었다."], ["Hitherto he had always looked on his father-in-law as a true partisan, though he knew him to be a man devoid of all the combative qualifications for that character.", "지금까지 그는 늘 장인을 진정한 같은 편으로 여겨 왔다. 그 노릇에 필요한 싸움의 기질이라고는 하나도 갖추지 못한 사람이라는 것은 알고 있었지만 말이다."], ["He had felt no fear that Mr. Harding would go over to the enemy, though he had never counted much on the ex-warden's prowess in breaking the hostile ranks.", "전임 워든이 적진을 무너뜨리는 무용을 보여 주리라고 크게 기대한 적은 없었으되, 하딩 씨가 적에게 넘어가리라는 두려움만은 품어 본 적이 없었다."], ["Now, however, it seemed that Eleanor, with her wiles, had completely trepanned and bewildered her father, cheated him out of his judgement, robbed him of the predilections and tastes of his life, and caused him to be tolerant of a man whose arrogance and vulgarity would, a few years since, have been unendurable to him.", "그런데 이제 보니 엘리너가 온갖 꾀로 제 아버지를 완전히 홀려 어지럽히고, 판단력을 빼앗고, 평생 지녀 온 취향과 애착을 앗아 가고, 몇 해 전이었다면 도저히 참아 내지 못했을 오만하고 천박한 사내를 너그럽게 보아 넘기게 만들어 놓은 것이었다."], ["That the whole thing was as good as arranged between Eleanor and Mr. Slope there was no longer any room to doubt.", "엘리너와 슬로프 씨 사이에 모든 것이 이미 정해진 것이나 다름없다는 데는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["That Mr. Harding knew that such was the case, even this could hardly be doubted.", "하딩 씨가 사정이 그러함을 알고 있다는 것 또한 좀처럼 의심하기 어려웠다."], ["It was too manifest that he at any rate suspected it and was prepared to sanction it.", "적어도 그가 그것을 짐작하면서도 묵인할 채비가 되어 있다는 것만은 너무나 뚜렷했다."]], [["And to tell the truth, such was the case. Mr.", "그리고 사실을 말하자면, 정말 그러했다."], ["Harding disliked Mr. Slope as much as it was in his nature to dislike any man.", "하딩 씨는 사람을 싫어할 수 있는 제 천성의 한계까지 슬로프 씨를 싫어했다."], ["Had his daughter wished to do her worst to displease him by a second marriage, she could hardly have succeeded better than by marrying Mr.", "딸이 두 번째 혼인으로 아버지를 최대한 언짢게 만들고자 했다면, 슬로프 씨를 택하는 것보다 더 잘해 낼 방법은 좀처럼 없었을 것이다."], ["Slope. But, as he said to himself now very often, what right had he to condemn her if she did nothing that was really wrong?", "그러나 요즘 그가 자주 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이, 딸이 정말로 그릇된 일을 하나도 하지 않았다면 자기에게 무슨 권리가 있어 딸을 단죄하겠는가?"], ["If she liked Mr. Slope, it was her affair.", "딸이 슬로프 씨를 좋아한다면 그것은 딸의 일이었다."], ["It was indeed miraculous to him that a woman with such a mind, so educated, so refined, so nice in her tastes, should like such a man.", "그런 정신을 지녔고 그토록 교양 있고 그토록 세련되고 취향이 그토록 까다로운 여자가 그런 사내를 좋아한다는 것은 그에게 참으로 불가사의한 일이기는 했다."], ["Then he asked himself whether it was possible that she did so.", "그러다 그는 딸이 정말 그럴 수 있는 일인지 스스로에게 물었다."]], [["Ah, thou weak man; most charitable, most Christian, but weakest of men!", "아, 그대 나약한 사람이여. 더없이 자애롭고 더없이 그리스도인답되 사내들 가운데 가장 약한 이여!"], ["Why couldn't thou not have asked herself?", "어찌하여 그대는 딸에게 직접 묻지 못하였는가?"], ["Was she not the daughter of thy loins, the child of thy heart, the best beloved to thee of all humanity?", "그 아이는 그대의 몸에서 난 딸이요 그대 마음의 자식이며, 온 인류 가운데 그대가 가장 사랑하는 이가 아니던가?"], ["Had she not proved to thee, by years of closest affection, her truth and goodness and filial obedience?", "그 아이는 여러 해에 걸친 더없이 가까운 정으로 제 진실함과 착함과 딸로서의 순종을 그대에게 증명하지 않았던가?"], ["And yet, knowing and feeling all this, thou couldst endure to go groping in darkness, hearing her named in strains which wounded thy loving heart, and being unable to defend her as thou shouldst have done!", "그런데도 이 모든 것을 알고 느끼면서, 그대는 어둠 속을 더듬어 다니며 사랑에 겨운 그대의 가슴을 찌르는 말투로 그 아이의 이름이 오르내리는 것을 듣고도 마땅히 그리했어야 할 만큼 그 아이를 지켜 주지 못한 채 견디고 있단 말인가!"]], [["Mr.", "하딩 씨는 제 딸이 그 사내와 혼인할 뜻이 있다고 믿지 않았고 지금도 믿지 않았으나, 그런 견해를 입 밖에 내어 스스로를 묶는 것이 두려웠다."], ["Harding had not believed, did not believe, that his daughter meant to marry this man, but he feared to commit himself to such an opinion.", "만일 딸이 정말 그렇게 한다면 그때는 물러설 길이 없을 터였다. 그의 마음이 바라는 바는 이러했다."], ["If she did do it there would be then no means of retreat.", "첫째로 부주교의 억측에 아무런 진실도 없기를, 그리고 감히 그럴 수만 있었다면 그는 이 바람에 온전히 기대고 싶었다."], ["The wishes of his heart were: first, that there should be no truth in the archdeacon's surmises; and in this wish he would have fain trusted entirely, had he dared so to do; secondly, that the match might be prevented, if unfortunately, it had been contemplated by Eleanor; thirdly, that should she be so infatuated as to marry this man, he might justify his conduct and declare that no cause existed for his separating himself from her.", "둘째로 불행히도 엘리너가 그런 혼인을 마음에 두었다면 그것이 막아지기를. 셋째로 딸이 그 사내와 혼인할 만큼 홀려 버린다 하더라도, 자기가 제 처신을 떳떳이 밝히고 딸과 갈라설 까닭이 조금도 없다고 말할 수 있기를."]], [["He wanted to believe her incapable of such a marriage; he wanted to show that he so believed of her; but he wanted also to be able to say hereafter that she had done nothing amiss, if she should unfortunately prove herself to be different from what he thought her to be.", "그는 딸이 그런 혼인을 할 수 있는 사람이 아니라고 믿고 싶었다. 자기가 딸을 그렇게 믿고 있음을 보이고도 싶었다. 그러나 불행히도 딸이 자기가 생각하던 사람과 다른 것으로 드러난다 하더라도, 훗날 그 아이는 잘못한 것이 하나도 없다고 말할 수 있기를 또한 바랐다."]], [["Nothing but affection could justify such fickleness, but affection did justify it.", "그런 변덕을 정당화할 수 있는 것은 애정뿐이었으나, 애정은 실제로 그것을 정당화했다."], ["There was but little of the Roman about Mr.", "하딩 씨에게는 로마인다운 구석이 거의 없었다."], ["Harding. He could not sacrifice his Lucretia even though she should be polluted by the accepted addresses of the clerical Tarquin at the palace.", "그는 제 루크레티아가 주교관에 있는 저 성직자 타르퀴니우스의 구애를 받아들여 더럽혀진다 하더라도 그 아이를 제물로 바칠 수 없었다."], ["If Tarquin could be prevented, well and good, but if not, the father would still open his heart to his daughter and accept her as she presented herself, Tarquin and all.", "타르퀴니우스를 막을 수 있다면 더없이 좋은 일이겠으나, 막지 못한다면 아버지는 그래도 딸에게 제 가슴을 열고 그 아이가 나타나는 그대로, 타르퀴니우스까지 함께 받아들일 터였다."]], [["Dr. Grantly's mind was of a stronger calibre, and he was by no means deficient in heart.", "그랜틀리 박사의 정신은 더 단단한 재질이었고, 그렇다고 마음이 모자란 사람도 결코 아니었다."], ["He loved with an honest genuine love his wife and children and friends.", "그는 아내와 자식들과 벗들을 정직하고 진실한 사랑으로 사랑했다."], ["He loved his father-in-law, and was quite prepared to love Eleanor too, if she would be one of his party, if she would be on his side, if she would regard the Slopes and the Proudies as the enemies of mankind and acknowledge and feel the comfortable merits of the Gwynnes and Arabins.", "장인도 사랑했고, 엘리너 또한 얼마든지 사랑할 채비가 되어 있었다. 다만 그 아이가 제 편의 한 사람이 되어 준다면, 제 쪽에 서 준다면, 슬로프 무리와 프라우디 무리를 인류의 적으로 여기고 그윈 씨네와 애러빈 씨네의 든든한 미덕을 인정하고 느껴 준다면 말이다."], ["He wished to be what he called \"safe\" with all those whom he had admitted to the penetralia of his house and heart.", "그는 제 집과 제 마음의 지성소에 들인 모든 이들과 이른바 “안전한”"], ["He could luxuriate in no society that was deficient in a certain feeling of faithful, staunch High Churchism, which to him was tantamount to freemasonry.", "사이이기를 바랐다. 그에게 프리메이슨의 결속이나 다름없었던 저 충실하고 흔들림 없는 고교회파의 정서가 모자란 자리에서는 조금도 흥겨울 수 없었다."], ["He was not strict in his lines of definition.", "그는 경계를 긋는 데 그리 엄격하지는 않았다."], ["He endured without impatience many different shades of Anglo-church conservatism; but with the Slopes and Proudies he could not go on all fours.", "영국 국교회 보수주의의 여러 가지 색조를 짜증 없이 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 슬로프 무리, 프라우디 무리와는 도저히 네 발을 맞추어 걸을 수가 없었다."]], [["He was wanting in, moreover, or perhaps it would be more correct to say, he was not troubled by that womanly tenderness which was so peculiar to Mr.", "게다가 그에게는 하딩 씨 특유의 저 여성적인 부드러움이 없었다. 아니, 그런 부드러움 때문에 시달리는 일이 없었다고 하는 편이 더 정확할 것이다."], ["Harding. His feelings towards his friends were that while they stuck to him, he would stick to them; that he would work with them shoulder and shoulder; that he would be faithful to the faithful.", "벗들을 향한 그의 마음은 이러했다."], ["He knew nothing of that beautiful love which can be true to a false friend.", "그들이 자기에게 붙어 있는 한 자기도 그들에게 붙어 있겠다는 것, 그들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 일하겠다는 것, 충실한 자에게 충실하겠다는 것. 그는 거짓된 벗에게도 참될 수 있는 저 아름다운 사랑을 알지 못했다."]], [["And thus these two men, each miserable enough in his own way, returned to Plumstead.", "그리하여 저마다의 방식으로 충분히 비참해진 두 사람은 플럼스테드로 돌아왔다."]], [["It was getting late when they arrived there, and the ladies had already gone up to dress.", "그들이 도착했을 때는 이미 늦은 시각이어서 부인들은 벌써 옷을 갈아입으러 올라간 뒤였다."], ["Nothing more was said as the two parted in the hall. As Mr.", "두 사람은 현관에서 헤어지면서 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Harding passed to his own room he knocked at Eleanor's door and handed in the letter.", "하딩 씨는 제 방으로 가는 길에 엘리너의 방문을 두드려 편지를 들이밀었다."], ["The archdeacon hurried to his own territory, there to unburden his heart to his faithful partner.", "부주교는 제 영역으로 서둘러 가서 충실한 반려에게 마음을 털어놓았다."]], [["What colloquy took place between the marital chamber and the adjoining dressing-room shall not be detailed.", "부부의 침실과 그에 딸린 옷방 사이에서 어떤 이야기가 오갔는지는 여기 적지 않겠다."], ["The reader, now intimate with the persons concerned, can well imagine it.", "이제 관련된 사람들과 친해진 독자라면 넉넉히 그려 볼 수 있을 것이다."], ["The whole tenor of it also might be read in Mrs. Grantly's brow as she came down to dinner.", "그 대화의 전체 기조 또한 만찬에 내려오는 그랜틀리 부인의 이마에서 읽어 낼 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["Eleanor, when she received the letter from her father's hand, had no idea from whom it came. She had never seen Mr.", "엘리너는 아버지의 손에서 편지를 받았을 때 그것이 누구에게서 온 것인지 짐작조차 하지 못했다."], ["Slope's handwriting, or if so had forgotten it, and did not think of him as she twisted the letter as people do twist letters when they do not immediately recognize their correspondents either by the writing or the seal.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨의 필적을 본 적이 없었고, 보았다 하더라도 잊어버린 터라, 사람들이 필적으로도 봉인으로도 상대를 얼른 알아보지 못할 때 흔히 그러듯 편지를 이리저리 돌려 보면서도 그를 떠올리지 않았다."], ["She was sitting at her glass, brushing her hair and rising every other minute to play with her boy, who was sprawling on the bed and who engaged pretty nearly the whole attention of the maid as well as of his mother.", "그녀는 거울 앞에 앉아 머리를 빗다가, 침대 위에 팔다리를 벌리고 누워 어미뿐 아니라 하녀의 주의까지 거의 다 차지하고 있는 아들과 놀아 주려고 잠깐씩 일어서곤 했다."]], [["At last, sitting before her toilet-table, she broke the seal and, turning over the leaf, saw Mr.", "마침내 그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 봉을 뜯었고, 장을 넘기다 슬로프 씨의 이름을 보았다."], ["Slope's name. She first felt surprised, and then annoyed, and then anxious.", "처음에는 놀랐고, 다음에는 언짢았고, 그다음에는 마음이 조마조마했다."], ["As she read it she became interested.", "읽어 가는 동안 그녀는 관심이 쏠렸다."], ["She was so delighted to find that all obstacles to her father's return to the hospital were apparently removed that she did not observe the fulsome language in which the tidings were conveyed.", "아버지가 요양원으로 돌아가는 데 걸림돌이 되던 것들이 모두 치워진 듯하다는 사실이 어찌나 반가웠던지, 그 소식을 전하는 낯간지러운 말투는 눈에 들어오지도 않았다."], ["She merely perceived that she was commissioned to tell her father that such was the case, and she did not realize the fact that such a communication should not have been made, in the first instance, to her by an unmarried young clergyman.", "그녀는 그저 사정이 그러하다는 것을 아버지에게 알리는 일을 자기가 맡았다고만 알아들었을 뿐, 그런 통지가 애초에 혼인하지 않은 젊은 성직자에게서 자기에게 먼저 와서는 안 되는 것이라는 사실은 깨닫지 못했다."], ["She felt, on the whole, grateful to Mr. Slope and anxious to get on her dress that she might run with the news to her father.", "전체적으로 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 고마움을 느꼈고, 어서 옷을 입고 아버지에게 그 소식을 들고 달려가고 싶었다."], ["Then she came to the allusion to her own pious labours, and she said in her heart that Mr. Slope was an affected ass.", "그러다 자신의 경건한 수고를 들먹인 대목에 이르자, 그녀는 슬로프 씨가 잘난 체하는 얼간이라고 속으로 말했다."], ["Then she went on again and was offended by her boy being called Mr.", "다시 읽어 나가다가 제 아들이 슬로프 씨의 귀염둥이라 불린 데서 기분이 상했다."], ["Slope's darling--he was nobody's darling but her own, or at any rate not the darling of a disagreeable stranger like Mr.", "그 아이는 오직 제 어미의 귀염둥이일 뿐, 적어도 슬로프 씨같이 달갑지 않은 낯선 이의 귀염둥이는 아니었다."], ["Slope. Lastly she arrived at the tresses and felt a qualm of disgust.", "마지막으로 그녀는 머릿단 이야기에 이르러 역겨움이 치미는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She looked up in the glass, and there they were before her, long and silken, certainly, and very beautiful.", "고개를 들어 거울을 보니 그것들이 눈앞에 있었다. 길고 비단결 같고 참으로 아름다웠다."], ["I will not say but that she knew them to be so, but she felt angry with them and brushed them roughly and carelessly.", "그녀가 그것을 몰랐다고 말하지는 않겠으나, 어쨌든 그녀는 그 머리카락에 화가 나서 거칠고 아무렇게나 빗어 내렸다."], ["She crumpled the letter up with angry violence, and resolved, almost without thinking of it, that she would not show it to her father.", "그녀는 성난 손길로 편지를 구겨 버렸고, 거의 생각할 겨를도 없이 그것을 아버지에게 보이지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["She would merely tell him the contents of it.", "내용만 말씀드리면 되리라."], ["She then comforted herself again with her boy, had her dress fastened, and went down to dinner.", "그러고는 다시 아들을 안고 마음을 달랜 뒤, 옷을 여미게 하고 만찬에 내려갔다."]], [["As she tripped down the stairs she began to ascertain that there was some difficulty in her situation.", "층계를 사뿐히 내려가면서 그녀는 제 처지에 얼마간 곤란한 데가 있음을 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["She could not keep from her father the news about the hospital, nor could she comfortably confess the letter from Mr. Slope before the Grantlys.", "요양원 소식을 아버지에게 감출 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 그랜틀리 내외 앞에서 슬로프 씨의 편지를 마음 편히 고백할 수도 없었다."], ["Her father had already gone down.", "아버지는 이미 내려간 뒤였다."], ["She had heard his step upon the lobby.", "복도에서 그 발소리를 들었던 것이다."], ["She resolved therefore to take him aside and tell him her little bit of news.", "그래서 그녀는 아버지를 따로 모셔다 그 자그마한 소식을 전하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Poor girl!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["She had no idea how severely the unfortunate letter had already been discussed.", "그녀는 그 불행한 편지가 이미 얼마나 매섭게 도마에 올랐는지 알지 못했다."]], [["When she entered the drawing-room, the whole party were there, including Mr. Arabin, and the whole party looked glum and sour.", "그녀가 응접실에 들어섰을 때 애러빈 씨를 포함해 온 식구가 다 모여 있었고, 다들 시무룩하고 뾰로통한 얼굴이었다."], ["The two girls sat silent and apart as though they were aware that something was wrong.", "두 소녀는 무언가 잘못되었음을 아는 듯이 말없이 떨어져 앉아 있었다."], ["Even Mr. Arabin was solemn and silent.", "애러빈 씨마저 엄숙하고 말이 없었다."], ["Eleanor had not seen him since breakfast.", "엘리너는 아침 식사 이후로 그를 보지 못했다."], ["He had been the whole day at St. Ewold's, and such having been the case, it was natural that he should tell how matters were going on there.", "그는 온종일 세인트 이월드에 가 있었으니, 그렇다면 그곳 일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 이야기하는 것이 자연스러웠을 것이다."], ["He did nothing of the kind, however, but remained solemn and silent.", "그러나 그는 그런 이야기라고는 하지 않고 여전히 엄숙하게 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["They were all solemn and silent.", "다들 엄숙했고 말이 없었다."], ["Eleanor knew in her heart that they had been talking about her, and her heart misgave her as she thought of Mr. Slope and his letter.", "엘리너는 그들이 제 이야기를 하고 있었음을 마음속으로 알았고, 슬로프 씨와 그 편지를 떠올리자 가슴이 켕겼다."], ["At any rate she felt it to be quite impossible to speak to her father alone while matters were in this state.", "어쨌든 사정이 이러한데 아버지에게 단둘이 말을 건네기란 도저히 불가능하겠다고 그녀는 느꼈다."]], [["Dinner was soon announced, and Dr. Grantly, as was his wont, gave Eleanor his arm.", "곧 만찬이 알려졌고, 그랜틀리 박사는 늘 하던 대로 엘리너에게 팔을 내주었다."], ["But he did so as though the doing it were an outrage on his feelings rendered necessary by sternest necessity.", "그러나 그렇게 하면서도 그것이 지극한 필요에 떠밀려 어쩔 수 없이 제 감정을 짓밟는 일이라는 투였다. 엘리너는 예민한 마음으로 그것을 느끼고 손가락을 그의 소매에 겨우 얹는 둥 마는 둥 했다."], ["With quick sympathy Eleanor felt this, and hardly put her fingers on his coat-sleeve.", "만찬 시간이 어떻게 흘러갔을지는 짐작이 갈 것이다. 그랜틀리 박사가 애러빈 씨에게 몇 마디 했고, 애러빈 씨가 그랜틀리 부인에게 몇 마디 했고, 그녀가 제 아버지에게 몇 마디 했고, 아버지는 엘리너에게 몇 마디 해 보려 애썼다."], ["It may be guessed in what way the dinner-hour was passed. Dr.", "그녀는 자기가 무언가로 재판을 받고 유죄가 되었다고 느꼈으나, 그것이 무엇인지는 알지 못했다."], ["Grantly said a few words to Mr. Arabin, Mr.", "그녀는 그들 모두에게 이렇게 외치고 싶었다."], ["Arabin said a few words to Mrs.", "“자, 제가 무엇을 했다는 겁니까."], ["Grantly, she said a few words to her father, and he tried to say a few words to Eleanor.", "어서 말씀들 하시고 제 죄가 무엇인지 알려 주세요. 제발 가장 나쁜 것까지 다 듣게 해 주세요.”"], ["She felt that she had been tried and found guilty of something, though she knew not what. She longed to say out to them all, \"Well, what is it that I have done; out with it, and let me know my crime; for heaven's sake let me hear the worst of it;\"", "그러나 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["but she could not. She could say nothing, but sat there silent, half-feeling that she was guilty, and trying in vain to pretend even to eat her dinner.", "그녀는 아무 말도 못 한 채 그저 말없이 앉아, 반쯤은 자기가 정말 죄인인 듯한 기분으로, 저녁을 먹는 시늉이라도 해 보려고 헛되이 애썼다."]], [["At last the cloth was drawn, and the ladies were not long following it.", "마침내 식탁보가 걷혔고, 부인들도 오래지 않아 자리를 떴다."], ["When they were gone, the gentlemen were somewhat more sociable but not much so.", "그들이 나가자 신사들은 조금 더 어울리는 편이 되었으나 그리 대단치는 않았다."], ["They could not of course talk over Eleanor's sins.", "물론 그들이 엘리너의 죄를 놓고 이야기할 수는 없었다."], ["The archdeacon had indeed so far betrayed his sister-in-law as to whisper into Mr. Arabin's ear in the study, as they met there before dinner, a hint of what he feared.", "사실 부주교는 만찬 전에 서재에서 마주쳤을 때 자기가 두려워하는 바를 애러빈 씨의 귀에 속삭임으로써 그만큼 처제를 팔아넘긴 터였다."], ["He did so with the gravest and saddest of fears, and Mr. Arabin became grave and apparently sad enough as he heard it.", "그는 더없이 무겁고 더없이 서글픈 두려움을 담아 그렇게 했고, 그것을 들은 애러빈 씨의 낯빛도 그만큼 무겁고 서글퍼 보였다."], ["He opened his eyes, and his mouth and said in a sort of whisper \"Mr.", "그는 눈을 크게 뜨고 입을 벌린 채 속삭이듯 “슬로프 씨라니!” 하고 말했다."], ["Slope!\" in the same way as he might have said \"The Cholera!\"", "벗이 그 끔찍한 병이 제 아이 방에 들어왔다고 알려 주었다면 “콜레라라니!”"], ["had his friend told him that that horrid disease was in his nursery.", "하고 말했을 그 투 그대로였다. “그런 것 같소, 그런 것 같소.”"], ["\"I fear so, I fear so,\" said the archdeacon, and then together they left the room.", "부주교가 말했고, 두 사람은 함께 방을 나섰다."]], [["We will not accurately analyse Mr. Arabin's feelings on receipt of such astounding tidings.", "그토록 놀라운 소식을 들은 애러빈 씨의 심경을 우리가 정확히 헤아려 보지는 않겠다."], ["It will suffice to say that he was surprised, vexed, sorrowful, and ill at ease.", "그가 놀랐고 언짢았고 서글펐고 마음이 편치 않았다고만 해 두면 충분하다."], ["He had not perhaps thought very much about Eleanor, but he had appreciated her influence, and had felt that close intimacy with her in a country-house was pleasant to him, and also beneficial.", "그는 아마 엘리너에 대해 그리 많이 생각해 본 적은 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 미치는 영향을 소중히 여겼고, 시골집에서 그녀와 가깝게 지내는 것이 즐겁고 또 이롭기까지 하다고 느껴 왔다."], ["He had spoken highly of her intelligence to the archdeacon, and had walked about the shrubberies with her, carrying her boy on his back.", "그는 부주교에게 그녀의 총명함을 높이 말했고, 그녀의 아들을 등에 업은 채 관목 숲길을 그녀와 함께 거닐기도 했다."], ["When Mr. Arabin had called Johnny his darling, Eleanor was not angry.", "애러빈 씨가 조니를 제 귀염둥이라고 불렀을 때 엘리너는 화를 내지 않았다."]], [["Thus the three men sat over their wine, all thinking of the same subject, but unable to speak of it to each other.", "그리하여 세 남자는 포도주를 앞에 두고 앉아 저마다 같은 일을 생각하면서도 서로 그것을 입에 올리지 못했다."], ["So we will leave them and follow the ladies into the drawing-room.", "그러니 우리는 그들을 두고 부인들을 따라 응접실로 가 보기로 하자."]], [["Mrs. Grantly had received a commission from her husband, and had undertaken it with some unwillingness.", "그랜틀리 부인은 남편에게서 임무를 받았고, 다소 내키지 않는 마음으로 그것을 떠맡았다."], ["He had desired her to speak gravely to Eleanor and to tell her that, if she persisted in her adherence to Mr. Slope, she could no longer look for the countenance of her present friends.", "남편은 그녀에게 엘리너와 진지하게 이야기하여, 슬로프 씨를 계속 가까이한다면 지금의 벗들에게서 더는 호의를 기대할 수 없으리라고 일러 주라 했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly probably knew her sister better than the doctor did, and assured him that it would be in vain to talk to her.", "그랜틀리 부인은 아마 박사보다 제 동생을 더 잘 알았기에, 그 아이에게 말해 보아야 소용없으리라고 남편에게 장담했다."], ["The only course likely to be of any service in her opinion was to keep Eleanor away from Barchester.", "그녀의 생각으로 조금이라도 도움이 될 만한 길은 엘리너를 바체스터에서 떼어 놓는 것뿐이었다."], ["Perhaps she might have added, for she had a very keen eye in such things, that there might also be ground for hope in keeping Eleanor near Mr.", "그런 일에는 눈이 몹시 밝은 사람이었으니, 어쩌면 엘리너를 애러빈 씨 가까이에 두는 데도 기대를 걸 만한 구석이 있다고 덧붙일 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Arabin. Of this, however, she said nothing.", "그러나 그 이야기는 입에 담지 않았다."], ["But the archdeacon would not be talked over; he spoke much of his conscience, and declared that, if Mrs.", "부주교는 설득에 넘어가지 않았다. 그는 제 양심을 길게 말했고, 그랜틀리 부인이 하지 않겠다면 자기가 하겠다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Grantly would not do it, he would. So instigated, the lady undertook the task, stating, however, her full conviction that her interference would be worse than useless.", "그렇게 떠밀린 부인은, 자기가 나서 봐야 소용없기는커녕 더 나빠지리라는 확신을 다 밝혀 두고서 그 일을 떠맡았다."], ["And so it proved.", "그리고 과연 그러했다."]], [["As soon as they were in the drawing-room Mrs. Grantly found some excuse for sending her girls away, and then began her task.", "응접실에 들어서자마자 그랜틀리 부인은 딸들을 내보낼 구실을 찾아냈고, 그런 다음 제 임무에 착수했다."], ["She knew well that she could exercise but very slight authority over her sister.", "그녀는 제가 동생에게 행사할 수 있는 권위가 아주 미미하다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Their various modes of life, and the distance between their residences, had prevented any very close confidence.", "서로 사는 방식이 다르고 사는 곳이 멀리 떨어져 있어 그리 깊은 속내를 나눌 사이가 되지 못했던 것이다."], ["They had hardly lived together since Eleanor was a child.", "엘리너가 어린아이였던 시절 이후로 둘은 함께 산 적이 거의 없었다."], ["Eleanor had, moreover, especially in latter years, resented in a quiet sort of way the dictatorial authority which the archdeacon seemed to exercise over her father, and on this account had been unwilling to allow the archdeacon's wife to exercise authority over herself.", "게다가 엘리너는 특히 근래에 들어, 부주교가 제 아버지에게 휘두르는 듯한 그 훈계조의 권위를 조용한 방식으로 못마땅해했고, 그런 까닭에 부주교의 아내가 제게 권위를 부리는 것도 받아들이려 하지 않았다."]], [["\"You got a note just before dinner, I believe,\"", "“만찬 직전에 쪽지를 한 통 받았다지.”"], ["began the eldest sister.", "맏언니가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["Eleanor acknowledged that she had done so, and felt that she turned red as she acknowledged it.", "엘리너는 그렇다고 인정했고, 인정하면서 제 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She would have given anything to have kept her colour, but the more she tried to do so the more signally she failed.", "낯빛을 지킬 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으련만, 그러려고 애쓸수록 보란 듯이 실패했다."]], [["\"Was it not from Mr. Slope?\"", "“슬로프 씨한테서 온 것 아니었니?”"]], [["Eleanor said that the letter was from Mr. Slope.", "엘리너는 그 편지가 슬로프 씨에게서 온 것이라고 말했다."]], [["\"Is he a regular correspondent of yours, Eleanor?\"", "“그 사람과 늘 편지를 주고받는 사이니, 엘리너?”"]], [["\"Not exactly,\"", "“꼭 그렇지는 않아요.”"], ["said she, already beginning to feel angry at the cross-examination.", "그녀가 말했다. 심문을 당하는 데 벌써 화가 나기 시작한 참이었다."], ["She determined, and why it would be difficult to say, that nothing should induce her to tell her sister Susan what was the subject of the letter.", "그 편지의 내용이 무엇인지는 무슨 일이 있어도 언니 수전에게 말하지 않겠다고 그녀는 마음을 정했는데, 왜 그랬는지는 말하기 어려울 것이다."], ["Mrs. Grantly, she knew, was instigated by the archdeacon, and she would not plead to any arraignment made against her by him.", "그랜틀리 부인이 부주교에게 떠밀려 나섰다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었고, 그가 자기에게 씌운 어떤 혐의에도 답변하지 않을 작정이었다."]], [["\"But, Eleanor dear, why do you get letters from Mr. Slope at all, knowing, as you do, he is a person so distasteful to Papa, and to the archdeacon, and indeed to all your friends?\"", "“하지만 엘리너, 그 사람이 아버지께도, 부주교께도, 아니 네 벗들 모두에게 그토록 거슬리는 사람인 줄 뻔히 알면서 어째서 그 사람한테서 편지를 받는 거니?”"]], [["\"In the first place, Susan, I don't get letters from him; and in the next place, as Mr.", "“우선 언니, 저는 그 사람한테서 편지를 받지 않아요."], ["Slope wrote the one letter which I have got, and as I only received it, which I could not very well help doing, as Papa handed it to me, I think you had better ask Mr.", "그리고 다음으로, 제가 받은 그 한 통의 편지는 슬로프 씨가 쓴 것이고 저는 그저 받았을 뿐인데, 아버지가 손수 건네주시는 것을 제가 어떻게 받지 않을 수 있었겠어요."], ["Slope instead of me.\"", "그러니 저 말고 슬로프 씨에게 물어보시는 편이 나을 것 같은데요.”"]], [["\"What was his letter about, Eleanor?\"", "“그 사람 편지가 무슨 이야기였니, 엘리너?”"]], [["\"I cannot tell you,\"", "“말씀드릴 수 없어요.”"], ["said she, \"because it was confidential.", "그녀가 말했다. “비밀로 하기로 한 것이니까요."], ["It was on business respecting a third person.\"", "제삼자에 관한 용무였어요.”"]], [["\"It was in no way personal to yourself then?\"", "“그럼 너 자신에 관한 이야기는 조금도 없었다는 거니?”"]], [["\"I won't exactly say that, Susan,\"", "“꼭 그렇다고는 하지 않겠어요, 언니.”"], ["said she, getting more and more angry at her sister's questions.", "언니의 질문에 점점 더 화가 치밀어 오르며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I must say it's rather singular,\" said Mrs.", "“글쎄, 나로서는 좀 이상하다고 말할 수밖에 없구나.”"], ["Grantly, affecting to laugh, \"that a young lady in your position should receive a letter from an unmarried gentleman of which she will not tell the contents and which she is ashamed to show to her sister.\"", "그랜틀리 부인이 웃는 시늉을 하며 말했다. “너 같은 처지의 젊은 부인이 혼인하지 않은 신사에게서 편지를 받고는, 그 내용을 말하려 하지도 않고 제 언니에게 보이기를 부끄러워하다니 말이다.”"]], [["\"I am not ashamed,\"", "“부끄러워하는 게 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor, blazing up.", "엘리너가 발끈해서 말했다."], ["\"I am not ashamed of anything in the matter; only I do not choose to be cross-examined as to my letters by anyone.\"", "“이 일에 부끄러울 것이라고는 하나도 없어요. 다만 제 편지를 두고 누구에게든 심문을 받고 싶지 않을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"Well, dear,\"", "“글쎄다.” 언니가 말했다."], ["said the other, \"I cannot but tell you that I do not think Mr. Slope a proper correspondent for you.\"", "“그래도 나는 슬로프 씨가 너와 편지를 주고받기에 마땅한 사람이라고는 생각하지 않는다고 말해 두어야겠구나.”"]], [["\"If he be ever so improper, how can I help his having written to me?", "“그 사람이 아무리 마땅치 않다 한들, 그 사람이 제게 편지를 쓴 것을 제가 어떻게 막겠어요?"], ["But you are all prejudiced against him to such an extent that that which would be kind and generous in another man is odious and impudent in him.", "하지만 다들 그 사람에게 얼마나 편견을 품고 있는지, 다른 사람이 하면 친절하고 너그러운 일이 그 사람이 하면 밉살스럽고 뻔뻔한 일이 되어 버리잖아요."], ["I hate a religion that teaches one to be so one-sided in one's charity.\"", "저는 사람더러 자비를 그렇게 한쪽으로만 베풀라고 가르치는 종교라면 질색이에요.”"]], [["\"I am sorry, Eleanor, that you hate the religion you find here, but surely you should remember that in such matters the archdeacon must know more of the world than you do.", "“엘리너, 네가 이 집에서 만나는 종교를 질색한다니 유감이구나. 하지만 그런 일에서는 부주교께서 너보다 세상을 더 많이 아신다는 것쯤은 기억해야지."], ["I don't ask you to respect or comply with me, although I am, unfortunately, so many years your senior; but surely, in such a matter as this, you might consent to be guided by the archdeacon.", "나야 안됐지만 네 손위이기는 해도, 나를 존중하라거나 내 말을 따르라고는 하지 않으마. 그래도 이런 일에서만은 부주교의 인도를 받겠다고 해 줄 수 있지 않겠니."], ["He is most anxious to be your friend, if you will let him.\"", "그분은 네가 허락만 한다면 네 벗이 되어 주시려고 무척 애쓰고 계신단다.”"]], [["\"In such a matter as what?\"", "“이런 일이라니 어떤 일 말인가요?”"], ["said Eleanor very testily.", "엘리너가 몹시 까칠하게 말했다."], ["\"Upon my word I don't know what this is all about.\"", "“정말이지 이게 다 무슨 이야기인지 저는 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"We all want you to drop Mr. Slope.\"", "“우리 모두는 네가 슬로프 씨를 끊었으면 한다.”"]], [["\"You all want me to be as illiberal as yourselves.", "“다들 저더러 언니들처럼 옹졸해지라는 거잖아요."], ["That I shall never be. I see no harm in Mr.", "저는 결코 그렇게 되지 않을 거예요."], ["Slope's acquaintance, and I shall not insult the man by telling him that I do.", "슬로프 씨와 아는 사이인 것에 아무 해로울 것이 없다고 저는 봐요. 해롭다고 그 사람에게 말해서 모욕을 줄 생각도 없고요."], ["He has thought it necessary to write to me, and I do not want the archdeacon's advice about the letter.", "그 사람은 제게 편지를 써야겠다고 여겼고, 저는 그 편지에 대해 부주교님의 조언을 바라지 않아요."], ["If I did, I would ask it.\"", "바랐다면 제가 여쭈었겠지요.”"]], [["\"Then, Eleanor, it is my duty to tell you,\"", "“그렇다면 엘리너, 이건 내가 네게 말해 두어야 할 일이구나.”"], ["and now she spoke with a tremendous gravity, \"that the archdeacon thinks that such a correspondence is disgraceful, and that he cannot allow it to go on in his house.\"", "이제 그녀는 무시무시하게 엄숙한 어조로 말했다. “부주교께서는 그런 편지 왕래가 수치스러운 일이며, 그것이 당신 집 안에서 이어지도록 두고 볼 수는 없다고 여기신단다.”"]], [["Eleanor's eyes flashed fire as she answered her sister, jumping up from her seat as she did so.", "엘리너는 눈에서 불꽃을 튀기며 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 언니에게 대답했다. “부주교님께 이렇게 전해 주세요."], ["\"You may tell the archdeacon that wherever I am I shall receive what letters I please and from whom I please.", "저는 어디에 있든 제가 원하는 편지를, 제가 원하는 사람에게서 받겠다고요. 그리고 ‘수치스럽다’는 말로 말하자면, 그랜틀리 박사가 저를 두고 그런 말을 쓰셨다면 그것은 사내답지 못하고 손님을 대접할 줄 모르는 처사예요.”"], ["And as for the word 'disgraceful,' if Dr.", "그러고는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["Grantly has used it of me, he has been unmanly and inhospitable,\" and she walked off to the door.", "“아버지가 식당에서 나오시거든 제 침실로 올라와 주십사고 여쭈어 주시면 고맙겠어요."], ["\"When Papa comes from the dining-room I will thank you to ask him to step up to my bedroom. I will show him Mr.", "아버지께는 슬로프 씨의 편지를 보여 드리겠어요."], ["Slope's letter, but I will show it to no one else.\"", "하지만 다른 누구에게도 보여 드리지 않겠어요.”"], ["And so saying, she retreated to her baby.", "그렇게 말하고 그녀는 제 아기에게로 물러갔다."]], [["She had no conception of the crime with which she was charged.", "그녀는 자기에게 씌워진 죄가 무엇인지 짐작조차 하지 못했다."], ["The idea that she could be thought by her friends to regard Mr. Slope as a lover had never flashed upon her.", "벗들이 자기가 슬로프 씨를 연인으로 여긴다고 생각할 수도 있으리라는 발상은 머리를 스친 적조차 없었다."], ["She conceived that they were all prejudiced and illiberal in their persecution of him, and therefore she would not join in the persecution, even though she greatly disliked the man.", "그녀는 그들이 편견에 사로잡혀 옹졸하게 그 사람을 몰아세우고 있다고 여겼으며, 그래서 자기가 그 사람을 몹시 싫어하면서도 그 몰이에 끼어들지 않으려 했던 것이다."]], [["Eleanor was very angry as she seated herself in a low chair by her open window at the foot of her child's bed.", "엘리너는 아이의 침대 발치, 열린 창가의 낮은 의자에 앉으면서 몹시 화가 나 있었다."], ["\"To dare to say I have disgraced myself,\"", "“내가 스스로를 욕되게 했다고 감히 말하다니.”"], ["she repeated to herself more than once.", "그녀는 몇 번이나 혼잣말로 되뇌었다."], ["\"How Papa can put up with that man's arrogance!", "“아버지는 저 사람의 오만을 어떻게 견디시는지!"], ["I will certainly not sit down to dinner in his house again unless he begs my pardon for that word.\" And then a thought struck her that Mr.", "그 말에 대해 용서를 빌기 전에는 다시는 저 사람 집 식탁에 앉지 않겠어.”"], ["Arabin might perchance hear of her \"disgraceful\" correspondence with Mr.", "그러다 문득 애러빈 씨가 자기와 슬로프 씨의 그 “수치스러운”"], ["Slope, and she turned crimson with pure vexation. Oh, if she had known the truth!", "편지 왕래에 대해 전해 들었을지도 모른다는 생각이 스치자, 그녀는 순전한 분함으로 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["If she could have conceived that Mr.", "아, 그녀가 진실을 알았더라면!"], ["Arabin had been informed as a fact that she was going to marry Mr. Slope!", "애러빈 씨가 그녀와 슬로프 씨의 혼인을 이미 기정사실로 전해 들었다는 것을 짐작할 수 있었더라면!"]], [["She had not been long in her room before her father joined her.", "그녀가 제 방에 들어온 지 얼마 되지 않아 아버지가 왔다."], ["As he left the drawing-room Mrs. Grantly took her husband into the recess of the window and told him how signally she had failed.", "그랜틀리 부인은 응접실을 나서면서 남편을 창가의 후미진 자리로 데려가 자기가 얼마나 보란 듯이 실패했는지를 일러 주었다."]], [["\"I will speak to her myself before I go to bed,\"", "“잠자리에 들기 전에 내가 직접 그 애에게 말하겠소.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Pray do no such thing,\"", "“제발 그러지 마세요.”"], ["said she; \"you can do no good and will only make an unseemly quarrel in the house.", "그녀가 말했다. “좋을 것도 없고 집 안에 보기 흉한 다툼만 일으킬 거예요."], ["You have no idea how headstrong she can be.\"", "그 애가 얼마나 고집이 센지 당신은 모르세요.”"]], [["The archdeacon declared that as to that he was quite indifferent.", "부주교는 그런 것쯤은 조금도 상관없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["He knew his duty and would do it. Mr.", "자기는 제 의무를 알고 있고 그것을 행하겠노라고 했다."], ["Harding was weak in the extreme in such matters.", "하딩 씨는 그런 일에 지나치게 물렀다."], ["He would not have it hereafter on his conscience that he had not done all that in him lay to prevent so disgraceful an alliance.", "그토록 수치스러운 혼인을 막기 위해 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 하지 않았다는 것을 훗날 양심에 얹어 두고 싶지 않았다."], ["It was in vain that Mrs. Grantly assured him that speaking to Eleanor angrily would only hasten such a crisis and render it certain, if at present there were any doubt.", "엘리너에게 성난 목소리로 말하는 것은 그런 위기를 앞당길 뿐이며, 지금 조금이라도 의심스러운 구석이 있다면 오히려 그것을 확실하게 만들 뿐이라고 그랜틀리 부인이 아무리 일러도 소용없었다."], ["He was angry, self-willed, and sore.", "그는 화가 나 있었고, 고집스러웠고, 속이 쓰렸다."], ["The fact that a lady of his household had received a letter from Mr. Slope had wounded his pride in the sorest place, and nothing could control him.", "제 집안의 부인이 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 사실이 그의 자존심을 가장 아픈 자리에서 찔러 놓았으니, 무엇으로도 그를 다스릴 수 없었다."]], [["Mr. Harding looked worn and woe-begone as he entered his daughter's room.", "하딩 씨는 딸의 방에 들어설 때 지치고 수심에 잠긴 얼굴이었다."], ["These sorrows worried him sadly.", "이런 근심들이 그를 슬프도록 괴롭혔다."], ["He felt that if they were continued, he must go to the wall in the manner so kindly prophesied to him by the chaplain.", "이런 일이 계속된다면 전속 사제가 그토록 친절하게 예언해 준 대로 자기가 벽으로 밀려나고 말리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다."], ["He knocked gently at his daughter's door, waited till he was distinctly bade to enter, and then appeared as though he and not she were the suspected criminal.", "그는 딸의 방문을 조용히 두드리고, 들어오라는 말이 또렷이 들릴 때까지 기다렸다가, 딸이 아니라 제가 혐의를 받는 죄인이기라도 한 듯한 모습으로 나타났다."]], [["Eleanor's arm was soon within his, and she had soon kissed his forehead and caressed him, not with joyous but with eager love.", "엘리너의 팔은 이내 아버지의 팔에 감겼고, 그녀는 곧 아버지의 이마에 입을 맞추며 기쁨이 아니라 간절함이 담긴 사랑으로 그를 어루만졌다."], ["\"Oh, Papa,\"", "“아버지,”"], ["she said, \"I do so want to speak to you.", "그녀가 말했다. “저 아버지께 꼭 드릴 말씀이 있어요."], ["They have been talking about me downstairs to-night--don't you know they have, Papa?\"", "오늘 밤 아래층에서 다들 제 이야기를 했지요. 그러셨죠, 아버지?”"]], [["Mr. Harding confessed with a sort of murmur that the archdeacon had been speaking of her.", "하딩 씨는 부주교가 그녀 이야기를 하기는 했다고 웅얼거리듯 털어놓았다."]], [["\"I shall hate Dr. Grantly soon--\"", "“저는 곧 그랜틀리 박사를 미워하게 될 거예요―”"]], [["\"Oh, my dear!\"", "“오, 얘야!”"]], [["\"Well, I shall.", "“정말 그럴 거예요."], ["I cannot help it.", "어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["He is so uncharitable, so unkind, so suspicious of everyone that does not worship himself: and then he is so monstrously arrogant to other people who have a right to their opinions as well as he has to his own.\"", "그분은 어찌나 각박하고 어찌나 매정하고, 자기를 떠받들지 않는 사람은 누구든 어찌나 의심하시는지요. 게다가 그분이 제 의견을 가질 권리가 있듯 남들도 제 의견을 가질 권리가 있는데, 남들에게 어찌나 무섭도록 오만하신지요.”"]], [["\"He is an earnest, eager man, my dear, but he never means to be unkind.\"", "“그분은 진지하고 열심인 사람이란다, 얘야. 하지만 매정하게 굴 뜻은 결코 없는 사람이야.”"]], [["\"He is unkind, Papa, most unkind.", "“매정하세요, 아버지. 더없이 매정하세요."], ["There, I got that letter from Mr. Slope before dinner.", "자, 만찬 전에 슬로프 씨에게서 받은 편지가 이것이에요."], ["It was you yourself who gave it to me.", "아버지가 손수 제게 주셨잖아요."], ["There, pray read it.", "자, 어서 읽어 보세요."], ["It is all for you.", "온통 아버지에 관한 거예요."], ["It should have been addressed to you.", "애초에 아버지 앞으로 왔어야 할 편지고요."], ["You know how they have been talking about it downstairs.", "아래층에서 다들 이 편지를 두고 어떤 이야기를 했는지 아시잖아요."], ["You know how they behaved to me at dinner.", "만찬 자리에서 저를 어떻게 대했는지도 아시잖아요."], ["And since dinner Susan has been preaching to me, till I could not remain in the room with her.", "게다가 만찬이 끝나고 수전 언니는 제가 그 방에 더 앉아 있을 수 없을 만큼 저를 붙들고 설교를 했어요. 읽어 보세요, 아버지."], ["Read it, Papa, and then say whether that is a letter that need make Dr. Grantly so outrageous.\"", "그러고 나서 이게 그랜틀리 박사를 그토록 노발대발하게 만들어야 할 편지인지 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding took his arm from his daughter's waist and slowly read the letter.", "하딩 씨는 딸의 허리에서 팔을 풀고 천천히 편지를 읽었다."], ["She expected to see his countenance lit with joy as he learnt that his path back to the hospital was made so smooth; but she was doomed to disappointment, as had once been the case before on a somewhat similar occasion.", "그녀는 아버지가 요양원으로 돌아가는 길이 이토록 평탄해졌음을 알고 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환해지리라 기대했다. 그러나 전에도 얼마쯤 비슷한 일에서 한 번 그랬듯이, 그녀는 실망할 운명이었다."], ["His first feeling was one of unmitigated disgust that Mr. Slope should have chosen to interfere in his behalf.", "아버지가 처음 느낀 것은 슬로프 씨가 감히 제 일에 끼어들기로 했다는 데 대한 걷잡을 수 없는 역겨움이었다."], ["He had been anxious to get back to the hospital, but he would have infinitely sooner resigned all pretensions to the place than have owed it in any manner to Mr.", "그는 요양원으로 돌아가기를 간절히 바라 왔으나, 그 자리를 슬로프 씨의 두둔에 조금이라도 빚지느니 그 자리에 대한 권리를 몽땅 내놓는 편이 한없이 나았다."], ["Slope's influence in his favour. Then he thoroughly disliked the tone of Mr.", "다음으로 그는 슬로프 씨의 편지가 풍기는 어조가 몹시 싫었다."], ["Slope's letter; it was unctuous, false, and unwholesome, like the man.", "그것은 그 사람과 똑같이 느끼하고 거짓되고 병들어 있었다."], ["He saw, which Eleanor had failed to see, that much more had been intended than was expressed.", "그는 엘리너가 보지 못한 것을 보았으니, 곧 적힌 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것이 뜻으로 담겨 있다는 사실이었다."], ["The appeal to Eleanor's pious labours as separate from his own grated sadly against his feelings as a father. And then, when he came to the \"darling boy\"", "엘리너의 경건한 수고를 제 것과 따로 떼어 들먹인 대목은 아비 된 그의 감정을 슬프도록 긁었다."], ["and the \"silken tresses,\" he slowly closed and folded the letter in despair.", "그리고 “귀염둥이 아들”과 “비단 같은 머릿단”에 이르자, 그는 절망 속에서 천천히 편지를 닫아 접었다."], ["It was impossible that Mr. Slope should so write unless he had been encouraged.", "부추김을 받지 않았다면 슬로프 씨가 그렇게 쓸 리가 없었다."], ["It was impossible Eleanor should have received such a letter, and have received it without annoyance, unless she were willing to encourage him.", "엘리너가 그를 부추길 마음이 없었다면 그런 편지를 받고도 언짢아하지 않을 리가 없었다."], ["So at least Mr. Harding argued to himself.", "적어도 하딩 씨는 스스로에게 그렇게 따져 나갔다."]], [["How hard it is to judge accurately of the feelings of others.", "남의 감정을 정확히 판단하기란 얼마나 어려운 일인가."], ["Mr. Harding, as he came to the close of the letter, in his heart condemned his daughter for indelicacy, and it made him miserable to do so.", "하딩 씨는 편지 끝에 이르면서 마음속으로 제 딸이 조심성 없다고 단죄했고, 그렇게 하는 것이 그를 비참하게 만들었다."], ["She was not responsible for what Mr. Slope might write.", "슬로프 씨가 무엇을 쓰든 그것은 딸의 책임이 아니다."], ["True.", "그렇다."], ["But then she expressed no disgust at it.", "그러나 딸은 그것에 아무런 역겨움도 드러내지 않았다."], ["She had rather expressed approval of the letter as a whole.", "오히려 그 편지를 전체로 보아 괜찮다고 여기는 듯한 기색이었다."], ["She had given it to him to read, as a vindication for herself and also for him.", "딸은 자신을 위해서, 또 아버지를 위해서 그것을 변호의 근거로 삼아 읽어 보라고 건넸다."], ["The father's spirits sank within him as he felt that he could not acquit her.", "아버지는 딸에게 무죄를 선고할 수 없다고 느끼면서 마음이 가라앉았다."]], [["And yet it was the true feminine delicacy of Eleanor's mind which brought on her this condemnation.", "그런데 이런 단죄를 그녀에게 불러온 것은 다름 아닌 엘리너의 참으로 여성다운 섬세함이었다."], ["Listen to me, ladies, and I beseech you to acquit her.", "부인들이여, 내 말을 들어 보시고 부디 그녀에게 무죄를 선고해 주시기를 청한다."], ["She thought of this man, this lover of whom she was so unconscious, exactly as her father did, exactly as the Grantlys did.", "그녀는 자기가 전혀 알아채지 못한 이 사내, 이 연인을 두고 제 아버지가 생각한 것과 똑같이, 그랜틀리 내외가 생각한 것과 똑같이 생각했다."], ["At least she esteemed him personally as they did.", "적어도 사람됨에 대한 평가만은 그들과 같았다."], ["But she believed him to be in the main an honest man, and one truly inclined to assist her father.", "다만 그녀는 그가 대체로 정직한 사람이며 제 아버지를 진심으로 돕고자 하는 사람이라고 믿었다."], ["She felt herself bound, after what had passed, to show this letter to Mr. Harding.", "그녀는 그런 일이 있었던 이상 이 편지를 하딩 씨에게 보여야 할 의무가 있다고 느꼈다."], ["She thought it necessary that he should know what Mr. Slope had to say.", "슬로프 씨가 무슨 말을 하는지 아버지가 아셔야 한다고 여겼다."], ["But she did not think it necessary to apologize for, or condemn, or even allude to the vulgarity of the man's tone, which arose, as does all vulgarity, from ignorance.", "그러나 그 사내의 어조가 지닌 천박함에 대해서는 굳이 변명하거나 나무라거나 언급할 필요가 있다고 생각지 않았다. 모든 천박함이 그렇듯 그것 또한 무지에서 나온 것이었다."], ["It was nauseous to her to have a man like Mr. Slope commenting on her personal attractions, and she did not think it necessary to dilate with her father upon what was nauseous.", "슬로프 씨 같은 사내가 제 용모를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은 그녀에게 구역질 나는 일이었으나, 구역질 나는 것을 아버지 앞에서 길게 늘어놓을 필요가 있다고는 생각지 않았다."], ["She never supposed they could disagree on such a subject.", "그런 문제에서 두 사람의 생각이 갈릴 수 있으리라고는 상상해 본 적도 없었다."], ["It would have been painful for her to point it out, painful for her to speak strongly against a man of whom, on the whole, she was anxious to think and speak well.", "그것을 짚어 보이는 일은 그녀에게 괴로웠을 것이고, 대체로 좋게 생각하고 좋게 말하고 싶은 사람을 두고 모질게 말하는 일도 괴로웠을 것이다."], ["In encountering such a man she had encountered what was disagreeable, as she might do in walking the streets.", "그런 사내와 마주친 것은 길을 걷다 마주칠 법한 불쾌한 일과 다르지 않았다."], ["But in such encounters she never thought it necessary to dwell on what disgusted her.", "그리고 그런 마주침에서 자기를 역겹게 한 것을 굳이 곱씹을 필요가 있다고 그녀는 한 번도 생각한 적이 없었다."]], [["And he, foolish, weak, loving man, would not say one word, though one word would have cleared up everything.", "그리고 그는, 어리석고 나약하고 사랑이 많은 그 사람은, 한마디면 모든 것이 밝혀졌을 텐데도 그 한마디를 하지 않으려 했다."], ["There would have been a deluge of tears, and in ten minutes everyone in the house would have understood how matters really were.", "눈물이 홍수처럼 쏟아졌을 것이고, 십 분이면 온 집안 사람이 사정이 실제로 어떠했는지 알게 되었을 것이다."], ["The father would have been delighted.", "아버지는 기뻐했을 것이다."], ["The sister would have kissed her sister and begged a thousand pardons.", "언니는 동생에게 입을 맞추며 천 번이라도 용서를 빌었을 것이다."], ["The archdeacon would have apologized and wondered, and raised his eyebrows, and gone to bed a happy man. And Mr.", "부주교는 사과하고 놀라워하며 눈썹을 치켜올린 뒤 행복한 사람이 되어 잠자리에 들었을 것이다."], ["Arabin--Mr. Arabin would have dreamt of Eleanor, have awoke in the morning with ideas of love, and retired to rest the next evening with schemes of marriage.", "그리고 애러빈 씨는, 애러빈 씨는 엘리너의 꿈을 꾸었을 것이고, 아침에 사랑의 상념과 함께 깨어났을 것이며, 이튿날 저녁에는 혼인의 계획을 품고 잠자리에 들었을 것이다."], ["But, alas, all this was not to be.", "그러나 아아, 이 모든 것은 그렇게 되지 못했다."]], [["Mr. Harding slowly folded the letter, handed it back to her, kissed her forehead, and bade God bless her.", "하딩 씨는 천천히 편지를 접어 딸에게 돌려주고, 그 이마에 입을 맞추고, 하느님의 축복을 빌어 주었다."], ["He then crept slowly away to his own room.", "그러고는 천천히 제 방으로 물러갔다."]], [["As soon as he had left the passage, another knock was given at Eleanor's door, and Mrs. Grantly's very demure own maid, entering on tiptoe, wanted to know would Mrs.", "그가 복도를 벗어나기가 무섭게 엘리너의 방문에 또 한 번 노크 소리가 났고, 그랜틀리 부인의 몹시 얌전한 몸종이 발끝으로 들어와, 언짢지 않으시다면 볼드 부인께서 부주교님의 서재로 오셔서 잠깐 2분만 말씀을 나누어 주실 수 있겠는지 여쭙는다고 했다."], ["Bold be so kind as to speak to the archdeacon for two minutes in the archdeacon's study, if not disagreeable. The archdeacon's compliments, and he wouldn't detain her two minutes.", "부주교님의 인사를 전하며, 2분도 채 붙들지 않으시겠다고 했다."]], [["Eleanor thought it was very disagreeable; she was tired and fagged and sick at heart; her present feelings towards Dr.", "엘리너는 몹시 언짢았다. 그녀는 지치고 기진하고 가슴이 아팠으며, 그랜틀리 박사를 향한 지금의 심정은 애정과는 도무지 거리가 멀었다."], ["Grantly were anything but those of affection. She was, however, no coward, and therefore promised to be in the study in five minutes.", "그러나 그녀는 겁쟁이가 아니었으므로 5분 뒤에 서재로 가겠다고 약속했다."], ["So she arranged her hair, tied on her cap, and went down with a palpitating heart.", "그리하여 그녀는 머리를 매만지고 모자를 매어 쓴 뒤, 두근거리는 가슴으로 아래층에 내려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX. A Serious Interview", "# 제29장. 심각한 면담"]], [["There are people who delight in serious interviews, especially when to them appertains the part of offering advice or administering rebuke, and perhaps the archdeacon was one of these.", "심각한 면담을 즐기는 사람들이 있다. 특히 그 자리에서 충고를 베풀거나 훈계를 내리는 몫이 자기에게 돌아올 때 그러하거니와, 어쩌면 부주교도 그런 사람 축에 들었을 것이다."], ["Yet on this occasion he did not prepare himself for the coming conversation with much anticipation of pleasure.", "그러나 이번만은 그도 다가올 대화를 앞두고 그리 즐거운 기대를 품지 못했다."], ["Whatever might be his faults he was not an inhospitable man, and he almost felt that he was sinning against hospitality in upbraiding Eleanor in his own house.", "그에게 어떤 결점이 있든 손님을 박대하는 사람은 아니었으므로, 제 집에서 엘리너를 나무라는 것이 손님 대접의 도리를 저버리는 죄처럼 느껴지기까지 했다."], ["Then, also, he was not quite sure that he would get the best of it.", "게다가 이 일에서 자기가 이기리라는 확신도 그리 서지 않았다."], ["His wife had told him that he decidedly would not, and he usually gave credit to what his wife said.", "아내는 결코 이기지 못하리라고 잘라 말했고, 그는 대개 아내의 말을 옳게 여기는 편이었다."], ["He was, however, so convinced of what he considered to be the impropriety of Eleanor's conduct, and so assured also of his own duty in trying to check it, that his conscience would not allow him to take his wife's advice and go to bed quietly.", "그러나 엘리너의 처신이 온당치 못하다는 확신이 워낙 굳었고 그것을 막아 보는 것이 제 의무라는 생각 또한 워낙 확고해서, 그의 양심은 아내의 충고를 받아들여 조용히 잠자리에 드는 것을 허락하지 않았다."]], [["Eleanor's face as she entered the room was not such as to reassure him.", "방으로 들어서는 엘리너의 얼굴은 그를 안심시켜 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다."], ["As a rule she was always mild in manner and gentle in conduct; but there was that in her eye which made it not an easy task to scold her.", "평소 엘리너는 태도가 온화하고 행동이 부드러운 사람이었으나, 그 눈에는 함부로 꾸짖기 어렵게 만드는 무엇이 있었다."], ["In truth she had been little used to scolding.", "실은 엘리너는 꾸중을 들어 본 일이 거의 없었다."], ["No one since her childhood had tried it but the archdeacon, and he had generally failed when he did try it.", "어린 시절 이후로 그에게 꾸중을 시도한 사람은 부주교뿐이었고, 부주교조차 시도할 때마다 대개 실패했다."], ["He had never done so since her marriage; and now, when he saw her quiet, easy step as she entered his room, he almost wished that he had taken his wife's advice.", "엘리너가 혼인한 뒤로는 한 번도 그런 일이 없었다. 그런데 이제 조용하고 편안한 걸음으로 방에 들어서는 처제를 보자, 그는 아내의 충고를 따를 것을 그랬다는 생각이 들 지경이었다."]], [["He began by apologizing for the trouble he was giving her.", "그는 수고를 끼쳐 미안하다는 말로 이야기를 시작했다."], ["She begged him not to mention it, assured him that walking downstairs was no trouble to her at all, and then took a seat and waited patiently for him to begin his attack.", "엘리너는 그런 말씀 마시라고, 아래층으로 내려오는 것쯤은 조금도 수고랄 것이 없다고 대꾸하고는, 자리에 앉아 그가 공격을 시작하기를 참을성 있게 기다렸다."]], [["\"My dear Eleanor,\"", "“엘리너,” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"I hope you believe me when I assure you that you have no sincerer friend than I am.\"", "“나만큼 처제를 진심으로 아끼는 벗은 없다고 내가 말하면, 그 말을 믿어 주리라 생각해요.”"], ["To this Eleanor answered nothing, and therefore he proceeded.", "이 말에 엘리너가 아무 대답도 하지 않았으므로 그는 말을 이었다."], ["\"If you had a brother of your own, I should not probably trouble you with what I am going to say.", "“처제에게 친오라비가 있었다면 아마 지금 하려는 이야기로 처제를 괴롭히지는 않았을 거예요."], ["But as it is I cannot but think that it must be a comfort to you to know that you have near you one who is as anxious for your welfare as any brother of your own could be.\"", "그러나 사정이 이러하니, 친오라비 못지않게 처제의 안녕을 걱정하는 사람이 가까이 있다는 것이 처제에게 위안이 되리라고 생각할 수밖에 없군요.”"]], [["\"I never had a brother,\"", "“저는 오라비가 있어 본 적이 없어요.”"], ["said she.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"I know you never had, and it is therefore that I speak to you.\"", "“없었다는 것을 나도 알아요. 그러니까 내가 이렇게 말을 꺼내는 것이지요.”"]], [["\"I never had a brother,\"", "“저는 오라비가 있어 본 적이 없어요.”"], ["she repeated, \"but I have hardly felt the want.", "엘리너가 되풀이했다. “하지만 아쉬웠던 적도 별로 없었어요."], ["Papa has been to me both father and brother.\"", "아버지가 제게는 아버지이자 오라비였으니까요.”"]], [["\"Your father is the fondest and most affectionate of men.", "“아버님은 세상에서 가장 자상하고 애정 깊은 분이지요."], ["But--\"", "그렇지만—”"]], [["\"He is--the fondest and most affectionate of men, and the best of counsellors.", "“그래요, 세상에서 가장 자상하고 애정 깊은 분이고, 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 조언자세요."], ["While he lives I can never want advice.\"", "아버지가 살아 계신 동안에는 제가 조언이 아쉬울 일은 없어요.”"]], [["This rather put the archdeacon out.", "이 말에 부주교는 다소 말문이 막혔다."], ["He could not exactly contradict what his sister-in-law said about her father, and yet he did not at all agree with her.", "처제가 제 아버지를 두고 하는 말을 대놓고 반박할 수는 없었으나, 그렇다고 그 말에 조금이라도 동의하는 것은 아니었다."], ["He wanted her to understand that he tendered his assistance because her father was a soft, good-natured gentleman not sufficiently knowing in the ways of the world; but he could not say this to her.", "그가 도움의 손길을 내미는 것은 그 아버지가 마음 여리고 사람 좋은 신사일 뿐 세상 물정에는 충분히 밝지 못한 까닭임을 처제가 알아주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그 말을 처제에게 할 수는 없었다."], ["So he had to rush into the subject-matter of his proffered counsel without any acknowledgement on her part that she could need it, or would be grateful for it.", "그리하여 그는 자기 충고가 필요하다거나 고맙겠다는 기색을 상대에게서 조금도 얻어 내지 못한 채, 곧바로 충고의 본론으로 뛰어들어야 했다."]], [["\"Susan tells me that you received a letter this evening from Mr. Slope.\"", "“수전에게 들으니 오늘 저녁에 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다더군요.”"]], [["\"Yes; Papa brought it in the brougham.", "“네, 아버지가 사륜마차 편에 가져다주셨어요."], ["Did he not tell you?\"", "아버지가 말씀 안 하시던가요?”"]], [["\"And Susan says that you objected to let her know what it was about.\"", "“그리고 수전 말로는 무슨 내용인지 알려 주기를 처제가 꺼렸다고 하던데요.”"]], [["\"I don't think she asked me.", "“언니가 제게 물어본 것 같지는 않아요."], ["But had she done so, I should not have told her.", "하지만 물었다 해도 말하지 않았을 거예요."], ["I don't think it nice to be asked about one's letters.", "남의 편지를 두고 물어보는 것은 좋지 않다고 생각해요."], ["If one wishes to show them, one does so without being asked.\"", "보여 주고 싶으면 묻지 않아도 보여 주는 법이니까요.”"]], [["\"True. Quite so.", "“옳은 말이에요."], ["What you say is quite true.", "그렇고말고요. 처제 말이 다 옳아요."], ["But is not the fact of your receiving letters from Mr.", "그렇지만 벗들에게 보이고 싶지 않은 편지를 슬로프 씨에게서 받는다는 사실 자체가 어느 정도의…"], ["Slope, which you do not wish to show to your friends, a circumstance which must excite some--some surprise--some suspicion--\"", "어느 정도의 놀라움을, 어느 정도의 의심을 불러일으킬 수밖에 없지 않겠어요…”"]], [["\"Suspicion!\"", "“의심이라니요!”"], ["said she, not speaking above her usual voice, speaking still in a soft, womanly tone but yet with indignation.", "엘리너가 말했다. 목소리를 평소보다 높이지도 않았고 여전히 부드럽고 여자다운 어조였으나, 거기에는 분노가 실려 있었다."], ["\"Suspicion!", "“의심이라니요!"], ["And who suspects me, and of what?\"", "누가 저를 의심하고, 또 무엇을 의심한다는 말씀이에요?”"], ["And then there was a pause, for the archdeacon was not quite ready to explain the ground of his suspicion.", "그러고는 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 부주교로서는 그 의심의 근거를 대뜸 설명할 채비가 되어 있지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"No, Dr. Grantly, I did not choose to show Mr.", "“네, 그랜틀리 박사님, 저는 슬로프 씨의 편지를 언니에게 보이고 싶지 않았어요."], ["Slope's letter to Susan. I could not show it to anyone till Papa had seen it.", "아버지가 보시기 전에는 누구에게도 보일 수 없었으니까요."], ["If you have any wish to read it now, you can do so,\"", "지금 읽어 보고 싶으시다면 그렇게 하세요.”"], ["and she handed the letter to him over the table.", "그러면서 엘리너는 탁자 너머로 편지를 그에게 건넸다."]], [["This was an amount of compliance which he had not at all expected, and which rather upset him in his tactics.", "이것은 그가 전혀 예상하지 못한 순순함이었고, 도리어 그의 작전을 흔들어 놓았다."], ["However, he took the letter, perused it carefully, and then refolding it, kept it on the table under his hand.", "어쨌든 그는 편지를 받아 꼼꼼히 읽고 나서 다시 접어 자기 손 밑 탁자 위에 두었다. 그가 보기에 그것은 거의 모든 점에서 사랑을 공언한 사내의 편지였다."], ["To him it appeared to be in almost every respect the letter of a declared lover; it seemed to corroborate his worst suspicions; and the fact of Eleanor's showing it to him was all but tantamount to a declaration on her part that it was her pleasure to receive love-letters from Mr.", "자기의 가장 나쁜 의심을 뒷받침해 주는 듯했다. 게다가 엘리너가 그것을 자기에게 보여 주었다는 사실은, 슬로프 씨에게서 연서를 받는 것이 제 기쁨이라고 스스로 선언한 것이나 진배없었다."], ["Slope. He almost entirely overlooked the real subject-matter of the epistle, so intent was he on the forthcoming courtship and marriage.", "그는 앞으로 벌어질 구애와 혼인에 온통 정신이 팔린 나머지, 그 서한의 진짜 용건은 거의 완전히 지나쳐 버렸다."]], [["\"I'll thank you to give it me back, if you please, Dr. Grantly.\"", "“이제 그 편지를 돌려주시면 고맙겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님.”"]], [["He took it in his hand and held it up, but made no immediate overture to return it.", "그는 편지를 손에 들어 쳐들었으나 곧바로 돌려주려는 기색은 보이지 않았다."], ["\"And Mr. Harding has seen this?\"", "“하딩 씨도 이 편지를 보셨나요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Of course he has,\"", "“물론 보셨지요.”"], ["said she; \"it was written that he might see it.", "엘리너가 말했다. “아버지가 보시라고 쓴 편지인걸요."], ["It refers solely to his business--of course I showed it to him.\"", "오로지 아버지 일만 적혀 있어요. 당연히 아버지께 보여 드렸지요.”"]], [["\"And, Eleanor, do you think that that is a proper letter for you--for a person in your condition--to receive from Mr. Slope?\"", "“그런데 엘리너, 이런 편지가 처제 같은 사람이, 처제 같은 처지에 있는 사람이 슬로프 씨에게서 받기에 온당한 편지라고 생각해요?”"]], [["\"Quite a proper letter,\"", "“아주 온당한 편지예요.”"], ["said she, speaking, perhaps, a little out of obstinacy, probably forgetting at the moment the objectionable mention of her silken curls.", "엘리너가 말했다. 어쩌면 순전한 고집에서 나온 말이었고, 아마 그 순간에는 제 비단결 같은 곱슬머리를 들먹인 그 못마땅한 대목을 잊고 있었을 것이다."]], [["\"Then, Eleanor, it is my duty to tell you that I wholly differ from you.\"", "“그렇다면 엘리너, 나는 처제와 생각이 전혀 다르다는 것을 말해 두는 것이 내 의무겠군요.”"]], [["\"So I suppose,\"", "“그러시겠지요.”"], ["said she, instigated now by sheer opposition and determination not to succumb.", "엘리너가 말했다. 이제는 순전한 반발심과 지지 않겠다는 오기가 그를 부추기고 있었다."], ["\"You think Mr. Slope is a messenger direct from Satan.", "“박사님은 슬로프 씨가 사탄이 곧장 보낸 사자라고 생각하시지요."], ["I think he is an industrious, well-meaning clergyman.", "저는 그분이 부지런하고 선의를 지닌 성직자라고 생각해요."], ["It's a pity that we differ as we do.", "이렇게 생각이 다르다니 안타까운 일이에요."], ["But, as we do differ, we had probably better not talk about it.\"", "하지만 이렇게 다른 이상, 이 이야기는 하지 않는 편이 나을 거예요.”"]], [["Here Eleanor undoubtedly put herself in the wrong.", "여기서 엘리너는 분명 잘못을 저질렀다."], ["She might probably have refused to talk to Dr. Grantly on the matter in dispute without any impropriety, but, having consented to listen to him, she had no business to tell him that he regarded Mr.", "다투는 사안을 두고 그랜틀리 박사와 이야기하기를 거절했더라도 무례랄 것은 없었겠지만, 이미 그의 말을 듣기로 승낙한 이상 그가 슬로프 씨를 악한 자의 사자로 여긴다고 면박할 자격은 없었다."], ["Slope as an emissary from the evil one; nor was she justified in praising Mr. Slope, seeing that in her heart of hearts she did not think well of him.", "또 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 슬로프 씨를 좋게 여기지 않으면서 그를 추어올린 것도 정당하지 못했다."], ["She was, however, wounded in spirit, and angry, and bitter.", "그러나 엘리너는 마음에 상처를 입었고, 화가 났고, 속이 쓰렸다."], ["She had been subjected to contumely and cross-questioning and ill-usage through the whole evening.", "저녁 내내 모욕과 심문과 박대를 당한 터였다."], ["No one, not even Mr. Arabin, not even her father, had been kind to her.", "애러빈 씨조차, 아버지조차 그에게 다정하지 않았다."], ["All this she attributed to the prejudice and conceit of the archdeacon, and therefore she resolved to set no bounds to her antagonism to him.", "이 모든 것을 엘리너는 부주교의 편견과 자만 탓으로 돌렸고, 그리하여 그에 대한 적대에 아무 한계도 두지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["She would neither give nor take quarter.", "봐주지도 않고 봐주기를 바라지도 않을 참이었다."], ["He had greatly presumed in daring to question her about her correspondence, and she was determined to show that she thought so.", "감히 제 서신을 두고 캐물었으니 이만저만 주제넘은 짓이 아니었고, 자기가 그렇게 여긴다는 것을 똑똑히 보여 주기로 작정했다."]], [["\"Eleanor, you are forgetting yourself,\"", "“엘리너, 지금 처제는 도리를 잊고 있어요.”"], ["said he, looking very sternly at her.", "그가 몹시 준엄한 눈으로 엘리너를 보며 말했다."], ["\"Otherwise you would never tell me that I conceive any man to be a messenger from Satan.\"", "“그렇지 않고서야 내가 어떤 사람을 사탄의 사자로 여긴다는 말을 할 리가 없지요.”"]], [["\"But you do,\" said she.", "“하지만 정말 그러시잖아요.”"], ["\"Nothing is too bad for him.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["Give me that letter, if you please;\"", "“그분에게라면 어떤 험담도 지나치지 않다고 여기시지요. 그 편지를 이리 주세요.”"], ["and she stretched out her hand and took it from him.", "그러면서 손을 뻗어 그의 손에서 편지를 가져갔다. “그분은 아버지를 돕느라 최선을 다하셨어요."], ["\"He has been doing his best to serve Papa, doing more than any of Papa's friends could do; and yet, because he is the chaplain of a bishop whom you don't like, you speak of him as though he had no right to the usage of a gentleman.\"", "아버지의 어느 벗도 하지 못한 일을 하셨고요. 그런데도 박사님이 싫어하시는 주교의 전속 사제라는 이유만으로, 그분에게는 신사로 대접받을 자격조차 없다는 듯이 말씀하시는군요.”"]], [["\"He has done nothing for your father.\"", "“그 사람은 처제 아버님을 위해 한 일이 아무것도 없어요.”"]], [["\"I believe that he has done a great deal; and, as far as I am concerned, I am grateful to him.", "“저는 그분이 아주 많은 일을 하셨다고 믿어요. 그리고 제게 관한 한, 저는 그분께 고마워하고 있어요."], ["Nothing that you can say can prevent my being so.", "박사님이 무슨 말씀을 하시든 그 마음을 막지는 못하실 거예요."], ["I judge people by their acts, and his, as far as I can see them, are good.\"", "저는 사람을 그 행실로 판단하는데, 제가 볼 수 있는 한 그분의 행실은 훌륭해요.”"], ["She then paused for a moment.", "그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었다."], ["\"If you have nothing further to say, I shall be obliged by being permitted to say good night--I am very tired.\"", "“더 하실 말씀이 없으시면 이만 안녕히 주무시라는 인사를 드리게 해 주시면 고맙겠어요. 몹시 피곤하거든요.”"]], [["Dr. Grantly had, as he thought, done his best to be gracious to his sister-in-law.", "그랜틀리 박사는 제 딴에는 처제에게 너그럽게 대하려고 최선을 다한 터였다."], ["He had endeavoured not to be harsh to her, and had striven to pluck the sting from his rebuke.", "모질게 굴지 않으려 애썼고, 제 훈계에서 가시를 뽑아내려고 힘썼다."], ["But he did not intend that she should leave him without hearing him.", "그러나 자기 말을 다 듣지도 않은 채 처제를 보내 줄 생각은 없었다."]], [["\"I have something to say, Eleanor, and I fear I must trouble you to hear it.", "“할 말이 있어요, 엘리너. 미안하지만 그것만은 들어 주어야겠어요."], ["You profess that it is quite proper that you should receive from Mr. Slope such letters as that you have in your hand.", "처제는 손에 든 그런 편지를 슬로프 씨에게서 받는 것이 아주 온당하다고 말하는군요."], ["Susan and I think very differently.", "수전과 나는 생각이 아주 다릅니다."], ["You are, of course, your own mistress, and much as we both must grieve should anything separate you from us, we have no power to prevent you from taking steps which may lead to such a separation.", "물론 처제는 제 몸의 주인이고, 무엇이든 처제를 우리에게서 갈라놓는 일이 생긴다면 우리 두 사람으로서는 몹시 슬프겠지만, 그런 이별로 이어질 수 있는 걸음을 처제가 내딛는 것을 막을 힘은 우리에게 없어요."], ["If you are so wilful as to reject the counsel of your friends, you must be allowed to cater for yourself.", "처제가 그토록 고집스럽게 벗들의 충고를 물리친다면, 제 앞가림은 제가 하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에요."], ["But, Eleanor, I may at any rate ask you this.", "그렇지만 엘리너, 이것만은 물어도 되겠지요."], ["Is it worth your while to break away from all those you have loved--from all who love you--for the sake of Mr. Slope?\"", "슬로프 씨 하나 때문에 처제가 사랑해 온 모든 사람에게서, 처제를 사랑하는 모든 사람에게서 등을 돌릴 만한 값어치가 있나요?”"]], [["\"I don't know what you mean, Dr.", "“무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요, 그랜틀리 박사님."], ["Grantly; I don't know what you're talking about.", "무슨 이야기를 하고 계신지 모르겠어요."], ["I don't want to break away from anybody.\"", "저는 누구에게서도 등을 돌리고 싶지 않아요.”"]], [["\"But you will do so if you connect yourself with Mr. Slope.", "“하지만 슬로프 씨와 인연을 맺는다면 그렇게 되고 말아요."], ["Eleanor, I must speak out to you.", "엘리너, 나는 터놓고 말해야겠어요."], ["You must choose between your sister and myself and our friends, and Mr. Slope and his friends.", "처제는 언니와 나와 우리 벗들 쪽이든, 슬로프 씨와 그 벗들 쪽이든 어느 한쪽을 골라야 해요."], ["I say nothing of your father, as you may probably understand his feelings better than I do.\"", "아버님에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요. 그분의 심경이야 나보다 처제가 더 잘 알 테니까요.”"]], [["\"What do you mean, Dr. Grantly?", "“무슨 말씀이세요, 그랜틀리 박사님?"], ["What am I to understand?", "제가 무엇을 알아야 한다는 거예요?"], ["I never heard such wicked prejudice in my life.\"", "평생 이렇게 고약한 편견은 들어 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["\"It is no prejudice, Eleanor.", "“편견이 아니에요, 엘리너."], ["I have known the world longer than you have done.", "나는 처제보다 세상을 오래 겪었어요."], ["Mr. Slope is altogether beneath you.", "슬로프 씨는 어느 모로 보나 처제에게 어울리지 않는 사람이에요."], ["You ought to know and feel that he is so.", "처제도 그것을 알고 느껴야 마땅해요."], ["Pray--pray think of this before it is too late.\"", "부디, 부디 너무 늦기 전에 이 일을 생각해 봐요.”"]], [["\"Too late!\"", "“너무 늦기 전이라니요!”"]], [["\"Or if you will not believe me, ask Susan; you cannot think she is prejudiced against you.", "“내 말을 믿지 못하겠거든 수전에게 물어봐요. 언니가 처제에게 편견을 품었다고야 생각하지 못하겠지요."], ["Or even consult your father--he is not prejudiced against you.", "아니면 아버님께 여쭤 봐도 좋아요. 그분이야 처제에게 편견이 있을 리 없으니까."], ["Ask Mr. Arabin--\"", "애러빈 씨에게 물어봐도—”"]], [["\"You haven't spoken to Mr. Arabin about this!\"", "“이 일을 애러빈 씨에게 이야기하신 건 아니겠지요!”"], ["said she, jumping up and standing before him.", "엘리너가 벌떡 일어나 그의 앞에 서며 말했다."]], [["\"Eleanor, all the world in and about Barchester will be speaking of it soon.\"", "“엘리너, 머지않아 바체스터 안팎의 온 세상이 이 이야기를 하게 될 거예요.”"]], [["\"But have you spoken to Mr. Arabin about me and Mr. Slope?\"", "“하지만 저와 슬로프 씨 이야기를 애러빈 씨에게 하셨어요?”"]], [["\"Certainly I have, and he quite agrees with me.\"", "“물론 했지요. 그리고 그분도 내 생각에 전적으로 동의하더군요.”"]], [["\"Agrees with what?\" said she.", "“무엇에 동의한다는 거예요?”"], ["\"I think you are trying to drive me mad.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “저를 미치게 만드시려는 것 같군요.”"]], [["\"He agrees with me and Susan that it is quite impossible you should be received at Plumstead as Mrs. Slope.\"", "“처제가 슬로프 부인이 되어 플럼스테드에 손님으로 오는 일은 도저히 있을 수 없다는 데에, 그분도 나와 수전과 같은 생각이더군요.”"]], [["Not being favourites with the tragic muse, we do not dare to attempt any description of Eleanor's face when she first heard the name of Mrs. Slope pronounced as that which would or should or might at some time appertain to herself.", "우리는 비극의 뮤즈에게 사랑받는 처지가 못 되므로, 슬로프 부인이라는 이름이 언젠가 제게 붙게 되리라거나 붙어야 한다거나 붙을지도 모른다는 뜻으로 발음되는 것을 처음 들었을 때 엘리너의 얼굴이 어떠했는지는 감히 묘사해 보려 하지 않겠다."], ["The look, such as it was, Dr. Grantly did not soon forget.", "그것이 어떤 얼굴이었든, 그랜틀리 박사는 그 표정을 쉬이 잊지 못했다."], ["For a moment or two she could find no words to express her deep anger and deep disgust; indeed, at this conjuncture, words did not come to her very freely.", "한두 순간 엘리너는 그 깊은 분노와 깊은 혐오를 담아낼 말을 찾지 못했다. 실로 이 대목에서 말이라는 것이 그에게 그리 수월하게 나와 주지 않았다."]], [["\"How dare you be so impertinent?\"", "“어쩌면 그렇게 무례하실 수가 있어요?”"], ["at last she said, and then she hurried out of the room without giving the archdeacon the opportunity of uttering another word.", "마침내 엘리너는 이렇게 말하고, 부주교에게 한마디 더 할 틈도 주지 않은 채 방을 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["It was with difficulty she contained herself till she reached her own room; and then, locking the door, she threw herself on her bed and sobbed as though her heart would break.", "제 방에 이를 때까지 마음을 다잡기가 힘겨웠다. 그러고는 문을 잠그고 침대에 몸을 던져 가슴이 미어지도록 흐느껴 울었다."]], [["But even yet she had no conception of the truth.", "그러나 엘리너는 아직도 진상을 조금도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["She had no idea that her father and her sister had for days past conceived in sober earnest the idea that she was going to marry this man.", "아버지와 언니가 며칠 전부터 자기가 그 사내와 혼인하려 한다는 생각을 진지하게 품어 왔다는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["She did not even then believe that the archdeacon thought that she would do so.", "부주교조차 자기가 그러리라고 정말로 여긴다고는 그때까지도 믿지 않았다."], ["By some manoeuvre of her brain she attributed the origin of the accusation to Mr.", "머릿속에서 무슨 조화가 일어났는지, 엘리너는 그 혐의의 발단을 애러빈 씨에게 돌렸다."], ["Arabin, and as she did so her anger against him was excessive, and the vexation of her spirit almost unendurable.", "그러자 그에 대한 노여움이 걷잡을 수 없이 커졌고, 마음의 괴로움은 견디기 어려울 지경이 되었다."], ["She could not bring herself to think that the charge was made seriously.", "그 힐난이 진지하게 나온 것이라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없었다."], ["It appeared to her most probable that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin had talked over her objectionable acquaintance with Mr.", "엘리너가 보기에 가장 그럴듯한 것은 이러했다."], ["Slope; that Mr. Arabin in his jeering, sarcastic way had suggested the odious match as being the severest way of treating with contumely her acquaintance with his enemy; and that the archdeacon, taking the idea from him, thought proper to punish her by the allusion.", "부주교와 애러빈 씨가 슬로프 씨와의 못마땅한 교분을 두고 이야기를 나누었고, 애러빈 씨가 그 특유의 비웃는 듯한 빈정거림으로 제 원수와의 교분을 가장 매섭게 모욕하는 방법으로서 그 끔찍한 혼인을 들먹였으며, 부주교가 그 착상을 그에게서 얻어다 자기를 벌주기에 알맞다고 여겨 그 말을 꺼냈다는 것이다."], ["The whole night she lay awake thinking of what had been said, and this appeared to be the most probable solution.", "밤새도록 엘리너는 잠들지 못한 채 오간 말들을 곱씹었고, 이것이 가장 그럴듯한 답으로 보였다."]], [["But the reflexion that Mr. Arabin should have in any way mentioned her name in connexion with that of Mr.", "그러나 애러빈 씨가 어떤 식으로든 제 이름을 슬로프 씨의 이름과 나란히 입에 올렸으리라는 생각은 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Slope was overpowering; and the spiteful ill-nature of the archdeacon in repeating the charge to her made her wish to leave his house almost before the day had broken.", "게다가 그 힐난을 굳이 제게 되풀이해 들려준 부주교의 짓궂은 심술 때문에, 엘리너는 날이 밝기도 전에 이 집을 떠나고 싶은 심정이었다. 한 가지는 분명했다."], ["One thing was certain: nothing should make her stay there beyond the following morning, and nothing should make her sit down to breakfast in company with Dr. Grantly.", "무슨 일이 있어도 이튿날 아침을 넘겨 이 집에 머물지 않겠으며, 무슨 일이 있어도 그랜틀리 박사와 마주 앉아 아침을 먹지 않겠다는 것이었다."], ["When she thought of the man whose name had been linked with her own, she cried from sheer disgust.", "제 이름과 나란히 놓인 그 사내를 떠올리면 순전한 역겨움에 눈물이 났다."], ["It was only because she would be thus disgusted, thus pained and shocked and cut to the quick, that the archdeacon had spoken the horrid word.", "자기가 이렇게 역겨워하고 이렇게 아파하고 충격을 받고 뼈아프게 찔릴 줄 알았기에 부주교가 그 끔찍한 말을 입에 올린 것이었다."], ["He wanted to make her quarrel with Mr. Slope, and therefore he had outraged her by his abominable vulgarity.", "그는 자기를 슬로프 씨와 다투게 만들고 싶었고, 그래서 그 역겨운 상스러움으로 자기를 모욕한 것이었다."], ["She determined that at any rate he should know that she appreciated it.", "엘리너는 적어도 자기가 그 뜻을 똑똑히 알아들었다는 것만은 그에게 알려 주기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Nor was the archdeacon a bit better satisfied with the result of his serious interview than was Eleanor.", "부주교라고 해서 이 심각한 면담의 결과에 엘리너보다 조금이라도 더 만족한 것은 아니었다."], ["He gathered from it, as indeed he could hardly fail to do, that she was very angry with him, but he thought that she was thus angry, not because she was suspected of an intention to marry Mr.", "그는 이 면담에서, 실로 모르려야 모를 수가 없었으므로, 처제가 자기에게 몹시 화가 났다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Slope, but because such an intention was imputed to her as a crime. Dr.", "다만 처제가 화를 내는 까닭은 슬로프 씨와 혼인할 뜻을 품었다고 의심받아서가 아니라 그런 뜻이 죄인 양 씌워졌기 때문이라고 생각했다."], ["Grantly regarded this supposed union with disgust, but it never occurred to him that Eleanor was outraged because she looked at it exactly in the same light.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그 짝지음을 역겹게 여겼으나, 엘리너 역시 그것을 똑같은 눈으로 바라보았기에 모욕감을 느낀 것이라는 데에는 생각이 미치지 못했다."]], [["He returned to his wife, vexed and somewhat disconsolate, but nevertheless confirmed in his wrath against his sister-in-law.", "그는 속이 상하고 다소 낙심한 채 아내에게로 돌아갔으나, 그럼에도 처제에 대한 노여움은 오히려 굳어져 있었다."], ["\"Her whole behaviour,\"", "“그 애 태도가 하나부터 열까지,”"], ["said he, \"has been most objectionable.", "그가 말했다. “도무지 못마땅하기 짝이 없구려."], ["She handed me his love-letter to read as though she were proud of it.", "그자의 연서를 마치 자랑스럽다는 듯이 내게 건네 읽으라고 합디다."], ["And she is proud of it.", "실제로 자랑스러워하고 있소."], ["She is proud of having this slavering, greedy man at her feet.", "침이나 흘리는 그 탐욕스러운 사내가 제 발밑에 엎드린 것을 자랑스러워하는 게요."], ["She will throw herself and John Bold's money into his lap; she will ruin her boy, disgrace her father and you, and be a wretched miserable woman.\"", "그 애는 제 몸과 존 볼드의 돈을 그자의 무릎에 던져 줄 것이고, 제 아들을 망치고 제 아비와 당신을 욕보이고, 저는 비참하고 가엾은 여자가 되고 말 것이오.”"]], [["His spouse, who was sitting at her toilet-table, continued her avocations, making no answer to all this.", "화장대 앞에 앉아 있던 아내는 하던 일을 계속했을 뿐 이 모든 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["She had known that the archdeacon would gain nothing by interfering, but she was too charitable to provoke him by saying so while he was in such deep sorrow.", "부주교가 끼어들어 봐야 얻을 것이 없으리라는 것을 그는 진작 알고 있었으나, 남편이 저토록 깊은 시름에 잠겨 있는 마당에 그 말을 꺼내 자극할 만큼 매정하지는 않았다."]], [["\"This comes of a man making such a will as that of Bold's,\"", "“볼드가 그따위 유언을 남기니 이런 일이 생기는 것이오.”"], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Eleanor is no more fitted to be trusted with such an amount of money in her own hands than is a charity-school girl.\"", "“엘리너에게 그만한 돈을 제 손에 맡겨 두는 것은 자선 학교 계집아이에게 맡기는 것이나 다를 바가 없소.” 그랜틀리 부인은 여전히 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["Still Mrs. Grantly made no reply.", "“그러나 나는 내 도리를 다했소."], ["\"But I have done my duty; I can do nothing further.", "더 할 수 있는 일이 없구려."], ["I have told her plainly that she cannot be allowed to form a link of connexion between me and that man.", "나와 그자 사이를 잇는 고리 노릇을 하도록 내버려 둘 수는 없다고 그 애에게 분명히 말해 두었소."], ["From henceforward it will not be in my power to make her welcome at Plumstead.", "이제부터는 그 애를 플럼스테드에서 반가이 맞을 수가 없게 되었소."], ["I cannot have Mr. Slope's love-letters coming here.", "슬로프 씨의 연서가 이 집으로 날아드는 것을 두고 볼 수는 없단 말이오."], ["Susan, I think you had better let her understand that, as her mind on this subject seems to be irrevocably fixed, it will be better for all parties that she should return to Barchester.\"", "수전, 이 문제에 대한 그 애 마음이 돌이킬 수 없이 굳은 듯하니, 모두를 위해서 바체스터로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 당신이 일러 주는 게 좋겠소.”"]], [["Now Mrs. Grantly was angry with Eleanor--nearly as angry as her husband--but she had no idea of turning her sister out of the house.", "그런데 그랜틀리 부인은 엘리너에게 화가 나 있기는 했고, 남편에 못지않게 화가 나 있었으나, 동생을 집에서 내보낼 생각은 조금도 없었다."], ["She therefore at length spoke out and explained to the archdeacon in her own mild, seducing way that he was fuming and fussing and fretting himself very unnecessarily.", "그리하여 마침내 입을 열어, 특유의 온화하고 사람을 홀리는 말투로 부주교에게 설명하기를, 당신은 지금 아무 까닭 없이 씩씩거리고 부산을 떨며 애를 태우고 있다고 했다."], ["She declared that things, if left alone, would arrange themselves much better than he could arrange them, and at last succeeded in inducing him to go to bed in a somewhat less inhospitable state of mind.", "내버려 두면 일이란 당신이 주무르는 것보다 훨씬 잘 풀리게 마련이라고 잘라 말했고, 마침내 남편을 어르는 데 성공하여 손님을 박대하려는 마음이 다소 누그러진 채로 잠자리에 들게 만들었다."]], [["On the following morning Eleanor's maid was commissioned to send word into the dining-room that her mistress was not well enough to attend prayers and that she would breakfast in her own room.", "이튿날 아침, 엘리너의 하녀는 마님이 기도에 나올 만큼 몸이 좋지 않아 자기 방에서 아침을 들겠다는 말을 식당으로 전하라는 분부를 받았다."], ["Here she was visited by her father, and declared to him her intention of returning immediately to Barchester.", "방으로 아버지가 찾아왔고, 엘리너는 당장 바체스터로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 아버지에게 밝혔다."], ["He was hardly surprised by the announcement.", "하딩 씨는 그 말에 별로 놀라지 않았다."], ["All the household seemed to be aware that something had gone wrong.", "온 집안이 무언가 잘못되었다는 것을 아는 듯했다."], ["Everyone walked about with subdued feet, and people's shoes seemed to creak more than usual.", "다들 발소리를 죽이고 다녔건만 사람들의 구두는 평소보다 더 삐걱거리는 것 같았다."], ["There was a look of conscious intelligence on the faces of the women, and the men attempted, but in vain, to converse as though nothing were the matter.", "여자들의 얼굴에는 무언가 알고 있다는 기색이 어렸고, 남자들은 아무 일도 없다는 듯이 이야기를 나누어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다."], ["All this had weighed heavily on the heart of Mr. Harding, and when Eleanor told him that her immediate return to Barchester was a necessity, he merely sighed piteously and said that he would be ready to accompany her.", "이 모든 것이 하딩 씨의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌러 온 터라, 엘리너가 당장 바체스터로 돌아가야만 하겠다고 말했을 때 그는 그저 가엾게 한숨을 쉬며 자기도 함께 갈 채비를 하겠다고만 했다."]], [["But here she objected strenuously.", "그러나 엘리너는 이 대목에서 완강히 반대했다."], ["She had a great wish, she said, to go alone; a great desire that it might be seen that her father was not implicated in her quarrel with Dr.", "혼자 가고 싶은 마음이 간절하다고, 아버지는 그랜틀리 박사와의 다툼에 얽혀 있지 않다는 것이 남들 눈에 보였으면 하는 마음이 간절하다고 했다. 하딩 씨는 마침내 뜻을 굽혔다."], ["Grantly. To this at last he gave way; but not a word passed between them about Mr.", "그러나 두 사람 사이에 슬로프 씨에 관한 말은 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Slope--not a word was said, not a question asked as to the serious interview on the preceding evening.", "전날 저녁의 그 심각한 면담에 대해서도 한마디 말이 없었고 한마디 물음도 없었다."], ["There was, indeed, very little confidence between them, though neither of them knew why it should be so.", "실로 두 사람 사이에는 서로 터놓는 마음이 거의 없었다. 어째서 그래야 하는지는 둘 다 알지 못했다."], ["Eleanor once asked him whether he would not call upon the bishop, but he answered rather tartly that he did not know--he did not think he should, but he could not say just at present.", "엘리너가 한번 주교를 찾아뵙지 않겠느냐고 물었으나, 아버지는 다소 퉁명스럽게 모르겠다고, 그러지 않을 성싶다고, 지금 당장은 뭐라 말할 수 없다고 대답했다."], ["And so they parted.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["Each was miserably anxious for some show of affection, for some return of confidence, for some sign of the feeling that usually bound them together.", "저마다 애정의 표시를, 다시 터놓는 마음을, 늘 두 사람을 이어 주던 그 정의 어떤 기미를 애타게 갈망했다. 그러나 아무것도 주어지지 않았다."], ["But none was given. The father could not bring himself to question his daughter about her supposed lover, and the daughter would not sully her mouth by repeating the odious word with which Dr.", "아버지는 차마 딸에게 그 연인이라는 사람에 대해 물어볼 수 없었고, 딸은 그랜틀리 박사가 제 노여움을 돋우며 쓴 그 역겨운 말을 되풀이해 제 입을 더럽히려 하지 않았다."], ["Grantly had roused her wrath. And so they parted.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["There was some trouble in arranging the method of Eleanor's return.", "엘리너가 돌아가는 방법을 정하는 데에도 얼마간 말썽이 있었다."], ["She begged her father to send for a post-chaise, but when Mrs. Grantly heard of this, she objected strongly.", "엘리너는 아버지에게 역마차를 불러 달라고 청했으나, 그랜틀리 부인이 그 말을 듣고 강하게 반대했다."], ["If Eleanor would go away in dudgeon with the archdeacon, why should she let all the servants and all the neighbourhood know that she had done so?", "엘리너가 부주교에게 골이 나서 떠날 참이라면, 어째서 하인들과 온 이웃에게 그 사실을 알려야 한단 말인가?"], ["So at last Eleanor consented to make use of the Plumstead carriage, and as the archdeacon had gone out immediately after breakfast and was not to return till dinner-time, she also consented to postpone her journey till after lunch, and to join the family at that time.", "그리하여 마침내 엘리너는 플럼스테드의 마차를 쓰기로 승낙했고, 부주교가 아침 식사 뒤에 곧바로 나가 저녁 식사 때까지 돌아오지 않을 것이었으므로 길 떠나기를 점심 뒤로 미루고 그때 식구들과 자리를 함께하는 데에도 승낙했다."], ["As to the subject of the quarrel not a word was said by anyone.", "다툼의 내용에 대해서는 누구도 한마디 하지 않았다."], ["The affair of the carriage was arranged by Mr.", "마차 문제는 두 부인 사이에서 메르쿠리우스 노릇을 한 하딩 씨가 처리했다."], ["Harding, who acted as Mercury between the two ladies; they, when they met, kissed each other very lovingly and then sat down each to her crochet work as though nothing was amiss in all the world.", "두 사람은 만나자 아주 다정하게 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 세상에 아무 탈도 없다는 듯이 저마다 코바늘뜨개질감을 들고 앉았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXX. Another Love Scene", "# 제30장. 또 하나의 사랑의 장면"]], [["But there was another visitor at the rectory whose feelings in this unfortunate matter must be somewhat strictly analysed. Mr.", "그런데 목사관에는 이 딱한 사태를 두고 그 심경을 다소 엄밀하게 따져 보아야 할 손님이 또 한 사람 있었다."], ["Arabin had heard from his friend of the probability of Eleanor's marriage with Mr. Slope with amazement, but not with incredulity.", "애러빈 씨는 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 결혼할지도 모른다는 이야기를 벗에게서 듣고 경악했으나, 믿지 못한 것은 아니었다."], ["It has been said that he was not in love with Eleanor, and up to this period this certainly had been true.", "그가 엘리너를 사랑하고 있지 않았다는 말은 앞서 해 두었고, 이 시점까지는 그것이 분명 사실이었다."], ["But as soon as he heard that she loved someone else, he began to be very fond of her himself.", "그러나 그녀가 다른 누군가를 사랑한다는 말을 듣자마자, 그는 스스로 그녀를 몹시 좋아하게 되었다. 그녀를 아내로 삼고 싶다고 마음을 정한 것은 아니었다."], ["He did not make up his mind that he wished to have her for his wife; he had never thought of her, and did not now think of her, in connexion with himself; but he experienced an inward, indefinable feeling of deep regret, a gnawing sorrow, an unconquerable depression of spirits, and also a species of self-abasement that he--he, Mr.", "그는 그녀를 자신과 결부시켜 생각해 본 적이 없었고 지금도 그러지 않았다. 다만 그는 내면에서 형언할 수 없는 깊은 회한을, 갉아먹는 듯한 슬픔을, 도무지 이겨 낼 수 없는 의기소침을 느꼈고, 아울러 일종의 자기 비하를 느꼈다."], ["Arabin--had not done something to prevent that other he, that vile he whom he so thoroughly despised, from carrying off this sweet prize.", "자기가, 다름 아닌 애러빈 씨 자신이, 그토록 철저히 경멸하는 저 사내가 이 어여쁜 상을 채어 가는 것을 막으려고 아무것도 하지 않았다는 자책이었다."]], [["Whatever man may have reached the age of forty unmarried without knowing something of such feelings must have been very successful or else very cold-hearted.", "마흔이 되도록 결혼하지 않은 남자가 이런 감정을 조금도 모른 채 지내 왔다면, 그는 매우 성공한 사람이었거나 아니면 매우 냉정한 사람이었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Arabin had never thought of trimming the sails of his bark so that he might sail as convoy to this rich argosy.", "애러빈 씨는 이 값진 상선단의 호위선으로 따라붙으려고 제 작은 배의 돛을 손볼 생각 따위는 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["He had seen that Mrs. Bold was beautiful, but he had not dreamt of making her beauty his own.", "볼드 부인이 아름답다는 것은 보아서 알았으나, 그 아름다움을 제 것으로 삼겠다는 꿈은 꾸어 본 적이 없었다."], ["He knew that Mrs. Bold was rich, but he had had no more idea of appropriating her wealth than that of Dr.", "볼드 부인이 부유하다는 것도 알았으나, 그 재산을 제 것으로 삼겠다는 생각은 그랜틀리 박사만큼이나 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Grantly. He had discovered that Mrs.", "볼드 부인이 총명하고 마음이 따뜻하고 상냥하고 분별 있다는 것을, 요컨대 남자가 제 아내에게 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다."], ["Bold was intelligent, warm-hearted, agreeable, sensible, all in fact that a man could wish his wife to be; but the higher were her attractions, the greater her claims to consideration, the less had he imagined that he might possibly become the possessor of them.", "그러나 그녀의 매력이 높으면 높을수록, 그녀가 존중받아 마땅한 까닭이 크면 클수록, 자기가 그것을 차지하는 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 상상은 그만큼 줄어들었다."], ["Such had been his instinct rather than his thoughts, so humble and so diffident.", "그것은 생각이라기보다 본능이었으니, 그만큼 그는 겸손하고 자신이 없었다."], ["Now his diffidence was to be rewarded by his seeing this woman, whose beauty was to his eyes perfect, whose wealth was such as to have deterred him from thinking of her, whose widowhood would have silenced him had he not been so deterred, by his seeing her become the prey of--Obadiah Slope!", "이제 그 소심함이 받을 보상이란, 제 눈에 그 아름다움이 더할 나위 없이 완벽하고, 그 재산이 도리어 그로 하여금 마음을 접게 만들었으며, 그렇게 접지만 않았더라면 그 미망인 신세 때문에라도 그의 입을 다물게 했을 이 여인이 하필 오바디아 슬로프의 먹이가 되는 것을 지켜보는 일이었다."]], [["On the morning of Mrs. Bold's departure he got on his horse to ride over to St.", "볼드 부인이 떠나는 날 아침, 그는 세인트 이월드로 가려고 말에 올랐다."], ["Ewold's. As he rode he kept muttering to himself a line from Van Artevelde,", "말을 몰면서 그는 반 아르테벨데의 한 구절을 계속 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["How little flattering is woman's love.", "여자의 사랑이란 어찌 그리 우쭐할 것이 못 되는가."]], [["And then he strove to recall his mind and to think of other affairs--his parish, his college, his creed--but his thoughts would revert to Mr. Slope and the Flemish chieftain.", "그러고 나서 그는 마음을 다잡아 다른 일들을, 제 교구를, 제 칼리지를, 제 신조를 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 그의 생각은 자꾸만 슬로프 씨와 저 플랑드르 수령에게로 되돌아갔다."]], [["When we think upon it, How little flattering is woman's love, Given commonly to whosoe'er is nearest And propped with most advantage.", "가만히 헤아려 보면, 여자의 사랑이란 어찌 그리 우쭐할 것이 못 되는가. 흔히 가장 가까이 있는 자에게, 가장 유리하게 떠받쳐진 자에게 주어지는 것을."]], [["It was not that Mrs.", "볼드 부인이 자기 아닌 다른 누구와 결혼한다는 것이 문제가 아니었다."], ["Bold should marry anyone but him--he had not put himself forward as a suitor--but that she should marry Mr.", "그는 구혼자로 나선 적조차 없었으니까. 문제는 그녀가 하필 슬로프 씨와 결혼한다는 것이었다."], ["Slope; and so he repeated over again--", "그리하여 그는 다시 한번 되뇌었다."]], [["Outward grace Nor inward light is needful--day by day Men wanting both are mated with the best And loftiest of God's feminine creation, Whose love takes no distinction but of gender, And ridicules the very name of choice.", "겉으로 드러난 기품도, 안에 깃든 빛도 필요치 않다. 날마다 그 둘을 다 갖추지 못한 사내들이 하느님께서 지으신 여성 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 고결한 이들과 짝을 이룬다. 그 사랑은 남녀의 구별 말고는 아무 구별도 두지 않으며, 선택이라는 말 자체를 비웃는다."]], [["And so he went on, troubled much in his mind.", "그렇게 그는 마음이 몹시 어지러운 채로 길을 갔다."]], [["He had but an uneasy ride of it that morning, and little good did he do at St. Ewold's.", "그날 아침 그의 말길은 편치 않았고, 세인트 이월드에서도 이렇다 할 일을 하지 못했다."]], [["The necessary alterations in his house were being fast completed, and he walked through the rooms, and went up and down the stairs, and rambled through the garden, but he could not wake himself to much interest about them.", "그의 집에 필요한 개축은 빠르게 마무리되어 가고 있었고, 그는 방들을 거닐고 계단을 오르내리고 정원을 어슬렁거렸으나, 그것들에 대해 이렇다 할 흥미를 불러일으킬 수가 없었다."], ["He stood still at every window to look out and think upon Mr. Slope.", "창마다 걸음을 멈추고 서서 밖을 내다보며 슬로프 씨를 생각했다."], ["At almost every window he had before stood and chatted with Eleanor. She and Mrs.", "거의 모든 창가에서 그는 예전에 엘리너와 나란히 서서 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Grantly had been there continually; and while Mrs. Grantly had been giving orders, and seeing that orders had been complied with, he and Eleanor had conversed on all things appertaining to a clergyman's profession.", "그녀와 그랜틀리 부인이 노상 이곳에 와 있었고, 그랜틀리 부인이 이런저런 지시를 내리고 그 지시가 제대로 이행되었는지 살피는 동안, 그와 엘리너는 성직자의 직분에 딸린 온갖 이야기를 나누었던 것이다."], ["He thought how often he had laid down the law to her and how sweetly she had borne with his somewhat dictatorial decrees.", "그는 자기가 그녀에게 얼마나 자주 법도를 늘어놓았던가를, 그리고 다소 독단적인 그 선언들을 그녀가 얼마나 곱게 받아 주었던가를 생각했다. 총명하게 귀 기울이던 모습, 부드러우면서도 재빠르던 대꾸, 교회에 관한 모든 일에, 그에게 관계된 모든 일에 보이던 관심이 떠올랐다."], ["He remembered her listening intelligence, her gentle but quick replies, her interest in all that concerned the church, in all that concerned him; and then he struck his riding-whip against the window-sill and declared to himself that it was impossible that Eleanor Bold should marry Mr. Slope.", "그러고는 승마용 채찍으로 창틀을 내리치며, 엘리너 볼드가 슬로프 씨와 결혼한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 스스로에게 단언했다."]], [["And yet he did not really believe, as he should have done, that it was impossible.", "그러면서도 그는 마땅히 그래야 했을 만큼 그것이 정말로 있을 수 없는 일이라고 믿지는 못했다."], ["He should have known her well enough to feel that it was truly impossible.", "그는 그것이 참으로 있을 수 없는 일임을 느낄 만큼 그녀를 잘 알았어야 했다."], ["He should have been aware that Eleanor had that within her which would surely protect her from such degradation.", "엘리너에게는 그런 전락으로부터 스스로를 지켜 줄 무엇인가가 안에 있음을 알았어야 했다."], ["But he, like so many others, was deficient in confidence in woman.", "그러나 그도 다른 숱한 남자들처럼 여자를 믿는 마음이 모자랐다."], ["He said to himself over and over again that it was impossible that Eleanor Bold should become Mrs.", "그는 엘리너 볼드가 슬로프 부인이 된다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 몇 번이고 되뇌면서도, 그녀가 그리하리라고 믿었다."], ["Slope, and yet he believed that she would do so. And so he rambled about, and could do and think of nothing.", "그리하여 그는 이리저리 서성거릴 뿐 아무것도 하지 못하고 아무 생각도 하지 못했다."], ["He was thoroughly uncomfortable, thoroughly ill at ease, cross with himself and everybody else, and feeding in his heart on animosity towards Mr. Slope.", "그는 철저히 불편했고 철저히 마음이 편치 않았으며, 자신에게도 남들에게도 짜증이 났고, 마음속으로는 슬로프 씨를 향한 적의를 곱씹고 있었다."], ["This was not as it should be, as he knew and felt, but he could not help himself. In truth Mr.", "이것이 마땅한 모습이 아님을 그도 알고 느꼈으나 어찌할 도리가 없었다."], ["Arabin was now in love with Mrs. Bold, though ignorant of the fact himself.", "실인즉 애러빈 씨는 이제 볼드 부인을 사랑하고 있었으되, 정작 본인은 그 사실을 몰랐다."], ["He was in love and, though forty years old, was in love without being aware of it.", "그는 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 마흔 살이나 되어서 그것을 자각하지도 못한 채 사랑에 빠져 있었다."], ["He fumed and fretted and did not know what was the matter, as a youth might do at one-and-twenty. And so having done no good at St.", "그는 스물한 살 젊은이가 그러하듯 속을 끓이고 안달하면서도 무엇이 문제인지 알지 못했다."], ["Ewold's, he rode back much earlier than was usual with him, instigated by some inward, unacknowledged hope that he might see Mrs. Bold before she left.", "그리하여 세인트 이월드에서 아무 일도 하지 못한 채, 볼드 부인이 떠나기 전에 한 번 볼 수 있을지도 모른다는 어떤 내밀하고 인정되지 않은 바람에 떠밀려, 그는 여느 때보다 훨씬 이른 시각에 말을 돌려 돌아왔다."]], [["Eleanor had not passed a pleasant morning.", "엘리너도 유쾌한 아침을 보내지는 못했다."], ["She was irritated with everyone, and not least with herself.", "그녀는 모두에게 짜증이 나 있었고, 무엇보다 자기 자신에게 짜증이 나 있었다."], ["She felt that she had been hardly used, but she felt also that she had not played her own cards well.", "자기가 부당한 대접을 받았다고 느꼈으나, 한편으로는 제 패를 잘 놀리지 못했다는 것도 느꼈다."], ["She should have held herself so far above suspicion as to have received her sister's innuendoes and the archdeacon's lecture with indifference.", "그녀는 언니의 비아냥과 부주교의 훈계를 무심하게 받아넘길 만큼 스스로를 의심 위에 두었어야 했다."], ["She had not done this, but had shown herself angry and sore, and was now ashamed of her own petulance, yet unable to discontinue it.", "그러나 그러지 못하고 성내고 아파하는 모습을 보이고 말았으니, 이제 와 제 앙칼짐이 부끄러웠으면서도 그것을 그만두지 못했다."]], [["The greater part of the morning she had spent alone, but after awhile her father joined her.", "아침나절 대부분을 그녀는 혼자 보냈으나, 얼마 뒤 아버지가 곁에 왔다."], ["He had fully made up his mind that, come what come might, nothing should separate him from his younger daughter.", "그는 무슨 일이 있더라도 작은딸과 갈라서지는 않겠다고 단단히 마음을 굳힌 터였다."], ["It was a hard task for him to reconcile himself to the idea of seeing her at the head of Mr. Slope's table, but he got through it.", "딸이 슬로프 씨의 식탁 상석에 앉아 있는 모습을 떠올리며 마음을 다잡기란 그에게 힘든 일이었으나, 그는 끝내 그것을 해냈다."], ["Mr. Slope, as he argued to himself, was a respectable man and a clergyman, and he, as Eleanor's father, had no right even to endeavour to prevent her from marrying such a one.", "슬로프 씨도 어엿한 사람이고 성직자이니 엘리너의 아버지로서 딸이 그런 사람과 결혼하는 것을 막으려 들 권리조차 자기에게는 없다고 그는 스스로에게 따져 보았다."], ["He longed to tell her how he had determined to prefer her to all the world, how he was prepared to admit that she was not wrong, how thoroughly he differed from Dr.", "그는 자기가 세상 무엇보다 딸을 먼저 두기로 정했다는 것을, 딸이 그르지 않다고 인정할 준비가 되어 있다는 것을, 그랜틀리 박사와는 생각이 전혀 다르다는 것을 딸에게 말해 주고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["Grantly; but he could not bring himself to mention Mr. Slope's name.", "그러나 슬로프 씨의 이름만은 차마 입에 올릴 수가 없었다."], ["There was yet a chance that they were all wrong in their surmise, and being thus in doubt, he could not bring himself to speak openly to her on the subject.", "그들 모두의 짐작이 어긋난 것일 가능성도 아직 남아 있었고, 그렇게 반신반의하는 터라 그 문제를 딸에게 터놓고 이야기할 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["He was sitting with her in the drawing-room, with his arm round her waist, saying every now and then some little soft words of affection and working hard with his imaginary fiddle-bow, when Mr. Arabin entered the room.", "그가 응접실에서 딸의 허리에 팔을 두르고 앉아 이따금 다정한 말을 나직이 건네며 보이지 않는 활을 열심히 켜고 있을 때, 애러빈 씨가 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He immediately got up, and the two made some trite remarks to each other, neither thinking of what he was saying, while Eleanor kept her seat on the sofa, mute and moody.", "하딩 씨는 곧바로 일어섰고, 두 사람은 서로 진부한 말을 몇 마디 주고받았는데 둘 다 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 헤아리지 않았다. 그동안 엘리너는 소파에 앉은 채 입을 다물고 시무룩해 있었다."], ["Mr. Arabin was included in the list of those against whom her anger was excited.", "애러빈 씨도 그녀의 분노가 향한 사람들의 명단에 들어 있었다."], ["He, too, had dared to talk about her acquaintance with Mr. Slope; he, too, had dared to blame her for not making an enemy of his enemy.", "그 역시 감히 그녀와 슬로프 씨의 교분을 입에 올렸고, 그 역시 감히 제 원수를 원수로 삼지 않는다고 그녀를 나무랐던 것이다."], ["She had not intended to see him before her departure, and was now but little inclined to be gracious.", "그녀는 떠나기 전에 그를 볼 생각이 없었고, 지금도 상냥하게 굴 마음이 별로 없었다."]], [["There was a feeling through the whole house that something was wrong.", "집안 전체에 무언가 잘못되었다는 기운이 감돌았다."], ["Mr. Arabin, when he saw Eleanor, could not succeed in looking or in speaking as though he knew nothing of all this.", "애러빈 씨는 엘리너를 보자, 이 모든 일을 전혀 모르는 사람인 양 표정을 짓거나 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["He could not be cheerful and positive and contradictory with her, as was his wont.", "여느 때처럼 그녀에게 쾌활하고 단호하고 짐짓 어깃장을 놓는 태도를 취할 수가 없었다."], ["He had not been two minutes in the room before he felt that he had done wrong to return; and the moment he heard her voice, he thoroughly wished himself back at St. Ewold's.", "방에 들어선 지 이 분도 되지 않아 그는 돌아온 것이 잘못이었다고 느꼈고, 그녀의 목소리를 듣는 순간 세인트 이월드로 돌아가 있었으면 하고 간절히 바랐다."], ["Why, indeed, should he have wished to have aught further to say to the future wife of Mr. Slope?", "대체 무엇 하러 슬로프 씨의 장차 아내가 될 사람에게 무슨 말을 더 건네고 싶어 했더란 말인가?"]], [["\"I am sorry to hear that you are to leave us so soon,\"", "“이렇게 빨리 떠나신다니 섭섭합니다.”"], ["said he, striving in vain to use his ordinary voice.", "그가 여느 때의 목소리를 내려 애썼으나 헛일이었다."], ["In answer to this she muttered something about the necessity of her being in Barchester, and betook herself most industriously to her crochet work.", "이 말에 그녀는 바체스터에 가 있어야 한다는 사정을 무어라 우물거리고는, 유난히 부지런히 코바늘 뜨개질에 몰두했다."]], [["Then there was a little more trite conversation between Mr. Arabin and Mr.", "그러고는 애러빈 씨와 하딩 씨 사이에 진부한 이야기가 조금 더 오갔다."], ["Harding--trite, and hard, and vapid, and senseless.", "진부하고 딱딱하고 맥없고 알맹이 없는 이야기였다."], ["Neither of them had anything to say to the other, and yet neither at such a moment liked to remain silent. At last Mr.", "둘 다 서로에게 할 말이 없었으나, 그렇다고 이런 순간에 잠자코 있는 것도 둘 다 내키지 않았다."], ["Harding, taking advantage of a pause, escaped out of the room, and Eleanor and Mr. Arabin were left together.", "마침내 하딩 씨는 말이 끊긴 틈을 타 방을 빠져나갔고, 엘리너와 애러빈 씨만 남게 되었다."]], [["\"Your going will be a great break-up to our party,\"", "“부인이 가시면 우리 모임도 크게 헐거워지겠지요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["She again muttered something which was all but inaudible, but kept her eyes fixed upon her work.", "그녀는 다시 무어라 중얼거렸으나 거의 알아들을 수 없었고, 눈은 뜨개질감에 붙박아 두었다."]], [["\"We have had a very pleasant month here,\"", "“여기서 아주 즐거운 한 달을 보냈습니다.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; \"at least I have; and I am sorry it should be so soon over.\"", "“적어도 저는 그랬습니다. 이렇게 빨리 끝나 버린다니 아쉽군요.”"]], [["\"I have already been from home longer than I intended,\"", "“저는 생각했던 것보다 이미 오래 집을 비웠어요.”"], ["said she, \"and it is time that I should return.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “이제는 돌아가야 할 때예요.”"]], [["\"Well, pleasant hours and pleasant days must come to an end.", "“글쎄요, 즐거운 시간도 즐거운 날들도 끝이 나게 마련이지요."], ["It is a pity that so few of them are pleasant; or perhaps, rather--\"", "그중 즐거운 날이 그리 적다는 것이 딱한 일이고요. 아니, 어쩌면 그보다는—”"]], [["\"It is a pity, certainly, that men and women do so much to destroy the pleasantness of their days,\"", "“사람들이 제 나날의 즐거움을 망가뜨리는 데 그토록 애쓴다는 것이야말로 딱한 일이지요.”"], ["said she, interrupting him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 자르며 말했다."], ["\"It is a pity that there should be so little charity abroad.\"", "“세상에 너그러움이 그리도 적다는 것이 딱한 일이고요.”"]], [["\"Charity should begin at home,\"", "“너그러움은 제 집안에서부터 시작되어야 하지요.”"], ["said he, and he was proceeding to explain that he as a clergyman could not be what she would call charitable at the expense of those principles which he considered it his duty to teach, when he remembered that it would be worse than vain to argue on such a matter with the future wife of Mr.", "그가 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 성직자로서 마땅히 가르쳐야 할 의무라 여기는 원칙들을 희생해 가면서까지 그녀가 말하는 그런 너그러움을 베풀 수는 없다고 설명하려다가, 슬로프 씨의 장차 아내가 될 사람과 그런 문제로 다투어 봐야 부질없다 못해 해롭기만 하리라는 데 생각이 미쳤다."], ["Slope. \"But you are just leaving us,\"", "“그러나 이제 곧 떠나실 텐데,”"], ["he continued, \"and I will not weary your last hour with another lecture.", "그가 말을 이었다. “마지막 한 시간까지 또 훈계로 지치게 해 드리지는 않겠습니다."], ["As it is, I fear I have given you too many.\"", "그러잖아도 훈계를 너무 많이 해 드린 것 같아 걱정입니다.”"]], [["\"You should practise as well as preach, Mr. Arabin.\"", "“설교하시는 대로 몸소 행하셔야지요, 애러빈 씨.”"]], [["\"Undoubtedly I should.", "“분명 그래야지요."], ["So should we all.", "우리 모두가 그래야 합니다."], ["All of us who presume to teach are bound to do our utmost towards fulfilling our own lessons.", "감히 남을 가르치겠다고 나서는 사람은 누구나 제가 한 말을 스스로 지키려 온 힘을 다할 의무가 있습니다."], ["I thoroughly allow my deficiency in doing so, but I do not quite know now to what you allude.", "제가 그 점에서 모자란다는 것은 기꺼이 인정합니다만, 지금 무엇을 두고 하시는 말씀인지는 잘 모르겠군요."], ["Have you any special reason for telling me now that I should practise as well as preach?\"", "하필 지금 저더러 설교하는 대로 행하라고 말씀하시는 데에는 무슨 특별한 까닭이라도 있습니까?”"]], [["Eleanor made no answer.", "엘리너는 대답하지 않았다."], ["She longed to let him know the cause of her anger, to upbraid him for speaking of her disrespectfully, and then at last to forgive him, and so part friends.", "그녀는 제가 화난 까닭을 그에게 알리고, 자기를 함부로 말했다고 그를 나무라고, 그런 다음 마침내 용서하여 벗으로 헤어지고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."], ["She felt that she would be unhappy to leave him in her present frame of mind, but yet she could hardly bring herself to speak to him of Mr. Slope.", "지금 같은 심경으로 그를 떠나보내면 마음이 편치 않으리라는 것을 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 그에게 슬로프 씨 이야기를 꺼내기란 좀처럼 어려웠다."], ["And how could she allude to the innuendo thrown out by the archdeacon, and thrown out, as she believed, at the instigation of Mr. Arabin?", "게다가 부주교가 던진 그 비아냥을, 그것도 그녀가 믿기로는 애러빈 씨가 부추겨서 나온 그 비아냥을 어떻게 입에 올린단 말인가?"], ["She wanted to make him know that he was wrong, to make him aware that he had ill-treated her, in order that the sweetness of her forgiveness might be enhanced.", "그녀는 그가 틀렸음을 알게 하고, 자기를 부당하게 대했음을 깨닫게 하여, 그리하여 용서의 달콤함이 한층 커지기를 바랐다."], ["She felt that she liked him too well to be contented to part with him in displeasure, yet she could not get over her deep displeasure without some explanation, some acknowledgement on his part, some assurance that he would never again so sin against her.", "노여운 채로 그와 헤어지고 말기에는 그를 너무 좋아한다는 것을 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 그 깊은 노여움을 그의 해명 없이, 그의 인정 없이, 다시는 자기에게 그런 잘못을 저지르지 않겠다는 다짐 없이 넘길 수도 없었다."]], [["\"Why do you tell me that I should practise what I preach?\"", "“어찌하여 제가 설교하는 대로 행해야 한다고 말씀하십니까?”"], ["continued he.", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"All men should do so.\"", "“모든 사람이 그래야 하니까요.”"]], [["\"Certainly.", "“그렇지요."], ["That is as it were understood and acknowledged.", "그건 말하자면 다들 알고 인정하는 바입니다."], ["But you do not say so to all men, or to all clergymen.", "그러나 부인께서는 모든 사람에게, 또 모든 성직자에게 그렇게 말씀하시지는 않습니다."], ["The advice, good as it is, is not given except in allusion to some special deficiency.", "그 충고는 옳은 말이기는 하지만, 어떤 특정한 결함을 두고서가 아니면 나오지 않는 법이지요."], ["If you will tell me my special deficiency, I will endeavour to profit by the advice.\"", "제 결함이 무엇인지 말씀해 주시면, 그 충고로 나아지도록 힘써 보겠습니다.”"]], [["She paused for awhile and then, looking full in his face, she said, \"You are not bold enough, Mr.", "그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들이더니, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다."], ["Arabin, to speak out to me openly and plainly, and yet you expect me, a woman, to speak openly to you.", "“애러빈 씨, 당신은 제게 터놓고 분명하게 말할 만큼 대담하지 못하시면서, 여자인 저더러는 당신께 터놓고 말하기를 바라시는군요."], ["Why did you speak calumny of me to Dr. Grantly behind my back?\"", "어찌하여 제 등 뒤에서 그랜틀리 박사께 제 험담을 하셨습니까?”"]], [["\"Calumny!\"", "“험담이라니요!”"], ["said he, and his whole face became suffused with blood.", "그가 말했고, 그의 얼굴이 온통 붉게 물들었다."], ["\"What calumny?", "“무슨 험담 말입니까?"], ["If I have spoken calumny of you, I will beg your pardon, and his to whom I spoke it, and God's pardon also.", "제가 부인의 험담을 했다면, 부인께 용서를 빌고, 그 말을 들은 분께도 용서를 빌고, 하느님께도 용서를 빌겠습니다."], ["But what calumny have I spoken of you to Dr. Grantly?\"", "그러나 제가 그랜틀리 박사께 부인의 무슨 험담을 했다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["She also blushed deeply.", "그녀 역시 얼굴이 붉게 달아올랐다."], ["She could not bring herself to ask him whether he had not spoken of her as another man's wife.", "자기를 다른 남자의 아내라고 말하지 않았느냐고 차마 물을 수가 없었다."], ["\"You know that best yourself,\"", "“그건 당신이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"But I ask you as a man of honour, if you have not spoken of me as you would not have spoken of your own sister--or rather I will not ask you,\"", "“다만 명예를 아는 분으로서 여쭙습니다. 당신은 제 이야기를, 당신의 친누이를 두고서는 결코 하지 않았을 방식으로 하지 않으셨습니까?"], ["she continued, finding that he did not immediately answer her.", "아니, 여쭙지 않겠어요.” 그가 얼른 대답하지 않는 것을 보고 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"I will not put you to the necessity of answering such a question.", "“그런 물음에 답해야 하는 처지로 몰지는 않겠습니다."], ["Dr. Grantly has told me what you said.\"", "당신이 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지는 그랜틀리 박사께 들었으니까요.”"]], [["\"Dr. Grantly certainly asked me for my advice, and I gave it.", "“그랜틀리 박사께서 제게 조언을 구하신 것은 사실이고, 저는 그것을 드렸습니다."], ["He asked me--\"", "그분이 제게 물으시기를—”"]], [["\"I know he did, Mr. Arabin.", "“압니다, 애러빈 씨."], ["He asked you whether he would be doing right to receive me at Plumstead if I continued my acquaintance with a gentleman who happens to be personally disagreeable to yourself and to him.\"", "당신과 그분에게 개인적으로 마뜩잖은 어떤 신사와 제가 계속 알고 지낸다면 저를 플럼스테드에 맞아들이는 것이 옳겠느냐고 물으셨겠지요.”"]], [["\"You are mistaken, Mrs.", "“오해십니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold. I have no personal knowledge of Mr.", "저는 슬로프 씨를 개인적으로 알지 못합니다."], ["Slope; I never met him in my life.\"", "평생 한 번도 만난 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"You are not the less individually hostile to him.", "“그렇다고 개인적으로 그분에게 적의를 품고 계시지 않은 것은 아니지요."], ["It is not for me to question the propriety of your enmity, but I had a right to expect that my name should not have been mixed up in your hostilities.", "당신의 적의가 온당한지 아닌지를 따지는 것은 제 몫이 아닙니다만, 제 이름만은 당신의 반목에 끌려 들어가지 않기를 바랄 권리가 제게 있었습니다."], ["This has been done, and been done by you in a manner the most injurious and the most distressing to me as a woman.", "그런데 그 일이 벌어졌고, 그것도 여자인 제게 가장 해롭고 가장 괴로운 방식으로 다름 아닌 당신에 의해 벌어졌습니다."], ["I must confess, Mr. Arabin, that from you I expected a different sort of usage.\"", "솔직히 말씀드리자면 애러빈 씨, 저는 당신께만은 다른 대접을 기대했습니다.”"]], [["As she spoke she with difficulty restrained her tears--but she did restrain them.", "이렇게 말하는 동안 그녀는 힘겹게 눈물을 참았다. 그러나 끝내 참아 냈다."], ["Had she given way and sobbed aloud, as in such cases a woman should do, he would have melted at once, implored her pardon, perhaps knelt at her feet and declared his love.", "만약 그녀가 무너져 이런 경우에 여자라면 마땅히 그러하듯 소리 내어 흐느꼈더라면, 그는 그 자리에서 마음이 녹아 용서를 빌었을 것이고, 어쩌면 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 사랑을 고백했을 것이다."], ["Everything would have been explained, and Eleanor would have gone back to Barchester with a contented mind.", "모든 것이 밝혀졌을 터이고, 엘리너는 흡족한 마음으로 바체스터로 돌아갔을 터이다."], ["How easily would she have forgiven and forgotten the archdeacon's suspicions had she but heard the whole truth from Mr.", "애러빈 씨에게서 온전한 진실을 듣기만 했다면 부주교의 의심쯤은 얼마나 쉽게 용서하고 잊어버렸겠는가."], ["Arabin. But then where would have been my novel?", "그러나 그랬더라면 내 소설은 어찌 되었겠는가?"], ["She did not cry, and Mr. Arabin did not melt.", "그녀는 울지 않았고, 애러빈 씨는 녹지 않았다."]], [["\"You do me an injustice,\"", "“저를 부당하게 보시는군요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"My advice was asked by Dr. Grantly, and I was obliged to give it.\"", "“그랜틀리 박사께서 제 조언을 구하셨으니, 저는 드리지 않을 수 없었습니다.”"]], [["\"Dr. Grantly has been most officious, most impertinent.", "“그랜틀리 박사는 지나치게 참견이 심하고 지나치게 무례하셨어요."], ["I have as complete a right to form my acquaintance as he has to form his.", "그분이 그분의 지인을 사귈 권리가 있듯, 저도 제 지인을 사귈 권리가 온전히 있습니다."], ["What would you have said had I consulted you as to the propriety of my banishing Dr. Grantly from my house because he knows Lord Tattenham Corner?", "그랜틀리 박사가 태튼햄 코너 경을 안다는 이유로 제 집에서 그분을 내치는 것이 옳겠느냐고 제가 당신께 여쭈었다면 뭐라고 하셨겠어요?"], ["I am sure Lord Tattenham is quite as objectionable an acquaintance for a clergyman as Mr. Slope is for a clergyman's daughter.\"", "태튼햄 경이 성직자에게 마뜩잖은 지인인 것은, 슬로프 씨가 성직자의 딸에게 마뜩잖은 지인인 것과 꼭 같을 텐데요.”"]], [["\"I do not know Lord Tattenham Corner.\"", "“저는 태튼햄 코너 경을 알지 못합니다.”"]], [["\"No, but Dr.", "“그러시겠지요."], ["Grantly does.", "하지만 그랜틀리 박사는 아십니다."], ["It is nothing to me if he knows all the young lords on every race-course in England.", "그분이 영국의 온갖 경마장에 있는 젊은 귀족을 죄다 안다 해도 제게는 아무 상관이 없어요."], ["I shall not interfere with him, nor shall he with me.\"", "저는 그분 일에 참견하지 않을 것이고, 그분도 제 일에 참견해서는 안 됩니다.”"]], [["\"I am sorry to differ with you, Mrs.", "“생각이 달라 죄송합니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold, but as you have spoken to me on this matter, and especially as you blame me for what little I said on the subject, I must tell you that I do differ from you. Dr.", "그러나 부인께서 이 문제를 제게 꺼내셨고, 더욱이 제가 이 일로 몇 마디 한 것을 나무라시니, 저는 부인과 생각이 다르다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["Grantly's position as a man in the world gives him a right to choose his own acquaintances, subject to certain influences.", "그랜틀리 박사께서는 세상 속의 한 남자로서 제 지인을 스스로 고를 권리가 있으시되, 거기에는 일정한 견제가 따릅니다."], ["If he chooses them badly, those influences will be used.", "그분이 그것을 잘못 고르시면 그 견제가 작동하지요."], ["If he consorts with persons unsuitable to him, his bishop will interfere. What the bishop is to Dr.", "그분이 어울리지 않는 사람들과 어울리시면 그분의 주교가 나설 것입니다."], ["Grantly, Dr. Grantly is to you.\"", "주교가 그랜틀리 박사께 그러한 자리라면, 그랜틀리 박사는 부인께 그러한 자리입니다.”"]], [["\"I deny it.", "“저는 그것을 부인합니다."], ["I utterly deny it,\"", "전적으로 부인합니다.”"], ["said Eleanor, jumping from her seat and literally flashing before Mr. Arabin, as she stood on the drawing-room floor.", "엘리너가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했고, 응접실 바닥에 선 그 모습은 애러빈 씨 앞에서 그야말로 번쩍였다."], ["He had never seen her so excited, he had never seen her look half so beautiful.", "그는 그녀가 그토록 격해진 것을 본 적이 없었고, 그녀가 그 반만큼이라도 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다."]], [["\"I utterly deny it,\"", "“전적으로 부인합니다.”"], ["said she. \"Dr.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Grantly has no sort of jurisdiction over me whatsoever.", "“그랜틀리 박사는 제게 그 어떤 종류의 관할권도 갖고 계시지 않습니다."], ["Do you and he forget that I am not altogether alone in the world?", "당신도 그분도 제가 이 세상에 아주 혼자인 것은 아니라는 사실을 잊으셨나요?"], ["Do you forget that I have a father?", "제게 아버지가 계시다는 것을 잊으셨나요?"], ["Dr. Grantly, I believe, always has forgotten it.", "그랜틀리 박사는 늘 그 사실을 잊고 계셨던 듯합니다."]], [["\"From you, Mr. Arabin,\"", "“애러빈 씨, 당신에게서라면,”"], ["she continued, \"I would have listened to advice because I should have expected it to have been given as one friend may advise another--not as a schoolmaster gives an order to a pupil.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “저는 조언을 들었을 거예요."], ["I might have differed from you--on this matter I should have done so--but had you spoken to me in your usual manner and with your usual freedom, I should not have been angry.", "벗이 벗에게 건네듯 주어지는 조언이려니 여겼을 테니까요. 훈장이 학생에게 명령을 내리듯 하는 것이 아니라요."], ["But now--was it manly of you, Mr.", "당신과 생각이 달랐을 수도 있고, 이 문제라면 분명 달랐겠지요."], ["Arabin, to speak of me in this way--so disrespectful--so--?", "하지만 당신이 여느 때의 말투로, 여느 때의 스스럼없음으로 제게 말씀하셨더라면 저는 화내지 않았을 거예요."], ["I cannot bring myself to repeat what you said.", "그런데 지금은, 애러빈 씨, 저를 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것이 사내다운 일이었나요? 그토록 무례하게, 그토록—?"], ["You must understand what I feel.", "당신이 하신 말씀을 저는 차마 옮기지 못하겠어요."], ["Was it just of you to speak of me in such a way and to advise my sister's husband to turn me out of my sister's house because I chose to know a man of whose doctrine you disapprove?\"", "제가 무엇을 느끼는지 아셔야 합니다. 제가 당신이 못마땅해하는 교리를 가진 사람과 알고 지내기로 했다는 이유로, 저를 그런 식으로 말하고 언니의 남편에게 저를 언니 집에서 내보내라고 조언하는 것이 온당한 일이었나요?”"]], [["\"I have no alternative left to me, Mrs. Bold,\"", "“달리 방도가 없군요, 볼드 부인.”"], ["said he, standing with his back to the fire-place, looking down intently at the carpet pattern, and speaking with a slow, measured voice, \"but to tell you plainly what did take place between me and Dr.", "그가 벽난로를 등지고 서서, 눈은 융단의 무늬를 뚫어져라 내려다보며, 느릿하고 고른 목소리로 말했다."], ["Grantly.\"", "“저와 그랜틀리 박사 사이에 오간 일을 있는 그대로 말씀드리는 수밖에요.”"]], [["\"Well,\"", "“말씀하세요.”"], ["said she, finding that he paused for a moment.", "그가 잠시 말을 멈추자 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I am afraid that what I may say may pain you.\"", "“제가 드릴 말씀이 부인을 아프게 할까 걱정입니다.”"]], [["\"It cannot well do so more than what you have already done,\"", "“이미 하신 일보다 더 아프게 하기야 하겠어요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Dr. Grantly asked me whether I thought it would be prudent for him to receive you in his house as the wife of Mr. Slope, and I told him that I thought it would be imprudent. Believing it to be utterly impossible that Mr. Slope and--\"", "“그랜틀리 박사께서 제게, 부인을 슬로프 씨의 아내로서 댁에 맞아들이는 것이 분별 있는 일이겠느냐고 물으셨고, 저는 분별없는 일이라고 대답했습니다. 슬로프 씨와, 그런 일은 도저히 있을 수 없다고 믿었기에—”"]], [["\"Thank you, Mr.", "“고맙습니다, 애러빈 씨."], ["Arabin, that is sufficient.", "그만하면 됐습니다. 그 까닭까지는 알고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["I do not want to know your reasons,\"", "그녀가 무섭도록 차분한 목소리로 말했다."], ["said she, speaking with a terribly calm voice.", "“저는 그 신사분께 이웃으로서의 예사로운 예의를 보였을 뿐입니다."], ["\"I have shown to this gentleman the commonplace civility of a neighbour; and because I have done so, because I have not indulged against him in all the rancour and hatred which you and Dr.", "그런데 그렇게 했다는 이유로, 당신과 그랜틀리 박사께서 당신들과 뜻이 다른 성직자라면 누구에게나 마땅히 품어야 한다고 여기시는 그 원한과 증오를 제가 그분께 품지 않았다는 이유로, 당신들은 제가 그분과 결혼할 것이라고 단정하시는군요. 아니, 사실은 그렇게 단정하시는 것도 아니지요."], ["Grantly consider due to all clergymen who do not agree with yourselves, you conclude that I am to marry him; or rather you do not conclude so--no rational man could really come to such an outrageous conclusion without better ground; you have not thought so, but, as I am in a position in which such an accusation must be peculiarly painful, it is made in order that I may be terrified into hostility against this enemy of yours.\"", "더 나은 근거도 없이 그런 터무니없는 결론에 이를 만큼 분별없는 사람은 없을 테니까요. 당신들은 그렇게 생각한 것이 아니라, 제가 그런 지목을 유난히 괴로워할 처지에 있으니 겁을 집어먹고 당신들의 저 적수에게 등을 돌리게 하려고 그런 말을 지어내신 겁니다.”"]], [["As she finished speaking, she walked to the drawing-room window and stepped out into the garden. Mr.", "말을 마치자 그녀는 응접실 창가로 걸어가 정원으로 나가 버렸다."], ["Arabin was left in the room, still occupied in counting the pattern on the carpet.", "애러빈 씨는 방에 남아 여전히 융단의 무늬를 세고 있었다."], ["He had, however, distinctly heard and accurately marked every word that she had spoken.", "그러나 그는 그녀가 한 말을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 또렷이 듣고 정확히 새겨 두었다."], ["Was it not clear from what she had said that the archdeacon had been wrong in imputing to her any attachment to Mr.", "그녀가 한 말로 미루어, 부주교가 그녀에게 슬로프 씨를 향한 애정이 있다고 넘겨짚은 것이 틀렸음은 분명하지 않은가?"], ["Slope? Was it not clear that Eleanor was still free to make another choice?", "엘리너가 여전히 다른 선택을 할 수 있는 자유로운 몸임이 분명하지 않은가?"], ["It may seem strange that he should for a moment have had a doubt, and yet he did doubt.", "그가 한순간이라도 의심을 품었다는 것이 이상해 보일지 모르나, 그는 정말로 의심했다."], ["She had not absolutely denied the charge; she had not expressly said that it was untrue. Mr.", "그녀는 그 혐의를 딱 잘라 부인하지 않았고, 그것이 사실이 아니라고 명시하여 말하지도 않았던 것이다."], ["Arabin understood little of the nature of a woman's feelings, or he would have known how improbable it was that she should make any clearer declaration than she had done.", "애러빈 씨는 여자의 감정이 어떤 것인지 거의 알지 못했으니, 알았더라면 그녀가 이미 한 것보다 더 분명한 선언을 하리라고 기대하는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지 알았을 것이다."], ["Few men do understand the nature of a woman's heart, till years have robbed such understanding of its value.", "여자의 마음이 어떤 것인지를 아는 남자는 드물고, 세월이 그 앎의 값어치를 앗아 간 뒤에야 겨우 알게 된다."], ["And it is well that it should be so, or men would triumph too easily.", "그리고 그러한 편이 다행이니, 그렇지 않았다면 남자들이 너무 쉽게 이겼을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Arabin stood counting the carpet, unhappy, wretchedly unhappy, at the hard words that had been spoken to him, and yet happy, exquisitely happy, as he thought that after all the woman whom he so regarded was not to become the wife of the man whom he so much disliked.", "애러빈 씨는 서서 융단을 세고 있었다."], ["As he stood there he began to be aware that he was himself in love.", "자기에게 던져진 모진 말들 때문에 불행했다, 비참하도록 불행했다. 그러면서도 자기가 그토록 마음에 둔 여인이 자기가 그토록 싫어하는 남자의 아내가 되지 않으리라는 생각에 행복했다, 더없이 행복했다."], ["Forty years had passed over his head, and as yet woman's beauty had never given him an uneasy hour.", "그렇게 서 있는 동안 그는 자기가 사랑에 빠져 있음을 깨닫기 시작했다. 마흔 해가 그의 머리 위로 지나가는 동안 여자의 아름다움이 그에게 불편한 한 시간을 안긴 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["His present hour was very uneasy.", "지금 이 시간은 몹시 불편했다."]], [["Not that he remained there for half or a quarter of that time.", "그렇다고 그가 그 시간의 절반이나 사분의 일이라도 거기 머물러 있었던 것은 아니다."], ["In spite of what Eleanor had said, Mr. Arabin was, in truth, a manly man.", "엘리너가 무어라 했든, 애러빈 씨는 사실 사내다운 사내였다."], ["Having ascertained that he loved this woman, and having now reason to believe that she was free to receive his love, at least if she pleased to do so, he followed her into the garden to make such wooing as he could.", "자기가 이 여인을 사랑한다는 것을 확인하고, 이제 그녀가 적어도 마음만 있다면 제 사랑을 받아들일 수 있는 몸이라고 믿을 만한 근거가 생기자, 그는 힘닿는 대로 구애해 보려고 그녀를 따라 정원으로 나갔다."]], [["He was not long in finding her.", "그녀를 찾는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["She was walking to and fro beneath the avenue of elms that stood in the archdeacon's grounds, skirting the churchyard.", "그녀는 부주교의 땅에 서 있는, 교회 묘지와 맞닿은 느릅나무 가로수 아래를 이리저리 거닐고 있었다."], ["What had passed between her and Mr. Arabin had not, alas, tended to lessen the acerbity of her spirit.", "그녀와 애러빈 씨 사이에 오간 일은 애석하게도 그녀의 격한 마음을 누그러뜨리는 데 조금도 도움이 되지 않았다."], ["She was very angry--more angry with him than with anyone.", "그녀는 몹시 화가 나 있었고, 누구보다도 그에게 화가 나 있었다."], ["How could he have so misunderstood her?", "어떻게 그가 자기를 그토록 잘못 알 수 있단 말인가?"], ["She had been so intimate with him, had allowed him such latitude in what he had chosen to say to her, had complied with his ideas, cherished his views, fostered his precepts, cared for his comforts, made much of him in every way in which a pretty woman can make much of an unmarried man without committing herself or her feelings!", "그녀는 그와 그토록 스스럼없이 지냈고, 그가 하고 싶은 말을 무엇이든 하도록 그토록 너그럽게 두었고, 그의 생각에 맞추었고, 그의 견해를 아꼈고, 그의 가르침을 북돋웠고, 그의 편안함을 살폈으며, 아리따운 여자가 제 몸도 제 마음도 내주지 않으면서 미혼 남자를 떠받들 수 있는 온갖 방식으로 그를 떠받들었다!"], ["She had been doing this, and while she had been doing it he had regarded her as the affianced wife of another man.", "그녀가 그렇게 하고 있는 동안, 그는 그녀를 다른 남자와 이미 약혼한 여자로 여기고 있었던 것이다."]], [["As she passed along the avenue, every now and then an unbidden tear would force itself on her cheek, and as she raised her hand to brush it away, she stamped with her little foot upon the sward with very spite to think that she had been so treated.", "가로수길을 걷는 동안 이따금 부르지 않은 눈물이 뺨으로 밀려 나왔고, 손을 들어 그것을 훔쳐 내며 그녀는 이런 대접을 받았다는 생각에 분해서 작은 발로 잔디를 굴렀다."]], [["Mr. Arabin was very near to her when she first saw him, and she turned short round and retraced her steps down the avenue, trying to rid her cheeks of all trace of the tell-tale tears.", "애러빈 씨가 눈에 들어왔을 때 그는 이미 아주 가까이 와 있었고, 그녀는 홱 돌아서서 속내를 들키는 눈물 자국을 뺨에서 지우려 애쓰며 가로수길을 되짚어 내려갔다."], ["It was a needless endeavour, for Mr. Arabin was in a state of mind that hardly allowed him to observe such trifles.", "부질없는 애씀이었으니, 애러빈 씨는 그런 사소한 것을 살필 수 있는 마음 상태가 아니었다."], ["He followed her down the walk and overtook her just as she reached the end of it.", "그는 그녀를 따라 길을 내려갔고, 그녀가 길 끝에 다다른 바로 그때 따라잡았다."]], [["He had not considered how he would address her; he had not thought what he would say.", "그는 그녀에게 어떻게 말을 붙일지 헤아려 보지 않았고, 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 두지도 않았다."], ["He had only felt that it was wretchedness to him to quarrel with her, and that it would be happiness to be allowed to love her.", "그저 그녀와 다투는 것이 자기에게 비참한 일이며, 그녀를 사랑하도록 허락받는 것이 행복이리라고 느꼈을 뿐이다."], ["And yet he could not lower himself by asking her pardon.", "그러면서도 용서를 구하며 스스로를 낮출 수는 없었다."], ["He had done her no wrong.", "그는 그녀에게 잘못한 것이 없었다."], ["He had not calumniated her, not injured her, as she had accused him of doing.", "그녀가 나무란 것처럼 그녀를 험담하지도, 해치지도 않았다."], ["He could not confess sins of which he had not been guilty.", "저지르지 않은 죄를 고백할 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["He could only let the past be past and ask her as to her and his hopes for the future.", "그가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 지나간 일을 지나간 대로 두고, 그녀와 자기의 앞날에 대한 바람을 그녀에게 묻는 것뿐이었다."]], [["\"I hope we are not to part as enemies?\"", "“우리가 원수로 헤어지는 것은 아니겠지요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"There shall be no enmity on my part,\"", "“제 쪽에서는 아무런 원한도 없을 겁니다.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"I endeavour to avoid all enmities.", "엘리너가 말했다. “저는 어떤 원한도 두지 않으려고 애씁니다."], ["It would be a hollow pretence were I to say that there can be true friendship between us, after what has just passed.", "다만 방금 있었던 일 뒤에 우리 사이에 참된 우정이 있을 수 있다고 말한다면 그건 속 빈 시늉이겠지요."], ["People cannot make their friends of those whom they despise.\"", "사람은 제가 업신여기는 이를 벗으로 삼지 못하는 법입니다.”"]], [["\"And am I despised?\"", "“그렇다면 제가 업신여김을 받고 있습니까?”"]], [["\"I must have been so before you could have spoken of me as you did.", "“그러셨으니 저를 두고 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있었겠지요."], ["And I was deceived, cruelly deceived.", "그리고 저는 속았습니다, 모질게 속았어요."], ["I believed that you thought well of me; I believed that you esteemed me.\"", "저는 당신이 저를 좋게 여기신다고 믿었습니다. 저를 귀히 여기신다고 믿었어요.”"]], [["\"Thought well of you and esteemed you!\"", "“좋게 여기고 귀히 여긴다니요!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"In justifying myself before you, I must use stronger words than those.\"", "“부인 앞에서 저를 변명하자면 그보다 더 센 말을 써야겠습니다.”"], ["He paused for a moment, and Eleanor's heart beat with painful violence within her bosom as she waited for him to go on.", "그가 잠시 말을 멈추자, 그가 이어 말하기를 기다리는 동안 엘리너의 가슴속에서 심장이 아프도록 세차게 뛰었다. “저는 부인을 귀히 여겨 왔고 지금도 귀히 여깁니다."], ["\"I have esteemed, do esteem you, as I never yet esteemed any woman.", "여태 어떤 여인도 그렇게 귀히 여긴 적이 없습니다. 좋게 여기다니요!"], ["Think well of you! I never thought to think so well, so much of any human creature.", "저는 어떤 사람에 대해서도 그토록 좋게, 그토록 크게 여기게 되리라고는 생각조차 하지 못했습니다."], ["Speak calumny of you!", "부인의 험담을 하다니요!"], ["Insult you!", "부인을 모욕하다니요!"], ["Wilfully injure you!", "짐짓 부인을 해치다니요!"], ["I wish it were my privilege to shield you from calumny, insult, and injury.", "저야말로 험담과 모욕과 해악으로부터 부인을 지켜 드리는 것이 제 몫이었으면 합니다."], ["Calumny! Ah me!", "험담이라니!"], ["'Twere almost better that it were so.", "아, 나로서는! 차라리 그랬더라면 나았을 것을."], ["Better than to worship with a sinful worship; sinful and vain also.\"", "죄스러운 흠모로 흠모하는 것보다는요. 죄스러울 뿐 아니라 헛되기도 한 흠모로 말입니다.”"], ["And then he walked along beside her, with his hands clasped behind his back, looking down on the grass beneath his feet and utterly at a loss how to express his meaning.", "그러고서 그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡고 발밑의 풀을 내려다보며, 제 뜻을 어떻게 나타내야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 그녀 곁에서 걸었다."], ["And Eleanor walked beside him determined at least to give him no assistance.", "그리고 엘리너는 적어도 그를 거들어 주지는 않겠다고 마음먹은 채 그의 곁에서 걸었다."]], [["\"Ah me!\"", "“아, 나로서는!”"], ["he uttered at last, speaking rather to himself than to her. \"Ah me!", "마침내 그가 그녀에게라기보다 제 자신에게 말하듯 내뱉었다."], ["These Plumstead walks were pleasant enough, if one could have but heart's ease, but without that the dull, dead stones of Oxford were far preferable--and St.", "“아, 나로서는! 이 플럼스테드의 산책길도 마음이 편안하기만 하다면야 넉넉히 즐거운 곳이겠으나, 그렇지 못하다면 옥스퍼드의 그 칙칙하고 죽은 돌들이 훨씬 나을 것입니다."], ["Ewold's, too. Mrs.", "세인트 이월드도 그렇고요."], ["Bold, I am beginning to think that I mistook myself when I came hither.", "볼드 부인, 저는 이곳에 올 때 제 자신을 잘못 알았다는 생각이 들기 시작합니다."], ["A Romish priest now would have escaped all this.", "로마 교회의 사제라면 이 모든 것을 면했겠지요."], ["Oh, Father of heaven, how good for us would it be if thou couldest vouchsafe to us a certain rule.\"", "오, 하늘에 계신 아버지시여, 저희에게 확실한 규범을 내려 주실 수 있다면 그것이 저희에게 얼마나 좋겠나이까.”"]], [["\"And have we not a certain rule, Mr. Arabin?\"", "“우리에게 확실한 규범이 없나요, 애러빈 씨?”"]], [["\"Yes--yes, surely; 'Lead us not into temptation but deliver us from evil.'", "“있지요, 있고말고요. ‘우리를 시험에 들게 하지 마시옵고 다만 악에서 구하시옵소서.’ 그러나 무엇이 시험입니까?"], ["But what is temptation? What is evil?", "무엇이 악입니까?"], ["Is this evil--is this temptation?\"", "이것이 악입니까, 이것이 시험입니까?”"]], [["Poor Mr. Arabin!", "가엾은 애러빈 씨!"], ["It would not come out of him, that deep, true love of his.", "그의 그 깊고 참된 사랑은 도무지 밖으로 나오지 않았다."], ["He could not bring himself to utter it in plain language that would require and demand an answer.", "그는 그것을 답을 요구하고 요청하는 평범한 말로 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다."], ["He knew not how to say to the woman by his side, \"Since the fact is that you do not love that other man, that you are not to be his wife, can you love me, will you be my wife?\"", "그는 곁에 선 여인에게 이렇게 말할 줄을 몰랐다. “당신이 그 다른 남자를 사랑하지 않고 그의 아내가 되지 않을 것이 사실이라면, 나를 사랑해 줄 수 있겠습니까, 내 아내가 되어 주겠습니까?”"], ["These were the words which were in his heart, but with all his sighs he could not draw them to his lips.", "이것이 그의 마음속에 있는 말이었으나, 그 숱한 한숨으로도 그것을 입술까지 끌어올릴 수가 없었다."], ["He would have given anything, everything for power to ask this simple question, but glib as was his tongue in pulpits and on platforms, now he could not find a word wherewith to express the plain wish of his heart.", "이 간단한 물음을 던질 힘을 얻을 수만 있다면 그는 무엇이든, 모든 것을 내주었을 것이나, 강단에서도 연단에서도 그토록 매끄럽던 혀가 지금은 제 마음의 소박한 바람을 나타낼 말 한마디를 찾아내지 못했다."]], [["And yet Eleanor understood him as thoroughly as though he had declared his passion with all the elegant fluency of a practised Lothario.", "그런데도 엘리너는 그가 노련한 난봉꾼의 우아한 유창함으로 제 열정을 고백하기라도 한 것처럼 그를 온전히 알아들었다."], ["With a woman's instinct, she followed every bend of his mind as he spoke of the pleasantness of Plumstead and the stones of Oxford, as he alluded to the safety of the Romish priest and the hidden perils of temptation.", "여자의 본능으로 그녀는 그가 플럼스테드의 즐거움과 옥스퍼드의 돌들을 말할 때, 로마 교회 사제의 안전함과 시험의 숨은 위험을 넌지시 비칠 때, 그 마음의 굽이굽이를 하나하나 따라갔다."], ["She knew that it all meant love.", "그녀는 그것이 모두 사랑을 뜻한다는 것을 알았다."], ["She knew that this man at her side, this accomplished scholar, this practised orator, this great polemical combatant, was striving and striving in vain to tell her that his heart was no longer his own.", "그녀는 제 곁의 이 남자가, 이 뛰어난 학자가, 이 노련한 웅변가가, 이 대단한 논쟁의 투사가, 제 마음이 이제 제 것이 아님을 말하려고 애쓰고 또 애쓰다 헛되이 무너지고 있음을 알았다."]], [["She knew this, and felt a sort of joy in knowing it; yet she would not come to his aid.", "그녀는 이것을 알았고, 알면서 일종의 기쁨을 느꼈다. 그러면서도 그를 거들어 주려 하지는 않았다."], ["He had offended her deeply, had treated her unworthily, the more unworthily seeing that he had learnt to love her, and Eleanor could not bring herself to abandon her revenge.", "그는 그녀를 깊이 언짢게 했고 합당치 않게 대했으며, 그녀를 사랑하게 되었다는 점을 생각하면 더욱 합당치 않게 대한 것이었으니, 엘리너는 제 앙갚음을 놓아 버릴 수가 없었다."], ["She did not ask herself whether or no she would ultimately accept his love.", "그녀는 끝내 그의 사랑을 받아들일 것인지 아닌지를 스스로에게 묻지 않았다."], ["She did not even acknowledge to herself that she now perceived it with pleasure.", "지금 자기가 그것을 기쁘게 알아차리고 있다는 것조차 스스로에게 인정하지 않았다."], ["At the present moment it did not touch her heart; it merely appeased her pride and flattered her vanity. Mr.", "그 순간 그것은 그녀의 마음을 건드리지 못했고, 그저 그녀의 자존심을 달래고 허영을 흡족하게 했을 뿐이다."], ["Arabin had dared to associate her name with that of Mr. Slope, and now her spirit was soothed by finding that he would fain associate it with his own.", "애러빈 씨는 감히 제 이름을 슬로프 씨의 이름과 엮었는데, 이제 그가 그것을 제 이름과 엮고 싶어 한다는 것을 알게 되자 그녀의 마음이 누그러진 것이다."], ["And so she walked on beside him, inhaling incense but giving out no sweetness in return.", "그리하여 그녀는 향을 들이마시면서도 그 대가로 아무런 향기도 내주지 않은 채 그의 곁에서 걸었다."]], [["\"Answer me this,\" said Mr.", "“이것 하나만 대답해 주십시오.”"], ["Arabin, stopping suddenly in his walk and stepping forward so that he faced his companion.", "애러빈 씨가 걸음을 뚝 멈추고 앞으로 나서서 동행과 마주 보고 서며 말했다."], ["\"Answer me this one question.", "“이 한 가지 물음에만 대답해 주십시오."], ["You do not love Mr. Slope?", "부인은 슬로프 씨를 사랑하지 않으시지요?"], ["You do not intend to be his wife?\"", "그의 아내가 될 뜻이 없으시지요?”"]], [["Mr. Arabin certainly did not go the right way to win such a woman as Eleanor Bold.", "애러빈 씨는 엘리너 볼드 같은 여자의 마음을 얻는 데 결코 옳은 길로 가고 있지 않았다."], ["Just as her wrath was evaporating, as it was disappearing before the true warmth of his untold love, he rekindled it by a most useless repetition of his original sin.", "그녀의 노여움이 막 걷혀 가던 참에, 말해지지 않은 그의 사랑이 뿜는 참된 온기 앞에서 막 사라져 가던 참에, 그는 제가 처음 지은 죄를 참으로 부질없이 되풀이하여 그것을 도로 지펴 놓았다."], ["Had he known what he was about, he should never have mentioned Mr. Slope's name before Eleanor Bold, till he had made her all his own.", "제가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알았더라면 그는 엘리너 볼드를 온전히 제 사람으로 만들기 전에는 그녀 앞에서 슬로프 씨의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 것이다."], ["Then, and not till then, he might have talked of Mr. Slope with as much triumph as he chose.", "그때가 되면, 그때에야 비로소 그는 제가 원하는 만큼 의기양양하게 슬로프 씨 이야기를 할 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["\"I shall answer no such question,\"", "“그런 물음에는 대답하지 않겠어요.”"], ["said she; \"and what is more, I must tell you that nothing can justify your asking it.", "그녀가 말했다. “게다가 말씀드리자면, 무엇으로도 당신이 그런 물음을 하는 것을 정당화할 수는 없습니다."], ["Good morning!\"", "안녕히 계세요!”"]], [["And so saying, she stepped proudly across the lawn and, passing through the drawing-room window, joined her father and sister at lunch in the dining-room.", "그러고는 그녀는 도도하게 잔디밭을 가로질러 응접실 창을 통해 들어가, 식당에서 점심을 들고 있던 아버지와 언니 곁에 앉았다."], ["Half an hour afterwards she was in the carriage, and so she left Plumstead without again seeing Mr. Arabin.", "반 시간 뒤 그녀는 마차 안에 있었고, 그리하여 애러빈 씨를 다시 보지 않은 채 플럼스테드를 떠났다."]], [["His walk was long and sad among the sombre trees that overshadowed the churchyard.", "교회 묘지를 그늘로 덮은 침침한 나무들 사이로 그의 산책은 길고 서글펐다."], ["He left the archdeacon's grounds that he might escape attention, and sauntered among the green hillocks under which lay at rest so many of the once loving swains and forgotten beauties of Plumstead.", "그는 남의 눈을 피하려고 부주교의 땅을 벗어나, 한때 사랑에 빠졌던 숱한 젊은이들과 잊힌 미녀들이 잠들어 있는 푸른 봉분들 사이를 어슬렁거렸다."], ["To his ears Eleanor's last words sounded like a knell never to be reversed.", "그의 귀에 엘리너의 마지막 말은 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 조종처럼 울렸다."], ["He could not comprehend that she might be angry with him, indignant with him, remorseless with him, and yet love him.", "그녀가 자기에게 화가 나 있고 분개해 있고 매정할 수 있으면서도 동시에 자기를 사랑할 수 있다는 것을 그는 이해하지 못했다."], ["He could not make up his mind whether or no Mr. Slope was in truth a favoured rival.", "슬로프 씨가 정말로 총애받는 연적인지 아닌지 그는 마음을 정하지 못했다."], ["If not, why should she not have answered his question?", "그렇지 않다면 어찌하여 그녀가 그의 물음에 대답하지 않았단 말인가?"]], [["Poor Mr. Arabin--untaught, illiterate, boorish, ignorant man!", "가엾은 애러빈 씨, 못 배우고 무식하고 촌스럽고 아무것도 모르는 사람이여!"], ["That at forty years of age you should know so little of the workings of a woman's heart!", "마흔 살이나 되어서 여자의 마음이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그토록 모른다니!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI. The Bishop's Library", "# 제31장. 주교의 서재"]], [["And thus the pleasant party at Plumstead was broken up. It had been a very pleasant party as long as they had all remained in good humour with one another. Mrs. Grantly had felt her house to be gayer and brighter than it had been for many a long day, and the archdeacon had been aware that the month had passed pleasantly without attributing the pleasure to any other special merits than those of his own hospitality. Within three or four days of Eleanor's departure, Mr. Harding had also returned, and Mr. Arabin had gone to Oxford to spend one week there previous to his settling at the vicarage of St. Ewold's. He had gone laden with many messages to Dr. Gwynne touching the iniquity of the doings in Barchester palace and the peril in which it was believed the hospital still stood in spite of the assurances contained in Mr. Slope's inauspicious letter.", "플럼스테드의 그 즐거운 모임은 이렇게 해서 흩어졌다. 모두가 서로 기분 좋게 지내는 동안에는 참으로 즐거운 모임이었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 자기 집이 오랜만에 한결 흥겹고 환해졌다고 느꼈고, 부주교는 그 한 달이 유쾌하게 지나갔다는 것을 알고 있었으되 그 유쾌함을 제 접대 솜씨 말고 다른 어떤 특별한 미덕에 돌리지는 않았다. 엘리너가 떠난 지 사나흘 안에 하딩 씨도 돌아갔고, 애러빈 씨는 세인트 이월드 목사관에 자리를 잡기 전에 한 주를 보내려고 옥스퍼드로 떠났다. 그는 바체스터 주교관에서 벌어지는 일들의 부당함과, 슬로프 씨의 불길한 편지에 담긴 장담에도 불구하고 요양원이 여전히 처해 있다고들 믿는 위태로움에 관하여, 그윈 박사에게 전할 말을 잔뜩 짊어지고 갔다."]], [["During Eleanor's drive into Barchester she had not much opportunity of reflecting on Mr. Arabin.", "엘리너는 마차로 바체스터까지 오는 동안 애러빈 씨를 두고 곰곰이 생각할 겨를이 별로 없었다."], ["She had been constrained to divert her mind both from his sins and his love by the necessity of conversing with her sister and maintaining the appearance of parting with her on good terms.", "언니와 이야기를 나누며 사이좋게 헤어지는 모양새를 지켜야 했던 탓에, 그의 죄과에서도 그의 사랑에서도 억지로 마음을 돌려놓아야 했던 것이다."], ["When the carriage reached her own door, and while she was in the act of giving her last kiss to her sister and nieces, Mary Bold ran out and exclaimed:", "마차가 제 집 문 앞에 닿아 언니와 조카들에게 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 있는 참에, 메리 볼드가 달려 나오며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Oh, Eleanor, have you heard?", "“아, 엘리너, 소식 들었어요?"], ["Oh, Mrs. Grantly, have you heard what has happened?", "아, 그랜틀리 부인, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨어요?"], ["The poor dean!\"", "가엾은 수석 사제님!”"]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“세상에!”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"What--what has happened?\"", "“대체 무슨, 무슨 일이 있었다는 게냐?”"]], [["\"This morning at nine he had a fit of apoplexy, and he has not spoken since.", "“오늘 아침 아홉 시에 졸중을 일으키셨는데, 그 뒤로 한마디도 못 하고 계세요."], ["I very much fear that by this time he is no more.\"", "지금쯤이면 이미 세상을 떠나셨을까 봐 몹시 걱정스러워요.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly had been very intimate with the dean, and was therefore much shocked.", "그랜틀리 부인은 수석 사제와 아주 가깝게 지내던 터라 크게 충격을 받았다."], ["Eleanor had not known him so well; nevertheless, she was sufficiently acquainted with his person and manners to feel startled and grieved also at the tidings she now received.", "엘리너는 그만큼 잘 아는 사이가 아니었으나, 그래도 그 인품과 몸가짐을 웬만큼은 알고 있었던지라 방금 들은 소식에 놀라고 슬퍼하기는 마찬가지였다."], ["\"I will go at once to the deanery,\" said Mrs.", "“나는 지금 곧장 수석사제관으로 가 봐야겠다.”"], ["Grantly; \"the archdeacon, I am sure, will be there.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “부주교도 분명 거기 있을 게다."], ["If there is any news to send you, I will let Thomas call before he leaves town.\"", "너희에게 보낼 소식이 있으면 토머스가 시내를 떠나기 전에 들르게 하마.”"], ["And so the carriage drove off, leaving Eleanor and her baby with Mary Bold.", "그러고서 마차는 떠나갔고, 엘리너와 아기는 메리 볼드와 함께 남았다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly had been quite right.", "그랜틀리 부인의 짐작은 옳았다."], ["The archdeacon was at the deanery.", "부주교는 수석사제관에 있었다."], ["He had come into Barchester that morning by himself, not caring to intrude himself upon Eleanor, and he also immediately on his arrival had heard of the dean's fit.", "그는 그날 아침 혼자서 바체스터로 들어왔는데, 엘리너에게 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않았던 까닭이었고, 그 역시 도착하자마자 수석 사제가 쓰러졌다는 소식을 들었다."], ["There was, as we have before said, a library or reading-room connecting the cathedral with the dean's house.", "앞서 말한 대로 대성당과 수석 사제의 집을 이어 주는 서재 겸 열람실이 하나 있었다."], ["This was generally called the bishop's library, because a certain bishop of Barchester was supposed to have added it to the cathedral.", "이곳은 대개 주교의 서재라고 불렸는데, 바체스터의 어느 주교가 대성당에 이 방을 덧붙였다고 여겨지기 때문이었다."], ["It was built immediately over a portion of the cloisters, and a flight of stairs descended from it into the room in which the cathedral clergymen put their surplices on and off.", "회랑의 한 부분 바로 위에 지어져 있었고, 계단 하나가 이 방에서 아래로 내려가 대성당 성직자들이 중백의를 입고 벗는 방으로 이어졌다."], ["As it also opened directly into the dean's house, it was the passage through which that dignitary usually went to his public devotions.", "또한 수석 사제의 집으로 곧장 통해 있었으므로, 그 어른이 공식 예배에 나갈 때 늘 지나다니는 통로이기도 했다."], ["Who had or had not the right of entry into it, it might be difficult to say; but the people of Barchester believed that it belonged to the dean, and the clergymen of Barchester believed that it belonged to the chapter.", "누구에게 이곳에 들어올 권리가 있고 누구에게 없는지는 말하기 어려운 노릇이었으나, 바체스터 사람들은 이 방이 수석 사제의 것이라고 믿었고, 바체스터의 성직자들은 이 방이 참사회의 것이라고 믿었다."]], [["On the morning in question most of the resident clergymen who constituted the chapter, and some few others, were here assembled, and among them as usual the archdeacon towered with high authority.", "문제의 그날 아침, 참사회를 이루는 상주 성직자 대부분과 그 밖의 몇몇이 이곳에 모여 있었고, 그 가운데서 여느 때처럼 부주교가 드높은 권위로 우뚝했다."], ["He had heard of the dean's fit before he was over the bridge which led into the town, and had at once come to the well-known clerical trysting place.", "그는 시내로 들어오는 다리를 채 건너기도 전에 수석 사제가 쓰러졌다는 소식을 듣고는, 곧장 이 이름난 성직자들의 회합 장소로 온 것이었다."], ["He had been there by eleven o'clock, and had remained ever since.", "열한 시부터 와 있었고 그 뒤로 줄곧 자리를 지켰다."], ["From time to time the medical men who had been called in came through from the deanery into the library, uttered little bulletins, and then returned.", "이따금 불려 온 의사들이 수석사제관 쪽에서 서재로 건너와 짤막한 소식을 전하고는 다시 돌아가곤 했다."], ["There was, it appears, very little hope of the old man's rallying, indeed no hope of anything like a final recovery.", "노인이 되살아날 가망은 거의 없어 보였고, 완전한 회복 같은 것은 실로 가망이 전혀 없었다."], ["The only question was whether he must die at once speechless, unconscious, stricken to death by his first heavy fit, or whether by due aid of medical skill he might not be so far brought back to this world as to become conscious of his state and enabled to address one prayer to his Maker before he was called to meet Him face to face at the judgement seat.", "문제는 오직, 첫 중한 발작에 쓰러져 말도 못 하고 의식도 없는 채 이대로 곧 숨을 거둘 것인가, 아니면 의술의 힘을 제대로 빌린 덕분에 제 처지를 알아볼 만큼은 이 세상으로 되돌아와, 심판대 앞에서 그분과 얼굴을 마주하도록 부름받기 전에 창조주께 기도 한마디를 올릴 수 있게 될 것인가 하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Sir Omicron Pie had been sent for from London.", "오미크론 파이 경을 부르러 런던으로 사람을 보내 두었다. 그 위대한 인물은 선량한 노 그랜틀리 주교 때에 늙은이의 심장 속에서 목숨을 계속 움직이게 하는 데 놀라운 재간을 보여 준 바 있으니, 수석 사제를 두고도 똑같이 성공하리라고 기대해 봄 직했다."], ["That great man had shown himself a wonderful adept at keeping life still moving within an old man's heart in the case of good old Bishop Grantly, and it might be reasonably expected that he would be equally successful with a dean. In the meantime Dr.", "그러는 동안 필그레이브 박사와 리어차일드 씨가 최선을 다하고 있었고, 가엾은 트리폴 양은 아버지의 머리맡에 앉아, 그런 처지의 딸들이 늘 그러하듯 제 사랑을 드러낼 무슨 일이든 하게 해 주기를 애타게 바라고 있었다."], ["Fillgrave and Mr. Rerechild were doing their best, and poor Miss Trefoil sat at the head of her father's bed, longing, as in such cases daughters do long, to be allowed to do something to show her love--if it were only to chafe his feet with her hands, or wait in menial offices on those autocratic doctors--anything so that now in the time of need she might be of use.", "제 손으로 아버지의 발을 문질러 드리는 일이든, 그 위세 등등한 의사들의 잔심부름을 하는 일이든, 이 절박한 때에 조금이라도 쓸모가 될 수만 있다면 무엇이든 좋았다."]], [["The archdeacon alone of the attendant clergy had been admitted for a moment into the sick man's chamber.", "곁을 지키던 성직자들 가운데 부주교만이 잠깐 병자의 방에 들어가는 것을 허락받았다."], ["He had crept in with creaking shoes, had said with smothered voice a word of consolation to the sorrowing daughter, had looked on the distorted face of his old friend with solemn but yet eager scrutinising eye, as though he said in his heart \"and so some day it will probably be with me,\"", "그는 삐걱대는 구두를 신은 채 살금살금 들어가, 슬픔에 잠긴 딸에게 낮게 죽인 목소리로 위로의 말을 한마디 건네고는, 일그러진 옛 벗의 얼굴을 엄숙하면서도 열심히 뜯어보는 눈길로 바라보았는데, 마음속으로는 마치 “언젠가는 나도 아마 이렇게 되겠지”"], ["and then, having whispered an unmeaning word or two to the doctors, had creaked his way back again into the library.", "하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러고 나서 의사들에게 뜻 모를 말을 한두 마디 속삭이고는 다시 삐걱삐걱 서재로 걸어 나왔다."]], [["\"He'll never speak again, I fear,\"", "“다시는 말을 못 하실 것 같소.”"], ["said the archdeacon as he noiselessly closed the door, as though the unconscious dying man, from whom all sense had fled, would have heard in his distant chamber the spring of the lock which was now so carefully handled.", "부주교가 소리 나지 않게 문을 닫으며 말했다. 감각이라고는 죄다 달아나 버린 그 의식 없는 죽어 가는 이가, 저 멀찍한 방에서 지금 이토록 조심스레 다루어지는 자물쇠의 용수철 소리를 알아듣기라도 할 것처럼 말이다."]], [["\"Indeed!", "“저런!"], ["Indeed!", "저런!"], ["Is he so bad?\"", "그렇게 나쁘단 말이오?”"], ["said the meagre little prebendary, turning over in his own mind all the probable candidates for the deanery and wondering whether the archdeacon would think it worth his while to accept it.", "야윈 자그마한 참사회원이 말하면서, 마음속으로는 수석사제직에 오를 만한 후보들을 하나하나 헤아려 보고 부주교가 그 자리를 받을 만하다고 여길지 궁금해했다."], ["\"The fit must have been very violent.\"", "“발작이 어지간히 심했던 모양이오.”"]], [["\"When a man over seventy has a stroke of apoplexy, it seldom comes very lightly,\"", "“일흔이 넘은 사람이 졸중을 맞으면 좀처럼 가볍게 지나가는 법이 없지요.”"], ["said the burly chancellor.", "몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"He was an excellent, sweet-tempered man,\"", "“더할 나위 없이 훌륭하고 성품이 온화한 어른이셨소.”"], ["said one of the vicars choral.", "성가대 사제 하나가 말했다."], ["\"Heaven knows how we shall repair his loss.\"", "“그 빈자리를 무엇으로 메울지는 하늘이나 아실 일이오.”"]], [["\"He was indeed,\"", "“참으로 그러셨소.”"], ["said a minor canon, \"and a great blessing to all those privileged to take a share in the services of our cathedral.", "하급 참사원이 말했다. “우리 대성당 예배에 몫을 나눠 받는 영광을 누린 모든 이에게 크나큰 축복이셨지요."], ["I suppose the government will appoint, Mr.", "후임은 정부에서 임명하겠지요, 부주교님."], ["Archdeacon. I trust we may have no stranger.\"", "부디 낯선 사람이 오지 않기만을 바랄 뿐이오.”"]], [["\"We will not talk about his successor,\"", "“후임 이야기는 하지 맙시다.”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"while there is yet hope.\"", "부주교가 말했다. “아직 가망이 남아 있는 마당에.”"]], [["\"Oh, no, of course not,\"", "“아, 그럼요, 물론이지요.”"], ["said the minor canon.", "하급 참사원이 말했다."], ["\"It would be exceedingly indecorous; but--\"", "“그런 이야기는 지극히 온당치 못한 노릇이오. 하지만…”"]], [["\"I know of no man,\"", "“내가 아는 한,” 야윈 자그마한 참사회원이 말했다."], ["said the meagre little prebendary, \"who has better interest with the present government than Mr. Slope.\"", "“지금 정부와 슬로프 씨보다 더 두터운 연줄을 가진 사람은 없소.”"]], [["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨라니.”"], ["said two or three at once almost sotto voce.", "두세 사람이 거의 소리를 죽인 채 한꺼번에 말했다."], ["\"Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester!\"", "“슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니!”"]], [["\"Pooh!\"", "“쳇!”"], ["exclaimed the burly chancellor.", "몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 내뱉었다."]], [["\"The bishop would do anything for him,\"", "“주교는 그 사람을 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 할 게요.”"], ["said the little prebendary.", "자그마한 참사회원이 말했다."]], [["\"And so would Mrs. Proudie,\"", "“프라우디 부인도 마찬가지고.”"], ["said the vicar choral.", "성가대 사제가 말했다."]], [["\"Pooh!\"", "“쳇!”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원이 말했다."]], [["The archdeacon had almost turned pale at the idea.", "부주교는 그 말에 하마터면 낯빛이 창백해질 뻔했다."], ["What if Mr. Slope should become Dean of Barchester?", "슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 된다면?"], ["To be sure there was no adequate ground, indeed no ground at all, for presuming that such a desecration could even be contemplated.", "물론 그런 신성모독이 검토될 수 있으리라고 짐작할 만한 충분한 근거는 없었고, 실은 근거라 할 것이 아예 없었다."], ["But nevertheless it was on the cards. Dr.", "그렇지만 그럼에도 있을 법한 일이기는 했다."], ["Proudie had interest with the government, and the man carried as it were Dr. Proudie in his pocket.", "프라우디 박사는 정부에 연줄이 있었고, 그 사내는 이를테면 프라우디 박사를 제 주머니에 넣고 다녔다."], ["How should they all conduct themselves if Mr. Slope were to become Dean of Barchester?", "슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 된다면 다들 어떻게 처신해야 한단 말인가?"], ["The bare idea for a moment struck even Dr. Grantly dumb.", "그 발상만으로도 그랜틀리 박사조차 한동안 말문이 막혔다."]], [["\"It would certainly not be very pleasant for us to have Mr. Slope at the deanery,\"", "“슬로프 씨를 수석사제관에 두게 된다면 우리로서는 그리 유쾌한 노릇은 아니겠지요.”"], ["said the little prebendary, chuckling inwardly at the evident consternation which his surmise had created.", "자그마한 참사회원이 제 억측이 불러일으킨 뚜렷한 낭패를 보며 속으로 낄낄거리면서 말했다."]], [["\"About as pleasant and as probable as having you in the palace,\"", "“그대를 주교관에 들이는 것만큼이나 유쾌하고, 또 그만큼이나 있을 법한 일이겠소.”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"I should think such an appointment highly improbable,\"", "“나는 그런 임명은 지극히 있을 법하지 않다고 보오.”"], ["said the minor canon, \"and, moreover, extremely injudicious.", "하급 참사원이 말했다. “게다가 더없이 사려 깊지 못한 처사이기도 하고."], ["Should not you, Mr. Archdeacon?\"", "부주교님께서도 그리 생각하지 않으시오?”"]], [["\"I should presume such a thing to be quite out of the question,\"", "“나로서는 그런 일은 아예 논할 거리도 못 된다고 봅니다.” 부주교가 말했다."], ["said the archdeacon, \"but at the present moment I am thinking rather of our poor friend who is lying so near us than of Mr. Slope.\"", "“하지만 지금 이 순간 내가 생각하고 있는 것은 슬로프 씨가 아니라, 바로 저 가까이에 누워 계신 우리의 가엾은 벗이오.”"]], [["\"Of course, of course,\"", "“물론이지요, 물론이오.”"], ["said the vicar choral with a very solemn air; \"of course you are.", "성가대 사제가 자못 엄숙한 낯빛으로 말했다. “물론 그러시겠지요."], ["So are we all.", "우리도 다들 그렇소."], ["Poor Dr. Trefoil; the best of men, but--\"", "가엾은 트리폴 박사님, 더없이 훌륭한 분이신데, 하지만…”"]], [["\"It's the most comfortable dean's residence in England,\"", "“영국에서 가장 안락한 수석 사제 사택이지요.”"], ["said a second prebendary.", "또 다른 참사회원이 말했다."], ["\"Fifteen acres in the grounds.", "“대지가 15에이커요."], ["It is better than many of the bishops' palaces.\"", "웬만한 주교관보다 낫소.”"]], [["\"And full two thousand a year,\"", "“게다가 꼬박 연 2,000파운드고.”"], ["said the meagre doctor.", "야윈 박사가 말했다."]], [["\"It is cut down to £1,200,\"", "“1,200파운드로 깎였소.”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"No,\"", "“아니오.”"], ["said the second prebendary.", "두 번째 참사회원이 말했다."], ["\"It is to be fifteen.", "“1,500으로 정해질 게요."], ["A special case was made.\"", "특례가 만들어졌소.”"]], [["\"No such thing,\"", "“그런 일 없소.”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"You'll find I'm right,\"", "“내 말이 맞다는 걸 알게 될 게요.”"], ["said the prebendary.", "참사회원이 말했다."]], [["\"I'm sure I read it in the report,\"", "“나도 분명 보고서에서 그렇게 읽었소.”"], ["said the minor canon.", "하급 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"Nonsense,\"", "“당치 않은 소리.”"], ["said the chancellor.", "학무 참사원이 말했다."], ["\"They couldn't do it.", "“그렇게는 못 하오."], ["There were to be no exceptions but London and Durham.\"", "런던과 더럼 말고는 예외를 두지 않기로 되어 있었소.”"]], [["\"And Canterbury and York,\"", "“캔터베리와 요크도 있지요.”"], ["said the vicar choral modestly.", "성가대 사제가 조심스레 말했다."]], [["\"What do you say, Grantly?\"", "“그대는 어떻게 보시오, 그랜틀리?”"], ["said the meagre little doctor.", "야윈 자그마한 박사가 말했다."]], [["\"Say about what?\"", "“무엇을 말이오?” 부주교가 말했다."], ["said the archdeacon, who had been looking as though he were thinking about his friend the dean, but who had in reality been thinking about Mr. Slope.", "그는 벗인 수석 사제를 생각하고 있는 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었으나, 실은 슬로프 씨를 생각하고 있던 참이었다."]], [["\"What is the next dean to have, twelve or fifteen?\"", "“다음 수석 사제는 얼마를 받게 되느냐 말이오, 1,200이오, 1,500이오?”"]], [["\"Twelve,\"", "“1,200이오.”"], ["said the archdeacon authoritatively, thereby putting an end at once to all doubt and dispute among his subordinates as far as that subject was concerned.", "부주교가 권위 있게 말했고, 그리하여 적어도 그 문제에 관한 한 아랫사람들 사이의 온갖 의심과 다툼에 단번에 종지부를 찍었다."]], [["\"Well, I certainly thought it was fifteen,\"", "“글쎄, 나는 분명 1,500인 줄로 알았는데.”"], ["said the minor canon.", "하급 참사원이 말했다."]], [["\"Pooh!\"", "“쳇!”"], ["said the burly chancellor.", "몸집 큰 학무 참사원이 말했다."], ["At this moment the door opened and in came Dr. Fillgrave.", "바로 그때 문이 열리며 필그레이브 박사가 들어왔다."]], [["\"How is he?\"", "“어떠십니까?”"], ["\"Is he conscious?\"", "“의식은 돌아오셨습니까?”"], ["\"Can he speak?\" \"I hope not dead?\"", "“말씀은 하실 수 있습니까?”"], ["\"No worse news, Doctor, I trust?\"", "“설마 돌아가신 건 아니겠지요?”"], ["\"I hope, I trust, something better, Doctor?\"", "“더 나쁜 소식은 아니겠지요, 박사님?”"], ["said half a dozen voices all at once, each in a tone of extremest anxiety.", "“뭔가 좀 나아졌기를 바랍니다만, 박사님?” 예닐곱 목소리가 저마다 더없이 애타는 어조로 한꺼번에 물었다."], ["It was pleasant to see how popular the good old dean was among his clergy.", "이 선량한 노 수석 사제가 휘하 성직자들 사이에서 얼마나 인망이 두터웠는지를 보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다."]], [["\"No change, gentlemen; not the slightest change.", "“변화는 없습니다, 여러분. 조금도 변화가 없습니다."], ["But a telegraphic message has arrived--Sir Omicron Pie will be here by the 9.15 P.M.", "다만 전보가 한 통 왔습니다. 오미크론 파이 경이 밤 9시 15분 기차로 이곳에 닿을 것입니다."], ["train. If any man can do anything, Sir Omicron Pie will do it.", "누구든 손을 쓸 수 있는 사람이 있다면 오미크론 파이 경이 해낼 것입니다."], ["But all that skill can do has been done.\"", "하지만 의술로 할 수 있는 일은 이미 다 해 놓았습니다.”"]], [["\"We are sure of that, Dr. Fillgrave,\"", "“저희도 그 점은 잘 알고 있습니다, 필그레이브 박사님.”"], ["said the archdeacon; \"we are quite sure of that.", "부주교가 말했다. “그 점은 조금도 의심하지 않습니다."], ["But yet you know--\"", "하지만 그래도 아시다시피…”"]], [["\"Oh, quite right,\" said the doctor, \"quite right--I should have done just the same--I advised it at once. I said to Rerechild at once that with such a life and such a man, Sir Omicron should be summoned--of course I knew expense was nothing--so distinguished, you know, and so popular. Nevertheless, all that human skill can do has been done.\"", "“아, 지당하십니다.” 박사가 말했다. “지당하십니다. 저라도 똑같이 했을 것입니다. 제가 곧바로 그렇게 하시라 권했지요. 이만한 목숨이고 이만한 어른이시니 오미크론 경을 모셔 와야 한다고 리어차일드에게 그 자리에서 말했습니다. 물론 비용 따위는 문제가 되지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었고요. 워낙 이름 높으신 분이고, 워낙 인망이 두터우신 분이니 말입니다. 그렇지만 사람의 의술로 할 수 있는 일은 이미 다 해 놓았습니다.”"]], [["Just at this period Mrs. Grantly's carriage drove into the close, and the archdeacon went down to confirm the news which she had heard before.", "바로 이 무렵 그랜틀리 부인의 마차가 대성당 경내로 들어왔고, 부주교는 아래로 내려가 그녀가 앞서 들었던 소식을 확인해 주었다."]], [["By the 9.15 P.M. train Sir Omicron Pie did arrive.", "밤 9시 15분 기차로 오미크론 파이 경은 정말로 도착했다."], ["And in the course of the night a sort of consciousness returned to the poor old dean.", "그리고 밤이 깊어 가는 동안 가엾은 노 수석 사제에게 일종의 의식이 돌아왔다."], ["Whether this was due to Sir Omicron Pie is a question on which it may be well not to offer an opinion.", "이것이 오미크론 파이 경 덕분이었는지는 견해를 내놓지 않는 편이 좋을 문제다."], ["Dr. Fillgrave was very clear in his own mind, but Sir Omicron himself is thought to have differed from that learned doctor.", "필그레이브 박사는 제 나름으로 아주 분명한 생각을 지니고 있었으나, 오미크론 경 본인은 그 학식 높은 박사와 견해를 달리했다고 전해진다."], ["At any rate Sir Omicron expressed an opinion that the dean had yet some days to live.", "어쨌거나 오미크론 경은 수석 사제가 아직 며칠은 더 살 것이라는 소견을 밝혔다."]], [["For the eight or ten next days, accordingly, the poor dean remained in the same state, half-conscious and half-comatose; and the attendant clergy began to think that no new appointment would be necessary for some few months to come.", "그리하여 그 뒤 여드레에서 열흘 동안 가엾은 수석 사제는 반쯤 의식이 있고 반쯤 혼수에 빠진 그 상태 그대로였고, 곁을 지키던 성직자들은 앞으로 몇 달 동안은 새로 임명할 일이 없으리라고 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII. A New Candidate for Ecclesiastical Honours", "# 제32장. 성직 영예를 노리는 새 후보"]], [["The dean's illness occasioned much mental turmoil in other places besides the deanery and adjoining library, and the idea which occurred to the meagre little prebendary about Mr. Slope did not occur to him alone.", "수석 사제의 병환은 수석사제관과 그에 딸린 도서관 말고도 여러 곳에서 적잖은 심란함을 불러일으켰고, 그 여윈 자그마한 참사회원이 슬로프 씨를 두고 떠올린 생각을 떠올린 사람은 그 하나만이 아니었다."]], [["The bishop was sitting listlessly in his study when the news reached him of the dean's illness.", "수석 사제가 병으로 쓰러졌다는 소식이 주교에게 닿았을 때 주교는 서재에 맥없이 앉아 있었다."], ["It was brought to him by Mr. Slope, who of course was not the last person in Barchester to hear it.", "소식을 가져온 것은 슬로프 씨였는데, 물론 그는 바체스터에서 그 소식을 맨 나중에 들은 사람이 아니었다."], ["It was also not slow in finding its way to Mrs. Proudie's ears.", "소식은 프라우디 부인의 귀에도 더디게 들어가지 않았다."], ["It may be presumed that there was not just then much friendly intercourse between these two rival claimants for his lordship's obedience.", "주교의 복종을 서로 차지하겠다고 다투는 이 두 사람 사이에 그 무렵 그리 다정한 왕래가 없었으리라는 것은 짐작하고도 남는다."], ["Indeed, though living in the same house, they had not met since the stormy interview between them in the bishop's study on the preceding day.", "실제로 두 사람은 한집에 살면서도 전날 주교의 서재에서 벌어진 그 험악한 담판 이후로 얼굴을 마주한 적이 없었다."]], [["On that occasion Mrs. Proudie had been defeated.", "그 자리에서 프라우디 부인은 패배했다."], ["That the prestige of continual victory should have been torn from her standards was a subject of great sorrow to that militant lady; but, though defeated, she was not overcome.", "늘 이겨 왔다는 위광이 제 군기에서 뜯겨 나갔다는 것은 그 호전적인 부인에게 크나큰 슬픔이었다. 그러나 패배했을 뿐 굴복한 것은 아니었다."], ["She felt that she might yet recover her lost ground, that she might yet hurl Mr. Slope down to the dust from which she had picked him, and force her sinning lord to sue for pardon in sackcloth and ashes.", "부인은 잃은 땅을 아직 되찾을 수 있다고, 제가 주워 올린 그 티끌 속으로 슬로프 씨를 다시 내던질 수 있다고, 그리고 죄를 지은 남편으로 하여금 굵은 베옷을 입고 재를 뒤집어쓴 채 용서를 빌게 만들 수 있다고 여겼다."]], [["On that memorable day, memorable for his mutiny and rebellion against her high behests, he had carried his way with a high hand, and had really begun to think it possible that the days of his slavery were counted.", "그 잊지 못할 날, 부인의 지엄한 분부에 대들어 반란을 일으켰다는 점에서 잊지 못할 그날, 주교는 강경하게 제 뜻을 관철했고, 정말로 제 노예살이도 이제 날수가 얼마 남지 않았을지 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["He had begun to hope that he was now about to enter into a free land, a land delicious with milk which he himself might quaff and honey which would not tantalize him by being only honey to the eye.", "이제 자유의 땅으로 들어서려는 참이라고, 제 손으로 들이켤 수 있는 젖이 흐르고 눈으로만 보라며 애를 태우지는 않을 꿀이 흐르는 그 감미로운 땅으로 들어서려는 참이라고 기대하기 시작했다."], ["When Mrs. Proudie banged the door as she left his room, he felt himself every inch a bishop.", "프라우디 부인이 방을 나서며 문을 쾅 닫았을 때 주교는 스스로가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 주교라고 느꼈다."], ["To be sure, his spirit had been a little cowed by his chaplain's subsequent lecture, but on the whole he was highly pleased with himself, and he flattered himself that the worst was over.", "물론 그 뒤에 이어진 전속 사제의 훈계에 기가 조금 죽기는 했으나, 대체로 주교는 제 자신이 몹시 흡족했고 최악은 지나갔다고 자위했다. “Ce n'est que le premier pas qui coûte.”"], ["\"Ce n'est que le premier pas qui coûte,\"", "그는 그렇게 되뇌었다."], ["he reflected, and now that the first step had been so magnanimously taken, all the rest would follow easily.", "첫걸음을 그토록 통 크게 내디딘 이상 나머지는 수월하게 따라오리라는 것이었다."]], [["He met his wife as a matter of course at dinner, where little or nothing was said that could ruffle the bishop's happiness.", "주교는 으레 그렇듯 만찬 자리에서 아내와 마주쳤는데, 그 자리에서는 주교의 행복을 어지럽힐 만한 말이 거의, 아니 전혀 오가지 않았다."], ["His daughters and the servants were present and protected him.", "딸들과 하인들이 자리에 있어 주교를 지켜 주었다."]], [["He made one or two trifling remarks on the subject of his projected visit to the archbishop, in order to show to all concerned that he intended to have his own way; the very servants, perceiving the change, transferred a little of their reverence from their mistress to their master.", "주교는 대주교를 찾아가기로 한 계획을 두고 사소한 말을 한두 마디 던졌는데, 이는 제 뜻대로 하겠다는 것을 관계된 모두에게 보이기 위함이었다. 하인들까지도 그 변화를 알아채고 안주인에게 바치던 공경을 조금쯤 바깥주인 쪽으로 옮겼다."], ["All which the master perceived, and so also did the mistress.", "바깥주인은 그것을 다 알아보았고, 안주인 또한 그러했다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie bided her time.", "그러나 프라우디 부인은 때를 기다렸다."]], [["After dinner he returned to his study, where Mr.", "만찬을 마치고 주교는 서재로 돌아갔고, 곧 슬로프 씨가 그리로 찾아와 둘이 함께 차를 마시며 여러 가지를 궁리했다."], ["Slope soon found him, and there they had tea together and planned many things.", "몇 분 동안 주교는 정말로 행복했다."], ["For some few minutes the bishop was really happy; but as the clock on the chimney-piece warned him that the stilly hours of night were drawing on, as he looked at his chamber candlestick and knew that he must use it, his heart sank within him again.", "그러나 벽난로 선반 위의 시계가 밤의 고요한 시각이 다가온다고 일러 주자, 침실용 촛대를 바라보며 저것을 써야만 한다는 것을 깨닫자, 주교의 가슴은 다시 내려앉았다. 그는 닭이 울면 이 위쪽 세상을 마음대로 떠도는 힘이 끝나 버리는 유령과도 같았다."], ["He was as a ghost, all whose power of wandering free through these upper regions ceases at cock-crow; or, rather, he was the opposite of the ghost, for till cock-crow he must again be a serf.", "아니, 차라리 유령의 반대였다. 닭이 울 때까지 그는 다시 종살이를 해야 했으니 말이다."], ["And would that be all?", "그리고 그것으로 끝일까?"], ["Could he trust himself to come down to breakfast a free man in the morning?", "아침에 자유로운 몸으로 조반상에 내려올 수 있으리라고 스스로를 믿어도 좋을까?"]], [["He was nearly an hour later than usual when he betook himself to his rest. Rest!", "주교가 잠자리에 들려고 몸을 일으킨 것은 여느 때보다 한 시간이나 늦어서였다."], ["What rest?", "잠자리라니!"], ["However, he took a couple of glasses of sherry and mounted the stairs.", "무슨 잠자리란 말인가? 어쨌든 그는 셰리를 두어 잔 마시고 계단을 올라갔다."], ["Far be it from us to follow him thither.", "우리로서는 거기까지 그를 따라갈 생각이 없다."], ["There are some things which no novelist, no historian, should attempt; some few scenes in life's drama which even no poet should dare to paint.", "어떤 소설가도, 어떤 역사가도 손대서는 안 될 일이 있는 법이며, 인생이라는 극에는 어떤 시인도 감히 그려 내려 해서는 안 될 몇몇 장면이 있는 법이다."], ["Let that which passed between Dr. Proudie and his wife on this night be understood to be among them.", "이날 밤 프라우디 박사와 그 아내 사이에 오간 일도 그런 것들 가운데 하나로 알아 두면 되겠다."]], [["He came down the following morning a sad and thoughtful man.", "이튿날 아침 주교는 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 사람이 되어 내려왔다."], ["He was attenuated in appearance--one might almost say emaciated.", "얼굴은 야위어 보였다. 수척해졌다고 해도 지나치지 않을 정도였다."], ["I doubt whether his now grizzled locks had not palpably become more grey than on the preceding evening.", "어느새 희끗해진 머리칼이 전날 저녁보다 눈에 띄게 더 세지 않았는지 의심스러울 지경이었다."], ["At any rate he had aged materially.", "아무튼 그는 부쩍 늙었다."], ["Years do not make a man old gradually and at an even pace.", "세월이 사람을 늙게 만드는 것은 서서히, 고른 걸음으로 이루어지는 일이 아니다."], ["Look through the world and see if this is not so always, except in those rare cases in which the human being lives and dies without joys and without sorrows, like a vegetable.", "세상을 두루 살펴보고 언제나 그러한지 아닌지 보라. 다만 기쁨도 슬픔도 없이 초목처럼 살다 죽는 드문 경우만은 예외다."], ["A man shall be possessed of florid, youthful blooming health till, it matters not what age--thirty; forty; fifty--then comes some nipping frost, some period of agony, that robs the fibres of the body of their succulence, and the hale and hearty man is counted among the old.", "사람은 혈색 좋고 젊고 활짝 핀 건강을 누리다가, 그 나이가 서른이든 마흔이든 쉰이든 상관없이 어느 날 살을 에는 서리가, 어떤 고통의 시절이 닥쳐와 육신의 결에서 물기를 앗아 가고, 그러면 그 정정하고 씩씩하던 사람이 노인 축에 들게 되는 것이다."]], [["He came down and breakfasted alone; Mrs.", "주교는 내려와 혼자 조반을 들었다."], ["Proudie, being indisposed, took her coffee in her bedroom, and her daughters waited upon her there.", "프라우디 부인은 몸이 편치 않아 침실에서 커피를 들었고 딸들이 거기서 시중을 들었다."], ["He ate his breakfast alone, and then, hardly knowing what he did, he betook himself to his usual seat in his study.", "그는 혼자 조반을 먹고 나서, 제가 무엇을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 서재의 늘 앉던 자리로 갔다."], ["He tried to solace himself with his coming visit to the archbishop.", "곧 있을 대주교 방문으로 마음을 달래 보려 했다."], ["That effort of his own free will at any rate remained to him as an enduring triumph.", "그 일만큼은 제 자유로운 뜻으로 이루어 낸 것이니 오래 남을 승리로 그에게 남아 있었다."], ["But somehow, now that he had achieved it, he did not seem to care so much about it.", "그런데 어쩐 일인지, 막상 이루고 나니 그것이 그리 대수롭게 여겨지지 않았다."], ["It was his ambition that had prompted him to take his place at the archiepiscopal table, and his ambition was now quite dead within him.", "대주교의 식탁에 자리를 잡도록 그를 부추긴 것은 야심이었는데, 그 야심이 이제 그의 안에서 아주 죽어 버린 것이다."]], [["He was thus seated when Mr. Slope made his appearance, with breathless impatience.", "그렇게 앉아 있는데 슬로프 씨가 숨이 턱에 닿아 안달하는 모습으로 나타났다."]], [["\"My lord, the dean is dead.\"", "“주교님, 수석 사제께서 세상을 떠나셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“맙소사!”"], ["exclaimed the bishop, startled out of his apathy by an announcement so sad and so sudden.", "주교가 외쳤다. 그토록 슬프고 그토록 갑작스러운 소식에 무기력에서 화들짝 깨어난 것이다."]], [["\"He is either dead or now dying.", "“이미 돌아가셨거나 지금 돌아가시는 중입니다."], ["He has had an apoplectic fit, and I am told that there is not the slightest hope; indeed, I do not doubt that by this time he is no more.\"", "졸중 발작으로 쓰러지셨는데 조금의 가망도 없다고 합니다. 아니, 지금쯤이면 이미 세상에 계시지 않으리라는 것을 저는 의심하지 않습니다.”"]], [["Bells were rung, and servants were immediately sent to inquire.", "종이 울리고 하인들이 곧바로 알아보러 나갔다."], ["In the course of the morning the bishop, leaning on his chaplain's arm, himself called at the deanery door.", "그날 오전에 주교는 전속 사제의 팔에 기대어 몸소 수석사제관 문 앞까지 찾아갔다."], ["Mrs. Proudie sent to Miss Trefoil all manner of offers of assistance.", "프라우디 부인은 미스 트리폴에게 온갖 도움을 주겠다는 전갈을 보냈다."], ["The Misses Proudie sent also, and there was immense sympathy between the palace and the deanery.", "프라우디 양들도 사람을 보냈으니, 주교관과 수석사제관 사이에는 어마어마한 동정이 오갔다."], ["The answer to all inquiries was unvaried.", "어느 문의에나 돌아오는 대답은 한결같았다."], ["The dean was just the same, and Sir Omicron Pie was expected down by the 9.15 P.M. train.", "수석 사제는 그대로이고, 오미크론 파이 경이 밤 9시 15분 기차로 내려오기로 되어 있다는 것이었다."]], [["And then Mr. Slope began to meditate, as others also had done, as to who might possibly be the new dean, and it occurred to him, as it had also occurred to others, that it might be possible that he should be the new dean himself.", "그러고 나서 슬로프 씨는, 다른 이들이 그러했듯이, 누가 새 수석 사제가 될 것인가를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했고, 다른 이들에게 그러했듯이 그에게도 자기 자신이 새 수석 사제가 될 수도 있지 않겠는가 하는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["And then the question as to the twelve hundred, or fifteen hundred, or two thousand ran in his mind, as it had run through those of the other clergymen in the cathedral library.", "그리고 1,200이냐 1,500이냐 2,000이냐 하는 문제가, 대성당 도서관에 있던 다른 성직자들의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔던 것과 똑같이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."]], [["Whether it might be two thousand, or fifteen, or twelve hundred, it would in any case undoubtedly be a great thing for him, if he could get it.", "2,000이든 1,500이든 1,200이든, 손에 넣기만 한다면 그에게는 어느 쪽이든 틀림없이 대단한 일이었다."], ["The gratification to his ambition would be greater even than that of his covetousness.", "탐욕이 채워지는 것보다 야심이 채워지는 쪽이 더 흡족할 터였다."], ["How glorious to out-top the archdeacon in his own cathedral city; to sit above prebendaries and canons and have the cathedral pulpit and all the cathedral services altogether at his own disposal!", "부주교의 본거지인 그 대성당 도시에서 부주교를 내려다보고, 참사회원과 참사원들 위에 앉아, 대성당 설교단과 대성당의 모든 예식을 통째로 제 마음대로 주무른다니 이 얼마나 영광스러운 일인가!"]], [["But it might be easier to wish for this than to obtain it. Mr.", "그러나 이것은 바라기는 쉬워도 얻기는 어려운 일일 수 있었다."], ["Slope, however, was not without some means of forwarding his views, and he at any rate did not let the grass grow under his feet.", "그렇지만 슬로프 씨에게 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 방도가 아주 없는 것은 아니었고, 적어도 그는 발밑에 풀이 자라도록 내버려 두는 사람이 아니었다."], ["In the first place, he thought--and not vainly--that he could count upon what assistance the bishop could give him.", "우선 그는 주교가 줄 수 있는 도움만큼은 기대해도 좋다고 생각했는데, 그것은 헛된 생각이 아니었다."], ["He immediately changed his views with regard to his patron; he made up his mind that if he became dean, he would hand his lordship back again to his wife's vassalage; and he thought it possible that his lordship might not be sorry to rid himself of one of his mentors.", "그는 제 후원자를 보는 눈을 곧바로 바꾸었다. 수석 사제가 되기만 하면 주교를 그 아내의 종살이로 도로 넘겨주리라 마음먹은 것이다."], ["Mr. Slope had also taken some steps towards making his name known to other men in power.", "그리고 주교로서도 훈수꾼 하나를 덜어 내는 것을 굳이 아쉬워하지는 않으리라고 보았다."], ["There was a certain chief-commissioner of national schools, who at the present moment was presumed to stand especially high in the good graces of the government bigwigs, and with him Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 권세 있는 다른 이들에게 제 이름을 알려 두는 일에도 얼마간 손을 써 두었다. 국립학교 총감독관이라는 사람이 하나 있었는데, 당시 정부 요인들의 총애를 각별히 받고 있다고들 하는 인물이었다."], ["Slope had contrived to establish a sort of epistolary intimacy.", "슬로프 씨는 그와 서신을 주고받는 사이쯤은 그럭저럭 맺어 두었다."], ["He thought that he might safely apply to Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, and he felt sure that if Sir Nicholas chose to exert himself, the promise of such a piece of preferment would be had for the asking.", "그는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경에게라면 마음 놓고 부탁해도 되겠다고 여겼고, 니컬러스 경이 나서 주기만 한다면 그만한 성직 승진의 약조쯤은 청하기만 해도 얻어지리라 확신했다."]], [["Then he also had the press at his bidding, or flattered himself that he had so.", "게다가 그에게는 언론이 제 손안에 있었다. 적어도 그는 그렇다고 자부했다."], ["\"The Daily Jupiter\" had taken his part in a very thorough manner in those polemical contests of his with Mr.", "애러빈 씨와 벌인 그 논쟁들에서 『데일리 주피터』는 아주 철저하게 그의 편을 들어 주었다."], ["Arabin; he had on more than one occasion absolutely had an interview with a gentleman on the staff of that paper who, if not the editor, was as good as the editor; and he had long been in the habit of writing telling letters on all manner of ecclesiastical abuses, which he signed with his initials, and sent to his editorial friend with private notes signed in his own name.", "그는 한 번 이상 그 신문사 직원 하나를 실제로 만난 적이 있었는데, 편집장은 아니라 해도 편집장이나 다름없는 사람이었다. 또 그는 오래전부터 온갖 교회의 폐단을 짚는 매서운 투고를 써서 제 이름 첫 글자로 서명해 그 편집자 친구에게 보내면서, 제 본명으로 서명한 사신을 함께 부치는 버릇이 있었다."], ["Indeed, he and Mr. Towers--such was the name of the powerful gentleman of the press with whom he was connected--were generally very amiable with each other.", "실제로 그와 타워스 씨는, 그가 연을 맺고 있던 그 세도 있는 언론인의 이름이 그러했는데, 대체로 서로 아주 살가운 사이였다."], ["Mr. Slope's little productions were always printed and occasionally commented upon; and thus, in a small sort of way, he had become a literary celebrity.", "슬로프 씨의 자잘한 글은 언제나 활자가 되었고 이따금 논평까지 얻었다."], ["This public life had great charms for him, though it certainly also had its drawbacks.", "그리하여 그는 소소하게나마 문필가로 이름이 났다. 이 공적인 생활은 그에게 크나큰 매력이 있었으나, 물론 그 나름의 폐단도 없지 않았다."], ["On one occasion, when speaking in the presence of reporters, he had failed to uphold and praise and swear by that special line of conduct which had been upheld and praised and sworn by in \"The Jupiter,\" and then he had been much surprised and at the moment not a little irritated to find himself lacerated most unmercifully by his old ally.", "한번은 기자들이 있는 자리에서 말을 하다가 『주피터』가 떠받들고 칭송하며 맹세하던 그 특정한 노선을 함께 떠받들고 칭송하며 맹세하지 못한 일이 있었는데, 그러자 그는 옛 동지에게 무자비하게 난도질당하는 자신을 발견하고는 몹시 놀랐고 그 순간에는 적잖이 화도 났다."], ["He was quizzed and bespattered and made a fool of, just as though, or rather worse than if, he had been a constant enemy instead of a constant friend.", "그는 놀림감이 되고 흙탕물을 뒤집어쓰고 웃음거리가 되었다. 한결같은 벗이 아니라 한결같은 적이기라도 했던 것처럼, 아니 그보다 더 심하게 당했다."], ["He had hitherto not learnt that a man who aspires to be on the staff of \"The Jupiter\" must surrender all individuality.", "『주피터』의 논객이 되기를 바라는 자는 제 개성을 죄다 내놓아야 한다는 것을 그는 그때까지 배우지 못했던 것이다."], ["But ultimately this little castigation had broken no bones between him and his friend Mr. Towers.", "그러나 이 자그마한 매질이 결국 그와 벗 타워스 씨 사이의 뼈를 부러뜨리지는 않았다."], ["Mr. Slope was one of those who understood the world too well to show himself angry with such a potentate as \"The Jupiter.\"", "슬로프 씨는 『주피터』 같은 권세가에게 성을 내 보이기에는 세상 물정을 너무 잘 아는 사람이었다."], ["He had kissed the rod that scourged him, and now thought that he might fairly look for his reward.", "그는 자기를 때린 매에 입을 맞추었고, 이제는 그 보답을 바라도 좋으리라 여겼다."], ["He determined that he would at once let Mr. Towers know that he was a candidate for the place which was about to become vacant.", "그는 곧 비게 될 그 자리의 후보가 자기라는 것을 당장 타워스 씨에게 알리기로 마음먹었다."], ["More than one piece of preferment had lately been given away much in accordance with advice tendered to the government in the columns of \"The Jupiter.\"", "근래에 한 자리 이상의 성직 승진이 『주피터』 지면에서 정부에 건넨 조언 그대로 주어진 바 있었다."]], [["But it was incumbent on Mr. Slope first to secure the bishop.", "그러나 슬로프 씨로서는 무엇보다 먼저 주교를 붙들어 두어야 했다."], ["He specially felt that it behoved him to do this before the visit to the archbishop was made.", "그는 특히 대주교 방문이 이루어지기 전에 그 일을 해 두어야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["It was really quite providential that the dean should have fallen ill just at the very nick of time.", "하필 이 절묘한 때에 수석 사제가 쓰러진 것은 참으로 하늘이 도운 일이었다."], ["If Dr. Proudie could be instigated to take the matter up warmly, he might manage a good deal while staying at the archbishop's palace.", "프라우디 박사가 이 일을 열심히 떠맡도록 부추길 수만 있다면, 대주교의 관저에 머무는 동안 상당한 일을 해낼 수도 있었다."], ["Feeling this very strongly, Mr. Slope determined to sound the bishop that very afternoon.", "이 점을 아주 절실하게 느낀 슬로프 씨는 바로 그날 오후에 주교의 속을 떠보기로 마음먹었다."], ["He was to start on the following morning to London, and therefore not a moment could be lost with safety.", "주교는 이튿날 아침 런던으로 떠날 참이었으니, 잠시도 마음 놓고 지체할 여유가 없었다."]], [["He went into the bishop's study about five o'clock and found him still sitting alone.", "그는 다섯 시쯤 주교의 서재로 들어갔고, 주교가 여전히 혼자 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["It might have been supposed that he had hardly moved since the little excitement occasioned by his walk to the dean's door.", "수석 사제의 집 문 앞까지 걸어갔던 그 잠깐의 소동 이후로 거의 움직이지도 않은 듯싶었다."], ["He still wore on his face that dull, dead look of half-unconscious suffering.", "얼굴에는 반쯤 넋이 나간 고통의 그 흐리고 죽은 빛이 그대로 서려 있었다."], ["He was doing nothing, reading nothing, thinking of nothing, but simply gazing on vacancy when Mr. Slope for the second time that day entered his room.", "그날 두 번째로 슬로프 씨가 방에 들어섰을 때, 주교는 아무것도 하지 않고 아무것도 읽지 않고 아무것도 생각하지 않은 채 그저 허공을 바라보고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Well, Slope,\"", "“왜 그러나, 슬로프.” 주교가 다소 짜증스레 말했다."], ["said he somewhat impatiently, for, to tell the truth, he was not anxious just at present to have much conversation with Mr. Slope.", "실은 그로서는 지금 슬로프 씨와 길게 이야기를 나누고 싶지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["\"Your lordship will be sorry to hear that as yet the poor dean has shown no sign of amendment.\"", "“애석하게도 가엾은 수석 사제께서 아직 차도를 보이지 않으신다고 하니, 주교님께서 마음이 아프시겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh--ah--hasn't he?", "“오, 아, 그런가?"], ["Poor man!", "딱한 사람 같으니!"], ["I'm sure I'm very sorry.", "나도 정말 안타깝네."], ["I suppose Sir Omicron has not arrived yet?\"", "오미크론 경은 아직 도착하지 않았겠지?”"]], [["\"No, not till the 9.15 P.M. train.\"", "“예, 밤 9시 15분 기차라야 도착하십니다.”"]], [["\"I wonder they didn't have a special.", "“특별 열차를 내지 않은 것이 이상하군."], ["They say Dr. Trefoil is very rich.\"", "트리폴 박사가 아주 부자라던데.”"]], [["\"Very rich, I believe,\"", "“대단한 부자시지요.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"But the truth is, all the doctors in London can do no good--no other good than to show that every possible care has been taken.", "“그러나 사실을 말씀드리자면 런던의 의사들이 다 온들 아무 소용이 없습니다. 할 수 있는 조치는 모두 취했다는 것을 보여 주는 것 말고는요."], ["Poor Dr. Trefoil is not long for this world, my lord.\"", "가엾은 트리폴 박사께서는 이 세상에 오래 계시지 못할 것입니다, 주교님.”"]], [["\"I suppose not--I suppose not.\"", "“그렇겠지, 그렇겠지.”"]], [["\"Oh, no; indeed, his best friends could not wish that he should outlive such a shock, for his intellects cannot possibly survive it.\"", "“예, 그렇습니다. 실은 아무리 가까운 벗이라도 그분이 이런 충격을 겪고 나서까지 살아 계시기를 바랄 수는 없을 것입니다. 지력이 도저히 온전할 수 없을 테니까요.”"]], [["\"Poor man!", "“딱한 사람!"], ["Poor man!\"", "딱한 사람!”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"It will naturally be a matter of much moment to your lordship who is to succeed him,\"", "“뒤를 누가 잇느냐 하는 것은 당연히 주교님께 대단히 중요한 문제입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["\"It would be a great thing if you could secure the appointment for some person of your own way of thinking on important points.", "“긴요한 문제에서 주교님과 생각을 같이하는 분에게 그 자리가 돌아가도록 해 두실 수 있다면 크나큰 일이 될 것입니다."], ["The party hostile to us are very strong here in Barchester--much too strong.\"", "저희에게 적대적인 쪽이 여기 바체스터에서는 아주 강합니다. 지나치게 강합니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, yes. If poor Dr.", "“그렇지, 그래."], ["Trefoil is to go, it will be a great thing to get a good man in his place.\"", "가엾은 트리폴 박사가 가야 한다면, 그 자리에 좋은 사람을 앉히는 것이 큰일이지.”"]], [["\"It will be everything to your lordship to get a man on whose co-operation you can reckon. Only think what trouble we might have if Dr.", "“주교님과 손발을 맞출 수 있는 사람을 얻으신다면 그야말로 전부를 얻으시는 셈입니다."], ["Grantly, or Dr. Hyandry, or any of that way of thinking were to get it.\"", "그랜틀리 박사나 하이앤드리 박사, 혹은 그런 부류의 누군가가 그 자리를 차지한다면 저희가 얼마나 곤란해질지 생각해 보십시오.”"]], [["\"It is not very probable that Lord ---- will give it to any of that school; why should he?\"", "“—— 경께서 그 학파의 누군가에게 그 자리를 주실 것 같지는 않네. 무엇 하러 그러시겠나?”"]], [["\"No.", "“예."], ["Not probable; certainly not; but it's possible.", "그럴 법하지는 않지요. 분명 그렇습니다."], ["Great interest will probably be made.", "그러나 있을 수는 있는 일입니다."], ["If I might venture to advise your lordship, I would suggest that you should discuss the matter with his grace next week.", "아마 대단한 청탁들이 오갈 것입니다. 감히 주교님께 말씀을 올려도 된다면, 다음 주에 대주교 각하와 이 일을 의논해 보시라고 권하고 싶습니다."], ["I have no doubt that your wishes, if made known and backed by his grace, would be paramount with Lord ----.\"", "주교님의 뜻이 알려지고 각하께서 밀어 주신다면 —— 경께 결정적인 힘이 되리라는 것을 저는 의심하지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"Well, I don't know that; Lord ---- has always been very kind to me, very kind.", "“글쎄, 그건 모를 일이지. —— 경은 늘 내게 아주 친절했네, 아주 친절했지."], ["But I am unwilling to interfere in such matters unless asked.", "하지만 청을 받지도 않았는데 그런 일에 끼어들기는 내키지 않아."], ["And indeed if asked, I don't know whom, at this moment, I should recommend.\"", "그리고 설령 청을 받는다 해도, 지금 이 순간 누구를 천거해야 할지 나는 모르겠네.”"]], [["Mr. Slope, even Mr.", "슬로프 씨는, 그 슬로프 씨조차도 이때만은 다소 머쓱해졌다."], ["Slope, felt at the present rather abashed. He hardly knew how to frame his little request in language sufficiently modest.", "제 자그마한 청을 어떻게 하면 충분히 겸손한 말에 담아낼 수 있을지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["He had recognized and acknowledged to himself the necessity of shocking the bishop in the first instance by the temerity of his application, and his difficulty was how best to remedy that by his adroitness and eloquence.", "그는 이 뻔뻔한 청으로 처음에는 주교를 놀라게 할 수밖에 없다는 것을 스스로 알고 인정하고 있었으므로, 그 놀라움을 제 능란함과 언변으로 어떻게 가장 잘 수습하느냐가 문제였다. “저로서는 과연 주교님께서 당장 마음에 두신 분이 계실지 의심스러웠습니다.”"], ["\"I doubted myself,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"whether your lordship would have anyone immediately in your eye, and it is on this account that I venture to submit to you an idea that I have been turning over in my own mind.", "“그래서 제가 혼자 이리저리 굴려 보던 생각을 감히 여쭙는 것입니다."], ["If poor Dr. Trefoil must go, I really do not see why, with your lordship's assistance, I should not hold the preferment myself.\"", "가엾은 트리폴 박사께서 정녕 가셔야 한다면, 주교님께서 도와주시기만 한다면, 그 자리를 제가 맡지 못할 까닭이 무엇인지 저로서는 정말 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"You!\"", "“자네가!”"], ["exclaimed the bishop in a manner that Mr. Slope could hardly have considered complimentary.", "주교가 외쳤는데, 슬로프 씨로서는 그 말투를 도저히 칭찬으로 여길 수 없었다."]], [["The ice was now broken, and Mr. Slope became fluent enough.", "이제 얼음이 깨졌고, 슬로프 씨는 제법 말이 술술 나왔다."], ["\"I have been thinking of looking for it.", "“저는 그 자리를 노려 볼까 생각해 왔습니다."], ["If your lordship will press the matter on the archbishop, I do not doubt but I shall succeed.", "주교님께서 대주교께 이 일을 밀어붙여 주신다면 제가 성공하리라는 것을 의심하지 않습니다."], ["You see I shall be the first to move, which is a great matter.", "보십시오, 제가 가장 먼저 움직이는 셈이 되는데 이것이 대단한 일입니다."], ["Then I can count upon assistance from the public press: my name is known, I may say, somewhat favourably known, to that portion of the press which is now most influential with the government; and I have friends also in the government.", "그리고 저는 언론의 도움도 기대할 수 있습니다. 제 이름은 지금 정부에 가장 영향력이 큰 그 언론 쪽에 알려져 있고, 다소 호의적으로 알려져 있다고 말씀드려도 좋을 것입니다."], ["But nevertheless it is to you, my lord, that I look for assistance.", "정부 안에도 제게는 벗이 있습니다. 그러나 그럼에도 제가 도움을 바라보는 곳은 바로 주교님이십니다."], ["It is from your hands that I would most willingly receive the benefit.", "제가 가장 기꺼이 이 은혜를 받고자 하는 것은 주교님의 손에서입니다."], ["And, which should ever be the chief consideration in such matters, you must know better than any other person whatsoever what qualifications I possess.\"", "그리고 이런 일에서 언제나 으뜸으로 헤아려야 할 바이겠습니다만, 제게 어떤 자질이 있는지는 다른 어느 누구보다 주교님께서 잘 아실 것입니다.”"]], [["The bishop sat for awhile dumbfounded.", "주교는 한동안 어안이 벙벙해져 앉아 있었다."], ["Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester!", "슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제라니!"], ["The idea of such a transformation of character would never have occurred to his own unaided intellect.", "그런 인물의 탈바꿈은 주교 혼자의 머리로는 결코 떠오르지 않았을 생각이었다."], ["At first he went on thinking why, for what reasons, on what account, Mr. Slope should be Dean of Barchester.", "처음에 주교는 어째서, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 근거로 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어야 하는지를 따져 보았다."], ["But by degrees the direction of his thoughts changed, and he began to think why, for what reasons, on what account, Mr. Slope should not be Dean of Barchester.", "그러나 차츰 생각의 방향이 바뀌어, 어째서, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 근거로 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어서는 안 되는지를 따지기 시작했다."], ["As far as he himself, the bishop, was concerned, he could well spare the services of his chaplain.", "주교 자신으로 말하자면 전속 사제의 시중쯤은 없어도 그만이었다."], ["That little idea of using Mr. Slope as a counterpoise to his wife had well nigh evaporated.", "슬로프 씨를 아내에 맞서는 저울추로 쓴다는 그 자그마한 생각은 이미 거의 증발해 버렸다."], ["He had all but acknowledged the futility of the scheme.", "그 계책이 부질없다는 것을 그는 사실상 인정한 터였다."], ["If indeed he could have slept in his chaplain's bedroom instead of his wife's, there might have been something in it. But--.", "정말이지 아내의 침실이 아니라 전속 사제의 침실에서 잘 수만 있다면 또 모를 일이었다."], ["And thus as Mr.", "그러나ㅡ."], ["Slope was speaking, the bishop began to recognize the idea that that gentleman might become Dean of Barchester without impropriety--not moved, indeed, by Mr.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨가 말하는 동안 주교는 저 사람이 바체스터 수석 사제가 되어도 온당치 못할 것은 없겠다는 생각에 이르렀다."], ["Slope's eloquence, for he did not follow the tenor of his speech, but led thereto by his own cogitations.", "슬로프 씨의 언변에 움직인 것은 물론 아니었으니, 주교는 그 말의 흐름을 따라가지도 않았다. 그저 제 궁리에 이끌려 그리로 간 것이다."]], [["\"I need not say,\" continued Mr.", "“새삼 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠습니다만,”"], ["Slope, \"that it would be my chief desire to act in all matters connected with the cathedral as far as possible in accordance with your views.", "슬로프 씨가 말을 이었다. “대성당에 관한 모든 일에서 되도록 주교님의 뜻에 맞추어 처리하는 것이 저의 으뜸가는 바람일 것입니다."], ["I know your lordship so well (and I hope you know me well enough to have the same feelings) that I am satisfied that my being in that position would add materially to your own comfort, and enable you to extend the sphere of your useful influence.", "저는 주교님을 워낙 잘 알고 있으며, 주교님께서도 저를 그만큼 아셔서 같은 마음이시기를 바랍니다만, 그래서 제가 그 자리에 있게 되면 주교님의 마음이 한결 편해지고 주교님의 유익한 영향력이 미치는 범위도 넓어지리라 확신합니다."], ["As I said before, it is most desirable that there should be but one opinion among the dignitaries of the same diocese.", "앞서 말씀드렸듯이, 같은 교구의 고위 성직자들 사이에 의견이 하나뿐인 것이 가장 바람직합니다."], ["I doubt much whether I would accept such an appointment in any diocese in which I should be constrained to differ much from the bishop.", "주교와 크게 어긋날 수밖에 없는 교구라면 저는 그런 자리를 과연 받아들일지 심히 의심스럽습니다."], ["In this case there would be a delightful uniformity of opinion.\"", "이 경우에는 참으로 흐뭇한 의견의 일치가 있을 것입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope perfectly well perceived that the bishop did not follow a word that he said, but nevertheless he went on talking.", "슬로프 씨는 주교가 제 말을 한마디도 따라오지 못한다는 것을 아주 잘 알아차렸으나, 그래도 계속 지껄였다."], ["He knew it was necessary that Dr. Proudie should recover from his surprise, and he knew also that he must give him the opportunity of appearing to have been persuaded by argument.", "프라우디 박사가 놀란 마음을 추스를 시간이 필요하다는 것을 알았고, 또 논변에 설득당한 것처럼 보일 기회를 주어야 한다는 것도 알았기 때문이다."], ["So he went on and produced a multitude of fitting reasons all tending to show that no one on earth could make so good a Dean of Barchester as himself, that the government and the public would assuredly coincide in desiring that he, Mr. Slope, should be Dean of Barchester, but that for high considerations of ecclesiastical polity it would be especially desirable that this piece of preferment should be so bestowed through the instrumentality of the bishop of the diocese.", "그리하여 그는 말을 이어 가며 온갖 그럴듯한 이유를 늘어놓았는데, 하나같이 이 세상 누구도 자기만큼 훌륭한 바체스터 수석 사제가 될 수 없다는 것, 자기 슬로프가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되기를 정부도 대중도 반드시 한마음으로 바라리라는 것, 그러면서도 교회 정치의 드높은 고려에서 볼 때 이 성직 승진이 교구 주교의 손을 거쳐 주어지는 것이 특별히 바람직하다는 것을 보이려는 이야기였다."]], [["\"But I really don't know what I could do in the matter,\"", "“허나 그 일에서 내가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 정말 모르겠네.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"If you would mention it to the archbishop; if you could tell his grace that you consider such an appointment very desirable, that you have it much at heart with a view to putting an end to schism in the diocese; if you did this with your usual energy, you would probably find no difficulty in inducing his grace to promise that he would mention it to Lord ----.", "“대주교께 말씀만 꺼내 주신다면, 그런 임명이 매우 바람직하다고 여기신다고, 교구의 분열을 끝내기 위해 그 일을 마음에 깊이 두고 계신다고 각하께 말씀해 주신다면, 그리고 늘 그러시듯 힘차게 그리해 주신다면, 각하께서 —— 경께 그 말씀을 전하겠노라 약조하시게 하는 데 아마 아무 어려움도 없으실 것입니다."], ["Of course you would let the archbishop know that I am not looking for the preferment solely through his intervention; that you do not exactly require him to ask it as a favour; that you expect that I shall get it through other sources, as is indeed the case; but that you are very anxious that his grace should express his approval of such an arrangement to Lord ----.\"", "물론 제가 오로지 각하의 주선만으로 이 자리를 노리는 것은 아니라는 것, 각하께 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하시는 것까지는 아니라는 것, 실제로 그러하듯 제가 다른 경로로도 이 자리를 얻으리라 보신다는 것을 대주교께 알려 주십시오. 다만 각하께서 —— 경께 그런 처사에 찬성한다고 표해 주시기를 주교님께서 몹시 바라신다고 말입니다.”"]], [["It ended in the bishop promising to do as he was bid.", "결국 주교는 시키는 대로 하겠노라 약속하고 말았다."], ["Not that he so promised without a stipulation.", "그렇다고 조건 없이 약속한 것은 아니었다."], ["\"About that hospital,\"", "“그 요양원 말인데.”"], ["he said in the middle of the conference.", "주교가 의논 도중에 말했다."], ["\"I was never so troubled in my life\"--which was about the truth.", "“내 평생 그렇게 애를 태운 적이 없네.” 이는 사실에 가까운 말이었다."], ["\"You haven't spoken to Mr. Harding since I saw you?\"", "“나를 만난 뒤로 하딩 씨에게 이야기를 하지는 않았겠지?”"]], [["Mr. Slope assured his patron that he had not.", "슬로프 씨는 그런 일이 없다고 제 후원자에게 다짐했다."]], [["\"Ah well, then--I think upon the whole it will be better to let Quiverful have it.", "“아, 그렇다면ㅡ아무래도 전체로 보아 퀴버풀에게 주는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이네."], ["It has been half-promised to him, and he has a large family and is very poor.", "반쯤은 약속이 된 셈이고, 식솔은 많은데 몹시 가난하지 않은가."], ["I think on the whole it will be better to make out the nomination for Mr. Quiverful.\"", "아무래도 퀴버풀 씨 앞으로 임명장을 작성하는 편이 낫겠어.”"]], [["\"But, my lord,\" said Mr.", "“하지만 주교님,”"], ["Slope, still thinking that he was bound to make a fight for his own view on this matter, and remembering that it still behoved him to maintain his lately acquired supremacy over Mrs.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. 이 문제에서만큼은 제 뜻을 위해 싸워야 한다고 여전히 생각했고, 수석사제관 건에서 낭패를 보지 않으려면 근래 프라우디 부인에게서 얻어 낸 우위를 지켜야 한다는 것도 잊지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Proudie, lest he should fail in his views regarding the deanery, \"but, my lord, I am really much afraid--\"", "“하지만 주교님, 저로서는 정말 몹시 염려스럽습니다ㅡ”"]], [["\"Remember, Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨, 기억해 두게.”"], ["said the bishop, \"I can hold out no sort of hope to you in this matter of succeeding poor Dr.", "주교가 말했다. “가엾은 트리폴 박사의 뒤를 잇는 이 일에서 나는 자네에게 아무런 기대도 걸어 줄 수 없네."], ["Trefoil. I will certainly speak to the archbishop, as you wish it, but I cannot think--\"", "자네가 바라니 대주교께 말씀은 분명히 드리겠네만, 아무래도 나는ㅡ”"]], [["\"Well, my lord,\" said Mr.", "“알겠습니다, 주교님.”"], ["Slope, fully understanding the bishop and in his turn interrupting him, \"perhaps your lordship is right about Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 주교의 뜻을 온전히 알아채고는 이번에는 제 쪽에서 말을 끊으며 말했다. “퀴버풀 씨 건은 아마 주교님 말씀이 옳으실 것입니다."], ["Quiverful. I have no doubt I can easily arrange matters with Mr.", "하딩 씨와는 제가 손쉽게 이야기를 매듭지을 수 있으리라 믿습니다."], ["Harding, and I will make out the nomination for your signature as you direct.\"", "분부하신 대로 주교님께서 서명하실 임명장을 작성해 두겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, Slope, I think that will be best; and you may be sure that any little that I can do to forward your views shall be done.\"", "“그래, 슬로프, 그게 가장 낫겠네. 그리고 자네 뜻을 밀어 주는 데 내가 할 수 있는 자잘한 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠으니 안심하게.”"]], [["And so they parted.", "그리하여 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["Mr. Slope had now much business on his hands.", "이제 슬로프 씨에게는 처리할 일이 많았다."], ["He had to make his daily visit to the signora.", "시뇨라를 날마다 찾아가는 일이 있었다."], ["This common prudence should have now induced him to omit, but he was infatuated, and could not bring himself to be commonly prudent.", "웬만한 분별이 있었다면 이제 그 일은 그만두어야 마땅했으나, 그는 홀려 있었고 웬만한 분별을 차릴 수가 없었다."], ["He determined therefore that he would drink tea at the Stanhopes', and he determined also, or thought that he determined, that having done so he would go thither no more.", "그리하여 그는 스탠호프 댁에서 차를 마시기로 마음먹었고, 그러고 나면 다시는 그리로 가지 않겠다고 마음먹었다. 아니, 마음먹었다고 스스로 여겼다."], ["He had also to arrange his matters with Mrs. Bold.", "볼드 부인과의 일도 매듭지어야 했다."], ["He was of opinion that Eleanor would grace the deanery as perfectly as she would the chaplain's cottage, and he thought, moreover, that Eleanor's fortune would excellently repair any dilapidations and curtailments in the dean's stipend which might have been made by that ruthless ecclesiastical commission.", "그가 보기에 엘리너는 전속 사제의 오두막을 빛내는 것 못지않게 수석사제관도 완벽하게 빛낼 것이었다. 게다가 그 무자비한 교회 재산 위원회가 수석 사제의 봉록을 헐어 내고 깎아 놓았을지도 모르니, 엘리너의 재산이 그것을 훌륭하게 메워 주리라고 생각했다."]], [["Touching Mrs. Bold his hopes now soared high. Mr. Slope was one of that numerous multitude of swains who think that all is fair in love, and he had accordingly not refrained from using the services of Mrs. Bold's own maid. From her he had learnt much of what had taken place at Plumstead--not exactly with truth, for \"the own maid\" had not been able to divine the exact truth, but with some sort of similitude to it. He had been told that the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly and Mr. Harding and Mr. Arabin had all quarrelled with \"missus\" for having received a letter from Mr. Slope; that \"missus\" had positively refused to give the letter up; that she had received from the archdeacon the option of giving up either Mr. Slope and his letter, or else the society of Plumstead Rectory; and that \"missus\" had declared, with much indignation, that \"she didn't care a straw for the society of Plumstead Rectory,\" and that she wouldn't give up Mr. Slope for any of them.", "볼드 부인에 관해서라면 그의 희망은 이제 드높이 솟아 있었다. 슬로프 씨는 사랑에서는 무엇이든 정당하다고 여기는 그 숱한 구애자 무리 가운데 하나였으니, 볼드 부인의 하녀를 부리는 일도 마다하지 않았다. 그 하녀에게서 그는 플럼스테드에서 있었던 일을 많이 들었다. 정확히 사실 그대로는 아니었으니 그 “하녀”가 정확한 사실을 알아낼 수는 없었기 때문이나, 그래도 얼마쯤 사실과 닮은 이야기였다. 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인과 하딩 씨와 애러빈 씨가 “마님”이 슬로프 씨에게서 편지를 받았다는 이유로 모두 마님과 다투었다는 것, “마님”이 그 편지를 내놓기를 딱 잘라 거절했다는 것, 부주교가 마님에게 슬로프 씨와 그 편지를 버리든가 아니면 플럼스테드 목사관 식구들과의 왕래를 버리든가 하라고 했다는 것, 그러자 “마님”이 잔뜩 성이 나서 “플럼스테드 목사관 식구들과의 왕래 따위는 지푸라기만큼도 아깝지 않다”고, 그리고 그들 누구를 위해서도 슬로프 씨를 버리지 않겠다고 선언했다는 것이었다."]], [["Considering the source from whence this came, it was not quite so untrue as might have been expected.", "이야기가 나온 출처를 생각하면 이것은 예상할 법한 것만큼 터무니없지는 않았다."], ["It showed pretty plainly what had been the nature of the conversation in the servants'", "하인방에서 어떤 이야기가 오갔는지가 제법 뚜렷이 드러나는 이야기였다."], ["hall; and, coupled as it was with the certainty of Eleanor's sudden return, it appeared to Mr. Slope to be so far worthy of credit as to justify him in thinking that the fair widow would in all human probability accept his offer.", "그리고 엘리너가 갑작스레 돌아왔다는 확실한 사실과 나란히 놓고 보니, 슬로프 씨에게는 이 이야기가 그 아리따운 미망인이 사람의 이치로 보아 십중팔구 제 청혼을 받아들이리라 여겨도 좋을 만큼 믿을 만하게 보였다."]], [["All this work was therefore to be done.", "그러니 이 모든 일을 해내야 했다."], ["It was desirable, he thought, that he should make his offer before it was known that Mr. Quiverful was finally appointed to the hospital.", "그가 보기에는 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원에 최종 임명되었다는 것이 알려지기 전에 청혼을 해 두는 것이 바람직했다."], ["In his letter to Eleanor he had plainly declared that Mr. Harding was to have the appointment.", "엘리너에게 보낸 편지에서 그는 하딩 씨가 그 자리를 맡게 되리라고 분명히 말해 두었던 것이다."], ["It would be very difficult to explain this away, and were he to write another letter to Eleanor, telling the truth and throwing the blame on the bishop, it would naturally injure him in her estimation.", "이것을 둘러대기란 몹시 어려운 일일 터였고, 그렇다고 엘리너에게 다시 편지를 써서 사실을 알리고 그 탓을 주교에게 돌린다면 자연히 그녀가 보는 그의 값이 떨어질 것이었다."], ["He determined therefore to let that matter disclose itself as it would, and to lose no time in throwing himself at her feet.", "그리하여 그는 그 일은 저절로 드러나도록 내버려 두고, 한시도 지체하지 않고 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던지기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Then he had to solicit the assistance of Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin and Mr. Towers, and he went directly from the bishop's presence to compose his letters to those gentlemen.", "그러고 나서 그는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경과 타워스 씨의 도움을 청해야 했으므로, 주교 앞에서 물러나자마자 곧장 그 두 신사에게 보낼 편지를 짓기 시작했다."], ["As Mr. Slope was esteemed an adept at letter writing, they shall be given in full.", "슬로프 씨는 편지 쓰기의 명수로 통했으니, 그 편지들을 여기 온전히 옮겨 둔다."]], [["(Private) Palace, Barchester, Sept. 185--", "(친전) 바체스터 주교관, 185ㅡ년 9월"]], [["I hope that the intercourse which has been between us will preclude you from regarding my present application as an intrusion.", "그동안 저희 사이에 오간 교분을 생각하시어 이번 청을 주제넘은 참견으로 여기지 않아 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["You cannot, I imagine, have yet heard that poor old Dr. Trefoil has been seized with apoplexy.", "가엾은 노인 트리폴 박사께서 졸중으로 쓰러지셨다는 소식은 아직 들으시지 못했으리라 짐작합니다."], ["It is a subject of profound grief to everyone in Barchester, for he has always been an excellent man--excellent as a man and as a clergyman.", "바체스터의 모든 이에게 깊은 슬픔이 되는 일이니, 그분은 늘 훌륭한 분이셨기 때문입니다. 사람으로서도 성직자로서도 훌륭한 분이셨습니다."], ["He is, however, full of years, and his life could not under any circumstances have been much longer spared.", "다만 연세가 워낙 높으시니 어떤 경우에도 훨씬 더 오래 사시기는 어려웠을 것입니다."], ["You may probably have known him.", "아마 경께서도 그분을 아셨을 줄 압니다."]], [["There is, it appears, no probable chance of his recovery.", "회복의 가망은 도무지 없어 보입니다."], ["Sir Omicron Pie is, I believe, at present with him.", "오미크론 파이 경이 지금 곁에 계신 줄로 압니다."], ["At any rate the medical men here have declared that one or two days more must limit the tether of his mortal coil.", "아무튼 이곳 의사들은 하루나 이틀이면 이승의 목숨줄이 다하리라고 단언했습니다."], ["I sincerely trust that his soul may wing its flight to that haven where it may forever be at rest and forever be happy.", "그분의 영혼이 길이 안식하고 길이 복될 그 항구로 날아가기를 진심으로 빕니다."]], [["The bishop has been speaking to me about the preferment, and he is anxious that it should be conferred on me.", "주교께서 이 성직 승진에 관하여 제게 말씀을 하셨는데, 그 자리가 제게 주어지기를 몹시 바라고 계십니다."], ["I confess that I can hardly venture, at my age, to look for such advancement, but I am so far encouraged by his lordship that I believe I shall be induced to do so.", "솔직히 제 나이에 그만한 승진을 감히 넘본다는 것이 저로서는 엄두가 나지 않습니다만, 주교님께서 하도 격려하시니 아무래도 그리하게 될 듯합니다."], ["His lordship goes to ---- to-morrow and is intent on mentioning the subject to the archbishop.", "주교님께서는 내일 ——로 떠나시며 대주교께 이 이야기를 꺼내실 작정이십니다."]], [["I know well how deservedly great is your weight with the present government.", "지금 정부에 대한 경의 무게가 얼마나 크고 또 얼마나 마땅한지 저는 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["In any matter touching church preferment you would of course be listened to.", "교회의 성직 승진에 관한 일이라면 무엇이든 경의 말씀에는 당연히 귀를 기울일 것입니다."], ["Now that the matter has been put into my head, I am of course anxious to be successful.", "이제 이 일이 제 머릿속에 들어온 이상 저로서는 당연히 성공하고 싶습니다."], ["If you can assist me by your good word, you will confer on me one additional favour.", "경께서 좋은 말씀으로 도와주신다면 제게 은혜를 하나 더 베푸시는 것이 되겠습니다."]], [["I had better add, that Lord ---- cannot as yet know of this piece of preferment having fallen in, or rather of its certainty of falling (for poor dear Dr.", "한 가지 덧붙이는 편이 낫겠습니다. —— 경께서는 이 성직 자리가 비었다는 것을, 아니 비게 되리라는 것이 확실하다는 것을 아직 모르실 것입니다(가엾은 트리폴 박사께서는 이미 가망이 없으니 말입니다)."], ["Trefoil is past hope). Should Lord ---- first hear it from you, that might probably be thought to give you a fair claim to express your opinion.", "—— 경께서 이 소식을 경에게서 먼저 들으신다면, 아마 경께서 의견을 말씀하실 정당한 자격이 있다고 여기실 것입니다."]], [["Of course our grand object is that we should all be of one opinion in church matters.", "물론 저희의 큰 목표는 교회 일에서 모두가 한 의견이 되는 것입니다."], ["This is most desirable at Barchester; it is this that makes our good bishop so anxious about it.", "바체스터에서는 이것이 무엇보다 바람직하며, 저희 선량한 주교께서 이 일을 그토록 염려하시는 것도 그 때문입니다."], ["You may probably think it expedient to point this out to Lord ---- if it shall be in your power to oblige me by mentioning the subject to his lordship.", "이 일을 —— 경께 말씀해 주시어 저를 도와주실 수 있게 된다면, 아마 이 점을 —— 경께 짚어 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여기실 것입니다."]], [["Believe me, My dear Sir Nicholas, Your most faithful servant,", "그럼 이만, 친애하는 니컬러스 경, 경의 가장 충실한 종으로부터,"]], [["His letter to Mr. Towers was written in quite a different strain.", "타워스 씨에게 보낸 편지는 사뭇 다른 가락으로 쓰였다."], ["Mr. Slope conceived that he completely understood the difference in character and position of the two men whom he addressed.", "슬로프 씨는 제가 붓을 든 두 사람의 성품과 처지의 차이를 완전히 꿰뚫고 있다고 여겼다."], ["He knew that for such a man as Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin a little flummery was necessary, and that it might be of the easy, everyday description.", "니컬러스 피츠위긴 경 같은 사람에게는 얼마간의 입에 발린 말이 필요하며 그것도 손쉽고 흔해 빠진 종류면 된다는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["Accordingly his letter to Sir Nicholas was written, currente calamo, with very little trouble. But to such a man as Mr.", "그리하여 니컬러스 경에게 보내는 편지는 currente calamo, 거의 힘들이지 않고 쓰였다."], ["Towers it was not so easy to write a letter that should be effective and yet not offensive, that should carry its point without undue interference. It was not difficult to flatter Dr.", "그러나 타워스 씨 같은 사람에게는 효과가 있으면서도 비위를 거스르지 않는 편지, 지나치게 참견하지 않으면서도 뜻을 관철하는 편지를 쓰기가 그리 쉽지 않았다."], ["Proudie or Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, but very difficult to flatter Mr. Towers without letting the flattery declare itself.", "프라우디 박사나 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경에게 아첨하기는 어렵지 않았으나, 아첨을 아첨으로 드러내지 않으면서 타워스 씨에게 아첨하기란 몹시 어려웠다."], ["This, however, had to be done. Moreover, this letter must, in appearance at least, be written without effort, and be fluent, unconstrained, and demonstrative of no doubt or fear on the part of the writer.", "그러나 그렇게 해내야만 했다."], ["Therefore the epistle to Mr. Towers was studied, and re-copied, and elaborated at the cost of so many minutes that Mr.", "게다가 이 편지는 적어도 겉보기로는 힘들이지 않고 쓴 듯해야 하고, 술술 흐르고 거리낌이 없어야 하며, 쓴 사람에게 아무런 의심도 두려움도 없음을 드러내야 했다."], ["Slope had hardly time to dress himself and reach Dr. Stanhope's that evening.", "그리하여 타워스 씨에게 보내는 서한은 공을 들이고 다시 베끼고 다듬느라 어찌나 많은 시간을 잡아먹었는지, 슬로프 씨는 그날 저녁 옷을 갈아입고 스탠호프 박사 댁에 닿기에도 빠듯할 지경이었다."]], [["When dispatched, it ran as follows:--", "부쳐진 편지는 다음과 같았다."]], [["(Private.) Barchester. Sept. 185--", "(친전) 바체스터, 185ㅡ년 9월"]], [["(He purposely omitted any allusion to the \"palace,\" thinking that Mr.", "(그는 “주교관”이라는 말은 일부러 빼놓았으니, 타워스 씨가 그것을 달갑게 여기지 않을지도 모른다고 생각한 까닭이었다."], ["Towers might not like it. A great man, he remembered, had been once much condemned for dating a letter from Windsor Castle.)", "어떤 대인물이 윈저 성에서 편지를 부쳤다 하여 크게 비난받은 일이 있었음을 그는 기억하고 있었다.)"]], [["We were all a good deal shocked here this morning by hearing that poor old Dean Trefoil had been stricken with apoplexy.", "오늘 아침 이곳에서는 가엾은 노인 트리폴 수석 사제가 졸중으로 쓰러졌다는 소식에 다들 적잖이 놀랐소."], ["The fit took him about 9 A.M.", "발작이 온 것은 오전 9시경이오."], ["I am writing now to save the post, and he is still alive, but past all hope or possibility, I believe, of living.", "우편 시간을 놓치지 않으려고 지금 쓰고 있는데, 그분은 아직 살아 있으나 살아날 가망도 여지도 전혀 없다고 보오."], ["Sir Omicron Pie is here, or will be very shortly, but all that even Sir Omicron can do is to ratify the sentence of his less distinguished brethren that nothing can be done.", "오미크론 파이 경이 와 있거나 곧 올 것이나, 오미크론 경이라 한들 할 수 있는 일이라고는 손쓸 도리가 없다는 덜 이름난 동료들의 판결을 확인해 주는 것뿐이오."], ["Poor Dr. Trefoil's race on this side the grave is run.", "가엾은 트리폴 박사가 무덤 이편에서 달릴 길은 이미 다 달렸소."], ["I do not know whether you knew him.", "그대가 그분을 알았는지는 모르겠소."], ["He was a good, quiet, charitable man, of the old school, of course, as any clergyman over seventy years of age must necessarily be.", "선량하고 조용하고 자비로운 분이었고, 물론 구식이었소. 일흔이 넘은 성직자라면 누구나 그럴 수밖에 없듯이 말이오."]], [["But I do not write merely with the object of sending you such news as this: doubtless someone of your Mercuries will have seen and heard and reported so much; I write, as you usually do yourself, rather with a view to the future than to the past.", "그러나 내가 붓을 든 것은 단지 이런 소식을 전하려는 목적만은 아니오. 그대의 메르쿠리우스들 가운데 누군가가 이미 보고 듣고 그만큼은 알렸을 터이니 말이오. 나는 그대가 늘 그러하듯 지나간 일보다는 앞일을 염두에 두고 쓰는 것이오."]], [["Rumour is already rife here as to Dr.", "트리폴 박사의 후임을 두고 이곳에는 벌써 소문이 자자하오."], ["Trefoil's successor, and among those named as possible future deans your humble servant is, I believe, not the least frequently spoken of; in short, I am looking for the preferment.", "앞으로 수석 사제가 될 만한 사람으로 거론되는 이들 가운데 이 몸도 결코 드물지 않게 입에 오르내리는 줄 아오. 요컨대 나는 그 자리를 노리고 있소."], ["You may probably know that since Bishop Proudie came to the diocese I have exerted myself here a good deal and, I may certainly say, not without some success.", "프라우디 주교가 이 교구에 온 뒤로 내가 이곳에서 적잖이 애를 썼고 얼마간 성과도 없지 않았다고 말해도 좋으리라는 것을 그대도 아마 알 것이오."], ["He and I are nearly always of the same opinion on points of doctrine as well as church discipline, and therefore I have had, as his confidential chaplain, very much in my own hands; but I confess to you that I have a higher ambition than to remain the chaplain of any bishop.", "그와 나는 교리 문제에서나 교회 규율 문제에서나 거의 언제나 의견이 같으며, 그리하여 나는 그의 심복 전속 사제로서 매우 많은 일을 내 손으로 처리해 왔소. 그러나 그대에게 털어놓거니와, 나에게는 어느 주교의 전속 사제로 머무는 것보다 더 높은 야심이 있소."]], [["There are no positions in which more energy is now needed than those of our deans.", "지금 우리 수석 사제직만큼 정력이 절실한 자리도 없소."], ["The whole of our enormous cathedral establishments have been allowed to go to sleep--nay, they are all but dead and ready for the sepulchre!", "우리의 저 거대한 대성당 조직 전체가 잠들도록 방치되어 왔소. 아니, 거의 죽어 무덤에 들어갈 채비를 하고 있소!"], ["And yet of what prodigious moment they might be made if, as was intended, they were so managed as to lead the way and show an example for all our parochial clergy!", "그러나 본디 뜻한 바대로 앞장서서 우리 모든 교구 성직자에게 본을 보이도록 운영되기만 한다면, 그것들이 얼마나 어마어마한 무게를 지닐 수 있겠소!"]], [["The bishop here is most anxious for my success; indeed, he goes to-morrow to press the matter on the archbishop.", "이곳 주교는 내가 성공하기를 몹시 바라고 있소. 실로 그는 내일 대주교에게 이 일을 밀어붙이러 가오."], ["I believe also I may count on the support of at least one most effective member of the government. But I confess that the support of \"The Jupiter,\"", "또한 정부의 가장 유력한 인사 가운데 적어도 한 사람의 지지도 기대할 수 있으리라 보오."], ["if I be thought worthy of it, would be more gratifying to me than any other; more gratifying if by it I should be successful, and more gratifying also if, although so supported, I should be unsuccessful.", "그러나 털어놓자면, 내가 그럴 만하다고 여겨진다면, 『주피터』의 지지야말로 다른 어떤 것보다 나를 흐뭇하게 할 것이오. 그 덕에 성공한다면 더 흐뭇할 것이고, 그런 지지를 받고도 실패한다 해도 역시 더 흐뭇할 것이오."]], [["The time has, in fact, come in which no government can venture to fill up the high places of the Church in defiance of the public press.", "사실 이제는 어떤 정부도 여론의 언론을 거스르며 교회의 높은 자리를 채울 엄두를 내지 못하는 때가 왔소."], ["The age of honourable bishops and noble deans has gone by, and any clergyman however humbly born can now hope for success if his industry, talent, and character be sufficient to call forth the manifest opinion of the public in his favour.", "귀족 주교와 명문 수석 사제의 시대는 지나갔고, 아무리 미천하게 태어난 성직자라도 그 근면과 재능과 인격이 대중의 뚜렷한 지지를 불러낼 만하다면 이제는 성공을 바랄 수 있소."]], [["At the present moment we all feel that any counsel given in such matters by \"The Jupiter\"", "지금 이 순간 우리는 이런 일에서 『주피터』가 내놓는 조언이 가장 큰 무게를 지니며 실로 대개 그대로 따라진다는 것을 다들 느끼고 있소."], ["has the greatest weight--is, indeed, generally followed; and we feel also--I am speaking of clergymen of my own age and standing--that it should be so.", "그리고 우리는, 나와 나이도 처지도 비슷한 성직자들을 두고 하는 말이오만, 그것이 마땅히 그러해야 한다고도 느끼고 있소."], ["There can be no patron less interested than \"The Jupiter,\" and none that more thoroughly understands the wants of the people.", "『주피터』보다 사심 없는 후견인은 없으며, 백성이 무엇을 바라는지를 그보다 더 속속들이 아는 후견인도 없소."]], [["I am sure you will not suspect me of asking from you any support which the paper with which you are connected cannot conscientiously give me.", "그대와 연이 닿은 그 신문이 양심상 내게 줄 수 없는 지지를 내가 청하고 있다고 그대가 의심하지 않으리라 믿소."], ["My object in writing is to let you know that I am a candidate for the appointment.", "내가 글을 쓰는 목적은 내가 그 자리의 후보라는 것을 그대에게 알리려는 데 있소."], ["It is for you to judge whether or no you can assist my views.", "내 뜻을 도울 수 있는지 없는지는 그대가 판단할 일이오."], ["I should not, of course, have written to you on such a matter had I not believed (and I have had good reason so to believe) that \"The Jupiter\" approves of my views on ecclesiastical polity.", "『주피터』가 교회 정치에 관한 내 견해에 찬동한다고 믿지 않았다면(그렇게 믿을 만한 충분한 까닭이 있었소) 나는 물론 이런 일로 그대에게 편지를 쓰지 않았을 것이오."]], [["The bishop expresses a fear that I may be considered too young for such a station, my age being thirty-six.", "주교는 내가 그런 자리에 앉기에는 너무 젊다고 여겨질까 염려하오. 내 나이 서른여섯이오."], ["I cannot think that at the present day any hesitation need be felt on such a point.", "요즘 세상에 그런 점을 두고 망설일 필요가 있다고는 생각지 않소."], ["The public has lost its love for antiquated servants.", "대중은 낡아 빠진 일꾼에 대한 애정을 잃었소."], ["If a man will ever be fit to do good work, he will be fit at thirty-six years of age.", "언젠가 좋은 일을 해낼 만한 사람이라면 서른여섯에도 능히 해낼 만하오."]], [["Believe me very faithfully yours, OBADIAH SLOPE", "그럼 이만, 그대의 충실한 벗, 오바디아 슬로프"]], [["T.", "T."], ["TOWERS, ESQ., ---- Court, Middle Temple.", "타워스 귀하, —— 코트, 미들 템플."]], [["Having thus exerted himself, Mr. Slope posted his letters and passed the remainder of the evening at the feet of his mistress.", "이렇게 애를 쓰고 나서 슬로프 씨는 편지를 부치고 그날 저녁의 나머지를 제 여인의 발치에서 보냈다."]], [["Mr. Slope will be accused of deceit in his mode of canvassing.", "슬로프 씨는 이렇게 표를 구하는 방식을 두고 속임수를 썼다는 비난을 받을 것이다."], ["It will be said that he lied in the application he made to each of his three patrons.", "세 후원자 각각에게 낸 청원에서 그가 거짓말을 했다는 말이 나올 것이다."], ["I believe it must be owned that he did so.", "그가 그리했음을 인정해야 한다고 나는 믿는다."], ["He could not hesitate on account of his youth and yet be quite assured that he was not too young.", "제 젊음을 두고 머뭇거리면서 동시에 자기가 너무 젊지 않다고 확신할 수는 없는 노릇이다."], ["He could not count chiefly on the bishop's support and chiefly also on that of the newspaper.", "주로 주교의 지지에 기대면서 동시에 주로 그 신문의 지지에도 기댈 수는 없는 노릇이다."], ["He did not think that the bishop was going to ---- to press the matter on the archbishop.", "그는 주교가 대주교에게 이 일을 밀어붙이러 ——로 가리라고 생각하지 않았다."], ["It must be owned that in his canvassing Mr. Slope was as false as he well could be.", "표를 구함에 있어 슬로프 씨는 될 수 있는 한 거짓되었음을 인정해야만 한다."]], [["Let it, however, be asked of those who are conversant with such matters, whether he was more false than men usually are on such occasions.", "그러나 이런 일에 밝은 이들에게 물어보기로 하자. 그가 이런 자리에서 사람들이 으레 거짓된 것보다 더 거짓되었던가?"], ["We English gentlemen hate the name of a lie, but how often do we find public men who believe each other's words?", "우리 영국 신사들은 거짓말이라는 이름을 몹시 싫어하지만, 서로의 말을 믿는 공인을 우리는 과연 얼마나 자주 보는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII. Mrs. Proudie Victrix", "# 제33장. 승리자 프라우디 부인"]], [["The next week passed over at Barchester with much apparent tranquillity.", "다음 한 주는 바체스터에서 겉보기에는 몹시 평온하게 지나갔다."], ["The hearts, however, of some of the inhabitants were not so tranquil as the streets of the city.", "그러나 이 도시 주민 가운데 더러는 그 마음이 거리만큼 평온하지 못했다."], ["The poor old dean still continued to live, just as Sir Omicron Pie had prophesied that he would do, much to the amazement, and some thought disgust, of Dr.", "가엾은 늙은 수석 사제는 오미크론 파이 경이 예언한 그대로 여전히 살아 있었으니, 필그레이브 박사에게는 그것이 몹시 놀라운 일이었고 더러는 못마땅한 일이기도 하리라고들 했다."], ["Fillgrave. The bishop still remained away.", "주교는 여전히 돌아오지 않고 있었다."], ["He had stayed a day or two in town and had also remained longer at the archbishop's than he had intended.", "그는 런던에서 하루 이틀을 묵었고, 대주교 댁에도 애초 작정했던 것보다 오래 머물렀다."], ["Mr. Slope had as yet received no line in answer to either of his letters, but he had learnt the cause of this.", "슬로프 씨는 제가 보낸 두 통의 편지 어느 쪽에도 아직 한 줄의 답장을 받지 못했으나, 그 까닭만은 알아냈다."], ["Sir Nicholas was stalking a deer, or attending the Queen, in the Highlands, and even the indefatigable Mr. Towers had stolen an autumn holiday, and had made one of the yearly tribe who now ascend Mont Blanc.", "니컬러스 경은 하일랜드에서 사슴을 몰거나 여왕을 수행하고 있었고, 지칠 줄 모르는 타워스 씨조차 가을 휴가를 훔쳐 내어 해마다 몽블랑에 오르는 무리 가운데 하나가 되어 있었다."], ["Mr. Slope learnt that he was not expected back till the last day of September.", "슬로프 씨는 그가 9월 마지막 날이나 되어야 돌아오리라는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["Mrs. Bold was thrown much with the Stanhopes, of whom she became fonder and fonder.", "볼드 부인은 스탠호프 일가와 어울리는 일이 잦아졌고, 그럴수록 그들이 점점 더 좋아졌다."], ["If asked, she would have said that Charlotte Stanhope was her especial friend, and so she would have thought.", "누가 물었더라면 샬럿 스탠호프야말로 제 각별한 벗이라고 대답했을 터이고, 스스로도 그렇게 여겼을 것이다."], ["But, to tell the truth, she liked Bertie nearly as well; she had no more idea of regarding him as a lover than she would have had of looking at a big tame dog in such a light.", "그러나 사실을 말하자면 버티도 그에 못지않게 좋아했다. 그를 연인으로 여긴다는 생각은, 덩치 크고 순한 개를 그런 눈으로 바라볼 생각만큼이나 머릿속에 없었다."], ["Bertie had become very intimate with her, and made little speeches to her, and said little things of a sort very different from the speeches and sayings of other men.", "버티는 그와 아주 허물없는 사이가 되어 자잘한 말들을 건네었고, 여느 남자들의 말이며 이야기와는 사뭇 다른 자잘한 이야기들을 했다."], ["But then this was almost always done before his sisters; and he, with his long silken beard, his light blue eyes, and strange dress, was so unlike other men.", "그러나 그런 일은 거의 언제나 누이들이 있는 자리에서 벌어졌고, 게다가 그 길고 비단결 같은 수염과 옅푸른 눈과 야릇한 옷차림을 한 그는 여느 남자들과 너무나 달랐다."], ["She admitted him to a kind of familiarity which she had never known with anyone else, and of which she by no means understood the danger.", "엘리너는 여태 누구에게도 허락한 적 없는 종류의 스스럼없음을 그에게 허락했으되, 그 위험은 조금도 알지 못했다."], ["She blushed once at finding that she had called him Bertie and, on the same day, only barely remembered her position in time to check herself from playing upon him some personal practical joke to which she was instigated by Charlotte.", "한번은 제가 그를 버티라고 불렀음을 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔고, 바로 그날 샬럿의 부추김에 넘어가 그에게 몸으로 하는 짓궂은 장난을 걸려다가 겨우 때맞춰 제 처지를 떠올리고 그만두었다."]], [["In all this Eleanor was perfectly innocent, and Bertie Stanhope could hardly be called guilty.", "이 모든 일에서 엘리너는 조금도 흠이 없었고, 버티 스탠호프 역시 죄가 있다고 하기는 어려웠다."], ["But every familiarity into which Eleanor was entrapped was deliberately planned by his sister.", "그러나 엘리너가 걸려든 스스럼없음은 하나하나 그 누이가 짜 놓은 것이었다."], ["She knew well how to play her game, and played it without mercy; she knew, none so well, what was her brother's character, and she would have handed over to him the young widow, and the young widow's money, and the money of the widow's child, without remorse.", "샬럿은 제 판을 어떻게 놀려야 하는지 잘 알았고 인정사정없이 놀렸다. 제 오라비의 사람됨이 어떠한지는 누구보다 잘 알면서도, 젊은 미망인과 그 재산과 미망인의 아이 몫 돈까지 통째로 오라비에게 넘겨주고도 눈 하나 깜짝하지 않았을 것이다."], ["With her pretended friendship and warm cordiality, she strove to connect Eleanor so closely with her brother as to make it impossible that she should go back even if she wished it.", "짐짓 꾸민 우정과 따스한 다정함으로, 샬럿은 엘리너를 제 오라비에게 바싹 붙들어 매어 설령 물러서고 싶어지더라도 물러설 수 없게 만들려 애썼다."], ["But Charlotte Stanhope knew really nothing of Eleanor's character, did not even understand that there were such characters.", "그러나 샬럿 스탠호프는 엘리너의 사람됨을 실은 조금도 알지 못했고, 세상에 그런 사람됨이 있다는 것조차 이해하지 못했다."], ["She did not comprehend that a young and pretty woman could be playful and familiar with a man such as Bertie Stanhope and yet have no idea in her head, no feeling in her heart, that she would have been ashamed to own to all the world.", "젊고 어여쁜 여자가 버티 스탠호프 같은 남자와 장난치고 허물없이 지내면서도 머릿속에 아무 생각이 없고 가슴속에 아무 감정이 없을 수 있다는 것을, 온 세상 앞에 내놓기 부끄러울 것이라고는 하나도 없을 수 있다는 것을 그는 헤아리지 못했다."], ["Charlotte Stanhope did not in the least conceive that her new friend was a woman whom nothing could entrap into an inconsiderate marriage, whose mind would have revolted from the slightest impropriety had she been aware that any impropriety existed.", "샬럿 스탠호프는 제 새 벗이 무엇으로도 경솔한 혼인에 끌어들일 수 없는 여자라는 것을, 조금이라도 온당치 못한 구석이 있음을 알아차렸다면 그 마음이 당장 뒷걸음질 쳤을 여자라는 것을 조금도 짐작하지 못했다."]], [["Miss Stanhope, however, had tact enough to make herself and her father's house very agreeable to Mrs. Bold.", "그렇지만 스탠호프 양에게는 저 자신과 제 아버지의 집을 볼드 부인에게 더없이 마음 편한 것으로 만들 만한 눈치가 있었다."], ["There was with them all an absence of stiffness and formality which was peculiarly agreeable to Eleanor after the great dose of clerical arrogance which she had lately been constrained to take.", "그 집 사람들에게는 뻣뻣함도 격식도 없었으니, 근래에 성직자들의 오만을 한꺼번에 잔뜩 들이켜야 했던 엘리너에게는 그것이 각별히 반가웠다."], ["She played chess with them, walked with them, and drank tea with them; studied or pretended to study astronomy; assisted them in writing stories in rhyme, in turning prose tragedy into comic verse, or comic stories into would-be tragic poetry.", "엘리너는 그들과 체스를 두고, 그들과 거닐고, 그들과 차를 마셨다. 천문학을 공부하거나 공부하는 시늉을 했고, 운을 맞추어 이야기를 짓는 일이며 산문 비극을 우스운 시로 옮기고 우스운 이야기를 비극인 체하는 시로 옮기는 일을 거들었다."], ["She had no idea before that she had any such talents.", "저에게 그런 재주가 있는 줄은 여태 몰랐다."], ["She had not conceived the possibility of her doing such things as she now did.", "지금 하고 있는 것 같은 일을 제가 할 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["She found with the Stanhopes new amusements and employments, new pursuits, which in themselves could not be wrong, and which were exceedingly alluring.", "스탠호프 일가에게서 엘리너는 새로운 놀이와 소일거리를, 그 자체로는 나쁠 것이 없으면서도 몹시 마음을 끄는 새로운 즐길 거리를 찾아냈다."]], [["Is it not a pity that people who are bright and clever should so often be exceedingly improper, and that those who are never improper should so often be dull and heavy?", "밝고 재기 있는 사람들은 어찌하여 그토록 자주 몹시 온당치 못하고, 결코 온당치 못한 법이 없는 사람들은 어찌하여 그토록 자주 따분하고 답답한지, 딱한 노릇이 아닌가?"], ["Now Charlotte Stanhope was always bright and never heavy, but then her propriety was doubtful.", "그런데 샬럿 스탠호프는 언제나 밝았고 답답한 적이 없었으되, 그 온당함은 미덥지 못했다."]], [["But during all this time Eleanor by no means forgot Mr. Arabin, nor did she forget Mr.", "그러나 이 모든 동안에도 엘리너는 애러빈 씨를 결코 잊지 않았고, 슬로프 씨 또한 잊지 않았다."], ["Slope. She had parted from Mr.", "그는 애러빈 씨와 화가 난 채로 헤어졌다."], ["Arabin in her anger. She was still angry at what she regarded as his impertinent interference, but nevertheless she looked forward to meeting him again, and also looked forward to forgiving him.", "주제넘은 참견이라 여겨지는 그 일에 여전히 화가 나 있었으나, 그러면서도 그를 다시 만날 날을 기다렸고 그를 용서할 날도 기다렸다."], ["The words that Mr. Arabin had uttered still sounded in her ears.", "애러빈 씨가 뱉은 말들이 아직도 귓가에 울렸다."], ["She knew that if not intended for a declaration of love, they did signify that he loved her, and she felt also that if he ever did make such a declaration, it might be that she should not receive it unkindly.", "사랑의 고백으로 뜻하고 한 말이 아니었다 하더라도 그 말이 곧 그가 저를 사랑한다는 뜻임을 엘리너는 알았고, 또 그가 언젠가 정말로 그런 고백을 한다면 저로서는 그것을 매정하게 받아들이지 않을지도 모른다고 느꼈다. 그는 여전히 애러빈 씨에게 화가 나 있었다."], ["She was still angry with him, very angry with him; so angry that she would bite her lip and stamp her foot as she thought of what he had said and done.", "몹시 화가 나 있었다. 어찌나 화가 났던지 그가 한 말과 한 짓을 떠올릴 때면 입술을 깨물고 발을 굴렀다."], ["Nevertheless, she yearned to let him know that he was forgiven; all that she required was that he should own that he had sinned.", "그럼에도 그가 용서받았음을 알려 주고 싶어 애가 탔다. 다만 필요한 것은 그가 제 잘못을 스스로 인정하는 일뿐이었다."]], [["She was to meet him at Ullathorne on the last day of the present month.", "엘리너는 이달 마지막 날에 울라손에서 그를 만나기로 되어 있었다."], ["Miss Thorne had invited all the country round to a breakfast on the lawn.", "손 양은 잔디밭 조찬에 인근 온 고장을 초대해 두었다."], ["There were to be tents, and archery, and dancing for the ladies on the lawn and for the swains and girls in the paddock.", "천막이 서고 활쏘기가 벌어지고, 숙녀들은 잔디밭에서 춤을 추고 시골 총각과 처녀들은 방목장에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있었다."], ["There were to be fiddlers and fifers, races for the boys, poles to be climbed, ditches full of water to be jumped over, horse-collars to be grinned through (this latter amusement was an addition of the stewards, and not arranged by Miss Thorne in the original programme), and every game to be played which, in a long course of reading, Miss Thorne could ascertain to have been played in the good days of Queen Elizabeth.", "바이올린 악사와 피리 악사가 오고, 사내아이들의 달리기가 있고, 기어오를 장대와 뛰어넘을 물도랑과 얼굴을 들이밀고 우스꽝스레 찡그릴 말 목걸이도 마련되었으며(이 마지막 놀이는 진행을 맡은 이들이 보탠 것이지 손 양이 애초 순서에 넣은 것은 아니었다), 오랜 독서 끝에 손 양이 엘리자베스 여왕의 좋은 시절에 행해졌다고 확인할 수 있었던 놀이란 놀이는 모두 벌이기로 되어 있었다."], ["Everything of more modern growth was to be tabooed, if possible.", "더 근래에 생겨난 것은 무엇이든 되도록 금하기로 했다."], ["On one subject Miss Thorne was very unhappy.", "다만 한 가지 일로 손 양은 몹시 마음이 언짢았다."], ["She had been turning in her mind the matter of a bull-ring, but could not succeed in making anything of it.", "투우장을 두고 이리저리 궁리해 보았으나 도무지 방도를 찾을 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["She would not for the world have done, or allowed to be done, anything that was cruel; as to the promoting the torture of a bull for the amusement of her young neighbours, it need hardly be said that Miss Thorne would be the last to think of it.", "잔인한 일이라면 무슨 일이 있어도 스스로 하지도, 남이 하도록 두지도 않을 사람이었다. 이웃 젊은이들을 즐겁게 하자고 황소를 괴롭히는 일을 부추긴다는 것은, 손 양이야말로 그런 생각을 할 사람이 결코 아니라고 굳이 말할 것도 없다."], ["And yet there was something so charming in the name.", "그런데도 그 이름에는 어딘가 그토록 매혹적인 데가 있었다."], ["A bull-ring, however, without a bull would only be a memento of the decadence of the times, and she felt herself constrained to abandon the idea.", "그러나 황소 없는 투우장이란 시대가 쇠락했음을 알리는 기념물에 지나지 않을 터라, 손 양은 그 생각을 접지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Quintains, however, she was determined to have, and had poles and swivels and bags of flour prepared accordingly.", "다만 퀸틴만은 기어이 갖추기로 하여 장대와 회전 축과 밀가루 자루를 그에 맞추어 마련해 두었다."], ["She would no doubt have been anxious for something small in the way of a tournament, but, as she said to her brother, that had been tried, and the age had proved itself too decidedly inferior to its forerunners to admit of such a pastime.", "마상 창시합 비슷한 것을 조촐하게라도 벌이고 싶어 안달했을 법하나, 오라비에게 말한 대로 그것은 이미 시도해 본 바가 있었고, 이 시대는 앞선 시대들보다 너무나 확연히 못하다는 것이 드러나 그런 소일거리를 감당할 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Thorne did not seem to participate much in her regret, feeling perhaps that a full suit of chain-armour would have added but little to his own personal comfort.", "손 씨는 누이의 그 아쉬움에 그다지 함께하는 기색이 없었으니, 아마도 사슬 갑옷을 온전히 한 벌 갖추어 입는다 한들 제 몸이 편안해질 것은 별로 없으리라 여긴 까닭이리라."]], [["This party at Ullathorne had been planned in the first place as a sort of welcoming to Mr. Arabin on his entrance into St.", "울라손의 이 모임은 애초에 애러빈 씨가 세인트 이월드 목사관에 들어오는 것을 환영하는 자리로 계획된 것이었는데, 뒤에 일꾼들과 그 아내며 아이들을 위해 마련하려던 추수 잔치가 여기에 합쳐지면서 지금과 같은 규모로 커졌다."], ["Ewold's parsonage; an intended harvest-home gala for the labourers and their wives and children had subsequently been amalgamated with it, and thus it had grown to its present dimensions. All the Plumstead party had of course been asked, and at the time of the invitation Eleanor had intended to have gone with her sister.", "플럼스테드 식구들도 물론 모두 초대를 받았고, 초대장이 왔을 무렵 엘리너는 언니와 함께 갈 작정이었다."], ["Now her plans were altered, and she was going with the Stanhopes.", "이제 계획이 바뀌어 스탠호프 일가와 함께 가게 되었다."], ["The Proudies were also to be there, and, as Mr. Slope had not been included in the invitation to the palace, the signora, whose impudence never deserted her, asked permission of Miss Thorne to bring him.", "프라우디 내외도 오기로 되어 있었는데, 주교관으로 간 초대에 슬로프 씨는 들어 있지 않았으므로, 뻔뻔함이 한 번도 저를 저버린 적 없는 시뇨라가 손 양에게 그를 데려가도 좋겠느냐고 청했다."]], [["This permission Miss Thorne gave, having no other alternative; but she did so with a trembling heart, fearing Mr. Arabin would be offended.", "손 양은 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어 그 허락을 내주었으나, 애러빈 씨가 언짢아하지나 않을까 두려워 마음을 떨며 그리했다."], ["Immediately on his return she apologized, almost with tears, so dire an enmity was presumed to rage between the two gentlemen. But Mr.", "그가 돌아오자마자 손 양은 거의 눈물까지 글썽이며 사과했으니, 두 신사 사이에 그만큼 지독한 원한이 들끓고 있으리라 여겨졌던 것이다."], ["Arabin comforted her by an assurance that he should meet Mr. Slope with the greatest pleasure imaginable and made her promise that she would introduce them to each other.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨으로 슬로프 씨를 맞겠노라 다짐하여 손 양을 달래고는, 두 사람을 서로 소개해 주겠다는 약속까지 받아 냈다."]], [["But this triumph of Mr. Slope's was not so agreeable to Eleanor, who since her return to Barchester had done her best to avoid him.", "그러나 슬로프 씨의 이 승리는 엘리너에게 그리 반갑지 않았다."], ["She would not give way to the Plumstead folk when they so ungenerously accused her of being in love with this odious man; but, nevertheless, knowing that she was so accused, she was fully alive to the expediency of keeping out of his way and dropping him by degrees.", "그는 바체스터로 돌아온 뒤로 그를 피하려 애써 온 터였다. 플럼스테드 사람들이 그토록 야박하게 저더러 그 역겨운 남자를 사랑한다고 몰아세울 때 엘리너는 물러서지 않았으나, 그럼에도 그런 말을 듣고 있는 처지인 만큼 그의 앞길에서 비켜서서 조금씩 그를 떼어 놓는 편이 낫다는 것쯤은 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["She had seen very little of him since her return.", "돌아온 뒤로 그를 만난 일은 거의 없었다."], ["Her servant had been instructed to say to all visitors that she was out.", "하인에게는 찾아오는 사람이 누구든 마님은 나가고 없다고 하라 일러두었다."], ["She could not bring herself to specify Mr. Slope particularly, and in order to avoid him she had thus debarred herself from all her friends.", "슬로프 씨만 콕 집어 말할 수는 없는 노릇이어서, 그를 피하자고 제 벗들 모두에게 문을 닫아걸어 버린 셈이었다."], ["She had excepted Charlotte Stanhope and, by degrees, a few others also.", "샬럿 스탠호프만은 예외로 두었고, 차차 몇몇 사람도 예외가 되었다."], ["Once she had met him at the Stanhopes', but as a rule, Mr. Slope's visits there were made in the morning and hers in the evening.", "한번은 스탠호프 댁에서 그와 마주친 적이 있었으나, 대개 슬로프 씨의 방문은 오전이었고 엘리너의 방문은 저녁이었다."], ["On that one occasion Charlotte had managed to preserve her from any annoyance.", "그 한 번의 자리에서는 샬럿이 용케 그를 성가신 일에서 지켜 주었다."], ["This was very good-natured on the part of Charlotte, as Eleanor thought, and also very sharp-witted, as Eleanor had told her friend nothing of her reasons for wishing to avoid that gentleman.", "엘리너가 보기에 그것은 샬럿으로서 참으로 마음씨 고운 일이었고 또 눈치 빠른 일이기도 했으니, 엘리너는 그 신사를 피하고 싶은 까닭을 벗에게 한마디도 말한 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["The fact, however, was that Charlotte had learnt from her sister that Mr. Slope would probably put himself forward as a suitor for the widow's hand, and she was consequently sufficiently alive to the expediency of guarding Bertie's future wife from any danger in that quarter.", "그러나 실상인즉 샬럿은 슬로프 씨가 아마도 미망인의 손을 노리는 구혼자로 나서리라는 것을 제 동생에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 따라서 버티의 장래 아내를 그쪽에서 오는 위험으로부터 지켜 두는 편이 낫다는 것을 충분히 알고 있었다."]], [["Nevertheless the Stanhopes were pledged to take Mr. Slope with them to Ullathorne.", "그렇지만 스탠호프 일가는 슬로프 씨를 울라손에 데려가기로 약조가 되어 있었다."], ["An arrangement was therefore necessarily made, which was very disagreeable to Eleanor. Dr.", "그리하여 어쩔 수 없이 한 가지 채비가 정해졌는데, 엘리너에게는 몹시 언짢은 것이었다."], ["Stanhope, with herself, Charlotte, and Mr. Slope, were to go together, and Bertie was to follow with his sister Madeline.", "스탠호프 박사가 엘리너와 샬럿과 슬로프 씨를 데리고 함께 가고, 버티는 누이 마들린과 뒤따라오기로 한 것이다."], ["It was clearly visible by Eleanor's face that this assortment was very disagreeable to her, and Charlotte, who was much encouraged thereby in her own little plan, made a thousand apologies.", "이 짝짓기가 저에게 몹시 언짢다는 것이 엘리너의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 드러났고, 그 덕에 제 조그만 계획에 크게 힘을 얻은 샬럿은 천 번이나 미안해했다."]], [["\"I see you don't like it, my dear,\"", "“마음에 안 드시는 줄 알아요.”"], ["said she, \"but we could not manage otherwise.", "샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었어요."], ["Bertie would give his eyes to go with you, but Madeline cannot possibly go without him.", "버티는 눈이라도 내주고 엘리너와 함께 가려 들겠지만, 마들린이 그 애 없이는 도무지 나설 수가 없거든요."], ["Nor could we possibly put Mr. Slope and Madeline in the same carriage without anyone else.", "그렇다고 슬로프 씨와 마들린을 다른 사람 하나 없이 같은 마차에 태울 수도 없는 노릇이고요."], ["They'd both be ruined forever, you know, and not admitted inside Ullathorne gates, I should imagine, after such an impropriety.\"", "그랬다간 둘 다 영영 신세를 망칠 테고, 그런 온당치 못한 일을 저지른 뒤에는 울라손 대문 안에도 못 들어가지 싶은걸요.”"]], [["\"Of course that wouldn't do,\"", "“그야 안 될 일이지요.”"], ["said Eleanor, \"but couldn't I go in the carriage with the signora and your brother?\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “하지만 제가 시뇨라와 동생분과 함께 마차를 타고 가면 안 될까요?”"]], [["\"Impossible!\"", "“그건 안 돼요!”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"When she is there, there is only room for two.\"", "“그 애가 타면 자리가 둘밖에 나지 않거든요.”"], ["The Signora, in truth, did not care to do her travelling in the presence of strangers.", "실은 시뇨라가 낯선 사람 앞에서 길을 가는 것을 달가워하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, then,\"", "“그렇다면,”"], ["said Eleanor, \"you are all so kind, Charlotte, and so good to me that I am sure you won't be offended, but I think I'll not go at all.\"", "엘리너가 말했다, “샬럿, 다들 어찌나 친절하고 제게 잘해 주시는지 언짢아하지 않으시리라 믿고 말씀드리는데, 저는 아예 가지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["\"Not go at all!--what nonsense!--indeed you shall.\"", "“아예 안 가겠다니, 무슨 그런 말씀을."], ["It had been absolutely determined in family counsel that Bertie should propose on that very occasion.", "무슨 일이 있어도 가셔야죠.” 바로 그 자리에서 버티가 청혼하기로 집안 회의에서 이미 못 박아 둔 터였다."]], [["\"Or I can take a fly,\"", "“아니면 삯마차를 부르면 돼요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"You know I am not embarrassed by so many difficulties as you young ladies; I can go alone.\"", "“아시다시피 저는 아가씨들처럼 거리낄 것이 많은 처지가 아니니, 혼자서도 갈 수 있어요.”"]], [["\"Nonsense, my dear!", "“무슨 말씀이에요. 그런 생각은 아예 마세요."], ["Don't think of such a thing; after all, it is only for an hour or so; and, to tell the truth, I don't know what it is you dislike so.", "어차피 한 시간 남짓일 뿐이잖아요. 게다가 솔직히 말하면 무엇이 그리 싫으신지 저는 모르겠어요."], ["I thought you and Mr. Slope were great friends.", "엘리너와 슬로프 씨는 아주 가까운 사이인 줄 알았는데요."], ["What is it you dislike?\"", "무엇이 그리 싫으신 거예요?”"]], [["\"Oh, nothing particular,\"", "“아, 별것 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"only I thought it would be a family party.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “그저 식구들끼리만 가는 자리인 줄 알았을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"Of course it would be much nicer, much more snug, if Bertie could go with us.", "“버티가 우리와 함께 갈 수 있다면 훨씬 좋고 훨씬 오붓하겠지요."], ["It is he that is badly treated.", "딱한 대접을 받는 건 그 애예요."], ["I can assure you he is much more afraid of Mr. Slope than you are.", "장담하는데 그 애가 엘리너보다 슬로프 씨를 훨씬 더 무서워한답니다."], ["But you see Madeline cannot go out without him--and she, poor creature, goes out so seldom!", "하지만 마들린이 그 애 없이는 나갈 수가 없으니 어쩌겠어요. 게다가 그 가엾은 것은 바깥나들이가 어찌나 드문지요."], ["I am sure you don't begrudge her this, though her vagary does knock about our own party a little.\"", "그 애 변덕 때문에 우리 쪽 자리가 조금 흐트러지기는 하지만, 그 정도야 엘리너도 아까워하지 않으시리라 믿어요.”"]], [["Of course Eleanor made a thousand protestations and uttered a thousand hopes that Madeline would enjoy herself.", "물론 엘리너는 천 번이나 아니라고 손사래를 쳤고 마들린이 부디 즐겁게 지내기를 천 번이나 빌었다."], ["And of course she had to give way and undertake to go in the carriage with Mr.", "그리고 물론 물러서서 슬로프 씨와 같은 마차를 타고 가겠노라 떠맡을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Slope. In fact, she was driven either to do this or to explain why she would not do so.", "실상 그리하든지, 아니면 어찌하여 그리하지 않으려는지를 밝히든지 둘 중 하나로 몰린 셈이었다."], ["Now she could not bring herself to explain to Charlotte Stanhope all that had passed at Plumstead.", "그런데 플럼스테드에서 있었던 일을 죄다 샬럿 스탠호프에게 털어놓을 마음은 도저히 나지 않았다."]], [["But it was to her a sore necessity.", "그러나 그것은 그에게 쓰라린 굴레였다."], ["She thought of a thousand little schemes for avoiding it; she would plead illness and not go at all; she would persuade Mary Bold to go, although not asked, and then make a necessity of having a carriage of her own to take her sister-in-law; anything, in fact, she could do, rather than be seen by Mr. Arabin getting out of the same carriage with Mr.", "그는 그 일을 면할 자잘한 꾀를 천 가지나 궁리했다."], ["Slope. However, when the momentous morning came, she had no scheme matured, and then Mr.", "몸이 아프다 핑계 대고 아예 가지 않을까, 초대받지도 않은 메리 볼드를 구슬려 함께 가게 한 다음 시누이를 태우자면 제 마차가 따로 있어야 한다고 우겨 볼까, 실로 애러빈 씨의 눈앞에서 슬로프 씨와 같은 마차에서 내리는 꼴을 보이느니 무슨 짓이든 다 하려 했다."], ["Slope handed her into Dr. Stanhope's carriage and, following her steps, sat opposite to her.", "그러나 정작 그 중대한 아침이 왔을 때 무르익은 꾀는 하나도 없었고, 그리하여 슬로프 씨가 그를 스탠호프 박사의 마차에 태워 올리고는 뒤따라 올라 맞은편에 앉았다."]], [["The bishop returned on the eve of the Ullathorne party, and was received at home with radiant smiles by the partner of all his cares.", "주교는 울라손 모임 전날 저녁에 돌아왔고, 제 모든 근심을 나누는 반려에게서 환한 미소로 맞아들여졌다."], ["On his arrival he crept up to his dressing-room with somewhat of a palpitating heart; he had overstayed his alloted time by three days, and was not without much fear of penalties.", "도착하자마자 그는 다소 가슴을 두근거리며 옷 갈아입는 방으로 살금살금 올라갔다. 정해진 기한을 사흘이나 넘긴 터라 벌이 두렵지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Nothing, however, could be more affectionately cordial than the greeting he received; the girls came out and kissed him in a manner that was quite soothing to his spirit; and Mrs.", "그러나 그가 받은 인사는 더할 나위 없이 다정하고 살가웠다. 딸들이 나와서 그의 마음을 아주 누그러뜨리는 태도로 입을 맞추었고, 프라우디 부인은 “비록 마음이 녹는 일에는 익숙지 아니하나”"], ["Proudie, \"albeit, unused to the melting mood,\" squeezed him in her arms and almost in words called him her dear, darling, good, pet, little bishop.", "그를 두 팔로 꼭 껴안고는, 내 사랑 내 귀염둥이 착하고 어여쁜 우리 작은 주교님이라고 말로도 거의 그렇게 부르다시피 했다."], ["All this was a very pleasant surprise.", "이 모두가 참으로 유쾌한 뜻밖의 일이었다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie had somewhat changed her tactics; not that she had seen any cause to disapprove of her former line of conduct, but she had now brought matters to such a point that she calculated that she might safely do so.", "프라우디 부인은 전술을 얼마간 바꾸었다."], ["She had got the better of Mr.", "이전의 방침에 잘못이 있다고 여겨서가 아니라, 이제는 일을 그만한 지경에 이르게 해 놓았으니 그리해도 안전하리라고 셈했기 때문이다."], ["Slope, and she now thought well to show her husband that when allowed to get the better of everybody, when obeyed by him and permitted to rule over others, she would take care that he should have his reward. Mr.", "그는 슬로프 씨를 이겨 냈고, 이제는 남편에게 이런 것을 보여 주는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["Slope had not a chance against her; not only could she stun the poor bishop by her midnight anger, but she could assuage and soothe him, if she so willed, by daily indulgences.", "곧 자기가 모두를 이기도록 내버려 두고 자기 말에 따르며 남들을 다스리도록 맡겨만 준다면, 남편이 그 상을 받도록 제가 마음 써 주리라는 것이었다. 슬로프 씨에게는 그를 당해 낼 가망이 없었다."], ["She could furnish his room for him, turn him out as smart a bishop as any on the bench, give him good dinners, warm fires, and an easy life--all this she would do if he would but be quietly obedient.", "그는 한밤중의 노여움으로 가엾은 주교를 얼떨떨하게 만들 수도 있었거니와, 마음만 먹으면 날마다의 호사로 그를 달래고 어루만질 수도 있었다. 방을 꾸며 줄 수도 있고, 주교단의 어느 주교 못지않게 말쑥한 주교로 차려 내보낼 수도 있고, 좋은 만찬과 따뜻한 난롯불과 편안한 삶을 줄 수도 있었다."], ["But, if not,--!", "남편이 조용히 순종하기만 한다면 이 모두를 해 줄 참이었다."], ["To speak sooth, however, his sufferings on that dreadful night had been so poignant as to leave him little spirit for further rebellion.", "그러나 그러지 않는다면, 그때는! 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 그 무시무시한 밤에 겪은 고초가 어찌나 뼈아팠던지 주교에게는 더 반항할 기운이 거의 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["As soon as he had dressed himself, she returned to his room.", "주교가 옷을 갈아입자마자 부인은 다시 그의 방으로 왔다."], ["\"I hope you enjoyed yourself at ----,\"", "“거기서 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바라요.”"], ["said she, seating herself on one side of the fire while he remained in his armchair on the other, stroking the calves of his legs.", "부인은 이렇게 말하며 난롯가 한쪽에 앉았고, 주교는 맞은편 안락의자에 앉은 채 제 종아리를 쓸어내리고 있었다."], ["It was the first time he had had a fire in his room since the summer, and it pleased him, for the good bishop loved to be warm and cosy.", "여름이 지난 뒤로 그의 방에 불을 지핀 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 착한 주교는 따뜻하고 아늑한 것을 좋아하는 사람이라 그것이 마음에 들었다."], ["Yes, he said, he had enjoyed himself very much.", "그럼, 아주 즐겁게 지냈지, 하고 그는 말했다."], ["Nothing could be more polite than the archbishop, and Mrs. Archbishop had been equally charming.", "대주교보다 더 정중할 수는 없었고, 대주교 부인 또한 그에 못지않게 사랑스러웠다고 했다."]], [["Mrs.", "프라우디 부인은 그 말을 듣고 기뻐했다."], ["Proudie was delighted to hear it; nothing, she declared, pleased her so much as to think", "부인은 이렇게 생각하는 것만큼 저를 기쁘게 하는 일은 없다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["Her bairn respectit like the lave.", "제 자식이 여느 사람들처럼 존중받는 것을."]], [["She did not put it precisely in these words, but what she said came to the same thing; and then, having petted and fondled her little man sufficiently, she proceeded to business.", "부인이 꼭 이런 말로 표현한 것은 아니었으나 한 말의 뜻은 같은 것이었다. 그러고 나서 제 작은 남자를 넉넉히 어루만지고 다독인 뒤에, 부인은 본론으로 들어갔다."]], [["\"The poor dean is still alive,\"", "“가엾은 수석 사제님은 아직 살아 계세요.”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"So I hear, so I hear,\"", "“그렇다더군, 그렇다더군.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."], ["\"I'll go to the deanery directly after breakfast to-morrow.\"", "“내일 아침 식사가 끝나는 대로 곧장 수석사제관에 가 보리다.”"]], [["\"We are going to this party at Ullathorne to-morrow morning, my dear; we must be there early, you know--by twelve o'clock I suppose.\"", "“내일 아침에는 울라손 모임에 가야 해요, 여보. 일찍 가 있어야 하는 것 아시잖아요. 열두 시까지는 가야겠지요.”"]], [["\"Oh--ah!\"", "“아, 그렇지!”"], ["said the bishop; \"then I'll certainly call the next day.\"", "주교가 말했다. “그럼 그다음 날에는 꼭 찾아가 보리다.”"]], [["\"Was much said about it at ----?\"", "“거기서는 그 이야기가 많이 나왔나요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"About what?\"", "“무엇에 대해서 말이오?”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Filling up the dean's place,\"", "“수석 사제 자리를 채우는 일 말이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["As she spoke, a spark of the wonted fire returned to her eye, and the bishop felt himself to be a little less comfortable than before.", "그렇게 말하는 동안 부인의 눈에는 늘 있던 불꽃이 한 점 되돌아왔고, 주교는 조금 전보다 마음이 조금 덜 편안해지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["\"Filling up the dean's place; that is, if the dean dies?", "“수석 사제 자리를 채우는 일이라, 그러니까 수석 사제가 세상을 떠난다면 말이오?"], ["Very little, my dear.", "거의 없었소, 여보."], ["It was mentioned, just mentioned.\"", "이야기가 나오기는 했지, 그저 나오기만 했소.”"]], [["\"And what did you say about it, Bishop?\"", "“그래서 그 일을 두고 뭐라고 하셨어요, 주교님?”"]], [["\"Why, I said that I thought that if, that is, should--should the dean die, that is, I said I thought--\"", "“그게, 나는 이렇게 말했소, 만일, 그러니까, 혹시라도 수석 사제가 세상을 떠난다면, 그러니까, 내 생각에는, 하고 말했지…”"], ["As he went on stammering and floundering, he saw that his wife's eye was fixed sternly on him.", "그가 이렇게 더듬거리며 갈팡질팡하는 동안, 아내의 눈이 저를 매섭게 쏘아보고 있음이 보였다."], ["Why should he encounter such evil for a man whom he loved so slightly as Mr. Slope?", "슬로프 씨처럼 애정도 별로 없는 사람을 위하여 어찌하여 이런 재앙을 무릅써야 한단 말인가?"], ["Why should he give up his enjoyments and his ease and such dignity as might be allowed to him to fight a losing battle for a chaplain?", "어찌하여 제 즐거움과 안락과 제게 허락된 그만한 위엄까지 내던지고 전속 사제 하나를 위하여 질 것이 뻔한 싸움을 벌여야 한단 말인가?"], ["The chaplain, after all, if successful, would be as great a tyrant as his wife.", "따지고 보면 그 전속 사제도 뜻을 이루고 나면 제 아내 못지않은 폭군이 될 터였다. 대체 무엇하러 싸운단 말인가?"], ["Why fight at all?", "무엇하러 겨룬단 말인가?"], ["Why contend? Why be uneasy?", "무엇하러 마음을 어지럽힌단 말인가?"], ["From that moment he determined to fling Mr. Slope to the winds and take the goods the gods provided.", "그 순간부터 그는 슬로프 씨를 바람결에 날려 버리고 신들이 내려 주는 좋은 것이나 받기로 마음먹었다."]], [["\"I am told,\" said Mrs.", "“듣자 하니,”"], ["Proudie, speaking very slowly, \"that Mr. Slope is looking to be the new dean.\"", "프라우디 부인이 아주 천천히 말했다, “슬로프 씨가 새 수석 사제 자리를 노리고 있다더군요.”"]], [["\"Yes--certainly, I believe he is,\"", "“그렇소, 아무렴, 그런 모양이오.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"And what does the archbishop say about that?\"", "“그래서 대주교님은 그 일을 두고 뭐라고 하시던가요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Well, my dear, to tell the truth, I promised Mr. Slope to speak to the archbishop.", "“글쎄, 여보, 사실대로 말하자면 내가 슬로프 씨에게 대주교께 말을 넣어 주겠다고 약속했소."], ["Mr. Slope spoke to me about it.", "슬로프 씨가 나에게 그 이야기를 꺼냈지."], ["It is very arrogant of him, I must say--but that is nothing to me.\"", "그 사람으로서는 참으로 주제넘은 짓이라 해야겠지만, 그거야 나와는 상관없는 일이오.”"]], [["\"Arrogant!\" said Mrs.", "“주제넘은 짓이라고요!”"], ["Proudie; \"it is the most impudent piece of pretension I ever heard of in my life.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내 평생 들어 본 가운데 가장 뻔뻔스러운 참칭이에요."], ["Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester, indeed!", "슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원!"], ["And what did you do in the matter, Bishop?\"", "그래서 그 일을 어떻게 하셨어요, 주교님?”"]], [["\"Why, my dear, I did speak to the archbishop.\"", "“그게, 여보, 대주교께 말씀을 드리기는 했소.”"]], [["\"You don't mean to tell me,\" said Mrs.", "“설마 이런 터무니없는 수작에 당신 이름을 빌려주어 웃음거리가 되시겠다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?”"], ["Proudie, \"that you are going to make yourself ridiculous by lending your name to such a preposterous attempt as this?", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["Mr. Slope Dean of Barchester, indeed!\"", "“슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원!”"], ["And she tossed her head and put her arms akimbo with an air of confident defiance that made her husband quite sure that Mr.", "그러고는 고개를 홱 젖히고 두 손을 허리에 얹었는데, 그 당당한 반항의 기색에 남편은 슬로프 씨가 결코 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되지 못하리라고 아주 확신하게 되었다."], ["Slope never would be Dean of Barchester.", "참으로 프라우디 부인은 거의 무적이었다."], ["In truth, Mrs. Proudie was all but invincible; had she married Petruchio, it may be doubted whether that arch wife-tamer would have been able to keep her legs out of those garments which are presumed by men to be peculiarly unfitted for feminine use.", "만일 그가 페트루치오와 혼인했더라면, 그 이름난 아내 길들이기의 명수라 한들 남자들이 여자에게는 유독 어울리지 않는다고 여기는 저 옷가지에 부인의 두 다리가 들어가는 것을 막을 수 있었을지는 의심스럽다."]], [["\"It is preposterous, my dear.\"", "“터무니없는 일이오, 여보.”"]], [["\"Then why have you endeavoured to assist him?\"", "“그런데 어찌하여 그를 도우려고 애쓰셨어요?”"]], [["\"Why--my dear, I haven't assisted him--much.\"", "“그게, 여보, 나는 그를 돕지 않았소, 그리 많이는.”"]], [["\"But why have you done it at all?", "“하지만 어찌하여 그런 일을 하시기는 하셨어요?"], ["Why have you mixed your name up in anything so ridiculous?", "어찌하여 그토록 우스운 일에 당신 이름을 섞으셨어요?"], ["What was it you did say to the archbishop?\"", "대주교님께 무슨 말씀을 드리셨는데요?”"]], [["\"Why, I just did mention it; I just did say that--that in the event of the poor dean's death, Mr.", "“그게, 그저 말을 꺼냈을 뿐이오."], ["Slope would--would--\"", "그저 이렇게 말했지, 가엾은 수석 사제가 세상을 떠나는 일이 생기면 슬로프 씨가, 그러니까, 그러니까…”"]], [["\"Would what?\"", "“그러니까 뭐라고요?”"]], [["\"I forget how I put it--would take it if he could get it; something of that sort.", "“어떻게 말했는지는 잊었소. 얻을 수만 있다면 그 자리를 맡을 것이라고, 그런 식이었지."], ["I didn't say much more than that.\"", "그보다 더 한 말은 하지 않았소.”"]], [["\"You shouldn't have said anything at all.", "“애초에 아무 말씀도 하지 마셨어야죠."], ["And what did the archbishop say?\"", "그래서 대주교님은 뭐라고 하시던가요?”"]], [["\"He didn't say anything; he just bowed and rubbed his hands.", "“아무 말씀도 없으셨소. 그저 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 비비셨지."], ["Somebody else came up at the moment, and as we were discussing the new parochial universal school committee, the matter of the new dean dropped; after that I didn't think it wise to renew it.\"", "마침 그때 다른 사람이 다가왔고, 우리가 새 교구 통합 학교 위원회 이야기를 하는 사이에 새 수석 사제 이야기는 흐지부지되었소. 그 뒤로는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내는 것이 현명해 보이지 않았고.”"]], [["\"Renew it!", "“다시 꺼내다니요!"], ["I am very sorry you ever mentioned it.", "저는 그 이야기를 꺼내신 것부터가 몹시 유감스러워요."], ["What will the archbishop think of you?\"", "대주교님이 당신을 어떻게 생각하시겠어요?”"]], [["\"You may be sure, my dear, the archbishop thought very little about it.\"", "“여보, 대주교께서는 그 일을 거의 마음에 두지 않으셨을 것이 틀림없소.”"]], [["\"But why did you think about it, Bishop?", "“하지만 당신은 어찌하여 그 일을 마음에 두셨어요, 주교님?"], ["How could you think of making such a creature as that Dean of Barchester?", "어떻게 그런 자를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 삼을 생각을 하실 수가 있어요?"], ["Dean of Barchester!", "바체스터의 수석 사제라니!"], ["I suppose he'll be looking for a bishopric some of these days--a man that hardly knows who his own father was; a man that I found without bread to his mouth or a coat to his back.", "그러다 머지않아 주교 자리까지 노리겠군요. 제 아비가 누구인지도 제대로 모르는 자, 입에 넣을 빵도 등에 걸칠 웃옷도 없던 것을 제가 거두어 준 자가 말이에요."], ["Dean of Barchester, indeed!", "바체스터의 수석 사제라니, 원!"], ["I'll dean him.\"", "내가 수석 사제로 만들어 주고말고요.”"]], [["Mrs. Proudie considered herself to be in politics a pure Whig; all her family belonged to the Whig party.", "프라우디 부인은 스스로를 정치에서 순수한 휘그라고 여겼고, 집안사람들이 모두 휘그당에 속해 있었다."], ["Now, among all ranks of Englishmen and Englishwomen (Mrs. Proudie should, I think, be ranked among the former on the score of her great strength of mind), no one is so hostile to lowly born pretenders to high station as the pure Whig.", "그런데 영국 남자와 영국 여자의 온갖 계층 가운데(프라우디 부인은 그 대단한 정신력을 보아 앞쪽에 넣어야 마땅하리라고 나는 생각한다), 낮은 데서 태어나 높은 자리를 넘보는 자에게 순수한 휘그만큼 적대적인 부류는 없다."]], [["The bishop thought it necessary to exculpate himself.", "주교는 제 결백을 밝힐 필요가 있다고 생각했다."], ["\"Why, my dear,\"", "“그게, 여보,”"], ["said he, \"it appeared to me that you and Mr. Slope did not get on quite so well as you used to do!\"", "그가 말했다, “내가 보기에는 당신과 슬로프 씨가 예전만큼 사이좋게 지내지 못하는 것 같았소!”"]], [["\"Get on!\" said Mrs.", "“사이좋게 지내다니요!”"], ["Proudie, moving her foot uneasily on the hearth-rug and compressing her lips in a manner that betokened much danger to the subject of their discourse.", "프라우디 부인은 난롯가 깔개 위에서 발을 불편하게 놀리고, 두 사람이 입에 올린 그 인물에게 큰 위험이 닥쳤음을 알리는 모양새로 입술을 꾹 다물며 말했다."]], [["\"I began to find that he was objectionable to you\"--Mrs.", "“나는 그 사람이 당신에게 못마땅한 존재라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했고”"], ["Proudie's foot worked on the hearth-rug with great rapidity--\"and that you would be more comfortable if he was out of the palace\"--Mrs.", "— 프라우디 부인의 발이 난롯가 깔개 위에서 몹시 빠르게 놀았다 — “그가 주교관에서 나가면 당신이 한결 편안하리라는 것도 알게 되었소”"], ["Proudie smiled, as a hyena may probably smile before he begins his laugh--\"and therefore I thought that if he got this place, and so ceased to be my chaplain, you might be pleased at such an arrangement.\"", "— 프라우디 부인은, 하이에나가 웃음을 터뜨리기에 앞서 짓는다는 그런 미소를 지었다 — “그래서 그가 이 자리를 얻어 내 전속 사제 노릇을 그만두게 되면 당신도 그런 처사를 반기리라 생각했던 것이오.”"]], [["And then the hyena laughed out.", "그러자 하이에나가 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["Pleased at such an arrangement!", "그런 처사를 반긴다니!"], ["Pleased at having her enemy converted into a dean with twelve hundred a year!", "제 원수가 한 해 1,200파운드를 받는 수석 사제로 둔갑하는 것을 반긴다니!"], ["Medea, when she describes the customs of her native country (I am quoting from Robson's edition), assures her astonished auditor that in her land captives, when taken, are eaten.", "메데이아는 제 고향의 풍습을 이야기하면서(나는 롭슨판에서 인용하고 있다), 놀라서 듣고 있는 상대에게 제 나라에서는 사로잡은 포로를 잡아먹는다고 일러 준다."]], [["\"You pardon them?\"", "“그들을 용서한다고요?”"], ["says Medea.", "메데이아가 말한다."]], [["\"We do indeed,\"", "“그렇고말고요.”"], ["says the mild Grecian.", "온순한 그리스 사람이 말한다."]], [["\"We eat them!\"", "“우리는 그들을 잡아먹지요!”"], ["says she of Colchis, with terrific energy.", "콜키스의 여인이 무시무시한 기세로 말한다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie was the Medea of Barchester; she had no idea of not eating Mr.", "프라우디 부인은 바체스터의 메데이아였다."], ["Slope. Pardon him!", "슬로프 씨를 잡아먹지 않는다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 아예 없었다."], ["Merely get rid of him!", "그를 용서한다니! 그저 치워 버리기만 한다니!"], ["Make a dean of him!", "그를 수석 사제로 만들어 준다니!"], ["It was not so they did with their captives in her country, among people of her sort! Mr.", "그의 고장에서, 그와 같은 부류의 사람들 사이에서 사로잡은 자들을 다루는 방식은 그런 것이 아니었다."], ["Slope had no such mercy to expect; she would pick him to the very last bone.", "슬로프 씨는 그런 자비를 기대할 처지가 못 되었다. 부인은 그를 마지막 뼈 한 조각까지 발라먹을 참이었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, my dear, of course he'll cease to be your chaplain,\"", "“아, 그럼요, 여보, 물론 그 사람은 당신의 전속 사제 노릇을 그만두게 되겠지요.”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"After what has passed, that must be a matter of course.", "“그동안 있었던 일을 생각하면 그거야 당연한 일이에요."], ["I couldn't for a moment think of living in the same house with such a man.", "그런 사람과 한집에 사는 것은 잠시라도 생각할 수 없어요."], ["Besides, he has shown himself quite unfit for such a situation; making broils and quarrels among the clergy; getting you, my dear, into scrapes; and taking upon himself as though he were as good as bishop himself.", "게다가 그 사람은 그런 자리에 도무지 어울리지 않는다는 것을 스스로 드러냈어요. 성직자들 사이에 다툼과 싸움을 만들어 내고, 여보, 당신을 곤경에 빠뜨리고, 제가 주교나 다름없다는 듯이 나서지 않았어요."], ["Of course he'll go.", "물론 나가야지요."], ["But because he leaves the palace, that is no reason why he should get into the deanery.\"", "하지만 주교관을 떠난다고 해서 그것이 수석사제관에 들어갈 이유가 되지는 않아요.”"]], [["\"Oh, of course not!\"", "“아, 물론 아니지!”"], ["said the bishop; \"but to save appearances, you know, my dear--\"", "주교가 말했다. “하지만 체면을 지키자면, 알잖소, 여보…”"]], [["\"I don't want to save appearances; I want Mr.", "“저는 체면을 지킬 생각이 없어요."], ["Slope to appear just what he is--a false, designing, mean, intriguing man.", "저는 슬로프 씨가 꼭 생긴 그대로 드러나기를 바라요."], ["I have my eye on him; he little knows what I see.", "거짓되고 음흉하고 비열하고 술수를 부리는 사람으로 말이에요."], ["He is misconducting himself in the most disgraceful way with that lame Italian woman.", "저는 그를 눈여겨보고 있어요. 제가 무엇을 보고 있는지 그 사람은 조금도 모르지요."], ["That family is a disgrace to Barchester, and Mr. Slope is a disgrace to Barchester.", "그는 그 다리 못 쓰는 이탈리아 여자와 더할 나위 없이 창피스러운 짓을 저지르고 있어요."], ["If he doesn't look well to it, he'll have his gown stripped off his back instead of having a dean's hat on his head.", "그 집안이 바체스터의 수치이고, 슬로프 씨도 바체스터의 수치예요. 몸조심하지 않으면 머리에 수석 사제의 모자를 쓰기는커녕 등에 걸친 성직복이 벗겨질 거예요."], ["Dean, indeed!", "수석 사제라니, 원!"], ["The man has gone mad with arrogance.\"", "그 사람은 오만이 지나쳐 미쳤어요.”"]], [["The bishop said nothing further to excuse either himself or his chaplain, and having shown himself passive and docile, was again taken into favour.", "주교는 저를 위해서든 제 전속 사제를 위해서든 더는 변명하지 않았고, 고분고분 순종하는 모습을 보인 덕에 다시 총애를 얻었다."], ["They soon went to dinner, and he spent the pleasantest evening he had had in his own house for a long time.", "두 사람은 곧 만찬 자리로 갔고, 그는 제 집에서 오랜만에 가장 즐거운 저녁을 보냈다."], ["His daughter played and sang to him as he sipped his coffee and read his newspaper, and Mrs.", "그가 커피를 홀짝이며 신문을 읽는 동안 딸이 연주를 하고 노래를 불러 주었고, 프라우디 부인은 대주교에 관하여 상냥하게 이런저런 것을 물었다."], ["Proudie asked good-natured little questions about the archbishop; and then he went happily to bed and slept as quietly as though Mrs. Proudie had been Griselda herself.", "그러고 나서 그는 흐뭇한 마음으로 잠자리에 들어, 프라우디 부인이 그리젤다 본인이기라도 한 듯 고요히 잠들었다."], ["While shaving himself in the morning and preparing for the festivities of Ullathorne, he fully resolved to run no more tilts against a warrior so fully armed at all points as was Mrs. Proudie.", "아침에 면도를 하며 울라손 잔치를 채비하는 동안, 그는 프라우디 부인처럼 온몸을 빈틈없이 무장한 전사를 상대로 다시는 창을 겨누지 않겠노라 굳게 마음먹었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV. Oxford--The Master and Tutor of Lazarus", "# 제34장. 옥스퍼드 — 라자러스의 학장과 지도교수"]], [["Mr. Arabin, as we have said, had but a sad walk of it under the trees of Plumstead churchyard.", "애러빈 씨는, 앞서 말한 대로, 플럼스테드 교회 묘지의 나무 아래를 서글프게 거닐었을 뿐이다."], ["He did not appear to any of the family till dinner-time, and then he seemed, as far as their judgement went, to be quite himself.", "그는 만찬 시간이 될 때까지 식구들 가운데 누구 앞에도 나타나지 않았고, 그때가 되자 그들이 판단하기로는 여느 때의 그와 조금도 다름없어 보였다. 그는 늘 하던 대로 스스로에게 숱한 질문을 던지고 숱한 대답을 내놓았다."], ["He had, as was his wont, asked himself a great many questions and given himself a great many answers; and the upshot of this was that he had sent himself down for an ass.", "그리고 그 결말은 자기 자신을 얼간이로 판정해 내려보내는 것이었다. 그는 이렇게 단정했다."], ["He had determined that he was much too old and much too rusty to commence the manoeuvres of love-making; that he had let the time slip through his hands which should have been used for such purposes; and that now he must lie on his bed as he had made it.", "자신은 연애의 술책을 이제 와서 시작하기에는 너무 늙었고 너무 녹슬었으며, 그런 일에 썼어야 할 시절을 손가락 사이로 흘려보냈으니, 이제는 제가 깔아 놓은 자리에 그대로 누울 수밖에 없다고. 그다음 그는 자신에게 물었다."], ["Then he asked himself whether in truth he did love this woman; and he answered himself, not without a long struggle, but at last honestly, that he certainly did love her.", "과연 자기가 정말로 이 여인을 사랑하는가? 그리고 그는 스스로에게 대답했다."], ["He then asked himself whether he did not also love her money, and he again answered himself that he did so.", "오랜 씨름 끝이기는 했으나 마침내 정직하게, 자신이 분명 그녀를 사랑한다고. 이어 그는 자기가 그녀의 재산도 아울러 사랑하는 것은 아닌지 물었고, 다시 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["But here he did not answer honestly.", "그러나 여기서는 정직하게 대답한 것이 아니었다."], ["It was and ever had been his weakness to look for impure motives for his own conduct.", "제 자신의 행위에서 불순한 동기를 굳이 찾아내려 드는 것은 예나 지금이나 그의 약점이었다. 물론 그가 처한 형편으로 보아 — 성직록은 보잘것없고 가진 것이라곤 펠로 자리뿐이며, 칼리지의 호사와 값비싼 안락에 길들어 있는 처지였으니 — 설령 그 여인 자체에 아무리 강한 끌림을 느꼈다 한들 무일푼의 여자와 혼인하기는 망설였을지 모른다."], ["No doubt, circumstanced as he was, with a small living and a fellowship, accustomed as he had been to collegiate luxuries and expensive comforts, he might have hesitated to marry a penniless woman had he felt ever so strong a predilection for the woman herself; no doubt Eleanor's fortune put all such difficulties out of the question; but it was equally without doubt that his love for her had crept upon him without the slightest idea on his part that he could ever benefit his own condition by sharing her wealth.", "물론 엘리너의 재산은 그런 난처함을 죄다 문제 밖으로 치워 주었다. 그러나 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑이, 그녀의 재산을 나누어 가짐으로써 제 처지를 낫게 할 수 있으리라는 생각은 털끝만큼도 없이 슬며시 그에게 스며들었다는 것 또한 똑같이 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["When he had stood on the hearth-rug, counting the pattern and counting also the future chances of his own life, the remembrances of Mrs.", "그가 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 그 무늬를 세고 아울러 제 앞날의 승산을 헤아리고 있었을 때, 볼드 부인의 넉넉한 수입에 대한 기억이 그녀를 향해 처음으로 확신하게 된 사랑을 식히지 않았던 것은 분명하다."], ["Bold's comfortable income had certainly not damped his first assured feeling of love for her.", "그리고 어찌하여 그것이 사랑을 식혔어야 하는가? 사내 가운데 가장 순결한 자라 한들 그래야 할 까닭이 있는가?"], ["And why should it have done so? Need it have done so with the purest of men?", "어떻든 간에 애러빈 씨는 자신에게 불리하게 판결을 내렸다."], ["Be that as it may, Mr. Arabin decided against himself; he decided that it had done so in his case, and that he was not the purest of men.", "그는 제 경우에는 그 기억이 사랑을 식혔다고, 그러므로 자신은 사내 가운데 가장 순결한 자가 아니라고 판결했다."]], [["He also decided, which was more to his purpose, that Eleanor did not care a straw for him, and that very probably she did care a straw for his rival.", "그는 또한, 이쪽이 그로서는 더 요긴한 결론이었는데, 엘리너가 자기 따위는 지푸라기만큼도 마음에 두지 않으며 아마도 제 연적에게라면 지푸라기만큼은 마음을 두고 있으리라고 판단했다."], ["Then he made up his mind not to think of her any more, and went on thinking of her till he was almost in a state to drown himself in the little brook which ran at the bottom of the archdeacon's grounds.", "그러고는 다시는 그녀를 생각하지 않기로 마음을 정한 뒤, 부주교네 땅 아래쪽으로 흐르는 작은 개울에 몸을 던지고 싶어질 지경이 되도록 줄곧 그녀만을 생각했다."]], [["And ever and again his mind would revert to the Signora Neroni, and he would make comparisons between her and Eleanor Bold, not always in favour of the latter.", "그리고 이따금 그의 마음은 시뇨라 네로니에게로 되돌아가곤 했고, 그는 그녀와 엘리너 볼드를 견주어 보았는데 언제나 후자에게 유리한 쪽으로만 견준 것은 아니었다."], ["The signora had listened to him, and flattered him, and believed in him; at least she had told him so.", "시뇨라는 그의 말에 귀를 기울였고, 그를 추어올렸으며, 그를 믿었다. 적어도 그렇다고 그에게 말해 주기는 했다."], ["Mrs. Bold had also listened to him, but had never flattered him; had not always believed in him; and now had broken from him in violent rage.", "볼드 부인 역시 그의 말에 귀를 기울이기는 했으나 결코 그를 추어올리지 않았고, 늘 그를 믿어 주지도 않았으며, 이제는 격한 노여움 속에 그와 갈라섰다."], ["The signora, too, was the more lovely woman of the two, and had also the additional attraction of her affliction--for to him it was an attraction.", "게다가 시뇨라는 둘 가운데 더 아리따운 여인이었고, 제 불구라는 덤의 매력까지 갖추고 있었다. 그에게는 그것이 매력이었으므로."]], [["But he never could have loved the Signora Neroni as he felt that he now loved Eleanor; and so he flung stones into the brook, instead of flinging in himself, and sat down on its margin as sad a gentleman as you shall meet in a summer's day.", "그러나 그는 지금 엘리너를 사랑한다고 느끼는 그만큼 시뇨라 네로니를 사랑할 수는 결코 없었을 것이다. 그리하여 그는 개울에 제 몸을 던지는 대신 돌을 던졌고, 여름날에 만날 수 있는 가장 서글픈 신사의 모습으로 그 물가에 주저앉았다."]], [["He heard the dinner-bell ring from the churchyard, and he knew that it was time to recover his self-possession.", "그는 교회 묘지에서 만찬을 알리는 종소리를 들었고, 이제 평정을 되찾아야 할 때임을 알았다."], ["He felt that he was disgracing himself in his own eyes, that he had been idling his time and neglecting the high duties which he had taken upon himself to perform.", "그는 제 눈에 스스로가 부끄러운 꼴이 되었다고, 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내며 스스로 떠맡은 높은 소임을 소홀히 했다고 느꼈다."], ["He should have spent this afternoon among the poor at St.", "이 오후를 그는 플럼스테드를 서성이며 보낼 것이 아니라 세인트 이월드의 가난한 이들 사이에서 보냈어야 했다."], ["Ewold's, instead of wandering about at Plumstead, an ancient, love-lorn swain, dejected and sighing, full of imaginary sorrows and Wertherian grief.", "늙어 가는 상사병 든 시골 총각처럼, 풀이 죽어 한숨을 쉬며, 지어낸 슬픔과 베르테르식 비탄으로 가득 찬 채 서성이고 있을 것이 아니라 말이다."], ["He was thoroughly ashamed of himself, and determined to lose no time in retrieving his character, so damaged in his own eyes.", "그는 자신이 몹시 부끄러웠고, 제 눈에 그토록 훼손된 제 인격을 되찾는 데 한시도 지체하지 않으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["Thus when he appeared at dinner he was as animated as ever and was the author of most of the conversation which graced the archdeacon's board on that evening. Mr.", "그리하여 만찬 자리에 나타났을 때 그는 여느 때만큼이나 활기찼고, 그날 저녁 부주교의 식탁을 빛낸 대화의 태반은 그가 이끌어 낸 것이었다."], ["Harding was ill at ease and sick at heart, and did not care to appear more comfortable than he really was; what little he did say was said to his daughter.", "하딩 씨는 마음이 편치 않고 속이 쓰려서, 실제보다 편안해 보이려 애쓸 마음도 없었다. 그가 한 얼마 안 되는 말은 딸에게 한 것이었다."], ["He thought that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin had leagued together against Eleanor's comfort, and his wish now was to break away from the pair and undergo in his Barchester lodgings whatever Fate had in store for him.", "그는 부주교와 애러빈 씨가 한편이 되어 엘리너의 안락을 해쳤다고 생각했고, 이제 그의 바람은 그 두 사람에게서 벗어나 바체스터의 제 하숙에서 운명이 자기를 위해 마련해 둔 무엇이든 감당하는 것이었다."], ["He hated the name of the hospital; his attempt to regain his lost inheritance there had brought upon him so much suffering.", "그는 요양원이라는 이름 자체가 미웠다. 그곳에서 잃었던 제 몫을 되찾으려던 시도가 그에게 그토록 많은 괴로움을 안겨 주었던 것이다."], ["As far as he was concerned, Mr. Quiverful was now welcome to the place.", "그로서는 이제 퀴버풀 씨가 그 자리를 차지해도 좋았다."]], [["And the archdeacon was not very lively.", "그리고 부주교도 그리 흥이 나 있지 않았다."], ["The poor dean's illness was of course discussed in the first place. Dr.", "물론 가엾은 수석 사제의 병환이 맨 먼저 화제에 올랐다."], ["Grantly did not mention Mr. Slope's name in connexion with the expected event of Dr.", "그랜틀리 박사는 트리폴 박사의 죽음이라는 예상되는 사태와 관련해 슬로프 씨의 이름을 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["Trefoil's death; he did not wish to say anything about Mr. Slope just at present, nor did he wish to make known his sad surmises; but the idea that his enemy might possibly become Dean of Barchester made him very gloomy.", "그는 지금 당장은 슬로프 씨에 관해 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않았고, 제 서글픈 억측을 알리고 싶지도 않았다."], ["Should such an event take place, such a dire catastrophe come about, there would be an end to his life as far as his life was connected with the city of Barchester.", "그러나 제 원수가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 될지도 모른다는 생각은 그를 몹시 침울하게 만들었다. 그런 일이 벌어진다면, 그런 참담한 재앙이 닥친다면, 그의 삶은 바체스터라는 도시와 얽혀 있는 한에서는 끝장이 날 것이었다."], ["He must give up all his old haunts, all his old habits, and live quietly as a retired rector at Plumstead. It had been a severe trial for him to have Dr.", "그는 오랜 걸음길도 오랜 습관도 모조리 버리고 플럼스테드에서 은퇴한 교구 목사로 조용히 살아야 하리라."], ["Proudie in the palace, but with Mr. Slope also in the deanery he felt that he should be unable to draw his breath in Barchester close.", "프라우디 박사가 주교관에 들어앉은 것만도 그에게는 혹독한 시련이었는데, 슬로프 씨까지 수석사제관에 들어앉는다면 바체스터 대성당 경내에서는 숨조차 쉴 수 없으리라고 그는 느꼈다."]], [["Thus it came to pass that in spite of the sorrow at his heart, Mr. Arabin was apparently the gayest of the party.", "그리하여 마음에는 슬픔을 품고 있으면서도 애러빈 씨가 그 자리에서 겉보기로는 가장 유쾌한 사람이 되는 일이 벌어졌다."], ["Both Mr. Harding and Mrs.", "하딩 씨도 그랜틀리 부인도 그가 침울해하지 않는다는 이유로 그에게 조금씩 화가 나 있었다."], ["Grantly were in a slight degree angry with him on account of his want of gloom. To the one it appeared as though he were triumphing at Eleanor's banishment, and to the other that he was not affected as he should have been by all the sad circumstances of the day--Eleanor's obstinacy, Mr.", "한 사람에게는 그가 엘리너의 추방을 두고 개가를 올리는 듯 보였고, 다른 한 사람에게는 그가 그날의 온갖 서글픈 사정들 — 엘리너의 고집, 슬로프 씨의 성공, 가엾은 수석 사제의 졸중 — 에 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼 마음이 흔들리지 않은 듯 보였다."], ["Slope's success, and the poor dean's apoplexy. And so they were all at cross-purposes.", "그렇게 그들은 모두 서로 어긋나 있었다."]], [["Mr. Harding left the room almost together with the ladies, and then the archdeacon opened his heart to Mr.", "하딩 씨는 부인들과 거의 함께 방을 나섰고, 그러자 부주교는 애러빈 씨에게 속내를 털어놓았다."], ["Arabin. He still harped upon the hospital.", "그는 여전히 요양원 이야기를 되풀이했다."], ["\"What did that fellow mean,\" said he, \"by saying in his letter to Mrs.", "“그 작자가 볼드 부인에게 보낸 편지에서, 하딩 씨가 주교를 찾아뵙기만 하면 만사가 잘될 것이라고 한 것은 대체 무슨 속셈이겠소?”"], ["Bold that if Mr. Harding would call on the bishop, it would be all right?", "그가 말했다."], ["Of course I would not be guided by anything he might say, but still it may be well that Mr. Harding should see the bishop.", "“물론 나야 그자가 하는 말에 이끌려 움직일 생각은 없소만, 그래도 하딩 씨가 주교를 만나 뵙는 편이 좋을지도 모르지요."], ["It would be foolish to let the thing slip through our fingers because Mrs. Bold is determined to make a fool of herself.\"", "볼드 부인이 기어이 제 앞길을 그르치려 든다고 해서 이 일까지 손가락 사이로 흘려보낸다면 그야말로 어리석은 짓일 테니.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin hinted that he was not quite so sure that Mrs. Bold would make a fool of herself. He said that he was not convinced that she did regard Mr. Slope so warmly as she was supposed to do. The archdeacon questioned and cross-questioned him about this, but elicited nothing, and at last remained firm in his own conviction that he was destined, malgré lui, to be the brother-in-law of Mr. Slope. Mr. Arabin strongly advised that Mr. Harding should take no step regarding the hospital in connexion with, or in consequence of, Mr. Slope's letter. \"If the bishop really means to confer the appointment on Mr. Harding,\" argued Mr. Arabin, \"he will take care to let him have some other intimation than a message conveyed through a letter to a lady. Were Mr. Harding to present himself at the palace, he might merely be playing Mr. Slope's game;\" and thus it was settled that nothing should be done till the great Dr. Gwynne's arrival, or at any rate without that potentate's sanction.", "애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인이 제 앞길을 그르치리라고는 그리 확신하지 않는다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다. 그는 그녀가 사람들이 짐작하는 만큼 슬로프 씨를 살갑게 여기고 있다고는 믿기지 않는다고 말했다. 부주교는 이 점을 두고 그에게 묻고 또 캐물었으나 아무것도 끌어내지 못했고, 결국 자신은 malgré lui 슬로프 씨의 손위 처남이 될 운명이라는 제 확신을 굳게 지켰다. 애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨가 슬로프 씨의 편지와 관련해서든 그 편지의 결과로서든 요양원에 관해 아무 조치도 취하지 말아야 한다고 힘주어 권했다. “주교가 정말로 하딩 씨에게 그 자리를 내줄 뜻이라면,” 애러빈 씨가 논했다. “한 부인에게 보낸 편지에 실려 전해지는 전갈 말고 다른 방식으로 그 뜻을 알리도록 마음을 쓸 것이오. 하딩 씨가 주교관에 몸소 나타난다면 그저 슬로프 씨의 판에 놀아나는 꼴이 될 수도 있소.” 그리하여 위대한 그윈 박사가 도착할 때까지, 아니 적어도 그 권세가의 재가 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않기로 정해졌다."]], [["It was droll to observe how these men talked of Mr. Harding as though he were a puppet, and planned their intrigues and small ecclesiastical manoeuvres in reference to Mr.", "이 사내들이 하딩 씨를 마치 꼭두각시인 양 이야기하며, 정작 그를 저희 속내에 끌어들일 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않은 채 하딩 씨의 앞날의 자리를 두고 음모와 자잘한 교회 정치의 술책을 꾸미는 꼴은 우스꽝스러웠다."], ["Harding's future position without dreaming of taking him into their confidence. There was a comfortable house and income in question, and it was very desirable, and certainly very just, that Mr.", "문제가 되는 것은 안락한 집 한 채와 수입이었고, 하딩 씨가 그것을 갖는 것은 매우 바람직하고 또한 분명 매우 온당한 일이었다."], ["Harding should have them; but that at present was not the main point; it was expedient to beat the bishop and, if possible, to smash Mr. Slope.", "그러나 지금으로서는 그것이 요점이 아니었다."], ["Mr. Slope had set up, or was supposed to have set up, a rival candidate.", "요긴한 것은 주교를 이기는 일이었고, 될 수만 있다면 슬로프 씨를 짓뭉개는 일이었다."], ["Of all things the most desirable would have been to have had Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 맞수 후보를 내세웠거나, 내세웠다고 여겨지고 있었다."], ["Quiverful's appointment published to the public and then annulled by the clamour of an indignant world, loud in the defence of Mr. Harding's rights.", "무엇보다도 가장 바람직한 일은 퀴버풀 씨의 임명이 세상에 공표되었다가 하딩 씨의 권리를 지키자고 목청을 높이는 분개한 세상의 아우성에 파기되는 것이었으리라."], ["But of such an event the chance was small; a slight fraction only of the world would be indignant, and that fraction would be one not accustomed to loud speaking.", "그러나 그런 일이 벌어질 가망은 적었다. 세상의 아주 작은 조각만이 분개할 터인데, 그 조각은 목청을 높이는 데 익숙한 부류가 아니었다."], ["And then the preferment had, in a sort of way, been offered to Mr. Harding and had, in a sort of way, been refused by him.", "게다가 그 자리는 어떻게 보면 하딩 씨에게 제안되었던 것이고, 또 어떻게 보면 그가 물리친 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Slope's wicked, cunning hand had been peculiarly conspicuous in the way in which this had been brought to pass, and it was the success of Mr. Slope's cunning which was so painfully grating to the feelings of the archdeacon. That which of all things he most dreaded was that he should be outgeneralled by Mr. Slope; and just at present it appeared probable that Mr. Slope would turn his flank, steal a march on him, cut off his provisions, carry his strong town by a coup de main, and at last beat him thoroughly in a regular pitched battle. The archdeacon felt that his flank had been turned when desired to wait on Mr. Slope instead of the bishop, that a march had been stolen when Mr. Harding was induced to refuse the bishop's offer, that his provisions would be cut off when Mr. Quiverful got the hospital, that Eleanor was the strong town doomed to be taken, and that Mr. Slope, as Dean of Barchester, would be regarded by all the world as conqueror in the final conflict.", "이 일이 그렇게 되도록 만든 방식에서 슬로프 씨의 사악하고 교활한 손길이 유난히 두드러졌고, 부주교의 심사를 그토록 아프게 긁은 것이 바로 그 슬로프 씨의 간계가 거둔 성공이었다. 그가 무엇보다도 두려워한 것은 슬로프 씨에게 지략으로 밀리는 일이었다. 그런데 지금으로서는 슬로프 씨가 그의 옆구리를 돌아 치고, 앞질러 진군하고, 병참을 끊고, coup de main으로 그의 견고한 성읍을 빼앗고, 마침내 정면으로 벌어진 회전에서 그를 철저히 쳐부술 성싶었다. 부주교는 주교 아닌 슬로프 씨를 찾아가라는 말을 들었을 때 제 옆구리가 돌파당했다고 느꼈고, 하딩 씨가 주교의 제안을 물리치도록 꾀임을 당했을 때 앞질러 진군당했다고 느꼈으며, 퀴버풀 씨가 요양원을 차지하면 병참이 끊길 것이고, 엘리너야말로 함락될 운명에 놓인 그 견고한 성읍이며, 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되면 온 세상이 그를 마지막 싸움의 승자로 여기리라고 느꼈다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne was the Deus ex machina who was to come down upon the Barchester stage and bring about deliverance from these terrible evils. But how can melodramatic dénouements be properly brought about, how can vice and Mr. Slope be punished, and virtue and the archdeacon be rewarded, while the avenging god is laid up with the gout? In the mean time evil may be triumphant, and poor innocence, transfixed to the earth by an arrow from Dr. Proudie's quiver, may lie dead upon the ground, not to be resuscitated even by Dr. Gwynne.", "그윈 박사는 바체스터라는 무대 위로 내려와 이 끔찍한 악들로부터 구원을 가져다줄 Deus ex machina였다. 그러나 복수의 신이 통풍으로 드러누워 있는 판에 어떻게 통속극의 dénouement이 제대로 이루어지겠으며, 어떻게 악덕과 슬로프 씨가 벌을 받고 미덕과 부주교가 상을 받겠는가? 그러는 동안 악이 개가를 올릴 수도 있고, 가엾은 무고함은 프라우디 박사의 화살통에서 날아온 화살에 땅에 꿰뚫린 채 죽어 널브러져 그윈 박사조차 되살리지 못할 수도 있는 것이다."]], [["Two or three days after Eleanor's departure, Mr. Arabin went to Oxford and soon found himself closeted with the august head of his college.", "엘리너가 떠난 지 이삼일 뒤, 애러빈 씨는 옥스퍼드로 가서 이내 제 칼리지의 존엄한 수장과 단둘이 마주 앉게 되었다."], ["It was quite clear that Dr. Gwynne was not very sanguine as to the effects of his journey to Barchester, and not over-anxious to interfere with the bishop.", "그윈 박사가 바체스터행이 가져올 효과에 대해 그리 낙관하지 않으며 주교의 일에 끼어들기를 그다지 달가워하지 않는다는 것은 아주 분명했다."], ["He had had the gout, but was very nearly convalescent, and Mr. Arabin at once saw that had the mission been one of which the master thoroughly approved, he would before this have been at Plumstead.", "그는 통풍을 앓았으나 이제 거의 회복된 상태였고, 애러빈 씨는 만일 이번 임무가 학장이 진심으로 찬동하는 종류의 것이었더라면 그가 진작에 플럼스테드에 와 있었으리라는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다."]], [["As it was, Dr. Gwynne was resolved on visiting his friend, and willingly promised to return to Barchester with Mr.", "그렇기는 해도 그윈 박사는 제 벗을 찾아가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 애러빈 씨와 함께 바체스터로 돌아가겠노라 선선히 약속했다."], ["Arabin. He could not bring himself to believe that there was any probability that Mr.", "그는 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 될 가망이 조금이라도 있다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["Slope would be made Dean of Barchester. Rumour, he said, had reached even his ears, not at all favourable to that gentleman's character, and he expressed himself strongly of opinion that any such appointment was quite out of the question.", "그 신사의 인품에 대해 조금도 좋지 않은 소문이 제 귀에까지 들어왔노라고 그는 말했고, 그런 임명이란 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이라는 의견을 힘주어 밝혔다."], ["At this stage of the proceedings, the master's right-hand man, Tom Staple, was called in to assist at the conference.", "일이 이 대목에 이르자 학장의 오른팔인 톰 스테이플이 이 의논에 거들라고 불려 들어왔다."], ["Tom Staple was the Tutor of Lazarus and, moreover, a great man at Oxford.", "톰 스테이플은 라자러스의 지도교수였고, 나아가 옥스퍼드의 거물이었다."], ["Though universally known by a species of nomenclature so very undignified, Tom Staple was one who maintained a high dignity in the university.", "그토록 위엄이라고는 없는 호칭으로 두루 불리기는 했으나, 톰 스테이플은 대학 안에서 높은 위엄을 지키는 사람이었다."], ["He was, as it were, the leader of the Oxford tutors, a body of men who consider themselves collectively as being by very little, if at all, second in importance to the heads themselves.", "그는 말하자면 옥스퍼드 지도교수들의 우두머리였는데, 이 무리는 스스로를 통틀어 놓고 보면 칼리지 수장들보다 중요성에서 조금도, 아니 설령 뒤진다 해도 아주 조금밖에 뒤지지 않는다고 여기는 사람들이었다."], ["It is not always the case that the master, or warden, or provost, or principal can hit it off exactly with his tutor.", "학장이든 워든이든 학감이든 총장이든 제 지도교수와 늘 사이가 척척 맞는 것은 아니다."], ["A tutor is by no means indisposed to have a will of his own.", "지도교수란 제 나름의 뜻을 지니기를 결코 꺼리지 않는 존재다."], ["But at Lazarus they were great friends and firm allies at the time of which we are writing.", "그러나 우리가 쓰고 있는 이 시절의 라자러스에서는 두 사람이 더없이 가까운 벗이자 굳건한 동맹이었다."]], [["Tom Staple was a hale, strong man of about forty-five, short in stature, swarthy in face, with strong, sturdy black hair and crisp black beard of which very little was allowed to show itself in shape of whiskers.", "톰 스테이플은 마흔다섯쯤 된 다부지고 건장한 사내로, 키는 작고 얼굴빛은 거무스름했으며, 뻣뻣하고 억센 검은 머리에 곱슬거리는 검은 수염을 길렀는데 그 수염이 구레나룻 꼴로 드러나는 것은 아주 조금밖에 허용하지 않았다."], ["He always wore a white neckcloth, clean indeed, but not tied with that scrupulous care which now distinguishes some of our younger clergy.", "그는 늘 흰 목도리를 둘렀는데, 깨끗하기는 했으나 요즘 우리 젊은 성직자 몇몇을 돋보이게 하는 저 꼼꼼한 정성으로 매어져 있지는 않았다."], ["He was, of course, always clothed in a seemly suit of solemn black. Mr.", "그는 물론 언제나 격식에 맞는 근엄한 검은 옷을 갖추어 입었다."], ["Staple was a decent cleanly liver, not over-addicted to any sensuality; but nevertheless a somewhat warmish hue was beginning to adorn his nose, the peculiar effect, as his friends averred, of a certain pipe of port introduced into the cellars of Lazarus the very same year in which the tutor entered it as a freshman.", "스테이플 씨는 점잖고 깔끔하게 사는 사람이었고 어떤 육욕에도 지나치게 빠지지 않았다. 그럼에도 어쩐지 불그레한 빛이 그의 코를 물들이기 시작하고 있었는데, 그의 벗들이 단언하기로 이는 그 지도교수가 신입생으로 라자러스에 들어오던 바로 그해에 라자러스의 지하 술광에 들여놓은 어떤 포트 술통이 낸 특유의 효험이었다."], ["There was also, perhaps, a little redolence of port wine, as it were the slightest possible twang, in Mr. Staple's voice.", "또한 스테이플 씨의 목소리에도 아마 포트 포도주의 향내가, 말하자면 아주 희미한 울림 같은 것이 조금은 배어 있었다."]], [["In these latter days Tom Staple was not a happy man; university reform had long been his bugbear, and now was his bane.", "요즈음 들어 톰 스테이플은 행복한 사람이 아니었다. 대학 개혁은 오랫동안 그의 골칫거리였고 이제는 그의 재앙이 되었다."], ["It was not with him, as with most others, an affair of politics, respecting which, when the need existed, he could, for parties'", "그에게 그것은 대다수 사람들에게 그렇듯 정치의 문제, 즉 필요할 때면 당파를 위해서나 원칙을 위해서 얼마간 마땅한 열의를 내보일 수 있는 그런 문제가 아니었다."], ["sake or on behalf of principle, maintain a certain amount of necessary zeal; it was not with him a subject for dilettante warfare and courteous, commonplace opposition.", "그에게 그것은 호사가의 전쟁놀이나 정중하고 상투적인 반대의 소재가 아니었다. 그에게 그것은 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 문제였다."], ["To him it was life and death. The status quo of the university was his only idea of life, and any reformation was as bad to him as death.", "대학의 status quo만이 그가 아는 유일한 삶이었고, 어떠한 개혁이든 그에게는 죽음만큼이나 나쁜 것이었다."], ["He would willingly have been a martyr in the cause, had the cause admitted of martyrdom.", "그 대의가 순교라는 것을 허락하기만 했더라면 그는 기꺼이 그 대의의 순교자가 되었으리라."]], [["At the present day, unfortunately, public affairs will allow of no martyrs, and therefore it is that there is such a deficiency of zeal.", "불행히도 오늘날 공적인 사안이란 순교자를 허락하지 않으며, 그래서 열의가 그토록 모자란 것이다."], ["Could gentlemen of £10,000 a year have died on their own door-steps in defence of protection, no doubt some half-dozen glorious old baronets would have so fallen, and the school of protection would at this day have been crowded with scholars.", "연 수입 10,000파운드의 신사들이 보호 관세를 지키자고 제집 문간에서 죽을 수 있었더라면, 필시 대여섯 명의 영광스러운 늙은 준남작들이 그렇게 쓰러졌을 터이고 보호 관세 학파는 오늘날 제자들로 붐볐을 것이다."], ["Who can fight strenuously in any combat in which there is no danger?", "아무런 위험도 없는 싸움에서 누가 힘껏 싸우겠는가?"], ["Tom Staple would have willingly been impaled before a Committee of the House, could he by such self-sacrifice have infused his own spirit into the component members of the hebdomadal board.", "톰 스테이플은 그런 자기희생으로써 대학 주간 평의회 위원들에게 제 정신을 불어넣을 수만 있었다면 기꺼이 의회 위원회 앞에서 꼬챙이에 꿰였을 것이다."]], [["Tom Staple was one of those who in his heart approved of the credit system which had of old been in vogue between the students and tradesmen of the university.", "톰 스테이플은 예로부터 대학의 학생들과 상인들 사이에 성행해 온 외상 제도를 속으로 찬성하는 사람들 가운데 하나였다."], ["He knew and acknowledged to himself that it was useless in these degenerate days publicly to contend with \"The Jupiter\" on such a subject.", "그는 이 타락한 시절에 그런 문제를 두고 『주피터』와 공공연히 겨루어 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알고 있었고 스스로에게도 인정했다."], ["\"The Jupiter\" had undertaken to rule the university, and Tom Staple was well aware that \"The Jupiter\"", "『주피터』는 대학을 다스리는 일을 떠맡고 나섰으며, 톰 스테이플은 『주피터』가 제게는 너무나 강대하다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["was too powerful for him. But in secret, and among his safe companions, he would argue that the system of credit was an ordeal good for young men to undergo.", "그러나 남모르게, 그리고 마음 놓을 만한 동무들 사이에서는, 외상 제도야말로 젊은이가 한번 겪어 볼 만한 시련이라고 그는 주장하곤 했다."]], [["The bad men, said he, the weak and worthless, blunder into danger and burn their feet; but the good men, they who have any character, they who have that within them which can reflect credit on their alma mater, they come through scatheless.", "못난 자들은, 하고 그는 말했다, 약하고 쓸모없는 자들은 위험 속으로 잘못 들어가 제 발을 데지만, 잘난 자들은, 인격이라 할 것을 지닌 자들은, 제 모교에 명예를 돌려줄 무엇을 안에 지닌 자들은 상처 하나 없이 빠져나온다는 것이다."], ["What merit will there be to a young man to get through safely, if he be guarded and protected and restrained like a schoolboy?", "젊은이가 학동처럼 지켜지고 보호되고 단속받은 끝에 무사히 빠져나온다면 거기에 무슨 공이 있겠는가?"], ["By so doing, the period of the ordeal is only postponed, and the manhood of the man will be deferred from the age of twenty to that of twenty-four.", "그렇게 해 보아야 시련의 시기가 미루어질 뿐이고, 사내가 사내다워지는 일이 스무 살에서 스물네 살로 늦추어질 따름이다."], ["If you bind him with leading-strings at college, he will break loose while eating for the bar in London; bind him there, and he will break loose afterwards, when he is a married man.", "칼리지에서 걸음마 끈으로 묶어 두면 그는 런던에서 법정 변호사가 되려고 만찬 횟수를 채우는 동안에 고삐를 끊을 것이요, 거기서 묶어 두면 나중에 혼인한 사내가 된 뒤에 고삐를 끊을 것이다."], ["The wild oats must be sown somewhere.", "젊은 날의 방종은 어디에선가는 뿌려져야 한다."], ["'Twas thus that Tom Staple would argue of young men, not, indeed, with much consistency, but still with some practical knowledge of the subject gathered from long experience.", "톰 스테이플은 젊은이들을 두고 이렇게 주장하곤 했는데, 사실 그리 앞뒤가 맞지는 않았으나 그래도 오랜 경험에서 얻은 이 문제에 대한 실질적인 지식이 조금은 담겨 있었다."]], [["And now Tom Staple proffered such wisdom as he had for the assistance of Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Arabin.", "그리고 이제 톰 스테이플은 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨를 돕고자 제가 지닌 지혜를 내놓았다."]], [["\"Quite out of the question,\"", "“전혀 있을 수 없는 일이오.”"], ["said he, arguing that Mr. Slope could not possibly be made the new Dean of Barchester.", "슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 새 수석 사제가 될 리는 없다고 논하면서 그가 말했다."]], [["\"So I think,\"", "“나도 그리 생각하오.”"], ["said the master.", "학장이 말했다."], ["\"He has no standing, and, if all I hear be true, very little character.\"", "“그자에게는 내세울 지위가 없고, 내가 들은 것이 죄다 사실이라면 인품이라 할 것도 거의 없소.”"]], [["\"As to character,\"", "“인품으로 말하자면,”"], ["said Tom Staple, \"I don't think much of that.", "톰 스테이플이 말했다. “나는 그것을 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않소."], ["They rather like loose parsons for deans; a little fast living, or a dash of infidelity, is no bad recommendation to a cathedral close.", "수석 사제 자리에는 오히려 헤픈 목사를 좋아하는 법이지요. 조금 방탕하게 산다거나 불신앙의 기미가 조금 있다거나 하는 것은 대성당 경내에 들어가는 데 나쁜 천거가 아니외다."], ["But they couldn't make Mr.", "그러나 그들이 슬로프 씨를 앉힐 수는 없을 것이오."], ["Slope; the last two deans have been Cambridge men; you'll not show me an instance of their making three men running from the same university.", "최근 두 수석 사제가 케임브리지 사람이었소. 같은 대학에서 내리 세 사람을 앉힌 전례를 그대는 내게 보여 주지 못할 것이오."], ["We don't get our share and never shall, I suppose, but we must at least have one out of three.\"", "우리는 우리 몫을 못 받고 있고 앞으로도 못 받겠지만, 그래도 셋에 하나쯤은 가져야 하지 않겠소.”"]], [["\"Those sort of rules are all gone by now,\"", "“그런 규칙 따위야 이제 다 지나간 옛일이오.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Everything has gone by, I believe,\"", "“내가 보기엔 모든 것이 다 지나가 버렸소.”"], ["said Tom Staple.", "톰 스테이플이 말했다."], ["\"The cigar has been smoked out, and we are the ashes.\"", "“여송연은 다 타 버렸고, 우리는 그 재요.”"]], [["\"Speak for yourself, Staple,\"", "“그건 그대 한 사람 이야기로 해 두시오, 스테이플.”"], ["said the master.", "학장이 말했다."]], [["\"I speak for all,\" said the tutor stoutly.", "“나는 모두를 대신해 말하는 것이오.”"], ["\"It is coming to that, that there will be no life left anywhere in the country.", "지도교수가 굳세게 말했다. “이 나라 어디에도 생기라고는 남지 않는 지경으로 가고 있소."], ["No one is any longer fit to rule himself, or those belonging to him.", "이제 아무도 제 자신을, 또 제게 딸린 이들을 다스릴 만한 자격이 없다는 것이오."], ["The Government is to find us all in everything, and the press is to find the Government.", "정부가 우리 모두에게 무엇이든 다 대 주어야 하고, 신문이 그 정부에게 무엇이든 다 대 주어야 한다는 것이지요."], ["Nevertheless, Mr. Slope won't be Dean of Barchester.\"", "그렇더라도 슬로프 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되지는 않을 것이오.”"]], [["\"And who will be warden of the hospital?\"", "“그러면 요양원의 워든은 누가 되겠소?”"], ["said Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I hear that Mr. Quiverful is already appointed,\"", "“퀴버풀 씨가 이미 임명되었다고 나는 들었소.”"], ["said Tom Staple.", "톰 스테이플이 말했다."]], [["\"I think not,\" said the master.", "“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않소.”"], ["\"And I think, moreover, that Dr.", "학장이 말했다."], ["Proudie will not be so short-sighted as to run against such a rock: Mr.", "“그리고 더욱이 프라우디 박사가 그런 암초를 향해 배를 몰 만큼 앞을 못 보지는 않으리라 생각하오."], ["Slope should himself have sense enough to prevent it.\"", "슬로프 씨 자신도 그것을 막을 만한 분별은 있어야 할 테고.”"]], [["\"But perhaps Mr. Slope may have no objection to see his patron on a rock,\"", "“그러나 슬로프 씨로서는 제 후원자가 암초에 걸리는 꼴을 보아도 개의치 않을지 모르지요.”"], ["said the suspicious tutor.", "의심 많은 지도교수가 말했다."]], [["\"What could he get by that?\"", "“그런다고 그자가 무엇을 얻겠소?”"], ["asked Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"It is impossible to see the doubles of such a man,\"", "“그런 사내의 속임수는 도무지 헤아릴 길이 없소.”"], ["said Mr. Staple.", "스테이플 씨가 말했다."], ["\"It seems quite clear that Bishop Proudie is altogether in his hands, and it is equally clear that he has been moving heaven and earth to get this Mr.", "“프라우디 주교가 완전히 그자의 손아귀에 있다는 것은 아주 분명해 보이오. 그리고 그자가 이 퀴버풀 씨를 요양원에 넣으려고 하늘과 땅을 뒤흔들어 왔다는 것 또한 똑같이 분명하지요."], ["Quiverful into the hospital, although he must know that such an appointment would be most damaging to the bishop.", "그런 임명이 주교에게 더없이 해로우리라는 것을 그자가 모를 리 없는데도 말이오. 그런 사내는 도무지 이해할 수가 없고,”"], ["It is impossible to understand such a man, and dreadful to think,\"", "톰 스테이플이 깊이 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다."], ["added Tom Staple, sighing deeply, \"that the welfare and fortunes of good men may depend on his intrigues.\"", "“선한 사람들의 안녕과 운수가 그자의 음모에 달려 있을지 모른다고 생각하면 참으로 끔찍하오.”"]], [["Dr. Gwynne or Mr. Staple were not in the least aware, nor even was Mr. Arabin, that this Mr. Slope, of whom they were talking, had been using his utmost efforts to put their own candidate into the hospital, and that in lieu of being permanent in the palace, his own expulsion therefrom had been already decided on by the high powers of the diocese.", "그윈 박사도 스테이플 씨도, 아니 애러빈 씨조차도 조금도 알지 못했다. 그들이 이야기하고 있는 이 슬로프 씨야말로 바로 저희 편 후보를 요양원에 앉히려고 온 힘을 다해 애써 왔다는 것을, 그리고 그가 주교관에 눌러앉기는커녕 그의 축출이 이미 교구의 높은 권세에 의해 결정되어 있다는 것을."]], [["\"I'll tell you what,\"", "“내 말해 두겠소만,”"], ["said the tutor, \"if this Quiverful is thrust into the hospital and Dr.", "지도교수가 말했다."], ["Trefoil does die, I should not wonder if the Government were to make Mr. Harding Dean of Barchester.", "“만일 이 퀴버풀이 요양원에 밀어 넣어지고 트리폴 박사가 정말로 죽는다면, 정부가 하딩 씨를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 앉힌다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 것이오."], ["They would feel bound to do something for him after all that was said when he resigned.\"", "그가 사임했을 때 그토록 말이 많았으니, 그들로서는 그를 위해 무언가 해야만 한다고 느낄 테니 말이오.”"]], [["Dr. Gwynne at the moment made no reply to this suggestion, but it did not the less impress itself on his mind.", "그윈 박사는 그 자리에서는 이 제안에 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그렇다고 그것이 그의 마음에 덜 새겨진 것은 아니었다."], ["If Mr. Harding could not be warden of the hospital, why should he not be Dean of Barchester?", "하딩 씨가 요양원의 워든이 될 수 없다면, 어찌하여 바체스터의 수석 사제는 되지 못하겠는가?"]], [["And so the conference ended without any very fixed resolution, and Dr. Gwynne and Mr. Arabin prepared for their journey to Plumstead on the morrow.", "그리하여 의논은 이렇다 할 결론 없이 끝났고, 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨는 이튿날 플럼스테드로 떠날 채비를 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV. Miss Thorne's Fête Champêtre", "# 제35장. 손 양의 야외 연회"]], [["The day of the Ullathorne party arrived, and all the world were there--or at least so much of the world as had been included in Miss Thorne's invitation.", "울라손 잔치의 날이 밝았고, 온 세상이 그 자리에 모였다 — 적어도 손 양의 초대장에 포함된 만큼의 세상은 다 모였다."], ["As we have said, the bishop returned home on the previous evening, and on the same evening and by the same train came Dr. Gwynne and Mr.", "앞서 말했듯 주교는 전날 저녁에 집으로 돌아왔고, 같은 날 저녁 같은 기차로 그윈 박사와 애러빈 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 내려왔다."], ["Arabin from Oxford. The archdeacon with his brougham was in waiting for the Master of Lazarus, so that there was a goodly show of church dignitaries on the platform of the railway.", "부주교는 사륜마차를 대령한 채 라자러스의 학장을 기다리고 있었으니, 철도 승강장에는 교회의 고위 인사들이 제법 볼만하게 늘어선 셈이었다."]], [["The Stanhope party was finally arranged in the odious manner already described, and Eleanor got into the doctor's carriage full of apprehension and presentiment of further misfortune, whereas Mr.", "스탠호프 일행의 자리 배정은 앞서 이야기한 그 얄궂은 방식으로 결국 굳어졌고, 엘리너는 앞으로 닥칠 불행에 대한 불안과 예감으로 가득한 채 박사의 마차에 올랐다."], ["Slope entered the vehicle elate with triumph.", "반면 슬로프 씨는 승리감에 들떠 그 마차에 올라탔다."]], [["He had received that morning a very civil note from Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin, not promising much, indeed, but then Mr.", "그는 그날 아침 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경으로부터 대단히 정중한 짧은 편지를 받았다."], ["Slope knew, or fancied that he knew, that it was not etiquette for government officers to make promises.", "사실 약속이라 할 만한 것은 별로 없었으나, 슬로프 씨는 정부 관리가 약속을 하는 것은 예법에 어긋난다는 사실을 알고 있었다 — 혹은 안다고 여겼다."], ["Though Sir Nicholas promised nothing he implied a good deal, declared his conviction that Mr. Slope would make an excellent dean, and wished him every kind of success.", "니컬러스 경은 아무것도 약속하지 않으면서 많은 것을 암시했고, 슬로프 씨가 훌륭한 수석 사제가 되리라 확신한다고 밝혔으며, 온갖 종류의 성공을 빌어 주었다."], ["To be sure he added that, not being in the cabinet, he was never consulted on such matters, and that even if he spoke on the subject, his voice would go for nothing.", "물론 그는 자신이 내각에 속해 있지 않아 그런 문제에 대해서는 한 번도 자문을 받은 일이 없으며, 설령 그 일에 대해 입을 연다 해도 자기 목소리는 아무 무게도 없으리라는 말을 덧붙이기는 했다."], ["But all this Mr. Slope took for the prudent reserve of official life.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 이 모두를 관직 생활의 신중한 자제로 받아들였다."], ["To complete his anticipated triumphs, another letter was brought to him just as he was about to start to Ullathorne.", "예상된 승리를 완성이라도 하려는 듯, 그가 막 울라손으로 떠나려는 참에 또 한 통의 편지가 그에게 전해졌다."]], [["Mr. Slope also enjoyed the idea of handing Mrs.", "슬로프 씨는 또한 울라손 대문 앞의 군중 앞에서 볼드 부인을 스탠호프 박사의 마차에서 손잡아 내려 주는 장면을 흐뭇하게 그려 보고 있었으니, 그 흐뭇함은 엘리너가 바로 그 절차를 두려워하는 정도와 꼭 같았다."], ["Bold out of Dr. Stanhope's carriage before the multitude at Ullathorne gate as much as Eleanor dreaded the same ceremony.", "그는 자신과 자신의 앞날을 저 미망인의 발치에 던지리라 이미 마음을 굳혔고, 그 일을 치를 날로 상서로운 오늘 아침을 고르기로 거의 정해 놓은 터였다."], ["He had fully made up his mind to throw himself and his fortune at the widow's feet, and had almost determined to select the present propitious morning for doing so.", "시뇨라는 근래 그에게 정중하달 수 없는 태도를 보였다. 그의 방문을 받아들이기는 했고 적어도 노여워하지는 않으면서 그의 사랑 고백을 들어 주기는 했으나, 그를 못살게 굴고 헐뜯고 비웃고 조롱했다."], ["The signora had of late been less than civil to him. She had indeed admitted his visits and listened, at any rate without anger, to his love, but she had tortured him and reviled him, jeered at him and ridiculed him, while she allowed him to call her the most beautiful of living women, to kiss her hand, and to proclaim himself with reiterated oaths her adorer, her slave and worshipper.", "그러면서도 그가 자기를 살아 있는 여인 가운데 가장 아름답다 부르고, 자기 손에 입 맞추고, 거듭 맹세하며 스스로를 그대의 숭배자요 노예요 예배자라 선언하는 것은 내버려 두었다."]], [["Miss Thorne was in great perturbation, yet in great glory, on the morning of the gala day. Mr.", "손 양은 축제 당일 아침 크나큰 동요 속에, 그러면서도 크나큰 영광 속에 있었다."], ["Thorne also, though the party was none of his giving, had much heavy work on his hands.", "손 씨 또한 자기가 여는 잔치는 아니었으되 손에 걸린 궂은일이 많았다."], ["But perhaps the most overtasked, the most anxious, and the most effective of all the Ullathorne household was Mr. Plomacy, the steward.", "그러나 울라손 집안 전체에서 가장 혹사당하고, 가장 노심초사하고, 가장 큰 몫을 해낸 사람은 아마도 집사 플로머시 씨였을 것이다."], ["This last personage had, in the time of Mr. Thorne's father, when the Directory held dominion in France, gone over to Paris with letters in his boot-heel for some of the royal party, and such had been his good luck that he had returned safe.", "이 마지막 인물은 손 씨의 부친 시절, 프랑스에 총재 정부가 위세를 떨치던 무렵에 왕당파 몇 사람에게 보내는 편지를 장화 뒤축에 숨기고 파리로 건너간 적이 있었는데, 운이 좋아 무사히 돌아왔다."], ["He had then been very young and was now very old, but the exploit gave him a character for political enterprise and secret discretion which still availed him as thoroughly as it had done in its freshest gloss. Mr.", "그때 그는 아주 젊었고 지금은 아주 늙었으나, 그 무용담은 그에게 정치적 담력과 은밀한 분별이라는 평판을 안겨 주었고, 그 평판은 광이 가장 새로웠던 시절과 다름없이 지금도 여전히 그에게 쓸모가 있었다."], ["Plomacy had been steward of Ullathorne for more than fifty years, and a very easy life he had had of it.", "플로머시 씨는 50년 넘게 울라손의 집사로 있었고, 그동안 대단히 편한 삶을 누렸다."], ["Who could require much absolute work from a man who had carried safely at his heel that which, if discovered, would have cost him his head?", "발각되었더라면 제 목이 달아났을 물건을 뒤축에 넣고 무사히 날랐던 사람에게 누가 감히 절대적인 노동을 많이 요구할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Consequently Mr. Plomacy had never worked hard, and of latter years had never worked at all.", "그리하여 플로머시 씨는 한 번도 고되게 일한 적이 없었고, 근년에는 아예 일이라는 것을 하지 않았다."], ["He had a taste for timber, and therefore he marked the trees that were to be cut down; he had a taste for gardening, and would therefore allow no shrub to be planted or bed to be made without his express sanction.", "그는 목재에 취미가 있었으므로 베어 낼 나무에 표를 했고, 원예에 취미가 있었으므로 자기가 명시적으로 승낙하지 않고서는 관목 한 그루도 심지 못하게 하고 화단 한 뙈기도 만들지 못하게 했다."], ["In these matters he was sometimes driven to run counter to his mistress, but he rarely allowed his mistress to carry the point against him.", "이런 일들에서 그는 이따금 여주인과 정면으로 맞서야 했지만, 여주인이 자기를 이기게 내버려 두는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."]], [["But on occasions such as the present Mr. Plomacy came out strong.", "그러나 오늘 같은 자리에서는 플로머시 씨의 진가가 드러났다."], ["He had the honour of the family at heart; he thoroughly appreciated the duties of hospitality; and therefore, when gala doings were going on, he always took the management into his own hands and reigned supreme over master and mistress.", "그는 가문의 명예를 마음에 품고 있었고, 손님을 맞는 도리를 속속들이 알고 있었다. 그러므로 축제가 벌어질 때면 그는 언제나 지휘권을 제 손에 거두어 쥐고 주인과 여주인 위에 군림했다."]], [["To give Mr. Plomacy his due, old as he was, he thoroughly understood such work as he had in hand, and did it well.", "플로머시 씨에게 공정을 기해 말하자면, 그는 늙었으되 제 손에 맡겨진 그런 일을 속속들이 알고 있었고, 또 그 일을 잘해 냈다."]], [["The order of the day was to be as follows.", "그날의 차례는 이러했다."], ["The quality, as the upper classes in rural districts are designated by the lower with so much true discrimination, were to eat a breakfast, and the non-quality were to eat a dinner.", "시골에서 아랫사람들이 참으로 정확한 안목으로 윗계층을 일컬어 '귀인'이라 하거니와, 그 귀인들은 조찬을 들고 귀인 아닌 이들은 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었다."], ["Two marquees had been erected for these two banquets: that for the quality on the esoteric or garden side of a certain deep ha-ha; and that for the non-quality on the exoteric or paddock side of the same.", "이 두 잔치를 위해 대형 천막 두 채가 세워졌다. 귀인용 천막은 어느 깊은 하하 도랑의 안쪽, 곧 정원 편에 있었고, 귀인 아닌 이들의 천막은 같은 도랑의 바깥쪽, 곧 방목장 편에 있었다."], ["Both were of huge dimensions--that on the outer side was, one may say, on an egregious scale--but Mr. Plomacy declared that neither would be sufficient.", "둘 다 엄청난 크기였다 — 바깥쪽 것은 터무니없는 규모라 해도 좋을 정도였다 — 그런데도 플로머시 씨는 둘 다 모자라리라고 단언했다."], ["To remedy this, an auxiliary banquet was prepared in the dining-room, and a subsidiary board was to be spread sub dio for the accommodation of the lower class of yokels on the Ullathorne property.", "이를 보완하기 위해 식당에 보조 연회석이 차려졌고, 울라손 영지의 더 낮은 계층 촌사람들을 위해 sub dio 곁상이 하나 더 펼쳐지게 되었다."]], [["No one who has not had a hand in the preparation of such an affair can understand the manifold difficulties which Miss Thorne encountered in her project.", "이런 일을 준비하는 데 손을 대 보지 않은 사람은 손 양이 제 계획에서 부딪힌 갖가지 난관을 이해할 수 없다."], ["Had she not been made throughout of the very finest whalebone, riveted with the best Yorkshire steel, she must have sunk under them.", "그가 최상급 고래수염으로 온몸이 짜여 요크셔 최고의 강철로 대갈못까지 박혀 있지 않았더라면 그 난관들 아래 주저앉고 말았으리라."], ["Had not Mr. Plomacy felt how much was justly expected from a man who at one time carried the destinies of Europe in his boot, he would have given way, and his mistress, so deserted, must have perished among her poles and canvas.", "한때 유럽의 운명을 제 장화에 넣고 날랐던 사람에게 세상이 응당 무엇을 기대하는지를 플로머시 씨가 절감하고 있지 않았더라면 그 역시 손을 놓았을 터이고, 그렇게 버림받은 그의 여주인은 장대와 천막 천 사이에서 스러지고 말았을 것이다."]], [["In the first place there was a dreadful line to be drawn.", "무엇보다 먼저, 무시무시한 선 하나를 그어야 했다."], ["Who were to dispose themselves within the ha-ha, and who without?", "누구를 하하 도랑 안에 앉히고 누구를 밖에 앉힐 것인가?"], ["To this the unthinking will give an off-hand answer, as they will to every ponderous question.", "무겁기 짝이 없는 물음에 늘 그러하듯, 생각 없는 이들은 이 물음에도 대뜸 답을 내놓을 것이다."], ["Oh, the bishop and such-like within the ha-ha, and Farmer Greenacre and such-like without.", "아, 주교와 그런 부류는 도랑 안이고, 농부 그린에이커와 그런 부류는 도랑 밖이지요."], ["True, my unthinking friend, but who shall define these such-likes?", "옳은 말이오, 생각 없는 나의 벗이여. 그러나 그 '그런 부류'를 대체 누가 규정한단 말인가?"], ["It is in such definitions that the whole difficulty of society consists.", "사교라는 것의 온갖 어려움은 바로 그런 규정에 있다."], ["To seat the bishop on an arm-chair on the lawn and place Farmer Greenacre at the end of a long table in the paddock is easy enough, but where will you put Mrs.", "주교를 잔디밭 안락의자에 앉히고 농부 그린에이커를 방목장의 긴 식탁 끝자리에 앉히는 일이야 어렵지 않지만, 룩어로프트 부인은 어디에 앉힐 것인가?"], ["Lookaloft, whose husband, though a tenant on the estate, hunts in a red coat, whose daughters go to a fashionable seminary in Barchester, who calls her farm-house Rosebank, and who has a pianoforte in her drawing-room?", "그 남편은 영지의 소작인이면서도 붉은 사냥옷을 입고 사냥을 나가고, 그 딸들은 바체스터의 유행하는 여학교에 다니며, 그는 제 농가를 로즈뱅크라 부르고 응접실에는 피아노포르테를 들여놓은 사람이 아닌가."], ["The Misses Lookaloft, as they call themselves, won't sit contented among the bumpkins. Mrs.", "스스로를 '룩어로프트 아가씨들'이라 일컫는 그 딸들이 촌뜨기들 틈에 흡족히 앉아 있을 리 없다."], ["Lookaloft won't squeeze her fine clothes on a bench and talk familiarly about cream and ducklings to good Mrs. Greenacre.", "룩어로프트 부인은 고운 옷을 구겨 가며 긴 걸상에 몸을 밀어 넣고 착한 그린에이커 부인과 크림이며 오리 새끼며를 두고 허물없이 이야기할 사람이 아니다."], ["And yet Mrs. Lookaloft is no fit companion and never has been the associate of the Thornes and the Grantlys.", "그러면서도 룩어로프트 부인은 손 집안과 그랜틀리 집안의 벗이 될 만한 사람이 못 되고, 여태 그들과 어울려 본 적도 없다."], ["And if Mrs. Lookaloft be admitted within the sanctum of fashionable life, if she be allowed with her three daughters to leap the ha-ha, why not the wives and daughters of other families also?", "그리고 만일 룩어로프트 부인이 상류 사교의 성역 안에 들어오도록 허락된다면, 세 딸을 거느리고 하하 도랑을 건너뛰도록 허락된다면, 다른 집안의 아내와 딸들은 어찌 안 된단 말인가?"], ["Mrs. Greenacre is at present well contented with the paddock, but she might cease to be so if she saw Mrs.", "그린에이커 부인은 지금이야 방목장으로 만족하고 있지만, 룩어로프트 부인이 잔디밭에 있는 것을 본다면 더는 만족하지 않을지도 모른다."], ["Lookaloft on the lawn. And thus poor Miss Thorne had a hard time of it.", "그리하여 가엾은 손 양은 참으로 고된 시간을 보냈다."]], [["And how was she to divide her guests between the marquee and the parlour?", "그리고 손님들을 대형 천막과 응접실에 어떻게 나누어 배치할 것인가?"], ["She had a countess coming, an Honourable John and an Honourable George, and a whole bevy of Ladies Amelia, Rosina, Margaretta, &c; she had a leash of baronets with their baronettes; and, as we all know, she had a bishop.", "백작부인이 오기로 되어 있었고, 아너러블 존과 아너러블 조지가 있었으며, 레이디 어밀리아니 로지나니 마거리타니 하는 이들이 한 무리였다. 준남작이 세 사람 각각 준남작 부인을 데리고 왔고, 다들 알다시피 주교도 있었다."], ["If she put them on the lawn, no one would go into the parlour; if she put them into the parlour, no one would go into the tent.", "그들을 잔디밭에 두면 아무도 응접실로 가지 않을 것이고, 응접실에 두면 아무도 천막으로 가지 않을 것이었다. 그는 나이 든 이들을 집 안에 붙들어 두고 잔디밭은 젊은 연인들에게 내줄까 생각했다."], ["She thought of keeping the old people in the house and leaving the lawn to the lovers.", "그럴 바에는 차라리 당장 말벌집에 주저앉는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["She might as well have seated herself at once in a hornet's nest. Mr.", "플로머시 씨는 그보다 사정을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Plomacy knew better than this.", "“원, 마님도 참,” 그가 말했다."], ["\"Bless your soul, ma'am,\"", "“나이 드신 부인네는 한 분도 안 오십니다."], ["said he, \"there won't be no old ladies--not one, barring yourself and old Mrs. Clantantram.\"", "마님과 늙은 클랜탠트럼 부인 말고는 한 분도 없습지요.”"]], [["Personally Miss Thorne accepted this distinction in her favour as a compliment to her good sense, but nevertheless she had no desire to be closeted on the coming occasion with Mrs. Clantantram.", "손 양은 개인적으로는 자기를 따로 떼어 준 이 구분을 제 분별력에 대한 찬사로 받아들였으나, 그렇다고 이번 자리에서 클랜탠트럼 부인과 단둘이 갇혀 있고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["She gave up all idea of any arbitrary division of her guests and determined if possible to put the bishop on the lawn and the countess in the house, to sprinkle the baronets, and thus divide the attractions.", "그는 손님을 제멋대로 갈라놓겠다는 생각을 아주 버리고, 되도록 주교는 잔디밭에 백작부인은 집 안에 두고 준남작들은 여기저기 흩뿌려 매력을 고루 나누기로 마음먹었다."], ["What to do with the Lookalofts even Mr. Plomacy could not decide.", "룩어로프트네를 어찌할지는 플로머시 씨조차 결정하지 못했다."], ["They must take their chance.", "그들은 운에 맡기는 수밖에 없었다."], ["They had been specially told in the invitation that all the tenants had been invited, and they might probably have the good sense to stay away if they objected to mix with the rest of the tenantry.", "초대장에는 소작인 전원을 초대했다는 말이 따로 적혀 있었으니, 나머지 소작인들과 섞이는 것이 싫다면 아마 오지 않을 만한 분별쯤은 있으리라."]], [["Then Mr. Plomacy declared his apprehension that the Honourable Johns and Honourable Georges would come in a sort of amphibious costume, half-morning, half-evening, satin neck-handkerchiefs, frock-coats, primrose gloves, and polished boots; and that, being so dressed, they would decline riding at the quintain, or taking part in any of the athletic games which Miss Thorne had prepared with so much fond care.", "이어 플로머시 씨는 아너러블 존이니 아너러블 조지니 하는 이들이 일종의 물뭍 양쪽에 걸친 차림으로, 반은 낮 예복이고 반은 밤 예복인 채로, 새틴 목수건에 프록코트에 앵초빛 장갑에 반질반질한 장화 차림으로 나타나리라는 우려를 내놓았다."], ["If the Lord Johns and Lord Georges didn't ride at the quintain, Miss Thorne might be sure that nobody else would.", "그렇게 차려입고 온 이상 그들은 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 것도, 손 양이 그토록 애틋한 정성으로 마련한 어떤 경기에 끼는 것도 마다하리라는 것이었다. 그리고 존 나리들과 조지 나리들이 퀸틴을 향해 달리지 않는다면 다른 누구도 그러지 않으리라는 것은 손 양도 확신해도 좋았다."]], [["\"But,\"", "“하지만,”"], ["said she in dolorous voice, all but overcome by her cares, \"it was specially signified that there were to be sports.\"", "근심에 짓눌릴 대로 짓눌린 그가 서글픈 목소리로 말했다. “경기가 있으리라고 각별히 알려 두었단 말이오.”"]], [["\"And so there will be, of course,\"", "“물론 있고말고요,”"], ["said Mr. Plomacy.", "플로머시 씨가 말했다."], ["\"They'll all be sporting with the young ladies in the laurel walks.", "“다들 월계수 길에서 젊은 아가씨들과 어울려 노느라 바쁠 겁니다."], ["Them's the sports they care most about now-a-days.", "요새 젊은것들이 제일 좋아하는 놀이가 바로 그것 아니겠습니까."], ["If you gets the young men at the quintain, you'll have all the young women in the pouts.\"", "젊은 사내들을 퀸틴 앞에 붙들어다 놓으시면 젊은 여자들은 모조리 뾰로통해질 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Can't they look on as their great grandmothers did before them?\"", "“증조모들이 예전에 그리했듯 구경하고 있으면 될 것 아니오?”"], ["said Miss Thorne.", "손 양이 말했다."]], [["\"It seems to me that the ladies ain't contented with looking now-a-days.", "“요새 부인네들은 구경만으로는 만족을 못 하는 모양입니다."], ["Whatever the men do they'll do.", "사내들이 하는 것이면 저희도 하겠다는 거지요."], ["If you'll have side-saddles on the nags; and let them go at the quintain too, it'll answer capital, no doubt.\"", "말들에 여자용 안장을 얹어 주시고 그이들도 퀸틴을 향해 달리게 하시면, 그야 더할 나위 없이 잘될 겁니다요.”"]], [["Miss Thorne made no reply.", "손 양은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["She felt that she had no good ground on which to defend her sex of the present generation from the sarcasm of Mr.", "그는 플로머시 씨의 빈정거림에서 이 시대의 제 성(性)을 변호할 만한 근거가 없음을 느꼈다."], ["Plomacy. She had once declared, in one of her warmer moments, \"that now-a-days the gentlemen were all women, and the ladies all men.\"", "그는 언젠가 좀 격해진 순간에 “요새는 신사가 죄다 여자고 숙녀가 죄다 남자”라고 단언한 적이 있었다."], ["She could not alter the debased character of the age.", "그는 이 시대의 타락한 성정을 바꿀 수 없었다."], ["But, such being the case, why should she take on herself to cater for the amusement of people of such degraded tastes?", "그러나 사정이 그러하다면, 어찌하여 그런 저열한 취향을 지닌 이들의 오락을 제 손으로 차려 주는 수고를 떠맡는단 말인가?"], ["This question she asked herself more than once, and she could only answer herself with a sigh.", "이 물음을 그는 한 번 이상 스스로에게 던졌고, 그때마다 한숨으로밖에는 답할 수 없었다."], ["There was her own brother Wilfred, on whose shoulders rested all the ancient honours of Ullathorne house; it was very doubtful whether even he would consent to \"go at the quintain,\" as Mr.", "울라손 저택의 온갖 유서 깊은 명예를 어깨에 짊어진 제 친오라비 윌프레드조차, 플로머시 씨가 제법 그럴듯하게 표현한 대로 '퀸틴을 향해 달리는'"], ["Plomacy not injudiciously expressed it.", "데 동의할지 대단히 의심스러웠다."]], [["And now the morning arrived.", "그리하여 아침이 왔다."], ["The Ullathorne household was early on the move.", "울라손 집안은 일찌감치 움직이기 시작했다."], ["Cooks were cooking in the kitchen long before daylight, and men were dragging out tables and hammering red baize on to benches at the earliest dawn.", "날이 밝기 한참 전부터 부엌에서는 요리사들이 요리를 하고 있었고, 동트기 무섭게 남자들은 식탁을 끌어내고 긴 걸상에 붉은 나사 천을 못질해 붙이고 있었다."], ["With what dread eagerness did Miss Thorne look out at the weather as soon as the parting veil of night permitted her to look at all!", "밤의 장막이 걷혀 무엇이든 내다볼 수 있게 되자마자 손 양은 얼마나 두려운 열심으로 날씨를 살폈던가!"], ["In this respect, at any rate, there was nothing to grieve her.", "적어도 이 점에서는 그를 슬프게 할 것이 없었다."], ["The glass had been rising for the last three days, and the morning broke with that dull, chill, steady, grey haze which in autumn generally presages a clear and dry day.", "기압계는 사흘째 오르고 있었고, 아침은 가을이면 대개 맑고 마른 하루를 예고하는 그 흐릿하고 차고 한결같은 잿빛 안개와 함께 밝았다. 7시에 그는 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 아래층에 내려와 있었다."], ["By seven she was dressed and down. Miss Thorne knew nothing of the modern luxury of déshabilles.", "손 양은 déshabilles 라는 근대의 사치를 조금도 알지 못했다."], ["She would as soon have thought of appearing before her brother without her stockings as without her stays--and Miss Thorne's stays were no trifle.", "오라비 앞에 코르셋 없이 나서는 일이나 양말 없이 나서는 일이나 그에게는 똑같이 생각조차 할 수 없는 노릇이었다 — 게다가 손 양의 코르셋은 결코 만만한 물건이 아니었다."]], [["And yet there was nothing for her to do when down.", "그러면서도 막상 내려와 보니 할 일이 없었다."], ["She fidgeted out to the lawn and then back into the kitchen.", "그는 안절부절못하며 잔디밭으로 나갔다가 다시 부엌으로 들어왔다."], ["She put on her high-heeled clogs and fidgeted out into the paddock.", "굽 높은 나막신을 신고 안달하며 방목장으로 나갔다."], ["Then she went into the small home park where the quintain was erected.", "그러고는 퀸틴을 세워 둔, 저택에 딸린 작은 공원으로 들어갔다."], ["The pole and cross-bar and the swivel and the target and the bag of flour were all complete.", "기둥과 가로대와 회전축과 표적과 밀가루 자루가 모두 갖추어져 있었다. 그는 목수의 작업대 위에 올라서서 표적을 손으로 건드려 보았다."], ["She got up on a carpenter's bench and touched the target with her hand; it went round with beautiful ease; the swivel had been oiled to perfection.", "표적은 놀랍도록 매끄럽게 돌아갔다. 회전축에는 기름이 완벽하게 먹여져 있었다."], ["She almost wished to take old Plomacy at his word, to get on a side-saddle and have a tilt at it herself.", "그는 늙은 플로머시의 말을 곧이곧대로 따라 여자용 안장에 올라앉아 제 손으로 한번 겨루어 보고 싶은 마음이 들 지경이었다."], ["What must a young man be, thought she, who could prefer maundering among laurel trees with a wishy-washy school-girl to such fun as this?", "이런 재미를 두고 밍밍한 여학생과 월계수 나무 사이나 어슬렁거리는 편을 택하는 젊은이란 대체 어떤 인간이란 말인가, 하고 그는 생각했다."], ["\"Well,\"", "“좋아,”"], ["said she aloud to herself, \"one man can take a horse to water, but a thousand can't make him drink.", "그는 소리 내어 혼잣말을 했다. “한 사람이 말을 물가로 끌고 갈 수는 있어도 천 사람이 그 말에게 물을 먹일 수는 없는 법이지."], ["There it is.", "사정이 그러한 것을."], ["If they haven't the spirit to enjoy it, the fault shan't be mine;\"", "저들에게 이것을 즐길 기개가 없다면 그건 내 잘못이 아니오.”"], ["and so she returned to the house.", "그러고는 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["At a little after eight her brother came down, and they had a sort of scrap breakfast in his study.", "8시가 조금 지나 오라비가 내려왔고, 남매는 그의 서재에서 대충 때우는 아침을 먹었다."], ["The tea was made without the customary urn, and they dispensed with the usual rolls and toast.", "차는 늘 쓰던 차 항아리 없이 우려냈고, 여느 때의 롤빵과 토스트도 없이 넘어갔다."], ["Eggs also were missing, for every egg in the parish had been whipped into custards, baked into pies, or boiled into lobster salad.", "달걀도 없었으니, 이 교구의 달걀이란 달걀은 모조리 커스터드로 저어지거나 파이에 구워 넣어지거나 바닷가재 샐러드용으로 삶아진 까닭이었다."], ["The allowance of fresh butter was short, and Mr. Thorne was obliged to eat the leg of a fowl without having it devilled in the manner he loved.", "신선한 버터도 넉넉지 못했고, 손 씨는 좋아하는 방식대로 맵게 구워 내지 않은 닭다리를 그냥 먹어야 했다."]], [["\"I have been looking at the quintain, Wilfred,\"", "“퀸틴을 살펴보았는데, 윌프레드,”"], ["said she, \"and it appears to be quite right.\"", "그가 말했다. “아주 멀쩡한 것 같더이다.”"]], [["\"Oh--ah, yes,\" said he.", "“오 — 아, 그렇더군요,”"], ["\"It seemed to be so yesterday when I saw it.\"", "그가 말했다. “어제 내가 보았을 때도 그런 것 같더이다.”"], ["Mr. Thorne was beginning to be rather bored by his sister's love of sports, and had especially no affection for this quintain post.", "손 씨는 누이의 경기 애호에 슬슬 넌더리가 나던 참이었고, 특히 이 퀸틴 기둥에는 아무 애정도 없었다."]], [["\"I wish you'd just try it after breakfast,\" said she.", "“아침을 들고 나서 한번 시험해 보시면 좋겠소,”"], ["\"You could have the saddle put on Mark Antony, and the pole is there all handy.", "그가 말했다. “마크 안토니에 안장을 얹으면 되고, 창대도 거기 다 갖추어져 있소."], ["You can take the flour bag off, you know, if you think Mark Antony won't be quick enough,\"", "마크 안토니가 그리 빠르지 못하겠다 싶으면 밀가루 자루는 떼어 놓아도 되고 말이오.”"], ["added Miss Thorne, seeing that her brother's countenance was not indicative of complete accordance with her little proposition.", "오라비의 낯빛이 이 자그마한 제안에 온전히 찬동하는 기색은 아님을 보고 손 양이 이렇게 덧붙인 것이었다."]], [["Now Mark Antony was a valuable old hunter, excellently suited to Mr.", "그런데 마크 안토니로 말하자면 값나가는 늙은 사냥말로서, 손 씨가 평소에 바라는 바에는 더할 나위 없이 알맞은 말이었다."], ["Thorne's usual requirements, steady indeed at his fences, but extremely sure, very good in deep ground, and safe on the roads.", "울타리 앞에서 서두르는 법이 없었고 발은 지극히 확실했으며, 진창에서도 잘 달렸고 길 위에서도 안전했다."], ["But he had never yet been ridden at a quintain, and Mr. Thorne was not inclined to put him to the trial, either with or without the bag of flour.", "그러나 이 말은 여태 퀸틴을 향해 달려 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 손 씨는 밀가루 자루가 있건 없건 그런 시험에 말을 내몰 마음이 없었다."], ["He hummed and hawed and finally declared that he was afraid Mark Antony would shy.", "그는 이러쿵저러쿵 얼버무리다가 결국 마크 안토니가 놀라 옆으로 뛸까 걱정된다고 말했다."]], [["\"Then try the cob,\"", "“그러면 그 땅딸막한 승용마를 타 보시오.”"], ["said the indefatigable Miss Thorne.", "지칠 줄 모르는 손 양이 말했다."]], [["\"He's in physic,\"", "“그놈은 약을 먹이는 중이오.”"], ["said Wilfred.", "윌프레드가 말했다."]], [["\"There's the Beelzebub colt,\"", "“베엘제붑 망아지가 있잖소.”"], ["said his sister.", "누이가 말했다."], ["\"I know he's in the stable because I saw Peter exercising him just now.\"", "“방금 피터가 그놈을 운동시키는 것을 보았으니 마구간에 있는 줄은 내가 아오.”"]], [["\"My dear Monica, he's so wild that it's as much as I can do to manage him at all.", "“모니카, 그놈은 어찌나 사나운지 내가 다루는 것만도 벅차오."], ["He'd destroy himself and me, too, if I attempted to ride him at such a rattletrap as that.\"", "그런 덜컹거리는 고물을 향해 그놈을 몰았다가는 제 몸도 내 몸도 결딴내고 말 거요.”"]], [["A rattletrap!", "덜컹거리는 고물이라니!"], ["The quintain that she had put up with so much anxious care; the game that she had prepared for the amusement of the stalwart yeomen of the country; the sport that had been honoured by the affection of so many of their ancestors!", "그토록 노심초사하며 세워 놓은 퀸틴이, 이 고장의 건장한 자영농들을 즐겁게 하려고 마련한 그 놀이가, 그토록 많은 선조들의 애호로 영예를 입어 온 그 경기가!"], ["It cut her to the heart to hear it so denominated by her own brother.", "다른 사람도 아닌 제 오라비의 입에서 그런 이름으로 불리는 것을 듣자니 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["There were but the two of them left together in the world, and it had ever been one of the rules by which Miss Thorne had regulated her conduct through life to say nothing that could provoke her brother.", "이 세상에 남은 것은 남매 둘뿐이었고, 오라비를 노엽게 할 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것은 손 양이 평생 제 처신을 다스려 온 규칙 가운데 하나였다."], ["She had often had to suffer from his indifference to time-honoured British customs, but she had always suffered in silence.", "유서 깊은 영국의 관습에 대한 오라비의 무심함 때문에 그는 여러 번 속을 썩였으나, 언제나 잠자코 썩였다."], ["It was part of her creed that the head of the family should never be upbraided in his own house, and Miss Thorne had lived up to her creed.", "가문의 어른을 그 집 안에서 나무라서는 안 된다는 것이 그의 신조의 일부였고, 손 양은 제 신조대로 살아왔다."], ["Now, however, she was greatly tried.", "그러나 이번만은 시험이 컸다."], ["The colour mounted to her ancient cheek, and the fire blazed in her still bright eyes; but yet she said nothing.", "늙은 뺨에 홍조가 오르고 여전히 빛나는 눈에는 불길이 일었으나, 그래도 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She resolved that, at any rate, to him nothing more should be said about the quintain that day.", "그는 적어도 오라비에게만은 그날 다시 퀸틴 이야기를 꺼내지 않으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["She sipped her tea in silent sorrow and thought with painful regret of the glorious days when her great ancestor Ealfried had successfully held Ullathorne against a Norman invader.", "그는 말없이 슬픔에 잠긴 채 차를 홀짝이며, 위대한 선조 엘프리드가 노르만 침입자에 맞서 울라손을 지켜 낸 영광의 나날을 아프게 그리워했다."], ["There was no such spirit now left in her family except that small useless spark which burnt in her own bosom.", "이제 그 집안에는 그런 기개가 남아 있지 않았다. 오직 제 가슴속에서 타는 작고 쓸모없는 불티 하나뿐이었다."], ["And she herself, was not she at this moment intent on entertaining a descendant of those very Normans, a vain proud countess with a Frenchified name who would only think that she graced Ullathorne too highly by entering its portals?", "그리고 그 자신은, 바로 이 순간 그 노르만인들의 후손을, 프랑스식 이름을 지녔으며 울라손의 문지방을 넘어 주는 것만으로도 이 집에 과분한 영광을 베푼다고 여길 허영 많고 오만한 백작부인을 대접하는 데 골몰하고 있지 않은가?"], ["Was it likely that an Honourable John, the son of an Earl De Courcy, should ride at a quintain in company with Saxon yeomen?", "드 코시 백작의 아들인 아너러블 존이 색슨 자영농들과 나란히 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 일이 있을 법한가?"], ["And why should she expect her brother to do that which her brother's guests would decline to do?", "그리고 제 오라비의 손님들이 하지 않으려는 일을 어찌 제 오라비에게 기대한단 말인가?"]], [["Some dim faint idea of the impracticability of her own views flitted across her brain.", "제 생각이 실행 불가능하다는 어렴풋하고 희미한 관념이 그의 머리를 스쳐 갔다."], ["Perhaps it was necessary that races doomed to live on the same soil should give way to each other and adopt each other's pursuits.", "같은 흙 위에서 살도록 운명 지어진 종족들이 서로에게 자리를 내주고 서로의 소일거리를 받아들이는 것이 어쩌면 필연인지도 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps it was impossible that after more than five centuries of close intercourse, Normans should remain Normans, and Saxons, Saxons.", "다섯 세기가 넘도록 가까이 부대끼고서도 노르만인이 노르만인으로, 색슨인이 색슨인으로 남아 있기란 어쩌면 불가능한 일인지도 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps, after all, her neighbours were wiser than herself.", "어쩌면 결국 이웃들이 제 자신보다 지혜로운지도 몰랐다."], ["Such ideas did occasionally present themselves to Miss Thorne's mind and make her sad enough.", "이런 생각이 이따금 손 양의 마음에 떠올라 그를 제법 서글프게 했다."], ["But it never occurred to her that her favourite quintain was but a modern copy of a Norman knight's amusement, an adaptation of the noble tourney to the tastes and habits of the Saxon yeomen.", "그러나 그가 그토록 아끼는 퀸틴이란 것이 실은 노르만 기사의 놀이를 근래에 본뜬 것이요, 고귀한 마상 시합을 색슨 자영농의 취향과 습속에 맞추어 고친 것임은 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Of this she was ignorant, and it would have been cruelty to instruct her.", "이 점을 그는 알지 못했고, 그것을 가르쳐 주는 것은 잔인한 일이었으리라."]], [["When Mr. Thorne saw the tear in her eye, he repented himself of his contemptuous expression.", "손 씨는 누이의 눈에 맺힌 눈물을 보고 제 경멸조의 표현을 뉘우쳤다."], ["By him also it was recognized as a binding law that every whim of his sister was to be respected.", "누이의 모든 변덕을 존중해야 한다는 것은 그에게도 구속력 있는 법이었다."], ["He was not perhaps so firm in his observances to her as she was in hers to him.", "그가 누이를 위해 지키는 정도는 누이가 그를 위해 지키는 정도만큼 굳지는 못했을 것이다."], ["But his intentions were equally good, and whenever he found that he had forgotten them, it was matter of grief to him.", "그러나 그 뜻만은 똑같이 좋았고, 그것을 잊었음을 깨달을 때마다 그것은 그에게 슬픔거리였다."]], [["\"My dear Monica,\"", "“모니카,”"], ["said he, \"I beg your pardon.", "그가 말했다. “내가 미안하오."], ["I don't in the least mean to speak ill of the game.", "그 놀이를 헐뜯을 뜻은 조금도 없었소."], ["When I called it a rattletrap, I merely meant that it was so for a man of my age.", "덜컹거리는 고물이라 한 것은 내 나이의 사람에게 그렇다는 말이었을 뿐이오."], ["You know you always forget that I an't a young man.\"", "내가 젊은 사람이 아니라는 걸 그대는 늘 잊어버리지 않소.”"]], [["\"I am quite sure you are not an old man, Wilfred,\"", "“그대가 늙은이가 아니라는 건 내가 잘 아오, 윌프레드.”"], ["said she, accepting the apology in her heart and smiling at him with the tear still on her cheek.", "그가 사과를 마음으로 받아들이고 뺨에 눈물이 아직 남은 채로 오라비에게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"If I was five-and-twenty, or thirty,\"", "“내가 스물다섯이나 서른이라면,”"], ["continued he, \"I should like nothing better than riding at the quintain all day.\"", "그가 말을 이었다. “하루 종일 퀸틴을 향해 말을 달리는 것보다 더 좋은 일은 없을 거요.”"]], [["\"But you are not too old to hunt or to shoot,\"", "“하지만 그대는 사냥이나 총질을 못 할 만큼 늙지는 않았잖소.”"], ["said she.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"If you can jump over a ditch and hedge, I am sure you could turn the quintain round.\"", "“도랑과 산울타리를 뛰어넘을 수 있다면 퀸틴쯤이야 능히 돌려놓을 수 있을 것이오.”"]], [["\"But when I ride over the hedges, my dear--and it isn't very often I do that--but when I do ride over the hedges, there isn't any bag of flour coming after me.", "“하지만 내가 산울타리를 넘어갈 때는 말이오 — 그것도 그리 자주 하는 일은 아니오만 — 아무튼 산울타리를 넘어갈 때는 밀가루 자루가 뒤에서 쫓아오지는 않소."], ["Think how I'd look taking the countess out to breakfast with the back of my head all covered with meal.\"", "내가 뒤통수에 온통 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 채 백작부인을 모시고 조찬에 들어가는 꼴을 한번 생각해 보시오.”"]], [["Miss Thorne said nothing further.", "손 양은 더 말하지 않았다."], ["She didn't like the allusion to the countess.", "백작부인을 들먹이는 것이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She couldn't be satisfied with the reflection that the sports at Ullathorne should be interfered with by the personal attentions necessary for a Lady De Courcy.", "울라손의 경기가 레이디 드 코시 한 사람에게 바쳐야 할 사사로운 시중 때문에 방해받아야 한다는 생각으로는 도저히 흡족할 수 없었다."], ["But she saw that it was useless for her to push the matter further. It was conceded that Mr.", "그러나 그는 이 문제를 더 밀어붙여야 아무 소용이 없음을 알았다."], ["Thorne was to be spared the quintain, and Miss Thorne determined to trust wholly to a youthful knight of hers, an immense favourite, who, as she often declared, was a pattern to the young men of the age and an excellent sample of an English yeoman.", "손 씨는 퀸틴을 면제받는 것으로 정해졌고, 손 양은 제가 몹시 아끼는 젊은 기사 하나에게 모든 것을 걸기로 마음먹었다. 그가 늘 말하기를, 그 젊은이야말로 이 시대 젊은이들의 본보기요 영국 자영농의 훌륭한 표본이었다."]], [["This was Farmer Greenacre's eldest son, who, to tell the truth, had from his earliest years taken the exact measure of Miss Thorne's foot.", "이 젊은이는 농부 그린에이커의 맏아들이었는데, 사실을 말하자면 어린 시절부터 손 양의 마음을 손바닥 들여다보듯 정확히 재고 있었다."], ["In his boyhood he had never failed to obtain from her apples, pocket-money, and forgiveness for his numerous trespasses; and now in his early manhood he got privileges and immunities which were equally valuable.", "소년 시절 그는 손 양에게서 사과와 용돈과, 수없이 저지른 무단 침입에 대한 용서를 얻어 내는 데 한 번도 실패한 적이 없었다. 그리고 이제 청년이 된 지금은 그에 못지않게 값진 특권과 면제를 누렸다."], ["He was allowed a day or two's shooting in September; he schooled the squire's horses; got slips of trees out of the orchard and roots of flowers out of the garden; and had the fishing of the little river altogether in his own hands.", "9월이면 하루 이틀 사냥이 허락되었고, 지주의 말들을 길들였으며, 과수원에서 묘목을 얻고 정원에서 꽃뿌리를 얻었으며, 작은 강의 낚시는 통째로 제 손아귀에 있었다."], ["He had undertaken to come mounted on a nag of his father's and show the way at the quintain post.", "그는 제 아버지의 말을 타고 나와 퀸틴 기둥 앞에서 본을 보이기로 약속해 둔 터였다."], ["Whatever young Greenacre did the others would do after him.", "젊은 그린에이커가 무엇을 하든 다른 이들은 그를 따라 할 것이었다."], ["The juvenile Lookalofts might stand aloof, but the rest of the youth of Ullathorne would be sure to venture if Harry Greenacre showed the way.", "어린 룩어로프트네야 멀찍이 물러서 있을지 몰라도, 해리 그린에이커가 본을 보이면 울라손의 나머지 젊은이들은 반드시 나서고야 말 것이었다."], ["And so Miss Thorne made up her mind to dispense with the noble Johns and Georges and trust, as her ancestors had done before her, to the thews and sinews of native Ullathorne growth.", "그리하여 손 양은 귀하신 존이며 조지며 하는 이들을 단념하고, 선조들이 그러했듯 울라손 땅에서 자란 토박이의 힘줄과 근육에 기대기로 마음먹었다."]], [["At about nine the lower orders began to congregate in the paddock and park, under the surveillance of Mr.", "9시쯤 되자 아래 계층 사람들이 방목장과 공원에 모여들기 시작했다."], ["Plomacy and the head gardener and head groom, who were sworn in as his deputies and were to assist him in keeping the peace and promoting the sports.", "플로머시 씨와, 그의 대리로 선서를 하고 치안 유지와 경기 진행을 돕게 된 수석 정원사와 수석 마부의 감독 아래였다."], ["Many of the younger inhabitants of the neighbourhood, thinking that they could not have too much of a good thing, had come at a very early hour, and the road between the house and the church had been thronged for some time before the gates were thrown open.", "이웃 마을의 젊은 축 가운데 여럿은 좋은 일은 아무리 많아도 지나치지 않다고 여겼는지 아주 이른 시각에 와 있었고, 저택과 교회 사이의 길은 대문이 열리기 한참 전부터 사람들로 붐볐다."]], [["And then another difficulty of huge dimensions arose, a difficulty which Mr. Plomacy had indeed foreseen and for which he was in some sort provided.", "그러자 또 하나의 커다란 난관이 일어났는데, 플로머시 씨가 진작에 내다보고 나름대로 대비해 둔 난관이었다."], ["Some of those who wished to share Miss Thorne's hospitality were not so particular as they should have been as to the preliminary ceremony of an invitation.", "손 양의 환대에 끼고자 하는 이들 가운데 더러는 초대라는 예비 절차에 대해 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 까다롭지 못했다."], ["They doubtless conceived that they had been overlooked by accident, and instead of taking this in dudgeon, as their betters would have done, they good-naturedly put up with the slight, and showed that they did so by presenting themselves at the gate in their Sunday best.", "그들은 필시 자기들이 실수로 빠뜨려졌으려니 여겼고, 윗사람들이라면 그것을 분개할 일로 삼았겠지만 그들은 사람 좋게 그 홀대를 참아 넘겼으며, 참아 넘겼다는 표시로 나들이옷을 차려입고 대문 앞에 나타났다."]], [["Mr. Plomacy, however, well-knew who were welcome and who were not.", "그러나 플로머시 씨는 누가 환영받을 사람이고 누가 아닌지를 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["To some, even though uninvited, he allowed ingress.", "초대받지 않은 이들 가운데서도 더러는 들여보내 주었다."], ["\"Don't be too particular, Plomacy,\"", "“너무 까다롭게 굴지 마시오, 플로머시,”"], ["his mistress had said, \"especially with the children.", "여주인이 그에게 이렇게 말해 두었던 것이다. “아이들에게는 특히 그러하오."], ["If they live anywhere near, let them in.\"", "근처에 사는 아이들이거든 들여보내 주시오.”"]], [["Acting on this hint, Mr. Plomacy did let in many an eager urchin and a few tidily dressed girls with their swains who in no way belonged to the property.", "이 언질에 따라 플로머시 씨는 눈이 반짝이는 개구쟁이 여럿과, 이 영지와는 아무 상관도 없는 말끔한 차림의 처녀들과 그 짝들을 더러 들여보내 주었다."], ["But to the denizens of the city he was inexorable.", "그러나 도시 사람들에게는 요지부동이었다. 그날 바체스터의 도제들이 여럿 그곳에 나타나 애처롭게 사정하기를, 자기는 한 주 내내 울라손에서 쓸 안장과 장화를 만들었노라고, 말들에게 먹일 약을 지었노라고, 부엌에 들어갈 고기를 발랐노라고 호소했다."], ["Many a Barchester apprentice made his appearance there that day and urged with piteous supplication that he had been working all the week in making saddles and boots for the use of Ullathorne, in compounding doses for the horses, or cutting up carcasses for the kitchen.", "그런 주장은 하나도 통하지 않았다. 플로머시 씨는 도시의 도제 따위는 알지 못했다."], ["No such claim was allowed. Mr.", "그가 들일 사람은 이 영지의 소작인과 일꾼들이었다."], ["Plomacy knew nothing about the city apprentices; he was to admit the tenants and labourers on the estate; Miss Thorne wasn't going to take in the whole city of Barchester; and so on.", "손 양이 바체스터 시 전체를 들일 생각은 없으시다. 그런 식이었다."]], [["Nevertheless, before the day was half over, all this was found to be useless.", "그럼에도 하루가 절반도 지나기 전에 이 모든 것이 소용없는 일임이 드러났다."], ["Almost anybody who chose to come made his way into the park, and the care of the guardians was transferred to the tables on which the banquet was spread.", "오려고 마음먹은 사람이면 거의 누구나 공원 안으로 들어왔고, 문지기들의 소임은 잔치 음식이 차려진 식탁 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Even here there was many an unauthorised claimant for a place, of whom it was impossible to get quit without more commotion than the place and food were worth.", "거기서도 자격 없이 자리를 요구하는 이가 많았는데, 그 자리와 음식이 지닌 값어치보다 더 큰 소동을 일으키지 않고서는 그들을 떼어 낼 도리가 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI. Ullathorne Sports--Act I", "# 제36장. 울라손 운동회 — 제1막"]], [["The trouble in civilized life of entertaining company, as it is called too generally without much regard to strict veracity, is so great that it cannot but be matter of wonder that people are so fond of attempting it.", "문명 생활에서 이른바 손님을 접대한다는 일 — 엄밀한 진실성은 별로 따지지 않고 흔히들 그렇게 부른다 — 이 얼마나 성가신지를 헤아려 보면, 사람들이 그토록 그 일에 열을 올린다는 것은 놀랍기 그지없다."], ["It is difficult to ascertain what is the quid pro quo.", "그 quid pro quo가 대체 무엇인지를 알아내기가 어렵다."], ["If they who give such laborious parties, and who endure such toil and turmoil in the vain hope of giving them successfully, really enjoyed the parties given by others, the matter could be understood.", "그토록 공들인 잔치를 베푸는 이들이, 그리고 그것을 성공적으로 치러 내겠다는 헛된 희망 속에서 그토록 애쓰고 법석을 떠는 이들이, 정말로 남이 베푼 잔치를 즐긴다면 그 일은 이해할 만하다."], ["A sense of justice would induce men and women to undergo, in behalf of others, those miseries which others had undergone in their behalf.", "공평하다는 감각이 남녀로 하여금 남을 위하여, 남이 저를 위하여 겪었던 그 고초를 마찬가지로 겪게 만들 것이다."], ["But they all profess that going out is as great a bore as receiving, and to look at them when they are out, one cannot but believe them.", "그러나 그들은 하나같이 남의 집에 나가는 것도 손님을 맞는 것 못지않게 지겨운 일이라고 공언하며, 정작 나가 있는 그들을 보고 있노라면 그 말을 믿지 않을 수 없다."]], [["Entertain!", "접대라니!"], ["Who shall have sufficient self-assurance, who shall feel sufficient confidence in his own powers to dare to boast that he can entertain his company?", "누가 감히 제 힘을 그토록 자신하고 제 능력을 그토록 믿어 의심치 않기에, 제 손님을 접대할 수 있노라고 큰소리칠 것인가?"], ["A clown can sometimes do so, and sometimes a dancer in short petticoats and stuffed pink legs; occasionally, perhaps, a singer.", "광대라면 더러 해내고, 짧은 치마에 살을 채운 분홍 다리를 한 무희도 더러 해내며, 어쩌면 가수도 이따금은 해낼 것이다."], ["But beyond these, success in this art of entertaining is not often achieved.", "그러나 그 밖에는 이 접대라는 기예에서 성공을 거두는 일이 흔치 않다."], ["Young men and girls linking themselves kind with kind, pairing like birds in spring because nature wills it, they, after a simple fashion, do entertain each other.", "젊은 사내와 처녀들이 끼리끼리 짝을 지어, 자연이 그리 시키는 까닭에 봄철의 새들처럼 짝을 이루는 것 — 그들은 소박한 방식으로나마 서로를 정말로 접대한다."], ["Few others even try.", "그 밖에는 시도라도 해 보는 이가 드물다."]], [["Ladies, when they open their houses, modestly confessing, it may be presumed, their own incapacity, mainly trust to wax candles and upholstery.", "부인네들은 제 집을 열 때, 아마도 저의 무능을 겸손히 시인하는 뜻이겠으나, 주로 밀랍 초와 실내 장식에 기댄다."], ["Gentlemen seem to rely on their white waistcoats.", "신사들은 제 흰 조끼를 믿는 듯하다."], ["To these are added, for the delight of the more sensual, champagne and such good things of the table as fashion allows to be still considered as comestible.", "여기에 좀 더 감각적인 이들의 즐거움을 위하여 샴페인과, 유행이 아직 먹을 것으로 쳐 주는 몇 가지 좋은 음식이 더해진다."], ["Even in this respect the world is deteriorating.", "이 점에서조차 세상은 나빠지고 있다."], ["All the good soups are now tabooed, and at the houses of one's accustomed friends--small barristers, doctors, government clerks, and such-like (for we cannot all of us always live as grandees, surrounded by an elysium of livery servants)--one gets a cold potato handed to one as a sort of finale to one's slice of mutton.", "훌륭한 수프는 이제 모두 금기가 되었고, 늘 드나드는 벗들의 집 — 별 볼 일 없는 법정 변호사, 의사, 관청 서기 따위의 집(우리가 모두 언제나 제복 입은 하인들의 낙원에 둘러싸인 귀인으로 살 수는 없는 노릇이니) — 에 가면 양고기 한 점 뒤에 마무리랍시고 식은 감자 한 알을 건네받는다."], ["Alas for those happy days when one could say to one's neighbour, \"Jones, shall I give you some mashed turnip?", "아, 옆자리 사람에게 “존스, 순무 으깬 것을 좀 드릴까요? 양배추를 조금 청해도 되겠습니까?”"], ["May I trouble you for a little cabbage?\"", "하고 말할 수 있던 그 행복한 시절이여!"], ["And then the pleasure of drinking wine with Mrs. Jones and Miss Smith--with all the Joneses and all the Smiths!", "그리고 존스 부인, 스미스 양과 — 존스 집안 전부, 스미스 집안 전부와 — 잔을 나누던 그 즐거움이여!"], ["These latter-day habits are certainly more economical.", "요즈음의 이런 습속이 더 알뜰한 것만은 분명하다."]], [["Miss Thorne, however, boldly attempted to leave the modern, beaten track, and made a positive effort to entertain her guests.", "그러나 손 양은 현대의 닳고 닳은 길에서 벗어나 보려고 대담하게 시도했고, 제 손님들을 정말로 접대해 보겠다는 적극적인 노력을 기울였다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["She did so with but moderate success.", "그 성과는 그저 그런 정도였다."], ["They had all their own way of going, and would not go her way.", "손님들에게는 저마다 제가 가는 길이 있었고, 그들은 그 여자의 길로 가려 하지 않았다."], ["She piped to them, but they would not dance.", "그 여자가 피리를 불었으나 그들은 춤추지 않았다."], ["She offered to them good, honest household cake made of currants and flour and eggs and sweetmeat, but they would feed themselves on trashy wafers from the shop of the Barchester pastry-cook, on chalk and gum and adulterated sugar.", "그 여자는 건포도와 밀가루와 달걀과 설탕절임으로 만든 정직하고 좋은 집 케이크를 내놓았으나, 그들은 바체스터 제과점에서 파는 값싼 웨이퍼 과자를, 백묵과 고무와 불순물 섞인 설탕을 먹고자 했다."], ["Poor Miss Thorne!", "가엾은 손 양이여!"], ["Yours is not the first honest soul that has vainly striven to recall the glories of happy days gone by!", "지나간 행복한 날들의 영광을 되불러 오려다 헛되이 애쓴 정직한 영혼이 그대가 처음은 아니로다!"], ["If fashion suggests to a Lady De Courcy that, when invited to a déjeuner at twelve she ought to come at three, no eloquence of thine will teach her the advantage of a nearer approach to punctuality.", "유행이 레이디 드 코시에게 12시 déjeuner에 초대받았거든 3시에 가는 것이 마땅하다고 일러 준다면, 그대의 어떤 웅변으로도 시간을 좀 더 지키는 편이 낫다는 것을 그 여자에게 가르치지는 못하리라."]], [["She had fondly thought that when she called on her friends to come at twelve, and specially begged them to believe that she meant it, she would be able to see them comfortably seated in their tents at two.", "그 여자는 벗들에게 12시에 오라고 청하면서 그것이 진심임을 특별히 믿어 달라고 당부해 두었으니 2시면 그들이 천막 안에 편안히 자리 잡은 것을 볼 수 있으리라고 다정하게 생각해 두었다."], ["Vain woman--or rather ignorant woman--ignorant of the advances of that civilization which the world had witnessed while she was growing old.", "헛된 여자여 — 아니 차라리 무지한 여자여 — 제가 늙어 가는 동안 세상이 목격한 저 문명의 진보를 알지 못하는 여자여."], ["At twelve she found herself alone, dressed in all the glory of the newest of her many suits of raiment--with strong shoes however, and a serviceable bonnet on her head, and a warm, rich shawl on her shoulders.", "12시가 되자 그 여자는 혼자였다. 여러 벌 되는 옷 가운데 가장 새것을 갖추어 화려하게 차려입되 신만은 튼튼한 것을 신고, 머리에는 실용적인 보닛을 쓰고, 어깨에는 따뜻하고 값진 숄을 걸친 차림이었다."], ["Thus clad, she peered out into the tent, went to the ha-ha, and satisfied herself that at any rate the youngsters were amusing themselves, spoke a word to Mrs. Greenacre over the ditch, and took one look at the quintain.", "그렇게 차려입고 그 여자는 천막을 들여다보고, 하하 도랑까지 나가 적어도 젊은것들만은 저희끼리 즐기고 있음을 확인하고, 도랑 너머로 그린에이커 부인과 한마디 나누고, 퀸틴을 한 번 살펴보았다."], ["Three or four young farmers were turning the machine round and round and poking at the bag of flour in a manner not at all intended by the inventor of the game; but no mounted sportsmen were there.", "젊은 농부 서넛이 그 장치를 빙빙 돌려 가며, 이 놀이를 고안한 사람이 전혀 의도하지 않은 방식으로 밀가루 자루를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있었다. 그러나 말에 오른 선수는 하나도 없었다."], ["Miss Thorne looked at her watch.", "손 양은 시계를 보았다."], ["It was only fifteen minutes past twelve, and it was understood that Harry Greenacre was not to begin till the half-hour.", "아직 12시 15분이었고, 해리 그린에이커는 12시 반이 되기 전에는 시작하지 않기로 되어 있었다."]], [["Miss Thorne returned to her drawing-room rather quicker than was her wont, fearing that the countess might come and find none to welcome her.", "손 양은 백작부인이 왔는데 맞아 줄 사람이 아무도 없을까 두려워, 평소보다는 조금 빠른 걸음으로 응접실로 돌아왔다."], ["She need not have hurried, for no one was there.", "서두를 것도 없었다. 거기에는 아무도 없었으니까."], ["At half-past twelve she peeped into the kitchen; at a quarter to one she was joined by her brother; and just then the first fashionable arrival took place.", "12시 반에 그 여자는 부엌을 들여다보았고, 1시 15분 전에는 오라비가 곁에 와 주었으며, 바로 그때 첫 번째 상류층 손님이 도착했다."], ["Mrs. Clantantram was announced.", "클랜탠트럼 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 있었다."]], [["No announcement was necessary, indeed, for the good lady's voice was heard as she walked across the courtyard to the house, scolding the unfortunate postilion who had driven her from Barchester.", "사실 전갈은 필요하지도 않았다. 그 착한 부인이 안뜰을 가로질러 집으로 걸어오면서, 바체스터에서 자기를 태우고 온 가엾은 마부를 나무라는 목소리가 다 들렸던 것이다."], ["At the moment Miss Thorne could not but be thankful that the other guests were more fashionable and were thus spared the fury of Mrs. Clantantram's indignation.", "그 순간 손 양은 다른 손님들이 더 상류층이어서 클랜탠트럼 부인의 분노가 퍼붓는 것을 면했다는 사실에 감사하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, Miss Thorne, look here!\"", "“오, 손 양, 이것 좀 보시오!”"], ["said she as soon as she found herself in the drawing-room; \"do look at my roque-laure.", "응접실에 들어서기가 무섭게 부인이 말했다. “내 로클로르 좀 보시오."], ["It's clean spoilt, and forever.", "아주 못 쓰게 되었소, 그것도 영영."], ["I wouldn't but wear it because I knew you wished us all to be grand to-day, and yet I had my misgivings.", "오늘은 우리 모두 잘 차려입기를 바라시는 줄 알았기에 굳이 이걸 입고 나선 것인데, 그러면서도 어쩐지 꺼림칙하기는 했다오."], ["Oh dear, oh dear!", "아이고, 아이고!"], ["It was five-and-twenty shillings a yard.\"", "야드당 25실링짜리였는데.”"]], [["The Barchester post-horses had misbehaved in some unfortunate manner just as Mrs. Clantantram was getting out of the chaise and had nearly thrown her under the wheel.", "바체스터의 역마들이 클랜탠트럼 부인이 마차에서 내리려는 바로 그 순간에 어떤 불운한 방식으로 말썽을 부려, 하마터면 부인을 바퀴 밑으로 넘어뜨릴 뻔했던 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Clantantram belonged to other days, and therefore, though she had but little else to recommend her, Miss Thorne was to a certain extent fond of her.", "클랜탠트럼 부인은 지나간 시절에 속한 사람이었고, 그래서 그 밖에는 딱히 내세울 것이 없었음에도 손 양은 그 여자를 어느 정도 좋아했다."], ["She sent the roque-laure away to be cleaned, and lent her one of her best shawls out of her own wardrobe.", "손 양은 로클로르를 손질하도록 내보내고, 제 옷장에서 가장 좋은 숄 하나를 꺼내 빌려 주었다."]], [["The next comer was Mr. Arabin, who was immediately informed of Mrs. Clantantram's misfortune and of her determination to pay neither master nor post-boy, although, as she remarked, she intended to get her lift home before she made known her mind upon that matter. Then a good deal of rustling was heard in the sort of lobby that was used for the ladies' outside cloaks, and the door having been thrown wide open, the servant announced, not in the most confident of voices, Mrs. Lookaloft, and the Miss Lookalofts, and Mr. Augustus Lookaloft.", "다음에 온 사람은 애러빈 씨였는데, 그는 곧장 클랜탠트럼 부인의 불운과, 마차 주인에게도 마부 아이에게도 삯을 치르지 않겠다는 부인의 결심을 전해 들었다. 물론 부인의 말인즉, 그 마음을 밝히기 전에 우선 집까지 태워다 달라고 할 작정이라는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 부인들의 겉옷을 두는 현관방 쪽에서 옷자락 스치는 소리가 한바탕 들리더니, 문이 활짝 열리며 하인이 그리 자신 있지 못한 목소리로 룩어로프트 부인과 룩어로프트 양들과 오거스터스 룩어로프트 씨의 도착을 알렸다."]], [["Poor man!--we mean the footman.", "가엾은 사람!"], ["He knew, none better, that Mrs.", "— 우리가 말하는 것은 그 하인이다."], ["Lookaloft had no business there, that she was not wanted there, and would not be welcome.", "그는 룩어로프트 부인이 거기 올 자격이 없다는 것, 아무도 그 여자를 원하지 않는다는 것, 환영받지 못하리라는 것을 누구보다 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But he had not the courage to tell a stout lady with a low dress, short sleeves, and satin at eight shillings a yard that she had come to the wrong tent; he had not dared to hint to young ladies with white dancing shoes and long gloves that there was a place ready for them in the paddock.", "그러나 목이 깊이 파인 옷에 소매가 짧고 야드당 8실링짜리 새틴을 걸친 뚱뚱한 부인에게 천막을 잘못 찾아왔다고 말할 용기가 그에게는 없었다. 흰 무도화를 신고 긴 장갑을 낀 아가씨들에게 방목장에 그대들 자리가 마련되어 있다고 넌지시 일러 줄 엄두도 내지 못했다."], ["And thus Mrs. Lookaloft carried her point, broke through the guards, and made her way into the citadel.", "그리하여 룩어로프트 부인은 뜻을 이루었고, 파수를 뚫었으며, 성채 안으로 들어섰다."], ["That she would have to pass an uncomfortable time there she had surmised before.", "거기서 편치 못한 시간을 보내야 하리라는 것쯤은 이미 짐작하고 있었다."], ["But nothing now could rob her of the power of boasting that she had consorted on the lawn with the squire and Miss Thorne, with a countess, a bishop, and the county grandees, while Mrs.", "그러나 이제 그 무엇도, 그린에이커 부인 따위가 공원에서 쟁기꾼 아이들과 어울려 거니는 동안 저는 잔디밭에서 지주와 손 양과, 백작부인과 주교와 이 고장 유지들과 나란히 어울렸노라고 자랑할 권리를 그 여자에게서 빼앗지 못할 것이었다."], ["Greenacre and such-like were walking about with the ploughboys in the park. It was a great point gained by Mrs.", "룩어로프트 부인이 크게 한 점 딴 셈이었으니, 이제부터 바체스터의 상인들이 그 남편에게 조금도 망설이지 않는 필치로 T."], ["Lookaloft, and it might be fairly expected that from this time forward the tradesmen of Barchester would, with undoubting pens, address her husband as T. Lookaloft, Esquire.", "룩어로프트 귀하라고 써 보내리라고 기대해도 무방했다."]], [["Mrs. Lookaloft's pluck carried her through everything, and she walked triumphant into the Ullathorne drawing-room; but her children did feel a little abashed at the sort of reception they met with.", "룩어로프트 부인의 뱃심은 무슨 일이든 뚫고 나아가게 했고, 그 여자는 의기양양하게 울라손 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["It was not in Miss Thorne's heart to insult her own guests, but neither was it in her disposition to overlook such effrontery.", "그러나 그 자식들은 저희가 받은 대접의 종류에 다소 기가 죽었다. 제 손님을 모욕하는 것은 손 양의 마음에 없는 일이었으나, 그런 뻔뻔함을 못 본 체 넘기는 것 또한 그 성미에 없는 일이었다."]], [["\"Oh, Mrs. Lookaloft, is this you?\"", "“오, 룩어로프트 부인, 부인이었구려.”"], ["said she.", "손 양이 말했다."], ["\"And your daughters and son?", "“따님들과 아드님까지 함께요?"], ["Well, we're very glad to see you, but I'm sorry you've come in such low dresses, as we are all going out of doors.", "그래요, 와 주어 매우 반갑소만, 우리가 모두 바깥으로 나갈 참인데 그렇게 목이 파인 옷을 입고 오셨으니 안됐구려."], ["Could we lend you anything?\"", "무엇이든 빌려 드릴까요?”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no thank ye, Miss Thorne,\"", "“아이고, 아닙니다요, 고맙습니다, 손 양.”"], ["said the mother; \"the girls and myself are quite used to low dresses, when we're out.\"", "어미가 말했다. “딸애들이나 저나 바깥에 나갈 적에는 목이 파인 옷에 아주 익숙합지요.”"]], [["\"Are you, indeed?\"", "“정말 그러시오?” 손 양이 몸서리를 치며 말했다."], ["said Miss Thorne shuddering--but the shudder was lost on Mrs. Lookaloft.", "그러나 그 몸서리는 룩어로프트 부인에게 가닿지 못했다."]], [["\"And where's Lookaloft?\"", "“그런데 룩어로프트는 어디 있소?”"], ["said the master of the house, coming up to welcome his tenant's wife.", "집주인이 제 소작인의 아내를 맞으러 다가오며 말했다."], ["Let the faults of the family be what they would, he could not but remember that their rent was well paid; he was therefore not willing to give them a cold shoulder.", "그 집안의 허물이 무엇이든 간에, 그는 그들이 소작료만은 잘 치른다는 것을 잊을 수 없었다. 그러니 그들을 쌀쌀맞게 대할 마음이 없었다."]], [["\"Such a headache, Mr. Thorne!\"", "“어찌나 머리가 아프다는지요, 손 씨!”"], ["said Mrs. Lookaloft.", "룩어로프트 부인이 말했다."], ["\"In fact he couldn't stir, or you may be certain on such a day he would not have absented hisself.\"", "“정말이지 꼼짝을 못 하지 뭡니까요. 안 그랬으면 이런 날에 빠질 사람이 아닌 줄은 잘 아시지 않습니까요.”"]], [["\"Dear me,\"", "“저런.”"], ["said Miss Thorne.", "손 양이 말했다."], ["\"If he is so ill, I'm sure you'd wish to be with him.\"", "“그렇게 편찮으시다면 부인도 곁에 있고 싶겠구려.”"]], [["\"Not at all!\"", "“천만에요!”"], ["said Mrs. Lookaloft.", "룩어로프트 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Not at all, Miss Thorne.", "“천만의 말씀입니다, 손 양."], ["It is only bilious you know, and when he's that way, he can bear nobody nigh him.\"", "그저 담증일 뿐이고요, 그이는 그럴 적엔 곁에 아무도 못 오게 합지요.”"]], [["The fact, however, was that Mr.", "그러나 사실인즉, 룩어로프트 씨는 아내보다 분별이 더 있었는지 아니면 용기가 덜했는지, 손 양의 응접실에 밀고 들어가지 않기로 한 것이었다."], ["Lookaloft, having either more sense or less courage than his wife, had not chosen to intrude on Miss Thorne's drawing-room, and as he could not very well have gone among the plebeians while his wife was with the patricians, he thought it most expedient to remain at Rosebank.", "그리고 아내가 귀족들 틈에 끼어 있는 마당에 저 혼자 평민들 사이로 갈 수도 없는 노릇이었으므로, 그는 로즈뱅크에 남아 있는 것이 가장 상책이라고 생각했다."]], [["Mrs. Lookaloft soon found herself on a sofa, and the Miss Lookalofts on two chairs, while Mr.", "룩어로프트 부인은 곧 소파 하나를 차지했고 룩어로프트 양들은 의자 둘을 차지했으며, 오거스터스 씨는 문 가까이 서 있었다."], ["Augustus stood near the door; and here they remained till in due time they were seated, all four together, at the bottom of the dining-room table.", "그들은 때가 되어 넷이 함께 식당 식탁의 맨 끄트머리에 앉게 될 때까지 그렇게 있었다."]], [["Then the Grantlys came--the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly and the two girls, and Dr.", "그다음에 그랜틀리 일행이 왔다 — 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인과 두 딸, 그리고 그윈 박사와 하딩 씨였다."], ["Gwynne and Mr. Harding.", "운 나쁘게도 그들 바로 뒤를 스탠호프 박사의 마차가 따라왔다."], ["As ill-luck would have it, they were closely followed by Dr. Stanhope's carriage.", "엘리너는 마차 창밖을 내다보다가 형부가 부인들이 내리는 것을 거들고 있는 것을 보고, 들킬까 겁이 나서 자리 안쪽으로 몸을 물렸다."], ["As Eleanor looked out of the carriage window, she saw her brother-in-law helping the ladies out and threw herself back into her seat, dreading to be discovered. She had had an odious journey.", "오는 길이 지긋지긋했던 것이다."], ["Mr. Slope's civility had been more than ordinarily greasy; and now, though he had not in fact said anything which she could notice, she had for the first time entertained a suspicion that he was intending to make love to her.", "슬로프 씨의 정중함은 여느 때보다도 더 느끼했고, 이제 와 보니, 딱히 트집 잡을 만한 말을 그가 실제로 한 것도 아닌데, 그 여자는 난생처음으로 그가 자기에게 구애할 작정이라는 의심을 품게 되었다."], ["Was it after all true that she had been conducting herself in a way that justified the world in thinking that she liked the man?", "자기가 저 사내를 좋아한다고 세상이 여겨도 할 말이 없을 만한 처신을 해 왔다는 것이 결국 사실이었단 말인가?"], ["After all, could it be possible that the archdeacon and Mr. Arabin were right, and that she was wrong?", "결국 부주교와 애러빈 씨가 옳고 제가 틀렸을 수도 있단 말인가?"], ["Charlotte Stanhope had also been watching Mr. Slope and had come to the conclusion that it behoved her brother to lose no further time, if he meant to gain the widow.", "샬럿 스탠호프 또한 슬로프 씨를 지켜보고 있었고, 제 오라비가 그 미망인을 차지할 작정이라면 더는 시간을 끌어서는 안 되겠다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["She almost regretted that it had not been contrived that Bertie should be at Ullathorne before them.", "버티가 자기들보다 먼저 울라손에 가 있도록 손을 써 두지 않은 것이 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다."]], [["Dr. Grantly did not see his sister-in-law in company with Mr. Slope, but Mr. Arabin did. Mr. Arabin came out with Mr. Thorne to the front door to welcome Mrs. Grantly, and he remained in the courtyard till all their party had passed on. Eleanor hung back in the carriage as long as she well could, but she was nearest to the door, and when Mr. Slope, having alighted, offered her his hand, she had no alternative but to take it. Mr. Arabin, standing at the open door while Mrs. Grantly was shaking hands with someone within, saw a clergyman alight from the carriage whom he at once knew to be Mr. Slope, and then he saw this clergyman hand out Mrs. Bold. Having seen so much, Mr. Arabin, rather sick at heart, followed Mrs. Grantly into the house.", "그랜틀리 박사는 처제가 슬로프 씨와 함께 있는 것을 보지 못했으나, 애러빈 씨는 보았다. 애러빈 씨는 그랜틀리 부인을 맞으려고 손 씨와 함께 현관까지 나왔고, 그 일행이 모두 지나가고 나서도 안뜰에 남아 있었다. 엘리너는 그럴듯하게 버틸 수 있는 데까지 마차 안에 뭉그적거리고 있었으나, 문에 가장 가까이 앉은 사람이 자기였고, 슬로프 씨가 먼저 내려서 손을 내밀자 그 손을 잡는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다. 그랜틀리 부인이 안쪽의 누군가와 악수를 나누는 동안 열린 문가에 서 있던 애러빈 씨는, 한 성직자가 마차에서 내리는 것을 보고 그가 슬로프 씨임을 대번에 알아보았으며, 이어 그 성직자가 볼드 부인을 부축해 내리는 것을 보았다. 그만큼을 보고 나서 애러빈 씨는 속이 몹시 상한 채 그랜틀리 부인을 따라 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Eleanor was, however, spared any further immediate degradation, for Dr.", "그러나 엘리너는 당장 더 창피를 당하는 일만은 면했다."], ["Stanhope gave her his arm across the courtyard, and Mr. Slope was fain to throw away his attention upon Charlotte.", "스탠호프 박사가 안뜰을 가로지르는 동안 팔을 내주었고, 슬로프 씨는 하는 수 없이 제 정성을 샬럿에게 허비할 수밖에 없었던 것이다."]], [["They had hardly passed into the house, and from the house to the lawn, when, with a loud rattle and such noise as great men and great women are entitled to make in their passage through the world, the Proudies drove up.", "그들이 집 안으로 들어가고 집을 지나 잔디밭으로 나서기가 무섭게, 요란한 바퀴 소리와 함께, 큰 어른과 큰 부인이 세상을 지나가면서 낼 자격이 있는 그런 소음을 일으키며 프라우디 내외가 마차를 몰고 들어섰다."], ["It was soon apparent that no everyday comer was at the door.", "문간에 온 것이 예사 손님이 아니라는 것이 곧 드러났다."], ["One servant whispered to another that it was the bishop, and the word soon ran through all the hangers-on and strange grooms and coachmen about the place. There was quite a little cortège to see the bishop and his \"lady\"", "하인 하나가 다른 하인에게 주교님이라고 속삭였고, 그 말은 이내 그곳에 얼쩡거리던 모든 사람과 낯선 마부와 마차꾼들에게 퍼졌다."], ["walk across the courtyard, and the good man was pleased to see that the church was held in such respect in the parish of St. Ewold's.", "주교와 그 “부인”이 안뜰을 가로질러 걸어가는 것을 구경하려고 제법 작은 행렬이 이루어졌으니, 이 착한 어른은 세인트 이월드 교구에서 교회가 이토록 존경받는 것을 보고 흐뭇해했다."]], [["And now the guests came fast and thick, and the lawn began to be crowded, and the room to be full. Voices buzzed, silk rustled against silk, and muslin crumpled against muslin.", "이제 손님들이 빠르게 몰려들어 잔디밭이 붐비기 시작하고 방이 가득 차기 시작했다."], ["Miss Thorne became more happy than she had been, and again bethought her of her sports.", "목소리들이 웅웅거리고, 비단이 비단에 스치고, 모슬린이 모슬린에 구겨졌다."], ["There were targets and bows and arrows prepared at the further end of the lawn.", "손 양은 아까보다 기분이 좋아져서 다시 제 운동회를 떠올렸다."], ["Here the gardens of the place encroached with a somewhat wide sweep upon the paddock and gave ample room for the doings of the toxophilites. Miss Thorne got together such daughters of Diana as could bend a bow and marshalled them to the targets.", "잔디밭 저편 끝에는 과녁과 활과 화살이 마련되어 있었다."], ["There were the Grantly girls and the Proudie girls and the Chadwick girls, and the two daughters of the burly chancellor, and Miss Knowle; and with them went Frederick and Augustus Chadwick, and young Knowle of Knowle Park, and Frank Foster of the Elms, and Mr. Vellem Deeds, the dashing attorney of the High Street, and the Rev.", "그곳에서는 이 저택의 정원이 제법 넓게 방목장 쪽으로 밀고 들어가 있어 궁술 애호가들이 놀기에 넉넉한 자리를 내주었다."], ["Mr. Green, and the Rev.", "손 양은 활을 당길 줄 아는 디아나의 딸들을 그러모아 과녁 앞으로 이끌었다."], ["Mr. Brown, and the Rev.", "그랜틀리 집안 딸들과 프라우디 집안 딸들과 채드윅 집안 딸들이 있었고, 덩치 큰 학무 참사원의 두 딸과 놀 양이 있었다."], ["Mr. White, all of whom, as in duty bound, attended the steps of the three Miss Proudies.", "그들과 함께 프레더릭 채드윅과 오거스터스 채드윅, 놀 파크의 젊은 놀, 엘름스의 프랭크 포스터, 하이 스트리트의 잘나가는 사무 변호사 벨럼 디즈 씨, 그리고 그린 목사와 브라운 목사와 화이트 목사가 따라나섰는데, 이 세 사람은 마땅한 도리로서 프라우디 양 세 자매의 걸음을 뒤따랐다."]], [["\"Did you ever ride at the quintain, Mr. Foster?\"", "“포스터 씨는 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 본 적이 있소?”"], ["said Miss Thorne as she walked with her party across the lawn.", "손 양이 일행과 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 걸으며 말했다."]], [["\"The quintain?\"", "“퀸틴이요?”"], ["said young Foster, who considered himself a dab at horsemanship.", "스스로 말타기에는 일가견이 있다고 여기는 젊은 포스터가 말했다."], ["\"Is it a sort of gate, Miss Thorne?\"", "“그게 무슨 문 같은 것입니까, 손 양?”"]], [["Miss Thorne had to explain the noble game she spoke of, and Frank Foster had to own that he never had ridden at the quintain.", "손 양은 자기가 말한 그 고귀한 놀이를 설명해 주어야 했고, 프랭크 포스터는 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 본 적이 한 번도 없노라고 실토해야 했다."]], [["\"Would you like to come and see?\"", "“가서 구경하겠소?”"], ["said Miss Thorne.", "손 양이 말했다."], ["\"There'll be plenty here you know without you, if you like it.\"", "“그대가 빠져도 여기는 사람이 넉넉하니, 마음이 내킨다면 말이오.”"]], [["\"Well, I don't mind,\"", "“뭐, 저는 상관없습니다.”"], ["said Frank.", "프랭크가 말했다."], ["\"I suppose the ladies can come too.\"", "“부인들도 함께 가도 되는 것이겠지요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“오, 그럼요.”"], ["said Miss Thorne; \"those who like it.", "손 양이 말했다. “가고 싶은 이들은요."], ["I have no doubt they'll go to see your prowess, if you'll ride, Mr. Foster.\"", "포스터 씨가 말을 타겠다면 다들 그 솜씨를 보러 갈 것이 분명하오.”"]], [["Mr. Foster looked down at a most unexceptionable pair of pantaloons, which had arrived from London only the day before.", "포스터 씨는 바로 어제 런던에서 도착한, 나무랄 데 없는 바지를 내려다보았다."], ["They were the very things, at least he thought so, for a picnic or fête champêtre, but he was not prepared to ride in them. Nor was he more encouraged than had been Mr.", "적어도 그의 생각으로는 그것이야말로 피크닉이나 야외 연회에 꼭 맞는 물건이었으나, 그것을 입고 말을 탈 준비까지는 되어 있지 않았다."], ["Thorne by the idea of being attacked from behind by the bag of flour, which Miss Thorne had graphically described to him.", "게다가 손 양이 생생하게 묘사해 준 밀가루 자루에 뒤에서 얻어맞는다는 발상 앞에서 그도 손 씨보다 더 마음이 동하지는 않았다."]], [["\"Well, I don't know about riding, Miss Thorne,\"", "“글쎄요, 말타기는 어떨지 모르겠습니다, 손 양.”"], ["said he; \"I fear I'm not quite prepared.\"", "그가 말했다. “아무래도 채비가 덜 된 듯합니다.”"]], [["Miss Thorne sighed but said nothing further.", "손 양은 한숨을 쉬었으나 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She left the toxophilites to their bows and arrows and returned towards the house.", "그 여자는 궁술 애호가들을 활과 화살에 맡겨 두고 집 쪽으로 돌아왔다."], ["But as she passed by the entrance to the small park, she thought that she might at any rate encourage the yeomen by her presence, as she could not induce her more fashionable guests to mix with them in their manly amusements.", "그러나 작은 공원으로 들어가는 어귀를 지나면서, 더 상류한 손님들을 저 사내다운 놀이에 끌어들일 수 없다면 적어도 제가 가 있음으로써 자작농들에게 힘을 실어 줄 수는 있으리라고 생각했다."], ["Accordingly she once more betook herself to the quintain post.", "그리하여 그 여자는 다시 한번 퀸틴 기둥으로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["Here to her great delight she found Harry Greenacre ready mounted, with his pole in his hand, and a lot of comrades standing round him, encouraging him to the assault.", "거기서 그 여자는 크게 기뻐하며, 해리 그린에이커가 장대를 손에 쥐고 말에 올라 채비를 갖춘 채 동무들에게 둘러싸여 어서 달려 보라는 부추김을 받고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She stood at a little distance and nodded to him in token of her good pleasure.", "그 여자는 조금 떨어져 서서 흡족하다는 표시로 그에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["\"Shall I begin, ma'am?\"", "“이제 시작해도 되겠습니까요, 마님?”"], ["said Harry, fingering his long staff in a rather awkward way, while his horse moved uneasily beneath him, not accustomed to a rider armed with such a weapon.", "해리가 제법 어설픈 손놀림으로 긴 장대를 만지작거리며 말했다. 그동안 그 밑의 말은 그런 무기를 든 기수가 익숙하지 않아 불안하게 몸을 움직였다."]], [["\"Yes, yes,\"", "“그래요, 그래요.”"], ["said Miss Thorne, standing triumphant as the queen of beauty on an inverted tub which some chance had brought thither from the farmyard.", "어쩌다 농가 마당에서 굴러 나온 통을 뒤집어 놓은 그 위에 미의 여왕처럼 의기양양하게 올라선 손 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Here goes then,\"", "“그럼 갑니다요.”"], ["said Harry as he wheeled his horse round to get the necessary momentum of a sharp gallop.", "해리가 힘차게 달려 나갈 기세를 얻으려고 말을 빙 돌리며 말했다."], ["The quintain post stood right before him, and the square board at which he was to tilt was fairly in his way.", "퀸틴 기둥은 바로 그의 앞에 서 있었고, 그가 창으로 찔러야 할 네모난 판자도 마침 가는 길목에 놓여 있었다."], ["If he hit that duly in the middle, and maintained his pace as he did so, it was calculated that he would be carried out of reach of the flour bag, which, suspended at the other end of the cross-bar on the post, would swing round when the board was struck.", "그 한복판을 제대로 맞히고 그러면서도 속도를 늦추지 않는다면, 기둥의 가로대 반대편 끝에 매달려 있다가 판자가 맞는 순간 휙 돌아오는 밀가루 자루가 닿지 않는 데까지 그가 빠져나가게 되어 있었다."], ["It was also calculated that if the rider did not maintain his pace, he would get a blow from the flour bag just at the back of his head, and bear about him the signs of his awkwardness to the great amusement of the lookers-on.", "반대로 기수가 속도를 늦추면 바로 뒤통수에 밀가루 자루를 한 대 얻어맞고, 구경꾼들의 큰 즐거움을 위하여 제 서투름의 표를 온몸에 뒤집어쓰게 되어 있었다."]], [["Harry Greenacre did not object to being powdered with flour in the service of his mistress and therefore gallantly touched his steed with his spur, having laid his lance in rest to the best of his ability.", "해리 그린에이커는 제 여주인을 섬기다가 밀가루를 뒤집어쓰는 것쯤은 마다하지 않았고, 그리하여 나름대로 최선을 다해 창을 겨드랑이에 끼고서 씩씩하게 말에 박차를 댔다."], ["But his ability in this respect was not great, and his appurtenances probably not very good; consequently, he struck his horse with his pole unintentionally on the side of the head as he started.", "그러나 이 방면의 그의 솜씨는 대단치 않았고 장비도 아마 그리 좋지 못했다. 그 결과 출발하면서 뜻하지 않게 장대로 제 말의 옆머리를 치고 말았다."], ["The animal swerved and shied and galloped off wide of the quintain.", "짐승은 옆으로 비켜서며 놀라 뛰더니 퀸틴을 한참 벗어나 내달렸다."], ["Harry, well-accustomed to manage a horse, but not to do so with a twelve-foot rod on his arm, lowered his right hand to the bridle, and thus the end of the lance came to the ground and got between the legs of the steed.", "말을 다루는 데는 익숙했으나 12피트짜리 장대를 팔에 낀 채 그리하는 데는 익숙하지 않았던 해리는 오른손을 내려 고삐를 잡았고, 그 바람에 창끝이 땅에 닿아 말의 다리 사이로 끼어들었다."], ["Down came rider and steed and staff.", "기수와 말과 장대가 함께 굴렀다."], ["Young Greenacre was thrown some six feet over the horse's head, and poor Miss Thorne almost fell off her tub in a swoon.", "젊은 그린에이커는 말 머리 너머로 6피트쯤 나가떨어졌고, 가엾은 손 양은 하마터면 통에서 굴러떨어져 혼절할 뻔했다."]], [["\"Oh, gracious, he's killed,\"", "“어머나, 저 사람 죽었네.”"], ["shrieked a woman who was near him when he fell.", "그가 떨어질 때 가까이 있던 여자가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["\"The Lord be good to him!", "“주여 저이를 굽어살피소서!"], ["His poor mother, his poor mother!\"", "가엾은 어미, 가엾은 어미!”"], ["said another.", "또 다른 여자가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, drat them dangerous plays all the world over,\"", "“아이고, 세상천지 어디서나 저런 위험한 놀음은 다 몹쓸 것이지.”"], ["said an old crone.", "한 늙은 노파가 말했다."]], [["\"He has broke his neck sure enough, if ever man did,\"", "“목이 부러진 게 틀림없어. 사람이 목을 부러뜨린 적이 있다면 딱 저 꼴이지.”"], ["said a fourth.", "네 번째 사람이 말했다."]], [["Poor Miss Thorne.", "가엾은 손 양."], ["She heard all this and yet did not quite swoon.", "그 여자는 이 말을 다 들으면서도 아주 혼절하지는 않았다."], ["She made her way through the crowd as best she could, sick herself almost to death.", "스스로도 죽을 만큼 속이 메스꺼운 채, 되는대로 사람들 틈을 헤치고 나아갔다."], ["Oh, his mother--his poor mother!", "아, 저 아이의 어미 — 가엾은 어미!"], ["How could she ever forgive herself.", "자신을 무슨 수로 용서한단 말인가."], ["The agony of that moment was terrific.", "그 순간의 고뇌는 끔찍했다."], ["She could hardly get to the place where the poor lad was lying, as three or four men in front were about the horse, which had risen with some difficulty, but at last she found herself close to the young farmer.", "앞쪽에서 서너 사람이 겨우 몸을 일으킨 말을 둘러싸고 있어 가엾은 젊은이가 누워 있는 자리까지 가기도 어려웠으나, 마침내 그 여자는 그 젊은 농부 곁에 이르렀다."]], [["\"Has he marked himself?", "“말이 상했습니까요?"], ["For heaven's sake tell me that: has he marked his knees?\"", "제발 그것만 말씀해 주십시오. 무릎이 까졌습니까요?”"], ["said Harry, slowly rising and rubbing his left shoulder with his right hand and thinking only of his horse's legs.", "해리가 천천히 몸을 일으켜 오른손으로 왼쪽 어깨를 문지르며, 오직 제 말의 다리만 생각하며 말했다."], ["Miss Thorne soon found that he had not broken his neck, nor any of his bones, nor been injured in any essential way.", "손 양은 그가 목도 부러지지 않았고 뼈 하나 부러지지 않았으며 크게 다친 데도 없다는 것을 이내 알게 되었다."], ["But from that time forth she never instigated anyone to ride at a quintain.", "그러나 그때부터 그 여자는 두 번 다시 누구에게도 퀸틴을 향해 말을 몰아 보라고 부추기지 않았다."]], [["Eleanor left Dr. Stanhope as soon as she could do so civilly and went in quest of her father, whom she found on the lawn in company with Mr.", "엘리너는 예의에 어긋나지 않을 만큼만 있다가 곧 스탠호프 박사 곁을 떠나 아버지를 찾아 나섰고, 잔디밭에서 애러빈 씨와 함께 있는 아버지를 만났다."], ["Arabin. She was not sorry to find them together.", "두 사람이 함께 있는 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["She was anxious to disabuse at any rate her father's mind as to this report which had got abroad respecting her, and would have been well pleased to have been able to do the same with regard to Mr. Arabin.", "그 여자는 적어도 아버지의 마음에서만은 자기를 두고 퍼진 그 소문을 씻어 내고 싶었고, 애러빈 씨에 대해서도 그럴 수 있다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다."], ["She put her own through her father's arm, coming up behind his back, and then tendered her hand also to the vicar of St. Ewold's.", "그 여자는 등 뒤로 다가가 제 팔을 아버지의 팔에 끼고, 이어 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제에게도 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"And how did you come?\" said Mr.", "“그래, 너는 어떻게 왔느냐?”"], ["Harding, when the first greeting was over.", "첫인사가 끝나자 하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"The Stanhopes brought me,\"", "“스탠호프 댁에서 데려다주었어요.” 그 여자가 말했다."], ["said she; \"their carriage was obliged to come twice, and has now gone back for the signora.\"", "“마차가 두 번 오가야 해서, 지금은 시뇨라를 데리러 되돌아갔고요.” 이렇게 말하다가 그 여자는 애러빈 씨와 눈이 마주쳤고, 그가 엄한 표정으로 자기를 빤히 바라보고 있음을 알았다."], ["As she spoke she caught Mr.", "그 눈길에 담긴 비난을 그 여자는 대번에 알아들었다."], ["Arabin's eye and saw that he was looking pointedly at her with a severe expression.", "그 눈은 눈이 말할 수 있는 한 가장 또렷하게 이렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["She understood at once the accusation contained in his glance.", "“그래요, 스탠호프 댁 사람들과 함께 왔지요."], ["It said as plainly as an eye could speak, \"Yes, you came with the Stanhopes, but you did so in order that you might be in company with Mr. Slope.\"", "그러나 그렇게 한 것은 슬로프 씨와 한자리에 있기 위해서였고요.”"]], [["\"Our party,\"", "“우리 일행은요,”"], ["said she, still addressing her father, \"consisted of the doctor and Charlotte Stanhope, myself, and Mr.", "그 여자가 여전히 아버지를 향해 말했다. “스탠호프 박사님과 샬럿 스탠호프와 저와 슬로프 씨였어요.”"], ["Slope.\" As she mentioned the last name she felt her father's arm quiver slightly beneath her touch.", "마지막 이름을 입에 올리는 순간, 그 여자는 제 손 밑에서 아버지의 팔이 살짝 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["At the same moment Mr. Arabin turned away from them and, joining his hands behind his back, strolled slowly away by one of the paths.", "바로 그때 애러빈 씨가 그들에게서 몸을 돌리더니, 두 손을 등 뒤로 마주 잡고는 길 하나를 따라 천천히 거닐어 가 버렸다."]], [["\"Papa,\"", "“아버지,”"], ["said she, \"it was impossible to help coming in the same carriage with Mr.", "그 여자가 말했다. “슬로프 씨와 같은 마차를 타는 것은 피할 도리가 없었어요."], ["Slope; it was quite impossible.", "정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요."], ["I had promised to come with them before I dreamt of his coming, and afterwards I could not get out of it without explaining and giving rise to talk.", "그이가 온다는 것은 꿈에도 모른 채 그 댁과 함께 가기로 이미 약속해 둔 터였고, 나중에는 이러쿵저러쿵 설명하고 말거리를 만들지 않고서는 빠져나올 수가 없었고요."], ["You weren't at home, you know.", "아버지는 그때 집에 안 계셨잖아요."], ["I couldn't possibly help it.\"", "정말 어쩔 도리가 없었어요.”"], ["She said all this so quickly that by the time her apology was spoken she was quite out of breath.", "그 여자가 어찌나 빨리 말했던지, 변명이 끝났을 때는 숨이 다 차 있었다."]], [["\"I don't know why you should have wished to help it, my dear,\"", "“네가 왜 그것을 피하고 싶어 했는지 모르겠구나, 얘야.”"], ["said her father.", "아버지가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, Papa, you do.", "“아니에요, 아버지, 아시잖아요."], ["You must know, you do know all the things they said at Plumstead.", "아시는 게 틀림없어요. 플럼스테드에서 그이들이 한 말을 다 아시잖아요."], ["I am sure you do.", "분명히 아세요."], ["You know all the archdeacon said.", "부주교가 한 말을 다 아시잖아요."], ["How unjust he was; and Mr.", "얼마나 부당했는지요. 애러빈 씨도 그랬고요."], ["Arabin too. He's a horrid man, a horrid odious man, but--\"", "그이는 끔찍한 사람이에요, 끔찍하고 역겨운 사람이에요, 하지만—”"]], [["\"Who is an odious man, my dear?", "“누가 역겨운 사람이라는 게냐, 얘야?"], ["Mr. Arabin?\"", "애러빈 씨 말이냐?”"]], [["\"No; but Mr. Slope.", "“아니요, 슬로프 씨요."], ["You know I mean Mr. Slope.", "제가 슬로프 씨를 말하는 거 아시잖아요."], ["He's the most odious man I ever met in my life, and it was most unfortunate my having to come here in the same carriage with him.", "그이는 제가 평생 만난 사람 가운데 가장 역겨운 사람이에요. 하필 그이와 같은 마차를 타고 여기 와야 했다니 정말이지 재수가 없었어요."], ["But how could I help it?\"", "하지만 어쩔 수가 있었나요?”"]], [["A great weight began to move itself off Mr. Harding's mind.", "하딩 씨의 마음에서 큰 짐 하나가 스스로 움직여 내려가기 시작했다."], ["So, after all, the archdeacon with all his wisdom, and Mrs. Grantly with all her tact, and Mr.", "그러니까 결국 그 모든 지혜를 갖춘 부주교도, 그 모든 요령을 갖춘 그랜틀리 부인도, 그 모든 재능을 갖춘 애러빈 씨도 틀렸던 것이다."], ["Arabin with all his talent, were in the wrong. His own child, his Eleanor, the daughter of whom he was so proud, was not to become the wife of a Mr.", "제 자식, 제 엘리너, 그토록 자랑스러워하던 딸이 슬로프 씨 따위의 아내가 되는 일은 없을 것이었다."], ["Slope. He had been about to give his sanction to the marriage, so certified had he been of the fact, and now he learnt that this imputed lover of Eleanor's was at any rate as much disliked by her as by any one of the family.", "그는 그것을 기정사실로 굳게 믿은 나머지 그 혼사에 동의를 내주려던 참이었는데, 이제 와서 알고 보니 엘리너의 연인이라던 그 사람은 적어도 집안의 그 누구 못지않게 딸 자신에게 미움받고 있었다."], ["Mr. Harding, however, was by no means sufficiently a man of the world to conceal the blunder he had made.", "그러나 하딩 씨는 제가 저지른 실수를 감출 만큼 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이 결코 아니었다."], ["He could not pretend that he had entertained no suspicion; he could not make believe that he had never joined the archdeacon in his surmises.", "아무 의심도 품지 않았던 척할 수 없었고, 부주교의 억측에 한 번도 동조한 적이 없다고 시치미를 뗄 수도 없었다."], ["He was greatly surprised, and gratified beyond measure, and he could not help showing that such was the case.", "그는 몹시 놀랐고 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻤으며, 그러하다는 것을 겉으로 드러내지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["\"My darling girl,\"", "“내 사랑하는 딸아,”"], ["said he, \"I am so delighted, so overjoyed.", "그가 말했다. “나는 참으로 기쁘구나, 참으로 반갑구나."], ["My own child; you have taken such a weight off my mind.\"", "내 자식아, 네가 내 마음에서 이렇게 큰 짐을 덜어 주었다.”"]], [["\"But surely, Papa, you didn't think--\"", "“하지만 아버지, 설마 아버지까지 그렇게 생각하신 건—”"]], [["\"I didn't know what to think, my dear.", "“나는 무엇을 생각해야 할지 몰랐단다, 얘야."], ["The archdeacon told me that--\"", "부주교가 나에게 말하기를—”"]], [["\"The archdeacon!\"", "“부주교가요!”"], ["said Eleanor, her face lighting up with passion.", "엘리너가 격정으로 얼굴이 달아오른 채 말했다."], ["\"A man like the archdeacon might, one would think, be better employed than in traducing his sister-in-law and creating bitterness between a father and his daughter!\"", "“부주교쯤 되는 사람이라면 처제를 헐뜯고 아비와 딸 사이에 앙금을 만드는 것보다는 더 나은 일에 힘을 쓸 수도 있었을 텐데요!”"]], [["\"He didn't mean to do that, Eleanor.\"", "“그이는 그럴 뜻으로 그런 것이 아니란다, 엘리너.”"]], [["\"What did he mean then?", "“그럼 무슨 뜻이었대요?"], ["Why did he interfere with me and fill your mind with such falsehood?\"", "왜 제 일에 끼어들어 아버지 마음을 그런 거짓으로 채웠대요?”"]], [["\"Never mind it now, my child; never mind it now.", "“이제 그만 잊자꾸나, 얘야. 이제 그만 잊으렴."], ["We shall all know you better now.\"", "우리 모두 이제부터는 너를 더 잘 알게 될 게다.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, that you should have thought it!", "“아, 아버지, 아버지가 그런 생각을 하시다니요!"], ["That you should have suspected me!\"", "아버지가 저를 의심하시다니요!”"]], [["\"I don't know what you mean by suspicion, Eleanor.", "“의심이라니 무슨 말인지 모르겠구나, 엘리너."], ["There would be nothing disgraceful, you know, nothing wrong in such a marriage.", "그런 혼인이라고 해서 부끄러울 것이야 없지 않으냐, 잘못될 것도 없고 말이다."], ["Nothing that could have justified my interfering as your father.\" And Mr.", "아비로서 끼어들 만한 일도 못 되지.”"], ["Harding would have proceeded in his own defence to make out that Mr. Slope after all was a very good sort of man and a very fitting second husband for a young widow, had he not been interrupted by Eleanor's greater energy.", "그리고 하딩 씨는 제 변명을 이어, 슬로프 씨도 알고 보면 꽤 괜찮은 사람이고 젊은 미망인의 두 번째 남편으로 아주 적합하다는 데까지 늘어놓을 참이었으나, 엘리너의 더 거센 기세에 말이 막혔다."]], [["\"It would be disgraceful,\"", "“부끄러운 일이에요.”"], ["said she; \"it would be wrong; it would be abominable.", "그 여자가 말했다. “잘못된 일이고요, 끔찍한 일이에요."], ["Could I do such a horrid thing, I should expect no one to speak to me.", "제가 그런 흉한 짓을 한다면 아무도 저와 말을 섞지 않는대도 할 말이 없어요."], ["Ugh--\"", "어휴—”"], ["and she shuddered as she thought of the matrimonial torch which her friends had been so ready to light on her behalf. \"I don't wonder at Dr.", "그러면서 그 여자는 벗들이 그토록 선뜻 자기를 위해 밝혀 주려 했던 그 혼인의 횃불을 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["Grantly; I don't wonder at Susan; but, oh, Papa, I do wonder at you.", "“그랜틀리 박사님이야 그렇다 쳐요. 언니야 그렇다 쳐요."], ["How could you, how could you believe it?\"", "하지만 아버지, 아버지는 정말 뜻밖이에요."], ["Poor Eleanor, as she thought of her father's defalcation, could resist her tears no longer, and was forced to cover her face with her handkerchief.", "어떻게 그러실 수가, 어떻게 그걸 믿으실 수가 있어요?” 가엾은 엘리너는 아버지가 저를 저버린 일을 생각하니 더는 눈물을 참을 수 없어, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The place was not very opportune for her grief.", "그 여자의 슬픔에 그 자리는 그리 알맞지 못했다."], ["They were walking through the shrubberies, and there were many people near them.", "그들은 관목 숲 사이를 걷고 있었고 가까이에 사람이 많았다."], ["Poor Mr. Harding stammered out his excuse as best he could, and Eleanor with an effort controlled her tears and returned her handkerchief to her pocket.", "가엾은 하딩 씨는 되는대로 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓았고, 엘리너는 애써 눈물을 다스리고 손수건을 주머니에 도로 넣었다."], ["She did not find it difficult to forgive her father, nor could she altogether refuse to join him in the returning gaiety of spirit to which her present avowal gave rise.", "아버지를 용서하는 것은 어렵지 않았고, 이제 제 고백에서 되살아난 아버지의 유쾌한 기분에 함께 어울리는 것을 아주 뿌리칠 수도 없었다."], ["It was such a load off his heart to think that he should not be called on to welcome Mr. Slope as his son-in-law.", "슬로프 씨를 사위로 맞아들이라는 요구를 받을 일이 없으리라는 생각은 그의 가슴에서 그토록 큰 짐을 덜어 주었다."], ["It was such a relief to him to find that his daughter's feelings and his own were now, as they ever had been, in unison.", "딸의 마음과 제 마음이 이제 언제나 그러했듯 하나라는 것을 알게 된 것은 그토록 큰 안도였다."], ["He had been so unhappy for the last six weeks about this wretched Mr. Slope!", "그는 지난 6주 동안 그 몹쓸 슬로프 씨 때문에 그토록 마음이 편치 못했던 것이다!"], ["He was so indifferent as to the loss of the hospital, so thankful for the recovery of his daughter, that, strong as was the ground for Eleanor's anger, she could not find it in her heart to be long angry with him.", "요양원을 잃은 일에는 그토록 무심하고 딸을 되찾은 일에는 그토록 감사해하니, 엘리너로서는 화를 낼 근거가 아무리 든든하다 해도 도무지 그를 오래 미워할 마음이 들지 않았다."]], [["\"Dear Papa,\"", "“아버지,” 그 여자가 아버지의 팔에 바싹 매달리며 말했다."], ["she said, hanging closely to his arm, \"never suspect me again: promise me that you never will.", "“다시는 저를 의심하지 마세요. 절대 그러지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요."], ["Whatever I do you may be sure I shall tell you first; you may be sure I shall consult you.\"", "제가 무슨 일을 하든 아버지께 가장 먼저 말씀드릴 테니까요. 아버지와 반드시 의논할 테니까요.”"]], [["And Mr. Harding did promise, and owned his sin, and promised again.", "그리하여 하딩 씨는 약속했고, 제 죄를 인정했고, 다시 약속했다."], ["And so, while he promised amendment and she uttered forgiveness, they returned together to the drawing-room windows.", "그렇게 그는 고치겠노라 다짐하고 그 여자는 용서한다고 말하면서, 둘은 함께 응접실 창가로 돌아왔다."]], [["And what had Eleanor meant when she declared that whatever she did, she would tell her father first?", "그런데 엘리너가 무슨 일을 하든 아버지께 가장 먼저 말하겠노라고 선언했을 때, 그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까?"], ["What was she thinking of doing?", "그 여자는 무슨 일을 할 생각이었을까?"]], [["So ended the first act of the melodrama which Eleanor was called on to perform this day at Ullathorne.", "이리하여 엘리너가 이날 울라손에서 연기해야 했던 멜로드라마의 제1막이 끝났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII. The Signora Neroni, the Countess De Courcy, and Mrs. Proudie Meet Each Other at Ullathorne", "# 제37장. 시뇨라 네로니와 드 코시 백작부인과 프라우디 부인이 울라손에서 마주치다"]], [["And now there were new arrivals.", "그리고 이제 새로 도착하는 이들이 있었다."], ["Just as Eleanor reached the drawing-room the signora was being wheeled into it.", "엘리너가 응접실에 다다른 바로 그때 시뇨라가 그 방으로 실려 들어오고 있었다."], ["She had been brought out of the carriage into the dining-room and there placed on a sofa, and was now in the act of entering the other room, by the joint aid of her brother and sister, Mr.", "그녀는 마차에서 들려 나와 식당으로 옮겨졌고 거기서 소파에 뉘어졌다가, 이제 남동생과 언니와 애러빈 씨와 제복 입은 하인 둘의 합동 부축을 받아 다른 방으로 들어서는 참이었다."], ["Arabin, and two servants in livery. She was all in her glory, and looked so pathetically happy, so full of affliction and grace, was so beautiful, so pitiable, and so charming that it was almost impossible not to be glad she was there.", "그녀는 온통 제 광채에 싸여 있었고, 어찌나 애처롭도록 행복해 보이고 어찌나 비탄과 우아함으로 가득하며 어찌나 아름답고 어찌나 가엾고 어찌나 매혹적인지, 그녀가 그 자리에 있는 것을 반기지 않기란 거의 불가능했다."]], [["Miss Thorne was unaffectedly glad to welcome her.", "손 양은 꾸밈없이 그녀를 반겼다."], ["In fact, the signora was a sort of lion; and though there was no drop of the Leohunter blood in Miss Thorne's veins, she nevertheless did like to see attractive people at her house.", "사실 시뇨라는 일종의 명사였다. 손 양의 핏줄에는 명사 사냥꾼의 피가 한 방울도 섞여 있지 않았으나, 그럼에도 그녀는 매력 있는 사람들이 제 집에 오는 것을 보기 좋아하기는 했다."], ["The signora was attractive, and on her first settlement in the dining-room she had whispered two or three soft feminine words into Miss Thorne's ear which, at the moment, had quite touched that lady's heart.", "시뇨라는 매력이 있었고, 식당에 처음 자리를 잡았을 때 손 양의 귀에 부드럽고 여자다운 말 두어 마디를 속삭였는데 그 말이 그 순간 그 노처녀의 마음을 제법 건드려 놓았던 것이다."]], [["\"Oh, Miss Thorne; where is Miss Thorne?\"", "“아, 손 양은요. 손 양은 어디 계신가요?”"], ["she said as soon as her attendants had placed her in her position just before one of the windows, from whence she could see all that was going on upon the lawn.", "시중드는 이들이 잔디밭에서 벌어지는 일을 죄다 내다볼 수 있는 창 하나 바로 앞에 그녀를 자리 잡아 주자마자 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"How am I to thank you for permitting a creature like me to be here?", "“저 같은 것이 여기 있도록 허락해 주신 데 대해 무어라 감사를 드려야 할까요?"], ["But if you knew the pleasure you give me, I am sure you would excuse the trouble I bring with me.\"", "하지만 제가 얼마나 기쁜지 아신다면, 제가 몰고 다니는 이 성가심쯤은 분명 눈감아 주실 거예요.”"], ["And as she spoke she squeezed the spinster's little hand between her own.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 그 노처녀의 작은 손을 제 두 손 사이에 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["\"We are delighted to see you here,\"", "“이렇게 와 주시니 우리는 더없이 반갑소이다.”"], ["said Miss Thorne; \"you give us no trouble at all, and we think it a great favour conferred by you to come and see us--don't we, Wilfred?\"", "손 양이 말했다. “성가시기는커녕 조금도 그렇지 않고, 이렇게 찾아와 주신 것을 우리는 도리어 큰 은혜로 여기고 있소 — 그렇지 않소, 윌프레드?”"]], [["\"A very great favour indeed,\" said Mr.", "“참으로 크나큰 은혜지요.”"], ["Thorne with a gallant bow but of a somewhat less cordial welcome than that conceded by his sister. Mr.", "손 씨가 정중히 허리를 굽히며 말했으나, 그 환대는 누이가 베푼 것보다는 다소 덜 다정했다."], ["Thorne had heard perhaps more of the antecedents of his guest than his sister had done, and had not as yet undergone the power of the signora's charms.", "손 씨는 아마 제 누이보다 이 손님의 내력을 더 많이 들었을 터였고, 아직 시뇨라의 매력에 굴복해 본 일이 없었다."]], [["But while the mother of the last of the Neros was thus in her full splendour, with crowds of people gazing at her and the élite of the company standing round her couch, her glory was paled by the arrival of the Countess De Courcy.", "그러나 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 낳은 어머니가 이렇듯 한창 광채를 뿜으며, 사람들이 무리 지어 그녀를 바라보고 그 자리의 정예 인사들이 그녀의 소파를 둘러싸고 서 있는 동안, 그 영광은 드 코시 백작부인의 도착으로 빛이 바랬다."], ["Miss Thorne had now been waiting three hours for the countess, and could not therefore but show very evident gratification when the arrival at last took place.", "손 양은 그때까지 세 시간째 백작부인을 기다리던 터라, 마침내 그 도착이 이루어지자 아주 뚜렷한 만족을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She and her brother of course went off to welcome the titled grandees, and with them, alas, went many of the signora's admirers.", "그녀와 그 오라비는 물론 작위 있는 귀인들을 맞으러 나갔고, 아, 그들과 함께 시뇨라의 숭배자들도 여럿 떠나갔다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Thorne,\"", "“아, 손 씨,”"], ["said the countess, while in the act of being disrobed of her fur cloaks and rerobed in her gauze shawls, \"what dreadful roads you have; perfectly frightful.\"", "백작부인이 모피 외투를 벗기우고 얇은 사 숄을 다시 걸치는 중에 말했다, “댁의 도로는 어찌 그리 끔찍하오. 아주 소름이 끼칠 지경이더군.”"]], [["It happened that Mr. Thorne was waywarden for the district and, not liking the attack, began to excuse his roads.", "마침 손 씨는 그 구역의 도로 관리 위원이었던지라, 그 공격이 달갑지 않아 제 도로를 변호하기 시작했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, indeed they are,\"", "“아, 그렇고말고, 정말 그렇다오.”"], ["said the countess not minding him in the least; \"perfectly dreadful--are they not, Margaretta?", "백작부인은 그의 말을 조금도 귀담아듣지 않고 말했다. “아주 끔찍했소 — 안 그러냐, 마거리타?"], ["Why, my dear Miss Thorne, we left Courcy Castle just at eleven; it was only just past eleven, was it not, George?", "아니 글쎄, 손 양, 우리는 열한 시에 드 코시 성을 떠났다오. 열한 시가 막 지났을 뿐이었지, 그렇지 않으냐, 조지?"], ["And--\"", "그리고—”"]], [["\"Just past one I think you mean,\"", "“한 시가 막 지났다는 말씀이시겠지요.”"], ["said the Honourable George, turning from the group and eyeing the signora through his glass.", "아너러블 조지가 무리에서 몸을 돌려 외알 안경 너머로 시뇨라를 훑어보며 말했다."], ["The signora gave him back his own, as the saying is, and more with it, so that the young nobleman was forced to avert his glance and drop his glass.", "시뇨라는 흔히들 말하듯 받은 것을 고스란히 되돌려 주었고 거기에 덤까지 얹어 주었으므로, 그 젊은 귀족은 눈길을 돌리고 안경을 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"I say, Thorne,\"", "“이보게, 손,”"], ["whispered he, \"who the deuce is that on the sofa?\"", "그가 속삭였다, “소파에 있는 저 여자는 대체 누군가?”"]], [["\"Dr. Stanhope's daughter,\"", "“스탠호프 박사의 딸이오.”"], ["whispered back Mr. Thorne.", "손 씨가 속삭여 답했다."], ["\"Signora Neroni, she calls herself.\"", "“스스로 시뇨라 네로니라 부르지요.”"]], [["\"Whew--ew--ew!\"", "“휘 — 휴 — 휘!”"], ["whistled the Honourable George.", "아너러블 조지가 휘파람을 불었다."], ["\"The devil she is.", "“저 여자가 바로 그 여자였군."], ["I have heard no end of stories about that filly.", "저 계집에 대해서라면 나도 이야기를 끝도 없이 들었네."], ["You must positively introduce me, Thorne; you positively must.\"", "손, 자네 나를 꼭 소개해 주어야 하네. 꼭 그래 주어야 해.”"]], [["Mr. Thorne, who was respectability itself, did not quite like having a guest about whom the Honourable George De Courcy had heard no end of stories, but he couldn't help himself.", "점잖음 그 자체였던 손 씨로서는 아너러블 조지 드 코시가 끝도 없이 이야기를 들었다는 그런 손님을 집에 두는 것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았으나, 어쩔 도리가 없었다."], ["He merely resolved that before he went to bed he would let his sister know somewhat of the history of the lady she was so willing to welcome.", "그는 다만 잠자리에 들기 전에 누이에게 그토록 기꺼이 맞아들인 그 부인의 내력을 얼마간은 알려 주리라 마음먹었다."], ["The innocence of Miss Thorne at her time of life was perfectly charming, but even innocence may be dangerous.", "그 나이에 이르러서도 손 양이 지닌 순진함은 더없이 사랑스러운 것이었으나, 순진함이라 해도 위험할 수 있는 법이었다."]], [["\"George may say what he likes,\"", "“조지야 무어라 하든 상관없소.”"], ["continued the countess, urging her excuses to Miss Thorne; \"I am sure we were past the castle gate before twelve--weren't we, Margaretta?\"", "백작부인이 손 양에게 변명을 늘어놓으며 말을 이었다. “우리가 열두 시 전에 성문을 지난 것만은 분명하다오 — 그렇지 않으냐, 마거리타?”"]], [["\"Upon my word I don't know,\"", "“글쎄요, 저는 정말 모르겠어요.”"], ["said the Lady Margaretta, \"for I was half-asleep.", "레이디 마거리타가 말했다. “반쯤 잠들어 있었으니까요."], ["But I do know that I was called some time in the middle of the night and was dressing myself before daylight.\"", "하지만 한밤중 어느 무렵엔가 누가 저를 깨웠고 제가 동트기 전에 옷을 입고 있었던 것만은 분명히 알아요.”"]], [["Wise people, when they are in the wrong, always put themselves right by finding fault with the people against whom they have sinned.", "지혜로운 사람들은 자기가 그른 편에 섰을 때면 언제나 자기가 잘못을 저지른 그 상대의 흠을 잡아 스스로를 옳은 편에 세운다."], ["Lady De Courcy was a wise woman, and therefore, having treated Miss Thorne very badly by staying away till three o'clock, she assumed the offensive and attacked Mr. Thorne's roads.", "레이디 드 코시는 지혜로운 여인이었으므로, 세 시까지 나타나지 않아 손 양을 몹시 홀대해 놓고는 도리어 공세로 돌아서서 손 씨의 도로를 공격했다."], ["Her daughter, not less wise, attacked Miss Thorne's early hours.", "못지않게 지혜로운 그 딸은 손 양의 이른 시각을 공격했다."], ["The art of doing this is among the most precious of those usually cultivated by persons who know how to live.", "이런 재주야말로 사는 법을 아는 이들이 흔히 갈고닦는 기술 가운데 가장 값진 축에 든다."], ["There is no withstanding it.", "이것을 당해 낼 도리는 없다."], ["Who can go systematically to work and, having done battle with the primary accusation and settled that, then bring forward a countercharge and support that also?", "누가 조목조목 따져 들어가 첫 번째 고발과 맞싸워 그것을 매듭짓고, 그런 다음 다시 맞고발을 꺼내 그것까지 뒷받침할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Life is not long enough for such labours.", "인생은 그런 수고를 감당하기에 충분히 길지 않다."], ["A man in the right relies easily on his rectitude and therefore goes about unarmed.", "옳은 편에 선 사람은 제 올바름에 태평히 기대는 까닭에 무장하지 않은 채 다닌다."], ["His very strength is his weakness.", "바로 그 힘이 그의 약점이다."], ["A man in the wrong knows that he must look to his weapons; his very weakness is his strength.", "그른 편에 선 사람은 제 무기를 챙겨야 함을 안다. 바로 그 약함이 그의 힘이다."], ["The one is never prepared for combat, the other is always ready.", "한쪽은 결코 싸울 채비가 되어 있지 않고, 다른 쪽은 언제나 준비가 되어 있다."], ["Therefore it is that in this world the man that is in the wrong almost invariably conquers the man that is in the right, and invariably despises him.", "그러므로 이 세상에서는 그른 편에 선 사람이 거의 어김없이 옳은 편에 선 사람을 이기며, 또 어김없이 그를 경멸한다."]], [["A man must be an idiot or else an angel who, after the age of forty, shall attempt to be just to his neighbours.", "마흔을 넘긴 뒤에도 제 이웃에게 공정하기를 꾀하는 사람은 바보이거나 아니면 천사임에 틀림없다."], ["Many like the Lady Margaretta have learnt their lesson at a much earlier age.", "레이디 마거리타 같은 이들은 그보다 훨씬 이른 나이에 그 교훈을 익혀 두었다."], ["But this of course depends on the school in which they have been taught.", "물론 이는 그들이 어떤 학교에서 배웠느냐에 달린 문제이기는 하다."]], [["Poor Miss Thorne was altogether overcome.", "가엾은 손 양은 완전히 압도당하고 말았다."], ["She knew very well that she had been ill-treated, and yet she found herself making apologies to Lady De Courcy.", "자기가 홀대를 받았다는 것을 그녀는 아주 잘 알고 있었으면서도, 어느새 레이디 드 코시에게 사과를 하고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["To do her ladyship justice, she received them very graciously, and allowed herself, with her train of daughters, to be led towards the lawn.", "백작부인의 명예를 위해 말해 두자면, 그 사과를 그녀는 아주 너그럽게 받아들였고, 딸들을 줄줄이 거느린 채 잔디밭 쪽으로 인도되는 데 몸을 맡겼다."]], [["There were two windows in the drawing-room wide open for the countess to pass through, but she saw that there was a woman on a sofa, at the third window, and that that woman had, as it were, a following attached to her.", "응접실에는 백작부인이 지나갈 수 있도록 창이 둘 활짝 열려 있었으나, 그녀는 세 번째 창가 소파에 한 여자가 있고 그 여자에게 이를테면 한 무리의 추종자가 붙어 있다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["Her ladyship therefore determined to investigate the woman.", "그리하여 백작부인은 그 여자를 조사해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["The De Courcy's were hereditarily shortsighted, and had been so for thirty centuries at least.", "드 코시 집안 사람들은 대대로 근시였고, 적어도 서른 세기 동안 그러했다."], ["So Lady De Courcy, who when she entered the family had adopted the family habits, did as her son had done before her and, taking her glass to investigate the Signora Neroni, pressed in among the gentlemen who surrounded the couch, and bowed slightly to those whom she chose to honour by her acquaintance.", "그러니 그 집안에 들어오면서 그 집안의 습속을 받아들인 레이디 드 코시는 앞서 아들이 한 그대로, 시뇨라 네로니를 살펴보려고 외알 안경을 들고서 소파를 둘러싼 신사들 틈으로 밀고 들어갔고, 알은체를 해 주기로 마음먹은 이들에게는 가볍게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["In order to get to the window she had to pass close to the front of the couch, and as she did so she stared hard at the occupant.", "창가에 이르려면 소파 앞을 바짝 지나야 했고, 지나가면서 그녀는 소파에 누운 이를 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다."], ["The occupant, in return, stared hard at the countess.", "누운 이도 답례로 백작부인을 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다."], ["The countess, who, since her countess-ship commenced, had been accustomed to see all eyes not royal, ducal, or marquesal fall before her own, paused as she went on, raised her eyebrows, and stared even harder than before.", "백작부인이 된 이래로 왕가나 공작가나 후작가에 속하지 않은 눈은 모조리 제 눈앞에서 내리깔리는 것을 보아 온 백작부인은, 가던 걸음을 멈추고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 아까보다 한층 더 뚫어져라 노려보았다."], ["But she had now to do with one who cared little for countesses.", "그러나 이번에 그녀가 상대한 것은 백작부인 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 사람이었다."], ["It was, one may say, impossible for mortal man or woman to abash Madeline Neroni.", "마들린 네로니를 무안하게 만드는 일은, 이렇게 말해도 좋다면, 인간으로 난 남자든 여자든 해낼 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["She opened her large, bright, lustrous eyes wider and wider, till she seemed to be all eyes.", "그녀는 크고 밝고 윤이 흐르는 눈을 점점 더 크게 뜨더니 마침내 온몸이 눈뿐인 듯이 보였다."], ["She gazed up into the lady's face, not as though she did it with an effort, but as if she delighted in doing it.", "그녀는 애를 써서 그리하는 것이 아니라 그리하는 것이 즐거워 못 견디겠다는 듯이 그 귀부인의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["She used no glass to assist her effrontery, and needed none.", "그녀는 제 뻔뻔함을 거들 안경 따위는 쓰지 않았고 쓸 필요도 없었다."], ["The faintest possible smile of derision played round her mouth, and her nostrils were slightly dilated, as if in sure anticipation of her triumph.", "더할 나위 없이 희미한 조롱의 미소가 입가에 어렸고, 승리를 확신하기라도 하는 듯 콧구멍이 살짝 벌어졌다. 그리고 그 승리는 확실했다."], ["And it was sure. The Countess De Courcy, in spite of her thirty centuries and De Courcy Castle, and the fact that Lord De Courcy was grand master of the ponies to the Prince of Wales, had not a chance with her.", "드 코시 백작부인은 서른 세기와 드 코시 성을 등에 업고, 드 코시 경이 웨일스 공의 조랑말 관리 총책이라는 사실까지 있었음에도, 그녀 앞에서는 승산이 없었다."], ["At first the little circlet of gold wavered in the countess's hand, then the hand shook, then the circlet fell, the countess's head tossed itself into the air, and the countess's feet shambled out to the lawn.", "처음에는 백작부인의 손에 들린 그 작은 금테가 흔들렸고, 다음에는 손이 떨렸고, 다음에는 그 금테가 떨어졌으며, 백작부인의 머리는 홱 치켜들려 허공을 향했고, 백작부인의 발은 잔디밭으로 허둥허둥 걸어 나갔다."], ["She did not, however, go so fast but what she heard the signora's voice, asking:", "그러나 그렇게까지 빨리 나가지는 못한 까닭에 그녀는 시뇨라의 목소리가 이렇게 묻는 것을 듣고야 말았다."]], [["\"Who on earth is that woman, Mr. Slope?\"", "“대체 저 여자는 누구예요, 슬로프 씨?”"]], [["\"That is Lady De Courcy.\"", "“드 코시 백작부인입니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, ah.", "“아, 그렇군요."], ["I might have supposed so.", "그럴 줄 알았어요."], ["Ha, ha, ha.", "하, 하, 하."], ["Well, that's as good as a play.\"", "그것참 연극 한 편만큼이나 볼만하군요.”"]], [["It was as good as a play to any there who had eyes to observe it and wit to comment on what they observed.", "그리고 그것은 볼 눈이 있고 본 것을 두고 한마디 할 재치가 있는 이라면 누구에게나 연극 한 편만큼이나 볼만한 일이었다."]], [["But the Lady De Courcy soon found a congenial spirit on the lawn. There she encountered Mrs.", "그러나 레이디 드 코시는 곧 잔디밭에서 마음 맞는 영혼을 하나 찾아냈다."], ["Proudie, and as Mrs. Proudie was not only the wife of a bishop but was also the cousin of an earl, Lady De Courcy considered her to be the fittest companion she was likely to meet in that assemblage.", "거기서 그녀는 프라우디 부인과 마주쳤는데, 프라우디 부인은 주교의 아내일 뿐 아니라 어느 백작의 사촌이기도 했으므로, 레이디 드 코시는 이 모임에서 만날 법한 이들 가운데 가장 어울리는 동반자가 그녀라고 여겼다."], ["They were accordingly delighted to see each other.", "그리하여 두 사람은 서로를 보고 기뻐했다."], ["Mrs. Proudie by no means despised a countess, and as this countess lived in the county and within a sort of extensive visiting distance of Barchester, she was glad to have this opportunity of ingratiating herself.", "프라우디 부인은 백작부인을 결코 하찮게 여기지 않았고, 이 백작부인이 같은 주에 살며 바체스터에서 왕래할 만한 거리 안에 있었으므로 이 기회에 환심을 사 둘 수 있게 된 것을 반겼다."]], [["\"My dear Lady De Courcy, I am so delighted,\"", "“드 코시 백작부인, 이렇게 뵈니 정말 반갑습니다.”"], ["said she, looking as little grim as it was in her nature to do.", "그녀는 제 천성이 허락하는 한 최대로 험상궂지 않은 얼굴을 하고 말했다."], ["\"I hardly expected to see you here.", "“여기서 뵈리라고는 생각지도 못했습니다."], ["It is such a distance, and then, you know, such a crowd.\"", "거리도 이렇게 멀고, 게다가 아시다시피 이렇게 사람이 많으니 말입니다.”"]], [["\"And such roads, Mrs. Proudie!", "“게다가 그 도로라니, 프라우디 부인!"], ["I really wonder how the people ever get about.", "이 고장 사람들이 대체 어떻게 돌아다니는지 참으로 모르겠소."], ["But I don't suppose they ever do.\"", "하기야 아예 돌아다니지 않는 모양이지.”"]], [["\"Well, I really don't know, but I suppose not.", "“글쎄요, 저도 정말 모르겠습니다만, 아마 다니지 않겠지요."], ["The Thornes don't, I know,\"", "손 남매가 다니지 않는다는 것만은 제가 압니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Very nice person, Miss Thorne, isn't she?\"", "“손 양은 아주 좋은 분이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["\"Oh, delightful, and so queer; I've known her these twenty years.", "“아, 더없이 유쾌한 사람이지, 게다가 어찌나 별난지. 나는 그이와 이십 년째 알고 지낸다오."], ["A great pet of mine is dear Miss Thorne.", "저 손 양으로 말하면 내가 아주 아끼는 사람이오."], ["She is so very strange, you know.", "알다시피 참으로 이상한 사람이지."], ["She always makes me think of the Eskimos and the Indians.", "나는 그이를 볼 때마다 늘 에스키모와 인디언이 떠오른다니까."], ["Isn't her dress quite delightful?\"", "저 옷차림이 정말 유쾌하지 않소?”"]], [["\"Delightful,\"", "“유쾌하고말고요.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I wonder now whether she paints.", "“저분이 얼굴에 분칠을 하는지 어떤지 저는 궁금하군요."], ["Did you ever see such colour?\"", "저런 낯빛을 보신 일이 있으신가요?”"]], [["\"Oh, of course,\"", "“아, 물론이지.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy; \"that is, I have no doubt she does.", "레이디 드 코시가 말했다. “아니, 내 말은 틀림없이 하리라는 것이오."], ["But, Mrs. Proudie, who is that woman on the sofa by the window?", "그런데 프라우디 부인, 저기 창가 소파에 있는 저 여자는 누구요?"], ["Just step this way and you'll see her, there--\"", "이쪽으로 한 걸음만 와 보시오, 그럼 보일 게요, 저기—”"], ["and the countess led her to a spot where she could plainly see the signora's well-remembered face and figure.", "그러고는 백작부인이 그녀를, 시뇨라의 잊으려야 잊히지 않는 얼굴과 자태가 훤히 보이는 자리로 데려갔다."]], [["She did not however do so without being equally well seen by the signora.", "그러나 그렇게 하면서 그녀들 역시 시뇨라의 눈에 똑같이 훤히 띄지 않을 도리는 없었다."], ["\"Look, look,\" said that lady to Mr.", "“보세요, 저것 좀 보세요.”"], ["Slope, who was still standing near to her; \"see the high spiritualities and temporalities of the land in league together, and all against poor me.", "그 부인이 아직 제 곁에 서 있던 슬로프 씨에게 말했다. “이 나라의 지엄한 영적 권세와 세속 권세가 한통속이 되어 죄다 가엾은 저 하나를 겨누고 있는 꼴을 좀 보세요."], ["I'll wager my bracelet, Mr.", "슬로프 씨, 제 팔찌를 걸고 당신의 다음 설교와 내기를 해도 좋아요."], ["Slope, against your next sermon that they've taken up their position there on purpose to pull me to pieces.", "저 두 사람은 저를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려고 일부러 저기다 진을 친 거예요. 뭐, 저야 싸움터로 달려나갈 수는 없지요."], ["Well, I can't rush to the combat, but I know how to protect myself if the enemy come near me.\"", "하지만 적이 제 가까이 오기만 한다면 제 몸 지키는 법쯤은 알고 있답니다.”"]], [["But the enemy knew better.", "그러나 적은 그보다는 영리했다."], ["They could gain nothing by contact with the Signora Neroni, and they could abuse her as they pleased at a distance from her on the lawn.", "시뇨라 네로니와 맞붙어서는 얻을 것이 없었고, 잔디밭에서 그녀와 떨어져 있는 한 마음껏 그녀를 헐뜯을 수 있었다."]], [["\"She's that horrid Italian woman, Lady De Courcy; you must have heard of her.\"", "“저 여자가 바로 그 끔찍한 이탈리아 여자입니다, 드 코시 백작부인. 백작부인께서도 이야기를 들으셨을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"What Italian woman?\"", "“이탈리아 여자라니, 무슨 이탈리아 여자 말이오?”"], ["said her ladyship, quite alive to the coming story.", "백작부인이 다가올 이야기에 대뜸 생기가 돌아 말했다."], ["\"I don't think I've heard of any Italian woman coming into the country.", "“이 고장에 이탈리아 여자가 들어왔다는 소리는 들은 적이 없는 것 같은데."], ["She doesn't look Italian, either.\"", "게다가 저 여자는 이탈리아 사람으로 보이지도 않소.”"]], [["\"Oh, you must have heard of her,\"", "“아, 분명 들으셨을 겁니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["\"No, she's not absolutely Italian. She is Dr.", "“아니요, 저 여자가 순전한 이탈리아 사람인 것은 아니지요."], ["Stanhope's daughter--Dr. Stanhope the prebendary--and she calls herself the Signora Neroni.\"", "스탠호프 박사의 딸입니다 — 참사회원인 스탠호프 박사 말입니다 — 그러면서 스스로를 시뇨라 네로니라 부른답니다.”"]], [["\"Oh-h-h-h!\"", "“오오오오!”"], ["exclaimed the countess.", "백작부인이 외쳤다."]], [["\"I was sure you had heard of her,\"", "“들으셨을 줄 알았습니다.”"], ["continued Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["\"I don't know anything about her husband.", "“그 남편이라는 자에 대해서는 저도 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["They do say that some man named Neroni is still alive.", "네로니라는 이름의 사내가 아직 살아 있다는 말들은 하더군요."], ["I believe she did marry such a man abroad, but I do not at all know who or what he was.\"", "저 여자가 외국에서 그런 사내와 혼인한 것은 사실인 모양인데, 그자가 대체 누구인지 무엇 하는 자였는지는 저도 도무지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh-h-h-h!\"", "“오오오오!”"], ["exclaimed the countess, shaking her head with much intelligence, as every additional \"h\"", "백작부인이 외치며, 입술에서 그 소리가 한 자락씩 더 떨어질 때마다 모든 것을 다 알겠다는 듯 고개를 주억거렸다."], ["fell from her lips.", "“이제야 죄다 알겠소."], ["\"I know all about it now. I have heard George mention her.", "조지가 저 여자 이야기를 하는 것을 내가 들은 적이 있소."], ["George knows all about her.", "조지는 저 여자에 대해 죄다 알고 있지."], ["George heard about her in Rome.\"", "조지가 로마에서 저 여자 이야기를 들었다오.”"]], [["\"She's an abominable woman, at any rate,\"", "“어쨌든 참으로 가증스러운 여자입니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Insufferable,\"", "“도저히 못 견딜 노릇이지.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"She made her way into the palace once, before I knew anything about her, and I cannot tell you how dreadfully indecent her conduct was.\"", "“제가 저 여자에 대해 아무것도 모르던 시절에 한번은 저 여자가 주교관에 발을 들인 적이 있는데, 그때 그 행실이 얼마나 끔찍하게 망측했는지는 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Was it?\"", "“그랬소?”"], ["said the delighted countess.", "신이 난 백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Insufferable,\"", "“도저히 못 견딜 노릇이었지요.”"], ["said the prelatess.", "주교 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But why does she lie on a sofa?\"", "“그런데 저 여자는 어째서 소파에 누워 있는 게요?”"], ["asked Lady De Courcy.", "레이디 드 코시가 물었다."]], [["\"She has only one leg,\"", "“다리가 하나뿐이니까요.”"], ["replied Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 대답했다."]], [["\"Only one leg!\"", "“다리가 하나뿐이라니!”"], ["said Lady De Courcy, who felt to a certain degree dissatisfied that the signora was thus incapacitated.", "레이디 드 코시가 말했는데, 시뇨라가 그렇게 성치 못한 몸이라는 데에 그녀는 어느 정도 불만스러운 기분이 들었다."], ["\"Was she born so?\"", "“나면서부터 그랬소?”"]], [["\"Oh, no,\" said Mrs.", "“아, 아니지요.”"], ["Proudie--and her ladyship felt some what recomforted by the assurance--\"she had two.", "프라우디 부인이 말했고 — 그 장담에 백작부인은 다소 마음이 놓였다 — “다리가 둘 다 있었답니다."], ["But that Signor Neroni beat her, I believe, till she was obliged to have one amputated.", "그런데 그 네로니라는 자가 저 여자를 하도 때려서 결국 한쪽을 잘라 내야 했다지 뭡니까."], ["At any rate, she entirely lost the use of it.\"", "어쨌든 저 여자는 그 다리를 아주 못 쓰게 되고 말았지요.”"]], [["\"Unfortunate creature!\"", "“가엾은 것 같으니!”"], ["said the countess, who herself knew something of matrimonial trials.", "백작부인이 말했으니, 그녀 자신도 부부 사이의 시련이라면 얼마간 아는 바가 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"Yes,\" said Mrs.", "“그렇지요.”"], ["Proudie, \"one would pity her in spite of her past bad conduct, if she now knew how to behave herself.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “저 여자가 지금이라도 처신할 줄만 안다면 지난날의 못된 행실에도 불구하고 딱하게 여겨 줄 만도 하겠지요."], ["But she does not.", "그러나 저 여자는 그러지를 않습니다."], ["She is the most insolent creature I ever put my eyes on.\"", "제 눈으로 본 것 가운데 가장 뻔뻔한 것이 저 여자입니다.”"]], [["\"Indeed she is,\"", "“정말 그렇구려.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy.", "레이디 드 코시가 말했다."]], [["\"And her conduct with men is so abominable that she is not fit to be admitted into any lady's drawing-room.\"", "“게다가 사내들을 대하는 행실이 어찌나 가증스러운지, 점잖은 부인네의 응접실에 들일 만한 여자가 못 됩니다.”"]], [["\"Dear me!\"", "“저런 저런!”"], ["said the countess, becoming again excited, happy and merciless.", "백작부인이 말했는데, 그녀는 다시금 흥이 올라 행복하고도 무자비해져 있었다."]], [["\"You saw that man standing near her--the clergyman with the red hair?\"", "“저 여자 곁에 서 있는 저 남자를 보셨습니까 — 머리가 붉은 저 성직자 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, yes.\"", "“보았지, 보았고말고.”"]], [["\"She has absolutely ruined that man.", "“저 여자가 저 사람을 아주 망쳐 놓았습니다."], ["The bishop--or I should rather take the blame on myself, for it was I--I brought him down from London to Barchester.", "주교가 — 아니, 그 탓은 차라리 제가 저 자신에게 돌려야겠군요, 그게 저였으니까요 — 제가 저 사람을 런던에서 바체스터로 데려왔습니다."], ["He is a tolerable preacher, an active young man, and I therefore introduced him to the bishop.", "설교는 그런대로 하는 편이고 부지런한 젊은이라 제가 주교에게 소개했지요."], ["That woman, Lady De Courcy, has got hold of him and has so disgraced him that I am forced to require that he shall leave the palace; and I doubt very much whether he won't lose his gown!\"", "그런데 드 코시 백작부인, 저 여자가 저 사람을 손아귀에 넣고는 어찌나 망신을 시켜 놓았는지, 저로서는 저 사람더러 주교관을 떠나라고 요구하지 않을 수 없게 되었습니다. 게다가 저 사람이 성직 예복을 벗게 되지나 않을지 저는 몹시 의심스럽습니다!”"]], [["\"Why, what an idiot the man must be!\"", "“아니, 그 사람은 대체 얼마나 얼간이란 말이오!”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"You don't know the intriguing villainy of that woman,\"", "“저 여자가 얼마나 음흉하고 간악한지 백작부인께서는 모르십니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, remembering her torn flounces.", "프라우디 부인이 찢겨 나간 제 옷자락 주름 장식을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["\"But you say she has only got one leg!\"", "“하지만 다리가 하나뿐이라 하지 않았소!”"]], [["\"She is as full of mischief as tho' she had ten.", "“다리가 열 개 있다 한들 저보다 더 말썽을 부리지는 못할 겁니다."], ["Look at her eyes, Lady De Courcy.", "저 눈을 좀 보십시오, 드 코시 백작부인."], ["Did you ever see such eyes in a decent woman's head?\"", "점잖은 여자의 얼굴에서 저런 눈을 보신 적이 있으십니까?”"]], [["\"Indeed, I never did, Mrs. Proudie.\"", "“정말이지 나도 본 적이 없소, 프라우디 부인.”"]], [["\"And her effrontery, and her voice!", "“게다가 저 뻔뻔함이며 저 목소리라니요!"], ["I quite pity her poor father, who is really a good sort of man.\"", "저는 저 여자의 가엾은 아비가 참으로 딱합니다. 그이는 정말 좋은 축에 드는 사람이거든요.”"]], [["\"Dr. Stanhope, isn't he?\"", "“스탠호프 박사라 하였소?”"]], [["\"Yes, Dr. Stanhope.", "“그렇습니다, 스탠호프 박사지요."], ["He is one of our prebendaries--a good, quiet sort of man himself.", "우리 참사회원 가운데 한 사람인데, 사람 자체는 착하고 조용한 편입니다."], ["But I am surprised that he should let his daughter conduct herself as she does.\"", "다만 제 딸이 저렇게 처신하도록 내버려 둔다는 것이 저로서는 놀라울 따름입니다.”"]], [["\"I suppose he can't help it,\"", "“어쩔 도리가 없나 보구려.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But a clergyman, you know, Lady De Courcy!", "“하지만 성직자가 아닙니까, 드 코시 백작부인!"], ["He should at any rate prevent her from exhibiting in public, if he cannot induce her to behave at home.", "집 안에서 얌전히 굴게 만들지는 못한다 하더라도, 적어도 사람들 앞에 저렇게 나서서 구경거리가 되는 것만은 막아야지요."], ["But he is to be pitied.", "그래도 딱하기는 딱한 사람입니다."], ["I believe he has a desperate life of it with the lot of them.", "저 식구들을 죄다 데리고 사느라 아주 절망스러운 나날을 보내는 모양이니까요."], ["That apish-looking man there, with the long beard and the loose trousers--he is the woman's brother.", "저기 저 원숭이처럼 생긴 사내, 수염이 길고 헐렁한 바지를 입은 저자가 저 여자의 오라비입니다."], ["He is nearly as bad as she is.", "저자도 누이 못지않게 못됐지요."], ["They are both of them infidels.\"", "둘 다 신앙이라고는 없는 자들입니다.”"]], [["\"Infidels!\"", "“신앙이 없다니!”"], ["said Lady De Courcy, \"and their father a prebendary!\"", "레이디 드 코시가 말했다. “그런데 그 아비가 참사회원이라니!”"]], [["\"Yes, and likely to be the new dean, too,\"", "“그렇습니다, 게다가 새 수석 사제가 될 가망까지 있는 사람이지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, poor dear Dr. Trefoil!\"", "“아, 그렇지, 가엾은 트리폴 박사!”"], ["said the countess, who had once in her life spoken to that gentleman.", "평생에 딱 한 번 그 신사와 말을 나누어 본 일이 있는 백작부인이 말했다."], ["\"I was so distressed to hear it, Mrs. Proudie.", "“그 소식을 듣고 나도 무척 마음이 아팠소, 프라우디 부인."], ["And so Dr. Stanhope is to be the new dean!", "그래서 스탠호프 박사가 새 수석 사제가 된다는 말이구려!"], ["He comes of an excellent family, and I wish him success in spite of his daughter.", "훌륭한 집안 출신이니, 그 딸이 저렇긴 해도 나는 그이가 잘되기를 바라오."], ["Perhaps, Mrs. Proudie, when he is dean, they'll be better able to see the error of their ways.\"", "프라우디 부인, 어쩌면 그이가 수석 사제가 되고 나면 저 사람들도 제 잘못된 길을 좀 더 잘 깨닫게 될지 모르지 않겠소.”"]], [["To this Mrs. Proudie said nothing.", "이 말에 프라우디 부인은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["Her dislike of the Signora Neroni was too deep to admit of her even hoping that that lady should see the error of her ways.", "시뇨라 네로니를 향한 그녀의 혐오는 너무나 깊어서, 그 부인이 제 잘못된 길을 깨닫게 되기를 바라는 것조차 그녀에게는 허락되지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Proudie looked on the signora as one of the lost--one of those beyond the reach of Christian charity--and was therefore able to enjoy the luxury of hating her without the drawback of wishing her eventually well out of her sins.", "프라우디 부인은 시뇨라를 이미 버림받은 자 가운데 하나로, 그리스도인의 사랑이 미치지 못하는 곳에 있는 자로 여겼고, 그러므로 언젠가 그 죄에서 벗어나 잘되기를 빌어 주어야 하는 부담 없이 그녀를 미워하는 호사를 마음껏 누릴 수 있었다."]], [["Any further conversation between these congenial souls was prevented by the advent of Mr. Thorne, who came to lead the countess to the tent.", "이 뜻이 맞는 두 영혼 사이의 대화가 더 이어지지 못한 것은 손 씨가 나타난 탓이었으니, 그는 백작부인을 천막으로 안내하러 온 것이었다."], ["Indeed, he had been desired to do so some ten minutes since, but he had been delayed in the drawing-room by the signora.", "실은 십 분쯤 전에 이미 그리하도록 부탁을 받았으나 응접실에서 시뇨라에게 붙들려 늦어졌다."], ["She had contrived to detain him, to get him near to her sofa, and at last to make him seat himself on a chair close to her beautiful arm.", "그녀는 용케 그를 붙들어 두었고, 제 소파 가까이로 끌어들였으며, 마침내는 제 아름다운 팔 바로 곁의 의자에 그를 앉히기에 이르렀다."], ["The fish took the bait, was hooked, and caught, and landed.", "물고기는 미끼를 물었고, 바늘에 꿰였고, 걸려들었고, 뭍으로 끌어올려졌다."], ["Within that ten minutes he had heard the whole of the signora's history in such strains as she chose to use in telling it.", "그 십 분 사이에 그는 시뇨라의 내력을 그녀가 골라 쓰는 어조 그대로 죄다 들었다."], ["He learnt from the lady's own lips the whole of that mysterious tale to which the Honourable George had merely alluded.", "아너러블 조지가 그저 넌지시 비추기만 했던 그 알 수 없는 이야기를 그는 당사자의 입에서 남김없이 들었다."], ["He discovered that the beautiful creature lying before him had been more sinned against than sinning.", "그는 자기 앞에 누워 있는 이 아름다운 사람이 죄를 지었다기보다는 죄를 당한 쪽이라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["She had owned to him that she had been weak, confiding, and indifferent to the world's opinion, and that she had therefore been ill-used, deceived, and evil spoken of.", "그녀는 자기가 약했고 남을 잘 믿었고 세상의 평판에 무심했으며, 그런 까닭에 학대당하고 속임을 당하고 나쁜 소문에 시달렸노라고 그에게 털어놓았다."], ["She had spoken to him of her mutilated limb, her youth destroyed in fullest bloom, her beauty robbed of its every charm, her life blighted, her hopes withered, and as she did so a tear dropped from her eye to her cheek.", "그녀는 잘려 나간 제 다리에 대해, 한창 꽃필 때에 무너진 제 젊음에 대해, 온갖 매력을 빼앗긴 제 아름다움에 대해, 시들어 버린 제 삶과 말라 버린 제 희망에 대해 그에게 이야기했고, 그러는 사이 눈에서 눈물 한 방울이 뺨으로 굴러떨어졌다."], ["She had told him of these things and asked for his sympathy.", "그녀는 그에게 이런 것들을 이야기하고 나서 그의 동정을 구했다."]], [["What could a good-natured, genial, Anglo-Saxon Squire Thorne do but promise to sympathize with her? Mr.", "마음씨 좋고 유쾌한 앵글로색슨 지주 손 씨로서 그녀에게 동정을 약속하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Thorne did promise to sympathize; promised also to come and see the last of the Neros, to hear more of those fearful Roman days, of those light and innocent but dangerous hours which flitted by so fast on the shores of Como, and to make himself the confidant of the signora's sorrows.", "손 씨는 동정하겠노라 약속했다. 아울러 네로 가문의 마지막 후손을 보러 가겠노라고, 그 무시무시하던 로마 시절 이야기를, 코메 호숫가에서 그토록 빨리 스쳐 지나간 그 가볍고 순진하나 위태롭던 시간들의 이야기를 더 듣겠노라고, 그리하여 시뇨라의 슬픔을 들어 주는 사람이 되겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["We need hardly say that he dropped all idea of warning his sister against the dangerous lady.", "그가 그 위험한 부인에 대해 누이에게 경고하겠다는 생각을 죄다 버렸다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라."], ["He had been mistaken--never so much mistaken in his life.", "그는 잘못 알고 있었던 것이다 — 평생에 그토록 잘못 안 적이 없을 만큼."], ["He had always regarded that Honourable George as a coarse, brutal-minded young man; now he was more convinced than ever that he was so.", "그는 늘 저 아너러블 조지를 거칠고 심성이 야비한 젊은이라 여겨 왔는데, 이제는 그것이 사실임을 그 어느 때보다 굳게 확신하게 되었다."], ["It was by such men as the Honourable George that the reputations of such women as Madeline Neroni were imperilled and damaged.", "마들린 네로니 같은 여인의 명예를 위태롭게 하고 더럽히는 것이 바로 아너러블 조지 같은 사내들이었다. 그는 그 부인의 집으로 찾아가 볼 참이었다."], ["He would go and see the lady in her own house; he was fully sure in his own mind of the soundness of his own judgement; if he found her, as he believed he should do, an injured, well-disposed, warm-hearted woman, he would get his sister Monica to invite her out to Ullathorne.", "제 판단이 옳다는 것을 그는 마음속으로 온전히 확신하고 있었다. 만일 자기가 믿는 대로 그녀가 상처 입고 마음씨 곧고 정 많은 여인임을 확인하게 된다면, 그는 누이 모니카에게 부탁하여 그녀를 울라손으로 초대하게 할 작정이었다."]], [["\"No,\"", "“안 돼요.”"], ["said she, as at her instance he got up to leave her and declared that he himself would attend upon her wants; \"no, no, my friend; I positively put a veto upon your doing so.", "그가 그녀의 권유에 따라 자리에서 일어나며 자기가 직접 그녀의 시중을 들겠노라 선언하자 그녀가 말했다. “안 돼요, 안 됩니다, 나의 벗이여."], ["What, in your own house, with an assemblage round you such as there is here!", "저는 그러시는 데 단호히 거부권을 놓겠어요. 아니, 당신 댁에서, 이렇게 사람들이 잔뜩 모여 있는 자리에서 그러시겠다고요!"], ["Do you wish to make every woman hate me and every man stare at me?", "여자란 여자는 죄다 저를 미워하게 만들고 남자란 남자는 죄다 저를 빤히 쳐다보게 만들고 싶으신가요?"], ["I lay a positive order on you not to come near me again to-day.", "오늘은 다시 제 곁에 오지 마시라고 저는 분명히 명을 내리겠어요."], ["Come and see me at home.", "저희 집으로 찾아오세요."], ["It is only at home that I can talk, it is only at home that I really can live and enjoy myself.", "제가 이야기라는 것을 할 수 있는 것은 집에서뿐이고, 제가 정말로 살아 있고 즐거울 수 있는 것도 집에서뿐이랍니다."], ["My days of going out, days such as these, are rare indeed.", "이렇게 바깥나들이를 하는 날은, 오늘 같은 날은 저에게 참으로 드물어요."], ["Come and see me at home, Mr.", "저희 집으로 찾아오세요, 손 씨."], ["Thorne, and then I will not bid you to leave me.\"", "그러면 그때는 제가 물러가시라는 말은 하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["It is, we believe, common with young men of five-and-twenty to look on their seniors--on men of, say, double their own age--as so many stocks and stones--stocks and stones, that is, in regard to feminine beauty.", "스물다섯 먹은 젊은이들은 흔히 제 윗세대를 — 이를테면 제 나이의 갑절이 되는 사내들을 — 한낱 나무토막이나 돌덩이로 여기곤 한다고 우리는 알고 있다. 여자의 아름다움에 관해서라면 나무토막이요 돌덩이라는 말이다."], ["There never was a greater mistake.", "이보다 더 큰 착각은 없다."], ["Women, indeed, generally know better, but on this subject men of one age are thoroughly ignorant of what is the very nature of mankind of other ages.", "여자들은 대체로 더 잘 알고 있으나, 이 문제에 관해서라면 어느 한 나이의 남자들은 다른 나이의 인간이 지닌 바로 그 본성에 대해 철저히 무지하다."], ["No experience of what goes on in the world, no reading of history, no observation of life, has any effect in teaching the truth.", "세상 돌아가는 일을 아무리 겪어 보아도, 역사를 아무리 읽어도, 삶을 아무리 살펴보아도 그 진실을 가르치는 데는 아무 소용이 없다."], ["Men of fifty don't dance mazurkas, being generally too fat and wheezy; nor do they sit for the hour together on river-banks at their mistresses'", "쉰 먹은 남자들은 마주르카를 추지 않으니, 대개 너무 살이 찌고 숨이 가쁜 까닭이다. 또 연인의 발치에서 몇 시간씩 강둑에 앉아 있지도 않으니, 류머티즘이 조금 두려운 까닭이다."], ["feet, being somewhat afraid of rheumatism. But for real true love--love at first sight, love to devotion, love that robs a man of his sleep, love that \"will gaze an eagle blind,\"", "그러나 진짜 참된 사랑으로 말하자면 — 첫눈에 빠지는 사랑, 헌신에 이르는 사랑, 사람에게서 잠을 앗아 가는 사랑, “독수리의 눈마저 멀게 할 만큼 응시하는”"], ["love that \"will hear the lowest sound when the suspicious tread of theft is stopped,\"", "사랑, “도둑의 수상쩍은 발소리가 멎는 그 가장 낮은 소리까지 듣는” 사랑, “헤스페리데스의 나무를 여전히 기어오르는 헤라클레스와 같은”"], ["love that is \"like a Hercules, still climbing trees in the Hesperides\"--we believe the best age is from forty-five to seventy; up to that, men are generally given to mere flirting.", "사랑으로 말하자면 — 우리가 보기에 가장 좋은 나이는 마흔다섯에서 일흔까지다. 그 전까지 남자들은 대개 한낱 희롱에 머물 뿐이다."]], [["At the present moment Mr. Thorne, ætat.", "지금 이 순간 손 씨는, ætat."], ["fifty, was over head and ears in love at first sight with the Signora Madeline Vesey Neroni, nata Stanhope.", "쉰의 몸으로, 시뇨라 마들린 베시 네로니, nata Stanhope에게 첫눈에 반해 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠져 있었다."]], [["Nevertheless, he was sufficiently master of himself to offer his arm with all propriety to Lady De Courcy, and the countess graciously permitted herself to be led to the tent.", "그럼에도 그는 예법에 어긋남 없이 레이디 드 코시에게 팔을 내밀 만큼은 자기를 다스리고 있었고, 백작부인은 너그러이 그의 인도를 받아 천막으로 갔다. 손 양의 지시가 그러했으니, 그녀는 주교를 구슬려 늙은 레이디 놀을 식당 상석으로 안내하게 하는 데 성공한 터였다."], ["Such had been Miss Thorne's orders, as she had succeeded in inducing the bishop to lead old Lady Knowle to the top of the dining-room.", "준남작 가운데 하나가 프라우디 부인을 찾으러 보내졌는데, 그는 그 부인이 잔디밭에서 그리 좋지 못한 기분으로 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["One of the baronets was sent off in quest of Mrs.", "손 씨와 백작부인이 너무 갑작스레 그녀를 두고 가 버린 것이다."], ["Proudie and found that lady on the lawn not in the best of humours. Mr.", "시중들어 줄 전속 사제나, 하다못해 떠도는 부목사라도 없나 하고 그녀는 헛되이 둘러보았다."], ["Thorne and the countess had left her too abruptly; she had in vain looked about for an attendant chaplain, or even a stray curate; they were all drawing long bows with the young ladies at the bottom of the lawn, or finding places for their graceful co-toxophilites in some snug corner of the tent. In such position Mrs.", "그들은 죄다 잔디밭 아래쪽에서 젊은 아가씨들과 함께 긴 활을 당기고 있거나, 천막 어느 아늑한 구석에 제 우아한 활쏘기 동무를 위한 자리를 마련해 주고 있었다."], ["Proudie had been wont in earlier days to fall back upon Mr. Slope, but now she could never fall back upon him again.", "그런 처지에 놓이면 프라우디 부인은 예전에는 으레 슬로프 씨에게 기대곤 했으나, 이제는 다시는 그에게 기댈 수 없었다."], ["She gave her head one shake as she thought of her lone position, and that shake was as good as a week deducted from Mr. Slope's longer sojourn in Barchester.", "자신의 외로운 처지를 생각하며 그녀는 고개를 한 번 저었는데, 그 한 번의 고갯짓은 슬로프 씨가 바체스터에 더 머무를 날에서 일주일을 깎아 낸 것이나 다름없었다."], ["Sir Harkaway Gorse, however, relieved her present misery, though his doing so by no means mitigated the sinning chaplain's doom.", "하카웨이 고스 경이 당장의 곤경은 덜어 주었으나, 그렇다고 해서 그 죄지은 전속 사제의 운명이 조금이라도 누그러진 것은 결코 아니었다."]], [["And now the eating and drinking began in earnest. Dr.", "이제 먹고 마시는 일이 본격적으로 시작되었다."], ["Grantly, to his great horror, found himself leagued to Mrs. Clantantram.", "그랜틀리 박사는 대경실색하게도 자기가 클랜탠트럼 부인과 짝지어진 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Mrs. Clantantram had a great regard for the archdeacon, which was not cordially returned, and when she, coming up to him, whispered in his ear, \"Come, Archdeacon, I'm sure you won't begrudge an old friend the favour of your arm,\"", "클랜탠트럼 부인은 부주교를 몹시 좋아했으나 그 마음이 살갑게 되돌아온 적은 없었는데, 그녀가 그에게 다가와 귀에 대고 “자, 부주교님, 오랜 벗에게 팔 하나 빌려주시는 것쯤이야 아까워하지 않으시겠지요”"], ["and then proceeded to tell him the whole history of her roquelaure, he resolved that he would shake her off before he was fifteen minutes older.", "하고 속삭인 다음 제 로클로르에 얽힌 사연을 처음부터 끝까지 늘어놓기 시작하자, 그는 십오 분이 지나기 전에 그녀를 떼어 놓으리라 마음먹었다."], ["But latterly the archdeacon had not been successful in his resolutions, and on the present occasion Mrs. Clantantram stuck to him till the banquet was over.", "그러나 근래 들어 부주교는 제 결심을 이루는 데 성공한 적이 없었고, 이번에도 클랜탠트럼 부인은 잔치가 끝날 때까지 그에게 착 달라붙어 있었다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne got a baronet's wife, and Mrs. Grantly fell to the lot of a baronet. Charlotte Stanhope attached herself to Mr. Harding in order to make room for Bertie, who succeeded in sitting down in the dining-room next to Mrs. Bold. To speak sooth, now that he had love in earnest to make, his heart almost failed him.", "그윈 박사에게는 어느 준남작의 부인이 돌아갔고, 그랜틀리 부인은 어느 준남작의 몫이 되었다. 샬럿 스탠호프는 버티에게 자리를 내주려고 하딩 씨에게 붙었고, 버티는 식당에서 볼드 부인 옆자리에 앉는 데 성공했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이제 진짜로 구애를 해야 하는 마당이 되고 보니 그는 거의 용기를 잃을 지경이었다."]], [["Eleanor had been right glad to avail herself of his arm, seeing that Mr. Slope was hovering nigh her.", "엘리너는 슬로프 씨가 제 가까이에서 맴도는 것을 보았던 터라 기꺼이 버티의 팔을 잡았다."], ["In striving to avoid that terrible Charybdis of a Slope she was in great danger of falling into an unseen Scylla on the other hand, that Scylla being Bertie Stanhope.", "슬로프라는 저 무서운 카리브디스를 피하려다가 그녀는 다른 쪽에 있는 보이지 않는 스킬라에 빠질 위험에 크게 처해 있었으니, 그 스킬라란 곧 버티 스탠호프였다."], ["Nothing could be more gracious than she was to Bertie.", "그녀가 버티에게 보인 태도보다 더 다정할 수는 없었다."], ["She almost jumped at his proffered arm.", "그녀는 그가 내민 팔에 거의 뛰어들다시피 했다."], ["Charlotte perceived this from a distance and triumphed in her heart; Bertie felt it and was encouraged; Mr. Slope saw it and glowered with jealousy.", "샬럿은 멀리서 이것을 알아보고 속으로 개가를 올렸고, 버티는 그것을 느끼고 용기를 얻었으며, 슬로프 씨는 그것을 보고 질투로 눈을 부라렸다."], ["Eleanor and Bertie sat down to table in the dining-room, and as she took her seat at his right hand she found that Mr. Slope was already in possession of the chair at her own.", "엘리너와 버티는 식당에서 식탁 앞에 앉았는데, 그의 오른편에 자리를 잡고 보니 그녀는 슬로프 씨가 이미 제 오른편 의자를 차지하고 있는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["As these things were going on in the dining-room, Mr. Arabin was hanging enraptured and alone over the signora's sofa, and Eleanor from her seat could look through the open door and see that he was doing so.", "식당에서 이런 일들이 벌어지고 있는 동안 애러빈 씨는 시뇨라의 소파 위로 홀린 듯 홀로 몸을 기울이고 있었고, 엘리너는 제 자리에서 열린 문 너머로 그가 그러고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII. The Bishop Sits Down to Breakfast, and the Dean Dies", "# 제38장. 주교는 아침 식탁에 앉고 수석 사제는 죽다"]], [["The Bishop of Barchester said grace over the well-spread board in the Ullathorne dining-room; while he did so, the last breath was flying from the Dean of Barchester as he lay in his sick room in the deanery.", "바체스터의 주교는 울라손 식당에 푸짐하게 차려진 식탁 앞에서 감사 기도를 올렸다. 그가 그러고 있는 동안, 바체스터의 수석 사제는 수석사제관 병실에 누운 채 마지막 숨을 내쉬고 있었다."], ["When the Bishop of Barchester raised his first glass of champagne to his lips, the deanship of Barchester was a good thing in the gift of the prime minister.", "바체스터의 주교가 첫 잔의 샴페인을 입술로 가져갔을 때, 바체스터의 수석 사제 자리는 총리가 내려 줄 수 있는 좋은 자리가 되어 있었다."], ["Before the Bishop of Barchester had left the table, the minister of the day was made aware of the fact at his country-seat in Hampshire, and had already turned over in his mind the names of five very respectable aspirants for the preferment.", "바체스터의 주교가 식탁을 뜨기도 전에, 그날의 총리는 햄프셔의 시골 저택에서 그 사실을 전해 듣고, 그 성직 승진을 노리는 대단히 존경할 만한 지망자 다섯 사람의 이름을 벌써 머릿속에 굴려 보고 있었다."], ["It is at present only necessary to say that Mr. Slope's name was not among the five.", "지금으로서는 그 다섯 안에 슬로프 씨의 이름이 없었다는 것만 말해 두면 되겠다."]], [["\"'Twas merry in the hall when the beards wagged all,\" and the clerical beards wagged merrily in the hall of Ullathorne that day.", "“수염이 모두 흔들릴 때 홀은 흥겨웠네”라 하거니와, 그날 울라손의 홀에서는 성직자들의 수염이 흥겹게 흔들렸다."], ["It was not till after the last cork had been drawn, the last speech made, the last nut cracked, that tidings reached and were whispered about that the poor dean was no more.", "마지막 코르크가 뽑히고, 마지막 연설이 끝나고, 마지막 견과가 깨어진 뒤에야 비로소 가엾은 수석 사제가 세상을 떠났다는 소식이 당도해 수군수군 퍼졌다."], ["It was well for the happiness of the clerical beards that this little delay took place, as otherwise decency would have forbidden them to wag at all.", "이 짧은 지체가 있었던 것은 성직자들의 수염의 행복을 위해 다행한 일이었다. 그러지 않았더라면 체면상 수염은 아예 흔들리지도 못했을 터이기 때문이다."]], [["But there was one sad man among them that day.", "그러나 그날 그들 가운데 슬픈 사람이 하나 있었다."], ["Mr. Arabin's beard did not wag as it should have done.", "애러빈 씨의 수염은 마땅히 흔들려야 할 만큼 흔들리지 않았다."], ["He had come there hoping the best, striving to think the best, about Eleanor; turning over in his mind all the words he remembered to have fallen from her about Mr.", "그는 엘리너에 대해 최선을 바라며, 최선을 생각해 보려 애쓰며 그곳에 왔다. 그녀가 슬로프 씨에 대해 흘렸던 말이라면 무엇이든 기억을 더듬어 머릿속에 굴려 보며, 거기에서 제 연적에게 불리한 확신을 그러모으려 애썼던 것이다."], ["Slope, and trying to gather from them a conviction unfavourable to his rival. He had not exactly resolved to come that day to some decisive proof as to the widow's intention, but he had meant, if possible, to recultivate his friendship with Eleanor, and in his present frame of mind any such recultivation must have ended in a declaration of love.", "그날 그 미망인의 뜻에 관해 결정적인 증거를 얻고야 말겠다고 딱히 작정한 것은 아니었으나, 되도록이면 엘리너와의 우정을 다시 가꾸어 볼 생각이었고, 지금 같은 마음 상태로는 그런 재배가 무엇이든 결국 사랑의 고백으로 끝날 수밖에 없었다."]], [["He had passed the previous night alone at his new parsonage, and it was the first night that he had so passed.", "그는 지난밤을 새 목사관에서 혼자 보냈는데, 그렇게 보낸 것은 그 밤이 처음이었다."], ["It had been dull and sombre enough. Mrs.", "그 밤은 어지간히 따분하고 침울했다."], ["Grantly had been right in saying that a priestess would be wanting at St. Ewold's.", "세인트 이월드에는 여사제가 하나 아쉬우리라던 그랜틀리 부인의 말은 옳았다."], ["He had sat there alone with his glass before him, and then with his tea-pot, thinking about Eleanor Bold.", "그는 앞에 잔을 놓고, 이어서 찻주전자를 놓고 홀로 앉아 엘리너 볼드를 생각했다."], ["As is usual in such meditations, he did little but blame her; blame her for liking Mr.", "그런 종류의 명상이 대개 그렇듯, 그가 한 일이라고는 그녀를 탓하는 것뿐이었다."], ["Slope, and blame her for not liking him; blame her for her cordiality to himself, and blame her for her want of cordiality; blame her for being stubborn, headstrong, and passionate; and yet the more he thought of her the higher she rose in his affection.", "슬로프 씨를 좋아한다고 탓하고, 자기를 좋아하지 않는다고 탓하고, 자기에게 다정했다고 탓하고, 다정하지 않았다고 탓하고, 고집스럽고 제멋대로이고 격정적이라고 탓했다. 그러면서도 그녀를 생각하면 할수록 그녀는 그의 애정 속에서 점점 더 높이 떠올랐다."], ["If only it should turn out, if only it could be made to turn out, that she had defended Mr. Slope, not from love, but on principle, all would be right.", "만일 그녀가 슬로프 씨를 두둔한 것이 사랑에서가 아니라 원칙에서였다는 것이 드러나기만 한다면, 그렇게 드러나도록 만들 수만 있다면, 모든 것이 괜찮아질 터였다."], ["Such principle in itself would be admirable, lovable, womanly; he felt that he could be pleased to allow Mr. Slope just so much favour as that.", "그런 원칙이라면 그 자체로 훌륭하고 사랑스럽고 여자다운 것이니, 슬로프 씨에게 딱 그만큼의 호의는 기꺼이 허락해도 좋겠다고 그는 느꼈다."], ["But if--And then Mr. Arabin poked his fire most unnecessarily, spoke crossly to his new parlour-maid who came in for the tea-things, and threw himself back in his chair determined to go to sleep.", "그러나 만일— 그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 아무 까닭도 없이 난롯불을 쑤셔 대고, 찻그릇을 치우러 들어온 새 응접실 하녀에게 퉁명스럽게 말하고, 잠이나 자야겠다고 마음먹으며 의자에 몸을 던졌다."], ["Why had she been so stiff-necked when asked a plain question?", "뻔한 물음을 받고 어째서 그토록 목을 뻣뻣이 세웠단 말인가?"], ["She could not but have known in what light he regarded her.", "자기가 그녀를 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 그녀가 모를 리 없었다."], ["Why had she not answered a plain question and so put an end to his misery?", "어째서 뻔한 물음에 답해 그의 비참함을 끝내 주지 않았단 말인가?"], ["Then, instead of going to sleep in his armchair, Mr. Arabin walked about the room as though he had been possessed.", "그러고는 안락의자에서 잠들기는커녕, 애러빈 씨는 무엇에 씐 사람처럼 방 안을 서성거렸다."]], [["On the following morning, when he attended Miss Thorne's behests, he was still in a somewhat confused state.", "이튿날 아침 손 양의 분부를 받들 때에도 그는 여전히 다소 어수선한 상태였다."], ["His first duty had been to converse with Mrs. Clantantram, and that lady had found it impossible to elicit the slightest sympathy from him on the subject of her roquelaure.", "그의 첫 임무는 클랜탠트럼 부인과 이야기를 나누는 것이었는데, 그 부인은 제 로클로르 이야기를 두고 그에게서 손톱만 한 동정도 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["Miss Thorne had asked him whether Mrs. Bold was coming with the Grantlys, and the two names of Bold and Grantly together had nearly made him jump from his seat.", "손 양이 볼드 부인은 그랜틀리 댁 사람들과 함께 오느냐고 묻자, 볼드와 그랜틀리라는 두 이름이 나란히 놓인 것만으로 그는 하마터면 자리에서 벌떡 일어설 뻔했다."]], [["He was in this state of confused uncertainty, hope, and doubt, when he saw Mr. Slope, with his most polished smile, handing Eleanor out of her carriage.", "희망과 의심이 뒤엉킨 그 어수선한 불확실 속에 있을 때, 그는 슬로프 씨가 더없이 세련된 미소를 띠고 엘리너를 마차에서 부축해 내리는 것을 보았다."], ["He thought of nothing more.", "그는 더 아무것도 생각하지 않았다."], ["He never considered whether the carriage belonged to her or to Mr. Slope, or to anyone else to whom they might both be mutually obliged without any concert between themselves.", "그 마차가 그녀의 것인지 슬로프 씨의 것인지, 아니면 둘 사이에 아무 약속이 없어도 둘 다 신세를 질 만한 제삼자의 것인지 따위는 따져 보지도 않았다."], ["This sight in his present state of mind was quite enough to upset him and his resolves.", "지금 같은 마음 상태에서 그 광경은 그와 그의 결심을 뒤엎기에 충분하고도 남았다."], ["It was clear as noon-day.", "한낮처럼 명백했다."], ["Had he seen her handed into a carriage by Mr. Slope at a church door with a white veil over her head, the truth could not be more manifest.", "머리에 흰 면사포를 쓴 그녀가 교회 문간에서 슬로프 씨의 손에 이끌려 마차에 오르는 것을 보았다 한들, 진실이 그보다 더 뚜렷할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["He went into the house and, as we have seen, soon found himself walking with Mr. Harding.", "그는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 앞서 본 대로 곧 하딩 씨와 나란히 걷고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Shortly afterwards Eleanor came up, and then he had to leave his companion and either go about alone or find another.", "조금 뒤 엘리너가 다가왔고, 그리하여 그는 동행을 두고 물러나 혼자 다니거나 다른 동행을 찾아야 했다."], ["While in this state he was encountered by the archdeacon.", "그러던 참에 부주교와 마주쳤다."]], [["\"I wonder,\" said Dr.", "“나는 궁금하오.”"], ["Grantly, \"if it be true that Mr.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."], ["Slope and Mrs. Bold came here together.", "“슬로프 씨와 볼드 부인이 함께 왔다는 것이 정말이오?"], ["Susan says she is almost sure she saw their faces in the same carriage as she got out of her own.\"", "수전은 제 마차에서 내리다가 같은 마차 안에 있는 두 사람의 얼굴을 본 것이 거의 틀림없다고 하는구려.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin had nothing for it but to bear his testimony to the correctness of Mrs. Grantly's eyesight.", "애러빈 씨로서는 그랜틀리 부인의 눈이 정확했다고 증언하는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"It is perfectly shameful,\"", "“참으로 수치스러운 일이오.”"], ["said the archdeacon; \"or, I should rather say, shameless.", "부주교가 말했다. “아니, 차라리 수치를 모르는 짓이라 해야겠구려."], ["She was asked here as my guest, and if she be determined to disgrace herself, she should have feeling enough not to do so before my immediate friends.", "그 사람은 내 손님으로 초대받아 온 거요. 제 몸을 망치기로 작정했다면, 적어도 내 가까운 벗들 앞에서만은 그러지 않을 만한 분별은 있어야 하지 않겠소."], ["I wonder how that man got himself invited.", "대체 저 사내가 어떻게 초대를 얻어 냈는지 모르겠구려."], ["I wonder whether she had the face to bring him.\"", "설마 그 사람이 낯짝 좋게 데려온 것은 아니겠지.”"]], [["To this Mr. Arabin could answer nothing, nor did he wish to answer anything.", "여기에 애러빈 씨는 아무 대답도 할 수 없었고, 무슨 말이든 하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["Though he abused Eleanor to himself, he did not choose to abuse her to anyone else, nor was he well-pleased to hear anyone else speak ill of her.", "그는 혼자서는 엘리너를 헐뜯었으나 남 앞에서 그녀를 헐뜯을 마음은 없었고, 남이 그녀를 나쁘게 말하는 것을 듣는 일도 달갑지 않았다."], ["Dr. Grantly, however, was very angry and did not spare his sister-in-law.", "그러나 그랜틀리 박사는 몹시 화가 나 있어 처제를 조금도 봐주지 않았다."], ["Mr. Arabin therefore left him as soon as he could and wandered back into the house.", "그래서 애러빈 씨는 되도록 빨리 그에게서 벗어나 다시 집 안으로 흘러들어 갔다."]], [["He had not been there long when the signora was brought in.", "그가 들어간 지 얼마 되지 않아 시뇨라가 실려 들어왔다."], ["For some time he kept himself out of temptation, and merely hovered round her at a distance; but as soon as Mr.", "한동안 그는 유혹을 피해 멀찍이서 그 언저리를 맴돌기만 했다."], ["Thorne had left her, he yielded himself up to the basilisk and allowed himself to be made prey of.", "그러나 손 씨가 그녀 곁을 떠나자마자, 그는 그 바실리스크에게 자신을 내맡기고 순순히 먹이가 되었다."]], [["It is impossible to say how the knowledge had been acquired, but the signora had a sort of instinctive knowledge that Mr. Arabin was an admirer of Mrs.", "그 앎이 어떻게 얻어진 것인지는 말할 도리가 없으나, 시뇨라는 애러빈 씨가 볼드 부인을 흠모한다는 것을 일종의 본능으로 알고 있었다."], ["Bold. Men hunt foxes by the aid of dogs, and are aware that they do so by the strong organ of smell with which the dog is endowed.", "사람은 개의 도움을 받아 여우를 사냥하며, 개에게 주어진 강력한 후각 기관 덕분에 그리한다는 것을 안다."], ["They do not, however, in the least comprehend how such a sense can work with such acuteness.", "그러면서도 그런 감각이 어떻게 그토록 예민하게 작동하는지는 조금도 이해하지 못한다."], ["The organ by which women instinctively, as it were, know and feel how other women are regarded by men, and how also men are regarded by other women, is equally strong, and equally incomprehensible.", "다른 여자가 남자들에게 어떻게 여겨지는지, 또 남자들이 다른 여자들에게 어떻게 여겨지는지를 여자가 이를테면 본능적으로 알고 느끼게 하는 그 기관도 그와 똑같이 강력하고 그와 똑같이 헤아리기 어렵다. 눈길 하나, 말 한마디, 몸짓 하나면 족하다."], ["A glance, a word, a motion, suffices: by some such acute exercise of her feminine senses the signora was aware that Mr.", "그런 식으로 제 여성적 감각을 예리하게 놀린 끝에 시뇨라는 애러빈 씨가 엘리너 볼드를 사랑한다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Arabin loved Eleanor Bold; therefore, by a further exercise of her peculiar feminine propensities, it was quite natural for her to entrap Mr. Arabin into her net.", "그러므로 그 특유의 여성적 성향을 한 번 더 발휘하여 애러빈 씨를 제 그물에 얽어 넣는 것은 그녀로서는 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다."]], [["The work was half-done before she came to Ullathorne, and when could she have a better opportunity of completing it?", "그 일은 그녀가 울라손에 오기 전에 이미 절반은 되어 있었으니, 그것을 마무리하기에 이보다 나은 기회가 또 어디 있겠는가?"], ["She had had almost enough of Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 이제 거의 물릴 만큼 겪었다."], ["Slope, though she could not quite resist the fun of driving a very sanctimonious clergyman to madness by a desperate and ruinous passion. Mr.", "물론 성인군자인 척하는 성직자 하나를 절망적이고 파멸적인 정열로 몰아 미치게 하는 재미만은 아주 뿌리치지 못했지만 말이다."], ["Thorne had fallen too easily to give much pleasure in the chase.", "손 씨는 너무 쉽게 넘어와 사냥의 즐거움이 별로 없었다."], ["His position as a man of wealth might make his alliance of value, but as a lover he was very second-rate.", "재산가라는 그의 처지는 인연을 맺어 둘 값어치가 있을지 모르나, 연인으로서는 몹시 이류였다."], ["We may say that she regarded him somewhat as a sportsman does a pheasant.", "그녀가 그를 두고 사냥꾼이 꿩을 대하듯 했다고 해도 좋겠다."], ["The bird is so easily shot that he would not be worth the shooting were it not for the very respectable appearance that he makes in a larder.", "그 새는 너무 쉽게 맞아 떨어져서, 식품 저장실에 걸어 두었을 때 제법 그럴듯해 보이지만 않는다면 쏠 값어치조차 없다."], ["The signora would not waste much time in shooting Mr. Thorne, but still he was worth bagging for family uses.", "시뇨라는 손 씨를 쏘는 데 많은 시간을 들이지 않겠지만, 그래도 집안에 쓸모가 있으니 자루에 넣어 둘 만은 했다."]], [["But Mr. Arabin was game of another sort. The signora was herself possessed of quite sufficient intelligence to know that Mr. Arabin was a man more than usually intellectual. She knew also that, as a clergyman, he was of a much higher stamp than Mr. Slope and that, as a gentleman, he was better educated than Mr. Thorne. She would never have attempted to drive Mr. Arabin into ridiculous misery as she did Mr. Slope, nor would she think it possible to dispose of him in ten minutes as she had done with Mr. Thorne.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 종류가 다른 사냥감이었다. 시뇨라 자신도 애러빈 씨가 보통 이상으로 지적인 사람임을 알아볼 만큼은 충분히 영리했다. 그녀는 또한 그가 성직자로서 슬로프 씨보다 훨씬 격이 높고, 신사로서 손 씨보다 교양이 낫다는 것도 알았다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 하듯 애러빈 씨를 우스꽝스러운 비참으로 몰아넣으려 들지는 않았을 것이며, 손 씨를 처리하듯 10분 만에 그를 처리할 수 있으리라고 여기지도 않았을 것이다."]], [["Such were her reflexions about Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨에 대한 그녀의 생각은 그러했다."], ["As to Mr. Arabin, it cannot be said that he reflected at all about the signora.", "애러빈 씨로 말하자면, 그가 시뇨라에 대해 무엇이든 생각했다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["He knew that she was beautiful, and he felt that she was able to charm him.", "그는 그녀가 아름답다는 것을 알았고, 그녀가 자기를 홀릴 수 있으리라고 느꼈다."], ["He required charming in his present misery, and therefore he went and stood at the head of her couch.", "지금의 비참함 속에서 그는 홀림이 필요했고, 그래서 가서 그녀의 긴 의자 머리맡에 섰다."], ["She knew all about it.", "그녀는 그 사정을 죄다 알고 있었다."], ["Such were her peculiar gifts.", "그런 것이 그녀의 남다른 재능이었다."], ["It was her nature to see that he required charming, and it was her province to charm him.", "그가 홀림을 필요로 한다는 것을 알아보는 것이 그녀의 천성이었고, 그를 홀리는 것이 그녀의 소임이었다."], ["As the Eastern idler swallows his dose of opium, as the London reprobate swallows his dose of gin, so with similar desires and for similar reasons did Mr. Arabin prepare to swallow the charms of the Signora Neroni.", "동방의 게으름뱅이가 제 몫의 아편을 삼키듯, 런던의 난봉꾼이 제 몫의 진을 삼키듯, 애러빈 씨도 그와 같은 욕구로 그와 같은 이유에서 시뇨라 네로니의 매혹을 삼킬 채비를 했다."]], [["\"Why an't you shooting with bows and arrows, Mr. Arabin?\"", "“애러빈 씨는 어째서 활을 쏘고 계시지 않나요?”"], ["said she, when they were nearly alone together in the drawing-room, \"or talking with young ladies in shady bowers, or turning your talents to account in some way?", "응접실에 둘만 남다시피 했을 때 그녀가 말했다. “아니면 그늘진 정자에서 젊은 아가씨들과 이야기라도 하시든지, 어떻게든 그 재주를 좀 쓰시든지요."], ["What was a bachelor like you asked here for?", "당신 같은 독신 남자가 대체 무엇 때문에 여기 초대를 받았겠어요?"], ["Don't you mean to earn your cold chicken and champagne?", "차가운 닭고기와 샴페인 값은 하실 생각이 없으신가요?"], ["Were I you, I should be ashamed to be so idle.\"", "나 같으면 그렇게 빈둥거리는 것이 부끄러울 텐데요.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin murmured some sort of answer.", "애러빈 씨는 대답 비슷한 것을 우물거렸다."], ["Though he wished to be charmed, he was hardly yet in a mood to be playful in return.", "홀리고 싶은 마음은 있었으나, 아직은 장난스럽게 되받을 기분이 되지 못했던 것이다."]], [["\"Why what ails you, Mr. Arabin?\"", "“대체 무슨 일이 있으신 거예요, 애러빈 씨?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Here you are in your own parish--Miss Thorne tells me that her party is given expressly in your honour--and yet you are the only dull man at it. Your friend Mr.", "“여기는 당신의 교구고, 손 양 말로는 이 잔치가 오로지 당신을 기려 베풀어진 것이라던데, 그런데도 이 자리에서 시무룩한 사람은 당신 하나뿐이군요."], ["Slope was with me a few minutes since, full of life and spirits; why don't you rival him?\"", "당신 친구 슬로프 씨는 조금 전까지 나와 함께 있었는데 활기와 기운이 넘치더군요. 어째서 그분과 겨루어 보지 않으세요?”"]], [["It was not difficult for so acute an observer as Madeline Neroni to see that she had hit the nail on the head and driven the bolt home. Mr.", "마들린 네로니처럼 예리한 관찰자가 자기가 정곡을 찔렀고 빗장을 끝까지 밀어 넣었음을 알아채기란 어렵지 않았다."], ["Arabin winced visibly before her attack, and she knew at once that he was jealous of Mr. Slope.", "애러빈 씨는 그 공격 앞에서 눈에 띄게 움찔했고, 그녀는 그가 슬로프 씨를 질투하고 있음을 대번에 알았다."]], [["\"But I look on you and Mr.", "“하지만 나는 당신과 슬로프 씨를 사람의 양극이라고 보고 있어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["Slope as the very antipodes of men,\" said she.", "“두 분이 서로 정반대가 아닌 데라고는 한 군데도 없어요."], ["\"There is nothing in which you are not each the reverse of the other, except in belonging to the same profession--and even in that you are so unlike as perfectly to maintain the rule.", "같은 직업에 몸담고 있다는 것만 빼면요. 게다가 그 점에서도 두 분은 어찌나 다른지 오히려 그 규칙을 완벽하게 지켜 주고 계시죠."], ["He is gregarious; you are given to solitude.", "그분은 무리를 좋아하고, 당신은 홀로 있기를 좋아해요."], ["He is active; you are passive.", "그분은 활동적이고, 당신은 가만있죠."], ["He works; you think.", "그분은 일하고, 당신은 생각해요."], ["He likes women; you despise them.", "그분은 여자를 좋아하고, 당신은 여자를 업신여기죠."], ["He is fond of position and power; and so are you, but for directly different reasons.", "그분은 지위와 권력을 좋아하는데, 당신도 그렇답니다. 다만 이유가 정반대일 뿐이죠."], ["He loves to be praised; you very foolishly abhor it.", "그분은 칭찬받기를 사랑하고, 당신은 어리석게도 그것을 질색하고요. 그분은 제 보상을 얻을 거예요."], ["He will gain his rewards, which will be an insipid, useful wife, a comfortable income, and a reputation for sanctimony; you will also gain yours.\"", "밋밋하지만 쓸모 있는 아내와 넉넉한 수입과 경건하다는 평판을 말이에요. 당신도 당신 몫의 보상을 얻을 테고요.”"]], [["\"Well, and what will they be?\" said Mr.", "“그래, 그것이 무엇이겠소?”"], ["Arabin, who knew that he was being flattered and yet suffered himself to put up with it.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다. 그는 자기가 아첨을 받고 있음을 알면서도 그것을 참고 받아들였다."], ["\"What will be my rewards?\"", "“내가 받을 보상이란 무엇이오?”"]], [["\"The heart of some woman whom you will be too austere to own that you love, and the respect of some few friends which you will be too proud to own that you value.\"", "“당신이 사랑한다고 차마 인정하지 못할 만큼 준엄한 어느 여인의 마음, 그리고 당신이 소중히 여긴다고 차마 인정하지 못할 만큼 자존심 센 몇몇 벗들의 존경이죠.”"]], [["\"Rich rewards,\"", "“푸짐한 보상이구려.”"], ["said he; \"but of little worth, if they are to be so treated.\"", "그가 말했다. “그렇게 다루어질 것이라면 값어치는 별로 없겠소만.”"]], [["\"Oh, you are not to look for such success as awaits Mr. Slope.", "“오, 당신은 슬로프 씨를 기다리는 그런 성공을 바라서는 안 돼요."], ["He is born to be a successful man.", "그분은 성공하도록 태어난 사람이거든요."], ["He suggests to himself an object and then starts for it with eager intention.", "스스로 목표를 세우고 열망을 품은 채 그리로 달려가죠."], ["Nothing will deter him from his pursuit.", "무엇도 그분을 그 추구에서 물러서게 하지 못해요."], ["He will have no scruples, no fears, no hesitation.", "그분에게는 거리낌도 두려움도 망설임도 없을 거예요."], ["His desire is to be a bishop with a rising family--the wife will come first, and in due time the apron.", "그분의 소망은 식구가 불어나는 주교가 되는 것이고, 아내가 먼저 오고 때가 되면 앞치마가 오겠죠."], ["You will see all this, and then--\"", "당신은 그 모든 것을 보게 될 거고, 그러면—”"]], [["\"Well, and what then?\"", "“그래, 그러면 어떻게 된단 말이오?”"]], [["\"Then you will begin to wish that you had done the same.\"", "“그러면 당신도 똑같이 해 둘걸 하고 바라기 시작하겠죠.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin looked placidly out at the lawn and, resting his shoulder on the head of the sofa, rubbed his chin with his hand.", "애러빈 씨는 잔디밭을 태연히 내다보며 어깨를 소파 등받이에 기댄 채 손으로 턱을 문질렀다."], ["It was a trick he had when he was thinking deeply, and what the signora said made him think.", "깊이 생각에 잠길 때면 나오는 버릇이었는데, 시뇨라의 말이 그를 생각에 잠기게 한 것이다."], ["Was it not all true?", "그것이 다 사실이 아닌가?"], ["Would he not hereafter look back, if not at Mr. Slope, at some others, perhaps not equally gifted with himself, who had risen in the world while he had lagged behind, and then wish that he had done the same?", "훗날 그는, 슬로프 씨는 아닐지언정 자기만큼 재능을 타고나지도 못한 어떤 이들이 자기가 뒤처져 있는 동안 세상에서 올라선 것을 돌아보며, 나도 똑같이 할걸 하고 바라게 되지 않겠는가?"]], [["\"Is not such the doom of all speculative men of talent?\"", "“재주 있는 사색가들이 다 그런 운명 아니겠어요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Do they not all sit wrapt as you now are, cutting imaginary silken cords with their fine edges, while those not so highly tempered sever the everyday Gordian knots of the world's struggle and win wealth and renown?", "“그런 사람들은 다들 지금 당신처럼 골똘히 앉아 그 고운 날로 있지도 않은 비단실을 자르고 있고, 그동안 그만큼 벼려지지 못한 이들은 세상살이 싸움의 저 평범한 고르디우스 매듭을 끊어 내고 부와 명성을 얻지 않던가요?"], ["Steel too highly polished, edges too sharp, do not do for this world's work, Mr. Arabin.\"", "너무 곱게 벼린 강철, 너무 날카로운 날은 이 세상 일에는 쓸모가 없답니다, 애러빈 씨.”"]], [["Who was this woman that thus read the secrets of his heart and re-uttered to him the unwelcome bodings of his own soul?", "제 마음속 비밀을 그렇게 읽어 내고 제 영혼이 품은 달갑지 않은 예감을 그대로 되뇌어 주는 이 여인은 대체 누구인가?"], ["He looked full into her face when she had done speaking and said, \"Am I one of those foolish blades, too sharp and too fine to do a useful day's work?\"", "그녀가 말을 마치자 그는 그 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다. “나도 하루치 쓸모 있는 일을 하기에는 너무 날카롭고 너무 고운 그 어리석은 칼날 가운데 하나요?”"]], [["\"Why do you let the Slopes of the world outdistance you?\"", "“어째서 세상의 슬로프 같은 이들이 당신을 앞지르도록 두시나요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Is not the blood in your veins as warm as his?", "“당신 핏줄 속의 피가 그분 것만큼 뜨겁지 않던가요?"], ["Does not your pulse beat as fast?", "당신 맥이 그만큼 빠르게 뛰지 않던가요?"], ["Has not God made you a man and intended you to do a man's work here, ay, and to take a man's wages also?\"", "하느님이 당신을 남자로 지으시고 여기서 남자의 일을 하라고, 그래요, 남자의 삯도 받으라고 뜻하지 않으셨던가요?”"]], [["Mr. Arabin sat ruminating, rubbing his face, and wondering why these things were said to him, but he replied nothing.", "애러빈 씨는 얼굴을 문지르며 곰곰이 앉아, 어째서 이런 말들이 자기에게 던져지는지 의아해했으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["The signora went on:", "시뇨라는 말을 이었다."]], [["\"The greatest mistake any man ever made is to suppose that the good things of the world are not worth the winning.", "“사람이 저지르는 가장 큰 잘못은 세상의 좋은 것들이 얻을 값어치가 없다고 여기는 거예요."], ["And it is a mistake so opposed to the religion which you preach!", "게다가 그건 당신이 설교하는 종교에도 그토록 어긋나는 잘못이고요!"], ["Why does God permit his bishops one after another to have their five thousands and ten thousands a year if such wealth be bad and not worth having?", "재물이 나쁘고 가질 값어치도 없는 것이라면, 하느님은 어째서 주교들에게 대대로 연 5,000파운드니 1만 파운드니 하는 것을 허락하시겠어요?"], ["Why are beautiful things given to us, and luxuries and pleasant enjoyments, if they be not intended to be used?", "쓰라고 주신 것이 아니라면 어째서 아름다운 것들이, 사치와 즐거운 향락이 우리에게 주어졌겠어요?"], ["They must be meant for someone, and what is good for a layman surely cannot be bad for a clerk.", "그것들은 누군가를 위한 것일 텐데, 속인에게 좋은 것이 성직자에게 나쁠 리야 없지 않겠어요."], ["You try to despise these good things, but you only try--you don't succeed.\"", "당신은 그 좋은 것들을 업신여기려 애쓰지만, 애쓸 뿐이지 성공하지는 못하고 있어요.”"]], [["\"Don't I?\" said Mr.", "“내가 성공하지 못하고 있소?”"], ["Arabin, still musing, not knowing what he said.", "애러빈 씨가 여전히 골똘한 채, 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르고 말했다."]], [["\"I ask you the question: do you succeed?\"", "“내가 여쭙는 거예요. 당신은 성공하고 계신가요?”"]], [["Mr. Arabin looked at her piteously.", "애러빈 씨는 애처로운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["It seemed to him as though he were being interrogated by some inner spirit of his own, to whom he could not refuse an answer, and to whom he did not dare to give a false reply.", "마치 제 안에 있는 어떤 영에게 심문을 받는 것 같았다. 대답을 거절할 수도 없고, 거짓으로 대답할 엄두도 나지 않는 그런 영에게 말이다."]], [["\"Come, Mr. Arabin, confess; do you succeed?", "“자, 애러빈 씨, 고백해 보세요."], ["Is money so contemptible?", "성공하고 계신가요? 돈이 그렇게 하찮은가요?"], ["Is worldly power so worthless?", "세속의 권력이 그렇게 값없는가요?"], ["Is feminine beauty a trifle to be so slightly regarded by a wise man?\"", "여자의 아름다움이란 지혜로운 사람이 그토록 가볍게 여겨도 좋을 하찮은 것인가요?”"]], [["\"Feminine beauty!\"", "“여자의 아름다움이라!”"], ["said he, gazing into her face, as though all the feminine beauty in the world were concentrated there.", "세상의 모든 여성적 아름다움이 거기 모여 있기라도 한 듯 그녀의 얼굴을 응시하며 그가 말했다."], ["\"Why do you say I do not regard it?\"", "“어째서 내가 그것을 눈여겨보지 않는다고 하시오?”"]], [["\"If you look at me like that, Mr.", "“나를 그런 눈으로 보신다면, 애러빈 씨, 나는 생각을 바꾸겠어요."], ["Arabin, I shall alter my opinion--or should do so, were I not of course aware that I have no beauty of my own worth regarding.\"", "아니, 내게는 눈여겨볼 만한 아름다움이 없다는 것을 물론 잘 알고 있으니, 바꾸어야 마땅하겠지요.”"]], [["The gentleman blushed crimson, but the lady did not blush at all.", "신사는 얼굴이 새빨개졌으나 부인은 조금도 붉어지지 않았다."], ["A slightly increased colour animated her face, just so much so as to give her an air of special interest.", "낯빛이 살짝 더 살아났을 뿐이고, 그것도 딱 각별한 관심을 품은 듯한 기색을 줄 만큼이었다."], ["She expected a compliment from her admirer, but she was rather gratified than otherwise by finding that he did not pay it to her. Messrs.", "그녀는 흠모하는 남자에게서 찬사가 나오기를 기다렸으나, 그가 찬사를 바치지 않는 것을 알고는 서운하기는커녕 오히려 흡족했다."], ["Slope and Thorne, Messrs. Brown, Jones, and Robinson, they all paid her compliments.", "슬로프 씨며 손 씨며, 브라운이며 존스며 로빈슨이며, 하나같이 그녀에게 찬사를 바쳤다."], ["She was rather in hopes that she would ultimately succeed in inducing Mr. Arabin to abuse her.", "그녀는 끝내 애러빈 씨가 자기를 헐뜯게 만들 수 있으리라고 은근히 기대하고 있었다."]], [["\"But your gaze,\" said she, \"is one of wonder, not of admiration.", "“하지만 당신의 그 눈길은 놀라움이지 흠모가 아니로군요.”"], ["You wonder at my audacity in asking you such questions about yourself.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “당신 자신에 대해 내가 그런 것을 묻는 그 대담함에 놀라신 거예요.”"]], [["\"Well, I do rather,\"", "“그렇소, 좀 그렇구려.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Nevertheless, I expect an answer, Mr.", "“그래도 나는 대답을 기다리겠어요, 애러빈 씨."], ["Arabin. Why were women made beautiful if men are not to regard them?\"", "남자들이 여자를 눈여겨보지 않을 것이라면 여자는 어째서 아름답게 지어졌겠어요?”"]], [["\"But men do regard them,\"", "“남자들은 여자를 눈여겨보고 있소.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["\"And why not you?\"", "“그런데 당신은 어째서 아니죠?”"]], [["\"You are begging the question, Madame Neroni.\"", "“그것은 논점을 구걸하는 것이오, 마담 네로니.”"]], [["\"I am sure I shall beg nothing, Mr. Arabin, which you will not grant, and I do beg for an answer.", "“나는 당신이 들어주지 않을 것은 무엇도 구걸하지 않을 거예요, 애러빈 씨."], ["Do you not as a rule think women below your notice as companions?", "그리고 대답만은 정말로 구걸하겠어요. 당신은 대체로 여자를 벗으로 삼기에는 눈 아래로 보시지 않나요?"], ["Let us see.", "어디 봅시다."], ["There is the Widow Bold looking round at you from her chair this minute.", "저기 볼드 미망인이 지금 이 순간 제 의자에서 당신을 돌아보고 있네요."], ["What would you say to her as a companion for life?\"", "저분을 평생의 반려로 삼는다면 뭐라고 하시겠어요?”"]], [["Mr. Arabin, rising from his position, leaned over the sofa and looked through the drawing-room door to the place where Eleanor was seated between Bertie Stanhope and Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 앉은 자리에서 몸을 일으켜 소파 위로 기대며 응접실 문 너머로, 엘리너가 버티 스탠호프와 슬로프 씨 사이에 앉아 있는 자리를 바라보았다."], ["Slope. She at once caught his glance and averted her own.", "그녀는 곧바로 그 눈길을 알아채고 제 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["She was not pleasantly placed in her present position. Mr.", "지금 앉은 자리는 그녀에게 편안하지 않았다."], ["Slope was doing his best to attract her attention, and she was striving to prevent his doing so by talking to Mr. Stanhope, while her mind was intently fixed on Mr.", "슬로프 씨는 그녀의 주의를 끌려고 있는 힘을 다하고 있었고, 그녀는 스탠호프 씨와 이야기함으로써 그것을 막으려 애쓰고 있었으며, 그러면서도 마음은 온통 애러빈 씨와 마담 네로니에게 쏠려 있었다."], ["Arabin and Madame Neroni. Bertie Stanhope endeavoured to take advantage of her favours, but he was thinking more of the manner in which he would by and by throw himself at her feet than of amusing her at the present moment.", "버티 스탠호프는 그녀가 베푸는 호의를 이용해 보려 했으나, 지금 이 순간 그녀를 즐겁게 하는 일보다는 이따가 어떤 식으로 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던질까 하는 궁리를 더 하고 있었다."]], [["\"There,\"", "“보세요.”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."], ["\"She was stretching her beautiful neck to look at you, and now you have disturbed her.", "“저분이 그 고운 목을 늘여 당신을 보고 있었는데, 이제 당신이 저분을 흔들어 놓았군요. 어머, 나는 정말이지 당신에 대해 잘못 알고 있었나 봐요."], ["Well, I declare I believe I am wrong about you; I believe that you do think Mrs. Bold a charming woman.", "당신은 참으로 볼드 부인이 매력적인 여인이라고 여기고 계시는군요."], ["Your looks seem to say so, and by her looks I should say that she is jealous of me. Come, Mr.", "당신 눈빛이 그렇다고 말하고, 저분 눈빛으로 보아서는 나를 질투하고 있다고 해야겠어요."], ["Arabin, confide in me, and if it is so, I'll do all in my power to make up the match.\"", "자, 애러빈 씨, 내게 털어놓으세요. 정말 그러하다면 내가 힘닿는 데까지 그 혼사를 이루어 드리죠.”"]], [["It is needless to say that the signora was not very sincere in her offer.", "시뇨라가 그 제안에 그리 진심이 아니었다는 말은 할 필요도 없다."], ["She was never sincere on such subjects.", "그녀는 그런 문제에서 결코 진심인 적이 없었다."], ["She never expected others to be so, nor did she expect others to think her so.", "남들이 진심이리라고 기대한 적도 없고, 남들이 자기를 진심이라 여겨 주기를 바란 적도 없었다."], ["Such matters were her playthings, her billiard table, her hounds and hunters, her waltzes and polkas, her picnics and summer-day excursions.", "그런 일들은 그녀의 장난감이었고, 당구대였고, 사냥개와 사냥말이었고, 왈츠와 폴카였고, 소풍과 여름날의 나들이였다."], ["She had little else to amuse her, and therefore played at love-making in all its forms.", "달리 즐길 거리가 별로 없었으므로 그녀는 온갖 형태로 연애놀이를 했다."], ["She was now playing at it with Mr. Arabin, and did not at all expect the earnestness and truth of his answer.", "지금은 애러빈 씨를 상대로 그 놀이를 하고 있었으니, 그의 대답이 그토록 진지하고 진실하리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다."]], [["\"All in your power would be nothing,\"", "“힘닿는 데까지라 해 봐야 아무 소용 없을 것이오.”"], ["said he, \"for Mrs.", "그가 말했다."], ["Bold is, I imagine, already engaged to another.\"", "“볼드 부인은 이미 다른 사람과 약혼했다고 나는 짐작하고 있으니 말이오.”"]], [["\"Then you own the impeachment yourself.\"", "“그럼 당신 입으로 그 혐의를 인정하시는군요.”"]], [["\"You cross-question me rather unfairly,\"", "“나를 너무 부당하게 추궁하시는구려.”"], ["he replied, \"and I do not know why I answer you at all.", "그가 대답했다. “내가 어째서 대답을 하고 있는지조차 모르겠소."], ["Mrs. Bold is a very beautiful woman, and as intelligent as beautiful.", "볼드 부인은 대단히 아름다운 여인이고, 아름다운 만큼 총명하오."], ["It is impossible to know her without admiring her.\"", "그 사람을 알고서 흠모하지 않기란 불가능하오.”"]], [["\"So you think the widow a very beautiful woman?\"", "“그러니까 그 미망인이 대단히 아름다운 여인이라고 여기시는군요?”"]], [["\"Indeed I do.\"", "“정말 그렇게 여기오.”"]], [["\"And one that would grace the parsonage of St. Ewold's.\"", "“그리고 세인트 이월드 목사관을 빛내 줄 만한 사람이라고요.”"]], [["\"One that would well grace any man's house.\"", "“어느 남자의 집이든 넉넉히 빛내 줄 사람이오.”"]], [["\"And you really have the effrontery to tell me this,\"", "“그런데도 정말 뻔뻔스럽게 내게 그런 말씀을 하시는군요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; \"to tell me, who, as you very well know, set up to be a beauty myself, and who am at this very moment taking such an interest in your affairs, you really have the effrontery to tell me that Mrs. Bold is the most beautiful woman you know.\"", "“당신도 잘 아시다시피 스스로 미인 행세를 하는 나에게, 게다가 바로 이 순간 당신 일에 이토록 관심을 쏟고 있는 나에게, 볼드 부인이 당신이 아는 가장 아름다운 여인이라고 정말 뻔뻔스럽게 말씀하시는군요.”"]], [["\"I did not say so,\" said Mr.", "“그렇게 말하지는 않았소.”"], ["Arabin; \"you are more beautiful--\"", "애러빈 씨가 말했다. “당신이 더 아름답소—”"]], [["\"Ah, come now, that is something like.", "“아, 이제야 좀 말이 되는군요."], ["I thought you could not be so unfeeling.\"", "나는 당신이 그렇게 무정할 리 없다고 생각했어요.”"]], [["\"You are more beautiful, perhaps more clever.\"", "“당신이 더 아름답고, 어쩌면 더 영리하기도 하오.”"]], [["\"Thank you, thank you, Mr.", "“고마워요, 고마워요, 애러빈 씨."], ["Arabin. I knew that you and I should be friends.\"", "당신과 내가 벗이 되리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었어요.”"]], [["\"But--\"", "“하지만—”"]], [["\"Not a word further.", "“한 마디도 더 하지 마세요."], ["I will not hear a word further.", "한 마디도 더 듣지 않겠어요."], ["If you talk till midnight you cannot improve what you have said.\"", "자정까지 말씀하셔도 방금 하신 말씀보다 나아질 수는 없으니까요.”"]], [["\"But Madame Neroni, Mrs. Bold--\"", "“하지만 마담 네로니, 볼드 부인은—”"]], [["\"I will not hear a word about Mrs. Bold.", "“볼드 부인에 대해서는 한 마디도 듣지 않겠어요."], ["Dread thoughts of strychnine did pass across my brain, but she is welcome to the second place.\"", "스트리크닌이라는 무시무시한 생각이 머리를 스쳐 가기는 했지만, 그분에게 둘째 자리쯤은 기꺼이 내드리죠.”"]], [["\"Her place--\"", "“그 사람의 자리는—”"]], [["\"I won't hear anything about her or her place.", "“그분에 대해서도 그분 자리에 대해서도 아무것도 듣지 않겠어요."], ["I am satisfied, and that is enough. But Mr.", "나는 만족했고, 그것으로 되었어요."], ["Arabin, I am dying with hunger; beautiful and clever as I am, you know I cannot go to my food, and yet you do not bring it to me.\"", "그런데 애러빈 씨, 나는 배가 고파 죽을 지경이랍니다. 아름답고 영리하기는 해도 내가 음식이 있는 데로 갈 수 없다는 것을 아시면서, 그런데도 내게 음식을 가져다주지 않으시는군요.”"]], [["This at any rate was so true as to make it necessary that Mr. Arabin should act upon it, and he accordingly went into the dining-room and supplied the signora's wants.", "이것만은 어쨌든 사실이어서 애러빈 씨는 그에 따라 움직이지 않을 수 없었고, 그리하여 식당으로 가서 시뇨라가 원하는 것을 챙겨 왔다."]], [["\"And yourself?\"", "“당신 것은요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh,\"", "“아,”"], ["said he, \"I am not hungry.", "그가 말했다. “나는 시장하지 않소."], ["I never eat at this hour.\"", "이 시각에는 원래 먹지 않소.”"]], [["\"Come, come, Mr. Arabin, don't let love interfere with your appetite.", "“자, 자, 애러빈 씨, 사랑 때문에 입맛을 잃으시면 못써요."], ["It never does with mine.", "내 입맛은 한 번도 그런 적이 없답니다."], ["Give me half a glass more champagne and then go to the table.", "샴페인을 반 잔만 더 주시고, 그러고는 식탁으로 가세요."], ["Mrs. Bold will do me an injury if you stay talking to me any longer.\"", "당신이 여기서 나와 더 오래 이야기하고 있으면 볼드 부인이 나를 해코지할 거예요.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin did as he was bid.", "애러빈 씨는 시키는 대로 했다."], ["He took her plate and glass from her and, going into the dining-room, helped himself to a sandwich from the crowded table and began munching it in a corner.", "그는 그녀에게서 접시와 잔을 받아 들고 식당으로 가서, 사람으로 붐비는 식탁에서 샌드위치 하나를 집어 구석에서 우물거리기 시작했다."]], [["As he was doing so Miss Thorne, who had hardly sat down for a moment, came into the room and, seeing him standing, was greatly distressed.", "그가 그러고 있을 때, 잠시도 앉아 있지 못하던 손 양이 방으로 들어왔다가 그가 서 있는 것을 보고 몹시 난처해했다."]], [["\"Oh, my dear Mr. Arabin,\"", "“어머나, 애러빈 씨.”"], ["said she, \"have you never sat down yet?", "그녀가 말했다. “아직도 자리에 앉지 못하셨소?"], ["I am so distressed.", "나는 참으로 민망하구려."], ["You of all men, too.\"", "하필이면 다른 분도 아니고 당신이.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin assured her that he had only just come into the room.", "애러빈 씨는 방금 막 방에 들어왔을 뿐이라고 그녀를 안심시켰다."]], [["\"That is the very reason why you should lose no more time.", "“그러니 더더욱 한시도 지체하시면 아니 되지요."], ["Come, I'll make room for you. Thank'ee, my dear,\"", "자, 내가 자리를 마련해 드리리다."], ["she said, seeing that Mrs.", "고맙구려, 그대여.”"], ["Bold was making an attempt to move from her chair, \"but I would not for worlds see you stir, for all the ladies would think it necessary to follow.", "볼드 부인이 제 의자에서 일어나려는 기색을 보이자 그녀가 말했다. “허나 그대가 몸을 움직이는 것만은 무슨 일이 있어도 보고 싶지 않소."], ["But, perhaps, if Mr. Stanhope has done--just for a minute, Mr.", "그리하면 부인들이 모두 따라 일어나야 한다고 여길 터이니."], ["Stanhope, till I can get another chair.\"", "하지만 혹시 스탠호프 씨가 다 드셨다면, 잠깐이면 되오, 스탠호프 씨, 내가 의자를 하나 더 구해 올 동안만.”"]], [["And so Bertie had to rise to make way for his rival.", "그리하여 버티는 제 연적에게 자리를 내주려 일어서야 했다."], ["This he did, as he did everything, with an air of good-humoured pleasantry which made it impossible for Mr. Arabin to refuse the proffered seat.", "그는 무슨 일이든 그러하듯 그 일도 사람 좋게 유쾌한 태도로 해치웠고, 그 바람에 애러빈 씨는 내미는 자리를 마다할 수 없게 되었다."]], [["\"His bishopric let another take,\"", "“그의 직분은 타인이 취하게 하소서.”"], ["said Bertie, the quotation being certainly not very appropriate either for the occasion or the person spoken to.", "버티가 말했다. 그 인용은 이 자리에도 이 상대에게도 도무지 어울리는 것이 아니었다."], ["\"I have eaten and am satisfied; Mr.", "“저는 먹었고 만족했습니다."], ["Arabin, pray take my chair.", "애러빈 씨, 부디 제 의자에 앉으시지요."], ["I wish for your sake that it really was a bishop's seat.\"", "당신을 위해서라면 이것이 정말 주교의 자리였으면 좋겠군요.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin did sit down, and as he did so Mrs. Bold got up as though to follow her neighbour.", "애러빈 씨는 자리에 앉았고, 그가 앉자 볼드 부인은 옆 사람을 따라가려는 듯 일어섰다."]], [["\"Pray, pray don't move,\"", "“제발, 제발 움직이지 마시오.”"], ["said Miss Thorne, almost forcing Eleanor back into her chair.", "손 양이 엘리너를 거의 억지로 의자에 도로 앉히며 말했다."], ["\"Mr. Stanhope is not going to leave us.", "“스탠호프 씨는 우리를 떠나지 않을 것이오."], ["He will stand behind you like a true knight as he is. And now I think of it, Mr.", "진짜 기사답게 그대 뒤에 서 있을 테니."], ["Arabin, let me introduce you to Mr. Slope.", "그러고 보니 생각났소만, 애러빈 씨, 슬로프 씨를 소개해 드리리다."], ["Mr. Slope, Mr.", "슬로프 씨, 애러빈 씨요.”"], ["Arabin.\" And the two gentlemen bowed stiffly to each other across the lady whom they both intended to marry, while the other gentleman who also intended to marry her stood behind, watching them.", "그리하여 두 신사는 저희가 나란히 아내로 맞으려는 부인을 사이에 두고 서로 뻣뻣하게 고개를 숙였고, 그 부인을 아내로 맞으려는 또 한 신사는 뒤에 서서 그 광경을 지켜보았다."]], [["The two had never met each other before, and the present was certainly not a good opportunity for much cordial conversation, even if cordial conversation between them had been possible.", "두 사람은 전에 서로 만난 적이 없었고, 설령 그들 사이에 정다운 대화가 가능했다 할지라도 지금은 결코 그럴 만한 자리가 아니었다."], ["As it was, the whole four who formed the party seemed as though their tongues were tied.", "사정이 그러하니, 그 자리를 이룬 네 사람은 하나같이 혀가 묶인 것 같았다."], ["Mr. Slope, who was wide awake to what he hoped was his coming opportunity, was not much concerned in the interest of the moment.", "슬로프 씨는 곧 다가오리라 바라 마지않는 기회에 온 신경이 깨어 있었으므로 지금 이 순간의 흥취에는 별 관심이 없었다."], ["His wish was to see Eleanor move, that he might pursue her.", "그의 바람은 엘리너가 자리를 뜨는 것을 보고 그 뒤를 쫓는 것이었다."], ["Bertie was not exactly in the same frame of mind; the evil day was near enough; there was no reason why he should precipitate it.", "버티는 마음가짐이 꼭 그렇지는 않았다. 궂은 날은 이미 충분히 가까웠으니 그것을 앞당길 까닭이 없었다."], ["He had made up his mind to marry Eleanor Bold if he could, and was resolved to-day to take the first preliminary step towards doing so.", "그는 할 수만 있다면 엘리너 볼드와 결혼하기로 마음을 정해 두었고, 오늘 그리로 가는 첫 준비 걸음을 떼기로 작정한 터였다."], ["But there was time enough before him.", "그러나 시간은 넉넉했다."], ["He was not going to make an offer of marriage over the table-cloth. Having thus good-naturedly made way for Mr.", "식탁보를 사이에 두고 청혼을 할 생각은 없었다."], ["Arabin, he was willing also to let him talk to the future Mrs. Stanhope as long as they remained in their present position.", "이렇게 사람 좋게 애러빈 씨에게 자리를 내준 김에, 그는 두 사람이 지금 자리에 그대로 있는 동안은 애러빈 씨가 장차의 스탠호프 부인과 이야기하도록 내버려 둘 마음도 있었다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, having bowed to Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨에게 고개를 숙인 뒤 더는 한 마디도 없이 음식을 먹기 시작했다."], ["Slope, began eating his food without saying a word further. He was full of thought, and though he ate he did so unconsciously.", "그는 생각으로 가득 차 있었고, 먹기는 하되 저도 모르게 먹었다."]], [["But poor Eleanor was the most to be pitied.", "그러나 가장 딱한 것은 가엾은 엘리너였다."], ["The only friend on whom she thought she could rely was Bertie Stanhope, and he, it seemed, was determined to desert her.", "기댈 수 있으리라 여겼던 단 하나의 벗이 버티 스탠호프였는데, 그마저 그녀를 저버리기로 작정한 듯했다."], ["Mr. Arabin did not attempt to address her.", "애러빈 씨는 그녀에게 말을 붙이려 하지 않았다."], ["She said a few words in reply to some remarks from Mr. Slope and then, feeling the situation too much for her, started from her chair in spite of Miss Thorne and hurried from the room.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨의 몇 마디에 몇 마디로 답하다가, 그 자리를 더는 견딜 수 없어 손 양이 말리는데도 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 방을 빠져나갔다."], ["Mr. Slope followed her, and young Stanhope lost the occasion.", "슬로프 씨가 그 뒤를 따랐고, 젊은 스탠호프는 기회를 놓쳤다."]], [["Madeline Neroni, when she was left alone, could not help pondering much on the singular interview she had had with this singular man.", "마들린 네로니는 혼자 남게 되자, 그 남다른 남자와 나눈 남다른 면담을 두고 곰곰 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Not a word that she had spoken to him had been intended by her to be received as true, and yet he had answered her in the very spirit of truth.", "그에게 한 말 가운데 진실로 받아들여지기를 바라고 한 말은 한 마디도 없었건만, 그는 그녀에게 진실 그 자체의 정신으로 대답했다."], ["He had done so, and she had been aware that he had so done.", "그는 그렇게 했고, 그녀도 그가 그렇게 했음을 알고 있었다."], ["She had wormed from him his secret, and he, debarred as it would seem from man's usual privilege of lying, had innocently laid bare his whole soul to her.", "그녀는 그에게서 비밀을 캐냈고, 그는 거짓말이라는 남자의 흔한 특권을 빼앗기기라도 한 듯 순진하게 제 영혼을 통째로 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 보였다."], ["He loved Eleanor Bold, but Eleanor was not in his eye so beautiful as herself.", "그는 엘리너 볼드를 사랑하지만, 그의 눈에 엘리너는 그녀만큼 아름답지 않았다."], ["He would fain have Eleanor for his wife, but yet he had acknowledged that she was the less gifted of the two.", "그는 엘리너를 아내로 삼고 싶어 하면서도 그녀가 둘 중 덜 뛰어난 쪽이라고 인정했다."], ["The man had literally been unable to falsify his thoughts when questioned, and had been compelled to be true malgré lui, even when truth must have been so disagreeable to him.", "그 남자는 질문을 받자 문자 그대로 제 생각을 거짓으로 꾸미지 못했고, 진실이 그토록 껄끄러울 수밖에 없는 자리에서조차 malgré lui 진실할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["This teacher of men, this Oxford pundit, this double-distilled quintessence of university perfection, this writer of religious treatises, this speaker of ecclesiastical speeches, had been like a little child in her hands; she had turned him inside out and read his very heart as she might have done that of a young girl.", "사람을 가르치는 이 스승, 이 옥스퍼드의 석학, 대학이 이룬 완성의 이 두 번 걸러 낸 정수, 종교 논설의 이 저술가, 교회 연설의 이 웅변가가 그녀의 손안에서는 어린아이 같았다. 그녀는 그를 뒤집어 놓고, 어린 처녀의 마음을 읽듯 그의 마음속을 그대로 읽어 냈다."], ["She could not but despise him for his facile openness, and yet she liked him for it, too.", "그 손쉬운 솔직함 때문에 그를 얕보지 않을 수 없었으나, 또 그 때문에 그가 마음에 들기도 했다."], ["It was a novelty to her, a new trait in a man's character.", "그것은 그녀에게 새로운 것이었고, 남자의 성품에서 여태 보지 못한 새로운 결이었다."], ["She felt also that she could never so completely make a fool of him as she did of the Slopes and Thornes.", "그녀는 또한 슬로프나 손 같은 이들에게 하듯 그를 완전히 바보로 만들 수는 결코 없으리라고 느꼈다."], ["She felt that she never could induce Mr. Arabin to make protestations to her that were not true, or to listen to nonsense that was mere nonsense.", "애러빈 씨에게 진실이 아닌 언약을 늘어놓게 하거나, 순전한 헛소리를 헛소리인 줄 알면서 듣고 있게 만들 수는 결코 없으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["It was quite clear that Mr. Arabin was heartily in love with Mrs.", "애러빈 씨가 볼드 부인을 진심으로 사랑하고 있다는 것은 아주 분명했다."], ["Bold; and the signora, with very unwonted good nature, began to turn it over in her mind whether she could not do him a good turn.", "그리하여 시뇨라는 평소답지 않게 인정을 발휘하여, 그에게 좋은 일을 하나 해 줄 수는 없을까 하고 마음속으로 굴려 보기 시작했다."], ["Of course Bertie was to have the first chance.", "물론 첫 기회는 버티의 것이었다."], ["It was an understood family arrangement that her brother was, if possible, to marry the Widow Bold.", "될 수만 있다면 아우가 볼드 미망인과 결혼해야 한다는 것은 집안에서 이미 합의된 일이었다."], ["Madeline knew too well his necessities and what was due to her sister to interfere with so excellent a plan, as long as it might be feasible.", "마들린은 아우의 궁핍한 사정도, 언니에게 마땅히 돌아가야 할 도리도 너무 잘 알고 있었으므로, 그 훌륭한 계획이 될 법한 동안에는 끼어들 마음이 없었다."], ["But she had strong suspicion that it was not feasible. She did not think it likely that Mrs.", "그러나 그 계획이 될 법하지 않다는 강한 의심을 품고 있었다."], ["Bold would accept a man in her brother's position, and she had frequently said so to Charlotte. She was inclined to believe that Mr.", "그녀는 볼드 부인이 제 아우 같은 처지의 남자를 받아들일 성싶지 않다고 여겼고, 샬럿에게도 그렇게 여러 번 말한 터였다."], ["Slope had more chance of success, and with her it would be a labour of love to rob Mr. Slope of his wife.", "오히려 슬로프 씨 쪽이 성공할 가망이 더 크다고 보았는데, 그녀로서는 슬로프 씨에게서 아내를 빼앗는 일이라면 사랑으로 하는 수고였다."]], [["And so the signora resolved, should Bertie fail, to do a good-natured act for once in her life and give up Mr. Arabin to the woman whom he loved.", "그리하여 시뇨라는, 버티가 실패한다면 평생 한 번쯤 인정 있는 짓을 해서 애러빈 씨를 그가 사랑하는 여인에게 넘겨주기로 마음먹었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX. The Lookalofts and the Greenacres", "# 제39장. 룩어로프트 일가와 그린에이커 일가"]], [["On the whole, Miss Thorne's provision for the amusement and feeding of the outer classes in the exoteric paddock was not unsuccessful.", "대체로 보아, 바깥 방목장에 모인 바깥 계층 사람들을 즐겁게 하고 먹이는 일에 대한 손 양의 채비는 실패라 할 수 없었다."]], [["Two little drawbacks to the general happiness did take place, but they were of a temporary nature, and apparent rather than real.", "전반적인 즐거움에 자그마한 흠 두 가지가 생기기는 했으나, 그것은 일시적인 것이었고 실재라기보다 겉보기의 것이었다."], ["The first was the downfall of young Harry Greenacre, and the other the uprise of Mrs. Lookaloft and her family.", "첫째는 젊은 해리 그린에이커의 추락이었고, 다른 하나는 룩어로프트 부인과 그 식구들의 상승이었다."]], [["As to the quintain, it became more popular among the boys on foot than it would ever have been among the men on horseback, even had young Greenacre been more successful.", "퀸틴으로 말하자면, 그것은 젊은 그린에이커가 훨씬 잘해 냈다 한들 말 탄 사내들 사이에서 누렸을 인기보다 더 큰 인기를 걸어 다니는 소년들 사이에서 누리게 되었다."], ["It was twirled round and round till it was nearly twirled out of the ground, and the bag of flour was used with great gusto in powdering the backs and heads of all who could be coaxed within its vicinity.", "그것은 땅에서 거의 뽑혀 나갈 지경이 되도록 뱅뱅 돌아갔고, 밀가루 자루는 그 근처로 꾀어들일 수 있는 모든 이의 등과 머리에 분칠을 하는 데 신나게 쓰였다."]], [["Of course it was reported all through the assemblage that Harry was dead, and there was a pathetic scene between him and his mother when it was found that he had escaped scatheless from the fall. A good deal of beer was drunk on the occasion, and the quintain was \"dratted\"", "물론 해리가 죽었다는 소문이 모인 사람들 사이에 두루 퍼졌고, 그가 그 낙마에서 아무 상처 없이 벗어났음이 밝혀졌을 때 그와 그 어머니 사이에 애처로운 장면이 벌어졌다."], ["and \"bothered,\" and very generally anathematized by all the mothers who had young sons likely to be placed in similar jeopardy.", "그 통에 맥주가 꽤 많이 소비되었고, 퀸틴은 “빌어먹을 것”이니 “성가신 것”이니 하는 소리를 들으며, 비슷한 위험에 처할 법한 어린 아들을 둔 모든 어머니에게서 두루 저주를 받았다."], ["But the affair of Mrs. Lookaloft was of a more serious nature.", "그러나 룩어로프트 부인의 일은 그보다 훨씬 심각한 성질의 것이었다."]], [["\"I do tell 'ee plainly--face to face--she be there in madam's drawing-room; herself and Gussy, and them two walloping gals, dressed up to their very eyeses.\" This was said by a very positive, very indignant, and very fat farmer's wife, who was sitting on the end of a bench leaning on the handle of a huge, cotton umbrella.", "“내 자네한테 똑똑히 말하네. 얼굴을 맞대고 말일세. 그 여편네가 마님네 응접실에 가 있다니까. 저하고 거시하고 그 덩치 큰 계집애 둘까지, 눈두덩까지 치장을 하고서 말이야.” 이렇게 말한 사람은 매우 단호하고 매우 분개하고 매우 살진 어느 농부의 아내였는데, 그는 긴 의자 끝에 앉아 커다란 무명 우산 손잡이에 몸을 기대고 있었다."]], [["\"But: you didn't zee her, Dame Guffern?\" said Mrs.", "“하지만 자네가 직접 본 건 아니잖은가, 구펀 댁?”"], ["Greenacre, whom this information, joined to the recent peril undergone by her son, almost overpowered.", "그린에이커 부인이 말했다."], ["Mr. Greenacre held just as much land as Mr.", "아들이 방금 겪은 위험에 이 소식까지 겹쳐 그는 거의 정신을 못 차릴 지경이었다."], ["Lookaloft, paid his rent quite as punctually, and his opinion in the vestry room was reckoned to be every whit as good. Mrs.", "그린에이커 씨는 룩어로프트 씨와 꼭 같은 넓이의 땅을 부치고 있었고, 소작료도 꼭 같이 꼬박꼬박 냈으며, 교구 회의실에서 그의 의견은 조금도 못하지 않게 대접받았다."], ["Lookaloft's rise in the world had been wormwood to Mrs. Greenacre.", "룩어로프트 부인이 세상에서 올라선 일은 그린에이커 부인에게 쓰디쓴 노릇이었다."], ["She had no taste herself for the sort of finery which had converted Barleystubb farm into Rosebank and which had occasionally graced Mr. Lookaloft's letters with the dignity of esquirehood.", "그 자신은 발리스터브 농장을 로즈뱅크로 바꾸어 놓고 이따금 룩어로프트 씨의 편지에 향사라는 위엄을 얹어 준 그런 종류의 겉치레에는 아무 취미가 없었다."], ["She had no wish to convert her own homestead into Violet Villa, or to see her goodman go about with a new-fangled handle to his name.", "제 살림집을 바이올릿 빌라로 바꾸고 싶은 마음도, 제 바깥양반이 이름 뒤에 새로 지어낸 꼬리표를 달고 다니는 꼴을 보고 싶은 마음도 없었다."], ["But it was a mortal injury to her that Mrs. Lookaloft should be successful in her hunt after such honours.", "그러나 룩어로프트 부인이 그런 영예를 사냥해 손에 넣는 데 성공한다는 것은 그에게 뼈에 사무치는 상처였다."], ["She had abused and ridiculed Mrs. Lookaloft to the extent of her little power.", "그는 제 작은 힘이 닿는 데까지 룩어로프트 부인을 헐뜯고 비웃어 왔다."], ["She had pushed against her going out of church, and had excused herself with all the easiness of equality.", "교회에서 나오면서 일부러 그를 밀치고는 대등한 사이다운 스스럼없는 태도로 사과하곤 했다."], ["\"Ah, dame, I axes pardon, but you be grown so mortal stout these times.\"", "“아이고 댁네, 미안하우."], ["She had inquired with apparent cordiality of Mr.", "근데 요새 댁네가 어찌나 몸이 나셨는지 원.”"], ["Lookaloft after \"the woman that owned him,\" and had, as she thought, been on the whole able to hold her own pretty well against her aspiring neighbour.", "그는 룩어로프트 씨를 보면 겉으로는 살갑게 “댁을 데리고 사는 그 여편네”의 안부를 묻곤 했고, 스스로 생각하기에 대체로 저 잘난 이웃을 상대로 제 몫을 꽤 잘 지켜 왔다."], ["Now, however, she found herself distinctly put into a separate and inferior class. Mrs.", "그러나 이제 그는 자기가 뚜렷이 따로 떨어진 낮은 계층으로 분류되었음을 알게 되었다."], ["Lookaloft was asked into the Ullathorne drawing-room merely because she called her house Rosebank and had talked over her husband into buying pianos and silk dresses instead of putting his money by to stock farms for his sons.", "룩어로프트 부인이 울라손 응접실로 청함을 받은 것은 오로지 제 집을 로즈뱅크라 부르고, 남편을 구슬려 아들들에게 농장을 마련해 줄 돈을 모으는 대신 피아노와 비단옷을 사게 만들었기 때문이었다."]], [["Mrs. Greenacre, much as she reverenced Miss Thorne, and highly as she respected her husband's landlord, could not but look on this as an act of injustice done to her and hers.", "그린에이커 부인은 손 양을 아무리 우러러보고 제 남편의 지주를 아무리 존경한다 해도, 이 일만은 자기와 제 식구들에게 저질러진 부당한 처사로 볼 수밖에 없었다."], ["Hitherto the Lookalofts had never been recognized as being of a different class from the Greenacres.", "지금까지 룩어로프트네가 그린에이커네와 다른 계층이라고 인정받은 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["Their pretensions were all self-pretensions, their finery was all paid for by themselves and not granted to them by others.", "그들의 잘난 체는 모조리 제가 저를 잘났다 하는 것이었고, 그들의 겉치레는 모조리 제 돈으로 산 것이지 남이 내려 준 것이 아니었다."], ["The local sovereigns of the vicinity, the district fountains of honour, had hitherto conferred on them the stamp of no rank.", "이 근방의 지역 군주들, 곧 이 고장에서 영예가 흘러나오는 샘들은 지금까지 그들에게 어떤 신분의 도장도 찍어 준 적이 없었다."], ["Hitherto their crinoline petticoats, late hours, and mincing gait had been a fair subject of Mrs. Greenacre's raillery, and this raillery had been a safety-valve for her envy.", "지금까지 그들의 크리놀린 속치마며 늦도록 자지 않는 버릇이며 종종거리는 걸음걸이는 그린에이커 부인이 놀려 먹기 좋은 재료였고, 그 놀림은 그의 시샘을 빼내는 안전판 노릇을 했다."], ["Now, however, and from henceforward, the case would be very different.", "그러나 이제, 그리고 이제부터는 사정이 아주 달라질 것이었다."], ["Now the Lookalofts would boast that their aspirations had been sanctioned by the gentry of the country; now they would declare with some show of truth that their claims to peculiar consideration had been recognized.", "이제 룩어로프트네는 제 야심이 이 고장 상류층의 재가를 받았노라고 뽐낼 것이고, 이제 그들은 저희가 각별한 대접을 받을 자격이 있다는 주장을 얼마간 사실인 양 내세울 것이었다."], ["They had sat as equal guests in the presence of bishops and baronets; they had been curtseyed to by Miss Thorne on her own drawing-room carpet; they were about to sit down to table in company with a live countess!", "그들은 주교와 준남작들 앞에 대등한 손님으로 앉았고, 손 양이 제 응접실 양탄자 위에서 그들에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했으며, 이제 살아 있는 백작부인과 한 상에 앉으려는 참이었다!"], ["Bab Lookaloft, as she had always been called by the young Greenacres in the days of their juvenile equality, might possibly sit next to the Honourable George, and that wretched Gussy might be permitted to hand a custard to the Lady Margaretta De Courcy.", "어린 시절 대등하던 때에 그린에이커네 아이들이 늘 그렇게 부르던 밥 룩어로프트가 어쩌면 아너러블 조지 옆에 앉을지도 몰랐고, 그 못난 거시가 레이디 마거리타 드 코시에게 커스터드를 건네도 좋다는 허락을 받을지도 몰랐다."]], [["The fruition of those honours, or such of them as fell to the lot of the envied family, was not such as should have caused much envy.", "그 영예의 열매는, 시샘받는 그 집안에 실제로 돌아간 몫으로 말하자면, 그다지 시샘할 만한 것이 못 되었다."], ["The attention paid to the Lookalofts by the De Courcys was very limited, and the amount of entertainment which they received from the bishop's society was hardly in itself a recompense for the dull monotony of their day. But of what they endured Mrs.", "드 코시 집안 사람들이 룩어로프트네에게 베푼 관심은 지극히 적었고, 주교 일행과 어울려 얻은 즐거움의 양도 그들이 보낸 하루의 따분한 단조로움에 대한 보상이 되기에는 어림없었다."], ["Greenacre took no account; she thought only of what she considered they must enjoy, and of the dreadfully exalted tone of living which would be manifested by the Rosebank family, as the consequence of their present distinction.", "그러나 그들이 견뎌 낸 것에 대해서는 그린에이커 부인은 조금도 셈에 넣지 않았다. 그는 오로지 그들이 틀림없이 누리고 있으리라 여기는 것만을, 그리고 이번 영광의 결과로 로즈뱅크 집안이 드러내 보일 무시무시하게 드높아진 생활의 어조만을 생각했다."]], [["\"But did 'ee zee 'em there, dame, did 'ee zee 'em there with your own eyes?\"", "“그래도 자네가 그이들을 거기서 봤는가, 댁네? 자네 두 눈으로 그이들이 거기 있는 걸 봤느냔 말일세.”"], ["asked poor Mrs.", "가엾은 그린에이커 부인이 물었다."], ["Greenacre, still hoping that there might be some ground for doubt.", "아직도 의심해 볼 만한 구석이 남아 있기를 바라면서였다."]], [["\"And how could I do that, unless so be I was there myself?\"", "“아니, 내가 거기 있지 않고서야 어찌 그럴 수 있겠나?”"], ["asked Mrs. Guffern.", "구펀 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I didn't zet eyes on none of them this blessed morning, but I zee'd them as did.", "“나야 이 좋은 아침나절에 그이들 얼굴 한 번 못 봤지. 하지만 본 사람을 내가 봤네."], ["You know our John; well, he will be for keeping company with Betsey Rusk, madam's own maid, you know.", "우리 존 알지? 그 녀석이 마님 몸종인 벳시 러스크하고 눈이 맞아 붙어 다니잖은가."], ["And Betsey isn't none of your common kitchen wenches.", "그 벳시가 여느 부엌데기 같은 아이가 아닐세."], ["So Betsey, she come out to our John, you know, and she's always vastly polite to me, is Betsey Rusk, I must say.", "그래 벳시가 우리 존한테 나오지 않았겠나. 그 애가 나한테는 늘 어찌나 깍듯한지 모르네, 벳시 러스크 그 애가 말이야."], ["So before she took so much as one turn with John she told me every ha'porth that was going on up in the house.\"", "그래 존하고 한 바퀴 돌기도 전에 나한테 저 위 저택에서 벌어지는 일을 반 푼어치까지 죄다 일러 주더군.”"]], [["\"Did she now?\"", "“그 애가 그러던가?”"], ["said Mrs. Greenacre.", "그린에이커 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed she did,\"", "“그럼, 그러고말고.”"], ["said Mrs. Guffern.", "구펀 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"And she told you them people was up there in the drawing-room?\"", "“그래 그 애가 그 사람들이 저 위 응접실에 있다고 하던가?”"]], [["\"She told me she zee'd 'em come in--that they was dressed finer by half nor any of the family, with all their neckses and buzoms stark naked as a born babby.\"", "“들어오는 걸 제 눈으로 봤다더군. 그 집 식구 누구보다도 갑절은 곱게 차려입고서, 목이며 가슴이며를 갓난애처럼 홀랑 드러내 놓고 말일세.”"]], [["\"The minxes!\" exclaimed Mrs.", "“저 발칙한 것들!”"], ["Greenacre, who felt herself more put about by this than any other mark of aristocratic distinction which her enemies had assumed.", "그린에이커 부인이 외쳤다. 원수들이 걸친 온갖 귀족 흉내 가운데 이것이 그를 가장 심란하게 했다."]], [["\"Yes, indeed,\"", "“암, 그렇고말고.”"], ["continued Mrs.", "구펀 부인이 말을 이었다. “아주 벗을 대로 벗고서 말일세."], ["Guffern, \"as naked as you please, while all the quality was dressed just as you and I be, Mrs. Greenacre.\"", "정작 귀인들은 자네나 나나 다름없이 차려입고 있었다지 뭔가, 그린에이커 댁.”"]], [["\"Drat their impudence,\" said Mrs.", "“뻔뻔스럽기도 하지.”"], ["Greenacre, from whose well-covered bosom all milk of human kindness was receding, as far as the family of the Lookalofts were concerned.", "그린에이커 부인이 말했다. 잘 여며 덮인 그의 가슴에서는 룩어로프트 집안에 관한 한 인정의 젖이 죄다 말라 가고 있었다."]], [["\"So says I,\" said Mrs.", "“내 말이 그 말일세.”"], ["Guffern; \"and so says my goodman, Thomas Guffern, when he hear'd it.", "구펀 부인이 말했다."], ["'Molly,' says he to me, 'if ever you takes to going about o'", "“우리 바깥양반 토머스 구펀도 그 얘기를 듣더니 똑같은 소리를 하더군."], ["mornings with yourself all naked in them ways, I begs you won't come back no more to the old house.'", "‘몰리,’ 그이가 나더러 그러데."], ["So says I, 'Thomas, no more I wull.' 'But,'", "‘자네가 만에 하나 아침나절에 그런 꼴로 홀랑 벗고 나다니게 되거든, 다시는 이 낡은 집으로 돌아오지 말게.’ 그래 내가 그랬지."], ["says he, 'drat it, how the deuce does she manage with her rheumatiz, and she not a rag on her;'\" and Mrs.", "‘토머스, 다시는 그럴 일 없으리다.’ 그랬더니 그이가 또, ‘그건 그렇고, 대체 그 여편네는 실오라기 하나 안 걸치고서 그 신경통을 어떻게 견딘담?’ 하더군.”"], ["Guffern laughed loudly as she thought of Mrs. Lookaloft's probable sufferings from rheumatic attacks.", "그러고서 구펀 부인은 룩어로프트 부인이 신경통 발작으로 겪을 법한 고생을 떠올리며 큰 소리로 웃었다."]], [["\"But to liken herself that way to folk that ha' blood in their veins,\"", "“그래도 그렇지, 핏줄에 진짜 피가 흐르는 사람들한테 저를 그렇게 견주다니.”"], ["said Mrs. Greenacre.", "그린에이커 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, but that warn't all neither that Betsey told.", "“그런데 벳시가 한 얘기가 그것뿐이 아닐세."], ["There they all swelled into madam's drawing-room, like so many turkey cocks, as much as to say, 'and who dare say no to us?'", "그이들이 죄다 칠면조 수컷처럼 부풀어서 마님네 응접실로 밀고 들어갔다는군. ‘누가 감히 우리더러 안 된다 하겠나?’ 하는 낯짝으로 말이야."], ["and Gregory was thinking of telling of 'em to come down here, only his heart failed him 'cause of the grand way they was dressed.", "그레고리가 그이들더러 이리 내려가라고 이를까 하다가, 워낙 거창하게 차려입은 통에 기가 죽어 못 그랬다지 뭔가."], ["So in they went, but madam looked at them as glum as death.\"", "그래 그대로 들어갔는데, 마님이 죽음처럼 침울한 낯으로 그이들을 쳐다보시더라네.”"]], [["\"Well, now,\" said Mrs.", "“그것 보게.”"], ["Greenacre, greatly relieved, \"so they wasn't axed different from us at all then?\"", "그린에이커 부인이 크게 안도하며 말했다. “그러니까 그이들도 우리하고 다르게 청함을 받은 게 아니었단 말이지?”"]], [["\"Betsey says that Gregory says that madam wasn't a bit too well pleased to see them where they was, and that to his believing they was expected to come here just like the rest of us.\"", "“벳시 말이, 그레고리가 그러는데 마님이 그이들이 거기 있는 걸 보고 조금도 달가워하지 않으셨다더군. 그레고리 생각에는 그이들도 우리 나머지처럼 이리로 오게 되어 있었다는 거야.”"]], [["There was great consolation in this.", "여기에는 큰 위로가 있었다."], ["Not that Mrs. Greenacre was altogether satisfied.", "그렇다고 그린에이커 부인이 아주 흡족해진 것은 아니었다."], ["She felt that justice to herself demanded that Mrs. Lookaloft should not only not be encouraged, but that she should also be absolutely punished.", "자기에게 정의가 이루어지려면 룩어로프트 부인이 부추김을 받지 않는 것만으로는 부족하고 아주 벌을 받아야 마땅하다고 그는 느꼈다."], ["What had been done at that scriptural banquet, of which Mrs. Greenacre so often read the account to her family?", "그린에이커 부인이 식구들에게 그토록 자주 읽어 주던 그 성경 속 잔치에서는 어떻게 하였던가?"], ["Why had not Miss Thorne boldly gone to the intruder and said, \"Friend, thou hast come up hither to high places not fitted to thee.", "어찌하여 손 양은 당당히 그 침입자에게 걸어가 이렇게 말하지 않았단 말인가. “벗이여, 그대는 그대에게 맞지 아니한 높은 자리에 올라왔도다."], ["Go down lower, and thou wilt find thy mates.\"", "더 낮은 데로 내려가라, 그리하면 그대의 짝들을 만나리라.”"], ["Let the Lookalofts be treated at the present moment with ever so cold a shoulder, they would still be enabled to boast hereafter of their position, their aspirations, and their honour.", "지금 이 순간 룩어로프트네가 아무리 냉대를 받는다 한들, 그들은 앞으로도 제 자리와 제 야심과 제 영예를 두고 뽐낼 수 있을 것이었다."]], [["\"Well, with all her grandeur, I do wonder that she be so mean,\"", "“그런 거창한 티를 다 내면서 어찌 그리 상스러울 수 있는지 나는 참 모르겠네.”"], ["continued Mrs. Greenacre, unable to dismiss the subject.", "그린에이커 부인이 이 이야기를 떨쳐 내지 못하고 말을 이었다."], ["\"Did you hear, goodman?\"", "“들었수, 영감?”"], ["she went on, about to repeat the whole story to her husband who then came up.", "그는 마침 다가온 남편에게 이 이야기를 죄다 되풀이할 참으로 말을 걸었다."], ["\"There's Dame Lookaloft and Bab and Gussy and the lot of 'em all sitting as grand as fivepence in madam's drawing-room, and they not axed no more nor you nor me.", "“룩어로프트 댁네하고 밥이하고 거시하고 그 집 식구들이 죄다 마님네 응접실에 앉아 오 펜스짜리 동전처럼 거들먹거리고 있다지 뭐요. 우리보다 더 청함을 받은 것도 아니면서 말이오."], ["Did you ever hear tell the like o' that?\"", "그런 소리 들어 본 적 있수?”"]], [["\"Well, and what for shouldn't they?\"", "“그래, 그이들이라고 그러지 말란 법이 어디 있소?”"], ["said Farmer Greenacre.", "농부 그린에이커가 말했다."]], [["\"Likening theyselves to the quality, as though they was estated folk, or the like o'", "“귀인들에게 저를 견주다니요."], ["that!\" said Mrs.", "무슨 땅마지기나 가진 사람들이라도 되는 것처럼 말이에요!”"], ["Guffern.", "구펀 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, if they likes it, and madam likes it, they's welcome for me,\"", "“뭐, 그이들이 좋다 하고 마님도 좋다 하시면 나야 얼마든지 좋소.”"], ["said the farmer.", "농부가 말했다."], ["\"Now I likes this place better, 'cause I be more at home-like, and don't have to pay for them fine clothes for the missus.", "“나로 말하면 여기가 더 좋소. 여기가 더 내 집 같고, 마누라한테 그런 좋은 옷을 사 입힐 일도 없으니 말이오."], ["Everyone to his taste, Mrs.", "사람마다 제 취향이 있는 법이오, 구펀 댁."], ["Guffern, and if neighbour Lookaloft thinks that he has the best of it, he's welcome.\"", "이웃 룩어로프트가 저희 쪽이 더 낫다고 여긴다면 그것도 얼마든지 좋소.”"]], [["Mrs.", "그린에이커 부인은 남편 옆에 앉아 잔치의 고된 일을 시작했고, 어느 정도는 마음의 평온을 되찾은 채로 그리했다."], ["Greenacre sat down by her husband's side to begin the heavy work of the banquet, and she did so in some measure with restored tranquillity, but nevertheless she shook her head at her gossip to show that in this instance she did not quite approve of her husband's doctrine.", "그러면서도 그는 이 일에서만은 남편의 지론에 썩 동의하지 않는다는 뜻으로 제 수다 동무를 향해 고개를 저었다."]], [["\"And I'll tell 'ee what, dames,\"", "“내 한마디 하리다, 아주머니들.”"], ["continued he; \"if so be that we cannot enjoy the dinner that madam gives us because Mother Lookaloft is sitting up there on a grand sofa, I think we ought all to go home.", "그가 말을 이었다. “룩어로프트 어멈이 저 위 훌륭한 소파에 앉아 있다고 해서 마님이 차려 주신 이 만찬을 즐길 수 없다면, 우리는 다 같이 집으로 돌아가는 게 옳소."], ["If we greet at that, what'll we do when true sorrow comes across us?", "그런 일에 운다면 참된 슬픔이 닥칠 때는 어쩌려고 그러오?"], ["How would you be now, Dame, if the boy there had broke his neck when he got the tumble?\"", "임자, 아까 그 애가 굴러떨어지면서 목이라도 부러졌다면 지금 어떻겠소?”"]], [["Mrs. Greenacre was humbled and said nothing further on the matter.", "그린에이커 부인은 기가 꺾여 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["But let prudent men such as Mr. Greenacre preach as they will, the family of the Lookalofts certainly does occasion a good deal of heart-burning in the world at large.", "그러나 그린에이커 씨 같은 사려 깊은 사람들이 아무리 설교를 한다 한들, 룩어로프트 같은 집안이 이 넓은 세상에 적잖은 속앓이를 일으키는 것만은 분명하다."]], [["It was pleasant to see Mr. Plomacy as, leaning on his stout stick, he went about among the rural guests, acting as a sort of head constable as well as master of the revels.", "플로머시 씨가 튼튼한 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 시골 손님들 사이를 돌아다니며 잔치의 사회자 노릇과 순경 우두머리 노릇을 겸하는 모습은 볼만했다."], ["\"Now, young'un, if you can't manage to get along without that screeching, you'd better go to the other side of the twelve-acre field and take your dinner with you.", "“이봐, 애송이, 그렇게 꽥꽥거리지 않고는 못 배기겠거든 열두 에이커 밭 저쪽으로 밥그릇을 들고 건너가는 게 좋을 걸세."], ["Come, girls, what do you stand there for, twirling of your thumbs?", "자, 처녀들, 손가락이나 배배 꼬면서 거기 왜들 서 있는가?"], ["Come out, and let the lads see you; you've no need to be so ashamed of your faces.", "나와서 총각들한테 얼굴을 보이게. 그렇게 부끄러워할 낯짝도 아니지 않은가."], ["Hollo there, who are you?", "여봐, 거기. 자네는 누구지?"], ["How did you make your way in here?\"", "여기는 어떻게 들어왔나?”"]], [["This last disagreeable question was put to a young man of about twenty-four who did not, in Mr. Plomacy's eye, bear sufficient vestiges of a rural education and residence.", "이 마지막의 달갑잖은 물음은 스물네 살쯤 된 젊은이에게 던져진 것이었는데, 플로머시 씨가 보기에 그 젊은이에게는 시골에서 배우고 시골에서 살아온 흔적이 넉넉하지 않았다."]], [["\"If you please, your Worship, Master Barrell the coachman let me in at the church wicket, 'cause I do be working mostly al'ays for the family.\"", "“황송합니다요, 나리. 마부 배럴 어른이 교회 쪽 쪽문으로 저를 들여보내 주셨습니다요. 제가 거의 늘 이 댁 일을 해 온 터라서요.”"]], [["\"Then Master Barrell the coachman may let you out again,\" said Mr.", "“그렇다면 마부 배럴 어른더러 도로 내보내 달라고 하게.”"], ["Plomacy, not even conciliated by the magisterial dignity which had been conceded to him.", "플로머시 씨가 말했다. 자기에게 바쳐진 치안판사 같은 위엄에도 그는 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."], ["\"What's your name?", "“이름이 무엇인가?"], ["And what trade are you?", "무슨 일을 하는 자인가?"], ["And who do you work for?\"", "그리고 누구 밑에서 일하는가?”"]], [["\"I'm Stubbs, your worship, Bob Stubbs; and--and--and--\"", "“저는 스텁스입니다요, 나리. 밥 스텁스입니다요. 그리고, 그리고, 그리고…”"]], [["\"And what's your trade, Stubbs?\"", "“그래 무슨 일을 하느냐고 물었네, 스텁스.”"]], [["\"Plasterer, please your worship.\"", "“미장이입니다요, 나리.”"]], [["\"I'll plaster you, and Barrell too; you'll just walk out of this 'ere field as quick as you walked in.", "“내가 자네를 발라 주지, 배럴까지 같이 발라 주지. 자네는 들어온 걸음 그대로 냉큼 이 밭에서 걸어 나가게."], ["We don't want no plasterers; when we do, we'll send for 'em.", "우리는 미장이 따위 필요 없네. 필요할 때는 사람을 보내 부를 걸세."], ["Come my buck, walk.\"", "자, 이보게, 걸으라니까.”"]], [["Stubbs the plasterer was much downcast at this dreadful edict.", "미장이 스텁스는 이 무서운 포고에 크게 풀이 죽었다."], ["He was a sprightly fellow, and had contrived since his ingress into the Ullathorne elysium to attract to himself a forest nymph, to whom he was whispering a plasterer's usual soft nothings, when he was encountered by the great Mr. Plomacy.", "그는 발랄한 사내였고, 울라손의 낙원에 들어선 이래 숲의 요정 하나를 제 곁으로 끌어들이는 데 성공하여 미장이들이 으레 하는 달콤하고 실없는 소리를 속삭이던 참이었는데, 하필 그때 위대한 플로머시 씨와 맞닥뜨린 것이었다."], ["It was dreadful to be thus dissevered from his dryad and sent howling back to a Barchester pandemonium just as the nectar and ambrosia were about to descend on the fields of asphodel.", "신들의 술과 신들의 음식이 아스포델 들판 위로 막 내려오려는 참에 제 나무 요정에게서 떼어져 울부짖으며 바체스터의 아수라장으로 쫓겨 간다는 것은 참혹한 일이었다."], ["He began to try what prayers would do, but city prayers were vain against the great rural potentate. Not only did Mr.", "그는 애원이 통할지 어떨지 시험해 보기 시작했으나, 도시의 애원은 이 위대한 시골 권세가 앞에서 헛일이었다."], ["Plomacy order his exit but, raising his stick to show the way which led to the gate that had been left in the custody of that false Cerberus Barrell, proceeded himself to see the edict of banishment carried out.", "플로머시 씨는 퇴장을 명했을 뿐 아니라, 저 사이비 케르베로스 배럴이 지키도록 맡겨 둔 대문으로 가는 길을 지팡이를 들어 가리키고는, 손수 그 추방령이 집행되는 것을 지켜보려고 따라나섰다."]], [["The goddess Mercy, however, the sweetest goddess that ever sat upon a cloud, and the dearest to poor, frail, erring man, appeared on the field in the person of Mr.", "그러나 구름 위에 앉은 여신 가운데 가장 다정하고, 가엾고 연약하고 허물 많은 인간에게 가장 소중한 여신인 자비의 여신이 그린에이커 씨의 모습으로 그 밭에 나타났다."], ["Greenacre. Never was interceding goddess more welcome.", "중재에 나선 여신이 이보다 더 반가운 적은 없었다."]], [["\"Come, man,\" said Mr.", "“여보시오, 이 사람아.”"], ["Greenacre, \"never stick at trifles such a day as this.", "그린에이커 씨가 말했다. “이런 날에 사소한 일로 걸고넘어지지 마시오."], ["I know the lad well.", "내가 저 아이를 잘 아오."], ["Let him bide at my axing.", "내가 청하는 것이니 그냥 두시구려."], ["Madam won't miss what he can eat and drink, I know.\"", "저 아이가 먹고 마셔야 얼마나 되겠소. 마님이 아쉬워하실 리 없다는 걸 나는 아오.”"]], [["Now Mr. Plomacy and Mr.", "그런데 플로머시 씨와 그린에이커 씨는 둘도 없는 벗이었다."], ["Greenacre were sworn friends. Mr.", "플로머시 씨는 울라손 저택에서 가장 안락한 방 하나를 제 마음대로 쓸 수 있었으나, 그는 독신이었고 그 방에 혼자였으며, 게다가 저택 안에서는 플로머시 씨라 해도 담배를 피울 수 없었다."], ["Plomacy had at his own disposal as comfortable a room as there was in Ullathorne House, but he was a bachelor, and alone there, and, moreover, smoking in the house was not allowed even to Mr. Plomacy.", "그가 참된 행복을 누리는 순간은 그린에이커 부인의 티 없이 깨끗한 앞부엌, 그중에서도 가장 따뜻한 구석에 놓인 커다란 안락의자에서 흘러갔다."], ["His moments of truest happiness were spent in a huge armchair in the warmest corner of Mrs. Greenacre's beautifully clean front kitchen.", "속에 든 사람이 녹아내려 유쾌한 이야기의 냇물이 되어 흘러나오는 곳이 바로 거기였고, 존경을 받으면서도 마음이 편안한 곳이 거기였으며, 윗사람의 위엄을 거스르지도 않고 아랫사람의 허물없음을 불러들이지도 않으면서 삶의 온갖 격식에서 짐을 벗을 수 있는 곳이 거기, 어쩌면 오직 거기뿐이었다."], ["'Twas there that the inner man dissolved itself and poured itself out in streams of pleasant chat; 'twas there that he was respected and yet at his ease; 'twas there, and perhaps there only, that he could unburden himself from the ceremonies of life without offending the dignity of those above him, or incurring the familiarity of those below. 'Twas there that his long pipe was always to be found on the accustomed chimney-board, not only permitted but encouraged.", "그의 긴 담뱃대가 늘 정해진 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있는 곳도 거기였는데, 그것은 허락받은 정도가 아니라 권장되기까지 했다."]], [["Such being the state of the case, it was not to be supposed that Mr. Plomacy could refuse such a favour to Mr.", "사정이 이러하였으니 플로머시 씨가 그린에이커 씨에게 그만한 부탁을 거절하리라고는 생각할 수 없었다."], ["Greenacre; but nevertheless he did not grant it without some further show of austere authority.", "그러나 그렇다 해도 그는 준엄한 권위를 조금 더 내보이지 않고서는 그 부탁을 들어주지 않았다."]], [["\"Eat and drink, Mr.", "“먹고 마시는 것이라니요, 그린에이커 씨!"], ["Greenacre! No.", "아니지요."], ["It's not what he eats and drinks, but the example such a chap shows, coming in where he's not invited--a chap of his age, too.", "저 녀석이 먹고 마시는 게 문제가 아니라, 청하지도 않은 자리에 들어와서 저런 녀석이 보이는 본보기가 문제요. 그것도 저만한 나이를 먹은 녀석이 말이오."], ["He too that never did a day's work about Ullathorne since he was born.", "게다가 태어난 뒤로 울라손에서 하루도 일해 본 적 없는 녀석이오."], ["Plasterer!", "미장이라!"], ["I'll plaster him!\"", "내가 발라 주지!”"]], [["\"He worked long enough for me, then, Mr. Plomacy.", "“그러면 우리 집 일은 오래도록 해 주었소, 플로머시 씨."], ["And a good hand he is at setting tiles as any in Barchester,\"", "기와 얹는 솜씨로 말하면 바체스터에서 저만한 사람이 없소.”"], ["said the other, not sticking quite to veracity, as indeed mercy never should.", "상대가 말했다. 진실에 그리 충실한 말은 아니었으나, 자비란 본디 그래야 하는 법이다."], ["\"Come, come, let him alone to-day and quarrel with him to-morrow.", "“자, 자, 오늘은 그냥 두고 싸움은 내일 하시구려."], ["You wouldn't shame him before his lass there?\"", "저기 저 처녀 앞에서 저 아이를 망신 주고 싶지는 않을 것 아니오?”"]], [["\"It goes against the grain with me, then,\"", "“내 성미에는 영 맞지 않는 일이오만.”"], ["said Mr. Plomacy.", "플로머시 씨가 말했다."], ["\"And take care, you Stubbs, and behave yourself.", "“그리고 이봐 스텁스, 조심해서 얌전히 굴게."], ["If I hear a row, I shall know where it comes from.", "소란이 났다는 소리가 들리면 그게 어디서 난 것인지 내가 다 알 걸세."], ["I'm up to you Barchester journeymen; I know what stuff you're made of.\"", "나는 자네 같은 바체스터 직공들을 훤히 꿰고 있네. 자네들이 어떤 물건으로 만들어졌는지 다 알고 있네.”"]], [["And so Stubbs went off happy, pulling at the forelock of his shock head of hair in honour of the steward's clemency and giving another double pull at it in honour of the farmer's kindness.", "그리하여 스텁스는 기분 좋게 물러갔다. 집사의 관대함에 경의를 표하며 제 텁수룩한 머리의 앞머리를 잡아당겼고, 농부의 친절에 경의를 표하며 한 번 더, 그것도 두 배로 잡아당겼다."], ["And as he went he swore within his grateful heart that if ever Farmer Greenacre wanted a day's work done for nothing, he was the lad to do it for him.", "그리고 걸어가면서 그는 고마움에 겨운 마음속으로 맹세하기를, 언제든 농부 그린에이커가 품삯 없이 하루치 일을 시키고 싶어 하거든 그 일을 해 줄 사람은 바로 저라고 했다."], ["Which promise it was not probable that he would ever be called on to perform.", "그러나 그 약속을 실제로 이행하라는 부름을 받을 일은 아마 없을 것이었다."]], [["But Mr. Plomacy was not quite happy in his mind, for he thought of the unjust steward and began to reflect whether he had not made for himself friends of the mammon of unrighteousness.", "그러나 플로머시 씨는 마음이 아주 편하지는 않았으니, 불의한 청지기가 떠올랐고 자기가 불의의 재물로 친구를 사귄 것은 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작한 까닭이었다."], ["This, however, did not interfere with the manner in which he performed his duties at the bottom of the long board; nor did Mr.", "그렇더라도 그 때문에 긴 식탁의 아래쪽 끝에서 제 소임을 다하는 그의 태도가 흐트러지지는 않았다."], ["Greenacre perform his the worse at the top on account of the good wishes of Stubbs the plasterer.", "미장이 스텁스의 축원을 받았다 하여 그린에이커 씨가 식탁 위쪽 끝에서 제 소임을 더 못하게 된 것도 아니었다."], ["Moreover the guests did not think it anything amiss when Mr. Plomacy, rising to say grace, prayed that God would make them all truly thankful for the good things which Madame Thorne in her great liberality had set before them!", "게다가 플로머시 씨가 일어나 식전 감사 기도를 올리면서, 손 마님께서 그 크신 너그러움으로 우리 앞에 차려 주신 좋은 것들에 대하여 우리 모두가 참으로 감사하게 하여 주시기를 하느님께 빌었을 때에도, 손님들은 그것을 조금도 잘못이라 여기지 않았다!"]], [["All this time the quality in the tent on the lawn were getting on swimmingly--that is, if champagne without restriction can enable quality folk to swim.", "그동안 잔디밭 천막 안의 귀인들은 술술 잘 헤엄쳐 나가고 있었다. 물론 무제한으로 나오는 샴페인이 귀인들을 헤엄치게 할 수 있다면 말이다."], ["Sir Harkaway Gorse proposed the health of Miss Thorne, and likened her to a blood race-horse, always in condition and not to be tired down by any amount of work.", "하커웨이 고스 경이 손 양의 건강을 위해 잔을 들자고 제안하면서, 그를 언제나 몸이 성하고 아무리 일을 시켜도 지치는 법이 없는 순종 경주마에 비겼다."], ["Mr. Thorne returned thanks, saying he hoped his sister would always be found able to run when called upon, and then gave the health and prosperity of the De Courcy family.", "손 씨가 답사를 하며, 제 누이가 부름을 받을 때면 언제나 달릴 수 있는 사람으로 남기를 바란다고 말한 뒤 드 코시 집안의 건강과 번영을 위해 잔을 들자고 했다."], ["His sister was very much honoured by seeing so many of them at her poor board.", "제 누이는 그 초라한 식탁에 그 집안 분들이 이토록 많이 와 주신 것을 크나큰 영광으로 여긴다고 했다."], ["They were all aware that important avocations made the absence of the earl necessary.", "중요한 공무 때문에 백작께서 부득이 자리를 비우실 수밖에 없었음은 여러분이 다 아시는 바라고 했다."], ["As his duty to his prince had called him from his family hearth, he, Mr. Thorne, could not venture to regret that he did not see him at Ullathorne; but nevertheless he would venture to say--that was, to express a wish--an opinion, he meant to say--And so Mr.", "임금을 섬기는 도리가 그분을 제 집 화롯가에서 불러낸 것이니, 자기로서는 그분을 울라손에서 뵙지 못한 것을 감히 유감이라 할 수 없으나, 그럼에도 감히 말씀드리자면, 그러니까 한 가지 바람을 말씀드리자면, 아니 의견을 말씀드리려던 것인데."], ["Thorne became somewhat gravelled, as country gentlemen in similar circumstances usually do; but he ultimately sat down, declaring that he had much satisfaction in drinking the noble earl's health, together with that of the countess, and all the family of De Courcy Castle.", "이리하여 손 씨는 이런 처지에 놓인 시골 신사들이 으레 그러하듯 다소 말이 막히고 말았다. 그러나 마침내 그는 저 고귀한 백작과 백작부인과 드 코시 성의 온 집안의 건강을 위해 잔을 드는 것이 참으로 흡족하다고 선언하며 자리에 앉았다."]], [["And then the Honourable George returned thanks.", "그다음에는 아너러블 조지가 답사를 했다."], ["We will not follow him through the different periods of his somewhat irregular eloquence.", "다소 고르지 못한 그의 웅변이 거쳐 간 여러 대목을 우리는 하나하나 따라가지 않으려 한다."], ["Those immediately in his neighbourhood found it at first rather difficult to get him on his legs, but much greater difficulty was soon experienced in inducing him to resume his seat.", "바로 곁에 있던 이들은 처음에는 그를 일으켜 세우기가 꽤 어렵다고 여겼으나, 곧이어 그를 다시 자리에 앉히는 데에서 훨씬 더 큰 어려움을 겪었다."], ["One of two arrangements should certainly be made in these days: either let all speech-making on festive occasions be utterly tabooed and made as it were impossible; or else let those who are to exercise the privilege be first subjected to a competing examination before the civil-service examining commissioners.", "요즘 같은 시대에는 둘 중 하나를 반드시 정해 두어야 한다. 잔치 자리에서의 연설을 아예 금기로 삼아 사실상 불가능하게 만들거나, 아니면 그 특권을 행사할 사람들에게 먼저 문관 임용 시험 위원들 앞에서 경쟁 시험을 치르게 하거나."], ["As it is now, the Honourable Georges do but little honour to our exertions in favour of British education.", "지금 형편으로는 아너러블 조지 같은 이들이 영국 교육을 위한 우리의 노고에 명예를 거의 돌려주지 못한다."]], [["In the dining-room the bishop went through the honours of the day with much more neatness and propriety.", "식당에서는 주교가 훨씬 더 말끔하고 격에 맞게 그날의 예를 치렀다."], ["He also drank Miss Thorne's health, and did it in a manner becoming the bench which he adorned.", "그 역시 손 양의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었는데, 자기가 빛내고 있는 주교석에 어울리는 태도로 그리했다."], ["The party there was perhaps a little more dull, a shade less lively than that in the tent.", "그곳의 자리는 아마도 천막 쪽보다 조금 더 지루하고 한 겹쯤 덜 활기찼을 것이다."], ["But what was lost in mirth was fully made up in decorum.", "그러나 흥에서 잃은 것은 예법에서 남김없이 채워졌다."]], [["And so the banquets passed off at the various tables with great éclat and universal delight.", "이리하여 여러 식탁에서 잔치는 큰 성황과 두루 미치는 기쁨 속에 끝이 났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL. Ullathorne Sports--Act II", "# 제40장. 울라손 운동회 — 제2막"]], [["\"That which has made them drunk has made me bold.\"", "“그들을 취하게 한 것이 나를 담대하게 하였도다.”"], ["'Twas thus that Mr. Slope encouraged himself, as he left the dining-room in pursuit of Eleanor.", "엘리너를 뒤쫓아 식당을 나서면서 슬로프 씨는 이런 말로 스스로를 북돋웠다."], ["He had not indeed seen in that room any person really intoxicated, but there had been a good deal of wine drunk, and Mr.", "사실 그 방에서 정말로 취한 사람을 본 것은 아니었으나 포도주가 꽤 많이 비워졌고, 슬로프 씨도 제 몫을 사양하지 않았다."], ["Slope had not hesitated to take his share, in order to screw himself up to the undertaking which he had in hand.", "손에 쥔 일을 치를 만큼 스스로를 조여 두기 위해서였다."], ["He is not the first man who has thought it expedient to call in the assistance of Bacchus on such an occasion.", "이런 자리에서 바쿠스의 도움을 청하는 편이 상책이라 여긴 사람이 그가 처음은 아니다."]], [["Eleanor was out through the window and on the grass before she perceived that she was followed.", "엘리너는 누군가 뒤따라오고 있다는 것을 알아채기도 전에 이미 창문을 지나 잔디밭에 나와 있었다."], ["Just at that moment the guests were nearly all occupied at the tables.", "마침 그때 손님들은 거의 모두 식탁에 붙들려 있었다."], ["Here and there were to be seen a constant couple or two, who preferred their own sweet discourse to the jingle of glasses or the charms of rhetoric which fell from the mouths of the Honourable George and the Bishop of Barchester; but the grounds were as nearly vacant as Mr.", "여기저기 다정한 남녀 한두 쌍이 눈에 띄기는 했으니, 잔 부딪는 소리나 아너러블 조지와 바체스터 주교의 입에서 흘러나오는 웅변의 매력보다 자기들끼리의 달콤한 이야기를 더 좋아하는 이들이었다."], ["Slope could wish them to be.", "그러나 정원은 슬로프 씨가 바랄 수 있는 만큼 거의 비어 있었다."]], [["Eleanor saw that she was pursued, and as a deer, when escape is no longer possible, will turn to bay and attack the hounds, so did she turn upon Mr. Slope.", "엘리너는 자기가 쫓기고 있음을 알았고, 더는 달아날 수 없게 된 사슴이 몸을 돌려 사냥개들에게 맞서듯 슬로프 씨에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["\"Pray don't let me take you from the room,\"", "“저 때문에 방에서 나오시게 할 마음은 없어요.”"], ["said she, speaking with all the stiffness which she knew how to use.", "그녀가 자기가 낼 수 있는 온갖 뻣뻣함을 실어 말했다."], ["\"I have come out to look for a friend.", "“저는 벗을 찾으러 나온 것이랍니다."], ["I must beg of you, Mr. Slope, to go back.\"", "슬로프 씨, 부디 돌아가 주시기를 청합니다.”"]], [["But Mr. Slope would not be thus entreated.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 그만한 청에 물러설 사람이 아니었다."], ["He had observed all day that Mrs. Bold was not cordial to him, and this had to a certain extent oppressed him.", "그는 하루 종일 볼드 부인이 자기에게 살갑지 않다는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 그 때문에 어느 정도 마음이 눌려 있기는 했다."], ["But he did not deduce from this any assurance that his aspirations were in vain.", "그러나 그 사실에서 제 소망이 헛되다는 확신을 끌어내지는 않았다."], ["He saw that she was angry with him.", "그가 보기에 그녀는 자기에게 화가 나 있었다."], ["Might she not be so because he had so long tampered with her feelings--might it not arise from his having, as he knew was the case, caused her name to be bruited about in conjunction with his own without having given her the opportunity of confessing to the world that henceforth their names were to be one and the same?", "그런데 그것은 그가 그토록 오래 그녀의 마음을 건드려 놓기만 한 탓이 아니겠는가—이제부터 두 사람의 이름이 하나가 되리라고 세상에 고백할 기회는 주지 않은 채, 스스로도 알고 있듯이 그녀의 이름을 제 이름과 나란히 사람들 입에 오르내리게 만든 탓에서 온 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["Poor lady.", "가엾은 부인."], ["He had within him a certain Christian conscience-stricken feeling of remorse on this head.", "이 대목에 관해서라면 그의 속에도 그리스도인다운 양심의 가책 비슷한 것이 없지는 않았다."], ["It might be that he had wronged her by his tardiness.", "자기가 꾸물거린 탓에 그녀에게 못할 짓을 했는지도 몰랐다."], ["He had, however, at the present moment imbibed too much of Mr. Thorne's champagne to have any inward misgivings.", "그러나 지금 이 순간 그는 손 씨의 샴페인을 너무 많이 들이켠 터라 속으로 흔들릴 여지가 없었다."], ["He was right in repeating the boast of Lady Macbeth: he was not drunk, but he was bold enough for anything.", "레이디 맥베스의 호언을 되뇐 것은 옳았다. 그는 취하지 않았으되 무슨 짓이든 할 만큼 담대했다."], ["It was a pity that in such a state he could not have encountered Mrs. Proudie.", "그런 상태로 프라우디 부인과 마주치지 못한 것이 애석할 따름이다."]], [["\"You must permit me to attend you,\"", "“제가 모시고 가도록 허락해 주셔야 합니다.”"], ["said he; \"I could not think of allowing you to go alone.\"", "그가 말했다. “혼자 가시게 두는 일은 생각조차 할 수 없어요.”"]], [["\"Indeed you must, Mr. Slope,\"", "“아니요, 그리하셔야 해요, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["said Eleanor still very stiffly, \"for it is my special wish to be alone.\"", "엘리너가 여전히 몹시 뻣뻣하게 말했다. “혼자 있고 싶다는 것이 제 각별한 바람이니까요.”"]], [["The time for letting the great secret escape him had already come. Mr.", "큰 비밀을 입 밖에 낼 때가 이미 와 있었다."], ["Slope saw that it must be now or never, and he was determined that it should be now.", "슬로프 씨는 지금이 아니면 영영 없다는 것을 알았고, 지금으로 하기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["This was not his first attempt at winning a fair lady.", "아리따운 여인의 마음을 얻어 보려 나선 것이 이번이 처음은 아니었다."], ["He had been on his knees, looked unutterable things with his eyes, and whispered honeyed words before this.", "그는 전에도 무릎을 꿇어 보았고, 말로 옮길 수 없는 것들을 눈빛에 담아 보았으며, 꿀 바른 말을 속삭여도 보았다."], ["Indeed, he was somewhat an adept at these things, and had only to adapt to the perhaps different taste of Mrs. Bold the well-remembered rhapsodies which had once so much gratified Olivia Proudie.", "실상 이런 일에는 제법 이력이 난 사람이라, 한때 올리비아 프라우디를 그토록 흡족하게 했고 아직도 또렷이 기억하고 있는 열띤 사설들을 볼드 부인의 다소 다를지 모를 취향에 맞추어 손보기만 하면 되었다."]], [["\"Do not ask me to leave you, Mrs. Bold,\"", "“가라고 하지 마세요, 볼드 부인.”"], ["said he with an impassioned look, impassioned and sanctified as well, with that sort of look which is not uncommon with gentlemen of Mr.", "그가 격정에 찬 눈길로, 격정에 차 있으면서 동시에 거룩하기도 한 눈길로 말했다."], ["Slope's school and which may perhaps be called the tender-pious.", "슬로프 씨 부류의 신사들에게 그리 드물지 않은, 이를테면 다정경건형이라 불러도 좋을 눈길이었다."], ["\"Do not ask me to leave you till I have spoken a few words with which my heart is full--which I have come hither purposely to say.\"", "“제 마음에 가득 찬 몇 마디를, 바로 그것을 아뢰려고 일부러 여기까지 온 그 몇 마디를 말씀드리기 전에는 가라고 하지 마세요.”"]], [["Eleanor saw how it was now.", "엘리너는 이제 사정이 어떠한지 알아차렸다."], ["She knew directly what it was she was about to go through, and very miserable the knowledge made her.", "자기가 무엇을 겪게 될지 대번에 알았고, 그 앎이 그녀를 몹시 비참하게 만들었다."], ["Of course she could refuse Mr. Slope, and there would be an end of that, one might say.", "물론 슬로프 씨를 거절하면 그것으로 끝이라고 말할 사람도 있을 것이다."], ["But there would not be an end of it, as far as Eleanor was concerned. The very fact of Mr.", "그러나 엘리너로서는 그것으로 끝이 아니었다."], ["Slope's making an offer to her would be a triumph to the archdeacon and, in a great measure, a vindication of Mr. Arabin's conduct.", "슬로프 씨가 자기에게 청혼했다는 사실 자체가 부주교에게는 승리요, 애러빈 씨의 처신을 상당 부분 옳았다고 증명해 주는 일이 될 터였다."], ["The widow could not bring herself to endure with patience the idea that she had been in the wrong. She had defended Mr.", "이 미망인은 자기가 틀렸었다는 생각을 참을성 있게 견뎌 낼 수가 없었다."], ["Slope, she had declared herself quite justified in admitting him among her acquaintance, had ridiculed the idea of his considering himself as more than an acquaintance, and had resented the archdeacon's caution in her behalf: now it was about to be proved to her in a manner sufficiently disagreeable that the archdeacon had been right, and she herself had been entirely wrong.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨를 두둔했고, 그를 아는 사이로 받아들인 것은 조금도 나무랄 데 없는 일이라고 잘라 말했으며, 그가 스스로를 그저 아는 사이 이상으로 여긴다는 생각을 비웃었고, 자기를 위한다며 부주교가 늘어놓은 경고에 분개했다. 그런데 이제 부주교가 옳았고 자기가 온통 틀렸다는 것이 충분히 불쾌한 방식으로 그녀에게 증명되려는 참이었다."]], [["\"I don't know what you can have to say to me, Mr. Slope, that you could not have said when we were sitting at table just now;\"", "“조금 전 식탁에 앉아 있을 때는 하지 못하고 이제야 제게 하셔야 할 말씀이 무엇인지 저는 모르겠어요.”"], ["and she closed her lips, and steadied her eyeballs, and looked at him in a manner that ought to have frozen him.", "그러고는 입을 다물고 눈길을 굳힌 채, 사람을 얼어붙게 만들었어야 마땅할 태도로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["But gentlemen are not easily frozen when they are full of champagne, and it would not at any time have been easy to freeze Mr. Slope.", "그러나 신사란 샴페인이 가득 차 있을 때는 좀처럼 얼어붙지 않는 법이고, 슬로프 씨로 말하면 어느 때든 얼어붙게 만들기가 쉬운 사람이 아니었다."]], [["\"There are things, Mrs.", "“볼드 부인, 사람이 여럿 앞에서는 좀처럼 하지 못하는 말이 있습니다."], ["Bold, which a man cannot well say before a crowd; which perhaps he cannot well say at any time; which indeed he may most fervently desire to get spoken, and which he may yet find it almost impossible to utter.", "어쩌면 어느 때든 좀처럼 하지 못할 말이, 실은 입 밖으로 내고 싶어 애가 타면서도 도무지 발설하기 어려운 말이 있지요."], ["It is such things as these that I now wish to say to you;\"", "제가 지금 부인께 하고자 하는 것이 바로 그런 말입니다.”"], ["and then the tender-pious look was repeated, with a little more emphasis even than before.", "그러고는 그 다정경건한 눈길이 아까보다 조금 더 힘을 실어 되풀이되었다."]], [["Eleanor had not found it practicable to stand stock still before the dining-room window, there receive his offer in full view of Miss Thorne's guests.", "엘리너로서는 식당 창 앞에 못 박힌 듯 서서 손 양의 손님들이 훤히 보는 앞에서 그의 청혼을 받는 것이 될 일이 아니었다."], ["She had therefore in self-defence walked on, and thus Mr. Slope had gained his object of walking with her.", "그래서 스스로를 지키느라 걸음을 옮겼고, 그 바람에 슬로프 씨는 그녀와 나란히 걷는다는 목적을 이루었다."], ["He now offered her his arm.", "그는 이제 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["\"Thank you, Mr.", "“고맙습니다, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope, I am much obliged to you; but for the very short time that I shall remain with you I shall prefer walking alone.\"", "그 마음은 감사히 받겠어요. 다만 제가 함께 있을 아주 짧은 동안은 혼자 걷는 편이 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"And must it be so short?\"", "“그토록 짧아야만 하나요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Must it be--\"", "“꼭 그래야만—”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said Eleanor, interrupting him, \"as short as possible, if you please, sir.\"", "엘리너가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. “되도록 짧게 부탁드려요.”"]], [["\"I had hoped, Mrs.", "“저는 바랐습니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold--I had hoped--\"", "저는 바랐어요—”"]], [["\"Pray hope nothing, Mr.", "“저에 관한 일이라면 아무것도 바라지 마세요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope, as far as I am concerned; pray do not; I do not know and need not know what hope you mean.", "부디 그러지 마세요. 무슨 바람을 말씀하시는지 저는 모르고, 알 필요도 없어요."], ["Our acquaintance is very slight, and will probably remain so.", "우리 사이는 아주 얕은 아는 사이일 뿐이고 아마 앞으로도 그럴 거예요."], ["Pray, pray let that be enough; there is at any rate no necessity for us to quarrel.\"", "부디, 부디 그것으로 족하다고 여겨 주세요. 어차피 우리가 다툴 까닭은 조금도 없으니까요.”"]], [["Mrs. Bold was certainly treating Mr.", "볼드 부인이 슬로프 씨를 다소 매몰차게 다루고 있는 것은 분명했고, 그도 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["Slope rather cavalierly, and he felt it so. She was rejecting him before he had offered himself, and informing him at the same time that he was taking a great deal too much on himself to be so familiar.", "그녀는 그가 청혼을 내놓기도 전에 그를 물리치고 있었으며, 동시에 그가 이토록 스스럼없이 구는 것은 분수에 넘치게 자기를 높이는 짓이라고 일러 주고 있었다."], ["She did not even make an attempt", "그녀는 이런 시늉조차 하지 않았다"]], [["From such a sharp and waspish word as \"no\" To pluck the sting.", "“아니오”라는 그 날카롭고 쏘아붙이는 말에서 독침을 뽑아내는 일 따위는."]], [["He was still determined to be very tender and very pious, seeing that, in spite of all Mrs.", "그는 여전히 몹시 다정하고 몹시 경건하기로 작정하고 있었다."], ["Bold had said to him, he had not yet abandoned hope; but he was inclined also to be somewhat angry.", "볼드 부인이 그렇게까지 말했는데도 아직 희망을 버리지 않았던 것이다. 그러면서도 한편으로는 슬며시 화가 나기도 했다."], ["The widow was bearing herself, as he thought, with too high a hand, was speaking of herself in much too imperious a tone.", "그가 보기에 이 미망인은 지나치게 높은 데서 굴었고, 제 이야기를 하는 말투가 너무 위압적이었다."], ["She had clearly no idea that an honour was being conferred on her.", "자기에게 영예가 내려지고 있다는 것을 도무지 모르는 눈치였다."], ["Mr. Slope would be tender as long as he could, but he began to think if that failed it would not be amiss if he also mounted himself for awhile on his high horse.", "슬로프 씨는 할 수 있는 데까지는 다정하게 굴 작정이었으나, 그것이 통하지 않는다면 자기도 잠시 높은 말 위에 올라앉는 편이 나쁘지 않겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["Mr. Slope could undoubtedly be very tender, but he could be very savage also, and he knew his own abilities.", "슬로프 씨는 두말할 것 없이 아주 다정해질 수 있는 사람이었지만 아주 사나워질 수도 있는 사람이었고, 제 재주를 스스로 알고 있었다."]], [["\"That is cruel,\" said he, \"and unchristian, too.", "“그것은 잔인한 말씀이에요.”"], ["The worst of us are still bidden to hope.", "그가 말했다. “그리스도인답지도 않고요."], ["What have I done that you should pass on me so severe a sentence?\"", "우리 가운데 가장 못난 자에게도 희망을 가지라는 분부가 내려져 있습니다."], ["And then he paused a moment, during which the widow walked steadily on with measured steps, saying nothing further.", "제가 무슨 일을 했기에 그토록 준엄한 선고를 내리시는 겁니까?” 그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었는데, 그동안 미망인은 아무 말 없이 고른 걸음으로 꾸준히 걸어갈 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Beautiful woman,\"", "“아름다운 분이여.”"], ["at last he burst forth, \"beautiful woman, you cannot pretend to be ignorant that I adore you.", "마침내 그가 터뜨렸다. “아름다운 분이여, 제가 당신을 흠모한다는 것을 모르는 체하실 수는 없을 겁니다."], ["Yes, Eleanor, yes, I love you.", "그렇습니다, 엘리너, 그래요, 저는 당신을 사랑합니다."], ["I love you with the truest affection which man can bear to woman.", "남자가 여자에게 품을 수 있는 가장 참된 애정으로 당신을 사랑합니다."], ["Next to my hopes of heaven are my hopes of possessing you.\" (Mr.", "천국을 바라는 소망 다음가는 것이 당신을 얻으려는 소망이에요.”"], ["Slope's memory here played him false, or he would not have omitted the deanery.) \"How sweet to walk to heaven with you by my side, with you for my guide, mutual guides.", "(여기서 슬로프 씨의 기억이 그를 속였다. 그러지 않았다면 수석사제관을 빠뜨릴 리 없었을 것이다.) “당신을 곁에 두고, 당신을 길잡이 삼아, 서로가 서로의 길잡이가 되어 천국까지 걸어간다면 얼마나 달겠습니까."], ["Say, Eleanor, dearest Eleanor, shall we walk that sweet path together?\"", "말해 주세요, 엘리너, 더없이 사랑하는 엘리너, 우리 그 달콤한 길을 함께 걸을까요?”"]], [["Eleanor had no intention of ever walking together with Mr. Slope on any other path than that special one of Miss Thorne's which they now occupied, but as she had been unable to prevent the expression of Mr. Slope's wishes and aspirations, she resolved to hear him out to the end before she answered him.", "엘리너로서는 지금 두 사람이 밟고 있는 손 양의 그 길 말고는 어떤 길도 슬로프 씨와 함께 걸을 마음이 없었으나, 슬로프 씨가 제 소망과 포부를 늘어놓는 것을 막을 수 없었던 터라 대답하기 전에 끝까지 들어 주기로 마음먹었다."]], [["\"Ah, Eleanor,\"", "“아, 엘리너.”"], ["he continued, and it seemed to be his idea that as he had once found courage to pronounce her Christian name, he could not utter it often enough.", "그가 말을 이었다. 한번 용기를 내어 그녀의 세례명을 불러 본 이상 아무리 자주 불러도 모자란다고 여기는 모양이었다."], ["\"Ah, Eleanor, will it not be sweet, with the Lord's assistance, to travel hand in hand through this mortal valley which His mercies will make pleasant to us, till hereafter we shall dwell together at the foot of His throne?\"", "“아, 엘리너, 주님의 도우심으로 손을 맞잡고 이 죽을 자들의 골짜기를 지나가되 주님의 자비가 그 길을 우리에게 즐겁게 하여 주시고, 마침내 저 너머에서 그분의 보좌 앞에 함께 거하게 된다면 얼마나 달겠습니까?”"], ["And then a more tenderly pious glance than ever beamed from the lover's eyes.", "그러고는 그 어느 때보다도 다정하고 경건한 눈빛이 이 연인의 눈에서 뿜어 나왔다."], ["\"Ah, Eleanor--\"", "“아, 엘리너—”"]], [["\"My name, Mr. Slope, is Mrs.", "“제 이름은 볼드 부인입니다, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Bold,\"", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["said Eleanor, who, though determined to hear out the tale of his love, was too much disgusted by his blasphemy to be able to bear much more of it.", "그의 사랑 타령을 끝까지 들어 주기로 작정하기는 했으나, 그 불경한 말투에 넌더리가 나서 더는 견딜 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Sweetest angel, be not so cold,\"", "“더없이 다정한 천사여, 그리 차갑게 굴지 마세요.”"], ["said he, and as he said it the champagne broke forth, and he contrived to pass his arm round her waist.", "그가 말했고, 그 말과 함께 샴페인이 터져 나왔으니, 그는 용케 팔을 둘러 그녀의 허리를 감았다."], ["He did this with considerable cleverness, for up to this point Eleanor had contrived with tolerable success to keep her distance from him.", "여기까지 엘리너가 제법 성공적으로 거리를 지켜 온 것을 생각하면 상당히 솜씨 좋은 짓이었다."], ["They had got into a walk nearly enveloped by shrubs, and Mr. Slope therefore no doubt considered that as they were now alone it was fitting that he should give her some outward demonstration of that affection of which he talked so much.", "두 사람은 관목에 거의 파묻힌 오솔길로 접어들어 있었고, 그러니 슬로프 씨로서는 이제 단둘이 되었으니 그토록 입에 올리던 애정을 겉으로도 얼마쯤 보여 주는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼음이 틀림없다."], ["It may perhaps be presumed that the same stamp of measures had been found to succeed with Olivia Proudie.", "어쩌면 똑같은 종류의 방책이 올리비아 프라우디에게는 잘 먹혔던 것이라고 짐작해도 좋겠다."], ["Be this as it may, it was not successful with Eleanor Bold.", "그야 어떻든 엘리너 볼드에게는 먹히지 않았다."]], [["She sprang from him as she would have jumped from an adder, but she did not spring far--not, indeed, beyond arm's length--and then, quick as thought, she raised her little hand and dealt him a box on the ear with such right goodwill that it sounded among the trees like a miniature thunderclap.", "그녀는 살무사에게서 뛰어 물러나듯 그에게서 뛰어 물러났으나 멀리 물러나지는 않았다. 실은 팔 길이를 벗어나지 않았다. 그러고는 생각보다도 빠르게 그 작은 손을 들어 그의 뺨을 후려쳤는데, 어찌나 시원스러운 손길이었던지 나무들 사이로 조그마한 벼락 소리처럼 울렸다."]], [["And now it is to be feared that every well-bred reader of these pages will lay down the book with disgust, feeling that, after all, the heroine is unworthy of sympathy.", "이제는 이 책장을 넘기던 교양 있는 독자마다 넌더리를 내며 책을 내려놓지 않을까 걱정스럽다. 결국 이 여주인공은 동정할 값어치가 없구나 하고 여기면서 말이다."], ["She is a hoyden, one will say.", "저 여자는 말괄량이라고 누군가는 말할 것이다."], ["At any rate she is not a lady, another will exclaim.", "적어도 숙녀는 아니라고 또 누군가는 소리칠 것이다."], ["I have suspected her all through, a third will declare; she has no idea of the dignity of a matron, or of the peculiar propriety which her position demands.", "나는 처음부터 저 여자가 미심쩍었다고 세 번째 사람은 단언할 것이다."], ["At one moment she is romping with young Stanhope; then she is making eyes at Mr.", "부인다운 품위도, 제 처지가 요구하는 각별한 몸가짐도 도무지 모르는 여자라고."], ["Arabin; anon she comes to fisticuffs with a third lover--and all before she is yet a widow of two years' standing.", "한때는 젊은 스탠호프와 어울려 뒹굴며 장난을 치더니, 다음에는 애러빈 씨에게 눈길을 보내고, 이내 세 번째 구애자와 주먹다짐에 이르렀으니, 그것도 아직 미망인이 된 지 두 해도 되지 않아서 말이다."]], [["She cannot altogether be defended, and yet it may be averred that she is not a hoyden, not given to romping nor prone to boxing.", "그녀를 온전히 두둔할 수는 없다. 그러나 그녀가 말괄량이가 아니며 뒹굴며 장난치는 버릇도, 주먹을 휘두르는 성미도 없다는 것만은 단언해 두어도 좋겠다."], ["It were to be wished devoutly that she had not struck Mr. Slope in the face.", "그녀가 슬로프 씨의 얼굴을 치지 않았더라면 참으로 좋았을 것이다."], ["In doing so she derogated from her dignity and committed herself.", "그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 제 품위를 깎았고 스스로를 곤란하게 만들었다."], ["Had she been educated in Belgravia, had she been brought up by any sterner mentor than that fond father, had she lived longer under the rule of a husband, she might, perhaps, have saved herself from this great fault.", "벨그레이비아에서 자랐더라면, 그 자애로운 아버지보다 좀 더 엄한 스승 밑에서 컸더라면, 남편의 다스림 아래 좀 더 오래 살았더라면 아마 이 큰 허물만은 면했을지도 모른다."], ["As it was, the provocation was too much for her, the temptation to instant resentment of the insult too strong.", "그러나 실상은 도발이 너무 심했고, 모욕을 그 자리에서 되갚고 싶은 유혹이 너무 강했다."], ["She was too keen in the feeling of independence, a feeling dangerous for a young woman, but one in which her position peculiarly tempted her to indulge. And then Mr.", "그녀는 독립의 감정을 지나치게 예민하게 느끼는 사람이었으니, 젊은 여인에게는 위험한 감정이로되 그녀의 처지가 유독 그것에 빠져들도록 부추기는 감정이었다."], ["Slope's face, tinted with a deeper dye than usual by the wine he had drunk, simpering and puckering itself with pseudo-pity and tender grimaces, seemed specially to call for such punishment.", "게다가 마신 술 때문에 여느 때보다 짙은 빛으로 물든 슬로프 씨의 얼굴이, 거짓 연민과 다정한 찡그림으로 히죽거리며 주름을 잡던 그 얼굴이 유독 그런 징벌을 불러들이는 듯싶었다."], ["She had, too, a true instinct as to the man; he was capable of rebuke in this way and in no other.", "또한 그녀는 이 사내에 대해 참으로 옳은 직감을 지니고 있었다. 이 사람은 오직 이런 식으로만 꾸짖을 수 있는 자였다."], ["To him the blow from her little hand was as much an insult as a blow from a man would have been to another.", "그 작은 손에서 날아온 일격은 다른 사람이라면 사내에게서 받았을 주먹만큼이나 그에게는 모욕이었다."], ["It went directly to his pride.", "그것은 곧바로 그의 자존심을 찔렀다."], ["He conceived himself lowered in his dignity and personally outraged.", "그는 제 품위가 낮아지고 제 몸이 능욕당했다고 여겼다."], ["He could almost have struck at her again in his rage.", "분한 나머지 그녀를 되받아 칠 뻔했다."], ["Even the pain was a great annoyance to him, and the feeling that his clerical character had been wholly disregarded sorely vexed him.", "아픔조차 그에게는 큰 성가심이었고, 제 성직자 신분이 깡그리 무시당했다는 느낌이 그를 몹시 언짢게 했다."]], [["There are such men: men who can endure no taint on their personal self-respect, even from a woman; men whose bodies are to themselves such sacred temples that a joke against them is desecration, and a rough touch downright sacrilege.", "그런 사내들이 있다. 제 자존심에 흠이 나는 것을 여자에게서조차 견디지 못하는 사내들, 제 몸이 스스로에게는 어찌나 거룩한 성전인지 저를 두고 하는 농담은 신성모독이요 거친 손길은 아예 성물 훼손이 되는 사내들 말이다."], ["Mr. Slope was such a man, and therefore the slap on the face that he got from Eleanor was, as far as he was concerned, the fittest rebuke which could have been administered to him.", "슬로프 씨가 바로 그런 사내였고, 따라서 엘리너에게 얻어맞은 따귀는 그로 말하자면 그에게 내려질 수 있는 가장 알맞은 꾸짖음이었다."]], [["But nevertheless, she should not have raised her hand against the man.", "그렇다 하더라도 그녀가 그 사내에게 손을 들어서는 안 되었다."], ["Ladies' hands, so soft, so sweet, so delicious to the touch, so graceful to the eye, so gracious in their gentle doings, were not made to belabour men's faces.", "그토록 부드럽고 그토록 곱고 손끝에 닿기에 그토록 감미로우며 눈에 그토록 우아하고 그 얌전한 놀림이 그토록 사랑스러운 숙녀의 손은 사내의 얼굴을 두들기라고 만들어진 것이 아니다."], ["The moment the deed was done Eleanor felt that she had sinned against all propriety, and would have given little worlds to recall the blow.", "일을 저지른 순간 엘리너는 자기가 온갖 예법에 죄를 지었다고 느꼈고, 그 일격을 도로 거두어들일 수만 있다면 작은 세상 하나쯤은 내주었을 것이다."], ["In her first agony of sorrow she all but begged the man's pardon.", "처음의 애타는 뉘우침 속에서 그녀는 하마터면 그 사내에게 용서를 빌 뻔했다."], ["Her next impulse, however, and the one which she obeyed, was to run away.", "그러나 뒤이어 밀려온 충동, 그리고 그녀가 따른 충동은 달아나는 것이었다."]], [["\"I never, never will speak another word to you,\"", "“저는 다시는, 다시는 당신께 한마디도 하지 않겠어요.”"], ["she said, gasping with emotion and the loss of breath which her exertion and violent feelings occasioned her, and so saying she put foot to the ground and ran quickly back along the path to the house.", "그녀가 격한 감정과, 힘을 쓴 데다 감정이 사나워진 탓에 가빠진 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. 그러고는 땅을 박차고 오솔길을 따라 집 쪽으로 재빠르게 달려갔다."]], [["But how shall I sing the divine wrath of Mr. Slope, or how invoke the tragic muse to describe the rage which swelled the celestial bosom of the bishop's chaplain?", "그러나 슬로프 씨의 신적인 진노를 내 어찌 노래하며, 주교의 전속 사제의 천상 같은 가슴을 부풀린 그 분노를 그려 내고자 비극의 뮤즈를 어찌 부르랴?"], ["Such an undertaking by no means befits the low-heeled buskin of modern fiction.", "그런 일은 근대 소설의 굽 낮은 비극 장화에는 도무지 어울리지 않는다."], ["The painter put a veil over Agamemnon's face when called on to depict the father's grief at the early doom of his devoted daughter.", "화가는 제 몸을 바친 딸의 이른 운명 앞에서 아버지가 느낀 슬픔을 그리라는 청을 받았을 때 아가멤논의 얼굴에 너울을 덮었다."], ["The god, when he resolved to punish the rebellious winds, abstained from mouthing empty threats.", "신은 거역하는 바람들을 벌하기로 마음먹었을 때 빈 위협을 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["We will not attempt to tell with what mighty surgings of the inner heart Mr. Slope swore to revenge himself on the woman who had disgraced him, nor will we vainly strive to depict his deep agony of soul.", "슬로프 씨가 자기를 욕보인 여인에게 복수하리라 맹세하며 속마음이 얼마나 크게 소용돌이쳤는지 우리는 이야기하려 들지 않을 것이며, 그의 깊은 영혼의 고통을 그려 내려 헛되이 애쓰지도 않을 것이다."]], [["There he is, however, alone in the garden walk, and we must contrive to bring him out of it.", "어쨌든 그는 저기 정원 오솔길에 홀로 서 있고, 우리는 어떻게든 그를 거기서 데리고 나와야 한다."], ["He was not willing to come forth quite at once.", "그는 당장 나설 마음이 없었다."], ["His cheek was stinging with the weight of Eleanor's fingers, and he fancied that everyone who looked at him would be able to see on his face the traces of what he had endured.", "뺨은 엘리너의 손가락이 남긴 무게로 얼얼했고, 자기를 쳐다보는 사람이면 누구나 제 얼굴에서 방금 당한 일의 자취를 읽어 내리라 여겨졌다."], ["He stood awhile, becoming redder and redder with rage.", "그는 한동안 서서 분노로 점점 더 붉어졌다."], ["He stood motionless, undecided, glaring with his eyes, thinking of the pains and penalties of Hades, and meditating how he might best devote his enemy to the infernal gods with all the passion of his accustomed eloquence.", "꼼짝 않고 서서, 어찌할 바를 정하지 못한 채 눈을 부라리며, 하데스의 고통과 형벌을 떠올리고, 제 익숙한 웅변의 온갖 열정을 다해 원수를 저승의 신들에게 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 바쳐 넘길 수 있을지 궁리했다."], ["He longed in his heart to be preaching at her.", "그의 마음은 그녀를 향해 설교하고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["'Twas thus that he was ordinarily avenged of sinning mortal men and women.", "죄지은 사내와 여인들에게 그가 늘 복수하는 방식이 바로 그것이었다."], ["Could he at once have ascended his Sunday rostrum and fulminated at her such denunciations as his spirit delighted in, his bosom would have been greatly eased.", "당장 주일 강단에 올라 제 영이 즐거워하는 그런 규탄을 그녀에게 퍼부을 수만 있었다면 가슴이 크게 후련해졌을 것이다."]], [["But how preach to Mr. Thorne's laurels, or how preach indeed at all in such a vanity fair as this now going on at Ullathorne?", "그러나 손 씨네 월계수를 앞에 두고 어떻게 설교하며, 지금 울라손에서 벌어지고 있는 이런 허영의 시장에서 대체 무슨 설교를 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["And then he began to feel a righteous disgust at the wickedness of the doings around him.", "이윽고 그는 제 둘레에서 벌어지는 일들의 사악함에 의로운 역겨움을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He had been justly chastised for lending, by his presence, a sanction to such worldly lures.", "제가 그 자리에 있음으로써 그런 세속의 유혹에 인정을 보태 주었으니 마땅히 벌을 받은 것이었다."], ["The gaiety of society, the mirth of banquets, the laughter of the young, and the eating and drinking of the elders were, for awhile, without excuse in his sight.", "사교의 흥겨움, 잔치의 즐거움, 젊은이들의 웃음, 나이 든 이들의 먹고 마심이 한동안 그의 눈에는 변명의 여지가 없는 것으로 비쳤다."], ["What had he now brought down upon himself by sojourning thus in the tents of the heathen?", "이렇게 이방인의 장막에 머무름으로써 그는 대체 무엇을 제 머리 위에 불러들였는가?"], ["He had consorted with idolaters round the altars of Baal, and therefore a sore punishment had come upon him.", "그는 바알의 제단 둘레에서 우상 숭배자들과 어울렸으니, 그리하여 쓰라린 형벌이 그에게 임한 것이었다."], ["He then thought of the Signora Neroni, and his soul within him was full of sorrow.", "이어 그는 시뇨라 네로니를 떠올렸고, 그의 속 영혼은 슬픔으로 가득했다."], ["He had an inkling--a true inkling--that he was a wicked, sinful man, but it led him in no right direction; he could admit no charity in his heart.", "그는 제가 사악하고 죄 많은 사람이라는 낌새를, 참으로 옳은 낌새를 어렴풋이 느끼고 있었으나 그것이 그를 바른 길로 이끌지는 못했다. 그의 마음속에는 사랑이 들어설 자리가 없었다."], ["He felt debasement coming on him, and he longed to shake it off, to rise up in his stirrup, to mount to high places and great power, that he might get up into a mighty pulpit and preach to the world a loud sermon against Mrs. Bold.", "그는 자기가 낮아지고 있음을 느꼈고, 그것을 떨쳐 버리기를, 등자를 딛고 일어서기를, 높은 자리와 큰 권세에 올라 웅장한 강단에 서서 볼드 부인을 규탄하는 설교를 세상에 우렁차게 외치기를 갈망했다."]], [["There he stood fixed to the gravel for about ten minutes.", "그는 자갈 위에 붙박인 듯 10분쯤 서 있었다."], ["Fortune favoured him so far that no prying eyes came to look upon him in his misery.", "그나마 운이 따라 주어 캐묻는 눈길이 그의 비참을 들여다보러 오지는 않았다."], ["Then a shudder passed over his whole frame; he collected himself and slowly wound his way round to the lawn, advancing along the path and not returning in the direction which Eleanor had taken.", "이윽고 온몸에 전율이 지나갔고, 그는 마음을 추스른 뒤 천천히 돌아 잔디밭 쪽으로 길을 잡았는데, 엘리너가 간 쪽으로 되돌아가지 않고 오솔길을 따라 나아갔다."], ["When he reached the tent, he found the bishop standing there in conversation with the Master of Lazarus.", "천막에 이르렀을 때 그는 주교가 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 나누며 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["His lordship had come out to air himself after the exertion of his speech.", "주교 각하는 연설의 노고를 치른 뒤 바람을 쐬러 나와 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"This is very pleasant--very pleasant, my lord, is it not?\"", "“참으로 흥겹습니다, 참으로 흥겹지 않습니까, 주교님?”"], ["said Mr. Slope with his most gracious smile, pointing to the tent; \"very pleasant.", "슬로프 씨가 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 천막을 가리키고 말했다."], ["It is delightful to see so many persons enjoying themselves so thoroughly.\"", "“참으로 흥겹습니다. 저리 많은 사람이 저토록 흠뻑 즐거워하는 것을 보니 기쁩니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope thought he might force the bishop to introduce him to Dr.", "슬로프 씨는 주교를 몰아붙여 그윈 박사에게 자기를 소개하게 할 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["Gwynne. A very great example had declared and practised the wisdom of being everything to everybody, and Mr.", "아주 위대한 본보기 한 분이 여러 사람에게 여러 모양이 되는 지혜를 선포하고 몸소 행하신 바 있으니, 슬로프 씨도 그것을 따르고 싶었던 것이다."], ["Slope was desirous of following it. His maxim was never to lose a chance.", "기회를 놓치지 않는다는 것이 그의 좌우명이었다."], ["The bishop, however, at the present moment was not very anxious to increase Mr. Slope's circle of acquaintance among his clerical brethren.", "그러나 주교는 지금 이 순간 슬로프 씨가 성직자 형제들 사이에서 아는 이를 늘리는 데 그다지 열의가 없었다."], ["He had his own reasons for dropping any marked allusion to his domestic chaplain, and he therefore made his shoulder rather cold for the occasion.", "제 전속 사제를 눈에 띄게 언급하는 일을 삼갈 나름의 사정이 있었던 터라, 이 자리에서는 어깨를 제법 쌀쌀하게 굴렸다."]], [["\"Very, very,\"", "“참으로, 참으로.”"], ["said he without turning round, or even deigning to look at Mr. Slope.", "그는 돌아보지도 않고, 슬로프 씨에게 눈길 한 번 주는 수고조차 하지 않은 채 말했다."], ["\"And therefore, Dr. Gwynne, I really think that you will find that the hebdomadal board will exercise as wide and as general an authority as at the present moment.", "“그러니 그윈 박사, 나는 대학 주간 평의회가 지금과 다름없이 넓고 두루 미치는 권한을 행사하게 되리라고 정말로 생각하오."], ["I, for one, Dr. Gwynne--\"", "나로 말하자면, 그윈 박사—”"]], [["\"Dr. Gwynne,\"", "“그윈 박사님.”"], ["said Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 모자를 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["Slope, raising his hat and resolving not to be outwitted by such an insignificant little goose as the Bishop of Barchester.", "바체스터 주교 따위의 하잘것없는 얼간이에게 허를 찔릴 수는 없다고 작정한 것이었다."]], [["The Master of Lazarus also raised his hat and bowed very politely to Mr. Slope.", "라자러스 학장도 모자를 들어 올리며 슬로프 씨에게 아주 정중하게 인사했다."], ["There is not a more courteous gentleman in the queen's dominions than the Master of Lazarus.", "여왕의 영토를 통틀어 라자러스 학장보다 더 예의 바른 신사는 없다."]], [["\"My lord,\"", "“주교님.”"], ["said Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Slope, \"pray do me the honour of introducing me to Dr. Gwynne.", "“부디 그윈 박사께 저를 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주십시오."], ["The opportunity is too much in my favour to be lost.\"", "이런 기회는 제게 너무나 유리한 것이라 놓칠 수가 없습니다.”"]], [["The bishop had no help for it.", "주교로서는 어찌할 도리가 없었다."], ["\"My chaplain, Dr.", "“내 전속 사제요, 그윈 박사.”"], ["Gwynne,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"my present chaplain, Mr. Slope.\"", "“지금의 내 전속 사제, 슬로프 씨요.”"], ["He certainly made the introduction as unsatisfactory to the chaplain as possible, and by the use of the word \"present\" seemed to indicate that Mr.", "그는 분명 그 소개를 전속 사제에게 되도록 흡족하지 않게 해 주었으니, ‘지금의’라는 말을 씀으로써 슬로프 씨가 현재 누리는 영예를 아마 오래 누리지는 못하리라는 뜻을 내비친 셈이었다."], ["Slope might probably not long enjoy the honour which he now held.", "그러나 슬로프 씨는 그런 것에 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["But Mr. Slope cared nothing for this.", "그는 그 빈정거림을 알아들었으되 무시했다."], ["He understood the innuendo, and disregarded it. It might probably come to pass that he would be in a situation to resign his chaplaincy before the bishop was in a situation to dismiss him from it.", "주교가 그를 전속 사제 자리에서 내칠 형편이 되기도 전에 그가 먼저 그 자리를 내놓을 형편이 될 공산이 컸다."], ["What need the future Dean of Barchester care for the bishop, or for the bishop's wife? Had not Mr.", "장차 바체스터 수석 사제가 될 사람이 주교를, 또는 주교의 부인을 무엇 때문에 마음에 두겠는가?"], ["Slope, just as he was entering Dr. Stanhope's carriage, received an all-important note from Tom Towers of \"The Jupiter\"?", "슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 박사의 마차에 오르려던 참에 『주피터』의 톰 타워스에게서 더없이 중요한 쪽지를 받지 않았던가?"], ["Had he not that note this moment in his pocket?", "바로 이 순간 그 쪽지가 제 주머니 속에 들어 있지 않은가?"]], [["So disregarding the bishop, he began to open out a conversation with the Master of Lazarus.", "그리하여 주교를 개의치 않고 그는 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 트기 시작했다."]], [["But suddenly an interruption came, not altogether unwelcome to Mr. Slope.", "그러나 갑자기 이야기가 끊겼는데, 슬로프 씨에게 아주 달갑지 않은 것만은 아니었다."], ["One of the bishop's servants came up to his master's shoulder with a long, grave face and whispered into the bishop's ear.", "주교의 하인 하나가 길고 엄숙한 얼굴로 주인의 어깨께로 다가와 주교의 귀에 대고 속삭였던 것이다."]], [["\"What is it, John?\"", "“무슨 일인가, 존?”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"The dean, my lord; he is dead.\"", "“수석 사제님 일입니다, 주교님. 돌아가셨습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope had no further desire to converse with the Master of Lazarus, and was very soon on his road back to Barchester.", "슬로프 씨는 더 이상 라자러스 학장과 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 없었고, 아주 이내 바체스터로 돌아가는 길에 올라 있었다."]], [["Eleanor, as we have said, having declared her intention of never holding further communication with Mr. Slope, ran hurriedly back towards the house.", "엘리너는, 앞서 말한 대로, 다시는 슬로프 씨와 말을 섞지 않겠다고 선언하고서 서둘러 집 쪽으로 달려갔다."], ["The thought, however, of what she had done grieved her greatly, and she could not abstain from bursting into tears.", "그러나 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하니 몹시 마음이 아팠고, 끝내 눈물이 터지는 것을 참을 수 없었다."], ["'Twas thus she played the second act in that day's melodrama.", "그날의 멜로드라마에서 그녀가 제2막을 연기한 것은 이러했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLI. Mrs. Bold Confides Her Sorrow to Her Friend Miss Stanhope", "# 제41장. 볼드 부인이 벗 스탠호프 양에게 근심을 털어놓다"]], [["When Mrs. Bold came to the end of the walk and faced the lawn, she began to bethink herself what she should do.", "볼드 부인은 오솔길 끝에 이르러 잔디밭을 마주하고서야 이제 어떻게 해야 하나 생각하기 시작했다."], ["Was she to wait there till Mr. Slope caught her, or was she to go in among the crowd with tears in her eyes and passion in her face?", "슬로프 씨가 뒤따라와 붙잡을 때까지 여기 서 있어야 하는가, 아니면 눈에는 눈물이 어리고 얼굴에는 노기가 서린 채로 사람들 틈에 들어가야 하는가?"], ["She might in truth have stood there long enough without any reasonable fear of further immediate persecution from Mr. Slope, but we are all inclined to magnify the bugbears which frighten us.", "사실을 말하자면 그 자리에 아무리 오래 서 있었더라도 슬로프 씨에게 당장 또 시달릴 염려는 딱히 없었으나, 우리는 누구나 저를 겁주는 도깨비를 실제보다 크게 부풀려 보게 마련이다."], ["In her present state of dread she did not know of what atrocity he might venture to be guilty.", "이렇듯 겁에 질린 상태에서 그는 저 사람이 무슨 흉악한 짓을 감히 저지를지 알 수 없었다."], ["Had anyone told her a week ago that he would have put his arm round her waist at this party of Miss Thorne's, she would have been utterly incredulous.", "일주일 전에 누가 그에게, 저 사람이 손 양의 이 연회에서 당신 허리에 팔을 두르리라고 말했더라면 그는 도무지 믿지 않았을 것이다."], ["Had she been informed that he would be seen on the following Sunday walking down the High Street in a scarlet coat and top boots, she would not have thought such a phenomenon more improbable.", "저 사람이 다음 일요일에 주홍색 상의에 장화 차림으로 하이 스트리트를 걸어 내려가는 모습이 목격되리라는 말을 들었더라도, 그런 괴변이 그보다 더 있을 법하지 않다고 여기지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["But this improbable iniquity he had committed, and now there was nothing she could not believe of him.", "그러나 그 있을 법하지 않은 악행을 슬로프 씨는 기어이 저질렀고, 이제 그 사람에 대해서라면 무엇이든 못 믿을 것이 없었다."], ["In the first place it was quite manifest that he was tipsy; in the next place it was to be taken as proved that all his religion was sheer hypocrisy; and finally the man was utterly shameless.", "우선 그가 술에 취했다는 것은 아주 명백했다. 다음으로 그의 신앙이라는 것이 죄다 순전한 위선임은 이미 입증된 것으로 쳐야 했다."], ["She therefore stood watching for the sound of his footfall, not without some fear that he might creep out at her suddenly from among the bushes.", "그리고 끝으로 그 사람은 염치라고는 조금도 없는 자였다. 그리하여 볼드 부인은 그자가 덤불 사이에서 불쑥 튀어나오지나 않을까 하는 두려움이 없지 않은 채로, 그 발소리가 들려오지는 않는지 살피며 서 있었다."]], [["As she thus stood she saw Charlotte Stanhope at a little distance from her, walking quickly across the grass.", "그렇게 서 있다가 그는 조금 떨어진 곳에서 샬럿 스탠호프가 빠른 걸음으로 잔디를 가로질러 오는 것을 보았다."], ["Eleanor's handkerchief was in her hand, and putting it to her face so as to conceal her tears, she ran across the lawn and joined her friend.", "엘리너는 손에 손수건을 쥐고 있었고, 그것을 얼굴에 갖다 대어 눈물을 가리면서 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 벗에게 갔다."]], [["\"Oh, Charlotte,\"", "“오, 샬럿.”"], ["she said, almost too much out of breath to speak very plainly; \"I am so glad I have found you.\"", "숨이 차서 또렷이 말하기도 어려운 채로 그가 말했다. “당신을 찾아냈으니 정말 다행이에요.”"]], [["\"Glad you have found me!\"", "“나를 찾아냈으니 다행이라고요!”"], ["said Charlotte, laughing; \"that's a good joke.", "샬럿이 웃으며 말했다. “그거 참 좋은 농담이네요."], ["Why Bertie and I have been looking for you everywhere.", "아니, 버티와 내가 사방으로 당신을 찾아다녔는걸요."], ["He swears that you have gone off with Mr. Slope, and is now on the point of hanging himself.\"", "그 애는 당신이 슬로프 씨와 달아나 버렸다고 장담하면서 지금 막 목이라도 맬 참이라니까요.”"]], [["\"Oh, Charlotte, don't,\"", "“오, 샬럿, 그런 말 마세요.”"], ["said Mrs. Bold.", "볼드 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Why, my child, what on earth is the matter with you?\"", "“아니, 대체 무슨 일이에요?”"], ["said Miss Stanhope, perceiving that Eleanor's hand trembled on her own arm, and finding also that her companion was still half-choked by tears.", "스탠호프 양은 엘리너의 손이 제 팔 위에서 떨리는 것을 느끼고, 또 그 벗이 아직도 눈물에 반쯤 목이 메어 있음을 알아채고서 말했다."], ["\"Goodness heaven!", "“어머나 세상에!"], ["Something has distressed you.", "무슨 일로 이렇게 상심했군요."], ["What is it?", "무슨 일이에요?"], ["What can I do for you?\"", "내가 뭘 해 드리면 될까요?”"]], [["Eleanor answered her only by a sort of spasmodic gurgle in her throat.", "엘리너는 목구멍에서 발작처럼 새어 나오는 흐느낌 소리로만 대답했다."], ["She was a good deal upset, as people say, and could not at the moment collect herself.", "흔히들 말하듯 그는 몹시 상해 있었고, 그 순간에는 도무지 마음을 추스를 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Come here, this way, Mrs.", "“이리로 와요, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold; come this way, and we shall not be seen.", "이쪽으로 오면 아무도 우리를 못 봐요."], ["What has happened to vex you so?", "무슨 일이 있었기에 이토록 속이 상했어요?"], ["What can I do for you?", "내가 뭘 해 드릴까요?"], ["Can Bertie do anything?\"", "버티가 뭐라도 할 수 있을까요?”"]], [["\"Oh, no, no, no, no,\" said Eleanor.", "“아, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.”"], ["\"There is nothing to be done.", "엘리너가 말했다. “어쩔 수 있는 일이 아니에요."], ["Only that horrid man--\"", "다만 그 끔찍한 사람이—”"]], [["\"What horrid man?\"", "“끔찍한 사람이라니, 누구요?”"], ["asked Charlotte.", "샬럿이 물었다."]], [["There are some moments in life in which both men and women feel themselves imperatively called on to make a confidence, in which not to do so requires a disagreeable resolution and also a disagreeable suspicion.", "살다 보면 남자든 여자든 속을 털어놓지 않고는 못 배기겠는 순간이 있다. 그럴 때 털어놓지 않으려면 불쾌한 결심이 있어야 하고 또한 불쾌한 의심도 있어야 한다."], ["There are people of both sexes who never make confidences, who are never tempted by momentary circumstances to disclose their secrets, but such are generally dull, close, unimpassioned spirits, \"gloomy gnomes, who live in cold dark mines.\"", "남녀를 통틀어 결코 속을 털어놓는 법이 없는 사람들, 순간의 사정에 이끌려 제 비밀을 내놓는 일이 결코 없는 사람들도 있기는 하다. 그러나 그런 이들은 대개 둔하고 닫혀 있고 정열이 없는 영혼, “차고 어두운 굴속에 사는 침침한 땅난쟁이들”이다."], ["There was nothing of the gnome about Eleanor, and she therefore resolved to tell Charlotte Stanhope the whole story about Mr. Slope.", "엘리너에게는 그런 땅난쟁이 구석이라고는 없었고, 그래서 그는 슬로프 씨에 관한 이야기를 죄다 샬럿 스탠호프에게 털어놓기로 마음먹었다."]], [["\"That horrid man; that Mr. Slope,\"", "“그 끔찍한 사람, 그 슬로프 씨 말이에요.”"], ["said she.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Did you not see that he followed me out of the dining-room?\"", "“그 사람이 나를 따라 식당에서 나오는 걸 못 봤어요?”"]], [["\"Of course I did, and was sorry enough, but I could not help it.", "“물론 봤죠. 딱하다고 여기기도 했고요."], ["I knew you would be annoyed.", "하지만 나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었어요."], ["But you and Bertie managed it badly between you.\"", "당신이 성가셔하리라는 건 알았죠. 하지만 당신과 버티가 둘이서 일을 서투르게 처리했어요.”"]], [["\"It was not his fault nor mine either.", "“그건 그이 잘못도 내 잘못도 아니에요."], ["You know how I disliked the idea of coming in the carriage with that man.\"", "내가 그 사람과 한 마차를 타고 오는 걸 얼마나 싫어했는지 당신도 알잖아요.”"]], [["\"I am sure I am very sorry if that has led to it.\"", "“그 일이 이런 결과를 불렀다면 정말이지 미안해요.”"]], [["\"I don't know what has led to it,\"", "“무엇이 이런 결과를 불렀는지는 나도 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Eleanor, almost crying again.", "다시 울음이 터질 듯한 목소리로 엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"But it has not been my fault.\"", "“하지만 내 잘못은 아니었어요.”"]], [["\"But what has he done, my dear?\"", "“그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거예요?”"]], [["\"He's an abominable, horrid, hypocritical man, and it would serve him right to tell the bishop all about it.\"", "“그 사람은 밉살스럽고 끔찍하고 위선적인 인간이에요. 주교님께 죄다 일러바쳐도 싼 사람이라고요.”"]], [["\"Believe me, if you want to do him an injury, you had far better tell Mrs.", "“내 말을 믿어요."], ["Proudie.", "그 사람에게 해를 입히고 싶거든 프라우디 부인에게 이르는 편이 훨씬 나아요."], ["But what did he do, Mrs. Bold?\"", "그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했다는 거예요, 볼드 부인?”"]], [["\"Ugh!\"", "“으!”"], ["exclaimed Eleanor.", "엘리너가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Well, I must confess he's not very nice,\"", "“하긴, 그리 좋은 사람이 아니라는 건 나도 인정해요.”"], ["said Charlotte Stanhope.", "샬럿 스탠호프가 말했다."]], [["\"Nice!\"", "“좋은 사람이라니요!”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"He is the most fulsome, fawning, abominable man I ever saw.", "“내가 여태 본 중에 가장 역겹고 알랑거리고 밉살스러운 인간이에요. 무슨 염치로 나한테 온단 말이에요?"], ["What business had he to come to me?--I that never gave him the slightest tittle of encouragement--I that always hated him, though I did take his part when others ran him down.\"", "나는 그 사람에게 티끌만 한 곁도 준 적이 없어요. 남들이 그 사람을 헐뜯을 때 내가 편을 들어 준 적은 있어도, 나는 늘 그 사람이 싫었단 말이에요.”"]], [["\"That's just where it is, my dear.", "“바로 거기예요."], ["He has heard that and therefore fancied that of course you were in love with him.\"", "그 사람이 그 말을 듣고서, 당연히 당신이 자기를 사랑하는 줄로 여긴 거죠.”"]], [["This was wormwood to Eleanor.", "이 말은 엘리너에게 쓸개즙 같았다."], ["It was in fact the very thing which all her friends had been saying for the last month past--and which experience now proved to be true.", "그것이야말로 지난 한 달 내내 그의 벗들이 죄다 해 온 말이었고, 이제 경험이 그 말이 옳았음을 증명해 보인 것이다."], ["Eleanor resolved within herself that she would never again take any man's part.", "엘리너는 다시는 어떤 남자의 편도 들지 않겠다고 속으로 다짐했다."], ["The world, with all its villainy and all its ill-nature, might wag as it liked: she would not again attempt to set crooked things straight.", "세상이 그 온갖 간악함과 온갖 심술궂음을 안고 제멋대로 굴러가든 말든, 그는 다시는 굽은 것을 바로잡으려 들지 않으리라."]], [["\"But what did he do, my dear?\"", "“그런데 그 사람이 무슨 짓을 했어요?”"], ["said Charlotte, who was really rather interested in the subject.", "샬럿이 말했다. 그는 사실 이 이야기에 자못 흥미가 동해 있었다."]], [["\"He--he--he--\"", "“그 사람이—그 사람이—그 사람이—”"]], [["\"Well--come, it can't have been anything so very horrid, for the man was not tipsy.\"", "“자, 어서요. 그리 끔찍한 일이었을 리는 없어요. 그 사람은 취하지도 않았는걸요.”"]], [["\"Oh, I am sure he was\"", "“아, 나는 분명히 취했다고 봐요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"I am sure he must have been tipsy.\"", "“틀림없이 취해 있었을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Well, I declare I didn't observe it.", "“글쎄, 나는 그런 낌새를 못 봤는걸요."], ["But what was it, my love?\"", "그래서 무슨 일이었어요?”"]], [["\"Why, I believe I can hardly tell you.", "“글쎄요, 나로서는 도저히 말 못 하겠어요."], ["He talked such horrid stuff that you never heard the like: about religion, and heaven, and love.", "그 사람이 늘어놓은 그런 끔찍한 소리는 당신도 들어 본 적 없을 거예요. 신앙이니 천국이니 사랑이니 하는 소리를요."], ["Oh, dear--he is such a nasty man.\"", "아이참, 정말 고약한 사람이에요.”"]], [["\"I can easily imagine the sort of stuff he would talk.", "“그 사람이 무슨 소리를 늘어놓았을지는 쉽게 짐작이 가요."], ["Well--and then--?\"", "그래서, 그다음엔요?”"]], [["\"And then--he took hold of me.\"", "“그러고는—나를 붙잡았어요.”"]], [["\"Took hold of you?\"", "“붙잡았다고요?”"]], [["\"Yes--he somehow got close to me and took hold of me--\"", "“네—어느새 바짝 다가와서는 나를 붙잡았어요—”"]], [["\"By the waist?\"", "“허리를요?”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said Eleanor shuddering.", "엘리너가 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["\"And then--\"", "“그래서요—”"]], [["\"Then I jumped away from him, and gave him a slap on the face, and ran away along the path till I saw you.\"", "“그래서 나는 그 사람에게서 펄쩍 물러나 뺨을 한 대 올려붙이고, 길을 따라 달아나다가 당신을 본 거예요.”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하하하!”"], ["Charlotte Stanhope laughed heartily at the finale to the tragedy.", "샬럿 스탠호프는 그 비극의 대단원에 마음껏 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["It was delightful to her to think that Mr. Slope had had his ears boxed.", "슬로프 씨가 따귀를 얻어맞았구나 생각하니 그로서는 유쾌하기 짝이 없었다."], ["She did not quite appreciate the feeling which made her friend so unhappy at the result of the interview.", "그 만남의 결말에 벗이 그토록 괴로워하는 심정을 그는 그리 깊이 헤아리지 못했다."], ["To her thinking the matter had ended happily enough as regarded the widow, who indeed was entitled to some sort of triumph among her friends.", "그가 보기에 이 일은 미망인 쪽으로 보자면 충분히 흡족하게 끝난 셈이었고, 실상 그 미망인은 벗들 사이에서 얼마간 개선의 대접을 받을 자격이 있었다."], ["Whereas to Mr. Slope would be due all those gibes and jeers which would naturally follow such an affair.", "반면 슬로프 씨에게는 그런 사건 뒤에 으레 따라붙게 마련인 온갖 비웃음과 놀림이 돌아갈 터였다."], ["His friends would ask him whether his ears tingled whenever he saw a widow, and he would be cautioned that beautiful things were made to be looked at and not to be touched.", "그의 벗들은 미망인만 보면 귀가 얼얼하지 않으냐고 물을 것이고, 아름다운 것은 보라고 있는 것이지 만지라고 있는 것이 아니라는 훈계를 들을 것이다."]], [["Such were Charlotte Stanhope's views on such matters, but she did not at the present moment clearly explain them to Mrs. Bold.", "그런 일들에 대한 샬럿 스탠호프의 생각은 이러했으나, 그는 그것을 지금 이 자리에서 볼드 부인에게 또렷이 설명하지는 않았다."], ["Her object was to endear herself to her friend, and therefore, having had her laugh, she was ready enough to offer sympathy.", "그의 목적은 벗의 마음을 제게로 끌어당기는 데 있었으므로, 한바탕 웃고 난 뒤에는 기꺼이 동정을 내놓을 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["Could Bertie do anything?", "버티가 무언가 할 수 있지 않을까?"], ["Should Bertie speak to the man and warn him that in future he must behave with more decorum?", "버티를 시켜 그 사람에게 앞으로는 좀 더 예의를 갖추라고 경고하게 할까?"], ["Bertie indeed, she declared, would be more angry than anyone else when he heard to what insult Mrs. Bold had been subjected.", "실로 버티야말로 볼드 부인이 어떤 모욕을 당했는지 들으면 누구보다도 화를 낼 것이라고 그는 단언했다."]], [["\"But you won't tell him?\" said Mrs.", "“하지만 그이에게 말하지는 않을 거죠?”"], ["Bold with a look of horror.", "볼드 부인이 질겁한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"Not if you don't like it,\"", "“당신이 싫다면 안 하죠.”"], ["said Charlotte; \"but considering everything, I would strongly advise it.", "샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 이것저것 헤아려 보면 나로서는 꼭 말하기를 권하고 싶어요."], ["If you had a brother, you know, it would be unnecessary.", "당신에게 오라비가 있다면야 그럴 필요도 없겠지요."], ["But it is very right that Mr. Slope should know that you have somebody by you that will and can protect you.\"", "하지만 슬로프 씨로서는 당신 곁에 당신을 지켜 줄 마음도 힘도 있는 사람이 있다는 걸 아는 편이 아주 마땅해요.”"]], [["\"But my father is here.\"", "“하지만 우리 아버지가 여기 계신걸요.”"]], [["\"Yes, but it is so disagreeable for clergymen to have to quarrel with each other; and circumstanced as your father is just at this moment, it would be very inexpedient that there should be anything unpleasant between him and Mr.", "“그렇지요."], ["Slope.", "하지만 성직자끼리 다투는 건 여간 거북한 일이 아니에요."], ["Surely you and Bertie are intimate enough for you to permit him to take your part.\"", "게다가 지금 당장 아버님이 처하신 형편으로 보면, 아버님과 슬로프 씨 사이에 불편한 일이 생기는 건 아주 이롭지 못해요. 당신과 버티는 그 애더러 당신 편을 들라고 해도 될 만큼은 가까운 사이잖아요.”"]], [["Charlotte Stanhope was very anxious that her brother should at once on that very day settle matters with his future wife.", "샬럿 스탠호프는 제 동생이 바로 그날 당장 장래의 아내와 일을 매듭짓기를 몹시 바라고 있었다."], ["Things had now come to that point between him and his father, and between him and his creditors, that he must either do so, or leave Barchester; either do that, or go back to his unwashed associates, dirty lodgings, and poor living at Carrara.", "동생과 아버지 사이, 그리고 동생과 빚쟁이들 사이의 일이 이제 그 지경에 이른 터라, 그는 그렇게 하든지 아니면 바체스터를 떠나든지 해야 했다. 그렇게 하든지, 아니면 씻지 않는 패거리와 지저분한 하숙과 궁핍한 살림이 기다리는 카라라로 돌아가든지 해야 했다."], ["Unless he could provide himself with an income, he must go to Carrara, or to ----.", "스스로 수입을 마련하지 못하면 카라라로 가거나, 아니면 —— 로 가야 했다."], ["His father the prebendary had not said this in so many words, but had he done so, he could not have signified it more plainly.", "참사회원인 아버지가 그렇게 말로 못 박은 것은 아니었으나, 설령 말로 했더라도 그보다 더 분명히 뜻을 전할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["Such being the state of the case it was very necessary that no more time should be lost.", "사정이 이러했으니 더는 시간을 허비해서는 안 되었다."], ["Charlotte had seen her brother's apathy, when he neglected to follow Mrs. Bold out of the room, with anger which she could hardly suppress.", "샬럿은 동생이 볼드 부인을 따라 방을 나서지 않고 굼뜨게 구는 꼴을 좀처럼 억누르기 어려운 노기를 품고 지켜본 터였다."], ["It was grievous to think that Mr. Slope should have so distanced him.", "슬로프 씨가 동생을 그토록 앞질렀다는 것은 생각만 해도 통탄할 노릇이었다."], ["Charlotte felt that she had played her part with sufficient skill.", "샬럿은 제 몫의 일은 충분히 솜씨 있게 해냈다고 여겼다."], ["She had brought them together and induced such a degree of intimacy that her brother was really relieved from all trouble and labour in the matter.", "그는 둘을 맺어 주었고, 동생이 이 일로 어떤 수고도 애도 들일 필요가 없을 만큼 두 사람을 가깝게 만들어 놓았다."], ["And moreover it was quite plain that Mrs. Bold was very fond of Bertie.", "게다가 볼드 부인이 버티를 몹시 마음에 들어 한다는 것도 아주 뚜렷했다."], ["And now it was plain enough also that he had nothing to fear from his rival, Mr. Slope.", "그리고 이제는 동생이 경쟁자인 슬로프 씨를 겁낼 것 없다는 사실 또한 뚜렷해졌다."]], [["There was certainly an awkwardness in subjecting Mrs. Bold to a second offer on the same day.", "볼드 부인에게 같은 날 두 번째 청혼을 받게 하는 데에는 물론 어색한 구석이 있었다."], ["It would have been well perhaps to have put the matter off for a week, could a week have been spared.", "한 주쯤 미룰 여유가 있었다면 아마 그러는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["But circumstances are frequently too peremptory to be arranged as we would wish to arrange them, and such was the case now.", "그러나 사정이란 우리가 바라는 대로 다룰 수 없을 만큼 성급할 때가 잦은 법이고, 지금이 바로 그러했다."], ["This being so, could not this affair of Mr. Slope's be turned to advantage?", "그렇다면 이 슬로프 씨 사건을 되레 이롭게 돌려쓸 수는 없을까?"], ["Could it not be made the excuse for bringing Bertie and Mrs. Bold into still closer connexion--into such close connexion that they could not fail to throw themselves into each other's arms?", "이것을 구실로 삼아 버티와 볼드 부인을 한층 더 가까이 붙여 놓을 수는, 서로의 품에 뛰어들지 않고는 못 배길 만큼 바싹 붙여 놓을 수는 없을까?"], ["Such was the game which Miss Stanhope now at a moment's notice resolved to play.", "스탠호프 양이 그 자리에서 즉각 벌이기로 작정한 노름은 바로 이런 것이었다."]], [["And very well she played it. In the first place it was arranged that Mr.", "그리고 그는 그 노름을 아주 잘 해냈다."], ["Slope should not return in the Stanhopes' carriage to Barchester.", "우선 슬로프 씨는 스탠호프 집안의 마차로 바체스터에 돌아가지 못하도록 정해졌다."], ["It so happened that Mr. Slope was already gone, but of that of course they knew nothing.", "마침 슬로프 씨는 이미 떠나고 없었으나, 물론 그들은 그런 줄을 알지 못했다."], ["The signora should be induced to go first, with only the servants and her sister, and Bertie should take Mr. Slope's place in the second journey.", "시뇨라를 구슬려 하인들과 제 언니만 데리고 먼저 가게 하고, 두 번째 편에 버티가 슬로프 씨의 자리를 차지하기로 했다."], ["Bertie was to be told in confidence of the whole affair, and when the carriage was gone off with its first load, Eleanor was to be left under Bertie's special protection, so as to insure her from any further aggression from Mr. Slope.", "버티에게는 이 일의 전말을 은밀히 일러 두고, 첫 짐을 싣고 마차가 떠나고 나면 엘리너를 버티의 각별한 보호 아래 남겨 슬로프 씨가 더는 침범하지 못하도록 하기로 했다."], ["While the carriage was getting ready, Bertie was to seek out that gentleman and make him understand that he must provide himself with another conveyance back to Barchester.", "마차를 채비하는 동안 버티는 그 신사를 찾아내어, 바체스터로 돌아갈 다른 탈것을 스스로 마련해야 한다는 것을 알아듣게 하기로 했다."], ["Their immediate object should be to walk about together in search of Bertie.", "그러니 두 사람이 당장 할 일은 함께 거닐며 버티를 찾는 것이어야 했다."], ["Bertie in short was to be the Pegasus on whose wings they were to ride out of their present dilemma.", "요컨대 버티는 그들이 지금의 곤경에서 타고 날아오를 페가수스인 셈이었다."]], [["There was a warmth of friendship and cordial kindliness in all this that was very soothing to the widow; but yet, though she gave way to it, she was hardly reconciled to doing so.", "이 모든 것에는 미망인의 마음을 매우 누그러뜨리는 따뜻한 우정과 살가운 친절이 담겨 있었다. 그러나 그는 거기에 몸을 맡기면서도 그렇게 하는 것이 썩 마음 편하지는 않았다."], ["It never occurred to her that, now that she had killed one dragon, another was about to spring up in her path; she had no remote idea that she would have to encounter another suitor in her proposed protector, but she hardly liked the thought of putting herself so much into the hands of young Stanhope.", "용 하나를 잡고 나니 또 다른 용이 제 앞길에 솟아오르려 한다는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 보호자로 내세운 사람에게서 또 하나의 구혼자를 만나게 되리라고는 조금도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["She felt that if she wanted protection, she should go to her father.", "다만 그는 젊은 스탠호프의 손에 저를 그토록 많이 내맡긴다는 것이 그리 내키지 않았다."], ["She felt that she should ask him to provide a carriage for her back to Barchester.", "보호가 필요하다면 아버지에게 가야 한다고 그는 느꼈다. 아버지에게 바체스터로 돌아갈 마차를 마련해 달라고 청해야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["Mrs. Clantantram she knew would give her a seat.", "클랜탠트럼 부인이라면 자리 하나쯤 내주리라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["She knew that she should not throw herself entirely upon friends whose friendship dated, as it were, but from yesterday.", "말하자면 어제부터 사귄 것이나 다름없는 벗들에게 저를 온통 내던져서는 안 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["But yet she could not say no to one who was so sisterly in her kindness, so eager in her good nature, so comfortably sympathetic as Charlotte Stanhope.", "그러나 샬럿 스탠호프처럼 자매같이 다정하고, 그토록 열심히 호의를 베풀고, 그토록 마음 편히 동정해 주는 사람에게 그는 아니라고 말할 수가 없었다."], ["And thus she gave way to all the propositions made to her.", "그리하여 그는 제게 건네진 모든 제안에 몸을 맡겼다."]], [["They first went into the dining-room, looking for their champion, and from thence to the drawing-room.", "두 사람은 저희 투사를 찾아 먼저 식당으로 갔다가, 거기서 응접실로 갔다."], ["Here they found Mr. Arabin, still hanging over the signora's sofa; or rather they found him sitting near her head, as a physician might have sat had the lady been his patient.", "거기서 그들은 애러빈 씨가 여전히 시뇨라의 소파 위로 몸을 기울이고 있는 것을, 아니 그보다는 그 부인이 제 환자이기라도 한 양 의사가 앉듯 그 머리맡에 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["There was no other person in the room.", "방에는 다른 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["The guests were some in the tent, some few still in the dining room, some at the bows and arrows, but most of them walking with Miss Thorne through the park and looking at the games that were going on.", "손님들은 더러는 천막에, 더러는 아직 식당에, 더러는 활쏘기 자리에 있었으나, 대부분은 손 양을 따라 정원을 거닐며 벌어지고 있는 놀이들을 구경하는 중이었다."]], [["All that had passed, and was passing between Mr. Arabin and the lady, it is unnecessary to give in detail.", "애러빈 씨와 그 부인 사이에 오갔고 또 오가고 있던 이야기를 낱낱이 옮길 필요는 없다."], ["She was doing with him as she did with all others.", "시뇨라는 남들에게 하던 대로 그에게도 하고 있었다."], ["It was her mission to make fools of men, and she was pursuing her mission with Mr. Arabin.", "남자들을 바보로 만드는 것이 그의 사명이었고, 그는 애러빈 씨를 상대로 그 사명을 수행하는 중이었다."], ["She had almost got him to own his love for Mrs. Bold and had subsequently almost induced him to acknowledge a passion for herself.", "그는 애러빈 씨에게서 볼드 부인을 사랑한다는 고백을 거의 받아 냈고, 뒤이어 제게 연정을 품었다는 시인까지 거의 이끌어 냈다."], ["He, poor man, was hardly aware what he was doing or saying, hardly conscious whether he was in heaven or in hell.", "가엾게도 그 사람은 제가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못했고, 제가 천국에 있는지 지옥에 있는지도 거의 의식하지 못했다."], ["So little had he known of female attractions of that peculiar class which the signora owned, that he became affected with a kind of temporary delirium when first subjected to its power.", "시뇨라가 지닌 그 독특한 부류의 여성적 매력을 그는 워낙 몰랐던 터라, 그 힘에 처음으로 내맡겨지자 일종의 일시적인 착란에 걸리고 말았다."], ["He lost his head rather than this heart, and toppled about mentally, reeling in his ideas as a drunken man does on his legs.", "그는 마음보다 정신을 잃었고, 술 취한 사람이 다리로 비틀거리듯 머릿속에서 생각들이 휘청거리며 이리저리 넘어졌다."], ["She had whispered to him words that really meant nothing but which, coming from such beautiful lips and accompanied by such lustrous glances, seemed to have a mysterious significance, which he felt though he could not understand.", "시뇨라가 그에게 속삭인 말들은 실상 아무 뜻도 없는 것이었으나, 그토록 아름다운 입술에서 나오고 그토록 빛나는 눈길이 곁들여지니 무언가 신비로운 의미를 띤 듯했고, 그는 그것을 이해하지는 못하면서도 느꼈다."]], [["In being thus besirened, Mr. Arabin behaved himself very differently from Mr.", "이렇듯 세이렌에게 홀리면서도 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨와는 아주 다르게 처신했다."], ["Slope. The signora had said truly that the two men were the contrasts of each other--that the one was all for action, the other all for thought.", "두 남자가 서로의 대조라고, 하나는 온통 행동이요 다른 하나는 온통 사색이라고 한 시뇨라의 말은 옳았다."], ["Mr. Slope, when this lady laid upon his senses the overpowering breath of her charms, immediately attempted to obtain some fruition, to achieve some mighty triumph.", "슬로프 씨는 이 부인이 제 감각 위에 그 매력의 숨결을 압도적으로 끼얹자 곧바로 무언가 결실을 얻으려, 무언가 대단한 승리를 거두려 들었다."], ["He began by catching at her hand and progressed by kissing it.", "그는 그 손을 붙잡는 것으로 시작해 거기에 입 맞추는 것으로 나아갔다."], ["He made vows of love and asked for vows in return.", "사랑을 맹세하고 그 대가로 맹세를 요구했다."], ["He promised everlasting devotion, knelt before her, and swore that had she been on Mount Ida, Juno would have had no cause to hate the offspring of Venus.", "영원한 헌신을 약속하고 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으며, 만일 그 부인이 이다산에 있었더라면 유노가 베누스의 자식을 미워할 까닭이 없었으리라고 장담했다."], ["But Mr. Arabin uttered no oaths, kept his hand mostly in his trousers pocket, and had no more thought of kissing Madame Neroni than of kissing the Countess De Courcy.", "그러나 애러빈 씨는 아무 맹세도 입에 올리지 않았고, 손은 대개 바지 주머니에 넣어 두었으며, 네로니 부인에게 입 맞출 생각이라고는 드 코시 백작부인에게 입 맞출 생각만큼이나 없었다."]], [["As soon as Mr. Arabin saw Mrs.", "애러빈 씨는 볼드 부인이 방에 들어오는 것을 보자마자 얼굴을 붉히며 의자에서 일어섰다가, 다시 앉았다가, 또다시 일어섰다."], ["Bold enter the room he blushed and rose from his chair; then he sat down again, and then again got up. The signora saw the blush at once and smiled at the poor victim, but Eleanor was too much confused to see anything.", "시뇨라는 그 홍조를 대번에 알아채고 가엾은 제물을 향해 미소 지었으나, 엘리너는 너무 심란한 나머지 아무것도 보지 못했다."]], [["\"Oh, Madeline,\"", "“오, 마들린.” 샬럿이 말했다."], ["said Charlotte, \"I want to speak to you particularly; we must arrange about the carriage, you know,\"", "“너한테 꼭 할 이야기가 있어. 마차 일을 정해야 하잖아.”"], ["and she stooped down to whisper to her sister. Mr.", "그러고는 몸을 굽혀 동생에게 귓속말을 했다."], ["Arabin immediately withdrew to a little distance, and as Charlotte had in fact much to explain before she could make the new carriage arrangement intelligible, he had nothing to do but to talk to Mrs. Bold.", "애러빈 씨는 곧바로 조금 떨어진 곳으로 물러났는데, 실상 샬럿이 새로운 마차 배정을 알아듣게 하려면 설명할 것이 많았으므로 그로서는 볼드 부인과 이야기하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다."]], [["\"We have had a very pleasant party,\"", "“아주 즐거운 모임이었지요.”"], ["said he, using the tone he would have used had he declared that the sun was shining very brightly, or the rain falling very fast.", "그가 말했다. 해가 매우 밝게 비친다거나 비가 매우 세차게 내린다고 말할 때 썼을 법한 어조였다."]], [["\"Very,\"", "“네, 그렇네요.”"], ["said Eleanor, who never in her life had passed a more unpleasant day.", "살면서 이보다 더 불쾌한 하루를 보낸 적이 없는 엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"I hope Mr. Harding has enjoyed himself.\"", "“하딩 씨께서도 즐겁게 지내셨으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, very much,\"", "“아, 네, 아주 즐거우셨어요.”"], ["said Eleanor, who had not seen her father since she parted from him soon after her arrival.", "도착하고 얼마 안 되어 헤어진 뒤로 아버지를 보지 못한 엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"He returns to Barchester to-night, I suppose.\"", "“오늘 밤 바체스터로 돌아가시겠지요.”"]], [["\"Yes, I believe so--that is, I think he is staying at Plumstead.\"", "“네, 그러실 거예요. 아니, 그러니까 플럼스테드에 묵으시는 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"Oh, staying at Plumstead,\"", "“아, 플럼스테드에 묵으시는군요.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"He came from there this morning.", "“오늘 아침에 거기서 오셨어요."], ["I believe he is going back, he didn't exactly say, however.\"", "돌아가실 것 같은데, 딱히 말씀은 없으셨어요.”"]], [["\"I hope Mrs. Grantly is quite well.\"", "“그랜틀리 부인도 안녕하시겠지요.”"]], [["\"She seemed to be quite well.", "“아주 잘 지내시는 것 같았어요."], ["She is here; that is, unless she has gone away.\"", "여기 와 계세요. 아니, 벌써 가시지 않았다면요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, to be sure.", "“아, 네, 그렇지요."], ["I was talking to her.", "나도 이야기를 나눴어요."], ["Looking very well indeed.\"", "정말 좋아 보이시더군요.”"], ["Then there was a considerable pause; for Charlotte could not at once make Madeline understand why she was to be sent home in a hurry without her brother.", "그러고는 한참 동안 말이 끊겼다. 샬럿이 마들린에게 어째서 버티도 없이 서둘러 먼저 돌아가야 하는지를 대번에 납득시키지 못한 까닭이었다."]], [["\"Are you returning to Plumstead, Mrs. Bold?\"", "“볼드 부인, 플럼스테드로 돌아가시나요?”"], ["Mr. Arabin merely asked this by way of making conversation, but he immediately perceived that he was approaching dangerous ground.", "애러빈 씨는 그저 말을 잇느라 이렇게 물었을 뿐이었으나, 제가 위험한 땅에 다가서고 있음을 곧바로 알아차렸다."]], [["\"No,\" said Mrs.", "“아니요.”"], ["Bold very quietly; \"I am going home to Barchester.\"", "볼드 부인이 아주 조용히 말했다. “바체스터의 집으로 돌아가요.”"]], [["\"Oh, ah, yes.", "“아, 아, 네."], ["I had forgotten that you had returned.\" And then Mr.", "돌아오신 걸 깜빡했군요.”"], ["Arabin, finding it impossible to say anything further, stood silent till Charlotte had completed her plans, and Mrs. Bold stood equally silent, intently occupied as it appeared in the arrangement of her rings.", "그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 더 할 말을 찾지 못한 채 샬럿이 계획을 다 짤 때까지 말없이 서 있었고, 볼드 부인 또한 제 반지들을 매만지는 데 골똘히 빠진 듯한 모습으로 마찬가지로 말없이 서 있었다."]], [["And yet these two people were thoroughly in love with each other; and though one was a middle-aged clergyman, and the other a lady at any rate past the wishy-washy bread-and-butter period of life, they were as unable to tell their own minds to each other as any Damon and Phillis, whose united ages would not make up that to which Mr.", "그런데도 이 두 사람은 서로에게 흠뻑 빠져 있었다."], ["Arabin had already attained.", "한 사람은 중년의 성직자였고 다른 한 사람은 아무리 보아도 밍밍한 빵과 버터 같은 시절은 지난 부인이었건만, 둘은 나이를 합쳐도 애러빈 씨가 이미 이른 나이에 미치지 못할 어떤 다몬과 필리스 못지않게 제 마음을 서로에게 말할 줄 몰랐다."]], [["Madeline Neroni consented to her sister's proposal, and then the two ladies again went off in quest of Bertie Stanhope.", "마들린 네로니는 언니의 제안에 동의했고, 그러고 나서 두 부인은 다시 버티 스탠호프를 찾아 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII. Ullathorne Sports--Act III", "# 제42장. 울라손 운동회 — 제3막"]], [["And now Miss Thorne's guests were beginning to take their departure, and the amusement of those who remained was becoming slack.", "이제 손 양의 손님들은 하나둘 자리를 뜨기 시작했고, 남은 이들의 흥도 시들해져 갔다."], ["It was getting dark, and ladies in morning costumes were thinking that, if they were to appear by candlelight, they ought to readjust themselves.", "날이 저물어 가고 있었고, 낮 나들이옷 차림의 부인들은 촛불 아래에 모습을 드러낼 요량이라면 매무새를 고쳐야 하리라 생각하는 중이었다."], ["Some young gentlemen had been heard to talk so loud that prudent mammas determined to retire judiciously, and the more discreet of the male sex, whose libations had been moderate, felt that there was not much more left for them to do.", "몇몇 젊은 신사가 목소리를 하도 높여 떠드는 통에 조심성 있는 어머니들은 슬기롭게 물러나기로 마음을 정했고, 술을 절제한 축에 드는 신중한 남자들은 이제 더 할 일이 별로 남지 않았다고 느꼈다."]], [["Morning parties, as a rule, are failures.", "낮 연회란 대체로 실패하기 마련이다."], ["People never know how to get away from them gracefully.", "사람들은 거기서 우아하게 빠져나오는 법을 도무지 알지 못한다."], ["A picnic on an island or a mountain or in a wood may perhaps be permitted.", "섬이나 산이나 숲에서 벌이는 소풍이라면 혹 봐줄 만하다."], ["There is no master of the mountain bound by courtesy to bid you stay while in his heart he is longing for your departure.", "산에는 속으로는 당신이 떠나 주기를 간절히 바라면서도 예의상 더 머무르라 청해야 하는 주인이 없기 때문이다."], ["But in a private house or in private grounds a morning party is a bore.", "그러나 남의 집이나 남의 정원에서 열리는 낮 연회는 지겨운 노릇이다."], ["One is called on to eat and drink at unnatural hours.", "어정쩡한 시각에 먹고 마시기를 강요당한다."], ["One is obliged to give up the day, which is useful, and is then left without resource for the evening, which is useless.", "쓸모 있는 낮을 통째로 내주고 나면, 정작 쓸모없는 저녁에는 아무 방도가 남지 않는다."], ["One gets home fagged and désoeuvré, and yet at an hour too early for bed.", "지친 몸에 désoeuvré 한 기분으로 집에 돌아오지만 잠자리에 들기에는 아직 이른 시각이다."], ["There is no comfortable resource left.", "편안하게 기댈 만한 것이 아무것도 없다."], ["Cards in these genteel days are among the things tabooed, and a rubber of whist is impracticable.", "이 점잖은 시대에 카드놀이는 금기시되는 것 가운데 하나요, 휘스트 한 판은 어림도 없는 일이다."]], [["All this began now to be felt.", "이제 이 모든 것이 느껴지기 시작했다."], ["Some young people had come with some amount of hope that they might get up a dance in the evening, and were unwilling to leave till all such hope was at an end.", "젊은 축 가운데 더러는 저녁에 춤판이라도 벌어질까 하는 기대를 품고 왔던 터라 그런 희망이 완전히 사라지기 전에는 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았다."], ["Others, fearful of staying longer than was expected, had ordered their carriages early, and were doing their best to go, solicitous for their servants and horses.", "또 더러는 예상보다 오래 머무르게 될까 저어하여 일찌감치 마차를 대령시켜 놓고, 하인과 말을 염려하며 어떻게든 떠나려고 애를 썼다."], ["The countess and her noble brood were among the first to leave, and as regarded the Hon. George, it was certainly time that he did so.", "백작부인과 그 고귀한 자식들은 가장 먼저 떠난 축에 들었는데, 아너러블 조지로 말하자면 그럴 때가 되고도 남았다."], ["Her ladyship was in a great fret and fume.", "백작부인은 몹시 초조하고 안달이 나 있었다."], ["Those horrid roads would, she was sure, be the death of her if unhappily she were caught in them by the dark night.", "그 끔찍한 길들은, 불행히도 캄캄한 밤에 그 길에 갇히기라도 하면 틀림없이 자기 목숨을 앗아 가고야 말리라는 것이었다."], ["The lamps she was assured were good, but no lamp could withstand the jolting of the roads of East Barsetshire.", "마차 등이 좋다는 말은 들었지만, 동부 바셋셔의 길이 흔들어 대는 데야 어떤 등인들 견디겠는가."], ["The De Courcy property lay in the western division of the county.", "드 코시 가문의 영지는 이 주의 서부에 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie could not stay when the countess was gone.", "백작부인이 떠난 마당에 프라우디 부인이 남아 있을 수는 없었다."], ["So the bishop was searched for by the Revs. Messrs.", "그리하여 그레이 목사와 그린 목사가 주교를 찾아 나섰고, 천막 한구석에서 주간 평의회를 논하며 한껏 즐거워하고 있는 그를 찾아냈다."], ["Grey and Green and found in one corner of the tent enjoying himself thoroughly in a disquisition on the hebdomadal board. He obeyed, however, the behests of his lady without finishing the sentence in which he was promising to Dr.", "그러나 그는 옥스퍼드에서 그윈 박사의 권위가 조금도 손상되지 않게 하겠노라 약속하던 문장을 채 맺지도 못한 채 부인의 분부에 따랐고, 주교의 말들은 주교관 마구간 쪽으로 코를 돌렸다."], ["Gwynne that his authority at Oxford should remain unimpaired, and the episcopal horses turned their noses towards the palatial stables. Then the Grantlys went.", "이어서 그랜틀리 내외가 떠났다."], ["Before they did so, Mr. Harding managed to whisper a word into his daughter's ear.", "떠나기 전에 하딩 씨는 딸의 귀에 한마디를 속삭일 짬을 냈다."], ["Of course, he said, he would undeceive the Grantlys as to that foolish rumour about Mr. Slope.", "물론 슬로프 씨에 관한 그 어리석은 소문에 대해서는 그랜틀리 내외의 오해를 풀어 주겠노라고 그는 말했다."]], [["\"No, no, no,\"", "“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"pray do not--pray wait till I see you.", "엘리너가 말했다. “제발 그러지 마세요 — 부디 제가 뵐 때까지 기다려 주세요."], ["You will be home in a day or two, and then I will explain to you everything.\"", "하루 이틀이면 집에 오실 테니, 그때 제가 아버지께 모든 것을 설명해 드릴게요.”"]], [["\"I shall be home to-morrow,\"", "“나는 내일이면 집에 돌아간단다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"I am so glad,\"", "“정말 잘됐어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"You will come and dine with me, and then we shall be so comfortable.\"", "“저희 집에 오셔서 저녁을 드세요. 그러면 우리 참 편안하게 지낼 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding promised.", "하딩 씨는 그러겠노라 약속했다."], ["He did not exactly know what there was to be explained, or why Dr. Grantly's mind should not be disabused of the mistake into which he had fallen, but nevertheless he promised.", "그는 무엇을 설명해야 한다는 것인지, 어째서 그랜틀리 박사가 빠져 있는 오해를 풀어 주면 안 된다는 것인지 정확히 알지 못했으나 그래도 약속했다."], ["He owed some reparation to his daughter, and he thought that he might best make it by obedience.", "그는 딸에게 갚아야 할 빚이 얼마간 있었고, 순순히 따르는 것이 그것을 갚는 가장 좋은 길이라고 생각했다."]], [["And thus the people were thinning off by degrees as Charlotte and Eleanor walked about in quest of Bertie.", "그렇게 사람들이 차츰 빠져나가는 동안 샬럿과 엘리너는 버티를 찾아 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["Their search might have been long had they not happened to hear his voice.", "그의 목소리가 마침 들려오지 않았더라면 두 사람의 수색은 꽤 길어졌을 것이다."], ["He was comfortably ensconced in the ha-ha, with his back to the sloping side, smoking a cigar, and eagerly engaged in conversation with some youngster from the further side of the county, whom he had never met before, who was also smoking under Bertie's pupilage and listening with open ears to an account given by his companion of some of the pastimes of Eastern clime.", "그는 하하 도랑 안에 편안히 자리 잡고 비스듬한 벽에 등을 기댄 채 시가를 피우면서, 전에 한 번도 만난 적 없는, 이 주 저편에서 온 어떤 젊은이와 열심히 이야기를 나누는 중이었다. 그 젊은이 역시 버티의 가르침을 받아 담배를 피우면서, 동방 나라들의 여러 소일거리에 관해 동행이 들려주는 이야기에 귀를 활짝 열고 듣고 있었다."]], [["\"Bertie, I am seeking you everywhere,\"", "“버티, 온 데를 다 뒤지며 널 찾고 있잖아.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"Come up here at once.\"", "“당장 이리로 올라와.”"]], [["Bertie looked up out of the ha-ha and saw the two ladies before him.", "버티는 하하 도랑 밖으로 고개를 들어 두 부인이 앞에 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["As there was nothing for him but to obey, he got up and threw away his cigar.", "따르는 수밖에 없었으므로 그는 일어나 시가를 던져 버렸다."], ["From the first moment of his acquaintance with her he had liked Eleanor Bold.", "그는 엘리너 볼드와 알게 된 첫 순간부터 그녀가 마음에 들었다."], ["Had he been left to his own devices, had she been penniless, and had it then been quite out of the question that he should marry her, he would most probably have fallen violently in love with her.", "제멋대로 하도록 내버려 두었더라면, 그리고 그녀가 무일푼이어서 그녀와 결혼한다는 것이 애초에 말도 안 되는 일이었더라면, 그는 십중팔구 그녀에게 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠졌을 것이다."], ["But now he could not help regarding her somewhat as he did the marble workshops at Carrara, as he had done his easel and palette, as he had done the lawyer's chambers in London--in fact, as he had invariably regarded everything by which it had been proposed to him to obtain the means of living.", "그러나 이제 그는 그녀를 카라라의 대리석 공방을 보듯이, 이젤과 팔레트를 보듯이, 런던의 변호사 사무실을 보듯이 — 요컨대 먹고살 방편을 마련하라며 제 앞에 내밀어진 모든 것을 늘 보아 온 그 눈으로 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Eleanor Bold appeared before him, no longer as a beautiful woman, but as a new profession called matrimony.", "엘리너 볼드는 이제 그의 앞에 아름다운 여인이 아니라 혼인이라는 이름의 새 직업으로 나타났다."], ["It was a profession indeed requiring but little labour, and one in which an income was insured to him.", "그것은 과연 품이 별로 들지 않으면서도 수입이 보장되는 직업이었다."], ["But nevertheless he had been as it were goaded on to it; his sister had talked to him of Eleanor, just as she had talked of busts and portraits.", "그럼에도 그는 말하자면 등을 떠밀려 그리로 몰려온 것이었다. 누이는 흉상과 초상화를 이야기하듯 그에게 엘리너를 이야기했다."], ["Bertie did not dislike money, but he hated the very thought of earning it.", "버티는 돈을 싫어하지는 않았으나 그것을 벌어야 한다는 생각만은 질색이었다."], ["He was now called away from his pleasant cigar to earn it, by offering himself as a husband to Mrs. Bold.", "그런데 지금 그는 볼드 부인에게 남편이 되겠노라 자청함으로써 돈을 벌기 위해 그 즐거운 시가에서 불려 나온 참이었다."], ["The work indeed was made easy enough, for in lieu of his having to seek the widow, the widow had apparently come to seek him.", "게다가 그 일은 아주 수월하게 마련되어 있었으니, 그가 미망인을 찾아 나설 것도 없이 미망인 쪽에서 그를 찾아온 모양새였기 때문이다."]], [["He made some sudden absurd excuse to his auditor and then, throwing away his cigar, climbed up the wall of the ha-ha and joined the ladies on the lawn.", "그는 제 이야기를 듣던 젊은이에게 불쑥 터무니없는 핑계를 대고는 시가를 던져 버리고 하하 도랑의 벽을 기어올라 잔디밭의 두 부인에게 합류했다."]], [["\"Come and give Mrs. Bold an arm,\"", "“이리 와서 볼드 부인께 팔을 내드려.”"], ["said Charlotte, \"while I set you on a piece of duty which, as a preux chevalier, you must immediately perform.", "샬럿이 말했다. “그동안 내가 너에게 임무를 하나 맡길 테니, preux chevalier로서 즉시 수행하도록 해."], ["Your personal danger will, I fear, be insignificant, as your antagonist is a clergyman.\"", "다만 네가 몸소 무릅쓸 위험은 유감스럽게도 보잘것없을 거야. 상대가 성직자니까.”"]], [["Bertie immediately gave his arm to Eleanor, walking between her and his sister.", "버티는 곧바로 엘리너에게 팔을 내주고 그녀와 누이 사이에 서서 걸었다."], ["He had lived too long abroad to fall into the Englishman's habit of offering each an arm to two ladies at the same time--a habit, by the by, which foreigners regard as an approach to bigamy, or a sort of incipient Mormonism.", "그는 외국에서 너무 오래 산 탓에 두 부인에게 양쪽 팔을 동시에 내주는 영국식 습관에는 물들지 않았다 — 말이 나왔으니 말이지만, 외국인들은 그 습관을 중혼에 가까운 것, 혹은 일종의 초기 모르몬교쯤으로 여긴다."]], [["The little history of Mr. Slope's misconduct was then told to Bertie by his sister, Eleanor's ears tingling the while.", "이어 누이는 슬로프 씨의 무례한 행실에 관한 짧은 내력을 버티에게 들려주었고, 그러는 동안 엘리너의 귀는 화끈거렸다."], ["And well they might tingle.", "화끈거릴 만도 했다."], ["If it were necessary to speak of the outrage at all, why should it be spoken of to such a person as Mr. Stanhope, and why in her own hearing?", "그 봉변을 굳이 입에 올려야 한다면, 어째서 하필 스탠호프 씨 같은 사람에게, 그것도 어째서 제가 듣는 자리에서 이야기해야 한단 말인가?"], ["She knew she was wrong, and was unhappy and dispirited, yet she could think of no way to extricate herself, no way to set herself right. Charlotte spared her as much as she possibly could, spoke of the whole thing as though Mr.", "그녀는 자기가 잘못했다는 것을 알았고, 불행하고 풀이 죽었으나, 이 자리에서 빠져나갈 길도 제 처지를 바로잡을 길도 도무지 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Slope had taken a glass of wine too much, said that of course there would be nothing more about it, but that steps must be taken to exclude Mr. Slope from the carriage.", "샬럿은 될 수 있는 대로 그녀를 감싸 주어, 마치 슬로프 씨가 포도주를 한 잔 과하게 마셨을 뿐인 것처럼 그 일을 이야기했고, 물론 더 문제 삼을 것은 없겠지만 슬로프 씨를 마차에서 빼기 위한 조치는 취해야 한다고 말했다."]], [["\"Mrs. Bold need be under no alarm about that,\"", "“볼드 부인께서 그 일로 걱정하실 것 없어요.”"], ["said Bertie, \"for Mr.", "버티가 말했다."], ["Slope has gone this hour past.", "“슬로프 씨는 한 시간 전에 벌써 갔으니까요."], ["He told me that business made it necessary that he should start at once for Barchester.\"", "볼일이 있어서 곧장 바체스터로 출발해야 한다고 저에게 말하더군요.”"]], [["\"He is not so tipsy, at any rate, but what he knows his fault,\"", "“어쨌든 제 잘못을 모를 만큼 취하지는 않았군요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"Well, my dear, that is one difficulty over.", "“자, 그럼 볼드 부인, 걱정거리 하나는 넘긴 셈이에요."], ["Now I'll leave you with your true knight and get Madeline off as quickly as I can.", "이제 부인을 이 충실한 기사에게 맡기고 저는 얼른 마들린을 데리고 나가야겠어요."], ["The carriage is here, I suppose, Bertie?\"", "마차는 와 있겠지, 버티?”"]], [["\"It has been here for the last hour.\"", "“한 시간 전부터 와서 기다리고 있는걸.”"]], [["\"That's well.", "“됐네."], ["Good-bye, my dear.", "그럼 안녕히 계세요."], ["Of course you'll come in to tea.", "물론 차 마시러 저희 집에 오셔야 해요."], ["I shall trust to you to bring her, Bertie, even by force if necessary.\"", "버티, 필요하면 억지로라도 부인을 모셔 오는 일은 너에게 맡길게.”"], ["And so saying, Charlotte ran off across the lawn, leaving her brother alone with the widow.", "그렇게 말하고 샬럿은 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 남동생을 미망인과 단둘이 남겨 두었다."]], [["As Miss Stanhope went off, Eleanor bethought herself that, as Mr. Slope had taken his departure, there no longer existed any necessity for separating Mr. Stanhope from his sister Madeline, who so much needed his aid. It had been arranged that he should remain so as to preoccupy Mr. Slope's place in the carriage, and act as a social policeman to effect the exclusion of that disagreeable gentleman. But Mr. Slope had effected his own exclusion, and there was no possible reason now why Bertie should not go with his sister--at least Eleanor saw none, and she said as much.", "스탠호프 양이 가 버리자 엘리너는 문득, 슬로프 씨가 이미 떠났으니 스탠호프 씨를 그의 도움이 그토록 필요한 누이 마들린에게서 떼어 놓을 이유가 더는 없다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 그가 남기로 한 것은 마차에서 슬로프 씨의 자리를 미리 차지하고 앉아, 그 불쾌한 신사를 배제하는 일종의 사교적 순경 노릇을 하기 위해서였다. 그런데 슬로프 씨가 스스로 알아서 물러났으니 이제 버티가 누이와 함께 가지 못할 까닭은 조금도 없었다 — 적어도 엘리너의 눈에는 그랬고, 그녀는 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, let Charlotte have her own way,\"", "“아, 샬럿이 하자는 대로 두세요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"She has arranged it, and there will be no end of confusion if we make another change.", "“누이가 그렇게 정해 놓았으니, 우리가 또 바꾸면 혼란이 끝이 없을 거예요."], ["Charlotte always arranges everything in our house and rules us like a despot.\"", "샬럿은 우리 집안일을 늘 혼자서 다 정하고, 우리를 폭군처럼 부린답니다.”"]], [["\"But the signora?\"", "“그렇지만 시뇨라는요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, the signora can do very well without me.", "“아, 시뇨라는 저 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어요."], ["Indeed, she will have to do without me,\"", "아니, 앞으로는 저 없이 지내야 할 테고요.”"], ["he added, thinking rather of his studies in Carrara than of his Barchester hymeneals.", "그는 바체스터의 혼사보다 카라라에서의 공부를 염두에 두고 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Why, you are not going to leave us?\"", "“아니, 저희 곁을 떠나실 생각이신가요?”"], ["asked Eleanor.", "엘리너가 물었다."]], [["It has been said that Bertie Stanhope was a man without principle.", "버티 스탠호프는 원칙이라고는 없는 사람이라고 앞에서 말한 바 있다."], ["He certainly was so.", "실제로 그는 그러했다."], ["He had no power of using active mental exertion to keep himself from doing evil.", "그는 악을 저지르지 않으려고 정신을 다잡아 애쓰는 힘이 없었다."], ["Evil had no ugliness in his eyes; virtue no beauty.", "그의 눈에는 악이 추해 보이지 않았고 덕이 아름다워 보이지도 않았다."], ["He was void of any of these feelings which actuate men to do good.", "그는 사람으로 하여금 선을 행하게 하는 감정이라고는 하나도 갖추지 못했다."], ["But he was perhaps equally void of those which actuate men to do evil.", "그러나 아마 사람으로 하여금 악을 행하게 하는 감정 또한 똑같이 갖추지 못했을 것이다."], ["He got into debt with utter recklessness, thinking nothing as to whether the tradesmen would ever be paid or not.", "그는 상인들이 언제 돈을 받게 될지 어떨지는 조금도 헤아리지 않고 그야말로 무모하게 빚을 졌다."], ["But he did not invent active schemes of deceit for the sake of extracting the goods of others.", "그러나 남의 재물을 우려내려고 능동적으로 속임수를 꾸미지는 않았다."], ["If a man gave him credit, that was the man's look-out; Bertie Stanhope troubled himself nothing further.", "누가 그에게 외상을 준다면 그것은 그 사람이 알아서 할 일이었고, 버티 스탠호프는 그 이상 마음 쓰지 않았다."], ["In borrowing money he did the same; he gave people references to \"his governor;\"", "돈을 빌릴 때도 마찬가지였다."], ["told them that the \"old chap\" had a good income; and agreed to pay sixty per cent for the accommodation.", "그는 사람들에게 ‘우리 영감’을 보증인으로 대고, 그 ‘노친네’가 수입이 넉넉하다고 일러 준 다음, 편의를 봐주는 값으로 60퍼센트를 치르기로 합의했다."], ["All this he did without a scruple of conscience; but then he never contrived active villainy.", "이 모든 일을 그는 양심의 거리낌 없이 했다. 그러나 그는 능동적인 악행을 꾸민 적만은 결코 없었다."]], [["In this affair of his marriage it had been represented to him as a matter of duty that he ought to put himself in possession of Mrs.", "이 혼사라는 문제에서도 그는 볼드 부인의 손과 재산을 제 것으로 만드는 것이 마땅한 도리라는 말을 들었고, 처음에는 그 자신도 그렇게 여겼다."], ["Bold's hand and fortune, and at first he had so regarded it.", "그녀에 대해서는 별로 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["About her he had thought but little. It was the customary thing for men situated as he was to marry for money, and there was no reason why he should not do what others around him did.", "그와 같은 처지에 놓인 남자가 돈을 보고 결혼하는 것은 흔한 일이었고, 주위 사람들이 다 하는 일을 그라고 하지 못할 까닭이 없었다."], ["And so he consented.", "그래서 그는 동의했다."], ["But now he began to see the matter in another light.", "그러나 이제 그는 그 일을 다른 빛 아래에서 보기 시작했다."], ["He was setting himself down to catch this woman, as a cat sits to catch a mouse.", "그는 고양이가 쥐를 잡으려고 앉아 있듯 이 여인을 잡으려고 앉아 있는 셈이었다."], ["He was to catch her, and swallow her up, her and her child, and her houses and land, in order that he might live on her instead of on his father.", "그는 그녀를 잡아 삼켜야 했으니, 그녀와 그녀의 아이와 그녀의 집들과 땅까지 통째로 삼켜, 아버지에게 얹혀사는 대신 그녀에게 얹혀살자는 것이었다."], ["There was a cold, calculating, cautious cunning about this quite at variance with Bertie's character.", "여기에는 버티의 천성과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 차갑고 계산적이고 조심스러운 교활함이 있었다."], ["The prudence of the measure was quite as antagonistic to his feelings as the iniquity.", "이 계책의 신중함은 그 부정함 못지않게 그의 감정에 거슬렸다."]], [["And then, should he be successful, what would be the reward?", "그리고 설령 성공한다 한들 그 보상은 무엇인가?"], ["Having satisfied his creditors with half of the widow's fortune, he would be allowed to sit down quietly at Barchester, keeping economical house with the remainder.", "미망인 재산의 절반으로 채권자들을 달래고 나면, 그는 남은 절반으로 알뜰하게 살림을 꾸리며 바체스터에 조용히 눌러앉도록 허락받을 것이다."], ["His duty would be to rock the cradle of the late Mr. Bold's child, and his highest excitement a demure party at Plumstead Rectory, should it ultimately turn out that the archdeacon would be sufficiently reconciled to receive him.", "그의 소임은 고인이 된 볼드 씨의 아이 요람을 흔드는 일이 될 터이고, 그가 누릴 최고의 흥분이란, 부주교가 마침내 그를 맞아들일 만큼 마음을 풀어 준다면, 플럼스테드 목사관에서 열리는 얌전한 모임 정도일 것이다."]], [["There was very little in the programme to allure such a man as Bertie Stanhope.", "버티 스탠호프 같은 사람의 마음을 끌 만한 것이 이 계획서에는 거의 없었다."], ["Would not the Carrara workshop, or whatever worldly career fortune might have in store for him, would not almost anything be better than this?", "카라라의 공방이든, 운명이 그를 위해 마련해 둔 어떤 세속의 진로든, 아니 거의 무엇이든 이것보다는 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["The lady herself was undoubtedly all that was desirable, but the most desirable lady becomes nauseous when she has to be taken as a pill.", "여인 자체야 두말할 나위 없이 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었지만, 아무리 바랄 만한 여인이라도 환약처럼 삼켜야 한다면 구역질이 나는 법이다."], ["He was pledged to his sister, however, and let him quarrel with whom he would, it behoved him not to quarrel with her.", "그러나 그는 누이에게 약속을 한 몸이었고, 누구와 다투든 누이와만은 다투지 않는 것이 옳았다."], ["If she were lost to him, all would be lost that he could ever hope to derive henceforward from the paternal roof-tree.", "누이를 잃는다면 앞으로 아버지의 지붕 아래서 그가 얻어 낼 수 있으리라 기대하던 모든 것을 잃는 셈이었다."], ["His mother was apparently indifferent to his weal or woe, to his wants or his warfare.", "어머니는 그의 안녕에도 불행에도, 그가 무엇이 아쉬운지 무엇과 싸우는지에도 아랑곳하지 않는 듯했다."], ["His father's brow got blacker and blacker from day to day, as the old man looked at his hopeless son.", "아버지의 이마는 가망 없는 아들을 바라볼수록 나날이 더 어두워져 갔다."], ["And as for Madeline--poor Madeline, whom of all of them he liked the best--she had enough to do to shift for herself.", "마들린으로 말하자면 — 온 식구 가운데 그가 가장 좋아하는 가엾은 마들린은 제 앞가림만으로도 벅찼다."], ["No; come what might, he must cling to his sister and obey her behests, let them be ever so stern--or at the very least seem to obey them.", "아니, 어떤 일이 있어도 그는 누이에게 매달려 그 분부를 따라야 했다. 그것이 아무리 모질더라도 — 아니면 적어도 따르는 시늉이라도 해야 했다."], ["Could not some happy deceit bring him through in this matter, so that he might save appearances with his sister and yet not betray the widow to her ruin?", "어떤 그럴듯한 속임수로 이 일을 헤쳐 나가, 누이 앞에서는 체면을 지키면서도 미망인을 파멸에 넘기지 않을 수는 없을까?"], ["What if he made a confederate of Eleanor?", "엘리너를 한패로 끌어들이면 어떨까?"], ["'Twas in this spirit that Bertie Stanhope set about his wooing.", "버티 스탠호프가 구애에 나선 것은 바로 이런 심정에서였다."]], [["\"But you are not going to leave Barchester?\"", "“그렇지만 바체스터를 떠나실 건 아니지요?”"], ["asked Eleanor.", "엘리너가 물었다."]], [["\"I do not know,\"", "“모르겠어요.”"], ["he replied; \"I hardly know yet what I am going to do.", "그가 대답했다. “제가 무엇을 하게 될지 아직 저도 잘 모르겠어요."], ["But it is at any rate certain that I must do something.\"", "하지만 무엇이든 해야 한다는 것만은 어쨌든 분명하고요.”"]], [["\"You mean about your profession?\"", "“직업 말씀이신가요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, about my profession, if you can call it one.\"", "“네, 제 직업 말입니다. 그것을 직업이라고 부를 수 있다면 말이지요.”"]], [["\"And is it not one?\"", "“직업이 아닌가요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"Were I a man, I know none I should prefer to it, except painting.", "“제가 남자라면 그림 말고는 그보다 더 하고 싶은 일이 없을 것 같아요."], ["And I believe the one is as much in your power as the other.\"", "그리고 제가 보기에는 그 두 가지가 다 스탠호프 씨께서 하실 수 있는 일이고요.”"]], [["\"Yes, just about equally so,\"", "“네, 딱 그만큼씩 그렇지요.”"], ["said Bertie with a little touch of inward satire directed at himself.", "버티는 제 자신을 겨눈 얼마간의 속내 섞인 야유를 담아 말했다."], ["He knew in his heart that he would never make a penny by either.", "그는 어느 쪽으로도 한 푼 벌지 못하리라는 것을 마음속으로 알고 있었다."]], [["\"I have often wondered, Mr. Stanhope, why you do not exert yourself more,\"", "“스탠호프 씨, 저는 어째서 좀 더 애쓰지 않으시는지 늘 궁금했어요.”"], ["said Eleanor, who felt a friendly fondness for the man with whom she was walking.", "엘리너가 말했다. 그녀는 함께 걷고 있는 이 남자에게 다정한 호감을 느끼고 있었다."], ["\"But I know it is very impertinent in me to say so.\"", "“그런 말을 하는 게 무척 주제넘은 짓인 줄은 알지만요.”"]], [["\"Impertinent!\"", "“주제넘다뇨!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Not so, but much too kind.", "“그런 게 아니라 너무 과분하게 친절하신 겁니다."], ["It is much too kind in you to take any interest in so idle a scamp.\"", "저 같은 게으른 건달에게 조금이라도 관심을 두시는 것만도 너무 과분한 친절이에요.”"]], [["\"But you are not a scamp, though you are perhaps idle.", "“게으르실지는 몰라도 건달은 아니시잖아요."], ["And I do take an interest in you, a very great interest,\"", "그리고 저는 정말로 관심이 있어요, 아주 큰 관심이요.”"], ["she added in a voice which almost made him resolve to change his mind.", "그가 마음을 고쳐먹을 뻔할 만한 목소리로 그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["\"And when I call you idle, I know you are only so for the present moment.", "“그리고 게으르다고 말했지만, 그건 지금 이 순간만 그러신 거라는 걸 알아요."], ["Why can't you settle steadily to work here in Barchester?\"", "어째서 여기 바체스터에 자리를 잡고 꾸준히 일하실 수는 없나요?”"]], [["\"And make busts of the bishop, dean, and chapter?", "“그래서 주교와 수석 사제와 참사회의 흉상이나 만들라고요?"], ["Or perhaps, if I achieve a great success, obtain a commission to put up an elaborate tombstone over a prebendary's widow, a dead lady with a Grecian nose, a bandeau, and an intricate lace veil; lying of course on a marble sofa from among the legs of which death will be creeping out and poking at his victim with a small toasting-fork.\"", "아니면 크게 성공한다면 참사회원의 미망인 무덤 위에 공들인 묘비를 세우는 일감을 얻을 수도 있겠지요. 그리스식 콧날에 머리띠를 두르고 정교한 레이스 베일을 쓴 죽은 부인이, 물론 대리석 소파에 누워 있고, 그 다리 사이에서는 죽음이 기어 나와 작은 고기 굽는 포크로 제 희생자를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있는 그런 묘비 말입니다.”"]], [["Eleanor laughed, but yet she thought that if the surviving prebendary paid the bill, the object of the artist as a professional man would in a great measure be obtained.", "엘리너는 웃었다. 그러면서도 살아남은 참사회원이 값을 치러 주기만 한다면 직업인으로서 예술가의 목적은 상당 부분 이루어지는 셈이라고 생각했다."]], [["\"I don't know about the dean and chapter and the prebendary's widow,\"", "“수석 사제니 참사회니 참사회원의 미망인이니 하는 건 저야 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"Of course you must take them as they come.", "“물론 오는 대로 받으셔야 하겠지요."], ["But the fact of your having a great cathedral in which such ornaments are required could not but be in your favour.\"", "하지만 그런 장식이 필요한 큰 대성당이 곁에 있다는 사실은 스탠호프 씨께 유리할 수밖에 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"No real artist could descend to the ornamentation of a cathedral,\"", "“진짜 예술가라면 대성당을 장식하는 일 따위로 몸을 낮출 수는 없어요.”"], ["said Bertie, who had his ideas of the high ecstatic ambition of art, as indeed all artists have who are not in receipt of a good income.", "버티가 말했다. 그에게는 예술의 드높고 황홀한 야심에 관한 나름의 생각이 있었으니, 실은 벌이가 시원치 않은 예술가라면 누구나 그런 생각을 품는 법이다."], ["\"Buildings should be fitted to grace the sculpture, not the sculpture to grace the building.\"", "“조각이 건물을 돋보이게 할 것이 아니라, 건물이 조각을 돋보이게 하도록 지어져야 하는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, when the work of art is good enough to merit it.", "“그야 예술 작품이 그럴 만큼 훌륭할 때 이야기지요."], ["Do you, Mr. Stanhope, do something sufficiently excellent and we ladies of Barchester will erect for it a fitting receptacle.", "스탠호프 씨께서 충분히 뛰어난 것을 하나 만들어 내시면, 저희 바체스터 부인들이 그것을 담을 알맞은 자리를 마련해 드리겠어요."], ["Come, what shall the subject be?\"", "자, 무엇을 소재로 하실까요?”"]], [["\"I'll put you in your pony chair, Mrs. Bold, as Dannecker put Ariadne on her lion.", "“단네커가 아리아드네를 사자 위에 앉혔듯이, 저는 볼드 부인을 조랑말 마차에 앉히겠습니다."], ["Only you must promise to sit for me.\"", "다만 저를 위해 모델로 앉아 주시겠다고 약속하셔야 해요.”"]], [["\"My ponies are too tame, I fear, and my broad-brimmed straw hat will not look so well in marble as the lace veil of the prebendary's wife.\"", "“제 조랑말들은 너무 순해서 안 될 것 같고, 챙 넓은 제 밀짚모자는 대리석으로 옮겨 놓으면 참사회원 부인의 레이스 베일만큼 보기 좋지도 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"If you will not consent to that, Mrs. Bold, I will consent to try no other subject in Barchester.\"", "“볼드 부인께서 거기에 응해 주지 않으신다면, 저도 바체스터에서 다른 소재는 시도하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"You are determined then to push your fortune in other lands?\"", "“그러면 다른 나라에 가서 운을 개척하시겠다고 마음을 굳히신 건가요?”"]], [["\"I am determined,\"", "“마음을 굳혔습니다.”"], ["said Bertie slowly and significantly, as he tried to bring up his mind to a great resolve; \"I am determined in this matter to be guided wholly by you.\"", "버티는 큰 결심에 이르려고 애쓰면서 천천히 의미심장하게 말했다. “이 일에 관해서는 전적으로 부인의 인도를 따르기로 마음을 굳혔어요.”"]], [["\"Wholly by me?\"", "“전적으로 제 인도를요?”"], ["said Eleanor, astonished at, and not quite liking, his altered manner.", "엘리너가 말했다. 달라진 그의 태도에 놀랐고, 또 그 태도가 그다지 마음에 들지도 않았다."]], [["\"Wholly by you,\"", "“전적으로 부인의 인도를요.”"], ["said Bertie, dropping his companion's arm and standing before her on the path.", "버티는 동행의 팔을 놓고 오솔길 위에서 그녀 앞에 마주 섰다."], ["In their walk they had come exactly to the spot in which Eleanor had been provoked into slapping Mr. Slope's face.", "걷다 보니 두 사람은 하필 엘리너가 참다못해 슬로프 씨의 뺨을 후려친 바로 그 자리에 이르러 있었다."], ["Could it be possible that this place was peculiarly unpropitious to her comfort?", "이 자리는 그녀의 평온에 유독 불길한 곳이란 말인가?"], ["Could it be possible that she should here have to encounter yet another amorous swain?", "그녀가 여기서 또 한 사람의 사랑에 들뜬 구혼자를 마주해야 한다는 말인가?"]], [["\"If you will be guided by me, Mr.", "“제 인도를 따르시겠다면, 스탠호프 씨, 꾸준하고 끈기 있는 일에 자리를 잡고 앉으세요."], ["Stanhope, you will set yourself down to steady and persevering work, and you will be ruled by your father as to the place in which it will be most advisable for you to do so.\"", "그리고 어디서 그렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지는 아버님의 뜻을 따르시고요.”"]], [["\"Nothing could be more prudent, if only it were practicable.", "“그보다 더 사려 깊은 말씀은 없겠지요, 실행할 수만 있다면요."], ["But now, if you will let me, I will tell you how it is that I will be guided by you, and why.", "하지만 이제 허락하신다면, 제가 어떤 식으로 부인의 인도를 따르겠다는 것인지, 또 어째서 그런지 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Will you let me tell you?\"", "말씀드려도 될까요?”"]], [["\"I really do not know what you can have to tell.\"", "“저에게 무슨 하실 말씀이 있는지 정말 모르겠네요.”"]], [["\"No, you cannot know.", "“그러시겠지요, 아실 리가 없습니다."], ["It is impossible that you should.", "아실 수가 없는 일이니까요."], ["But we have been very good friends, Mrs. Bold, have we not?\"", "그런데 볼드 부인, 우리는 아주 좋은 벗이었지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["\"Yes, I think we have,\"", "“네, 그랬다고 생각해요.”"], ["said she, observing in his demeanour an earnestness very unusual with him.", "그녀는 그의 태도에서 그답지 않은 진지함을 알아채고 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"You were kind enough to say just now that you took an interest in me, and I was perhaps vain enough to believe you.\"", "“조금 전에 부인께서는 친절하게도 저에게 관심이 있다고 말씀하셨고, 저는 어쩌면 주제넘게도 그 말을 믿었습니다.”"]], [["\"There is no vanity in that; I do so as your sister's brother--and as my own friend also.\"", "“거기에 주제넘을 것은 없어요. 저는 스탠호프 씨를 제 벗의 남동생으로서, 또 제 자신의 벗으로서도 그렇게 여기고 있으니까요.”"]], [["\"Well, I don't deserve that you should feel so kindly towards me,\"", "“글쎄요, 저는 부인께서 그렇게 다정하게 여겨 주실 만한 사람이 못 됩니다.”"], ["said Bertie, \"but upon my word I am very grateful for it,\"", "버티가 말했다. “그렇지만 맹세코 정말 고맙게 생각해요.”"], ["and he paused awhile, hardly knowing how to introduce the subject that he had in hand.", "그러고는 손에 쥔 이야기를 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 몰라 잠시 말을 멈추었다."]], [["And it was no wonder that he found it difficult.", "그가 어려워한 것도 무리는 아니었다."], ["He had to make known to his companion the scheme that had been prepared to rob her of her wealth, he had to tell her that he had intended to marry her without loving her, or else that he loved her without intending to marry her; and he had also to bespeak from her not only his own pardon, but also that of his sister, and induce Mrs.", "그는 동행에게 그녀의 재산을 빼앗으려고 마련된 계획을 알려야 했다. 그녀를 사랑하지 않으면서 결혼하려 했노라고, 아니면 결혼할 뜻도 없으면서 그녀를 사랑했노라고 말해야 했다."], ["Bold to protest in her future communion with Charlotte that an offer had been duly made to her and duly rejected.", "게다가 자기 자신에 대한 용서만이 아니라 누이에 대한 용서까지 구해야 했고, 앞으로 샬럿과 이야기를 나눌 때 정식 청혼을 분명히 받았고 분명히 거절했노라고 볼드 부인이 말해 주도록 만들어야 했다."]], [["Bertie Stanhope was not prone to be very diffident of his own conversational powers, but it did seem to him that he was about to tax them almost too far.", "버티 스탠호프는 제 말솜씨를 그리 의심하는 편이 아니었으나, 이번만은 그 솜씨를 거의 지나칠 만큼 시험하게 되리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["He hardly knew where to begin, and he hardly knew where he should end.", "그는 어디서 시작해야 할지도, 어디서 끝맺어야 할지도 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["By this time Eleanor was again walking on slowly by his side, not taking his arm as she had heretofore done but listening very intently for whatever Bertie might have to say to her.", "이때쯤 엘리너는 다시 그의 곁에서 천천히 걷고 있었으나, 여태처럼 그의 팔을 잡지는 않은 채 버티가 무슨 말을 하려는지 몹시 골똘히 귀를 기울이고 있었다."]], [["\"I wish to be guided by you,\"", "“저는 부인의 인도를 받고 싶습니다.”"], ["said he; \"indeed, in this matter there is no one else who can set me right.\"", "그가 말했다. “실은 이 일에서 저를 바로잡아 줄 수 있는 사람은 달리 아무도 없거든요.”"]], [["\"Oh, that must be nonsense,\"", "“아유, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리예요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, listen to me now, Mrs.", "“그럼 이제 제 말을 들어 주십시오, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold, and if you can help it, pray don't be angry with me.\"", "그리고 참으실 수 있다면 부디 저에게 화를 내지 말아 주세요.”"]], [["\"Angry!\"", "“화라니요!”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, indeed you will have cause to be so.", "“아니요, 화를 내실 만한 까닭이 정말 있을 겁니다."], ["You know how very much attached to you my sister Charlotte is.\"", "제 누이 샬럿이 부인을 얼마나 각별히 따르는지는 아시지요.”"]], [["Eleanor acknowledged that she did.", "엘리너는 그렇다고 인정했다."]], [["\"Indeed she is; I never knew her to love anyone so warmly on so short an acquaintance.", "“정말 그렇습니다. 그렇게 짧게 알고 지낸 사이에 누이가 누구를 그토록 따뜻하게 아끼는 것은 저도 처음 봤어요."], ["You know also how well she loves me?\"", "그리고 누이가 저를 얼마나 아끼는지도 아시지요?”"]], [["Eleanor now made no answer, but she felt the blood tingle in her cheek as she gathered from what he said the probable result of this double-barrelled love on the part of Miss Stanhope.", "엘리너는 이번에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 스탠호프 양의 이 쌍발총 같은 애정이 어떤 결말로 이어질지를 그의 말에서 헤아리자 뺨에 피가 확 도는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["\"I am her only brother, Mrs. Bold, and it is not to be wondered at that she should love me.", "“저는 누이의 하나뿐인 남동생이니, 볼드 부인, 누이가 저를 아끼는 것은 이상할 게 없지요."], ["But you do not yet know Charlotte--you do not know how entirely the well-being of our family hangs on her.", "하지만 부인께서는 아직 샬럿을 모르십니다 — 우리 집안의 안녕이 얼마나 오롯이 누이 한 사람에게 매달려 있는지 모르세요."], ["Without her to manage for us, I do not know how we should get on from day to day.", "누이가 우리 대신 살림을 꾸려 주지 않는다면 우리가 하루하루를 어떻게 넘길지 저는 모르겠습니다."], ["You cannot yet have observed all this.\"", "부인께서 아직 이런 것까지 다 보셨을 리는 없지요.”"]], [["Eleanor had indeed observed a good deal of this; she did not, however, now say so, but allowed him to proceed with his story.", "엘리너는 실은 그런 것을 꽤 많이 보아 온 터였다. 그러나 지금은 그렇다고 말하지 않고 그가 이야기를 계속하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["\"You cannot therefore be surprised that Charlotte should be most anxious to do the best for us all.\"", "“그러니 샬럿이 우리 모두를 위해 가장 좋은 길을 찾으려고 그토록 애쓰는 것이 놀랍지는 않으실 겁니다.”"]], [["Eleanor said that she was not at all surprised.", "엘리너는 조금도 놀랍지 않다고 말했다."]], [["\"And she has had a very difficult game to play, Mrs. Bold--a very difficult game.", "“그런데 누이는 아주 어려운 판을 벌여 왔습니다, 볼드 부인 — 아주 어려운 판이지요."], ["Poor Madeline's unfortunate marriage and terrible accident, my mother's ill-health, my father's absence from England, and last, and worse perhaps, my own roving, idle spirit have almost been too much for her.", "가엾은 마들린의 불행한 결혼과 끔찍한 사고, 어머니의 병약함, 아버지가 영국을 떠나 계셨던 일, 그리고 마지막으로, 어쩌면 그 무엇보다 고약하게도 떠돌기만 하는 저의 게으른 성정까지, 누이에게는 거의 감당하기 벅찬 것들이었어요."], ["You cannot wonder if among all her cares one of the foremost is to see me settled in the world.\"", "그 숱한 걱정거리 가운데 저를 세상에 자리 잡게 하는 일이 첫손에 꼽힌다 해도 이상할 게 없지요.”"]], [["Eleanor on this occasion expressed no acquiescence.", "이번에는 엘리너도 맞장구를 치지 않았다."], ["She certainly supposed that a formal offer was to be made and could not but think that so singular an exordium was never before made by a gentleman in a similar position.", "그녀는 정식 청혼이 있으리라고 짐작했고, 비슷한 처지에 놓인 신사가 이토록 별난 머리말을 늘어놓은 적은 일찍이 없었으리라 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Slope had annoyed her by the excess of his ardour.", "슬로프 씨는 지나친 열정으로 그녀를 성가시게 했다."], ["It was quite clear that no such danger was to be feared from Mr. Stanhope.", "스탠호프 씨에게서는 그런 위험을 걱정할 필요가 전혀 없다는 것이 아주 분명했다."], ["Prudential motives alone actuated him.", "그를 움직이는 것은 오로지 타산이었다."], ["Not only was he about to make love because his sister told him, but he also took the precaution of explaining all this before he began.", "누이가 시켜서 구애하려는 것도 모자라, 그는 시작하기도 전에 그 사정을 미리 다 설명해 두는 조심성까지 부렸다."], ["'Twas thus, we may presume, that the matter presented itself to Mrs. Bold.", "우리가 짐작하기로는 볼드 부인의 눈에 이 일이 그렇게 비쳤을 것이다."]], [["When he had got so far, Bertie began poking the gravel with a little cane which he carried.", "여기까지 이야기하고 나서 버티는 들고 있던 작은 지팡이로 자갈을 쿡쿡 쑤시기 시작했다."], ["He still kept moving on, but very slowly, and his companion moved slowly by his side, not inclined to assist him in the task the performance of which appeared to be difficult to him.", "그는 여전히 걸음을 옮겼으나 아주 느렸고, 동행도 그 곁에서 느릿느릿 걸으면서 그가 어려워하는 듯한 그 일을 도와줄 마음은 조금도 없었다."]], [["\"Knowing how fond she is of yourself, Mrs. Bold, cannot you imagine what scheme should have occurred to her?\"", "“누이가 부인을 얼마나 좋아하는지 아신다면, 볼드 부인, 누이 머리에 어떤 계책이 떠올랐을지 짐작하지 못하시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"I can imagine no better scheme, Mr. Stanhope, than the one I proposed to you just now.\"", "“스탠호프 씨, 저는 조금 전에 제가 말씀드린 것보다 더 나은 계책은 떠올릴 수가 없네요.”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“그렇지요.”"], ["said he somewhat lackadaisically; \"I suppose that would be the best, but Charlotte thinks another plan might be joined with it.", "그가 다소 맥없이 말했다. “그게 최선이기는 하겠지요."], ["She wants me to marry you.\"", "하지만 샬럿은 거기에 다른 계획을 하나 더 붙일 수 있다고 생각한답니다. 누이는 제가 부인과 결혼하기를 바라요.”"]], [["A thousand remembrances flashed across Eleanor's mind all in a moment--how Charlotte had talked about and praised her brother, how she had continually contrived to throw the two of them together, how she had encouraged all manner of little intimacies, how she had with singular cordiality persisted in treating Eleanor as one of the family.", "천 가지 기억이 한순간에 엘리너의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 샬럿이 남동생 이야기를 꺼내며 칭찬하던 일, 끊임없이 두 사람을 한자리에 몰아넣던 일, 온갖 자잘한 친밀함을 부추기던 일, 유별난 살가움으로 엘리너를 한 식구처럼 대하기를 고집하던 일."], ["All this had been done to secure her comfortable income for the benefit of one of the family!", "그 모든 것이 식구 하나의 이익을 위해 그녀의 넉넉한 수입을 확보하려고 벌인 짓이었다!"]], [["Such a feeling as this is very bitter when it first impresses itself on a young mind.", "이런 느낌은 젊은 마음에 처음 새겨질 때 몹시 쓰라리다."], ["To the old, such plots and plans, such matured schemes for obtaining the goods of this world without the trouble of earning them, such long-headed attempts to convert \"tuum\" into \"meum\"", "나이 든 이들에게는 그런 음모와 계획, 애써 벌지 않고도 이 세상의 재물을 손에 넣으려는 그런 무르익은 계책, “tuum”을 “meum”으로 바꿔 놓으려는 그런 약삭빠른 시도야말로 익숙한 삶의 방식이다."], ["are the ways of life to which they are accustomed. 'Tis thus that many live, and it therefore behoves all those who are well-to-do in the world to be on their guard against those who are not.", "많은 이가 그렇게 살아가니, 세상에서 넉넉한 사람은 누구나 넉넉하지 못한 이들을 경계해야 마땅하다."], ["With them it is the success that disgusts, not the attempt.", "그런 이들에게 역겨운 것은 시도가 아니라 성공이다."], ["But Eleanor had not yet learnt to look on her money as a source of danger; she had not begun to regard herself as fair game to be hunted down by hungry gentlemen.", "그러나 엘리너는 아직 제 돈을 위험의 원천으로 보는 법을 배우지 못했다. 굶주린 신사들에게 사냥당하기 좋은 먹잇감으로 스스로를 여기기 시작하지도 않았다."], ["She had enjoyed the society of the Stanhopes, she had greatly liked the cordiality of Charlotte, and had been happy in her new friends.", "그녀는 스탠호프 일가와 어울리는 것이 즐거웠고, 샬럿의 살가움이 무척 좋았으며, 새로 사귄 벗들 덕에 행복했다."], ["Now she saw the cause of all this kindness, and her mind was opened to a new phase of human life.", "이제 그녀는 그 모든 친절의 까닭을 알게 되었고, 그녀의 마음은 인간 삶의 새로운 국면 앞에 열렸다."]], [["\"Miss Stanhope,\"", "“스탠호프 양께서는,” 그녀가 도도하게 말했다."], ["said she haughtily, \"has been contriving for me a great deal of honour, but she might have saved herself the trouble.", "“저를 위해 크나큰 영광을 마련해 주셨군요. 그렇지만 그런 수고는 하지 않으셔도 좋았을 것을."], ["I am not sufficiently ambitious.\"", "저는 그만한 야심이 없답니다.”"]], [["\"Pray don't be angry with her, Mrs. Bold,\"", "“부디 누이에게 화내지 마십시오, 볼드 부인.”"], ["said he, \"or with me either.\"", "그가 말했다. “저에게도 화내지 마시고요.”"]], [["\"Certainly not with you, Mr. Stanhope,\"", "“스탠호프 씨께야 물론 화내지 않지요.”"], ["said she with considerable sarcasm in her tone.", "그녀가 상당한 빈정거림을 담은 어조로 말했다."], ["\"Certainly not with you.\"", "“스탠호프 씨께야 물론 화내지 않고말고요.”"]], [["\"No--nor with her,\"", "“아니요 — 누이에게도 내지 마십시오.”"], ["said he imploringly.", "그가 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["\"And why, may I ask you, Mr.", "“그러면 여쭈어도 될까요, 스탠호프 씨."], ["Stanhope, have you told me this singular story?", "어째서 이 별난 이야기를 저에게 하신 건가요?"], ["For I may presume I may judge by your manner of telling it that--that--that you and your sister are not exactly of one mind on the subject.\"", "이야기하시는 태도로 미루어 보건대 — 그러니까 — 그러니까 이 문제에서 스탠호프 씨와 누이님의 뜻이 꼭 같지는 않은 모양이니 말이에요.”"]], [["\"No, we are not.\"", "“네, 같지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"And if so,\" said Mrs.", "“그렇다면,”"], ["Bold, who was now really angry with the unnecessary insult which she thought had been offered to her.", "볼드 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 자신이 굳이 받지 않아도 될 모욕을 당했다는 생각에 이제 정말로 화가 나 있었다."], ["\"And if so, why has it been worth your while to tell me all this?\"", "“그렇다면 어째서 저에게 이 모든 이야기를 하실 만한 값어치가 있었던 건가요?”"]], [["\"I did once think, Mrs. Bold--that you--that you--\"", "“저는 한때 이런 생각을 했습니다, 볼드 부인 — 부인께서 — 부인께서 —”"]], [["The widow now again became entirely impassive, and would not lend the slightest assistance to her companion.", "미망인은 이제 다시 완전히 무표정해져서 동행에게 조금의 도움도 주려 하지 않았다."]], [["\"I did once think that you perhaps might--might have been taught to regard me as more than a friend.\"", "“저는 한때 부인께서 어쩌면 — 어쩌면 저를 벗 이상으로 여기게 되실 수도 있으리라 생각했습니다.”"]], [["\"Never!\" said Mrs.", "“결코 그럴 일 없어요!”"], ["Bold, \"never.", "볼드 부인이 말했다."], ["If I have ever allowed myself to do anything to encourage such an idea, I have been very much to blame--very much to blame indeed.\"", "“결코요. 제가 그런 생각을 부추길 만한 일을 조금이라도 스스로에게 허락한 적이 있다면 그건 제 잘못이 아주 큽니다 — 정말이지 아주 큰 잘못이고말고요.”"]], [["\"You never have,\"", "“그러신 적 없습니다.”"], ["said Bertie, who really had a good-natured anxiety to make what he said as little unpleasant as possible.", "버티가 말했다. 그는 제 말이 되도록 덜 언짢게 들리기를 진심으로 인정 있게 걱정하고 있었다."], ["\"You never have, and I have seen for some time that I had no chance--but my sister's hopes ran higher.", "“그러신 적 없으시고, 저도 얼마 전부터 제게 가망이 없다는 것을 알고 있었습니다 — 다만 누이의 기대가 더 컸던 거지요."], ["I have not mistaken you, Mrs.", "저는 부인을 오해하지 않았습니다, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold, though perhaps she has.\"", "누이는 어쩌면 오해했을지 몰라도요.”"]], [["\"Then why have you said all this to me?\"", "“그러면 어째서 저에게 이 모든 말씀을 하신 건가요?”"]], [["\"Because I must not anger her.\"", "“누이를 화나게 해서는 안 되기 때문입니다.”"]], [["\"And will not this anger her? Upon my word, Mr.", "“그럼 이 일은 누이님을 화나게 하지 않는다는 건가요?"], ["Stanhope, I do not understand the policy of your family.", "정말이지, 스탠호프 씨, 저는 댁 식구들의 방침을 이해할 수가 없네요."], ["Oh, how I wish I was at home!\"", "아, 집에 있었으면 얼마나 좋을까!”"], ["And as she expressed the wish she could restrain herself no longer and burst out into a flood of tears.", "그렇게 바람을 입 밖에 내면서 그녀는 더는 스스로를 억누르지 못하고 눈물을 왈칵 쏟아 냈다."]], [["Poor Bertie was greatly moved.", "가엾은 버티는 크게 마음이 움직였다."], ["\"You shall have the carriage to yourself going home,\"", "“돌아가실 때 마차는 부인 혼자 쓰십시오.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; \"at least you and my father.", "“적어도 부인과 제 아버지만요."], ["As for me, I can walk, or for the matter of that it does not much signify what I do.\"", "저야 걸어가면 되고, 사실 제가 어떻게 하든 대수로울 것도 없으니까요.”"], ["He perfectly understood that part of Eleanor's grief arose from the apparent necessity of her going back to Barchester in the carriage with her second suitor.", "엘리너의 슬픔이 얼마간은 두 번째 구혼자와 함께 마차를 타고 바체스터로 돌아가야 할 듯싶은 사정에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그는 훤히 알고 있었다."]], [["This somewhat mollified her.", "이 말에 그녀의 마음이 얼마간 누그러졌다."], ["\"Oh, Mr. Stanhope,\"", "“아, 스탠호프 씨.”"], ["said she, \"why should you have made me so miserable?", "그녀가 말했다. “어째서 저를 이토록 비참하게 만드셔야 했나요?"], ["What will you have gained by telling me all this?\"", "이 모든 말씀을 하셔서 무엇을 얻으시겠어요?”"]], [["He had not even yet explained to her the most difficult part of his proposition; he had not told her that she was to be a party to the little deception which he intended to play off upon his sister.", "그는 제 제안에서 가장 까다로운 대목을 아직 그녀에게 설명하지도 않은 터였다. 누이에게 부려 볼 작정인 그 자그마한 속임수에 그녀도 한몫 끼어야 한다는 말은 아직 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["This suggestion had still to be made, and as it was absolutely necessary, he proceeded to make it.", "이 제안은 여태 남아 있었고 반드시 필요한 것이었으므로, 그는 그것을 꺼내는 일로 넘어갔다."]], [["We need not follow him through the whole of his statement.", "그의 진술을 처음부터 끝까지 따라갈 필요는 없다."], ["At last, and not without considerable difficulty, he made Eleanor understand why he had let her into his confidence, seeing that he no longer intended her the honour of a formal offer.", "마침내, 그리고 적잖은 애를 먹은 끝에 그는 엘리너에게, 이제는 정식 청혼이라는 영광을 바칠 뜻이 없으면서도 어째서 그녀를 제 속내에 끌어들였는지를 납득시켰다."], ["At last he made her comprehend the part which she was destined to play in this little family comedy.", "마침내 그는 이 자그마한 집안 희극에서 그녀가 맡도록 정해진 배역을 그녀에게 이해시켰다."]], [["But when she did understand it, she was only more angry with him than ever; more angry, not only with him, but with Charlotte also.", "그러나 막상 그것을 이해하고 나자 그녀는 어느 때보다 그에게 화가 났을 뿐이었다. 그에게만이 아니라 샬럿에게도 화가 났다."], ["Her fair name was to be bandied about between them in different senses, and each sense false.", "그녀의 깨끗한 이름이 두 사람 사이에서 서로 다른 뜻으로 이리저리 오갈 참이었는데, 그 뜻이 어느 쪽이나 거짓이었다."], ["She was to be played off by the sister against the father, and then by the brother against the sister.", "그녀는 누이가 아버지를 상대로 써먹는 패가 되고, 다시 남동생이 누이를 상대로 써먹는 패가 될 참이었다."], ["Her dear friend Charlotte, with all her agreeable sympathy and affection, was striving to sacrifice her for the Stanhope family welfare; and Bertie, who, as he now proclaimed himself, was over head and ears in debt, completed the compliment of owning that he did not care to have his debts paid at so great a sacrifice of himself.", "그 다정한 공감과 애정을 다 갖춘 소중한 벗 샬럿은 스탠호프 집안의 안녕을 위해 그녀를 제물로 바치려 애쓰고 있었다. 그리고 버티는, 스스로 이제 밝힌 대로 빚에 목까지 잠겨 있으면서도, 제 몸을 그렇게까지 크게 희생해 가며 빚을 갚고 싶지는 않다고 인정함으로써 그 찬사를 완성했다."], ["Then she was asked to conspire together with this unwilling suitor for the sake of making the family believe that he had in obedience to their commands done his best to throw himself thus away!", "그러고는 식구들에게 그가 그들의 분부를 좇아 제 몸을 그렇게 내던지려고 최선을 다했다고 믿게 하려는 목적으로, 이 내키지 않는 구혼자와 한통속이 되어 달라는 부탁까지 받은 것이다!"]], [["She lifted up her face when he had finished, and looking at him with much dignity, even through her tears, she said:", "그가 말을 마치자 그녀는 얼굴을 들었고, 눈물 너머로도 자못 위엄 있게 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"I regret to say it, Mr. Stanhope, but after what has passed I believe that all intercourse between your family and myself had better cease.\"", "“이런 말씀을 드리게 되어 유감입니다만, 스탠호프 씨, 지금까지 있었던 일을 생각하면 댁 식구들과 저 사이의 왕래는 모두 끊는 편이 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"Well, perhaps it had,\"", "“글쎄요, 그러는 편이 낫겠지요.” 버티가 천진하게 말했다."], ["said Bertie naïvely; \"perhaps that will be better at any rate for a time; and then Charlotte will think you are offended at what I have done.\"", "“어쨌든 한동안은 그편이 나을 겁니다. 그러면 샬럿도 부인께서 제가 한 일에 노하신 줄로 알 테고요.”"]], [["\"And now I will go back to the house, if you please,\"", "“이제 괜찮으시다면 저는 집 쪽으로 돌아가겠어요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"I can find my way by myself, Mr.", "“길은 혼자서도 찾을 수 있습니다, 스탠호프 씨."], ["Stanhope: after what has passed,\"", "지금까지 있었던 일을 생각하면,”"], ["she added, \"I would rather go alone.\"", "그녀가 덧붙였다. “혼자 가는 편이 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"But I must find the carriage for you, Mrs.", "“하지만 부인을 위해 마차를 찾아 드려야 합니다, 볼드 부인. 그리고 아버지께 부인이 아버지와 단둘이 돌아가시게 되었다고 말씀드려야 하고, 제가 함께 가지 않는 데 대해 무슨 핑계든 대야 하고요."], ["Bold; and I must tell my father that you will return with him alone; and I must make some excuse to him for not going with you; and I must bid the servant put you down at your own house, for I suppose you will not now choose to see them again in the close.\"", "또 하인더러 부인 댁 앞에 내려 드리라고 일러 두어야 합니다. 이제는 부인께서 대성당 경내에서 저희 식구를 다시 보고 싶어 하지 않으실 테니까요.”"]], [["There was a truth about this, and a perspicuity in making arrangements for lessening her immediate embarrassment, which had some effect in softening Eleanor's anger.", "이 말에는 진실함이 있었고, 당장의 곤란을 덜어 줄 조치를 챙기는 데는 명민함이 있었으니, 그것이 엘리너의 노여움을 누그러뜨리는 데 얼마간 효과가 있었다."], ["So she suffered herself to walk by his side over the now deserted lawn, till they came to the drawing-room window.", "그리하여 그녀는 이제 텅 빈 잔디밭을 지나 응접실 창가에 이르기까지 그의 곁에서 걷도록 스스로를 내맡겼다."], ["There was something about Bertie Stanhope which gave him, in the estimation of everyone, a different standing from that which any other man would occupy under similar circumstances.", "버티 스탠호프에게는 누구의 눈에나 그를, 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 어떤 남자와도 다른 자리에 세워 주는 무언가가 있었다."], ["Angry as Eleanor was, and great as was her cause for anger, she was not half as angry with him as she would have been with anyone else.", "엘리너가 아무리 화가 났다 한들, 화를 낼 까닭이 아무리 컸다 한들, 그녀는 다른 사람에게였다면 냈을 화의 절반도 그에게는 내지 못했다."], ["He was apparently so simple, so good-natured, so unaffected and easy to talk to, that she had already half-forgiven him before he was at the drawing-room window.", "그는 보기에 하도 단순하고 하도 사람 좋고 하도 꾸밈없고 말 붙이기 편해서, 그녀는 그가 응접실 창가에 이르기도 전에 이미 반쯤 그를 용서해 버린 뒤였다."]], [["When they arrived there, Dr. Stanhope was sitting nearly alone with Mr.", "두 사람이 그리로 갔을 때 스탠호프 박사는 손 씨와 손 양과 함께 거의 단둘이라 할 만하게 앉아 있었다."], ["and Miss Thorne; one or two other unfortunates were there, who from one cause or another were still delayed in getting away, but they were every moment getting fewer in number.", "이런저런 사정으로 아직 떠나지 못하고 붙들려 있는 불운한 이가 한둘 더 있기는 했으나, 그 수는 시시각각 줄어들고 있었다."]], [["As soon as he had handed Eleanor over to his father, Bertie started off to the front gate in search of the carriage, and there he waited leaning patiently against the front wall, comfortably smoking a cigar, till it came up.", "엘리너를 아버지에게 넘겨주자마자 버티는 마차를 찾아 정문 쪽으로 나갔고, 거기서 마차가 올 때까지 앞담에 느긋이 기대선 채 편안히 시가를 피우며 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["When he returned to the room, Dr. Stanhope and Eleanor were alone with their hosts.", "그가 방으로 돌아왔을 때 스탠호프 박사와 엘리너는 주인 남매와만 남아 있었다."]], [["\"At last, Miss Thorne,\"", "“드디어 왔습니다, 손 양.” 그가 쾌활하게 말했다."], ["said he cheerily, \"I have come to relieve you. Mrs.", "“이제 짐을 덜어 드리러 왔어요."], ["Bold and my father are the last roses of the very delightful summer you have given us, and desirable as Mrs. Bold's society always is, now at least you must be glad to see the last flowers plucked from the tree.\"", "볼드 부인과 제 아버지는 손 양께서 저희에게 베풀어 주신 그 더없이 즐거운 여름의 마지막 장미인데, 볼드 부인과 함께하는 자리야 늘 바랄 만한 것이라 해도, 적어도 지금만은 마지막 꽃이 나무에서 꺾여 나가는 것을 반가워하셔야 할 테니까요.”"]], [["Miss Thorne declared that she was delighted to have Mrs. Bold and Dr.", "손 양은 볼드 부인과 스탠호프 박사가 아직 곁에 남아 있어 기쁘기 그지없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Stanhope still with her, and Mr. Thorne would have said the same, had he not been checked by a yawn, which he could not suppress.", "손 씨도 같은 말을 했을 터이나, 참을 수 없는 하품에 가로막히고 말았다."]], [["\"Father, will you give your arm to Mrs.", "“아버지, 볼드 부인께 팔을 내드리시겠어요?”"], ["Bold?\"", "버티가 말했다."], ["said Bertie: and so the last adieux were made, and the prebendary led out Mrs. Bold, followed by his son.", "그리하여 마지막 작별 인사가 오갔고, 참사회원이 볼드 부인을 이끌고 나가는 뒤를 그 아들이 따랐다."]], [["\"I shall be home soon after you,\"", "“저도 곧 뒤따라 집에 가겠습니다.”"], ["said he as the two got into the carriage.", "두 사람이 마차에 오를 때 그가 말했다."]], [["\"Are you not coming in the carriage?\"", "“마차에 안 타느냐?”"], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했다."]], [["\"No, no; I have someone to see on the road, and shall walk.", "“아뇨, 아뇨. 가는 길에 만날 사람이 있어서 걸어가겠어요."], ["John, mind you drive to Mrs. Bold's house first.\"", "존, 볼드 부인 댁으로 먼저 가는 것을 잊지 말게.”"]], [["Eleanor, looking out of the window, saw him with his hat in his hand, bowing to her with his usual gay smile, as though nothing had happened to mar the tranquillity of the day.", "엘리너가 창밖을 내다보니 그는 모자를 손에 든 채, 그날의 평온을 흐트러뜨린 일이라고는 아무것도 없었다는 듯 여느 때처럼 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 절을 하고 있었다."], ["It was many a long year before she saw him again.", "그녀가 그를 다시 보기까지는 기나긴 세월이 흘렀다."], ["Dr. Stanhope hardly spoke to her on her way home, and she was safely deposited by John at her own hall-door before the carriage drove into the close.", "스탠호프 박사는 돌아가는 길에 그녀에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았고, 그녀는 마차가 대성당 경내로 들어서기 전에 존의 손으로 무사히 제 집 현관 앞에 내려졌다."]], [["And thus our heroine played the last act of that day's melodrama.", "이리하여 우리의 여주인공은 그날 멜로드라마의 마지막 막을 연기했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII. Mr. and Mrs. Quiverful Are Made Happy. Mr. Slope is Encouraged by the Press", "# 제43장. 퀴버풀 씨 내외가 행복해지고 슬로프 씨는 신문의 격려를 받다"]], [["Before she started for Ullathorne, Mrs.", "울라손으로 떠나기에 앞서, 매사에 빈틈이 없는 프라우디 부인은 편지 두 통을 쓰게 했다."], ["Proudie, careful soul, caused two letters to be written, one by herself and one by her lord, to the inhabitants of Puddingdale vicarage, which made happy the hearth of those within it.", "한 통은 자기가 쓰고 한 통은 남편이 쓴 것으로, 둘 다 푸딩데일 목사관에 사는 이들에게 보내는 것이었으며, 그 집 화롯가를 행복으로 채워 놓았다."]], [["As soon as the departure of the horses left the bishop's stable-groom free for other services, that humble denizen of the diocese started on the bishop's own pony with the two dispatches.", "말들이 떠나 주교의 마부가 다른 일에 손이 비게 되자마자, 이 교구의 미천한 주민은 주교의 조랑말에 올라 두 통의 급보를 들고 출발했다."], ["We have had so many letters lately that we will spare ourselves these.", "근래 편지가 하도 많았으니 이번 것들은 우리끼리 생략하기로 하자."], ["That from the bishop was simply a request that Mr. Quiverful would wait upon his lordship the next morning at 11 A.M.; that from the lady was as simply a request that Mrs.", "주교가 쓴 것은 이튿날 아침 11시에 퀴버풀 씨가 주교를 찾아뵈어 달라는 요청에 지나지 않았고, 부인이 쓴 것 역시 퀴버풀 부인이 자기에게 그리해 달라는 요청에 지나지 않았다."], ["Quiverful would do the same by her, though it was couched in somewhat longer and more grandiloquent phraseology.", "다만 그쪽은 훨씬 길고 훨씬 거창한 문투로 짜여 있었다."]], [["It had become a point of conscience with Mrs. Proudie to urge the settlement of this great hospital question.", "프라우디 부인에게 이 중대한 요양원 문제를 매듭짓는 일은 이제 양심의 문제가 되어 있었다."], ["She was resolved that Mr. Quiverful should have it.", "그녀는 퀴버풀 씨가 그 자리를 차지해야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["She was resolved that there should be no more doubt or delay, no more refusals and resignations, no more secret negotiations carried on by Mr. Slope on his own account in opposition to her behests.", "더 이상의 의심도 지체도, 더 이상의 거절도 사임도, 자기 명령을 거슬러 슬로프 씨가 제 잇속을 챙기려 벌이는 밀약도 다시는 없어야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["\"Bishop,\"", "“주교님,”"], ["she said immediately after breakfast on the morning of that eventful day, \"have you signed the appointment yet?\"", "그 뜻깊은 날 아침, 그녀는 식사가 끝나기가 무섭게 말했다. “임명장에 서명은 하셨어요?”"]], [["\"No, my dear, not yet; it is not exactly signed as yet.\"", "“아니오, 여보, 아직이오. 아직 정확히 서명이 된 것은 아니라오.”"]], [["\"Then do it,\"", "“그럼 지금 하세요.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["The bishop did it, and a very pleasant day indeed he spent at Ullathorne.", "주교는 그대로 했고, 과연 울라손에서 더없이 즐거운 하루를 보냈다."], ["And when he got home, he had a glass of hot negus in his wife's sitting-room, and read the last number of the Little Dorrit of the day with great inward satisfaction.", "그리고 집에 돌아와서는 아내의 거실에서 따뜻한 니거스 한 잔을 마시며, 그 시절 유행하던 『리틀 도릿』의 최신 호를 속으로 대단히 흡족해하며 읽었다."], ["Oh, husbands, oh, my marital friends, what great comfort is there to be derived from a wife well obeyed!", "아아, 남편들이여, 아아, 나의 혼인한 벗들이여, 잘 순종한 아내에게서 길어 올릴 수 있는 위안이란 얼마나 큰 것인가!"]], [["Much perturbation and flutter, high expectation and renewed hopes, were occasioned at Puddingdale, by the receipt of these episcopal dispatches.", "이 주교관 급보가 도착하자 푸딩데일에는 큰 소동과 설렘이, 드높은 기대와 되살아난 희망이 일어났다."], ["Mrs. Quiverful, whose careful ear caught the sound of the pony's feet as he trotted up to the vicarage kitchen door, brought them in hurriedly to her husband.", "조랑말이 목사관 부엌문으로 총총 다가오는 발굽 소리를 놓치지 않고 잡아낸 퀴버풀 부인은 그것들을 부랴부랴 남편에게 가져갔다."], ["She was at the moment concocting the Irish stew destined to satisfy the noonday wants of fourteen young birds, let alone the parent couple.", "그때 그녀는 어린 새 열넷과 그 부모까지 점심때의 허기를 달래 줄 아일랜드 스튜를 젓고 있던 참이었다."], ["She had taken the letters from the man's hands between the folds of her capacious apron so as to save them from the contamination of the stew, and in this guise she brought them to her husband's desk.", "그녀는 스튜가 묻지 않도록 자기 널찍한 앞치마 자락 사이로 심부름꾼의 손에서 편지를 받아 들었고, 그 꼴 그대로 남편의 책상까지 들고 온 것이었다."]], [["They at once divided the spoil, each taking that addressed to the other.", "두 사람은 곧바로 전리품을 나누었는데, 각자 상대에게 온 것을 집어 들었다."], ["\"Quiverful,\"", "“퀴버풀,”"], ["said she with impressive voice, \"you are to be at the palace at eleven to-morrow.\"", "부인이 자못 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다. “내일 열한 시에 주교관에 가 있어야 한대요.”"]], [["\"And so are you, my dear,\"", "“당신도 그렇다는구려, 여보.”"], ["said he, almost gasping with the importance of the tidings--and then they exchanged letters.", "남편은 소식의 무게에 숨이 막힐 지경이 되어 말했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 편지를 맞바꾸었다."]], [["\"She'd never have sent for me again,\"", "“일이 제대로 되지 않았다면 나를 다시 부르실 리가 없어요.”"], ["said the lady, \"if it wasn't all right.\"", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, my dear, don't be too certain,\"", "“여보, 너무 장담하지는 마시오.”"], ["said the gentleman, \"Only think if it should be wrong.\"", "남편이 말했다. “일이 어그러지기라도 하면 어쩌겠소.”"]], [["\"She'd never have sent for me, Q., if it wasn't all right,\"", "“일이 제대로 되지 않았다면 나를 부르실 리가 없다니까요, Q.”"], ["again argued the lady.", "부인이 다시 우겼다."], ["\"She's stiff and hard and proud as piecrust, but I think she's right at bottom.\" Such was Mrs.", "“그분이야 파이 껍질처럼 뻣뻣하고 딱딱하고 도도하지만, 속은 바른 분이라고 나는 봐요.”"], ["Quiverful's verdict about Mrs. Proudie, to which in after times she always adhered.", "프라우디 부인에 대한 퀴버풀 부인의 판정은 그러했고, 그녀는 뒷날에도 늘 이 판정을 굽히지 않았다."], ["People when they get their income doubled usually think that those through whose instrumentality this little ceremony is performed are right at bottom.", "사람이란 제 수입이 갑절이 되면, 그 자그마한 의식을 치러 준 사람은 대개 속이 바른 사람이라고 여기게 마련이다."]], [["\"Oh, Letty!\"", "“아, 레티!”"], ["said Mr. Quiverful, rising from his well-worn seat.", "퀴버풀 씨가 닳고 닳은 의자에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Q.!\" said Mrs.", "“아, Q.!”"], ["Quiverful, and then the two, unmindful of the kitchen apron, the greasy fingers, and the adherent Irish stew, threw themselves warmly into each other's arms.", "퀴버풀 부인이 말했다. 그러고는 두 사람이, 부엌 앞치마도 기름투성이 손가락도 거기 들러붙은 아일랜드 스튜도 아랑곳하지 않고, 서로의 품에 뜨겁게 몸을 던졌다."]], [["\"For heaven's sake, don't let anyone cajole you out of it again,\"", "“제발 부탁이니 이번엔 누구한테도 구슬려서 빼앗기지 말아요.”"], ["said the wife.", "아내가 말했다."]], [["\"Let me alone for that,\"", "“그 일은 내게 맡겨 두시오.”"], ["said the husband with a look of almost fierce determination, pressing his fist as he spoke rigidly on his desk, as though he had Mr. Slope's head below his knuckles and meant to keep it there.", "남편이 사나울 만큼 단호한 낯빛으로 말하며, 마치 슬로프 씨의 머리통이 제 주먹 밑에 깔려 있고 그것을 그대로 눌러 두려는 사람처럼 책상을 뻣뻣하게 내리눌렀다."]], [["\"I wonder how soon it will be?\"", "“얼마나 있으면 될까요?”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I wonder whether it will be at all?\"", "“되기는 될까 모르겠소.”"], ["said he, still doubtful.", "남편은 여전히 미심쩍어하며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I won't say too much,\"", "“글쎄요, 너무 큰소리는 안 할래요.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"The cup has slipped twice before, and it may fall altogether this time, but I'll not believe it.", "“잔이 입에 닿기 전에 두 번이나 미끄러졌으니, 이번엔 아주 엎어질지도 모르지요. 그래도 나는 그렇게는 안 믿어요."], ["He'll give you the appointment to-morrow.", "그분이 내일 당신에게 그 자리를 주실 거예요."], ["You'll find he will.\"", "두고 보세요, 꼭 그러실 테니.”"]], [["\"Heaven send he may,\"", "“하늘이 그리 보내 주시기를.”"], ["said Mr. Quiverful solemnly.", "퀴버풀 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["And who that considers the weight of the burden on this man's back will say that the prayer was an improper one?", "그런데 이 사람의 등에 얹힌 짐의 무게를 헤아리는 이라면 누가 그 기도를 온당치 못하다 하겠는가?"], ["There were fourteen of them--fourteen of them living--as Mrs.", "열넷이었다. 살아 있는 아이가 열넷이었다."], ["Quiverful had so powerfully urged in the presence of the bishop's wife.", "퀴버풀 부인이 주교 부인 앞에서 그토록 힘주어 내세웠던 그대로다."], ["As long as promotion cometh from any human source, whether north or south, east or west, will not such a claim as this hold good, in spite of all our examination tests, detur digniori's, and optimist tendencies?", "승진이 남에서든 북에서든 동에서든 서에서든 어차피 사람의 손에서 나오는 것인 한, 우리의 온갖 시험과 심사와 detur digniori's와 낙관적인 성향에도 불구하고, 이런 청구야말로 유효한 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["It is fervently to be hoped that it may.", "그러하기를 간절히 바랄 뿐이다."], ["Till we can become divine, we must be content to be human, lest in our hurry for a change we sink to something lower.", "우리가 신이 되기 전까지는 사람으로 있는 데 만족해야 한다. 서둘러 바꾸려다가 도리어 더 낮은 것으로 떨어지지 않으려면."]], [["And then the pair, sitting down lovingly together, talked over all their difficulties, as they so often did, and all their hopes, as they so seldom were enabled to do.", "그러고 나서 부부는 다정하게 나란히 앉아, 늘 그러했듯이 온갖 어려움을 이야기했고, 좀처럼 그럴 기회가 없었던 온갖 희망도 이야기했다."]], [["\"You had better call on that man, Q., as you come away from the palace,\" said Mrs.", "“주교관에서 나오는 길에 그 사람한테 들르는 게 좋겠어요, Q.”"], ["Quiverful, pointing to an angry call for money from the Barchester draper, which the postman had left at the vicarage that morning.", "퀴버풀 부인이 말하며, 그날 아침 우편배달부가 목사관에 놓고 간 바체스터 포목상의 성난 독촉장을 가리켰다."], ["Cormorant that he was, unjust, hungry cormorant!", "가마우지 같은 자, 부당하고 게걸스러운 가마우지!"], ["When rumour first got abroad that the Quiverfuls were to go to the hospital, this fellow with fawning eagerness had pressed his goods upon the wants of the poor clergyman.", "퀴버풀 일가가 요양원으로 가게 되었다는 소문이 처음 퍼졌을 때, 이 사내는 알랑거리는 열의로 이 가난한 성직자에게 필요하다 싶은 물건을 앞다투어 들이밀었다."], ["He had done so, feeling that he should be paid from the hospital funds, and flattering himself that a man with fourteen children, and money wherewithal to clothe them, could not but be an excellent customer.", "요양원 재산에서 돈을 받게 되리라 여겼고, 아이가 열넷인 데다 그 아이들을 입힐 돈까지 생긴 사람이라면 더없이 훌륭한 손님일 수밖에 없다며 혼자 흐뭇해했던 것이다."], ["As soon as the second rumour reached him, he applied for his money angrily.", "그러다 두 번째 소문이 귀에 들어오자마자, 그는 성이 나서 대금을 청구했다."]], [["And \"the fourteen\"--or such of them as were old enough to hope and discuss their hopes--talked over their golden future.", "그리고 “그 열넷”은, 아니 그중에서 희망을 품고 그 희망을 논할 만큼 자란 아이들은, 저희의 황금빛 앞날을 두고 이야기꽃을 피웠다."], ["The tall grown girls whispered to each other of possible Barchester parties, of possible allowances for dress, of a possible piano--the one they had in the vicarage was so weather-beaten with the storms of years and children as to be no longer worthy of the name--of the pretty garden, and the pretty house.", "다 자란 큰 딸들은 바체스터에서 열릴 법한 모임이며, 옷값으로 받게 될 법한 용돈이며, 어쩌면 생길지도 모를 피아노며 — 목사관에 있는 것은 세월과 아이들의 풍파에 하도 시달려 이제 피아노라는 이름을 붙이기도 민망했다 — 그 예쁜 정원과 예쁜 집을 두고 서로 소곤거렸다."], ["'Twas of such things it most behoved them to whisper.", "큰 딸들이 소곤거릴 만한 것이라면 마땅히 그런 것들이었다."]], [["And the younger fry, they did not content themselves with whispers, but shouted to each other of their new playground beneath our dear ex-warden's well-loved elms, of their future own gardens, of marbles to be procured in the wished-for city, and of the rumour which had reached them of a Barchester school.", "그리고 어린 것들은 소곤거리는 데서 그치지 않고, 우리의 정다운 전임 워든이 그토록 아끼던 느릅나무 아래에 생길 새 놀이터며, 앞으로 저마다 갖게 될 뜰이며, 그토록 바라던 도시에 가면 사게 될 구슬이며, 바체스터에 학교가 있다더라는 소문을 두고 서로 소리를 질러 댔다."]], [["'Twas in vain that their cautious mother tried to instil into their breasts the very feeling she had striven to banish from that of their father; 'twas in vain that she repeated to the girls that \"there's many a slip 'twixt the cup and the lip;\" 'twas in vain she attempted to make the children believe that they were to live at Puddingdale all their lives.", "조심성 많은 어머니가 아이들 가슴에는 정작 아버지 가슴에서 몰아내려 애쓰던 바로 그 감정을 심어 보려 한 것도 헛일이었고, 딸들에게 “잔이 입에 닿기까지는 미끄러질 일이 숱하다”고 되뇐 것도 헛일이었으며, 아이들에게 우리는 평생 푸딩데일에서 살 거라고 믿게 하려 한 것도 헛일이었다."], ["Hopes mounted high, and would not have themselves quelled.", "희망은 높이 솟구쳐 도무지 가라앉으려 하지 않았다."], ["The neighbouring farmers heard the news and came in to congratulate them.", "이웃 농부들이 소식을 듣고 축하하러 찾아왔다."], ["'Twas Mrs. Quiverful herself who had kindled the fire, and in the first outbreak of her renewed expectations she did it so thoroughly that it was quite past her power to put it out again.", "애초에 불을 지핀 것은 다름 아닌 퀴버풀 부인 자신이었으니, 되살아난 기대가 처음 터져 나오던 순간에 어찌나 야무지게 지펴 놓았던지 이제는 제힘으로 도로 끌 수가 없게 되어 버린 것이다."]], [["Poor matron!", "가엾은 안주인!"], ["Good, honest matron, doing thy duty in the state to which thou hast been called, heartily if not contentedly; let the fire burn on; on this occasion the flames will not scorch; they shall warm thee and thine.", "착하고 정직한 안주인이여, 그대가 부르심을 받은 처지에서 흡족하지는 못할지언정 진심으로 제 본분을 다하는 이여. 불은 그대로 타게 두라."], ["'Tis ordained that that husband of thine, that Q.", "이번만은 그 불길이 그대를 태우지 않으리라."], ["of thy bosom, shall reign supreme for years to come over the bedesmen of Hiram's Hospital.", "그것은 그대와 그대의 식구를 따뜻하게 데워 주리라. 그대의 그 남편, 그대 가슴에 품은 그 Q.가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 히램 요양원의 구호 노인들 위에 군림하도록 이미 정해진 것이다."]], [["And the last in all Barchester to mar their hopes, had he heard and seen all that passed at Puddingdale that day, would have been Mr. Harding.", "그리고 그날 푸딩데일에서 오간 일을 모두 듣고 보았던들, 온 바체스터에서 그들의 희망을 꺾을 마음이 가장 없었을 사람은 하딩 씨였을 것이다."], ["What wants had he to set in opposition to those of such a regiment of young ravens?", "저 어린 갈까마귀 한 부대의 필요에 맞세울 만한 무슨 필요가 그에게 있었겠는가?"], ["There are fourteen of them living!", "살아 있는 아이가 열넷이다!"], ["With him, at any rate, let us say that that argument would have been sufficient for the appointment of Mr. Quiverful.", "적어도 그에게만은 그 한 가지가 퀴버풀 씨를 임명할 충분한 논거가 되었으리라고 말해 두자."]], [["In the morning Q. and his wife kept their appointments with that punctuality which bespeaks an expectant mind.", "아침이 되자 Q.와 그 아내는 기대에 부푼 마음을 그대로 드러내는 정확함으로 약속 시간을 지켰다."], ["The friendly farmer's gig was borrowed, and in that they went, discussing many things by the way.", "친절한 농부의 이륜마차를 빌려 그것을 타고 갔는데, 가는 길에 온갖 이야기를 나누었다."], ["They had instructed the household to expect them back by one, and injunctions were given to the eldest pledge to have ready by that accustomed hour the remainder of the huge stew which the provident mother had prepared on the previous day.", "두 사람은 집안 식구들에게 한 시까지는 돌아오겠다고 일러 두었고, 맏이에게는 그 익숙한 시각에 맞춰, 알뜰한 어머니가 전날 끓여 둔 큰 솥 스튜의 남은 것을 차려 놓으라고 단단히 당부해 두었다."], ["The hands of the kitchen clock came round to two, three, four, before the farmer's gig wheels were again heard at the vicarage gate.", "부엌 시계 바늘이 두 시, 세 시, 네 시를 돌아 지나서야 농부의 마차 바퀴 소리가 다시 목사관 문간에 들렸다."], ["With what palpitating hearts were the returning wanderers greeted!", "돌아온 나그네들은 얼마나 두근거리는 가슴들의 맞이를 받았던가!"]], [["\"I suppose, children, you all thought we were never coming back any more?\"", "“얘들아, 너희는 아마 엄마 아빠가 영영 안 돌아오는 줄 알았지?”"], ["said the mother as she slowly let down her solid foot till it rested on the step of the gig.", "어머니가 그 튼실한 발을 천천히 내려 마차 발판에 딛으며 말했다."], ["\"Well, such a day as we've had!\"", "“아이고, 오늘 같은 날이 또 있을까!”"], ["and then leaning heavily on a big boy's shoulder, she stepped once more on terra firma.", "그러고는 큰아이의 어깨에 몸을 실은 채 다시 한번 단단한 땅을 밟았다."]], [["There was no need for more than the tone of her voice to tell them that all was right.", "그 목소리의 가락 하나면 족했으니, 모든 일이 잘되었다는 것을 알리는 데는 더 필요한 것이 없었다."], ["The Irish stew might burn itself to cinders now.", "아일랜드 스튜쯤이야 이제 새까맣게 타든 말든 상관없었다."]], [["Then there was such kissing and hugging, such crying and laughing.", "이어 어찌나 입을 맞추고 얼싸안았는지, 어찌나 울고 웃었는지."], ["Mr. Quiverful could not sit still at all, but kept walking from room to room, then out into the garden, then down the avenue into the road, and then back again to his wife.", "퀴버풀 씨는 도무지 가만히 앉아 있지를 못하고 이 방 저 방을 돌아다니다가, 정원으로 나갔다가, 가로수길을 따라 큰길까지 내려갔다가, 다시 아내에게로 돌아오기를 되풀이했다."], ["She, however, lost no time so idly.", "그러나 아내는 그렇게 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내지 않았다."]], [["\"We must go to work at once, girls, and that in earnest. Mrs.", "“얘들아, 당장 일을 시작해야 한다, 그것도 단단히 마음먹고 말이야."], ["Proudie expects us to be in the hospital house on the 15th of October.\"", "프라우디 부인께서 10월 15일까지는 우리가 요양원 집에 들어가 있기를 바라신다.”"]], [["Had Mrs. Proudie expressed a wish that they should all be there on the next morning, the girls would have had nothing to say against it.", "프라우디 부인이 이튿날 아침까지 모두 그리로 들어와 있으라고 했더라도, 딸들은 아무 불평이 없었을 것이다."]], [["\"And when will the pay begin?\"", "“그럼 돈은 언제부터 나와요?”"], ["asked the eldest boy.", "맏아들이 물었다."]], [["\"To-day, my dear,\"", "“오늘부터란다, 얘야.”"], ["said the gratified mother.", "흐뭇해진 어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, that is jolly,\"", "“야, 신난다.”"], ["said the boy.", "아이가 말했다."]], [["\"Mrs. Proudie insisted on our going down to the house,\"", "“프라우디 부인께서 그 집에 꼭 내려가 봐야 한다고 하시더구나.”"], ["continued the mother, \"and when there, I thought I might save a journey by measuring some of the rooms and windows; so I got a knot of tape from Bobbins.", "어머니가 말을 이었다. “가 본 김에 방이랑 창문을 좀 재 두면 걸음을 하나 덜겠다 싶어서, 보빈스한테 줄자 한 뭉치를 얻었지."], ["Bobbins is as civil as you please, now.\"", "보빈스가 요새는 어찌나 굽실거리는지 모른다.”"]], [["\"I wouldn't thank him,\"", "“나 같으면 고맙단 소리 안 할 텐데.”"], ["said Letty the younger.", "작은 레티가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, it's the way of the world, my dear.", "“얘야, 세상 이치가 다 그런 거란다."], ["They all do just the same.", "다들 똑같이 그런다."], ["You might just as well be angry with the turkey cock for gobbling at you.", "칠면조가 꼬르륵거린다고 화를 내 봐야 무슨 소용이겠니."], ["It's the bird's nature.\"", "그게 그 새의 천성인걸.”"], ["And as she enunciated to her bairns the upshot of her practical experience, she pulled from her pocket the portions of tape which showed the length and breadth of the various rooms at the hospital house.", "그러고는 제 살아온 경험의 결론을 아이들에게 그렇게 일러 주면서, 그녀는 주머니에서 요양원 집 방들의 길이와 너비를 재어 둔 줄자 토막들을 꺼냈다."]], [["And so we will leave her happy in her toils.", "그러니 우리는 그녀를 그 고단하고도 행복한 일거리 속에 남겨 두기로 하자."]], [["The Quiverfuls had hardly left the palace, and Mrs. Proudie was still holding forth on the matter to her husband, when another visitor was announced in the person of Dr. Gwynne. The Master of Lazarus had asked for the bishop and not for Mrs. Proudie, and therefore when he was shown into the study, he was surprised rather than rejoiced to find the lady there.", "퀴버풀 내외가 주교관을 나선 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 프라우디 부인이 아직 그 일을 두고 남편에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있는데, 또 한 사람의 손님이 왔다고 알려 왔으니 그윈 박사였다. 라자러스의 학장은 프라우디 부인이 아니라 주교를 찾은 것이었으므로, 서재로 안내되어 들어가 부인이 거기 있는 것을 보고는 기뻐하기보다 오히려 놀랐다."]], [["But we must go back a little, and it shall be but a little, for a difficulty begins to make itself manifest in the necessity of disposing of all our friends in the small remainder of this one volume.", "그러나 우리는 조금 뒤로 되돌아가야 한다. 아주 조금만이다."], ["Oh, that Mr. Longman would allow me a fourth!", "이 한 권의 얼마 남지 않은 지면 안에 우리의 벗들을 모조리 처리해야 한다는 데서 슬슬 난처함이 드러나기 시작하는 까닭이다."], ["It should transcend the other three as the seventh heaven transcends all the lower stages of celestial bliss.", "아아, 롱맨 씨가 네 번째 권을 허락해 주었더라면! 일곱째 하늘이 그 아래 온갖 천상의 복락의 단계들을 뛰어넘듯, 그 권은 앞의 세 권을 뛰어넘었을 것을."]], [["Going home in the carriage that evening from Ullathorne, Dr. Gwynne had not without difficulty brought round his friend the archdeacon to a line of tactics much less bellicose than that which his own taste would have preferred.", "그날 저녁 울라손에서 마차를 타고 돌아오는 길에, 그윈 박사는 적잖은 애를 쓴 끝에 벗인 부주교를 그의 취향에 맞는 것보다 훨씬 덜 호전적인 방책 쪽으로 끌어당겨 놓았다."], ["\"It will be unseemly in us to show ourselves in a bad humour; moreover, we have no power in this matter, and it will therefore be bad policy to act as though we had.\"", "“우리가 언짢은 낯을 드러내는 것은 볼썽사나운 일이오. 게다가 이 문제에 우리에게는 아무 권한이 없으니, 있는 척 굴어 봐야 서투른 계책일 뿐이오.”"], ["'Twas thus the Master of Lazarus argued. \"If,\"", "라자러스의 학장은 이렇게 주장했다."], ["he continued, \"the bishop be determined to appoint another to the hospital, threats will not prevent him, and threats should not be lightly used by an archdeacon to his bishop.", "“주교가 요양원에 다른 사람을 앉히기로 작정했다면, 으름장으로 막을 수는 없소. 게다가 부주교가 제 주교에게 으름장을 가벼이 놓을 일도 아니오."], ["If he will place a stranger in the hospital, we can only leave him to the indignation of others.", "그가 요양원에 낯선 사람을 들여앉힌다면, 우리로서는 다른 이들의 분노에 그를 맡겨 두는 수밖에 없소."], ["It is probable that such a step may not eventually injure your father-in-law.", "그런 처사가 결국에 가서는 장인어른께 해가 되지 않을 공산도 크오."], ["I will see the bishop, if you will allow me--alone.\"", "허락해 주신다면 내가 주교를 만나 보겠소 — 혼자서.”"], ["At this the archdeacon winced visibly.", "이 대목에서 부주교는 눈에 띄게 움찔했다. “그렇소, 혼자서요."], ["\"Yes, alone; for so I shall be calmer; and then I shall at any rate learn what he does mean to do in the matter.\"", "그래야 내가 더 침착할 테니. 그러면 적어도 그가 이 일을 어찌할 작정인지는 알아낼 수 있을 것이오.”"]], [["The archdeacon puffed and blew, put up the carriage window and then put it down again, argued the matter up to his own gate, and at last gave way.", "부주교는 씩씩거리며 숨을 몰아쉬었고, 마차 창을 올렸다가 다시 내렸으며, 제 집 대문에 이르도록 그 문제를 두고 따지다가 마침내 물러섰다."], ["Everybody was against him, his own wife, Mr.", "모두가 그에게 맞서고 있었다."], ["Harding, and Dr. Gwynne.", "제 아내도, 하딩 씨도, 그윈 박사도."]], [["\"Pray keep him out of hot water, Dr. Gwynne,\"", "“부디 그이가 곤경에 빠지지 않게 해 주세요, 그윈 박사님.”"], ["Mrs. Grantly had said to her guest.", "그랜틀리 부인이 손님에게 그렇게 말해 두었던 것이다."]], [["\"My dearest madam, I'll do my best,\"", "“부인, 힘닿는 데까지 해 보겠습니다.”"], ["the courteous master had replied.", "정중한 학장은 그렇게 대답했다."], ["'Twas thus he did it and earned for himself the gratitude of Mrs. Grantly.", "그가 일을 해낸 방식이 바로 그러했고, 그리하여 그는 그랜틀리 부인의 고마움을 얻었다."]], [["And now we may return to the bishop's study.", "이제 우리는 주교의 서재로 돌아가도 좋겠다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne had certainly not foreseen the difficulty which here presented itself.", "그윈 박사가 여기서 맞닥뜨린 난처함은 그로서도 미처 내다보지 못한 것이었다."], ["He--together with all the clerical world of England--had heard it rumoured about that Mrs.", "그도 — 잉글랜드의 온 성직자 사회와 마찬가지로 — 프라우디 부인이 옷장과 저장실과 세탁장에만 머물러 있지 않는다는 소문쯤은 들어 알고 있었다."], ["Proudie did not confine herself to her wardrobes, still-rooms, and laundries; but yet it had never occurred to him that if he called on a bishop at one o'clock in the day, he could by any possibility find him closeted with his wife; or that if he did so, the wife would remain longer than necessary to make her curtsey.", "그러나 대낮 한 시에 주교를 찾아갔다가 그가 아내와 단둘이 틀어박혀 있는 광경을 마주할 수도 있으리라고는, 설령 그렇더라도 그 아내가 인사를 하고 물러가는 데 필요한 시간 이상으로 남아 있으리라고는 여태 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["It appeared, however, as though in the present case Mrs. Proudie had no idea of retreating.", "그런데 지금 이 경우에는 프라우디 부인이 물러날 뜻이 조금도 없어 보였다."]], [["The bishop had been very much pleased with Dr. Gwynne on the preceding day, and of course thought that Dr.", "주교는 전날 그윈 박사가 여간 마음에 들지 않았고, 그러니 당연히 그윈 박사도 자기가 그만큼 마음에 들었으리라 여기고 있었다."], ["Gwynne had been as much pleased with him. He attributed the visit solely to compliment, and thought it an extremely gracious and proper thing for the Master of Lazarus to drive over from Plumstead specially to call at the palace so soon after his arrival in the country.", "그는 이번 방문을 순전히 예를 갖춘 것으로 여겼고, 라자러스의 학장이 이 고장에 내려오자마자 이렇게 일찍 플럼스테드에서 마차를 몰아 일부러 주교관에 들러 주었으니 참으로 은혜롭고도 온당한 처사라고 생각했다."], ["The fact that they were not on the same side either in politics or doctrines made the compliment the greater.", "두 사람이 정치로 보나 교리로 보나 같은 편이 아니라는 사실이 그 예를 한층 더 값지게 만들었다."], ["The bishop, therefore, was all smiles.", "그리하여 주교는 온통 웃음이었다."], ["And Mrs. Proudie, who liked people with good handles to their names, was also very well disposed to welcome the Master of Lazarus.", "그리고 이름 뒤에 그럴듯한 직함이 붙은 사람을 좋아하는 프라우디 부인 역시 라자러스의 학장을 맞이할 마음이 아주 흡족했다."]], [["\"We had a charming party at Ullathorne, Master, had we not?\"", "“울라손 모임은 참 즐거웠지요, 학장님, 그렇지 않던가요?”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"I hope Mrs. Grantly got home without fatigue.\"", "“그랜틀리 부인은 지치지 않고 잘 돌아가셨기를 바라요.”"]], [["Dr. Gwynne said that they had all been a little tired, but were none the worse this morning.", "그윈 박사는 다들 조금 고단하기는 했으나 오늘 아침에는 아무렇지도 않더라고 말했다."]], [["\"An excellent person, Miss Thorne,\"", "“손 양은 훌륭한 분이지요.”"], ["suggested the bishop.", "주교가 한마디 거들었다."]], [["\"And an exemplary Christian, I am told,\"", "“그리고 본받을 만한 기독교인이라고들 하더군요.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne declared that he was very glad to hear it.", "그윈 박사는 그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘다고 밝혔다."]], [["\"I have not seen her Sabbath-day schools yet,\"", "“그 댁 안식일 학교는 아직 못 보았소만,”"], ["continued the lady, \"but I shall make a point of doing so before long.\"", "부인이 말을 이었다. “머지않아 꼭 한번 가 볼 작정이오.”"]], [["Dr. Gwynne merely bowed at this intimation. He had heard something of Mrs. Proudie and her Sunday-schools, both from Dr. Grantly and Mr. Harding.", "그윈 박사는 그 말에 그저 고개를 숙여 보였다. 그는 프라우디 부인과 그 주일학교에 관한 이야기를 그랜틀리 박사와 하딩 씨에게서 익히 들은 바 있었다."]], [["\"By the by, Master,\"", "“그런데 학장님,”"], ["continued the lady, \"I wonder whether Mrs.", "부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Grantly would like me to drive over and inspect her Sabbath-day school.", "“내가 마차를 몰고 가서 그랜틀리 부인의 안식일 학교를 살펴보면 그이가 반가워할지 모르겠소."], ["I hear that it is most excellently kept.\"", "아주 훌륭하게 꾸려 나간다는 말을 들었는데.”"]], [["Dr. Gwynne really could not say. He had no doubt Mrs. Grantly would be most happy to see Mrs. Proudie any day Mrs. Proudie would do her the honour of calling: that was, of course, if Mrs. Grantly should happen to be at home.", "그윈 박사로서는 정말이지 뭐라 말할 수가 없었다. 프라우디 부인이 왕림해 주시는 영광을 베푸신다면 그랜틀리 부인은 어느 날이든 더없이 기꺼이 맞이할 것이라고 그는 믿어 의심치 않았다. 물론 그랜틀리 부인이 마침 집에 있기만 하다면 말이지만."]], [["A slight cloud darkened the lady's brow.", "부인의 이마에 옅은 구름이 드리웠다."], ["She saw that her offer was not taken in good part.", "그녀는 제 호의가 좋게 받아들여지지 않았음을 알아차렸다."], ["This generation of unregenerated vipers was still perverse, stiff-necked, and hardened in their iniquity. \"The archdeacon, I know,\"", "거듭나지 못한 이 독사의 무리는 여전히 비뚤어지고 목이 뻣뻣하며 제 죄악 속에 굳어 있는 것이다."], ["said she, \"sets his face against these institutions.\"", "“부주교가 이런 시설에 낯을 찌푸린다는 건 나도 알고 있소.” 그녀가 말했다."]], [["At this Dr. Gwynne laughed slightly.", "이 말에 그윈 박사는 슬며시 웃었다."], ["It was but a smile.", "웃음이랄 것도 없이 미소에 지나지 않았다."], ["Had he given his cap for it he could not have helped it.", "그것을 참을 수만 있다면 제 모자라도 내주었으련만, 그로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie frowned again.", "프라우디 부인이 다시 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."], ["\"'Suffer little children, and forbid them not,'\"", "“‘어린아이들을 용납하고 금하지 말라.’”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Are we not to remember that, Dr. Gwynne?", "“우리가 그 말씀을 기억해야 하지 않겠소, 그윈 박사?"], ["'Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones.' Are we not to remember that, Dr.", "‘삼가 이 작은 자 중의 하나도 업신여기지 말라.’ 우리가 그 말씀을 기억해야 하지 않겠소, 그윈 박사?”"], ["Gwynne?\" And at each of these questions she raised at him her menacing forefinger.", "그러고는 그 물음을 던질 때마다 그를 향해 으르는 집게손가락을 치켜들었다."]], [["\"Certainly, madam, certainly,\"", "“여부가 있겠습니까, 부인, 여부가 있겠습니까.” 학장이 말했다."], ["said the master, \"and so does the archdeacon, I am sure, on weekdays as well as on Sundays.\"", "“그리고 부주교 역시 그러하리라 확신합니다. 주일에만이 아니라 주중에도 말입니다.”"]], [["\"On weekdays you can't take heed not to despise them,\" said Mrs.", "“주중에는 그 아이들을 업신여기지 않으려야 않을 수가 없소.”"], ["Proudie, \"because then they are out in the fields.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그때는 아이들이 들에 나가 있으니까."], ["On weekdays they belong to their parents, but on Sundays they ought to belong to the clergyman.\"", "주중에 아이들은 제 부모의 것이지만, 주일에는 성직자의 것이 되어야 마땅하오.”"], ["And the finger was again raised.", "그러고는 다시 손가락이 올라갔다."]], [["The master began to understand and to share the intense disgust which the archdeacon always expressed when Mrs. Proudie's name was mentioned.", "학장은 프라우디 부인의 이름이 나올 때마다 부주교가 늘 드러내던 그 지독한 혐오를 이제야 알아들었고 또 함께 느끼기 시작했다."], ["What was he to do with such a woman as this?", "이런 여자를 대체 어찌해야 한단 말인가?"], ["To take his hat and go would have been his natural resource, but then he did not wish to be foiled in his object.", "모자를 집어 들고 나가 버리는 것이 그로서는 자연스러운 방책이었겠으나, 그러자니 제 볼일을 그르치고 싶지가 않았다."]], [["\"My lord,\"", "“주교님,”"], ["said he, \"I wanted to ask you a question on business, if you could spare me one moment's leisure.", "그가 말했다. “한순간만 짬을 내주실 수 있다면 볼일로 여쭐 것이 하나 있습니다."], ["I know I must apologize for so disturbing you, but in truth I will not detain you five minutes.\"", "이렇게 방해를 놓으니 사과부터 드려야 마땅한 줄 압니다만, 정말이지 5분도 붙들지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Certainly, Master, certainly,\"", "“여부가 있겠습니까, 학장님, 여부가 있겠습니까.” 주교가 말했다."], ["said the bishop; \"my time is quite yours--pray make no apology, pray make no apology.\"", "“내 시간은 온전히 학장님 것입니다. 부디 사과는 마십시오, 부디 사과는 마십시오.”"]], [["\"You have a great deal to do just at the present moment, Bishop.", "“주교님, 지금은 하실 일이 아주 많으시잖아요."], ["Do not forget how extremely busy you are at present,\"", "요즘 얼마나 바쁘신지 잊지 마세요.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Proudie, whose spirit was now up, for she was angry with her visitor.", "손님에게 성이 나서 이제 그녀의 기세가 오른 참이었다."]], [["\"I will not delay his lordship much above a minute,\"", "“주교님을 1분 남짓 이상은 붙들지 않겠습니다.”"], ["said the Master of Lazarus, rising from his chair and expecting that Mrs.", "라자러스의 학장이 의자에서 일어서며 말했다."], ["Proudie would now go, or else that the bishop would lead the way into another room.", "이제는 프라우디 부인이 나가든지, 아니면 주교가 앞장서서 다른 방으로 데려가든지 하리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["But neither event seemed likely to occur, and Dr. Gwynne stood for a moment silent in the middle of the room.", "그러나 어느 쪽도 일어날 낌새가 없었고, 그윈 박사는 잠시 말없이 방 한가운데에 서 있었다."]], [["\"Perhaps it's about Hiram's Hospital?\"", "“히램 요양원 일이 아니오?”"], ["suggested Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 넌지시 물었다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne, lost in astonishment, and not knowing what else on earth to do, confessed that his business with the bishop was connected with Hiram's Hospital.", "그윈 박사는 어이가 없어 넋을 놓은 채, 달리 무엇을 어찌해야 좋을지 몰라, 주교에게 여쭐 볼일이란 히램 요양원에 관한 것이라고 실토하고 말았다."]], [["\"His lordship has finally conferred the appointment on Mr. Quiverful this morning,\"", "“주교님께서는 오늘 아침에 그 자리를 퀴버풀 씨에게 최종적으로 내리셨소.”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."]], [["Dr. Gwynne made a simple reference to the bishop, and finding that the lady's statement was formally confirmed, he took his leave.", "그윈 박사는 주교 쪽을 향해 짤막하게 확인을 구했고, 부인의 말이 격식대로 확인되는 것을 보고는 자리를 떴다."], ["\"That comes of the reform bill,\"", "“저것이 선거법 개정안이 낳은 결과로군.”"], ["he said to himself as he walked down the bishop's avenue.", "주교관의 가로수길을 걸어 내려가며 그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"Well, at any rate the Greek play bishops were not so bad as that.\"", "“뭐, 어쨌든 그리스 희곡을 읽던 주교들이 저것보다야 나빴겠나.”"]], [["It has been said that Mr. Slope, as he started for Ullathorne, received a dispatch from his friend Mr.", "앞서 이야기한 대로, 슬로프 씨는 울라손으로 떠나던 참에 벗인 타워스 씨에게서 급보를 한 통 받았고, 그 덕분에 몹시 기분이 좋아졌던 것인데 그 뒤에 벌어진 일들이 얄궂게도 그 기분에 찬물을 끼얹었다."], ["Towers, which had the effect of putting him in that high good humour which subsequent events somewhat untowardly damped. It ran as follows.", "편지는 다음과 같았다."], ["Its shortness will be its sufficient apology.", "짧다는 것 자체가 넉넉한 변명이 되어 주리라."]], [["I wish you every success.", "부디 만사가 뜻대로 되시기를 빕니다."], ["I don't know that I can help you, but if I can, I will.", "내가 도움이 될지는 모르겠으나, 될 수만 있다면 돕겠습니다."]], [["Yours ever, T.", "언제나 그대의 벗, T."], ["T.", "T."]], [["30/9/185--", "185-년 9월 30일"]], [["There was more in this than in all Sir Nicholas Fitzwhiggin's flummery; more than in all the bishop's promises, even had they been ever so sincere; more than in any archbishop's good word, even had it been possible to obtain it.", "여기에는 니컬러스 피츠위긴 경의 온갖 입에 발린 소리를 다 합친 것보다 더한 것이 담겨 있었다. 주교의 온갖 약속을 다 합친 것보다, 설령 그 약속이 아무리 진심이었다 한들 그보다 더한 것이."], ["Tom Towers would do for him what he could.", "대주교의 좋은 말 한마디보다, 설령 그것을 얻어 낼 수 있었다 한들 그보다 더한 것이. 톰 타워스가 그를 위해 할 수 있는 일을 해 주겠다는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Slope had from his youth upwards been a firm believer in the public press.", "슬로프 씨는 젊은 시절부터 줄곧 신문의 힘을 굳게 믿는 사람이었다."], ["He had dabbled in it himself ever since he had taken his degree, and he regarded it as the great arranger and distributor of all future British terrestrial affairs whatever.", "학위를 받은 뒤로 줄곧 자신도 거기에 손을 담가 왔고, 그것이야말로 앞으로 영국의 지상사를 모조리 정리하고 분배할 위대한 기관이라 여겼다."], ["He had not yet arrived at the age, an age which sooner or later comes to most of us, which dissipates the golden dreams of youth.", "그는 아직, 우리 대부분에게 늦든 이르든 찾아오고야 마는 그 나이, 젊은 날의 황금빛 꿈이 흩어져 버리는 그 나이에 이르지 않았다."], ["He delighted in the idea of wresting power from the hands of his country's magnates and placing it in a custody which was at any rate nearer to his own reach.", "그는 제 나라 권세가들의 손에서 권력을 빼앗아, 적어도 제 손이 닿기에는 더 가까운 곳에 맡겨 둔다는 발상에 신이 났다."], ["Sixty thousand broadsheets dispersing themselves daily among his reading fellow citizens formed in his eyes a better depot for supremacy than a throne at Windsor, a cabinet in Downing Street, or even an assembly at Westminster.", "날마다 6만 장의 신문이 글 읽는 동포들 사이로 흩어져 나가는 것이야말로, 그가 보기에는 윈저의 왕좌보다도, 다우닝가의 내각보다도, 심지어 웨스트민스터의 의회보다도 더 나은 최고 권력의 보관소였다."], ["And on this subject we must not quarrel with Mr.", "그리고 이 문제를 두고 우리가 슬로프 씨와 다툴 일은 아니다."], ["Slope, for the feeling is too general to be met with disrespect.", "그런 생각은 너무나 널리 퍼져 있어 함부로 업신여길 것이 못 되기 때문이다."]], [["Tom Towers was as good, if not better, than his promise. On the following morning \"The Jupiter,\"", "톰 타워스는 제 약속만큼은, 아니 그 이상으로 해냈다."], ["spouting forth public opinion with sixty thousand loud clarions, did proclaim to the world that Mr. Slope was the fitting man for the vacant post.", "이튿날 아침 『주피터』는 6만 개의 우렁찬 나팔로 여론을 뿜어내며, 슬로프 씨야말로 비어 있는 그 자리에 어울리는 사람이라고 온 세상에 알렸다."], ["It was pleasant for Mr. Slope to read the following lines in the Barchester news-room, which he did within thirty minutes after the morning train from London had reached the city.", "슬로프 씨는 런던발 아침 기차가 이 도시에 닿은 지 30분 만에 바체스터 신문 열람실에서 다음과 같은 대목을 읽었으니, 그로서는 흐뭇한 일이었다."]], [["It is just now five years since we called the attention of our readers to the quiet city of Barchester.", "우리가 조용한 도시 바체스터에 독자 여러분의 주의를 돌린 지 이제 꼭 5년이 되었다."], ["From that day to this, we have in no way meddled with the affairs of that happy ecclesiastical community.", "그날 이후 오늘까지 우리는 그 행복한 성직 사회의 일에 조금도 참견한 바가 없다."], ["Since then, an old bishop has died there, and a young bishop has been installed; but we believe we did not do more than give some customary record of the interesting event.", "그동안 그곳에서는 늙은 주교가 죽고 젊은 주교가 취임하였으나, 우리는 그 흥미로운 사건에 관하여 관례적인 기록을 남긴 것 이상은 하지 않았다고 믿는다."], ["Nor are we now about to meddle very deeply in the affairs of the diocese.", "지금도 우리는 그 교구의 일에 깊이 참견할 뜻이 없다."], ["If any of the chapter feel a qualm of conscience on reading thus far, let it be quieted.", "참사회의 어느 분이든 여기까지 읽고 양심에 거리끼는 바가 있거든 부디 마음을 놓으시라."], ["Above all, let the mind of the new bishop be at rest.", "무엇보다 새 주교의 마음이 편안하시기를."], ["We are now not armed for war, but approach the reverend towers of the old cathedral with an olive branch in our hands.", "우리는 지금 싸울 무장을 갖춘 것이 아니라, 손에 올리브 가지를 들고 저 유서 깊은 대성당의 존엄한 탑들에 다가서는 것이다."]], [["It will be remembered that at the time alluded to, now five years past, we had occasion to remark on the state of a charity in Barchester called Hiram's Hospital.", "이제 5년 전인 그 시절에 우리가 바체스터의 히램 요양원이라 불리는 자선 시설의 형편을 두고 논평할 일이 있었음을 기억하실 것이다."], ["We thought that it was maladministered, and that the very estimable and reverend gentleman who held the office of warden was somewhat too highly paid for duties which were somewhat too easily performed.", "우리는 그것이 잘못 운영되고 있으며, 워든의 직을 맡고 있던 그 대단히 존경스럽고 경건한 신사가 지나치게 쉽게 수행되는 직무에 견주어 지나치게 후한 보수를 받고 있다고 보았다."], ["This gentleman--and we say it in all sincerity and with no touch of sarcasm--had never looked on the matter in this light before.", "이 신사는 — 그리고 우리는 이 말을 아무런 빈정거림 없이 온전한 진심으로 하거니와 — 그때까지 이 문제를 그런 눈으로 바라본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["We do not wish to take praise to ourselves whether praise be due to us or not.", "우리는 그 공이 우리에게 돌아올 것이든 아니든 스스로 공을 취할 뜻이 없다."], ["But the consequence of our remark was that the warden did look into the matter, and finding on so doing that he himself could come to no other opinion than that expressed by us, he very creditably threw up the appointment.", "다만 우리의 논평이 낳은 결과는, 워든이 실제로 그 문제를 들여다보았고, 들여다본 끝에 자신도 우리가 말한 것 말고는 달리 어떤 견해에도 이를 수 없음을 깨닫고는 매우 훌륭하게도 그 직을 내던졌다는 것이다."], ["The then bishop as creditably declined to fill the vacancy till the affair was put on a better footing.", "당시의 주교 또한 그에 못지않게 훌륭하게도, 이 일이 더 나은 기틀 위에 놓이기 전까지는 그 빈자리를 채우기를 사양하였다."], ["Parliament then took it up, and we have now the satisfaction of informing our readers that Hiram's Hospital will be immediately reopened under new auspices.", "그 뒤 의회가 이 문제를 다루었으며, 우리는 이제 히램 요양원이 곧 새로운 후원 아래 다시 문을 열게 되었음을 독자 여러분께 알리게 되어 흡족하다."], ["Heretofore, provision was made for the maintenance of twelve old men.", "종래에는 노인 열두 사람을 부양할 몫이 마련되어 있었다."], ["This will now be extended to the fair sex, and twelve elderly women, if any such can be found in Barchester, will be added to the establishment.", "이것이 이제는 여성에게까지 넓혀져, 바체스터에 그런 분들이 있기만 하다면 나이 든 여인 열두 사람이 그 시설에 더해질 것이다."], ["There will be a matron; there will, it is hoped, be schools attached for the poorest of the children of the poor, and there will be a steward.", "여사감이 한 사람 있을 것이고, 바라건대 가난한 이들 가운데서도 가장 가난한 아이들을 위한 학교가 딸려 있을 것이며, 재산 관리인도 한 사람 있을 것이다."], ["The warden, for there will still be a warden, will receive an income more in keeping with the extent of the charity than that heretofore paid.", "워든은 — 워든은 여전히 있을 것이므로 — 종래에 지급되던 것보다 이 자선 시설의 규모에 한결 걸맞은 수입을 받게 될 것이다."], ["The stipend we believe will be £450.", "그 봉급은 450파운드가 되리라 우리는 알고 있다."], ["We may add that the excellent house which the former warden inhabited will still be attached to the situation.", "아울러 전임 워든이 살던 그 훌륭한 집도 여전히 이 직에 딸려 있으리라는 것을 덧붙여 둔다."]], [["Barchester Hospital cannot perhaps boast a world-wide reputation, but as we adverted to its state of decadence, we think it right also to advert to its renaissance.", "바체스터 요양원이 세계에 이름을 떨친다고 뽐낼 수야 없겠으나, 우리가 그 쇠락한 형편을 언급한 바 있으니 그 부흥에도 언급하는 것이 옳다고 여긴다."], ["May it go on and prosper.", "부디 나아가고 번창하기를."], ["Whether the salutary reform which has been introduced within its walls has been carried as far as could have been desired may be doubtful.", "그 담장 안에 들여온 유익한 개혁이 바랄 만한 데까지 이르렀는지는 의심스러울 수도 있다."], ["The important question of the school appears to be somewhat left to the discretion of the new warden.", "학교라는 저 중요한 문제가 다소간 새 워든의 재량에 맡겨진 듯 보이는 까닭이다."], ["This might have been made the most important part of the establishment, and the new warden, whom we trust we shall not offend by the freedom of our remarks, might have been selected with some view to his fitness as schoolmaster.", "이것이야말로 이 시설에서 가장 중요한 부분이 될 수도 있었으니, 새 워든은 — 우리의 거리낌 없는 논평에 그가 부디 언짢아하지 않기를 바라거니와 — 교사로서의 자질을 어느 정도 염두에 두고 뽑을 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["But we will not now look a gift-horse in the mouth.", "그러나 우리는 지금 얻은 말의 이빨을 들여다보지는 않겠다."], ["May the hospital go on and prosper!", "부디 이 요양원이 나아가고 번창하기를!"], ["The situation of warden has of course been offered to the gentleman who so honourably vacated it five years since, but we are given to understand that he has declined it.", "물론 워든 자리는 5년 전 그토록 명예롭게 그것을 내놓은 그 신사에게 제안되었으나, 우리가 듣기로 그는 이를 사양하였다고 한다."], ["Whether the ladies who have been introduced be in his estimation too much for his powers of control, whether it be that the diminished income does not offer to him sufficient temptation to resume his old place, or that he has in the meantime assumed other clerical duties, we do not know.", "새로 들어오게 된 여인들이 그의 눈에는 감당하기 벅차 보였던 것인지, 줄어든 수입이 옛 자리로 돌아갈 만한 유인이 되지 못했던 것인지, 아니면 그사이에 다른 성직 임무를 맡게 된 것인지 우리로서는 알 수 없다."], ["We are, however, informed that he has refused the offer and that the situation has been accepted by Mr. Quiverful, the vicar of Puddingdale.", "다만 그가 그 제안을 거절하였고 그 자리는 푸딩데일의 교구 사제 퀴버풀 씨가 받아들였다는 소식은 전해 들었다."]], [["So much we think is due to Hiram redivivus.", "되살아난 히램에게 이만큼은 마땅히 돌려야 할 몫이라고 우리는 생각한다."], ["But while we are on the subject of Barchester, we will venture with all respectful humility to express our opinion on another matter connected with the ecclesiastical polity of that ancient city.", "그러나 바체스터 이야기가 나온 김에, 이 유서 깊은 도시의 교회 정치와 관련한 또 한 가지 문제를 두고 정중하고 겸허하게 우리의 의견을 밝혀 볼까 한다."], ["Dr. Trefoil, the dean, died yesterday.", "수석 사제 트리폴 박사가 어제 세상을 떠났다."], ["A short record of his death, giving his age and the various pieces of preferment which he has at different times held, will be found in another column of this paper.", "그의 별세에 관한 짧은 기록은 나이와 그가 여러 시기에 맡았던 여러 성직을 담아 본지의 다른 난에 실릴 것이다."], ["The only fault we knew in him was his age, and as that is a crime of which we all hope to be guilty, we will not bear heavily on it.", "우리가 그에게서 알고 있는 유일한 흠은 나이였는데, 그것은 우리 모두가 저지르게 되기를 바라 마지않는 죄인 만큼 무겁게 나무라지는 않겠다."], ["May he rest in peace!", "부디 평안히 잠드시기를!"], ["But though the great age of an expiring dean cannot be made matter of reproach, we are not inclined to look on such a fault as at all pardonable in a dean just brought to the birth.", "그러나 세상을 떠나는 수석 사제의 많은 나이는 나무랄 거리가 못 된다 하더라도, 이제 막 태어나는 수석 사제에게 그런 흠이 조금이라도 용납될 만하다고는 우리는 생각하지 않는다."], ["We do hope that the days of sexagenarian appointments are past.", "예순 넘은 이를 임명하던 시절은 이제 지나갔기를 우리는 진심으로 바란다."], ["If we want deans, we must want them for some purpose.", "우리에게 수석 사제가 필요하다면, 그것은 어떤 목적을 위해서일 터이다."], ["That purpose will necessarily be better fulfilled by a man of forty than by a man of sixty.", "그 목적은 예순 된 사람보다 마흔 된 사람이 반드시 더 잘 이루어 낼 것이다."], ["If we are to pay deans at all, we are to pay them for some sort of work.", "수석 사제에게 보수를 준다면, 그것은 어떤 종류의 일에 대한 보수일 터이다."], ["That work, be it what it may, will be best performed by a workman in the prime of life.", "그 일이 무엇이든 한창때의 일꾼이 가장 잘해 낼 것이다."], ["Dr. Trefoil, we see, was eighty when he died.", "보아하니 트리폴 박사는 죽을 때 여든이었다."], ["As we have as yet completed no plan for pensioning superannuated clergymen, we do not wish to get rid of any existing deans of that age.", "우리는 늙은 성직자를 위한 연금 제도를 아직 아무것도 마련해 두지 못했으니, 그 나이의 현직 수석 사제들을 내치자는 뜻은 아니다."], ["But we prefer having as few such as possible.", "다만 그런 이가 되도록 적기를 바랄 뿐이다."], ["If a man of seventy be now appointed, we beg to point out to Lord ---- that he will be past all use in a year or two, if indeed he be not so at the present moment.", "지금 일흔 된 이를 임명한다면, 그는 한두 해 안에 아무 쓸모가 없어질 것임을 — 지금 당장 이미 그렇지 않다면 말이지만 — ○○ 경께 삼가 지적해 두는 바이다."], ["His lordship will allow us to remind him that all men are not evergreens like himself.", "모든 사람이 각하처럼 사철 푸른 나무는 아니라는 점을 일깨워 드려도 각하께서는 너그러이 받아 주시리라."]], [["We hear that Mr. Slope's name has been mentioned for this preferment.", "슬로프 씨의 이름이 이 성직 자리에 거론되고 있다는 말을 우리는 듣고 있다."], ["Mr. Slope is at present chaplain to the bishop.", "슬로프 씨는 현재 주교의 전속 사제이다."], ["A better man could hardly be selected.", "이보다 나은 사람을 뽑기란 좀처럼 어려울 것이다."], ["He is a man of talent, young, active, and conversant with the affairs of the cathedral; he is moreover, we conscientiously believe, a truly pious clergyman.", "그는 재능 있는 사람이며, 젊고 활동적이고 대성당의 사정에 밝다. 게다가 우리가 양심을 걸고 믿는 바로는 참으로 경건한 성직자이다."], ["We know that his services in the city of Barchester have been highly appreciated.", "그가 바체스터시에서 해 온 봉사가 높이 평가받아 왔음을 우리는 알고 있다."], ["He is an eloquent preacher and a ripe scholar.", "그는 뛰어난 설교자이며 원숙한 학자이다."], ["Such a selection as this would go far to raise the confidence of the public in the present administration of church patronage and would teach men to believe that from henceforth the establishment of our church will not afford easy couches to worn-out clerical voluptuaries.", "이런 선임이라면 교회 성직 수여의 현 운영에 대한 대중의 신뢰를 크게 끌어올릴 것이며, 이제부터 우리 국교회의 제도가 낡아 빠진 성직 향락가들에게 안락한 자리를 내주지 않으리라는 것을 사람들이 믿게 만들 것이다."]], [["Standing at a reading-desk in the Barchester news-room, Mr. Slope digested this article with considerable satisfaction.", "바체스터 신문 열람실의 독서대 앞에 선 채로 슬로프 씨는 이 기사를 상당한 만족감 속에 소화했다."], ["What was therein said as to the hospital was now comparatively a matter of indifference to him.", "요양원에 관하여 거기 적힌 내용은 이제 그에게 비교적 아무래도 좋은 문제였다."], ["He was certainly glad that he had not succeeded in restoring to the place the father of that virago who had so audaciously outraged all decency in his person, and was so far satisfied.", "그의 인격을 향해 그토록 뻔뻔스럽게 온갖 예의를 짓밟은 그 악녀의 아버지를 그 자리에 되돌려 놓는 데 실패한 것은 물론 다행스러웠고, 그런 점에서는 만족스러웠다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie's nominee was appointed, and he was so far dissatisfied.", "그러나 프라우디 부인이 미는 사람이 임명되었으니, 그런 점에서는 불만스러웠다."], ["His mind, however, was now soaring above Mrs. Bold or Mrs.", "다만 그의 마음은 이제 볼드 부인이나 프라우디 부인 따위보다 훨씬 높이 날아오르고 있었다."], ["Proudie. He was sufficiently conversant with the tactics of \"The Jupiter\"", "그는 『주피터』의 수법에 익히 밝았으므로 기사의 핵심이 마지막 문단에 놓인다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["to know that the pith of the article would lie in the last paragraph. The place of honour was given to him, and it was indeed as honourable as even he could have wished.", "명예로운 그 자리가 자기에게 주어졌고, 그것은 참으로 그가 바랄 수 있었던 만큼이나 명예로웠다."], ["He was very grateful to his friend Mr. Towers, and with full heart looked forward to the day when he might entertain him in princely style at his own full-spread board in the deanery dining-room.", "그는 벗 타워스 씨에게 무척 고마웠고, 언젠가 수석사제관 식당의 제 성찬 차린 식탁에서 왕후처럼 그를 대접할 날을 마음 가득 그려 보았다."]], [["It had been well for Mr. Slope that Dr.", "트리폴 박사가 가을에 죽어 준 것은 슬로프 씨로서는 다행한 일이었다."], ["Trefoil had died in the autumn. Those caterers for our morning repast, the staff of \"The Jupiter,\"", "우리의 아침 식탁에 먹을거리를 대는 저 『주피터』 편집진은 지난 한 달 동안 넉넉한 꼴을 구하느라 몹시 애를 먹던 참이었다."], ["had been sorely put to it for the last month to find a sufficiency of proper pabulum. Just then there was no talk of a new American president.", "마침 새 미국 대통령 이야기도 없었다."], ["No wonderful tragedies had occurred on railway trains in Georgia, or elsewhere. There was a dearth of broken banks, and a dead dean with the necessity for a live one was a godsend.", "조지아든 어디든 철도 열차에서 놀라운 참사가 일어나지도 않았다."], ["Had Dr. Trefoil died in June, Mr.", "파산한 은행도 씨가 말랐으니, 죽은 수석 사제 하나와 산 수석 사제 하나가 필요하다는 사정은 하늘이 내린 선물이었다."], ["Towers would probably not have known so much about the piety of Mr. Slope.", "트리폴 박사가 6월에 죽었더라면 타워스 씨는 아마 슬로프 씨의 경건함에 관하여 그토록 소상히 알지 못했을 것이다."]], [["And here we will leave Mr. Slope for awhile in his triumph, explaining, however, that his feelings were not altogether of a triumphant nature.", "그리고 여기서 우리는 승리에 젖은 슬로프 씨를 잠시 남겨 두기로 한다."], ["His rejection by the widow, or rather the method of his rejection, galled him terribly.", "다만 그의 심경이 온통 승리감뿐이었던 것은 아니라는 점은 밝혀 두어야겠다."], ["For days to come he positively felt the sting upon his cheek whenever he thought of what had been done to him.", "그 미망인에게 거절당한 일, 아니 그보다도 거절당한 그 방식이 그의 속을 지독히도 긁어 놓았다."], ["He could not refrain from calling her by harsh names, speaking to himself as he walked through the streets of Barchester.", "그 뒤로 며칠 동안 그는 자기가 당한 일을 떠올릴 때마다 뺨에 그 얼얼함을 실제로 느꼈다. 바체스터 거리를 걸으며 혼잣말로 그녀를 험한 이름으로 부르지 않고는 배길 수가 없었다."], ["When he said his prayers, he could not bring himself to forgive her.", "기도를 올릴 때에도 그녀를 용서할 마음이 도무지 들지 않았다."], ["When he strove to do so, his mind recoiled from the attempt, and in lieu of forgiving ran off in a double spirit of vindictiveness, dwelling on the extent of the injury he had received.", "억지로라도 그리해 보려 하면 마음이 그 시도에서 움츠러들었고, 용서하기는커녕 두 배의 앙심으로 내달아 제가 입은 상처가 얼마나 큰지를 곱씹었다."], ["And so his prayers dropped senseless from his lips.", "그리하여 그의 기도는 아무 뜻 없이 입술에서 굴러떨어졌다."]], [["And then the signora--what would he not have given to be able to hate her also?", "그리고 시뇨라 — 그녀까지 미워할 수만 있다면 그는 무엇인들 내주지 않았으랴?"], ["As it was, he worshipped the very sofa on which she was ever lying.", "그러기는커녕 그는 그녀가 늘 누워 있는 그 소파마저 떠받들고 있었다."]], [["And thus it was not all rose colour with Mr. Slope, although his hopes ran high.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨에게도 모든 것이 장밋빛만은 아니었으니, 그의 희망만은 드높이 치솟고 있었지만 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV. Mrs. Bold at Home", "# 제44장. 집에 있는 볼드 부인"]], [["Poor Mrs. Bold, when she got home from Ullathorne on the evening of Miss Thorne's party, was very unhappy and, moreover, very tired.", "손 양의 연회가 있던 날 저녁, 울라손에서 집으로 돌아왔을 때 가엾은 볼드 부인은 몹시 불행했고, 게다가 몹시 지쳐 있었다."], ["Nothing fatigues the body so much as weariness of spirit, and Eleanor's spirit was indeed weary.", "마음의 고달픔만큼 몸을 지치게 하는 것은 없거니와, 엘리너의 마음은 참으로 고달팠다."]], [["Dr. Stanhope had civilly but not very cordially asked her in to tea, and her manner of refusal convinced the worthy doctor that he need not repeat the invitation.", "스탠호프 박사는 정중하게, 그러나 그리 살갑지는 않게 그녀에게 들어와 차를 들라고 청했고, 그녀가 사양하는 태도를 보고 이 훌륭한 박사는 그 청을 되풀이할 필요가 없겠다고 확신했다."], ["He had not exactly made himself a party to the intrigue which was to convert the late Mr. Bold's patrimony into an income for his hopeful son, but he had been well aware what was going on.", "그는 고인이 된 볼드 씨의 유산을 제 촉망받는 아들의 수입으로 바꾸어 놓으려던 그 음모에 딱히 한몫 낀 것은 아니었으나, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["And he was well aware also, when he perceived that Bertie declined accompanying them home in the carriage, that the affair had gone off.", "그리고 버티가 마차로 함께 집에 돌아가기를 마다하는 것을 보았을 때, 그 일이 틀어졌다는 것 또한 훤히 알아차렸다."]], [["Eleanor was very much afraid that Charlotte would have darted out upon her, as the prebendary got out at his own door, but Bertie had thoughtfully saved her from this by causing the carriage to go round by her own house.", "엘리너는 참사회원이 제 집 문 앞에서 내릴 때 샬럿이 튀어나와 저를 덮치지나 않을까 몹시 두려웠으나, 버티가 사려 깊게도 마차를 그녀의 집 쪽으로 돌아가게 하여 그 일을 면하게 해 주었다."], ["This also Dr. Stanhope understood and allowed to pass by without remark.", "이것 역시 스탠호프 박사는 알아차렸으되 아무 말 없이 넘어가 주었다."]], [["When she got home, she found Mary Bold in the drawing-room with the child in her lap.", "집에 이르렀을 때 그녀는 메리 볼드가 아기를 무릎에 앉힌 채 응접실에 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She rushed forward and, throwing herself on her knees, kissed the little fellow till she almost frightened him.", "그녀는 달려들어 무릎을 꿇고는, 그 조그만 녀석이 놀랄 지경이 되도록 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"Oh, Mary, I am so glad you did not go.", "“아, 메리, 함께 가지 않기를 정말 잘했어요."], ["It was an odious party.\"", "지긋지긋한 파티였어요.”"]], [["Now the question of Mary's going had been one greatly mooted between them. Mrs.", "그런데 메리가 가느냐 마느냐 하는 문제는 두 사람 사이에서 크게 논의되었던 일이다."], ["Bold, when invited, had been the guest of the Grantlys, and Miss Thorne, who had chiefly known Eleanor at the hospital or at Plumstead Rectory, had forgotten all about Mary Bold.", "볼드 부인은 초대를 받았을 때 그랜틀리 집안의 손님으로 가 있었고, 엘리너를 주로 요양원이나 플럼스테드 목사관에서 보아 온 손 양은 메리 볼드에 대해서는 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["Her sister-in-law had implored her to go under her wing and had offered to write to Miss Thorne, or to call on her.", "올케는 제 날개 밑에 들어와 함께 가자고 간청하며 손 양에게 편지를 쓰거나 찾아가 보겠다고까지 했다."], ["But Miss Bold had declined.", "그러나 볼드 양은 사양했다."], ["In fact, Mr. Bold had not been very popular with such people as the Thornes, and his sister would not go among them unless she were specially asked to do so.", "실은 볼드 씨가 손 집안 같은 사람들에게 그리 인기가 있지 않았던 터라, 그 누이는 특별히 청을 받지 않는 한 그들 틈에 끼려 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, then,\"", "“그렇다면요,”"], ["said Mary cheerfully, \"I have the less to regret.\"", "메리가 밝게 말했다. “아쉬워할 일이 그만큼 줄어든 셈이네요.”"]], [["\"You have nothing to regret; but oh!", "“아쉬워할 일이야 하나도 없지요. 하지만 아!"], ["Mary, I have--so much--so much;\"", "메리, 나는… 너무 많아요, 너무 많아요.”"], ["and then she began kissing her boy, whom her caresses had roused from his slumbers.", "그러고서 그녀는 제 어루만짐에 잠에서 깨어난 아들에게 입을 맞추기 시작했다."], ["When she raised her head, Mary saw that the tears were running down her cheeks.", "고개를 들었을 때, 메리는 그녀의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 보았다."]], [["\"Good heavens, Eleanor, what is the matter?", "“세상에, 엘리너, 무슨 일이에요?"], ["What has happened to you--Eleanor--dearest Eleanor--what is the matter?\"", "무슨 일이 있었기에… 엘리너, 가엾은 엘리너, 대체 무슨 일이에요?”"], ["and Mary got up with the boy still in her arms.", "그러면서 메리는 아이를 여전히 품에 안은 채 일어섰다."]], [["\"Give him to me--give him to me,\"", "“이리 줘요, 이리 줘요.”"], ["said the young mother.", "젊은 어머니가 말했다."], ["\"Give him to me, Mary,\"", "“이리 줘요, 메리.”"], ["and she almost tore the child out of her sister's arms.", "그러면서 그녀는 시누이의 팔에서 아이를 거의 빼앗다시피 했다."], ["The poor little fellow murmured somewhat at the disturbance but nevertheless nestled himself close into his mother's bosom.", "가엾은 조그만 녀석은 방해를 받고 무어라 칭얼거렸으나, 그래도 제 어머니의 품속으로 파고들었다."]], [["\"Here, Mary, take the cloak from me.", "“여기, 메리, 이 외투 좀 받아 줘요."], ["My own, own darling, darling, darling jewel.", "내 것, 오직 내 것인 귀염둥이, 귀염둥이, 귀염둥이 보석아."], ["You are not false to me.", "너는 나를 속이지 않지."], ["Everybody else is false; everybody else is cruel.", "다른 사람들은 다 거짓되고, 다른 사람들은 다 모질단다."], ["Mamma will care for nobody, nobody, nobody, but her own, own, own little man;\"", "엄마는 아무도, 아무도, 아무도 아끼지 않을 거야. 오직 제 것, 제 것, 제 것인 조그만 사내만 빼고.”"], ["and she again kissed and pressed the baby and cried till the tears ran down over the child's face.", "그러면서 그녀는 다시 아기에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안았고, 아이의 얼굴 위로 눈물이 흘러내릴 때까지 울었다."]], [["\"Who has been cruel to you, Eleanor?\"", "“누가 그렇게 모질게 굴었어요, 엘리너?”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["\"I hope I have not.\"", "“나는 아니었으면 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["Now in this matter Eleanor had great cause for mental uneasiness.", "그런데 이 문제에 관해서는 엘리너에게도 마음이 편치 않을 큰 까닭이 있었다."], ["She could not certainly accuse her loving sister-in-law of cruelty; but she had to do that which was more galling: she had to accuse herself of imprudence against which her sister-in-law had warned her.", "다정한 시누이를 모질다고 나무랄 수야 없는 노릇이었으나, 그보다 더 속 쓰린 일을 해야 했으니, 곧 시누이가 미리 일러 주었던 그 경솔함을 두고 자기 자신을 나무라야 했던 것이다."], ["Miss Bold had never encouraged Eleanor's acquaintance with Mr. Slope, and she had positively discouraged the friendship of the Stanhopes, as far as her usual gentle mode of speaking had permitted.", "볼드 양은 엘리너가 슬로프 씨와 가까이 지내는 것을 한 번도 부추긴 적이 없었고, 스탠호프 집안과의 우정에 대해서는 그 부드러운 말투가 허락하는 한도 안에서 분명하게 말렸다."], ["Eleanor had only laughed at her, however, when she said that she disapproved of married women who lived apart from their husbands and suggested that Charlotte Stanhope never went to church.", "그러나 남편과 떨어져 사는 유부녀는 못마땅하다고 하고 샬럿 스탠호프가 도무지 교회에 나가지 않는다고 짚었을 때, 엘리너는 그저 웃어넘겼을 뿐이었다."], ["Now, however, Eleanor must either hold her tongue, which was quite impossible, or confess herself to have been utterly wrong, which was nearly equally so.", "그런데 이제 엘리너로서는 입을 다물고 있거나 — 이는 도무지 될 수 없는 일이었다 — 아니면 제가 아주 틀렸음을 고백하거나 해야 했는데, 이 또한 거의 마찬가지로 될 수 없는 일이었다."], ["So she staved off the evil day by more tears, and consoled herself by inducing little Johnny to rouse himself sufficiently to return her caresses.", "그리하여 그녀는 눈물을 더 흘려 그 궂은 날을 미루었고, 조그만 조니를 흔들어 깨워 제 어루만짐에 응하게 함으로써 스스로를 달랬다."]], [["\"He is a darling--as true as gold.", "“얘는 귀염둥이야. 금처럼 참되지."], ["What would mamma do without him?", "얘가 없으면 엄마는 어떻게 살까?"], ["Mamma would lie down and die if she had not her own Johnny Bold to give her comfort.\"", "저를 위로해 줄 제 조니 볼드가 없으면 엄마는 그냥 드러누워 죽어 버릴 거야.”"], ["This and much more she said of the same kind, and for a time made no other answer to Mary's inquiries.", "이런 말과 이와 비슷한 말을 그녀는 훨씬 더 많이 했고, 한동안 메리가 묻는 말에는 그 밖의 다른 대답을 하지 않았다."]], [["This kind of consolation from the world's deceit is very common.", "세상의 속임수에서 이런 식으로 위안을 얻는 일은 아주 흔하다."], ["Mothers obtain it from their children, and men from their dogs.", "어머니는 제 아이에게서 그것을 얻고, 사내들은 제 개에게서 얻는다."], ["Some men even do so from their walking-sticks, which is just as rational.", "어떤 이들은 제 지팡이에게서까지 그것을 얻는데, 이 또한 꼭 그만큼은 사리에 맞는 일이다."], ["How is it that we can take joy to ourselves in that we are not deceived by those who have not attained the art to deceive us?", "우리를 속이는 재주를 아직 익히지 못한 것들에게 속지 않았다 하여 우리가 어찌 스스로 기뻐할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["In a true man, if such can be found, or a true woman, much consolation may indeed be taken.", "참된 사내가 — 그런 것이 있기나 하다면 — 혹은 참된 여인이 있다면, 거기서는 실로 큰 위안을 얻을 수 있으리라."]], [["In the caresses of her child, however, Eleanor did receive consolation, and may ill befall the man who would begrudge it to her.", "그러나 제 아이를 어루만지는 가운데 엘리너는 참으로 위안을 얻었으니, 그것을 그녀에게 아까워하는 자에게는 화가 있을진저."], ["The evil day, however, was only postponed.", "그러나 궂은 날은 미루어졌을 뿐이었다."], ["She had to tell her disagreeable tale to Mary, and she had also to tell it to her father.", "그녀는 그 언짢은 이야기를 메리에게 해야 했고, 아버지에게도 해야 했다."], ["Must it not, indeed, be told to the whole circle of her acquaintance before she could be made to stand all right with them?", "아니, 아는 이들 앞에서 온전히 떳떳해지려면 그 무리 전체에게 그 이야기를 해야 하지 않겠는가?"], ["At the present moment there was no one to whom she could turn for comfort.", "지금 이 순간 그녀가 위로를 구할 만한 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 슬로프 씨를 미워했다."], ["She hated Mr. Slope; that was a matter of course; in that feeling she revelled.", "그것은 두말할 나위가 없었고, 그 감정 속에서는 오히려 마음이 후련했다."], ["She hated and despised the Stanhopes; but that feeling distressed her greatly.", "그녀는 스탠호프 집안을 미워하고 경멸했다. 그러나 그 감정은 그녀를 몹시 괴롭혔다."], ["She had, as it were, separated herself from her old friends to throw herself into the arms of this family; and then how had they intended to use her?", "그녀는 말하자면 옛 벗들에게서 저를 떼어 내어 이 집안의 품에 몸을 던졌던 것인데, 그러고 나서 그들은 그녀를 어찌 이용하려 들었던가?"], ["She could hardly reconcile herself to her own father, who had believed ill of her.", "저를 나쁘게 여겼던 제 아버지와도 마음을 풀기가 쉽지 않았다."], ["Mary Bold had turned Mentor.", "메리 볼드는 훈계자 노릇을 했다."], ["That she could have forgiven had the Mentor turned out to be in the wrong, but Mentors in the right are not to be pardoned.", "그 훈계자가 틀린 것으로 드러났다면 용서할 수도 있었으련만, 옳은 훈계자는 용서받을 수 없는 법이다."], ["She could not but hate the archdeacon, and now she hated him worse than ever, for she must in some sort humble herself before him.", "부주교를 미워하지 않을 수 없었고, 이제는 어느 만큼 그 앞에서 스스로를 낮추어야 하는 까닭에 전보다 더 미웠다."], ["She hated her sister, for she was part and parcel of the archdeacon.", "언니도 미웠으니, 언니는 부주교와 한 몸이나 다름없었기 때문이다."], ["And she would have hated Mr. Arabin if she could.", "그리고 할 수만 있었다면 애러빈 씨도 미워했을 것이다."], ["He had pretended to regard her, and yet before her face he had hung over that Italian woman as though there had been no beauty in the world but hers--no other woman worth a moment's attention.", "그는 저를 마음에 두는 척하고서도, 그녀가 보는 앞에서 세상에 그 여자 말고는 아름다운 이가 없다는 듯, 잠깐의 눈길이라도 받을 만한 여자가 달리 없다는 듯 그 이탈리아 여자에게 몸을 기울이고 있지 않았던가."], ["And Mr. Arabin would have to learn all this about Mr.", "그런데 애러빈 씨는 슬로프 씨에 관한 이 모든 일을 알게 될 터였다!"], ["Slope! She told herself that she hated him, and she knew that she was lying to herself as she did so.", "그녀는 스스로에게 그를 미워한다고 말했고, 그렇게 말하면서 스스로에게 거짓말을 하고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She had no consolation but her baby, and of that she made the most.", "아기 말고는 위안이 없었으므로, 그녀는 그것을 한껏 누렸다."], ["Mary, though she could not surmise what it was that had so violently affected her sister-in-law, saw at once that her grief was too great to be kept under control and waited patiently till the child should be in his cradle.", "메리는 무엇이 올케를 이토록 격렬하게 뒤흔들어 놓았는지 짐작할 수 없었으나, 그 슬픔이 억눌러 둘 수 있기에는 너무 크다는 것을 이내 알아보고 아이가 요람에 들 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다."]], [["\"You'll have some tea, Eleanor,\"", "“차 좀 들어요, 엘리너.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, I don't care,\"", "“아, 아무래도 좋아요.”"], ["said she, though in fact she must have been very hungry, for she had eaten nothing at Ullathorne.", "그녀가 말했으나, 실은 울라손에서 아무것도 먹지 않았으므로 몹시 시장했을 것이 틀림없었다."]], [["Mary quietly made the tea, and buttered the bread, laid aside the cloak, and made things look comfortable.", "메리는 조용히 차를 끓이고 빵에 버터를 발랐으며, 외투를 치우고 방 안을 편안하게 꾸며 놓았다."]], [["\"He's fast asleep,\"", "“푹 잠들었네요.”"], ["said she; \"you're very tired; let me take him up to bed.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “많이 지쳤을 텐데, 내가 안아다 침대에 뉘어 올게요.”"]], [["But Eleanor would not let her sister touch him.", "그러나 엘리너는 시누이가 아이에게 손을 대게 하려 하지 않았다."], ["She looked wistfully at her baby's eyes, saw that they were lost in the deepest slumber, and then made a sort of couch for him on the sofa.", "그녀는 아기의 눈을 그윽이 들여다보고 그 눈이 가장 깊은 잠에 잠겨 있음을 확인한 뒤, 소파 위에 아이가 누울 자리를 마련해 주었다."], ["She was determined that nothing should prevail upon her to let him out of her sight that night.", "그날 밤만은 무슨 일이 있어도 아이를 제 눈에서 떼어 놓지 않겠다고 그녀는 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["\"Come, Nelly,\"", "“자, 넬리,”"], ["said Mary, \"don't be cross with me.", "메리가 말했다. “나한테 성내지 말아요."], ["I at least have done nothing to offend you.\"", "적어도 나는 엘리너 기분을 상하게 한 일이 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"I an't cross,\"", "“성내는 거 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"Are you angry then?", "“그럼 화가 났어요?"], ["Surely you can't be angry with me.\"", "설마 나한테 화가 났을 리는 없을 텐데요.”"]], [["\"No, I an't angry--at least not with you.\"", "“아니에요, 화 안 났어요. 적어도 메리한테는요.”"]], [["\"If you are not, drink the tea I have made for you.", "“화가 안 났으면 내가 끓여 놓은 차를 마셔요."], ["I am sure you must want it.\"", "분명 마시고 싶을 텐데요.”"]], [["Eleanor did drink it, and allowed herself to be persuaded.", "엘리너는 정말로 그것을 마셨고, 설득당해 주었다."], ["She ate and drank, and as the inner woman was recruited she felt a little more charitable towards the world at large.", "그녀는 먹고 마셨으며, 속사람이 기운을 되찾자 세상 전반에 대해 조금은 너그러워졌다."], ["At last she found words to begin her story, and before she went to bed she had made a clean breast of it and told everything--everything, that is, as to the lovers she had rejected; of Mr.", "마침내 그녀는 제 이야기를 시작할 말을 찾아냈고, 잠자리에 들기 전에 속을 죄다 털어놓고 모든 것을 이야기했다. 모든 것이라고는 하나, 자기가 물리친 구혼자들에 관해서만이었다."], ["Arabin she said not a word.", "애러빈 씨에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"I know I was wrong,\"", "“내가 잘못한 건 알아요.”"], ["said she, speaking of the blow she had given to Mr.", "슬로프 씨에게 날린 손찌검을 두고 그녀가 말했다."], ["Slope; \"but I didn't know what he might do, and I had to protect myself.\"", "“하지만 그이가 무슨 짓을 할지 알 수 없었고, 나는 나를 지켜야 했어요.”"]], [["\"He richly deserved it,\"", "“그런 꼴을 당하고도 남을 사람이지요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."]], [["\"Deserved it!\"", "“당하고도 남다니요!”"], ["said Eleanor, whose mind as regarded Mr. Slope was almost bloodthirsty.", "슬로프 씨에 관한 한 마음이 거의 피에 굶주려 있던 엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"Had I stabbed him with a dagger, he would have deserved it.", "“내가 단검으로 찔렀더라도 그이는 당해도 쌌을 거예요."], ["But what will they say about it at Plumstead?\"", "그런데 플럼스테드에서는 이 일을 두고 뭐라고 할까요?”"]], [["\"I don't think I should tell them,\"", "“나 같으면 그쪽에는 말하지 않겠어요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["Eleanor began to think that she would not.", "엘리너도 말하지 말아야겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["There could have been no kinder comforter than Mary Bold.", "메리 볼드보다 더 다정한 위로자는 없었을 것이다."], ["There was not the slightest dash of triumph about her when she heard of the Stanhope scheme, nor did she allude to her former opinion when Eleanor called her late friend Charlotte a base, designing woman.", "스탠호프 집안의 꿍꿍이를 전해 들었을 때 그녀에게는 우쭐한 기색이라고는 티끌만큼도 없었고, 엘리너가 얼마 전까지 벗이었던 샬럿을 비열하고 음흉한 여자라고 불렀을 때에도 전에 제가 했던 말을 들먹이지 않았다."], ["She re-echoed all the abuse that was heaped on Mr. Slope's head and never hinted that she had said as much before.", "슬로프 씨의 머리 위로 퍼부어지는 온갖 욕설을 그녀는 그대로 받아 되뇌었을 뿐, 자기가 진작 그렇게 말했노라고는 한 번도 내비치지 않았다."], ["\"I told you so, I told you so!\"", "“내가 그랬잖아, 내가 그랬잖아!”"], ["is the croak of a true Job's comforter.", "하는 것은 참된 욥의 친구가 내는 까마귀 울음소리다."], ["But Mary, when she found her friend lying in her sorrow and scraping herself with potsherds, forbore to argue and to exult.", "그러나 메리는 제 벗이 슬픔 가운데 누워 질그릇 조각으로 제 몸을 긁고 있는 것을 보고는, 따지지도 우쭐대지도 않고 삼갔다."], ["Eleanor acknowledged the merit of the forbearance, and at length allowed herself to be tranquilised.", "엘리너는 그 삼감의 고마움을 알아보았고, 마침내 스스로 마음을 가라앉히도록 저를 놓아두었다."]], [["On the next day she did not go out of the house.", "이튿날 그녀는 집 밖으로 나가지 않았다."], ["Barchester she thought would be crowded with Stanhopes and Slopes; perhaps also with Arabins and Grantlys.", "바체스터는 스탠호프네와 슬로프네 사람들로, 어쩌면 애러빈네와 그랜틀리네 사람들로까지 그득할 것 같았다."], ["Indeed, there was hardly anyone among her friends whom she could have met without some cause of uneasiness.", "실로 그녀의 벗들 가운데는 마음이 켕기지 않고 마주칠 수 있는 사람이 거의 없었다."]], [["In the course of the afternoon she heard that the dean was dead, and she also heard that Mr. Quiverful had been finally appointed to the hospital.", "그날 오후에 그녀는 수석 사제가 죽었다는 소식을 들었고, 퀴버풀 씨가 마침내 요양원에 임명되었다는 소식도 들었다."]], [["In the evening her father came to her, and then the story, or as much of it as she could bring herself to tell him, had to be repeated.", "저녁에 아버지가 찾아왔고, 그리하여 그 이야기를, 적어도 아버지에게 차마 말할 수 있는 만큼은 다시 되풀이해야 했다."], ["He was not in truth much surprised at Mr. Slope's effrontery, but he was obliged to act as though he had been to save his daughter's feelings.", "그는 사실 슬로프 씨의 뻔뻔함에 그리 놀라지 않았으나, 딸의 마음을 다치지 않게 하려고 놀란 척해야만 했다."], ["He was, however, anything but skilful in his deceit, and she saw through it.", "그러나 그는 속이는 데에는 도무지 서툴렀으므로, 그녀는 그것을 훤히 알아보았다."]], [["\"I see,\"", "“보아하니,”"], ["said she, \"that you think it only in the common course of things that Mr.", "그녀가 말했다. “아버지는 슬로프 씨가 저를 그런 식으로 대한 것이 지극히 흔한 일이라고 여기시는군요.”"], ["Slope should have treated me in this way.\" She had said nothing to him about the embrace, nor yet of the way in which it had been met.", "그녀는 그 포옹에 대해서도, 그것을 어떻게 맞받았는지에 대해서도 아버지에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"I do not think it at all strange,\"", "“나는 조금도 이상하다고 생각하지 않는단다.”"], ["said he, \"that anyone should admire my Eleanor.\"", "그가 말했다. “누구든 우리 엘리너를 흠모한다는 게 말이다.”"]], [["\"It is strange to me,\"", "“저로서는 이상해요.”"], ["said she, \"that any man should have so much audacity, without ever having received the slightest encouragement.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “털끝만큼도 부추김을 받은 일이 없으면서 어떻게 그런 뻔뻔함을 부릴 수 있는지요.”"]], [["To this Mr. Harding answered nothing.", "이에 대해 하딩 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["With the archdeacon it would have been the text for a rejoinder which would not have disgraced Bildad the Shuhite.", "부주교였다면 이는 수아 사람 빌닷에게도 부끄럽지 않을 반박의 실마리가 되었을 것이다."]], [["\"But you'll tell the archdeacon?\"", "“그렇지만 부주교에게는 말할 테지?”"], ["asked Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"Tell him what?\"", "“무얼 말이에요?”"], ["said she sharply.", "그녀가 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["\"Or Susan?\"", "“아니면 수전에게라도.”"], ["continued Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["\"You'll tell Susan; you'll let them know that they wronged you in supposing that this man's addresses would be agreeable to you.\"", "“수전에게는 말하려무나. 그 사람의 구애가 네게 반가운 것이리라고 짐작한 것이 너를 그르치는 일이었음을 알게 해 주려무나.”"]], [["\"They may find that out their own way,\"", "“그건 그분들이 알아서 알아내시겠지요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; \"I shall not ever willingly mention Mr. Slope's name to either of them.\"", "“저는 두 분 어느 쪽에게도 슬로프 씨의 이름을 제 입으로 꺼내는 일은 없을 거예요.”"]], [["\"But I may.\"", "“그러면 내가 하마.”"]], [["\"I have no right to hinder you from doing anything that may be necessary to your own comfort, but pray do not do it for my sake.", "“아버지 마음이 편해지시는 데 필요한 일이라면 무엇이든 제가 막을 권리는 없지요. 하지만 저를 위해서라면 부디 하지 마세요."], ["Dr. Grantly never thought well of me, and never will.", "그랜틀리 박사는 저를 좋게 여긴 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 거예요."], ["I don't know now that I am even anxious that he should do so.\"", "그리고 이제는 제가 그분에게 좋게 여겨지기를 바라는지조차 잘 모르겠어요.”"]], [["And then they went to the affair of the hospital.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 요양원 일로 넘어갔다."], ["\"But is it true, Papa?\"", "“그런데 그게 사실인가요, 아버지?”"]], [["\"What, my dear?\"", "“무엇이 말이냐, 얘야?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"About the dean?", "“수석 사제 말이냐?"], ["Yes, I fear quite true.", "그래, 아무래도 사실인 듯싶구나."], ["Indeed I know there is no doubt about it.\"", "아니, 그 일에 의심할 여지가 없다는 것을 나는 알고 있단다.”"]], [["\"Poor Miss Trefoil, I am so sorry for her.", "“가엾은 트리폴 양, 정말 안됐어요."], ["But I did not mean that,\"", "하지만 제가 여쭌 건 그게 아니에요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["\"But about the hospital, Papa?\"", "“요양원 말이에요, 아버지.”"]], [["\"Yes, my dear.", "“그래, 얘야."], ["I believe it is true that Mr. Quiverful is to have it.\"", "퀴버풀 씨가 그것을 맡게 되었다는 게 사실인 모양이다.”"]], [["\"Oh, what a shame.\"", "“아, 너무해요.”"]], [["\"No, my dear, not at all, not at all a shame: I am sure I hope it will suit him.\"", "“아니다, 얘야, 조금도, 조금도 너무한 일이 아니란다. 나는 그 자리가 그에게 잘 맞기를 진심으로 바라고 있다.”"]], [["\"But, Papa, you know it is a shame.", "“하지만 아버지, 너무한 일인 줄 아시잖아요."], ["After all your hopes, all your expectations to get back to your old house, to see it given away in this way to a perfect stranger!\"", "그토록 바라시고 그토록 기다리시며 옛집으로 돌아가려 하셨는데, 그것이 이런 식으로 생판 남에게 넘어가다니요!”"]], [["\"My dear, the bishop had a right to give it to whom he pleased.\"", "“얘야, 주교에게는 제 뜻에 드는 사람에게 그것을 줄 권리가 있단다.”"]], [["\"I deny that, Papa. He had no such right.", "“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요, 아버지."], ["It is not as though you were a candidate for a new piece of preferment.", "그런 권리는 없어요. 아버지가 새로 무슨 성직 자리를 노리는 후보인 것도 아니잖아요."], ["If the bishop has a grain of justice--\"", "주교에게 티끌만큼이라도 공정함이 있다면…”"]], [["\"The bishop offered it to me on his terms, and as I did not like the terms, I refused it.", "“주교는 제 조건을 붙여 그것을 내게 내주었고, 나는 그 조건이 마음에 들지 않아 거절했다."], ["After that, I cannot complain.\"", "그러고 나서 불평할 수는 없는 노릇이지.”"]], [["\"Terms!", "“조건이라니요!"], ["He had no right to make terms.\"", "조건을 붙일 권리는 없었어요.”"]], [["\"I don't know about that; but it seems he had the power.", "“그건 나도 모르겠다만, 그럴 힘은 있었던 모양이더구나."], ["But to tell you the truth, Nelly, I am as well satisfied as it is.", "그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 넬리, 나는 지금 이대로도 흡족하단다."], ["When the affair became the subject of angry discussion, I thoroughly wished to be rid of it altogether.\"", "그 일이 노여운 논쟁거리가 되었을 때, 나는 아예 그것을 다 털어 버리고 싶었다.”"]], [["\"But you did want to go back to the old house, Papa.", "“하지만 옛집으로 돌아가고 싶어 하셨잖아요, 아버지."], ["You told me so yourself.\"", "저한테 그렇게 말씀하셨어요.”"]], [["\"Yes, my dear, I did.", "“그래, 얘야, 그랬지."], ["For a short time I did wish it.", "잠시 동안은 그러기를 바랐다."], ["And I was foolish in doing so.", "그리고 그건 어리석은 일이었다."], ["I am getting old now, and my chief worldly wish is for peace and rest.", "나는 이제 늙어 가고 있고, 이 세상에서 바라는 것이라고는 무엇보다 평온과 안식이다."], ["Had I gone back to the hospital, I should have had endless contentions with the bishop, contentions with his chaplain, and contentions with the archdeacon.", "요양원으로 돌아갔더라면 주교와 끝없이 다투고, 그 전속 사제와 다투고, 부주교와 다투었을 게다. 나는 이제 그럴 만한 사람이 못 된다."], ["I am not up to this now; I am not able to meet such troubles; and therefore I am not ill-pleased to find myself left to the little church of St.", "그런 성가신 일들을 감당할 수가 없다. 그러니 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회에 남겨진 것이 내게는 그리 언짢지 않구나."], ["Cuthbert's. I shall never starve,\"", "굶어 죽을 일이야 결코 없을 게다.”"], ["added he, laughing, \"as long as you are here.\"", "그가 웃으며 덧붙였다. “네가 여기 있는 한은 말이다.”"]], [["\"But will you come and live with me, Papa?\"", "“그럼 저와 함께 와서 사시겠어요, 아버지?”"], ["she said earnestly, taking him by both his hands.", "그녀가 아버지의 두 손을 잡으며 간절하게 말했다."], ["\"If you will do that, if you will promise that, I will own that you are right.\"", "“그렇게 해 주신다면, 그렇게 하겠다고 약속해 주신다면, 아버지 말씀이 옳다고 인정하겠어요.”"]], [["\"I will dine with you to-day at any rate.\"", "“오늘은 어쨌든 너와 함께 저녁을 먹으마.”"]], [["\"No, but live here altogether.", "“그게 아니라 아주 여기서 사시라고요."], ["Give up that close, odious little room in High Street.\"", "하이 스트리트의 그 답답하고 지긋지긋한 조그만 방은 버리시고요.”"]], [["\"My dear, it's a very nice little room, and you are really quite uncivil.\"", "“얘야, 그건 아주 근사한 조그만 방이란다. 너는 정말이지 무례하기 짝이 없구나.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, don't joke.", "“아, 아버지, 농담하지 마세요."], ["It's not a nice place for you.", "아버지가 계실 만한 데가 못 돼요."], ["You say you are growing old, though I am sure you are not.\"", "늙어 가신다고 말씀하시지만, 저는 아버지가 늙지 않으셨다고 믿어요.”"]], [["\"Am not I, my dear?\"", "“늙지 않았다고, 얘야?”"]], [["\"No, Papa, not old--not to say old.", "“네, 아버지, 늙지 않으셨어요."], ["But you are quite old enough to feel the want of a decent room to sit in.", "늙었다고 할 정도는 아니에요. 하지만 앉아 지낼 만한 반반한 방이 아쉬울 만큼은 넉넉히 나이 드셨지요."], ["You know how lonely Mary and I are here.", "메리와 제가 여기서 얼마나 쓸쓸한지 아시잖아요."], ["You know nobody ever sleeps in the big front bedroom.", "앞쪽 큰 침실에서는 아무도 자는 사람이 없다는 것도 아시고요."], ["It is really unkind of you to remain up there alone, when you are so much wanted here.\"", "여기서 이토록 아버지를 바라는데 저 위에 혼자 계신다는 건 정말 매정한 일이에요.”"]], [["\"Thank you, Nelly--thank you.", "“고맙구나, 넬리. 고맙다."], ["But, my dear--\"", "하지만 얘야…”"]], [["\"If you had been living here, Papa, with us, as I really think you ought to have done, considering how lonely we are, there would have been none of all this dreadful affair about Mr. Slope.\"", "“아버지가 여기 저희와 함께 사셨더라면, 저희가 얼마나 쓸쓸한지를 생각하면 정말 그러셨어야 했다고 저는 여기는데요, 슬로프 씨에 관한 이 끔찍한 일도 하나도 없었을 거예요.”"]], [["Mr. Harding, however, did not allow himself to be talked over into giving up his own and only little pied à terre in the High Street.", "그러나 하딩 씨는 하이 스트리트에 있는 자기만의, 하나뿐인 조그만 pied à terre를 포기하도록 설득당하지는 않았다."], ["He promised to come and dine with his daughter, and stay with her, and visit her, and do everything but absolutely live with her. It did not suit the peculiar feelings of the man to tell his daughter that though she had rejected Mr.", "그는 딸과 함께 저녁을 먹고, 딸의 집에 머물고, 딸을 찾아오고, 아주 함께 사는 것 말고는 무엇이든 하겠노라고 약속했다."], ["Slope, and been ready to reject Mr. Stanhope, some other more favoured suitor would probably soon appear, and that on the appearance of such a suitor the big front bedroom might perhaps be more frequently in requisition than at present.", "딸이 슬로프 씨를 물리쳤고 스탠호프 씨도 물리칠 참이었다고는 하나 머지않아 더 마음에 드는 다른 구혼자가 나타날 성싶으며, 그런 구혼자가 나타나는 날에는 앞쪽 큰 침실이 지금보다 더 자주 쓰이게 될지도 모른다는 말을 딸에게 하는 것은 이 사람의 유별난 심성에 맞지 않는 일이었다."], ["But doubtless such an idea crossed his mind, and added its weight to the other reasons which made him decide on still keeping the close, odious little room in High Street.", "그러나 그런 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳐 지나간 것은 틀림없으며, 그것이 하이 스트리트의 답답하고 지긋지긋한 조그만 방을 그대로 두기로 마음먹게 한 다른 이유들에 무게를 보태었을 것이다."]], [["The evening passed over quietly and in comfort.", "저녁은 조용하고 편안하게 흘러갔다."], ["Eleanor was always happier with her father than with anyone else.", "엘리너는 언제나 다른 누구와 있을 때보다 아버지와 있을 때 더 행복했다."], ["He had not, perhaps, any natural taste for baby-worship, but he was always ready to sacrifice himself, and therefore made an excellent third in a trio with his daughter and Mary Bold in singing the praises of the wonderful child.", "그에게 아기 숭배를 향한 타고난 취향이 있었다고는 할 수 없겠으나, 그는 언제나 제 몸을 기꺼이 바칠 채비가 되어 있었으므로, 딸과 메리 볼드와 더불어 그 놀라운 아이를 찬미하는 삼중창에서 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 제3성부를 맡았다."]], [["They were standing together over their music in the evening, the baby having again been put to bed upon the sofa, when the servant brought in a very small note in a beautiful pink envelope.", "저녁에 아기를 다시 소파에 뉘어 재워 놓고 세 사람이 함께 악보 앞에 서 있는데, 하녀가 아주 조그만 쪽지 한 장을 어여쁜 분홍빛 봉투에 담아 들여왔다."], ["It quite filled the room with perfume as it lay upon the small salver. Mary Bold and Mrs.", "그것은 조그만 쟁반 위에 놓인 채로 방 안을 온통 향내로 채웠다."], ["Bold were both at the piano, and Mr. Harding was sitting close to them, with the violoncello between his legs, so that the elegancy of the epistle was visible to them all.", "메리 볼드와 볼드 부인은 둘 다 피아노 앞에 있었고, 하딩 씨는 두 다리 사이에 첼로를 끼고 그 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있었으므로, 그 서한의 우아함은 세 사람 모두의 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["\"Please ma'am, Dr. Stanhope's coachman says he is to wait for an answer,\"", "“저, 마님, 스탠호프 박사님 댁 마부가 답장을 기다리라는 분부를 받았다고 합니다.”"], ["said the servant.", "하녀가 말했다."]], [["Eleanor got very red in the face as she took the note in her hand.", "엘리너는 그 쪽지를 손에 받아 들면서 얼굴이 몹시 붉어졌다."], ["She had never seen the writing before.", "그 글씨는 전에 본 적이 없는 것이었다."], ["Charlotte's epistles, to which she was well accustomed, were of a very different style and kind.", "익히 보아 온 샬럿의 편지는 아주 다른 투와 모양새였다."], ["She generally wrote on large note-paper; she twisted up her letters into the shape and sometimes into the size of cocked hats; she addressed them in a sprawling, manly hand, and not unusually added a blot or a smudge, as though such were her own peculiar sign-manual.", "샬럿은 대개 큰 편지지에 썼고, 편지를 삼각모 모양으로, 때로는 그 크기만 하게 비틀어 접었으며, 겉봉은 사내처럼 제멋대로 뻗은 글씨로 썼고, 마치 그것이 제 고유한 서명이라도 되는 양 잉크 방울이나 얼룩을 하나 더하는 일도 드물지 않았다."], ["The address of this note was written in a beautiful female hand, and the gummed wafer bore on it an impress of a gilt coronet.", "이 쪽지의 겉봉은 어여쁜 여자 글씨로 쓰여 있었고, 붙여 놓은 봉함지에는 금빛 작은 관이 도장처럼 찍혀 있었다."], ["Though Eleanor had never seen such a one before, she guessed that it came from the signora.", "엘리너는 그런 것을 전에 본 적이 없었으나, 그것이 시뇨라에게서 온 것이리라 짐작했다."], ["Such epistles were very numerously sent out from any house in which the signora might happen to be dwelling, but they were rarely addressed to ladies.", "그런 서한은 시뇨라가 머무는 집이면 어디서든 수없이 많이 나갔으되, 부인네들에게 보내지는 일은 드물었다."], ["When the coachman was told by the lady's maid to take the letter to Mrs. Bold, he openly expressed his opinion that there was some mistake about it.", "마부는 시녀에게서 그 편지를 볼드 부인에게 가져가라는 말을 듣고, 무언가 잘못된 것이 아니냐는 뜻을 대놓고 내비쳤다."], ["Whereupon the lady's maid boxed the coachman's ears.", "그러자 시녀는 마부의 따귀를 올려붙였다."], ["Had Mr. Slope seen in how meek a spirit the coachman took the rebuke, he might have learnt a useful lesson, both in philosophy and religion.", "마부가 그 꾸지람을 얼마나 온순한 마음으로 받아들이는지를 슬로프 씨가 보았더라면, 철학에서도 종교에서도 쓸모 있는 교훈을 하나 배웠을 것이다."]], [["The note was as follows.", "쪽지의 내용은 아래와 같다."], ["It may be taken as a faithful promise that no further letter whatever shall be transcribed at length in these pages.", "이 책장 안에 그 어떤 편지도 다시는 전문 그대로 옮겨 적지 않겠노라는 미더운 약속으로 여겨 주셔도 좋다."]], [["May I ask you, as a great favour, to call on me to-morrow.", "큰 호의로 여기시고, 내일 저를 찾아와 주십사 청해도 되겠는지요."], ["You can say what hour will best suit you, but quite early, if you can.", "어느 시간이 가장 편하신지 말씀해 주시면 좋겠으나, 되도록 이른 시간이었으면 합니다."], ["I need hardly say that if I could call upon you, I should not take this liberty with you.", "제가 부인을 찾아뵐 수 있는 몸이라면 이런 무례를 범하지 않으리라는 것은 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지요."]], [["I partly know what occurred the other day, and I promise you that you shall meet with no annoyance if you will come to me.", "요전 날 무슨 일이 있었는지 저는 얼마쯤 알고 있으며, 부인께서 저를 찾아와 주신다면 언짢은 일은 조금도 겪지 않으시리라 약속드립니다."], ["My brother leaves us for London to-day; from thence he goes to Italy.", "제 오라비는 오늘 저희 곁을 떠나 런던으로 가고, 거기서 이탈리아로 갑니다."]], [["It will probably occur to you that I should not thus intrude on you, unless I had that to say to you which may be of considerable moment.", "적잖이 중요한 말씀을 드릴 것이 없고서야 제가 이렇게 부인을 성가시게 하지는 않으리라는 생각이 아마 드실 것입니다."], ["Pray therefore excuse me, even if you do not grant my request.", "그러니 제 청을 들어주지 않으시더라도 부디 저를 너그러이 보아 주십시오."]], [["And believe me, Very sincerely yours,", "그러면 부디 저를 믿어 주시기를, 진심을 다하여,"]], [["Thursday Evening", "목요일 저녁"]], [["The three of them sat in consultation on this epistle for some ten or fifteen minutes, and then decided that Eleanor should write a line saying that she would see the signora the next morning at twelve o'clock.", "세 사람은 이 서한을 두고 10분이나 15분쯤 의논하며 앉아 있다가, 엘리너가 이튿날 아침 12시에 시뇨라를 찾아가겠다는 한 줄을 써 보내기로 정했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLV. The Stanhopes at Home", "# 제45장. 집에 있는 스탠호프 일가"]], [["We must now return to the Stanhopes and see how they behaved themselves on their return from Ullathorne.", "이제 우리는 스탠호프 일가로 돌아가, 그들이 울라손에서 돌아온 뒤 어떻게 처신했는지를 보아야 한다."]], [["Charlotte, who came back in the first homeward journey with her sister, waited in palpitating expectation till the carriage drove up to the door a second time.", "동생과 함께 첫 번째 귀갓길에 돌아온 샬럿은 마차가 두 번째로 문 앞에 닿을 때까지 가슴을 두근거리며 기다렸다."], ["She did not run down, or stand at the window, or show in any outward manner that she looked for anything wonderful to occur; but when she heard the carriage wheels, she stood up with erect ears, listening for Eleanor's footfall on the pavement, or the cheery sound of Bertie's voice welcoming her in.", "아래층으로 뛰어 내려가지도, 창가에 서지도, 무언가 놀라운 일이 벌어지기를 기대한다는 기색을 겉으로 드러내지도 않았다. 그러나 마차 바퀴 소리가 들리자 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 귀를 곤두세우고, 포도를 밟는 엘리너의 발소리나 그녀를 맞아들이는 버티의 쾌활한 목소리가 나기를 기다렸다."], ["Had she heard either, she would have felt that all was right; but neither sound was there for her to hear.", "둘 중 하나라도 들렸다면 만사가 잘되었다고 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 귀에는 어느 쪽도 들려오지 않았다."], ["She heard only her father's slow step as he ponderously let himself down from the carriage and slowly walked along the hall, till he got into his own private room on the ground floor.", "들린 것은 아버지의 느린 발소리뿐이었다. 아버지는 육중하게 마차에서 내려 복도를 천천히 걸어, 1층에 있는 자기 방으로 들어갔다."], ["\"Send Miss Stanhope to me,\"", "“스탠호프 양을 들여보내라.”"], ["he said to the servant.", "그가 하인에게 말했다."]], [["\"There's something wrong now,\"", "“뭔가 잘못됐어.”"], ["said Madeline, who was lying on her sofa in the back drawing-room.", "뒤쪽 응접실 소파에 누운 채로 마들린이 말했다."]], [["\"It's all up with Bertie,\"", "“버티는 이제 끝났어.”"], ["replied Charlotte.", "샬럿이 대꾸했다."], ["\"I know, I know,\"", "“알았어, 알았다니까.”"], ["she said to the servant as he brought up the message.", "전갈을 가지고 올라온 하인에게 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Tell my father I will be with him immediately.\"", "“곧 가겠다고 아버지께 전해라.”"]], [["\"Bertie's wooing has gone astray,\"", "“버티의 구애가 어긋난 모양이야.”"], ["said Madeline.", "마들린이 말했다."], ["\"I knew it would.\"", "“그럴 줄 알았어.”"]], [["\"It has been his own fault then.", "“그렇다면 제 잘못이지."], ["She was ready enough, I am quite sure,\"", "그 여자 쪽은 마음이 충분히 있었을 거야, 난 확신해.”"], ["said Charlotte with that sort of ill-nature which is not uncommon when one woman speaks of another.", "샬럿이 말했다. 한 여자가 다른 여자를 두고 말할 때 드물지 않게 나오는 그런 심술궂은 어조였다."]], [["\"What will you say to him now?\"", "“이제 그분께는 뭐라고 할 거야?”"], ["By \"him,\" the signora meant their father.", "‘그분’이란 시뇨라가 아버지를 두고 한 말이었다."]], [["\"That will be as I find him.", "“그건 아버지가 어떤 상태이신가에 달렸지."], ["He was ready to pay two hundred pounds for Bertie to stave off the worst of his creditors, if this marriage had gone on.", "이 혼사가 성사되기만 했으면 아버지는 버티의 급한 빚쟁이들을 막아 주려고 200파운드를 낼 참이셨어."], ["Bertie must now have the money instead and go and take his chance.\"", "이제는 그 돈을 버티가 대신 받아서 나가 제 운을 시험해 봐야지.”"]], [["\"Where is he now?\"", "“지금 걔는 어디 있어?”"]], [["\"Heaven knows!", "“하늘이나 알겠지!"], ["Smoking in the bottom of Mr. Thorne's ha-ha, or philandering with some of those Miss Chadwicks.", "손 씨 댁 정원 도랑 바닥에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있거나, 채드윅 양들 가운데 누구랑 시시덕거리고 있겠지."], ["Nothing will ever make an impression on him.", "저 애한테는 무슨 말을 해도 새겨지는 법이 없어."], ["But he'll be furious if I don't go down.\"", "하지만 내가 내려가지 않으면 아버지가 노발대발하실 거야.”"]], [["\"No, nothing ever will.", "“그래, 무슨 말을 해도 새겨지지 않지."], ["But don't be long, Charlotte, for I want my tea.\"", "하지만 오래 끌지는 마, 샬럿. 나 차 마시고 싶으니까.”"]], [["And so Charlotte went down to her father.", "그리하여 샬럿은 아버지에게로 내려갔다."], ["There was a very black cloud on the old man's brow--blacker than his daughter could ever yet remember to have seen there.", "노인의 이마에는 아주 검은 구름이 드리워 있었다. 딸이 여태껏 보았던 기억이 있는 어떤 것보다도 검은 구름이었다."], ["He was sitting in his own armchair, not comfortably over the fire, but in the middle of the room, waiting till she should come and listen to him.", "그는 자기 안락의자에 앉아 있었으되, 난롯가에 편안히 자리 잡은 것이 아니라 방 한가운데에 의자를 놓고 앉아 딸이 와서 자기 말을 들을 때까지 기다리고 있었다."]], [["\"What has become of your brother?\"", "“네 동생은 어떻게 된 거냐?”"], ["he said as soon as the door was shut.", "문이 닫히자마자 그가 말했다."]], [["\"I should rather ask you,\"", "“제가 오히려 여쭤봐야 할 것 같은데요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"I left you both at Ullathorne when I came away.", "“저는 먼저 오면서 두 분을 울라손에 두고 왔잖아요."], ["What have you done with Mrs. Bold?\"", "볼드 부인은 어떻게 하셨어요?”"]], [["\"Mrs. Bold!", "“볼드 부인이라니!"], ["Nonsense.", "말도 안 되는 소리."], ["The woman has gone home as she ought to do.", "그 부인은 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["And heartily glad I am that she should not be sacrificed to so heartless a reprobate.\"", "그런 무정한 난봉꾼에게 제물로 바쳐지지 않았으니 나로서는 진심으로 다행한 일이지.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa!\"", "“어머, 아버지도!”"]], [["\"A heartless reprobate!", "“무정한 난봉꾼이고말고!"], ["Tell me now where he is and what he is going to do.", "자, 그 애가 지금 어디 있고 앞으로 무얼 할 작정인지 말해 보아라."], ["I have allowed myself to be fooled between you.", "내가 너희들 사이에서 놀아나도록 나 자신을 내버려 두었구나."], ["Marriage, indeed!", "결혼이라니, 원!"], ["Who on earth that has money, or credit, or respect in the world to lose would marry him?\"", "잃을 돈이나 신용이나 세상의 존경을 조금이라도 지닌 사람이라면 대체 누가 그 애와 결혼하겠느냐?”"]], [["\"It is no use your scolding me, Papa.", "“저를 나무라셔야 소용없어요, 아버지."], ["I have done the best I could for him and you.\"", "저는 그 애를 위해서도 아버지를 위해서도 할 수 있는 만큼은 다 했어요.”"]], [["\"And Madeline is nearly as bad,\"", "“마들린도 그 애 못지않게 못됐지.”"], ["said the prebendary, who was in truth very, very angry.", "참사회원이 말했다. 그는 사실 몹시, 몹시 화가 나 있었다."]], [["\"Oh, I suppose we are all bad,\"", "“아, 저희는 다들 못된 것들이겠지요.”"], ["replied Charlotte.", "샬럿이 대꾸했다."]], [["The old man emitted a huge, leonine sigh.", "노인은 사자 같은 커다란 한숨을 내뱉었다."], ["If they were all bad, who had made them so?", "그들이 모두 못되었다면, 그들을 그렇게 만든 것은 누구인가?"], ["If they were unprincipled, selfish, and disreputable, who was to be blamed for the education which had had so injurious an effect?", "그들이 줏대 없고 이기적이고 볼썽사납다면, 그토록 해로운 결과를 낳은 그 교육의 책임은 누구에게 물어야 하는가?"]], [["\"I know you'll ruin me among you,\"", "“너희가 한통속이 되어 나를 파산시키고 말 게다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Why, Papa, what nonsense that is.", "“아니, 아버지, 그게 무슨 터무니없는 말씀이세요."], ["You are living within your income this minute, and if there are any new debts, I don't know of them.", "지금 이 순간에도 아버지는 수입 안에서 사시잖아요. 새 빚이 있다면 저는 모르는 일이고요."], ["I am sure there ought to be none, for we are dull enough here.\"", "새 빚이 있을 리도 없어요. 여기 사는 게 어지간히 심심하니까요.”"]], [["\"Are those bills of Madeline's paid?\"", "“마들린의 그 청구서들은 다 치렀느냐?”"]], [["\"No, they are not.", "“아뇨, 안 치렀어요."], ["Who was to pay them?\"", "그걸 누가 치를 수 있었겠어요?”"]], [["\"Her husband may pay them.\"", "“그 애 남편더러 치르라고 해라.”"]], [["\"Her husband!", "“남편이라뇨!"], ["Would you wish me to tell her you say so?", "아버지가 그러셨다고 제가 그 애한테 전하기를 바라세요?"], ["Do you wish to turn her out of your house?\"", "그 애를 이 집에서 내쫓고 싶으신 거예요?”"]], [["\"I wish she would know how to behave herself.\"", "“나는 그 애가 제 몸가짐을 좀 알았으면 싶을 뿐이다.”"]], [["\"Why, what on earth has she done now?", "“아니, 그 애가 대체 이번엔 무얼 했다는 거예요?"], ["Poor Madeline!", "가엾은 마들린!"], ["To-day is only the second time she has gone out since we came to this vile town.\"", "이 고약한 도시에 온 뒤로 그 애가 바깥에 나간 것은 오늘로 겨우 두 번째예요.”"]], [["He then sat silent for a time, thinking in what shape he would declare his resolve.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 결심을 어떤 형태로 선언할까 궁리하며 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. “그래서요, 아버지.”"], ["\"Well, Papa,\"", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["said Charlotte, \"shall I stay here, or may I go upstairs and give Mamma her tea?\"", "“제가 여기 더 있을까요, 아니면 위층에 올라가 어머니 차 시중을 들어도 될까요?”"]], [["\"You are in your brother's confidence.", "“너는 네 동생이 속을 털어놓는 상대가 아니냐."], ["Tell me what he is going to do.\"", "그 애가 무얼 할 작정인지 말해 보아라.”"]], [["\"Nothing, that I am aware of.\"", "“제가 아는 한, 아무것도 하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Nothing--nothing!", "“아무것도, 아무것도라니!"], ["Nothing but eat and drink and spend every shilling of my money he can lay his hands upon.", "먹고 마시고, 손에 닿는 대로 내 돈을 한 실링까지 다 써 버리는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않겠지."], ["I have made up my mind, Charlotte.", "나는 마음을 정했다, 샬럿."], ["He shall eat and drink no more in this house.\"", "그 애는 이 집에서 더는 먹지도 마시지도 못한다.”"]], [["\"Very well.", "“좋아요."], ["Then I suppose he must go back to Italy.\"", "그렇다면 그 애는 이탈리아로 돌아가야겠네요.”"]], [["\"He may go where he pleases.\"", "“제가 가고 싶은 데로 가라지.”"]], [["\"That's easily said, Papa, but what does it mean?", "“말씀이야 쉽지요, 아버지. 그런데 그게 무슨 뜻이에요?"], ["You can't let him--\"", "설마 그 애를…”"]], [["\"It means this?\"", "“이런 뜻이다.”"], ["said the doctor, speaking more loudly than was his wont and with wrath flashing from his eyes; \"that as sure as God rules in heaven I will not maintain him any longer in idleness.\"", "평소보다 목소리를 높이고 눈에서 노기를 번뜩이며 박사가 말했다. “하나님이 하늘에서 다스리심이 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게, 나는 그 애를 더 이상 놀고먹도록 거두어 주지 않겠다는 뜻이다.”"]], [["\"Oh, ruling in heaven!\"", "“아유, 하늘에서 다스리시기는요!”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"It is no use talking about that.", "“그런 이야기는 해 봐야 소용없어요."], ["You must rule him here on earth; and the question is, how can you do it.", "아버지가 다스리셔야 할 곳은 여기 땅 위이고, 문제는 그것을 어떻게 하시느냐는 것이에요."], ["You can't turn him out of the house penniless, to beg about the street.\"", "그 애를 한 푼도 없이 집 밖으로 내몰아 거리에서 구걸하게 하실 수는 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"He may beg where he likes.\"", "“구걸이야 제가 하고 싶은 데서 하라지.”"]], [["\"He must go back to Carrara.", "“그 애는 카라라로 돌아가야 해요."], ["That is the cheapest place he can live at, and nobody there will give him credit for above two or three hundred pauls.", "그 애가 살 수 있는 곳 가운데 값이 가장 싸고, 거기서는 아무도 그 애한테 200~300파울 넘게 외상을 주지 않을 테니까요."], ["But you must let him have the means of going.\"", "하지만 갈 수 있는 노자만은 마련해 주셔야 해요.”"]], [["\"As sure as--\"", "“하늘에 맹세코…”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, don't swear.", "“아유, 아버지, 맹세는 하지 마세요."], ["You know you must do it.", "아버지도 그렇게 하셔야 한다는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["You were ready to pay two hundred pounds for him if this marriage came off.", "이 혼사가 성사되었더라면 그 애를 위해 200파운드를 내실 참이셨어요."], ["Half that will start him to Carrara.\"", "그 절반이면 그 애를 카라라로 떠나보낼 수 있어요.”"]], [["\"What?", "“뭐라고?"], ["Give him a hundred pounds?\"", "그 애한테 100파운드를 주라고?”"]], [["\"You know we are all in the dark, Papa,\"", "“저희는 아무것도 모르고 있잖아요, 아버지.”"], ["said she, thinking it expedient to change the conversation.", "화제를 돌리는 편이 낫겠다고 여기며 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"For anything we know he may be at this moment engaged to Mrs. Bold.\"", "“저희가 모르는 사이에 그 애가 지금 이 순간 볼드 부인과 약혼했을지도 모르는 일이고요.”"]], [["\"Fiddlestick,\"", "“허튼소리.”"], ["said the father, who had seen the way in which Mrs.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["Bold had got into the carriage while his son stood apart without even offering her his hand.", "그는 아들이 손 한 번 내밀지도 않고 멀찍이 떨어져 서 있는 동안 볼드 부인이 마차에 오르던 모양을 보았던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, then, he must go to Carrara,\"", "“그럼 그 애는 카라라로 가야지요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["Just at this moment the lock of the front door was heard, and Charlotte's quick ears detected her brother's catlike step in the hall.", "바로 그때 현관문 자물쇠 소리가 났고, 샬럿의 밝은 귀는 복도에서 나는 동생의 고양이 같은 발소리를 알아챘다."], ["She said nothing, feeling that for the present Bertie had better keep out of her father's way.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 당분간은 버티가 아버지 눈에 띄지 않는 편이 낫다고 여긴 것이다."], ["But Dr. Stanhope also heard the sound of the lock.", "그러나 스탠호프 박사에게도 자물쇠 소리는 들렸다."]], [["\"Who's that?\"", "“누구냐?”"], ["he demanded.", "그가 캐물었다."], ["Charlotte made no reply, and he asked again, \"Who is that that has just come in?", "샬럿이 아무 대답도 하지 않자 그가 다시 물었다. “방금 들어온 게 누구냐?"], ["Open the door.", "문을 열어라."], ["Who is it?\"", "누구야?”"]], [["\"I suppose it is Bertie.\"", "“버티겠지요.”"]], [["\"Bid him come here,\"", "“들어오라고 일러라.”"], ["said the father.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["But Bertie, who was close to the door and heard the call, required no further bidding, but walked in with a perfectly unconcerned and cheerful air.", "그러나 문 가까이에 있다가 그 부름을 들은 버티는 더 이를 것도 없이, 조금도 개의치 않는 쾌활한 낯빛으로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["It was this peculiar insouciance which angered Dr. Stanhope, even more than his son's extravagance.", "스탠호프 박사를 아들의 낭비벽보다도 더 화나게 하는 것이 바로 이 독특한 insouciance였다."]], [["\"Well, sir?\"", "“그래, 어떻게 된 거냐?”"], ["said the doctor.", "박사가 말했다."]], [["\"And how did you get home, sir, with your fair companion?\"", "“그런데 아버지, 그 아리따운 동행분과는 어떻게 돌아오셨어요?”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"I suppose she is not upstairs, Charlotte?\"", "“설마 위층에 계시지는 않겠지, 샬럿?”"]], [["\"Bertie,\"", "“버티.”"], ["said Charlotte, \"Papa is in no humour for joking.", "샬럿이 말했다. “아버지는 농담을 받으실 기분이 아니야."], ["He is very angry with you.\"", "너한테 몹시 화가 나셨어.”"]], [["\"Angry!\"", "“화가 나셨다고!”"], ["said Bertie, raising his eyebrows as though he had never yet given his parent cause for a single moment's uneasiness.", "여태껏 부모에게 단 한순간의 불편함도 끼친 적이 없다는 듯 눈썹을 치켜올리며 버티가 말했다."]], [["\"Sit down, if you please, sir,\"", "“앉거라.”"], ["said Dr. Stanhope very sternly but not now very loudly.", "스탠호프 박사가 매우 준엄하게, 그러나 이번에는 그리 큰 소리가 아니게 말했다."], ["\"And I'll trouble you to sit down, too, Charlotte.", "“샬럿, 너도 좀 앉아 주었으면 좋겠구나."], ["Your mother can wait for her tea a few minutes.\"", "네 어미는 차를 몇 분쯤 더 기다려도 된다.”"]], [["Charlotte sat down on the chair nearest to the door in somewhat of a perverse sort of manner, as much as though she would say--\"Well, here I am; you shan't say I don't do what I am bid; but I'll be whipped if I give way to you.\"", "샬럿은 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 어딘가 심사가 뒤틀린 태도로 앉았다. 이렇게 말하는 것이나 다름없는 태도였다."], ["And she was determined not to give way.", "“자, 앉았어요. 시키시는 대로 하지 않는다는 말씀은 못 하시겠지요."], ["She too was angry with Bertie, but she was not the less ready on that account to defend him from his father.", "하지만 제가 아버지께 굽히고 들어간다면 매를 맞아도 좋아요.” 실제로 그녀는 굽히지 않기로 작정하고 있었다."], ["Bertie also sat down. He drew his chair close to the library-table, upon which he put his elbow, and then resting his face comfortably on one hand, he began drawing little pictures on a sheet of paper with the other.", "그녀 역시 버티에게 화가 나 있었지만, 그렇다고 해서 아버지로부터 그를 감싸 줄 태세가 조금이라도 덜한 것은 아니었다."], ["Before the scene was over he had completed admirable figures of Miss Thorne, Mrs.", "버티도 자리에 앉았다. 그는 의자를 서재 탁자 가까이 끌어다 놓고 탁자 위에 팔꿈치를 올린 다음, 한 손에 얼굴을 편안히 괴고 다른 손으로 종이 한 장에 자잘한 그림들을 그리기 시작했다."], ["Proudie, and Lady De Courcy, and begun a family piece to comprise the whole set of the Lookalofts.", "이 장면이 끝나기 전에 그는 손 양과 프라우디 부인과 레이디 드 코시의 훌륭한 초상을 완성했고, 룩어로프트 일가 전원을 담을 가족화까지 시작해 놓았다."]], [["\"Would it suit you, sir,\" said the father, \"to give me some idea as to what your present intentions are?", "“네 지금 속셈이 무엇인지 나에게 조금이나마 일러 줄 마음이 있느냐?”"], ["What way of living you propose to yourself?\"", "아버지가 말했다. “앞으로 어떤 방식으로 살아갈 작정인지 말이다.”"]], [["\"I'll do anything you can suggest, sir,\"", "“아버지가 무엇을 이르시든 그대로 하겠어요.”"], ["replied Bertie.", "버티가 대답했다."]], [["\"No, I shall suggest nothing further.", "“아니, 나는 이제 아무것도 이르지 않겠다."], ["My time for suggesting has gone by.", "내가 이를 때는 이미 지나갔어."], ["I have only one order to give, and that is that you leave my house.\"", "내릴 명령은 하나뿐이다. 내 집에서 나가라는 것이다.”"]], [["\"To-night?\"", "“오늘 밤에요?”"], ["said Bertie, and the simple tone of the question left the doctor without any adequately dignified method of reply.", "버티가 말했다. 그 물음의 담백한 어조 앞에서 박사는 위엄을 갖추어 대답할 방도를 찾지 못했다."]], [["\"Papa does not quite mean to-night,\"", "“아버지가 꼭 오늘 밤을 말씀하시는 건 아니야.”"], ["said Charlotte; \"at least I suppose not.\"", "샬럿이 말했다. “적어도 내 생각엔 그래.”"]], [["\"To-morrow, perhaps,\"", "“그럼 내일쯤일까요.”"], ["suggested Bertie.", "버티가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, sir, to-morrow,\"", "“그래, 내일이다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["\"You shall leave this to-morrow.\"", "“내일 이 집을 나가야 한다.”"]], [["\"Very well, sir.", "“알겠어요, 아버지."], ["Will the 4.30 P.M. train be soon enough?\"", "오후 4시 30분 기차면 충분히 이르겠지요?”"], ["and Bertie, as he asked, put the finishing touch to Miss Thorne's high-heeled boots.", "그렇게 물으면서 버티는 손 양의 굽 높은 장화에 마지막 손질을 넣었다."]], [["\"You may go how and when and where you please, so that you leave my house to-morrow.", "“내일 내 집을 나가기만 한다면 어떻게 언제 어디로 가든 네 마음대로 해라."], ["You have disgraced me, sir; you have disgraced yourself, and me, and your sisters.\"", "너는 나를 욕보였다. 너 자신을, 나를, 그리고 네 누이들을 욕보였어.”"]], [["\"I am glad at least, sir, that I have not disgraced my mother,\"", "“적어도 어머니는 욕보이지 않았으니 다행이에요, 아버지.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."]], [["Charlotte could hardly keep her countenance, but the doctor's brow grew still blacker than ever.", "샬럿은 웃음을 참느라 얼굴을 가누기가 어려웠으나 박사의 이마는 전보다 한층 더 검어졌다."], ["Bertie was executing his chef d'oeuvre in the delineation of Mrs. Proudie's nose and mouth.", "버티는 프라우디 부인의 코와 입을 그려 내며 자신의 chef d’oeuvre를 완성하는 중이었다."]], [["\"You are a heartless reprobate, sir; a heartless, thankless, good-for-nothing reprobate.", "“너는 무정한 난봉꾼이다. 무정하고 은혜도 모르고 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 난봉꾼이야."], ["I have done with you.", "나는 너와는 끝이다."], ["You are my son--that I cannot help--but you shall have no more part or parcel in me as my child, nor I in you as your father.\"", "네가 내 아들인 것이야 어쩔 수 없다만, 이제 너는 내 자식으로서 나와 아무 상관이 없고, 나도 네 아비로서 너와 아무 상관이 없다.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, Papa!", "“어머, 아버지, 아버지!"], ["You must not, shall not say so,\"", "그런 말씀 하시면 안 돼요, 하셔서도 안 되고요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"I will say so, and do say so,\"", "“하겠다, 그리고 지금 하고 있다.”"], ["said the father, rising from his chair.", "아버지가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다."], ["\"And now leave the room, sir.\"", "“자, 이제 방에서 나가거라.”"]], [["\"Stop, stop,\"", "“잠깐만요, 잠깐만요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"Why don't you speak, Bertie?", "“버티, 왜 말을 안 해?"], ["Why don't you look up and speak?", "왜 고개를 들고 말을 안 하느냐고."], ["It is your manner that makes Papa so angry.\"", "아버지를 저렇게 화나게 만드는 건 바로 네 그 태도야.”"]], [["\"He is perfectly indifferent to all decency, to all propriety,\"", "“저 애는 예의도 체면도 조금도 아랑곳하지 않는구나.” 박사가 말했다."], ["said the doctor; then he shouted out, \"Leave the room, sir!", "그러고는 소리를 질렀다. “방에서 나가!"], ["Do you hear what I say?\"", "내 말이 들리지 않느냐?”"]], [["\"Papa, Papa, I will not let you part so.", "“아버지, 아버지, 이렇게 갈라서시게 두지는 않겠어요."], ["I know you will be sorry for it.\"", "아버지가 나중에 후회하실 걸 저는 알아요.”"], ["And then she added, getting up and whispering into his ear, \"Is he only to blame?", "그러고는 자리에서 일어나 아버지의 귀에 대고 속삭이며 덧붙였다. “잘못이 저 애한테만 있을까요?"], ["Think of that.", "그것을 생각해 보세요."], ["We have made our own bed, and, such as it is, we must lie on it.", "우리 잠자리는 우리가 편 것이고, 그것이 어떤 자리든 우리는 그 위에 누워야 해요."], ["It is no use for us to quarrel among ourselves,\"", "우리끼리 다투어 봐야 아무 소용이 없어요.”"], ["and as she finished her whisper, Bertie finished off the countess's bustle, which was so well done that it absolutely seemed to be swaying to and fro on the paper with its usual lateral motion.", "그녀가 속삭임을 마쳤을 때 버티는 백작부인의 허리받이를 마무리했는데, 어찌나 잘 그렸던지 종이 위에서 그것이 평소의 그 좌우 흔들림으로 실제로 살랑거리는 듯이 보였다."]], [["\"My father is angry at the present time,\"", "“아버지가 지금 화가 나신 건.”"], ["said Bertie, looking up for a moment from his sketches, \"because I am not going to marry Mrs.", "스케치에서 잠깐 눈을 들며 버티가 말했다. “제가 볼드 부인과 결혼하지 않을 것이기 때문이에요."], ["Bold. What can I say on the matter?", "그 일에 대해 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["It is true that I am not going to marry her.", "제가 그분과 결혼하지 않으리라는 건 사실이에요."], ["In the first place--\"", "우선 첫째로…”"]], [["\"That is not true, sir,\" said Dr.", "“그것은 사실이 아니다.”"], ["Stanhope, \"but I will not argue with you.\"", "스탠호프 박사가 말했다. “허나 너와 입씨름을 하지는 않겠다.”"]], [["\"You were angry just this moment because I would not speak,\"", "“방금 전에는 제가 말을 하지 않는다고 화를 내셨잖아요.”"], ["said Bertie, going on with a young Lookaloft.", "어린 룩어로프트를 계속 그려 나가며 버티가 말했다."]], [["\"Give over drawing,\"", "“그림은 그만 그려.”"], ["said Charlotte, going up to him and taking the paper from under his hand.", "샬럿이 그에게 다가가 손 밑에서 종이를 빼내며 말했다."], ["The caricatures, however, she preserved and showed them afterwards to the friends of the Thornes, the Proudies, and De Courcys.", "그러나 그 풍자화들은 잘 간직해 두었다가 나중에 손 씨네와 프라우디 씨네와 드 코시 씨네의 지인들에게 보여 주었다."], ["Bertie, deprived of his occupation, threw himself back in his chair and waited further orders.", "소일거리를 빼앗긴 버티는 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 다음 분부를 기다렸다."]], [["\"I think it will certainly be for the best that Bertie should leave this at once; perhaps to-morrow,\"", "“버티가 곧바로, 어쩌면 내일이라도 이곳을 떠나는 편이 가장 좋으리라는 데는 저도 분명 동의해요.”"], ["said Charlotte; \"but pray, Papa, let us arrange some scheme together.\"", "샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 아버지, 부디 우리 함께 무슨 방도를 마련하기로 해요.”"]], [["\"If he will leave this to-morrow, I will give him £10, and he shall be paid £5 a month by the banker at Carrara as long as he stays permanently in that place.\"", "“내일 이곳을 떠난다면 10파운드를 주겠다. 그리고 그곳에 눌러 있는 동안에는 카라라의 은행가를 통해 다달이 5파운드씩 지급하도록 하마.”"]], [["\"Well, sir, it won't be long,\"", "“그럼 오래가지는 않겠네요, 아버지.”"], ["said Bertie, \"for I shall be starved to death in about three months.\"", "버티가 말했다. “석 달쯤이면 굶어 죽을 테니까요.”"]], [["\"He must have marble to work with,\"", "“작업할 대리석은 있어야지요.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"I have plenty there in the studio to last me three months,\"", "“석 달 버틸 만큼은 그곳 작업실에 넉넉히 있어.”"], ["said Bertie.", "버티가 말했다."], ["\"It will be no use attempting anything large in so limited a time--unless I do my own tombstone.\"", "“그렇게 한정된 기간에 큰 작품을 시도해 봐야 소용없겠지. 내 묘비를 만드는 것이라면 몰라도.”"]], [["Terms, however, were ultimately come to somewhat more liberal than those proposed, and the doctor was induced to shake hands with his son and bid him good night.", "그러나 결국 처음 제시된 것보다는 얼마간 후한 조건으로 합의가 이루어졌고, 박사는 아들과 악수를 하고 잘 자라는 인사를 건네기에 이르렀다."], ["Dr. Stanhope would not go up to tea, but had it brought to him in his study by his daughter.", "스탠호프 박사는 차를 마시러 위층에 올라가지 않고, 딸에게 서재로 차를 가져오게 했다."]], [["But Bertie went upstairs and spent a pleasant evening.", "그러나 버티는 위층으로 올라가 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈다."], ["He finished the Lookalofts, greatly to the delight of his sisters, though the manner of portraying their décolleté dresses was not the most refined.", "그는 룩어로프트 일가의 그림을 완성해 누이들을 크게 즐겁게 했는데, 그들의 décolleté 드레스를 묘사한 방식은 그리 고상하다고 할 수 없었다."], ["Finding how matters were going, he by degrees allowed it to escape from him that he had not pressed his suit upon the widow in a very urgent way.", "일이 어떻게 되어 가는지를 알아차린 그는 자기가 그 미망인에게 그리 다급하게 구애를 밀어붙이지는 않았다는 사실을 조금씩 흘려 놓았다."]], [["\"I suppose, in point of fact, you never proposed at all?\"", "“실은 청혼이란 걸 아예 하지도 않은 거지?”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, she understood that she might have me if she wished,\"", "“아, 원하기만 하면 나를 가질 수 있다는 건 그분도 알아들었어.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"And she didn't wish,\"", "“그런데 원하지 않았던 거고.”"], ["said the Signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."]], [["\"You have thrown me over in the most shameful manner,\"", "“너는 참으로 창피스러운 방식으로 나를 저버렸어.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."], ["\"I suppose you told her all about my little plan?\"", "“내 자그마한 계획도 죄다 그분한테 말해 버렸겠지?”"]], [["\"Well, it came out somehow--at least the most of it.\"", "“뭐, 어쩌다 보니 나와 버렸어. 적어도 그 대부분은 말이야.”"]], [["\"There's an end of that alliance,\"", "“그 혼담도 이걸로 끝이네.”"], ["said Charlotte, \"but it doesn't matter much.", "샬럿이 말했다. “하지만 그리 대수로울 것도 없지."], ["I suppose we shall all be back at Como soon.\"", "우리 모두 머잖아 코메 호수로 돌아가게 될 테니까.”"]], [["\"I am sure I hope so,\"", "“정말이지 나도 그러기를 바라.”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."], ["\"I'm sick of the sight of black coats.", "“검은 상의는 이제 보기만 해도 신물이 나."], ["If that Mr. Slope comes here any more, he'll be the death of me.\"", "그 슬로프 씨가 여기 한 번만 더 오면 나는 그이 때문에 죽고 말 거야.”"]], [["\"You've been the ruin of him, I think,\"", "“내가 보기엔 네가 그이를 망쳐 놓았지 싶은데.”"], ["said Charlotte.", "샬럿이 말했다."]], [["\"And as for a second black-coated lover of mine, I am going to make a present of him to another lady with most singular disinterestedness.\"", "“그리고 검은 상의를 입은 내 두 번째 연인으로 말하자면, 나는 그이를 지극히 유별난 무욕으로 다른 부인께 선물해 드릴 참이야.”"]], [["The next day, true to his promise, Bertie packed up and went off by the 4.30 P.M.", "이튿날 버티는 약속대로 짐을 꾸려 오후 4시 30분 기차로 떠났다."], ["train, with £20 in his pocket, bound for the marble quarries of Carrara.", "주머니에 20파운드를 넣고 카라라의 대리석 채석장을 향해서였다."], ["And so he disappears from our scene.", "그리하여 그는 우리 무대에서 사라진다."]], [["At twelve o'clock on the day following that on which Bertie went, Mrs. Bold, true also to her word, knocked at Dr.", "버티가 떠난 다음 날 열두 시에, 볼드 부인 역시 자기 말대로 겁먹은 손과 두근거리는 가슴으로 스탠호프 박사 댁 문을 두드렸다."], ["Stanhope's door with a timid hand and palpitating heart. She was at once shown up to the back drawing-room, the folding doors of which were closed, so that in visiting the signora Eleanor was not necessarily thrown into any communion with those in the front room.", "그녀는 곧바로 뒤쪽 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그 접이문이 닫혀 있었으므로 시뇨라를 찾아온 엘리너가 앞쪽 방에 있는 사람들과 굳이 얼굴을 맞대야 할 일은 없었다."], ["As she went up the stairs, she saw none of the family and was so far saved much of the annoyance which she had dreaded.", "계단을 올라가는 동안 그녀는 식구 가운데 아무도 마주치지 않았고, 그만큼 그녀가 두려워하던 곤혹스러움을 많이 면할 수 있었다."]], [["\"This is very kind of you, Mrs.", "“참으로 고마워요, 볼드 부인."], ["Bold; very kind, after what has happened,\"", "그런 일이 있고 나서도 이렇게 와 주시다니 참으로 고마워요.”"], ["said the lady on the sofa with her sweetest smile.", "소파에 누운 부인이 더없이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"You wrote in such a strain that I could not but come to you.\"", "“그런 어조로 편지를 쓰셨으니 제가 오지 않을 수가 없었어요.”"]], [["\"I did, I did; I wanted to force you to see me.\"", "“그랬지요, 그랬어요. 억지로라도 나를 만나시게 하고 싶었으니까요.”"]], [["\"Well, signora, I am here.\"", "“그래서 이렇게 왔어요, 시뇨라.”"]], [["\"How cold you are to me.", "“어쩌면 그렇게 나에게 쌀쌀맞으실까."], ["But I suppose I must put up with that.", "하기야 그건 내가 참아야겠지요."], ["I know you think you have reason to be displeased with us all.", "우리 식구 모두에게 언짢아할 까닭이 있다고 여기시는 걸 나도 알아요."], ["Poor Bertie; if you knew all, you would not be angry with him.\"", "가엾은 버티. 사정을 다 아신다면 그 애한테 화를 내지는 않으실 텐데.”"]], [["\"I am not angry with your brother--not in the least.", "“저는 동생분께 화가 나지 않았어요. 조금도요."], ["But I hope you did not send for me here to talk about him.\"", "하지만 그 이야기를 하시려고 저를 부르신 것은 아니기를 바라요.”"]], [["\"If you are angry with Charlotte, that is worse, for you have no warmer friend in all Barchester.", "“샬럿에게 화가 나셨다면 그것이 더 나쁜 일이에요. 바체스터를 통틀어 그보다 더 따뜻한 벗은 부인께 없으니까요."], ["But I did not send for you to talk about this--pray bring your chair nearer, Mrs.", "하지만 이 이야기를 하려고 부른 건 아니에요. 볼드 부인, 부디 의자를 좀 더 가까이 끌어당겨서 내가 부인을 볼 수 있게 해 주세요."], ["Bold, so that I may look at you. It is so unnatural to see you keeping so far off from me.\"", "그렇게 멀찍이 떨어져 계시는 걸 보고 있자니 어쩐지 부자연스러워요.”"]], [["Eleanor did as she was bid and brought her chair close to the sofa.", "엘리너는 시키는 대로 의자를 소파 가까이 끌어다 놓았다."]], [["\"And now, Mrs.", "“자, 볼드 부인, 이제 나는 어쩌면 부인이 무례하다고 여기실 만한 이야기를 하려고 해요."], ["Bold, I am going to tell you something which you may perhaps think indelicate, but yet I know that I am right in doing so.\"", "그래도 이렇게 하는 것이 옳다는 걸 나는 알고 있어요.”"]], [["Hereupon Mrs. Bold said nothing but felt inclined to shake in her chair.", "이 말에 볼드 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 의자에 앉은 채 몸이 떨릴 것만 같았다."], ["The signora, she knew, was not very particular, and that which to her appeared to be indelicate might to Mrs.", "시뇨라가 그리 가리는 것이 없는 사람임을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["Bold appear to be extremely indecent.", "시뇨라에게 무례한 정도로 보이는 것이 볼드 부인에게는 지극히 상스러운 것으로 보일 수도 있었다."]], [["\"I believe you know Mr. Arabin?\"", "“애러빈 씨를 아시지요?”"]], [["Mrs. Bold would have given the world not to blush, but her blood was not at her own command.", "볼드 부인은 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수만 있다면 온 세상이라도 내주었을 테지만, 피는 그녀의 뜻대로 움직여 주지 않았다."], ["She did blush up to her forehead, and the signora, who had made her sit in a special light in order that she might watch her, saw that she did so.", "그녀는 이마까지 붉어졌고, 그 모습을 살피려고 일부러 빛이 특정하게 드는 자리에 그녀를 앉혀 놓았던 시뇨라는 그것을 놓치지 않고 보았다."]], [["\"Yes, I am acquainted with him. That is, slightly.", "“네, 아는 사이예요."], ["He is an intimate friend of Dr.", "그러니까 조금 아는 정도지요."], ["Grantly, and Dr. Grantly is my brother-in-law.\"", "그분은 그랜틀리 박사님과 절친한 벗이고, 그랜틀리 박사님은 제 형부시니까요.”"]], [["\"Well, if you know Mr. Arabin, I am sure you must like him.", "“그러면, 애러빈 씨를 아신다면 분명 그분을 좋아하실 거예요."], ["I know and like him much.", "나도 그분을 알고, 아주 좋아하고 있어요."], ["Everybody that knows him must like him.\"", "그분을 아는 사람이라면 누구든 좋아하지 않을 수 없지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Bold felt it quite impossible to say anything in reply to this.", "볼드 부인은 여기에 무어라 대답한다는 것이 도무지 불가능하게 느껴졌다."], ["Her blood was rushing about her body she knew not how or why.", "어찌 된 영문인지도 모르는 채 피가 온몸을 내달렸다."], ["She felt as though she were swinging in her chair, and she knew that she was not only red in the face but also almost suffocated with heat.", "그녀는 자기가 의자에 앉은 채 흔들리고 있는 것만 같았고, 얼굴이 붉어졌을 뿐 아니라 열기에 숨이 막힐 지경이라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["However, she sat still and said nothing.", "그러나 그녀는 가만히 앉아 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"How stiff you are with me, Mrs. Bold,\"", "“나에게 어쩌면 그렇게 뻣뻣하실까요, 볼드 부인.”"], ["said the signora; \"and I the while am doing for you all that one woman can do to serve another.\"", "시뇨라가 말했다. “그러는 동안 나는 한 여자가 다른 여자를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일을 모조리 해 드리고 있는데요.”"]], [["A kind of thought came over the widow's mind that perhaps the signora's friendship was real, and that at any rate it could not hurt her; and another kind of thought, a glimmering of a thought, came to her also--that Mr.", "어쩌면 시뇨라의 우정이 진짜일지도 모르고, 어쨌든 그것이 자기에게 해가 될 리는 없으리라는 생각이 미망인의 마음을 스쳤다. 그리고 또 하나의 생각, 어렴풋이 밝아 오는 생각도 함께 떠올랐다."], ["Arabin was too precious to be lost.", "애러빈 씨는 잃어버리기에는 너무나 귀한 사람이라는 생각이었다."], ["She despised the signora, but might she not stoop to conquer?", "그녀는 시뇨라를 경멸했다. 그러나 이기기 위해서 조금 몸을 굽히지 못할 것도 없지 않은가?"], ["It should be but the smallest fraction of a stoop!", "굽히더라도 아주 지극히 조금이면 될 터였다!"]], [["\"I don't want to be stiff,\"", "“뻣뻣하게 굴 생각은 없어요.”"], ["she said, \"but your questions are so very singular.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 물으시는 것이 하도 유별나서요.”"]], [["\"Well, then, I will ask you one more singular still,\"", "“그렇다면 더 유별난 것을 하나 더 물어보지요.”"], ["said Madeline Neroni, raising herself on her elbow and turning her own face full upon her companion's.", "마들린 네로니가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키고 자기 얼굴을 상대의 얼굴 쪽으로 온전히 돌리며 말했다."], ["\"Do you love him, love him with all your heart and soul, with all the love your bosom can feel?", "“그분을 사랑하시나요? 온 마음과 온 영혼을 다해, 가슴이 느낄 수 있는 온 사랑을 다해 그분을 사랑하시나요?"], ["For I can tell you that he loves you, adores you, worships you, thinks of you and nothing else, is now thinking of you as he attempts to write his sermon for next Sunday's preaching.", "그분이 부인을 사랑하고, 흠모하고, 떠받들고, 부인 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않는다고 내가 말씀드릴 수 있으니 하는 말이에요. 다음 주일 설교를 쓰려고 애쓰고 있는 지금 이 순간에도 그분은 부인을 생각하고 있어요."], ["What would I not give to be loved in such a way by such a man, that is, if I were an object fit for any man to love!\"", "그런 남자에게 그런 식으로 사랑받을 수만 있다면 나는 무엇인들 내주지 않겠어요. 하기야 내가 어느 남자에게든 사랑받기에 어울리는 존재이기라도 하다면 말이지만!”"]], [["Mrs. Bold got up from her seat and stood speechless before the woman who was now addressing her in this impassioned way.", "볼드 부인은 자리에서 일어나, 이렇게 격정적인 어조로 자기에게 말을 건네는 여인 앞에 아무 말도 못 한 채 서 있었다."], ["When the signora thus alluded to herself, the widow's heart was softened, and she put her own hand, as though caressingly, on that of her companion, which was resting on the table.", "시뇨라가 그렇게 자기 자신을 빗대어 말하자 미망인의 마음은 누그러졌고, 그녀는 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 상대의 손 위에 쓰다듬듯이 자기 손을 얹었다."], ["The signora grasped it and went on speaking.", "시뇨라는 그 손을 붙들고 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"What I tell you is God's own truth; and it is for you to use it as may be best for your own happiness.", "“내가 하는 말은 하나님의 진리 그대로예요. 그것을 부인 자신의 행복에 가장 이롭도록 쓰는 것은 부인의 몫이고요."], ["But you must not betray me.", "다만 나를 발설해서는 안 돼요."], ["He knows nothing of this.", "그분은 이 일을 전혀 몰라요."], ["He knows nothing of my knowing his inmost heart.", "내가 그분의 속마음을 다 알고 있다는 것도 전혀 모르지요."], ["He is simple as a child in these matters.", "이런 일에서 그분은 어린아이처럼 단순해요."], ["He told me his secret in a thousand ways because he could not dissemble, but he does not dream that he has told it.", "그분은 시치미를 뗄 줄 몰라서 천 가지 방식으로 자기 비밀을 나에게 말해 버렸지만, 자기가 그것을 말했다는 것은 꿈에도 모르고 있어요."], ["You know it now, and I advise you to use it.\"", "이제 부인이 그것을 아셨으니, 그것을 쓰시라고 나는 권해요.”"]], [["Eleanor returned the pressure of the other's hand with an infinitesimal soupçon of a squeeze.", "엘리너는 상대가 쥔 손에, 지극히 미세한 soupçon만큼 힘을 주어 답했다."]], [["\"And remember,\"", "“그리고 기억해 두세요.”"], ["continued the signora, \"he is not like other men.", "시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “그분은 다른 남자들과 달라요."], ["You must not expect him to come to you with vows and oaths and pretty presents, to kneel at your feet, and kiss your shoe-strings.", "그분이 맹세와 서약과 예쁜 선물을 들고 부인께 와서 발치에 무릎 꿇고 신발 끈에 입 맞추리라고 기대해서는 안 돼요."], ["If you want that, there are plenty to do it, but he won't be one of them.\"", "그런 것을 원하신다면 그리해 줄 사람은 얼마든지 있지만, 그분은 그런 사람이 아니에요.”"], ["Eleanor's bosom nearly burst with a sigh, but Madeline, not heeding her, went on.", "엘리너의 가슴은 한숨으로 터질 듯했으나, 마들린은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다."], ["\"With him, yea will stand for yea, and nay for nay.", "“그분에게는 예가 예이고, 아니오가 아니오예요."], ["Though his heart should break for it, the woman who shall reject him once will have rejected him once and for all.", "설령 그 때문에 심장이 부서진다 해도, 한 번 그분을 물리친 여자는 영영 그분을 물리친 것이 되고 말지요."], ["Remember that. And now, Mrs.", "그것을 기억해 두세요."], ["Bold, I will not keep you, for you are fluttered.", "자, 볼드 부인, 마음이 설레실 테니 붙들어 두지는 않겠어요."], ["I partly guess what use you will make of what I have said to you.", "내가 드린 말씀을 부인이 어떻게 쓰실지는 어렴풋이 짐작이 가요."], ["If ever you are a happy wife in that man's house, we shall be far away, but I shall expect you to write me one line to say that you have forgiven the sins of the family.\"", "언젠가 그분의 집에서 행복한 아내가 되신다면, 그때 우리는 멀리 떠나 있겠지만, 이 집안의 죄를 용서했노라고 한 줄만 적어 보내 주시기를 나는 기다리고 있겠어요.”"]], [["Eleanor half-whispered that she would, and then, without uttering another word, crept out of the room and down the stairs, opened the front door for herself without hearing or seeing anyone, and found herself in the close.", "엘리너는 그러겠다고 반쯤 속삭이듯 대답하고는, 더 한마디도 하지 않은 채 방을 빠져나와 계단을 내려갔고, 아무도 보지 못하고 아무 소리도 듣지 못한 채 스스로 현관문을 열고 나와 어느새 대성당 경내에 서 있었다."]], [["It would be difficult to analyse Eleanor's feelings as she walked home.", "집으로 걸어가는 동안 엘리너의 심정을 헤아려 분석하기란 어려운 일이었을 것이다."], ["She was nearly stupefied by the things that had been said to her.", "그녀는 자기에게 건네진 말들에 거의 넋이 나가 있었다."], ["She felt sore that her heart should have been so searched and riddled by a comparative stranger, by a woman whom she had never liked and never could like.", "자기 마음이 거의 남이나 다름없는 사람에게, 그것도 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없고 앞으로도 좋아할 수 없을 여자에게 그토록 샅샅이 뒤져지고 파헤쳐졌다는 것이 쓰라렸다."], ["She was mortified that the man whom she owned to herself that she loved should have concealed his love from her and shown it to another.", "스스로 사랑한다고 인정하는 그 남자가 자기에게는 사랑을 감추고 다른 여자에게 그것을 내보였다는 것도 분했다."], ["There was much to vex her proud spirit.", "그녀의 자존심을 상하게 할 것이 많았다."], ["But there was, nevertheless, an under stratum of joy in all this which buoyed her up wondrously.", "그러나 그럼에도 그 모든 것 밑에는 기쁨의 지층이 하나 깔려 있어서 그녀를 신기하리만큼 떠받쳐 주었다."], ["She tried if she could disbelieve what Madame Neroni had said to her, but she found that she could not.", "그녀는 마담 네로니가 한 말을 믿지 않을 수 있을지 시험해 보았으나, 그럴 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 그것은 사실이었다."], ["It was true; it must be true.", "사실일 수밖에 없었다."], ["She could not, would not, did not doubt it.", "그녀는 그것을 의심할 수 없었고, 의심하려 하지도 않았고, 의심하지도 않았다."]], [["On one point she fully resolved to follow the advice given her.", "한 가지 점에서 그녀는 자기에게 주어진 충고를 그대로 따르기로 온전히 결심했다."], ["If it should ever please Mr. Arabin to put such a question to her as that suggested, her \"yea\"", "만일 언젠가 애러빈 씨가 그 암시된 것과 같은 물음을 그녀에게 던져 주기를 마음먹는다면, 그녀의 “예”는 “예”가 될 터였다."], ["should be \"yea.\" Would not all her miseries be at an end if she could talk of them to him openly, with her head resting on his shoulder?", "그의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 자기의 온갖 괴로움을 그에게 툭 터놓고 이야기할 수 있게 된다면, 그 괴로움이 죄다 끝나 버리지 않겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI. Mr. Slope's Parting Interview with the Signora", "# 제46장. 슬로프 씨가 시뇨라와 마지막으로 마주 앉다"]], [["On the following day the signora was in her pride.", "이튿날 시뇨라는 한껏 우쭐해 있었다."], ["She was dressed in her brightest of morning dresses, and had quite a levée round her couch.", "그녀는 가진 것 가운데 가장 화사한 아침 옷을 차려입고서, 제 소파 둘레에 그야말로 접견회를 벌여 놓고 있었다. 아름답고 화창한 10월의 오후였다."], ["It was a beautifully bright October afternoon; all the gentlemen of the neighbourhood were in Barchester, and those who had the entry of Dr. Stanhope's house were in the signora's back drawing-room.", "인근의 신사라는 신사는 죄다 바체스터에 있었고, 그 가운데 스탠호프 박사 집에 드나들 자격이 있는 이들은 시뇨라의 안쪽 응접실에 있었다."], ["Charlotte and Mrs. Stanhope were in the front room, and such of the lady's squires as could not for the moment get near the centre of attraction had to waste their fragrance on the mother and sister.", "샬럿과 스탠호프 부인은 앞쪽 방에 있었고, 이 부인을 모시는 기사들 가운데 당장은 관심의 중심에 다가가지 못한 이들은 그 어머니와 언니를 상대로 제 향기를 허비해야만 했다."]], [["The first who came and the last to leave was Mr. Arabin.", "가장 먼저 와서 가장 늦게까지 남은 것은 애러빈 씨였다."], ["This was the second visit he had paid to Madame Neroni since he had met her at Ullathorne.", "울라손에서 그녀를 만난 이래 그가 마담 네로니를 찾은 것은 이번이 두 번째였다."], ["He came, he knew not why, to talk about, he knew not what.", "그는 왜인지도 모르는 채 찾아와서, 무엇인지도 모를 것에 관하여 이야기했다."], ["But, in truth, the feelings which now troubled him were new to him, and he could not analyse them.", "그러나 실상 지금 그를 어지럽히는 감정은 그에게 낯선 것이었고, 그는 그것을 헤아려 볼 수가 없었다."], ["It may seem strange that he should thus come dangling about Madame Neroni because he was in love with Mrs.", "볼드 부인을 사랑하기 때문에 마담 네로니의 주위를 이렇게 맴돈다는 것이 이상하게 보일지도 모르나, 그럼에도 그것은 사실이었다."], ["Bold; but it was nevertheless the fact; and though he could not understand why he did so, Madame Neroni understood it well enough.", "그리고 그는 제가 어째서 그러는지 알지 못했으되, 마담 네로니는 그것을 넉넉히 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["She had been gentle and kind to him and had encouraged his staying.", "그녀는 그에게 다정하고 상냥하게 굴며 더 머물기를 부추겼다."], ["Therefore he stayed on.", "그래서 그는 그대로 머물렀다."], ["She pressed his hand when he first greeted her; she made him remain near her and whispered to him little nothings.", "그가 처음 인사를 건넬 때 그녀는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그녀는 그를 제 곁에 붙들어 두고 아무것도 아닌 말들을 속삭였다."], ["And then her eye, brilliant and bright, now mirthful, now melancholy, and invincible in either way!", "그리고 그 눈, 광채가 돌고 빛나는, 어느 때는 즐겁고 어느 때는 우울하며, 어느 쪽이든 당해 낼 도리가 없는 그 눈!"], ["What man with warm feelings, blood unchilled, and a heart not guarded by a triple steel of experience could have withstood those eyes!", "따뜻한 감정을 지니고, 피가 아직 식지 않았으며, 심장이 세 겹 강철 같은 경험으로 지켜지지 않은 사내라면 그 눈을 어찌 견뎌 낼 수 있었겠는가!"], ["The lady, it is true, intended to do him no mortal injury; she merely chose to inhale a slight breath of incense before she handed the casket over to another.", "하기야 이 부인은 그에게 치명적인 상처를 입힐 뜻은 없었다. 그저 보석함을 다른 이에게 넘겨주기 전에 향내를 한 줄기 살짝 들이마시기로 했을 뿐이었다."], ["Whether Mrs. Bold would willingly have spared even so much is another question.", "볼드 부인이 그만한 것이나마 기꺼이 내주었을지는 또 다른 문제다."]], [["And then came Mr. Slope.", "그리고 이어서 슬로프 씨가 왔다."], ["All the world now knew that Mr. Slope was a candidate for the deanery and that he was generally considered to be the favourite.", "이제 온 세상이 슬로프 씨가 수석 사제 자리의 후보이며 대체로 가장 유력한 사람으로 여겨진다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Mr. Slope, therefore, walked rather largely upon the earth.", "그리하여 슬로프 씨는 땅을 제법 넓게 밟고 다녔다."], ["He gave to himself a portly air, such as might become a dean, spoke but little to other clergymen, and shunned the bishop as much as possible.", "그는 수석 사제에게나 어울릴 법한 위엄 있는 태도를 스스로에게 부여했고, 다른 성직자들에게는 말을 별로 걸지 않았으며, 주교는 될 수 있는 대로 피했다."], ["How the meagre little prebendary, and the burly chancellor, and all the minor canons and vicars choral, ay, and all the choristers, too, cowered and shook and walked about with long faces when they read or heard of that article in \"The Jupiter.\"", "『주피터』의 그 기사를 읽거나 전해 들었을 때, 그 빼빼 마른 자그마한 참사회원과 우람한 학무 참사원과 모든 하급 참사원과 성가대 사제들, 그렇다, 성가대원들까지도 어찌나 움츠러들고 떨며 긴 얼굴을 하고 돌아다녔던지."], ["Now were coming the days when nothing would avail to keep the impure spirit from the cathedral pulpit.", "이제 그 부정한 영을 대성당 설교단에서 막아 낼 길이 아무것도 없는 날들이 오고 있었다."], ["That pulpit would indeed be his own.", "그 설교단은 참으로 그의 것이 될 터였다."], ["Precentors, vicars, and choristers might hang up their harps on the willows.", "선창자도 사제도 성가대원도 제 수금을 버드나무에 걸어 두는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Ichabod!", "이가봇!"], ["Ichabod!", "이가봇!"], ["The glory of their house was departing from them.", "그 집의 영광이 그들에게서 떠나고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Slope, great as he was with embryo grandeur, still came to see the signora.", "슬로프 씨는 아직 껍질을 벗지 않은 위엄으로 그토록 대단해졌으면서도 여전히 시뇨라를 보러 왔다."], ["Indeed, he could not keep himself away.", "아니, 그는 스스로를 떼어 놓을 수가 없었다."], ["He dreamed of that soft hand which he had kissed so often, and of that imperial brow which his lips had once pressed; and he then dreamed also of further favours.", "그는 그토록 자주 입 맞추었던 그 부드러운 손을 꿈꾸었고, 제 입술이 한 번 눌러 보았던 그 제왕 같은 이마를 꿈꾸었으며, 그러고는 더 나아간 은총까지도 꿈꾸었다."]], [["And Mr. Thorne was there also.", "그리고 손 씨 또한 그 자리에 있었다."], ["It was the first visit he had ever paid to the signora, and he made it not without due preparation.", "그가 시뇨라를 찾은 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 그는 마땅한 채비 없이 그 걸음을 하지는 않았다."], ["Mr. Thorne was a gentleman usually precise in his dress and prone to make the most of himself in an unpretending way.", "손 씨는 평소에도 옷차림이 꼼꼼하고, 나서지 않는 방식으로 제 몸을 최대한 돋보이게 하는 데 마음을 쓰는 신사였다."], ["The grey hairs in his whiskers were eliminated perhaps once a month; those on his head were softened by a mixture which we will not call a dye--it was only a wash.", "구레나룻의 센 털은 아마도 한 달에 한 번쯤 뽑혀 나갔고, 머리의 센 털은 우리가 염색약이라고는 부르지 않을 어떤 혼합물로 누그러뜨렸으니 — 그것은 그저 물약이었을 뿐이다."], ["His tailor lived in St. James's Street, and his bootmaker at the corner of that street and Piccadilly.", "그의 재봉사는 세인트 제임스 스트리트에 살았고, 구두장이는 그 거리와 피커딜리가 만나는 모퉁이에 살았다."], ["He was particular in the article of gloves, and the getting up of his shirts was a matter not lightly thought of in the Ullathorne laundry.", "그는 장갑이라는 품목에 까다로웠고, 그의 셔츠를 매만지는 일은 울라손의 세탁실에서 가볍게 여겨지는 일이 아니었다."], ["On the occasion of the present visit he had rather overdone his usual efforts, and caused some little uneasiness to his sister, who had not hitherto received very cordially the proposition for a lengthened visit from the signora at Ullathorne.", "이번 방문에 즈음하여 그는 평소의 공을 다소 지나치게 들였고, 그 바람에 누이가 적잖이 불안해졌으니, 누이는 시뇨라를 울라손에 오래 묵게 하자는 제안을 여태 그다지 살갑게 받아들이지 않고 있던 터였다."]], [["There were others also there--young men about the city who had not much to do and who were induced by the lady's charms to neglect that little--but all gave way to Mr. Thorne, who was somewhat of a grand signor, as a country gentleman always is in a provincial city.", "그 자리에는 다른 이들도 있었다 — 딱히 할 일이 많지 않은 데다 이 부인의 매력에 이끌려 그 얼마 안 되는 일마저 팽개친 시내의 젊은이들이었다 — 그러나 모두가 손 씨에게 자리를 내주었으니, 그는 지방 도시에서 시골 신사가 늘 그러하듯 일종의 대감 같은 인물이었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Thorne, this is so kind of you!\"", "“어머, 손 씨, 이렇게 와 주시다니 참으로 고마우셔라!”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."], ["\"You promised to come, but I really did not expect it.", "“오시겠다고 약속은 하셨지만 정말로 기대하지는 않았답니다."], ["I thought you country gentlemen never kept your pledges.\"", "시골 신사분들은 약속이란 걸 지키는 법이 없다고 여겼거든요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, sometimes,\" said Mr.", "“아, 그야 더러는 지키지요.”"], ["Thorne, looking rather sheepish and making his salutations a little too much in the style of the last century.", "손 씨가 다소 수줍은 낯빛으로, 인사하는 몸짓을 지난 세기의 투로 조금 지나치게 차리며 말했다."]], [["\"You deceive none but your consti--stit--stit--what do you call the people that carry you about in chairs and pelt you with eggs and apples when they make you a member of Parliament?\"", "“속이시는 건 오직 당신네 유권—권—권—, 아니, 당신을 의회 의원으로 만들어 줄 때 의자에 태워 메고 다니며 달걀이며 사과를 던지는 그 사람들, 그 사람들을 뭐라고 부르던가요? 그 사람들뿐이겠지요.”"]], [["\"One another also, sometimes, signora,\"", "“저희끼리도 더러 속이지요, 시뇨라.”"], ["said Mr. Slope, with a very deanish sort of smirk on his face.", "슬로프 씨가 자못 수석 사제다운 히죽거림을 얼굴에 띠고 말했다."], ["\"Country gentlemen do deceive one another sometimes, don't they, Mr. Thorne?\"", "“시골 신사들은 저희끼리도 더러 속이지 않습니까, 손 씨?”"]], [["Mr. Thorne gave him a look which undeaned him completely for the moment, but he soon remembered his high hopes and, recovering himself quickly, sustained his probable coming dignity by a laugh at Mr.", "손 씨는 그에게 눈길을 한 번 던졌는데, 그 눈길은 잠시나마 그에게서 수석 사제다움을 깡그리 벗겨 버렸다."], ["Thorne's expense.", "그러나 그는 곧 제 드높은 희망을 떠올렸고, 얼른 정신을 추슬러 손 씨를 비웃는 웃음으로 머지않아 다가올 제 위엄을 떠받쳤다."]], [["\"I never deceive a lady, at any rate,\" said Mr.", "“저는 어쨌거나 숙녀를 속이는 일은 없습니다.”"], ["Thorne, \"especially when the gratification of my own wishes is so strong an inducement to keep me true, as it now is.\"", "손 씨가 말했다. “더욱이 제 소원을 이루는 것이 지금처럼 저를 진실하게 붙들어 두는 강한 유인이 될 때에는 말이지요.”"]], [["Mr. Thorne went on thus awhile with antediluvian grimaces and compliments which he had picked up from Sir Charles Grandison, and the signora at every grimace and at every bow smiled a little smile and bowed a little bow.", "손 씨는 한동안 그런 식으로 『찰스 그랜디슨 경』에서 주워 온 태곳적 표정과 인사말을 이어 갔고, 시뇨라는 그 표정 하나마다 그 절 하나마다 작은 미소를 짓고 작은 목례를 하였다."], ["Mr. Thorne, however, was kept standing at the foot of the couch, for the new dean sat in the seat of honour near the table.", "그러나 손 씨는 소파 발치에 선 채로 있어야 했으니, 탁자 곁의 상석에는 새 수석 사제가 앉아 있었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Arabin the while was standing with his back to the fire, his coat-tails under his arms, gazing at her with all his eyes--not quite in vain, for every now and again a glance came up at him, bright as a meteor out of heaven.", "그동안 애러빈 씨는 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 연미복 자락을 팔 밑에 끼운 채 온 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었는데 — 아주 헛된 일만은 아니었으니, 이따금 하늘에서 떨어지는 유성처럼 밝은 눈길 하나가 그에게로 올라왔기 때문이다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Thorne, you promised to let me introduce my little girl to you.", "“참, 손 씨, 제 어린 딸아이를 소개해 드리게 해 주마고 약속하셨지요."], ["Can you spare a moment--will you see her now?\"", "잠깐 짬을 내 주시겠어요 — 지금 그 아이를 보시겠어요?”"]], [["Mr. Thorne assured her that he could and would see the young lady with the greatest pleasure in life. \"Mr. Slope, might I trouble you to ring the bell?\" said she, and when Mr. Slope got up, she looked at Mr. Thorne and pointed to the chair. Mr. Thorne, however, was much too slow to understand her, and Mr. Slope would have recovered his seat had not the signora, who never chose to be unsuccessful, somewhat summarily ordered him out of it.", "손 씨는 세상에 더없는 기쁨으로 그 어린 아가씨를 볼 수 있고 또 보겠노라고 다짐했다. “슬로프 씨, 수고스럽지만 종을 좀 울려 주시겠어요?” 그녀가 말했고, 슬로프 씨가 일어서자 그녀는 손 씨를 바라보며 그 의자를 가리켰다. 그러나 손 씨는 그 뜻을 알아채기에는 너무나 굼떴고, 결코 실패하기를 택하는 법이 없는 시뇨라가 다소 대뜸 그를 그 자리에서 내보내지 않았더라면 슬로프 씨는 제 자리를 도로 차지했을 것이었다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Slope, I must ask you to let Mr.", "“아, 슬로프 씨, 잠깐만, 아주 잠깐만 손 씨더러 여기 앉으시라고 해야겠어요."], ["Thorne sit here just for a moment or two.", "너그러이 봐주시리라 믿어요."], ["I am sure you will pardon me. We can take a liberty with you this week.", "이번 주까지는 당신께 이만한 무례를 부려도 되겠지요."], ["Next week, you know, when you move into the dean's house, we shall all be afraid of you.\"", "다음 주에, 아시다시피 수석사제관으로 옮겨 가시고 나면 우리 모두 당신을 두려워하게 될 테니까요.”"]], [["Mr. Slope, with an air of much indifference, rose from his seat and, walking into the next room, became greatly interested in Mrs. Stanhope's worsted work.", "슬로프 씨는 자못 무심한 태도로 자리에서 일어나 옆방으로 걸어가더니 스탠호프 부인의 털실 자수에 크나큰 관심을 보이기 시작했다."]], [["And then the child was brought in.", "그러고 나서 아이가 들려 들어왔다."], ["She was a little girl, about eight years of age, like her mother, only that her enormous eyes were black, and her hair quite jet.", "여덟 살쯤 된 계집아이로, 제 어미를 닮았으되 그 커다란 눈은 검고 머리카락은 아주 새까맸다."], ["Her complexion, too, was very dark and bespoke her foreign blood.", "살빛 또한 매우 짙어 이국의 피를 드러냈다."], ["She was dressed in the most outlandish and extravagant way in which clothes could be put on a child's back.", "아이는 어린아이의 몸에 옷을 걸칠 수 있는 방식 가운데 가장 괴상하고 가장 야단스러운 차림을 하고 있었다."], ["She had great bracelets on her naked little arms, a crimson fillet braided with gold round her head, and scarlet shoes with high heels.", "맨살이 드러난 자그마한 팔에는 커다란 팔찌를 끼웠고, 머리에는 금실을 섞어 땋은 진홍색 머리띠를 둘렀으며, 굽 높은 다홍색 신을 신었다."], ["Her dress was all flounces and stuck out from her as though the object were to make it lie off horizontally from her little hips.", "옷은 온통 주름 장식이어서, 마치 그 작은 엉덩이에서 옷자락이 수평으로 뻗어 나가게 하는 것이 목적이기라도 한 양 몸에서 툭 불거져 있었다."], ["It did not nearly cover her knees, but this was atoned for by a loose pair of drawers, which seemed made throughout of lace; then she had on pink silk stockings.", "옷은 무릎을 채 덮지 못했으나, 그 대신 온통 레이스로만 지은 듯한 헐렁한 속바지가 그것을 메워 주었다. 게다가 아이는 분홍빛 비단 양말을 신고 있었다."], ["It was thus that the last of the Neros was habitually dressed at the hour when visitors were wont to call.", "네로 가문의 마지막 후손은 손님이 찾아오기 마련인 시각이면 늘 이렇게 차려입혀졌다."]], [["\"Julia, my love,\"", "“줄리아, 아가.”"], ["said the mother--Julia was ever a favourite name with the ladies of that family.", "어미가 말했다 — 줄리아는 그 집안 여자들에게 예로부터 늘 사랑받아 온 이름이었다."], ["\"Julia, my love, come here.", "“줄리아, 아가, 이리 오렴."], ["I was telling you about the beautiful party poor Mamma went to.", "가엾은 엄마가 갔던 아름다운 잔치 이야기를 해 주고 있었지."], ["This is Mr.", "이분이 손 씨란다."], ["Thorne; will you give him a kiss, dearest?\"", "아가야, 이분께 입 맞춰 드리겠니?”"]], [["Julia put up her face to be kissed, as she did to all her mother's visitors, and then Mr. Thorne found that he had got her and, what was much more terrific to him, all her finery, into his arms.", "줄리아는 제 어미의 손님 모두에게 그리하듯 얼굴을 들어 입맞춤을 받았고, 그러자 손 씨는 아이를, 그리고 그에게 훨씬 더 끔찍하게도 그 아이의 온갖 치장을 제 팔에 안게 되었음을 깨달았다."], ["The lace and starch crumpled against his waistcoat and trousers, the greasy black curls hung upon his cheek, and one of the bracelet clasps scratched his ear.", "레이스와 풀 먹인 천이 그의 조끼와 바지에 눌려 구겨졌고, 기름진 검은 곱슬머리가 그의 뺨에 늘어졌으며, 팔찌 고리 하나가 그의 귀를 긁었다."], ["He did not at all know how to hold so magnificent a lady, nor holding her what to do with her.", "그는 그토록 대단한 숙녀를 어떻게 안아야 할지도, 안은 다음 그 숙녀를 어찌해야 할지도 도무지 알지 못했다."], ["However, he had on other occasions been compelled to fondle little nieces and nephews, and now set about the task in the mode he always had used.", "그러나 그도 다른 자리에서 어린 조카들을 어르지 않을 수 없었던 적이 있었으므로, 이제 늘 쓰던 방식으로 그 일에 착수했다."]], [["\"Diddle, diddle, diddle, diddle,\"", "“디들, 디들, 디들, 디들.”"], ["said he, putting the child on one knee and working away with it as though he were turning a knife-grinder's wheel with his foot.", "그가 아이를 한쪽 무릎에 앉히고, 마치 칼갈이의 숫돌 바퀴를 발로 돌리기라도 하듯 아이를 흔들어 대며 말했다."]], [["\"Mamma, Mamma,\"", "“엄마, 엄마.”"], ["said Julia crossly, \"I don't want to be diddle diddled.", "줄리아가 짜증스레 말했다. “나 디들디들 당하기 싫어."], ["Let me go, you naughty old man, you.\"", "놔줘요, 이 못된 늙은 아저씨.”"]], [["Poor Mr. Thorne put the child down quietly on the ground and drew back his chair; Mr.", "가엾은 손 씨는 아이를 조용히 바닥에 내려놓고 제 의자를 뒤로 물렸다."], ["Slope, who had returned to the pole star that attracted him, laughed aloud; Mr.", "저를 끌어당기는 북극성 곁으로 돌아와 있던 슬로프 씨는 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["Arabin winced and shut his eyes; and the signora pretended not to hear her daughter.", "애러빈 씨는 움찔하며 눈을 감았다. 그리고 시뇨라는 제 딸의 말을 못 들은 척했다."]], [["\"Go to Aunt Charlotte, lovey,\"", "“샬럿 이모께 가 보렴, 아가.”"], ["said the mamma, \"and ask her if it is not time for you to go out.\"", "어미가 말했다. “이모께 이제 나갈 시간이 되지 않았는지 여쭈어 보고.”"]], [["But little Miss Julia, though she had not exactly liked the nature of Mr. Thorne's attention, was accustomed to be played with by gentlemen, and did not relish the idea of being sent so soon to her aunt.", "그러나 어린 줄리아 양은 손 씨가 보인 관심의 성격이 딱히 마음에 들지는 않았으되 신사들에게 놀림받는 데 익숙한 터라, 이렇게 빨리 이모에게 보내진다는 생각이 달갑지 않았다."]], [["\"Julia, go when I tell you, my dear.\"", "“줄리아, 가라고 하면 가야지, 아가.”"], ["But Julia still went pouting about the room.", "그래도 줄리아는 여전히 입을 삐죽인 채 방 안을 돌아다녔다."], ["\"Charlotte, do come and take her,\"", "“샬럿, 와서 이 아이 좀 데려가.”"], ["said the signora.", "시뇨라가 말했다."], ["\"She must go out, and the days get so short now.\"", "“나가야 하는데 요새는 해가 어찌나 짧은지.”"], ["And thus ended the much-talked-of interview between Mr. Thorne and the last of the Neros.", "그리하여 그토록 말이 많던 손 씨와 네로 가문의 마지막 후손 사이의 면담은 끝이 났다."]], [["Mr. Thorne recovered from the child's crossness sooner than from Mr.", "손 씨는 아이의 짜증에서보다 슬로프 씨의 웃음에서 더 늦게 회복되었다."], ["Slope's laughter. He could put up with being called an old man by an infant, but he did not like to be laughed at by the bishop's chaplain, even though that chaplain was about to become a dean.", "그는 젖먹이에게 늙은이 소리를 듣는 것쯤은 참을 수 있었으나, 주교의 전속 사제에게 비웃음을 사는 것은, 설령 그 전속 사제가 곧 수석 사제가 될 참이라 하더라도 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["He said nothing, but he showed plainly enough that he was angry.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 화가 났다는 것만은 넉넉히 드러내 보였다."]], [["The signora was ready enough to avenge him.", "시뇨라는 기꺼이 그의 원수를 갚아 줄 참이었다."], ["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨.”"], ["said she, \"I hear that you are triumphing on all sides.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “당신이 사방에서 개가를 올리고 계시다고 들었어요.”"]], [["\"How so?\" said he, smiling.", "“어째서 그런 말씀을?”"], ["He did not dislike being talked to about the deanery, though, of course, he strongly denied the imputation.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그는 수석 사제직에 관한 이야기를 듣는 것이 싫지 않았으나, 물론 그런 혐의는 강하게 부인했다."]], [["\"You carry the day both in love and war.\" Mr.", "“사랑에서도 전쟁에서도 이기고 계시잖아요.”"], ["Slope hereupon did not look quite so satisfied as he had done.", "이 말에 슬로프 씨는 아까만큼 흡족한 낯빛은 아니게 되었다."]], [["\"Mr. Arabin,\"", "“애러빈 씨.”"], ["continued the signora, \"don't you think Mr.", "시뇨라가 말을 이었다."], ["Slope is a very lucky man?\"", "“슬로프 씨는 참으로 운 좋은 분이라고 생각지 않으세요?”"]], [["\"Not more so than he deserves, I am sure,\"", "“그가 마땅히 누릴 만한 것 이상은 아니라고 나는 믿소.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Only think, Mr.", "“생각 좀 해 보세요, 손 씨."], ["Thorne, he is to be our new dean; of course we all know that.\"", "이분이 우리의 새 수석 사제가 되신답니다. 물론 우리야 다들 아는 일이지만요.”"]], [["\"Indeed, signora,\"", "“원, 시뇨라.”"], ["said Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Slope, \"we all know nothing about it.", "“우리 모두 그런 일은 전혀 알지 못합니다."], ["I can assure you I myself--\"", "저 자신도 정말이지 장담하건대—”"]], [["\"He is to be the new dean--there is no manner of doubt of it, Mr. Thorne.\"", "“이분이 새 수석 사제가 되실 겁니다 — 그 점은 조금도 의심할 여지가 없어요, 손 씨.”"]], [["\"Hum!\"", "“흠!”"], ["said Mr. Thorne.", "손 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Passing over the heads of old men like my father and Archdeacon Grantly--\"", "“제 아버지나 그랜틀리 부주교 같은 늙은 어른들의 머리 위를 훌쩍 넘어서 말이지요—”"]], [["\"Oh--oh!\"", "“원, 원!”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"The archdeacon would not accept it,\" said Mr.", "“부주교라면 그 자리를 받지 않을 것이오.”"], ["Arabin, whereupon Mr. Slope smiled abominably and said, as plainly as a look could speak, that the grapes were sour.", "애러빈 씨가 말했고, 그러자 슬로프 씨는 밉살스럽게 미소 지으며, 눈빛만으로 말할 수 있는 한 또렷하게 저 포도는 실 것이라고 말했다."]], [["\"Going over all our heads,\"", "“우리 모두의 머리 위를 넘어서 말이에요.”"], ["continued the signora, \"for of course I consider myself one of the chapter.\"", "시뇨라가 말을 이었다. “저야 물론 스스로를 참사회의 한 사람으로 여기고 있으니까요.”"]], [["\"If I am ever dean,\" said Mr.", "“제가 언젠가 수석 사제가 된다면,”"], ["Slope, \"that is, were I ever to become so, I should glory in such a canoness.\"", "슬로프 씨가 말했다. “아니, 설령 제가 그리된다 한들, 저는 그런 여참사원을 모시는 것을 영광으로 알겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Slope, stop; I haven't half done.", "“어머, 슬로프 씨, 잠깐만요."], ["There is another canoness for you to glory in.", "아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았답니다. 당신이 영광으로 알 여참사원이 하나 더 있으니까요."], ["Mr. Slope is not only to have the deanery but a wife to put in it.\"", "슬로프 씨는 수석사제관만 얻는 것이 아니라 그 안에 들여앉힐 아내까지 얻으신답니다.”"]], [["Mr. Slope again looked disconcerted.", "슬로프 씨는 또다시 당황한 낯빛이었다."]], [["\"A wife with a large fortune, too.", "“게다가 큰 재산을 지닌 아내지요."], ["It never rains but it pours, does it, Mr. Thorne?\"", "비가 왔다 하면 퍼붓는 법이지요, 그렇지 않나요, 손 씨?”"]], [["\"No, never,\" said Mr.", "“예, 결코 그러는 법이 없지요.”"], ["Thorne, who did not quite relish talking about Mr. Slope and his affairs.", "손 씨가 말했으나, 그는 슬로프 씨와 그 신상에 관해 이야기하는 것이 그다지 달갑지 않았다."]], [["\"When will it be, Mr. Slope?\"", "“그게 언제쯤이 될까요, 슬로프 씨?”"]], [["\"When will what be?\"", "“무엇이 언제쯤이라는 말씀입니까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, we know when the affair of the dean will be: a week will settle that.", "“아, 수석 사제 일이야 언제일지 우리도 알지요. 일주일이면 결판이 날 테고요."], ["The new hat, I have no doubt, has been already ordered.", "새 모자도 벌써 맞추어 놓으셨을 테지요, 그건 의심할 것도 없어요."], ["But when will the marriage come off?\"", "그런데 혼인은 언제 치르시나요?”"]], [["\"Do you mean mine or Mr. Arabin's?\"", "“제 것 말씀입니까, 아니면 애러빈 씨 것 말씀입니까?”"], ["said he, striving to be facetious.", "그가 익살을 부려 보려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, just then I meant yours, though, perhaps, after all, Mr. Arabin's may be first.", "“글쎄요, 방금은 당신 것을 두고 한 말이었지만, 어쩌면 결국 애러빈 씨 쪽이 먼저일지도 모르지요."], ["But we know nothing of him. He is too close for any of us.", "그렇지만 우리는 저분에 대해서는 아무것도 모른답니다."], ["Now all is open and above board with you--which, by the by, Mr.", "저분은 우리 누구에게도 너무 입이 무거우세요."], ["Arabin, I beg to tell you I like much the best. He who runs can read that Mr.", "그런데 당신은 모든 것이 훤히 드러나 있고 떳떳하지요 — 그런데 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 애러빈 씨, 나는 그편이 훨씬 낫다고 말해 두고 싶소."], ["Slope is a favoured lover. Come, Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 사랑받는 구혼자라는 것쯤은 달려가면서도 읽을 수 있는 일이지요."], ["Slope, when is the widow to be made Mrs. Dean?\"", "자, 슬로프 씨, 그 미망인은 언제 수석 사제 부인이 되나요?”"]], [["To Mr. Arabin this badinage was peculiarly painful, and yet he could not tear himself away and leave it.", "애러빈 씨에게 이 희롱은 유독 괴로웠고, 그러면서도 그는 몸을 떼어 그 자리를 떠날 수가 없었다."], ["He believed, still believed with that sort of belief which the fear of a thing engenders, that Mrs. Bold would probably become the wife of Mr.", "그는 여전히, 무언가를 두려워하는 마음이 낳는 그런 종류의 믿음으로, 볼드 부인이 아마도 슬로프 씨의 아내가 되리라고 믿고 있었다."], ["Slope. Of Mr.", "정원에서 있었던 슬로프 씨의 자그마한 사건에 대해서 그는 아무것도 알지 못했다."], ["Slope's little adventure in the garden he knew nothing. For aught he knew, Mr.", "그가 아는 한 슬로프 씨는 전혀 다른 성격의 일을 겪었을 수도 있었다."], ["Slope might have had an adventure of quite a different character. He might have thrown himself at the widow's feet, been accepted, and then returned to town a jolly, thriving wooer.", "그는 미망인의 발치에 몸을 던져 승낙을 얻고, 그런 뒤에 흥겹고 잘나가는 구혼자가 되어 시내로 돌아왔을 수도 있었다."], ["The signora's jokes were bitter enough to Mr. Slope, but they were quite as bitter to Mr.", "시뇨라의 농담은 슬로프 씨에게 충분히 쓰라렸으나, 애러빈 씨에게도 꼭 그만큼 쓰라렸다."], ["Arabin. He still stood leaning against the fire-place, fumbling with his hands in his trousers pockets.", "그는 여전히 벽난로에 기대선 채 바지 주머니에 손을 넣고 만지작거리고 있었다."]], [["\"Come, come, Mr. Slope, don't be so bashful,\"", "“자, 자, 슬로프 씨, 그렇게 수줍어하지 마세요.”"], ["continued the signora.", "시뇨라가 말을 이었다."], ["\"We all know that you proposed to the lady the other day at Ullathorne.", "“요전 날 울라손에서 당신이 그 부인께 청혼하셨다는 것쯤은 우리 모두 알고 있답니다."], ["Tell us with what words she accepted you.", "그분이 어떤 말로 승낙하셨는지 들려주세요."], ["Was it with a simple 'yes,' or with the two 'no no's'", "그저 ‘네’ 한마디였나요, 아니면 둘이 모여 긍정이 되는 그 ‘아니요, 아니요’였나요?"], ["which make an affirmative? Or did silence give consent?", "아니면 침묵이 승낙을 대신했나요?"], ["Or did she speak out with that spirit which so well becomes a widow and say openly, 'By my troth, sir, you shall make me Mrs. Slope as soon as it is your pleasure to do so.'\"", "아니면 미망인에게 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 기개로 대놓고 말씀하시기를, ‘맹세코, 선생님, 당신이 원하시는 때가 되면 언제든 저를 슬로프 부인으로 만들어 주세요.’ 하셨나요?”"]], [["Mr. Slope had seldom in his life felt himself less at his ease.", "슬로프 씨는 평생 그때만큼 마음이 편치 않았던 적이 별로 없었다."], ["There sat Mr. Thorne, laughing silently.", "저기에는 손 씨가 앉아 소리 없이 웃고 있었다."], ["There stood his old antagonist, Mr. Arabin, gazing at him with all his eyes.", "저기에는 그의 오랜 적수 애러빈 씨가 서서 온 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다."], ["There round the door between the two rooms were clustered a little group of people, including Miss Stanhope and the Revs. Messrs.", "두 방 사이의 문간 언저리에는 스탠호프 양과 그레이 목사와 그린 목사를 비롯한 사람들이 작은 무리를 이루어 모여, 하나같이 그가 낭패 보는 꼴에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Grey and Green, all listening to his discomfiture. He knew that it depended solely on his own wit whether or no he could throw the joke back upon the lady.", "그는 이 농담을 저 부인에게 되던질 수 있느냐 없느냐가 오로지 제 재치에 달려 있음을 알았다."], ["He knew that it stood him to do so if he possibly could, but he had not a word.", "될 수만 있다면 그리하는 편이 제게 이롭다는 것도 알았다. 그러나 그에게는 말 한마디가 없었다."], ["\"'Tis conscience that makes cowards of us all.\"", "“양심이라는 것이 우리 모두를 겁쟁이로 만드는구나.”"], ["He felt on his cheek the sharp points of Eleanor's fingers, and did not know who might have seen the blow, who might have told the tale to this pestilent woman who took such delight in jeering him.", "그는 제 뺨에 닿았던 엘리너의 날카로운 손가락 끝을 느꼈고, 누가 그 매질을 보았을지, 누가 저를 조롱하는 데 그토록 재미를 붙인 이 고약한 여자에게 그 이야기를 전했을지 알 수 없었다."], ["He stood there, therefore, red as a carbuncle and mute as a fish; grinning sufficiently to show his teeth; an object of pity.", "그리하여 그는 거기 홍옥처럼 붉고 물고기처럼 말없이 서서, 이를 드러낼 만큼만 히죽거리는, 가엾다고밖에 할 수 없는 물건이 되어 있었다."]], [["But the signora had no pity; she knew nothing of mercy.", "그러나 시뇨라에게는 연민이 없었다. 그녀는 자비라는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["Her present object was to put Mr. Slope down, and she was determined to do it thoroughly, now that she had him in her power.", "지금 그녀의 목적은 슬로프 씨를 눌러 놓는 것이었고, 이제 그를 제 손아귀에 넣은 마당에 그것을 철저히 해치우기로 작정하고 있었다."]], [["\"What, Mr. Slope, no answer?", "“아니, 슬로프 씨, 대답이 없으세요?"], ["Why it can't possibly be that the woman has been fool enough to refuse you?", "설마 그 여자가 당신을 물리칠 만큼 어리석었을 리는 없잖아요?"], ["She can't surely be looking out after a bishop.", "그분이 설마 주교 자리를 노리고 있는 건 아닐 테고요."], ["But I see how it is, Mr. Slope.", "아, 하지만 어찌 된 일인지 알겠어요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Widows are proverbially cautious.", "미망인은 조심스럽기로 이름난 사람들이지요."], ["You should have let her alone till the new hat was on your head, till you could show her the key of the deanery.\"", "새 모자가 당신 머리에 얹힐 때까지, 수석사제관 열쇠를 그분께 보여 드릴 수 있을 때까지 그분을 가만히 두셨어야 했어요.”"]], [["\"Signora,\"", "“시뇨라.”"], ["said he at last, trying to speak in a tone of dignified reproach, \"you really permit yourself to talk on solemn subjects in a very improper way.\"", "마침내 그가 위엄 있게 나무라는 어조로 말해 보려 애쓰며 말했다. “참으로 엄숙한 주제를 두고 몹시 온당치 못한 방식으로 말씀하고 계십니다.”"]], [["\"Solemn subjects--what solemn subject?", "“엄숙한 주제라니 — 무슨 엄숙한 주제 말인가요?"], ["Surely a dean's hat is not such a solemn subject.\"", "수석 사제의 모자가 그렇게까지 엄숙한 주제일 리는 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"I have no aspirations such as those you impute to me.", "“저에게는 당신이 저에게 씌우시는 그런 야심 따위는 없습니다."], ["Perhaps you will drop the subject.\"", "그 이야기는 그만두시는 것이 좋겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, certainly, Mr.", "“아, 그러지요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope; but one word first.", "그렇지만 한마디만 먼저 하겠어요."], ["Go to her again with the prime minister's letter in your pocket.", "총리의 편지를 주머니에 넣고 그분께 다시 가 보세요."], ["I'll wager my shawl to your shovel she does not refuse you then.\"", "그때는 그분이 당신을 물리치지 않는다는 데에 제 숄을 걸고 당신 삽 모자와 내기하겠어요.”"]], [["\"I must say, signora, that I think you are speaking of the lady in a very unjustifiable manner.\"", "“시뇨라, 제가 말씀드려야겠습니다만, 당신은 그 부인에 관해 참으로 변명의 여지가 없는 방식으로 말씀하고 계십니다.”"]], [["\"And one other piece of advice, Mr.", "“그리고 한 가지 더 충고를 드리지요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope; I'll only offer you one other;\"", "딱 한 가지만 더 드리겠어요.”"], ["and then she commenced singing--", "그러고서 그녀는 노래를 부르기 시작했다 —"]], [["\"It's gude to be merry and wise, Mr.", "“유쾌하고 슬기롭게 지내는 것이 좋지요, 슬로프 씨."], ["Slope; It's gude to be honest and true; It's gude to be off with the old love--Mr.", "정직하고 참되게 지내는 것이 좋지요."], ["Slope, Before you are on with the new.", "새 사랑과 맺어지기 전에, 슬로프 씨, 옛 사랑과는 헤어져 두는 것이 좋지요.”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하!”"]], [["And the signora, throwing herself back on her sofa, laughed merrily.", "그리고 시뇨라는 소파에 몸을 뒤로 던지며 즐겁게 웃었다."], ["She little recked how those who heard her would, in their own imaginations, fill up the little history of Mr. Slope's first love.", "제 말을 들은 이들이 저마다의 상상 속에서 슬로프 씨의 첫사랑에 관한 자그마한 내력을 어떻게 채워 넣을지 그녀는 조금도 헤아리지 않았다."], ["She little cared that some among them might attribute to her the honour of his earlier admiration. She was tired of Mr.", "그들 가운데 몇몇이 그가 더 일찍 흠모했던 상대라는 영예를 제게 돌릴지도 모른다는 것 또한 그녀는 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["Slope and wanted to get rid of him; she had ground for anger with him, and she chose to be revenged.", "그녀는 슬로프 씨에게 싫증이 났고 그를 떨쳐 버리고 싶었다. 그녀에게는 그에게 화를 낼 만한 까닭이 있었고, 그리하여 그녀는 앙갚음하기를 택한 것이었다."]], [["How Mr. Slope got out of that room he never himself knew.", "슬로프 씨가 어떻게 그 방을 빠져나왔는지는 그 자신도 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["He did succeed ultimately, and probably with some assistance, in getting his hat and escaping into the air.", "그는 마침내, 아마도 누군가의 도움을 받아 제 모자를 챙겨 바깥 공기 속으로 달아나는 데 성공했다."], ["At last his love for the signora was cured.", "시뇨라를 향한 그의 사랑은 마침내 나았다."], ["Whenever he again thought of her in his dreams, it was not as of an angel with azure wings.", "그 뒤로 꿈속에서 그녀를 다시 떠올릴 때, 그것은 하늘빛 날개를 단 천사의 모습이 아니었다."], ["He connected her rather with fire and brimstone, and though he could still believe her to be a spirit, he banished her entirely out of heaven and found a place for her among the infernal gods.", "그는 차라리 그녀를 불과 유황에 결부시켰고, 여전히 그녀를 하나의 영으로 믿을 수는 있었으되 그녀를 하늘에서 아주 내쫓아 지옥의 신들 사이에 자리를 마련해 주었다."], ["When he weighed in the balance, as he not seldom did, the two women to whom he had attached himself in Barchester, the pre-eminent place in his soul's hatred was usually allotted to the signora.", "바체스터에서 제 마음을 붙였던 두 여자를 저울에 달아 볼 때 — 그는 그런 일을 드물지 않게 했다 — 그의 영혼이 품은 증오에서 으뜸가는 자리는 대개 시뇨라에게 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII. The Dean Elect", "# 제47장. 수석 사제 당선자"]], [["During the entire next week Barchester was ignorant who was to be its new dean. On Sunday morning Mr.", "그 뒤 한 주일 내내 바체스터는 누가 제 도시의 새 수석 사제가 될지 알지 못했다."], ["Slope was decidedly the favourite, but he did not show himself in the cathedral, and then he sank a point or two in the betting.", "일요일 아침만 해도 슬로프 씨가 단연 유력한 후보였으나, 그가 대성당에 얼굴을 내밀지 않자 그의 배당은 한두 점 내려앉았다."], ["On Monday he got a scolding from the bishop in the hearing of the servants, and down he went till nobody would have him at any price; but on Tuesday he received a letter, in an official cover, marked private, by which he fully recovered his place in the public favour.", "월요일에는 하인들이 다 듣는 자리에서 주교에게 꾸지람을 들었고, 그리하여 값을 아무리 낮추어도 그를 사겠다는 사람이 없을 지경까지 떨어졌다. 그러나 화요일에 관용 봉투에 담겨 친전이라 적힌 편지 한 통을 받으면서 그는 세간의 지지를 온전히 되찾았다."], ["On Wednesday he was said to be ill, and that did not look well; but on Thursday morning he went down to the railway station with a very jaunty air; and when it was ascertained that he had taken a first-class ticket for London, there was no longer any room for doubt on the matter.", "수요일에는 그가 앓아누웠다는 말이 돌아 형세가 좋아 보이지 않았으나, 목요일 아침 그가 자못 의기양양한 태도로 철도역에 내려갔고 런던행 일등석 표를 끊었다는 것이 확인되자, 이 문제에는 더 이상 의심이 들어설 자리가 없었다."]], [["While matters were in this state of ferment at Barchester, there was not much mental comfort at Plumstead.", "바체스터가 이렇듯 술렁이고 있는 동안 플럼스테드에서도 마음이 그리 편치는 않았다."], ["Our friend the archdeacon had many grounds for inward grief.", "우리의 벗 부주교에게는 속으로 끙끙 앓을 까닭이 여럿 있었다."], ["He was much displeased at the result of Dr. Gwynne's diplomatic mission to the palace, and did not even scruple to say to his wife that had he gone himself, he would have managed the affair much better.", "그는 그윈 박사가 주교관에 가서 벌인 담판의 결과가 몹시 언짢았고, 자기가 몸소 갔더라면 일을 훨씬 잘 처리했으리라고 아내에게 말하기까지 서슴지 않았다."], ["His wife did not agree with him, but that did not mend the matter.", "아내는 그 말에 동의하지 않았으나, 그렇다고 사정이 나아지는 것도 아니었다."]], [["Mr. Quiverful's appointment to the hospital was, however, a fait accompli, and Mr. Harding's acquiescence in that appointment was not less so. Nothing would induce Mr. Harding to make a public appeal against the bishop, and the Master of Lazarus quite approved of his not doing so.", "그러나 퀴버풀 씨의 요양원 임명은 이미 fait accompli였고, 하딩 씨가 그 임명을 순순히 받아들인 것 또한 그에 못지않게 돌이킬 수 없는 일이었다. 무슨 수를 써도 하딩 씨가 주교를 상대로 공개적인 항의에 나설 리는 없었고, 라자러스 학장 또한 그가 그러지 않는 편이 옳다고 전적으로 여겼다."]], [["\"I don't know what has come to the master,\"", "“학장께 무슨 일이 생긴 것인지 도무지 모르겠소.”"], ["said the archdeacon over and over again.", "부주교는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말했다."], ["\"He used to be ready enough to stand up for his order.\"", "“예전에는 제 신분의 권리를 지키겠다고 얼마든지 나서던 분이었는데.”"]], [["\"My dear Archdeacon,\"", "“부주교님,”"], ["Mrs. Grantly would say in reply, \"what is the use of always fighting?", "그랜틀리 부인은 그럴 때마다 이렇게 대답했다."], ["I really think the master is right.\"", "“늘 싸움만 해서 무슨 소용이 있어요?"], ["The master, however, had taken steps of his own of which neither the archdeacon nor his wife knew anything.", "저는 정말이지 학장님이 옳다고 생각해요.” 그러나 그 학장은 부주교도 그 아내도 전혀 모르는 나름의 조치를 이미 취해 둔 터였다."]], [["Then Mr. Slope's successes were henbane to Dr. Grantly, and Mrs. Bold's improprieties were as bad. What would be all the world to Archdeacon Grantly if Mr. Slope should become Dean of Barchester and marry his wife's sister! He talked of it and talked of it till he was nearly ill. Mrs. Grantly almost wished that the marriage were done and over, so that she might hear no more about it.", "게다가 슬로프 씨가 잇달아 거두는 성공은 그랜틀리 박사에게 독초와도 같았고, 볼드 부인의 온당치 못한 처신도 그에 못지않았다. 슬로프 씨가 바체스터 수석 사제가 되고 게다가 제 아내의 여동생과 혼인까지 한다면, 그랜틀리 부주교에게 이 세상 전부가 다 무슨 소용이겠는가! 그는 그 이야기를 하고 또 하다가 거의 앓아누울 지경이 되었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 차라리 그 혼사가 얼른 치러져 끝나 버려서 더는 그 말을 듣지 않게 되기를 바랄 지경이었다."]], [["And there was yet another ground of misery which cut him to the quick nearly as closely as either of the others. That paragon of a clergyman whom he had bestowed upon St.", "그리고 앞의 두 가지 못지않게 그의 가슴을 후벼 파는 괴로움의 근거가 하나 더 있었다."], ["Ewold's, that college friend of whom he had boasted so loudly, that ecclesiastical knight before whose lance Mr. Slope was to fall and bite the dust, that worthy bulwark of the church as it should be, that honoured representative of Oxford's best spirit, was--so at least his wife had told him half a dozen times--misconducting himself!", "그가 세인트 이월드에 앉혀 준 그 성직자의 귀감, 그가 그토록 소리 높여 자랑하던 그 대학 시절의 벗, 그 창끝 앞에서 슬로프 씨가 쓰러져 흙을 물게 되어 있던 그 교회의 기사, 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습의 교회를 떠받치는 그 훌륭한 보루, 옥스퍼드 최고의 정신을 대표하는 그 영예로운 인물이 — 적어도 아내가 예닐곱 번은 일러 준 바에 따르면 — 처신을 그르치고 있다는 것이었다!"]], [["Nothing had been seen of Mr. Arabin at Plumstead for the last week, but a good deal had, unfortunately, been heard of him.", "지난 한 주 동안 애러빈 씨는 플럼스테드에 코빼기도 보이지 않았으나, 유감스럽게도 그에 관한 말은 적잖이 들려왔다."], ["As soon as Mrs. Grantly had found herself alone with the archdeacon, on the evening of the Ullathorne party, she had expressed herself very forcibly as to Mr.", "울라손 연회가 있던 날 저녁 부주교와 단둘이 남게 되자마자 그랜틀리 부인은 그날 애러빈 씨의 행실에 대해 아주 힘주어 제 생각을 밝혔다."], ["Arabin's conduct on that occasion. He had, she declared, looked and acted and talked very unlike a decent parish clergyman.", "그가 짓던 낯빛이며 몸가짐이며 말투가 점잖은 교구 성직자답기는커녕 영 딴판이었다는 것이었다."], ["At first the archdeacon had laughed at this, and assured her that she need not trouble herself--that Mr. Arabin would be found to be quite safe.", "처음에 부주교는 그 말을 웃어넘기며, 그런 걱정은 할 것 없다고, 애러빈 씨는 조금도 위태로울 것 없는 사람으로 판명되리라고 아내를 안심시켰다."], ["But by degrees he began to find that his wife's eyes had been sharper than his own. Other people coupled the signora's name with that of Mr.", "그러나 차츰 그는 아내의 눈이 제 눈보다 밝았음을 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["Arabin. The meagre little prebendary who lived in the close told him to a nicety how often Mr.", "다른 사람들도 시뇨라의 이름을 애러빈 씨의 이름과 나란히 놓았다."], ["Arabin had visited at Dr. Stanhope's, and how long he had remained on the occasion of each visit.", "경내에 사는 그 비쩍 마른 자그마한 참사회원은 애러빈 씨가 스탠호프 박사 댁을 몇 번이나 찾아갔는지, 또 갈 때마다 얼마나 오래 머물렀는지를 낱낱이 일러 주었다."], ["He had asked after Mr. Arabin at the cathedral library, and an officious little vicar choral had offered to go and see whether he could be found at Dr.", "그가 대성당 도서관에서 애러빈 씨의 행방을 묻자, 유난히 설쳐 대는 자그마한 성가대 사제 하나가 스탠호프 박사 댁에 가 계신지 알아보고 오겠노라 나서기까지 했다."], ["Stanhope's. Rumour, when she has contrived to sound the first note on her trumpet, soon makes a loud peal audible enough.", "소문이란 제 나팔로 첫 음을 울려 내는 데 한번 성공하고 나면 이내 제법 크게 울려 퍼지는 법이다."], ["It was too clear that Mr. Arabin had succumbed to the Italian woman, and that the archdeacon's credit would suffer fearfully if something were not done to rescue the brand from the burning.", "애러빈 씨가 그 이탈리아 여자에게 넘어가고 말았다는 것, 그리고 불붙은 나무를 그 불에서 끄집어내기 위해 무슨 수를 쓰지 않는다면 부주교의 체면이 무섭게 상하리라는 것은 너무나 분명했다."], ["Besides, to give the archdeacon his due, he was really attached to Mr. Arabin, and grieved greatly at his backsliding.", "게다가 부주교에게 공평을 기하자면, 그는 애러빈 씨에게 참으로 정을 붙이고 있었으므로 그가 미끄러지는 것을 크게 슬퍼하기도 했다."]], [["They were sitting, talking over their sorrows, in the drawing-room before dinner on the day after Mr. Slope's departure for London, and on this occasion Mrs.", "슬로프 씨가 런던으로 떠난 이튿날, 두 사람은 만찬 전 응접실에 앉아 이런 근심거리들을 두고 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 이 자리에서 그랜틀리 부인은 마음속 생각을 거리낌 없이 털어놓았다."], ["Grantly spoke out her mind freely. She had opinions of her own about parish clergymen, and now thought it right to give vent to them.", "그녀에게는 교구 성직자들에 대한 나름의 견해가 있었고, 이제야말로 그것을 쏟아 놓아도 좋을 때라고 여겼다."]], [["\"If you would have been led by me, Archdeacon, you would never have put a bachelor into St. Ewold's.\"", "“제 말대로 하셨더라면, 부주교님, 세인트 이월드에 독신 남자를 앉히지는 않으셨을 거예요.”"]], [["\"But my dear, you don't meant to say that all bachelor clergymen misbehave themselves.\"", "“허나 여보, 독신인 성직자는 죄다 처신을 그르친다는 말은 아닐 테지.”"]], [["\"I don't know that clergymen are so much better than other men,\"", "“성직자라고 해서 다른 남자들보다 그리 나을 게 있는지 저는 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"It's all very well with a curate, whom you have under your own eye and whom you can get rid of if he persists in improprieties.\"", "“부목사라면야 괜찮지요. 늘 제 눈앞에 두고 볼 수 있고, 온당치 못한 짓을 고집하면 내보낼 수도 있으니까요.”"]], [["\"But Mr. Arabin was a fellow, and couldn't have had a wife.\"", "“허나 애러빈 씨는 펠로였으니 아내를 둘 수가 없었소.”"]], [["\"Then I would have found someone who could.\"", "“그렇다면 아내를 둘 수 있는 사람으로 찾아보셨어야지요.”"]], [["\"But, my dear, are fellows never to get livings?\"", "“그럼 여보, 펠로들은 성직록을 영영 받아서는 안 된다는 말이오?”"]], [["\"Yes, to be sure they are, when they get engaged.", "“아니지요, 물론 받아야지요. 약혼을 하고 나서 말이에요."], ["I never would put a young man into a living unless he were married, or engaged to be married.", "저라면 혼인을 했거나 혼인을 약속한 젊은이가 아니고서는 결코 성직록 자리에 앉히지 않겠어요."], ["Now, here is Mr. Arabin.", "그런데 여기 애러빈 씨를 보세요."], ["The whole responsibility lies upon you.\"", "책임은 온통 당신께 있는 거예요.”"]], [["\"There is not at this moment a clergyman in all Oxford more respected for morals and conduct than Arabin.\"", "“지금 이 순간 옥스퍼드를 통틀어 애러빈만큼 품행과 몸가짐으로 존경받는 성직자는 없소.”"]], [["\"Oh, Oxford!\"", "“어머, 옥스퍼드요!”"], ["said the lady, with a sneer.", "부인은 코웃음을 치며 말했다."], ["\"What men choose to do at Oxford nobody ever hears of.", "“옥스퍼드에서 남자들이 무슨 짓을 하고 다니든 아무도 듣지 못하는걸요."], ["A man may do very well at Oxford who would bring disgrace on a parish; and to tell you the truth, it seems to me that Mr.", "교구에 나오면 망신을 살 사람도 옥스퍼드에서라면 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있어요."], ["Arabin is just such a man.\"", "그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 제 눈에는 애러빈 씨가 꼭 그런 사람으로 보여요.”"]], [["The archdeacon groaned deeply, but he had no further answer to make.", "부주교는 깊이 신음했으나, 더 내놓을 대답이 없었다."]], [["\"You really must speak to him, Archdeacon.", "“정말이지 그이에게 말씀을 하셔야 해요, 부주교님."], ["Only think what the Thornes will say if they hear that their parish clergyman spends his whole time philandering with this woman.\"", "자기네 교구 성직자가 그 여자와 시시덕거리는 데 온 시간을 다 쓰고 있다는 말을 손 남매가 듣기라도 하면 뭐라고 하겠는지 좀 생각해 보세요.”"]], [["The archdeacon groaned again.", "부주교는 또 한 번 신음했다."], ["He was a courageous man, and knew well enough how to rebuke the younger clergymen of the diocese, when necessary.", "그는 담대한 사람이었고, 필요할 때 교구의 젊은 성직자들을 어떻게 나무라야 하는지도 훤히 알았다."], ["But there was that about Mr. Arabin which made the doctor feel that it would be very difficult to rebuke him with good effect.", "그러나 애러빈 씨에게는 어딘지 모르게, 그를 나무라 봐야 이렇다 할 효험을 보기가 몹시 어려우리라는 느낌을 이 박사에게 안겨 주는 데가 있었다."]], [["\"You can advise him to find a wife for himself, and he will understand well enough what that means,\"", "“아내를 하나 얻으라고 권해 보세요. 그러면 그이도 그게 무슨 뜻인지 넉넉히 알아들을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["The archdeacon had nothing for it but groaning.", "부주교로서는 신음하는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없었다."], ["There was Mr.", "저기 슬로프 씨가 있었다."], ["Slope: he was going to be made dean; he was going to take a wife; he was about to achieve respectability and wealth, an excellent family mansion, and a family carriage; he would soon be among the comfortable élite of the ecclesiastical world of Barchester; whereas his own protégé, the true scion of the true church, by whom he had sworn, would be still but a poor vicar, and that with a very indifferent character for moral conduct!", "그는 수석 사제가 될 참이었고, 아내를 맞이할 참이었으며, 남부럽지 않은 지체와 재산과 훌륭한 저택 한 채와 집안 마차 한 대를 손에 넣을 참이었다. 머지않아 그는 바체스터 성직 사회의 안락한 élite 축에 들 것이었다."], ["It might be all very well recommending Mr. Arabin to marry, but how would Mr.", "그런데 정작 제가 밀어 준 protégé, 참된 교회의 참된 후예, 그를 걸고 맹세하다시피 했던 그 사람은 여전히 가난한 교구 사제 신세를 면치 못할 것이요, 그나마도 품행이 매우 미덥지 못하다는 평판까지 달고 있을 것이었다!"], ["Arabin, when married, support a wife?", "애러빈 씨에게 혼인을 권하는 것이야 얼마든지 좋은 일이겠으나, 애러빈 씨가 혼인을 한들 무엇으로 아내를 먹여 살린단 말인가?"]], [["Things were ordering themselves thus in Plumstead drawing-room when Dr. and Mrs.", "플럼스테드 응접실에서 일이 이렇게 정리되어 가고 있을 때, 그랜틀리 박사 내외의 이 달콤한 담론은 자갈 깔린 진입로 위를 요란하게 굴러 들어오는 쌍두마차 소리에 방해를 받았다."], ["Grantly were disturbed in their sweet discourse by the quick rattle of a carriage and pair of horses on the gravel sweep.", "그것은 방문객의 소리가 아니었다. 방문객의 자가용 마차라면 대개 얌전하고 예절 바르게 시골 저택의 문 앞에 대는 법이다."], ["The sound was not that of visitors, whose private carriages are generally brought up to country-house doors with demure propriety, but betokened rather the advent of some person or persons who were in a hurry to reach the house, and had no intention of immediately leaving it.", "그 소리는 오히려 이 집에 서둘러 닿으려 하며, 닿고 나서는 당장 떠날 뜻이 없는 누군가의 도착을 알리는 소리였다."], ["Guests invited to stay a week, and who were conscious of arriving after the first dinner-bell, would probably approach in such a manner.", "한 주 묵으라는 초대를 받은 손님이 만찬을 알리는 첫 종이 울린 뒤에야 당도했음을 의식하고 있다면 아마 그런 식으로 다가올 것이다."], ["So might arrive an attorney with the news of a granduncle's death, or a son from college with all the fresh honours of a double first.", "종조부의 부고를 들고 오는 변호사나, 두 과목 최우등의 갓 딴 영예를 안고 대학에서 돌아오는 아들도 그렇게 도착할 것이다."], ["No one would have had himself driven up to the door of a country-house in such a manner who had the slightest doubt of his own right to force an entry.", "제게 그 문을 밀고 들어설 권리가 있는지 조금이라도 의심하는 사람이라면, 결코 그런 식으로 시골 저택 현관에 마차를 몰아 들이대지 않을 것이었다."]], [["\"Who is it?\"", "“누굴까요?”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, looking at her husband.", "그랜틀리 부인이 남편을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["\"Who on earth can it be?\"", "“대체 누구란 말이오?”"], ["said the archdeacon to his wife.", "부주교가 아내에게 말했다."], ["He then quietly got up and stood with the drawing-room door open in his hand.", "그러고는 조용히 일어나 응접실 문을 열고 손잡이를 잡은 채 섰다."], ["\"Why, it's your father!\"", "“아니, 장인어른이시잖소!”"]], [["It was indeed Mr. Harding, and Mr.", "과연 하딩 씨였고, 그것도 하딩 씨 혼자였다."], ["Harding alone. He had come by himself in a post-chaise with a couple of horses from Barchester, arriving almost after dark, and evidently full of news.", "그는 바체스터에서 말 두 필이 끄는 역마차를 혼자 타고 와 거의 날이 저문 뒤에 닿았는데, 보아하니 전할 소식이 가득한 모양이었다."], ["His visits had usually been made in the quietest manner; he had rarely presumed to come without notice, and had always been driven up in a modest old green fly, with one horse, that hardly made itself heard as it crawled up to the hall-door.", "그가 이곳을 찾을 때는 늘 더없이 조용한 방식이었다. 미리 기별 없이 오는 일은 좀처럼 없었고, 언제나 말 한 필이 끄는 수수한 낡은 초록색 삯마차를 타고 왔는데, 그 마차는 현관 앞까지 기어오다시피 하는 동안 소리 하나 제대로 내지 못했다."]], [["\"Good gracious, Warden, is it you?\"", "“아이고 이런, 워든, 장인어른이십니까?”"], ["said the archdeacon, forgetting in his surprise the events of the last few years.", "부주교는 놀란 나머지 지난 몇 해 사이의 일들을 깜빡 잊고 말했다."], ["\"But come in; nothing the matter, I hope.\"", "“어서 들어오십시오. 별일은 없으시겠지요.”"]], [["\"We are very glad you are come, Papa,\"", "“오셔서 정말 기뻐요, 아버지.”"], ["said his daughter.", "딸이 말했다."], ["\"I'll go and get your room ready at once.\"", "“제가 지금 곧장 아버지 방을 준비해 놓을게요.”"]], [["\"I an't warden, Archdeacon,\"", "“나는 워든이 아닐세, 부주교.”"], ["said Mr. Harding; \"Mr.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["Quiverful is warden.\"", "“이제 퀴버풀 씨가 워든이지.”"]], [["\"Oh, I know, I know,\"", "“아, 압니다, 알아요.”"], ["said the archdeacon petulantly.", "부주교가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["\"I forgot all about it at the moment.", "“잠깐 깜빡했을 뿐입니다."], ["Is anything the matter?\"", "무슨 일이라도 생겼습니까?”"]], [["\"Don't go this moment, Susan,\"", "“지금 가지 말거라, 수전.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["\"I have something to tell you.\"", "“너희에게 할 이야기가 있단다.”"]], [["\"The dinner-bell will ring in five minutes,\"", "“5분 뒤면 만찬 종이 울릴 텐데요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Will it?\"", "“그런가?”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Then perhaps I had better wait.\"", "“그럼 좀 기다리는 편이 낫겠구나.”"], ["He was big with news which he had come to tell, but which he knew could not be told without much discussion.", "그는 전하러 온 소식으로 잔뜩 부풀어 있었으나, 그 소식이 긴 의논 없이는 전해질 수 없다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["He had hurried away to Plumstead as fast as two horses could bring him, and now, finding himself there, he was willing to accept the reprieve which dinner would give him.", "그는 말 두 필이 낼 수 있는 가장 빠른 걸음으로 플럼스테드까지 달려왔건만, 막상 와 놓고 보니 만찬이 벌어 줄 유예를 기꺼이 받아들이고 싶어졌다."]], [["\"If you have anything of moment to tell us,\"", "“중대한 이야기가 있으시다면,”"], ["said the archdeacon, \"pray let us hear it at once.", "부주교가 말했다. “부디 지금 당장 들려주십시오."], ["Has Eleanor gone off?\"", "엘리너가 달아나기라도 했습니까?”"]], [["\"No, she has not,\" said Mr.", "“아닐세, 그러지 않았네.”"], ["Harding with a look of great displeasure.", "하딩 씨가 몹시 언짢은 낯빛으로 말했다."]], [["\"Has Slope been made dean?\"", "“슬로프가 수석 사제가 되었습니까?”"]], [["\"No, he has not, but--\"", "“아닐세, 그것도 아니야. 다만―”"]], [["\"But what?\"", "“다만 무엇입니까?”"], ["said the archdeacon, who was becoming very impatient.", "몹시 조바심이 나기 시작한 부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"They have--\"", "“그쪽에서 말이지―”"]], [["\"They have what?\"", "“그쪽에서 무엇을 어쨌다는 말씀입니까?”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"They have offered it to me,\" said Mr.", "“그 자리를 나에게 주겠다고 하네.”"], ["Harding, with a modesty which almost prevented his speaking.", "하딩 씨는 겸연쩍은 나머지 거의 말을 잇지 못할 지경으로 말했다."]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“맙소사!”"], ["said the archdeacon, and sunk back exhausted in an easy chair.", "부주교는 이렇게 말하고는 기진하여 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["\"My dear, dear father,\" said Mrs.", "“아버지, 사랑하는 우리 아버지.”"], ["Grantly, and threw her arms round her father's neck.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이렇게 말하며 아버지의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["\"So I thought I had better come out and consult with you at once,\"", "“그래서 곧장 나와 자네들과 의논하는 편이 낫겠다 싶었네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Consult!\"", "“의논이라니요!”"], ["shouted the archdeacon.", "부주교가 소리쳤다."], ["\"But, my dear Harding, I congratulate you with my whole heart--with my whole heart; I do indeed.", "“아니 장인어른, 진심으로 축하드립니다. 온 마음을 다해서 말입니다."], ["I never heard anything in my life that gave me so much pleasure;\"", "정말 그렇습니다. 제 평생 이토록 기쁜 소식을 들어 본 적이 없습니다.”"], ["and he got hold of both his father-in-law's hands, and shook them as though he were going to shake them off, and walked round and round the room, twirling a copy of \"The Jupiter\" over his head to show his extreme exultation.", "그러고는 장인의 두 손을 붙들고 그 손이 떨어져 나가기라도 할 듯이 흔들어 대더니, 더할 나위 없는 기쁨을 드러내려는 듯 『주피터』 한 부를 머리 위로 빙빙 돌리며 방 안을 몇 바퀴고 돌았다."]], [["\"But--\"", "“허나―”"], ["began Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"But me no buts,\"", "“허나라는 말씀은 마십시오.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"I never was so happy in my life.", "“제 평생 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없습니다."], ["It was just the proper thing to do.", "이야말로 마땅히 그리 되었어야 할 일이지요."], ["Upon my honour I'll never say another word against Lord ---- the longest day I have to live.\"", "명예를 걸고 말씀드리거니와, 저는 살아 있는 동안 다시는 ○○ 경을 두고 험담 한마디 하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"That's Dr. Gwynne's doing, you may be sure,\"", "“그건 틀림없이 그윈 박사님이 애쓰신 덕이에요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["Grantly, who greatly liked the Master of Lazarus, he being an orderly married man with a large family.", "그녀는 라자러스 학장을 무척 좋아했는데, 그가 대가족을 거느린 반듯한 기혼 남자였기 때문이다."]], [["\"I suppose it is,\"", "“그렇겠지.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Papa, I am so truly delighted!\"", "“아, 아버지, 저는 정말 너무나 기뻐요!”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, getting up and kissing her father.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이렇게 말하며 일어나 아버지에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"But, my dear,\"", "“허나 얘야.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["It was all in vain that he strove to speak; nobody would listen to him.", "말을 해 보려 애써도 다 헛일이었다. 아무도 그의 말에 귀를 기울이지 않았다."]], [["\"Well, Mr. Dean,\"", "“자, 수석 사제님.”"], ["said the archdeacon, triumphing, \"the deanery gardens will be some consolation for the hospital elms.", "부주교가 의기양양하게 말했다. “요양원 느릅나무를 잃으신 것쯤은 수석사제관 정원이 얼마간 달래 줄 겁니다."], ["Well, poor Quiverful!", "자, 가엾은 퀴버풀!"], ["I won't begrudge him his good fortune any longer.\"", "이제는 그 사람 복을 더 이상 시샘하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"No, indeed,\"", "“그럼요, 정말이지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Poor woman, she has fourteen children.", "“가엾은 부인, 아이가 열넷이나 되잖아요."], ["I am sure I am very glad they have got it.\"", "그 자리가 그 댁에 돌아간 것이 저는 참으로 다행스러워요.”"]], [["\"So am I,\"", "“나도 그렇단다.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I would give twenty pounds,\" said the archdeacon, \"to see how Mr.", "“슬로프 씨가 그 소식을 들을 때 어떤 낯빛이 되는지 볼 수만 있다면 20파운드라도 내겠습니다.”"], ["Slope will look when he hears it.\"", "부주교가 말했다."], ["The idea of Mr. Slope's discomfiture formed no small part of the archdeacon's pleasure.", "슬로프 씨가 낭패를 볼 광경을 그려 보는 것은 부주교의 기쁨에서 적잖은 몫을 차지했다."]], [["At last Mr. Harding was allowed to go upstairs and wash his hands, having, in fact, said very little of all that he had come out to Plumstead on purpose to say. Nor could anything more be said till the servants were gone after dinner. The joy of Dr. Grantly was so uncontrollable that he could not refrain from calling his father-in-law Mr. Dean before the men, and therefore it was soon matter of discussion in the lower regions how Mr. Harding, instead of his daughter's future husband, was to be the new dean, and various were the opinions on the matter. The cook and butler, who were advanced in years, thought that it was just as it should be; but the footman and lady's maid, who were younger, thought it was a great shame that Mr. Slope should lose his chance.", "마침내 하딩 씨는 이층에 올라가 손을 씻어도 좋다는 허락을 받았는데, 실인즉 플럼스테드까지 일부러 나와서 하려던 이야기는 거의 한마디도 하지 못한 뒤였다. 그리고 만찬이 끝나고 하인들이 물러가기 전에는 더 이야기할 수도 없었다. 그랜틀리 박사의 기쁨은 어찌나 걷잡을 수 없었던지 그는 하인들 앞에서 장인을 수석 사제님이라 부르는 것을 삼가지 못했고, 그리하여 아래층에서는 곧 따님의 미래 남편이 아니라 하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 될 모양이라는 것이 화제에 올랐으며, 그에 대한 의견도 갖가지였다. 나이가 지긋한 요리사와 집사는 그렇게 되는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼으나, 더 젊은 하인과 몸종은 슬로프 씨가 기회를 놓친 것이 참으로 애석한 일이라고 여겼다."]], [["\"He's a mean chap all the same,\"", "“그래도 그 양반은 쩨쩨한 사람입지요.”"], ["said the footman, \"and it an't along of him that I says so.", "하인이 말했다. “제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 건 그 양반 때문이 아닙니다요."], ["But I always did admire the missus's sister; and she'd well become the situation.\"", "다만 저야 늘 마님 동생분을 우러러 뵈었는데, 그분이라면 그 자리에 아주 잘 어울리셨을 겁니다요.”"]], [["While these were the ideas downstairs, a very great difference of opinion existed above.", "아래층 생각이 이러한 동안, 위층에서는 아주 크게 다른 의견이 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["As soon as the cloth was drawn and the wine on the table, Mr. Harding made for himself an opportunity of speaking.", "식탁보가 걷히고 포도주가 식탁에 오르자마자 하딩 씨는 말을 꺼낼 기회를 스스로 만들었다."], ["It was, however, with much inward troubling that he said:", "그러나 그가 입을 뗀 것은 속으로 몹시 마음을 졸이면서였다."]], [["\"It's very kind of Lord ----, very kind, and I feel it deeply, most deeply.", "“○○ 경께서 참으로 고맙게 대해 주셨네. 참으로 고마운 일이고, 나도 그것을 깊이, 아주 깊이 느끼고 있네."], ["I am, I must confess, gratified by the offer--\"", "고백하자면 나도 그 제안이 기쁘기는 했어―”"]], [["\"I should think so,\"", "“그러셨겠지요.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"But all the same I am afraid that I can't accept it.\"", "“허나 그래도 나는 아무래도 그것을 받아들일 수 없을 것 같네.”"]], [["The decanter almost fell from the archdeacon's hand upon the table, and the start he made was so great as to make his wife jump up from her chair.", "술병이 부주교의 손에서 식탁 위로 떨어질 뻔했고, 그가 어찌나 크게 몸을 움찔했던지 아내가 의자에서 벌떡 일어날 정도였다."], ["Not accept the deanship!", "수석 사제 자리를 받지 않겠다니!"], ["If it really ended in this, there would be no longer any doubt that his father-in-law was demented.", "정말로 일이 이렇게 끝난다면, 장인이 실성했다는 데에 더는 의심의 여지가 없을 것이었다."], ["The question now was whether a clergyman with low rank and preferment amounting to less than £200 a year should accept high rank, £1,200 a year, and one of the most desirable positions which his profession had to afford!", "이제 문제는, 지체도 낮고 성직 승진이라야 한 해 200파운드에도 못 미치는 성직자가 높은 지체와 한 해 1,200파운드와 제 직업이 내줄 수 있는 가장 탐나는 자리 가운데 하나를 받아야 하느냐 마느냐 하는 것이었다!"]], [["\"What!\"", "“무어라고요!”"], ["said the archdeacon, gasping for breath and staring at his guest as though the violence of his emotion had almost thrown him into a fit.", "부주교는 숨을 몰아쉬며, 격한 감정에 못 이겨 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 듯한 얼굴로 손님을 빤히 바라보며 말했다."], ["\"What!\"", "“무어라고요!”"]], [["\"I do not find myself fit for new duties,\"", "“나는 새 직무를 감당할 만한 사람이 못 되네.”"], ["urged Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["\"New duties!", "“새 직무라니요!"], ["What duties?\"", "무슨 직무 말씀입니까?”"], ["said the archdeacon with unintended sarcasm.", "부주교는 본의 아니게 빈정거리는 말을 했다."]], [["\"Oh, Papa,\" said Mrs.", "“아, 아버지.”"], ["Grantly, \"nothing can be easier than what a dean has to do.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “수석 사제가 할 일보다 쉬운 일이 어디 있겠어요."], ["Surely you are more active than Dr. Trefoil.\"", "아버지는 분명 트리폴 박사님보다야 정정하시잖아요.”"]], [["\"He won't have half as much to do as he has at present,\"", "“지금 하고 계신 일의 절반도 되지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["said Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사가 말했다."]], [["\"Did you see what 'The Jupiter' said the other day about young men?\"", "“요전에 『주피터』가 젊은 사람들을 두고 뭐라고 썼는지 보았는가?”"]], [["\"Yes, and I saw that 'The Jupiter' said all that it could to induce the appointment of Mr. Slope. Perhaps you would wish to see Mr. Slope made dean.\"", "“예, 그리고 『주피터』가 슬로프 씨를 임명시키려고 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다는 것도 보았습니다. 혹시 슬로프 씨가 수석 사제가 되는 꼴을 보고 싶으신 겁니까.”"]], [["Mr. Harding made no reply to this rebuke, though he felt it strongly.", "하딩 씨는 이 책망을 뼈아프게 느꼈으면서도 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["He had not come over to Plumstead to have further contention with his son-in-law about Mr. Slope, so he allowed it to pass by.", "그는 슬로프 씨를 두고 사위와 또 다투려고 플럼스테드까지 건너온 것이 아니었으므로 그냥 흘려보냈다."]], [["\"I know I cannot make you understand my feeling,\"", "“내 심정을 자네에게 이해시킬 수 없다는 것은 나도 아네.”"], ["he said, \"for we have been cast in different moulds.", "그가 말했다. “우리는 서로 다른 거푸집에서 나온 사람들이니 말일세."], ["I may wish that I had your spirit and energy and power of combatting; but I have not.", "나에게도 자네 같은 기백과 기운과 맞서 싸우는 힘이 있었으면 싶기는 하네만, 나에게는 그것이 없어."], ["Every day that is added to my life increases my wish for peace and rest.\"", "살아온 날이 하루씩 늘어 갈수록 나는 평온과 안식을 더 바라게 된다네.”"]], [["\"And where on earth can a man have peace and rest if not in a deanery!\"", "“아니, 수석사제관이 아니라면 대체 어디서 평온과 안식을 누린단 말씀입니까!”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"People will say that I am too old for it.\"", "“사람들은 내가 그 자리에 앉기에는 너무 늙었다고 할 걸세.”"]], [["\"Good heavens!", "“맙소사! 사람들이라니요!"], ["People! What people?", "어떤 사람들 말씀입니까?"], ["What need you care for any people?\"", "그깟 사람들을 무엇 하러 신경 쓰십니까?”"]], [["\"But I think myself I am too old for any new place.\"", "“허나 나 스스로도 어떤 새 자리든 맡기에는 너무 늙었다고 생각하네.”"]], [["\"Dear Papa,\" said Mrs.", "“아버지.”"], ["Grantly, \"men ten years older than you are appointed to new situations day after day.\"", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “아버지보다 열 살이나 위인 분들도 날마다 새 자리에 임명되고 있어요.”"]], [["\"My dear,\"", "“얘야.”"], ["said he, \"it is impossible that I should make you understand my feelings, nor do I pretend to any great virtue in the matter.", "그가 말했다. “내 심정을 너희에게 이해시키기란 불가능한 일이고, 나도 이 일에 무슨 대단한 덕이 있다고 내세울 생각은 없다."], ["The truth is, I want the force of character which might enable me to stand against the spirit of the times.", "사실인즉 나에게는 시대의 기풍에 맞설 만한 기질의 힘이 없구나."], ["The call on all sides now is for young men, and I have not the nerve to put myself in opposition to the demand.", "요즈음은 사방에서 젊은 사람을 부르짖고 있는데, 나에게는 그 요구에 맞서 나설 배짱이 없어."], ["Were 'The Jupiter,' when it hears of my appointment, to write article after article setting forth my incompetency, I am sure it would cost me my reason.", "만일 『주피터』가 내 임명 소식을 듣고서 내가 그 자리에 걸맞지 않다는 논설을 쓰고 또 쓴다면, 나는 그 때문에 정신을 놓고 말 게다."], ["I ought to be able to bear with such things, you will say.", "그런 것쯤은 견딜 수 있어야 한다고 너희는 말하겠지."], ["Well, my dear, I own that I ought.", "그래, 얘야, 나도 견뎌야 마땅하다는 것은 인정한다."], ["But I feel my weakness, and I know that I can't.", "허나 나는 내 약함을 느끼고, 내가 그러지 못하리라는 것을 안다."], ["And to tell you the truth, I know no more than a child what the dean has to do.\"", "그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 수석 사제가 무슨 일을 해야 하는지 나는 어린아이만큼도 알지 못한다.”"]], [["\"Pshaw!\"", "“쯧!”"], ["exclaimed the archdeacon.", "부주교가 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Don't be angry with me, Archdeacon: don't let us quarrel about it, Susan.", "“나에게 화내지 말게, 부주교. 그리고 수전, 이 일로 우리 다투지는 말자꾸나."], ["If you knew how keenly I feel the necessity of having to disoblige you in this matter, you would not be angry with me.\"", "이 문제에서 너희 뜻을 거스를 수밖에 없다는 것을 내가 얼마나 사무치게 느끼는지 안다면, 너희도 나에게 화내지는 못할 게다.”"]], [["This was a dreadful blow to Dr. Grantly.", "이것은 그랜틀리 박사에게 끔찍한 타격이었다."], ["Nothing could possibly have suited him better than having Mr. Harding in the deanery.", "하딩 씨가 수석사제관에 들어앉는 것보다 그에게 더 흡족할 일은 도무지 있을 수 없었다."], ["Though he had never looked down on Mr. Harding on account of his recent poverty, he did fully recognize the satisfaction of having those belonging to him in comfortable positions.", "그는 하딩 씨가 근래 궁색해졌다 하여 그를 얕본 적은 한 번도 없었으나, 제 사람들이 편안한 자리에 앉아 있는 데서 오는 흡족함만큼은 온전히 알아보는 사람이었다."], ["It would be much more suitable that Mr. Harding should be Dean of Barchester than vicar of St.", "하딩 씨로 말하면 세인트 커스버트 교구 사제에 선창자까지 겸하고 있느니 바체스터 수석 사제가 되는 편이 훨씬 걸맞은 일이었다."], ["Cuthbert's and precentor to boot. And then the great discomfiture of that arch-enemy of all that was respectable in Barchester, of that new Low Church clerical parvenu that had fallen amongst them, that alone would be worth more, almost, than the situation itself.", "게다가 바체스터의 점잖은 모든 것을 적대하는 저 원수, 그들 틈에 굴러떨어진 저 저교회파 성직 벼락출세자가 크게 낭패를 보는 것만 해도, 그것만으로도 그 자리 자체보다 거의 더 값진 일일 터였다."], ["It was frightful to think that such unhoped-for good fortune should be marred by the absurd crotchets and unwholesome hallucinations by which Mr. Harding allowed himself to be led astray.", "바라지도 못했던 이런 행운이, 하딩 씨가 스스로 끌려다니도록 내버려 두는 터무니없는 옹고집과 병적인 망상 때문에 망가진다고 생각하니 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["To have the cup so near his lips and then to lose the drinking of it was more than Dr. Grantly could endure.", "잔을 입술에 그토록 가까이 대어 놓고서 그것을 마시지 못한다는 것은 그랜틀리 박사가 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어섰다."]], [["And yet it appeared as though he would have to endure it.", "그런데도 아무래도 그는 그것을 견뎌야 할 듯싶었다."], ["In vain he threatened and in vain he coaxed. Mr.", "으름장을 놓아도 헛일이었고 구슬려도 헛일이었다."], ["Harding did not indeed speak with perfect decision of refusing the proffered glory, but he would not speak with anything like decision of accepting it.", "하딩 씨가 제의된 그 영예를 딱 잘라 거절한다고 말한 것은 아니었으나, 받아들이겠다고 딱 잘라 말하려 하지도 않았다."], ["When pressed again and again, he would again and again allege that he was wholly unfitted to new duties.", "거듭거듭 다그침을 받으면 그는 거듭거듭 제가 새 직무에는 전혀 맞지 않는 사람이라고 우겼다."], ["It was in vain that the archdeacon tried to insinuate, though he could not plainly declare, that there were no new duties to perform.", "부주교가, 드러내 놓고 말하지는 못한 채, 새로 할 직무 따위는 없다고 넌지시 흘려 보아도 헛일이었다."], ["It was in vain he hinted that in all cases of difficulty he, he the archdeacon, was willing and able to guide a weak-minded dean. Mr.", "어려운 일이 생길 때마다 자기가, 이 부주교가 기꺼이 나서서 마음 약한 수석 사제를 이끌어 줄 수 있노라고 넌지시 비쳐 보아도 헛일이었다."], ["Harding seemed to have a foolish idea, not only that there were new duties to do, but that no one should accept the place who was not himself prepared to do them.", "하딩 씨는 새로 할 직무가 있다는 어리석은 생각뿐 아니라, 그 직무를 몸소 감당할 각오가 되어 있지 않은 사람이라면 그 자리를 맡아서는 안 된다는 생각까지 품고 있는 듯했다."]], [["The conference ended in an understanding that Mr. Harding should at once acknowledge the letter he had received from the minister's private secretary, and should beg that he might be allowed two days to make up his mind; and that during those two days the matter should be considered.", "이 회담은, 하딩 씨가 장관의 개인 비서에게서 받은 편지에 곧바로 잘 받았다는 회신을 보내되 마음을 정할 시간을 이틀만 달라고 청하고, 그 이틀 동안 이 문제를 함께 생각해 보기로 한다는 양해로 끝이 났다."]], [["On the following morning the archdeacon was to drive Mr. Harding back to Barchester.", "이튿날 아침에 부주교가 하딩 씨를 마차로 바체스터까지 데려다주기로 되어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII. Miss Thorne Shows Her Talent at Match-Making", "# 제48장. 손 양이 중매 솜씨를 발휘하다"]], [["On Mr. Harding's return to Barchester from Plumstead, which was effected by him in due course in company with the archdeacon, more tidings of a surprising nature met him.", "하딩 씨가 플럼스테드에서 바체스터로 돌아왔을 때 — 물론 부주교와 동행하여 돌아온 것이었다 — 또 한 번 놀랄 만한 소식이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He was, during the journey, subjected to such a weight of unanswerable argument, all of which went to prove that it was his bounden duty not to interfere with the paternal Government that was so anxious to make him a dean, that when he arrived at the chemist's door in High Street, he hardly knew which way to turn himself in the matter.", "오는 길 내내 그는 반박할 수 없는 논변의 무게에 짓눌렸는데, 그 논변이 한결같이 증명하는 바는 자기를 수석 사제로 만들어 주려 그토록 애쓰는 자애로운 정부의 뜻에 끼어들지 않는 것이 그의 마땅한 도리라는 것이었다. 그리하여 하이 스트리트의 약방 문 앞에 이르렀을 무렵 그는 이 일을 어느 쪽으로 돌려놓아야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["But, perplexed as he was, he was doomed to further perplexity.", "그러나 그렇게 갈피를 못 잡는 그에게 또 다른 갈피 없음이 예정되어 있었다."], ["He found a note there from his daughter begging him most urgently to come to her immediately.", "거기서 그는 딸이 남긴 쪽지를 발견했으니, 당장 자기에게 와 달라고 몹시도 다급하게 청하는 내용이었다."], ["But we must again go back a little in our story.", "그러나 우리는 이야기를 다시 조금 뒤로 돌려야겠다."]], [["Miss Thorne had not been slow to hear the rumours respecting Mr. Arabin which had so much disturbed the happiness of Mrs.", "손 양은 그랜틀리 부인의 마음을 그토록 어지럽혔던 애러빈 씨에 관한 소문을 진작에 들어 알고 있었다."], ["Grantly. And she, also, was unhappy to think that her parish clergyman should be accused of worshipping a strange goddess.", "그리고 그 역시 제 교구의 성직자가 낯선 여신을 섬긴다는 혐의를 받는다는 것이 마음 아팠다."], ["She, also, was of opinion that rectors and vicars should all be married, and with that good-natured energy which was characteristic of her, she put her wits to work to find a fitting match for Mr. Arabin.", "그 역시 교구 목사든 교구 사제든 모두 혼인을 해야 한다는 견해를 지녔던지라, 특유의 선량한 열의를 발휘하여 애러빈 씨에게 어울릴 짝을 찾아내려고 머리를 굴리기 시작했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly, in this difficulty, could think of no better remedy than a lecture from the archdeacon.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이 난처한 일에 부주교의 훈계 말고는 더 나은 처방을 생각해 내지 못했다."], ["Miss Thorne thought that a young lady, marriageable and with a dowry, might be of more efficacy.", "손 양은 혼기가 찼고 지참금도 있는 젊은 부인이 더 효험이 있으리라고 보았다."], ["In looking through the catalogue of her unmarried friends who might possibly be in want of a husband, and might also be fit for such promotion as a country parsonage affords, she could think of no one more eligible than Mrs. Bold; consequently, losing no time, she went into Barchester on the day of Mr.", "남편을 필요로 할 법하고 시골 목사관이 줄 수 있는 정도의 영전을 감당할 만한 미혼의 벗들을 하나하나 헤아려 보았으나, 볼드 부인보다 더 알맞은 이는 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Slope's discomfiture, the same day that her brother had had his interesting interview with the last of the Neros, and invited Mrs. Bold to bring her nurse and baby to Ullathorne and make them a protracted visit.", "그리하여 그는 지체 없이, 슬로프 씨가 낭패를 본 바로 그날, 곧 제 오라비가 네로 가문의 마지막 후손과 그 흥미로운 면담을 나눈 바로 그날, 바체스터로 들어가 볼드 부인에게 유모와 아기를 데리고 울라손으로 와서 오래 머물다 가라고 청했다."]], [["Miss Thorne suggested a month or two, intending to use her influence afterwards in prolonging it so as to last out the winter, in order that Mr.", "손 양은 한두 달을 제안했으나, 속으로는 나중에 제 영향력을 써서 그 기간을 겨울이 다 가도록 늘려 볼 작정이었다."], ["Arabin might have an opportunity of becoming fairly intimate with his intended bride.", "애러빈 씨가 장차 신부가 될 사람과 제법 친해질 기회를 얻게 하자는 것이었다."], ["\"We'll have Mr. Arabin, too,\"", "“애러빈 씨도 오시게 해야지.”"], ["said Miss Thorne to herself; \"and before the spring they'll know each other; and in twelve or eighteen months'", "손 양은 혼자 이렇게 생각했다."], ["time, if all goes well, Mrs. Bold will be domiciled at St.", "“봄이 오기 전에 서로 알게 될 테고, 열두 달이나 열여덟 달쯤 지나 모든 일이 잘 풀리면 볼드 부인은 세인트 이월드에 자리를 잡고 계실 게야.”"], ["Ewold's;\" and then the kind-hearted lady gave herself some not undeserved praise for her match-making genius.", "그러고 나서 이 마음씨 고운 부인은 제 중매 솜씨에 대해 스스로에게 과분하지 않은 칭찬을 얼마간 베풀었다."]], [["Eleanor was taken a little by surprise, but the matter ended in her promising to go to Ullathorne for at any rate a week or two; on the day previous to that on which her father drove out to Plumstead, she had had herself driven out to Ullathorne.", "엘리너는 조금 놀랐으나, 일은 아무튼 한두 주쯤은 울라손에 가 있겠다고 약속하는 것으로 마무리되었다. 그리하여 그의 아버지가 플럼스테드로 마차를 몰고 나가기 전날, 그는 마차를 불러 울라손으로 나갔다."]], [["Miss Thorne would not perplex her with her embryo lord on that same evening, thinking that she would allow her a few hours to make herself at home; but on the following morning Mr.", "손 양은 그날 저녁만큼은 아직 싹도 트지 않은 낭군감으로 그를 성가시게 하지 않으려 했으니, 몇 시간쯤은 편히 자리를 잡게 두자는 생각에서였다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 애러빈 씨가 당도했다."], ["Arabin arrived. \"And now,\"", "“자.”"], ["said Miss Thorne to herself, \"I must contrive to throw them in each other's way.\"", "손 양은 혼자 중얼거렸다. “이제 어떻게든 두 사람이 서로 마주치도록 손을 써야겠구먼.”"], ["That same day, after dinner, Eleanor, with an assumed air of dignity which she could not maintain, with tears which she could not suppress, with a flutter which she could not conquer, and a joy which she could not hide, told Miss Thorne that she was engaged to marry Mr. Arabin and that it behoved her to get back home to Barchester as quick as she could.", "바로 그날, 만찬이 끝난 뒤 엘리너는 지어 보이려 했으나 지켜 내지 못한 위엄과, 참으려 했으나 참지 못한 눈물과, 이겨 내려 했으나 이기지 못한 설렘과, 감추려 했으나 감추지 못한 기쁨을 안고서, 자기가 애러빈 씨와 혼인을 약속했으며 되도록 빨리 바체스터의 집으로 돌아가야 하겠다고 손 양에게 말했다."]], [["To say simply that Miss Thorne was rejoiced at the success of the scheme would give a very faint idea of her feelings on the occasion.", "손 양이 제 계략의 성공을 기뻐했다고만 말한다면 그때 그의 심경을 아주 희미하게밖에 전하지 못할 것이다."], ["My readers may probably have dreamt before now that they have had before them some terribly long walk to accomplish, some journey of twenty or thirty miles, an amount of labour frightful to anticipate, and that immediately on starting they have ingeniously found some accommodating short cut which has brought them without fatigue to their work's end in five minutes.", "독자 여러분도 아마 이런 꿈을 꾸어 본 적이 있으리라. 앞에 무시무시하게 긴 도보 여정이, 20마일이나 30마일쯤 되는 길이, 생각만 해도 끔찍한 노고가 놓여 있는데, 막상 출발하자마자 용케 편리한 지름길을 찾아내어 피로 한 점 없이 5분 만에 그 일의 끝에 다다랐던 꿈 말이다."], ["Miss Thorne's waking feelings were somewhat of the same nature.", "손 양이 깨어서 느낀 심경도 얼마간 그런 종류였다."], ["My readers may perhaps have had to do with children, and may on some occasion have promised to their young charges some great gratification intended to come off, perhaps at the end of the winter, or at the beginning of summer.", "또 독자 여러분은 아이들을 상대해 본 적이 있을 터인즉, 어느 땐가 어린것들에게 굉장한 즐길 거리를 하나 약속하되 이를테면 겨울이 끝날 무렵이나 여름이 시작될 무렵에 있을 일이라고 일러 준 적이 있을 것이다."], ["The impatient juveniles, however, will not wait, and clamorously demand their treat before they go to bed.", "그러나 성마른 아이들은 기다리지 않고, 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 대접을 내놓으라고 아우성을 친다."], ["Miss Thorne had a sort of feeling that her children were equally unreasonable.", "손 양은 제 아이들도 그만큼이나 막무가내라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["She was like an inexperienced gunner, who has ill-calculated the length of the train that he has laid.", "그는 제가 깔아 둔 도화선의 길이를 잘못 헤아린 서투른 포수와도 같았다."], ["The gun-powder exploded much too soon, and poor Miss Thorne felt that she was blown up by the strength of her own petard.", "화약은 너무 일찍 터졌고, 가엾은 손 양은 제가 놓은 화약의 힘에 제가 날아간 꼴이라고 느꼈다."]], [["Miss Thorne had had lovers of her own, but they had been gentlemen of old-fashioned and deliberate habits.", "손 양에게도 한때 구애하는 이들이 있었으나, 그들은 하나같이 예스럽고 신중한 습성의 신사들이었다."], ["Miss Thorne's heart also had not always been hard, though she was still a virgin spinster; but it had never yielded in this way at the first assault.", "손 양의 심장도 늘 굳어만 있었던 것은 아니다 — 비록 그가 여태 처녀의 몸으로 늙어 가는 노처녀이기는 하지만. 그러나 그 심장이 첫 번째 공격에 이런 식으로 무너진 적은 결코 없었다."], ["She had intended to bring together a middle-aged, studious clergyman and a discreet matron who might possibly be induced to marry again, and in doing so she had thrown fire among tinder.", "그는 중년의 학구적인 성직자와, 어쩌면 재혼할 마음을 품게 될지도 모를 신중한 부인을 맺어 주려 했던 것인데, 그러다 보니 부싯깃 더미에 불씨를 던져 넣은 셈이 되었다."], ["Well, it was all as it should be, but she did feel perhaps a little put out by the precipitancy of her own success, and perhaps a little vexed at the readiness of Mrs. Bold to be wooed.", "그야 다 마땅히 그리될 일이었다마는, 그래도 그는 제 성공이 너무 성급했던 것에 얼마간 마음이 어수선했고, 볼드 부인이 구애를 그토록 선뜻 받아들인 데에도 얼마간 언짢은 마음이 들었던 듯하다."]], [["She said, however, nothing about it to anyone, and ascribed it all to the altered manners of the new age.", "그러나 그는 그 일을 두고 누구에게도 아무 말을 하지 않았고, 모든 것을 달라진 새 시대의 풍속 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["Their mothers and grandmothers were perhaps a little more deliberate, but it was admitted on all sides that things were conducted very differently now than in former times.", "그들의 어머니와 할머니 세대는 어쩌면 조금 더 느긋했을 터이나, 요즘 일이 옛날과는 사뭇 다르게 굴러간다는 것만은 어느 쪽에서나 인정하는 바였다."], ["For aught Miss Thorne knew of the matter, a couple of hours might be quite sufficient under the new régime to complete that for which she in her ignorance had allotted twelve months.", "손 양이 이 문제에 대해 아는 바로는, 새로운 régime 아래서는 두 시간이면 넉넉히 마칠 수 있는 일을 그는 아무것도 모르고 열두 달이나 잡아 두었던 것인지도 몰랐다."]], [["But we must not pass over the wooing so cavalierly.", "그러나 우리가 이 구애를 이렇게 대충 지나쳐 버려서는 안 되겠다."], ["It has been told, with perhaps tedious accuracy, how Eleanor disposed of two of her lovers at Ullathorne; and it must also be told with equal accuracy, and if possible with less tedium, how she encountered Mr. Arabin.", "엘리너가 울라손에서 제 구혼자 둘을 어떻게 처리했는지는 어쩌면 지루하리만큼 정확하게 이야기했으니, 그가 애러빈 씨와 어떻게 마주하게 되었는지도 그에 못지않게 정확하게, 그러면서도 되도록이면 덜 지루하게 이야기해야 마땅하다."]], [["It cannot be denied that when Eleanor accepted Miss Thorne's invitation she remembered that Ullathorne was in the parish of St. Ewold's.", "엘리너가 손 양의 초대를 받아들일 때 울라손이 세인트 이월드 교구에 속해 있다는 사실을 떠올렸다는 것은 부인할 수 없다."], ["Since her interview with the signora she had done little else than think about Mr. Arabin and the appeal that had been made to her.", "시뇨라와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 그는 애러빈 씨와 자기에게 던져진 그 호소 말고는 거의 아무것도 생각하지 못했다."], ["She could not bring herself to believe, or try to bring herself to believe, that what she had been told was untrue.", "그는 자기가 들은 말이 사실이 아니라고 믿을 수도, 믿어 보려 애쓸 수도 없었다."], ["Think of it how she would, she could not but accept it as a fact that Mr.", "아무리 이리저리 생각해 보아도 애러빈 씨가 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 사실로 받아들이지 않을 도리가 없었다."], ["Arabin was fond of her; and then when she went further and asked herself the question, she could not but accept it as a fact also that she was fond of him.", "그리고 거기서 한 걸음 더 나아가 스스로에게 물었을 때에는, 자기 또한 그를 좋아한다는 것 역시 사실로 받아들이지 않을 도리가 없었다."], ["If it were destined for her to be the partner of his hopes and sorrows, to whom could she look for friendship so properly as to Miss Thorne?", "만일 그가 그이의 희망과 슬픔을 함께 나눌 사람이 되도록 정해져 있다면, 손 양 말고 또 누구에게 이만큼 걸맞은 우정을 구할 수 있겠는가?"], ["This invitation was like an ordained step towards the fulfilment of her destiny, and when she also heard that Mr. Arabin was expected to be at Ullathorne on the following day, it seemed as though all the world were conspiring in her favour.", "이 초대는 제 운명의 성취를 향해 내디디도록 정해진 한 걸음 같았고, 게다가 애러빈 씨가 이튿날 울라손에 오리라는 말까지 듣고 나니 온 세상이 자기 편을 들어 짜고 도는 것만 같았다."], ["Well, did she not deserve it?", "하기야 그럴 만하지 않은가?"], ["In that affair of Mr. Slope had not all the world conspired against her?", "슬로프 씨의 그 일에서는 온 세상이 그를 거슬러 짜고 돌지 않았던가?"]], [["She could not, however, make herself easy and at home.", "그러나 그는 마음을 놓고 편히 지낼 수가 없었다."], ["When, in the evening after dinner, Miss Thorne expatiated on the excellence of Mr. Arabin's qualities, and hinted that any little rumour which might be ill-naturedly spread abroad concerning him really meant nothing, Mrs.", "만찬이 끝난 저녁에 손 양이 애러빈 씨의 여러 미덕을 늘어놓으며, 그를 두고 짓궂게 퍼진 자잘한 소문 따위는 아무 뜻도 없는 것이라고 넌지시 일러 주었을 때, 볼드 부인은 무어라 대답할 수가 없었다."], ["Bold found herself unable to answer. When Miss Thorne went a little further and declared that she did not know a prettier vicarage-house in the county than St.", "손 양이 조금 더 나아가, 이 주에서 세인트 이월드보다 더 어여쁜 교구 사제관은 알지 못한다고 잘라 말했을 때에도, 볼드 부인은 계획된 퇴창과 계획된 여사제를 떠올리며 여전히 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["Ewold's, Mrs. Bold, remembering the projected bow-window and the projected priestess, still held her tongue, though her ears tingled with the conviction that all the world knew that she was in love with Mr.", "다만 온 세상이 자기가 애러빈 씨를 사랑한다는 것을 다 안다는 확신에 두 귀가 화끈거렸다."], ["Arabin. Well, what would that matter if they could only meet and tell each other what each now longed to tell?", "하기야 두 사람이 만나서 지금 서로에게 말하고 싶어 못 견디는 그 말을 주고받을 수만 있다면, 그런 것이 무슨 대수이겠는가?"]], [["And they did meet. Mr.", "그리고 두 사람은 정말로 만났다."], ["Arabin came early in the day and found the two ladies together at work in the drawing-room.", "애러빈 씨는 그날 이른 시각에 와서 두 부인이 응접실에서 함께 바느질을 하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Miss Thorne, who, had she known all the truth, would have vanished into air at once, had no conception that her immediate absence would be a blessing, and remained chatting with them till luncheon-time.", "손 양은, 만일 사정을 죄다 알았더라면 그 자리에서 공기 속으로 사라져 버렸을 사람인데도, 제가 당장 자리를 비켜 주는 것이 얼마나 큰 은혜인지는 짐작도 못 한 채 점심 시간까지 두 사람과 이야기를 나누며 남아 있었다."], ["Mr. Arabin could talk about nothing but the Signora Neroni's beauty, would discuss no people but the Stanhopes.", "애러빈 씨는 시뇨라 네로니의 아름다움 말고는 아무 이야기도 하지 못했고, 스탠호프 일가 말고는 아무도 화제에 올리려 하지 않았다."], ["This was very distressing to Eleanor and not very satisfactory to Miss Thorne.", "이는 엘리너에게 몹시 괴로운 일이었고 손 양에게도 그리 흡족한 일이 아니었다."], ["But yet there was evidence of innocence in his open avowal of admiration.", "그러나 그가 그렇게 대놓고 감탄을 털어놓는 데에는 그래도 결백의 증거가 있었다."]], [["And then they had lunch, and then Mr. Arabin went out on parish duty, and Eleanor and Miss Thorne were left to take a walk together.", "그러고 나서 점심을 들었고, 그러고 나서 애러빈 씨는 교구 일로 나갔으며, 엘리너와 손 양만 남아 함께 산책을 하게 되었다."]], [["\"Do you think the Signora Neroni is so lovely as people say?\"", "“시뇨라 네로니가 사람들 말대로 그렇게 어여쁜가요?”"], ["Eleanor asked as they were coming home.", "돌아오는 길에 엘리너가 물었다."]], [["\"She is very beautiful, certainly, very beautiful,\"", "“아주 아름답지요, 분명히, 아주 아름다워요.”"], ["Miss Thorne answered; \"but I do not know that anyone considers her lovely.", "손 양이 대답했다. “허나 누구든 그이를 사랑스럽다고 여기는지는 모르겠구려."], ["She is a woman all men would like to look at, but few, I imagine, would be glad to take her to their hearths, even were she unmarried and not afflicted as she is.\"", "사내라면 누구나 한 번 바라보고 싶어 할 여인이기는 하나, 설령 혼인한 몸이 아니고 지금처럼 몸이 성치 못한 것도 아니라 한들, 제 집 화롯가에 기꺼이 들이려 할 사내는 몇 없지 싶소.”"]], [["There was some little comfort in this.", "이 말에는 얼마간 위로가 있었다."], ["Eleanor made the most of it till she got back to the house.", "엘리너는 집에 돌아올 때까지 그 위로를 한껏 곱씹었다."], ["She was then left alone in the drawing-room, and just as it was getting dark Mr. Arabin came in.", "그러고는 응접실에 홀로 남겨졌는데, 막 어둑어둑해질 무렵 애러빈 씨가 들어왔다."]], [["It was a beautiful afternoon in the beginning of October, and Eleanor was sitting in the window to get the advantage of the last daylight for her novel.", "10월 초의 고운 오후였고, 엘리너는 마지막 햇빛을 소설 읽는 데 쓰려고 창가에 앉아 있었다."], ["There was a fire in the comfortable room, but the weather was not cold enough to make it attractive; and as she could see the sun set from where she sat, she was not very attentive to her book.", "아늑한 방에는 불이 피워져 있었으나 날씨가 그 앞으로 끌릴 만큼 춥지는 않았고, 앉은 자리에서 해가 지는 것이 보였으므로 그는 책에 그리 마음을 두고 있지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, when he entered, stood awhile with his back to the fire in his usual way, merely uttering a few commonplace remarks about the beauty of the weather, while he plucked up courage for more interesting converse.", "애러빈 씨는 들어오자 늘 하던 대로 잠시 난롯불을 등지고 서서, 더 흥미로운 이야기를 꺼낼 용기를 그러모으는 동안 날씨가 좋다는 따위의 평범한 말만 몇 마디 늘어놓았다."], ["It cannot probably be said that he had resolved then and there to make an offer to Eleanor.", "그가 그때 그 자리에서 엘리너에게 청혼하기로 마음을 굳혔다고는 아마 말할 수 없을 것이다."], ["Men, we believe, seldom make such resolves. Mr.", "남자란 대개 그런 결심을 하지 않는 법이라고 우리는 생각한다."], ["Slope and Mr. Stanhope had done so, it is true, but gentlemen generally propose without any absolutely defined determination as to their doing so.", "슬로프 씨와 스탠호프 씨는 과연 그런 결심을 했다마는, 신사들은 대개 하겠다는 뚜렷한 작정도 없이 청혼을 한다."], ["Such was now the case with Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨의 경우도 지금 그러했다."]], [["\"It is a lovely sunset,\"", "“해 지는 빛이 곱네요.”"], ["said Eleanor, answering him on the dreadfully trite subject which he had chosen.", "엘리너가 그가 골라 놓은 그 지독히도 진부한 화제에 응하며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Arabin could not see the sunset from the hearth-rug, so he had to go close to her.", "애러빈 씨는 난로 앞 깔개에서는 지는 해가 보이지 않았으므로 그의 곁으로 다가가야만 했다."]], [["\"Very lovely,\"", "“참으로 곱군요.”"], ["said he, standing modestly so far away from her as to avoid touching the flounces of her dress.", "그는 이렇게 말하며, 그의 옷자락 주름에 닿지 않을 만큼 얌전히 멀찍이 떨어져 섰다."], ["Then it appeared that he had nothing further to say; so, after gazing for a moment in silence at the brightness of the setting sun, he returned to the fire.", "그러고 나니 더는 할 말이 없는 모양이었다. 그리하여 잠시 말없이 저무는 해의 밝음을 바라보고 나서 그는 난로 쪽으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Eleanor found that it was quite impossible for herself to commence a conversation.", "엘리너는 자기 쪽에서 먼저 이야기를 꺼내기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["In the first place she could find nothing to say; words, which were generally plenty enough with her, would not come to her relief.", "우선 무슨 말을 해야 할지 찾을 수가 없었다. 평소에는 넉넉하고도 남던 말이 도와주러 나와 주지 않았다."], ["And moreover, do what she would, she could hardly prevent herself from crying.", "게다가 아무리 애를 써도 눈물이 나오는 것을 좀처럼 막을 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Do you like Ullathorne?\" said Mr.", "“울라손이 마음에 드시나요?”"], ["Arabin, speaking from the safely distant position which he had assumed on the hearth-rug.", "애러빈 씨가 안전하게 멀찍한 자리, 곧 난로 앞 깔개에 자리를 잡고 서서 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, indeed, very much!\"", "“네, 정말이지 아주 마음에 들어요!”"]], [["\"I don't mean Mr. and Miss Thorne--I know you like them--but the style of the house.", "“손 씨와 손 양을 말하는 것이 아니라 — 두 분을 좋아하시는 건 저도 알지요 — 이 집의 양식 말입니다."], ["There is something about old-fashioned mansions, built as this is, and old-fashioned gardens, that to me is especially delightful.\"", "이렇게 지은 예스러운 저택과 예스러운 정원에는 저로서는 유난히 마음이 끌리는 무언가가 있어요.”"]], [["\"I like everything old-fashioned,\"", "“저는 예스러운 것이라면 무엇이든 좋아요.”"], ["said Eleanor; \"old-fashioned things are so much the honestest.\"", "엘리너가 말했다. “예스러운 것들은 그만큼 더 정직하니까요.”"]], [["\"I don't know about that,\"", "“그건 어떨지 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin, gently laughing.", "애러빈 씨가 나직이 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"That is an opinion on which very much may be said on either side.", "“그건 이쪽으로든 저쪽으로든 할 말이 아주 많은 문제지요."], ["It is strange how widely the world is divided on a subject which so nearly concerns us all, and which is so close beneath our eyes.", "우리 모두에게 그토록 가까이 걸린 문제, 우리 눈앞에 그토록 바싹 놓여 있는 문제를 두고 세상이 그토록 넓게 갈라져 있다는 것이 이상합니다."], ["Some think that we are quickly progressing towards perfection, while others imagine that virtue is disappearing from the earth.\"", "어떤 이들은 우리가 완전함을 향해 빠르게 나아가고 있다고 여기는데, 또 어떤 이들은 미덕이 이 땅에서 사라져 가고 있다고 여기니까요.”"]], [["\"And you, Mr. Arabin, what do you think?\"", "“그러면 애러빈 씨는 어떻게 생각하세요?”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["She felt somewhat surprised at the tone which his conversation was taking, and yet she was relieved at his saying something which enabled herself to speak without showing her own emotion.", "그는 이야기가 흘러가는 어조에 얼마간 놀라면서도, 자기 감정을 드러내지 않고 말할 수 있게 해 주는 무언가를 그가 꺼내 준 것이 다행스러웠다."]], [["\"What do I think, Mrs. Bold?\"", "“제가 어떻게 생각하느냐고요, 볼드 부인?”"], ["and then he rumbled his money with his hands in his trousers pockets, and looked and spoke very little like a thriving lover.", "그러고서 그는 바지 주머니에 손을 넣고 돈을 짤랑거렸는데, 그 꼴이나 말투나 일이 잘 풀리는 구애자와는 도무지 닮은 데가 없었다."], ["\"It is the bane of my life that on important subjects I acquire no fixed opinion.", "“중요한 문제에 대해서는 도무지 굳은 견해를 얻지 못한다는 것이 제 삶의 골칫거리랍니다."], ["I think, and think, and go on thinking, and yet my thoughts are running ever in different directions.", "생각하고, 생각하고, 계속 생각하는데도 제 생각은 늘 서로 다른 방향으로 달려가지요."], ["I hardly know whether or no we do lean more confidently than our fathers did on those high hopes to which we profess to aspire.\"", "우리가 우러러 바란다고 공언하는 그 높은 소망들에 우리가 우리 아버지들보다 더 든든히 기대고 있는지 어떤지, 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I think the world grows more worldly every day,\"", "“저는 세상이 날마다 더 세속적으로 되어 간다고 생각해요.”"], ["said Eleanor.", "엘리너가 말했다."]], [["\"That is because you see more of it than when you were younger.", "“그건 부인이 젊으셨을 때보다 세상을 더 많이 보시기 때문이지요."], ["But we should hardly judge by what we see--we see so very, very little.\"", "허나 우리가 보는 것으로 판단해서는 안 될 겁니다 — 우리가 보는 것은 참으로, 참으로 적으니까요.”"], ["There was then a pause for awhile, during which Mr. Arabin continued to turn over his shillings and half-crowns.", "그러고서 얼마간 말이 끊겼고, 그동안 애러빈 씨는 계속 실링과 반 크라운 은화를 뒤적였다."], ["\"If we believe in Scripture, we can hardly think that mankind in general will now be allowed to retrograde.\"", "“성경을 믿는다면야, 인류가 전체로서 이제 와 뒷걸음질하도록 허락되리라고는 좀처럼 생각할 수 없지요.”"]], [["Eleanor, whose mind was certainly engaged otherwise than on the general state of mankind, made no answer to this.", "엘리너는 마음이 인류의 일반적 형편 따위와는 사뭇 다른 데에 붙들려 있었으므로 이 말에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["She felt thoroughly dissatisfied with herself.", "그는 자기 자신이 철저히 못마땅했다."], ["She could not force her thoughts away from the topic on which the signora had spoken to her in so strange a way, and yet she knew that she could not converse with Mr. Arabin in an unrestrained, natural tone till she did so.", "시뇨라가 그토록 이상한 방식으로 자기에게 꺼낸 그 이야기에서 생각을 억지로 떼어 낼 수가 없었고, 그러면서도 그 이야기를 매듭짓기 전에는 애러빈 씨와 거리낌 없는 자연스러운 어조로 말을 나눌 수 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She was most anxious not to show to him any special emotion, and yet she felt that if he looked at her, he would at once see that she was not at ease.", "그는 그에게 어떤 특별한 감정도 내보이지 않기를 몹시 바랐으나, 그가 자기를 바라보기만 하면 자기가 편치 않다는 것을 대번에 알아채리라는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["But he did not look at her.", "그러나 그는 그를 바라보지 않았다."], ["Instead of doing so, he left the fire-place and began walking up and down the room.", "바라보는 대신 그는 난롯가를 떠나 방 안을 오르내리며 거닐기 시작했다. 엘리너는 마음을 다잡고 책을 집어 들었으나 읽을 수가 없었다."], ["Eleanor took up her book resolutely, but she could not read, for there was a tear in her eye, and do what she would, it fell on her cheek.", "눈에 눈물이 고여 있었고, 아무리 애를 써도 그것이 뺨으로 흘러내렸다. 애러빈 씨가 자기에게 등을 돌렸을 때 그는 그것을 닦아 냈다."], ["When Mr. Arabin's back was turned to her, she wiped it away; but another was soon coursing down her face in its place.", "그러나 이내 또 한 줄기가 그 자리를 대신해 얼굴을 타고 흘렀다."], ["They would come--not a deluge of tears that would have betrayed her at once, but one by one, single monitors.", "눈물은 계속 나왔다 — 단번에 그를 들키게 만들 홍수 같은 눈물이 아니라, 하나씩 하나씩, 홀로 오는 파수꾼들처럼."], ["Mr. Arabin did not observe her closely, and they passed unseen.", "애러빈 씨는 그를 유심히 살피지 않았으므로 눈물은 들키지 않고 지나갔다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, thus pacing up and down the room, took four or five turns before he spoke another word, and Eleanor sat equally silent with her face bent over her book.", "이렇게 방 안을 오르내리던 애러빈 씨는 네댓 번을 돌고서야 다음 말을 꺼냈고, 엘리너도 똑같이 말없이 책 위로 얼굴을 숙이고 앉아 있었다."], ["She was afraid that her tears would get the better of her, and was preparing for an escape from the room, when Mr. Arabin in his walk stood opposite to her.", "그는 눈물에 지고 말까 봐 두려워 방에서 빠져나갈 채비를 하던 참이었는데, 그때 애러빈 씨가 거닐다가 그의 맞은편에 멈춰 섰다."], ["He did not come close up but stood exactly on the spot to which his course brought him, and then, with his hands under his coat-tails, thus made his confession.", "그는 바싹 다가오지는 않고 걸음이 데려다 놓은 바로 그 자리에 선 채, 두 손을 웃옷 자락 밑에 넣고서 이렇게 고백을 시작했다."]], [["\"Mrs. Bold,\"", "“볼드 부인.”"], ["said he, \"I owe you retribution for a great offence of which I have been guilty towards you.\"", "그가 말했다. “저는 부인께 저지른 큰 잘못에 대해 갚아야 할 빚이 있습니다.”"], ["Eleanor's heart beat so that she could not trust herself to say that he had never been guilty of any offence.", "엘리너는 심장이 어찌나 뛰던지 그가 아무 잘못도 저지른 적이 없다는 말조차 제 입으로 할 자신이 없었다."], ["So Mr. Arabin thus went on.", "그리하여 애러빈 씨는 이렇게 말을 이었다."]], [["\"I have thought much of it since, and I am now aware that I was wholly unwarranted in putting to you a question which I once asked you.", "“그 뒤로 그 일을 많이 생각해 보았고, 이제는 언젠가 제가 부인께 여쭈었던 그 물음이 도무지 아무 근거도 없는 것이었음을 압니다."], ["It was indelicate on my part, and perhaps unmanly.", "제 쪽에서 무례한 일이었고, 어쩌면 사내답지 못한 일이었지요."], ["No intimacy which may exist between myself and your connexion, Dr. Grantly, could justify it.", "저와 부인의 인척이신 그랜틀리 박사 사이에 어떤 친분이 있다 한들 그것이 정당화될 수는 없습니다."], ["Nor could the acquaintance which existed between ourselves.\"", "우리 두 사람 사이에 있었던 아는 사이라는 것으로도 마찬가지고요.”"], ["This word acquaintance struck cold on Eleanor's heart.", "이 ‘아는 사이’라는 말이 엘리너의 가슴을 서늘하게 쳤다."], ["Was this to be her doom after all?", "결국 이것이 자기에게 정해진 운명이란 말인가?"], ["\"I therefore think it right to beg your pardon in a humble spirit, and I now do so.\"", "“그래서 저는 겸허한 마음으로 부인께 용서를 구하는 것이 옳다고 생각하며, 지금 그리하는 것입니다.”"]], [["What was Eleanor to say to him?", "엘리너는 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 했을까?"], ["She could not say much because she was crying, and yet she must say something.", "울고 있었으므로 많은 말을 할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 무슨 말이든 해야 했다."], ["She was most anxious to say that something graciously, kindly, and yet not in such a manner as to betray herself.", "그는 그 무슨 말을 정중하게, 다정하게, 그러면서도 제 속을 드러내지 않는 방식으로 하기를 몹시 바랐다."], ["She had never felt herself so much at a loss for words.", "그는 여태 그토록 말이 궁했던 적이 없었다."]], [["\"Indeed, I took no offence, Mr. Arabin.\"", "“정말이지 저는 아무 언짢음도 느끼지 않았어요, 애러빈 씨.”"]], [["\"Oh, but you did!", "“아니, 느끼셨습니다!"], ["And had you not done so, you would not have been yourself.", "그리고 느끼지 않으셨다면 부인은 부인이 아니었겠지요."], ["You were as right to be offended as I was wrong so to offend you.", "제가 그렇게 언짢게 해 드린 것이 그른 만큼 부인이 언짢아하신 것은 옳았습니다."], ["I have not forgiven myself, but I hope to hear that you forgive me.\"", "저는 아직 제 자신을 용서하지 못했습니다만, 부인께서 저를 용서하신다는 말은 듣고 싶습니다.”"]], [["She was now past speaking calmly, though she still continued to hide her tears; and Mr.", "이제 그는 차분히 말할 수 있는 지경을 넘어서 있었으나, 그래도 여전히 눈물은 감추고 있었다."], ["Arabin, after pausing a moment in vain for her reply, was walking off towards the door.", "애러빈 씨는 잠시 대답을 기다렸으나 헛일이었으므로 문 쪽으로 걸어가려 했다."], ["She felt that she could not allow him to go unanswered without grievously sinning against all charity; so, rising from her seat, she gently touched his arm and said, \"Oh, Mr.", "그는 그를 이렇게 대답 없이 보내는 것이야말로 온갖 자애로움을 크게 거스르는 죄라고 느꼈다. 그래서 자리에서 일어나 살며시 그의 팔에 손을 대고 말했다."], ["Arabin, do not go till I speak to you!", "“아, 애러빈 씨, 제 말을 들으시기 전에는 가지 마세요!"], ["I do forgive you.", "저는 용서해요."], ["You know that I forgive you.\"", "제가 용서하는 걸 아시잖아요.”"]], [["He took the hand that had so gently touched his arm and then gazed into her face as if he would peruse there, as though written in a book, the whole future destiny of his life; as he did so, there was a sober, sad seriousness in his own countenance which Eleanor found herself unable to sustain.", "그는 그토록 살며시 제 팔에 닿았던 그 손을 잡고서, 마치 책에 씌어 있기라도 한 것처럼 제 남은 생의 온 운명을 거기서 읽어 내려는 듯 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그렇게 바라보는 그의 얼굴에는 가라앉은 슬픈 진지함이 서려 있었는데, 엘리너는 그것을 마주 감당할 수가 없었다."], ["She could only look down upon the carpet, let her tears trickle as they would, and leave her hand within his.", "그는 그저 융단만 내려다보며, 눈물은 흐르는 대로 내버려 두고, 제 손도 그의 손 안에 그대로 두고 있을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["It was but for a minute that they stood so, but the duration of that minute was sufficient to make it ever memorable to them both.", "두 사람이 그렇게 서 있었던 것은 겨우 1분이었지만, 그 1분의 길이는 두 사람 모두에게 그것을 영원히 잊지 못할 것으로 만들기에 넉넉했다."], ["Eleanor was sure now that she was loved.", "엘리너는 이제 자기가 사랑받고 있음을 확신했다."], ["No words, be their eloquence what it might, could be more impressive than that eager, melancholy gaze.", "그 어떤 말도, 그 웅변이 어떠하든, 저 간절하고 우수 어린 눈길보다 더 깊이 사무칠 수는 없었다."]], [["Why did he look so into her eyes?", "어찌하여 그는 그렇게 그의 눈을 들여다보았을까?"], ["Why did he not speak to her?", "어찌하여 그는 그에게 말을 하지 않았을까?"], ["Could it be that he looked for her to make the first sign?", "혹시 그가 먼저 신호를 보내 주기를 바라고 있었던 것일까?"]], [["And he, though he knew but little of women, even he knew that he was loved.", "그리고 그 또한, 여자에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없는 그조차도, 자기가 사랑받고 있음을 알았다."], ["He had only to ask, and it would be all his own, that inexpressible loveliness, those ever-speaking but yet now mute eyes, that feminine brightness and eager, loving spirit which had so attracted him since first he had encountered it at St.", "청하기만 하면 그 모든 것이 다 제 것이 될 터였다. 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 사랑스러움, 늘 말을 하지만 지금은 말이 없는 저 두 눈, 세인트 이월드에서 처음 마주친 뒤로 그토록 그를 끌어당겼던 저 여인다운 환함과 간절하고 정 많은 마음이."], ["Ewold's. It might, must, all be his own now.", "그것이 이제 다 제 것이 될 수 있었고, 되어야만 했다."], ["On no other supposition was it possible that she should allow her hand to remain thus clasped within his own.", "그렇지 않고서야 그가 제 손을 그의 손 안에 이렇게 잡힌 채로 두었을 리가 없었다."], ["He had only to ask.", "청하기만 하면 되었다."], ["Ah, but that was the difficulty.", "아, 그러나 그것이 바로 어려운 일이었다."], ["Did a minute suffice for all this?", "이 모든 것에 1분이면 넉넉했을까?"], ["Nay, perhaps it might be more than a minute.", "아니, 어쩌면 1분이 넘었는지도 모른다."]], [["\"Mrs. Bold--\"", "“볼드 부인 —”"], ["at last he said and then stopped himself.", "마침내 그가 이렇게 말하고는 스스로 말을 멈추었다."]], [["If he could not speak, how was she to do so?", "그가 말을 하지 못한다면 그가 어떻게 말을 하겠는가?"], ["He had called her by her name, the same name that any merest stranger would have used!", "그는 그를 성으로 불렀다. 생판 남이라도 썼을 바로 그 호칭으로!"], ["She withdrew her hand from his and moved as though to return to her seat.", "그는 그의 손에서 제 손을 빼내고 제자리로 돌아가려는 듯 몸을 움직였다."], ["\"Eleanor!\"", "“엘리너!”"], ["he then said in his softest tone, as though the courage of a lover were as yet but half-assumed, as though he were still afraid of giving offence by the freedom which he took.", "그러자 그가 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했으니, 마치 연인의 용기를 아직 절반밖에 꾸어 오지 못한 듯이, 마치 제가 취한 그 스스럼없음으로 그를 언짢게 할까 아직도 두려운 듯이."], ["She looked slowly, gently, almost piteously up into his face.", "그는 천천히, 부드럽게, 거의 애처로울 만큼 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["There was at any rate no anger there to deter him.", "거기에는 적어도 그를 물러서게 할 노여움은 없었다."]], [["\"Eleanor!\"", "“엘리너!”"], ["he again exclaimed, and in a moment he had her clasped to his bosom.", "그가 다시 소리쳐 부르더니 순식간에 그를 제 품에 끌어안았다."], ["How this was done, whether the doing was with him or her, whether she had flown thither conquered by the tenderness of his voice, or he with a violence not likely to give offence had drawn her to his breast, neither of them knew; nor can I declare.", "어떻게 그리되었는지, 그리한 것이 그인지 그인지, 그가 그 목소리의 다정함에 사로잡혀 그리로 날아든 것인지, 아니면 그가 언짢게 할 성싶지 않은 힘으로 그를 제 가슴으로 끌어당긴 것인지, 두 사람 중 누구도 알지 못했고, 나 또한 단언할 수 없다."], ["There was now that sympathy between them which hardly admitted of individual motion.", "이제 두 사람 사이에는 각자 따로 움직인다는 것이 거의 있을 수 없는 그런 공명이 있었다."], ["They were one and the same--one flesh--one spirit--one life.", "두 사람은 하나요 같은 것이었다 — 한 몸, 한 영, 한 생명이었다."]], [["\"Eleanor, my own Eleanor, my own, my wife!\"", "“엘리너, 나의 엘리너, 나의 사람, 나의 아내!”"], ["She ventured to look up at him through her tears, and he, bowing his face down over hers, pressed his lips upon her brow--his virgin lips, which, since a beard first grew upon his chin, had never yet tasted the luxury of a woman's cheek.", "그는 눈물 너머로 그를 올려다볼 용기를 냈고, 그는 제 얼굴을 그의 얼굴 위로 숙여 그의 이마에 입술을 대었다 — 턱에 처음 수염이 난 이래 여태 여인의 뺨이라는 호사를 맛본 적이 없는 그 숫된 입술을."]], [["She had been told that her yea must be yea, or her nay, nay, but she was called on for neither the one nor the other.", "그는 제 ‘예’는 ‘예’여야 하고 제 ‘아니오’는 ‘아니오’여야 한다고 배워 왔으나, 이번에는 그 어느 쪽도 요구받지 않았다."], ["She told Miss Thorne that she was engaged to Mr. Arabin, but no such words had passed between them, no promises had been asked or given.", "그는 손 양에게 자기가 애러빈 씨와 혼인을 약속했다고 말했지만, 두 사람 사이에 그런 말이 오간 적은 없었고 약조를 청하거나 준 일도 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, let me go,\"", "“아, 저를 보내 주세요.”"], ["said she, \"let me go now.", "그가 말했다. “이제 저를 보내 주세요."], ["I am too happy to remain--let me go, that I may be alone.\"", "너무 행복해서 여기 있을 수가 없어요 — 혼자 있게 저를 보내 주세요.”"], ["He did not try to hinder her; he did not repeat the kiss; he did not press another on her lips.", "그는 그를 붙들려 하지 않았다. 입맞춤을 되풀이하지도 않았고, 그의 입술에 또 하나를 누르지도 않았다."], ["He might have done so, had he been so minded.", "마음만 먹었다면 그리할 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["She was now all his own.", "이제 그는 온전히 그의 사람이었다."], ["He took his arm from round her waist, his arm that was trembling with a new delight, and let her go.", "그는 그의 허리를 감았던 팔을, 새로운 기쁨으로 떨리던 그 팔을 거두고 그를 놓아주었다."], ["She fled like a roe to her own chamber, and then, having turned the bolt, she enjoyed the full luxury of her love.", "그는 노루처럼 제 방으로 달아났고, 빗장을 지른 다음 제 사랑의 호사를 남김없이 누렸다."], ["She idolised, almost worshipped this man who had so meekly begged her pardon.", "그는 그토록 온순하게 제게 용서를 빌던 이 사내를 우러렀고, 거의 떠받들다시피 했다."], ["And he was now her own.", "그리고 이제 그는 제 사람이었다."], ["Oh, how she wept and cried and laughed as the hopes and fears and miseries of the last few weeks passed in remembrance through her mind.", "아, 지난 몇 주의 희망과 두려움과 비참함이 기억을 스쳐 지나갈 때 그는 얼마나 울고 흐느끼고 또 웃었던가."]], [["Mr. Slope!", "슬로프 씨라니!"], ["That anyone should have dared to think that she who had been chosen by him could possibly have mated herself with Mr. Slope!", "그이에게 택함을 받은 이 사람이 슬로프 씨와 짝이 되었을 수도 있다고 감히 생각한 자가 있었다니!"], ["That they should have dared to tell him, also, and subject her bright happiness to such needless risk!", "게다가 그런 말을 감히 그이에게까지 전하여, 제 눈부신 행복을 그런 부질없는 위험에 내던졌다니!"], ["And then she smiled with joy as she thought of all the comforts that she could give him--not that he cared for comforts, but that it would be so delicious for her to give.", "그러다가 그는 자기가 그이에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 안락을 떠올리며 기쁘게 미소를 지었다 — 그이가 안락 따위에 마음을 쓰는 사람이어서가 아니라, 베푸는 일이 저로서는 그토록 달콤할 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["She got up and rang for her maid that she might tell her little boy of his new father, and in her own way she did tell him.", "그는 일어나 종을 울려 하녀를 불렀으니, 어린 아들에게 새 아버지가 생긴 일을 일러 주기 위해서였다. 그리고 제 나름의 방식으로 정말 그렇게 일러 주었다."], ["She desired her maid to leave her, in order that she might be alone with her child; and then, while he lay sprawling on the bed, she poured forth the praises, all unmeaning to him, of the man she had selected to guard his infancy.", "그는 아이와 단둘이 있으려고 하녀에게 물러가라 이르고는, 아이가 침대 위에서 팔다리를 뻗고 뒹구는 동안, 이 아이의 어린 시절을 지켜 주도록 자기가 골라 놓은 사내에 대한 찬사를 아이로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말로 쏟아 놓았다."]], [["She could not be happy, however, till she had made Mr. Arabin take the child to himself and thus, as it were, adopt him as his own.", "그러나 그는 애러빈 씨가 이 아이를 제 품에 받아들여 말하자면 제 자식으로 삼게 하기 전에는 행복할 수가 없었다."], ["The moment the idea struck her she took the baby up in her arms and, opening her door, ran quickly down to the drawing-room.", "그 생각이 떠오른 순간 그는 아기를 두 팔에 안아 들고 문을 열어 잰걸음으로 응접실까지 뛰어 내려갔다."], ["She at once found, by his step still pacing on the floor, that he was there, and a glance within the room told her that he was alone.", "여전히 방바닥을 오가는 그의 발소리로 그가 거기 있음을 대번에 알았고, 방 안을 한 번 들여다보고서 그가 혼자임을 알았다."], ["She hesitated a moment and then hurried in with her precious charge.", "그는 잠시 망설이다가 제 귀한 짐을 안고 서둘러 들어갔다."]], [["Mr. Arabin met her in the middle of the room.", "애러빈 씨가 방 한가운데서 그를 맞았다."], ["\"There,\"", "“여기요.”"], ["said she, breathless with her haste; \"there, take him--take him, and love him.\"", "그가 급히 달려오느라 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. “여기, 이 아이를 받으세요 — 받아서 사랑해 주세요.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin took the little fellow from her and, kissing him again and again, prayed God to bless him.", "애러빈 씨는 그에게서 이 작은 사내아이를 받아 들고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추며 하느님께 이 아이를 축복해 주십사 기도했다."], ["\"He shall be all as my own--all as my own,\" said he.", "“이 아이는 온전히 내 자식이 될 겁니다 — 온전히 내 자식이.”"], ["Eleanor, as she stooped to take back her child, kissed the hand that held him and then rushed back with her treasure to her chamber.", "그가 말했다. 엘리너는 아이를 도로 받으려 몸을 숙이면서 아이를 안고 있던 그 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 제 보물을 안고 다시 제 방으로 달려갔다."]], [["It was thus that Mr. Harding's younger daughter was won for the second time.", "하딩 씨의 작은딸이 두 번째로 얻어진 것은 이러하였다."], ["At dinner neither she nor Mr. Arabin were very bright, but their silence occasioned no remark.", "만찬 자리에서 그도 애러빈 씨도 그리 밝지 못했으나, 두 사람의 말없음은 아무 말거리도 되지 않았다."], ["In the drawing-room, as we have before said, she told Miss Thorne what had occurred.", "앞서 말한 대로 응접실에서 그는 손 양에게 있었던 일을 이야기했다."], ["The next morning she returned to Barchester, and Mr. Arabin went over with his budget of news to the archdeacon.", "이튿날 아침 그는 바체스터로 돌아갔고, 애러빈 씨는 소식 보따리를 들고 부주교에게 건너갔다."], ["As Doctor Grantly was not there, he could only satisfy himself by telling Mrs. Grantly how that he intended himself the honour of becoming her brother-in-law.", "그랜틀리 박사가 자리에 없었으므로 그는 그랜틀리 부인에게 자기가 처형의 매제가 되는 영광을 누리고자 한다고 말하는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었다."], ["In the ecstasy of her joy at hearing such tidings Mrs. Grantly vouchsafed him a warmer welcome than any he had yet received from Eleanor.", "그런 소식을 듣고 기쁨에 황홀해진 그랜틀리 부인은 그가 엘리너에게서 여태 받았던 그 어떤 환영보다 더 따뜻한 환영을 그에게 베풀어 주었다."]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“어머나 세상에!”"], ["she exclaimed--it was the general exclamation of the rectory.", "그가 소리쳤다 — 이것은 목사관 전체의 한결같은 외침이었다."], ["\"Poor Eleanor!", "“가엾은 엘리너!"], ["Dear Eleanor!", "사랑스러운 엘리너!"], ["What a monstrous injustice has been done her!", "그 애가 얼마나 터무니없는 억울한 일을 당했는지!"], ["Well, it shall all be made up now.\"", "그래, 이제 다 보상받게 될 거예요.”"], ["And then she thought of the signora.", "그러고 나서 그는 시뇨라를 떠올렸다."], ["\"What lies people tell,\"", "“사람들이 무슨 거짓말을 지어내는 건지.”"], ["she said to herself.", "그는 혼자 이렇게 말했다."]], [["But people in this matter had told no lies at all.", "그러나 이 일에서 사람들은 거짓말이라고는 조금도 하지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX. The Beelzebub Colt", "# 제49장. 베엘제붑이라는 망아지"]], [["When Miss Thorne left the dining-room, Eleanor had formed no intention of revealing to her what had occurred, but when she was seated beside her hostess on the sofa, the secret dropped from her almost unawares.", "손 양이 식당을 나설 때만 해도 엘리너는 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀에게 털어놓을 생각이 조금도 없었다. 그러나 소파에 안주인과 나란히 앉고 보니 그 비밀은 저도 모르는 사이에 입 밖으로 떨어져 나왔다."], ["Eleanor was but a bad hypocrite, and she found herself quite unable to continue talking about Mr. Arabin as though he were a stranger while her heart was full of him.", "엘리너는 위선에 도무지 서툰 사람이었고, 마음이 온통 애러빈 씨로 가득한 판에 그를 남 이야기하듯 계속 이야기한다는 것은 도저히 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["When Miss Thorne, pursuing her own scheme with discreet zeal, asked the young widow whether, in her opinion, it would not be a good thing for Mr. Arabin to get married, she had nothing for it but to confess the truth.", "손 양이 조심스러운 열의로 제 계획을 밀고 나가며 애러빈 씨가 혼인을 하는 것이 좋지 않겠느냐고 젊은 미망인의 의견을 물었을 때, 엘리너로서는 사실을 고백하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["\"I suppose it would,\"", "“그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Eleanor rather sheepishly.", "엘리너가 조금 겸연쩍게 말했다."], ["Whereupon Miss Thorne amplified on the idea.", "그러자 손 양은 그 생각을 한층 넓혀 이야기했다. “아, 손 양.”"], ["\"Oh, Miss Thorne,\"", "엘리너가 말했다."], ["said Eleanor, \"he is going to be married: I am engaged to him.\"", "“그분은 혼인을 하실 거예요. 제가 그분과 약혼했답니다.”"]], [["Now Miss Thorne knew very well that there had been no such engagement when she had been walking with Mrs. Bold in the morning.", "그런데 손 양은 그날 아침 볼드 부인과 함께 거닐 때만 해도 그런 약혼 따위는 없었다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."], ["She had also heard enough to be tolerably sure that there had been no preliminaries to such an engagement.", "또한 그런 약혼에 이르는 사전 절차조차 없었으리라는 것을 어지간히 확신할 만큼은 들어 알고 있었다."], ["She was, therefore, as we have before described, taken a little by surprise.", "그러니 앞서 우리가 적어 둔 대로 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐던 것이다."], ["But nevertheless, she embraced her guest and cordially congratulated her.", "그럼에도 그녀는 손님을 끌어안고 진심으로 축하해 주었다."]], [["Eleanor had no opportunity of speaking another word to Mr.", "엘리너는 그날 저녁 애러빈 씨에게 다른 말을 한마디도 건넬 기회가 없었다."], ["Arabin that evening, except such words as all the world might hear; and these, as may be supposed, were few enough.", "온 세상이 들어도 좋을 말이라면 몰라도, 그런 말이 몇 마디나 되었겠는가."], ["Miss Thorne did her best to leave them in privacy, but Mr. Thorne, who knew nothing of what had occurred, and another guest, a friend of his, entirely interfered with her good intentions.", "손 양은 두 사람을 단둘이 있게 해 주려고 애를 썼으나, 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 모르는 손 씨와 또 한 사람의 손님, 곧 그의 벗이 그 갸륵한 뜻을 아주 망쳐 놓았다."], ["So poor Eleanor had to go to bed without one sign of affection.", "그리하여 가엾은 엘리너는 애정의 표시 하나 받지 못한 채 잠자리에 들어야 했다."], ["Her state, nevertheless, was not to be pitied.", "그러나 그녀의 처지를 딱하다고 할 것은 아니었다."]], [["The next morning she was up early.", "이튿날 아침 그녀는 일찍 일어났다."], ["It was probable, she thought, that by going down a little before the usual hour of breakfast she might find Mr. Arabin alone in the dining-room.", "평소의 아침 식사 시간보다 조금 일찍 내려가면 식당에서 애러빈 씨를 혼자 만날 수 있을지도 모른다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["Might it not be that he also would calculate that an interview would thus be possible?", "그이 역시 그렇게 하면 마주할 수 있으리라고 헤아리고 있지는 않을까?"], ["Thus thinking, Eleanor was dressed a full hour before the time fixed in the Ullathorne household for morning prayers.", "이런 생각으로 엘리너는 울라손 집안의 아침 기도 시각보다 꼬박 한 시간이나 먼저 옷을 갖추어 입었다."], ["She did not at once go down.", "그러고도 곧바로 내려가지는 않았다."], ["She was afraid to seem to be too anxious to meet her lover, though heaven knows her anxiety was intense enough.", "연인을 만나고 싶어 안달하는 것처럼 보일까 겁이 났던 것이다. 하기야 그녀의 안달이 얼마나 대단했는지는 하늘이 알 일이었다."], ["She therefore sat herself down at her window, and repeatedly looking at her watch, nursed her child till she thought she might venture forth.", "그래서 그녀는 창가에 앉아 몇 번이고 시계를 들여다보면서, 이제는 나서도 되겠다 싶어질 때까지 아이에게 젖을 물리고 있었다."]], [["When she found herself at the dining-room door, she stood a moment, hesitating to turn the handle; but when she heard Mr.", "식당 문 앞에 이르러 그녀는 손잡이를 돌리기를 망설이며 잠시 서 있었다."], ["Thorne's voice inside she hesitated no longer.", "그러나 안에서 손 씨의 목소리가 들리자 더는 망설이지 않았다."], ["Her object was defeated, and she might now go in as soon as she liked without the slightest imputation on her delicacy. Mr.", "목적은 어그러졌고, 이제는 조심스러운 처신에 조금도 흠이 갈 염려 없이 아무 때나 들어가도 좋았다."], ["Thorne and Mr. Arabin were standing on the hearth-rug, discussing the merits of the Beelzebub colt; or rather, Mr.", "손 씨와 애러빈 씨는 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 베엘제붑 망아지의 됨됨이를 논하고 있었다."], ["Thorne was discussing, and Mr. Arabin was listening.", "아니, 정확히는 손 씨가 논하고 애러빈 씨는 듣고 있었다."], ["That interesting animal had rubbed the stump of his tail against the wall of his stable and occasioned much uneasiness to the Ullathorne master of the horse.", "그 흥미로운 짐승은 마구간 벽에 꼬리 밑동을 문질러 대어 울라손의 말 관리 책임자에게 적잖은 불안을 안겨 준 참이었다."], ["Had Eleanor but waited another minute, Mr. Thorne would have been in the stables.", "엘리너가 1분만 더 기다렸던들 손 씨는 마구간에 가 있었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Thorne, when he saw his lady guest, repressed his anxiety.", "손 씨는 여자 손님을 보자 제 근심을 눌러 두었다."], ["The Beelzebub colt must do without him.", "베엘제붑 망아지는 그 없이 견뎌야 했다."], ["And so the three stood, saying little or nothing to each other, till at last the master of the house, finding that he could no longer bear his present state of suspense respecting his favourite young steed, made an elaborate apology to Mrs.", "그리하여 세 사람은 서로 이렇다 할 말도 없이 서 있었는데, 마침내 주인은 아끼는 어린 말을 두고 조마조마해하는 지금의 상태를 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 볼드 부인에게 정성 들여 양해를 구하고 빠져나갔다."], ["Bold and escaped. As he shut the door behind him Eleanor almost wished that he had remained.", "그가 등 뒤로 문을 닫자 엘리너는 차라리 그가 남아 있었더라면 싶었다."], ["It was not that she was afraid of Mr. Arabin, but she hardly yet knew how to address him.", "애러빈 씨가 두려워서가 아니라, 그에게 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지 아직 알 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["He, however, soon relieved her from her embarrassment.", "그러나 그는 곧 그녀를 그 난처함에서 건져 주었다."], ["He came up to her, and taking both her hands in his, he said, \"So, Eleanor, you and I are to be man and wife.", "그가 다가와 그녀의 두 손을 제 손에 쥐고 말했다. “자, 엘리너, 당신과 나는 부부가 되는 것이지요."], ["Is it so?\"", "그렇지요?”"]], [["She looked up into his face, and her lips formed themselves into a single syllable.", "그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그녀의 입술은 한 음절을 지어 보였다."], ["She uttered no sound, but he could read the affirmative plainly in her face.", "소리는 내지 않았으나 그는 그 얼굴에서 그렇다는 대답을 또렷이 읽을 수 있었다."]], [["\"It is a great trust,\"", "“이것은 큰 소임입니다.”"], ["said he, \"a very great trust.\"", "그가 말했다. “참으로 큰 소임이지요.”"]], [["\"It is--it is,\"", "“그래요, 그래요.”"], ["said Eleanor, not exactly taking what he had said in the sense that he had meant.", "엘리너가 말했다. 그가 한 말을 그가 뜻한 그대로의 뜻으로 받아들인 것은 아니었다."], ["\"It is a very, very great trust, and I will do my utmost to deserve it.\"", "“참으로, 참으로 큰 소임이지요. 저는 그에 값하도록 온 힘을 다하겠어요.”"]], [["\"And I also will do my utmost to deserve it,\"", "“나 또한 그에 값하도록 온 힘을 다하겠습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Arabin very solemnly.", "애러빈 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["And then, winding his arm round her waist, he stood there gazing at the fire, and she, with her head leaning on his shoulder, stood by him, well satisfied with her position.", "그러고는 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두른 채 그 자리에 서서 불을 바라보았고, 그녀는 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 그 곁에 서서 제 자리에 흡족해했다."], ["They neither of them spoke, or found any want of speaking.", "둘 다 말이 없었고, 말이 아쉽지도 않았다."], ["All that was needful for them to say had been said.", "서로에게 해야 할 말은 이미 다 한 뒤였다."], ["The yea, yea, had been spoken by Eleanor in her own way--and that way had been perfectly satisfactory to Mr. Arabin.", "그렇다, 그렇다는 대답을 엘리너는 제 나름의 방식으로 했고, 그 방식은 애러빈 씨에게 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 것이었다."]], [["And now it remained to them each to enjoy the assurance of the other's love.", "그리고 이제 두 사람에게는 서로의 사랑을 확신하는 그 기쁨을 누리는 일이 남아 있었다."], ["And how great that luxury is!", "그 호사가 얼마나 큰 것인가!"], ["How far it surpasses any other pleasure which God has allowed to his creatures!", "하느님이 그 피조물에게 허락하신 다른 어떤 즐거움도 그것에 얼마나 미치지 못하는가!"], ["And to a woman's heart how doubly delightful!", "그리고 여자의 마음에는 그것이 곱절로 감미로운 것이다!"]], [["When the ivy has found its tower, when the delicate creeper has found its strong wall, we know how the parasite plants grow and prosper.", "담쟁이가 제 탑을 찾았을 때, 여린 덩굴이 제 튼튼한 담을 찾았을 때, 그 붙어사는 식물들이 어떻게 자라고 번성하는지를 우리는 안다."], ["They were not created to stretch forth their branches alone, and endure without protection the summer's sun and the winter's storm.", "그것들은 홀로 가지를 뻗어 여름의 볕과 겨울의 폭풍을 보호도 없이 견디도록 지어진 것이 아니다."], ["Alone they but spread themselves on the ground and cower unseen in the dingy shade.", "홀로 있으면 그것들은 땅바닥에 퍼져 침침한 그늘 속에 눈에 띄지 않게 웅크릴 뿐이다."], ["But when they have found their firm supporters, how wonderful is their beauty; how all-pervading and victorious!", "그러나 든든한 버팀목을 찾고 나면 그 아름다움이 얼마나 놀라운가. 얼마나 온 사방에 뻗치며 얼마나 당당한가!"], ["What is the turret without its ivy, or the high garden wall without the jasmine which gives it its beauty and fragrance?", "담쟁이 없는 망루가 무엇이며, 아름다움과 향기를 주는 재스민 없는 높다란 정원 담이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["The hedge without the honeysuckle is but a hedge.", "인동덩굴 없는 산울타리는 그저 산울타리일 뿐이다."]], [["There is a feeling still half-existing, but now half-conquered by the force of human nature, that a woman should be ashamed of her love till the husband's right to her compels her to acknowledge it.", "아직도 반쯤은 남아 있으나 이제 인간 본성의 힘에 반쯤은 꺾인 생각이 하나 있다. 여자는 남편의 권리가 그것을 인정하도록 강요하기 전까지는 제 사랑을 부끄러워해야 한다는 생각이다."], ["We would fain preach a different doctrine.", "우리는 기꺼이 그와 다른 교리를 설파하고자 한다."], ["A woman should glory in her love, but on that account let her take the more care that it be such as to justify her glory.", "여자는 제 사랑을 자랑스러워해야 한다. 다만 그런 까닭에, 그 사랑이 제 자랑에 값하는 것이 되도록 더욱 조심할 일이다."]], [["Eleanor did glory in hers, and she felt, and had cause to feel, that it deserved to be held as glorious.", "엘리너는 실로 제 사랑을 자랑스러워했고, 그것이 자랑스러운 것으로 여겨질 만하다고 느꼈으며 또 그렇게 느낄 만한 근거가 있었다."], ["She could have stood there for hours with his arm round her, had fate and Mr. Thorne permitted it.", "운명과 손 씨가 허락하기만 했다면 그녀는 그의 팔에 안긴 채 몇 시간이라도 그렇게 서 있을 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Each moment she crept nearer to his bosom and felt more and more certain that there was her home.", "순간순간 그녀는 그의 품으로 더 가까이 파고들었고, 제 집이 바로 여기라는 것을 점점 더 확신했다."], ["What now to her was the archdeacon's arrogance, her sister's coldness, or her dear father's weakness?", "이제 그녀에게 부주교의 오만함이며 언니의 냉랭함이며 사랑하는 아버지의 유약함이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What need she care for the duplicity of such friends as Charlotte Stanhope?", "샬럿 스탠호프 같은 벗들의 표리부동을 무엇 하러 마음에 두겠는가?"], ["She had found the strong shield that should guard her from all wrongs, the trusty pilot that should henceforward guide her through the shoals and rocks.", "그녀는 온갖 부당함에서 자기를 지켜 줄 튼튼한 방패를, 이제부터 여울과 암초 사이로 자기를 이끌어 줄 믿음직한 키잡이를 찾아낸 것이다."], ["She would give up the heavy burden of her independence, and once more assume the position of a woman and the duties of a trusting and loving wife.", "그녀는 홀로 서는 무거운 짐을 내려놓고, 다시 한번 여자의 자리와 믿고 사랑하는 아내의 본분을 떠맡으려 했다."]], [["And he, too, stood there fully satisfied with his place.", "그리고 그 역시 제 자리에 온전히 만족하여 그곳에 서 있었다."], ["They were both looking intently on the fire, as though they could read there their future fate, till at last Eleanor turned her face towards his.", "두 사람은 마치 거기서 제 앞날의 운명을 읽어 낼 수 있기라도 한 듯 불을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 마침내 엘리너가 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸다. “어쩌면 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요.”"], ["\"How sad you are,\"", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["she said, smiling; and indeed his face was, if not sad, at least serious.", "실로 그의 얼굴은 슬프다고까지는 못 해도 적어도 심각하기는 했다."], ["\"How sad you are, love!\"", "“어쩌면 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요, 내 사랑.”"]], [["\"Sad,\"", "“슬프다니요.”"], ["said he, looking down at her; \"no, certainly not sad.\"", "그가 그녀를 내려다보며 말했다. “아니오, 결코 슬프지 않습니다.”"], ["Her sweet, loving eyes were turned towards him, and she smiled softly as he answered her.", "그녀의 달콤하고 사랑스러운 눈이 그를 향하고 있었고, 그가 대답하는 동안 그녀는 부드럽게 미소 지었다."], ["The temptation was too strong even for the demure propriety of Mr. Arabin, and bending over her, he pressed his lips to hers.", "그 유혹은 애러빈 씨의 조신한 예법으로도 당해 낼 수 없을 만큼 강했고, 그는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혀 제 입술을 그녀의 입술에 갖다 대었다."]], [["Immediately after this Mr.", "이 일이 있고 곧바로 손 씨가 나타났다."], ["Thorne appeared, and they were both delighted to hear that the tail of the Beelzebub colt was not materially injured.", "그리고 두 사람은 베엘제붑 망아지의 꼬리가 크게 상하지는 않았다는 소식을 듣고 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다."]], [["It had been Mr. Harding's intention to hurry over to Ullathorne as soon as possible after his return to Barchester, in order to secure the support of his daughter in his meditated revolt against the archdeacon as touching the deanery; but he was spared the additional journey by hearing that Mrs.", "하딩 씨는 바체스터로 돌아오는 대로 되도록 빨리 울라손으로 달려갈 작정이었다."], ["Bold had returned unexpectedly home.", "수석사제직을 두고 부주교에게 반기를 들려는 참이라 딸의 지지를 얻어 두기 위해서였다."], ["As soon as he had read her note he started off, and found her waiting for him in her own house.", "그러나 볼드 부인이 뜻밖에 집으로 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고 그 걸음을 덜게 되었다. 딸의 쪽지를 읽자마자 그는 길을 나섰고, 제 집에서 자기를 기다리고 있는 딸을 만났다."]], [["How much each of them had to tell the other, and how certain each was that the story which he or she had to tell would astonish the other!", "두 사람이 서로에게 들려줄 이야기가 얼마나 많았으며, 제가 들려줄 이야기가 상대를 깜짝 놀라게 하리라고 두 사람 다 얼마나 확신하고 있었던가!"]], [["\"My dear, I am so anxious to see you,\"", "“얘야, 어찌나 너를 보고 싶었는지 모른다.”"], ["said Mr. Harding, kissing his daughter.", "하딩 씨가 딸에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Papa, I have so much to tell you!\"", "“아버지, 저도 드릴 말씀이 얼마나 많은지 몰라요!”"], ["said the daughter, returning the embrace.", "딸이 그 포옹에 답하며 말했다."]], [["\"My dear, they have offered me the deanery!\" said Mr.", "“얘야, 저쪽에서 나에게 수석사제 자리를 주겠다는구나!”"], ["Harding, anticipating by the suddenness of the revelation the tidings which Eleanor had to give him.", "하딩 씨가 말했다. 그 느닷없는 고백으로 그는 엘리너가 전할 소식보다 앞서 나가 버렸다."]], [["\"Oh, Papa,\"", "“어머, 아버지.”"], ["said she, forgetting her own love and happiness in her joy at the surprising news.", "그녀가 말했다. 놀라운 소식이 안겨 준 기쁨에 제 사랑과 행복은 잊어버린 것이었다."], ["\"Oh, Papa, can it be possible?", "“어머, 아버지, 그게 정말인가요?"], ["Dear Papa, how thoroughly, thoroughly happy that makes me!\"", "아버지, 그 소식에 저는 얼마나, 얼마나 마음이 온통 기쁜지 몰라요!”"]], [["\"But, my dear, I think it best to refuse it.\"", "“그런데 얘야, 나는 그 자리를 사양하는 것이 좋겠다고 생각한단다.”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa!\"", "“어머, 아버지!”"]], [["\"I am sure you will agree with me, Eleanor, when I explain it to you.", "“내가 설명해 주면 너도 내 생각에 동의할 게다, 엘리너."], ["You know, my dear, how old I am.", "얘야, 내가 몇 살인지 너도 알지 않느냐."], ["If I live I--\"", "앞으로 산다 한들 나는—”"]], [["\"But, Papa, I must tell you about myself.\"", "“하지만 아버지, 제 이야기도 말씀드려야 해요.”"]], [["\"Well, my dear.\"", "“그래, 얘야.”"]], [["\"I do so wonder how you'll take it.\"", "“아버지가 이 일을 어떻게 받아들이실지 정말 궁금해요.”"]], [["\"Take what?\"", "“무엇을 받아들인단 말이냐?”"]], [["\"If you don't rejoice at it, if it doesn't make you happy, if you don't encourage me, I shall break my heart.\"", "“아버지가 기뻐해 주지 않으시면, 그 일이 아버지를 행복하게 해 드리지 못하면, 아버지가 저를 북돋아 주지 않으시면, 저는 가슴이 무너지고 말 거예요.”"]], [["\"If that be the case, Nelly, I certainly will encourage you.\"", "“그런 일이라면 넬리, 나야 반드시 너를 북돋아 주고말고.”"]], [["\"But I fear you won't.", "“하지만 그러지 않으실까 봐 겁이 나요."], ["I do so fear you won't.", "정말이지 그러지 않으실까 봐 겁이 나요."], ["And yet you can't but think I am the most fortunate woman living on God's earth.\"", "그렇지만 아버지도 제가 하느님의 땅 위에 사는 여자 가운데 가장 복 받은 여자라고 여기지 않으실 수 없을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Are you, dearest?", "“정말 그러냐, 우리 딸?"], ["Then I certainly will rejoice with you.", "그렇다면 나도 반드시 너와 함께 기뻐하마."], ["Come, Nelly, come to me and tell me what it is.\"", "자, 넬리, 이리 오너라. 그게 무슨 일인지 아비에게 말해 보렴.”"]], [["\"I am going--\"", "“저는 이제—”"]], [["He led her to the sofa and, seating himself beside her, took both her hands in his.", "그는 딸을 소파로 이끌고 그 곁에 앉아 딸의 두 손을 제 손에 쥐었다."], ["\"You are going to be married, Nelly.", "“너 혼인을 하려는 게로구나, 넬리."], ["Is not that it?\"", "그렇지 않으냐?”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["she said faintly.", "그녀가 가냘프게 말했다."], ["\"That is, if you will approve;\"", "“아버지가 허락해 주신다면요.”"], ["and then she blushed as she remembered the promise which she had so lately volunteered to him and which she had so utterly forgotten in making her engagement with Mr. Arabin.", "그러고는 얼마 전에 제 입으로 아버지께 드린 약속, 애러빈 씨와 약혼하면서 까맣게 잊어버렸던 그 약속이 떠올라 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["Mr. Harding thought for a moment who the man could be whom he was to be called upon to welcome as his son-in-law.", "하딩 씨는 사위로 맞아들이라고 청함을 받게 될 사내가 대체 누구일까 잠시 생각해 보았다."], ["A week since he would have had no doubt whom to name.", "일주일 전이었다면 누구의 이름을 댈지 의심할 것도 없었으리라."], ["In that case he would have been prepared to give his sanction, although he would have done so with a heavy heart.", "그런 경우라면 그는 무거운 마음으로일망정 승낙할 채비가 되어 있었을 것이다."], ["Now he knew that at any rate it would not be Mr. Slope, though he was perfectly at a loss to guess who could possibly have filled the place.", "이제 그는 적어도 슬로프 씨는 아니라는 것을 알고 있었으나, 그렇다면 그 자리를 채울 만한 사람이 대체 누구일지 도무지 짐작할 수가 없었다."], ["For a moment he thought that the man might be Bertie Stanhope, and his very soul sank within him.", "한순간 그는 그 사내가 버티 스탠호프일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었고, 그러자 그의 넋이 아주 내려앉았다."]], [["\"Well, Nelly?\"", "“그래, 넬리?”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, promise to me that, for my sake, you will love him.\"", "“아버지, 저를 봐서라도 그이를 사랑해 주시겠다고 약속해 주세요.”"]], [["\"Come, Nelly, come; tell me who it is.\"", "“자, 넬리, 어서. 그이가 누구인지 말해 보렴.”"]], [["\"But will you love him, Papa?\"", "“그렇지만 그이를 사랑해 주실 거지요, 아버지?”"]], [["\"Dearest, I must love anyone that you love.\"", "“얘야, 네가 사랑하는 사람이라면 나도 사랑할 수밖에 없지 않겠느냐.”"], ["Then she turned her face to his and whispered into his ear the name of Mr. Arabin.", "그러자 그녀는 아버지 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려 그 귀에 애러빈 씨의 이름을 속삭였다."]], [["No man that she could have named could have more surprised or more delighted him.", "그녀가 댈 수 있었을 어떤 이름도 그를 그보다 더 놀라게 하거나 더 기쁘게 할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["Had he looked round the world for a son-in-law to his taste, he could have selected no one whom he would have preferred to Mr. Arabin.", "제 마음에 드는 사위를 찾아 온 세상을 둘러보았다 한들 애러빈 씨보다 더 마음에 드는 사람은 하나도 고를 수 없었으리라."], ["He was a clergyman; he held a living in the neighbourhood; he was of a set to which all Mr.", "그는 성직자였다. 그는 이웃에 성직록을 가지고 있었다."], ["Harding's own partialities most closely adhered; he was the great friend of Dr.", "그는 하딩 씨 자신의 온갖 애착이 가장 가까이 붙어 있는 부류에 속했다. 그는 그랜틀리 박사의 둘도 없는 벗이었다."], ["Grantly; and he was, moreover, a man of whom Mr. Harding knew nothing but what he approved.", "게다가 그는 하딩 씨가 그에 관하여 마땅찮게 여길 것이라고는 아무것도 알지 못하는 사람이었다."], ["Nevertheless, his surprise was so great as to prevent the immediate expression of his joy.", "그럼에도 그의 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지 기쁨을 곧바로 드러내지 못할 지경이었다."], ["He had never thought of Mr. Arabin in connexion with his daughter; he had never imagined that they had any feeling in common.", "그는 애러빈 씨를 제 딸과 결부해 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["He had feared that his daughter had been made hostile to clergymen of Mr.", "두 사람에게 무슨 공통된 감정이 있으리라고는 상상조차 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Arabin's stamp by her intolerance of the archdeacon's pretensions. Had he been put to wish, he might have wished for Mr.", "그는 딸이 부주교의 허세를 못 견뎌 하는 통에 애러빈 씨 같은 유의 성직자에게 적의를 품게 되었으리라고 걱정하고 있었다."], ["Arabin for a son-in-law; but had he been put to guess, the name would never have occurred to him.", "바라 보라고 했다면 그는 애러빈 씨를 사위로 바랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 알아맞혀 보라고 했다면 그 이름은 결코 떠오르지 않았을 것이다."]], [["\"Mr. Arabin!\"", "“애러빈 씨라니!”"], ["he exclaimed; \"impossible!\"", "그가 외쳤다. “그럴 리가 없다!”"]], [["\"Oh, Papa, for heaven's sake don't say anything against him!", "“아, 아버지, 부디 그이를 나쁘게 말씀하지 말아 주세요!"], ["If you love me, don't say anything against him.", "저를 사랑하신다면 그이를 나쁘게 말씀하지 말아 주세요."], ["Oh, Papa, it's done and mustn't be undone--oh, Papa!\"", "아, 아버지, 이미 다 된 일이고 되돌려서도 안 되는 일이에요. 아, 아버지!”"]], [["Fickle Eleanor!", "변덕스러운 엘리너여!"], ["Where was the promise that she would make no choice for herself without her father's approval?", "아버지의 승낙 없이는 혼자서 아무것도 정하지 않겠다던 그 약속은 어디로 갔는가?"], ["She had chosen, and now demanded his acquiescence.", "그녀는 이미 정해 놓고서 이제 와 아버지의 묵인을 요구하고 있었다."], ["\"Oh, Papa, isn't he good?", "“아, 아버지, 그이는 훌륭하지 않아요?"], ["Isn't he noble?", "고결하지 않아요?"], ["Isn't he religious, high-minded, everything that a good man possibly can be?\"", "신앙 깊고 높은 뜻을 지녔고, 좋은 사람이 갖출 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추지 않았어요?”"], ["She clung to her father, beseeching him for his consent.", "그녀는 아버지에게 매달려 승낙해 달라고 애원했다."]], [["\"My Nelly, my child, my own daughter!", "“내 넬리, 내 아이, 내 귀한 딸아!"], ["He is; he is noble and good and high-minded; he is all that a woman can love and a man admire.", "그렇고말고, 그이는 고결하고 훌륭하고 높은 뜻을 지녔지. 여자가 사랑할 만하고 남자가 우러러볼 만한 것을 다 갖추었지."], ["He shall be my son, my own son.", "그이는 내 아들이 될 게다, 내 친아들이."], ["He shall be as close to my heart as you are.", "그이는 너만큼이나 내 마음에 가까운 사람이 될 게다."], ["My Nelly, my child, my happy, happy child!\"", "내 넬리, 내 아이, 행복한, 행복한 내 아이야!”"]], [["We need not pursue the interview any further.", "이 만남을 더 좇을 필요는 없겠다."], ["By degrees they returned to the subject of the new promotion.", "두 사람은 차츰 새 승진 이야기로 돌아갔다."], ["Eleanor tried to prove to him, as the Grantlys had done, that his age could be no bar to his being a very excellent dean, but those arguments had now even less weight on him than before.", "엘리너는 그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 그랬듯이 나이가 아주 훌륭한 수석 사제가 되는 데 걸림돌이 될 수는 없다는 것을 아버지에게 입증해 보이려 했으나, 그런 논거는 이제 전보다도 그에게 힘을 쓰지 못했다."], ["He said little or nothing but sat, meditative.", "그는 이렇다 할 말 없이 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Every now and then he would kiss his daughter and say \"yes,\" or \"no,\"", "이따금 딸에게 입을 맞추고 “그래”라거나 “아니다”라거나 “참으로 그렇구나”라거나 “글쎄다, 얘야, 그 점은 나도 썩 동의하지는 못하겠구나”"], ["or \"very true,\" or \"well, my dear, I can't quite agree with you there,\"", "하고 말할 뿐, 바체스터 수석 사제가 “될 것이냐 말 것이냐”"], ["but he could not be got to enter sharply into the question of \"to be, or not to be\" Dean of Barchester.", "하는 문제를 놓고 딱 부러지게 이야기하도록 그를 끌어들일 수는 없었다."], ["Of her and her happiness, of Mr. Arabin and his virtues, he would talk as much as Eleanor desired--and to tell the truth, that was not a little--but about the deanery he would now say nothing further.", "딸과 딸의 행복에 관하여, 애러빈 씨와 그의 미덕에 관하여라면 그는 엘리너가 바라는 만큼 얼마든지 이야기했고 — 사실대로 말하자면 그 바라는 바가 적지 않았다 — 그러나 수석사제직에 관해서는 이제 더 아무 말도 하지 않으려 했다."], ["He had got a new idea into his head--why should not Mr.", "그의 머릿속에 새로운 생각 하나가 들어앉은 것이다."], ["Arabin be the new dean?", "애러빈 씨가 새 수석 사제가 되면 어떻겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER L. The Archdeacon Is Satisfied with the State of Affairs", "# 제50장. 부주교가 사태에 만족하다"]], [["The archdeacon, in his journey into Barchester, had been assured by Mr.", "부주교는 바체스터로 가는 길에 하딩 씨로부터 슬로프 씨와 엘리너에 관한 그들의 온갖 예측이 근거 없는 것이라는 확언을 들은 터였다."], ["Harding that all their prognostications about Mr. Slope and Eleanor were groundless.", "그러나 하딩 씨로서는 제 통찰력에 대한 사위의 믿음을 흔들어 놓기가 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["Mr. Harding, however, had found it very difficult to shake his son-in-law's faith in his own acuteness.", "그랜틀리 박사에게는 그 일이 너무나도 훤한 증거로 뒷받침되고 끝도 없는 정황으로 입증된 것이었던 터라, 그는 처음에는 엘리너가 그 혐의를 제 입으로 부인하더라는 하딩 씨의 단호한 진술을 사실로 받아들이기를 거부했다."], ["The matter had, to Dr.", "그러나 마침내 그는 단서를 붙여 물러섰다."], ["Grantly, been so plainly corroborated by such patent evidence, borne out by such endless circumstances, that he at first refused to take as true the positive statement which Mr. Harding made to him of Eleanor's own disavowal of the impeachment.", "그는 지금으로서는 지난날의 확신을 착오로 여기겠노라고 인정하기에 이르렀는데, 그러면서도 어찌나 빠져나갈 구멍을 남겨 두었던지, 훗날 언제고 엘리너가 슬로프 부인이 되어 세상에 나서는 일이 생긴다면 여전히 이렇게 말할 수 있을 정도였다."], ["But at last he yielded in a qualified way.", "“거 보십시오, 제가 그러지 않았습니까."], ["He brought himself to admit that he would at the present regard his past convictions as a mistake, but in doing this he so guarded himself that if, at any future time, Eleanor should come forth to the world as Mrs. Slope, he might still be able to say: \"There, I told you so.", "그때 뭐라고 하셨고 제가 뭐라고 했는지 기억해 두십시오."], ["Remember what you said and what I said; and remember also for coming years, that I was right in this matter--as in all others.\"", "그리고 앞으로 두고두고 기억해 두십시오, 이 일에서도 제가 옳았다는 것을 — 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 말입니다.”"]], [["He carried, however, his concession so far as to bring himself to undertake to call at Eleanor's house, and he did call accordingly, while the father and daughter were yet in the middle of their conference. Mr.", "그러나 그는 양보를 밀고 나가 엘리너의 집에 들르겠노라고 스스로 나서기까지 했고, 실제로 아버지와 딸이 아직 이야기를 한창 나누고 있는 중에 찾아왔다."], ["Harding had had so much to hear and to say that he had forgotten to advise Eleanor of the honour that awaited her, and she heard her brother-in-law's voice in the hall while she was quite unprepared to see him.", "하딩 씨는 들을 말도 할 말도 너무 많았던 터라 그런 영광이 기다리고 있다는 것을 엘리너에게 미리 일러 주는 것을 잊고 있었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그를 맞을 채비가 전혀 되지 않은 채로 현관에서 형부의 목소리를 들었다."]], [["\"There's the archdeacon,\"", "“부주교님이 오셨어요.”"], ["she said, springing up.", "그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, my dear.", "“그래, 얘야."], ["He told me to tell you that he would come and see you; but to tell the truth I had forgotten all about it.\"", "너를 보러 오겠다고 나더러 전하라 하더구나. 그런데 솔직히 말하면 나는 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었단다.”"]], [["Eleanor fled away, regardless of all her father's entreaties.", "엘리너는 아버지가 아무리 붙드는데도 아랑곳없이 달아나 버렸다."], ["She could not now, in the first hours of her joy, bring herself to bear all the archdeacon's retractions, apologies, and congratulations.", "기쁨에 젖은 그 첫 시간에 그녀로서는 부주교의 온갖 철회와 사과와 축하를 도저히 견뎌 낼 수가 없었던 것이다. 그는 할 말이 무척 많을 터였고, 그것을 말하는 품이 어지간히 지루할 터였다."], ["He would have so much to say, and would be so tedious in saying it; consequently, the archdeacon, when he was shown into the drawing-room, found no one there but Mr. Harding.", "그리하여 부주교는 응접실로 안내되었을 때 거기서 하딩 씨 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다."]], [["\"You must excuse Eleanor,\"", "“엘리너는 좀 양해해 주시게.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Is anything the matter?\"", "“무슨 일이라도 있습니까?” 박사가 물었다."], ["asked the doctor, who at once anticipated that the whole truth about Mr. Slope had at last come out.", "그는 대뜸 슬로프 씨에 관한 진상이 마침내 죄다 드러난 모양이라고 넘겨짚었던 것이다."]], [["\"Well, something is the matter.", "“그래, 일이 좀 있네."], ["I wonder now whether you will be much surprised.\"", "자네가 얼마나 놀랄지 나는 그것이 궁금하구먼.”"]], [["The archdeacon saw by his father-in-law's manner that after all he had nothing to tell him about Mr.", "부주교는 장인의 태도로 미루어 결국 슬로프 씨에 관해서는 자기에게 들려줄 이야기가 없다는 것을 알아차렸다. “아닙니다.”"], ["Slope. \"No,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"certainly not--nothing will ever surprise me again.\"", "“결코 놀라지 않을 겁니다. 이제 저를 놀라게 할 일은 아무것도 없을 테니까요.”"], ["Very many men now-a-days besides the archdeacon adopt or affect to adopt the nil admirari doctrine; but nevertheless, to judge from their appearance, they are just as subject to sudden emotions as their grandfathers and grandmothers were before them.", "요즈음은 부주교 말고도 아주 많은 사람들이 nil admirari 신조를 받아들이거나 받아들이는 척한다. 그럼에도 그 낯빛으로 판단하건대 그들 역시 저희 할아버지 할머니가 그러했던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 갑작스러운 감정에 사로잡히기 마련이다."]], [["\"What do you think Mr. Arabin has done?\"", "“애러빈 씨가 무슨 일을 했는지 자네는 짐작이나 하겠나?”"]], [["\"Mr. Arabin!", "“애러빈 씨라니요!"], ["It's nothing about that daughter of Stanhope's, I hope?\"", "설마 그 스탠호프네 딸에 관한 일은 아니겠지요?”"]], [["\"No, not that woman,\" said Mr.", "“아니, 그 여자는 아닐세.”"], ["Harding, enjoying his joke in his sleeve.", "하딩 씨가 남몰래 제 농담을 즐기며 말했다."]], [["\"Not that woman!", "“그 여자가 아니라니요!"], ["Is he going to do anything about any woman?", "그럼 그가 어떤 여자에 관해 무슨 일이라도 저지른답니까?"], ["Why can't you speak out, if you have anything to say?", "하실 말씀이 있으시면 어째서 툭 털어놓지 않으십니까?"], ["There is nothing I hate so much as these sort of mysteries.\"", "저는 이런 식으로 알쏭달쏭하게 구는 것을 세상에서 제일 싫어합니다.”"]], [["\"There shall be no mystery with you, Archdeacon, though of course it must go no further at present.\"", "“자네에게 수수께끼를 낼 생각은 없네, 부주교. 물론 지금으로서는 더 퍼져 나가서는 안 될 이야기이네만.”"]], [["\"Well.\"", "“말씀해 보시지요.”"]], [["\"Except Susan.", "“수전은 예외로 하고 말일세."], ["You must promise me you'll tell no one else.\"", "그 밖의 누구에게도 말하지 않겠다고 나에게 약속해 주게.”"]], [["\"Nonsense!\"", "“당치 않은 말씀입니다!”"], ["exclaimed the archdeacon, who was becoming angry in his suspense.", "부주교가 소리쳤다. 애를 태우다 못해 슬슬 화가 나던 참이었다."], ["\"You can't have any secret about Mr. Arabin.\"", "“애러빈 씨에 관해서 무슨 비밀이 있을 리가 없지 않습니까.”"]], [["\"Only this--that he and Eleanor are engaged.\"", "“다름이 아니라, 그와 엘리너가 약혼을 했다네.”"]], [["It was quite clear to see, by the archdeacon's face, that he did not believe a word of it.", "부주교의 얼굴을 보면 그가 그 말을 한마디도 믿지 않는다는 것이 훤히 드러났다."], ["\"Mr. Arabin!", "“애러빈 씨가요!"], ["It's impossible!\"", "그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다.”"]], [["\"Eleanor, at any rate, has just now told me so.\"", "“어쨌거나 엘리너가 방금 나에게 그렇게 말했네.”"]], [["\"It's impossible,\"", "“있을 수 없는 일입니다.”"], ["repeated the archdeacon.", "부주교가 되풀이했다."]], [["\"Well, I can't say I think it impossible.", "“글쎄, 나로서는 그것이 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말하지는 못하겠네."], ["It certainly took me by surprise, but that does not make it impossible.\"", "나도 분명 놀라기는 했네만, 그렇다고 있을 수 없는 일이 되는 것은 아니지 않은가.”"]], [["\"She must be mistaken.\"", "“그 사람이 뭔가 잘못 안 것입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Harding assured him that there was no mistake; that he would find, on returning home, that Mr.", "하딩 씨는 잘못 안 것이 결코 아니라고 잘라 말했다."], ["Arabin had been at Plumstead with the express object of making the same declaration; that even Miss Thorne knew all about it; and that, in fact, the thing was as clearly settled as any such arrangement between a lady and a gentleman could well be.", "집에 돌아가 보면 애러빈 씨가 바로 그 이야기를 하겠다는 뚜렷한 목적으로 플럼스테드에 다녀갔다는 것을 알게 될 터이며, 손 양까지도 그 일을 죄다 알고 있고, 실상 그 일은 숙녀와 신사 사이의 그런 약속이 정해질 수 있는 한 더없이 분명하게 정해진 일이라고 했다."]], [["\"Good heavens!\"", "“맙소사!”"], ["said the archdeacon, walking up and down Eleanor's drawing-room.", "부주교가 엘리너의 응접실을 이리저리 오가며 말했다."], ["\"Good heavens!", "“맙소사!"], ["Good heavens!\"", "맙소사!”"]], [["Now these exclamations certainly betokened faith. Mr.", "그런데 이 감탄사들은 분명 믿음을 나타내는 것이었다."], ["Harding properly gathered from it that, at last, Dr. Grantly did believe the fact.", "하딩 씨는 마침내 그랜틀리 박사가 그 사실을 믿게 되었다고 제대로 헤아렸다."], ["The first utterance clearly evinced a certain amount of distaste at the information he had received; the second simply indicated surprise; in the tone of the third Mr. Harding fancied that he could catch a certain gleam of satisfaction.", "첫 번째 감탄은 자기가 들은 소식에 대한 어느 정도의 언짢음을 뚜렷이 드러냈고, 두 번째는 그저 놀라움을 나타냈으며, 세 번째의 어조에서는 어떤 만족의 기색이 언뜻 비치는 것을 하딩 씨가 알아챌 수 있을 듯싶었다."]], [["The archdeacon had truly expressed the workings of his mind.", "부주교는 참으로 제 마음속에서 일어나는 바를 그대로 드러내 보였던 것이다."], ["He could not but be disgusted to find how utterly astray he had been in all his anticipations.", "그는 자기 예상이 온통 얼마나 빗나갔는지를 깨닫고 언짢아하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Had he only been lucky enough to have suggested this marriage himself when he first brought Mr. Arabin into the country, his character for judgement and wisdom would have received an addition which would have classed him at any rate next to Solomon.", "애러빈 씨를 처음 이 고장으로 데려왔을 때 이 혼사를 제 입으로 제안할 만한 복이 있었더라면, 판단력과 지혜에 관한 그의 명성은 적어도 그를 솔로몬 다음가는 자리에 올려놓을 만큼 더해졌으리라."], ["And why had he not done so?", "그런데 어째서 그러지 못했던가?"], ["Might he not have foreseen that Mr. Arabin would want a wife in his parsonage?", "애러빈 씨가 목사관에 안주인을 두고 싶어 하리라는 것쯤은 내다볼 수 있지 않았던가?"], ["He had foreseen that Eleanor would want a husband, but should he not also have perceived that Mr. Arabin was a man much more likely to attract her than Mr.", "엘리너에게 남편이 필요하리라는 것은 내다보았으면서, 애러빈 씨가 슬로프 씨보다 그녀의 마음을 끌기에 훨씬 알맞은 사람이라는 것 또한 알아차렸어야 하지 않았던가?"], ["Slope? The archdeacon found that he had been at fault and, of course, could not immediately get over his discomfiture.", "부주교는 자기에게 잘못이 있었음을 깨달았고, 당연하게도 그 낭패감을 대뜸 떨쳐 버리지는 못했다."]], [["Then his surprise was intense.", "그다음으로 그의 놀라움이 대단했다."], ["How sly this pair of young turtle-doves had been with him. How egregiously they had hoaxed him.", "이 한 쌍의 젊은 멧비둘기가 자기를 상대로 어찌나 시치미를 뗐던지."], ["He had preached to Eleanor against her fancied attachment to Mr. Slope at the very time that she was in love with his own protégé, Mr.", "어찌나 감쪽같이 자기를 속여 넘겼던지."], ["Arabin, and had absolutely taken that same Mr. Arabin into his confidence with reference to his dread of Mr.", "그는 엘리너가 실은 제 후원을 받는 애러빈 씨를 사랑하고 있던 바로 그때에 슬로프 씨에게 마음을 두었다는 억측을 놓고 그녀에게 설교를 늘어놓았으며, 게다가 슬로프 씨와의 결합이 두렵다며 다름 아닌 그 애러빈 씨에게 속을 죄다 털어놓기까지 했던 것이다."], ["Slope's alliance. It was very natural that the archdeacon should feel surprise.", "부주교가 놀라는 것도 지극히 당연한 일이었다."]], [["But there was also great ground for satisfaction.", "그러나 흡족해할 근거 또한 대단히 컸다."], ["Looking at the match by itself, it was the very thing to help the doctor out of his difficulties.", "이 혼사만 따로 놓고 보면 그것은 박사를 곤경에서 건져 내기에 더없이 알맞은 일이었다."], ["In the first place, the assurance that he should never have Mr. Slope for his brother-in-law was in itself a great comfort.", "우선 슬로프 씨를 동서로 맞을 일이 결코 없으리라는 확신 자체가 큰 위안이었다."], ["Then Mr. Arabin was, of all men, the one with whom it would best suit him to be so intimately connected.", "다음으로 애러빈 씨는 그토록 가까운 사이로 얽히기에 그에게 가장 잘 맞는 사람이었다."], ["But the crowning comfort was the blow which this marriage would give to Mr. Slope.", "그러나 무엇보다 큰 위안은 이 혼사가 슬로프 씨에게 안길 타격이었다."], ["He had now certainly lost his wife; rumour was beginning to whisper that he might possibly lose his position in the palace; and if Mr.", "그는 이제 아내를 잃은 것이 분명했다. 어쩌면 주교관에서의 자리마저 잃을지 모른다는 소문이 수군수군 돌기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Harding would only be true, the great danger of all would be surmounted.", "그리고 하딩 씨가 뜻만 굽히지 않는다면 가장 큰 위험도 넘어서게 될 터였다."], ["In such case it might be expected that Mr. Slope would own himself vanquished, and take himself altogether away from Barchester.", "그리 되면 슬로프 씨가 스스로 졌음을 인정하고 바체스터를 아주 떠나 버리리라 기대해 봄 직했다."], ["And so the archdeacon would again be able to breathe pure air.", "그러면 부주교는 다시 맑은 공기를 마실 수 있을 것이었다."]], [["\"Well, well,\" said he.", "“그것 참, 그것 참.”"], ["\"Good heavens!", "그가 말했다."], ["Good heavens!\"", "“맙소사! 맙소사!”"], ["and the tone of the fifth exclamation made Mr. Harding fully aware that content was reigning in the archdeacon's bosom.", "그리고 이 다섯 번째 감탄의 어조에서 하딩 씨는 부주교의 가슴속에 흡족함이 자리 잡았음을 온전히 알아차렸다."]], [["And then slowly, gradually, and craftily Mr. Harding propounded his own new scheme.", "그러고 나서 하딩 씨는 천천히, 차근차근, 그리고 슬며시 자기 나름의 새 계책을 내놓았다."], ["Why should not Mr. Arabin be the new dean?", "애러빈 씨를 새 수석 사제로 앉히면 어떻겠는가?"]], [["Slowly, gradually, and thoughtfully Dr. Grantly fell into his father-in-law's views.", "천천히, 차근차근, 그리고 곰곰이 헤아리며 그랜틀리 박사는 장인의 견해로 기울었다."], ["Much as he liked Mr. Arabin, sincere as was his admiration for that gentleman's ecclesiastical abilities, he would not have sanctioned a measure which would rob his father-in-law of his fairly earned promotion, were it at all practicable to induce his father-in-law to accept the promotion which he had earned.", "그가 애러빈 씨를 아무리 좋아한다 한들, 그 신사의 교회 관련 역량에 대한 그의 감탄이 아무리 진심이라 한들, 장인이 정당하게 얻어 낸 승진을 빼앗는 조치를 그가 승인하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But the archdeacon had, on a former occasion, received proof of the obstinacy with which Mr.", "장인으로 하여금 그 얻어 낸 승진을 받아들이게 하는 일이 조금이라도 될 법한 일이기만 했다면 말이다."], ["Harding could adhere to his own views in opposition to the advice of all his friends.", "그러나 부주교는 예전에 한 번, 하딩 씨가 모든 벗들의 권고를 거스르면서까지 제 뜻을 고집스레 지킬 수 있다는 증거를 받아 든 적이 있었다."], ["He knew tolerably well that nothing would induce the meek, mild man before him to take the high place offered to him, if he thought it wrong to do so.", "눈앞의 이 온순하고 순한 사람이 그것을 그르다고 여기는 한 어떤 수를 써도 제게 주어진 높은 자리를 맡지 않으리라는 것을 그는 어지간히 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Knowing this, he also said to himself more than once: \"Why should not Mr. Arabin be Dean of Barchester?\"", "그것을 알기에 그 역시 속으로 몇 번이나 이렇게 되뇌었다."], ["It was at last arranged between them that they would together start to London by the earliest train on the following morning, making a little detour to Oxford on their journey.", "“애러빈 씨를 바체스터의 수석 사제로 앉히면 어떤가?” 마침내 두 사람 사이에 이야기가 정해져, 이튿날 아침 가장 이른 기차로 함께 런던을 향해 떠나되 가는 길에 옥스퍼드에 잠깐 들르기로 했다."], ["Dr. Gwynne's counsels, they imagined, might perhaps be of assistance to them.", "그윈 박사의 조언이 어쩌면 도움이 되리라고 그들은 생각했던 것이다."]], [["These matters settled, the archdeacon hurried off, that he might return to Plumstead and prepare for his journey.", "이런 일들이 정해지자 부주교는 플럼스테드로 돌아가 여행 채비를 하려고 서둘러 자리를 떴다."], ["The day was extremely fine, and he came into the city in an open gig.", "날이 몹시 좋았던 터라 그는 포장 없는 이륜마차를 타고 시내로 들어와 있었다."], ["As he was driving up the High Street he encountered Mr. Slope at a crossing.", "하이 스트리트를 몰아 올라가다가 그는 건널목에서 슬로프 씨와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["Had he not pulled up rather sharply, he would have run over him.", "고삐를 꽤 급히 당기지 않았더라면 그를 치고 지나갈 뻔했다."], ["The two had never spoken to each other since they had met on a memorable occasion in the bishop's study.", "두 사람은 주교의 서재에서 잊지 못할 마주침을 가진 이래 서로 한마디도 나눈 적이 없었다."], ["They did not speak now, but they looked each other full in the face, and Mr. Slope's countenance was as impudent, as triumphant, as defiant as ever.", "지금도 말은 하지 않았으나 서로 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 슬로프 씨의 낯빛은 여전히 뻔뻔스럽고 여전히 의기양양하며 여전히 도전적이었다."], ["Had Dr. Grantly not known to the contrary, he would have imagined that his enemy had won the deanship, the wife, and all the rich honours for which he had been striving.", "그랜틀리 박사가 사정을 달리 알고 있지 않았더라면, 제 적수가 수석 사제 자리며 아내며 그가 얻으려 애쓰던 온갖 값진 영예를 죄다 손에 넣었으려니 여겼을 것이다."], ["As it was, he had lost everything that he had in the world, and had just received his congé from the bishop.", "그러나 실상 그는 이 세상에서 가진 것을 몽땅 잃었고, 방금 주교에게서 congé를 받아 든 참이었다."]], [["In leaving the town the archdeacon drove by the well-remembered entrance of Hiram's Hospital.", "시내를 벗어나면서 부주교는 눈에 익은 히램 요양원 입구를 지나 마차를 몰았다."], ["There, at the gate, was a large, untidy farmer's wagon, laden with untidy-looking furniture; and there, inspecting the arrival, was good Mrs.", "거기 문간에는 어수선한 세간을 잔뜩 실은 크고 어수선한 농가 짐마차가 서 있었고, 그 도착을 살펴보고 있는 이는 다름 아닌 착한 퀴버풀 부인이었다."], ["Quiverful--not dressed in her Sunday best, not very clean in her apparel, not graceful as to her bonnet and shawl, or, indeed, with many feminine charms as to her whole appearance.", "나들이옷을 차려입지도 않았고, 옷차림이 그리 깨끗하지도 않았으며, 보닛과 숄이 맵시 있지도 않았고, 실상 온몸 어디를 보아도 여자다운 매력이랄 것이 별로 없었다."], ["She was busy at domestic work in her new house, and had just ventured out, expecting to see no one on the arrival of the family chattels.", "그녀는 새집에서 집안일에 바빴고, 세간이 닿을 때 아무도 마주치지 않으려니 여기고 막 밖으로 나와 본 참이었다."], ["The archdeacon was down upon her before she knew where she was.", "부주교는 그녀가 정신을 차리기도 전에 그녀 앞으로 들이닥쳤다."]], [["Her acquaintance with Dr. Grantly or his family was very slight indeed.", "그랜틀리 박사나 그 집안과 그녀의 친분은 실로 아주 얕았다."], ["The archdeacon, as a matter of course, knew every clergyman in the archdeaconry--it may almost be said in the diocese--and had some acquaintance, more or less intimate, with their wives and families. With Mr.", "부주교는 으레 그렇듯 부주교 관구 안의, 거의 교구 전체라고 해도 좋을 성직자를 모조리 알고 있었고, 그 아내들과 식솔들과도 깊고 얕은 차이는 있으나 얼마간 안면이 있었다."], ["Quiverful he had been concerned on various matters of business, but of Mrs. Q.", "퀴버풀 씨와는 이런저런 일로 얽힌 적이 있었으나 퀴버풀 부인은 거의 본 일이 없었다."], ["he had seen very little. Now, however, he was in too gracious a mood to pass her by unnoticed.", "그러나 지금 그는 그녀를 못 본 체 지나치기에는 너무나 너그러운 기분이었다."], ["The Quiverfuls, one and all, had looked for the bitterest hostility from Dr.", "퀴버풀 집안 사람들은 하나같이 그랜틀리 박사에게서 더없이 모진 적대를 예상하고 있었다."], ["Grantly; they knew his anxiety that Mr. Harding should return to his old home at the hospital, and they did not know that a new home had been offered to him at the deanery.", "그들은 하딩 씨가 요양원의 옛집으로 돌아가기를 그가 얼마나 바라는지 알고 있었고, 하딩 씨에게 수석사제관이라는 새 거처가 제안되었다는 것은 알지 못했다."], ["Mrs. Quiverful was therefore not a little surprised, and not a little rejoiced also, at the tone in which she was addressed.", "그러니 퀴버풀 부인이 자기에게 건네진 말투에 적잖이 놀라고 또 적잖이 기뻐한 것도 무리가 아니었다."]], [["\"How do you do, Mrs. Quiverful, how do you do?\"", "“안녕하시오, 퀴버풀 부인, 그동안 안녕하셨소?”"], ["said he, stretching his left hand out of the gig as he spoke to her.", "그가 말을 건네며 마차 밖으로 왼손을 내밀었다."], ["\"I am very glad to see you employed in so pleasant and useful a manner; very glad indeed.\"", "“이렇게 즐겁고 쓸모 있는 일에 힘쓰고 계신 것을 보니 참으로 반갑소이다. 참으로 반갑소.”"]], [["Mrs. Quiverful thanked him, and shook hands with him, and looked into his face suspiciously.", "퀴버풀 부인은 고맙다고 인사하고 그와 악수를 나누며 미심쩍은 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["She was not sure whether the congratulations and kindness were or were not ironical.", "그 축하와 친절이 비아냥인지 아닌지 분간할 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"Pray tell Mr. Quiverful from me,\"", "“퀴버풀 씨에게 내 말이라고 꼭 전해 주시오.”"], ["he continued, \"that I am rejoiced at his appointment.", "그가 말을 이었다. “그가 그 자리에 임명된 것이 나는 기쁘다고 말이오."], ["It's a comfortable place, Mrs.", "편안한 자리요, 퀴버풀 부인."], ["Quiverful, and a comfortable house, and I am very glad to see you in it.", "집도 편안하고. 부인이 여기 들어와 있는 것을 보니 참으로 반갑소."], ["Good-bye--good-bye.\"", "잘 있으시오, 잘 있으시오.”"], ["And he drove on, leaving the lady well pleased and astonished at his good nature.", "그러고는 마차를 몰아 가 버렸고, 부인은 그의 후한 마음씨에 흐뭇해하면서도 어리둥절해했다."], ["On the whole things were going well with the archdeacon, and he could afford to be charitable to Mrs. Quiverful.", "대체로 부주교에게는 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있었으니 퀴버풀 부인에게 너그러움을 베풀 여유쯤은 있었던 것이다."], ["He looked forth from his gig smilingly on all the world, and forgave everyone in Barchester their sins, excepting only Mrs. Proudie and Mr.", "그는 마차에서 온 세상을 향해 웃음 띤 얼굴로 내다보며, 오직 프라우디 부인과 슬로프 씨만 빼고 바체스터의 모든 사람의 죄를 용서해 주었다."], ["Slope. Had he seen the bishop, he would have felt inclined to pat even him kindly on the head.", "주교를 만났더라면 그 주교의 머리마저 다정하게 쓰다듬어 주고 싶은 마음이 들었으리라."]], [["He determined to go home by St. Ewold's.", "그는 세인트 이월드를 거쳐 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["This would take him some three miles out of his way, but he felt that he could not leave Plumstead comfortably without saying one word of good-fellowship to Mr.", "그리 하면 길을 3마일쯤 돌게 되지만, 애러빈 씨에게 우정 어린 말 한마디 건네지 않고서는 마음 편히 플럼스테드를 떠날 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["Arabin. When he reached the parsonage, the vicar was still out, but from what he had heard, he did not doubt but that he would meet him on the road between their two houses.", "목사관에 이르러 보니 교구 사제는 아직 나가 있었으나, 들은 바로 미루어 두 집 사이의 길에서 그를 만나게 되리라는 것을 그는 의심하지 않았다."], ["He was right in this, for about half-way home, at a narrow turn, he came upon Mr. Arabin, who was on horseback.", "과연 그의 짐작이 맞아서, 집으로 가는 길 절반쯤 되는 좁은 모퉁이에서 그는 말을 타고 오는 애러빈 씨와 마주쳤다."]], [["\"Well, well, well, well,\"", "“허, 그것 참, 그것 참, 그것 참.”"], ["said the archdeacon loudly, joyously, and with supreme good humour; \"well, well, well, well; so, after all, we have no further cause to fear Mr.", "부주교가 큰 소리로, 유쾌하게, 더없이 좋은 기분으로 말했다. “허, 그것 참, 그것 참, 그것 참."], ["Slope.\"", "그러니까 결국 우리가 더는 슬로프 씨를 두려워할 까닭이 없게 된 것이오.”"]], [["\"I hear from Mrs. Grantly that they have offered the deanery to Mr.", "“그랜틀리 부인께 들으니 하딩 씨에게 수석 사제 자리를 제안했다더구려.”"], ["Harding,\" said the other.", "상대가 말했다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope has lost more than the deanery I find,\"", "“슬로프 씨는 수석 사제 자리보다 더한 것을 잃은 모양이오.”"], ["and then the archdeacon laughed jocosely.", "그러고 나서 부주교는 익살스럽게 웃었다."], ["\"Come, come, Arabin, you have kept your secret well enough.", "“자, 자, 애러빈, 비밀 하나는 야무지게도 지켰구려."], ["I know all about it now.\"", "이제 나도 죄다 알고 있소.”"]], [["\"I have had no secret, Archdeacon,\"", "“나는 비밀이랄 것이 없었소, 부주교.”"], ["said the other with a quiet smile.", "상대가 조용히 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"None at all--not for a day.", "“도무지 없었소이다."], ["It was only yesterday that I knew my own good fortune, and to-day I went over to Plumstead to ask your approval.", "하루도 없었소. 내 행운을 안 것이 바로 어제요, 오늘은 당신의 승낙을 청하러 플럼스테드에 다녀왔소."], ["From what Mrs. Grantly has said to me, I am led to hope that I shall have it.\"", "그랜틀리 부인이 내게 해 준 말로 미루어 그 승낙을 받으리라는 기대를 품게 되었소.”"]], [["\"With all my heart, with all my heart,\"", "“진심으로, 진심으로 그러겠소.”"], ["said the archdeacon cordially, holding his friend fast by the hand.", "부주교가 다정하게 말하며 벗의 손을 굳게 붙들었다."], ["\"It's just as I would have it.", "“내가 바라던 바로 그대로요."], ["She is an excellent young woman; she will not come to you empty-handed; and I think she will make you a good wife.", "그 사람은 참으로 훌륭한 젊은 부인이오. 빈손으로 그대에게 가지도 않을 것이고, 좋은 아내가 되어 주리라고 나는 생각하오."], ["If she does her duty by you as her sister does by me, you'll be a happy man; that's all I can say.\"", "그 언니가 나에게 하는 만큼 그 사람이 그대에게 도리를 다한다면 그대는 행복한 사람이 될 것이오. 내가 할 수 있는 말은 그뿐이오.”"], ["And as he finished speaking a tear might have been observed in each of the doctor's eyes.", "그리고 말을 마칠 즈음 박사의 두 눈에는 눈물이 한 방울씩 맺혀 있는 것이 보였을 법도 하다."]], [["Mr. Arabin warmly returned the archdeacon's grasp, but he said little.", "애러빈 씨는 부주교가 잡은 손을 따뜻하게 마주 잡았으나 말은 별로 하지 않았다."], ["His heart was too full for speaking, and he could not express the gratitude which he felt.", "가슴이 너무 벅차 말이 나오지 않았고, 자기가 느끼는 고마움을 표현할 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["Dr. Grantly understood him as well as though he had spoken for an hour.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그가 한 시간을 이야기한 것이나 다름없이 그를 알아들었다."]], [["\"And mind, Arabin,\"", "“그리고 잘 들어 두시오, 애러빈.”"], ["said he, \"no one but myself shall tie the knot.", "그가 말했다. “나 아닌 그 누구도 그 매듭을 묶지 못하오."], ["We'll get Eleanor out to Plumstead, and it shall come off there.", "엘리너를 플럼스테드로 데려올 테니 식은 거기서 치릅시다."], ["I'll make Susan stir herself, and we'll do it in style.", "내가 수전을 다그쳐 놓을 테니 격식을 갖추어 제대로 해 봅시다."], ["I must be off to London to-morrow on special business.", "나는 내일 특별한 볼일로 런던에 가야 하오."], ["Harding goes with me.", "하딩 씨가 나와 함께 가오."], ["But I'll be back before your bride has got her wedding-dress ready.\"", "그러나 그대의 신부가 혼례복을 갖추기 전에 돌아와 있을 것이오.”"], ["And so they parted.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["On his journey home the archdeacon occupied his mind with preparations for the marriage festivities.", "집으로 돌아가는 길에 부주교는 혼례 잔치 준비로 마음을 채웠다."], ["He made a great resolve that he would atone to Eleanor for all the injury he had done her by the munificence of his future treatment.", "그는 앞으로 후하게 대접함으로써 자기가 엘리너에게 끼친 온갖 상처를 갚아 주리라고 크게 결심했다."], ["He would show her what was the difference in his eyes between a Slope and an Arabin.", "슬로프 같은 자와 애러빈 같은 이가 제 눈에 얼마나 다른지를 그녀에게 보여 줄 참이었다."], ["On one other thing also he decided with a firm mind: if the affair of the dean should not be settled in Mr.", "또 한 가지도 그는 단단히 마음을 정했다."], ["Arabin's favour, nothing should prevent him putting a new front and bow-window to the dining-room at St. Ewold's parsonage.", "수석 사제 일이 애러빈 씨에게 유리하게 매듭지어지지 않는다면, 세인트 이월드 목사관 식당에 새 정면과 내닫이창을 내는 것을 무엇으로도 막지 못하리라는 것이었다."]], [["\"So we're sold after all, Sue,\"", "“그래서 우리는 결국 한 방 먹은 셈이오, 수.”"], ["said he to his wife, accosting her with a kiss as soon as he entered his house.", "그가 집에 들어서자마자 아내에게 입을 맞추며 말을 건넸다."], ["He did not call his wife Sue above twice or thrice in a year, and these occasions were great high days.", "그가 아내를 수라고 부르는 일은 일 년에 두세 번을 넘지 않았고, 그런 날은 크게 경사스러운 날이었다."]], [["\"Eleanor has had more sense than we gave her credit for,\"", "“엘리너는 우리가 여겼던 것보다 분별이 있었던 게지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["And there was great content in Plumstead Rectory that evening.", "그리고 그날 저녁 플럼스테드 목사관에는 큰 흡족함이 감돌았다."], ["Mrs. Grantly promised her husband that she would now open her heart and take Mr.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이제 마음을 열어 애러빈 씨를 그 안에 받아들이겠노라고 남편에게 약속했다."], ["Arabin into it. Hitherto she had declined to do so.", "그때까지 그녀는 그러기를 마다해 왔던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LI. Mr. Slope Bids Farewell to the Palace and Its Inhabitants", "# 제51장. 슬로프 씨가 주교관과 그 식구들에게 작별을 고하다"]], [["We must now take leave of Mr. Slope, and of the bishop also, and of Mrs.", "이제 우리는 슬로프 씨와, 또한 주교와, 그리고 프라우디 부인과도 작별해야 한다."], ["Proudie. These leave-takings in novels are as disagreeable as they are in real life; not so sad, indeed, for they want the reality of sadness; but quite as perplexing, and generally less satisfactory.", "소설에서의 이런 작별은 실제 삶에서의 그것만큼이나 거북한 일이다."], ["What novelist, what Fielding, what Scott, what George Sand, or Sue, or Dumas, can impart an interest to the last chapter of his fictitious history?", "슬픔의 실감이 없으니 실은 그만큼 슬프지야 않지만, 곤혹스럽기로는 조금도 덜하지 않고 대개는 만족스럽지도 못하다. 어떤 소설가가, 어떤 필딩이, 어떤 스콧이, 어떤 조르주 상드나 쉬나 뒤마가 제가 지어낸 이야기의 마지막 장에 흥미를 불어넣을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Promises of two children and superhuman happiness are of no avail, nor assurance of extreme respectability carried to an age far exceeding that usually allotted to mortals.", "아이 둘과 인간의 한계를 넘어선 행복을 약속해 보아야 아무 소용이 없고, 필멸자에게 흔히 허락된 세월을 훌쩍 넘도록 더없이 점잖게 살았노라 장담해 보아야 마찬가지다."], ["The sorrows of our heroes and heroines, they are your delight, oh public!--their sorrows, or their sins, or their absurdities; not their virtues, good sense, and consequent rewards.", "오, 대중이여, 그대들을 기쁘게 하는 것은 우리 주인공과 여주인공의 슬픔이다. 그들의 슬픔이거나 그들의 죄이거나 그들의 어리석음이지, 그들의 미덕이나 분별이나 그에 따라 주어지는 보상이 아니다."], ["When we begin to tint our final pages with couleur de rose, as in accordance with fixed rule we must do, we altogether extinguish our own powers of pleasing.", "정해진 규칙에 따라 마땅히 그리해야 하는 대로 우리가 마지막 몇 쪽을 couleur de rose 로 물들이기 시작하는 순간, 우리는 남을 즐겁게 하는 우리 자신의 능력을 아주 꺼 버리고 만다."], ["When we become dull, we offend your intellect; and we must become dull or we should offend your taste.", "우리가 지루해지면 그대들의 지성을 거스르는 것이요, 그러나 지루해지지 않으면 그대들의 취향을 거스르게 되니 지루해질 수밖에 없다."], ["A late writer, wishing to sustain his interest to the last page, hung his hero at the end of the third volume.", "근래의 어느 작가는 마지막 쪽까지 흥미를 이어 가고 싶은 마음에 제3권 끝에서 제 주인공을 목매달았다."], ["The consequence was that no one would read his novel.", "그 결과 아무도 그의 소설을 읽으려 들지 않았다."], ["And who can apportion out and dovetail his incidents, dialogues, characters, and descriptive morsels so as to fit them all exactly into 930 pages, without either compressing them unnaturally, or extending them artificially at the end of his labour?", "게다가 누가 제 사건과 대화와 인물과 묘사의 토막들을, 마지막에 이르러 부자연스럽게 눌러 담거나 억지로 늘여 붙이지 않고서, 정확히 930쪽에 꼭 들어맞도록 배분하고 짜 맞출 수 있겠는가? 나 자신도 바로 이 순간 열두 쪽이 모자라, 그것을 어디서 찾아낼까 머리를 쥐어짜다 진력이 나 있음을 내가 모르겠는가?"], ["Do I not myself know that I am at this moment in want of a dozen pages, and that I am sick with cudgelling my brains to find them?", "그러고 나서 모든 것이 끝나고 나면, 그들 가운데 마음이 가장 너그러운 비평가조차 어김없이 우리 결말의 무능함과 어설픔을 들어 우리를 놀린다."], ["And then, when everything is done, the kindest-hearted critic of them all invariably twits us with the incompetency and lameness of our conclusion.", "우리가 게을러져서 결말을 소홀히 했거나, 아니면 지루하게 지나친 공을 들였다는 것이다."], ["We have either become idle and neglected it, or tedious and overlaboured it.", "결말은 맹탕이거나 부자연스럽고, 지나치게 힘이 들어갔거나 얼이 빠져 있다."], ["It is insipid or unnatural, overstrained or imbecile.", "아무 뜻도 없거나 너무 많은 것을 시도한다."], ["It means nothing, or attempts too much. The last scene of all, as all last scenes we fear must be,", "그리고 모든 장면 가운데 마지막 장면은, 우리가 염려하기로 모든 마지막 장면이 그러할 수밖에 없듯이,"]], [["Is second childishness, and mere oblivion, Sans teeth, sans eyes, sans taste, sans everything.", "두 번째 어린 시절이요 그저 망각일 뿐, 이도 없고 눈도 없고 맛도 없고 아무것도 없느니."]], [["I can only say that if some critic who thoroughly knows his work, and has laboured on it till experience has made him perfect, will write the last fifty pages of a novel in the way they should be written, I, for one, will in future do my best to copy the example.", "내가 할 수 있는 말이라고는 이것뿐이다. 제 일을 속속들이 아는 어떤 비평가가, 경험이 그를 완벽하게 만들어 줄 때까지 그 일에 공을 들인 끝에, 소설의 마지막 쉰 쪽을 마땅히 쓰여야 할 방식대로 써 보인다면, 나로서는 앞으로 그 본을 힘껏 본뜨는 데 최선을 다하겠노라고."], ["Guided by my own lights only, I confess that I despair of success.", "오직 내 눈에 비치는 빛만을 길잡이로 삼는 한, 나는 성공을 단념하고 있음을 고백한다."]], [["For the last week or ten days Mr. Slope had seen nothing of Mrs.", "지난 한 주나 열흘 동안 슬로프 씨는 프라우디 부인을 전혀 보지 못했고 주교도 거의 보지 못했다."], ["Proudie, and very little of the bishop. He still lived in the palace, and still went through his usual routine work; but the confidential doings of the diocese had passed into other hands.", "그는 여전히 주교관에 살면서 여전히 늘 하던 일과를 해 나갔으나, 교구의 은밀한 일들은 이미 다른 이의 손으로 넘어가 있었다."], ["He had seen this clearly and marked it well, but it had not much disturbed him.", "그도 이를 똑똑히 보았고 잘 새겨 두었지만, 그 일로 크게 마음이 흔들리지는 않았다."], ["He had indulged in other hopes till the bishop's affairs had become dull to him, and he was moreover aware that, as regarded the diocese, Mrs. Proudie had checkmated him.", "주교의 일이 그에게 시들해질 무렵 그는 다른 희망에 빠져 있었고, 게다가 교구에 관한 한 프라우디 부인이 자기를 외통수에 몰아넣었다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["It has been explained, in the beginning of these pages, how three or four were contending together as to who, in fact, should be Bishop of Barchester.", "이 이야기의 첫머리에서, 대체 누가 바체스터의 주교가 될 것인가를 두고 서넛이 어떻게 다투었는지는 이미 설명한 바 있다."], ["Each of these had now admitted to himself (or boasted to herself) that Mrs. Proudie was victorious in the struggle.", "그들 각자는 이제 프라우디 부인이 그 싸움의 승자라는 것을 스스로 인정하거나, 혹은 스스로 자랑하거나 하게 되었다."], ["They had gone through a competitive examination of considerable severity, and she had come forth the winner, facile princeps. Mr.", "그들은 꽤나 혹독한 경쟁 시험을 치렀고, 부인이 facile princeps 로 승자가 되어 나왔다."], ["Slope had for a moment run her hard, but it was only for a moment. It had become, as it were, acknowledged that Hiram's Hospital should be the testing-point between them, and now Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 한때 부인을 바짝 몰아붙이기는 했으나 그것도 잠시뿐이었다."], ["Quiverful was already in the hospital, the proof of Mrs. Proudie's skill and courage.", "히램 요양원이 두 사람 사이의 시금석이 되어야 한다는 것은 말하자면 서로 인정된 바였는데, 이제 퀴버풀 씨가 이미 요양원에 들어앉았으니 그것이 곧 프라우디 부인의 수완과 배짱을 증명하는 것이었다."]], [["All this did not break down Mr. Slope's spirit, because he had other hopes.", "이 모든 일도 슬로프 씨의 기운을 꺾지는 못했으니, 그에게는 다른 희망이 있었기 때문이다."], ["But, alas, at last there came to him a note from his friend Sir Nicholas, informing him that the deanship was disposed of.", "그러나 아아, 마침내 그의 벗 니컬러스 경에게서 수석 사제 자리가 이미 정해졌음을 알리는 쪽지가 날아들었다."], ["Let us give Mr. Slope his due.", "슬로프 씨에게도 마땅한 몫은 돌려주기로 하자."], ["He did not lie prostrate under this blow, or give himself up to vain lamentations; he did not henceforward despair of life and call upon gods above and gods below to carry him off.", "그는 이 일격을 맞고 넋을 놓은 채 엎어지지도 않았고, 부질없는 탄식에 제 몸을 내맡기지도 않았다. 그때부터 삶에 절망하여 위의 신들과 아래의 신들을 불러 자기를 데려가 달라고 하지도 않았다."], ["He sat himself down in his chair, counted out what monies he had in hand for present purposes and what others were coming in to him, bethought himself as to the best sphere for his future exertions, and at once wrote off a letter to a rich sugar-refiner's wife in Baker Street, who, as he well knew, was much given to the entertainment and encouragement of serious young evangelical clergymen.", "그는 제 의자에 앉아 당장 쓸 돈이 얼마나 손에 있고 앞으로 들어올 돈이 얼마나 되는지 세어 보고, 앞으로 힘을 쏟기에 가장 좋은 무대가 어디일지 궁리한 뒤, 곧바로 베이커 스트리트에 사는 어느 부유한 제당업자의 아내에게 편지를 써 보냈다. 그가 익히 알기로 그 부인은 진지한 젊은 복음주의 성직자를 대접하고 북돋우는 일에 몹시 열심인 사람이었다."], ["He was again, he said, \"upon the world, having found the air of a cathedral town, and the very nature of cathedral services, uncongenial to his spirit;\"", "그는 다시금, 제 말로는, “대성당 도시의 공기와 대성당 예배의 성격 그 자체가 제 정신에 맞지 않음을 알게 되어 세상에 나선”"], ["and then he sat awhile, making firm resolves as to his manner of parting from the bishop, and also as to his future conduct.", "처지였다. 그러고는 한동안 앉아, 주교와 어떤 식으로 갈라설지, 또 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지 굳게 마음을 정했다."]], [["At last he rose, and twitched his mantle blue (black), To-morrow to fresh woods and pastures new.", "마침내 그는 일어나 푸른(검은) 겉옷을 여미었으니, 내일이면 새로운 숲과 새로운 풀밭으로."]], [["Having received a formal command to wait upon the bishop, he rose and proceeded to obey it.", "주교를 뵈러 오라는 정식 명령을 받고서 그는 자리에서 일어나 그 명을 따르러 갔다."], ["He rang the bell and desired the servant to inform his master that, if it suited his lordship, he, Mr. Slope, was ready to wait upon him.", "그는 종을 울려, 주교님만 괜찮으시다면 자기, 곧 슬로프 씨가 뵈러 갈 준비가 되어 있다고 주인에게 전하라 하인에게 일렀다."], ["The servant, who well understood that Mr. Slope was no longer in the ascendant, brought back a message saying that \"his lordship desired that Mr.", "슬로프 씨가 이제 더는 세도를 부리지 못한다는 것을 잘 아는 하인은 “주교님께서 슬로프 씨더러 지금 곧 서재로 오라 하십니다”"], ["Slope would attend him immediately in his study.\"", "하는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["Mr. Slope waited about ten minutes more to prove his independence, and then he went into the bishop's room.", "슬로프 씨는 제 독립을 내보이려고 십 분쯤 더 기다렸다가 주교의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["There, as he had expected, he found Mrs. Proudie, together with her husband.", "거기에는 그가 예상한 대로 프라우디 부인이 남편과 함께 있었다."]], [["\"Hum, ha--Mr. Slope, pray take a chair,\"", "“흠, 하 — 슬로프 씨, 자, 앉으시오.”"], ["said the gentleman bishop.", "신사 주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Pray be seated, Mr. Slope,\"", "“앉게, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["said the lady bishop.", "여자 주교가 말했다."]], [["\"Thank ye, thank ye,\" said Mr.", "“고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.”"], ["Slope, and walking round to the fire, he threw himself into one of the armchairs that graced the hearth-rug.", "슬로프 씨는 이렇게 말하고는 난롯가로 돌아가, 난로 앞 깔개를 장식하고 있던 안락의자 가운데 하나에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨.”"], ["said the bishop, \"it has become necessary that I should speak to you definitively on a matter that has for some time been pressing itself on my attention.\"", "주교가 말했다. “얼마 전부터 내 마음을 눌러 온 문제를 두고 이제는 그대에게 분명히 말해야 할 필요가 생겼소.”"]], [["\"May I ask whether the subject is in any way connected with myself?\"", "“그 문제가 저와 어떤 식으로든 관계가 있는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”"], ["said Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"It is so--certainly--yes, it certainly is connected with yourself, Mr. Slope.\"", "“그렇소 — 물론이오 — 그렇소, 그것은 분명 그대와 관계된 일이오, 슬로프 씨.”"]], [["\"Then, my lord, if I may be allowed to express a wish, I would prefer that no discussion on the subject should take place between us in the presence of a third person.\"", "“그렇다면 주교님, 제가 바람을 하나 말씀드려도 된다면, 저는 그 문제에 관한 논의가 제삼자가 있는 자리에서 우리 사이에 오가지 않기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["\"Don't alarm yourself, Mr.", "“놀랄 것 없네, 슬로프 씨.”"], ["Slope,\" said Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["Proudie, \"no discussion is at all necessary.", "“논의 따위는 조금도 필요치 않아."], ["The bishop merely intends to express his own wishes.\"", "주교님은 그저 당신의 뜻을 밝히려 하실 뿐이니까.”"]], [["\"I merely intend, Mr. Slope, to express my own wishes--no discussion will be at all necessary,\"", "“나는 그저 내 뜻을 밝히려 할 뿐이오, 슬로프 씨."], ["said the bishop, reiterating his wife's words.", "논의 따위는 조금도 필요치 않소.” 주교가 아내의 말을 그대로 되풀이하며 말했다."]], [["\"That is more, my lord, than we any of us can be sure of,\" said Mr.", "“그것은, 주교님, 우리 가운데 누구도 장담할 수 있는 일이 아닙니다.”"], ["Slope; \"I cannot, however, force Mrs.", "슬로프 씨가 말했다."], ["Proudie to leave the room; nor can I refuse to remain here if it be your lordship's wish that I should do so.\"", "“그러나 저로서는 프라우디 부인을 이 방에서 나가시게 할 수도 없고, 제가 여기 남아 있기를 주교님께서 바라신다면 그것을 마다할 수도 없습니다.”"]], [["\"It is his lordship's wish, certainly,\"", "“주교님의 뜻이 그러하시네, 물론.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Mr. Slope,\"", "“슬로프 씨.”"], ["began the bishop in a solemn, serious voice, \"it grieves me to have to find fault.", "주교가 엄숙하고 진지한 목소리로 입을 열었다. “잘못을 나무라야 한다는 것은 나로서는 괴로운 일이오."], ["It grieves me much to have to find fault with a clergyman--but especially so with a clergyman in your position.\"", "성직자의 잘못을 나무라야 한다는 것은 더욱 괴로운 일이고, 더구나 그대와 같은 자리에 있는 성직자라면 유난히 그러하오.”"]], [["\"Why, what have I done amiss, my lord?\"", "“아니, 제가 무엇을 잘못했다는 말씀입니까, 주교님?”"], ["demanded Mr. Slope boldly.", "슬로프 씨가 당돌하게 따져 물었다."]], [["\"What have you done amiss, Mr. Slope?\"", "“무엇을 잘못했느냐고, 슬로프 씨?”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, standing erect before the culprit and raising that terrible forefinger.", "프라우디 부인이 죄인 앞에 꼿꼿이 서서 그 무시무시한 집게손가락을 치켜들며 말했다."], ["\"Do you dare to ask the bishop what you have done amiss?", "“무엇을 잘못했느냐고 감히 주교님께 여쭙는 겐가?"], ["Does not your conscience--\"", "자네 양심이 —”"]], [["\"Mrs. Proudie, pray let it be understood, once for all, that I will have no words with you.\"", "“프라우디 부인, 저는 부인과 한마디도 나누지 않겠다는 것을 이 자리에서 아주 분명히 해 두고 싶습니다.”"]], [["\"Ah, sir, but you will have words,\"", "“아니지, 이 사람아, 자네는 말을 나누게 될 걸세.”"], ["said she; \"you must have words.", "부인이 말했다. “나누어야만 하고말고."], ["Why have you had so many words with that Signora Neroni?", "어째서 자네는 그 시뇨라 네로니와 그토록 많은 말을 나누었는가?"], ["Why have you disgraced yourself, you a clergyman, too, by constantly consorting with such a woman as that--with a married woman--with one altogether unfit for a clergyman's society?\"", "어째서 자네는, 그것도 성직자인 자네가, 그런 여자와, 남편이 있는 여자와, 성직자가 어울리기에는 도무지 마땅치 않은 여자와 노상 어울려 다니며 스스로를 욕되게 하였는가?”"]], [["\"At any rate I was introduced to her in your drawing-room,\"", "“어쨌든 저는 그분을 부인의 응접실에서 소개받았습니다.”"], ["retorted Mr. Slope.", "슬로프 씨가 되받았다."]], [["\"And shamefully you behaved there,\" said Mrs.", "“그리고 자네는 거기서 부끄러운 짓을 했지.”"], ["Proudie; \"most shamefully.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["I was wrong to allow you to remain in the house a day after what I then saw.", "“참으로 부끄러운 짓을. 그때 내가 본 것을 보고도 자네를 이 집에 하루라도 더 두었던 것이 내 잘못이었네."], ["I should have insisted on your instant dismissal.\"", "그 자리에서 당장 내보내라고 우겼어야 했어.”"]], [["\"I have yet to learn, Mrs. Proudie, that you have the power to insist either on my going from hence or on my staying here.\"", "“프라우디 부인, 부인께 제가 여기서 나가라거나 여기 머무르라거나 우기실 권한이 있다는 것은 저로서는 아직 배우지 못했습니다.”"]], [["\"What!\"", "“뭐라고!”"], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"I am not to have the privilege of saying who shall and who shall not frequent my own drawing-room!", "“내 응접실에 누가 드나들어도 되고 누가 안 되는지를 내가 말할 권리가 없다고!"], ["I am not to save my servants and dependants from having their morals corrupted by improper conduct!", "내 하인과 내게 딸린 사람들이 옳지 못한 행실로 그 품행을 물들이지 않도록 지킬 권리가 없다고!"], ["I am not to save my own daughters from impurity!", "내 딸들을 더러움에서 지킬 권리가 없다고!"], ["I will let you see, Mr. Slope, whether I have the power or whether I have not.", "슬로프 씨, 내게 그 권한이 있는지 없는지 자네에게 똑똑히 보여 주겠네."], ["You will have the goodness to understand that you no longer fill any situation about the bishop, and as your room will be immediately wanted in the palace for another chaplain, I must ask you to provide yourself with apartments as soon as may be convenient to you.\"", "자네는 이제 주교님 곁에서 아무 직임도 맡고 있지 않다는 것을 알아 두는 게 좋을 걸세. 그리고 자네가 쓰던 방은 다른 전속 사제를 위해 주교관에서 곧 필요하게 될 터이니, 형편이 닿는 대로 되도록 빨리 거처를 마련하기 바라네.”"]], [["\"My lord,\" said Mr.", "“주교님.”"], ["Slope, appealing to the bishop, and so turning his back completely on the lady, \"will you permit me to ask that I may have from your own lips any decision that you may have come to on this matter?\"", "슬로프 씨가 주교에게 호소하며, 그리하여 부인에게는 완전히 등을 돌린 채 말했다. “이 일을 두고 주교님께서 내리신 결정이 있다면 그것을 주교님 입으로 직접 듣기를 청해도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["\"Certainly, Mr. Slope, certainly,\"", "“물론이오, 슬로프 씨, 물론이오.”"], ["said the bishop; \"that is but reasonable.", "주교가 말했다. “그것은 당연한 요구요."], ["Well, my decision is that you had better look for some other preferment.", "그래, 내 결정은 그대가 다른 성직 승진 자리를 찾아보는 편이 낫겠다는 것이오."], ["For the situation which you have lately held I do not think that you are well suited.\"", "그대가 근래에 맡아 온 자리에는 그대가 잘 맞지 않는다고 나는 생각하오.”"]], [["\"And what, my lord, has been my fault?\"", "“그러면 주교님, 제 잘못이 무엇이었습니까?”"]], [["\"That Signora Neroni is one fault,\"", "“그 시뇨라 네로니가 한 가지 잘못일세.”"], ["said Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["Proudie; \"and a very abominable fault she is; very abominable and very disgraceful.", "“참으로 언어도단인 잘못이지. 아주 언어도단이고 아주 망측한 일이야."], ["Fie, Mr. Slope, fie!", "저런, 슬로프 씨, 저런!"], ["You an evangelical clergyman indeed!\"", "자네가 복음주의 성직자라니, 참으로 대단하이!”"]], [["\"My lord, I desire to know for what fault I am turned out of your lordship's house.\"", "“주교님, 저는 제가 무슨 잘못으로 주교님 댁에서 쫓겨나는지 알고 싶습니다.”"]], [["\"You hear what Mrs. Proudie says,\"", "“프라우디 부인이 하는 말을 그대도 들었잖소.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["\"When I publish the history of this transaction, my lord, as I decidedly shall do in my own vindication, I presume you will not wish me to state that you have discarded me at your wife's bidding--because she has objected to my being acquainted with another lady, the daughter of one of the prebendaries of the chapter?\"", "“주교님, 제가 이 일의 전말을 세상에 밝힐 때에는, 제 자신을 변호하기 위해 반드시 그리하겠습니다만, 주교님께서도 제가 이렇게 적기를 바라지는 않으실 테지요. 곧 주교님께서 사모님의 분부에 따라 저를 내치셨다고, 그것도 제가 참사회의 어느 참사회원의 딸인 다른 부인과 알고 지낸다는 이유로 사모님께서 반대하셨기 때문이라고 말입니다.”"]], [["\"You may publish what you please, sir,\"", "“쓰고 싶은 대로 쓰게, 이 사람.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["\"But you will not be insane enough to publish any of your doings in Barchester.", "“허나 자네가 바체스터에서 한 짓을 세상에 알릴 만큼 제정신이 아니지는 않을 걸세."], ["Do you think I have not heard of your kneelings at that creature's feet--that is, if she has any feet--and of your constant slobbering over her hand?", "자네가 그 계집의 발치에 무릎 꿇던 일을, 그 여자에게 발이란 게 있기나 하다면 말이지만, 그리고 그 손등에 노상 침을 발라 대던 일을 내가 못 들었을 성싶은가?"], ["I advise you to beware, Mr. Slope, of what you do and say.", "슬로프 씨, 자네가 하는 짓과 하는 말을 삼가라고 충고하네."], ["Clergymen have been unfrocked for less than what you have been guilty of.\"", "자네가 저지른 것보다 못한 일로도 성직을 박탈당한 성직자들이 있었으니까.”"]], [["\"My lord, if this goes on I shall be obliged to indict this woman--Mrs. Proudie I mean--for defamation of character.\"", "“주교님, 이대로 계속된다면 저는 이 여자를, 프라우디 부인 말입니다, 명예 훼손으로 고발할 수밖에 없겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I think, Mr. Slope, you had better now retire,\"", "“슬로프 씨, 이제 그만 물러가는 편이 좋겠소.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."], ["\"I will enclose to you a cheque for any balance that may be due to you; under the present circumstances, it will of course be better for all parties that you should leave the palace at the earliest possible moment.", "“그대에게 치러야 할 잔액이 있다면 수표를 넣어 보내겠소. 지금 형편으로는 그대가 되도록 이른 시각에 주교관을 떠나는 편이 물론 모두에게 나을 것이오."], ["I will allow you for your journey back to London and for your maintenance in Barchester for a week from this date.\"", "런던으로 돌아갈 여비와 오늘부터 한 주 동안 바체스터에서 지낼 비용은 내가 대 주겠소.”"]], [["\"If, however, you wish to remain in this neighbourhood;\" said Mrs.", "“그러나 만일 자네가 이 근처에 남아 있고 싶다면,”"], ["Proudie, \"and will solemnly pledge yourself never again to see that woman, and will promise also to be more circumspect in your conduct, the bishop will mention your name to Mr.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 다시는 그 여자를 만나지 않겠다고 엄숙히 서약하고, 또 앞으로 처신을 더 조심하겠다고 약속한다면, 주교님께서 자네 이름을 퀴버풀 씨에게 말씀해 주실 걸세."], ["Quiverful, who now wants a curate at Puddingdale.", "그이가 지금 푸딩데일에서 부목사를 구하고 있으니까."], ["The house is, I imagine, quite sufficient for your requirements, and there will moreover be a stipend of fifty pounds a year.\"", "집은 내 짐작에 자네가 쓰기에 넉넉할 테고, 게다가 한 해 50파운드의 봉급도 나올 것이네.”"]], [["\"May God forgive you, madam, for the manner in which you have treated me,\" said Mr.", "“부인, 부인께서 저를 대하신 그 방식을 하느님께서 용서하시기를 빕니다.”"], ["Slope, looking at her with a very heavenly look; \"and remember this, madam, that you yourself may still have a fall;\"", "슬로프 씨가 지극히 천상적인 눈빛으로 부인을 바라보며 말했다. “그리고 부인, 이것을 기억하십시오."], ["and he looked at her with a very worldly look.", "부인께서도 아직 넘어지실 날이 있을지 모른다는 것을.”"], ["\"As to the bishop, I pity him!\"", "그러고는 지극히 세속적인 눈빛으로 부인을 바라보았다."], ["And so saying, Mr. Slope left the room.", "“주교님으로 말하자면, 저는 그분이 가엾습니다!”"], ["Thus ended the intimacy of the Bishop of Barchester with his first confidential chaplain.", "이렇게 말하고 슬로프 씨는 방을 나갔다. 이렇게 하여 바체스터 주교와 그의 첫 심복 전속 사제 사이의 친분은 끝이 났다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie was right in this; namely, that Mr.", "프라우디 부인의 말은 이 점에서 옳았다."], ["Slope was not insane enough to publish to the world any of his doings in Barchester. He did not trouble his friend Mr.", "곧 슬로프 씨는 바체스터에서 자기가 한 짓을 세상에 알릴 만큼 제정신이 아니지는 않았다는 것이다."], ["Towers with any written statement of the iniquity of Mrs. Proudie, or the imbecility of her husband.", "그는 프라우디 부인의 부당함이나 그 남편의 무능함에 관한 글 한 줄로도 제 벗 타워스 씨를 성가시게 하지 않았다."], ["He was aware that it would be wise in him to drop for the future all allusion to his doings in the cathedral city.", "앞으로는 그 대성당 도시에서 자기가 한 일에 대한 언급을 모두 접어 두는 것이 현명하리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Soon after the interview just recorded he left Barchester, shaking the dust off his feet as he entered the railway carriage; and he gave no longing, lingering look after the cathedral towers as the train hurried him quickly out of their sight.", "방금 적은 면담이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 바체스터를 떠났는데, 기차에 오르면서 제 발의 먼지를 털어 냈다. 그리고 기차가 그를 서둘러 실어 내어 대성당의 탑들이 시야에서 사라질 때에도, 그는 그것을 그리워하며 아쉬운 눈길로 돌아보는 일이 없었다."]], [["It is well known that the family of the Slopes never starve: they always fall on their feet, like cats; and let them fall where they will, they live on the fat of the land.", "슬로프 집안 사람들이 결코 굶는 법이 없다는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이다. 그들은 고양이처럼 언제나 제 발로 떨어지고, 어디에 떨어지든 그 땅의 기름진 것을 먹고 산다."], ["Our Mr. Slope did so.", "우리의 슬로프 씨도 그러했다."], ["On his return to town he found that the sugar-refiner had died and that his widow was inconsolable--in other words, in want of consolation.", "도시로 돌아온 그는 그 제당업자가 죽었고 그 미망인이 위로받을 길 없는 처지임을, 달리 말해 위로가 필요한 처지임을 알게 되었다."], ["Mr. Slope consoled her, and soon found himself settled with much comfort in the house in Baker Street.", "슬로프 씨가 그 부인을 위로했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 베이커 스트리트의 그 집에 아주 안락하게 자리를 잡은 자신을 발견했다."], ["He possessed himself, also, before long, of a church in the vicinity of the Red Road, and became known to fame as one of the most eloquent preachers and pious clergymen in that part of the metropolis.", "그는 또한 오래지 않아 레드 로드 부근의 교회 하나를 손에 넣었고, 그 도시의 그 일대에서 가장 언변이 뛰어나고 가장 경건한 성직자의 한 사람으로 이름을 떨치게 되었다."], ["There let us leave him.", "그를 거기에 두고 떠나기로 하자."]], [["Of the bishop and his wife very little further need be said.", "주교와 그 부인에 대해서는 더 할 말이 별로 없다."], ["From that time forth nothing material occurred to interrupt the even course of their domestic harmony.", "그때부터 그들의 가정의 화목이 순탄하게 흘러가는 것을 가로막을 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Very speedily, a further vacancy on the bench of bishops gave to Dr. Proudie the seat in the House of Lords, which he at first so anxiously longed for.", "아주 이내 주교단에 자리가 또 하나 비면서 프라우디 박사는 처음에 그토록 애타게 바라던 상원 의석을 얻었다."], ["But by this time he had become a wiser man.", "그러나 그때쯤 그는 더 지혜로운 사람이 되어 있었다."], ["He did certainly take his seat, and occasionally registered a vote in favour of Government views on ecclesiastical matters.", "그는 분명 그 자리에 앉았고 이따금 교회 문제에 관한 정부의 견해에 찬성표를 던지기도 했다."], ["But he had thoroughly learnt that his proper sphere of action lay in close contiguity with Mrs. Proudie's wardrobe.", "그러나 제가 힘을 쓸 알맞은 무대는 프라우디 부인의 옷장에 바짝 붙은 자리에 있다는 것을 그는 확실히 배웠다."], ["He never again aspired to disobey, or seemed even to wish for autocratic diocesan authority.", "그는 다시는 거스르려 들지 않았고, 교구를 홀로 다스리는 권위를 바라는 기색조차 보이지 않았다."], ["If ever he thought of freedom, he did so as men think of the millennium, as of a good time which may be coming, but which nobody expects to come in their day.", "혹시라도 자유를 생각할 때면, 사람들이 천년왕국을 생각하듯 그리하였다. 곧 언젠가 올지도 모르나 제 살아생전에 오리라고는 아무도 기대하지 않는 좋은 시절로 여긴 것이다."], ["Mrs. Proudie might be said still to bloom, and was, at any rate, strong, and the bishop had no reason to apprehend that he would be speedily visited with the sorrows of a widower's life.", "프라우디 부인은 여전히 한창때라 할 만하였고 어쨌든 정정했으니, 주교로서는 머잖아 홀아비의 설움을 겪게 되리라고 걱정할 이유가 없었다."]], [["He is still Bishop of Barchester.", "그는 아직도 바체스터의 주교다."], ["He has so graced that throne that the Government has been averse to translate him, even to higher dignities.", "그가 그 자리를 어찌나 훌륭히 빛냈던지 정부는 그를 더 높은 지위로라도 옮기기를 꺼려 왔다."], ["There may he remain, under safe pupilage, till the newfangled manners of the age have discovered him to be superannuated and bestowed on him a pension.", "이 시대의 새삼스러운 풍조가 그를 노후한 사람으로 판정하여 연금을 내려 줄 때까지, 부디 그가 안전한 후견 아래 거기 머물기를."], ["As for Mrs. Proudie, our prayers for her are that she may live forever.", "프라우디 부인으로 말하자면, 우리가 그 부인을 위해 드리는 기도는 부디 영원히 살아 주십사 하는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LII. The New Dean Takes Possession of the Deanery, and the New Warden of the Hospital", "# 제52장. 새 수석 사제가 수석사제관에, 새 워든이 요양원에 들어서다"]], [["Mr. Harding and the archdeacon together made their way to Oxford, and there, by dint of cunning argument, they induced the Master of Lazarus also to ask himself this momentous question: \"Why should not Mr.", "하딩 씨와 부주교는 함께 옥스퍼드로 향했고, 거기서 교묘한 논변을 거듭한 끝에 라자러스 학장으로 하여금 스스로 이 중대한 물음을 던지게 만들었다."], ["Arabin be Dean of Barchester?\" He, of course, for awhile tried his hand at persuading Mr.", "“애러빈 씨가 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되어서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?”"], ["Harding that he was foolish, overscrupulous, self-willed, and weak-minded; but he tried in vain. If Mr.", "물론 학장도 한동안은 하딩 씨를 향해, 당신은 어리석고 지나치게 결벽하며 고집스럽고 마음이 여리다고 설득해 보려 애썼다."], ["Harding would not give way to Dr.", "그러나 헛수고였다."], ["Grantly, it was not likely that he would give way to Dr. Gwynne, more especially now that so admirable a scheme as that of inducting Mr.", "하딩 씨가 그랜틀리 박사에게 굽히지 않았거늘, 하물며 그윈 박사에게 굽힐 리가 있겠는가."], ["Arabin into the deanery had been set on foot. When the master found that his eloquence was vain, and heard also that Mr.", "더구나 애러빈 씨를 수석사제관에 들여앉힌다는 그토록 훌륭한 계획이 이미 첫발을 뗀 마당이었다."], ["Arabin was about to become Mr. Harding's son-in-law, he confessed that he also would, under such circumstances, be glad to see his old friend and protégé, the fellow of his college, placed in the comfortable position that was going a-begging.", "학장은 자신의 웅변이 소용없음을 깨닫고, 게다가 애러빈 씨가 곧 하딩 씨의 사위가 된다는 소식까지 듣게 되자, 사정이 그러하다면 자기 또한 오랜 벗이자 후원의 대상이며 제 칼리지의 펠로인 그 사람이 지금 주인 없이 굴러다니는 저 안락한 자리에 앉는 것을 기꺼이 보고 싶노라고 고백했다."]], [["\"It might be the means you know, Master, of keeping Mr. Slope out,\"", "“그렇게 되면 슬로프 씨를 막아 내는 방편도 되겠지요, 학장.”"], ["said the archdeacon with grave caution.", "부주교가 자못 신중한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"He has no more chance of it,\" said the master, \"than our college chaplain.", "“그자에게 그럴 가망이 있다면 우리 칼리지 전속 사제에게도 있겠소.”"], ["I know more about it than that.\"", "학장이 말했다. “나는 그 일에 관해서라면 그보다는 사정을 잘 알고 있소.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly had been right in her surmise.", "그랜틀리 부인의 짐작은 옳았다."], ["It was the Master of Lazarus who had been instrumental in representing in high places the claims which Mr. Harding had upon the Government, and he now consented to use his best endeavours towards getting the offer transferred to Mr.", "하딩 씨가 정부에 대해 지닌 자격을 높은 자리에 있는 이들에게 알린 장본인이 바로 라자러스 학장이었고, 그는 이제 그 제안을 애러빈 씨에게로 옮겨 놓기 위해 힘닿는 데까지 애써 보겠노라 승낙했다."], ["Arabin. The three of them went on to London together, and there they remained a week, to the great disgust of Mrs.", "세 사람은 함께 런던으로 올라가 일주일을 머물렀는데, 이는 그랜틀리 부인에게 크나큰 불만이었고 필시 그윈 부인에게도 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["Grantly, and most probably also of Mrs. Gwynne.", "장관은 이쪽으로 출타 중이었고 그 개인 비서는 저쪽으로 출타 중이었다."], ["The minister was out of town in one direction, and his private secretary in another.", "남아 있는 서기들이야 이런 일에 아무것도 할 수 없었으니, 모든 것이 난감하고 어수선했다."], ["The clerks who remained could do nothing in such a matter as this, and all was difficulty and confusion.", "두 박사는 할 일이 넘치는 듯 보였다."], ["The two doctors seemed to have plenty to do; they bustled here and they bustled there, and complained at their club in the evenings that they had been driven off their legs; but Mr.", "이리 부산을 떨고 저리 부산을 떨고는, 저녁이면 클럽에서 다리가 부러지도록 끌려다녔노라고 투덜거렸다. 그러나 하딩 씨에게는 아무 소임이 없었다."], ["Harding had no occupation. Once or twice he suggested that he might perhaps return to Barchester.", "그는 한두 번, 자기는 바체스터로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다고 제안해 보았다."], ["His request, however, was peremptorily refused, and he had nothing for it but to while away his time in Westminster Abbey.", "그러나 그 청은 단칼에 거절당했고, 그로서는 웨스트민스터 사원에서 시간을 흘려보내는 수밖에 달리 도리가 없었다."]], [["At length an answer from the great man came.", "마침내 그 높으신 어른에게서 회답이 왔다."], ["The Master of Lazarus had made his proposition through the Bishop of Belgravia.", "라자러스 학장은 벨그레이비아 주교를 통해 자신의 제안을 넣어 두었던 터였다."], ["Now this bishop, though but newly gifted with his diocesan honours, was a man of much weight in the clerico-political world.", "그런데 이 주교로 말하자면, 교구를 다스리는 영예를 얻은 것은 얼마 되지 않았으나 성직과 정치가 뒤엉킨 세계에서는 무게가 자못 대단한 인물이었다."], ["He was, if not as pious, at any rate as wise as St. Paul, and had been with so much effect all things to all men that, though he was great among the dons of Oxford, he had been selected for the most favourite seat on the bench by a Whig prime minister.", "그는 성 바울만큼 경건하지는 않았을지 몰라도 적어도 성 바울만큼 지혜로웠고, 여러 사람에게 여러 모습이 되어 주는 재주를 어찌나 요긴하게 부렸던지, 옥스퍼드의 학감들 사이에서 큰 인물이면서도 휘그당 총리의 손에 뽑혀 주교석 가운데 가장 탐나는 자리를 차지하기에 이르렀다."], ["To him Dr. Gwynne had made known his wishes and his arguments, and the bishop had made them known to the Marquis of Kensington-Gore.", "그윈 박사는 이 주교에게 자신의 소원과 논거를 알렸고, 주교는 그것을 다시 켄싱턴고어 후작의 귀에 넣었다."], ["The marquis, who was Lord High Steward of the Pantry Board, and who by most men was supposed to hold the highest office out of the cabinet, trafficked much in affairs of this kind.", "후작은 식품국 대집사장이자, 내각 바깥의 직위 중에서는 가장 높은 자리에 앉아 있다고 세상 사람 대다수가 여기는 인물이었으며, 이런 종류의 일이라면 거래에 이골이 나 있었다."], ["He not only suggested the arrangement to the minister over a cup of coffee, standing on a drawing-room rug in Windsor Castle, but he also favourably mentioned Mr. Arabin's name in the ear of a distinguished person.", "그는 윈저 성의 응접실 양탄자 위에 선 채 커피 한 잔을 들며 장관에게 그 조처를 넌지시 권했을 뿐 아니라, 어느 지체 높은 분의 귀에다 애러빈 씨의 이름을 좋게 흘려 넣어 두기까지 했다."]], [["And so the matter was arranged.", "그리하여 일은 매듭지어졌다."], ["The answer of the great man came, and Mr. Arabin was made Dean of Barchester.", "높으신 어른의 회답이 도착했고, 애러빈 씨는 바체스터의 수석 사제가 되었다."], ["The three clergymen who had come up to town on this important mission dined together with great glee on the day on which the news reached them.", "이 중대한 사명을 띠고 런던에 올라왔던 세 성직자는 소식이 당도한 그날 함께 저녁을 들며 크게 흡족해했다."], ["In a silent, decent, clerical manner they toasted Mr. Arabin with full bumpers of claret.", "그들은 조용하고 점잖은, 성직자다운 태도로 클라레를 가득 채운 잔을 들어 애러빈 씨를 위해 건배했다."], ["The satisfaction of all of them was supreme.", "세 사람 모두 더할 나위 없이 만족스러웠다."], ["The Master of Lazarus had been successful in his attempt, and success is dear to us all. The archdeacon had trampled upon Mr.", "라자러스 학장은 자신의 시도에 성공을 거두었으니, 성공이란 누구에게나 달콤한 법이다."], ["Slope, and had lifted to high honours the young clergyman whom he had induced to quit the retirement and comfort of the university.", "부주교는 슬로프 씨를 짓밟았고, 대학의 은거와 안락에서 끌어낸 그 젊은 성직자를 높은 영예의 자리에 올려놓았다. 적어도 부주교는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["So at least the archdeacon thought; though, to speak sooth, not he, but circumstances, had trampled on Mr. Slope.", "그러나 사실대로 말하자면 슬로프 씨를 짓밟은 것은 부주교가 아니라 정황이었다."], ["But the satisfaction of Mr. Harding was, of all, perhaps, the most complete.", "그렇지만 그 가운데 아마도 가장 온전한 만족을 누린 사람은 하딩 씨였을 것이다."], ["He laid aside his usual melancholy manner and brought forth little quiet jokes from the inmost mirth of his heart; he poked his fun at the archdeacon about Mr.", "그는 늘 몸에 지니고 다니던 우울한 태도를 잠시 내려놓고 마음속 가장 깊은 흥에서 길어 올린 조용한 농담을 몇 마디 내놓았다."], ["Slope's marriage and quizzed him for his improper love for Mrs. Proudie.", "슬로프 씨의 혼사를 두고 부주교를 놀렸고, 프라우디 부인을 향한 부주교의 온당치 못한 연정을 들먹이며 그를 골렸다."], ["On the following day they all returned to Barchester.", "이튿날 그들은 모두 바체스터로 돌아갔다."]], [["It was arranged that Mr. Arabin should know nothing of what had been done till he received the minister's letter from the hands of his embryo father-in-law.", "애러빈 씨는 장인이 될 사람의 손에서 장관의 서한을 건네받기 전까지는 그동안 벌어진 일을 아무것도 모르고 있어야 한다는 것이 그들의 계획이었다."], ["In order that no time might be lost, a message had been sent to him by the preceding night's post, begging him to be at the deanery at the hour that the train from London arrived. There was nothing in this which surprised Mr.", "시간을 조금도 허비하지 않으려고 전날 밤 우편으로 그에게 전갈을 띄워, 런던발 기차가 닿는 시각에 수석사제관에 와 있어 달라고 청해 두었다."], ["Arabin. It had somehow got about through all Barchester that Mr.", "애러빈 씨로서는 이 일에 놀랄 구석이 없었다."], ["Harding was the new dean, and all Barchester was prepared to welcome him with pealing bells and full hearts. Mr.", "어느 결엔가 하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제라는 말이 바체스터 전역에 퍼져 있었고, 바체스터 전체가 종을 울리고 마음을 다해 그를 맞이할 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["Slope had certainly had a party; there had certainly been those in Barchester who were prepared to congratulate him on his promotion with assumed sincerity, but even his own party was not broken-hearted by his failure.", "슬로프 씨에게도 물론 제 편이 있기는 했다. 그의 승진을 두고 진심인 척 축하할 태세를 갖춘 자들이 바체스터에 없지는 않았다."], ["The inhabitants of the city, even the high-souled, ecstatic young ladies of thirty-five, had begun to comprehend that their welfare, and the welfare of the place, was connected in some mysterious manner with daily chants and bi-weekly anthems.", "그러나 그의 실패에 가슴이 미어진 사람은 그 편에서조차 없었다. 이 도시의 주민들은, 심지어 마음이 드높고 감격에 잘 젖는 서른다섯 살 처녀들까지도, 자기네의 복락과 이 고장의 복락이 날마다의 성가와 주 두 번의 찬송가에 어떤 신비로운 방식으로 얽혀 있음을 차츰 깨닫기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["The expenditure of the palace had not added much to the popularity of the bishop's side of the question; and, on the whole, there was a strong reaction.", "주교관의 씀씀이는 주교 쪽 주장의 인기를 그다지 높여 주지 못했다. 요컨대 여론은 크게 되돌아서 있었다."], ["When it became known to all the world that Mr. Harding was to be the new dean, all the world rejoiced heartily.", "하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 된다는 것이 온 세상에 알려지자, 온 세상이 진심으로 기뻐했다."]], [["Mr. Arabin, we have said, was not surprised at the summons which called him to the deanery. He had not as yet seen Mr. Harding since Eleanor had accepted him, nor had he seen him since he had learnt his future father-in-law's preferment. There was nothing more natural, more necessary, than that they should meet each other at the earliest possible moment. Mr. Arabin was waiting in the deanery parlour when Mr. Harding and Dr. Grantly were driven up from the station.", "앞서 말했듯 애러빈 씨는 자기를 수석사제관으로 부르는 전갈에 놀라지 않았다. 그는 엘리너가 자기를 받아들인 뒤로 아직 하딩 씨를 만난 적이 없었고, 장차 장인이 될 이의 서임 소식을 들은 뒤로도 마찬가지였다. 두 사람이 되도록 이른 시각에 서로 얼굴을 마주하는 것보다 더 자연스럽고 더 긴요한 일은 없었다. 하딩 씨와 그랜틀리 박사가 역에서 마차로 실려 왔을 때 애러빈 씨는 수석사제관 응접실에서 기다리고 있었다."]], [["There was some excitement in the bosoms of them all, as they met and shook hands; by far too much to enable either of them to begin his story and tell it in a proper equable style of narrative.", "세 사람이 만나 악수를 나눌 때 그 가슴속에는 저마다 적잖은 흥분이 일었다."], ["Mr. Harding was some minutes quite dumbfounded, and Mr.", "누구든 제 이야기를 꺼내어 차분하고 조리 있는 화법으로 늘어놓기에는 지나치게 큰 흥분이었다."], ["Arabin could only talk in short, spasmodic sentences about his love and good fortune.", "하딩 씨는 몇 분 동안 아예 말문이 막혀 있었고, 애러빈 씨는 자신의 사랑과 행운에 관해 짧고 발작하듯 끊기는 문장으로밖에 말하지 못했다."], ["He slipped in, as best he could, some sort of congratulation about the deanship, and then went on with his hopes and fears--hopes that he might be received as a son, and fears that he hardly deserved such good fortune.", "그는 수석 사제직에 대한 축하의 말을 나름대로 애써 끼워 넣고는, 다시 제 희망과 두려움으로 돌아갔다. 아들로 받아들여지기를 바란다는 희망과, 이만한 행운을 누릴 자격이 자기에게 있을까 하는 두려움이었다."], ["Then he went back to the dean; it was the most thoroughly satisfactory appointment, he said, of which he had ever heard.", "그러고는 또 수석 사제 이야기로 돌아와, 이제껏 들어 본 것 중 가장 흡족한 임명이라고 말했다."]], [["\"But! But!", "“하지만!"], ["But--\"", "하지만!"], ["said Mr.", "하지만…….”"], ["Harding, and then, failing to get any further, he looked imploringly at the archdeacon.", "하딩 씨는 그렇게 말하고는 더 나아가지 못한 채 애원하는 눈으로 부주교를 바라보았다."]], [["\"The truth is, Arabin,\"", "“사실은 이렇소, 애러빈.”"], ["said the doctor, \"that, after all you are not destined to be son-in-law to a dean.", "박사가 말했다. “결국 그대는 수석 사제의 사위가 될 운명이 아니오."], ["Nor am I either: more's the pity.\"", "나 또한 아니고. 그 점이 더욱 애석한 일이지.”"]], [["Mr. Arabin looked at him for explanation.", "애러빈 씨는 설명을 구하는 눈길로 그를 보았다."], ["\"Is not Mr. Harding to be the new dean?\"", "“하딩 씨가 새 수석 사제가 되시는 것이 아니오?”"]], [["\"It appears not,\"", "“아무래도 아닌 모양이오.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["Mr. Arabin's face fell a little, and he looked from one to the other.", "애러빈 씨의 얼굴이 조금 어두워졌고, 그는 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["It was plainly to be seen from them both that there was no cause of unhappiness in the matter, at least not of unhappiness to them; but there was as yet no elucidation of the mystery.", "이 일에 슬퍼할 까닭이 없다는 것은, 적어도 저 두 사람에게는 슬퍼할 까닭이 없다는 것은 그들의 표정에서 훤히 읽을 수 있었다. 그러나 수수께끼는 아직 풀리지 않은 채였다."]], [["\"Think how old I am,\" said Mr.", "“내 나이가 몇인지 좀 생각해 보게.”"], ["Harding imploringly.", "하딩 씨가 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["\"Fiddlestick!\"", "“당치 않은 말씀이십니다!”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["\"That's all very well, but it won't make a young man of me,\"", "“그렇게 말하면야 좋지. 하지만 그런다고 내가 젊은이가 되는 것은 아니지 않은가.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"And who is to be dean?\"", "“그러면 수석 사제는 누가 되는 것이오?”"], ["asked Mr. Arabin.", "애러빈 씨가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, that's the question,\"", "“그렇지, 바로 그것이 문제요.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"Come, Mr. Precentor, since you obstinately refuse to be anything else, let us know who is to be the man.", "“자, 선창자님, 다른 것은 무엇이든 한사코 마다하시니, 그러면 그 사람이 누구인지나 알려 주시지요."], ["He has got the nomination in his pocket.\"", "임명장은 저분 주머니 속에 들어 있소.”"]], [["With eyes brim full of tears, Mr. Harding pulled out the letter and handed it to his future son-in-law.", "눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로 하딩 씨는 편지를 꺼내어 장차 사위가 될 사람에게 건넸다."], ["He tried to make a little speech but failed altogether.", "그는 짧은 말을 한마디 하려다가 끝내 하지 못했다."], ["Having given up the document, he turned round to the wall, feigning to blow his nose, and then sat himself down on the old dean's dingy horsehair sofa.", "서한을 넘겨준 뒤 그는 코를 푸는 시늉을 하며 벽 쪽으로 돌아섰고, 이윽고 전임 수석 사제가 쓰던 낡고 칙칙한 말총 소파에 앉았다."], ["And here we find it necessary to bring our account of the interview to an end.", "그리고 이 자리에서 이 만남에 대한 우리의 서술을 끝맺는 것이 마땅하리라 여겨진다."]], [["Nor can we pretend to describe the rapture with which Mr.", "하딩 씨가 딸에게서 어떤 환희로 맞아들여졌는지는 우리로서도 감히 그려 낼 재간이 없다."], ["Harding was received by his daughter.", "그녀는 슬픔으로 울고 또 기쁨으로 울었다."], ["She wept with grief and wept with joy--with grief that her father should, in his old age, still be without that rank and worldly position which, according to her ideas, he had so well earned; and with joy in that he, her darling father, should have bestowed on that other dear one the good things of which he himself would not open his hand to take possession. And here Mr.", "아버지가 그 노년에 이르러서도, 제 생각으로는 그토록 마땅히 얻으신 지위와 세속의 자리를 여전히 갖지 못하신 것이 슬퍼서 울었고, 사랑하는 그 아버지가 정작 당신은 손을 펴서 가지려 하지 않으신 그 좋은 것을 또 하나의 소중한 이에게 내어 주신 것이 기뻐서 울었다."], ["Harding again showed his weakness. In the mêlée of this exposal of their loves and reciprocal affection, he found himself unable to resist the entreaties of all parties that the lodgings in the High Street should be given up.", "그리고 여기서 하딩 씨는 다시 한번 자신의 여림을 드러냈다."], ["Eleanor would not live in the deanery, she said, unless her father lived there also. Mr.", "서로의 사랑과 애정을 털어놓느라 벌어진 이 북새통 속에서 그는 하이 스트리트의 셋방을 정리하라는 모두의 간청을 끝내 물리치지 못했다."], ["Arabin would not be dean, unless Mr. Harding would be co-dean with him.", "엘리너는 아버지가 함께 사시지 않는다면 수석사제관에서 살지 않겠다고 했다."], ["The archdeacon declared that his father-in-law should not have his own way in everything, and Mrs. Grantly carried him off to Plumstead, that he might remain there till Mr.", "애러빈 씨는 하딩 씨가 자기와 더불어 공동 수석 사제가 되어 주지 않으신다면 수석 사제 노릇을 하지 않겠다고 했다."], ["and Mrs. Arabin were in a state to receive him in their own mansion.", "부주교는 장인이 매사를 제 뜻대로 하시게 둘 수는 없다고 선언했고, 그랜틀리 부인은 애러빈 씨 내외가 제 저택에서 아버지를 모실 형편이 될 때까지 머무시게 하려고 그를 플럼스테드로 데려갔다."]], [["Pressed by such arguments as these, what could a weak old man do but yield?", "이런 논거들에 밀렸으니, 여린 노인이 굽히는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["But there was yet another task which it behoved Mr. Harding to do before he could allow himself to be at rest.", "그러나 하딩 씨가 마음 놓고 쉬기 전에 아직 치러야 할 일이 하나 더 남아 있었다."], ["Little has been said in these pages of the state of those remaining old men who had lived under his sway at the hospital.", "요양원에서 그의 다스림 아래 살던 노인들이 그 뒤로 어찌 지내는지에 관해서는 이 지면에서 별로 이야기하지 않았다."], ["But not on this account must it be presumed that he had forgotten them, or that in their state of anarchy and in their want of due government he had omitted to visit them.", "그렇다고 해서 그가 그들을 잊었다거나, 다스리는 이 없이 어지러운 그 처지에 놓인 그들을 찾아보지 않았다고 짐작해서는 안 된다."], ["He visited them constantly, and had latterly given them to understand that they would soon be required to subscribe their adherence to a new master.", "그는 꾸준히 그들을 찾아갔고, 근래에는 머지않아 새 주인에게 복종을 서약해야 할 것이라는 뜻을 그들에게 알려 두었다. 이제 남은 사람은 다섯뿐이었다."], ["There were now but five of them, one of them having been but quite lately carried to his rest--but five of the full number, which had hitherto been twelve, and which was now to be raised to twenty-four, including women.", "그중 하나는 바로 얼마 전에 안식의 자리로 실려 간 터였다. 본래 정원은 열두 명이었으나 남은 이는 다섯뿐이었고, 이제 그 정원은 여자들까지 넣어 스물네 명으로 늘어나게 되어 있었다."], ["Of these, old Bunce, who for many years had been the favourite of the late warden, was one; and Abel Handy, who had been the humble means of driving that warden from his home, was another.", "남은 다섯 중에는 오랜 세월 전임 워든의 각별한 사랑을 받아 온 늙은 번스가 있었고, 그 워든을 제집에서 몰아내는 데 보잘것없는 앞잡이 노릇을 했던 에이블 핸디도 있었다."]], [["Mr. Harding now resolved that he himself would introduce the new warden to the hospital.", "하딩 씨는 이제 자기가 손수 새 워든을 요양원에 소개하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He felt that many circumstances might conspire to make the men receive Mr. Quiverful with aversion and disrespect; he felt also that Mr.", "그는 여러 사정이 겹쳐 노인들이 퀴버풀 씨를 못마땅해하고 업신여기며 맞을지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["Quiverful might himself feel some qualms of conscience if he entered the hospital with an idea that he did so in hostility to his predecessor. Mr.", "또한 퀴버풀 씨 자신도 전임자를 밀어내고 들어간다는 생각을 품은 채 요양원에 들어선다면 양심에 거리끼는 바가 없지 않으리라고 여겼다."], ["Harding therefore determined to walk in, arm in arm with Mr. Quiverful, and to ask from these men their respectful obedience to their new master.", "그리하여 하딩 씨는 퀴버풀 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 함께 걸어 들어가, 그 노인들에게 새 주인을 삼가 따르라고 당부하기로 작정했다."]], [["On returning to Barchester, he found that Mr. Quiverful had not yet slept in the hospital house, or entered on his new duties.", "바체스터로 돌아온 그는 퀴버풀 씨가 아직 요양원 사택에서 하룻밤도 자지 않았고 새 소임을 시작하지도 않았음을 알게 되었다."], ["He accordingly made known to that gentleman his wishes, and his proposition was not rejected.", "그래서 그는 자신의 뜻을 그 신사에게 알렸고, 그의 제안은 거절당하지 않았다."]], [["It was a bright, clear morning, though in November, that Mr. Harding and Mr.", "11월이었으나 밝고 맑은 아침에 하딩 씨와 퀴버풀 씨는 팔짱을 낀 채 요양원 문을 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Quiverful, arm in arm, walked through the hospital gate. It was one trait in our old friend's character that he did nothing with parade.", "무슨 일이든 요란을 떨지 않는 것이 우리 오랜 벗의 한 가지 됨됨이였다."], ["He omitted, even in the more important doings of his life, that sort of parade by which most of us deem it necessary to grace our important doings.", "그는 제 인생의 자못 중대한 일에서조차, 우리 대다수가 중대한 일에는 으레 격을 갖춰야 한다고 여기는 그런 요란을 생략했다. 우리는 집들이를 하고 세례식을 하고 경축일을 벌인다."], ["We have house-warmings, christenings, and gala days; we keep, if not our own birthdays, those of our children; we are apt to fuss ourselves if called upon to change our residences and have, almost all of us, our little state occasions.", "제 생일은 아니더라도 자식들의 생일은 챙긴다. 사는 집을 옮겨야 할 일이라도 생기면 곧잘 부산을 떨며, 거의 누구나 저마다 자그마한 예식의 날을 지닌다."], ["Mr. Harding had no state occasions.", "하딩 씨에게는 그런 예식의 날이 없었다."], ["When he left his old house, he went forth from it with the same quiet composure as though he were merely taking his daily walk; now that he re-entered it with another warden under his wing, he did so with the same quiet step and calm demeanour.", "옛집을 떠날 때 그는 그저 날마다의 산책을 나서는 사람처럼 조용하고 담담하게 그곳을 나섰고, 이제 또 한 사람의 워든을 팔에 끼고 그 집으로 되돌아 들어가면서도 똑같이 조용한 걸음과 잔잔한 몸가짐이었다. 그는 5년 전, 아니 이제는 6년이 다 되었으니, 그때보다 허리가 조금 덜 곧았다."], ["He was a little less upright than he had been five years, nay, it was now nearly six years ago; he walked perhaps a little slower; his footfall was perhaps a thought less firm; otherwise one might have said that he was merely returning with a friend under his arm.", "걸음도 아마 조금은 느려졌을 것이고, 발소리도 아마 어딘가 덜 단단해졌을 것이다. 그 밖에는, 그저 벗을 팔에 끼고 돌아오는 길이라 해도 좋았을 법했다."]], [["This friendliness was everything to Mr. Quiverful.", "이 다정함이 퀴버풀 씨에게는 무엇보다 큰 것이었다."], ["To him, even in his poverty, the thought that he was supplanting a brother clergyman so kind and courteous as Mr.", "그토록 가난한 처지에 있으면서도, 하딩 씨처럼 친절하고 정중한 동료 성직자를 밀어내고 그 자리에 들어앉는다는 생각은 그에게 몹시 쓰라린 것이었다."], ["Harding had been very bitter.", "형편이 형편이니만큼 그로서는 내밀어진 은혜를 마다할 도리가 없었다."], ["Under his circumstances it had been impossible for him to refuse the proffered boon; he could not reject the bread that was offered to his children, or refuse to ease the heavy burden that had so long oppressed that poor wife of his; nevertheless, it had been very grievous to him to think that in going to the hospital he might encounter the ill-will of his brethren in the diocese.", "자식들에게 돌아올 빵을 물리칠 수도 없었고, 그토록 오래 짓눌려 온 가엾은 아내의 무거운 짐을 덜어 주기를 마다할 수도 없었다. 그럼에도 요양원에 들어감으로써 교구 안 형제들의 미움을 사게 될지 모른다는 생각은 그에게 몹시 괴로웠다."], ["All this Mr. Harding had fully comprehended.", "하딩 씨는 이 모든 것을 남김없이 헤아리고 있었다."], ["It was for such feelings as these, for the nice comprehension of such motives, that his heart and intellect were peculiarly fitted.", "그런 감정들, 그런 속마음의 결을 섬세하게 알아채는 데에는 그의 마음과 지성이 유별나게 잘 맞았다."], ["In most matters of worldly import the archdeacon set down his father-in-law as little better than a fool.", "세상살이에 속하는 일이라면 부주교는 제 장인을 바보나 다름없다고 여겼다."], ["And perhaps he was right.", "그리고 그 판단은 어쩌면 옳았을 것이다."], ["But in some other matters, equally important if they be rightly judged, Mr. Harding, had he been so minded, might with as much propriety have set down his son-in-law for a fool.", "그러나 제대로 헤아리기만 한다면 그에 못지않게 중요한 다른 몇 가지 일에서는, 하딩 씨가 마음만 먹었다면 제 사위를 바보로 여겨도 그만큼 무리가 없었을 것이다."], ["Few men, however, are constituted as was Mr.", "그러나 하딩 씨처럼 생겨먹은 사람은 드물다."], ["Harding. He had that nice appreciation of the feelings of others which belongs of right exclusively to women.", "그는 본디 여자에게만 오롯이 허락된 저 섬세한 감수성, 남의 마음을 알아보는 그 감각을 지니고 있었다."]], [["Arm in arm they walked into the inner quadrangle of the building, and there the five old men met them. Mr. Harding shook hands with them all, and then Mr. Quiverful did the same. With Bunce Mr. Harding shook hands twice, and Mr. Quiverful was about to repeat the same ceremony, but the old man gave him no encouragement.", "두 사람은 팔짱을 낀 채 건물 안쪽 안뜰로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기서 다섯 노인이 그들을 맞았다. 하딩 씨는 그들 모두와 악수를 나누었고, 이어 퀴버풀 씨도 그렇게 했다. 번스와는 하딩 씨가 두 번 악수를 했고, 퀴버풀 씨도 같은 예를 되풀이하려 했으나 노인은 그에게 그럴 틈을 주지 않았다."]], [["\"I am very glad to know that at last you have a new warden,\"", "“드디어 자네들에게 새 워든이 생겼다니 나는 참으로 기쁘네.”"], ["said Mr. Harding in a very cheery voice.", "하딩 씨가 아주 밝은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"We be very old for any change,\"", "“저희야 이제 무슨 변화든 겪기에는 너무 늙었습죠.”"], ["said one of them, \"but we do suppose it be all for the best.\"", "그중 한 사람이 말했다. “그래도 다 잘되자고 하는 일이겠습니다요.”"]], [["\"Certainly--certainly it is for the best,\"", "“그렇고말고, 그렇고말고, 다 잘되자는 일이지.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["\"You will again have a clergyman of your own church under the same roof with you, and a very excellent clergyman you will have.", "“자네들은 다시 한 지붕 아래에서 자네들 교회의 성직자를 모시게 되었고, 그것도 아주 훌륭한 분을 모시게 되었네."], ["It is a great satisfaction to me to know that so good a man is coming to take care of you, and that it is no stranger, but a friend of my own who will allow me from time to time to come in and see you.\"", "저렇게 좋은 분이 자네들을 돌보러 오신다는 것, 그것도 낯선 사람이 아니라 내 벗이어서 내가 이따금 들러 자네들을 보고 갈 수 있게 해 주리라는 것이 나에게는 큰 기쁨일세.”"]], [["\"We be very thankful to your Reverence,\"", "“목사님께 참으로 감사드립니다요.”"], ["said another of them.", "그중 또 한 사람이 말했다."]], [["\"I need not tell you, my good friends,\" said Mr.", "“여러분, 하딩 씨가 저에게 베풀어 주신 친절에, 그것도 청하지도 않았고 뜻하지도 않았던 그 친절에 제가 얼마나 깊이 감사하고 있는지는 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지요.”"], ["Quiverful, \"how extremely grateful I am to Mr. Harding for his kindness to me--I must say his uncalled-for, unexpected kindness.\"", "퀴버풀 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"He be always very kind,\"", "“저분은 늘 인정이 깊으셨습죠.”"], ["said a third.", "세 번째 사람이 말했다."]], [["\"What I can do to fill the void which he left here I will do.", "“저분이 여기 남기고 가신 빈자리를 메우기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠습니다."], ["For your sake and my own I will do so, and especially for his sake.", "여러분을 위해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서 그리하겠고, 무엇보다 저분을 위해서 그리하겠습니다."], ["But to you who have known him, I can never be the same well-loved friend and father that he has been.\"", "그러나 저분을 겪어 아시는 여러분께 제가 저분처럼 사랑받는 벗이자 아버지가 될 수는 결코 없을 것입니다.”"]], [["\"No, sir, no,\"", "“아니올시다, 나리, 아니올시다.”"], ["said old Bunce, who hitherto had held his peace; \"no one can be that.", "여태 잠자코 있던 늙은 번스가 말했다. “그리될 사람은 아무도 없습죠."], ["Not if the new bishop sent a hangel to us out of heaven.", "새 주교님이 하늘에서 천사를 하나 내려보내 주신대도 안 될 일입니다요."], ["We doesn't doubt you'll do your best, sir, but you'll not be like the old master--not to us old ones.\"", "나리께서 힘껏 하시리라는 것이야 저희도 의심치 않습니다만, 나리는 옛 주인님 같지는 않으실 겁니다요. 적어도 저희 늙은것들에게는요.”"]], [["\"Fie, Bunce, fie; how dare you talk in that way?\"", "“쯧쯧, 번스, 쯧쯧. 어찌 그런 말을 하는가?”"], ["said Mr. Harding; but as he scolded the old man he still held him by his arm and pressed it with warm affection.", "하딩 씨가 그렇게 나무랐으나, 노인을 꾸짖으면서도 그는 여전히 그의 팔을 붙든 채 따뜻한 정을 담아 꼭 눌러 주었다."]], [["There was no getting up any enthusiasm in the matter.", "이 일에서 열띤 기색이라고는 도무지 끌어낼 수가 없었다."], ["How could five old men tottering away to their final resting place be enthusiastic on the reception of a stranger? What could Mr.", "마지막 안식처를 향해 비틀비틀 걸어가고 있는 다섯 노인이 낯선 사람을 맞아들이는 일에 어찌 열을 낼 수 있겠는가?"], ["Quiverful be to them, or they to Mr. Quiverful?", "퀴버풀 씨가 그들에게 무엇이며, 그들이 퀴버풀 씨에게 또 무엇이겠는가?"], ["Had Mr. Harding indeed come back to them, some last flicker of joyous light might have shone forth on their aged cheeks; but it was in vain to bid them rejoice because Mr.", "하딩 씨가 정말로 그들에게 돌아왔더라면 늙은 뺨 위에 기쁨의 빛이 마지막으로 한 번쯤 반짝였을지도 모른다."], ["Quiverful was about to move his fourteen children from Puddingdale into the hospital house.", "그러나 퀴버풀 씨가 자녀 열넷을 푸딩데일에서 요양원 사택으로 옮겨 온다고 해서 기뻐하라고 하는 것은 부질없는 노릇이었다."], ["In reality they did no doubt receive advantage, spiritual as well as corporal, but this they could neither anticipate nor acknowledge.", "사실 그들이 몸으로나 영으로나 이로움을 얻게 되리라는 것은 의심할 바 없었으나, 그것은 그들이 미리 헤아릴 수도 없었고 인정할 수도 없는 것이었다."]], [["It was a dull affair enough, this introduction of Mr. Quiverful, but still it had its effect. The good which Mr. Harding intended did not fall to the ground. All the Barchester world, including the five old bedesmen, treated Mr. Quiverful with the more respect because Mr. Harding had thus walked in, arm in arm with him, on his first entrance to his duties.", "퀴버풀 씨의 이 소개는 어지간히도 밋밋한 일이었으나, 그래도 효험이 없지는 않았다. 하딩 씨가 뜻한 선의는 땅에 떨어지지 않았다. 다섯 구호 노인을 비롯한 바체스터의 온 세상은, 하딩 씨가 그렇게 퀴버풀 씨와 팔짱을 끼고 그의 첫 취임 길에 함께 걸어 들어갔다는 이유로 그를 한결 더 존중하게 되었다."]], [["And here in their new abode we will leave Mr. and Mrs.", "그리고 우리는 이 새 거처에 퀴버풀 씨 내외와 그 열네 자녀를 남겨 두고 떠나기로 한다."], ["Quiverful and their fourteen children. May they enjoy the good things which Providence has at length given to them!", "섭리가 마침내 그들에게 내려 준 좋은 것들을 부디 마음껏 누리기를!"]], [["# CHAPTER LIII. Conclusion", "# 제53장. 맺음말"]], [["The end of a novel, like the end of a children's dinner party, must be made up of sweetmeats and sugar-plums. There is now nothing else to be told but the gala doings of Mr.", "소설의 끝은 아이들 만찬회의 끝이 그러하듯 과자와 사탕으로 채워져야 하는 법이다."], ["Arabin's marriage, nothing more to be described than the wedding-dresses, no further dialogue to be recorded than that which took place between the archdeacon, who married them, and Mr. Arabin and Eleanor, who were married.", "이제 더 들려줄 것이라고는 애러빈 씨의 혼례에 얽힌 잔치 이야기뿐이고, 더 그려 볼 것이라고는 혼례복밖에 없으며, 더 적어 둘 대화라고는 두 사람을 혼인시킨 부주교와 혼인한 애러빈 씨와 엘리너 사이에 오간 말뿐이다."]], [["\"Wilt thou have this woman to thy wedded wife,\" and \"wilt thou have this man to thy wedded husband, to live together according to God's ordinance?\"", "“그대는 이 여자를 아내로 맞아들이겠는가”, 그리고 “그대는 이 남자를 남편으로 맞아들여 하느님이 정하신 법도를 따라 함께 살겠는가?”"]], [["Mr.", "애러빈 씨와 엘리너는 저마다 “그리하겠나이다”"], ["Arabin and Eleanor each answered, \"I will.\"", "하고 대답했다."]], [["We have no doubt that they will keep their promises, the more especially as the Signora Neroni had left Barchester before the ceremony was performed.", "두 사람이 그 약속을 지키리라는 데에는 우리로서도 의심할 바가 없다. 더구나 시뇨라 네로니는 예식이 치러지기 전에 이미 바체스터를 떠난 뒤였으니 더욱 그러하다."]], [["Mrs. Bold had been somewhat more than two years a widow before she was married to her second husband, and little Johnny was then able with due assistance to walk on his own legs into the drawing-room to receive the salutations of the assembled guests.", "볼드 부인은 두 번째 남편과 혼인하기까지 이태 남짓 미망인으로 지냈고, 그 무렵 어린 조니는 부축만 받으면 제 다리로 응접실까지 걸어 들어와 모여 있는 손님들의 인사를 받을 만큼 자라 있었다."], ["Mr. Harding gave away the bride, the archdeacon performed the service, and the two Miss Grantlys, who were joined in their labours by other young ladies of the neighbourhood, performed the duties of bridesmaids with equal diligence and grace.", "하딩 씨가 신부를 넘겨주었고, 부주교가 예식을 집전했으며, 이웃의 다른 젊은 아가씨들까지 거들어 함께 나선 그랜틀리 집안의 두 딸이 부지런하고도 우아하게 들러리 소임을 다했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly superintended the breakfast and bouquets, and Mary Bold distributed the cards and cake.", "그랜틀리 부인은 피로연 음식과 꽃다발을 감독했고, 메리 볼드는 청첩 카드와 케이크를 나누어 주었다."], ["The archdeacon's three sons had also come home for the occasion.", "부주교의 세 아들도 이 일을 맞아 집에 돌아와 있었다."], ["The elder was great with learning, being regarded by all who knew him as a certain future double first.", "맏이는 학문이 대단하여 그를 아는 이라면 누구나 장차 반드시 양과 최우등을 받으리라 여겼다."], ["The second, however, bore the palm on this occasion, being resplendent in a new uniform.", "그러나 이날 으뜸가는 영광은 둘째의 몫이었으니, 새 군복 차림으로 눈부셨던 까닭이다."], ["The third was just entering the university, and was probably the proudest of the three.", "셋째는 이제 막 대학에 들어가는 참이었는데, 아마 셋 가운데 가장 우쭐한 것이 그였을 것이다."]], [["But the most remarkable feature in the whole occasion was the excessive liberality of the archdeacon.", "그러나 이날의 모든 일 가운데 가장 볼만한 대목은 부주교의 지나칠 만큼 후한 인심이었다."], ["He literally made presents to everybody. As Mr.", "그는 말 그대로 아무에게나 선물을 안겼다."], ["Arabin had already moved out of the parsonage of St. Ewold's, that scheme of elongating the dining-room was of course abandoned; but he would have refurnished the whole deanery had he been allowed.", "애러빈 씨가 이미 세인트 이월드 목사관에서 나온 뒤였으므로 식당을 늘려 짓겠다던 계획은 물론 없던 일이 되었지만, 허락만 받았더라면 그는 수석사제관의 세간을 죄다 새로 들여놓았을 것이다."], ["He sent down a magnificent piano by Erard, gave Mr. Arabin a cob which any dean in the land might have been proud to bestride, and made a special present to Eleanor of a new pony chair that had gained a prize in the Exhibition.", "그는 에라르제 훌륭한 피아노를 내려보냈고, 이 나라 어느 수석 사제가 올라타도 자랑스러웠을 만한 승용마를 애러빈 씨에게 주었으며, 엘리너에게는 박람회에서 상을 받은 새 조랑말 마차를 따로 선물했다."], ["Nor did he even stay his hand here; he bought a set of cameos for his wife and a sapphire bracelet for Miss Bold; showered pearls and work-boxes on his daughters; and to each of his sons he presented a check for £20.", "그는 거기서 손을 멈추지도 않았다. 아내에게는 카메오 한 벌을, 볼드 양에게는 사파이어 팔찌를 사 주었고, 딸들에게는 진주와 반짇고리를 쏟아부었으며, 아들들에게는 저마다 20파운드짜리 수표를 한 장씩 안겼다."], ["On Mr. Harding he bestowed a magnificent violoncello with all the new-fashioned arrangements and expensive additions, which on account of these novelties that gentleman could never use with satisfaction to his audience or pleasure to himself.", "하딩 씨에게는 새로운 방식의 장치와 값비싼 부속을 모조리 갖춘 훌륭한 첼로를 내렸는데, 정작 그 새로운 것들 때문에 이 신사는 듣는 이를 흡족하게 하지도 못하고 스스로 즐겁지도 못하여 끝내 그 악기를 제대로 쓰지 못했다."]], [["Those who knew the archdeacon well perfectly understood the causes of his extravagance.", "부주교를 잘 아는 이들은 그 씀씀이의 까닭을 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["'Twas thus that he sang his song of triumph over Mr. Slope.", "그것이야말로 그가 슬로프 씨를 두고 부르는 개선의 노래였다."], ["This was his pæan, his hymn of thanksgiving, his loud oration.", "그것이 그의 찬가요, 감사의 송가요, 목청 높은 연설이었다."], ["He had girded himself with his sword and gone forth to the war; now he was returning from the field laden with the spoils of the foe.", "그는 허리에 칼을 차고 싸움터로 나아갔고, 이제 적에게서 빼앗은 전리품을 잔뜩 지고 싸움터에서 돌아오는 길이었다."], ["The cob and the cameos, the violoncello and the pianoforte, were all as it were trophies reft from the tent of his now-conquered enemy.", "승용마와 카메오, 첼로와 피아노는 하나같이 말하자면 이제는 정복당한 적의 천막에서 앗아 온 전승 기념물이었다."]], [["The Arabins after their marriage went abroad for a couple of months, according to the custom in such matters now duly established, and then commenced their deanery life under good auspices. And nothing can be more pleasant than the present arrangement of ecclesiastical affairs in Barchester.", "애러빈 부부는 혼인한 뒤 요즈음 이런 일에 으레 굳어진 관례를 좇아 두어 달 외국에 나가 있다가, 좋은 조짐 속에 수석사제관의 살림을 시작했다."], ["The titular bishop never interfered, and Mrs. Proudie not often.", "그리고 바체스터의 교회 사정이 지금처럼 흐뭇하게 정돈된 적은 없다."], ["Her sphere is more extended, more noble, and more suited to her ambition than that of a cathedral city.", "이름뿐인 주교는 결코 참견하는 법이 없고, 프라우디 부인도 자주는 참견하지 않는다."], ["As long as she can do what she pleases with the diocese, she is willing to leave the dean and chapter to themselves.", "부인의 활동 무대는 대성당이 있는 도시 하나보다 더 넓고 더 드높으며 그 야심에도 더 걸맞다."], ["Mr. Slope tried his hand at subverting the old-established customs of the close, and from his failure she had learnt experience.", "교구를 제 뜻대로 주무를 수만 있다면 부인은 수석 사제와 참사회쯤은 저희끼리 하도록 기꺼이 내버려 둔다."], ["The burly chancellor and the meagre little prebendary are not teased by any application respecting Sabbath-day schools, the dean is left to his own dominions, and the intercourse between Mrs. Proudie and Mrs.", "슬로프 씨가 대성당 경내의 오랜 관습을 뒤엎어 보려다 실패한 일에서 부인은 교훈을 얻었던 것이다."], ["Arabin is confined to a yearly dinner given by each to the other. At these dinners Dr.", "몸집 좋은 학무 참사원과 여윈 자그마한 참사회원은 안식일 학교 문제로 이래라저래라 하는 청을 받아 시달리는 일이 없고, 수석 사제는 제 영역을 제 뜻대로 다스리도록 맡겨져 있으며, 프라우디 부인과 애러빈 부인의 왕래는 서로 한 해에 한 번씩 베푸는 만찬에 그친다."], ["Grantly will not take a part, but he never fails to ask for and receive a full account of all that Mrs. Proudie either does or says.", "그랜틀리 박사는 그 만찬에는 끼지 않으나, 프라우디 부인이 무슨 짓을 하고 무슨 말을 했는지 낱낱이 묻고 들어 두는 일만은 한 번도 거른 적이 없다."]], [["His ecclesiastical authority has been greatly shorn since the palmy days in which he reigned supreme as mayor of the palace to his father, but nevertheless such authority as is now left to him he can enjoy without interference.", "그의 교회 내 권위는 아버지를 대신해 주교관의 궁재로 군림하던 전성기에 견주면 크게 깎여 나갔으나, 그럼에도 이제 그에게 남은 만큼의 권위는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 누릴 수 있다."], ["He can walk down the High Street of Barchester without feeling that those who see him are comparing his claims with those of Mr. Slope.", "그는 자기를 보는 이들이 자기의 자격을 슬로프 씨의 그것과 견주고 있다는 느낌 없이 바체스터의 하이 스트리트를 걸어 내려갈 수 있다."], ["The intercourse between Plumstead and the deanery is of the most constant and familiar description.", "플럼스테드와 수석사제관 사이의 왕래는 더할 나위 없이 잦고 허물없다."], ["Since Eleanor has been married to a clergyman, and especially to a dignitary of the church, Mrs. Grantly has found many more points of sympathy with her sister; and on a coming occasion, which is much looked forward to by all parties, she intends to spend a month or two at the deanery.", "엘리너가 성직자와, 그것도 교회의 고위 성직자와 혼인한 뒤로 그랜틀리 부인은 동생과 마음이 맞는 구석을 훨씬 더 많이 찾아냈고, 모두가 자못 기다리고 있는 다가올 일을 맞아 수석사제관에서 한두 달 지낼 작정이다."], ["She never thought of spending a month in Barchester when little Johnny Bold was born!", "어린 조니 볼드가 태어났을 때에는 바체스터에서 한 달을 지낼 생각 따위는 해 본 적도 없던 사람이!"]], [["The two sisters do not quite agree on matters of church doctrine, though their differences are of the most amicable description. Mrs.", "두 자매는 교리 문제에서는 그리 뜻이 맞지 않으나, 그 견해차는 더없이 정다운 종류의 것이다."], ["Arabin's church is two degrees higher than that of Mrs. Grantly.", "애러빈 부인의 신앙은 그랜틀리 부인의 그것보다 두 단계쯤 높다."], ["This may seem strange to those who will remember that Eleanor was once accused of partiality to Mr. Slope, but it is no less the fact.", "엘리너가 한때 슬로프 씨에게 마음이 기울었다는 혐의를 받았던 일을 기억하는 이에게는 이것이 이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 사실은 그래도 사실이다."], ["She likes her husband's silken vest, she likes his adherence to the rubric, she specially likes the eloquent philosophy of his sermons, and she likes the red letters in her own prayer-book.", "부인은 남편의 비단 조끼를 좋아하고, 남편이 예식 규정을 지키는 것을 좋아하며, 특히 남편의 설교에 담긴 유려한 사색을 좋아하고, 제 기도서에 붉은 글씨로 적힌 대목을 좋아한다."], ["It must not be presumed that she has a taste for candles, or that she is at all astray about the real presence, but she has an inkling that way.", "그렇다고 부인이 촛불을 즐긴다거나 실재 임재를 두고 갈피를 못 잡고 있다고 지레짐작해서는 안 되겠지만, 그쪽으로 어렴풋이 마음이 기울어 있기는 하다."], ["She sent a handsome subscription towards certain very heavy ecclesiastical legal expenses which have lately been incurred in Bath, her name of course not appearing; she assumes a smile of gentle ridicule when the Archbishop of Canterbury is named; and she has put up a memorial window in the cathedral.", "부인은 근래 배스에서 치러진 어떤 몹시 무거운 교회 소송 비용에 넉넉한 기부금을 보냈는데, 물론 이름은 내지 않았다. 캔터베리 대주교의 이름이 나오면 부드럽게 비웃는 미소를 지으며, 대성당에는 기념 창을 하나 세워 두었다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly, who belongs to the high and dry church, the High Church as it was some fifty years since, before tracts were written and young clergymen took upon themselves the highly meritorious duty of cleaning churches, rather laughs at her sister.", "소책자들이 쓰이고 젊은 성직자들이 교회를 청소하는 지극히 갸륵한 소임을 떠맡기 이전, 그러니까 오십 년쯤 전의 고교회파에 해당하는 고답건조파에 속하는 그랜틀리 부인은 동생을 두고 적잖이 웃는다."], ["She shrugs her shoulders and tells Miss Thorne that she supposes Eleanor will have an oratory in the deanery before she has done.", "부인은 어깨를 으쓱하며 손 양에게, 엘리너가 저러다 끝내는 수석사제관 안에 기도실까지 두게 될 모양이라고 말한다."], ["But she is not on that account a whit displeased.", "그렇다고 그 때문에 조금이라도 언짢아하는 것은 아니다."], ["A few High Church vagaries do not, she thinks, sit amiss on the shoulders of a young dean's wife.", "고교회파다운 별난 짓 몇 가지쯤은 젊은 수석 사제 부인의 어깨에 얹혀도 어울리지 않을 것이 없다고 부인은 생각한다."], ["It shows at any rate that her heart is in the subject, and it shows moreover that she is removed, wide as the poles asunder, from that cesspool of abomination in which it was once suspected that she would wallow and grovel.", "그것은 어쨌든 동생이 그 문제에 진심을 두고 있다는 표시요, 나아가 한때 동생이 그 안에서 뒹굴고 기게 되리라 의심받았던 그 가증스러운 시궁창에서 하늘과 땅만큼 멀리 떨어져 나왔다는 표시이기도 하다. Anathema maranatha!"], ["Anathema maranatha! Let anything else be held as blessed, so that that be well cursed.", "저것만 제대로 저주받는다면 다른 무엇이든 복되다 하여도 좋다."], ["Welcome kneelings and bowings, welcome matins and complines, welcome bell, book, and candle, so that Mr. Slope's dirty surplices and ceremonial Sabbaths be held in due execration!", "무릎 꿇음도 좋고 절함도 좋고, 아침기도도 좋고 끝기도도 좋고, 종과 책과 촛불도 다 좋으니, 다만 슬로프 씨의 더러운 중백의와 형식뿐인 안식일이 마땅한 저주를 받기만 한다면!"]], [["If it be essentially and absolutely necessary to choose between the two, we are inclined to agree with Mrs. Grantly that the bell, book, and candle are the lesser evil of the two.", "굳이 둘 가운데 하나를 반드시 골라야 한다면, 우리로서도 종과 책과 촛불 쪽이 그래도 덜한 해악이라는 그랜틀리 부인의 말에 손을 들어 주고 싶다."], ["Let it however be understood that no such necessity is admitted in these pages.", "다만 이 책장 안에서는 그렇게 골라야 할 필요 따위는 인정하지 않는다는 점을 밝혀 두기로 하자."]], [["Dr. Arabin (we suppose he must have become a doctor when he became a dean) is more moderate and less outspoken on doctrinal points than his wife, as indeed in his station it behoves him to be.", "애러빈 박사는(수석 사제가 되었으니 박사도 되었으려니 우리는 짐작한다) 교리 문제에서 아내보다 온건하고 말수도 적은데, 실상 그 자리에 있는 사람으로서는 마땅히 그래야 한다."], ["He is a studious, thoughtful, hard-working man.", "그는 학구적이고 사려 깊으며 부지런히 일하는 사람이다."], ["He lives constantly at the deanery and preaches nearly every Sunday.", "수석사제관에 늘 머물러 살며 거의 주일마다 설교한다."], ["His time is spent in sifting and editing old ecclesiastical literature and in producing the same articles new.", "그의 시간은 옛 교회 문헌을 가려 뽑아 엮는 일과 같은 것을 새로 써내는 일에 바쳐진다."], ["At Oxford he is generally regarded as the most promising clerical ornament of the age.", "옥스퍼드에서 그는 이 시대 성직자 가운데 가장 앞날이 밝은 자랑거리로 여겨진다."], ["He and his wife live together in perfect mutual confidence.", "그와 아내는 서로를 온전히 믿으며 함께 산다."], ["There is but one secret in her bosom which he has not shared.", "아내의 가슴속에는 남편과 나누지 않은 비밀이 단 하나 있다."], ["He has never yet learned how Mr. Slope had his ears boxed.", "그는 슬로프 씨가 어쩌다 따귀를 맞았는지를 아직껏 알지 못한다."]], [["The Stanhopes soon found that Mr. Slope's power need no longer operate to keep them from the delight of their Italian villa.", "스탠호프 일가는 이제 슬로프 씨의 힘이 저희를 이탈리아 별장의 즐거움에서 더는 떼어 놓을 수 없게 되었음을 이내 알아차렸다."], ["Before Eleanor's marriage they had all migrated back to the shores of Como.", "엘리너가 혼인하기 전에 그들은 벌써 모두 코메 호숫가로 돌아가 있었다."], ["They had not been resettled long before the signora received from Mrs. Arabin a very pretty though very short epistle, in which she was informed of the fate of the writer.", "다시 자리를 잡은 지 얼마 되지 않아 시뇨라는 애러빈 부인에게서 아주 예쁘장하되 아주 짧은 편지를 한 통 받았고, 그것으로 쓴 이의 신상에 일어난 일을 알게 되었다."], ["This letter was answered by another--bright, charming, and witty, as the signora's letters always were--and so ended the friendship between Eleanor and the Stanhopes.", "이 편지에는 또 한 통의 답장이 갔으니, 시뇨라의 편지가 언제나 그러하듯 반짝이고 매혹적이고 재치 있는 것이었다. 그리고 그것으로 엘리너와 스탠호프 일가의 우정은 끝이 났다."]], [["One word of Mr. Harding, and we have done.", "하딩 씨에 관해 한마디만 하면 우리 일은 끝난다."], ["He is still precentor of Barchester and still pastor of the little church of St. Cuthbert's.", "그는 여전히 바체스터의 선창자이며, 여전히 세인트 커스버트의 작은 교회를 맡은 목자다."], ["In spite of what he has so often said himself, he is not even yet an old man.", "스스로 그토록 자주 말해 온 것과는 달리, 그는 아직 노인도 아니다."], ["He does such duties as fall to his lot well and conscientiously, and is thankful that he has never been tempted to assume others for which he might be less fitted.", "제 몫으로 떨어진 소임을 훌륭하고 양심껏 해내며, 자기에게 덜 어울렸을 법한 다른 소임을 떠맡고 싶은 유혹에 한 번도 빠져 본 적이 없음을 고맙게 여긴다."]], [["The author now leaves him in the hands of his readers: not as a hero, not as a man to be admired and talked of, not as a man who should be toasted at public dinners and spoken of with conventional absurdity as a perfect divine, but as a good man, without guile, believing humbly in the religion which he has striven to teach, and guided by the precepts which he has striven to learn.", "이제 글쓴이는 그를 독자의 손에 맡긴다. 영웅으로서가 아니고, 우러르고 입에 올릴 만한 인물로서도 아니며, 공식 만찬 자리에서 축배의 대상이 되고 완전무결한 성직자라는 상투적인 허튼소리로 일컬어질 만한 인물로서도 아니라, 다만 거짓 없는 선한 사람으로서, 자기가 가르치려 애써 온 종교를 겸손히 믿고 자기가 배우려 애써 온 계율을 따라 살아온 사람으로서 맡긴다."]]];
